19630433-7701-48e0-8e65-3f9d66f07484	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-10	Quiz 10: Diabetic Ketoacidosis, Adrenoceptors & Skull Base Foramina	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 10\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 10 years old child admitted in hospital and being treated for DKA. In DKA treating complication cerebral edema, hypertonic saline is used. How hypertonic saline will decrease cerebral edema?\n\n- A. K move out of the cell\n- **B. Water moves out of the cells** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Na and k move out of cells\n- D. There is no net movement of water\n- E. Water moves into the cell\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nwhen hypertonic saline is administrated ECF becomes more concentrated compared with ICF. Osmotic potential draw movement of water molecules from inside to out of the cells.\n\nNote: After heavy exercise when person drink water it increases intracellular fluid ICF volume (Recall) because water is hypotonic to body fluids dilute extracellular fluid relative to intracellular fluid, osmotic potential draws water into cells.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nAll of the following drugs causes high anion gap metabolic acidosis except?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Normal anion | Causes of a normal anion gap |\n> | gap | acidosis: Mnemonic FUSEDCARS |\n> |  | e Fistula |\n> |  | (pancreaticoduodenal) |\n> |  | e Ureteroenteric conduit |\n> |  |  Saline administration |\n> |  | Endocrine |\n> |  | (hyperparathyroidism) |\n> |  | Diarrhea (Recall) |\n> |  | Carbonic anhydrase |\n> |  | inhibitors (e.g., |\n> |  | acetazolamide) |\n> |  |  Ammonium chloride |\n> |  | Renal tubular acidosis |\n> |  | Spironolactone |\n> | High anion | Causes of Increased Anion Gap |\n> |  | Metabolic Acidosis |\n> |  | Mnemonic MUDPILES |\n> |  | Methanol |\n> |  | e Uremia (in renal failure) |\n> |  | Diabetic ketoacidosis |\n> |  | Propylene glycol overdose |\n> |  | Infection/Iron |\n> |  | overdose/Isoniazid |\n> |  | Lactic acidosis |\n> |  | e Ethylene glycol overdose |\n> |  | Salicylate (Aspirin) overdose |\n> |  | Multiple myeloma |\n\n- **A. Methylene glycol** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Propylene glycol\n- C. Isoniazid\n- D. Aspirin\n- E. D lactates\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAnion gap can be calculated as (Na + K ) -- (Cl + Hco3) Recall. Normal anion gap is 10-16 mmol/L\n\nNote: salicylate (Aspirin) overdose initially causes respiratory alkalosis (Recall) followed by metabolic acidosis\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 45 years old male presents to the ED with complaint of dizziness and increased pigmentation all over the body. Initial assessment at triage he was hypotensive. Lab value reveals hyponatremia and hyperkalemia. Which one of the following hormone deficiency caused this condition?\n\n- A. PTH\n- B. ADH\n- C. Androgens\n- **D. Aldosterone** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Cortisol\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInsufficiency of adrenal cortex is called Addison's disease\n\nDeficiency of aldosterone as seen is adrenal Insufficiency result in hyponatremia ,hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis (Recall)\n\nCushing's Syndrome: Set of symptoms that occur when there is excess of cortisol\n\nMost common cause is exogenous Glucosteroids\n\nCushing's disease: when Cushing syndrome is caused by ACTH producing pituitary tumor (Most common endogenous cause (Recall)\n\nACTH secreting pituitary adenoma Excess cortisol due to adrenal adenoma (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 25-year-old man presents with diabetic ketoacidosis. You start to treat him with short-acting insulin and intravenous fluids as per your local protocol.\n\nWhich electrolyte disturbance is the most at risk of developing as a result of your treatment?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | HYPOKALEMIA | Prominent U wave |\n> |  | QT prolongation (Recall) |\n> |  | T wave flattening |\n> |  | Progressive lengthening |\n> |  | of PR interval |\n> | HYPERKALEMIA | Peaked t waves (Recall) |\n> |  | Shortened QT interval |\n> |  | P wave eventually |\n> |  | disappear |\n> |  | Broad QRS complex |\n> |  | {Immediately prior to |\n> |  | cardiac arrest) |\n\n- A. Hypermagnesemia\n- B. Hypophosphatasemia\n- **C. Hypokalemia** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Hyponatremia\n- E. Hyperchloremia\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInsulin excess lowers the serum potassium concentration by driving the K+ into the cells primarily by enhancing the activity of NA-K-ATPase pum p.\n\nECG changes in hypokalemia and hyperkalemia\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 45-year-old patient presents with sudden onset of facial paralysis affecting one side. After examination, the diagnosis of Bell's palsy is made. The most common cause of Bell's palsy is:\n\n- A. Viral\n- B. Autoimmune\n- C. Hereditary\n- **D. Idiopathic** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Trauma\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBell's palsy occurs due to inammation and swelling of the seventh cranial nerve, which is responsible for controlling the muscles of the face. While specic conditions, such as viral infections, can lead to this inammation, many instances of Bell's palsy arise without a denitive cause, hence they are classied as idiopathic.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 40 years old male presents to the ED with exacerbation of asthma. Which one of the following beta blockers is safe for asthma?\n\n- **A. Bisoprolol** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Nadolol\n- C. Propanol\n- D. Carvedilol\n- E. Labetalol\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCardio selective beta blockers like nebivolol, bisoprolol and metoprolol are safe in both asthma an d diabetes\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nWhich one of the following receptors is mainly responsible for smooth muscle contraction and blood vessels constriction?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Agents |\n> | Thromboxane A2 | Prostacyclin |\n> | Angiotensin Il | Beta-agonists |\n> | Noradrenaline {alpha 1- |  |\n> | receptors) |  |\n\n- A. Beta 3\n- B. Beta 1\n- C. Alpha 2\n- **D. Alpha 1** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Beta 2\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nA useful way to remember: Alpha 1 for constriction, Alpha 2 for inhibition, B 1 for heart, and B2 for relaxation\n\nHigh yield:\n\nNote: Nitroprusside is metabolized to nitric oxide, which causes both arterial and venous dilation (Recall) This effect is immediate and results i n a decrease in both preload and afterload. (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 60-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts a medication that increases insulin release by closing ATP-sensitive potassium channels. Which drug class is responsible for this effect?\n\n- A. SGLT-2 inhibitors\n- B. GLP-1 receptor agonists\n- C. Biguanides\n- **D. Sulfonylureas** (Correct Answer)\n- E. DPP-4 inhibitors\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDrug Class along with pharmacolgi cal action Biguanides: Decreases hepatic glucose production and increases insulin sensitivity in peripheral tissues (e.g., muscles). Common example: Metfor min.\n\nDPP-4 Inhibitors: Inhibits the enzyme DPP-4, which breaks down incretins like GLP-1. This prolon gs incretin action, increasing insulin release and decreasing glucagon secretion.\n\nSulfonylureas: Stimulates insulin release by binding to and inhibiting ATP-sensitive potassium channels on pancreatic beta cells. Common example: Glipizide.\n\nSGLT-2 Inhibitors: Inhibits sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT-2) in the kidneys, reducing glucose reabsorption and increasing urinary glucose excretion.\n\nGLP-1 Receptor Agonists: Mimics GLP-1, enhancing insulin secretion, slowing gastric emptying, and reducing glucagon release. Often leads to weight loss.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nWhat is the normal right ventricular systolic pressure?\n\n- A. 3-7mmhg\n- B. 7-12mmhg\n- C. 5-10mmhg\n- D. 10-20mmhg\n- **E. 20-30mmhg** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNormal RV systolic pressure is 20-30 mmHg and normal diastolic pressure is 3-7 mmHg.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nWhich one of the following organelles has essential role in achieving uterine involution?\n\n- A. Mitochondria\n- B. Smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n- C. Golgi body\n- D. Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n- **E. Lysosome** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCell organelle involved in uterine involution is lysosome\n\nWhile Hormone uterine involution is oxytocin\n\nNote: Mitochondria are responsible for the production of chemical energy in the form of ATP. (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\n45 years old male presented to the ED post thyroid surgery with complain of periorbital numbness, paresthesia of hand and feet and abnormal movement what is the cause of his condition?\n\n- A. Thyroid Crisis\n- B. Hypokalemia\n- **C. Hypocalcemia** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Hypomagnesemia\n- E. Hypothyroidism\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPTH is primarily released in response to decrease d calcium. PTH causes demineralization of bones and causes increased plasma calcium level (Recall) and decreased phosphate levels\n\nPost-thyroid surgery most likely complication is parathyroid insufficiency leads to hypocalcemia\n\nNote: Parathyroid Harmone increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule of nephron (Recall) and increases excretion of phosphate by inhibiting reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule of nephron\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\n45 years old male presented to the ED post thyroid surgery with complain of periorbital numbness, paresthesia of hand and feet and abnormal movement what is the cause of his condition?\n\n- A. Hypothyroidism\n- B. Hypokalemia\n- C. Thyroid Crisis\n- **D. Hypocalcemia** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Hypomagnesemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPTH is primarily released in response to decrease d calcium. PTH causes demineralization of bones and causes increased plasma calcium level (Recall) and decreased phosphate levels\n\nPost-thyroid surgery most likely complication is parathyroid insufficiency leads to hypocalcemia\n\nNote: Parathyroid Harmone increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule of nephron (Recall) and increases excretion of phosphate by inhibiting reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule of nephron\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nRegarding the epithelium of respiratory tract. Which of the following is true?\n\n- A. Transitional epithelium\n- B. Simple squamous epithelium\n- C. Simple stratified epithelium\n- **D. Ciliated columnar epithelium** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Stratified columnar epithelium\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRespiratory tract from the trachea down until th e respiratory bronchioles is lined with Ciliated columnar epithelium\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nWhich of the following type of blood vessels contributes the most of systemic vascular resistance?\n\n- **A. Arteriole** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Venules\n- C. Capillaries\n- D. Arteries\n- E. Veins\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nResistance is a force that opposes the flow of a fluid. In blood vessels, most of the resistance is due to vessel diameter. As vessel diameter decreases, the resistance increases and blood flow decreases.\n\nsmaller arteries and arterioles contain more muscle and are resistance vessels.\n\nVeins have large diameter than equivalent arteries and provide less resistance\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nDuring a skull examination, a medical student is asked to identify the structure that passes through the foramen spinosum. Which of the following structures passes through foramen spinosum?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Optic nerve\n- B. Hypoglossal nerve\n- C. Internal carotid artery\n- D. Meningeal branch of mandibular artery\n- **E. Meningeal branch of the mandibular nerve >** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe structures that pass through the foramen s pinosum are:\n\nMeningeal branch of the mandibular nerve Middle meningeal artery Middle meningeal vein\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 10:** 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C, 5-D, 6-A, 7-D, 8-D, 9-E, 10-E, 11-C, 12-D, 13-D, 14-A, 15-E\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
2f622cef-0694-416b-8346-e1a4d057f983	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-11	Quiz 11: Blood Gas Physiology, Electrolyte ECG & Hematological Disorders	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 11\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nWhilst in your physiology lecture you ask medical students, which of the following causes a right shift in the oxygen dissociation curve?\n\n- A. Increase PH\n- B. Fetal Hb\n- **C. Decrease PH** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Decrease PCO2\n- E. Decrease H+\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCauses of left ward shift\n\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up oxygen)\n\nIncreased pH Decreased pCO2 Decreased temperature Decreased H+ Decreased 2-3DPG CO poisoning Methemoglobinemia Fetal hemoglobin (Recall)\n\nCauses of Right ward shift\n\n: Decreased affinity for oxygen (More willing to gi ve up oxygen)\n\nDecreased pH (Recall)\n\nIncreased pCO2 Increased H+ Increased Temperature Increased 2-3DPG Pregnancy (oxygen to the fetus)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 70-year-old woman has a pathological fracture of her femur. Her blood tests reveal hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, and raised parathyroid hormone.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Primary hyperparathyroidism |  | Calcium increase |\n> |  | . | Decreased phosphate |\n> |  | . | Increased PTH and increased vitD |\n> | Secondary hyperparathyroidism | . | Decreased calcium |\n> |  | . | Phosphate decreased or increased |\n> |  | . | Increased PTH and decreased vitD |\n> | Tertiary hyperparathyroidism | . | Increased Calcium |\n> |  |  | Increased phosphate |\n> |  |  | Increased PTH and decreased vitD |\n\n- A. Myeloma\n- B. Osteomalacia\n- **C. Primary hyperparathyroidism** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Bony metastasis\n- E. Paget's disease\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: In osteomalacia : Elevated alkaline phosphatase, Decreased vit D, Hypocalcemia and increased PTH\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nElderly male presented with c/o high-grade fever with chills rigors and dysphagia. Also had h/o tooth sensitivity, for which consulted dentist and found to have decayed tooth. On examination you bilateral swelling in the floor of mouth. Infection of which space is most common for above presentation?\n\n- **A. Submandibular space** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Sub lingual space\n- C. Sub mental space\n- D. Parapharyngeal space\n- E. Retropharyngeal space\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLudwigs's angina is life threatening cellulitis of t he soft tissue of the floor of the mouth and neck.\n\nOriginates from dental infection in the molars specially 2nd and 3rd molarsinvolves three compartments of the floor of the mouth, sublingual, submental, and submandibular (Most commonly).\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nRegarding the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease which one of the following statements is true?\n\n- A. Gastric inhibitory polypeptide secretion from K cells\n- B. Bicarbonate secretion from pancreatic ductal cells\n- C. H+ Secretion from Brunner's glands\n- D. H+ Secretion from pancreatic ductal cells\n- **E. Bicarbonate secretion from Brunner's glands** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBrunner's glands are located in the submucosa o f the duodenum. They secrete an alkaline fluid containing mucin, which protects the mucosa from the acidic stomach contents entering the duodenum and provide alkaline condition for the intestinal enzymes to be active\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nA cell of pancreas =Glucagon B cells of pancreas -=Insulin D cells of pancreas =Somatostatin E cells of pancreas = Ghrelin (Ghrelin released mainly by the stomach and in small amounts released by small intestine and pancreas. Ghrelin regulates appetite and satiety (Recall)\n\nF cells of pancreas = Pancreatic polypeptide G cells in pyloric antrum = Gastrin I cell in duodenum and jejunum = CCK K cells in duodenum and jejunum = GIP S cells in duodenum =Secretin\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 50-year-old woman presents to the ED with palpitations. Her ECG reveals QT prolongation. She has been taking an antibiotic for a skin infection.\n\nWhich antibiotic is the most likely to have caused her ECG abnormalities?\n\n- A. Doxycycline\n- B. Clindamycin\n- **C. Clarithromycin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Flucloxacillin\n- E. Tetracycline\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDRUGS CAUSING QT PROLONGATION Remember with ABCDEF ANTI-ARRYTHMICS: amiodarone, procainamide, quinidine, sotalol ANTIBIOTICS: macrolides, fluoroquinolones ANTIPSYCHOTICS: chlorpromazine, haloperidol D ANTIDEPRESSANTS: SSRIS (citalopram, escital opram); TCAS (amitriptyline)\n\nANTI-EMETICS: domperidone, ondansetron ANTIFUNGALS: azoles (fluconazole), pentamidine\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 5o years old male presents to the ED with complaint of shortness of breath. He is known case of restrictive lung disease. In restrictive lung disease which of the following results would you expect to find on spirometry?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | asthma, COPD, | Intrinsic causes: |\n> | emphysema, | interstitial lung disease |\n> | bronchiectasis | (Lung fibrosis) , pulmonary |\n> |  | oedema, pneumonia |\n> |  | Extrinsic causes: pleural |\n> |  | effusion, pneumothorax, |\n> |  | chest wall deformities, |\n> |  | neuromuscular disease, |\n> |  | connective tissue disease |\n> |  | and obesity |\n> | (Recall) |  |\n> | Normal or high |  |\n\n- A. High FVC\n- B. Low FEV1/FVC ratio\n- C. Normal FVC\n- **D. Increased FEV1 /FVC ratio** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Increased FEV1\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn restrictive pattern both FEV1 and FVC reduced but FEV1/FVC ratio increased due to greater elastic recoil\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 30 years old male presents to the ED with complain of abdominal bloating, nausea, and bouts of watery diarrhea. You suspect infection of giardia lamblia. Giardia lamblia attaches to gut mucosa via?\n\n- **A. Specialized sucking disc** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Attaches by its haemagglutinin antigen\n- C. Curli fibers\n- D. Using fimbriae\n- E. Specialized hooks\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMicroorganism & Mechanism of Attachment Neisseria gonorrhoeae, E-Coli=Adheres to the genital mucosa using fimbriae\n\nInfluenza Attaches =by its haemagglutinin antigen (Recall)\n\nGiardia lamblia=Attaches to gut mucosa via a specialized sucking disc\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 4-year-old African child with sickle cell disease was brought into the ED with a complaint of bone pain associated with a limp. Imaging revealed lucent bone lesion, you suspect osteomyelitis, what is the most likely causative pathogen?\n\n- A. Pseudomonas\n- B. Staph aureus\n- **C. Salmonella** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Staph epidermidis\n- E. E-Coli\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSalmonella enterica is a gram-negative rod that causes enterocolitis (Food poisoning), Enteric fev er (Typhoid), Septicemia, and osteomyelitis\n\nPrimarily transmitted through fecal-oral route usually from ingestion of contaminated poultry, eggs, and uncooked meat\n\nHigh yield:\n\nRegarding osteomyelitis Most common cause generally: Staph Aureus (Recall)\n\nThe most common cause in IV drug abuser: Staph Aureus\n\nThe most common cause in sickle cell disease: Salmonella\n\nMost common cause after hip replacement surgery: Staph epidermidis\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nWhich of the following is the earliest electrocardiogram (EKG) finding in acute myocardial infarction (MI)?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | HYPOKALEMIA | Prominent U wave |\n> |  | QT prolongation (Recall) |\n> |  | T wave flattening |\n> |  | Progressive lengthening of |\n> |  | PR interval |\n> | HYPERKALEMIA | Peaked t waves (Recall) |\n> |  | Shortened QT interval |\n> |  | P wave eventually |\n> |  | disappear |\n> |  | Broad QRS complex |\n> |  | (Immediately prior to |\n> |  | cardiac arrest) |\n> |  | Note: Hyperacute T waves |\n> |  | are the earliest ECG |\n> |  | findings in acute |\n> |  | myocardial infraction. |\n\n- A. ST depression\n- B. Q waves\n- C. T-wave inversion\n- **D. Hyperacute T waves** (Correct Answer)\n- E. ST elevation\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 32 years old male was brought into the ED by paramedics with a complaint of head injury. After initial assessment and imaging results empty Sella turcica. Empty Sella turcica occurs due to fracture of which bone?\n\n- A. Temporal\n- B. Occipital\n- **C. Sphenoid** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Frontal\n- E. Parietal\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Sella turcica is a saddle-shape depression in the sphenoid bone of the human skull contains t he pituitary gland and distal portion of the pituitary stalk\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA patient requires intubation due to respiratory failure. Your consultant wants to ensure you correctly preoxygenate the patient to replace nitrogen with oxygen, allowing a safer apnea time during Intubation\n\nWhich lung volume measurement best describes the volume of nitrogen that you are displacing?\n\n- A. Total lung capacity\n- B. V Vital capacity\n- **C. Functional residual capacity** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Inspiratory capacity\n- E. Residual volume\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe functional residual capacity (FRC) is the most important store of oxygen in the body. The greater the FRC, the longer apnoea can be tolerated before critical hypoxia develops. The aim of preoxygenation is to replace nitrogen in the FRC with oxygen; this process is also referred to as denitrogenation . This has a significant impact on body oxygen store and therefore increases tolerance to apnoea substantially.\n\nIntubation in the supine position can lead to a decrease in functional residual capacity (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 32-year-old dialysis patient collapses at home. He is due for dialysis today. He has severe hyperkaliemia k+ 7.4, and you perform a 12 lead ECG\n\nWhat ECG feature would you be most likely to see immediately prior to deterioration to cardiac arrest?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | HYPOKALEMIA | Prominent U wave |\n> |  | QT prolongation (Recall) |\n> |  | T wave flattening |\n> |  | Progressive lengthening |\n> |  | of PR interval |\n> | HYPERKALEMIA | Peaked t waves (Recall) |\n> |  | Shortened QT interval |\n> |  | P wave eventually |\n> |  | disappear |\n> |  | Broad QRS complex |\n> |  | {Immediately prior to |\n> |  | cardiac arrest) |\n\n- **A. Broad QRS complexes** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Flattened P waves\n- C. Sinus tachycardia\n- D. Prolonged PR interval\n- E. Peaked T waves\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA patient with a history of sinusitis presents unwell with fever, periorbital edema, visual disturbance, ptosis and altered sensation to the face. You suspect cavernous sinus thrombosis.\n\nWhich cranial nerve is most commonly affected?\n\n- A. Trigeminal\n- **B. Abducent** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Facial\n- D. Oculomotor\n- E. Trochlear\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCavernous sinus thrombosis (CST) refers to the formation of a clot within the cavernous sinus. It is important to note that the superior ophthalmic vein forms an anastomosis with the facial vein. Therefore, the ophthalmic veins represent a potential route by which infection can spread from an extracranial to an intracranial site.\n\nNote: The abducens nerve (CN VI) is most commonly affected in cavernous sinus thrombosis\n\nStructures passing through cavernous sinus useful mnemonic: O TOM CAT OTOM (oculomotor nerve, trochlear nerve, ophthalmic branch, maxillary branch) refers to the lateral wall contents from superior to inferior CAT (internal carotid artery, abducens nerve, trochlear nerve) refers to the horizontal contents, from medial to lateral.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nWhich of the following is not a physiological change in pregnancy?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Increased | Increased | Increased tidal | Increased factor |\n> | cardiac output | plasma volume | volume (Recall) | Vii, viii,ix,x,xii, von |\n> | Increasedheart | + IncreasedRed | Decrease residual | Willibrand factor |\n> | rate | cell volume | volume | and fibrinogen. |\n> |  Decreased | Anemia | DecreasedFRC | Decreased |\n> | peripheral |  | (Recall) | protein S |\n> | vascular |  |  |  |\n> | resistance |  |  |  |\n\n- **A. Decrease in tidal volume** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Increased plasma volume\n- C. Increased cardiac output\n- D. Increased red cell volume\n- E. Decreased gastric motility\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPhysiological changes in pregnancy\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA patient with a known spinal cord ependymoma presents to his neurologist for a checkup. He complains that he has had difficulty walking, which he attributes to left leg weakness. On exam, he is noted to have 1/5 strength in his left lower extremity, as well as decreased vibration and position sensation in the left lower extremity and decreased pain and temperature sensation in the right lower extremity. Which of the following spinal cord lesions is most consistent with his presentation?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Card |  |  |\n> | syndrome Mechanism | Tract injury | Symptoms |\n> | C let d |  | > Death if c1-c3 injured. |\n> | Cor |  | > Quadriplegia |\n> | transection Major trauma | All tracts | Paraplegia |\n> |  |  | > Complete sensory loss below lesion |\n> |  |  | > Urinary and faecal continence |\n> | Brown- Hemitransection or |  | > Ipsilateral hemiparesis |\n> | | unilateral | tract on one | > Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, |\n> | compression of the | sige | vibration and fine touch sensation |\n> | cord |  | > Contralateral loss of crude touch, |\n> |  |  | pain and temperature sensation |\n> | Central cord | usually seen in older |  |  Bilateral motor loss |\n> |  | Corticospinal | > Varying degree of sensory loss |\n> | syndrome | who | > Greater loss in upper limb than lower limb] |\n> | sustain a hyperextension | SPinothalamic |  | > Greater loss of motor in upper limb than |\n> | injury | tract | sensory function |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Posterior cord syndrome\n- **B. Left-sided Brown-Sequard (hemisection)** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Syringomyelia\n- D. Right-sided Brown-Sequard (hemisection)\n- E. Anterior cord syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
4194397b-f336-490c-bb06-c5a49cd8bfc0	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-16	Quiz 16: Sepsis Biomarkers, Adrenal Crisis & Limb Compartments	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 16\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nRegarding the sole of foot, which one of the following muscles is a part of 1st layer of foot?\n\n- A. Adductor hallucis\n- B. Flexor hallucis Brevis\n- C. Lumbricals\n- **D. Abductor hallucis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Interosseous muscles\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMuscles of sole of foot. (4 layers)\n\nFirst layer: abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis & abductor digitorum minimi Second layer: flexor digitorum accessorius, 4 lumbricals and tendons of flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus Third layer: flexor hallucis brevis, flexor digitorum minimi brevis & adductor hallucis Fourth layer: Plantar and dorsal interossei muscles, Two long tendons of extrinsic muscles - tibialis posterior and peroneus longus.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 45-year-old woman with a newly diagnosis of type ii diabetes has significant hyperglycemia.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of her elevated blood glucose?\n\n- A. Production of abnormal insulin\n- **B. Increased cellular resistance to insulin** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Excessive glucocorticoid production\n- D. Reduced insulin production by pancreatic beta cells\n- E. Reduced glucagon secretion\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nType 2 diabetes mellitus is characterized by hyperglycemia, insulin resistance, and relative impairment in insulin secretion.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 70-year-old man who is a chronic smoker presents with shortness of breath. An arterial blood sample shows a raised PCO2. Which finding would best indicate this is a long-standing increase in arterial PCO2?\n\n- A. Increased renal potassium excretion\n- B. Increased urinary bicarbonate excretion\n- **C. Increased renal bicarbonate formation** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Decreased plasma potassium concentration\n- E. Decreased urinary ammonium salts\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRespiratory acidosis: Compensated by Increased renal bicarbonate formation and resulting metabolic alkalosis\n\nMetabolic acidosis: Compensated by decreased Pco2 formation through hyperventilating and resulting in respiratory alkalosis.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nMifepristone is indicated in the medical termination of pregnancy. It acts as an antagonist at which one of the following receptors?\n\n- A. Estrogen receptors\n- B. Adrenergic receptors\n- C. Dopamine receptors\n- D. Muscarinic receptors\n- **E. Progesterone receptors** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMifepristone is an antagonist at progesterone an d glucocorticoid receptors. It stimulates uterine contractility and increases prostaglandins through inhibition of prostaglandin dehydrogenase. It also blocks cortisol receptor binding and reduce s excess cortisol effects is cases of Cushing syndrome.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA comatose patient has a respiratory rate of 5 breaths per minute following an overdose of heroin. He receives naloxone and his respiratory rate rapidly improves to 16 breaths per minute. Which component of arterial blood gas sampling is most likely to rapid decrease following this intervention?\n\n- A. Po2\n- **B. Pco2** (Correct Answer)\n- C. pH\n- D. Base excess\n- E. Hco3\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCo2 is vasodilator causes increases cerebral blood flow (Recall)\n\nHypoventilation increases C02 and increases cerebral blood flow Hyperventilation causes decrease Pco2 and decreases cerebral blood flow\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 23-year-old male presents to the ED with a lacerated wound on his wrist. You ask junior fellows wrist joint is an example of ?\n\n- A. Synovial pivot joint\n- **B. Synovial condyloid joint** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Synovial plane joint\n- D. Synovial ball and socket joint\n- E. Synovial hinge joint\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWrist joint: Synovial condyloid joint Sternoclavicular joint: Synovial saddle-shaped joint Acromioclavicular joint: Synovial plane joint Shoulder joint: Synovial ball and socket joint Elbow joint: Synovial hinge joint Radioulnar joints: Synovial pivot joint The joint between atlas and axis is Pivot joint\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 22-year-old woman involved in a road traffic accident sustains a fractured pelvis. You suspect an injury to sciatic nerve.\n\nWhich joint movements are most likely to be affected?\n\n- A. Hip adduction and flexion\n- B. Hip adduction and extension\n- C. Hip rotation and knee flexion\n- **D. Extend thigh at hip and knee flexion** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Hip adduction and rotation\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSciatic nerve Innervates the muscles of the posterior compartment of thigh (biceps femoris, semimembranosus and semitendinosus) and the hamstring portion of the adductor magnus (remaining portion of which is supplied by the obturator nerve).\n\nSciatic nerve extend thigh at hip joint and flex leg at knee joint.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nRegarding respiratory muscles, which one of the following muscles is a forced muscle of expiration?\n\n- A. External Intercostal\n- **B. Rectus abdominis** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Scalenus Medius\n- D. Sternocleidomastoid\n- E. Scalenus anterior\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMuscles of forced expiration include:\n\nAnterior abdominal muscles (rectus abdominis is the main muscle)\n\ninternal Intercostal muscles External intercostal muscles are the muscles of inspiration.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nTaste sensation from anterior 2/3 of tongue is carried by?\n\n- A. Vagus nerve\n- B. Hypoglossal nerve\n- C. Trigeminal nerve\n- D. Glossopharyngeal\n- **E. Facial nerve** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve supplies taste sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue (RECALL).\n\nIn the anterior 2/3, general sensation is supplied by the trigeminal nerve (CNV). Specifically, the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve (C N V3). (RECALL)\n\nThe posterior 1/3 of the tongue both touch and taste are carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CNIX) (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nYou are asked to review a 16-year-old girl who presents with a right shoulder dislocation. This is her seventh right shoulder dislocation within a year. You suspect a hypermobility or ligament pathology. Which one of the following ligaments is most likely injured in acromioclavicular fracture?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Glenohumeral ligaments\n- B. Transverse humeral ligament\n- **C. Coracoclavicular ligament** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Coracoacromial ligament\n- E. Coracohumeral ligament\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAcromioclavicular joint\n\nTYPE= Plane type synovial joint\n\nThe acromioclavicular joint consists of an articulation between the lateral end of the clavicle and the acromion of the scapula\n\nLIGAMENTS OF THE JOINT\n\nThere are three main ligaments that support the acromioclavicular joint\n\nAcromioclavicular ligament Conoid ligament Trapezoid ligament\n\nCollectively, the conoid and trapezoid ligaments are known as the coracoclavicular ligament. It is the strongest ligament and suspends weight of the upper limb\n\nHigh yield:\n\nThe shoulder joint\n\nType: synovial ball and socket joint\n\nShoulder joint is formed by the articulation of th e head of the humerus with the glenoid cavity o f the scapula\n\nLigaments of the shoulder joint:\n\nIn the shoulder joint, the ligaments play a key role in stabilizing the bony structures.\n\nGlenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle and inferior) They act to stabilize the anterior aspect o f the joint. They are the main source of stability for the shoulder (Recall), holding it in place and preventing it from dislocating anteriorly. Coracohumeral ligament: It supports the superior part of the joint capsule.\n\nTransverse humeral ligament: This ligament spa ns the distance between the two tubercles of the humerus. It holds the tendon of the long head of the biceps in the intertubercular groove Coraco-clavicular ligament: This ligament composed of the trapezoid and conoid ligaments and runs from the clavicle to the coracoid process of t he scapula. They work alongside the acromioclavicular ligament to maintain the alignment of the clavicle in relation to the scapula.\n\nCoracoacromial ligament. This ligament runs between the acromion and coracoid process of the scapula it forms the coraco-acromial arch. This structure overlies the shoulder joint, preventing superior displacement of the humeral head. Stability of the joint\n\nRotator cuff muscles [SITS MUSCLES] Recall Glenoid labrum: A fibrocartilaginous ridge surrounding the glenoid cavity Ligaments: Extra Capsular ligaments form the coraco-acromial arch.\n\nBiceps tendon - it acts as a minor humeral head depressor, thereby contributing to stability.\n\nMovements of the shoulder joint: Shoulder joint is highly mobile ball and socket joint allows various movements like flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, medial rotation, lateral rotation and ci rcumduction\n\nCLINICAL PEARL:\n\nShoulder joint dislocations\n\nAnterior shoulder dislocation: Most common injury results from forceful abduction, extension a nd external rotation\n\nClinical features:\n\nArm usually abducted and externally rotated Humeral head often palpated anteriorly Patient cannot touch contralateral shoulder with hand of affected extremity.\n\nComplications of anterior shoulder dislocation:\n\nInjury to axillary nerve (Recall)\n\nInjury to axillary artery Injury to rotator cuff\n\nPosterior Shoulder dislocation: occurs occasionally results from direct blow to the arm that produces forceful internal rotation and adduction.\n\nClinical features:\n\nArm usually adducted and internally rotated Patient unable to perform external rotation\n\nComplications of posterior shoulder dislocation [ less common neurovascular injury]\n\nFracture of posterior glenoid labrum Fracture of humeral shaft or lesser tuberosity\n\nNote: Light bulb sign on the X-ray is the diagnostic feature of posterior shoulder dislocation\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nCarbonic anhydrase enzyme is present in which one of the following parts of nephron?\n\n- A. The distal convoluted tubules\n- **B. The proximal convoluted tubules** (Correct Answer)\n- C. The ascending loop of Henle\n- D. The collecting tubule and duct\n- E. The descending loop of Henle\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCarbonic anhydrase pre-dominant location is epithelial cells of the Proximal convoluted tubule Carbonic anhydrase catalyzes formation of Hco3-and H+ from H20 and Co2\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nType-III hypersensitivity is triggered by?\n\n- A. T-cells\n- B. Nk cells and IgG\n- C. Mast cells and IgE\n- D. IgM\n- **E. Deposition of antigen antibody complexes** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMechanism of hypersensitivity reactions:\n\nType I reactions (i.e., immediate hypersensitivity reactions) involve immunoglobulin E (IgE)-mediated release of histamine and other mediators from mast cells and basophils. (RECALL)\n\nType II reactions (i.e., cytotoxic hypersensitivity reactions) involve immunoglobulin G or immunoglobulin M antibodies bound to cell surface antigens, with subsequent complement fixation.\n\nType III reactions (i.e., Immune-complex reactions) involve circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes that deposit in post capillary venules, with subsequent complement fixation. An example is serum sickness.\n\nType IV reactions (i.e., delayed hypersensitivity reactions, cell-mediated immunity) are mediated by T cells rather than by antibodies (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 55-year-old female with history of fall brought into the ED with complain of Severe right hip pain. Xray reveals posterior hip dislocation, which one of the following ligaments is torn in posterior dislocation of hip?\n\n- **A. Ischiofemoral** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Pubofemoral\n- C. Iliofemoral\n- D. All the extracapsular ligaments\n- E. Ligament of head of femur\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIntracapsular\n\nThe only intracapsular ligament is the ligament of head of femur. It is a relatively small structure, which runs from the acetabular fossa to the fovea of the femur.\n\nExtracapsular ligaments:\n\nIliofemoral ligament: Strongest of all located anteriorly (Recall)\n\nIschiofemoral: Reinforcing the capsule posteriorly.\n\nPubofemoral: Reinforcing the capsule anteriorly and inferiorly\n\nNote: Most common hip dislocation is posterior a nd sciatic nerve is most commonly injured (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 72-year-old woman with a history of progressively worsening dysphagia is discovered to have an oesophageal tumor. The tumor is located on the anterior aspect of the thoracic oesophagus. Which of the following is an anterior relation of the thoracic oesophagus?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Anterior relation of oesophagus | Posterior relation of oesophagus |\n> | Trachea |  Thoracic vertebral bodies |\n> |  |  Thoracic duct |\n> |  | Azygous veins |\n> |  | Descending aorta |\n\n- A. Azygous veins\n- B. Right vagus nerve\n- **C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Subclavian artery\n- E. Lesser sac of peritoneum\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: Larynx: Innervation to the intrinsic muscles of the larynx is achieved via the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Injury of recurrent laryngeal nerve may result in hoarseness (Recall), respiratory obstruction and inability to speak\n\nsuperior laryngeal nerve provides both motor an d sensory function to different areas of the throat and mouth that are primarily involved in speech (Recall) and keeping food and drink out of your airway.\n\nThe superior laryngeal nerve (SLN) provides motor innervation to the cricothyroid muscle.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 40-year-old man presents to the emergency department intoxicated with an abdominal stab wound, situated just above the umbilicus in the midline. Small bowel has herniated through the anterior abdominal wall through the hole created by the knife\n\nThe most superficial structure damaged in stabbing?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Rectus abdominis muscle\n- B. Aponeurosis of internal oblique\n- C. Scarpa's fascia\n- **D. Camper's fascia** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Transversalis fascia\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCamper's fascia is the most superficial fatty layer of the anterior abdominal wall.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 16:** 1-D, 2-B, 3-C, 4-E, 5-B, 6-B, 7-D, 8-B, 9-E, 10-C, 11-B, 12-E, 13-A, 14-C, 15-D\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
250dbd4b-6f72-4266-b2e2-7730e8dd4d46	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-17	Quiz 17: Emergency Toxicology, Antibiotic Mechanisms & Cellular Respiration	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 17\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nWhich one of the following pathogens causes st ophthalmia neonatorum occurring in the 1 28 days of\n\nlife?\n\n- A. Gardnerella vaginalis\n- B. Treponema pallidum\n- **C. Chlamydia trachomatis** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- E. Trichomonas vaginalis\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOphthalmia neonatorum is conjunctivitis occurring within the first 4 weeks of life.\n\nThe most serious cause is infection with Neisseria gonorrhea in first week most likely in 3-4 days of life, but in the UK, it is caused more commonly by Chlamydia in first 28 days of life.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 40-year-old woman presents with a lump in the lateral upper right quadrant of her breast. You are suspicious this is breast cancer. Which lymph node would be most likely to be affected first?\n\n- A. Supraclavicular\n- B. Interpectoral\n- C. Parasternal\n- **D. Axillary** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Subclavian\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe lymphatic drainage of the breast is of great clinical importance due to its role in the metastasis of breast cancer cells.\n\nThere are three groups of lymph nodes that receive lymph from breast tissue\n\nAxillary nodes (75%): Receives lymphatic drainage from lateral and superior quadrant of breastparasternal nodes (20%): Receives lymphatic drainage from medial quadrant\n\nNote: Breast cancer in axillary tail of breast most commonly involved lymph node is axillary (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 25 years old woman from sub-Saharan Africa presents to the ED with complaint of fever and chills. Thick and thin films reveal malarial parasite. which organism would most likely be seen?\n\n- A. Plasmodium ovale\n- B. Plasmodium malariae\n- C. Plasmodium knowlesi\n- **D. Plasmodium falciparum** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Plasmodium vivax\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n1. falciparum is the deadliest malaria parasite and t he most prevalent on the African continent. P. vivax is the dominant malaria parasite in most countries outside of sub-Saharan Africa.\n\nNote: The Giemsa stain (Thick and thin film. Recall) is used as the gold standard for the diagnosis of malaria on blood smears.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\n17 years old female presented to the ED with complaint of fever since 3 days lethargy, stiff neck and non-blanching skin rash that appears on the lower limb.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n- **A. Meningococcal Septicemia** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Rheumatic fever\n- C. HSP\n- D. Endocarditis\n- E. Autoimmune TTP\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeisseria meningitidis is gram-negative encapsulated diplococci causing Neisseria meningitis and meningococcal sepsis\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\n22 years old young female came into ED with complain of sudden onset of lower limb weakness. She has history of Sore throat 10 days ago. On Examination her reflexes are absent in his lower limbs. What is the most likely causative organism?\n\n- A. Clostridium difficile\n- B. Staphylococcus aureus\n- C. Clostridium perfringens\n- **D. Campylobacter jejuni** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Salmonella\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGBS cases seem to occur after a viral or bacterial infection like those that cause sore throats or diarrhea characterized by lower limb weakness, paresthesia and hyporeflexia\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 42-year-old male with history of assault presents to the ED with complaint of incised wound on the neck. Which one of the following arteries is most likely injured?\n\n- **A. Internal carotid artery** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Facial artery\n- C. Brachial artery\n- D. Vertebral artery\n- E. Basillar artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeck injury may result in the laceration of major vessels, potentially leading to hemorrhagic shock. Internal carotid artery is most likely injured.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nBlood brain barrier is the barrier between cerebral capillary blood and the CSF consists of which one of the following cells?\n\n- A. Ependymal cells\n- B. Astrocytes\n- **C. Endothelial cells** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Plasma cells\n- E. Oligodendrocytes\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBlood brain barrier is the barrier between cerebral capillary blood and the CSE CSF fills the ventricles and the subarachnoid space.\n\nIt consists of the endothelial cells of the cerebral capillaries and the choroid plexus epithelium.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 25-year-old man attends the ED with abdominal pain. He has a history of Crohn's disease and has had a previous resection of his terminal ileum.\n\nWhich of the following is most likely to be deficient due to the absence of the terminal ileum?\n\n- **A. Vitamin D** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Dietary carbohydrate\n- C. Vitamin C\n- D. Dietary protein\n- E. Zinc\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMaximal water reabsorption occurs in Jejunum (Recall)\n\nMaximal Iron absorption occurs in duodenum (Recall)\n\nMaximal calcium absorption occurs in duodenum (Recall)\n\nMaximal vit B12 and vit D absorption occurs in terminal ileum\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 10 years old child brought into the ED with complaint of tiredness, fever and joint pain. His mother states that he had acute sore throat 10 days back. You suspect acute rheumatic fever secondary to untreated sore throat. Rheumatic fever is immunological sequalae of which of the following pathogens?\n\n- A. Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **B. Streptococcus pyogenes** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Staph viridians\n- D. Staphylococcus aureus\n- E. Staph epidermidis\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nStreptococcu Streptococcus Pyogenes is gra s Pyogenes m +Ve Cocci in chains\n\nBeta hemolytic group A Causes acute sore throat in children (Recall), skin and soft tissue infection and necrotizing fas ciitis Erysipelas: Superficial skin infection involving upper dermis and superficial lymphatics caused by Group A beta hemolytic streptococcus pyogenes (Recall)\n\nPost-infectious immune-mediated reactions of Streptococcus Pyogenes include Rheumatic fever, erythema nodosum, acute glomerulonephritis\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 40-year-old patient, known to be an IV drug user, presents to ED with 15 days history of fever, night sweats and malaise, and is noted to have a systolic murmur on examination. You suspect acute infective endocarditis, which of the following pathogens is the most likely cause?\n\n- A. Staph epidermidis\n- B. Staph viridans\n- C. Streptococcus pyogenes\n- D. Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **E. Staphylococcus aureus** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nMost common cause generally: Staph Aureus (Recall)\n\nMost common cause in IV drug abuser: Staph Aureus Most common cause of infective endocarditis inprosthetic heart valves: Staph epidermidis Most common cause of infective endocarditis after tooth extraction surgery: Staph viridians\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nAn 87-year-old woman from a residential home with a 2-day history of diarrhea. She has had 10 episodes of watery diarrhea but no vomiting. She complains of colicky lower abdominal pain. She had a 5-day course of antibiotics for a chest infection 2 weeks ago. Observations show a respiratory rate 18 bpm, Sp02 95% in air, heart rate 89 bpm,\n\nBP 146/89 mmHg, temp 36.8'C and BM 5.6 Examination reveals that her abdomen is soft but there is generalized tenderness especially across her lower abdomen. What is the most likely causative organism?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Clostridium | |Gram +Ve rod produces |\n> | Tetani | exotoxin tetanospasmin |\n> |  | (Recall) causes tetanus |\n> | Clostridium | |Gram +Ve rod Spore- |\n> | Difficile | forming, Produces Toxin A |\n> |  | (cytotoxin) causes |\n> |  | Pseudomembranous colitis |\n> | Clostridium | +Ve rod Spore- |\n> | Perfringens |forming, produces |  |\n> |  | exotoxin causes Gas |\n> |  | gangrene (Recall) |\n> | Clostridium |Gram +Ve rod Spore- |  |\n> | botulinum | produces |\n> |  | exotoxin botulinum. |\n> |  | Botulinum toxin, the most |\n> |  | potent of the neurotoxins, |\n> |  | produces paralysis by |\n> |  | blocking presynaptic release |\n> |  | of the neurotransmitter |\n> |  | (acetylcholine) at the |\n> |  | neuromuscular junction |\n> |  | (Recall) |\n\n- **A. Clostridium difficile** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Campylobacter\n- C. Norovirus\n- D. Rotavirus\n- E. E. coli\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBroad spectrum antibiotics disturb the normal g ut flora and cause overgrowth of clostridium difficile spores and causing Pseudomembranous colitis Antibiotics causing clostridium difficile colitis: Co -Amoxiclav, Cephalosporins, Clindamycin and quinolones\n\nTreatment of choice of clostridium difficile colitis is Vancomycin (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 33-year-old woman presents with severe headache and double vision. She has had severe facial pain and purulent nasal discharge for the past week. CT shows cavernous sinus thrombosis. Which one of the following veins is source of infection of cavernous sinus?\n\n- A. Central vein of retina\n- B. Azygous vein\n- C. Middle cerebral vein\n- D. Facial vein\n- **E. Ophthalmic vein** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCavernous sinus thrombosis (CST) refers to the formation of a clot within the cavernous sinus. It i s important to note that the superior ophthalmic vein forms an anastomosis with the facial vein. Therefore, the ophthalmic veins represent a potential route by which infection can spread from anextracranial to an intracranial site.\n\nNote: The abducens nerve (CN VI) is most commonly affected in cavernous sinus thrombosis\n\nStructures passing through cavernous sinus\n\nuseful mnemonic: O TOM CAT\n\nOTOM (oculomotor nerve, trochlear nerve, ophthalmic branch, maxillary branch) refers to the lateral wall contents from superior to inferior\n\nCAT (internal carotid artery, abducens nerve, trochlear nerve) refers to the horizontal contents, from medial to lateral.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 48-year-old man is found to have a right lower limb weakness and sensory loss. He also has apraxia. You suspect a stroke.\n\nOcclusion of which artery is most likely to cause these symptoms?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Blood vessel | Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness in lower limb, |\n> |  | dysarthria, dysphasia, apraxia and urinary |\n> | Anterior cerebral | incontinence |\n> | artery | . |\n> |  |  Parietal lobe: contralateral somatosensory loss in |\n> |  | the lower limb |\n> |  | Occipital lobe: contralateral homonymous |\n> |  | hemianopia with macular sparing, cortical |\n> | ; | blindness (if bilateral) |\n> | Posterior cerebral |  |\n> | artery |  Temporal lobe: memory deficit |\n> |  | Occipitotemporal region: prosopagnosia, and |\n> |  | color blindness |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness (face/arm |\n> |  | greater than leg), conjugate deviation of the eyes |\n> |  | to affected side, expressive dysphasia. |\n> | Middle cerebral |  |\n> |  | Temporal lobe: deafness (if bilateral), receptive |\n> | artery | dysphasia, auditory illusions and hallucinations, |\n> |  | contralateral superior quadrantanopia. |\n> |  |  Parietal lobe: loss of sensory discrimination, |\n> |  | hemineglect, apraxia, contralateral inferior |\n> |  | quadrantanopia (RECALL) |\n\n- A. Posterior communicating artery\n- **B. Anterior cerebral artery** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Internal carotid artery\n- D. Middle cerebral artery\n- E. Basilar artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield: Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries\n\nNote: Pontine infarct /Stroke: Results in ipsilateral facial and contralateral limb weakness (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 34-year-old man presents to the ED complaining of a green urethral discharge and dysuria. He reports unprotected sexual intercourse with a new partner 3 days ago. What is the most likely causative organism?\n\n- A. Treponema pallidum\n- B. Chlamydia trachomatis\n- **C. Neisseria gonorrhoeae** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Trichomonas vaginalis\n- E. Gardnerella vaginalis\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGram -Ve diplococci transmitted through Sexual and peripartum causing Urethritis, epididymo-orchitis, PID, septic arthritis, neonatal conjunctivitis (ophthalmia neonatorum)\n\nNote: Urethritis in 1st week (3-4 d Neisseria go ays) post exposure and unprotecte norrhoeae d sex while Chlamydia trachomatis causes urethritis after 2-3 week s post exposure and unprotected s ex\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 45-Year-old male suffered penetrating trauma to the parotid gland with facial nerve injury presents with complaint of inability to purse lips. Which of the following terminal branch of facial nerve is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Facial nerve branches in temporal bone | Facial nerve terminal branches in the parotid |  |\n> |  |  | gland |\n> | * Chorda tympani: taste from Ant 2/3 of | * Temporal branch: innervate muscles in |  |\n> | tongue (RECALL) | the temple, forehead (Frontalis muscle) |  |\n> | * While Ant 2/3 of general sensation of | and supraorbital areas (upper half of |  |\n> | tongue: mandibular division of trigeminal | orbicularis oculi muscle). |  |\n> | nerve | * | muscle: |\n> |  | Frontal belly: Elevates eyebrows, wrinkles |  |\n> |  | skin of forehead innervated by temporal |  |\n> |  | branch |  |\n> |  | Occipital belly: Retracts scalp innervated |  |\n> |  | by posterior auricular nerve |  |\n> |  | Zygomatic branch: innervate muscles in |  |\n> | * Greater petrosal nerve: Parasympathetic | the infraorbital area (Lower half of |  |\n> | innervation to lacrimal and mucous glands | orbicularis oculi muscle) Recall lateral |  |\n> |  | nasal area and upper lip |  |\n> |  | * Buccal branch: muscles in the cheek and |  |\n> |  | upper lip (Recall) |  |\n> |  | Pursing of the lips |  |\n> |  | * | innervate |\n> |  | muscles of lower lip and chin. |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Zygomatic branch\n- B. Marginal mandibular branch\n- C. Temporal branch\n- **D. Buccal branch** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Greater petrosal nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
788ff77e-c97f-4df3-a4dc-8ae911abbebf	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	brachial-plexus-nerves	Brachial plexus and nerves of the upper limb	article	## Brachial plexus: the building blocks\n\nThe plexus is built in five steps: roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches. Mnemonic: Randy Travis Drinks Cold Beer.\n\n- **Roots:** anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1.\n- **Trunks:** upper (C5-C6), middle (C7), lower (C8-T1), formed at the base of the neck.\n- **Divisions:** each trunk splits into an anterior and a posterior division behind the clavicle.\n- **Cords:** named by their position around the axillary artery. Lateral cord = anterior divisions of upper and middle trunks. Medial cord = anterior division of lower trunk. Posterior cord = posterior divisions of all three trunks.\n\n## Branches by cord\n\n- **Lateral cord (LML):** Lateral pectoral, Musculocutaneous, Lateral root of median.\n- **Medial cord (M4U):** Medial pectoral, Medial cutaneous nerve of arm, Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm, Medial root of median, Ulnar.\n- **Posterior cord (ULTRA):** Upper subscapular, Lower subscapular, Thoracodorsal, Radial, Axillary.\n\n## Plexus injuries\n\n- **Upper trunk (C5-C6), Erb-Duchenne palsy:** the arm hangs adducted and medially rotated, the forearm is pronated, the wrist is flexed ("waiter's tip"). Typical causes are traction injuries such as shoulder dystocia or a fall on the shoulder.\n- **Lower trunk (C8-T1), Klumpke palsy:** weak small muscles of the hand and a claw hand. Horner syndrome can accompany it if T1 sympathetic fibres are involved. Think hyperabduction injury or a Pancoast tumour.\n\n## Axillary nerve (C5-C6, posterior cord)\n\n- Motor: deltoid and teres minor.\n- Sensory: skin over the lower deltoid (the "regimental badge" area).\n- Course: leaves the axilla through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery and wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus.\n- Injured by: fracture of the surgical neck or anterior shoulder dislocation.\n- Findings: weak abduction beyond the first 15 degrees (supraspinatus still starts abduction), flattened shoulder contour, loss of sensation over the badge area.\n\n## Median nerve (C5-T1, medial and lateral cords)\n\n- **Forearm motor:** all flexors and pronators except flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (both ulnar).\n- **Anterior interosseous branch:** flexor pollicis longus, lateral half of FDP, pronator quadratus.\n- **Hand motor (LOAF):** Lateral two Lumbricals, Opponens pollicis, Abductor pollicis brevis, Flexor pollicis brevis.\n- **Sensory:** palm and palmar surface of the lateral three and a half digits. The palmar cutaneous branch leaves before the carpal tunnel.\n- **Carpal tunnel syndrome:** numbness, tingling and pain in the median distribution, thenar wasting later on. The palm is usually spared because the palmar cutaneous branch does not pass through the tunnel.\n\n**Lesion at the elbow** (for example supracondylar fracture): weak pronation and wrist flexion, loss of thumb flexion and of index and middle finger flexion. On trying to make a fist only the ring and little fingers curl: the "hand of benediction". Ring and little finger flexion survives because the medial FDP and medial lumbricals are ulnar.\n\n**Lesion at the wrist** (for example a laceration): paralysis of the thenar muscles and lateral two lumbricals, thenar wasting, weak thumb opposition. Forearm muscles are spared.\n\n## Radial nerve (C5-T1, posterior cord)\n\n- **Motor:** triceps, anconeus, brachioradialis and all extensors of the forearm, plus supinator.\n- **Course:** behind the axillary artery, then through the triceps in the radial groove with the profunda brachii artery, pierces the lateral intermuscular septum, passes between brachialis and brachioradialis into the cubital fossa, and divides into a superficial (sensory) and a deep (motor, continues as posterior interosseous nerve) branch.\n- **Sensory:** back of the arm and forearm, and the back of the lateral three and a half digits (excluding the fingertips).\n- **Wrist drop** is the classic sign of injury.\n\nWhere it is injured matters:\n- **Axilla or proximal humerus** (crutches, "Saturday night palsy"): triceps is weak as well as wrist drop.\n- **Mid-shaft humeral fracture:** triceps is spared, wrist drop and sensory loss over the back of the hand.\n- **Posterior interosseous nerve** (radial head fracture or dislocation): finger drop but wrist extension survives because ECRL is spared, and there is no sensory loss.\n\n## Ulnar nerve (C8-T1, medial cord)\n\n- **Forearm motor:** flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of FDP.\n- **Hand motor:** hypothenar muscles, medial two lumbricals, all interossei, adductor pollicis, palmaris brevis.\n- **Sensory:** medial one and a half digits, the hypothenar area and the medial back of the hand.\n- **Course:** down the medial arm, pierces the medial intermuscular septum, passes behind the medial epicondyle (cubital tunnel, "funny bone"), between the two heads of FCU, then enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum and lateral to the pisiform (Guyon canal).\n- **Claw hand:** ring and little fingers hyperextended at the MCP joints and flexed at the IP joints. Paradox: a lesion at the wrist gives a more obvious claw than a lesion at the elbow, because at the elbow the medial FDP is also paralysed.\n- **Froment sign:** the patient holds paper between thumb and index finger. When it is pulled away, the thumb IP joint flexes (flexor pollicis longus, median nerve) to compensate for the paralysed adductor pollicis.\n\n## Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7, lateral cord)\n\n- Pierces coracobrachialis and runs between biceps and brachialis.\n- Motor: coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, brachialis (the anterior compartment of the arm).\n- Sensory: lateral forearm, through the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm.\n- Injury: weak elbow flexion and supination, absent biceps reflex, sensory loss over the lateral forearm. Brachioradialis (radial nerve) can still flex the elbow.\n\n## Autonomous sensory zones (quick exam recall)\n\n- Median: tip of the index finger.\n- Ulnar: tip of the little finger.\n- Radial: the skin over the first dorsal web space.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
3297d1cd-c232-4bb3-bed8-104dc01b7bdd	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	muscles-upper-limb	Muscles of the upper limb	article	## Anterior forearm (flexors and pronators)\n\nAll arise mainly from the common flexor origin on the medial epicondyle. Nerve supply: **median nerve, except flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (ulnar)**.\n\n- **Superficial layer:** pronator teres (pronation), flexor carpi radialis (wrist flexion and abduction), palmaris longus (wrist flexion, tenses palmar aponeurosis), flexor carpi ulnaris (wrist flexion and adduction, ulnar nerve).\n- **Intermediate layer:** flexor digitorum superficialis, flexes the PIP joints of the four fingers (inserts on the middle phalanges).\n- **Deep layer:** flexor digitorum profundus (flexes the DIP joints, inserts on the distal phalanges, dual supply), flexor pollicis longus (flexes the thumb IP joint), pronator quadratus (pronation).\n\n## Posterior forearm (extensors and supinator)\n\nAll supplied by the **radial nerve** (the deep ones by its posterior interosseous branch).\n\n- **Superficial:** brachioradialis (flexes the elbow, best in mid-prone position), extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis (extend and abduct the wrist), extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris (extends and adducts the wrist), anconeus (assists triceps).\n- **Deep:** supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis.\n\n## Arm\n\n- **Anterior compartment, musculocutaneous nerve:** biceps brachii (flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm), brachialis (main elbow flexor), coracobrachialis (flexes and adducts the arm).\n- **Posterior compartment, radial nerve:** triceps brachii (extends the elbow, long head also extends the shoulder).\n\n## Hand\n\n- **Thenar muscles (median nerve, recurrent branch):** abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis. Adductor pollicis is supplied by the ulnar nerve.\n- **Hypothenar muscles (ulnar nerve):** abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, opponens digiti minimi. They form the bulge on the ulnar side of the palm, at the base of the little finger.\n- **Lumbricals:** arise from the FDP tendons, flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints. Lateral two = median nerve, medial two = ulnar nerve.\n- **Interossei (all ulnar nerve):** Dorsal interossei ABduct the fingers, Palmar interossei ADduct them (DAB, PAD). Both flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints.\n\n## Shoulder region\n\n**Extrinsic muscles** (link the limb to the trunk):\n- Trapezius: spinal accessory nerve (CN XI). Elevates and rotates the scapula.\n- Latissimus dorsi: thoracodorsal nerve (C6-C8). Extends, adducts and medially rotates the arm.\n- Levator scapulae: dorsal scapular nerve and C3-C4.\n- Rhomboid major and minor: dorsal scapular nerve (C4-C5). Retract the scapula.\n\n**Intrinsic muscles** (link the scapula to the humerus):\n- Deltoid: axillary nerve (C5-C6). Abducts the arm (anterior fibres flex, posterior fibres extend).\n- Teres major: lower subscapular nerve. Medially rotates and adducts.\n- **Rotator cuff (SITS):** Supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve, starts abduction in the first 15 degrees), Infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve, lateral rotation), Teres minor (axillary nerve, lateral rotation), Subscapularis (upper and lower subscapular nerves, medial rotation). Every cuff muscle is a lateral rotator except subscapularis.\n\n**Rotator cuff tests:**\n- Supraspinatus: empty can test (arm elevated 90 degrees in the scapular plane, thumbs down, resist downward pressure).\n- Infraspinatus: arm by the side, elbow flexed 90 degrees, resist internal rotation. Pain or weakness is positive.\n- Teres minor: Hornblower sign (cannot externally rotate with the arm abducted and the elbow flexed).\n- Subscapularis: lift-off test (hand on the lower back, cannot lift it away against resistance).\n\nSupraspinatus is the most commonly injured cuff tendon.\n\n## Pectoral region\n\n- **Pectoralis major:** adducts and medially rotates the arm. Lateral and medial pectoral nerves.\n- **Pectoralis minor:** depresses and protracts the scapula (ribs 3-5 to the coracoid). Medial pectoral nerve.\n- **Serratus anterior:** holds the scapula against the rib cage and rotates it for overhead abduction. **Long thoracic nerve.**\n- **Subclavius:** anchors and depresses the clavicle. Nerve to subclavius.\n\n## Clinical pearls\n\n- **Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis):** overuse of the extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle.\n- **Golfer's elbow (medial epicondylitis):** overuse of the flexor origin on the medial epicondyle.\n- **Nursemaid's elbow:** subluxation of the radial head under the annular ligament when a toddler is pulled by the hand or wrist.\n- **Winged scapula:** serratus anterior paralysis from long thoracic nerve injury (for example during axillary surgery).\n- **Cannot shrug the shoulder:** trapezius paralysis, spinal accessory nerve injury.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
a3dfd924-34dd-40c2-a8dd-a237dac179d3	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	venous-drainage-of-upper-limb	Venous Drainage of Upper Limb	article	Venous Drainage of the Upper Limb\nDeep and superficial venous arches: Are formed by a pair of\nvenae comitantes, which accompany each of the deep and\nsuperficial palmar arterial arches.\nThe superficial veins of the upper limb lie in the superficial\nfascia and of great clinical importance.\nDorsal venous network lies on the dorsum of hand. It is\ndrained on the lateral side by cephalic vein and on the\nmedial side by the basilic vein.\nNote:\n\n### 1. The median cubital vein connects the cephalic vein to the\n\nbasilic vein. It lies superficial to bicipital aponeurosis, which\nseparates it from the brachial artery. The median cubital vein\nis a common site of venipuncture.\n\n### 2. Deep veins of the arm and forearm: Follow the course of the\n\narteries, accompanying them as their venae comitantes.\n\n### 3. The brachial veins are the vena comitantes of the brachial\n\nartery and are joined by the basilic vein to form the axillary\nvein.\nAxillary Vein:\nIs formed at the lower border of the teres major muscle by\nthe union of the brachial veins (vena comitantes of the brachial\nartery) and the basilic vein and ascends along the medial side\nof the axillary artery. Continues as the subclavian vein at the\ninferior margin of the first rib.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
2f4b417e-62d9-4bef-93d4-f5d2f603377a	9db23e52-af3e-4f01-9ef3-48e9c44fed87	status-epilepticus-management	Status Epilepticus Management	article	Status epilepticus management\n\n![Status Epilepticus Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/status-epilepticus-management/figure_01_p1.jpg)\n*Status Epilepticus Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
eacb3864-99e3-43a4-8a8d-d9ee1e2edf5d	9db23e52-af3e-4f01-9ef3-48e9c44fed87	tricyclic-antidepressants	Tricyclic - Antidepressants	article	Tri-cyclic antidepressants:\nMechanism of action: TCA e.g. Amitriptyline is in the tricyclic\nantidepressant (TCA) drug, block the reuptake of\nnorepinephrine and serotonin from the synaptic cleft, thereby\npotentiating the effects of serotonin and norepinephrine on\nthe postsynaptic receptors. The TCAs have also been shown to\ninhibit muscarinic, histamine, and α -adrenergic receptors,\nthereby leading to a host of undesirable side effects.\nTca also block muscarinic, histamine and  α  receptors and has\nnon-specific sedative effects.\nAnticholinergic effects/parasympathetic blocking actions are\ndue to muscarinic receptor blockade (leads to Tachycardia,\nurinary retention, blurred vision, acute angle closure\nglaucoma, confusion, constipation, and dry\nmouth\nSedation (due to histamine receptor blockade)\nPostural hypotension (due to α  adrenergic receptor\nblockade)\ncardiac arrhythmias (due to sodium channel blocker)\nIndications: Used in depression, neuropathic pain and\nnocturnal enuresis.\nClinical features of TCA Toxicity:\nTachycardia - Sinus tachycardia is the most common rhythm\nin TCA overdose. Wide complex tachycardia occurs with severe\nTCA overdose.\nHypotension - As a result of alpha-1 antagonism, hypotension\nis quite common.\nMydriasis,\nWarm, dry, flushed skin\nDecreased or absent bowel sounds\nUrinary retention\nOphthalmic signs - Divergent squint, internuclear\nophthalmoplegic and gaze paralysis; nystagmus may also be seen.\nNeurological signs - Ataxia, and myoclonic and\nchoreoathetoid movements, may be seen. Increased muscle\ntone, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses may occur.\nSevere metabolic acidosis on ABG findingsInvestigations\nECG - This should be done as soon as possible to assess for\npresence of QRS prolongation.\nAny QRS duration >100 milliseconds in the setting of overdose,\nor unknown cause of altered mental status, is suspicious for\nTCA overdose.\nThe most common ECG abnormality seen in TCA overdose\nis sinus tachycardia.\nOther ECG changes that can occur include:\nprolonged QTc duration\nprolonged PR interval\nAV block\nnon-specific ST and T wave changes\ndownward sloping ST elevation in leads V1 to V3 with right\nbundle branch block (Brugada wave).\nTreatment:\nFollow ABC protocol, obtain intravenous access, attach a\ncardiac monitor and obtain an ECG to look for QRS\nprolongation.\nGastrointestinal decontamination (Time limited) - consider in\npatients with ingestion within 1 hour .\nActivated charcoal: 1 g/kg orally as a single dose, or 50g in\nadults repeat every 1-2 hours if required.\nPt with QRS >100 milliseconds or pH <7.45 - Give sodium\nbicarbonate bolus followed by infusion.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
35c1c393-9d2a-4202-a9f0-263590a35f20	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-05	Quiz 05: Cardiovascular Physiology, Coronary Anatomy & Renal Mechanisms	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 05\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\n27 years old male presents to the ED with complain of dysphagia. Which of the following nerve is responsible for contracting the esophageal phase of swallowing?\n\n- A. Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- B. Auriculotemporal nerve\n- **C. Vagus nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Hypoglossal nerve\n- E. Facial nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPharyngeal phase of swallowing: glossopharyngeal nerve and vagus nerve Esophageal phase of swallowing: Vagus nerve\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nWhilst in your physiology lecture you ask medical students, what is the method to calculate mean arterial pressure?\n\n- **A. Diastolic pressure + 1/3 (pulse pressure)** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Diastolic pressure + Systolic pressure/2\n- C. Diastolic pressure + Systolic pressure\n- D. Systolic pressure + 1/3 (pulse pressure)\n- E. Diastolic pressure + pulse pressure\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPulse pressure is difference between the Systolic and Diastolic pressure e.g., 120-80=40\n\nMean arterial pressure: Diastolic pressure + 1/3 (pulse pressure) e.g., 80+ 1/3 (120-80) = 93.2\n\nCerebral perfusion pressure= MAP- intracranial pressure (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 30 years old male presents to the ED with lacerated wound on his posterolateral back. Which of the following muscle is most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  |  | W/Z |  | Levator scapulae |\n> | us |  |  |  | SS |  |\n> | Trapezius | ---- |  | = | Ze - Rhomboid minor |  |\n> |  |  | als |  |  | Rhomboid maj |\n> |  |  | ols |  | Va! | omboid major |\n> |  |  |  | BAA |  |  |\n> |  |  |  |  | ZED % |  |\n> | Latissimus dorsi |  |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | \\ | AY: 4 IX |  |  |\n> |  | Lumbar triangle |  |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Rhomboid minor\n- **B. Latissimus dorsi** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Sternocleidomastoid\n- D. Trapezius\n- E. Levator Scapulae\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLatissimus dorsi is the superficial muscle of the posterolateral back innervated by Thoracodorsal Nerve (RECALL)\n\nAsk the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if patient unable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve injury and paralysis of trapezius muscle (RECALL) Winging of scapula is due to paralysis of serratus anterior muscle innervated by Long thoracic nerve (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA cardiac patient presents to the Emergency department with an acutely painful, red, hot, swollen big toe. He has past medical history of gout and severe heart failure. Gout is diagnosed. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this patient?\n\n- A. Allopurinol\n- **B. Colchicine** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Prednisolone\n- D. Paracetamol\n- E. NSAID\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nColchicine mechanism of action: Colchicine has anti-inflammatory properties. It disrupts cytoskeletal functions by inhibiting beta-tubulin polymerization into microtubules (Recall), preventing activation, degranulation, and migration of neutrophils associated with mediating gout symptoms.\n\nColchicine is useful in patients with heart failure and those taking anticoagulants Avoid colchicine in patients with blood dyscrasias and bone marrow disease NSAIDs are contraindicated in a patient with heart failure. (Diclofenac is contraindicated in people with ischemic heart disease).\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nYou are discussing ECG waveform with group of medical students. You ask ur students, What does the second heart sound correspond to on the ECG waveform:\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | : | Correspondence |\n> |  | Mechanical event | with ECG |\n> | Ist heart sound | Closure of mitral and tricuspid |  QRS Complex |\n> |  | valves |  |\n> |  | Closure of aortic and pulmonary | Twave |\n> |  | valves |  |\n> |  |  Also known as ventricular gallop |  |\n> |  | Caused by rapid flow of blood from |  |\n> | 3rd heart sound | the atria into the ventricles during | Beginning of P |\n> |  | the ventricular filling phase | wave |\n> |  | Can normally be present in |  |\n> |  | children |  |\n> |  Ath heart sound |  degrees | Between P and Q |\n> |  | stiff ventricle in atrial systole |  |\n\n- A. P wave\n- B. ST segment\n- C. PR interval\n- **D. T wave** (Correct Answer)\n- E. QRS complex\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHeart sounds, Mechanical event andcorrespondence with ECG (High YIELD)\n\nRemember the table on your fingertips many recallsfrom this table\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nwhich of the following best describes the function of mitochondria is?\n\n- A. Protein packaging\n- B. Lipid synthesis\n- **C. Energy production** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Carbohydrate synthesis\n- E. Protein synthesis\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIt acts as the site of aerobic respiration and produces cellular energy in the form of ATP (powerHouse of the cell).\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nVibrio cholera causes watery diarrhea due to which of following final pathways?\n\n- A. Decrease Camp\n- B. Increased hco3 secretory channels\n- C. Decreased cl- ion secretion\n- **D. Increase cl- ion secretion** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Increase proton secretory channels\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCholera toxin activates adenylate cyclase and increases cyclic adenosine monophosphate (CAMP). CAMP activates the Cl-secretory channels and produces a primary secretion of Cl- with Na+ and H20.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 52-year-old male presents to ED complaining of dizziness and discomfort. After initial assessment at triage his heart rate is found to be 40-60 bpm regular rhythm. ECG demonstrates a normal QRS complex with absent P waves. Which part of the conducting system is most likely acting as pacemaker in this patient?\n\n- **A. Atrioventricular node** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Purkinje fibers\n- C. Bundle of His\n- D. Bundle branches\n- E. Sinoatrial node\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nConductive Pathway of Heart:\n\nSA node initiates the cardiac muscle (pacemaker) -> Internodal fibers and AV node (delay impulse from the atria to ventricle) -> AV bundle branches -> Purkinje fibers (from apex to ventricles).\n\nThe sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker located in Right atrium. The SAN will normally discharge at a rate of 60-100 bpm. If the impulse generated in the Sa node is blocked, a group of cells further down the heart will become its pacemaker. The atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary pacemaker located between right atrium and ventricle near the atrial septum, and will normally discharge at about 40-60 beats per minute Purkinje fibers produce spontaneous action potential at rate of 20-40 bpm.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nADH acts primarily at which of the following sites in renal nephron?\n\n- **A. Distal convoluted tubule** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Proximal convoluted tubule\n- C. Descending limb of loop of Henle\n- D. Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- E. Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nADH binds to V2 receptors (Recall) and increases the number of aquaporin-2 at the apical plasma membrane of Distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct and increases water permeability allowing greater water reabsorption.\n\nDiabetes insipidus\n\nCentral DI: Low ADH (Recall) less water absorbed Nephrogenic DI: ADH resistance, Lithium is the most common acquired cause of nephrogenic DI. (Recall)\n\nBiochemical values include high plasma osmolality >295 mOsm/kg, low urine osmolality <300 mOsm/kg (Recall) and hypernatremia > 145.\n\nNote:\n\nSyndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion: increased ADH activity (Recall) (Euvolemic hypo osmolar hyponatremia) Recall Biochemical values include low plasma osmolality < 275 mOsm/kg, increased urine osmolality > 100 mOsm/kg and increased urine sodium or decreased plasma sodium (Recall)\n\nDrugs causing S.I.A.D.H= S.S.R.I (Fluoxetine Recall), antipsychotics, anticonvulsants and T.C.A\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 35-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department with a laceration on his left forearm after motor vehicle collision. Following injury, which one of the following is abundant on the sixth day of wound healing?\n\n- A. Neutrophils\n- B. Remodeling\n- C. Inflammation\n- **D. Proliferation** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Haemostasis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn the event of a tissue injury, the body initiates a cascade of overlapping cellular and extracellular activities that immediately start post-injury and lasts several months. This process comprises four distinct yet overlapping phases.\n\n1: Haemostasis: Starts immediately following injury. 2: Inflammation: Activation of the complement cascade within 1-2 days of the injury.\n\nWound infiltration by neutrophilis Macrophages activation in 48-72hrs and act as the regulatory cells for repair by producing growth factors responsible for the proliferation.\n\n3: Proliferation: Starts about day three and last 2-4 weeks.\n\n4: Remodeling and scar maturation: May continue for several months.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 40 years old male presents to the ED with chest pain and the following ECG is taken. Which artery is most likely be occluded? \n\n- A. Left anterior descending\n- **B. Right coronary artery** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Obtuse marginal artery\n- D. left main coronary artery\n- E. Left circumflex\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nECG demonstrates Acute Inferior Myocardial Infarction due to occlusion of RCA\n\nSt-elevation in inferior leads (II, III,aVF) with reciprocal ST-depression in I, aVL\n\nNote: Both inferior wall MI and posterior wall MI occurs due to occlusion of Right coronary artery.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 60-year-old man takes an overdose of his regular medications. Bedside testing reveals hypoglycemia.\n\nWhich drug is the most likely cause?\n\n- A. Citalopram\n- B. Metformin\n- C. Bisoprolol\n- D. Prednisolone\n- **E. Glibenclamide** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGlibenclamide (also known as glyburide) is an oral antidiabetic medication belonging to the class of sulfonylureas, which stimulate insulin secretion from the pancreas. Overdose or excessive use of glibenclamide can lead to significant hypoglycemia, especially in older adults or those with compromised liver function.\n\nThe other options are less likely to cause hypoglycemia:\n\nBisoprolol: A beta-blocker that is primarily used for hypertension and heart failure, but it does not cause hypoglycemia.\n\nCitalopram: An antidepressant that does not typically affect blood glucose levels.\n\nMetformin: Generally, does not cause hypoglycemia by itself; it primarily reduces hepatic glucose production.\n\nPrednisolone: A corticosteroid that can increase blood glucose levels.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 42-year-old man has abdominal pain and hemorrhagic shock. He has chronic pancreatitis with a known pseudoaneurysm of the main artery supplying the pancreas. Bleeding from which artery is most likely to account for his presentation?\n\n- A. Marginal\n- **B. Splenic** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Left gastric\n- D. Common hepatic\n- E. Left gastroepiploic\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBlood supply: Receives blood from branches of the splenic artery The head is additionally supplied by the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries which are branches of the gastroduodenal from (coeliac trunk) and superior mesenteric arteries respectively. Innervation: Visceral afferent fibres travel in the greater and lesser splanchnic nerves.\n\nThe segmental level of innervation is T6 -T10. Pain typically radiates to the middle of the back due to irritation of the parietal peritoneum covering the pancreas.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 52-year-old man is unwell with crushing central chest pain. An ECG shows significant ST segment changes. Angiography shows occlusion of the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery.\n\nThis condition most commonly results in ischemia in which area of the heart?\n\n- A. Inferior part of the right ventricle\n- **B. Posterior part of the left ventricle** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Inferior part of the left ventricle\n- D. Anterior part of the right ventricle\n- E. Anterior part of the left ventricle\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLCA divides into the anterior interventricular branch and the circumflex branch. The circumflex branch follows the coronary sulcus to the left border and onto the posterior surface of the heart and supply left atrium and left ventricle\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nWhich of the following NSAIDs has the least gastrointestinal side effects?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Piroxicam\n- B. Naproxen\n- C. Indomethacin\n- **D. Ibuprofen** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Diclofenac\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNSAIDS-induced GI side effects:\n\nLowest risk=ibuprofen Highest risk=piroxicam, ketorolac NSAIDs cause GERD by inhibiting the COX 1 enzymes (cox 1 stimulation produce prostaglandins th at help to maintain the gastric mucosal integrity) NSAIDs can cause ulcers by interfering with the stomach's ability to protect itself from gastric acid (by inhibiting the function of prostaglandins) RECALL NSAID causes hemolytic anemia=Mefenamic acid (RECALL).\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 5:** 1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-B, 5-D, 6-C, 7-D, 8-A, 9-A, 10-D, 11-B, 12-E, 13-B, 14-B, 15-D\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
506a7590-f98e-48d8-9ab7-429c7854e18c	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-06	Quiz 06: Endocrine Emergencies, Cardiac Cycle & Toxicological Crises	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 06\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 55-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of fatigue, weight gain, and feeling cold all the time for the past 6 months. He has no significant family history of cardiovascular disease or hyperlipidemia. Physical examination reveals dry, coarse skin and slow reflexes. Lab Results:\n\nTSH: 15 mU/L (Normal range: 0.4-4.0 mU/L)\n\nFree T4: 0.5 ng/dL (Normal range: 0.9-1.7 ng/dL)\n\nTotal Cholesterol: 260 mg/dL (Normal: <200 mg/dL)\n\nLDL: 180 mg/dL (Normal: <100 mg/dL)\n\nHDL: 35 mg/dL (Normal: >50 mg/dL) What is the most likely cause of this patient's hypercholesterolemia?\n\n- A. Diet-induced hypercholesterolemia\n- B. polycystic ovarian syndrome-related hyperlipidemia\n- C. Nephrotic syndrome-associated dyslipidemia\n- **D. Hypothyroidism-induced secondary hypercholesterolemia** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Primary hypercholesterolemia due to familial hyperlipidemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe patient's elevated TSH and low free T4 levels are consistent with primary hypothyroidism.\n\nHypothyroidism slows down metabolism, including lipid metabolism, leading to reduced clearance of LDL cholesterol from the blood. This results in secondary hypercholesterolemia, which is typically reversible with thyroid hormone replacement therapy.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 55-year-old woman presents with weakness in her right shoulder two days post right mastectomy with axillary clearance. She has limited extension, adduction and internal rotation of the shoulder joint. Which nerve is most likely to have been damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Spinal part of |  |  |  |  |\n> |  | accessory | XI cranial | Upper fibers elevate the scapula; middle fibers |  |  |\n> | Trapezius | nerve (motor) | nerve (spinal | pull scapula medially and lower fibers pull medial |  |  |\n> |  | and C3 and 4 | part) |  | border of scapula downward |  |\n> |  | (sensory) |  |  |  |  |\n> | Latissimus | Thoracodorsal |  |  | Extends, adducts, and |  |\n> |  |  | C6, 7, 8, |  | medially rotates the |  |\n> | dorsi | nerve |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  |  |  |  | arm |\n> |  | C3 C4 and |  |  | . | . |\n> | Levator | dorsal |  |  | Raises medial border of |  |\n> | scapulae |  |  |  |  | scapula |\n> |  | scapular nerve |  |  |  |  |\n> | Rhomboid | Dorsal scapular |  |  | Raises medial border of |  |\n> | minor | nerve |  |  | scapula upward and medially |  |\n> | Rhomboid | Dorsal scapular | C4, C5 |  | Raises medial border of |  |\n> |  | nerve |  |  | scapula upward and medially |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | 1 | Levator scapulae |\n> | Trapezius |  | Rhomboid minor |\n> |  |  | yg Rhomboid major |\n> | Latissimus dorsi |  |  |\n> |  | she |  |\n> |  | fi ij A |  |\n\n- **A. Thoracodorsal** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Suprascapular\n- C. Axillary\n- D. Long thoracic\n- E. Lateral pectoral nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMuscles of the Shoulder region:\n\nExtrinsic muscles, Intrinsic Muscles\n\nExtrinsic muscles: Connecting the Upper Limb to the Vertebral Column and divided into superficial and deep group.\n\nSuperficial: Trapezius and Latissimus dorsi\n\nDeep: Levator Scapulae, Rhomboid minor and Rhomboid major\n\nClinical pearl: Ask the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if patient unable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve injury and paralysis of trapezius muscle.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 20-week pregnant lady presents to the ED with exposure to chicken pox. What is your next step in management?\n\n- A. Immediate reinforcing of vaccine\n- B. No action required\n- C. Immediate reinforcing of immunoglobulin\n- D. Attend to antenatal clinic\n- **E. Check her antibody status** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nKey Points:\n\nIf you have already had chickenpox, you have nothing to worry about.\n\nOnce you have had chickenpox you cannot catch it a second time, this is known as being immune. Nine out of ten UK adults are immune to chickenpox.\n\nChickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in the UK.\n\nIt affects very few babies in the womb and although it can be very serious in pregnancy st especially in 1 trimester in first 12 weeks (Recall) If you have been in contact with chickenpox and are not sure if you are immune, contact your GP or midwife and not to attend the antenatal clinic. If you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other pregnant women and new babies until at least five days after the rash appears, or until all the blisters have crusted over.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nYou are reviewing a patient with infected arteriovenous fistula and he will undergo hemofiltration tomorrow. Which of the following best describes the Transport of molecules across the membrane that occurs in haemofiltration?\n\n- A. Osmosis\n- B. Simple diffusion\n- C. Active transport\n- D. Facilitated diffusion\n- **E. Ultrafiltration** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nUltrafiltration refers to the process of movement of fluids through a membrane caused by a pressure gradient In dialysis, fluid is removed by ultrafiltration using the dialysis membrane. The pressure on the dialysate side is lower so water moves from the blood (place of higher pressure) to the dialysate (place of lower pressure). This is how the hemodialysis treatment removes fluid.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nYou are going to perform full cardiovascular examination in\n\na patient with known cardiac disease. Your consultant asks th how does the 4 heart is produced?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | 3' heart sound | Also Known as |\n> |  | ventricular gallop |\n> |  | caused by rapid flow |\n> |  | of blood from the |\n> |  | atria into the |\n> |  | ventricles during the |\n> |  | ventricular filling |\n> | 4th heart sound | The fourth heart |\n> |  | sound, S4, also |\n> |  | known as 'atrial |\n> |  | gallop' results from |\n> |  | the contraction of the |\n> |  | atria pushing blood |\n> |  | into a stiff or |\n> |  | hypertrophic |\n> |  | ventricle, indicating |\n> |  | failure of the left |\n> |  | ventricle |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | contraction |\n> | c wave | C: Contraction: |\n> |  | lsovolumic |\n> |  | contraction |\n> |  | ventricular ejection |\n> | Vv wave | V: Ventricular |\n> |  | ejection followed by |\n> |  | isovolumetric |\n> |  | relaxation |\n\n- A. Caused by closure of the semilunar (aortic and pulmonary) valves\n- B. Caused by closure of the atrioventricular (mitral and tricuspid) valves\n- C. Caused by rapid flow of blood from the atria into the ventricles during the ventricular filling\n- D. Caused by filling of an abnormally stiff ventricle in atrial diastole\n- **E. Caused by filling of an abnormally stiff ventricle in atrial systole** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: Dicrotic notch on the arterial waveform represent closure of aortic valve (Recall) used to demarcate the end of systole and the beginning of diastole.\n\nHigh Yield\n\nThe isovolumic relaxation phase starts when the aortic valve closes (Recall) and ends when the mitral valve opens. During this phase, the left ventricular pressure drops until it becomes lower than that of the left atrium. This allows opening of the atrioventricular valve and the filling of the ventricle.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nAn 87-year-old woman from a residential home with a 2-day history of diarrhea. She has had 10 episodes of watery diarrhea but no vomiting. She complains of colicky lower abdominal pain. She had a 5-day course of antibiotics for a chest infection 2 weeks ago. After initial assessment diagnosis of clostridium colitis was made. Which of the following is the best way to prevent spread by C.difficle to others?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> Clostridium difficile Gram +Ve anerobic\n> bacterium transmitted\n> by fecal-oral route\n> e Alcohol gel is not\n> effective against\n> spores of\n> Clostridium difficile\n> e Use soap and\n> water\n\n- **A. Wash hand with soap and water** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Antibiotic treatment\n- C. Wash hand with alcohol gel\n- D. Wash hand with sanitizer\n- E. Wash hand with sterile water\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 22 years old girl presents to the ED with complaint of amenorrhea. Excess of which of the following Harmone caused this condition?\n\n- A. TSH\n- B. Growth Harmone\n- **C. Prolactin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Oxytocin\n- E. FSH\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHyperprolactinemia causesamenorrhea/oligomenorrhea (Recall), loss of libido and erectile dysfunction\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 45 years old male presents to the ED with history of severe vomiting for the last 4 days.. The patient is irritable, weak, and reporting muscle cramping and weakness. On assessment, the patient is experiencing bradypnea with a respiratory rate of 10. The patient has the following ABGs result: HCO3 36, pH 7.52, PaCO2 48. Which condition below is presenting?\n\n- A. Metabolic alkalosis fully compensated\n- B. Respiratory alkalosis fully compensated\n- C. Metabolic acidosis not compensated\n- D. Metabolic acidosis partially compensated\n- **E. Metabolic alkalosis partially compensated** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIf the primary acid-base disorder is from a respiratory cause, the kidneys will compensate (renal compensation).\n\nIf the primary disorder is from a metabolic cause, the lungs will compensate (respiratory compensation).\n\nMetabolic acidosis= Respiratory compensation (decrease in PaCO2)\n\nMetabolic alkalosis= Respiratory compensation (increase in PaCO2)\n\nRespiratory acidosis= Renal compensation (increase in HCO3-)\n\nRespiratory alkalosis= Renal compensation (decrease in HCO3-)\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 40-year-old male patient attends ED with palpitations. ECG shows a now complex tachycardia, and you decide to administer adenosine intravenously.What is the most likely mode of action of this drug on the heart?\n\n- A. Stimulate vagal activity\n- **B. Hyperpolarizes the cell membrane in the AV node** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Inhibit atrial contraction\n- D. Strongly depressed conduction in the myocardium\n- E. Anticholinergic effect\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAdenosine is commonly used to treat paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT). Its primary mechanism of action is to hyperpolarize the cell membrane, particularly in the atrioventricular (AV) node. By doing so, adenosine increases the outward potassium current, which leads to a slowing of conduction through the AV node and temporary AV block, which helps terminate reentrant tachycardiasinvolving the AV node.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 38-year-old woman known case of hyperthyroidism presented with palpitations, sweating, and agitation. On examination, she is hyperpyrexic, hypertensive, and tachycardic. She has not been able to take her medicine. which of the following medication would you prescribe immediately to treat her symptoms?\n\n- A. Atropine\n- B. Hydrocortisone\n- C. Iodine solution\n- D. Carbimazole\n- **E. Propranolol** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPropranolol is the preferred agent in hyperthyroidism and thyroid storm due to its additional effect of blocking the peripheral conversion of inactive T4 to active form T3. They are highly effective for symptomatic relief (tachycardia, tremors)\n\nIn hyperthyroidism (grave's disease) =increased T3, T4 levels, decrease TSH levels (RECALLL) In hypothyroidism (Hashimoto's thyroiditis) =Decrease T3, T4, increase TSH (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nWhilst in your cardiology rotation coronary angiography of a 44-year-old male patient revealed an occlusion of the circumflex branch of left coronary artery. This patient has been suffering from myocardial infarction in which of the following area?\n\n- A. Right and left atria\n- B. Right and left ventricle\n- C. Apex of heart\n- **D. Left atrium and ventricle** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Interventricular septum\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLeft coronary artery gives rise to two branches\n\nThe anterior interventricular branch: supply the right and left ventricle, the apex and anterior two third of interventricular septum.\n\nLeft circumflex branch: supplying the left atrium and left ventricle\n\nThe right coronary artery supplies:\n\nthe right atrium and most of right ventricle the sinoatrial and atrioventricular node\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nWhich one of the following hormones is released from posterior pituitary?\n\n- A. Prolactin\n- B. FSH\n- C. TSH\n- **D. ADH** (Correct Answer)\n- E. ACTH\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTwo hormones released from the posterior pituitary gland are ADH and Oxytocin\n\nNote: ADH release from posterior pituitary synthesized in hypothalamus. (Recall)\n\nADH is released in response to raised plasma osmolality seen in dehydration (Recall)\n\nPituitary Clinical Inhibited By Excess Harmone deficiency Growth Somatostatindwarfism in Acromegaly in adults or Harmone(Recall) children gigantism in children Hyperprolactinemia causes amenorrhea/oligomenorrhea Failure of (Recall), loss of libido and Dopamine Prolactin postpartum erectile dysfunction (Recall)\n\nlactation\n\nCortisol Adrenal ACTH Cushing's disease (Recall) insufficiency Prolactin and FSH/LH oestrogen Infertility Gonadal insufficiency /testosterone\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 20 Years old girl presents to the ED with complaint of headache, Palpitations and diaphoresis. After initial assessment you suspect pheochromocytoma. This clinical condition occurs due to excess of which of the following hormones?\n\n- A. PTH\n- B. ACTH\n- C. Oxytocin\n- **D. Catecholamines** (Correct Answer)\n- E. ADH\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPheochromocytoma is catecholamine secreting tumor arises from chromaffin cells of adrenal medulla\n\nCatecholamines include adrenaline, nor adrenaline and dopamine.\n\nAdrenaline is predominant Harmone in pheochromocytoma\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 62-year-old patient with pericardial effusion comes to a local hospital for aspiration of pericardial fluid by pericardiocentesis. The needle is inserted into the pericardial cavity through which of the following intercostal spaces adjacent to the sternum?\n\n- A. Left fourth intercostal space\n- B. Right sixth intercostal space\n- C. Right fourth intercostal space\n- D. Right fifth intercostal space\n- **E. Left fifth intercostal space** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPericardiocentesis is a procedure performed to remove pericardial fluid from the pericardial sac that surrounds the myocardium The needle insertion site is 1-2 cm lateral to the apex beat within the fifth intercostal space. Advance the needle over the superior border of the rib to avoid intercostal nerves and vessels.\n\nHigh yield:\n\nSites for Abdominal Paracentesis:\n\nMidline Approach =2 cm below Umbilicus in midline Lateral Approach = 3 to 5 cm Superior and Medial to Anterior Superior Iliac Spine.\n\nNeedle Thoracocentesis in Pneumothorax =2nd Intercostal Space Midclavicular line. (RECALL) Chest Tube Intubation, Pleural tap = Midaxillary Line 5th Intercostal Space.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 7-year-old child is brought into the ED by his mother. His mother states that child had wild berries then he suddenly started to have tachycardia, dry skin and urinary retention. What is the likely cause of this condition?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Cholinergic response\n- **B. Parasympathetic block** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Increased parasympathetic response\n- D. Sympathomimetic activity\n- E. Hypnotic response of wild berries\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAtropa belladonna commonly known as deadly nightshade is a perennial herbaceous plant with leaves and berries that are extremely toxic contains atropine and scopolamine both antagonist at muscarinic receptor and block parasympathetic\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 6:** 1-D, 2-A, 3-E, 4-E, 5-E, 6-A, 7-C, 8-E, 9-B, 10-E, 11-D, 12-D, 13-D, 14-E, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
f52d37be-7d50-4d86-a684-a272f6974eb7	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-09	Quiz 09: Lower Limb Nerves, Resuscitation Drugs & Gastrointestinal System	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 09\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 20-year-old woman underwent arthroscopy on her right ankle because of chronic ankle pain after a sprain. After arthroscopy, the patient complained of pain on the dorsum of her right foot and felt a radiating pain from the anterolateral portal to the dorsomedial aspect of her foot, this was most probably due to injury to which of the following nerve?\n\n- A. Tibial nerve\n- B. Deep fibular nerve\n- C. Saphenous nerve\n- **D. Superficial fibular nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Sural nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMotor innervation of superficial fibular nerve\n\nThe superficial fibular nerve supplies the fibularis longus and the fibularis brevis. These muscles form the lateral compartment of the lower leg. They act to evert the foot (Recall) and thus act at the subtalar joint (eversion is the action of turningthe foot outwards).\n\nSensory Functions\n\nSuperficial peroneal nerve carries sensations from the anterolateral aspect of the leg and the greater part of the dorsum of the foot (except for the firstweb space).\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 30-year-old male patient brought into ED unresponsive after initial assessment you suspect opioid toxicity you started naloxone. Naloxone works on which one of the following receptors?\n\n- A. Gaba receptor\n- **B. Opioid mu receptors antagonist** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Opioid mu receptors agonist\n- D. NMDA receptor Antagonist\n- E. NMDA receptor agonist\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNaloxone is opioid mu receptor antagonist rapidly reverse the symptoms of opioid toxicity.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nWhich one of the following is the primary ion movement responsible for the repolarization phase during an action potential?\n\n- A. Sodium influx\n- B. Sodium efflux\n- C. Chloride influx\n- D. Calcium influx\n- **E. Potassium efflux** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPotassium ion (K+) efflux: K+ channels open, allowing K+ ions to leave the cell, moving the membrane potential back towards the resting potential.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nGeneration of Action potential Once triggered an action potential will travel over the entire surface of an excitable cell and will always have the same amplitude. An action potential is an all or nothing response; because the size of the action potential is constant, the intensity of the stimulus is coded by the frequency of firing of a neuron.\n\nThreshold Potential Action potentials are initiated in nerves by activation of ligand-gated Na+ channels by neurotransmitters. Opening of these Na+ channels results in a small influx of sodium and depolarization of the negative resting membrane potential (-70 mV).\n\nIf the stimulus is sufficiently strong, the resting membrane depolarises enough to reach threshold potential (generally around -55 mV), at which point an action potential can occur.\n\nDepolarisation Voltage-gated Na+ channels open, causing further depolarisation and activating more voltage-gated Na+ channels and there is a sudden and massive sodium influx, driving the cell membrane potential to about +40 mV.\n\nRepolarisation The spike of the action potential is transient because as the membrane potential becomes positive, the voltage-gated Na+ channels inactivate preventing further sodium influx.\n\nVoltage-gated K+ channels are also activated, causing the K+ permeability to again be much greater than that for Na+, and the potassium efflux leads to repolarization.\n\nDelayed closure of these rectifier K+ channels cause a transient hyperpolarization.\n\nRefractory Period Following the action potential, Na+ channels remain inactive for a time in a period known as the absolute refractory period where they cannot be opened by any amount of depolarization.\n\nFollowing this there is a relative refractory period where the temporary hyperpolarization makes the cell more difficult to depolarise and an action potential can be generated only in response to a larger than normal stimulus.\n\nThe refractory period limits the frequency at which action potentials can be generated, and ensures that, once initiated, an action potential can travel only in one direction\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 30-year-old farmer has sustained a deep laceration to the popliteal fossa and damaged the more medial nerve. Which of the following clinical features you would most likely to see on examination?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Superolateral border | Biceps femoris |\n> | Inferomedial border | Medial head of gastrocnemius (Recall) |\n> | Inferolateral border | Lateral head of gastrocnemius and plantaris |\n> |  | The floor of the popliteal fossa is formed by the posterior surface of the knee |\n> | Floor | joint capsule, popliteus muscle and posterior femur. |\n> |  | The roof is made of up two layers: popliteal fascia and skin. The popliteal |\n> | Roof | fascia is continuous with the deep fascia of the leg. |\n> | Contents | Popliteal artery, popliteal vein, tibial nerve, common fibular nerve |\n\n- **A. Weakness of plantarflexion and inversion** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Weakness of dorsiflexion and eversion\n- C. Foot drop\n- D. Loss of flexion of the leg at the knee joint\n- E. Loss of extension of the leg at the knee joint\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTibial and common fibular nerve are the most superficial contents of the popliteal fossa. Both are the branches of sciatic nerve\n\nThe common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris tendon, travelling along the lateral margin of the popliteal Tibial nerve lies more medial\n\nBoundaries and contents of popliteal fossa\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 30-year-old male presents to the ED with a 3.5-week history of a cough and hemoptysis. These symptoms began after he was diagnosed with upper respiratory tract infection. He denies having any fever, night sweats, and has not noticed any changes to his weight. Currently, his blood pressure is 155/90 mm Hg, and his pulse is 85 bpm. A physical exam is notable for bilateral crackles and he also has edema on his lower extremities. Lab values reveal a creatinine of 4.5 mg/dL. Urine tests show the presence of proteinuria and also hematuria. A chest X-ray demonstrates the presence of bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Pulmonary function testing reveals an increased carbon monoxide diffusion capacity (DLCO). What condition might this patient have?\n\n- A. IgA nephropathy\n- B. Nephrotic syndrome\n- C. Lupus nephritis\n- D. PSGN\n- **E. Goodpasture syndrome** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nChief complaints in case of Goodpasture syndrome (GPS):\n\nShortness of breath: caused by alveolar hemorrhage. Hemoptysis: due to lung involvement Hematuria due to kidney involvement\n\nClinical Workup:\n\nPresence of pulmonary infiltrates on chest X-ray: caused by pulmonary hemorrhage.\n\nIncreased carbon monoxide diffusion capacity (DLCO) on pulmonary function testing: the present of blood in the alveoli (due to alveolar hemorrhage) results in increased DLCO Linear deposition of IgG in the glomeruli (immunofluorescence)\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 35-year-old male presents with fever and cough productive of small amounts of clear sputum. Chest radiography documents bilateral lower lobe infiltrates that are more pronounced on the right side. Causative agent is?\n\n- A. Influenza virus\n- B. Mycoplasma pneumoniae\n- C. S. aureus\n- **D. Streptococcus pnemoniae** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Pseudomonas\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPneumococcal pneumonia is a common lung infection caused by the organism Streptococcus pneumoniae.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae is one of the most common causes of community-acquired pneumonia. The clinical presentation of pneumococcal pneumonia includes fever, cough and malaise and may also include chest pain and dyspnea or tachypnoea. Radiographic appearances considered classic for pneumococcal pneumonia include lobar consolidations and air bronchograms in adults and round pneumonia in children.\n\nNote: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a commensal pathogen (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA new born baby brought into ED by his mother with complaint of grunting and respiratory distress. You suspect infant respiratory distress syndrome. Regarding surfactant which one of the following is true?\n\n- A. Produced by type 1 pneumocytes\n- B. Decrease lung compliance\n- C. Surfactant increases in smokers\n- **D. Decrease surface tension** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Increase surface tension\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nsurfactant produced by type 2 alveolar epithelial cells pneumocytes (Recall). it decreases surface tension and increases lung compliance\n\nSurfactant is made-up of 80% of phospholipids (Recall)\n\n10% of proteins 10% of neutral lipids, mainly cholesterol\n\nNote: Type 1 alveolar epithelium is responsible for gaseous exchange. Impaired gas exchange (Hypoxia) is due to deficiency of Type 1 (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nBrunner's glands are primarily found in which part of the gastrointestinal tract, and what is their main function?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | type |  |  |  |\n> |  |  |  |  | GIP |\n> |  |  | | Stomach pH |  |  |\n> | * Gastric acid (hydrochloric |  | Activates pepsinogen to |  |  |\n> |  | Gastric parietal cells |  | Gastrin | * Secretin |\n> | acid) |  | pepsin. |  |  |\n> |  |  | Kills microorganisms | Histamine | Somatostatin |\n> |  |  | g |  | * Cholecystokinin |\n> |  |  |  |  | GIP |\n> |  Intrinsic factor (IF; vitamin | 