9c6a800d-436e-46a3-880f-1c356994c45d	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-24	Quiz 24: Abdominal Hernias, Intestinal Pathology & CSF Diagnostics	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 24\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nYou are examining a patient of femoral hernia; your consultant asks which structure is bordered medially to the femoral canal?\n\n- A. Pectineal ligament\n- B. Femoral nerve\n- C. Inguinal ligament\n- D. Femoral vein\n- **E. Lacunar ligament** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFemoral canal lies most medial of the femoral sheath.\n\nFemoral sheath encloses femoral artery, femoral vein and femoral canal. Femoral nerve does not lie within femoral sheath. (Recall)\n\nBorders of the femoral canal: (Useful mnemonic LAMP: FILP)\n\nLateral border: Femoral vein (Immediately lateral) Recall.\n\nAnterior border: Inguinal ligament Medial border: Lacunar ligament Posterior ligament: Pectineal ligament, Pectineus muscle and superior ramus of pubic bone.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nContents of femoral triangle:\n\nFrom lateral to medial: Useful mnemonic (NAVEL)\n\nFemoral nerve Femoral artery Femoral vein Empty space (consists of femoral canal) Lymph nodes\n\nNote: Femoral nerve lies lateral to femoral artery and vein (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 14 years old child swallows a coin, it lodged in esophagus, which one of the following structures compress esophagus at thoracic level?\n\n- A. Left brachiocephalic vein\n- B. Left vagus nerve\n- C. Posterior surface of the heart\n- **D. Aortic Arch** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Diaphragmatic hiatus\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe anatomical relations of the oesophagus give rise to four constrictions, where food/foreign objects are most likely to become impacted\n\nThe esophagus has 3 anatomic constrictions.\n\n1: Pharyngoesophageal constriction is at the junction with the pharynx\n\n2: Thoracic(aortobronchial) constriction: The second is at the crossing with the aortic arch and th e left main bronchus\n\n3: Diaphragmatic constriction: The third is at the junction with the stomach\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 27 years old male patient diagnosed and treated for hepatitis B. In hep-B infection, which one of the following serology reports you will see?\n\n- A. Anti-HBs, anti-HBe\n- **B. HbsAg+ and HbeAg+ve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Anti HBs\n- D. HbeAg+ and HbeAb-ve\n- E. HbsAg+ and HbeAg-ve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHBsAg +Ve, HBeAg +Ve, anti Acute hepatitis B -HBc IgM\n\nHBsAg +Ve, HBeAg +Ve, anti Chronic hepatitis B -HBc IgG\n\nImmunity following infAnti-HBs, anti-HBe, anti-HBc\n\nection IgG\n\nImmunity due to vacc Anti-HBs ination\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 32-year-old male with a history of RTA presented to ED with Complain of pain in his Right shoulder. X-ray was done which shows anterior shoulder dislocation. Which one of the following ligaments is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Coraco-clavicular ligament\n- B. Acromioclavicular ligament\n- C. Transverse humeral ligament\n- D. Coracoacromial ligament\n- **E. Glenohumeral ligament** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe shoulder joint\n\nType: synovial ball and socket joint Shoulder joint is formed by the articulation of th e head of the humerus with the glenoid cavity o f the scapula\n\nLigaments of the shoulder joint:\n\nIn the shoulder joint, the ligaments play a key role in stabilizing the bony structures.\n\nGlenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle and inferior) They act to stabilize the anterior aspect o f the joint. They are the main source of stability for the shoulder (Recall), holding it in place and preventing it from dislocating anteriorly. Coracohumeral ligament: It supports the superior part of the joint capsule.\n\nTransverse humeral ligament: This ligament spans the distance between the two tubercles of the humerus. It holds the tendon of the long head of the biceps in the intertubercular groove Coraco-clavicular ligament: This ligament composed of the trapezoid and conoid ligaments and runs from the clavicle to the coracoid process of t he scapula. They work alongside the acromioclavicular ligament to maintain the alignment of the clavicle in relation to the scapula.\n\nCoracoacromial ligament. This ligament runs between the acromion and coracoid process of the scapula it forms the coraco-acromial arch. This structure overlies the shoulder joint, preventing superior displacement of the humeral head.\n\nStability of the joint\n\nRotator cuff muscles [SITS MUSCLES] Recall Glenoid labrum: A fibrocartilaginous ridge surrounding the glenoid cavity Ligaments: Extra Capsular ligaments form the coraco-acromial arch.\n\nBiceps tendon - it acts as a minor humeral head depressor, thereby contributing to stability. Movements of the shoulder joint: Shoulder joint is highly mobile ball and socket joint allows various movements like flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, medial rotation, lateral rotation and circumduction\n\nCLINICAL PEARL:\n\nShoulder joint dislocations\n\nAnterior shoulder dislocation: Most common injury results from forceful abduction, extension a nd external rotation\n\nClinical features:\n\nArm usually abducted and externally rotated Humeral head often palpated anteriorly Patient cannot touch contralateral shoulder with hand of affected extremity.\n\nComplications of anterior shoulder dislocation:\n\nInjury to axillary nerve (Recall)\n\nInjury to axillary artery Injury to rotator cuff\n\nPosterior Shoulder dislocation: occurs occasionally results from direct blow to the arm that produces forceful internal rotation and adduction.\n\nClinical features:\n\nArm usually adducted and internally rotated Patient unable to perform external rotation\n\nComplications of posterior shoulder dislocation [ less common neurovascular injury]\n\nFracture of posterior glenoid labrum Fracture of humeral shaft or lesser tuberosity\n\nNote: Light bulb sign on the X-ray is the diagnostic feature of posterior shoulder dislocation\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 55 years old male known case of benign prostatic hyperplasia presents into the ED with complaint of urinary retention. He was prescribed doxazosin for urinary retention, drug given act on which receptor?\n\n- A. dopamine\n- B. Anti muscarinic\n- C. Anticholinesterase\n- **D. Alpha 1 adrenoreceptor blocker** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Acetylcholine\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDoxazosin is an Alpha 1 adrenoreceptor blocker. I t relieves the symptoms of urinary retention\n\nNote: Anti-muscarinic drugs cause urinary retention, dry mouth, pupil dilation and constipation (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 36 years old male sustained trauma to his right foot and presents to the ED with infected wound. Which one of the following local factors impede wound healing?\n\n- A. Damage to the sensory nerves around the wound\n- B. Decreased local mobility\n- C. Decreased skin tension\n- D. Increased blood supply\n- **E. The presence of necrotic tissue** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWound healing is impaired by a poor blood supply to the wound and a poor venous drainage from the wound. Increased skin tension caused by tight sutures and the presence of infection, foreign bodies and necrotic tissue are other local factors that impede healing.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 50 years old female known case of inflammatory bowel disease presents to the ED with complaint of pain in right lower quadrant and bloody diarrhea. Regarding management of I.B.D which one of the following treatment options is best given initially?\n\n- A. Rectal prednisolone\n- **B. Rectal mesalazine** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Oral azathioprine\n- D. Oral prednisolone\n- E. Hydrocortisone IV\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nA stepwise approach that is used for IBD management is outlined below.\n\nThe first step in pharmacologic therapy for IBD isaminosalicylates (Mesalazine) If the patient does not respond to an appropriate dose of aminosalicylates, the second step is the addition of corticosteroids, which tend to result in a significant decrease in inflammation. Once the response is see n, the dose can be tapered\n\nFor those with mild to moderate disease limited to the rectum, aminosalicylate agents like mesalamine are the mainstay of treatment and are be st given rectally\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 14 years old male brought into the ED with complaint of fever and neck rigidity. You suspect meningitis, what are the typical CSF changes in meningitis?\n\n- **A. CSF can appear clear in viral meningitis** (Correct Answer)\n- B. CSF can appear turbid in Viral meningitis\n- C. Tuberculous meningitis has more polymorphs than mononuclear cells in CSF\n- D. In bacterial meningitis the CSF protein is 0.4 g/L\n- E. Glucose is less than half of blood glucose in viral meningitis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNormal CSF glucose is less and 60% of the level o f serum glucose\n\nGlucose is more than half of blood glucose (Normal) in viral meningitis. In bacterial and TB meningitis CSF to serum glucose ratio falls Tuberculous meningitis has more mononuclear cells than polymorphs in CSF. Predominant cells in viral and TB meningitis are Lymphocytes while predominant cells in bacterial meningitis are Neutrophils In bacterial meningitis CSF protein is raised to 0. 5-2.0 g/L.)\n\nCSF is clear in viral meningitis and CSF is turbid i n bacterial meningitis\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 10 days old baby brought into the ED with complaint of intestinal obstruction and diagnosed to have Hirschsprung disease. Hirschsprung disease occur due to absence of which one of the following enteric neurons?\n\n- **A. Myenteric plexus** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Pacinian corpuscles\n- C. Glial cells\n- D. Endothelial cells\n- E. Mucosal plexus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHirschsprung disease results from the absence o f parasympathetic ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexus of the rectum and/or colon\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 10-year-old child was brought into the ED following head trauma with complaint of hydrocephalus. Imaging resulted in swelling of one lateral ventricle which of the following is the most likely site of blockage\n\n- A. Aqueduct of Sylvius\n- B. Foramen Luska\n- C. Sub arachnoid cisterns\n- **D. Foramen Monro** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Subarachnoid space\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe flow of CSF Useful Mnemonic\n\nLittle Infants Try Crying for Food. Sub arachnoid space\n\nLateral ventricle Interventricular foramina of (Monro)\n\nThird ventricle Cerebral aqueduct Forth ventricle Foramina of Luschka Sub arachnoid space\n\nNote: From subarachnoid space CSF is reabsorb ed by arachnoid granulations (Recall) back into the circulation\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nRegarding Einthoven's triangle, which one following show maximum deflection during ventricular depolarization?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. Right arm and left leg** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Left arm and right arm\n- C. Left arm and left leg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
64159eb7-789a-4d13-b074-10c7c63ff0de	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	arterial-supply-upper-limb	Arterial supply of the upper limb	article	## The main arterial pathway\n\nSubclavian artery, then axillary artery (from the outer border of the first rib to the lower border of teres major), then brachial artery, then it divides into the radial and ulnar arteries at the level of the radial neck in the cubital fossa.\n\n## Axillary artery\n\nIt is divided into three parts by pectoralis minor. The axillary vein lies medial to it. Mnemonic: Screw The Lawyers Save A Patient.\n\n- **Part 1:** Superior thoracic artery.\n- **Part 2:** Thoracoacromial artery and Lateral thoracic artery.\n- **Part 3:** Subscapular artery, Anterior and Posterior circumflex humeral arteries.\n\nThe two circumflex humeral arteries form an anastomosis around the surgical neck of the humerus and can be damaged in fractures there.\n\n## Brachial artery\n\nThe main supply of the arm. It runs on brachialis and is palpable medial to the biceps tendon in the cubital fossa.\n\nBranches:\n- **Profunda brachii:** the largest, travels with the radial nerve in the radial groove and supplies triceps.\n- Superior ulnar collateral artery (travels with the ulnar nerve).\n- Inferior ulnar collateral artery (elbow anastomosis).\n- Muscular branches and the nutrient artery of the humerus.\n\n## Radial artery\n\n- Runs along the lateral forearm under brachioradialis.\n- The pulse is felt just lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon at the wrist.\n- Winds around the wrist, crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox and enters the palm between the first and second metacarpals.\n- Forms the **deep palmar arch**.\n\n## Ulnar artery\n\n- The larger terminal branch. Runs down the ulnar side of the forearm deep to the superficial flexors.\n- Gives off the common interosseous artery.\n- Enters the hand through Guyon canal (between the pisiform and the hook of the hamate) with the ulnar nerve.\n- Forms the **superficial palmar arch**.\n\n## Palmar arches\n\n- **Superficial arch:** lies just under the palmar aponeurosis, mainly ulnar artery, completed by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery. Gives the common palmar digital arteries to the fingers.\n- **Deep arch:** lies deeper, mainly radial artery, completed by the deep branch of the ulnar artery. Supplies the metacarpals and wrist joint.\n- The two arches anastomose, which is why the hand tolerates loss of one artery. The Allen test checks this collateral flow.\n\n## Clinical pearls\n\n- **Volkmann ischaemic contracture:** brachial artery injury or compartment syndrome (classically after a supracondylar fracture in a child) leads to ischaemia and necrosis of the forearm flexors, replaced by fibrosis. The result is a fixed flexion deformity of the wrist and fingers. Warning signs are the "5 Ps": pain on passive stretch, pallor, paraesthesia, paralysis, pulselessness (a late sign).\n- **Guyon canal syndrome:** ulnar nerve compression at the wrist. Common causes are a ganglion, lipoma or trauma.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
124e0bfb-c244-4ecd-a08d-c20567e627c2	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	joints-upper-limb	Joints of the upper limb	article	## Acromioclavicular (AC) joint\n\n- Plane synovial joint between the lateral end of the clavicle and the acromion.\n- Ligaments: acromioclavicular ligament, and the **coracoclavicular ligament** (conoid and trapezoid parts). The coracoclavicular ligament is the strongest and suspends the weight of the limb from the clavicle.\n- **Shoulder separation:** a heavy fall onto the point of the shoulder can tear the coracoclavicular ligament, and the outer end of the clavicle rides up and becomes easy to palpate.\n\n## Sternoclavicular (SC) joint\n\n- Saddle-type synovial joint between the clavicle, the manubrium and the first costal cartilage. It is the only bony link between the upper limb and the trunk.\n- Ligaments: anterior and posterior sternoclavicular, interclavicular, and the strong costoclavicular ligament.\n- Force along the clavicle usually breaks the clavicle rather than dislocating this joint. Dislocation is uncommon, and a posterior dislocation is dangerous because of the structures behind the joint.\n\n## Shoulder (glenohumeral) joint\n\n- Ball-and-socket synovial joint: head of the humerus in the glenoid cavity. The most mobile joint in the body, and therefore the least stable.\n- **Static stabilisers:** glenoid labrum, joint capsule, glenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle, inferior), coracohumeral ligament, coraco-acromial arch. The transverse humeral ligament holds the long head of biceps in the bicipital groove.\n- **Dynamic stabilisers:** the rotator cuff (SITS) and the long head of biceps tendon, which acts as a minor depressor of the humeral head.\n- Movements: flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, medial and lateral rotation, circumduction.\n\n**Anterior dislocation** (most common): from forced abduction, extension and external rotation. The arm is held abducted and externally rotated, the humeral head is felt anteriorly, and the patient cannot touch the opposite shoulder. Risks: axillary nerve, axillary artery, rotator cuff.\n\n**Posterior dislocation** (uncommon): from direct force with internal rotation and adduction, and classically from seizures or electric shock. The arm is held adducted and internally rotated, and external rotation is impossible. The X-ray may show the **light bulb sign**.\n\n## Elbow joint\n\n- Hinge synovial joint: ulna (trochlear notch) with the trochlea, and radial head with the capitulum.\n- Ligaments: radial (lateral) collateral, ulnar (medial) collateral, annular ligament around the radial head.\n- **Posterior dislocation** is the usual type, from a fall on the hand with the elbow flexed. Check the ulnar nerve and brachial artery. In children, a supracondylar fracture threatens the brachial artery and the median nerve (especially the anterior interosseous branch).\n\n## Radioulnar joints and interosseous membrane\n\n- Pivot-type synovial joints that allow pronation and supination.\n- Proximal: radial head in the radial notch of the ulna, held by the annular ligament.\n- Distal: head of the ulna with the ulnar notch of the radius.\n- The interosseous membrane joins the radius and ulna and gives attachment to forearm muscles. Force on the radius travels across it to the ulna.\n\n## Wrist joint\n\n- Condyloid synovial joint between the distal radius plus articular disc above and the scaphoid, lunate and triquetrum below. **The ulna is not part of the wrist joint.**\n- Ligaments: palmar and dorsal radiocarpal, and the ulnar and radial collateral ligaments, which limit deviation to the opposite side.\n- Movements: flexion, extension, abduction (radial deviation), adduction (ulnar deviation).\n- **Fall on an outstretched hand:** the scaphoid is the most commonly fractured carpal bone (tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox, risk of avascular necrosis of the proximal pole), and **Colles fracture** (distal radius, dorsal displacement, "dinner fork" deformity) is the most common wrist fracture overall.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
709af10b-ce86-4f12-a562-a0372e4c692a	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	muscles-of-the-upper-limb	Muscles of the Upper Limb	article	Forearm muscles\n\n#### Anterior forearm muscles superficial\n\ncompartment\n\n#### Anterior Forearm muscles intermediate\n\ncompartment:\n\n#### Anterior Forearm muscles deep compartment\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Key Point: All the muscles of anterior forearm are innervated**\n> by median nerve except flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_03_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nflexor digitorum profundus innervated by ulnar nerve.\nDigital attachment of flexor muscles\nof forearm:\nAll the anterior forearm muscles originate from the medial\nhumeral epicondyle.\nMuscles of Anterior Arm:\nMuscles of posterior Arm:\nTriceps brachii:\nTriceps brachii is a large thick muscle in the posterior arm and\nappears as a horseshoe-shaped muscle\nFunction:  Extension of forearm at elbow and extension of arm\nat shoulder joint.\nInnervation: Radial nerve\nMuscles of posterior forearm\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_04_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_05_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nThe muscles in the posterior compartment of the forearm are\ncommonly known as the extensor muscles.\nExtensor muscles are divided into superficial and deep\ncompartment.\n\n#### Superficial compartment\n\n#### Deep Compartment\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n\n### 1: Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis): is caused by a chronic\n\ninflammation or irritation of the origin of the extensor muscles\nof the forearm from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus as a\nresult of repetitive strain. It is a painful condition and common\nin tennis players.\n\n### 2: Golfer's elbow (medial epicondylitis):  is a painful condition\n\ncaused by a tear or chronic inflammation in the origin of flexor\nmuscles of the forearm\n\n### 3: Nursemaid's elbow or pulled elbow is a radial head\n\nsubluxation and occurs in toddlers when the child is lifted by\nthe wrist. The annular ligament (and other elbow ligaments) is\nnot fully developed in terms of strength, and thus, the radial\nhead can slip out of position.\nMuscles of the hand:\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_06_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_07_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nThenar muscles:\nThree short muscles located in the base of the thumb. All are\ninnervated by median nerve.\nHypothenar muscles:\nHypothenar muscle located at base of middle finger and form\nhypothenar eminence. All are innervated by ulnar nerve\nLumbrical Muscles:\nThere are four lumbrical muscles. Each one of these originates\nfrom a tendon of the flexor digitorum profundus. They pass\ndorsally and laterally around each finger, and inserts into the\nextensor hood.\nInnervation:\nMedial two lumbricals: Ulnar nerve\nLateral two lumbricals: Median nerve\nFunction:\nFlexion at the MCP joint and extension at the interphalangeal\n(IP) joints of each digit.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Denervation of these muscles is the basis of claw hand and\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_08_p4.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_09_p4.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\nhand of benediction.\nInterossei muscles: Located between the metacarpals of the\nhand. Divided into Dorsal and palmar Interossei\nAll the interossei muscles are innervated by ulnar nerve.\nDorsal Interossei:\nAbduction of the digits. Assists in flexion at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and extension at the\ninterphalangeal joints.\nPalmar Interossei:\nAdduction of the digits. Assists in flexion at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and extension at the\ninterphalangeal joints.\nMuscles of the Shoulder region:\nExtrinsic muscles, Intrinsic Muscles\nExtrinsic muscles: Connecting the Upper Limb to the Vertebral\nColumn and divided into superficial and deep group.\nSuperficial: Trapezius and Latissimus dorsi\nDeep: Levator Scapulae, Rhomboid minor and Rhomboid\nmajor\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_10_p5.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Ask the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if**\n> patient unable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve injury\n> and paralysis of trapezius muscle.\n\nIntrinsic muscles of the shoulder: Connecting the scapula to\nthe humerus\nThere are six muscles in this group the deltoid, teres major, and\nthe four rotator cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus,\nsubscapularis and teres minor).\nNote: All the rotator cuff muscles (SITS) are lateral rotators of\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 11](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_11_p6.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 11 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 12](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_12_p6.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 12 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\narm except "S" Subscapularis\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n> Assessment of rotator cuff muscles:\n> Rotator cuff tendonitis:\n> Refers to inflammation of tendons of rotator cuff muscles.\n\nSupraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator\ncuff muscle.\nMuscles of pectoral region:\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 13](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_13_p7.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 13 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 14](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_14_p7.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 14 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\nPectoralis Major: large fan shaped and most superficial muscle\nof the pectoral region.\nOrigin:\nClavicular head: Medial clavicle\nSternocostal head originates from the anterior surface of the\nsternum, the superior six costal cartilages and the\naponeurosis of the external oblique muscle.\nInsertion: intertubercular sulcus of the humerus\nFunction: Adduction and medial rotation of the upper limb\nand draws the scapula Antero inferiorly.\nInnervation: Lateral and medial pectoral nerves\nPectoralis minor: small muscle lies underneath the pectoralis\nmajor.\nOrigin: Originates from the 3rd-5th ribs\nInsertion: coracoid process of the scapula\nFunction: stabilization, depression, abduction or protraction,\ninternal rotation and downward rotation of the scapula.\nInnervation: Medial pectoral nerve\nSerratus anterior: Located more laterally in the chest wall\nOrigin: Consists of several muscular strips originate from lateral\nsurface of ribs 1-8\nInsertion: Attach to the costal surface of medial border of\nscapula\nFunction: holds the scapula against the ribcage and assist in\nprotraction and rotation of scapula\nInnervation: Long thoracic nerve\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n> winging of scapula: Scapula no longer held against the rib\n> cage and protrudes out of the back due to paralysis of serratus\n> anterior and injury to Long thoracic nerve.\n\nSubclavius: Small muscle located underneath the clavicle\nOrigin: First rib and its costal cartilage\nInsertion: Inferior surface of middle third of clavicle\nFunction: Anchors and depresses clavicle\nInnervation: Nerve to subclavius.\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 15](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_15_p9.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 15 (Page 9 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8a29e7ae-8b8d-4e4d-8029-6c2f2e9602e5	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	upper-limb-other-important-structures	Upper Limb Other Important Structures	article	Carpal Tunnel:\nThe carpel tunnel is the space that lies between the flexor\nretinaculum.\nContents of the carpal tunnel\nNote:\nThe ulnar nerve, ulnar artery and tendon of Palmaris longus\nruns superficial to flexor retinaculum and thus are not within\ncarpel tunnel.\nFlexor pollicis longus enter the carpel tunnel before reaching\nits insertion on the distal phalanx of the thumb.\nPronator quadratus is confined to the distal forearm and does\nnot reach the carpel tunnel.\nAnatomical snuffbox\nis a triangular depression found on the lateral aspect of the\ndorsum of the hand. It is best seen when the thumb is\nextended.\n\n![Upper Limb Other Important Structures - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-other-important-structures/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Other Important Structures — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Limb Other Important Structures - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-other-important-structures/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Other Important Structures — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Fracture of scaphoid bone: Falling on outstretched hand. The\n> scaphoid bone of the hand is the most commonly fractured\n> carpal bone\n> Clinical feature: Pain and tenderness in the anatomical\n> snuffbox.\n\nUlnar canal (Guyon's canal)\nTransmits ulnar neurovascular bundle from forearm into the\nhand.\nBorders:\nMedial (ulnar): pisiform, flexor carpi ulnaris tendon\nLateral (radial): hook of hamate.\nRoof: palmar carpal ligament.\nFloor: flexor retinaculum\nContents: Ulnar nerve and artery\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Ulnar canal syndrome refers to compression of the ulnar\n> nerve within the ulnar canal.\n\nFlexor Retinaculum:\nThe flexor retinaculum is a thickened band of fibrous\nconnective tissue on the volar aspect of the hand which forms\nthe roof of the carpal tunnel. The flexor retinaculum holds the\nflexor tendons in place at the wrist and prevents them from\nbowstringing.\nRelations of flexor retinaculum:\nThe ulnar artery, ulnar nerve, tendon of the palmaris longus\nand palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve pass\ninto the hand anterior to the flexor retinaculum, and\ntherefore do not pass through the carpal tunnel.\nThe flexor carpi radialis tendon passes through the lateral\naspect of the flexor retinaculum into the hand.\nThe four tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus, the four\ntendons of the flexor digitorum superficialis, the tendon of\nthe flexor pollicis longus and the median nerve pass into the\nhand posterior to the flexor retinaculum, within the carpal\ntunnel.\n\nExtensor Retinaculum:\nThe extensor retinaculum lies obliquely across the extensor\nsurface of the wrist joint, thickened part of the antecubital\nfossa and that holds the extensor tendons in place during\nmovement of the wrist.\nAttachments:\nLaterally: Lower part of the anterior border of the radius\nMedially: Styloid process of the ulna, Triquetral, and Pisiform\nContents\nThe extensor tendons pass through the extensor tunnel, deep\nto the extensor retinaculum, in six compartments lined by\nsynovial sheaths:\nThe tendons of the extensor carpi ulnaris and extensor digiti\nminimi have separate sheaths on the medial side of the\nwrist.\nThe tendons of the extensor digitorum and extensor indicis\nshare a synovial sheath on the posterior surface of the wrist.\nThe tendons of the abductor pollicis longus and extensor\npollicis brevis, the extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor\ncarpi radialis brevis, and the extensor pollicis longus have\nthree separate sheaths on the lateral side of the wrist.\nOther Clinical High Yield Stuff:\n\n### 1: Dupuytren contracture: is a progressive thickening,\n\nshortening, and fibrosis of the palmar fascia. producing a fixed\nflexion contracture of the hand, in which the fingers bend\ntoward the palm and cannot be fully extended (commonly\nfourth and fifth fingers).\n\n### 2: Volkmann contracture: is an ischemic contracture (flexion\n\ndeformity) resulting from necrosis of the forearm flexor\nmuscles.\n\n### 3: Tenosynovitis:  is commonly caused by an infection of the\n\nsynovial sheath and tendon (the long flexor tendon), causing\npain, swelling, and difficulty moving a joint. Rupture of the\ntendon sheath (puncture injury of the palm) may cause the\nspread of infection to the mid-palmar space and to the carpal tunnel. Likewise, tenosynovitis in the thumb may spread to the\nradial bursa and in the little finger to the ulnar bursa.\nNote: Infection in the middle three fingers is usually contained\nas they have separate synovial sheaths.\nThe synovial sheath of the little finger is usually continuous\nwith the common flexor sheath (the ulnar bursa) and thus\ninfection may spread to this sheath and from here to the\nmid-palmar space.\nInfections in the thumb may spread to the mid-palmar space\nvia the continuous synovial sheath of the flexor pollicis\nlongus (the radial bursa).\n\n### 4: Trigger finger: occurs when the long flexor tendon develops\n\na nodule or swelling that interferes with its gliding through the\npulley, causing pain at the joint and an audible clicking or\nsnapping. The condition is caused by rheumatoid arthritis,\nrepetitive trauma, and wear and tear of aging of the tendon.\n\n### 5: Mallet finger (hammer finger or baseball finger) is a finger\n\nwith permanent flexion of the distal phalanx as a result of an\navulsion of the lateral bands of the extensor tendon to the\ndistal phalanx.\n\n### 6:  Boutonniere deformity is a finger with abnormal flexion of\n\nthe middle phalanx and hyperextension of the distal phalanx as\na result of a rupture of the central band of the extensor tendon\n\n### 7:  Pancoast tumor in the apex of the lung can result in lower\n\ntrunk injury and, occasionally, Horner syndrome because of\nsympathetic ganglion compression (ptosis, anhidrosis, and\npupillary constriction). It can also cause thoracic outlet\nsyndrome.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
733c9e6f-4b61-4691-ac81-2716fecee075	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	microcirculation-and-lymph	Microcirculation and Lymph	article	Microcirculation and Lymph\nStructure of capillary beds\nMetarterioles branch into the capillary beds. At the junction\nof the arterioles and capillaries is a smooth muscle band\ncalled the precapillary sphincter.\nTrue capillaries do not have smooth muscle; they consist of a\nsingle layer of endothelial cells surrounded by a basement\nmembrane.\nClefts (pores) between the endothelial cells allow passage of\nwater-soluble substances.\nBlood flow through the capillaries is regulated by contraction\nand relaxation of the arterioles and pre-capillary sphincter.\nPassage of substances across the capillary\nwall\nLipid-soluble substances across the membranes of the\ncapillary endothelial cells by simple diffusion, include O2 and\nCO2\nSmall water-soluble substances cross via the water-filled\nclefts between the endothelial cells which include water,\nglucose, and amino acids.\nGenerally, protein molecules are too large to pass freely\nthrough the clefts.\nIn the brain, the clefts between endothelial cells are\nexceptionally tight (blood-brain barrier).\nIn the liver and intestine, the clefts are exceptionally wide\nand allow passage of protein. These capillaries are called\nsinusoids.\nLarge water-soluble substances can cross by pinocytosis.\nLymph:\nFunction of lymph: Normally, filtration of fluid out of the\ncapillaries is slightly greater than absorption of fluid into the\ncapillaries. The excess filtered fluid is returned to the circulation via the lymph also returns any filtered protein to the\ncirculation.\nUnidirectional flow of lymph One-way flap valves permit\ninterstitial fluid to enter, but not leave the lymph vessels.\nFlow through larger lymphatic vessels is also unidirectional\nand is aided by one-way valves and skeletal muscle\ncontraction.\nEdema:\noccurs when the volume of interstitial fluid exceeds the\ncapacity of the lymphatics to return it to the circulation.\nCan be caused by excess filtration or blocked lymphatics.\nHistamine causes both arteriolar dilation and venous\nconstriction, which together produce a large increase in\ncapillary hydrostatic pressure and local edema.\nIn nephrotic syndrome urinary loss of protein result in\nhypoalbuminemia and edema is due to increased capillary\nhydrostatic pressure and decreased plasma oncotic\npressure\nNitric oxide (NO):\nSodium nitroprusside is broken down into the circulation to\nrelease Nitric Oxide (NO)\nis produced in the endothelial cells. causes local relaxation of\nvascular smooth muscle.\nMechanism of action involves the activation of guanylate\ncyclase and production of cyclic guanosine\nmonophosphate (cGMP)\nNO is one form of endothelial-derived relaxing factor (EDRF).\nCirculating Acetylcholine causes vasodilation by stimulating\nthe production of NO in vascular smooth muscle.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
134203cf-bd40-40af-9819-dc284ff68ae7	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-03	Quiz 03: Clinical Neuroanatomy, Cranial Nerves & Head-and-Neck Trauma	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 03\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 30 years old male was brought into the ED following a fall from a height. His examination reveals bilateral weakness in both arm power of 3/5 and his lower limb power is 4/5, he also has variable sensory impairment\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Dorsal column | Located: posteromedial aspect | Transmit ipsilateral proprioception, vibration |\n> |  | of spinal cord | and light touch sensation |\n> | Spinothalamic tract | Anterolateral aspect of cord | Transmit contralateral pain, crude touch |\n> |  |  | and temperature sensation |\n> | Lateral | Posterolateral aspect of cord | Control ipsilateral motor power |\n> | corticospinal tract |  |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Card |  |  |  |\n> | syndrome | Mechanism | Tract injury | Symptoms |\n> | let | 4 |  | > Death if c1-c3 injured. |\n> | omprete cor |  |  | > Quadriplegia |\n> | transection | Major trauma | All tracts | Paraplegia |\n> |  |  |  | > Complete sensory loss below lesion |\n> |  |  |  | > Urinary and faecal continence |\n> | Brown- | Hemitransection or |  | > Ipsilateral hemiparesis |\n> |  | unilateral | All tract on one | > Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, |\n> | y om | compression of the | side | vibration and fine touch sensation |\n> |  | cord |  | > Contralateral loss of crude touch, |\n> | WH |  |  | pain and temperature sensation |\n> | Central cord | usually seen in older |  |  |  Bilateral motor loss |\n> |  |  | Corticospinal | > Varying degree of sensory loss |\n> | syndrome |  |  | > Greater loss in upper limb than lower limb! |\n> |  | sustain a hyperextension | spinothalamic | > Greater loss of motor in upper limb than |\n> |  | injury | tract | sensory function |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Anterior cord | Direct anterior cord | Corticospinal, | > Bilateral motor loss |\n> | syndrome | compression | spinothalamic and | > Bilateral loss of crude touch, |\n> |  | Flexion of cervical spine | spinocerebellar tracts | pain and temperature sensation |\n> |  | Thrombosis of anterior |  |  |\n> | 62 | spinal artery |  |  |\n> | Posterior cord | hyperextension injury |  | > Bilateral loss of proprioception, |\n> | syndrome | associated with | Dorsal column | vibration and light touch |\n> |  | vertebral arch fractures |  | sensation |\n> | aw | or penetrating trauma to |  |  |\n> |  | the back |  |  |\n\n- **A. Central Cord Syndrome** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Brown Sequard Syndrome\n- C. Posterior Cord Syndrome\n- D. Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- E. Complete Cord Transection\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n-Spinal cord injuries spinal tracts and their normal function\n\nSpinal cord syndrome\n\nAnterior cord syndrome (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 35 Years old male with a history of RTA was brought into the ED with injury on occipital region. On examination, patient has bitemporal hemianopia. Which structure Is most likely damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  | 1: lesion at optic nerve- monocular vision loss | O |\n> |  |  | 2: Optic chiasm- bitemporal hemianopia |  |\n> | optic | > |  |  |\n> | CHIASM |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | 3: Optic tract- contralateral homonymous hemianopia q | q |\n> | LATERAL |  |  |  |\n> | GENICULATE | (C) |  | a 4a |\n> | NUCLEUS |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | 4: Temporal lower optic radiation- contralateral |  |\n> |  |  | superior quadrantanopia |  |\n> | OPTIC | = |  |  |\n> | RADIATIONS |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | 5: Parietal upper optic radiation- contralateral inferior |  |\n> |  | 4 | quadrantanopia |  |\n> |  |  | 6: Occipital visual cortex- contralateral homonymous |  |\n> |  |  | hemianopia with macular sparing |  |\n\n- A. Optic radiation\n- B. Visual area\n- **C. Optic chiasm** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Optic tract\n- E. Lateral geniculate body\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nContralateral Homonyms hemianopia = optic tract (Recall)\n\nBitemporal hemianopia = optic chiasma Parietal upper optic radiation: Contralateral homonymous inferior quadrantanopia Temporal lower optic radiation: Contralateral homonymous superior quadrantanopia (Recall) Occipital visual cortex: Contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing e.g.: Lesion of right temporal lobe would result in left homonymous superior quadrantanopia causing left temporal and right nasal vision loss (Recall) Remember the lesions of visual pathway (High yield)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nYou are going to attempt carotid sinus massage in a patient with SVT. You ask your colleague at which of the following level you should apply pressure?\n\n- A. Temporomandibular joint\n- **B. Thyroid cartilage** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Cricoid cartilage\n- D. Hyoid bone\n- E. Thyrohyoid membrane\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe carotid sinus is the reflex area of the carotid artery consisting of baroreceptors which monitor blood pressure. The carotid sinus is located at the bifurcation of the common carotid artery, which is found at the level of the thyroid cartilage and medial to the sternocleidomastoid muscle\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 45 Years old male suffered penetrating trauma to the parotid gland with facial nerve injury. Orbicularis oculi muscle is affected and frontalis muscle is spared. Which of the following terminal branch of the facial nerve is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Facial nerve branches in temporal bone | Facial nerve terminal branches in the parotid |  |\n> |  |  | gland |\n> | * Chorda tympani: taste from Ant 2/3 of | * Temporal branch: innervate muscles in |  |\n> | tongue (RECALL) | the temple, forehead (Frontalis muscle) |  |\n> | * While Ant 2/3 of general sensation of | and supraorbital areas (upper half of |  |\n> | tongue: mandibular division of trigeminal | orbicularis oculi muscle). |  |\n> | nerve | * | muscle: |\n> |  | Frontal belly: Elevates eyebrows, wrinkles |  |\n> |  | skin of forehead innervated by temporal |  |\n> |  | branch |  |\n> |  | Occipital belly: Retracts scalp innervated |  |\n> |  | by posterior auricular nerve |  |\n> |  | Zygomatic branch: innervate muscles in |  |\n> | * Greater petrosal nerve: Parasympathetic | the infraorbital area (Lower half of |  |\n> | innervation to lacrimal and mucous glands | orbicularis oculi muscle) Recall lateral |  |\n> |  | nasal area and upper lip |  |\n> |  | * Buccal branch: muscles in the cheek and |  |\n> |  | upper lip (Recall) |  |\n> |  | Pursing of the lips |  |\n> |  | * | innervate |\n> |  | muscles of lower lip and chin. |  |\n\n- A. Buccal branch\n- **B. Zygomatic branch** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Marginal mandibular branch\n- D. Greater petrosal nerve\n- E. Temporal branch\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: Skin of upper forehead, eye lid and conjunctiva are innervated by supraorbital nerve (RECALL) branch of Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve\n\nSupratrochlear nerve: Innervate skin of lower central forehead and conjunctiva.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 25 years old male presents to the ED with a complaint of progressive difficulty in walking. When you observe him, he walks with waddling gait and pelvis falling towards the left side on each step. Which of the following nerve is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Obturator nerve\n- B. Ilioinguinal nerve\n- **C. Superior gluteal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Inferior gluteal nerve\n- E. Sciatic nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTrendelenburg gait or waddling gait occurs when your hip abductor muscles are weakened. This muscle group includes the gluteus medius andminimus innervated by superior gluteal nerve.The weakness of these muscles causes drooping of the pelvis to the contralateral side while walking (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\n70 years old male presents to the ED with complain of expressive Broca's dysphasia. The brocas speech area is located in which of the following regions?\n\n- A. Temporal lobe\n- **B. Frontal lobe** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Occipital lobe\n- D. Parietal lobe\n- E. Midbrain\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBroca's speech area is located in frontal lobe Broca's aphasia or expressive aphasia is when people find it very difficult to find and say the right words. People with Broca's aphasia may only be able to say single words or very short sentences, although it's usually possible for other people to understand what they mean\n\nWernicke's aphasia (fluent aphasia) in the temporal lobe.\n\nWernicke's aphasia or receptive aphasia is when someone is able to speak well and use long sentences, but what they say does not make any sense.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 53-year-old woman presents to the ED with intermittent palpitations and a loud first heart sound (S1) on physical exam. What is the most likely cause of the loud first heart sound?\n\n- A. Pericarditis\n- B. Pulmonary hypertension\n- C. Aortic regurgitation\n- D. Aortic Stenosis\n- **E. Mitral stenosis** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAbnormal heart Sounds:\n\nFirst Heart Sound (S1):\n\nLoud S1: Can be due to mitral stenosis or hyperdynamic states (e.g., fever, hyperthyroidism) Soft S1: Can be seen in mitral regurgitation or a poorly functioning left ventricle.\n\nSecond Heart Sound (S2):\n\nLoud S2: Can indicate hypertension (systemic or pulmonary).\n\nSplit S2: May occur physiologically during inspiration or pathologically in conditions like atrial septal defect or right bundle branch block.\n\nThird Heart Sound (S3):\n\nNormal in children or athletes, but pathological in adults, associated with heart failure or volume overload (e.g., dilated cardiomyopathy).\n\nFourth Heart Sound (S4):\n\nCaused by atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, associated with conditions like left ventricular hypertrophy or aortic stenosis\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 52 years old chronic smoker presented to the ED with complaint of sudden severe headache. After initial assessment and imaging revealed a ruptured berry aneurysm. Which of the following blood vessel is most likely location of the aneurysm?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Blood vessel | Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral |  |\n> |  |  | arteries |\n> |  | FRONTAL LOBE: contralateral weakness in lower |  |\n> | . | limb, dysarthria, dysphasia, apraxia and urinary |  |\n> | Anterior cerebellar artery | incontinence. |  |\n> |  | PARIETAL LOBE: contralateral somatosensory loss |  |\n> |  | in the lower limb |  |\n> |  |  | contralateral homonymous |\n> |  | hemianopia with macular sparing, cortical |  |\n> |  | blindness (if bilateral). |  |\n> | Posterior cerebellar artery |  |  |\n> |  | TEMPORAL LOBE: memory deficit |  |\n> |  | OCCIPITOTEMPORAL REGION: prosopagnosia, |  |\n> |  | and color blindness |  |\n> |  | FRONTAL LOBE: contralateral weakness (face/arm |  |\n> |  | greater than leg), conjugate deviation of the eyes to |  |\n> |  | affected side, expressive dysphasia. |  |\n> |  | TEMPORAL LOBE: deafness | receptive |\n> | . | dysphasia, auditory illusions and hallucinations, |  |\n> | Middle cerebellar artery | contralateral superior quadrantanopia. |  |\n> |  | PARIETAL LOBE: loss of sensory discrimination, |  |\n> |  | hemineglect, apraxia, contralateral inferior |  |\n> |  | quadrantanopia (RECALL) |  |\n\n- A. Posterior cerebellar artery\n- **B. Anterior communicating artery** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Anterior cerebellar artery\n- D. Internal carotid artery\n- E. Middle cerebral artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBerry (saccular) aneurysms are the most common type of intracranial aneurysm, representing 90% of cerebral aneurysms. The most frequent location is the anterior communicating artery (35%), followed by the internal carotid artery\n\nHigh Yield: Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 55-year-old male presents with numbness and tingling along the lateral border of his left foot after receiving a posterior tibial nerve block after he sustained heal injury. Which nerve is most likely affected, leading to his symptoms?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Tibial nerve, Common | Lower posterolateral leg, heel and |\n> |  | fibular nerve | lateral foot (Little toe) |\n> | Medial calcaneal | Tibial nerve | Skin over the heel |\n> | nerve |  |  |\n> | Medial and lateral | Tibial nerve | Medial plantar: Innervates the plantar |\n> | plantar nerves |  | surface of the medial three and a half digits, |\n> |  |  | and the associated sole area. |\n> |  |  | Lateral plantar: lateral one and a half digits, |\n> |  |  | and the associated sole area. |\n\n- A. Tibial nerve\n- B. Common peroneal nerve\n- **C. Sural nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Superficial peroneal nerve\n- E. Medial plantar nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nComplications of posterior tibial nerve block\n\nSural nerve injury: The sural nerve is at risk due to its proximity to the posterior tibial nerve.\n\nNerve damage or neuropathy Infection Bleeding or hematoma Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST)\n\nAllergic reactions Nerve entrapment Sensory branches of tibial nerve:\n\nClinical pearls: Damage to the tibial nerve causes loss of plantar flexion of the foot and impaired inversion. This presents with difficulty in getting the heel off the ground and a shuffling gait, resulting in characteristic clawing of the toes and sensory loss on the sole of the foot, affecting posture and locomotion.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 20 years old young boy after assault in the college presents to the ED with superficial lacerated wound on his anterior triangle of neck. Which of the following muscle is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Suprahyoid\n- B. Scalene muscle\n- C. Sternocleidomastoid\n- **D. Platysma** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Trapezius muscle\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeck fascia is divided into superficial and deep fascia Superficial fascia includes: Superficial veins (External jugular), superficial lymph nodes, fat and platysma muscle Deep fascia includes: Trapezius, sternocleidomastoid, Visceras of neck and vasculature includes common carotid artery, internal jugular vein and vagus nerve in the carotid sheath.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 25-year-old athlete suffers a fibular fracture and experiences deep fibular nerve injury. Which movement is most likely lost due to this nerve damage?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | * Origin: Lateral surface of |  |  |\n> |  | tibia |  |  |\n> | Tibialis anterior |  Insertion: Medial |  degrees eine |  |\n> |  |  | inversion of foot | nerve |\n> |  | cuneiform and base of |  |  |\n> |  | Metatarsal 1 |  |  |\n> |  | Origin: Medial surface of |  |  |\n> |  |  |  Extension of great |  |\n> | Extensor hallucis | fibular shaft |  | Deep fibular |\n> |  |  | toe and dorsiflexion |  |\n> | longus | Insertion: Distal phalanx |  | nerve |\n> |  |  | of foot |  |\n> |  | of the great toe. |  |  |\n> |  | Origin: Lateral condyle of |  |  |\n> |  | tibia and medial surface of |  |  |\n> |  |  | Extension of lateral |  |\n> |  Extensor digitorum | fibula |  | Deep fibular |\n> |  |  | four toes and |  |\n> | longus | Insertion: splits into 4 |  | nerve |\n> |  |  | dorsiflexion of foot |  |\n> |  | tendons inserts onto each |  |  |\n> |  | toe |  |  |\n> |  | Origin: Medial surface of |  |  |\n> |  Fibularis tertius | fibular shaft |  |  |\n> |  | . . | eversion of foot | nerve |\n> |  |  Insertion: Fifth metatarsal |  |  |\n\n- **A. Dorsiflexion and inversion** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Plantarflexion and eversion\n- C. Dorsiflexion and adduction\n- D. Plantarflexion and inversion\n- E. Eversion and hip flexion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMuscles in the anterior compartment of leg:\n\nAnterior leg consists of four muscles\n\n3: Deep fibular nerve:\n\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in foot drop. In foot drop foot will appear permanently plantarflexed, foot drop with high-stepping gait, weakness of foot inversion.\n\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes.\n\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or common peroneal nerve or also due to deep fibular nerve injury.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 26-Year-old girl presents to the ED with trauma to the internal auditory meatus. Which of the following nerves are most likely injured:\n\n- **A. Vestibulocochlear nerve and facial nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Vestibulocochlear nerve and accessory nerve\n- C. Facial nerve and abducens nerve\n- D. Trochlear nerve and abducens nerve\n- E. Facial nerve and glossopharyngeal nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe internal auditory meatus also referred to as the internal acoustic meatus lies in the temporal bone and exists between the inner ear and posterior cranial fossa. It includes the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), facial nerve (CN VII), the labyrinthineartery, and the vestibular ganglion.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 50-year-old male known case of hypertension presents to the ED with complaint of vertigo, ataxia, ptosis, and miosis. Which of the following blood vessel is most likely occluded?\n\n- A. Pontine artery\n- **B. Posterior inferior cerebral artery** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Middle cerebral artery\n- D. Superior cerebral artery\n- E. Anterior inferior cerebral artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPosterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion result in type of stroke called Lateral medullary syndrome or Wallenberg's syndrome characterized by variety of symptoms which include:\n\nVertigo, vomiting, nystagmus Crossed sensory disturbance Ipsilateral Horner sign Dysphagia, dysarthria Ipsilateral ataxia\n\nOcclusion of AICA is considered rare, but generally results in a lateral pontine syndrome, also known as AICA syndrome\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 40 years old male presents into the ED with a complaint of being unable to move his jaw. You ask your junior colleague to test the jaw reflex. If jaw reflex is lost which of the following nerve is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Afferent: optic nerve |\n> | Pupillary light reflex |  |\n> |  | Efferent: oculomotor nerve |\n> |  | Afferent: Opthalmic division of |\n> | Corneal reflex | trigeminal nerve |\n> |  | Efferent: Facial nerve(RECALL) |\n> | Jaw reflex | Mandibular division of trigeminal |\n> |  | nerve |\n> |  | Afferent: Glossopharyngeal nerve |\n> | Gag reflex |  |\n> |  | Efferent: Vagus nerve |\n\n- A. Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve\n- B. Facial nerve\n- **C. Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- E. Vagus nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 30 Years old presents into the ED with a complaint of pain in his jaw radiating to his ear. Which of the following nerve is most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Q) Buccal nerve | = Sensory: |\n> |  | cheek(RECALL) |\n> |  | = Sensory fibersinnervate |\n> |  | Anterior part of the auricle |\n> |  | Lateral part of the temple |\n> | Auriculotem poral Nerve | Anterior external meatus |\n> |  | Anterior tympanic membrane |\n> |  | = The parasympathetic root carries |\n> |  | postganglionic fibers to the parotid gland. |\n> | Inferior Alveolar Nerve | * Sensory: |\n> |  | lower teeth and gingiva, skin of lower lip and |\n> |  | chin |\n> |  | = Motor: |\n> |  | Mylohyoid and anterior belly of digastric |\n> | Lingual Nerve |  |\n> |  | Anterior two-thirds of tongue, floor of oral |\n> |  | cavity |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Buccal Nerve\n- B. Inferior alveolar nerve\n- C. Supratrochlear nerve\n- D. Facial nerve\n- **E. Auriculotemporal Nerve** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: Skin of ear lobule is innervated by greater auricular nerve (Sensory branch from cervical plexus) RECALL\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 3:** 1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-B, 5-C, 6-B, 7-E, 8-B, 9-C, 10-D, 11-A, 12-A, 13-B, 14-C, 15-E\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
ca9e0e67-6d4f-44a5-8743-b9ab62eb4e9e	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-04	Quiz 04: Thoracic Cavity, Upper Limb Neurovascular & Trauma Mechanics	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 04\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nWhich one of the following vessels is compressed in thoracic outlet syndrome?\n\n- A. Internal carotid artery\n- B. Vertebral artery\n- **C. Subclavian artery and vein** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Brachiocephalic trunk\n- E. Common carotid artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) refers to the clinical features that arise from compression of the neurovascular bundle within the thoracic outlet.\n\nCompression of brachial plexus Compression of subclavian artery Compression of subclavian vein Structures that pass through the thoracic inlet include: (High Yield)\n\nTrachea and oesophagus Thoracic duct and apices of the lung phrenic nerve vagus nerve Recurrent laryngeal nerves Sympathetic trunks Left and right common carotid arteries.\n\nleft subclavian arteries.\n\nInternal jugular veins Brachiocephalic veins Subclavian veins lymph nodes and lymphatic vessels\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nWhich of the following nerve is damaged in supracondylar fracture?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Radial nerve injury: Loss of | * In Axilla: Proximal humerus fracture |\n> |  | + In spiral groove: Mid shaft humerus fracture (RECALL) |\n> | wrist extension ( RECALL) | i |\n> |  | In forearm (superficial branch): laceration of forearm |\n> |  | + forearm deep branch: Radial head fracture |\n> |  | Proximal median nerve injury at elbow may occur due to |\n> |  | supracondylar fracture |\n> |  | * Loss of flexion of index and middle finger. |\n> |  | Loss of flexion, abduction and opposition of thumb (RECALL) due to |\n> | Median nerve injury | paralysis of thenar muscles and flexor pollicis longus |\n> |  | * Flexion of ring and little finger preserved innervated by ulnar nerve. |\n> |  | (RECALL) |\n> |  | + Hand of benediction: Inability to flex at the MCP and IP joints of the |\n> |  | middle and index fingers (RECALL) |\n> |  | + Froment sign paralysis of adductor pollicis muscle(RECALL) |\n> | Ulnar nerve injury | Partial claw hand: ulnar nerve injury below elbow causes flexion of 4* |\n> |  | and 5* fingers. |\n> |  | + Loss of sensation over lateral aspect of forearm (RECALL) |\n> | Musculocutaneous | + Loss of flexion of arm and loss of flexion and supination of |\n> |  | forearm (RECALL) |\n> | nerve injury |  |\n\n- A. Axillary nerve\n- B. Ulnar nerve\n- **C. Median nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Radial nerve\n- E. Common peroneal\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: Nerve most likely damaged in antecubital fossa is Median nerve.(RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nTransverse thoracic plane lies at the level of which of the following anatomically relations?\n\n- A. 4th intercostal space\n- B. 3rd intercostal space\n- **C. 2nd intercostal space** (Correct Answer)\n- D. 5th intercostal space\n- E. 1st intercostal space\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTransverse thoracic plane lies at the level of 2nd intercostal space and intervertebral disc between T4 & T5\n\nCLAPTRAP is useful mnemonic to remember structures transected by transverse thoracic plane.\n\nC: cardiac plexus L: ligamentum arteriosum A: aortic arch (inner concavity) RECALL P: pulmonary trunk bifurcation T: tracheal bifurcation (carina) RECALL R: right-to-left movement of the thoracic duct (posterior to the esophagus)\n\nA: azygos vein drains into superior vena cava P: pre-vertebral fascia and pre-tracheal fascia end\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 4-year-old girl is brought to the emergency room by her parents. She has been experiencing vomiting and watery diarrhea for the last 12 hours. Her parents mention that several children in her playgroup have also been sick recently after eating at a picnic. What is the most appropriate test to confirm if she has norovirus?\n\n- A. Urine test\n- B. Enzyme immunoassay (EIA)\n- C. Stool culture\n- D. Blood test\n- **E. RT-PCR test** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRT Q-PCR, or Reverse Transcription Quantitative Polymerase Chain Reaction assays are the preferred diagnostic method to detect norovirus. Enzyme Immune Assay tests should not replace RT-qPCR during outbreak investigations; they are not recommended for testing single samples fromsporadic cases.\n\nOther tests like stool culture and blood tests are not useful for diagnosing norovirus.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 28-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after sustaining blunt abdominal trauma from a high-speed motor vehicle accident. During an emergency laparotomy, the surgeon encounters a ligament that attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall. This structure divides the liver into left and right lobes and contains the ligamentum teres in its free edge. Which of the following is the most likely structure being identified?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Falciform ligament | Coronary ligaments |* Triangular ligaments |* Lesser omentum |  |  |\n> |  | (left and right) | (left and right) |  |\n> | Attaches the anterior | Attach the superior | Attach the superior | * Consists of the |\n> | surface of the liver to | surface of the liver to | surface of the liver to the | hepatoduodenal ligament |\n> | the anterior abdominal | the diaphragm. | diaphragm. | (extends from the |\n> | wall. |  |  | duodenum to the liver), |\n> | The free edge of this |  |  | and the hepatogastric |\n> | ligament contains the |  |  | ligament (extends from |\n> | ligamentum teres, a |  |  | the stomach to the liver) |\n> | remnant of the |  |  |  |\n> | umbilical vein |  |  |  |\n\n- **A. Falciform ligament** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Hepatoduodenal ligament\n- C. Round ligament of the liver\n- D. Coronary ligament\n- E. Left triangular ligament\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLigaments of the Liver\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 32-year-old male with a history of RTA presented to ED with Complain of pain in his Right shoulder. X-ray was done which shows anterior shoulder dislocation. Which of the following nerve is most likely damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Deltoid |  | Supraspinatus | Infraspinatus |  |  | Subscapularis |\n> | = Origin: | = Origin: | = Origin: | = Origin: | = Origin: | = Origin: |\n> | + Lateral third of | + oflateral | + Supraspinous fossa | + Infraspinousfossaof | + Uppertwo-thirds of | + Subscapular fossa |\n> | clavicle,acromion, | border of scapula | of scapula | scapula | lateral border of |  |\n> | spine of scapula |  |  |  | scapula | = Insertion |\n> |  | = Insertion | = Insertion | = Insertion |  | + Lessertuberosity of |\n> | = Insertion | + Medial lip of bicipital | + Greatertuberosityof | + Greatertuberosityof | = Insertion | humerus |\n> | + Middle of lateral | groove of humerus | humerus; capsule of | humerus; capsuleof | Greater tuberosity of |  |\n> | surface of shaft |  | shoulder joint | shoulder joint | humerus; capsuleof | Nervesupply |\n> | of humerus |  |  |  |  | + Upper and lower |\n> |  | = Nervesupply | = Nervesupply | = Nervesupply |  | subscapular |\n> | = Nerve supply | + Lower subscapular |  | + Suprascapularnerve | = Nervesupply |  |\n> | + Axillary nerve (C5- | nerve (C6--C7) | (C4, 5, 6) | (C4), 5,6 | + Axillary nerve |  |\n> | C6) |  |  |  | (C4), C5,6 | Function |\n> |  | = Function | = Function | = Function |  | 9 rotates arm |\n> | = Function | + Medially rotatesand | + Abductsarmtoinitial | + Laterallyrotatesarm | = Function | and stabilizes |\n> | + Abducts arm; anterior | adducts arm and | 15 degrees and | and stabilizes | Laterally rotates arm |  |\n> | fibers flex and | stabilizes shoulder | stabilizes shoulder |  | and stabilizes |  |\n> | medially rotate arm; |  | joint |  |  |  |\n> | posterior fibers |  |  |  |  |  |\n> | extend and laterally |  |  |  |  |  |\n> | rotate arm |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Median nerve\n- B. Lower subscapular nerve\n- C. Suprascapular nerve\n- D. Ulnar nerve\n- **E. Axillary nerve** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIntrinsic muscles of the shoulder: Connecting the scapula to the humerus\n\nThere are six muscles in this group the deltoid, teres major, and the four rotator cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, subscapularis, and teres minor).\n\nAnatomical course: (High Yield)\n\nAxillary nerve arises in the axilla passes medially to the surgical neck of the humerus, where it divides into three terminal branches:\n\nAnterior terminal branch: winds around the surgical neck of the humerus and provides motor innervation to the anterior aspect of the deltoid muscle.\n\nPosterior terminal branch: Gives motor innervation to the posterior aspect of the deltoid muscle and teres minor. It also innervates the skin over the inferior part of the deltoid as the upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm.\n\nArticular branch: supplies the glenohumeral joint\n\nClinical Pearls:\n\nAxillary nerve injury is commonly caused by fracture of surgical neck of humerus or inferior dislocation of humerus.\n\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of the arm. (Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but not to a horizontal level).\n\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of the deltoid (the 'regimental badge area').\n\nQuadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery and vein pass through it and can be compressed as a result of trauma, muscle hypertrophy resulting in weakness of deltoid and teres minor.\n\nNote: Axillary nerve injury also results in weakness of deltoid (RECALL) and teres minor and resulting in weakness of abduction and lateral rotation.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students' full neurological examination while assessing dermatomal supply of trunk you ask medical students, what is the dermatome of nipple?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | T1 | Medial antecubital fossa |\n> | T2 | Apex of axilla |\n> | T3 | Third intercostal space |\n> | T4 | Level of nipples |\n> | T5 | Fifth intercostal space |\n> | T6 | Level of xiphisternum |\n> |  | Distributed anteriorly between T6 and |\n> | T7-T9 | T10 dermatomes |\n> | T10 | Level of umbilicus |\n> |  | Midway between umbilicus and inguinal |\n> |  | ligament |\n> | T12 | Midpoint of inguinal ligament |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  Level of xiphisternum |\n> | 110 |  Level of umbilicus |\n> | 112 |  Midpoint of inguinal ligament |\n\n- A. T6\n- B. T3\n- **C. T4** (Correct Answer)\n- D. T8\n- E. T2\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDermatomal supply of trunk:\n\nHigh yield:\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nWhich of the following best describes the course of the great saphenous vein:\n\n- A. Posterior to lateral malleolus\n- **B. Anterior to medial malleolus** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Posterior to medial malleolus\n- D. Anterior to lateral malleolus\n- E. Passes behind posterior malleolus\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe great saphenous vein passes anterior to the medial malleolus, travels up the medial side of the leg, and thigh to pass through the saphenous opening in the deep fascia of thigh, and joins with femoral vein.\n\nSmall saphenous vein passes posterior to lateral malleolus\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nWhich one of the following viscera Iie immediately posterior to the left atrium?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> * Trachea Thoracic vertebral bodies\n>  Azygous veins\n> * Descending aorta\n\n- A. Thymus gland\n- **B. Esophagus** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Subclavian artery\n- D. Arch of aorta\n- E. Trachea\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nEsophagus Iie immediately posterior to the left atrium.\n\nDysphagia megalatriensis, also known as cardiovascular dysphagia is an impairment of swallowing due to esophageal compression from a dilated left atrium.\n\nOesophagus: (High Yield)\n\nFibromuscular tube that transports food from pharynx to stomach, divided into three parts:\n\nCervical: continuous with the oropharynx, commences at the lower border of cricoid cartilage (at level of C5/6) or cricopharyngeus muscle Thoracic: From thoracic aperture (T1) to the oesophageal hiatus (T11)\n\nAbdominal: From oesophageal hiatus and is continuous with the cardia of the stomach at the gastro-oesophageal junction Oesophageal constrictions\n\nThe anatomical relations of the oesophagus give rise to four constrictions, where food/foreign objects are most likely to become impacted.\n\nOesophageal constrictions should not be confused with pathological constrictions.\n\nCervical constriction: due to cricoid cartilage at the level of C5/6 Thoracic constriction: due to aortic arch at the level of T4/5 Abdominal constriction: at oesophageal hiatus at T10/11 vertebral Relations of the oesophagus\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 60-year-old female is admitted to the emergency department with signs of increased intracranial pressure (ICP). you decide to administer 150 mL of 3% hypertonic saline as part of her treatment. How much sodium chloride (NaCl) is present in the 150 mL of 3% hypertonic saline?\n\n- A. 7.5 grams\n- B. 6 grams\n- **C. 4.5 grams** (Correct Answer)\n- D. 2.25 grams\n- E. 3 grams\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n3% hypertonic saline contains 3 grams of sodium chloride (NaCl) per 100 mL.\n\nTo calculate for 150 mL:\n\n3 grams/ 100ml x 150 = 4.5grams\n\nNote:9% Normal Saline contains 0.9 grams of Nacl in 100 ml.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 23-year-old male presents to the ED with a complaint of loss of sensation in the medial half of palm. Regarding ulnar nerve receives its fibers from which of the following nerve roots?\n\n- A. C7-T1\n- B. C7-C8\n- C. C5-C6\n- D. C6-C7\n- **E. C8-T1** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBRANCHES OF LATERAL CORD "LML" 1.Lateral pectoral nerve 2.Musculocutaneous nerve 3.Lateral root of median nerve BRANCHES OF MEDIAL CORD "M4U" 1.Medial pectoral nerve 2.Medial cutaneous nerve of arm 3.Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm 4.Medial root of median nerve 5.Ulnar nerve BRANCHES OF POSTERIOR CORD "ULTRA" 1.Upper subscapular nerve 2.Lower subscapular nerve 3.Thoracodorsal nerve 4.Radial nerve 5.Axillary nerve Way to remember root value of upper limb nerves C3, C4: Supraclavicular nerve C5, C6: Axillary nerve (RECALL)\n\nC5, C6, C7: Musculocutaneous Nerve and long thoracic nerve C6, C7, C8: Thoracodorsal Nerve C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1: Median and Radial Nerve C8-T1: Ulnar nerve\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 29-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department after injuring her left elbow. She is complaining of a weak grip on her left hand. You ask her to hold on to a piece of paper with both hands as you try to pull it away from her. The result of this clinical test is shown in image What nerve is most likely to be damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Froment's Sign |  |\n> | Bending the |  |\n> | -- | Positive |\n> |  | (Ulnar nerve palsy) |\n> |  | joint |\n> |  | Negative |\n> |  | (Normal ulnar |\n> |  | nerve) |\n\n- A. Musculocutaneous\n- B. Radial\n- **C. Ulnar** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Axillary\n- E. Median\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFroment sign caused by damage to the ulnar nerve and paralysis of adductor pollicis muscle (RECALL)\n\nClinical Pearls: (High Yield)\n\nFroment's sign: Froment's sign is a test for ulnar nerve palsy specifically paralysis of the adductor pollicis:\n\nPatient is asked to hold a piece of paper between thumb and index finger as paper is pulled away, patient will flex thumb at inter-phalangeal joint to maintain hold, this sign tests the function of adductor pollicis muscle which is supplied by the ulnar nerve.\n\nA positive test is when the patient is unable to adduct the thumb.\n\nClaw hand:\n\nInjury to the ulnar nerve is commonly caused by a fracture of the medial epicondyle and results in a claw hand, in which the ring and little fingers are hyperextended at the metacarpophalangeal joints and flexed at the interphalangeal joints.\n\nPartial claw hand deformity is due to an isolated ulnar nerve injury.\n\nComplete claw hand deformity: Involves all digits and results from combined ulnar and median nerve injury.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nWhilst in your cardiology rotation you are asked to perform cardiovascular examination. One of your senior registrars asks, what is the best site to auscultate mitral valve?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Second right intercostal space |\n> | Pulmonary valve | Second, left intercostal space |\n> | Tricuspid valve | 5th intercostal space to the lower left |\n> | P | sternal border |\n> |  | Apex, Left 5th ICS in midclavicular |\n> | Mitral valve | line |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | : | Correspondence |\n> |  | Mechanical event | with ECG |\n> | Ist heart sound | Closure of mitral and tricuspid |  QRS Complex |\n> |  | valves |  |\n> |  | Closure of aortic and pulmonary | Twave |\n> |  | valves |  |\n> |  |  Also known as ventricular gallop |  |\n> |  | Caused by rapid flow of blood from |  |\n> | 3rd heart sound | the atria into the ventricles during | Beginning of P |\n> |  | the ventricular filling phase | wave |\n> |  | Can normally be present in |  |\n> |  | children |  |\n> |  Ath heart sound |  degrees | Between P and Q |\n> |  | stiff ventricle in atrial systole |  |\n\n- A. Second right intercostal space\n- **B. Apex of heart** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Right 5th ICS in midclavicular line\n- D. Second left intercostal space\n- E. 5th intercostal space to the lower left sternal border\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAreas of auscultation of heart:\n\nNote: Aortic valve: Second right intercostal space (Recall)\n\nHeart sounds, Mechanical event and correspondence with ECG (High Yield)\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nYou are required to perform resuscitative thoracotomy in a patient with massive hemothorax. Which one of the following muscles are divided during anterolateral thoracotomy?\n\n- A. Pectoralis minor\n- B. Trapezius\n- **C. Serratus anterior** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Anterior scalene\n- E. Latissimus dorsi\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAntero lateral thoracotomy muscle divided serratus anterior and pectoralis major Antero lateral thoracotomy: Nerve injury long thoracic nerve (Recall)\n\nPost: lateral thoracotomy muscle divided latissimus dorsi Post: lateral thoracotomy nerve injury: Thoracodorsal nerve injury Internal mammary artery (RECALL) and Aorta are likely to be damaged also\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nWhilst in your emergency department your consultant asks you to perform chest examination. Righr middle lobe is best auscultated between which of the following ribs?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. Rib 4-6** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Rib 2-4\n- C. Rib 2-3\n- D. Rib 1-2\n- E. Rib 3-4\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe superior lobe of the right lung is best auscultated above the 4th rib anteriorly.\n\nThe middle lobe of the right lung is best auscultated between the 4th and the 6th rib anteriorly. The superior lobe of the left lung is best auscultated above the 6th rib anteriorly.\n\nThe inferior lobes of the left and right lungs are best auscultated in the back between T3 & T10.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 4:** 1-C, 2-C, 3-C, 4-E, 5-A, 6-E, 7-C, 8-B, 9-B, 10-C, 11-E, 12-C, 13-B, 14-C, 15-A\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
a3fc45cf-295d-48bf-92f1-e72ce4416d78	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-aortic-aneurysm	Aortic Aneurysm	quiz	# Aortic Aneurysm\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Aortic Aneurysm\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Aortic Aneurysm**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
b6e70e26-878d-4d36-ba14-d86abbdf5354	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-21	Quiz 21: Biophysics of Flow, Arrhythmia Mechanisms & Critical Care Burns	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 21\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nWhich viral encephalitis causes Hemorrhagic necrosis of temporal lobes?\n\n- A. Enterovirus\n- B. EBV\n- C. Varicella\n- **D. Herpes Simplex** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Rabies\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe characteristic feature of Herpes simplex encephalitis is hemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal lobe. Infection tends to begin unilaterally, only spreading to the contralateral temporal lobe in the late stages of the disease.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 28-year-old pregnant female presents to the obstetric clinic referred by her GP with complaint of glycosuria with repeated and random urine sample. What is the likely cause of glycosuria in pregnancy?\n\n- A. Decreased insulin resistance\n- B. Increased glucagon resistance\n- C. Increased renal threshold for glucose\n- D. Increased reabsorption of glucose from renal tubules\n- **E. Decreased renal threshold for glucose** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGlycosuria is more common during pregnancy because of the lowering of the renal threshold for glucose excretion.\n\nThe increase in the glomerular filtration rate delivers an overwhelming glucose load to the renal tubules. Reabsorption, which is normally complete, is thus compromised.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nYou are teaching group of nursing students flow of fluids through an iv cannula. You ask students if we double the radius of cannula, how will it affect rate of flow through cannula?\n\n- A. Decrease 16-fold\n- B. Increase 8-fold\n- **C. Increase 16-fold** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Decreases 4 times\n- E. Increased 4-fold\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFlow rate is directly proportional to radius 4 (r 4)\n\nTubing diameter: An important and frequently cited relationship is that of the tubing's radius. Doubling the radius of a catheter increases the flow rate by 16-fold (r4). The larger the IV catheter the greater the flow.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nAn 87-year-old woman from a residential home with a 2-day history of diarrhea. She has had 10 episodes of watery diarrhea but no vomiting. She complains of colicky lower abdominal pain. She had a 5-day course of antibiotics for a chest infection 2 weeks ago. After initial assessment diagnosis of clostridium colitis was made. Which of the following is the best way to prevent spread by C.difficle to others?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> Clostridium difficile Gram +Ve anerobic\n> bacterium transmitted\n> by fecal-oral route\n> e Alcohol gel is not\n> effective against\n> spores of\n> Clostridium difficile\n> e Use soap and\n> water\n\n- A. Wash hand with sterile water\n- **B. Wash hand with soap and water** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Wash hand with alcohol gel\n- D. Antibiotic treatment\n- E. Wash hand with sanitizer\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nWhich one of the following myocardial cell structures is responsible for the conduction of action potential in cardiac cells?\n\n- A. Sarcoplasmic reticulum\n- B. Intercalated disc\n- **C. Gap junctions** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Sarcomere\n- E. T-tubules\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGap Junctions: Allow for rapid electrical spread o f action potential T-tubules: Carry action potential into the cell interior\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 70-year-old man presents into the ED with complaint of frequent falls after standing up from sitting position. Postural hypotension occurs due to which mechanism?\n\n- A. Chronic venous insufficiency\n- **B. Impaired baroreceptor reflex** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Patient treated on Sympathomimetic agent\n- D. Impaired Chemoreceptor reflex\n- E. Peripheral pooling of blood in old age\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOrthostatic hypotension (Postural hypotension): fainting or light-headedness on standing may occur in individuals whose baroreceptor reflex mechanism is impaired (e.g., individuals treated with sympatholytic agents) or who are volume depleted.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nWhilst performing venipuncture you apply torniquet and ask medical students what is the cause of reactive hyperemia after torniquet removal?\n\n- A. Adrenaline\n- B. Vasoconstriction\n- C. Increased PaO2\n- D. Inflammation\n- **E. Adenosine** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nReactive hyperemia is the transient increase in organ blood flow. Reactive hyperemia occurs following the removal of a tourniquet, unclamping an artery during surgery, the hyperemia occurs because during the period of occlusion, tissue hypoxia and a buildup of vasodilator metabolites (e.g., adenosine) dilate arterioles and decrease vascular resistance. Then when perfusion pressure is restored (i.e., occlusion released), flow becomes elevated because of the reduced vascular resistance. During the hyperemia, the tissue becomes reoxygenated and vasodilator metabolites are washed out of the tissue.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 30-year-old female presented to the ED with Complain of Palpitations, tremors, and restlessness, her observations include a temperature is 39  degreesC, irregular pulse, and blood pressure 155/85 mmHg She has a history of hyperthyroidism but is uncompliant with treatment.\n\nWhat is the best initial management plan?\n\n- A. Lugol iodine\n- B. Propylthiouracil\n- C. Carbimazole\n- D. Verapamil\n- **E. Propanolol** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTreatment of thyroid storm consists of supportive measures like intravenous (IV) fluids, oxygen, cooling blankets, and acetaminophen, as well as specific measures to treat hyperthyroidism. After initial supportive measures, a beta-blocker e.g. propranolol should be started for any case of suspected thyroid storm.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 70 years old male brought into the ED with complaint of lethargy and generalized ecchymoses. He is known to be alcoholic. Lab values reveal platelets 20000, TLC count 20000, increased PT, increased APTT and low fibrinogen concentration. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Hemophilia | Normal PT |\n> |  | Prolonged APTT |\n> |  | Low factor Viii |\n> |  | Normal platelets |\n> | Von | Normal PT |\n> | Willibrand | Prolonged APTT |\n> | disease | Low factor Viii |\n> |  | Low VWF level |\n> |  | Normal platelets |\n\n- A. Von Willibrand disease\n- B. Acute fulminant hepatic failure\n- **C. Disseminated intravascular coagulation** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura\n- E. Septic shock syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLab values of Disseminated intravascular\n\nLow platelet count Low fibrinogen concentration Prolonged PT Prolonged PT Prolonged APTT High levels of D-Dimers Features of hemolysis\n\nHigh yield:\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nRegarding the DVLA guidelines of diabetes, which one of the following statements is true?\n\n- A. Patient on oral hypoglycemic agents must not drive\n- B. Drivers whose diabetes is treated with insulin do not drive with a blood glucose less than 10mmol/l\n- C. Drivers whose diabetes is treated with insulin can drive safely without notifying DVLA\n- D. People who are on temporary insulin treatment should stop driving\n- **E. Drivers whose diabetes is treated with insulin do not drive with a blood glucose less than 5mmol/l** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPrecautions advised for people with diabetes treated with insulin:\n\nDo not drive with a blood glucose less than 5mmol/l.\n\nPeople on Oral Hypoglycaemic Agents (OHA's) (Must notify DVLA)\n\nFor people on oral hypoglycaemic agents a license lasting until age 70 will be issued in the absence of other complications.\n\nPeople on Insulin (Must notify DVLA)\n\nDrivers whose diabetes is treated with insulin will be issued with a 1-, 2- or 3-year license, provided they meet the set criteria. DVLA have now stipulated that any driver on insulin who has more than 2 episodes of severe hypoglycemia (including nocturnal) in a 12-month interval should be advised not to drive and to inform the DVLA. Severe hypoglycemia is classed as any episode of hypoglycemia that requires the assistance of a third party for management. It is likely these individuals will have their license revoked for a period of 12 months.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nRegarding the measurement of glomerular filtrate rate, why creatinine is being used?\n\n- A. Creatinine is not filtered by nephron\n- B. Not freely filtered but freely secreted\n- C. Not freely filtered and not secreted by nephron\n- D. Creatinine is freely filtered and secreted by nephron\n- **E. Creatinine is freely filtered and not secreted by nephron** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nClearance of a substance can provide an accurate estimate of the glomerular filtration rate (GFR)\n\nCreatinine is used to calculate GFR because:\n\nFreely filtered Not reabsorbed in the nephron Not secreted in the nephron Not synthesized or metabolized by the kidney\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 55-year-old man with hypertension is prescribed an antiarrhythmic agent that alters the flow of cations in myocardial tissue. The image is a trace of a myocardial action potential. Each phase is associated with the opening and/ or closing of various ion channels. Which of the following would be affected by an agent that affects phase 1 of the myocardial action potential?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  |  |  | Plateau phase: Slow L- |  |  |\n> |  | inactivate, transient |  |  | type Ca2+ channels |  |  |\n> |  | K+ channels open and |  |  | activate and a rapid |  |  |\n> |  | K+ efflux returns |  |  | influx of Ca2+ is |  |  |\n> |  | membrane potential \\ |  | a balanced by ongoing K+ |  |  |  |\n> |  | toomv |  |  | efflux through delayed |  |  |\n> |  |  | 1 | 2 | rectifier K+ channels |  |  |\n> |  |  |  |  |  |  | Ca2+ channels close |\n> |  |  |  |  |  |  | but delayed rectifier |\n> |  |  |  |  |  |  | K+ channels remain |\n> | Fast Nat channels |  |  |  |  |  | open, ongoing K+ |\n> | activate andarapid |  |  |  |  | 4 efflux returns resting |  |\n> | Na+ influx depolarises |  |  |  |  |  | membrane potential |\n> | membrane potential |  |  |  |  | 3 |  |\n> | to about + 30 mv |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | Na+ and Ca2+ channels |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | closed, open K+ |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | rectifier channels |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | keep resting |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | membrane potential |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | stable at -90 mV |  |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Ligand-gated Ca2+ channels opening\n- B. Voltage-gated Na+ channels opening\n- C. Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels opening\n- **D. Voltage-gated Na+ channels closing** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Ligand-gated Na+ channels opening\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 4-year-old African child with sickle cell disease was brought into the ED with complaint of lethargy, and pain in the left upper quadrant. On examination, spleen is enlarged. Lab findings revealed anemia and reticulocytosis. What is the most likely complication child has developed?\n\n- **A. RBC sequestration** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Pulmonary hypertension\n- C. Vasoo active crises\n- D. Cholecystitis\n- E. Aplastic crises\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAcute Splenic Sequestration\n\nSudden trapping of RBC within the spleen Usually occurs in infants under 2 years of age Spleen enlarged on physical exam Circulatory collapse and death can occur\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 20-week pregnant lady presents to the ED with exposure to chicken pox. What is your next step in management?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Attend to antenatal clinic\n- B. Immediate reinforcing of vaccine\n- **C. Check her antibody status** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Immediate reinforcing of immunoglobulin\n- E. No action required\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nKey Points:\n\nIf you have already had chickenpox, you have nothing to worry about.\n\nOnce you have had chickenpox you cannot catchit a second time, this is known as being immune. Nine out of ten UK adults are immune to chicken pox.\n\nChickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in th e UK.\n\nIt affects very few babies in the womb and although it can be very serious in pregnancy especistally in 1 trimester in first 12 weeks (Recall) If you have been in contact with chickenpox and are not sure if you are immune, contact your GP or midwife and not to attend the antenatal clinic. If you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other pregnant women and new babies until at least five days after the rash appears, or until all the blisters have crusted over.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 39-year-old female patient is brought into ED following an explosion after rescuing her dog from a house fire. She has full-thickness burn all over the trunk. In burn patient's distributive shock occurs due to which mechanism?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Decrease intracellular fluid osmolality\n- B. Decreased interstitial fluid\n- C. Increase in intracellular fluid\n- **D. Increased interstitial fluid** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Increase extra cellular fluid osmolarity\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBurns initially cause capillary leakage resulting in severe hypovolemia and massive edema (increased interstitial fluid)\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 21:** 1-D, 2-E, 3-C, 4-B, 5-C, 6-B, 7-E, 8-E, 9-C, 10-E, 11-E, 12-D, 13-A, 14-C, 15-D\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
7de971b1-b6d4-4b10-bd67-64e4a9e329b5	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	venous-drainage-upper-limb	Venous drainage of the upper limb	article	## Superficial veins\n\nThey run in the subcutaneous tissue and are the veins you use clinically.\n\n- **Dorsal venous network** on the back of the hand drains laterally into the **cephalic vein** and medially into the **basilic vein**.\n- **Cephalic vein:** runs up the lateral side, along the deltopectoral groove, and drains into the axillary vein.\n- **Basilic vein:** runs up the medial side, pierces the deep fascia in the arm and joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein.\n- **Median cubital vein:** connects the cephalic to the basilic vein in the cubital fossa. It lies over the bicipital aponeurosis, which separates it from the brachial artery and median nerve. It is the usual site for venepuncture because of this protection.\n\n## Deep veins\n\n- Deep and superficial palmar venous arches accompany the arterial arches.\n- In the forearm and arm, the deep veins follow the arteries as paired venae comitantes.\n- The brachial veins (comitantes of the brachial artery) are joined by the basilic vein at the lower border of teres major to form the **axillary vein**.\n- The axillary vein runs medial to the axillary artery and becomes the **subclavian vein** at the outer border of the first rib.\n\n## Exam points\n\n- Common sites for blood sampling: median cubital vein first, then cephalic and basilic.\n- The bicipital aponeurosis is the key landmark protecting the brachial artery during venepuncture.\n- A PICC line is usually threaded through the basilic vein or brachial vein, because it is the larger one.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
16848f9f-c546-4370-8372-a1e3214d31cb	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-22	Quiz 22: Hematological Malignancies, Bone Healing & Surgical Anatomy	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 22\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 26-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of fever, fatigue, and sore throat. He further stated that few days back his wife was diagnosed with infectious mononucleosis. Which of the following cells are the most predominant?\n\n- **A. Lymphocytes** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Neutrophils\n- C. Eosinophils\n- D. Basophils\n- E. Monocytes\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn infectious mononucleosis (glandular fever caused by EBV), the characteristic cells seen on blood film are atypical lymphocytes (activated CD8+ T-cells). These reactive lymphocytes appear large with abundant basophilic cytoplasm, and the monospot test is positive. This is distinct from neutrophilia (bacterial infections), eosinophilia (allergy/parasites), or the Reed-Sternberg cells of Hodgkin's lymphoma.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nWhilst in your rheumatology rotation you are taking the history of a suspected SLE patient. Your consultant asks which of the following antibody is always high and the most sensitive diagnostic test to confirm SLE.\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> antibody\n> factor\n> Anti-gliadin disease\n> and\n> endomysial\n> antibodies\n> Acetylcholine | Myasthenia gravis\n> receptor\n> antibodies\n> Anti- Good pastures syndrome\n> glomerular\n> basement\n> antibody\n> (Anti GBM)\n\n- A. Rheumatoid factor\n- B. Anti gliadin antibody\n- **C. Antinuclear antibody** (Correct Answer)\n- D. C-Anca\n- E. P-Anca\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAntinuclear antibody (ANA) is the screening test for SLE (systemic lupus erythematosus) with ~95% sensitivity. Anti-dsDNA is more specific but less sensitive. ANCA is associated with vasculitides (GPA/MPA). Rheumatoid factor is for RA. Anti-CCP is specific for RA. ANA is the first-line screening test for SLE.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\n10 years old boy with an allergy to nuts was brought into ED with complain of widespread urticarial rash associated with breathlessness. You suspect anaphylaxis, which one of the following cells is responsible for that?\n\n- A. Monocytes\n- B. Neutrophils\n- C. Basophils\n- **D. Mast cells** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Eosinophils\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMast cells are the primary effector cells in anaphylaxis (Type I IgE-mediated hypersensitivity). On allergen re-exposure, cross-linking of IgE bound to mast cell FcRI receptors triggers degranulation, releasing histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4), and prostaglandins causing vasodilation, bronchoconstriction, and increased vascular permeability.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nRegarding the attachment of metatarsal bone, which one of the following metatarsal attaches to lateral coniform?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | * Navicular posteriorly |\n> | Lateral cuneiform | * Cuboid laterally |\n> |  | 3rd metatarsal anteriorly |\n> | Intermediate cuneiform | * Navicular posteriorly |\n> |  | 2nd metatarsal anteriorly |\n> | Medial cuneiform | * Navicular posteriorly |\n> |  | * 1st metatarsal anteriorly |\n\n- A. 4th metatarsal\n- B. 2nd metatarsal\n- **C. 3rd metatarsal** (Correct Answer)\n- D. 1st metatarsal\n- E. 5th metatarsal\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe 3rd metatarsal articulates with the intermediate (middle) cuneiform at its base. The cuboid articulates with the 4th and 5th metatarsals. The medial cuneiform articulates with the 1st metatarsal, and the lateral cuneiform with the 3rd metatarsal's lateral facet and 4th metatarsal proximally. However, the direct articulation of the 3rd metatarsal base is with the lateral cuneiform (medially) and intermediate cuneiform (centrally).\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 20 years old young female with severe iron deficiency anemia HB:4 presents to the ED with complaints of lethargy and pallor. After giving a blood transfusion patient developed a hypersensitivity reaction what type of reaction is this?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | mediated |  |  | mediated |\n> | Urticaria | Red blood | Post-streptococcal | Mostly |\n> | Angioedema| cell |  | glomerulonephritis | T |  |\n> | Allergic | destruction |  | Contact |\n> | rhinitis | after | Rheumatoid | (Touch) |\n> | Hay fever | transfusion | (Recall) | dermatitis |\n> | Anaphylaxis | with |  | systemic lupus | (Recall) |\n> |  |  | erythematosus | Tuberculin |\n> |  | blood |  | skin test |\n> |  | (Recall) or |  | Type 1 |\n> |  | during |  | diabetes |\n> |  | hemolytic |  | Transplant |\n> |  | disease of |  | rejection |\n> |  | the |  |  |\n> |  | newborn. |  |  |\n\n- **A. Type 2** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Type 4\n- C. type 1 hypersensitivity 2\n- D. Type 5\n- E. Type 3\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHaemolytic transfusion reaction is a classic example of Type II (cytotoxic) hypersensitivity. Pre-formed IgM or IgG antibodies in the recipient bind to ABO antigens on donor RBCs, activating complement and causing intravascular haemolysis. This is antibody-mediated cytotoxicity -- the defining feature of Type II hypersensitivity.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 60-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of fatigue. Lab value reveals anemia, Lymphocytosis, and thrombocytopenia. Which one of the following malignancies is most common in UK?\n\n- A. CML\n- B. Hodgkin lymphoma\n- C. ALL\n- D. AML\n- **E. CLL** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nChronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL) is the most common leukaemia in adults in the UK, particularly in the elderly (median age at diagnosis ~70 years). It accounts for ~40% of adult leukaemias. CLL is a B-cell malignancy presenting with lymphocytosis, lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 52-year-old woman presents with a hoarse voice two weeks after neck surgery for a thyroid adenoma.\n\nWhich structure is most likely to have been injured during her surgery?\n\n- A. Superior laryngeal nerve\n- **B. Recurrent laryngeal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Cricothyroid muscle\n- D. Thyrohyoid muscle\n- E. Vagus nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN), a branch of the vagus nerve, is at risk during thyroidectomy. Unilateral injury causes hoarseness (ipsilateral vocal cord palsy). Bilateral injury causes stridor and airway obstruction. The RLN supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid (external branch of superior laryngeal nerve).\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 45-year-old man fell from a rooftop and presented with a head injury and hyperventilation. What could be the consequence of hyperventilation?\n\n- **A. Decrease in cerebral blood flow** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Increased in Pao2\n- C. Increase in Pco2\n- D. Decrease washout of Pco2\n- E. Increase in cerebral blood flow\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHyperventilation causes hypocapnia (low pCO2), which leads to cerebral vasoconstriction via reduced carbonic acid and alkalosis. This decreases cerebral blood flow. This is the physiological basis for using controlled hyperventilation as a temporising measure in raised intracranial pressure (ICP) -- but it can worsen cerebral ischaemia if prolonged.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 15 years old male known case of sickle cell disease with a history of splenectomy 1 year back presented to the ED with complaint of bloody diarrhea. What is the most likely causative agent?\n\n- **A. E-Coli** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Hemophilus influenza\n- C. Staph aureus\n- D. Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- E. Neisseria meningitides\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nE. coli (particularly EHEC O157:H7) is the leading cause of bloody diarrhoea (haemorrhagic colitis) and haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS). Post-splenectomy patients are particularly susceptible to encapsulated organisms, but E. coli causing bloody diarrhoea is a distinct epidemiological concern in this scenario.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nWhich one of the following stages of bone healing is followed by inflammation and proliferation?\n\n- **A. Callus formation** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Organization\n- C. Consolidation\n- D. Hematoma formation\n- E. Remodeling\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCallus formation is the 3rd stage of fracture healing, following: 1) Haematoma formation (hours-days), 2) Inflammatory phase (days), 3) Soft callus/proliferative phase (weeks -- periosteal cells and fibroblasts proliferate), 4) Hard callus (woven bone formation), 5) Remodelling. Callus formation follows the inflammatory phase.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 24-year female with a history of C-section2 weeks back presents to the ED came with complaint of delayed wound healing. What is the most likely cause?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Causes of | e Frequent trauma, Tissue hypoxia and |\n> | decreased wound | Infection |\n> | healing | Others include: Increased age, |\n> |  | immunocompromised and deficiency of vit |\n> |  | A, Cand zinc |\n\n- A. Collagen decrease in pregnancy\n- B. In this age wound heals slowly\n- C. Increased collagen activity\n- **D. Infection on the site** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Reduced fibroblast\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInfection is the most common cause of delayed wound healing. It causes persistent inflammation, tissue destruction by bacterial proteases, and impairs normal wound healing progression. Other causes include ischaemia, poor nutrition (especially Vitamin C deficiency), diabetes, and corticosteroid use.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nWhich one of the following is not a notifiable disease in the UK?\n\n- A. Covid-19\n- B. Small pox\n- C. Malaria\n- **D. Chicken pox** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Measles\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nChickenpox (varicella) is NOT a notifiable disease in England (though it is in Scotland). Notifiable diseases in England include: meningococcal septicaemia, measles, mumps, rubella, TB, cholera, typhoid, etc. Chickenpox is excluded because it is extremely common and the notification would be impractical.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 5-month-old baby with signs of meningitis was brought into the ED. Her mother states that the baby is up to date with immunization schedule. Which one of the following vaccines are not given?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Rotavirus | 2 and 3 months |\n> | Hepatitis B | 2, 3, and 4 months |\n> | Hib | 2, 3, 4and 12 months (Recall) |\n> | Pneumococcal | 3 and 12 months |\n> | Diphtheria, polio and tetanus | 2, 3, 4months, preschool and 14 years |\n> | MMR | 12 months and preschool (Recall) |\n> | MenC | 12 months |\n> | MenACWY | 14 years |\n\n- A. Hep-B\n- B. Rota virus\n- C. Polio\n- **D. Men-C** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Diphtheria\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe UK immunisation schedule includes MenC vaccine at 12-13 months (not 5 months). The 5-month schedule includes: 6-in-1 (3rd dose), MenB (2nd dose), rotavirus (2nd dose). At 12-13 months: MMR (1st dose), MenB (booster), Hib/MenC. At 5 years: 4-in-1 pre-school booster, MMR (2nd dose).\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 27-year-old male presents to the ED with a superficial wound on his right thigh. Your senior registrar decided to let it heal with primary intention. After 2 days which one of the following cells will be predominant?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | inflammation |  |\n> | immunity |  |\n> | Neutrophil | Macrophages and |\n> | predominant | lymphocytes |  |\n> |  | predominant |\n> |  | Note: The granulomatous |\n> |  | inflammatory response is |\n> |  | a special type of chronic |\n> |  | inflammation |\n> |  | characterized by often |\n> |  | focal collections of |\n> |  | macrophages, epithelioid |\n> |  | cells and multinucleated |\n> |  | giant cells (Recall) |\n\n- A. Macrophages\n- B. Lymphocytes\n- C. Fibroblasts\n- **D. Neutrophils** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Eosinophils\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeutrophils are the predominant cells in the early inflammatory phase of wound healing (day 1-3). They arrive first (within hours) to phagocytose bacteria and debris. Macrophages replace neutrophils by day 3-5 and orchestrate subsequent repair. Lymphocytes arrive later in the proliferative phase.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 62-year-old man presents with a stab wound to his scalp. The wound appears superficial but it is bleeding profusely\n\nWhat pathway is most important for the primary haemostasis of this wound?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Activation of Protein C\n- B. Conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin\n- C. Conversion of prothrombin to thrombin\n- D. Activation of Protein S\n- **E. Activation of von Willebrand factor** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nvon Willebrand factor (vWF) is essential for primary haemostasis -- it bridges between collagen at the site of vascular injury and platelet GPIb receptors, enabling platelet adhesion. vWF also carries and protects Factor VIII from premature degradation. vWD (von Willebrand Disease) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
90e2c1d7-a94a-4585-bce2-6839d24ca9ac	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-23	Quiz 23: Gastrointestinal Secretions, Pancreatic Enzymes & Lower Limb Blocks	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 23\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 45-year-old male known case of diabetes mellitus was brought into the ED with an altered level of unconsciousness. After initial assessment, his BM was 2.0. you decided to give him 1mg IM glucagon. What is the mechanism of action of glucagon in hypoglycemia?\n\n- A. Promote glycogen formation\n- B. Glucogenolysis\n- C. Decrease glucose release\n- **D. Glycogenolysis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Glycogenesis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGlucagon released from alpha cells of pancreas promotes hepatic conversion of glycogen to glucose (glycogenolysis), stimulates glucose synthesis (gluconeogenesis), and inhibits glucose breakdown (glycolysis) and glycogen formation (glycogenesis)\n\nInsulin is released from the beta cells of pancreas. insulin act on tyrosine kinase receptor to activate intracellular pathway (Recall) result in translocation of glut-4 glucose transporter to the cell membrane.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 12-year-old Mediterranean child was brought into the ED with complaint of lethargy and growth retardation. On examination of the abdomen hepatosplenomegaly was found. Which one of the following hypochromic microcytic anemias is the most likely the cause of his condition?\n\n- A. Anemia of chronic disease\n- B. Hemolytic anemia\n- C. Iron deficiency anemia\n- **D. Thalassemia** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Sideroblastic anemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSign and symptoms of Beta thalassemia major: Pallor, fatigue, irritability 2 Growth retardation.\n\nRecurrent infections Bony abnormalities specially of the facial bones Enlarged spleen and liver.\n\nDelayed sexual development\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 25-year-old man presents with an incised wound to the antecubital fossa. There is a complete transection of the brachial artery at this level.\n\nWhich artery is most likely to remain intact?\n\n- **A. Profunda brachii** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Deep palmar arch\n- C. Ulnar\n- D. Radial\n- E. Superficial palmar arch\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nImmediately distal to the teres major, the brachial artery gives rise to the profunda brachii (deep artery), which travels with the radial nerve in the radial groove of the humerus and supplies structures in the posterior aspect of the upper arm (e. g., triceps brachii). Incised wound at antecubital fossa and complete transection of brachial artery at this level will spare deep branch of brachial artery that is profunda brachii.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nYou are discussing facial nerve and their branches with a group of medical students. Which one of the following facial nerve branches is involved in the Wrinkling of the forehead?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Facial nerve branches | nerve terminal |\n> | in temporal bone | branches in the parotid |\n> |  | gland |\n> | Chorda tympani: Ant Temporal branch: innervate |  |\n> | 2/3 of taste (RECALL) | muscles in the temple, |  |\n> | While Ant 2/3 of | forehead (Frontalis muscle) |\n> | general sensation: | and supraorbital areas |\n> | mandibular division of (upper half of orbicularis |  |\n> | trigeminal nerve | oculi muscle) |\n> |  | Occipitofrontalis muscle: |\n> |  | Frontal belly: Elevates |\n> |  | eyebrows, wrinkles skin of |\n> |  | forehead innervated by |\n> |  | temporal branch |\n> |  | Occipital belly: Retracts |\n> |  | scalp innervated by |\n> |  | posterior auricular nerve |\n> | Greater petrosal | zygomatic branch: |\n> | nerve: | innervate muscles in the |\n> | Parasympathetic | infraorbital area (Lower |\n> | innervation to lacrimal half of orbicularis oculi |  |\n> | and mucous glands | muscle) Recall lateral nasal |\n> |  | area and upper lip |\n> |  | Buccal branch: muscles in |\n> |  | the cheek and upper lip |\n> |  | (Recall) |\n> |  | Marginal mandibular |\n> |  | branch: innervate muscles |\n> |  | of lower lip and chin |\n\n- A. Buccal branch\n- B. Greater petrosal nerve\n- C. Zygomatic branch\n- **D. Temporal branch of facial nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Posterior auricular nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nYou prescribed H2 receptor blocker to a patient with duodenal ulcer. your junior fellow asks, what is the effect of H2 blockers on HCL secretion ?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> intrinsic factor\n> (Recall)\n\n- **A. Decrease gastric acid** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Increase gastric acid\n- C. Increase gastric acid acting on chief cells of stomach\n- D. Decrease gastric acid acting on G-cells\n- E. Decrease gastric acid acting on chief cells of stomach\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nH2 blocker decrease gastric acid secretion by reversibly binding to histamine H2 receptors located on gastric parietal cells, thereby inhibiting the binding and activation of the endogenous ligand histamine\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 55 years old male known case of HTN and CKD presents with complain of easy fatigability and pale skin. Lab values reveal HB: 8 and decreased level of hematocrit, Peripheral film shows normocytic normochromic anemia. What is the most likely cause?\n\n- A. Increase erythropoietin\n- B. Anemia of chronic disease\n- **C. Decrease erythropoietin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Hemolytic anemia\n- E. Sideroblastic anemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn patients with chronic kidney disease, normochromic normocytic anemia mainly develops from decreased renal synthesis of erythropoietin.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nWhich one of the following muscles of thigh is responsible for adduction of thigh at hip joint and flexion of leg at knee joint?\n\n- **A. Gracilis** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Adductor longus\n- C. Sartorius\n- D. Rectus femoris\n- E. Adductor magnus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMotor supply: Medial thigh muscles (adductor longus, brevis and magnus, gracilis, obturator externus) Sensory supply Upper medial thigh Gracilis: Adducts the thigh at hip joint and flexes leg at knee joint\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nRegarding the metabolism of carbohydrates. Which pancreatic enzyme causes digestion of carbohydrates?\n\n- A. Chymotrypsin\n- **B. Amylase** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Trypsin\n- D. Maltase\n- E. Lipase\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDigestion of carbohydrates start from mouth with salivary amylase but primarily takes place in small intestine by action of pancreatic enzyme amylase\n\nFat digestion in small intestine by pancreatic enzyme lipase\n\nProtein digestion in small intestines by pancreatic enzyme Trypsin and Chymotrypsin\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nYou prescribed proton pump inhibitor to a patient with duodenal ulcer. Regarding pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease which one of the following statements is correct?\n\n- A. Thick gastric mucosa formation\n- **B. Decrease prostaglandin synthesis** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Increased prostaglandin synthesis\n- D. Decrease gastrin secretion\n- E. Decreased Hcl secretion from gastric parietal cells\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe gastrointestinal mucosa contains relatively large amounts of prostaglandin. Prostaglandins a re important in the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease and possibly in its prevention and treatment as well. Prostaglandins have been shown to inhibit gastric secretion, stimulate bicarbonate secretion, and increase gastric blood volume.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 60-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis attends with symptoms of malabsorption.\n\nA decrease in which enzyme is most likely to be affecting protein digestion?\n\n- A. Esterase\n- B. lipase\n- C. Amylase\n- D. lactase\n- **E. Trypsin** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDigestion of carbohydrates start from mouth with salivary amylase but primarily takes place in small intestine by action of pancreatic enzyme amylase (Recall)\n\nFat digestion in small intestine by pancreatic enzyme lipase Protein digestion in small intestines by pancreatic enzyme Trypsin and Chymotrypsin\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nRegarding gastric emptying which one of the following Hormone inhibit gastric emptying?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Gastric emptying | Gastric emptying |\n> | increased by: | decreased by |\n> | e Fall in the pH of | hormones |\n> | chyme in stomach cholecystokinin and |  |\n> | e Pyloric antrum | secretin |\n> | distention | e Fallin the pH of |\n> | e Parasympathetic | chyme in |\n> | stimulation | duodenum (Recall) |\n> |  | e Duodenal |\n> |  | distention |\n\n- A. Vasoactive intestinal peptide\n- **B. Secretin** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Insulin\n- D. Gastric inhibitory peptide\n- E. Glucagon\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGastric emptying\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 35-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of pain in epigastrium secondary to chronic NSAID use. If you suspect a peptic ulcer, which one of the following arteries is responsible for bleeding peptic ulcer disease?\n\n- A. Inferior mesenteric artery\n- **B. Gastroduodenal artery** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Right gastric artery\n- D. Common hepatic artery\n- E. Splenic artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPeptic ulcer:\n\nBleeding gastric ulcer = Left gastric artery (Recall)\n\nBleeding duodenal ulcer = Gastroduodenal artery\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nWhich of the following substances is released from neurons in the GI tract and produces lower oesophageal relaxation?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Cholecystokinin | Increase production of bile + |\n> |  | stimulate release of stored bile |\n> |  | e Inhibit gastric emptying |\n> |  | e Inhibit gastric acid |\n> |  | production |\n> |  | e Release inhibited by |\n> |  | somatostatin |\n> | Secretin | Increase production of bile |\n> |  | e Inhibit gastric emptying |\n> |  | e Inhibit gastric acid |\n> |  | production |\n> |  | e Release inhibited by |\n> |  | somatostatin |\n> |  | Stimulated by gastric distention + |\n> |  | Raised gastric PH and function as |\n> |  | raising gastric acid secretion |\n> |  | from gastric parietal cells |\n> |  | (Recall), Pepsinogen from gastric |\n> |  | chief cells |\n> |  | Release inhibited by |\n> |  | Cholecystokinin, Secretin and |\n> |  | somatostatin |\n> | Somatostatin | Somatostatin produces |\n> |  | predominantly neuroendocrine |\n> |  | across multiple systems. It is |\n> |  | known to inhibit Gl, endocrine, |\n> |  | exocrine, pancreatic, and |\n> |  | pituitary secretions, |\n\n- **A. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Secretin\n- C. Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)\n- D. Gastrin\n- E. Cholecystokinin (CCK)\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nVasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is a gastrointestinal neurocrine that causes the relaxation of GI smooth muscle. For example, VIP mediates the relaxation response of the lower esophageal sphincter when a bolus of food approaches it, allowing passage of the bolus into the stomach.\n\nNote: Ileocecal valve relaxation is due to gastrin\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nThe following statements regarding the obturator nerve are correct except which?\n\n- A. It divides into an anterior and a posterior division\n- B. It innervates the adductor muscles of the thigh.\n- **C. It enters the thigh immediately beneath the inguinal ligament** (Correct Answer)\n- D. It originates from the lumbar plexus\n- E. It supplies the skin on the medial side of the thigh\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe obturator nerve arises from the lumbar plexus (L2, 3, and 4) and runs forward on the lateral wall of the pelvis to reach the obturator canal\n\nObturator nerve:\n\nMotor supply: Medial thigh muscles (adductor longus, brevis and magnus, gracilis, obturator externus)\n\nSensory supply Upper medial thigh Injury Motor Loss: Loss of hip adduction (Recall) Sensory Loss: Loss of sensation over upper media l thigh\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nYou prepare to perform a femoral nerve block on a 23-year-old man that has suffered a femoral shaft fracture in a motorcycle accident.\n\nWhich SINGLE statement regarding the femoral nerve is FALSE?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. It's formed within piriformis muscle** (Correct Answer)\n- B. It is formed within the psoas major muscle\n- C. It divides into anterior and posterior divisions in the femoral triangle\n- D. It enters the thigh beneath the mid-point of the inguinal ligament\n- E. It is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus. It is derived from the anterior rami of nerve roots L2, L3, and L4.\n\nAfter arising from the lumbar plexus, the femoral nerve travels inferiorly through the psoas major muscle of the posterior abdominal wall. It supplies branches to the iliacus and pectineus muscles prior to entering the thigh. The femoral nerve then passes underneath the inguinal ligament to enter the femoral triangle. Within this triangle, th e nerve is located lateral to the femoral vessels (Recall) (unlike the nerve, the femoral artery an d vein are enclosed within the femoral sheath).\n\nApproximately 4cm below the inguinal ligament, the femoral nerve divides into anterior and posterior divisions:\n\nThe function of femoral nerve: Motor functions: Innervates the anterior thigh muscles\n\nSensory functions: Supplies cutaneous branches to the anteromedial thigh (Recall) (anterior cutaneous branches of the femoral nerve)\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 23:** 1-D, 2-D, 3-A, 4-D, 5-A, 6-C, 7-A, 8-B, 9-B, 10-E, 11-B, 12-B, 13-A, 14-C, 15-A\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
a38e18ae-5200-4e76-a9fd-6d7befab81a2	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes	Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes	article	Dermatomes of Upper Limb:\nDermatome: A dermatome is defined as 'a strip of skin that is\ninnervated by a single spinal nerve and clinically very\nimportant and help in diagnosis of spinal cord injury\nMyotomes and associated\nmovements:\nMyotome: Group of muscles innervated by a single spinal\nnerve\nReflexes of upper Limb:\n\n![Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
7d04ebf1-d490-4beb-9b22-295704bc0898	40e7b149-db6f-4acb-a33f-f4f9196a71c0	ankle-foot-injuries	Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures	article	# Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Musculoskeletal (non-traumatic)\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\n-Ankle injuries\n-Ottawa ankle rules for adults and Xray the ankles if patients:\n> Were unable to weight-bear for four steps both immediately\nafter the injury and at the time of examination.\n>Have tenderness over the posterior surface of the distal 6cm\n(or tip) of the lateral malleolus.\n>Have tenderness over the posterior surface of the distal 6cm\n(or tip) of the medial malleolus.\n-A foot radiograph is required if there\nis any pain in midfoot zone and any\nof these finding:\n>Bone tenderness at navicular bone\n>Bone tenderness at the base of fifth metatarsal\n>Inability to bear weight for at least four steps both\nimmediately after injury and at the time of evaluation\n-Weber classification of lateral\nmalleolus fractures\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n-Ankle fracture\n>Radiographically, ankle fractures are described as\nunimalleolar, bimalleolar and trimalleolar.\n> A bimalleolar fracture is fracture of the lateral and medial\nmalleoli.\n>A trimalleolar fracture additionally involves the posterior\nmalleolus\n>The ankle mortice joint allows very little rotation or angulation\nat the ankle joint, so forced twisting or angulation of the ankle\njoint causes fractures,ligament injuries.it also disrupt the distal\ntibiofibular syndesmosis.\n-Management\n>Small avulsion fracture treated with rest, elevation, analgesia\nand early mobilization.\n>Larger avulsion fractures require initial immobilization in a\nboot ± crutches and orthopaedic follow-up\n>Undisplaced, isolated medial or lateral malleolar fractures are\nusually stable. provide analgesia and crutches, and immobilize\nin a well-padded BKPOP cast. Advise limb elevation, and\narrange orthopaedic follow-up.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n>Displaced fractures of the medial or lateral malleolus and\nBimalleolar or trimalleolar fractures are unstable. Having\nattempted to reduce any significant talar shift (with\nappropriate sedation), place in a BKPOP, obtain fresh X-rays,\nand refer to the orthopaedic team.\n-Ankle dislocation\n>Dislocation of the ankle is a true emergency. Treat promptly\non diagnosis.\n>Most ankle dislocations are associated with a fracture\n-Clinical features: Examination reveal gross deformity of the\nankle, severe stretching of the skin (resulting in fracture\nblisters, skin necrosis, or even converting the injury to a\ncompound fracture), and often deficits in peripheral pulses or\nsensation.\n-Management:\n>Prompt closed reduction and immobilization in POP without\npre-reduction x-ray however Pt require adequate analgesia or\nsedation for reduction.\n>Give Penthrox®, IV analgesia/sedation, as needed, with full\nprecautions.\n>Warn that there may be a brief increase in discomfort as the\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nankle reduces.\n>After reduction, re-check pulses and sensation; immobilize in\na POP slab, and arrange check X-rays\n>Refer the patient to the orthopaedic immediately\n-Ankle sprain\n>The most common type of ankle sprain is one to the lateral\nankle. Typically, these are minor and are due to an inversion\ninjury when the ankle is plantarflexed.\n>The structures most frequently injured in inversion injuries are\nthe lateral joint capsule and the anterior talofibular ligament.\n-Management:\n> RICE (rest, ice, compression, elevation)\n> Most patients with minor sprains can expect full recovery in 4\nweeks. Pt can resume sports within 2 weeks.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> If Pt inability to bear weight Provide crutches to those\ncompletely unable to weight-bear despite analgesia, with\nadvice to elevate the ankle. Arrange review at 2-4 days.\n> On review if Pt still unable to weightbear, consider 10 days'\nimmobilization in a boot or below-knee cast, with subsequent\noutpatient follow-up.\n-Complication: Peroneal tendon subluxation, Peroneal nerve\ninjury.\n-Foot fracture and dislocations\n>Foot injuries are a common presentation in the ED and are\nfrequently missed.1-3 These injuries typically occur in work,\nathletic, and traffic environments.\n-Talar injuries\n-MOI: extreme dorsiflexion or a fall from a great height. "Major"\ntalus fractures are those involving the head, neck, or body of\nthe talus and can result in avascular necrosis.\n-Diagnosis: plain radiograph however CT provide better\nvisualization.\n-Management: provide analgesia and immobilization in a\nbackslab POP, and refer promptly to orthopaedic for MUA or\nORIF.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n-Calcaneal fracture\n-MOI: Calcaneal fractures most often follow a fall from height\ndirectly onto the heels.\n>Always exclude associated injuries of the cervical and lumbar\nspine, pelvis, hips, or knees\n>Calcaneal fractures are usually bilateral\n-Clinical features: swelling, bruising, and tenderness over the\ncalcaneum.\n-Diagnosis: Plain radiographs, specifically the lateral view, are\nneeded for fracture diagnosis. The Boehler angle is measured\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nusing the lateral view and represents the intersection of two\nlines\n-Management: Refer all fractures to orthopedic\n-Navicular injuries\n-MOI: Navicular fractures are typically caused by a direct blow\nor axial loading.\n-Clinical features: tenderness and ecchymosis are found about\nthe navicular\n-Diagnosis: Radiograph however, CT is best for evaluating the\ntalonavicular joint surfaces.\n-Management: Nondisplaced fractures should be treated in a\nnon-weight-bearing short leg cast for 6 to 8 weeks and arrange\northopedic follow-up in 2 weeks.\n>Displaced fractures refer to orthopedic.\n-Complication: avascular necrosis , nonunion, flatfoot\ndeformity.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n-Metatarsal fractures and dislocations\n(Lis-franc fracture-dislocation)\n-Moi: Heavy objects falling onto the feet or being run over by a\nvehicle tyre or wheel.\n>It is common in football and soccer players.\n-Diagnosis: request a standard foot x-ray.\n>Look to check that the medial side of the second MT is\ncorrectly aligned with the medial side of the middle cuneiform.\n-Management:\n>Support in a backslab POP, and refer if there are multiple,\ndisplaced, or dislocated MT fractures.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n-Isolated avulsion fractures of the\nfifth MT base (dancer's fracture)\n-MOI: Inversion may avulse the base of the fifth MT by the\nperoneus brevis.\n>Always examine this area in ankle injuries, and request foot Xrays if tender.\n-Management: Give analgesia, elevation, and support in a\npadded crepe bandage.\n>In severe cases arrange follow-up with fracture clinic.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n-Jones fracture (of the fifth MT)\n>This is a transverse fracture of the fifth MT just distal to the\ninter-MT join.\n-Management: Provide analgesia, crutches, BKPOP or boot,\nand orthopaedic follow-up.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n-Stress fracture MTs (March fracture)\n-MOI: Prolonged or unusual exercise\n>The most common site is the second MT shaft.\n-Clinical features: swelling, localized tenderness over the MT\nshaft.\n-Management: Provide analgesia, elevation, rest, and modified\ndaily activity, as required. Suggest a padded insole.\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:25 AM Ankle & Foot injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n\n## Clinical Figures, Diagnostic Algorithms & Radiographs\n\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Click any figure below to examine diagnostic algorithms, anatomy landmarks, or radiological details at full clinical resolution.\n\n\n\n![Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/ankle-foot-injuries/figure_01_p2.png)\n\n*Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures - Figure 1: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2) (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/ankle-foot-injuries/figure_02_p3.png)\n\n*Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures - Figure 2: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3) (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Ankle & Foot Injuries, Ottawa Ankle Rules & Fractures**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
9ee09d6e-3901-4221-a159-f2a75a4af8c7	40e7b149-db6f-4acb-a33f-f4f9196a71c0	forearm-injuries	Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia)	article	# Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia)\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Musculoskeletal (non-traumatic)\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\n-Ulna fractures\n-Isolated ulnar fractures (nightstick\nfracture)\n>It's often result from a direct blow to the forearm while in\ndefensive stance.\n-Management : treat undisplaced fracture with elbow POP\nflexed at 90 degrees\n>Refer displaced or angulated fractures to orthopedic\n-Monteggia's fracture-dislocation\n>Fracture of the proximal third of the ulna with a radial head\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:22 AM Forearm injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\ndislocation is referred to as monteggia's fracture\n>It occurs following a fall onto an outstretched hand or a direct\nblow\n-Clinical features: pain and swelling at the elbow.\n-Diagnosis : ulnar fracture is clearly visible on radiographs\n>Check entire elbow x-ray to confirm that radial shaft normally\naligned with radial head. An abnormal radiocapitellar line may\naid in the diagnosis\n-Management: Early consult with Orthopedic, Monteggia's\nfracture-dislocations are generally treated with open reduction\nand internal fixation of the ulna fracture and closed reduction\nof the radial head dislocation.\n-Complications: nonunion, recurrent dislocation, chronic pain,\nparalysis of the posterior interosseous nerve, the deep branch\nof the radial nerve that controls finger extension\n/26, 10:22 AM Forearm injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nmonteggia's fracture (Reference wikipedia)\n-Radial fractures\n-Galeazzi fracture-dislocation\n>Fracture of the distal third of the radial shaft accompanied by\na dislocation of the distal radioulnar joint is known as Galeazzi\nfracture-dislocation.\n>Always look for subluxation of ulna in radial fracture\n>Mechanism of injury results from falls on the outstretched\nhand in forced pronation or from a direct blow\n-Clinical features\n/26, 10:22 AM Forearm injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n>Localized tenderness and swelling over the distal radius and\nwrist\n-Diagnosis\n>AP radiograph will reveal slightly increased distal radioulnar\njoint space\n>Lateral view will reveal displacement of ulna dorsally.\n-Management\n>Urgent orthopedic consultation in the ED.\n>Control pain with analgesia and immobilization in a\ntemporary POP backslab\n/26, 10:22 AM Forearm injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nGaleazzi fracture-dislocation\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:22 AM Forearm injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n\n## Clinical Figures, Diagnostic Algorithms & Radiographs\n\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Click any figure below to examine diagnostic algorithms, anatomy landmarks, or radiological details at full clinical resolution.\n\n\n\n![Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia) - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 1)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/forearm-injuries/figure_01_p1.png)\n\n*Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia) - Figure 1: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 1) (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia) - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/forearm-injuries/figure_02_p3.png)\n\n*Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia) - Figure 2: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3) (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia) - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 5)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/forearm-injuries/figure_03_p5.png)\n\n*Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia) - Figure 3: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 5) (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Forearm & Wrist Injuries (Colles, Smith, Galeazzi & Monteggia)**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
0c7ae7b6-f069-4e96-92d0-62cc82c8e3db	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-aortic-dissection	Aortic Dissection	quiz	# Aortic Dissection\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Aortic Dissection\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Aortic Dissection**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
124d4412-4cd9-481b-8038-f1bb8b04045f	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	constibation	Acute Constipation & Bowel Management	article	# Constipation & Faecal Impaction\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Anxiety and/or depression.\n- Somatization disorders.\n- Eating disorders.\n- History of sexual abuse.\n- Physical . Female sex.\n- Older age.\n- Pyrexia, poor fluid intake/dehydration, immobility.\n- Sitting position on a toilet seat (compared with the squatting position for defecation).\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\ndifficulty passing stools, or a sensation of incomplete emptying\n- Lower abdominal pain or discomfort, distension, or bloating.\n- Consider a diagnosis of constipation in the elderly if there are non-specific symptoms, such as:\n- Confusion or delirium, functional decline.\n- Nausea or loss of appetite.\n- Overflow diarrhoea. Urinary retention.\n- Suspect a diagnosis of faecal loading or impaction if there is history of:\n- Hard, lumpy stools, which may be large and infrequent (for example passed every 7—10 days), or small and relatively frequent (for example passed every 2—3 days).\n- Having to use manual methods to extract faeces.\n- Overflow faecal incontinence, or loose stool.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nConsider, investigate and manage any underlying secondary cause of constipation,\n- and if possible and appropriate advise the person to reduce or stop any drug treatmentthat may be causing or contributing to symptoms. Identify if faecal loading and/or impaction is present as this will need treatrnent to\n- resolve and may need enemas, suppositories, or disimpaction.\n- Advise-on-lifestyJe-measures increasing dietary fibre,\n- Ensuring adequate fluid intake, and activity levels\n-\n- If the above measures fail then Offer a bulk-forming laxative first-line, such as ispaghula, and increase fluid intake\n-\n- If stools remain hard or difficult to pass, add or switch to an osmotic laxative, such as a macrogol. If a macrogol is ineffective or not tolerated, offer treatment with a lactulose\n- second-line. If stools are soft but difficult to pass, or there is a sensation of inadequate emptying,\n- add a stimulant laxative. Note: If the person has opioid-induced constipation:\n- Do not prescribe bulk-forming laxatives.\n-\n- Offer an osmotic laxative and a stimulant laxative.\n- Complications Faecal loading and impaction.\n- Progressive faecal retention, distension of the rectum, and loss of sensory and\n- motor function. Haemorrhoids or anal fissure.\n-\n\n- Chronic dilatation of the colon may cause a megacolon.\n- Bowel obstruction, perforation, or ulceration.\n- Recurrent urinary tract infections, obstructive uropathy.\n- Rectal bleeding.\n- Rectal prolapse previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Constibation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/constibation/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Constibation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
32b2475a-e6ed-4e13-980c-dfea0939c578	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-18	Quiz 18: Evidence-Based Medicine, Cardiac Conduction & Clinical Trials	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 18\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nThe mean was calculated for study with normal distribution. The values distribution around the mean are measured by?\n\n- A. Mode\n- B. Median\n- C. Variance\n- D. Interquartal range\n- **E. Standard deviation** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nStandard error of the mean = standard deviation / square root (number of patients)\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nMean or average: is the sum of a collection of numbers divided by the number of numbers in the collection\n\nThe mode is the value that appears most often i n a set of data\n\nThe median is the number separating the higher half of a data sample, a population, or a probability distribution, from the lower half. The median o f a finite list of numbers can be found by arranging all the observations from lowest value to highest value and picking the middle one\n\nFor example, a study looks at the number of attendances brittle asthmatics have at A&E's across t he north of England each quarter. Each A&E submits the number of attendances as below:\n\n1,3,4,5,5,5,6,7,7,9,12,14,17\n\nMean = 7.5\n\nMode = 5\n\nMedian = 6\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nIn the control of breathing, which of the following statements is CORRECT?\n\n- **A. When expiratory neurons fire they inhibit the activity of inspiratory neurons** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Afferents from stretch receptors feed into the pneumotaxic Centre to modulate breathing\n- C. The pneumotaxic Centre is located in the thalamus\n- D. Voluntary control of breathing is mediated by cortical motor neurons directly innervating respiratory group neurons in the medulla, altering their activity\n- E. The pons contains dorsal and ventral respiratory groups that receive input from chemoreceptors.\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInvoluntary respiration\n\nInvoluntary respiration is under subconscious control. The diaphragm and intercostal muscles are the primary respiratory muscles. They are stimulated by groups of neurons located in the pons a nd medulla. These neurons form the respiratory control centre. They send impulses to the primary respiratory muscles, via the phrenic and intercostal nerves, which stimulate their contraction. There are three main groups of neurons involved in respiration:\n\nThe ventral respiratory group controls expiration The dorsal respiratory group controls inspiration The pontine respiratory group controls the rate a nd pattern of breathing.\n\nNote: When expiratory neurons fire they inhibit t he activity of inspiratory neurons and once the neurons stop firing, the inspiratory muscles relax, and expiration occurs.\n\nVoluntary respiration Voluntary respiration is under conscious control. I t is controlled via the motor cortex in the cerebrum, which receives inputs from the limbic system and hypothalamus. Signals are thought to be sen t to the spinal cord from the motor cortex, which are then passed onto the respiratory muscles. Central control of breathing (brainstem and cerebral cortex: High Yield)\n\n1. Medullary respiratory center (Recall)\n\nA: Dorsal respiratory group is primarily responsible for inspiration and generates the basic rhythm for breathing\n\nInput to the dorsal respiratory group comes from the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves. The vagus nerve relays information from peripheral chemoreceptors in the lung. The glossopharyngeal nerve relays information from peripheral chemoreceptors and Output from the dorsal respiratory group travels, via the phrenic nerve, to the diaphragm\n\nB: Ventral respiratory group is primarily responsible for expiration, is not active during normal, quiet breathing, when expiration is passive.\n\nActivated during exercise, when expiration becomes an active process.\n\n2. Apneustic center is located in the lower pons. Stimulates inspiration, producing a deep and prolonged inspiratory gasp (apneusis).\n\n3. Pneumotaxic centeris located in the upper pons. inhibits inspiration and, therefore, regulates inspiratory volume and respiratory rate.\n\n4.Cerebral cortex: Breathing can be under voluntary control; therefore, a person can voluntarily hyper ventilate or hypo ventilate.\n\nNote:Hypoventilation (breath-holding) is limited by the resulting increase in Pco2 and decrease in P02\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 30-year-old man presents with symmetrical ascending muscle weakness. He had a diarrheal illness a few weeks previously\n\nWhat is the most likely pathogen?\n\n- A. Epstein-Barr virus\n- **B. Campylobacter jejuni** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Clostridium difficle\n- D. Borrelia burgdorferi\n- E. Cytomegalovirus\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGBS cases seem to occur after a viral or bacterial infection like those that cause sore throats or diarrhea characterized by lower limb weakness, paresthesia and hyporeflexia\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nIn a randomised controlled trial, the following types of biases are reduced by randomisation:\n\n- A. Publication bias\n- B. Recall bias\n- **C. Selection bias** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Ascertainment bias\n- E. Bias in handling dropouts\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSubjects are randomised in a randomised con trolled trial to ensure that the groups are iden tical a part from the intervention. Randomisation mean s that each subject has a predetermined chanceof being allocated to each group, but the group t o be allocated can not be predicted. This will minimise any selec tion bias.\n\nAscertainment bias occurs when the results of atrial are systematically distorted by knowledge o f which intervention each partici pant is receiving. This type of bias is minimised by blinding of researchers, clinicians, and patients. Bias caused by dropouts occur because characteristics of subjects who left the trial before completion may bediverent from those who do not. This type of bias is minimised by appropriate analysis (for exam pl e, intention to treat analysis, worst case scenario analysis).\n\nRecall bias occurs when the subjects' recall of past events is distorted by subsequent events. This type of bias occurs mainly in retrospectivestudies (for example, case control studies Recall) rather than randomised controlled trials. Publication bias occurs dur ing the dissemination of t he results.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nWhilst in your physiology lecture you ask medical students what is the physiological purpose of AV nodal delay?\n\n- A. Sympathetic activity causes increase in AV nodal delay\n- **B. Increase time for ventricular filling** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Parasympathetic activity decreases AV nodal delay\n- D. Increase time for filling of atria\n- E. Ejection fraction time\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSignificance of AV nodal delay The cardiac impulse does not travel from the atria to the ventricles too rapidly.\n\nThis delay allows time for the atria to empty their blood into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins. This increases the efficiency of the pumping action of the heart.\n\nIt is primarily the AV node and its adjacent fibers that delay this transmission into the ventricles.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nRegarding the power of study which one of the following statements is correct?\n\n- A. Power does not change with sample size\n- **B. Power increases with sample size** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Power increases as the variability increases\n- D. Power decreases with sample size\n- E. Power decreases as the variability decrease\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe power of a study is the probability of (correctly) rejecting the null hypothesis when it is false, i. e. the probability of detecting a statistically significant difference\n\npower can be increased by increasing the sample size Power increases as the variability decrease\n\nNote: Increasing sample size can decrease bias /error. (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 44-year-old woman presents with a two-month history of nausea, weight loss, and diarrhea. She returned from working in Nigeria five months ago. Do you suspect tapeworm infestation\n\nWhat diagnostic method is most likely to confirm acute Infestation?\n\n- A. Colonoscopy\n- B. Eosinophil count\n- **C. Visualization of eggs in stool on light microscopy** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Serum antibodies\n- E. Abdominal CT\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nStool: parasites Direct light microscopy Sputum: TB Ziehl-Nelsen stain\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 72-year-old woman presents to the ED with a 24-hour history of blistering rash on the left side of her forehead. You suspect varicella zoster virus infection (shingles). What is the most appropriate way to make the diagnosis prior to starting treatment?\n\n- **A. Clinical history and examination alone** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Polymerase chain reaction from skin scraping sample\n- C. IgG ELISA serology from blood sample\n- D. Direct fluorescent antibody staining from skin scraping sample\n- E. IgM ELISA serology from blood sample\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nVaricella zoster virus causes:\n\nChicken pox Shingles usually affects older\n\nVaricella zoster Key Points\n\nThe incubation period is 10 - 21 days (Recall) (mean time is 14 days).\n\nPrimary infection typically occurs in 4-10 years children Diagnosis is usually clinical After primary infection virus lies dormant in posterior root ganglion and reactivates in 20% of patients causing shingles School exclusion period: when all lesions are crusted over (Recall\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 19-year-old man presents with an open tibial fracture contaminated with soil. His immunizations are up to date.\n\nWith regard to tetanus prophylaxis what is the most appropriate management?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Up to date immunization status and last | No tetanus immunoglobin or vaccine |\n> | dose less than 10 years |  |\n> | Up to date immunization status and last | e Clean wound: NO IG or vaccine |\n> | dose greater than 10 years ago | e High risk wound: Both IG and vaccine |\n> |  | e Clean wound: Immediate reinforcing |\n> | No immunization or | dose of vaccine |\n> | Immunization of born | e High risk wound: Both immediate IG |\n> |  | and vaccine |\n\n- A. Administration of tetanus immunoglobulin\n- **B. No further tetanus prophylaxis** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Administration of tetanus vaccination now and in one month\n- D. Administration of tetanus immunoglobulin and vaccination\n- E. Administration of tetanus vaccination\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDiphtheria, tetanus, polio vaccine schedule: 2, 3, 4 months, preschool and 14 years\n\nChild's immunization status is up to date and his last vaccination schedule was in 14 years. Less th an 10 years are passed\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA study was performed in two group of people one with heart disease and associated risk factor is compared to another group with heart but no associated risk factor what type of study is it?\n\n- A. Cross Sectional\n- **B. Cohort** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Interventional\n- D. Randomised control trials\n- E. Case control\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCase control study:\n\nRetrospective longitudinal observational study Disease vs non-Disease Subject to recall bias Related to ODD Ratio case-control studies are usually less expensive an d also shorter in duration.\n\nCohort Study:\n\nForward Study or Prospective longitudinal observational study Group with Risk Factors and Group without Risk Factors Related to Relative Risk Cause to Effect Compared to case control study, cohort study takes more time\n\nCross-sectional:\n\nCross-sectional observational study Disease and Risk Factors Subject to recall bias\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 50-year-old male presents to ER with worsening breathlessness, on examination you find signs of heart failure. In heart failure, the frank starling curve is moved downward due to:\n\n- A. Increase in preload\n- B. Decrease in preload\n- C. Decrease in Afterload\n- D. Increase in Afterload\n- **E. Decrease in contractility** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe ability of the heart to change its force of contraction and therefore stroke volume in response to changes in venous return is called the Frank-Starling mechanism.\n\nThe heart will contract with greater force when preload/EDV is increased. Preload is diastolic filling volume of left ventricle reflect stretch of cardia c muscle cells, greater the stretch more forcefully heart will contract. when there is increased ED V/ preload there is increased is Stroke volume this intrinsic relationship between EDV and stroke volume is known as frank starling law of heart...\n\nIn HEART FAILURE, the Frank-Starling curve is moved down and flattened due to reduced inotropy, so that more venous return and hence preload is required to increase stroke volume. As venous return and preload rise, myocyte stretching occurs that increases sarcomere length.\n\nFACTORS AFFECTING THE FRANK STARLING CURVE\n\nincrease in contractility shift the curve upward a nd to the left e.g.: positive inotropic drugs (RECALL)\n\nDecrease in contractility= downward and to the right Increase in preload = rightward shift decrease in preload=leftward shift Increase in Afterload=Downward and right decrease in Afterload=upward and left\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nBaby defecate after eating due to which one of the following physiological reflex?\n\n- A. Oesophageo-gastric reflex\n- **B. Gastro-colic reflex** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Oesophageo-colic reflex\n- D. Entero-colic reflex\n- E. Vagal reflex\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe gastrocolic reflex is a physiological reflex that controls the motility of the lower gastrointestinal tract following a meal. As a result of the gastro colic reflex, the colon has increased motility in response to the stretch of the stomach with the ingestion of food.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA woman sustained a human bite from someone known to have hepatitis B two hours ago. She has not had any previous hepatitis B vaccinations\n\nRegarding prophylaxis against hepatitis B, what is the most appropriate schedule to follow?\n\n- A. Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately and Hepatitis 8 vaccine three doses over six months\n- B. Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately\n- C. Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over six months\n- **D. Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately and Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three months** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three months\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nEmergency hepatitis B vaccination\n\nIf you have been exposed to the hepatitis B virus and have not been vaccinated before, you should get immediate medical advice, as you may benefit from having the hepatitis B vaccine.\n\nIn some situations, you may also need to have an injection of antibodies, called specific hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG), along with the hepatitis B vaccine.\n\nHBIG should ideally be given within 48 hours po st exposure Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three months\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nAn eight-year-old girl presents to the ED with impetigo around her mouth and nose.\n\nWhat is the most likely method of spread?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Infection | Investigation |\n> | Stool: parasites | Direct light microscopy (Recall) |\n> | Sputum: TB | Ziehl-Nelsen stain |\n> | Blood: malaria | Giemsa stain (Recall) |\n> | Penetration through intact skin | Leptospira and Treponema |\n> | Penetration through breached skin | If skin integrity is broken e.g., by intravenous |\n> |  | cannulation, blood-borne viruses such as |\n> |  | hepatitis B, Hepatitis c and HIV can be |\n> |  | transmitted. |\n> |  | if the skin is punctured or broken, staph |\n> |  | aureus can enter the wound and cause an |\n> |  | infection. |\n\n- A. Droplet spread\n- B. Invasion of intact skin\n- C. Hematogenous spread\n- **D. Invasion of breached skin** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Via fecal oral route\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nImpetigo is highly contagious skin infection caused by staph aureus\n\nNote: Fusidic acid is used for staphylococcus skin infection (impetigo) Recall\n\nNote: Malaria is blood borne pathogen( Recall) introduced via the bite of a vector\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nAn 8-year-old child brought into the ED with a complaint of severe vomiting. ABGs revealed metabolic alkalosis. Metabolic alkalosis occurs after vomiting due to?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Decreased pco2\n- **B. Decrease H+** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Increase H+\n- D. Decrease HC03\n- E. Increased pco2\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMetabolic alkalosis: Occurs due to loss of H+ in GI tract i.e., vomiting, pyloric stenosis or in kidney (diuretic therapy)\n\nMetabolic alkalosis is associated with hypoventilation and hypokalemia (Recall).\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 18:** 1-E, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C, 5-B, 6-B, 7-C, 8-A, 9-B, 10-B, 11-E, 12-B, 13-D, 14-D, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
2857da7d-c1d6-4cae-9bd1-a9601ae70c07	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-19	Quiz 19: Pediatric Emergencies, Joint Ligaments & Parasitology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 19\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 55-year-old male known case of COPD came with a complaint of breathlessness. His observations include BP=100/70 Pulse=102bpm and Sa02 is 78%. You keep the patient on 02, what is the targeted 02 saturation prior to discharge?\n\n- A. 80-84%\n- **B. 88 - 92%** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Unless SaO2 becomes 100%\n- D. Unless SaO2 becomes 98%\n- E. Unless SaO2 becomes 94%\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCOPD and conditions associated with chronic respiratory failure. In the treatment of exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), oxygen should be titrated to achieve a target oxygen saturation range of 88-92%.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 71-year-old man with a history of heart failure and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with an acutely painful, swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. A clinical diagnosis of gout is made. Which treatment would be most appropriate for the acute management of this Patient?\n\n- **A. Colchicine** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Diclofenac\n- C. Febuxostat\n- D. Allopurinol\n- E. Prednisolone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nManagement of gout Acute gout: NSAIDS (specially naproxen) Recall Gout with heart failure: Colchicine Chronic gout: Allopurinol also reduces further attacks of gout by reducing uric acid\n\nNote: In gout under polarized light, gout crystals appear needle-shaped and yellow, a property called negative birefringence (Recall)\n\nGout crystals are monosodium urate crystals In Pseudo gout: Positive birefringence and pyrophosphate crystals (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\n18 weeks Pregnant lady previously non-immune comes in contact with a chickenpox patient. Which one of the following management plans is correct?\n\n- A. Vaccine and IV acyclovir\n- B. Immunoglobulin and vaccine\n- **C. Immunoglobulin+ iv acyclovir for 7 days** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Immunoglobulin+ iv acyclovir for 14 days\n- E. Treat with IV antibiotics\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nChickenpox in pregnancy is managed by two different methods - one is used before the onset of symptoms and the other after the onset of rash. O ne is varicella-zoster immune globulin (VZIG), which strengthens the immune system for a short time. If you are not immune to chickenpox you ca n be treated with VZIG, usually by injection, up t o ten days after you come into contact with chickenpox. It is used before any symptoms appear; i t does not work afterwards. The other is Acyclovir, which may reduce fever and symptoms if it is started within 24 hours of the rash developing an d continue for 7 days Key points:\n\nIf you have already had chickenpox, you have nothing to worry about.\n\nOnce you have had chickenpox you cannot catch it a second time, this is known as being immune.\n\nNine out of ten UK adults are immune to chicken pox.\n\nChickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in th e UK.\n\nIt affects very few babies in the womb and although it can be very serious in pregnancy especially in 1st trimester in first 12 weeks (Recall)\n\nIf you have been in contact with chickenpox and are not sure if you are immune, contact your GP or midwife not attend the antenatal clinic.\n\nIf you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other pregnant women and new babies until at least five days after the rash appears, or until all the blisters have crusted over.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nRepeated stimulation of a skeletal muscle fiber causes a sustained contraction (tetanus). Accumulation of which solute in intracellular fluid is responsible for the tetanus?\n\n- A. Potassium\n- B. Troponin\n- C. Calmodulin\n- D. Sodium\n- **E. Calcium** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDuring repeated stimulation of a muscle fiber, Calcium is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) more quickly than it can be reaccumulated; therefore, the intracellular [Ca2+] does not return to resting levels as it would after a single twitch. The increased [Ca2+] allows more cross-bridge s to form and, therefore, produces increased tension (tetanus)\n\nNote: In skeletal muscle, Ca binds to troponin C (Recall) initiating the cross-bridge cycle. In smooth muscle, Ca2+ binds to calmodulin.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nMuscle filaments:\n\nThick filaments: Present in the A band in the center of the sarcomere\n\nContains Myosin (Recall)\n\nThin filaments: Present in the I bands\n\nAnchored at the Z lines Contains actin, tropomyosin and troponin\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nRegarding the bursae of knee joint, which one of the following statements is correct?\n\n- A. Prepatellar bursa found between the quadriceps femoris and the femur\n- B. Housemaid knee is due to inflammation of infrapatellar bursa\n- C. Prepatellar bursa located in the sartorius\n- **D. Suprapatellar bursa located between the quadriceps femoris and the femur** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Suprapatellar bursa located in the sartorius\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBursa: synovial fluid filled sac within joints provide low friction surfaces for the movement of tendons associated with joint\n\nSuprapatellar bursa located between the quadriceps femoris and the femur (Recall)\n\nPrepatellar bursa found between the apex of the patella and the skin. Housemaid knee is due to inflammation of Prepatellar bursa Infrapatellar bursa: split into deep and superficial. The deep bursa lies between the tibia and the patella ligament. The superficial lies between the patella ligament and the skin. Clergyman's Knee is due to inflammation of infrapatellar bursa.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 16-year-old young male brought into the ED with complaint of dizziness, fatigue and edema on his face and feet. After initial assessment you suspect Nephrotic syndrome, regarding nephrotic syndrome what is the cause of edema?\n\n- **A. Hypoalbuminemia** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Hyperalbuminemia\n- C. Increased intravascular oncotic pressure\n- D. Increased salt excretion\n- E. Decreased extravascular oncotic pressure\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNephrotic syndrome leads to urinary loss of albumin (Hypoalbuminemia) which reduces intravascular oncotic pressure and fluid shift to extracellular compartment.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA mother brought her 3-year-old child into ED with complaint of runny nose, severe coughing from the last 2 weeks and developed subconjunctival hemorrhage. What is the most likely causative agent?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Bordetella | Gram negative Cocco bacilli causing |\n> | Pertussis | whopping cough characterized by |\n> |  | severe coughing from more than 2 |\n> |  | weeks, nasal congestion, red eyes |\n> |  | and post tussive vomiting |\n> |  | Diagnosis: |\n> |  | e If the cough is of 2 weeks' |\n> |  | duration or less: culture of |\n> |  | nasopharyngeal swab in all |\n> |  | ages (Recall) |\n> |  | e If the cough is of less than 3 |\n> |  | weeks PCR testing of |\n> |  | nasopharyngeal swabs can also |\n> |  | be used to confirm in all ages |\n> |  | e If the cough is of more than 2 |\n> |  | duration, anti-pertussis |\n> |  | toxin immunoglobulin G (IgG) |\n> |  | serology may be employed in |\n> |  | people aged over 17 (Recall) |\n> |  | e If the cough is of more than 2 |\n> |  | weeks' duration, Anti-pertussis |\n> |  | toxin IgG detection in oral fluid |\n> |  | can be used in children aged 5 |\n> |  | to 16 years. (Recall) |\n> | Parainfluenza| Causing Croup characterized by |  |\n> |  | hoarseness of the voice |\n\n- A. RSV\n- **B. Bordetella Pertussis** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Parainfluenza virus\n- D. Strep group A\n- E. Hemophilus influenzae type B\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: Hemophilus influenza is also gram-negative Cocco bacilli (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nRegarding preventive measures and spread of infection of thread worm, which one of the following statements is correct?\n\n- **A. Treat everyone in your home even if they do not have symptoms** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Do not treat other family members\n- C. Treat only infected persons with mebendazole\n- D. Treat infected persons with mebendazole who have symptoms\n- E. Treat everyone in your home if they have symptoms only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThread worm is the most common helminth in t he UK, commonly infect children\n\nMebendazole is the drug of choice and you must treat everyone in your home even if they do not have symptoms.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 25-year-old man attends ED after injury to his Right knee while skiing. He has a valgus deformity of knee. Which ligament is most likely to be damaged?\n\n- A. Posterior cruciate\n- B. Lateral collateral\n- **C. Medial collateral** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Patellar ligament\n- E. Anterior cruciate\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nValgus deformity: Also known as Knock-Kneed More stress on lateral compartment Ligament damaged is Medial collateral Varus deformity: Also known as bow legged More stress on medial compartment Ligament damaged is lateral collateral\n\nNote: lateral collateral ligament inserts on the head of fibula where it joins with bicep femoris tendon (Recall) to form conjoint tendon.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 32-year-old male with history of trauma to the hand presents with pain in the right hand associated with tingling and numbness. You suspect median nerve injury. Which one of the following is the most lateral structure forming the carpal arch?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Roof |  Flexor retinaculum |\n> |  | Medially: Pisiform and hook of the hamate |\n> |  | Laterally: tubercles of the scaphoid and trapezium |\n> | Carpal arch |  |\n> |  |  Four tendons of flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) |\n> |  |  Four tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS): Most |\n> |  | superficial structure (Recall) |\n> | Contents | Tendon of flexor pollicis longus (FPL): Lateral structure |\n> |  | (Recall) |\n> |  | * median nerve laterally |\n\n- A. Ulnar nerve\n- **B. Trapezium** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Hook of hamate\n- D. Pisiform\n- E. Median nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCarpal tunnel structures\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 2-year-old child brought into the ED by his parents with a history of fever associated with harsh cough, runny nose and hoarse of the voice for the last two days. On examination the child has mild tachycardia, tachypnoea and his SpO2 is 98%. Which of the following is the most likely infectious cause of his condition?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Bordetella | Gram negative Cocco bacilli causing | |\n> | Pertussis | whopping cough characterized by |\n> |  | severe coughing from more than 2 |\n> |  | weeks, nasal congestion, red eyes |\n> |  | and post tussive vomiting |\n> |  | Diagnosis: |\n> |  | e If the cough is of 2 weeks' |\n> |  | duration or less: culture of |\n> |  | nasopharyngeal swab in all ages |\n> |  | e If the cough is of less than 3 |\n> |  | weeks PCR testing of |\n> |  | nasopharyngeal swabs can also |\n> |  | be used to confirm in all ages |\n> |  | e If the cough is of more than 2 |\n> |  | weeks' duration, anti-pertussis |\n> |  | toxin immunoglobulin G (IgG) |\n> |  | serology may be employed in |\n> |  | people aged over 17 |\n> |  | e If the cough is of more than 2 |\n> |  | weeks' duration, Anti-pertussis |\n> |  | toxin IgG detection in oral fluid |\n> |  | can be used in children aged 5 |\n> |  | to 16 years. (Recall) |\n> |  | RX: 1st line antibiotic: Macrolide |\n> |  | (erythromycin) Recall |\n> | Parainfluenza| Causing Croup characterized by |  |\n> | virus | harsh barking cough, runny nose and |\n> |  | hoarseness of the voice |\n\n- A. Hemophilus influenzae type B\n- B. RSV\n- C. Glandular fever\n- D. Strep group A\n- **E. Parainfluenza** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 25-year-old woman presents with shortness of breath and right-sided chest pain. You suspect pulmonary embolism.\n\nWhich parameter is most likely to be decreased?\n\n- A. Ventilation to perfusion ratio\n- B. Left atrial pressure\n- C. Pulmonary vascular resistance\n- D. Right atrial pressure\n- **E. Po2 in pulmonary venous blood** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn PE, pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) increases due to the mechanical obstruction of the vascular bed with thrombus and hypoxic vasoconstriction. Pulmonary artery pressure (PAP) increases if thromboemboli occludes greater than 30% t o 50% of the total cross-sectional area of the pulmonary arterial bed.\n\nIncreased PVR increases the right ventricular afterload, which impedes right ventricular outflow, which, in turn, causes right ventricular dilation andflattening or bowing of the interventricular septum. The desynchronization of the ventricles may b e increased by the development of the right bundle branch block. The decreased RV outflow and concomitant RV dilation reduce left ventricular filling, thereby compromising cardiac output. As a result, LV filling is reduced in early diastole, and th is leads to a reduction in the cardiac output (CO) and cause systemic hypotension and hemodynamic instability. Low cardiac output leads to increased extraction of oxygen in the tissues, thereby decreasing the partial pressure of oxygen in venous blood below normal level. Right ventricle (RV) failure due to acute pressure overload is the primary cause of death in severe PE.\n\nNote: Due to increase after load in right ventricle right ventricular dilation and right atrial pressure increases in pulmonary embolism (Recall)\n\nPulmonary embolism: Increase physiological dead space (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 20-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of pain in his right foot after falling from upstairs. Which one of the following ankle ligaments is torn during eversion injury?\n\n- A. Posterior talofibular\n- B. Anterior talofibular\n- **C. Medial deltoid ligament** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Lateral collateral ligament\n- E. Calcaneofibular ligament\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAnkle ligaments\n\nEversion injury = medial deltoid ligament injury\n\ninversion injury= Lateral collateral ligament injured (subdivided into 3 ligaments) most frequent ligament injury during ankle sprain or inversion injury commonly affected ligament is Anterior talofibular ligament (Recall) followed by calcaneofibular ligament (both are ligaments of lateral collateral ligament)\n\nLeast injured during inversion injury post talofibular ligament.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 35-year-old woman presents with fatigue and yellow sclera. Blood tests reveal a high bilirubin which is found to be unconjugated bilirubin. What pathology is the most likely cause for her jaundice?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Intrahepatic causes: | Sickle cell anemia |\n> | Viral hepatitis, | Thalassemia |\n> | Alcoholic hepatitis, | Gilberte syndrome |\n> | Primary biliary cirrhosis | Crigler- Najjar syndrome |\n> | Hepatocellular carcinoma | Glucose 6 phosphate |\n> | Extrahepatic causes: | dehydrogenase deficiency |\n> | Choledocholithiasis |  |\n> | Cholangitis |  |\n> |  Cholangiocarcinoma |  |\n> | Chronic pancreatitis and |  |\n> | pancreatic adenocarcinoma |  |\n\n- A. Gallstone in the bile duct\n- **B. Sickle cell disease** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Hepatocellular carcinoma\n- D. Hepatitis\n- E. Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nRegarding prophylaxis of malaria which one of the following antibiotics is used?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Vancomycin\n- **B. Doxycycline** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Rifampicin\n- D. Clarithromycin\n- E. Clindamycin\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDoxycycline is used as prophylaxis for malaria who travel to countries with extensive resistance t o Chloroquine.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 19:** 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-E, 5-D, 6-A, 7-B, 8-A, 9-C, 10-B, 11-E, 12-E, 13-C, 14-B, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
524b9997-4bb0-4838-8751-941fa00b9f48	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-20	Quiz 20: Emergency Anesthetics, Toxicology Antidotes & Resuscitation Pharmacology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 20\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 40-Year-old male who is known hypertensive taking ACE inhibitors presents with dry cough. What is the mechanism of cough in ACE inhibitor?\n\n- A. Increased bradykinin breakdown\n- **B. Decreased bradykinin breakdown** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Increased production of prostaglandin\n- D. Stimulation of irritant receptors\n- E. Histamine release\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nACE inhibitor decreases the breakdown of the potent vasodilator bradykinin which is the cause of the persistent dry cough.\n\nSide effects of ACE inhibitor includes:\n\nAcute kidney injury Hyperkalemia Non allergic angioedema (Recall)\n\nPersistent dry cough (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation in a patient who require ketamine for the hip fracture. Ketamine primarily acts on what receptor?\n\n- A. Muscarinic receptor\n- B. GABA receptor\n- **C. NMDA receptor** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Mu-opioid receptor\n- E. Nicotinic receptor\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nKetamine Is NMDA receptor antagonist\n\nUses: Ketamine increases sympathetic drive increases Blood pressure and heart rate so considered in hemodynamically unstable patient\n\nKetamine has minimal effect on respiratory drive that's why can be used for induction in Asthma (Recall)\n\nContraindications: Head trauma, Stroke, Hypertension\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 40-year-old woman presents to the ED with palpitations. She has no other symptoms. She has been prescribed fluoxetine for psychiatric disorder and recently completed a Course of erythromycin for chest infection.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Increasing the rate of metabolism | Decrease hepatic clearance and |\n> | and hepatic clearance of concurrently | increase pharmacologic effect. |\n> | administered drugs, which typically |  |\n> | results in a decreased pharmacologic |  |\n> | effect. |  |\n> | Useful mnemonic SCRAP GP | Useful mnemonic: SICKFACES.COM |\n> | * Sulphonylureas | Sodium valproate |\n> | * Carbamazepine | * Isoniazid |\n> | Rifampicin | Cimetidine |\n> | * Alcohol | Ketoconazole |\n> | * Phenytoin (Recall) | Fluconazole |\n> | * Griseofulvin | * Alcohol |\n> | Phenobarbital | Chloramphenicol |\n> |  | Erythromycin |\n> |  | Sulfonamides |\n> |  | Ciprofloxacin |\n> |  | * Omeprazole |\n> |  | * Metronidazole |\n\n- A. VF\n- B. Third degree AV block\n- C. Polymorphic VT\n- **D. Torsade de pointes** (Correct Answer)\n- E. AF\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAll the enzyme inhibitor drugs prolong QT interval and induce Torsade de pointes.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 25-year-old woman presents with clinical features of anaphylaxis. You consider administering intramuscular adrenaline. Which strength of adrenaline is most appropriate in this situation?\n\n- A. 1:100,000\n- B. 1:10\n- C. 1:10,000\n- D. 1:100\n- **E. 1:1000** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAdrenaline dose in anaphylaxis: 0.5 mg/ 0.5 ml of 1:1000 adrenaline\n\nAdrenaline dose in cardiac arrest during CPR: 1 mg of 1: 10000 concentration (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation in a patient who requires propofol. What is the mechanism of action of propofol?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | > NMDA receptor antagonist | > GABA receptor activation |\n> | > Ketamine increases sympathetic | > Used for both induction and |\n> | drive increases Blood pressure and | maintenance of anesthesia |\n> | heart rate. |  |\n> |  | > Decreases sympathetic drive |\n> | * Best given in haemodynamically | decreases Blood pressure and |\n> | unstable patients and asthma | heart rate |\n\n- A. NMDA receptor agonist\n- **B. GABA receptor activation** (Correct Answer)\n- C. NMDA receptor antagonist\n- D. GABA receptor inactivation\n- E. Na channel blocker\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 35-year-old male patient is being treated in your ED for multiple lacerations sustained. Your junior colleague has infiltrated multiple wounds with 1% lidocaine. What is the mechanism of action of Lidocaine?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  Most widely used | e Used for regional |  Local anesthetic |\n> | local anesthetic | anesthesia (Bier | with longest |\n> |  | block) Recall | duration of action |\n> |  |  | (Recall) |\n> |  |  | e Used in Lumbar |\n> |  |  | epidural block |\n\n- A. Calcium channel blocker\n- B. Activation of K+ Channel\n- C. Activation of Na+ Channel\n- **D. Na+ Channel blocker** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Activation of Ca+ Channel\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLocal anesthetics reversibly inhibit nerve transmission by binding voltage-gated sodium channels\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 22-year-old male presents with palpitations. ECG was done at triage which shows S.V.T. You are considering adenosine, in which of the following conditions adenosine is contraindicated?\n\n- A. Diabetes mellitus\n- B. Myasthenia gravis\n- C. Blood dyscariasis\n- **D. Asthma** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Peripheral vascular disease\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAdenosine is treatment of choice in terminating supraventricular tachycardia\n\nContraindications of adenosine include:\n\nAsthma and COPD Decompensated heart failure Long QT syndrome Second- and third-degree heart block Severe hypotension\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 77-year-old nursing home resident is brought into the ED pyrexial with shortness of breath and purulent sputum. She is diagnosed with pneumonia.\n\nWhich pathogen is most in keeping with an endogenous infection source?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> nursing home infection (mainly pneumonia, UTI and wound\n> infection)\n> Legionella pneumophila Gram negative coccobacilli\n> e Reservoir: air-conditioning units\n> e High risk factors include older people, alcoholic and\n> immunosuppression\n> e Causing pneumonia\n> Note: Hyponatremia is important laboratory finding occurs more\n> common in Legionella pneumophila (Recall)\n\n- A. Legionella pneumophila\n- B. Chlamydophila psittaci\n- C. Mycoplasma pneumonia\n- D. Pneumocystis Jiroveci\n- **E. Pseudomonas aeruginosa** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 25 Years old male presents to the ED with complaint of fever, photophobia and neck stiffness. You suspect meningitis. Which tactic is used by Neisseria meningitidis to evade host defense?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Neisseria meningitidis | Secretes an protease. |\n> | Staphylococcus aureus | Staph aureus avoids opsonophagocytic by expressing |\n> |  | on its surface a capsule, clumping factor A, protein A |\n> | Streptococcus pneumoniae | Has a polysaccharide capsule which inhibits |\n> |  | phagocytosis by neutrophils |\n\n- A. Using motile flagellum,\n- B. Produces exotoxin\n- **C. Secretes an IgA1 protease** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Express protein A\n- E. Polysaccharide capsule which inhibits phagocytosis by neutrophils\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTactics of bacteria to evade host defense\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 26-year-old woman attends complaining of lower abdominal pain. She has a positive pregnancy test despite taking the contraceptive pill. She has epilepsy and has recently had her medication changed. Which antiepileptic is most likely to have had an effect on her oral contraceptive?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Increasing the rate of metabolism | Decrease hepatic clearance and |\n> | and hepatic clearance of concurrently | increase pharmacologic effect. |\n> | administered drugs, which typically |  |\n> | results in a decreased pharmacologic |  |\n> | effect. |  |\n> | Useful mnemonic SCRAP GP | Useful mnemonic: SICKFACES.COM |\n> | * Sulphonylureas | Sodium valproate |\n> | * Carbamazepine | * Isoniazid |\n> | Rifampicin | Cimetidine |\n> | * Alcohol | Ketoconazole |\n> | * Phenytoin (Recall) | Fluconazole |\n> | * Griseofulvin | * Alcohol |\n> | Phenobarbital | Chloramphenicol |\n> |  | Erythromycin |\n> |  | Sulfonamides |\n> |  | Ciprofloxacin |\n> |  | * Omeprazole |\n> |  | * Metronidazole |\n\n- A. Lamotrigine\n- **B. Carbamazepine** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Levetiracetam\n- D. Clobazam\n- E. Sodium valproate\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNote: In pregnancy on contraceptive pills, enzyme inducer drugs will increase hepatic clearance of contraceptive pills and pregnancy can still happen\n\nEnzyme inhibitors raise plasma theophylline levels\n\nEnzyme inducers decrease plasma theophylline level\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 55-Year-old male presents to the ED with minor bleeding from nose. He is currently on warfarin for atrial fibrillation, which one of the following drugs is best given to reverse adverse effects?\n\n- **A. Phytomenadione IV** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Cyproheptadine\n- C. Protamine sulfate\n- D. Idaracizumab\n- E. Fresh frozen plasma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIf INR> 8 and no bleeding = Phytomenadione orally If INR> 8 and minor bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv If INR> 8 and major bleeding = Phytomenadione I v and dried prothrombin complex (Recall) If INR 5-8 and no bleeding = Withhold warfarin If INR 5-8 and minor bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 32-year-old pregnant female presented to the ED with severe vomiting. Initial fluid resuscitation and antiemetic drug metoclopramide was given. In a short period of time, she has developed extrapyramidal symptoms. Which of the following is the drug of choice in her condition?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Lidocaine | Intralipid emulsion |\n> | Prilocaine | Methylene blue |\n> | Tricyclic antidepressants | Sodium bicarbonate |\n> | Metoclopramide | Procyclidine |\n> | Benzodiazepines | Flumazenil |\n> | Opioid | Naloxone |\n> | Organophosphate Poisoning | Atropine |\n> | Paracetamol poisoning | N-acetyl cysteine |\n> | Hyperkalemia | Ca gluconate |\n\n- A. Diazepam\n- B. Benztropine\n- C. Midazolam\n- D. Haloperidol with promethazine\n- **E. Procyclidine** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMetoclopramide is dopamine receptor antagonist (Recall) , commonly associated with extrapyramidal symptoms induce acute dystonic reaction s causing fascial and skeletal muscle spasm Antidote: Injection of anti-parkinsonism drug Su ch as "Procyclidine will abort dystonic attack.\n\nNote: Extrapyramidal symptoms are most commonly associated with antiemetics and antipsychotics.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation on a patient who requires a neuromuscular blocking agent. What is the contraindication of Suxamethonium?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Non-depolarizing neuromuscular | Depolarizing neuromuscular blocking |\n> | blocking agents | agents |\n> | > Atracurium,Rocuronium | > Suxamethonium |\n> | * Mechanism: Bind with Ach | Adepolarizing neuromuscular |\n> | receptors on neuromuscular | blocking agent, succinylcholine |\n> | junction blocking action of | adheres to post-synaptic |\n> | acetylcholine and prevent | cholinergic receptors of the motor |\n> | depolarization | endplate, inducing continuous |\n> |  | disruption that results in transient |\n> |  | fasciculations or involuntary |\n> |  | muscle contractions and |\n> |  | subsequent skeletal muscle |\n> |  | paralysis |\n> | Antidote: | * Contraindications: Hyperkalemia, |\n> | Neostigmine reverse the effects of | Risk of developing hyperkalemia |\n> | non-depolarization drugs | (Burn injury (Recall), Muscular |\n> |  | dystrophy and crush injury). |\n\n- A. Renal impairment\n- **B. Hyperkalemia** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Hyponatremia\n- D. Hypotension\n- E. Hypokalemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeuromuscular blocking agents\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 33-year-old man presented to the ED with 2 hours history of Palpitations ECG was done and it Shows SVT. He has no past medical history and denies any chest pain and breathlessness. His observations are recorded pulse 270bpm R.R 20 GCS 15/15. What is your 1st line treatment in this?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Regular narrow complex: SVT | Ventricular tachycardia: Amiodarone |\n> | vagal/ Valsalva | 300mg |\n> | maneuver. |  |\n> |  | Trosades-de-pointes: Magnesium |\n> | And then treat with Adenosine 6mg, | sulfate 2mg (Recall) |\n> | followed by 12 and 18mg |  |\n> |  | + All the enzyme inhibitor drugs prolong |\n> | Irregular narrow complex: Atrial | QT interval and induce Trosa-des- |\n> | fibrillation (follow AF protocol) | pointes |\n\n- A. IV Amiodarone\n- B. IV fluids\n- **C. Valsalva maneuver** (Correct Answer)\n- D. DC cardioversion\n- E. IV adenosine\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAny tachyarrhythmia presenting with adverse features should be treated with DC shock\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 5-year-old school-going child gets exposed to measles. What should parents be advised about returning to school?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. 5 days from onset of rash\n- B. After 24 hours of fever\n- C. 6 days after rash has gone\n- D. When all lesions are crusted over\n- **E. 4 days from onset of rash** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPatients are considered infectious for 3 days before the rash emerges and are advised to stay away from school or for four days from onset of the rash.\n\nNote: Regarding rubella: Patients are infectious from 7 days before the rash appears and until 4 days after the rash appears (Recall)\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 20:** 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-E, 5-B, 6-D, 7-D, 8-E, 9-C, 10-B, 11-A, 12-E, 13-B, 14-C, 15-E\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
56cb9718-050a-40ad-9f9b-e9feb55cb9b9	40e7b149-db6f-4acb-a33f-f4f9196a71c0	acute-limping-child	Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria	article	# Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Musculoskeletal (non-traumatic)\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nLimping Child:\nInfective causes:\n- Osteomyelitis\n> It's an infection of the bone\n> It's not very common, but it happens approximately 2% of\nchildren who present to an ED with limp will have\nosteomyelitis.\n> Investigations: Plain films, ESR, and CRP, however, MRI is\nbest modality to evaluate.\n>Management: non-operative with IV antibiotics\n- Septic arthritis\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> The hip is the most common joint affected, followed by the\nknee.\n> Clinical features: unable to weight bear and fever and joint\ninflammation\n> Investigation: lab work can be helpful, as well as adjunct use\nof US of the hip to look for an effusion.\n> Management: Prompt surgical drainage and IV antibiotics\n- Transient Synovitis\n> It's an inflammatory disease of the hip\n> The typical age is between 3 and 6 years, sometimes just after\na URI (upper respiratory infection) or viral illness\n> Clinical feature:\n> child should be well, afebrile, no or mild restriction of hip\nmovement.\n> Investigation: US to look for effusion\n> Child well response to NSAID\n> Kocher's criteria is useful to differentiate between septic\narthritis and transient synovitis.\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> The four predictors are:\n- Inability to walk\n- Feverof 38.5 °C or greater\n- ESR> 40 mm/h\n- WBC> 12,000/mm3 (12 x 109/L)\n> Mnemonics: WALK FEW\n> Probability of Septic Arthritis (Kocher)\n- 0Predictor - <0.2 % (less likely SA)\n- 1Predictor - 3%\n- 2Predictors - 40%\n- 3Predictors - 93.1%\n- 4Predictors - 99.6% (highest likelihood of SA)\n- Pyomyositis\n> It can be viral or bacterial and sometimes occurs after\nsubacute minor trauma\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Clinical features:\n> Pain , fever and Pt may appear irritable\n> Calves are affected and always tender\n> Management: viral cause manage with hydration and\nsupportive therapy\n> Bacterial cause manage with antibiotics and require\nadmission\nOther causes\n- Perthes' disease\n> It's also known as Legg-Calvé-Perthes.\n> It's an idiopathic avascular necrosis of the hip, usually\naffecting children from 3 to 12 years\n> Clinical features:\n> Pt present with a slow onset pain and with an antalgic gait.\nAnd limitation to internal rotation and abduction of the hip.\n> Investigation: frog-leg lateral view x-ray will reveal widening\nof joint space and decrease in size of femoral head with patchy\ndensity. MRI can evaluate better.\n> Complications: chronic pain and osteoarthritis\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nSlipped upper femoral epiphysis:\n> It's a displacement of femoral epiphysis from metaphysis\n> More common in children who are overweight and common\nin 10-15 years of age\n> It may associated with endocrine pathology\n> Clinical features:\n> Pt will present with severe pain with externally rotated leg.\n> There maybe shortening of the affected limb\n> Investigation: AP or frog-leg lateral view x-ray will reveal\ndisplacement of femoral head.\n> The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper\nfemoral epiphysis . In this sign, the line of Klein passes above\nthe femoral head instead of intersecting the femoral epiphysis\nas in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.\n> Management:\n> Urgent evaluation and management is crucial to avoid\nfurther displacement and development of avascular necrosis.\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nToddler fracture\n> A toddler's fracture is a spiral fracture of the tibia caused by a\ntwisting motion when falling.\n> Toddler's fractures occur in children 1 to 3 years of age.\n> most commonly in the distal of the tibia.\n> Clinical features:\n> Bone tenderness, redness or bruising and swelling in the\naffected area.\n> Toddler may refuse to bear weight\n> Investigation: initial x-ray maybe normal so repeat the film\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Management: Managed with an above or below knee\nbackslab.\nOsgood-Schlatter disease:\n> It is an apophysitis of the tibial tubercle resulting from\nrepeated normal stresses or overuse.\n> Children are usually between 10 and 15 years of age at time of\nonset; it more commonly occurs in running or jumping\nathletes.\n> Clinical features:\n> Pain and tenderness over the anterior aspect of the knee and\nthe tibial tuberosity.\n> Pain is aggravated by activities such as running, kneeling,\nsquatting, and climbing stairs\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Pain improve with rest\n> Investigations: x-ray is not required routinely but are usually\nobtained.\n> It may reveal soft tissue swelling and fragmentation of tibial\ntubercle.\n> Management: he disease is self-limited, and most patients'\nsymptoms respond to rest and temporary avoidance of the\noffending activity.\nSever's disease:\n> Sever's disease also known as calcaneal apophysitis.\n> It is an inflammation of the growth plate in the calcaneus\n(heel).\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> It is one of the most common causes of heel pain in growing\nchildren, especially children who are doing running and\njumping sports.\n> Clinical features:\n> Heel pain and tenderness underneath the heel\n> Mild swelling at the heel\n> Investigation: x-rays are generally not used to diagnose\nSever's disease but in some cases may request a heel x-ray\n> Management: Limiting the pain-inducing activities for a\nshort period of time may be beneficial\nSalter-harris classification:\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Salter-harris x-rays interpretation\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:31 AM Acute Limping Child | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n\n## Clinical Figures, Diagnostic Algorithms & Radiographs\n\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Click any figure below to examine diagnostic algorithms, anatomy landmarks, or radiological details at full clinical resolution.\n\n\n\n![Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 1)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/acute-limping-child/figure_01_p1.png)\n\n*Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Figure 1: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 1) (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/acute-limping-child/figure_02_p2.png)\n\n*Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Figure 2: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2) (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 4)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/acute-limping-child/figure_03_p4.png)\n\n*Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Figure 3: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 4) (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 6)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/acute-limping-child/figure_04_p6.png)\n\n*Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Figure 4: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 6) (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 7)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/acute-limping-child/figure_05_p7.png)\n\n*Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Figure 5: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 7) (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 8)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/acute-limping-child/figure_06_p8.png)\n\n*Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria - Figure 6: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 8) (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Acute Limping Child Differential & Kocher Criteria**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
34bb4b5e-df64-4cd8-9509-97ebd8aca54d	40e7b149-db6f-4acb-a33f-f4f9196a71c0	knee-injuries	Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment	article	# Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Musculoskeletal (non-traumatic)\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nGeneral approach to knee\ninjuries\nHistory\nKnee injuries most commonly results from sport, particularly\nfootball and rugby.\nCarefully elicit the exact mechanism of injury, as it provides\nclues to the diagnosis.\nEg: valgus stress can damage medial collateral ligament\nVarus stress can damage lateral collateral ligament\nUnhappy triad anterior cruciate ligament associated with\nmedial collateral and/or medial meniscal injuries may tear\nduring forced flexion or hyperextension.\nPosterior cruciate injuries may follow falls or dashboard\nimpact where the tibia is forced backwards violently.\nRapid-onset tense swelling in a knee is usually an acute\nhaemarthrosis.\nIn a hot, swollen, painful, and stiff knee without a history of\nsignificant trauma, consider and exclude septic arthritis.\nExamination\nCarefully examine both legs with Pt supine\nLook for bruising, swelling, redness, abrasions, or other\nwounds\n- Assess tone and bulk of the quadriceps muscle, and compare\nsides\n- Assess the collateral ligaments: valgus and varus stress tests\n- Assess cruciate ligaments with anterior and posterior drawer\ntest.\nOttawa knee rule\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nUse the Ottawa knee rules to assist the decision (in those\naged between 18 and 55y) as to whether or not to X-ray.\nTenderness at the head of the fibula\nIsolated tenderness of the patella\nInability to flex knee to 90 degrees\nInability to transfer weight for four steps both immediately\nafter the injury and in the ED.\nPatellar fracture\nMOI\nA direct blow or fall onto the patella or sudden violent knee\nflexion or contraction of the quadriceps muscle.\nClinical features: pain, swelling, crepitus, and difficulty\nextending the knee. There may be associated haemarthrosis.\nDiagnosis: X-rays may be difficult to interpret. Take care not\nto mistake a bipartite patella for a fracture. the accessory\nbone is typically upper and lateral part of patella in bipartite\npatella.\nManagement:\nVertical patellar fracture : Give analgesia ,immobilize in a nonweightbearing above-knee POP backslab, supply crutches,\nand arrange orthopaedic follow-up\nTranverse fractures:\nHigh risk of displacement due to the pull of the quadriceps.\nGive analgesia ,immobilization in a POP backslab, and refer\nto the orthopaedic team for probable ORIF.\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nBipartite patella\nBipartite patella is caused by the kneecap failing to form\nproperly during childhood.\n\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nDislocation of Patella\nTypically dislocate laterally\nPt may be a history of recurrent dislocation\nClinical features: Painful knee, held in flexion, with obvious\nlateral displacement of the patella.\nNote: X-rays are not generally required prior to reduction of\nthe dislocation\nReduction:\nusually be achieved using Entonox\nStand on the lateral side of the affected limb and hold the\naffected knee gently. Using a thumb, lever the patella\nmedially in one smooth, firm movement, whilst gently\nextending the knee at the same time.\nOnce reduced, obtain X-rays, immobilize in a canvas ('cricket\npad') back-splint or backslab cast POP, provide analgesia,\nand arrange orthopaedic follow-up.\nMRI scan is recommended at follow-up may help to identify\nthe extent of damage.\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nKnee dislocation\nPrimarily occurs in younger population\nAnterior dislocations are more common than posterior\ndislocation.\nMOI : It is a result of tremendous ligamentous disruption due\nto either high-velocity mechanisms eg: motor vehicle\ncrashes, fall from height or low-velocity mechanisms, martial\narts, walking, or trampoline falls.\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nDislocations can also occur spontaneously in morbidly obese\npatients\nThe popliteal artery and nerve are commonly injured,\ntherefore, document the distal pulse and sensation\nManagement:\nGive the analgesia (opiod)\nReduce the dislocation by simple traction on the limb under\nGA or sedation\n- Check distal pulses and sensation after reduction\nImmobilize in a long leg POP backslab, and arrange\northopaedic admission.\nAny sign of vascular injury, need emergent vascular surgery\nconsultation for surgical exploration and possible\nangiography in the operating room.\nComplications\nInjury to popliteal artery and vein\nPeroneal nerve injury\nCompartment syndrome\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nTibial plateau fracture\nFalls onto an extended leg can cause compression fractures\nof the proximal tibia\nValgus stress: leads to crush or fracture the lateral tibial\nplateau. It's commonly seen in pedestrians injured following\nimpact with car bumpers\nVarus stress: leads to crush or fracture the medial tibial\nplateau and usually associated with rupture of the opposite\ncollateral ligaments\nClinical features: tenderness over medial and lateral margin\nof tibia. Look for swelling, haemarthrosis, or ligamentous\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\ninstability.\nDiagnosis : Radiograph or CT\nManagement: Give analgesia, immobilization in a long POP\nbackslab and refer to orthopedic for ± ORIF and bone\ngrafting\nAdmit all patients with acute haemarthrosis.\nSmall isolated avulsion without haemarthrosis require\nimmobilization, crutches, and analgesia, and arrange\northopaedic follow-up.\nSoft tissue Knee injuries\nAcute haemarthrosis\nAn acute haemarthrosis indicates serious injury\nCommon causes include cruciate ligament rupture, tibial\navulsion, and tibial plateau or other fractures\nClinical features: Rapid-onset swelling following a knee\ninjury.\nKnee often warm, tense, and painful\nDiagnosis: Radiograph will reveal effusion or normal\nManagement: Give analgesia and aspirate haemarthrosis\nhowever require strict aseptic technique. Or refer to\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\northopedic.\n\nCruciate ligament rupture\nAnterior cruciate ligament injury\nAn audible 'pop' at the time of injury is highly suggestive of\nanterior cruciate ligament rupture.\nAnterior cruciate ligament tears often occur in association\nwith tears of the medial collateral ligament and medial\nmeniscus (unhappy triad)\nExamination will reveal presence of haemarthrosis,\nabnormal increase anterior glide of the tibia ('+ve anterior\ndrawer test').\n+ve Lachman and pivot shift test\nX-rays reveal avulsion of the anterior tibial spine.\nManagement: Give analgesia and refer to orthopedic.\nPosterior cruciate ligament injury\nLess common\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nThe tibia may appear to sag back when the knee is flexed, so\nthe tibia can be pulled into a more normal position, causing a\n'false +ve' anterior drawer test.\nX-rays may reveal the relevant posterior tibial spine to be\navulsed\nManagement: Give analgesia and refer to orthopedic\nCollateral ligament injuries\nMedial collateral ligament (MCL)\nMCL injuries of the knee are one of the commonest sportsrelated knee injuries. MCL injuries can occur in almost all\nsports and all age groups.\nAn audible 'pop' is often reported at the time of injury,\nMOI -It usually result from a direct blow to the knee causing\nexcessive valgus stress.\nIt is commonly injured in association with anterior cruciate\nligament(ACL) and medial miniscal tear\nExamination will reveal tenderness along the medial aspect\nof the knee and bruising, and a knee effusion\nManagement - Provide crutches, analgesia, quadriceps\nexercises, and orthopaedic follow-up\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nRuptured patellar tendon\nClincal features:\nPt completely unable to straight or raise the leg and a highriding patella\nThere is frequently an associated avulsion of the tibial\ntuberosity.\nManagement - Refer to orthopedic\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nRupture of quadricep tendon\nMOI: it results Complete rupture of the distal quadriceps\ninsertion can result from a direct injury or from sudden,\nviolent contraction of the quadriceps muscle.\nExamination reveal complete inability to straight or raise the\nleg.\nManagement: Refer to orthopedic for repair\nLocked knee\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nThe "locked knee" describes when a knee cannot actively or\npassively fully extend\nThe most common cause of an acutely locked knee is a torn\nmeniscus (meniscal injury)\nInvestigations - Obtain knee X-rays (including a tunnel\nview).\nManagement - Give analgesia, and refer to orthopedic for\narthroscopy.\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:24 AM Knee injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n\n## Clinical Figures, Diagnostic Algorithms & Radiographs\n\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Click any figure below to examine diagnostic algorithms, anatomy landmarks, or radiological details at full clinical resolution.\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_01_p3.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 1: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3) (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_02_p3.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 2: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3) (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 5)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_03_p5.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 3: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 5) (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 7)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_04_p7.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 4: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 7) (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 8)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_05_p8.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 5: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 8) (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 9)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_06_p9.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 6: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 9) (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 11)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_07_p11.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 7: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 11) (Page 11 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 12)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_08_p12.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 8: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 12) (Page 12 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 13)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/knee-injuries/figure_09_p13.png)\n\n*Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment - Figure 9: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 13) (Page 13 Reference)*\n\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Knee Injuries, Ottawa Knee Rules & Ligamentous Assessment**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
70d24beb-e789-4784-b433-07ff3c0460f7	bb9a3e24-9932-4721-8fd4-8b24fa26b67d	structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium	Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium	article	Heart and pericardium\nPericardium\nFibro-serous, fluid-filled sack that surrounds the muscular\nbody of the heart and the roots of the great vessels. Great\nvessels include: The aorta, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins,\nand the superior and inferior vena cavae.\nPericardium is made up of two main layers fibrous and serous\npericardium.\nFibrous Pericardium: Surrounds the heart and forms a\nprotective covering for the cardiac Chambers. It also acts as an\nanchor for the heart that keeps it restricted in the chest cavity\nby binding itself to the adjoining regions. It also prevents the\noverexpansion of the heart when there is a rise in the volume of\nblood inside the body.\nSerous Pericardium: It is closed sac within fibrous pericardium\nhaving Visceral & Parietal layer, separated by the fluid-filled\npericardial cavity\nThe visceral layer of serous pericardium (epicardium):\ncovers the surface of the heart. It also reflects onto the great\nvessels This transparent membrane surrounds and protects\nthe heart. It also contains a thin film of serous fluid that\nallows the heart to move and provides it with a frictionless\nenvironment to expand as well as contract.\nThe parietal layer lines the internal surface of the fibrous\npericardium.\nInnervation of the pericardium\nThe visceral pericardium: Receives autonomic innervation\nfrom the cardiac plexus.\n\nThe parietal and fibrous pericardium are innervated mainly by\nthe phrenic nerve, for this reason that pain related to a\npericardial problem may be referred to the supraclavicular\nregion of the shoulder or the lateral neck area.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pericardiocentesis:  surgical puncture of the pericardial cavity\n> for the aspiration of fluid, which is necessary to relieve the\n> pressure of accumulated fluid in the heart. A needle is inserted\n> into the pericardial cavity 1 - 2 cm inferior to the left of the\n> xiphochondral junction (between the xiphisternum and the\n> left costal margin)\n> Complications:\n> Pneumothorax secondary to lung puncture\n> Aspiration of ventricular blood instead of pericardial blood\n> Laceration of ventricular epicardium or myocardium\n> Laceration of coronary artery or vein\n> Ventricular fibrillation\n> Puncture of great vessels with worsening pericardial\n> tamponade\n> Heart:\n> The heart is a myocardial muscular pump consisting of four\n> chambers, two auricles, four valves, and a muscular septum all\n> enclosed within a fluid filled sac, the pericardium\n> The heart wall is composed of three distinct layers:\n\n### 1: The epicardium (outermost layer)\n\n### 2: The myocardium (middle layer, comprised of muscular\n\nfibers)\n\n### 3: The endocardium (innermost layer), lines the cavities and\n\nvalves of the heart\nBorders\n\nThe heart has four borders:\nThe right border: right atrium and is in line with the superior\nand inferior vena cavae.\nThe left border: the left ventricle and very slightly by the left\nauricle.\nThe superior border: is where the great vessels enter and\nleave the heart. It is formed by both atria\nThe inferior border: The right ventricle, and slightly by the left\nventricle near the apex.\nThe heart has been described as "a pyramid which has fallen\nover". The apex of this pyramid pointing in an anterior-inferior\ndirection.\nIn its typical anatomical orientation, the heart has 5 surfaces,\nformed by different internal divisions of the heart:\nAnterior (or sternocostal): Right ventricle.\nPosterior (or base): Left atrium.\nInferior (or diaphragmatic): Left and right ventricles.\nRight pulmonary: Right atrium.\nLeft pulmonary: Left ventricle.\nNOTE: Esophagus lies immediately posterior to the base (left\natrium). The term cardiac dysphagia referred as impairment of\nswallowing due to esophageal compression from a dilated left\natrium\nCardiac Conduction system: collection of specialized\nconduction cells and nodes that initiate and co-ordinate\ncontraction of the heart muscle. It consists of:\nSinoatrial node\nAtrioventricular node\nAtrioventricular bundle (bundle of His)\nPurkinje fibres\nThe sinoatrial node, the cardiac pacemaker, is located at the\nsuperior end of the crista terminalis at the junction of the\nsuperior vena cava and the right atrium. Impulses generated\nby the sinoatrial node are propagated by the atrial myocardium to the atrioventricular node, Here the impulse is\nbriefly delayed before being spread to the ventricles.\nThe atrioventricular bundle (bundle of His) is a direct\ncontinuation of the atrioventricular node and continues along\nthe lower margin of the membranous part of the\ninterventricular septum before dividing into the right and left\nbundles which continue themselves as Purkinje fibres, the final\ncomponent of the conducting system.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> The sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker. The SAN\n> will normally discharge at a rate of 60-100 bpm. if the\n> impulse generated in the Sa node is blocked, a group of cells\n> further down the heart will become its pacemaker.\n\nThe atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary pacemaker\nand will normally discharge at about 40-60 beats per minute.\nAV nodal abnormality is responsible for prolonged PR\ninterval (1st degree heart block)\nGreat Vessels of the heart: Mnemonic\n\n## (Sippa)\n\n#### Superior vena cava\n\n#### Inferior vena cava\n\nPulmonary arteries\nPulmonary veins\nAorta\n\n### 1. Superior vena cava: Receives deoxygenated blood from the\n\nupper body excluding the lungs and heart superior to the\ndiaphragm, delivering it to the right atrium\n\n### 2. Inferior vena cava: receives deoxygenated blood from the\n\nlower body inferior to the diaphragm, delivering it back to the\nheart\n\n### 3. Pulmonary arteries: Receive deoxygenated blood from the\n\nright ventricle and deliver it to the lungs for gas exchange to\ntake place through pulmonary valve\n\n### 4. Pulmonary veins: Receive oxygenated blood from the lungs,\n\ndelivering it to the left side of the heart to be pumped back\naround the body.\n\n### 5. Aorta: Largest artery carry oxygenated blood pumped by left\n\nside of the heart to all body. The aorta arises from the aortic\norifice at the base of the left ventricle, with inflow via the\naortic valve. First segment is known as the ascending aorta,\nwhich lies within the pericardium. The second continuous\nsegment is the arch of the aorta, from which branch the\nmajor arteries to the head, neck and upper limbs. These are:\nBrachiocephalic trunk\nLeft common carotid artery\nLeft subclavian artery\nAfter the arch of the aorta, the aorta then becomes the\ndescending aorta which continues down through the\ndiaphragm into the abdomen\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Areas of auscultation of heart:\n> Cardiac blood supply:\n\n### 1: The Right coronary artery supplies:\n\nThe right atrium and most of right ventricle\nThe sinoatrial and atrioventricular node\nPosterior interventricular artery, also known as the posterior\ndescending artery (PDA), is a branch of the right coronary\nartery supply posterior  of interventricular septum and\nposterior aspect of right and left ventricle. Most common\nartery infracted in posterior wall MI\nNote: Occlusion of right coronary artery can result into\nischemia of SA and AV node and subsequently bradycardia\nor heart block\n\n### 2: The left coronary artery supplies via main branches below:\n\nAnterior interventricular branch (LAD)\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium/figure_01_p5.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nLCX\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Localization of lesion in ST elevation MI and occluded coronary\n> artery. Only remember myocardial area and occluded coronary\n> artery.\n\nEasy to remember:\nSeptal and Anterior wall MI: LAD (Anterior interventricular\nbranch) occluded.\nLateral wall MI: LCX occluded.\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium/figure_02_p6.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium/figure_03_p6.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d2f1f31a-6f73-4d27-9466-390ee9083d37	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	diverticulr-disease	Diverticular Disease & Diverticulitis	article	# Diverticular Disease & Acute Diverticulitis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Diverticulosis\n- Asymptomatic, At may present with a large, painless rectal bleed Clinical features\n- FBC, CRP,\n- Faecal occult blood test Investigations\n- Kidney function test\n- Endoscopy, colonoscopy, CT colonography to confirm the presence of diverticula. Advise\n- the condition is asymptomatic and no specific treatments are Diverticular disease Antermittent abdominal pain in the left lower quadrant (pain may be triggered by eating and may be relieved by the passage of stool or flatus).\n- Other symptoms\n- Bloating, constipation, diarrhoea, nausea. nectal bleeding.\n- Fever.\n- Dysuria Same\n- Arrange urgent admission a person with confirmed diverticular Diverticulitis\n- Constant abdominal pain (which is usually severe and starts in the hypogastrium before localizing in the left lower quadrant).\n- Sudden change in bowel habit and significant rectal bleeding or passage of mucus from the rectum.\n- Tenderness in the left lower quadrant, a palpable abdominal mass or distention on abdominal examination Same\n- Referral pathway\n- Urgent refer to surgery if patient has acute complicated diverticuliY\n\n- needed.\n- Eat a healthy balanced diet including whole grains, fruit and vegetables\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- drink adequate fluid\n- Consider offering bulk- forming laxatives for people with constipation disease has significant rectal bleeding (for example, if the person is haemodynamically unstable), as urgent blood transfusion may be required. Af a person is symptomatic but does not need admission:\n- Advise Increase fiber diet and fluid intake\n- Quit smoking, weight loss\n- Avoid NSAID & Opiod\n- Offer Paracetamol for pain\n- Offer Bulk-forming laxatives if a high-fibre diet is unacceptable to the person. not prescribe antibiotics is (for example with peritonitis and sepsis), and for people who do not improve with medical treatment.\n- Offer an oral antibiotic if the person with acute diverticulitis is systemically unwell. but does not meet the criteria for referral.\n- Prescribe co-amoxiclav  mg tds for 5 days cefalexin 500 mg BD/tds + metronidazole 400 mg tds for 5 days.\n- For people with acute diverticulitis who are systemically well:\n- Offer paracetamol for pain\n- Advise lifestyle diet modification ( increase fiber diet & fluid intake).\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Diverticulr Disease - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/diverticulr-disease/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Diverticulr Disease — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Diverticulr Disease - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/diverticulr-disease/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Diverticulr Disease — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Diverticulr Disease - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/diverticulr-disease/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Diverticulr Disease — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
cec141a6-5f58-44d6-8209-d62e3a3b3817	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-25	Quiz 25: Traumatic Brain Injury, Tuberculosis & Enzyme Induction	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 25\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 25 years old pregnant lady presents to the ED with complaint of nausea, vomiting, pelvic cramps and spotting. She gives history of taking doxycycline prescribed by her GP. Which of the following is common side effect of doxycycline use in pregnancy?\n\n- A. CNS malformation\n- B. Cardiac defect\n- **C. Tooth discoloration** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Congenital goiter\n- E. Neural tube defect\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIntake of tetracycline (Doxycycline) by pregnant women results in the discoloration and deformity of teeth in the fetus. It can also produce impairment of bone growth. tetracyclines are also contraindicated in children less than 10 years\n\nNote:\n\nDoxycycline is used for malaria prophylaxis (Recall)\n\nLithium can cause fetal goiter and cardiac abnormalities. (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 32-year-old presents to the ED with complaint of cough with sputum, fever, and night sweats for 1 month. On examination, there is hilar lymphadenopathy. Regarding features of Active TB accompanied with which one of the following features?\n\n- A. Usually lower lobe consolidation on X-Ray\n- B. ZN staining negative\n- **C. X-Ray cavitations** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Fibrotic lung\n- E. Positive Mantoux test\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn the case of active pulmonary TB, a patient can have crepitations, and bronchial breath sounds, a nd specially over upper lobes cavitations or consolidations The Mantoux test is a widely used test for latent TB (Recall): It involves injecting a small amount of a substance called PPD tuberculin into the skin of your forearm. It's also called the tuberculin skin test (TST).\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 15-year-old girl is found to be hyperglycemic. Urine testing reveals glucose. This urinalysis result is most likely to be due to what process in the carrier protein of proximal tubule?\n\n- A. . Inborn error of metabolism\n- B. . Activation\n- C. Inhibition\n- D. Destruction\n- **E. Saturation** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe renal tubule plays a significant role in glucose reabsorption. If the plasma glucose rises, renal tubular reabsorption of glucose will increase linearly until it reaches its maximum tubular resorptive capacity. The capacity of the proximal tubule t o reabsorb glucose to prevent its passing to the urine is known as the renal threshold. At threshold, the system becomes saturated and the maximal reabsorption rate, the glucose transport maximum is reached and no more glucose can be absorbed. Due to increase in plasma glucose, the filtered glucose exceeds the capacity of the tubular system and the kidneys begin excreting it in th e urine, the beginning of glycosuria\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 30 years old laborer is brought into the ED by Ambulance after falling approximately 6m on building site and sustained fracture to the base of skull. On Examination there is numbness over lip and maxilla.\n\nWhich part of skull bone is fractured?\n\n- A. Foramen ovale\n- **B. Foramen rotundum** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Stylomastoid foramen\n- D. Foramen spinosum\n- E. Superior orbital fissure\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMandibular branch of trigeminal: Passes through foramen ovale\n\nMaxillary branch of trigeminal: Passes through foramen rotundum\n\nNote: Maxillary division of trigeminal via its branch infraorbital nerve gives sensory innervation to lower eyelid, cheek, side of nose and upper lip (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 30 years old laborer is brought into the ED by Ambulance after falling approximately 6m on building site and sustain head injury. His Gcs 5/15 early treatment given, patient intubated stabilized and shifted to icu. After some time, patient started to deteriorate again with uncontrolled hypertension and bradycardia. Which one of the following reflex leads to this condition?\n\n- **A. Cushing reflex** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Medullary reflex\n- C. Autonomic reflex\n- D. Baroreceptor reflex\n- E. Cortical reflex\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCushing reflex is physiological response to increase intracranial pressure resulting in Cushing tria d of:\n\nIncreased intracranial pressure Increased blood pressure Bradycardia\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nWhat type of immunity is provided by intravenous (IV) administration of immunoglobulin G?\n\n- A. . Humoral\n- B. . Active\n- **C. Passive** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Both humoral and cell mediated\n- E. Cell mediated\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIntravenous administration of immunoglobulin provides the patient with passive immunity. Cellular(cell-mediated) immune response acts directly to destroy pathogens without using antibodies b ut instead fights infection from macrophages th at kills pathogens. The humoral immune response (or antibody-mediated response) protects the body by circulating antibodies to fight against pathogens (e.g., bacteria).\n\nPassive immunity occurs when antibodies are transferred by antibodies from an immune host, such as from a placenta to a fetus. Passive immunity is short lived. Active immunity is longer livedand comes from the host itself.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nRegarding the movements of extrinsic muscles of shoulder joint, which one of the following muscles performs adduction and internal rotation?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Muscle | Nerve supply Nerve Function |  |  |\n> |  |  | root |  |\n> | Trapezius |  | XI | Upper fibers elevate the scapula; middle |\n> |  | accessory | cranial fibers pull scapula medially; lower fibers pull medial |  |\n> |  | nerve (motor) |  | border of scapula downward |\n> |  |  | (spinal |  |\n> |  | and C3 |  |  |\n> |  |  | part) |  |\n> |  | (sensory) |  |  |\n> | Latissimus Thoracodorsal |  |  | Extends, adducts, and |\n> | dorsi | nerve | 8, | medially rotates the |\n> |  |  |  | arm |\n> | Levator | C3C4and |  | Raises medial border of |\n> |  |  | 5 | scapula |\n> |  | scapular |  |  |\n> |  | nerve |  |  |\n> | Rhomboid Dorsal |  | C4,C5 Raises medial border of |  |\n> | minor | scapular |  | scapula upward and medially |\n> |  | nerve |  |  |\n> | Rhomboid Dorsal |  | C4,C5 Raises medial border of |  |\n> | major | scapular |  | scapula upward and medially |\n> |  | nerve |  |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  |  | W/Z |  | Levator scapulae |\n> | us |  |  |  | SS |  |\n> | Trapezius | ---- |  | = | Ze - Rhomboid minor |  |\n> |  |  | als |  |  | Rhomboid maj |\n> |  |  | ols |  | Va! | omboid major |\n> |  |  |  | BAA |  |  |\n> |  |  |  |  | ZED % |  |\n> | Latissimus dorsi |  |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | \\ | AY: 4 IX |  |  |\n> |  | Lumbar triangle |  |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Levator scapulae\n- B. Rhomboid major\n- C. Rhomboid minor\n- **D. Latissimus dorsi** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Pectoralis minor\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nExtrinsic muscles of shoulder Joint:\n\nClinical pearl: Ask the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if the patient is unable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve injury and paralysis of trapezius muscle\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\n-blocker was prescribed for hypertension in a female asthma patient. After about a week of treatment, the asthma attacks got worse, and the patient was asked to stop taking the beta-blocker. Which of the following beta-blockers would you suggest as an alternative in this patient that is less likely to worsen her asthma?\n\n- A. Propranolol\n- B. Sotalol\n- C. Labetalol\n- **D. Atenolol** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Carvedilol\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBeta-blockers antagonize catecholamine-induce d increases in heart rate, reduce blood pressure and improve left ventricular function, producingproven clinical benefits in people with cardiovascular disease but blocking airway beta2-receptors c an cause severe and sometimes fatal bronchoconstriction in people with asthma, although cardio selective beta1-blockers may be safer than non-selective\n\nThe cardio-selective beta-blockers include atenolol, Bisoprolol, Esmolol, Acebutolol, Metoprolol, an d Nebivolol.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 26-year-old male returns home from a holiday and complains of three days of dysuria and a purulent urethral discharge. He is diagnosed to be a case of gonorrhea.\n\nWhich of the following is the appropriate treatment?\n\n- **A. Ceftriaxone IM** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Streptomycin\n- C. Gentamicin\n- D. Clarithromycin\n- E. Vancomycin IV\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae causes urethritis in 1st week (3-4 days) post exposure and unprotected s ex while Chlamydia trachomatis causes urethritis after 2-3 weeks post exposure and unprotected sex\n\nGonorrhoea is usually treated with a single antibiotic injection (usually in the buttocks or thigh). With effective treatment, most of your symptom s should improve within a few days.\n\nFirst line IM Ceftriaxone 500mg single dose.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 44 years old woman presents to the ED with pain in his right eye, after initial assessment you suspect corneal ulcer. Regarding sensory innervation, which one of the following nerves carry pain sensation from cornea?\n\n- A. Facial nerve\n- B. Maxillary division of trigeminal nerve\n- **C. Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve\n- E. Optic nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOpthalmic division of trigeminal nerve gives sensory innervation to:\n\nForehead and scalp Upper eyelid and conjunctiva Cornea Dorsum of nose\n\nHigh Yield:\n\n1: Supraorbital nerve: (Branch of Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve) innervate Skin of upper eyelid, conjunctiva and upper forehead\n\n2: Infraorbital nerve (Branch of maxillary division of trigeminal nerve) innervating skin of lower eyelid, side of nose, cheek and upper lip 2: Supratrochlear nerve:(Branch of Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve) innervate Skin of lower forehead\n\n3: Naso ciliary nerve (Branch of Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve) innervate cornea, ciliary body and iris.\n\nInfratrochlear nerve: Bridge of nose\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nThe following anti-tubercular drug is a potent CYP450 enzyme inducer?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | * Increasing the rate of metabolism | * Decrease hepatic clearance and |\n> | and hepatic clearance of concurrently | increase pharmacologic effect. |\n> | administered drugs, which typically |  |\n> | results in a decreased pharmacologic |  |\n> | effect. |  |\n> | Useful mnemonic SCRAP GP | Useful mnemonic: SICKFACES.COM |\n> | Sulphonylureas | * Sodium valproate |\n> | * Carbamazepine | * Isoniazid |\n> | Rifampicin | * Cimetidine |\n> | * Alcohol | * Ketoconazole |\n> | Phenytoin (Recall) | Fluconazole |\n> | * Griseofulvin | * Alcohol |\n> | Phenobarbital | Chloramphenicol |\n> |  | Erythromycin |\n> |  | * Sulfonamides |\n> |  | * Ciprofloxacin |\n> |  | * Omeprazole |\n> |  | * Metronidazole |\n\n- A. Ethambutol\n- B. Pyrazinamide\n- **C. Rifampicin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Streptomycin\n- E. Isoniazid\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRifampin is a potent inducer of cytochrome P45 0 enzymes Side effects of Anti TB drugs:\n\n1: Rifampicin: Orange-red discoloration of body fluids, Hepatitis 2: Isoniazid: Hepatitis, Peripheral neuritis 3: Pyrazinamide: Hepatotoxicity, Hyperuricemia 4: Ethambutol: Optic neuritis, Red-green color blindness 5: Streptomycin: ototoxicity, Nephrotoxicity, and contraindicated in pregnancy\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 22-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of pain in his fingers, while assessing movements you suspect loss of extension of distal interphalangeal joint, DIP joint injury causes?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Mallet deformity | Loss of extension of distal interphalangeal joint or fixed |\n> |  | flexion of DIP. |\n> | Boutonniere deformity | Loss of extension of proximal interphalangeal joint. |\n\n- A. Metacarpo-phalangeal joint subluxation\n- B. Z-Deformity\n- **C. Mallet finger** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Carpal tunnel syndrome\n- E. Boutonniere deformity\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 43-year-old driver by profession presents to the ED with seasonal allergies. Which of the following medications would be most appropriate for the management of his allergy symptoms?\n\n- **A. Fexofenadine** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Chlorphenamine\n- C. Cyclizine\n- D. Hydroxyzine\n- E. Doxylamine\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFirst generation H1 antihistamine are sedating a nd is contraindicated in the treatment of pilots, machine operator and drivers.\n\nThe non-sedating antihistamine include (Recall): Fexofenadine, loratadine, Desloratadine, and certizine\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 48-year-old woman complains of bilateral leg swelling which has developed over the last week. She has recently been diagnosed with hypertension and commenced on treatment 2 weeks ago. Which hypertensive is most likely to have been prescribed?\n\n- A. . Sotalol\n- **B. Amlodipine** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Ramipril\n- D. . Atenolol\n- E. Bendroflumethiazide\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPedal edema is one of the most common adverse effects of calcium channel blockers. It has been more common with dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as: amlodipine, felodipine, nifedipine (the main cause for calcium channel blocker induced edema increased capillary hydrostatic pressure by arteriolar dilation). but it also seems to occur to a lesser extent with non-dihydropyridine such as verapamil and diltiazem.\n\nThe most common side effect of verapamil is constipation (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 50 years old male with history of RTA brought into the ED in Shock. After initial assessment CVP line placed in his left subclavian, what is the most common complication of placing CVP in subclavian vein?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Site | Internal jugular vein |  | Subclavian vein |\n> |  | * Low risk of pneumothorax |  | * Better landmark in obese Pt |\n> |  | + Low failure rate |  | Easy to maintain dressing |\n> | Advantage | + | access | + Accessible when airway |\n> |  | + Easy to stop procedural |  | control is being established |\n> |  | related bleeding with direct |  | + Low chance of infection |\n> |  | pressure. |  |  |\n> |  | + Risk of carotid artery injury |  |  |\n> |  | + Dressing and catheter |  | Low success rate with |\n> |  | difficult to maintain |  | inexperienced |\n> | Disadvantage] |  degrees Thoracic duct injury on left |  | . |\n> |  |  |  | Catheter malposition |\n> |  | + Poor landmark for obese Pt |  | more common |\n> |  | + | during | Interfere with CPR |\n> |  | emergency when airway |  | . |\n> |  | control being established |  | OF |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. External jugular injury\n- B. Hydrothorax\n- C. High risk of carotid artery injury\n- **D. Pneumothorax** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Local haematoma\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
0aaf99bd-24b1-4bca-a308-25974a90cd74	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	lymphatic-drainage-upper-limb-breast	Lymphatic drainage of the upper limb and breast	article	## Principles\n\n- Superficial lymphatics travel with the superficial veins. Deep lymphatics travel with the deep arteries and veins.\n- All lymph from the upper limb ends in the **axillary nodes**.\n\n## Node groups\n\n- **Axillary nodes:** drain the whole upper limb, and also the lateral part of the breast, the chest wall and the upper abdominal wall. They are arranged as pectoral (anterior), subscapular (posterior), humeral (lateral), central and apical groups.\n- **Infraclavicular (deltopectoral) nodes:** lie between deltoid and pectoralis major beside the cephalic vein. They receive lymph from the superficial vessels along the radial (lateral) side of the hand, forearm and arm.\n- **Supratrochlear (cubital) nodes:** lie in the superficial fascia just above the medial epicondyle. They receive lymph from the medial fingers, medial hand and medial forearm. An enlarged, tender supratrochlear node points to infection on that side of the hand or forearm.\n\n## Breast drainage\n\n- **About 75% goes to the axillary nodes:** the lateral and upper quadrants, including the axillary tail.\n- **About 20% goes to the parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes:** the medial quadrants. These can also spread to the opposite breast.\n- The rest goes to posterior intercostal and other nodes.\n- Breast cancer in the axillary tail most often involves the axillary nodes first.\n\n## Clinical pearls\n\n- Sentinel node biopsy or axillary clearance is used to stage breast cancer.\n- Surgery risks: **long thoracic nerve** injury causes a winged scapula, **thoracodorsal nerve** injury weakens latissimus dorsi (adduction and medial rotation), and **intercostobrachial nerve** injury causes numbness of the medial upper arm.\n- Lymphoedema of the arm can follow axillary clearance or radiotherapy.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
053fd704-f0a0-4396-918b-a3d8b43adfb5	e085e872-faa5-4dd4-a818-e9304c9a4b61	child-abuse-non-accidental-injuries	Child Abuse & Non-Accidental Injury (NAI) Recognition	article	# Child Abuse & Non-Accidental Injury (NAI) Recognition\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Safeguarding & Psycho-social emergencies in children\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nChild abuse: head injuries,\nwounds and burns\nFollowing features suggest NAI\n- Bruise over medial aspect of upper arms or thighs\n- Bruise in non-mobile babies\n- bruises on any non-bony part of the body or face including\nthe eyes, ears and buttocks\n- Uncommon injuries bilaterally\n- Finger 'imprinting eg: slap mark\n- Imprints or marks of belt or stick\n- Petechiae on the face may reflect smothering and\nasphyxiation (it was suggested that 2-10% of SIDS may have\nbeen smothered), however, petechiae also occur with forceful\ncoughing or vomiting.\nBruising\n- Bruise over the knees and shins is consider as normal finding\nin children (toddler)\n- bruises over the lateral aspect of their elbows and hips in\nolder children during play are considered as normal finding.\nFractures\n> Following fracture suggests NAI\n- Rib and spinal fractures\n- Multiple fractures of different ages\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:34 AM Child Abuse-Non-Accidental Injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n- Fracture in infant who are not independently mobile\n- Long bone fracture (eg: humerus, femur, radius , ulna etc) in\nchildren<3 years\n- Salter-Harris type I and II fractures of knee, wrist and elbow.\nHead injuries (intracranial injury)\n> Suspect child NAI if a child has an intracranial injury\nin the absence of major confirmed accidental trauma\nor known medical cause, in one or more of the\nfollowing circumstances:\n- The explanation is absent or unsuitable\n- The child is aged under 3 years\n- retinal haemorrhages\n- rib or long bone fractures\n- there are multiple subdural haemorrhages with or without\nsubarachnoid haemorrhage in infant or toddler\nThermal injuries\n> Suspect NAI if a child has burn or scald injury\n- the explanation for the injury is unsuitable or absent\n- if the child is not independently mobile\n- Burn to any soft tissue area for example, the backs of hands,\nsoles of feet, buttocks, back.\n- in the shape of an implement eg: cigarette, iron\n> Following scalds indicates force immersion\n- scalds to buttocks, perineum and lower limbs\n- scalds to limbs in a glove or stocking distribution\n- scalds to limbs with symmetrical distribution\n- scalds with sharply delineated borders.\n/26, 10:34 AM Child Abuse-Non-Accidental Injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nEmotional abuse\n-following signs may be indicators of emotional abuse:\n-Children who are excessively withdrawn, fearful, or anxious\nabout doing\nsomething wrong;\n-Parents or carers who withdraw their attention from their\nchild, giving the child the 'cold shoulder'\n-Parents or carers blaming their problems on their child; and\n-Parents or carers who humiliate their child, for example, by\nname-calling or making negative comparisons.\nNeglect\n-following signs may be indicators of neglect\n-Children who are living in a home that is indisputably dirty or\nunsafe;\n-Children who are left hungry or dirty\n-Children who are left without adequate clothing, e.g. not\nhaving a winter coat;\n-Children who are living in dangerous conditions, i.e. around\ndrugs, alcohol or violence\n-Children who are often angry, aggressive or self-harm\n-Children who fail to receive basic health care\n-Parents who fail to seek medical treatment when their\nchildren are ill or are injured\nManagement of child abuse\n- Any suspicion of child abuse, involve senior clinician\npaediatrician or ED consultant\n- All hospitals have their own protocols and designated lead\nprofessional ,who is available for advice\n- He or she will assess the child and arrange hospital\nadmission for further investigations (eg: skeletal survey).\n- Involve the police and social care\n- Child may require forensic examination\n/26, 10:34 AM Child Abuse-Non-Accidental Injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n- Treat painful or life threatening injuries\n- Ensure that all documentation is legible and meticulous\n- Follow local protocol for multi-agency referral and social care\nSexual abuse\n- Both boys and girls are affected\n- The abuser is often a male relative or carer who is well known\nto the child.\n> Following features suggest child abuse\n- injury to the genitalia or anus.\n- Perineal pain, discharge, or bleeding.\n- Behavioural disturbance, enuresis, and encopresis.\n- Inappropriate sexual behaviour.\n- The child may allege sexual abuse.\n- STI (including anogenital warts).\n- Pregnancy.\n- Repeated UTIs.\nManagement:\n- Record child statement word by word\n- Don't perform genital examination\n- Involve senior doctor at early stage\n- Treat injuries which require urgent intervention\n- Advise Pt to avoid destroying evidence (eg: don't shower,\nbath, change or wash clothes etc)\n- Refer or consult urgently to a specialist in the forensic\nassessment of sexual assaults for the purposes of conducting a\nforensic examination and to consider the need for\n/26, 10:34 AM Child Abuse-Non-Accidental Injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\ncontraception and potential for sexually transmitted infection\n(STI).\n- Police or a Sexual Assault Referral Centre (SARC) will be able\nto arrange a specialist assessment.\n- Child may require follow-up for STIs and psychological\nsequelae and support.\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:34 AM Child Abuse-Non-Accidental Injuries | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Child Abuse & Non-Accidental Injury (NAI) Recognition**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
bcc5296f-0b81-431f-97a1-76645404d112	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	lymphatic-drainage-of-upper-limb	Lymphatic Drainage of Upper Limb	article	Lymphatic drainage of the Upper\nLimb:\nThe superficial lymph vessels ascend the limb in the superficial\nfascia and accompany the superficial veins. The deep lymph\nvessels lie deep to the deep fascia and follow the deep arteries\nand veins. All lymph vessels of the limb ultimately drain into\nlymph nodes in axilla.\n\n### 1: Axillary lymph nodes: The axillary lymph nodes drain lymph\n\nvessels from the entire upper limb. In addition, they drain\nlymph vessels from the lateral part of the breast\n\n### 2: Infraclavicular nodes: They lie in the interval between the\n\ndeltoid and the pectoralis major muscles. They receive lymph\nfrom the superficial vessels of the radial side of hand, the\nforearm, and the arm.\n\n### 3: Supratrochlear lymph nodes: The supratrochlear lymph\n\nnode lies in the superficial fascia in the cubital fossa close to\nthe trochlea of the humerus. It receives lymph from the medial\nfingers, the medial part of the hand, and the medial side of the\nforearm.\nLymphatic drainage of the Breast:\nThe lymphatic drainage of the breast is of great clinical\nimportance due to its role in the metastasis of breast cancer\ncells.\nThere are three groups of lymph nodes that receive lymph\nfrom breast tissue.\nAxillary nodes (75%): Receives lymphatic drainage from lateral\nand superior quadrant of breast.\n\nparasternal nodes (20%): Receives lymphatic drainage from\nmedial quadrant.\nNote: Breast cancer in axillary tail of breast most commonly\ninvolved lymph node is axillary\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Removal and analysis of the axillary lymph nodes is often a vital\n> tool for the staging of breast cancers. During this procedure\n> there is also a risk of damage to either of the long thoracic\n> nerve or the thoracodorsal nerve causing winged scapula.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
63c77ef2-a8d6-434b-aaba-4dedbf90634f	bb9a3e24-9932-4721-8fd4-8b24fa26b67d	structures-of-the-thorax-lungs-and-pleurae	Structures of the Thorax - Lungs and Pleurae	article	## Lung:\n\nLungs lie either side of the mediastinum, within the thoracic\ncavity. Each lung is surrounded by pleural cavity, which is\nformed by the visceral and parietal pleura. Lungs are\nsuspended from the mediastinum by the lung root a collection\nof structures entering and leaving the lungs.\nEach root contains a bronchus, pulmonary artery, two\npulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, pulmonary plexus of nerves\nand lymphatic vessels. All these structures enter or leave the\nlung via the hilum. The hila are located at the level of the 3rd -\n4th costal cartilage anteriorly, and the T5 - T7 vertebrae\nposteriorly. In the mediastinum, the vagus nerves pass\nimmediately posteriorly to the lung roots, while the phrenic\nnerves pass immediately anterior to them.\nNote: The right lung is normally a little larger than the left lung\nbecause the middle mediastinum, containing the heart,\nbulges more to the left than to the right.\nRelations:\nLymphatic vessels of the lung: Drain the bronchial tree,\npulmonary vessels, and connective tissue septa. Run along\nbronchioles and bronchi toward the hilum, where they drain to\nthe pulmonary (intrapulmonary) and then bronchopulmonary\nnodes, which in turn drain to the inferior and superior\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Lungs and Pleurae - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-lungs-and-pleurae/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Lungs and Pleurae — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\ntracheobronchial nodes, the tracheal (paratracheal) nodes,\nbroncho mediastinal nodes and trunks, and eventually to the\nthoracic duct on the left and right lymphatic duct on the right.\nAre not present in the walls of the pulmonary alveoli.\n\n### 1. Thoracic duct: Begins in the abdomen at the cisterna chyli,\n\nwhich is the dilated junction of the intestinal, lumbar, and\ndescending intercostal trunks. Drains the lower limbs,\npelvis, abdomen, left thorax, left upper limb, and left side\nof the head and neck. Passes through the aortic opening of\nthe diaphragm and ascends through the posterior\nmediastinum between the aorta and the azygos vein, Arches\nlaterally over the apex of the left pleura and between the left\ncarotid sheath in front and the vertebral artery behind, runs\nbehind the left internal jugular vein, and then usually\nempties into the junction of the left internal jugular and\nsubclavian veins.\n\n### 2. Right lymphatic duct: Drains the right sides of the thorax,\n\nupper limb, head, and neck. Much of the left lower lung lobe\ndrains to the right lymphatic duct. Empties into the junction\nof the right internal jugular and subclavian veins.\nVasculature of lung:\nThe pulmonary artery conveys the venous blood to the\nlungs.\nThe pulmonary veins commence in the pulmonary\ncapillaries, open into the left atrium of the heart, conveying\noxygenated blood to be distributed to all parts of the body by\nthe aorta.\nThe bronchial arteries supply blood for the nutrition of the\nlung.\nThe bronchial vein is formed at the root of the lung, receiving\nsuperficial and deep veins corresponding to branches of the\nbronchial artery\nInnervation of lung: The nerve supply to the lungs is from the\nanterior and posterior pulmonary plexus (networks of nerves derived from the sympathetic trunk and vagus nerve).\nLobes of the lung: The left lung is divided into two lobes, an\nupper and a lower, by an interlobular fissure, which extends\nfrom the costal to the mediastinal surface of the lung both\nabove and below the hilus.The superior lobe lies above and in\nfront of this fissure, and includes the apex, the anterior\nborder, and a considerable part of the costal surface and the\ngreater part of the mediastinal surface of the lung.\nThe inferior lobe, the larger of the two, is situated below and\nbehind the fissure, and comprises almost the whole of the\nbase, a large portion of the costal surface, and the greater part\nof the posterior border.\nThe right lung is divided into three lobes, superior, middle, and\ninferior, by two interlobular fissures. the oblique fissure\nseparates the inferior lobe from the superior and middle lobe\nwhilst the horizontal fissure separates the superior lobe from\nthe middle lobe.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Lung auscultation: to make it summarize remember, sounds\n> heard anteriorly think upper lobes, sounds heard posteriorly\n> think lower lobes and middle lobe will be heard to the right,\n> near the midaxillary line\n> The superior lobe of the right lung is best auscultated above\n> the 4th rib anteriorly.\n\nThe middle lobe of the right lung is best auscultated\nbetween the 4th and the 6th rib anteriorly.\nThe superior lobe of the left lung is best auscultated above\nthe 6th rib anteriorly.\nThe inferior lobes of the left and right lungs are best\nauscultated in the back between T3 & T10.\nPancoast tumor (superior pulmonary sulcus tumor) is a\nmalignant neoplasm of the lung aper may cause a lower trunk\nbrachial plexopathy and a lesion of cervical sympathetic chain ganglion and Horner syndrome (ptosis, enophthalmos,\nmiosis, anhidrosis, and vasodilation).\nThoracotomy: surgical procedure to gain access into the\npleural space of the chest\nAntero lateral thoracotomy muscle divided serratus anterior\nand pectoralis major\nAntero lateral thoracotomy: Nerve injury long thoracic\nnerve\nPost: lateral thoracotomy muscle divided latissimus dorsi\nPost: lateral thoracotomy nerve injury: Thoracodorsal nerve\ninjury\nInternal mammary artery and Aorta are likely to be\ndamaged also\nPleurae:\nEach lung is surrounded by a pleura. The pleura consists of two\nparts.\nThe parietal pleura covers internal surface of the thoracic\ncavity. Parietal pleura includes mediastinal pleura, costal\npleura and diaphragmatic pleura\nThe visceral pleura lines the lungs.\nThe two parts of the pleura are continuous with each other\nat the hilum of each lung. The potential space between these\ntwo parts of the pleura is called the pleural cavity.\nPleural Recesses: Pleural cavity is not completely filled by the\nlungs, anteriorly and posteroinferiorly, this gives rise to\nrecesses where the opposing surfaces of the parietal pleura\ntouch.\nThere are two recesses present in each pleural cavity, recesses\nare of clinical importance, as they provide a location where\nfluid can collect i.e., in a pleural effusion\nCosto diaphragmatic: located between the costal pleurae\nand the diaphragmatic pleura.\n\nCosto mediastinal: located between the costal pleurae and\nthe mediastinal pleurae, behind the sternum.\nSurface markings of pleura:\nSuperiorly the pleural cavity projects as much as 3-4 cm\nabove the first costal cartilage but does not extend above the\nneck of rib 1.\nInferiorly the pleura reflects onto the diaphragm above the\ncostal margin. The inferior margin of the pleural cavity is a\nline that runs between rib 8 in the midclavicular line, rib 10 in\nthe midaxillary line and T12 vertebra posteriorly.\nInnervation of pleura\nThe parietal pleura is innervated by somatic afferent fibres.\nParietal pleura includes mediastinal pleura, costal pleura and\ndiaphragmatic pleura.\nThe costal pleura (pleura related to the ribs and intercostal\nspaces) is innervated by branches of the intercostal nerves,\nand pain is felt in relation to the thoracic wall.\nThe diaphragmatic pleura and the mediastinal pleura are\ninnervated mainly by the phrenic nerves (C3 - C5), and pain is\nreferred the C3 - C5 dermatomes include lateral neck and\nsupraclavicular region of shoulder.\nThe visceral pleura is innervated by visceral afferent fibres,\nnot sensitive to pain or temperature.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Thoracentesis (or pleural tap): is surgical entry through the\n> thoracic wall into the pleural cavity for aspiration of fluid. An\n> accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity is known by clinical\n> names, such as a hydrothorax (water), a hemothorax (blood), a\n> chylothorax (lymph), and a pyothorax (pus).\n\nSite: Pleural tap is performed at or posterior to the midaxillary\nline, one or two intercostal spaces below the fluid level ideal\nsite is the sixth, seventh, eighth, or ninth intercostal space but\nnot below the ninth intercostal space\nNote: A needle should be inserted immediately above the\nsuperior margin of a rib to avoid injury to the intercostal neurovascular bundle, which travels in a notch below each rib	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
b18bb3ca-4aef-4b61-bb40-0839bd1aff42	93dd26aa-6675-4d16-bfda-6ade22fb676d	urinary-tract	Urinary Tract	article	Kidney\nThe kidneys are two bean-shaped organs, located on either\nside of the spine, in the retroperitoneal space, extend\nbetween vertebrae T12 - L3.\nThe left kidney is situated a little higher than the right one,\nbecause of the liver on the right side of the abdominal cavity,\nabove the right kidney. The kidneys have a medial dimple\ncalled the renal hilum, which is the entry and exit point for\nstructures that supply or drain the kidneys such as the\nnerves, ureters, vessels, and lymphatics.\nEach kidney covered by fibrous capsule and surrounded by\nrenal fascia,\nAnatomical Relations\nArterial supply: Renal arteries arising from the abdominal aorta\nimmediately distal to the origin of the superior mesenteric\nartery\nInnervation: The kidneys receive autonomic nerve fibers via\nthe renal plexus which contains parasympathetic fibers from\nthe vagus nerve and sympathetic fibers from the thoracic\nsplanchnic nerves.\n\n![Urinary Tract - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/urinary-tract/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Urinary Tract — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLymphatic drainage: (Important to remember): Lumbar (Para-\naortic lymph nodes) located at origin of renal arteries.\nUreter:\nMuscular tube that transmits urine by peristaltic waves\nArises in the abdomen as continuation of renal pelvis.\nAfter arising from the ureteropelvic junction, the ureters\ndescend through the abdomen, along the anterior surface of\nthe psoas major. Here the ureter lies anteriorly and slightly\nmedial to the tips of L2-L5 vertebrae.\nAt the area of the sacroiliac joints, the ureters cross the\npelvic brim, thus entering the pelvic cavity. At this point,\nthey also cross the bifurcation of the common iliac\narteries.\nOnce within the pelvic cavity, the ureters travel down the\nlateral pelvic walls. At the level of the ischial spines, they turn\nanteromedially, moving in a transverse plane towards the\nbladder.\nUpon reaching the bladder wall, the ureters pierce its lateral\naspect in an oblique manner. This creates a one-way valve,\nwhere high intramural pressure collapses the ureters,\npreventing the backflow of urine.\n\nInnervation: Sensory fibres from the ureters enter the spinal\ncord at T11-L2, with ureteric pain referred to those dermatomal\nareas.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> In females ureters are in close proximity to the ovaries. Care\n> must be taken not the damage the ureters during an\n> oophorectomy, especially during the ligation of the ovarian\n> arteries.\n\nDamage may occur during hysterectomy or surgical repair of\na prolapsed uterus because it runs under the uterine artery.\nThe Ureter is inadvertently clamped, ligated during\nhysterectomy when uterine artery is being ligated to control\nuterine bleeding.\nIn men, instead of the uterine arteries, the vas deferens cross\nthe ureters anteriorly and lies Infront of seminal vesicles\n\n![Urinary Tract - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/urinary-tract/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Urinary Tract — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nbefore entering the posterolateral aspect of the bladder.\nUreteric calculus:\nThere are three locations where the ureters are at their\nnarrowest, this is where a stone is more likely to become stuck.\nUretopelvic junction (Usually Coincides with L2)\nPelvic brim\nWhere the ureter enters the bladder\nUrethra\nIt serves as a passage for urine from the urinary bladder to\nthe exterior, but in men, it also serves as a passage for\nsemen.\nMale urethra: Male urethra is approximately 20 cm long and\nconsists of: prostatic, membranous, bulbous and spongy\nurethra.\n\n#### Anterior urethra\n\nPenile (spongy) urethra (16 cm long): encased by corpus\nspongiosum of the penis (the longest portion).\nBulbar (bulbous) urethra traverses the root of the penis, it\nreceives the ducts from the bulbourethral glands and the\nglands of Littre\n.\n\n#### Posterior urethra\n\nMembranous urethra (1 cm long): passes through the\nurogenital diaphragm, surrounded by sphincter urethrae. The\nshortest and narrowest portion. The lowest part of the\nmembranous urethra is susceptible to rupture or to\npenetration by a catheter.\nProstatic Urethra (3 cm long), surrounded by the prostate\ngland; on its posterior wall. It receives the ejaculatory ducts\nand numerous small prostatic ducts.\nFemale urethra\nis approximately 4 cm long, and its external urethral orifice is\nsituated between the labia minora, anterior to the vaginal opening, and posterior to the glans clitoris.\nBlood supply:\nPenile (spongy) urethra and Membranous urethra: Supplied\nby branch of internal pudendal artery\nProstatic Urethra: Branch of internal iliac artery\nInnervation: prostatic plexus, which contains a mixture of\nsympathetic, parasympathetic and visceral afferent fibres.\nUrinary bladder:\nFunction as temporary storage of urine as well as expulsion\nThere are also two muscular sphincters located in the urethra\nInternal urethral sphincter:\nMale: consists of circular smooth fibres, which are under\nautonomic control. It is thought to prevent seminal\nregurgitation during ejaculation.\nExternal urethral sphincter\nhas the same structure in both sexes. It is a skeletal muscle,\nand under voluntary control.\nMicturition (urination):\nIs initiated by stimulating stretch receptors in the detrusor\nmuscle in the bladder wall as the urine volume increases\n(approximately 400 ml for adults). Can be assisted by\ncontraction of the abdominal muscles, which increases the\nintra-abdominal and pelvic pressures.\nInvolves the following processes:\n\n### 1. Sympathetic (visceral efferent [VE)) fibers constrict the\n\ninternal sphincter, inhibiting emptying of the bladder (They\nalso activate during ejaculation to constrict the internal\nsphincter in order to prevent seminal reflux into the bladder\n\n### 2. Parasympathetic preganglionic (VE) fibers in the pelvic\n\nsplanchnic nerves pass through the pelvic (inferior hypogastric)\n\nplexus to synapse within the bladder wall; postganglionic fibers\nto the bladder musculature induce a reflex contraction of the\ndetrusor muscle and relaxation of the internal urethral\nsphincter in concert with reduced stimulation by sympathetic\nfibers.\n\n### 3. Somatic efferent fibers in the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)\n\ninnervates the external urethral sphincter to cause voluntary\nrelaxation of the external urethral sphincter, and the bladder\nbegins to void.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Reflex Bladder: Spinal Cord Transection Above T12\n> In this case, the afferent signals from the bladder wall are\n> unable to reach the brain, and the patient will have no\n> awareness of bladder filling.\n\nThere is also no descending control over the external urethral\nsphincter, and it is constantly relaxed. There is a functioning\nspinal reflex, where parasympathetic system initiates\ndetrusor contraction in response to bladder wall stretch.\nThus, the bladder automatically empties as it fills - known as\nthe reflex bladder.\nFlaccid Bladder: Spinal Cord Transection Below T12\nA spinal cord transection at this level will have damaged the\nparasympathetic outflow to the bladder. The detrusor muscle\nwill be paralyzed, unable to contract.\nThe spinal reflex does not function. In this scenario, the\nbladder will fill uncontrollably, becoming abnormally\ndistended until overflow incontinence occurs.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
87cf6cfd-db6d-4a5e-9966-a690ea812a3f	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-atrial-fibrillation-flutter	Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter	quiz	# Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	20	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
49754b55-d3f9-4a30-8dfd-630f41e5454c	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-1-olfactory-nerve	Cranial Nerve 1 - Olfactory Nerve	article	Olfactory nerve:\nFirst and shortest cranial nerve transmits sensory information\nfrom the nasal epithelium via the cribriform plate of the\nethmoid bone to the primary olfactory cortex\n\n![Cranial Nerve 1 - Olfactory Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-1-olfactory-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve 1 - Olfactory Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
856dab5e-0b56-4c9d-902f-09785188dc37	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-ii-optic-nerve	Cranial Nerve Ii_ Optic Nerve	article	Optic nerve:\nTransmit special sensory information from the retina of the eye\nto the primary visual cortex of the brain.\nEnters skull via optic canal of the sphenoid bone\nNote:Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD): Seen in optic\nnerve disease or asymmetrical disease of the retina in which\npupils respond differently to light stimuli shone in one eye at a\ntime\nswinging light test if there is no RAPD the pupils of both eyes\nconstrict equally regardless of which eye is stimulated by the\nlight.\nIn an abnormal swinging-light test if there is a RAPD there is\nless pupil constriction in the eye with the retinal or optic\nnerve disease\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pituitary adenoma: Pituitary gland lies in close proximity to\n> optic chiasm,tumor of pituitary gland compresses optic nerve\n> at chiasm produces visual defect called bitemporal hemianopia\n\n![Cranial Nerve Ii_ Optic Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-ii-optic-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Ii_ Optic Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
298b3b65-ce49-41f6-934b-a52555559618	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	regulation-of-blood-pressure	Regulation of Blood Pressure	article	Regulation of Blood pressure\nMechanism of baroreceptor and\nchemoreceptor reflex:\nImpaired baroreceptor reflex:\nOrthostatic hypotension (Postural hypotension): fainting or\nlight-headedness on standing may occur in individuals whose\nbaroreceptor reflex mechanism is impaired (e.g., individuals\ntreated with sympatholytic agents) or who are volume\ndepleted.\nOn standing from a prone position, gravity causes blood to pool\ninto veins in the legs. Central venous pressure (CVP) falls,\ncausing a fall in stroke volume and cardiac output and thus a\nfall in blood pressure.\nOther regulation of Arterial blood\npressure:\nCerebral ischemia: When brain is ischemic, the partial pressure\nof carbon dioxide (Pco2) in brain tissue increases\nChemoreceptors in the vasomotor center respond by\nincreasing sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels.\nConstriction of arterioles causes intense peripheral\nvasoconstriction and increased TPR. Blood flow to other\norgans (e.g., kidneys) is significantly reduced to preserve\n\n![Regulation of Blood Pressure - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/regulation-of-blood-pressure/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Regulation of Blood Pressure — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nblood flow to the brain. Mean arterial pressure can increase to\nlife-threatening levels.\nThe Cushing reaction is an example of the response to cerebral\nischemia. Increases in intracranial pressure cause compression\nof the cerebral blood vessels, leading to cerebral ischemia and\nincreased cerebral Pco ₂ . The vasomotor center directs an\nincrease in sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels,\nwhich causes a profound increase in arterial pressure.	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d5653395-17ba-4a96-9c85-cb9ea6a6638a	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-ecg-axis-interpretation	ECG Axis Interpretation	quiz	# ECG Axis Interpretation\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: ECG Axis Interpretation\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **ECG Axis Interpretation**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	30	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
2e1ea712-b2e8-4534-9750-30c3d0b245bd	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	ventricular-myocyte-vs-pacemaker-action-potential	Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential	article	Ventricular myocyte action\npotential\nRMP of Ventricular myocyte is -90mv depolarization initiates\nwhen RMP reaches threshold of -65mv\nFast Na channels activate and a rapid Na+ influx\ndepolarizes membrane potential.\nPacemaker action potential:\nAction potential of SA node differs from ventricular muscle.\nRMP of SA node is -60mv depolarization initiates when RMP\nreaches threshold of -40mv.\nSlow calcium channels activation and calcium influx\ninitiates depolarization unlike of other ventricular muscle Na\ninflux initiates depolarization\n\n![Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/ventricular-myocyte-vs-pacemaker-action-potential/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/ventricular-myocyte-vs-pacemaker-action-potential/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
9d9aebcc-e858-4f82-913e-b3f1dcf37637	93dd26aa-6675-4d16-bfda-6ade22fb676d	dermatomes-of-trunk-2	Dermatomes of Trunk	article	# Dermatomes of Trunk\n\nDermatomal supply of trunk\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Previous Next\n\n> Diet ii Late 2026)\n> Overview  Comments\n\n> 1/2\n> 2/2	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00
3a05c163-2ea8-4736-92ac-fae5f456634e	578aab1b-1362-436f-9615-ab63d01cddc9	carpal-tunnel-snuffbox-structures	Carpal tunnel, snuffbox and other important structures	article	## Carpal tunnel\n\n- **Roof:** flexor retinaculum, attached medially to the pisiform and hook of the hamate, and laterally to the tubercles of the scaphoid and trapezium.\n- **Floor:** the carpal bones.\n- **Contents (10 structures):** four tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis (the most superficial), four tendons of flexor digitorum profundus, the tendon of flexor pollicis longus (lateral), and the median nerve.\n\n**Not inside the tunnel** (they pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum): the ulnar nerve and artery, the palmaris longus tendon, and the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve. Flexor carpi radialis runs in its own sheath on the lateral side. Pronator quadratus stays in the distal forearm.\n\n## Anatomical snuffbox\n\nA triangular hollow on the lateral side of the back of the hand, seen best with the thumb extended.\n\n- **Medial (ulnar) border:** tendon of extensor pollicis longus.\n- **Lateral (radial) border:** tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis.\n- **Proximal limit:** styloid process of the radius. **Distal limit:** base of the first metacarpal.\n- **Floor:** scaphoid and trapezium.\n- **Contents:** radial artery, superficial branch of the radial nerve, cephalic vein.\n- Tenderness here after a fall on the outstretched hand suggests a scaphoid fracture.\n\n## Ulnar (Guyon) canal\n\n- Carries the ulnar nerve and artery into the hand.\n- Borders: pisiform and flexor carpi ulnaris tendon medially, hook of the hamate laterally, palmar carpal ligament forms the roof, flexor retinaculum forms the floor.\n- Ulnar canal syndrome means compression of the ulnar nerve here (ganglion, lipoma, trauma, repetitive pressure such as cycling).\n\n## Extensor retinaculum and the six compartments\n\nThe retinaculum holds the extensor tendons in place at the wrist. Tendons pass under it in six synovial compartments, from lateral to medial:\n\n1. Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis\n2. Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis\n3. Extensor pollicis longus\n4. Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis\n5. Extensor digiti minimi\n6. Extensor carpi ulnaris\n\nInflammation of compartment 1 is de Quervain tenosynovitis.\n\n## High-yield hand and arm conditions\n\n- **Dupuytren contracture:** thickening and shortening of the palmar fascia, producing a fixed flexion of the ring and little fingers.\n- **Volkmann contracture:** ischaemic contracture of the forearm flexors.\n- **Flexor tenosynovitis:** infection of a flexor tendon sheath after a puncture wound, with pain, swelling and difficulty moving the finger. In the thumb it can spread through the radial bursa, and in the little finger through the ulnar bursa, to the mid-palmar space. Infection in the middle three fingers usually stays in its own sheath.\n- **Trigger finger:** a nodule on a flexor tendon catches in its pulley, causing painful clicking.\n- **Mallet finger:** avulsion of the extensor tendon from the distal phalanx, so the fingertip stays flexed.\n- **Boutonniere deformity:** rupture of the central slip of the extensor tendon, so the middle joint is flexed and the distal joint hyperextended.\n- **Pancoast tumour:** a lung apex tumour that can damage the lower trunk of the brachial plexus (C8-T1), cause Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) and thoracic outlet symptoms.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 09:57:24.481779+00	2026-11-10 04:31:13.45105+00	\N
9ce03b98-532e-4c8e-a891-dc6b2e765d4d	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	arterial-supply-of-upper-limb	Arterial Supply of Upper Limb	article	Axillary Artery:\nIs considered to be the central structure of the axilla. Extends\nfrom the outer border of the first rib to the inferior border of\nthe teres major muscle, where it becomes the brachial artery.\nThe axillary artery runs with and is lateral to the axillary vein.\nIs divided into three parts by the pectoralis minor muscle.\nFirst part: Superior thoracic artery\nSecond part: Thoracoacromial artery and Lateral thoracic\nartery\nThird Part: Subscapular artery, Anterior and posterior\ncircumflex arteries\nNote: The anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries\nform an anastomotic network around the surgical neck of\nthe humerus and can be damaged in cases of fracture.\nBrachial Artery:\nThe brachial artery is a continuation of the axillary artery past\nthe lower border of the teres major. It is the main supply of\nblood for the arm.\nAnatomical course:\nIt descends through the anterior compartment of the arm on\nthe brachialis muscle enters the cubital fossa, and then ends\nat the level of the neck of the radius by dividing into the\nradial and the ulnar arteries.\n\n#### Branches of Brachial artery\n\n### 1: Muscular branches\n\n### 2: Nutrient artery: to the humerus\n\n### 3: Profunda brachii artery: Immediately distal to the teres\n\nmajor, the brachial artery gives rise to the profunda brachii\n(deep artery), which travels with the radial nerve in the radial\ngroove of the humerus and supplies structures in the\nposterior aspect of the upper arm (e.g., triceps brachii).\n\n### 4: Superior ulnar collateral artery, which follows the ulnar\n\nnerve\n\n### 5: Inferior ulnar collateral artery, which takes part in\n\nanastomosis around the elbow joint.\nRadial artery:\nSmaller of two terminal branches of brachial artery. Passing\nalong the lateral aspect of the forearm lying deep to\nbrachioradialis muscle, distally being covered by only skin\nand fascia. The radial pulse can be palpated in the distal\nforearm, immediately lateral to the prominent tendon of\nthe flexor carpi radialis muscle. The radial artery enters the\nhand by curving around the lateral side of the wrist, passing\nover the floor of the anatomical snuffbox and penetrating the\ndorsolateral aspect of the hand between the bases of the first\nand second metacarpal bones.\nUlnar artery:\nLargest of the two terminal branches, courses along the ulnar\naspect of the forearm deep to the flexor digitorum\nsuperficialis, pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis muscles.\nIt enters the hand with the ulnar nerve and vein by passing\nthrough the Guyon canal (ulnar canal) located between the\npisiform and the hook of hamate.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Brachial artery is palpated in antecubital fossa, immediately\n> medial to the tendon of biceps brachii.\n\nThe radial pulse can be palpated in the distal forearm,\nimmediately lateral to the prominent tendon of the flexor\ncarpi radialis muscle.\nVolkman ischemic contracture:\nComplete occlusion of brachial artery can cause necrosis of\nforearm muscles and are replaced by scar tissue and shorten\nconsiderably this can cause a characteristic flexion deformity,\ncalled Volkmann's ischemic contracture.\nGuyon's or Ulnar canal: The ulnar (Guyon's) canal is a fibro-\nosseous tunnel located at the level of the palm. The ulnar canal conveys the ulnar neurovascular bundle into the hand.\nContents: Ulnar nerve, ulnar artery and Venae comitantes of\nulnar artery\nUlnar canal syndrome refers to compression of the ulnar nerve\nwithin the ulnar canal. The most common causes are ganglion\ncyst, lipoma and trauma.\n\n#### Superficial and Deep palmar arches\n\nThe hand has a rich arterial supply with many anastomoses\nbetween vessels\n\n### 1: The superficial palmar arch lies just beneath the palmar\n\naponeurosis on the long flexor tendons. It is a continuation of\nthe ulnar artery, and it is often completed on the lateral side by\nthe superficial palmar branch of the radial artery.\nPalmar aponeurosis: is a thickened area of palmar fascia.\nPalmar aponeurosis protects the underlying soft tissues and\nlong flexor tendons\nBlood supply: The superficial palmar arch It gives rise to the\ndigital arteries, which supply the four fingers.\n\n### 2: The deep palmar arch is deeply placed in the palm, and it\n\nextends from the proximal end of the space between the first\nand second metacarpal bones to the base of the fifth\nmetacarpal bone. It is formed as a continuation of the radial\nartery, and it terminates by anastomosing with the deep\nbranch of the ulnar artery.\nBlood supply: Blood supply to digits and wrist joint.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
5b2bef96-855d-4020-84d9-781d934da207	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	innervation-of-upper-limb	Innervation of Upper Limb	article	Brachial Plexus:\nThe brachial plexus is a network of nerve fibers that supply the\nskin and muscles of upper extremity. The plexus is formed by\nthe anterior rami (divisions) of cervical spinal nerves C5, C6, C7,\nand C8, and the first thoracic spinal nerve, T1.\nThe brachial plexus is divided into five parts\nRoots\nTrunks\nDivisions\nCords\nbranches\n\n### 1: Root:\n\nFormed by the union of anterior rami (divisions) of cervical\nspinal nerves C5, C6, C7, and C8, and the first thoracic spinal\nnerve, T1.\n\n### 2: Trunk:\n\nAt the base of the neck, the roots of the brachial plexus\nconverge to form three trunks.\nSuperior trunk: Combination of C5 and C6 roots.\nMiddle trunk: Continuation of C7.\nInferior trunk: Combination of C8 and T1 roots\n\n### 3: Division:\n\nTrunks are divided into anterior and posterior division within\nposterior triangle of neck.\n\n### 4: Cords:\n\nOnce the anterior and posterior divisions have entered the\naxilla, they combine together to form three cords\nLateral cord: Anterior division of superior and middle trunk\nMedial cord: Anterior division of inferior trunk\nPosterior cord: Posterior union of all three trunks\n\n### 5: Branches:\n\n#### BRANCHES OF LATERAL CORD   "LML"\n\n#### Lateral pectoral nerve\n\nMusculocutaneous nerve\n\n#### Lateral root of median nerve\n\n#### BRANCHES OF MEDIAL CORD     "M4U"\n\n#### Medial pectoral nerve\n\n#### Medial cutaneous nerve of arm\n\n#### Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm\n\n#### Medial root of median nerve\n\nUlnar nerve\n\n#### BRANCHES OF POSTERIOR CORD   "ULTRA"\n\nUpper subscapular nerve\nLower subscapular nerve\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Brachial plexus injury:\n> Axillary Nerve:\n> Axillary Nerve is a direct continuation of the posterior cord from\n> the brachial plexus.\n\nSpinal roots: C5 and C6.\nPlexus cord: Posterior\nMotor functions: Innervates the teres minor and deltoid\nmuscles.\nSensory functions: Gives rise to the upper lateral cutaneous\nnerve of the arm, which innervates the skin over the lower\ndeltoid ('regimental badge area').\nNote: Lateral supraclavicular nerve arising from the cervical\nplexus (C3, C4 ) gives sensory innervation to upper half of\ndeltoid.\nAnatomical course:\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nAxillary nerve arises in the axilla passes medially to the surgical\nneck of the humerus, where it divides into three terminal\nbranches:\nAnterior terminal branch:  winds around the surgical neck of\nthe humerus and provides motor innervation to the anterior\naspect of the deltoid muscle.\nPosterior terminal branch:  Gives motor innervation to the\nposterior aspect of the deltoid muscle and teres minor. It also\ninnervates the skin over the inferior part of the deltoid as\nthe upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm.\nArticular branch: supplies the glenohumeral joint\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Axillary nerve injury is commonly caused by fracture of\n> surgical neck of humerus or inferior dislocation of humerus.\n\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of the arm.\n(Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but not to a horizontal\nlevel).\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of the deltoid (the\n'regimental badge area').\nQuadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex\nhumeral artery and vein pass through it and can be\ncompressed as a result of trauma, muscle hypertrophy\nresulting in weakness of deltoid and teres minor.\nMedian Nerve:\nAfter originating from the brachial plexus in the axilla the\nmedian nerve descends down the arm, initially lateral to the\nbrachial artery. Halfway down the arm, the nerve crosses over\nthe brachial artery, and becomes situated medially. The median\nnerve enters the anterior compartment of the forearm via the\ncubital fossa\n\n#### Branches of Median Nerve in the Forearm\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n#### Branches of Median Nerve in the Hand\n\nAfter giving off the anterior interosseous and palmar cutaneous\nbranches, the median nerve enters the hand via the carpal\ntunnel where it terminates by dividing into two branches\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Carpal tunnel syndrome: Compression of the median nerve\n> within the carpal tunnel can cause carpal tunnel syndrome\n\n## (Cts).\n\nClinical features: include numbness, tingling, and pain in the\ndistribution of the median nerve.\nNote: In carpal tunnel syndrome Importantly, the palm is\nusually spared  as the palmar cutaneous branch does not travel\nthrough the carpal tunnel.\nNote: Flexion of the ring and little fingers at MCPJ and DIPJ are\npreserved as these are functions of medial half of flexor\ndigitorum profundus and medial two lumbricals innervated by\nulnar nerve.\nRadial Nerve:\nThe radial nerve is the terminal continuation of the posterior\ncord of the brachial plexus\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_04_p4.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_05_p4.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\nRadial nerve arises from the posterior cord and the largest\nbranch of the brachial plexus.\nThe nerve arises in the axilla region, where it is situated\nposteriorly to the axillary artery, descends posteriorly between\nthe long and medial heads of the triceps, after which it passes\ninferolaterally with the profunda brachii artery in the spiral\n(radial) groove on the posterior surface of the humerus\nbetween the medial and lateral heads of the triceps. Pierces\nthe lateral intermuscular septum to enter the anterior\ncompartment and descends anterior to the lateral epicondyle\nbetween the brachialis and brachioradialis muscles to enter the\ncubital fossa, where it divides into superficial and deep\nbranches.\nDeep branch (motor): innervates the muscles in the posterior\ncompartment of the forearm and continues as posterior\ninterosseous nerve\nSuperficial branch (sensory): contributes to the cutaneous\ninnervation of the skin of dorsal hand and lateral three and\nhalf fingers.\nOther branches which give cutaneous innervation to skin of\nupper limb.\nLower lateral cutaneous nerve of arm: Innervates the lateral\naspect of the arm, inferior to the insertion of the deltoid\nmuscle.\nPosterior cutaneous nerve of arm: Innervates the posterior\nsurface of the arm.\nPosterior cutaneous nerve of forearm: Innervates a strip of\nskin down the middle of the posterior forearm.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Radial nerve injury:\n> Wrist Drop:\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_06_p5.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nInjury to the radial nerve is commonly caused by a fracture of\nthe midshaft of the humerus. Radial nerve injury results in loss\nof function in the extensors of the forearm, hand, metacarpals,\nand phalanges. It also results in the loss of wrist extension,\nleading to wrist drop, and produces weakness of abduction and\nadduction of the hand.\nNote: When the radial nerve is injured in proximal humerus\nfracture triceps brachii muscle will be affected and patient\npresents with unable to extend at the forearm. While midshaft\nof humerus fracture triceps brachii will remain spared\nUlnar Nerve:\nThe ulnar nerve arises from the brachial plexus within the axilla\nregion. It is a continuation of the medial cord and contains\nfibers from spinal roots C8 and T1.\nAnatomical Course of Ulnar Nerve:\nArises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus, runs down\nthe medial aspect of the arm, pierces the medial\nintermuscular septum at the middle of the arm, and\ndescends together with the superior ulnar collateral branch\nof the brachial artery. It then Descends behind the medial\nepicondyle in a tunnel (cubital tunnel), where it is readily\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_07_p6.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_08_p6.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\npalpated and most commonly injured. It may be damaged\nby a fracture of the medial epicondyle. When irritated, it\ncauses funny bone symptoms of pain, numbness, and\ntingling in the medial elbow, forearm, and digits.\nIt then enters the forearm by passing between the two heads\nof the flexor carpi ulnaris and descends between the flexor\ncarpi ulnaris and gives rise to following branches in forearm.\nUlnar nerve branches in hand\nEnters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum and\nlateral to the pisiform bone, where it is vulnerable to damage\nfrom cuts or stab wounds and gives rise to following\nbranches in hand.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Froment's sign: Froment's sign is a test for ulnar nerve palsy\n> specifically paralysis of the adductor pollicis:\n> Patient is asked to hold a piece of paper between thumb and\n> index finger as paper is pulled away, patient will flex thumb\n> at inter-phalangeal joint to maintain hold, this sign tests the\n> function of adductor pollicis muscle which is supplied by the\n> ulnar nerve.\n\nA positive test is when the patient is unable to adduct the\nthumb.\nClaw hand:\nInjury to the ulnar nerve is commonly caused by a fracture of\nthe medial epicondyle and results in a claw hand, in which the\nring and little fingers are hyperextended at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and flexed at the interphalangeal\njoints.\nPartial claw hand deformity is due to an isolated ulnar nerve\ninjury.\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_09_p7.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_10_p7.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\nComplete claw hand deformity: Involves all digits and results\nfrom combined ulnar and median nerve injury.\nUlnar nerve injury at elbow and wrist:\nSensory innervation of Median, Radial and\nulnar (High Yield):\nMusculocutaneous Nerve:\nThe musculocutaneous nerve is the terminal branch of the\nlateral cord of the brachial plexus.\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 11](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_11_p8.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 11 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 12](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_12_p8.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 12 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\nAnatomical Course:\nMusculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7) Pierces the coracobrachialis\nmuscle and descends between the biceps and brachialis\nmuscles. Innervates all of the flexor muscles in the anterior\ncompartment of the arm, such as the coraco- brachialis, biceps,\nand brachialis muscles. Continues into the forearm as the\nlateral cutaneous nerve of forearm.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Injury to the musculocutaneous nerve results in weakness of\n> supination (biceps) but can still be performed by the\n> brachioradialis (Innervated by Radial nerve)\n> Weakness of flexion at the shoulder and elbow but can still\n> be performed by the pectoralis major and brachioradialis\n> respectively and loss of sensation on lateral side of the\n> forearm.\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 13](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_13_p9.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 13 (Page 9 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
687131bf-711a-4415-a44e-0fcf670dbd51	ffa7b392-b5d9-4189-97fd-9eedce2b59e9	joints-of-the-upper-limb	Joints of the Upper Limb	article	Acromioclavicular Joint:\nTYPE= Plane type synovial joint\nThe acromioclavicular joint consists of an articulation between\nthe lateral end of the clavicle and the acromion of the scapula\nLigaments of the joint:\nLigaments\nThere are three main ligaments that support the\nacromioclavicular joint\nAcromioclavicular ligament\nConoid ligament\nTrapezoid ligament\nCollectively, the conoid and trapezoid ligaments are known as\nthe coracoclavicular ligament. It is the strongest ligament\nand suspends weight of the upper limb\n\nAcromioclavicular joint dislocation\nA severe blow on the point of the shoulder, as is incurred\nduring blocking or tackling in football or any severe fall,\ncan result in the acromion being thrust beneath the lateral\nend of the clavicle, tearing the coracoclavicular ligament. This\ncondition is known as shoulder separation. The displaced\nouter end of the clavicle is easily palpable.\nNote: If the coracoclavicular ligament is torn weight of the\nupper limb is not supported\nSternoclavicular Joint\nType: Synovial typed Saddle joint\nSternoclavicular joint is formed between the clavicle,\nmanubrium of the sternum and the first costal cartilage.\n\n![Joints of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-upper-limb/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Joints of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nLigaments of Sternoclavicular Joint\nSternoclavicular ligaments (anterior and posterior)\nstrengthen the joint anteriorly and posteriorly.\nInterclavicular ligament strengthen the joint capsule\nsuperiorly.\nCostoclavicular ligament strong ligament and stabilizes the\njoint capsule.\nThe sternoclavicular joint has a high degree of mobility and is\nrequired to accommodate the movements of the upper limb\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Violent forces directed along the long axis of the**\n> clavicle usually result in fracture of that bone, but dislocation of\n> the sternoclavicular joint takes place rarely.\n\nElbow joint\nType: Synovial hinge joint assists in flexion and extension of\nelbow\nFormed by articulations between\nTrochlear notch of the ulna and the trochlea of the humerus\nHead of the radius and the capitulum of the humerus\nLigaments of the elbow joint:\nThe radial collateral ligament is found on the lateral side of\nthe joint, extending from the lateral epicondyle and\nblending with the annular ligament.\nThe ulnar collateral ligament referred as Medial collateral\nligament (MCL) of the elbow originates from the\nmedial epicondyle and attaches to the coronoid process and\nolecranon of the ulna.\nAnnular ligament: Annular ligament stabilizes the elbow\njoint and is a strong band of fibers that encircles the head of\nthe radius and holds it in contact with the radial notch of the\nulna\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n> Elbow dislocation: Mostly posterior occurs due to fall on\n> hand with the elbow flexed\n> Complications: The ulnar collateral ligament is usually torn\n> and there can also be ulnar nerve involvement.\n\nRadioulnar joints (Pronator and\nsupinator of the forearm)\nType: Pivot typed synovial joint\nProximal Radioulnar joint: Formed by articulation between the\nhead of the radius and the radial notch of the ulna\nAnnular ligament stabilizes the elbow joint and is a strong\nband of fibers that encircles the head of the radius and holds\nit in contact with the radial notch of the ulna\nDistal Radioulnar joint: formed by articulation between the\nulnar notch of the radius and the head of the ulna.\nInterosseous Membrane\nThe interosseous membrane is a sheet of connective tissue\nthat joins the radius and ulna together between the\nradioulnar joints along with acts as a site of attachment for\nmuscles in the anterior and posterior compartments of\nforearm.\nThe collateral ligament is usually torn and there can also be\nulnar nerve involvement The ulnar collateral ligament is\nusually torn and there can also be ulnar nerve involvement\nThe ulnar collateral ligament is usually torn and there can\nalso be ulnar nerve involvement.\n\nWrist joint\nType: Synovial condyloid joint\nFormed between the distal end of the radius and the articular\ndisc above and the scaphoid, lunate and triquetral bones\nbelow\nNote: The ulna is not part of the wrist joint it articulates with\nthe radius, just proximal to the wrist joint.\nLigaments of the joint:\nPalmar radiocarpal:  Found on anterior side of the hand. It\npasses from the radius to both rows of carpal bones. Its\nfunction, apart from increasing stability, is to ensure that the\nhand follows the forearm during supination.\nDorsal radiocarpal: Found on the posterior side of the hand.\nIt contributes to the stability of the wrist, but also ensures\nthat the hand follows the forearm during pronation.\nUlnar collateral: Runs from the ulnar styloid process to the\ntriquetrum and pisiform. It acts to prevent excessive radial\n(lateral) deviation of the hand.\nRadial collateral: Runs from the radial styloid process to the\nscaphoid and trapezium. It acts to prevent excessive ulnar\n(medial) deviation of the hand.\n\n![Joints of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-upper-limb/figure_02_p5.png)\n*Joints of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nMovements of the wrist joint: Wrist joint allow for movement\nalong two axes. This means that flexion, extension, adduction\nand abduction can all occur at the wrist joint\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Falling on outstretched hand most commonly**\n> fractured carpal bone in wrist joint is Scaphoid. While most\n> common fracture of the wrist joint is Colles' fracture.\n\nThe shoulder Joint\nType: synovial ball and socket joint\nShoulder joint is formed by the articulation of the head of the\nhumerus with the glenoid cavity of the scapula\nLigaments of the shoulder joint:\nIn the shoulder joint, the ligaments play a key role in stabilizing\nthe bony structures.\nGlenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle and inferior) They\nact to stabilize the anterior aspect of the joint. They are the\nmain source of stability for the shoulder, holding it in place\nand preventing it from dislocating anteriorly.\nCoracohumeral ligament: It supports the superior part of the\njoint capsule.\nTransverse humeral ligament: This ligament spans the\ndistance between the two tubercles of the humerus. It holds\nthe tendon of the long head of the biceps in the\nintertubercular groove or bicipital groove\nCoraco-clavicular ligament:  This ligament composed of the\ntrapezoid and conoid ligaments and runs from the clavicle to\nthe coracoid process of the scapula. They work alongside the\nacromioclavicular ligament to maintain the alignment of the\nclavicle in relation to the scapula.\nCoracoacromial ligament. This ligament runs between the\nacromion and coracoid process of the scapula it forms the\ncoraco-acromial arch.  This structure overlies the shoulder\njoint, preventing superior displacement of the humeral\nhead.\nStability of the shoulder joint:\nRotator cuff muscles [SITS MUSCLES]\n\nGlenoid labrum: A fibrocartilaginous ridge surrounding the\nglenoid cavity.\nLigaments: Extra Capsular ligaments form the\ncoracoacromial arch.\nBiceps tendon - it acts as a minor humeral head depressor,\nthereby contributing to stability.\nMovements of the shoulder joint: Shoulder joint is highly\nmobile ball and socket joint allows various movements like\nflexion, extension, adduction, abduction, medial rotation, lateral\nrotation and circumduction.\nNOTE: All the movements of the shoulder joint by different\nmuscles and the innervation is discussed in muscles of upper\narm\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Shoulder joint dislocation\n> Anterior shoulder dislocation: Most common injury results\n> from forceful abduction, extension and external rotation\n> Clinical features:\n> Arm usually abducted and externally rotated.\n\nHumeral head often palpated anteriorly.\n\n![Joints of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-upper-limb/figure_03_p7.png)\n*Joints of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\nPatient cannot touch contralateral shoulder with hand of\naffected extremity.\nComplications of anterior shoulder dislocation:\nInjury to axillary nerve\nInjury to axillary artery\nInjury to rotator cuff\nPosterior Shoulder dislocation: occurs occasionally results\nfrom direct blow to the arm that produces forceful internal\nrotation and adduction.\nClinical features:\nArm usually adducted and internally rotated.\nPatient unable to perform external rotation.\nComplications of posterior shoulder dislocation: less\ncommon neurovascular injury\nFracture of posterior glenoid labrum\nFracture of humeral shaft or lesser tuberosity\nNote: Light bulb sign on the X-ray is the diagnostic feature of\nposterior shoulder dislocation	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
5215fd61-aefc-43d6-ac9a-62398fad7f5f	60d5abf2-5add-4661-9b03-d4d02aa30e0f	bones-of-the-lower-limb	Bones of the Lower Limb	article	Hip Bone:\nThe bones of the pelvis consist of the right and left hip bones,\nthe sacrum and the coccyx.\nThe hip bones articulate posteriorly with the sacrum at the\nsacroiliac joints and with each other anteriorly at the pubic\nsymphysis.\nHip bone has a large articular socket on the lateral surface,\nthe acetabulum, which together with the head of the femur,\nforms the hip joint.\nEach of the hip bones is formed by\nIlium (superiorly)\nPubis (anteroinferiorly)\nIschium (posteroinferiorly).\nJoints of the pelvis:\nLumbosacral joint: It is the joint between vertebra L5 and the\nbase of the sacrum, joined by an intervertebral disk and\nsupported by the illolumbar ligaments.\nSacroiliac joint: is a synovial joint of an irregular plane type\nbetween the articular surfaces of the sacrum and illum Is\ncovered by cartilage and is supported by the anterior, posterior,\nand interosseous sacroiliac ligaments. Transmits the weight of\nthe body to the hip bone.\nSacrococcygeal joint Is a cartilaginous joint between the\nsacrum and coccyx, reinforced by the anterior, posterior, and\nlateral sacrococcygeal ligaments.\nPubic symphysis Is a cartilaginous or fibrocartilaginous joint\nbetween the pubic bones in the median plane.\nSacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments:\n\nPatella:\nPatella is largest sesamoid bone lies within the tendon of\nquadriceps femoris muscle.\nThe patellar ligament is an inferior continuation of the\nquadriceps tendon that inserts at the tibial tuberosity.\nFactors stabilizing patella\nThere are several structures that work together to keep the\npatella aligned and stabilized on the femur to prevent\nexcessive lateral movement of the patella\nVastus medialis muscle on the medial thigh acts to draw\nthe patella in a medial direction., the patella is much more\nsusceptible to dislocation if there is weak vastus medialis\nmuscle.\nThe medial patellofemoral ligament, extending from the\nmedial aspect of the patella to the medial femoral condyle,\nprovides a significant force against lateral displacement.\nThe lateral femoral condyle on the lateral aspect of the\ntrochlear groove is normally slightly higher than the medial\naspect, providing a buttress to the patella on the lateral side.\n\n![Bones of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/bones-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Bones of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nPatellar dislocation: Most common dislocations of the occur\nlaterally and are caused by high force impact on the patella.\nBones of the foot:\nThere are three groups of bones in the foot\n\n### 1: Seven-tarsal bone\n\n### 2: Five metatarsal bones\n\n### 3: Fourteen phalanges.\n\nThe tarsal bones are organized into three rows:\nProximal group: Talus and Calcaneus\nIntermediate: Navicular\nDistal group: Cuboid and Cuneiforms\n\n![Bones of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/bones-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p4.png)\n*Bones of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
09e3266d-ca30-4ac4-98f5-63ffa281b16e	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks	Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks	quiz	# Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	40	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
179c36a5-32d8-470b-b2dd-02df01ca5dd6	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-emergency-bradycardia-management	Emergency Bradycardia Management	quiz	# Emergency Bradycardia Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Emergency Bradycardia Management\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Emergency Bradycardia Management**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	50	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
8721f576-ba1a-45b7-98f2-2e87bf6c7719	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema	Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema	quiz	# Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	60	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
9eeb5d28-3b71-4c25-b1c4-2baeabf7fd71	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-xi-spinal-accessory-nerve	Cranial Nerve Xi_ Spinal Accessory Nerve	article	Spinal Accessory Nerve (CN XI):\nArises from cervical segments C1 - C5/C6, enters cranial cavity\nthrough foramen magnum, travels through posterior cranial\nfossa and exits skull through jugular foramen.\nOutside the cranium, it descends along the internal carotid\nartery and innervate sternocleidomastoid muscle.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Xi_ Spinal Accessory Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-xi-spinal-accessory-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Xi_ Spinal Accessory Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
5e68094f-1d07-48c7-aa93-9582cddd0426	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	alveolar-arterial-gradient	Alveolar Arterial Gradient	article	A-a gradient\nDifference between alveolar Po2 and arterial Po2\nA-a gradient: PA02-Pao2 (where PA02 alveolar po2 and Pao2\nis arterial po2)\nThe A-a gradient is used to determine whether O2 has\nequilibrated between alveolar gas and arterial blood.\nThe normal A-a gradient is between 0- and 10mm Hg. Since\nO2 normally equilibrates between alveolar gas and arterial\nblood, PAo2 is approximately equal to Pao2. e.g.,\nHypoventilation: CNS depression, Neuromuscular disease\n(including myasthenia gravis and kyphoscoliosis), Obesity\nand Low inspired fio2 on high altitude\nThe A-a gradient is increased (>10 mm Hg) if O2 does not\nequilibrate between alveolar gas and arterial blood (e.g.,\ndiffusion defect, V/Q defect, and right-to-left shunt) and\nPAo2 is greater than Pao ₂\nHypoxia and Hypoxemia\nHypoxia: decreased o2 delivery to the tissues caused by\ndecreased blood flow, hypoxemia, decreased haemoglobin\nconcentration, Co poisoning and Cyanide poisoning\nNote: Erythropoietin (EPO) is growth factor that is synthesized\nin the kidneys in response to hypoxia\nHypoxemia: Decrease in arterial pao2 caused by decreased\nAlveolar PAo2, diffusion defect, V/Q defects and right to left\nshunt	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
f5150646-4e29-4c66-92f9-0c9d559ae061	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed	Congenital Heart Disease in the ED	quiz	# Congenital Heart Disease in the ED\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Congenital Heart Disease in the ED\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Congenital Heart Disease in the ED**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	70	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
45ab261b-9122-4e54-bef0-fc1233f9d044	60d5abf2-5add-4661-9b03-d4d02aa30e0f	lower-limb-other-significant-structures	Lower Limb Other Significant Structures	article	Femoral Triangle:\nThe wedge-shaped area is located within the superomedial\naspect of the anterior thigh.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High Yield:**\n> Contents of Femoral Triangle:\n> From lateral to medial: Useful mnemonic (NAVEL)\n> Femoral nerve\n> Femoral artery\n> Femoral vein\n> Empty space (consists of femoral canal)\n> Lymph nodes\n> Note: Femoral nerve lies lateral to femoral artery and vein\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: In Femoral hernia part of the bowel pushes**\n> into the femoral canal underneath the femoral triangle.\n\nFemoral Sheath:\nThe femoral sheath originates as a prolongation of\nextraperitoneal fascia and encloses the femoral artery, femoral\nvein and femoral canal.\nNote: The femoral nerve does not lie within the femoral sheath,\nbut instead lies lateral to it.\n\n![Lower Limb Other Significant Structures - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/lower-limb-other-significant-structures/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Lower Limb Other Significant Structures — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nFemoral Canal:\nThe Femoral canal lies most medial of the femoral sheath.\nBorders of the femoral canal: (Useful mnemonic LAMP: FILP)\nLateral border: Femoral vein (Immediately lateral)\nAnterior border: Inguinal ligament\nMedial border: Lacunar ligament\nPosterior ligament: Pectineal ligament, Pectineus muscle\nand superior ramus of pubic bone.\nContents: Deep inguinal lymph nodes, lymphatic vessels,\nempty space and loose connective tissue.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: In Femoral hernia part of the bowel pushes**\n> into the femoral canal underneath the femoral triangle.\n\nThe Arches of Foot:\nFoot has three arches, one transverse arch and two\nlongitudinal (medial and lateral) arches. Shape of arches allows\nthem to act in the same way as a spring, bearing the weight of\nthe body and absorbing the shock produced during\nlocomotion.\nFormed by tarsal, metatarsal bones and supported by\nligaments.\nPopliteal Fossa:\nDiamond shaped area located on the posterior aspect of the\nknee, and is the pathway to pass by which vessels and nerves\npass between the thigh and the leg.\n\n![Lower Limb Other Significant Structures - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/lower-limb-other-significant-structures/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Lower Limb Other Significant Structures — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nBoundaries and Contents of Popliteal\nFossa:\nContents (From medial to lateral): Popliteal artery, popliteal\nvein, tibial nerve and common fibular nerve.\nNote: Tibial and common fibular nerve are the most superficial\ncontents of the popliteal fossa. Both are the branches of sciatic\nnerve\nThe common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris\ntendon, travelling along the lateral margin of the popliteal\nTibial nerve lies more medial to common fibular nerve.\n\n![Lower Limb Other Significant Structures - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/lower-limb-other-significant-structures/figure_03_p3.png)\n*Lower Limb Other Significant Structures — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
7ec6cca2-5803-46af-96fd-1e8e0c11c8a4	60d5abf2-5add-4661-9b03-d4d02aa30e0f	venous-drainage-of-lower-limb	Venous Drainage of Lower Limb	article	#### Superficial Veins of the Lower Limb\n\nThe great saphenous vein: originate from medial side of the\ndorsal venous arch in the foot, passes anterior to the medial\nmalleolus (not posterior), travels up the medial side of leg,\nknee and thigh to pass into saphenous opening in deep\nfascia covering the femoral triangle and join with the femoral\nvein below the inguinal ligament.\nThe small saphenous vein: originate from lateral side of\ndorsal venous arch in the foot, passes posterior to lateral\nmalleolus and up the back of leg to pierce the deep fascia\nand join the popliteal vein in popliteal fossa posterior to\nknee.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Varicose veins: Develop in superficial veins of the lower limb\n> because of reduced elasticity and incompetent valves in the\n> veins causing them to become dilated and tortuous.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
74e6ee4c-1987-4320-a653-7a971115d563	95d17db8-a9a1-4a31-8009-2b025318e82e	structure-of-the-eyeball-and-eyelid	Structure of the Eyeball and Eyelid	article	Structure of the Eyeball\nThe eyeball consists of 3 coats:\n\n### 1. External protective fibrous coat: Made up of anterior\n\ntransparent part the cornea and a posterior opaque part the\nsclera\n\n### 2. middle: vascular pigmented coat consists from behind\n\nforward.\nChoroid\nCiliary body (connects the choroid to iris)\nIris: Circular pigmented diaphragm, its aperture known as\nthe pupil, lies Infront of the lens, divides space between\ncornea and lens into anterior and posterior chambers\n\n### 3. Internal nervous coat: Called the Retina\n\nOptic Disc: Where the optic nerve leaves the retina. Optic\ndisc is pierced by the central artery of the retina.\nContents of the Eyeball: Consists of aqueous humour, lens\nand vitreous body\nAqueous Humour: Fills anterior and posterior chambers of eye\nLens: Transparent and Bi-Convex\nVitreous body: Fills the eyeball behind the lens.\nSupports posterior surface of lens.\nBlood Supply: Opthalmic artery\nCentral retinal artery is a branch of ophthalmic artery, and\nOpthalmic artery is a first major branch of internal carotid\nartery\nVenous drainage: Into Cavernous sinus\nMovements of the eyeball (Extra\nocular muscles)\nAll the extraocular muscles are innervated by oculomotor\nnerve except LR-6 and S0-4\n\nEye Lid:\nThe eyelids are thin, soft tissue structures that cover the\neyeball anteriorly. They offer protection from excessive light\nor injury and maintain lubrication by distributing tears over\nthe surface of the eyeball. The eyelids split into upper and\nlower portions, which meet at the medial and lateral canthi\nof the eye. The opening between the two eyelids is called the\npalpebral aperture.\nThe eyelid consists of five main layers (superficial to deep):\nSkin and subcutaneous tissue (Most superficial structure)\nOrbicularis oculi\nTarsal plates\nLevator apparatus\nConjunctiva\nOrbicularis oculi (Closes the eyelids): The orbicularis oculi\nmuscle is innervated by the branches of facial nerve and\ncloses the eyelids. Loss of function of the orbicularis oculi\ncauses an inability to close the eyelids tightly and a drooping\nof the lower eyelid (ectropion) resulting in spillage of tears.\nThis leads to drying of the cornea. The orbicularis oculi\nmuscle has three distinct parts: palpebral, lacrimal and\norbital part.\nActions:\nPalpebral part: gently closes the eyelids.\nLacrimal part: involved in the drainage of tears.\n\n![Structure of the Eyeball and Eyelid - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/structure-of-the-eyeball-and-eyelid/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Structure of the Eyeball and Eyelid — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nOrbital part: tightly closes the eyelids.\nOther muscles: (High Yield)\nThe levator palpebrae superioris muscle: innervated by the\noculomotor nerve and acts to raise the upper eyelid. Loss of\nfunction of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle results in\na complete ptosis.\nThe superior tarsal muscle is innervated by postganglionic\nsympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion, which\nhelps to maintain upper eyelid opening. Loss of function of\nthe superior tarsal muscle results in a partial ptosis.\nTarsal plate: The tarsal plates are located deep to the\norbicularis oculi muscle. There are two plates; the superior\ntarsus (upper eyelid) and inferior tarsus (lower eyelid).\nThe tarsal plate in the eyelid provides structural support,\nserves as an attachment site for muscles, houses meibomian\nglands, and helps distribute tears, distributed tear film can\nhelp flush away foreign particles and potential pathogens,\nreducing the risk of infection.\nIn the tarsal plates lie the Meibomian glands (also known as\ntarsal glands). These are a specialised type of sebaceous\ngland that secretes an oily substance onto the eye to slow\nthe evaporation of the eye's tear film. The oily substance also\nprevents the eyelids from sticking together when closed.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Dysfunction in these glands, often due to**\n> blockages or inflammation, can lead to reduced tear stability\n> and dry eye.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
682a0869-7d0a-4aeb-a79b-1a6c12a9216d	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-infective-endocarditis	Infective Endocarditis	quiz	# Infective Endocarditis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Infective Endocarditis\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Infective Endocarditis**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	80	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
8eb913b2-efc4-4528-bc90-ed8be6a83bbc	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-07	Quiz 07: Pharmacology & Toxicology, Autonomic System & Airway Anatomy	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 07\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 55-year-old male presented to ER with Achilles tendon rupture. He has recently started a course of antibiotics from his general physician. Which of the following antibiotics was he most likely prescribed?\n\n- **A. Ciprofloxacin** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Metronidazole\n- C. Doxycycline\n- D. Tetracycline\n- E. Penicillin\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSide effects of Quinolone (Ciprofloxacin)\n\nruptured tendon QT prolongation (recall)\n\nPhotosensitivity convulsions\n\nThere is increased risk of convulsion when Quinolones are taken with NSAIDS or theophylline. There is an increased risk of tendon damage when Quinolones are taken with corticosteroids. (RECALL)\n\nCiprofloxacin increases plasma concentration of theophylline(aminophylline). (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nThe phrenic nerve receive contributions from which of the following?\n\n- A. Anterior rami of C1 -C3\n- B. Anterior rami to C4 -C7\n- **C. Anterior rami of C3 - C5** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Posterior rami of C1 -C3\n- E. Posterior rami of C3 -C5\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe phrenic nerve originates from cervical spinal roots C3, C4 and C5 provide motor innervation to the diaphragm (RECALL).\n\nprovide sensory innervation the central part of the diaphragm, the pericardium and the mediastinal part of the parietal pleura. (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 40-year-old male presents to ER with a deep laceration to the proximal part of the forearm. On examination when patient is asked to make a fist, he is unable to flex the metacarpophalangeal joints or interphalangeal joints of index and middle or thumb, ring and little fingers are intact but there is loss of sensation over the lateral palm and the Palmer surface of lateral three and a half fingers. Nerve has most likely been affected is?\n\n- A. Musculocutaneous nerve\n- B. Ulnar nerve\n- C. Radial Nerve\n- D. Median and radial nerve\n- **E. Median nerve** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNerve roots: C6 - T1 (also contains fibers from C5 in some individuals).\n\nMotor functions: Innervates the flexor and pronator muscles in the anterior compartment of the forearm (except the flexor carpi ulnaris and part of the flexor digitorum profundus, innervated by the ulnar nerve (RECALL)). Also supplies innervation to the thenar muscles and lateral two lumbricals in the hand. (RECALL)\n\nSensory functions: Gives rise to the palmar cutaneous branch, which innervates the lateral aspect of the palm, and the digital cutaneous branch, which innervates the lateral three and a half fingers on the anterior (palmar) surface of the hand In Median nerve injury little finger is spared. (Innervated by ulnar nerve) RECALL wasting of thenar eminence: nerve involved= median nerve (RECALL)\n\nwasting of Hypothenar eminence: nerve involved= ulnar nerve (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nWhich of the following are the phagocytic cells derived from monocytes?\n\n- A. Neutrophils\n- B. Mast cells\n- **C. Macrophages** (Correct Answer)\n- D. NK cells\n- E. Eosinophils\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMonocytes circulate in the bloodstream for about one to three days and then typically migrate into tissues throughout the body where theydifferentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nSensory innervation of the nasopharynx is supplied by which of the following nerve?\n\n- A. Facial nerve\n- B. Mandibular nerve\n- C. Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **D. Maxillary nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Vagus nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nEach subdivision of the pharynx has different sensory innervation:\n\nThe nasopharynx is supplied by the maxillary nerve The oropharynx is supplied d by the glossopharyngeal (RECALL)\n\nThe laryngopharynx is supplied by the vagus nerve.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nWhich one of the following hormones helps in the secretion of HCL from the stomach?\n\n- A. Pepsin\n- B. Cholecystokinin\n- C. Secretin\n- D. Renin\n- **E. Gastrin** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGastrin is a hormone that is produced by G-cells in the lining of the stomach and upper small intestine. Gastrin stimulates the stomach to release gastric acid.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 34-year-old male presents to ER with an upper limb injury. He complains of the weakness of flexion of the thumb, index, and middle finger of his right hand. Which of the following nerves is most likely affected?\n\n- A. Posterior interosseous nerve\n- B. Palmer Digital branch\n- C. Deep branch of ulnar nerve\n- **D. Anterior interosseous nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Posterior interosseous nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe anterior interosseous nerve is the terminal motor branch of the median nerve. It branches from the median nerve in the proximal forearm between the two heads of the pronator teres muscle to run deep along the interosseous membrane. From proximal to distal, it innervates the flexor pollicis longus, lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus , and the pronator quadratus Anterior interosseous nerve syndrome is an isolated palsy of these three muscles. It manifests mostly as pain in the forearm accompanied frequently by a characteristic weakness of the flexion index and thumb finger.\n\nIn supracondylar fracture, the Most common nerve injured is the median nerve (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 50-year-old male presents to ER with generalized weakness, on performing neurological examination you noticed that he has unilateral absence of biceps reflex. Which spinal nerves is responsible for this reflex?\n\n- **A. C5-C6** (Correct Answer)\n- B. C7-C8\n- C. L1-L2\n- D. C8- T1\n- E. C6-C7\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBiceps reflex root value =C5-C Triceps reflex root value= C7-C8 knee = L3-L4(RECALL)\n\nAnkle= S1\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nDetoxification of lipid drugs and other harmful compounds in ER is carried out by?\n\n- A. Cytochrome F\n- B. Cytochrome D\n- **C. Cytochrome P450** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Cytochrome 360\n- E. Cytochrome bf\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCytochrome P450 enzymes function to metabolizepotentially toxic compounds. There are 57 humangenes coding for various cytochrome450.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 24-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after ingesting a large quantity of prescription pills half an hour ago. You are considering administering activated charcoal as part of the treatment. What is the primary mechanism by which activated charcoal decreases drug toxicity in this patient?\n\n- A. Promotes liver metabolism of the drug\n- B. Aids in renal elimination of toxins\n- C. Neutralizes the toxin chemically\n- D. Binds to toxins in the bloodstream\n- **E. Decrease absorption in GIT** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nActivated charcoal works by binding to toxins in the gastrointestinal tract, thereby reducing their absorption into the bloodstream. This is most effective when administered within a short time of ingestion ideally within the first hour. It does not aid in renal elimination, bind to toxins in the bloodstream, promote liver metabolism, or chemically neutralize toxins. Instead, it acts locally in the gut to adsorb drugs and toxins, limiting their systemic availability\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nThe orbicularis Oris muscle perform which of the following function?\n\n- A. Closing of eyes\n- B. Opening of the mouth\n- C. Blowing of the cheeks\n- D. Closing of the mouth\n- **E. Pursing the lips** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe orbicularis Oris muscle act to close purse the lips. This muscle also helps in puckering the lips due to which it is sometimes known as the "kissing muscle." The musicians also use this muscle.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 58-year-old male with chronic liver disease is being treated for hepatic encephalopathy. He is prescribed lactulose as part of his management. The primary goal of the treatment is to reduce his elevated serum ammonia levels. What is the primary physiological mechanism by which lactulose helps reduce ammonia levels in this patient?\n\n- A. Promotes the metabolism of ammonia in the liver\n- B. Enhances renal excretion of ammonia\n- C. Directly neutralizes ammonia in the bloodstream\n- D. Inhibits ammonia production by intestinal bacteria\n- **E. Acts as an osmotically active agent, drawing water into the colon** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLactulose decreases ammonia levels through the following mechanisms:\n\nLactulose is a disaccharide that remains undigested in the small intestine and exerts its therapeutic effect mainly in the colon Osmotic Activity: Lactulose draws water into the colon, softening the stool and increasing bowel movements. The increased bowel movements help eliminate ammonia from the gut before it can enter the bloodstream Acidic Environment: As lactulose is broken down by bacteria in the colon, it produces short-chain fatty acids, which lower the pH and create an acidic environment. In this acidic environment, ammonia (NH3) is converted to ammonium (NH4+), which is less likely to be absorbed into the bloodstream.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nAn 18-year-old boy presents to the ED with a nosebleed. On examination, you suspect the site of bleeding to be in little's area which of the following blood vessels are likely to be involved?\n\n- A. Descending palatine and ascending pharyngeal arteries\n- **B. Sphenopalatine and superior labial arteries** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Ascending pharyngeal and descending palatine arteries\n- D. Anterior ethmoidal and posterior superior alveolar arteries\n- E. Descending palatine and anterior ethmoidal arteries\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLittle's area also known as Kieselbach's plexus is formed by:\n\nSuperior labial artery Sphenopalatine artery The greater palatine artery The anterior ethmoidal artery\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 22-year-old male is brought to the emergency department by his friends after taking a high dose of amphetamines at a party. He appears agitated, has a rapid heart rate, and is sweating profusely. which of the following signs is most likely to be present as a direct effect of amphetamine intoxication?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  Antihistamines, tricyclic |  Tachycardia, hyperthermia, |\n> |  | antidepressants, | mydriasis, warm and dry skin, |\n> | Anticholinergic | carbamazepine, | urinary retention, loss of |\n> |  | phenothiazines | sweating, agitation/delirium, |\n> |  |  | seizures. |\n> |  | Carbamates and | * Salivation, lacrimation, miosis, |\n> |  | organophosphate | urination, diarrhoea, |\n> | Cholinergic | insecticides, some | bronchorrhoea, |\n> |  | mushrooms. | bronchospasm, bradycardia, |\n> |  |  | hypotension, vomiting, muscle |\n> |  |  | weakness. |\n> | Hallucinogenic | Amphetamines, cocaine, |  Hallucinations, panic, |\n> |  | MDMA. | seizures, hypertension, |\n> |  |  | tachycardia, tachypnoea |\n> | Opioid |  Morphine, heroin, codeine, | Hypoventilation, hypotension, |\n> |  | methadone | miosis, sedation, bradycardia. |\n> |  |  |  Ataxia, blurred vision, |\n> |  |  Anticonvulsants, | sedation, hallucinations, |\n> | Sedative/hypnotic | benzodiazepines, ethanol. | slurred speech, hypotonia, |\n> |  |  | hyporeflexia, hypotension, |\n> |  |  | nystagmus. |\n> |  |  Serotonergic drugs (for |  Agitation, confusion, |\n> | Serotonin | example citalopram, | myoclonus, hyperreflexia, |\n> |  | tramadol, ecstasy | sweating, tremor, diarrhoea, |\n> |  |  | incoordination/ataxia, fever. |\n> |  |  |  Tachycardia, hypertension, |\n> | Sympathomimetic | Amphetamines, cocaine, | tachypnoea, mydriasis, |\n> |  | MDMA. | agitation, seizures, |\n> |  |  | hyperthermia, tremor, |\n> |  |  | sweating. |\n\n- A. Constricted pupils\n- B. Decreased respiratory rate\n- C. Bradycardia\n- D. Hypotension\n- **E. Dilated pupils** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nToxidromes (High Yield):\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nThe conus medullaris of the spinal cord lies at which of the following vertebral level in adults?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. L1/L2** (Correct Answer)\n- B. T11/T12\n- C. T12/L1\n- D. L3/L4\n- E. L4/L5\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nConus medullaris lies at birth= L3 level By the age of 21= L1/L2\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 7:** 1-A, 2-C, 3-E, 4-C, 5-D, 6-E, 7-D, 8-A, 9-C, 10-E, 11-E, 12-E, 13-B, 14-E, 15-A\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
0db347ac-5aaf-4889-8c22-1c29bd48719e	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-31	Quiz 31: Lower Limb Anatomy, Gastrointestinal Surgery & Pharmacology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 31\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 45-year-old woman presents to the ED with complaint of difficulty swallowing and occasional regurgitation of undigested food. You suspect achalasia cardia. if your suspected diagnosis is correct pathology lies in?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  Neurons and ganglia that |  | local |  |\n> |  Submucosal plexus | lie within the submucosal |  |  | secretion |\n> | (Meissner plexus) | the alimentary | and nutrient absorption. |  |  |\n> |  |  Neurons and ganglia that |  | inherent |  |\n> | Myenteric plexus | lie between the longitudinal | m | enic motility of the |  |\n> | (Auerbach plexus) | and circular smooth muscle | Gir g |  | y |\n> |  | layers of the muscularis |  |  |  |\n> |  | propria. |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Fibrosis of the esophageal submucosa\n- B. Inflammation of the esophageal mucosa\n- C. Dysfunction of the Meissner plexus\n- D. Atrophy of the lower esophageal sphincter\n- **E. Dysfunction of the myenteric plexus ganglion cells** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAchalasia cardia:\n\nPrimary esophageal motility disorder characterized by the impaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) and absence of peristalsis i n the esophagus. This condition is primarily attributed to dysfunction in the myenteric plexus, which is a network of nerve fibers and ganglion cells located between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the esophagus.\n\nMeissner and Myenteric plexus :\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 30-year-old man is involved in a road traffic accident and suffers a serious injury to his right thigh. As a consequence of his injury, he damages the obturator canal. Which one of the following clinical manifestations is possible?\n\n- A. Anterior thigh motor loss\n- B. Posterior compartment motor and sensory loss\n- **C. Medial thigh motor loss** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Lateral thigh motor loss\n- E. Anterior thigh Sensory loss\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe obturator canal transmits the obturator nerve and vessels, which primarily supply the muscles of the medial thigh. Damage to the obturator canal could result in motor an d sensory deficits in medial thigh Obturator nerve\n\nNerve roots: L2-L4 Motor functions: Innervates the medial thigh muscles.\n\nSensory functions: Upper medial thigh via cutaneous branches\n\nAnatomical course: Arises from the lumbar plexus formed within psoas major muscle and enters the thigh through obturator canal. (a small opening in the superior aspect of the obturator foramen)\n\nNote: The obturator nerve innervates all of the muscles of the medial compartment of the thigh (except for the hamstring part of the adductor magnus innervated by the tibial portion of the sciatic nerve).\n\nClinical: Damage to the obturator nerve causes weakness of adduction, resulting in a lateral swinging of the limb during walking Sensory Loss: Loss of sensation over upper medial thigh\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 35-year-old male patient brought into the ED with femoral fracture Requiring general anesthesia for surgery. Which one of the following glands inhibited is most responsible for saliva production during GA?\n\n- A. Adrenal gland\n- **B. Parotid gland** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Thyroid gland\n- D. Sub mandibular\n- E. Sublingual\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDuring General Anesthesia, the parotid gland is the major salivary gland most responsible for saliva production, and it is more likely to be affect ed by the anesthesia-induced inhibition of salivation compared to the sublingual gland.\n\nParotid Gland: The parotid gland is primarily responsible for producing serous saliva. During GA, t he parasympathetic nervous system is suppressed, which can lead to a significant reduction in saliva production from the parotid gland.\n\nSublingual Gland: The sublingual gland primarily produces mucous saliva. While it is also influence d by the autonomic nervous system, the effect of GA on the sublingual gland may not be as pronounced as on the parotid gland. The reduction in saliva from the sublingual gland is typically less significant during GA.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 40-year-old male was brought into to the ED after he falls approximately 20 feet whilst tiling a roof. He suffers a femoral shaft fracture that damages a branch of the posterior division of the femoral nerve. Which of the following muscles is supplied by a branch of the posterior division of the femoral nerve?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Femoral nerve | Muscle attached |\n> | In abdomen |  |\n> | Anterior division of femoral nerve | Sartorius |\n> |  | Pectineus |\n> | Posterior division of femoral nerve | Rectus femoris |\n> |  | Vastus lateralis |\n> |  | Vastus intermedius |\n> |  | Vastus medialis |\n\n- **A. Vastus medialis** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Pectineus\n- C. Sartorius\n- D. Psoas major\n- E. Iliacus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield:\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nWhich one of the following dermatomes is likely affected in medial knee side?\n\n- A. L4\n- B. L2\n- C. L1\n- D. L5\n- **E. L3** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nDermatomes of Lower extremity: Important to r emeber all the landmarks of dermatomes\n\nL1: The inguinal region and the very top of the medial thigh, Upper anterior thigh L2: The middle and lateral aspect of the anterior t high.\n\nL3: Medial knee (Medial femoral condyle) L4: Medial malleolus, Medial surface of big toe L5: Toes 2-3,4, lateral surface of big toe and Dorsal and plantar aspects of foot S1: Toe 5 (Little finger), lateral malleolus S2: At the midpoint of the popliteal fossa. S3: At the horizontal gluteal crease (fold of the Bu ttocks), Ischial tuberosity S4/5: The perianal area.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 25-year-old male patient exhibits a loss of sensation and pain at lateral thigh. Which one of the following nerves is responsible for this?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Lateral | nerve of | Lumbar plexus (L2, L3) | Lateral thigh |\n\n- A. Saphenous nerve\n- **B. Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Obturator nerve\n- D. Anterior cutaneous branches\n- E. Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield to Remeber:\n\nAnterior division of the femoral nerve\n\nAnterior cutaneous branches Branch to sartorius Branch to pectineus\n\n2: Posterior division of the femoral nerve\n\nSaphenous nerve Branches to quadriceps femoris(Which include Rectus femoris, Vastus lateralis, Vastus medialis and Vastus intermedius)\n\nFemoral nerveSensory innervation of lateral and posterior thigh: (Important to remember)\n\nClinical pearls: Damage to the posterior division of femoral nerve causing impaired flexion at hip and impaired extension of the leg resulting from paralysis of the quadriceps femoris (Which include Rectus femoris, Vastus lateralis, Vastus medialis and Vastus intermedius.\n\nSensory loss: Loss of sensation over anteromedial thigh, anteromedial knee, medial leg and medial foot.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 55-year-old male brought into the ED with advanced liver failure. Which one of the following drugs does not need dose adjustments in this patient?\n\n- A. Furosemide\n- B. Diazepam\n- C. Morphine\n- **D. Digoxin** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Bisoprolol\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLiver failure: Liver failure has little influence on digoxin metabolism or clearance; therefore, no dose adjustment is necessary.\n\nRenal failure: Digoxin is excreted renally, and impaired renal function can cause increased digoxin levels and digoxin toxicity. In this case the patient should have their digoxin dose reduced and their digoxin level and electrolytes should be carefullymonitored.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 24-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of pain in umbilical region radiating towards right lower abdomen. You suspect appendicitis and perform psoas muscle inflammation in appendicitis. When you perform psoas sign pain is aggravated by which one of the following movements?\n\n- **A. Extension** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Medial rotation\n- C. Adduction\n- D. Abduction\n- E. Lateral rotation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPsoas sign: Pain on extension Obturator sign: Pain on internal or medial rotation\n\nHigh yield:\n\nRovsing's sign:Pain in the right lower quadrant i s complained of when palpation pressure is exerted in the left lower quadrant. It is also called referred rebound tenderness and when present is quite helpful in supporting the diagnosis. Psoas sign:This test is performed by having the patient lie on his left side. The examiner then slowly extends the patient's right thigh, thus stretching the iliopsoas muscle. This will produce pain to make the sign positive. This indicates presence ofirritative inflamed appendix in close proximity to the psoas muscle. This is possible in retrocaecal appendicitis.\n\nObturator test:Passive internal rotation of flexed right thigh with the patient in supine position will elicit pain. This positive obturator sign is diagnostic of pelvic appendicitis.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 12-year-old boy presents with evidenced by dark stools (melena). He has had no other gastrointestinal symptoms and is otherwise healthy. Considering his age and the symptom pattern, Meckel's diverticulum is suspected. Which one of the following is the most common mode of presentation in this child?\n\n- A. Obstruction\n- B. Hernia\n- **C. Bleeding** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Persistent fever and diarrhea\n- E. Diverticulitis\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMeckel's Diverticulum:\n\nTrue diverticula of all 3 layers of the small intestines Caused by incomplete obliteration of the vitellineduct.\n\nRule of 2s: 2 years old, 2 ft from ileocecal valve, 2 i n long, 2% of population Usually, asymptomatic Painless bleeding due to an ulcer caused by heterotopic gastric tissue most common presentation in children Obstruction is caused by intussusception, volvulus, and hernia, the most common presentation in adults.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 60-year-old patient was involved in a road traffic accident and subsequently complained of unable to perform extension of thigh at hip and flexion at knee joint. Which one of the following nerves is likely affected?\n\n- A. Common peroneal nerve\n- **B. Sciatic nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Obturator nerve\n- D. Femoral nerve\n- E. Saphenous nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAll the muscles of the posterior compartment of thigh are extensors of thigh at hip and flexors at knee joint. While Biceps femoris is also lateral rotator of thigh at hip joint and Semitendinosus and Semimembranosus also medial rotators of thigh at hip joint\n\nClinical pearls: All the muscles of the posterior thigh originate from the ischial tuberosity of the pelvis. In some cases, mostly in young athletes, sudden and forceful contraction of the hamstring muscles can result in an avulsion fracture of the ischial tuberosity. It can also be associated with sciatic nerve damage.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 34-year-old woman is rushed to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain radiating to her back. She describes the pain as sudden and excruciating, and it is accompanied by nausea and vomiting. Her past medical history includes heavy alcohol consumption. Which one of the following enzymes is secreted in an active status in pancreatic juice, causing this condition?\n\n- A. Trypsinogen\n- B. Pepsinogen\n- **C. Trypsin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. elastase\n- E. Chymotrypsinogen\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInflammation of the pancreas occurs in when digestive enzymes secreted in pancreatic juice start digesting its own tissues.\n\nPathophysiology:\n\nTrypsin is an enzyme that is involved in the digestion of proteins. Normally, trypsinogen (the inactive precursor of trypsin) is activated to trypsin in t he small intestine, where it aids in protein digestion. However, in cases of pancreatitis, trypsin can become prematurely activated within the pancreas itself due to factors like alcohol consumption o r other triggers. This activation of trypsin within t he pancreas can lead to the autodigestion of pancreatic tissue, causing inflammation.\n\nChymotrypsinogen is the inactive precursor of chymotrypsin. Activation of chymotrypsinogen to its active form, chymotrypsin, occurs through th e action of trypsin. Once trypsin is produced through the activation of trypsinogen, it can then activate chymotrypsinogen by cleaving it to chymotrypsin. Chymotrypsin further breaks down protein molecules into smaller peptides and amino acids that can be absorbed by the body.\n\nNote: Both Pancreatic lipase and amylase can be elevated in cases of pancreatitis, but lipase is often considered a more reliable marker due to its higher specificity and longer elevation period. Clinically, lipase levels are preferred for diagnosingand assessing the severity of pancreatitis.\n\nNote: In the context of pancreatitis, an intraluminal fluid collection occurs within the lumina of the pancreatic ducts (Recall). When there is an intraluminal fluid collection in pancreatitis, it may contribute to the progression of the disease, exacerbate inflammation, and cause further damage to the pancreas. This condition can lead to complications such as pseudocysts, acute necrotic collections, or walled-off necrosis, which may require medical or surgical intervention to manage and resolve.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 22-year-old college student received a severe blow on the inferolateral side of the left knee joint while playing football. Radiographic examination reveals a fracture of the head of fibula. Which one of the following nerves is damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Nerve roots | L4--S2 |\n> | Motor supply | Short head of the biceps femoris |\n> |  | Terminal Branches: |\n> |  | Deep fibular nerve: All muscles in the anterior compartment of leg and |\n> |  | intrinsic dorsal foot muscles |\n> |  | Superficial fibular nerve: Muscles of lateral compartments of the leg. |\n> | Sensory | Directly: Skin over the upper lateral leg via lateral sural cutaneous nerves. |\n> | supply |  |\n> |  | Terminal Branches: |\n> |  | Deep fibular nerve: Skin over the web-space between first and second toe. |\n> |  | Superficial fibular nerve: Skin over lower anterolateral leg, dorsum of the |\n> |  | foot except web space between first and second toe. |\n\n- A. Deep peroneal nerve\n- **B. Common peroneal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Tibial nerve\n- D. Sciatic nerve\n- E. Saphenous nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCommon fibular (Peroneal nerve)\n\nArises as the smaller terminal portion of the sciatic nerve at the apex of the popliteal fossa. The common fibular nerve continues by wrapping around the neck of the fibula and passing between t he attachments of the fibularis longus muscle to enter the lateral compartment of the leg where i t divides into its terminal branches, the superficial and deep fibular nerves.\n\nNote: It is very important to remember direct innervation of common fibular nerve.\n\nDeep fibular nerve root: L4-L5 Superficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.\n\nClinical pearls:\n\n1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the common fibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a result of fracture of the head or neck of the fibula because it passes behind the head of the fibula and then winds laterally around the neck. The nerve damage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion) and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the foot and lateral aspect of the leg and causes para lysis of all muscles in the anterior and lateral compartments of the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors muscles of the foot).\n\n2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by fracture of the fibula. As the muscles that the superficial fibular nerve innervates are evertors, injury to the nerve may result in a loss of eversion a nd loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral leg and dorsum of foot.\n\n3: Deep fibular nerve:\n\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in foot drop. In foot drop foot will appear permanently plantarflexed, foot drop with high-stepping gait, weakness of foot inversion.\n\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes.\n\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or common peroneal nerve or also due to deep fibular nerve injury.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 25-year-old patient presents with a stab injury to the groin area. He has damaged a structure that lies immediately medially to the femoral nerve in femoral triangle. Which one of the following is correct?\n\n- A. Sartorius\n- B. Femoral canal\n- **C. Femoral artery** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Femoral vein\n- E. Iliacus muscle\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nContents of Femoral Triangle:\n\nFrom lateral to medial: Useful mnemonic (NAVEL)\n\nFemoral nerve Femoral artery Femoral vein Empty space (consists of femoral canal) Lymph nodes\n\nNote: Femoral nerve lies lateral to femoral artery and vein\n\nClinical pearls: In Femoral hernia part of the bowel pushes into the femoral canal underneath the femoral triangle Important to remember here :\n\nFemoral Canal:\n\nThe Femoral canal lies most medial of the femoral sheath.\n\nBorders of the femoral canal: (Useful mnemonic LAMP: FILP)\n\nLateral border: Femoral vein (Immediately lateral)\n\nAnterior border: Inguinal ligament Medial border: Lacunar ligament Posterior ligament: Pectineal ligament, Pectineus muscle and superior ramus of pubic bone.\n\nContents: Deep inguinal lymph nodes, lymphatic vessels, empty space and loose connective tissue.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nYou have been asked to administer an intramuscular injection to a patient in the Emergency Department. Which of the following anatomical points is the safest location in the gluteal region?\n\n- A. Lower medial\n- B. Upper medial\n- **C. Upper outer** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Centrally within gluteal region\n- E. Lower lateral\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nClinical pearls:\n\nIntramuscular injections in the gluteal region: Th e anatomical course of the sciatic nerve must be considered when administering intramuscular injections into the gluteal region. The region can b e divided into quadrants using 2 lines.\n\nOne line descends vertically from the highest point on the iliac crest.\n\nOther lines pass through vertical line halfway between the highest point.\n\nNote: Sciatic nerve lies in the lower medial quadrant. To avoid injury of sciatic nerve IM injections in the gluteal region only be given in upper lateral or upper outer quadrant.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 50-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of pain and numbness in his lower limb. On further examination he has loss of sensation in lower leg near the medial malleolus. Which one of the following dermatomes is likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. L1\n- **B. L4** (Correct Answer)\n- C. L3\n- D. L2\n- E. L5\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nDermatomes of Lower extremity: Important to r emeber all the landmarks of dermatomes\n\nL1: The inguinal region and the very top of the medial thigh, Upper anterior thigh L2: The middle and lateral aspect of the anterior t high.\n\nL3: Medial knee (Medial femoral condyle) L4: Medial malleolus, Medial surface of big toe L5: Toes 2-3,4, lateral surface of big toe and Dorsal and plantar aspects of foot S1: Toe 5 (Little finger), lateral malleolus S2: At the midpoint of the popliteal fossa. S3: At the horizontal gluteal crease (fold of the Bu ttocks), Ischial tuberosity S4/5: The perianal area.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 31:** 1-E, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A, 5-E, 6-B, 7-D, 8-A, 9-C, 10-B, 11-C, 12-B, 13-C, 14-C, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
960d0642-a407-4224-9416-c08331eab1f3	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-40	Quiz 40: Neurotoxins, Enzyme Induction & Autoimmune Pathologies	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 40\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 30-year-old man falls off his bicycle and hits his head. He has a 4 cm deep cut on his scalp, which is actively bleeding. He is otherwise stable. What causes the blood to clot in this case?\n\n- A. Increased blood pressure\n- B. Conversion of prothrombin to thrombin\n- C. Dehydration\n- D. Conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin\n- **E. Activation of the external clotting cascade** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWhen a person suffers a scalp laceration, the coagulation process begins with the extrinsic (external) clotting cascade. This pathway is triggered when tissue factor (TF) is released from damage d endothelial cells. TF binds with Factor VII, leading to the activation of Factor X, which then contributes to clot formation by generating thrombin and fibrin (forms the final stable clot).\n\nExtrinsic Pathway (Quick & External Trigger)\n\nTriggered by: Tissue damage (e.g., cuts, trauma) Key player: Tissue Factor (TF) (Factor III) released from injured cells Speed: Fast (happens within seconds)\n\nMain function: Quickly forms a clot to stop external bleeding Example: A scalp laceration from a bicycle fall\n\nIntrinsic Pathway (Slow & Internal Trigger)\n\nTriggered by: Damage inside the blood vessel (e. g., endothelial injury, inflammation)\n\nKey players: Factors XII, XI, IX, and VIII (activated in a stepwise manner)\n\nSpeed: Slow (takes minutes)\n\nMain function: Forms a stable clot in internal injuries or vascular damage Example: Clotting in deep vein thrombosis (DV T) or inside blood vessels\n\nFinal Common Pathway (Where Both Meet):\n\nBoth pathways activate Factor X, leading to: 1. Conversion of prothrombin -> thrombin 2. Conversion of fibrinogen -> fibrin (forms the final stable clot)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 38-year-old man presents to the ED with muscle weakness and sensory loss after consuming food that was improperly stored. He has a history of food poisoning symptoms several hours after eating rice. Nerve conduction studies show impairment of nerve function. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism responsible for his symptoms caused by Bacillus cereus?\n\n- A. Inhibition of acetylcholine release\n- **B. Direct axonal damage due to neurotoxins** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Reduced glucose uptake by neurons\n- D. Viral-induced demyelination\n- E. Autoimmune-mediated nerve injury\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBacillus cereus produces neurotoxins that can lead to direct axonal damage. After ingestion of contaminated food (often improperly stored rice), these toxins affect the peripheral nerves, causing neuropathy. The axonal damage manifests a s muscle weakness, sensory loss, and impairment of nerve conduction, which is consistent with t he symptoms and findings in this patient\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 65-year-old man on chronic warfarin therapy for atrial fibrillation is started on carbamazepine for new-onset seizures. One month later, his INR is found to be subtherapeutic. Which of the following best explains the interaction between carbamazepine and warfarin?\n\n- A. Carbamazepine competes with warfarin for plasma protein binding, increasing warfarin levels\n- B. Carbamazepine inhibits warfarin metabolism, increasing bleeding risk\n- C. Carbamazepine enhances warfarin absorption from the gastrointestinal tract\n- **D. Carbamazepine induces liver enzymes, reducing warfarin effect** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Carbamazepine directly inhibits vitamin K activity\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCarbamazepine is a strong inducer of cytochrome P450 enzymes. Warfarin is metabolized by cytochrome P450 enzymes. Enzyme induction leads to faster metabolism of warfarin, lower warfarin blood levels, and reduced anticoagulant effect -> making the patient at higher risk of clotting rather than bleeding. Therefore, warfarin doses may need to be increased when a patient is on carbamazepine.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 55-year-old woman presents with painful swelling in her hands, especially around the joints, and morning stiffness lasting more than an hour. Clinical examination suggests active synovitis. Which of the following bones/joints of the hand is most commonly involved in synovitis?\n\n- **A. Metacarpophalangeal joints** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Scaphoid bone\n- C. Carpometacarpal joint of the thumb\n- D. Distal phalanges\n- E. Distal interphalangeal joints\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSynovitis is inflammation of the synovial membrane that lines joints. In conditions like rheumatoid arthritis, which commonly causes synovitis, the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints are classicallyinvolved, leading to swelling, tenderness, and reduced function in the hand. MCP joint involvement leads to the characteristic "puffy hands" and difficulty making a fist seen in active synovitis.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 20-year-old male patient with chronic diarrhea and bloating is diagnosed with carbohydrate malabsorption. Further testing reveals a deficiency of enzymes responsible for breaking down disaccharides into monosaccharides. Which structure is primarily responsible for this enzymatic activity?\n\n- A. Pancreatic acini\n- B. Gastric mucosa\n- **C. Brush border of small intestine** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Large intestine\n- E. Submucosa of small intestine\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe brush border of the small intestine contains enzymes such as maltase, sucrase, and lactase, which break down disaccharides into absorbablemonosaccharides. This enzymatic activity occurs at the microvilli (Brush border) of the enterocyte s, allowing for efficient carbohydrate digestion a nd absorption. A deficiency in these enzymes (e. g., lactase deficiency results in lactose intolerance) leads to malabsorption, causing symptoms like diarrhea and bloating.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 28-year-old male with a history of asthma presents to the emergency department with shortness of breath and wheezing after exposure to dust. He also complains of skin itching and hives. Laboratory tests show elevated serum tryptase levels. Which of the following cells is primarily responsible for the release of histamine in this patient?\n\n- A. Eosinophils\n- B. Neutrophils\n- C. Basophils\n- **D. Mast cells** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Macrophages\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMast cells play a crucial role in allergic reactions and anaphylaxis by releasing histamine, tryptase, and other inflammatory mediators upon activation by allergens. These cells are widely distributed in connective tissues, especially near blood vessels, and are activated when IgE antibodies bound to their surface receptors encounter an allergen. This leads to degranulation, releasing histamine, which causes vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and bronchoconstriction all seen in asthma and allergic reactions.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 17-year female student, with known epilepsy, is brought into ED by ambulance with ongoing generalized tonic-clonic seizures for 30 minutes. She has received two doses of benzodiazepines, the First dose at home buccal midazolam, and the second dose of midazolam by paramedics on the way to the hospital. The most recent dose was 10 minutes ago but her seizure is ongoing. What is the next most appropriate management step?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  | * Protect the patient. Do not restrain. |\n> |  | 7 | * Consider airway adjunct. |\n> | 0-5 |  | Administer oxygen. |\n> | Immediate |  | * Place patient in a semi-prone position, head down |\n> | management |  | * Gain IV access |\n> |  |  | * Obtain BM - If hypoglycaemic: Give 150-200ml 10% glucose IV stat |\n> |  |  | * If suspicion of alcohol excess or malnutrition commence 1 pair Pabrinex IV BEFORE |\n> |  |  | glucose replacement. |\n> |  |  | = Ifno IV access or (pre-hospital, paramedics hospital transfer) |\n> |  |  | * Buccal midazolam 10mg or Rectal diazepam 10-20 mg if buccal midazolam is not |\n> | 5-10 minutes: |  |  |\n> |  |  | available. |\n> | Early SE |  |  |\n> |  |  | = access gained |\n> |  |  | * Lorazepam (intravenous) 0.1 mg/kg (usually a 4 mg bolus, repeated once after 10-20 |\n> |  |  | minutes; rate not critical). |\n> |  |  | * if seizures continue, treat as below |\n> |  |  | * Alert the anesthetic and ICU |\n> |  |  | = Give 2"4 line anticonvulsants |\n> | 20-40 minutes: |  | IV Levetiracetam 60mg/kg, maximum 4500mg (in sodium chloride 0.9% over |\n> | established SE |  | 10 minutes) |\n> |  |  | * Phenytoin infusion at a dose of 15-18 mg/kg at a rate of 50 mg/minute or |\n> |  |  | fosphenytoin infusion at a dose of 15-20 mg phenytoin equivalents (PE)/kg at a rate of |\n> |  |  | 50-100 mg PE/minute |\n> |  |  | * Phenobarbital bolus of 10-15 mg/kg at a rate of 100 mg/minute (If any patient |\n> |  |  | already on phenytoin, consider phenobarbital in such patients) |\n> |  |  | = General anaesthesia, with one of: |\n> |  | A . | * Propofol (1-2 mg/kg bolus, then 2-10 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect |\n> | 40- 60 minutes: |  | * Midazolam (0.1-0.2 mg/kg bolus, then 0.05-0.5 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect. |\n> | Refractory SE |  | * Thiopental sodium (3-5 mg/kg bolus, then 3-5 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect; after |\n> |  |  | 2-3 days infusion rate needs reduction as fat stores are saturated. |\n> |  |  | * Anaesthetic continued for 12-24 hours after the last clinical or electrographic seizure, |\n> |  |  | then dose tapered. |\n\n- **A. IV Levetiracetam** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Phenytoin\n- C. Phenobarbital bolus\n- D. Third dose of IV lorazepam\n- E. RSI with thiopental sodium\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nExplanation Nice guideline second line recommendation: If convulsive status epilepticus does not respond to 2 doses of a benzodiazepine, give any of the following medicines intravenously as a second-li ne treatment:\n\nlevetiracetam phenytoin sodium valproate.\n\nNote: Take into account that levetiracetam may be quicker to administer and have fewer adverse effects than the alternative options.\n\nStatus epilepticus management\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 33-year-old female presents with complaints of dry eyes and an inability to produce tears when crying. She also reports weakness on the right side of her face, including difficulty closing her right eye and an asymmetric smile. Which nerve is primarily responsible for the parasympathetic innervation of the lacrimal gland?\n\n- A. Trigeminal nerve (CN V)\n- B. Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)\n- C. Oculomotor nerve (CN III)\n- D. Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)\n- **E. Facial nerve (CN VII)** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nParasympathetic innervation of the lacrimal gland:\n\nThe lacrimal gland is responsible for tear production and is primarily innervated by the parasympathetic fibers of the facial nerve (CN VII). The greater petrosal nerve, a branch of CN VII, carries these preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to th e pterygopalatine ganglion, from where postganglionic fibers travel via the zygomatic branch o f CN V2 (maxillary nerve) and then to the lacrimal nerve (branch of CN V1) to reach the lacrimal gland.\n\nNote: Trigeminal nerve (CN V): lacrimal nerve (branch of CN V1) helps carry parasympathetic fibers to the lacrimal gland, the actual parasympathetic innervation originates from the facial nerve (C N VII).\n\nSympathetic innervation of the lacrimal gland:\n\nSympathetic fibres originate from the superior cervical ganglion and are carried by the internal carotid plexus and deep petrosal nerve. They join with the parasympathetic fibres in the nerve of p terygoid canal and follow the same route to supply the gland and inhibits fluid secretion from t he lacrimal gland.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 30-year-old man presents to the ED with muscle stiffness, painful spasms, and difficulty opening his mouth after stepping on a rusty nail. The symptoms are attributed to infection with Clostridium tetani. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of the toxin responsible for his symptoms?\n\n- A. Activation of excitatory neurotransmitter release at neuromuscular junctions\n- B. Destruction of myelin sheaths around motor neurons\n- C. Direct destruction of muscle fibers\n- D. A) Inhibition of acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction\n- **E. Inhibition of inhibitory neurotransmitter release in the central nervous system** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nClostridium tetani produces tetanospasmin, a potent neurotoxin, Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely along motor neurons to the central nervous system (CNS). It inhibits the release of inhibitoryneurotransmitters mainly gamma-aminobutyricacid (GABA) and glycine from interneurons. With out inhibitory signals, motor neurons become overactive, leading to uncontrolled muscle contraction (spastic paralysis), seen as muscle stiffness, spasms, and lockjaw (trismus).\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 55-year-old man with hypertension and type 1 diabetes mellitus is prescribed propranolol for migraine prophylaxis. A week later, he experiences an episode of severe hypoglycemia but reports an absence of typical warning symptoms such as tremors and palpitations. Which of the following best explains this phenomenon?\n\n- **A. Propranolol impairs hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis by blocking beta-adrenergic receptors** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Propranolol increases peripheral glucose uptake by enhancing insulin sensitivity\n- C. Propranolol enhances insulin secretion, leading to hypoglycemia.\n- D. Propranolol stimulates pancreatic alpha-cell activity, increasing glucagon release\n- E. Propranolol induces hypoglycemia by promoting renal glucose excretion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNon-selective beta-blockers like propranolol inhibit both beta and beta-adrenergic receptors. The blockade of beta receptors in the liver impairs gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, which are critical processes for increasing blood glucose levels during hypoglycemia. Additionally, beta blockade blunts the adrenergic symptoms (e.g., tremors, palpitations) that typically alert patients to low blood sugar levels, making hypoglycemia worse due to delayed recognition In contrast, cardioselective beta-blockers (e.g., atenolol, metoprolol) primarily block beta receptors a nd have a lesser effect on glucose metabolism, making them a safer option for diabetic patients when beta-blockade is necessary\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 45-year-old woman with known Addison's disease presents to the ED for a routine check-up. Her blood pressure is slightly elevated. The doctor considers prescribing an antihypertensive. Which of the following classes of drugs would be contraindicated in this patient?\n\n- A. Calcium channel blockers\n- B. ACE inhibitors\n- **C. Thiazide diuretics** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs)\n- E. Beta-blockers\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAddison's disease is a form of primary adrenal insufficiency where the adrenal glands produce insufficient cortisol and aldosterone. Thiazide diuretic s (like hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone) cause sodium loss, potassium loss, and volume depletion. In Addison's disease, patients are already prone to hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and hypovolemia due to low aldosterone. Giving a thiazide canworsen electrolyte imbalance and cause a dangerous drop in blood pressure (leading to adrenal crisis).\n\nThiazide Diuretics Contraindications:\n\nSevere renal failure Hyponatremia Hypokalemia Gout (Thiazides raise uric acid levels -> can trigger gout attacks)\n\nAddison's disease (risk of severe electrolyte imbalance and crisis)\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 48-year-old woman undergoes imaging for evaluation of chronic headaches. MRI reveals a lesion in the pituitary fossa. Which of the following anatomical structures is located directly inferior to the pituitary fossa?\n\n- **A. Sphenoid sinus** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Ethmoid sinus\n- C. Maxillary sinus\n- D. Cavernous sinus\n- E. Frontal sinus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe pituitary fossa also called the Sella turcica is a depression in the sphenoid bone that houses t he pituitary gland. Directly beneath the pituitary fossa lies the sphenoid sinus a paired air-filled space within the body of the sphenoid bone. This close relationship is clinically important, especially for transsphenoidal surgery to access pituitary tumors through the nasal cavity and sphenoid sinus.\n\nAnatomy of pituitary gland (Overview):\n\nThe pituitary gland (hypophysis) is a small endocrine gland located in the Sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, at the base of the brain. It is connected to the hypothalamus above by the pituitary stalk (infundibulum).\n\nLobes:It has two lobes\n\nAnterior lobe (adenohypophysis): Produces and secretes hormones (e.g., ACTH, TSH, GH, LH, FSH, prolactin).\n\nPosterior lobe (neurohypophysis): Stores and releases hormones from the hypothalamus (ADH an d oxytocin).\n\nRelations:\n\nSuperior: Optic chiasm (important because tumors can cause visual problems)\n\nInferior: Sphenoid sinus Lateral: Cavernous sinuses (containing cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2, and VI)\n\nBlood supply:\n\nMainly from the superior and inferior hypophyseal arteries (branches of the internal carotid artery).\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 45-year-old man had a surgery that damaged the corpus callosum, a major commissural fiber in the brain. After surgery, he has difficulty coordinating actions between his left and right hand. What is the function of commissural fibers?\n\n- A. Control muscle movements\n- **B. Connect the two hemispheres of the brain** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Regulate emotions\n- D. Carry signals from the brain to the spinal cord\n- E. Process sensory information\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCommissural fibers are axonal connections that link the left and right hemispheres of the brain, allowing them to communicate and coordinate functions. The largest commissural fiber is the corpus callosum, which facilitates the exchange of motor, sensory, and cognitive information between the two sides of the brain\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 35-year-old male presents with difficulty breathing and neck pain after a car accident. On examination, there is increased use of accessory muscles of respiration, especially in the neck region. The scalene muscles are visibly contracting with each breath. Which of the following best describes the function of the scalene muscles?\n\n- A. Depression of the first and second ribs during expiration\n- B. Extension of the cervical spine\n- C. Primary muscle of quiet respiration\n- D. Adduction of the scapula\n- **E. Elevation of the upper ribs during inspiration** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe scalene muscles (anterior, middle, and posterior) are accessory muscles of respiration located in the lateral aspect of the neck. Their primary function is to assist with inspiration by elevating the first and second ribs, helping to expand the thoracic cavity when there is increased respiratory effort (e.g., in trauma, respiratory distress, or conditions like COPD).\n\nElevation of the upper ribs during inspiration: The anterior and middle scalene muscles elevate the first rib, while th e posterior scalene elevates the second rib, aiding in inspiration.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 55-year-old man with history of hypertension and ischemic heart disease is started on Ramipril 5 mg once daily. Two weeks later, he presents with a persistent dry cough and mild shortness of breath. His chest examination is clear, and his oxygen saturation is 97% on room air. He denies fever, chest pain, or sputum production. What is the most likely cause of his symptoms?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Pulmonary embolism\n- B. Community-acquired pneumonia\n- C. Anxiety-related hyperventilation\n- **D. ACE inhibitor induced bradykinin accumulation** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Progression of heart failure\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nACE inhibitors like Ramipril prevent the breakdown of bradykinin, leading to its accumulation. Bradykinin stimulates sensory nerves in the lungs, causing a persistent dry cough and occasionally mild dyspnea.\n\nMnemonic to remember ACE Inhibitors side effects: (Ramipril)\n\nCATCHH\n\nCough Angioedema Teratogenic (avoid in pregnancy)\n\nCreatinine ^ (Acute kidney injury)\n\nHyperkalemia Hypotension\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 40:** 1-E, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A, 5-C, 6-D, 7-A, 8-E, 9-E, 10-A, 11-C, 12-A, 13-B, 14-E, 15-D\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
04a7338a-4ebc-422a-9cd9-5bcf3d44a328	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-41	Quiz 41: Neuroleptic Syndrome, Laryngeal Anatomy & Portal Hypertension	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 41\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 32-year-old man with schizophrenia is brought to the emergency department with high-grade fever, generalized muscle rigidity, confusion, and sweating. His blood pressure is fluctuating, and his heart rate is elevated. He was started on haloperidol 3 days ago for agitation. Laboratory tests reveal an elevated creatine kinase (CK) level. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n- **A. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Malignant hyperthermia\n- C. Acute dystonic reaction\n- D. Serotonin syndrome\n- E. Anticholinergic toxicity\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) is a rare but serious side effect of antipsychotic drugs like haloperidol.\n\nKey features include:\n\nFever Severe "lead-pipe" muscle rigidity Autonomic instability (fluctuating BP, tachycardia, sweating)\n\nAltered mental status Elevated CK (due to muscle breakdown)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 70-year-old patient presents with a painful, dermatomal vesicular rash over the left supraclavicular fossa, suggestive of herpes zoster. Which of the following factors is the strongest indication for hospital admission?\n\n- A. Site of the rash\n- B. Age of the patient\n- **C. Involvement of trigeminal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Pain of the rash\n- E. Extent of rash\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWhile age, pain, extent, and location can raise clinical concern, the most serious indication for hospital admission in herpes zoster is cranial nerve involvement, especially trigeminal (V1) involvement, due to the risk of ocular complications, vision loss, and neurological spread.\n\nA (extent): Mild or localized rash doesn't usually need admission unless disseminated B (pain): Severe pain may need stronger outpatient analgesia but not always admission C (age): Age increases risk of complications but is not alone an admission criterion D (site): Supraclavicular region is unusual but not in itself an emergency E Trigeminal involvement, especially ophthalmic (V1), requires admission\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 37-year-old man presents with acute left flank pain. A plain abdominal X-ray (KUB) shows an opacity near the ureteropelvic junction. What is the most appropriate next investigation?\n\n- A. MRI abdomen\n- B. Urinalysis only\n- **C. Ultrasound (USG) abdomen and pelvis** (Correct Answer)\n- D. CT abdomen without contrast\n- E. Intravenous pyelogram (IVP)\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) is a common sit e for renal calculi (kidney stones). X-ray KUB candetect radiopaque stones, but further imaging is needed for confirmation and to assess complications like hydronephrosis. Ultrasound is a non-invasive, radiation-free, and widely available first-line investigation to evaluate the kidneys, ureters, a nd bladder. It can detect:\n\nHydronephrosis Renal stones (especially in the renal pelvis) Obstructive uropathy\n\nAlthough non-contrast CT (NCCT) is more sensitive and specific for detecting stones, USG is often the first recommended test, especially in initial or uncomplicated cases, and in young or pregnant patients.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 65-year-old man with a history of hypertension and mild peripheral edema is started on Bendroflumethiazide. After two weeks, his blood pressure improves, but he reports increased urination and mild leg cramps. Which of the following best describes the transporter targeted by this medication?\n\n- **A. Na/Cl symporter in the distal convoluted tubule** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the collecting duct\n- C. Na/K/2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle\n- D. . H/K ATPase in the gastric parietal cells\n- E. Na/Cl antiporter in the distal convoluted tubule\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMechanism of Action:\n\nThiazide diuretics, including Bendroflumethiazide, act on the early distal convoluted tubule of the nephron. They inhibit the Na/Cl symporter, leading to decreased reabsorption of sodium and chloride.\n\nEffects on Electrolytes:\n\nNa (Sodium): v - Loss in urine, contributes to diuresis and lowered blood pressure.\n\nCl (Chloride): v - Lost along with sodium due to symporter blockade.\n\nK (Potassium): v - Increased distal sodium delivery enhances potassium secretion via ENaC in the collecting duct.\n\nCa (Calcium): ^ - Thiazides increase calcium reabsorption, which can help reduce kidney stone risk but may cause hypercalcemia in susceptible individuals.\n\nSide Effects of Thiazide diuretics:\n\nHypokalemia Hyponatremia Hypomagnesemia Hypercalcemia Hyperglycemia Dehydration Dizziness (Often due to fluid or electrolyte imbalance.)\n\nPhotosensitivity Gout: (Due to elevated uric acid levels.)\n\nOther diuretics mechanism (High Yield):\n\nLoop Diuretics (e.g., Furosemide): Act on the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. Inhibit th e Na/K/2Cl cotransporter, causing significant sodium, potassium, and chloride loss.\n\nPotassium-Sparing Diuretics (e.g., Spironolactone, Amiloride): Act on the collecting duct. Spironolactone blocks aldosterone (which normally increases sodium reabsorption), while Amiloride blocks the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC).\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 50-year-old man is started on lansoprazole for treatment of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). Two weeks later, he presents with persistent watery diarrhea. Which of the following is the most likely explanation?\n\n- A. Antibiotic-associated colitis\n- B. Irritable bowel syndrome\n- **C. Proton pump inhibitor associated diarrhea** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Acute viral gastroenteritis\n- E. Osmotic laxative overuse\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nProton pump inhibitors (PPIs) like lansoprazole a re commonly used for acid suppression in GERD, peptic ulcers, and other acid-related disorders. One of their potential side effects is diarrhea, which can be:\n\nMild and self-limiting, due to changes in gut flora from reduced stomach acid.\n\nSerious such as Clostridium difficile infection, especially with long-term or high dose\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nDuring direct laryngoscopy, which single cartilage is typically seen as the most prominent structure?\n\n- A. Cricoid cartilage\n- B. Thyroid cartilage\n- **C. Epiglottis** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Corniculate cartilage\n- E. Arytenoid cartilage\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe epiglottis is the most visible single cartilage seen during direct laryngoscopy. It appears as a leaf-shaped flap at the base of the tongue and is crucial in protecting the airway during swallowing. It is lifted during laryngoscopy to visualize the vocal cords and the entrance to the trachea. Other cartilages like the arytenoids, corniculates, an d cricoid are also part of the laryngeal framework but are either paired or located deeper and are not typically the first or most prominent structure visualized\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 60-year-old man with a history of hepatitis C presents with hematemesis and abdominal swelling. On examination, he has spider angiomas, palmar erythema, and ascites. Upper GI endoscopy reveals esophageal varices. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of his findings?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. . Hypersecretion of bile acids causing splanchnic vasodilation\n- **B. Increased resistance to portal blood flow within the liver** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Increased lymphatic drainage from the liver\n- D. Obstruction of hepatic artery flow\n- E. . Decreased resistance in hepatic sinusoids\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn cirrhosis, the normal liver architecture is replaced by fibrous tissue and regenerative nodules. This disrupts the blood flow through the hepatic sinusoids, leading to increased intrahepatic vascular resistance. As a result, the portal vein pressure rises, causing portal hypertension. To compensate this body forms portosystemic collateral vessels (abnormal vascular channels that divert bloo d from the high-pressure portal system to lower-pressure systemic veins). These include esophageal and gastric varices, which are fragile and prone to rupture, leading to hematemesis. Additionally, increased hydrostatic pressure in the splanchnic circulation promotes the leakage of fluid into the peritoneal cavity, resulting in ascites. Compounding this, splanchnic vasodilation and hypoalbuminemia (due to impaired liver synthesis) contribute to further third-spacing of fluid. This entire process is a hallmark of intrahepatic portal hypertension and is most commonly seen in patient s with chronic liver disease such as hepatitis C or alcohol-related liver damage.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 41:** 1-A, 2-C, 3-C, 4-A, 5-C, 6-C, 7-B\n	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
72e61dcf-9b5f-4190-b38f-1aef4a4eab3e	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	breathing-cycle-description-of-pressure-and-airflow	Breathing Cycle - Description of Pressure and Airflow	article	Breathing cycle (description of\npressures and airflow)\nAt rest (before inspiration begins):\nAlveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure, because lung\npressures are expressed relative to atmospheric pressure,\nalveolar pressure is said to be zero.\nIntrapleural pressure is negative.\nAt FRC, the opposing forces of the lungs trying to collapse\nand the chest wall trying to expand create a negative\npressure in the intrapleural space between them. Intrapleural\npressure can be measured by a balloon catheter in the\nesophagus.\nLung volume is the Functional residual capacity.\nDuring inspiration:\nThe inspiratory muscles contract and cause the volume of the\nthorax to increase. As lung volume increases, alveolar pressure\ndecreases to less than atmospheric pressure (i.e. becomes\nnegative).\nThe pressure gradient between the atmosphere and the\nalveoli now causes air to flow into the lungs; airflow will\ncontinue until the pressure gradient dissipates.\nIntrapleural pressure becomes more negative.\nBecause lung volume increases during inspiration, the elastic\nrecoil strength of the lungs also increases. As a result,\nintrapleural pressure becomes even more negative than it\nwas at rest. Changes in intrapleural pressure during\ninspiration are used to measure the dynamic compliance of\nthe lungs.\nAt the peak of inspiration, lung volume is the FRC plus one\nTidal volume.\nDuring expiration:\n\nAlveolar pressure becomes greater than atmospheric\npressure. "The alveolar pressure becomes greater (i.e. becomes\npositive) because alveolar gas is compressed by the elastic\nforces of the lung.\nThus, alveolar pressure is now higher than atmospheric\npressure, the pressure gradient is reversed, and air flows out\nof the lungs.\nIntrapleural pressure returns to its resting value during a\nnormal (passive) expiration. However, during a forced\nexpiration, intrapleural pressure actually becomes\npositive.This positive intrapleural pressure compresses the\nairways and makes expiration more difficult.\nIn COPD, in which airway resistance is increased, patients\nlearn to expire slowly with "pursed lips." By creating a\nresistance at the mouth, airway pressure is increased and\nprevents the airway collapse that may occur with a forced\nexpiration.\nLung volume returns to FRC.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
74fa4e4d-b68d-418a-9cc3-3d06903612d5	60d5abf2-5add-4661-9b03-d4d02aa30e0f	muscles-of-the-lower-limb	Muscles of the Lower Limb	article	Muscles of Gluteal Region:\nMuscles of the gluteal region are divided into two groups.\n\n### 1: Superficial gluteal muscles\n\nNote: Greater trochanter of femur is site of insertion of gluteus\nMedius, minimums and piriformis.\n\n### 2: Deep gluteal muscles:\n\nNote: The piriformis muscle is an important landmark in the\ngluteal region. The superior gluteal nerve and vessels emerge\ninto the gluteal region superiorly to the piriformis. Sciatic nerve\nenters the gluteal region directly inferior to the piriformis\nmuscle.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nTrendelenburg Sign: Trendelenburg gait or waddling gait\noccurs when your hip abductor muscles are weakened. This\nmuscle group includes the gluteus Medius and minimus\ninnervated by superior gluteal nerve. The weakness of these\nmuscles causes drooping of the pelvis to the contralateral\nside while walking.\nThe Trendelenburg sign is produced when the patient is asked\nto stand without support on each leg. In a positive sign, pelvic\ndrop will occur on the unsupported leg. For example, if the\nright gluteal muscles are weak, the left side of the pelvis will\ndrop.\n\n#### Anterior compartment of thigh\n\nmuscles:\nNote: All the muscles of anterior compartment of thigh are\nflexors of thigh at hip joint, while quadriceps femoris is\nextensor of thigh at knee joint and Sartorius also flexes thigh at\nknee.\nNote:    Sartorius originates from anterior superior iliac spine\nRectus femoris originates from anterior inferior iliac spine.\n\n#### Medial Compartment of thigh\n\nmuscles:\nMedial compartment consists of five Indvidual muscles.\nAll the muscles of medial compartment of thigh causes\nadduction of thigh at hip joint except obturator externus which\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\ncauses lateral rotation of thigh at hip joint.\nGracilis along with adduction at hip joint also assist in flexion at\nknee joint.\nPosterior thigh muscles: Also known as hamstrings, consists\nof three individual muscles.\nNote: All the muscles of posterior compartment of thigh are\nextensors of thigh at hip and flexors at knee joint. While Biceps\nfemoris is also lateral rotator of thigh at hip joint and\nSemitendinosus and Semimembranosus also medial rotators of\nthigh at hip joint\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_04_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_05_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: All the muscles of the posterior thigh originate**\n> from the ischial tuberosity of the pelvis. In some cases, mostly\n> in young athletes, sudden and forceful contraction of the\n> hamstring muscles can result in an avulsion fracture of the\n> ischial tuberosity. It can also be associated with sciatic nerve\n> damage.\n\nMuscles in the anterior compartment\nof leg:\n\n#### Anterior leg consists of four muscles\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Foot Drop (Discussed in nerves of the lower**\n> limb)\n> Muscles in the Posterior compartment of leg: Consists of\n> seven muscles divided into superficial and deep groups. All are\n> innervated by tibial nerve\n> Superficial group: Mnemonic (GPS)\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_06_p4.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n#### Deep muscles in the posterior leg\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Ruptured calcaneal tendon: Tear of calcaneal tendon occurs\n> during forceful plantarflexion of the foot. Patients will be\n> unable to plantarflex the foot and affected foot will be\n> permanently dorsiflexed.\n\nMuscles in the lateral compartment\nof leg:\nConsists of two muscles, fibularis longus and brevis also\ncalled peroneus longus and peroneus brevis.\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_07_p5.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_08_p5.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nMuscles of the Sole of foot:\nMuscles of sole of foot. (4 layers) = Must remember\nhighlighted one\nFirst layer: Abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis &\nabductor digitorum minimi\nSecond layer: flexor digitorum accessorius, 4 lumbricals and\ntendons of flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum\nlongus\nThird layer: flexor hallucis brevis, flexor digitorum minimi\nbrevis & adductor hallucis\nFourth layer: Plantar and dorsal interossei muscles, Two long\ntendons of extrinsic muscles - tibialis posterior and fibularis\nlongus.\nNote: Plantar arteries and nerves lie within the first and\nsecond layer of the sole of foot.\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_09_p6.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
42c7457d-ddcd-486a-a61e-10e1e92bcc36	60d5abf2-5add-4661-9b03-d4d02aa30e0f	nerves-of-the-lower-limb	Nerves of the Lower Limb	article	Femoral nerve\nNerve roots: L2-L4\nMotor functions: Innervates the anterior thigh muscles.\nSensory functions:\nAnatomical course:\nThe femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus.\nIt is derived from the anterior rami of nerve roots L2, L3 and\nL4\nAfter arising from the lumbar plexus, the femoral nerve\ntravels inferiorly through the psoas major muscle of the\nposterior abdominal wall. It supplies branches to the iliacus\nand pectineus muscles in the abdomen prior to entering the\nthigh.\nPassing underneath the inguinal ligament to enter the\nfemoral triangle, in this triangle femoral nerve is located\nlateral to femoral vessels (Femoral artery and Vein). Useful\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Mnemonic from lateral to medial (NAVEL). Below the inguinal**\nligament femoral nerve divides into anterior and posterior\ndivisions\n\n### 1: Anterior division of the femoral nerve\n\n#### Anterior cutaneous branches\n\nBranch to sartorius\nBranch to pectineus\n\n### 2: Posterior division of the femoral nerve\n\nSaphenous nerve\nBranches to quadriceps femoris\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nNote: In posterior abdominal wall. Femoral nerve supplies\nbranches to the iliacus and pectineus muscles prior to entering\nthe thigh.\nFemoral nerve Sensory innervation of lateral and posterior\nthigh: (Important to remember)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Damage to the femoral nerve causing impaired**\n> flexion at hip and impaired extension of the leg resulting from\n> paralysis of the quadriceps femoris (Which include Rectus\n> femoris, Vastus lateralis, Vastus medialis and Vastus\n> intermedius.\n\nSensory loss: Loss of sensation over anteromedial thigh,\nanteromedial knee, medial leg and medial foot.\nObturator nerve\nNerve roots: L2-L4\nMotor functions: Innervates the medial thigh muscles.\nSensory functions: Upper medial thigh via cutaneous\nbranches\nAnatomical course: Arises from the lumbar plexus and enters\nthe thigh through obturator foramen.\nNote: The obturator nerve innervates all of the muscles of the\nmedial compartment of the thigh (except for the hamstring\npart of the adductor magnus innervated by the tibial portion\nof the sciatic nerve).\nClinical: Damage to the obturator nerve causes weakness of\nadduction, resulting in a lateral swinging of the limb during\nwalking\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over upper medial thigh\nSciatic nerve\nIs the largest nerve in the body. Derived from the\nlumbosacral plexus and receives fibers from L4-S3.\nFunction:\n\n### 1: Motor functions: Innervates the muscles of the posterior\n\nthigh (biceps femoris, semimembranosus and semitendinosus)\nand the hamstring portion of the adductor magnus (remaining\nportion of which is supplied by the obturator nerve).\nIndirectly innervates (via its terminal branches) all the\nmuscles of the leg and foot.\n\n### 2: Sensory functions: No direct sensory functions. Indirectly\n\ninnervates (via its terminal branches) the skin of the lateral leg,\nheel, and both the dorsal and plantar surfaces of the foot.\nAnatomical course: Sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis and enters\nthe gluteal region via greater sciatic foramen. It emerges\ninferiorly to the piriformis muscle and descends in an\ninferolateral direction and entering the posterior compartment\nof the thigh. In the posterior compartment of the thigh, the\nsciatic nerve lies on the adductor magnus muscle and is\ncrossed by the long head of the biceps femoris muscle. Within\nthe posterior thigh, the sciatic nerve gives rise to branches to\nthe hamstring muscles and adductor magus. When the\nsciatic nerve reaches the apex of the popliteal fossa, it\nterminates by bifurcating into the tibial and common fibular\nnerves.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Intramuscular injections in the gluteal region: The anatomical\n> course of the sciatic nerve must be considered when\n> administering intramuscular injections into the gluteal region.\n\nThe region can be divided into quadrants using 2 lines.\n\nOne line descends vertically from the highest point on the\niliac crest.\nOther line passes through vertical line halfway between the\nhighest point.\nNote: Sciatic nerve lies in the lower medial quadrant. To avoid\ninjury of sciatic nerve IM injections in the gluteal region only\nbe given in upper lateral quadrant.\nTibial nerve\nBranch of sciatic nerve. Arises at apex of popliteal fossa\nreceiving nerve fibers from L4-S3.\nFunctions:\nMotor: Innervates all the muscles in posterior compartment of\nthe leg and the majority of the intrinsic foot muscles.\nSensory: In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve gives off\ncutaneous branches. These combine with branches from the\ncommon fibular nerve to form the sural nerve. This sensory\nnerve innervates the skin of the posterolateral side of the leg\nand the lateral side of the foot and heel.\nSensory branches of tibial nerve:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Damage to the tibial nerve causes loss of**\n> plantar flexion of the foot and impaired inversion. This\n> presents with difficulty in getting the heel off the ground and a\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\nshuffling gait, resulting in characteristic clawing of the toes and\nsensory loss on the sole of the foot, affecting posture and\nlocomotion.\nCommon fibular (Peroneal\nnerve)\nArises as the smaller terminal portion of the sciatic nerve at the\napex of the popliteal fossa. The common fibular nerve\ncontinues by wrapping around the neck of the fibula and\npassing between the attachments of the fibularis longus\nmuscle to enter the lateral compartment of the leg where it\ndivides into its terminal branches, the superficial and deep\nfibular nerves.\nNote: It is very important to remember direct innervation of\ncommon fibular nerve.\nDeep fibular nerve root: L4-L5\nSuperficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n\n### 1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the common\n\nfibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a result of fracture of the\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_04_p5.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nhead or neck of the fibula because it passes behind the head of\nthe fibula and then winds laterally around the neck. The nerve\ndamage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion) and loss of\nsensation on the dorsum of the foot and lateral aspect of the\nleg and causes paralysis of all muscles in the anterior and\nlateral compartments of the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors\nmuscles of the foot).\n\n### 2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by fracture of the\n\nfibula. As the muscles that the superficial fibular nerve\ninnervates are evertors, injury to the nerve may result in a loss\nof eversion and loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral\nleg and dorsum of foot\n\n### 3: Deep fibular nerve:\n\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in foot drop.\nIn foot drop foot will appear permanently plantarflexed, foot\ndrop with high-stepping gait, weakness of foot inversion.\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace between 1st and\n2nd toes.\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or common\nperoneal nerve or also due to deep fibular nerve injury.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
1d1d1cbc-285a-4901-8c70-f65b5a2107fc	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-ix-glassopharyngeal-nerve	Cranial Nerve Ix_ Glassopharyngeal Nerve	article	Glossopharyngeal Nerve (CN IX):\noriginates in the medulla oblongata of the brain, moves\nlaterally in the posterior cranial fossa, and leaves the skull\nthrough jugular foramen.\nNote: Facial nerve in parotid gland splits into five terminal\nbranches but parotid gland is innervated by\nglossopharyngeal nerve\n\n![Cranial Nerve Ix_ Glassopharyngeal Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-ix-glassopharyngeal-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Ix_ Glassopharyngeal Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8ccc6672-4a1a-4633-b597-87c96ab6c219	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	gas-exchange	Gas Exchange	article	## Gas Exchange\n\nDalton law of partial pressures\nDaltons law of partial pressure can be expressed by the\nfollowing equation:\nPartial pressure = Total pressure x Fractional gas\nconcentration\nIn dry inspired air, the partial pressure of O2 can be\ncalculated as follows. Assume that total pressure is\natmospheric (760) and the fractional concentration of O2 is\n0.21.\nPo2 760 mm Hg 0.21 =160 mm Hg\nIn humidified tracheal air at 37°C, the calculation is modified\nto correct for the partial pressure of H ₂ O, which is 47 mm Hg.\nP.total=760 mm Hg-47 mm Hg =713 mm Hg\nPo2= 713 mm Hg x 0.21 =150 mm Hg\nPartial pressure of 02 and CO2 in\ninspired air, alveolar air and blood\nApproximately 2% of the systemic cardiac output bypasses\nthe pulmonary circulation ("physiologic shunt"). The resulting\nadmixture of mixed venous blood with oxygen arterial blood\nmakes the Po2 of arterial blood slightly lower than that of\nalveolar air.\nDissolved gases\nHenry law: states that the amount of dissolved gas in a liquid\n(such as blood) is proportional to its partial pressure above the\nliquid.\nSo, breathing gas at higher-than-normal pressures will cause\ntheir bodies to absorb more gases than are absorbed at lower pressures. Absorbed gases can cause decompression\nsickness as well as toxic effects.\nHenry law is applicable in Scuva diving or deep-sea diver.\nThe following calculation uses O2 in arterial blood as an\nexample: Dissolved [O2] =Po2 × Solubility of O2 in blood 100\nmm Hg x 0.003 mL  mL/mm Hg =0.3 mL  mL\nblood\nwhere:\n[02] =02concentration in blood\nPo2partial pressure of O2 in blood\n0.003 mL O2/100 mL/mm Hg= solubility of O2 in blood\nPartial pressure of O2 and Co2 (mm Hg)\n\n![Gas Exchange - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/gas-exchange/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Gas Exchange — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
6a423b3f-4e57-4822-b6a1-8cb0f44ce7d6	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	lung-elastance-and-compliance	Lung Elastance and Compliance	article	Lung elastance and Lung\nCompliance:\nLung elastance: Tendency of lungs to collapse.\nCompliance: The ease with which the lungs can expand\nknown as compliance\nCompliance increases in Obstructive lung disease.\nCompliance decreases in Restrictive Lung disease.\nChanges in lung compliance\nIn a patient with emphysema, lung compliance is increased,\nelastic recoil is decreased and the tendency of the lungs to\ncollapse is decreased. Therefore, at the original FRC the\ntendency of the lungs to collapse is less than the tendency of\nthe chest wall to expand. The lung-chest wall system will\nseek a new, higher FRC so that the two opposing forces can\nbe balanced again; the patient's chest becomes barrel-\nshaped, reflecting this higher volume.\nIn a patient with fibrosis, lung compliance is decreased,\nelastic recoil is increased. and the tendency of the lungs to\ncollapse is increased, Therefore, at the original FRC the\ntendency of the lungs to collapse is greater than the\ntendency of the chest wall to expand. The lung-chest wall\nsystem will seek a new, lower FRC so that the two opposing\nforces can be balanced again.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
3cb05f35-474d-46e3-8243-59ddb0663bf5	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	lung-receptors	Lung Receptors	article	Lung receptors:\nIrritant Receptors in the Airways: The epithelium of the\ntrachea, bronchi, and bronchioles is supplied with sensory\nnerve endings called pulmonary irritant receptors that are\nstimulated by many incidents. These receptors initiate\ncoughing and sneezing and cause bronchial constriction in\npeople with such diseases as asthma and emphysema. They\nare rapidly adapting.\nJ-receptors: respond to events such as pulmonary edema,\npulmonary emboli pneumonia, and barotrauma, which cause a\ndecrease in oxygenation and thus lead to an increase in\nventilation/respiration.\nJ receptors are activated in pulmonary edema in a patient\nwith congestive heart failure and causes reflex increase in\nbreathing rate (Rapid shallow breathing) also thought to be\ninvolve in sensation of dyspnea\nLung stretch receptors (Hering-Breuer reflex): Self-regulatory\nnegative feedback reflex.\nLie within the smooth muscle of large and small airways.\nAfferent is carried via vagus nerve.\nStimulus for receptor:\nDistension of the lungs, when TV is more than 1 L\nHering-Breuer inflation reflex: inflation of the lungs tended to\ninhibit further inspiratory muscle activity (dorsal neuron).\nIncrease in duration of expiration.\nHering-Breuer Deflation reflex: Decrease in duration of\nexpiration produced by marked deflation of lung.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
7b022059-0b78-498d-8b02-2d3edf949c4f	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-hypertensive-emergencies	Hypertensive Emergencies	quiz	# Hypertensive Emergencies\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Hypertensive Emergencies\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Hypertensive Emergencies**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	90	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
1dd12800-560c-496d-9732-72078f82e207	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-hypertension-in-pregnancy-preeclampsia	Hypertension in Pregnancy & Preeclampsia	quiz	# Hypertension in Pregnancy & Preeclampsia\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Hypertension in Pregnancy & Preeclampsia\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Hypertension in Pregnancy & Preeclampsia**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	100	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
c099f610-dbfb-4cbe-bb08-2e7c2e85df86	93dd26aa-6675-4d16-bfda-6ade22fb676d	male-and-female-reproductive-system	Male and Female Reproductive System	article	Male Genital organs:\nTestis and Epididymis:\nPaired structure located within the scrotum\nTestis and Epididymis are Suspended in the scrotum by the\nspermatic cord.\nTestes are site of sperm production\nEpididymis stores the sperm\nAnatomic structure:\nTestis is covered by tunica albuginea which lies beneath the\nvisceral layer of the tunica veginalis\nEach testis is composed of seminiferous tubules which\nproduce spermatozoa and interstitial cells (of Leydig)\nsecrete testosterone\nInnervation of testis and epididymis: testicular plexus - a\nnetwork of nerves derived from the renal and aortic plexus\nLymphatic drainage:(Important to remember)\nTestes lumbar (para-aortic) nodes in the abdomen\nscrotum drains to the superficial inguinal nodes.\nBlood supply: Paired testicular arteries arise from abdominal\naorta\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Testicular torsion is the twisting of a testis and the spermatic\n> cord, obstructing blood vessels to the testis and causing\n> sudden urgent pain and swelling of the scrotum.\n\nCaused by trauma or spasm of cremasteric muscle.\nDuctus deferens:\nIs continuous with the tail of the epididymis and serves to\ntransport sperm to the ejaculatory ducts prior to ejaculation.\n\nIs a thick-walled tube that ascends within the spermatic cord\nto enter the pelvis at the deep inguinal ring lateral to the\ninferior epigastric artery.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Vasectomy is the surgical excision of a portion**\n> of the vas deferens (ductus deferens) through the scrotum. It\n> stops the passage of spermatozoa.\n\nSpermatic cord and contents:\nMust remember important spermatic cord covering and\ncontents with rule of "3"\nThree arteries, three nerves and three other structures\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Penis:\n> The penis consists of a root (two crura and the bulb of the\n> penis) and the body, which is formed by the single corpus\n> spongiosum which contains the spongy urethra and paired\n> corpora cavernosa.  Its head is called the glans penis which is\n> the terminal part of the corpus spongiosum.\n\nInnervation: The penis is supplied by S2-S4 spinal cord\nsegments and spinal ganglia.\nSensory and sympathetic innervation to the skin and glans\npenis is supplied by the dorsal nerve of the penis, a branch of\n\n![Male and Female Reproductive System - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/male-and-female-reproductive-system/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Male and Female Reproductive System — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nthe pudendal nerve.\nParasympathetic innervation is carried by cavernous nerves\nfrom the peri-prostatic nerve plexus and is responsible for\nthe vascular changes which cause erection.\nBlood supply: Dorsal arteries of the penis, Deep arteries of the\npenis and Bulbourethral artery branches of the internal\npudendal artery.\nLymphatic drainage: (Important to remember)\nPenile Skin and prepuce: Drain into Superficial inguinal\nnodes.\nGlans penis and penile urethra drain into deep inguinal\nnodes.\nProstate gland:\nlargest accessory structure of male reproductive system lies\nimmediately inferior to the bladder and the internal urethral\nsphincter\n\n#### Anterior to the rectum\n\nsuperior to the external urethral sphincter\nFunctional anatomy: Secretes a fluid that produces the\ncharacteristic odor of semen. This fluid, the secretion from the\nseminal vesicles and the bulbourethral glands, and the\nspermatozoa constitute the semen or seminal fluid.\nProstate has four zones:\nAnterior zone: which lies in front of the urethra.\nTransitional zone: encompasses the urethra proximal to the\nejaculatory ducts and is prone to benign hypertrophy\nobstructing the internal urethral orifice\nCentral zone: encompasses the ejaculatory ducts up to the\nurethra.\nPeripheral zone: situated posteroinferior and lateral to the\ntransitional and central zones, forming the main mass of the\ngland and is thus the most prone to carcinomatous\ntransformation.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Hypertrophy of the prostate is a benign**\n> enlargement of the prostate that affects older men and occurs most often in the transitional zone, obstructing the internal\nurethral orifice and thus leading to nocturia (excessive\nurination at night), dysuria (difficulty or pain in urination), and\nurgency (sudden desire to urinate).\nProstate cancer is a slow-growing cancer that occurs\nparticularly in the peripheral zone.\nBlood supply: Prostatic arteries derived from internal iliac\narteries.\nInnervation : The prostate receives sympathetic,\nparasympathetic and sensory innervation from the inferior\nhypogastric plexus\nFemale genital organs:\nOvary:\nFemale gonads: Sites of egg production\nOvaries Lie on the posterior aspect of the broad ligament on\nthe side wall of the pelvis minor and are bounded by the\nexternal and internal iliac vessels.\nNot covered by the peritoneum, and thus, the ovum or\noocyte is expelled into the peritoneal cavity and then into the\nuterine tube.\nAre not enclosed in the broad ligament, but their anterior\nsurface is attached to the posterior layer of the broad\nligament by the mesovarium\nLigaments of the ovary:\nOvarian ligament (Round ligament of ovary) Attaches the\novaries to the lateral wall of uterus\nSuspensory ligament of ovary: fold of peritoneum extending\nfrom the mesovarium to the pelvic wall. Contains neurovascular\nstructures\nBlood supply: Are supplied primarily by the ovarian arteries,\nwhich are contained in the suspensory ligament and\nanastomose with branches of the uterine artery.\n\nVenous drainage:\nAre drained by the ovarian veins; the right ovarian vein joins the\ninferior vena cava, and the left ovarian vein joins the left renal\nvein\nInnervation: Autonomic: via the ovarian plexus\nVisceral afferents: accompany the sympathetic fibers to T10 -\nT12 level of the spinal cord sensory ganglia\nPain is thus referred to the periumbilical region, suprapubic\nregion and groin.\nUterine tube:\nThe uterine tube extends from the uterus to the ovary and\nconsists of the isthmus, ampulla, and infundibulum. The\nfimbriated distal end creates currents, drawing an ovulated\noocyte into the uterine tube.\nNote: Ectopic pregnancy most commonly occurs in the\nampulla (70% of cases).\nUterus:\n\n![Male and Female Reproductive System - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/male-and-female-reproductive-system/figure_02_p5.png)\n*Male and Female Reproductive System — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nContains a fundus, body, isthmus and cervix and is supported\nby ligaments.\nRelations:\nAnteriorly: vesicouterine pouch and the urinary bladder\nPosteriorly: the rectouterine pouch and the anterior surface\nof rectum\nLaterally: the broad ligament\nSuspensory ligaments:\nThe uterosacral ligament\nThe round ligaments\nThe cardinal ligaments\nArterial supply:\nUterine arteries: branch of internal iliac artery\nOvarian arteries: branch of the abdominal aorta at the level of\nL1.\nVenous drainage:\nUterine veins: they are formed from the plexus and drain into\ninternal iliac veins\nInnervation: Autonomic\nHypogastric plexus (sympathetic)\nS3-S4 (parasympathetic)\nVisceral afferents: T11-T12\nVagina:\nRelations:\nAnteriorly: urethral orifice\nPosteriorly: The rectum\nArterial supply: Uterine arteries supply the superior\nportion: branch of the internal iliac artery\nVaginal artery and internal pudendal arteries supply the middle\nand inferior parts: branches of the internal iliac artery.\n\nVenous drainage: Uterovaginal plexus: drains into internal iliac\nveins\nInnervation\nAutonomic: inferior hypogastric plexus, pelvic splanchnic,\npudendal nerve (all provide parasympathetic Innervation\nfrom S2-S4)\nVisceral afferents: accompany parasympathetic fibers of\ninferior hypogastric and pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2-S4.\nSomatic afferents: accompany pudendal nerve to S2-S4.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Digital vaginal examination:\n> Digital vaginal examination allows palpation of the:\n> urethra and bladder through the anterior fornix of the vagina\n> the perineal body, rectum, coccyx and sacrum through the\n> posterior fornix\n> ovaries, uterine tubes, ureters and ischial spines through the\n> lateral fornices	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
c0965ae3-0f21-4284-b031-c78da68ece67	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-viii-vestibulocochlear-nerve	Cranial Nerve Viii_ Vestibulocochlear Nerve	article	Vestibulocochlear Nerve (CN VIII):\nComprised of two vestibular and cochlear portions, emerges\nfrom the brainstem at the cerebellopontine angle, enters\ninternal acoustic meatus of temporal bone.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Viii_ Vestibulocochlear Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-viii-vestibulocochlear-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Viii_ Vestibulocochlear Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
529b3985-cfd2-4e07-b14d-5f33d899546c	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-vii-facial-nerve	Cranial Nerve Vii_ Facial Nerve	article	Facial nerve:\nThe facial nerve exits the cranial cavity through the internal\nacoustic meatus and enters the facial canal of the temporal\nbone. The nerve then exits the skull through the Stylomastoid\nforamen (lies between the styloid and mastoid processes of the\ntemporal bone) to enter the parotid gland, ultimately\nbranching into five terminal branches to supply the face.\nCause of injury: Bell's palsy, Ramsay-Hunt syndrome, Guillain-\nBarre syndrome, mumps, parotid tumor, and injury to the\nmiddle ear.\nClinical effects of injury:  impaired sense of taste to anterior\ntwo-thirds of tongue, loss of efferent corneal reflex, impaired\nlacrimal fluid production, hyperacusis, and impaired salivation\nFacial nerve branches in the temporal bone:\nThe nerve to the stapedius: Innervate stapedius muscle.\nThe greater petrosal nerve: parasympathetic innervation to\nthe lacrimal gland and the mucous glands of the oral cavity,\nnose and pharynx\nThe chorda tympani: supply taste to the anterior two-thirds\nof the tongue\nFacial nerve branches between the stylomastoid foramen\nand parotid gland:3 motor branches\nPosterior auricular nerve innervates the intrinsic and\nextrinsic muscles of the outer ear.\nNerve to the posterior belly of the digastric muscle:\nInnervates the posterior belly of the digastric muscle one of\nthe suprahyoid muscles of neck responsible for raising the\nhyoid bone.\nNerve to the stylohyoid muscle: Innervates the stylohyoid\nmuscle, one of the suprahyoid muscles of neck responsible\nfor raising the hyoid bone.\n\nNote: Greater auricular nerve cutaneous branch of the cervical\nplexus provides sensory innervation to ear lobule, skin over the\nparotid gland and mastoid process of the temporal bone. Pain\nof parotitis and during ear piercing is due to innervation of\ngreater auricular nerve.\nFacial nerve terminal branches in parotid gland:\nThe cervical branch: innervating the platysma muscle.\nThe temporal branch: innervating muscles in the temple,\nforehead muscles (frontalis) and supraorbital areas (upper\nhalf of orbicularis oculi muscle)\nThe zygomatic branch: innervating muscles in the\ninfraorbital area (Lower half of orbicularis oculi muscle), the\nlateral nasal area and the upper lip\nThe buccal branch: innervating muscles in the cheek and\nupper lip\nThe marginal mandibular branch: innervating muscles of\nthe lower lip and chin\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> The chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve supplies taste\n> sensation to the anterior  of the tongue.\n\nIn the anterior , general sensation is supplied by the\ntrigeminal nerve (CNV). Specifically, the lingual nerve, a\nbranch of the mandibular nerve (CN V3).\nThe posterior  of the tongue both touch and taste are\ncarried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CNIX)	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d5b25994-e5aa-41b1-b2d1-09bc386a671a	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	lung-volumes-and-capacities	Lung Volumes and Capacities	article	Lung volume and capacities\nTidal Volume (TV): The volume of air that is inhaled and then\nexhaled with each normal breath.\nNormal tidal volume is 500ml or 7ml/kg.\nInspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV): The maximum volume of air\nthat can be inhaled following and above a normal tidal\ninspiration.\nNormal Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is 3300ml.\nExpiratory Reserve Volume (ERV): The maximum volume of air\nthat can be exhaled from the resting expiration of tidal\nvolume.\nNormal Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is 1700ml.\nResidual Volume (RV): The volume of air that remains in the\nlung following a maximum expiration.\nResidual Volume cannot be measured using spirometry.\nNormal residual volume is 1800ml.\nCapacities\nInspiratory Capacity (IC): is the maximum volume of air that\ncan be inhaled at the end of resting expiration.\nInspiratory capacity is sum of IC= TV+ IRV\nFunctional Residual Capacity (FRC): the volume of air\nremaining in the lungs at the resting end-expiratory level or\nVolume remaining in the lungs after tidal volume is expired in\nair.\nFRC is sum of: FRC= ERV+ RV\nFRC include Residual volume so FRC cannot be measured\nusing spirometry.\nNormal FRC is 3500ml.\nFactors affecting FRC.\n\nFactors increasing FRC: Emphysema, Air trapping in asthma,\nAgeing (due to loss of elastic properties) and Increasing\nheight of patient.\nFactors decreasing FRC: Restrictive ventilatory defects e.g.,\npulmonary fibrosis, Posture - lying supine, Increased intra-\nabdominal pressure (e.g., obesity, pregnancy, ascites) and\nReduced muscle tone of diaphragm e.g., muscle relaxants in\nanesthesia, neuromuscular disease.\nVital Capacity (VC) is the maximum volume of air that may be exhaled\nfollowing a maximum inspiration OR inhaled following a maximum\nexpiration.\n\n## Vc= Irv+Tv+ Erv Or Vc= Ic+Erv\n\nNormal VC in young adult (70kg) is 5500ml.\nTotal Lung Capacity (TLC) is the volume of air contained within\nthe lungs following a maximum inspiration.\nTLC include Residual volume so it cannot be measured\nusing spirometry.\nTLC= IRV + TV+ ERV + RV OR TLC= IC + FRC Or TLC = VC+ RV\nNormal TLC in young adult (70kg) is 7300ml.\nForced expiratory volume (FEV1)\nFEV1 is the volume of air that can be expired in the first\nsecond of a forced maximal expiration.\nFEV1 is normally 80% of the forced vital capacity, which is\nexpressed as\n\n## Fev1/ Fvc = 0.8\n\nIn obstructive lung disease such as asthma and chronic\nobstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), both FEV1 and FVC\nare reduced, but FEV1 is reduced more than FVC. FEV1/FVC\nratio is less than 0.7.\nIn restrictive lung disease such as fibrosis, both FEV1 and\nFVC are reduced, but FEV1 is reduced less than FVC is thus,\nFev1 /FVC increased.\nLung volume measurements and\nPeak Expiratory flow rate:\nLung volume can be measured with Spirometry.\nResidual volume cannot be measured using spirometry.\nResidual volume and thus (FRC and TLC) can be measured\nusing helium dilution.\nPeak Expiratory flow rate:\n\nThe easiest and quickest measurement is the peak expiratory\nflow rate (PEFR)\nAirway resistance and lung compliance can be assessed\nindirectly by measuring the forced expiratory flows and\nvolumes.\nLung volumes can be measured with spirometry.\nPEFR is reduced if airway resistance is increased in\nobstructive disease and is commonly used to monitor\nasthma.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d305472c-f331-492c-b3f1-c3c5a08d64ab	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb	Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB)	quiz	# Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB)\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB)**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	110	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
5e2c87aa-cd2e-409c-a43d-868ee2bfa003	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block	Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block	quiz	# Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	120	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
5fcfec5a-6faa-4ea2-99dd-25c25b8abe4f	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-long-qt-syndrome-torsades	Long QT Syndrome & Torsades	quiz	# Long QT Syndrome & Torsades\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Long QT Syndrome & Torsades\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Long QT Syndrome & Torsades**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	130	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
fe01c1e0-944b-4b51-b8d4-8a1153c77c86	93dd26aa-6675-4d16-bfda-6ade22fb676d	areas-of-abdomen	Areas of Abdomen	article	Areas of the abdomen\nInguinal canal: pathway by which structures can pass from\nthe abdominal wall to the external genitalia.\nInguinal canal boundaries\nInguinal ring: Two openings to the inguinal canal are\nknown as rings\nNote:\nMid-inguinal point: Halfway between the pubic symphysis\nand the anterior superior iliac spine. The femoral pulse can\nbe palpated here.\nMidpoint of the inguinal ligament: Halfway between the\npubic tubercle and the anterior superior iliac spine. The\nopening to the inguinal canal is located just above this point.\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield clinical**\n> Direct inguinal hernia lies medial to inferior epigastric vessels\n> while indirect inguinal hernia lies lateral to inferior epigastric\n> vessels. (RECALL)\n> Most common type of inguinal hernia=indirect inguinal\n> hernia (Recall)\n> Most common side of inguinal hernia=Right side (RECALL)\n> The floor of the inguinal canal is the inguinal ligament,\n> otherwise known as the Poupart ligament, which is formed\n> by the external oblique aponeurosis (RECALL)\n> Type of hernia that enters the deep inguinal ring=indirect\n> inguinal hernia (RECALL)\n> Type of hernia that protrudes through Hesselbach's\n> triangle=Direct inguinal hernia (RECALL).\n\nHesselbach triangle - Region in anterior abdominal wall\nlocated within the inferomedial aspect of the abdominal wall\nBorders:\nMedial: Lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle.\nLateral: inferior epigastric vessels.\nInferior: inguinal ligament\nClinical significance:  Direct inguinal hernia protrudes\nthrough Hesselbach's triangle.\nThe Peritoneum\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nContinuous double-layered membrane which lines the\nabdominal cavity and covers the abdominal viscera.\nConsists of parietal and visceral peritoneum\nParietal peritoneum: Lines the abdominal and pelvic walls\nand the inferior surface of the diaphragm.\nParietal peritoneum Is innervated by somatic nerves, such as\nthe phrenic, lower intercostal, subcostal, iliohypogastric, and\nilioinguinal nerves.\nvisceral peritoneum: Covers the viscera, is innervated by\nvisceral nerves and is insensitive to pain\nPeritoneal cavity: This is a potential space between the\nparietal and visceral layers of peritoneum and contains fluid\nthat lubricates the surface of the peritoneum and facilitates\nfree movements\nAbdominal viscera are either suspended in the peritoneal\ncavity by folds of peritoneum called mesenteries and are\ntermed as intraperitoneal viscera\nVisceras outside the peritoneal cavity bound to the posterior\nabdominal wall termed as retroperitoneal viscera.\nRetroperitoneal organs are only covered in peritoneum on\ntheir anterior surface.\nSADPUCKER useful mnemonic to remember\nretroperitoneal organs.\nS: Suprarenal (adrenal) gland\nA: aorta/IVC\nD: duodenum (Second and third part)\nP: pancreas (except tail)\nU: ureters\nC: colon (ascending and descending)\nK: kidneys\nE: (o)esophagus\nR: rectum\nGreater and lesser sac\nThe peritoneal cavity is divided into greater and lesser sacs\n\nNote:\nSupracolic compartment: Contains stomach, liver and\nspleen, lies under cover of costal limits of thoracic cage.\nInfracolic compartment: Divided into left and right parts by\noblique attachment of mesentery, contains small intestine\nand ascending/descending colon, continuous with pelvic\ncavity\nPeritoneal reflections: Support the viscera and provide\npathway for associated neurovascular structure.\nOmentum: Peritoneal fold extending from the stomach to\nadjacent neurovascular structures divided into the greater and\nlesser omentum\nMesenteries: Peritoneal fold that attaches Visceras to the\nposterior abdominal wall\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_04_p4.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_05_p4.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_06_p5.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d7a2fa38-2f7f-4b13-b3fa-cda798a8c2c2	ab812750-760a-4eae-8a6c-e5d4b3f1520b	spinal-cord-syndrome	Spinal Cord Syndrome	article	Spinal cord syndrome:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n\n![Spinal Cord Syndrome - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/spinal-cord-syndrome/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Spinal Cord Syndrome — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Spinal Cord Syndrome - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/spinal-cord-syndrome/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Spinal Cord Syndrome — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
40b076ed-5062-4bdc-87d6-48d364f7556b	8068b12b-317b-4499-8871-45eb82ce7703	vessel-fluid-dynamics	Vessel Fluid Dynamics	article	Vessel fluid dynamics\nFlow rate(Q) = vA (where v is flow velocity and A cross sectional\nvector Area\nTube flow:\nFlow is directly proportional to radius 4 (r4)\nTubing diameter: An important and frequently cited\nrelationship is that of the tubing's radius. Doubling the radius\nof a catheter increases the flow rate by 16-fold (r4). The larger\nthe IV catheter the greater the flow\nThe larger the flow capacity (cv) the larger the flow\nIn Coronary circulation if flow capacity(cv) is reduced to 50%\nor half flow will be decreased, or radius decreased to half flow\nwill decrease by 16 times.\nFluids with higher viscosity also have a slower rate of flow.\nPlasma has a similar viscosity to water, but blood contains\ncells which effectively increase the viscosity by three- to four-\nfold. Changes in cell number e.g., polycythemia, therefore,\naffect blood flow.\nTurbulent vs Laminar Flow\nTurbulent blood flow is multidirectional and travels at\ndifferent velocities leading to increased resistance and\nadditional shear stress on the vessel wall.\nLaminar flow: Frictional forces at the sides of a vessel cause a\ndrag force on the fluid touching them, creating a velocity\ngradient where the flow is greatest at the centre.\nWall Tention:\nPressure across the wall of a flexible tube (transmural\npressure) tends to extend it and increases wall tension.\nThis can be described by Laplace's Law: Pt = (Tw)/r\nwhere,\nPt = Transmural Pressure\nT = Wall Tension w = Wall Thickness\nr = Radius\nLaplace's Law also means that a large, dilated heart (e.g., heart\nfailure) has to develop more wall tension (contractile force) in\norder to obtain the same ventricular pressure, making it less\nefficient.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8db2dc3a-87b0-4920-9eb7-3081ca8f7133	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	oxygen-haemoglobin-dissociation-curve	Oxygen Haemoglobin Dissociation Curve	article	Oxygen-Haemoglobin\nDissociation Curve\nThe Haemoglobin oxygen Dissociation Curve is a plot of\npercent saturation of haemoglobin as a function of po2.\nChanges in the hemoglobin-02\ndissociation curve\n\n### 1. Shifts to the right:\n\noccur when the affinity of hemoglobin for O2 is decreased.\nThe P50 is increased, and unloading of O2 from arterial blood\nto the tissues is facilitated.\nCauses of Right ward shift\nDecreased affinity for oxygen (More willing to give up oxygen)\nDecreased pH (Recall)\nIncreased pCO2\nIncreased H+\nIncreased Temperature\nIncreased 2-3DPG.\nPregnancy (oxygen to the fetus)\n\n### 2. Shifts to the left:\n\noccur when the affinity of hemoglobin for 02 is increased.\nThe P50 is decreased, and unloading of O2 from arterial\nblood into the tissues is more difficult.\nCauses of left ward shift\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up oxygen)\nIncreased pH\nDecreased pCO2.\nDecreased temperature.\nDecreased H+\nDecreased 2-3DPG.\nCO poisoning\n\nMethemoglobinemia\nFetal hemoglobin\nNote:\nFetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult\nhemoglobin. Maternal and Fetal blood never mix during\npregnancy, but they come close to each other in the\nplacenta.  Oxygen diffuses easily to fetal hemoglobin here.\nThe reason fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for 02. It is\ncomposed of two alpha and two gamma subunits, while\nadult hemoglobin is composed of two alpha and two beta\nsubunits.\n2,3DPG present in RBCs enhances the ability of RBCs to\nrelease o2 near tissue and 2,3DPG only binds with beta\nglobulin chains, fetal hemoglobin (HbF) lack of beta\nglobulin chains that's why fetal hemoglobin has higher\naffinity for O2	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
1c20380a-9d13-449e-8d26-468478113dbb	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-acute-myocarditis	Acute Myocarditis	quiz	# Acute Myocarditis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Acute Myocarditis\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Acute Myocarditis**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	140	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
1c0fc0e9-b686-4618-9603-f8dcf600ab42	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	topic-non-invasive-ventilation-niv-guidelines	Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines	quiz	# Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	150	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
