7e3dd87e-5e47-42ff-8722-d24f335fc69e	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support	Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support	article	Basic life support and Advanced life\nsupport algorithms\n- Key difference b/w Paeds &\nadult ALS\n\n![Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n- Identify and treat reversible causes\nHypoxia\nHypovolemia (massive blood/fluid loss).\nHyperkalemia (renal patient).\nHypothermia (Pt may require prolong CPR).\nIntubated Pt (tracheal tube may lie below the cord.\nThrombosis (massive PE if that's the cause of cardiac arrest\nthen consider 50mg alteplase).\nTension pneumothorax following central line insertion.\nCardiac tamponade should always consider in penetration\ninjury.\nAfter ROSC\nUse an ABCDE approach.\nAim for SpO2 of 94-98% and normal PaCO2\n12-lead ECG\nIdentify and treat the cause.\nTargeted temperature management\n\n![Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
5fe17356-be7e-4c84-81e7-f55fce6e5e79	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-34	Quiz 34: Resuscitation Protocols, Airway Interventions & Cellular Biology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 34\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 26-year-old man presents to the ED with a cut wound on his cubital fossa. Which muscles action will be preserved?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Imaginary line between two epicondyles |\n> | Medial | Pronator teres |\n> | Lateral | Brachioradialis |\n> | Contents | From Medial to Lateral |\n> |  | Median nerve |\n> |  | Brachial artery |\n> |  | * Tendon of biceps |\n> |  |  Radial artery and nerve |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | | | Ulnar nerve |\n> |  | Median nerve |\n> |  | Brachial artery |\n> | \\ 4 a | Biceps tendon |\n> | a | Superficial branch |\n> | | | of radial nerve |\n> | | | Ulnar artery |\n> | / 7 | Radial artery |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Muscle | | |\n> |  | Ligame |\n> | Cubital " |  |\n> | tunnel |  |\n\n- A. Forearm pronation\n- B. Flexion of thumb\n- C. Flexion of metacarpophalangeal joints\n- D. Opposition of thumb\n- **E. Flexion of small and ring fingers** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDeep laceration or cut wound can damage the contents of cubital fossa. Ulnar nerve will be preserved and its most likely injured in cubital tunnel.\n\nCubital fossa and its contents:\n\nNote: (Clinical pearls)\n\nSupracondylar fracture is the most common distal humerus fracture in children. In Supracondylarfracture anterior interosseous nerve (branch of t he median nerve), ulnar nerve or radial nerve ca n be damaged. The anterior interosseous nerve can be tested by asking the patient to make an 'OK' sign, testing for weakness of flexor pollicis longus. (Recall)\n\nUlnar nerve descends behind medial epicondyle in a cubital tunnel where it is most commonly palpated and damaged.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nWhich of the following is most important in eliciting a hyperventilatory response to changes in acidotic PH in CSF?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  Carotid body and aortic body |  |\n> |  | Hypoxia, Hypercapnia and Acidosis |\n> |  | 1X (sinus nerve of Hering) and X (vagus) |\n> |  | Medulla |\n> |  | Sympathetic and parasympathetic |\n> |  | Increases Ventilation (main) and increases BP |\n> |  | also |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Central chemoreceptors (Central for | Peripheral chemoreceptors (both CO2 |  |  |\n> | CO2) only |  | and O2) |\n> | Respond to increase in PCO2 and decrease |  |  |\n> | in PH | Respond to increase in CO2 and H+ (Acidosis) and |  |\n> | Hypoxia can not stimulate central | Decrease in | in arterial blood |\n> | chemoreceptors |  |  |\n> | Keep PCO2 of CSF within normal limits |  |  |\n> | * CO2 is lipid soluble readily crosses blood |  |  |\n> | brain barrier, Increased PCO2 in CSF is | * Primary stimulus for peripheral chemoreceptors is |  |\n> | rapidly sensed by central chemoreceptors | hypoxia while increased PCO2 is the most |  |\n> | and stimulate hyperventilation which washes | sensitive stimulus |  |\n> | out CO2 and ultimately decrease in CSF |  |  |\n> | PCO2 |  |  |\n\n- A. Stretch receptors\n- B. Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid body\n- C. Peripheral chemoreceptors in the aortic arch\n- D. Apneustic centre\n- **E. Central chemoreceptor** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCentral chemoreceptor in medulla is responsible f or about 80% of the ventilatory response to changes in Pco2 (Increased Pco2) and decrease in PH (Acidosis).\n\nPeripheral chemoreceptors in carotid body/ Aortic arch respond to decrease in 02 and increase in arterial Pco2 and H+.\n\nControl of ventilation is mediated via central and Peripheral chemoreceptors.\n\nChemoreceptor overview: (High Yield)\n\nCentral chemoreceptor Vs Peripheral chemoreceptor\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nWhich one of the following cells organelles is involved in protein synthesis?\n\n- A. Lysosome\n- B. Smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n- **C. Rough endoplasmic reticulum** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Peroxisomes\n- E. Golgi bodies\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCell organelles quick revision:\n\n1: Lysosomes contains hydrolases responsible for digestion and breakdown of defective proteins Uterus and breast after pregnancy regress by lysosomes 2: Mitochondria: Powerhouse responsible for production of chemical energy in the form of ATP Unique feature in mitochondria is of own DNA. 3: Endoplasmic reticulum:\n\nRough endoplasmic reticulum most likely synthesizes proteins.\n\nDetoxification of drug occurs in smooth endoplasmic reticulum.\n\nThe smooth endoplasmic reticulum serves as a store for intracellular Ca2+ and is the major site of lipid production.\n\n4: Golgi body: Secretory vesicles found The Golgi apparatus packages proteins for delivery to specific intracellular destinations or into vesicles which can then be secreted from the cell for extracellular action.\n\n5: Nucleus contains genetic material and nucleol us responsible for ribosomes production. 6: Ribosomes: Ribosomes are the cellular structures where protein synthesis takes place. They can be found in the cytoplasm (free ribosomes) or attached to the endoplasmic reticulum (bound ribosomes).\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nWhich one of the following vaccines is given in pregnancy considering selective immunization program in UK?\n\n- **A. Inactivated influenza** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Rota virus\n- C. Hepatitis B\n- D. BCG\n- E. DPT Vaccine\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSelective immunisation progr ammes\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 40-year-old male was brought into the ED after he was found collapsed at home due to heroin overdose. He is in apnoea. Which one of the following respiratory centre is likely damaged?\n\n- A. Cerebral cortex\n- B. Dorsal respiratory group\n- **C. Pneumotaxic centre** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Apneustic centre\n- E. Cushing centre\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPneumotaxic centeris located in the upper pons inhibits inspiration and, therefore, regulates both the rate (Increases) and pattern of breathing. Pneumotaxic center normally prevents excessive deep breathing, damage to pneumotaxic results in increase in depth of respiration (deep breathing) and decrease in respiratory rate/Apnoea The Apneustic center is located in the lower pons. Stimulates inspiration, producing a deep an d prolonged inspiratory gasp (apneusis). Normally apneustic center promotes deep breathing, damage to apneustic center promotes shallow irregular/ Erratic breathing (Recall)\n\nNote: Focus on the question stem, if they ask you normal functions of apneustic center or Pneumotaxic centre then your answer would be changed according to their normal functions and damaged clinical significance. For example: Normally apneusis/ Deep prolonged inspiration is induced by apneustic center while damage to apneustic center promotes shallow/irregular or erratic breathing.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 30-year-old female was brought into the ED after she had been trapped in burning house. She is cyanotic, Pulse oximetry reveals 100% Sa02. Which one of the following is common reason of pulse oximeter defect in clinical setup?\n\n- A. COPD\n- B. Methhaemoglobinemia\n- C. Dark skin\n- **D. Carboxyhaemoglobin** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Asthma\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCoHb (Carboxyhaemoglobin): Gives falsely high reading MetHb(Methemoglobinemia): Gives falsely low reading.\n\nDark Skin: No effect\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nThe anaesthetic team are called to the resus area of your ED to intubate a trauma patient. His age is 25. Following successfully intubating the patient they set up the ventilator and prepared to transfer him to ICU. What is the approximate tidal volume in an average sized, healthy young male?\n\n- **A. 7 ml/kg** (Correct Answer)\n- B. 4 ml/kg\n- C. 20 ml/kg\n- D. 15 ml/kg\n- E. 17 ml/kg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe tidal volume is the volume of air displaced between normal inhalation and normal exhalatio n. In a healthy, young adult male tidal volume is approximately 500 ml per inspiration or 7 ml/kg. Lung volume and capacities (High Yield)\n\nTidal Volume (TV): The volume of air that is inhaled and then exhaled with each normal breath.\n\nNormal tidal volume is 500ml or 7ml/kg.\n\nInspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV): The maximum volume of air that can be inhaled following an d above a normal tidal inspiration.\n\nNormal Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is 3300m l.\n\nExpiratory Reserve Volume (ERV): The maximum volume of air that can be exhaled from the resting expiration of tidal volume.\n\nNormal Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is 1700m l.\n\nResidual Volume (RV): The volume of air that remains in the lung following a maximum expiration.\n\nResidual Volume cannot be measured using spirometry.\n\nNormal residual volume is 1800ml.\n\nCapacities\n\nInspiratory Capacity (IC): is the maximum volume of air that can be inhaled at the end of resting expiration.\n\nInspiratory capacity is sum of IC= TV+ IRV Functional Residual Capacity (FRC): the volume of air remaining in the lungs at the resting end-expiratory level or Volume remaining in the lungs after tidal volume is expired in air.\n\nFRC is sum of: FRC= ERV+ RV FRC include Residual volume so FRC cannot be measured using spirometry.\n\nNormal FRC is 3500ml.\n\nFactors affecting FRC.\n\nFactors increasing FRC: Emphysema, Air trapping in asthma, Ageing (due to loss of elastic properties) and Increasing height of patient.\n\nFactors decreasing FRC: Restrictive ventilatory defects e.g., pulmonary fibrosis, Posture - lying supine, Increased intra-abdominal pressure (e.g., obesity, pregnancy, ascites) and Reduced muscle tone of diaphragm e.g., muscle relaxants in anesthesia, neuromuscular disease.\n\nVital Capacity (VC) is the maximum volume of air that may be exhaled following a maximum inspiration OR inhaled following a maximum expiration.\n\nVC= IRV+TV+ ERV OR VC= IC+ERV Normal VC in young adult (70kg) is 5500ml.\n\nTotal Lung Capacity (TLC) is the volume of air contained within the lungs following a maximum inspiration.\n\nTLC include Residual volume so it cannot be measured using spirometry.\n\nTLC= IRV + TV+ ERV + RV OR TLC= IC + FRC Or TLC = VC+ RV Normal TLC in young adult (70kg) is 7300ml.\n\nForced expiratory volume (FEV1)\n\nFEV1 is the volume of air that can be expired in th e first second of a forced maximal expiration. FEV1 is normally 80% of the forced vital capacity, which is expressed as FEV1/ FVC = 0.8 In obstructive lung disease such as asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), both FEV1 and FVC are reduced, but FEV1 is reduce d more than FVC. FEV1/FVC ratio is less than 0. 7.\n\nIn restrictive lung disease such as fibrosis, both FEV1 and FVC are reduced, but FEV1 is reduced less than FVC is thus, Fev1 /FVC increased. Lung volume measurements a nd Peak Expiratory flow rate:\n\nLung volume can be measured with Spirometry. Residual volume cannot be measured using spirometry.\n\nResidual volume and thus (FRC and TLC) can be measured using helium dilution.\n\nPeak Expiratory flow rate:\n\nThe easiest and quickest measurement is the peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR)\n\nAirway resistance and lung compliance can be as sessed indirectly by measuring the forced expiratory flows and volumes.\n\nLung volumes can be measured with spirometry. PEFR is reduced if airway resistance is increased in obstructive disease and is commonly used to monitor asthma.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 45-year-old male presents to the ED after a recent cerebrovascular accident (CVA), the patient presents with dysphagia, struggling to swallow both solids and liquids. Upon examination, it is determined that the dysfunction has likely involved one of the cranial nerves, which one of the following nerves is most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Cranial Nerve | Vagus Nerve (CN X) |\n> | Cause of injury | jugular foramen syndrome, Trauma, neck surgery, tumors, aneurysms |\n> |  | Taste around epiglottis and pharynx Larynx, laryngopharynx, external |\n> | Sensory function | ear, external acoustic meatus, |\n> |  | Visceral sensation to heart and abdominal viscera |\n> |  | Efferent pathway of gag reflex |\n> |  | Muscles of soft palate except tensor veli palatini. |\n> | Motor function | Muscles of pharynx except stylopharyngeus |\n> |  | Muscles of larynx, palatoglossus muscle of tongue, visceral efferent |\n> |  | fibres to viscera of neck, thorax and abdomen |\n> | Assessment | Look for uvular deviation, gag reflex and swallowing |\n> |  | Dysarthria, dysphonia, dysphagia, stridor, loss of gag reflex, uvular |\n> | Clinical effects of injury | 2 ee |\n> |  | deviation away from affected side |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> Decrease in venous return\n> Reduced ventricular filling\n> Increased sympathetic tone\n> Hypercontractility of ventricles with underfilled chamber\n> Bradycardia and Hypotension\n> Syncope\n\n- A. Abducent nerve (VI)\n- B. Vestibulocochlear nerve\n- C. Facial nerve (VII)\n- **D. Vagus nerve (X)** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Hypoglossal nerve (XII)\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nVagus nerve\n\noriginates in the medulla oblongata of the brain and leaves skull through jugular foramen together with CN IX and CN XI respectively.\n\nHigh yield:\n\nBranches of vagus nerve:\n\nSuperior laryngeal nerve: innervate cricothyroid muscle and gives sensory innervation to laryngopharynx and superior part of the larynx\n\nRight Recurrent laryngeal nerve: Hooks underneath right subclavian artery and ascends toward s larynx and innervate muscles of larynx\n\nLeft Recurrent laryngeal nerve: Hooks underneath the arch of aorta, and ascends towards larynx and innervate muscles of larynx\n\nClinical pearls:\n\nVasovagal syncope: Causes heart rate and bloo d pressure to drop suddenly during a period of emotional stress caused by initial increase in sympathetic outflow followed by a rebound reduction in sympathetic activity leaving unopposed parasympathetic activity causing vasodilatation, bradycardia, and hypotension\n\nOrthostatic syncope: Defined as an orthostatic drop of >20 mm systolic blood pressure or >10 m m diastolic blood pressure. This may be due to absolute volume depletion from dehydration or haemorrhage or to venodilatation caused by medications or autonomic insufficiency (eg as occurs in Parkinsons disease).\n\nCauses of syncope:\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 40-year-old male presents to the ED with infected surgical wound not healing and suture line separated. Which one of the following is the most likely reason for delayed wound healing?\n\n- A. Fibrin degradation\n- B. Increased angiogenesis\n- C. Extra cellular matrix shortage\n- **D. Ongoing inflammation** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Decreased oxidative stress\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPersistent inflammation, as a hallmark of chronic wounds, is connected to the dysregulation of the immune response during wound healing by various factors and leads to excessive levels of pro-inflammatory signals, reactive oxygen species (RO S), changes in the proteolytic balance, and an increased amount of matrix metalloproteinases (M MPs) that eventually cause damage to the extracellular matrix (ECM) and impaired epithelializatio n and proliferation of keratinocytes.\n\nWound healing (High Yield)\n\nHealing is the process of replacing dead and damaging tissue with healthy tissue; this may occur through regeneration or repair.\n\nThe regenerative capacity of cells can be categor ized in three main ways:\n\nLabile cells are constantly dividing and have a good capacity for regeneration, this allows the replacement of ageing tissue such as the surface epithelia of the skin, gastrointestinal tract and uterus; blood cells are derived from labile cells of the bone marrow.\n\nStable cells slowly replicate e.g., cells of liver, renal tubular epithelium, endocrine glands. Permanent tissues: incapable of division and can not be regenerated e.g., brain cells, myocardial cells, skeletal muscle cells.\n\nHealing by primary intention:\n\nHealing of a clean, uninfected surgical incision ap proximated by surgical sutures.\n\nDay-1: Neutrophilic infiltration + fibrin clot Day-2: Epithelial cells from both edges have begu n to migrate and proliferate along the dermis, yiel ding a thin but continuous epithelial layer. Day-3: Macrophages replace neutrophils Appearance of granulation tissue. Type III collagen deposition begins but do not bridge the incision. Day 5: Abundant granulation tissue Collagen fibri ls bridge the incision.\n\nNeovascularization is maximum.\n\nWeek-2: Dense scar tissue produced from granulation tissue contains type III collagen (weak collagen) that must be remodeled. Remodeling increases the tensile strength of scar tissue.\n\n1 month: Replacement of collagen type III with collagen type I (has greater tensile strength) due to action of collagenase enzyme.\n\nThe predominant collagen in adult skin is type I, whereas in early granulation tissue, it is type III. Healing by secondary intention Occurs in large wounds abscess formation and u lceration.\n\nMain features:\n\nA larger clot or scab Inflammation: more intense Granulation tissue: much larger amount Scar tissue: greater mass Wound contraction: by myofibroblast e.g. Within 6 weeks, large skin defects may be reduced to 5% to 10% of their original size, largely by contraction.\n\nHealing by tertiary intention\n\nContaminated wound is initially treated with deb ridement and antibiotics followed by surgical wound closure (suture, skin graft replacement, flap)\n\nNote: Regarding the neat surgical scar after 2 months of surgery fibroblasts have predominant role Most important for delayed wound healing is due to infection.\n\nFactors affecting wound healing:\n\nLocal factors affecting wound healing\n\nMechanical injury Infection Edema Ischemia/necrotic tissue Topical agents lionizing radiation Low oxygen tension Foreign bodies\n\nSystemic factors affecting wound healing\n\nDiabetes mellitus Anaemia Malnutrition Immunodeficiency Immunosuppressive medications Age Obesity Smoking Vit A, C and Zinc deficiency Complications of Healing Hypertrophic scar Keloid formation Failure to heal (abscess or empyema formation) Failure to unite (skin, muscle or fascia wound breakdown)\n\nFracture complications\n\nRemember the cells involved in different stage s of wound healing.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 35-year-old male was brought into the ED with dysphasia, contralateral sensory loss in the face and upper limb, and contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Which cerebral artery is most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Blood vessel | Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness in lower limb, |\n> |  | dysarthria, dysphasia, apraxia and urinary |\n> | Anterior cerebral | incontinence |\n> | arter |  |\n> | y | Parietal lobe: contralateral somatosensory loss in |\n> |  | the lower limb |\n> |  | * Occipital lobe: contralateral homonymous |\n> |  | hemianopia with macular sparing, cortical |\n> |  | blindness (if bilateral) |\n> | Posterior cerebral |  |\n> | artery | * memory deficit |\n> |  | * Occipitotemporal region: prosopagnosia, and |\n> |  | color blindness |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness (face/arm |\n> |  | greater than leg), conjugate deviation of the eyes |\n> |  | to affected side, expressive dysphasia. |\n> | Middle cerebral |  |\n> | rt | Temporal lobe: deafness (if bilateral), receptive |\n> |  | dysphasia, auditory illusions and hallucinations, |\n> |  | contralateral superior quadrantanopia. |\n> |  | Parietal lobe: loss of sensory discrimination, |\n> |  | hemineglect, apraxia, contralateral inferior |\n> |  | quadrantanopia (RECALL) |\n\n- A. Vertebral Artery\n- B. Posterior Cerebral Artery (PCA)\n- C. Anterior Cerebral Artery (ACA)\n- **D. Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA)** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Anterior choroidal artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMCA infarct leads to contralateral motor and sensory deficits usually face/Arm greater than leg\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA mother is positive for hepatitis B surface antigen gives birth recently. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step for the baby?\n\n- A. No treatment required\n- B. Administration of hepatitis B immunoglobulin\n- C. Treatment only if the baby tested positive for hepatitis B surface antigen\n- D. Administration of hepatitis B vaccine\n- **E. Administration of both hepatitis B vaccine and immunoglobulin** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAdministration of both hepatitis B vaccine and immunoglobulin- Transmission of hepatitis B infection to babies of infected mothers is reduced by 95% if both vaccine and immunoglobulin are given early.\n\nSelective immunization schedule:\n\nBabies born to hep-B infected mother: Vaccine at birth, 4 weeks and 12 months.\n\nUsual site of Hep-B Vaccine is thigh.\n\nSelective immunisation programmes\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nWhich one of the following selective vaccines is given to health care professionals annually?\n\n- **A. Influenza** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Rota virus\n- C. Varicella vaccine\n- D. Hepatitis B\n- E. BCG Vaccine\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSelected vaccines\n\nBCG vaccine is recommended for healthcare workers who may have close contact with infectious patients. It is particularly important to test and immunize staff working in maternity and paediatric departments and departments in which the patients are likely to be immunocompromised, e.g. transplant, oncology and HIV units.\n\nHepatitis B vaccination is recommended for healthcare workers who may have direct contact with patients' blood or blood-stained body fluids. Influenza immunisation helps to prevent influenza in staff and may also reduce the transmission of influenza to vulnerable patients. Influenza vaccination is therefore, recommended for healthcare workers directly involved in patient care, who should be offered influenza immunisation on an annual basis.\n\nVaricella vaccine is recommended for susceptible healthcare workers who have direct patient contact. Those with a definite history of chickenpox o r herpes zoster can be considered protected. Healthcare workers with a negative or uncertain history of chickenpox or herpes zoster should be serologically tested and vaccine only offered to those without the varicella zoster antibody.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA Patient has a history of a rare genetic disorder affecting energy production. This disorder is maternally inherited and primarily involves a malfunction in which organelle?"\n\n- A. Endoplasmic reticulum\n- B. Lysosome\n- C. Golgi apparatus\n- D. Nucleus\n- **E. Mitochondria** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMitochondria, as many mitochondrial disorders a re maternally inherited due to the presence of mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) in the egg cell.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nRegarding the anatomy of pituitary gland, pituitary fossa lies inferior to which one of the following structures?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Superior |  | Anterior |  |  |\n> |  | orbital fissure |  | clinoid process |  |  |\n> |  |  | Optic Optic |  | Lesser |  |\n> | cy |  | 9 | F |  | \\ |\n> | Foramen |  |  |  |  | Greater |\n> | rotundum |  |  | | UWS | 6 f | wing |\n> | Foramen |  |  |  |  | id |\n> | spinosum |  |  |  | Tuberculum |  |\n> |  | Foramen | Dorsum |  | sellae |  |\n> |  | ovale | sellae | Posterior |  |  |\n> | com |  |  | clinoid process |  |  |\n\n- **A. Diaphragm sellae** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Pituitary stalk\n- C. tuberculum sellae\n- D. Posterior clinoid process\n- E. Dorsum sellae\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe pituitary gland is a pea-sized structure, suspended from the underside of the brain by the pituitary stalk known as the infundibulum. It sits within a small depression in the sphenoid bone, known as the Sella turcica or the hypophyseal fossa or pituitary fossa\n\nThe diaphragma sellae is a sheet of dura mater th at forms the superior surface of the pituitary or hypophyseal fossa.\n\nNote: while the tuberculum sellae is a bony prominence on the superior surface of the Sella turcica. If they ask you, bony prominence on the superior surface of Sella turcica or pituitary fossa choose tuberculum sellae.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA patient presents with difficulty breathing and diminished breath sounds on auscultation. The chest X-ray reveals lung collapse and further evaluation it was found lung surfactant is reduced. Which one of the following lung volumes affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Normal FVC\n- B. High FVC\n- C. Low FEV1\n- D. FEV1/FVC Low\n- **E. FRC reduced** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nlung collapse, atelectasis and restrictive pattern frc decreases.\n\nFactors affecting FRC.\n\nFactors increasing FRC: Emphysema, Air trapping in asthma, Ageing (due to loss of elastic properties) and Increasing height of patient.\n\nFactors decreasing FRC: Restrictive ventilatory defects e.g., pulmonary fibrosis, Posture - lying supine, Increased intra-abdominal pressure (e.g., obesity, pregnancy, ascites) and Reduced muscle tone of diaphragm e.g., muscle relaxants in anesthesia, neuromuscular disease, Lung collapse and atelectasis.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 34:** 1-E, 2-E, 3-C, 4-A, 5-C, 6-D, 7-A, 8-D, 9-D, 10-D, 11-E, 12-A, 13-E, 14-A, 15-E\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
61f9a849-45f2-4ef1-b461-fe494f6616fd	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	topic-hyperkalaemic-cardiac-arrest	Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest	quiz	# Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
669bfc48-c7af-4eb9-8368-b58abea3cff3	ab812750-760a-4eae-8a6c-e5d4b3f1520b	basal-ganglia	Basal Ganglia	article	Basal Ganglia:\nThe cluster of sub-cortical nuclei located deep to cerebral\nhemispheres. Basal ganglia's largest component is corpus\nstriatum which contains the caudate and lenticular nuclei\n(the putamen, globus pallidus externus, and internus), the\nsubthalamic nucleus (STN), and the substantia nigra (SN).\nFunction: Basal ganglia provide a feedback mechanism to\ncortex modulating movements; preventing unwanted\nmovements\nCaudate Nucleus: Forms the lateral wall of lateral ventricle.\ncaudate nucleus is separated from the putamen by\ndescending white matter fibres, which at this level are known\nas internal capsule.\nLenticular Nuclei:\n1:Putamen: Forms lateral aspect of lenticular nuclei\n2:Globus pallidus: On inner surface of putamen lies globus\npallidus; externus, and internus.\nSubstantia Nigra: located in the midbrain but considered\nfunctionally as a part of the basal ganglia\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Basal ganglia are particularly associated with movement\n> disorders.  tremors; involuntary muscle movements;\n> abnormal increase in tone, difficulty initiating movements,\n> and abnormal posture\n> Parkinson's is the most important and notorious disease of\n> the basal ganglia. Classic clinical symptoms include\n> bradykinesia, resting tremor, postural instability, and\n> shuffling gait	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
