750e55b6-a71e-4d7a-af68-ccf831a1740e	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	topic-hypernatremia	Hypernatremia	quiz	# Hypernatremia\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Hypernatremia\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Hypernatremia**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	30	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
87df7577-34b7-40c3-a6dc-c734e0f251c8	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-35	Quiz 35: Sepsis Resuscitation, Toxicology Antidotes & Immunization Rules	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 35\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA patient was brought into the ED with septic shock. What is the preferred resuscitation fluid for patients with Sepsis and Septic Shock?\n\n- A. Normal Saline 0.9%\n- B. Gelatins\n- C. Dextran\n- **D. Ringer's lactate** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Albumin\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFluid resuscitation is a critical component to the emergency department (ED) management of patients with sepsis and septic shock. Fluids are administered to patients with sepsis in order to augment cardiac output and improve tissue perfusion and oxygenation. Recent evidence has suggest ed that the composition of fluids used in sepsis resuscitation may affect patient-centered outcomes.\n\nCrystalloid solutions remain the resuscitative fluid of choice for patients with sepsis and septic shock. Balanced crystalloid solutions may improve patient-centered outcomes and should be considered as an alternative to 0.9% normal saline (when available) in patients with sepsis. The use of albumin does not improve mortality in patients with sepsis of any severity. Hydroxyethyl starch solutions are associated with increased mortality and should not be used to resuscitate patients with severe sepsis or septic shock.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nFluids have traditionally been separated into crystalloid and colloid solutions. Crystalloid solutions can be further divided into balanced and unbalanced solutions. The term balanced solution is commonly used to refer to solutions that contain different electrolyte concentrations that more closely resemble the composition of plasma and minimally affect acid-base equilibrium. Examples of balanced solutions include Ringer's lactate, Ringer's acetate, Hartmann's solution, and Plasma -Lyte. In contrast, unbalanced solutions do not have physiologic concentrations of electrolytes an d can adversely affect acid-base equilibrium. The most common unbalanced fluid solution is 0.9% normal saline (NS). Colloid solutions primarily include albumin, hydroxyethylstarch (HES) solution s, dextrans, and gelatins\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 45-year-old male patient with a history of chronic alcohol abuse is admitted to the emergency department exhibiting symptoms of severe alcohol withdrawal, the medical team decides to administer Prochlorperazine. What is the main mechanism of action of Prochlorperazine?\n\n- A. Serotonin-5HT3 antagonist\n- B. Dopamine-D2 agonist\n- C. Histamine-H1 antagonist\n- D. Histamine-H2 antagonist\n- **E. Dopamine-D2 antagonist** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nProchlorperazine are dopamine-D2 receptor antagonist and act centrally by blocking the chemoreceptor trigger zone.\n\nAnti-psychotics (High Yield)\n\nAnti-psychotics acts by blocking dopamine D2 receptors in the brain .\n\nSide effects:\n\nExtrapyramidal side effects occur most commonly associated with antiemetic (metoclopramide, prochlorperazine) and anti-psychotics (haloperidol). Haloperidol is the most common causative antipsychotic drug.\n\nDystonia (abnormal face and body movements) most commonly occur in young female and children treated with Procyclidine or benztropine . Tardive dyskinesia (rhythmic, involuntary movements of tongue, face and jaw)\n\nOther side effects:\n\nParkinsonian symptoms (Tremor, Rigidity, bradykinesia and postural instability)\n\nHyperprolactinemia (Dopamine inhibits release o f prolactin)\n\nQT-interval prolongation\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 64-year-old woman presents to the hospital with an acutely painful, red, hot, swollen big toe. He has a past medical history of gout, diabetes mellitus, and severe heart failure. Gout is Diagnosed, you plan on commencing her on Colchicine . Which of the following is most common side effect of Colchicine?\n\n- A. Muscle paralysis\n- B. Ulcer\n- C. Anemia\n- D. Bleeding\n- **E. Gastrointestinal disturbance** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nGastrointestinal disorders are the most common adverse reactions with colchicine, these include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and particularly diarrhea.\n\nColchicine acts to induce microtubular depolymerization by Binding tubulin, thereby leading to decreased activation, Migration, and degranulation of neutrophils to the affected site.(RECALL) In patients with CVD or HF who present with an acute gout flare, colchicine is considered safe and potentially reduces the risk of myocardial infarction (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nAccording to Uk immunization protocol Hep-B vaccine is given prophylactically in which one of the following patients?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Additional vaccines for individuals with underlying medical conditions |  |  |  |\n> | Medical condition |  | Diseases protected against | Vaccines |\n> |  |  |  | MenACWY |\n> |  |  | Meningococcal groups A, B, C, W and Y | MenB |\n> | Asplenia or splenic dysfunction (including |  |  |  |\n> | due to sickle cell | disease) | Pneumococcal | (up to ten years of age)' |\n> |  |  | Influenza | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> |  |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n> |  |  |  | (up to ten years of age)? |\n> | Cochlear implants |  | Pneumococcal | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> | Chronic respiratory and heart conditions |  |  | PCV13 (up to ten years of |\n> | (such as severe asthma, chronic pulmonary |  |  | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> | disease, and heart failure) |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n> | Chronic neurological conditions |  |  | PCV13 (up to ten years of age)? |\n> | (such as | or motor neurone | Preumococcal | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> | disease, or learning disability) |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n> |  |  |  | (up to ten years of age)? |\n> | Diabetes |  | Preumococcal | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> |  |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n> |  |  |  | (up to ten years of age)? |\n> | Co | XD |  | or |\n> | including haemodialysis) |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n> |  |  | Hepatitis B (stage 4 and 5 CKD) | Hepatitis B |\n> |  |  |  | (up to ten years of age)? |\n> |  |  |  | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> | Chronic liver conditions |  | Hepatitis A | Annual flu vaccine |\n> |  |  |  | Hepatitis A |\n> |  |  | Hepatitis B | Hepatitis B |\n> |  |  | Hepatitis A | Hepatitis A |\n> | Haemophilia |  | Hepatitis B | Hepatitis B |\n> |  |  |  | (up to ten years of age)?? |\n> | Immunosuppression due to disease or |  | Pneumococcal |  |\n> | treatment* |  | Influenza | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> |  |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n> |  |  |  | MenACWY |\n> | Complement disorders |  | Meningococcal groups A, B, C, W and Y | MenB |\n> | (including those receiving complement |  | Pneumococcal | PCV13 (up to ten years of age)? |\n> | inhibitor therapy) |  | Influenza | PPV (from two years of age) |\n> |  |  |  | Annual flu vaccine |\n\n- A. CKD Stage 1\n- **B. Haemophilia** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Splenectomy patient\n- D. Sickle cell disease\n- E. Diabetes\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAdditional vaccines for individuals with underlying medical conditions High Yield\n\nAlthough the risk of hepatitis B is low in the UK, children and adults in high-risk groups are also offered the vaccine.\n\nPeople who are at risk of getting hepatitis B or developing serious complications from it should consider being vaccinated.\n\nThese groups include:\n\npeople who inject drugs or have a partner who in jects drugs people who change their sexual partners frequently men who have sex with men babies born to infected mothers close family or sexual partners of someone with hepatitis B people who have regular blood transfusions or blood products, and their carers people with any form of chronic liver disease or chronic kidney disease people travelling to high-risk countries (Asia, Africa). Recall male and female sex workers people whose work puts them at risk of contact with blood or body fluids, such as nurses, prison staff, doctors, dentists and laboratory staff prisoners families adopting or fostering children from high -risk countries some foster carers people who live in accommodation for people with learning disabilities people who work with people with a severe learn ing disability\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nStimulation of muscarinic receptors by pilocarpine is competitively inhibited by which of the following medications?\n\n- **A. Atropine** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Edrophonium\n- C. Naloxone\n- D. Physostigmine\n- E. Pralidoxime\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAtropine\n\nAtropine antagonises the action of the parasympathetic neurotransmitter acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. It therefore blocks the effects of the vagus nerve on both the SA node and the AV node, increasing sinus automaticity and facilitating AV node conduction.\n\nSide effects: (Anti-muscarinic effects) -Dry mouth - Nausea and vomiting - Blurred vision - Urinary retention - Tachyarrhythmias\n\nNote: Anti-muscarinic should be avoided in Myasthenia gravis, paralytic ileus, GI obstruction, Urinary retention and prostatic enlargement\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nWhich of the following is the first line therapy for gastroparesis in patients with diabetes?\n\n- A. Omeprazole\n- B. Cyclizine\n- **C. Metoclopramide** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Cimetidine\n- E. Ondansetron\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMetoclopramide is used to treat the symptoms of slow stomach emptying (gastroparesis) Metoclopramide relieves symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, heartburn, a feeling of fullness after meals, and loss of appetite.\n\nNote: In anesthesia practice, medications that ca n be used to increase gastric emptying before general anesthesia include prokinetic agents. One commonly used prokinetic agent for this purpose is metoclopramide. (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nKeloids, an abnormal overgrowth of scar tissue, are most commonly located on which part of the body?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. Chest and shoulders** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Face and forehead\n- C. Foot\n- D. Thigh and Hip\n- E. Abdomen and lower back\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nKeloids can occur on any part of the body, but they are more commonly found on the chest, shoulders, chin, neck, lower legs and ear lobes. Keloids are raised, firm, frequently pruritic and painful, and generally do not spontaneously regress. A keloid is a growth of dense fibrous tissue that extends beyond the boundaries of the original wound. This is different from a hypertrophic scar, which is typically confined to the original wound borders and tends to flatten with time.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nFever in inflammation is mediated by which one of the following cytokines?\n\n- **A. Interleukin-1 (IL-1)** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Interleukin-6 (IL-6)\n- C. Interleukin-10 (IL-10)\n- D. Interferon-gamma (IFN-gamma)\n- E. Tumor Necrosis Factor-alpha (TNF-alpha)\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInterleukin functions:\n\nMnemonic: Fever Tablet Means Effective Antipyr etics Action\n\nIL-1: Fever IL-2: T cell stimulator IL-3: Marrow stimulator IL-4: IgE stimulator IL-5: Class switching to IgA IL-6: Stimulates Acute Phase Protein Production\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA patient with a history of heart failure is prescribed digoxin. Which one of the following ecg changes demonstrating the digoxin effect?\n\n- A. QT interval prolongation\n- B. Peaked T waves\n- C. ST segment elevation\n- **D. PR Prolongation** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Shortening of PR interval\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDigoxin effect refers to the presence on the ECG of:\n\nDown sloping ST depression with a characteristic "reverse tick" appearance Flattened, inverted, or biphasic T waves. Shortened QT interval.\n\nPR Prolongation\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nMonitoring: Digoxin has narrow therapeutic index. Narrow therapeutic index means difference between the effective therapeutic dose and the potentially toxic dose is relatively small. This narrow window makes proper dosing and monitoring critically important to ensure the medication's safety, effectiveness and to prevent toxicity. The therapeutic plasma levels of digoxin are usually between 1.0-1.5 nmol/l. The likelihood of toxicity risesdramatically at levels above 2 nmol/l.\n\nDose adjustments in renal failure and liver failure:\n\nRenal failure: Digoxin is excreted renally, and impaired renal function can cause increased digoxin levels and leads to digoxin toxicity. In this case the patient should have their digoxin dose reduced and their digoxin level and electrolytes should be carefully monitored.\n\nLiver failure: Liver failure has little influence on digoxin metabolism or clearance; therefore, no dose adjustment is necessary.\n\nDigoxin toxicity: Precipitated by hypokalemia o r drugs causing hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, acidosis and renal failure.\n\nThe features of digoxin toxicity include:\n\nHyperkaliemia Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea .\n\nAbdominal pain Confusion, dizziness and psychosis Tachyarrhythmias or bradyarrhythmia's Xanthopsia (yellow-green vision)\n\nTreatment: Digoxin immune fab is the reversal agent (Antidote) of digoxin toxicity.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 45-year-old patient is scheduled to undergo elective knee arthroscopy due to persistent joint pain. The anesthesiologist decides to administer Bupivacaine, a local anesthetic, to manage perioperative pain effectively. The primary mechanism of action of Bupivacaine is?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Lidocaine | Prilocaine | Bupivacaine |\n> | Most widely used local | Local anesthetic drug used | Bupivacaine can be used as |\n> | anesthetic. Lidocaine tends to | widely in Bier's block. | an epidural or spinal |\n> | cause vasodilatation. This is due | + EMLAcream is mixture of | during childbirth to |\n> | to inhibition of action | 50% lidocaine and 50% | numb the lower part of the |\n> | potentials via sodium channel | prilocaine and can be used |  |\n> | blocking | numb the skin before | Other uses: Local anesthetic in |\n> |  | performing a venipuncture | surgical procedures where long- |\n> | may also be given in treatment of |  |  |\n> | ventricular arrythmia due to its |  | lasting pain reliefis required and |\n> | properties of inhibiting voltage |  |  |\n> | gated Na channels. The half-life |  | management. |\n> | of lidocaine is 1.5-2 hours. Its |  |  |\n> | onset of action is rapid within a |  | Local anesthetic toxicity: |\n> | few minutes, and it has a duration |  | Sedation, Hypotension, |\n> | of action of 30-60 minutes when |  | Bradycardia, Myocardial |\n> | used alone. Its duration of |  | depression, tremors, visual |\n> | action is prolonged by |  | disturbance |\n> | coadministration with |  | Antidote: Intralipid emulsion |\n> | adrenaline. Adrenaline is |  | therapy: used widely in L.A |\n> | vasoconstrictor, it prolongs the |  |  |\n> | action of local anesthetics. |  | toxicity. |\n\n- A. Blockade the GABA-gated chloride channels\n- **B. Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Stimulation of voltage-gated N-type calcium channels\n- D. Activation of ligand-gated potassium channels\n- E. blockage of Magnesium channels\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe local anesthetics (Bupivacaine) work by blocking voltage-gated Na+channels in nerve cell membranes, thus inhibiting the influx of sodium ion s and preventing the initiation and conduction o f nerve impulses.\n\nLocal anesthetic drugs: (High Yield)\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nWhich of the following errors is considered if the researcher states that there is no difference when one exists?\n\n- **A. Type II error** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Type III error\n- C. Type 1 error\n- D. Type-4 error\n- E. No-Error\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nExamples of Type 1 and Type 2 errors: (In simple type 1 is false positive and type 2 is false negative.\n\nResearcher states that there is no difference when one exists that means there is actual difference, and researcher made a mistake is type 2 error. Researcher states that there is difference when it does not exist that means there is no difference, and researcher made a mistake is type 1 error. Example of fire alarm: False firearm leading to inconvenience. (Creates trouble but actually the alarm is false is type 1 error.\n\nMissed fire alarm leading to disaster is type-2 error.\n\nType I error (false positive): the test result says you have coronavirus, but you actually don't. Type II error (false negative): the test result says you don't have coronavirus, but you actually do.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 44-year-old woman presents to the with ED complaining of palpitations. She has recently been started on Amitriptyline by her GP. What is the mechanism of tachycardia in Amitriptyline overdose?\n\n- **A. Muscarinic receptor blockade** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Histamine receptor blockade\n- C. Potassium channel blockade\n- D. alpha-Receptor blockade\n- E. Sodium channel blockade\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAmitriptyline is in the tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) drug, block the reuptake of norepinephrine an d serotonin from the synaptic cleft, thereby potentiating the effects of serotonin and norepinephrine on the postsynaptic receptors. The TCAs have also been shown to inhibit muscarinic, histamine, and alpha-adrenergic receptors, thereby leading to a host of undesirable side effects.\n\nAnticholinergic effects/parasympathetic blocking actions are due to muscarinic receptor blockade (leads to Tachycardia, urinary retention, blurred vision, acute angle closure glaucoma, confusion, constipation, and dry mouth\n\nNote:\n\nSedation (due to histamine receptor blockade)\n\nPostural hypotension (due to alpha adrenergic receptor blockade)\n\ncardiac arrhythmias (due to sodium channel blocker)\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA patient was brought into the ED with signs and symptoms of meningococcal disease, what advice will you give to staff members regarding post exposure prophylaxis?\n\n- A. Indication of vaccine\n- B. Wash hand with soap\n- **C. Antibiotics** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Perform blood and LP\n- E. No indication\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nChemoprophylaxis (Antibiotics) is administered a s soon as possible after exposure, ideally less than 24 hours after identification of an index patient.\n\nRifampin, ciprofloxacin, and ceftriaxone are 90%- 95% effective in reducing nasopharyngeal carriage of N. meningitidis and are all acceptable antimicrobial agents for chemoprophylaxis.\n\nThe indications for chemoprophylaxis implementation are as follows.\n\nClose contact with a patient within 7 days before the disease onset, regardless of vaccination status. "Close contact" applies to:\n\nHousehold members living with a sick person People who are in intimate contact with the patient People sleeping in the same bedroom with the patient (students, soldiers, officers)\n\nPeople who have been in direct contact with secretions from the patient's respiratory tract during medical procedures, including mouth-to-mouth resuscitation, suctioning, and endotracheal intubation, before or less than 24 hours after antimicrobial therapy was initiated People traveling with the patient in the same plane, ship, bus, or car for longer than 8 hours\n\nNote: You will advise all to avoid close contact an d if close contact has occurred chemoprophylaxisindicated in less than 24 hours.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA patient was prescribed an antacid by his GP presents with a complaint of constipation. Which of the following antacids is more likely to be associated with constipation as a side effect?\n\n- A. Magnesium hydroxide\n- B. Sodium bicarbonate\n- C. Carbonate\n- D. Calcium carbonate\n- **E. Aluminum hydroxide** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAntacid Side Effects:\n\nAntacids are relatively harmless, but they can ca use rare side effects.\n\nAluminum compounds can cause constipation by reducing bowel motility.\n\nMagnesium Compounds may cause diarrhea. Carbonates may generate carbon dioxide leading to bloating and flatulence\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 55-year-old man, experiences constant thirst, fatigue, and sudden weight loss. Medical tests reveal inhibited insulin secretion due to a genetic predisposition of type 2 diabetes. Which of the following is an inhibitor of insulin synthesis and secretion?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Secretin\n- B. Glycogen\n- C. Parasympathetic stimulation\n- D. Growth hormone\n- **E. Sympathetic stimulation** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFactors that stimulate insulin release include:\n\nAmino acids (arginine and leucine)\n\nParasympathetic stimulation (via acetylcholine) Gastrin Secretin Cholecystokinin Glucagon Growth hormone Cortisol Glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1)\n\nGastric inhibitory polypeptide (GIP)\n\nFactors that inhibit insulin release include:\n\nReduced blood glucose Fasting Sympathetic stimulation Somatostatin\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 35:** 1-D, 2-E, 3-E, 4-B, 5-A, 6-C, 7-A, 8-A, 9-D, 10-B, 11-A, 12-A, 13-C, 14-E, 15-E\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
e03cb1e3-1633-44f5-8ecf-b944ce1c6396	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-36	Quiz 36: Adrenal-Renal Physiology, Bowel Histology & Clinical Evidence	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 36\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA child with a forearm fracture requires a general anaesthetic for open reduction and internal fixation. The anesthetist asks when he ate his last meal and considers various factors that can affect gastric emptying as part of his assessment. Which of the following factors promotes gastric emptying?\n\n- A. Sympathetic innervation\n- B. Cholecystokinin\n- **C. Gastrin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Pregnancy\n- E. Secretin\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe following factors promote gastric emptying:\n\nIncreased food volume in the stomach Gastrin Motilin Parasympathetic innervation (via the vagus nerv e)\n\nProkinetics such as metoclopramide and erythro mycin\n\nThe following factors inhibit gastric emptying:\n\nDuodenal distension by vagal inhibition Cholecystokinin Secretin Somatostatin Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)\n\nGastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)\n\nSympathetic innervation (via the coeliac plexus) Pregnancy (via increased progesterone levels) Anti muscarinic e.g. Atropine Duodenal chyme high in H+, fat and osmolality (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 45-year-old patient presents with symptoms such as fatigue, weight loss, low blood pressure, and increased pigmentation of the skin. Laboratory tests reveal deficient adrenal hormone levels. What is the reason for skin darkening in this patient due to Addison's disease?\n\n- A. Skin darkening is unrelated to Addison's disease and might be due to other factors.\n- **B. Excessive melanin production stimulated by increased ACTH levels.** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Skin darkening is a result of high cortisol levels suppressing melanin production.\n- D. : Excessive melanin production stimulated by decrease in aldosterone\n- E. Decreased melanin production caused by adrenal hormone deficiency.\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn Addison disease the adrenal glands don't make enough cortisol and aldosterone, a steroid hormone that regulates salt and water balance. The low level of cortisol triggers the release of another hormone called adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). High ACTH leads to high levels of melanin, the chemical that gives skin its color and causes skin discoloration known as hyperpigmentation/bronzing.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA Patient presents to ED with uncontrolled high blood pressure and electrolyte imbalances. Nephrologists suspect dysregulation in the juxtaglomerular cells secretions. Extensive tests reveal overactive JG cells. Which of the following is secreted by JG cells?\n\n- A. Aldosterone\n- B. ADH\n- **C. Renin** (Correct Answer)\n- D. ACTH\n- E. Cortisol\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRenin is produced and stored in granular juxtaglomerular (JG) cells, which are modified aortic smooth muscle cells found in the media of afferent arterioles mainly.\n\nRenin angiotensin aldosterone system (High Yield)\n\nThe Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) is a hormone system within the body that is essential for the regulation of blood pressure and fluid balance. The system is mainly comprised of t he three hormones renin, angiotensin II and aldosterone.\n\nA fall in CPV (reflecting blood volume) is detected by atrial and other cardiopulmonary stretch receptors. Fall in blood volume sufficient to reduce th e blood pressure activates the baroreceptor reflex which in turn causes increased sympathetic stimulation resulting in Renal vasoconstriction decreasing the GFR.\n\nADH release causing H2O reabsorption.\n\nRenin release from granular cells of JG apparatus Peripheral vasoconstriction resulting in increased total peripheral vasoconstriction.\n\nRenin Release\n\nThe first stage of the RAAS is the release of the enzyme renin. Renin released from granular cells of the renal juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) in response to one of three factors:\n\nReduced sodium delivery to the distal convoluted tubule detected by macula densa cells. Reduced perfusion pressure in the kidney detected by baroreceptors in the afferent arteriole. Sympathetic stimulation of the JGA via beta1 adrenoreceptors.\n\nDecreased GFR Decreased ECF volume, arterial blood pressure or CVP.\n\nNote: The release of renin is inhibited by atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP), which is released by stretched atria in response to increases in blood pressure.\n\nAngiotensin II production\n\nAngiotensinogen is a precursor protein produce d in the liver and cleaved by renin to form angiotensin I. Angiotensin I is then converted to angiotensin II by angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE). This conversion occurs mainly in the lungs where ACE is produced by vascular endothelial cells, although ACE is also generated in smaller quantitie s within the renal endothelium.\n\nFunctions of angiotensin II\n\nAngiotensin 2 acts on AT1 receptors found in the endothelium of arterioles throughout the circulation to achieve vasoconstriction. The net effect of t his is an increase in total peripheral resistance an d consequently, blood pressure.\n\nAngiotensin II acts at the hypothalamus to stimulate the sensation of thirst, resulting in an increase in fluid consumption. This helps to raise the circulating volume and in turn, blood pressure. It also increases the secretion of ADH from the posterior pituitary gland - resulting in the production of more concentrated urine to reduce the loss o f fluid from urination.\n\nAngiotensin II acts on the adrenal cortex to stimulate the release of aldosterone from zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex. Which in turn causes increased Na reabsorption and K loss. It also stimulates the sympathetic nervous system to increase the release of noradrenaline (NA). which in turn increases cardiac output, release of renin, vasoconstriction of arterioles.\n\nCauses vasoconstriction of renal arterioles predominantly efferent arterioles thereby maintaining or increasing the GFR Activation of Na-H pump thereby increasing the reabsorption of Na from proximal tubule. Inhibits the renin production from granular cells of JG apparatus (negative feedback)\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA patient presents to the emergency department with a penetrating injury just inside the bowel wall. The next layer involved is the?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> Serosa (hyperechoic layer)\n> Muscularis propria (hyporechoic layer)\n> | Submucosa (hyperechoic layer)\n> Muscularis mucosae (hyporechoic layer)\n> Mucosa (hyperechoic layer)\n\n- A. Submucosa\n- B. Subserosa\n- C. Mucosa\n- **D. Muscularis propria** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Serosa\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn this scenario, the injury has penetrated just inside the bowel wall, indicating that it has breache d the serosa layer. The next layer, which is affected by the injury, is the muscularis propria.\n\nThe layers of the GI tract, from outermost to innermost\n\nSerosa or adventitia lined with visceral peritoneum.\n\nMuscularis propria Submucosa Mucosa\n\nHigh yield:\n\nAnterolateral abdominal wall layers:\n\nSkin Subcutaneous tissues (further divided into the superficial Camper's fascia and the deeper Scarpa's fascia)\n\nExternal oblique muscle Internal oblique muscle Transversus abdominis muscle Transversalis fascia Parietal peritoneum\n\nNote: The mucosa is the innermost layer responsible for absorption and secretion in the digestive process. If they ask you in simple inner most layer choose mucosa or outer most bowel wall layer choose serosa.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 35-year-old male presents to ED with sudden-onset hypertension, severe headache, and palpitations. The patient is diaphoretic, and a computed tomography (CT) scan reveals an adrenal mass consistent with Pheochromocytoma. The primary mechanism responsible for the symptoms in this patient involves the activation of?\n\n- A. Serotonin receptors\n- **B. Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Beta-2 adrenergic receptors\n- D. Dopamine receptors\n- E. GABA receptors\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPheochromocytoma is a rare adrenal gland tumor that can produce excessive amounts of catecholamines, including adrenaline and noradrenaline. The symptoms of Pheochromocytoma are mainly attributed to the effects of elevated catecholamines. Activation of alpha-1 adrenergic receptor s plays a key role in the manifestation of symptoms such as hypertension, headache, and diaphoresis. Therefore, the primary mechanism responsible for pheochromocytoma symptoms is the stimulation of alpha-1 receptors by increased catecholamines.\n\nTreatment of Pheochromocytoma (High Yield):\n\nDefinitive treatment is by surgical resection of th e tumor.\n\nPrior to surgery, acute crises are treated medically to control the effects of excess catecholamines.\n\nMedical therapy consists of:\n\nAlpha blocker (phenoxybenzamine), followed by Beta blocker (propranolol). Beta adrenoceptor blockade can be instituted to control tachycardia. Beta blockers must never be given before alpha-blocker as this may cause a paradoxical rise in blood pressure due to unopposed a-mediated vasoconstriction.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nWhen the Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is activated, it plays a crucial role in regulating renal blood flow and filtration in response to various physiological signals. Angiotensin-II, a key component of this system, increases Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) by which mechanism?\n\n- A. Dilatation of afferent arterioles\n- B. Relaxation of Glomerular capsule\n- C. Dilatation of efferent arterioles\n- D. Inhibition of tubular reabsorption\n- **E. Constriction of efferent arterioles** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAngiotensin-II (AT-II) preferentially constricts renal efferent arterioles, resulting in increased glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and decreased renal plasma flow (RPF). Thus, Angiotensin II production (High Yield)\n\nAngiotensinogen is a precursor protein produce d in the liver and cleaved by renin to form angiotensin I. Angiotensin I is then converted to angiotensin II by angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE). This conversion occurs mainly in the lungs where ACE is produced by vascular endothelial cells, although ACE is also generated in smaller quantitie s within the renal endothelium.\n\nFunctions of angiotensin II\n\nAngiotensin 2 acts on AT1 receptors found in the endothelium of arterioles throughout the circulation to achieve vasoconstriction. The net effect of t his is an increase in total peripheral resistance an d consequently, blood pressure.\n\nAngiotensin II acts at the hypothalamus to stimulate the sensation of thirst, resulting in an increase in fluid consumption. This helps to raise the circulating volume and in turn, blood pressure. It also increases the secretion of ADH from the posterior pituitary gland - resulting in the production of more concentrated urine to reduce the loss o f fluid from urination.\n\nAngiotensin II acts on the adrenal cortex to stimulate the release of aldosterone from zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex. Which in turn causes increased Na reabsorption and K loss. It also stimulates the sympathetic nervous system to increase the release of noradrenaline (NA). which in turn increases cardiac output, release of renin, vasoconstriction of arterioles.\n\nCauses vasoconstriction of renal arterioles predominantly efferent arterioles thereby maintaining or increasing the GFR Activation of Na-H pump thereby increasing the reabsorption of Na from proximal tubule. Inhibits the renin production from granular cells of JG apparatus (negative feedback)\n\nproducing a net increase in filtration fraction (Filtration Fraction = GFR/RPF).\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 40 year old lady patient arrives in the ED complaining of severe right hypochondrium pain. The diagnosis points towards acute cholecystitis. What hormone plays a pivotal role in triggering gallbladder contractions, contributing to the pain in this condition?\n\n- A. Gastrin\n- **B. Cholecystokinin (CCK)** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Somatostatin\n- D. Glucagon\n- E. Insulin\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRight hypochondrium pain due to Cholecystitis i s primarily caused by increased levels of Cholecystokinin (CCK). This hormone is released in response to the presence of fatty foods, stimulating th e contraction of the gallbladder and leading to heightened pressure, contributing to the pain in t he upper right abdomen.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 72-year-old woman who takes Bendroflumethiazide for hypertension is admitted to the Emergency Department. Admission bloods show the following: Na 131 mmol/l K: 2.2 mmol/l Urea 3.1 mmol/l Creatinine56 mumol/l Glucose:4.3 mmol/l Which one of the following ECG features is most likely to be seen?\n\n- A. Flattened P waves\n- B. Short PR interval\n- **C. U waves** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Short QT interval\n- E. J waves\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nECG features of hypokalemia\n\nU waves small or absent T waves (occasionally inversion) prolong PR interval.\n\nST depression long QT (QT prolongation)\n\nNote: J waves are seen in hypothermia whilst delta waves are associated with Wolff Parkinson White syndrome.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 50-year-old man is brought to the emergency room after a head injury caused by a fall. He is disoriented and experiencing severe nausea. Initial tests reveal low sodium levels (126 mEq/L) and concentrated urine, suggesting SIADH. Which hormone is elevated in this patient due to the head injury, leading to the development of SIADH symptoms?\n\n- A. Aldosterone\n- **B. Vasopressin** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Cortisol\n- D. Insulin\n- E. Thyroxine\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) is characterized by a physiologically inappropriate release of Antidiuretic hormone(vasopressin) either from the posterior pituitary gland, or an abnormal non-pituitary source. In most patients with head Injury, hyponatremia is a feature of the syndrome of inappropriate anti diuretic hormone (SIADH) secretion due to pituitary dysfunction after head injury.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 40-year-old patient brought to the ED following a road traffic accident. CT scan reveals retroperitoneal bleeding. Which of the following anatomical structure is involved?\n\n- A. Spleen\n- B. Tail of pancreas\n- C. Appendix\n- D. Sigmoid colon\n- **E. 3rd part of duodenum** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nA useful mnemonic to remember which organs are retroperitoneal is SAD PUCKER S = Suprarenal (adrenal) Glands A = Aorta and inferior vena cava D =Duodenum (2nd and 3rd part)\n\nP = Pancreas (except the tail)\n\nU = Ureters C = Colon (ascending and descending parts) K = Kidneys E = Esophagus R = Rectum\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nWhen comparing normally distributed data to negative skewed data on a graph. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | (a) Negatively skewed | (b) Normal (no skew) | (c) Positively skewed |  |\n> |  | Mean |  |  |\n> |  | Median |  |  |\n> | Mode | Mode | Mode |  |\n> | Median |  |  | Median |\n> | Mean | | |  | Mean |\n> |  | The normal curve |  |  |\n> | Negative direction | represents a perfectly | Positive direction |  |\n> |  | symmetrical distribution |  |  |\n\n- A. Moving away from the peak and toward the tail, the order is always from: mean to mode to median\n- B. In left skew the median moves left relative to the mean\n- C. In right skew the mean moves left relative to the median\n- **D. Moving away from the peak and toward the tail, the order is always from: mode to median to Mean** (Correct Answer)\n- E. The median is farther out in the long tail than is the mean\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWhen comparing normally distributed data to skewed data the following are true:\n\nMoving away from the peak and toward the tail, t he order is always from mode to median to mean.\n\nSkewness is of two types:\n\nIn right skew (Positive skew) the mean moves right relative to the median and the tail is longer o n the right side. For a positive skewness mean > median > mode.\n\nIn left skew (Negative skew) the mean moves left relative to the median and the tail is longer on left side. For a negative skewness mean < media n < mode (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nRelatively higher dose of aminophylline is required to attain therapeutic plasma concentration in which one of the following patients?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Anti- | Ipratropium: Blocks vagally mediated bronchoconstriction. |  | mouth, Constipation, Urinary retention, |\n> | muscarinic/cholinergic | + Short term relief of asthma/COPD delivered via inhaler |  | Blurred vision, tachycardia , reduced |\n> |  |  |  | bronchial secretions and acute closed angle |\n> |  |  |  | glaucoma. |\n> | Beta 2 agonist | Salbutamol/terbutaline: Acts directly on smooth muscle cells |  | +/- hypotension low dose |\n> |  | relaxation and bronchodilation. |  | has B2 effects |\n> |  | + Short term relief of bronchospasm in acute asthma/COPD |  | + Hypokalemia 'stimulates Na+/K+-ATPase |\n> |  | delivered via inhaler. |  | transporting K+ into cells. |\n> |  | + | and more potent than ipratropium. | + Tachycardia (high dose has B1 effects) |\n> |  |  |  | Others: Seizures, headache and QT interval |\n> |  |  |  | prolongation. |\n> | Theophylline | Theophylline: Theophylline acts by inhibition of |  | Theophylline: Has narrow therapeutic |\n> |  | phosphodiesterase and therefore inhibits the metabolism of |  | index Therapeutic plasma level is 10- |\n> |  | cyclic AMP results in increased tissue concentration of cyclic |  |  |\n> |  | AMP. |  | Side effects include: |\n> |  | + Used in severe or life threatening asthma |  | + Palpitations and tachycardia |\n> |  | + | used in exacerbation of COPD | + Headache |\n> |  |  |  | * Insomnia |\n> |  |  |  | + Hypokalemia (additive to 82 agonists) |\n> |  |  |  | Levels above 20mg/liter can produce |\n> |  |  |  | toxicity |\n> |  |  |  | * Cardiac arrhythmias including VF |\n> |  |  |  | + Tremor and seizures |\n> |  |  |  | + Nausea, vomiting and |\n> |  |  |  | + Rhabdomyolysis |\n\n- A. Congestive heart failure patients\n- B. Hepatic impairment\n- C. Infection\n- D. Those receiving cimetidine\n- **E. Smokers** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn smokers increased clearance and the decrease d half- life of theophylline, and in order to prevent the nighttime life-threatening attacks, it is necessary to recommend maximal doses of theophylline.\n\nTheophylline/aminophylline is metabolized in the liver, and has a narrow therapeutic index. The plasma theophylline concentration is decreased in smokers, and by alcohol consumption.\n\nNote: The plasma theophylline concentration is increased in heart failure, hepatic impairment, infections, fever and in the elderly, reduction in dosage may be necessary to avoid toxic accumulation.\n\nBronchodilator therapy:\n\nRemember theophylline mechanism of action as well as indications and adverse effects.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nWhich of the following statements about case-control studies is correct?\n\n- A. They are a type of experimental study\n- **B. They are usually shorter in duration than prospective cohort studies** (Correct Answer)\n- C. They are usually more expensive to run than a randomised controlled trial\n- D. They provide more evidence for causal inference than a randomised controlled trial\n- E. They are usually longer in duration than prospective cohort studies\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\ncase-control study is a type of observational study in which two groups of patients, one with the disease and one without, are compared on the basis of a proposed causative factor that occurred in t he past. They are therefore retrospective in nature and are useful in hypothesis generation. They are suitable to be used when investigating a rare disease or as a preliminary study in cases where Little is known about the disease and the proposed aetiological factor. They can look at multiple risk-factors (exposures) but can only look at a single outcome.\n\nCompared with randomised controlled trials, case-control studies are generally relatively inexpensive to run but provide less evidence for causal inference.\n\nCompared with prospective cohort studies, case-control studies are usually less expensive and also shorter in duration.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 45-year-old patient was brought into the emergency department after a penetrating injury involved outermost layer of the bowel wall, which layer is likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> Serosa (hyperechoic layer)\n> Muscularis propria (hyporechoic layer)\n> | Submucosa (hyperechoic layer)\n> Muscularis mucosae (hyporechoic layer)\n> Mucosa (hyperechoic layer)\n\n- **A. Serosa** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Submucosa\n- C. Subcutaneous tissue\n- D. Mucosa\n- E. Muscularis propria\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe layers of the GI tract, from outermost to innermost\n\nSerosa or adventitia lined with visceral peritoneum Muscularis propria Submucosa Mucosa\n\nNote: If they ask you penetrating injury just outside of the bowel wall that refers injury has not bre ached serosa (Outer layer) in that case parietal peritoneum is appropriate\n\nAnterolateral abdominal wall layers: (High Yield)\n\nSkin Subcutaneous tissues (further divided into the superficial Camper's fascia and the deeper Scarpa's fascia)\n\nExternal oblique muscle Internal oblique muscle Transversus abdominis muscle Transversalis fascia Parietal peritoneum\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA new blood test to screen patients for heart failure is trialled on 500 patients. The test was positive in 40 of the 50 patients shown to have heart failure by echocardiography. It was also positive in 20 patients who were shown not to have heart failure. What is the positive predictive value of the test?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Disease Present | Disease Absent |  |\n> | Test Positive |  |  |\n> |  | True Positive (TP) | False Positive (FP) |\n> | Test Negative |  | Cc D |\n> |  | False Negative True Negative (TN) |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Sensitivity | TP/(TP+FN) | Proportion of patients with the condition who |\n> |  |  | have a positive test result |\n> | Specificity | TN / (TN + FP) | Proportion of patients without the condition who |\n> |  |  | have a negative test result |\n> | Positive predictive | TP / (TP + FP) | The chance that the patient has the condition if |\n> | value |  | the diagnostic test is positive |\n> | Negative predictive | TN /(TN+FN) | The chance that the patient does not have the |\n> | value |  | condition if the diagnostic test is negative |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. 0.66** (Correct Answer)\n- B. 0.33\n- C. Cannot be calculated\n- D. 0.8\n- E. 0.1\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPositive predictive value can be calculated as: TP / (TP + FP) = 40 / (40 + 20) = 0.66 Heart failure: (Disease present)\n\nTest positive or True positive (TP)= 40 Test negative or False negative (FN) 10 No heart failure (Disease absent)\n\nTest positive or False positive (FP)= 20 Test negative or True negative (TN)= 430\n\nHigh Yield:\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 36:** 1-C, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D, 5-B, 6-E, 7-B, 8-C, 9-B, 10-E, 11-D, 12-E, 13-B, 14-A, 15-A\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
87125d21-ae6c-4f83-b4e9-4f058bf52501	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	components-of-the-vasculature	Components of the Vasculature	article	Components of the vasculature\n\n### 1: Arteries:\n\ndeliver oxygenated blood to the tissues. are thick walled, with\nextensive elastic tissue and smooth muscle.\nare under high pressure. The blood volume contained in the\narteries is called the stressed volume.\n\n### 2. Arterioles:\n\nAre the smallest branches of the arteries.\nAre the site of highest resistance in the cardiovascular\nsystem. have a smooth muscle wall that is extensively\ninnervated by autonomic nerve fibers.\nThe greater the change in resistance at any point in the\nvasculature, the greater the loss of pressure at that point.\nArterioles have the most increase in resistance and cause\nthe largest decrease in blood pressure.\nArteriolar resistance is regulated by the autonomic nervous\nsystem (ANS).\nAlpha-1Adrenergic receptors are found on the arterioles of\nthe skin, splanchnic, and renal circulations.\nBeta-2 Adrenergic receptors are found on arterioles of\nskeletal muscle.\n\n### 3. Capillaries:\n\nHave the largest total cross-sectional and surface area.\nConsist of a single layer of endothelial cells surrounded by\nbasal lamina.\nAre thin walled.\nAre the site of exchange of nutrients, water, and gases.\nCapillary permeability:\nCapillaries throughout the body vary in their permeability\nbased on the size of their pores. There are three basic types:\nContinuous capillaries, found in the skin, lungs, muscles and\nCNS, are the most selective with low permeability, as junctions between the endothelial cells are very tight,\nrestricting the flow of molecules with MW > 10,000.\nFenestrated capillaries, found in renal glomeruli, endocrine\nglands and intestinal villi, are more permeable with less tight\njunctions, and the endothelial cells are also punctured by\npores which allow large amounts of fluids or metabolites to\npass.\nDiscontinuous capillaries, found in the reticuloendothelial\nsystem (bone marrow, liver and spleen), have large gaps\nbetween endothelial cells and are permeable to red blood\ncells.\n\n### 4:  Venules\n\nare formed from merged capillaries.\n\n### 5: Veins:\n\nprogressively merge to form larger veins. The largest vein, the\nvena cava, returns blood to the heart.\nAre thin walled.\nare under low pressure.\ncontain the highest proportion of blood in the cardiovascular\nsystem. The blood volume contained in the veins is called the\nunstressed volume.\nHave alpha 1 adrenergic receptors.\nPressure profile in blood vessels:\nAs blood flows through the systemic circulation, pressure\ndecreases progressively because of the resistance to blood\nflow.\nThus, pressure is highest in the aorta and large arteries and\nlowest in the venae cavae. The largest decrease in pressure\noccurs across the arterioles because they are the site of\nhighest resistance.\nMean pressures in the systemic circulation are as follows:\nAorta, 100 mm Hg\nArterioles, 50 mm Hg\nCapillaries, 20 mm Hg\nVena cava, 4 mm Hg	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
e5c812a9-02e1-47bd-9078-ff214acfb1d6	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-37	Quiz 37: Atypical Infections, Skull Base Pathology & Syncope Mechanisms	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 37\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 10-year-old child presents with pneumonia symptoms which include fever, cough, and fatigue. The child's chest auscultation is relatively clear, with minimal findings. A chest X-ray reveals a diffuse, patchy infiltrate. Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of this presentation?\n\n- A. Klebsiella pneumoniae\n- **B. Mycoplasma pneumoniae** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Staphylococcus aureus\n- D. Streptococcus pneumoniae\n- E. Haemophilus influenzae\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae is a common cause ofatypical pneumonia, especially occur in school-aged children and young adults. This organism often presents with milder symptoms compared t o typical bacterial pneumonias, and physical examination findings, including chest auscultation, may be minimal or unremarkable. The diffuse, patchy infiltrate seen on the chest X-ray is characteristic of Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. This distinguishes it from typical bacterial pneumonias caused by organisms like Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Staphylococcus aureus, and Klebsiella pneumoniae, which typically present with more pronounced auscultatory findings.\n\nNote: A petechial rash in a child, especially when associated with a clear chest on auscultation raises suspicion for meningococcal infection caused by Neisseria meningitidis. Meningococcal infection in children present with a nonspecific prodrome of cough, headache, and sore throat. After a few days of upper respiratory symptoms, the temperature rises abruptly\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA-35-year-old male patient presents to the ED with complaint of numbness in the cheek and anterior tongue. Which foramen is most likely involved in this presentation?\n\n- A. Foramen magnum\n- **B. Foramen ovale** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Jugular foramen\n- D. ) Foramen spinosum\n- E. Foramen rotundum\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNumbness in the cheek and anterior tongue suggests involvement of the sensory pathways of the trigeminal nerve (CN V). Specifically, the mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve, which passes through the foramen ovale to exit skull, provides general sensory innervation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue via the lingual nerve an d skin and mucosa over cheek via buccal nerve Therefore, involvement of the foramen ovale is most likely to cause this presentation.\n\nThe foramen rotundum transmits the maxillary division (V2) of the trigeminal nerve, which innervates the midface but does not affect the anterior tongue\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nDuring thymus surgery, which nerve is most commonly at risk of injury, potentially leading to respiratory complications?\n\n- A. Recurrent laryngeal nerve\n- B. Vagus nerve\n- C. Hypoglossal nerve\n- D. Accessory nerve\n- **E. Phrenic nerve** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nClinical pearls: During thymus surgery, particularly procedures involving the removal (thymectomy) or manipulation of the thymus gland, the phrenic nerve is at risk of damage.\n\nComplications that may arise during a thymectomy include:\n\nBleeding and damage to surrounding structures. The phrenic nerves are also closely invested with the thymus particularly on its lateral aspects and can be injured during dissection. This could lead to dysfunction of the diaphragm postoperatively. Pneumothorax or persistent air leak may be appreciated during the postoperative period, requiring prolonged tube thoracostomy.\n\nChylothorax is another complication that can arise due to damage to underlying lymphatic tissue, especially along the thymus' poles.\n\nPericardial injury, injury to the heart, or the phrenic nerve.\n\nIncomplete thymectomy for myasthenia gravis\n\nNote: During thyroid surgery, particularly procedures involving the removal (thyroidectomy) or manipulation of the thyroid gland, the recurrent laryngeal nerve is most commonly at risk of injury.\n\nInnervation of thymus gland (High Yield) The thymus gland is innervated by sympathetic nerves arising from the cervical sympathetic ganglia. These sympathetic nerves regulate blood flow to the thymus and play a role in its function. In addition, parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve may also contribute to the innervation of t he thymus gland nevertheless an autonomic nervous system of both sympathetic and parasympathetic components plays a role in regulating function of thymus gland\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely explanation for the Cushingoid features observed in a female patient, including thin sparse hair, central obesity, peripheral weakness, and decreased levels of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)?\n\n- **A. Exogenous glucocorticoid use** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Adrenal hyperplasia\n- C. Adrenal adenoma\n- D. Adrenal trauma\n- E. Pituitary adenoma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe administration of exogenous glucocorticoids is a common cause of iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome. Patients on long-term glucocorticoid therapy often develop Cushingoid features due to the systemic effects of the steroids. These steroids suppress the natural production of ACTH via negative feedback mechanisms Cushing syndrome It is a disorder caused by any condition that produces excessive glucocorticoids (cortisol). While th e term "Cushing syndrome" can be applied to any cause of hypercortisolism, "Cushing disease" refers specifically to secondary hypercortisolism that results from excessive production of ACTH by pituitary adenomas.\n\ncauses Iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome (most common cause) caused by exogenous administration of glucocorticoids.\n\nEndogenous: Cushing's disease (i.e., Cushing's syndrome secondary to pituitary tumor): Pituitary adenoma ACTH secretion-> bilateral adrenal gland hyperplasia -> increases cortisol secretion Adrenal tumors: adrenal adenoma & adrenal carcinoma Ectopic ACTH production Small cell lung cancer Clinical features Thin, easily bruisable skin with ecchymoses Strech marks (purple abdominal striae) Hirsutism Acne Delayed wound healing.\n\nAnxiety, irritability, Lethargy, fatigue, Sleep, disturbance, Memory deficits, Depression, Psychosis Osteopenia, osteoporosis, pathological fractures, avascular necrosis of the femoral head Endocrine and metabolic effects: Insulin resistance -> hyperglycemia Dyslipidemia Weight gain characterized by central obesity, moon facies, and a dorsocervical fat pad (buffalohump)\n\n: Decreased libido : Decreased libido, virilization, and/or irregular menstrual cycles (e.g., amenorrhea)\n\nGrowth delay (in children)\n\nNote: In Cushing disease: often hyperpigmentation (darkening of the skin due to an overproduction of melanin), especially in areas that are not normally exposed to the sun (e.g., palm creases, oral cavity) Overproduction of melanin Caused by excessive ACTH production because melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH) is cleaved from the same precursor as ACTH called proopiomelanocortin (POMC)\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA nurse accidentally pricked herself with a needle while drawing blood from a patient, which one of the following most acquired virus via needle stick injury?\n\n- A. Hepatitis C virus (HCV)\n- B. Hepatitis A virus (HAV)\n- **C. Hepatitis B virus (HBV)** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)\n- E. Cytomegalovirus (CMV)\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAmong healthcare workers, the most acquired virus through needlestick injuries is Hepatitis B virus (HBV). This is due to its higher prevalence and the greater infectivity of HBV compared to otherbloodborne pathogens.\n\nNote:\n\nThe most significant risk from needlestick injuries and exposures is the transmission of blood born e viruses.\n\nHepatitis B Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)\n\nHepatitis C\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA patient presents with sudden-onset facial weakness on one side of the face following a severe episode of vertigo and hearing loss. On further examination, it's noted that both the facial nerve (VII) and the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) are affected. Which structure does the facial nerve (VII) traverse alongside the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) within the temporal bone?\n\n- A. External auditory canal\n- B. Jugular foramen\n- C. Facial canal\n- **D. Internal auditory meatus** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Carotid canal\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe internal auditory meatus is a canal within the temporal bone that transmits both the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves. Compression or damage within this canal can lead to symptoms aecting both nerves, such as facial weakness (from involvement of the facial nerve) and vertigo/hearing loss (from involvement of the vestibulocochlear nerve)\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA patient presents with an abdominal hematoma involving the left umbilical area. Which muscle's aponeurosis primarily forms the posterior wall of the rectus sheath at this level?\n\n- A. Pyramidalis\n- B. External oblique\n- C. Internal oblique\n- **D. Transversus abdominis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Rectus abdominis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRectus sheath: The rectus sheath is formed by th e aponeuroses of the three flat muscles and encloses the rectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles. It has an anterior and posterior wall for most o f its length.\n\nAnterior wall: Formed by the aponeuroses of the external oblique, and of half of the internal oblique.\n\nPosterior wall: Half of the aponeuroses of the internal oblique and of the transversus abdominis. Approximately midway between the umbilicus a nd the pubic symphysis, all the aponeuroses move to the anterior wall of the rectus sheath. At this point, there is no posterior wall to the sheath; the rectus abdominis is in direct contact with the transversalis fascia. Point, where the posterior layer of the rectus sheath ends, is the arcuate line. Contents of the rectus sheath Rectus abdominus and the pyramidalis muscle The superior and inferior epigastric vessels The termination of intercostal nerves T7-T11 The 12th thoracic nerve and the accompanying vessels\n\nClinical pearls: Rectus sheath hematoma is a condition characterized by the accumulation of blood within the rectus sheath the fibrous compartment that contains the rectus abdominis muscle s. Hematoma is caused by rupture of inferior epigastric artery, this can occur due to trauma, anticoagulation therapy, intense physical activity, or abdominal surgery. The hematoma can cause abdominal pain, swelling, and a palpable mass.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 30-year-old male presents to the ED feeling unwell after he was injected prilocaine for a minor surgical procedure. Blood taken for arterial blood gas analysis appears chocolate brown. What is the immediate management for this patient?\n\n- A. Administer lipid emulsion\n- B. Administer activated charcoal\n- C. Administer flumazenil\n- D. Administer N-acetylcysteine\n- **E. Administer methylene blue** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe patient's symptoms, cyanosis, and the chocolate brown appearance of the blood suggest methemoglobinemia, a condition that can be induce d by certain local anesthetics such as prilocaine. The immediate management of methemoglobinemia involves administering methylene blue, which acts as a reducing agent to convert methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, thereby restoring t he oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. Lipid emulsion therapy is used for systemic toxicity of local anesthetics.\n\nNote: Lipid emulsion therapy is primarily used in the management of local anesthetic systemic toxicity which involves cardiovascular and central nervous system symptoms such as arrhythmias, seizures, or cardiac arrest. Lipid emulsion is not th e primary treatment for methemoglobinemia while it can be used as antidote in local anesthetic systemic toxicity\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 03-year-old boy with history of asthma brought into ED with complain of widespread urticarial rash associated with breathlessness. You suspect anaphylaxis. What is the dose of adrenaline for this patient?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  |  |  | > Establish an airway if |\n> |  |  |  |  | necessary and apply high |\n> |  |  |  |  | flow oxygen |\n> |  | Age group | IM adrenaline 1:1000 |  |  |\n> |  |  | = 500mcg (0.5ml) |  | > Apply appropriate |\n> | = Initial ste |  |  |  | monitoring (blood |\n> | initial step |  |  |  | pressure, pulse oximetry, |\n> |  | Child>12y | = | (0.5ml) | ECG) |\n> | > Call for help, lie the |  |  |  |  |\n> | atient flat and raise |  |  |  | . i |\n> | the patient's legs | * Child 6-22y |  | (0.3m!) | > fluid |\n> | . |  |  |  | bolus |\n> | (anterolateral |  | | + |  | | |\n> | aspect |  |  |  |  Adults: |\n> |  |  |  |  | intravenously |\n> | third of thigh) | Child |  | (0.1- |  |\n> |  | <6months | 0.15ml) |  | > Children: 10ml/kg |\n> |  |  |  |  | intravenously |\n\n- A. 400mcg\n- B. 100mcg\n- C. 0.5ml\n- **D. 150mcg** (Correct Answer)\n- E. 300mcg\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n-Management\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 30-year-old woman presents to the ED with symptoms of dysuria, increased urinary frequency, and suprapubic pain. She is diagnosed with an uncomplicated lower urinary tract infection (UTI). What is the first-line management for this condition?\n\n- A. Amoxicillin-clavulanate\n- **B. Trimethoprim** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Ciprofloxacin\n- D. Vancomycin\n- E. Metronidazole\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nlower urinary tract infection (UTI) in women It is an infection of the bladder (also known as cy stitis) usually caused by bacteria from the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nUncomplicated UTI -- UTI caused by typical pathogens in people with a normal urinary tract and kidney function, and no predisposing co-morbiditi es.\n\nComplicated UTI -- UTI with an increased likelihood of complications such as persistent infection, treatment failure and recurrent infection Causes Most common E-COLI Less common- Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Klebsiella species, Streptococcus, and Proteus mira bilis (more common in males, associated with renal tract abnormalities.\n\nEntry route Retrograde -- bacteria ascend through the urethra into the bladder.\n\nVia the blood stream -- more likely in people who are immunosuppressed.\n\nDirect -- for example with insertion of a catheter into the bladder, instrumentation, or surgery Clinical features Dysuria-- discomfort, pain, burning, tingling or st inging associated with urination.\n\nFrequency --passing urine more often than usual.\n\nUrgency -- a strong desire to empty the bladder, which may lead to urinary incontinence. Nocturia-- passing urine more often than usual a t night.\n\nSuprapubic discomfort/tenderness Urine may appear cloudy to the naked eye, or change colour or odour.\n\nHaematuria may present as red/brown discolour ation of urine or as frank blood Typical features may be absent, in particular i n elderly women with underlying cognitive impairment (Alzheimer's disease, early onset demen tia) -- consider UTI if the woman presents with: Generalized non-specific clinical features such as delirium, lethargy, reduced ability to carry out activities of daily living and anorexia.\n\nAlternative sources of infection and causes of deli rium other than UTI must be excluded before a working diagnosis of UTI is made Management Pain relief: paracetamol or ibuprofen Antibiotic therapy-non-pregnant women aged 1 6 years and over, treat according to sensitivities from recent urine culture (if available), otherwise treat empirically taking account of local antimicro bial resistance patterns First choice - Nitrofurantoin 100mg modified-release twice a day for 3 days (if eGFR >=45ml/minute)\n\nOR-Trimethoprim 200mg twice a day for 3 days (i f low risk of resistance)\n\nSecond choice -Nitrofurantoin 100mg modified-release twice a day for 3 days (if eGFR >=45ml/minute and not used as first-choice) or Pivmecillinam (a penicillin) 400mg initial dose, then 200mg three times a day for a total of 3 days o r Fosfomycin 3g single dose sachet Antibiotic therapy-pregnant women aged 12 years and over First choice: Nitrofurantoin (avoid at term): if esti mated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) >=45 ml/minute 100mg BD 7 days Second choice: Amoxicillin (only if culture results available and susceptible) or Cefalexin 500 mg twice a day for 7 days Advise Encourage intake of enough fluids to avoid dehydration.\n\nDo not recommend cranberry products or urine a lkalinising agents Complications Pyelonephritis renal and peri-renal abscess impaired renal function renal failure Urosepsis\n\nlower urinary tract infection (UTI) i n Men First choice Trimethoprim 200 mg twice daily for 7 days. Nitrofurantoin 100 mg modified release twice daily (or if unavailable 50 mg four times daily) for 7 days\n\nNote: Nitrofurantoin is not recommended for men with prostate involvement as it is unlikely to reach therapeutic levels in the prostate. If acute pros tatitis is suspected quinolones are the first-choice antibiotic.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA patient presents with a stab wound to the thenar eminence. Which of the following muscles is most likely involved?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Abductor pollicis brevis | Abducts thumb at MCP) | Median nerve |\n> | Flexor pollicis brevis | Flexes thumb at MCPJ | Median nerve |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Abductor digiti minimi | Abducts little finger at MCPJ | Ulnar nerve |\n> | Flexor digiti minimi brevis | Flexes little finger at MCPJ | Ulnar nerve |\n\n- A. Lumbricals\n- B. Adductor digiit minimi\n- C. Extensor pollicis longus\n- **D. Abductor pollicis brevis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Flexor pollicis longus\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe thenar eminence is the muscular bulge on t he radial side of the palm, responsible for the movements of the thumb. The muscles contained within the thenar eminence are primarily responsible for the fine motor movements of the thumb and include the abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, and opponens pollicis. Among these, the abductor pollicis brevis is the most superficial and therefore most likely to be involved in a stab wound.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nMuscles of the hand:\n\nThenar muscles:\n\nThree short muscles located in the base of the thumb. All are innervated by median nerve.\n\nHypothenar muscles:\n\nHypothenar muscle located at base of middle finger and form hypothenar eminence. All are innervated by ulnar nerve Lumbrical Muscles:\n\nThere are four lumbrical muscles. Each one of these originates from a tendon of the flexor digitorum profundus. They pass dorsally and laterally around each finger, and inserts into the extensor hood.\n\nInnervation:\n\nMedial two lumbricals: Ulnar nerve\n\nLateral two lumbricals: Median nerve\n\nFunction:\n\nFlexion at the MCP joint and extension at the interphalangeal (IP) joints of each digit.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 7-year-old child experiencing fever and lower extremity pain and swelling was diagnosed with osteomyelitis following radiological examination, which showed the elevation of the periosteum and bone destruction. Which one of the following organisms is most likely to have caused this child's osteomyelitis?\n\n- A. Mycobacterium tuberculosis\n- **B. Staphylococcus aureus** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Haemophilus influenzae\n- D. Escherichia coli\n- E. Streptococcus pneumoniae\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMost cases of osteomyelitis in children are cause d by Staphylococcus aureus, primarily methicillin -resistant strains. Osteomyelitis shows up as localized bone pain and swelling with concomitant fever. The radiologic findings may include periosteal elevation and bone destruction.\n\nRegarding osteomyelitis (High Yield)\n\nMost common cause generally: Staph Aureus (Recall)\n\nThe most common cause in IV drug abuser: Staph Aureus The most common cause in sickle cell disease: Salmonella Most common cause after hip replacement surgery: Staph epidermidis\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 30-year-old patient presents with facial paralysis and deafness. Imaging reveals a lesion involving a specific area of the temporal bone. Which part of the temporal bone is most likely involved in this presentation?\n\n- A. Zygomatic process\n- **B. Petrous part** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Squamous part\n- D. Mastoid part\n- E. Tympanic part\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe petrous part of the temporal bone contains t he internal acoustic meatus, a canal through which both the facial nerve (CN VII) and the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) pass. Lesions affectingpetrous part of temporal bone, such as tumors o r inflammation, can lead to symptoms of facial paralysis and deafness.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA-20-year-old young male patient experiences a vasovagal reaction after donating blood. Which hormone or compound is primarily responsible for the physiological response seen in this situation?\n\n- A. Acetylcholine\n- B. Epinephrine\n- C. Norepinephrine\n- **D. Adenosine** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Cortisol\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn Vasovagal reaction, there is a complex interplay of neurocardiogenic mechanisms leading to sudden hypotension and bradycardia, where adenosine can play a significant role. Adenosine is a compound that has potent vasodilatory properties and can influence heart rate by slowing atrioventricular conduction. During a vasovagal reaction, adenosine is released, contributing to vasodilation and bradycardia. This release can cause the drop in blood pressure and heart rate that is characteristic of a vasovagal episode. On the other hand, acetylcholine, released from vagus nerve endings, also contributes to these effects but adenosine role is more pronounced in directly affecting the cardiovascular system to produce the vasovagal response. Therefore, adenosine is the primary compound responsible for the physiological changes seen in this situation.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 45-year-old patient with osteoarthritis is prescribed naproxen for pain management. The patient asks about how the medication works. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of naproxen?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Inhibition of cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) enzyme only\n- B. Selective inhibition of cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) enzyme\n- **C. Non-selective inhibition of cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes >** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Antagonism of histamine H1 receptors\n- E. Inhibition of lipoxygenase pathway\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNaproxen is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that works by non-selectively inhibiting both cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) and (COX-2) enzymes. These enzymes are involved in the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, which are mediators of inflammation, pain, and fever. By inhibiting these enzymes, naproxen reduces th e synthesis of prostaglandins, thereby exerting it s anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic effects.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nNSAIDS: (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug s): Class of analgesics used to treat pain, fever an d inflammation.\n\nMechanism:Cyclo-oxygenase (COX) catalyzes th e production of prostaglandins through the intermediate arachidonic acid and Prostaglandins regulate the body's inflammatory response.\n\nCOX has 2 isoenzymes:\n\nCOX-I regulates many physiological processes including protection of gastric mucosa.\n\nCOX-II is expressed in inflammatory cells.\n\nNote: Most NSAIDs inhibit both COX-I and COX-I I. Some newer drugs inhibit just COX-II, therefore reducing gastric side effects.\n\nibuprofen (Safest NSAID) but low anti-inflammatory properties not useful in inflammatory conditions.\n\nNaproxen: Good efficacy, lower side effects but greater than ibuprofen. Used in musculoskeletal disorders, Rheumatoid arthritis, Gout and dysmenorrhea.\n\nDiclofenac: Similar in efficacy to naproxen but greater side effects.\n\nMefenamic acid: Low anti-inflammatory properties but greater side effects which include aplastic anemia and hemolytic anemia.\n\nNote: NSAIDs cause GERD by inhibiting the COX 1 enzymes (cox 1 stimulation produce prostaglandins that help to maintain the gastric mucosal integrity) NSAIDs can cause ulcers by interfering with the stomach's ability to protect itself from gastric acid (by inhibiting the function of prostaglandins)\n\nNSAIDS Adverse effects:\n\nGl adverse effects (Most common) which include NSAIDS induced peptic ulcer.\n\nHypersensitivity reactions Renal failure contraindicated in reduced GFR MI, Heart failure Tinnitus Hypertension Thrombotic events Fluid retention Haemolytic anemia (Mefenamic acid)\n\nNote: NSAIDs are contraindicated in a patient with heart failure. (Diclofenac is contraindicated in people with ischemic heart disease).\n\nRisk of GI Side effects:\n\nLowest risk=ibuprofen (Safest NSAID) Intermediate risk: Aspirin, naproxen Highest risk=piroxicam, ketorolac, indomethacin\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 37:** 1-B, 2-B, 3-E, 4-A, 5-C, 6-D, 7-D, 8-E, 9-D, 10-B, 11-D, 12-B, 13-B, 14-D, 15-C\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
52ee7dff-66e3-46f7-ab5f-242f2f0d947c	e779296a-994f-4512-b742-2eb98a064704	primary-quiz-38	Quiz 38: Genitourinary Emergencies, Hip/Knee Muscles & Spinal Landmarks	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 38\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 60-year-old male presents to ED with the complaint of scrotal pain and swelling, you suspect epididymo-orchitis, and prescribe Quinolone. What is the mechanism of action of Quinolone?\n\n- A. Inhibition of bacterial metabolism\n- B. Inhibition of bacterial cell membrane synthesis\n- C. Inhibition the bacterial cell wall synthesis\n- **D. Inhibition of bacterial nucleic acid synthesis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Inhibition of bacterial protein synthesis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nQuinolone interferes with the bacterial nucleic acid synthesis Acyclovir= inhibits bacterial DNA synthesis (RECALL)\n\nAminoglycosides=inhibit bacterial protein synthesis (bind bacterial 30s subunit) RECALL Cephalosporin=bacterial cell wall synthesis inhibitor chloramphenicol= inhibit bacterial protein synthesis macrolides=inhibit protein synthesis (bind to the bacterial 50s ribosomal subunit)\n\nMetronidazole= bacterial nucleic acid synthesis inhibitor (RECALL)\n\npenicillin=inhibit bacterial cell wall synthesis Tetracycline= inhibits bacterial protein synthesis (binds to 30S subunit of ribosomes)\n\nTrimethoprim=inhibit bacterial metabolism.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nWhich one of the following muscles are flexor of thigh at hip joint and flexor of leg at knee joint?\n\n- A. Iliacus\n- B. Pectineus\n- C. Psoas major\n- D. Rectus femoris\n- **E. Sartorius** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAll the muscles of anterior compartment of thigh are flexors of thigh at hip joint while Quadriceps femoris (Rectus femoris, Vastus lateralis, Vastus medialis and Vastus intermedius) is extensor of leg at knee joint. Some exceptions are:\n\nSartorius is also flexor of leg at knee joint and lateral rotator and abductor at hip joint Rectus femoris is also flexor of thigh at hip joint (RECALL)\n\nLateral rotators of hip: Anterior C.O.Thigh muscles include Sartorius, iliacus and psoas major Lateral rotators of hip: Muscles of the hip region include Piriformis (RECALL), obturator internus a nd externus, gamellus superior and inferior and quadratus lumborum\n\nNote: All the muscles of anterior compartment of thigh are innervated by femoral nerve except psoas major that is through lumbar plexus (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 30-year-old woman presents to the ED with weakness and fatigue. On examination, she has yellow tinge skin, jaundice, and mild splenomegaly. CBC shows her Hb 7g/dl. The patient only takes one medication. Which of the following medications is most likely to cause hemolytic anemia?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | * Co-Amoxiclav |  |  Obstructive jaundice or cholestatic jaundice |\n> | > |  | Extra pyramidal symptoms and parkinsonian like |\n> |  |  | symptoms |\n> | > | Druss |  degrees Dry mouth, Urinary retention and |\n> | Loop diuretics (Furosemide) and |  |  Hypokalemia, Metabolic alkalosis and hyperuricemia. In |\n> | thiazide diuretics |  | addition, Thiazides causes hyperglycemia too. |\n> |  | diuretics (Spironolactone) | Hyperkalemia, Metabolic acidosis and gynecomastia |\n> |  Ace inhibitors (Captopril) |  | Persistent dry cough |\n> | + Cv channel lotr |  |  |\n> | Aspirin |  | e Reyes syndrome |\n> | > |  |  degrees Respiratory alkalosis (Hyperventilation initially) later on |\n> |  |  | metabolic acidosis |\n> | > |  |  degrees Cushing syndrome, Myopathy and |\n> |  |  |  degrees Leukocytosis (Commonly), Diabetes insipidus, tremor |\n> |  |  | Hypothyroidism, Hyperreflexia and muscle twitch |\n> | Traumadol |  | * Constipation, Vomiting |\n> |  Fluroquinolones (Ciprofloxacin) |  |  Tendon rupture, Torsade's de pointes |\n> |  degrees |  | Nephrotoxic, Ototoxic and neurotoxic |\n> | Vancomycin |  | Red man syndrome, Nephrotoxic and Ototoxic |\n> |  Allopurinol |  | Steven Johnson syndrome |\n> |  Tetracyclines |  | Teeth discoloration |\n> |  Beta-2 Agonist (Salbutamol) |  | Hypokalemia |\n> | Nitrous oxide |  |  Vitamin B12 Deficiency and megaloblastic anemia |\n> |  degrees |  | Clostridium difficle colitis |\n> | Isoniazid |  |  Peripheral neuropathy |\n> | Macrolides (Erythromycin), |  |  |\n> | Aminoglycosides and Quinolones |  | QT interval prolongation leads to Torsades de pointes |\n> | (Cipro) |  |  |\n> | Suxamethonium |  |  Hyperkalemia |\n> | Mefenamic acid |  |  Haemolytic anemia |\n\n- A. Ibuprofen\n- B. Lithium\n- C. TCA\n- D. Propranolol\n- **E. Mefenamic acid** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAmong NSAIDs, Mefenamic acid causes hemolytic anemia.\n\nCo-amoxiclav causes Cholestatic jaundice (RECALL)\n\nSummary Of Drugs And Their Side Effects (High Yield)\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 30 years old male with a history of RTA was brought into the ED with a complaint of irregular breathing. Which of the following muscle is the accessory muscle of expiration?\n\n- A. Internal intercostal muscle\n- B. External intercostal muscle\n- C. Diaphragm\n- **D. Transversus abdominus** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Sternocleidomastoid\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDiaphragm: Normal quiet inspiration Normal expiration: Passive recoil of lungs Inspiration: External intercostal muscle Expiration: Internal intercostal muscle Accessory muscle of inspiration: Sternocleidomastoid Accessory muscle of expiration: Transversus abdominal muscles Forced expiration: Internal intercostal muscle an d rectus abdominus (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 29 years old male presents to the ED with complaint of foot drop and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the foot. Which of the following nerve is most likely damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Fibularislongus | 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform |\n> |  | (RECALL) |\n> | Fibularis brevis | 5th metatarsal |\n\n- A. Sciatic nerve\n- **B. Common peroneal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Sural nerve\n- D. Posterior tibial nerve\n- E. Tibial nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCommon fibular nerve wraps around the neck of fibula and enter lateral compartment of leg where it gives rise terminal branches:\n\nSuperficial fibular nerve: Runs around lateral compartment of leg and skin over lower anterolateral leg, and dorsum of foot (except skin over webspace of 1st and 2nd toe)\n\nDeep fibular nerve: Anterior compartment of leg and skin over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes (RECALL)\n\nClinical pearls:\n\n1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the common fibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a result of fracture of the head or neck of the fibula because it passes behind the head of the fibula and then winds laterally around the neck. T he nerve damage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion) and loss of sensation on the dorsum o f the foot and lateral aspect of the leg and cause s paralysis of all muscles in the anterior and lateral compartments of the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors muscles of the foot).\n\n2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by fracture of the fibula. As the muscles that the superficial fibular nerve innervates are evertors, injury to the nerve may result in a loss of eversion a nd loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral leg and dorsum of foot.\n\n3: Deep fibular nerve:\n\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in foot drop. In foot drop foot will appear permanently plantarflexed, foot drop with high-stepping gait, weakness of foot inversion.\n\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes.\n\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or common peroneal nerve or also due to deep fibular nerve injury.\n\nNote: It is very important to remember direct innervation of common fibular nerve.\n\nDeep fibular nerve root: L4-L5 Superficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.\n\nMuscles of lateral compartment of leg: (High Yield)\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 40 years old male presents to the ED with complain of ptosis with left-sided droopy eye and dry left face. You suspect Horner's syndrome. Which of the following nerve is most likely affected?\n\n- A. Facial nerve\n- B. Infratrochlear nerve\n- C. Long Ciliary nerve\n- **D. Cervical Sympathetic chain** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Vagus nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSympathetic fibers if unilaterally damaged may produce ipsilateral Horner's syndrome. Horner's syndrome characterized by triad of:\n\nPtosis Miosis Anhidrosis\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 55 Years old male with history of hypertension came into the ED with a complaint of sudden severe abdominal pain. After initial assessment at triage, you suspect abdominal aortic aneurysm. Which one of the following is the most common site of abdominal aortic aneurysm?\n\n- A. Below diaphragm\n- B. Suprarenal aorta\n- C. Epigastrium\n- **D. Infrarenal aorta** (Correct Answer)\n- E. At the level of umbilicus\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe most common location of arterial aneurysm formation is the abdominal aorta, while the most common site of abdominal aortic aneurysm isinfrarenal aorta (90 to 99%)\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nYou are going to attempt lumbar puncture in a patient with suspected meningitis. What is the recommended vertebral level to perform LP?\n\n- A. T11/T12\n- B. L1/L2\n- **C. L4/L5** (Correct Answer)\n- D. T12/L1\n- E. L2/L3\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nA lumbar puncture (LP) is a procedure in which a needle is inserted into the spinal canal. It is most commonly used to collect a sample of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) for diagnostic testing (e.g., for suspected meningitis and subarachnoid hemorrhage)\n\nLumbar puncture is preferentially performed at L3/L4 or L4/L5 (cauda equina level), to avoid the spinal cord which ends at L1/L2.\n\nThe layers penetrated by the spinal needle in an LP are as follows:\n\nSkin subcutaneous tissue supraspinous ligament (RECALL)\n\nInterspinous ligament ligamentum flavum Epidural space or extradural dura mater arachnoid mater subarachnoid space (contains CSF)\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 30-year-old diabetic patient presented to the emergency department with a two-day history of pain in her right eye worsened with eye movement. The diagnosis of optic neuritis was made by ophthalmologist. What will be the cause of optic neuritis?\n\n- A. Retinal detachment\n- B. Neuromyelitis\n- **C. Demyelination** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Retinitis\n- E. Hyperglycemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOptic Neuritis is an acute inflammatory demyelinating disorder of optic nerve. The myelin undergoes destruction causing axons to poorly conduct impulses.\n\nDemyelination disrupts the normal saltatory conduction process, leading to impaired transmission of signals along the optic nerve. inflammatorydemyelination as the pathological hallmark of disease; after the acute event, axonal damage leading to axonal loss can lead to severe and sometimes irreversible neurological impairment.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students regarding anatomy of soles of foot. You ask medical student's plantar arteries and nerves lie between the which layer of sole of foot.\n\n- A. 2nd and 3th layer\n- B. 4th and 5th layer\n- **C. 1st and 2nd layer** (Correct Answer)\n- D. 2nd and 4th layer\n- E. 3rd and 4th layer\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTendon of flexor digitorum brevis lie within the is t layer Plantar arteries and nerves lie within ist and 2nd layer Tendons of flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus lie within 2nd layer Flexor hallucis brevis muscle lies within 3rd layer Tendons of tibialis posterior and fibularis longus muscle lie within 4th layer\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 65 Years old woman presents with a painful shoulder after falling on the ground. On examination and rotator cuff assessment she is unable to perform the Lift Off test. Which rotator cuff muscle has been most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | . | Empty | In this test, the patient is tested at 90 degrees elevationin the |  |\n> | Supraspinatus |  | plane and full internal rotation and pronation of the | thumbs |\n> |  | pointing downwards the patient resists downward pressure exerted by the |  |  |\n> |  | examiner at the patients elbow or wrist |  |  |\n> |  | Infraspinatus test: Patient arm fully adducted with elbow flexed to 90 degrees. |  |  |\n> |  | The examiner will apply an internal rotation force along the |  | forearm |\n> | Infraspinatus |  | the patient resists. The test is positive if the patient experiences pain or |  |\n> |  | weakness. |  |  |\n> |  | Hornblower test: The examiner places the patient's arm to 90 degreesin the scapular |  |  |\n> |  | plane and flexes the elbowto 90 degrees. The patient is then asked to externally rotate |  |  |\n> | Teres minor | against resistance. The testis positive if the patient is unable to perform external |  |  |\n> |  | rotation. |  |  |\n> |  | Lift-off test: Position the patient in full medial rotation with the dorsum of the |  |  |\n> |  | hand on the lower back. Ask the patient to lift their hand away from their back |  |  |\n> | Subscapularis | against resistance. Testis positive if patient has pain or weakness. |  |  |\n\n- A. Teres minor\n- B. Teres major\n- C. Infraspinatus\n- D. Supraspinatus\n- **E. Subscapularis** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAssessment of rotator cuff muscles\n\nNote: Supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle and helps to initiate abduction from 0 degree to 15 degrees (RECALL) and then assist the deltoid with continued abduction.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 3O years old male with history of trauma came into the ED with abdominal pain. Which of the following vessels are compromised in rectus sheath hematoma?\n\n- A. Celiac artery\n- B. Superior mesenteric\n- C. Gastroepiploic vessels\n- **D. Inferior epigastric vessel** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Inferior mesenteric\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRectus sheath encloses rectus abdominis, pyramidalis muscle (RECALL), inferior and superior epigastric vessels, and termination of intercostal nerves T7-T11.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nRectus sheath: The rectus sheath is formed by th e aponeuroses of the three flat muscles and encloses the rectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles. It has an anterior and posterior wall for most o f its length.\n\nAnterior wall: Formed by the aponeuroses of the external oblique, and of half of the internal oblique.\n\nPosterior wall: Half of the aponeuroses of the internal oblique and of the transversus abdominis. Approximately midway between the umbilicus a nd the pubic symphysis, all the aponeuroses move to the anterior wall of the rectus sheath. At this point, there is no posterior wall to the sheath; the rectus abdominis is in direct contact with the transversalis fascia. Point, where the posterior layer of the rectus sheath ends, is the arcuate line. Contents of the rectus sheath Rectus abdominus and the pyramidalis muscle The superior and inferior epigastric vessels The termination of intercostal nerves T7-T11 The 12th thoracic nerve and the accompanying vessels\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 60 Years old male known case of Diabetes mellitus presents into the ED with complaint of eye pain that's worsened by eye movement and loss of color vision. You suspect optic neuritis which one of the following conditions is strongly associated with it?\n\n- A. Hyperglycemia\n- B. Sarcoidosis\n- C. Crohn's disease\n- D. Diabetes Mellitus\n- **E. Multiple sclerosis** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOptic neuritis is an inflammatory demyelinationof the optic nerve, and is strongly associated with multiple sclerosis.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nThe probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true;\n\n- A. Power of statistical test\n- B. Type 2 error\n- C. Type 3 error\n- **D. Type 1 error** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Level of significance\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn statistical hypothesis testing, a type1 error is th e mistaken rejection of an actually true null hypothesis (also known as false positive" finding or conclusion; example: innocent person is convicted").\n\nWhile type 2 error is the failure to reject the null hypothesis that is actually false (also known as false negative. Finding or conclusion example: guilty person is not convicted) much of statistical theory revolves around the minimization of one or both of these errors, though the complete elimination of either is a statistical impossibility if the outcome is not determined by a known, observablecasual process. By selecting low threshold (cut-of f) value and modifying the alpha level, the qualityof hypothesis test can be increased. The knowledge of type 1 error and type 2 error is widely used i n medical science, biometrics and computer science.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students about origin and insertion of muscles. Which of the following muscle originate from the anterior superior iliac spine?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | lliac crest |  | \\ |  |  |\n> | Abdominal muscles |  |  |  |  |  |\n> | Anterior superior |  | y | ae |  |  |\n> |  | iliac spine |  |  |  |  |\n> |  | Sartorius |  |  |  |  |\n> | Anterior inferior |  |  | a |  |  |\n> |  | iliac spine |  |  |  |  |\n> | Rectus femoris |  |  |  | ~ |  |\n> |  |  | > |  |  | corner |\n> |  |  |  |  |  | pubic symphysis |\n> |  |  |  |  | | | Rectus |\n> |  |  |  | \\ |  | abdominus |\n> |  |  |  | SS -- schial tuberosity |  |  |\n> |  |  | \\ |  | Hamstrings |  |\n> |  |  |  | Lesser trochanter |  |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. Sartorius** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Psoas major\n- C. Rectus femoris\n- D. Iliacus\n- E. Pectineus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOrigin of most important lower limb muscles\n\nNote: Greater trochanter of femur is site of insertion of gluteus Medius, gluteus minims and piriformis (RECALL)\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 38:** 1-D, 2-E, 3-E, 4-D, 5-B, 6-D, 7-D, 8-C, 9-C, 10-C, 11-E, 12-D, 13-E, 14-D, 15-A\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
e46864f0-8274-4e85-8ef8-0f4adb110c9c	ab812750-760a-4eae-8a6c-e5d4b3f1520b	central-nervous-system-overview	Central Nervous System - Overview	article	Central Nervous system overview\nThe central nervous system consists of the brain and the spinal\ncord. The peripheral nervous system consists of the extensions\nof neural structures beyond the central nervous system and\nincludes somatic and autonomic divisions.\nThe brain is composed of 3 main structural divisions:\nThe cerebrum (Cerebral hemispheres and diencephalon)\nThe brainstem\nThe cerebellum\nNOTE:  At the base of the brain is the brainstem, which extends\nfrom the upper cervical spinal cord to the diencephalon of the\ncerebrum. The brainstem is divided into the medulla, pons, and\nmidbrain. Posterior to the brainstem lies the cerebellum.\nSpinal cord:\nThe spinal cord is housed in the vertebral canal. It is\ncontinuation of medulla oblongata and terminates as the\nconus medullaris at the second lumbar vertebrae of adults.\nAt birth, the conus medullaris lies at L3. By the age of 21, it\nsits at L1/L2, thus the spinal cord only occupies about 2/3s of\nthe space available to it in the vertebral canal.\nBelow the conus medullaris is cauda equina which lies\nbelow L1 from L2 to sacrum\nInternal anatomy:\nA transverse section of the spinal cord reveals a distinct\n"butterfly" pattern of dark, inner grey" matter surrounded by\nthe light white matter.\nThe white matter contains the ascending and descending\npathways connecting the brain and spinal cord.\nThe grey matter is rich in nerve cell bodies which are divided\ninto four main columns.\n\nAnterior horn (Ventral horn): largely contains motor neurons\nthat exit the spinal cord to innervate skeletal muscle.\nPosterior division (Dorsal horn): Receive somatosensory\ninformation from the body, which is then transmitted via the\nascending pathways, to the brain.\nIntermediate horn: Innervate visceral and pelvic organs.\nLateral horn: Innervate visceral and pelvic organs.\nSpinal nerve roots:\nThe spinal nerves are formed by the union of posterior and\nanterior roots.\nSpinal rootlets emerge from the spinal cord in the\nsubarachnoid space and amalgamate shortly afterwards into\nroots.\nAnterior roots: Containing motor nerve fibres\nposterior roots: containing the processes of sensory neurons\nthen emerge from their individual intervertebral foramina.\nAfter invaginating the dura, the roots combine into mixed\nspinal nerves which divide into a posterior ramus innervating\nintrinsic back muscles and an associated strip of skin on the\nback.\nAnterior ramus (innervating the remaining muscles and skin\nof the trunk and limbs and the visceral organs).\nVasculature of spinal cord:\nThe spinal cord is supplied primarily by branches of the\nvertebral artery:\nThe single anterior spinal artery is formed from the left and\nright vertebral arteries: Supplies the anterior two-thirds of the\nspinal cord, including the anterior and lateral horns and the\nanterior and lateral columns.\nThe paired posterior spinal arteries arise from either the\nposterior inferior cerebellar artery or directly from the\nvertebral artery and supply the posterior third of the spinal\ncord including the posterior horns and columns.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
4403dbbf-0848-4431-9a1e-0e8ef81b1893	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	conducting-system-of-heart	Conducting System of Heart	article	Conducting system of heart\nSpecial system of heart that generates rhythmical impulses\nand conducts these impulses through heart, is called excitatory\nand conductive system of heart. Consists of:\nSA node\nInternodal pathway\nAV node (slowest conducting fiber)\nAV bundle (bundle of His)\nRight and Left branches of Purkinje\nConductive Pathway of Heart:\nSA node initiates the cardiac muscle (pacemaker) →  Internodal\nfibers and AV node (delay impulse from the atria to ventricle)\n→  AV bundle branches →  Purkinje fibers (from apex to\nventricles).\nThe sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker located\nin Right atrium. The SAN will normally discharge at a rate of\n60-100 bpm. If the impulse generated in the Sa node is\nblocked, a group of cells further down the heart will become\nits pacemaker.\nThe atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary pacemaker\nlocated between right atrium and ventricle near the atrial\nseptum, and will normally discharge at about 40-60 beats\nper minute\nPurkinje fibers produce spontaneous action potential at rate\nof 20-40 bpm.\nNote: AV nodal abnormality is responsible for prolonged PR\ninterval (1st degree heart block)	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
