cb256e63-38f7-44af-bfb1-3b54f88111d7	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-viii-vestibulocochlear-nerve	Cranial Nerve Viii_ Vestibulocochlear Nerve	article	Vestibulocochlear Nerve (CN VIII):\nComprised of two vestibular and cochlear portions, emerges\nfrom the brainstem at the cerebellopontine angle, enters\ninternal acoustic meatus of temporal bone.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Viii_ Vestibulocochlear Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-viii-vestibulocochlear-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Viii_ Vestibulocochlear Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
c577e6d8-a30d-4293-8df8-f1a2fe731cf8	8068b12b-317b-4499-8871-45eb82ce7703	homeostasis	Homeostasis	article	Homeostasis\nMaintenance of steady states in body by coordinated\nphysiologic mechanisms.\n\n### 1: Extracellular fluid: Internal environment in body in which the\n\ncells live and the fluid outside the cell\nDivisions of ECF\nPlasma: Part of ECF present in blood constitute ¼ OF ECF\nInterstitial fluid: Part of ECF present in spaces between cells\nconstitute ¾ of ECF.\n\n### 2: Intracellular fluid: Fluid inside the cells\n\nAverage 70 kg adult has approximately 40 liters of water.\nAround  is ICF\nTotal body water= 0.6 x bodyweight\nECF 0.2X Body weight\nICF 0.4X Body weight\nElectrolytes distribution in ECF and\nICF\n\n![Homeostasis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/homeostasis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Homeostasis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nOperating mechanism for\nhomeostasis:\n\n### 1: Negative feedback:\n\nComponents: Regulated variable, Sensor, Controller, and\nEffector.\nA regulated variable is sensed, information is fed back to the\ncontroller, and the effector acts to oppose change (hence the\nterm negative) Control most systems of body."\nExamples:  Osmoregulation, regulation of blood, pressure,\ntemperature regulation, regulation of CO2 in ECF.\n\n### 2: Positive feedback:\n\nPromotes changes in one direction leading amplified effect.\nCause vicious cycle and death, sometimes it can be useful.\nExamples:\nChildbirth (During labor): The release of oxytocin from the\nposterior gland during childbirth and labor\nBlood clotting.\nGeneration of nerve signals.\nSensation of needing to urinate.\nFollicular phase of menstrual cycle\n\n![Homeostasis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/homeostasis/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Homeostasis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
ee9b28da-b35d-4150-8550-e7477377bd55	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	reflex-control-of-heart	Reflex Control of Heart	article	Reflex control of heart by\nautonomic nervous system\nSympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and strength,\nthereby increasing cardiac pumping. Sympathetic\nstimulation causes vasoconstriction and vasoconstriction\nincreases blood pressure by increasing total peripheral\nresistance (TPR). If resistance increases, then more pressure is\nneeded to keep blood moving.\nParasympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate and\nstrength, thereby decreasing cardiac pumping.	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d0d1409f-b9e9-448c-8745-b9954e96109d	9db23e52-af3e-4f01-9ef3-48e9c44fed87	anti-epileptic-drugs	Anti - Epileptic Drugs	article	Anti-epileptic drugs\nCarbamazepine, sodium valproate\nand phenytoin\nMechanism of action: Carbamazepine, sodium valproate\nand phenytoin all three drugs act by inhibiting Voltage gated\nNa+ channels in neuronal cells.\nSodium valproate: May also enhance the inhibitory effects of\ngamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), an\nimportant neurotransmitter that reduces neuronal\nexcitability.\nAntiepileptic drugs in pregnancy: (Adverse features)\nCarbamazepine: Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn and\nNeural tube defects.\nPhenytoin: Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn., Risk of\ncongenital malformations e.g. cleft lip, hypospadias and\ncardiovascular defects.\n\n![Anti - Epileptic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/anti-epileptic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Epileptic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nSodium valproate: Risk of major congenital malformations e.g.\nneural tube, cardiac, craniofacial and limb defects.\nAntiepileptic drug interaction:\nAll the cytochrome P450 enzyme inducer drugs which also\ninclude antiepileptics phenytoin and carbamazepine increase\nrate of metabolism and hepatic clearance of\nconcurrently administrated drugs which typically results in\ndecreased pharmacologic effects. Enzyme inducer drugs\nreduce the effectiveness of Oral contraceptives pills taken in\npregnancy and thus pregnancy happens.\nNote: Sodium valproate is metabolized in liver all the enzyme\ninhibitor drugs decrease rate of metabolism of valproate and\ntoxicity occur.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
1080d226-d008-4ee4-9923-7bd5fb942aaa	4e8e8cf6-923c-4953-ab05-58defec7082e	lymphocytes	Lymphocytes	article	Types of lymphocytes\n\n![Lymphocytes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/lymphocytes/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Lymphocytes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
00920a7f-42d0-4982-b276-18ab2c047360	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-01	Quiz 01: Emergency Resuscitation, Cranial Nerves & Infection Microbiology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 01\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 45-year-old male was found unconscious at home and was brought into the ED by paramedics. On initial assessment at triage, his BM was 1.5mmol/l. According to hypoglycemia management guidelines what initial treatment should be given in unconscious patient:\n\n- A. 5% dextrose orally\n- B. Wait for iv-line access and give iv 10 % dextrose\n- C. 70 to 80 ml of 50% glucose\n- D. IM Pabrinex\n- **E. 1mg IM glucagon** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIM glucagon should be given initially in an unconscious patient while those patients who are alert and able to swallow oral dextrose should be given\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 25 Years old male presents to the ED with complaints of fever, photophobia, and neck stiffness. You suspect meningitis. Which of the following is the feature of Neisseria meningitis?\n\n- A. Gram +Ve Cocco bacilli\n- **B. Gram -Ve diplococci** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Gram +Ve rod\n- D. Gram -Ve rod\n- E. Gram +Ve diplococci\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeisseria meningitis is gram negative encapsulated diplococci causing Neisseria meningitis and meningococcal sepsis\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 45-year-old patient presents to the ED with complaints of double vision, especially when looking down. The patient states that their symptoms improve when they tilt their head to the right. The rest of the physical examination reveals weakness in downward gaze of the left eye. Which cranial nerve is most likely affected in this patient?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Cranial Nerve |  | Trochlear Nerve (CN IV) |\n> |  | Idiopathic (Most common), trauma, multiple sclerosis and mid |  |\n> | Cause of injury |  | . |\n> |  |  | brain lesion |\n> |  | Motor: superior oblique muscle of eye (intorsion, depression and |  |\n> | Function |  |  |\n> |  |  | abduction of eye) |\n> | Assessment | Eye movements assessed with abducent and oculomotor nerve. |  |\n> |  | vertical diplopia, exacerbated when looking downwards and |  |\n> |  | inwards (such as when reading or walking down the stairs)., eye is |  |\n> | Clinical effects of injury | extorted and may be elevated (patient head tilts to opposite side to |  |\n> |  |  | compensate) |\n\n- A. Oculomotor nerve\n- **B. Trochlear nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Optic nerve\n- D. Abducent nerve\n- E. Trigeminal nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe trochlear nerve innervates the superior oblique muscle, which helps control downward and inward movement of the eye. Damage to this nerve results in difficulty with downward gaze, especially when looking at near objects, causing vertical diplopia. The compensatory head tilt in this case, to the right helps align the eyes and reduce the diplopia. Trochlear Nerve: (High Yield)\n\nsmallest cranial nerve (by number of axons) yet has the longest intracranial course. Emerges from posterior aspect of midbrain, travels through lateral aspect of cavernous sinus, exits skull through superior orbital fissure.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 25 years old male patient with a history of trauma presents into the ED with complaint of pain in epigastrium radiating to the back. Which of the following is the segmental innervation of the pancreas?\n\n- A. T4-T8\n- B. T4 -T10\n- C. L2-L4\n- D. L1-L2\n- **E. T6 -T10** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInnervations:\n\nThe segmental level of innervation of pancreas= T6- T10 Gallbladder=phrenic(C3-C5)\n\nAscending and transverse colon= fibers travel through lesser splanchnic nerve to =T10-T11 spinal cord segment Descending and sigmoid colon=pain fibers travel through lumber splanchnic nerve to= L1 and L2 spinal segments Ureter=Sympathetic supply from inferior hypogastric plexus parasympathetic supply from pelvic splanchnic nerve(S2-S4)\n\nVisceral afferent fibers follow sympathetic fibers to enter in spinal cord via =T10-L1 nerve fibers Bladder= Efferent parasympathetic fibers from pelvic splanchnic nerve=S2 to S4 ovary=sympathetic fibers of ovarian and lumber splanchnic nerve to cell bodies in the = T10-T12 and L1 spinal sensory ganglion Testes= visceral afferent fibers follow the sympathetic fibers to=T10-L1 Rectum= visceral afferent fibers follow parasympathetic supply to =S2-S4 sensory ganglia.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 43 years old undergone surgery of posterior part of the neck after which he lost sensation around the clavicle. Which of the following dermatome is best localized on the supraclavicular region?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | C2 | Occipital Protuberance |  |\n> | C3 | Supraclavicular Fossa at mid clavicular line |  |\n> | C4 | Acromioclavicular Joint |  |\n> | C5 | Lateral Antecubital Fossa |  |\n> | C6 |  | Thumb |\n> | C7 |  | Middle Finger |\n> | C8 |  | Little Finger |\n> | T1 | Medial Antecubital Fossa |  |\n> | T2 |  | Apex of Axilla |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | C6 | Wrist extension |\n> | C7 | Elbow extension |\n> | C8 | Finger flexion |\n> | T1 | Finger abduction |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Supinator | C6 --C7 | Brachioradialis |\n> | Triceps | C7-C8 | Triceps brachii |\n\n- A. T2\n- B. C4\n- C. C5\n- **D. C3** (Correct Answer)\n- E. C2\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nDermatomes of Upper Limb:\n\nDermatome:A dermatome is defined as 'a strip of skin that is innervated by a single spinal nerve and clinically very important and help in diagnosis of spinal cord injury\n\nMyotomes and associated movements:\n\nMyotome: Group of muscles innervated by a single spinal nerve Reflexes of upper Limb:\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 32 Years old lady came into the ED with Complaint of pain in the foot. She gives history of taking certain antibiotics prescribed by her GP. Which of the following antibiotics class are associated with tendon injury?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | * Co-Amoxiclav |  |  Obstructive jaundice or cholestatic jaundice |\n> | > |  | Extra pyramidal symptoms and parkinsonian like |\n> |  |  | symptoms |\n> | > | Druss |  degrees Dry mouth, Urinary retention and |\n> | Loop diuretics (Furosemide) and |  |  Hypokalemia, Metabolic alkalosis and hyperuricemia. In |\n> | thiazide diuretics |  | addition, Thiazides causes hyperglycemia too. |\n> |  | diuretics (Spironolactone) | Hyperkalemia, Metabolic acidosis and gynecomastia |\n> |  Ace inhibitors (Captopril) |  | Persistent dry cough |\n> | + Cv channel lotr |  |  |\n> | Aspirin |  | e Reyes syndrome |\n> | > |  |  degrees Respiratory alkalosis (Hyperventilation initially) later on |\n> |  |  | metabolic acidosis |\n> | > |  |  degrees Cushing syndrome, Myopathy and |\n> |  |  |  degrees Leukocytosis (Commonly), Diabetes insipidus, tremor |\n> |  |  | Hypothyroidism, Hyperreflexia and muscle twitch |\n> | Traumadol |  | * Constipation, Vomiting |\n> |  Fluroquinolones (Ciprofloxacin) |  |  Tendon rupture, Torsade's de pointes |\n> |  degrees |  | Nephrotoxic, Ototoxic and neurotoxic |\n> | Vancomycin |  | Red man syndrome, Nephrotoxic and Ototoxic |\n> |  Allopurinol |  | Steven Johnson syndrome |\n> |  Tetracyclines |  | Teeth discoloration |\n> |  Beta-2 Agonist (Salbutamol) |  | Hypokalemia |\n> | Nitrous oxide |  |  Vitamin B12 Deficiency and megaloblastic anemia |\n> |  degrees |  | Clostridium difficle colitis |\n> | Isoniazid |  |  Peripheral neuropathy |\n> | Macrolides (Erythromycin), |  |  |\n> | Aminoglycosides and Quinolones |  | QT interval prolongation leads to Torsades de pointes |\n> | (Cipro) |  |  |\n> | Suxamethonium |  |  Hyperkalemia |\n> | Mefenamic acid |  |  Haemolytic anemia |\n\n- A. Macrolides\n- B. Aminoglycosides\n- C. Cephalosporins\n- **D. Quinolones** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Tetracyclines\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTendon damage and QT interval prolongation both are the common side effects of quinolones QT prolongation leads to Torsades de pointes\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nSummary Of Drugs And Their Side Effects\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nWhich of the following diet is a rich source of calcium?\n\n- A. Broccoli\n- B. Sun flower\n- C. Tomato\n- **D. Soyabean** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Broccoli\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRcem key: soyabean is a rich source of calcium\n\nNote: Dairy products which include cheese, leafy vegetables, and Soybean is rich in calcium. Almond is also rich in calcium but if both almond and cheese are in option choose dairy product cheese because cheese has more calcium than almond per 100gm.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 23 years old pregnant woman from southern Vietnam came into the ED with complaint of fever with chills for 2 days. You suspect malaria. What is the antimalarial drug of choice for this patient?\n\n- A. Primaquine\n- B. Chloroquine\n- C. Doxycycline\n- **D. Quinine** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Clindamycin\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nQuinine is the antimalarial drug of choice in pregnancy; however, chloroquine should be considered in non-falciparum malaria while those areas where chloroquine is resistant especially southern Vietnam Quinine is best given in pregnancy\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 45-year-old male sustained a lacerated wound on his right knee 2 days back and came into ED with a Complaint of Severe pain in his right knee associated with fever and swelling of the joint. Gram staining revealed gram +ve cocci in clusters what is the single most causative agent in this case?\n\n- A. S.Epidermidis\n- B. Staph pyogenes\n- **C. Staph Aureus** (Correct Answer)\n- D. E- Coli\n- E. Streptococcus\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nStaph aureus is gram +Ve diplococci in clusters causing septic arthritis\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 25 Years old male presents to the ED with complaints of fever, photophobia, neck stiffness, and non-blanching skin rash. What is the most likely Causative agent?\n\n- A. E-Coli\n- B. Hemophilus influenza\n- **C. Neisseria meningitis** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Streptococcus pyogenes\n- E. Streptococcus aureus\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNeisseria meningitidis is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis. Meningococci/Neisseria meningitidis are gram-negative diplococci that cause meningitis and meningococcemia. Symptoms, usually severe, include headache, nausea, vomiting, photophobia, lethargy, rash, multiple organ failure, shock, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Diagnosis is clinical, confirmed by culture\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 45-year-old male sustained lacerated wound on his right knee 2 days back and came into ED with a Complaint of Severe pain in his right knee associated with fever and swelling of joint. You suspect septic arthritis. what is the single most causative agent in this case?\n\n- **A. Staph Aureus** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Streptococcus\n- C. E- Coli\n- D. S.Epidermidis\n- E. Staph pyogenes\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSeptic arthritis is an infection of any kind that finds its way into joint space, it is a most rapid and destructive joint disease, acute onset of monoarticular arthritis with pain swelling, and warmth, the knee is the most common site others include the hip, wrist, shoulder, and ankle.\n\nthe most common type of bacteria that causes septic arthritis is called staphylococcus aureus. others include streptococcus, gram-negative rods,\n\nNeisseria Gonorrhea causes septic arthritis in sexually active young adults (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 32 Years old male sustained a dirty wound on his leg while running on the ground. Which of the following pathogen is conditional causing infection after puncture wounds?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Obligate: Always associated with disease and | HIV, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, |\n> | cannot survive outside of human body | Treponema pallidum, Neisseria gonorrhoeae |\n> | Conditional: Cause disease if certain conditions |  |\n> | are met e.g., Neisseria meningitidis, a normal | Bacteroides fragilis, Neisseria meningitidis, |\n> | nasopharynx commensal, may cause meningitis by Staphylococcus aureus |  |\n> | direct or haematogenous spread. |  |\n> | Cause | Candida albicans, Pneumocystis jiroveci, |\n> | defense system is compromised |  |\n> |  | Pseudomonas aeruginosa |\n> | (Immunocompromised patients) |  |\n\n- A. Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- B. Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- C. Treponema pallidum\n- **D. Staphylococcus aureus** (Correct Answer)\n- E. E.Coli\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPathogen and Types: (High Yield)\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 25-year-old male presents to the emergency department after being struck under the chin during a basketball game. He reports severe pain in front of his ears and has jaw locked in closed position. On physical examination, his jaw appears to be displaced posteriorly. Which ligament is most likely compromised due to this injury?\n\n- A. Stylomandibular ligament\n- B. Sphenomandibular ligament\n- C. Lateral pterygoid muscle\n- **D. Temporomandibular ligament** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Sternoclavicular ligament\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Temporomandibular joint (TMJ):\n\nThe temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is a hinge type synovial joint that connects the mandible to the temporal bone of the skull. It is responsible for the movement of the jaw, allowing functions such as chewing, speaking, and yawning.\n\nMovements of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) Hinge movement: Opening and closing the mouth. Gliding movement: Forward (protrusion) and backward (retrusion) movements of the jaw. Lateral movements: Side-to-side jaw motion. Muscles Involved:\n\nTemporalis: Elevates and retracts the jaw.\n\nMasseter: Elevates the jaw.\n\nMedial and Lateral Pterygoid: Assist in side-to-side movements and protrusion.\n\nLigaments of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ):\n\nLateral capsular (Temporomandibular) ligament: Prevents posterior and inferior dislocation of the TMJ. It is the strongest ligament in the joint and reinforces the lateral side of the joint capsule. Sphenomandibular ligament originates from the sphenoid spine, and attaches to the mandible. Acts as a passive support to the mandible, especially when the mouth is wide open.\n\nStylomandibular ligament: Provides stability and limits excessive jaw movements. Overstretching can result in hypermobility of the jaw, increasing the risk of dislocation.\n\nInnervation:The TMJ is primarily innervated by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (V3).\n\nClinical pearls:\n\nTemporomandibular joint (TMJ) Dislocation:\n\nAnterior dislocation:The most common form, where the condyle slips forward over the articular eminence, locking the mouth open. Trauma or excessive opening of the mouth (e.g., yawning, dental procedures) can stretch or tear ligaments, resulting in dislocation.\n\nPosterior dislocation:Posterior temporomandibular joint (TMJ) dislocation is a rare condition compared to anterior dislocation. In this type of dislocation, the mandibular condyle is displaced backward, towards the external auditory canal and the base of the skull. Jaw locked in a closed position, In contrast to anterior dislocation, Patients are unable to close their mouths.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students in ED and topic of discussion is blood supply of the heart. Which structure is most likely affected by occlusion of right coronary artery?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Left circumflex branch. |  Left atrium and left ventricle |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Affected myocardial area | Occluded coronary | Arte |\n\n- A. Left atrium\n- B. Bundles of His\n- **C. SA node** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Interventricular septum\n- E. Left ventricle\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCardiac blood supply:\n\n1: The Right coronary artery supplies:\n\nThe right atrium and most of right ventricle The sinoatrial and atrioventricular node Posterior interventricular artery, also known as the posterior descending artery (PDA), is a branch of the right coronary artery supply posterior 1/3 of interventricular septum and posterior aspect of right and left ventricle. Most common artery infracted in posterior wall MI\n\nNote: Occlusion of right coronary artery can result into ischemia of SA and AV node and subsequently bradycardia or heart block (Recall)\n\n2: The left coronary artery supplies via main branches below:\n\nAnterior interventricular branch (LAD)\n\nLCX\n\nHigh yield:\n\nLocalization of lesion in ST elevation MI and occluded coronary artery. Only remember myocardial area and occluded coronary artery.\n\nEasy to remember:\n\nSeptal and Anterior wall MI: LAD (Anterior interventricular branch) occluded.\n\nLateral wall MI: LCX occluded.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 65-year-old male presents to the ED with complaints of frequent urination, especially at night, difficulty initiating urination, and a sensation of incomplete bladder emptying. On physical examination, his prostate is enlarged, smooth, and non-tender. He is diagnosed with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). Which of the following medications can help alleviate bladder spasms and provide symptom relief in this patient?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Finasteride\n- B. Tamsulosin\n- **C. Hyoscine** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Doxazosin\n- E. Silodosin\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHyoscine, an anticholinergic agent, is effective in relaxing bladder smooth muscle, reducing spasms, and relieving urinary urgency often associated with BPH.\n\nTamsulosin an alpha-blocker is more commonly used to relieve obstruction and increase urine flow. finasteride (a 5-alpha reductase inhibitor) is more commonly used to reduce prostate size and relieve obstruction.\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nSide Effects of Anti-Cholinergic (Muscarinic) agent:\n\nMnemonic: "BCDU" Blurred Vision Constipation Dry Mouth Urinary Retention\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 1:** 1-E, 2-B, 3-B, 4-E, 5-D, 6-D, 7-D, 8-D, 9-C, 10-C, 11-A, 12-D, 13-D, 14-C, 15-C\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
bae34473-8e14-450b-809e-1ba2db27731a	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-02	Quiz 02: Abdominal Viscera, Inguinal Anatomy & Autonomic Neuro-Ophthalmology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 02\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students' lymphatic system and lymph nodes of the body. You ask medical students lymphatics of glans penis drain primarily to which of the following lymph nodes?\n\n- A. Internal iliac nodes\n- B. External iliac nodes\n- C. Superficial inguinal nodes\n- **D. Deep inguinal node** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Paraaortic lymph nodes\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLymphatics from glans penis and penile urethra drain primarily into the deep inguinal nodes Lymphatics from penile skin and prepuce drain into superficial inguinal nodes (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 70-year-old male is scheduled to undergo repair of an inguinal hernia that is easily reduced by pushing the abdominal contents back through the external ring. Direct inguinal hernia is due to weakness in which of the following layer?\n\n- A. External oblique aponeurosis\n- B. Deep inguinal ring\n- C. lacunar ligament\n- **D. Transversalis fascia** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Transversus abdominis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDirect inguinal hernia is common in elderly bulgethrough weakened fascia of abdominal wall (Transversalis fascia)) directly behind superficial inguinal ring and medial to the inferior epigastric vessels (Recall)\n\nIndirect inguinal hernia enters inguinal canal at the deep inguinal ring and lateral to inferior epigastric vessels\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 25-year-old male with history of dysphagia to solid foods came with complaint of nausea and vomiting. The esophagus passes through diaphragm at which vertebral level?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Opening | Vertebra level | Contents |\n> |  |  | Esophagus, anterior and |\n> | Esophageal opening | T-10 (Recall) | posterior vagal trunks |\n> |  |  | Aorta, thoracic duct (RECALL), |\n> | Aortic opening |  | Azygous and hemiazygos vein |\n> |  |  | Inferior vena cava (RECALL), |\n> | Caval opening | T-8 | Right phrenic nerve (RECALL) |\n\n- A. L2\n- **B. T10** (Correct Answer)\n- C. T11\n- D. T8\n- E. T12\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 10-year-old male presents to the ED with abdominal pain, vomiting and bleeding per rectum. After initial assessment and imaging is suggestive of Meckel's diverticulum. The blood supply of Meckel's diverticulum is from which of the following:\n\n- A. Inferior mesenteric artery\n- **B. Superior mesenteric artery** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery\n- D. Gastroduodenal artery\n- E. Celiac trunk\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nRich blood supply to Meckle's diverticulum is from Superior mesenteric artery Proximal to the major duodenal papilla - supplied by the gastroduodenal artery (branch of the common hepatic artery from the coeliac trunk Distal to the major duodenal papilla - supplied by the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (branch of a superior mesenteric artery)\n\nMeckel's Diverticulum: (High Yield) True diverticula of all 3 layers of the small intestines Caused by incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct.\n\nRule of 2s: 2 years old, 2 ft from ileocecal valve, 2 in long, 2% of population Usually, asymptomatic Painless bleeding due to an ulcer caused by heterotopic gastric tissue most common presentation in children.\n\nObstruction is caused by intussusception, volvulus, and hernia most common presentation in adults.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 21-year-old male patient brought into the ED after road traffic collision. After initial assessment and imaging showed damage to the L1 nerve root. Which of the following muscles are most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | | Spinal Segment | Motor Function | Sensory Function |\n> | lliohypogastric | 1 | Internal oblique and |  |\n> | nerve |  | Transversus abdominis |  |\n> |  |  |  | aspect of gluteal region |\n> |  |  |  | Skin in upper medial |\n> | llioinguinal nerve | L1 | Internal oblique and | thigh, skin of base of |\n> |  |  | Transversus abdominis | penis, anterior scrotum |\n> |  |  |  | mons pubis, and |\n> |  |  |  | labium majus |\n\n- A. Rectus abdominis and transverse abdominis\n- B. Gluteus maximus and gluteus minimus\n- C. External oblique and transversus abdominis\n- **D. Internal oblique and transversus abdominis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Internal and external oblique\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 30 years old man is brought into the Ed by paramedics with a lacerated wound on his abdomen. which of the following Structures is most likely affected by a tear of the superior mesenteric artery?\n\n- **A. Ascending and transverse colon** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Transverse colon only\n- C. Transverse and descending colon\n- D. Sigmoid colon\n- E. Only ascending colon\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgutstructures from the second part of duodenum including the caecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. Inferior mesenteric artery supplies the descending colon and sigmoid colon.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 24 years old female who is 3 months pregnant came into the ED with complaint of lower abdominal pain. You suspect ectopic pregnancy. Ectopic pregnancy occurs most commonly in which part of uterine tube?\n\n- **A. Ampulla** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Infundibulum\n- C. Isthmus\n- D. Cervix\n- E. Fimbriae\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nEctopic pregnancies most commonly occur in ampulla.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\n30 years old man is brought into the Ed by paramedics with a gunshot wound on his abdomen. On laparotomy retroperitoneal hemorrhage found. Which of the following structures is the most likely source of bleeding?\n\n- A. Liver\n- B. Sigmoid colon\n- **C. Suprarenal glands** (Correct Answer)\n- D. First part of duodenum\n- E. Spleen\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSADPUCKER useful mnemonic to remember retroperitoneal organs\n\nS: Suprarenal (adrenal) gland A: aorta/IVC D: duodenum (Second and third part) P: pancreas (except tail)\n\nU: ureters C: colon (ascending and descending) K: kidneys E: (o)esophagus R: rectum\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 65-year-old male patient presents with symptoms of weight loss, abdominal pain, and nausea. Imaging studies suggest a tumor in the pylorus of the stomach. If the cancer spreads anteroinferiorly, which structure is most likely to be affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Superior = Oesophagus and left dome of the diaphragm |  |\n> |  | Diaphragm, greater omentum, anterior abdominal wall, left lobe of liver and |\n> | Anterior |  |\n> |  | gall bladder. |\n> | Posterior | Lesser sac, pancreas, left kidney, left adrenal gland, spleen, splenic artery, |\n> |  | transverse mesocolon. |\n\n- A. Transverse mesocolon\n- B. Liver\n- C. Pancreas\n- D. Lessar Sac\n- **E. Greater omentum** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe greater omentum is located anteroinferior to the pylorus, making it the most likely structure to be involved in anteroinferior spread of pyloric cancer. The liver is located anterior and superior to the pylorus, the transverse mesocolon is located posterior and inferior, and the pancreas and lesser sac is also posterior to the stomach.\n\nAnatomical relation of stomach\n\nThe stomach lies anterior to the stomach bed\n\nstomach bed formed by:\n\nUseful mnemonic "Dr. S3 kills patients mercilessly." Diaphragm Splenic flexure Suprarenal gland (Left)\n\nSplenic artery Kidney (Left)\n\nPancreas Mesocolon (Transverse)\n\nBlood supply:\n\nThe arterial supply to the stomach is derived from all three branches (left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic branches) of the coeliac trunk.\n\nInnervation:\n\nParasympathetic from anterior and posterior vagal trunk derived from vagus nerve Sympathetic nerve supply: From the T6 through T9 segments of the spinal cord passes to celiac plexus through the Greater splanchnic nerve mediates epigastric and visceral referred pain from gastric ulcer.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nYou are asked to perform ascitic tap on a CLD patient and teach a group of medical students. You ask medical students what is the site to perform ascitic tap.\n\n- A. Left perihepatic space\n- B. Left subphrenic space\n- C. Left lower quadrant\n- D. Right subphrenic space\n- **E. Right perihepatic space** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAscites or hemoperitoneum accumulates in Morrisons pouch or Hepatorenal recess or Right perihepatic space which is lowest space in peritoneal cavity in Supine position Subphrenic or right subphrenic (pus from appendicitis or ruptured duodenal ulcer accumulate here\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 50-year-old male with SVT is treated with adenosine, following the administration, the patient's heart rate begins to slow down. Which phase of the cardiac action potential does the AV nodal delay primarily occur?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Phase 4 | Phase 0 | Phase 1 | Phase 2 | Phase 3 |\n> |  | ECF | Na+ |  | Ca2+ |  |\n> |  | ICF K+ |  | K+ | K+ | K+ |\n> |  |  | Transient K+ channels open and |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | K+ efflux returns TMP to OmV |  |  |  |\n> | Ee 0 |  |  |  |  |  |\n> | S | Rapid Na+ | infl | 2 |  |  |\n> | = |  | fast | Influx of Ca2+ through |  |  |\n> |  | rough open fast |  |  |  |  |\n> | = | Na+ channels |  | L-type Ca2+ channels |  |  |\n> | g |  |  | is electrically balanced |  | Ca2+ channels close but |\n> |  |  | 0 | by K+ efflux through |  | delayed rectifier K+ |\n> | c |  |  | delayed rectifier K+ |  | channels remain open and |\n> | g --50 |  |  | channels |  | return TMP to |\n> | E | Na+, Ca2+ channels |  |  |  |  |\n> | > | closed, open K+ |  |  |  |  |\n> |  | rectifier channels keep |  |  |  |  |\n> |  | TMP stable at -90mV |  |  |  |  |\n> |  |  |  | Time |  |  |\n\n- A. Phase 0\n- B. Phase 3\n- **C. Phase 4** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Phase 2\n- E. Phase 1\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe AV nodal delay primarily occurs during Phase 4: Resting potential of the cardiac action potential. During this phase, adenosine hyperpolarizes the AV nodal cells, making them less excitable and prolonging the delay in conduction. This is particularly important in managing SVT, as it allows more time for the atria to contract and fill the ventricles with blood before they contract.\n\nNote: Physiological AV nodal delay primarily occurs during atrial systole, which allows the atria to contract and fill the ventricles with blood before they contract. This delay occurs in the atrioventricular (AV) node and allows for:\n\nAtrial contraction and ventricular filling.\n\nOptimization of cardiac output.\n\nThe physiological AV delay is approximately 100- 120 milliseconds, allowing for:\n\nAtrial systole to complete Ventricular diastole (filling) to begin This delay is essential for maintaining efficient cardiac function and ensuring proper coordination between atrial and ventricular contractions. Factors influencing physiological AV delay include: Heart rate Age Autonomic nervous system tone (sympathetic and parasympathetic)\n\nElectrolyte balance (e.g., potassium, calcium)\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nRMP of Ventricular myocyte is -90mv depolarization initiates when RMP reaches threshold of -65mv Fast Na channels activate and a rapid Na+ influx depolarizes membrane potential.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 54-year-old man presents with visual impairment. On examination right eye is fixed dilated and deviated downwards and outwards. Which of the following nerve is most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  |  | | uncon |\n> |  |  | Elevation and medial |\n> | Superior rectus | Oculomotor nerve | rotation of eyeball |\n> |  |  | Depression, lateral |\n> | Inferior rectus | Oculomotor nerve | rotation and adduction |\n> | Medial rectus | Oculomotor nerve | Adduction |\n> |  |  | Abduction of eyeball |\n> | Lateral rectus (LR6) | Abducent nerve | (Horizontal diplopia |\n> |  |  | (Recall) |\n> |  |  | Depression, abduction |\n> | Superior oblique (SO4) | Trochlear nerve | and medial rotation of |\n> |  |  | eyeball (Vertical |\n> |  |  | diplopia) |\n> | Inferior oblique | Oculomotor nerve | Elevation, abduction |\n> |  |  | and lateral rotation |\n\n- A. Ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve\n- **B. Oculomotor nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Optic nerve\n- D. Abducent nerve\n- E. Trochlear nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDepressed and abducted eye, diplopia on downwards movement, ptosis, fixed and dilated pupil with loss of accommodation, and abnormal pupillary light reflex are all clinical effects of injury of oculomotor nerve.\n\nNote: Orbital blowout fracture and diplopia on upward gaze is due to entrapment of inferior rectus muscle (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nWhich lung capacity is most likely reduced during the Valsalva maneuver?\n\n- A. Residual Volume\n- **B. Vital Capacity** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Functional residual capacity\n- D. Inspiratory Capacity\n- E. Tidal Volume\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Valsalva maneuver involves forced exhalationagainst a closed airway, which increasesintrathoracic pressure. The increased intrathoracicpressure from the Valsalva maneuver compresses the lungs, reducing the amount of air that can beforcefully exhaled, leading to a reduction in vital capacity. Residual volume remains relatively constant.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nFirst part of duodenum lies at which level of vertebral level?\n\n- A. T8\n- **B. L1** (Correct Answer)\n- C. L2\n- D. T12\n- E. T6\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFirst part of the duodenum extends from the pyloric sphincter of the stomach wraps around head of pancreas in C-shaped and ends at duodenojejunal flexure (Recall)\n\nFirst part lies at vertebral level L1.\n\n2nd part L2-L3 3rd part longest section crossing inferior vena cava and Aorta at level L3 4th part: pass upward on left of abdominal Aorta at L2\n\nClinical pearls: (High Yield)\n\nDuodenal ulcer: erosion of the mucosa in the duodenum most likely to occur in the superior portion of the duodenum.\n\nThe most common causes of duodenal ulcers are Helicobacter pylori infection and chronic NSAID therapy.\n\nDuodenal ulcer most likely erosion of the gastroduodenal artery\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 54-year-old man presents with visual impairment. When testing the pupillary light reaction, the right pupil dilates when the light source moves from the right pupil to the left.\n\nWhich nerve is likely to be abnormal?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Cause of injur | Orbital trauma (penetrating injury to eye), Optic neuritis in multiple sclerosis or secondary |  |\n> | jury | to mumps, optic nerve compression secondary to orbital cellulitis and glaucoma |  |\n> |  | Sensory: Vision |  |\n> | Function | . | . . |\n> |  | Carries sensory information (afferent pathway) of pupillary light reflex. |  |\n> |  | Visual acuity (Snellen chart), color vision (Ishihara plates), pupillary light response, optic |  |\n> |  | disc (fundoscopy), visual fields (tests visual pathway) |  |\n> |  | Loss of vision and color vision in ipsilateral eye |  |\n> |  | In complete transection of the optic nerve: |  |\n> |  |  | direct reflex lost (Same eye) |\n> |  |  | consensual reflex intact (opposite eye) |\n> |  | * Contralateral direct reflex intact |  |\n> | Clinical effects of injury |  |  |\n> |  | * Contralateral consensual reflex lost. |  |\n> |  | In simple when optic nerve is transected sensory information from the retina will not |  |\n> |  | receive in visual cortex and there will be no pupillary constriction and no direct light reflex |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Right optic nerve\n- B. Ophthalmic division of the left trigeminal nerve\n- **C. Left optic nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Left oculomotor nerve\n- E. Right oculomotor nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIn this question examiner is more focused on left eye that means when light source is tested in left eye, left eye do not constrict that means loss of ipsilateral direct pupillary reflex and right pupil dilation refers to loss of contralateral consensual pupillary light reflex.\n\nIn Complete transection of optic nerve:\n\nloss of ipsilateral direct pupillary reflex loss of contralateral consensual pupillary light reflex Optic nerve: (High yield)\n\nTransmit special sensory information from the retina of the eye to the primary visual cortex of the brain.\n\nEnters skull via optic canal of the sphenoid bone\n\nNote: Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD): Seen in optic nerve disease or asymmetrical disease of the retina in which pupils respond differently to light stimuli shone in one eye at a time\n\nswinging light test if there is no RAPD the pupils of both eyes constrict equally regardless of which eye is stimulated by the light.\n\nIn an abnormal swinging-light test if there is a RAPD there is less pupil constriction in the eye with the retinal or optic nerve disease\n\nClinical pearls:\n\nPituitary adenoma: Pituitary gland lies in close proximity to optic chiasm,tumor of pituitary gland compresses optic nerve at chiasm produces visual defect called bitemporal hemianopia\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 2:** 1-D, 2-D, 3-B, 4-B, 5-D, 6-A, 7-A, 8-C, 9-E, 10-E, 11-C, 12-B, 13-B, 14-B, 15-C\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\