931f4b55-782e-4620-a0a9-a90ca047afb9	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	topic-neutropenic-sepsis	Neutropenic Sepsis	quiz	# Neutropenic Sepsis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Neutropenic Sepsis\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Neutropenic Sepsis**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	60	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
6fd8cc06-b0ea-494a-971c-adc3e413e845	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-transfusion-major-hemorrhage	Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 40-year-old trauma victim is receiving massive transfusion protocol (MTP). After receiving 8 units of packed red blood cells, he develops muscle spasms, prolonged QT interval on ECG, and carpopedal spasm upon blood pressure cuff inflation (Trousseau's sign). What acute electrolyte derangement caused this?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/transfusion-major-hemorrhage/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hypocalcaemia caused by citrate binding of ionized calcium\n- **B.** Severe hypernatraemia\n- **C.** Hypokalaemia from cellular shift\n- **D.** Hypermagnesaemia from packed cells\n- **E.** Severe metabolic alkalosis from lactate\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCitrate used as an anticoagulant in stored blood products chelates ionized calcium in the patient's circulation. Rapid massive transfusion overwhelms hepatic metabolism of citrate, resulting in acute severe hypocalcaemia manifested by tetany, Trousseau's sign, QT prolongation, myocardial depression, and worsened coagulopathy. IV 10% calcium chloride or gluconate must be given proactively.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nIn the Emergency Department, an exsanguinating trauma patient with uncrossmatched emergency blood requirements arrives. What blood product should be released immediately for an adult female of unknown age and unknown blood group?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/transfusion-major-hemorrhage/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Group O RhD-positive packed red blood cells\n- **B.** Group O RhD-negative packed red blood cells\n- **C.** Group AB RhD-positive packed red blood cells\n- **D.** Group A RhD-negative packed red blood cells\n- **E.** Wait 45 minutes for full crossmatched blood\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nIn an emergent situation of unknown blood group, Group O RhD-negative red cells are administered to females of childbearing potential (<50 years old) to prevent RhD alloimmunisation and subsequent haemolytic disease of the fetus/newborn. In males and postmenopausal females, Group O RhD-positive cells can be used to preserve scarce O RhD-negative stocks.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nDuring a massive haemorrhage resuscitation, the laboratory reports a fibrinogen level of 0.8 g/L (normal 2.0-4.0 g/L). What is the blood product of choice to rapidly replenish fibrinogen?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/transfusion-major-hemorrhage/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Packed red blood cells\n- **B.** Cryoprecipitate (or Fibrinogen Concentrate)\n- **C.** Platelet concentrate\n- **D.** Unfractionated heparin\n- **E.** Fresh Frozen Plasma 1 unit\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nCryoprecipitate (standard adult dose: 2 pools of 5 units = 10 units total) or Fibrinogen Concentrate is the treatment of choice to rapidly correct hypofibrinogenaemia (<1.5-2.0 g/L in trauma/obstetric haemorrhage). FFP contains fibrinogen but in lower concentrations, requiring excessive volume.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich transfusion-related complication is characterised by acute hypoxemia (PaO2/FiO2 <= 300), bilateral non-cardiogenic pulmonary infiltrates on chest radiograph, and fever developing within 6 hours of transfusion, caused by antileucocyte antibodies in donor plasma?\n\n- **A.** Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO)\n- **B.** Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI)\n- **C.** Haemolytic Transfusion Reaction (HTR)\n- **D.** Transfusion-Associated Graft-versus-Host Disease (TA-GvHD)\n- **E.** Post-Transfusion Purpura\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nTRALI is a non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema occurring within 6 hours of blood product transfusion, mediated by donor anti-HLA or anti-HNA antibodies activating recipient neutrophils in pulmonary capillaries. Unlike TACO, TRALI features normal jugular venous pressure, normal or low BNP, and no signs of fluid overload.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nHow can Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) be clinically differentiated from Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI)?\n\n- **A.** TACO presents with hypertension, elevated JVP, third heart sound (S3), and elevated NT-proBNP, responding rapidly to loop diuretics\n- **B.** TRALI always responds dramatically to IV furosemide\n- **C.** TACO produces high fever and neutropenia\n- **D.** TRALI is caused by red cells only, never plasma\n- **E.** There is no clinical or radiological method to differentiate them\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTACO is hydrostatic/cardiogenic pulmonary oedema from volume overload. Diagnostic hallmarks include hypertension, elevated JVP, peripheral oedema, raised BNP/NT-proBNP, and rapid improvement with IV furosemide and fluid restriction. TRALI is non-cardiogenic permeability oedema with normal or low JVP and hypotension.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	19	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
1fee505a-dcba-40e2-8899-3ef6df5861cc	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-v-trigeminal-nerve	Cranial Nerve V_ Trigeminal Nerve	article	Trigeminal nerve:\nMixed motor and sensory nerve. It is also the largest cranial\nnerve. Arises from several nuclei in the brainstem, exits\nbrainstem from pons It has three main divisions:\n\n### 1: Opthalmic V1\n\n### 2: Maxillary V2\n\n### 3: Mandibular V3\n\n![Cranial Nerve V_ Trigeminal Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-v-trigeminal-nerve/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Cranial Nerve V_ Trigeminal Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
16590cd5-04b6-4aba-beac-72e9c36c32bb	e39442eb-f8da-4063-bb1a-bf03238c5299	cranial-nerve-xii-hypoglossal-nerve	Cranial Nerve Xii_ Hypoglossal Nerve	article	Hypoglossal Nerve:\nPure motor nerve arises from medulla oblongata passes\nlaterally across the posterior cranial fossa, within the\nsubarachnoid space, exits the cranium via the hypoglossal\ncanal of the occipital bone.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Xii_ Hypoglossal Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-xii-hypoglossal-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Xii_ Hypoglossal Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
3081e006-7eb1-411b-86c7-be0c8795dbe1	8068b12b-317b-4499-8871-45eb82ce7703	action-potential	Action Potential	article	Generation of Action potential\nOnce triggered an action potential will travel over the entire\nsurface of an excitable cell and will always have the same\namplitude. An action potential is an all or nothing response;\nbecause the size of the action potential is constant, the\nintensity of the stimulus is coded by the frequency of firing of\na neuron.\nThreshold Potential\nAction potentials are initiated in nerves by activation of\nligand-gated Na+ channels by neurotransmitters. Opening of\nthese Na+ channels results in a small influx of sodium and\ndepolarization of the negative resting membrane potential\n(-70 mV).\nIf the stimulus is sufficiently strong, the resting membrane\ndepolarises enough to reach threshold potential (generally\naround -55 mV), at which point an action potential can\noccur.\nDepolarisation\nVoltage-gated Na+ channels open, causing further\ndepolarisation and activating more voltage-gated Na+\nchannels and there is a sudden and massive sodium influx,\ndriving the cell membrane potential to about +40 mV.\nRepolarisation\nThe spike of the action potential is transient because as the\nmembrane potential becomes positive, the voltage-gated\nNa+ channels inactivate preventing further sodium influx.\nVoltage-gated K+ channels are also activated, causing the K+\npermeability to again be much greater than that for Na+, and\nthe potassium efflux leads to repolarization.\nDelayed closure of these rectifier K+ channels cause a\ntransient hyperpolarization.\nRefractory Period\n\nFollowing the action potential, Na+ channels remain inactive\nfor a time in a period known as the absolute refractory period\nwhere they cannot be opened by any amount of\ndepolarization.\nFollowing this there is a relative refractory period where the\ntemporary hyperpolarization makes the cell more difficult to\ndepolarise and an action potential can be generated only in\nresponse to a larger than normal stimulus.\nThe refractory period limits the frequency at which action\npotentials can be generated, and ensures that, once initiated,\nan action potential can travel only in one direction.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
