037fa169-d744-4bea-9ab2-cecc383b95e1	1a55d389-4f82-4d5b-aef6-803c12e21e68	topic-testicular-torsion	Testicular Torsion	quiz	# Testicular Torsion\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Topic Quiz:** This lesson covers clinical SBA questions based on the topic.\n> Work through each question before reviewing the detailed answer rationale.\n\n## Topic: Testicular Torsion\n\nThis quiz covers high-yield single best answer (SBA) questions for the MRCEM\nexamination based on current UK emergency medicine guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Clinical Objectives\n\nBy the end of this topic quiz you should be able to:\n- Understand key clinical presentations related to **Testicular Torsion**\n- Apply UK evidence-based management guidelines\n- Distinguish between similar clinical conditions\n- Select the single best answer in SBA examination format\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> All questions follow RCEM, NICE, BTS, and Royal College guidelines.\n> Use the Admin panel to add specific SBA questions to this lesson.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	80	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
2d636bf6-8775-42bb-9e0f-f042ec8eb940	8068b12b-317b-4499-8871-45eb82ce7703	teatnus-and-rigor-mortis	Teatnus and Rigor Mortis	article	Tetanus and Rigor mortis\nTetanus: During repeated stimulation of a muscle fiber,\nCalcium is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) more\nquickly than it can be reaccumulated; therefore, the\nintracellular [Ca2+] does not return to resting levels as it would\nafter a single twitch. The increased [Ca2+] allows more cross-\nbridges to form and, therefore, produces increased tension\n(tetanus)\nRigor mortis: The ability of muscles to go in state of\ncontracture formation after death is called rigor mortis.\nThis is due to the loss of ATP that is required to separate the\ncross bridges from actin during contraction.\nIt remains until after 15 to 20 hours, when the muse protein\ndetoriates.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
