1fd6e833-4639-4851-aca6-1a9cf3aac344	a33921a8-de21-4251-b844-facd6afc8a7e	tricyclic-antidepressants	Tricyclic - Antidepressants	article	Tri-cyclic antidepressants:\nMechanism of action: TCA e.g. Amitriptyline is in the tricyclic\nantidepressant (TCA) drug, block the reuptake of\nnorepinephrine and serotonin from the synaptic cleft, thereby\npotentiating the effects of serotonin and norepinephrine on\nthe postsynaptic receptors. The TCAs have also been shown to\ninhibit muscarinic, histamine, and α -adrenergic receptors,\nthereby leading to a host of undesirable side effects.\nTca also block muscarinic, histamine and  α  receptors and has\nnon-specific sedative effects.\nAnticholinergic effects/parasympathetic blocking actions are\ndue to muscarinic receptor blockade (leads to Tachycardia,\nurinary retention, blurred vision, acute angle closure\nglaucoma, confusion, constipation, and dry\nmouth\nSedation (due to histamine receptor blockade)\nPostural hypotension (due to α  adrenergic receptor\nblockade)\ncardiac arrhythmias (due to sodium channel blocker)\nIndications: Used in depression, neuropathic pain and\nnocturnal enuresis.\nClinical features of TCA Toxicity:\nTachycardia - Sinus tachycardia is the most common rhythm\nin TCA overdose. Wide complex tachycardia occurs with severe\nTCA overdose.\nHypotension - As a result of alpha-1 antagonism, hypotension\nis quite common.\nMydriasis,\nWarm, dry, flushed skin\nDecreased or absent bowel sounds\nUrinary retention\nOphthalmic signs - Divergent squint, internuclear\nophthalmoplegic and gaze paralysis; nystagmus may also be seen.\nNeurological signs - Ataxia, and myoclonic and\nchoreoathetoid movements, may be seen. Increased muscle\ntone, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses may occur.\nSevere metabolic acidosis on ABG findingsInvestigations\nECG - This should be done as soon as possible to assess for\npresence of QRS prolongation.\nAny QRS duration >100 milliseconds in the setting of overdose,\nor unknown cause of altered mental status, is suspicious for\nTCA overdose.\nThe most common ECG abnormality seen in TCA overdose\nis sinus tachycardia.\nOther ECG changes that can occur include:\nprolonged QTc duration\nprolonged PR interval\nAV block\nnon-specific ST and T wave changes\ndownward sloping ST elevation in leads V1 to V3 with right\nbundle branch block (Brugada wave).\nTreatment:\nFollow ABC protocol, obtain intravenous access, attach a\ncardiac monitor and obtain an ECG to look for QRS\nprolongation.\nGastrointestinal decontamination (Time limited) - consider in\npatients with ingestion within 1 hour .\nActivated charcoal: 1 g/kg orally as a single dose, or 50g in\nadults repeat every 1-2 hours if required.\nPt with QRS >100 milliseconds or pH <7.45 - Give sodium\nbicarbonate bolus followed by infusion.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
5929234a-6667-4179-95e0-466aa7f0fc95	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	heart-volumes	Heart Volumes	article	Heart volume\nThe total blood volume in the circulatory system of a healthy\nadult is about 5 L.\nStroke Volume Output: The volume of blood that is ejected\nby each ventricle during systole is called stroke volume\noutput (Value:70 ml/beat)\nHeart rate= 70bpm\nCardiac output (CO) = Stroke volume x Heart rate = 70\nmL/beat x 70 beats/min = 4900 mL/min\nMean arterial pressure: Average arterial pressure during\nsingle cardiac cycle. MAP can be calculated by:  Diastolic\npressure +  of pulse pressure (SBP-DBP) OR 80+  (120-\n80) = 93mmhg where BP\nEnd-Diastolic Volume: The volume of blood in each ventricle\nat the end of the diastole is called end-diastolic volume.\n(Value:120-130ml)\nEnd-Systolic Volume: The volume of blood in each ventricle\nat end of systole is called end-systolic volume. (Value: 50-\n60ml)\nEjection Fraction: Fraction of end-diastolic volume that is\nejected during systole, is called ejection fraction. (Value: 60%)	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
1cc8d3d8-01d6-461f-ba9c-bafc9e1dbdf1	1f7633e6-613c-4343-9757-091175bffbbe	ketamine-procedural-sedation-in-children	Ketamine Procedural Sedation in Children	article	# Ketamine Procedural Sedation in Children\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Pain & Sedation\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nKetamine Procedural Sedation\nIn Children:\n> Ketamine is a unique dissociative drug and that acts as a\ndissociative sedative through binding of the N-methyl-Daspartate (NMDA) receptor.\n> Characteristics of ketamine sedation\n- Dissociation - trance-like state with eyes open but not\nresponding\n- Catalepsy - normal or slightly increased muscle tone\nmaintained\n- Analgesia - excellent analgesia is typical\n- Amnesia - usually total\n- Airway reflexes maintained\n- Cardiovascular state - blood pressure and heart rate increase\nslightly\n- Nystagmus is typical, usually horizontal; eyes remain open\nand glazed\n> Ketamine dose & duration:\n- Initial dose : 1mg/kg over 60 seconds\n- Further dose: 0.5mg/kg slow injection\n- Clinical onset: 1 minutes\n- Effective sedation: 10-20 minutes\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:28 AM Ketamine Procedural Sedation In Children | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n- Time to discharge average: 90 minutes\n> Ketamine indications\n- Ketamine can be used for procedural sedation in children\nwho will need a painful or frightening procedure during the\ncourse of their emergency care.\n- Children over 12 months of age (there is an increased risk of\nairway complications in children less than 12 months and\nespecially less than 3months).\n- potential procedures where ketamine maybe employed\n- Suturing\n- Fracture reduction / manipulation\n- Joint reduction\n- Burn management\n- Incision and Drainage of abscess\n- Tube thoracostomy placement\n- Foreign body removal\n- Wound exploration / irrigation\n> Contraindications:\n- Pulmonary Hypertension\n- Age less than 12 months due to an increased risk of\nlaryngospasm and airway complications\n- A high risk of laryngospasm (active respiratory infection,\nactive asthma)\n- Unstable or abnormal airway. Tracheal surgery or stenosis.\n- Active upper or lower respiratory tract infection\n/26, 10:28 AM Ketamine Procedural Sedation In Children | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n- Proposed procedure within the mouth or pharynx\n- Patients with severe psychological problems such as\ncognitive or motor delay or severe behavioural problems\n- Significant cardiac disease (angina, heart failure, malignant\nhypertension)\n- Intracranial hypertension with CSF obstruction\n- Intra-ocular pathology (glaucoma, penetrating injury)\n- Previous psychotic illness\n- Uncontrolled epilepsy\n- Hyperthyroidism or Thyroid medication\n- Porphyria\n- Prior adverse reaction to ketamine\n- Altered conscious level due to acute illness or injury\n- Drug / alcohol intoxication\n> Adverse Effects\n- Mild agitation (20%)\n- Hypersalivation and lacrimation (<10%), evidence suggests coadministration of anti-cholinergic agents (e.g. atropine) are not\nnecessary.\n- Involuntary movements / ataxia (5%)\n- Vomiting 5-10% of children will vomit in recovery period\n- Transient rash 10%\n> Prior to procedure following things are required and be\nchecked:\n> At least three dedicated staff are required for the duration of\n/26, 10:28 AM Ketamine Procedural Sedation In Children | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nthe procedure:\n- A doctor to manage the sedation and airway,\n- A clinician to perform the procedure\n- An experienced nurse to monitor and support the patient,\nfamily and clinical staff.\n> Ketamine procedural sedation should be only used by\nclinicians experienced in its use and capable of managing any\ncomplications\n> The child should be managed in high dependency or\nresuscitation area with immediate access to full resuscitation\nfacilities\n> Supplemental oxygen should be given prior to and during the\nprocedure\n> Discuss the proposed procedure and use of ketamine with\nparent or guardian and obtain written consent.\n> Where time permits, topical anaesthesia (EMLA®, Ametop®,\netc.) should be used to reduce the pain of intravenous\ncannulation.\n> During procedures:\n- Draw up ketamine dose. Calculate key resuscitation drugs\nand ensure they are readily available.\n- Encourage the child and parents to talk (dream) about happy\ntopics. This helps minimise unpleasant emergence\nphenomena.\n- Parents should be encouraged to stay with the child until\nsedation is achieved and whilst the child is recovering.\n- The effects of ketamine are usually evident 1-2 minutes after\nadministration. Painful procedures should not be initiated until\n2 minutes after ketamine has been administered.\n/26, 10:28 AM Ketamine Procedural Sedation In Children | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\n- Adequate sedation is usually indicated by loss of response to\nverbal stimuli and nystagmus, heart rate, blood pressure and\nrespiration rate may all increase slightly.\n- Lacrimation or salivation may be observed.\n- After the procedure the child should recover in a quiet,\nobserved and monitored area under the continuous\nobservation of a trained member of staff\n> Recovery:\n> Recovery should be complete between 60 and 120 minutes,\ndepending on the dose used.\n> The child can be safely discharged once they are at\npresedation levels:\n- Conscious and responding appropriately\n- Nystagmus resolved\n- Able to walk unassisted (older children)\n- Vital signs are within normal limits\n- Respiratory status not compromised\n- Pain and discomfort addressed\n> Complications: laryngospasm, apnoea, airway\nmisalignment/noisy breathing and emergency phenomena\n(ketamine is known to induce agitation and hallucinations in\nboth adults and children as the dissociative effects wear off).\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:28 AM Ketamine Procedural Sedation In Children | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Ketamine Procedural Sedation in Children**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
07418728-90c0-4e58-9e4d-795e9054f930	1f7633e6-613c-4343-9757-091175bffbbe	biers-block-ivra	Bier's Block (Intravenous Regional Anaesthesia)	article	# Bier's Block (Intravenous Regional Anaesthesia)\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Pain & Sedation\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nAnaesthesia for Distal Forearm\nFractures (Bier's Block)\n-Indication: Reduction of wrist fractures, most commonly\nColles' fracture.\n-Contraindication:\n> Allergy to local anaesthetic\n> Children - consider whether appropriate on individual basis\n> Hypertension >200mm Hg\n> Infection in the limb\n> Lymphoedema\n> Methaemoglobinaemia\n> Morbid obesity (as the cuff is unreliable on obese arms)\n> Peripheral vascular disease\n> Procedures needed in both arms\n> Raynaud's phenomenon\n> Scleroderma\n> Severe hypertension\n> Sickle cell disease or trait\n> Pagets Disease\n> Uncooperative or confused patient\n-Drugs and dose:\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:27 AM Bier's Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Drug:\n> 0.5% or 1% prilocaine without preservative\n> No preparation with adrenaline\n> If 0.5% prilocaine unavailable, use half volume of 1% plain\nprilocaine and the same volume of normal saline (eg instead of\n40ml 0.5% plain prilocaine, use 20 ml 1% plain prilocaine and\ndilute with 20ml normal saline).\n> Dose:\n> Prilocaine 3mg/Kg.There are no reported problems at this\ndose.\n> During a period of prilocaine shortage in the UK, the\nfollowing regime was found to be an acceptable alternative;\n0.5% plain lidocaine at a dose of 3 mg/kg up to a maximum of\n200 mg (40 ml)\n-Technique:\n> Immediately prior to the procedure an invasive procedures\nchecklist should be completed which include consent, Pt\nweigh in kg, drug dose etc\n- Procedure\n> Place double cuff tourniquet on upper arm and not on\nforearm as adequate arterial compression cannot be obtained.\n> Padding must always be applied prior to cuff placement and\napplying a well fitted double cuff will be a 2 person procedure\n> Inflate the proximal cuff to 100mmHg above the systolic BP or\nup to a maximum of 300mmHg. If rotation of cuff is considered\nthen the machine should have a fail-safe selected preventing\ndecompression of proximal cuff prior to inflation of distal cuff.\n> Record the time of inflation\n/26, 10:27 AM Bier's Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Check for the absence of radial pulse\n> Inject 0.5% plain prilocaine, prepared according to patient\nweight, slowly and record the time of injection.\n> Remove the cannula and apply pressure as the venupuncture\nsite is prone to bleed (thus use of 22G)\n> Warn the patient about the cold/hot sensation and mottled\nappearance of the arm\n> Check for anaesthesia, may sense movement but not pain\nafter 10 minutes which is when the manipulation should be\ndone and plaster applied by 2nd member of staff\n> Lower arm on to a pillow and obtain check x-ray\n> Tourniquet dials must be under observation at all times\n> Watch for signs of toxicity\n> The cuff must be inflated for a minimum of 20 minutes and a\nmaximum of 45 minutes. If rotation of cuff required because of\npain at cuff site or prolonged procedure (2nd manipulation)\nthis should done after the manipulation and plaster is applied.\nThe distal cuff will then be inflated over an anesthetised area\nand be more comfortable for the patient.\n> If satisfied with the post reduction position of fracture, deflate\nthe cuff observing the patient and monitor\n> If fail safe selection applied the distal cuff will need to be\ninflated first prior to deflations\n> Record the time of deflation\n> Check limb circulation prior to discharge and arrange patient\nfollow up and analgesia as appropriate\n-Complications:\n- Systemic toxicity and Methaemoglobinaemia (A problem\nspecific to prilocaine, usually in doses >16mg/kg but can occur\n/26, 10:27 AM Bier's Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nwith other drugs (lidocaine, GTN, phenytoin, metoclopramide,\npoppers, cocaine)\n> Intralipid is helpful in local anaesthetic toxicity\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:27 AM Bier's Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Bier's Block (Intravenous Regional Anaesthesia)**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
cdfefcff-491b-41b6-a1fd-5f368eec2779	8068b12b-317b-4499-8871-45eb82ce7703	nerve-muscle	Nerve Muscle	article	Nerve muscle\nSkeletal muscle structure:\nThe connective tissue surrounding the whole muscle is called\nthe epimysium. The connective tissue that extends beyond\nthe body of the muscle eventually blends into a tendon,\nwhich is attached to bone or cartilage. Skeletal muscle is\ncomposed of numerous parallel, elongated, multinucleated\ncells called muscle fibers (or myofibers) each enclosed by\nendomysium, which are grouped together to form fascicles.\nEach fascicle is surrounded by perimysium.\nThe sarcomere is the functional unit of the muscle.\nEach muscle fiber is divided at regular intervals along its\nlength into sarcomeres separated by Z-lines.\nMuscle filaments:\nThick filaments: Present in the A band in the center of the\nsarcomere\nContains Myosin (Recall)\nThin filaments: Present in the I band.\nAnchored at the Z lines.\nContains actin, tropomyosin and troponin.\nThere are three types of muscle:\nSkeletal\nCardiac\nSmooth\nComparison between them:\n\n![Nerve Muscle - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/nerve-muscle/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Nerve Muscle — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
b1595217-d719-4d12-b3dd-5af10657961a	8068b12b-317b-4499-8871-45eb82ce7703	skeletal-muscle-excitation-contraction-coupling	Skeletal Muscle Excitation Contraction Coupling	article	Skeletal excitation contraction\ncoupling\nMuscle contraction occurs when the concentration of\nintracellular Ca2+ rises.\nAn action potential travels down tube-shaped invaginations\nof the sarcolemma called T-tubules which penetrate\nthroughout the muscle fiber and lie adjacent to the terminal\ncisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\nVoltage changes in the T-tubules results in the opening of\nsarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ channels and the release of\nstored Ca2+ into the sarcoplasm.\nMolecular Events in Muscle\nContraction and Sliding\nfilament theory.\nMotor neuron axon releases acetylcholine\nAcetylcholine diffuses across synaptic cleft.\nThis stimulates the sarcolemma. The impulse travels over the\nmuscle fiber surface and down the T-tubules to the\nsarcoplasmic reticulum (SR).\nCalcium is bound to calsequestrin from SR, Calcium ions\ndiffuse out of the SR into the sarcoplasm and bind to\ntroponin.\nTropomyosin moves and exposes sites on actin filaments.\nActin and myosin form linkages.\nActin filaments are pulled inward by myosin cross-bridges\n(Sliding filament theory Muscle fibers shorten as contraction\noccurs.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
9c0b8297-82c0-4309-a538-fd4c00a92655	1f7633e6-613c-4343-9757-091175bffbbe	fascia-iliaca-block	Fascia Iliaca Compartment Block (FICB)	article	# Fascia Iliaca Compartment Block (FICB)\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Pain & Sedation\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\n- Fascia iliaca compartment block\n> The fascia iliaca compartment is a potential space lying\nbetween the fascia ilaca anteriorly and the iliacus and psoas\nmuscles (iliopsoas) posteriorly. The fascia iliaca attaches to the\niliac crest laterally and to the fascia overlying the psoas muscle\nmedially.\n> Anatomical landmarks are the inguinal ligament, anterior\nsuperior iliac spine (ASIS), and pubic tubercle.\n> The advantage of FIB over Femoral Nerve Block:\n> there is lower risk of intraneural and intra-vascular injection\n> There is also a theoretical advantage of Lateral Cutaneous\nNerve of the thigh blockade.\n> Indication:\n> Pre-, peri- and postoperative analgesia after fractured neck of\nfemur (NOF).\n> hip and knee surgery, above knee amputation.\n> Contraindications\n> Allergy to LA\n> Infection and/or inflammation over the injection site\n> Previous femoral bypass surgery or near a graft site\n> Anticoagulation.\n> Procedure: Explain the procedure and gain consent from the\npatient.\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:27 AM Fascia Iliaca Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n> Prepare the equipment, including relevant specialized long\nneedles and LA:\n- Use 30mL of 0.25% levobupivacaine in adults weighing ≤ 50kg.\n- Use 40mL of 0.25% levobupivacaine in adults weighing >50kg.\n> Clean the skin and identify the anterior superior iliac spine\nand pubic tubercle on the side of the fracture, and place a\nfinger on each of these, then draw an imaginary line between\nthem and divide this line up into thirds, thereby identifying the\njunction of the lateral third and medial two-thirds-the\ninjection site is 1cm below (caudal to) this point. The femoral\npulse is well medial to this.\n> Direct the needle in the sagittal plane, and having pierced\nthe skin, advance through two distinct palpable 'pops'\n(penetration through first the fascia lata and then the fascia\niliaca).\n> Advance a further 1-2mm, then attempt to aspirate, and if no\nblood is aspirated.\n> Stop if there is pain or paraesthesiae on injection. Attempt to\naspirate after every 5mL of injection.\n> At the end of procedure, withdraw the needle and apply a\nsmall amount of pressure to the injection site.\n> Continue regular observations (pulse, BP, RR, SpO2 ) every\n5min for at least 30min.\n> Complications: block failure, haematoma, neuropraxia, local\nanaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST), quadriceps weakness,\nperforation of peritoneal cavity contents and bladder puncture.\n> Treat local anaesthetic toxicity with intralipid\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:27 AM Fascia Iliaca Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\n/26, 10:27 AM Fascia Iliaca Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Fascia Iliaca Compartment Block (FICB)**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
98259024-3737-458a-b046-cfcd8ac26471	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	control-of-ventilation	Control of Ventilation	article	Control of ventilation is mediated via\ncentral and Peripheral\nchemoreceptors.\nChemoreceptor overview:\nCentral chemoreceptor Vs Peripheral\nchemoreceptor\n\n![Control of Ventilation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/control-of-ventilation/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Control of Ventilation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Control of Ventilation - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/control-of-ventilation/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Control of Ventilation — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
1430d525-9062-4bcf-abcc-aca0704f9909	e085e872-faa5-4dd4-a818-e9304c9a4b61	female-genital-mutilation-fgm	Female Genital Mutilation (FGM) Management & Legal Duty	article	# Female Genital Mutilation (FGM) Management & Legal Duty\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Safeguarding & Psycho-social emergencies in children\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nFemale Genital Mutilation:\n> It is also known as female genital circumcision or cutting\n> Common in African countries\nFGM and UK law\n> FGM is illegal unless it is a surgical operation on a girl or\nwoman irrespective of her age:\n- which is necessary for her physical or mental health\n- she is in any stage of labour, or has just given birth, for\npurposes connected with the labour or birth.\n> It is illegal to arrange, or assist in arranging, for a UK national\nor UK resident to be taken overseas for the purpose of FGM.\n> It is an offence for those with parental responsibility to fail to\nprotect a girl from the risk of FGM.\n> If FGM is confirmed in a girl under 18 years of age, must\nreport to police within 1 month\nDiagnosis Features:\n- Pt may report history of prior genital instrumentation\n- Painful period and pain with urination\n- Definitive diagnosis made after physical examination\nComplication of FGM\n> Short-term complication:\n- Haemorrhage\n- Urinary retention\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:34 AM Female Genital Mutilation (FGM) | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\n- Genital swelling\n- Infection\n> Long-term complication\n- Genital scarring\n- Recurrent urinary infection\n- Dyspareunia\n- Genital infection and pelvic inflammatory disease\n- HIV, Hep B\n- Infertility\nManagement:\n- Accurately record examination findings\n- All women and girls with acute or recent FGM require police\nand social services referral.\n- All children with FGM or suspected FGM should be seen\nwithin child safeguarding service\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:34 AM Female Genital Mutilation (FGM) | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Female Genital Mutilation (FGM) Management & Legal Duty**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
8264c12b-61ac-45ca-a814-fe2788e729bc	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	atrial-arrhythmias	Atrial Arrhythmias & Atrial Flutter	article	# Atrial Arrhythmias & Atrial Flutter\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
61766857-e9bf-4567-8d0b-5e89ea91e8a7	3017cd2a-787b-4d30-a6df-b97e73d315fa	kawasaki-disease	Kawasaki Disease Diagnosis & Emergency Management	article	# Kawasaki Disease Diagnosis & Emergency Management\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Infectious Diseases & Global Health\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nKawasaki Disease:\n- It's an acute, febrile, self-limiting, systemic vasculitis of\nunknown origin that almost exclusively affects young children.\nRisk factors\n- Asian ancestry\n- age 3 months to 4 years\n- male sex\nDiagnostic features\n- Fever for more than 5 days at least 4 of the following features\n- Polymorphic rash\n- Bilateral conjunctival injection\n- mucosal erythema with strawberry tongue.\n- Swelling and erythema of the hands and feet occur in the\nacute stage, followed by desquamation in the second week.\n- Unilateral non-purulent cervical lymphadenopathy is present\nin about 40% of cases.\n- Coronary aneurysms develop in 20% to 25% of untreated\npatients.\nInvestigation:\n> FBC\n- Haemoglobin low for age (Mild-moderate normocytic anemia\npresent in acute phase)\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:34 AM Kawasaki Disease | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\n- Platelet > 450,000/mm ( thrombocytosis present in subacute\nphase)\n- WBC>15,000/mm\n- Serum albumin<3.0 g/dl\n- serum erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)\n- serum CRP\n- LFTs (Elevated alanine aminotransferase)\n- ECG\n- Echocardiogram( perform during and after acute illness and if\necho reveal cardiac abnormality then refer child to\npaediatrician for further cardiac work-up\nManagement:\n- intravenous immunoglobulin and/or aspirin. In resistant\ncases, corticosteroids or a tumour necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha\ninhibitor may be indicated.\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:34 AM Kawasaki Disease | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Kawasaki Disease Diagnosis & Emergency Management**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
7433e8df-4bd6-4f08-87ef-ece3d4c7764a	57c14b4d-9d2e-4403-bb56-0ae5e1851813	regulation-of-cardiac-pumping	Regulation of Cardiac Pumping	article	Regulation of Cardiac Pumping\nCardiac pumping (i.e., volume of blood pumped by heart) is\nregulated by:\nIntrinsic regulation: pumping in response to changes in\nvolume of blood flowing into heart (obeys Frank starling Law\nof heart).\nFrank Starling Law:\nWhen venous return of blood increases, the heart muscle\nstretches more, which makes it pump with a greater force of\ncontraction. The Frank- Starling mechanism of the heart can be\nstated in another way:\nWithin physiological limits, the heart pumps all the blood that\ncomes to it without allowing excess accumulation of blood in\nthe veins.\nMechanism of Frank Starling Law\nStates that "Within physiologic limits, the heart pumps all the\nblood that returns to it by the way of veins.\nExtra volume of blood enters heart chambers.\ncardiac muscle over-stretched\ncardiac muscle contracts with increased force because the\ninitial length of fiber increases and pumps extra blood into\narteries.\nConcepts of preload and after load:\nThe heart will contract with greater force when preload/EDV\nis increased. Preload is diastolic filling volume of left\nventricle reflect stretch of cardiac muscle cells, greater the\nstretch more forcefully heart will contract. when there is\nincreased EDV/ preload there is increased in Stroke volume\nthis intrinsic relationship between EDV and stroke volume is\nknown as frank starling law of heart.\n\nAfterload is the pressure that the chambers of the heart\nmust generate in order to eject blood out of the heart occurs\nin systole.\nNote: In heart failure, the Frank-Starling curve is moved down\nand flattened due to reduced inotropy, so that more venous\nreturn and hence preload is required to increase stroke volume.\nAs venous return and preload rise, myocyte stretching occurs\nthat increases sarcomere length.\nFactors affecting frank starling curve.\nincrease in contractility shift the curve upward and to the\nleft e.g.: positive inotropic drugs (RECALL)\nDecrease in contractility= downward and to the right\nIncrease in preload = rightward shift\ndecrease in preload=leftward shift\nIncrease in Afterload=Downward and right\nDecrease in Afterload=upward and left\nChronotropic and ionotropic effects\nFactors that affect intracellular [Ca2+] concentration and\nhence cardiac contractility are called inotropes.\nChromotropes increase sinus nodal discharge increase heart\nrate.\nSympathetic stimulation increases cardiac muscle\ncontractility because it causes the release of noradrenaline.\nNoradrenaline is a positive inotrope; it binds to β 1-\nadrenoceptors on the membrane and causes increased Ca2+\nentry via L-type channels during the AP and thus increases\nCa2+ release from the SR. Noradrenaline also increases Ca2+\nsequestration into the SR and thus more Ca2+ is available for\nthe next contraction.\nAdrenaline is a non-selective adrenergic agonist with potent\nβ 1 and moderate α 1 and β 2-receptor activity. Increase\nmyocardial force of contraction (positive inotrope) and heart\nrate (positive chronotrope) occur as a result of β 1 receptor\nstimulation. Systemic vascular resistance is increased\noverall because the stimulation of α 1 receptors results in\n\n![Regulation of Cardiac Pumping - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/regulation-of-cardiac-pumping/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Regulation of Cardiac Pumping — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nperipheral vasoconstriction, which counters the vasodilation\ndue to β 2 receptor activation.\nDigoxin slows the removal of Ca2+ from the cell by inhibiting\nthe membrane Na+ pump which generates the Na+ gradient\nrequired for driving the export of Ca2+; consequently, the\nremoval of Ca2+ from the myocyte is slowed and more Ca2+\nis available inside the myocyte for the next contraction.\nExamples:\nAdrenaline is positive inotrope as well as positive\nchronotropic agent, increases both heart rate and\ncontractility.\nDopamine is positive inotropic drug increases contractility.\nDigoxin is positive inotrope and negative chronotrope.	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
cfc4fcb2-eb43-42ee-b73d-3f12be79669b	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-cardiac-arrest-pregnancy	Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 32-year-old woman at 32 weeks gestation suffers a sudden out-of-hospital cardiac arrest and is brought to the resuscitation bay. Chest compressions are ongoing. To relieve aortocaval compression, what is the recommended immediate physical manoeuvre?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/cardiac-arrest-pregnancy/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Place the patient in a full 90-degree left lateral decubitus position\n- **B.** Manual left uterine displacement (LUD) while keeping the patient supine on a firm backboard\n- **C.** Trendelenburg position (head down 30 degrees)\n- **D.** Elevate both lower limbs to 45 degrees\n- **E.** Prone positioning\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nIn pregnant patients >20 weeks gestation, the gravid uterus compresses the inferior vena cava and aorta, reducing venous return and cardiac output during CPR. Resuscitation guidelines recommend Manual Left Uterine Displacement (LUD) performed with one or two hands while the patient remains supine on a firm surface, avoiding tilting tables which degrade compression quality.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nIn maternal cardiac arrest at >=20 weeks gestation, if Return of Spontaneous Circulation (ROSC) is NOT achieved despite high-quality CPR, within how many minutes from the time of arrest should Resuscitative Hysterotomy (Perimortem Caesarean Section) be initiated and delivered?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/cardiac-arrest-pregnancy/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Initiate at 15 minutes, deliver by 20 minutes\n- **B.** Initiate at 10 minutes, deliver by 15 minutes\n- **C.** Initiate by 4 minutes, deliver by 5 minutes ('The 4-5 Minute Rule')\n- **D.** Wait until 30 minutes of full ALS cycles are completed\n- **E.** Only perform hysterotomy after maternal death is formally certified by two physicians\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n**Explanation:**\nThe '4-5 Minute Rule': If there is no ROSC after 4 minutes of resuscitation in a pregnant woman >=20 weeks gestation, resuscitative hysterotomy should be commenced immediately with the goal of delivering the fetus by 5 minutes. This dramatically relieves aortocaval compression, restoring maternal venous return and giving the best chance of maternal (and fetal) survival.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhere should a Resuscitative Hysterotomy (Perimortem Caesarean Section) take place when maternal cardiac arrest occurs in the Emergency Department?\n\n- **A.** Transfer urgently to the main hospital obstetric operating theatre\n- **B.** In the ED Resuscitation Bay where the patient is located, without moving her\n- **C.** In the radiology CT scanner room\n- **D.** In the labour ward triage area\n- **E.** In the post-anaesthetic recovery unit\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nResuscitative hysterotomy must NEVER be delayed by transporting the patient to an operating theatre. It should be performed immediately at the site of arrest (ED Resus room) using a scalpel. Time to delivery is the primary determinant of maternal brain viability.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old primigravida at 34 weeks gestation with severe pre-eclampsia receiving IV magnesium sulphate suddenly becomes apnoeic, bradycardic, and loses her pulse. What specific antidote should be administered immediately?\n\n- **A.** IV Naloxone 400mcg\n- **B.** IV Calcium Gluconate 10% (10ml) or Calcium Chloride 10%\n- **C.** IV Flumazenil 500mcg\n- **D.** IV Sodium Bicarbonate 50ml 8.4%\n- **E.** IV Lipid Emulsion 20%\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nMagnesium sulphate toxicity causes loss of deep tendon reflexes, respiratory depression, and cardiac arrest. The specific antagonist is intravenous calcium (10ml of 10% calcium gluconate or calcium chloride IV over 5-10 minutes), which competitively reverses neuromuscular and cardiac toxicity.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nRegarding defibrillation pad placement and energy selection in maternal cardiac arrest, which statement is correct?\n\n- **A.** Defibrillation energy should be halved to protect the fetal myocardium\n- **B.** Defibrillation is strictly contraindicated in pregnancy due to amniotic fluid conduction\n- **C.** Standard adult energy levels (e.g. 150-200J biphasic) should be used, with anterolateral or anterior-posterior pad placement avoiding breast tissue\n- **D.** Defibrillate only if the fetus is confirmed non-viable by bedside ultrasound\n- **E.** Synchronised cardioversion only must be used even for ventricular fibrillation\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n**Explanation:**\nStandard adult defibrillation protocols apply in pregnancy. Transthoracic impedance is unchanged, and standard biphasic energy doses (150-200J) must be used. Defibrillation does not deliver harmful electrical current to the fetus. Do not delay defibrillation.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
6ecfc944-025b-41d7-9045-11d39d3bbe96	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	chocking-andult-and-pedio	Choking (Adult & Paediatric)	article	# Choking (Adult & Paediatric)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nMild obstruction\n- Patient able to: Speak Cough Breath Response Mnemonics BRCS\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nin adult\n- Sever obstruction Patient unable to: Speak Cough Breath Response (maybe unconscious)\n\n-\n- Mild obstruction Patient able to BRCS. Encourage patient to continue coughing Severe obstruction but patient is conscious. Give 5 back blows If not relived Give 5 abdominal thrusts If not relived continue alternating 5 back blows & abdominal thrusts patient become unconscious. Call resuscitation team and start CPR\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nin Paeds\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Chocking Andult and Pedio - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/chocking-andult-and-pedio/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Chocking Andult and Pedio — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Chocking Andult and Pedio - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/chocking-andult-and-pedio/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n*Chocking Andult and Pedio — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Chocking Andult and Pedio - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/chocking-andult-and-pedio/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Chocking Andult and Pedio — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Chocking Andult and Pedio - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/chocking-andult-and-pedio/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n*Chocking Andult and Pedio — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
ce106637-59a5-40d9-8464-d5da5436d3d3	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	diffusion-and-diffusion-capacity	Diffusion and Diffusion Capacity	article	Diffusion of gases such as 02\nand CO ₂\nThe diffusion rates of O2 and CO2 depend on the partial\npressure differences across the membrane and the area\navailable for diffusion.\nFor example, the diffusion of O2 from alveolar air into the\npulmonary capillary depends on the partial pressure\ndifference for O2 between alveolar air and pulmonary\ncapillary blood.\nNormally, capillary blood with alveolar gas; when the partial\npressures of 02 become equal there is no more net diffusion\nof O2.\nGas diffusion across the alveolar-pulmonary capillary barrier\noccurs according to Ficks law.\nFick's law\nshows the relationship between the variables that affect\ndiffusion: Rate of diffusion directly proportional to surface\narea x concentration difference and inversely proportional to\nthickness of membrane.\nFick's law tells us that the rate of diffusion of a gas increases:\nthe larger the surface area involved in gas exchange.\nthe greater the partial pressure gradient across the\nmembrane\nthe thinner the membrane\nthe more soluble the gas in the membrane\nthe lower the molecular weight of the gas\nDiffusion Capacity (DLCO)\nLung diffusing capacity is the equivalent of permeability of the\nalveolar-pulmonary capillary barrier and is proportional to\ndiffusion coefficient of the gas and surface area and inversely\nproportional to thickness of the barrier. DLCO is measured with\ncarbon monoxide (i.e., DLCO).\n\nDLCO increases during exercise because there are more\nopen capillaries and thus more surface area for diffusion.\nDLCO decreases in emphysema (because of decreased\nsurface area) and in fibrosis and pulmonary edema (because\nof increased diffusion distance).\nHigh DLCO and Low DLCO\n\n![Diffusion and Diffusion Capacity - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/diffusion-and-diffusion-capacity/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Diffusion and Diffusion Capacity — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
e295429e-cc71-48e1-811c-fc8a6dfe5d06	c5a116c4-d2b6-4edb-bf1f-d05abfe7a1d8	digestion-and-absorption-of-carbohydrates	Digestion and Absorption of Carbohydrates	article	Carbohydrate\nCarbohydrates in the diet provide the major exogenous source\nfor glucose, which is the primary energy source for cells. They\nprovide 17kJ (4kcal) of energy per gram. Most of the dietary\ncarbohydrates are in the form of polysaccharides, the principal\ningested polysaccharides are starch which is derived from\nplant sources and glycogen which is derived from animal\nsources, other sources include disaccharides and\nmonosaccharides.\nDigestion and Absorption of\ncarbohydrate\nCarbohydrates are neutral compounds composed of carbon,\nhydrogen, and oxygen that serve as the primary sources of\nenergy in the human body. They can be divided into simple\ncarbohydrates, which include monosaccharides (e.g., glucose,\nfructose, galactose) and disaccharides (e.g., maltose, sucrose,\nlactose), and complex carbohydrates, which include starch\npolysaccharides (e.g., starch, glycogen and Non starch\npolysaccharides (e.g., glucan, cellulose). Monosaccharides are\ndirectly absorbed by enterocytes. Disaccharides and\npolysaccharides require degradation into monosaccharides to\nbe absorbed by hydrolytic enzymes, which are secreted by the\nsalivary glands in the mouth (salivary amylase), intestinal villi\n(maltase, lactase), and pancreas (pancreatic amylase).\nConditions that decrease the secretion of these enzymes result\nin malabsorption and maldigestion.\nDigestion\n\nGlucose and galactose are absorbed via sodium-glucose linked\ntransporters (SGLTs) in the intestinal epithelial cells and\ntranslocated into the circulation via glucose transporter 2\n(GLUT2). Fructose is absorbed into enterocytes via glucose\ntransporter 5 (GLUT5) through facilitated diffusion. The uptake\nof glucose into other cells is mediated by glucose transporters 1\nto 5. Intracellularly, monosaccharides are further metabolized\nby a series of enzymatic reactions that release ATP.\nIndigestible carbohydrates found in plants food such as\ncellulose (humans unable to digest cellulose because lack of\nappropriate enzymes cellulase). it does not provide any energy;\nfiber adds bulk to the bowel contents and increases the gut\nmotility.\n\n![Digestion and Absorption of Carbohydrates - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/digestion-and-absorption-of-carbohydrates/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Digestion and Absorption of Carbohydrates — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
48bbf181-85f7-464f-893a-0720b2d8aedc	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	paediatric-life-support-pls-pals	Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS)	article	# Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nStep-I Step-2 Step-3 Step-4 unresponsive call for help Open airway reathing normal y, NO 5 rescue breath Competent providers should use bag-mask ventilation with oxygen. If unable/unsafe to ventilate perform continuous chest compressions, ventilate as soon as possible If no signs of life observed during rescue breaths, Immediately commence chest compressions(15:2) Yes Assess ABC 02 monitoring Vascular access Call further assistance as appropriate\n\nstep-5  monitoring/defibrilla tor when available Follow PAEDIATRIC ADVANCED LIFE SUPPORT ALGORITHM on arrival of in-hospital clinical emergency team\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/paediatric-life-support-pls-pals/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/paediatric-life-support-pls-pals/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/paediatric-life-support-pls-pals/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Paediatric Life Support (PLS / PALS) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
821d20b4-5e23-4883-9e28-d6d23eb90121	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	transfution-mangement-of-major-hge	Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage	article	# Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Ptbleeding/collapsed or in clinical shock\n- Trigger the major blood loss protocol\n- If trauma and <3hrs from injury, give tranexamic acid lg bolus over IO minutes followed by infusion of lg over 8hr (Consider tranexamic acid lg bolus in non-traumatic patient)\n- Take baseline blood sample for: Cross-match, FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Give initial MHP-1which include 4 or 6 units RBC 4 units Add 1 dose of plt in trauma patients earlier Reassess the patient If require further transfusion then take baseline sample for: FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, I-J&E and Calcium level Order MHP-2 which include 4-6 units of RBC, 4units of FFP, I dose of plt 2 packs of cryoprecipitate Transfusion of MHP-2 depends on lab results eg: if Hb in lab value <8 then transfuse RBC otherwise not\n- Aim to maintain following\n- values PLT PT ratio APTT ratio Fibrinogen Ca2+ 8-1 Ogldl X 109 IL\n- 1.5gn\n- lmmol/l\n\n- Repeat the baseline investigation which include:\n- FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Correct complications: hypocalcaemia, hypothermia, acidosis  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Transfction Mangement of Major Hge - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/transfction-mangement-of-major-hge/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Transfction Mangement of Major Hge — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Transfction Mangement of Major Hge - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/transfction-mangement-of-major-hge/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Transfction Mangement of Major Hge — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Transfction Mangement of Major Hge - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/transfction-mangement-of-major-hge/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Transfction Mangement of Major Hge — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a518dcde-58a8-4480-8e19-93e960a37b0e	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	central-control-of-breathing	Central Control of Breathing	article	Involuntary respiration\nInvoluntary respiration is under subconscious control. The\ndiaphragm and intercostal muscles are the primary respiratory\nmuscles. They are stimulated by groups of neurons located in\nthe pons and medulla. These neurons form the respiratory\ncontrol centre. They send impulses to the primary respiratory\nmuscles, via the phrenic and intercostal nerves, which\nstimulate their contraction. There are three main groups of\nneurons involved in respiration:\nThe ventral respiratory group controls expiration\nThe dorsal respiratory group controls inspiration\nThe pontine respiratory group controls the rate and pattern\nof breathing.\nNote: When expiratory neurons fire they inhibit the activity of\ninspiratory neurons and once the neurons stop firing, the\ninspiratory muscles relax, and expiration occurs.\nVoluntary respiration\nVoluntary respiration is under conscious control. It is\ncontrolled via the motor cortex in the cerebrum, which\nreceives inputs from the limbic system and hypothalamus.\nSignals are thought to be sent to the spinal cord from the\nmotor cortex, which are then passed onto the respiratory\nmuscles.\nCentral control of breathing\n(brainstem and cerebral cortex)\n\n### 1. Medullary respiratory center (Recall)\n\nA: Dorsal respiratory group is primarily responsible for\ninspiration and generates the basic rhythm for breathing\nInput to the dorsal respiratory group comes from the vagus\nand glossopharyngeal nerves. The vagus nerve relays\ninformation from peripheral chemoreceptors in the lung. The\nglossopharyngeal nerve relays information from peripheral chemoreceptors and Output from the dorsal respiratory\ngroup travels, via the phrenic nerve, to the diaphragm\nB: Ventral respiratory group is primarily responsible for\nexpiration, is not active during normal, quiet breathing, when\nexpiration is passive.\nActivated during exercise, when expiration becomes an\nactive process.\n\n### 2. Apneustic center is located in the lower pons. Stimulates\n\ninspiration, producing a deep and prolonged inspiratory gasp\n(apneusis). Normally apneustic center promotes deep\nbreathing, damage to apneustic center promotes shallow\nirregular/ Erratic breathing.\n\n### 3. Pneumotaxic center is located in the upper pons inhibits\n\ninspiration and, therefore, regulates both the rate and pattern\nof breathing. Pneumotaxic center normally prevents\nexcessive deep breathing, damage to pneumotaxic results in\nincrease in depth of respiration (deep breathing) and\ndecrease in respiratory rate.\n4.Cerebral cortex: Breathing can be under voluntary control;\ntherefore, a person can voluntarily hyper ventilate or hypo\nventilate.\nNote: Hypoventilation (breath-holding) is limited by the\nresulting increase in Pco2 and decrease in P02	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
60a1f73b-7bc1-44b4-8f01-2bc82380db24	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	transfction-mangement-of-major-hge	Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage	article	# Transfusion Management of Major Haemorrhage\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Ptbleeding/collapsed or in clinical shock\n- Trigger the major blood loss protocol\n- If trauma and <3hrs from injury, give tranexamic acid lg bolus over IO minutes followed by infusion of lg over 8hr (Consider tranexamic acid lg bolus in non-traumatic patient)\n- Take baseline blood sample for: Cross-match, FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Give initial MHP-1which include 4 or 6 units RBC 4 units Add 1 dose of plt in trauma patients earlier Reassess the patient If require further transfusion then take baseline sample for: FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, I-J&E and Calcium level Order MHP-2 which include 4-6 units of RBC, 4units of FFP, I dose of plt 2 packs of cryoprecipitate Transfusion of MHP-2 depends on lab results eg: if Hb in lab value <8 then transfuse RBC otherwise not\n- Aim to maintain following\n- values PLT PT ratio APTT ratio Fibrinogen Ca2+ 8-1 Ogldl X 109 IL\n- 1.5gn\n- lmmol/l\n\n- Repeat the baseline investigation which include:\n- FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Correct complications: hypocalcaemia, hypothermia, acidosis  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Transfction Mangement of Major Hge - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/transfction-mangement-of-major-hge/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Transfction Mangement of Major Hge — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Transfction Mangement of Major Hge - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/transfction-mangement-of-major-hge/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Transfction Mangement of Major Hge — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Transfction Mangement of Major Hge - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/transfction-mangement-of-major-hge/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Transfction Mangement of Major Hge — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
17f46b11-98f1-4858-836d-008783aaf12b	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	acute-coronary-syndromes-acs	Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS)	article	# Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- ACS describes a group of clinical conditions all of which presented with chest pain or discomfort resulting from myocardial ischemia, eventually myocardial cells death the condition known as MI\n- ACS classification:\n- STEM':\n- ST-segment elevation or new LBBB present on ECG.\n- NSTE-ACS:\n- NSTEMI: ST-segment depression & T wave inversion on ECG or normal ECG with deranged Troponin.\n- Unstable angina: normal ECG and Troponin (negative)\n- Risk Factors:\n- Positive family history\n- Advanced age\n- Hyperlipidaemia\n- Diabetic Mellitus Hypertension\n- Smoking\n- Aspirin usage\n- Obesity, sedentary lifestyle\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nChest pain features:\n- Heavy, aching, or tight\n- Centre or left side of the chest\n- Not related to respiration or rnovernent\n-\n- It May radiate to one or both arms, neck, or jaw.\n- Chest pain association: Breathlessness, sweating, hemodynamically instability, nausea, and vomiting.\n-\n- Likelihood of AMI Increase likelihood ratio:\n- Describe as pressure\n- Associated with nausea, diaphoresis, or breathlessness\n- Radiation to either or both arms.\n- Worse than previous angina\n- Decrease likelihood ratio:\n- Describe as a sharp or positional.\n- Reproducible with palpation\n- Inframammary location\n- Not associated with exertion\n- Note: The highest likelihood for AMI is radiation of the pain to the right arm or\n- shoulder, or to both arms or shoulders [/boxl\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG finding New ST-segment elevation\n- New Q wave formation\n- New ST-segment depression\n- New conduction deficit\n- T wave peaking or inversion\n- Other\n- Troponin level, ECHO, CXR\n- ST segment depression T wave inversion\n- Note: decrease the likelihood of MI if ECG is normal however it may be normal in UA and NSTEMI\n\n- Localization of the lesion in STEM': Affected myocardial area Septal Anterior Lateral\n-\n- Anteroseptal Anterolateral Inferior\n- Posterior Right ventricular Subendocardial Leads with ST- elevation VI-V2 V3-V4 Or VI-V4 R ST-dep in II, Ill, aVF V5-V6, l, aVL VI-V4 (R II, Ill, aVF) II Ill, aVF (R l, aVL) R v2-v3 ( V7-V9) 111>11 VI, V4R (R Q) aVR (R V2-V6 1, II, aVF) ccluded corona LAD LAD LCX LAD LAD or LCX RCA RCA RCA Arte Left main coronary Artery\n- ST-elevation ECG\n- Acute anterior myocardial infarction:\n- ST-elevation in v3-v4 OR\n- ST-elevation vl-v4 with reciprocal ST-depression in II Ill aVF\n- Example-I\n\n- Anterolateral Myocardial Infarction:\n- ST-elevation v3-v6 1, aVL with reciprocal ST- depression in II, Ill, aVF\n- ST- elevation high in lead I and aVL\n- Example-I\n\n- Example-22\n\n- Acute Inferior Myocardial Infarction:\n- Inferior MI:\n- St-elevation in inferior leads(ll, Ill,aVF) with reciprocal ST-depression in l, aVL\n- But if reciprocal St depression in anteroseptal leads (vl-v4) then posterior infarct likely, needs to confirm with additional leads.\n- Note: mostly right-sided and posterior infarcts occur due to the extension of inferior infarcts so they need to be confirmed with additional leads such as v7- v9 for posterior infarcts and V4R for right-sided infarcts [/box]\n\n- Posterior MI confirmed with additional leads v7-v9\n\n- Right-ventricular-infarction;\n- ST-elevation in 111>11, VI, V4R reciprocal depression in v2.\n- Note: any patient with inferior leads MI in 12 leads needs to routinely check for posterior and right-sided MI.\n\n- Initial management of ACS:\n- Any patient with ACS( STEMI, NSTEMI, or UA) MONA therapy should be started immediately.\n- Morphine (analgesia)\n\n- Oxygen (if needed)\n- Nitrites (GTN)\n- Aspirin\n- Further management of therapy(PCI or Fibrinolysis)\n- Angiography with follow-on primary PCI:\n- Offer if patient presents within 12 hours of symptoms and PCl can be delivered within 120 minutes.\n- Consider any patient presenting hours after symptoms and continuing myocardial ischemia or cardiogenic shock\n- Drug therapy for primary PCI:\n- In addition to aspirin,offer platelet ADP receptor blocker (clopidogrel, prasugrel)\n- Offer heparin or bivalirudin alternative to heparin with bailout CPI (abciximab, tirofiban).\n- Fibrinolysis_(reteplase or tenecteplase);\n- Offer if patient is presenting in 12 hours of symptoms and PCl not possible in 120 mins.\n- In addition to aspirin, Give antithrombin (unfractionated or LMW heparin) at the same time.\n- Offer ECG 60-90 minutes after fibrinolysis.\n- Offer plet ADP receptors blockers (prasugrel) with aspirin if Cl then aspirin alone.\n\n- Do not repeat fibrinolysis; offer immediate angiography with follow-on PCl if indicated by ECG or Seek specialist advice for recurrent myocardial ischaemia and offer angiography with follow-on PCl if appropriate.\n- Consider angiography during the same admission if stable after successful fibrinolysis.\n- Assess the left ventricular function\n- Further management of NSTEMI:\n- In addition to aspirin offer antithrombin, and unfractionated heparin in patient who are likely to undergo PCl within 24hrs\n- LMW who don't have high-risk bleeding and PCl not planned within 24hrs\n- Further treatment depends on a risk scoring system, such as TIMI or GRACE, to predict 6-month mortality\n\n### Low-Risk Criteria\n(predicted 6-month mortality S 3%);\n- Consider conservative management without angiography.\n- Offer ticagrelor with aspirin unless high bleeding risk:\n- Consider clopidogrel with aspirin, or aspirin alone, for high bleeding risk\n- Consider ischemia testing before discharge\n- Offer immediate angiography if the clinical condition is unstable Otherwise, consider angiography (with follow-on PCI if indicated) within 72 hours if no CI such as comorbidity or active bleeding.\n- Offer prasugrel* or ticagrelor with aspirin\n- Offer systemic unfractionated heparin in the catheter laboratory if having PCl\n\n- Secondary Prevention: Antithrombin therapy: Iow dose aspirin 75mg daily. Clopidogrel 75mg or prasugrel 10mg daily\n- ACEI to reduce the risk or severity of HF and MI Beta Blocker to reduce mortality Statin to reduce the future coronary event Antihypertensive therapy Smoking cessation\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_04_p6.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_06_p10.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 10 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/acute-coronary-syndromes-acs/figure_07_p14.jpeg)\n*Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 14 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
507505c8-0128-4503-8c4b-c2884e3b0ac3	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	atrial-septal-defect	Atrial Septal Defect (ASD)	article	# Atrial Septal Defect (ASD)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
6291835d-317f-448b-ac57-3f9bc816aa0f	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	infective-endocarditis	Infective Endocarditis	article	# Infective Endocarditis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Endocarditis is defined as damage to the endocardium (due turbulent flow) associated with thrombus formation. The endothelial lining of the heart covers all internal structure of the heart including valvular surfaces. Endocarditis can be infective if colonised by bacteria or fungi or Non Bacterial Thrombotic Endocarditis (NBTE).\n- The hallmark of endocarditis is formation of 'vegetation' that binds to valves or mural endocardium.\n- Risk factor\n- Heart valve disease, prosthetic valve disease\n- Structural heart disease, Rheumatic heart disease\n- Previous infective endocarditis, Hypertrophic myocarditis\n- Cause\n- Native valve endocarditis\n- Overall most common Staph aureus\n- Dental procedure strep viridans\n- IVDU : Staph aureus\n- Prosthetic valve endocarditis\n- Most common both staph aureus and coagulase-ve Staph epidermis\n- Note: Tricuspid valve is most commonly affected.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\npatient often presents with emboli or cardiac failure. Therefore any patient with fever in\n- conjunction with chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, stroke like symptoms and visual disturbance need to be evaluated urgently for IE.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\nSoft right-sided murmurs can be heard on auscultation.\n- Four peripheral signs of endocarditis:\n- Roth spot\n- Osler's nodes\n- Janeway lesion\n- Splinter haemorrhages\n-\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nFBC, Rheumatic factor, CRP/ESR, blood culture\n- ECHO (TOE, ITE) TOE has high sensitivity.\n- ECG\n- CT but can miss minor vegetation MRI in suspected with cerebral involvement in IE\n\nLV 4 Note: Blood culture and ECHO are significant for the diagnosis of IE.\n-\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nTreatment should be based on complication eg: in sepsis patient, follow sepsis protocol\n- and cardiac failure patient may require diuresis and ventilatory support. use broad-spectrum antibiotics for eradicating bacteria\n- Surgical in patient with\n- Sinus of Valvsalva Abscess\n- Valve obstruction by vegetation\n- Acute Aortic or Mitral Regurgitation with N YHA Class 3 or 4 failure\n- Septal Perforation\n- Peri-valvular extension of abscess\n-\n\n- Lack of effective antibiotic therapy\n- Key Summary_\n- Any patient present with chest pain elevate with coughing or leaning forward along with pericardial friction rub and ECG changes think about pericarditis.\n- Any patient presented with chest pain associated with fever and has History of viral illness think about myocarditis.\n- For IE focus on its module. previous  Next\n- 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Infective Endocarditis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/infective-endocarditis/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Infective Endocarditis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Infective Endocarditis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/infective-endocarditis/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Infective Endocarditis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Infective Endocarditis - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/infective-endocarditis/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Infective Endocarditis — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Infective Endocarditis - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/infective-endocarditis/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Infective Endocarditis — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
5775a146-16fb-48c1-9d0d-20b0171efed6	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb	Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB)	article	# Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nRight bundle branch block RBBB is due to an anatomical or functional dysfunction in the right bundle branch, such that the electrical impulse is block.\n- ECG criteria for Riqht bundle branch block:\n- QRS> 0.12 seconds Lead VI — V2: The QRS complex appears as the letter "M". To be specific the QRS complex displays rsr' , Rsr' or rsR' pattern.\n- The second R- wave (denoted R') is virtually always larger than the first R' wave.\n- Lead V5, V6, l, aVL : Broad S- wave duration is greater than R- wave duration or S- wave duration is greater than 40ms in V6 and I. ST- T changes: VI\n- V2 shows downsloping ST-segment and inverted T- waves. Lead V5- V6, I and aVL shows positive T-waves. Left bundle branch block LBBB is due to an anatomical or functional dysfunction in the left bundle branch, such that the electrical impulse is block. ECG criteria for Riqht bundle branch block:\n- QRS> 0.12 seconds Lead VI — V2: Deep and broad S-wave. The small r-wave is missing or smaller than normal. If it is missing, a QRS complex appears in VI and occasionally V2, but rarely V3. The S-wave in VI maybe notched and resemble the letter\n- Lead V5, V6, l, aVL: broad, clumsy, completely positive and often notched R\n- wave\n- ST- T changes: left sided leads (V5, V6, I and aVL) shows T-wave inversion and ST segment depressions. VI-V3 shows ST\n- segment elevation and positive rarely exceeds 5mm.\n\nNormal conduction VI V4 Left bundle branch block VI Deep S' wave Broad & clumsy R- wave Right bundle branch block VI V4 A second and largerR wave Broad and deep S-wave\n\n- Examples RBBB Ill avR\n- Isolated RBBB\n- Typical RSR' pattern in Nil-2\n- Widened S waves again demonstrated in lateral leads, especially V4-6 Appropriate discordance in leads VI-2\n- Example — 2\n\nIsolated RBBB. RSR' pattern in VI-3\n-\n- Lateral S wave changed are not evident here Note note normal axis in isolated RBBB\n- Example of LBBB\n-\n\n- Broad notched R waves are best appreciated in leads aVL and I here. There is absence of Q waves in leads V5-6. previOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bundle-branch-blocks-lbbb-rbbb/figure_07_p6.jpeg)\n*Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	26	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3d434acf-d1aa-4485-9b9c-ed5e7589e37e	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-choking-adult-pediatric	Choking (Adult & Paediatric) — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Choking (Adult & Paediatric) — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 58-year-old man in a restaurant suddenly stands up, clutching his neck with both hands. He is conscious, coughing forcefully, and able to speak in short sentences. What is the most appropriate initial management?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Choking (Adult & Paediatric))](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/choking-adult-pediatric/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Perform 5 sharp abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) immediately\n- **B.** Encourage him to continue coughing forcefully and monitor closely\n- **C.** Perform a blind finger sweep of the posterior pharynx\n- **D.** Deliver 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades\n- **E.** Perform emergency needle cricothyroidotomy\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nIn mild foreign body airway obstruction (effective cough, able to speak, responsive), natural coughing is the most effective way to clear the airway. Intervention (back blows or thrusts) is indicated ONLY if the victim shows signs of severe airway obstruction (ineffective cough, inability to speak, cyanosis, stridor, or quiet struggling).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 6-year-old child who was eating peanuts suddenly develops severe airway obstruction: she cannot vocalise, has a silent cough, and is becoming cyanosed but remains conscious. What is the correct sequence of physical interventions according to UK Resuscitation guidelines?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Choking (Adult & Paediatric))](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/choking-adult-pediatric/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Give up to 5 back blows followed by up to 5 abdominal thrusts; alternate cycles\n- **B.** Give 5 chest thrusts followed by 5 abdominal thrusts\n- **C.** Start 30 chest compressions immediately\n- **D.** Perform blind finger sweep\n- **E.** Deliver 5 back blows only and repeat indefinitely\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor conscious children >1 year with severe foreign body airway obstruction (FBAO): Give up to 5 sharp back blows. If unresolved, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich). Repeat the cycle of 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts until dislodged or the child becomes unresponsive.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nAn 8-month-old infant is brought into the ED choking on a plastic toy part. The infant is conscious but has ineffective coughing, severe stridor, and cyanosis. Which physical intervention is CONTRAINDICATED in an infant under 1 year of age?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Choking (Adult & Paediatric))](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/choking-adult-pediatric/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Back blows with the infant positioned head-down on the rescuer's forearm\n- **B.** Chest thrusts using two fingers over the lower sternum\n- **C.** Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre)\n- **D.** Direct laryngoscopy with Magill forceps under direct vision\n- **E.** Oxygen administration by blow-by once cleared\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n**Explanation:**\nAbdominal thrusts are CONTRAINDICATED in infants (<1 year) because of the horizontal orientation of the ribs and the relative lack of protection for the liver and spleen, which risks fatal hepatic rupture or visceral laceration. Infants receive alternating cycles of 5 back blows and 5 CHEST thrusts.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 45-year-old choking victim collapses and becomes unconscious while receiving back blows. What is the immediate next step in management?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Choking (Adult & Paediatric))](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/choking-adult-pediatric/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Perform a blind finger sweep to retrieve the object\n- **B.** Carefully support the patient to the ground and start CPR immediately with 30 chest compressions\n- **C.** Attempt 5 abdominal thrusts on the floor\n- **D.** Perform needle cricothyroidotomy immediately\n- **E.** Check a carotid pulse for 60 seconds before acting\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nWhen any choking victim becomes unresponsive, immediately place them on a firm flat surface, call for emergency resuscitation team, and begin CPR starting with 30 chest compressions. Chest compressions generate higher intrathoracic airway pressures than abdominal thrusts and may dislodge the foreign body.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nFollowing successful relief of severe choking in a 35-year-old woman using 5 abdominal thrusts, she is breathing comfortably and speaking normally. What clinical evaluation is mandatory before safe discharge?\n\n- **A.** No evaluation needed, discharge home immediately\n- **B.** Careful abdominal examination and clinical assessment for internal traumatic injuries\n- **C.** Mandatory whole-body CT scan\n- **D.** Prophylactic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics for 7 days\n- **E.** Immediate barium swallow study\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nAbdominal thrusts (and chest thrusts) can cause serious internal injuries, including gastric rupture, splenic or hepatic laceration, and rib/sternal fractures. Guidelines mandate that all patients who have received abdominal thrusts must be examined by a medical practitioner to rule out occult intra-abdominal trauma.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
83f5bda9-d078-4864-af77-2b99de50174d	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-hemorrhagic-shock	Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old motorcyclist arrives after a high-speed collision. He is pale and clammy. HR 126 bpm, BP 86/52 mmHg, RR 28/min, GCS 13. Urine output via catheter is 15 ml/hr. According to the ATLS classification, which class of haemorrhagic shock is this patient exhibiting?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/hemorrhagic-shock/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Class I (Compensated)\n- **B.** Class II (Mild)\n- **C.** Class III (Moderate)\n- **D.** Class IV (Severe / Exsanguinating)\n- **E.** Neurogenic shock\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n**Explanation:**\nClass III haemorrhagic shock is characterised by 30-40% blood volume loss (1500-2000 ml), marked tachycardia (>120 bpm), HYPOTENSION (systolic BP drops), tachypnoea (20-30), decreased urine output (5-15 ml/hr), and anxiety/confusion. In Class II BP is still normal.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nIn the initial resuscitation of severe haemorrhagic trauma shock prior to definitive surgical or radiological haemostasis, what is the target systolic blood pressure in 'Permissive Hypotension' (damage control resuscitation) in the absence of traumatic brain injury?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/hemorrhagic-shock/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Systolic BP 120-130 mmHg\n- **B.** Systolic BP 80-90 mmHg (or palpable radial pulse)\n- **C.** Systolic BP 60-70 mmHg\n- **D.** Systolic BP 140-150 mmHg\n- **E.** Mean Arterial Pressure >95 mmHg\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nPermissive hypotension targets a systolic BP of 80-90 mmHg (MAP 50-65 mmHg) in patients with penetrating or blunt haemorrhagic trauma until surgical haemostasis is achieved. Overzealous fluid administration 'pops the clot', dilutes clotting factors, and worsens hypothermia. (Note: in traumatic brain injury, target systolic is >=100-110 mmHg to maintain CPP).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nBased on the landmark CRASH-2 trial, what is the recommended dose and administration window for Tranexamic Acid (TXA) in trauma patients with significant haemorrhage?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/hemorrhagic-shock/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 1g IV bolus over 10 minutes followed by 1g IV infusion over 8 hours, started within 3 hours of injury\n- **B.** 2g IV bolus given at 6 hours post-injury\n- **C.** 500mg IV single dose within 12 hours\n- **D.** 1g orally once daily for 3 days\n- **E.** TXA is only indicated if fibrinogen is <1.0 g/L on laboratory testing\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCRASH-2 demonstrated that Tranexamic Acid (TXA) reduces all-cause mortality in trauma haemorrhage when given within 3 hours of injury. The protocol is 1g IV over 10 minutes, followed by 1g IV over 8 hours. Giving TXA >3 hours after injury is associated with increased mortality and is contraindicated.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat constitutes the 'Lethal Triad' in trauma resuscitation that drives uncorrectable coagulopathic haemorrhage and death?\n\n- **A.** Hypertension, Bradycardia, Irregular breathing\n- **B.** Hypothermia, Coagulopathy, and Acidosis\n- **C.** Hypokalaemia, Hyperglycaemia, Hypernatraemia\n- **D.** Tachypnoea, Tachycardia, Oliguria\n- **E.** Hypocalcaemia, Thrombocytosis, Leucopenia\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nThe Trauma Lethal Triad consists of Hypothermia, Acidosis, and Coagulopathy. Hypothermia impairs enzyme coagulation kinetics; acidosis reduces thrombin generation; coagulopathy accelerates blood loss, perpetuating the vicious cycle.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nIn damage control resuscitation for massive haemorrhage, what is the recommended ratio of Packed Red Blood Cells (PRBC) to Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) to Platelets in empirical massive transfusion protocols (MTP)?\n\n- **A.** 4 : 1 : 1\n- **B.** 1 : 1 : 1 (balanced haemostatic resuscitation)\n- **C.** 10 : 2 : 1\n- **D.** 1 : 4 : 0\n- **E.** PRBC only until haemoglobin is >100 g/L\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nThe PROPPR trial and international major trauma guidelines recommend a balanced 1:1:1 ratio of PRBCs, FFP, and platelets (or reconstituted whole blood) to prevent early dilution of platelets and clotting factors during massive transfusion.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
bd2a36a2-daca-499c-87dd-f40e0fe25305	a91bc382-0d11-491a-96eb-7b1607b7e3d4	ventilation-perfusion-mismatch	Ventilation Perfusion Mismatch	article	Ventilation- Perfusion mismatch\nThe ratio of alveolar ventilation to pulmonary blood flow is V/Q\nratio.\nFor the lung at rest, it is about 0.8.\nVentilation-Perfusion at base and apex of lung:\nBoth ventilation and perfusion are highest towards the lung\nbase because of gravitational effects and low at the apex of\nlung\nGravitational effects are greater on perfusion than ventilation,\nso V/Q ratio is high at apex and low at base\nAbnormal V/Q:\nVentilation perfusion mismatch (V/Q) most common cause of\nHypoxemia\n\n### 1: Ventilation defect: Alveoli perfused but not ventilated\n\nProduce intrapulmonary shunting. In large ventilation defect,\nV/Q is zero, which is called a shunt.\nThere is no gas exchange, increased A-a gradient.\nPartial pressure of pulmonary CO2 increases while that of\noxygen decreases because normal amount of oxygen for\nventilation is not available.\nV/Q ratio zero-pulmonary shunt: No ventilation but\nperfusion remains normal Causes: ARDS, Compression\natelectasis due to hydrothorax or pneumothorax.\nV/Q ratio <1: Low ventilation relative to its perfusion such as\nin partial obstruction of airways. Causes: Low compliance\ne.g., pulmonary fibrosis, High airway resistance e.g., COPD,\nasthma\n\n### 2: Perfusion defect (Ventilation normal but perfusion\n\ndecreased)\nIf blood flow to a lung is completely blocked (e.g., by an\nembolism occluding a pulmonary artery), then blood flow to that lung is zero. If ventilation is normal, then V/Q is infinite,\nwhich is called dead space.\nPartial pressure of pulmonary CO2 decreases while that of\noxygen increases.\nThere is no gas exchange. ↑A-a gradient. As a result, partial\npressure of oxygen and CO2 equal to inspired air.\nV/Q ratio of infinity-dead space:\nNo perfusion but ventilation remains normal.\nCauses: Embolism\nAnatomic and physiologic\nshunt\nThe shunted blood will not have been oxygenated or been\nable to offload CO2 and thus its levels of PO2 and PCO2 are\nthat of mixed venous blood. Changes in PCO2 and\nPO2 stimulate the chemoreceptors and increase ventilation,\nso that arterial PCO2 returns to normal.\nHowever, increased ventilation cannot increase blood\nO2 content, as the haemoglobin of the blood passing\nthrough the lungs is already close to saturation. Thus, right to\nleft shunts commonly result in a low arterial PO2 but a\nnormal or low PCO2.\n\n![Ventilation Perfusion Mismatch - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/ventilation-perfusion-mismatch/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Ventilation Perfusion Mismatch — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
435e66d3-ab6c-42bf-bfd6-cbfb2c515850	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	acid-base-balance	Acid Base Balance	article	Acid base balance\nAcid production\nTwo types of acids are produced in the body: volatile acid and\nnon-volatile acids.\nVolatile acid\nIs CO2\nProduced from aerobic metabolism of cells.\nCO2 combines with H2O to form weak acid H2CO3, which\ndissociates into H and HCO3.\nCarbonic anhydrase is present in most cells, catalyzes the\nreversible reaction between CO2 and H2O\nNon-volatile acids\nAre also called fixed acids.\nInclude sulphuric acid and phosphoric acid.\nAre normally produced at a rate of 40-60mmoles/day.\nOther fixed acids include ketoacids, lactic acid, and salicylic\nacid.\nBuffers\nPrevent a change in PH when H ions are added to or\nremoved from a solution.\nExtracellular buffers\nThe major EC buffer is HCO3, which is produced from CO2\nand H2O\nPhosphate is a minor EC buffer, PO4 is most important as\nurinary buffer; excretion of H as H2PO4 is called titratable\nacid.\nIntracellular buffers\nOrganic phosphates e.g., AMP, ADP, ATP, 2,3-\ndisphosphoglycerate (DPG)\nHemoglobin is a major IC buffer.\n\nIn the physiologic PH range deoxyhemoglobin is a better\nbuffer than oxyhemoglobin.\nRenal acid base\nReabsorption of HCO3-\nReabsorption of filtered HCO3- occurs primarily in the\nproximal tubule.\nH+ and HCO3- are produced in the proximal tubular cells\nfrom CO2 and H2O. CO2 and H2O combine to form H2CO3,\ncatalyzed by IC carbonic anhydrase; H2CO3 dissociates into\nH+ and HCO3. H+ is secreted into the lumen via Na-H\nexchange mechanism in the luminal membrane. The HCO3 is\nreabsorbed.\nIn the lumen, the secreted H+ combines with filtered HCO3-\nto form H2CO3, which dissociates into CO2 and H2O,\ncatalyzed by brush border carbonic anhydrase. CO2 and H2O\ndiffuse into the cell to start cycle again.\nThe process results in net reabsorption of filtered HCO3-.\nHowever, it does not result in net secretion of H+\nRegulation of reabsorption of filtered HCO3\nIncreases in the filtered load of HCO3- result in increased\nrates of HCO3- reabsorption. However, if plasma HCO3 conc\nbecomes very high (metabolic alkalosis), the filtered load will\nexceed the re absorptive capacity and HCO3- will be excreted\nin urine.\nIncrease in Pco² results in increased rates of HCO3-\nreabsorption because the supply of IC H+ for secretion is\nincreased. This mechanism is the basis for renal\ncompensation for respiratory acidosis.\nDecrease in Pco² results in decreased rates of HCO3-\nreabsorption because the supply of IC H+ for secretion is\ndecreased. This mechanism is the basis for the renal\ncompensation for respiratory alkalosis.\nECF volume expansion results in decreased HCO3\nreabsorption.\nECF volume contraction results in increased HCO3\nreabsorption (contraction alkalosis)\nAngiotensin II stimulates Na-H exchange and thus increases\nHCO3- reabsorption, contributing to the contraction alkalosis\nthat occurs secondary to ECF volume contraction.\n\nExcretion of fixed H\nFixed H+ produced from the catabolism of protein and\nphospholipid is excreted by two mechanism, titratable acid\nand NH ⁴\nThe amount of H+ excreted as titratable acid (H2PO4)\ndepends on amount of urinary buffer present (usually HPO4)\nand the pK of buffer.\nH+ and HCO3- are produced in the intercalated cells from\nCO2 and H2O. The H is secreted into the lumen by an H-\nATPase, and the HCO3- is reabsorbed into the blood (new\nHCO3). In the urine secreted H+ combines with filtered HPO4\nto form H2PO4, which is excreted as titratable acid, The H-\nATPase is increased by aldosterone.\nThis process results in net secretion of H+ and net\nreabsorption of newly synthesized HCO3-.\nAs a result of H+ secretion, the PH of urine becomes\nprogressively lower. The minimum urinary PH is 4.4.\nThe amount of excreted as titratable acid is determined by\nthe amount of urinary buffer and pK of buffer.\nExcretion of H as NH ⁴\nThe amount of H excreted as NH ⁴  depends on both amount\nof NH3 synthesized by renal cells and urine PH.\nNH3 is produced in renal cells from glutamine. It diffuses\ndown its concentration gradient from the cells into the\nlumen.\nH and HCO3- are produced from intercalated cells from CO2\nand H2O. The H is secreted into lumen via an H- ATPase and\ncombines with NH3 to form NH4 which is excreted (diffusion\ntrapping). The HCO3 is reabsorbed into the blood (new\n\n## Hco3).\n\nThe lower the PH of tubular fluid, the greater the excretion of\nH as NH4; at low urine PH, there is more NH4 relative to NH3\nin the urine, thus increasing the gradient for NH3 diffusion.\nIn acidosis, an adaptive increase in NH3 synthesis occurs and\naids in the excretion of excess H.\nHyperkalemia inhibits NH3 synthesis, which produces a\ndecrease in H excretion as NH4 (Type 4 renal tubular\nacidosis). For example, Hypoaldosteronism causes\nhyperkalemia and thus also causes type 4 RTA. Conversely, hypokalemia stimulates NH3 synthesis, which produces an\nincrease in H excretion.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
760a38cb-74d4-4560-a8d4-b6aa98358702	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	acid-base-disorder	Acid Base Disorder	article	Acid base disorders\nAcid-base disorders are pathologic changes in carbon dioxide\npartial pressure (Pco2) or serum bicarbonate (HCO3−) that\ntypically produce abnormal arterial pH values.\nAcidemia is serum pH < 7.35.\nAlkalemia is serum pH > 7.45.\nAcidosis refers to physiologic processes that cause acid\naccumulation or alkali loss.\nAlkalosis refers to physiologic processes that cause alkali\naccumulation or acid loss.\nVentilation controls the CO2 levels and kidney controls the\nHCO3 levels.\nMetabolic acidosis\nOverproduction or ingestion of fixed acid or loss of base\nproduces a decrease in arterial [HCO3]. This decrease is the\nprimary disturbance in metabolic acidosis.\nDecreased HCO3 concentration causes a decrease in blood\nPH (Acidemia)\nAcidemia causes hyperventilation (Kussmaul breathing),\nwhich is respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis.\nCorrection of metabolic acidosis consists of increased\nexcretion of excess fixed H as titratable acid and NH4 and\nincreased reabsorption of new HCO3, which replenishes the\nblood [HCO3]\nIn chronic metabolic acidosis, an adaptive increase in NH3\nsynthesis aids in the excretion of excess H.\nSerum anion gap = ([Na+] + [K+]) - ([Cl-] + [HCO3-])\nSerum anion gap represents the difference between\nmeasured cations and measured anions in plasma. As\nplasma is always electrically neutral, the anion gap\ndetermines the presence of unmeasured anions. These\nusually include proteins, sulphate, phosphate and organic\nacids.\n\nNormal value of serum anion gap is 12meq/L (range 8-\n16meq/L)\nIn metabolic acidosis, serum [HCO3-] decreases. For\nelectroneutrality, the concentration of another anion must\nincrease to replace HCO3-. That anion can be Cl-, or it can be\nan unmeasured anion.\nSerum anion gap is increased if the new acid is added to the\nbody, this dissociates producing new H+ (which uses up\nHCO3-) and anions (which replaces HCO3-)\nSerum anion gap is normal if the concentration of Cl- is\nincreased to replace HCO3+ (hyperchloremic metabolic\nacidosis\nNormal anion gap metabolic acidosis\n\n## Nagma:\n\nA normal anion gap occurs if there is loss of HCO3. This\ncauses a compensatory rise in plasma [Cl], so the anion gap is\nnormal (hyperchloremic acidosis).\nCauses: (Mnemonic: USED-CAR)\nUreteroenteric conduit\nSmall bowel fistula/ Saline administration\nExogenous Acid\nDiarrhea\nCarbonic anhydrase inhibitor\nAdrenal insufficiency\nRenal tubular acidosis\nCauses of high anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA)\n\n## (Mudpiles)\n\nMethanol, metformin\nUremia\nDiabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)\nPropylene glycol\nIsoniazid, iron overdose, infection\nLactic acidosis\nEthanol, ethylene glycol\nSalicylates\nNote: The most common cause of low anion gap\nis hypoalbuminemia\n\nMetabolic alkalosis\nLoss of fixed H+ or gain of base produces an increase in\narterial [HCO3-]. This increase is the primary disturbance in\nmetabolic alkalosis.\nIn vomiting H is lost from stomach, HCO3 remains behind in\nthe blood, and the [HCO3] increases, increased [HCO3] causes\nan increase in blood PH (Alkalemia). Alkalemia causes\nhypoventilation which is the respiratory compensation for\nmetabolic alkalosis. It has features of hypocalcemia\n(decreased unbound plasma Ca) and hypokalemia (because\nof IC shift of K) e.g., tetany, paresthesia, muscle cramps.\nCorrection of metabolic alkalosis consists of increased\nexcretion of HCO3 because the filtered load of HCO3 exceeds\nthe ability of renal tubule to reabsorb it.\nIf metabolic alkalosis is accompanied by ECF volume\ncontraction (vomiting), the reabsorption of HCO3 increases\n(secondary to ECF volume contraction and activation of the\nrenin angiotensin II aldosterone system), worsening the\nmetabolic alkalosis (contraction alkalosis).\nCauses:\nGastrointestinal losses\nVomiting, nasogastric drainage, pyloric stenosis\nSome villous adenomas\nRenal losses\nChloruretic drugs (e.g., thiazides, furosemide)\nMineralocorticoid excess syndromes\nPrimary Hyperaldosteronism\nOther causes:\nAlkali intake or administration\nMilk alkali syndrome\nsevere potassium depletion (serum potassium <2 mmol/L)\nRespiratory acidosis\n\nRespiratory acidosis is carbon dioxide (CO2) accumulation\n(hypercapnia) due to a decrease in respiratory rate and/or\nrespiratory volume (hypoventilation).\nIt is caused by decreased alveolar ventilation\n(Hypoventilation) and retention of CO2.\nIncreased arteriolar Pco², which is primary disturbance,\ncauses an increase in [H] and [HCO3] by mass action.\nThere is no respiratory compensation for respiratory acidosis.\nRenal compensation consists of increased excretion of H as\ntitratable acid and NH4 and increased reabsorption of new\nHCO3. This process is aided by increased Pco², which supplies\nmore H to the renal cells for secretion. The resulting increase\nin serum [HCO3] helps to normalize the PH.\nIn acute respiratory acidosis, renal compensation has not yet\noccurred.\nIn chronic respiratory acidosis, renal compensation has\noccurred. Thus, arterial PH is increased towards normal (i.e\ncompensation)\nCauses\nSedatives\nOpioid overdose\nAirway obstruction, COPD, asthma, pulmonary oedema\nAdult RDS\nNeuromuscular dysfunction including Myasthenia gravis,\nMultiple sclerosis, GBS, polio.\nRespiratory alkalosis\nIt is caused by increased alveolar ventilation\n(Hyperventilation) and loss of CO2.\nDecreased arterial Pco², which is the primary disturbance,\ncauses decrease in [H] and [HCO3] by mass action.\nThere is no respiratory compensation for respiratory.\nMore common than respiratory acidosis, usually acute and\nuncompensated.\nRenal compensation consists of decreased excretion of H as\ntitratable acid and NH4 and decreased reabsorption of new\nHCO3, this process is aided by the decreased Pco2 which\ncauses a deficit of H in the renal cells for secretion. The\nresulting decrease in serum [HCO3] helps to normalize the\nPH.\n\nIn acute respiratory alkalosis, renal compensation has not\nyet occurred.\nIn chronic respiratory alkalosis, renal compensation\n(decreased HCO3 reabsorption has occurred. Thus, arterial PH\nis decreased towards normal (i.e., compensation)\nCauses\nHigh altitude (Hypoxia induced)\nSalicylate intoxication (aspirin overdose)\nSepsis\nStress\nThyrotoxicosis\nPregnancy\nPulmonary disorder\nHyperventilation (Due to heart disorder or other, including\nimproper mechanical ventilation)\nVocal cord paralysis (compensation for loss of vocal volume\nresults in over-breathing/breathlessness).	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
599df14c-d81e-4178-a000-64ad71d979ac	c5a116c4-d2b6-4edb-bf1f-d05abfe7a1d8	gastrointestinal-tract	Gastrointestinal Tract	article	Gastrointestinal tract\nThe gastrointestinal tract (GI tract, digestive tract, alimentary\ncanal) is the tract or passageway of the digestive system that\nleads from the mouth to the anus. The GI tract contains all the\nmajor organs of the digestive system, in humans and other\nanimals, including the esophagus, stomach, and intestines.\nFood taken in through the mouth is digested to extract\nnutrients and absorb energy, and the waste expelled at the\nanus as feces.\nThe complete human digestive system is made up of the\ngastrointestinal tract plus the accessory organs of digestion\n(the tongue, salivary glands, pancreas, liver and gallbladder)\nFunction of GIT\nIngestion of food and water\nMastication\nTransport of the food bolus or chyme through the\ngastrointestinal tract\nDigestion (i.e., breaking food down into nutrients)\nAbsorption of nutrients and water\nExcretion of waste as feces\nImmunity\n\nStructures of GI tract:\nAll segments of the GI tract are divided into four layers (from\ninnermost to outer most) are:\n1.The innermost mucosa composed of:\nthe epithelial layer (specialized in different parts of the GI\ntract for secretion and absorption\nthe lamina propria (consisting of loose tissue, blood\nvessels and lymph tissue)\nthe muscularis mucosa (contraction causes a change in\nthe surface area for secretion or absorption)\n\n### 2. The submucosa (containing a second layer of connective\n\ntissue, large blood vessels and lymphatic vessels and\nsubmucosal plexus\n\n![Gastrointestinal Tract - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/gastrointestinal-tract/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Tract — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n3.The muscularis externa composed of:\nThe inner circular muscle layer (contraction causes a\ndecrease in diameter of the lumen of the GI tract)\nThe outer longitudinal muscle layer (contraction causes\nshortening of a segment of the GI tract)\nThe myenteric plexus which lies between these two layers\nof muscle\n\n### 4. The outer most serosa (another connective tissue layer\n\ncovered with squamous mesothelial cells)	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.389724+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
1fbd918d-88a9-4fc8-bb76-21fe8019a03a	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	bradycardias	Bradycardias	article	Definition\nBradycardia is a heart rate of fewer than 60 beats per minute.\nTypes\nSinus bradycardia\nSinus arrest\nJunctional bradycardia\nAtrioventricular blocks:\nFirst degree\nSecond degree (Mobitz types I and II)\nThird-degree (complete heart block)\nCauses\nNormal physiological variants as seen in athletes\nCardiac causes (ischemic & structural heart disease)\nInfectious\nSystemic illness (hypothyroidism, hypothermia)\nAutonomic mediated (carotid hypersensitivity, vasovagal\nsyncope)\nHead injury\nHyper-hypokalaemia\nDrugs (High yield)\nBeta-blockers\nCCB\nAmitriptyline\nQuinidine\nClonidine\nDigoxin\nOrganophosphate\n\nManagement approach (unstable Pt)\nThe ALS bradycardia algorithm recommends Atropine 500\nmcg IV up to 3mg maximum if any Pt presents with the\nfollowing:\nAdverse features or risk of asystole:\nShock\nSyncope\nMyocardial ischemia\nHeart failure\nRisk of asystole:\nRecent asystole\nMobiztype-2 Av block\nComplete heart block\nVentricular pause >3 seconds\nBradycardia (Resus council of the UK and European\nresuscitation council guideline)\nIf bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock,\nSyncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure as mentioned\nabove) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat\nevery 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\nIf treatment with atropine is ineffective\nconsider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline\n(5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\nConsider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel\nblockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\nConsider pacing in patients who are unstable, with\nsymptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\nIf transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous\npacing.\nAtropine\nAtropine antagonises the action of the parasympathetic\nneurotransmitter acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. It\ntherefore blocks the effects of the vagus nerve on both the SA\nnode and\nthe AV node, increasing sinus automaticity and facilitating AV node conduction.\nSide effects: (Anti-muscarinic effects)\n- Dry mouth\n- Nausea and vomiting\n- Blurred vision\n- Urinary retention\n- Tachyarrhythmias\nNote: Anti-muscarinic should be avoided in Myasthenia\ngravis, paralytic ileus, GI obstruction, Urinary retention and\nprostatic enlargement	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
7e8a2aa2-8c22-442a-b085-8f839e079862	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	ecg-axis-interpretation	ECG Axis Interpretation	article	# ECG Axis Interpretation\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- 1500 aVR 1800\n- 900 Extreme AXIS Deviation RAD aVF LAD\n- 300 aVL + 1200 +900 NORMAL QRS AXIS + 600\n\n- Methods of ECG Axis Interpretation There are several complementary approaches to estimating QRS axis, however, we will focus on two:\n- The Quadrant Method- (Lead I and aVF)\n- Three Lead analysis— (Lead I, Lead II and III)\n- The Quadrant Method\n- The rnost efficient way to estimate axis is to look at LEAD I and LEAD aVF.\n- Examine the QRS complex in each lead and determine if it is Positive, Isoelectric (Equiphasic) or Negative. iiiiiäi POSITIVE s s EQUIPHASIC NEGATIVE\n- A positive QRS in Lead I puts the axis in roughly the same direction as lead l.\n- A positive QRS in Lead aVF similarly aligns the axis with lead aVF.\n\n- Combining both coloured areas — the quadrant of overlap determines the axis. So If Lead I and aVF are both positive, the axis is between 00 and +900 (i.e. normal axis).\n- Example — I gift\n- To use Quadrant Method\n- Lead I = NEGATIVE\n- Lead II = Equiphasic\n- Lead aVF = POSITIVE\n- This puts the axis in the quadrant, between +900 and +1800, i.e.RAD (Right axis deviation)\n- Three Lead\n\n- Lead Illor aVF can both be used in three lead analysis Normal Axis 0 to 90 Left Axis Physiological O to-30 Left Axis Pathological\n- 30 to\n- 90 Right Axis 90 to 180 Extreme Axis\n- 90 to\n- 180 Indeterminate Lead I Lead 11 Lead 111\n- Example\n- 1\n\n-\n- To use Lead l, II, aVF Lead I = POSITIVE Lead II = POSITIVE aVF = POSITIVE\n- This puts the axis in the quadrant between 00 and +900 — i.e.normal axis previ0US\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![ECG Axis Interpretation - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ecg-axis-interpretation/figure_07_p7.jpeg)\n*ECG Axis Interpretation — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 7 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
2b5807d6-f016-46cf-801b-2cdb9e7fc513	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks	Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks	article	# Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Examples of complete Trifascicular heart block\n- Complete Trifascicular Block\n- Right bundle branch block\n- Left axis deviation (Left anterior fascicular block)\n- Third-degree heart block Example — 2\n\niiih — Incomplete trifascicular block (bifascicular block + first-degree AV\n- Example block)\n\n- Incomplete heart block\n- Right bundle branch block\n- Left axis deviation (= left anterior fascicular block)\n- First-degree AV block\n- Example\n- Bifascicular block\n\n- bifascicular block pattern:RBBB combined with LAFB (manifested as LAD). Example\n- 2 *Æiiii"" "i itiiiiiütfi$fiii iiiii iii\\i iiiiiiiiii iiiii iiiii iiifiiiiiifiiii iiiii Wiii iiiii iiiiifiiiiiiiii iiii Éiij iiiii iiiiiiii#iiiii iiiiiiiii iii liiii liiii iliiiiiiiiiiili)iiii gliii\n\n- RBBB with LAFB\n- RBBB pattern in precordial leads with RSR' complex in VI-2\n- Prominent LAD indicating LAFB. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_06_p5.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/bifascicular-trifascicular-blocks/figure_07_p6.jpeg)\n*Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f90d6a12-2fa0-4684-a553-45ff66ef9c75	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	autoregulation	Autoregulation	article	Autoregulation\nBlood flow throughout the kidney: renal artery=arcuate\nartery=afferent arteriole=glomerular capillaries=efferent\narterioles=peritubular capillaries=vasa recta=arcuate\nveins=renal vein\nThe kidneys are very effective in auto regulating blood flow.\nThis is primarily due to changes in the resistance of the\nafferent arterioles, for which 2 mechanisms are involved.\nMyogenic responses:\nMR is a function of smooth muscle to contract in response to\nexternal stretching force. In the case of vascular smooth\nmuscle, this causes vasoconstriction on a rise in arterial\npressure, thus allowing for autoregulation.\nTubuloglomerular feedback (TGF)\nIncreased MAP leads to an increase in RBF and GFR\nTGF is a regulating mechanism specific to the kidney that\nleads to vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole in response\nto an increase in the luminal concentration of NaCl at the\nmacula densa in the early distal tubule. Because salt\nreabsorption from the ascending part of the loop of Henle is\nan active and more rate-limited process than the passive\ndiffusion of water out of the descending loop, the\nconcentration of NaCl reaching the macula densa is\ndependent on the rate of tubular flow with larger flow\nresulting in a higher distal tubular concentration. An increase\nin arterial pressure will enhance tubular flow due to\nenhanced glomerular filtration and reduced proximal tubular\nreabsorption. This will raise the NaCl concentration at the\nmacula densa and cause afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction,\nproviding restoration of filtration and autoregulation of RBF.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
56aa2eda-35cc-46b1-8dd4-d316ef03a73f	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	functional-anatomy-of-kidney	Functional Anatomy of Kidney	article	Anatomy of Kidney\nThe kidneys are two bean-shaped organs, located on either\nside of the spine, in the retroperitoneal space. The left kidney is\nsituated a little higher than the right one, because of the liver\non the right side of the abdominal cavity, above the right\nkidney. The kidneys have a medial dimple called the renal\nhilum, which is the entry and exit point for structures that\nsupply or drain the kidneys such as the nerves, ureters, vessels,\nand lymphatics.\nParts of the Kidney\nThe cortex is the outer renal tissue located under the renal\ncapsule. It appears lighter in colour compared to the other\nparts of the kidney. The renal corpuscles, as well as the\nproximal and distal convoluted tubules are found here. It\nreaches into the inner renal tissue or medulla, splitting it into\ntriangular parts called renal pyramids.\nThe renal pyramids contain the loops of Henle of each renal\ntubule, as well as the collecting ducts. Each of the renal\npyramids opens at the apex, or renal papilla. Here the urine\nformed inside the pyramids is collected through a structure\ncalled the minor calyx. Several of the minor calices join to\nform a major calyx. Urine moves through the major calices to\nreach the renal pelvis. The pelvis is a funnel-shaped\ncollecting structure formed by the junction of all the major\ncalices. It carries urine from the whole kidney to the ureter,\nwhich begins where the pelvis ends, at the pelvi-ureteral\njunction.\nNephrons\nThe nephrons are the functional units of the kidneys, and\nnumber about 1.3 million per kidney.\nThere are two types of nephrons, cortical nephrons (85% of\nnephrons) and juxtamedullary nephrons (15% of nephrons)\n\nA nephron has two main parts - tubules (proximal\nconvoluted tubule, the loop of Henle and the distal\nconvoluted tubule, collecting duct) and renal corpuscle\n(Bowman's capsule and glomerulus).\nCortical nephrons have their renal corpuscles in the outer\ncortex and relatively short loops of Henle (LOH).\nJuxtamedullary nephrons have their corpuscles in the inner\nthird of cortex near corticomedullary junction with long LOH\nextending into renal medulla.\nGlomerulus is formed by the invagination of ball of capillaries\ninto the bowman's capsule.\nMain functions of nephron\nGlomerulus - filtration\nProximal tubule - Reabsorption of solutes\nLoop of Henle - urine concentration\nDistal tubule and collecting duct - Acid base balance and\nH2O homeostasis.\nFunctions of kidney\nExcretes waste products: Urea, uric acid, creatinine.\nWater and electrolyte balance\nAcid/base balance\nMetabolism of proteins including PTH and calcitonin\nSecretes the hormone erythropoietin and the enzyme, renin\ninto the circulation, synthesis of prostaglandins\nHydroxylates 25-hydroxy-Vit D to form the active form of\nVitamin D (1,25 dihydroxy-Vit D)\nJuxtaglomerular apparatus\nThe juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) is located between the\nafferent arteriole and the returning distal convoluted tubule of\nthe same nephron. It is responsible for regulating both\nintrarenal (tubuloglomerular feedback) and extrarenal (renin-\nangiotensin-aldosterone) mechanisms necessary to maintain\nboth renal and entire body volume status.\nThe three components of the JGA are the following:\nThe juxtaglomerular cells (granular cells) of the afferent\narteriole, synthesize and store renin, which is secreted in\nresponse to specific stimuli (e.g., low blood flow, decreased\n\nNaCl delivery). The juxtaglomerular cells could be considered\nthe "effector arm" of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis.\nThe macula densa, a region of the distal convoluted tubule\ncharacterized by columnar tubular epithelial cells which are\nmore densely packed than in other regions of the nephron.\nThe macula densa can be considered the "sensory arm" of\nthe renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis in that these are the\ncells which sense and respond to changes in tubular Na Cl\nconcentration. They are also involved in the mechanism of\ntubuloglomerular feedback.\nmesangial cells, which form connections via actin and\nmicrotubules which allow for selective\nvasoconstriction/vasodilation of the renal afferent and\nefferent arterioles with mesangial cell contraction.\nGlomerular filtration barrier\nA membrane that selectively allows the passage of molecules\nbased on size and charge. Plasma is selectively filtered through\nglomerular capillary wall into the bowman's capsule\n(Ultrafiltrate = fluid filtered into bowman' space)\nThis barrier has three major components.\n\n### 1: Glomerular capillary endothelium: It has fenestrations\n\nwhich allows plasma components with a molecular weight of\n<7000 Da to pass freely. These fenestrations actually do not\nrestrict the movement of water and proteins or large\nmolecules but instead prevent the filtration of blood cells\n(e.g. RBCs).\nFenestrated endothelium has magnitude greater than\nproteins; in addition, the wall is covered with negatively\ncharged compounds.\n\n### 2: Glomerular basement membrane: Made up of matrix of\n\nextracellular negatively charged proteins and other\ncompounds, help restrict the movement of negatively\ncharged molecules across the basement membrane. e.g.,\nAlbumin\nMain site of ultrafiltrate\nThe basement membrane consists of 3 layers: These layers\nhelp to limit the filtration of intermediate and large sized\nsolutes e.g., Albumin.\n\n### 3: Epithelial cell layer of podocytes: Next to the bowman's\n\nspace; the podocytes have foot processes directly contacting the basement membrane.\nRegular gaps between foot processes (pedicles) are called\nfiltration slits, across these slits a protein network forms slit\npores in the epithelial lining which prevent the passage of\nlarger molecules through this final layer.\nPodocytes also exhibit phagocytic and contractile activity like\nthat of mesangial cells.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
59f94060-25a0-43eb-8742-16f2aa07b64d	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	hyperkalemia-and-hypokalemia	Hyperkalemia and Hypokalemia	article	Hyperkalemia\nNormal value of serum K = 3.0- 4.5 mmol/L\nCauses\nInsulin deficiency\nExcessive dietary K\nRenal failure\nAddison's disease (Hypoaldosteronism)\nK+ sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs,\ntrimethoprim and pentamidine therapy, B adrenergic\nantagonists\nAcidosis\nHyperosmolarity (H2O flows out of cell; K diffuses out with\nwater)\nNa-K pump inhibitors (digitalis) when pump is blocked, K is\nnot taken up into cells.\nExercise\nCell lysis/ rhabdomyolysis / trauma /burns/ crush syndrome,\nchemotherapy\nPseudo hyperkalemia:  occurs in\nTest tube hemolysis\nProlonged tourniquet time\nDifficulty collecting sample.\nLength of storage of specimen\nSample from limb receiving IV fluid containing K\nECG findings of hyperkalemia\nCauses rapid reduction in RMP leading to increased cardiac\ndepolarization and muscle excitability which causes ECG\nchanges that can progress to ventricular fibrillation of\nasystole.\nTall, peaked T wave\nShort QT interval\nLengthening of PR interval\nWidening of QRS complex\nLoss of ST segment\nLoss of P wave\nSinus wave appearance\n\nLate 2026)\n\nTreatment of hyperkalemia\nIf there are ECG changes: Stabilize the cardiac membrane\nfirst, e.g., with IV calcium gluconate (10 ml of 10% solution IV\nover 2 minutes).   Followed by or treatment of hyperkalemia\nwithout ECG changes:\nshift the K+ into cells:\nIv glucose and insulin\nInhaled beta-2 agonist (e.g., salbutamol)\nIV sodium bicarbonate (if acidosis present)\nRemove K from Body:\nIV furosemide and normal saline (if adequate renal function)\nIon-exchange resin (sodium polystyrene sulfonate)\nDialysis (in renal failure or severe refractory cases)\nHypokalemia\nCauses\nRenal Losses\nThiazide or loop diuretics\nRenal tubular acidosis\nHypomagnesaemia\n\n![Hyperkalemia and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/renal-physiology-acid-base-balance/hyperkalemia-and-hypokalemia/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Hyperkalemia and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nDrugs causing increased K+ excretion like penicillin,\naminoglycosides, amphotericin\nGI loss\nDiarrhea and vomiting\nFactors causing increased IC shift of K+\nInsulin excess or overdose\nCatecholamines in acute stress\nB2 agonists\nDecreased intake.\nMalnutrition, anorexia nervosa, inadequate replacement\nwhile on TPN or NPO\nNote  :\nAcidosis redistributes K + out of cells=\nHyperkalemia\nAlkalosis redistributes K+ into cells= Hypokalemia\nEGG findings of Hypokalemia\nIncreases automaticity and delays cardiac cells depolarization\nleading to dysrhythmias e.g ectopic AV block, atrial and\nventricular fibrillation.\nProgressive lengthening of PR interval\nST segment depression\nFlattening of T wave\nProminent U wave\nProlongation of QT interval\nTreatment of hypokalemia\nTreat the underlying disorder.\nOral and/or IV potassium replacement\nMagnesium replacement, if hypomagnesemia is present.\nCauses of increased distal K secretion\nHigh K diet\nHyperaldosteronism\nAlkalosis\nThiazide diuretics\nLoop diuretics\nLuminal anions\nCauses of decreased distal K secretion\nLow K diet\nHypoaldosteronism\nAcidosis\nK sparing diuretics	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
20846bda-62ba-4426-b342-ae011770b68f	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	long-qt-syndrome-torsades	Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes	article	# Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n\n\n## Key Clinical Features\npalpitations\n- chest pain, and dyspnea\n- dizziness\n- syncope during heightened adrenergic tones\n- Patients with LQTI (mutations in\n- the KCNQI gene) typically have cardiac events during exercise, particularly swimming. Angina\n- Fatigue\n- Muscle weakness\n-\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG for LQTI, LQT2, LQT3\n- ECG for hypokalaemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia ECG for heart block\n- Serum calcium, potassium, magnesium\n- Consider genetic testing\n- Consider echocardiography\n- Consider Holter monitor\n-\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nManagement of Acquired LQTS\n-\n\nAcquired LQTS without previous cardiac events First line\n- Remove or treat the causative factors\n- i-e correct electrolyte imbalance or discontinue the causative drugs. Consider beta blocker therapy: Low-dose\n- beta-blockers are prescribed initially and titrated up as tolerated\n- Advise on lifestyle modification\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof-Congeni.taLLQTS Acquired LQTS with previous cardiac events First line\n- Remove or treat the causative factors\n- Advise on lifestyle modification\n- Consider beta blocker therapy\n- Consider implantable cardioverter- defibrillator (ICD) Second line Temporary or permanent pacing\n\nCongenital LQTS without previous cardiac events Low risk Remove or treat the causative\n- factors\n- Advise on lifestyle modification\n- Consider beta blocker therapy High risk\n- Remove or treat the causative\n- factors.\n- Plus beta blocker therapy\n- Consider implantable cardioverter- defibrillator (ICD) Consider mexiletine Second line\n-\n- Consider left cervicothoracic sympathetic denervation.\n- Complications\n- Torsades de pointes\n- Cardiac arrest\n- Sudden cardiac death\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof torsade de pointes\n\nCongenital LQTS with previous cardiac events First line Remove or treat the causative factors. Plus beta blocker therapy Consider implantable cardioverter- defibrillator (ICD) Consider mexiletine Second line left cervicothoracic sympathetic denervation. Third line Permanent pacing\n\n- Treat or remove the causative agents\n- Correct electrolyte imbalance\n- If patient has adverse features (hypotension, shock, etc) treat immediately with synchronized cardioversion\n- Other-a.LLQa.tj.e.n.ts-give intravenous magnesium sulfate 2g over IO minutes\n- ECG torsade de pointes\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Long QT Syndrome & Torsades - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/long-qt-syndrome-torsades/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Long QT Syndrome & Torsades — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Long QT Syndrome & Torsades - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/long-qt-syndrome-torsades/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Long QT Syndrome & Torsades — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Long QT Syndrome & Torsades - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/long-qt-syndrome-torsades/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Long QT Syndrome & Torsades — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Long QT Syndrome & Torsades - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/long-qt-syndrome-torsades/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Long QT Syndrome & Torsades — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	28	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c39caa8d-b614-42ae-867e-6a4cabce6d6d	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	non-invasive-ventilation-niv-guidelines	Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines	article	# Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nNIV Indications Types of NIV\n- CPAP RAO 3 tin Neuromuscular disease *4th vcstl 6.0 Absolute (pH«7.25 nd Indications for referral to ICU Mth MV failing to ln\n- bait" to need Contra- indications Mnemonic: she has a Beautiful face CPR675 for (c02 6.5, PH<7.35 RR_23) Fixed upper airway obstruction eg: the tongue, oropharynx and larynx, trauma, foreign body, and infection etc\n- Indicate for type-I respiratory failure eg: acute cardiogenic oedema and obstructive sleep apnoea\n- Procedure:\n- Start with a CPAP 5-10cmH20 can increase to 15cmH20 Try to achieve saturation 94-98%\n\n- BiPAp\n- Indicate for type-2 failure eg: COPD and neuromuscular disease\n- Start with initial IPAP 12-15cmH20 up-to maximum 30cmH20 as per need and EPAP 3-5cmH20 Try to achieve saturation of 88-92% in COPD and 94-98% in other patients\n- Note: NIV isn't indicated in asthma and pneumonia. previous  Next\n- 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/non-invasive-ventilation-niv-guidelines/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/non-invasive-ventilation-niv-guidelines/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n*Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/non-invasive-ventilation-niv-guidelines/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Non-Invasive Ventilation (