6d8b8e8e-06f9-4a95-829d-6c78eff3bb37	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	ventricular-tachycardia-vt	Ventricular Tachycardia (VT)	article	# Ventricular Tachycardia (VT)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (PVT) — Continually changing QRS morphology.\n- sustained monomorphiclpoJymorphicyentricuNar-tachycardia-(SMYTlSPMT); Continuous VT for at least 30 s, or which requires an intervention for termination.\n- Torsades de pointes ventricular tachycardia (TdP) —Subtype of a polymorphic VT in the context of QT prolongation with continually changing QRS complexes that appear to spiral around the baseline of the electrocardiogram (ECG) lead in a sinusoidal pattern.\n- Ventricular fibrillation (ME); A chaotic rhythm with undulations that are irregular in timing and morphology, without discrete QRS complexes on the surface ECG. Acute management of sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia (SMVT)\n- Unstable patient\n- Patients presenting with haemodynamic instability require immediate synchronized cardioversion. If synchronization is not possible, an unsynchronized shock should be used. Cardioversion is not indicated in patients with repetitive NSVTs.\n- Haemodynamically_ stable patients\n- Administration of adenosine or vagal manoeuvres with continuous recording of 12- lead ECG should be considered if supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is likely.\n\n- Prompt termination of SMVT is recommended even for tolerated SMVT, as rapid haemodynamical deterioration may occur. Termination can be achieved with electrical cardioversion, anti-arrhythmic medications, or pacing techniques.\n- For pharmacological termination of a haemodynamically tolerated VT of unknown aetiology, intravenous procainamide or amiodarone can be used.\n- Intravenous procainamide should not be used in patients with severe heart failure, acute MI, and end-stage renal disease.\n- In case of a known idiopathic VT, treatrnent with beta-blockers for Right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) tachycardia (for RVOT VT)or verapamil (for fascicular VT) is recommended for acute conversion.\n\nAcute management of a patient with regular wide QRS complex tachycardia\n- overslon rl atton (Class l) fascicular VT rapam (Class l) aem ynaml tolerated or susp of structural heart disease Idiopathic VT nown outflow tract VT ta-blockers (Class l) er type or uncertain aeste ris for cardioversionb Procainamide (Class Ila) Flecainide Ajmaline Sotalol Amiodarone' (Class 11b) Cardioversion (Class l)\n- story, p Ica examination, 12-lead ECG Adenosine or vagal manoeuvres if SVT likely (Class Ila) for cardioversions Procainamide (Class Ila) Amiodarone (Class 11b)  ESC\n\n- Example-=-01\n- Monomorphic Sustained Ventricular Tachycardia ECG\n- Treatment depends on clinical features.\n\n- Example-02 av ECG show Idiopathic Fascicular VT:\n- Broad-complex complex tachycardia with modest increase in QRS width fr120 ms)\n-\n- RBBB morphology (RSR' in VI) Left axis deviation (-90 degrees)\n- Narrow-complex capture beat (complex #6)\n- Several dissociated P waves are seen in the lead II rhythm strip (associated with the\n- 3rd, 10th, 14th, 18th and 22nd QRS complexes)\n\nRVOTVT (LBBB-like, inferior axis, V4 transition) LV fascicular VT (RBBB- like, superior axis, QRS 130 ms) O ESC\n\nycardia\n- 90% of patients presenting with monomorphic VT are secondary to structural heart disease, including ischaemic heart disease, congenital heart disease, valvular dysfunction and myocardial dysfunction\n- 10% of cases of monomorphic VT occur in the absence of structural heart disease and are termed idiopathicVT\n- 75-90% of idiopathic VT arise from the right ventricle — e.gright ventricular outflow tract tachycardia\n- 10-25% of idiopathic VT arise from an ectopic focus within the left ventricle, especially the left posterior fascicle (Fascicular VT).\n- Diagnosis can be difficult and this rhythm is often misdiagnosed as SVT with RBBB; the diagnosis is made by observing specific features of VT, e.g. fusion/capture beats, AV dissociation.\n- Classification\n- Posterior fascicular VT — (90-95% of cases) RBBBmorphoIogy + left axis deviation; arises close to the left posterior fascicle\n- Anterior fascicular VT — (5-10% of cases) RBBBmorphoIogy + right axis deviation; arises close to the left anterior fascicle\n- Upper septal fascicular VT — (rare) atypical morphology — usually RBBB but may resemble LBBB instead; cases with narrow QRS and normal axis have also been reported. Arises from the region of the upper septum.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nin-stab-e I patient . Verapamil\n\n- Digoxin-induced fascicular VT is responsive to Digoxin Immune Fab\n- Above ineffective then cardioversion.\n- Example-—-03 RVOT tachycardia: Regular broad complex tachycardia\n-\n- LBBB-like morphology with rs complex in VI and R complex in V6 Precordial transition at V3\n- Inferior axis (+ 90 degrees)\n-\n\n- Right-Ventricu/ar-Outflow-Tract-Tachygardia-(RMOT)\n- Right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) tachycardia is a form of monomorphic VT originating from the outflow tract of the right ventricle or occasionally from the tricuspid annulus. It is usually seen in patients without underlying structural heart disease. The majority (80%) of outflow tract ventricular arrhythmias (OTVAs) originate from\n- the AVOT.\n- RVOT tachycardia is most commonly a form of idiopathic VT, whereby no structural heart disease, metabolic/electrolyte abnormalities, or long QT syndrome can be found.\n- RVOT tachycardia can be difficult to distinguish from SVT with I-BBB Inferior axis (+90 degrees) is usually the most differentiating feature, but other\n- features of VT such as AV dissociation may be present ECG features\n- LBBB morphology Inferior axis\n-\n- rs complex in VI and R complex in V6 Precordial transition usually 2 V3, with the exception of septal origin, which occurs\n- at V3\n- Beta-blockers\n- Above ineffective then cardioversion.\n-\n\nNon-sustained ventricular tachycardias\n- Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia (NSVT) is an ectopic ventricular rhythm with wide QRS complex (120 milliseconds or greater), rate faster than 100 bpm, lasting for at least 3 beats that spontaneously resolves in less than 30 seconds.\n- It may occur in the absence of any underlying heart disease. However, it is more commonly associated with ischaemic and non-ischaemic heart disease; known genetic disorders such as long QT syndrome, Brugada's syndrome, and arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy; congenital heart disease; metabolic problems, including drug toxicity; or electrolyte imbalance.\n- Risk factors\n- CAD (coronary artery disease)\n- LVF\n- Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\n- Brugada's syndrome\n- Long QT syndrome\n- Electrolyte imbalance Digoxin toxicity\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nIn the vast majority of cases, NSVT is asymptomatic\n- Patient may presents with tachycardia, dizziness, pre-syncope or syncope\n- Other features depends on history of condition\n- ECG features\n\n- wide QRS complex (120 milliseconds or greater), rate\n- IOO bpm lasting for 3 or more beats, spontaneously resolving in <30 seconds. VI\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- Example\n- 01\n\n- Initiation of wide QRS rhythm is not preceded by a P-wave, suggesting non- sustained ventricular tachycardia\n- Example\n- 02 ECG from a patient with long QT syndrome\n\n- Example\n- 03\n\n- ECG finding;\n- Normal sinus rhythm\n- Non-sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a rate of slightly less than 300.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n— depends on clinical features, usually patients are asymptomatic, hence required reassurance and monitoring only. Symptomatic cause without electrolyte abnormality require beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers therapy as the first line.\n- Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance is the cause.\n- ICD if criteria match.\n\nVentricular-fibrillatiom(UE);\n- A chaotic rhythm with undulations that are irregular in timing and morphology, without discrete QRS complexes on the surface ECG.\n- The diagnosis of idiopathic ventricular fibrillation (IVF) is made in SCA (sudden cardiac arrest) survivors, preferably with documented VF, after exclusion of structural, channelopathic, metabolic, or toxicological aetiologies.\n- Diagnostic tests — include blood chemistry, ECG (including high lead), cardiac CT/coronary angiography, telemetry/HoIter, exercise stress test, echocardiogram, sodium-channel blocker testing and CMR.\n- Genetic testing for channelopathy and cardiomyopathy genes may be considered\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nIn IVF patients, ICD implantation reduces the risk of arrhythmic death by up to 68% compared to amiodarone.\n- Isoproterenol, verapamil, or quinidine have been employed for acute treatrnent of recurrent ICD discharges or electrical storrn.\n- In patients with recurrent episodes of VF triggered by a similar PVC unresponsive to medical treatment, catheter ablation has shown success\n\npreviOUS  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_05_p10.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 10 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_06_p13.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 13 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_07_p14.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 14 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/ventricular-tachycardia-vt/figure_08_p22.jpeg)\n*Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 22 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	40	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
ed8605fc-13d6-4d1f-93ae-e4f78bcbb8d9	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-ventricular-tachycardia	Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nWhich of the following 12-lead ECG findings provides pathognomonic proof of Atrioventricular (AV) Dissociation, definitively confirming that a regular wide-complex tachycardia is Ventricular Tachycardia rather than SVT with aberrancy?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Presence of Fusion and Capture beats\n- **B.** QRS duration of 110 ms\n- **C.** Heart rate exactly 150 bpm\n- **D.** Normal frontal QRS axis\n- **E.** Inverted T waves in lead III\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAV dissociation is the diagnostic hallmark of VT. It occurs when atria and ventricles beat independently. Intermittent conduction of a sinus impulse to the ventricles during VT produces either a 'Capture beat' (normal narrow QRS complex) or a 'Fusion beat' (hybrid complex between sinus and ventricular morphology), proving ventricular origin.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_06_p13.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to the Brugada Algorithm, which single criterion in Step 1 confirms Ventricular Tachycardia immediately?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Absence of an RS complex in ALL precordial leads V1 through V6 (concordance)\n- **B.** Presence of a classic rSR' in lead V1\n- **C.** PR interval >200 ms\n- **D.** Rate less than 120 bpm\n- **E.** Normal QT interval\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nStep 1 of the Brugada algorithm assesses for the absence of an RS complex across all chest leads (V1-V6). If there is no RS complex (i.e. positive concordance: all R waves; or negative concordance: all QS complexes), the diagnosis of Ventricular Tachycardia is confirmed with 100% specificity.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_07_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 64-year-old male with a history of ischaemic cardiomyopathy presents with sustained monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia at 160 bpm. He is fully conscious, conversing, and completely haemodynamically stable with BP 124/80 mmHg and no chest pain. What is the recommended first-line antiarrhythmic therapy per Resuscitation Council UK / ESC guidelines?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Amiodarone 300mg infused over 20-60 minutes, followed by 900mg infusion over 24 hours\n- **B.** IV Verapamil 10mg bolus\n- **C.** Immediate unsynchronised defibrillation 200J without sedation\n- **D.** Oral Digoxin 250mcg\n- **E.** IV Metoprolol 15mg rapid push\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor stable sustained monomorphic VT, IV Amiodarone (300 mg over 20-60 min followed by 900 mg/24h) is first-line in structural heart disease. Alternative in preserved LV function is IV Procainamide (PROCAMIO study). If medical therapy fails or patient deteriorates, elective synchronised DC cardioversion under sedation is performed.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_08_p22.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the Vereckei aVR algorithm criterion that strongly indicates Ventricular Tachycardia?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Initial R wave in lead aVR\n- **B.** Negative QRS in lead aVR\n- **C.** Absent P wave in lead aVR\n- **D.** T wave inversion in lead aVR\n- **E.** Isoelectric QRS in lead aVR\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe simplified Vereckei algorithm looks exclusively at lead aVR. An initial dominant R wave in aVR indicates that ventricular activation is moving from the apex up towards the base and right shoulder, characteristic of ventricular tachycardia (normal ventricular conduction moves away from aVR, producing a negative complex).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat genetic inherited arrhythmogenic disorder is characterized by incomplete RBBB with coved-type ST-segment elevation >=2 mm followed by a negative T wave in leads V1-V2 (Type 1 pattern), predisposing to nocturnal polymorphic VT and sudden cardiac death?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Ventricular Tachycardia (VT))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/ventricular-tachycardia/figure_05_p10.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Brugada Syndrome (SCN5A sodium channelopathy)\n- **B.** Long QT Syndrome Type 2\n- **C.** Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy (ARVC) with epsilon waves\n- **D.** Short QT Syndrome\n- **E.** Catecholaminergic Polymorphic VT\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBrugada syndrome (autosomal dominant sodium channel SCN5A mutation) features the Type 1 Brugada pattern: coved ST elevation >=2 mm descending into an inverted T wave in >=1 right precordial lead (V1, V2). It causes sudden cardiac death due to polymorphic VT/VF, typically during rest or sleep, unmasked by fever.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	41	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
db96b0ca-2222-4ce2-9acf-48aa83806b08	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	abdominal-trauma	Abdominal Trauma	article	# Abdominal Trauma\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- ln the UK, this is most commonly knife- related injury penetrating trauma, the injuries sustained are caused by direct injury as the penetrating item passes through the organs and tissues in its path. Stab wounds and Iow-energy gunshot wounds cause tissue damage by lacerating and tearing. High- energy gunshot wounds transfer more kinetic energy, causing increased damage surrounding the track of the missile due to temporary cavitation\n- Commonly affected organs\n- Liver 40%\n- Small bowel 30%\n- Diaphragm 20%\n- colon 15%\n- Gunshot injury\n- SmaII bowel 50%\n- colon 40%\n- Liver 30%\n- Wound assessment\n- Caution is required in assessrnent of penetrating abdominal wounds. Patients with penetrating injury, even with normal vital signs, should be assessed by a senior surgeon.\n\n- Do not remove any object remaining in the wound, such as a knife, as this may be tamponading underlying vessels\n- Blunt abdominal trauma\n- The spleen and liver are the rnost commonly injured intraperitoneal organs in blunt trauma.\n- Blunt abdominal trauma commonly results from either a compression force, a deceleration injury or after direct impact with a car steering wheel . It has greater mortality than penetrating as there is often injury to multiple organ systems\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\n- lnspection — of the injured abdomen may show evidence of bruising, abrasions or penetrating trauma.\n- PaIpate- the abdomen in all quadrants, making note of general or regional tenderness and signs of acute abdomen (rigidity, rebound, guarding). Regardless of imaging, document serial exams for patients who remain in the ED for several hours.\n- RectaI examination — is useful if it demonstrates bleeding or a high-riding prostate suggestive of a pelvic injury and involvement of the genitourinary tract. Anal tone and sensation should be tested\n- Top-to-toe examination — including a log-roll is imperative to ensure there are no other injuries.\n- Solid organ injury_\n\n- Signs and symptoms of a solid organ injury are generally due to pain and blood loss.\n- Hepatic and splenic injuries are at high risk for hemorrhage in both penetrating and blunt abdominal trauma.\n- An y-blunt-abdominal-trauma- patient-with-diffuseperjtonjtis-or-who-is hemodynamically unstable should be taken urgently for laparotomy.\n- Splenic injury is graded according to CT findings and treatment is guided by grade:\n- Grad-1 Minor subcapsular tear or haematoma\n- Grad-2 Parenchymal injury not extending to the hilum\n- Grad-3 Major parenchymal injury involving vessels and hilum\n- Grad-4 Shattered spleen\n- Grade I or 2 injuries can usually be managed conservatively\n- Other injuries\n- Hollow viscus injury_\n- Peritoneal contamination with bowel contents will produce peritonism. —Pancreatic injury\n- lnjury to the pancreas may cause pancreatitis, which may develop over days. —Diaphragmatic injury\n\n- Diaphragmatic injuries are uncommon (0.8% to 5% of patients with thoracoabdominal injury).\n- Delayed diagnosis may lead to herniation or strangulation of abdominal contents through the diaphragmatic defect.\n- Diagnosis tools\n- FAST/US indication patient unstable hemodynamically with blunt trauma or penetrating trauma but without immediate laparotomy It is accurate, rapid, noninvasive, repeatable, and portable. Massive hemoperitoneum is quickly detected with a single view Of Morison's pouch\n- Disadvantage: US compared to CT is the inability to identify the exact source of free intraperitoneal fluid. Cl is indication for laparotomy\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nCT CT with IV contrast is the noninvasive gold standard study for the evaluation of abdominal injury patient should be hemodynamically stable\n- Disadvantage: high cost longer time can miss some viscus or pancreatic injuries GI: hemodynamically unstable PT. uncooperative or allergic PT DPL indication in hemodynamically unstable patient with blunt trauma or penetrating but without indication of laparotomy\n- Disadvantage: Invasive and risk of procedure related injury\n- Cl indication Of laparotomv. previous abdominal surgery or!iver cirrhosis\n\n- Indication in blunt trauma\n- Absolute: Anterior abdominal injury with hypotension Abdominal wall disruption peritonitis Free air under diaphragm on chest radiograph Positive FAST or DPL in hemodynamically unstable patient CT-diagnosed injury requiring surgery\n- Relative Positive FAST or DPL in hemodynamically stable patient Solid visceral injury in stable patient Hemoperitoneum on CT without clear source\n- Note: A definitive patient management plan should be formulated as early as possible, including early consultation by relevant speciality team. This may include surgeons, radiologists, anaesthetists, theatre staff, critical care and transfer teams\n- Indication of laparotomy in penetrating trauma\n\n- Indication of laparotomy in penetratinq trauma\n- Absolute Injury to abdomen, back, and flank with hypotension Abdominal tenderness GI evisceration High suspicion for transabdominal trajectory after gunshot wound CT-diagnosed injury requiring surgery\n- Relative Positive local wound exploration after stab wound previous\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Abdominal Trauma - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/abdominal-trauma/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Abdominal Trauma — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Abdominal Trauma - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/abdominal-trauma/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Abdominal Trauma — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Abdominal Trauma - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/abdominal-trauma/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n*Abdominal Trauma — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Abdominal Trauma - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/abdominal-trauma/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n*Abdominal Trauma — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Abdominal Trauma - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/abdominal-trauma/figure_05_p7.jpeg)\n*Abdominal Trauma — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Abdominal Trauma - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/abdominal-trauma/figure_06_p8.jpeg)\n*Abdominal Trauma — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f1d55dab-56f2-4608-941a-a851e805d17a	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	concentration-and-dilution-of-urine	Concentration and Dilution of Urine	article	Concentration and dilution of\nurine\nRegulation of plasma osmolarity\nIt is accomplished by varying the amount of H2O excreted\nrelative to the amount of solute excreted (i.e by varying urine\nosmolarity)\nProduction of concentrated urine\nAlso called hyperosmotic urine, in which urine osmolarity >\nBlood osmolarity\nIt is produced when circulating ADH levels are high (e.g.,\nwater deprivation, volume depletion, SIADH).\nCorticopapillary osmotic gradient - high ADH\nIs the gradient of osmolarity from the cortex (300mOsm/L) to\nthe papilla (1200mOsm/L) and is composed primarily of NaCl\nand urea.\nIs established by countercurrent multiplication and urea\nrecycling.\nIs maintained by countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta.\nCountercurrent multiplication in the LOH\nDepends upon NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb\nand countercurrent flow in the descending and ascending\nlimbs of LOH.\nIt is augmented by ADH, which stimulates NaCl reabsorption\nin the thick ascending limb. Therefore, the presence of ADH\nincreases the size of corticopapillary osmotic gradient.\nUrea recycling\nUrea recycling from inner medullary collecting ducts into the\nmedullary interstitial fluid is also augmented by ADH (by\nstimulating UT1 transporter)\nVasa recta\n\nAre the capillaries that supply the LOH. They maintain the\ncorticopapillary gradient by serving as osmotic exchanger.\nVasa recta blood equilibrates osmotically with interstitial fluid\nof medulla and papilla.\nProximal tubule - high ADH\nThe osmolarity of glomerular filtrate is identical to that of\nplasma (300mOsm/L).\nTwo third of filtered H2O is reabsorbed isosmotically (with Na,\nCl, H2O, glucose, amino acids and so forth) in the proximal\ntubule.\nTF/Posm = 1.0 throughout the proximal tubule because water\nis reabsorbed isosmotically with solute.\nThick ascending limb of LOH - high ADH\nIt is called diluting segment.\nReabsorbs NaCl by the NA-K-2Cl cotransporter, reabsorption\nof Na+ is further enhanced by ADH.\nImpermeable to H2O, therefore H2O is not reabsorbed with\nNaCl, and the tubular fluid becomes diluted.\nThe fluid that leaves the thick ascending limb has an\nosmolarity of 100 mOsm/L and TF/Posm < 1.0 as a result of\ndilution process.\nEarly distal tubule - high ADH\nIt is called cortical diluting segment.\nLike the thick ascending limb, early distal tubule reabsorbs\nNaCl but is impermeable to water. Consequently, tubular\nfluid is further diluted.\nLate distal tubule - high ADH\nADH increases the H2O permeability in the principal cells of\nlate distal tubule.\nH2O is reabsorbed from the tubular fluid until the osmolarity\nof tubular fluid equals that of surrounding interstitial fluid in\nrenal cortex (300mOsm/L)\nTF/Posm = 1.0 at the end of the distal tubule because osmotic\nequilibration occurs in the presence of ADH.\nCollecting ducts - high ADH\nAs in the late distal tubule, ADH increases the H2O\npermeability of the principal cells of the collecting ducts.\n\nAs tubular fluid flows through the collecting ducts, it passes\nthrough the corticopapillary gradient (regions of increasingly\nhigher osmolarity), which was previously established by\ncountercurrent multiplication and urea recycling.\nH2O is reabsorbed from the collecting ducts until the\nosmolarity of tubular fluid equals that of the surrounding\nfluid.\nThe osmolarity of the final urine equals that at the bend of\nLOH and tip of papilla (1200mOsm/L)\nTF/Posm= >1.0 because osmotic equilibration occurs with the\ncorticopapillary gradient in the presence of ADH.\nProduction of dilute urine\nIs called hypoosmotic urine, in which urine osmolarity <\nblood osmolarity.\nProduced when circulating levels of ADH are low (e.g., water\nintake, central diabetes insipidus) or when ADH is ineffective\n(nephrogenic diabetes insipidus)\nCorticopapillary osmotic gradient - no ADH\nIs smaller than in the presence of ADH because ADH\nstimulates both countercurrent multiplication and urea\nrecycling\nProximal tubule - no ADH\nTwo third of filtered water is reabsorbed isosmotically.\nTF/Posm = 1.0 throughout the proximal tubule\nThick ascending limb of LOH - no ADH\nNaCl is reabsorbed without water and tubular fluid becomes\n(although not quite as dilute as in the presence of ADH.\nTF/Posm = < 1.0\nEarly distal tubule - no ADH\nNaCl is reabsorbed without water and tubular fluid is further\ndiluted.\nTF/Posm = < 1.0\nLate distal tubule - no ADH\nIn the absence of ADH, the cells of late distal tubule and\ncollecting ducts are impermeable to water.\nThus, even though the tubular fluid flows through the\ncorticopapillary osmotic gradient, osmotic equilibration does not occur.\nThe osmolarity of final urine will be diluted with an osmolarity\nas low as 50mOsm/L.\nTF/Posm = < 1.0\nFree water clearance (CH2O)\nIt is used to estimate the ability to concentrate or dilute the\nurine.\nFree water or solute free water is produced in the diluting\nsegment of kidney (i.e. thick ascending limb and early distal\ntubule), where NaCl is reabsorbed, and free H2O is left behind\nin the tubular fluid.\nIn absence of ADH, this solute free water is excreted and\nCH2O is positive.\nIn presence of ADH, this solute free water is not excreted but\nis reabsorbed by the late distal tubule and collecting ducts\nand CH2O is negative.\nUrine that is isosmotic to plasma\nCH2O is zero\nProduced during a treatment with a loop diuretic, which\ninhibits NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb,\ninhibiting both dilution in the thick ascending limb and\nproduction of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient.\nTherefore, the urine can't be diluted during high water intake\nor concentrated during water deprivation.\nUrine that is hypoosmotic to plasma (low ADH) CH2O is\npositive.\nProduced with high water intake (in which ADH released\nfrom the posterior pituitary is suppressed), central diabetes\ninsipidus or nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.\nUrine that is hyperosmotic to plasma (high ADH)\nCH2O is negative.\nIt is produced in water deprivation or SIADH.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
eee2f276-dde5-4f78-82d1-a85c9af7a0a4	c5a116c4-d2b6-4edb-bf1f-d05abfe7a1d8	gastrointestinal-tract-2	Gastrointestinal Tract	article	# Gastrointestinal Tract\n\nGastrointestinal tract\nThe gastrointestinal tract (GI tract, digestive tract, alimentary\ncanal) is the tract or passageway of the digestive system that\nleads from the mouth to the anus. The GI tract contains all the\nmajor organs of the digestive system, in humans and other\nanimals, including the esophagus, stomach, and intestines.\nFood taken in through the mouth is digested to extract\nnutrients and absorb energy, and the waste expelled at the\nanus as feces.\nThe complete human digestive system is made up of the\ngastrointestinal tract plus the accessory organs of digestion\n(the tongue, salivary glands, pancreas, liver and gallbladder)\nFunction of GIT\nIngestion of food and water\nMastication\nTransport of the food bolus or chyme through the\ngastrointestinal tract\nDigestion (i.e., breaking food down into nutrients)\nAbsorption of nutrients and water\nExcretion of waste as feces\nImmunity\n\nDiet ii Late 2026)\nOverview  Comments\n\n1/3\nStructures of GI tract:\nAll segments of the GI tract are divided into four layers (from\ninnermost to outer most) are:\n1.The innermost mucosa composed of:\nthe epithelial layer (specialized in different parts of the GI\ntract for secretion and absorption\nthe lamina propria (consisting of loose tissue, blood\nvessels and lymph tissue)\nthe muscularis mucosa (contraction causes a change in\nthe surface area for secretion or absorption)\n\n### 2. The submucosa (containing a second layer of connective\n\ntissue, large blood vessels and lymphatic vessels and\nsubmucosal plexus\n\n2/3\n3.The muscularis externa composed of:\nThe inner circular muscle layer (contraction causes a\ndecrease in diameter of the lumen of the GI tract)\nThe outer longitudinal muscle layer (contraction causes\nshortening of a segment of the GI tract)\nThe myenteric plexus which lies between these two layers\nof muscle\n\n### 4. The outer most serosa (another connective tissue layer\n\ncovered with squamous mesothelial cells)\nPrevious Next\n\n3/3	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-10-15 01:32:48.207423+00	\N
b5a89fc5-6a48-4ef4-b9a1-403bd89aa913	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	devastatiing-brain-injury	Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication	article	# Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Discuss with donor coordinator Decision to test for brain death and brain death confirmed Consider or offer donation of organ after brain stem death (DBD)  Consider withdraw life support Discuss with donor coordinator Decision to withdraw life support treatment Consider DCD(donation after cardiac death) in those patients who's DBI isn't improved even after 72 hrs of life support treatment\n- 9 Review management plan\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Devastatiing Brain Injury - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/devastatiing-brain-injury/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Devastatiing Brain Injury — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Devastatiing Brain Injury - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/devastatiing-brain-injury/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n*Devastatiing Brain Injury — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Devastatiing Brain Injury - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/devastatiing-brain-injury/figure_03_p2.jpeg)\n*Devastatiing Brain Injury — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a613a2e0-bd3a-4071-b5f5-b461e3b1032f	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	spinal-cord-injury	Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes	article	# Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7bc0364c-a92c-4eb1-b819-2d84f7470f61	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	venous-drainage-of-upper-limb	Venous Drainage of Upper Limb	article	Venous Drainage of the Upper Limb\nDeep and superficial venous arches: Are formed by a pair of\nvenae comitantes, which accompany each of the deep and\nsuperficial palmar arterial arches.\nThe superficial veins of the upper limb lie in the superficial\nfascia and of great clinical importance.\nDorsal venous network lies on the dorsum of hand. It is\ndrained on the lateral side by cephalic vein and on the\nmedial side by the basilic vein.\nNote:\n\n### 1. The median cubital vein connects the cephalic vein to the\n\nbasilic vein. It lies superficial to bicipital aponeurosis, which\nseparates it from the brachial artery. The median cubital vein\nis a common site of venipuncture.\n\n### 2. Deep veins of the arm and forearm: Follow the course of the\n\narteries, accompanying them as their venae comitantes.\n\n### 3. The brachial veins are the vena comitantes of the brachial\n\nartery and are joined by the basilic vein to form the axillary\nvein.\nAxillary Vein:\nIs formed at the lower border of the teres major muscle by\nthe union of the brachial veins (vena comitantes of the brachial\nartery) and the basilic vein and ascends along the medial side\nof the axillary artery. Continues as the subclavian vein at the\ninferior margin of the first rib.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
1232666f-f194-4398-8363-3e5120af39ee	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-spinal-cord-injury	Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old male suffers a stab wound to the right side of his thoracic spinal cord (T8 level). Examination demonstrates ipsilateral loss of motor function (spastic paralysis) and proprioception/vibration sense, accompanied by contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning 1-2 segments below the lesion. What incomplete spinal cord syndrome is this?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_01_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Brown-Séquard Syndrome (spinal cord hemisection)\n- **B.** Central Cord Syndrome\n- **C.** Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- **D.** Posterior Cord Syndrome\n- **E.** Cauda Equina Syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBrown-Séquard syndrome (hemisection of the spinal cord): (1) Ipsilateral motor loss (corticospinal tract crosses at medulla); (2) Ipsilateral loss of proprioception/vibration (dorsal columns cross at medulla); (3) Contralateral loss of pain and temperature (spinothalamic tract crosses 1-2 levels above entry in the spinal cord).\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_06_p11.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 72-year-old man with cervical spondylosis sustains a hyperextension injury to his neck after falling forward onto his forehead. On examination, he has marked weakness and sensory loss in his UPPER limbs, with relatively preserved motor strength in his lower limbs, and variable bladder dysfunction. What syndrome is this?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_02_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Central Cord Syndrome\n- **B.** Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- **C.** Brown-Séquard Syndrome\n- **D.** Complete spinal cord transection\n- **E.** Guillain-Barré syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCentral Cord Syndrome is the most common incomplete spinal cord injury. Typically seen in elderly patients with pre-existing cervical spondylosis undergoing hyperextension trauma. The central corticospinal fibers supplying the cervical/upper extremities are selectively damaged, leading to greater motor deficit in upper limbs than lower limbs ('hands worse than feet').\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_07_p13.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich incomplete spinal cord injury is caused by occlusion or trauma to the Anterior Spinal Artery, producing complete bilateral loss of motor function and bilateral loss of pain and temperature below the lesion, while sparing dorsal column light touch, vibration, and proprioception?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_03_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- **B.** Brown-Séquard Syndrome\n- **C.** Central Cord Syndrome\n- **D.** Conus Medullaris Syndrome\n- **E.** Posterior Cord Syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAnterior Cord Syndrome results from infarction of the anterior two-thirds of the spinal cord supplied by the anterior spinal artery (or flexion-compression bone retropulsion). Corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts are destroyed (bilateral paralysis, loss of pain/temp), but dorsal columns (posterior third supplied by paired posterior spinal arteries) are spared, preserving proprioception and vibration.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_08_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the critical clinical difference between 'Neurogenic Shock' and 'Spinal Shock' in acute trauma management?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Neurogenic shock is a haemodynamic condition (loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone producing hypotension and bradycardia), whereas spinal shock is a neurological state of temporary flaccidity and loss of all spinal reflexes below the lesion\n- **B.** Spinal shock causes severe hypertension and tachycardia\n- **C.** Neurogenic shock causes hypovolaemic tachycardia\n- **D.** They are identical terms with no difference\n- **E.** Spinal shock requires emergency thoracotomy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNeurogenic shock is a form of distributive shock caused by disruption of descending sympathetic autonomic pathways (typically cervical/high thoracic injuries >=T6), producing arterial vasodilation (hypotension) and loss of cardiac accelerator tone (bradycardia with warm peripheries). Spinal shock refers to the temporary total loss of spinal reflex function, motor tone, and sensation below a severe spinal cord injury, which resolves over days to weeks (signalled by return of the bulbocavernosus reflex).\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_09_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich reflex marks the end of 'Spinal Shock' and indicates return of spinal reflex arcs (transitioning to spasticity)?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_05_p9.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Bulbocavernosus reflex (anal sphincter contraction in response to squeezing the glans penis or tugging a Foley catheter)\n- **B.** Patellar tendon reflex\n- **C.** Corneal reflex\n- **D.** Gag reflex\n- **E.** Abdominal cutaneous reflex\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe bulbocavernosus reflex (mediated by S2-S4) tests the integrity of the sacral reflex arc. During acute spinal shock, all reflexes including the bulbocavernosus reflex are absent. Its reappearance signals the termination of spinal shock; if profound motor and sensory deficits persist after the reflex returns, the spinal cord injury is classified as neurologically complete.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_10_p15.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 6 (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_11_p17.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 7 (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_12_p18.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 8 (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_13_p19.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 9 (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_14_p20.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 10 (Spinal Cord Injury & Incomplete Syndromes)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-cord-injury/figure_15_p20.jpeg)\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
38d93420-c4af-4383-ae93-9ba8b702366d	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	spinal-injury-autonomic-dysreflexia	Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia	article	# Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c0aefb9a-44e1-4777-9be2-8a61410e5f27	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-spinal-injury-autonomic-dysreflexia	Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 36-year-old male with a complete T4 spinal cord injury (wheelchair-dependent) presents with a pounding headache, facial flushing, severe diaphoresis above his nipple line, and profound nasal congestion. His BP is 218/124 mmHg, and heart rate is 46 bpm. What life-threatening medical emergency is this?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-injury-autonomic-dysreflexia/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Autonomic Dysreflexia (Hyperreflexia)\n- **B.** Neurogenic shock\n- **C.** Pheochromocytoma crisis\n- **D.** Ischaemic stroke\n- **E.** Opiate withdrawal\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAutonomic Dysreflexia is a medical emergency occurring in spinal cord injuries at or above T6. Noxious visceral stimuli below the lesion trigger uncontrolled sympathetic discharge (vasoconstriction, severe hypertension). Intact cranial baroreceptors attempt compensation via the vagus nerve (bradycardia, vasodilation/flushing/sweating ABOVE the lesion), but inhibitory impulses cannot traverse the damaged spinal cord.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common trigger of an acute episode of Autonomic Dysreflexia?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-injury-autonomic-dysreflexia/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Bladder distension / blocked urinary catheter (responsible for 75-85% of cases)\n- **B.** Hypothermia\n- **C.** Coughing\n- **D.** Hunger\n- **E.** Excess fluid intake\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBladder-related triggers cause 75-85% of episodes (distended bladder, kinked/blocked Foley catheter, UTI, cystoscopy). Bowel impaction/constipation is the second most common trigger (~15%), followed by pressure ulcers, ingrown toenails, or bone fractures.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the FIRST immediate physical action to take when a patient is diagnosed with an acute episode of Autonomic Dysreflexia in the ED?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Spinal Injury & Autonomic Dysreflexia)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/spinal-injury-autonomic-dysreflexia/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Sit the patient upright (90 degrees) with legs dangling over the edge of the bed, and loosen all tight clothing\n- **B.** Place the patient flat in Trendelenburg position\n- **C.** Infuse 2 litres of intravenous fluid rapidly\n- **D.** Administer intravenous atropine 3mg\n- **E.** Place in the prone position\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nImmediate non-pharmacological management: Sit the patient fully upright (90 degrees) with legs dependent. This uses gravitational venous pooling in the lower extremities to acutely lower blood pressure (orthostatic drop). Loosen all restrictive belts, clothing, and abdominal binders immediately, and check the catheter.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nIf sitting the patient upright and checking/relieving the urinary catheter does not immediately reduce blood pressure in severe Autonomic Dysreflexia (systolic BP remains >160-170 mmHg), which rapidly acting vasodilator is first-line?\n\n- **A.** Sublingual Glyceryl Trinitrate (GTN) spray or topical GTN transdermal paste, or oral Nifedipine (bite-and-swallow modified-release)\n- **B.** IV Adrenaline infusion\n- **C.** Oral propranolol\n- **D.** IV Noradrenaline\n- **E.** Subcutaneous morphine\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIf BP remains severely elevated, rapid short-acting antihypertensives should be administered while investigating secondary triggers. Sublingual GTN spray (or topical GTN paste which can be wiped off if hypotension develops) or oral/sublingual captopril or modified-release nifedipine are recommended.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat catastrophic complications can occur if acute severe Autonomic Dysreflexia is left unrecognised or untreated?\n\n- **A.** Intracranial haemorrhage, hypertensive encephalopathy, seizures, acute pulmonary oedema, retinal detachment, or fatal cardiac arrest\n- **B.** Acute hypoglycemia\n- **C.** Permanent paralysis of cranial nerves\n- **D.** Acute liver failure\n- **E.** Hyperglycaemic hyperosmolar state\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSevere acute hypertension in autonomic dysreflexia can reach systolic pressures >250-300 mmHg. Fatal complications include hypertensive encephalopathy, intracerebral/subarachnoid haemorrhage, status epilepticus, acute myocardial infarction, and ventricular arrhythmias.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	19	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
dee19f20-c219-4ee7-91c9-94e93bb9325d	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-asthma	Acute Severe & Life-Threatening Asthma in Adults — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Severe & Life-Threatening Asthma in Adults — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old woman presents with acute breathlessness and wheezing. Her peak expiratory flow (PEF) is 45% of predicted, respiratory rate is 28 breaths/min, heart rate is 115 bpm, and she is unable to complete sentences in one breath. According to BTS/SIGN guidelines, how is this acute asthma exacerbation classified?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Severe & Life-Threatening Asthma in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Severe Asthma\n- **B.** Moderate asthma exacerbation\n- **C.** Life-threatening asthma\n- **D.** Near-fatal asthma\n- **E.** Brittle asthma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS/SIGN criteria for Acute Severe Asthma: PEF 33-50% best/predicted, respiratory rate >=25/min, heart rate >=110 bpm, or inability to complete sentences in one breath. Moderate is PEF 50-75% with no severe features. Life-threatening features include PEF <33%, SpO2 <92%, silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, or normal/high PaCO2.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich of the following clinical and arterial blood gas (ABG) findings is a critical sign of LIFE-THREATENING asthma requiring immediate critical care involvement?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Severe & Life-Threatening Asthma in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** A 'normal' or elevated PaCO2 (>=6.0 kPa / 45 mmHg) in a fatigued, exhausted patient\n- **B.** PaCO2 of 3.2 kPa (respiratory alkalosis)\n- **C.** Heart rate of 112 bpm\n- **D.** Bilateral expiratory wheezing on chest auscultation\n- **E.** PEF of 48% of predicted\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn early/moderate asthma exacerbations, hyperventilation drives PaCO2 down (respiratory alkalosis). Finding a 'normal' or elevated PaCO2 indicates respiratory muscle exhaustion, impending alveolar hypoventilation, and imminent respiratory arrest. It is a hallmark of life-threatening/near-fatal asthma.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended first-line pharmacological bronchodilator regimen for acute severe asthma according to BTS/SIGN guidelines?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Severe & Life-Threatening Asthma in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oxygen-driven nebulised Salbutamol 5mg combined with Ipratropium Bromide 500mcg, plus early systemic corticosteroids (oral Prednisolone 40-50mg or IV Hydrocortisone 100mg)\n- **B.** Oral montelukast alone\n- **C.** Inhaled fluticasone low dose\n- **D.** Intravenous aminophylline bolus without nebulisers\n- **E.** Subcutaneous terbutaline without oxygen\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFirst-line emergency therapy: High-dose inhaled beta-2 agonist (Salbutamol 5mg) driven by high-flow oxygen, combined with an anticholinergic (Ipratropium bromide 500mcg) repeated every 15-20 minutes, alongside early systemic corticosteroids (Prednisolone 40-50mg orally or IV Hydrocortisone 100mg) which take 4-6 hours to exert anti-inflammatory effects.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old male with acute severe asthma has not improved despite back-to-back salbutamol and ipratropium nebulisers and IV hydrocortisone. PEF remains 38%. What intravenous adjunctive medication should be administered next according to BTS/SIGN guidelines?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Severe & Life-Threatening Asthma in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Magnesium Sulphate (1.2 - 2.0g infusion over 20 minutes)\n- **B.** Intravenous Aminophylline loading dose\n- **C.** Intravenous Ketamine\n- **D.** Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate\n- **E.** Intravenous Furosemide\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIV Magnesium Sulphate (1.2 - 2.0 g IV over 20 min) is recommended as a safe and effective second-line bronchodilator for patients with acute severe asthma not responding to initial inhaled therapy (PEF <50%) or life-threatening asthma. It causes bronchial smooth muscle relaxation by blocking calcium influx.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhy should oxygen NOT be withheld in severe acute asthma exacerbations, and what is the target pulse oximetry (SpO2) range?\n\n- **A.** Asthma patients do not have chronic hypoxic drive; target SpO2 is 94-98% using high-flow oxygen-driven nebulisers\n- **B.** Target SpO2 is strictly 88-92% as in COPD\n- **C.** Oxygen is toxic to asthmatic airways\n- **D.** Target SpO2 is 80-85%\n- **E.** Asthma requires room air only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nUnlike chronic hypercapnic COPD patients, patients with acute asthma are typically young with normal baseline lung function and do not rely on hypoxic drive. Hypoxia is the primary cause of cardiac arrest in asthma. High-flow oxygen is used to drive nebulisers with a target SpO2 of 94-98%.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3cc7ac75-150d-41e1-b088-9c1f6cd64b35	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	asthma-children	Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze	article	# Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
77e844cc-d95e-4a84-9d10-bb13f4b2b69d	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	breathlessness	Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness	article	# Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f33fcbbe-3a10-49f1-a97b-9f5ec7bc25e3	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-breathlessness	Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 72-year-old male with a history of both COPD and ischemic heart disease presents with worsening shortness of breath and ankle swelling. Bedside point-of-care lung ultrasound (POCUS) demonstrates multiple bilateral 'B-lines' (comet-tail artefacts >3 per intercostal space) throughout anterior and lateral lung zones. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/breathlessness/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema (interstitial-alveolar fluid syndrome)\n- **B.** Pure COPD exacerbation without pulmonary congestion\n- **C.** Large pneumothorax\n- **D.** Aspiration of a foreign body\n- **E.** Chest wall muscular contusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn point-of-care lung ultrasound (BLUE protocol), multiple bilateral B-lines (vertical laser-like comet-tail reverberation artefacts arising from the pleural line) represent fluid-thickened interlobular septa and extravascular lung water, with >90% sensitivity and specificity for acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. COPD exacerbation without pulmonary edema classically shows horizontal A-lines.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich serum biomarker has a high negative predictive value (>95%) to exclude acute congestive heart failure as the cause of acute breathlessness in the ED?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/breathlessness/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP <100 pg/ml) or NT-proBNP (<300 pg/ml)\n- **B.** Serum Troponin T\n- **C.** C-reactive protein (CRP)\n- **D.** Serum D-dimer\n- **E.** Serum amylase\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBNP (<100 pg/ml) or NT-proBNP (<300 pg/ml) is highly accurate for ruling out acute decompensated heart failure in patients presenting with acute dyspnoea. Natriuretic peptides are released by ventricular myocytes in response to wall stretch and increased ventricular filling pressures.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 65-year-old woman presents with sudden onset sharp right-sided pleuritic chest pain and shortness of breath 10 days after a total hip replacement. Heart rate is 112 bpm, BP 120/75 mmHg, SpO2 93% on room air. The chest radiograph is completely normal. What is the single most likely diagnosis to exclude?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/breathlessness/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Pulmonary Embolism (PE)\n- **B.** Lobar pneumonia\n- **C.** Pneumothorax\n- **D.** Gastro-oesophageal reflux\n- **E.** Rib fracture\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic presentation of acute Pulmonary Embolism is sudden-onset dyspnoea and pleuritic chest pain with tachycardia and a normal or near-normal chest X-ray in a patient with risk factors (recent major orthopaedic surgery). A normal CXR in a hypoxic, tachypnoeic patient strongly points toward PE.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the physiological hallmark of Type 1 Respiratory Failure on an Arterial Blood Gas (ABG)?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/breathlessness/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hypoxaemia (PaO2 < 8.0 kPa / 60 mmHg) with normal or low PaCO2 (< 6.0 kPa / 45 mmHg)\n- **B.** Hypoxaemia with elevated PaCO2 (> 6.0 kPa)\n- **C.** Isolated hypercapnia with PaO2 > 12.0 kPa\n- **D.** Normal PaO2 with metabolic alkalosis\n- **E.** Elevated carboxyhaemoglobin with normal PaO2\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nType 1 (hypoxaemic) respiratory failure is caused by V/Q mismatch or shunt: PaO2 < 8.0 kPa (60 mmHg) on air with normal or low PaCO2 (due to hyperventilation). Examples: pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, ARDS. Type 2 (hypercapnic) failure has PaCO2 > 6.0 kPa (ventilatory failure: COPD, neuromuscular, drug overdose).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 23-year-old university student is brought to the ED hyperventilating, reporting severe breathlessness, light-headedness, and bilateral tingling in her hands and around her mouth (carpopedal spasm). Arterial blood gas shows: pH 7.58, PaCO2 2.6 kPa (20 mmHg), PaO2 14.5 kPa (110 mmHg), HCO3- 24 mmol/L. What is the acid-base disorder and cause of tetany?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Approach to Acute Breathlessness)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/breathlessness/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Respiratory Alkalosis; hypocapnic alkalosis increases protein binding of calcium, causing acute reduction in ionized calcium (hypocalcaemic tetany)\n- **B.** Acute Metabolic Acidosis; hyperkalaemia\n- **C.** Type 2 respiratory failure; CO2 narcosis\n- **D.** Lactic acidosis from sepsis\n- **E.** Salicylate poisoning Stage 3\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHyperventilation blows off CO2, producing acute respiratory alkalosis (pH >7.45, low PaCO2). Alkalosis causes hydrogen ions to dissociate from albumin, freeing negative binding sites that bind ionized calcium. The resulting drop in free ionized calcium increases neuronal membrane excitability, causing circumoral tingling, paresthesias, and carpopedal spasm (tetany).\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9e2f329b-b965-4a88-b204-a30280e0ed93	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	broncholilis	Acute Bronchiolitis in Infants	article	# Acute Bronchiolitis in Infants\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n\n\nDo not routinely perform a chest X-ray and blood tests in babies or children with bronchiolitis. Consider performing a chest X-ray if intensive care is being proposed for a baby or child\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nDo not perform chest physiotherapy on babies and children with bronchiolitis who do not have relevant comorbidities (for example spinal muscular atrophy, severe tracheomalacia). Do not use any of the following to treat bronchiolitis in babies or children: antibiotics hypertonic saline adrenaline (nebulised) salbutamol montelukast ipratropium bromide systemic or inhaled corticosteroids a combination of systemic corticosteroids and nebulised adrenaline. Give oxygen supplementation to babies and children with bronchiolitis if their oxygen-saturation-is; persistently less than 90%, for children aged 6 weeks and over persistently less than 92%, for babies under 6 weeks or children of any age with underlying health conditions. Consider continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) in babies and children with bronchiolitis who have impending respiratory failure.\n\n- Consider upper airway suctioning in babies and children who have respiratory distress or feeding difficulties because of upper airway secretions.\n- Perform upper airway suctioning_in babies and children with bronchiolitis presenting with apnoea even if there are no obvious upper airway secretions.\n- Give fluids by-nasogastric-or-orogastric-tube in babies and children with bronchiolitis if they cannot take enough fluid by mouth. Or give IV fluid if patient do not tolerate nasogastric or orogastric fluids or have impending respiratory failure. or child if he has\n- apnoea (observed or reported)\n- persistent oxygen saturation (when breathing air) of'. less than 90%, for children aged 6 weeks and over or less than 92%, for babies under 6 weeks or children of any age with underlying health conditions\n- inadequate oral fluid intake (50% to 75% of usual volume, taking account of risk factors [see recommendation 1.3.3] and using clinical judgement)\n- persisting severe respiratory distress, for example grunting, marked chest recession, or a respiratory rate of over 70 breaths/minute.\n- If the child does not require hospital admission:\n- Advise the parents/carers that bronchiolitis is usually a self-limiting illness and that symptoms tend to peak between three and five days of onset.\n- Provide parents or carers with key safety information.\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Broncholilis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/broncholilis/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Broncholilis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Broncholilis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/broncholilis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Broncholilis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9e0d7812-b986-4378-baa6-e14117f2f713	0b1ae2f2-d24d-4aeb-96dd-5cec22dcd4aa	creatinine-clearance	Creatinine Clearance	article	Creatinine Clearance\nClearance refers to a theoretical volume of plasma from\nwhich a substance is removed over a period of time.\nClearance = Urine conc of substance × Urine flow rate\n/plasma concentration of substance\nClearance of substance can provide an accurate estimate of\nGFR when the substance is.\nFreely filtered.\nNot reabsorbed in nephron\nNot secreted in nephron\nNeither synthesized nor metabolized by kidney\nInulin is such substance but not used in routine clinical\npractice due to complicated clearance.\nCreatinine which is steadily released from skeletal muscle is\npreferred clinical method for determining the relatively\naccurate approximation of GFR (except when plasma\ncreatinine or GFR is abnormally low) because it is\nendogenously produced, not necessary to infuse it to get a\nclearance measurement as has to be done to measure inulin\nclearance and because it is freely filtered not reabsorbed and\nonly a small amount of it is secreted, creatinine clearance\nparallels inulin clearance and is independent of production rate\n(excretion rises as plasma concentration increases	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
66b33be7-7d7d-4ac5-9f42-e098e68f555b	c5a116c4-d2b6-4edb-bf1f-d05abfe7a1d8	bile-and-bile-salts	Bile and Bile Salts	article	Bile\nBile is an aqueous, alkaline, greenish-yellow liquid whose\nmain function is to emulsify fats in the small intestine and to\neliminate substances from the liver. The liver produces 0.25-\n1L of bile per day.\nBile contains bile salts, phospholipid, cholesterol, and bile\npigments.\nFormation of bile\nBile is produced continuously by hepatocytes.\nBile drains into the hepatic ducts and is stored in the\ngallbladder for subsequent release.\nCholeretic agents increase the formation of bile.\nConstituents of Bile\nBile is made up of bile acids, cholesterol, phospholipids,\nbile pigments (such as bilirubin and biliverdin), electrolytes\nand water.\nThese are split into two main groups, known as the bile\nacid-dependent and bile acid-independent components:\nThe bile acid-dependent component is produced by\nhepatocytes. The hepatocytes secrete bile acids, bile\npigments and cholesterol into canaliculi, which are small\nchannels that transport the bile acid dependent portion\ntowards the bile ducts.\nThe bile acid-independent component is made by the\nductal cells that line the bile ducts. These cells secrete an\nalkaline solution - similar to the fluid made by pancreatic\nduct cells. The hormone secretin stimulates this secretion.\nBoth the dependent and independent components of bile\nenter the intrahepatic bile ducts which drain into the biliary\ntree, a series of ducts which transport bile from the liver to\nthe gallbladder and duodenum.\nBile is continuously produced, but we only need it during and\nafter meals. Therefore, the gallbladder concentrates and stores bile, by removing the water and ions.\nAfter eating, the hormone cholecystokinin is released from\nthe duodenum (in response to small peptides and fatty acids\nin the duodenum). This stimulates gallbladder contraction\nand relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, thus allowing bile to flow\ninto the duodenum.\nRecirculation of bile acids to the liver\nThe terminal ileum contains a Na+ -bile acid cotransporter,\nwhich is a secondary active transporter that recirculates bile\nacids to the liver.\nBecause bile acids are not recirculated until they reach the\nterminal ileum, bile acids are present for maximal absorption\nof lipids throughout the upper small intestine.\nAfter ileal resection, bile acids do not recirculate to the liver\nbut are excreted in feces. The bile acid pool is thereby\ndepleted, and fat absorption is impaired, resulting in\nsteatorrhea.\nBile salts\nBile salts are amphipathic molecules because they have both\nhydrophilic and hydrophobic portions. In aqueous solution, bile\nsalts orient themselves around the droplet of lipid and keep\nthe liquid droplets dispersed (emulsification). Aid in intestinal\ndigestion and absorption of lipids by emulsifying and\nsolubilizing them in micelles.\nProduction\nPrimary bile acids (cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid)\nare synthesized from Cholesterol by hepatocytes.\nIn the intestine, bacteria convert a portion of each of the\nprimary bile acids to secondary bile acids (deoxycholic acid\nand lithocholic acid).\nSynthesis of new bile acids occur as needed, to replace bile\nacids that are excreted in the feces.\nThe bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine to form\ntheir respective bile salts.\nFunction of bile acids\n\nEmulsification of lipids to increase the surface area available\nfor digestion by pancreatic lipase.\nMicelles formation to facilitate lipid transport and absorption.\nStimulate the bile production by osmotically attracting water\nand electrolytes into the bile canaliculi.\nRegulation of bile acid synthesis via negative feedback\nmechanism	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
ac736e66-6970-4b1c-9079-9cfea746e5f1	c5a116c4-d2b6-4edb-bf1f-d05abfe7a1d8	calcium-absorption	Calcium Absorption	article	Calcium absorption\nAbsorption of calcium in small intestine depends on\npresence of adequate amounts of the active form of vitamin\nD, 1 ,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, which is produced in the\nkidney. 1 ,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the synthesis of\nan intestinal Ca+2 -binding protein, calbindin D-28K\nVitamin D deficiency or chronic renal failure results in\ninadequate intestinal Ca+ absorption, causing rickets in\nchildren and osteomalacia in adults.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
38dbe586-6258-40fd-a50f-397680aa71f1	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	cap-children	Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children	article	# Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
79d639ea-2621-4a4e-b0f4-49b70a32027d	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-cap-children	Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 3-year-old fully vaccinated boy presents with high fever (39.5C), productive cough, tachypnoea (RR 48/min), and focal crackles with bronchial breathing at the right lung base. Chest radiograph confirms dense right lower lobe lobar consolidation. What is the most likely bacterial pathogen?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/cap-children/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Streptococcus pneumoniae (Pneumococcus)\n- **B.** Mycoplasma pneumoniae\n- **C.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **D.** Haemophilus influenzae type b\n- **E.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nStreptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause of typical bacterial lobar pneumonia in children beyond the neonatal period. Characterized by high remittent fever, tachypnoea, focal bronchial breath sounds, and dense lobar consolidation on chest radiograph.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to British Thoracic Society (BTS) paediatric pneumonia guidelines, what is the first-line oral antibiotic of choice for children with mild-to-moderate community-acquired pneumonia?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/cap-children/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oral Amoxicillin (first-line in all ages)\n- **B.** Oral Ciprofloxacin\n- **C.** Oral Doxycycline\n- **D.** Oral Clarithromycin monotherapy in an infant\n- **E.** Oral Metronidazole\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS guidelines recommend oral Amoxicillin as the first-line empirical antibiotic for all children with mild-to-moderate community-acquired pneumonia because it provides excellent coverage against Streptococcus pneumoniae. Macrolides (e.g. clarithromycin or azithromycin) are added if atypical pneumonia (Mycoplasma) is suspected in children >=5 years.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nAn 8-year-old girl presents with persistent low-grade fever, dry hacking cough, headache, malaise, and patchy bilateral reticulonodular interstitial infiltrates on chest radiograph. Auscultation reveals diffuse fine crackles rather than lobar consolidation. What is the most likely pathogen?\n\n- **A.** Mycoplasma pneumoniae\n- **B.** Streptococcus pneumoniae\n- **C.** Group B Streptococcus\n- **D.** Klebsiella pneumoniae\n- **E.** Legionella pneumophila\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMycoplasma pneumoniae is the classic cause of 'atypical' community-acquired pneumonia in school-aged children and adolescents (>5 years). It features gradual onset, prominent headache, dry hacking cough, constitutional symptoms, and diffuse bilateral chest infiltrates that appear worse than clinical examination ('walking pneumonia'). Treatment is with a macrolide (clarithromycin/azithromycin).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 4-year-old girl with severe staphylococcal pneumonia develops worsening breathlessness, high fevers, and dullness to percussion over the left hemithorax despite 48 hours of IV co-amoxiclav. Diagnostic pleural aspiration yields cloudy, purulent fluid with pH 6.9, glucose 1.2 mmol/L, and LDH >1000 IU/L. What complication has occurred?\n\n- **A.** Parapneumonic Empyema\n- **B.** Simple reactive pleural effusion\n- **C.** Pneumothorax\n- **D.** Chylothorax\n- **E.** Congestive heart failure\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAn empyema is pus or infected fluid in the pleural space, confirmed by macroscopic pus or pleural fluid pH <7.2, low glucose (<2.2 mmol/L), and elevated LDH (>1000 IU/L). Parapneumonic empyema in children requires IV antibiotics (e.g. co-amoxiclav or cefotaxime) plus chest tube drainage, often with intrapleural fibrinolytics (urokinase) or VATS surgical debridement.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nAccording to the World Health Organization (WHO) and BTS, what single clinical sign is the most sensitive and specific bedside physical marker of pneumonia in a febrile young child?\n\n- **A.** Tachypnoea (age-adjusted fast breathing: >50/min in 2-11 months, >40/min in 1-5 years)\n- **B.** Presence of chest pain\n- **C.** Purulent sputum\n- **D.** Night sweats\n- **E.** Digital clubbing\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nWHO and BTS recognize Tachypnoea (fast breathing) as the most reliable clinical indicator of pneumonia in children. Thresholds: >=60 breaths/min in infants <2 months; >=50 breaths/min in infants 2-11 months; >=40 breaths/min in children 1-5 years. In the absence of tachypnoea, bacterial pneumonia is highly unlikely.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
425ffc8d-d36c-4031-b65e-646a4e3a32c2	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	community-acquired-pneumonia	Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults	article	# Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
42767edc-955f-4db0-8f8e-c8d575dab10b	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-community-acquired-pneumonia	Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 72-year-old man presents with fever, productive cough with rust-coloured sputum, and right pleuritic chest pain. Examination: GCS 15 (alert), RR 32/min, BP 106/68 mmHg, Urea 8.4 mmol/L. Based on the CURB-65 scoring system, what is his score and the recommended management setting?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/community-acquired-pneumonia/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** CURB-65 score 3 (Urea >7, RR >=30, Age >=65); High risk, requires urgent hospital admission and consideration of HDU/ICU\n- **B.** CURB-65 score 1; Low risk, suitable for home treatment with oral amoxicillin\n- **C.** CURB-65 score 0; Discharge immediately\n- **D.** CURB-65 score 5; Palliative care only\n- **E.** CURB-65 is only used for children\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCURB-65 criteria (1 point each): Confusion (AMTS <=8 or new disorientation); Urea >7 mmol/L; Respiratory rate >=30/min; Blood pressure (systolic <90 or diastolic <=60 mmHg); Age >=65 years. Patient scores 3 points: Urea 8.4 (1), RR 32 (1), Age 72 (1). Score 3-5 indicates severe pneumonia with 30-day mortality >15-20%, mandating hospital admission and urgent consideration of critical care.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic bacterial pathogen responsible for pneumonia associated with rust-coloured sputum and lobar consolidation on chest X-ray?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/community-acquired-pneumonia/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Streptococcus pneumoniae (Pneumococcus)\n- **B.** Klebsiella pneumoniae\n- **C.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **D.** Mycoplasma pneumoniae\n- **E.** Legionella pneumophila\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nStreptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause of CAP (accounting for ~50% of identifiable cases). The sputum is characteristically 'rust-coloured' due to red cell extravasation into the alveoli during the red hepatisation stage of lobar pneumonia.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 56-year-old hotel maintenance worker presents with high fever, dry cough, severe diarrhoea, confusion, and hyponatraemia (serum sodium 124 mmol/L). Transaminases are elevated, and chest radiograph shows bilateral consolidation. What is the most likely pathogen and diagnostic test of choice?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/community-acquired-pneumonia/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Legionella pneumophila; Urinary Antigen Test\n- **B.** Streptococcus pneumoniae; Blood culture\n- **C.** Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Cold agglutinins\n- **D.** Chlamydia psittaci; Throat swab\n- **E.** Coxiella burnetii; Liver biopsy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLegionella pneumophila (Legionnaires' disease) is transmitted via aerosolised droplets from colonized water systems (cooling towers, hot tubs, air conditioning). Hallmarks: severe pneumonia with prominent extrapulmonary features (gastrointestinal symptoms/diarrhoea, neurological confusion/headache, deranged LFTs, and marked hyponatraemia). Diagnosis is made rapidly by urinary antigen testing (detects L. pneumophila serogroup 1).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guideline NG138, what is the empirical antibiotic regimen of choice for patients with severe Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CURB-65 score >=3)?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) in Adults)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/community-acquired-pneumonia/figure_04_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Co-amoxiclav (or Ceftriaxone) PLUS a Macrolide (IV Clarithromycin)\n- **B.** Oral Amoxicillin 500mg TDS alone\n- **C.** Oral Doxycycline 100mg once daily monotherapy\n- **D.** IV Gentamicin monotherapy\n- **E.** Oral Ciprofloxacin alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor severe CAP (CURB-65 >=3), NICE recommends dual therapy with a broad-spectrum beta-lactam (IV Co-amoxiclav 1.2g TDS or IV Ceftriaxone 2g daily) PLUS a macrolide (IV Clarithromycin 500mg BD) within 4 hours of admission to cover typical pathogens (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae) and atypical pathogens (Legionella, Mycoplasma).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nAn alcoholic patient presents with severe right upper lobe cavitation and producing thick, gelatinous, dark red 'redcurrant jelly' sputum. What pathogen is classically responsible?\n\n- **A.** Klebsiella pneumoniae\n- **B.** Streptococcus pneumoniae\n- **C.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **D.** Pneumocystis jirovecii\n- **E.** Moraxella catarrhalis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nKlebsiella pneumoniae is an encapsulated Gram-negative bacillus classically causing severe necrotizing lobar pneumonia in alcoholics, diabetics, and debilitated patients. It produces thick, mucoid 'redcurrant jelly' sputum (due to blood and necrotic mucoid capsule material) with bulging fissures and cavitation on CXR.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
