1b9b7e0e-2445-4f7a-9140-72a2b34b7a74	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	loin-pain-pancreatitis	Pancreatitis & Loin Pain Differential	article	# Pancreatitis & Loin Pain Differential\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nSevere and sudden onset of abdominal pain which becomes constant, is located\n- in the epigastric region, right and/or left upper quadrant, and may radiate to the back or flanks. If caused by gallstones, pain may be described as sudden and knife-like, and may\n- be worse after food. nausea, anorexia, and vomiting.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\n\n- Signs of shock — such as tachycardia, tachypnoea, and hypotension.\n- Abdominal distension\n- Abdominal tenderness — may be signs of generalized peritonitis, rebound tenderness, and guarding if severe episode. Cullen's sign — Ecchymoses (bluish discolouration) around the umbilicus.\n- Grey-Turner's sign — Ecchymoses (bluish discolouration) around the flank.\n-\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nBlood tests — FBC, CRP, serum amylase & lipase, LFTS\n- CT or MRI\n- Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) and/or endoscopic ultrasonography (EUS) may be needed if the underlying cause is unknown Management Resuscitation with intravenous fluids.\n- Supplemental oxygen.\n- Intravenous analgesia.\n\ntissue.\n- Complications Pancreatic necrosis\n- Pancreatic abscess\n- Pancreatic fistulae\n- Sepsis\n- DIC previous  Intravenous antibiotics for treatment of infected pancreatic necrosis and/or associated cholangitis. Early nutritional support which may involve initial parenteral feeding if the person is unable to tolerate oral intake SurgicaLmanagement Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) to relieve the obstruction within 72 hours of the onset of pain, for those with gallstones and cholangitis, jaundice, or common bile duct obstruction.\n- Cholecystectomy may be arranged during the same hospital admission for mild acute gallstone pancreatitis or may be deferred until after the resolution of severe acute pancreatitis. Percutaneous or endoscopic drainage of pancreatic collections, and potential surgical management of other complications such as debridement of necrotic Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Loin Pain Pancreatitis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/loin-pain-pancreatitis/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Loin Pain Pancreatitis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	30	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
14ebdb4a-9596-42cd-b8a0-c5b663a37569	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-loin-pain-pancreatitis	Pancreatitis & Loin Pain Differential — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Pancreatitis & Loin Pain Differential — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 48-year-old male with a history of alcohol misuse presents with severe, constant epigastric pain that radiates directly through to his back, accompanied by persistent vomiting and relief when leaning forward. Serum Lipase is 1450 IU/L (normal <60 IU/L). According to the revised Atlanta classification, how many criteria are required to establish the diagnosis of Acute Pancreatitis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Pancreatitis & Loin Pain Differential)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/loin-pain-pancreatitis/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** At least 2 of 3 criteria: (1) Characteristic epigastric pain radiating to back; (2) Serum amylase or lipase >= 3 times upper limit of normal; (3) Characteristic cross-sectional imaging findings\n- **B.** All 3 criteria plus mandatory exploratory laparotomy\n- **C.** Only an elevated lipase is required, regardless of symptoms\n- **D.** Positive blood culture alone\n- **E.** Serum amylase >10,000 IU/L\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRevised Atlanta criteria: Diagnosis of acute pancreatitis requires at least 2 of the following 3 features: (1) Typical abdominal pain (severe, constant epigastric pain radiating to the back, relieved by sitting forward); (2) Serum amylase or lipase elevated >= 3 times the upper limit of normal; (3) Characteristic findings on contrast CT, MRI, or ultrasound. If criteria 1 and 2 are met, routine early CT is NOT necessary.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhy is Serum Lipase preferred over Serum Amylase in the diagnostic evaluation of suspected acute pancreatitis in the ED?\n\n- **A.** Lipase has higher sensitivity and specificity (95% vs 80%), rises similarly, but remains elevated for 8-14 days (longer diagnostic window) compared to amylase which normalizes within 48-72 hours\n- **B.** Amylase cannot be measured in the laboratory\n- **C.** Lipase is elevated only in pancreatitis and nowhere else\n- **D.** Amylase is only found in salivary glands\n- **E.** Lipase levels determine the need for antibiotics\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSerum Lipase is superior to amylase: it is produced almost exclusively by the pancreas (higher specificity), while amylase is also produced by salivary glands, fallopian tubes, and small bowel. Furthermore, lipase remains elevated for up to 8-14 days, whereas amylase falls rapidly within 2-4 days, making lipase far more reliable in patients with delayed presentation.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich bedside clinical signs denote retroperitoneal necrotizing haemorrhage in severe acute pancreatitis?\n\n- **A.** Grey Turner's sign (flank ecchymosis) and Cullen's sign (periumbilical ecchymosis)\n- **B.** Murphy's sign and Rovsing's sign\n- **C.** Trousseau's sign and Chvostek's sign\n- **D.** Kussmaul's sign and Pulsus paradoxus\n- **E.** Osler's nodes and Janeway lesions\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nGrey Turner's sign is blue-red/purple ecchymosis of the flanks caused by retroperitoneal blood tracking along the transversalis fascia to the lateral abdominal wall. Cullen's sign is periumbilical ecchymosis caused by blood tracking along the falciform ligament. Both indicate severe necrotizing pancreatitis with retroperitoneal haemorrhage.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common cause of Acute Pancreatitis in the UK and worldwide, followed by alcohol misuse?\n\n- **A.** Gallstones (Cholelithiasis / biliary pancreatitis, causing 45-50% of cases)\n- **B.** Hypertriglyceridaemia\n- **C.** Autoimmune pancreatitis\n- **D.** ERCP complications\n- **E.** Scorpion sting venom\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe leading causes of acute pancreatitis (mnemonic: I GET SMASHED): Gallstones account for ~45-50%, followed by Alcohol (~30-35%). Other causes include Hypertriglyceridaemia (>11 mmol/L / 1000 mg/dL), ERCP, trauma, drugs (azathioprine, valproate, thiazides), hypercalcaemia, and structural anomalies.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended approach to early intravenous fluid resuscitation in mild-to-moderate acute pancreatitis according to modern international (IAP/APA and BSG) guidelines?\n\n- **A.** Goal-directed, moderate fluid resuscitation using a balanced crystalloid (e.g. Hartmann's / Ringer's lactate) at 1.5-2 ml/kg/hr, avoiding aggressive excessive fluid overload\n- **B.** Hyper-aggressive resuscitation with >6 litres of 0.9% normal saline in the first 12 hours\n- **C.** Fluid restriction to 500 ml/day\n- **D.** Immediate 20% albumin infusion in all patients\n- **E.** Dextrose 5% only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe landmark WATERFALL trial (NEJM 2022) showed that aggressive early fluid resuscitation (>3-4 L in 12h) significantly increases the incidence of fluid overload and heart failure without improving clinical outcomes. Modern guidelines recommend goal-directed, moderate fluid resuscitation with balanced crystalloids (Hartmann's at ~1.5-2 ml/kg/h) titrated to clinical parameters (urine output >0.5 ml/kg/h, hematocrit, BUN).\n	\N	[]	\N	f	31	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d0424baa-b808-4b67-9c92-f5734d7f7218	7d6ad555-2a31-4365-ad9b-d6abfb9b25c1	plateletes-bleeding-disorder	Plateletes Bleeding Disorder	article	Platelets bleeding disorder\nThrombocytopenia or abnormal platelet function is\ncharacterized by spontaneous skin purpura, mucosal\nhaemorrhage and prolonged bleeding after trauma.\nImportant to note here difference between\nTTP and haemolytic uremic syndrome.\nNote: Difference between TTP and haemolytic uremic\nsyndrome\n\n![Plateletes Bleeding Disorder - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/plateletes-bleeding-disorder/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Plateletes Bleeding Disorder — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Plateletes Bleeding Disorder - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/plateletes-bleeding-disorder/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Plateletes Bleeding Disorder — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
7aa9dd5a-be80-45d6-808b-adb5915da420	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	lower-gi-bleeding	Acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding	article	# Acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nSevere lower GI bleeding has been defined as continued bleeding within the first\n- 24 hours of hospitalization (transfusion of 22 units of blood and/or hematocrit decrease of 220%).\n- The-following bleeding;\n- Systolic BP <115 mmHg Heart rate\n- IOO bpm\n-\n- Syncope Nontender abdomen\n- Bleeding per rectum during the first 4 hours of presentation\n- Aspirin use\n- More than 2 active comorbid states\n- Shock index\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 21:\nThe shock index is calculated by dividing the patient's heart rate by\n- the systolic blood pressure and is a marker of hemodynamic instability. Detail history\n- Age of patient: Diverticular disease and angiodysplasia are more common in older patients (age\n- 60 years). Duration of the bleeding\n- Color of the blood: The color of the blood in the stool, and whether the blood coats\n- the stool or is mixed with the stool, helps to localize the site of bleeding\n- Associated dizziness or syncope: Presence of syncope may suggest that the patient has lost a significant arnount of blood.\n- Other associated features include abdominal pain, constipation, rectal pain, weight loss, diarrhea, and tenesmus. The presence of abdominal pain and diarrhea with hematochezia (bright red rectal bleeding) suggests colitis, either inflammatory or ischemic, while angiodysplasia does not usually cause pain.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\nAssessment of vital signs — Presence of hemodynamic instability suggests presence of severe bleeding.\n- Palpation of the abdomen — for tenderness, hepatomegaly or abdominal masses. Hepatomegaly or abdominal masses prompt evaluation for colorectal cancer as the source of the bleeding.\n- Rectal exam — It is essential to perform a rectal exam in all patients with lower GI bleeding to exclude a palpable rectal mass.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nFBC ESR\n- C-Reactive protein\n- Stool culture\n- Coagulation screen\n- LFTs\n- Cross-match\n- VBC\n- CXR\n- Anoscopy — In a patient with suspected anorectal source of bleeding, anoscopy can be a useful diagnostic test. Internal hemorrhoids can be visualized on anoscopy.\n- Colonoscopy is usually the initial imaging procedure in all patients with suspected lower GI bleeding in whom an upper GI or anorectal source has been excluded.\n- Radionuclide imaging — In patients with a normal colonoscopy and esophagogastroduodenoscopy and persistent bleeding, radionucleotide imaging may be useful to localize the source of even minimal bleeding (as little as 0.1 mL/minute).\n\n- Angiography — is performed in patients with persistent GI bleeding with negative colonoscopy or in whom endoscopy is not feasible.\n- CT scan of the abdomen — is useful in the diagnosis of ischemic colitis and aortoenteric fistula. CT scan of the abdomen is the first test done in evaluation of patients with suspected colonic ischemia.\n- Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) — is the diagnostic test of choice to rule out the upper GI tract as the source of the bleeding. It is also useful for the diagnosis of aortoenteric fistula.\n- Serologic markers (ANA, ANCA) — performed if the bleeding is suspected to be secondary to a vasculitic disease such as systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nResuscitation\n- Two large-bore peripheral intravenous catheters or a central venous line are necessary.\n- Resuscitation with intravenous fluids and blood transfusions and correction of any underlying coagulopathy or thrombocytopenia is required.\n- Colonoscopy_(used for both diagnostic and therapeutic purpose)\n- Guidelines published by the American Society for Gastroenterology strongly recommend that a Colonoscopy should be the initial diagnostic procedure for nearly all patients presenting with acute LGIB.\n- In patients with high-risk clinical features and signs or symptoms of ongoing bleeding, a rapid bowel purge should be initiated following hemodynamic resuscitation and performed within 24 h of patient presentation.\n- During colonoscopy, it is possible to perform coagulation to stop the bleeding (thermal contact or epinephrine injection, haemoclips and band ligation).\n- it is vital to consider the risk of perforation when using this treatment modality.\n\n- Angiography\n- Angiography is recommended in patients with a massive lower GI haemorrhage or if a colonoscopy proves unsuccessful.\n- Superselective embolisation aims to decrease the arterial blood flow. This reduces pressure to the bleeding site so haemostasis occurs. There is a small risk of ischemia\n- Surgical-intervention-(segmental-resec.tion)\n- Surgery acutely for LGIB should be considered if massive bleed, and after other therapeutic options have failed.\n- Note: other Iow risk condition i-e anal fissure , hemorrhoid manage accordingly. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Lower Gi Bleeding - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/lower-gi-bleeding/figure_01_p5.jpeg)\n*Lower Gi Bleeding — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	32	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9de74c1c-6659-4dde-b269-48c4a0ea1172	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-expert-clinical-sba-mock-2	Expert Clinical SBA Mock 2	quiz	# Expert Clinical SBA Mock 2\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1000	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
82f53b61-71be-4b05-a0cc-5d70097135de	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	mallory-weiss-syndrome-upper-gi-bleeding	Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding	article	# Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nDefinition Clinical features Investigations Mallory-Weiss Tear Mallory-Weiss tear, also known as Mallory-Weiss syndrome, is a longitudinal mucosal tear or laceration of the mucous membrane in the region of the gastroesophageal junction and gastric cardia. Vomiting Retching Hematemesis Epigastric pain Endoscopy confirm diagnosis Boerhaave syndrome It is due to the rupture of the esophagus due to forceful emesis after excessive food or alcohol consumptions. Esophageal transmural tear Sudden, severe chest pain. Nausea and vomiting. Pain or coughing when swallowing. Short, rapid breathing. Swelling in the tissues of the chest cavity with air or fluid. Hard, rigid abdomen. Signs of infection like fever and sweating. Esophagram CXR findings Pneumomediastinum Left sided pleural effusion Pneumothorax Subcutaneous emphysema\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nResuscitation & supportive care. Consider anti-emetic Endoscopic intervention for continual bleed Iv fluid & antibiotics Surgical repair. Ideally, the rupture should be repaired directly within 24 hours.\n\nEsophageal rupture- yellow arrow indicates subcutaneous emphysema and the black arrows indicate pneumomediastinum\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/mallory-weiss-syndrome-upper-gi-bleeding/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/mallory-weiss-syndrome-upper-gi-bleeding/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/mallory-weiss-syndrome-upper-gi-bleeding/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/mallory-weiss-syndrome-upper-gi-bleeding/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	34	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
aa9e45d2-9764-4380-b9ed-5447781c7fb2	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-mallory-weiss-tear	Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 32-year-old male presents with hematemesis after a night of heavy alcohol binge drinking. He reports multiple episodes of non-bloody, forceful retching and vomiting, after which the vomit abruptly became mixed with bright red blood. He is haemodynamically stable. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/mallory-weiss-tear/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Mallory-Weiss Syndrome (mucosal tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction)\n- **B.** Boerhaave's syndrome (transmural rupture)\n- **C.** Bleeding peptic ulcer\n- **D.** Ruptured oesophageal varices\n- **E.** Gastric adenocarcinoma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMallory-Weiss syndrome is a longitudinal, non-transmural mucosal laceration occurring at the gastro-oesophageal junction or gastric cardia, classic in alcohol misuse or gastroenteritis. The hallmark clinical history is repeated episodes of severe, forceful non-bloody vomiting or retching, followed abruptly by fresh haematemesis.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nHow does the anatomical depth of tissue injury in Mallory-Weiss Syndrome fundamentally differ from Boerhaave's Syndrome?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/mallory-weiss-tear/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Mallory-Weiss is a non-transmural mucosal/submucosal tear; Boerhaave's is a complete, full-thickness transmural perforation of the oesophageal wall\n- **B.** Mallory-Weiss causes pneumomediastinum, Boerhaave's does not\n- **C.** Boerhaave's involves only the oral mucosa\n- **D.** Mallory-Weiss is always fatal within 1 hour\n- **E.** There is no anatomical difference\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMallory-Weiss tear is confined to the mucosa and submucosa (causing bleeding without perforation). Boerhaave's syndrome is spontaneous full-thickness transmural rupture of the oesophagus (typically left posterolateral distal oesophagus), leading to mediastinitis, subcutaneous emphysema, septic shock, and Mackler's triad.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the natural clinical course and primary management for the vast majority (>85-90%) of patients presenting with Mallory-Weiss tears who are haemodynamically stable?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/mallory-weiss-tear/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Spontaneous resolution and cessation of bleeding with supportive care, IV proton pump inhibitors, and antiemetics\n- **B.** Mandatory emergency open thoracotomy and surgical repair\n- **C.** Endoscopic mucosal resection\n- **D.** Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS)\n- **E.** Balloon tamponade with a Sengstaken tube\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOver 85-90% of Mallory-Weiss mucosal tears stop bleeding spontaneously without any endoscopic or surgical intervention. Management consists of supportive care, IV antiemetics (e.g. ondansetron) to eliminate retching, and IV high-dose proton pump inhibitors to promote mucosal healing.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhen active arterial spurting or an oozing vessel is visualized at a Mallory-Weiss tear during endoscopy, which endoscopic haemostatic modalities are recommended?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Mallory-Weiss Syndrome & Upper GI Bleeding)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/mallory-weiss-tear/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Endoscopic haemostatic clipping (hemoclips) or thermal coagulation with dilute adrenaline injection\n- **B.** Total gastrectomy\n- **C.** Radiation therapy\n- **D.** High-volume saline irrigation only\n- **E.** Insertion of a permanent metallic stent\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn the minority of patients with active spurting or persistent oozing at the tear site, endoscopic haemostasis is highly effective (>95% success). Endoscopic haemostatic clips (through-the-scope hemoclips) are preferred as they avoid tissue necrosis, often combined with submucosal adrenaline injection.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat pre-existing anatomical condition is present in up to 75% of patients who develop Mallory-Weiss tears, predisposing the gastro-oesophageal junction to shearing forces during vomiting?\n\n- **A.** Hiatus Hernia (sliding type)\n- **B.** Achalasia cardia\n- **C.** Zenker's diverticulum\n- **D.** Oesophageal web (Plummer-Vinson)\n- **E.** Eosinophilic oesophagitis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nA sliding hiatus hernia is present in over 75% of patients with Mallory-Weiss syndrome. The displacement of the gastro-oesophageal junction into the negative-pressure thoracic cavity creates an altered transmural pressure gradient during retching, concentrating mechanical shear stress directly on the mucosal junction.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	35	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4215c110-498a-4161-80ce-dc8b6c7900e1	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	peptic-ulcer	Peptic Ulcer Disease & Complications	article	# Peptic Ulcer Disease & Complications\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Test the person for H. pyloriinfection\n- A carbon-13 urea breath test or stool antigen test — ensure the person has not taken a PPI in the past 2 weeks, or antibiotics in the past 4 weeks, or\n- Laboratory serological testing if these options are not available, providing the test has been locally validated.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nConfirmed H-pylori infection associated with NSAID use\n- PPI therapy- prescribe full-dose PPI therapy (Lansoprazole 30 mg, omeprazole 20— 40 mg, esomeprazole 20 mg, pantoprazole 40 mg) for 2 months\n- H-pylori eradication therapy — PPI twice daily and amoxicillin 1 g twice daily and eitherclarithromycin 500 mg twice-daily or metronidazole 400 mg twice-daily for 7 — IO days\n- Ifthe person is allergic to penicillin, BNF recommends, offer a 7-day triple therapy regimen inlcude PPI twice daily and clarithromycin 250 mg twice daily and metronidazole 400 mg twice-daily.\n- Confirmed H-pylori infection, Not associated with NSAID use\n- prescribe 7-day first-line IA-pylori eradication therapy only.\n- 'f the person tests negative for H. pylori infection with a proven gastric or duodenal ulcer\n- Prescribe full-dose PPI therapy for 4—8 weeks, depending on clinical judgement.\n- Prevention measures\n- Offer advice on lifestyle measures which may improve symptoms — Lose weight if they are overweight or obese\n\nAvoid any trigger foods, such as coffee, chocolate, tomatoes, fatty or spicy foods.\n- Eat smaller meals and eat their evening meal 3—4 hours before going to bed, if\n- possible. Smoking cessation\n- Reduce alcohol consumption\n- Assess for stress, anxiety, and depression which may worsen symptoms. Encourage\n- relaxation strategies, consider referral for psychological therapies, and manage depression symptoms if needed. Review the person's medication:\n- stop NSAID Consider stopping or reducing — Aspirin, bisphosphonates, corticosteroids,\n- potassium supplements, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), or recreational drugs such as crack cocaine. Advise that self-treatment with an antacid and/or alginate may be used for short- term symptom control, but long-term, continuous use is not recommended.\n- Advise a person with a proven gastric or duodenal ulcer to arrange a follow-up appointment if there are refractory or recurrent symptoms.\n- Complications Haemorrhage\n- Perforation\n- Gastric outlet obstruction — this may result from strictures and stenosis of\n- the pylorus and/or duodenum due to chronic inflammation and scarring, Gastric malignancy — there is an increased risk in Helicobacter pyloripositive\n- gastric ulcer disease.\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Peptic Ulcer - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/peptic-ulcer/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Peptic Ulcer — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	36	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
bef746dd-211c-4308-9b2d-cff8bde2205a	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-peptic-ulcer	Peptic Ulcer Disease & Complications — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Peptic Ulcer Disease & Complications — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 52-year-old male with chronic dyspepsia treated with over-the-counter antacids presents with sudden-onset, catastrophic 'thunderclap' epigastric pain 2 hours ago that rapidly became generalized across the entire abdomen. On examination, he lies motionless; the abdomen is rigid, board-like, and exquisitely tender with absent bowel sounds. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peptic Ulcer Disease & Complications)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/peptic-ulcer/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Perforated Peptic Ulcer with generalized chemical peritonitis\n- **B.** Acute biliary colic\n- **C.** Uncomplicated acute gastritis\n- **D.** Irritable bowel syndrome\n- **E.** Acute non-perforated appendicitis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPerforated peptic ulcer (typically anterior duodenal or gastric ulcer) releases acidic gastric juices and gastroduodenal contents directly into the peritoneal cavity, causing sudden, dramatic, explosive peritonitis. Hallmark: sudden agonizing pain, patient lies motionless (movement worsens somatic pain), rigid board-like abdominal wall, and absent bowel sounds.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic diagnostic finding on an erect chest radiograph (CXR) in over 70-80% of patients with a perforated peptic ulcer?\n\n- **A.** Pneumoperitoneum (free subdiaphragmatic crescentic air under the right hemidiaphragm)\n- **B.** Bilateral pleural effusions\n- **C.** Widened mediastinum\n- **D.** Air bronchograms in the left base\n- **E.** Ground-glass lung opacities\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPneumoperitoneum (free intraperitoneal gas) is visible on an erect chest X-ray in 70-85% of visceral perforations as a thin crescent of radiolucent air beneath the right hemidiaphragm (between liver and diaphragm). If erect CXR is non-diagnostic or the patient is unable to stand, a left lateral decubitus abdominal X-ray or CT abdomen confirms free air.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich two aetiological factors account for more than 90% of all Peptic Ulcer Disease cases worldwide?\n\n- **A.** Helicobacter pylori infection and Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs / Aspirin)\n- **B.** Spicy food consumption and psychological stress\n- **C.** Alcohol intake and caffeine\n- **D.** Viral hepatitis and paracetamol\n- **E.** Gluten enteropathy and milk protein allergy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHelicobacter pylori (Gram-negative microaerophilic spiral bacillus that colonizes gastric mucosa) and NSAIDs/Aspirin (which inhibit COX-1 and deplete mucosal cytoprotective prostaglandins) together account for over 90% of all gastric and duodenal ulcers. Eradication of H. pylori and avoidance of NSAIDs cures peptic ulcer disease in the vast majority.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat endoscopic grading classification stratifies the risk of recurrent haemorrhage and mortality in bleeding peptic ulcers to determine the necessity of endoscopic therapy?\n\n- **A.** Forrest Classification (Ia, Ib, IIa, IIb, IIc, III)\n- **B.** Hinchey classification\n- **C.** Child-Pugh classification\n- **D.** Los Angeles classification\n- **E.** Bormann classification\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nForrest Classification: Active bleeding: Ia (spurting arterial - 90% rebleed risk), Ib (oozing); Recent bleeding: IIa (non-bleeding visible vessel - 50% rebleed), IIb (adherent clot - 30% rebleed), IIc (flat pigmented spot - 10% rebleed); No bleeding: III (clean ulcer base - <5% rebleed). Forrest Ia, Ib, and IIa mandate dual endoscopic haemostatic therapy.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the standard triple-therapy eradication regimen recommended by NICE for Helicobacter pylori infection?\n\n- **A.** High-dose Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI, e.g. Omeprazole 20mg BD) PLUS Amoxicillin 1g BD PLUS either Clarithromycin 500mg BD or Metronidazole 400mg BD for 7 days\n- **B.** Ciprofloxacin, Metronidazole, and Aspirin for 14 days\n- **C.** Omeprazole monotherapy for 6 months\n- **D.** Vancomycin, Gentamicin, and Prednisolone\n- **E.** Doxycycline and Fluconazole\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE recommends first-line H. pylori eradication with a 7-day, twice-daily triple regimen: (1) Full-dose PPI twice daily; (2) Amoxicillin 1g twice daily; (3) Clarithromycin 500mg BD (or Metronidazole 400mg BD in penicillin allergy). For areas with high clarithromycin resistance, bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI + Bismuth + Metronidazole + Tetracycline) is preferred.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	37	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9cabb1d0-57ae-4a72-b461-99eef406b575	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-upper-gi-bleeding	Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 60-year-old male presents with sudden-onset haematemesis and melaena. Pulse is 118 bpm, BP is 92/58 mmHg, and Hb is 82 g/L. Which validated risk assessment tool is recommended by NICE and BSG for pre-endoscopy risk stratification in all patients presenting with acute Upper GI Bleeding to determine who requires urgent hospital admission vs outpatient management?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Glasgow-Blatchford Bleeding Score (GBS)\n- **B.** Rockall Score (post-endoscopy)\n- **C.** Oakland Score\n- **D.** Child-Pugh Score\n- **E.** Alvarado Score\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE CG141 and BSG guidelines recommend the Glasgow-Blatchford Score (GBS) for pre-endoscopy risk assessment. It uses urea, haemoglobin, systolic BP, pulse, and clinical comorbidities. A GBS of 0 (or 0-1) identifies very low risk patients (<1% mortality or intervention) who can be safely managed as outpatients. A GBS >=2 mandates inpatient admission; GBS >=6 identifies high risk requiring early intervention.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the target haemoglobin threshold for blood transfusion in acute upper GI bleeding according to NICE guidelines and the landmark Villanueva restrictive transfusion trial?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Restrictive transfusion threshold: Transfuse only when haemoglobin is < 70 g/L (targeting 70-90 g/L), unless the patient has active cardiovascular disease (target 80-100 g/L)\n- **B.** Liberal transfusion: Transfuse all patients with Hb < 100 g/L\n- **C.** Transfuse only when Hb < 40 g/L\n- **D.** Transfuse 4 units immediately to all patients with haematemesis regardless of Hb\n- **E.** Never transfuse red cells in GI bleeding\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe landmark Villanueva RCT (NEJM 2013) demonstrated that a RESTRICTIVE transfusion strategy (trigger Hb <70 g/L, target 70-90 g/L) significantly improved survival and reduced rebleeding compared to a liberal strategy (trigger <90 g/L). Liberal transfusion increases portal venous pressure and reverses physiological splanchnic vasoconstriction, dislodging fresh clots. (In active coronary artery disease, trigger is <80 g/L).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE and BSG guidelines, when should emergency therapeutic Upper Gastrointestinal Endoscopy (OGD) be performed in high-risk patients presenting with acute upper GI bleeding after initial resuscitation?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_03_p12.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Within 24 hours of admission for all stable inpatients, and immediately (<2-6 hours) for patients who remain haemodynamically unstable despite active resuscitation\n- **B.** Within 7 days\n- **C.** Within 15 minutes of arrival before resuscitation\n- **D.** Only after the patient is discharged home\n- **E.** Endoscopy is not recommended for upper GI bleeding\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE recommends endoscopy within 24 hours of presentation for all patients with acute upper GI bleeding. For patients with severe ongoing bleeding, haemodynamic instability refractory to fluid resuscitation, or suspected variceal bleeding, immediate endoscopy should be performed as soon as initial physiological resuscitation is established (within 2-6 hours).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat dual endoscopic haemostatic therapy is recommended by NICE for bleeding peptic ulcers with high-risk stigmata (Forrest Ia, Ib, or IIa)?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_04_p13.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Submucosal injection of dilute adrenaline (epinephrine) combined with a second mechanical modality (e.g. through-the-scope hemoclips) or thermal coagulation\n- **B.** Adrenaline injection monotherapy\n- **C.** Oral antacids only\n- **D.** Intravenous tranexamic acid infusion alone\n- **E.** Barium swallow\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE explicitly recommends that adrenaline injection must NOT be used as monotherapy (as it only produces temporary local vasoconstriction and carries a high rebleeding rate). Endoscopic haemostasis requires combination dual therapy: adrenaline injection PLUS a mechanical modality (hemoclips) or thermal coagulation (heater probe, bipolar electrocoagulation).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 70-year-old male with a prior infra-renal abdominal aortic aneurysm repair with a Dacron prosthetic graft 3 years ago presents with an episode of minor 'herald' haematemesis followed 6 hours later by sudden, catastrophic, exsanguinating upper GI haemorrhage. What life-threatening vascular complication must be suspected?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_05_p13.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Secondary Aorto-Enteric Fistula (classically eroding into the third or fourth portion of the duodenum)\n- **B.** Mallory-Weiss tear\n- **C.** Gastric varices\n- **D.** Bleeding diverticulum\n- **E.** Acute appendiceal fistula\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAorto-enteric fistula (AEF) is an erosion of a prosthetic aortic graft directly into the adjacent gastrointestinal tract (most commonly the third or fourth part of the duodenum, which lies directly over the graft). Hallmark presentation is a small, self-limiting 'herald bleed' (minor haematemesis/melaena) followed hours to days later by catastrophic, fatal exsanguinating haemorrhage. Urgent contrast CT angiography and emergency vascular surgery are life-saving.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	41	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
24f8a64d-2d40-479e-aa33-224012a7b643	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-expert-clinical-sba-mock-2-alt	Expert Clinical SBA Mock 2 (Alt)	quiz	# Expert Clinical SBA Mock 2 (Alt)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1001	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
8e895230-fa5c-4b9e-acba-8130ed053fd8	c5a116c4-d2b6-4edb-bf1f-d05abfe7a1d8	large-intestine	Large Intestine	article	Large intestine\nThe large intestine is the terminal portion of the\ngastrointestinal tract and is approx. 1.5 m (5 ft) long. The large\nintestine is divided into the cecum and appendix, the\nascending colon, transverse colon, descending colon,\nsigmoid colon, rectum and anal canal.\nThe large intestine has 3 primary functions: absorbing water\nand electrolytes, producing and absorbing vitamins, and\nforming and propelling feces toward the rectum for\nelimination. By the time indigestible materials have reached\nthe colon, most nutrients and up to 90% of the water has\nbeen absorbed by the small intestine.\nMotility\nThe intestinal wall is made up of multiple layers. The 4 layers of\nthe large intestine from the lumen outward are the mucosa,\nsubmucosa, muscular layer, and serosa. The muscular layer is\nmade up of 2 layers of smooth muscle, the inner, circular layer,\nand the outer, longitudinal layer. These layers contribute to the\nmotility of the large intestine. There are 2 types of motility\npresent in the colon, haustral contraction and mass movement.\nHaustra are saccules in the colon that give it its segmented\nappearance. Haustral contraction is activated by the presence\nof chyme and serves to move food slowly to the next haustra,\nalong with mixing the chyme to help with water absorption.\nMass movements are stronger and serve to move the chyme to\nthe rectum quickly.\nAbsorption of water and electrolytes\nAbsorption of water occurs by osmosis. Water diffuses in\nresponse to an osmotic gradient established by the\nabsorption of electrolytes. Sodium is actively absorbed in the\ncolon by sodium channels. Potassium is either absorbed or\nsecreted depending on the concentration in the lumen and the electrochemical gradient created by the active\nabsorption of sodium.\nChloride and bicarbonate - the movement of sodium into the\nplasma produces an electrochemical gradient to allow\nabsorption of chloride. Chloride ions are exchanged for\nbicarbonate ions (causing net bicarbonate secretion).\nProduction/Absorption of Vitamins\nThe colon houses trillions of bacteria that protect our gut and\nproduce vitamins. The bacteria in the colon produce\nsubstantial amounts of vitamins by fermentation. Vitamin K\nand B vitamins, including biotin, are produced by colonic\nbacteria. These vitamins are then absorbed into the blood.\nWhen dietary intake of these vitamins is low in an individual,\nthe colon plays a significant role in minimizing vitamin\ndisparity.\n\n![Large Intestine - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/large-intestine/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Large Intestine — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8462293a-9efd-4ef8-9589-fe8b71b251cf	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	aldosterone	Aldosterone	article	Aldosterone\nAldosterone is the main mineralocorticoid steroid hormone\nproduced by the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex in the\nadrenal gland. It is essential for sodium conservation in the\nkidney. It plays a central role in the homeostatic regulation of\nblood pressure, plasma sodium (Na+), and potassium (K+)\nlevels.\nAldosterone release is stimulated by\n\n## Acth\n\nAngiotensin II\nHyperkalemia\nAldosterone release inhibited by\nHypokalemia\nInhibitors of renin angiotensin system (ACE, ARBs)\nDopamine and ANP\nMechanism of action: aldosterone binds to intracellular\nmineralocorticoid receptors in the distal tubule and collecting\nduct of the kidney, inducing protein synthesis and following\nchanges:\n↑ Na+/K+-ATPase in the basolateral membrane →\ntransportation of Na+ out and K+ into the tubule cells\n↑ Apical H+-ATPase →  H+ excretion\n↑ Na+ channels (ENaC; epithelial sodium channel) →  ↑ Na+\nreabsorption\n↑ K+ channels (ROMK; renal outer medullary potassium\nchannel) in the luminal membrane →  ↑ K+ excretion\nEffects\nThis aldosterone induced changes produce a concentration\ngradient →  Na+ reabsorption →  water reabsorption and K+\nsecretion into the urine.\n\nUltimately, these effects →  ↑ blood pressure, hypokalemia\nand ↑ pH level	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
03455c69-ec13-46df-9aaa-0277edc846c5	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-expert-clinical-sba-mock-3-moka-edition	Expert Clinical SBA Mock 3 (MOKA Edition)	quiz	# Expert Clinical SBA Mock 3 (MOKA Edition)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1002	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	\N
