e04052ba-bead-4a06-b195-3ac1f86e5603	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	pykoric-stenosis	Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis	article	# Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement, followed by pyloromyotomy (open or laparoscopic).\n- Complications of pyloromyotomy_\n- wound infection, gastric or duodenal mucosal perforation, or incomplete myotomy.\n\n- Blood Gas Values ( 7350\n- 7450 350\n- 450 t pco, poa cBase(Ecf)c Oximetry Values ctHb FCOHb FHHb FMetHb Hctc Electrolyte Values 134\n- 146 cNa\n- 1 n\n- 132 ccaa\n- ccr Metabolite Values t cGlu t cLac 7.618 54.4 164 56.2 30.1 99.3 108 1.2 0.7 1.5 33.2 136 3.0 097 72 8.7 1.7 mmHg ntmHg mmobL mmol,L gfL mmobL r,nmobl_ nmol,L mmol,L mmol,L mmobL 210\n- 30 34 98\n- 280\n- 30\n- 60\n- 50\n- 108 Figure: Blood gas demonstrating hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis.\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Pykoric Stenosis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/pykoric-stenosis/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Pykoric Stenosis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Pykoric Stenosis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/pykoric-stenosis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Pykoric Stenosis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	38	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3b57adcb-cd4a-40b2-a69f-0ed024dfb835	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-acute-kidney-injury	Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) & KDIGO Staging — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) & KDIGO Staging — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 72-year-old dehydrated male is admitted with sepsis. Baseline serum creatinine was 80 umol/L. Repeat creatinine in the ED is 176 umol/L (more than a 2-fold increase), and urine output over the past 12 hours has been 0.3 ml/kg/hr. According to the Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) classification, what stage of Acute Kidney Injury is this?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) & KDIGO Staging)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** KDIGO Stage 2 AKI\n- **B.** KDIGO Stage 1 AKI\n- **C.** KDIGO Stage 3 AKI\n- **D.** End-Stage Renal Disease\n- **E.** Chronic Kidney Disease Stage 5\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nKDIGO AKI Staging criteria: Stage 1: Serum creatinine 1.5-1.9x baseline OR increase >=26.5 umol/L, or urine output <0.5 ml/kg/hr for 6-12h. Stage 2: Creatinine 2.0-2.9x baseline, or urine output <0.5 ml/kg/hr for >=12h. Stage 3: Creatinine >=3.0x baseline OR >=353.6 umol/L OR initiation of RRT, or anuria for >=12h / urine <0.3 ml/kg/hr for >=24h. This patient meets Stage 2 criteria (creatinine 2.2x baseline and urine <0.5 ml/kg/hr for 12h).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich of the following medications is a notorious cause of pre-renal hemodynamically mediated AKI in hypovolaemic patients by inhibiting renal prostaglandin synthesis and causing afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) & KDIGO Staging)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs, e.g. Ibuprofen, Naproxen)\n- **B.** Paracetamol\n- **C.** Amoxicillin\n- **D.** Inhaled salbutamol\n- **E.** Oral lactulose\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRenal prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2) dilate the afferent arteriole to maintain glomerular capillary plasma flow and GFR during states of reduced renal perfusion (hypovolaemia, heart failure). NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase (COX-1/COX-2), depleting prostaglandins and causing profound afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, precipitating acute pre-renal azotemia.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat classic triad of medications comprises the 'Triple Whammy' that precipitously induces severe acute renal failure in elderly patients?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) & KDIGO Staging)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/figure_03_p9.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** ACE inhibitor (or ARB) + Diuretic + NSAID\n- **B.** Aspirin + Statin + Beta-blocker\n- **C.** Paracetamol + Codeine + Omeprazole\n- **D.** Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin + Prednisolone\n- **E.** Metformin + Insulin + Gliclazide\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe 'Triple Whammy' causes synergistic renal hemodynamic collapse: (1) Diuretics reduce circulating intravascular volume (hypovolaemia); (2) NSAIDs block afferent arteriolar vasodilatory prostaglandins (afferent vasoconstriction); (3) ACE inhibitors/ARBs block angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriolar vasoconstriction (efferent vasodilation). Together, they destroy the transglomerular hydraulic pressure gradient, causing severe AKI.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat are the absolute emergency indications for initiating acute Renal Replacement Therapy (haemodialysis / hemofiltration) in a patient with severe AKI (mnemonic: AEIOU)?\n\n- **A.** Acidosis (refractory metabolic pH <7.15-7.20), Electrolytes (severe refractory hyperkalaemia >6.5 mmol/L with ECG changes), Ingestion/toxins (toxic alcohols, lithium, salicylates), Overload (refractory pulmonary oedema), Uraemic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy)\n- **B.** Isolated mild elevation of urea of 12 mmol/L with normal potassium\n- **C.** Mild asymptomatic hypernatraemia\n- **D.** Presence of trace proteinuria on dipstick\n- **E.** Stage 1 AKI responding to intravenous fluids\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nEmergency indications for dialysis in AKI (AEIOU): (A) Acidosis: severe metabolic acidosis (pH <7.15) refractory to medical management; (E) Electrolytes: severe refractory hyperkalaemia (>6.5 mmol/L) with ECG changes; (I) Ingestion: dialyzable intoxications (methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylates); (O) Overload: volume overload / pulmonary oedema refractory to diuretics; (U) Uraemia: uraemic pericarditis, encephalopathy, neuropathy, or bleeding.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nIn the differentiation of acute intrinsic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN) from Pre-Renal Azotemia, which urinary diagnostic parameters are classic for Pre-Renal Azotemia?\n\n- **A.** Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FENa) < 1.0%, Urine Sodium < 20 mmol/L, and high Urine Osmolality (>500 mOsm/kg)\n- **B.** FENa > 2.0%, Urine Sodium > 40 mmol/L, and isosthenuric Urine Osmolality (~300 mOsm/kg)\n- **C.** Presence of muddy brown granular casts in 100% of cases\n- **D.** Urine specific gravity < 1.005\n- **E.** FENa > 5.0%\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn pre-renal azotemia, tubular function is intact. The kidneys avidly reabsorb sodium and water to restore intravascular volume, resulting in: low urine sodium (<20 mmol/L), low fractional excretion of sodium (FENa <1.0%), and highly concentrated urine (osmolality >500 mOsm/kg, specific gravity >1.020). In ATN, tubular epithelial cells are damaged and cannot reabsorb sodium, so FENa >2% and urine sodium >40 mmol/L with muddy brown granular casts.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f2d743d8-9a6f-4faa-964a-248a2eec6ddc	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	antidiuretic-harmone	Antidiuretic Harmone	article	Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as vasopressin, is a\nsmall peptide hormone which regulates the body's retention\nof water. The synthesis of ADH occurs in the supraoptic and\nparaventricular nuclei in the hypothalamus. It is then\ntransported to the posterior pituitary gland via the\nneurohypophysial capillaries.\nADH regulates the blood pressure (v1 receptors) and serum\nosmolality (v2 receptor). The primary function is serum\nosmolality regulation (ADH decreases serum osmolality and\nincreases urine osmolality) via regulation of aquaporin\nchannel insertion in principal cell of renal collecting duct.\nRegulation of plasma osmolality\nMediated by V2 receptors.\nInsertion of aquaporin channels in the principal cells of the\nrenal collecting duct and DCT\nResults in increased water reabsorption\nRegulation of blood pressure\nMediated by V1 receptors.\nVasoconstrictive effects at higher levels\nIncrease reabsorption of urea in the collecting duct,\nincreases the corticomedullary gradient and facilitates urine\nconcentration.\nADH release is inhibited by atrial natriuretic peptide\n(ANP), which is released by stretched atria in response to\nincreases in blood pressure, as well as alcohol and certain\nmedications.\nRegulation of ADH secretion\n\nClinical relevance\nElevated in SIADH despite plasma hypoosmolality.\nADH deficiency or resistance can lead to diabetes insipidus.\nAlcohol inhibits ADH release and causes diuresis.\n\n![Antidiuretic Harmone - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/antidiuretic-harmone/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Antidiuretic Harmone — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
089c0713-3e08-47f4-acbf-4d19ad90bcec	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	adult-sepsis-septic-shock	Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six	article	# Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- people with any breach of skin integrity (for example, cuts, burns, blisters or skin infections)\n- people who misuse drugs intravenously\n- people with indwelling lines or catheters.\n- Sepsis risk stratification and assessment in people aged 18 or over (Nice guideline) or tongue) Behaviour Respiratory Heart rate Blood pressure Green (low risk) Normal No high risk or moderate to high risk None of or red signs Amber (moderate risk) from patient, friend or relative of new-onset altered behaviour or mental history Of acute deterioration Of functional ability\n- Trauma, surgery or invasive procedures in\n- 21—24 breaths per minute\n- 91—130 per minute for pregnant women, 100—130 beats per minute\n- New-onset arrhythmia Systolic blood pressure 91—100 mmHg Red (High risk) Pale. mottled. ashen. blue Objective evidence Of altered behaviour or mental state\n- all 25 breaths per minute or more\n- new need for 40% oxygen or more to maintain saturation more than 92% (or more than in known chronic Obstructive pulmonary disease)\n- more than 130 per minute\n- 90 mmHgor less OR more than 40 mmHg below normal Not passed urine in the past 12—18 hours, Not passed urine in previous 18 hours, or for catheterised patients passed 0.5—1 or for catheterised patients passed less ml/kg Of urine per hour than OS mukg of urine per hour Tympanic temperature less than WC Signs of potential infection:\n- Non-blanching rash\n- Redness\n- swelling or discharge at surgical site\n- Cyanosis Of Skin. lips or tongue\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nin out of hospital (healthcare community, school, workplace, aged 18 years and over —Green light: discharge with appropriate advice\n\n- —Amber light:\n- Can definitive condition diagnosed and treated in out of hospital setting ? yes! Then Treat definitive condition access medical care and/or provide information to safety net. not! Urgent referral to emergency care (which has resuscitation facility) —Red light: Urgent referral to emergency care (which has resuscitation facility)\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof patient with green and amber risk factors in hospital setting:pegple aged 18 years and over Low risk Suspected sepsis, no high or high to moderate risk criteria Manage according to clinical judgment Moderate risk 2 or more moderate to high risk criteria met OR systolic blood pressure Of 91- 100 mmHg send tests blood gas lactate blood culture full blood C-reactive protein urea and electrolytes creatinine Lactate over 2 mmol/L Manage as high risk Only I moderate to high risk criteria met Consider blood test in I hr and clinician review Lactate 2mmol-less, no AKI Can definitive condition be diagnosed and treated? Yes! Manage definitive condition. If appropriate, discharge With information definitive condition Sn\n- t diagnosed then consider antibiotic and senior decision maker review within 3 hrs\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof patient with High-risk factors in hospital setting:people aged 18 years and over\n- 1 or more high risk criteria met\n- Arrange immediate review by senior clinical decision maker (emergency care ST4 or above) Carry out venous blood tests for the following:\n- blood gas for glucose and lactate\n- blood culture\n- full blood count\n-\n- C-reactive protein\n- urea and electrolytes\n- . creatinine\n-\n- clotting screen Give intravenous antibiotics without delay (within a maximum of 1 hour) Discuss with consultant Lactate over 4 mmol/L Give IV fluid bolus 500ml without delay and discuss with critical care unit Lactate 2-4 mmol/L Give IV fluid bolus 500ml without delay Lactate< 2 mmol/L Consider IV fluid bolus 500m! without delay Continuous monitoring in emergency department Consultant review if patient condition isn't improved\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/adult-sepsis-septic-shock/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/adult-sepsis-septic-shock/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/adult-sepsis-septic-shock/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/adult-sepsis-septic-shock/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Adult Sepsis & Septic Shock — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
8358b46b-c290-47d3-94be-7c8374d40e1e	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block	Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block	article	# Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Left anterior fascicular block (LAFB) Left anterior/superiorfascicular block is due to to anatomical Of functional block in the anterior fascicle. Depolarization entirely depends on posterior fascicle. The initial vector will be directed inferiorly yieldinga small in inferior leads (II, Ill, aVF) and small q-wave lateral leads(aVL, l, aVR) The second vector which is considerably stronger be directed to the left, back and upwards. This result in deep S- wave in inferior leads and large R-wave in left lateral leads. Hence, inferior leadsrS complex and lateral leads shows qR complex (means small q-wave large-R wave). The electrical axis will be shifted to the left (left axis deviation) range between\n- 450 and\n- 900\n- Causes of LAFB Cardiomyopathy, hyperkalemia, hypertension, MI , LVF, myocarditits\n- 300 aVL AV node LAFB ECG Criteria Large-R wave LAFB The electrical axis will be shifted to the left (left axis deviation) range between\n- 450 and\n- 900. If the electrical axis is between\n- 300 to\n- 450 more likely to suggests LAFB QRS duration <0.12 seconds but slightly prolonged aVL show qR complex. V5-V6 usually also shows qR complexes. Left posterior fascicular block (LPFB) It's much less common Depolarization ofthe left ventricle depends entirely on impulses from the anterior fascicle if posterior one is defect. The vector initially directed upwards and to the left which yield q-wave in aVF and R-wave in lead in l. The second vector is directed downwards to the right, which results in prominent R- wave in lead aVF and equally prominentS-wave in lead l. The electrical axis will be more positive than 90' (right axis deviation). Causes of LAFB Cardiomyopathy, hyperkalemia, hypertension, MI , LVF, myocarditits\n- 30 aVL 2 LPFB\n- LPFB ECG Criteria Electrical axis +900 to +1800\n- rs complexes in lead I and aVL qR complexes in inferior leads (II, Ill and aVF). Q —wave is mandatoryin leads Ill and aVF.\n\nQHSdurauon<U.12 seconds Lead II, Ill, aVF displaysrS complexes.\n- ECG morphology of LAFB\n- qR complexes in lateral limb leads, and rs complexes in inferior leads\n- Axis deviation\n\n- Left Axis Deviation (LAD)\n- Leads I and aVL are POSITIVE;\n- Leads II, Ill and aVF are NEGATIVE\n- Example of LAFB\n\n- Left axis deviation\n- qR complexes in I, aVL\n- rScomplexes in II, Ill, aVF\n- Prolonged R wave peak time in aVL\n- Increased QRS voltage in limb leads\n- ECG morphology of LPFB\n\nVR 111\n- As above, there are rs complexes in leads I and aVL, and qR complexes in leads II, Ill and aVF\n- Axis deviation\n\n- 111 Right Axis Deviation (RAD) Leads II, Ill and aVF are POSITIVE; Leads I and aVL are NEGATIVE Example of LPFB\n\nVF previOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_07_p7.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_08_p8.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block/figure_09_p8.jpeg)\n*Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	24	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
0ac3fbe0-d54f-4f80-8f0f-e826da8b6d4e	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	pancreas	Pancreas	article	Pancreas\nThe pancreas is a retroperitoneal, abdominal organ found\nposterior and inferior to the stomach The pancreas has two\nessential and very important functions in the body:\nendocrine (production of hormones that regulate blood\nsugar levels and glandular secretion) and exocrine (the\nfunction of the digestive gland).\nEndocrine activity is performed by the Langerhans islets and\ninvolves the production of hormones. The Islets of\nLangerhans contain the following cell types, each of which\nproduce different endocrine hormone.\n\n![Pancreas - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/pancreas/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Pancreas — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
d3865481-2b6a-40c7-a84a-feecd409f5f6	467b25a7-fb2b-4ba8-ba4f-cad51944c5ea	poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes	Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach	article	# Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Pharmacology and poisoning\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nPoisoning and overdose\nIn toxicology, poisoning is defined as the state resulting from\nthe administration of excessive amounts of any\npharmaceutical agent.\nA poison may be a drug, household product, industrial\nchemical, or plant or animal derivative\nPoisoning may be accidental or deliberate:\nPoisoning is usually accidental in children aged under 6\nyears, and is most commonly due to exploration of their\nenvironment, where children place objects in their mouth\nand accidental ingestion occurs.\nDeliberate self-poisoning is the deliberate ingestion of more\nthan the prescribed amount of a medical substance, or of a\nsubstance not meant for human consumption, irrespective of\nthe motive for the episode.\nOverdose is a term used to describe the use of a quantity of\ndrug in excess of its intended or prescribed dose and is a\nmajor cause of poisoning. It may be accidental or deliberate\nand involve the use of prescribed or illicit drugs.\nTable - A: Drugs and clinical features\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:30 AM Poisoning, overdose and Toxidromes | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\nTable - B: Toxidromes\n/26, 10:30 AM Poisoning, overdose and Toxidromes | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:30 AM Poisoning, overdose and Toxidromes | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n\n## Clinical Figures, Diagnostic Algorithms & Radiographs\n\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Click any figure below to examine diagnostic algorithms, anatomy landmarks, or radiological details at full clinical resolution.\n\n\n\n![Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2)](/lessons/images/sba/pharmacology-and-poisoning/poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes/figure_01_p2.png)\n\n*Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach - Figure 1: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2) (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3)](/lessons/images/sba/pharmacology-and-poisoning/poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes/figure_02_p3.png)\n\n*Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach - Figure 2: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3) (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Clinical Toxidromes & Poisoning Approach**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
93c69afa-5297-4e97-aff0-95facb326f71	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-atrial-arrhythmias	Atrial Arrhythmias & Atrial Flutter — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Atrial Arrhythmias & Atrial Flutter — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 65-year-old woman presents with sudden palpitations. ECG demonstrates a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at exactly 150 bpm with a 'sawtooth' baseline pattern best visualized in leads II, III, and aVF. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Arrhythmias & Atrial Flutter)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-arrhythmias/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Atrial Flutter with 2:1 AV block\n- **B.** Sinus tachycardia\n- **C.** Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response\n- **D.** AV Nodal Re-entrant Tachycardia (AVNRT)\n- **E.** Ventricular tachycardia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nClassic (typical) atrial flutter arises from a cavotricuspid isthmus counter-clockwise macro-re-entrant circuit in the right atrium with an atrial rate of ~300 bpm. When conducted with 2:1 AV block, the ventricular rate is precisely 150 bpm with pathognomonic negative 'sawtooth' flutter waves in inferior leads.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nIn a patient presenting with regular narrow complex tachycardia at 150 bpm where flutter waves are obscured by T waves, what diagnostic and therapeutic pharmacological manoeuvre unmasks the underlying atrial activity?\n\n- **A.** Rapid IV Adenosine bolus (6mg followed by flush)\n- **B.** IV Atropine 500mcg\n- **C.** Oral Digoxin 250mcg\n- **D.** IV Adrenaline 1mg\n- **E.** IV Flecainide infusion over 2 hours\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAdenosine causes transient, high-grade AV nodal block. In atrial flutter, it temporarily blocks conduction through the AV node without terminating the atrial flutter circuit, revealing the underlying baseline flutter (sawtooth) waves and confirming the diagnosis.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 70-year-old male with atrial flutter is haemodynamically unstable with BP 78/45 mmHg, diaphoresis, and acute chest pain. What is the emergency treatment of choice?\n\n- **A.** Synchronised DC cardioversion (starting at 50-100J biphasic)\n- **B.** IV Amiodarone 300mg over 1 hour\n- **C.** Oral Bisoprolol 5mg\n- **D.** IV Verapamil 5mg\n- **E.** Carotid sinus massage alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAny tachyarrhythmia with adverse features (shock, syncope, myocardial ischaemia, or severe heart failure) requires immediate synchronised DC cardioversion under sedation/analgesia per Resuscitation Council UK guidelines. Atrial flutter responds exquisitely to low-energy cardioversion (50-100 J).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the definitive, curative catheter ablation target for typical counter-clockwise atrial flutter?\n\n- **A.** Cavotricuspid Isthmus (CTI) radiofrequency ablation\n- **B.** Pulmonary vein isolation (PVI)\n- **C.** Bundle of His ablation\n- **D.** Left atrial appendage occlusion\n- **E.** Sinoatrial node excision\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCavotricuspid isthmus (CTI) ablation creates a line of bidirectional block between the tricuspid valve annulus and the inferior vena cava, curing typical atrial flutter with a success rate exceeding 90-95%.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nRegarding systemic thromboembolism risk, how does stroke prevention in typical atrial flutter compare to atrial fibrillation?\n\n- **A.** The thromboembolic risk is identical; anticoagulation decisions follow the CHA2DS2-VASc score exactly as in AF\n- **B.** Atrial flutter carries zero risk of thrombus formation; anticoagulation is never indicated\n- **C.** Aspirin alone is sufficient for all flutter patients\n- **D.** Anticoagulation is only needed if the ventricular rate exceeds 180 bpm\n- **E.** Warfarin is mandatory; DOACs are ineffective in atrial flutter\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCurrent ESC and NICE guidelines state that the thromboembolic risk in atrial flutter is comparable to atrial fibrillation. Anticoagulation is recommended using the same criteria and CHA2DS2-VASc scoring thresholds as for atrial fibrillation.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3cf1bb4c-0089-4fe6-b64c-df71c5c66516	7d6ad555-2a31-4365-ad9b-d6abfb9b25c1	haemostasis	Haemostasis	article	Haemostasis\nPrevention of blood loss\nStages of haemostasis: Three stages\nVasoconstriction\nPlatelet plug formation.\nCoagulation of blood\n\n![Haemostasis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/haemostasis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Haemostasis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
43352cfb-e747-45f5-ae58-fd92b1c35058	bb0e5b2d-ecab-4654-8aba-13849df3c95c	anaphylaxis-management	Anaphylaxis Management	article	- Anaphylaxis\n>It's a severe, life-threatening, generalized or systemic\nhypersensitivity reaction, characterized by rapidly developing\nand life-threatening airway, breathing and/or circulation\nproblems, usually associated with skin and mucosal changes.\n- Causes\n- Medication:\n>Antibiotic (penicillin most common)\n\n## >Nsaid\n\n>Aspirin\n\n## >Acei\n\n>Anesthetic agent e.g.: muscle relaxant (Suxamethonium and\nrocuronium) and contrast media\n- Others\n>Food: e.g.: peanut, egg seafood (most common in children).\n>Insect sting\n\n![Anaphylaxis Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/anaphylaxis-management/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anaphylaxis Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n>latex\n>idiopathic\n>C1 esterase etc.\n- Management\n- Key points\n- Anaphylactic likely when following 3 criteria match:\n> 1: Acute onset of illness with a sudden progression.\n> 2: Life-threatening airway and/or breathing and/or circulation\nproblem.\n> 3: Possible skin or mucosal changes (although these may be\nabsent and are not required for diagnosis).\n>there is no role of antihistamine however it may\nhelp alleviate cutaneous symptoms.\n>The routine use of corticosteroids to treat anaphylaxis is not\nadvised. If the patient is suffering from ongoing asthma/shock\nafter initial resuscitation steroids may be considered.\n>Use glucagon in patients who are taking beta blockers may\nbe resistant to the therapeutic effects of adrenaline.\n>Adrenaline : can give second dose if reaction persist after 5\nminutes interval. After 2 doses, adrenaline infusion should be\nstarted.\n\n![Anaphylaxis Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/anaphylaxis-management/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Anaphylaxis Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n>Nice recommend: consider fast-track discharge after 2 hours\nobservation from resolution of anaphylaxis if : Good response\n(within 5-10 minutes) to a singles dose of adrenaline given\nwithin 30 minutes of onset of reaction and complete resolution\nof symptoms and Pt already has unused adrenaline auto-\ninjectors and has been trained how to use them.\n>6 hours observation: those pt who has 2 doses of IM\nadrenaline or previous biphasic reaction.\n>12 hours observation: those pt who has required more than\ntwo doses of adrenaline, has severe asthma or their reaction\nhas involved severe respiratory compromise.\n>The frequency of food sensitization in asthmatic children is\nhigher than expected in a general population. it has been\nfound that asthma is a risk factor for severe or fatal\nanaphylactic reactions to foods\n>Investigation : resus council recommend 3 tryptase level. First\nas resuscitation started, 2nd 1-2 hours after symptoms started.\n24 hours later.\n- Anaphylactoid vs anaphylaxis:\n>Anaphylactoid reaction: first time exposure to allergen, direct\nrelease of histamine and other mediators from mast cells and\ndoesn't cause degranulation. Non-immune (IgE) mediated\nreaction.\n>Anaphylaxis reaction: On further exposure to that allergen,\nthe responsible allergenic antigen binds to an antigen-specific\nIgE antibody, leading to mast cell degranulation Histamine and\nother mediators, including leukotrienes and cytokines are\nreleased from mast cells and basophils following exposure to\nthis antigen.\n>Summary: anaphylactoid first exposure and non-immune\nmediated\n>Anaphylaxis: further exposure/ Pt has exposed to allergen\npreviously (history) and immune mediated.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
296ed3c2-d251-4a40-818e-5b9787fc950a	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-atrial-fibrillation	Atrial Fibrillation Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Atrial Fibrillation Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 71-year-old man presents with palpitations and breathlessness. ECG confirms atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 135 bpm. BP is 128/82 mmHg. He has a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and had an ischaemic stroke 2 years ago. What is his CHA2DS2-VASc score and anticoagulation indication?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Fibrillation Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-fibrillation/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Score 5; anticoagulation with a DOAC is strongly indicated\n- **B.** Score 2; prescribe aspirin monotherapy\n- **C.** Score 1; no anticoagulation required\n- **D.** Score 3; clopidogrel is preferred\n- **E.** Score 0; low risk\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCHA2DS2-VASc: Congestive HF (0), Hypertension (1), Age >=75 (0, he is 71), Diabetes (1), Stroke/TIA (2), Vascular disease (0), Age 65-74 (1), Sex category female (0). Total = 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 5. A score >=2 in men strongly mandates oral anticoagulation (DOAC first-line per NICE).\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Atrial Fibrillation Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-fibrillation/figure_06_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 66-year-old woman with new-onset AF of 6 hours duration is haemodynamically stable. She has no structural heart disease, normal renal function, and normal baseline ECG. Which pharmacological agent is recommended for chemical cardioversion?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Fibrillation Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-fibrillation/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** IV Flecainide 2mg/kg (max 150mg) over 30 minutes (or IV Ibutilide)\n- **B.** IV Digoxin 500mcg\n- **C.** IV Adenosine 12mg\n- **D.** Oral Warfarin 10mg single dose\n- **E.** IV Metoprolol 5mg bolus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor acute chemical cardioversion of AF (<48 hours) in patients WITHOUT structural or ischemic heart disease, Class 1c antiarrhythmics (IV Flecainide) or IV Vernakalant/Ibutilide are first-line. In patients with known coronary artery disease or heart failure, IV Amiodarone is the agent of choice.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 75-year-old woman with known permanent AF presents with fatigue and HR 130 bpm. Echo shows severe Left Ventricular Systolic Dysfunction with an ejection fraction (LVEF) of 28%. Which rate-control medication is CONTRAINDICATED?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Fibrillation Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-fibrillation/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** IV Verapamil (or Diltiazem)\n- **B.** Oral Digoxin\n- **C.** Oral Bisoprolol low dose\n- **D.** Oral Carvedilol\n- **E.** IV Amiodarone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNon-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (Verapamil, Diltiazem) have potent negative inotropic properties and can precipitate acute decompensated heart failure and cardiogenic shock in patients with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF, LVEF <40%). Beta-blockers and/or digoxin are indicated.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nIn a patient presenting with AF of unknown duration (or >48 hours), what is the minimum duration of therapeutic anticoagulation required before and after elective electrical cardioversion to minimize thromboembolic risk?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Fibrillation Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-fibrillation/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** At least 3 weeks before and at least 4 weeks after cardioversion\n- **B.** 24 hours before and 24 hours after\n- **C.** 6 months before and indefinitely after\n- **D.** No pre-treatment needed if aspirin is given\n- **E.** 1 week before and 1 week after\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIf AF duration is >48 hours or unknown, elective cardioversion requires either: (1) at least 3 weeks of therapeutic oral anticoagulation before cardioversion, followed by at least 4 weeks post-procedure; or (2) transoesophageal echocardiography (TOE) to exclude left atrial thrombus prior to cardioversion.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old male with known Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome presents in rapid, irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with rates varying from 200 to 280 bpm (pre-excited AF). He is conscious with BP 115/70. Which class of drugs is strictly CONTRAINDICATED?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Fibrillation Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-fibrillation/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** AV-nodal blocking agents (Adenosine, Digoxin, Verapamil, Beta-blockers)\n- **B.** Procainamide\n- **C.** Ibutilide\n- **D.** Electrical DC cardioversion\n- **E.** Oxygen therapy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn pre-excited AF (AF with WPW), conduction travels down both the AV node and the accessory pathway. Drugs that selectively block the AV node (adenosine, verapamil, digoxin, beta-blockers) divert all impulses down the accessory pathway without physiological decrement, precipitating ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest. IV Procainamide or DC cardioversion is the treatment of choice.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a85b88ac-bec9-498f-917e-4249da4c9953	bb0e5b2d-ecab-4654-8aba-13849df3c95c	corticosteroids	Corticosteroids	article	Corticosteroids:\nCorticosteroids are a class of steroid hormones that are\nnaturally produced in the adrenal cortex as well\nas administered as medications for therapeutic purposes.\nWe have already discussed naturally produced corticosteroids\nin physiology. Now we will discuss in detail pharmacology of\nadministrated steroids.\nNaturally occurring Classes of corticosteroids: Glucocorticoids\nand mineralocorticoids, are involved in a wide range of\nphysiological processes, including stress response,\nimmune response, and regulation of inflammation.\nClassification of administrated steroids:\nInhaled steroids: (beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone)\nUses: Prophylaxis in chronic asthma if: Exacerbations in last\n2 years requiring systemic corticosteroid or nebulized\nbronchodilator or B-agonists used more than twice per\nweek or Symptoms disturb sleep more than twice per week.\nHay fever (Nasal spray)\n\n## Copd\n\nSide effects: Rare which include Oral candidiasis, hoarseness\nand sore mouth.\nNote:  In high doses systemic adverse effects can occur which\ninclude Adrenal suppression, Osteoporosis and Glaucoma.\nOral and injectable corticosteroids:\nOral and intravenous steroids: (prednisolone, dexamethasone,\nhydrocortisone).\nSpecific uses:\n\nPrednisolone: Has mainly glucocorticoid action 4 times more\npotent than hydrocortisone primary used for disease\nsuppression in autoimmune/Rheumatoid conditions, Temporal\narteritis, Asthma and acute adrenal insufficiency.\nHydrocortisone: Short acting primarily known for its\nglucocorticoid activity, but it also possesses mineralocorticoid\nactivity. Because of mineralocorticoid effect result into fluid\nretention means unsuitable for long-term treatment for\ndisease suppression but is used long-term with fludrocortisone\nin adrenal suppression.\nUses:\nAdrenal insufficiency\nAddison's disease\nAcute allergy\nAsthma\nShock\nAngioedema\nHypersensitivity reactions\nSevere inflammatory bowel disease\nRheumatic disease\nDexamethasone: Long acting primarily known for its very high\nglucocorticoid activity and insignificant mineralocorticoid\nactivity.\nUses:\nCerebral oedema\nPerioperatively\nInflammatory disorders\nCroup\nIntractable nausea\nDiagnosis of Cushing's syndrome (Overnight Dexamethasone\nsuppression test)\nTemporal arteritis\nSide effects\nPatients on long-term steroid use are at risk of the following:\n\nAdrenal suppression: In long-term use, e.g., prednisolone\n>7.5mg a day →  adrenal atrophy due to corticotrophin\nsuppression, therefore, withdraw steroid slowly to prevent\nAddisonian crisis\nSteroids should be doubled in times of stress/illness (unless on\nhigh dose) because the body cannot respond to increased\nrequirements, to prevent Addisonian crisis .\nInfections: Due to the suppression of the inflammatory\nresponse patients are more susceptible to infection.\nReactivation of dormant infections, e.g., TB, may occur.\nComplications:\nPatients who are not immune to chicken pox (not previously\ninfected) are at risk of severe chicken pox, with complications\nsuch as encephalitis, pneumonia, and hepatitis. Varicella\nzoster immunoglobulin is required for postexposure\nprophylaxis; this provides passive immunity.\nPatients should also be advised to avoid exposure to measles\nand should receive postexposure immunoglobulin within 72\nhours if they are exposed.\nAll patients on long-term steroids should carry a 'steroid card'\nto inform medical practitioners that they are on steroids or\nhave been on steroids (for up to a year as adrenal suppression\ncan be prolonged).	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
e748adda-0063-4262-b3f3-53f18f2b3f43	1f7633e6-613c-4343-9757-091175bffbbe	digital-nerve-block	Digital Nerve Block Technique & Anaesthesia	article	# Digital Nerve Block Technique & Anaesthesia\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Pain & Sedation\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\nDigital Nerve Block\n> Digital nerve block is used frequently for simple procedures\non the fingers and toes.\n> Indication:\n> Nailbed injuries\n> Finger/toe laceration\n> Nail avulsion\n> Foreign bodies in digit\n> Digit dislocation and fracture\n> Contraindications;\n> Allergic to LA\n> Compromised digital circulation or infection\n> Drug and dose:\n> Plain lidocaine 1% is often used, but bupivacaine 0.5% plain is\npreferable.\n> Use 1-2mL of LA on each side of the finger, thumb, or big toe.\nUse smaller volumes in the other toes or in children.\n> Avoid to use adrenaline or any other vasoconstrictor\n> Excessive pain or paraesthesia suggest needle is against or in\na nerve,therefore, needle should be removed immediately\n> Aspirate before injection to avoid injection into blood vessels.\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:27 AM Digital Nerve Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\n> Each digit is innervated by four digital nerves,therefore, It Is\nnecessary to block all fours nerves to achieve complete\nanaesthesia (a ring block).\n> Procedure:\n> Place the hand or toe on sterile surface\n> Hold the syringe containing 1% lignocaine or bupivacaine\n0.5% perpendicular to the digit and insert the needle into\nsubcutaneous tissue of the digital webspace.\n> Aspirate to make sure needle isn't in vessels then slowly inject\n2ml of anaesthetic into subcutaneous tissue and infiltrate,\nsurrounding the nerve as you withdraw.\n> Repeat same procedure on the opposite side of digit.\n> Anaesthesia will develop after 5 minutes\n> Complications: infection, bleeding, distal paresthesia from\nnerve injury, distal infarction from vasospasm.\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:27 AM Digital Nerve Block | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Digital Nerve Block Technique & Anaesthesia**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
1188d8fb-0158-4c53-a40e-f3b101c45119	7ac849e4-6b82-4bf3-b8f0-7f1abbc39105	drugs-toxicity-and-antidotes	Drugs Toxicity and Antidotes	article	![Drugs Toxicity and Antidotes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/drugs-toxicity-and-antidotes/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Drugs Toxicity and Antidotes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
a3b03d97-41aa-4096-b768-4d9d0eed432c	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-atrial-septal-defect	Atrial Septal Defect (ASD) — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Atrial Septal Defect (ASD) — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 34-year-old woman is evaluated for exertional dyspnoea. Cardiac auscultation reveals an ejection systolic murmur loudest at the upper left sternal border and fixed, wide splitting of the second heart sound (S2) during both inspiration and expiration. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Septal Defect (ASD))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-septal-defect/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Ostium secundum Atrial Septal Defect (ASD)\n- **B.** Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD)\n- **C.** Coarctation of the aorta\n- **D.** Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy\n- **E.** Aortic stenosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFixed wide splitting of S2 is the classic hallmark of Atrial Septal Defect (ASD). The left-to-right shunt increases right ventricular volume and ejection time, delaying pulmonic valve closure equally in both inspiration and expiration. The systolic murmur is due to increased flow across the normal pulmonary valve.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich anatomical subtype accounts for approximately 75-80% of all congenital Atrial Septal Defects in adults?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Septal Defect (ASD))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-septal-defect/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Ostium secundum defect (in the region of the fossa ovalis)\n- **B.** Ostium primum defect\n- **C.** Sinus venosus defect\n- **D.** Coronary sinus defect\n- **E.** Patent ductus arteriosus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOstium secundum ASD is by far the most common type (75-80%), resulting from excessive resorption of the septum primum or deficient growth of the septum secundum. Ostium primum defects (15%) are associated with Down syndrome and endocardial cushion defects.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic 12-lead ECG finding associated with an Ostium Secundum ASD?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Atrial Septal Defect (ASD))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/atrial-septal-defect/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Right axis deviation with incomplete Right Bundle Branch Block (rSR' pattern in V1)\n- **B.** Left axis deviation with Left Bundle Branch Block\n- **C.** Delta waves and short PR interval\n- **D.** Deep Q waves in leads II, III, aVF\n- **E.** ST elevation in V1-V3\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOstium secundum ASD characteristically produces right axis deviation and incomplete right bundle branch block (rSR' pattern in V1) reflecting right ventricular volume overload. Conversely, ostium primum ASD classically demonstrates left axis deviation.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 42-year-old patient with an untreated large ASD develops progressive cyanosis, clubbing, and severe pulmonary arterial hypertension. Bedside echo reveals reversal of the intracardiac shunt to right-to-left. What is this clinical entity known as?\n\n- **A.** Eisenmenger's syndrome\n- **B.** Ebstein's anomaly\n- **C.** Tetralogy of Fallot\n- **D.** Cor pulmonale from COPD\n- **E.** Lutembacher's syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nEisenmenger's syndrome is the development of severe irreversible pulmonary vascular disease and pulmonary arterial hypertension secondary to a longstanding systemic-to-pulmonary (left-to-right) shunt, causing shunt reversal (right-to-left), cyanosis, and polycythaemia.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 25-year-old scuba diver with an unclosed Patent Foramen Ovale (PFO) / small ASD develops a sudden focal neurological deficit (stroke) after deep-vein thrombosis. What mechanism explains this phenomenon?\n\n- **A.** Paradoxical embolism\n- **B.** Atherosclerotic plaque rupture\n- **C.** Bacterial endocarditis\n- **D.** Carotid artery dissection\n- **E.** Fat embolism syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nParadoxical embolism occurs when a venous thrombus (e.g. from DVT) crosses an intracardiac right-to-left communication (PFO or ASD) into the systemic arterial circulation, bypassing pulmonary filtration and causing embolic stroke or peripheral arterial occlusion.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
6d9c4aa6-cbfc-4ea5-b811-670567327dfe	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-bradycardia	Emergency Bradycardia Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Emergency Bradycardia Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 74-year-old male presents with profound light-headedness. His pulse is 32 bpm and regular. BP is 80/48 mmHg. The ECG shows complete dissociation between P waves (rate 75 bpm) and wide QRS complexes (rate 32 bpm). According to the Resuscitation Council UK algorithm, what is the initial first-line drug therapy?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Bradycardia Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bradycardia/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** IV Atropine 500 micrograms\n- **B.** IV Adrenaline 1mg rapid bolus\n- **C.** IV Amiodarone 300mg\n- **D.** IV Metoprolol 5mg\n- **E.** IV Flecainide 150mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor any bradycardia with adverse features (shock, syncope, myocardial ischaemia, or heart failure), IV Atropine 500 mcg is the first-line medication, repeatable up to a maximum total of 3 mg (6 doses). If ineffective, use pacing or inotropes (isoprenaline/adrenaline).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich of the following is considered an 'Adverse Feature' in the RCUK Bradycardia algorithm that mandates immediate emergency active intervention?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Emergency Bradycardia Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bradycardia/figure_02_p10.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Shock (systolic BP <90 mmHg, pallor, diaphoresis) or Syncope\n- **B.** PR interval of 220 ms with normal BP\n- **C.** Resting heart rate of 52 bpm in a trained athlete\n- **D.** Incomplete RBBB on ECG\n- **E.** Serum potassium of 4.2 mmol/L\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe 4 life-threatening adverse features in bradycardia are: (1) Shock (systolic BP <90 mmHg, oliguria, cold peripheries); (2) Syncope; (3) Myocardial ischaemia; (4) Severe heart failure (pulmonary oedema). Their presence dictates immediate atropine and pacing.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhy should Atropine NOT be administered in doses less than 500 micrograms in adult patients?\n\n- **A.** Paradoxical bradycardia may occur due to central vagal stimulation\n- **B.** It induces immediate renal failure\n- **C.** It causes profound hypokalaemia\n- **D.** It causes severe metabolic acidosis\n- **E.** It precipitates immediate ventricular fibrillation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSubtherapeutic doses of atropine (<500 mcg) can paradoxically worsen bradycardia through central vagomimetic effects and presynaptic muscarinic M1 receptor blockade before complete postsynaptic M2 blockade is achieved.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nIn which type of second-degree AV block is the PR interval constant prior to a non-conducted P wave, originating below the AV node and carrying high risk of progression to asystole?\n\n- **A.** Mobitz Type II AV block\n- **B.** Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) AV block\n- **C.** First-degree AV block\n- **D.** Sinus arrhythmia\n- **E.** Wandering atrial pacemaker\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMobitz Type II AV block has fixed PR intervals with randomly dropped QRS complexes. It occurs infranodally (His-Purkinje system), is often irreversible, frequently progresses to complete heart block/asystole, and is an absolute indication for pacing.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA patient with complete heart block following an anterior myocardial infarction is completely refractory to 3mg total atropine. Transcutaneous pacing capture is intermittent. Which intravenous infusion is recommended as a second-line pharmacological bridge to transvenous pacing?\n\n- **A.** IV Isoprenaline infusion (5 mcg/min) or Adrenaline infusion (2-10 mcg/min)\n- **B.** IV Adenosine infusion\n- **C.** IV Labetalol infusion\n- **D.** IV Salbutamol nebuliser alone\n- **E.** IV Furosemide infusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nWhen atropine is ineffective or pacing is unavailable/failing, second-line inotropic/chronotropic infusions are indicated: Adrenaline (2-10 mcg/min), Isoprenaline (5 mcg/min), or Dopamine (2-10 mcg/kg/min) to maintain heart rate and perfusion.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
eb8300ad-fe96-4dac-89d5-8e67e546c2ad	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema	Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema	article	# Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nHF define clinically as a syndrome in which patient has sign and symptoms resulting\n- from abnormality of cardiac structure or function. Acute HF refers to rapid onset worsening of the symptoms and sign of the HF, requiring urgent evaluation and treatment.\n- Causes Cardiac disease\n- Coronary artery disease (most common)\n-\n- Hypertension Cardiomyopathy\n- Valvular HD\n- Congenital HD\n- Pericardial D\n- Others\n- High-cardiacou.tputstate=Thyrotoxicosis, Anaemia, Pheochromocytoma, Liver failure, Septecaemia\n- Drugs (cocaine, alcohol NSAID, BB, CCB)\n- Clinical Approach\n- Distinguishing CPO from other pathologies\n\nSymptom SOB Cough Chest pain Palpitations Sweating Acuity Nocturnal cpo Pink frothy sputum COPD Dry cough Pneumonia Green sputum Precipitating Cause e.g. ACS Haemoptysis\n\nSign Heart rate Blood pressure Temperature 02 saturations Chest signs Skin Heart sounds CPO Bilateral crepitati ons Cold, pale and clammy COPD Diffuse wheeze Dry and warm Murmurs, 3rd/4th Normal heart sounds Pneumonia Focal crepitations Flushed and warm Normal Clear Dry Normal\n- Key points CPO: symptom: Breathlessness( orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea\n- Coughing(Pink frothy sputum), edema, lightheadedness(syncope)\n- Sign: tachycardia, tachypnoea(basal crepitation, wheeze, plural effusion), hypertension, raised jvp, heart murmur, ascites and poor peripheral circulation.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG: it may reveal precipitating causes such as ST segment changes associated with ACS or arrhythmia\n- CXR features Upper lobe diversion\n- Alveolar edema\n- Pleural effusion\n\n- Cardiomegaly\n- ABC: (Type-I failure if with COPD then type-2)\n- LFTs, Troponin, TSH).\n- dysfunction, tamponed, ventricular rupture) ERECT\n\nTypical CPO ABG on air pH pC02 p02 Sats HC03 BXS Lactate\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- 7.29 4.92 6.80 84% 16\n- 5 3.2 mmol/L The priorities are to relieve symptoms and to restore haemodynamic stability and tissue perfusion. Follow ABCDE approach\n- Airway & Breathing\n- If the airway is compromised intervene as required\n- BTS suggest maintain oxygen 94-98% in acute HF, if HF coexist with COPD then 88- 92%\n- Start with NIV on high flow oxygen 15L/min\n- Diuretics (furosemide)\n- Nitrates (initially sublingual then IV infusion 10-20mcg/min)\n- Nitroprusside (the ESC suggests commencing at the rate of 0.3-0.5 mcg/min). Its Cl if SBP<90mmHg\n- Inotropes ( dobutamine as a first-line agent).\n\nDisability;lV opiate for chest pain Exposure: Position the patient sitting upright supported with pillows as appropriate for their conscious level. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/cardiogenic-pulmonary-oedema/figure_07_p7.jpeg)\n*Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 7 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
8cce78b1-a1ed-4be6-bf27-4fbcf871bd28	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema	Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 68-year-old woman with a history of ischaemic heart disease presents with severe orthopnoea and pink frothy sputum. HR 122 bpm, BP 184/102 mmHg, RR 34/min, SpO2 84% on room air. Auscultation reveals extensive bilateral pulmonary crackles and wheezes. What is the immediate non-invasive respiratory support of choice?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) at 5-10 cm H2O with high-flow oxygen\n- **B.** Immediate endotracheal intubation and paralysis\n- **C.** Low-flow nasal cannula at 2 L/min\n- **D.** Venturi mask 24% oxygen\n- **E.** Incentive spirometry\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCPAP (or BiPAP) is the first-line respiratory support for acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. CPAP recruits collapsed alveoli, increases functional residual capacity, reduces work of breathing, and decreases both LV preload and afterload, dramatically reducing intubation and mortality rates.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema/figure_06_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nIn acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema with preserved or elevated blood pressure (systolic BP >110 mmHg), which vasodilator reduces cardiac preload and afterload most rapidly and safely?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Sublingual or IV Glyceryl Trinitrate (GTN)\n- **B.** IV Metoprolol\n- **C.** Oral Amlodipine\n- **D.** IV Hydralazine bolus\n- **E.** IV Milrinone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNitrates (sublingual GTN spray 400-800 mcg followed by IV infusion titrated to BP) are the cornerstone of medical therapy in hypertensive cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. They induce venodilatation (reducing preload and LV filling pressure) and arterial dilatation (reducing LV afterload).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended initial intravenous dose of Furosemide in a patient presenting with acute decompensated heart failure who is already taking 40mg oral furosemide daily at home?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 40-80 mg IV bolus (equivalent to 1-2 times their daily oral dose)\n- **B.** 10 mg IV bolus\n- **C.** 250 mg IV bolus immediately\n- **D.** Oral spironolactone 25mg alone\n- **E.** Loop diuretics are contraindicated in pulmonary oedema\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE and ESC guidelines recommend giving an initial IV bolus of furosemide equal to 1-2 times the patient's existing total daily oral dose. For loop diuretic-naive patients, 20-40 mg IV is typically sufficient.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhy has the routine use of intravenous opiates (such as Diamorphine or Morphine) fallen out of favour in modern management of acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** They are associated with increased rates of respiratory depression, ICU admission, mechanical ventilation, and excess mortality\n- **B.** They cause severe hyperkalaemia\n- **C.** They induce acute hypertensive crisis\n- **D.** They cause intractable pulmonary fibrosis\n- **E.** They neutralize the effect of loop diuretics\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLarge registry data and meta-analyses show routine morphine use in acute pulmonary oedema is associated with sedation, hypoventilation, hypercapnia, increased invasive mechanical ventilation, and higher in-hospital mortality. Opiates are now reserved strictly for severe intractable distress or palliative care.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 59-year-old male with acute anterior STEMI presents in cardiogenic shock: cold extremities, oliguria, confusion, BP 76/44 mmHg, and severe pulmonary oedema. What inotrope/vasopressor strategy is recommended by ESC guidelines?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Cardiogenic Pulmonary Oedema)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/cardiogenic-pulmonary-edema/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Noradrenaline infusion to restore coronary perfusion pressure, plus Dobutamine if cardiogenic inotropy is needed\n- **B.** Aggressive 3-litre 0.9% normal saline fluid challenge\n- **C.** High-dose IV GTN infusion\n- **D.** Oral beta-blockers immediately\n- **E.** Verapamil infusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn cardiogenic shock with severe hypotension, Noradrenaline is preferred over dopamine to restore MAP and coronary perfusion with lower arrhythmia risk. Dobutamine is added to augment inotropy. Reperfusion (emergency primary PCI) is the definitive life-saving therapy.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a5968611-33ac-40ad-93fe-ad72454e2c22	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-left-right-bundle-branch-block	Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB) — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nWhich set of ECG features defines complete Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** QRS duration >=120 ms, broad notched or slurred R waves in lateral leads (I, aVL, V5-V6), absence of Q waves in lateral leads, and deep QS complex in V1\n- **B.** QRS duration <100 ms with tall R wave in V1\n- **C.** rSR' pattern in lead V1 ('rabbit ears') with broad S wave in lead I\n- **D.** PR interval >200 ms with delta waves\n- **E.** Extreme right axis deviation with QRS duration 90 ms\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nComplete LBBB criteria: (1) QRS >=120 ms; (2) Broad, notched or monophasic R wave in lateral leads (I, aVL, V5, V6); (3) Absence of Q waves in leads I, V5, V6; (4) Deep QS or rS pattern in lead V1; (5) ST-T wave discordance (opposite to main QRS deflection).\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich clinical criteria are validated to diagnose acute myocardial infarction in the presence of pre-existing Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Modified Sgarbossa Criteria\n- **B.** Wellens Criteria\n- **C.** Centor Criteria\n- **D.** GCS scoring\n- **E.** San Francisco Syncope Rule\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe Sgarbossa (and Smith-modified Sgarbossa) criteria diagnose acute STEMI in the presence of LBBB or ventricular paced rhythm: (1) Concordant ST elevation >=1 mm in leads with positive QRS (5 points); (2) Concordant ST depression >=1 mm in V1, V2, or V3 (3 points); (3) Disproportionate discordant ST elevation >=1 mm with ST/S ratio <= -0.25 (Smith modification).\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_07_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 12-lead ECG demonstrates QRS duration of 135 ms, a classic rSR' pattern ('M-shaped' rabbit ears) in Lead V1, and wide slurred S waves in Leads I and V6. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Complete Right Bundle Branch Block (RBBB)\n- **B.** Complete Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)\n- **C.** Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome\n- **D.** Brugada syndrome Type 1\n- **E.** Left ventricular hypertrophy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nComplete RBBB criteria: (1) QRS >=120 ms; (2) rsR', rSR', or M-shaped notched QRS complex in V1-V2; (3) Wide, slurred S wave in lateral leads (I, aVL, V5-V6). In RBBB, normal initial septal activation is preserved (from left to right), so Q waves in MI can still be reliably interpreted.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old healthy male has an ECG performed for a sports medical showing an rSr' complex in Lead V1, normal axis, normal intervals, and a QRS duration of 105 ms. What is the correct interpretation?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Incomplete Right Bundle Branch Block (IRBBB), a common normal physiological variant in young healthy individuals\n- **B.** Severe cardiomyopathy requiring immediate implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD)\n- **C.** High-risk Brugada syndrome requiring sports cessation\n- **D.** Complete heart block\n- **E.** Acute anterior myocardial infarction\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIncomplete RBBB (QRS duration 100-119 ms with RBBB morphology in V1) is a common, benign physiological finding in up to 5-10% of healthy young individuals and athletes due to physiological RV enlargement, with excellent prognosis.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhy does pre-existing Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB) obscure the classic ECG diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction on standard interpretation?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bundle Branch Blocks (LBBB & RBBB))](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/left-right-bundle-branch-block/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Altered septal activation eliminates normal physiological Q waves, and baseline secondary ST-T wave discordance mimics or masks ischaemic ST shifts\n- **B.** LBBB completely stops blood flow to coronary arteries\n- **C.** LBBB causes electrical silence in leads V1 to V6\n- **D.** Troponin is never released in patients with LBBB\n- **E.** The heart rate in LBBB is always faster than 200 bpm\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn LBBB, the septum is depolarised abnormally from right to left (reversing normal septal vectors), obliterating septal Q waves. Furthermore, LBBB induces marked baseline secondary repolarisation abnormalities (discordant ST elevation in right precordial leads and ST depression in lateral leads) that mask or mimic acute ischaemia.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	27	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
fcc62808-61d9-4b8d-aa86-5895d8147e49	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-hyper-hypokalemia	Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 66-year-old male with end-stage renal disease misses two dialysis sessions. He presents with generalized muscle weakness. Serum potassium is 7.4 mmol/L. What is the chronological progression of ECG changes as hyperkalaemia worsens from mild to life-threatening?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyper-hypokalemia/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Tall peaked (tented) T waves -> PR interval prolongation and flattening of P waves -> QRS widening -> Sine-wave pattern -> Ventricular fibrillation / Asystole\n- **B.** ST elevation in all leads -> T wave inversion -> Normalisation\n- **C.** Prolonged QT interval -> U waves -> Torsades de pointes\n- **D.** Delta waves -> Short PR interval -> Atrial flutter\n- **E.** Biphasic T waves in V2-V3 only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nProgression of hyperkalaemic ECG changes: (1) Tall, narrow, symmetrical peaked 'tented' T waves (K+ >5.5); (2) PR prolongation, flattening/loss of P waves, and bradycardia (K+ >6.5); (3) Progressive QRS widening and bundle branch block patterns (K+ >7.0); (4) Merging of wide QRS with T wave forming a biphasic 'Sine Wave' pattern (K+ >8.0); (5) Ventricular fibrillation or asystole.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyper-hypokalemia/figure_06_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most critical, immediate first step in the emergency pharmacological management of severe hyperkalaemia (K+ >6.5 mmol/L or any hyperkalaemic ECG change)?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyper-hypokalemia/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Calcium (10ml of 10% Calcium Chloride or 30ml of 10% Calcium Gluconate over 5-10 minutes) to stabilize myocardial membrane excitability\n- **B.** Intravenous Insulin and Dextrose\n- **C.** Nebulised Salbutamol 10-20mg\n- **D.** Oral Calcium Resonium resin\n- **E.** Intravenous Furosemide 40mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIV Calcium is the essential FIRST intervention. Calcium does NOT lower serum potassium; it directly antagonizes the cardiotoxic effects of hyperkalaemia on the cardiac cell membrane by raising the threshold potential, restoring normal resting membrane excitability within 1-3 minutes. Note: 10ml of 10% Calcium Chloride contains three times more elemental calcium than 10ml of 10% Calcium Gluconate.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat standard intravenous regimen is administered to shift potassium rapidly from the extracellular space into the intracellular space?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyper-hypokalemia/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 10 units of soluble Insulin (e.g. Actrapid) in 50ml of 50% Dextrose (or 100ml of 20% Dextrose) infused over 15-30 minutes\n- **B.** 50 units of subcutaneous long-acting insulin alone\n- **C.** 500ml of 5% Dextrose without insulin\n- **D.** Intravenous Potassium Chloride 40 mmol\n- **E.** Intravenous Digoxin 500mcg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nInsulin stimulates the Na+/K+-ATPase pump in skeletal muscle and liver, driving potassium into cells. Standard dose: 10 units of rapid-acting soluble insulin in 50 ml of 50% dextrose (or 100-125 ml 20% dextrose) IV over 15-30 minutes. It lowers potassium by 0.5-1.0 mmol/L within 15-30 minutes, lasting 4-6 hours. Blood glucose must be monitored closely for hypoglycemia.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat are the classic 12-lead ECG manifestations of acute severe Hypokalaemia (K+ < 2.5 mmol/L)?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyper-hypokalemia/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** ST depression, flattened or inverted T waves, prominent U waves, and pseudo-prolongation of the QT interval (prolonged QU interval)\n- **B.** Tall peaked tented T waves with narrow QRS\n- **C.** Shortened PR interval with delta waves\n- **D.** Widespread concave ST elevation\n- **E.** Sine wave appearance\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nECG signs of hypokalaemia: (1) Flattened, decreased amplitude, or inverted T waves; (2) ST-segment depression; (3) Prominent U waves (positive deflection following T wave, best seen in V2-V4); (4) Apparent prolongation of the QT interval due to fusion of T and U waves (prolonged QU interval); (5) Predisposition to atrial and ventricular tachyarrhythmias (Torsades de pointes).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhy must serum Magnesium be checked and concurrently corrected when treating refractory Hypokalaemia?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyper-hypokalemia/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Magnesium is a critical cofactor that maintains Na+/K+-ATPase pump activity and closes renal outer medullary potassium (ROMK) channels; hypomagnesaemia promotes ongoing, uninhibited renal potassium wasting\n- **B.** Magnesium converts potassium into calcium in the liver\n- **C.** Hypomagnesaemia only affects the nervous system\n- **D.** Potassium cannot be absorbed without vitamin D\n- **E.** There is no physiological interaction between potassium and magnesium\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMagnesium inhibits the renal outer medullary potassium (ROMK) channels in the collecting tubule. When magnesium is depleted, this physiological inhibition is lost, allowing potassium to pour freely into the urine. Furthermore, hypomagnesaemia impairs Na+/K+-ATPase activity. Hypokalaemia is virtually impossible to correct until concurrent hypomagnesaemia is aggressively repleted.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
79044ffe-7ea3-403c-88f5-c224d33980e6	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	hypercalemia	Hypercalcaemia Emergency Management	article	# Hypercalcaemia Emergency Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nNon-parathyroid endocrine diseases (Thyrotoxicosis, Addison's disease,\n- Phaeochromocytoma) disease\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n- SkeletaLzBone pain, skeletal deformities, osteoporosis Neuromuscular and neuropsychiatric\n- Drowsiness, delirium, coma\n- Fatigue, lethargy, muscle weakness, insomnia\n- Depression, anxiety, irritability, psychosis\n- Neurological signs (for example upper rnotor neurone deficits, hypotonia,\n- hyporeflexia, and ataxia). Gastrointestinal — Nausea, vomiting, anorexia, weight loss, constipation, abdominal\n- pain Renal — Renal colic due to renal stones (nephrolithiasis), thirst, polyuria, polydipsia,\n- nocturia, and dehydration (due to nephrogenic diabetes insipidus) Cardiovascular — Hypertension\n- Shortened QT interval; prolonged PR interval on electrocardiogram (ECG)\n- Cardiac arrhythmias such as ventricular fibrillation (rare).\n-\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nArrange a serum calcium blood test and interpret the result: Avoid prolonged application of a tourniquet during venepuncture.\n- Ensure the laboratory result shows the calcium concentration is adjusted\n- ('corrected') for the serum albumin concentration FBC\n- ESR Estimated glomerular filtration rate (eCFR) and creatinine — to assess hydration\n- status, for acute kidney injury (AKI) and chronic kidney disease (CKD)\n\n- Serum and urine protein electrophoresis, including testing for urine Bence-Jones protein — to exclude myeloma.\n- PTH level\n- Serum cortisol (morning sample at 8-9 am) — if Addison's disease is suspected\n- Vitamin D level\n- Chest X-ray — to exclude lung cancer or metastases, lymphoma, sarcoidosis, or tuberculosis.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nIf the person has severe hy_percalcaemia or severe symptoms:\n- Arrange emergency admission to hospital for further specialist assessment and management\n- Start intravenous fluids and intravenous bisphosphonate therapy (pamidronate or zoledronate).\n- Referral pathway.\n- If the person is asymptomatic or has minimal symptoms, with mild or moderate hypercalcaemia and\n-\n- A new diagnosis of malignancy_is suspected, arrange urgent referral (to be seen within 2 weeks) to an appropriate specialist, depending on clinical judgement.\n- A diagnosis of primary_hyperparathyroidism is suspected on the basis of serum parathyroid hormone (PTH) results, arrange referral to endocrinology for specialist assessrnent and management\n- A diagnosis of non-parathyroid endocrine disease is suspected, arrange referral to endocrinology for specialist assessment and management.\n- A diagnosis of familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia is suspected, arrange referral to endocrinology for specialist assessment and management, the urgency\n\ndepending on clinical judgement.\n- A diagnosis of sarcoidosis or other granulomatous disease is suspected, refer the person to a respiratory or other specialist, depending on disease manifestation.\n- Encourage mobilization in patient with paget's disease if possible, and monitor the serum calcium concentration. See the Scenario on Follow-up in primary care for more information on follow-up monitoring.\n- If the drug is the cause, reduce or stop them if appropriate and recheck the serum calcium at least 3 weeks after stopping the drug. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Hypercalemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypercalemia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Hypercalemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Hypercalemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypercalemia/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Hypercalemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e031700f-1ac9-412c-bb88-9d9fbd94d988	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-hypercalcemia	Hypercalcaemia Emergency Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Hypercalcaemia Emergency Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 62-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer presents with profound lethargy, confusion, constipation, generalized weakness, and polydipsia with polyuria. Adjusted serum calcium is 3.65 mmol/L (normal 2.20-2.60 mmol/L). What classic mnemonic summarizes the clinical manifestations of severe hypercalcaemia?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hypercalcaemia Emergency Management)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hypercalcemia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 'Stones, Bones, Abdominal groans, and Psychic moans' (renal calculi, bone pain/fractures, constipation/pancreatitis, and confusion/depression)\n- **B.** 'Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, and Paralysis'\n- **C.** 'Charcot's triad and Reynolds' pentad'\n- **D.** 'Virchow's triad of thrombosis'\n- **E.** 'Cushing's triad of increased intracranial pressure'\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe clinical features of hypercalcaemia are summarized as: Stones (nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis); Bones (bone pain, osteopenia, pathological fractures); Abdominal groans (anorexia, nausea, constipation, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis); Psychic moans (lethargy, cognitive impairment, depression, psychosis, coma). Shortened QT interval on ECG is classic.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the two most common underlying aetiologies responsible for over 90% of all cases of Hypercalcaemia in adults?\n\n- **A.** Primary Hyperparathyroidism (outpatient setting) and Malignancy (hospital inpatient setting)\n- **B.** Excessive dietary milk intake and antacids\n- **C.** Hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency\n- **D.** Vitamin C toxicity and gout\n- **E.** Chronic viral hepatitis and cirrhosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPrimary hyperparathyroidism (solitary parathyroid adenoma) is the leading cause of hypercalcaemia in ambulatory outpatients (>60%). Malignancy (humoral hypercalcaemia via PTHrP in squamous cell cancers, or osteolytic bone metastases in breast/myeloma) is the leading cause in hospital inpatients (~30%), presenting with rapid onset, high calcium levels (>3.0 mmol/L), and suppressed intact PTH.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the absolute FIRST-LINE medical therapy in the emergency management of severe acute hypercalcaemia (calcium >3.5 mmol/L)?\n\n- **A.** Aggressive intravenous hydration with 0.9% Normal Saline (typically 3 to 4 litres in the first 24 hours, titrated to cardiovascular status)\n- **B.** Immediate intravenous loop diuretics (Furosemide) before giving any fluids\n- **C.** Intravenous bisphosphonates immediately with fluid restriction\n- **D.** Oral calcitriol 0.5mcg daily\n- **E.** Oral sodium bicarbonate\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSevere hypercalcaemia induces nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, severe polyuria, anorexia, and vomiting, resulting in profound volume depletion (often 3-5 litres fluid deficit). Immediate aggressive volume expansion with 0.9% Normal Saline (3-4 litres in 24 hours) restores GFR, dilutes serum calcium, and promotes urinary calciuria. Loop diuretics are NOT given until the patient is fully volume-resuscitated.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich pharmacological class of anti-resorptive agents is administered following initial saline rehydration for hypercalcaemia of malignancy, and why does its calcium-lowering effect take 48 to 72 hours to manifest?\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Bisphosphonates (e.g. Zoledronic Acid 4mg IV over 15 minutes or Pamidronate 60-90mg); they inhibit osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, taking 2-4 days to reach peak effect\n- **B.** Intravenous Furosemide; works by depleting calcium in the proximal tubule\n- **C.** Oral Cinacalcet alone\n- **D.** Subcutaneous Insulin\n- **E.** Intravenous Hydrocortisone; works instantly\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIV Bisphosphonates (Zoledronic acid 4mg IV or Pamidronate) bind to hydroxyapatite crystals in bone and inhibit osteoclast enzyme activity and recruitment. Because they act on osteoclast bone turnover, their maximal hypocalcaemic effect is delayed, peaking at 48-72 hours. Calcitonin (subcutaneous or IM) can be given concurrently for rapid temporary reduction within 4-6 hours (tachyphylaxis limits use to 48 hours).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat classic 12-lead ECG alteration is characteristically seen in acute severe hypercalcaemia?\n\n- **A.** Shortened QT interval (shortened ST segment)\n- **B.** Prolonged QT interval with prominent U waves\n- **C.** Broad notched P waves\n- **D.** Right bundle branch block\n- **E.** Delta waves\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHypercalcaemia accelerates ventricular myocardial repolarisation (phase 2 of cardiac action potential), which produces a classic shortening of the ST segment and a Shortened QT interval on 12-lead ECG. Severe hypercalcaemia can precipitate cardiac arrest in systole.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3aaf85fa-20bb-43e9-9e58-265ab765d73f	40e7b149-db6f-4acb-a33f-f4f9196a71c0	bone-malignancy	Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care	article	# Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Masterclass Track:** Musculoskeletal (non-traumatic)\n> High-yield clinical guidance, diagnostic algorithms, and evidence-based emergency management based on RCEM, NICE, and UK guidelines.\n\n\n- Osteosarcoma (osteogenic sarcoma)\n> Primary tumor in less than 20 years of age\n> In adults usually secondary predisposing factors, such as\nPaget disease of bone, bone infarcts, radiation, familial\nretinoblastoma, Li-Fraumeni syndrome.\n> Location: Metaphysis of long bones (often in knee region).\n> Clinical features: painful enlarging mass or pathologic\nfractures\n> Investigation: sunburst pattern on x-ray\n> Management: surgery, chemotherapy\n\nMRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA ( Complete Course Guide With Question bank)\nFor Diet 2 Late 2026\nOverview Comments\n/26, 10:31 AM Bone Malignancy | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n- Ewing sarcoma\n> Most common in White patients, generally males < 15 years\nold.\n> Location: Diaphysis of long bones (especially femur), pelvic\nflat bones.\n> Investigation: x-ray reveal ill-defined lytic lesion of the femur\ndiaphysis with a permeative pattern of destruction\n> Management: Chemotherapy\n/26, 10:31 AM Bone Malignancy | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n- Giant cell tumor\n> Common in 20-40 years of age\n> Location: Epiphysis of long bones (often in knee region)\n> Locally aggressive benign tumor Neoplastic mononuclear\ncells that express RANKL and reactive multinucleated giant\n(osteoclastlike) cells. "Osteoclastoma".\n> Investigation: "Soap bubble" appearance on x-ray.\n> Management: Radiotherapy and chemotherapy\n/26, 10:31 AM Bone Malignancy | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n22% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\n/26, 10:31 AM Bone Malignancy | MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n\n\n## Clinical Figures, Diagnostic Algorithms & Radiographs\n\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Click any figure below to examine diagnostic algorithms, anatomy landmarks, or radiological details at full clinical resolution.\n\n\n\n![Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/bone-malignancy/figure_01_p2.png)\n\n*Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care - Figure 1: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 2) (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/bone-malignancy/figure_02_p3.png)\n\n*Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care - Figure 2: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 3) (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n\n\n![Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care - Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 4)](/lessons/images/sba/musculoskeletal-non-traumatic/bone-malignancy/figure_03_p4.png)\n\n*Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care - Figure 3: Clinical Evaluation & Algorithm (Page 4) (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n\n\n## High-Yield RCEM Examination Pearls\n\n\n- **Core Syllabus Alignment:** Master the evidence-based diagnostic criteria, dosages, and contraindications for **Bone Malignancies & Pathological Fractures in Emergency Care**.\n- **Emergency Disposition:** Always document clear clinical justification when discharging or admitting patients under UK emergency guidelines.\n- **SBA Decision Making:** In Single Best Answer questions, select the safest, first-line evidence-backed management step before secondary investigations.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-11-10 04:43:49.893923+00	2026-11-10 05:09:04.286472+00	\N
e4f456bb-95ec-451f-aae1-829779b9fc05	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	pituitary-gland	Pituitary Gland	article	Pituitary gland\nThe pituitary endocrine gland, which is located in the bony\nSella turcica, is attached to the base of the brain and has a\nunique connection with the hypothalamus. The pituitary gland\nconsists of two anatomically and functionally distinct regions,\nthe anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) and the posterior lobe\n(neurohypophysis).\nThe pituitary gland is connected to the hypothalamus through\na stalk of blood vessels and nerves (the pituitary stalk). Through\nthat stalk, hypothalamus communicates with the anterior\npituitary lobe via hormones and the posterior lobe through\nnerve impulses. The hypothalamus also creates oxytocin and\nantidiuretic hormone and tells posterior pituitary when to store\nand release these hormones. hypothalamus makes the\nfollowing hormones to communicate with and stimulate\npituitary gland:\nCorticotrophin-releasing hormone (CRH).\nDopamine.\nGonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH).\nGrowth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH).\nSomatostatin.\nThyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH).\nSince your pituitary gland and hypothalamus work together so\nclosely, if one of them becomes damaged, it can affect the\nhormonal function of the other.\n\n#### Anterior lobe of pituitary\n\ngland\nThe anterior pituitary is derived from oral ectoderm (Rathke's\npouch). The anterior lobe of pituitary gland is linked to\nhypothalamus by the hypothalamic-pituitary hypophysial portal system. Thus, blood from hypothalamus that contain\nhigh concentration of hypothalamic hormone is delivered\ndirectly to the anterior pituitary. Hypothalamic hormone (e.g.,\ngrowth hormone releasing hormone GHRH) then stimulate or\ninhibit the anterior pituitary hormone (e.g., growth hormone\nGH)\nThe following hormone secreted from anterior pituitary gland:\nFollicle-stimulating hormone (FSH\nLuteinizing hormone (LH)\nProlactin\nGrowth hormone\n\n## (Gh)\n\nAdrenocorticotropin (ACTH)\nThyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\n\n#### Anterior pituitary hormone\n\nPosterior pituitary gland (neurohypophysis)\nThe posterior lobe of pituitary gland arises from an evagination\nof neuroectoderm from diencephalon. It stores and releases\nvasopressin (antidiuretic hormone or ADH) and oxytocin,\nboth made in hypothalamus (supra optic nuclei and\nparaventricular nuclei) and transported to posterior pituitary\nvia neurophysins (carrier protein)\n\n![Pituitary Gland - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/pituitary-gland/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Pituitary Gland — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nHormones of posterior pituitary gland\n\n![Pituitary Gland - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/pituitary-gland/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Pituitary Gland — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
77fd839b-a5fa-4cf5-b61c-d9a11a0e6427	bb0e5b2d-ecab-4654-8aba-13849df3c95c	bronchodilator-drugs	Bronchodilator Drugs	article	Bronchodilator therapy:\nNote: Important points of theophylline:\nTheophylline has narrow window between therapeutic and\ntoxic doses, and the half-life can be affected by a number of\nfactors, making a therapeutic dose difficult to achieve. It is\nadvised that serum levels of 10-20 mg/I six to eight hours after\nthe last tablet should be aimed for.\nFactors that increase the half-life of theophylline include:\nHeart failure\nCirrhosis\nViral infection\nDrugs (Enzyme inhibitors)\nFactors that decrease the half-life of theophylline include:\nSmoking\nHeavy drinking\nDrugs (Enzyme inducers)\nEnzyme inducers and Enzyme inhibitors\n\n![Bronchodilator Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/bronchodilator-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Bronchodilator Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nAminophylline:\nAminophylline is a compound of theophylline with\nethylenediamine in a 2:1 ratio. The ethylenediamine improves\nits solubility. It is less potent and shorter acting than\ntheophylline.\nIt is used in the treatment of:\nAsthma\n\n## Copd\n\nHeart failure\nBradycardias\nNote: Aminophylline has mild positive inotropic and\nchronotropic effects, producing an increase in cardiac output\nand a decrease in systemic vascular resistance, leading to a\ndecrease in arterial blood pressure. It has been used historically\nin the treatment of refractory heart failure and is\nrecommended by the current ALS guidelines as an alternative\ntreatment for bradycardias.\n\n![Bronchodilator Drugs - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/bronchodilator-drugs/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Bronchodilator Drugs — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
952be614-b44c-45e9-87a8-13a290e52ab5	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-resuscitation-sepsis	Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 68-year-old woman is brought to the ED with confusion, rigors, and dysuria. HR 122 bpm, BP 82/48 mmHg, RR 26/min, Temp 38.9C, Lactate 4.6 mmol/L. According to the Surviving Sepsis Campaign and NICE NG51, what initial crystalloid fluid bolus volume should be administered for sepsis-induced hypoperfusion?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/resuscitation-sepsis/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 10 ml/kg of 0.9% Normal Saline over 4 hours\n- **B.** 30 ml/kg of a balanced crystalloid (e.g. Hartmann's or Plasmalyte) within the first 3 hours\n- **C.** 500 ml 5% Dextrose bolus\n- **D.** 20 ml/kg 20% Albumin infusion\n- **E.** Zero fluids, start noradrenaline infusion immediately\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nThe Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines recommend administering at least 30 ml/kg of intravenous balanced crystalloid solution within the first 3 hours of resuscitation for patients with sepsis-induced hypoperfusion (lactate >=4 mmol/L or hypotension systolic <90 mmHg / MAP <65 mmHg).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nDespite 30 ml/kg balanced crystalloid infusion, the 68-year-old patient's blood pressure remains 84/50 mmHg (MAP 61 mmHg) and lactate is 4.1 mmol/L. What is the first-line vasopressor of choice in septic shock?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/resuscitation-sepsis/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Dopamine infusion\n- **B.** Noradrenaline (Norepinephrine) target MAP >= 65 mmHg\n- **C.** Phenylephrine boluses\n- **D.** Adrenaline (Epinephrine) infusion as initial monotherapy\n- **E.** Vasopressin monotherapy at 0.08 units/min\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nNoradrenaline is the first-line vasopressor of choice in septic shock to restore and maintain a Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) of >=65 mmHg. Dopamine is associated with significantly higher rates of tachyarrhythmias and excess mortality.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the key advantage of using balanced crystalloids (such as Hartmann's solution or Ringer's lactate) over 0.9% Normal Saline in large-volume resuscitation for septic shock?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/resuscitation-sepsis/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Higher sodium concentration preventing cerebral oedema\n- **B.** Avoidance of hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis and reduced incidence of acute kidney injury (AKI)\n- **C.** Immediate reversal of thrombocytopenia\n- **D.** Prevention of hypocalcaemia\n- **E.** Bactericidal action against Gram-negative bacteria\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nLarge infusions of 0.9% Normal Saline (chloride 154 mmol/L) cause hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, renal vasoconstriction, reduced glomerular filtration rate, and higher risk of requiring renal replacement therapy. Balanced crystalloids reduce this risk.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nIn patients with septic shock refractory to fluid resuscitation and high-dose noradrenaline and vasopressin, which adjunctive pharmacological therapy is recommended?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/resuscitation-sepsis/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Hydrocortisone 200mg/day (given as 50mg every 6 hours or continuous infusion)\n- **B.** High-dose Dexamethasone 20mg IV daily\n- **C.** Intravenous Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid) megadose\n- **D.** Activated Protein C infusion\n- **E.** Recombinant Erythropoietin\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSurviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines recommend low-dose corticosteroid therapy (IV Hydrocortisone 200 mg/day, either as 50 mg IV q6h or continuous infusion) for patients with septic shock who require ongoing vasopressor therapy (e.g. noradrenaline >=0.25 mcg/kg/min) despite adequate fluid resuscitation.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended target Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) during vasopressor titration in adult patients with septic shock without chronic hypertension?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Resuscitation in Sepsis & Septic Shock)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/resuscitation-sepsis/figure_05_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** MAP >= 55 mmHg\n- **B.** MAP >= 65 mmHg\n- **C.** MAP >= 85 mmHg\n- **D.** MAP >= 95 mmHg\n- **E.** MAP >= 110 mmHg\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nSurviving Sepsis Campaign recommends an initial target MAP of 65 mmHg. Targeting higher MAP values (e.g. 80-85 mmHg) does not improve survival or renal outcomes in general, but increases vasopressor exposure, myocardial oxygen demand, and risk of arrhythmias.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9d1ab385-4337-49e2-bef8-2fa07ba484da	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-sepsis-adult	Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 74-year-old male nursing home resident presents with productive cough, fever of 39.1C, HR 118 bpm, RR 26/min, BP 98/58 mmHg, and new confusion. His NEWS2 score is 11. Which six interventions comprise the UK 'Sepsis Six' care bundle to be delivered within 1 hour?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/sepsis-adult/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Give oxygen, Give IV antibiotics, Give IV fluids, Take blood cultures, Measure lactate, Measure urine output\n- **B.** Give paracetamol, Take sputum, Give oral fluids, Insert arterial line, Measure CRP, Chest X-ray\n- **C.** Intubate, Give noradrenaline, Give hydrocortisone, Take CSF, Measure troponin, Insert CVP\n- **D.** Give blood transfusion, Take CT chest, Give bronchodilators, Check D-dimer, Measure troponin, Start heparin\n- **E.** Give oral co-amoxiclav, Give 5% dextrose, Take urine dipstick, Check blood glucose, Measure ESR, Electrocardiogram\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe UK Sepsis Six pathway consists of 3 diagnostic and 3 therapeutic actions within 1 hour: (1) Give supplemental oxygen (target 94-98% or 88-92% in hypercapnic COPD); (2) Take blood cultures; (3) Give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics; (4) Give IV fluid challenge; (5) Measure serum lactate; (6) Measure urine output (monitor fluid balance).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to the Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3), how is 'Sepsis' formally defined?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/sepsis-adult/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Presence of 2 or more SIRS criteria in the presence of confirmed infection\n- **B.** Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection (quantified by an acute increase in SOFA score >= 2)\n- **C.** Bacteraemia with a positive blood culture\n- **D.** Fever >38.5C with neutrophilia >15 x 10^9/L\n- **E.** Hypotension requiring vasopressors with elevated procalcitonin\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nSepsis-3 defines sepsis as a 'life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection'. Organ dysfunction is clinically identified by an acute change in total SOFA score >= 2 points consequent to the infection, which is associated with an in-hospital mortality >10%.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich three clinical variables constitute the bedside 'quick SOFA' (qSOFA) criteria used to prompt clinicians to consider sepsis and impending organ failure?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/sepsis-adult/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Temperature >38C, Heart rate >90 bpm, White cell count >12\n- **B.** Respiratory rate >= 22/min, Altered mentation (GCS <15), and Systolic BP <= 100 mmHg\n- **C.** Urine output <0.5 ml/kg/hr, Serum lactate >2 mmol/L, Platelets <100\n- **D.** Bilirubin >33 umol/L, Creatinine >170 umol/L, PaO2/FiO2 <300\n- **E.** Capillary refill >3s, Core-peripheral temp gradient >3C, Blood glucose >10\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nqSOFA criteria consist of: (1) Respiratory rate >= 22/min; (2) Altered mental status (GCS <15); (3) Systolic blood pressure <= 100 mmHg. Presence of >=2 criteria indicates high risk of poor outcome.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhen obtaining blood cultures in a patient with suspected severe sepsis, which practice adheres to RCEM best practice standards?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Adult Sepsis & The Sepsis Six)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/sepsis-adult/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Take blood cultures from an existing central venous line placed 2 weeks ago only\n- **B.** Draw at least two sets of peripheral blood cultures (aerobic and anaerobic bottles) using strict aseptic technique prior to antibiotic administration\n- **C.** Wait for the fever spike above 39.5C before taking any blood culture bottles\n- **D.** Only take blood cultures if the white cell count is abnormal\n- **E.** Administer IV antibiotics first, then draw cultures 4 hours later\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nBlood cultures should be drawn before initiating antimicrobial therapy, provided this does not cause significant delay (>45 min). At least two sets (aerobic and anaerobic) should be taken from separate peripheral venepuncture sites with aseptic technique.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the clinical significance of a serum lactate level > 4.0 mmol/L in an ED patient with suspected sepsis, even if the systolic blood pressure is currently 110/70 mmHg?\n\n- **A.** It represents 'Cryptic Septic Shock' (occult hypoperfusion) and carries a mortality equivalent to hypotensive septic shock\n- **B.** It is clinically insignificant because blood pressure is preserved\n- **C.** It is purely caused by hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis\n- **D.** It indicates that IV fluids should be strictly withheld to avoid pulmonary oedema\n- **E.** It indicates primary renal tubular necrosis rather than sepsis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLactate >4.0 mmol/L in normotensive sepsis represents 'cryptic septic shock' (cellular hypoxia and occult microcirculatory failure). These patients have an in-hospital mortality rate comparable to patients with overt hypotension and require immediate aggressive resuscitation with 30 ml/kg crystalloid and critical care admission.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
126c7646-c282-4991-8186-3572238eef92	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	tachyarrhythmias-management	Tachyarrhythmias Management	article	# Tachyarrhythmias Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n\n\n- Two Types Regular or irregular narrow complex Regular or Irregular broad complex Ismall box= 0.04s 1 Large box=O.20s\n- PR interval 0.12-0.20 In narrow complex tachycardia QRS <O.12s In broad complex QRS>O.12s\n- Tachycardia Unstable patient\n- Adverse features\n- Shock (SBP<90, confusion, drowsiness, cold extremities, pallor).\n- Syncope (loss of consciousness) Myocardial ischemia (Chest pain, breathlessness, ECG changes) Heart failure (Pulmonary edema, raised JVP, Hepatomegaly). Extreme heart rate:\n- 150 mostly patient are unstable\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nGive 3 synchronized DC shocks (120-150j for broad complex and 70-120 for narrow complex)\n\n- If not successful then administer Amiodarone 300mg over IO to 20 minutes and repeat the shock\n- Followed by 900mg over 24hrs\n- Note: Any unstable patient requires electrical cardioversion rather than drugs regardless of the underlying rhythm. Regular narrow complex tachycardia\n- Sinus tachycardia(only HR>IOObpm)\n- Atrial flutter\n\nRegular narrow complex SVT AVNRT — most common\n-\n- HR: 150-250bpm Retrograde P wave, don't always see them\n-\n\n- Supraventricular Tachycardia:AVnodaI reentrant _\n- slow-fast- conduction p-wave me QRS\n\n- Regular narrow complex SVT\n- AVRT — Delta wave and short PR interval\n\nNormal electrical pathways (AV) node (SA) Abnormal el«trieal pathway in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nFirst line Vagal maneuver\n- Carotid massage\n- Valsalva maneuver\n- Second line\n- According to NICE guidelines Adenosine 6mg if no response\n- 12mg if no response further\n- 12mg adenosine\n-\n\n- According to RESUS COUNCIL UK guidelines Adenosine 6mg if no response\n- 12 mg if no response further\n- 18 mg can be given\n- If contraindicates or not effective then rate limiting CCB verapamil or Beta blockers. If they're also ineffective then synchronized cardioversion.\n- Note: if sinus rhythm isn't restored even after adenosine then think about atrial flutter and treat with beta-blocker and seek expert help.\n- Adenosine in Cl in heart block, sick sinus syndrome, and asthma.\n- Adenosine in heart transplant patient dose should be half 3mg\n- Irregular narrow complex tachycardia\n- Atrial Fibrillation\n- Example: 1\n\n- Example:2\n- Atrial flutter with a variable block\n\n### Management\n\n- AF: rate, rhythm control.\n- Atrial flutter: Rate control drugs (beta blocker)\n- Regular-broad-complex-tachycardia\n- Ventricular tachycardia\n- SVTwith BBB\n- Note: Any patient\n- 50 years with ischemic or structural heart disease assumed to have\n\napture beat stp:patient\n- t: AV dissociation\n- f\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nIF VT or uncertain rhythm Fusion beat\n- Amiodarone 300mg over 20 to 60 minutes then 900mg over 24hrs\n- If previously confirmed SVT with BBB then adenosine as for regular narrow complex tachycardia\n- Irregular broad complex tachycardia\n- AF with bundle branch block( most common).\n- Treat as narrow complex tachycardia\n\n- Torsades de pointes\n- Treatment: Magnesium 2g over IO minutes\n\n- Pre-excited atrial fibrillation( in patient with WPW)\n- Treatment: DC cardioversion. Drug treatment can consider flecainide or procainamide\n\navR avu 6 Key Points\n- Any patient with adverse features regardless of rhythm treated with DC cardioversion.\n- Approach:\n- focus on the QRS complex, Is it narrow or wide? Then on rhythm: is it regular? How's the rate? SVT: narrow complex tachycardia is always SVT. Sometimes SVT presents as broad complex tachycardia.\n- AVNRT: look for inverted P waves in inferior leads 11, 111, and AFV, sometimes causing a 'pseudo' S wave. P wave simultaneous with QRS.\n- AVRT: triad: narrow complex, delta wave, and short PR interval.\n\n- Atrial flutter: Sawtooth's appearance in lead Ill and aVF previous\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_03_p2.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_04_p3.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_05_p4.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_06_p5.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_07_p6.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_08_p7.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_09_p8.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_10_p10.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 10 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 11](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_11_p10.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 11 (Page 10 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 12](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_12_p11.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 12 (Page 11 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 13](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_13_p12.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 13 (Page 12 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 14](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_14_p13.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 14 (Page 13 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 15](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_15_p14.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 15 (Page 14 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 16](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_16_p15.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 16 (Page 15 Reference)*\n\n![Tachyarrhythmias Management - Clinical Figure 17](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/tachyarrhythmias\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_17_p16.jpeg)\n*Tachyarrhythmias Management — Clinical Figure 17 (Page 16 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	36	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b01113e2-4842-4ddb-902e-39deefc767b0	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-tachyarrhythmia	Tachyarrhythmias Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Tachyarrhythmias Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 35-year-old woman presents with sudden onset racing heart and lightheadedness. ECG shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia (QRS 80 ms) at 190 bpm with retrograde P waves buried within the QRS complexes. BP is 114/72 mmHg. Vagal manoeuvres (modified Valsalva) fail. What is the first-line pharmacological treatment per Resuscitation Council UK guidelines?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** IV Adenosine 6mg rapid bolus with immediate flush (titrating to 12mg, then 18mg if needed)\n- **B.** IV Amiodarone 300mg infusion\n- **C.** IV Metoprolol 5mg slow bolus\n- **D.** Synchronised DC cardioversion 100J\n- **E.** Oral Digoxin 500mcg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor haemodynamically stable regular narrow-complex tachycardia (SVT / AVNRT), after vagal manoeuvres, IV Adenosine (initial 6 mg rapid bolus via large proximal vein with fast saline flush) is first-line. If unsuccessful, give 12 mg, then 18 mg. Adenosine terminates AV nodal re-entrant circuits in over 90% of cases.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nIn which medical condition is the use of intravenous Adenosine strictly CONTRAINDICATED due to the risk of precipitating fatal bronchospasm?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Severe active bronchial asthma\n- **B.** Type 2 diabetes mellitus\n- **C.** Systemic hypertension\n- **D.** Osteoarthritis\n- **E.** Previous appendicectomy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAdenosine causes bronchoconstriction via purinergic A2B/A3 receptors on mast cells. It is contraindicated in patients with severe active asthma, bronchospastic lung disease, or known adenosine hypersensitivity. Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (e.g. Verapamil) are the preferred alternative.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_07_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 52-year-old man presents with rapid palpitations, sweating, and severe central chest tightness. The monitor shows a regular wide-complex tachycardia at 170 bpm. BP is 74/48 mmHg, and GCS is 13. According to ALS guidelines, what is the immediate treatment?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Immediate Synchronised DC Cardioversion (120-150J biphasic) with sedation/analgesia\n- **B.** IV Adenosine 6mg\n- **C.** IV Amiodarone 300mg over 30 minutes\n- **D.** Oral Sotalol 160mg\n- **E.** Carotid sinus massage for 60 seconds\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe presence of any adverse feature (shock, syncope, myocardial ischaemia, or acute heart failure) in a tachyarrhythmia mandates immediate synchronised DC cardioversion. Up to 3 synchronized shocks should be attempted before considering amiodarone.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_08_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended initial physical manoeuvre with highest proven cardioversion success rates (43% vs 17% standard) for terminating stable supraventricular tachycardia (AVNRT/AVRT) in the ED?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Modified Valsalva manoeuvre (straining against 40 mmHg for 15s followed immediately by passive leg elevation to 45 degrees)\n- **B.** Bilateral simultaneous eyeball pressure\n- **C.** Bilateral simultaneous carotid sinus massage\n- **D.** Deep knee bends for 2 minutes\n- **E.** Trendelenburg position for 1 hour\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe REVERT trial demonstrated that the Modified Valsalva Manoeuvre (blowing into a 10ml syringe to generate 40 mmHg pressure for 15 seconds, followed immediately by lying flat with passive leg elevation to 45 degrees for 15 seconds) achieved a 43% conversion rate to sinus rhythm compared to 17% for the standard Valsalva manoeuvre.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_09_p10.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhen a regular wide-complex tachycardia is encountered in an adult patient with no prior ECG, how should it be managed medically if the clinician is uncertain whether it is VT or SVT with aberrancy?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Treat as Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) until proven otherwise\n- **B.** Treat as SVT and administer IV Verapamil\n- **C.** Give IV Digoxin loading dose\n- **D.** Administer IV Atropine\n- **E.** Perform vagal manoeuvres only and discharge home if stable\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOver 80% of all regular wide-complex tachycardias are Ventricular Tachycardia (and >90% in patients with previous MI or structural heart disease). Giving AV-nodal blockers like verapamil to a patient in VT can precipitate refractory hypotension, ventricular fibrillation, and death. Always treat wide-complex tachycardia as VT unless proven otherwise.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_10_p10.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 6 (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_11_p11.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 7 (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_12_p12.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 8 (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_13_p13.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 9 (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_14_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 10 (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_15_p15.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 11 (Tachyarrhythmias Management)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/tachyarrhythmia/figure_16_p16.jpeg)\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	37	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
2c054245-c441-493a-baa0-86b0dc30c682	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	valvular-heart-disease-in-the-ed	Valvular Heart Disease in the ED	article	# Valvular Heart Disease in the ED\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n\n\n-\n- Early systolic Associated with acute severe\n- Aortic Stenosis\n- Stenosis: narrowing of the valve (restricted opening), Heart murmur due to forward flow through a narrowed outlet Regurgitant-valvular incompetence (failure of closure) Heart murmur due to backwards or regurgitant flow through a leaking/ incompetent valve\n- Diastolic murmur: AR, PR, MS and TS systolic murmurs: MR, TR, AS, PS, VSD Clinical classification of systolic murmurs Cardiac murmur heard nocent physiological due to high-output state Pathological outflow obstruction AS, PS, HCM\n- athological MR, VSO\n- AS is defined as a restricted opening of the valve cusps causing an obstruction to left ventricular (LV) outflow.\n- AS is severe once the valve area has decreased to lcm2 or less (normal 3-4cm2).\n- Causes Congenital (most common in young adults)\n\nAcquired\n-\n- Rheumatic: (Commonest cause worldwide) Age-related degenerative calcific (commonest cause in the UK)\n- Pathophysiology: _ As the aortic valve area reduces the LV contracts more forcefully to maintain the\n- stroke volume, eventually resulting in LV hypertrophy. In severe disease, the ventricle can no longer respond and LV function becomes\n- abnormal. The balance between cardiac output and myocardial muscle oxygen demand can be easily disrupted, causing severe pump failure\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nClassic triad (breathlessness, chest pain, exertional syncope)\n- pulse: Slow rising small volume with a sustained peak (pulsus parvus et tardus)\n- Cardiac impulsezl-aterally displaced apex beat indicates the onset of heart failure.\n- Auscultation: Harsh systolic ejection murmur second intercostal space right sternal\n- edge and radiating to the carotids. Fourth heart sound 'gallop rhythm'\n-\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG: LVH criteria (or strain pattern) but may be absent despite severe obstruction:\n- 10-15% of severe AS have normal ECGs, and may show RBBB or I-BBB.\n- CXR: May show normal-sized heart and a dilated proximal ascending aorta. Late signs are LV/LA dilatation and pulmonary oedema.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof AS\n- Pulmonary oedema:\n- Treat with diuretic and CPAP\n- Nitrates and ACE inhibitors may cause a drop in after load and a significant drop in blood pressure and should be avoided.\n- Atrial fibrillation:\n- Acute-onset AF can be treated with digoxin to slow ventricular response and improve stroke volume.\n- Chest pain:\n- Chest pain may be relieved by beta-blockers, which reduce myocardial oxygen demand and may improve coronary blood flow.\n- Cardiogenic shock:\n- These patients need aggressive rnedjcaLth.e.rapy-anderne.rgency-\n- Aortic Regurgitation\n- Aortic valve incompetence results from failure of the valve to prevent leakage (regurgitation) of some of the stroke volume back into the left ventricle from the aorta.\n- Causes\n- Acute AR:\n\nAortic dissection\n- Infective endocarditis\n- Prosthetic valve dysfunction\n- Rupture of aortic valve leaflet e:g trauma\n- Chronic AR\n- Calcific degeneration\n- Aortic root dilatation\n-\n- Rheumatic Connective tissue and autoimmune disorders\n-\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nCommon symptoms Palpitation\n-\n- Chest pain Angina\n- Heart failure\n- Angina\n- Pulse: Rapid rise and quick collapse (water hammer pulse), double impulse, wide\n- pulse pressure. Corrigan's sign — visible carotid pulsation\n- Traube's sign — 'pistol shot' sound heard over the femoral artery\n- Quincke's pulse — capillary pulsation visible on shining a light through the fingertips\n- Auscultation: Early diastolic murmur best heard with the patient sitting forward in fully\n- held expiration. Austin Flint murmur\n- apical diastolic murmur caused by obstruction of mitral flow\n- produced by the partial closure of the Mitral Valve by the regurgitant jet and rapid\n\nrising LV diastolic pressure\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG LVH\n- CXR: Cardiomegaly with LV prominence and possibly dilated aorta\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nAcute AR\n- Contact specialist services as may need surgical intervention in an emergency\n- Treatr-rnent should be focused on reducing pulmonary venous pressure and increasing cardiac output. They will include the use of vasodilators, intubation and positive pressure ventilation.\n- Inotropic support may be needed but can worsen the AR\n- Nitrates and diuretics have little effect and the intra-aortic balloon pump is contraindicated.\n- Chronic AR\n- adjustment of medical therapies such as diuretics, vasodilators, and rate and rhythm control is needed acutely.\n- Other management:\n- Blood cultures should be taken unless there is an obvious underlying cause (e.g. Aortic dissection or AMI).\n- AR patients have an increased risk of developing endocarditis and should receive appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis.\n\nMitral Stenosis MS is where mitral valve narrowing restricts the flow of blood from the left atrium\n- into the ventricle impairing left ventricular filling. Symptoms may not occur until the valve area is reduced to 1-1.5cm2 (normal 4-6cm2).\n- Causes\n- Rheumatic heart disease (commonest cause worldwide) Infective endocarditis\n- Calcification of the mitral annulus\n-\n- SLE Carcinoid syndrome\n- Left atrial myxoma\n-\n- Pathophysiology: .\n- The obstruction to atrial emptying in MS causes an elevation in left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure and LA hypertrophy, leading to reduced lung compliance and breathlessness on exertion. Reactive pulmonary arterial hypertension causes right ventricular hypertrophy and\n- failure.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nExertional breathlessness, orthopnoea and PND, AF and pulmonary edema\n- Breathlessness is the first common presentation\n- Haemoptysis is the second rnost common presentation\n- volume, irregular (usually AF)\n-\n\n- Auscultation: Loud first heart sound (Sl) (in sinus rhythm), opening snap, and rumbling mid-diastolic murmur. Early diastolic murmur of pulmonary regurgitation (Graham Steell murmur due to secondary pulmonary hypertension).\n- Mitral-facies;-peripheral cyanosis of the cheeks\n- ECG;-Broad or biphasic p wave best seen in Lead 2 indicating LA hypertrophy. R axis deviation. AF common, RV hypertrophy in later stages\n- CXR: Straightening of the left heart border indicating a dilated LA (double atrial shadow). Pulmonary congestion.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nFind and treat the underlying infection if suspected\n- Diuretics may be needed to relieve pulmonary congestion.\n- Rate control with beta-blockers, digoxin, or calcium channel blockers will be required for rapid atrial fibrillation. Any consideration of cardioversion must recognize the significant incidence of atrial thrombus and the risks of embolization\n- Acute haemoptysWs relatively rare but can be severe, may require referral to a cardiothoracic surgeon.\n- AILMSpatients in atrial fibrillation should be on long-term anticoagulants. Mitral Regurgitation\n- Mitral regurgitation is the backflow of blood from the left ventricle into the left atrium during systole.\n- Causes\n\n- Ruptured chordae tendinae or partial or complete papillary muscle rupture (e.g. due to acute myocardial infarction, trauma or infective endocarditis).\n- Rheumatic heart disease\n- LV dilatation secondary to ischaemic heart disease or cardiomyopathy Myxomatous degeneration\n- Mitral valve prolapse\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nPulse: Tachycardia/AF common (Prominent 'a' wave in JVP in MR)\n- Cardiac impulsezDiffuse and displaced laterally. Systolic thrill at the apex\n- Auscultation: Pansystolic murmur radiating to the axilla and back third heart sou nd.\n- ECG: LA and LV hypertrophy AF common\n- CXRzPuImonary oedema with a normal-sized heart or minimally enlarged LA\n- ECHO: Urgent\n- to rule out the ventricular septal defect, diagnose MP, and assess LV function.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nAcute MR associated with myocardial infarction is a cardiovascular emergency and may require surgical intervention.\n- Treat pulmonary oedema: (intubation in case of cardiogenic shock, CPAP, diuretics, nitrates, inotropes, and ACEI).\n- Patients with cardiogenic shock with acute MR may benefit from Intra aortic balloon pump.\n\n- Treat AF: Therapy is directed at reducing afterload to reduce LV work and controlling AF. Mitral Valve Prolapse\n- MVP is the prolapse of a portion of the valve leaflets into the left atrium during systole associated with a small amount of regurgitation of blood.\n- Clinical examination is normal apart from Mid-systolic click a high-pitched sound on auscultation caused by sudden tensing of the Mitral Valve apparatus as the leaflets prolapse\n- Normal antibiotic prophylaxis precautions should be followed if no other symptoms require treatment.\n- Tricuspid regurgitation\n- Insufficiency of the tricuspid valve\n- Causes Congenital: Ebstein's anomaly RV dilatation — mitral valve disease, RV infarction, pulmonary hypertension\n- Infective endocarditis (intravenous drug abuse) Marfan's syndrome\n\n## Key Clinical Features\npatient is usually asymptomatic\n- Can develop right HF, edema, ascities due to liver congestion\n\n- Pulse;_large C-V waves in JVP\n- Auscultation;\n- Soft pansystolic murmur at LSE louder on inspiration\n- Third heart sound (S3) often heard\n- CXR: shows Cardiomegaly\n- Others: liver congestion\n- Intravenous drug abusers may present acutely unwell with staphylococcal endocarditis.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nA drug abuser needs blood cultures and aggressive antibiotic therapy covering staphylococcal infection. Early surgery may be needed. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Valvular Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/valvular-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Valvular Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Valvular Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/valvular-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Valvular Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Valvular Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/valvular-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_03_p2.jpeg)\n*Valvular Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Valvular Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/valvular-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_04_p11.jpeg)\n*Valvular Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 11 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	38	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a75e33ff-2511-48ce-b3e0-5d9d990424d8	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-12	Quiz 12: Peripheral Neuropathy, Hernia Anatomy & Inguinal Architecture	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 12\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 50-year-old male presents to ED with the complaint of headache and chest pain, he is currently taking captopril and Hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Thiazide diuretic act at which of the following sites in nephron?\n\n- A. Collecting duct\n- B. Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **C. Early part of distal convoluted tubule** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- E. Late part of distal convoluted tubule\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThiazides act mainly on early segment of distal tubule where they inhibit the nacl reabsorption by binding to the Na-cl- cotransporter.\n\nwhile loop diuretic inhibits the Na-k-2CL- symporter on luminal membrane in the thick ascending limb of loop of Henle thus preventing the reabsorption of NaCl and water (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 50-year-old man complains of intermittent abdominal pain on lifting and coughing. You suspect a Spigelian hernia. Weakness of which structure is most likely to be responsible?\n\n- **A. Transversus abdominus aponeurosis** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Transversalis fascia\n- C. Internal inguinal ring\n- D. Iliopsoas fascia\n- E. Femoral sheath\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSpigelian hernia also known as hernia of Linea semilunaris is a protrusion through a defect in the aponeurosis of the transversus abdominis muscle.\n\nClinically, they present as a small tender mass at the lower lateral edge of the rectus abdominus*. They have a high risk of strangulation, and so should be repaired urgently.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 54-year-old woman presents with acute back pain and complains of numbness around her rectum. Which dermatome best describes this area of numbness?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | $2 | Popliteal fossa |\n> | S3 | Buttocks (Ischial tuberosity) |\n> |  | Perianal region (Recall) |\n\n- A. S2\n- B. L5\n- C. S1\n- D. S3\n- **E. S5** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 30-year-old man presents to the ED with an anterior shoulder dislocation while playing, now he is unable to initiate abduction of the arm following reduction of a dislocated shoulder. Which one of the following muscles is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Teres major\n- B. Teres minor\n- C. Infraspinatus\n- **D. Supraspinatus** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Subscapularis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSupraspinatus is part of the rotator cuff group of muscles which initiate the abduction of shoulder from 0 to 15 degrees and then assists the deltoid with continued abduction. Supraspinatus muscle supplied by suprascapular nerve, "empty can test" use to examine this muscle.\n\nTeres minor muscle assessment test=Hornblower's test) RECALL)\n\nSubscapularis muscle assessment test (lift-off Test) RECALL Most commonly torn tendon of rotator cuff is supraspinatus muscle (RECALL)\n\nTeres minor and deltoid muscle innervated by Axillary nerve (RECALL)\n\nSupraspinatus Is innervated by suprascapular ner ve Subscapular is innervated by subscapular nerve\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nWhich agent is released or secreted after a hemorrhage and causes an increase in renal Na* reabsorption?\n\n- **A. Aldosterone** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\n- C. Atrial natriuretic peptide\n- D. Angiotensin I\n- E. Angiotensinogen\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAngiotensin I and aldosterone are increased in response to a decrease in renal perfusion pressure. Angiotensinogen is the precursor for angiotensin I.\n\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH) is released when atrial receptors detect a decrease in blood volume. O f these, Atrial natriuretic peptide is released in response t o an increase in atrial pressure, (Recall) and an increase in its secretion would not be anticipatedafter blood loss\n\nNote: Renin is an enzyme produced by juxtaglomerular cells of nephron (Recall)\n\nRenin Angiotensin- aldosterone system\n\nSteps in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system\n\nA decrease in renal perfusion pressure causes the juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole to secrete renin Renin is an enzyme that catalyzes the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I in plasma. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) catalyzes t he conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, primarily in the lungs.\n\nAngiotensin II has four effects:\n\nIt stimulates the synthesis and secretion of aldosterone by the adrenal cortex. Aldosterone increases Na* reabsorption by the renal (distal tubule, thereby increasing extracellular fluid (ECF) volume, blood volume, and arterial pressure.\n\nIt increases Na+-H exchange in the proximal convoluted tubule.\n\nIt increases thirst and therefore water intake It causes vasoconstriction of the arterioles, there by increasing TPR and arterial pressure\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 30-year-old male unable to laterally rotate his arm following fracture of surgical neck of humerus, which of the following muscle most likely to be involved?\n\n- A. Latissimus dorsi\n- **B. Teres minor** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Rhomboid major\n- D. Supraspinatus\n- E. Teres major\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe axillary nerve is most commonly damaged due to fracture of surgical neck of humerus, which supply the teres minor muscle which helps is lateral rotation of arm and also supply the deltoid muscle which cause abduction of arm...\n\nAxillary nerve root value =C5 and C6 (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 20 years old male presents to the ED following stab wound to antecubital fossa with complaint of unable to flex his index and middle finger resulting in unopposed extension of index and middle finger. Which one of the following nerves is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Radial nerve\n- B. Ulnar nerve\n- **C. Median nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Musculocutaneous nerve\n- E. Axillary nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\ncommon injuries affecting the median nerve include:\n\nSupracondylar fracture of humerus Stab wound to antecubital fossa Compression of carpel tunnel syndrome (RECALL)\n\nMedian nerve innervation\n\nMotor supply: all the anterior compartment of forearm muscles (Flexor compartment) (except flex or Carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus), the thenar muscles (flexor policies brevis, abductor pollicis brevis, opponent policies) and lateral two lumbricals are supplied by median nerve Sensory supply: lateral aspect of palm and palmar surface and fingertips of lateral three and half digits. (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 27-year-old man falls from a ladder. Neurological examination reveals an isolated defect of proprioception. Which tract is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Dorsal column | Located: posteromedial aspect | Transmit ipsilateral proprioception, vibration |\n> |  | of spinal cord | and light touch sensation |\n> | Spinothalamic tract | Anterolateral aspect of cord | Transmit contralateral pain, crude touch |\n> |  |  | and temperature sensation |\n> | Lateral | Posterolateral aspect of cord | Control ipsilateral motor power |\n> | corticospinal tract |  |  |\n\n- A. Lateral spinothalamic\n- B. Anterior spinothalamic\n- **C. Dorsal column** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Lateral corticospinal\n- E. Posterior spinothalamic tract\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 35 years old male presents to the ED with complaint of swelling in the right inguinal region. Regarding hernia which one of the following types protrudes through Hesselbach's triangle?\n\n- A. Indirect inguinal hernia\n- **B. Direct inguinal hernia** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Paraumbilical hernia\n- D. Spigelian hernia\n- E. Femoral hernia\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDirect inguinal hernia lies medial to inferior epigastric vessels while indirect inguinal hernia lies lateral to inferior epigastric vessels. (RECALL)\n\nMost common type of inguinal hernia=indirect inguinal hernia (Recall)\n\nMost common side of inguinal hernia=Right side (RECALL)\n\nThe floor of the inguinal canal is the inguinal ligament, otherwise known as the Poupart ligament, which is formed by the external oblique aponeurosis (RECALL)\n\nType of hernia that enters the deep inguinal ring =indirect inguinal hernia (RECALL)\n\nType of hernia that protrudes through Hesselbach's triangle=Direct inguinal hernia (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nRegarding the blood supply of abdomen, superior mesenteric artery supply which one of the following structures?\n\n- **A. Ascending colon** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Sigmoid colon\n- C. Descending colon\n- D. Kidneys\n- E. Oesophagus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n1. coeliac trunk supply the Abdominal oesophagus stomach pancreas liver spleen gallbladder duodenum proximal to major duodenal papilla\n\n2. Superior mesenteric artery supply the: Distal duodenum Jejunum Ileum cecum Appendix Ascending colon (RECALL)\n\nProximal two third of transverse colon\n\n3.Inferior mesenteric artery supply the:\n\nDistal transverse colon Descending colon (RECALL)\n\nSigmoid colon Proximal rectum\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA young athlete presents to the ED after sustaining an injury to the right upper extremity. Physical examination reveals weak right forearm flexion and an absent biceps reflex. Given these findings, sensation loss in which of the following areas is most likely to be found in this patient?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | | | | Sensorysupply | Function |  |\n> |  Musculocutane | = Anterior | + Lateral forearm (via | * Supinator of forearm |\n> | ous nerve | compartment of | the lateral |  degrees Also arm at |\n> |  | arm | nerve of the forearm) | elbow and shoulder |\n> |  | (coracobrachialis, |  |  |\n> |  | biceps brachii, |  |  |\n> |  | brachialis) |  |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Triceps reflex |  | Triceps brachii |\n> | Knee jerk reflex | L3-L4 | Quadriceps (Recall) |\n> | Ankle | $1 (Recall) | Gastrocnemius |\n\n- A. Thenar eminence\n- B. Posterior forearm\n- C. Posterior arm\n- **D. Lateral forearm** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Medial forearm\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh yield:\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students' venous drainage of lower limb, Regarding the course of great saphenous vein, which one of the following is correct?\n\n- A. Travels with saphenous nerve along its course\n- B. Runs behind the medial malleolus\n- C. Begins as the upward continuation of the lateral marginal vein of the foot\n- **D. Enters the femoral vein on its anteromedial side** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Join the femoral vein above the inguinal ligament\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe great saphenous vein: originate from media l side of the dorsal venous arch in the foot, passes anterior to the medial malleolus (not posterior), travels up the medial side of leg, knee and thigh to pass in to saphenous opening in deep fascia covering the femoral triangle and join with the femoral vein below the inguinal ligament.\n\nThe small saphenous vein: originate from lateral side of dorsal venous arch in the foot, passes posterior to lateral malleolus and up the back of leg t o pierce the deep fascia and join the popliteal vein in popliteal fossa posterior to knee...\n\nNote: Cutaneous innervation to medial side of foot by=saphenous nerve (Recall).\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nRegarding the intrinsic muscles of hand, Froment's sign tests the function of which one of the following muscles?\n\n- A. Abductor pollicis\n- B. Opponents pollicis\n- **C. Adductor pollicis** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Flexor policies breves\n- E. Flexor policies Longus\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFroment's sign: Patient is asked to hold a piece of paper between thumb and flat palm as paper is pulled away, patient will flex thumb at inter-phalangeal joint to maintain hold, this sign tests the function of adductor pollicis muscle which is supply by the ulnar nerve. (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 55 years old male known case of HIV presented to the ED with Complain of Cough and breathlessness. You suspect pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia, which one of the following is treatment of choice?\n\n- A. Respiratory Quinolones\n- B. Gentamycin\n- C. Tetracyclines and sulfamethoxazole\n- D. Ceftriaxone\n- **E. Trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTrimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole is recommended first line treatment against pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia.\n\nNote: Trimethoprim causes folate deficiency that's why teratogenic in early pregnancy.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 50-year-old male presents to the Emergency department with the complaint of swelling in ankle. She has recently been started on loop diuretics by her GP. loops diuretic act to inhibit the?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Na-K-ATPase\n- **B. Na-K-2CL- channel** (Correct Answer)\n- C. H-K- channel\n- D. Na-K-channel\n- E. carbonic anhydrase\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nloop diuretic e.g.: furosemide inhibit the Na+k+2C L- symporter on luminal membrane in the thick ascending limb of loop of Henle thus preventing t he reabsorption of Nacl and water while thiazides act mainly on early segment of distal tubule where they inhibit the Nacl reabsorption by binding to the Na+cl- cotransporter (RECALL).\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 12:** 1-C, 2-A, 3-E, 4-D, 5-A, 6-B, 7-C, 8-C, 9-B, 10-A, 11-D, 12-D, 13-C, 14-E, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
34f5c7dc-9d21-4656-b615-5ba888d829db	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-valvular-heart-disease	Valvular Heart Disease in the ED — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Valvular Heart Disease in the ED — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 78-year-old male presents with exertional syncope. On examination, he has a slow-rising, low-volume pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus), an ejection systolic murmur loudest in the right second intercostal space radiating to the carotids, and an absent or soft A2 component of S2. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Valvular Heart Disease in the ED)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/valvular-heart-disease/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Severe Aortic Stenosis (AS)\n- **B.** Aortic Regurgitation\n- **C.** Mitral Regurgitation\n- **D.** Tricuspid Stenosis\n- **E.** Pulmonary Regurgitation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic triad of symptomatic severe aortic stenosis is Angina, Syncope, and Dyspnoea (SAD). Characteristic examination findings: slow-rising, plateau pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus), harsh crescendo-decrescendo ejection systolic murmur at the aortic area radiating to the carotids, and soft/absent A2.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhy must aggressive preload-reducing medications (such as high-dose nitrates and vigorous loop diuretics) be used with extreme caution or avoided in patients with severe Aortic Stenosis presenting with angina or heart failure?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Valvular Heart Disease in the ED)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/valvular-heart-disease/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** The hypertrophied left ventricle is fixed-afterload and exquisitely preload-dependent; excessive vasodilation causes precipitous drops in cardiac output and fatal shock\n- **B.** They cause severe acute aortic valve rupture\n- **C.** They induce acute massive pulmonary embolism\n- **D.** They cause irreversible complete heart block\n- **E.** They neutralize cardiac troponin\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSevere aortic stenosis represents a fixed mechanical outflow obstruction. The hypertrophic, non-compliant LV requires high end-diastolic filling pressures (preload) to generate adequate forward stroke volume. Venodilators (nitrates) or excessive diuresis sharply drop venous return, precipitating profound hypotension, syncope, and cardiac arrest.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 45-year-old woman with a history of rheumatic heart disease presents with worsening dyspnoea, orthopnoea, and new-onset atrial fibrillation. Auscultation reveals a loud first heart sound (S1), an opening snap in early diastole, and a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumbling murmur heard best at the apex in the left lateral position. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Valvular Heart Disease in the ED)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/valvular-heart-disease/figure_03_p11.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Mitral Stenosis (MS)\n- **B.** Aortic Stenosis\n- **C.** Mitral Valve Prolapse\n- **D.** Ventricular Septal Defect\n- **E.** Tricuspid Regurgitation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMitral stenosis (predominantly caused by rheumatic fever) is characterized by: loud S1, opening snap (pliant valve leaflet opening), and a low-pitched rumbling mid-diastolic murmur best heard with the bell of the stethoscope at the apex in the left lateral decubitus position. It frequently leads to left atrial dilation, AF, and pulmonary hypertension.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich peripheral physical signs are associated with chronic severe Aortic Regurgitation (AR)?\n\n- **A.** Corrigan's water-hammer pulse, wide pulse pressure, de Musset's sign (head nodding), and Quincke's capillary pulsations\n- **B.** Pulsus parvus et tardus with narrow pulse pressure\n- **C.** Cyanosis and digital clubbing\n- **D.** Pulsus paradoxus and electrical alternans\n- **E.** Splinter haemorrhages and Osler's nodes only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nChronic severe aortic regurgitation causes a huge stroke volume ejected into the aorta followed by massive diastolic regurgitation back into the LV, leading to a hyperdynamic circulation and wide pulse pressure: Corrigan's (water-hammer) pulse, Quincke's sign (capillary nailbed pulsation), de Musset's sign (head nodding with heartbeats), and Duroziez's sign.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 55-year-old male with a mechanical St. Jude bileaflet aortic valve presents with sudden severe pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock. His INR is 1.4 (therapeutic target 2.5-3.5). Bedside echo demonstrates restricted leaflet motion and massive transvalvular gradient. What is the primary diagnosis?\n\n- **A.** Prosthetic Valve Thrombosis\n- **B.** Cardiac tamponade\n- **C.** Acute anterior myocardial infarction\n- **D.** Bacterial pneumonia\n- **E.** Normal prosthetic valve function\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSubtherapeutic INR in a patient with a mechanical prosthetic valve presenting with acute cardiogenic shock or pulmonary oedema strongly indicates Prosthetic Valve Thrombosis. It is a critical surgical emergency requiring urgent echocardiography/fluoroscopy and either emergency surgical thrombectomy/replacement or slow-infusion low-dose fibrinolytic therapy.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	39	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3254664a-ae47-4457-9c65-9b288dce56bd	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	vitamin-d-synthesis	Vitamin - D Synthesis	article	Vitamin D synthesis\nVitamin D is a long-term regulator of serum calcium. Its main\nfunction is to increase the intestinal absorption of calcium.\nIt can be ingested, or synthesized from a cholesterol precursor\nas follows:\nLiver: cholesterol →  7-dehydrocholesterol (provitamin D3)\nSkin Storage of 7-dehydrocholesterol, Cleavage of 7-\ndehydrocholesterol via irradiation with UV light →\ncholecalciferol (in the stratum Basale)\nLiver: hydroxylation of cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxyvitamin D\nKidneys: 25-hydroxyvitamin D → 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D\nwhich is the active form of vitamin D\nRegulation of vitamin D synthesis: via regulation of 1 α -\nhydroxylase activity in proximal convoluted tubule\n↓ Calcium, ↓ phosphate, and ↑ PTH →  ↑ 1 α -hydroxylase\nactivity →  ↑ 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D biosynthesis\n↑ Calcium, ↑ phosphate, and ↑ 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D\n(feedback inhibition) →  ↓ 1 α -hydroxylase activity →  ↓ 1,25-\n\n![Vitamin - D Synthesis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/vitamin-d-synthesis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Vitamin - D Synthesis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\ndihydroxyvitamin D biosynthesis	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8ca2216c-2c9d-4e3d-a9f3-c382ab2a4be6	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	anti-platelet-statins-nitrate-and-fibrinolytic-drugs	Anti - Platelet, Statins, Nitrate and Fibrinolytic Drugs	article	Anti-platelet, Statins, nitrate and\nfibrinolytic drugs:\n\n![Anti - Platelet, Statins, Nitrate and Fibrinolytic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anti-platelet-statins-nitrate-and-fibrinolytic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Platelet, Statins, Nitrate and Fibrinolytic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
79b46653-18e0-4bf9-8c2c-3474aaed0858	9db23e52-af3e-4f01-9ef3-48e9c44fed87	anti-emetic-drugs	Anti - Emetic Drugs	article	Antiemetics drug class, Members and\nclinical use:\nMetoclopramide\nMetoclopramide is dopamine receptor antagonist acts directly\non GI tract as prokinetic drug, commonly associated with\nextrapyramidal symptoms induce acute dystonic\nreactions causing fascial and skeletal muscle spasm .\nAntidote: Injection of anti-parkinsonism drug Such as\n"Procyclidine will abort dystonic attack.\nNote: Extrapyramidal symptoms are most commonly\nassociated with antiemetics and antipsychotics\n\n![Anti - Emetic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/anti-emetic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Emetic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
eb8b6cbb-0924-4544-94dc-768eb1bb3d2d	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-long-qt-syndrome	Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 22-year-old woman collapses with syncope while running a marathon. In the ED, telemetry reveals episodes of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a characteristic twist of the QRS peaks around the isoelectric baseline at a rate of 220 bpm. What is this arrhythmia termed?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/long-qt-syndrome/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Torsades de Pointes (TdP)\n- **B.** Monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia\n- **C.** Ventricular fibrillation\n- **D.** Atrial flutter with 1:1 conduction\n- **E.** Accelerated idioventricular rhythm\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTorsades de Pointes ('twisting of the points') is a specific form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia occurring in the setting of acquired or congenital prolonged QT interval. The QRS complexes continuously twist in polarity around the isoelectric baseline.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the immediate first-line intravenous pharmacological treatment for haemodynamically stable Torsades de Pointes, regardless of the patient's baseline serum magnesium level?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/long-qt-syndrome/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Magnesium Sulphate (2g IV bolus over 1-2 minutes)\n- **B.** Intravenous Amiodarone 300mg\n- **C.** Intravenous Procainamide 100mg\n- **D.** Intravenous Sotalol 80mg\n- **E.** Intravenous Flecainide 150mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIV Magnesium Sulphate (2 g IV push over 1-2 minutes, repeatable in 5-10 minutes) is the drug of choice for Torsades de Pointes. Magnesium acts by blocking early after-depolarisations (EADs) and suppressing triggered activity, even in normomagnesaemic patients. Note: Class Ia and III antiarrhythmics (amiodarone, procainamide, sotalol) prolong QT and are strictly CONTRAINDICATED.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich of the following formula is standardly used to correct the measured QT interval for heart rate on ECG, and what value is generally considered prolonged in adult females?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/long-qt-syndrome/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Bazett formula (QTc = QT / sqrt(RR)); QTc >460-470 ms\n- **B.** Framingham formula; QTc >350 ms\n- **C.** Cockcroft-Gault formula; QTc >500 ms\n- **D.** Winter formula; QTc >400 ms\n- **E.** Haskell formula; QTc >300 ms\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe Bazett formula (QTc = QT / sqrt(RR in seconds)) is the most widely used heart-rate correction. Prolonged QTc is typically defined as >450 ms in adult males and >460-470 ms in adult females. A QTc >500 ms carries high risk of degenerating into Torsades de Pointes.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich commonly prescribed emergency department antimicrobials and antiemetics are well-known causes of acquired QT prolongation that can precipitate Torsades de Pointes?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Long QT Syndrome & Torsades de Pointes)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/long-qt-syndrome/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Macrolides (Clarithromycin, Azithromycin), Fluoroquinolones (Ciprofloxacin), and Ondansetron / Haloperidol\n- **B.** Amoxicillin and Paracetamol\n- **C.** Cephalexin and Metronidazole\n- **D.** Doxycycline and Ibuprofen\n- **E.** Gentamicin and Meropenem\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcquired long QT is frequently triggered by drugs that block the I_Kr potassium channel: macrolides, fluoroquinolones, antipsychotics (haloperidol), antiemetics (ondansetron, droperidol), and antiarrhythmics (sotalol, amiodarone), especially when combined with hypokalaemia or hypomagnesaemia.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat acute emergency intervention is indicated for recurrent pauses and bradycardia-dependent Torsades de Pointes that fails to terminate with intravenous magnesium boluses?\n\n- **A.** Temporary overdrive pacing (target HR 90-110 bpm) or Isoprenaline infusion\n- **B.** IV Metoprolol bolus to slow heart rate down to 40 bpm\n- **C.** Immediate hyperventilation to induce alkalosis\n- **D.** Ice water immersion\n- **E.** High-dose adenosine bolus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTorsades de Pointes is frequently pause-dependent and worsened by bradycardia (which lengthens QT). Overdrive electrical pacing (or chronotropic infusion with Isoprenaline) shortens the ventricular cycle length, shortens the QT interval, and eliminates the pause-dependent trigger.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	29	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7c237945-9007-4724-bf04-6b4a1fbbc7d3	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-myocarditis	Acute Myocarditis — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Myocarditis — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old previously fit athlete presents with sharp central chest pain radiating to his left shoulder, mild fever, and shortness of breath, 10 days after a flu-like viral illness. 12-lead ECG shows widespread concave ST-segment elevation. High-sensitivity Troponin T is markedly elevated at 1450 ng/L. Urgent coronary angiography reveals unobstructed coronary arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Myocarditis)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/myocarditis/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Myopericarditis\n- **B.** Atherosclerotic STEMI\n- **C.** Aortic dissection\n- **D.** Bacterial pneumonia\n- **E.** Costochondritis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute myopericarditis typically affects young adults following a viral prodrome (Coxsackievirus, Parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2). It mimics acute STEMI with chest pain, ECG ST-elevation, and significant troponin elevation, but coronary angiography shows normal patent arteries.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the gold standard, non-invasive imaging modality of choice to confirm myocardial inflammation, oedema, and necrosis in suspected acute myocarditis?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Myocarditis)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/myocarditis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Cardiovascular Magnetic Resonance (CMR) using Lake Louise Criteria\n- **B.** CT Pulmonary Angiogram\n- **C.** Transthoracic Echocardiogram alone\n- **D.** Technetium bone scan\n- **E.** Barium swallow\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCardiac MRI (CMR) is the reference non-invasive investigation. The updated Lake Louise Criteria (myocardial oedema on T2-weighted imaging plus late gadolinium enhancement [LGE] showing non-ischaemic epicardial/mid-wall pattern) has high diagnostic sensitivity and specificity for acute myocarditis.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich histological subtype of myocarditis presents with rapid, fulminant heart failure, refractory ventricular arrhythmias, and heart block, classically requiring urgent immunosuppressive therapy and early mechanical circulatory support (ECMO/LVAD)?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Myocarditis)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/myocarditis/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Giant Cell Myocarditis\n- **B.** Lymphocytic viral myocarditis\n- **C.** Eosinophilic myocarditis\n- **D.** Chagas disease\n- **E.** Toxoplasmosis myocarditis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nGiant Cell Myocarditis is a rare, aggressive autoimmune disorder of young adults characterized by multinucleated giant cells and widespread myocardial necrosis. It carries a dismal prognosis without urgent combined immunosuppressive therapy (cyclosporine, corticosteroids) and mechanical circulatory support or heart transplant.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat physical activity advice is strongly recommended by ESC and AHA guidelines for athletes diagnosed with acute myocarditis?\n\n- **A.** Strict cessation of competitive and recreational strenuous athletic activity for at least 3 to 6 months until clinical and CMR resolution\n- **B.** Continue regular marathon training if asymptomatic\n- **C.** No physical restrictions needed after 48 hours\n- **D.** Only avoid swimming\n- **E.** Permanent lifelong ban from all forms of walking\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAthletes with acute myocarditis are at high risk of sudden cardiac death due to ventricular arrhythmias triggered by intense exercise during active inflammation. Current guidelines mandate abstaining from competitive and strenuous sports for a minimum of 3-6 months, resuming only after reassessment confirms normalized ventricular function, biomarkers, and 24h ECG.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich parasitic infection transmitted by the Reduviid (triatomine) kissing bug in Central and South America causes chronic myocarditis, apical LV aneurysms, megacolon, and mega-oesophagus?\n\n- **A.** Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas Disease)\n- **B.** Plasmodium falciparum\n- **C.** Leishmania donovani\n- **D.** Entamoeba histolytica\n- **E.** Schistosoma mansoni\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nChagas disease is caused by Trypanosoma cruzi. Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy develops decades after initial infection, causing conduction system fibrosis (RBBB + LAFB), apical left ventricular aneurysms, lethal ventricular arrhythmias, and dilated cardiomyopathy, often with gastrointestinal mega-syndromes.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	31	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4b1f3336-4f08-4961-abc8-7ffba3116c2f	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-13	Quiz 13: Pharmacokinetics, Neuromuscular Junction & Shock Physiology	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 13\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 45-year-old man presents to the ED with pain around the back and side of his foot, he has been assessed by the physiotherapist who suspects tendonitis of his peroneus tendons, the peroneus Longus muscle attaches distally to which of the following structure?\n\n- A. Base of 5th metatarsal\n- B. Calcaneal\n- **C. Base of 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Base of 5th metatarsal and lateral cuneiform\n- E. Lateral cuneiform\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nOrigin: Head of fibula, proximal 2/3 of lateral surface of fibula\n\nInsertion: inferior surface of the base of the 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform bones.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 65-year-old women with hypertension being treated with thiazide, her blood pressure responds well and reads at 120/75, after several months on the medication, she complains of being tired and weak, which one of the following complications she has likely developed?\n\n- A. Decreased Uric acid\n- B. Decreased calcium\n- C. Decreased Sodium\n- **D. Decreased potassium** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Decreased glucose\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHypokalemia is common adverse effect of thiazide and furosemide, and cause fatigue and lethargy in the patient.\n\nFood rich in potassium, potassium chloride can correct the hypokalemia alternatively potassium sparing diuretics e.g., spironolactone may be added\n\nNote: Calcium uric acid and glucose are usually elevated by thiazide. Sodium loss wouldn't weaken the patient.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nFrom sitting to standing there is compensation by which mechanism?\n\n- **A. Increase heart rate** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Increase venous return\n- C. Decrease Cardiac output\n- D. Decrease heart rate\n- E. Increase TPR\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNormally from sitting to standing position, there i s of pooling of blood in veins of the leg due to gravity that's why central venous pressure, stroke volume and cardiac output fall and fall in blood pressure this fall in BP rapidly sense by baroreceptor reflex which cause vasoconstriction and an increase in heart rate and contractility, so restoring the stroke volume, cardiac output and blood pressure.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nStudents are asked to review a 25-year-old male who present to the ED after a road traffic accident, on examination students note weakness in his right hand and loss of sensation to the medial half of the palm. students suspect ulnar nerve injury, Ulnar nerve receives its fibers from which of the following nerve roots?\n\n- A. C5 -C6\n- **B. C8-T1** (Correct Answer)\n- C. C5 -T1\n- D. C7-C8\n- E. C5-C7\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nUlnar Nerve Roots: C8 and T1.\n\nMusculocutaneous Nerve Roots: C5, C6, C7 Axillary Nerve Roots: C5 and C6(RECALL) Median Nerve Roots: C6 - T1. (Also contains fibers from C5 in some people).\n\nRadial Nerve Roots: C5 - T1\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nRegarding pathophysiology of asthma which one of the following neurotransmitters plays important role in bronchoconstriction?\n\n- A. Anticholinergic\n- B. Acetylcholine\n- C. Serotonin\n- D. GABA\n- **E. Histamine** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHistamine has been a well-known chemical mediator released from mast cells in the immediate allergic reaction for a long time and has been thought to have a critical role in the asthma pathophysiology\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nAn 18 years old young girl brought into the ED with severe respiratory depression. Her mum reports that she has finished packet of her sleeping pills, you suspect benzodiazepines overdose. Which one of the following drugs will reverse the effect?\n\n- **A. Flumazenil** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Doxepin\n- C. Naloxone\n- D. Diazepam\n- E. Ramelteon\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nflumazenil is indicated to reverse the effects of benzodiazepines via antagonizing the Benzodiazepines receptor.\n\nANTIDOTES: (High yield)\n\nBenzodiazepines = flumazenil Lidocaine: Intralipid emulsion Prilocaine: methylene blue Tricyclic antidepressants: sodium bicarbonate Metoclopramide = procyclidine Opioid = naloxone (RECALL)\n\nOrganophosphate= atropine Paracetamol poisoning= N-acetyl cysteine Hyperkalemia = Ca gluconate (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nWhat is the quantitative relationship between gas solubility and pressure?\n\n- A. Boyle's law\n- B. Entropy\n- **C. Henry's law** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Charles law\n- E. Ficks law\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHenry's law: At a constant temperature, the amount of a given gas that dissolves in a given type a nd volume of liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of that gas in equilibrium with th at liquid." An equivalent way of stating the law is that the solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas above the liquid.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 50-year-old patient presents to the ED with heartburn for which he is taking antacids daily, which of the following drug can be affected if taken with the combination of antacids?\n\n- A. Simvastatin\n- B. Biguanide\n- C. Amlodipine\n- **D. Digoxin** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Furosemide\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWhen antacids are taken with acidic drugs, for example, digoxin, phenytoin, chlorpromazine, and isoniazid they cause the absorption of the acidic drugs to be decreased, which causes low blood concentrations of the drugs, which ultimately result s in reduced effects of the drugs.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 44-year-old male suffered from fracture of neck of the fibula now he is unable to do dorsiflexion of ankle, which one of the following nerves is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Tibial nerve\n- B. Obturator nerve\n- **C. Common peroneal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Sciatic nerve\n- E. Femoral nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe common peroneal nerve is also known as common fibular nerve is a branch of sciatic nerve, arise at apex of popliteal fossa, continue by wrapping around the neck of fibula where it is most likely to be damaged (RECALL)\n\nFunction: The common peroneal nerve Innervate the short head of the biceps femoris muscle. common fibular nerve innervates all the muscles of anterior and later compartments of the lower leg. The anterior muscular compartment of the low er extremity consists of the tibialis anterior muscle, the extensor hallucis longus muscle, and th e extensor digitorum longus muscle. This compartment is responsible primarily for the dorsiflexion of the foot (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 54-year-old patient is taking furosemide drug for his cardiac condition. Which one of the following complications patient will likely develop?\n\n- A. Hyperkalemia\n- B. Hypercalcemia\n- **C. Hypokalemia** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Dyslipidemia\n- E. Hypertension\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLoop diuretics adverse effects:\n\nhypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis hypomagnesaemia, hyponatremia, hypocalcemia, hypochloremia hyperuricemia acute hypovolemia ototoxicity blood disorder venous thromboembolism Hypokalemia is common adverse effect of thiazide and furosemide.\n\nHypokalemia management: Food rich in potassium, potassium chloride can correct the hypokalemia alternatively potassium sparing diuretics e.g., spironolactone may be added.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Mechanism of action of lorazepam?\n\n- **A. Potentiate GABA release** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Muscarinic receptors agonist\n- C. Ca channel blocker\n- D. GABA antagonist\n- E. Stimulate acetylcholine release\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBenzodiazepines e.g.: lorazepam is gamma amino butyric acid (GABA) receptors agonist GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system...\n\nLorazepam overdose can be treated with a specific benzodiazepine antidote medication known as flumazenil (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA patient presents to emergency department with itching and swelling of lips, he is known to be allergic to nuts, he receives adrenaline but there is no improvement of symptoms. Which of the following drug is most commonly implicated as a cause of refractory anaphylaxis?\n\n- A. Penicillin\n- B. Alpha blocker\n- C. NSAIDs\n- **D. Beta blocker** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Ca channel blocker\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAdrenaline is drug of choice in the emergency management of acute allergic and anaphylactic reactions. Patient taking beta blocker may not respond to adrenaline therapy, beta blocker interactswith one of the receptor sites for adrenaline even adrenaline cause severe hypertension in patient taking non cardio selective beta blockers. If adrenaline is ineffective in treating anaphylaxis in patients taking b-blockers, then glucagon administration might be necessary (Recall). Glucagon can reverse refractory bronchospasm and hypotension during anaphylaxis in patients on b-blockers by activating adenylyl cyclase directly and bypassing the b-adrenergic receptor\n\nNote: Anaphylactic reaction is IgE mediated released from mast cell and basophils (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 35-year-old male patient brought into the ED by paramedics with history of RTA. He is bleeding profusely and undergoes shock with BP of 80/60 what will be the first response in this type of shock?\n\n- A. Cortisol\n- **B. Baroreceptor** (Correct Answer)\n- C. CNS ischemic response\n- D. Sympathoadrenal activation\n- E. RAAS\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBaroreceptors are mechanoreceptors located in t he blood vessels near the heart. Baroreceptors function by sensing the pressure changes by responding to change in the tension of the arterial wall. The baroreflex mechanism is a fast response to changes in blood pressure.\n\nChanges in PH and Co2 sensed by Central and Peripheral Chemoreceptors (RECALL)\n\nChanges in Po2 sensed by = peripheral chemoreceptors only (RECALL)\n\nChanges in Arterial pressure sensed by = Baroreceptors (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nYou have been asked to review a 32-year-old pastry chef. she sustained a laceration to her left thenar eminence while working. which of the following muscle is a part of the thenar eminence?\n\n- **A. Abductor pollicis brevis** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Abductor pollicis Longus\n- C. Extensor pollicis brevis\n- D. Extensor pollicis Longus\n- E. Flexor policies Longus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThenar eminence muscles include:\n\nAbductor pollicis brevis Flexor policies brevis Opponents pollicis\n\nHypothenar muscles include: (RECALL)\n\nAbductor digitorum minimi Flexor digitorum minimi brevis muscle Opponents digitorum minimi muscle\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 55-year-old man who has been injured in car accident is brought into the ED, his blood alcohol level on admission is 280 mg/dl. Hospital record show a prior hospitalization for alcohol related seizure. his wife confirms that he has been drinking heavily for 3 weeks. what treatment should be provided to treat this condition?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Phenobarbital\n- B. Buspirone\n- **C. Lorazepam** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Phenytoin\n- E. Ketamine\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIt is important to treat the seizures associated with alcohol withdrawal, Benzodiazepines such aschlordiazepoxide, diazepam or short-acting lorazepam are effective in controlling this problem. they are less sedating than phenytoin or phenobarbital.\n\nNote: lorazepam side effect=respiratory depression (RECALL). Should be used cautiously in a patient with muscular disease.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 13:** 1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B, 5-E, 6-A, 7-C, 8-D, 9-C, 10-C, 11-A, 12-D, 13-B, 14-A, 15-C\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
ace365d1-bab0-4511-a004-9073a9aa87b9	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-14	Quiz 14: Respiratory Mechanics, Acid-Base Chemoreceptors & Visual Pathways	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 14\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nBilirubin is a byproduct of heme catabolism and is excreted in the bile and stool. Bilirubin is likely derived from?\n\n- A. Adipocytes\n- B. Enterocytes\n- **C. Red blood cells** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Epithelial cells\n- E. Mesangial cells\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHeme is an important functional group of hemoglobin. Bilirubin, which is a byproduct of heme breakdown, comes from red blood cells. The liver receives unconjugated bilirubin from the blood a nd conjugates it via phase II metabolism to make it more soluble for excretion via the bile and stool. Fat cells (adipocytes), skin cells (epithelial cells), and small intestine absorptive cells (enterocytes) do not contain heme to be processed into bilirubin.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with painful swollen digit, the infection appears to have spread to the common flexor sheath. Which of the following digit is most likely to be involved with spread of infection to this location?\n\n- **A. Little finger** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Thumb\n- C. Ring finger\n- D. Middle finger\n- E. Index finger\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe synovial sheath of the little finger is usually continued with the common flexor sheath which i s also called ulnar bursa thus infection of ulnar bursa most likely to involve the little finger. In a same way infection of thumb may spread to mid palmer space through the continuous synovial sheath of the flexor pollicis longus which is als o called radial bursa.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 45-year-old man fell from a rooftop and presented with a head injury and hyperventilation. What could be the consequence of hyperventilation?\n\n- **A. Decrease in cerebral blood flow** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Increase in cerebral blood flow\n- C. Decrease washout of Pco2\n- D. Increase in Pco2\n- E. Increased in Pao2\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCo2 is vasodilator causes increases cerebral blood flow (Recall)\n\nHypoventilation increases C02 and increases cerebral blood flow Hyperventilation causes decrease Pco2 and decreases cerebral blood flow\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 55-year-old patient presents to the emergency department with a persistent cough and hemoptysis. on x-ray you note hilar lymphadenopathy which of the following lymph nodes are most likely enlarged?\n\n- A. Inferior tracheobronchial lymph node\n- **B. Bronchopulmonary lymph node** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Cervical lymph node\n- D. Superior tracheobronchial lymph node\n- E. Anterior mediastinal lymph node\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Bronchopulmonary lymph node most frequently involved in pathologies of lung.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nWhich among the following, prevents dislocation of femur backward at the knee joint?\n\n- **A. Anterior cruciate** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Medial deltoid ligament\n- C. Posterior cruciate\n- D. Middle cruciate\n- E. Lateral cruciate\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAnterior cruciate prevents anterior dislocation of tibia on femur and posterior dislocation of femur on tibia Posterior cruciate prevents posterior dislocation of tibia onto femur and anterior dislocation of femur on tibia.\n\nIn Simple: ACL prevents anterior dislocation of tibia and posterior dislocation of femur.\n\nPCL prevents posterior dislocation of tibia and anterior dislocation of femur.\n\nNote:\n\nUnhappy triad: common in Rugby players. lateral blow to the extended knee with a twisting fall to the same side\n\nRefers to injury of Medial collateral, medial meniscus and anterior cruciate ligament\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nRegarding central respiratory chemoreceptors, which of the following statement is correct?\n\n- A. Respond directly to changes in external PCO2\n- B. Located in dorsal surface of medulla\n- C. Located in the medulla near exit of cranial nerve X,XI\n- **D. Respond to pH of CSF** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Respond directly to changes in arterial PCO2\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCentral chemoreceptors are sensitive to the PH o f CSF, decrease in the pH of the CSF produce increase in breathing rate(hyperventilation). H+ does n't Cross the blood brain barrier as well as co2 does. co2 diffuse from arterial blood into the CSF because co2 is lipid soluble and readily cross bloo d brain barrier. in the CSF, co2 combines with ho 2 to produce H+ and HCO3-. the resulting H+ act directly on the central chemoreceptors, thus increase in pco2 and H+ stimulate the breathing and decrease in pco2 and H+ inhibit the breathing. th is resulting hyperventilation or hypoventilation then returns the arterial Pco2 toward normal. Central chemoreceptors are located near the ventrolateral surface of the medulla.\n\nPeripheral chemoreceptors are located in carotid and aortic body. The carotid bodies are located at the bifurcation of common carotid arteries, the aortic bodies are located above and below the aortic arch.\n\nDecrease in arterial po2: stimulate the peripheral chemoreceptor and increase breathing rate. po2 must decrease to low levels (<60mmHg) before breathing is stimulated. when po2 is less than 60 mmHg, breathing rate is exquisitely sensitive to p o2.\n\nincrease in arterial Pco2: stimulate the peripheral chemoreceptors and increase breathing rate increase in arterial H+: stimulate the carotid body peripheral chemoreceptors directly, independent of Changes in pco2.\n\nIn metabolic acidosis, breathing rate is increased (hyperventilation) because arterial H+ is increase d and pH is decreased.\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nThe greatest increase in physiological dead space would be expected with which of the following conditions?\n\n- A. Bronchoconstriction\n- **B. Pulmonary embolism** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Pneumothorax\n- D. Obesity\n- E. Atelectasis\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDead Space: Volume of ventilated air that does not participate in gas exchange.\n\nTwo types of Dead Space;\n\n1: Anatomical Dead Space\n\n2: Physiologic Dead Space\n\n1: Anatomical Dead Space Volume of Air that fills the conducting Zone of respiration made up by t he Nose, Trachea, and Bronchi. The value of anatomical Dead Space is 150 ml.\n\n2: Physiologic or Total Dead Space Anatomic Pl us Alveolar Dead Space (Volume of air in the Respiratory zone that does not take part in gas exchange).\n\nRespiratory Zone starts from Respiratory Bronchioles, Alveolar Duct, Alveolar sac, and Alveoli. In patients with acute PE, total dead space increases because lung units continue to be ventilated despite diminished or absent perfusion.\n\nCauses of Increased Dead Space:\n\nEmphysema Pneumonia ARDS(RECALL)\n\nSmoking ETT intubation Bronchitis Asthma Cardiac Failure Pulmonary Embolism Neck Extension Standing Hypotension bronchodilation\n\nCauses of decreased Dead Space:\n\nAtelectasis Tracheostomy Sleep Maxillectomy Hyperventilation Neck Flexion Supine Position Bronchoconstriction\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nFetal hemoglobin shifts the o2 saturation curve to left due to?\n\n- A. Strongly bind with 2,3DPG\n- **B. Increased affinity for O2** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Increased P50\n- D. Decrease in pH\n- E. Decreased affinity for O2\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin. Maternal and Fetal blood never mix during pregnancy, but they come close to each other in the placenta. Oxygen diffuses easily to fetal hemoglobin here.\n\nThe reason fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for 02. It is composed of two alpha and two gamma subunits, while adult hemoglobin is composed of two alpha and two beta subunits.\n\n2,3DPG present in RBCs enhance the ability of RBCs to release o2 near tissue and 2,3DPG only binds with beta globulin chains, fetal hemoglobin (HbF) lack of beta globulin chains that's why fetal hemoglobin has higher affinity for O2(RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nWhich factors contribute to the Bohr Effect?\n\n- A. High pH, low CO2\n- B. Low pH, high CO2, low temperature\n- C. Low pH, low CO2\n- **D. Low pH, high CO2** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Low pH, high CO2, high temperature\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Bohr effect describes hemoglobin's affinity f or oxygen as a function of blood pH and carbon dioxide content. An increase in CO2 concentration will lower the blood pH, causing the hemoglobin affinity for oxygen to reduce. High temperature also causes oxygen to be released from hemoglobin but it's not related to Bohr's effect.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nWhich one of the following nerves supply the skin above the eye brow extending back to the vertex of the scalp?\n\n- A. Zygomaticotemporal nerve\n- B. Zygomaticofacial nerve\n- **C. Supraorbital nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Greater occipital nerve\n- E. Supratrochlear nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nsupraorbital nerve innervating the skin of upper eye lid and conjunctiva, skin of the upper forehead extending to the vertex of scalp.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 50-year-old male presents to the emergency department with complain of visual disturbance in his right eye on examination you note when you show a light in his right eye, neither his right or Left pupil constrict but when you show a light in his left eye both his left and right pupil constrict. which of the following nerve is most likely affected?\n\n- A. Trochlear nerve\n- **B. Optic nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Trigeminal nerve\n- D. Oculomotor nerve\n- E. Abducens nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLoss of afferent pupillary light reflex is seen in complete palsy of optic nerve. the ipsilateral direct and contralateral consensual reflexes are lost as t he afferent optic nerve does not sense the light shown in affected eye but the contralateral direct and ipsilateral consensual reflexes are intact as th e efferent oculomotor nerve is normal\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nWhich of the following shift oxygen hemoglobin dissociation curve to left?\n\n- A. Increase temperature\n- B. Increased Pco2\n- **C. CO Poisoning** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Decreased PH\n- E. Increased 2-3DPG\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLeftward shift: Increased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up oxygen)\n\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up oxygen)\n\nIncreased pH Decreased pCO2 Decreased temperature Decreased H+(RECALL)\n\nDecreased 2-3DPG CO (RECALLL)\n\nMethemoglobinemia Fetal hemoglobin (RECALL)\n\nRightward shift: Decreased affinity for oxygen (More willing to give up oxygen)\n\nDecreased ph (RECALL)\n\nIncreased pCO2 Increased H+ Increased Temperature Increased 2-3DPG\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nBerry aneurysms are the most common type of intracranial aneurysm arising from which one of the following arteries?\n\n- A. Posterior communicating artery\n- B. Posterior cerebral artery\n- C. Middle cerebral artery\n- **D. Anterior communicating artery** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Anterior Cerebral artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAnterior communicating artery is most common site of berry aneurysm. Second most common sit e is posterior communicating.\n\nAneurysm of anterior communicating artery compress the optic chiasma and cause bitemporal hemianopia, aneurysm of posterior communicating artery may cause 3rd nerve palsy. (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nDuring examination of cardiovascular system you are discussing heart sounds and cardiac cycle. which of the following stage of cardiac cycle occurs immediately after the aortic valve closes?\n\n- A. Ventricular filling\n- B. Ventricular ejection\n- C. Isovolumetric contraction\n- D. Atrial systole\n- **E. Isovolumetric relaxation** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIsovolumetric Relaxation\n\nWhen the aortic and pulmonary valves close, the heart relaxes isovolumetrically because both the outflow valves and inflow valves are closed, and s o no fluid moves across them. This phase lasts until the intraventricular pressure falls below the pressure in the atria, at which time the mitral an d tricuspid valves open again. The isovolumetric relaxation lasts about 0.08 S. The volume throughout this phase is the end systolic volume, approximately 50 ml. The atrioventricular (AV) valves open at an atrial pressure of about 7 mmHg.\n\nIsovolumetric relaxation:\n\nlasts about 0.08 seconds Aortic and pulmonary valves closed Mitral and tricuspid valves closed.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nWhich of the following is the site of highest air way resistance?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Smallest bronchi\n- B. Largest bronchi\n- C. Trachea\n- D. Alveoli\n- **E. Medium sized bronchi** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe medium-sized bronchi actually constitute th e site of highest resistance along the bronchial tree, although small radii of the alveoli might predict that they would have highest resistance, they don't because of parallel arrangement. In fact, early changes in resistance in small air ways may besilent and go undetected because of their small overall contribution to resistance.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 14:** 1-C, 2-A, 3-A, 4-B, 5-A, 6-D, 7-B, 8-B, 9-D, 10-C, 11-B, 12-C, 13-D, 14-E, 15-E\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
0a7b5f96-2a75-4958-a4ac-00f5b92a5686	9f523d95-eb11-4d95-b5cc-668c7fb4d993	primary-quiz-15	Quiz 15: Cardiac Action Potential, Autonomic Pharmacology & Meninges	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 15\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 24-year-old female presents with severe headache, photophobia, and stiffness of her back. Physical examination reveals positive signs for meningitis. The attending physician decides to perform a lumbar puncture to determine if a pathogen is in the CSF. What is the last structure the needle will penetrate before reaching the CSF?\n\n- A. Pia mater\n- **B. Arachnoid mater** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Ligamentum flavum\n- D. Posterior longitudinal ligament\n- E. Dura mater\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLumber puncture needle Peirce in order:\n\nSkin Subcutaneous tissue Supraspinous ligament Inter spinous ligament ligamentum flavum Epidural space Dura Arachnoid Sub arachnoid space (contain CSF)\n\nNote: The subarachnoid space is filled with CSF which is produced by the choroid plexus within t he ventricles of the brain.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nEpinephrine cause vasoconstriction by acting on which receptors?\n\n- A. AChR\n- B. Beta-2\n- C. Beta-1\n- **D. Alpha-1** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Alpha-2\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAlpha 1: vasoconstriction Alpha 2: vasodilation Beta 1: centrally increases automaticity, heart rate, myocardial contractility Beta 1: Peripherally vasodilates against alpha 1 vasoconstriction Beta 2 - bronchodilation\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 54-year-old man presents with hematemesis. He has recent diagnosed with a pulmonary embolus and started on lmwh. What is the most appropriate treatment to prevent adverse effects of heparin?\n\n- A. Heparan sulfate\n- B. Prothrombin complex\n- **C. Protamine sulfate** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Dextran sulfate\n- E. Fresh frozen plasma\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCommon drugs and their antidote:\n\nHeparin=protamine sulphate Acetaminophen/paracetamol = Acetylcysteine Organophosphates =Atropine Digoxin=Digoxin immune fab Benzodiazepine= flumazenil Opioid= naloxone Iron=Deferoxamine Warfarin=Vitamin K, Fresh frozen plasma, Prothrombin complex concentrate Tricyclic antidepressants=Sodium bicarbonate Calcium channel blocker=Calcium chloride Theophylline poisoning=beta blocker Methotrexate=Leucovorin Aspirin=Sodium bicarbonate Beta blockers= glucagon Insulin = glucose Isoniazid= vitamin B6\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH) acts primarily at which of the following sites in renal nephron?\n\n- A. Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- B. Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **C. Distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Descending limb of loop of Henle\n- E. Proximal tubule\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as vasopressin, is a small peptide hormone which regulates the body's retention of water. The main action of ADH in the kidney is to regulate the volume and osmolarity of the urine. Specifically, it acts i n the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and collecting ducts (CD).\n\nADH synthesized by hypothalamus and secreted from Posterior pituitary (RECALL)\n\nADH binds to V2 receptors on renal principal cells of late distal tubules and collecting ducts (RECALL)\n\nADH also binds with V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscles.\n\nCentral diabetes insipidus result from deficiency ADH secretion (RECALL)\n\nNephrogenic diabetes insipidus result from inappropriate renal response to ADH\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nDamage to Wernicke's area will most likely result in which of the following clinical features?\n\n- A. Nystagmus\n- B. Visual loss\n- C. Expressive dysphasia\n- **D. Receptive dysphasia** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Dysarthria\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBroca's aphasia: This is a non-fluent aphasia which causes individuals to speak slower. It occurs when the brain's frontal lobe suffers damage. Individuals with Broca's Aphasia often feel frustrated due to being able to comprehend what others are saying, but unable to express themselves. als o called Expressive dysphasia. Individuals affected only can speak in very short phrases, or have trouble getting words out. (RECALL)\n\nWernicke's aphasia: This is a fluent aphasia as th is type of aphasia does not affect the speed of th e individual's speech. Individuals with this type o f aphasia have damage to their brain's temporal lobe. As a result, those of Wernicke's aphasia are sometimes unable to comprehend what others are saying, but are able to speak in long sentences. However, their communication doesn't follow standard grammar patterns and uses made-up words. also called receptive dysphasia.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nDamage to Broca's area will most likely result in which of the following clinical features?\n\n- **A. Expressive dysphasia** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Visual loss\n- C. Receptive dysphasia\n- D. Dysarthria\n- E. Nystagmus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBroca's aphasia: This is a non-fluent aphasia which causes individuals to speak slower. It occurs when the brain's frontal lobe suffers damage. Individuals with Broca's aphasia often feel frustrated due to being able to comprehend what others are saying, but unable to express themselves also called Expressive dysphasia. Individuals affected only can speak in very short phrases, or have trouble getting words out.\n\nWernicke's aphasia: This is a fluent aphasia as th is type of aphasia does not affect the speed of th e individual's speech. Individuals with this type o f aphasia have damage to their brain's temporal lobe. As a result, those of Wernicke's aphasia are sometimes unable to comprehend what others are saying, but are able to speak in long sentences. However, their communication doesn't follow standard grammar patterns and uses made-up words. also called receptive diaphasia.(RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nYou are teaching group of medical students anatomy of inguinal region. You ask medical students Deep inguinal Ring is a defect in which of the following structure?\n\n- **A. Transversalis fascia** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Internal oblique aponeurosis\n- C. Transversus abdominis aponeurosis\n- D. Rectus abdominis aponeurosis\n- E. External oblique aponeurosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDeep inguinal ring is formed by transversalis fascia.\n\nSuperficial inguinal ring is formed by External Oblique. (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA patient complains of headache and visual loss. CT scan demonstrate a lesion of the temporal lobe what type of visual defect would u most expect to see in this patient?\n\n- A. Bitemporal hemianopia\n- B. Homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing\n- C. Contralateral homonymous hemianopia\n- D. Contralateral homonymous inferior Quadrantanopia\n- **E. Contralateral homonymous superior Quadrantanopia** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHomonymous denotes a condition which affects the same portion of the visual field of each eye.\n\nHomonymous inferior quadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same lower quadrant of visual field i n both eyes whereas a homonymous superior quadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same upper quadrant of visual field in both eyes.\n\nA lesion affecting one side of the temporal lobe may cause damage to the inferior optic radiation s (known as the temporal pathway or Meyer's loop) which can lead to superior quadrantanopia on the contralateral side of both eyes if the superior optic radiations (parietal pathway) are lesioned, t he visual loss occurs on the inferior contralateral side of both eyes and is referred to as an inferior quadrantanopia.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 55-year-old lady presents to the Emergency department with complaint of visual loss in Lower left quadrant in both visual fields. The lesion would most likely be in which of the following?\n\n- A. Right occipital lobe\n- B. Left temporal lobe\n- C. Left parietal lobe\n- **D. Right parietal lobe** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Right temporal lobe\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHomonymous denotes a condition which affects the same portion of the visual field of each eye.\n\nHomonymous inferior quadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same lower quadrant of visual field i n both eyes whereas a homonymous superior quadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same upper quadrant of visual field in both eyes.\n\nA lesion affecting one side of the temporal lobe may cause damage to the inferior optic radiation s (known as the temporal pathway or Meyer's loop) which can lead to superior quadrantanopia on the contralateral side of both eyes (RECALL), if th e superior optic radiations (parietal pathway) are lesioned, the visual loss occurs on the inferior contralateral side of the both eyes and is referred to a s an inferior Quadrantanopia (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\n30 years old male patient brought into ED Unresponsive after initial assessment you suspect opioid toxicity, what is the antidote of morphine toxicity?\n\n- A. Esmolol\n- **B. Naloxone** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Atropine sulphate\n- D. N-acetyl cysteine\n- E. Flumazenil\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nNaloxone is an opioid antagonist rapidly reverse the adverse effects of opioid toxicity.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nWhich vitamin is also known as anti-sterility vitamin?\n\n- A. Vitamin C\n- B. Vitamin D\n- C. Vitamin K\n- D. Vitamin A\n- **E. Vitamin E** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nvitamin e act as antioxidant prevents the cells from damage caused by free radicals. Sperm DNA damage is an emerging cause of male infertility that is likely to be more common among men with increased reactive oxygen species in the ejaculate. Vitamin E can protect the sperm membrane from oxidative damage and increased levels are associated with low levels of reactive oxygen species.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nHow does parathyroid (PTH) affect renal handling of calcium?\n\n- A. Decrease calcium reabsorption of proximal tubule\n- B. Decrease calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule\n- **C. Increase calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Increase calcium reabsorption of proximal tubule\n- E. Increase reabsorption of calcium at glomerulus\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPTH increases calcium reabsorption exclusively in the distal nephron.\n\n25-30% of dietary Calcium is absorbed from gut, o ut of this % of calcium, 50% is ionized (means free or unbound in plasma) and 45% is bound to albumin. Remaining 5% with other ions\n\nOnly free ionized Calcium is filtered freely in glomerulus:\n\n70% absorbed in PCT (RECALL)\n\n20% in thick ascending loop of Henle 9% in DCT 1% in Collecting duct\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 55 years old male known case of hypertension presents to the ED with complaint of tingling and numbness in his right index finger. You suspect carpal tunnel syndrome. Which one of the following structures lie within the carpal tunnel?\n\n- **A. Flexor pollicis longus tendon** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Palmaris longus tendon\n- C. Pronator quadratus\n- D. Ulnar nerve\n- E. Ulnar artery\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe carpel tunnel is the space that lies between t he flexor retinaculum. The ulnar nerve, ulnar artery and tendon of Palmaris longus runs superficial to flexor retinaculum and thus are not within carpel tunnel.\n\nFlexor pollicis longus enter the carpel tunnel before reaching its insertion on the distal phalanx of t he thumb Pronator quadratus is confined to the distal forearm and does not reach the carpel tunnel.\n\nNote: Carpal tunnel syndrome is caused by pressure on the median nerve (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 5-year-old male child brought into the ED with an infection after circumcision. The lymphatic from the penile skin and prepuce drains into which of the following group of lymph nodes?\n\n- A. External iliac lymph nodes\n- B. Internal iliac lymph nodes\n- C. Para aortic lymph nodes\n- D. Deep inguinal lymph nodes\n- **E. Superficial inguinal lymph nodes** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nPenile skin and prepuce = superficial inguinal lymph nodes Glans penis and penile urethra=deep inguinal lymph nodes(recall)\n\nScrotum=Superficial inguinal lymph nodes (RECALL)\n\nTestes= lumber or para-aortic lymph nodes (RECALL)\n\nprostate= internal iliac nodes (recall)\n\nHigh Yield:\n\nPre aortic lymph nodes:\n\n1.coeliac group of preaortic lymph nodes drain:\n\nAbdominal oesophagus Stomach Pancreas(recall)\n\nLiver Spleen Gallbladder Duodenum proximal to major duodenal papilla\n\n2. Superior mesenteric group of preaortic lymph nodes drain:\n\ndistal duodenum Jejunum Ileum Cecum Appendix Ascending colon Proximal two third of transverse colon\n\n3. 3. Inferior mesenteric group of preaortic lymph: Distal transverse colon Descending colon Sigmoid colon Proximal rectum\n\nLumbar or Paraaortic lymph nodes:\n\nKidney (Recall)\n\nAdrenal gland Testes (recall)\n\nOvaries (recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nyou are prescribing ipratropium bromide for a patient who has presented with an exacerbation of her COPD. what is mechanism of action of ipratropium bromide?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Beta agonist\n- **B. Muscarinic antagonist** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Ca channel blocker\n- D. Muscarinic agonist\n- E. Alpha agonist\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIpratropium is an acetylcholine antagonist via blockade of muscarinic cholinergic receptors. Blocking cholinergic receptors decreases the production of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGM P). This decrease in the lung airways will lead to decreased contraction of the smooth muscles.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 15:** 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-C, 5-D, 6-A, 7-A, 8-E, 9-D, 10-B, 11-E, 12-C, 13-A, 14-E, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
76d7804f-d316-49a4-94d8-25ab14527ed3	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	neutropenic-sepsis-guidelines	Neutropenic Sepsis	article	# Neutropenic Sepsis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- therapy to patients with suspected neutropenic sepsis immediately.\n- Send full blood count, kidney and liver function tests (including albumin), C-reactive protein, lactate and blood culture.\n- urinalysis in all children aged under 5 years\n- 0ffer prophylaxis with a fluoroquinolone during the expected period of neutropenia only\n- previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Neutropenic Sepsis Guidelines - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/neutropenic-sepsis-guidelines/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Neutropenic Sepsis Guidelines — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
57e5eb40-7244-4ff8-8e45-f1b9edd2a3e8	7d6ad555-2a31-4365-ad9b-d6abfb9b25c1	heriditary-coagulation-disorder-of-blood	Heriditary Coagulation Disorder of Blood	article	Coagulation disorder\nHereditary coagulation disorder:\n\n![Heriditary Coagulation Disorder of Blood - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/heriditary-coagulation-disorder-of-blood/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Heriditary Coagulation Disorder of Blood — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
dbce9c54-6814-44ca-8d2c-ef04da17e45d	7d6ad555-2a31-4365-ad9b-d6abfb9b25c1	platelate-and-platelate-plug	Platelate and Platelate Plug	article	Platelets and platelet plug\nPlatelets:\nThese are minute, round or oval discs formed by fragmentation\nof megakaryocytes. The normal half-life of platelets is 10 days.\nPlatelets contain three types of storage granules.\ndense granules containing ADP, ATP, serotonin and calcium.\nalpha granules containing clotting factors, von Willebrand\nfactor (VWF), platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and\nother proteins.\nlysosomes containing hydrolytic enzymes.\nFunctional characteristics of platelets:\nHave no nucleus, so cannot reproduce.\nHave actin and myosin molecules and thrombosthenin, so\ncan contract to cause clot retraction.\nHave residual ER and Golgi apparatus to synthesize enzymes\nand to store Ca ions.\nHave enzyme systems to synthesize ATP from ADP.\nHave enzyme Systems to synthesize prostaglandins.\nHave fibrin-stabilizing factor that causes polymerization of\nfibrin monomers to form fibrin threads.\nHave growth factor that promote growth and multiplication\nof vascular endothelial cells, vascular smooth muscle cells\nand fibroblasts. So, repair damaged vascular wall.\nIts surface has a glycoprotein-coat that adheres it to injured\nendothelial cells and exposed collagen fibers form deeper\nvessel wall.\nIts membrane has phospholipids that activates intrinsic\nsystem of blood doting.\nIts membrane has Adenyl Cyclase that causes formation of\ncyclic AMP that excites other activities of platelets.\nFormation of Platelet Plug\n\nPlatelets get adhered to the collagen of ruptured blood\nvessel and secrete adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and\nthromboxane A2. These two substances attract more and\nmore platelets and activate them. All these platelets\naggregate together and form a loose temporary platelet plug\nor temporary hemostatic plug, which closes the ruptured\nvessel and prevents further blood loss.\nPlatelet aggregation is accelerated by platelet activating\nfactor (PAF)\nImportance:\nPlatelet plug is important to close small rents in vessel wall.\nNatural Inhibitors of Platelet\nFunction\nThe release reaction is inhibited by substances that increase\nthe level of platelet cAMP.\nProstacyclin (PGI2), synthesized by endothelial cells, is one\nsuch substance, has the opposing effects of TXA2; it is a\npotent inhibitor of platelet adhesion and aggregation on\nnormal vascular endothelium (and a potent vasodilator).\nNitric oxide is constitutively released from endothelial cells\nand also from macrophages and platelets; it inhibits platelet\nactivation and promotes vasodilation.\nAnti-clotting factor:\nSurface of the cell: Cell membrane is covered by the glycocalyx\n(it makes the cell surface slippery), clotting factor slip the cell\nsurface and move forward.\nAntithrombin III: It prevents to convert the prothrombin to\nthrombin.\nInactivates the already made thrombin.\nProtein C and Protein S formed by liver\nPrevent to convert the prothrombin to thrombin.\nPrevent to convert the fibrinogen to fibrin.\nHeparin: It increases the activity of antithrombin III by 1000\ntimes\n\nPlasmin:  It breaks already formed clot.\nReleased by all the tissues.\nConvert the inactive plasminogen to plasmin.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
f18a5e65-3237-4ce0-91df-7f802ed19189	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	betablocker-and-calcium-channel-blockers	Betablocker and Calcium Channel Blockers	article	Beta blockers and calcium channel\nblockers overview:\nLabetalol: Labetalol is nonselective alpha and betablocker\ncommonly used to manage high blood pressure, especially in\nhypertensive emergencies\nLabetalol is used in the treatment of pheochromocytoma\ntogether with alpha blocker.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Antiarrhythmic drugs mechanism of action:\n> (High Yield)\n> Adenosine: Opens K+ channels in heart, slowing conduction\n> in the AV node.\n\nDigoxin Inhibits the Na/K ATPase in cardiac myocytes.\n\n![Betablocker and Calcium Channel Blockers - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/betablocker-and-calcium-channel-blockers/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Betablocker and Calcium Channel Blockers — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nFlecainide Blocks Na+ channel in heart, slowing conduction\nof the cardiac impulse.\nLidocaine Blocks Na+ channels in heart, slowing conduction\nof the cardiac impulse.\nSotalol: Blocks beta-adrenoreceptors and K+ channels,\nreducing heart rate and slowing conduction in the AV node.\nVerapamil Blocks Ca2+ channels in heart, slowing conduction\nin the AV node.\nAmiodarone: Blocks Na+ and K+ channels and beta\nadrenoreceptors prolonging phase 3 of cardiac action\npotential and slowing conduction at SA and AV nodes.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
24c2a612-4474-4d21-ab71-e5088619a94e	4e8e8cf6-923c-4953-ab05-58defec7082e	sickle-cell-disease	Sickle Cell Disease	article	Sickle cell disease:\nAutosomal recessive disorder\nType of normocytic anemia with high reticulocyte count\nClinical features:\nVaso-occlusive crisis: Most common crisis\nSickling causes blockage of small vessels\nCommon precipitants are infection, dehydration, acidosis and\ndeoxygenation\nAbdominal pain is caused by infarction affecting abdominal\norgans\nBone pain may occur in the back, pelvis, ribs and long bones\nInfarction may affect the central nervous system, lungs,\nspleen or kidneys\nHand-foot syndrome in children caused by infarction of\nmetaphyses of small bones is frequently the first\npresentation of the disease\nAcute sickle chest syndrome is the most common cause of\ndeath in adults and children (dyspnoea, falling arterial PO2,\nchest pain, pulmonary infiltrates on CXR)\nVisceral sequestration crisis : Sickling with pooling of red\ncells in the liver, spleen or lungs\nAplastic crisis :Usually following infection with B19\nparvovirus\nTemporary arrest of erythropoiesis, rapidly causing severe\nanaemia requiring transfusion\nIncreased susceptibility to infection\nSplenic function is reduced because infarction leads to\nhyposplenism\nPneumococcal infections may lead to pneumonia and\nmeningitis.\nInfarction of intestinal mucosa predisposes to Salmonella\ninfection and osteomyelitis.\nLab values:\nCBC= Normocytic anemia with raised reticulocyte count\n\nPeripheral film: Sickle cells, Howell- jolly bodies\nDiagnosis: HB Electrophoresis most accurate test	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
87e5530e-5d14-4d4e-9de1-feec61db0f62	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	sba-quiz-resuscitation-neutropenic-sepsis	Neutropenic Sepsis — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Neutropenic Sepsis — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 52-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for breast cancer presents to the ED with rigors and lethargy. Her temperature is 38.6C, HR 112 bpm, BP 104/62. Blood tests show an absolute neutrophil count (ANC) of 0.3 x 10^9/L. According to NICE guidelines (CG151), what is the immediate priority antibiotic therapy?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Neutropenic Sepsis)](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation/neutropenic-sepsis/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oral ciprofloxacin 500mg twice daily with home discharge\n- **B.** Immediate IV Piperacillin with Tazobactam (Tazocin) within 1 hour ('The Golden Hour')\n- **C.** Wait for blood culture and urine culture results before starting antibiotics\n- **D.** IV Vancomycin monotherapy\n- **E.** IV Metronidazole and oral co-amoxiclav\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nNICE CG151 mandates that suspected neutropenic sepsis is a medical emergency requiring immediate empirical broad-spectrum IV antipseudomonal beta-lactam monotherapy (first-line IV piperacillin/tazobactam - Tazocin) within 60 minutes of arrival ('door-to-needle time < 1 hour'). Do not delay for blood counts or cultures.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich of the following defines neutropenia in the context of oncology patients presenting with suspected neutropenic sepsis?\n\n- **A.** Absolute neutrophil count (ANC) < 0.5 x 10^9/L, or < 1.0 x 10^9/L with an expected fall\n- **B.** Total white blood cell count < 4.0 x 10^9/L\n- **C.** Platelet count < 50 x 10^9/L\n- **D.** Lymphocyte count < 0.8 x 10^9/L\n- **E.** Neutrophil percentage < 40% regardless of absolute count\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNeutropenic sepsis is diagnosed when a patient on active chemotherapy (or within 6 weeks) has a fever (>38.0C) or symptoms/signs of sepsis and an absolute neutrophil count < 0.5 x 10^9/L (or < 1.0 x 10^9/L that is predicted to fall below 0.5).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich risk stratification score is validated and recommended by ESMO/NICE to identify oncology patients with febrile neutropenia who may be candidates for early oral step-down therapy or outpatient management?\n\n- **A.** MASCC (Multinational Association for Supportive Care in Cancer) score\n- **B.** APACHE II score\n- **C.** Glasgow Coma Scale\n- **D.** Rockall score\n- **E.** CURB-65 score\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe MASCC score is used to identify low-risk patients with febrile neutropenia. A MASCC score >= 21 indicates low risk of serious medical complications, identifying potential candidates for outpatient oral antibiotic therapy following an initial period of ED assessment.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 60-year-old man with neutropenic sepsis and an indwelling tunnelled central venous line (Hickman catheter) remains persistently hypotensive despite 30 ml/kg fluid resuscitation and Tazocin. Examination reveals erythema and purulent discharge along the subcutaneous catheter tunnel. What antibiotic should be added immediately?\n\n- **A.** IV Vancomycin (or Teicoplanin) for MRSA and coagulase-negative Staphylococci coverage\n- **B.** Oral Fluconazole 200mg\n- **C.** IV Clindamycin alone\n- **D.** IV Ampicillin\n- **E.** Inhaled colistin\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE recommends adding a glycopeptide (IV Vancomycin or Teicoplanin) to Tazocin when there is clinical suspicion of central venous catheter-related infection, severe tunnel/port infection, suspected MRSA, or persistent hemodynamic instability.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhy must digital rectal examination (DRE), rectal temperature measurement, and intramuscular injections be avoided in patients with severe neutropenia?\n\n- **A.** Risk of inducing cardiac vagal reflex\n- **B.** Risk of mucosal trauma precipitating overwhelming systemic bacteremia / perirectal abscess, and severe bleeding from concurrent thrombocytopenia\n- **C.** They cause severe false elevations in neutrophil elastase\n- **D.** They interfere with PET-CT imaging\n- **E.** They cause allergic histamine degranulation\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n**Explanation:**\nMucosal barriers in neutropenic patients are thin and fragile from chemotherapy (mucositis). Rectal manipulations (DRE, suppositories, rectal thermometers) can breach the rectal mucosa, allowing gut flora (Gram-negative bacilli and enterococci) to enter the bloodstream and cause fatal septicaemia. Concomitant thrombocytopenia also risks severe haematoma from IM injections.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
0705a44a-66b5-4bad-834f-aaf55f2b7192	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-niv-guidelines	Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 67-year-old male with severe COPD presents with acute breathlessness and drowsiness. Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) on 28% Venturi mask reveals: pH 7.26, PaCO2 8.4 kPa (63 mmHg), PaO2 7.8 kPa (58 mmHg), HCO3- 32 mmol/L. According to British Thoracic Society (BTS) guidelines, what is the immediate respiratory intervention?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/niv-guidelines/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Initiate Non-Invasive Ventilation (Bilevel Positive Airway Pressure - BiPAP)\n- **B.** Increase oxygen to 15 L/min via non-rebreathe reservoir mask\n- **C.** Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) at 12 cm H2O\n- **D.** Immediate endotracheal intubation without trial of NIV\n- **E.** Administer IV sodium bicarbonate infusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS guidelines recommend NIV (BiPAP) as first-line therapy for acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (AHRF) secondary to COPD exacerbation when persistent respiratory acidosis (pH <7.35 and PaCO2 >6.0 kPa) persists despite optimal medical therapy and controlled oxygen for 60 minutes.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended initial pressure setting for Bilevel Positive Airway Pressure (BiPAP) in acute hypercapnic COPD exacerbation according to BTS guidelines?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/niv-guidelines/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** IPAP 10-12 cm H2O and EPAP 4-5 cm H2O, titrating IPAP upwards to 15-20 cm H2O\n- **B.** IPAP 25 cm H2O and EPAP 15 cm H2O immediately\n- **C.** IPAP 5 cm H2O and EPAP 10 cm H2O\n- **D.** Fixed CPAP 10 cm H2O without IPAP\n- **E.** IPAP 30 cm H2O and EPAP 2 cm H2O\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRecommended initial BiPAP settings: Inspiratory Positive Airway Pressure (IPAP) 10-12 cm H2O and Expiratory Positive Airway Pressure (EPAP) 4-5 cm H2O. IPAP is titrated upward in steps of 2-3 cm H2O every 15-30 minutes towards 16-20 cm H2O based on clinical tolerance, tidal volume (6-8 ml/kg), and repeat ABG.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich of the following represents an ABSOLUTE contraindication to initiating non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIV)?\n\n- **A.** Undrained tension pneumothorax or facial trauma/burns preventing mask seal\n- **B.** pH of 7.28\n- **C.** PaCO2 of 7.5 kPa\n- **D.** Mild confusion\n- **E.** Respiratory rate of 28/min\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAbsolute contraindications to NIV: undrained pneumothorax (positive pressure can convert simple into fatal tension pneumothorax), severe facial trauma/deformity preventing mask seal, bowel obstruction/severe vomiting (high aspiration risk), and inability to maintain airway.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhen should an arterial blood gas (ABG) be repeated after initiating Non-Invasive Ventilation to evaluate response and determine if treatment failure is occurring?\n\n- **A.** At 1 to 2 hours after commencing NIV\n- **B.** At 24 hours\n- **C.** At 5 minutes\n- **D.** Only when the patient is ready for discharge\n- **E.** ABG monitoring is not necessary if pulse oximetry is >90%\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS guidelines mandate a repeat ABG at 1 hour (and 4 hours) following initiation of NIV. Failure of pH and PaCO2 to improve, or worsening acidosis/sensorium, indicates NIV failure and should prompt immediate evaluation for invasive mechanical ventilation.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the key physiological difference between CPAP and BiPAP in emergency non-invasive ventilation?\n\n- **A.** CPAP delivers a constant single positive airway pressure throughout inspiration and expiration (ideal for type 1 failure / pulmonary oedema), whereas BiPAP delivers two distinct pressure levels (IPAP and EPAP) to assist ventilation in hypercapnic type 2 failure\n- **B.** CPAP requires an endotracheal tube whereas BiPAP uses a mask\n- **C.** BiPAP only delivers oxygen at atmospheric pressure\n- **D.** CPAP cannot be used in acute pulmonary oedema\n- **E.** BiPAP is contraindicated in COPD\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCPAP provides a continuous constant positive pressure that keeps alveoli open and improves oxygenation (type 1 respiratory failure, e.g. cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, atelectasis). BiPAP provides dual pressure: IPAP assists alveolar ventilation and clears CO2, while EPAP recruits alveoli (type 2 hypercapnic respiratory failure, e.g. COPD, neuromuscular weakness).\n	\N	[]	\N	f	33	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b9775870-7945-420b-9fb6-e3bf1f3a9280	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-pericarditis	Acute Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 32-year-old male presents with sharp retrosternal chest pain that worsens on inspiration and lying flat, and improves dramatically when sitting forward. Auscultation reveals a superficial, high-pitched scratching friction rub at the left lower sternal border. What is the classic 12-lead ECG finding in Stage 1 acute pericarditis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/pericarditis/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Widespread, concave-upward ST elevation with reciprocal PR depression in Lead aVR and widespread PR segment depression\n- **B.** Convex 'tombstone' ST elevation in leads II, III, aVF\n- **C.** Deep S wave in lead I, Q wave in lead III, inverted T wave in lead III (S1Q3T3)\n- **D.** Low voltage QRS complexes in all leads with delta waves\n- **E.** Biphasic T waves in leads V2-V3 only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nStage 1 acute pericarditis classic ECG features: widespread concave ST-segment elevation across almost all limb and precordial leads, with reciprocal ST depression in aVR (and V1), and pathognomonic widespread PR-segment depression (with PR elevation in aVR) reflecting atrial injury.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the first-line medical therapy recommended by ESC guidelines for acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis to reduce symptoms and prevent recurrent attacks?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/pericarditis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** High-dose NSAIDs (e.g. Ibuprofen 600-800mg TDS or Aspirin 750-1000mg TDS) plus Colchicine (0.5mg once or twice daily for 3 months)\n- **B.** High-dose systemic oral Prednisolone monotherapy\n- **C.** Intravenous Heparin infusion\n- **D.** Oral Warfarin targeting INR 3.0\n- **E.** Broad-spectrum IV Vancomycin\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFirst-line therapy for acute pericarditis: NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 600-800 mg TDS or Aspirin) combined with Colchicine (0.5 mg daily or BD based on body weight) for 3 months. Colchicine significantly halves the rate of recurrence and treatment failure. Corticosteroids are second-line because they increase recurrence rates.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 48-year-old male with malignant pericardial effusion presents with severe dyspnoea, lethargy, and cold extremities. Examination reveals hypotension (BP 82/56 mmHg), distended neck veins (elevated JVP), and distant, muffled heart sounds. What is this classic clinical triad termed?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/pericarditis/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Beck's Triad of Cardiac Tamponade\n- **B.** Virchow's Triad\n- **C.** Charcot's Triad\n- **D.** Cushing's Triad\n- **E.** Reynolds' Pentad\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBeck's triad of acute cardiac tamponade consists of: (1) Hypotension (decreased stroke volume); (2) Jugular venous distension (impaired venous return to the right heart); (3) Muffled, distant heart sounds (insulating layer of pericardial fluid).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat physical examination finding is defined as an abnormally large drop in systolic blood pressure (>10 mmHg) during normal inspiration, pathognomonic of cardiac tamponade?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/pericarditis/figure_04_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Pulsus paradoxus\n- **B.** Pulsus alternans\n- **C.** Pulsus bisferiens\n- **D.** Water-hammer pulse\n- **E.** Kussmaul's sign\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPulsus paradoxus is an exaggeration (>10 mmHg) of the normal physiological drop in systolic BP during inspiration. In cardiac tamponade, ventricular interdependence within the rigid pericardium causes inspiratory right ventricular filling to bulge the interventricular septum into the left ventricle, compromising LV stroke volume.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the emergency life-saving procedure of choice for a patient with cardiac tamponade presenting in obstructive shock and PEA cardiac arrest?\n\n- **A.** Emergency Pericardiocentesis (subxiphoid or ultrasound-guided apical approach)\n- **B.** High-dose IV Furosemide 120mg bolus\n- **C.** Immediate DC cardioversion 200J\n- **D.** Chest tube insertion into the right pleural space\n- **E.** Nitroglycerin sublingual spray\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCardiac tamponade is a mechanical obstructive cause of cardiac arrest ('4 Ts - Tamponade'). Emergency pericardiocentesis (aspirating even 20-50 ml of fluid) decompresses the pericardial space, restores diastolic ventricular filling, and can immediately restore cardiac output.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	35	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
64409412-85f1-4af5-bdb2-8393880ac19b	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-26	Quiz 26: Diving Physics, Musculoskeletal Trauma & Cardiovascular Drugs	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 26\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 19-Year-old girl presents to the ED with complaint of fever and non-painful pump on site of injury. She gives history of exposure to cats and scratch by young cats. Cat scratch disease is caused by which one of the following types of bacteria?\n\n- A. Gram negative cocci\n- B. Gram positive bacilli\n- C. Acid fast bacteria\n- **D. Gram negative bacilli** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Gram positive cocci\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBartonella henselae: Gram negative bacilli or coccobacilli\n\nCat-scratch disease (CSD) or felinosis is an infectious disease caused by Bartonella henselae most often results from a scratch or bite of a cat.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 24-year-old man self presents to ED following a fight in a nearby street. He has sustained multiple stab wounds and laceration medial compartment of thigh. Which one of the following nerves is most likely injured in adductor canal?\n\n- A. Tibial nerve\n- B. Obturator nerve\n- C. Femoral nerve\n- D. Sciatic nerve\n- **E. Saphenous nerve** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe adductor canal serves as a passageway for structures moving between the anterior thigh and posterior leg.\n\nIt transmits the femoral artery, femoral vein (posterior to the artery), nerve to the vastus medialis a nd the saphenous nerve - the largest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation in a patient who require ketamine. Which of the following are among the most common adverse effects associated with ketamine?\n\n- A. Bradycardia\n- B. Hypotension\n- C. Hyperkalemia\n- D. Hyponatremia\n- **E. Tachycardia** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nKetamine is an anesthetic, used to induce loss of consciousness. Ketamine is NMDA receptor antagonist Psychosis and tachycardia are among the most common adverse events associated with ketamine.\n\nContraindications: Head trauma, Raised intracranial pressure and hypertension\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 22-year-old professional marathon runner presents with shortness of breath. Which resting parameter would you expect to have a higher value when compared to an untrained patient?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Trained Athlete | Non- Athlete | |\n> | Stroke volume at rest: 100ml | Stroke volume at rest:70ml |\n> | Heart rate at rest: 50ml | Heart rate at rest: 70ml |\n> | Stroke volume at maximal exercise: 160ml | Stroke volume at: maximal exercise: 110ml |\n> | Heart rate at maximal exercise: 180ml | Heart rate at maximal exercise: 190ml |\n\n- A. Blood pressure\n- B. Oxygen consumption\n- C. Heart rate\n- **D. Cardiac stroke volume** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Cardiac output\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nYou see a 32-year-old woman in the resuscitation room who has presented with tachyarrhythmia. You decide to perform carotid sinus massage. At what level in the neck is the carotid sinus most likely to be found?\n\n- A. cricothyroid membrane\n- B. inferior border of 5th cervical vertebra\n- C. superior border of cricoid cartilage\n- **D. superior border of thyroid cartilage** (Correct Answer)\n- E. hyoid bone\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCarotid Sinus Massage is a Vagal maneuver transiently increase the arterial pressure in the carotidsinuses and aortic arch. This action triggers the baroreceptor reflex, which results in increased parasympathetic output to the heart via the vagus nerve and lowers the heart rate.\n\nThe carotid sinus Is located at the superior border of the thyroid cartilage, medial to the sternocleidomastoid muscle.\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 19-year-old man attends the ED after sustaining a head injury. He has a facial nerve palsy and a CT sacn sacn shows a fracture of the temporal bone. His facial nerve has most likely been injured at which foramen of the skull base?\n\n- **A. Stylomastoid foramen** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Jugular foramen\n- C. Foramen lacerum\n- D. Foramen ovale\n- E. Foramen Magnum\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe facial nerve exits the cranial cavity through t he internal acoustic meatus (Recall) and enters the facial canal of the temporal bone. The nerve then exits the skull through the Stylomastoid foramen (lie between the styloid and mastoid processes of the temporal bone) to enter the parotid gland, ultimately branching into five terminal branches to supply the face.\n\nThe facial nerve and its branches pass through the parotid gland, as does the external carotid artery, which gives off its two terminal branches, the maxillary artery and the superficial temporal artery, inside the gland. Deep penetrating wound to the parotid gland will damage these vessels (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 49 years old deep sea driver having decompression sickness. Which one of the following laws demonstrate mechanism of dissolved gases in deep sea drivers?\n\n- **A. Henry law** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Daltons law\n- C. Charles law\n- D. Ficks law\n- E. Boyles law\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHenry law states that the amount of dissolved g as in a liquid is proportional to its partial pressure above the liquid.\n\nSo, breathing gas at higher-than-normal pressures will cause their bodies to absorb more gases than are absorbed at lower pressures. Absorbed gases can cause decompression sickness as well a s toxic effects.\n\nHenry law is applicable in Scuva diving (Recall) or deep-sea diver\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 43-year-old overweight patient presents to ED complaining of fever and severe right upper quadrant pain. The pain radiates to her right shoulder tip. Murphy's sign is positive and you diagnose acute cholecystitis. Which of the following nerves is responsible for the pain referred to as the shoulder tip?\n\n- A. Intercostal nerves\n- B. Greater thoracic splanchnic nerve\n- **C. Phrenic nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Vagus nerve\n- E. Lesser thoracic splanchnic nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nMotor innervation: The phrenic nerve provides motor innervation to the diaphragm; the main muscle of respiration.\n\nSensory Functions Sensory fibers from the phrenic nerve supply the central part of the diaphragm, including the surrounding pleura and peritoneum. The nerve also supplies sensation to the mediastinal pleura and the pericardium (Pain of pericarditis)\n\nNote: Inflammation of gall bladder may present with pain in the right shoulder due to irritation o f phrenic nerve (Diaphragmatic peritoneum).\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 24-year-old man is stabbed in the abdomen with a kitchen knife. A CT demonstrates a diaphragmatic injury involving the esophageal hiatus.\n\nWhich structure is most likely to be damaged?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Opening | Vertebra level | Contents |\n> |  |  | Esophagus, anterior and |\n> | Esophageal opening | T-10 (Recall) | posterior vagal trunks |\n> |  |  | Aorta, thoracic duct (RECALL), |\n> | Aortic opening |  | Azygous and hemiazygos vein |\n> |  |  | Inferior vena cava (RECALL), |\n> | Caval opening | T-8 | Right phrenic nerve (RECALL) |\n\n- A. Aorta\n- B. Inferior vena cava\n- C. Thoracic duct\n- **D. Vagus nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Azygos vein\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 60-year-old man presents to ED complaining of difficulty passing urine and nocturia. On examination, you note the prostate is enlarged and you suspect BPH. Which of the following lobes of the prostate are most likely hypertrophied:\n\n- A. Peripheral zone\n- **B. Median lobe** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Lateral lobe\n- D. Anterior lobe\n- E. Posterior lobe\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nProstate gland is largest accessory gland of the male reproductive system. Histologically divided into three zones\n\n1: Central zone: surrounds the ejaculatory ducts\n\n2: Transitional zone: centrally and surrounds the urethra. The glands of the transitional zone are those that typically undergo hyperplasia (BPH) also median lobe of prostate most hypertrophied i n BPH.\n\n3: Peripheral zone: makes up the main body of th e gland and is located posteriorly. High incidence of prostatic carcinoma. This is zone palpated on PR examination.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nHow is aspirin beneficial in inhibiting platelets aggregation by blocking which of the following pathway?\n\n- A. Inhibition of binding of ADP to its platelet receptor\n- B. Inhibition of the breakdown of cAMP\n- C. Inhibition of the calcium release\n- **D. Inhibition of platelet thromboxane A2 synthesis** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Inhibition of GPilb/Illa receptor sites\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAspirin works as an antiplatelet agent by irreversibly blocking the enzyme cyclooxygenase-1 (COX -1) inside the platelets. This enzyme is necessary t o generate thromboxane A2, a potent platelet activator from arachidonic acid. Aspirin also inhibit s COX-2 which explains part of its anti-inflammatory properties.\n\nClopidogrel selectively inhibits the binding of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) to its platelet P2Y12 receptor, inhibiting the platelet adhesion and aggregation.\n\nAbciximab binds to the glycoprotein (GP) IIb/IIIareceptor of platelets and inhibits platelet aggregation.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 74-year-old man with a previous anaphylactic reaction to amoxicillin has had symptoms and signs of pneumonia with a CURB 65 score of 4. His blood cultures grew mycoplasma pneumonia.\n\nWhich single antibiotic is most appropriate for this patient??\n\n- A. Co amoxiclav\n- **B. Clarithromycin** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Ciprofloxacin\n- D. Meropenem\n- E. : Clavulanic acid\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nCURB-65 Score is used for Severity assessment of community acquired pneumonia (Recall)\n\nConfusion (abbreviated mental test, AMT scores 8)\n\nUrea (>7 mmol/L)\n\nRespiratory rate (2 30/min)\n\nBlood pressure (< 90 systolic or $ 60 diastolic) 65 (age >= 65\n\nRisk\n\nScore 0 to 1 low risk Score 2: Intermediate risk Score 3 to 5: High risk\n\nLoe severity CAP: Ist choice Amoxicillin\n\nHigh severity CAP: Ist choice Co-Amoxiclav\n\nNote: Clarithromycin is widely used alternative treatment in patients with penicillin allergy\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nWhat is the main mechanism of action of diltiazem?\n\n- **A. Blocks Ca+ channels** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Close the K+ channels\n- C. Opens the Ca+ channels\n- D. Blocks the Na+ channel\n- E. Opens the Na+channel\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDiltiazem and verapamil are non-dipyridamole calcium channel blocker\n\nFDA-approved Indications of diltiazem\n\nAtrial arrhythmia, including atrial fibrillation with the rapid ventricular rate (RVR)\n\nHypertension Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia Chronic stable angina Angina Due to Coronary Artery Spasm( Prinzmet al's angina)\n\nDihydropyridine calcium channel blockers include amlodipine, felodipine and nifedipine\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nWhilst in your surgical rotation your consultant performs appendectomy. What is the most common site for the appendix found at appendicectomy?\n\n- **A. Retrocecal** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Below terminal ileum overlying psoas\n- C. Pelvic\n- D. Retro-ileal\n- E. Anterior to terminal ileum\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe most common position of the appendix over all is retrocecal, followed by pelvic type.\n\nNote: Pus from ruptured appendix accumulatein right subphrenic space (Recall)\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 34-year-old presents to ER with severe right shoulder pain after a fall from a height. X-ray reveals a fracture in the middle third of the right clavicle. The medial end of the fractured clavicle is displaced upward due to traction by the?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Serratus anterior muscle\n- B. Subclavius muscle\n- C. Pectoralis minor muscle\n- D. Rhomboid muscle\n- **E. Sternocleidomastoid muscle** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nClavicle acts to transmit forces from upper limb t o axial skeleton.\n\nAfter fracture:\n\nLateral end is displaced inferiorly by the weight o f the arm and displaced medially by the pectoralis major Medial end is pulled superiorly due to traction by the sternocleidomastoid muscle.\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 26:** 1-D, 2-E, 3-E, 4-D, 5-D, 6-A, 7-A, 8-C, 9-D, 10-B, 11-D, 12-B, 13-A, 14-A, 15-E\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
4ce22461-6f8f-4bdc-98d6-211d8704c0ad	2187847b-4343-42ff-b157-7e4872bc597f	primary-quiz-27	Quiz 27: Upper & Lower Limb Trauma, Compartment Syndromes & Scaphoid Fracture	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 27\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nA 40-year-old male was playing basketball when he fell and landed on his right shoulder. He experienced immediate pain and had difficulty moving his arm. He was taken to the emergency room, where an X-ray showed that he had fractured the surgical neck of his right humerus. On examination he has loss of sensation over inferior portion of deltoid. Which one of the following muscles is likely to be injured?\n\n- A. Supraspinatus\n- B. Teres major\n- **C. Teres minor** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Subscapularis\n- E. Biceps Bracchi\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAxillary nerve injury is commonly caused by fracture of surgical neck of humerus or inferior dislocation of humerus.\n\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of th e arm. (Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but no t to a horizontal level).\n\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of th e deltoid (the 'regimental badge area'). Quadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery and vein pass through it and can be compressed as a result of trauma, muscle hypertrophy resulting in weakness of deltoid and teres minor.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 27-year-old runner is out for her morning jog when she steps on a rock and her left foot suddenly twists inward and she also sustained a lateral malleolus tear. Which one of the following ligaments is commonly torn in this injury?\n\n- A. Syndesmotic ligament\n- B. Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)\n- C. Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL)\n- D. Deltoid ligament\n- **E. Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInversion injury along with tear of lateral malleolus, tear of ATFL > CFL> PTFL Ankle ligaments\n\nEversion injury = medial ligament or medial deltoid ligament injury associated with tear of medial malleolus most commonly inversion injury= Lateral collateral ligament injured (subdivided into 3 ligaments) most frequent ligament injury during ankle sprain or inversion injury commonly affected ligament is Anterior talofibular ligament followed by calcaneofibular ligament (both are ligaments of lateral collateral ligament) associated with tear of lateral malleolus most commonly Least injured during inversion injury post talofibular ligament\n\nThe spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular ligament) is a broad thick ligament that extends from the calcaneus to the navicular bone. It supports the head of the talus, maintaining the stability of the talocalcaneonavicular joint and resists depression of the medial arch of the foot. The spring ligament takes the weight of the body from t he talus and transmits it to the forefoot.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nA 35-year-old patient presents to the clinic with a complaint of difficulty walking due to pain in the front of their lower leg. The patient reports that the pain started a few days ago after a strenuous workout session. On physical examination, you note tenderness and swelling over the tibialis anterior muscle. Injury of tibialis anterior muscle will result in loss of which movement of foot?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | * Origin: Lateral surface of |  |  |  |\n> |  | tibia |  |  |  |\n> | * Tibialis anterior | Insertion: Medial |  | ne |  |\n> |  | cuneiform and base of |  |  |  |\n> |  | Metatarsal 1 |  |  |  |\n> |  | * Origin: Medial surface of | o |  |  |\n> | * Extensor hallucis | fibular shaft |  | ree | * Deep fibular |\n> |  |  | toe and dorsiflexion |  |  |\n> | longus | Insertion: Distal phalanx | of foot |  | nerve |\n> |  | of the great toe. |  |  |  |\n> |  | * Origin: Lateral condyle of |  |  |  |\n> |  | tibia and medial surface |  |  |  |\n> | Extensor digitorum | fibula |  |  | Deep fibular |\n> |  |  | four toes and |  |  |\n> | longus | * Insertion: splits into 4 |  |  | nerve |\n> |  | tendons inserts onto each |  |  |  |\n> |  | toe |  |  |  |\n> |  | * Origin: Medial surface of | . |  | . |\n> | Fibularis tertius | fibular shaft |  | and |  |\n> |  | Insertion: Fifth metatarsal |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Dorsiflexion and eversion\n- B. Plantarflexion and inversion\n- C. Dorsiflexion and great toe extension\n- D. Plantarflexion and eversion\n- **E. Dorsiflexion and inversion** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAnterior leg consists of four muscles\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 32-year-old male presents to the ED with complain of difficulty gripping objects in his right hand. On examination, he had a weak grip strength in his right hand and was unable to flex his index and middle fingers. However, he was able to flex his ring and little finger. Which one of the following nerves is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Lesion | Damaged at elbow |  | Damaged at wrist |\n> | Mechanisms | Supracondylar fracture of the humerus | Lacerations just proximal to flexor retinaculum |  |\n> |  | The flexors and pronators in the forearm are paralyzed, with the exception of the |  |  |\n> |  | flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. The forearm |  |  |\n> |  | constantly supinated, and weak wrist flexion. | Paralysis of thenar muscles and lateral two |  |\n> |  | * Flexion, abduction and opposition of thumb is lost due to paralysis of thenar | lumbricals. This affects opposition of the thumb |  |\n> | Motor Loss |  |  |  |\n> |  | muscles and flexor digitorum profundus | and flexion of the index and middle fingers |  |\n> |  | * flexion of the index and middle fingers is lost due to paralysis of lateral two | respectively |  |\n> |  | lumbricals |  |  |\n> |  | Lateral aspect of palm and palmar surface and fingertips of lateral three anda | Palmar surface and fingertips of lateral three and |  |\n> | Sensory Loss |  |  |  |\n> |  | half digits | a half digits |  |\n> |  | The thenar eminence is wasted, due to atrophy of the thenar muscles. If patient |  |  |\n> | Signs | tries to make a fist, only the little and ring fingers can flex completely. This |  | eminence wasting |\n> |  | in a characteristic shape of the hand, known as hand of benediction. |  |  |\n\n- A. Ulnar nerve\n- B. Brachial plexus\n- C. Radial nerve\n- **D. Median nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Musculocutaneous nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nHigh yield median nerve injury\n\nNote: Flexion of the ring and little fingers at MCPJ and DIPJ are preserved as these are functions of medial half of flexor digitorum profundus and medial two lumbricals innervated by ulnar nerve\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nA 27-year-old patient presents to the emergency department with a gluteal stab wound that he sustained during an altercation. The patient reports difficulty moving his right leg and is unable to flex his knee. On physical examination, you note decreased sensation to light touch over the lateral aspect of his right leg and foot. Which one of the following nerves is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. Tibial nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Sural nerve\n- C. Common fibular nerve\n- D. Obturator nerve\n- E. Saphenous nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTibial nerve or posterior tibial nerve gives sensory innervation to posterolateral side of the leg, lateral side of the foot and heel (Plantar aspect of foot)\n\nTibial nerve or Posterior tibial nerve\n\nBranch of sciatic nerve. Arises at apex of popliteal fossa receiving nerve fibers from L4-S3. Functions:\n\nMotor: Innervates all the muscles in posterior compartment of the leg and the majority of the intrinsic foot muscles.\n\nSensory: In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve gives off cutaneous branches. These combine with branches from the common fibular nerve to for m the sural nerve. This sensory nerve innervates t he skin of the posterolateral side of the leg and t he lateral side of the foot and heel.\n\nNote: In summary, the tibial nerve provides both motor and sensory innervation to the lower leg a nd foot, in this scenario patient unable to flex his knee is because of tibial nerve injury while the sural nerve only provides sensory innervation to a small area of the lateral lower leg and foot.\n\nAlso note sensory innervation specifically to Achilles tendon and plantar foot is because of tibial nerve and sensory innervation to lateral aspect of lower leg and lateral heel is because of sural nerve (Arises from both tibial and common fibular nerve)\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nA 65-year-old man with a history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus presents to the emergency room with sudden onset right-sided weakness and facial droop. Upon examination, the physician notes right-sided hemiparesis, dysarthria, and neglect of the left side of his body. The patient reports a severe headache and dizziness prior to the onset of his symptoms. Which one of the following vessels is most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Blood vessel | Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness in lower limb, |\n> |  | dysarthria, dysphasia, apraxia and urinary |\n> | Anterior cerebral | incontinence |\n> | arter |  |\n> | y | Parietal lobe: contralateral somatosensory loss in |\n> |  | the lower limb |\n> |  | * Occipital lobe: contralateral homonymous |\n> |  | hemianopia with macular sparing, cortical |\n> |  | blindness (if bilateral) |\n> | Posterior cerebral |  |\n> | artery | * memory deficit |\n> |  | * Occipitotemporal region: prosopagnosia, and |\n> |  | color blindness |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness (face/arm |\n> |  | greater than leg), conjugate deviation of the eyes |\n> |  | to affected side, expressive dysphasia. |\n> | Middle cerebral |  |\n> | rt | Temporal lobe: deafness (if bilateral), receptive |\n> |  | dysphasia, auditory illusions and hallucinations, |\n> |  | contralateral superior quadrantanopia. |\n> |  | Parietal lobe: loss of sensory discrimination, |\n> |  | hemineglect, apraxia, contralateral inferior |\n> |  | quadrantanopia (RECALL) |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Blood vessel | Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness in lower limb, |\n> |  | dysarthria, dysphasia, apraxia and urinary |\n> | Anterior cerebral | incontinence |\n> | arter |  |\n> | y | Parietal lobe: contralateral somatosensory loss in |\n> |  | the lower limb |\n> |  | * Occipital lobe: contralateral homonymous |\n> |  | hemianopia with macular sparing, cortical |\n> |  | blindness (if bilateral) |\n> | Posterior cerebral |  |\n> | artery | * memory deficit |\n> |  | * Occipitotemporal region: prosopagnosia, and |\n> |  | color blindness |\n> |  |  Frontal lobe: contralateral weakness (face/arm |\n> |  | greater than leg), conjugate deviation of the eyes |\n> |  | to affected side, expressive dysphasia. |\n> | Middle cerebral |  |\n> | rt | Temporal lobe: deafness (if bilateral), receptive |\n> |  | dysphasia, auditory illusions and hallucinations, |\n> |  | contralateral superior quadrantanopia. |\n> |  | Parietal lobe: loss of sensory discrimination, |\n> |  | hemineglect, apraxia, contralateral inferior |\n> |  | quadrantanopia (RECALL) |\n\n- A. Internal carotid artery (ICA)\n- B. Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)\n- C. Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)\n- D. Basilar artery\n- **E. Middle cerebral artery (MCA)** (Correct Answer)\n\n**Correct Answer:** E\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms, the most likely affected vessel is the Middle cerebral artery. Th e MCA supplies blood to the lateral surface of the brain, including the motor and sensory areas responsible for movement and sensation on the opposite side of the body. The patient's right-sidedhemiparesis and neglect of the left side of his body are consistent with an MCA infarct\n\nMore easy to remember:\n\nACA: Contralateral weakness in lower limb MCA: Contralateral weakness in face/arm greater than lower limb\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nAn 18-year-old patient presents to the Emergency Department following an injury to the distal crease of her wrist. An image of her hand at rest is shown below. Paralysis to which of the following muscles is most likely responsible for this deformity?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Mechanism _ Fracture of medial epicondyle |  | Laceration at wrist |\n> |  |  | Only the intrinsic muscles of the hand |\n> |  |  | are affected. |\n> |  | All the muscles of innervated by the ulnar nerve are affected. | * Abduction and adduction of the |\n> |  | * Flexion of the wrist can still occur, but is accompanied by | fingers cannot occur (due to paralysis |\n> |  | abduction (due to paralysis of flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half | of the interossei). |\n> |  | of flexor digitorum profundus). | * Movement of the 4th and Sth digits is |\n> |  | * Abduction and adduction of the fingers cannot occur (due to | impaired (due to paralysis of the |\n> | Motor Loss |  |  |\n> |  | paralysis of the interossei). | medial two lumbricals and hypothenar |\n> |  | * Movement of the 4th and 5th digits is impaired (due to paralysis of | muscles). |  |\n> |  | the medial two lumbricals and hypothenar muscles). | Adduction of the thumb is impaired, |\n> |  | Adduction of the thumb is impaired, and the patient will have a | and the patient will have a positive |\n> |  | positive Froment's sign (due to paralysis of adductor pollicis). | Froment's sign (due to paralysis of |\n> |  |  | adductor pollicis). |\n> |  | Medial half of palm, palmar and dorsal surface of medialone anda | Palmar surface of medial one and a half |\n> | Sensory Loss |  |  |\n> |  | half fingers and medial dorsum of hand | fingers |\n> |  |  | Claw hand |\n> | Signs | Froment's sign | Froment's sign |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Mechanism _ Fracture of medial epicondyle |  | Laceration at wrist |\n> |  |  | Only the intrinsic muscles of the hand |\n> |  |  | are affected. |\n> |  | All the muscles of innervated by the ulnar nerve are affected. | * Abduction and adduction of the |\n> |  | * Flexion of the wrist can still occur, but is accompanied by | fingers cannot occur (due to paralysis |\n> |  | abduction (due to paralysis of flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half | of the interossei). |\n> |  | of flexor digitorum profundus). | * Movement of the 4th and Sth digits is |\n> |  | * Abduction and adduction of the fingers cannot occur (due to | impaired (due to paralysis of the |\n> | Motor Loss |  |  |\n> |  | paralysis of the interossei). | medial two lumbricals and hypothenar |\n> |  | * Movement of the 4th and 5th digits is impaired (due to paralysis of | muscles). |  |\n> |  | the medial two lumbricals and hypothenar muscles). | Adduction of the thumb is impaired, |\n> |  | Adduction of the thumb is impaired, and the patient will have a | and the patient will have a positive |\n> |  | positive Froment's sign (due to paralysis of adductor pollicis). | Froment's sign (due to paralysis of |\n> |  |  | adductor pollicis). |\n> |  | Medial half of palm, palmar and dorsal surface of medialone anda | Palmar surface of medial one and a half |\n> | Sensory Loss |  |  |\n> |  | half fingers and medial dorsum of hand | fingers |\n> |  |  | Claw hand |\n> | Signs | Froment's sign | Froment's sign |\n\n- A. Extensor digiti minimi\n- B. Extensor digitorum\n- C. Flexor digitorum longus\n- **D. Interossei and medial two lumbricals** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Flexor digitorum profundus\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nInterossei and medial two lumbricals are innervated by ulnar nerve\n\nClaw hand: Injury to the ulnar nerve is commonly caused by a fracture of the medial epicondyle and results in a claw hand, in which the ring and little fingers are hyperextended at the metacarpophalangeal joints and flexed at the interphalangeal joints.\n\nPartial claw hand deformity is due to an isolated ulnar nerve injury.\n\nComplete claw hand deformity: Involves all digits and results from combined ulnar and median ner ve injury.\n\nUlnar nerve injury at elbow and wrist\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nA 60-year-old man, fell down a flight of stairs and landed awkwardly on his left hip. He immediately felt a sharp pain in his hip and was unable to bear the weight on his left leg. He was taken to the emergency room, where X-rays revealed a fracture in his left femur and greater trochanter. Regarding greater trochanter of femur which one of the following muscles is inserted on it?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Origin of most important |  |  |  |\n> | lower limb muscles |  |  |  |\n> | lliac crest |  |  |  |\n> | Abdominal muscles |  |  |  |\n> | Rectus femoris |  |  |  |\n> |  |  | = | Superior corner |\n> |  |  |  | symphysis |\n> |  | ) |  | Ischial tuberosity |\n> |  | \\ |  | Hamstrings |\n> |  |  | Lesser trochanter |  |\n> | Note: Greater trochanter of |  |  |  |\n> | femur is site of insertion of |  |  |  |\n> | gluteus Medius, gluteus minims |  |  |  |\n> | and piriformis (RECALL) |  |  |  |\n\n- A. Quadratus femoris\n- B. Tensor fasciae Latae\n- **C. Gluteus Medius** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Gluteus maximus\n- E. Sartorius\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe greater trochanter of the femur serves as an attachment site for several muscles, including th e gluteus medius and minimus, which are important for hip stability and movement. The gluteus medius attaches to the lateral surface of the greater trochanter and functions to abduct and medially rotate the hip.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 35-year-old patient who works as a mechanic presents with complaints of numbness and tingling in the ring finger and little finger of his right hand. On examination, you suspect that he may have ulnar nerve entrapment at Guyon's canal. Which one of the following carpal bone is most likely injured?\n\n- A. Trapezoid\n- B. Lunate\n- **C. Hook of hamate** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Scaphoid\n- E. Capitate\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nFracture of both pisiform and hook of hamate ca n result into entrapment of ulnar nerve in Guyon's canal Ulnar canal (Guyon's canal)\n\nTransmits ulnar neurovascular bundle from forearm into the hand.\n\nBorders:\n\nMedial (ulnar): pisiform, flexor carpi ulnaris tendon Lateral (radial): hook of hamate.\n\nRoof: palmar carpal ligament.\n\nFloor: flexor retinaculum Contents: Ulnar nerve and artery\n\nClinical Pearl:\n\nUlnar canal syndrome refers to compression of th e ulnar nerve within the ulnar canal.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA 50-year-old patient named John has been admitted to the hospital with a hip fracture after a fall. He is scheduled to undergo surgery to repair the fracture the following day. The anesthesiologist has recommended a fascia iliaca block to manage his pain after the surgery. Which one of the following nerves is blocked by fascia iliaca rather than femoral block?\n\n- A. Saphenous nerve\n- **B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve of thigh** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Femoral nerve\n- D. Sural nerve\n- E. Sciatic nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBoth the fascia iliaca block and the femoral block are regional anesthesia techniques used to manage pain in the lower extremities. The fascia iliaca block involves the injection of local anesthetic bupivacaine into the fascial compartment located deep to the inguinal ligament, targeting the femoral, lateral femoral cutaneous, and obturator nerves. This block provides analgesia to the hip joint, femur, and anterior thigh.\n\nThe femoral block, on the other hand, targets only the femoral nerve by injecting local anesthetic around the nerve at the level of the inguinal crease. This block provides analgesia to the anterior thigh and knee.\n\nWhile both blocks can be effective in managingpain, the fascia iliaca block provides a broader area of analgesia and may be preferred for procedures involving the hip joint or for patients who have pain in the hip and thigh region. The femoral block may be preferred for procedures involving the anterior thigh or knee. The choice of block will depend on the specific needs of the patient an d the nature of the procedure\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\n17-year-old male presented to the ED following road traffic accident. On examination, he had penetrating wound over Achilles' tendon, and she had loss of sensation over posterolateral distal leg and planter foot.\n\nWhat nerve was injured in this injury?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> |  | Tibial nerve, Common | Lower posterolateral leg, heel and |\n> |  | fibular nerve | lateral foot (Little toe) |\n> | Medial calcaneal | Tibial nerve | Skin over the heel |\n> | nerve |  |  |\n> | Medial and lateral | Tibial nerve | Medial plantar: Innervates the plantar |\n> | plantar nerves |  | surface of the medial three and a half digits, |\n> |  |  | and the associated sole area. |\n> |  |  | Lateral plantar: lateral one and a half digits, |\n> |  |  | and the associated sole area. |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Cluneal nn. |  |\n> | Genitofemoral n. |  |\n> | Posterior femoral |  |\n> | cutaneous n. |  |\n> | Lateral femoral |  |\n> | cutaneous n. |  |\n> | Obturator n- |  |\n> | Anterior cutaneous |  |\n> | branches of femoral n. |  |\n> |  | ral n.) |\n> | Common peroneal n. |  |\n> | (Sciatic n.) |  |\n> | Sural n. |  |\n> | (Sciatic n.) |  |\n> | Superficial peroneal n. |  |\n> | (Sciade n) | Tibial n. |\n> |  | (Sciatic n.) |\n> | Deep peroneal n. |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Cluneal nn. |  |\n> | Genitofemoral n. |  |\n> | Posterior femoral |  |\n> | cutaneous n. |  |\n> | Lateral femoral |  |\n> | cutaneous n. |  |\n> | Obturator n- |  |\n> | Anterior cutaneous |  |\n> | branches of femoral n. |  |\n> |  | ral n.) |\n> | Common peroneal n. |  |\n> | (Sciatic n.) |  |\n> | Sural n. |  |\n> | (Sciatic n.) |  |\n> | Superficial peroneal n. |  |\n> | (Sciade n) | Tibial n. |\n> |  | (Sciatic n.) |\n> | Deep peroneal n. |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Sural nerve\n- B. Anterior-tibial nerve\n- **C. Posterior tibial nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Sciatic nerve\n- E. Common fibular nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nTibial nerve or posterior tibial nerve gives sensory innervation to posterolateral side of the leg, lateral side of the foot and heel (Plantar aspect of foot)\n\nTibial nerve or Posterior tibial nerve\n\nBranch of sciatic nerve. Arises at apex of popliteal fossa receiving nerve fibers from L4-S3. Functions:\n\nMotor: Innervates all the muscles in posterior compartment of the leg and the majority of the intrinsic foot muscles.\n\nSensory: In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve gives off cutaneous branches. These combine with branches from the common fibular nerve to for m the sural nerve. This sensory nerve innervates the skin of the posterolateral side of the leg and the lateral side of the foot and heel.\n\nSensory branches of tibial nerve:\n\nClinical pearls: Damage to the tibial nerve ca uses loss of plantar flexion of the foot and impaired inversion. This presents difficulty in getting the heel off the ground and a shuffling gait, resulting in characteristic clawing of th e toes and sensory loss on the sole of the foot, affecting posture and locomotion.\n\nNote: The nerve block that is commonly use d for pain management in heel injuries is the posterior tibial nerve block. (RECALL)\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 32-year-old male presents to the ED with complain of difficulty gripping objects in his right hand. On examination, he had a weak grip strength in his right hand and was unable to flex his index and middle fingers accompanied by loss of opposition of thumb. Which one of the following nerves is most likely injured?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Lesion | Damaged at elbow |  | Damaged at wrist |\n> | Mechanisms | Supracondylar fracture of the humerus | Lacerations just proximal to flexor retinaculum |  |\n> |  | The flexors and pronators in the forearm are paralyzed, with the exception of the |  |  |\n> |  | flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. The forearm |  |  |\n> |  | constantly supinated, and weak wrist flexion. | Paralysis of thenar muscles and lateral two |  |\n> |  | * Flexion, abduction and opposition of thumb is lost due to paralysis of thenar | lumbricals. This affects opposition of the thumb |  |\n> | Motor Loss |  |  |  |\n> |  | muscles and flexor digitorum profundus | and flexion of the index and middle fingers |  |\n> |  | * flexion of the index and middle fingers is lost due to paralysis of lateral two | respectively |  |\n> |  | lumbricals |  |  |\n> |  | Lateral aspect of palm and palmar surface and fingertips of lateral three anda | Palmar surface and fingertips of lateral three and |  |\n> | Sensory Loss |  |  |  |\n> |  | half digits | a half digits |  |\n> |  | The thenar eminence is wasted, due to atrophy of the thenar muscles. If patient |  |  |\n> | Signs | tries to make a fist, only the little and ring fingers can flex completely. This |  | eminence wasting |\n> |  | in a characteristic shape of the hand, known as hand of benediction. |  |  |\n\n- A. Musculocutaneous nerve\n- B. Radial nerve\n- **C. Median nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Brachial plexus\n- E. Ulnar nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 35-year-old construction worker reports pain and swelling in the anatomical snuff box of his right hand after falling off a ladder and landing on his outstretched hand. You suspect injury of structures in anatomical snuff box. The medial border of the snuff box is formed by the tendon of which muscle?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Anatomical Boundaries |  |\n> | * Medial border (Ulnar) | Tendon of extensor pollicis longus |\n> |  | * Tendons of the abductor pollicis longus and |\n> |  | extensor pollicis brevis |\n> | * Proximal border | Styloid process of radius |\n> | Distal border | 1st metacarpal |\n> |  Floor |  Scaphoid and trapezium bones |\n> | * Roof | Skin |\n> |  |  Radial artery, terminal portion of the superficial |\n> | * Contents | A |\n> |  | branch of the radial nerve, cephalic vein |\n\n- A. Opponent pollicis\n- B. Flexor pollicis brevis\n- C. Flexor pollicis longus\n- **D. Extensor pollicis longus** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Abductor pollicis longus\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAnatomical snuffbox\n\nis a triangular depression found on the lateral aspect of the dorsum of the hand. It is best seen when the thumb is extended.\n\nClinical Pearl:\n\nFracture of scaphoid bone: Falling on outstretched hand. The scaphoid bone of the hand is the most commonly fractured carpal bone\n\nClinical feature: Pain and tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox.\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nA 55-year-old man with a history of hypertension and high cholesterol presents to the emergency room with complaints of chest pain and shortness of breath. Upon examination, the physician notes ST segment elevation in leads V4-V6 of the electrocardiogram. The patient is diagnosed with a myocardial infarction (MI) of the lateral wall of the heart. Further tests confirm the occlusion of which artery?\n\n- A. Posterior descending artery (PDA)\n- B. Right coronary artery (RCA)\n- **C. Circumflex artery (LCX)** (Correct Answer)\n- D. Left anterior descending artery (LAD)\n- E. Left main coronary artery (LMCA)\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nEasy to remember:\n\nSeptal and Anterior wall MI: LAD (Anterior interventricular branch) occluded.\n\nLateral wall MI: LCX occluded.\n\nCardiac blood supply:\n\n1: The Right coronary artery supplies:\n\nThe right atrium and most of right ventricle The sinoatrial and atrioventricular node Posterior interventricular artery, also known as th e posterior descending artery (PDA), is a branch o f the right coronary artery supply posterior 1/3 of interventricular septum and posterior aspect of right and left ventricle. Most common artery infrac ted in posterior wall MI\n\nNote: Occlusion of right coronary artery can result into ischemia of SA and AV node and subsequently bradycardia or heart block\n\n2: The left coronary artery supplies via main branches below:\n\nAnterior interventricular branch (LAD)\n\nLCX\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 30-year-old farmer has sustained a deep laceration to the popliteal fossa. During examination your consultant ask what anatomical structure makes the upper lateral boundary of the popliteal fossa?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | Superolateral border | Biceps femoris |\n> | Inferomedial border | Medial head of gastrocnemius (Recall) |\n> | Inferolateral border | Lateral head of gastrocnemius and plantaris |\n> |  | The floor of the popliteal fossa is formed by the posterior surface of the knee |\n> | Floor | joint capsule, popliteus muscle and posterior femur. |\n> |  | The roof is made of up two layers: popliteal fascia and skin. The popliteal |\n> | Roof | fascia is continuous with the deep fascia of the leg. |\n> | Contents | Popliteal artery, popliteal vein, tibial nerve, common fibular nerve |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- **A. Biceps femoris muscle** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Tibial collateral ligament\n- C. Lateral head of the gastrocnemius muscle\n- D. Lateral epicondyle of the femur\n- E. Popliteus muscle\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nBoundaries and contents of popliteal fossa\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 27:** 1-C, 2-E, 3-E, 4-D, 5-A, 6-E, 7-D, 8-C, 9-C, 10-B, 11-C, 12-C, 13-D, 14-C, 15-A\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
7b8b610e-8ae4-4e69-a351-61f2c9b8fffa	c50aafa5-cef2-4f48-acdf-653d3cac2571	primary-quiz-08	Quiz 08: Respiratory Tract Anatomy, Facial Innervation & Metabolic Disorders	quiz	# MRCEM Primary Question Bank - Quiz 08\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer questions from the MRCEM Primary Basic Sciences Question Bank. Select the single best answer before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1\n\nWhat is the vertebral level of bifurcation of the trachea in a living person?\n\n- **A. T4/T5** (Correct Answer)\n- B. T6/T7\n- C. T2/T3\n- D. T3/T4\n- E. T5/T6\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe trachea extends from inferior end of larynx at C6 vertebra and ends at the level of sternal angle (T4-T5 Intervertebral disc) where it divides into left and right main bronchi.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2\n\nA 40-year-old male presents to ER with headache and hypertensive emergency. You prescribe sodium nitroprusside. What is the mechanism of action of sodium nitroprusside?\n\n- A. Alpha adrenoceptor agonist\n- B. Ca+ channel activator\n- C. Renin inhibitor\n- **D. Release nitric oxide** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Ca+ channel blocker\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSodium nitroprusside breaks down in circulation to release nitric oxide (NO).\n\nNitrates are potent coronary vasodilators. Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, causing increase in the intracellular concentration of cGMPin vascular smooth muscle cells. cGMP activates the protein kinase G, which ultimately cause vascular smooth muscle relaxation (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 3\n\nWhat is the vertebral level of bifurcation of the trachea in a living person?\n\n- A. T3/T4\n- B. T5/T6\n- **C. T4/T5** (Correct Answer)\n- D. T2/T3\n- E. T6/T7\n\n**Correct Answer:** C\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe trachea extends from inferior end of larynx at C6 vertebra and ends at the level of sternal angle (T4-T5 Intervertebral disc) where it divides into left and right main bronchi.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4\n\nA 32-year-old male presents to ED with ongoing continuous vomiting. He is several dehydrated. Which of the following hormone released in response to dehydration?\n\n- A. Oxytocin\n- **B. Antidiuretic hormone** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Somatostatin\n- D. Growth hormone\n- E. Parathyroid\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nADH reduces water loss via lowered urine volume. Extracellular dehydration (hypovolemia) stimulates specific vascular receptors that signal brain centersto initiate drinking and ADH release\n\nADH acts on kidney to promote water retention and on the vascular system cause vasoconstriction ADH also known as vasopressin.\n\n- ADH and oxytocin are released from posterior pituitary gland (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 5\n\nWhilst performing neurological examination, you ask medical students what is the sensory level of Umbilicus?\n\n- A. T9\n- **B. T10** (Correct Answer)\n- C. T7\n- D. T8\n- E. T11\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nT1= medial antecubital fossa (Recall) T2= Apex of axilla T3= third intercostal space T4= level of nipples (RECALL)\n\nT5= 5th intercostal space T6= level of xiphisternum (RECALL) T10= level of Umbilicus T11= midway between Umbilicus and inguinal ligament T12= midpoint of inguinal ligament\n\n---\n\n## Question 6\n\nAn elderly male patient presents to the ED with acute confusion. Lab value reveals Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia, which one of the following hormones is most likely deficient?\n\n- A. ADH\n- **B. Aldosterone** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Growth hormone\n- D. Thyroxine\n- E. Cortisol\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAldosterone deficit results in hyponatremia and hypovolemia, accompanied by hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis\n\nAldosterone acts mainly at renal distal convoluted tubule to cause sodium retention and potassium loss (RECALL)\n\nConn's syndrome (hyperaldosteronism) cause hypernatremia and hypokalemia (RECALL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 7\n\nA 38-year-old male brought into the ER having trauma to the face, his frontalis muscle is spared but the orbicularis oculi muscle is affected. Which of the following branch of facial nerve most likely involved?\n\n- **A. Zygomatic branch** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Cervical Branch\n- C. Buccal Branch\n- D. Mental Branch\n- E. Temporal branch\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nWithin the parotid gland, the facial nerve terminates by bifurcating into five motor branches. These innervate the muscles of facial expression: Temporal: Innervates the frontalis, orbicularis oculi (upper half) and corrugator supercilii.\n\nZygomatic: Innervates the orbicularis oculi. (Lower half)\n\nIn this case frontalis muscle is spared which is innervated by temporal branch, so here most likely affected nerve is zygomatic branch (lower half of orbicularis oculi)\n\nBuccal: Innervates the orbicularis Oris, buccinators and zygomaticus.\n\nMarginal mandibular: Innervates the depressor labii inferioris, depressor anguli orris and mentalis. Cervical : Innervates the platysma (RECALLL).\n\n---\n\n## Question 8\n\nYou have been asked to make a Quick assessment of patient's upper limb neurology as a part of secondary survey. You test each dermatome and note the patient has no sensation at the medial antecubital fossa. The medial antecubital fossa tests which of the following dermatomes?\n\n- A. C6\n- B. C8\n- C. C7\n- **D. T1** (Correct Answer)\n- E. C5\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDERMATOMES C2= occipital protuberance (RECALL) C3= supraclavicular fossa C4= Acromioclavicular joint (RECALL) C5 = lateral antecubital fossa C6= thumb (RECALL)\n\nC7=middle finger C8=little finger T1= medial antecubital fossa T2= Apex of axilla\n\n---\n\n## Question 9\n\nA 30-year-old male presents to the emergency department with limited mouth opening following a mandibular fracture. Which of the following muscles is primarily responsible for the opening of the mouth?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | e Lateral Pterygoid | e Depresses and protrudes mandible |  |\n> |  |  | e Opens the mouth |\n> | (anterior | e Depresses mandible |  |\n> | belly) |  | e Opens the mouth |\n> |  | e Elevates floor of mouth and assists in |  |\n> |  | depressing mandible | e Opens the mouth |\n> | e Geniohyoid | e Pulls hyoid bone anteriorly and assists | Opens the mouth |\n> |  | in depressing mandible |  |\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> | e Elevates mandible | e Closes the mouth |\n> | e Elevates and retracts mandible | e Closes the mouth |\n> | @ mandible | e Closes the mouth |\n\n- **A. Lateral pterygoid** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Sternothyroid\n- C. Temporalis\n- D. Medial pterygoid\n- E. Masseter\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSummarizing the muscles involved in mouth opening and closing, along with their functions:\n\n---\n\n## Question 10\n\nA Patient presents to the ED complaining of jaw pain which radiates towards his ear while eating, which of the following nerve is responsible for this sensation of pain?\n\n- A. Supra-Trochlear nerve\n- B. Zygomaticofacial Nerve\n- C. Infra trochlear nerve\n- **D. Auriculotemporal nerve** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Zygomaticotemporal nerve\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe Auriculotemporal nerve branch of mandibular innervates the skin behind the temporomandibular joint and within the superior surface of the parotid gland. It has a course along a temporalis superficialis and innervates the tragus (RECALL) and part of theadjoining auricle of the ear and the posterior part of the temple.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11\n\nA 17-year-old girl presents with a maculopapular rash. She has a seven-day history of fever and sore throat and was prescribed an antibiotic 3 days ago.\n\nWhich antibiotic is most likely to have been prescribed?\n\n- A. Amoxicillin\n- B. Cephalexin\n- C. Ciprofloxacin\n- **D. Phenoxymethylpenicillin** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Clarithromycin\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nIt is given primarily in streptococcal tonsillitis Phenoxymethylpenicillin also known as penicillin v is Used to treat bacterial infections, including ear, chest, throat and skin infection.\n\nIt is also used for prophylaxis against streptococcal infection following rheumatic fever and againstpneumococcal infection following splenectomy or sickle cell disease.\n\n---\n\n## Question 12\n\nA 65-year-old male passenger on a cruise ship presents to the ship's medical facility with fever, cough, shortness of breath, and muscle aches. He reports using the ship's hot tub several days ago. His symptoms began 3 days after boarding. Given the clinical presentation and the potential exposure source, what is the most likely causative organism?\n\n- A. Streptococcus pneumoniae\n- B. SARS-CoV-2\n- C. Influenza virus\n- **D. Legionella pneumophila** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Mycobacterium tuberculosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe patient's symptoms of fever, cough, shortness of breath, and muscle aches, along with recent exposure to the cruise ship's hot tub, raise suspicion for Legionella infection, which causes Legionnaires' disease. Cruise ships, with their complex water systems and potential for stagnant water, are a known risk factor for Legionella outbreaks, particularly when passengers use hot tubs or pools.\n\n---\n\n## Question 13\n\nA 60-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe epistaxis for the past 30 minutes. She is dizzy, has a heart rate of 115 beats per minute, and her blood pressure is 92/58 mmHg. What is the most likely cardiovascular consequence of this condition?\n\n- A. Increased preload\n- B. Bradycardia\n- C. Increased stroke volume\n- **D. Decreased stroke volume** (Correct Answer)\n- E. Increased cardiac output\n\n**Correct Answer:** D\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSevere epistaxis can lead to significant blood loss, resulting in decreased circulating blood volume and venous return to the heart, which decreases stroke volume. This can contribute to the patient's hypotension and tachycardia\n\n---\n\n## Question 14\n\nWhich of the following anesthetic agent cause vitamin B12 deficiency?\n\n- **A. Nitrous oxide** (Correct Answer)\n- B. Propofol\n- C. Etomidate\n- D. Thiopental sodium\n- E. Ketamine\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nExposure to nitrous oxide for prolonged periods, can cause vitamin B12 deficiency. Due to interference with the action of vitamin B12, resulting in megaloblastic anemia, neurologic manifestations such as myeloneuropathy and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord.\n\n---\n\n## Question 15\n\nA 40-year-old male is brought to the ER having sustained a stab wound to the posterolateral back. Which of the following muscle most likely affected?\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*\n> *(no readable text in the image)*\n\n- A. Rhomboid major\n- **B. Latissimus dorsi** (Correct Answer)\n- C. Trapezius\n- D. Serratus anterior\n- E. Anterior scalene muscle\n\n**Correct Answer:** B\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nLatissimus dorsi muscle is a broad, flat muscle that occupies the majority of the lower posterior thorax. The muscle's primary function is of the upper extremity but is also considered to be a respiratory accessory muscle\n\nlatissimus dorsi muscle is innervated by thoracodorsal nerve (RECALL)\n\nlatissimus dorsi produces adduction, medial rotation and extension of humerus at glenohumeral joint (RECALL).\n\n**Answer key, Quiz 8:** 1-A, 2-D, 3-C, 4-B, 5-B, 6-B, 7-A, 8-D, 9-A, 10-D, 11-D, 12-D, 13-D, 14-A, 15-B\n\n\n---\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.625966+00	\N
c92c8857-23d0-4905-a41d-b815510d5650	bb0e5b2d-ecab-4654-8aba-13849df3c95c	asthma-in-adults-management	Asthma in Adults Management	article	Levels of severity of acute asthma\nattacks in adults\nExample -1 : In exam, if they give you a scenario that a 34-\nyear-old asthma patient who is unable to complete a\nsentence in one breath, his HR is 115 beats/min and SpO2 91%.\nHow would you classify the severity of asthma in this patient?\nAnswer: Life-threatening asthma\nExplanation: Any patient with features of acute severe\nasthma along with any one feature of life-threatening\nasthma (as in above scenario his saturation is below <92%)\nwould be classified as having life-threatening asthma.\n\n![Asthma in Adults Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/asthma-in-adults-management/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Asthma in Adults Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nManagement of acute asthma in\nadults in the emergency department\nDischarge plan\nIn all patients who received nebulized β 2 bronchodilator prior\nto presentation, consider an extended observation period\nprior to discharge.\nIf PEF <50% on presentation, give prednisolone 40-50\nmg/day until recovery (minimum 5 days).\nIn all patients ensure treatment supply of inhaled steroid and\nβ 2 bronchodilator and check inhaler technique\nArrange GP follow-up within 2 working days post-discharge.\nFax or email discharge letter to GP\nRefer to asthma liaison nurse/chest clinic.\n\n![Asthma in Adults Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/asthma-in-adults-management/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Asthma in Adults Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
6b3e00c9-ac09-4a83-851d-17751e5f5e91	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	anticoagulant-drugs	Anticoagulant Drugs	article	Antiplatelet and anticoagulant\ndrugs\nAnticoagulant drugs\nHeparin:\nHeparin is used for:\nPrevention and treatment of DVT and Pulmonary embolism\nTreatment of disseminated intravascular coagulation .\nHeparin side effects:\nHeparin induced thrombocytopenia .\nBleeding\nOsteoporosis\nAlopecia\nHyperkalemia\nContraindications:\nPeople with active bleeding history or active peptic or\nduodenal ulcer\nPrevious history of Heparin induced thrombocytopenia .\n\n![Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anticoagulant-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLow molecular weight heparin vs Un\nfractioned heparin:\nWarfarin:\nWarfarin is vitamin K antagonist acts by inhibiting vitamin K\ndependent clotting factors (ii, vii, ix and x) or simply\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **remember 27910 (Mnemonic) as well as anticoagulant protein**\nC and S\nIndications:\nProphylaxis and treatment of venous thromboembolism\nProphylaxis in atrial fibrillation\nProphylaxis in people with Mechanical Heart Valves\nSide effects:\nBleeding is the most common adverse feature .\nNarrow Therapeutic Window: Warfarin has a narrow\ntherapeutic window, meaning that even small fluctuations in\ndosage can lead to a significant change in its anticoagulant\neffects. Maintaining the correct INR range is crucial for\noptimal benefit and to avoid complications.\nSkin Necrosis and Purple Toe Syndrome\nNausea, vomiting , Diarrhea, Rash, jaundice, hepatic\ndysfunction and pancreatitis\nWarfarin in pregnancy: If given in 1st trimester may cause fetal\nwarfarin syndrome (nasal hypoplasia, bone stippling, bilateral\noptic atrophy and intellectual disability)\nIf given in 2nd or 3rd trimester can cause optic atrophy,\ncataracts, microcephaly, microphthalmia, intellectual disability\nand fetal and/or maternal haemorrhage.\n\n![Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anticoagulant-drugs/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nContraindications:\nActive bleeding\nActive peptic ulcer\nBleeding disorders (ITP, Haemophilia)\nPregnancy\nRecent trauma or surgery\nMonitoring: Both PT and aPTT are prolonged after warfarin\nadministration but PT is more prolonged. Warfarin therapy is\ntypically monitored using the prothrombin time (PT) test\nalong with the International Normalized Ratio (INR), rather\nthan the activated partial thromboplastin time  (aPTT)  test.\nThe target International Normalized Ratio (INR) value for\nwarfarin therapy varies depending on the medical condition\nbeing treated. Warfarin therapy can be withhold or omitted if\nINR rises above the range. Phytomenadione (Vitamin K)\nantidote used to reverse the bleeding. Dried prothrombin\ncomplex consists of factors ii, vii, ix and x can also be given in\nmajor bleeding if prothrombin complex unavailable give\nfresh frozen plasma\n(High yield )\nManaging the patients with increased INR and bleeding\nIf INR> 8 and no bleeding = Phytomenadione orally\nIf INR> 8 and minor bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv\nIf INR> 8 and major bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv and dried\nprothrombin complex (Recall)\nIf INR 5-8 and no bleeding = Withhold warfarin\nIf INR 5-8 and minor bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv\nMetabolism: Warfarin undergoes extensive metabolism in the\nliver via the cytochrome P450 enzyme system, mainly through\nthe CYP2C9 enzyme.  cytochrome P450 enzyme inducer drugs\nincrease rate of metabolism and hepatic clearance of\nconcurrently administrated drugs which typically results in\ndecreased pharmacologic effects and decreasing\nanticoagulant effects of warfarin. While enzyme inhibitor\ndrugs decrease hepatic clearance and increase pharmacologic\neffect/increased anticoagulant effects and risk of bleeding.\n\n![Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anticoagulant-drugs/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
2ed175ee-9e9e-4d9d-a68d-9b704c356f70	9db23e52-af3e-4f01-9ef3-48e9c44fed87	anti-psychotic-drugs	Anti - Psychotic Drugs	article	Anti-psychotics\nAnti-psychotics acts by blocking dopamine D2 receptors in\nthe brain .\nSide effects:\nExtrapyramidal side effects occur most commonly associated\nwith antiemetic (metoclopramide, prochlorperazine) and anti-\npsychotics (haloperidol). Haloperidol is the most\ncommon causative antipsychotic drug.\nDystonia (abnormal face and body movements) most\ncommonly occur in young female and children treated with\nProcyclidine or benztropine .\nTardive dyskinesia (rhythmic, involuntary movements of\ntongue, face and jaw)\nOther side effects:\nParkinsonian symptoms (Tremor, Rigidity, bradykinesia and\npostural instability)\nHyperprolactinemia (Dopamine inhibits release of prolactin)\nQT-interval prolongation	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
80353fc4-d645-4595-a59e-3659f437202d	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	hemorrhagic-shock-massive-transfusion	Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion	article	# Haemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Class of shock Volume of blood loss (mi) Volume of blood loss (%) Heart rate Blood pressure Pulse pressure Respiratory rate Mental state Class I Up to 750 0-15% <100 Normal Normal or increased 14-20 Slightly anxious Class II 750-1500 15-30%\n- 100 Normal Decreased 20-30 Mildly anxious Class Ill 1500-2000 30-40%\n- 120 Decreased Decreased 30-40 Class IV\n- 2000\n- 140 Decreased Decreased Confused, Anxious, confused lethargic —Management of hemorrhagic shock\n- In the ED, priorities are: 1 restore intravascular volume 2 maintain oxygen-carrying capacity so that tissue oxygen delivery meets demand Z limit ongoing blood loss 4_ Prevent the development of coagulopathy and hypothermia\n- Follow ABC approach\n\n- lf spontaneous ventilation is not adequate, intubate and ventilate to achieve an arterial hemoglobin oxygen saturation of 294%.\n- Vascular access and monitoring\n- Establish two large bore 14-16 guage peripheral lines or intraosseous/central line if peripheral problematic.\n- Draw blood sample for cross match , FBC, blood gas analysis , toxicology study and pregnancy testing for all female of childbearing age. —Initial fluid replacement\n- Administer IL bolus of isotonic fluid in adult and 20ml/kg for paediatric patient\n- Observe the patient response to initial fluid administration and base further therapeutic and diagnostic decision on this response.\n- Early resuscitation with blood and blood product should be considered in patient with evidence of class Ill and IV hemorrhage.\n- urine output is one of the prime indicator of fluid resuscitation and patient response.\n- Adequate resuscitation should produce 0.5mI/kg/hr urine out in adult and Iml/kg/hr in children or 2ml/kg/hr in infant.\n- Base deficit or lactate level can be useful to determine the presence and severity of the shock —Packed red blood cells (blood replacement)\n- Those patients who doesn't or little response to initial fluid therapy, they require pRBC, plasma and platelet as an early part of their resuscitation.\n\n- W'hen possible, typed and cross-matched blood is preferable. However, if time and the patient's clinical status do not permit full crossmatching, type-specific blood is the next option, followed by low-titer O-negative blood.\n- Rh-ve pRBC are preferred for female of child bearing age\n- Some patient needs massive transfusion. It defines as unit pRBC within 24 hours or\n- 4 units in I hour. —Coagulopathy\n- severe hemorrhage leads to consumption of coagulation factor and early coagulopathy.\n- The administration of RBC units without additional clotting factors or platelets during massive transfusion results in further impairment of haemostasis from both haemodilution (dilutional coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia) and metabolic derangement (acidosis and hypocalcaemia from citrate in storage solution, and hypothermia from refrigeration). Acidosis and hypocalcaemia are detrimental to normal haemostasis. Furthermore, hypothermia is associated with impairment of both platelet and coagulation factor activity.\n- AII of these factor contributes to progressive coagulopathy.\n- Progressive coagulopathy can be prevented if other blood products should be added to massive resuscitation eg; FFP, Platelets in patient with haemorrhagic shock. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/hemorrhagic-shock-massive-transfusion/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/hemorrhagic-shock-massive-transfusion/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/hemorrhagic-shock-massive-transfusion/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
734d0b1f-70b7-46c9-9e84-571f1f65f96e	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-devastating-brain-injury	Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 50-year-old woman is brought to the ED after a severe traumatic brain injury. On arrival, she opens eyes only to painful sternal rub (2), produces incomprehensible sounds (2), and shows abnormal flexion (decorticate posturing) to pain (3). What is her Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/devastating-brain-injury/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** GCS 7 (E2 V2 M3)\n- **B.** GCS 9\n- **C.** GCS 5\n- **D.** GCS 11\n- **E.** GCS 3\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nGCS calculation: Eye opening to pain = 2; Verbal incomprehensible sounds = 2; Motor abnormal flexion (decorticate) = 3. Total GCS = 7. Severe traumatic brain injury is defined as GCS <= 8, mandating emergency airway protection (RSI intubation).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to Brain Trauma Foundation and NICE guidelines, what is the target Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) and Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) in the resuscitation of severe traumatic brain injury?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/devastating-brain-injury/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Target CPP 60-70 mmHg (avoiding systolic BP <100-110 mmHg)\n- **B.** Target CPP 30-40 mmHg\n- **C.** Target systolic BP 80-90 mmHg (permissive hypotension)\n- **D.** Target CPP >100 mmHg with aggressive vasopressors\n- **E.** Target ICP >30 mmHg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCPP = MAP - ICP. The Brain Trauma Foundation recommends maintaining CPP between 60-70 mmHg. Even a single episode of hypotension (systolic BP <90 mmHg, or <100-110 mmHg in older adults) doubles mortality in severe TBI. Permissive hypotension is strictly contraindicated in TBI.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich osmotherapy agent is indicated in the ED for acute intracranial hypertension with signs of imminent transtentorial herniation (unilateral dilated pupil, decerebrate posturing)?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Devastating Brain Injury & Prognostication)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/devastating-brain-injury/figure_03_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hypertonic Saline (e.g. 3% or 5% NaCl 250-500ml bolus) or Mannitol 20% (0.5-1.0 g/kg)\n- **B.** Dextrose 5% in water 1000ml rapid bolus\n- **C.** 0.45% half-normal saline infusion\n- **D.** Intravenous furosemide 10mg monotherapy\n- **E.** Oral glycerol\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOsmotherapy draws free water from cerebral parenchyma into the intravascular space, rapidly lowering ICP. Hypertonic saline (3% or 5%) or 20% Mannitol (0.5-1.0 g/kg) is first-line for acute herniation. Hypertonic saline is preferred in hypotensive patients as it expands plasma volume without inducing osmotic diuresis.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended target arterial PaCO2 when using temporary hyperventilation as a rescue measure for acute cerebral herniation in severe TBI?\n\n- **A.** PaCO2 4.0 - 4.5 kPa (30 - 35 mmHg) for brief periods only\n- **B.** PaCO2 < 2.5 kPa (< 20 mmHg) for 24 hours\n- **C.** PaCO2 6.5 - 7.5 kPa (hypercapnia)\n- **D.** PaCO2 does not affect cerebral blood flow\n- **E.** Target PaCO2 5.5 - 6.0 kPa\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHypocapnia induces cerebral arteriolar vasoconstriction, lowering cerebral blood volume and ICP. However, prolonged or aggressive hyperventilation (PaCO2 <4.0 kPa / <30 mmHg) causes profound cerebral ischaemia. Guidelines mandate brief, controlled mild hyperventilation (PaCO2 4.0-4.5 kPa / 30-35 mmHg) only as a temporary bridge to definitive surgical decompression.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nIn the UK Academy of Medical Royal Colleges (AoMRC) code of practice for the diagnosis of death using neurological criteria (brainstem death testing), which prerequisite condition must be rigorously fulfilled prior to testing?\n\n- **A.** Irreversible brain damage of known etiology, exclusion of reversible metabolic/endocrine disturbances, normothermia (>34C), and complete exclusion of depressant drugs / neuromuscular blockade\n- **B.** Flat EEG for 48 hours is mandatory\n- **C.** Cerebral angiography must show absent flow in all four vessels\n- **D.** Patient must be aged over 18 years\n- **E.** Serum potassium must be >5.5 mmol/L\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAoMRC prerequisites for brainstem death testing: (1) Apnoeic coma of known irreversible cause; (2) Exclusion of reversible causes: core temperature >34C, no intoxication/recreational drugs, no residual neuromuscular blocking or sedative agents (verified by train-of-four / adequate drug clearance time), and no severe electrolyte, acid-base, or endocrine abnormalities.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f2f5f5b2-2183-4e20-a862-138a17c92cef	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	maxilofascial-trauma	Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma	article	# Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- lf the patient has got the missing tooth with them, further questions to ask are:\n- What medium was the tooth kept in (ideal medium is saliva or milk)?\n- ls the patient aware if this is an adult or a baby tooth?\n- If-thepatjent-js-not-aware-where-the-mjssin following considerations should be thought_Qf;\n- if there are deep lacerations to the lips or cheeks, it is important to consider that the fragment may have become lodged in these areas. A soft tissue x-ray can help identify if there are any foreign bodies in the lips\n- lf there is a risk of the teeth and/or teeth fragments being aspirated, it is worth considering taking a chest x-ray to exclude this. As enamel is the most calcified structure in the human body, it often appears quite easily on an x-ray.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\n- CXR: Used to exclude aspiration risk when patient is unaware of where a missing tooth or tooth fragment is.\n- Soft tissue view: Useful to exclude fragments of teeth becoming lodged within deep lacerations to the lips, cheeks and face.\n- Orthopantomogram (OPT or OPG): Can be useful if doubt exists over whether tooth is a deciduous (baby) or adult tooth, and useful to assess degree of damage to dental and periodontal structures.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- Immediate manaqement of avulsed tooth hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth. The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable medium (saliva or milk) before re-implantation. Primarv teeth manaqement If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldn't be re-implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth. f the primary teeth are very loose and present a potential airway risk, then they should be removed Adult tooth management Adult Luxated intruded and extruded teeth should be replaced into the correct position, and a splint(wire splint or silk suture splints) should be used to stabilize them and arrange follow-up with dentist. Avulsed adult teeth should be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth. saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried out. Tetanus booster should be consider and antibiotic (tetracycline or penicillin V) should be recommended patients advised to rinse with chlorhexidine 0.1% mouthwash for 1-2 weeks Eygomaticomaxillary Complex (ZMC) fractures\n- ZMC fractures, also known as tripod, tetrapod, quadripod, malar or trimalar fractures.\n- functionally it is more correct to consider it as having four legs, like a four-legged stool ponding-to-the-four-legs.-Q.f-the-stQQl-ar.e.2\n- Lateral orbital rim\n- lnferior orbital rim\n- Zygomaticomaxillary buttress — the thickened area of bone forming the lateral wall of the maxillary sinus\n\n- Zygomatic arch —MOI: blow to the side of the face from a fist, from an object, or secondary to motor vehicle accidents.\n- Initial assessment\n- Follow ABDE approach\n- The airway is of particular concern, either from direct injury or from complications of local trauma, bleeding, swelling or foreign body\n- Allowing a conscious patient to sit up may help maintain airway patency and reduce the risk of aspiration (only if no spinal injuries are present).\n- left lateral position may be required in patient with spinal injury\n- Prompt haemostasis of external bleeding may involve direct pressure, sutures or staples.\n- lnternal haemorrhage control may require packing, balloon tamponade, temporary reduction of fractures, or either embolization or surgical ligation of bleeding vessels.\n- AII maxillofacial traurna patients should be considered high risk for cervical spine injuries. —On physical examination, flattening of the malar eminence will be noted in the absence of often significant swelling\n- The position of the eye and visual acuity r\n- nust be checked.\n- Enophthalmos and proptosis both indicate a significant orbital injury. Features suggesting progressive retrobulbar haemorrhage require an emergency\n\ndecompression (lateral canthotomy).\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\n- ;\n- Request Occipitomental 150 (OM15) or occipitomental 300 (OM30) views\n- SubmentoverticaI view\n- CT scan is far more accurate at identifying ZMC and zygomatic arch fractures, its use in the ED is restricted to either a second line investigation, usually initiated by the maxillofacial team ZManagement;\n- Most of ZMC fracture can be manage expectantly until local swelling subsides and and reviewed by a maxillofacial surgeon.\n- urgent referral to maxillofacial surgeon or ophthalmologist if patient with reduced visual acuity or diplopia due to orbital involvement.\n- Advise patient avoidance of nose blowing as this may produce surgical emphysema, not to occlude the nose when sneezing and application of ice packs to the area to reduce swelling to infraorbital nerve\n\n- e Eygomatic arch fractures\n- The zygomatic arch is predominantly formed by the zygomatic process of the temporal bone which articulates with the much smaller temporal process of the zygoma forming the arch. —MOI: Assault, motor vehicle or motorcycle accidents —Clinical features: patient may have alteration in bite or difficulty moving the jaw.\n- Sensory disturbance to the cheek and upper gum due to infraorbital nerve injury. —Investigation: Request Submentovertical view or lateral facial view\n- FaciaI CT is needed to define the extent of the injury. —Management: mostly patient are manage conservatively and follow-up\n\n- Urgent referral to maxillofacial surgeon if patient has restriction to mouth opening due to trapping of the temporalis muscle or mandibular condyle.\n- Advise patient avoidance of nose blowing as this may produce surgical emphysema, not to occlude the nose when sneezing and application of ice packs to the area to reduce swelling Submentovertex view\n- Orbital blow out fracture\n\n- —MOI: Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly from a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a fracture of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents into the maxillary sinus. —Clinical features: patient may have diplopia and can't look up and medially due to inferior rectus entrapment.\n- Test infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital nerve commonly damage in this injury. —Investigation: X-ray will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema. —Management: Urgently Refer all patient with orbital blow-out fracture (e.g. diplopia or reduced eye movements) to a maxillofacial surgeon and/or an ophthalmologist\n- facial CT scan will be needed to visualise the fracture in detail and plan surgical repair.\n\n- Nasal bone fracture\n- The nasal bones are easily fractured due to them being broad and flat, however, proximal nasal bone fracture may be accompanied by fractures of adjacent bones which may lead to CSF rhinorrhoea (due to involvement of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone) and orbital emphysema from fracture of the orbital plate. —Clinical features: Epistaxis, Septal haematoma, nasal discharge eg CSF rhinorrhoea and supraorbital or supratrochlear nerve paraesthesia. —Diagnosis: Nasal fracture is a clinical diagnosis and displaced fractures must be reduced before 14 days. —Management: referral to otolaryngologist if patient has following features:\n- UncontroIIed haemorrhage, septal haematoma/ abscess or CSF rhinorrhoea\n- Refe.r.raLtQ-o.tQ.laryngQ.logist-wj.thinüzlQ-days.-Q.f-jnjury-if-e.t-wjth-fQ.llowj.ng features: obvious new deformity or new septal deviation.\n- 0ther all manage conservatively and arrange otolaryngology follow-up within 7 to IO days.\n- Mid facial fractures/ Le Fort facial fractures\n- MOI: Midfacial fractures can be caused by motor vehicle crashes, sports, assault, and falls due to seizures or intoxication or in the elderly.\n- Le Fort fractures lie between the frontal bone, the skull base, and the mandible. They involve the upper jaw, teeth, nose, and maxillary and ethmoid air sinuses.\n\n—Le Fort I is a transverse fracture separating the body of the maxilla from the pterygoid plate and nasal septum. Only the hard palate and teeth move, similar to a loose upper denture. —Le Fort II is a pyramidal fracture through the central maxilla and hard palate. Movement of the hard palate and nose occurs, but not the eyes. —Le Fort III is craniofacial dysjunction when the entire face is separated from the skull from fractures of the frontozygomatic suture line, across the orbit and through the base of the nose and ethmoids. the entire face shifts with the globes held in place only by the optic nerve. —Le Fort IV fracture includes characteristics of the Le Fort Ill and also involves the frontal bone.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n- patient often present with significant hemorrhage, early swelling, bilateral orbital ecchymosis, and cerebrospinal fluid leaks in Le Fort II and Ill injuries. —Investigation: CT scan of the face with coronal and axial slices with three- dimensional reconstructions. —Management:\n- Resuscitate and establish a clear airway:\n- patient often present with significant haemorrhage, requiring nasal or oral packing.\n- Refer all patients with middle third or naso-ethmoidal fractures to the maxillofacial surgeons for admission.\n\n- Discuss patients with CSF leaks with the neurosurgeons\n- CIean and dress compound facial lacerations and may need formal debridement and they provide access to fractures for open reduction and internal fixation.\n\n- Mandibular fracture\n- Mandible fractures are the second rnost common facial fracture after nasal fractures. —MOI: Assaults, motor vehicle crashes and falls.\n- The mandible may be fractured at a site distant from the point of impact eg a fall on the chin may cause condylar fractures. —Clinical features:\n- Pain aggravated by jaw rnovement or biting.\n- patient may present with swelling, tenderness and intraoral bruising.\n- Numbness of the lower lip indicates injury to the inferior dental nerve. —Investigation:\n- Request an orthopantomogram (OPG). —Management:\n- Manage undisplaced fractures not involving teeth with analgesia , soft diet, prophylaxis antibiotic (penicillin , co-amoxiclav), tetanus cover and arrange outpatient follow-up with maxillofacial surgeon.\n- Refer displaced or multiple fractures, bilateral condyle fractures or single fractures with malocclusion or deviation of the jaw on opening to on-call maxillofacial surgeon.\n\n- Temporomandibular joint dislocation\n- Anterior dislocations are the rnost common, however, it can be uni- or bilateral. —MOI: Acute dislocations can be seen after trauma or dystonic reactions, but they are usually a result of extrerne mouth opening such as with yawning. —Clinical features:\n- Pain over the temporal fossa\n- Patient cannot close the mouth.\n- The jaw protrudes anteriorly, and difficulty in swallowing leads to drooling of saliva\n\n- —Investigation: obtain x-ray incase of direct traurna only. —Management:\n- Reduce the dislocated joint\n- After reduction, advise the patient to take a soft diet and not to yawn or open the mouth widely for 24hr previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Maxilofascial Trauma - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/maxilofascial-trauma/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Maxilofascial Trauma — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Maxilofascial Trauma - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/maxilofascial-trauma/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n*Maxilofascial Trauma — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Maxilofascial Trauma - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/maxilofascial-trauma/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n*Maxilofascial Trauma — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Maxilofascial Trauma - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/maxilofascial-trauma/figure_04_p14.jpeg)\n*Maxilofascial Trauma — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 14 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
60d14e6c-79e6-486e-9071-d33535c9168d	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	renal-cancer-bladder-cancer	Renal & Bladder Malignancies	article	# Renal & Bladder Malignancies\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d0acaac2-4d0a-49f1-8ea5-76e01b51352a	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-renal-cancer-bladder-cancer	Renal & Bladder Malignancies — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Renal & Bladder Malignancies — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 64-year-old male smoker presents with intermittent, painless macroscopic (gross) haematuria for 3 weeks. He has no dysuria, fever, or loin pain. What is the single most urgent investigation indicated to exclude bladder cancer?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Renal & Bladder Malignancies)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/renal-cancer-bladder-cancer/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Urgent 2-week-wait urology referral for Flexible Cystoscopy and CT Urogram\n- **B.** Course of oral trimethoprim for 7 days\n- **C.** Repeat urine dipstick in 3 months\n- **D.** Reassurance that painless haematuria is benign\n- **E.** Serum PSA level alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPainless gross (macroscopic) haematuria in an adult (especially aged >45 or with smoking history) is considered urothelial malignancy until proven otherwise. NICE guidelines mandate urgent 2-week-wait cancer referral for flexible cystoscopy (to visualize the bladder urothelium) and contrast CT urography (to assess the upper urinary tracts).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic clinical triad of Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC), although found in less than 10% of patients and denoting advanced disease?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Renal & Bladder Malignancies)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/renal-cancer-bladder-cancer/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Flank / loin pain, Palpable abdominal/flank mass, and Haematuria\n- **B.** Jaundice, Ascites, and Caput medusae\n- **C.** Fever, Dysuria, and Frequency\n- **D.** Weight loss, Polyuria, and Polydipsia\n- **E.** Hypertension, Headache, and Diaphoresis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic 'Too Late Triad' of Renal Cell Carcinoma is: (1) Flank pain; (2) Palpable renal mass; (3) Gross or microscopic haematuria. Most renal cell carcinomas are now detected incidentally on abdominal cross-sectional imaging for other indications.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 58-year-old man presents with a newly developed, non-tender left-sided varicocele that fails to decompress or empty when the patient assumes the supine position. What underlying pathology must be urgently excluded?\n\n- **A.** Renal Cell Carcinoma invading the left renal vein and obstructing the left testicular vein\n- **B.** Inguinal hernia\n- **C.** Epididymal cyst\n- **D.** Benign testicular hydrocele\n- **E.** Torsion of the appendix testis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe left gonadal (testicular) vein drains at a right angle directly into the left renal vein (unlike the right testicular vein, which drains directly into the IVC). An acute, non-reducible left varicocele in a middle-aged/older man is a classic red flag for a left renal cell carcinoma with tumor thrombus extension into the left renal vein obstructing venous outflow.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich histological subtype represents approximately 75-80% of all primary Renal Cell Carcinomas and is classically associated with mutations in the von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) tumour suppressor gene on chromosome 3p?\n\n- **A.** Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma\n- **B.** Papillary RCC (chromophilic)\n- **C.** Chromophobe RCC\n- **D.** Collecting duct carcinoma\n- **E.** Renal oncocytoma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nClear cell RCC is the predominant histological subtype (75-80%). Inactivation or mutation of the VHL gene on chromosome 3p causes accumulation of Hypoxia-Inducible Factor (HIF), driving overexpression of VEGF and PDGF, making clear cell RCC a highly vascular tumor responsive to tyrosine kinase inhibitors and immunotherapy.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common histological type of Bladder Cancer in the UK and Western Europe, and what is its most important modifiable risk factor?\n\n- **A.** Transitional Cell Carcinoma (Urothelial Carcinoma); Cigarette smoking\n- **B.** Squamous Cell Carcinoma; Schistosomiasis\n- **C.** Adenocarcinoma; Bladder extrophy\n- **D.** Small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma; Alcohol\n- **E.** Sarcoma; Radiotherapy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nUrothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma accounts for >90% of bladder cancers in Western countries. Cigarette smoking is by far the leading risk factor, responsible for approximately 50% of cases due to aromatic amines and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons excreted in the urine.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e91fc407-40f6-4e25-9f2f-bd37a723e195	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	tachyarrhythmia	Tachyarrhythmia	article	Tachycardias\nTwo Types\nRegular or irregular narrow complex\nRegular or Irregular broad complex\n1small box= 0.04s\n1Large box=0.20s\nPR interval 0.12-0.20\nIn narrow complex tachycardia QRS <0.12s\nIn broad complex QRS>0.12s\nNote: Adenosine is a purine nucleoside acts by stimulating A1-\nadenosine receptors and opening acetylcholine-sensitive\npotassium channels. This hyperpolarizes the cell membrane in\nthe atrio-ventricular (AV) node and, by inhibiting the calcium\nchannels, slows conduction in the AV node.\nContraindications of adenosine (High yield)\nAsthma and COPD\n2nd or 3rd degree AV block\nSick sinus syndrome\nLong QT syndrome\nSevere hypotension\nDecompensated heart failure\n\n![Tachyarrhythmia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/tachyarrhythmia/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Tachyarrhythmia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nTachycardia Unstable Pt\nAdverse features\nShock (SBP<90, confusion, drowsiness, cold extremities,\npallor).\nSyncope (loss of consciousness)\nMyocardial ischemia (Chest pain, breathlessness, ECG\nchanges)\nHeart failure (Pulmonary edema, raised JVP, Hepatomegaly).\nExtreme heart rate: >150 mostly Pt are unstable.\nManagement\nGive 3 synchronized DC shocks (120-150j for broad complex\nand 70-120 for narrow complex)\nIf not successful, then administer Amiodarone 300mg over 10\nto 20 minutes and repeat the shock.\nFollowed by 900mg over 24hrs.\nNote: Any unstable patient requires electrical cardioversion\nrather than drugs regardless of the underlying rhythm.\nAtrial fibrillation managment\nAF is diagnosed using an ECG which typically shows an\nabsolutely irregular RR interval and absent p waves.\nNICE guideline classification\nParoxysmal AF\nAF terminates spontaneously within seven days of onset and\nmost often within 48 hours of onset.\nPersistent AF\nAF presents continuously for seven days or more or is\nterminated by cardioversion.\nPermanent AF\nAF which is accepted without attempted cardioversion, or\nwhich cannot be terminated by cardioversion.\nPathophysiology\n\nThe primary pathological change seen in AF is progressive\natrial fibrosis.\nProthrombotic state: Structural and functional changes in\nthe atrial myocardium along with stasis of blood especially in\nthe Left atrial appendage allow an environment in which a\nthrombus can form.\nDamage to the myocardium causes the expression of\nprothrombotic factors on the atrial endothelial surface. This\nalong with activation of platelets and inflammatory cells\nleads to clot formation.\nClinical features\nbreathlessness\npalpitations\nsyncope or dizziness\nchest discomfort\nstroke or transient ischaemic attack\nManagement of hemodynamically-\nunstable AF\nDC shock 120-150J should be given if a patient presents\nwith the following adverse features:\nShock\nSyncope/reduce the level of consciousness.\nHeart failure\nMyocardial ischemia\nManagement of previously-\nidentified AF\nIn patients presenting to the ED who are known to have AF\nlong-term (>48 hours). a reason for why they are tachycardic\nshould be sorted. If a secondary/reversible cause has been\nexcluded then rate control should be considered\nNICE recommends rate control (beta blockers and rate-\nlimiting CCB) as the first-line strategy for people with atrial\nfibrillation, except in people:\nwhose atrial fibrillation has a reversible cause.\n\nwho have heart failure thought to be primarily caused by\natrial fibrillation.\nwith new-onset atrial fibrillation (<48hrs)\nFor whom a rhythm control strategy would be more suitable\nbased on clinical judgment\nNote: digoxin can be used in sedentary patients.\nManagement of Recent-onset, Non-\nLife-threatening AF\nIn the ED if a patient presents within 48 hrs. of onset\nconsider either pharmacological or electrical cardioversion.\nElectrical: synchronized DC 120-150J.\nPharmacological: A choice of flecainide in people with no\nevidence of structural or ischemic heart disease.\nAmiodarone should be used in people with evidence of\nstructural heart disease.\nNote: if Pt with AF is uncertain of time or greater than 48\nhrs then anti-coagulate this pt for 3 weeks before\ncardioversion and rate should be controlled with Beta\nblockers.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
4bde1c44-089d-47b7-86cc-1171e73ce51e	9db23e52-af3e-4f01-9ef3-48e9c44fed87	analgesics	Analgesics	article	Analgesics:\nClassification of analgesics:\nNon opioid analgesics:\nParacetamol: Used in Pyrexia and mild to moderate pain\nSide effects: Rare (rashes, blood disorders)\nMetabolism: Paracetamol metabolized to mainly glucuronide\nconjugates, small amount converted to the toxic NAPQI (N-\nacetyl P-amino-benzoquinoneimine), which is quickly made\nsafe by conjugation with glutathione\nToxic overdose: More NAPQI produced glucuronide pathway\nbecomes saturated leads to cellular death\nand hepatic necrosis.\nAntidote overdose: N-acetylcysteine\nNSAIDS: (Non-Steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs): Class of\nanalgesics used to treat pain, fever and inflammation.\nMechanism: Cyclo-oxygenase (COX) catalyzes the production\nof prostaglandins through the intermediate arachidonic\nacid and Prostaglandins regulate the body's inflammatory\nresponse.\nCOX has 2 isoenzymes:\nCOX-I regulates many physiological processes including\nprotection of gastric mucosa.\nCOX-II is expressed in inflammatory cells.\nNote:  Most NSAIDs inhibit both COX-I and COX-II. Some newer\ndrugs inhibit just COX-II, therefore reducing gastric side\neffects.\nibuprofen (Safest NSAID) but low anti-inflammatory\nproperties not useful in inflammatory conditions.\n\nNaproxen: Good efficacy, lower side effects but greater\nthan ibuprofen. Used in musculoskeletal\ndisorders, Rheumatoid arthritis, Gout and dysmenorrhea.\nDiclofenac: Similar in efficacy to naproxen but greater side\neffects.\nMefenamic acid: Low anti-inflammatory properties but\ngreater side effects which include aplastic anemia\nand hemolytic anemia.\nNote: NSAIDs cause GERD by inhibiting the COX 1 enzymes (cox\n1 stimulation produce prostaglandins that help to maintain the\ngastric mucosal integrity) NSAIDs can cause ulcers by\ninterfering with the stomach's ability to protect itself from\ngastric acid (by inhibiting the function of prostaglandins)\nNSAIDS Adverse effects:\nGl adverse effects (Most common) which include NSAIDS\ninduced peptic ulcer.\nHypersensitivity reactions\nRenal failure contraindicated in reduced GFR\nMI, Heart failure\nTinnitus\nHypertension\nThrombotic events\nFluid retention\nHaemolytic anemia (Mefenamic acid)\nNote: NSAIDs are contraindicated in a patient with heart\nfailure. (Diclofenac is contraindicated in people with ischemic\nheart disease).\nRisk of GI Side effects:\nLowest risk=ibuprofen (Safest NSAID)\nIntermediate risk: Aspirin, naproxen\nHighest risk=piroxicam, ketorolac, indomethacin\nManagement of gout (Important to know\nhere)\nAcute gout: NSAIDS (specially naproxen)\nGout with heart failure: Colchicine\nChronic gout: Allopurinol also reduces further attacks of gout\nby reducing uric acid.\n\nNote: In gout under polarized light, gout crystals appear\nneedle-shaped and yellow, a property called negative\nbirefringence. Gout crystals are monosodium urate crystals\nIn Pseudo gout: Positive birefringence and pyrophosphate\ncrystals.\nColchicine mechanism of action: Colchicine has anti-\ninflammatory properties. It disrupts cytoskeletal functions by\ninhibiting beta-tubulin polymerization into microtubules,\npreventing activation, degranulation, and migration of\nneutrophils associated with mediating gout symptoms.\nColchicine is useful in patients with heart failure and those\ntaking anticoagulants\nAvoid colchicine in patients with blood dyscrasias and bone\nmarrow disease .\nOpioid analgesics:\nClass of analgesics used to relieve moderate to severe\npain. Opioids have affinity for opioid µ receptors found in the\nCNS, spinal cord and periphery\nClassification of opioids:\nContraindications:\nRaised ICP\nParalytic ileus\nAcute respiratory depression\n\n![Analgesics - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/analgesics/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Analgesics — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nExacerbation of Asthma\nOpioid toxicity: Severe respiratory depression coma\nAntidote: Naloxone rapidly reverse the symptoms.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
e664ce05-20cf-42c8-b35b-6af9c39054cd	7ac849e4-6b82-4bf3-b8f0-7f1abbc39105	summary-of-drugs-and-their-side-effects	Summary of Drugs and Their Side Effects	article	Summary Of Drugs And Their Side\nEffects\n\n![Summary of Drugs and Their Side Effects - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/summary-of-drugs-and-their-side-effects/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Summary of Drugs and Their Side Effects — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
