0fcb581e-b1c4-4158-9ee0-ed2a26f3ea4c	4e8e8cf6-923c-4953-ab05-58defec7082e	anemia	Anemia	article	Anemia\nAnemia: Reduction in Hb concentration below the normal\nrange\nRange: Men= Hct < 41% or HB less than 13.5 g/dl\nWomen: Hct< 36% or HB less than 11.5g/dl\nAnemia is classified on the basis of MCV, (Normal 80-100)\nMicrocytic anemia: MCV< 80%, Macrocytic anemia MCV>\n100%\nMicrocytic anemia:\nMacrocytic anemia:\nMegaloblastic anemia (Vit. B12 and folate deficiency\nAlcoholic liver disease\nHypothyroidism\nMyelodysplastic syndrome\n\n![Anemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/anemia/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLaboratory Features\nMegaloblastic anaemia is characterized by:\nMacrocytic anaemia with oval macrocytes and hyper\nsegmented neutrophils\nModerate reduction in leucocyte and platelet counts\nRaised serum bilirubin (indirect) and lactate dehydrogenase\n(LDH) (due to haemolysis of defective red cells)\nLow B12 +/- folate\nHypercellular bone marrow, megaloblastic erythropoiesis and\ngiant metamyelocytes\n\n![Anemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/anemia/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Anemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
9a9645b3-1432-4776-84cd-d05e09987310	4e8e8cf6-923c-4953-ab05-58defec7082e	cell-death	Cell Death	article	Cell death\n\n![Cell Death - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/cell-death/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cell Death — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8e608e13-8ab9-4fc7-894e-e6b7b6b1c882	4e8e8cf6-923c-4953-ab05-58defec7082e	wound-healing	Wound Healing	article	Wound healing\nHealing is the process of replacing dead and damaging tissue\nwith healthy tissue; this may occur through regeneration or\nrepair.\nThe regenerative capacity of cells can be categorized in three\nmain ways:\nLabile cells are constantly dividing and have a good capacity\nfor regeneration, this allows the replacement of ageing tissue\nsuch as the surface epithelia of the skin, gastrointestinal tract\nand uterus; blood cells are derived from labile cells of the\nbone marrow.\nStable cells slowly replicate e.g., cells of liver, renal tubular\nepithelium, endocrine glands.\nPermanent tissues: incapable of division and cannot be\nregenerated e.g., brain cells, myocardial cells, skeletal muscle\ncells.\nHealing by primary intention:\nHealing of a clean, uninfected surgical incision approximated\nby surgical sutures.\nDay-1: Neutrophilic infiltration + fibrin clot\nDay-2: Epithelial cells from both edges have begun to\nmigrate and proliferate along the dermis, yielding a thin but\ncontinuous epithelial layer.\nDay-3: Macrophages replace neutrophils Appearance of\ngranulation tissue. Type III collagen deposition begins but do\nnot bridge the incision.\nDay 5: Abundant granulation tissue Collagen fibrils bridge the\nincision.\nNeovascularization is maximum.\nWeek-2: Dense scar tissue produced from granulation tissue\ncontains type III collagen (weak collagen) that must be\nremodeled. Remodeling increases the tensile strength of scar\ntissue.\n\n1 month: Replacement of collagen type III with collagen type I\n(has greater tensile strength) due to action of collagenase\nenzyme.\nThe predominant collagen in adult skin is type I, whereas in\nearly granulation tissue, it is type III.\nHealing by secondary intention\nOccurs in large wounds abscess formation and ulceration.\nMain features:\nA larger clot or scab\nInflammation: more intense Granulation tissue: much larger\namount\nScar tissue: greater mass\nWound contraction: by myofibroblast e.g. Within 6 weeks,\nlarge skin defects may be reduced to 5% to 10% of their\noriginal size, largely by contraction.\nHealing by tertiary intention\nContaminated wound is initially treated with debridement\nand antibiotics followed by surgical wound closure (suture,\nskin graft replacement, flap)\nNote: Regarding the neat surgical scar after 2 months of\nsurgery fibroblasts have predominant role\nMost important for delayed wound healing is due\nto infection.\nFactors affecting wound healing:\nLocal factors affecting wound healing\nMechanical injury / Frequent trauma\nInfection\nEdema\nIschemia/necrotic tissue\nTopical agents\nlionizing radiation\nLow oxygen tension\nForeign bodies\nSystemic factors affecting wound healing\nDiabetes mellitus\nAnaemia\nMalnutrition\n\nImmunodeficiency\nImmunosuppressive medications\nAge\nObesity\nSmoking\nVit A, C and Zinc deficiency\nComplications of Healing\nHypertrophic scar\nKeloid formation\nFailure to heal (abscess or empyema formation)\nFailure to unite (skin, muscle or fascia wound breakdown)\nFracture complications\nStages of bone/fracture healing:\nHematoma formation (Immediately in Day 1)\nInflammation and proliferation (8 hours to 1-2 weeks)\npredominant cells neutrophils and macrophages\nCallus formation (2-3 weeks)\nConsolidation (2 to 3 months)\nRe-modelling (2 months to years) Osteoclasts are\nresponsible.\nFactors that prevent efficient healing:\nMalnutrition\nInfection\nCorticosteroids therapy\nPoor blood supply	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
c4d46ae6-888b-456b-96e9-5a34fb76242a	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-axis-interpretation	ECG Axis Interpretation — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# ECG Axis Interpretation — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 12-lead ECG demonstrates a positive QRS net deflection in Lead I (predominantly upright) and a negative net deflection in Lead aVF (predominantly downwards), with a negative net deflection in Lead II. What is the QRS electrical axis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Left Axis Deviation (LAD)\n- **B.** Normal cardiac axis\n- **C.** Right Axis Deviation (RAD)\n- **D.** Extreme right axis deviation ('Northwest axis')\n- **E.** Indeterminate axis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLeft Axis Deviation is defined as a QRS axis between -30 and -90 degrees. Quick rule: Lead I is positive, Lead aVF is negative, and Lead II is negative (QRS complexes are 'leaving' each other). Causes include Left Anterior Fascicular Block (LAFB), inferior MI, LVH, and artificial pacing.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_06_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 12-lead ECG shows a predominantly negative QRS deflection in Lead I and a predominantly positive QRS deflection in Lead aVF. What is the QRS axis?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Right Axis Deviation (RAD)\n- **B.** Normal axis\n- **C.** Left Axis Deviation\n- **D.** Extreme axis deviation\n- **E.** Isoelectric axis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRight Axis Deviation is defined as a QRS axis between +90 and +180 degrees. Quick rule: Lead I is negative and Lead aVF is positive (QRS complexes are 'reaching towards' each other). Causes include Right Ventricular Hypertrophy (RVH), acute PE, COPD/cor pulmonale, lateral MI, and Left Posterior Fascicular Block (LPFB).\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_07_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 12-lead ECG shows negative net QRS deflections in BOTH Lead I and Lead aVF, with a positive QRS in lead aVR. What is this axis deviation termed?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Extreme Right Axis Deviation ('Northwest axis' / -90 to 180 degrees)\n- **B.** Normal axis\n- **C.** Physiological left axis\n- **D.** Artifactual reversal\n- **E.** Vertical axis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nExtreme axis deviation (Northwest axis, -90 to 180 degrees) occurs when QRS is negative in both I and aVF. Highly characteristic of ventricular tachycardia (VT), severe hyperkalaemia, apical pacing, or limb-lead reversal.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nIn which lead is the QRS complex equiphasic (net zero, where positive R equals negative S) when the frontal electrical axis is exactly +60 degrees?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Lead aVL\n- **B.** Lead I\n- **C.** Lead II\n- **D.** Lead III\n- **E.** Lead aVR\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe axis is perpendicular to the lead that is equiphasic (isoelectric). Lead aVL is at -30 degrees. The perpendicular to -30 degrees is +60 degrees (Lead II). Thus, when the axis is +60 degrees (normal axis parallel to Lead II), Lead aVL is equiphasic.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nAccidental reversal of the Right Arm (RA) and Left Arm (LA) limb electrodes during ECG recording produces which characteristic diagnostic pattern?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (ECG Axis Interpretation)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/axis-interpretation/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Lead I is completely inverted (negative P wave, negative QRS, and negative T wave), mimicking dextrocardia with normal precordial R-wave progression\n- **B.** Complete asystole in lead II\n- **C.** Giant Osborne waves in leads V4-V6\n- **D.** Loss of all precordial R waves\n- **E.** Pseudo-Brugada sign in V1\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLA/RA electrode reversal inverts lead I (negative P, QRS, T). Leads II and III swap, and leads aVR and aVL swap. Unlike true dextrocardia, the chest leads (V1-V6) show completely normal R-wave progression.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b10fdd81-543e-44a0-8013-20b2c1f4036a	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-bifascicular-trifascicular-block	Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 76-year-old man presents with recurrent episodes of syncope. His ECG reveals complete Right Bundle Branch Block (RBBB) and Left Anterior Fascicular Block (marked left axis deviation of -60 degrees). What conduction disorder does this combination represent?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Bifascicular Block\n- **B.** Isolated RBBB\n- **C.** Wenckebach phenomenon\n- **D.** Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome\n- **E.** Complete heart block\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe intraventricular conduction system has 3 fascicles: RBB, Left Anterior Fascicle (LAF), and Left Posterior Fascicle (LPF). Bifascicular block refers to block of two of these three pathways: typically RBBB + LAFB (or RBBB + LPFB). When accompanied by syncope, it carries high risk of progressing to complete heart block.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_06_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhen a patient with Bifascicular Block (RBBB + LAFB) also demonstrates a prolonged PR interval (>200 ms) on 12-lead ECG, what is this clinical triad traditionally termed?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Trifascicular Block (bifascicular block with first-degree AV block)\n- **B.** Mobitz Type II AV block\n- **C.** Sick Sinus Syndrome\n- **D.** Sinus node dysfunction with LBBB\n- **E.** Brugada syndrome Type 3\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTrifascicular block (incomplete) is defined as bifascicular block (e.g. RBBB + LAFB or LPFB) in the presence of first-degree AV block (PR > 200 ms). It indicates disease throughout all three fascicles and requires urgent cardiology evaluation for permanent pacemaker implantation if symptomatic.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_07_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich fascicle of the cardiac conduction system is anatomically the most robust, possesses a dual blood supply (from LAD and posterior descending artery), and is therefore least commonly blocked in isolation?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Left Posterior Fascicle (LPF)\n- **B.** Left Anterior Fascicle (LAF)\n- **C.** Right Bundle Branch (RBB)\n- **D.** Sinoatrial nodal artery branch\n- **E.** Bachmann's bundle\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe Left Posterior Fascicle is broad, thick, and has a dual blood supply from both the anterior and posterior descending coronary arteries. Consequently, isolated Left Posterior Fascicular Block (LPFB) is rare and implies extensive myocardial or conducting system damage.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nAn 82-year-old woman with known bifascicular block presents to the ED with a mechanical fall and sudden blackout. During telemetry monitoring, her rhythm strip shows a sudden dropped QRS without prior PR prolongation (Mobitz II / high-degree AV block). What is the definitive treatment?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Permanent Pacemaker (PPM) insertion\n- **B.** Oral bisoprolol titration\n- **C.** Oral amiodarone 200mg daily\n- **D.** Elective direct current cardioversion\n- **E.** No intervention needed if she recovers consciousness\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSymptomatic advanced conduction disease (syncope in the setting of bifascicular block with documented high-degree AV block or intermittent complete heart block) is a Class I indication for permanent pacemaker (PPM) implantation to prevent sudden cardiac death.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich temporary emergency measure is indicated in the ED for a patient with trifascicular block presenting with severe symptomatic bradycardia (HR 26 bpm, BP 76/44 mmHg) refractory to IV Atropine?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Bifascicular & Trifascicular Blocks)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/bifascicular-trifascicular-block/figure_05_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Transcutaneous Pacing (TCP) with analgesia/sedation, preparing for transvenous wire insertion\n- **B.** IV Adenosine 6mg rapid bolus\n- **C.** IV Digoxin 500mcg over 2 hours\n- **D.** Oral verapamil\n- **E.** Synchronised DC shock 200J\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nInfrastructural/infranodal AV blocks (such as in trifascicular disease) respond poorly to atropine. For haemodynamic compromise, immediate transcutaneous electrical pacing (TCP) is the treatment of choice, serving as a bridge to emergency transvenous pacing.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c8d775c5-b5ad-4076-8b88-54fea06825a3	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-bronchiolitis	Acute Bronchiolitis in Infants — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Bronchiolitis in Infants — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 3-month-old infant presents during winter with a 3-day history of coryza, low-grade fever, worsening cough, and difficulty feeding. Examination reveals tachypnoea (RR 62/min), subcostal and intercostal retractions, nasal flaring, and widespread fine bilateral inspiratory crackles and high-pitched expiratory wheezes throughout both lung fields. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Bronchiolitis in Infants)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/bronchiolitis/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Bronchiolitis\n- **B.** Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)\n- **C.** Foreign body aspiration\n- **D.** Bacterial tracheitis\n- **E.** Congenital lobar emphysema\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute bronchiolitis is the most common lower respiratory tract infection in infants <1 year (peak age 2-6 months). Hallmark clinical features: viral prodrome followed by tachypnoea, hyperinflation, intercostal recession, and the characteristic combination of fine inspiratory crackles and expiratory wheeze.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common causative viral pathogen of acute bronchiolitis in infants worldwide?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Bronchiolitis in Infants)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/bronchiolitis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV, causing 75-80% of cases)\n- **B.** Human Metapneumovirus\n- **C.** Rhinovirus\n- **D.** Parainfluenza virus Type 1\n- **E.** Adenovirus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRespiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV) is responsible for approximately 75-80% of all bronchiolitis admissions. Rhinovirus, human metapneumovirus, and bocavirus are other causes.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guideline NG9 (Bronchiolitis in children), which of the following interventions is recommended in routine management?\n\n- **A.** Oxygen supplementation if SpO2 <92% and supportive fluid/nutritional maintenance via nasogastric feeding if feeding is <50% of normal\n- **B.** Routine inhaled bronchodilators (salbutamol or ipratropium)\n- **C.** Routine systemic oral corticosteroids\n- **D.** Routine broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics\n- **E.** Routine chest physiotherapy and percussion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG9 explicitly advises AGAINST: bronchodilators (salbutamol/ipratropium), systemic or inhaled corticosteroids, antibiotics, and routine chest physiotherapy, because none have shown efficacy in bronchiolitis. Supportive care (humidified oxygen for SpO2 <92%, nasal suctioning for feeding difficulties, and NG/IV fluids) is the only evidence-based management.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich clinical presentation is recognized by NICE guidelines as a presentation of acute bronchiolitis specifically in young infants under 6 weeks of age, even before significant respiratory signs develop?\n\n- **A.** Apnoea (cessation of breathing >20 seconds)\n- **B.** Severe stridor\n- **C.** Maculopapular rash\n- **D.** Neck stiffness\n- **E.** Profuse watery diarrhoea\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nYoung infants (<6-8 weeks of age) and premature infants with RSV bronchiolitis are at high risk of presenting with severe recurrent APNOEA, often occurring before prominent wheezing or crackles appear. They require immediate hospital admission with continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guidelines, what is an absolute 'Red Flag' criterion for immediate hospital admission in an infant with acute bronchiolitis?\n\n- **A.** Persistent SpO2 <92% in room air, observed apnoea, or oral fluid intake less than 50% of typical volume\n- **B.** Temperature of 37.8C\n- **C.** Presence of mild nasal discharge\n- **D.** Mild cough for 2 days\n- **E.** Weight in the 50th centile\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG9 criteria for admission: Persistent SpO2 <92% on room air; clinical dehydration; inadequate oral fluid intake (<50% of normal volume); respiratory rate >60/min in infants <1 year; observed or reported apnoea; severe respiratory distress (grunting, marked recession); or social vulnerability.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4a60fd0b-c5c3-4f4b-b808-9553784a9af9	4e8e8cf6-923c-4953-ab05-58defec7082e	blood-groups	Blood Groups	article	Blood group\nThe ABO blood group phenotypes are O (genotype OO), A\n(genotype AA or AO), B (genotype BB or BO) and AB (genotype\n\n## Ab).\n\nBlood group O is the most common in the UK (46 %), followed\nby group A (42 %), group B (9 %) and finally group AB (3 %).\nBlood group O has no antigens, but both anti-A and anti-B\nantibodies and thus is the universal donor.\nBlood group AB has both A and B antigens but no antibodies\nand thus is the universal recipient.\nBlood group A has A antigens and anti-B antibodies, and\nblood group B has B antigens and anti-A antibodies.\n\n![Blood Groups - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/blood-groups/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Blood Groups — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n## Rh-D\n\nThe Rh D gene may be either absent or present, giving the RhD\nnegative or RhD positive phenotype respectively. About 85% of\nthe UK population is Rh D positive. Anti-D antibodies don't\noccur naturally and are therefore immune antibodies that\nresult from previous transfusions or pregnancy.\nRhesus Disease:  A condition that occurs during pregnancy if\na woman has Rh-ve blood and her baby has Rh+ve blood.\nMother's body create Rh antibodies against the baby's blood\nalso known as hemolytic disease of newborn\n.\nPathogenesis: Rh-ve mother carrying Rh+ve fetus⟶  Entry of\nfetus Rh+ve Rbc into maternal circulation⟶  Development of\nRh antibodies by the mother\nPrevention and Prophylaxis\nanti-D immunoglobulin to all Rh-negative women at 28 and 34\nweeks routinely\nOr give anti-D immunoglobulin if she has a:\nTherapeutic abortion.\nSpontaneous abortion/ectopic pregnancy.\nAmniocentesis.\nAny bleeding in pregnancy/threatened miscarriage.\n\n![Blood Groups - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/blood-groups/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Blood Groups — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nAfter delivery at any gestation within 72Hours.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
ab3bc84b-2dac-477d-a95a-ba6048ca75ba	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	hellicobacter-pylori-and-compylobacter-jejuni-bacteria	Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria	article	Helicobacter Pylori\nCampylobacter Jejuni\nNote:\nReactive arthritis (Reiter's syndrome) is a triad of urethritis,\narthritis and conjunctivitis.\nOrganisms causing reactive arthritis: Chlamydia,\nSalmonella, Campylobacter jejuni and Shigella\n\n![Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/hellicobacter-pylori-and-compylobacter-jejuni-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/hellicobacter-pylori-and-compylobacter-jejuni-bacteria/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
5dc3a591-1c1a-42fd-9dbf-b40900fba804	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	genetourinary-trauma	Genitourinary Trauma & Urethral Injury	article	# Genitourinary Trauma & Urethral Injury\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- motor vehicle crashes, falls, assaults and sports injuries are the rnost common mechanisms for blunt GU injuries, whereas gunshot wounds and stab wounds are the most common causes for penetrating injuries.\n- SeatbeIt use and air bag deployment have increasingly been associated with decreased rates of nephrectomy in renal trauma patients.\n- Renal trauma (kidney_jnjury) following-features-indicates-renaLinjury\n- Flank contusions or ecchymosis,\n- palpable mass,\n- lower rib fractures,\n- penetrating wounds in the flank\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n- patient will complain flank and loin pain followed by haematuria. :P.iagnosi.s; contrast-enhanced CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis is the imaging "gold standard" for the stable patient with suspected renal injury\n- RenaI angiography can identify renal vascular injury\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- mostly conservatively (analgesia, bed rest, antibiotic\n- Bladder trauma\n- only 2%\n\n- Associated with pelvic fracture\n- Suspect bladder injury in alcohol-intoxicated patients (bladder often distended) who are in a motor vehicle crash (potentially high-energy transfer resulting in pelvic fracture).\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n- ;\n- Lower abdominal pain and tenderness and gross hematuria are commonly associated findings cystogram is the "gold standard"\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- Extraperitoneal rupture is most common and usually manage with catheter drainage\n- lntraperitoneal rupture always require surgical repair\n- ExtraperitoneaI ruptures may need surgical intervention in following situation .\n- Urinary catheter does not allow appropriate drainage\n- Associated rectal or vaginal injury\n- Associated bladder neck injury\n- Open fixation of a pelvic fracture (to avoid contamination of the hardware)\n- Pelvic fracture with bone spicules in bladder lumen\n- Urethral injury_\n\n- The posterior urethra consists of prostatic and membranous portions.\n- Associated with rnotor vehicle collisions or falls from heights.\n- The anterior urethra is divided into the relatively fixed bulbar segment and the penile segment.\n- Associated with direct perineal trauma\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n- Symptoms may include hematuria, dysuria, or inability to void. Rectal and perineal examination may demonstrate a perineal hematoma or high-riding prostate.\n- Posterior urethral injury is suggested by the triad of urinary retention, blood at the meatus, and a high-riding prostate\n- Following feature indicates consideration of suprapubic catheter and for bladder drainage and retrograde urethrogram for diagnosis of urethral injury so for that patient urgently refer to urologist\n- high-riding prostate,\n- meatal blood,\n- perineal ecchymosis,\n- scrotal hematoma,\n- pelvic fracture, or gross hematuria,\n- any resistance to gentle passage of a Foley catheter\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- FoIIow ATLS protocol\n- Male PT with posterior urethral injury secondary to pelvic fracture need immediate placement of suprapubic catheter for bladder drainage with delay surgical repair\n- Most penetrating injuries to the anterior urethra require surgical exploration and repair. —Note: scrotal and testicular trauma (scrotal or testicular hematoma) ,manage conservatively with good analgesia however testicular rupture need surgical intervention.\n- Fracture of penis refer urgently to urologist for surgical exploration, evacuation of haematoma and repair. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Genetourinary Trauma - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/genetourinary-trauma/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Genetourinary Trauma — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Genetourinary Trauma - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/genetourinary-trauma/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Genetourinary Trauma — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
98e2e876-c2fd-4a7c-ac2a-81ee906f918e	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	pulmonology-basics	Pulmonology Foundations & Respiratory Physiology	article	# Pulmonology Foundations & Respiratory Physiology\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	22	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d56ea9fd-4344-4ad9-8f89-d68d5eb9d8ff	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones	Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones	article	Endocrine System\nIntroduction: All the physiological activities of the body are\nregulated. by two major systems\n\n### 1. Nervous system\n\n### 2. Endocrine system.\n\nEndocrine glands: Endocrine glands are glands which\nsynthesize and release classical hormones into the blood.\nEndocrine glands are also called ductless glands because the\nhormones secreted by them are released directly into blood\nwithout any ducts. Endocrine glands are distinct from exocrine\nglands which release their secretions through ducts. Endocrine\nglands play an important role in homeostasis and control of\nvarious other activities in the body through their hormones.\n\nEndocrinology hormones overview:\nHarmons secreted by adrenal gland:\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nHormone secreted by Gonads.\nHormone secreted by other organs.\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_03_p3.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_04_p4.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
68e85888-ea29-4c10-b10c-a41a7f419329	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	herpes-simplex-and-varicella-zooster-virus	Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus	article	Herpes Virus:\nVaricella zoster Key Points\nThe incubation period is 10 - 21 days (Recall) (mean time is\n14 days).\nPrimary infection typically occurs in 4-10 years children.\nDiagnosis is usually clinical.\n\n![Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/herpes-simplex-and-varicella-zooster-virus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/herpes-simplex-and-varicella-zooster-virus/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nAfter primary infection virus lies dormant in posterior root\nganglion and reactivates in 20% of patients causing shingles\nSchool exclusion period: when all lesions are crusted over\n(Recall\nChickenpox in pregnancy:\nChickenpox in pregnancy is managed by two different\nmethods - one is used before the onset of symptoms and the\nother after the onset of rash. One is varicella-zoster immune\nglobulin (VZIG), which strengthens the immune system for a\nshort time. If you are not immune to chickenpox you can be\ntreated with VZIG, usually by injection, up to ten days after\nyou come into contact with chickenpox. It is used before any\nsymptoms appear; it does not work afterwards. The other is\nAcyclovir, which may reduce fever and symptoms if it is\nstarted within 24 hours of the rash developing and continue\nfor 7 days\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Key points:**\n> If you have already had chickenpox, you have nothing to\n> worry about.\n\nOnce you have had chickenpox you cannot catch it a second\ntime, this is known as being immune.\nNine out of ten UK adults are immune to chickenpox.\nChickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in the UK.\nIt affects very few babies in the womb and although it can\nbe very serious in pregnancy especially in 1st trimester in\nfirst 12 weeks (Recall)\nIf you have been in contact with chickenpox and are not sure\nif you are immune, contact your GP or midwife and do not\nattend the antenatal clinic.\nIf you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other pregnant\nwomen and new babies until at least five days after the rash\nappears, or until all the blisters have crusted over.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
9342dd1b-83a8-49cc-ba3c-7731867f1039	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	principles-of-investigation	Principles of Investigation	article	Principles of Investigations\n\n![Principles of Investigation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-investigation/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Principles of Investigation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
f4a7510d-1ad7-497d-afc6-33c4cf57215f	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-copd	COPD & Acute Exacerbations — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# COPD & Acute Exacerbations — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nAccording to the Anthonisen criteria, what three cardinal symptoms define a Type 1 (Severe) Acute Exacerbation of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (AECOPD)?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (COPD & Acute Exacerbations)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/copd/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Increased dyspnoea, Increased sputum volume, and Increased sputum purulence\n- **B.** Fever, Haemoptysis, and Pleuritic chest pain\n- **C.** Weight loss, Night sweats, and Anorexia\n- **D.** Confusion, Headache, and Tremor\n- **E.** Wheezing, Stridor, and Dysphagia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe Anthonisen criteria classify AECOPD: Type 1 (severe) has all 3 cardinal symptoms: worsening dyspnoea, increased sputum volume, and increased sputum purulence. Increased sputum purulence is the strongest predictor of bacterial infection and is the primary indication for antibiotic therapy.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 66-year-old male with severe COPD is brought to the ED by ambulance on a high-flow 15 L/min non-rebreather reservoir mask. He is now obtunded and difficult to rouse, with warm hands and a bounding pulse with flapping tremor (asterixis). Arterial blood gas reveals: pH 7.18, PaCO2 11.2 kPa (84 mmHg), PaO2 24.5 kPa (184 mmHg), HCO3- 33 mmol/L. What acute physiological crisis has developed?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (COPD & Acute Exacerbations)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/copd/figure_02_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oxygen-induced hypercapnia with severe acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis ('CO2 Narcosis')\n- **B.** Septic shock\n- **C.** Acute pulmonary embolism\n- **D.** Lactic acidosis from metformin\n- **E.** Opiate overdose alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAdministering unregulated high-flow oxygen to a patient with severe chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure worsens hypercapnia through three mechanisms: (1) worsening V/Q mismatch by reversing hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction in poorly ventilated alveoli; (2) the Haldane effect (deoxygenated haemoglobin binds CO2 with higher affinity than oxygenated haemoglobin); (3) blunting of carotid body hypoxic drive. Result: massive CO2 retention, acute acidosis, and CO2 narcosis.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended target oxygen saturation (SpO2) range for a patient with acute exacerbation of COPD at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure according to BTS guidelines?\n\n- **A.** 88% to 92% (using a 24% or 28% Venturi mask or 1-2 L/min nasal prongs)\n- **B.** 94% to 98%\n- **C.** Strictly 100%\n- **D.** 80% to 84%\n- **E.** Oxygen is strictly contraindicated in all COPD patients\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS emergency oxygen guidelines recommend a target oxygen saturation of 88-92% for patients with known COPD, prior hypercapnia, or risk factors for hypercapnic respiratory failure (cystic fibrosis, neuromuscular disease, severe chest wall deformity, morbid obesity). Controlled oxygen is initiated with a 24% or 28% Venturi mask or low-flow nasal cannula.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guideline NG115, what is the recommended dose and duration of systemic corticosteroids for an acute exacerbation of COPD?\n\n- **A.** Oral Prednisolone 30mg once daily for 5 days\n- **B.** Oral Prednisolone 60mg once daily for 28 days\n- **C.** IV Dexamethasone 20mg daily for 14 days\n- **D.** Inhaled fluticasone monotherapy\n- **E.** Steroids are never indicated in COPD\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe REDUCE trial and updated NICE guidelines recommend a short 5-day course of oral Prednisolone 30 mg daily for AECOPD. Short courses are as effective as traditional 10-14 day regimens for recovery, lung function, and relapse prevention, while significantly reducing adverse corticosteroid side effects (hyperglycaemia, infection, osteoporosis).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich three bacterial microorganisms are responsible for the vast majority of bacterial exacerbations of COPD?\n\n- **A.** Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Moraxella catarrhalis\n- **B.** Staphylococcus aureus, E. coli, and Klebsiella\n- **C.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Legionella, and Mycoplasma\n- **D.** Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Nocardia, and Actinomyces\n- **E.** Bacteroides fragilis, Enterococcus, and Listeria\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic bacterial trio in AECOPD is: (1) Haemophilus influenzae (most common, ~40-50%); (2) Streptococcus pneumoniae (~20-30%); (3) Moraxella catarrhalis (~15-20%). First-line empirical oral antibiotics recommended by NICE include amoxicillin, doxycycline, or clarithromycin.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
