6f8aec17-1729-49b9-8bcf-87f4fa89a94a	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	jaundice	Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED	article	# Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	28	published	2026-11-02 07:13:02.748929+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
dbcffa08-bce9-4c23-8806-e13f92fe49f8	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	arterial-supply-of-upper-limb	Arterial Supply of Upper Limb	article	Axillary Artery:\nIs considered to be the central structure of the axilla. Extends\nfrom the outer border of the first rib to the inferior border of\nthe teres major muscle, where it becomes the brachial artery.\nThe axillary artery runs with and is lateral to the axillary vein.\nIs divided into three parts by the pectoralis minor muscle.\nFirst part: Superior thoracic artery\nSecond part: Thoracoacromial artery and Lateral thoracic\nartery\nThird Part: Subscapular artery, Anterior and posterior\ncircumflex arteries\nNote: The anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries\nform an anastomotic network around the surgical neck of\nthe humerus and can be damaged in cases of fracture.\nBrachial Artery:\nThe brachial artery is a continuation of the axillary artery past\nthe lower border of the teres major. It is the main supply of\nblood for the arm.\nAnatomical course:\nIt descends through the anterior compartment of the arm on\nthe brachialis muscle enters the cubital fossa, and then ends\nat the level of the neck of the radius by dividing into the\nradial and the ulnar arteries.\n\n#### Branches of Brachial artery\n\n### 1: Muscular branches\n\n### 2: Nutrient artery: to the humerus\n\n### 3: Profunda brachii artery: Immediately distal to the teres\n\nmajor, the brachial artery gives rise to the profunda brachii\n(deep artery), which travels with the radial nerve in the radial\ngroove of the humerus and supplies structures in the\nposterior aspect of the upper arm (e.g., triceps brachii).\n\n### 4: Superior ulnar collateral artery, which follows the ulnar\n\nnerve\n\n### 5: Inferior ulnar collateral artery, which takes part in\n\nanastomosis around the elbow joint.\nRadial artery:\nSmaller of two terminal branches of brachial artery. Passing\nalong the lateral aspect of the forearm lying deep to\nbrachioradialis muscle, distally being covered by only skin\nand fascia. The radial pulse can be palpated in the distal\nforearm, immediately lateral to the prominent tendon of\nthe flexor carpi radialis muscle. The radial artery enters the\nhand by curving around the lateral side of the wrist, passing\nover the floor of the anatomical snuffbox and penetrating the\ndorsolateral aspect of the hand between the bases of the first\nand second metacarpal bones.\nUlnar artery:\nLargest of the two terminal branches, courses along the ulnar\naspect of the forearm deep to the flexor digitorum\nsuperficialis, pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis muscles.\nIt enters the hand with the ulnar nerve and vein by passing\nthrough the Guyon canal (ulnar canal) located between the\npisiform and the hook of hamate.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Brachial artery is palpated in antecubital fossa, immediately\n> medial to the tendon of biceps brachii.\n\nThe radial pulse can be palpated in the distal forearm,\nimmediately lateral to the prominent tendon of the flexor\ncarpi radialis muscle.\nVolkman ischemic contracture:\nComplete occlusion of brachial artery can cause necrosis of\nforearm muscles and are replaced by scar tissue and shorten\nconsiderably this can cause a characteristic flexion deformity,\ncalled Volkmann's ischemic contracture.\nGuyon's or Ulnar canal: The ulnar (Guyon's) canal is a fibro-\nosseous tunnel located at the level of the palm. The ulnar canal conveys the ulnar neurovascular bundle into the hand.\nContents: Ulnar nerve, ulnar artery and Venae comitantes of\nulnar artery\nUlnar canal syndrome refers to compression of the ulnar nerve\nwithin the ulnar canal. The most common causes are ganglion\ncyst, lipoma and trauma.\n\n#### Superficial and Deep palmar arches\n\nThe hand has a rich arterial supply with many anastomoses\nbetween vessels\n\n### 1: The superficial palmar arch lies just beneath the palmar\n\naponeurosis on the long flexor tendons. It is a continuation of\nthe ulnar artery, and it is often completed on the lateral side by\nthe superficial palmar branch of the radial artery.\nPalmar aponeurosis: is a thickened area of palmar fascia.\nPalmar aponeurosis protects the underlying soft tissues and\nlong flexor tendons\nBlood supply: The superficial palmar arch It gives rise to the\ndigital arteries, which supply the four fingers.\n\n### 2: The deep palmar arch is deeply placed in the palm, and it\n\nextends from the proximal end of the space between the first\nand second metacarpal bones to the base of the fifth\nmetacarpal bone. It is formed as a continuation of the radial\nartery, and it terminates by anastomosing with the deep\nbranch of the ulnar artery.\nBlood supply: Blood supply to digits and wrist joint.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
eaa6bf01-e186-4d36-b8f7-f188aa25ac79	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	innervation-of-upper-limb	Innervation of Upper Limb	article	Brachial Plexus:\nThe brachial plexus is a network of nerve fibers that supply the\nskin and muscles of upper extremity. The plexus is formed by\nthe anterior rami (divisions) of cervical spinal nerves C5, C6, C7,\nand C8, and the first thoracic spinal nerve, T1.\nThe brachial plexus is divided into five parts\nRoots\nTrunks\nDivisions\nCords\nbranches\n\n### 1: Root:\n\nFormed by the union of anterior rami (divisions) of cervical\nspinal nerves C5, C6, C7, and C8, and the first thoracic spinal\nnerve, T1.\n\n### 2: Trunk:\n\nAt the base of the neck, the roots of the brachial plexus\nconverge to form three trunks.\nSuperior trunk: Combination of C5 and C6 roots.\nMiddle trunk: Continuation of C7.\nInferior trunk: Combination of C8 and T1 roots\n\n### 3: Division:\n\nTrunks are divided into anterior and posterior division within\nposterior triangle of neck.\n\n### 4: Cords:\n\nOnce the anterior and posterior divisions have entered the\naxilla, they combine together to form three cords\nLateral cord: Anterior division of superior and middle trunk\nMedial cord: Anterior division of inferior trunk\nPosterior cord: Posterior union of all three trunks\n\n### 5: Branches:\n\n#### BRANCHES OF LATERAL CORD   "LML"\n\n#### Lateral pectoral nerve\n\nMusculocutaneous nerve\n\n#### Lateral root of median nerve\n\n#### BRANCHES OF MEDIAL CORD     "M4U"\n\n#### Medial pectoral nerve\n\n#### Medial cutaneous nerve of arm\n\n#### Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm\n\n#### Medial root of median nerve\n\nUlnar nerve\n\n#### BRANCHES OF POSTERIOR CORD   "ULTRA"\n\nUpper subscapular nerve\nLower subscapular nerve\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Brachial plexus injury:\n> Axillary Nerve:\n> Axillary Nerve is a direct continuation of the posterior cord from\n> the brachial plexus.\n\nSpinal roots: C5 and C6.\nPlexus cord: Posterior\nMotor functions: Innervates the teres minor and deltoid\nmuscles.\nSensory functions: Gives rise to the upper lateral cutaneous\nnerve of the arm, which innervates the skin over the lower\ndeltoid ('regimental badge area').\nNote: Lateral supraclavicular nerve arising from the cervical\nplexus (C3, C4 ) gives sensory innervation to upper half of\ndeltoid.\nAnatomical course:\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nAxillary nerve arises in the axilla passes medially to the surgical\nneck of the humerus, where it divides into three terminal\nbranches:\nAnterior terminal branch:  winds around the surgical neck of\nthe humerus and provides motor innervation to the anterior\naspect of the deltoid muscle.\nPosterior terminal branch:  Gives motor innervation to the\nposterior aspect of the deltoid muscle and teres minor. It also\ninnervates the skin over the inferior part of the deltoid as\nthe upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm.\nArticular branch: supplies the glenohumeral joint\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Axillary nerve injury is commonly caused by fracture of\n> surgical neck of humerus or inferior dislocation of humerus.\n\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of the arm.\n(Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but not to a horizontal\nlevel).\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of the deltoid (the\n'regimental badge area').\nQuadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex\nhumeral artery and vein pass through it and can be\ncompressed as a result of trauma, muscle hypertrophy\nresulting in weakness of deltoid and teres minor.\nMedian Nerve:\nAfter originating from the brachial plexus in the axilla the\nmedian nerve descends down the arm, initially lateral to the\nbrachial artery. Halfway down the arm, the nerve crosses over\nthe brachial artery, and becomes situated medially. The median\nnerve enters the anterior compartment of the forearm via the\ncubital fossa\n\n#### Branches of Median Nerve in the Forearm\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n#### Branches of Median Nerve in the Hand\n\nAfter giving off the anterior interosseous and palmar cutaneous\nbranches, the median nerve enters the hand via the carpal\ntunnel where it terminates by dividing into two branches\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Carpal tunnel syndrome: Compression of the median nerve\n> within the carpal tunnel can cause carpal tunnel syndrome\n\n## (Cts).\n\nClinical features: include numbness, tingling, and pain in the\ndistribution of the median nerve.\nNote: In carpal tunnel syndrome Importantly, the palm is\nusually spared  as the palmar cutaneous branch does not travel\nthrough the carpal tunnel.\nNote: Flexion of the ring and little fingers at MCPJ and DIPJ are\npreserved as these are functions of medial half of flexor\ndigitorum profundus and medial two lumbricals innervated by\nulnar nerve.\nRadial Nerve:\nThe radial nerve is the terminal continuation of the posterior\ncord of the brachial plexus\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_04_p4.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_05_p4.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\nRadial nerve arises from the posterior cord and the largest\nbranch of the brachial plexus.\nThe nerve arises in the axilla region, where it is situated\nposteriorly to the axillary artery, descends posteriorly between\nthe long and medial heads of the triceps, after which it passes\ninferolaterally with the profunda brachii artery in the spiral\n(radial) groove on the posterior surface of the humerus\nbetween the medial and lateral heads of the triceps. Pierces\nthe lateral intermuscular septum to enter the anterior\ncompartment and descends anterior to the lateral epicondyle\nbetween the brachialis and brachioradialis muscles to enter the\ncubital fossa, where it divides into superficial and deep\nbranches.\nDeep branch (motor): innervates the muscles in the posterior\ncompartment of the forearm and continues as posterior\ninterosseous nerve\nSuperficial branch (sensory): contributes to the cutaneous\ninnervation of the skin of dorsal hand and lateral three and\nhalf fingers.\nOther branches which give cutaneous innervation to skin of\nupper limb.\nLower lateral cutaneous nerve of arm: Innervates the lateral\naspect of the arm, inferior to the insertion of the deltoid\nmuscle.\nPosterior cutaneous nerve of arm: Innervates the posterior\nsurface of the arm.\nPosterior cutaneous nerve of forearm: Innervates a strip of\nskin down the middle of the posterior forearm.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Radial nerve injury:\n> Wrist Drop:\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_06_p5.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nInjury to the radial nerve is commonly caused by a fracture of\nthe midshaft of the humerus. Radial nerve injury results in loss\nof function in the extensors of the forearm, hand, metacarpals,\nand phalanges. It also results in the loss of wrist extension,\nleading to wrist drop, and produces weakness of abduction and\nadduction of the hand.\nNote: When the radial nerve is injured in proximal humerus\nfracture triceps brachii muscle will be affected and patient\npresents with unable to extend at the forearm. While midshaft\nof humerus fracture triceps brachii will remain spared\nUlnar Nerve:\nThe ulnar nerve arises from the brachial plexus within the axilla\nregion. It is a continuation of the medial cord and contains\nfibers from spinal roots C8 and T1.\nAnatomical Course of Ulnar Nerve:\nArises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus, runs down\nthe medial aspect of the arm, pierces the medial\nintermuscular septum at the middle of the arm, and\ndescends together with the superior ulnar collateral branch\nof the brachial artery. It then Descends behind the medial\nepicondyle in a tunnel (cubital tunnel), where it is readily\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_07_p6.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_08_p6.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\npalpated and most commonly injured. It may be damaged\nby a fracture of the medial epicondyle. When irritated, it\ncauses funny bone symptoms of pain, numbness, and\ntingling in the medial elbow, forearm, and digits.\nIt then enters the forearm by passing between the two heads\nof the flexor carpi ulnaris and descends between the flexor\ncarpi ulnaris and gives rise to following branches in forearm.\nUlnar nerve branches in hand\nEnters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum and\nlateral to the pisiform bone, where it is vulnerable to damage\nfrom cuts or stab wounds and gives rise to following\nbranches in hand.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Froment's sign: Froment's sign is a test for ulnar nerve palsy\n> specifically paralysis of the adductor pollicis:\n> Patient is asked to hold a piece of paper between thumb and\n> index finger as paper is pulled away, patient will flex thumb\n> at inter-phalangeal joint to maintain hold, this sign tests the\n> function of adductor pollicis muscle which is supplied by the\n> ulnar nerve.\n\nA positive test is when the patient is unable to adduct the\nthumb.\nClaw hand:\nInjury to the ulnar nerve is commonly caused by a fracture of\nthe medial epicondyle and results in a claw hand, in which the\nring and little fingers are hyperextended at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and flexed at the interphalangeal\njoints.\nPartial claw hand deformity is due to an isolated ulnar nerve\ninjury.\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_09_p7.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_10_p7.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\nComplete claw hand deformity: Involves all digits and results\nfrom combined ulnar and median nerve injury.\nUlnar nerve injury at elbow and wrist:\nSensory innervation of Median, Radial and\nulnar (High Yield):\nMusculocutaneous Nerve:\nThe musculocutaneous nerve is the terminal branch of the\nlateral cord of the brachial plexus.\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 11](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_11_p8.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 11 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 12](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_12_p8.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 12 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\nAnatomical Course:\nMusculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7) Pierces the coracobrachialis\nmuscle and descends between the biceps and brachialis\nmuscles. Innervates all of the flexor muscles in the anterior\ncompartment of the arm, such as the coraco- brachialis, biceps,\nand brachialis muscles. Continues into the forearm as the\nlateral cutaneous nerve of forearm.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Injury to the musculocutaneous nerve results in weakness of\n> supination (biceps) but can still be performed by the\n> brachioradialis (Innervated by Radial nerve)\n> Weakness of flexion at the shoulder and elbow but can still\n> be performed by the pectoralis major and brachioradialis\n> respectively and loss of sensation on lateral side of the\n> forearm.\n\n![Innervation of Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 13](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/innervation-of-upper-limb/figure_13_p9.png)\n*Innervation of Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 13 (Page 9 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
5b804dde-6f4c-4dac-9435-3fbc1805fc0f	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	joints-of-the-upper-limb	Joints of the Upper Limb	article	Acromioclavicular Joint:\nTYPE= Plane type synovial joint\nThe acromioclavicular joint consists of an articulation between\nthe lateral end of the clavicle and the acromion of the scapula\nLigaments of the joint:\nLigaments\nThere are three main ligaments that support the\nacromioclavicular joint\nAcromioclavicular ligament\nConoid ligament\nTrapezoid ligament\nCollectively, the conoid and trapezoid ligaments are known as\nthe coracoclavicular ligament. It is the strongest ligament\nand suspends weight of the upper limb\n\nAcromioclavicular joint dislocation\nA severe blow on the point of the shoulder, as is incurred\nduring blocking or tackling in football or any severe fall,\ncan result in the acromion being thrust beneath the lateral\nend of the clavicle, tearing the coracoclavicular ligament. This\ncondition is known as shoulder separation. The displaced\nouter end of the clavicle is easily palpable.\nNote: If the coracoclavicular ligament is torn weight of the\nupper limb is not supported\nSternoclavicular Joint\nType: Synovial typed Saddle joint\nSternoclavicular joint is formed between the clavicle,\nmanubrium of the sternum and the first costal cartilage.\n\n![Joints of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-upper-limb/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Joints of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nLigaments of Sternoclavicular Joint\nSternoclavicular ligaments (anterior and posterior)\nstrengthen the joint anteriorly and posteriorly.\nInterclavicular ligament strengthen the joint capsule\nsuperiorly.\nCostoclavicular ligament strong ligament and stabilizes the\njoint capsule.\nThe sternoclavicular joint has a high degree of mobility and is\nrequired to accommodate the movements of the upper limb\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Violent forces directed along the long axis of the**\n> clavicle usually result in fracture of that bone, but dislocation of\n> the sternoclavicular joint takes place rarely.\n\nElbow joint\nType: Synovial hinge joint assists in flexion and extension of\nelbow\nFormed by articulations between\nTrochlear notch of the ulna and the trochlea of the humerus\nHead of the radius and the capitulum of the humerus\nLigaments of the elbow joint:\nThe radial collateral ligament is found on the lateral side of\nthe joint, extending from the lateral epicondyle and\nblending with the annular ligament.\nThe ulnar collateral ligament referred as Medial collateral\nligament (MCL) of the elbow originates from the\nmedial epicondyle and attaches to the coronoid process and\nolecranon of the ulna.\nAnnular ligament: Annular ligament stabilizes the elbow\njoint and is a strong band of fibers that encircles the head of\nthe radius and holds it in contact with the radial notch of the\nulna\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n> Elbow dislocation: Mostly posterior occurs due to fall on\n> hand with the elbow flexed\n> Complications: The ulnar collateral ligament is usually torn\n> and there can also be ulnar nerve involvement.\n\nRadioulnar joints (Pronator and\nsupinator of the forearm)\nType: Pivot typed synovial joint\nProximal Radioulnar joint: Formed by articulation between the\nhead of the radius and the radial notch of the ulna\nAnnular ligament stabilizes the elbow joint and is a strong\nband of fibers that encircles the head of the radius and holds\nit in contact with the radial notch of the ulna\nDistal Radioulnar joint: formed by articulation between the\nulnar notch of the radius and the head of the ulna.\nInterosseous Membrane\nThe interosseous membrane is a sheet of connective tissue\nthat joins the radius and ulna together between the\nradioulnar joints along with acts as a site of attachment for\nmuscles in the anterior and posterior compartments of\nforearm.\nThe collateral ligament is usually torn and there can also be\nulnar nerve involvement The ulnar collateral ligament is\nusually torn and there can also be ulnar nerve involvement\nThe ulnar collateral ligament is usually torn and there can\nalso be ulnar nerve involvement.\n\nWrist joint\nType: Synovial condyloid joint\nFormed between the distal end of the radius and the articular\ndisc above and the scaphoid, lunate and triquetral bones\nbelow\nNote: The ulna is not part of the wrist joint it articulates with\nthe radius, just proximal to the wrist joint.\nLigaments of the joint:\nPalmar radiocarpal:  Found on anterior side of the hand. It\npasses from the radius to both rows of carpal bones. Its\nfunction, apart from increasing stability, is to ensure that the\nhand follows the forearm during supination.\nDorsal radiocarpal: Found on the posterior side of the hand.\nIt contributes to the stability of the wrist, but also ensures\nthat the hand follows the forearm during pronation.\nUlnar collateral: Runs from the ulnar styloid process to the\ntriquetrum and pisiform. It acts to prevent excessive radial\n(lateral) deviation of the hand.\nRadial collateral: Runs from the radial styloid process to the\nscaphoid and trapezium. It acts to prevent excessive ulnar\n(medial) deviation of the hand.\n\n![Joints of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-upper-limb/figure_02_p5.png)\n*Joints of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nMovements of the wrist joint: Wrist joint allow for movement\nalong two axes. This means that flexion, extension, adduction\nand abduction can all occur at the wrist joint\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Falling on outstretched hand most commonly**\n> fractured carpal bone in wrist joint is Scaphoid. While most\n> common fracture of the wrist joint is Colles' fracture.\n\nThe shoulder Joint\nType: synovial ball and socket joint\nShoulder joint is formed by the articulation of the head of the\nhumerus with the glenoid cavity of the scapula\nLigaments of the shoulder joint:\nIn the shoulder joint, the ligaments play a key role in stabilizing\nthe bony structures.\nGlenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle and inferior) They\nact to stabilize the anterior aspect of the joint. They are the\nmain source of stability for the shoulder, holding it in place\nand preventing it from dislocating anteriorly.\nCoracohumeral ligament: It supports the superior part of the\njoint capsule.\nTransverse humeral ligament: This ligament spans the\ndistance between the two tubercles of the humerus. It holds\nthe tendon of the long head of the biceps in the\nintertubercular groove or bicipital groove\nCoraco-clavicular ligament:  This ligament composed of the\ntrapezoid and conoid ligaments and runs from the clavicle to\nthe coracoid process of the scapula. They work alongside the\nacromioclavicular ligament to maintain the alignment of the\nclavicle in relation to the scapula.\nCoracoacromial ligament. This ligament runs between the\nacromion and coracoid process of the scapula it forms the\ncoraco-acromial arch.  This structure overlies the shoulder\njoint, preventing superior displacement of the humeral\nhead.\nStability of the shoulder joint:\nRotator cuff muscles [SITS MUSCLES]\n\nGlenoid labrum: A fibrocartilaginous ridge surrounding the\nglenoid cavity.\nLigaments: Extra Capsular ligaments form the\ncoracoacromial arch.\nBiceps tendon - it acts as a minor humeral head depressor,\nthereby contributing to stability.\nMovements of the shoulder joint: Shoulder joint is highly\nmobile ball and socket joint allows various movements like\nflexion, extension, adduction, abduction, medial rotation, lateral\nrotation and circumduction.\nNOTE: All the movements of the shoulder joint by different\nmuscles and the innervation is discussed in muscles of upper\narm\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Shoulder joint dislocation\n> Anterior shoulder dislocation: Most common injury results\n> from forceful abduction, extension and external rotation\n> Clinical features:\n> Arm usually abducted and externally rotated.\n\nHumeral head often palpated anteriorly.\n\n![Joints of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-upper-limb/figure_03_p7.png)\n*Joints of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\nPatient cannot touch contralateral shoulder with hand of\naffected extremity.\nComplications of anterior shoulder dislocation:\nInjury to axillary nerve\nInjury to axillary artery\nInjury to rotator cuff\nPosterior Shoulder dislocation: occurs occasionally results\nfrom direct blow to the arm that produces forceful internal\nrotation and adduction.\nClinical features:\nArm usually adducted and internally rotated.\nPatient unable to perform external rotation.\nComplications of posterior shoulder dislocation: less\ncommon neurovascular injury\nFracture of posterior glenoid labrum\nFracture of humeral shaft or lesser tuberosity\nNote: Light bulb sign on the X-ray is the diagnostic feature of\nposterior shoulder dislocation	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
1e48ba3a-7c22-40ee-9961-11a3f54dc7b7	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	arterial-supply-of-lower-limb	Arterial Supply of Lower Limb	article	### 1: Femoral Artery:\n\nThe femoral artery is a continuation of the external iliac artery.\nIt begins behind the inguinal ligament, where it lies midway\nbetween the anterior superior iliac spine and the symphysis\npubis (the site for taking a femoral pulse). The artery\ndescends through the femoral triangle and the adductor canal,\nand it leaves the front of the thigh by passing through the\nopening in the adductor magnus and then entering the\npopliteal space as the popliteal artery.\nNote: In the femoral triangle, the artery is related laterally to\nthe femoral nerve and medially, in the upper part of its\ncourse, to the femoral vein\n\n#### Branches of femoral artery\n\nSuperficial circumflex iliac artery, which arises just below the\ninguinal ligament and runs laterally toward the anterior\nsuperior iliac spine.\nSuperficial epigastric artery, which arises just below the\ninguinal ligament and runs upward to the abdominal wall.\nDeep external pudendal artery and the superficial external\npudendal artery arise just below the inguinal ligament and\nrun medially to supply the skin of the scrotum (or labium\nmajus).\nProfunda femoris artery, which is a large branch that arises\nfrom the femoral artery approximately 4 cm below the\ninguinal ligament. It supplies structures in the anterior,\nmedial, and posterior fascial compartments of the thigh.\n\n### 2: Popliteal artery:\n\nPopliteal artery is a continuation of femoral artery. It extends\nfrom the opening in the adductor magnus to the lower border of popliteus muscle, where it divides into anterior and posterior\ntibial arteries.\n\n#### Anterior Tibial Artery\n\nThe anterior tibial artery arises at the bifurcation of the\npopliteal artery in the popliteal fossa. It passes forward\nbetween the tibia and the fibula through the upper part of\nthe interosseus membrane and enters the anterior\ncompartment of the leg. It then descends with the deep\nperoneal nerve to the front of the ankle joint, where it\nbecomes the dorsalis pedis artery\n\n#### Posterior Tibial Artery\n\nThe posterior tibial artery arises at the bifurcation of the\npopliteal artery in the popliteal fossa. It descends in the\nposterior compartment of the leg and is accompanied by the\ntibial nerve. The artery terminates behind the medial malleolus\nby dividing into the medial and the lateral plantar arteries.\n\n#### Medial Plantar Artery\n\nThe medial plantar artery is the smaller of the terminal\nbranches of the posterior tibial artery. It runs forward along the\nmedial border of the foot with the medial plantar nerve, and it\ngives off many muscular and cutaneous branches.\n\n#### Lateral Plantar Artery\n\nThe lateral plantar artery is the larger of the terminal\nbranches of the posterior tibial artery. It runs forward deep\nto the abductor hallucis and the flexor digitorum brevis\nmuscles with the lateral plantar nerve, and it ends by curving\nmedially to form the plantar arch through anastomosis with\nthe dorsalis pedis artery. The plantar arch gives off\nperforating and metatarsal arteries; the metatarsal arteries\ngive rise to digital arteries.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical high yield:**\n> Medical personal should know the precise position of the main\n> arteries within the lower limb.\n\nFemoral artery: This enters the thigh behind the inguinal\nligament at a point midway between the anterior superior\niliac spine and the symphysis pubis.\n\nPopliteal artery: This artery can be felt by gentle palpation in\nthe depths of the popliteal space, provided that the deep\nfascia is fully relaxed by passively flexing the knee joint.\nDorsalis pedis artery: This artery lies between the tendons of\nthe extensor hallucis longus and the extensor digitorum\nlongus, midway between the medial and lateral malleoli on\nthe front of the ankle.\nPosterior tibial artery: This artery passes behind the medial\nmalleolus, beneath the flexor retinaculum, and lies between\nthe tendons of flexor digitorum longus and the flexor hallucis\nlongus. The pulsations of the artery can be felt midway\nbetween the medial malleolus and the heel.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
196eaddc-d9fa-4512-9b01-26a2b71eb621	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	respiratory-viruses	Respiratory Viruses	article	Croup:\nCroup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a common respiratory\ndisease that typically occurs in children aged between 6\nmonths and 3 years.\nCauses\nParainfluenza virus types 1 or 3\nDiagnosis: Clinical\nClinical features\nSudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which associated\nwith:\nHoarseness\nStridor\nRespiratory distress.\nFever\nSymptoms are typically worse at night and increase with\nagitation.\nTreatment: Usually supportive in severe cases Dexamethasone\noral IV and nebulized adrenaline\nBronchiolitis:\nIt is a condition which predominately affects children aged\nless than 2 years, with a peak incidence between the ages of\nthree and six months.\nIt is caused by viral infections, most commonly respiratory\nsyncytial virus (RSV)\nClinical features\nSuspect bronchiolitis in infants who have experienced a\ncoryzal prodrome lasting 1-3 days, followed by persistent\ncough and:\nEither tachypnoea or chest recession (or both) and\nEither wheeze or crackles on chest auscultation (or both).\nOther common symptoms include:\nFever (usually of less than 39°C).\n\nPoor feeding (typically after 3 to 5 days of illness).\nApnoea without other clinical signs in young infants (in\nparticular those under 6 weeks of age).\nDiagnosis: Usually clinical\nTreatment: Supportive\nAdmit the baby or child if he has the following features:\nApnoea (observed or reported)\npersistent oxygen saturation (when breathing air) of less than\n90%, for children aged 6 weeks and over or less than 92%, for\nbabies under 6 weeks or children of any age with underlying\nhealth conditions\ninadequate oral fluid intake (50% to 75% of usual volume,\ntaking account of risk factors and using clinical judgement\nPersisting severe respiratory distress, for example grunting,\nmarked chest recession, or a respiratory rate of over 70\nbreaths/minute.\nRhino Virus:\nCauses Common Cold transmitted via respiratory droplets.\nIncubation period usually 24-72 hours\nDiagnosis: Clinical\nTreatment: Supportive\nInfluenza Virus:\nCauses seasonal epidemics fever, cough, headache and\nrunny nose\nIncubation period usually 24-96 hours\nDiagnosis: Clinical\nTreatment: Supportive\nImportant regarding influenza\nvaccine:\nRecommended annually for certain patients including:\npeople over 65 years old\nhealthcare professionals\npregnant women\npeople with chronic disease e.g., cystic fibrosis, renal disease,\nheart disease, asthma\nimmunocompromised individuals e.g., asplenism, HIV/AIDs\nand patients on chemotherapy\n\nWhooping cough:\nWhooping cough, also known as pertussis, is a highly\ninfectious disease caused by the bacterium Bordetella\npertussis.\nBordetella pertussis is gram-negative Cocco-bacilli\ntransmitted via respiratory droplet.\nIn young infants, the 'whoop' may be absent and coughing\nspasms may be followed by periods of apnoea and/or\ncyanosis.\nA protracted cough following pertussis infection may last for\n3 months or more and has been referred to as 'the one-\nhundred-day cough'.\nClinical features\nWhooping cough has 3 phases of symptoms.\nThe catarrhal phase (lasts between one and two weeks)\nNasal discharge.\nConjunctivitis.\nMalaise.\nSore throat.\nLow-grade fever.\nDry, unproductive cough.\nMaking a clinical diagnosis\nWhooping cough should be suspected if a person has an\nacute cough that has lasted for 14 days or more without\nanother apparent cause, and has one or more of the\nfollowing features:\nParoxysmal cough.\nInspiratory whoop.\nPost-tussive vomiting.\nUndiagnosed apnoeic attacks in young infants.\nClinical suspicion should be raised if the person is not fully\nimmunized or has been in contact with a person who is\nconfirmed or suspected of having whooping cough.\nInvestigations\nculture of a nasopharyngeal aspirate or swab from the\nposterior nasopharynx\nCollect cultures from the nasopharynx within 2 weeks of\ncough onset.\nDefinitive test for diagnosis, with a specificity of 100%.\nSensitivity is 30% to 60% if the specimen is obtained <2 weeks after symptoms start.\nNucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) of nasopharyngeal\naspirate or posterior nasopharyngeal swab\nNAAT, including polymerase chain reaction (PCR), can be\nobtained within 4 weeks of cough onset.\nNAAT testing has increased sensitivity compared with culture\n(94%) and is recommended as adjunct to culture.\nSerology\nOrder between 2 and 12 weeks after cough onset in patients\nwho have not received a pertussis-containing vaccine in the\npreceding 6 months.\nA serum anti-pertussis toxin antibody concentration of >100\nIU/mL is suggestive of infection.\nOral fluid testing\nDetection of anti-pertussis immunoglobulin G (IgG) in oral\nfluid is available in the UK for confirmation of pertussis in\nchildren >2 years of age and adolescents.\nFBC: An elevated WBC count and lymphocytosis are\nsuggestive of pertussis and are common in young children.\nManagement\nArrange admission if the patient is:\nIs 6 months of age or younger and acutely unwell.\nHas significant breathing difficulties (for example apnoea\nepisodes, severe paroxysms, or cyanosis).\nHas a significant complication (for example seizures or\npneumonia).\nIf admission is not needed\nprescribe an antibiotic if the onset of cough is within the\nprevious 21 days.\nClarithromycin for infants< 1 month\nAzithromycin or clarithromycin for children aged 1 month or\nolder, and non-pregnant adults.\nErythromycin for pregnant women.\nNote:The recommended exclusion period from nursery or\nschool is 48 hours after start of antibiotics or 21 days from\nstart of symptoms	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
8ff63772-ad6e-460c-a6bc-b13c88846e34	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	bones-of-the-lower-limb	Bones of the Lower Limb	article	Hip Bone:\nThe bones of the pelvis consist of the right and left hip bones,\nthe sacrum and the coccyx.\nThe hip bones articulate posteriorly with the sacrum at the\nsacroiliac joints and with each other anteriorly at the pubic\nsymphysis.\nHip bone has a large articular socket on the lateral surface,\nthe acetabulum, which together with the head of the femur,\nforms the hip joint.\nEach of the hip bones is formed by\nIlium (superiorly)\nPubis (anteroinferiorly)\nIschium (posteroinferiorly).\nJoints of the pelvis:\nLumbosacral joint: It is the joint between vertebra L5 and the\nbase of the sacrum, joined by an intervertebral disk and\nsupported by the illolumbar ligaments.\nSacroiliac joint: is a synovial joint of an irregular plane type\nbetween the articular surfaces of the sacrum and illum Is\ncovered by cartilage and is supported by the anterior, posterior,\nand interosseous sacroiliac ligaments. Transmits the weight of\nthe body to the hip bone.\nSacrococcygeal joint Is a cartilaginous joint between the\nsacrum and coccyx, reinforced by the anterior, posterior, and\nlateral sacrococcygeal ligaments.\nPubic symphysis Is a cartilaginous or fibrocartilaginous joint\nbetween the pubic bones in the median plane.\nSacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments:\n\nPatella:\nPatella is largest sesamoid bone lies within the tendon of\nquadriceps femoris muscle.\nThe patellar ligament is an inferior continuation of the\nquadriceps tendon that inserts at the tibial tuberosity.\nFactors stabilizing patella\nThere are several structures that work together to keep the\npatella aligned and stabilized on the femur to prevent\nexcessive lateral movement of the patella\nVastus medialis muscle on the medial thigh acts to draw\nthe patella in a medial direction., the patella is much more\nsusceptible to dislocation if there is weak vastus medialis\nmuscle.\nThe medial patellofemoral ligament, extending from the\nmedial aspect of the patella to the medial femoral condyle,\nprovides a significant force against lateral displacement.\nThe lateral femoral condyle on the lateral aspect of the\ntrochlear groove is normally slightly higher than the medial\naspect, providing a buttress to the patella on the lateral side.\n\n![Bones of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/bones-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Bones of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nPatellar dislocation: Most common dislocations of the occur\nlaterally and are caused by high force impact on the patella.\nBones of the foot:\nThere are three groups of bones in the foot\n\n### 1: Seven-tarsal bone\n\n### 2: Five metatarsal bones\n\n### 3: Fourteen phalanges.\n\nThe tarsal bones are organized into three rows:\nProximal group: Talus and Calcaneus\nIntermediate: Navicular\nDistal group: Cuboid and Cuneiforms\n\n![Bones of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/bones-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p4.png)\n*Bones of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
715302a4-84d8-47bd-b43b-e63ed292de19	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes-of-lower-limb	Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes of Lower Limb	article	Dermatomes and Myotomes of Lower\nLimb:\nLower Limb Reflexes:\n\n![Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes of Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes-of-lower-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes of Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes of Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes-of-lower-limb/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes of Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
afd8bc79-32c0-468d-8224-5b0b9e3e7340	ea7d744d-14da-4b24-81ac-b4371af3e1ce	anesthetic-drugs	Anesthetic Drugs	article	Benzodiazepines and Muscle relaxants:\nLocal anesthetic drugs:\nAnesthetic induction and\nmaintenance agents:\n\n![Anesthetic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/anesthesia-sedation-drugs/anesthetic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anesthetic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Anesthetic Drugs - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/anesthesia-sedation-drugs/anesthetic-drugs/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Anesthetic Drugs — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nAnesthetic induction and\nmaintenance agents: (Continues)\n\n![Anesthetic Drugs - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/anesthesia-sedation-drugs/anesthetic-drugs/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Anesthetic Drugs — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Anesthetic Drugs - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/anesthesia-sedation-drugs/anesthetic-drugs/figure_04_p2.png)\n*Anesthetic Drugs — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
ed4e4078-fdd0-48e6-b91b-5867f75b216c	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	muscles-of-the-upper-limb	Muscles of the Upper Limb	article	Forearm muscles\n\n#### Anterior forearm muscles superficial\n\ncompartment\n\n#### Anterior Forearm muscles intermediate\n\ncompartment:\n\n#### Anterior Forearm muscles deep compartment\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Key Point: All the muscles of anterior forearm are innervated**\n> by median nerve except flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_03_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nflexor digitorum profundus innervated by ulnar nerve.\nDigital attachment of flexor muscles\nof forearm:\nAll the anterior forearm muscles originate from the medial\nhumeral epicondyle.\nMuscles of Anterior Arm:\nMuscles of posterior Arm:\nTriceps brachii:\nTriceps brachii is a large thick muscle in the posterior arm and\nappears as a horseshoe-shaped muscle\nFunction:  Extension of forearm at elbow and extension of arm\nat shoulder joint.\nInnervation: Radial nerve\nMuscles of posterior forearm\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_04_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_05_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nThe muscles in the posterior compartment of the forearm are\ncommonly known as the extensor muscles.\nExtensor muscles are divided into superficial and deep\ncompartment.\n\n#### Superficial compartment\n\n#### Deep Compartment\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n\n### 1: Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis): is caused by a chronic\n\ninflammation or irritation of the origin of the extensor muscles\nof the forearm from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus as a\nresult of repetitive strain. It is a painful condition and common\nin tennis players.\n\n### 2: Golfer's elbow (medial epicondylitis):  is a painful condition\n\ncaused by a tear or chronic inflammation in the origin of flexor\nmuscles of the forearm\n\n### 3: Nursemaid's elbow or pulled elbow is a radial head\n\nsubluxation and occurs in toddlers when the child is lifted by\nthe wrist. The annular ligament (and other elbow ligaments) is\nnot fully developed in terms of strength, and thus, the radial\nhead can slip out of position.\nMuscles of the hand:\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_06_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_07_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nThenar muscles:\nThree short muscles located in the base of the thumb. All are\ninnervated by median nerve.\nHypothenar muscles:\nHypothenar muscle located at base of middle finger and form\nhypothenar eminence. All are innervated by ulnar nerve\nLumbrical Muscles:\nThere are four lumbrical muscles. Each one of these originates\nfrom a tendon of the flexor digitorum profundus. They pass\ndorsally and laterally around each finger, and inserts into the\nextensor hood.\nInnervation:\nMedial two lumbricals: Ulnar nerve\nLateral two lumbricals: Median nerve\nFunction:\nFlexion at the MCP joint and extension at the interphalangeal\n(IP) joints of each digit.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Denervation of these muscles is the basis of claw hand and\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_08_p4.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_09_p4.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\nhand of benediction.\nInterossei muscles: Located between the metacarpals of the\nhand. Divided into Dorsal and palmar Interossei\nAll the interossei muscles are innervated by ulnar nerve.\nDorsal Interossei:\nAbduction of the digits. Assists in flexion at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and extension at the\ninterphalangeal joints.\nPalmar Interossei:\nAdduction of the digits. Assists in flexion at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and extension at the\ninterphalangeal joints.\nMuscles of the Shoulder region:\nExtrinsic muscles, Intrinsic Muscles\nExtrinsic muscles: Connecting the Upper Limb to the Vertebral\nColumn and divided into superficial and deep group.\nSuperficial: Trapezius and Latissimus dorsi\nDeep: Levator Scapulae, Rhomboid minor and Rhomboid\nmajor\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_10_p5.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Ask the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if**\n> patient unable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve injury\n> and paralysis of trapezius muscle.\n\nIntrinsic muscles of the shoulder: Connecting the scapula to\nthe humerus\nThere are six muscles in this group the deltoid, teres major, and\nthe four rotator cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus,\nsubscapularis and teres minor).\nNote: All the rotator cuff muscles (SITS) are lateral rotators of\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 11](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_11_p6.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 11 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 12](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_12_p6.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 12 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\narm except "S" Subscapularis\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n> Assessment of rotator cuff muscles:\n> Rotator cuff tendonitis:\n> Refers to inflammation of tendons of rotator cuff muscles.\n\nSupraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator\ncuff muscle.\nMuscles of pectoral region:\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 13](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_13_p7.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 13 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 14](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_14_p7.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 14 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\nPectoralis Major: large fan shaped and most superficial muscle\nof the pectoral region.\nOrigin:\nClavicular head: Medial clavicle\nSternocostal head originates from the anterior surface of the\nsternum, the superior six costal cartilages and the\naponeurosis of the external oblique muscle.\nInsertion: intertubercular sulcus of the humerus\nFunction: Adduction and medial rotation of the upper limb\nand draws the scapula Antero inferiorly.\nInnervation: Lateral and medial pectoral nerves\nPectoralis minor: small muscle lies underneath the pectoralis\nmajor.\nOrigin: Originates from the 3rd-5th ribs\nInsertion: coracoid process of the scapula\nFunction: stabilization, depression, abduction or protraction,\ninternal rotation and downward rotation of the scapula.\nInnervation: Medial pectoral nerve\nSerratus anterior: Located more laterally in the chest wall\nOrigin: Consists of several muscular strips originate from lateral\nsurface of ribs 1-8\nInsertion: Attach to the costal surface of medial border of\nscapula\nFunction: holds the scapula against the ribcage and assist in\nprotraction and rotation of scapula\nInnervation: Long thoracic nerve\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl:**\n> winging of scapula: Scapula no longer held against the rib\n> cage and protrudes out of the back due to paralysis of serratus\n> anterior and injury to Long thoracic nerve.\n\nSubclavius: Small muscle located underneath the clavicle\nOrigin: First rib and its costal cartilage\nInsertion: Inferior surface of middle third of clavicle\nFunction: Anchors and depresses clavicle\nInnervation: Nerve to subclavius.\n\n![Muscles of the Upper Limb - Clinical Figure 15](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-upper-limb/figure_15_p9.png)\n*Muscles of the Upper Limb — Clinical Figure 15 (Page 9 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
a1577f01-4124-41d5-8021-f5afb5d55dbb	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	upper-limb-other-important-structures	Upper Limb Other Important Structures	article	Carpal Tunnel:\nThe carpel tunnel is the space that lies between the flexor\nretinaculum.\nContents of the carpal tunnel\nNote:\nThe ulnar nerve, ulnar artery and tendon of Palmaris longus\nruns superficial to flexor retinaculum and thus are not within\ncarpel tunnel.\nFlexor pollicis longus enter the carpel tunnel before reaching\nits insertion on the distal phalanx of the thumb.\nPronator quadratus is confined to the distal forearm and does\nnot reach the carpel tunnel.\nAnatomical snuffbox\nis a triangular depression found on the lateral aspect of the\ndorsum of the hand. It is best seen when the thumb is\nextended.\n\n![Upper Limb Other Important Structures - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-other-important-structures/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Other Important Structures — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Limb Other Important Structures - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-other-important-structures/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Other Important Structures — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Fracture of scaphoid bone: Falling on outstretched hand. The\n> scaphoid bone of the hand is the most commonly fractured\n> carpal bone\n> Clinical feature: Pain and tenderness in the anatomical\n> snuffbox.\n\nUlnar canal (Guyon's canal)\nTransmits ulnar neurovascular bundle from forearm into the\nhand.\nBorders:\nMedial (ulnar): pisiform, flexor carpi ulnaris tendon\nLateral (radial): hook of hamate.\nRoof: palmar carpal ligament.\nFloor: flexor retinaculum\nContents: Ulnar nerve and artery\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Ulnar canal syndrome refers to compression of the ulnar\n> nerve within the ulnar canal.\n\nFlexor Retinaculum:\nThe flexor retinaculum is a thickened band of fibrous\nconnective tissue on the volar aspect of the hand which forms\nthe roof of the carpal tunnel. The flexor retinaculum holds the\nflexor tendons in place at the wrist and prevents them from\nbowstringing.\nRelations of flexor retinaculum:\nThe ulnar artery, ulnar nerve, tendon of the palmaris longus\nand palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve pass\ninto the hand anterior to the flexor retinaculum, and\ntherefore do not pass through the carpal tunnel.\nThe flexor carpi radialis tendon passes through the lateral\naspect of the flexor retinaculum into the hand.\nThe four tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus, the four\ntendons of the flexor digitorum superficialis, the tendon of\nthe flexor pollicis longus and the median nerve pass into the\nhand posterior to the flexor retinaculum, within the carpal\ntunnel.\n\nExtensor Retinaculum:\nThe extensor retinaculum lies obliquely across the extensor\nsurface of the wrist joint, thickened part of the antecubital\nfossa and that holds the extensor tendons in place during\nmovement of the wrist.\nAttachments:\nLaterally: Lower part of the anterior border of the radius\nMedially: Styloid process of the ulna, Triquetral, and Pisiform\nContents\nThe extensor tendons pass through the extensor tunnel, deep\nto the extensor retinaculum, in six compartments lined by\nsynovial sheaths:\nThe tendons of the extensor carpi ulnaris and extensor digiti\nminimi have separate sheaths on the medial side of the\nwrist.\nThe tendons of the extensor digitorum and extensor indicis\nshare a synovial sheath on the posterior surface of the wrist.\nThe tendons of the abductor pollicis longus and extensor\npollicis brevis, the extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor\ncarpi radialis brevis, and the extensor pollicis longus have\nthree separate sheaths on the lateral side of the wrist.\nOther Clinical High Yield Stuff:\n\n### 1: Dupuytren contracture: is a progressive thickening,\n\nshortening, and fibrosis of the palmar fascia. producing a fixed\nflexion contracture of the hand, in which the fingers bend\ntoward the palm and cannot be fully extended (commonly\nfourth and fifth fingers).\n\n### 2: Volkmann contracture: is an ischemic contracture (flexion\n\ndeformity) resulting from necrosis of the forearm flexor\nmuscles.\n\n### 3: Tenosynovitis:  is commonly caused by an infection of the\n\nsynovial sheath and tendon (the long flexor tendon), causing\npain, swelling, and difficulty moving a joint. Rupture of the\ntendon sheath (puncture injury of the palm) may cause the\nspread of infection to the mid-palmar space and to the carpal tunnel. Likewise, tenosynovitis in the thumb may spread to the\nradial bursa and in the little finger to the ulnar bursa.\nNote: Infection in the middle three fingers is usually contained\nas they have separate synovial sheaths.\nThe synovial sheath of the little finger is usually continuous\nwith the common flexor sheath (the ulnar bursa) and thus\ninfection may spread to this sheath and from here to the\nmid-palmar space.\nInfections in the thumb may spread to the mid-palmar space\nvia the continuous synovial sheath of the flexor pollicis\nlongus (the radial bursa).\n\n### 4: Trigger finger: occurs when the long flexor tendon develops\n\na nodule or swelling that interferes with its gliding through the\npulley, causing pain at the joint and an audible clicking or\nsnapping. The condition is caused by rheumatoid arthritis,\nrepetitive trauma, and wear and tear of aging of the tendon.\n\n### 5: Mallet finger (hammer finger or baseball finger) is a finger\n\nwith permanent flexion of the distal phalanx as a result of an\navulsion of the lateral bands of the extensor tendon to the\ndistal phalanx.\n\n### 6:  Boutonniere deformity is a finger with abnormal flexion of\n\nthe middle phalanx and hyperextension of the distal phalanx as\na result of a rupture of the central band of the extensor tendon\n\n### 7:  Pancoast tumor in the apex of the lung can result in lower\n\ntrunk injury and, occasionally, Horner syndrome because of\nsympathetic ganglion compression (ptosis, anhidrosis, and\npupillary constriction). It can also cause thoracic outlet\nsyndrome.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
a481c744-8c1b-4025-81af-51cfae2653fc	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	lower-limb-other-significant-structures	Lower Limb Other Significant Structures	article	Femoral Triangle:\nThe wedge-shaped area is located within the superomedial\naspect of the anterior thigh.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High Yield:**\n> Contents of Femoral Triangle:\n> From lateral to medial: Useful mnemonic (NAVEL)\n> Femoral nerve\n> Femoral artery\n> Femoral vein\n> Empty space (consists of femoral canal)\n> Lymph nodes\n> Note: Femoral nerve lies lateral to femoral artery and vein\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: In Femoral hernia part of the bowel pushes**\n> into the femoral canal underneath the femoral triangle.\n\nFemoral Sheath:\nThe femoral sheath originates as a prolongation of\nextraperitoneal fascia and encloses the femoral artery, femoral\nvein and femoral canal.\nNote: The femoral nerve does not lie within the femoral sheath,\nbut instead lies lateral to it.\n\n![Lower Limb Other Significant Structures - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/lower-limb-other-significant-structures/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Lower Limb Other Significant Structures — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nFemoral Canal:\nThe Femoral canal lies most medial of the femoral sheath.\nBorders of the femoral canal: (Useful mnemonic LAMP: FILP)\nLateral border: Femoral vein (Immediately lateral)\nAnterior border: Inguinal ligament\nMedial border: Lacunar ligament\nPosterior ligament: Pectineal ligament, Pectineus muscle\nand superior ramus of pubic bone.\nContents: Deep inguinal lymph nodes, lymphatic vessels,\nempty space and loose connective tissue.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: In Femoral hernia part of the bowel pushes**\n> into the femoral canal underneath the femoral triangle.\n\nThe Arches of Foot:\nFoot has three arches, one transverse arch and two\nlongitudinal (medial and lateral) arches. Shape of arches allows\nthem to act in the same way as a spring, bearing the weight of\nthe body and absorbing the shock produced during\nlocomotion.\nFormed by tarsal, metatarsal bones and supported by\nligaments.\nPopliteal Fossa:\nDiamond shaped area located on the posterior aspect of the\nknee, and is the pathway to pass by which vessels and nerves\npass between the thigh and the leg.\n\n![Lower Limb Other Significant Structures - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/lower-limb-other-significant-structures/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Lower Limb Other Significant Structures — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nBoundaries and Contents of Popliteal\nFossa:\nContents (From medial to lateral): Popliteal artery, popliteal\nvein, tibial nerve and common fibular nerve.\nNote: Tibial and common fibular nerve are the most superficial\ncontents of the popliteal fossa. Both are the branches of sciatic\nnerve\nThe common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris\ntendon, travelling along the lateral margin of the popliteal\nTibial nerve lies more medial to common fibular nerve.\n\n![Lower Limb Other Significant Structures - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/lower-limb-other-significant-structures/figure_03_p3.png)\n*Lower Limb Other Significant Structures — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
9e0a7503-c39a-4c08-9eee-06f9abfb0dd4	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	lymphatic-drainage-of-lower-limb	Lymphatic Drainage of Lower Limb	article	Inguinal lymph Nodes:\nThe inguinal lymph nodes are found in the upper aspect of the\nfemoral triangle.\n\n#### Superficial Inguinal Nodes\n\nThe superficial inguinal lymph nodes are located in the\nsuperficial fascia, forming a line below the inguinal ligament,\nand medially extending inferiorly along the terminal part of the\ngreat saphenous vein. They receive lymph from the gluteal\nregion, lower abdominal wall, perineum, and superficial regions\nof the lower limb.\nSuperficial inguinal lymph nodes drain into deep inguinal\nnodes.\n\n#### Deep Inguinal Nodes\n\nLie along the medial side of femoral vein within the femoral\ncanal. They receive all lymph from superficial inguinal lymph\nnodes, deep lymphatics associated with the femoral vessels\nand from the glans penis or clitoris in the perineum.\nThe inguinal lymph nodes drain to the external iliac lymph\nnodes associated with the external iliac artery in the\nabdomen.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High Yield to remember:**\n> Penile skin and prepuce = superficial inguinal lymph nodes\n> Glans penis and penile urethra=deep inguinal lymph nodes\n> Scrotum=Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\n> Testes= lumber or para-aortic lymph nodes\n> prostate= internal iliac nodes	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
3993bb38-ef27-4457-b640-44f2f1170988	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	bones-of-thorax	Bones of Thorax	article	Ribs:\nConsist of 12 pairs of bones that form the main part of the bony\nthoracic wall, extending from the vertebrae posteriorly to or\ntoward the sternum anteriorly. Increase the anteroposterior\nand transverse diameters of the thorax by their movements.\n\n### 1. Structure\n\nTypical ribs are ribs 3 through 9, each of which has a head,\nneck, tubercle, and body (shaft). The head articulates with the\ncorresponding vertebral bodies and intervertebral disks and\nsupra-adjacent vertebral bodies.\nThe body (shaft) is thin and flat and turns sharply anteriorly at\nthe angle; it has a costal groove that follows the inferior and\ninternal surface of the rib and shields the Intercostal vessels\nand nerves from injury.\nThe tubercle articulates with the transverse processes of the\ncorresponding vertebrae, except ribs 11 and 12.\n\n### 2. Classification\n\nTrue ribs are the first seven ribs (ribs 1-7), which are attached\nto the sternum by their costal cartilages.\nFalse ribs are the lower five ribs (ribs 8-12); ribs 8 to 10 are\nconnected to the costal cartilages immediately above, and\nthus, the 7th to 10th costal cartilages form the anterior costal\narch or costal margin.\nFloating ribs are the last two ribs (ribs 11 and 12), which are\nconnected only to the vertebrae.\nIntercostal space:\nThe intercostal space lies between adjacent ribs and mostly\ncontains intercostal muscles. Since there are 12 ribs on each\nside, there are 11 intercostal spaces, each numbered for the rib\nsuperior to it. Intercostal nerves, arteries and veins lie in the costal groove along the inferior margin of the rib, lying in a\nplane between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> vein is the most superior structure in each intercostal space\n> and is therefore highest in the costal groove. The artery is\n> inferior to the vein and the nerve inferior to the artery and\n> often not protected by the groove, (Useful mnemonic VAN).\n\nTherefore, the nerve is the most vulnerable structure in a\npenetrating injury to the upper aspect of the intercostal space.\nAny needle or tube inserted in the intercostal space should be\nplaced immediately above the superior margin of a rib to avoid\ninjury to the intercostal neurovascular bundle.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
32ee60dc-29b7-4169-bcd1-3550979e4c38	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	dermatomes-of-trunk	Dermatomes of Trunk	article	Dermatomal supply of trunk:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n\n![Dermatomes of Trunk - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/dermatomes-of-trunk/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Dermatomes of Trunk — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Dermatomes of Trunk - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/dermatomes-of-trunk/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Dermatomes of Trunk — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
7a095dd2-8334-4bc7-86e3-6c20e53f4ee7	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	structures-of-the-thorax-mediastinum	Structures of the Thorax - Mediastinum	article	Mediastinum:\nThe mediastinum is an interpleural space and consists of the\nsuperior mediastinum and inferior mediastinum. The inferior\nmediastinum further divides into the anterior, middle, and\nposterior mediastina.\nThe middle mediastinum contains the heart.\nThe posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus, thoracic\naorta, azygos and hemiazygos veins, thoracic duct, vagus\nnerves, and sympathetic splanchnic nerves	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
7f9c6f39-7f3c-4297-8742-8762a8f1f5bd	797f6930-be16-40f1-b028-303cf92c244a	drugs-toxicity-and-antidotes	Drugs Toxicity and Antidotes	article	![Drugs Toxicity and Antidotes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/drugs-toxicity-and-antidotes/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Drugs Toxicity and Antidotes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
42082273-b05e-44ca-a9c2-da1fcc0b2091	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-abdominal-trauma	Abdominal Trauma — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Abdominal Trauma — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 26-year-old male is brought to the resuscitation room following a stab wound to the left upper quadrant of his abdomen. On examination, he is pale and diaphoretic with heart rate 130 bpm, BP 82/46 mmHg, and obvious peritonitis with involuntary abdominal guarding. What is the immediate definitive management?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Abdominal Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/abdominal-trauma/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Immediate transfer to the operating theatre for Emergency Laparotomy\n- **B.** Perform a contrast-enhanced abdominal CT scan first\n- **C.** Diagnostic peritoneal lavage (DPL) at the bedside\n- **D.** Administer 3 litres of 0.9% normal saline and observe in the ICU\n- **E.** Perform upper gastrointestinal endoscopy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn abdominal trauma (penetrating or blunt), haemodynamic instability (hypotension, tachycardia) or overt signs of peritonitis/evisceration are absolute indications for immediate emergency laparotomy. CT scanning is strictly contraindicated in unstable patients.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Abdominal Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/abdominal-trauma/figure_06_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 32-year-old restrained driver involved in a high-speed road traffic collision is haemodynamically stable. A Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma (FAST) scan is performed in the resuscitation room. What are the four standard anatomical acoustic windows evaluated during a FAST examination?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Abdominal Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/abdominal-trauma/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Right upper quadrant (Morison's pouch), Left upper quadrant (splenorenal recess), Pelvic / retrovesical pouch, and Subxiphoid pericardial window\n- **B.** Gallbladder, Appendix, Spleen, and Urinary bladder\n- **C.** Aorta, IVC, Carotid arteries, and Femoral vessels\n- **D.** Bilateral lungs, Stomach, and Pancreas\n- **E.** Scrotum, Inguinal canals, Popliteal fossa, and Umbilicus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe 4 standard FAST windows evaluate for pathological free fluid (haemoperitoneum / haemopericardium): (1) RUQ (hepatorenal space / Morison's pouch - most sensitive dependent space); (2) LUQ (splenorenal recess and subphrenic space); (3) Pelvic (pouch of Douglas / retrovesical space); (4) Subxiphoid (pericardial space). Extended FAST (eFAST) adds bilateral anterior thoracic views for pneumothorax/haemothorax.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich solid abdominal organ is most frequently injured in blunt abdominal trauma, and what physical examination sign denotes referred left shoulder tip pain caused by diaphragmatic irritation from splenic rupture?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Abdominal Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/abdominal-trauma/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Spleen; Kehr's sign\n- **B.** Liver; Murphy's sign\n- **C.** Pancreas; Grey Turner's sign\n- **D.** Kidney; Cullen's sign\n- **E.** Urinary bladder; Rovsing's sign\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe spleen is the most commonly injured visceral organ in blunt trauma (followed closely by the liver). Kehr's sign is referred pain to the left shoulder tip caused by blood irritating the peritoneal surface of the left hemidiaphragm (innervated by the phrenic nerve C3-C5).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 40-year-old unrestrained driver involved in a head-on collision sustains steering wheel impact to his epigastrium. Forty-eight hours later, he develops fever, persistent vomiting, epigastric tenderness, and back pain. Serum amylase is elevated. Contrast CT demonstrates retroperitoneal air and fluid surrounding the second portion of the duodenum. What injury has occurred?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Abdominal Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/abdominal-trauma/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Traumatic retroperitoneal duodenal rupture\n- **B.** Splenic laceration Grade I\n- **C.** Acute cholecystitis\n- **D.** Diaphragmatic herniation\n- **E.** Mesenteric tear of the sigmoid colon\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBlunt trauma to the epigastrium (steering wheel, bicycle handlebar) compresses the retroperitoneal duodenum against the lumbar spine. Duodenal rupture often has an insidious, delayed clinical presentation because retroperitoneal extravasation does not cause early classical signs of anterior peritonitis. CT hallmark is retroperitoneal air and fluid around the right kidney/duodenum.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the primary management strategy for a haemodynamically stable patient with a Grade III blunt hepatic or splenic laceration confirmed on contrast CT who has no signs of peritonitis?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Abdominal Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/abdominal-trauma/figure_05_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Non-Operative Management (NOM) with intensive HDU monitoring, serial clinical exams, and angiographic embolisation if needed\n- **B.** Immediate mandatory total splenectomy\n- **C.** Immediate exploratory laparotomy\n- **D.** Bedside diagnostic peritoneal lavage\n- **E.** Discharge home with bed rest instructions\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn blunt hepatic and splenic injuries, Non-Operative Management (NOM) is the gold standard for haemodynamically stable patients regardless of injury grade, preserving organ function with success rates >85-90%. It requires monitored care, serial abdominal exams, haemoglobin tracking, and interventional radiology angio-embolisation for active contrast extravasation ('blush').\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f85510bb-4d60-4080-af86-a9910d912d05	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	burn-injury	Burn Injury & Resuscitation	article	# Burn Injury & Resuscitation\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- A burn is defined as a traumatic injury to the skin or other organic tissue primarily caused by thermal or other acute exposures\n- Children and adults are more vulnerable to burn injury. It is important to exclude NAI in children ly-Pes of burn: Thermal : This is the most common type of burn and includes flame burns, scalds (from hot liquids) and contact burns (from hot objects e.g. an iron or radiator). Acids and alkalis found in household chemical products can produce very deep burns through coagulative and liquefactive necrosis.4They will continue to burn the skin until completely removed. Electrical: As an electrical current travels through the body it creates an entry and exit point, damaging tissue along its path as it is converted from electrical to thermal energy. Cold exposure (frostbite)zThese burns are caused by ice crystals which can form both intra and extracellularly. Radiation;-Radio frequency energy or ionising radiation causes tissue damage. The most common type of radiation burn is sun burn. Pathophysiology: _\n\nJackson's burn wound model\n- Local response Zone of Coagulation: occurs at the point of maximum damage i.e. the nearest point to the heat source. There is irreversible tissue necrosis here due to coagulation of proteins. Zone of Stasis:surrounding the zone of coagulation, this area is characterised by decreased tissue perfusion. Burns resuscitation can help to increase tissue perfusion and prevent this damage from becoming irreversible. otherwise it could evolve into an area of necrosis. Zone of Hyperaemia: the outermost zone as its name suggests is where there is increased tissue perfusion. This is a reversible zone. Note: The release of cytokines and other inflammatory mediators at the site of injury has a systemic effect once the burn reaches 20-30% of total body surface area. Such as CVS changes (Capillary permeability is increased). Respiratory changes(bronchoconstriction). metabolic rate increases. Initial assessment\n- Need to follow trauma protocol such as primary or secondary survey\n\n- Use ABCDE approach to the primary survey is critical to ensure that any life- threatening issues are not missed Airway\n- Following features that increase the suspicion of airway obstruction or inhalation injury\n- Assessment Singed nasal/facial hairs Burns to the face/oropharynx Inflamed oropharynx Breathing\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nSit patient upright Any suspected airway\n\n- Circulation & Fluid resuscitation\n- Assessment\n- Burns total body surface area (TBSA) in Adults and in children can cause profound circulatory shock that can occur from both large fluid losses through tissue damage and from a systemic inflammatory response\n- Burns <15% in adults and in children do not require immediate fluid resuscitation.\n- Capillary refill time (CRT), blood pressure and mucous membrane assessment are important indicators of hydration status.\n- early catheterisation is important as urine output is a reliable sign that can demonstrate poor perfusion and serve as a guide to ongoing resuscitation.\n\n- ln circumferential limb burns, blood supply to the extremities should be checked regularly.\n- ln a significant burn there can be an increased metabolic demand on the patient which can cause organ dysfunction therefore:\n- blood tests (FBC, U&E, C-reactive Protein, LFTs and capillary blood glucose) should be send.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- Fluid resuscitation:\n- AduIts with burns\n- 15% TBSA and children with burns TBSA require fluid resuscitation.\n- Get IV access, If it's not possible to get IV or IO access through unburnt skin then place the IV in burnt skin.\n- use parkland formula (2-4mI x weight in kg x %TBSA).\n- AduIt: 2-ml (Hartman or plasma-lyte) x weight in kg x %TBSA\n- Paeds 3-ml x weight in kg x %TBSA\n- the total resuscitation fluid required over 24 hours from the time of the burn. One half of fluid should be given in first 8hrs and remaining half in 16 hours\n- Note: Urine output targets should be doubled if rhabdomyolysis is suspected\n- Send blood tests\n\n- Creatine kinase is a useful test to perform to assess muscle breakdown\n- EvaIuate any areas of circumferential burns in limbs and constantly reassess perfusion.any deterioration in the circulation to a limb could indicate ischaemia or a compartment syndrome. require urgent intervention such as escharotomy or fasciotomy. Disability-exposure\n- Full exposure to assess of size and depth of burns\n- maintain body temperature by both active and passive warming.\n- Clean the burn with normal saline and cover with strips of cling film\n- Analgesia:\n- Non-pharmacoIogical\n- cooling the burn under cold running water and covering with cling film. Cling film also helps to reduce heat loss and the risk of infection. and NSAIDs or opiods and ketamine Assessment of the burn (Secondary survey) _\n- Assessment of burn Formulas/charts\n\n- Hoad and neck Anterior Lund and Browder chart Arm 9%\n- Assess depth of the burn Superficial epidermal burn: only epidermis affected . Skin is red and painful but not blistered eg: sun burn\n\nSuperficial partial thickness : burn extend to superficial layer. Skin is moist painfully hypersensitive and blanch to touch Deeæpartial thickness burn: Extend to deeper layer dermis. Skin is drier ,cherry red, less painful, blistered red or mottled appearance and doesn't blanch to touch Full thickness burn : extends through entire dermis to subcutaneous tissue skin appears waxy white, brown , painless , generally dry and doesn't blanch to pressure\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof burn wound Chemical burn & management\n\nElectrical burn & management\n\n- Referral to specialist burn center\n- AII burns 22% TBSA in children or 23% TBSA in adults\n- AII full thickness burns\n- AII circumferential burns\n- Any burn not healed in 2 weeks\n- Any burn with suspicion of non-accidental injury should be referred to a burn unit/centre for expert assessment within 24 hours\n- AII burns to hands, feet, face, perineum, or genitalia\n\n- Any chemical, electrical or friction burn\n- Any cold injury\n- Any unwell/febrile child with a burn\n- Any concerns regarding burn injuries and co-morbidities(e.g. serious medical conditions (such cardiac dysfunction, diabetes, cancer or immunosuppression) that may affect treatment or healing of the burn Escharotomy\n- VVith full thickness and deep partial thickness burns, the dermis can become very stiff. If this occurs circumferentially over the chest it can restrict chest wall mover-nent and lead to mechanical respiratory failure. Equally if there are circumferential burns to the limbs this can cause limb ischaemia. In these cases an emergency escharotomy needs to be undertaken to release the rigid skin to allow: adequate ventilation (if the chest is involved) or circulation (in the limb).\n- Procedure: The limb should be kept in the anatomical position.\n- The area is cleaned and incised along the anatomical lines with a scalpel down to the fat\n- The incision should not go down to the muscle or fascia\n- For the limbs the incisions need to release both medial and lateral aspects\n- For the chest the incision needs to release the whole breast plate\n- Care must be taken to avoid the named important structures\n\nprevious  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_06_p8.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_07_p9.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_08_p9.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_09_p14.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 14 Reference)*\n\n![Burn Injury - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/burn-injury/figure_10_p15.jpeg)\n*Burn Injury — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 15 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b300c04d-d44a-4ae8-9bf3-5eba761a0833	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-burn-injury	Burn Injury & Resuscitation — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Burn Injury & Resuscitation — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 70 kg man sustains deep partial-thickness burns involving his entire anterior torso (18%) and the entirety of both upper limbs (9% + 9% = 18%), totaling 36% Total Body Surface Area (TBSA). Using the Parkland formula (4 ml x kg x %TBSA), how much Hartmann's solution should be administered over the FIRST 8 hours from the time of injury?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 5,040 ml (half of the 10,080 ml 24-hour total)\n- **B.** 10,080 ml\n- **C.** 2,500 ml\n- **D.** 1,000 ml\n- **E.** 15,000 ml\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nParkland formula: Total 24-hour fluid = 4 ml x body weight (kg) x % TBSA burned. Here: 4 x 70 x 36 = 10,080 ml of Hartmann's. Half of this total (5,040 ml) must be administered in the first 8 hours calculated from the time of the BURN (not time of arrival), and the remaining half over the subsequent 16 hours.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_06_p9.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most reliable physiological endpoint to titrate fluid resuscitation volume in an adult burn patient without electrical injury?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Target urine output of 0.5 to 1.0 ml/kg/hour\n- **B.** Systolic blood pressure >160 mmHg\n- **C.** Central venous pressure of exactly 15 mmHg\n- **D.** Serum sodium level >145 mmol/L\n- **E.** Skin turgor over the chest\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn burn resuscitation, fluid rates must be titrated hourly based on target urine output: 0.5 - 1.0 ml/kg/hr in adults (and 1.0 - 1.5 ml/kg/hr in children). In high-voltage electrical injuries with myoglobinuria, target urine output is higher (1.5 - 2.0 ml/kg/hr) until urine clears.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_07_p9.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 35-year-old firefighter was rescued from an enclosed room fire. He has facial burns, singed nasal hairs, carbonaceous soot in his oropharynx, and is hoarse with early inspiratory stridor. What is the immediate priority management?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Immediate endotracheal intubation by an experienced airway operator\n- **B.** Inhaled nebulised salbutamol and observation\n- **C.** Oral dexamethasone 8mg\n- **D.** Chest radiography before any airway intervention\n- **E.** High-flow oxygen via nasal prongs\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSigns of upper airway thermal injury and inhalation injury (facial burns, soot in sputum/oropharynx, hoarseness, stridor) warn of rapid, catastrophic pharyngeal and glottic oedema. Endotracheal intubation must be performed early before anatomical landmarks are obliterated by massive swelling.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_08_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA patient rescued from a house fire is confused with cherry-red skin appearance and normal pulse oximetry (SpO2 99% on room air). Arterial blood gas co-oximetry reveals a carboxyhaemoglobin (COHb) level of 34%. What is the definitive treatment?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 100% high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather reservoir mask, and consider hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) therapy\n- **B.** Wean oxygen because SpO2 is normal\n- **C.** Administer IV methylene blue\n- **D.** Intravenous N-acetylcysteine infusion\n- **E.** Perform therapeutic phlebotomy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nStandard pulse oximeters cannot distinguish carboxyhaemoglobin from oxyhaemoglobin, giving falsely normal SpO2 readings in carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning. Management is 100% normobaric oxygen via reservoir mask (reduces CO half-life from 320 min to 80 min). Hyperbaric oxygen is considered for COHb >25% (>15% in pregnancy), loss of consciousness, severe metabolic acidosis, or neurological deficits.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_09_p15.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 45-year-old man sustains full-thickness circumferential burns to his entire chest. During resuscitation, the ventilator peak inspiratory pressures rise sharply to 50 cm H2O, chest expansion becomes rigid and severely restricted, and systolic BP falls to 75 mmHg. What emergency bedside procedure is indicated?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Burn Injury & Resuscitation)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/burn-injury/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Emergency Chest Escharotomy\n- **B.** Needle thoracocentesis in the second intercostal space\n- **C.** Administration of high-dose intravenous muscle relaxants only\n- **D.** Pericardiocentesis\n- **E.** Incision and drainage of burn blisters\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCircumferential full-thickness chest burns create a rigid, inelastic eschar that acts as a tourniquet. As underlying tissue oedema accumulates, chest wall compliance drops severely, preventing ventilation and compressing the great veins (thoracic compartment syndrome). Emergency escharotomy (incising the burnt dead tissue down to subcutaneous fat in anterior axillary lines and transverse upper/lower margins) relieves restriction.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9ab7f895-a6ab-4076-86bf-1088356db1b2	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	joints-of-the-lower-limb	Joints of the Lower Limb	article	Joints of the lower limb:\n\n### 1: Ligaments of the hip joint:\n\nIntracapsular\nThe only intracapsular ligament is the ligament of head of\nfemur (Ligamentum teres). It is a relatively small structure,\nwhich runs from the acetabular fossa to the fovea of the femur.\nExtracapsular ligaments:\nIliofemoral ligament: Strongest of all located anteriorly,\nprevents hyperextension of the hip joint.\nIschiofemoral:  Reinforcing the capsule posteriorly. Preventing\nhyperextension and medial rotation of the femur at hip joint\n\n![Joints of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Joints of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nPubofemoral:  Reinforcing the capsule anteriorly and inferiorly\npreventing excessive abduction and extension.\nNote: Most common hip dislocation is posterior and sciatic\nnerve is most commonly injured\n\n### 2: Ligaments of knee joint:\n\nThe major ligaments in the knee joint are:\nPatellar ligament: continuation of the quadriceps femoris\ntendon distal to the patella. It attaches to the tibial\ntuberosity.\nCollateral ligaments: Prevent excessive medial or lateral\nmovement.\nTibial (medial) collateral ligament: wide and flat ligament,\nfound on the medial side of the joint, act to stabilize the joint\nmedially.\nFibular (lateral) collateral ligament: Attached distally to the\nlateral fibula act to stabilize the knee joint laterally.\nValgus deformity: Also known as Knock-Kneed.\nMore stress on lateral compartment\nLigament damaged is Medial collateral.\nVarus deformity: Also known as bow legged.\nMore stress on medial compartment\nLigament damaged is lateral collateral.\nNote: lateral collateral ligament inserts on the head of fibula\nwhere it joins with bicep femoris tendon to form conjoint\ntendon.\nCruciate Ligaments\nAnterior cruciate ligament: Attaches at the anterior\nintercondylar region of the tibia where it blends with the\nmedial meniscus. It ascends posteriorly to attach to the femur\nin the intercondylar fossa. It prevents anterior dislocation of the tibia onto the femur. ACL relax during flexion and taut\nduring extension torn in hyperextension injury.\nPosterior cruciate ligament: Attaches at the posterior\nintercondylar region of the tibia and ascends anteriorly to\nattach to the anteromedial femoral condyle. It prevents\nposterior dislocation of the tibia onto the femur. PCL relax\nduring extension and taut during flexion, torn in hyperflexion\ninjury\nNote:\nUnhappy triad:  common in Rugby players. lateral blow to the\nextended knee with a twisting fall to the same side\nRefers to injury of Medial collateral, medial meniscus and\nanterior cruciate ligament\nBursae of the knee joint:\nBursa: synovial fluid filled sac within joints provide low friction\nsurfaces for the movement of tendons associated with joint.\nSuprapatellar bursa located between the quadriceps\nfemoris and the femur (Recall)\nPrepatellar bursa found between the apex of the patella and\nthe skin. Housemaid knee (Swelling on the anterior side of\nknee) is due to inflammation of Prepatellar bursa\nInfrapatellar bursa: split into deep and superficial. The deep\nbursa lies between the tibia and the patella ligament. The\nsuperficial lies between the patella ligament and the skin.\nClergyman's Knee is due to inflammation of infrapatellar\nbursa caused by clergymen kneeling on hard surfaces during\nprayer\nMenisci of knee joint:\nThe medial and lateral menisci are fibrocartilage structures in\nthe knee that serve two functions:\n\nDeepen the articular surface of the tibia, thus increasing\nstability of the joint.\nActing as a shock absorber by increasing surface area to\nfurther dissipate forces\nMedial menisci also fixed to tibial collateral ligament,\nDamage to the tibial collateral ligament also results in a\nmedial meniscal tear.\nNote: Locking- Unlocking mechanism of knee joint\nLocking of Knee: During fully extended knee, the knee\npassively locks because of medial rotation of the femoral\ncondyles on the tibial plateau (the "screw-ho mechanism"). Full\nmedial rotation and extension tightens all the ligaments.\nUnlocking of Knee: Locking is reversed by contraction of\npopliteus muscle by initiating lateral movement of femur on\ntibia\nAnkle ligaments\nEversion injury = medial ligament or medial deltoid ligament\ninjury associated with tear of medial malleolus most\ncommonly\ninversion injury= Lateral collateral ligament injured (subdivided\ninto 3 ligaments) most frequent ligament injury during ankle\nsprain or inversion injury commonly affected ligament is\nAnterior talofibular ligament followed by calcaneofibular\nligament (both are ligaments of lateral collateral ligament)\nassociated with tear of lateral malleolus most commonly\nLeast injured during inversion injury post talofibular\nligament\nThe spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular ligament) is a\nbroad thick ligament that extends from the calcaneus to the\nnavicular bone. It supports the head of the talus, maintaining\nthe stability of the talocalcaneonavicular joint and resists\ndepression of the medial arch of the foot. The spring ligament\ntakes the weight of the body from the talus and transmits it to\nthe forefoot.\n\nTibiofibular joint\nSub talar joint:\nTalocalcaneonavicular joint:\n\n![Joints of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p5.png)\n*Joints of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Joints of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-lower-limb/figure_03_p5.png)\n*Joints of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Joints of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/joints-of-the-lower-limb/figure_04_p5.png)\n*Joints of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
b0dc15d0-1e61-4c37-9bf3-af7def460e18	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	muscles-of-the-lower-limb	Muscles of the Lower Limb	article	Muscles of Gluteal Region:\nMuscles of the gluteal region are divided into two groups.\n\n### 1: Superficial gluteal muscles\n\nNote: Greater trochanter of femur is site of insertion of gluteus\nMedius, minimums and piriformis.\n\n### 2: Deep gluteal muscles:\n\nNote: The piriformis muscle is an important landmark in the\ngluteal region. The superior gluteal nerve and vessels emerge\ninto the gluteal region superiorly to the piriformis. Sciatic nerve\nenters the gluteal region directly inferior to the piriformis\nmuscle.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nTrendelenburg Sign: Trendelenburg gait or waddling gait\noccurs when your hip abductor muscles are weakened. This\nmuscle group includes the gluteus Medius and minimus\ninnervated by superior gluteal nerve. The weakness of these\nmuscles causes drooping of the pelvis to the contralateral\nside while walking.\nThe Trendelenburg sign is produced when the patient is asked\nto stand without support on each leg. In a positive sign, pelvic\ndrop will occur on the unsupported leg. For example, if the\nright gluteal muscles are weak, the left side of the pelvis will\ndrop.\n\n#### Anterior compartment of thigh\n\nmuscles:\nNote: All the muscles of anterior compartment of thigh are\nflexors of thigh at hip joint, while quadriceps femoris is\nextensor of thigh at knee joint and Sartorius also flexes thigh at\nknee.\nNote:    Sartorius originates from anterior superior iliac spine\nRectus femoris originates from anterior inferior iliac spine.\n\n#### Medial Compartment of thigh\n\nmuscles:\nMedial compartment consists of five Indvidual muscles.\nAll the muscles of medial compartment of thigh causes\nadduction of thigh at hip joint except obturator externus which\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\ncauses lateral rotation of thigh at hip joint.\nGracilis along with adduction at hip joint also assist in flexion at\nknee joint.\nPosterior thigh muscles: Also known as hamstrings, consists\nof three individual muscles.\nNote: All the muscles of posterior compartment of thigh are\nextensors of thigh at hip and flexors at knee joint. While Biceps\nfemoris is also lateral rotator of thigh at hip joint and\nSemitendinosus and Semimembranosus also medial rotators of\nthigh at hip joint\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_04_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_05_p3.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: All the muscles of the posterior thigh originate**\n> from the ischial tuberosity of the pelvis. In some cases, mostly\n> in young athletes, sudden and forceful contraction of the\n> hamstring muscles can result in an avulsion fracture of the\n> ischial tuberosity. It can also be associated with sciatic nerve\n> damage.\n\nMuscles in the anterior compartment\nof leg:\n\n#### Anterior leg consists of four muscles\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Foot Drop (Discussed in nerves of the lower**\n> limb)\n> Muscles in the Posterior compartment of leg: Consists of\n> seven muscles divided into superficial and deep groups. All are\n> innervated by tibial nerve\n> Superficial group: Mnemonic (GPS)\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_06_p4.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n#### Deep muscles in the posterior leg\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Ruptured calcaneal tendon: Tear of calcaneal tendon occurs\n> during forceful plantarflexion of the foot. Patients will be\n> unable to plantarflex the foot and affected foot will be\n> permanently dorsiflexed.\n\nMuscles in the lateral compartment\nof leg:\nConsists of two muscles, fibularis longus and brevis also\ncalled peroneus longus and peroneus brevis.\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_07_p5.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_08_p5.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nMuscles of the Sole of foot:\nMuscles of sole of foot. (4 layers) = Must remember\nhighlighted one\nFirst layer: Abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis &\nabductor digitorum minimi\nSecond layer: flexor digitorum accessorius, 4 lumbricals and\ntendons of flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum\nlongus\nThird layer: flexor hallucis brevis, flexor digitorum minimi\nbrevis & adductor hallucis\nFourth layer: Plantar and dorsal interossei muscles, Two long\ntendons of extrinsic muscles - tibialis posterior and fibularis\nlongus.\nNote: Plantar arteries and nerves lie within the first and\nsecond layer of the sole of foot.\n\n![Muscles of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/muscles-of-the-lower-limb/figure_09_p6.png)\n*Muscles of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
4a13c203-e1fc-41ab-b32b-ca3a69e77310	79ca1dff-2503-4854-83e8-e4348455baa6	nerves-of-the-lower-limb	Nerves of the Lower Limb	article	Femoral nerve\nNerve roots: L2-L4\nMotor functions: Innervates the anterior thigh muscles.\nSensory functions:\nAnatomical course:\nThe femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus.\nIt is derived from the anterior rami of nerve roots L2, L3 and\nL4\nAfter arising from the lumbar plexus, the femoral nerve\ntravels inferiorly through the psoas major muscle of the\nposterior abdominal wall. It supplies branches to the iliacus\nand pectineus muscles in the abdomen prior to entering the\nthigh.\nPassing underneath the inguinal ligament to enter the\nfemoral triangle, in this triangle femoral nerve is located\nlateral to femoral vessels (Femoral artery and Vein). Useful\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Mnemonic from lateral to medial (NAVEL). Below the inguinal**\nligament femoral nerve divides into anterior and posterior\ndivisions\n\n### 1: Anterior division of the femoral nerve\n\n#### Anterior cutaneous branches\n\nBranch to sartorius\nBranch to pectineus\n\n### 2: Posterior division of the femoral nerve\n\nSaphenous nerve\nBranches to quadriceps femoris\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nNote: In posterior abdominal wall. Femoral nerve supplies\nbranches to the iliacus and pectineus muscles prior to entering\nthe thigh.\nFemoral nerve Sensory innervation of lateral and posterior\nthigh: (Important to remember)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Damage to the femoral nerve causing impaired**\n> flexion at hip and impaired extension of the leg resulting from\n> paralysis of the quadriceps femoris (Which include Rectus\n> femoris, Vastus lateralis, Vastus medialis and Vastus\n> intermedius.\n\nSensory loss: Loss of sensation over anteromedial thigh,\nanteromedial knee, medial leg and medial foot.\nObturator nerve\nNerve roots: L2-L4\nMotor functions: Innervates the medial thigh muscles.\nSensory functions: Upper medial thigh via cutaneous\nbranches\nAnatomical course: Arises from the lumbar plexus and enters\nthe thigh through obturator foramen.\nNote: The obturator nerve innervates all of the muscles of the\nmedial compartment of the thigh (except for the hamstring\npart of the adductor magnus innervated by the tibial portion\nof the sciatic nerve).\nClinical: Damage to the obturator nerve causes weakness of\nadduction, resulting in a lateral swinging of the limb during\nwalking\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over upper medial thigh\nSciatic nerve\nIs the largest nerve in the body. Derived from the\nlumbosacral plexus and receives fibers from L4-S3.\nFunction:\n\n### 1: Motor functions: Innervates the muscles of the posterior\n\nthigh (biceps femoris, semimembranosus and semitendinosus)\nand the hamstring portion of the adductor magnus (remaining\nportion of which is supplied by the obturator nerve).\nIndirectly innervates (via its terminal branches) all the\nmuscles of the leg and foot.\n\n### 2: Sensory functions: No direct sensory functions. Indirectly\n\ninnervates (via its terminal branches) the skin of the lateral leg,\nheel, and both the dorsal and plantar surfaces of the foot.\nAnatomical course: Sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis and enters\nthe gluteal region via greater sciatic foramen. It emerges\ninferiorly to the piriformis muscle and descends in an\ninferolateral direction and entering the posterior compartment\nof the thigh. In the posterior compartment of the thigh, the\nsciatic nerve lies on the adductor magnus muscle and is\ncrossed by the long head of the biceps femoris muscle. Within\nthe posterior thigh, the sciatic nerve gives rise to branches to\nthe hamstring muscles and adductor magus. When the\nsciatic nerve reaches the apex of the popliteal fossa, it\nterminates by bifurcating into the tibial and common fibular\nnerves.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Intramuscular injections in the gluteal region: The anatomical\n> course of the sciatic nerve must be considered when\n> administering intramuscular injections into the gluteal region.\n\nThe region can be divided into quadrants using 2 lines.\n\nOne line descends vertically from the highest point on the\niliac crest.\nOther line passes through vertical line halfway between the\nhighest point.\nNote: Sciatic nerve lies in the lower medial quadrant. To avoid\ninjury of sciatic nerve IM injections in the gluteal region only\nbe given in upper lateral quadrant.\nTibial nerve\nBranch of sciatic nerve. Arises at apex of popliteal fossa\nreceiving nerve fibers from L4-S3.\nFunctions:\nMotor: Innervates all the muscles in posterior compartment of\nthe leg and the majority of the intrinsic foot muscles.\nSensory: In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve gives off\ncutaneous branches. These combine with branches from the\ncommon fibular nerve to form the sural nerve. This sensory\nnerve innervates the skin of the posterolateral side of the leg\nand the lateral side of the foot and heel.\nSensory branches of tibial nerve:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Damage to the tibial nerve causes loss of**\n> plantar flexion of the foot and impaired inversion. This\n> presents with difficulty in getting the heel off the ground and a\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\nshuffling gait, resulting in characteristic clawing of the toes and\nsensory loss on the sole of the foot, affecting posture and\nlocomotion.\nCommon fibular (Peroneal\nnerve)\nArises as the smaller terminal portion of the sciatic nerve at the\napex of the popliteal fossa. The common fibular nerve\ncontinues by wrapping around the neck of the fibula and\npassing between the attachments of the fibularis longus\nmuscle to enter the lateral compartment of the leg where it\ndivides into its terminal branches, the superficial and deep\nfibular nerves.\nNote: It is very important to remember direct innervation of\ncommon fibular nerve.\nDeep fibular nerve root: L4-L5\nSuperficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n\n### 1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the common\n\nfibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a result of fracture of the\n\n![Nerves of the Lower Limb - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/lower-limb-anatomy/nerves-of-the-lower-limb/figure_04_p5.png)\n*Nerves of the Lower Limb — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nhead or neck of the fibula because it passes behind the head of\nthe fibula and then winds laterally around the neck. The nerve\ndamage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion) and loss of\nsensation on the dorsum of the foot and lateral aspect of the\nleg and causes paralysis of all muscles in the anterior and\nlateral compartments of the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors\nmuscles of the foot).\n\n### 2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by fracture of the\n\nfibula. As the muscles that the superficial fibular nerve\ninnervates are evertors, injury to the nerve may result in a loss\nof eversion and loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral\nleg and dorsum of foot\n\n### 3: Deep fibular nerve:\n\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in foot drop.\nIn foot drop foot will appear permanently plantarflexed, foot\ndrop with high-stepping gait, weakness of foot inversion.\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace between 1st and\n2nd toes.\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or common\nperoneal nerve or also due to deep fibular nerve injury.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
f2e3131a-5e7d-4485-9866-58c727c8a787	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	structures-of-the-thorax-tracheobronchial-tree	Structures of the Thorax - Tracheobronchial Tree	article	Tracheobronchial Tree\nTrachea:\nThe trachea marks the beginning of the tracheobronchial tree,\narises at the lower border of cricoid cartilage in the neck (C6),\nas a continuation of the larynx. It then travels inferiorly into the\nsuperior mediastinum, bifurcating at the level of the sternal\nangle (T4-T5) (forming the right and left main bronchi).\nNote: As trachea descends, it is located anteriorly to the\noesophagus, and inclines slightly to the right.\nThe trachea is held open by cartilage in C-shaped rings\nsupported by the trachealis muscle.\nThe trachea and bronchi are lined by ciliated pseudostratified\ncolumnar epithelium, interspersed by goblet cells, which\nproduce mucus.\nThe combination of sweeping movements by the cilia and\nmucus from the goblet cells function as mucociliary\nescalator.\nNeurovascular Supply\nSensory innervation: Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve.\nArterial supply: Tracheal branches of the inferior thyroid\nartery\nVenous drainage: via the brachiocephalic, azygos and\naccessory hemiazygos veins.\nBronchi\nAt the level of the vertebrae T4-T5, the trachea bifurcates into\nthe right and left main bronchi. They undergo further\nbranching to produce the secondary bronchi.\nStructure\n\nRight main bronchus: Wider, shorter, and descends more\nvertically than left bronchus.\nClinically, this results in a higher incidence of foreign body\ninhalation.\nNote: Within the lungs, the main (primary) bronchi branch into\nlobar (secondary) bronchi.\nEach secondary bronchi supplies a lobe of the lung, thus\nthere are 3 right lobar bronchi and 2 left.\nThe lobar bronchi then bifurcate into several segmental\n(tertiary) bronchi each of which supplies a\nbronchopulmonary segment.\nBronchopulmonary segments are subdivisions of the lung\nlobes, and act as the functional unit of the lungs.\nWithin each bronchopulmonary segment, the segmental\nbronchi give rise to multiple generations of divisions and\nultimately to bronchioles (conducting bronchioles, terminal\nbronchioles, respiratory bronchioles)\nThe respiratory bronchioles continue as alveolar ducts and\nsacs, the walls of which form alveoli.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
04ab7124-1bf9-4f41-8e58-15c7638712f1	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium	Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium	article	Heart and pericardium\nPericardium\nFibro-serous, fluid-filled sack that surrounds the muscular\nbody of the heart and the roots of the great vessels. Great\nvessels include: The aorta, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins,\nand the superior and inferior vena cavae.\nPericardium is made up of two main layers fibrous and serous\npericardium.\nFibrous Pericardium: Surrounds the heart and forms a\nprotective covering for the cardiac Chambers. It also acts as an\nanchor for the heart that keeps it restricted in the chest cavity\nby binding itself to the adjoining regions. It also prevents the\noverexpansion of the heart when there is a rise in the volume of\nblood inside the body.\nSerous Pericardium: It is closed sac within fibrous pericardium\nhaving Visceral & Parietal layer, separated by the fluid-filled\npericardial cavity\nThe visceral layer of serous pericardium (epicardium):\ncovers the surface of the heart. It also reflects onto the great\nvessels This transparent membrane surrounds and protects\nthe heart. It also contains a thin film of serous fluid that\nallows the heart to move and provides it with a frictionless\nenvironment to expand as well as contract.\nThe parietal layer lines the internal surface of the fibrous\npericardium.\nInnervation of the pericardium\nThe visceral pericardium: Receives autonomic innervation\nfrom the cardiac plexus.\n\nThe parietal and fibrous pericardium are innervated mainly by\nthe phrenic nerve, for this reason that pain related to a\npericardial problem may be referred to the supraclavicular\nregion of the shoulder or the lateral neck area.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pericardiocentesis:  surgical puncture of the pericardial cavity\n> for the aspiration of fluid, which is necessary to relieve the\n> pressure of accumulated fluid in the heart. A needle is inserted\n> into the pericardial cavity 1 - 2 cm inferior to the left of the\n> xiphochondral junction (between the xiphisternum and the\n> left costal margin)\n> Complications:\n> Pneumothorax secondary to lung puncture\n> Aspiration of ventricular blood instead of pericardial blood\n> Laceration of ventricular epicardium or myocardium\n> Laceration of coronary artery or vein\n> Ventricular fibrillation\n> Puncture of great vessels with worsening pericardial\n> tamponade\n> Heart:\n> The heart is a myocardial muscular pump consisting of four\n> chambers, two auricles, four valves, and a muscular septum all\n> enclosed within a fluid filled sac, the pericardium\n> The heart wall is composed of three distinct layers:\n\n### 1: The epicardium (outermost layer)\n\n### 2: The myocardium (middle layer, comprised of muscular\n\nfibers)\n\n### 3: The endocardium (innermost layer), lines the cavities and\n\nvalves of the heart\nBorders\n\nThe heart has four borders:\nThe right border: right atrium and is in line with the superior\nand inferior vena cavae.\nThe left border: the left ventricle and very slightly by the left\nauricle.\nThe superior border: is where the great vessels enter and\nleave the heart. It is formed by both atria\nThe inferior border: The right ventricle, and slightly by the left\nventricle near the apex.\nThe heart has been described as "a pyramid which has fallen\nover". The apex of this pyramid pointing in an anterior-inferior\ndirection.\nIn its typical anatomical orientation, the heart has 5 surfaces,\nformed by different internal divisions of the heart:\nAnterior (or sternocostal): Right ventricle.\nPosterior (or base): Left atrium.\nInferior (or diaphragmatic): Left and right ventricles.\nRight pulmonary: Right atrium.\nLeft pulmonary: Left ventricle.\nNOTE: Esophagus lies immediately posterior to the base (left\natrium). The term cardiac dysphagia referred as impairment of\nswallowing due to esophageal compression from a dilated left\natrium\nCardiac Conduction system: collection of specialized\nconduction cells and nodes that initiate and co-ordinate\ncontraction of the heart muscle. It consists of:\nSinoatrial node\nAtrioventricular node\nAtrioventricular bundle (bundle of His)\nPurkinje fibres\nThe sinoatrial node, the cardiac pacemaker, is located at the\nsuperior end of the crista terminalis at the junction of the\nsuperior vena cava and the right atrium. Impulses generated\nby the sinoatrial node are propagated by the atrial myocardium to the atrioventricular node, Here the impulse is\nbriefly delayed before being spread to the ventricles.\nThe atrioventricular bundle (bundle of His) is a direct\ncontinuation of the atrioventricular node and continues along\nthe lower margin of the membranous part of the\ninterventricular septum before dividing into the right and left\nbundles which continue themselves as Purkinje fibres, the final\ncomponent of the conducting system.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> The sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker. The SAN\n> will normally discharge at a rate of 60-100 bpm. if the\n> impulse generated in the Sa node is blocked, a group of cells\n> further down the heart will become its pacemaker.\n\nThe atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary pacemaker\nand will normally discharge at about 40-60 beats per minute.\nAV nodal abnormality is responsible for prolonged PR\ninterval (1st degree heart block)\nGreat Vessels of the heart: Mnemonic\n\n## (Sippa)\n\n#### Superior vena cava\n\n#### Inferior vena cava\n\nPulmonary arteries\nPulmonary veins\nAorta\n\n### 1. Superior vena cava: Receives deoxygenated blood from the\n\nupper body excluding the lungs and heart superior to the\ndiaphragm, delivering it to the right atrium\n\n### 2. Inferior vena cava: receives deoxygenated blood from the\n\nlower body inferior to the diaphragm, delivering it back to the\nheart\n\n### 3. Pulmonary arteries: Receive deoxygenated blood from the\n\nright ventricle and deliver it to the lungs for gas exchange to\ntake place through pulmonary valve\n\n### 4. Pulmonary veins: Receive oxygenated blood from the lungs,\n\ndelivering it to the left side of the heart to be pumped back\naround the body.\n\n### 5. Aorta: Largest artery carry oxygenated blood pumped by left\n\nside of the heart to all body. The aorta arises from the aortic\norifice at the base of the left ventricle, with inflow via the\naortic valve. First segment is known as the ascending aorta,\nwhich lies within the pericardium. The second continuous\nsegment is the arch of the aorta, from which branch the\nmajor arteries to the head, neck and upper limbs. These are:\nBrachiocephalic trunk\nLeft common carotid artery\nLeft subclavian artery\nAfter the arch of the aorta, the aorta then becomes the\ndescending aorta which continues down through the\ndiaphragm into the abdomen\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Areas of auscultation of heart:\n> Cardiac blood supply:\n\n### 1: The Right coronary artery supplies:\n\nThe right atrium and most of right ventricle\nThe sinoatrial and atrioventricular node\nPosterior interventricular artery, also known as the posterior\ndescending artery (PDA), is a branch of the right coronary\nartery supply posterior  of interventricular septum and\nposterior aspect of right and left ventricle. Most common\nartery infracted in posterior wall MI\nNote: Occlusion of right coronary artery can result into\nischemia of SA and AV node and subsequently bradycardia\nor heart block\n\n### 2: The left coronary artery supplies via main branches below:\n\nAnterior interventricular branch (LAD)\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium/figure_01_p5.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nLCX\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Localization of lesion in ST elevation MI and occluded coronary\n> artery. Only remember myocardial area and occluded coronary\n> artery.\n\nEasy to remember:\nSeptal and Anterior wall MI: LAD (Anterior interventricular\nbranch) occluded.\nLateral wall MI: LCX occluded.\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium/figure_02_p6.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-heart-and-pericardium/figure_03_p6.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Heart and Pericardium — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
21ad8cc4-b23f-4fa0-a6b7-f5991ca7f56b	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	structures-of-the-thorax-lungs-and-pleurae	Structures of the Thorax - Lungs and Pleurae	article	## Lung:\n\nLungs lie either side of the mediastinum, within the thoracic\ncavity. Each lung is surrounded by pleural cavity, which is\nformed by the visceral and parietal pleura. Lungs are\nsuspended from the mediastinum by the lung root a collection\nof structures entering and leaving the lungs.\nEach root contains a bronchus, pulmonary artery, two\npulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, pulmonary plexus of nerves\nand lymphatic vessels. All these structures enter or leave the\nlung via the hilum. The hila are located at the level of the 3rd -\n4th costal cartilage anteriorly, and the T5 - T7 vertebrae\nposteriorly. In the mediastinum, the vagus nerves pass\nimmediately posteriorly to the lung roots, while the phrenic\nnerves pass immediately anterior to them.\nNote: The right lung is normally a little larger than the left lung\nbecause the middle mediastinum, containing the heart,\nbulges more to the left than to the right.\nRelations:\nLymphatic vessels of the lung: Drain the bronchial tree,\npulmonary vessels, and connective tissue septa. Run along\nbronchioles and bronchi toward the hilum, where they drain to\nthe pulmonary (intrapulmonary) and then bronchopulmonary\nnodes, which in turn drain to the inferior and superior\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Lungs and Pleurae - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-lungs-and-pleurae/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Lungs and Pleurae — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\ntracheobronchial nodes, the tracheal (paratracheal) nodes,\nbroncho mediastinal nodes and trunks, and eventually to the\nthoracic duct on the left and right lymphatic duct on the right.\nAre not present in the walls of the pulmonary alveoli.\n\n### 1. Thoracic duct: Begins in the abdomen at the cisterna chyli,\n\nwhich is the dilated junction of the intestinal, lumbar, and\ndescending intercostal trunks. Drains the lower limbs,\npelvis, abdomen, left thorax, left upper limb, and left side\nof the head and neck. Passes through the aortic opening of\nthe diaphragm and ascends through the posterior\nmediastinum between the aorta and the azygos vein, Arches\nlaterally over the apex of the left pleura and between the left\ncarotid sheath in front and the vertebral artery behind, runs\nbehind the left internal jugular vein, and then usually\nempties into the junction of the left internal jugular and\nsubclavian veins.\n\n### 2. Right lymphatic duct: Drains the right sides of the thorax,\n\nupper limb, head, and neck. Much of the left lower lung lobe\ndrains to the right lymphatic duct. Empties into the junction\nof the right internal jugular and subclavian veins.\nVasculature of lung:\nThe pulmonary artery conveys the venous blood to the\nlungs.\nThe pulmonary veins commence in the pulmonary\ncapillaries, open into the left atrium of the heart, conveying\noxygenated blood to be distributed to all parts of the body by\nthe aorta.\nThe bronchial arteries supply blood for the nutrition of the\nlung.\nThe bronchial vein is formed at the root of the lung, receiving\nsuperficial and deep veins corresponding to branches of the\nbronchial artery\nInnervation of lung: The nerve supply to the lungs is from the\nanterior and posterior pulmonary plexus (networks of nerves derived from the sympathetic trunk and vagus nerve).\nLobes of the lung: The left lung is divided into two lobes, an\nupper and a lower, by an interlobular fissure, which extends\nfrom the costal to the mediastinal surface of the lung both\nabove and below the hilus.The superior lobe lies above and in\nfront of this fissure, and includes the apex, the anterior\nborder, and a considerable part of the costal surface and the\ngreater part of the mediastinal surface of the lung.\nThe inferior lobe, the larger of the two, is situated below and\nbehind the fissure, and comprises almost the whole of the\nbase, a large portion of the costal surface, and the greater part\nof the posterior border.\nThe right lung is divided into three lobes, superior, middle, and\ninferior, by two interlobular fissures. the oblique fissure\nseparates the inferior lobe from the superior and middle lobe\nwhilst the horizontal fissure separates the superior lobe from\nthe middle lobe.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Lung auscultation: to make it summarize remember, sounds\n> heard anteriorly think upper lobes, sounds heard posteriorly\n> think lower lobes and middle lobe will be heard to the right,\n> near the midaxillary line\n> The superior lobe of the right lung is best auscultated above\n> the 4th rib anteriorly.\n\nThe middle lobe of the right lung is best auscultated\nbetween the 4th and the 6th rib anteriorly.\nThe superior lobe of the left lung is best auscultated above\nthe 6th rib anteriorly.\nThe inferior lobes of the left and right lungs are best\nauscultated in the back between T3 & T10.\nPancoast tumor (superior pulmonary sulcus tumor) is a\nmalignant neoplasm of the lung aper may cause a lower trunk\nbrachial plexopathy and a lesion of cervical sympathetic chain ganglion and Horner syndrome (ptosis, enophthalmos,\nmiosis, anhidrosis, and vasodilation).\nThoracotomy: surgical procedure to gain access into the\npleural space of the chest\nAntero lateral thoracotomy muscle divided serratus anterior\nand pectoralis major\nAntero lateral thoracotomy: Nerve injury long thoracic\nnerve\nPost: lateral thoracotomy muscle divided latissimus dorsi\nPost: lateral thoracotomy nerve injury: Thoracodorsal nerve\ninjury\nInternal mammary artery and Aorta are likely to be\ndamaged also\nPleurae:\nEach lung is surrounded by a pleura. The pleura consists of two\nparts.\nThe parietal pleura covers internal surface of the thoracic\ncavity. Parietal pleura includes mediastinal pleura, costal\npleura and diaphragmatic pleura\nThe visceral pleura lines the lungs.\nThe two parts of the pleura are continuous with each other\nat the hilum of each lung. The potential space between these\ntwo parts of the pleura is called the pleural cavity.\nPleural Recesses: Pleural cavity is not completely filled by the\nlungs, anteriorly and posteroinferiorly, this gives rise to\nrecesses where the opposing surfaces of the parietal pleura\ntouch.\nThere are two recesses present in each pleural cavity, recesses\nare of clinical importance, as they provide a location where\nfluid can collect i.e., in a pleural effusion\nCosto diaphragmatic: located between the costal pleurae\nand the diaphragmatic pleura.\n\nCosto mediastinal: located between the costal pleurae and\nthe mediastinal pleurae, behind the sternum.\nSurface markings of pleura:\nSuperiorly the pleural cavity projects as much as 3-4 cm\nabove the first costal cartilage but does not extend above the\nneck of rib 1.\nInferiorly the pleura reflects onto the diaphragm above the\ncostal margin. The inferior margin of the pleural cavity is a\nline that runs between rib 8 in the midclavicular line, rib 10 in\nthe midaxillary line and T12 vertebra posteriorly.\nInnervation of pleura\nThe parietal pleura is innervated by somatic afferent fibres.\nParietal pleura includes mediastinal pleura, costal pleura and\ndiaphragmatic pleura.\nThe costal pleura (pleura related to the ribs and intercostal\nspaces) is innervated by branches of the intercostal nerves,\nand pain is felt in relation to the thoracic wall.\nThe diaphragmatic pleura and the mediastinal pleura are\ninnervated mainly by the phrenic nerves (C3 - C5), and pain is\nreferred the C3 - C5 dermatomes include lateral neck and\nsupraclavicular region of shoulder.\nThe visceral pleura is innervated by visceral afferent fibres,\nnot sensitive to pain or temperature.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Thoracentesis (or pleural tap): is surgical entry through the\n> thoracic wall into the pleural cavity for aspiration of fluid. An\n> accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity is known by clinical\n> names, such as a hydrothorax (water), a hemothorax (blood), a\n> chylothorax (lymph), and a pyothorax (pus).\n\nSite: Pleural tap is performed at or posterior to the midaxillary\nline, one or two intercostal spaces below the fluid level ideal\nsite is the sixth, seventh, eighth, or ninth intercostal space but\nnot below the ninth intercostal space\nNote: A needle should be inserted immediately above the\nsuperior margin of a rib to avoid injury to the intercostal neurovascular bundle, which travels in a notch below each rib	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
fe3ba6a0-9784-40ad-9518-e729a29b10c9	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	structures-of-the-thorax-oesophagus	Structures of the Thorax - Oesophagus	article	Oesophagus:\nFibromuscular tube that transports food from pharynx to\nstomach, divided into three parts:\nCervical: continuous with the oropharynx, commences at the\nlower border of cricoid cartilage (at level of C5/6) or\ncricopharyngeus muscle\nThoracic: From thoracic aperture (T1) to the oesophageal hiatus\n\n## (T11)\n\nAbdominal: From oesophageal hiatus and is continuous with\nthe cardia of the stomach at the gastro-oesophageal junction\nOesophageal constrictions\nThe anatomical relations of the oesophagus give rise to four\nconstrictions, where food/foreign objects are most likely to\nbecome impacted.\nOesophageal constrictions should not be confused with\npathological constrictions.\nCervical constriction: due to cricoid cartilage at the level of\n\n## C5/6\n\nThoracic constriction: due to aortic arch at the level of T4/5\nAbdominal constriction: at oesophageal hiatus at T10/11\nvertebral\nRelations of the oesophagus\n\n![Structures of the Thorax - Oesophagus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/structures-of-the-thorax-oesophagus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Structures of the Thorax - Oesophagus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nInnervation of the oesophagus:\nsympathetic trunk (sympathetic fibres)\nvagal plexus (parasympathetic fibres)	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
e392ba50-9964-4915-a048-abd81a8953e1	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	structures-of-the-thorax-thymus	Structures of the Thorax - Thymus	article	The thymus\nlobulated lymphoid organ, located in the thoracic cavity and\nneck. In the adolescent, it is involved the development of the\nimmune system\nIs a lobulated specialized primary lymphoid organ of the\nimmune system, within which T-lymphocytes mature in the\nadolescent\nAfter puberty, it decreases in size and is slowly replaced by\nfat.\nIt is mainly located within the superior mediastinum,\nposterior to the manubrium sterni.\nA certain degree of anatomical variation exists, and in some\nindividuals, it can extend superiorly into the neck or inferiorly\ninto the anterior mediastinum.\nThe thymus has an asymmetrical, flat shape with a lobular\nstructure.\nThe lobules are comprised of a series of follicles, which\nconsist of a medullary and a cortical portion.\nThe cortex: Is located peripherally within each follicle and is\nmainly composed of lymphocytes, supported by a network of\nfinely branched epithelial reticular cells.\nThe medulla: Is located centrally within each follicle and\nlymphoid cells are fewer than cortical portion. There are also\na number of concentric arrangements of epithelial cells\nknown as Hassall's corpuscles.\nVasculature of the thymus:\nThe arterial supply to the thymus gland is via the anterior\nintercostal arteries and small branches from the internal\nthoracic arteries. Venous blood drains into the left\nbrachiocephalic and internal thoracic veins\nInnervation of the thymus:\n\nThe thymus gland is innervated by sympathetic nerves\narising from the cervical sympathetic ganglia. These\nsympathetic nerves regulate blood flow to the thymus and\nplay a role in its function. In addition, parasympathetic\nfibers from the vagus nerve may also contribute to the\ninnervation of the thymus gland nevertheless an autonomic\nnervous system of both sympathetic and parasympathetic\ncomponents plays a role in regulating function of thymus\ngland.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
8ced1419-49f8-4e42-991a-aad13d7e7f2d	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	thoracic-wall-movements	Thoracic Wall Movements	article	Thoracic wall movements:\nIn general, the ribs move around two axes, Movement of the\nribs at the costovertebral joints in respiration results in the\n'pump handle' and 'bucket handle' movements:\n\n### 1: Pump handle movement:\n\nPump-handle is a movement of the ribs that results in a\nchange in the anteroposterior diameter of the thorax\nMovement at costovertebral joints from ribs (2 to 6) about a\nside-to-side axis results in raising and lowering the sternal\nend of the rib, the "pumphandle"movement.\nThe downward slope of the rib ensures that, in elevation, the\nsternum moves upward and forward, increasing the\nanteroposterior diameter of the thorax.\n\n### 2: Bucket handle movement:\n\nBucket handle is a movement of the ribs that results in a\nchange in the transverse diameter of the thorax\nMovement at costovertebral joints from ribs (7 to 10) about an\nanteroposterior axis result in raising and lowering the middle\nof the rib, the "buckethandle" movement. In elevation, this\nincreases the transverse diameter of the thorax.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Flail chest: loss of stability of the thoracic cage that occurs\n> when a segment of the anterior or lateral thoracic wall moves\n> freely because of multiple rib fractures, allowing the loose\n> segment to move inward on inspiration and outward on\n> expiration. Flail chest is an extremely painful injury and impairs\n> ventilation, thereby affecting blood oxygen levels and causing\n> respiratory failure.\n\nRib fractures:\nFracture of the first rib may injure the brachial plexus and\nsubclavian vessels.\nThe middle ribs are most commonly fractured and usually\nresult from direct blows or crush injuries. The broken ends of\nribs may cause pneumothorax and lung or spleen injury.\nLower rib fractures may tear the diaphragm, resulting in a\ndiaphragmatic hernia.\n\n![Thoracic Wall Movements - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/thoracic-wall-movements/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Thoracic Wall Movements — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
768010ad-f3fb-4c26-822f-b4d88e4146b5	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	transverse-thoracic-plane-thoracic-inlet-and-outlet	Transverse Thoracic Plane - Thoracic Inlet and Outlet	article	Transverse thoracic plane\nlies at the level of 2nd intercostal space and intervertebral disc\nbetween T4 & T5\nCLAPTRAP is a useful mnemonic to remember structures\ntransected by the transverse thoracic plane.\nC: cardiac plexus\nL: ligamentum arteriosum\nA: aortic arch (inner concavity) RECALL\nP: pulmonary trunk bifurcation\nT: tracheal bifurcation (carina) RECALL\nR: right-to-left movement of the thoracic duct (posterior to\nthe esophagus)\nA: azygos vein drains into superior vena cava\nP: pre-vertebral fascia and pre-tracheal fascia end\nThoracic inlet: Superior thoracic aperture also known as\nthoracic outlet or inlet refers to opening at top of thoracic\ncavity\nThe thoracic inlet is bordered anteriorly by the manubrium of\nthe sternum and the first costal cartilages.\nPosteriorly by the body of vertebra T1.\nlaterally by the first rib\nStructures that pass through the thoracic inlet\ninclude:\nTrachea and oesophagus\nThoracic duct and apices of the lung\nphrenic nerve\nvagus nerve\nRecurrent laryngeal nerves\nSympathetic trunks\nLeft and right common carotid arteries.\nleft subclavian arteries.\n\nInternal jugular veins\nBrachiocephalic veins\nSubclavian veins\nlymph nodes and lymphatic vessels\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) refers to the clinical features\n> that arise from compression of the neurovascular bundle\n> within the thoracic outlet\n> Compression of brachial plexus\n> Compression of subclavian artery\n> Compression of subclavian vein	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
8cf709bb-11c9-4f6b-be52-4050e114eddc	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	abdominal-wall-muscles	Abdominal Wall Muscles	article	Layers of the Abdominal Wall\n(external to internal)\nSkin\nSuperficial fascia (Camper's fascia: Superficial fatty\nlayer Scarpa's fascia: deep fibrous layer )\nMuscles\nTransversalis fascia\nParietal peritoneum\n\n#### Anterior abdominal wall muscles\n\nThe muscles of the anterolateral abdominal wall can be\ndivided into two main groups that lie deep in the superficial\nfascia.\nThree flat muscles: (External oblique, internal oblique and\ntransversus abdominus), located laterally, each flat muscle\nforms an aponeurosis (a broad, flat tendon) in the\nanteromedial aspect of the abdominal wall which covers the\nvertical rectus abdominis muscle. The aponeuroses of all the\nflat muscles become entwined in the midline, forming\nthe Linea alba.\nTwo vertical muscles: Rectus abdominus and pyramidalis\nsituated near midline of body\nInnervation: The anterior abdominal wall muscles are\ninnervated by the anterior rami of T7-T12 and L1.\n\nOther contents\nRectus sheath: The rectus sheath is formed by the\naponeuroses of the three flat muscles and encloses the\nrectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles. It has an anterior\nand posterior wall for most of its length.\nAnterior wall: Formed by the aponeuroses of the external\noblique, and of half of the internal oblique.\nPosterior wall:  Half of the aponeuroses of the internal\noblique and of the transversus abdominis.\nApproximately midway between the umbilicus and the pubic\nsymphysis, all the aponeuroses move to the anterior wall of\nthe rectus sheath. At this point, there is no posterior wall to\nthe sheath; the rectus abdominis is in direct contact with the\ntransversalis fascia. Point, where the posterior layer of the\nrectus sheath ends, is the arcuate line.\n\n![Abdominal Wall Muscles - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/abdominal-wall-muscles/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Abdominal Wall Muscles — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nContents of the rectus sheath\nRectus abdominus and the pyramidalis muscle\nThe superior and inferior epigastric vessels\nThe termination of intercostal nerves T7-T11\nThe 12th thoracic nerve and the accompanying vessels\n\n#### Posterior abdominal wall muscle\n\nImportant posterior abdominal wall muscles are the psoas\nmajor, minor, the iliacus and the quadratus lumborum which\neach possess a strong fascial covering.\nIliacus and Psoas major origin and insertion\nIliacus: Originates from surface of the iliac fossa and anterior\ninferior iliac spine. Its fibres combine with the tendon of the\npsoas major, inserting into the lesser trochanter of\nthe femur.\nPsoas major: Originates from the transverse processes and\nvertebral bodies of T12 - L5. It then moves inferiorly and\nlaterally, running deep to the inguinal ligament, and\ninserting to the lesser trochanter of the femur.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls**\n> Psoas sign: Irritation of iliopsoas muscle, psoas sign positive\n> when thigh is flexed pain is felt in lower abdominal wall,\n> indication of appendicitis.\n\n![Abdominal Wall Muscles - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/abdominal-wall-muscles/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Abdominal Wall Muscles — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Abdominal Wall Muscles - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/abdominal-wall-muscles/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Abdominal Wall Muscles — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
18eeb884-5853-41c2-b306-09848b32cce9	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	areas-of-abdomen	Areas of Abdomen	article	Areas of the abdomen\nInguinal canal: pathway by which structures can pass from\nthe abdominal wall to the external genitalia.\nInguinal canal boundaries\nInguinal ring: Two openings to the inguinal canal are\nknown as rings\nNote:\nMid-inguinal point: Halfway between the pubic symphysis\nand the anterior superior iliac spine. The femoral pulse can\nbe palpated here.\nMidpoint of the inguinal ligament: Halfway between the\npubic tubercle and the anterior superior iliac spine. The\nopening to the inguinal canal is located just above this point.\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield clinical**\n> Direct inguinal hernia lies medial to inferior epigastric vessels\n> while indirect inguinal hernia lies lateral to inferior epigastric\n> vessels. (RECALL)\n> Most common type of inguinal hernia=indirect inguinal\n> hernia (Recall)\n> Most common side of inguinal hernia=Right side (RECALL)\n> The floor of the inguinal canal is the inguinal ligament,\n> otherwise known as the Poupart ligament, which is formed\n> by the external oblique aponeurosis (RECALL)\n> Type of hernia that enters the deep inguinal ring=indirect\n> inguinal hernia (RECALL)\n> Type of hernia that protrudes through Hesselbach's\n> triangle=Direct inguinal hernia (RECALL).\n\nHesselbach triangle - Region in anterior abdominal wall\nlocated within the inferomedial aspect of the abdominal wall\nBorders:\nMedial: Lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle.\nLateral: inferior epigastric vessels.\nInferior: inguinal ligament\nClinical significance:  Direct inguinal hernia protrudes\nthrough Hesselbach's triangle.\nThe Peritoneum\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nContinuous double-layered membrane which lines the\nabdominal cavity and covers the abdominal viscera.\nConsists of parietal and visceral peritoneum\nParietal peritoneum: Lines the abdominal and pelvic walls\nand the inferior surface of the diaphragm.\nParietal peritoneum Is innervated by somatic nerves, such as\nthe phrenic, lower intercostal, subcostal, iliohypogastric, and\nilioinguinal nerves.\nvisceral peritoneum: Covers the viscera, is innervated by\nvisceral nerves and is insensitive to pain\nPeritoneal cavity: This is a potential space between the\nparietal and visceral layers of peritoneum and contains fluid\nthat lubricates the surface of the peritoneum and facilitates\nfree movements\nAbdominal viscera are either suspended in the peritoneal\ncavity by folds of peritoneum called mesenteries and are\ntermed as intraperitoneal viscera\nVisceras outside the peritoneal cavity bound to the posterior\nabdominal wall termed as retroperitoneal viscera.\nRetroperitoneal organs are only covered in peritoneum on\ntheir anterior surface.\nSADPUCKER useful mnemonic to remember\nretroperitoneal organs.\nS: Suprarenal (adrenal) gland\nA: aorta/IVC\nD: duodenum (Second and third part)\nP: pancreas (except tail)\nU: ureters\nC: colon (ascending and descending)\nK: kidneys\nE: (o)esophagus\nR: rectum\nGreater and lesser sac\nThe peritoneal cavity is divided into greater and lesser sacs\n\nNote:\nSupracolic compartment: Contains stomach, liver and\nspleen, lies under cover of costal limits of thoracic cage.\nInfracolic compartment: Divided into left and right parts by\noblique attachment of mesentery, contains small intestine\nand ascending/descending colon, continuous with pelvic\ncavity\nPeritoneal reflections: Support the viscera and provide\npathway for associated neurovascular structure.\nOmentum: Peritoneal fold extending from the stomach to\nadjacent neurovascular structures divided into the greater and\nlesser omentum\nMesenteries: Peritoneal fold that attaches Visceras to the\nposterior abdominal wall\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_04_p4.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_05_p4.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Areas of Abdomen - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/areas-of-abdomen/figure_06_p5.png)\n*Areas of Abdomen — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
7e2077b5-5363-49d2-b374-0ad471eecd46	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	dermatomes-of-trunk	Dermatomes of Trunk	article	Dermatomal supply of trunk\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n\n![Dermatomes of Trunk - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/dermatomes-of-trunk/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Dermatomes of Trunk — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Dermatomes of Trunk - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/dermatomes-of-trunk/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Dermatomes of Trunk — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
22780277-0879-45a2-a949-6c62437dac2b	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	gastrointestinal-tract	Gastrointestinal Tract	article	Stomach:\nIntraperitoneal organ located between oesophagus and\nduodenum, lie in epigastrium, umblical and left\nhypochondrium.\nStomach is divided into four regions\nThe cardia which surrounds opening of the oesophagus into\nstomach\nThe fundus\nThe body\nThe Pylorus: Connects the stomach to the duodenum. It is\ndivided into the pyloric antrum, pyloric canal and pyloric\nsphincter\nThe pyloric sphincter lies between the pylorus and the first\npart of the duodenum. It controls of the exit of chyme.\nAnatomical relation of stomach\nThe stomach lies anterior to the stomach bed\nstomach bed formed by:\nUseful mnemonic "Dr. S3 kills patients mercilessly."\nDiaphragm\nSplenic flexure\nSuprarenal gland (Left)\nSplenic artery\nKidney (Left)\nPancreas\nMesocolon (Transverse)\nBlood supply:\nThe arterial supply to the stomach is derived from all three\nbranches (left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic branches)\n\n![Gastrointestinal Tract - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/gastrointestinal-tract/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Tract — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nof the coeliac trunk.\nInnervation:\nParasympathetic from anterior and posterior vagal trunk\nderived from vagus nerve\nSympathetic nerve supply: From the T6 through T9 segments\nof the spinal cord passes to celiac plexus through the Greater\nsplanchnic nerve mediates epigastric and visceral referred\npain from gastric ulcer.\nSmall intestine:\nlongest part of the gastrointestinal tract and extends from the\npyloric orifice of the stomach to the ileocaecal fold, comprising\nthe duodenum, jejunum and ileum.\nDuodenum:\nC shaped structure lying Infront of, and to the right of inferior\nvena cava and aorta\nDuodenum is retroperitoneal organ except for its proximal\npart\nParts of duodenum:\nFirst part: The superior (first) part extends from the pyloric\norifice of the stomach to the neck of the gallbladder, lies just to\nthe right of the body of vertebra L1, and passes anterior to the\ninferior vena cava and superior to the head of the pancreas\nSecond part: The Descending (2nd) Part of the duodenum\nIt lies to the right of the bodies of L1 to L3 vertebrae. Runs\ninferiorly, curving around the head of the pancreas. It extends\nfrom the superior duodenal flexure to the inferior duodenal\nflexure.\nDuring its descent, it passes right of and parallel with the\ninferior vena cava.\nThe common bile duct and main pancreatic duct (these\nducts usually unite to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla of\nVater) enter the posteromedial wall of this part of the\nduodenum, about  of the way along its length.\nThe lesser duodenal papilla lies about 6-8 cm distal to the\npylorus and has at its summit the opening of the accessory pancreatic duct.\nThird part: The inferior or horizontal part of the duodenum\nRuns transversely to the left, passing over the IVC, aorta, and L3\nvertebra.\nIt is crossed by the superior mesenteric artery and vein and\nthe root of the mesentery.\nSuperior to it is the head of the pancreas and its uncinate\nprocess.\nFourth part: The Ascending (4th) Part of the Duodenum\nThis ascends on the left side of the aorta anterior to the left\nrenal vessels to the level of L2 vertebra.\nHere it meets the jejunum at the duodenojejunal flexure. The\nduodenojejunal flexure is supported by a fibromuscular band\ncalled the suspensory muscle (ligament) of the duodenum\n(ligament of Treitz).\nBlood supply: Branches of coeliac trunk and superior\nmesenteric artery\nProximal to the major duodenal papilla: supplied by the\ngastroduodenal artery (branch of the common hepatic artery\nfrom the coeliac trunk).\nDistal to the major duodenal papilla: supplied by the inferior\npancreaticoduodenal artery (branch of superior mesenteric\nartery).\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Duodenal ulcer: erosion of the mucosa in the duodenum\n> most likely to occur in the superior portion of the\n> duodenum.\n\nThe most common causes of duodenal ulcers are\nHelicobacter pylori infection and chronic NSAID therapy.\nDuodenal ulcer most likely erosion of the gastroduodenal\nartery\nSmall intestine jejunum and ileum:\n\n![Gastrointestinal Tract - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/gastrointestinal-tract/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Tract — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nBlood supply: Superior mesenteric artery\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Meckel's Diverticulum:\n> True diverticula of all 3 layers of the small intestines\n> Caused by incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct.\n\nRule of 2s: 2 years old, 2 ft from ileocecal valve, 2 in long, 2% of\npopulation\nUsually, asymptomatic\nPainless bleeding due to an ulcer caused by heterotopic\ngastric tissue most common presentation in children\nObstruction is caused by intussusception, volvulus, and\nhernia most common presentation in adults.\nRich blood supply to Meckle's diverticulum is from Superior\nmesenteric artery.\nProximal to the major duodenal papilla: supplied by the\ngastroduodenal artery (branch of the common hepatic\nartery from the coeliac trunk\nDistal to the major duodenal papilla: supplied by the\ninferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (branch of a superior\nmesenteric artery)\nLarge intestine\nExtends from the ileocecal junction to the anus and is\napproximately 1.5 m (5 ft) long. Consists of the cecum,\nappendix, colon, rectum, and anal canal.\nCecum: First part of the large intestine, blind end pouch of\nthe large intestine\nIt lies in the right iliac fossa.\nSurrounded by peritoneum.\n\nColon\nHas ascending and descending colons that are\nretroperitoneal and transverse and sigmoid colons that are\nsurrounded by peritoneum (intraperitoneal).\nAscending Colon:The colon begins as the ascending colon.\nIs a retroperitoneal structure which ascends superiorly from\nthe cecum.\nWhen it meets the right lobe of the liver, it turns 90 degrees\nto move horizontally. This turn is known as the right colic\nflexure (or hepatic flexure) and marks the start of the\ntransverse colon.\nTransverse Colon\nThe transverse colon extends from the right colic flexure to\nthe spleen, where it turns another 90 degrees to point\n\n![Gastrointestinal Tract - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/gastrointestinal-tract/figure_03_p5.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Tract — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\ninferiorly. This turn is known as the left colic flexure (or\nsplenic flexure)\nBlood supply of colon:\nCecum, Appendix, the ascending colon, hepatic flexure and\nproximal  of the transverse colon are supplied by the\nsuperior mesenteric artery.\nDistal  of transverse colon, Splenic flexure and the\ndescending and sigmoid colons are supplied by the inferior\nmesenteric artery.\nLymphatic nodes:\nThe ascending and transverse colons: superior mesenteric\nnodes\nThe descending and sigmoid colons: Inferior mesenteric\nnodes\nNote: Most of the lymph from the superior and inferior\nmesenteric nodes passes into the intestinal lymph trunks,\nand on to the cisterna chyli, where it ultimately empties into\nthe thoracic duct.\nInnervation:\nAscending colon and proximal  of the transverse colon:\nreceive their sympathetic, parasympathetic and sensory\nsupply via nerves from the superior mesenteric plexus.\nDistal  of the transverse colon, descending colon and\nsigmoid colon: receive their sympathetic, parasympathetic\nand sensory supply via nerves from the inferior mesenteric\nplexus:\nParasympathetic innervation via the pelvic splanchnic\nnerves\nSympathetic innervation via the lumbar splanchnic nerves.\nLarge intestine-Appendix: Is a narrow, hollow, muscular tube\nwith large aggregations of lymphoid tissue in its wall.\nsuspended from the terminal ileum by a small mesentery,\nthe mesoappendix, which contains the appendicular vessels.\nCauses spasm and distention when inflamed, resulting in\npain that is referred to the periumbilical region and moves\ndown and to the right.\n\nHas a base that lies deep to McBurney point, which occurs at\nthe junction of the lateral one-third of an imaginary line\nbetween the right anterior superior iliac spine and the\numbilicus. This is the site of maximum tenderness in acute\nappendicitis.\nPosition of appendix: Most common appendix position is\nretrocaecal followed by pelvic.\nIt may be:\nPosterior to the caecum (retrocaecal)\nsuspended over the pelvic brim in a pelvic or descending\nposition.\nBelow the caecum in a subcaecal position\nBlood supply: Branch of superior mesenteric artery\nNote: sympathetic afferent fibres of the appendix arise from\nT10 of the spinal cord, thus explaining why the visceral pain\nof early appendicitis is felt centrally within the abdomen in\nperiumbilical region.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Rovsing's sign: Pain in the right lower quadrant is\n> complained of when palpation pressure is exerted in the left\n> lower quadrant. It is also called referred rebound tenderness\n> and when present is quite helpful in supporting the\n> diagnosis.\n\nPsoas sign: This test is performed by having the patient lie\non his left side. The examiner then slowly extends the\npatient's right thigh, thus stretching the iliopsoas muscle.\nThis will produce pain to make the sign positive. This\nindicates presence of irritative inflamed appendix in close\nproximity to the psoas muscle. This is possible in retrocaecal\nappendicitis.\nObturator test: Passive internal rotation of flexed right thigh\nwith the patient in supine position will elicit pain. This\npositive obturator sign is diagnostic of pelvic appendicitis.\nRectum and anal canal:\nRectum is a part of the large intestine that extends from the\nsigmoid colon to the anal canal and follows the curvature of\nsacrum and coccyx.\nRectum is retroperitoneal structure.\n\nIn men the rectum lies posterior to the bladder, prostate and\nseminal vesicle.\nIn woman lies posterior to uterus, vagina and cervix.\nPeritoneal Coverings: In the superior third of the rectum, the\nanterior surface and lateral sides are covered by peritoneum.\nThe middle third only has an anterior peritoneal covering\nLower  has no peritoneum associated with it.\nIn males, the reflection of peritoneum from the rectum to the\nposterior bladder wall forms the rectovesical pouch.\nIn females, the peritoneum reflects to the posterior vagina\nand cervix, forming the rectouterine pouch (pouch of\nDouglas\nBlood supply:\nSuperior rectal artery: terminal continuation of the inferior\nmesenteric artery.\nMiddle rectal artery: branch of the internal iliac artery.\nInferior rectal artery: branch of the internal pudendal artery.\nInnervation of Rectum:\nSympathetic nervous supply to the rectum is from the\nlumbar splanchnic nerves and superior and inferior\nhypogastric plexuses.\nParasympathetic supply is from S2-4 via the pelvic\nsplanchnic nerves and inferior hypogastric plexuses.\nLymphatic drainage: Pararectal lymph nodes\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Digital rectal examination: Performed by inserting a gloved\n> lubricated finger into the rectum\n> The anterior wall of the rectum has a number of close\n> anatomical structures, and these structures can therefore be\n> palpated digitally via the rectum.\n\nMost significant are the prostate and seminal vesicles in\nmales, and cervix in females.\nBony structures, such as the sacrum and coccyx, may also be\npalpated in both sexes.\nAnal canal:\n\nLies below the pelvic diaphragm and ends at the anus\nAnal sphincter:\nInternal anal sphincter: Consists of involuntary smooth\nmuscle that surrounds the upper  of the anal canal.\nExternal anal sphincter: voluntary muscle that surrounds\nthe lower  of the anal canal (and so overlaps with the\ninternal sphincter). It blends superiorly with the puborectalis\nmuscle of the pelvic floor.\nIin the anal canal, the mucosa is organized into longitudinal\nfolds, known as anal columns. These are joined at their\ninferior ends by anal valves. The anal valves collectively form\nan irregular circle known as the pectinate line (or dentate\nline). This line divides the anal canal into upper and lower\nparts.\nDefecation: Is initiated by distention of the rectum, filled\nfrom the sigmoid colon, and afferent impulses are\ntransmitted to the spinal cord by pelvic splanchnic nerves.\nThe pelvic splanchnic nerves increase peristalsis (contracts\nsmooth muscles in the rectum). In contrast, sympathetic\nnerves cause a decrease in peristalsis, maintain tone in the\ninternal sphincter, and contain vasomotor and sensory (pain)\nfibers. Involves the following:\nThe intra-abdominal pressure is increased by holding the\nbreath and contracting the diaphragm, the abdominal\nmuscles, and the levator ani, thus facilitating the expulsion of\nfeces.\nThe puborectalis relaxes, which decreases the angle between\nthe ampulla of the rectum and the upper portion of the anal\ncanal, thus facilitating defecation.\nThe smooth muscle in the wall of the rectum contracts, the\ninternal anal sphincter relaxes, and the external anal\nsphincter relaxes to pass the feces.\nAfter evacuation, the contraction of the puborectalis and the\nanal sphincters closes the anal canal.\nNeurovascular supply and lymphatic drainage of anal\ncanal:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Hemorrhoids are dilated internal and external venous\n> plexuses around the rectum and anal canal.\n\nInternal hemorrhoids occur above the pectinate line, are\ncovered by a mucous membrane, and their pain fibers are\ncarried by Visceral afferent fibers of the sympathetic nerves.\nExternal hemorrhoids are situated below the pectinate line,\nare covered by skin, and are more painful than internal\nhemorrhoids because their pain fibers are carried by Somatic\nfibers of the inferior rectal nerves.\n\n![Gastrointestinal Tract - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/gastrointestinal-tract/figure_04_p10.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Tract — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 10 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
ca0d3762-0abe-47f6-8e64-1db3585240f9	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	lumbar-spine-and-plexus	Lumbar Spine and Plexus	article	Lumbar spine:\nlocated between the thoracic and sacral vertebral segments.\nLigaments of the lumbar spine\nNote: In the cervical spine, the interspinous and supraspinous\nligaments thicken and combine to form the ligamentum\nnuchae which Supports head, resists flexion of neck and helps\nreturn head to anatomical position\nLumbar plexus:\nFormed by the union of the ventral rami of the first three\nlumbar nerves and a part of the fourth lumbar nerve.\nLies anterior to the transverse processes of the lumbar\nvertebrae within the substance of psoas major muscle.\n\n![Lumbar Spine and Plexus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/lumbar-spine-and-plexus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Lumbar Spine and Plexus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nNote:Sub-costal nerve: (T12)Largest thoracic spinal nerve\noriginates from anterior ramus of spinal nerve T12, Innervates\nexternal oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominus,\nrectus abdominus and pyramidalis muscle\n\n![Lumbar Spine and Plexus - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/lumbar-spine-and-plexus/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Lumbar Spine and Plexus — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
fdfaab5c-27cf-44fb-8b35-f6282fe6a557	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	male-and-female-reproductive-system	Male and Female Reproductive System	article	Male Genital organs:\nTestis and Epididymis:\nPaired structure located within the scrotum\nTestis and Epididymis are Suspended in the scrotum by the\nspermatic cord.\nTestes are site of sperm production\nEpididymis stores the sperm\nAnatomic structure:\nTestis is covered by tunica albuginea which lies beneath the\nvisceral layer of the tunica veginalis\nEach testis is composed of seminiferous tubules which\nproduce spermatozoa and interstitial cells (of Leydig)\nsecrete testosterone\nInnervation of testis and epididymis: testicular plexus - a\nnetwork of nerves derived from the renal and aortic plexus\nLymphatic drainage:(Important to remember)\nTestes lumbar (para-aortic) nodes in the abdomen\nscrotum drains to the superficial inguinal nodes.\nBlood supply: Paired testicular arteries arise from abdominal\naorta\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Testicular torsion is the twisting of a testis and the spermatic\n> cord, obstructing blood vessels to the testis and causing\n> sudden urgent pain and swelling of the scrotum.\n\nCaused by trauma or spasm of cremasteric muscle.\nDuctus deferens:\nIs continuous with the tail of the epididymis and serves to\ntransport sperm to the ejaculatory ducts prior to ejaculation.\n\nIs a thick-walled tube that ascends within the spermatic cord\nto enter the pelvis at the deep inguinal ring lateral to the\ninferior epigastric artery.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Vasectomy is the surgical excision of a portion**\n> of the vas deferens (ductus deferens) through the scrotum. It\n> stops the passage of spermatozoa.\n\nSpermatic cord and contents:\nMust remember important spermatic cord covering and\ncontents with rule of "3"\nThree arteries, three nerves and three other structures\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Penis:\n> The penis consists of a root (two crura and the bulb of the\n> penis) and the body, which is formed by the single corpus\n> spongiosum which contains the spongy urethra and paired\n> corpora cavernosa.  Its head is called the glans penis which is\n> the terminal part of the corpus spongiosum.\n\nInnervation: The penis is supplied by S2-S4 spinal cord\nsegments and spinal ganglia.\nSensory and sympathetic innervation to the skin and glans\npenis is supplied by the dorsal nerve of the penis, a branch of\n\n![Male and Female Reproductive System - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/male-and-female-reproductive-system/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Male and Female Reproductive System — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nthe pudendal nerve.\nParasympathetic innervation is carried by cavernous nerves\nfrom the peri-prostatic nerve plexus and is responsible for\nthe vascular changes which cause erection.\nBlood supply: Dorsal arteries of the penis, Deep arteries of the\npenis and Bulbourethral artery branches of the internal\npudendal artery.\nLymphatic drainage: (Important to remember)\nPenile Skin and prepuce: Drain into Superficial inguinal\nnodes.\nGlans penis and penile urethra drain into deep inguinal\nnodes.\nProstate gland:\nlargest accessory structure of male reproductive system lies\nimmediately inferior to the bladder and the internal urethral\nsphincter\n\n#### Anterior to the rectum\n\nsuperior to the external urethral sphincter\nFunctional anatomy: Secretes a fluid that produces the\ncharacteristic odor of semen. This fluid, the secretion from the\nseminal vesicles and the bulbourethral glands, and the\nspermatozoa constitute the semen or seminal fluid.\nProstate has four zones:\nAnterior zone: which lies in front of the urethra.\nTransitional zone: encompasses the urethra proximal to the\nejaculatory ducts and is prone to benign hypertrophy\nobstructing the internal urethral orifice\nCentral zone: encompasses the ejaculatory ducts up to the\nurethra.\nPeripheral zone: situated posteroinferior and lateral to the\ntransitional and central zones, forming the main mass of the\ngland and is thus the most prone to carcinomatous\ntransformation.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Hypertrophy of the prostate is a benign**\n> enlargement of the prostate that affects older men and occurs most often in the transitional zone, obstructing the internal\nurethral orifice and thus leading to nocturia (excessive\nurination at night), dysuria (difficulty or pain in urination), and\nurgency (sudden desire to urinate).\nProstate cancer is a slow-growing cancer that occurs\nparticularly in the peripheral zone.\nBlood supply: Prostatic arteries derived from internal iliac\narteries.\nInnervation : The prostate receives sympathetic,\nparasympathetic and sensory innervation from the inferior\nhypogastric plexus\nFemale genital organs:\nOvary:\nFemale gonads: Sites of egg production\nOvaries Lie on the posterior aspect of the broad ligament on\nthe side wall of the pelvis minor and are bounded by the\nexternal and internal iliac vessels.\nNot covered by the peritoneum, and thus, the ovum or\noocyte is expelled into the peritoneal cavity and then into the\nuterine tube.\nAre not enclosed in the broad ligament, but their anterior\nsurface is attached to the posterior layer of the broad\nligament by the mesovarium\nLigaments of the ovary:\nOvarian ligament (Round ligament of ovary) Attaches the\novaries to the lateral wall of uterus\nSuspensory ligament of ovary: fold of peritoneum extending\nfrom the mesovarium to the pelvic wall. Contains neurovascular\nstructures\nBlood supply: Are supplied primarily by the ovarian arteries,\nwhich are contained in the suspensory ligament and\nanastomose with branches of the uterine artery.\n\nVenous drainage:\nAre drained by the ovarian veins; the right ovarian vein joins the\ninferior vena cava, and the left ovarian vein joins the left renal\nvein\nInnervation: Autonomic: via the ovarian plexus\nVisceral afferents: accompany the sympathetic fibers to T10 -\nT12 level of the spinal cord sensory ganglia\nPain is thus referred to the periumbilical region, suprapubic\nregion and groin.\nUterine tube:\nThe uterine tube extends from the uterus to the ovary and\nconsists of the isthmus, ampulla, and infundibulum. The\nfimbriated distal end creates currents, drawing an ovulated\noocyte into the uterine tube.\nNote: Ectopic pregnancy most commonly occurs in the\nampulla (70% of cases).\nUterus:\n\n![Male and Female Reproductive System - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/male-and-female-reproductive-system/figure_02_p5.png)\n*Male and Female Reproductive System — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\nContains a fundus, body, isthmus and cervix and is supported\nby ligaments.\nRelations:\nAnteriorly: vesicouterine pouch and the urinary bladder\nPosteriorly: the rectouterine pouch and the anterior surface\nof rectum\nLaterally: the broad ligament\nSuspensory ligaments:\nThe uterosacral ligament\nThe round ligaments\nThe cardinal ligaments\nArterial supply:\nUterine arteries: branch of internal iliac artery\nOvarian arteries: branch of the abdominal aorta at the level of\nL1.\nVenous drainage:\nUterine veins: they are formed from the plexus and drain into\ninternal iliac veins\nInnervation: Autonomic\nHypogastric plexus (sympathetic)\nS3-S4 (parasympathetic)\nVisceral afferents: T11-T12\nVagina:\nRelations:\nAnteriorly: urethral orifice\nPosteriorly: The rectum\nArterial supply: Uterine arteries supply the superior\nportion: branch of the internal iliac artery\nVaginal artery and internal pudendal arteries supply the middle\nand inferior parts: branches of the internal iliac artery.\n\nVenous drainage: Uterovaginal plexus: drains into internal iliac\nveins\nInnervation\nAutonomic: inferior hypogastric plexus, pelvic splanchnic,\npudendal nerve (all provide parasympathetic Innervation\nfrom S2-S4)\nVisceral afferents: accompany parasympathetic fibers of\ninferior hypogastric and pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2-S4.\nSomatic afferents: accompany pudendal nerve to S2-S4.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Digital vaginal examination:\n> Digital vaginal examination allows palpation of the:\n> urethra and bladder through the anterior fornix of the vagina\n> the perineal body, rectum, coccyx and sacrum through the\n> posterior fornix\n> ovaries, uterine tubes, ureters and ischial spines through the\n> lateral fornices	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
828ae1cd-6b57-49d2-a8f9-4cfc6ba493fc	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	pelvic-diaphragm-and-supporting-structures	Pelvic Diaphragm and Supporting Structures	article	Pelvic diaphragm and supporting\nstructures:\nPelvic Diaphragm:\nForms the pelvic floor and supports all of the pelvic viscera.\nIs formed by the levator ani and coccygeus muscles and their\nfascial coverings\nLies posterior and deep to the urogenital diaphragm and\nmedial and deep to the ischiorectal fossa\nOn contraction, raises the entire pelvic floor.\nFlexes the anorectal canal during defecation and helps the\nvoluntary control of micturition.\nHelps direct the fetal head toward the birth canal at\nparturition.\nSupporting structures:\nBroad ligament of the uterus:\nConsists of two layers of the peritoneum, extends from the\nlateral margin of the uterus to the lateral pelvic wall, and\nserves to hold the uterus in position.\nContains the uterine tube, uterine vessels, round ligament of\nthe uterus, ovarian ligament, ureter (lower part), uterovaginal\nnerve plexus, and lymphatic vessels.\nDoes not contain the ovary but gives attachment to the ovary\nthrough the mesovarium\nRound ligament of the uterus: Is attached to the uterus in\nfront of and below the attachment of the uterine tube.\nRuns within the layers of the broad ligament, contains\nsmooth muscle fibers, and holds the fundus of the uterus\nforward, keeping the uterus anteverted and anteflexed.\nEnters the inguinal canal at the deep inguinal ring, emerges\nfrom the superficial ring and becomes lost in the\nsubcutaneous tissue of the labium majora.\n\nOvarian ligament: Is a fibromuscular cord that extends from\nthe ovary to the uterus below the uterine tube, running within\nthe layers of the broad ligament.\nSuspensory ligament of the ovary Is a band of peritoneum\nthat extends upward from the ovary over the posterior pelvic\nbrim and transmits the ovarian vessels, nerves, and lymphatics.\nRectouterine pouch and Rectovesical pouch:\nRectouterine pouch: Is a sac or recess formed by a fold of the\nperitoneum dipping down between the rectum and the\nuterus. Lies behind the posterior fornix of the vagina and\ncontains peritoneal fluid and some of the small intestine.\nRectovesical pouch: Is a peritoneal recess between the bladder\nand the rectum in males, and the vesicouterine pouch is a\nperitoneal sac between the bladder and the uterus in females	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
30092823-8e99-4623-bdc5-0df9e827bbda	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	urinary-tract	Urinary Tract	article	Kidney\nThe kidneys are two bean-shaped organs, located on either\nside of the spine, in the retroperitoneal space, extend\nbetween vertebrae T12 - L3.\nThe left kidney is situated a little higher than the right one,\nbecause of the liver on the right side of the abdominal cavity,\nabove the right kidney. The kidneys have a medial dimple\ncalled the renal hilum, which is the entry and exit point for\nstructures that supply or drain the kidneys such as the\nnerves, ureters, vessels, and lymphatics.\nEach kidney covered by fibrous capsule and surrounded by\nrenal fascia,\nAnatomical Relations\nArterial supply: Renal arteries arising from the abdominal aorta\nimmediately distal to the origin of the superior mesenteric\nartery\nInnervation: The kidneys receive autonomic nerve fibers via\nthe renal plexus which contains parasympathetic fibers from\nthe vagus nerve and sympathetic fibers from the thoracic\nsplanchnic nerves.\n\n![Urinary Tract - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/urinary-tract/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Urinary Tract — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLymphatic drainage: (Important to remember): Lumbar (Para-\naortic lymph nodes) located at origin of renal arteries.\nUreter:\nMuscular tube that transmits urine by peristaltic waves\nArises in the abdomen as continuation of renal pelvis.\nAfter arising from the ureteropelvic junction, the ureters\ndescend through the abdomen, along the anterior surface of\nthe psoas major. Here the ureter lies anteriorly and slightly\nmedial to the tips of L2-L5 vertebrae.\nAt the area of the sacroiliac joints, the ureters cross the\npelvic brim, thus entering the pelvic cavity. At this point,\nthey also cross the bifurcation of the common iliac\narteries.\nOnce within the pelvic cavity, the ureters travel down the\nlateral pelvic walls. At the level of the ischial spines, they turn\nanteromedially, moving in a transverse plane towards the\nbladder.\nUpon reaching the bladder wall, the ureters pierce its lateral\naspect in an oblique manner. This creates a one-way valve,\nwhere high intramural pressure collapses the ureters,\npreventing the backflow of urine.\n\nInnervation: Sensory fibres from the ureters enter the spinal\ncord at T11-L2, with ureteric pain referred to those dermatomal\nareas.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> In females ureters are in close proximity to the ovaries. Care\n> must be taken not the damage the ureters during an\n> oophorectomy, especially during the ligation of the ovarian\n> arteries.\n\nDamage may occur during hysterectomy or surgical repair of\na prolapsed uterus because it runs under the uterine artery.\nThe Ureter is inadvertently clamped, ligated during\nhysterectomy when uterine artery is being ligated to control\nuterine bleeding.\nIn men, instead of the uterine arteries, the vas deferens cross\nthe ureters anteriorly and lies Infront of seminal vesicles\n\n![Urinary Tract - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/urinary-tract/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Urinary Tract — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nbefore entering the posterolateral aspect of the bladder.\nUreteric calculus:\nThere are three locations where the ureters are at their\nnarrowest, this is where a stone is more likely to become stuck.\nUretopelvic junction (Usually Coincides with L2)\nPelvic brim\nWhere the ureter enters the bladder\nUrethra\nIt serves as a passage for urine from the urinary bladder to\nthe exterior, but in men, it also serves as a passage for\nsemen.\nMale urethra: Male urethra is approximately 20 cm long and\nconsists of: prostatic, membranous, bulbous and spongy\nurethra.\n\n#### Anterior urethra\n\nPenile (spongy) urethra (16 cm long): encased by corpus\nspongiosum of the penis (the longest portion).\nBulbar (bulbous) urethra traverses the root of the penis, it\nreceives the ducts from the bulbourethral glands and the\nglands of Littre\n.\n\n#### Posterior urethra\n\nMembranous urethra (1 cm long): passes through the\nurogenital diaphragm, surrounded by sphincter urethrae. The\nshortest and narrowest portion. The lowest part of the\nmembranous urethra is susceptible to rupture or to\npenetration by a catheter.\nProstatic Urethra (3 cm long), surrounded by the prostate\ngland; on its posterior wall. It receives the ejaculatory ducts\nand numerous small prostatic ducts.\nFemale urethra\nis approximately 4 cm long, and its external urethral orifice is\nsituated between the labia minora, anterior to the vaginal opening, and posterior to the glans clitoris.\nBlood supply:\nPenile (spongy) urethra and Membranous urethra: Supplied\nby branch of internal pudendal artery\nProstatic Urethra: Branch of internal iliac artery\nInnervation: prostatic plexus, which contains a mixture of\nsympathetic, parasympathetic and visceral afferent fibres.\nUrinary bladder:\nFunction as temporary storage of urine as well as expulsion\nThere are also two muscular sphincters located in the urethra\nInternal urethral sphincter:\nMale: consists of circular smooth fibres, which are under\nautonomic control. It is thought to prevent seminal\nregurgitation during ejaculation.\nExternal urethral sphincter\nhas the same structure in both sexes. It is a skeletal muscle,\nand under voluntary control.\nMicturition (urination):\nIs initiated by stimulating stretch receptors in the detrusor\nmuscle in the bladder wall as the urine volume increases\n(approximately 400 ml for adults). Can be assisted by\ncontraction of the abdominal muscles, which increases the\nintra-abdominal and pelvic pressures.\nInvolves the following processes:\n\n### 1. Sympathetic (visceral efferent [VE)) fibers constrict the\n\ninternal sphincter, inhibiting emptying of the bladder (They\nalso activate during ejaculation to constrict the internal\nsphincter in order to prevent seminal reflux into the bladder\n\n### 2. Parasympathetic preganglionic (VE) fibers in the pelvic\n\nsplanchnic nerves pass through the pelvic (inferior hypogastric)\n\nplexus to synapse within the bladder wall; postganglionic fibers\nto the bladder musculature induce a reflex contraction of the\ndetrusor muscle and relaxation of the internal urethral\nsphincter in concert with reduced stimulation by sympathetic\nfibers.\n\n### 3. Somatic efferent fibers in the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)\n\ninnervates the external urethral sphincter to cause voluntary\nrelaxation of the external urethral sphincter, and the bladder\nbegins to void.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Reflex Bladder: Spinal Cord Transection Above T12\n> In this case, the afferent signals from the bladder wall are\n> unable to reach the brain, and the patient will have no\n> awareness of bladder filling.\n\nThere is also no descending control over the external urethral\nsphincter, and it is constantly relaxed. There is a functioning\nspinal reflex, where parasympathetic system initiates\ndetrusor contraction in response to bladder wall stretch.\nThus, the bladder automatically empties as it fills - known as\nthe reflex bladder.\nFlaccid Bladder: Spinal Cord Transection Below T12\nA spinal cord transection at this level will have damaged the\nparasympathetic outflow to the bladder. The detrusor muscle\nwill be paralyzed, unable to contract.\nThe spinal reflex does not function. In this scenario, the\nbladder will fill uncontrollably, becoming abnormally\ndistended until overflow incontinence occurs.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
e352b072-e725-4fdc-b32a-7dbd3f7828fa	66192b35-5116-4c41-a018-54b8468dc26e	anterior-and-posterior-triangle-of-neck	Anterior and Posterior Triangle of Neck	article	#### Posterior triangle\n\nBorders:\nPosterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle\n\n#### Anterior border of the trapezius muscle\n\nSuperior border of the clavicle.\nRoof:\nPlatysma and the investing (superficial) layer of the deep\ncervical fascia.\nContents of posterior triangle of neck\nMuscles: Inferior belly of omohyoid, The scalenus, splenius and\nlevator scapulae.\nVeins: Terminal part of external jugular vein.\nArteries: Third part of subclavian artery, Transverse cervical and\nsuprascapular arteries.\nNerves: Accessory nerve (CN XI), Phrenic nerve, Branches of\ncervical plexus and Root and trunks of brachial plexus,\n\n#### Anterior triangle\n\nBorders:\n\n#### Anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid\n\n#### Anterior midline of the neck\n\nInferior border of the mandible.\nRoof:\nPlatysma and the investing layer of the deep cervical fascia.\nAnterior triangle is further divided by the omohyoid anterior\nbelly and the anterior and posterior bellies of digastric into 4\nTriangles:\nDigastric (submandibular): submandibular gland.\n\nSubmental (suprahyoid): Submental Lymph Nodes.\nCarotid: 3C (CNXII, Ansa cervicalis & carotid sheath)\nMuscular (inferior carotid): Thyroid and Parathyroid Glands.\nThe contents of the anterior triangle of the neck include:\nMuscles: Digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, Geniohyoid,\nsternohyoid, omohyoid, Thyrohyoid, sternothyroid and\nplatysma\nVeins: Internal jugular vein, anterior jugular and external\njugular veins, Facial and retromandibular veins\nArteries: Carotid sheath and branches of external carotid\nartery\nNerves: Nerve to Mylohyoid, CN XII & Ansa Cervicalis\n\n![Anterior and Posterior Triangle of Neck - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/anterior-and-posterior-triangle-of-neck/figure_01_p2.jpg)\n*Anterior and Posterior Triangle of Neck — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
429bb23c-549d-4032-a915-8e8293600a64	66192b35-5116-4c41-a018-54b8468dc26e	thyroid-gland	Thyroid Gland	article	Thyroid Gland\nThe thyroid gland is an endocrine organ which regulates basal\nmetabolic rate & stimulates growth\nParts\n2 lobes: Right & Left lobes\nIsthmus: which connects the lobes\nPosition: The gland lies at the level of C5, C6, C7\neach lobe extends from middle of thyroid cartilage to 4th or\n5th tracheal ring.\nisthmus extends from 2nd to 3rd tracheal ring.\n\n![Thyroid Gland - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/thyroid-gland/figure_01_p1.jpg)\n*Thyroid Gland — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nRelations of the lobe\nAnterolateral:\nsternothyroid\nsternohyoid\nsuperior belly of omohyoid\nanterior border of sternocleidomastoid\nPosterolateral\ncarotid sheath & its contents: CCA, IJV and Vagus nerve\nMedially\nlarynx\nTrachea\nesophagus\nexternal laryngeal nerve and Recurrent laryngeal nerve\nArterial Supply\nsuperior thyroid artery from External carotid artery\ninferior thyroid artery from thyrocervical trunk\nThyroid Ima artery from brachiocephalic artery\nVenous Drainage\nsuperior thyroid vein: drain into internal jugular vein.\nmiddle thyroid vein: drain into internal jugular vein.\ninferior thyroid vein: anastomose with Left brachiocephalic\nvein.\nLymphatic Drainage: deep cervical Lymph nodes\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Structure vulnerable to damage in thyroid surgery include.\n\nRecurrent laryngeal nerve\nThyroid Ima artery\n\n#### Inferior thyroid vein\n\n#### Anterior jugular vein\n\nParathyroid gland\nOesophagus	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
0fb547f9-2a25-4133-b65b-cf7515e8ad98	66192b35-5116-4c41-a018-54b8468dc26e	visual-pathway	Visual Pathway	article	Visual pathway\nThe visual pathway consists of the retina, optic nerves, optic\nchiasm, optic tracts, lateral geniculate bodies, optic radiations,\nand visual cortex.\nAt the visual cortex brain processes sensory information and\nresponds appropriately\nOptic nerve\nOptic chiasm: Within the middle cranial fossa, optic nerves\nfrom each eye unite to form optic chiasm.\nOptic tract: At the optic chiasm fibers from the medial (Nasal)\nhalf of each retina crossover forming the left and right optic\ntract.\nLateral geniculate nucleus: Each optic tract travels to its\ncorresponding cerebral hemisphere to reach the lateral\ngeniculate nucleus (LGN), a relay system located in the\nthalamus; the fibers synapse here.\nOptic radiation: Axons from the LGN then carry visual\ninformation via a pathway known as optic radiation. The\npathway itself can be divided into:\nUpper optic radiation carries fibres from the superior retinal\nquadrants (corresponding to the inferior visual field\nquadrants). It travels through the parietal lobe to reach the\nvisual cortex.\nLower optic radiation carries fibres from the inferior retinal\nquadrants (corresponding to the superior visual field\nquadrants). It travels through the temporal lobe, via a\npathway known as Meyers' loop, to reach the visual cortex.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Remember the lesions of visual pathway (High yield)**\n\n![Visual Pathway - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/visual-pathway/figure_01_p2.jpg)\n*Visual Pathway — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
0462480c-6e07-4844-af56-8503ac8f4f1a	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	brainstem-pons	Brainstem, Pons	article	Brainstem:\nConsists of midbrain, pons and medulla.\nMid brain:\nSmallest of the three regions lies predominantly in the\nposterior cranial fossa.\nIt can be divided into two main parts:\nTectum: located posterior to the cerebral aqueduct The\ntectum houses four rounded prominences named colliculi\n(collectively the corpora quadrigemina) which sit directly\ninferior to the pineal gland. The colliculi are separated by the\ncruciform sulcus; there are two superior and two inferior\ncolliculi.\nPaired cerebral peduncles: located anteriorly and laterally\nextend from the cerebral hemispheres to converge as they\nmeet the pons. They are separated anteriorly in the midline\nby the interpeduncular fossa\nThe oculomotor nerve (CNIII) is seen exiting from between\nthe peduncles while the optic tract runs around the superior\nborder of the midbrain.\nNote: Dopaminergic neurons are essential for controlling key\nfunctions of the brain, such as voluntary movement, reward\nprocessing, and working memory. The largest populations of\nthese neurons sit within the midbrain in substantia nigra.\n\nVasculature of the mid brain:\nThe midbrain receives vascular supply from the basilar artery,\nand its branches. The major vessels are:\nPosterior cerebral artery and its peduncular branch\n\n#### Superior cerebellar artery\n\n#### Posterior choroidal artery\n\nInterpeduncular branches of the basilar artery\nPons:\nlargest part of the brainstem, located above the medulla and\nbelow the midbrain. The pons houses nuclei of the several\ncranial nerves which include trigeminal nerve(v), the abducens\nnerve (VI), the facial nerve (VII) and the vestibulocochlear nerve\n\n## (Viii).\n\nTrigeminal nuclei (paralysis of muscles of mastication, jaw\ndeviation, loss of facial sensation)\nAbducens nucleus (lateral rectus paralysis)\nFacial nucleus (facial nerve paralysis and loss of taste\nsensation from anterior tongue)\nVestibular nuclei (nystagmus, nausea, vomiting, vertigo)\nCochlear nuclei (central nerve deafness\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Cerebellopontine Angle Syndrome: Cerebellopontine Angle is\n> clinically important because of its closeness to cranial nerves\n> Clinical features:\n\n![Brainstem, Pons - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/brainstem-pons/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Brainstem, Pons — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nUnilateral hearing loss (85%), speech impediments,\ndisequilibrium, tremors or other loss of motor control.\nFacial paralysis (compression of CN VII), Reduced corneal\nreflex (involvement of both CN V and CN VII), Trigeminal palsy\n(compression of CN V) and Ipsilateral cerebellar signs in large\ntumors noted	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
a3cf0282-102a-4714-8562-9a4ff54c019a	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	basal-ganglia	Basal Ganglia	article	Basal Ganglia:\nThe cluster of sub-cortical nuclei located deep to cerebral\nhemispheres. Basal ganglia's largest component is corpus\nstriatum which contains the caudate and lenticular nuclei\n(the putamen, globus pallidus externus, and internus), the\nsubthalamic nucleus (STN), and the substantia nigra (SN).\nFunction: Basal ganglia provide a feedback mechanism to\ncortex modulating movements; preventing unwanted\nmovements\nCaudate Nucleus: Forms the lateral wall of lateral ventricle.\ncaudate nucleus is separated from the putamen by\ndescending white matter fibres, which at this level are known\nas internal capsule.\nLenticular Nuclei:\n1:Putamen: Forms lateral aspect of lenticular nuclei\n2:Globus pallidus: On inner surface of putamen lies globus\npallidus; externus, and internus.\nSubstantia Nigra: located in the midbrain but considered\nfunctionally as a part of the basal ganglia\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Basal ganglia are particularly associated with movement\n> disorders.  tremors; involuntary muscle movements;\n> abnormal increase in tone, difficulty initiating movements,\n> and abnormal posture\n> Parkinson's is the most important and notorious disease of\n> the basal ganglia. Classic clinical symptoms include\n> bradykinesia, resting tremor, postural instability, and\n> shuffling gait	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
a5817495-e209-4c48-a5da-b1cb603026a5	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	central-nervous-system-overview	Central Nervous System - Overview	article	Central Nervous system overview\nThe central nervous system consists of the brain and the spinal\ncord. The peripheral nervous system consists of the extensions\nof neural structures beyond the central nervous system and\nincludes somatic and autonomic divisions.\nThe brain is composed of 3 main structural divisions:\nThe cerebrum (Cerebral hemispheres and diencephalon)\nThe brainstem\nThe cerebellum\nNOTE:  At the base of the brain is the brainstem, which extends\nfrom the upper cervical spinal cord to the diencephalon of the\ncerebrum. The brainstem is divided into the medulla, pons, and\nmidbrain. Posterior to the brainstem lies the cerebellum.\nSpinal cord:\nThe spinal cord is housed in the vertebral canal. It is\ncontinuation of medulla oblongata and terminates as the\nconus medullaris at the second lumbar vertebrae of adults.\nAt birth, the conus medullaris lies at L3. By the age of 21, it\nsits at L1/L2, thus the spinal cord only occupies about 2/3s of\nthe space available to it in the vertebral canal.\nBelow the conus medullaris is cauda equina which lies\nbelow L1 from L2 to sacrum\nInternal anatomy:\nA transverse section of the spinal cord reveals a distinct\n"butterfly" pattern of dark, inner grey" matter surrounded by\nthe light white matter.\nThe white matter contains the ascending and descending\npathways connecting the brain and spinal cord.\nThe grey matter is rich in nerve cell bodies which are divided\ninto four main columns.\n\nAnterior horn (Ventral horn): largely contains motor neurons\nthat exit the spinal cord to innervate skeletal muscle.\nPosterior division (Dorsal horn): Receive somatosensory\ninformation from the body, which is then transmitted via the\nascending pathways, to the brain.\nIntermediate horn: Innervate visceral and pelvic organs.\nLateral horn: Innervate visceral and pelvic organs.\nSpinal nerve roots:\nThe spinal nerves are formed by the union of posterior and\nanterior roots.\nSpinal rootlets emerge from the spinal cord in the\nsubarachnoid space and amalgamate shortly afterwards into\nroots.\nAnterior roots: Containing motor nerve fibres\nposterior roots: containing the processes of sensory neurons\nthen emerge from their individual intervertebral foramina.\nAfter invaginating the dura, the roots combine into mixed\nspinal nerves which divide into a posterior ramus innervating\nintrinsic back muscles and an associated strip of skin on the\nback.\nAnterior ramus (innervating the remaining muscles and skin\nof the trunk and limbs and the visceral organs).\nVasculature of spinal cord:\nThe spinal cord is supplied primarily by branches of the\nvertebral artery:\nThe single anterior spinal artery is formed from the left and\nright vertebral arteries: Supplies the anterior two-thirds of the\nspinal cord, including the anterior and lateral horns and the\nanterior and lateral columns.\nThe paired posterior spinal arteries arise from either the\nposterior inferior cerebellar artery or directly from the\nvertebral artery and supply the posterior third of the spinal\ncord including the posterior horns and columns.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
b441905e-4a1e-49e4-8f6c-65386c42ddad	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	cerebellum	Cerebellum	article	Cerebellum:\nThe cerebellum is located at the back of the brain, immediately\ninferior to the occipital and temporal lobes. It is separated from\nthese lobes by the tentorium cerebelli\nFunction: The cerebellum has three primary functions:\nMaintenance of posture and balance\nMaintenance of muscle tone\nCoordination of voluntary motor activity\nVasculature of Cerebellum: The cerebellum receives its blood\nsupply from three paired arteries:\nSuperior cerebellar artery (SCA)\nAnterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA)\nPosterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> In Cerebellar dysfunction wide variety of manifestations are\n> possible. These can be remembered using the acronym\n\n## 'Danish'\n\nDysdiadochokinesia (difficulty in carrying out rapid,\nalternating movements)\nAtaxia\nNystagmus (coarse)\nIntention tremor\nScanning speech\nHypotonia\nCerebellar vascular lesion:\n\nNote: Dysphagia is the differentiating symptom between AICA\nand PICA. Way to remember PICA can't chew.\n\n![Cerebellum - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/cerebellum/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Cerebellum — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
b3fd6ac4-0200-4851-8419-33d52ff445a0	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	cerebrum	Cerebrum	article	Cerebrum:\nThe cerebrum is the largest part of the brain, located\nsuperiorly and anteriorly in relation to the brainstem.\nIt consists of two cerebral hemispheres (left and right),\nseparated by the falx cerebri of the dura mater.\nExternal structure:\nThe cerebral hemispheres are covered in a thin layer of grey\nmatter called the cerebral cortex which is folded into gyri\n(elevations) and separated by sulci (depressions). The left and\nright hemispheres are partially separated by a deep\nlongitudinal fissure (a major sulcus that runs in the median\nsagittal plane)\nThe two cerebral hemispheres are connected by a white\nmatter structure, called the corpus callosum which is\ncomposed primarily of commissural fibers.\nInternal structure: The cerebrum is comprised of two different\ntypes of tissue:\nGrey matter forms the surface of each cerebral hemisphere\n(known as the cerebral cortex) and is associated with\nprocessing and cognition.\nWhite matter forms the bulk of the deeper parts of the brain.\nIt consists of glial cells and myelinated axons that connect\nthe various grey matter areas.\nLobes of cerebrum:\nThe cerebral hemispheres fill the area of the skull above the\ntentorium cerebelli and are subdivided into lobes based on\ntheir position relative to the major sulci:\n\nNote:\n\n### 1: The Broca speech area (in the dominant hemisphere only) is\n\nthe 'expressive' Centre for the production of speech. Broca's\narea is connected to the Wernicke speech area by the arcuate\nfasciculus.\n\n### 2: The blood supply to the occipital lobe is from the posterior\n\ncerebral artery, but the occipital poles, serving macular vision,\nhave an additional supply from a branch of the middle cerebral\nartery\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High Yield: Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral arteries**\n\n![Cerebrum - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/cerebrum/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Cerebrum — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Cerebrum - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/cerebrum/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Cerebrum — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Cerebrum - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/cerebrum/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Cerebrum — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
5df9ce1b-8769-4540-a718-d57a3291a924	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	spinal-cord-syndrome	Spinal Cord Syndrome	article	Spinal cord syndrome:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n\n![Spinal Cord Syndrome - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/spinal-cord-syndrome/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Spinal Cord Syndrome — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Spinal Cord Syndrome - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/spinal-cord-syndrome/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Spinal Cord Syndrome — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
175b86ae-c052-4a33-af5e-546ed5231bcb	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	spinal-cord-tracts	Spinal Cord Tracts	article	Tracts of Spinal Cord:\nThe spinal cord consists of various groups of nerve fibers\ncarrying sensory information toward the brain and receiving\nmotor information from the brain.\nAscending and descending tracts of the spinal cord are\nresponsible for carrying motor and sensory stimuli to and\nfrom the periphery to higher levels\nAscending tracts of the spinal cord:\nDescending Tracts of the Spinal Cord:\n\n![Spinal Cord Tracts - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/spinal-cord-tracts/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Spinal Cord Tracts — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Spinal Cord Tracts - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/central-nervous-system-spine/spinal-cord-tracts/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Spinal Cord Tracts — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
bd3d53e8-b0f7-44db-9473-3acb65653ea1	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	the-ventricles	The Ventricles	article	The Ventricles:\nThe ventricles are a set of communicating cavities within the\nbrain that are responsible for the production, transport and\nremoval of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), which bathes the central\nnervous system. There are four ventricles; right and left lateral\nventricles, the third ventricle and the fourth ventricle.\nLateral Ventricles:The lateral ventricles are C-shaped cavities\nlocated within their respective hemispheres of the\ncerebrum.  The lateral ventricles are connected to the third\nventricle by the interventricular foramen of Monro.\nThird Ventricle: The third ventricle is a slit-like space in the\nsagittal plane, situated between the right and left thalamus\nand superior to the hypothalamus. The third ventricle is\nconnected to the fourth ventricle by the cerebral\naqueduct.\nFourth Ventricle: The fourth ventricle lies within the\nbrainstem, at the junction between the pons and the\nmedulla oblongata.\nCerebrospinal fluid:\nCSF is secreted by the ependymal cells of the ventricular\nchoroid plexus. The majority of CSF arises from the plexuses of\nthe lateral ventricles.\nCSF Flow:\nFrom the lateral ventricles, CSF passes through the\ninterventricular foramina to the third ventricle\nFrom 3rd ventricle CSF passes through the cerebral aqueduct\nto the fourth ventricle.\nFrom fourth ventricle, the CSF drains via foramina into the\ncentral spinal canal (bathing the spinal cord) and the\nsubarachnoid cisterns in the subarachnoid space located\nbetween the arachnoid mater and pia mater (bathing the\nbrain).\n\nCSF Reabsorption:\nFrom the subarachnoid cisterns, CSF is reabsorbed by\narachnoid granulations, which protrude into the dura mater,\ninto the Dural venous sinuses and from here back into the\ncirculation.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
cfaaabc3-4762-459b-b425-2804fe282bed	920faef5-8497-4c4e-b6fa-4225c5ff8449	vasculature-of-the-cns	Vasculature of the CNS	article	Vasculature of the central nervous\nsystem:\nVasculature of brain:\nTwo paired arteries are responsible for the blood supply to\nthe brain; the vertebral arteries, and the internal carotid\narteries. These arteries arise in the neck and ascend to the\ncranium.\nWithin the cranial vault, the terminal branches of these\narteries form an anastomotic circle, called the Circle of Willis.\nFrom this circle, branches arise which supply the majority of\nthe cerebrum.\n\n#### Branches of internal carotid artery\n\nThe two internal carotid arteries arise from the common\ncarotid arteries at the level of vertebra C4 and enter the cranial\ncavity through the carotid canal in the temporal bone. Entering\nthe cranial cavity, each internal carotid artery gives off the\nfollowing paired branches:\nThe ophthalmic artery (supplying structures in the orbit and\ngiving rise to the central retinal artery supplying the retina)\nThe posterior communicating artery: acts as an anastomotic\n'connecting vessel' in the Circle of Willis\nThe anterior choroidal artery (supplying parts of the optic\npathway, the internal capsule, the midbrain and the choroid\nplexus)\nThe anterior cerebral artery (supplying the medial cerebral\nhemisphere)\nThe anterior communicating artery (joining the two anterior\ncerebral arteries)\nThe middle cerebral artery (supplying the lateral cerebral\nhemispheres)\n\n#### Branches of vertebral arteries\n\nThe two vertebral arteries (left and right), branches of the\nsubclavian arteries, ascend through the transverse foramina of\nthe upper six cervical vertebrae before entering the cranial\ncavity through the foramen magnum.\nThe vertebral arteries give rise to the following branches:\nThe single anterior spinal artery (supplying the anterior\nportion of the spinal cord)\nThe posterior inferior cerebellar arteries (supplying the\ncerebellum)\nThe posterior spinal arteries (supplying the posterior portion\nof the spinal cord), either a direct branch of the vertebral\nartery or of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery\nThe basilar artery is formed by the joining of the two\nterminal vertebral arteries inferior to the pons.\nArterial circle of Willis: Terminal branches of the vertebral and\ninternal carotid arteries all anastomose to form a circular blood\nvessel, called the Circle of Willis.\nAnterior cerebral arteries: branches of the internal carotid\narteries.\nAnterior communicating artery: connects the two anterior\ncerebral arteries.\nInternal carotid arteries: located immediately proximal to\nthe origin of the middle cerebral arteries.\nPosterior cerebral arteries: Terminal branches of the basilar\nartery from vertebral artery\nPosterior communicating artery: Branch of the internal\ncarotid, this artery connects the ICA to the posterior cerebral\nartery.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Anterior communicating artery is most common site of\n> berry aneurysm. Second most common site is posterior\n> communicating.\n\nAneurysm of anterior communicating artery compress the\noptic chiasma and cause bitemporal hemianopia, aneurysm\nof posterior communicating artery may cause 3rd nerve\npalsy.\n\nRupture of berry aneurysm is a common cause of\nspontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage.\nVasculature of spinal cord:\nThe spinal cord is supplied primarily by branches of the\nvertebral artery.\nThe single anterior spinal artery is formed from the left and\nright vertebral arteries: Supplies the anterior two-thirds of the\nspinal cord, including the anterior and lateral horns and the\nanterior and lateral columns.\nThe paired posterior spinal arteries arise from either the\nposterior inferior cerebellar artery or directly from the\nvertebral artery and supply the posterior third of the spinal\ncord including the posterior horns and columns.	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
80377460-4905-43d6-86c4-69bfac46d71f	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-1-olfactory-nerve	Cranial Nerve 1 - Olfactory Nerve	article	Olfactory nerve:\nFirst and shortest cranial nerve transmits sensory information\nfrom the nasal epithelium via the cribriform plate of the\nethmoid bone to the primary olfactory cortex\n\n![Cranial Nerve 1 - Olfactory Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-1-olfactory-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve 1 - Olfactory Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
53266853-7235-4176-a42b-22a531c7c45e	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-iii-oculomotor-nerve	Cranial Nerve Iii_ Oculomotor Nerve	article	Oculomotor Nerve (CN III):\nMotor nerve arises from midbrain, passes through the lateral\naspect of cavernous sinus and exits skull through the superior\norbital fissure\n\n![Cranial Nerve Iii_ Oculomotor Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-iii-oculomotor-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Iii_ Oculomotor Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
01322766-0b09-4da5-b6e4-3c5fd2240673	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-ii-optic-nerve	Cranial Nerve Ii_ Optic Nerve	article	Optic nerve:\nTransmit special sensory information from the retina of the eye\nto the primary visual cortex of the brain.\nEnters skull via optic canal of the sphenoid bone\nNote:Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD): Seen in optic\nnerve disease or asymmetrical disease of the retina in which\npupils respond differently to light stimuli shone in one eye at a\ntime\nswinging light test if there is no RAPD the pupils of both eyes\nconstrict equally regardless of which eye is stimulated by the\nlight.\nIn an abnormal swinging-light test if there is a RAPD there is\nless pupil constriction in the eye with the retinal or optic\nnerve disease\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pituitary adenoma: Pituitary gland lies in close proximity to\n> optic chiasm,tumor of pituitary gland compresses optic nerve\n> at chiasm produces visual defect called bitemporal hemianopia\n\n![Cranial Nerve Ii_ Optic Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-ii-optic-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Ii_ Optic Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
c7dca7b2-94cc-48df-ba53-a1ff1a724aa6	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	innate-vs-acquired-immunity	Innate Vs Acquired Immunity	article	Innate vs Acquired immunity.\nImmune responses: Capacity of the body to resist pathogenic\nagents.\n\n![Innate Vs Acquired Immunity - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/innate-vs-acquired-immunity/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Innate Vs Acquired Immunity — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
8f000efc-7a3a-45c2-8bcd-ac9a96b21b90	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-iv-trochlear-nerve	Cranial Nerve Iv_ Trochlear Nerve	article	Trochlear Nerve:\nsmallest cranial nerve (by number of axons) yet has the longest\nintracranial course. Emerges from posterior aspect of midbrain,\ntravels through lateral aspect of cavernous sinus, exits skull\nthrough superior orbital fissure.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Iv_ Trochlear Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-iv-trochlear-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Iv_ Trochlear Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
83f3d842-667a-46ed-b111-29a3b0176c52	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-ix-glassopharyngeal-nerve	Cranial Nerve Ix_ Glassopharyngeal Nerve	article	Glossopharyngeal Nerve (CN IX):\noriginates in the medulla oblongata of the brain, moves\nlaterally in the posterior cranial fossa, and leaves the skull\nthrough jugular foramen.\nNote: Facial nerve in parotid gland splits into five terminal\nbranches but parotid gland is innervated by\nglossopharyngeal nerve\n\n![Cranial Nerve Ix_ Glassopharyngeal Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-ix-glassopharyngeal-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Ix_ Glassopharyngeal Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
2e6cb9dc-3bb9-42f4-bb7f-d17a08c6f78f	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-vii-facial-nerve	Cranial Nerve Vii_ Facial Nerve	article	Facial nerve:\nThe facial nerve exits the cranial cavity through the internal\nacoustic meatus and enters the facial canal of the temporal\nbone. The nerve then exits the skull through the Stylomastoid\nforamen (lies between the styloid and mastoid processes of the\ntemporal bone) to enter the parotid gland, ultimately\nbranching into five terminal branches to supply the face.\nCause of injury: Bell's palsy, Ramsay-Hunt syndrome, Guillain-\nBarre syndrome, mumps, parotid tumor, and injury to the\nmiddle ear.\nClinical effects of injury:  impaired sense of taste to anterior\ntwo-thirds of tongue, loss of efferent corneal reflex, impaired\nlacrimal fluid production, hyperacusis, and impaired salivation\nFacial nerve branches in the temporal bone:\nThe nerve to the stapedius: Innervate stapedius muscle.\nThe greater petrosal nerve: parasympathetic innervation to\nthe lacrimal gland and the mucous glands of the oral cavity,\nnose and pharynx\nThe chorda tympani: supply taste to the anterior two-thirds\nof the tongue\nFacial nerve branches between the stylomastoid foramen\nand parotid gland:3 motor branches\nPosterior auricular nerve innervates the intrinsic and\nextrinsic muscles of the outer ear.\nNerve to the posterior belly of the digastric muscle:\nInnervates the posterior belly of the digastric muscle one of\nthe suprahyoid muscles of neck responsible for raising the\nhyoid bone.\nNerve to the stylohyoid muscle: Innervates the stylohyoid\nmuscle, one of the suprahyoid muscles of neck responsible\nfor raising the hyoid bone.\n\nNote: Greater auricular nerve cutaneous branch of the cervical\nplexus provides sensory innervation to ear lobule, skin over the\nparotid gland and mastoid process of the temporal bone. Pain\nof parotitis and during ear piercing is due to innervation of\ngreater auricular nerve.\nFacial nerve terminal branches in parotid gland:\nThe cervical branch: innervating the platysma muscle.\nThe temporal branch: innervating muscles in the temple,\nforehead muscles (frontalis) and supraorbital areas (upper\nhalf of orbicularis oculi muscle)\nThe zygomatic branch: innervating muscles in the\ninfraorbital area (Lower half of orbicularis oculi muscle), the\nlateral nasal area and the upper lip\nThe buccal branch: innervating muscles in the cheek and\nupper lip\nThe marginal mandibular branch: innervating muscles of\nthe lower lip and chin\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> The chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve supplies taste\n> sensation to the anterior  of the tongue.\n\nIn the anterior , general sensation is supplied by the\ntrigeminal nerve (CNV). Specifically, the lingual nerve, a\nbranch of the mandibular nerve (CN V3).\nThe posterior  of the tongue both touch and taste are\ncarried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CNIX)	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
e2921263-7a2d-41f5-8189-327cc404330d	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-vi-abducent-nerve	Cranial Nerve Vi_ Abducent Nerve	article	Abducent nerve:\nPurely motor nerve supplying lateral rectus muscle, arises from\npons of the midbrain, travels through cavernous sinus, exits\nskull through superior orbital fissure.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Vi_ Abducent Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-vi-abducent-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Vi_ Abducent Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
c583b41b-d8b2-4338-a129-268f5b4976a4	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-v-trigeminal-nerve	Cranial Nerve V_ Trigeminal Nerve	article	Trigeminal nerve:\nMixed motor and sensory nerve. It is also the largest cranial\nnerve. Arises from several nuclei in the brainstem, exits\nbrainstem from pons It has three main divisions:\n\n### 1: Opthalmic V1\n\n### 2: Maxillary V2\n\n### 3: Mandibular V3\n\n![Cranial Nerve V_ Trigeminal Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-v-trigeminal-nerve/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Cranial Nerve V_ Trigeminal Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
154070ce-29a8-4229-96dd-98f9c5a827e9	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-xii-hypoglossal-nerve	Cranial Nerve Xii_ Hypoglossal Nerve	article	Hypoglossal Nerve:\nPure motor nerve arises from medulla oblongata passes\nlaterally across the posterior cranial fossa, within the\nsubarachnoid space, exits the cranium via the hypoglossal\ncanal of the occipital bone.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Xii_ Hypoglossal Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-xii-hypoglossal-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Xii_ Hypoglossal Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
b668ab38-b420-4833-9f9c-b0e19ccfcb46	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-xi-spinal-accessory-nerve	Cranial Nerve Xi_ Spinal Accessory Nerve	article	Spinal Accessory Nerve (CN XI):\nArises from cervical segments C1 - C5/C6, enters cranial cavity\nthrough foramen magnum, travels through posterior cranial\nfossa and exits skull through jugular foramen.\nOutside the cranium, it descends along the internal carotid\nartery and innervate sternocleidomastoid muscle.\n\n![Cranial Nerve Xi_ Spinal Accessory Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-xi-spinal-accessory-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve Xi_ Spinal Accessory Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
24df2922-230e-4f24-b857-5370dac4fe21	08aebe82-5789-46b8-88cb-3acf28e2d98d	cranial-nerve-x-vagus-nerve	Cranial Nerve X_ Vagus Nerve	article	Vagus nerve\noriginates in the medulla oblongata of the brain and leaves\nskull through jugular foramen together with CN IX and CN XI\nrespectively.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n\n#### Branches of vagus nerve\n\nSuperior laryngeal nerve: innervate cricothyroid muscle and\ngives sensory innervation to laryngopharynx and superior part\nof the larynx\nRight Recurrent laryngeal nerve: Hooks underneath right\nsubclavian artery and ascends towards larynx and innervate\nmuscles of larynx\nLeft Recurrent laryngeal nerve: Hooks underneath the arch of\naorta, and ascends towards larynx and innervate muscles of\nlarynx\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Vasovagal syncope:  Causes heart rate and blood pressure to\n> drop suddenly during a period of emotional stress caused by\n> initial increase in sympathetic outflow followed by a rebound\n> reduction in sympathetic activity leaving unopposed\n\n![Cranial Nerve X_ Vagus Nerve - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-x-vagus-nerve/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cranial Nerve X_ Vagus Nerve — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nparasympathetic activity causing vasodilatation, bradycardia,\nand hypotension\nOrthostatic syncope: Defined as an orthostatic drop of >20\nmm systolic blood pressure or >10 mm diastolic blood pressure.\nThis may be due to absolute volume depletion from\ndehydration or haemorrhage or to venodilatation caused by\nmedications or autonomic insufficiency (eg as occurs in\nParkinsons disease).\nCauses of syncope:\n\n![Cranial Nerve X_ Vagus Nerve - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cranial-nerves-in-depth/cranial-nerve-x-vagus-nerve/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Cranial Nerve X_ Vagus Nerve — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
6f314c31-36fb-4042-a260-b1d865911574	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	action-potential	Action Potential	article	Generation of Action potential\nOnce triggered an action potential will travel over the entire\nsurface of an excitable cell and will always have the same\namplitude. An action potential is an all or nothing response;\nbecause the size of the action potential is constant, the\nintensity of the stimulus is coded by the frequency of firing of\na neuron.\nThreshold Potential\nAction potentials are initiated in nerves by activation of\nligand-gated Na+ channels by neurotransmitters. Opening of\nthese Na+ channels results in a small influx of sodium and\ndepolarization of the negative resting membrane potential\n(-70 mV).\nIf the stimulus is sufficiently strong, the resting membrane\ndepolarises enough to reach threshold potential (generally\naround -55 mV), at which point an action potential can\noccur.\nDepolarisation\nVoltage-gated Na+ channels open, causing further\ndepolarisation and activating more voltage-gated Na+\nchannels and there is a sudden and massive sodium influx,\ndriving the cell membrane potential to about +40 mV.\nRepolarisation\nThe spike of the action potential is transient because as the\nmembrane potential becomes positive, the voltage-gated\nNa+ channels inactivate preventing further sodium influx.\nVoltage-gated K+ channels are also activated, causing the K+\npermeability to again be much greater than that for Na+, and\nthe potassium efflux leads to repolarization.\nDelayed closure of these rectifier K+ channels cause a\ntransient hyperpolarization.\nRefractory Period\n\nFollowing the action potential, Na+ channels remain inactive\nfor a time in a period known as the absolute refractory period\nwhere they cannot be opened by any amount of\ndepolarization.\nFollowing this there is a relative refractory period where the\ntemporary hyperpolarization makes the cell more difficult to\ndepolarise and an action potential can be generated only in\nresponse to a larger than normal stimulus.\nThe refractory period limits the frequency at which action\npotentials can be generated, and ensures that, once initiated,\nan action potential can travel only in one direction.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
f45ef030-40a6-4811-87d8-3e2a626f2cbf	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	homeostasis	Homeostasis	article	Homeostasis\nMaintenance of steady states in body by coordinated\nphysiologic mechanisms.\n\n### 1: Extracellular fluid: Internal environment in body in which the\n\ncells live and the fluid outside the cell\nDivisions of ECF\nPlasma: Part of ECF present in blood constitute ¼ OF ECF\nInterstitial fluid: Part of ECF present in spaces between cells\nconstitute ¾ of ECF.\n\n### 2: Intracellular fluid: Fluid inside the cells\n\nAverage 70 kg adult has approximately 40 liters of water.\nAround  is ICF\nTotal body water= 0.6 x bodyweight\nECF 0.2X Body weight\nICF 0.4X Body weight\nElectrolytes distribution in ECF and\nICF\n\n![Homeostasis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/homeostasis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Homeostasis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nOperating mechanism for\nhomeostasis:\n\n### 1: Negative feedback:\n\nComponents: Regulated variable, Sensor, Controller, and\nEffector.\nA regulated variable is sensed, information is fed back to the\ncontroller, and the effector acts to oppose change (hence the\nterm negative) Control most systems of body."\nExamples:  Osmoregulation, regulation of blood, pressure,\ntemperature regulation, regulation of CO2 in ECF.\n\n### 2: Positive feedback:\n\nPromotes changes in one direction leading amplified effect.\nCause vicious cycle and death, sometimes it can be useful.\nExamples:\nChildbirth (During labor): The release of oxytocin from the\nposterior gland during childbirth and labor\nBlood clotting.\nGeneration of nerve signals.\nSensation of needing to urinate.\nFollicular phase of menstrual cycle\n\n![Homeostasis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/homeostasis/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Homeostasis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
31437d4c-9475-4747-b3aa-43d8e5ccb512	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	nerve-muscle	Nerve Muscle	article	Nerve muscle\nSkeletal muscle structure:\nThe connective tissue surrounding the whole muscle is called\nthe epimysium. The connective tissue that extends beyond\nthe body of the muscle eventually blends into a tendon,\nwhich is attached to bone or cartilage. Skeletal muscle is\ncomposed of numerous parallel, elongated, multinucleated\ncells called muscle fibers (or myofibers) each enclosed by\nendomysium, which are grouped together to form fascicles.\nEach fascicle is surrounded by perimysium.\nThe sarcomere is the functional unit of the muscle.\nEach muscle fiber is divided at regular intervals along its\nlength into sarcomeres separated by Z-lines.\nMuscle filaments:\nThick filaments: Present in the A band in the center of the\nsarcomere\nContains Myosin (Recall)\nThin filaments: Present in the I band.\nAnchored at the Z lines.\nContains actin, tropomyosin and troponin.\nThere are three types of muscle:\nSkeletal\nCardiac\nSmooth\nComparison between them:\n\n![Nerve Muscle - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/nerve-muscle/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Nerve Muscle — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
19da1c3d-066e-46fd-a1a3-9906977c828e	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	neuromuscular-junction	Neuromuscular Junction	article	Neuromuscular junction\nJunction between a nerve ending and muscle membrane.\nSynapse: Junction between excitable cells that allows\ntransmission of a signal from first cell to next is called synapse\nAcetylcholine is a neurotransmitter synthesized in cytosol of\naxon terminal and is stored in small vesicles formed by Golgi\napparatus in cell body of motor neuron of spinal cord, that have\nbeen transported from cell body to axon terminal.\nSecretion: Action potential reach axon terminal →  Ca++ enter\ncytosol →  Cause Ach vesicle to fuse with membrane →  Ach is\nsecreted by rupturing of vesicle.\nFunction: Ach binds with receptor of Ach-gated channels on\nmuscle membrane and open them to cause tremendous Na+\ninflux giving rise to muscle action potential.\nDestruction: Released Ach is destroyed by acetylcholinesterase\nin basal lamina; this prevents re-excitation.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
b47cb924-16f2-4c1d-a7c3-b63f13a95853	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-maxillofacial-trauma	Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 25-year-old male is struck forcefully in the left eye by a squash ball. He presents with periorbital ecchymosis, enophthalmos, vertical diplopia (especially on upward gaze), and numbness over his left cheek and upper lip. What specific injury has occurred?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/maxillofacial-trauma/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Orbital floor blowout fracture with inferior rectus muscle entrapment and infraorbital nerve injury\n- **B.** Le Fort I fracture\n- **C.** Ruptured globe\n- **D.** Isolated nasal bone fracture\n- **E.** Zygomatic arch fracture without orbital involvement\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOrbital floor blowout fractures occur when blunt impact increases intraorbital pressure, fracturing the thin orbital floor (maxillary bone). Diplopia on upward gaze occurs due to entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle or periorbital fat in the fracture line. Hypoaesthesia over the cheek and upper teeth results from injury to the infraorbital nerve (branch of V2).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich classification system categorizes midface fractures resulting from high-energy blunt facial trauma?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/maxillofacial-trauma/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Le Fort Classification (Types I, II, III)\n- **B.** Gustilo-Anderson Classification\n- **C.** Garden Classification\n- **D.** Salter-Harris Classification\n- **E.** Neer Classification\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMidface fractures are classified using René Le Fort's system: Le Fort I = horizontal maxillary fracture separating alveolar process from maxilla ('floating palate'); Le Fort II = pyramidal fracture involving maxilla, nasal bones, and medial orbital floors ('floating maxilla'); Le Fort III = complete craniofacial disjunction ('floating face').\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 30-year-old male involved in an assault sustains a high-energy Le Fort III craniofacial disjunction fracture. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication facing this patient in the resuscitation bay?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/maxillofacial-trauma/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Catastrophic upper airway obstruction from posterior displacement of the maxilla/tongue and massive bleeding/secretions\n- **B.** Temporary dental malocclusion\n- **C.** Permanent facial scarring\n- **D.** Sinusitis\n- **E.** Conductive hearing loss\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMaxillofacial trauma threatens life primarily through Upper Airway Obstruction caused by posterior displacement of the detached midface/tongue against the pharynx, compounded by massive haemorrhage from branches of the maxillary artery, soft tissue oedema, aspirated teeth, and vomit. Early airway control is paramount.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 22-year-old boxer is struck on the jaw. He has severe pain, malocclusion (teeth do not meet properly), and sublingual haematoma. Why does a mandibular fracture almost always occur in at least TWO places ('ring bone' principle)?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Maxillofacial & Mandibular Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/maxillofacial-trauma/figure_04_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** The mandible functions as a rigid anatomical ring with the skull base; force applied to one side typically produces a direct fracture at the impact site and a contralateral fracture (e.g. body and opposite condyle)\n- **B.** The mandible has no muscle attachments\n- **C.** Mandibular bone consists only of cartilage\n- **D.** Mandibular trauma always involves the cervical spine\n- **E.** Chewing forces divide every fracture into two\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLike the pelvis, the mandible is a rigid U-shaped bone forming a closed ring with the temporomandibular joints and skull base. Blunt impact commonly produces bilateral fractures: a direct fracture at the site of impact (e.g. angle or parasymphysis) and an indirect fracture on the opposite side (e.g. contralateral condylar neck).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nOn examination of a patient with a nasal fracture, a bilateral, boggy, bluish-purple swelling of the anterior nasal septum is visualised on anterior rhinoscopy. Why must this Septal Haematoma be urgently drained?\n\n- **A.** Blood strips the perichondrium from the septal cartilage, leading to ischaemic avascular necrosis, septal perforation, and 'saddle nose' deformity, or septal abscess\n- **B.** It inevitably causes cavernous sinus thrombosis within 1 hour\n- **C.** It causes permanent anosmia\n- **D.** It causes optic nerve atrophy\n- **E.** It will cause severe epistaxis if not drained\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSeptal cartilage derives its entire blood supply from the overlying mucoperichondrium. A septal haematoma strips the perichondrium away; within 24-48 hours, ischaemic necrosis occurs, leading to destruction of the cartilaginous septum, permanent collapse of the nasal bridge ('saddle-nose deformity'), and potential bacterial abscess. Immediate incision and drainage is mandatory.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
db7af871-b6e5-4cb6-9611-a7382cf50c4f	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	human-immune-virus	Human Immune Virus	article	Human Immune virus (HIV)\nHigh-risk source for HIV\nMSM (men who have sex with men)\nFemale sexual contacts of MSM\nPeople reporting current or prior injecting drug use\nSex workers\nTrans women\nPeople from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence\n(>1%) * i-e Originally from sub-Saharan Africa.\nPeople reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country\nwith high diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where\ncontact occurs\nNote - Anal course is incorrect unless, in the option they\nmentioned unprotected anal course with sub-Saharan\npeople/ high risk source.\nTypes of Exposure\nNon-occupational exposure\nConsider patient at potential risk for HIV acquisition if:\nThe source has HIV and is not receiving antiretroviral therapy\n(ART), is at high risk for HIV infection, or is from a group with\nhigh prevalence of detectable HIV viraemia, AND\nThere has been exposure of vagina, rectum, mouth, or non-\nintact skin with blood, semen (Splash exposure to mucous\nmembranes, most commonly the eye), vaginal secretions,\nrectal secretions, breast milk, or anybody fluid that visibly\ncontains blood.\nOccupational exposure\nA percutaneous injury (e.g., a needlestick or cut with a sharp\nobject). Deep injury, visible blood, or injury with a device\nthat has been used intravascularly, all increase the risk of\nHIV transmission.\n\nContact of mucous membrane or non-intact skin (e.g.,\nexposed skin that is chapped, abraded, or afflicted with\ndermatitis). If the skin remains intact, there is no risk of HIV\ntransmission.\nA bite if the skin is broken as a result of trauma.\nNote - Exposure with urine, nasal secretions, saliva, sweat, or\ntears without the presence of blood is considered negligible\nrisk for acquisition of HIV.\nPathogenesis:\nHIV mainly infects CD4+ T helper cells. Viral replication\nresults in progressive T-cell depletion and impaired cell-\nmediated immunity with subsequent secondary\nopportunistic infections\nHIV-related opportunistic infection:\nNote: You must remember CD4 count of important\nopportunistic pathogens like Tuberculosis, Pneumocystis\njiroveci pneumonia, Pneumonia, Mycobacterium avium\ncomplex and toxoplasmosis Gondi\n\nDiagnosis: Combined HIV antibody and p24 antigen test\nMonitoring: HIV viral load (PCR) and CD4+ count\n\n![Human Immune Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/human-immune-virus/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Human Immune Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
a6aceb68-3091-4d58-ac43-1362716122f3	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	organisation-of-cell	Organisation of Cell	article	Organization of cell\nA typical cell is made up of four basic structures, which from\noutside to inside are:\nCell membrane\nCytoplasm\nNuclear membrane and nucleus\nCell membrane:\nOuter covering of cell that isolate individual cell from its\nNeighbours, composed of phospholipid bilayer with embedded\nproteins which provide signaling.\nThe membrane is selectively permeable; lipid-soluble\nmolecules such as O2 and CO2, and small molecules such as\nwater and urea can readily pass through the lipid bilayer, but\nlarger molecules such as glucose, and polar molecules such\nas ions cannot, and their transport is mediated by transporter\nand ion channel membrane proteins.\nCell membrane consists of thin layer called glycocalyx which\nattaches one cell to the adjacent cells.\nCell organelles quick revision:\n\n### 1: Lysosomes contains hydrolases responsible for digestion\n\nand breakdown of defective proteins\nUterus and breast after pregnancy regress by lysosomes\n\n### 2: Mitochondria: Powerhouse responsible for production of\n\nchemical energy in the form of ATP\nUnique feature in mitochondria is of own DNA.\n\n### 3: Endoplasmic reticulum:\n\nRough endoplasmic reticulum most likely synthesizes\nproteins.\nDetoxification of drug occurs in smooth endoplasmic\nreticulum.\nThe smooth endoplasmic reticulum serves as a store for\nintracellular Ca2+ and is the major site of lipid production.\n\n### 4: Golgi body: Secretory vesicles found\n\nThe Golgi apparatus packages proteins for delivery to specific\nintracellular destinations or into vesicles which can then be\nsecreted from the cell for extracellular action.\n\n### 5: Nucleus contains genetic material and nucleolus\n\nresponsible for ribosomes production.\n\n### 6: Ribosomes: Ribosomes are the cellular structures where\n\nprotein synthesis takes place. They can be found in the\ncytoplasm (free ribosomes) or attached to the endoplasmic\nreticulum (bound ribosomes).	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
71dd8f63-ce1c-4159-b9e6-8c150b49864a	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	osmolality-and-osmolal-gap	Osmolality and Osmolal Gap	article	Osmolality and osmolal gap\n\n![Osmolality and Osmolal Gap - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cell-physiology-membrane-transport/osmolality-and-osmolal-gap/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Osmolality and Osmolal Gap — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
b48edef5-9f4d-40d5-bbdd-ccbc55d805b6	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	skeletal-muscle-excitation-contraction-coupling	Skeletal Muscle Excitation Contraction Coupling	article	Skeletal excitation contraction\ncoupling\nMuscle contraction occurs when the concentration of\nintracellular Ca2+ rises.\nAn action potential travels down tube-shaped invaginations\nof the sarcolemma called T-tubules which penetrate\nthroughout the muscle fiber and lie adjacent to the terminal\ncisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\nVoltage changes in the T-tubules results in the opening of\nsarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ channels and the release of\nstored Ca2+ into the sarcoplasm.\nMolecular Events in Muscle\nContraction and Sliding\nfilament theory.\nMotor neuron axon releases acetylcholine\nAcetylcholine diffuses across synaptic cleft.\nThis stimulates the sarcolemma. The impulse travels over the\nmuscle fiber surface and down the T-tubules to the\nsarcoplasmic reticulum (SR).\nCalcium is bound to calsequestrin from SR, Calcium ions\ndiffuse out of the SR into the sarcoplasm and bind to\ntroponin.\nTropomyosin moves and exposes sites on actin filaments.\nActin and myosin form linkages.\nActin filaments are pulled inward by myosin cross-bridges\n(Sliding filament theory Muscle fibers shorten as contraction\noccurs.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
6eb1e826-003f-49ec-a37f-efb80f64e25b	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	teatnus-and-rigor-mortis	Teatnus and Rigor Mortis	article	Tetanus and Rigor mortis\nTetanus: During repeated stimulation of a muscle fiber,\nCalcium is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) more\nquickly than it can be reaccumulated; therefore, the\nintracellular [Ca2+] does not return to resting levels as it would\nafter a single twitch. The increased [Ca2+] allows more cross-\nbridges to form and, therefore, produces increased tension\n(tetanus)\nRigor mortis: The ability of muscles to go in state of\ncontracture formation after death is called rigor mortis.\nThis is due to the loss of ATP that is required to separate the\ncross bridges from actin during contraction.\nIt remains until after 15 to 20 hours, when the muse protein\ndetoriates.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
76bbc42f-059c-4a83-a9ba-7a38f6a63637	c1f0ed0c-68a3-41aa-9098-81e493a96913	vessel-fluid-dynamics	Vessel Fluid Dynamics	article	Vessel fluid dynamics\nFlow rate(Q) = vA (where v is flow velocity and A cross sectional\nvector Area\nTube flow:\nFlow is directly proportional to radius 4 (r4)\nTubing diameter: An important and frequently cited\nrelationship is that of the tubing's radius. Doubling the radius\nof a catheter increases the flow rate by 16-fold (r4). The larger\nthe IV catheter the greater the flow\nThe larger the flow capacity (cv) the larger the flow\nIn Coronary circulation if flow capacity(cv) is reduced to 50%\nor half flow will be decreased, or radius decreased to half flow\nwill decrease by 16 times.\nFluids with higher viscosity also have a slower rate of flow.\nPlasma has a similar viscosity to water, but blood contains\ncells which effectively increase the viscosity by three- to four-\nfold. Changes in cell number e.g., polycythemia, therefore,\naffect blood flow.\nTurbulent vs Laminar Flow\nTurbulent blood flow is multidirectional and travels at\ndifferent velocities leading to increased resistance and\nadditional shear stress on the vessel wall.\nLaminar flow: Frictional forces at the sides of a vessel cause a\ndrag force on the fluid touching them, creating a velocity\ngradient where the flow is greatest at the centre.\nWall Tention:\nPressure across the wall of a flexible tube (transmural\npressure) tends to extend it and increases wall tension.\nThis can be described by Laplace's Law: Pt = (Tw)/r\nwhere,\nPt = Transmural Pressure\nT = Wall Tension w = Wall Thickness\nr = Radius\nLaplace's Law also means that a large, dilated heart (e.g., heart\nfailure) has to develop more wall tension (contractile force) in\norder to obtain the same ventricular pressure, making it less\nefficient.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
79abf830-b47b-435d-9976-ba670cae443c	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	cardiac-cycle	Cardiac Cycle	article	Heart as pump\nThe heart is a →  chambered muscular organ that contracts\nrhythmically to pump blood into arteries and receive it back\nthrough veins, thereby keeping blood in constant circulation.\nComposed of Two separate pumps, right and left, each\nconsisting of an atrium and a ventricle.\nCardiac Cycle\nThe events that occur at the beginning of a heartbeat and last\nuntil the beginning of the next heartbeat are called the cardiac\ncycle.\nEach beat of the heart begins with a spontaneous action\npotential that is initiated in the sinus node of the right atrium\nnear the opening of the superior vena cava.\nThe action potential travels through both atria and the A-V\nnode and bundle into the ventricles. A delay of more than\nof a second occurs in the A-V node and bundle, which\nallows the atria to contract before the ventricles contract.\nPhases of Cardiac Cycle\nTwo,\nDiastole\nPeriod of relaxation or filling is called diastole. Can be divided\ninto:\n\n### 1. Period of Isovolumic or Isometric Relaxation\n\nDuring this, ventricles begin to relax (after systole ended),\nIntra-ventricular pressure falls aortic and pulmonary valves\nclose.\nBut ventricular vol. does not change.\n\n### 2.  Period of Rapid Filling of Ventricles (First ): During this,\n\natrial pressure becomes greater than    intra-ventricular pressure (just after systole is over), AV valves (tricuspid and\nmitral) open, and blood flows rapidly into respective ventricles.\n\n### 3. Diastasis (Middle ): During this, blood continues to empty\n\ninto atria from veins and passes on thru atria directly into\nventricles\n4.Atrial Systole (Late ): During this, atria contracts and\npump 20-30% blood into ventricles. Systolic pressure of right\natrium rises to 4-6 mm Hg. and that of left atrium to 7-8 mm\nHg.\nSystole:\nPeriod of Contraction can be divided into\n\n### 1. Period of isovolumic or isometric contraction During this,\n\nventricles begin to contract.\nventricular pressure rise\nAV valves are closed.\nsemilunar (aortic & pulmonary) valves do not open. So, there\nis no emptying and no volume change.\n\n### 2. Period of Ejection: During this, left ventricular pressure rises\n\nslightly above 80 mm Hg and right ventricular pressure slightly\nabove 8 mm Hg. These pressures push aortic and pulmonary\nvalves open and empty blood into the aorta and pulmonary\ntrunk. Divided into.\nPeriod of Rapid Ejection: During first , 70% emptying\noccurs.\nPeriod of Slow Ejection During last , 30% emptying occur.\nRelationship of the\nElectrocardiogram to the Cardiac\nCycle\nThe P wave is caused by the spread of depolarization across\nthe atria, which causes atrial contraction. Atrial pressure\nincreases just after the P wave.\nThe QRS waves appear as a result of ventricular\ndepolarization about 0.16 second after the onset of the P wave, and this initiates ventricular contraction; then the\nventricular pressure begins to increase.\nThe ventricular T wave is caused by repolarization of the\nventricle.\nFunctions of Heart Valves\nAV (tricuspid and mitral valves) prevent backflow of blood\nfrom ventricles to atria during systole.\nSemilunar (aortic and pulmonary) valves prevent backflow\nof blood from aorta and pulmonary trunk into ventricles\nduring diastole.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
2c72ae31-b49b-45eb-96c3-2c72d6c73b93	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	cardiac-excitation-contraction-coupling	Cardiac Excitation Contraction Coupling	article	Cardiac excitation contraction\ncoupling:\nCalcium entry during action potential is essential for\ncontraction but it accounts for only 25% of the rise in\nintracellular Ca2+. The rest is released from Ca2+ stores in the\nsarcoplasmic reticulum (SR).\nCalcium enters the cell through voltage-gated L-type calcium\nchannels.\nCalcium activates the ryanodine receptor on the\nsarcoplasmic reticulum, which releases more calcium into\nthe sarcoplasm (Calcium Induced Calcium Release)\nCalcium initiates muscle contraction by binding to troponin.\nContraction occurs as it does in skeletal muscle.\nTreppe Effect:\nMore action potentials occur ⟶  More Ca2+ enters the cell\nduring the AP plateau ⟶  more Ca2+ is stored in the SR ⟶\nmore Ca2+ is released from the SR and thus more Ca2+ is left\ninside the cell and greater tension is produced during\ncontraction. Increased heart rate increases the force of\ncontraction.\nRelaxation:\nIn relaxation, about 80% of Ca2+ is rapidly pumped back into\nthe SR by Ca2+ ATPase pumps.\nThe Ca2+ that entered the cell during the AP is transported\nout of the cell primarily by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger in the\nmembrane which pumps one Ca2+ ion out in exchange for\nthree Na+ ions in, using the Na+ electrochemical gradient as\nan energy source.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
07f8b37d-3316-42bf-ab66-7ec19a295c7d	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	cardiac-myocyte	Cardiac Myocyte	article	Cardiac myocyte\nCardiac myocyte and comparison with other\ncells\n\n![Cardiac Myocyte - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/cardiac-myocyte/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cardiac Myocyte — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
3f4add4a-1e97-4a6d-bbab-fc5b4613d53b	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	components-of-the-vasculature	Components of the Vasculature	article	Components of the vasculature\n\n### 1: Arteries:\n\ndeliver oxygenated blood to the tissues. are thick walled, with\nextensive elastic tissue and smooth muscle.\nare under high pressure. The blood volume contained in the\narteries is called the stressed volume.\n\n### 2. Arterioles:\n\nAre the smallest branches of the arteries.\nAre the site of highest resistance in the cardiovascular\nsystem. have a smooth muscle wall that is extensively\ninnervated by autonomic nerve fibers.\nThe greater the change in resistance at any point in the\nvasculature, the greater the loss of pressure at that point.\nArterioles have the most increase in resistance and cause\nthe largest decrease in blood pressure.\nArteriolar resistance is regulated by the autonomic nervous\nsystem (ANS).\nAlpha-1Adrenergic receptors are found on the arterioles of\nthe skin, splanchnic, and renal circulations.\nBeta-2 Adrenergic receptors are found on arterioles of\nskeletal muscle.\n\n### 3. Capillaries:\n\nHave the largest total cross-sectional and surface area.\nConsist of a single layer of endothelial cells surrounded by\nbasal lamina.\nAre thin walled.\nAre the site of exchange of nutrients, water, and gases.\nCapillary permeability:\nCapillaries throughout the body vary in their permeability\nbased on the size of their pores. There are three basic types:\nContinuous capillaries, found in the skin, lungs, muscles and\nCNS, are the most selective with low permeability, as junctions between the endothelial cells are very tight,\nrestricting the flow of molecules with MW > 10,000.\nFenestrated capillaries, found in renal glomeruli, endocrine\nglands and intestinal villi, are more permeable with less tight\njunctions, and the endothelial cells are also punctured by\npores which allow large amounts of fluids or metabolites to\npass.\nDiscontinuous capillaries, found in the reticuloendothelial\nsystem (bone marrow, liver and spleen), have large gaps\nbetween endothelial cells and are permeable to red blood\ncells.\n\n### 4:  Venules\n\nare formed from merged capillaries.\n\n### 5: Veins:\n\nprogressively merge to form larger veins. The largest vein, the\nvena cava, returns blood to the heart.\nAre thin walled.\nare under low pressure.\ncontain the highest proportion of blood in the cardiovascular\nsystem. The blood volume contained in the veins is called the\nunstressed volume.\nHave alpha 1 adrenergic receptors.\nPressure profile in blood vessels:\nAs blood flows through the systemic circulation, pressure\ndecreases progressively because of the resistance to blood\nflow.\nThus, pressure is highest in the aorta and large arteries and\nlowest in the venae cavae. The largest decrease in pressure\noccurs across the arterioles because they are the site of\nhighest resistance.\nMean pressures in the systemic circulation are as follows:\nAorta, 100 mm Hg\nArterioles, 50 mm Hg\nCapillaries, 20 mm Hg\nVena cava, 4 mm Hg	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
34420193-4efc-4fce-95ff-7167d7cbe87d	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	conducting-system-of-heart	Conducting System of Heart	article	Conducting system of heart\nSpecial system of heart that generates rhythmical impulses\nand conducts these impulses through heart, is called excitatory\nand conductive system of heart. Consists of:\nSA node\nInternodal pathway\nAV node (slowest conducting fiber)\nAV bundle (bundle of His)\nRight and Left branches of Purkinje\nConductive Pathway of Heart:\nSA node initiates the cardiac muscle (pacemaker) →  Internodal\nfibers and AV node (delay impulse from the atria to ventricle)\n→  AV bundle branches →  Purkinje fibers (from apex to\nventricles).\nThe sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker located\nin Right atrium. The SAN will normally discharge at a rate of\n60-100 bpm. If the impulse generated in the Sa node is\nblocked, a group of cells further down the heart will become\nits pacemaker.\nThe atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary pacemaker\nlocated between right atrium and ventricle near the atrial\nseptum, and will normally discharge at about 40-60 beats\nper minute\nPurkinje fibers produce spontaneous action potential at rate\nof 20-40 bpm.\nNote: AV nodal abnormality is responsible for prolonged PR\ninterval (1st degree heart block)	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
304f5260-c3e0-46ce-8d4b-1858c03187f1	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	electrocardiography-ecg	Electrocardiography (ecg)	article	ECG\n12 Conventional ECG leads record the difference in potential.\nSix extremity limb leads and six chest pericardial leads.\nSix Extremity leads: Three bipolar leads (I, II, III) and three\nunipolar leads aVR, aVL, aVF\nSix chest leads:\nV1:4TH intercostal space, just to right the sternum\nV2:4TH intercostals space, just to left of the sternum.\nV3: Midway between V2 and V4\nV4: Midclavicular line, fifth intercostal space\nV5: Anterior axillary line, same level as V4\nV5: Midaxillary line, same level as V4 and V5\nThe three limb electrodes, I, II and III form a triangle\n(Einthoven's Triangle), at the right arm (RA), left arm (LA) and\nleft leg (LL).\n\n![Electrocardiography (ecg) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/electrocardiography-ecg/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Electrocardiography (ecg) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
dca93237-04b7-437c-8eb0-8191813d3332	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	heart-sounds-mechanical-events-and-correspondance-with-ecg	Heart Sounds, Mechanical Events and Correspondance with ECG	article	Heart sounds, Mechanical event and correspondence with\nECG\n\n![Heart Sounds, Mechanical Events and Correspondance with ECG - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/heart-sounds-mechanical-events-and-correspondance-with-ecg/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Heart Sounds, Mechanical Events and Correspondance with ECG — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
e0213708-cb14-4c52-95df-a8ed8ae33615	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	heart-volumes	Heart Volumes	article	Heart volume\nThe total blood volume in the circulatory system of a healthy\nadult is about 5 L.\nStroke Volume Output: The volume of blood that is ejected\nby each ventricle during systole is called stroke volume\noutput (Value:70 ml/beat)\nHeart rate= 70bpm\nCardiac output (CO) = Stroke volume x Heart rate = 70\nmL/beat x 70 beats/min = 4900 mL/min\nMean arterial pressure: Average arterial pressure during\nsingle cardiac cycle. MAP can be calculated by:  Diastolic\npressure +  of pulse pressure (SBP-DBP) OR 80+  (120-\n80) = 93mmhg where BP\nEnd-Diastolic Volume: The volume of blood in each ventricle\nat the end of the diastole is called end-diastolic volume.\n(Value:120-130ml)\nEnd-Systolic Volume: The volume of blood in each ventricle\nat end of systole is called end-systolic volume. (Value: 50-\n60ml)\nEjection Fraction: Fraction of end-diastolic volume that is\nejected during systole, is called ejection fraction. (Value: 60%)	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
4682aa84-d422-43ff-b3be-0e6d86152bd6	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	harmonal-control-of-blood-flow	Harmonal Control of Blood Flow	article	Hormonal Control of Blood Flow\nHistamine: causes arteriolar dilation and venous constriction.\nThe combined effects of arteriolar dilation and venous\nconstriction cause increased capillary hydrostatic pressure and\nincreased filtration out of the capillaries resulting in local\nedema and is released in response to tissue trauma.\nBradykinin: causes arteriolar dilation and venous constriction.\nproduces increased filtration out of the capillaries (similar to\nhistamine) and causes local edema.\nSerotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine): causes arteriolar\nconstriction and is released in response to blood vessel\ndamage.\nHelp prevent blood loss. has been implicated in the vascular\nspasms of migraine headaches.\nProstaglandins: Prostacyclin is a vasodilator in several vascular\nbeds.\nE-series prostaglandins are vasodilators.\nF- Series prostaglandins are vasoconstrictors.\nThromboxane A2 is a vasoconstrictor.\nCoronary circulation: is controlled almost entirely by local\nmetabolic factors.\nExhibits autoregulation. exhibits active and reactive\nhyperemia.\nThe most important local metabolic factors are hypoxia and\nadenosine.\nFor example, increases in myocardial contractility are\naccompanied by an increased demand for O2. To meet this\ndemand, compensatory vasodilation of coronary vessels\noccurs and, accordingly, both blood flow and O2 delivery to\nthe contracting heart muscle increase (active hyperemia).\n\nDuring systole, mechanical compression of the coronary\nvessels reduces blood flow. After        the period of occlusion,\nblood flow increases to repay the O2 debt (reactive\nhyperemia).\nCerebral Circulation:\nCerebral circulation is controlled almost entirely by local\nmetabolic factors.\nexhibits autoregulation. exhibits active and reactive\nhyperemia.\nThe most important local vasodilator for the cerebral\ncirculation is CO2 Increases in PCO2 cause vasodilation of the\ncerebral arterioles and increased blood flow to the brain.\nDecreases in PC02 cause vasoconstriction of cerebral\narterioles and decreased blood flow to the brain,\nSkeletal muscle: is controlled by the extrinsic sympathetic\ninnervation of blood vessels in skeletal muscle and by local\nmetabolic factors. is the primary regulator of blood flow to the\nskeletal muscle at rest.\n\n### 1. Sympathetic innervation: The arterioles of skeletal muscle\n\nare densely innervated by sympathetic fibers. The veins also\nare innervated, but less densely. There are both alpha-1 and\nbeta-2 receptors on the blood vessels of skeletal muscle.\nStimulation of alpha-1 receptors causes vasoconstriction.\nStimulation of ß2 receptors causes vasodilation. The state of\nconstriction of skeletal muscle arterioles is a major\ncontributor to the TPR (because of the large mass of skeletal\nmuscle).\n\n### 2. Local metabolic control: Blood flow in skeletal muscle\n\nexhibits autoregulation and active and reactive hyperemia\nDemand for O2 in skeletal muscle varies with metabolic\nactivity level, and blood flow. is regulated to meet demand.\nDuring exercise, when demand is high, these local metabolic\nmechanisms are dominant.\nThe local vasodilator substances are lactate, adenosine, and\nK. Mechanical effects during exercise temporarily compress\nthe arteries and decrease blood flow. During the post occlusion period, reactive hyperemia increases blood flow to\nrepay the O2 debt.\nSkin: Skin has extensive sympathetic innervation. Cutaneous\nblood flow is under extrinsic control. Temperature regulation is\nthe principal function of the cutaneous sympathetic nerves.\nIncreased temperature leads to cutaneous vasodilation,\nallowing dissipation of excess body heat.\nTrauma produces the "triple response" in skin-a red line, a red\nflare, and a wheal. A wheal is local edema that results from\nthe local release of histamine, which increases capillary\nfiltration.\nVasoconstricting and Vasodilating agent:\n\n![Harmonal Control of Blood Flow - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/harmonal-control-of-blood-flow/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Harmonal Control of Blood Flow — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
c0ee7d9c-8f4d-46b8-9688-954f6583c96d	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	microcirculation-and-lymph	Microcirculation and Lymph	article	Microcirculation and Lymph\nStructure of capillary beds\nMetarterioles branch into the capillary beds. At the junction\nof the arterioles and capillaries is a smooth muscle band\ncalled the precapillary sphincter.\nTrue capillaries do not have smooth muscle; they consist of a\nsingle layer of endothelial cells surrounded by a basement\nmembrane.\nClefts (pores) between the endothelial cells allow passage of\nwater-soluble substances.\nBlood flow through the capillaries is regulated by contraction\nand relaxation of the arterioles and pre-capillary sphincter.\nPassage of substances across the capillary\nwall\nLipid-soluble substances across the membranes of the\ncapillary endothelial cells by simple diffusion, include O2 and\nCO2\nSmall water-soluble substances cross via the water-filled\nclefts between the endothelial cells which include water,\nglucose, and amino acids.\nGenerally, protein molecules are too large to pass freely\nthrough the clefts.\nIn the brain, the clefts between endothelial cells are\nexceptionally tight (blood-brain barrier).\nIn the liver and intestine, the clefts are exceptionally wide\nand allow passage of protein. These capillaries are called\nsinusoids.\nLarge water-soluble substances can cross by pinocytosis.\nLymph:\nFunction of lymph: Normally, filtration of fluid out of the\ncapillaries is slightly greater than absorption of fluid into the\ncapillaries. The excess filtered fluid is returned to the circulation via the lymph also returns any filtered protein to the\ncirculation.\nUnidirectional flow of lymph One-way flap valves permit\ninterstitial fluid to enter, but not leave the lymph vessels.\nFlow through larger lymphatic vessels is also unidirectional\nand is aided by one-way valves and skeletal muscle\ncontraction.\nEdema:\noccurs when the volume of interstitial fluid exceeds the\ncapacity of the lymphatics to return it to the circulation.\nCan be caused by excess filtration or blocked lymphatics.\nHistamine causes both arteriolar dilation and venous\nconstriction, which together produce a large increase in\ncapillary hydrostatic pressure and local edema.\nIn nephrotic syndrome urinary loss of protein result in\nhypoalbuminemia and edema is due to increased capillary\nhydrostatic pressure and decreased plasma oncotic\npressure\nNitric oxide (NO):\nSodium nitroprusside is broken down into the circulation to\nrelease Nitric Oxide (NO)\nis produced in the endothelial cells. causes local relaxation of\nvascular smooth muscle.\nMechanism of action involves the activation of guanylate\ncyclase and production of cyclic guanosine\nmonophosphate (cGMP)\nNO is one form of endothelial-derived relaxing factor (EDRF).\nCirculating Acetylcholine causes vasodilation by stimulating\nthe production of NO in vascular smooth muscle.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
3f6290d8-5ed3-46aa-a846-64d455d2c9d3	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	regulation-of-blood-pressure	Regulation of Blood Pressure	article	Regulation of Blood pressure\nMechanism of baroreceptor and\nchemoreceptor reflex:\nImpaired baroreceptor reflex:\nOrthostatic hypotension (Postural hypotension): fainting or\nlight-headedness on standing may occur in individuals whose\nbaroreceptor reflex mechanism is impaired (e.g., individuals\ntreated with sympatholytic agents) or who are volume\ndepleted.\nOn standing from a prone position, gravity causes blood to pool\ninto veins in the legs. Central venous pressure (CVP) falls,\ncausing a fall in stroke volume and cardiac output and thus a\nfall in blood pressure.\nOther regulation of Arterial blood\npressure:\nCerebral ischemia: When brain is ischemic, the partial pressure\nof carbon dioxide (Pco2) in brain tissue increases\nChemoreceptors in the vasomotor center respond by\nincreasing sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels.\nConstriction of arterioles causes intense peripheral\nvasoconstriction and increased TPR. Blood flow to other\norgans (e.g., kidneys) is significantly reduced to preserve\n\n![Regulation of Blood Pressure - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/regulation-of-blood-pressure/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Regulation of Blood Pressure — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nblood flow to the brain. Mean arterial pressure can increase to\nlife-threatening levels.\nThe Cushing reaction is an example of the response to cerebral\nischemia. Increases in intracranial pressure cause compression\nof the cerebral blood vessels, leading to cerebral ischemia and\nincreased cerebral Pco ₂ . The vasomotor center directs an\nincrease in sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels,\nwhich causes a profound increase in arterial pressure.	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
96ba0be9-8d73-41c5-839e-6ae787519539	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	ventricular-myocyte-vs-pacemaker-action-potential	Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential	article	Ventricular myocyte action\npotential\nRMP of Ventricular myocyte is -90mv depolarization initiates\nwhen RMP reaches threshold of -65mv\nFast Na channels activate and a rapid Na+ influx\ndepolarizes membrane potential.\nPacemaker action potential:\nAction potential of SA node differs from ventricular muscle.\nRMP of SA node is -60mv depolarization initiates when RMP\nreaches threshold of -40mv.\nSlow calcium channels activation and calcium influx\ninitiates depolarization unlike of other ventricular muscle Na\ninflux initiates depolarization\n\n![Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/ventricular-myocyte-vs-pacemaker-action-potential/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/ventricular-myocyte-vs-pacemaker-action-potential/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Ventricular Myocyte Vs Pacemaker Action Potential — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
5dd881d0-2f83-4d2d-a910-49628c175565	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	alveolar-arterial-gradient	Alveolar Arterial Gradient	article	A-a gradient\nDifference between alveolar Po2 and arterial Po2\nA-a gradient: PA02-Pao2 (where PA02 alveolar po2 and Pao2\nis arterial po2)\nThe A-a gradient is used to determine whether O2 has\nequilibrated between alveolar gas and arterial blood.\nThe normal A-a gradient is between 0- and 10mm Hg. Since\nO2 normally equilibrates between alveolar gas and arterial\nblood, PAo2 is approximately equal to Pao2. e.g.,\nHypoventilation: CNS depression, Neuromuscular disease\n(including myasthenia gravis and kyphoscoliosis), Obesity\nand Low inspired fio2 on high altitude\nThe A-a gradient is increased (>10 mm Hg) if O2 does not\nequilibrate between alveolar gas and arterial blood (e.g.,\ndiffusion defect, V/Q defect, and right-to-left shunt) and\nPAo2 is greater than Pao ₂\nHypoxia and Hypoxemia\nHypoxia: decreased o2 delivery to the tissues caused by\ndecreased blood flow, hypoxemia, decreased haemoglobin\nconcentration, Co poisoning and Cyanide poisoning\nNote: Erythropoietin (EPO) is growth factor that is synthesized\nin the kidneys in response to hypoxia\nHypoxemia: Decrease in arterial pao2 caused by decreased\nAlveolar PAo2, diffusion defect, V/Q defects and right to left\nshunt	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
c0dcc77e-295e-4b0c-af7c-4b7f91afc103	0d0079e7-df95-4869-9b31-7ad2ce332c77	regulation-of-cardiac-pumping	Regulation of Cardiac Pumping	article	Regulation of Cardiac Pumping\nCardiac pumping (i.e., volume of blood pumped by heart) is\nregulated by:\nIntrinsic regulation: pumping in response to changes in\nvolume of blood flowing into heart (obeys Frank starling Law\nof heart).\nFrank Starling Law:\nWhen venous return of blood increases, the heart muscle\nstretches more, which makes it pump with a greater force of\ncontraction. The Frank- Starling mechanism of the heart can be\nstated in another way:\nWithin physiological limits, the heart pumps all the blood that\ncomes to it without allowing excess accumulation of blood in\nthe veins.\nMechanism of Frank Starling Law\nStates that "Within physiologic limits, the heart pumps all the\nblood that returns to it by the way of veins.\nExtra volume of blood enters heart chambers.\ncardiac muscle over-stretched\ncardiac muscle contracts with increased force because the\ninitial length of fiber increases and pumps extra blood into\narteries.\nConcepts of preload and after load:\nThe heart will contract with greater force when preload/EDV\nis increased. Preload is diastolic filling volume of left\nventricle reflect stretch of cardiac muscle cells, greater the\nstretch more forcefully heart will contract. when there is\nincreased EDV/ preload there is increased in Stroke volume\nthis intrinsic relationship between EDV and stroke volume is\nknown as frank starling law of heart.\n\nAfterload is the pressure that the chambers of the heart\nmust generate in order to eject blood out of the heart occurs\nin systole.\nNote: In heart failure, the Frank-Starling curve is moved down\nand flattened due to reduced inotropy, so that more venous\nreturn and hence preload is required to increase stroke volume.\nAs venous return and preload rise, myocyte stretching occurs\nthat increases sarcomere length.\nFactors affecting frank starling curve.\nincrease in contractility shift the curve upward and to the\nleft e.g.: positive inotropic drugs (RECALL)\nDecrease in contractility= downward and to the right\nIncrease in preload = rightward shift\ndecrease in preload=leftward shift\nIncrease in Afterload=Downward and right\nDecrease in Afterload=upward and left\nChronotropic and ionotropic effects\nFactors that affect intracellular [Ca2+] concentration and\nhence cardiac contractility are called inotropes.\nChromotropes increase sinus nodal discharge increase heart\nrate.\nSympathetic stimulation increases cardiac muscle\ncontractility because it causes the release of noradrenaline.\nNoradrenaline is a positive inotrope; it binds to β 1-\nadrenoceptors on the membrane and causes increased Ca2+\nentry via L-type channels during the AP and thus increases\nCa2+ release from the SR. Noradrenaline also increases Ca2+\nsequestration into the SR and thus more Ca2+ is available for\nthe next contraction.\nAdrenaline is a non-selective adrenergic agonist with potent\nβ 1 and moderate α 1 and β 2-receptor activity. Increase\nmyocardial force of contraction (positive inotrope) and heart\nrate (positive chronotrope) occur as a result of β 1 receptor\nstimulation. Systemic vascular resistance is increased\noverall because the stimulation of α 1 receptors results in\n\n![Regulation of Cardiac Pumping - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-physiology-hemodynamics/regulation-of-cardiac-pumping/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Regulation of Cardiac Pumping — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nperipheral vasoconstriction, which counters the vasodilation\ndue to β 2 receptor activation.\nDigoxin slows the removal of Ca2+ from the cell by inhibiting\nthe membrane Na+ pump which generates the Na+ gradient\nrequired for driving the export of Ca2+; consequently, the\nremoval of Ca2+ from the myocyte is slowed and more Ca2+\nis available inside the myocyte for the next contraction.\nExamples:\nAdrenaline is positive inotrope as well as positive\nchronotropic agent, increases both heart rate and\ncontractility.\nDopamine is positive inotropic drug increases contractility.\nDigoxin is positive inotrope and negative chronotrope.	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
e276555a-ec8a-418b-a31a-05f513543193	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	breathing-cycle-description-of-pressure-and-airflow	Breathing Cycle - Description of Pressure and Airflow	article	Breathing cycle (description of\npressures and airflow)\nAt rest (before inspiration begins):\nAlveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure, because lung\npressures are expressed relative to atmospheric pressure,\nalveolar pressure is said to be zero.\nIntrapleural pressure is negative.\nAt FRC, the opposing forces of the lungs trying to collapse\nand the chest wall trying to expand create a negative\npressure in the intrapleural space between them. Intrapleural\npressure can be measured by a balloon catheter in the\nesophagus.\nLung volume is the Functional residual capacity.\nDuring inspiration:\nThe inspiratory muscles contract and cause the volume of the\nthorax to increase. As lung volume increases, alveolar pressure\ndecreases to less than atmospheric pressure (i.e. becomes\nnegative).\nThe pressure gradient between the atmosphere and the\nalveoli now causes air to flow into the lungs; airflow will\ncontinue until the pressure gradient dissipates.\nIntrapleural pressure becomes more negative.\nBecause lung volume increases during inspiration, the elastic\nrecoil strength of the lungs also increases. As a result,\nintrapleural pressure becomes even more negative than it\nwas at rest. Changes in intrapleural pressure during\ninspiration are used to measure the dynamic compliance of\nthe lungs.\nAt the peak of inspiration, lung volume is the FRC plus one\nTidal volume.\nDuring expiration:\n\nAlveolar pressure becomes greater than atmospheric\npressure. "The alveolar pressure becomes greater (i.e. becomes\npositive) because alveolar gas is compressed by the elastic\nforces of the lung.\nThus, alveolar pressure is now higher than atmospheric\npressure, the pressure gradient is reversed, and air flows out\nof the lungs.\nIntrapleural pressure returns to its resting value during a\nnormal (passive) expiration. However, during a forced\nexpiration, intrapleural pressure actually becomes\npositive.This positive intrapleural pressure compresses the\nairways and makes expiration more difficult.\nIn COPD, in which airway resistance is increased, patients\nlearn to expire slowly with "pursed lips." By creating a\nresistance at the mouth, airway pressure is increased and\nprevents the airway collapse that may occur with a forced\nexpiration.\nLung volume returns to FRC.	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
5bc099af-938d-4312-9a5a-effc868bd84e	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	central-control-of-breathing	Central Control of Breathing	article	Involuntary respiration\nInvoluntary respiration is under subconscious control. The\ndiaphragm and intercostal muscles are the primary respiratory\nmuscles. They are stimulated by groups of neurons located in\nthe pons and medulla. These neurons form the respiratory\ncontrol centre. They send impulses to the primary respiratory\nmuscles, via the phrenic and intercostal nerves, which\nstimulate their contraction. There are three main groups of\nneurons involved in respiration:\nThe ventral respiratory group controls expiration\nThe dorsal respiratory group controls inspiration\nThe pontine respiratory group controls the rate and pattern\nof breathing.\nNote: When expiratory neurons fire they inhibit the activity of\ninspiratory neurons and once the neurons stop firing, the\ninspiratory muscles relax, and expiration occurs.\nVoluntary respiration\nVoluntary respiration is under conscious control. It is\ncontrolled via the motor cortex in the cerebrum, which\nreceives inputs from the limbic system and hypothalamus.\nSignals are thought to be sent to the spinal cord from the\nmotor cortex, which are then passed onto the respiratory\nmuscles.\nCentral control of breathing\n(brainstem and cerebral cortex)\n\n### 1. Medullary respiratory center (Recall)\n\nA: Dorsal respiratory group is primarily responsible for\ninspiration and generates the basic rhythm for breathing\nInput to the dorsal respiratory group comes from the vagus\nand glossopharyngeal nerves. The vagus nerve relays\ninformation from peripheral chemoreceptors in the lung. The\nglossopharyngeal nerve relays information from peripheral chemoreceptors and Output from the dorsal respiratory\ngroup travels, via the phrenic nerve, to the diaphragm\nB: Ventral respiratory group is primarily responsible for\nexpiration, is not active during normal, quiet breathing, when\nexpiration is passive.\nActivated during exercise, when expiration becomes an\nactive process.\n\n### 2. Apneustic center is located in the lower pons. Stimulates\n\ninspiration, producing a deep and prolonged inspiratory gasp\n(apneusis). Normally apneustic center promotes deep\nbreathing, damage to apneustic center promotes shallow\nirregular/ Erratic breathing.\n\n### 3. Pneumotaxic center is located in the upper pons inhibits\n\ninspiration and, therefore, regulates both the rate and pattern\nof breathing. Pneumotaxic center normally prevents\nexcessive deep breathing, damage to pneumotaxic results in\nincrease in depth of respiration (deep breathing) and\ndecrease in respiratory rate.\n4.Cerebral cortex: Breathing can be under voluntary control;\ntherefore, a person can voluntarily hyper ventilate or hypo\nventilate.\nNote: Hypoventilation (breath-holding) is limited by the\nresulting increase in Pco2 and decrease in P02	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
c1e4258e-e29f-4704-a626-45282de89917	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	control-of-ventilation	Control of Ventilation	article	Control of ventilation is mediated via\ncentral and Peripheral\nchemoreceptors.\nChemoreceptor overview:\nCentral chemoreceptor Vs Peripheral\nchemoreceptor\n\n![Control of Ventilation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/control-of-ventilation/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Control of Ventilation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Control of Ventilation - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/control-of-ventilation/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Control of Ventilation — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
c71f0767-92b6-441e-84ae-654ce4043afe	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	dead-space	Dead Space	article	Dead Space\nAnatomic dead space\nRefers to volume of conducting airways.\nNormally approximately 150 ml\nAlveolar dead space:\nRefers to alveoli incapable of gas exchange, in normal healthy\nindividual it is negligible, but it may become significant in\nV/Q mismatch i.e., in pulmonary embolism when alveoli do\nnot take part in gas exchange.\nPhysiologic dead space: sum of anatomical and alveolar dead\nspace.\nDefined as the volume of the lungs that does not participate\nin gas exchange. is approximately equal to the anatomic\ndead space in normal lungs may be greater than the\nanatomic dead space in lung diseases in which the\nventilation/perfusion (V/Q) defects.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
7a741110-c26f-4e71-b524-a4841c4ae16a	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	diffusion-and-diffusion-capacity	Diffusion and Diffusion Capacity	article	Diffusion of gases such as 02\nand CO ₂\nThe diffusion rates of O2 and CO2 depend on the partial\npressure differences across the membrane and the area\navailable for diffusion.\nFor example, the diffusion of O2 from alveolar air into the\npulmonary capillary depends on the partial pressure\ndifference for O2 between alveolar air and pulmonary\ncapillary blood.\nNormally, capillary blood with alveolar gas; when the partial\npressures of 02 become equal there is no more net diffusion\nof O2.\nGas diffusion across the alveolar-pulmonary capillary barrier\noccurs according to Ficks law.\nFick's law\nshows the relationship between the variables that affect\ndiffusion: Rate of diffusion directly proportional to surface\narea x concentration difference and inversely proportional to\nthickness of membrane.\nFick's law tells us that the rate of diffusion of a gas increases:\nthe larger the surface area involved in gas exchange.\nthe greater the partial pressure gradient across the\nmembrane\nthe thinner the membrane\nthe more soluble the gas in the membrane\nthe lower the molecular weight of the gas\nDiffusion Capacity (DLCO)\nLung diffusing capacity is the equivalent of permeability of the\nalveolar-pulmonary capillary barrier and is proportional to\ndiffusion coefficient of the gas and surface area and inversely\nproportional to thickness of the barrier. DLCO is measured with\ncarbon monoxide (i.e., DLCO).\n\nDLCO increases during exercise because there are more\nopen capillaries and thus more surface area for diffusion.\nDLCO decreases in emphysema (because of decreased\nsurface area) and in fibrosis and pulmonary edema (because\nof increased diffusion distance).\nHigh DLCO and Low DLCO\n\n![Diffusion and Diffusion Capacity - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/diffusion-and-diffusion-capacity/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Diffusion and Diffusion Capacity — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
bc906e3e-78b2-40b2-ae89-2de6473aeb8d	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	lymphocytes	Lymphocytes	article	Types of lymphocytes\n\n![Lymphocytes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/lymphocytes/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Lymphocytes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
75cdbee8-c399-4ef8-b7d6-8dada153803f	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed	Congenital Heart Disease in the ED	article	# Congenital Heart Disease in the ED\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\ne o\n\n## Key Clinical Features\ncentral cyanosis, hypoxia not improved by 02 CHD TAPVD Clinical presentation & management pulmonary veins do not drain into the left atrium patient may presents with PO,Pul-hypertension & cyanosis at birth CXR: imaging are ECHO, MRI management: surgical repair Truncus arteriosus single artery function as both aorta and Pul-A TGA Tetralogy of Fallot Tricuspid atresia\n- CXR signs wide pulse pressure, single heart sound. M: surgical repair Septation failure, Aorta-Rv and PA-LV.CXR: Egg on the string M:Prostaglandin infusion &balloon atrial septostomy to create an ASD TF 4 Lesion VSD, OV-aorta, RV-hypertrophy, PS. CXR ;b00t shaped Heart, M: cyanotic at birth then IV prostaglandin most have elective surgical repair at 6-9 months Lack of tricuspid valve , underdeveloped or small RV Harsh systolic murmur and large p-wave on ECG M: Definitive management is to establish Fontan circulation by 3-5 years of ag Time of presentation Birth-1st-2nd weeks However TGA present in the neonate period and TF can survive to adulthood after surgery\n- Boot-shaped heart:(tetralogy of fallout) upturned apex due to ventricular hypertrophy and a concave pulmonary artery\n\n- Snowman sign: (TAPVD): the shadow at the right hila is the prominent SVC. The head of snowman made of the innominate vessels and vertical vein. The body of snowman made of the heart.\n- Egg on the string: (TGA) heart appears globular due to convex RA border and LA enlargement.\n- CHD lesion presenting as Shock\n- Shock (poor feeding, lethargy, tachycardia, pallor/pale grey skin, weak peripheral pulse, increase capillary refill, hypotension, decrease BP to lower extremities).\n- Coarctation of the aorta (severe)\n\nSignificant narrowing in the thoracic aorta distal to left subclavian artery.\n- femoral pulse or radio-femoral delay. 4 limb BP discrepancies — gradient of\n- IOmmHg between upper and lower limbs is clinically significant and cyanosis\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG, LVH, and CXR may show 3 signs. All patient should have an MRI or CT for evaluation of the thoracic aorta. Usually diagnosed antenatally via USS scan\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n;-prostaglandin infusion, diuretic, surgery usually within 24hrs.\n- Hypoplastic left heart syndrome The lesion causes severe mitral and aortic valve stenosis or atresia\n- It is usually diagnosed prenatally.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\nAbsent femoral and brachial pulses, delayed CRT, hypotension secondary to shock and PDA murmur can be found.\n- Signs of right heart failure — enlarged liver, tachypnoea, rales.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nECG absent LV force, CXR: Cardiomegaly\n- ECHO is the gold standard\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nprostaglandin infusion, surgery 3-5 days\n- Congestive heart failure-the breathless baby_\n\n- Symptoms: Feeding difficulty sweating/tachypnoea with feeds, failure to thrive, respiratory distress, and fussiness.\n- Sign;_Tachypnoea and laboured breathing rales, hepatomegaly, cyanosis if severe faltering growth, displaced apex beat, cool peripheries, gallop rhythm.\n- VSD\n- Usually asymptomatic\n- May have a pansystolic-murmur-\n- ECG reveal Bi-ventricular hypertrophy by 2 months\n- CXR may show pulmonary vascular marking and cardiomegaly\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nzloop diuretics\n- Surgical intervention for perimembranous VSD\n- ASD\n- There is an opening in the septum between the two atria\n- Isolated ASD rarely causes any symptoms in infants. CCF may develop at 2-3 years\n- Soft-systolic-murmur\n- CXR: cardiomegaly\n- ECG: RVH\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nzmedically if CCF developed.\n- CCF can develop once pulmonary vascular resistance falls in the first few months of life\n- Machinery murmur (continuous systolic murmur)\n- Mostly PDAs close with NSAIDs (ibuprofen, indomethacin)\n\n-\n- Eisenmenger-syndrome Mneumonic for cyanotic CHD The 5 T's:\n- I. Truncus arteriosus (I vessel)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nTransposition (2 switched vessels)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nTricuspid atresia (3 = Tri)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nTetralogy of Fallot (4 = Tetra)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nTAPVR (5 letters in the name) previous\n- Key_points\n- There is a right-left shunt in cyanotic CHD\n- There is a left to right shunt in congestive heart failure CHD (VSD, ASD, PDA). Large VSD will begin manifesting symptoms of CCF at the age ofl month. Uncorrected left-to-right shunt (VSD, ASD, PDA) leads to an increase in pulmonary blood flow pathological remodeling of vasculature Pulmonary arterial hypertension. RVH occurs to compensate\n- Shunt becomes right to left the condition knows Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_02_p2.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_03_p2.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_04_p3.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_05_p3.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_06_p4.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Congenital Heart Disease in the ED - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/congenital-heart-disease-in-the-ed/figure_07_p6.jpeg)\n*Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4656c9c3-d51b-447f-9a1e-0b411487bfbd	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-cardiology-congenital-heart-disease	Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Congenital Heart Disease in the ED — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 10-day-old newborn infant is brought to the ED in severe cardiogenic shock: grey, mottled, lethargic, with weak femoral pulses and profound metabolic acidosis. The ductus arteriosus is suspected of closing. What life-saving intravenous infusion must be initiated immediately?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Congenital Heart Disease in the ED)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/congenital-heart-disease/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Prostaglandin E1 (Alprostadil / Dinoprostone) infusion\n- **B.** Indomethacin infusion\n- **C.** Ibuprofen IV\n- **D.** High-dose furosemide\n- **E.** Sodium polystyrene sulfonate\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn duct-dependent congenital cardiac lesions (e.g. Coarctation of the aorta, Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome, Transposition of the Great Arteries), closure of the ductus arteriosus at 7-14 days causes sudden cardiovascular collapse. Prostaglandin E1 maintains or reopens ductal patency, restoring systemic or pulmonary blood flow.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich of the following is a classic side effect of intravenous Prostaglandin E1 (Alprostadil) infusion in neonates that requires the resuscitation team to be prepared for immediate endotracheal intubation?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Congenital Heart Disease in the ED)](/lessons/images/sba/cardiology/congenital-heart-disease/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Apnoea\n- **B.** Hypertensive crisis\n- **C.** Severe hypernatraemia\n- **D.** Aplastic anaemia\n- **E.** Severe polycythaemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nApnoea is a very common and well-documented adverse effect of Prostaglandin E1 infusion (occurring in 10-15% of neonates, particularly in the first hour of infusion). Clinicians must always be prepared to secure the airway and intubate.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 9-month-old infant with known unrepaired Tetralogy of Fallot suddenly becomes irritable, intensely cyanosed, and hyperpnoeic during feeding (a 'Tet spell'). What initial physical manoeuvre helps abort this hypercyanotic crisis?\n\n- **A.** Knee-to-chest position (or squatting)\n- **B.** Trendelenburg position\n- **C.** Valsalva manoeuvre with cold water\n- **D.** Rapid vertical suspension\n- **E.** Prone position with neck hyperextended\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn a 'Tet spell' (hypercyanotic episode), infundibular spasm increases right-to-left shunting through the VSD. Bringing the infant's knees to the chest (or squatting in older children) kinks the femoral arteries, acutely increasing systemic vascular resistance (SVR), which forces blood from the RV into the pulmonary circulation rather than across the VSD.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat pharmacological therapy reduces infundibular spasm and calms agitation during a severe paediatric Tetralogy of Fallot hypercyanotic spell?\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Morphine and IV Propranolol (beta-blocker)\n- **B.** IV Adrenaline bolus\n- **C.** IV Digoxin loading\n- **D.** Inhaled nitric oxide monotherapy\n- **E.** Oral Sildenafil alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTreatment of a Tet spell includes: (1) Knee-to-chest position; (2) High-flow oxygen; (3) Morphine (calms agitation and reduces systemic venous return); (4) IV fluids bolus (expands circulating volume); (5) IV Propranolol or esmolol (relaxes infundibular spasm); (6) Phenylephrine (increases SVR).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat four cardiac anomalies compose the classic anatomical tetrad of Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)?\n\n- **A.** Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD), Pulmonary infundibular stenosis, Overriding aorta, and Right Ventricular Hypertrophy (RVH)\n- **B.** Atrial Septal Defect, Mitral stenosis, Tricuspid regurgitation, and LVH\n- **C.** Coarctation of the aorta, Bicuspid aortic valve, VSD, and PDA\n- **D.** Transposition of great vessels, ASD, VSD, and pulmonic atresia\n- **E.** Dextrocardia, Situs inversus, Bronchiectasis, and Sinusitis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTetralogy of Fallot consists of: (1) Ventricular Septal Defect; (2) Pulmonary outflow tract obstruction / infundibular stenosis; (3) Overriding aorta; (4) Right Ventricular Hypertrophy resulting from the high pressure in the RV.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
72a49a57-36c5-4899-8c0b-ebe829fcf938	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	gas-exchange	Gas Exchange	article	## Gas Exchange\n\nDalton law of partial pressures\nDaltons law of partial pressure can be expressed by the\nfollowing equation:\nPartial pressure = Total pressure x Fractional gas\nconcentration\nIn dry inspired air, the partial pressure of O2 can be\ncalculated as follows. Assume that total pressure is\natmospheric (760) and the fractional concentration of O2 is\n0.21.\nPo2 760 mm Hg 0.21 =160 mm Hg\nIn humidified tracheal air at 37°C, the calculation is modified\nto correct for the partial pressure of H ₂ O, which is 47 mm Hg.\nP.total=760 mm Hg-47 mm Hg =713 mm Hg\nPo2= 713 mm Hg x 0.21 =150 mm Hg\nPartial pressure of 02 and CO2 in\ninspired air, alveolar air and blood\nApproximately 2% of the systemic cardiac output bypasses\nthe pulmonary circulation ("physiologic shunt"). The resulting\nadmixture of mixed venous blood with oxygen arterial blood\nmakes the Po2 of arterial blood slightly lower than that of\nalveolar air.\nDissolved gases\nHenry law: states that the amount of dissolved gas in a liquid\n(such as blood) is proportional to its partial pressure above the\nliquid.\nSo, breathing gas at higher-than-normal pressures will cause\ntheir bodies to absorb more gases than are absorbed at lower pressures. Absorbed gases can cause decompression\nsickness as well as toxic effects.\nHenry law is applicable in Scuva diving or deep-sea diver.\nThe following calculation uses O2 in arterial blood as an\nexample: Dissolved [O2] =Po2 × Solubility of O2 in blood 100\nmm Hg x 0.003 mL  mL/mm Hg =0.3 mL  mL\nblood\nwhere:\n[02] =02concentration in blood\nPo2partial pressure of O2 in blood\n0.003 mL O2/100 mL/mm Hg= solubility of O2 in blood\nPartial pressure of O2 and Co2 (mm Hg)\n\n![Gas Exchange - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/gas-exchange/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Gas Exchange — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
39ba6236-48e2-46a3-87d2-bc9d47d12006	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	lung-receptors	Lung Receptors	article	Lung receptors:\nIrritant Receptors in the Airways: The epithelium of the\ntrachea, bronchi, and bronchioles is supplied with sensory\nnerve endings called pulmonary irritant receptors that are\nstimulated by many incidents. These receptors initiate\ncoughing and sneezing and cause bronchial constriction in\npeople with such diseases as asthma and emphysema. They\nare rapidly adapting.\nJ-receptors: respond to events such as pulmonary edema,\npulmonary emboli pneumonia, and barotrauma, which cause a\ndecrease in oxygenation and thus lead to an increase in\nventilation/respiration.\nJ receptors are activated in pulmonary edema in a patient\nwith congestive heart failure and causes reflex increase in\nbreathing rate (Rapid shallow breathing) also thought to be\ninvolve in sensation of dyspnea\nLung stretch receptors (Hering-Breuer reflex): Self-regulatory\nnegative feedback reflex.\nLie within the smooth muscle of large and small airways.\nAfferent is carried via vagus nerve.\nStimulus for receptor:\nDistension of the lungs, when TV is more than 1 L\nHering-Breuer inflation reflex: inflation of the lungs tended to\ninhibit further inspiratory muscle activity (dorsal neuron).\nIncrease in duration of expiration.\nHering-Breuer Deflation reflex: Decrease in duration of\nexpiration produced by marked deflation of lung.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
51706d60-ae85-4aba-bc75-704a83f998c5	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	lung-volumes-and-capacities	Lung Volumes and Capacities	article	Lung volume and capacities\nTidal Volume (TV): The volume of air that is inhaled and then\nexhaled with each normal breath.\nNormal tidal volume is 500ml or 7ml/kg.\nInspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV): The maximum volume of air\nthat can be inhaled following and above a normal tidal\ninspiration.\nNormal Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is 3300ml.\nExpiratory Reserve Volume (ERV): The maximum volume of air\nthat can be exhaled from the resting expiration of tidal\nvolume.\nNormal Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is 1700ml.\nResidual Volume (RV): The volume of air that remains in the\nlung following a maximum expiration.\nResidual Volume cannot be measured using spirometry.\nNormal residual volume is 1800ml.\nCapacities\nInspiratory Capacity (IC): is the maximum volume of air that\ncan be inhaled at the end of resting expiration.\nInspiratory capacity is sum of IC= TV+ IRV\nFunctional Residual Capacity (FRC): the volume of air\nremaining in the lungs at the resting end-expiratory level or\nVolume remaining in the lungs after tidal volume is expired in\nair.\nFRC is sum of: FRC= ERV+ RV\nFRC include Residual volume so FRC cannot be measured\nusing spirometry.\nNormal FRC is 3500ml.\nFactors affecting FRC.\n\nFactors increasing FRC: Emphysema, Air trapping in asthma,\nAgeing (due to loss of elastic properties) and Increasing\nheight of patient.\nFactors decreasing FRC: Restrictive ventilatory defects e.g.,\npulmonary fibrosis, Posture - lying supine, Increased intra-\nabdominal pressure (e.g., obesity, pregnancy, ascites) and\nReduced muscle tone of diaphragm e.g., muscle relaxants in\nanesthesia, neuromuscular disease.\nVital Capacity (VC) is the maximum volume of air that may be exhaled\nfollowing a maximum inspiration OR inhaled following a maximum\nexpiration.\n\n## Vc= Irv+Tv+ Erv Or Vc= Ic+Erv\n\nNormal VC in young adult (70kg) is 5500ml.\nTotal Lung Capacity (TLC) is the volume of air contained within\nthe lungs following a maximum inspiration.\nTLC include Residual volume so it cannot be measured\nusing spirometry.\nTLC= IRV + TV+ ERV + RV OR TLC= IC + FRC Or TLC = VC+ RV\nNormal TLC in young adult (70kg) is 7300ml.\nForced expiratory volume (FEV1)\nFEV1 is the volume of air that can be expired in the first\nsecond of a forced maximal expiration.\nFEV1 is normally 80% of the forced vital capacity, which is\nexpressed as\n\n## Fev1/ Fvc = 0.8\n\nIn obstructive lung disease such as asthma and chronic\nobstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), both FEV1 and FVC\nare reduced, but FEV1 is reduced more than FVC. FEV1/FVC\nratio is less than 0.7.\nIn restrictive lung disease such as fibrosis, both FEV1 and\nFVC are reduced, but FEV1 is reduced less than FVC is thus,\nFev1 /FVC increased.\nLung volume measurements and\nPeak Expiratory flow rate:\nLung volume can be measured with Spirometry.\nResidual volume cannot be measured using spirometry.\nResidual volume and thus (FRC and TLC) can be measured\nusing helium dilution.\nPeak Expiratory flow rate:\n\nThe easiest and quickest measurement is the peak expiratory\nflow rate (PEFR)\nAirway resistance and lung compliance can be assessed\nindirectly by measuring the forced expiratory flows and\nvolumes.\nLung volumes can be measured with spirometry.\nPEFR is reduced if airway resistance is increased in\nobstructive disease and is commonly used to monitor\nasthma.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
0a6cc004-33e4-452b-8644-a942c27f915d	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	oxygen-haemoglobin-dissociation-curve	Oxygen Haemoglobin Dissociation Curve	article	Oxygen-Haemoglobin\nDissociation Curve\nThe Haemoglobin oxygen Dissociation Curve is a plot of\npercent saturation of haemoglobin as a function of po2.\nChanges in the hemoglobin-02\ndissociation curve\n\n### 1. Shifts to the right:\n\noccur when the affinity of hemoglobin for O2 is decreased.\nThe P50 is increased, and unloading of O2 from arterial blood\nto the tissues is facilitated.\nCauses of Right ward shift\nDecreased affinity for oxygen (More willing to give up oxygen)\nDecreased pH (Recall)\nIncreased pCO2\nIncreased H+\nIncreased Temperature\nIncreased 2-3DPG.\nPregnancy (oxygen to the fetus)\n\n### 2. Shifts to the left:\n\noccur when the affinity of hemoglobin for 02 is increased.\nThe P50 is decreased, and unloading of O2 from arterial\nblood into the tissues is more difficult.\nCauses of left ward shift\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up oxygen)\nIncreased pH\nDecreased pCO2.\nDecreased temperature.\nDecreased H+\nDecreased 2-3DPG.\nCO poisoning\n\nMethemoglobinemia\nFetal hemoglobin\nNote:\nFetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult\nhemoglobin. Maternal and Fetal blood never mix during\npregnancy, but they come close to each other in the\nplacenta.  Oxygen diffuses easily to fetal hemoglobin here.\nThe reason fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for 02. It is\ncomposed of two alpha and two gamma subunits, while\nadult hemoglobin is composed of two alpha and two beta\nsubunits.\n2,3DPG present in RBCs enhances the ability of RBCs to\nrelease o2 near tissue and 2,3DPG only binds with beta\nglobulin chains, fetal hemoglobin (HbF) lack of beta\nglobulin chains that's why fetal hemoglobin has higher\naffinity for O2	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
eb195deb-aec5-450f-be1d-c7cc1a0c06f8	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	resistance-of-the-airways	Resistance of the Airways	article	Resistance of the airways\nis described by Poiseuille law, as shown in the following\nequation: R= 8 n l/ r4 where:\nR= resistance\nn=viscosity of the inspired gas\nl-length of the airway r-radius of the airway\nNote the powerful inverse fourth-power relationship\nbetween resistance and the size (radius) of the airway.\nFor example, if airway radius decreases by a factor of 4, then\nresistance will increase by a factor of 256 and airflow will\ndecrease by a factor of 256.\nFactors that change airway resistance\nThe major site of airway resistance is the medium-sized\nbronchi.\nThe smallest airways would seem to offer the highest\nresistance, but they do not because of their parallel\narrangement.\nContraction or relaxation of bronchial\nsmooth muscle:  changes airway resistance\nby altering the radius of the airways.\nParasympathetic stimulation, irritants, and the slow-reacting\nsubstance of anaphylaxis (asthma) constrict the airways,\ndecrease the radius, and increase the resistance to airflow.\nSympathetic stimulation and sympathetic agonists\n(isoproterenol) dilate the airway via beta2 receptors, increase\nthe radius, and decrease the resistance to airflow.	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
ffa4a90e-486b-48ef-b61a-46ceb4d21363	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	surface-tention-and-surfactant	Surface Tention and Surfactant	article	Surface tension and surfactant\nSurface tension of the alveoli:\nSurface tension of the alveoli results from the attractive forces\nbetween liquid molecules lining the alveoli at the air-liquid\ninterface.\nCreates a collapsing pressure that is directly proportional to\nsurface tension and inversely proportional to alveolar radius\n(Laplace law), as shown in the following equation: P 2T/r\nwhere:\nP-collapsing pressure on alveolus (or pressure required to\nkeep alveolus open) [dynes/cm2)\nT-surface tension (dynes/cm)\nr-radius of alveolus (cm)\nNote:\nLarge alveoli (large radii) have low collapsing pressures and\nare easy to keep open.\nSmall alveoli (small radii) have high collapsing pressures and\nare more difficult to keep open.\nIn the absence of surfactant, the small alveoli have a\ntendency to collapse (atelectasis).\nSurfactant\nlines the alveoli. reduces surface tension by disrupting the\nintermolecular forces between liquid molecules. This\nreduction in surface tension prevents small alveoli from\ncollapsing and increases compliance.\nsynthesized by type II alveolar cells and consists primarily\nof the phospholipid dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine (DPPC).\nNote: Type 1 alveolar cells comprise the major gas exchange\nsurface of the alveolus\nNeonatal respiratory distress syndrome: can occur in\npremature infants because of the lack of surfactant. The infant exhibits atelectasis (lungs collapse), difficulty reinflating the\nlungs (as a result of decreased compliance), decreased V/Q and\nright-to-left shunt, and hypoxemia (as a result of decreased V/Q\nand right-to-left shunt).	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
347d041c-4807-4ae3-bf73-d8f170d61201	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	transport-of-gases-in-circulation	Transport of Gases in Circulation	article	Transport of Gases in Circulation:\nTransport of CO2:\nLarge amount of CO2 is continuously produced in the body.\nUnder normal resting conditions each 100 ml of deoxygenated\nblood contains 4 ml of CO, which is carried in the blood in three\nforms:\nBicarbonate ions: 60% of CO2 is transported in bicarbonate\nform.\nCarbamino compounds: 30% combines with the globin part\nof haemoglobin to form carbamino haemoglobin\nDissolved C02: 10% is dissolved in plasma.\nTransport of oxygen:\nOxygen is transported from alveoli to tissues by two forms.\nDissolved in plasma by simple diffusion. The amount of oxygen\ntransport in this way is very negligible. It is only 0.3ml/100ml of\nplasma.\nIn combination with hemoglobin: Combines with HB and is\ntransported as Oxyhaemoglobin\nEach molecule of haemoglobin can bind up to four\nmolecules of oxygen.\nWhen the oxygen molecule binds, there is a conformational\nchange in its protein structure and its affinity for oxygen\nincreases, making it easier to bind the next oxygen molecule.\nThe actual amount of oxygen bound to haemoglobin\ndepends on the PO2.\nLow PO2 in tissue capillaries promotes oxygen release from\nhaemoglobin\nHigh PO2 in pulmonary capillaries promotes oxygen binding.\nEach gram of haemoglobin binds with up to 1.34 mL oxygen,\nso with a haemoglobin concentration of 140 g/L, blood\ncontains a maximum of 187 mL/L	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
5fd1fe1b-1740-4f9a-b894-5d0ad7765b44	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	ventilation-perfusion-mismatch	Ventilation Perfusion Mismatch	article	Ventilation- Perfusion mismatch\nThe ratio of alveolar ventilation to pulmonary blood flow is V/Q\nratio.\nFor the lung at rest, it is about 0.8.\nVentilation-Perfusion at base and apex of lung:\nBoth ventilation and perfusion are highest towards the lung\nbase because of gravitational effects and low at the apex of\nlung\nGravitational effects are greater on perfusion than ventilation,\nso V/Q ratio is high at apex and low at base\nAbnormal V/Q:\nVentilation perfusion mismatch (V/Q) most common cause of\nHypoxemia\n\n### 1: Ventilation defect: Alveoli perfused but not ventilated\n\nProduce intrapulmonary shunting. In large ventilation defect,\nV/Q is zero, which is called a shunt.\nThere is no gas exchange, increased A-a gradient.\nPartial pressure of pulmonary CO2 increases while that of\noxygen decreases because normal amount of oxygen for\nventilation is not available.\nV/Q ratio zero-pulmonary shunt: No ventilation but\nperfusion remains normal Causes: ARDS, Compression\natelectasis due to hydrothorax or pneumothorax.\nV/Q ratio <1: Low ventilation relative to its perfusion such as\nin partial obstruction of airways. Causes: Low compliance\ne.g., pulmonary fibrosis, High airway resistance e.g., COPD,\nasthma\n\n### 2: Perfusion defect (Ventilation normal but perfusion\n\ndecreased)\nIf blood flow to a lung is completely blocked (e.g., by an\nembolism occluding a pulmonary artery), then blood flow to that lung is zero. If ventilation is normal, then V/Q is infinite,\nwhich is called dead space.\nPartial pressure of pulmonary CO2 decreases while that of\noxygen increases.\nThere is no gas exchange. ↑A-a gradient. As a result, partial\npressure of oxygen and CO2 equal to inspired air.\nV/Q ratio of infinity-dead space:\nNo perfusion but ventilation remains normal.\nCauses: Embolism\nAnatomic and physiologic\nshunt\nThe shunted blood will not have been oxygenated or been\nable to offload CO2 and thus its levels of PO2 and PCO2 are\nthat of mixed venous blood. Changes in PCO2 and\nPO2 stimulate the chemoreceptors and increase ventilation,\nso that arterial PCO2 returns to normal.\nHowever, increased ventilation cannot increase blood\nO2 content, as the haemoglobin of the blood passing\nthrough the lungs is already close to saturation. Thus, right to\nleft shunts commonly result in a low arterial PO2 but a\nnormal or low PCO2.\n\n![Ventilation Perfusion Mismatch - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-physiology-ventilation/ventilation-perfusion-mismatch/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Ventilation Perfusion Mismatch — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
84d20106-584d-4f78-8c78-2d58919ae615	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	aldosterone-adh-and-anp	Aldosterone, ADH and ANP	article	Aldosterone\nAldosterone release is stimulated by\nACTH (It is less important as regulator, so pituitary failure\ndoes not severely impair the aldosterone secretion.\nHigh serum [K]\nAngiotensin II (2° to fall in blood pressure/volume, [Na]\nFunctions\nNa reabsorption and K secretion in DCT\nUltimately causes rise in blood pressure, hypokalemia and\nincrease PH level.\nAntidiuretic hormone\nVasopressin or antidiuretic hormone (ADH) or\narginine vasopressin (AVP) is a non-peptide, Synthesized in the\nhypothalamus. Osmoreceptors in the anterior hypothalamus\ncontrol the water intake by altering thirst and control the renal\nwater excretion by altering the ADH release.\nSynthesized in the hypothalamus and transported to\nposterior pituitary within nerve fibers.\nRapidly removed from plasma decreasing to 50% in\n10minutes due to metabolism in liver and kidney.\nADH release is stimulated by.\nHigh plasma osmolarity detected by Osmoreceptors in\nanterior hypothalamus.\nECF volume depletion\nAngiotensin II\nNausea, stress, exercise, pain\nHypoglycemia\nIt increases the synthesis of transport mechanism in DCT\nincluding Na pump, Na-H antiporter; and Na, K channels in\nprincipal cells and H- ATPase in intercalated cells. Thereby\nenhancing the Na and H2O reabsorption and H, K secretion.\n\nADH release is inhibited by\nHypoosmolality\nAlcohol\nCaffeine\nGlucocorticoid\nAtrial natriuretic peptide\nFunctions\nADH binds to V2 receptors present on principal cells of late\nDCT and collecting duct thereby increasing the reabsorption\nof H2O from these segments (which otherwise are\nimpermeable to H2O when there is no ADH)\nADH binds V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscles causing\nvasoconstriction.\nAlso increases NaCl reabsorption from thick ascending limb\nof LOH.\nAtrial natriuretic peptide\nReleased from cardiac atrial muscle cells in response to atrial\nstretch resulting from increased intravascular volume.\nAlso produced in collecting duct cells\nFunctions\nSuppress the production of renin, ADH, aldosterone.\nCauses renal vasodilation thereby increasing the GFR.\nInhibits Na reabsorption in distal nephron by inhibiting the\nENaC in principal cells.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
487589a2-860e-44b5-a276-7dbba12fb226	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	acid-base-balance	Acid Base Balance	article	Acid base balance\nAcid production\nTwo types of acids are produced in the body: volatile acid and\nnon-volatile acids.\nVolatile acid\nIs CO2\nProduced from aerobic metabolism of cells.\nCO2 combines with H2O to form weak acid H2CO3, which\ndissociates into H and HCO3.\nCarbonic anhydrase is present in most cells, catalyzes the\nreversible reaction between CO2 and H2O\nNon-volatile acids\nAre also called fixed acids.\nInclude sulphuric acid and phosphoric acid.\nAre normally produced at a rate of 40-60mmoles/day.\nOther fixed acids include ketoacids, lactic acid, and salicylic\nacid.\nBuffers\nPrevent a change in PH when H ions are added to or\nremoved from a solution.\nExtracellular buffers\nThe major EC buffer is HCO3, which is produced from CO2\nand H2O\nPhosphate is a minor EC buffer, PO4 is most important as\nurinary buffer; excretion of H as H2PO4 is called titratable\nacid.\nIntracellular buffers\nOrganic phosphates e.g., AMP, ADP, ATP, 2,3-\ndisphosphoglycerate (DPG)\nHemoglobin is a major IC buffer.\n\nIn the physiologic PH range deoxyhemoglobin is a better\nbuffer than oxyhemoglobin.\nRenal acid base\nReabsorption of HCO3-\nReabsorption of filtered HCO3- occurs primarily in the\nproximal tubule.\nH+ and HCO3- are produced in the proximal tubular cells\nfrom CO2 and H2O. CO2 and H2O combine to form H2CO3,\ncatalyzed by IC carbonic anhydrase; H2CO3 dissociates into\nH+ and HCO3. H+ is secreted into the lumen via Na-H\nexchange mechanism in the luminal membrane. The HCO3 is\nreabsorbed.\nIn the lumen, the secreted H+ combines with filtered HCO3-\nto form H2CO3, which dissociates into CO2 and H2O,\ncatalyzed by brush border carbonic anhydrase. CO2 and H2O\ndiffuse into the cell to start cycle again.\nThe process results in net reabsorption of filtered HCO3-.\nHowever, it does not result in net secretion of H+\nRegulation of reabsorption of filtered HCO3\nIncreases in the filtered load of HCO3- result in increased\nrates of HCO3- reabsorption. However, if plasma HCO3 conc\nbecomes very high (metabolic alkalosis), the filtered load will\nexceed the re absorptive capacity and HCO3- will be excreted\nin urine.\nIncrease in Pco² results in increased rates of HCO3-\nreabsorption because the supply of IC H+ for secretion is\nincreased. This mechanism is the basis for renal\ncompensation for respiratory acidosis.\nDecrease in Pco² results in decreased rates of HCO3-\nreabsorption because the supply of IC H+ for secretion is\ndecreased. This mechanism is the basis for the renal\ncompensation for respiratory alkalosis.\nECF volume expansion results in decreased HCO3\nreabsorption.\nECF volume contraction results in increased HCO3\nreabsorption (contraction alkalosis)\nAngiotensin II stimulates Na-H exchange and thus increases\nHCO3- reabsorption, contributing to the contraction alkalosis\nthat occurs secondary to ECF volume contraction.\n\nExcretion of fixed H\nFixed H+ produced from the catabolism of protein and\nphospholipid is excreted by two mechanism, titratable acid\nand NH ⁴\nThe amount of H+ excreted as titratable acid (H2PO4)\ndepends on amount of urinary buffer present (usually HPO4)\nand the pK of buffer.\nH+ and HCO3- are produced in the intercalated cells from\nCO2 and H2O. The H is secreted into the lumen by an H-\nATPase, and the HCO3- is reabsorbed into the blood (new\nHCO3). In the urine secreted H+ combines with filtered HPO4\nto form H2PO4, which is excreted as titratable acid, The H-\nATPase is increased by aldosterone.\nThis process results in net secretion of H+ and net\nreabsorption of newly synthesized HCO3-.\nAs a result of H+ secretion, the PH of urine becomes\nprogressively lower. The minimum urinary PH is 4.4.\nThe amount of excreted as titratable acid is determined by\nthe amount of urinary buffer and pK of buffer.\nExcretion of H as NH ⁴\nThe amount of H excreted as NH ⁴  depends on both amount\nof NH3 synthesized by renal cells and urine PH.\nNH3 is produced in renal cells from glutamine. It diffuses\ndown its concentration gradient from the cells into the\nlumen.\nH and HCO3- are produced from intercalated cells from CO2\nand H2O. The H is secreted into lumen via an H- ATPase and\ncombines with NH3 to form NH4 which is excreted (diffusion\ntrapping). The HCO3 is reabsorbed into the blood (new\n\n## Hco3).\n\nThe lower the PH of tubular fluid, the greater the excretion of\nH as NH4; at low urine PH, there is more NH4 relative to NH3\nin the urine, thus increasing the gradient for NH3 diffusion.\nIn acidosis, an adaptive increase in NH3 synthesis occurs and\naids in the excretion of excess H.\nHyperkalemia inhibits NH3 synthesis, which produces a\ndecrease in H excretion as NH4 (Type 4 renal tubular\nacidosis). For example, Hypoaldosteronism causes\nhyperkalemia and thus also causes type 4 RTA. Conversely, hypokalemia stimulates NH3 synthesis, which produces an\nincrease in H excretion.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
bb55a927-af46-483f-bf7d-c495159405d0	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	acid-base-disorder	Acid Base Disorder	article	Acid base disorders\nAcid-base disorders are pathologic changes in carbon dioxide\npartial pressure (Pco2) or serum bicarbonate (HCO3−) that\ntypically produce abnormal arterial pH values.\nAcidemia is serum pH < 7.35.\nAlkalemia is serum pH > 7.45.\nAcidosis refers to physiologic processes that cause acid\naccumulation or alkali loss.\nAlkalosis refers to physiologic processes that cause alkali\naccumulation or acid loss.\nVentilation controls the CO2 levels and kidney controls the\nHCO3 levels.\nMetabolic acidosis\nOverproduction or ingestion of fixed acid or loss of base\nproduces a decrease in arterial [HCO3]. This decrease is the\nprimary disturbance in metabolic acidosis.\nDecreased HCO3 concentration causes a decrease in blood\nPH (Acidemia)\nAcidemia causes hyperventilation (Kussmaul breathing),\nwhich is respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis.\nCorrection of metabolic acidosis consists of increased\nexcretion of excess fixed H as titratable acid and NH4 and\nincreased reabsorption of new HCO3, which replenishes the\nblood [HCO3]\nIn chronic metabolic acidosis, an adaptive increase in NH3\nsynthesis aids in the excretion of excess H.\nSerum anion gap = ([Na+] + [K+]) - ([Cl-] + [HCO3-])\nSerum anion gap represents the difference between\nmeasured cations and measured anions in plasma. As\nplasma is always electrically neutral, the anion gap\ndetermines the presence of unmeasured anions. These\nusually include proteins, sulphate, phosphate and organic\nacids.\n\nNormal value of serum anion gap is 12meq/L (range 8-\n16meq/L)\nIn metabolic acidosis, serum [HCO3-] decreases. For\nelectroneutrality, the concentration of another anion must\nincrease to replace HCO3-. That anion can be Cl-, or it can be\nan unmeasured anion.\nSerum anion gap is increased if the new acid is added to the\nbody, this dissociates producing new H+ (which uses up\nHCO3-) and anions (which replaces HCO3-)\nSerum anion gap is normal if the concentration of Cl- is\nincreased to replace HCO3+ (hyperchloremic metabolic\nacidosis\nNormal anion gap metabolic acidosis\n\n## Nagma:\n\nA normal anion gap occurs if there is loss of HCO3. This\ncauses a compensatory rise in plasma [Cl], so the anion gap is\nnormal (hyperchloremic acidosis).\nCauses: (Mnemonic: USED-CAR)\nUreteroenteric conduit\nSmall bowel fistula/ Saline administration\nExogenous Acid\nDiarrhea\nCarbonic anhydrase inhibitor\nAdrenal insufficiency\nRenal tubular acidosis\nCauses of high anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA)\n\n## (Mudpiles)\n\nMethanol, metformin\nUremia\nDiabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)\nPropylene glycol\nIsoniazid, iron overdose, infection\nLactic acidosis\nEthanol, ethylene glycol\nSalicylates\nNote: The most common cause of low anion gap\nis hypoalbuminemia\n\nMetabolic alkalosis\nLoss of fixed H+ or gain of base produces an increase in\narterial [HCO3-]. This increase is the primary disturbance in\nmetabolic alkalosis.\nIn vomiting H is lost from stomach, HCO3 remains behind in\nthe blood, and the [HCO3] increases, increased [HCO3] causes\nan increase in blood PH (Alkalemia). Alkalemia causes\nhypoventilation which is the respiratory compensation for\nmetabolic alkalosis. It has features of hypocalcemia\n(decreased unbound plasma Ca) and hypokalemia (because\nof IC shift of K) e.g., tetany, paresthesia, muscle cramps.\nCorrection of metabolic alkalosis consists of increased\nexcretion of HCO3 because the filtered load of HCO3 exceeds\nthe ability of renal tubule to reabsorb it.\nIf metabolic alkalosis is accompanied by ECF volume\ncontraction (vomiting), the reabsorption of HCO3 increases\n(secondary to ECF volume contraction and activation of the\nrenin angiotensin II aldosterone system), worsening the\nmetabolic alkalosis (contraction alkalosis).\nCauses:\nGastrointestinal losses\nVomiting, nasogastric drainage, pyloric stenosis\nSome villous adenomas\nRenal losses\nChloruretic drugs (e.g., thiazides, furosemide)\nMineralocorticoid excess syndromes\nPrimary Hyperaldosteronism\nOther causes:\nAlkali intake or administration\nMilk alkali syndrome\nsevere potassium depletion (serum potassium <2 mmol/L)\nRespiratory acidosis\n\nRespiratory acidosis is carbon dioxide (CO2) accumulation\n(hypercapnia) due to a decrease in respiratory rate and/or\nrespiratory volume (hypoventilation).\nIt is caused by decreased alveolar ventilation\n(Hypoventilation) and retention of CO2.\nIncreased arteriolar Pco², which is primary disturbance,\ncauses an increase in [H] and [HCO3] by mass action.\nThere is no respiratory compensation for respiratory acidosis.\nRenal compensation consists of increased excretion of H as\ntitratable acid and NH4 and increased reabsorption of new\nHCO3. This process is aided by increased Pco², which supplies\nmore H to the renal cells for secretion. The resulting increase\nin serum [HCO3] helps to normalize the PH.\nIn acute respiratory acidosis, renal compensation has not yet\noccurred.\nIn chronic respiratory acidosis, renal compensation has\noccurred. Thus, arterial PH is increased towards normal (i.e\ncompensation)\nCauses\nSedatives\nOpioid overdose\nAirway obstruction, COPD, asthma, pulmonary oedema\nAdult RDS\nNeuromuscular dysfunction including Myasthenia gravis,\nMultiple sclerosis, GBS, polio.\nRespiratory alkalosis\nIt is caused by increased alveolar ventilation\n(Hyperventilation) and loss of CO2.\nDecreased arterial Pco², which is the primary disturbance,\ncauses decrease in [H] and [HCO3] by mass action.\nThere is no respiratory compensation for respiratory.\nMore common than respiratory acidosis, usually acute and\nuncompensated.\nRenal compensation consists of decreased excretion of H as\ntitratable acid and NH4 and decreased reabsorption of new\nHCO3, this process is aided by the decreased Pco2 which\ncauses a deficit of H in the renal cells for secretion. The\nresulting decrease in serum [HCO3] helps to normalize the\nPH.\n\nIn acute respiratory alkalosis, renal compensation has not\nyet occurred.\nIn chronic respiratory alkalosis, renal compensation\n(decreased HCO3 reabsorption has occurred. Thus, arterial PH\nis decreased towards normal (i.e., compensation)\nCauses\nHigh altitude (Hypoxia induced)\nSalicylate intoxication (aspirin overdose)\nSepsis\nStress\nThyrotoxicosis\nPregnancy\nPulmonary disorder\nHyperventilation (Due to heart disorder or other, including\nimproper mechanical ventilation)\nVocal cord paralysis (compensation for loss of vocal volume\nresults in over-breathing/breathlessness).	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
fc41fd90-5eb1-45a0-8def-7e80e1690588	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	autoregulation	Autoregulation	article	Autoregulation\nBlood flow throughout the kidney: renal artery=arcuate\nartery=afferent arteriole=glomerular capillaries=efferent\narterioles=peritubular capillaries=vasa recta=arcuate\nveins=renal vein\nThe kidneys are very effective in auto regulating blood flow.\nThis is primarily due to changes in the resistance of the\nafferent arterioles, for which 2 mechanisms are involved.\nMyogenic responses:\nMR is a function of smooth muscle to contract in response to\nexternal stretching force. In the case of vascular smooth\nmuscle, this causes vasoconstriction on a rise in arterial\npressure, thus allowing for autoregulation.\nTubuloglomerular feedback (TGF)\nIncreased MAP leads to an increase in RBF and GFR\nTGF is a regulating mechanism specific to the kidney that\nleads to vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole in response\nto an increase in the luminal concentration of NaCl at the\nmacula densa in the early distal tubule. Because salt\nreabsorption from the ascending part of the loop of Henle is\nan active and more rate-limited process than the passive\ndiffusion of water out of the descending loop, the\nconcentration of NaCl reaching the macula densa is\ndependent on the rate of tubular flow with larger flow\nresulting in a higher distal tubular concentration. An increase\nin arterial pressure will enhance tubular flow due to\nenhanced glomerular filtration and reduced proximal tubular\nreabsorption. This will raise the NaCl concentration at the\nmacula densa and cause afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction,\nproviding restoration of filtration and autoregulation of RBF.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
e1cc3c70-f9db-452c-8cae-818ef0ee9df6	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	creatinine-clearance	Creatinine Clearance	article	Creatinine Clearance\nClearance refers to a theoretical volume of plasma from\nwhich a substance is removed over a period of time.\nClearance = Urine conc of substance × Urine flow rate\n/plasma concentration of substance\nClearance of substance can provide an accurate estimate of\nGFR when the substance is.\nFreely filtered.\nNot reabsorbed in nephron\nNot secreted in nephron\nNeither synthesized nor metabolized by kidney\nInulin is such substance but not used in routine clinical\npractice due to complicated clearance.\nCreatinine which is steadily released from skeletal muscle is\npreferred clinical method for determining the relatively\naccurate approximation of GFR (except when plasma\ncreatinine or GFR is abnormally low) because it is\nendogenously produced, not necessary to infuse it to get a\nclearance measurement as has to be done to measure inulin\nclearance and because it is freely filtered not reabsorbed and\nonly a small amount of it is secreted, creatinine clearance\nparallels inulin clearance and is independent of production rate\n(excretion rises as plasma concentration increases	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
033a121f-45ac-46c1-a0d2-0bf230f8a00f	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	glomerular-filtration-rate	Glomerular Filtration Rate	article	Glomerular Filtration rate\nGlomerular filtration rate is the amount of glomerular filtrate\nformed in all the of both the kidneys per minute. In a healthy\nindividual, it is about 125 mL/minute.\nGFR is approximately 125ml per min (180 L per day).\nThe RPF is approximately 600 to 720 ml per minute.\nFiltration fraction is = GFR/RPF; therefore, the proportion of\nplasma that is filtered each minute is about 20%\nFactors determining net filtration pressure.\na) Hydrostatic pressure of glomerular capillaries (PGC)\nThe only force that promotes filtration.\nMain factor that determines the GFR\nNormal value 45mmhg\nb) Oncotic pressure of the plasma ( π GC)\nVaries with the concentration of plasma proteins.\nOpposes the filtration.\nIncreases from the beginning to the end of glomerular.\nNormal value = 24mmhg\nc) Hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's space (PBS)\nOpposes filtration.\nNormally low and constant does not affect filtration.\nThis pressure increases whenever there is an obstruction\ndownstream (blocked ureter /urethra) and reduces filtration.\nNormal value = 8mmhg\nd) oncotic pressure in bowman's space ( π BS)\nConsidered zero because of very little or no proteins in\nbowman's space.\nNet filtration pressure = PGC- π GC - PBS\n= 45 - 24 - 8 = 13mmhg\n\nFiltration at the glomeruli depends\nupon starling forces.\nGlomerular capillaries have very high hydrostatic pressure;\nthis is why filtration occurs.\nIncreasing glomerular hydrostatic pressure increases GFR,\nand decreasing it decreases the GFR\nOncotic pressure opposes GFR, increased plasma proteins\nincrease the oncotic pressure and decrease the GFR and vice\nversa.\nMain force driving the reabsorption at proximal tubule is the\noncotic pressure in peritubular capillaries. Increased\nconcentration of proteins is carried into peritubular\ncapillaries which promotes a greater net force of\nreabsorption.\nHigh pressure in the glomerular capillary forces the filtrate\nthrough filtration barrier, this pressure is reduced by afferent\narteriolar constriction and increased by efferent arteriolar\nconstriction.\nRenal disease, circulating and local vasoconstrictors,\nsympathetic stimulation all reduce GFR, however angiotensin\nII preferentially constricts efferent arterioles therefore\nincreases GFR.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
c1d53bcd-1cd8-4019-a375-c3b1e3ef4d82	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	renal-regulation-of-urea-phosphate-calcium-and-magnessium	Renal Regulation of Urea, Phosphate, Calcium and Magnessium	article	Renal regulation of urea,\nphosphate, calcium and\nmagnesium\nUrea\nUrea is reabsorbed and secreted in nephron by diffusion,\nsimple of facilitated, depending on segment of nephron.\n50% of filtered urea is reabsorbed in the early PCT by simple\ndiffusion.\nUrea is secreted in thin descending limb of LOH by simple\ndiffusion (from high conc of urea in medullary interstitial\nfluid)\nThe distal tubule, cortical collecting ducts and outer\nmedullary collecting ducts are impermeable to urea; thus,\nno urea is reabsorbed by these segments.\nADH stimulates a facilitated diffusion transport of urea (UT1)\nin medullary collecting ducts. Urea reabsorption from inner\nmedullary collecting ducts contributes to urea recycling in\nthe inner medulla and to addition of urea to the\ncorticopapillary osmotic gradient.\nUrea excretion varies with urine flow rate. At high levels of\nwater (low urine flow rate), there is greater reabsorption and\ndecreased urea excretion. At low levels of water reabsorption\n(high urine flow rate) there is less urea reabsorption and\nincreased urea excretion.\nPhosphate\n85% of filtered PO4 is reabsorbed in the early PCT by Na-PO4\ncotransport. Because the distal segment of the nephron does\nnot reabsorb PO4, 15% of filtered load is excreted in urine.\nPTH inhibits PO4 reabsorption in the early PCT by inhibiting\nNa-PO4 cotransport. Therefore, PTH causes phosphaturia and\nincreased urinary cAMP.\nPO4 is a urinary buffer for H+; excretion of H2PO4 is called\ntitratable acid.\n\nCalcium\n60% of plasma Ca is filtered across the glomerular capillaries.\nTogether the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb\nreabsorb more than 90% of filtered Ca by passive processes\nthat are coupled to Na reabsorption.\nLoop diuretics (furosemide) cause increased urinary calcium\nexcretion (hypercalciuria). Because Ca reabsorption is linked\nto Na reabsorption in LOH, inhibiting Na reabsorption with a\nloop diuretic also inhibits Ca reabsorption. If volume is\nreplaced, loop diuretics can be used in the treatment of\nhypercalcemia.\nTogether, distal tubule and collecting duct reabsorbs 8% of\nfiltered Ca by an active process.\nPTH increases Ca reabsorption by activating adenylate\ncyclase in the distal tubule.\nThiazide diuretics increase Ca reabsorption in the early distal\ntubule and therefore decrease Ca excretion. For this reason,\nthiazides are used in the treatment of idiopathic\nhypercalciuria.\nMagnesium\nIt is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, thick ascending limb\nof LOH, distal tubule.\nIn thick ascending limb, Mg and Ca compete for\nreabsorption; therefore, hypercalcemia causes an increase in\nMg excretion (by inhibiting Mg reabsorption). Likewise,\nhypermagnesemia causes an increase in Ca excretion (by\ninhibiting Ca reabsorption)	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
28b1f1b3-2b00-4a75-beb4-97d12e2dbf4d	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	plasma-autoantibodies	Plasma Autoantibodies	article	Plasma autoantibodies\n\n![Plasma Autoantibodies - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/plasma-autoantibodies/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Plasma Autoantibodies — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
5d31dcad-797c-49d7-a654-757ec8917f67	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-asthma-children	Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 4-year-old boy presents to the paediatric ED with acute breathlessness and audible wheeze. He is agitated, breathless to talk or feed, respiratory rate is 45 breaths/min, heart rate is 145 bpm, and he has marked intercostal and subcostal retractions. SpO2 is 91% in room air. How is this acute attack categorized under BTS/SIGN paediatric guidelines?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma-children/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Severe Asthma\n- **B.** Moderate asthma\n- **C.** Mild episodic viral wheeze\n- **D.** Croup\n- **E.** Life-threatening asthma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS/SIGN criteria for Acute Severe Asthma in children aged 2-5: SpO2 <92%, too breathless to talk or feed, RR >40/min, HR >130 bpm, or use of accessory neck muscles. Life-threatening features in this age group include silent chest, cyanosis, poor respiratory effort, hypotension, exhaustion, or confusion.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the preferred route and delivery device for administering inhaled Salbutamol to a 3-year-old child with mild-to-moderate acute wheeze?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma-children/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Pressurised Metered-Dose Inhaler (pMDI) with an appropriately sized spacer and mask (giving 2-10 puffs, 1 puff every 30-60 seconds with 5 breaths per puff)\n- **B.** Dry powder inhaler (turbohaler)\n- **C.** Jet nebuliser driven by compressed air is mandatory for all children\n- **D.** Oral salbutamol syrup\n- **E.** Subcutaneous adrenaline\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNumerous clinical trials demonstrate that pMDI plus spacer is as effective as (and often superior to) a nebuliser for delivering bronchodilators in mild-to-moderate paediatric asthma, producing fewer side effects (less tachycardia) and causing less distress to the child.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended oral steroid and dose for an acute severe asthma exacerbation in a 4-year-old child?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma-children/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oral Prednisolone 20mg once daily (or 1-2 mg/kg, max 40mg) for 3 days\n- **B.** Oral Dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg for 14 days\n- **C.** Oral Hydrocortisone 5mg once weekly\n- **D.** Inhaled budesonide alone without oral steroids\n- **E.** Systemic steroids are contraindicated in children under 5\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS/SIGN recommends early administration of oral prednisolone (20 mg for children aged 2-5 years; 30-40 mg for children >5 years, or 1-2 mg/kg up to max 40 mg) for 3 days. Soluble prednisolone tablets or oral dexamethasone (0.3-0.6 mg/kg single dose) are effective.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 5-year-old girl with acute severe asthma has not responded to frequent inhaled bronchodilators and oral steroids. Inhaled oxygen SpO2 is 89%, and she is becoming exhausted. What is the intravenous bronchodilator of choice for refractory severe paediatric asthma in the ED?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/asthma-children/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Magnesium Sulphate (40 mg/kg, max 2g, infused over 20 minutes)\n- **B.** Intravenous Salbutamol bolus 5mg\n- **C.** Intravenous Aminophylline without monitoring\n- **D.** Intravenous Morphine\n- **E.** Intravenous Furosemide\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBTS/SIGN guidelines recommend IV Magnesium Sulphate (40 mg/kg, max 2 g, over 20 min) as first-line intravenous therapy for children with acute severe asthma unresponsive to initial inhaled bronchodilators. IV Salbutamol (infusion or slow bolus) and IV Aminophylline are second-line add-ons.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nIn pre-school children (<3 years), what is the most common clinical phenotype of recurrent wheezing triggered exclusively by viral upper respiratory tract infections with no interval symptoms?\n\n- **A.** Viral-Induced Episodic (intermittent) Wheeze\n- **B.** Multiple-trigger wheeze / Atopic asthma\n- **C.** Cystic fibrosis\n- **D.** Foreign body aspiration\n- **E.** Tracheomalacia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPre-school wheezing is broadly classified into: (1) Episodic (viral) wheeze: child wheezes only during acute viral colds and is completely symptom-free between episodes; and (2) Multiple-trigger wheeze: child wheezes with viral infections and also with interval triggers like exercise, cold air, crying, or pet allergens, which has a higher risk of evolving into true atopic asthma.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
0059703b-3499-449c-992f-7eba002728be	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	acute-pediatric-asthma-wheeze	Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze	article	# Acute Paediatric Asthma & Wheeze\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Moderate acute asthma Acute severe asthma Life-threatening asthma sp02 292% PEF 250% best or predicted No clinical features of severe asthma NB: If a patient has signs and symptoms across categories, always treat according to their most severe features sp02 PEF 33-50% best or predicted Heart rate\n- 140/min in children aged 1-5 years\n- 125/min in children age years Respiratory rate\n- 40/min in children aged 1-5 years\n- 30/min in children\n- 5 years Use of accessory neck muscles S 02 lusa of: PEF <33% best or predicted Silent chest Poor respiratory effort Altered consciousness Cyanosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof acute asthma in children in the emergency&partment (BTS guideline)\n\n- Moderate asthma PEF best or redicted Oral nisolone 30-40 mg in children\n- 5 years, 20mg below 5 years Reassess within 1 hour If great response, consider discharge PEF 33-50% best or via face mask/nasal prongs to achieve Sp02 94— B2 bronchodilator nebuliser (preferably oxygen- driven), salbutamol 5 mg\n- or, if nebuliser not available, via spacer* orl kgii 30-40 mg ne 4 mg/ Life-threatenink asthma via face mask/nasal prong$o achieve Sp02 BZ bronchodilator with nebuliser\n- driven), salbutamol 5 mg and ipratropium 0.25 mg bronchodilators Oral prednisolone mg/kg, 20mgin children years or IV hydrocortisone 4 mg/kg if vomiting\n- Discuss PICU team or paediatrician If poor response add 0.25 mg nebulised ipratropium bromide to every nebulised ß2 bronchodilator and repeat every 20 minutes for 2 hours accordin to res Consider adding 150 mg magnesium sulphate to each ipratropium in the first hour in children with a short duration of acute severe asthma symptoms presenting with an sp02\n- NOT RESPONDING TO ABOVE TREATMENT (FIRST LINE) Continue 20—30 minute nebulisers\n- Consider chest X-ray and blood gases\n- Consider admission to HDIJ/PICLJ\n- Consider follow\n-\n- Bolus IV infusion of magnesium sulphate 40 mg/kg (max2 g) over\n- Bolus IV salbutamol 15 micrograms/kg if not already given mol infusion 1—5 micrograms/kg/min (200 uous sa mi\n- 'V aminophylline 5 theevewihk&åfifiébbhyllines) followed by continuous infusion Y?æ/kg/hour. Assess response before initiating each new treatment\n\nNote ß2 bronchodilator via spacer given one puff at a time, inhaled separately using tidal breathing; according to response, give another puff every 60 seconds up to a maximum of IO puffs.\n- Dischargeplan Continue ß2bronchodilator 4 hourly as necessary Continue prednisolone 30—40 mg daily until recovery (minimum 3—5 days) Advise to contact GP if not controlled on above treatment Provide a written asthma action plan Review regular treatment y Check inhaler technique\n- Arrange GP follow up within 48 hours\n- Arrange hospital asthma clinic follow up in 4—6 weeks if 2nd or subsequent attack in past 12 months.\n- 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/acute-pediatric-asthma-wheeze/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/acute-pediatric-asthma-wheeze/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/acute-pediatric-asthma-wheeze/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/acute-pediatric-asthma-wheeze/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Acute Pediatric Asthma & Wheeze — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 07:07:45.576678+00	\N
e04e591f-1d80-497a-a232-309fde07fc11	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	concentration-and-dilution-of-urine	Concentration and Dilution of Urine	article	Concentration and dilution of\nurine\nRegulation of plasma osmolarity\nIt is accomplished by varying the amount of H2O excreted\nrelative to the amount of solute excreted (i.e by varying urine\nosmolarity)\nProduction of concentrated urine\nAlso called hyperosmotic urine, in which urine osmolarity >\nBlood osmolarity\nIt is produced when circulating ADH levels are high (e.g.,\nwater deprivation, volume depletion, SIADH).\nCorticopapillary osmotic gradient - high ADH\nIs the gradient of osmolarity from the cortex (300mOsm/L) to\nthe papilla (1200mOsm/L) and is composed primarily of NaCl\nand urea.\nIs established by countercurrent multiplication and urea\nrecycling.\nIs maintained by countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta.\nCountercurrent multiplication in the LOH\nDepends upon NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb\nand countercurrent flow in the descending and ascending\nlimbs of LOH.\nIt is augmented by ADH, which stimulates NaCl reabsorption\nin the thick ascending limb. Therefore, the presence of ADH\nincreases the size of corticopapillary osmotic gradient.\nUrea recycling\nUrea recycling from inner medullary collecting ducts into the\nmedullary interstitial fluid is also augmented by ADH (by\nstimulating UT1 transporter)\nVasa recta\n\nAre the capillaries that supply the LOH. They maintain the\ncorticopapillary gradient by serving as osmotic exchanger.\nVasa recta blood equilibrates osmotically with interstitial fluid\nof medulla and papilla.\nProximal tubule - high ADH\nThe osmolarity of glomerular filtrate is identical to that of\nplasma (300mOsm/L).\nTwo third of filtered H2O is reabsorbed isosmotically (with Na,\nCl, H2O, glucose, amino acids and so forth) in the proximal\ntubule.\nTF/Posm = 1.0 throughout the proximal tubule because water\nis reabsorbed isosmotically with solute.\nThick ascending limb of LOH - high ADH\nIt is called diluting segment.\nReabsorbs NaCl by the NA-K-2Cl cotransporter, reabsorption\nof Na+ is further enhanced by ADH.\nImpermeable to H2O, therefore H2O is not reabsorbed with\nNaCl, and the tubular fluid becomes diluted.\nThe fluid that leaves the thick ascending limb has an\nosmolarity of 100 mOsm/L and TF/Posm < 1.0 as a result of\ndilution process.\nEarly distal tubule - high ADH\nIt is called cortical diluting segment.\nLike the thick ascending limb, early distal tubule reabsorbs\nNaCl but is impermeable to water. Consequently, tubular\nfluid is further diluted.\nLate distal tubule - high ADH\nADH increases the H2O permeability in the principal cells of\nlate distal tubule.\nH2O is reabsorbed from the tubular fluid until the osmolarity\nof tubular fluid equals that of surrounding interstitial fluid in\nrenal cortex (300mOsm/L)\nTF/Posm = 1.0 at the end of the distal tubule because osmotic\nequilibration occurs in the presence of ADH.\nCollecting ducts - high ADH\nAs in the late distal tubule, ADH increases the H2O\npermeability of the principal cells of the collecting ducts.\n\nAs tubular fluid flows through the collecting ducts, it passes\nthrough the corticopapillary gradient (regions of increasingly\nhigher osmolarity), which was previously established by\ncountercurrent multiplication and urea recycling.\nH2O is reabsorbed from the collecting ducts until the\nosmolarity of tubular fluid equals that of the surrounding\nfluid.\nThe osmolarity of the final urine equals that at the bend of\nLOH and tip of papilla (1200mOsm/L)\nTF/Posm= >1.0 because osmotic equilibration occurs with the\ncorticopapillary gradient in the presence of ADH.\nProduction of dilute urine\nIs called hypoosmotic urine, in which urine osmolarity <\nblood osmolarity.\nProduced when circulating levels of ADH are low (e.g., water\nintake, central diabetes insipidus) or when ADH is ineffective\n(nephrogenic diabetes insipidus)\nCorticopapillary osmotic gradient - no ADH\nIs smaller than in the presence of ADH because ADH\nstimulates both countercurrent multiplication and urea\nrecycling\nProximal tubule - no ADH\nTwo third of filtered water is reabsorbed isosmotically.\nTF/Posm = 1.0 throughout the proximal tubule\nThick ascending limb of LOH - no ADH\nNaCl is reabsorbed without water and tubular fluid becomes\n(although not quite as dilute as in the presence of ADH.\nTF/Posm = < 1.0\nEarly distal tubule - no ADH\nNaCl is reabsorbed without water and tubular fluid is further\ndiluted.\nTF/Posm = < 1.0\nLate distal tubule - no ADH\nIn the absence of ADH, the cells of late distal tubule and\ncollecting ducts are impermeable to water.\nThus, even though the tubular fluid flows through the\ncorticopapillary osmotic gradient, osmotic equilibration does not occur.\nThe osmolarity of final urine will be diluted with an osmolarity\nas low as 50mOsm/L.\nTF/Posm = < 1.0\nFree water clearance (CH2O)\nIt is used to estimate the ability to concentrate or dilute the\nurine.\nFree water or solute free water is produced in the diluting\nsegment of kidney (i.e. thick ascending limb and early distal\ntubule), where NaCl is reabsorbed, and free H2O is left behind\nin the tubular fluid.\nIn absence of ADH, this solute free water is excreted and\nCH2O is positive.\nIn presence of ADH, this solute free water is not excreted but\nis reabsorbed by the late distal tubule and collecting ducts\nand CH2O is negative.\nUrine that is isosmotic to plasma\nCH2O is zero\nProduced during a treatment with a loop diuretic, which\ninhibits NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb,\ninhibiting both dilution in the thick ascending limb and\nproduction of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient.\nTherefore, the urine can't be diluted during high water intake\nor concentrated during water deprivation.\nUrine that is hypoosmotic to plasma (low ADH) CH2O is\npositive.\nProduced with high water intake (in which ADH released\nfrom the posterior pituitary is suppressed), central diabetes\ninsipidus or nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.\nUrine that is hyperosmotic to plasma (high ADH)\nCH2O is negative.\nIt is produced in water deprivation or SIADH.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
2fbf7067-658c-49f8-a458-0c5ad508b24e	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	functional-anatomy-of-kidney	Functional Anatomy of Kidney	article	Anatomy of Kidney\nThe kidneys are two bean-shaped organs, located on either\nside of the spine, in the retroperitoneal space. The left kidney is\nsituated a little higher than the right one, because of the liver\non the right side of the abdominal cavity, above the right\nkidney. The kidneys have a medial dimple called the renal\nhilum, which is the entry and exit point for structures that\nsupply or drain the kidneys such as the nerves, ureters, vessels,\nand lymphatics.\nParts of the Kidney\nThe cortex is the outer renal tissue located under the renal\ncapsule. It appears lighter in colour compared to the other\nparts of the kidney. The renal corpuscles, as well as the\nproximal and distal convoluted tubules are found here. It\nreaches into the inner renal tissue or medulla, splitting it into\ntriangular parts called renal pyramids.\nThe renal pyramids contain the loops of Henle of each renal\ntubule, as well as the collecting ducts. Each of the renal\npyramids opens at the apex, or renal papilla. Here the urine\nformed inside the pyramids is collected through a structure\ncalled the minor calyx. Several of the minor calices join to\nform a major calyx. Urine moves through the major calices to\nreach the renal pelvis. The pelvis is a funnel-shaped\ncollecting structure formed by the junction of all the major\ncalices. It carries urine from the whole kidney to the ureter,\nwhich begins where the pelvis ends, at the pelvi-ureteral\njunction.\nNephrons\nThe nephrons are the functional units of the kidneys, and\nnumber about 1.3 million per kidney.\nThere are two types of nephrons, cortical nephrons (85% of\nnephrons) and juxtamedullary nephrons (15% of nephrons)\n\nA nephron has two main parts - tubules (proximal\nconvoluted tubule, the loop of Henle and the distal\nconvoluted tubule, collecting duct) and renal corpuscle\n(Bowman's capsule and glomerulus).\nCortical nephrons have their renal corpuscles in the outer\ncortex and relatively short loops of Henle (LOH).\nJuxtamedullary nephrons have their corpuscles in the inner\nthird of cortex near corticomedullary junction with long LOH\nextending into renal medulla.\nGlomerulus is formed by the invagination of ball of capillaries\ninto the bowman's capsule.\nMain functions of nephron\nGlomerulus - filtration\nProximal tubule - Reabsorption of solutes\nLoop of Henle - urine concentration\nDistal tubule and collecting duct - Acid base balance and\nH2O homeostasis.\nFunctions of kidney\nExcretes waste products: Urea, uric acid, creatinine.\nWater and electrolyte balance\nAcid/base balance\nMetabolism of proteins including PTH and calcitonin\nSecretes the hormone erythropoietin and the enzyme, renin\ninto the circulation, synthesis of prostaglandins\nHydroxylates 25-hydroxy-Vit D to form the active form of\nVitamin D (1,25 dihydroxy-Vit D)\nJuxtaglomerular apparatus\nThe juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) is located between the\nafferent arteriole and the returning distal convoluted tubule of\nthe same nephron. It is responsible for regulating both\nintrarenal (tubuloglomerular feedback) and extrarenal (renin-\nangiotensin-aldosterone) mechanisms necessary to maintain\nboth renal and entire body volume status.\nThe three components of the JGA are the following:\nThe juxtaglomerular cells (granular cells) of the afferent\narteriole, synthesize and store renin, which is secreted in\nresponse to specific stimuli (e.g., low blood flow, decreased\n\nNaCl delivery). The juxtaglomerular cells could be considered\nthe "effector arm" of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis.\nThe macula densa, a region of the distal convoluted tubule\ncharacterized by columnar tubular epithelial cells which are\nmore densely packed than in other regions of the nephron.\nThe macula densa can be considered the "sensory arm" of\nthe renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis in that these are the\ncells which sense and respond to changes in tubular Na Cl\nconcentration. They are also involved in the mechanism of\ntubuloglomerular feedback.\nmesangial cells, which form connections via actin and\nmicrotubules which allow for selective\nvasoconstriction/vasodilation of the renal afferent and\nefferent arterioles with mesangial cell contraction.\nGlomerular filtration barrier\nA membrane that selectively allows the passage of molecules\nbased on size and charge. Plasma is selectively filtered through\nglomerular capillary wall into the bowman's capsule\n(Ultrafiltrate = fluid filtered into bowman' space)\nThis barrier has three major components.\n\n### 1: Glomerular capillary endothelium: It has fenestrations\n\nwhich allows plasma components with a molecular weight of\n<7000 Da to pass freely. These fenestrations actually do not\nrestrict the movement of water and proteins or large\nmolecules but instead prevent the filtration of blood cells\n(e.g. RBCs).\nFenestrated endothelium has magnitude greater than\nproteins; in addition, the wall is covered with negatively\ncharged compounds.\n\n### 2: Glomerular basement membrane: Made up of matrix of\n\nextracellular negatively charged proteins and other\ncompounds, help restrict the movement of negatively\ncharged molecules across the basement membrane. e.g.,\nAlbumin\nMain site of ultrafiltrate\nThe basement membrane consists of 3 layers: These layers\nhelp to limit the filtration of intermediate and large sized\nsolutes e.g., Albumin.\n\n### 3: Epithelial cell layer of podocytes: Next to the bowman's\n\nspace; the podocytes have foot processes directly contacting the basement membrane.\nRegular gaps between foot processes (pedicles) are called\nfiltration slits, across these slits a protein network forms slit\npores in the epithelial lining which prevent the passage of\nlarger molecules through this final layer.\nPodocytes also exhibit phagocytic and contractile activity like\nthat of mesangial cells.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
59454d93-036e-4d11-81b2-211f15f1db60	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	hyperkalemia-and-hypokalemia	Hyperkalemia and Hypokalemia	article	Hyperkalemia\nNormal value of serum K = 3.0- 4.5 mmol/L\nCauses\nInsulin deficiency\nExcessive dietary K\nRenal failure\nAddison's disease (Hypoaldosteronism)\nK+ sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs,\ntrimethoprim and pentamidine therapy, B adrenergic\nantagonists\nAcidosis\nHyperosmolarity (H2O flows out of cell; K diffuses out with\nwater)\nNa-K pump inhibitors (digitalis) when pump is blocked, K is\nnot taken up into cells.\nExercise\nCell lysis/ rhabdomyolysis / trauma /burns/ crush syndrome,\nchemotherapy\nPseudo hyperkalemia:  occurs in\nTest tube hemolysis\nProlonged tourniquet time\nDifficulty collecting sample.\nLength of storage of specimen\nSample from limb receiving IV fluid containing K\nECG findings of hyperkalemia\nCauses rapid reduction in RMP leading to increased cardiac\ndepolarization and muscle excitability which causes ECG\nchanges that can progress to ventricular fibrillation of\nasystole.\nTall, peaked T wave\nShort QT interval\nLengthening of PR interval\nWidening of QRS complex\nLoss of ST segment\nLoss of P wave\nSinus wave appearance\n\nLate 2026)\n\nTreatment of hyperkalemia\nIf there are ECG changes: Stabilize the cardiac membrane\nfirst, e.g., with IV calcium gluconate (10 ml of 10% solution IV\nover 2 minutes).   Followed by or treatment of hyperkalemia\nwithout ECG changes:\nshift the K+ into cells:\nIv glucose and insulin\nInhaled beta-2 agonist (e.g., salbutamol)\nIV sodium bicarbonate (if acidosis present)\nRemove K from Body:\nIV furosemide and normal saline (if adequate renal function)\nIon-exchange resin (sodium polystyrene sulfonate)\nDialysis (in renal failure or severe refractory cases)\nHypokalemia\nCauses\nRenal Losses\nThiazide or loop diuretics\nRenal tubular acidosis\nHypomagnesaemia\n\n![Hyperkalemia and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/renal-physiology-acid-base-balance/hyperkalemia-and-hypokalemia/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Hyperkalemia and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nDrugs causing increased K+ excretion like penicillin,\naminoglycosides, amphotericin\nGI loss\nDiarrhea and vomiting\nFactors causing increased IC shift of K+\nInsulin excess or overdose\nCatecholamines in acute stress\nB2 agonists\nDecreased intake.\nMalnutrition, anorexia nervosa, inadequate replacement\nwhile on TPN or NPO\nNote  :\nAcidosis redistributes K + out of cells=\nHyperkalemia\nAlkalosis redistributes K+ into cells= Hypokalemia\nEGG findings of Hypokalemia\nIncreases automaticity and delays cardiac cells depolarization\nleading to dysrhythmias e.g ectopic AV block, atrial and\nventricular fibrillation.\nProgressive lengthening of PR interval\nST segment depression\nFlattening of T wave\nProminent U wave\nProlongation of QT interval\nTreatment of hypokalemia\nTreat the underlying disorder.\nOral and/or IV potassium replacement\nMagnesium replacement, if hypomagnesemia is present.\nCauses of increased distal K secretion\nHigh K diet\nHyperaldosteronism\nAlkalosis\nThiazide diuretics\nLoop diuretics\nLuminal anions\nCauses of decreased distal K secretion\nLow K diet\nHypoaldosteronism\nAcidosis\nK sparing diuretics	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
fbde1e00-1b8d-48bb-ab9d-f9cd27d33bdd	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	nacl-regulation	NACL Regulation	article	NaCl Regulation\nTubular fluid (TF) is urine at any point along the nephron.\nPlasma (P) is systemic plasma. It is considered to be\nconstant.\nTF/P ratio: Compares the conc of a substance in tubular fluid at\nany point along the nephron with the conc in plasma.\nIf TF/P= 1.0, then either there has been no reabsorption of the\nsubstance or reabsorption of substance has been exactly\nproportional to the reabsorption of water.\nIf TF/P < 1.0, then reabsorption of substance has been greater\nthan the reabsorption of water and the conc in tubular fluid\nis less than that in plasma.\nIf TF/P>1.0, then either reabsorption of the substance has\nbeen less than the reabsorption of water or there has been\nsecretion of the substance.\nNa reabsorption along the nephron:\nNa is freely filtered across the glomerular capillaries; therefore,\nthe Na in the TF of bowman space equals that in plasma i.e.\nTF/PNa = 1.0\nProximal tubule:\nIs the site of glomerulotubular balance.\nThe process is isosmotic. The reabsorption of Na and H2O in\nPT is exactly proportional.\nEarly proximal tubule:\nReabsorbs 2/3rd, or 67% of the filtered Na and H2O more than\nany other part of the nephron.\nReabsorbs Na and H2O with HCO3, glucose, amino acids,\nphosphate and lactate.\nNa is reabsorbed by cotransport with glucose, amino acids,\nphosphate and lactate. These cotransport processes account for the reabsorption of all the filtered glucose and amino\nacids.\nNa is also reabsorbed by counter transport via Na-H\nexchange, which is linked directly to the reabsorption of\nfiltered HCO3\nCarbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide) are diuretics\nthat act in the early pct by inhibiting the reabsorption of\nfiltered HCO3.\nReabsorbs 67% of the filtered K along with Na and H2O\nReabsorbs 50% of filtered urea.\nReabsorbs 85% of filtered phosphate.\nLate proximal tubule:\nFiltered glucose, amino acids and HCO3 have already been\ncompletely removed from the tubular fluid by reabsorption in\nearly pct.\nIn the late pct, Na is reabsorbed with Cl (Cotransport).\nEffects of ECF volume on PT reabsorption:\nECF volume contraction increases PT reabsorption.\nECF volume expansion decreases PT reabsorption.\nThick ascending limb of loop of Henle:\nReabsorbs 25% of the filtered Na\nReabsorbs 20% of filtered K\nContains a Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the luminal membrane.\nIs the site of action of the loop diuretics (furosemide,\nethacrynic acid, bumetanide), which inhibit the Na-K-2Cl\ncotransporter.\nIs impermeable to H2O. Thus, NaCl is reabsorbed without\nwater. As a result, tubular fluid [Na] and tubular fluid\nosmolarity decreases to less than their conc in plasma. This\nsegment therefore is called the diluting segment.\nIt has a lumen positive potential difference. Although the Na-\nK-2Cl cotransport appears to be electroneutral, some K\ndiffuses back into the lumen, making the lumen electrically\npositive.\nDistal tubule and collecting duct:\nTogether reabsorb 8% of filtered Na.\nEarly DCT:\nReabsorbs NaCl by a Na-Cl cotransporter.\n\nIs the site of action of thiazide diuretics\nIs impermeable to water, as thick ascending limb. Thus,\nreabsorption of NaCl occurs without water, which further\ndilutes the tubular fluid, therefore called cortical diluting\nsegment.\nThiazide diuretics cause hypocalciuria, in a way that they\nblock the Na-Cl cotransporter and decrease the Na\nreabsorption and also Na-K ATPase on the basolateral\nmembrane maintains a low intracellular conc: of Na, which\ncauses the Na from interstitial fluid/peritubular capillary\nblood to move into the cell in exchange with Ca through Na-\nCa exchanger/counter transport thereby increasing the\nreabsorption of Ca into blood.\nPTH increases the activity of Na-Ca exchanger and increases\nthe reabsorption of Ca.\nLate DCT: Have two cell types.\nPrincipal cells Reabsorbs Na and H2O\nSecretes K\nAldosterone increases Na reabsorption and increases K\nsecretion. Like other steroid hormones, the action of\naldosterone takes several hours to develop because new\nprotein synthesis of Na channels (ENaC) is required. About 2%\nof overall Na reabsorption is affected by aldosterone.\nADH increases H2O permeability by directing the insertion of\nH2O channels in the luminal membrane. In the absence of\nADH, the principal cells are virtually impermeable to water.\nK- sparing diuretics (spironolactone, triamterene, amiloride)\ndecreases K secretion.\nAlpha intercalated cells:\nSecretes H+ by an H-adenosine triphosphatase (H-ATPase),\nwhich is stimulated by aldosterone.\nReabsorbs K+ by an H-K-ATPase	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
ce432f1f-2b1e-48ea-b668-b646d5cd7ff6	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	measles-mumps-rubella-virus	Measles, Mumps, Rubella - Virus	article	Measles:\nAcute viral illness transmitted by respiratory droplets\ncharacterized by 3C Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and fever.\nMeasles rash: Maculopapular rash appearing first behind the\nears and spreading to whole body.\nIncubation period: 9-12 days\nInfectious period: Patients are considered infectious for 3\ndays before the rash emerges and four days from onset of\nthe rash.\nPatients are advised to stay away from school.\nTreatment: Supportive\nComplications: Pneumonia, bronchitis, otitis media,\nencephalitis, subacute sclerosing panencephalitis\nMumps:\nRubella:\nAcute viral illness transmitted by respiratory droplets\ncharacterized by fever, malaise, arthralgia and sore throat\nRubella rash: Maculopapular rash appearing first on the face\nand spreading downward associated with lymphadenopathy.\nIncubation period: 14-21 days\nInfectious period: Patients are considered infectious for 4\ndays from onset of the rash and seven days before the rash\n\n![Measles, Mumps, Rubella - Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/measles-mumps-rubella-virus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Measles, Mumps, Rubella - Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nappears.\nPatients are advised to stay away from school in infectious\nperiod.\nTreatment:  Supportive\nComplications: Arthritis, Thrombocytopenic purpura,\nEncephalitis and congenital rubella syndrome\nRubella in pregnancy: Is very serious if a woman is infected\nin the first trimester it can have serious consequences for\nthe baby's health for example congenital abnormalities such\nas heart defects, cataracts, intrauterine growth restriction,\ncentral nervous system defects and deafness.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
88fd6a72-5de2-4c66-81c5-7b392f73c879	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	potassium-regulation	Potassium Regulation	article	K+ Regulation\nK is filtered, reabsorbed and secreted by nephron.\nK balance is achieved when urinary excretion of K+ exactly\nequals intake of K+ in the diet.\nK excretion can vary widely from the filtered load, depending\non dietary K intake, aldosterone levels and acid base.\nGlomerular capillaries:\nFiltration occurs freely across the glomerular capillaries.\nTherefore TF/P for K in bowman space is 1.0.\nProximal tubule:\nReabsorbs 67% of the filtered K+ along with Na+ and H2O.\nThick ascending limb of LOH:\nReabsorbs 20% of the filtered K+.\nReabsorption involves Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the\nluminal membrane of cells in the thick ascending limb.\nDistal tubule and collecting duct:\nEither reabsorbs or secretes K+ depending on dietary K\nintake.\nReabsorption of K+ involves an H-K ATPase in the luminal\nmembrane of the alpha intercalated cells of late DCT, and its\noccurs only on a low K+ diet (K+ depletion). Under these\nconditions, K+ excretion can be as low as 1% of the filtered\nload because the kidney conserves as much K as possible.\nSecretion occurs in the principal cells. It is variable and\naccounts for the wide range of urinary K+ excretion. It\ndepends upon factors such as dietary K+, aldosterone levels,\nacid base status and urine flow rate.\nMechanism of distal K+ secretion:\nAt the basolateral membrane, K+ is actively transported into\nthe cell by the Na-K pump, as in all cells this maintains a high\nintracellular K+ conc.\n\nAt the luminal membrane, K+ is passively secreted into the\nlumen through K+ channels. The magnitude of this passive\nsecretion 1st determined by the chemical and electrical\ndriving forces on K+ across the luminal membrane.\nFactors that change the distal K secretion:\nDistal K+ secretion by the principal cells is increased when\nthe electrochemical driving force for K+ across the luminal\nmembrane is increased. Secretion is decreased when the\nelectrochemical driving force is decreased.\nDietary K\nA diet high in K+ increases K+ secretion, and a diet low in K+\ndecreases K+ secretion.\nOn a high K+ diet, intracellular K+ increases so that the\ndriving force for K+ secretion also increases.\nOn a low K+ diet, intracellular K+ decreases so that the\ndriving for K+ secretion decreases. Also, the alpha\nintercalated cells are stimulated to reabsorb K+ by the H+-K+\nATPase.\nAldosterone\nIncreases K+ secretion.\nMechanism involves aldosterone increases the no Na+\nchannels across the luminal membrane and increases Na+\nentry into the cell (principal cell of distal tubule), it also\nincreases the activity of Na-K ATPase pump which in turn\ncauses increased pumping of Na+ out of the cells.\nStimulation of NA-K pump simultaneously increases K+\nuptake into the cells, increasing the IC K conc and increased\ndriving force for K+ secretion. Aldosterone also increases the\nnumber of luminal membrane K channels thereby enhancing\nits secretion.\nHyperaldosteronism: Increases K secretion and causes\nhypokalemia.\nHypoaldosteronism: Decreases K secretion and causes\nhyperkalemia.\nAcid base\nEffectively, H and K exchange for each other across the\nbasolateral cell membrane.\n\nAcidosis decreases K secretion. The blood contains\nexcessive H+; therefore, H+ enters the cell across the\nbasolateral membrane and K+ leaves the cell and moves into\nblood through H-K exchanger. As a result, the IC K+ conc\ndecreases and driving force for K+ secretion decreases.\nAlkalosis increases K+ secretion. The blood contains too little\nH+; therefore, H+ leaves the cell across the basolateral\nmembrane and enters the blood in exchange with K+\nthrough the H-K exchange mechanism thereby increasing IC\nK+ conc and its secretion.\nThiazide and loop diuretics\nIncrease K secretion.\nDiuretics that increase the flow rate through the distal tubule\nand collecting duct e.g., thiazide and loop diuretics cause\ndilution of the luminal K conc, increasing the driving force for\nK secretion. Also, as a result of increased K secretion, these\ndiuretics cause hypokalemia.\nK sparing diuretics\nDecrease K+ secretion. If used alone they cause\nhyperkalemia.\nSpironolactone is an antagonist of aldosterone; triamterene\nand amiloride act directly on the principal cells.\nThe most important use of K+ sparing diuretics is in\ncombination with thiazide or loop diuretics to reduce urinary\nK losses.\nLuminal anions\nExcess anions (HCO3-) in the lumen cause an increase in K+\nsecretion by increasing the negativity of the lumen and\nincreasing the driving force for K+ secretion.	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
19c9fdc9-ddba-43b5-adfc-9db4fd37c819	7c664ce9-6710-48a0-b9e1-ee3c14f16ed9	renin-angiotensin-aldosterone-system	Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System	article	Renin angiotensin aldosterone\nsystem\nThe Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) is a\nhormone system within the body that is essential for the\nregulation of blood pressure and fluid balance. The system is\nmainly comprised of the three hormones renin, angiotensin\nII and aldosterone.\nA fall in CPV (reflecting blood volume) is detected by atrial\nand other cardiopulmonary stretch receptors. Fall in blood\nvolume sufficient to reduce the blood pressure activates the\nbaroreceptor reflex which in turn causes increased\nsympathetic stimulation resulting in Renal vasoconstriction\ndecreasing the GFR.\nADH release causing H2O reabsorption.\nRenin release from granular cells of JG apparatus\nPeripheral vasoconstriction resulting in increased total\nperipheral vasoconstriction.\nRenin Release\nThe first stage of the RAAS is the release of the enzyme renin.\nRenin released from granular cells of the renal juxtaglomerular\napparatus (JGA) in response to one of three factors:\nReduced sodium delivery to the distal convoluted tubule\ndetected by macula densa cells.\nReduced perfusion pressure in the kidney detected\nby baroreceptors in the afferent arteriole.\nSympathetic stimulation of the JGA via β 1 adrenoreceptors.\nDecreased GFR\nDecreased ECF volume, arterial blood pressure or CVP.\nNote: The release of renin is inhibited by atrial natriuretic\npeptide (ANP), which is released by stretched atria in response\nto increases in blood pressure.\nAngiotensin II production\n\nAngiotensinogen is a precursor protein produced in the liver\nand cleaved by renin to form angiotensin I. Angiotensin I is then\nconverted to angiotensin II by angiotensin converting\nenzyme (ACE). This conversion occurs mainly in the lungs where\nACE is produced by vascular endothelial cells, although ACE is\nalso generated in smaller quantities within the renal\nendothelium.\nFunctions of angiotensin II\nAngiotensin 2 acts on AT1 receptors found in the endothelium\nof arterioles throughout the circulation to\nachieve vasoconstriction. The net effect of this is an increase\nin total peripheral resistance and consequently, blood\npressure.\nAngiotensin II acts at the hypothalamus to stimulate the\nsensation of thirst, resulting in an increase in fluid\nconsumption. This helps to raise the circulating volume and\nin turn, blood pressure.\nIt also increases the secretion of ADH from the posterior\npituitary gland - resulting in the production of more\nconcentrated urine to reduce the loss of fluid from urination.\nAngiotensin II acts on the adrenal cortex to stimulate the\nrelease of aldosterone from zona glomerulosa of the adrenal\ncortex. Which in turn causes increased Na reabsorption and\nK loss. It also stimulates the sympathetic nervous system to\nincrease the release of noradrenaline (NA). which in turn\nincreases cardiac output, release of renin, vasoconstriction of\narterioles.\nCauses vasoconstriction of renal arterioles predominantly\nefferent arterioles thereby maintaining or increasing the GFR\nActivation of Na-H pump thereby increasing the reabsorption\nof Na from proximal tubule.\nInhibits the renin production from granular cells of JG\napparatus (negative feedback)	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
2155fb39-2f53-4bb6-8a39-7ccee433888b	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	defecation	Defecation	article	Defecation\nThere are two main anal sphincters, an internal and external\nsphincter. The internal anal sphincter is controlled by\nparasympathetic fibers which relax involuntarily. The\nexternal anal sphincter is a skeletal muscle that is controlled\nby somatic nerve supply from the Inferior anal branch of the\nPudendal nerve (S2,3,4), which allows conscious control of\ndefecation.\nWhen the rectum is distended the recto sphincteric reflex is\ninitiated and relaxes the internal sphincter. If defecation is not\ndesired, voluntary contraction of the external sphincter can\ndelay it. If defecation is appropriate, then a series of reflexes\ntake place that lead to:\nRelaxation of the external sphincter.\nContraction of abdominal wall muscles\nRelaxation of pelvic wall muscles\nPeristaltic waves then facilitate the movement of faeces\nthrough the anal canal. Defecation can also be assisted by\ntaking a deep breath and attempting to expel the air against a\nclosed glottis, this is known as the Valsalva maneuver. However,\nif a delay in defection is needed then voluntary contraction of\nthe external sphincter is usually sufficient to override the\nreflexes that anal distension initiates.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
385e3097-e1d6-4e3e-9d05-e7629600523f	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	digestion-and-absorption-of-carbohydrates	Digestion and Absorption of Carbohydrates	article	Carbohydrate\nCarbohydrates in the diet provide the major exogenous source\nfor glucose, which is the primary energy source for cells. They\nprovide 17kJ (4kcal) of energy per gram. Most of the dietary\ncarbohydrates are in the form of polysaccharides, the principal\ningested polysaccharides are starch which is derived from\nplant sources and glycogen which is derived from animal\nsources, other sources include disaccharides and\nmonosaccharides.\nDigestion and Absorption of\ncarbohydrate\nCarbohydrates are neutral compounds composed of carbon,\nhydrogen, and oxygen that serve as the primary sources of\nenergy in the human body. They can be divided into simple\ncarbohydrates, which include monosaccharides (e.g., glucose,\nfructose, galactose) and disaccharides (e.g., maltose, sucrose,\nlactose), and complex carbohydrates, which include starch\npolysaccharides (e.g., starch, glycogen and Non starch\npolysaccharides (e.g., glucan, cellulose). Monosaccharides are\ndirectly absorbed by enterocytes. Disaccharides and\npolysaccharides require degradation into monosaccharides to\nbe absorbed by hydrolytic enzymes, which are secreted by the\nsalivary glands in the mouth (salivary amylase), intestinal villi\n(maltase, lactase), and pancreas (pancreatic amylase).\nConditions that decrease the secretion of these enzymes result\nin malabsorption and maldigestion.\nDigestion\n\nGlucose and galactose are absorbed via sodium-glucose linked\ntransporters (SGLTs) in the intestinal epithelial cells and\ntranslocated into the circulation via glucose transporter 2\n(GLUT2). Fructose is absorbed into enterocytes via glucose\ntransporter 5 (GLUT5) through facilitated diffusion. The uptake\nof glucose into other cells is mediated by glucose transporters 1\nto 5. Intracellularly, monosaccharides are further metabolized\nby a series of enzymatic reactions that release ATP.\nIndigestible carbohydrates found in plants food such as\ncellulose (humans unable to digest cellulose because lack of\nappropriate enzymes cellulase). it does not provide any energy;\nfiber adds bulk to the bowel contents and increases the gut\nmotility.\n\n![Digestion and Absorption of Carbohydrates - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/digestion-and-absorption-of-carbohydrates/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Digestion and Absorption of Carbohydrates — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
40916e76-d263-49ba-ae29-fb24670468c1	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	digestion-and-absorption-of-lipids	Digestion and Absorption of Lipids	article	Lipid\nFats in the diet are a more concentrated form of energy than\ncarbohydrates and have an energy yield of 37 kJ (9\nkilocalories) per gram. Adipose (fatty) tissue in the fat depots\nof the body serves as an energy reserve as well as helping to\ninsulate the body and cushion the internal organs.\nThe lipids of nutritional importance are triglycerides (fats and\noils), phospholipids (e.g., lecithin), and sterols (e.g.,\ncholesterol). Lipids in the diet transport the four fat-soluble\nvitamins (vitamins A, D, E, and K) and assist in their\nabsorption in the small intestine.\nDigestion and absorption of lipid\nDigestion\nLingual and gastric lipase begin the hydrolysis of triglycerides\nto monoglycerides and fatty acids. However, most of the\ningested lipids are digested in the intestine by pancreatic\nlipases.\nCCK slows gastric emptying. Thus, delivery of lipids from the\nstomach to the duodenum is slowed to allow adequate time\nfor digestion and absorption in the intestine.\nIn Small intestine\nBile acids emulsify lipids in the small intestine, increasing the\nsurface area for Digestion.\npancreatic lipases into the small intestine to enzymatically\ndigest triglycerides. Triglycerides are broken down to fatty\nacids, monoglycerides (glycerol backbone with one fatty acid\nstill attached), and some free glycerol. Cholesterol and fat-\nsoluble vitamins do not need to be enzymatically digested\nThose products of fat digestion (fatty acids, monoglycerides,\nglycerol, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins) need to enter\ninto the circulation so that they can be used by cells around\nthe body. Bile salts cluster around the products of fat\ndigestion to form structures called micelles, which help the fats get close enough to the microvilli of intestinal cells so\nthat they can be absorbed.\nAbsorption of lipids\nMicelles bring the products of lipid digestion into contact\nwith the absorptive surface of the intestinal cells. Then, fatty\nacids, monoglycerides, cholesterol and fat-soluble vitamins\ndiffuse across the luminal membrane into the cells. Once\ninside the intestinal cell, short- and medium-chain fatty acids\nand glycerol can be directly absorbed into the bloodstream,\nbut larger lipids such as long-chain fatty acids,\nmonoglycerides, fat-soluble vitamins, and cholesterol need\nhelp with absorption and transport to the bloodstream.\nLong-chain fatty acids and monoglycerides reesterified into\ntriglycerides within the intestinal cell, and along with\ncholesterol and fat-soluble vitamins, are then incorporated\ninto transport vehicles called chylomicrons. Chylomicrons\nare large structures with a core of triglycerides and\ncholesterol and an outer membrane made up of\nphospholipids, interspersed with proteins (called\napolipoproteins) and cholesterol.  Lack of apoprotein B\nresults in the inability to transport chylomicrons out of the\nintestinal cells and causes abetalipoproteinemia.\nChylomicrons are transported out of the intestinal cells by\nexocytosis. Because chylomicrons are too large to enter the\ncapillaries, they are transferred to lymph vessels (lacteals) and\nare added to the bloodstream via the thoracic duct.\n\n![Digestion and Absorption of Lipids - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/digestion-and-absorption-of-lipids/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Digestion and Absorption of Lipids — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Digestion and Absorption of Lipids - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/digestion-and-absorption-of-lipids/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Digestion and Absorption of Lipids — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
00a9a559-cd09-4a52-ae8e-4905f798b643	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	digestion-and-absorption-of-proteins	Digestion and Absorption of Proteins	article	Protein\nProteins are important nutrients that your body uses to build\ncellular structures that carry out vital functions.\nThe building blocks of proteins are amino acids. Amino acids\nare divided into two groups- essential and non-essential\namino acids. The essential amino acids cannot be made by\nour bodies and instead must be acquired through our diet.\nBy contrast, the non-essential amino acids can be\nsynthesized in the human body.\nProtein provides the same amount of energy as\ncarbohydrates (4 calories per gram).\nDigestion and absorption of protein\nEnzyme responsible for digestion of protein are proteolytic\nenzymes.\nIn the mouth\nDigestion of protein doesn't occur in the mouth since\nsaliva does not contain any proteolytic enzymes.\nIn the stomach\nDigestion of protein starts in stomach.\nGastric acid causes denaturation.\nCleavage via pepsin into polypeptides\nIn the small intestine\nThe intestinal phase is responsible for the bulk of proteolysis\nand is mainly due to the actions of the pancreatic and\nintestinal proteases. 70% of proteins are converted to\noligopeptides and amino acids in the intestines. There are two\nmain forms of pancreatic enzymes: endopeptidase cleaves\ninternal bonds and ectopeptidase cleaves AA at C-terminus.\nEndopeptidases include trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase.\nEctopeptidase includes carboxypeptidase A and\ncarboxypeptidase B. Carboxypeptidase A acts on neutral AA\nand carboxypeptidase B acts on basic AA. The process of digestion of protein is completed in the small intestine with\nbrush border and pancreatic enzymes.\nAbsorption:\nEnterocytes contain microvilli, which form the brush border\nthat enables absorption and transport of nutrients.\nAmino acids are absorbed via a Sodium cotransporter, in a\nsimilar mechanism to the monosaccharides. They are then\ntransported across the basolateral membrane via facilitated\ndiffusion. Di and tripeptides are absorbed via separate H+\ndependent cotransporters and once inside the cell are\nhydrolyzed to amino acids.\n\n![Digestion and Absorption of Proteins - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/digestion-and-absorption-of-proteins/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Digestion and Absorption of Proteins — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
7f7e08d2-fbf9-4245-817e-dc799cecf442	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	gastrointestinal-tract	Gastrointestinal Tract	article	Gastrointestinal tract\nThe gastrointestinal tract (GI tract, digestive tract, alimentary\ncanal) is the tract or passageway of the digestive system that\nleads from the mouth to the anus. The GI tract contains all the\nmajor organs of the digestive system, in humans and other\nanimals, including the esophagus, stomach, and intestines.\nFood taken in through the mouth is digested to extract\nnutrients and absorb energy, and the waste expelled at the\nanus as feces.\nThe complete human digestive system is made up of the\ngastrointestinal tract plus the accessory organs of digestion\n(the tongue, salivary glands, pancreas, liver and gallbladder)\nFunction of GIT\nIngestion of food and water\nMastication\nTransport of the food bolus or chyme through the\ngastrointestinal tract\nDigestion (i.e., breaking food down into nutrients)\nAbsorption of nutrients and water\nExcretion of waste as feces\nImmunity\n\nStructures of GI tract:\nAll segments of the GI tract are divided into four layers (from\ninnermost to outer most) are:\n1.The innermost mucosa composed of:\nthe epithelial layer (specialized in different parts of the GI\ntract for secretion and absorption\nthe lamina propria (consisting of loose tissue, blood\nvessels and lymph tissue)\nthe muscularis mucosa (contraction causes a change in\nthe surface area for secretion or absorption)\n\n### 2. The submucosa (containing a second layer of connective\n\ntissue, large blood vessels and lymphatic vessels and\nsubmucosal plexus\n\n![Gastrointestinal Tract - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/gastrointestinal-tract/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Tract — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n3.The muscularis externa composed of:\nThe inner circular muscle layer (contraction causes a\ndecrease in diameter of the lumen of the GI tract)\nThe outer longitudinal muscle layer (contraction causes\nshortening of a segment of the GI tract)\nThe myenteric plexus which lies between these two layers\nof muscle\n\n### 4. The outer most serosa (another connective tissue layer\n\ncovered with squamous mesothelial cells)	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
087930b8-fa44-464c-9f18-af4b86f73c6d	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	innervation-of-gi-tract	Innervation of Gi Tract	article	Innervation of Gastrointestinal tract\nThe gastrointestinal tract is innervated by the autonomic\nnervous system.\nThe autonomic system of GI tract comprises both extrinsic\nand intrinsic nervous system.\nExtrinsic innervation: Parasympathetic and sympathetic\nnervous system\nIntrinsic innervation: enteric nervous system\nRegulated by the autonomic nervous system but can\nfunction independently.\nCrucial for gastrointestinal motility and secretion\nMeissner and Myenteric plexus\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Achalasia cardia:\n\n![Innervation of Gi Tract - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/innervation-of-gi-tract/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Innervation of Gi Tract — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Innervation of Gi Tract - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/innervation-of-gi-tract/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Innervation of Gi Tract — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nPrimary esophageal motility disorder characterized by the\nimpaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES)\nand absence of peristalsis in the esophagus. This condition is\nprimarily attributed to dysfunction in the myenteric plexus,\nwhich is a network of nerve fibers and ganglion cells located\nbetween the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the\nesophagus.	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
b6858ca7-3f26-4d7a-90fb-8e276defdb4b	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	iron-handling	Iron Handling	article	Iron\nIron is a vital trace element for humans, as it plays a crucial role\nin oxygen transport, oxidative metabolism, cellular proliferation,\nand many catalytic reactions.\nDietary iron, Heme iron: from meat, non-heme iron: from\nplants\nFerrous iron (Fe 2+): in hemoglobin\nFerric iron (Fe3+): in methemoglobin\nFree iron: Fe2+ can lead to reactive oxygen species via the\nFenton reaction.\nH2O2 + Fe2+ →  OH- + Fe3+ + OH (hydroxyl radical) →  oxidative\nstress →  DNA damage\nIron absorption in small intestine\nGastric acid leads to low pH and promotes dissolution of iron\nin the duodenum.\nAbsorption occurs in the duodenum and upper jejunum.\nThe enzyme hepcidin regulates intestinal absorption of iron.\nHepcidin is synthesized in the liver.\nIt's the main hormonal regulator of iron homeostasis.\nit inhibits the iron release from macrophages in the\nreticuloendothelial system and from intestinal epithelial cells\nand inhibits intestinal iron absorption.\nHepcidin is decreased by erythropoietin, ineffective\nerythropoiesis, pregnancy and hypoxia.\nIncreased in inflammation and iron overload.\nIron deficiency →  ↓ hepcidin →  ↑ iron absorption\nFerric iron (non-heme iron, Fe3+) is mainly reduced to\nferrous iron (Fe2+) and then absorbed.\nFe2+ is taken across the enterocyte apical membrane by\ndivalent metal transporter (DMT1)\nVitamin C increases absorption (converts Fe3+ →  Fe2+).\nCalcium decreases absorption (due to chelation of iron).\nA minority of iron is absorbed as ferric iron (Fe3+).\n\nHeme iron can be directly absorbed into intestinal cells.\nIron transport\nFerroportin (inhibited by hepcidin) transports ferrous iron\n(Fe 2+) from the enterocytes to the bloodstream.\nThe enzyme ferroxidase oxidizes ferrous iron back to ferric\niron (converts Fe2+ →  Fe3+).\nTransferrin binds and transports the ferric iron (Fe3+) to the\nerythroid precursor cells (in bone marrow) for hemoglobin\nsynthesis.\nIron storage\nTotal amount of iron in the body is 3-4 g\nIron is present in the body in two forms:\nFunctional iron (80%): hemoglobin (largest proportion),\nmyoglobin, cytochrome enzymes\nStorage iron (20%): as Fe3+\nFerritin: hepatocytes, macrophages, enterocyte\nHemosiderin: hepatocytes\nIron recycling\nReticuloendothelial macrophages (in the spleen and liver)\nphagocytose senescent RBCs and release iron from\nhemoglobin.\nTransferrin binds the released iron and transports it to the\nbone marrow for erythropoiesis.\nIron loss\nShedding of skin and mucosal epithelial cell →  daily loss of 1-\n2 mg of iron\n\n![Iron Handling - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/iron-handling/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Iron Handling — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nAny source of bleeding (e.g., menstruation, occult GI bleed)\nincreases iron loss.	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
306db209-5cbc-44ce-9138-6dc256ff67b3	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	mycobacterium-tuberculosis-bacteria	Mycobacterium Tuberculosis - Bacteria	article	Mycobacterium Tuberculosis\nAcid fast bacilli Causes tuberculosis transmitted via respiratory\naerosol and characterized by chronic granulomatous\ninflammation.\nClinical Features:\nFever, Night sweats and weight loss\nPersistent cough for more than 3 weeks\nHemoptysis\nHilar lymphadenopathy\nPathophysiology:\nThe Ghon focus is a primary lesion caused by tuberculosis that\ndevelops in the lung of previously unaffected individuals. It\nrepresents a calcified caseating granuloma. It typically\nappears in the middle-third of the lung (the lower part of the\nupper lobe or upper part of the lower lobe).\nDiagnosis:\nsputum samples Microscopy with Z-N (Ziehl-Nelsen stain)\nshows acid fast bacilli.\nIn the case of active pulmonary TB, a patient can have\ncrepitations, and bronchial breath sounds, and specially over\nupper lobes x-ray cavitations or consolidations.\nThe Mantoux test is a widely used test for latent TB: It\ninvolves injecting a small amount of a substance called PPD\ntuberculin into the skin of your forearm. It's also called the\ntuberculin skin test (TST).	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
724a3d25-a193-4773-93c9-02f423d16218	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	exocrine-pancreas	Exocrine Pancreas	article	Exocrine pancreas\nThe pancreas is an abdominal organ located deep in the\nretroperitoneum. It is a gland with mixed functions: both\nexocrine and endocrine. the endocrine gland containing the\nislets of Langerhans. Most of the pancreas is made up of the\nexocrine portion (>90% by mass) and secretes a major\ndigestive fluid called pancreatic juice (digestive enzymes, water\nand bicarbonate to assist in digestion), which is secreted into\nthe duodenum via the pancreatic duct. When food is present in\nthe duodenum the sphincter of Oddi relaxes, allowing both bile\nand pancreatic secretions to enter the duodenum.\nExocrine secretion\npancreatic juice contains bicarbonate, which neutralizes acid\nentering the duodenum from the stomach; and digestive\nenzymes, which break down carbohydrates, proteins, and fats\nin food entering the duodenum from the stomach)\nThe exocrine pancreas includes the acinus and its duct\nsystem. These acinar cells are specialized in enzyme\nsynthesis, storage and secretion. The duct system modifies\nthe initial pancreatic secretion by secreting HCO3- and\nabsorbing CL- via CL- -HCO3- exchange mechanism in the\nluminal membrane.\n\n![Exocrine Pancreas - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/exocrine-pancreas/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Exocrine Pancreas — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nEnzymes secreted:\nMost of the proteolytic enzymes are secreted in an inactive\nform termed zymogens or proenzymes to protect the pancreas\nfrom autodigestion. When released into the duodenum, they\nare activated by the enzyme enterokinase present in the lining\nof the duodenum.\nProteases\nChymotrypsinogen and Trypsinogen\nDigest proteins and peptides to single amino acid\nPancreatic lipase\nDigests triglycerides, monoglyceride and free fatty acids\nAmylase\nStarch and maltose (disaccharides)\nOther enzymes include ribonuclease, gelatinase, elastase\netc.\nRegulation of pancreatic secretion\nStimulation\nParasympathetic stimulation enhances secretion of both the\nenzyme and aqueous components.\nCholecystokinin stimulates secretion of the enzyme rich fluid\nfrom acinar cells.\nSecretin stimulates secretion of alkaline rich fluid from ductal\ncells.\nInhibition\nSympathetic stimulation inhibits the pancreatic secretion.\nSomatostatin inhibits secretion of both acinar and ductal\ncells.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Acute pancreatitis:\n> Inflammation of the pancreas occurs in when digestive\n> enzymes secreted in pancreatic juice start digesting its own\n> tissues\n> Pathophysiology: Trypsin is an enzyme that is involved in the\n> digestion of proteins. Normally, trypsinogen (the inactive\n> precursor of trypsin) is activated to trypsin in the small\n> intestine, where it aids in protein digestion. However, in cases of pancreatitis, trypsin can become prematurely activated\nwithin the pancreas itself due to factors like alcohol\nconsumption or other triggers. This activation of trypsin within\nthe pancreas can lead to the autodigestion of pancreatic tissue,\ncausing inflammation.\nChymotrypsinogen is the inactive precursor of chymotrypsin.\nActivation of chymotrypsinogen to its active form,\nchymotrypsin, occurs through the action of trypsin. Once\ntrypsin is produced through the activation of trypsinogen, it\ncan then activate chymotrypsinogen by cleaving it to\nchymotrypsin. Chymotrypsin further breaks down protein\nmolecules into smaller peptides and amino acids that can be\nabsorbed by the body.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
78bde031-5ac2-4961-bda9-09b32f6e1ab7	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	gastrointestinal-hormones	Gastrointestinal Hormones	article	Gastrointestinal hormones\nGI Hormone are released from endocrine cells in the GI mucosa\ninto portal circulation, enter the general circulation, and have\nphysiologic actions on target cells.\nGastrin\nGastrin hormone is released from G-cells in the lining pyloric\nantrum of stomach.\nStimulating factors\nGastrin secretion is stimulated by:\nThe presence of small peptides and amino acids in chyme\nGastric distension\nVagal stimulation directly via acetylcholine and indirectly via\ngastrin releasing peptide (GRP)\nRaised gastric PH (H+)\nInhibiting factors\nGastrin secretion is inhibited by.\nLow gastric PH by negative feedback mechanism\nSomatostatin\nSecretin\nGastric inhibitory polypeptide (GIP)\nCholecystokinin\nAction of Gastrin\nStimulate the acid secretion from gastric parietal cells (both\nby directly and indirectly by stimulating release of histamine\nfrom ECL cells\nStimulate pepsinogen secretion from chief cells.\nStimulate growth of gastric mucosa by stimulating the\nsynthesis of RNA and new protein\n\nZollinger-Ellison syndrome (Gastrinoma)\nOccurs when gastrin is secreted by non-β-cell tumors of the\npancreas.\nSecretin\nSecretin is released by S-cells in the duodenum.\nStimulating factors\nSecretin secretion is stimulated by:\nA low PH (H+) in the duodenum\nThe presence of products of fat digestion in the duodenum\nInhibitory factors\nSecretin release is inhibited by somatostatin.\nAction of secretin\nSecretin acts to:\nDecrease gastric acid secretion by gastric parietal cells\n(through inhibition of gastrin release)\nInhibit the gastric emptying.\nStimulate the release of pepsinogen by gastric chief cells.\nStimulate the pancreatic HCO3- secretion.\nPotentiate cholecystokinin-induced stimulation of pancreatic\nenzyme secretion.\nStimulate HCO3- and H2O secretion by the liver and increase\nbile production.\nCholecystokinin (CCK)\nCCK is released from I cells of the duodenal and jejunal\nmucosa.\nStimulating factors\nCholecystokinin release is stimulated by:\nThe presence of small peptides and amino acids in the\nduodenum\nThe presence of fatty acids and monoglycerides in the\nduodenum\nTriglycerides do not stimulate the release of CCK because\nthey cannot cross intestinal cell membranes.\n\nInhibiting factors\nThe release of cholecystokinin is inhibited by somatostatin.\nAction of CCK\nStimulate the pancreatic enzyme secretion.\nStimulate the secretion of pepsinogen from chief cells.\nStimulate the contraction of gall bladder and simultaneously\ncause relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi for secretion of bile.\nPotentiates secretin-induced stimulation of pancreatic\nHCO3- secretion.\nInhibit the Gastric acid production.\nInhibit the gastric emptying, thus meals containing fat\nstimulate the secretion of CCK, which slow gastric emptying\nto allow more time for intestinal digestion and absorption.\nOverview of GIT hormones\nSomatostatin\nIn the gut, somatostatin is secreted from the D-cells in the\ngastric and intestinal mucosa. Somatostatin inhibits the\nrelease of all GI Hormones.\nIn the pancreas, somatostatin is produced by delta cells of\nislets of Langerhans.\nSomatostatin is secreted by cells throughout the GI tract in\nresponse to H+ in the lumen.\nDue to its effect on growth hormone, it is also referred to as\ngrowth hormone inhibiting hormone.\nSomatostatin secretion is inhibited by vagal stimulation.\n\n![Gastrointestinal Hormones - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/gastrointestinal-hormones/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Hormones — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nSomatostatin inhibits the gastric acid secretion both directly\nat the parietal cell via a G-protein coupled receptor and\nindirectly via inhibition gastrin and histamine secretion.\nAction of somatostatin\nInhibit the release of all GI hormone (gastrin, secretin, CCK,\nGIP, insulin glucagon)\nDecrease gastrin release leading to reduced gastric acid.\nDecrease gastric and intestinal secretions.\nDecrease bile production and release.\nDecrease pancreatic exocrine and endocrine function.\nParacrine inhibition of insulin and glucagon secretion from α\nand β -cells of the Islets of Langerhans\nDecrease blood glucose concentration.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
b3ad7a4f-30fd-4c41-9b46-f413dded9f0a	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	large-intestine	Large Intestine	article	Large intestine\nThe large intestine is the terminal portion of the\ngastrointestinal tract and is approx. 1.5 m (5 ft) long. The large\nintestine is divided into the cecum and appendix, the\nascending colon, transverse colon, descending colon,\nsigmoid colon, rectum and anal canal.\nThe large intestine has 3 primary functions: absorbing water\nand electrolytes, producing and absorbing vitamins, and\nforming and propelling feces toward the rectum for\nelimination. By the time indigestible materials have reached\nthe colon, most nutrients and up to 90% of the water has\nbeen absorbed by the small intestine.\nMotility\nThe intestinal wall is made up of multiple layers. The 4 layers of\nthe large intestine from the lumen outward are the mucosa,\nsubmucosa, muscular layer, and serosa. The muscular layer is\nmade up of 2 layers of smooth muscle, the inner, circular layer,\nand the outer, longitudinal layer. These layers contribute to the\nmotility of the large intestine. There are 2 types of motility\npresent in the colon, haustral contraction and mass movement.\nHaustra are saccules in the colon that give it its segmented\nappearance. Haustral contraction is activated by the presence\nof chyme and serves to move food slowly to the next haustra,\nalong with mixing the chyme to help with water absorption.\nMass movements are stronger and serve to move the chyme to\nthe rectum quickly.\nAbsorption of water and electrolytes\nAbsorption of water occurs by osmosis. Water diffuses in\nresponse to an osmotic gradient established by the\nabsorption of electrolytes. Sodium is actively absorbed in the\ncolon by sodium channels. Potassium is either absorbed or\nsecreted depending on the concentration in the lumen and the electrochemical gradient created by the active\nabsorption of sodium.\nChloride and bicarbonate - the movement of sodium into the\nplasma produces an electrochemical gradient to allow\nabsorption of chloride. Chloride ions are exchanged for\nbicarbonate ions (causing net bicarbonate secretion).\nProduction/Absorption of Vitamins\nThe colon houses trillions of bacteria that protect our gut and\nproduce vitamins. The bacteria in the colon produce\nsubstantial amounts of vitamins by fermentation. Vitamin K\nand B vitamins, including biotin, are produced by colonic\nbacteria. These vitamins are then absorbed into the blood.\nWhen dietary intake of these vitamins is low in an individual,\nthe colon plays a significant role in minimizing vitamin\ndisparity.\n\n![Large Intestine - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/large-intestine/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Large Intestine — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
8780b432-ded7-477d-9742-eedc5e72016e	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	liver	Liver	article	Liver\nLargest gland in the body\nWeight: 1.2-1.5 kg in adults (2.6-3.3 pounds)\nWedge-shaped\nConsists of four lobes: Right (largest), Left, Quadrate and\nCaudate\nTypically divided into 8 segments\nSurrounded by the hepatic capsule (two layers)\nOuter serous layer derived from peritoneum, which covers\nthe entire liver (except the bare area of the liver)\nFibrous inner layer (the Glisson capsule) that covers the\nentire liver (including the bare area of the liver, the hepatic\nartery, portal vein, and bile ducts\nPorta hepatis structures: Common hepatic duct (leaving),\nHepatic artery proper (entering), Hepatic portal vein\n(entering) Hepatic nerve plexus and lymphatic vessels.\nLocation: Under the diaphragm in the right upper abdomen\nFunctions of liver\nEnergy metabolism\nGlycolysis\nBeta-oxidation\nAmino acid degradation\nLipid metabolism\nSynthesis\nGluconeogenesis\nProduction of ketone bodies that provide energy for the\nmuscles and the brain: fatty acids and amino acids →\nacetoacetate + beta-hydroxybutyrate.\nSynthesis of plasma proteins (e.g., albumin, globulins,\nclotting factors, acute-phase reactants such as ferritin, CRP,\nand hepcidin)\n\nCholesterol biosynthesis\nFatty acid synthesis\nBile acid synthesis\nVitamin D: conversion of vitamin D3 and D2 to 25-OH (which\nis subsequently transformed to 1,25-(OH)2, the active form of\nvitamin D, in the kidney)\nRegulation\nGlucose homeostasis\nRaise blood glucose via: Glycogenolysis and\nGluconeogenesis\nLower blood glucose via: Glycogen synthesis and Glycolysis\nStorage\nGlycogen\nLipoproteins\nFat-soluble vitamins (A, K, E, D), folate, and vitamin B12\nIron and copper\nDetoxification and clearance/excretion\nUrea cycle (amino acid metabolism): Liver produces urea,\nwhich is excreted by the kidneys.\nCytochrome-P450 system involved in detoxification.\nBiotransformation: Conversion of lipophilic substances into\nwater-soluble substances, which can then be eliminated with\nthe urine or bile.\nBreakdown of ethanol\nRemoval of unconjugated bilirubin (liver conjugates it with\nglucuronate and excretes it in bile)\nFetal: Site of erythropoiesis from 6 weeks gestation until\nbirth\nImmunological function: The liver is part of the mononuclear\nphagocyte system. Cells involved in immune activity, Kupffer\ncells, are found in high numbers in the liver.	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
d50f182a-14b0-4085-9ab0-b31d5b1ca666	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	hepatitis-virus	Hepatitis - Virus	article	Hepatitis:\nNote: Emergency hepatitis B vaccination\nIf you have been exposed to the hepatitis B virus and have\nnot been vaccinated before, you should get immediate\nmedical advice, as you may benefit from having the hepatitis\nB vaccine.\nIn some situations, you may also need to have an injection of\nantibodies, called specific hepatitis B immunoglobulin\n(HBIG), along with the hepatitis B vaccine. HBIG should\nideally be given within 48 hours post exposure Hepatitis B\nvaccine in three doses over three months.\nHepatitis-D:\nCo-infection with HBV results in fulminant hepatitis\nSuperinfection with HBV results in fulminant hepatitis or\nchronic hepatitis commonly\nHepatitis-E:\nTransmitted via fecal oral route causes acute fulminant\nhepatitis in pregnant woman.\nNo progression to chronic hepatitis\n\n![Hepatitis - Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/hepatitis-virus/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Hepatitis - Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
af4721d3-eff1-4f23-bb8d-f4fe355ebf83	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	mastication-and-swallowing	Mastication and Swallowing	article	Mouth\nThe primary function of the mouth is eating, and it has a few\nother important functions also.\nFunctions of mouth include:\nIngestion of food materials\nChewing the food and mixing it with saliva\nAppreciation of the taste of the food\nTransfer of food (bolus) to the esophagus by swallowing\nRole in speech\nSocial function such as smiling and other expressions\nMastication (chewing)\nThe process in which food is cut and ground in small pieces\nwith the help of teeth, is called mastication. It is caused by\nchewing reflex.\nChewing reflex\nBolus in the mouth →  reflex inhibition of mastication muscles\n→  lower jaw drops →  Strech reflex of jaw muscles →  rebound\ncontraction of jaw muscles →  closure of teeth- compression of\nbolus again against mouth lining →  again reflex inhibition of\nmastication muscles →  this cycle repeats again and again.\nImportance of chewing\nincrease the surface area of food particles and thus help their\ndigestion by enzymes.\ngrind food to very fine particulate consistency and thus\npreventing excoriation of GIT.\nincrease the ease with which food is emptied from stomach\ninto small intestine.\nDeglutition (swallowing)\nIngested food moves from oral cavity into the stomach by\ndeglutition.\n\nThere are three stages of swallowing: oral, pharyngeal and\nesophageal. Oral stage is voluntary the other two are\ninvoluntary.\nOral stage\nDuring this phase, food is chewed and mixed with saliva to\nform a food bolus, tongue movements then push this food\nbolus upward and backward against the hard palate, forcing\ninto pharynx.\nPharyngeal stage\nOnce the bolus has been moved to the pharynx, pressure\nreceptors are activated in the palate and anterior pharynx.\nThis signals the swallowing center in the brain stem which\ncauses:\nThe base of the tongue retracts, pushing the bolus against\nthe pharyngeal walls.\nLaryngeal muscles contract to close the glottis and elevate\nthe larynx, closing off the airway.\nInhibition of respiration\nThe tip of epiglottis covers over tracheal opening, closing off\nthe airway.\nRaises the larynx.\nCloses the glottis.\nOpens the upper esophageal sphincter.\nThe soft palate is elevated to close the nasopharynx to allow\npassage of food. In addition to this, the true vocal cords close\nto prevent aspiration.\nAfter this, the bolus is moved towards the esophagus via\nperistalsis of the pharyngeal constrictor muscles. Gravity\nmakes very little contribution to this process and the main\nfactors affecting the speed of this are the viscosity and\nvolume of the bolus.\nEsophageal stage\nThe upper third of the esophagus is voluntary skeletal muscle\nand the lower two thirds are involuntary smooth muscle.\nAt the beginning of this phase, Food bolus is propelled down\nthe esophagus by peristalsis (a wave of muscular contraction\nthat pushes the bolus down), the larynx lowers, returning to\nits normal position. The cricopharyngeal muscle then\ncontracts to prevent reflux and respiration begins again.	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
9ba69de6-be54-45b8-b863-72397b881491	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	saliva	Saliva	article	Saliva\nThe body produces around 1.5 liters of saliva every day and it\nplays a vital role in lubricating food, digestion and protecting\nthe oral environment.\nFunction\nSaliva has many important roles within the gastrointestinal\nsystem. Not only does it help to lubricate wet food, but it also\nbegins the digestion of carbohydrates by amylase.\nInitial starch digestion by α -amylase (ptyalin) and initial\ntriglyceride digestion by lingual lipase\nLubrication of ingested food by mucous for bolus formation\nand swallowing\nDissolving of chemicals in food allowing them to interact\nmore efficiently with taste buds.\nProtection of the mouth and esophagus by dilution and\nbuffering of ingested foods\nMaintaining an alkaline environment\nSecretion of antibacterial enzyme lysozyme\nSecretion of IgA to protect against invasion of\nmicroorganisms.\nFormation of saliva:\nAbout 1-2 L of saliva is produced per day, and almost all is\nswallowed and absorbed.\nThere are three paired salivary glands that are responsible for\nthe production of saliva, the submandibular glands, the\nsublingual glands and the parotid glands. They are all\nducted, exocrine glands. The structure of each gland is like a\nbunch of grapes. The acinus (the blind end of each duct) is\nlined with acinar cells and secretes an initial saliva. A\nbranching duct system is lined with columnar epithelial cells,\nwhich modify the initial saliva. When saliva production is\nstimulated, myoepithelial cells, which lines the acinus and\ninitial ducts, contract and eject saliva into the mouth.\n\nEach of the glands produces a slightly different secretion.\nThe parotid glands produce serous saliva: a watery secretion\nthat is rich in enzymes. The secretion from the sub-lingual\nglands is known as mucous saliva; a viscous secretion\ncontaining no enzymes and large amounts of mucus. Finally,\nthe sub-mandibular glands produce a mixed secretion, that\nis both serous and mucous.\nComposition of saliva\nSaliva is hypotonic and alkaline and contains a mixture of both\norganic and inorganic constituents. Saliva composition varies\naccording to rate and site of production, but the main\ncomponents are:\nWater (99%)\nElectrolytes: sodium and chloride ions are usually at a lower\nconcentration than plasma (hypotonic). Potassium, calcium\nand iodide ions are usually at a higher concentration than\nplasma (hypertonic)\nBicarbonate: present at a higher concentration than in\nplasma to maintain an alkaline environment\nBacteriostats: chemicals that prevent the multiplication of\nbacteria. These include lysozymes, lactoferrin,\nimmunoglobulin A as well as salivary lactoperoxidase.\nMucus- aids to moisten food contents and eases swallowing.\nEnzymes: salivary amylase, lingual lipase and kallikrein (helps\nto produce bradykinin-> a vasodilator)\nRegulation of saliva: The secretion of saliva by each gland is\ncontrolled by two different types of nerves; sympathetic and\nparasympathetic nerves.\nThe parasympathetic outflow is coordinated via centers in the\nmedulla, and innervation occurs via the facial and\nglossopharyngeal nerves. The parasympathetic nerve supply\nis most active during the day, whilst eating and creates\nwaterier, or serous saliva; predominantly produced by the\nparotid gland, and partly by the submandibular gland.\n\nThe parasympathetic system turns up the flow of saliva by\nreleasing a chemical, acetylcholine, which stimulates the\nglands to make more saliva. If these glands get diseased,\ndamaged, or affected by drugs, they may not make enough\nsaliva, leading to dry mouth.\nThe sympathetic control of salivary production is via the\nsuperior cervical ganglion. Sympathetic stimulation results in\nthe release of noradrenaline, which acts upon alpha- and beta-\nadrenergic receptors.\nThe sympathetic nerve supply produces predominantly\nthicker mucous saliva mainly by the sublingual and partly the\nsubmandibular glands. This may occur when in certain\nsituations, fear, stress or anger are aroused. This is also the\ncase during hard physical exercise.\nSaliva production is decrease by inhibition of\nparasympathetic nervous system e.g., sleep dehydration\nanticholinergic drugs (e.g., atropine)\n\n![Saliva - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/saliva/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Saliva — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
0fdfa726-4ca6-44fd-8055-e79f60df8440	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	small-intestine	Small Intestine	article	Small intestine\nThe small intestine (small bowel) is a hollow, tubular\nstructure with an average adult length of 22 feet (7 meters),\nmaking it the longest portion of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract\nand where most of the digestion occurs. The small intestine\nextends from the stomach pylorus to the ileocecal junction\nand is subdivided into three sections: the duodenum,\njejunum, and ileum.\nThe chyme first enters the duodenum is acidic, hypertonic\nand partially digested. There is osmotic movement of water\nacross the freely permeable wall which leads to the contents\nbecome isotonic, and addition of both bicarbonate from\npancreas and bile from the liver which neutralizes the acidity,\nallowing further digestion of chyme performed by addition of\nenzymes from the pancreas, liver and intestine itself.\nFunction of small intestine\nDuodenum\nNeutralization of acidic chyme\nBreakdown of food by digestive pancreatic enzymes and bile\nJejunum and ileum\nAbsorption of water and nutrients by enterocytes\nGlucose and amino acids are co transported with Na+ into\nenterocytes.\nWater is absorbed along the osmotic gradient created by the\nabsorption of Na+.\nBile acids emulsify fats to form micelles, which further\nbreakdown fats into monoglycerides that are then absorbed\nby enterocytes.\nMost nutrients are absorbed in the jejunum.\nVitamin B12 and bile salts are absorbed in the terminal ileum\n(i.e., enterohepatic circulation).\nImmunity\n\nIntestinal villi\nFinger-like projections of mucosa\nShortest in the duodenum\nTallest in the jejunum\nLined by simple columnar epithelium and the specialized\ncells of the small intestine.\nEach villus has an arteriole, venule and lymphatic channel at\nits core.\nEach villus contains a single, blinded ended lymphatic vessel\ncalled a lacteal and also a capillary network, via which\nnutrients are absorbed.\nMicrovilli\nMinute projections from the epithelial cells of the villi\nGive the small intestinal mucosal surface a "brush border"\nappearance.\nHave a superficial layer of glycocalyx which contain many\nenzymes which are involved in digestion and absorption.\nAbsorption of water\nMost of the water in ingested food and beverages is absorbed\nby osmosis.\nApproximately 9 L of water is absorbed from GIT tract per\nday, most of it from small intestine, especially in the\njejunum, 80% is absorbed by the small intestine, 10% by the\nlarge intestine and the remaining 10% excreted in the feces.\nMost absorption of water and electrolytes occurs in the small\nintestine, with some water absorbed in the colon as well.\nWater absorption is dependent on the absorption of solutes\nsuch as (Na+ and Cl−). Na+ is absorbed from the intestinal\nlumen, most use the cotransport with glucose and amino\nacids and the Na+/H exchange, which allow Na+ ions to move\nfrom the lumen into the enterocyte. Na+ is rapidly removed\nfrom the enterocyte via the Na+ pumps, allowing water via\nosmosis to follow either transcellularly or paracellularly\n(between the tight junctions of the enterocytes). Water as\nwell as Na+ ions can then diffuse into the capillaries.	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
0ee26da1-440b-42dd-adc8-54d3786b7c0e	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	summary-of-harmones-and-secretion-of-git	Summary of Harmones and Secretion of GIT	article	Summary of hormones and\nsecretion of git\nGastric secretory and Regulatory\nproducts\nSmall intestine regulatory products:\n\n![Summary of Harmones and Secretion of GIT - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/summary-of-harmones-and-secretion-of-git/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Summary of Harmones and Secretion of GIT — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Summary of Harmones and Secretion of GIT - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/summary-of-harmones-and-secretion-of-git/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Summary of Harmones and Secretion of GIT — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
5aece4b8-0839-46db-9bdb-669a9f289d73	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	vitamins-overview	Vitamins Overview	article	Vitamins\n\n![Vitamins Overview - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/vitamins-overview/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Vitamins Overview — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	19	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
98af67d9-c835-4dee-a990-36d8e8a74082	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	adrenal-gland	Adrenal Gland	article	Adrenal gland\nAdrenal glands are paired of endocrine glands located on the\nsuperior pole of each kidney. they are composed of an inner\nmedulla and an outer cortex, which is turn divided in to three\nzones.\nThe adrenal medulla is composed of chromaffin cells, which\nsynthesize and secrete catecholamines (i.e., epinephrine, nor-\nepinephrine).\nThe adrenal cortex has three zones, responsible for the\nsynthesis of three different types of steroids.\nZona glomerulosa = outermost zone; synthesizes\nmineralocorticoid (Aldosterone)\nZona fasciculata = middle zone; synthesizes glucocorticoid\n(cortisol)\nZona reticularis = innermost zone; synthesizes sex steroids\n(Estrogens & Androgens)\nHypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal Axis:\nCorticotropin releasing hormone (CRH) is produced by\nhypothalamus.\nCRH stimulates anterior pituitary to produce\nadrenocorticotropin hormone (ACTH)\nACTH acts on all zones of adrenal cortex increasing steroid\nhormone synthesis.\nCortisol inhibits the secretion of CRH from hypothalamus\nand secretion of ACTH from anterior pituitary in a negative-\nfeedback pattern.\nAldosterone is under tonic control by ACTH but is separately\nregulated by the renin-angiotensin system and by potassium.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
31d6b28f-9fd7-46fe-872d-f627eb66f5b3	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	adrenal-medulla-and-pheochromocytoma	Adrenal Medulla and Pheochromocytoma	article	Adrenal medulla\nThe adrenal medulla is composed of chromaffin cells, which\nsynthesize and secrete catecholamines epinephrine and nor-\nepinephrine (adrenaline and noradrenaline) but also has other\nsecretory functions such as the production of dopamine. Both\nadrenaline and noradrenaline are produced from the amino\nacid tyrosine, through multiple reactions.\nCatecholamine function\nMechanism: Catecholamines bind to various adrenergic\nreceptors (see table below) located on different organs and\ntissues. Binding triggers tissue-specific responses. This leads to\nsympathetic activation to prepare the human body for a fight-\nor-flight reaction.\n\n![Adrenal Medulla and Pheochromocytoma - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/adrenal-medulla-and-pheochromocytoma/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Adrenal Medulla and Pheochromocytoma — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nPheochromocytoma\nPheochromocytoma is a type of neuroendocrine tumor that\ngrows from cells called chromaffin cells. These cells produce\nhormones needed for the body and are found in the adrenal\nglands. About 80-85% of pheochromocytomas grow in the\ninner layer of the adrenal gland, called the adrenal medulla.\nSigns and symptoms\ncharacteristic feature of pheochromocytoma is hypertension.\nThis type of hypertension is known as endocrine or secondary\nhypertension.\nheadache, chest pain\ntachycardia, tremors, weight loss, fever\nprofuse sweating, Pallor, blur vision\nHyperglycemia\nnausea, vomiting, constipation\nDiagnosis:\nPheochromocytoma is detected by measuring meta-nephrines\nand vanillylmandelic acid (breakdown products of epinephrine\nand norepinephrine) in urine and catecholamines in plasma.\nTreatment:\nDefinitive treatment is by surgical resection of the tumor.\nPrior to surgery, acute crises are treated medically to control\nthe effects of excess catecholamines.\nMedical therapy consists of:\nAlpha blocker (phenoxybenzamine), followed by\nBeta blocker (propranolol). Beta adrenoceptor blockade can\nbe instituted to control tachycardia.\nBeta blockers must never be given before alpha-blocker as\nthis may cause a paradoxical rise in blood pressure due to\nunopposed a-mediated vasoconstriction.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
cb47d11e-20c5-4662-8637-cbd3a4ef387b	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	aldosterone	Aldosterone	article	Aldosterone\nAldosterone is the main mineralocorticoid steroid hormone\nproduced by the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex in the\nadrenal gland. It is essential for sodium conservation in the\nkidney. It plays a central role in the homeostatic regulation of\nblood pressure, plasma sodium (Na+), and potassium (K+)\nlevels.\nAldosterone release is stimulated by\n\n## Acth\n\nAngiotensin II\nHyperkalemia\nAldosterone release inhibited by\nHypokalemia\nInhibitors of renin angiotensin system (ACE, ARBs)\nDopamine and ANP\nMechanism of action: aldosterone binds to intracellular\nmineralocorticoid receptors in the distal tubule and collecting\nduct of the kidney, inducing protein synthesis and following\nchanges:\n↑ Na+/K+-ATPase in the basolateral membrane →\ntransportation of Na+ out and K+ into the tubule cells\n↑ Apical H+-ATPase →  H+ excretion\n↑ Na+ channels (ENaC; epithelial sodium channel) →  ↑ Na+\nreabsorption\n↑ K+ channels (ROMK; renal outer medullary potassium\nchannel) in the luminal membrane →  ↑ K+ excretion\nEffects\nThis aldosterone induced changes produce a concentration\ngradient →  Na+ reabsorption →  water reabsorption and K+\nsecretion into the urine.\n\nUltimately, these effects →  ↑ blood pressure, hypokalemia\nand ↑ pH level	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
8b73ec0f-8eed-41fb-91a7-927895a7c721	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	adrenal-insufficiency	Adrenal Insufficiency	article	Adrenal Insufficiency\nAdrenal insufficiency is a failure of the adrenal glands to\nproduce adequate amounts of adrenocortical hormone. It\ncan be primary, secondary or tertiary.\nPrimary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease): refers to\nchronic, primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Adrenal cortex)\nwhich leads to\n↓ Glucocorticoids, androgen and ↓ mineralocorticoid\n↑ ACTH (low cortisol levels stimulate ACTH secretion)\nCauses:\nAutoimmune: (autoimmune adrenalitis), which has been\nidentified to be the cause of 80-90% of primary adrenal\ninsufficiency\nCongenital: e.g., congenital adrenal hyperplasia\nInfection: e.g., tuberculosis, AIDS, CMV, histoplasmosis\nIatrogenic: bilateral adrenalectomy, Drugs\nVascular: e.g., hemorrhage from sepsis, adrenal vein\nthrombosis, hypercoagulable states such as heparin-induced\nthrombocytopenia and antiphospholipid syndrome\nNeoplasia: e.g., adenoma (tumor) of the adrenal gland\nDeposition disease: e.g., hemochromatosis, amyloidosis,\nsarcoidosis\nIdiopathic\nSymptoms\nWeakness and fatigability\nOrthostatic hypotension\nNausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, and weight loss\nSkin hyperpigmentation (low cortisol levels stimulate ACTH\nsecretion, ACTH contains the melanocyte stimulating\nhormone fragment): Dark skin.\nBluish-black color around the nipples, mouth, rectum,\nscrotum, or vagina\nFluid loss (dehydration)\nLow blood pressure\nIn women, irregular or no menstrual periods\nPubic and axillary hair in women (caused by deficiency of\nadrenal androgens)\nElectrolyte abnormalities:   (High Yield)\nHyperkalemia\nHyponatremia\nMetabolic acidosis\nHypoglycemia (caused by cortisol deficiency)\nDiagnosis:\nShort ACTH-stimulation Test:  It involves administration of 250\nµg ACTH by IM injection at any time of day.\nNormal = cortisol increase> 500 nmol/1 (> 18 µg/dL)\n\nAdrenal insufficiency = cortisol fails to increase (both primary\n& secondary)\nPrimary adrenal insufficiency will have High ACTH.\nSecondary adrenal insufficiency will have Low ACTH.\nTreatment:\nPrimary adrenal insufficiency:\nGlucocorticoid = hydrocortisone (cortisol) is the drug of\nchoice.\nMineralocorticoid = fludrocortisone\nAndrogen replacement therapy may be recommended for\nwoman.\nThe goal of treatment is to stabilize hormone levels and\nrelieve symptoms.\nComplication\nAdrenal Crisis:\nAddisonian crisis is a life-threatening situation, refers to a\nsevere episode of Addison's disease associated with:\nHypotension, Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia,\nhypoglycemia Dehydration\npale, cold, clammy skin\nsweating\nrapid, shallow breathing, dizziness\npigmentation\nsevere diarrhea and vomiting\nabdominal pain, fatigue and severe muscle weakness\nheadaches\nsevere drowsiness or loss of consciousness\nOften with precipitating infection, trauma, or operation\nTreatment: (Needs urgent medical attention)\nThe 5 S's of adrenal crisis treatment are Salt (0.9% saline.),\nSugar (50% dextrose) Steroid (100mg hydrocortisone IV once,\nthen 200mg over 24 hours), Support (normal saline to\nhypotension and electrolyte abnormalities), and Search (for the\nunderlying\ndisorder)\nSecondary adrenal insufficiency\nThis starts when the pituitary gland doesn't make enough of\nthe hormone ACTH (adrenocorticotropin). As a result, the\nadrenal glands don't make enough cortisol. A lack of the\nhormone ACTH leads to secondary adrenal insufficiency. That\ncan happen if a patient must take certain steroids for a long\ntime due to a health problem. Other causes include:\nPituitary gland tumors\nLoss of blood flow to the pituitary (postpartum pituitary\nnecrosis)\nPituitary gland is removed, or radiation treatment of the\npituitary gland.\n\nHypothalamic tumors\nTreatment\nOnly glucocorticoid.\nMineralocorticoid (Aldosterone) is not given as it is not\nACTH-dependent.\nPrimary v/s Secondary Adrenal insufficiency:\nIn contrast to Addison's disease (primary adrenal\ninsufficiency), secondary adrenal insufficiency is\ncharacterized by:\nDecreased pituitary secretion.\nNo skin hyperpigmentation (because there is deficiency of\n\n## Acth)\n\nno hyperkalemia or metabolic acidosis (because aldosterone\nlevels are normal)\n\n![Adrenal Insufficiency - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/adrenal-insufficiency/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Adrenal Insufficiency — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
91c22372-a0a9-4eec-85e1-fa190f21965a	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	antidiuretic-harmone	Antidiuretic Harmone	article	Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as vasopressin, is a\nsmall peptide hormone which regulates the body's retention\nof water. The synthesis of ADH occurs in the supraoptic and\nparaventricular nuclei in the hypothalamus. It is then\ntransported to the posterior pituitary gland via the\nneurohypophysial capillaries.\nADH regulates the blood pressure (v1 receptors) and serum\nosmolality (v2 receptor). The primary function is serum\nosmolality regulation (ADH decreases serum osmolality and\nincreases urine osmolality) via regulation of aquaporin\nchannel insertion in principal cell of renal collecting duct.\nRegulation of plasma osmolality\nMediated by V2 receptors.\nInsertion of aquaporin channels in the principal cells of the\nrenal collecting duct and DCT\nResults in increased water reabsorption\nRegulation of blood pressure\nMediated by V1 receptors.\nVasoconstrictive effects at higher levels\nIncrease reabsorption of urea in the collecting duct,\nincreases the corticomedullary gradient and facilitates urine\nconcentration.\nADH release is inhibited by atrial natriuretic peptide\n(ANP), which is released by stretched atria in response to\nincreases in blood pressure, as well as alcohol and certain\nmedications.\nRegulation of ADH secretion\n\nClinical relevance\nElevated in SIADH despite plasma hypoosmolality.\nADH deficiency or resistance can lead to diabetes insipidus.\nAlcohol inhibits ADH release and causes diuresis.\n\n![Antidiuretic Harmone - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/antidiuretic-harmone/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Antidiuretic Harmone — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
ab86552e-970c-4969-8d6f-208340746c36	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	calcium-homeostasis	Calcium Homeostasis	article	Calcium homeostasis\nThe physiological role of\ncalcium\nIonized Ca2+ is responsible for stabilizing the resting\nmembrane potential of cells.\n↓ Serum Ca2+ →  ↑ membrane excitability\n↑ Serum Ca2+ →  ↓ membrane excitability\nActs as a second messenger in signaling pathways.\nCofactor for several enzymes (e.g., phospholipase A, gamma-\nglutamyl transferase)\nRequired for the promotion of coagulation pathways.\nTotal and ionized calcium concentrations\nTotal calcium: the total amount of calcium circulating in the\nserum, comprising protein-bound, anion-bound, and ionized\ncalcium.\nApprox. 40% of the total serum calcium is bound to\nproteins (mostly albumin) and is physiologically inactive.\nHypoproteinemia (due to, e.g., nephrotic syndrome, liver\ncirrhosis, severe malnutrition, malabsorption) →  ↓ total Ca2+\nlevel but ionized Ca2+ level is unaffected →  factitious\nhypocalcemia.\npH influences the binding of calcium to serum proteins.\n↑ pH →  ↓ H+ in serum binding to proteins →  ↑ Ca2+ binding\nto proteins →  ↓ ionized Ca2+ concentration compensatory ↑\nPTH\n↓ pH →  ↑ H+ in serum binding to proteins →  ↓ Ca2+ binding\nto proteins →  ↑ ionized Ca2+ concentration compensatory ↓\nPTH\n\nIonized calcium: the calcium fraction that is not bound to\nany proteins but is physiologically active 45% of the total\nserum calcium.\nFunctions as the main regulator of PTH secretion\nExcess causes true hypercalcemia whereas deficiency\ncauses true hypocalcemia.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
0136424f-7312-4f51-ba31-9cd0afbbcdc7	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	cartisol	Cartisol	article	Cortisol\nCortisol is one of the major glucocorticoids synthesized in the\nzona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex. Cortisol secretion is\nregulated by the hypothalamic hormone CRH, and the pituitary\nhormone ACTH, in the hypothalamus-pituitary-adrenal axis.\nCortisol is known as stress hormone involved in the response\nto physical and/or emotional stress.\nFunction\n↑ Increase appetite\nIncrease Blood pressure (. at high concentration, can bind to\nmineralocorticoid (aldosterone) receptors.\nIncrease Insulin resistance (diabetogenic)\nIncreases Gluconeogenesis, Lipolysis, and Proteolysis\n↓ Glucose utilization\n↓ Fibroblast activity (poor wound healing,\n↓ collagen synthesis\n↑ striae\n↓ Inflammatory and Immune response\nInhibit WBC adhesion Neutrophilia.\nBlocks histamine release from mast cells\nDecrease Bone formation.\nDecrease osteoblastic activity.\nRegulation\nCRH (hypothalamus) stimulates ACTH release (pituitary) →\ncortisol production in adrenal zona fasciculata. Excess cortisol\n↓ CRH, ACTH, and cortisol secretion by negative feedback\nmechanism	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
c9107699-a9ac-4a1b-b43b-9eacd655b02a	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	diabetes-insipidus-and-siadh	Diabetes Insipidus and Siadh	article	Diabetes insipidus (DI):\nIt is a condition characterized by inability to produce\nconcentrated urine due to ADH dysfunction, It is of two types:\nCentral Diabetes Insipidus (CDI)\nNephrogenic Diabetes Insipidus (NDI)\nCentral Diabetes insipidus\nIt is due to deficiency of ADH. It may be caused by:\nHead trauma\nTumors of hypothalamus\nInflammation of hypothalamus\nCraniopharyngioma\nNephrogenic Diabetes insipidus It is due to\nunresponsiveness (resistance) of collecting tubules to ADH. It\nmay be caused by:\nLithium\nDemeclocycline\nHypokalemia, hypercalcemia\nNephrocalcinosis\nDiagnosis\nCDI = ADH level is low\nNDI = ADH level is high\nDecreased urine osmolality: excretion of large volumes of\ndilute urine (<300 m0sm/kg)\nIncreased serum osmolality (>295 m0sm/kg)\nIncreased serum sodium (hypernatremia), which results in:\nExcessive thirst and polydipsia.\nNeurologic features like confusion, disorientation, seizures\nAn ADH stimulation test can distinguish between cranial and\nnephrogenic DI, as nephrogenic DI shows an inability to concentrate urine even after administration of synthetic\n\n## Adh.\n\nTreatment\nCentral DI = Desmopressin (ADH analogue)\nNephrogenic DI.\nCorrect potassium and calcium\nStop offending drugs.\nThiazide diuretics (e.g., Bendroflumethiazide or amiloride)\nNSAIDs\nSyndrome of Inappropriate ADH Secretion (SIADH)\nIt is characterized by persistent ADH release independent of\nserum osmolality.\nIt causes reabsorption of excess amounts of free water.\nCauses:\nsmall cell carcinoma of lung (most common)\nSarcoidosis\nCNS injury\nTuberculosis\nDrugs (anti-depressants, anti-psychotics)\nDiagnosis:\nIncreased urine osmolality (> 100 mOsm/kg)\nDecreased serum osmolality (< 275 mOsm/kg)\nDecreased serum sodium (hyponatremia).\nurinary sodium excretion > 40 mmol/L\nHyponatremia presents entirely with CNS symptoms\nLethargy; confusion, disorientation, seizures\nTreatment:\nFluid restriction is the cornerstone of SIADH treatment.\nDemeclocycline: ADH receptor antagonist.	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
3953eb12-025d-4622-8f3b-ad407d0f4a03	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	neisseria-spp-bacteria	Neisseria Spp - Bacteria	article	Neisseria species\nNeisseria Gonorrhea and Neisseria meningitis\nGeneral features:\nNeisseria meningitis:\nNeisseria meningitis is gram negative encapsulated\ndiplococci causing Neisseria meningitis and meningococcal\nsepsis transmitted via respiratory droplet\nNeisseria meningitidis is the most common cause of\nbacterial meningitis.\nClinical features\ninclude headache, nausea, vomiting, photophobia, lethargy,\nrash, multiple organ failure, shock, and disseminated\nintravascular coagulation.\nDiagnosis is clinical, confirmed by blood culture and CSF\naspirate.\nBacterial meningitis typically reveals cloud turbid\nappearance of CSF, Neutrophil predominant, High protein\nand low glucose (<40mg/dl)\nThe most important to remember here:\n\n![Neisseria Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/neisseria-spp-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Neisseria Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nTreatment:\nIf meningococcal disease is suspected out of hospital,\npatients should receive a single dose of parenteral (IM or IV)\nbenzylpenicillin as soon as possible or initial blind therapy in\nhospital\nIn Hospital when diagnosis is made Ceftriaxone antibiotic of\nchoice\n\n![Neisseria Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/neisseria-spp-bacteria/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Neisseria Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
70def094-a0d5-4421-a6ae-35eb5366429f	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones	Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones	article	Endocrine System\nIntroduction: All the physiological activities of the body are\nregulated. by two major systems\n\n### 1. Nervous system\n\n### 2. Endocrine system.\n\nEndocrine glands: Endocrine glands are glands which\nsynthesize and release classical hormones into the blood.\nEndocrine glands are also called ductless glands because the\nhormones secreted by them are released directly into blood\nwithout any ducts. Endocrine glands are distinct from exocrine\nglands which release their secretions through ducts. Endocrine\nglands play an important role in homeostasis and control of\nvarious other activities in the body through their hormones.\n\nEndocrinology hormones overview:\nHarmons secreted by adrenal gland:\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nHormone secreted by Gonads.\nHormone secreted by other organs.\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_03_p3.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/endocrine-glands-and-their-harmones/figure_04_p4.png)\n*Endocrine Glands and Their Harmones — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
5b26b6e1-a8bd-4e92-a73e-183e618b1814	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	hyperaldosteronism-and-renal-insufficiency	Hyperaldosteronism and Renal Insufficiency	article	Hyperaldosteronism\nIt is a small group of disorders characterized by excessive\nsecretion of aldosterone (mineralocorticoid).\nIt is classified into primary and secondary\nhyperaldosteronism.\nPrimary Hyperaldosteronism\nIt refers to hyperaldosteronism associated with HIGH\naldosterone and LOW renin.\nCauses\nAldosterone-secreting adenoma (Conn Syndrome)\nAdrenocortical carcinoma\nFamilial hyperaldosteronism.\nDiagnosis:\nHypertension\nHeadache, muscle weakness\nPolyuria, polydipsia; no peripheral edema\nLab Findings:\nHypernatremia (elevated serum sodium)\nHypokalemia\nMetabolic alkalosis\nElevated aldosterone and decreased plasma renin.\nPlasma aldosterone: renin ratio > 20.\nSodium suppression test = fails to suppress aldosterone after\nsodium load.\nTreatment\nSpironolactone:  It is an aldosterone antagonist used to\nnormalize whole-body electrolyte balance\nFollowed by surgical removal (adrenalectomy) which is\ndefinitive treatment.\nSecondary Hyperaldosteronism\nIt refers to hyperaldosteronism associated with HIGH\naldosterone and HIGH renin.\nCauses:\nRenal artery stenosis\nCongestive heart failure\nCirrhosis\nNephritic syndrome\nPregnancy (estrogen increases renin substrate)\nRenin-secreting renal\ntumor\n\nLab Findings:\nElevated aldosterone\nElevated plasma renin\nTreatment:\nSpironolactone	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
1b31c423-73d5-4897-8f89-d2afc6c1723d	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	hypothyroidism-and-hyperthyroidism	Hypothyroidism and Hyperthyroidism	article	Hypothyroidism and\nHyperthyroidism:\n\n![Hypothyroidism and Hyperthyroidism - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/hypothyroidism-and-hyperthyroidism/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Hypothyroidism and Hyperthyroidism — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nNote: Propranolol is the preferred agent in hyperthyroidism\nand thyroid storm due to its additional effect of blocking the\nperipheral conversion of inactive T4 to active form T3. They are\nhighly effective for symptomatic relief (tachycardia, tremors)	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
f5a28f47-db42-46a1-9179-5e3601a17d7e	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	insulin-and-glucagon	Insulin and Glucagon	article	Insulin\nInsulin is a polypeptide hormone secreted by the β  cells of\nthe pancreatic islets of Langerhans, containing an A chain\nand a B chain joined by disulfide bridges.\nProinsulin is synthesized as a single chain peptide. Within\nstorage granules, a connecting peptide (c peptide) is\nremoved by proteases to yield insulin. The c peptide is\npackaged and secreted along with insulin, and its\nconcentration is used to monitor beta cell function in\ndiabetic patients who are receiving exogenous insulin\nRegulation of insulin secretion\nGlucose is a major factor that regulates insulin secretion.\nInsulin is released from the beta cells primarily in response to\nthe elevated blood glucose levels and increased\nparasympathetic stimulation associated with digestion of a\nmeal. Increased blood levels of certain amino acids also\nstimulate insulin secretion.\nDecreased insulin secretion results from decreasing blood\nglucose levels and from stimulation of the pancreas by the\nsympathetic division of the nervous system, which occurs\nduring physical activity. Decreased insulin levels allow blood\nglucose to be conserved to provide the brain with adequate\nglucose and to allow other tissues to metabolize fatty acids\nand glycogen stored in the cells.\n\nFunction of insulin\nInsulin can be considered the "anabolic"or building hormone it\nassists processes that build compounds for storage and\ndecrease processes that break down those storage reserves.\nIn muscle and liver, insulin increases glycogenesis and\ndecreases glycogenolysis.\nDecreased gluconeogenesis in the liver.\nIncreased glycolysis in liver and adipose tissue.\nDecreased the breakdown of amino acids in the liver.\nIncreased amino acid uptake and protein synthesis in\nmuscle, liver, and adipose tissue (decrease blood amino acid\nconcentration)\nDecreased lipolysis (decrease blood fatty acid and ketoacid\nconcentration)\nIncreased lipogenesis and esterification of fatty acids in the\nliver and adipose tissue.\ninsulin increase K+ uptake into cells, thereby decreasing\nblood K+\nGlucagon\nGlucagon is produced by α  cells, located peripherally with in\nislets of Langerhans, in the endocrine tissue of pancreas.\nRegulation of glucagon secretion\nThe major factor that regulates glucagon secretion is the\nblood glucose concentration. Decreased blood glucose\nstimulates glucagon secretion.\n\n![Insulin and Glucagon - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/insulin-and-glucagon/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Insulin and Glucagon — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nIncreased blood amino acids stimulate glucagon secretion,\nwhich prevents hypoglycemia caused by unopposed insulin\nin response to a high protein meal.\nRegulation of glucagon secretion\nFunction of glucagon\nGlucagon acts on the liver and adipose tissue.\nThe second messenger for glucagon is CAMP.\nEffects of Glucagon:\nIncreased glycogenolysis\nDecreased glycogenesis.\nIncreased gluconeogenesis\nIncreased ketogenesis\nincreases lipolysis in adipose tissue.\nDifference between Insulin and Glucagon\n\n![Insulin and Glucagon - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/insulin-and-glucagon/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Insulin and Glucagon — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Insulin and Glucagon - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/insulin-and-glucagon/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Insulin and Glucagon — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
8ac38010-235f-4de3-b5bd-3e68951ced56	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	pancreas	Pancreas	article	Pancreas\nThe pancreas is a retroperitoneal, abdominal organ found\nposterior and inferior to the stomach The pancreas has two\nessential and very important functions in the body:\nendocrine (production of hormones that regulate blood\nsugar levels and glandular secretion) and exocrine (the\nfunction of the digestive gland).\nEndocrine activity is performed by the Langerhans islets and\ninvolves the production of hormones. The Islets of\nLangerhans contain the following cell types, each of which\nproduce different endocrine hormone.\n\n![Pancreas - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/pancreas/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Pancreas — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
51687b1c-b59e-49ee-875d-e9fcf027fcc5	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	parathyroid-harmone	Parathyroid Harmone	article	Parathyroid hormone (PTH)\nParathyroid hormone is secreted from chief cells of\nparathyroid gland which is located immediately behind the\nthyroid gland.\nIt is a major hormone for regulation of serum calcium.\nRegulation pf PTH\nPTH secretion is stimulated by:\nDecrease in serum Ca2+\nHyperphosphatemia\nLow calcitriol levels\nPTH secretion is inhibited by:\nIncrease in serum Ca2+\nDecrease in serum phosphate.\nhypomagnesemia\nAction of PTH\nAre coordinated to produce an increase serum (Ca+) and\ndecrease in serum phosphate.\nthe second messenger for PTH actions on its target tissue\nis cAMP.\nBone: PTH increases bone resorption, which brings both Ca²+\nand phosphate from bone mineral into the ECF (Stimulate\nthe osteoclast cells)\nkidney\nIncreases Ca2+ reabsorption in the distal tubule.\nDecreases phosphate reabsorption in the proximal\nconvoluted tubules.\nIncreases active vitamin D (calcitriol) production via\nstimulation of 1-alpha-hydroxylase synthesis in the kidneys.\nGIT\n\nPTH increases intestinal Ca+ and phosphate absorption,\nindirectly by stimulating the production of 1,25-\ndihydroxycholecalciferol in the kidney.	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
728f57fb-307b-49bf-a8a3-8728673f513e	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	pituitary-gland	Pituitary Gland	article	Pituitary gland\nThe pituitary endocrine gland, which is located in the bony\nSella turcica, is attached to the base of the brain and has a\nunique connection with the hypothalamus. The pituitary gland\nconsists of two anatomically and functionally distinct regions,\nthe anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) and the posterior lobe\n(neurohypophysis).\nThe pituitary gland is connected to the hypothalamus through\na stalk of blood vessels and nerves (the pituitary stalk). Through\nthat stalk, hypothalamus communicates with the anterior\npituitary lobe via hormones and the posterior lobe through\nnerve impulses. The hypothalamus also creates oxytocin and\nantidiuretic hormone and tells posterior pituitary when to store\nand release these hormones. hypothalamus makes the\nfollowing hormones to communicate with and stimulate\npituitary gland:\nCorticotrophin-releasing hormone (CRH).\nDopamine.\nGonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH).\nGrowth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH).\nSomatostatin.\nThyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH).\nSince your pituitary gland and hypothalamus work together so\nclosely, if one of them becomes damaged, it can affect the\nhormonal function of the other.\n\n#### Anterior lobe of pituitary\n\ngland\nThe anterior pituitary is derived from oral ectoderm (Rathke's\npouch). The anterior lobe of pituitary gland is linked to\nhypothalamus by the hypothalamic-pituitary hypophysial portal system. Thus, blood from hypothalamus that contain\nhigh concentration of hypothalamic hormone is delivered\ndirectly to the anterior pituitary. Hypothalamic hormone (e.g.,\ngrowth hormone releasing hormone GHRH) then stimulate or\ninhibit the anterior pituitary hormone (e.g., growth hormone\nGH)\nThe following hormone secreted from anterior pituitary gland:\nFollicle-stimulating hormone (FSH\nLuteinizing hormone (LH)\nProlactin\nGrowth hormone\n\n## (Gh)\n\nAdrenocorticotropin (ACTH)\nThyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\n\n#### Anterior pituitary hormone\n\nPosterior pituitary gland (neurohypophysis)\nThe posterior lobe of pituitary gland arises from an evagination\nof neuroectoderm from diencephalon. It stores and releases\nvasopressin (antidiuretic hormone or ADH) and oxytocin,\nboth made in hypothalamus (supra optic nuclei and\nparaventricular nuclei) and transported to posterior pituitary\nvia neurophysins (carrier protein)\n\n![Pituitary Gland - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/pituitary-gland/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Pituitary Gland — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nHormones of posterior pituitary gland\n\n![Pituitary Gland - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/pituitary-gland/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Pituitary Gland — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
83bfabfc-730b-4844-a0d8-e65a414cd63c	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	vitamin-d-synthesis	Vitamin - D Synthesis	article	Vitamin D synthesis\nVitamin D is a long-term regulator of serum calcium. Its main\nfunction is to increase the intestinal absorption of calcium.\nIt can be ingested, or synthesized from a cholesterol precursor\nas follows:\nLiver: cholesterol →  7-dehydrocholesterol (provitamin D3)\nSkin Storage of 7-dehydrocholesterol, Cleavage of 7-\ndehydrocholesterol via irradiation with UV light →\ncholecalciferol (in the stratum Basale)\nLiver: hydroxylation of cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxyvitamin D\nKidneys: 25-hydroxyvitamin D → 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D\nwhich is the active form of vitamin D\nRegulation of vitamin D synthesis: via regulation of 1 α -\nhydroxylase activity in proximal convoluted tubule\n↓ Calcium, ↓ phosphate, and ↑ PTH →  ↑ 1 α -hydroxylase\nactivity →  ↑ 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D biosynthesis\n↑ Calcium, ↑ phosphate, and ↑ 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D\n(feedback inhibition) →  ↓ 1 α -hydroxylase activity →  ↓ 1,25-\n\n![Vitamin - D Synthesis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/vitamin-d-synthesis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Vitamin - D Synthesis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\ndihydroxyvitamin D biosynthesis	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
df9c0b33-1c4a-44d8-8ecf-20d787b3d70f	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	acquired-coagulation-disorder-of-blood	Acquired Coagulation Disorder of Blood	article	Acquired coagulation disorder:\n\n### 1: Vitamin K Deficiency\n\n### 2: Liver disease: Factors synthesized in liver V, VII, IX, X, XI and\n\nXII\nAbsence of these factors contribute to bleeding tendency.\n3.Disseminated intravascular Coagulation:\nDIC is characterized by a widespread inappropriate\nintravascular deposition of fibrin with consumption of\ncoagulation factors and platelets.\nCause: This may occur as a consequence of many disorders\nthat release procoagulant material into the circulation or cause\nwidespread endothelial damage or platelet aggregation.\nLab values of Disseminated intravascular.\n\n![Acquired Coagulation Disorder of Blood - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/acquired-coagulation-disorder-of-blood/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Acquired Coagulation Disorder of Blood — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLow platelet count\nLow fibrinogen concentration\nProlonged PT\nProlonged PT\nProlonged APTT\nHigh levels of D-Dimers\nFeatures of hemolysis\nCoagulation tests:\n\n![Acquired Coagulation Disorder of Blood - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/acquired-coagulation-disorder-of-blood/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Acquired Coagulation Disorder of Blood — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
d7fb6af4-46b7-40e0-bc6b-36147f2b7fba	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	antiplatelet-and-anticoagulant-drugs	Antiplatelet and Anticoagulant Drugs	article	Antiplatelet and anticoagulant\ndrugs\n\n![Antiplatelet and Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/antiplatelet-and-anticoagulant-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Antiplatelet and Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
9fe08b30-5602-4727-8610-71cafa9208c0	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	haemoglobin	Haemoglobin	article	Haemoglobin\nRed blood cells contain the specialised protein haemoglobin\nwhich is responsible for carrying oxygen and carbon dioxide\nin the blood.\nHaemoglobin is composed of four polypeptide globin chains\neach with its own iron containing haem molecule.\nSynthesis of Haemoglobin\nHaemoglobin synthesis occurs in immature red blood cells.\nHaem synthesis occurs largely in the mitochondria by a\nseries of biochemical reactions commencing with the\ncondensation of glycine and succinyl coenzyme A under the\naction of the key rate-limiting enzyme delta-aminolaevulinic\nacid (ALA) synthase; ultimately protoporphyrin combines\nwith iron in the ferrous (Fe2+) state to form haem.\nThe globin chains are synthesized by ribosomes in the\ncytosol.\nHaemoglobin Degradation\nRed cells are destroyed by macrophages in the liver and\nspleen after 120 days.\nThe haem group is split from the haemoglobin and\nconverted to biliverdin and then bilirubin. The iron is\nconserved and recycled to plasma via transferrin or stored in\nmacrophages as ferritin and haemosiderin. An increased rate\nof haemoglobin breakdown results in excess bilirubin and\njaundice.\nTypes of Hemoglobin\nThere are three types of haemoglobin in normal adult blood:\nhaemoglobin A, A2 and F.\nFetal HB has high affinity for o2 as compared to adult.\n\n![Haemoglobin - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/haemoglobin/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Haemoglobin — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
33dbd7ef-8e53-469c-9cd5-4e98c0b315ad	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	heriditary-coagulation-disorder-of-blood	Heriditary Coagulation Disorder of Blood	article	Coagulation disorder\nHereditary coagulation disorder:\n\n![Heriditary Coagulation Disorder of Blood - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/heriditary-coagulation-disorder-of-blood/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Heriditary Coagulation Disorder of Blood — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
6b743c3a-5aa8-4a64-ae83-0759a0f7c54a	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	plateletes-bleeding-disorder	Plateletes Bleeding Disorder	article	Platelets bleeding disorder\nThrombocytopenia or abnormal platelet function is\ncharacterized by spontaneous skin purpura, mucosal\nhaemorrhage and prolonged bleeding after trauma.\nImportant to note here difference between\nTTP and haemolytic uremic syndrome.\nNote: Difference between TTP and haemolytic uremic\nsyndrome\n\n![Plateletes Bleeding Disorder - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/plateletes-bleeding-disorder/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Plateletes Bleeding Disorder — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Plateletes Bleeding Disorder - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/plateletes-bleeding-disorder/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Plateletes Bleeding Disorder — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
6d1508b6-2f87-4f49-b72c-2f893c9c3180	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	haemostasis	Haemostasis	article	Haemostasis\nPrevention of blood loss\nStages of haemostasis: Three stages\nVasoconstriction\nPlatelet plug formation.\nCoagulation of blood\n\n![Haemostasis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/haemostasis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Haemostasis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
9b47d055-92bc-4123-bbe3-916b31996529	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	sites-of-haemopoiesis	Sites of Haemopoiesis	article	Haemopoiesis Sites:\nImportant to remember:\nHaemopoietic Stem Cells: All blood cells\ndevelop from Haemopoietic Stem Cells\n\n![Sites of Haemopoiesis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/sites-of-haemopoiesis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Sites of Haemopoiesis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Sites of Haemopoiesis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/sites-of-haemopoiesis/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Sites of Haemopoiesis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
0d6ba367-a7b7-48a6-8350-e8f0fe4c631e	69aa3acc-66d4-42b4-92ed-670a190ab74d	anaphylaxis-management	Anaphylaxis Management	article	- Anaphylaxis\n>It's a severe, life-threatening, generalized or systemic\nhypersensitivity reaction, characterized by rapidly developing\nand life-threatening airway, breathing and/or circulation\nproblems, usually associated with skin and mucosal changes.\n- Causes\n- Medication:\n>Antibiotic (penicillin most common)\n\n## >Nsaid\n\n>Aspirin\n\n## >Acei\n\n>Anesthetic agent e.g.: muscle relaxant (Suxamethonium and\nrocuronium) and contrast media\n- Others\n>Food: e.g.: peanut, egg seafood (most common in children).\n>Insect sting\n\n![Anaphylaxis Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/anaphylaxis-management/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anaphylaxis Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n>latex\n>idiopathic\n>C1 esterase etc.\n- Management\n- Key points\n- Anaphylactic likely when following 3 criteria match:\n> 1: Acute onset of illness with a sudden progression.\n> 2: Life-threatening airway and/or breathing and/or circulation\nproblem.\n> 3: Possible skin or mucosal changes (although these may be\nabsent and are not required for diagnosis).\n>there is no role of antihistamine however it may\nhelp alleviate cutaneous symptoms.\n>The routine use of corticosteroids to treat anaphylaxis is not\nadvised. If the patient is suffering from ongoing asthma/shock\nafter initial resuscitation steroids may be considered.\n>Use glucagon in patients who are taking beta blockers may\nbe resistant to the therapeutic effects of adrenaline.\n>Adrenaline : can give second dose if reaction persist after 5\nminutes interval. After 2 doses, adrenaline infusion should be\nstarted.\n\n![Anaphylaxis Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/anaphylaxis-management/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Anaphylaxis Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n>Nice recommend: consider fast-track discharge after 2 hours\nobservation from resolution of anaphylaxis if : Good response\n(within 5-10 minutes) to a singles dose of adrenaline given\nwithin 30 minutes of onset of reaction and complete resolution\nof symptoms and Pt already has unused adrenaline auto-\ninjectors and has been trained how to use them.\n>6 hours observation: those pt who has 2 doses of IM\nadrenaline or previous biphasic reaction.\n>12 hours observation: those pt who has required more than\ntwo doses of adrenaline, has severe asthma or their reaction\nhas involved severe respiratory compromise.\n>The frequency of food sensitization in asthmatic children is\nhigher than expected in a general population. it has been\nfound that asthma is a risk factor for severe or fatal\nanaphylactic reactions to foods\n>Investigation : resus council recommend 3 tryptase level. First\nas resuscitation started, 2nd 1-2 hours after symptoms started.\n24 hours later.\n- Anaphylactoid vs anaphylaxis:\n>Anaphylactoid reaction: first time exposure to allergen, direct\nrelease of histamine and other mediators from mast cells and\ndoesn't cause degranulation. Non-immune (IgE) mediated\nreaction.\n>Anaphylaxis reaction: On further exposure to that allergen,\nthe responsible allergenic antigen binds to an antigen-specific\nIgE antibody, leading to mast cell degranulation Histamine and\nother mediators, including leukotrienes and cytokines are\nreleased from mast cells and basophils following exposure to\nthis antigen.\n>Summary: anaphylactoid first exposure and non-immune\nmediated\n>Anaphylaxis: further exposure/ Pt has exposed to allergen\npreviously (history) and immune mediated.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
24c3df0e-dd91-47f0-ab31-48d8fc0f6f97	69aa3acc-66d4-42b4-92ed-670a190ab74d	anti-histamine-drugs	Anti - Histamine Drugs	article	Anti histamine:\nBlock H1 histamine receptors\nIndications:\nAllergic rhinitis\nUrticaria\nPruritis\nInsect bites and stings\nAnaphylaxis\nAngioedema\nNausea and vomiting\nMotion sickness and vertigo\nInsomnia\nDivided into 2 groups:\n\n![Anti - Histamine Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/anti-histamine-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Histamine Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
97f6e162-9d0d-492d-86a7-b417eb691f22	69aa3acc-66d4-42b4-92ed-670a190ab74d	bronchodilator-drugs	Bronchodilator Drugs	article	Bronchodilator therapy:\nNote: Important points of theophylline:\nTheophylline has narrow window between therapeutic and\ntoxic doses, and the half-life can be affected by a number of\nfactors, making a therapeutic dose difficult to achieve. It is\nadvised that serum levels of 10-20 mg/I six to eight hours after\nthe last tablet should be aimed for.\nFactors that increase the half-life of theophylline include:\nHeart failure\nCirrhosis\nViral infection\nDrugs (Enzyme inhibitors)\nFactors that decrease the half-life of theophylline include:\nSmoking\nHeavy drinking\nDrugs (Enzyme inducers)\nEnzyme inducers and Enzyme inhibitors\n\n![Bronchodilator Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/bronchodilator-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Bronchodilator Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nAminophylline:\nAminophylline is a compound of theophylline with\nethylenediamine in a 2:1 ratio. The ethylenediamine improves\nits solubility. It is less potent and shorter acting than\ntheophylline.\nIt is used in the treatment of:\nAsthma\n\n## Copd\n\nHeart failure\nBradycardias\nNote: Aminophylline has mild positive inotropic and\nchronotropic effects, producing an increase in cardiac output\nand a decrease in systemic vascular resistance, leading to a\ndecrease in arterial blood pressure. It has been used historically\nin the treatment of refractory heart failure and is\nrecommended by the current ALS guidelines as an alternative\ntreatment for bradycardias.\n\n![Bronchodilator Drugs - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/bronchodilator-drugs/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Bronchodilator Drugs — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
ca7d1b24-c9d8-4f47-b00b-db443afe5a6f	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	anti-platelet-statins-nitrate-and-fibrinolytic-drugs	Anti - Platelet, Statins, Nitrate and Fibrinolytic Drugs	article	Anti-platelet, Statins, nitrate and\nfibrinolytic drugs:\n\n![Anti - Platelet, Statins, Nitrate and Fibrinolytic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anti-platelet-statins-nitrate-and-fibrinolytic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Platelet, Statins, Nitrate and Fibrinolytic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
f69f364f-094a-4399-a829-44c1adef141c	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	anticoagulant-drugs	Anticoagulant Drugs	article	Antiplatelet and anticoagulant\ndrugs\nAnticoagulant drugs\nHeparin:\nHeparin is used for:\nPrevention and treatment of DVT and Pulmonary embolism\nTreatment of disseminated intravascular coagulation .\nHeparin side effects:\nHeparin induced thrombocytopenia .\nBleeding\nOsteoporosis\nAlopecia\nHyperkalemia\nContraindications:\nPeople with active bleeding history or active peptic or\nduodenal ulcer\nPrevious history of Heparin induced thrombocytopenia .\n\n![Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anticoagulant-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLow molecular weight heparin vs Un\nfractioned heparin:\nWarfarin:\nWarfarin is vitamin K antagonist acts by inhibiting vitamin K\ndependent clotting factors (ii, vii, ix and x) or simply\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **remember 27910 (Mnemonic) as well as anticoagulant protein**\nC and S\nIndications:\nProphylaxis and treatment of venous thromboembolism\nProphylaxis in atrial fibrillation\nProphylaxis in people with Mechanical Heart Valves\nSide effects:\nBleeding is the most common adverse feature .\nNarrow Therapeutic Window: Warfarin has a narrow\ntherapeutic window, meaning that even small fluctuations in\ndosage can lead to a significant change in its anticoagulant\neffects. Maintaining the correct INR range is crucial for\noptimal benefit and to avoid complications.\nSkin Necrosis and Purple Toe Syndrome\nNausea, vomiting , Diarrhea, Rash, jaundice, hepatic\ndysfunction and pancreatitis\nWarfarin in pregnancy: If given in 1st trimester may cause fetal\nwarfarin syndrome (nasal hypoplasia, bone stippling, bilateral\noptic atrophy and intellectual disability)\nIf given in 2nd or 3rd trimester can cause optic atrophy,\ncataracts, microcephaly, microphthalmia, intellectual disability\nand fetal and/or maternal haemorrhage.\n\n![Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anticoagulant-drugs/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nContraindications:\nActive bleeding\nActive peptic ulcer\nBleeding disorders (ITP, Haemophilia)\nPregnancy\nRecent trauma or surgery\nMonitoring: Both PT and aPTT are prolonged after warfarin\nadministration but PT is more prolonged. Warfarin therapy is\ntypically monitored using the prothrombin time (PT) test\nalong with the International Normalized Ratio (INR), rather\nthan the activated partial thromboplastin time  (aPTT)  test.\nThe target International Normalized Ratio (INR) value for\nwarfarin therapy varies depending on the medical condition\nbeing treated. Warfarin therapy can be withhold or omitted if\nINR rises above the range. Phytomenadione (Vitamin K)\nantidote used to reverse the bleeding. Dried prothrombin\ncomplex consists of factors ii, vii, ix and x can also be given in\nmajor bleeding if prothrombin complex unavailable give\nfresh frozen plasma\n(High yield )\nManaging the patients with increased INR and bleeding\nIf INR> 8 and no bleeding = Phytomenadione orally\nIf INR> 8 and minor bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv\nIf INR> 8 and major bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv and dried\nprothrombin complex (Recall)\nIf INR 5-8 and no bleeding = Withhold warfarin\nIf INR 5-8 and minor bleeding = Phytomenadione Iv\nMetabolism: Warfarin undergoes extensive metabolism in the\nliver via the cytochrome P450 enzyme system, mainly through\nthe CYP2C9 enzyme.  cytochrome P450 enzyme inducer drugs\nincrease rate of metabolism and hepatic clearance of\nconcurrently administrated drugs which typically results in\ndecreased pharmacologic effects and decreasing\nanticoagulant effects of warfarin. While enzyme inhibitor\ndrugs decrease hepatic clearance and increase pharmacologic\neffect/increased anticoagulant effects and risk of bleeding.\n\n![Anticoagulant Drugs - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/anticoagulant-drugs/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Anticoagulant Drugs — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
9f18044d-879a-423d-a6be-7293ae5bc0a5	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	bradycardias	Bradycardias	article	Definition\nBradycardia is a heart rate of fewer than 60 beats per minute.\nTypes\nSinus bradycardia\nSinus arrest\nJunctional bradycardia\nAtrioventricular blocks:\nFirst degree\nSecond degree (Mobitz types I and II)\nThird-degree (complete heart block)\nCauses\nNormal physiological variants as seen in athletes\nCardiac causes (ischemic & structural heart disease)\nInfectious\nSystemic illness (hypothyroidism, hypothermia)\nAutonomic mediated (carotid hypersensitivity, vasovagal\nsyncope)\nHead injury\nHyper-hypokalaemia\nDrugs (High yield)\nBeta-blockers\nCCB\nAmitriptyline\nQuinidine\nClonidine\nDigoxin\nOrganophosphate\n\nManagement approach (unstable Pt)\nThe ALS bradycardia algorithm recommends Atropine 500\nmcg IV up to 3mg maximum if any Pt presents with the\nfollowing:\nAdverse features or risk of asystole:\nShock\nSyncope\nMyocardial ischemia\nHeart failure\nRisk of asystole:\nRecent asystole\nMobiztype-2 Av block\nComplete heart block\nVentricular pause >3 seconds\nBradycardia (Resus council of the UK and European\nresuscitation council guideline)\nIf bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock,\nSyncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure as mentioned\nabove) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat\nevery 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\nIf treatment with atropine is ineffective\nconsider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline\n(5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\nConsider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel\nblockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\nConsider pacing in patients who are unstable, with\nsymptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\nIf transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous\npacing.\nAtropine\nAtropine antagonises the action of the parasympathetic\nneurotransmitter acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. It\ntherefore blocks the effects of the vagus nerve on both the SA\nnode and\nthe AV node, increasing sinus automaticity and facilitating AV node conduction.\nSide effects: (Anti-muscarinic effects)\n- Dry mouth\n- Nausea and vomiting\n- Blurred vision\n- Urinary retention\n- Tachyarrhythmias\nNote: Anti-muscarinic should be avoided in Myasthenia\ngravis, paralytic ileus, GI obstruction, Urinary retention and\nprostatic enlargement	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
a371d3da-25f2-4806-848e-105edadc1e55	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	notifiable-disease	Notifiable Disease	article	Diseases notifiable to local authority\nproper officers under the Health\nProtection (Notification) Regulations\n2010:\nAcute encephalitis\nAcute infectious hepatitis\nAcute meningitis\nAcute poliomyelitis\nAnthrax\nBotulism\nBrucellosis\nCholera\n\n## Covid-19\n\nDiphtheria\nEnteric fever (typhoid or paratyphoid fever)\nFood poisoning\nHaemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)\nInfectious bloody diarrhea\nInvasive group A streptococcal disease\nLegionnaires' disease\nLeprosy\nMalaria\nMeasles\nMeningococcal septicaemia\nMonkeypox\nMumps\nPlague\nRabies\nRubella\nSevere Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS)\nScarlet fever\nSmallpox\nTetanus\nTuberculosis\n\nTyphus\nViral hemorrhagic fever (VHF)\nWhooping cough\nYellow fever	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
0129a63a-ddaa-4dd0-ac97-1aac1c738ac4	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support	Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support	article	Basic life support and Advanced life\nsupport algorithms\n- Key difference b/w Paeds &\nadult ALS\n\n![Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n- Identify and treat reversible causes\nHypoxia\nHypovolemia (massive blood/fluid loss).\nHyperkalemia (renal patient).\nHypothermia (Pt may require prolong CPR).\nIntubated Pt (tracheal tube may lie below the cord.\nThrombosis (massive PE if that's the cause of cardiac arrest\nthen consider 50mg alteplase).\nTension pneumothorax following central line insertion.\nCardiac tamponade should always consider in penetration\ninjury.\nAfter ROSC\nUse an ABCDE approach.\nAim for SpO2 of 94-98% and normal PaCO2\n12-lead ECG\nIdentify and treat the cause.\nTargeted temperature management\n\n![Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/basic-life-support-and-advanced-life-support/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Basic Life Support and Advanced Life Support — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
c851661b-757c-4772-96c3-68619ada30e6	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	betablocker-and-calcium-channel-blockers	Betablocker and Calcium Channel Blockers	article	Beta blockers and calcium channel\nblockers overview:\nLabetalol: Labetalol is nonselective alpha and betablocker\ncommonly used to manage high blood pressure, especially in\nhypertensive emergencies\nLabetalol is used in the treatment of pheochromocytoma\ntogether with alpha blocker.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Antiarrhythmic drugs mechanism of action:\n> (High Yield)\n> Adenosine: Opens K+ channels in heart, slowing conduction\n> in the AV node.\n\nDigoxin Inhibits the Na/K ATPase in cardiac myocytes.\n\n![Betablocker and Calcium Channel Blockers - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/betablocker-and-calcium-channel-blockers/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Betablocker and Calcium Channel Blockers — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nFlecainide Blocks Na+ channel in heart, slowing conduction\nof the cardiac impulse.\nLidocaine Blocks Na+ channels in heart, slowing conduction\nof the cardiac impulse.\nSotalol: Blocks beta-adrenoreceptors and K+ channels,\nreducing heart rate and slowing conduction in the AV node.\nVerapamil Blocks Ca2+ channels in heart, slowing conduction\nin the AV node.\nAmiodarone: Blocks Na+ and K+ channels and beta\nadrenoreceptors prolonging phase 3 of cardiac action\npotential and slowing conduction at SA and AV nodes.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
7d3dfdae-bd15-4407-bef6-7a8af7615d7e	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	digoxin	Digoxin	article	Digoxin\nDigoxin is a cardiac glycoside derived from the\nplant foxglove that primarily works by inhibiting the Na+/K+\nATPase in the myocardium.\nMechanism: Digoxin has positive inotropic effects and\nnegative chronotropic effects\nBinds to and inhibits the Na+/K+ pump on the myocyte cell\nmembrane (competes with the K+ ion) Increases\nintracellular Na+ concentration.\nReduces the extrusion of intracellular Ca2+ via the Na+/Ca2+\nexchange transporter Increased Ca2+ is stored in\nthe sarcoplasmic reticulum Increased amounts of Ca2+\nreleased during each action potential force of\ncontraction increased .\nDigoxin also increases vagal tone by enhancing Ach release at\nmuscarinic receptors, thus slowing the heart\nrate, atrioventricular (AV) conduction, and the AV nodal\nrefractory period.\nIndications: Heart failure, persistent and permanent atrial\nfibrillation and flutter.\nNote: In heart failure digoxin is useful in patient with reduced\nejection fraction, with an ejection fraction below 40%. However,\nit has no benefit in mortality reduction.\nDigoxin can only be used as monotherapy in sedentary\npatients because it controls ventricular response at rest.\nMonitoring: Digoxin has narrow therapeutic index.\nNarrow therapeutic index means difference between the\neffective therapeutic dose and the potentially toxic dose is\nrelatively small. This narrow window makes proper dosing\nand monitoring critically important to ensure the medication's\nsafety, effectiveness and to prevent toxicity. The\ntherapeutic plasma levels of digoxin are usually between 1.0-1.5 nmol/l. The likelihood of toxicity rises dramatically at levels\nabove 2 nmol/l.\nDose adjustments in renal failure and liver failure:\nRenal failure: Digoxin is excreted renally, and impaired renal\nfunction can cause increased digoxin levels and leads to\ndigoxin toxicity. In this case the patient should have their\ndigoxin dose reduced and their digoxin level and electrolytes\nshould be carefully monitored.\nLiver failure: Liver failure has little influence on digoxin\nmetabolism or clearance; therefore, no dose adjustment is\nnecessary.\nDigoxin toxicity: Precipitated by hypokalemia or drugs\ncausing hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, acidosis and renal\nfailure.\nThe features of digoxin toxicity include:\nHyperkaliemia\nNausea, vomiting and diarrhea .\nAbdominal pain\nConfusion, dizziness and psychosis\nTachyarrhythmias or bradyarrhythmia's\nXanthopsia (yellow-green vision)\nTreatment: Digoxin immune fab is the reversal agent\n(Antidote) of digoxin toxicity.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
6c2a5ca0-f6eb-4f1f-ac8c-f4db64ad52ab	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	hypertensive-emergency	Hypertensive Emergency	article	Hypertensive Emergency\nA hypertensive emergency is defined as a clinical situation\nin which there is a marked elevation of blood pressure (BP)\nusually >180/120mmhg and associated with acute or\nprogressive end organ damage, e.g. cardiovascular, renal or\nneurological dysfunction.\nThe commonest form of hypertension is essential\nhypertension in which no specific cause can be identified\nbut various risk factors are associated such as : age, family\nhistory, obesity, and salt ingestion.\nSecondary hypertension: is high BP caused by another\nmedical condition such as renal disease, endocrine disorders,\nneurological disease, coarctation of aorta, pregnancy-related\npre-eclampsia, eclampsia and immunological.\nThe most vulnerable organs are CNS, CVS Kidneys and uterus\nin pregnancy.\nClinical features\nPhysical examination\n\n![Hypertensive Emergency - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/hypertensive-emergency/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Hypertensive Emergency — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nCardiovascular finding: Elevated JVP, the difference in pulse\nand BP in limbs, 3rd heart sound, pulmonary crepitation.\nCentral neurological: Reduced level of consciousness/focal\nneurological deficit retinal haemorrhages / papillodema\nManagement\nGeneral approach\nHypertensive urgencies: Most patients presenting to the ED\nwith significantly elevated BP will not demonstrate acute\nprogressive target organ damage so treatment option for\nthem either to moderate increase in their current anti-\nhypertensive drugs or compliance with anti-hypertensive\nmedication should be ensured.\nHypertensive emergency management\nReduce MAP up to 25% in the first hour.\nIf Pt remains stable then further reduce BP to normal range\nover 24hrs\nLabetalol first line therapy in hypertensive\nencephalopathy/malignant hypertension or intracranial\nhaemorrhage\nIV beta blockers and nitrates are choice of agents for HE\ncomplicated with cardiac ischemia/MI.\nstandard treatment (IV nitrates, diuretic, opiates) is the\ntreatment choice for HE with LV failure or pulmonary\noedema.\nBeta-blocker (labetalol atenolol, esmolol) along with nitrates\nor nitroprusside treatment of choice for aortic dissection\nAlpha with Beta blocker (phentolamine and labetalol)\nchoice for phaeochromocytoma\nFenoldopam choice for AKI\nIV labetalol/ hydralazine drug of choice for pre-eclampsia\nand MgSo4 for eclampsia.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
38bfdc16-994c-47ed-aa52-110d6462ac0f	a33921a8-de21-4251-b844-facd6afc8a7e	anti-psychotic-drugs	Anti - Psychotic Drugs	article	Anti-psychotics\nAnti-psychotics acts by blocking dopamine D2 receptors in\nthe brain .\nSide effects:\nExtrapyramidal side effects occur most commonly associated\nwith antiemetic (metoclopramide, prochlorperazine) and anti-\npsychotics (haloperidol). Haloperidol is the most\ncommon causative antipsychotic drug.\nDystonia (abnormal face and body movements) most\ncommonly occur in young female and children treated with\nProcyclidine or benztropine .\nTardive dyskinesia (rhythmic, involuntary movements of\ntongue, face and jaw)\nOther side effects:\nParkinsonian symptoms (Tremor, Rigidity, bradykinesia and\npostural instability)\nHyperprolactinemia (Dopamine inhibits release of prolactin)\nQT-interval prolongation	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
26e233c9-ba28-423f-aeda-654e41bc7191	a33921a8-de21-4251-b844-facd6afc8a7e	anti-emetic-drugs	Anti - Emetic Drugs	article	Antiemetics drug class, Members and\nclinical use:\nMetoclopramide\nMetoclopramide is dopamine receptor antagonist acts directly\non GI tract as prokinetic drug, commonly associated with\nextrapyramidal symptoms induce acute dystonic\nreactions causing fascial and skeletal muscle spasm .\nAntidote: Injection of anti-parkinsonism drug Such as\n"Procyclidine will abort dystonic attack.\nNote: Extrapyramidal symptoms are most commonly\nassociated with antiemetics and antipsychotics\n\n![Anti - Emetic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/anti-emetic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Emetic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
858a668c-2f3b-4a41-a7f9-22647b5783af	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	gastrointestinal-virus	Gastrointestinal Virus	article	Gastrointestinal Virus:\n\n![Gastrointestinal Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/gastrointestinal-virus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Gastrointestinal Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
023f9499-984f-41df-aa70-7d09421688b9	22c0eda6-99c0-4744-ada5-5733ba61fc6b	tachyarrhythmia	Tachyarrhythmia	article	Tachycardias\nTwo Types\nRegular or irregular narrow complex\nRegular or Irregular broad complex\n1small box= 0.04s\n1Large box=0.20s\nPR interval 0.12-0.20\nIn narrow complex tachycardia QRS <0.12s\nIn broad complex QRS>0.12s\nNote: Adenosine is a purine nucleoside acts by stimulating A1-\nadenosine receptors and opening acetylcholine-sensitive\npotassium channels. This hyperpolarizes the cell membrane in\nthe atrio-ventricular (AV) node and, by inhibiting the calcium\nchannels, slows conduction in the AV node.\nContraindications of adenosine (High yield)\nAsthma and COPD\n2nd or 3rd degree AV block\nSick sinus syndrome\nLong QT syndrome\nSevere hypotension\nDecompensated heart failure\n\n![Tachyarrhythmia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cardiovascular-pharmacology-anticoagulants/tachyarrhythmia/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Tachyarrhythmia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nTachycardia Unstable Pt\nAdverse features\nShock (SBP<90, confusion, drowsiness, cold extremities,\npallor).\nSyncope (loss of consciousness)\nMyocardial ischemia (Chest pain, breathlessness, ECG\nchanges)\nHeart failure (Pulmonary edema, raised JVP, Hepatomegaly).\nExtreme heart rate: >150 mostly Pt are unstable.\nManagement\nGive 3 synchronized DC shocks (120-150j for broad complex\nand 70-120 for narrow complex)\nIf not successful, then administer Amiodarone 300mg over 10\nto 20 minutes and repeat the shock.\nFollowed by 900mg over 24hrs.\nNote: Any unstable patient requires electrical cardioversion\nrather than drugs regardless of the underlying rhythm.\nAtrial fibrillation managment\nAF is diagnosed using an ECG which typically shows an\nabsolutely irregular RR interval and absent p waves.\nNICE guideline classification\nParoxysmal AF\nAF terminates spontaneously within seven days of onset and\nmost often within 48 hours of onset.\nPersistent AF\nAF presents continuously for seven days or more or is\nterminated by cardioversion.\nPermanent AF\nAF which is accepted without attempted cardioversion, or\nwhich cannot be terminated by cardioversion.\nPathophysiology\n\nThe primary pathological change seen in AF is progressive\natrial fibrosis.\nProthrombotic state: Structural and functional changes in\nthe atrial myocardium along with stasis of blood especially in\nthe Left atrial appendage allow an environment in which a\nthrombus can form.\nDamage to the myocardium causes the expression of\nprothrombotic factors on the atrial endothelial surface. This\nalong with activation of platelets and inflammatory cells\nleads to clot formation.\nClinical features\nbreathlessness\npalpitations\nsyncope or dizziness\nchest discomfort\nstroke or transient ischaemic attack\nManagement of hemodynamically-\nunstable AF\nDC shock 120-150J should be given if a patient presents\nwith the following adverse features:\nShock\nSyncope/reduce the level of consciousness.\nHeart failure\nMyocardial ischemia\nManagement of previously-\nidentified AF\nIn patients presenting to the ED who are known to have AF\nlong-term (>48 hours). a reason for why they are tachycardic\nshould be sorted. If a secondary/reversible cause has been\nexcluded then rate control should be considered\nNICE recommends rate control (beta blockers and rate-\nlimiting CCB) as the first-line strategy for people with atrial\nfibrillation, except in people:\nwhose atrial fibrillation has a reversible cause.\n\nwho have heart failure thought to be primarily caused by\natrial fibrillation.\nwith new-onset atrial fibrillation (<48hrs)\nFor whom a rhythm control strategy would be more suitable\nbased on clinical judgment\nNote: digoxin can be used in sedentary patients.\nManagement of Recent-onset, Non-\nLife-threatening AF\nIn the ED if a patient presents within 48 hrs. of onset\nconsider either pharmacological or electrical cardioversion.\nElectrical: synchronized DC 120-150J.\nPharmacological: A choice of flecainide in people with no\nevidence of structural or ischemic heart disease.\nAmiodarone should be used in people with evidence of\nstructural heart disease.\nNote: if Pt with AF is uncertain of time or greater than 48\nhrs then anti-coagulate this pt for 3 weeks before\ncardioversion and rate should be controlled with Beta\nblockers.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
36682100-307b-4b52-b444-8a0a6928b6a4	a33921a8-de21-4251-b844-facd6afc8a7e	analgesics	Analgesics	article	Analgesics:\nClassification of analgesics:\nNon opioid analgesics:\nParacetamol: Used in Pyrexia and mild to moderate pain\nSide effects: Rare (rashes, blood disorders)\nMetabolism: Paracetamol metabolized to mainly glucuronide\nconjugates, small amount converted to the toxic NAPQI (N-\nacetyl P-amino-benzoquinoneimine), which is quickly made\nsafe by conjugation with glutathione\nToxic overdose: More NAPQI produced glucuronide pathway\nbecomes saturated leads to cellular death\nand hepatic necrosis.\nAntidote overdose: N-acetylcysteine\nNSAIDS: (Non-Steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs): Class of\nanalgesics used to treat pain, fever and inflammation.\nMechanism: Cyclo-oxygenase (COX) catalyzes the production\nof prostaglandins through the intermediate arachidonic\nacid and Prostaglandins regulate the body's inflammatory\nresponse.\nCOX has 2 isoenzymes:\nCOX-I regulates many physiological processes including\nprotection of gastric mucosa.\nCOX-II is expressed in inflammatory cells.\nNote:  Most NSAIDs inhibit both COX-I and COX-II. Some newer\ndrugs inhibit just COX-II, therefore reducing gastric side\neffects.\nibuprofen (Safest NSAID) but low anti-inflammatory\nproperties not useful in inflammatory conditions.\n\nNaproxen: Good efficacy, lower side effects but greater\nthan ibuprofen. Used in musculoskeletal\ndisorders, Rheumatoid arthritis, Gout and dysmenorrhea.\nDiclofenac: Similar in efficacy to naproxen but greater side\neffects.\nMefenamic acid: Low anti-inflammatory properties but\ngreater side effects which include aplastic anemia\nand hemolytic anemia.\nNote: NSAIDs cause GERD by inhibiting the COX 1 enzymes (cox\n1 stimulation produce prostaglandins that help to maintain the\ngastric mucosal integrity) NSAIDs can cause ulcers by\ninterfering with the stomach's ability to protect itself from\ngastric acid (by inhibiting the function of prostaglandins)\nNSAIDS Adverse effects:\nGl adverse effects (Most common) which include NSAIDS\ninduced peptic ulcer.\nHypersensitivity reactions\nRenal failure contraindicated in reduced GFR\nMI, Heart failure\nTinnitus\nHypertension\nThrombotic events\nFluid retention\nHaemolytic anemia (Mefenamic acid)\nNote: NSAIDs are contraindicated in a patient with heart\nfailure. (Diclofenac is contraindicated in people with ischemic\nheart disease).\nRisk of GI Side effects:\nLowest risk=ibuprofen (Safest NSAID)\nIntermediate risk: Aspirin, naproxen\nHighest risk=piroxicam, ketorolac, indomethacin\nManagement of gout (Important to know\nhere)\nAcute gout: NSAIDS (specially naproxen)\nGout with heart failure: Colchicine\nChronic gout: Allopurinol also reduces further attacks of gout\nby reducing uric acid.\n\nNote: In gout under polarized light, gout crystals appear\nneedle-shaped and yellow, a property called negative\nbirefringence. Gout crystals are monosodium urate crystals\nIn Pseudo gout: Positive birefringence and pyrophosphate\ncrystals.\nColchicine mechanism of action: Colchicine has anti-\ninflammatory properties. It disrupts cytoskeletal functions by\ninhibiting beta-tubulin polymerization into microtubules,\npreventing activation, degranulation, and migration of\nneutrophils associated with mediating gout symptoms.\nColchicine is useful in patients with heart failure and those\ntaking anticoagulants\nAvoid colchicine in patients with blood dyscrasias and bone\nmarrow disease .\nOpioid analgesics:\nClass of analgesics used to relieve moderate to severe\npain. Opioids have affinity for opioid µ receptors found in the\nCNS, spinal cord and periphery\nClassification of opioids:\nContraindications:\nRaised ICP\nParalytic ileus\nAcute respiratory depression\n\n![Analgesics - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/analgesics/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Analgesics — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nExacerbation of Asthma\nOpioid toxicity: Severe respiratory depression coma\nAntidote: Naloxone rapidly reverse the symptoms.	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
aa2c6ae3-e3da-41ed-8add-79c516d3f41e	a33921a8-de21-4251-b844-facd6afc8a7e	anti-epileptic-drugs	Anti - Epileptic Drugs	article	Anti-epileptic drugs\nCarbamazepine, sodium valproate\nand phenytoin\nMechanism of action: Carbamazepine, sodium valproate\nand phenytoin all three drugs act by inhibiting Voltage gated\nNa+ channels in neuronal cells.\nSodium valproate: May also enhance the inhibitory effects of\ngamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), an\nimportant neurotransmitter that reduces neuronal\nexcitability.\nAntiepileptic drugs in pregnancy: (Adverse features)\nCarbamazepine: Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn and\nNeural tube defects.\nPhenytoin: Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn., Risk of\ncongenital malformations e.g. cleft lip, hypospadias and\ncardiovascular defects.\n\n![Anti - Epileptic Drugs - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/cns-pharmacology-analgesia/anti-epileptic-drugs/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anti - Epileptic Drugs — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nSodium valproate: Risk of major congenital malformations e.g.\nneural tube, cardiac, craniofacial and limb defects.\nAntiepileptic drug interaction:\nAll the cytochrome P450 enzyme inducer drugs which also\ninclude antiepileptics phenytoin and carbamazepine increase\nrate of metabolism and hepatic clearance of\nconcurrently administrated drugs which typically results in\ndecreased pharmacologic effects. Enzyme inducer drugs\nreduce the effectiveness of Oral contraceptives pills taken in\npregnancy and thus pregnancy happens.\nNote: Sodium valproate is metabolized in liver all the enzyme\ninhibitor drugs decrease rate of metabolism of valproate and\ntoxicity occur.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
1e88a9c0-5b11-4849-96b6-033b38a61fdb	a33921a8-de21-4251-b844-facd6afc8a7e	lithium	Lithium	article	Lithium:\nUsed in the treatment of mania, bipolar disorders and\ndepression.\nSide effects:\nGl disturbance (Nausea, Diarrhea)\nMuscle weakness, Fine tremor\nPolyuria and polydipsia (ADH antagonism - nephrogenic\ndiabetes insipidus)\nWeight gain\nThyroid enlargement +/- hypothyroidism\nContraindications:\nNa imbalance\nDehydration\nThyroid dysfunction\nRenal impairment\nLithium monitoring:  Lithium has a narrow therapeutic effects.\nWhile starting lithium routine monitoring is\nrecommended. The usual therapeutic range for lithium is 0.4-\n0.8 mmol/l . The lower end of the range is usually the target for\nmaintenance therapy and treatment in the elderly. Toxic\neffects are generally seen at levels > 1.5 mmol/l.\nLithium toxicity:\nEarly features:\nNausea\nVomiting\nDiarrhea\nSlurred speech\nPolyuria\nMuscle weakness\n\nLate features:\nAtaxia, coarse hand tremor\nBlurred vision\nSeizures, Stupor\nSevere hypotension\nComa and death	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
5e94f57f-2330-4546-825f-d4be7de3a11a	797f6930-be16-40f1-b028-303cf92c244a	poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes	Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes	article	Poisoning and overdose\nIn toxicology, poisoning is defined as the state resulting from\nthe administration of excessive amounts of any\npharmaceutical agent.\nA poison may be a drug, household product, industrial\nchemical, or plant or animal derivative\nPoisoning may be accidental or deliberate:\nPoisoning is usually accidental in children aged under 6\nyears, and is most commonly due to exploration of their\nenvironment, where children place objects in their mouth\nand accidental ingestion occurs.\nDeliberate self-poisoning is the deliberate ingestion of more\nthan the prescribed amount of a medical substance, or of a\nsubstance not meant for human consumption, irrespective of\nthe motive for the episode.\nOverdose is a term used to describe the use of a quantity of\ndrug in excess of its intended or prescribed dose and is a\nmajor cause of poisoning. It may be accidental or deliberate\nand involve the use of prescribed or illicit drugs.\nTable - A: Drugs and clinical features\n\nTable - B: Toxidromes\n\n![Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/poisoning-overdose-and-toxidromes/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Poisoning, Overdose and Toxidromes — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
f0d37835-2ddf-4cd3-ace1-292c0b1bf18f	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	pathogens-fungi	Pathogens - Fungi	article	Fungi:\nCandida: Form part of the normal flora which includes skin, GI\ntract, and female genital tract\nCandidiasis and treatment\n\n![Pathogens - Fungi - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/pathogens-fungi/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Pathogens - Fungi — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
9b914ecc-25d2-4d33-bc65-015d0eb36db8	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	complement-cascade	Complement Cascade	article	Complement cascade:\nCollection of twenty proteins many of which are enzyme\nprecursors. constituting an amplification enzyme system which\nis capable of lysis of bacteria or blood cells or opsonization of\nbacteria or cells for phagocytosis.\nActivation of complement system: Enzyme precursors of\ncomplement system are normally inactive. They can be\nactivated in two ways classic and alternate pathway.\n\n### 1: Classic pathway: Activated by antigen antibody reaction\n\nWhen antibody binds with its specific antigen, "specific\nreaction site" on constant portion of antibody becomes\nuncovered or activated; this bind with complement factor Cl,\ngiving rise to a cascade of reaction\nMultiple reaction products (C3a, C3b, C4a Q C5b6789) are\nformed which cause following effects that prevent damage by\ninvading organisms and toxins (0-PLAN-CIA)\n(1) Opsonization (C3b)\n(2) Phagocytosis (C3b)\n(3) Lysis (C5b6789)\n(4) Agglutination\n(5) Neutralization\n(6) Chemotaxis (C5a)\n(7) Inflammatory effects\n(8) Activation of mast cells & basophils (C3a, C4a and C5a)\nNote: C3 deficiency leads to increased susceptibility of infection\nwith encapsulated organisms (e.g., S. pneumoniae, H.\n\ninfluenzae).\nC5 - C9 deficiency results in increased susceptibility to\nNeisseria infections.\n\n### 2: Alternate pathway: is activated without intermediation of\n\nantibody-antigen reaction. When complement factors B & D\nreact with some polysaccharide in cell membrane of invading\norganism, it activates complement factor C3, giving rise to\nsame cascade of reactions beyond C3 level. Same reaction\nproducts are formed which cause same effects as in classical\npathway.\nNote Since alternate pathway does not involve antigen-\nantibody reaction, it is one of first lines of defense against\ninvading organisms.\nRegulation\nThe complement pathway is regulated by complement\ninhibitors e.g., decay accelerating factor or C1 inhibitor.\nDeficiency in C1 inhibitor results in hereditary angioedema.\nDeficiency in complement decay-accelerating factor results in\nparoxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
1f3b3011-f4f9-4dde-833b-9439396ec129	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	inflammation	Inflammation	article	Inflammation\nNote: Chronic nonspecific inflammation: Nonspecific\ninflammatory cell infiltration seen in chronic osteomyelitis, lung\nabscess\nChronic specific inflammation: Formation of granuloma e.g., in\nT.B, Leprosy, syphilis and sarcoidosis\nNote: TNF-Alpha and IL-1 are key cytokines in acute\ninflammation\nNeutrophils and macrophages produce IL-1 which act on the\nhypothalamus and induce fever in inflammation.\nBlood changes in inflammation include Increased ESR,\nLeukocytosis and increased CRP.\nInflammation inducing substances:\nHistamine\nSerotonin\nBradykinin\nProstaglandin\nInterleukin functions:\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Mnemonic: Fever Tablet Means Effective Antipyretics Action**\nIL-1: Fever\nIL-2: T cell stimulator\nIL-3: Marrow stimulator\n\n![Inflammation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/inflammation/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Inflammation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nIL-4: IgE stimulator\nIL-5: Class switching to IgA\nIL-6: Stimulates Acute Phase Protein Production	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
3fa2b66a-d314-44a7-996c-cb783629b282	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	anemia	Anemia	article	Anemia\nAnemia: Reduction in Hb concentration below the normal\nrange\nRange: Men= Hct < 41% or HB less than 13.5 g/dl\nWomen: Hct< 36% or HB less than 11.5g/dl\nAnemia is classified on the basis of MCV, (Normal 80-100)\nMicrocytic anemia: MCV< 80%, Macrocytic anemia MCV>\n100%\nMicrocytic anemia:\nMacrocytic anemia:\nMegaloblastic anemia (Vit. B12 and folate deficiency\nAlcoholic liver disease\nHypothyroidism\nMyelodysplastic syndrome\n\n![Anemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/anemia/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Anemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nLaboratory Features\nMegaloblastic anaemia is characterized by:\nMacrocytic anaemia with oval macrocytes and hyper\nsegmented neutrophils\nModerate reduction in leucocyte and platelet counts\nRaised serum bilirubin (indirect) and lactate dehydrogenase\n(LDH) (due to haemolysis of defective red cells)\nLow B12 +/- folate\nHypercellular bone marrow, megaloblastic erythropoiesis and\ngiant metamyelocytes\n\n![Anemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/anemia/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Anemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
c9986a89-fcc4-408f-8a38-ddf134c79287	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	antibodies-and-hypersensitivity-reactions	Antibodies and Hypersensitivity Reactions	article	Antibodies:\nHypersensitivity reaction:\n\n![Antibodies and Hypersensitivity Reactions - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/antibodies-and-hypersensitivity-reactions/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Antibodies and Hypersensitivity Reactions — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Antibodies and Hypersensitivity Reactions - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/antibodies-and-hypersensitivity-reactions/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Antibodies and Hypersensitivity Reactions — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
598655ff-8368-4388-93b5-c34bb99b330e	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	cell-death	Cell Death	article	Cell death\n\n![Cell Death - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/cell-death/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Cell Death — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
0aa51968-c616-411b-aa3c-8620cad1ba48	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	sickle-cell-disease	Sickle Cell Disease	article	Sickle cell disease:\nAutosomal recessive disorder\nType of normocytic anemia with high reticulocyte count\nClinical features:\nVaso-occlusive crisis: Most common crisis\nSickling causes blockage of small vessels\nCommon precipitants are infection, dehydration, acidosis and\ndeoxygenation\nAbdominal pain is caused by infarction affecting abdominal\norgans\nBone pain may occur in the back, pelvis, ribs and long bones\nInfarction may affect the central nervous system, lungs,\nspleen or kidneys\nHand-foot syndrome in children caused by infarction of\nmetaphyses of small bones is frequently the first\npresentation of the disease\nAcute sickle chest syndrome is the most common cause of\ndeath in adults and children (dyspnoea, falling arterial PO2,\nchest pain, pulmonary infiltrates on CXR)\nVisceral sequestration crisis : Sickling with pooling of red\ncells in the liver, spleen or lungs\nAplastic crisis :Usually following infection with B19\nparvovirus\nTemporary arrest of erythropoiesis, rapidly causing severe\nanaemia requiring transfusion\nIncreased susceptibility to infection\nSplenic function is reduced because infarction leads to\nhyposplenism\nPneumococcal infections may lead to pneumonia and\nmeningitis.\nInfarction of intestinal mucosa predisposes to Salmonella\ninfection and osteomyelitis.\nLab values:\nCBC= Normocytic anemia with raised reticulocyte count\n\nPeripheral film: Sickle cells, Howell- jolly bodies\nDiagnosis: HB Electrophoresis most accurate test	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
a16d0737-e17b-44a3-b452-0c48f2a1ce01	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	pathogens-protozoa	Pathogens - Protozoa	article	Malaria\nCaused by protozoan plasmodium.\nThere are four different Plasmodium species relevant in\nhumans:\nPlasmodium falciparum\nPlasmodium vivax\nPlasmodium ovale\nPlasmodium malariae\nPlasmodium falciparum is the deadliest malaria parasite and\nthe most prevalent on the African continent. P. vivax is the\ndominant malaria parasite in most countries outside of sub-\nSaharan Africa.\nDiagnosis: The Giemsa stain (Thick and thin film) is used as\nthe gold standard for the diagnosis of malaria on blood smears\nTreatment: Non-Falciparum malaria: Chloroquine drug of\nchoice\nFalciparum malaria: Quinine or Artemether with\nlumefantrine\nNote: vivax and P.ovale have dormant liver stages\n("hypnozoites") that can activate and cause relapse of fever and\nmalaria. Primaquine is drug of choice to kill liver hypnozoites.\nAntimalarial drug In Pregnancy:\nQuinine is the antimalarial drug of choice in pregnancy;\nhowever, chloroquine should be considered in non-\nfalciparum malaria while those areas where chloroquine is\nresistant especially southern Vietnam Quinine is best given in\npregnancy.	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
348d36f6-c14a-4a81-b5ca-2b011e411248	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	pathogens-worms	Pathogens - Worms	article	Thread worm\nis the most common helminth in the UK, commonly infect\nchildren.\nMebendazole is the drug of choice, and you must treat\neveryone in your home even if they do not have symptoms.\nTapeworm\nPork tapeworm and beef tapeworm transmitted via eating\ncontaminated undercooked meat.\nPork tapeworm (Taenia solium): Infection occurs through\ningestion of food or water contaminated with eggs of solium\nCysticercosis:  infection caused by larval cysts of the\ntapeworm Taenia solium, infect brain, muscle, skin and eye\nDiagnosis: Direct visualization of characteristic eggs in\nstool\nTreatment: Nicolsamide\nRound worm\nCauses: Ascaris lumbricoides\nDiagnosis: Direct visualization of characteristic eggs in\nstool\nTreatment: Mebendazole\nHook worm\nIntestinal parasite causes gastrointestinal symptoms and iron\ndeficiency anemia.\nDiagnosis: Direct visualization of characteristic eggs in\nstool\nTreatment: Mebendazole	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
7ca09e79-8b09-493a-9db6-20d07483bf64	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	escherichia-coli-klebsiella-salmonella-bacteria	Escherichia Coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella - Bacteria	article	Escherichia Coli:\nNote: E. coli serotype O157 causes bloody diarrhoea associated\nwith hemolytic uremic syndrome in children, characterized by\nhemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia and acute renal failure.\nKlebsiella spp:\nSalmonella enterica\nis a gram-negative rod that causes enterocolitis (Food\npoisoning), Enteric fever (Typhoid), Septicemia, and\nosteomyelitis\nPrimarily transmitted through fecal-oral route usually from\ningestion of contaminated poultry, eggs, and uncooked\nmeat\nTreatment:\n\n![Escherichia Coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/escherichia-coli-klebsiella-salmonella-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Escherichia Coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Escherichia Coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/escherichia-coli-klebsiella-salmonella-bacteria/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Escherichia Coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nSalmonella enterocolitis: Usually supportive management in\nresistant cases ciprofloxacin\nEnteric fever: First line ciprofloxacin if organism sensitive, and\nin resistant cases cefotaxime or azithromycin\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Regarding osteomyelitis\n> Most common cause generally: Staph Aureus (Recall)\n> The most common cause in IV drug abuser: Staph Aureus\n> The most common cause in sickle cell disease: Salmonella\n> Most common cause after hip replacement surgery: Staph\n> epidermidis	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
4eb084a1-61e0-4f52-b737-b66872a5eebf	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	wound-healing	Wound Healing	article	Wound healing\nHealing is the process of replacing dead and damaging tissue\nwith healthy tissue; this may occur through regeneration or\nrepair.\nThe regenerative capacity of cells can be categorized in three\nmain ways:\nLabile cells are constantly dividing and have a good capacity\nfor regeneration, this allows the replacement of ageing tissue\nsuch as the surface epithelia of the skin, gastrointestinal tract\nand uterus; blood cells are derived from labile cells of the\nbone marrow.\nStable cells slowly replicate e.g., cells of liver, renal tubular\nepithelium, endocrine glands.\nPermanent tissues: incapable of division and cannot be\nregenerated e.g., brain cells, myocardial cells, skeletal muscle\ncells.\nHealing by primary intention:\nHealing of a clean, uninfected surgical incision approximated\nby surgical sutures.\nDay-1: Neutrophilic infiltration + fibrin clot\nDay-2: Epithelial cells from both edges have begun to\nmigrate and proliferate along the dermis, yielding a thin but\ncontinuous epithelial layer.\nDay-3: Macrophages replace neutrophils Appearance of\ngranulation tissue. Type III collagen deposition begins but do\nnot bridge the incision.\nDay 5: Abundant granulation tissue Collagen fibrils bridge the\nincision.\nNeovascularization is maximum.\nWeek-2: Dense scar tissue produced from granulation tissue\ncontains type III collagen (weak collagen) that must be\nremodeled. Remodeling increases the tensile strength of scar\ntissue.\n\n1 month: Replacement of collagen type III with collagen type I\n(has greater tensile strength) due to action of collagenase\nenzyme.\nThe predominant collagen in adult skin is type I, whereas in\nearly granulation tissue, it is type III.\nHealing by secondary intention\nOccurs in large wounds abscess formation and ulceration.\nMain features:\nA larger clot or scab\nInflammation: more intense Granulation tissue: much larger\namount\nScar tissue: greater mass\nWound contraction: by myofibroblast e.g. Within 6 weeks,\nlarge skin defects may be reduced to 5% to 10% of their\noriginal size, largely by contraction.\nHealing by tertiary intention\nContaminated wound is initially treated with debridement\nand antibiotics followed by surgical wound closure (suture,\nskin graft replacement, flap)\nNote: Regarding the neat surgical scar after 2 months of\nsurgery fibroblasts have predominant role\nMost important for delayed wound healing is due\nto infection.\nFactors affecting wound healing:\nLocal factors affecting wound healing\nMechanical injury / Frequent trauma\nInfection\nEdema\nIschemia/necrotic tissue\nTopical agents\nlionizing radiation\nLow oxygen tension\nForeign bodies\nSystemic factors affecting wound healing\nDiabetes mellitus\nAnaemia\nMalnutrition\n\nImmunodeficiency\nImmunosuppressive medications\nAge\nObesity\nSmoking\nVit A, C and Zinc deficiency\nComplications of Healing\nHypertrophic scar\nKeloid formation\nFailure to heal (abscess or empyema formation)\nFailure to unite (skin, muscle or fascia wound breakdown)\nFracture complications\nStages of bone/fracture healing:\nHematoma formation (Immediately in Day 1)\nInflammation and proliferation (8 hours to 1-2 weeks)\npredominant cells neutrophils and macrophages\nCallus formation (2-3 weeks)\nConsolidation (2 to 3 months)\nRe-modelling (2 months to years) Osteoclasts are\nresponsible.\nFactors that prevent efficient healing:\nMalnutrition\nInfection\nCorticosteroids therapy\nPoor blood supply	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
7415119e-e3f0-4cbf-80fd-90ef88ba7836	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	hellicobacter-pylori-and-compylobacter-jejuni-bacteria	Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria	article	Helicobacter Pylori\nCampylobacter Jejuni\nNote:\nReactive arthritis (Reiter's syndrome) is a triad of urethritis,\narthritis and conjunctivitis.\nOrganisms causing reactive arthritis: Chlamydia,\nSalmonella, Campylobacter jejuni and Shigella\n\n![Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/hellicobacter-pylori-and-compylobacter-jejuni-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/hellicobacter-pylori-and-compylobacter-jejuni-bacteria/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Hellicobacter Pylori and Compylobacter Jejuni - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
342247c6-a0a9-4c08-8941-51a66586a6e8	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	hepatitis-virus	Hepatitis - Virus	article	Hepatitis:\nNote: Emergency hepatitis B vaccination\nIf you have been exposed to the hepatitis B virus and have\nnot been vaccinated before, you should get immediate\nmedical advice, as you may benefit from having the hepatitis\nB vaccine.\nIn some situations, you may also need to have an injection of\nantibodies, called specific hepatitis B immunoglobulin\n(HBIG), along with the hepatitis B vaccine. HBIG should\nideally be given within 48 hours post exposure Hepatitis B\nvaccine in three doses over three months.\nHepatitis-D:\nCo-infection with HBV results in fulminant hepatitis\nSuperinfection with HBV results in fulminant hepatitis or\nchronic hepatitis commonly\nHepatitis-E:\nTransmitted via fecal oral route causes acute fulminant\nhepatitis in pregnant woman.\nNo progression to chronic hepatitis\n\n![Hepatitis - Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/hepatitis-virus/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Hepatitis - Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
c5f7736e-0e5b-4523-8b8d-7783cb0776df	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	herpes-simplex-and-varicella-zooster-virus	Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus	article	Herpes Virus:\nVaricella zoster Key Points\nThe incubation period is 10 - 21 days (Recall) (mean time is\n14 days).\nPrimary infection typically occurs in 4-10 years children.\nDiagnosis is usually clinical.\n\n![Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/herpes-simplex-and-varicella-zooster-virus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/herpes-simplex-and-varicella-zooster-virus/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Herpes Simplex and Varicella Zooster Virus — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nAfter primary infection virus lies dormant in posterior root\nganglion and reactivates in 20% of patients causing shingles\nSchool exclusion period: when all lesions are crusted over\n(Recall\nChickenpox in pregnancy:\nChickenpox in pregnancy is managed by two different\nmethods - one is used before the onset of symptoms and the\nother after the onset of rash. One is varicella-zoster immune\nglobulin (VZIG), which strengthens the immune system for a\nshort time. If you are not immune to chickenpox you can be\ntreated with VZIG, usually by injection, up to ten days after\nyou come into contact with chickenpox. It is used before any\nsymptoms appear; it does not work afterwards. The other is\nAcyclovir, which may reduce fever and symptoms if it is\nstarted within 24 hours of the rash developing and continue\nfor 7 days\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Key points:**\n> If you have already had chickenpox, you have nothing to\n> worry about.\n\nOnce you have had chickenpox you cannot catch it a second\ntime, this is known as being immune.\nNine out of ten UK adults are immune to chickenpox.\nChickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in the UK.\nIt affects very few babies in the womb and although it can\nbe very serious in pregnancy especially in 1st trimester in\nfirst 12 weeks (Recall)\nIf you have been in contact with chickenpox and are not sure\nif you are immune, contact your GP or midwife and do not\nattend the antenatal clinic.\nIf you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other pregnant\nwomen and new babies until at least five days after the rash\nappears, or until all the blisters have crusted over.	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
fd9e7b0e-4600-49e9-b553-ec2220c65075	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	human-immune-virus	Human Immune Virus	article	Human Immune virus (HIV)\nHigh-risk source for HIV\nMSM (men who have sex with men)\nFemale sexual contacts of MSM\nPeople reporting current or prior injecting drug use\nSex workers\nTrans women\nPeople from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence\n(>1%) * i-e Originally from sub-Saharan Africa.\nPeople reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country\nwith high diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where\ncontact occurs\nNote - Anal course is incorrect unless, in the option they\nmentioned unprotected anal course with sub-Saharan\npeople/ high risk source.\nTypes of Exposure\nNon-occupational exposure\nConsider patient at potential risk for HIV acquisition if:\nThe source has HIV and is not receiving antiretroviral therapy\n(ART), is at high risk for HIV infection, or is from a group with\nhigh prevalence of detectable HIV viraemia, AND\nThere has been exposure of vagina, rectum, mouth, or non-\nintact skin with blood, semen (Splash exposure to mucous\nmembranes, most commonly the eye), vaginal secretions,\nrectal secretions, breast milk, or anybody fluid that visibly\ncontains blood.\nOccupational exposure\nA percutaneous injury (e.g., a needlestick or cut with a sharp\nobject). Deep injury, visible blood, or injury with a device\nthat has been used intravascularly, all increase the risk of\nHIV transmission.\n\nContact of mucous membrane or non-intact skin (e.g.,\nexposed skin that is chapped, abraded, or afflicted with\ndermatitis). If the skin remains intact, there is no risk of HIV\ntransmission.\nA bite if the skin is broken as a result of trauma.\nNote - Exposure with urine, nasal secretions, saliva, sweat, or\ntears without the presence of blood is considered negligible\nrisk for acquisition of HIV.\nPathogenesis:\nHIV mainly infects CD4+ T helper cells. Viral replication\nresults in progressive T-cell depletion and impaired cell-\nmediated immunity with subsequent secondary\nopportunistic infections\nHIV-related opportunistic infection:\nNote: You must remember CD4 count of important\nopportunistic pathogens like Tuberculosis, Pneumocystis\njiroveci pneumonia, Pneumonia, Mycobacterium avium\ncomplex and toxoplasmosis Gondi\n\nDiagnosis: Combined HIV antibody and p24 antigen test\nMonitoring: HIV viral load (PCR) and CD4+ count\n\n![Human Immune Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/human-immune-virus/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Human Immune Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
8ddd5169-61eb-434c-8e1b-a68fad0d08a5	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	measles-mumps-rubella-virus	Measles, Mumps, Rubella - Virus	article	Measles:\nAcute viral illness transmitted by respiratory droplets\ncharacterized by 3C Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and fever.\nMeasles rash: Maculopapular rash appearing first behind the\nears and spreading to whole body.\nIncubation period: 9-12 days\nInfectious period: Patients are considered infectious for 3\ndays before the rash emerges and four days from onset of\nthe rash.\nPatients are advised to stay away from school.\nTreatment: Supportive\nComplications: Pneumonia, bronchitis, otitis media,\nencephalitis, subacute sclerosing panencephalitis\nMumps:\nRubella:\nAcute viral illness transmitted by respiratory droplets\ncharacterized by fever, malaise, arthralgia and sore throat\nRubella rash: Maculopapular rash appearing first on the face\nand spreading downward associated with lymphadenopathy.\nIncubation period: 14-21 days\nInfectious period: Patients are considered infectious for 4\ndays from onset of the rash and seven days before the rash\n\n![Measles, Mumps, Rubella - Virus - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/measles-mumps-rubella-virus/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Measles, Mumps, Rubella - Virus — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nappears.\nPatients are advised to stay away from school in infectious\nperiod.\nTreatment:  Supportive\nComplications: Arthritis, Thrombocytopenic purpura,\nEncephalitis and congenital rubella syndrome\nRubella in pregnancy: Is very serious if a woman is infected\nin the first trimester it can have serious consequences for\nthe baby's health for example congenital abnormalities such\nas heart defects, cataracts, intrauterine growth restriction,\ncentral nervous system defects and deafness.	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
146c256f-5922-49e5-b08f-759b9620e02c	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	mycobacterium-tuberculosis-bacteria	Mycobacterium Tuberculosis - Bacteria	article	Mycobacterium Tuberculosis\nAcid fast bacilli Causes tuberculosis transmitted via respiratory\naerosol and characterized by chronic granulomatous\ninflammation.\nClinical Features:\nFever, Night sweats and weight loss\nPersistent cough for more than 3 weeks\nHemoptysis\nHilar lymphadenopathy\nPathophysiology:\nThe Ghon focus is a primary lesion caused by tuberculosis that\ndevelops in the lung of previously unaffected individuals. It\nrepresents a calcified caseating granuloma. It typically\nappears in the middle-third of the lung (the lower part of the\nupper lobe or upper part of the lower lobe).\nDiagnosis:\nsputum samples Microscopy with Z-N (Ziehl-Nelsen stain)\nshows acid fast bacilli.\nIn the case of active pulmonary TB, a patient can have\ncrepitations, and bronchial breath sounds, and specially over\nupper lobes x-ray cavitations or consolidations.\nThe Mantoux test is a widely used test for latent TB: It\ninvolves injecting a small amount of a substance called PPD\ntuberculin into the skin of your forearm. It's also called the\ntuberculin skin test (TST).	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
df4c8098-c812-4ac5-8fab-c4f6ee692ec5	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	neisseria-spp-bacteria	Neisseria Spp - Bacteria	article	Neisseria species\nNeisseria Gonorrhea and Neisseria meningitis\nGeneral features:\nNeisseria meningitis:\nNeisseria meningitis is gram negative encapsulated\ndiplococci causing Neisseria meningitis and meningococcal\nsepsis transmitted via respiratory droplet\nNeisseria meningitidis is the most common cause of\nbacterial meningitis.\nClinical features\ninclude headache, nausea, vomiting, photophobia, lethargy,\nrash, multiple organ failure, shock, and disseminated\nintravascular coagulation.\nDiagnosis is clinical, confirmed by blood culture and CSF\naspirate.\nBacterial meningitis typically reveals cloud turbid\nappearance of CSF, Neutrophil predominant, High protein\nand low glucose (<40mg/dl)\nThe most important to remember here:\n\n![Neisseria Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/neisseria-spp-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Neisseria Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nTreatment:\nIf meningococcal disease is suspected out of hospital,\npatients should receive a single dose of parenteral (IM or IV)\nbenzylpenicillin as soon as possible or initial blind therapy in\nhospital\nIn Hospital when diagnosis is made Ceftriaxone antibiotic of\nchoice\n\n![Neisseria Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/neisseria-spp-bacteria/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Neisseria Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
bb7fbd48-5f8e-4d9e-b956-1c89b3246d9c	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	notifiable-disease	Notifiable Disease	article	Diseases notifiable to local authority\nproper officers under the Health\nProtection (Notification) Regulations\n2010:\nAcute encephalitis\nAcute infectious hepatitis\nAcute meningitis\nAcute poliomyelitis\nAnthrax\nBotulism\nBrucellosis\nCholera\n\n## Covid-19\n\nDiphtheria\nEnteric fever (typhoid or paratyphoid fever)\nFood poisoning\nHaemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)\nInfectious bloody diarrhea\nInvasive group A streptococcal disease\nLegionnaires' disease\nLeprosy\nMalaria\nMeasles\nMeningococcal septicaemia\nMonkeypox\nMumps\nPlague\nRabies\nRubella\nSevere Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS)\nScarlet fever\nSmallpox\nTetanus\nTuberculosis\n\nTyphus\nViral hemorrhagic fever (VHF)\nWhooping cough\nYellow fever	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
f85a80da-e1cd-4a76-8cbb-447756cf0826	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-63	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 63	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 63\n\nprev\n- Explanation See Result\n- Flush the line with 0.9% saline via the extension set and observe for extravasation. After IO placement you should attempt aspiration; absence of aspirate does not necessarily mean failure. Next, flush with saline to confirm patency and check for leakage/extravasation. If patent, you may infuse drugs/fluids (use pressure bag or syringe).\n- Intraosseous CQ) insertion in adult\n- Common adult sites - Proximal humerus, proximal tibia, distal tibia.\n- Contraindications - fracture/major traurna at target bone, infection/burns over site, prosthesis in that bone, previous/failed IO in same limb within 48 h, or can't identify landmarks.\n- Training - use the specific device you have; landmarks and insertion angles differ.\n- Procedure\n- After insertion, attempt to aspirate marrow/blood. Presence confirms placement; failure to aspirate does not always mean failure.\n- Flush (extension set primed with 0.9% saline) to check patency; watch for leakage/extravasation.\n- Once confirmed, give resuscitation drugs (e.g., adrenaline, amiodarone), fluids and blood products. Use a pressure bag or syringe to achieve flow.\n- Secure per manufacturer guidance and observe dwell-time limits; convert to IV as soon as feasible.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Complications to watch for Extravasation/infiltration - compartment syndrome or skin necrosis\n- Dislodgement/malposition Bone fracture or cortical chipping during insertion Pain with infusion (especially conscious patients) Fat/air embolus (rare) Infection (cellulitis/osteomyelitis)  -9\n	\N	[]	\N	f	60	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
18793759-7ff1-43e9-ba1c-4b3788b4078d	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	pathogens-protozoa	Pathogens - Protozoa	article	Malaria\nCaused by protozoan plasmodium.\nThere are four different Plasmodium species relevant in\nhumans:\nPlasmodium falciparum\nPlasmodium vivax\nPlasmodium ovale\nPlasmodium malariae\nPlasmodium falciparum is the deadliest malaria parasite and\nthe most prevalent on the African continent. P. vivax is the\ndominant malaria parasite in most countries outside of sub-\nSaharan Africa.\nDiagnosis: The Giemsa stain (Thick and thin film) is used as\nthe gold standard for the diagnosis of malaria on blood smears\nTreatment: Non-Falciparum malaria: Chloroquine drug of\nchoice\nFalciparum malaria: Quinine or Artemether with\nlumefantrine\nNote: vivax and P.ovale have dormant liver stages\n("hypnozoites") that can activate and cause relapse of fever and\nmalaria. Primaquine is drug of choice to kill liver hypnozoites.\nAntimalarial drug In Pregnancy:\nQuinine is the antimalarial drug of choice in pregnancy;\nhowever, chloroquine should be considered in non-\nfalciparum malaria while those areas where chloroquine is\nresistant especially southern Vietnam Quinine is best given in\npregnancy.	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
72f903df-50d8-4359-b6c8-933e679f3e18	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	pathogens-worms	Pathogens - Worms	article	Thread worm\nis the most common helminth in the UK, commonly infect\nchildren.\nMebendazole is the drug of choice, and you must treat\neveryone in your home even if they do not have symptoms.\nTapeworm\nPork tapeworm and beef tapeworm transmitted via eating\ncontaminated undercooked meat.\nPork tapeworm (Taenia solium): Infection occurs through\ningestion of food or water contaminated with eggs of solium\nCysticercosis:  infection caused by larval cysts of the\ntapeworm Taenia solium, infect brain, muscle, skin and eye\nDiagnosis: Direct visualization of characteristic eggs in\nstool\nTreatment: Nicolsamide\nRound worm\nCauses: Ascaris lumbricoides\nDiagnosis: Direct visualization of characteristic eggs in\nstool\nTreatment: Mebendazole\nHook worm\nIntestinal parasite causes gastrointestinal symptoms and iron\ndeficiency anemia.\nDiagnosis: Direct visualization of characteristic eggs in\nstool\nTreatment: Mebendazole	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
96e25a27-75e6-4e3e-a534-8a74f969a4ac	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	principles-of-investigation	Principles of Investigation	article	Principles of Investigations\n\n![Principles of Investigation - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-investigation/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Principles of Investigation — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
52b57947-3280-4ec7-97e5-2bd5c94b3b9f	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	principles-of-microbiology	Principles of Microbiology	article	Microbiology Principles:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Normal bacterial flora: The microorganisms that grow in or on\n> the body without producing harmful effects on the host also\n> compete with other pathogens for colonisation sites\n> Antibiotics suppress normal flora which allows for\n> colonisation and infection by opportunistic pathogens such\n> as Candida albicans or Clostridium difficile.\n\nAlso act as barrier to infection\nNote: Skin as barrier to infection but certain organism can\npenetrate intact skin e.g., Leptospira and Treponema\nIf skin integrity is broken e.g., by intravenous cannulation,\nblood-borne viruses such as hepatitis B and HIV can be\ntransmitted.\nPathogen and Types:\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nControl of infection/ Hospital-acquired infection\nDisinfection: Process that inactivates many diseases\nproducing microbes on inanimate objects with exception\nof bacterial spores\nSterlisation; Inactivation of all infectious agents and is mainly\nachieved via autoclaving machine\nCommon Disinfectants:\nIodine is a slow-acting skin anti-bacterial disinfectant.\nChlorhexidine is an anti-staphylococcal agent.\nAlcohol gel is not effective against C. difficile spores, so the\nuse of soap and water is essential.\nMicroorganism & Mechanism of Attachment\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae, E-Coli=Adheres to the genital mucosa\nusing fimbriae\nInfluenza Attaches =by its haemagglutinin antigen\nGiardia lamblia=Attaches to gut mucosa via a specialized\nsucking disc\nMechanism of immune evasion\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nSpread of infection:\nFood supply (issues of hygiene standards, temperature of\nfood, transport to ward\nLegionella pneumophila spread via poorly maintained air-\nconditioning cooling towers\nSalmonella enterica spread via ingested poultry.\nAir supply (via theatre air supply and air conditioning\nsystems)\nFomites (any inanimate object that may act as a vehicle for\ntransmission e.g., MRSA on a stethoscope, Pseudomonas spp.\non vases)\nwater supply: Legionella pneumophila may colonise the\nsystem in redundant areas of lukewarm pipework and be\ntransmitted to air conditioning system\nRoute of transmission of microorganisms\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_04_p3.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
b433fcc3-d331-42fb-9f5e-ad9e414ed532	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	pseudomonas-aeruginosa-legionella-pneumophilia-and-bortonella-henslae-bacteria	Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Legionella Pneumophilia and Bortonella Henslae - Bacteria	article	Pseudomonas Aeruginosa\nGram-negative rod transmitted via direct/indirect contact\nlikely in the hospital environment.\nLegionella Pneumophilia\nGram-negative coccobacilli are transmitted via respiratory\naerosol.\nLegionnaires disease\nDiagnosis: Legionella antigen detection in urine\nTreatment: Quinolones like levofloxacin or macrolides like\nclarithromycin\nBartonella Henselae\nGram negative bacilli or coccobacilli\nCat-scratch disease (CSD) or felinosis is an infectious disease\ncaused by Bartonella henselae most often results from a\nscratch or bite of a cat.\n\n/2026, 10:54 PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILIA AND BORTONELLA HENSLAE-BACTERIA | MRCEM EX…\n\n![Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Legionella Pneumophilia and Bortonella Henslae - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/pseudomonas-aeruginosa-legionella-pneumophilia-and-bortonella-henslae-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Legionella Pneumophilia and Bortonella Henslae - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n/2026, 10:54 PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILIA AND BORTONELLA HENSLAE-BACTERIA | MRCEM EX…\n\n/2026, 10:54 PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILIA AND BORTONELLA HENSLAE-BACTERIA | MRCEM EX…	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
87e29623-a15a-431c-8ecd-6c6a41e98408	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	sexually-transmitted-bacteria	Sexually Transmitted Bacteria	article	Sexually Transmitted Bacteria:\nChlamydia Trachomatis:\nObligate intracellular bacterium transmitted vis sexual and\nperipartum causing Chlamydia, trachoma, ophthalmia\nneonatorum, pneumonia.\nOphthalmia neonatorum: is conjunctivitis occurring within the\nfirst 4 weeks of life.\nThe most serious cause is infection with Neisseria gonorrhea\nin first week most likely in 3-4 days of life, but in the UK, it is\ncaused more commonly by Chlamydia in first 28 days of life.\nNote: Conjunctivitis in first week of life is caused by Neisseria\ngonorrhea\nOther species of chlamydia causing pneumonia in humans\nChlamydophila pneumophila infect humans causes\npneumonia.\nChlamydophila psittaci infects birds. Humans are infected\nprimarily by inhaling organisms in dry bird faeces.\nNeisseria Gonorrhea:\nGram negative diplococci transmitted via sexual and\nperipartum.\nGeneral features of Neisseria spp. (Neisseria gonorrhea and\nNeisseria meningitis)\nComparison Between Neisseria\nGonorrhea and\nChlamydia trachomatis:\n\n![Sexually Transmitted Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/sexually-transmitted-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Sexually Transmitted Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Sexually Transmitted Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/sexually-transmitted-bacteria/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Sexually Transmitted Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	19	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
c89b3f8c-d04d-4a2d-9211-1a296201124d	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	uk-immunization-schedule	Uk - Immunization Schedule	article	UK Immunization Schedule:\nThe routine immunization schedule\nfrom February 2022\nNote: Trade name is not necessary to remember.\n\n![Uk - Immunization Schedule - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/uk-immunization-schedule/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Uk - Immunization Schedule — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nSelective immunisation programmes\nAdditional vaccines for individuals\nwith underlying medical conditions\n\n![Uk - Immunization Schedule - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/uk-immunization-schedule/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Uk - Immunization Schedule — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Uk - Immunization Schedule - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/uk-immunization-schedule/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Uk - Immunization Schedule — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	21	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
7aef7703-c373-4541-84c3-ed06b2c4b28e	bb0f2013-a048-4fa5-9698-0e0d91e39ffc	digoxin	Digoxin	article	Digoxin\nDigoxin is a cardiac glycoside derived from the\nplant foxglove that primarily works by inhibiting the Na+/K+\nATPase in the myocardium.\nMechanism: Digoxin has positive inotropic effects and\nnegative chronotropic effects\nBinds to and inhibits the Na+/K+ pump on the myocyte cell\nmembrane (competes with the K+ ion) Increases\nintracellular Na+ concentration.\nReduces the extrusion of intracellular Ca2+ via the Na+/Ca2+\nexchange transporter Increased Ca2+ is stored in\nthe sarcoplasmic reticulum Increased amounts of Ca2+\nreleased during each action potential force of\ncontraction increased .\nDigoxin also increases vagal tone by enhancing Ach release at\nmuscarinic receptors, thus slowing the heart\nrate, atrioventricular (AV) conduction, and the AV nodal\nrefractory period.\nIndications: Heart failure, persistent and permanent atrial\nfibrillation and flutter.\nNote: In heart failure digoxin is useful in patient with reduced\nejection fraction, with an ejection fraction below 40%. However,\nit has no benefit in mortality reduction.\nDigoxin can only be used as monotherapy in sedentary\npatients because it controls ventricular response at rest.\nMonitoring: Digoxin has narrow therapeutic index.\nNarrow therapeutic index means difference between the\neffective therapeutic dose and the potentially toxic dose is\nrelatively small. This narrow window makes proper dosing\nand monitoring critically important to ensure the medication's\nsafety, effectiveness and to prevent toxicity. The\ntherapeutic plasma levels of digoxin are usually between 1.0-1.5 nmol/l. The likelihood of toxicity rises dramatically at levels\nabove 2 nmol/l.\nDose adjustments in renal failure and liver failure:\nRenal failure: Digoxin is excreted renally, and impaired renal\nfunction can cause increased digoxin levels and leads to\ndigoxin toxicity. In this case the patient should have their\ndigoxin dose reduced and their digoxin level and electrolytes\nshould be carefully monitored.\nLiver failure: Liver failure has little influence on digoxin\nmetabolism or clearance; therefore, no dose adjustment is\nnecessary.\nDigoxin toxicity: Precipitated by hypokalemia or drugs\ncausing hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, acidosis and renal\nfailure.\nThe features of digoxin toxicity include:\nHyperkaliemia\nNausea, vomiting and diarrhea .\nAbdominal pain\nConfusion, dizziness and psychosis\nTachyarrhythmias or bradyarrhythmia's\nXanthopsia (yellow-green vision)\nTreatment: Digoxin immune fab is the reversal agent\n(Antidote) of digoxin toxicity.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
bf21ed63-3802-4e6a-af79-076d88044daf	719c875b-baff-4b7c-90a3-fe88bacbc53c	lung-elastance-and-compliance	Lung Elastance and Compliance	article	Lung elastance and Lung\nCompliance:\nLung elastance: Tendency of lungs to collapse.\nCompliance: The ease with which the lungs can expand\nknown as compliance\nCompliance increases in Obstructive lung disease.\nCompliance decreases in Restrictive Lung disease.\nChanges in lung compliance\nIn a patient with emphysema, lung compliance is increased,\nelastic recoil is decreased and the tendency of the lungs to\ncollapse is decreased. Therefore, at the original FRC the\ntendency of the lungs to collapse is less than the tendency of\nthe chest wall to expand. The lung-chest wall system will\nseek a new, higher FRC so that the two opposing forces can\nbe balanced again; the patient's chest becomes barrel-\nshaped, reflecting this higher volume.\nIn a patient with fibrosis, lung compliance is decreased,\nelastic recoil is increased. and the tendency of the lungs to\ncollapse is increased, Therefore, at the original FRC the\ntendency of the lungs to collapse is greater than the\ntendency of the chest wall to expand. The lung-chest wall\nsystem will seek a new, lower FRC so that the two opposing\nforces can be balanced again.	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
50d011b5-a804-4d1b-929c-1ede570dbdd4	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	principles-of-microbiology	Principles of Microbiology	article	Microbiology Principles:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Normal bacterial flora: The microorganisms that grow in or on\n> the body without producing harmful effects on the host also\n> compete with other pathogens for colonisation sites\n> Antibiotics suppress normal flora which allows for\n> colonisation and infection by opportunistic pathogens such\n> as Candida albicans or Clostridium difficile.\n\nAlso act as barrier to infection\nNote: Skin as barrier to infection but certain organism can\npenetrate intact skin e.g., Leptospira and Treponema\nIf skin integrity is broken e.g., by intravenous cannulation,\nblood-borne viruses such as hepatitis B and HIV can be\ntransmitted.\nPathogen and Types:\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nControl of infection/ Hospital-acquired infection\nDisinfection: Process that inactivates many diseases\nproducing microbes on inanimate objects with exception\nof bacterial spores\nSterlisation; Inactivation of all infectious agents and is mainly\nachieved via autoclaving machine\nCommon Disinfectants:\nIodine is a slow-acting skin anti-bacterial disinfectant.\nChlorhexidine is an anti-staphylococcal agent.\nAlcohol gel is not effective against C. difficile spores, so the\nuse of soap and water is essential.\nMicroorganism & Mechanism of Attachment\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae, E-Coli=Adheres to the genital mucosa\nusing fimbriae\nInfluenza Attaches =by its haemagglutinin antigen\nGiardia lamblia=Attaches to gut mucosa via a specialized\nsucking disc\nMechanism of immune evasion\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nSpread of infection:\nFood supply (issues of hygiene standards, temperature of\nfood, transport to ward\nLegionella pneumophila spread via poorly maintained air-\nconditioning cooling towers\nSalmonella enterica spread via ingested poultry.\nAir supply (via theatre air supply and air conditioning\nsystems)\nFomites (any inanimate object that may act as a vehicle for\ntransmission e.g., MRSA on a stethoscope, Pseudomonas spp.\non vases)\nwater supply: Legionella pneumophila may colonise the\nsystem in redundant areas of lukewarm pipework and be\ntransmitted to air conditioning system\nRoute of transmission of microorganisms\n\n![Principles of Microbiology - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/principles-of-microbiology/figure_04_p3.png)\n*Principles of Microbiology — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
fe47bca9-7863-4b50-a243-21820427f498	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-genitourinary-trauma	Genitourinary Trauma & Urethral Injury — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Genitourinary Trauma & Urethral Injury — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 23-year-old male motorcyclist involved in a collision presents with an unstable pelvic fracture. On secondary survey, blood is noted at the external urethral meatus, and a butterfly perineal haematoma is visible. Rectal exam reveals a 'high-riding' boggy prostate. What is the single most important contraindication in the immediate management?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Genitourinary Trauma & Urethral Injury)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/genitourinary-trauma/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Do NOT attempt blind transurethral catheterisation\n- **B.** Do NOT perform pelvis X-ray\n- **C.** Do NOT give intravenous analgesia\n- **D.** Do NOT place a pelvic binder\n- **E.** Do NOT give tetanus toxoid\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic triad of urethral injury is: blood at the urethral meatus, perineal/scrotal haematoma ('butterfly' ecchymosis), and a high-riding/floating prostate on DRE. Blind urethral catheterisation is strictly CONTRAINDICATED as it can convert a partial urethral tear into a complete transection and introduce infection into pelvic haematomas.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the gold standard diagnostic investigation to evaluate for urethral trauma in an adult male before any catheter insertion?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Genitourinary Trauma & Urethral Injury)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/genitourinary-trauma/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Retrograde Urethrogram (RUG) / Ascending Urethrography\n- **B.** Intravenous Pyelogram (IVP)\n- **C.** Renal ultrasound\n- **D.** Non-contrast pelvic CT scan\n- **E.** Rigid cystoscopy in the ED\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRetrograde Urethrography (RUG) is performed by injecting 20-30 ml of water-soluble contrast via a small Foley catheter tip placed in the navicular fossa of the distal penis with oblique pelvic radiographs. It definitively identifies the presence, site, and extent (partial vs complete) of urethral disruption.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old male presents with acute penile pain, a loud 'cracking' sound heard during vigorous intercourse, immediate detumescence, and a swollen, deviated, ecchymotic penis ('eggplant deformity'). What anatomical structure has ruptured?\n\n- **A.** Tunica albuginea of the corpus cavernosum (penile fracture)\n- **B.** Bulbospongiosus muscle\n- **C.** External urethral sphincter\n- **D.** Suspensory ligament of the penis\n- **E.** Superficial dorsal vein alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPenile fracture is caused by blunt trauma to the erect penis, causing rupture of the fibrous tunica albuginea enclosing the corpus cavernosum (and occasionally the corpus spongiosum/urethra). Hallmark features are sudden pain, cracking pop, rapid detumescence, and the 'eggplant deformity'. It is a surgical emergency requiring immediate exploration and repair.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 35-year-old driver with blunt lower abdominal trauma after a road collision has gross haematuria and suprapubic pain. CT cystography with 350 ml of instilled dilute contrast demonstrates contrast extravasation outlining loops of small bowel in the peritoneal cavity. What is the diagnosis and definitive management?\n\n- **A.** Intraperitoneal bladder rupture; requires emergent surgical exploration and formal operative repair\n- **B.** Extraperitoneal bladder rupture; managed conservatively with Foley catheter drainage for 14 days\n- **C.** Ureteral transection; requires nephrectomy\n- **D.** Renal contusion; bed rest only\n- **E.** Normal CT cystogram finding\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIntraperitoneal bladder rupture occurs when a distended bladder bursts at the dome (weakest point covered by peritoneum) from blunt compression. Contrast freely extravasates into the peritoneal cavity around the bowel loops. Intraperitoneal tears ALWAYS require emergency open or laparoscopic operative repair to prevent peritonitis and sepsis.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nIn blunt renal trauma, which clinical finding is the most common and important indication for contrast-enhanced multiphase abdominal CT imaging?\n\n- **A.** Gross haematuria, or microscopic haematuria with a systolic BP <90 mmHg\n- **B.** Microscopic haematuria with normal vital signs in a minor fall\n- **C.** Mild dysuria without pain\n- **D.** Presence of trace proteinuria on dipstick\n- **E.** Isolated minor rib contusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIndications for contrast CT in blunt renal trauma: (1) All cases of macroscopic (gross) haematuria; (2) Microscopic haematuria accompanied by shock (systolic BP <90 mmHg); (3) Rapid deceleration mechanism (fall from height, high-speed collision); (4) Significant flank contusion or lower rib fractures (10th-12th ribs).\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
0986345e-c4cd-4d0d-b80c-17b2d7c00b5e	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	head-reauma	Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage	article	# Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Other causes of increase ICP, including cerebral oedema and hypercapnia, Epileptic fits, Infection.\n- Cerebral arterioles remain sensitive to pC02 , however, with an increase pC02 resulting in marked arterial vasodilatation and unwanted increase ICP. Controlling pC02 to within normal levels is therefore important.\n- Assessment in the ED\n- Be aware that the priority for all emergency department patients is the stabilisation of airway, breathing and circulation (ABC) before attention to other injuries: patients with GCS 8 or less, ensure there is early involvement of an anaesthetist or critical care physician to provide appropriate airway management:\n- A trained member of staff should assess all patients presenting to an emergency department with a head injury within a maximum of 15 minutes of arrival at hospital.\n- Head injury examination\n- Consider the possibility of cervical spine injury in all cases Signs of base of skull fracture This is often a clinical diagnosis. One or more of the following may be seen:\n\n- Bilateral orbital bruising confined to the orbital margin ('panda eyes').\n- Subconjunctival haemorrhage (no posterior margin of bleeding seen).\n- Haemotympanum or bleeding from the auditory rneatus.\n- CSF otorrhoea or rhinorrhoea (± anosmia). Fluid mixtures containing relatively similar quantities of blood and CSF will separate into a 'double ring' when dropped onto blotting paper.\n- BattIe's sign: bruising over the mastoid process without local direct trauma follows a petrous temporal bone fracture but takes several days to appear.\n- Glasgow coma score (adults)\n\n- Score 1 2 3 4 5 6 Eyes Does not open eyes Opens eyes in response to painful stimuli Opens eyes in response to voice Opens eyes spontaneously Verbal Makes no sounds Incomprehensible sounds Utters inappropriate words Confused, disorientated Orientated, Motor Makes no movements Extension to painful stimuli(decerebrate response) Abnormal flexion to painful stimuli(decorticate response) Flexion/withdrawal to painful stimuli Localized to painful converses normally stimuli Obeys commands\n- Specific head injury. zEpidural haematoma\n- An epidural hematoma results when blood collects in the potential space between the skull and the dura mater.\n- lnjury to middle meningeal artery\n- CT reveal biconvex (football-shaped) mass.\n- Early recognition and evacuation reduce morbidity and mortality.\n\n- Subdural haematoma:\n- Subdural hematoma is caused by sudden acceleration-deceleration of brain parenchyma with subsequent tearing of the bridging dural veins.\n- This results in hematoma formation between the dura rnater and the arachnoid\n- Brains with extensive atrophy, such as in the elderly or in chronic alcoholics, are more susceptible to the development of acute subdural hematoma.\n- On CT scan, acute subdural hematomas are hyperdense (white), crescent-shaped lesions that cross suture lines\n\n- Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n- Traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage results from the disruption of the parenchyma and subarachnoid vessels and presents with blood in the cerebrospinal fluid.\n- Traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage is the most common CT abnormality in patients with moderate to severe T Bl.\n- CT scans performed 6 to 8 hours after injury are sensitive for detecting traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage.\n\n- Diffuse axonal injury_\n- Diffuse axonal injury is the disruption of axonal fibers in the white matter and brainstem.\n- Shearing forces on the neurons generated by sudden deceleration cause diffuse axonal injury.\n- CT scan of a patient with diffuse axonal injury may appear normal, however, classic CT findings include punctuate hemorrhagic injury along the gray white junction of\n\n- the cerebral cortex. n\n- Contusion and intracerebral haematoma\n- Contusions rnost commonly occur in the subfrontal cortex, in the frontal and temporal lobes, and, occasionally, in the occipital lobes\n- lntracerebral hemorrhage can occur days after significant blunt trauma, often at the site of resolving contusions.\n\n- CT immediately after injury maybe normal.\n- Obtain serial CTs if any change in mental status occurs in a patient with coagulopathy until the clot is stable\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\n(Nice guideline recommendations) Criteria for performing a CT head scan in Adults\n\nFor adults who have sustained a head injury and have any of the following risk\n- factors, perform a CT head scan within 1 hour of the risk factor being identified: GCS less than 13 on initial assessment in the emergency department.\n- GCS less than 15 at 2 hours after the injury on assessment in the emergency department. Suspected open or depressed skull fracture.\n- Any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum, 'panda' eyes, cerebrospinal fluid\n- leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign). Post-traumatic seizure.\n- Focal neurological deficit.\n- More than I episode of vomiting.\n- —Note: A provisional written radiology report should be made available within 1 hour\n- of the scan being performed Eocau-s-wi-h-any-gf-th.e-fo"owing-r.isk-factors-who-haye-experienced some loss of consciousness or amnesia since the injury,_perform a CT head scan within 8 hours-of-the-head-injury; Age 65 years or older.\n- Any history of bleeding or clotting disorders.\n- Dangerous mechanism of injury (a pedestrian or cyclist struck by a motor vehicle, an occupant ejected from a motor vehicle or a fall from a height of greater than 1 metre or 5 stairs).\n- More than 30 minutes' retrograde amnesia of events immediately before the head injury.\n- —Note: A provisional written radiology report should be made available within 1 hour of the scan being performed.\n\nAlgorithm 1: selecting people 16 and over for a CT head scan Person 16 or over presenting to the emergency department with a head injury Are any Of these risk factors present:\n- a GCS score of 12 or less on initial assessment in the emergency department\n- a GCS score of less than 15 at 2 hours after the injury on assessment in the emergency department\n- suspected open or depressed skull fracture\n- any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum, 'panda' eyes, cerebrospinal fluid leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign)\n- post-traumatic seizure\n- focal neurological deficit\n- more than 1 episode of vomiting Yes No Is there loss Of consciousness or amnesia since the head injury? Yes Are any Of these risk factors present:\n- age 65 years or over\n- any bleeding or clotting disorders (liver failure, haemophilia, taking anticoagulants or antiplatelets)\n- dangerous mechanism of injury (a pedestrian or cyclist struck by a motor vehicle, an occupant ejected from a motor vehicle, or fall from a height of more than 1 m or 5 stairs)\n- more than 30 minutes' retrograde amnesia Of events NO immediately before the head injury Yes NO they takütg anticoagulants (including vitamin antagonists, direct-acting oral anticoagulants, heparin and IOW molecular weight heparins) or antiplatelets (excluding aspirin monotherapy)?\n\nCT head scan not needed hour of any of the risk ctors being identified Make a provisional written radiology report available ithin 1 hour of the scan NICE Nation. nstitute for Hætth and scan w n hours of the injury, or within 1 hour if they present more than 8 hours after injury Make a provisional written radiology report available Within I hour Of the scan Consider a CT head scan within 8 hours of injury, or within 1 hour if they present more than 8 hours after injury Make a provisional written radiology report available Within I hour Of the scan Criteria for performing a CT head scan in under 16 years age:\n- For children who have sustained a head injury and have any of the following risk factors, perform a CT head scan within I hour of the risk factor being identified:\n- Suspicion of non-accidental injury\n- Post-traumatic seizure but no history of epilepsy.\n- On initial emergency department assessment, GCS less than 14, or for children under 1 year GCS (paediatric) less than 15.\n- At 2 hours after the injury, GCS less than 15.\n- Suspected open or depressed skull fracture or tense fontanelle.\n- Any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum, 'panda' eyes, cerebrospinal fluid leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign).\n- Focal neurological deficit.\n- For children under I year, presence of bruise, swelling or laceration of more than 5 cm on the head.\n- —Note: A provisional written radiology report should be made available within I hour of the scan being performed.\n\n- jury-and-have-more-than-lof-the following risk factors (and none listed above),_perform a CT head scan within 1 hour-of-the-risk-factors&eingjdentified;\n- Loss of consciousness lasting more than 5 minutes (witnessed).\n- Abnormal drowsiness.\n- Three or more discrete episodes of vomiting.\n- Dangerous mechanism of injury (high-speed road traffic accident either as pedestrian, cyclist or vehicle occupant, fall from a height of greater than 3 metres, high-speed injury from a projectile or other object).\n- Amnesia (antegrade or retrograde) lasting more than 5 minutes.\n- However, Assessment of amnesia will not be possible in preverbal children and is unlikely to be possible in children aged under 5 years.\n- Children who have sustained a head injury and have only 1 of the risk factors above should be observed for a minimum of 4 hours after the head injury. If during observation any of the risk factors below are identified, perform a CT head scan within 1 hour:\n- GCS less than 15\n- Further vomiting.\n- A further episode of abnormal drowsiness.\n\nAlgorithm 2: selecting people under 16 for a CT head scan Person under 16 presenting to the emergency department with a head injury Are any of these risk factors present:\n- suspicion Of non-accidental injury\n- post-traumatic seizure but no history of epilepsy\n- a CCS score Of less than 14 or, for children under I, a GCS (paediatric) score Of less than 15, on initial assessment in the emergency department\n- a GCS score of less than 15 at 2 hours after the injury\n- suspected open or depressed skull fracture, or tense fontanelle\n- any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum, 'panda' eyes, cerebrospinal fluid leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign)\n- focal neurological deficit\n- for children under 1 year, a bruise, swelling or laceration of more than 5 cm on the head Yes Are any of these risk factors present: No\n- loss of consciousness lasting more than 5 minutes (witnessed)\n- any bleeding or clotting disorders (liver failure, haemophilia, taking anticoagulants or antiplatelets)\n- abnormal drowsiness\n- 3 or more discrete episodes of vomiting\n- dangerous mechanism of injury (high-speed road traffic accident as a pedestrian, cyclist or vehicle occupant, fall from a height Of more than 3 m, high-speed injury from a projectile or other object)\n- amnesia (antegrade or retrograde) lasting more than 5 minutes (it will not be possible to assess amnesia in children Who are preverbal and is unlikely to be possible in children under 5) Yes, more than 1 factor Yes, 1 factor No Are any Of these risk factors present While observing:\n- a GCS score of less than 15\n- further vomiting\n- further episodes of abnormal drowsiness Yes NO Are they taking anticoagulants (including vitamin K antagonists, direct-acting oral anticoagulants, heparin and low molecular weight hoarinsi or antiDlatelets (excludina asoirin monotheraov)?\n\n- Yes ns era scan w n any of the risk factors being entified Make a provisional written radiology report available within ur of the scan NICE NationS hstnute tor Health and Cae hours of injury, or within I hour they present more than 8 hours after injury Make a provisional written radiology report available within 1 hour of the scan No CT head scan not needed Use clinical judgement to determine when further observation is needed a 2023. A" su*t\n- Investigating injuries to the cervical spine\n- Criteria for performing a CT cervical spine scan in adults\n- For adults who have sustained a head injury and have any of the following risk factors, perform a CT cervical spine scan within I hour of the risk factor being identified:\n- GCS less than 13 on initial assessment.\n- The patient has been intubated.\n- Plain X-rays are technically inadequate (for example, the desired view is unavailable).\n- Plain X-rays are suspicious or definitely abnormal.\n- A definitive diagnosis of cervical spine injury is needed urgently (for example, before surgery). The patient is having other body areas scanned for head injury or multi-region traurna.\n\n- The patient is alert and stable, there is clinical suspicion of cervical spine injury and any of the following apply: Age 65 years or older\n- Dangerous mechanism of injury (fall from a height of greater than I meter or 5 stairs; axial load to the head, for example, diving; high-speed motor vehicle collision; rollover rnotor accident; ejection from a rnotor vehicle; accident involving motorized recreational vehicles; bicycle collision)\n- Focal peripheral neurological deficit\n- Paraesthesia in the upper or lower limbs Eon peo jury,-and-haye-neckpain-or tenderness but no high-risk indications for a CT cervical spine scan do a CT cer-uicaLs pine-scan-within-Jhour-focany-of-these-risk-factors;\n- It is not considered safe to assess the range of movement in the neck.\n- Safe assessment of range of neck movement shows that the patient cannot actively rotate their neck to 45 degrees to the left and right.\n- the person has a condition predisposing them to a higher risk of injury to the cervical spine (for example, axial spondylarthritis) — Criteria for performing a CT cervical spine scan in children For children who have sustained a head injury, perform a CT cervical spine scan only if any of the following apply (because of the increased risk to the thyroid gland from ionising radiation and the generally lower risk of significant spinal injury):\n- GCS less than 13 on initial assessment.\n\n- The patient has been intubated.\n- Focal peripheral neurological signs.\n- Paraesthesia in the upper or lower limbs.\n- A definitive diagnosis of cervical spine injury is needed urgently (for example, before surgery).\n- The patient is having other body areas scanned for head injury or multi-region trauma.\n- There is strong clinical suspicion of injury despite normal X-rays.\n- Plain X-rays are technically difficult or inadequate.\n- Plain X-rays identify a significant bony injury.\n- Note: Do the scan within I hour of the risk factor being identified.\n- Three view cervical spine x-ray in Children.\n- For children who have sustained a head injury and have neck pain or tenderness but no indications for a CT cervical spine scan, perform 3-view cervical spine X-rays before assessing range of movement in the neck if either of these risk factors are identified:\n- Dangerous mechanism of injury (that is, fall from a height of greater than 1 metre or 5 stairs; axial load to the head, for example, diving; high-speed motor vehicle collision; rollover motor accident; ejection from a motor vehicle; accident involving motorized recreational vehicles; bicycle collision).\n- Safe assessrnent of range of movement in the neck is not possible.\n- child cannot actively rotate their neck 45 degrees to the left and right.\n- Note: The X-rays should be done within 1 hour of this risk factor being identified, and reviewed by a clinician trained in their interpretation\n- Assessing range of movement in the neck\n- Be aware that in adults and children who have sustained a head injury and in whom there is clinical suspicion of cervical spine injury, range of movement in the neck can\n\nbe assessed safely before imaging only if no high-risk factors and at least 1 of the following Iow-risk features apply. The patient: was involved in a simple rear-end motor vehicle collision.\n- is comfortable in a sitting position in the emergency department.\n- has been ambulatory at any time since injury.\n- has no midline cervical spine tenderness.\n- presents with delayed onset of neck pain.\n- ED management of serious head injury\n- Follow ABC protocol.\n- The primary goals of treatment are to maintain cerebral perfusion and oxygenation by optimizing intravascular volume and ventilation.\n- Recognize and treat elevated ICP —Airway & breathing\n- Treat any condition that compromises ventilation (e.g., altered mental status, facial/neck trauma, pneumothorax).\n- Patients with severe injury (GCS score of '8) require intubation. Circulation-Provide aggressive fluid resuscitation to prevent hypotension and secondary brain injury. Normal saline is recommended for volume resuscitation.\n- Maintain systolic blood pressure at 2100 mm Hg for patients 50 to 69 years old or at 2110 mm Hg for patients 15 to 49 years old or\n- 70 years old\n\n- —Pain control Give titrated IV opioid analgesia —Patient positioning\n- Raising the head of the bed to 30 degrees may improve cerebral blood flow by lowering ICP. —Temporary_byperventilation\n- Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep paC02 at 4.5 to 5kpa.\n- HyperventiIation will lower ICP in deterioration patient with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy. —Mannitol and hypertonic saline\n- Mannitol is osmotic diuretic.it is used to decrease ICP\n- Avoid mannitol in hypotensive patients\n- Hypertonic saline is alternative to mannitol in the patient who is not adequately fluid resuscitated or hypotensive. —Seizure treatment and prophylaxis\n- Prolonged seizures can worsen secondary injury.\n- Treat acute seizures with midazolam or lorazepam, and if seizures continue, treat as for status epilepticus.\n- Temperature-control\n\n- —Glucose control maintain at 100 to 180 milligrams/dL or 5.55 to 9.99 mmol/L\n- Involving the neurosurgical department\n- Discuss with a neurosurgeon the care of all patients with new, surgically significant abnormalities on imaging. The definition of 'surgically significant' should be developed by local neurosurgical centres and agreed with referring hospitals, along with referral procedures. Regardless of imaging, other reasons for discussing a patient's care plan with a\n- neurosurgeon include: Persisting coma (GCS 8 or less) after initial resuscitation.\n- Unexplained confusion which persists for more than 4 hours.\n- Deterioration in GCS score after admission (greater attention should be paid to\n- motor response deterioration). Progressive focal neurological signs.\n-\n- A seizure without full recovery. Definite or suspected penetrating injury.\n- A cerebrospinal fluid leak\n- Transfer from hospital to a neuroscience unit\n- Transfer is often urgent, complete the initial resuscitation and stabilisation of the patient and establish comprehensive monitoring before transfer to avoid complications during the journey.\n- Do not transport a patient with persistent hypotension, despite resuscitation, until the cause of the hypotension has been identified and the patient stabilized.\n- Intubate and ventilate all patients with GCS 8 or less requiring transfer to a neuroscience unit. Antubate-and-yentilate-thepatient-immediate.'y-in-the-fo."owi.n gsircumstances:\n\n-\n- Coma — not obeying commands, not speaking, not eye opening (that is, GCS 8 or Loss of protective laryngeal reflexes. Ventilatory insufficiency as judged by blood gases: hypoxaemia (Pa02 < 13 kPa on oxygen) or hypercarbia (PaC02\n- 6 kPa). Spontaneous hyperventilation causing PaC02 < 4 kPa. Irregular respirations —Use intubation and ventilation before the start of the journey in the following circumstances: Significantly deteriorating conscious level (1 or more points on the motor score), even if not coma.\n- Unstable fractures of the facial skeleton.\n- Copious bleeding into mouth (for example, from skull base fracture).\n- Seizure\n- Ventilate an intubated patient with muscle relaxation and appropriate short-acting sedation and analgesia. Aim for a Pa02 greater than 13 kPa, PaC02 4.5 to 5.0 kPa unless there is clinical or radiological evidence of raised intracranial pressure, in which case more aggressive hyperventilation is justified.\n- Maintain the mean arterial pressure at 80 mm Hg or more by infusion of fluid and vasopressors as indicated.\n- Admission: use the criteria below for admitting patients to hospital following a head injury:\n- Patients with new, clinically significant abnormalities on imaging.\n- Patients whose GCS has not returned to 15 after imaging, regardless of the imaging results.\n- When a patient has indications for CT scanning but this cannot be done within the appropriate period, either because CT is not available or because the patient is not\n\nsufficiently cooperative to allow scanning.\n- Continuing worrying signs (for example, persistent vomiting, severe headaches) of concern to the clinician.\n- Other sources of concern to the clinician (for example, drug or alcohol intoxication, other injuries, shock, suspected non-accidental injury, meningism, cerebrospinal fluid leak)\n- Observation of admitted patients\n- For patients admitted for head injury observation the minimum acceptable documented neurological observations are: GCS; pupil size and reactivity; limb movements; respiratory rate; heart rate; blood pressure; temperature; blood oxygen saturation.\n- Perform and record observations on a half-hourly basis until GCS equal to 15 has been achieved. The minimum frequency of observations for patients with GCS equal to 15 should be as follows, starting after the initial assessrnent in the emergency department:\n- Half-hourly for 2 hours.\n- Then I-hourly for 4 hours.\n- Then 2-hourly thereafter.\n- Discharge and follow-up\n- After normal imaging of the cervical spine the clinician may conclude that the risk of injury to the cervical spine is low enough to warrant transfer to the community, as long as the patient has returned to GCS equal to 15 and their clinical examination is normal, and no other factors that would warrant a hospital admission are present (for example, drug or alcohol intoxication, other injuries, shock, suspected non-accidental injury, meningism, cerebrospinal fluid leak) and there are appropriate support structures for safe transfer to the community and for subsequent care (for example, competent supervision at home.\n\n- Discharge advise\n- Printed advice for patients, families and carers should be age-appropriate and include:\n- Details of the nature and severity of the injury.\n- A specification that a responsible adult should stay with the patient for the first 24 hours after their injury.\n- Details about the recovery process, including the fact that some patients may appear to make a quick recovery but later experience difficulties or complications.\n- Contact details of community and hospital services in case of delayed complications.\n- Information about return to everyday activities, including school, work, sports and driving.\n- Details of support organisations. previous  Next 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_06_p9.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_07_p11.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 11 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_08_p12.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 12 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 9](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_09_p14.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 9 (Page 14 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 10](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_10_p15.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 10 (Page 15 Reference)*\n\n![Head Reauma - Clinical Figure 11](/lessons/images/sba/major-trauma-injury/head-reauma/figure_11_p23.jpeg)\n*Head Reauma — Clinical Figure 11 (Page 23 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	8	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e8799b93-091f-419a-b8c9-058e00690fe5	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-head-trauma	Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 21-year-old rugby player is struck on the temporoparietal skull. He experiences a brief loss of consciousness, recovers fully, and converses normally for 2 hours (lucid interval). Later, he rapidly deteriorates with vomiting, GCS drop to 6, and a dilated, unreactive right pupil (Hutchinson's pupil). What intracranial pathology is most likely?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Extradural (Epidural) Haematoma secondary to Middle Meningeal Artery laceration\n- **B.** Acute Subdural Haematoma from bridging cortical veins\n- **C.** Traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage\n- **D.** Diffuse axonal injury\n- **E.** Post-concussion syndrome\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nExtradural haematoma (EDH) typically results from a blow to the pterion fracturing the squamous temporal bone and tearing the middle meningeal artery. The classic presentation is a 'lucid interval' followed by rapid herniation with ipsilateral uncal pupil dilation and contralateral hemiparesis. CT shows a hyperdense, biconvex (lenticular) collection limited by cranial sutures.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_06_p9.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the characteristic appearance of an Acute Subdural Haematoma (SDH) on a non-contrast brain CT scan?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hyperdense crescentic (concave / sickle-shaped) collection that crosses cranial sutures and extends along the cerebral convexity\n- **B.** Biconvex, lenticular hyperdensity strictly limited by cranial suture lines\n- **C.** Punctate micro-haemorrhages confined to the grey-white matter junction\n- **D.** Star-shaped blood collection confined to the basal cisterns\n- **E.** Widespread cerebral atrophy without extra-axial fluid\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute Subdural Haematoma (SDH) arises from rupture of bridging cortical veins between the cerebral cortex and dural venous sinuses (common in elderly, alcoholics, and high-energy deceleration). CT demonstrates a hyperdense crescentic (crescent-shaped) extra-axial collection that freely crosses cranial suture lines, causing significant midline shift.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_07_p11.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich physical examination signs are classic clinical indicators of a Basilar Skull Fracture?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Battle's sign (mastoid ecchymosis), Raccoon eyes (periorbital ecchymosis), Haemotympanum, and CSF rhinorrhoea/otorrhoea\n- **B.** Marcus Gunn pupil and papilloedema\n- **C.** Horner's syndrome and facial nerve spasm\n- **D.** Bilateral proptosis and chemosis only\n- **E.** Nuchal rigidity and positive Kernig's sign\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBasilar skull fracture signs: (1) Battle's sign (retroauricular ecchymosis over mastoid process); (2) 'Panda / Raccoon' eyes (bilateral periorbital ecchymosis with tarsal plate sparing); (3) Haemotympanum or blood in external auditory canal; (4) CSF rhinorrhoea or otorrhoea (Halo sign on filter paper). Nasogastric tubes are contraindicated.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_08_p12.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guideline NG232, which adult patient with head injury requires an immediate emergency non-contrast CT brain scan within 1 hour?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** GCS 13 on initial assessment in the ED, or GCS <15 at 2 hours post-injury\n- **B.** Amnesia of 10 minutes prior to impact with GCS 15 and no other features\n- **C.** Single episode of vomiting 30 minutes after injury with normal GCS\n- **D.** Minor frontal scalp haematoma in an alert 25-year-old\n- **E.** Age 66 with a minor forehead bump and no amnesia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG232 criteria for CT brain within 1 hour: GCS <13 on initial ED assessment; GCS <15 at 2 hours post-injury; suspected open or depressed skull fracture; signs of basal skull fracture; post-traumatic seizure; focal neurological deficit; >1 episode of vomiting in adults.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_09_p14.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 78-year-old male on Warfarin presents with confusion 3 weeks after a minor mechanical fall at home. Brain CT reveals a hypodense (dark), crescentic fluid collection along the right hemisphere with midline shift. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Chronic Subdural Haematoma (SDH)\n- **B.** Acute Extradural Haematoma\n- **C.** Acute Subarachnoid Haemorrhage\n- **D.** Ischaemic stroke with haemorrhagic transformation\n- **E.** Glioblastoma multiforme\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nChronic SDH is common in elderly patients (cerebral atrophy stretches bridging veins, predisposing to minor tearing with minimal trauma, amplified by anticoagulation). Over weeks, blood liquefies and becomes hypodense (dark) on CT compared to acute hyperdense (white) blood. Symptomatic chronic SDH with mass effect is treated with burr hole craniostomy.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_10_p15.jpeg)\n\n\n![Supplementary Clinical Reference 6 (Head Trauma & Intracranial Haemorrhage)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/head-trauma/figure_11_p23.jpeg)\n\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
75f1a0a6-fe0e-4b85-b4fd-8c26425499a6	8c878187-e4b3-4ade-b3c8-b1ce3132ae40	sba-quiz-trauma-laryngotracheal-injury	Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old motorcyclist sustains a 'clothesline' impact to the anterior neck from a low-hanging wire. In the ED, he has hoarseness, anterior neck tenderness, extensive subcutaneous emphysema over the neck and upper chest, and mild stridor. What is the clinical triad pathognomonic of acute Laryngotracheal Disruption?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/laryngotracheal-injury/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hoarseness (dysphonia), Subcutaneous emphysema, and Palpable fracture/loss of thyroid cartilage prominence\n- **B.** Stridor, Dysphagia, and Tracheal deviation to the left\n- **C.** Hypotension, Distended neck veins, and Muffled heart sounds\n- **D.** Horner's syndrome, Carotid bruit, and Syncope\n- **E.** Trismus, Drooling, and Uvular deviation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic triad of acute blunt laryngeal fracture/tracheal disruption: (1) Hoarseness / altered voice; (2) Subcutaneous emphysema of the neck; (3) Palpable crepitus or loss of normal thyroid cartilage anatomical landmarks. Haemoptysis and respiratory distress often accompany.\n\n![Clinical Algorithm & Advanced Reference (Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/laryngotracheal-injury/figure_06_p9.jpeg)\n\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the safest method to secure the airway in a patient with suspected acute blunt laryngotracheal fracture and impending airway compromise?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/laryngotracheal-injury/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Awake flexible fiberoptic intubation in the operating theatre (or tracheostomy under local anaesthesia) with a surgeon scrubbed and prepared\n- **B.** Standard rapid sequence induction (RSI) with blind bougie insertion\n- **C.** Laryngeal mask airway (LMA) insertion\n- **D.** Needle cricothyroidotomy directly through the fractured thyroid cartilage\n- **E.** Deep sedation with propofol and bag-mask ventilation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn blunt laryngeal fracture, standard blind or rapid sequence intubation with paralysis carries severe danger of completely transecting a partial tracheal tear or creating a false tract in the mediastinum. The safest approach is awake flexible fiberoptic intubation (or awake surgical tracheostomy under local anaesthesia) in the OR with ENT/thoracic surgery scrubbed.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nIn penetrating neck trauma, what anatomical landmarks define Neck Zone II, which is the most commonly injured and surgically accessible zone?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/laryngotracheal-injury/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Between the cricoid cartilage inferiorly and the angle of the mandible superiorly\n- **B.** Between the clavicles and the cricoid cartilage\n- **C.** Above the angle of the mandible to the skull base\n- **D.** Posterior to the anterior border of the trapezius\n- **E.** Submandibular triangle only\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAnatomical neck zones for penetrating trauma: Zone I = clavicles/sternal notch to cricoid cartilage (high vascular mortality, thoracic inlet access required); Zone II = cricoid cartilage to angle of mandible (most commonly injured, easily examined and explored); Zone III = angle of mandible to skull base (difficult exposure).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich 'Hard Sign' of penetrating aerodigestive tract injury mandates immediate operative neck exploration rather than imaging in a stable patient?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/laryngotracheal-injury/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Massive subcutaneous emphysema, bubbling air through the neck wound, or severe stridor\n- **B.** Superficial skin laceration <1 cm\n- **C.** Mild dysphagia\n- **D.** Localized non-expanding haematoma\n- **E.** Small puncture mark without active bleeding\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\n'Hard signs' of airway injury in penetrating trauma: air bubbling from wound, massive expanding subcutaneous emphysema, severe respiratory distress/stridor. 'Hard signs' of vascular injury: expanding/pulsatile haematoma, active arterial bleeding, absent distal pulses, bruit/thrill. Both mandate immediate surgical exploration.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhy is surgical cricothyroidotomy CONTRAINDICATED in children under the age of 8 to 12 years with airway trauma?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Laryngotracheal Injury & Airway Trauma)](/lessons/images/sba/trauma/laryngotracheal-injury/figure_05_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** The cricothyroid membrane is extremely small and the cricoid cartilage is the only complete ring supporting the upper airway; damage causes subglottic stenosis\n- **B.** Children do not possess a cricoid cartilage\n- **C.** The thyroid cartilage is entirely calcified\n- **D.** Children never experience airway trauma\n- **E.** The trachea is located posterior to the oesophagus in children\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn young children (<8-12 years), surgical cricothyroidotomy is contraindicated because the cricoid cartilage is the narrowest part of the airway and the sole circumferential cartilaginous support of the subglottis. Incising it risks crushing the cartilage, resulting in permanent subglottic stenosis. Needle cricothyroidotomy with jet insufflation or formal tracheostomy is preferred.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4f4c483a-d7d4-4d95-95f1-07c0252b5a9d	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-lower-gi-bleeding	Acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 72-year-old male with a history of hypertension presents with a sudden onset of painless, large-volume, bright red to maroon rectal bleeding (haematochezia). Pulse is 95 bpm, BP 124/78 mmHg. Abdominal examination is entirely soft and non-tender. What is the single most common cause of acute lower gastrointestinal bleeding in adults?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/lower-gi-bleeding/figure_01_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Colonic Diverticular Bleeding (Diverticulosis, causing 30-40% of cases)\n- **B.** Haemorrhoids\n- **C.** Colorectal cancer\n- **D.** Ischaemic colitis\n- **E.** Angiodysplasia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nColonic diverticular bleeding is the leading cause of acute major lower GI bleeding in adults (responsible for 30-40% of cases). It occurs when asymmetric mechanical shear stress ruptures the penetrating vasa recta branch stretching across the dome of a diverticulum, producing painless, brisk haematochezia. Approximately 75-80% stop bleeding spontaneously.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich clinical risk score is recommended by the British Society of Gastroenterology (BSG) guidelines to risk-stratify acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding and identify patients safe for outpatient investigation?\n\n- **A.** Oakland Score (score <= 8 defines safe outpatient management)\n- **B.** Glasgow-Blatchford Score\n- **C.** Rockall Score\n- **D.** Alvarado Score\n- **E.** Ranson Score\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe BSG 2019 guidelines strongly recommend the Oakland Score for acute lower GI bleeding. Incorporating age, sex, prior lower GI bleed, DRE findings, heart rate, systolic BP, and haemoglobin, an Oakland score <= 8 identifies low-risk patients who can be safely discharged from the ED for outpatient investigation.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 68-year-old male presents with massive, bright red haematochezia and acute cardiovascular collapse (HR 135 bpm, BP 76/40 mmHg). After initial fluid resuscitation, what mandatory diagnostic examination must be considered before assuming the haemorrhage is from the colon?\n\n- **A.** Urgent Upper GI Endoscopy (OGD) to rule out an exsanguinating upper gastrointestinal bleed with rapid transit\n- **B.** Immediate exploratory laparotomy\n- **C.** Barium enema\n- **D.** Oral iron therapy\n- **E.** Wait 24 hours for elective colonoscopy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nApproximately 10-15% of patients presenting with severe, brisk haematochezia and haemodynamic shock actually have an Upper GI source (e.g. massive duodenal ulcer, aorto-enteric fistula) with hyperperistaltic transit. BSG guidelines mandate emergency upper GI endoscopy (OGD) in patients with massive haematochezia and hemodynamic instability before proceeding to lower GI investigations.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the diagnostic and therapeutic interventional modality of choice for an unstable patient with ongoing, active severe lower GI bleeding who cannot undergo colonoscopy?\n\n- **A.** Catheter Angiography and Transcatheter Embolisation (preceded by CT Angiography if feasible)\n- **B.** Emergency blind total colectomy\n- **C.** Capsule endoscopy in the ED\n- **D.** Tagged red blood cell scan alone\n- **E.** Intravenous vasopressin infusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBSG guidelines recommend that patients with hemodynamically significant, active lower GI bleeding should undergo urgent CT Angiography (CTA), which detects bleeding rates as low as 0.3-0.5 ml/min. If positive, immediate catheter angiography and superselective transcatheter embolisation (with microcoils) achieves definitive haemostasis with low complication rates.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat vascular malformation of dilated mucosal and submucosal tortuous venules/capillaries, classically located in the caecum and ascending colon of elderly patients and associated with aortic stenosis (Heyde's syndrome), is a major cause of recurrent lower GI bleeding?\n\n- **A.** Angiodysplasia (Arteriovenous Malformation)\n- **B.** Dieulafoy's lesion\n- **C.** Cavernous haemangioma\n- **D.** Kaposi's sarcoma\n- **E.** Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nColonic angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia) is degenerative dilation of submucosal vessels, most common in the caecum and right colon of patients >60 years. Heyde's syndrome is the triad of aortic stenosis, acquired von Willebrand disease (loss of high-molecular-weight multimers sheared by the stenotic valve), and bleeding from intestinal angiodysplasias, which resolves after aortic valve replacement.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	33	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e2a2207c-1329-4142-affd-d321d2b5d725	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	community-acquired-pneumonia-in-children	Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children	article	# Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nCyanosis.\n- Raised respiratory rate (greater than 60 breaths per minute, age 0—5 months; greater than 50 breaths per minute, age 6—12 months; greater than 40 breaths per minute, age older than 12 months). Signs of increased work of breathing, such as chest indrawing and nasal flaring.\n- Persistently focal crackles. Oxygen saturation of 95% or less in air.\n- Absent breath sounds with a dull percussion note (possibility of pneumonia complicated by an effusion).\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nChest radiography, acute phase reactant (inflammation markers) and CRP should not be considered a routine investigation in children thought to have community acquired pneumonia (CAP).\n- Microbiological diagnosis should only be attempted in children with severe pneumonia sufficient to require paediatric intensive care admission, or those with complications of CAP.\n- Microbiological methods used should include:\n- Blood culture\n- Nasopharyngeal secretions and/or nasal swabs for viral detection by PCR and/or immunofluorescence\n- Acute and convalescent serology for respiratory viruses, Mycoplasma and Chlamydia\n- If present, pleural fluid should be sent for microscopy, culture, pneumococcal antigen detection and/or PCR Management\n- Children under I month refer to paediatric specialist\n\n- Families of children who are well enough to be cared for at home should be given information on managing fever, preventing dehydration and identifying any deterioration.\n- Patients whose oxygen saturation is <92% while breathing air should be treated with oxygen given by nasal cannulae, high flow delivery device, head box or face mask to maintain oxygen saturation\n- 92%.\n- Plasma sodium, potassium, urea and/or creatinine should be measured at baseline and at least daily when on intravenous fluids.\n- Antibiotic therapy;\n- All children with a clear clinical diagnosis of pneumonia should receive antibiotics as bacterial and viral pneumonia cannot reliably be distinguished from each other.\n- Choice of antibiotics\n\nChildren age & severity Children under 1 month First-choice oral antibiotic for children 1 month and over if non-severe symptoms or signs First-choice antibiotic(s) for children 1 month and over If severe symptoms or signs Antibiotics Refer to paediatric specialist\n- Amoxicillin\n-\n- Alternative oral antibiotics for penicillin allergy or if amoxicillin unsuitable — Clarithromycin, erythromycin (in pregnancy),or Doxycycline (>12 years) Co-amoxiclav oral/lV\n- if atypical pathogen suspected\n- then: Co-amoxiclav with oral/lV Clarithromycin or erythromycin (in pregnancy) Alternative oral antibiotics for\n- penicillin allergy — consult with microbiologist\n\n- Complications Empyema\n- Lung abscesses\n- Pleural effusion\n- Septicaemia\n- Respiratory failure\n- Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/community-acquired-pneumonia-in-children/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology-respiratory/community-acquired-pneumonia-in-children/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Children — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 07:07:45.576678+00	\N
c7ade838-a24c-4a9e-b131-6f037c858a2b	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	pseudomonas-aeruginosa-legionella-pneumophilia-and-bortonella-henslae-bacteria	Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Legionella Pneumophilia and Bortonella Henslae - Bacteria	article	Pseudomonas Aeruginosa\nGram-negative rod transmitted via direct/indirect contact\nlikely in the hospital environment.\nLegionella Pneumophilia\nGram-negative coccobacilli are transmitted via respiratory\naerosol.\nLegionnaires disease\nDiagnosis: Legionella antigen detection in urine\nTreatment: Quinolones like levofloxacin or macrolides like\nclarithromycin\nBartonella Henselae\nGram negative bacilli or coccobacilli\nCat-scratch disease (CSD) or felinosis is an infectious disease\ncaused by Bartonella henselae most often results from a\nscratch or bite of a cat.\n\n/2026, 10:54 PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILIA AND BORTONELLA HENSLAE-BACTERIA | MRCEM EX…\n\n![Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Legionella Pneumophilia and Bortonella Henslae - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/pseudomonas-aeruginosa-legionella-pneumophilia-and-bortonella-henslae-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Legionella Pneumophilia and Bortonella Henslae - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n/2026, 10:54 PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILIA AND BORTONELLA HENSLAE-BACTERIA | MRCEM EX…\n\n/2026, 10:54 PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILIA AND BORTONELLA HENSLAE-BACTERIA | MRCEM EX…	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
61021717-9411-4a1f-9476-84032f53fd9f	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	sexually-transmitted-bacteria	Sexually Transmitted Bacteria	article	Sexually Transmitted Bacteria:\nChlamydia Trachomatis:\nObligate intracellular bacterium transmitted vis sexual and\nperipartum causing Chlamydia, trachoma, ophthalmia\nneonatorum, pneumonia.\nOphthalmia neonatorum: is conjunctivitis occurring within the\nfirst 4 weeks of life.\nThe most serious cause is infection with Neisseria gonorrhea\nin first week most likely in 3-4 days of life, but in the UK, it is\ncaused more commonly by Chlamydia in first 28 days of life.\nNote: Conjunctivitis in first week of life is caused by Neisseria\ngonorrhea\nOther species of chlamydia causing pneumonia in humans\nChlamydophila pneumophila infect humans causes\npneumonia.\nChlamydophila psittaci infects birds. Humans are infected\nprimarily by inhaling organisms in dry bird faeces.\nNeisseria Gonorrhea:\nGram negative diplococci transmitted via sexual and\nperipartum.\nGeneral features of Neisseria spp. (Neisseria gonorrhea and\nNeisseria meningitis)\nComparison Between Neisseria\nGonorrhea and\nChlamydia trachomatis:\n\n![Sexually Transmitted Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/sexually-transmitted-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Sexually Transmitted Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Sexually Transmitted Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/sexually-transmitted-bacteria/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Sexually Transmitted Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	19	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
0b587d2e-8065-4edb-9c96-6e9778c5ea30	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	absorption-of-vitamins-in-small-intestine	Absorption of Vitamins in Small Intestine	article	Vitamins absorption\nFat soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) are incorporated into\nmicelles and absorb along with other lipids.\nMost water-soluble vitamins are absorbed by Na+ -\ndependent cotransport mechanisms.\nVitamin B12 is absorbed in the ileum and requires intrinsic\nfactors.\nThe vitamin B12-intrinsic factor complex binds to a receptor\non the ileal cells and is absorbed.\nGastrectomy results in loss of parietal cells, which are source\nof intrinsic factor. Injection of vitamin B12 required to prevent\npernicious anemia\nIleectomy results in loss of absorption of vitamin B12-\nintrinsic factor complex and thus requires injection of\nvitamin B12	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
90b46537-e47f-4d63-84d7-42b41cf4eb3e	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	bile-and-bile-salts	Bile and Bile Salts	article	Bile\nBile is an aqueous, alkaline, greenish-yellow liquid whose\nmain function is to emulsify fats in the small intestine and to\neliminate substances from the liver. The liver produces 0.25-\n1L of bile per day.\nBile contains bile salts, phospholipid, cholesterol, and bile\npigments.\nFormation of bile\nBile is produced continuously by hepatocytes.\nBile drains into the hepatic ducts and is stored in the\ngallbladder for subsequent release.\nCholeretic agents increase the formation of bile.\nConstituents of Bile\nBile is made up of bile acids, cholesterol, phospholipids,\nbile pigments (such as bilirubin and biliverdin), electrolytes\nand water.\nThese are split into two main groups, known as the bile\nacid-dependent and bile acid-independent components:\nThe bile acid-dependent component is produced by\nhepatocytes. The hepatocytes secrete bile acids, bile\npigments and cholesterol into canaliculi, which are small\nchannels that transport the bile acid dependent portion\ntowards the bile ducts.\nThe bile acid-independent component is made by the\nductal cells that line the bile ducts. These cells secrete an\nalkaline solution - similar to the fluid made by pancreatic\nduct cells. The hormone secretin stimulates this secretion.\nBoth the dependent and independent components of bile\nenter the intrahepatic bile ducts which drain into the biliary\ntree, a series of ducts which transport bile from the liver to\nthe gallbladder and duodenum.\nBile is continuously produced, but we only need it during and\nafter meals. Therefore, the gallbladder concentrates and stores bile, by removing the water and ions.\nAfter eating, the hormone cholecystokinin is released from\nthe duodenum (in response to small peptides and fatty acids\nin the duodenum). This stimulates gallbladder contraction\nand relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, thus allowing bile to flow\ninto the duodenum.\nRecirculation of bile acids to the liver\nThe terminal ileum contains a Na+ -bile acid cotransporter,\nwhich is a secondary active transporter that recirculates bile\nacids to the liver.\nBecause bile acids are not recirculated until they reach the\nterminal ileum, bile acids are present for maximal absorption\nof lipids throughout the upper small intestine.\nAfter ileal resection, bile acids do not recirculate to the liver\nbut are excreted in feces. The bile acid pool is thereby\ndepleted, and fat absorption is impaired, resulting in\nsteatorrhea.\nBile salts\nBile salts are amphipathic molecules because they have both\nhydrophilic and hydrophobic portions. In aqueous solution, bile\nsalts orient themselves around the droplet of lipid and keep\nthe liquid droplets dispersed (emulsification). Aid in intestinal\ndigestion and absorption of lipids by emulsifying and\nsolubilizing them in micelles.\nProduction\nPrimary bile acids (cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid)\nare synthesized from Cholesterol by hepatocytes.\nIn the intestine, bacteria convert a portion of each of the\nprimary bile acids to secondary bile acids (deoxycholic acid\nand lithocholic acid).\nSynthesis of new bile acids occur as needed, to replace bile\nacids that are excreted in the feces.\nThe bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine to form\ntheir respective bile salts.\nFunction of bile acids\n\nEmulsification of lipids to increase the surface area available\nfor digestion by pancreatic lipase.\nMicelles formation to facilitate lipid transport and absorption.\nStimulate the bile production by osmotically attracting water\nand electrolytes into the bile canaliculi.\nRegulation of bile acid synthesis via negative feedback\nmechanism	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
eb76c254-d805-4b52-9ac4-760cdbb3a58a	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	uk-immunization-schedule	Uk - Immunization Schedule	article	UK Immunization Schedule:\nThe routine immunization schedule\nfrom February 2022\nNote: Trade name is not necessary to remember.\n\n![Uk - Immunization Schedule - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/uk-immunization-schedule/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Uk - Immunization Schedule — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nSelective immunisation programmes\nAdditional vaccines for individuals\nwith underlying medical conditions\n\n![Uk - Immunization Schedule - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/uk-immunization-schedule/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Uk - Immunization Schedule — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Uk - Immunization Schedule - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/uk-immunization-schedule/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Uk - Immunization Schedule — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	21	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
46cdb639-24b1-4754-9943-b96e2b632f5f	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-croup	Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis) — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis) — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nAn 18-month-old boy is brought to the ED at 2 AM with a sudden onset of a harsh, barking seal-like cough, hoarseness, and inspiratory stridor that worsens when he is distressed. His temperature is 38.0C. What is the most common viral causative agent of Croup?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis))](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/croup/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Parainfluenza Virus (predominantly Type 1)\n- **B.** Respiratory Syncytial Virus\n- **C.** Influenza A\n- **D.** Adenovirus\n- **E.** Epstein-Barr virus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHuman Parainfluenza Virus (types 1 and 2) accounts for >75-80% of croup cases. Peak incidence is in infants and toddlers aged 6 months to 3 years, typically occurring in late autumn and winter.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guidelines and the Westley Croup Score, what is the single most effective first-line medication for all children presenting with mild, moderate, or severe croup?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis))](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/croup/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oral Dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg single dose)\n- **B.** Oral amoxicillin for 7 days\n- **C.** Inhaled warm steam / humidified air\n- **D.** Oral codeine linctus\n- **E.** Intravenous hydrocortisone 200mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE recommends a single dose of oral Dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg) for all children with croup regardless of severity. It reduces hospital admission rates, shortens ED stays, reduces return visits, and decreases the need for nebulised adrenaline. (Soluble prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg is an alternative if dexamethasone is unavailable).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 2-year-old boy presents with severe croup: he has continuous inspiratory stridor at rest, marked sternal recession, agitation, and tracheal tug. While waiting for oral dexamethasone to take effect, what rapid-acting rescue therapy should be administered?\n\n- **A.** Nebulised Adrenaline (1:1,000 / 1 mg/ml, dose 0.5 ml/kg up to max 5ml) with oxygen\n- **B.** Nebulised salbutamol 5mg\n- **C.** Nebulised ipratropium bromide\n- **D.** Subcutaneous terbutaline\n- **E.** Oral paracetamol alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor moderate-to-severe croup with stridor at rest and marked retractions, Nebulised Adrenaline (L-adrenaline 1:1,000, 4-5 ml undiluted driven by oxygen) produces rapid alpha-1 mediated mucosal vasoconstriction, dramatically shrinking subglottic airway oedema within 10-30 minutes. The child must be observed for at least 2-4 hours post-nebulisation due to the risk of rebound oedema.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat classic radiological finding is visible on an anteroposterior (AP) soft-tissue neck radiograph in acute viral croup?\n\n- **A.** 'Steeple sign' (symmetric subglottic tracheal narrowing)\n- **B.** 'Thumbprint sign' (enlarged epiglottis)\n- **C.** Widened retropharyngeal soft tissue space\n- **D.** Pneumomediastinum\n- **E.** Subcutaneous foreign body\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe 'Steeple sign' (subglottic tracheal narrowing resembling an inverted church steeple) is seen on AP neck X-rays due to circumferential mucosal oedema of the cricoid ring. (Note: the 'Thumbprint sign' on lateral neck radiograph indicates acute epiglottitis). Routine X-rays are not required and are indicated only if diagnosis is atypical.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich clinical feature strongly suggests Acute Epiglottitis or Bacterial Tracheitis rather than typical viral croup?\n\n- **A.** High toxic fever (>39C), absence of barking cough, drooling of saliva, and preference for the 'tripod' sniffing position\n- **B.** Barking cough with mild hoarseness\n- **C.** Coryza for 2 days followed by symptoms worse at night\n- **D.** Improvement with cool night air\n- **E.** Normal swallowing of liquids\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute Epiglottitis (traditionally caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b or Group A Strep) presents with toxic appearance, high fever, severe sore throat, dysphagia, DROOLING, absent barking cough, and the classic 'tripod position' (sitting forward with neck extended). It is a life-threatening airway emergency; do NOT examine the throat with a tongue depressor.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
17563649-d85b-4d21-aaa0-52a14708b2c9	ef15ba2e-2dca-4639-9b94-5740ccb572f9	mock-i-paper-i	Mock 1 — Paper 1 (Clinical SBA Exam)	quiz	# Mock - I Paper - I\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer scenario. Read carefully before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\nMrcem Primary Complete Course ,High Yield Stuff and Questions Bank ( For Diet ii Late 2026)\nOverview  Comments\nShare your score colleagues\n\nLinkedIn  Facebook  Twitter\n\n## Question 19 of 90\n\n2. A 54-year-old man presents with visual impairment.\nWhen testing the pupillary light reaction, the right pupil\ndilates when the light source moves from the right pupil to\nthe left.\nWhich nerve is likely to be abnormal?\nIn this question examiner is more focused on left\neye that means when light source is tested in left\neye, left eye do not constrict that means loss of\nipsilateral direct pupillary reflex and right pupil\ndilation refers to loss of contralateral consensual\npupillary light reflex.\nIn Complete transection of optic nerve:\nloss of ipsilateral direct pupillary reflex\nloss of contralateral consensual pupillary light reflex\nOptic nerve: (High yield)\nTransmit special sensory information from the\nretina of the eye to the primary visual cortex of the\nbrain.\nEnters skull via optic canal of the sphenoid bone\n\n## Question 2 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left optic nerve\n- **B.** Right oculomotor nerve\n- **C.** Ophthalmic division of the left trigeminal nerve\n- **D.** Right optic nerve\n- **E.** Left oculomotor nerve\n\n## Question 1 of 54\n\nNote: Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD):\nSeen in optic nerve disease or asymmetrical disease\nof the retina in which pupils respond differently to\nlight stimuli shone in one eye at a time\nswinging light test if there is no RAPD the pupils of\nboth eyes constrict equally regardless of which eye\nis stimulated by the light.\nIn an abnormal swinging-light test if there is a\nRAPD there is less pupil constriction in the eye with\nthe retinal or optic nerve disease\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pituitary adenoma: Pituitary gland lies in close\n> proximity to optic chiasm,tumor of pituitary gland\n> compresses optic nerve at chiasm produces visual\n> defect called bitemporal hemianopia\n> 5. Transverse thoracic plane lies at the level of which of the\n> following anatomically relations?\n> Transverse thoracic plane lies at the level of 2nd\n> intercostal space and intervertebral disc between\n> T4 & T5\n> CLAPTRAP is useful mnemonic to remember\n> structures transected by transverse thoracic plane.\n\n- **C.** cardiac plexus\nL: ligamentum arteriosum\n- **A.** aortic arch (inner concavity) RECALL\nP: pulmonary trunk bifurcation\nT: tracheal bifurcation (carina) RECALL\nR: right-to-left movement of the thoracic duct\n(posterior to the esophagus)\n- **A.** azygos vein drains into superior vena cava\n\n## Question 5 of 90\n\n- **A.** 4th intercostal space\n- **B.** 3rd intercostal space\n- **C.** 1st intercostal space\n- **D.** 2nd intercostal space\n- **E.** 5th intercostal space\n\n## Question 2 of 54\n\nP: pre-vertebral fascia and pre-tracheal fascia end\n6. A 30 years old male presents to the ED with lacerated\nwound on his posterolateral back. Which of the following\nmuscle is most likely affected?\nLatissimus dorsi is the superficial muscle of the\nposterolateral back innervated by Thoracodorsal\nNerve (RECALL)\nAsk the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if patient\nunable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve\ninjury and paralysis of trapezius muscle (RECALL)\nWinging of scapula is due to paralysis of serratus\nanterior muscle innervated by Long thoracic nerve\n(RECALL)\n\n## Question 6 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sternocleidomastoid\n- **B.** Trapezius\n- **C.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **D.** Rhomboid minor\n- **E.** Levator Scapulae\n7. A 29-year-old woman presents to the Emergency\nDepartment after injuring her left elbow. She is complaining\nof a weak grip on her left hand. You ask her to hold on to a\npiece of paper with both hands as you try to pull it away\nfrom her. The result of this clinical test is shown in image\nWhat nerve is most likely to be damaged?\n\n## Question 7 of 90\n\n- **A.** Axillary\n- **B.** Radial\n- **C.** Median\n\n## Question 3 of 54\n\nFroment sign caused by damage to the ulnar nerve\nand paralysis of adductor pollicis muscle (RECALL)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls: (High Yield)**\n> Froment's sign: Froment’s sign is a test for ulnar\n> nerve palsy specifically paralysis of the adductor\n> pollicis:\n> Patient is asked to hold a piece of paper between\n> thumb and index finger as paper is pulled away,\n> patient will flex thumb at inter-phalangeal joint to\n> maintain hold, this sign tests the function of\n> adductor pollicis muscle which is supplied by the\n> ulnar nerve.\n\nA positive test is when the patient is unable to\nadduct the thumb.\nClaw hand:\nInjury to the ulnar nerve is commonly caused by a\nfracture of the medial epicondyle and results in a\nclaw hand, in which the ring and little fingers are\nhyperextended at the metacarpophalangeal joints\nand flexed at the interphalangeal joints.\nPartial claw hand deformity is due to an isolated\nulnar nerve injury.\nComplete claw hand deformity: Involves all digits\nand results from combined ulnar and median nerve\ninjury.\n- **D.** Ulnar\n- **E.** Musculocutaneous\n9. A 23 years old pregnant woman from southern Vietnam\ncame into the ED with complaint of fever with chills for 2\ndays. You suspect malaria. What is the antimalarial drug of\nchoice for this patient?\n\n## Question 9 of 90\n\n- **A.** Quinine\n- **B.** Doxycycline\n- **C.** Primaquine\n\n## Question 4 of 54\n\nQuinine is the antimalarial drug of choice in\npregnancy; however, chloroquine should be\nconsidered in non-falciparum malaria while those\nareas where chloroquine is resistant especially\nsouthern Vietnam Quinine is best given in\npregnancy\n- **D.** Clindamycin\n- **E.** Chloroquine\n10. A 54-year-old man presents with visual impairment. On\nexamination right eye is fixed dilated and deviated\ndownwards and outwards. Which of the following nerve is\nmost likely affected?\nDepressed and abducted eye, diplopia on\ndownwards movement, ptosis, fixed and dilated\npupil with loss of accommodation, and abnormal\npupillary light reflex are all clinical effects of injury\nof oculomotor nerve.\nNote: Orbital blowout fracture and diplopia on\nupward gaze is due to entrapment of inferior\nrectus muscle (RECALL)\n\n## Question 10 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve\n- **B.** Abducent nerve\n- **C.** Optic nerve\n- **D.** Oculomotor nerve\n- **E.** Trochlear nerve\n11. A 22 years old girl presents to the ED with complaint of\namenorrhea. Excess of which of the following Harmone\ncaused this condition?\n\n## Question 11 of 90\n\n## Question 5 of 54\n\nHyperprolactinemia causes\namenorrhea/oligomenorrhea (Recall), loss of libido\nand erectile dysfunction\n- **A.** Prolactin\n- **B.** Growth Harmone\n- **C.** FSH\n- **D.** TSH\n- **E.** Oxytocin\n12. ADH acts primarily at which of the following sites in\nrenal nephron?\nADH binds to V2 receptors (Recall) and increases\nthe number of aquaporin-2 at the apical plasma\nmembrane of Distal convoluted tubule and\ncollecting duct and increases water permeability\nallowing greater water reabsorption.\nDiabetes insipidus\nCentral DI: Low ADH (Recall) less water absorbed\nNephrogenic DI: ADH resistance, Lithium is the\nmost common acquired cause of nephrogenic DI.\n(Recall)\nBiochemical values include high plasma osmolality\n>295 mOsm/kg, low urine osmolality <300\nmOsm/kg (Recall) and hypernatremia > 145.\nNote:\nSyndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion:\nincreased ADH activity (Recall) (Euvolemic hypo\nosmolar hyponatremia) Recall\nBiochemical values include low plasma osmolality <\n275 mOsm/kg, increased urine osmolality > 100\nmOsm/kg and increased urine sodium or decreased\nplasma sodium (Recall)\nDrugs causing S.I.A.D.H= S.S.R.I (Fluoxetine Recall),\nantipsychotics, anticonvulsants and T.C.A\n\n## Question 12 of 90\n\n- **A.** Distal convoluted tubule\n- **B.** Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **C.** Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **D.** Descending limb of loop of Henle\n- **E.** Proximal convoluted tubule\n\n## Question 6 of 54\n\n13. A 25 Years old male presents to the ED with complaints\nof fever, photophobia, and neck stiffness. You suspect\nmeningitis. Which of the following is the feature of\nNeisseria meningitis?\nNeisseria meningitis is gram negative encapsulated\ndiplococci causing Neisseria meningitis and\nmeningococcal sepsis\n\n## Question 13 of 90\n\n- **A.** Gram –Ve diplococci\n- **B.** Gram +Ve rod\n- **C.** Gram –Ve rod\n- **D.** Gram +Ve Cocco bacilli\n- **E.** Gram +Ve diplococci\n15. A 30-year-old diabetic patient presented to the\nemergency department with a two-day history of pain in her\nright eye worsened with eye movement. The diagnosis of\noptic neuritis was made by ophthalmologist. What will be\nthe cause of optic neuritis?\nOptic Neuritis is an acute inflammatory\ndemyelinating disorder of optic nerve. The myelin\nundergoes destruction causing axons to poorly\nconduct impulses.\nDemyelination disrupts the normal saltatory\nconduction process, leading to impaired\ntransmission of signals along the optic nerve.\ninflammatory demyelination as the pathological\nhallmark of disease; after the acute event, axonal\ndamage leading to axonal loss can lead to severe\nand sometimes irreversible neurological\nimpairment.\n\n## Question 15 of 90\n\n- **A.** Retinitis\n- **B.** Retinal detachment\n- **C.** Hyperglycemia\n- **D.** Demyelination\n- **E.** Neuromyelitis\n\n## Question 7 of 54\n\n17. Which of the following nerve is damaged in\nsupracondylar fracture?\nNote: Nerve most likely damaged in antecubital\nfossa is Median nerve.(RECALL)\n\n## Question 17 of 90\n\n- **A.** Axillary nerve\n- **B.** Ulnar nerve\n- **C.** Median nerve\n- **D.** Radial nerve\n- **E.** Common peroneal\n18. A 45 Years old male suffered penetrating trauma to the\nparotid gland with facial nerve injury. Orbicularis oculi\nmuscle is affected and frontalis muscle is spared. Which of\nthe following terminal branch of the facial nerve is most\nlikely injured?\n\n## Question 18 of 90\n\n- **A.** Zygomatic branch\n- **B.** Temporal branch\n- **C.** Buccal branch\n- **D.** Marginal mandibular branch\n- **E.** Greater petrosal nerve\n\n## Question 8 of 54\n\nNote: Skin of upper forehead, eye lid and\nconjunctiva are innervated by supraorbital nerve\n(RECALL) branch of Opthalmic division of\ntrigeminal nerve\nSupratrochlear nerve:  Innervate skin of lower\ncentral forehead and conjunctiva.\n19. A 29 years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof foot drop and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the\nfoot. Which of the following nerve is most likely damaged?\nCommon fibular nerve wraps around the neck of\nfibula and enter lateral compartment of leg where it\ngives rise terminal branches:\nSuperficial fibular nerve: Runs around lateral\ncompartment of leg and skin over lower\nanterolateral leg, and dorsum of foot (except skin\nover webspace of 1st and 2nd toe)\nDeep fibular nerve: Anterior compartment of leg\nand skin over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes\n(RECALL)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> 1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the\n> common fibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a\n> result of fracture of the head or neck of the fibula\n> because it passes behind the head of the fibula and\n> then winds laterally around the neck. The nerve\n> damage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion)\n> and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the foot and\n> lateral aspect of the leg and causes paralysis of all\n\n## Question 19 of 90\n\n- **A.** Common peroneal nerve\n- **B.** Sciatic nerve\n- **C.** Sural nerve\n- **D.** Tibial nerve\n- **E.** Posterior tibial nerve\n\n## Question 9 of 54\n\nmuscles in the anterior and lateral compartments\nof the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors muscles of the\nfoot).\n2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by\nfracture of the fibula. As the muscles that the\nsuperficial fibular nerve innervates are evertors,\ninjury to the nerve may result in a loss of eversion\nand loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral\nleg and dorsum of foot.\n3: Deep fibular nerve:\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in\nfoot drop. In foot drop foot will appear permanently\nplantarflexed, foot drop with high-stepping gait,\nweakness of foot inversion.\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace\nbetween 1st and 2nd toes.\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or\ncommon peroneal nerve or also due to deep\nfibular nerve injury.\nNote: It is very important to remember direct\ninnervation of common fibular nerve.\nDeep fibular nerve root: L4-L5\nSuperficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.\nMuscles of lateral compartment of leg: (High\nYield)\n20. A 52 years old chronic smoker presented to the ED with\ncomplaint of sudden severe headache. After initial\nassessment and imaging revealed a ruptured berry\naneurysm. Which of the following blood vessel is most\nlikely location of the aneurysm?\nBerry (saccular) aneurysms are the most common\ntype of intracranial aneurysm, representing 90% of\ncerebral aneurysms. The most frequent location is\nthe anterior communicating artery (35%), followed\nby the internal carotid artery\n\n## Question 20 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior cerebellar artery\n- **B.** Anterior communicating artery\n- **C.** Internal carotid artery\n- **D.** Anterior cerebellar artery\n- **E.** Middle cerebral artery\n\n## Question 10 of 54\n\nHigh Yield: Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral\narteries\n21. Which one of the following viscera Iie immediately\nposterior to the left atrium?\nEsophagus Iie immediately posterior to the left\natrium.\nDysphagia megalatriensis, also known as\ncardiovascular dysphagia is an impairment of\nswallowing due to esophageal compression from a\ndilated left atrium.\nOesophagus: (High Yield)\nFibromuscular tube that transports food from\npharynx to stomach, divided into three parts:\nCervical: continuous with the oropharynx,\ncommences at the lower border of cricoid cartilage\n(at level of C5/6) or cricopharyngeus muscle\nThoracic: From thoracic aperture (T1) to the\noesophageal hiatus (T11)\nAbdominal: From oesophageal hiatus and is\ncontinuous with the cardia of the stomach at the\ngastro-oesophageal junction\nOesophageal constrictions\nThe anatomical relations of the oesophagus give\nrise to four constrictions, where food/foreign\nobjects are most likely to become impacted.\n\n## Question 21 of 90\n\n- **A.** Subclavian artery\n- **B.** Trachea\n- **C.** Arch of aorta\n- **D.** Esophagus\n- **E.** Thymus gland\n\n## Question 11 of 54\n\nOesophageal constrictions should not be confused\nwith pathological constrictions.\nCervical constriction: due to cricoid cartilage at the\nlevel of C5/6\nThoracic constriction: due to aortic arch at the level\nof T4/5\nAbdominal constriction: at oesophageal hiatus at\nT10/11 vertebral\nRelations of the oesophagus\n22. You are teaching group of medical students regarding\nanatomy of soles of foot. You ask medical student's plantar\narteries and nerves lie between the which layer of sole of\nfoot.\nTendon of flexor digitorum brevis lie within the ist\nlayer\nPlantar arteries and nerves lie within ist and 2nd\nlayer\nTendons of flexor hallucis longus and flexor\ndigitorum longus lie within 2nd layer\nFlexor hallucis brevis muscle lies within 3rd layer\nTendons of tibialis posterior and fibularis longus\nmuscle lie within 4th layer\n\n## Question 22 of 90\n\n- **A.** 4th and 5th layer\n- **B.** 2nd and 3th layer\n- **C.** 1st and 2nd layer\n- **D.** 2nd and 4th layer\n- **E.** 3rd and 4th layer\n24. 30 years old man is brought into the Ed by paramedics\nwith a gunshot wound on his abdomen. On laparotomy\nretroperitoneal hemorrhage found. Which of the following\nstructures is the most likely source of bleeding?\n\n## Question 24 of 90\n\n- **A.** Liver\n- **B.** Suprarenal glands\n- **C.** First part of duodenum\n- **D.** Spleen\n\n## Question 12 of 54\n\nSADPUCKER useful mnemonic to remember\nretroperitoneal organs\nS: Suprarenal (adrenal) gland\n- **A.** aorta/IVC\n- **D.** duodenum (Second and third part)\nP: pancreas (except tail)\nU: ureters\n- **C.** colon (ascending and descending)\nK: kidneys\n- **E.** (o)esophagus\nR: rectum\n- **E.** Sigmoid colon\n26. A 35 Years old male with a history of RTA was brought\ninto the ED with injury on occipital region. On examination,\npatient has bitemporal hemianopia. Which structure Is most\nlikely damaged?\nContralateral Homonyms hemianopia = optic tract\n(Recall)\nBitemporal hemianopia = optic chiasma\nParietal upper optic radiation: Contralateral\nhomonymous inferior quadrantanopia\nTemporal lower optic radiation: Contralateral\nhomonymous superior quadrantanopia (Recall)\nOccipital visual cortex: Contralateral homonymous\nhemianopia with macular sparing e.g.: Lesion of\nright temporal lobe would result in left\nhomonymous superior quadrantanopia causing\nleft temporal and right nasal vision loss (Recall)\nRemember the lesions of visual pathway (High\nyield)\n\n## Question 26 of 90\n\n- **A.** Optic radiation\n- **B.** Optic tract\n- **C.** Optic chiasm\n- **D.** Lateral geniculate body\n- **E.** Visual area\n\n## Question 13 of 54\n\n27. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with complain\nof ptosis with left-sided droopy eye and dry left face. You\nsuspect Horner's syndrome. Which of the following nerve is\nmost likely affected?\nSympathetic fibers if unilaterally damaged may\nproduce ipsilateral Horner's syndrome. Horner's\nsyndrome characterized by triad of:\nPtosis\nMiosis\nAnhidrosis\n\n## Question 27 of 90\n\n- **A.** Cervical Sympathetic chain\n- **B.** Infratrochlear nerve\n- **C.** Long Ciliary nerve\n- **D.** Vagus nerve\n- **E.** Facial nerve\n28. A 3O years old male with history of trauma came into\nthe ED with abdominal pain. Which of the following vessels\nare compromised in rectus sheath hematoma?\nRectus sheath encloses rectus abdominis,\npyramidalis muscle (RECALL), inferior and\nsuperior epigastric vessels, and termination of\nintercostal nerves T7-T11.\nHigh Yield:\nRectus sheath: The rectus sheath is formed by the\naponeuroses of the three flat muscles and encloses\nthe rectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles. It\nhas an anterior and posterior wall for most of its\nlength.\nAnterior wall: Formed by the aponeuroses of the\nexternal oblique, and of half of the internal oblique.\nPosterior wall:  Half of the aponeuroses of the\ninternal oblique and of the transversus abdominis.\nApproximately midway between the umbilicus and\nthe pubic symphysis, all the aponeuroses move to\n\n## Question 28 of 90\n\n- **A.** Inferior mesenteric\n- **B.** Superior mesenteric\n- **C.** Gastroepiploic vessels\n- **D.** Inferior epigastric vessel\n- **E.** Celiac artery\n\n## Question 14 of 54\n\nthe anterior wall of the rectus sheath. At this point,\nthere is no posterior wall to the sheath; the rectus\nabdominis is in direct contact with the transversalis\nfascia. Point, where the posterior layer of the rectus\nsheath ends, is the arcuate line.\nContents of the rectus sheath\nRectus abdominus and the pyramidalis muscle\nThe superior and inferior epigastric vessels\nThe termination of intercostal nerves T7-T11\nThe 12th thoracic nerve and the accompanying\nvessels\n29. Whilst in your cardiology rotation you are asked to\nperform cardiovascular examination. One of your senior\nregistrars asks, what is the best site to auscultate mitral\nvalve?\nAreas of auscultation of heart:\nNote: Aortic valve: Second right intercostal space\n(Recall)\nHeart sounds, Mechanical event and\ncorrespondence with ECG (High Yield)\n\n## Question 29 of 90\n\n- **A.** Right 5th ICS in midclavicular line\n- **B.** Apex of heart\n- **C.** 5th intercostal space to the lower left sternal\nborder\n- **D.** Second right intercostal space\n- **E.** Second left intercostal space\n30. You are teaching group of medical students' lymphatic\nsystem and lymph nodes of the body. You ask medical\n\n## Question 30 of 90\n\n## Question 15 of 54\n\nstudents lymphatics of glans penis drain primarily to which\nof the following lymph nodes?\nLymphatics from glans penis and penile urethra\ndrain primarily into the deep inguinal nodes\nLymphatics from penile skin and prepuce drain\ninto superficial inguinal nodes (RECALL)\n- **A.** Deep inguinal node\n- **B.** External iliac nodes\n- **C.** Superficial inguinal nodes\n- **D.** Internal iliac nodes\n- **E.** Paraaortic lymph nodes\n31. A 32-year-old male with a history of RTA presented to\nED with Complain of pain in his Right shoulder. X-ray was\ndone which shows anterior shoulder dislocation. Which of\nthe following nerve is most likely damaged?\nIntrinsic muscles of the shoulder: Connecting the\nscapula to the humerus\nThere are six muscles in this group the deltoid, teres\nmajor, and the four rotator cuff muscles\n(supraspinatus, infraspinatus, subscapularis, and\nteres minor).\nAnatomical course: (High Yield)\nAxillary nerve arises in the axilla passes medially to\nthe surgical neck of the humerus, where it divides\ninto three terminal branches:\n\n## Question 31 of 90\n\n- **A.** Lower subscapular nerve\n- **B.** Axillary nerve\n- **C.** Median nerve\n- **D.** Suprascapular nerve\n- **E.** Ulnar nerve\n\n## Question 16 of 54\n\nAnterior terminal branch:  winds around the\nsurgical neck of the humerus and provides motor\ninnervation to the anterior aspect of the deltoid\nmuscle.\nPosterior terminal branch:  Gives motor innervation\nto the posterior aspect of the deltoid muscle and\nteres minor. It also innervates the skin over the\ninferior part of the deltoid as the upper lateral\ncutaneous nerve of the arm.\nArticular branch: supplies the glenohumeral joint\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Axillary nerve injury is commonly caused by\n> fracture of surgical neck of humerus or inferior\n> dislocation of humerus.\n\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of the\narm. (Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but not to\na horizontal level).\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of the\ndeltoid (the ‘regimental badge area’).\nQuadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior\ncircumflex humeral artery and vein pass through it\nand can be compressed as a result of trauma,\nmuscle hypertrophy resulting in weakness of deltoid\nand teres minor.\nNote: Axillary nerve injury also results in\nweakness of deltoid (RECALL) and teres minor\nand resulting in weakness of abduction and\nlateral rotation.\n32. A 30 years old man is brought into the Ed by\nparamedics with a lacerated wound on his abdomen. which\nof the following Structures is most likely affected by a tear\nof the superior mesenteric artery?\nThe superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut\nstructures from the second part of duodenum\nincluding the caecum, appendix, ascending colon,\nand proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.\nInferior mesenteric artery supplies the descending\ncolon and sigmoid colon.\n\n## Question 32 of 90\n\n- **A.** Only ascending colon\n- **B.** Ascending and transverse colon\n- **C.** Transverse colon only\n- **D.** Transverse and descending colon\n- **E.** Sigmoid colon\n33. The probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when it is\ntrue;\n\n## Question 33 of 90\n\n## Question 17 of 54\n\nIn statistical hypothesis testing, a type1 error is the\nmistaken rejection of an actually true null\nhypothesis (also known as false positive” finding or\nconclusion; example: innocent person is convicted”).\nWhile type 2 error is the failure to reject the null\nhypothesis that is actually false (also known as false\nnegative. Finding or conclusion example: guilty\nperson is not convicted) much of statistical theory\nrevolves around the minimization of one or both of\nthese errors, though the complete elimination of\neither is a statistical impossibility if the outcome is\nnot determined by a known, observable casual\nprocess. By selecting low threshold (cut-off) value\nand modifying the alpha level, the quality of\nhypothesis test can be increased. The knowledge of\ntype 1 error and type 2 error is widely used in\nmedical science, biometrics and computer science.\n- **A.** Type 1 error\n- **B.** Level of significance\n- **C.** Type 2 error\n- **D.** Power of statistical test\n- **E.** Type 3 error\n34.\nYou are reviewing a patient with infected arteriovenous\nfistula and he will undergo hemofiltration tomorrow. Which\nof the following best describes the Transport of molecules\nacross the membrane that occurs in haemofiltration?\nUltrafiltration refers to the process of movement of\nfluids through a membrane caused by a pressure\ngradient\nIn dialysis, fluid is removed by ultrafiltration using\nthe dialysis membrane. The pressure on the\ndialysate side is lower so water moves from the\nblood (place of higher pressure) to the dialysate\n(place of lower pressure). This is how the\nhemodialysis treatment removes fluid.\n\n## Question 34 of 90\n\n- **A.** Active transport\n- **B.** Facilitated diffusion\n- **C.** Simple diffusion\n- **D.** Ultrafiltration\n- **E.** Osmosis\n\n## Question 18 of 54\n\n35. A 45-year-old male sustained lacerated wound on his\nright knee 2 days back and came into ED with a Complaint\nof Severe pain in his right knee associated with fever and\nswelling of joint. You suspect septic arthritis. what is the\nsingle most causative agent in this case?\nSeptic arthritis is an infection of any kind that finds\nits way into joint space, it is a most rapid and\ndestructive joint disease, acute onset of\nmonoarticular arthritis with pain swelling, and\nwarmth, the knee is the most common site others\ninclude the hip, wrist, shoulder, and ankle.\nthe most common type of bacteria that causes\nseptic arthritis is called staphylococcus aureus.\nothers include streptococcus, gram-negative rods,\nNeisseria Gonorrhea causes septic arthritis in\nsexually active young adults (Recall)\n\n## Question 35 of 90\n\n- **A.** S.Epidermidis\n- **B.** Streptococcus\n- **C.** Staph Aureus\n- **D.** Staph pyogenes\n- **E.** E- Coli\n37. You are teaching group of medical students' full\nneurological examination while assessing dermatomal\nsupply of trunk you ask medical students, what is the\ndermatome of nipple?\nDermatomal supply of trunk:\n\n## Question 37 of 90\n\n- **A.** T2\n- **B.** T3\n- **C.** T4\n- **D.** T8\n- **E.** T6\n\n## Question 19 of 54\n\nHigh yield:\n38. Whilst in your emergency department your consultant\nasks you to perform chest examination. Righr middle lobe\nis best auscultated between which of the following ribs?\nThe superior lobe of the right lung is best\nauscultated above the 4th rib anteriorly.\nThe middle lobe of the right lung is best auscultated\nbetween the 4th and the 6th rib anteriorly.\nThe superior lobe of the left lung is best auscultated\nabove the 6th rib anteriorly.\nThe inferior lobes of the left and right lungs are best\nauscultated in the back between T3 & T10.\n\n## Question 38 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rib 1-2\n- **B.** Rib 4-6\n- **C.** Rib 2-3\n- **D.** Rib 2-4\n- **E.** Rib 3-4\n39. Which one of the following hormones is released from\nposterior pituitary?\n\n## Question 39 of 90\n\n- **A.** FSH\n\n## Question 20 of 54\n\nTwo hormones released from the posterior pituitary\ngland are ADH and Oxytocin\nNote: ADH release from posterior pituitary\nsynthesized in hypothalamus. (Recall)\nADH is released in response to raised plasma\nosmolality seen in dehydration (Recall)\nPituitary\nHarmone\nInhibited By\nClinical\ndeficiency\nExcess\nGrowth\nHarmone\nSomatostatin\n(Recall)\ndwarfism in\nchildren\nAcromegaly in adults or\ngigantism in children\nProlactin\nDopamine\n(Recall)\nFailure of\npostpartum\nlactation\nHyperprolactinemia causes\namenorrhea/oligomenorrhea\n(Recall), loss of libido and\nerectile dysfunction\nACTH Cortisol\n(Recall)\nAdrenal\ninsufficiency\nCushing's disease\nFSH/LH\nProlactin and\noestrogen\n/testosterone\nInfertility Gonadal insufficiency\n- **B.** ADH\n- **C.** ACTH\n- **D.** TSH\n- **E.** Prolactin\n40. You are going to perform full cardiovascular\nexamination in a patient with known cardiac disease. Your\nconsultant asks how does the 4  heart is produced?\n\n## Question 40 of 90\n\nth\n- **A.** Caused by filling of an abnormally stiff ventricle\nin atrial diastole\n- **B.** Caused by closure of the semilunar (aortic and\npulmonary) valves\n- **C.** Caused by filling of an abnormally stiff ventricle\nin atrial systole\n- **D.** Caused by closure of the atrioventricular (mitral\nand tricuspid) valves\n- **E.** Caused by rapid flow of blood from the atria into\nthe ventricles during the ventricular filling\n\n## Question 21 of 54\n\nNote: Dicrotic notch on the arterial waveform\nrepresent closure of aortic valve (Recall) used to\ndemarcate the end of systole and the beginning\nof diastole.\nHigh Yield\nThe isovolumic relaxation phase starts when the\naortic valve closes (Recall) and ends when the\nmitral valve opens. During this phase, the left\nventricular pressure drops until it becomes lower\nthan that of the left atrium. This allows opening of\nthe atrioventricular valve and the filling of the\nventricle.\n41. A 20 Years old girl presents to the ED with complaint of\nheadache, Palpitations and diaphoresis. After initial\nassessment you suspect pheochromocytoma. This clinical\ncondition occurs due to excess of which of the following\nhormones?\n\n## Question 41 of 90\n\n- **A.** ACTH\n- **B.** PTH\n- **C.** Catecholamines\n\n## Question 22 of 54\n\nPheochromocytoma is catecholamine secreting\ntumor arises from chromaffin cells of adrenal\nmedulla\nCatecholamines include adrenaline, nor adrenaline\nand dopamine.\nAdrenaline is predominant Harmone in\npheochromocytoma\n- **D.** Oxytocin\n- **E.** ADH\n44. which of the following muscle unlock the knee from the\nextended position?\nAt full extension, the tibia and femur “lock” into\nposition, providing stability in the leg and improving\nload-bearing capacity. The popliteus muscle at the\nback of the leg unlocks the knee by rotating the\nfemur on the tibia, allowing flexion of the joint.\n\n## Question 44 of 90\n\n- **A.** Popliteus\n- **B.** Soleus\n- **C.** Sartorius\n- **D.** Gastrocnemius\n- **E.** Plantaris\n45. A 45-year-old male sustained a lacerated wound on his\nright knee 2 days back and came into ED with a Complaint\nof Severe pain in his right knee associated with fever and\nswelling of the joint. Gram staining revealed gram +ve cocci\nin clusters what is the single most causative agent in this\ncase?\nStaph aureus is gram +Ve diplococci in clusters\ncausing septic arthritis\n\n## Question 45 of 90\n\n- **A.** Staph pyogenes\n- **B.** Streptococcus\n- **C.** Staph Aureus\n- **D.** E- Coli\n- **E.** S.Epidermidis\n\n## Question 23 of 54\n\n46. 70 years old male presents to the ED with complain of\nexpressive Broca's dysphasia. The brocas speech area is\nlocated in which of the following regions?\nBroca’s speech area is located in frontal lobe\nBroca’s aphasia or expressive aphasia is when\npeople find it very difficult to find and say the right\nwords. People with Broca's aphasia may only be\nable to say single words or very short sentences,\nalthough it’s usually possible for other people to\nunderstand what they mean\nWernicke's aphasia (fluent aphasia) in the\ntemporal lobe.\nWernicke’s aphasia or receptive aphasia is when\nsomeone is able to speak well and use long\nsentences, but what they say does not make any\nsense.\n\n## Question 46 of 90\n\n- **A.** Midbrain\n- **B.** Parietal lobe\n- **C.** Temporal lobe\n- **D.** Frontal lobe\n- **E.** Occipital lobe\n47. A 45 years old male presents to the ED with history of\nsevere vomiting for the last 4 days.. The patient is irritable,\nweak, and reporting muscle cramping and weakness. On\nassessment, the patient is experiencing bradypnea with a\nrespiratory rate of 10. The patient has the following ABGs\nresult: HCO3 36, pH 7.52, PaCO2 48. Which condition\nbelow is presenting?\nIf the primary acid-base disorder is from a\nrespiratory cause, the kidneys will compensate\n(renal compensation).\nIf the primary disorder is from a metabolic cause,\nthe lungs will compensate (respiratory\n\n## Question 47 of 90\n\n- **A.** Respiratory alkalosis fully compensated\n- **B.** Metabolic alkalosis partially compensated\n- **C.** Metabolic acidosis not compensated\n- **D.** Metabolic alkalosis fully compensated\n- **E.** Metabolic acidosis partially compensated\n\n## Question 24 of 54\n\ncompensation).\nMetabolic acidosis= Respiratory compensation\n(decrease in PaCO2)\nMetabolic alkalosis= Respiratory compensation\n(increase in PaCO2)\nRespiratory acidosis= Renal compensation (increase\nin HCO3-)\nRespiratory alkalosis= Renal compensation\n(decrease in HCO3-)\n48. Regarding propranolol use in thyroid crises, which one\nof the following is true?\nPropranolol is used as short-term symptomatic\ntherapy till the effect of Carbimazole develops.\n\n## Question 48 of 90\n\n- **A.** As short-term symptomatic therapy till effect of\nCarbimazole develops\n- **B.** To potentiate the effect of radioactive iodine\n- **C.** As long-term therapy after subtotal\nthyroidectomy\n- **D.** In patients not responding to Carbimazole\n- **E.** In patients Carbimazole contraindicated\n50. You are teaching group of medical students about origin\nand insertion of muscles. Which of the following muscle\noriginate from the anterior superior iliac spine?\nOrigin of most important lower limb\nmuscles\n\n## Question 50 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sartorius\n- **B.** Psoas major\n- **C.** Pectineus\n- **D.** Rectus femoris\n- **E.** Iliacus\n\n## Question 25 of 54\n\nNote: Greater trochanter of femur is site of\ninsertion of gluteus Medius, gluteus minims and\npiriformis (RECALL)\n51. 27 years old male presents to the ED with complain of\ndysphagia. Which of the following nerve is responsible for\ncontracting the esophageal phase of swallowing?\nPharyngeal phase of swallowing: glossopharyngeal\nnerve and vagus nerve\nEsophageal phase of swallowing: Vagus nerve\n\n## Question 51 of 90\n\n- **A.** Vagus nerve\n- **B.** Hypoglossal nerve\n- **C.** Facial nerve\n- **D.** Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **E.** Auriculotemporal nerve\n53. A 25 years old male patient with a history of trauma\npresents into the ED with complaint of pain in epigastrium\nradiating to the back. Which of the following is the\nsegmental innervation of the pancreas?\nInnervations:\n\n## Question 53 of 90\n\n- **A.** T6 –T10\n- **B.** L2-L4\n- **C.** T4-T8\n- **D.** T4 –T10\n- **E.** L1-L2\n\n## Question 26 of 54\n\nThe segmental level of innervation of pancreas= T6-\nT10\nGallbladder=phrenic(C3-C5)\nAscending and transverse colon= fibers travel\nthrough lesser splanchnic nerve to =T10-T11 spinal\ncord segment\nDescending and sigmoid colon=pain fibers travel\nthrough lumber splanchnic nerve to= L1 and L2\nspinal segments\nUreter=Sympathetic supply from inferior\nhypogastric plexus\nparasympathetic supply from pelvic splanchnic\nnerve(S2-S4)\nVisceral afferent fibers follow sympathetic fibers to\nenter in spinal cord via =T10-L1 nerve fibers\nBladder= Efferent parasympathetic fibers from\npelvic splanchnic nerve=S2 to S4\novary=sympathetic fibers of ovarian and lumber\nsplanchnic nerve to cell bodies in the = T10-T12 and\nL1 spinal sensory ganglion\nTestes= visceral afferent fibers follow the\nsympathetic fibers to=T10-L1\nRectum= visceral afferent fibers follow\nparasympathetic supply to =S2-S4 sensory ganglia.\n54. You are required to perform resuscitative thoracotomy\nin a patient with massive hemothorax. Which one of the\nfollowing muscles are divided during anterolateral\nthoracotomy?\nAntero lateral thoracotomy muscle divided serratus\nanterior and pectoralis major\nAntero lateral thoracotomy: Nerve injury long\nthoracic nerve (Recall)\nPost: lateral thoracotomy muscle divided latissimus\ndorsi\nPost: lateral thoracotomy nerve injury:\nThoracodorsal nerve injury\nInternal mammary artery (RECALL) and Aorta are\nlikely to be damaged also\n\n## Question 54 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anterior scalene\n- **B.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **C.** Serratus anterior\n- **D.** Trapezius\n- **E.** Pectoralis minor\n55. Which of the following best describes the course of the\ngreat saphenous vein:\n\n## Question 55 of 90\n\n- **A.** Passes behind posterior malleolus\n\n## Question 27 of 54\n\nThe great saphenous vein passes anterior to the\nmedial malleolus, travels up the medial side of the\nleg, and thigh to pass through the saphenous\nopening in the deep fascia of thigh, and joins with\nfemoral vein.\nSmall saphenous vein passes posterior to lateral\nmalleolus\n- **B.** Anterior to medial malleolus\n- **C.** Posterior to medial malleolus\n- **D.** Anterior to lateral malleolus\n- **E.** Posterior to lateral malleolus\n56. A 25 Years old male presents to the ED with complaints\nof fever, photophobia, neck stiffness, and non-blanching\nskin rash. What is the most likely Causative agent?\nNeisseria meningitidis is the most common cause\nof bacterial meningitis. Meningococci/Neisseria\nmeningitidis are gram-negative diplococci that\ncause meningitis and meningococcemia.\nSymptoms, usually severe, include headache,\nnausea, vomiting, photophobia, lethargy, rash,\nmultiple organ failure, shock, and disseminated\nintravascular coagulation. Diagnosis is clinical,\nconfirmed by culture\n\n## Question 56 of 90\n\n- **A.** Streptococcus aureus\n- **B.** Streptococcus pyogenes\n- **C.** Hemophilus influenza\n- **D.** Neisseria meningitis\n- **E.** E-Coli\n57. which of the following best describes the function of\nmitochondria is?\n\n## Question 57 of 90\n\n- **A.** Carbohydrate synthesis\n- **B.** Energy production\n- **C.** Protein packaging\n- **D.** Lipid synthesis\n- **E.** Protein synthesis\n\n## Question 28 of 54\n\nIt acts as the site of aerobic respiration and\nproduces cellular energy in the form of ATP\n(powerHouse of the cell).\n58. A 43 years old undergone surgery of posterior part of\nthe neck after which he lost sensation around the clavicle.\nWhich of the following dermatome is best localized on the\nsupraclavicular region?\nHigh Yield:\nDermatomes of Upper Limb:\nDermatome: A dermatome is defined as ‘a strip of\nskin that is innervated by a single spinal nerve and\nclinically very important and help in diagnosis of\nspinal cord injury\nMyotomes and associated\nmovements:\nMyotome: Group of muscles innervated by a single\nspinal nerve\nReflexes of upper Limb:\n\n## Question 58 of 90\n\n- **A.** C3\n- **B.** T2\n- **C.** C2\n- **D.** C5\n- **E.** C4\n59. A 30-year-old woman presents to the ED with\nweakness and fatigue. On examination, she has yellow\ntinge skin, jaundice, and mild splenomegaly. CBC shows\n\n## Question 59 of 90\n\n## Question 29 of 54\n\nher Hb 7g/dl. The patient only takes one medication. Which\nof the following medications is most likely to cause\nhemolytic anemia?\nAmong NSAIDs, Mefenamic acid causes hemolytic\nanemia.\nCo-amoxiclav causes Cholestatic jaundice\n(RECALL)\nSummary Of Drugs And Their\nSide Effects (High Yield)\n- **A.** Propranolol\n- **B.** Mefenamic acid\n- **C.** Lithium\n- **D.** TCA\n- **E.** Ibuprofen\n61. A 60 Years old male known case of Diabetes mellitus\npresents into the ED with complaint of eye pain that’s\nworsened by eye movement and loss of color vision. You\nsuspect optic neuritis which one of the following conditions\nis strongly associated with it?\n\n## Question 61 of 90\n\n- **A.** Diabetes Mellitus\n- **B.** Multiple sclerosis\n- **C.** Hyperglycemia\n- **D.** Sarcoidosis\n\n## Question 30 of 54\n\nOptic neuritis is an inflammatory demyelination of\nthe optic nerve, and is strongly associated with\nmultiple sclerosis.\n- **E.** Crohn's disease\n62. A 30 years old male was brought into the ED following a\nfall from a height. His examination reveals bilateral\nweakness in both arm power of 3/5 and his lower limb\npower is 4/5, he also has variable sensory impairment\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n-Spinal cord injuries spinal\ntracts and their normal\nfunction\nSpinal cord syndrome\nAnterior cord syndrome\n(Recall)\n\n## Question 62 of 90\n\n- **A.** Central Cord Syndrome\n- **B.** Complete Cord Transection\n- **C.** Posterior Cord Syndrome\n- **D.** Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- **E.** Brown Sequard Syndrome\n\n## Question 31 of 54\n\n63. A 38-year-old woman known case of hyperthyroidism\npresented with palpitations, sweating, and agitation. On\nexamination, she is hyperpyrexic, hypertensive, and\ntachycardic. She has not been able to take her medicine.\nwhich of the following medication would you prescribe\nimmediately to treat her symptoms?\nPropranolol is the preferred agent in\nhyperthyroidism and thyroid storm due to its\nadditional effect of blocking the peripheral\nconversion of inactive T4 to active form T3. They are\nhighly effective for symptomatic relief (tachycardia,\ntremors)\nIn hyperthyroidism (grave’s disease) =increased T3,\nT4 levels, decrease TSH levels (RECALLL)\nIn hypothyroidism (Hashimoto's thyroiditis)\n=Decrease T3, T4, increase TSH (RECALL)\n\n## Question 63 of 90\n\n- **A.** Atropine\n- **B.** Carbimazole\n- **C.** Hydrocortisone\n- **D.** Propranolol\n- **E.** Iodine solution\n64. A 24-year-old male presented to ER with a fracture of\nboth the tibia and fibula while playing. The patient\ndemonstrates foot drop. Most common nerve involved in\nfoot drop?\nMost commonly, foot drop is caused by an injury to\nthe peroneal nerve (fibular nerve)\ncommon peroneal/fibular nerve (L4 to S2) supply\nskin over the lower lateral leg (RECALL) and dorsal\naspect of foot and toes (except lateral side of little\ntoe supplied by sural branch of tibial nerve)\nWrist drop= Radial Nerve (RECALL)\nClaw hand= ulnar nerve (RECALL)\nApe hand= median nerve\n\n## Question 64 of 90\n\n- **A.** Tibial nerve\n- **B.** Common peroneal nerve\n- **C.** Sciatic nerve\n- **D.** Obturator nerve\n- **E.** Femoral nerve\n\n## Question 32 of 54\n\n65. You are going to attempt lumbar puncture in a patient\nwith suspected meningitis. What is the recommended\nvertebral level to perform LP?\nA lumbar puncture (LP) is a procedure in which a\nneedle is inserted into the spinal canal. It is most\ncommonly used to collect a sample of\ncerebrospinal fluid (CSF) for diagnostic testing (e.g.,\nfor suspected meningitis and subarachnoid\nhemorrhage)\nLumbar puncture is preferentially performed at\nL3/L4 or L4/L5 (cauda equina level), to avoid the\nspinal cord which ends at L1/L2.\nThe layers penetrated by the spinal needle in an LP\nare as follows:\nSkin\nsubcutaneous tissue\nsupraspinous ligament (RECALL)\nInterspinous ligament\nligamentum flavum\nEpidural space or extradural\ndura mater\narachnoid mater\nsubarachnoid space (contains CSF)\n\n## Question 65 of 90\n\n- **A.** L4/L5\n- **B.** T11/T12\n- **C.** L2/L3\n- **D.** L1/L2\n- **E.** T12/L1\n66. A 70-year-old male is scheduled to undergo repair of an\ninguinal hernia that is easily reduced by pushing the\nabdominal contents back through the external ring. Direct\ninguinal hernia is due to weakness in which of the following\nlayer?\n\n## Question 66 of 90\n\n- **A.** lacunar ligament\n- **B.** External oblique aponeurosis\n- **C.** Transversalis fascia\n- **D.** Transversus abdominis\n- **E.** Deep inguinal ring\n\n## Question 33 of 54\n\nDirect inguinal hernia is common in elderly bulge\nthrough weakened fascia of abdominal wall\n(Transversalis fascia)) directly behind superficial\ninguinal ring and medial to the inferior epigastric\nvessels (Recall)\nIndirect inguinal hernia enters inguinal canal at the\ndeep inguinal ring and lateral to inferior epigastric\nvessels\n67. You are discussing ECG waveform with group of\nmedical students. You ask ur students, What does the\nsecond heart sound correspond to on the ECG waveform:\nHeart sounds, Mechanical event and\ncorrespondence with ECG (High\nYIELD)\nRemember the table on your fingertips many recalls\nfrom this table\n\n## Question 67 of 90\n\n- **A.** P wave\n- **B.** T wave\n- **C.** QRS complex\n- **D.** ST segment\n- **E.** PR interval\n68. A 20 years old young boy after assault in the college\npresents to the ED with superficial lacerated wound on his\nanterior triangle of neck. Which of the following muscle is\nmost likely injured?\n\n## Question 68 of 90\n\n- **A.** Platysma\n- **B.** Sternocleidomastoid\n- **C.** Scalene muscle\n\n## Question 34 of 54\n\nNeck fascia is divided into superficial and deep\nfascia\nSuperficial fascia includes: Superficial veins (External\njugular), superficial lymph nodes, fat and platysma\nmuscle\nDeep fascia includes: Trapezius,\nsternocleidomastoid, Visceras of neck and\nvasculature includes common carotid artery,\ninternal jugular vein and vagus nerve in the carotid\nsheath.\n- **D.** Trapezius muscle\n- **E.** Suprahyoid\n70. You are asked to perform ascitic tap on a CLD patient\nand teach a group of medical students. You ask medical\nstudents what is the site to perform ascitic tap.\nAscites or hemoperitoneum accumulates in\nMorrisons pouch or Hepatorenal recess or Right\nperihepatic space which is lowest space in\nperitoneal cavity in Supine position\nSubphrenic or right subphrenic (pus from\nappendicitis or ruptured duodenal ulcer accumulate\nhere\n\n## Question 70 of 90\n\n- **A.** Right perihepatic space\n- **B.** Left subphrenic space\n- **C.** Right subphrenic space\n- **D.** Left perihepatic space\n- **E.** Left lower quadrant\n71. A 65 Years old woman presents with a painful shoulder\nafter falling on the ground. On examination and rotator cuff\nassessment she is unable to perform the Lift Off test.\nWhich rotator cuff muscle has been most likely injured?\nAssessment of rotator cuff muscles\n\n## Question 71 of 90\n\n- **A.** Subscapularis\n- **B.** Supraspinatus\n- **C.** Infraspinatus\n- **D.** Teres major\n- **E.** Teres minor\n\n## Question 35 of 54\n\nNote: Supraspinatus is the most commonly\ninjured rotator cuff muscle and helps to initiate\nabduction from 0 degree to 15 degrees (RECALL)\nand then assist the deltoid with continued\nabduction.\n73. A 24 years old female who is 3 months pregnant came\ninto the ED with complaint of lower abdominal pain. You\nsuspect ectopic pregnancy. Ectopic pregnancy occurs most\ncommonly in which part of uterine tube?\nEctopic pregnancies most commonly occur in\nampulla.\n\n## Question 73 of 90\n\n- **A.** Infundibulum\n- **B.** Fimbriae\n- **C.** Ampulla\n- **D.** Isthmus\n- **E.** Cervix\n74. Vibrio cholera causes watery diarrhea due to which of\nfollowing final pathways?\nCholera toxin activates adenylate cyclase and\nincreases cyclic adenosine monophosphate (CAMP).\nCAMP activates the Cl-secretory channels and\nproduces a primary secretion of Cl- with Na+ and\nH20.\n\n## Question 74 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decrease Camp\n- **B.** Increase proton secretory channels\n- **C.** Increase cl- ion secretion\n- **D.** Increased hco3 secretory channels\n- **E.** Decreased cl- ion secretion\n\n## Question 36 of 54\n\n76. A 62-year-old patient with pericardial effusion comes to\na local hospital for aspiration of pericardial fluid by\npericardiocentesis. The needle is inserted into the\npericardial cavity through which of the following intercostal\nspaces adjacent to the sternum?\nPericardiocentesis is a procedure performed to\nremove pericardial fluid from the pericardial sac\nthat surrounds the myocardium\nThe needle insertion site is 1-2 cm lateral to the apex\nbeat within the fifth intercostal space. Advance the\nneedle over the superior border of the rib to avoid\nintercostal nerves and vessels.\nHigh yield:\nSites for Abdominal Paracentesis:\nMidline Approach =2 cm below Umbilicus in midline\nLateral Approach = 3 to 5 cm Superior and Medial to\nAnterior Superior Iliac Spine.\nNeedle Thoracocentesis in Pneumothorax =2nd\nIntercostal Space Midclavicular line. (RECALL)\nChest Tube Intubation, Pleural tap = Midaxillary Line\n5th Intercostal Space.\n\n## Question 76 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left fifth intercostal space\n- **B.** Left fourth intercostal space\n- **C.** Right sixth intercostal space\n- **D.** Right fourth intercostal space\n- **E.** Right fifth intercostal space\n77. At which of the following site maximal absorption of\nvitamin B12 occur?\nMaximal water reabsorption occurs in Jejunum\n(Recall)\nMaximal Iron absorption occurs in duodenum\n(Recall)\n\n## Question 77 of 90\n\n- **A.** Duodenum\n- **B.** Jejunum\n- **C.** Proximal ileum\n- **D.** Terminal ileum\n- **E.** Stomach\n\n## Question 37 of 54\n\nMaximal calcium absorption occurs in duodenum\n(Recall)\nMaximal vit B12 absorption occurs in terminal ileum\nNote: Sub acute cord syndrome: Due to vitamin B12\ndeficiency also known as subacute combined\ndegeneration of spinal cord (Recall) there is\nmainly degeneration pf posterior and lateral cord\nDrugs causing Vit B12 Deficiency: Ethanol,\nchloramphenicol, H2 receptor blockers, PPI\n(Omeprazole) Recall\n78. Which one of the following vessels is compressed in\nthoracic outlet syndrome?\nThoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) refers to the clinical\nfeatures that arise from compression of the\nneurovascular bundle within the thoracic outlet.\nCompression of brachial plexus\nCompression of subclavian artery\nCompression of subclavian vein\nStructures that pass through the thoracic inlet\ninclude: (High Yield)\nTrachea and oesophagus\nThoracic duct and apices of the lung\nphrenic nerve\nvagus nerve\nRecurrent laryngeal nerves\nSympathetic trunks\nLeft and right common carotid arteries.\nleft subclavian arteries.\nInternal jugular veins\nBrachiocephalic veins\nSubclavian veins\nlymph nodes and lymphatic vessels\n\n## Question 78 of 90\n\n- **A.** Vertebral artery\n- **B.** Subclavian artery and vein\n- **C.** Brachiocephalic trunk\n- **D.** Internal carotid artery\n- **E.** Common carotid artery\n79. A 25 years old male presents to the ED with a\ncomplaint of progressive difficulty in walking. When you\nobserve him, he walks with waddling gait and pelvis falling\ntowards the left side on each step. Which of the following\nnerve is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 79 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sciatic nerve\n\n## Question 38 of 54\n\nTrendelenburg gait or waddling gait occurs when\nyour hip abductor muscles are weakened. This\nmuscle group includes the gluteus medius and\nminimus innervated by superior gluteal nerve. The\nweakness of these muscles causes drooping of\nthe pelvis to the contralateral side while walking\n(RECALL)\n- **B.** Inferior gluteal nerve\n- **C.** Ilioinguinal nerve\n- **D.** Superior gluteal nerve\n- **E.** Obturator nerve\n80. A 45-year-old male was found unconscious at home\nand was brought into the ED by paramedics. On initial\nassessment at triage, his BM was 1.5mmol/l. According to\nhypoglycemia management guidelines what initial\ntreatment should be given in unconscious patient:\nIM glucagon should be given initially in an\nunconscious patient while those patients who are\nalert and able to swallow oral dextrose should be\ngiven\n\n## Question 80 of 90\n\n- **A.** 70 to 80 ml of 50% glucose\n- **B.** Wait for iv-line access and give iv 10 % dextrose\n- **C.** 1mg IM glucagon\n- **D.** IM Pabrinex\n- **E.** 5% dextrose orally\n81. A 55 Years old male with history of hypertension came\ninto the ED with a complaint of sudden severe abdominal\npain. After initial assessment at triage, you suspect\nabdominal aortic aneurysm. Which one of the following is\nthe most common site of abdominal aortic aneurysm?\n\n## Question 81 of 90\n\n- **A.** Suprarenal aorta\n- **B.** Epigastrium\n- **C.** Below diaphragm\n\n## Question 39 of 54\n\nThe most common location of arterial aneurysm\nformation is the abdominal aorta, while the most\ncommon site of abdominal aortic aneurysm is\ninfrarenal aorta (90 to 99%)\n- **D.** Infrarenal aorta\n- **E.** At the level of umbilicus\n82. A cardiac patient presents to the Emergency\ndepartment with an acutely painful, red, hot, swollen big\ntoe. He has past medical history of gout and severe heart\nfailure. Gout is diagnosed. Which of the following is the\ndrug of choice for this patient?\nColchicine mechanism of action: Colchicine\nhas anti-inflammatory properties. It disrupts\ncytoskeletal functions by inhibiting beta-tubulin\npolymerization into microtubules (Recall),\npreventing activation, degranulation, and migration\nof neutrophils associated with mediating gout\nsymptoms.\nColchicine is useful in patients with heart failure and\nthose taking anticoagulants\nAvoid colchicine in patients with blood dyscrasias\nand bone marrow disease\nNSAIDs are contraindicated in a patient with heart\nfailure. (Diclofenac is contraindicated in people with\nischemic heart disease).\n\n## Question 82 of 90\n\n- **A.** NSAID\n- **B.** Paracetamol\n- **C.** Allopurinol\n- **D.** Colchicine\n- **E.** Prednisolone\n84. Which of the following NSAIDs has the least\ngastrointestinal side effects?\n\n## Question 84 of 90\n\n- **A.** Piroxicam\n- **B.** Ibuprofen\n- **C.** Naproxen\n- **D.** Diclofenac\n- **E.** Indomethacin\n\n## Question 40 of 54\n\nNSAIDS-induced GI side effects:\nLowest risk=ibuprofen\nHighest risk=piroxicam, ketorolac\nNSAIDs cause GERD by inhibiting the COX 1\nenzymes (cox 1 stimulation produce prostaglandins\nthat help to maintain the gastric mucosal integrity)\nNSAIDs can cause ulcers by interfering with the\nstomach's ability to protect itself from gastric acid\n(by inhibiting the function of prostaglandins)\nRECALL\nNSAID causes hemolytic anemia=Mefenamic acid\n(RECALL).\n85. A 32 Years old lady came into the ED with Complaint of\npain in the foot. She gives history of taking certain\nantibiotics prescribed by her GP. Which of the following\nantibiotics class are associated with tendon injury?\nTendon damage and QT interval prolongation both\nare the common side effects of quinolones\nQT prolongation leads to Torsades de pointes\nHigh Yield:\nSummary Of Drugs And Their\nSide Effects\n\n## Question 85 of 90\n\n- **A.** Quinolones\n- **B.** Cephalosporins\n- **C.** Macrolides\n- **D.** Tetracyclines\n- **E.** Aminoglycosides\n\n## Question 41 of 54\n\n86. A 32 Years old male sustained a dirty wound on his leg\nwhile running on the ground. Which of the following\npathogen is conditional causing infection after puncture\nwounds?\nPathogen and Types: (High\nYield)\n\n## Question 86 of 90\n\n- **A.** E.Coli\n- **B.** Treponema pallidum\n- **C.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **D.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **E.** Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n88. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with chest pain\nand the following ECG is taken. Which artery is most likely\nbe occluded? ￼\nECG demonstrates Acute Inferior Myocardial\nInfarction due to occlusion of RCA\nSt-elevation in inferior leads (II, III,aVF) with\nreciprocal ST-depression in I, aVL\nNote: Both inferior wall MI and posterior wall MI\noccurs due to occlusion of Right coronary artery.\n\n## Question 88 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left anterior descending\n- **B.** Right coronary artery\n- **C.** Obtuse marginal artery\n- **D.** left main coronary artery\n- **E.** Left circumflex\n89. A 52-year-old man is unwell with crushing central chest\npain. An ECG shows significant ST segment changes.\n\n## Question 89 of 90\n\n## Question 42 of 54\n\nAngiography shows occlusion of the circumflex branch of\nthe left coronary artery.\nThis condition most commonly results in ischemia in which\narea of the heart?\nLCA divides into the anterior interventricular branch\nand the circumflex branch. The circumflex branch\nfollows the coronary sulcus to the left border and\nonto the posterior surface of the heart and supply\nleft atrium and left ventricle\n- **A.** Anterior part of the left ventricle\n- **B.** Anterior part of the right ventricle\n- **C.** Inferior part of the left ventricle\n- **D.** Posterior part of the left ventricle\n- **E.** Inferior part of the right ventricle\n90. A 10-year-old male presents to the ED with abdominal\npain, vomiting and bleeding per rectum. After initial\nassessment and imaging is suggestive of Meckel's\ndiverticulum. The blood supply of Meckel's diverticulum is\nfrom which of the following:\nRich blood supply to Meckle's diverticulum is from\nSuperior mesenteric artery\nProximal to the major duodenal papilla – supplied\nby the gastroduodenal artery (branch of the\ncommon hepatic artery from the coeliac trunk\nDistal to the major duodenal papilla – supplied by\nthe inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (branch of a\nsuperior mesenteric artery)\nMeckel's Diverticulum: (High Yield)\nTrue diverticula of all 3 layers of the small intestines\nCaused by incomplete obliteration of the vitelline\nduct.\nRule of 2s: 2 years old, 2 ft from ileocecal valve, 2 in\nlong, 2% of population\nUsually, asymptomatic\n\n## Question 90 of 90\n\n- **A.** Gastroduodenal artery\n- **B.** Inferior mesenteric artery\n- **C.** Superior mesenteric artery\n- **D.** Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery\n- **E.** Celiac trunk\n\n## Question 43 of 54\n\nPainless bleeding due to an ulcer caused by\nheterotopic gastric tissue most common\npresentation in children.\nObstruction is caused by intussusception, volvulus,\nand hernia most common presentation in adults.\n1. A 26-Year-old girl presents to the ED with trauma to the\ninternal auditory meatus. Which of the following nerves are\nmost likely injured:\nThe internal auditory meatus also referred to as the\ninternal acoustic meatus lies in the temporal bone\nand exists between the inner ear and posterior\ncranial fossa. It includes the vestibulocochlear nerve\n(CN VIII), facial nerve (CN VII), the labyrinthine\nartery, and the vestibular ganglion.\n\n## Question 1 of 90\n\n- **A.** Vestibulocochlear nerve and accessory nerve\n- **B.** Facial nerve and abducens nerve\n- **C.** Trochlear nerve and abducens nerve\n- **D.** Facial nerve and glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **E.** Vestibulocochlear nerve and facial nerve\n3. Whilst in your physiology lecture you ask medical\nstudents, what is the method to calculate mean arterial\npressure?\nPulse pressure is difference between the Systolic\nand Diastolic pressure e.g., 120-80=40\nMean arterial pressure:  Diastolic pressure + 1/3\n(pulse pressure) e.g., 80+ 1/3 (120-80) = 93.2\nCerebral perfusion pressure= MAP- intracranial\npressure (RECALL)\n\n## Question 3 of 90\n\n- **A.** Systolic pressure + 1/3 (pulse pressure)\n- **B.** Diastolic pressure + Systolic pressure/2\n- **C.** Diastolic pressure + Systolic pressure\n- **D.** Diastolic pressure + pulse pressure\n- **E.** Diastolic pressure + 1/3 (pulse pressure)\n\n## Question 44 of 54\n\n4. A 60-year-old male presents to ED with the complaint of\nscrotal pain and swelling, you suspect epididymo-orchitis,\nand prescribe Quinolone. What is the mechanism of action\nof Quinolone?\nQuinolone interferes with the bacterial nucleic acid\nsynthesis\nAcyclovir= inhibits bacterial DNA synthesis\n(RECALL)\nAminoglycosides=inhibit bacterial protein synthesis\n(bind bacterial 30s subunit) RECALL\nCephalosporin=bacterial cell wall synthesis inhibitor\nchloramphenicol= inhibit bacterial protein synthesis\nmacrolides=inhibit protein synthesis (bind to the\nbacterial 50s ribosomal subunit)\nMetronidazole= bacterial nucleic acid synthesis\ninhibitor (RECALL)\npenicillin=inhibit bacterial cell wall synthesis\nTetracycline= inhibits bacterial protein synthesis\n(binds to 30S subunit of ribosomes)\nTrimethoprim=inhibit bacterial metabolism.\n\n## Question 4 of 90\n\n- **A.** Inhibition of bacterial protein synthesis\n- **B.** Inhibition of bacterial metabolism\n- **C.** Inhibition the bacterial cell wall synthesis\n- **D.** Inhibition of bacterial cell membrane synthesis\n- **E.** Inhibition of bacterial nucleic acid synthesis\n8. First part of duodenum lies at which level of vertebral\nlevel?\nFirst part of the duodenum extends from the\npyloric sphincter of the stomach wraps around\nhead of pancreas in C-shaped and ends at\nduodenojejunal flexure (Recall)\nFirst part lies at vertebral level L1.\n2nd part L2-L3\n3rd part longest section crossing inferior vena cava\nand Aorta at level L3\n\n## Question 8 of 90\n\n- **A.** T8\n- **B.** L2\n- **C.** T12\n- **D.** T6\n- **E.** L1\n\n## Question 45 of 54\n\n4th part: pass upward on left of abdominal Aorta at\nL2\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: (High Yield)**\n> Duodenal ulcer: erosion of the mucosa in the\n> duodenum\n> most likely to occur in the superior portion of the\n> duodenum.\n\nThe most common causes of duodenal ulcers are\nHelicobacter pylori infection and chronic NSAID\ntherapy.\nDuodenal ulcer most likely erosion of the\ngastroduodenal artery\n14. A 21-year-old male patient brought into the ED after\nroad traffic collision. After initial assessment and imaging\nshowed damage to the L1 nerve root. Which of the\nfollowing muscles are most likely affected?\n\n## Question 14 of 90\n\n- **A.** Internal and external oblique\n- **B.** Rectus abdominis and transverse abdominis\n- **C.** External oblique and transversus abdominis\n- **D.** Gluteus maximus and gluteus minimus\n- **E.** Internal oblique and transversus abdominis\n16. A 25-year-old male with history of dysphagia to solid\nfoods came with complaint of nausea and vomiting. The\nesophagus passes through diaphragm at which vertebral\nlevel?\n\n## Question 16 of 90\n\n- **A.** T8\n- **B.** T12\n- **C.** L2\n- **D.** T11\n- **E.** T10\n\n## Question 46 of 54\n\n23. A 7-year-old child is brought into the ED by his mother.\nHis mother states that child had wild berries then he\nsuddenly started to have tachycardia, dry skin and urinary\nretention. What is the likely cause of this condition?\nAtropa belladonna commonly known as deadly\nnightshade is a perennial herbaceous plant with\nleaves and berries that are extremely toxic contains\natropine and scopolamine both antagonist at\nmuscarinic receptor and block parasympathetic\n\n## Question 23 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increased parasympathetic response\n- **B.** Sympathomimetic activity\n- **C.** Hypnotic response of wild berries\n- **D.** Cholinergic response\n- **E.** Parasympathetic block\n25. You are going to attempt carotid sinus massage in a\npatient with SVT. You ask your colleague at which of the\nfollowing level you should apply pressure?\nThe carotid sinus is the reflex area of the carotid\nartery consisting of baroreceptors which monitor\nblood pressure. The carotid sinus is located at the\nbifurcation of the common carotid artery, which is\nfound at the level of the thyroid cartilage and\nmedial to the sternocleidomastoid muscle\n\n## Question 25 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hyoid bone\n- **B.** Thyrohyoid membrane\n- **C.** Cricoid cartilage\n- **D.** Temporomandibular joint\n- **E.** Thyroid cartilage\n36. Which of the following diet is a rich source of calcium?\n\n## Question 36 of 90\n\n## Question 47 of 54\n\nRcem key:  soyabean is a rich source of calcium\nNote: Dairy products which include cheese, leafy\nvegetables, and Soybean is rich in calcium.\nAlmond is also rich in calcium but if both almond\nand cheese are in option choose dairy product\ncheese because cheese has more calcium than\nalmond per 100gm.\n- **A.** Broccoli\n- **B.** Broccoli\n- **C.** Tomato\n- **D.** Sun flower\n- **E.** Soyabean\n42. A 23-year-old male presents to the ED with a complaint\nof loss of sensation in the medial half of palm. Regarding\nulnar nerve receives its fibers from which of the following\nnerve roots?\nBRANCHES OF LATERAL CORD   "LML"\n1.Lateral pectoral nerve\n2.Musculocutaneous nerve\n3.Lateral root of median nerve\nBRANCHES OF MEDIAL CORD     "M4U"\n1.Medial pectoral nerve\n2.Medial cutaneous nerve of arm\n3.Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm\n4.Medial root of median nerve\n5.Ulnar nerve\nBRANCHES OF POSTERIOR CORD   "ULTRA"\n1.Upper subscapular nerve\n2.Lower subscapular nerve\n\n## Question 42 of 90\n\n- **A.** C6-C7\n- **B.** C5-C6\n- **C.** C7-C8\n- **D.** C7-T1\n- **E.** C8-T1\n\n## Question 48 of 54\n\n3.Thoracodorsal nerve\n4.Radial nerve\n5.Axillary nerve\nWay to remember root value of upper limb\nnerves\nC3, C4: Supraclavicular nerve\nC5, C6: Axillary nerve (RECALL)\nC5, C6, C7: Musculocutaneous Nerve and long\nthoracic nerve\nC6, C7, C8: Thoracodorsal Nerve\nC5, C6, C7, C8 and T1: Median and Radial Nerve\nC8-T1: Ulnar nerve\n43. You are teaching group of medical students in ED and\ntopic of discussion is blood supply of the heart. Which\nstructure is most likely affected by occlusion of right\ncoronary artery?\nCardiac blood supply:\n1: The Right coronary artery supplies:\nThe right atrium and most of right ventricle\nThe sinoatrial and atrioventricular node\nPosterior interventricular artery, also known as the\nposterior descending artery (PDA), is a branch of the\nright coronary artery supply posterior 1/3 of\ninterventricular septum and posterior aspect of\nright and left ventricle. Most common artery\ninfracted in posterior wall MI\nNote: Occlusion of right coronary artery can result\ninto ischemia of SA and AV node and\nsubsequently bradycardia or heart block (Recall)\n2: The left coronary artery supplies via main\nbranches below:\nAnterior interventricular branch (LAD)\nLCX\nHigh yield:\n\n## Question 43 of 90\n\n- **A.** Bundles of His\n- **B.** Left atrium\n- **C.** Interventricular septum\n- **D.** Left ventricle\n- **E.** SA node\n\n## Question 49 of 54\n\nLocalization of lesion in ST elevation MI and\noccluded coronary artery. Only remember\nmyocardial area and occluded coronary artery.\nEasy to remember:\nSeptal and Anterior wall MI: LAD (Anterior\ninterventricular branch) occluded.\nLateral wall MI: LCX occluded.\n49. A 30 years old male with a history of RTA was brought\ninto the ED with a complaint of irregular breathing. Which of\nthe following muscle is the accessory muscle of expiration?\nDiaphragm: Normal quiet inspiration\nNormal expiration: Passive recoil of lungs\nInspiration: External intercostal muscle\nExpiration: Internal intercostal muscle\nAccessory muscle of inspiration:\nSternocleidomastoid\nAccessory muscle of expiration: Transversus\nabdominal muscles\nForced expiration: Internal intercostal muscle and\nrectus abdominus (RECALL)\n\n## Question 49 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sternocleidomastoid\n- **B.** Internal intercostal muscle\n- **C.** External intercostal muscle\n- **D.** Diaphragm\n- **E.** Transversus abdominus\n52. A 50-year-old male known case of hypertension\npresents to the ED with complaint of vertigo, ataxia, ptosis,\nand miosis. Which of the following blood vessel is most\nlikely occluded?\n\n## Question 52 of 90\n\n## Question 50 of 54\n\nPosterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion result\nin type of stroke called Lateral medullary syndrome\nor Wallenberg's syndrome characterized by variety\nof symptoms which include:\nVertigo, vomiting, nystagmus\nCrossed sensory disturbance\nIpsilateral Horner sign\nDysphagia, dysarthria\nIpsilateral ataxia\nOcclusion of AICA is considered rare, but generally\nresults in a lateral pontine syndrome, also known as\nAICA syndrome\n- **A.** Middle cerebral artery\n- **B.** Superior cerebral artery\n- **C.** Anterior inferior cerebral artery\n- **D.** Pontine artery\n- **E.** Posterior inferior cerebral artery\n60. A 40 years old male presents into the ED with a\ncomplaint of being unable to move his jaw. You ask your\njunior colleague to test the jaw reflex. If jaw reflex is lost\nwhich of the following nerve is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 60 of 90\n\n- **A.** Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve\n- **B.** Vagus nerve\n- **C.** Facial nerve\n- **D.** Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **E.** Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve\n\n## Question 51 of 54\n\n69. Which one of the following muscles are flexor of thigh\nat hip joint and flexor of leg at knee joint?\nAll the muscles of anterior compartment of thigh\nare flexors of thigh at hip joint while Quadriceps\nfemoris (Rectus femoris, Vastus lateralis, Vastus\nmedialis and Vastus intermedius) is extensor of leg\nat knee joint. Some exceptions are:\nSartorius is also flexor of leg at knee joint and lateral\nrotator and abductor at hip joint\nRectus femoris is also flexor of thigh at hip joint\n(RECALL)\nLateral rotators of hip: Anterior C.O.Thigh muscles\ninclude Sartorius, iliacus and psoas major\nLateral rotators of hip: Muscles of the hip region\ninclude Piriformis (RECALL), obturator internus and\nexternus, gamellus superior and inferior and\nquadratus lumborum\nNote: All the muscles of anterior compartment of\nthigh are innervated by femoral nerve except psoas\nmajor that is through lumbar plexus (RECALL)\n\n## Question 69 of 90\n\n- **A.** Pectineus\n- **B.** Rectus femoris\n- **C.** Psoas major\n- **D.** Iliacus\n- **E.** Sartorius\n72. Regarding propranolol use in thyroid crises, which one\nof the following is true?\nPropranolol is used as short-term symptomatic\ntherapy till the effect of Carbimazole develops.\n\n## Question 72 of 90\n\n- **A.** To potentiate the effect of radioactive iodine\n- **B.** In patients Carbimazole contraindicated\n- **C.** In patients not responding to Carbimazole\n- **D.** As long-term therapy after subtotal\nthyroidectomy\n- **E.** As short-term symptomatic therapy till effect of\nCarbimazole develops\n\n## Question 52 of 54\n\n75. A 30 Years old presents into the ED with a complaint of\npain in his jaw radiating to his ear. Which of the following\nnerve is most likely affected?\nNote: Skin of ear lobule is innervated by greater\nauricular nerve (Sensory branch from cervical\nplexus) RECALL\n\n## Question 75 of 90\n\n- **A.** Supratrochlear nerve\n- **B.** Facial nerve\n- **C.** Buccal Nerve\n- **D.** Inferior alveolar nerve\n- **E.** Auriculotemporal Nerve\n83. A 52-year-old male presents to ED complaining of\ndizziness and discomfort. After initial assessment at triage\nhis heart rate is found to be 40-60 bpm regular rhythm.\nECG demonstrates a normal QRS complex with absent P\nwaves. Which part of the conducting system is most likely\nacting as pacemaker in this patient?\nConductive Pathway of Heart:\nSA node initiates the cardiac muscle (pacemaker) →\nInternodal fibers and AV node (delay impulse\nfrom the atria to ventricle) →  AV bundle branches\n→  Purkinje fibers (from apex to ventricles).\n\n## Question 83 of 90\n\n- **A.** Bundle branches\n- **B.** Bundle of His\n- **C.** Sinoatrial node\n- **D.** Purkinje fibers\n- **E.** Atrioventricular node\n\n## Question 53 of 54\n\n0% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\nThe sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker\nlocated in Right atrium. The SAN will normally\ndischarge at a rate of 60-100 bpm. If the impulse\ngenerated in the Sa node is blocked, a group of cells\nfurther down the heart will become its pacemaker.\nThe atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary\npacemaker located between right atrium and\nventricle near the atrial septum, and will normally\ndischarge at about 40-60 beats per minute\nPurkinje fibers produce spontaneous action\npotential at rate of 20-40 bpm.\n87. Whilst in your cardiology rotation coronary angiography\nof a 44-year-old male patient revealed an occlusion of the\ncircumflex branch of left coronary artery. This patient has\nbeen suffering from myocardial infarction in which of the\nfollowing area?\nLeft coronary artery gives rise to two branches\nThe anterior interventricular branch: supply the\nright and left ventricle, the apex and anterior two\nthird of interventricular septum.\nLeft circumflex branch: supplying the left atrium\nand left ventricle\nThe right coronary artery supplies:\nthe right atrium and most of right ventricle\nthe sinoatrial and atrioventricular node\n\n## Question 87 of 90\n\n- **A.** Right and left ventricle\n- **B.** Right and left atria\n- **C.** Interventricular septum\n- **D.** Apex of heart\n- **E.** Left atrium and ventricle\n\n## Question 54 of 54	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c17b4951-8260-454d-a2bc-f914f42d586a	ef15ba2e-2dca-4639-9b94-5740ccb572f9	mock-i-paper-ii	Mock 1 — Paper 2 (Clinical SBA Exam)	quiz	# Mock – I Paper – Ii\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer scenario. Read carefully before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\nMrcem Primary Complete Course ,High Yield Stuff and Questions Bank ( For\nDiet ii Late 2026)\nOverview  Comments\nShare your score colleagues\n\nLinkedIn  Facebook  Twitter\n\n## Question 18 of 90\n\n1. A 27-year-old man falls from a ladder. Neurological\nexamination reveals an isolated defect of proprioception.\nWhich tract is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 1 of 90\n\n- **A.** Dorsal column\n- **B.** Lateral corticospinal\n- **C.** Lateral spinothalamic\n- **D.** Anterior spinothalamic\n- **E.** Posterior spinothalamic tract\n\n## Question 1 of 70\n\n2. What is the normal right ventricular systolic pressure?\nNormal RV systolic pressure is 20–30 mmHg and\nnormal diastolic pressure is 3–7 mmHg.\n\n## Question 2 of 90\n\n- **A.** 3-7mmhg\n- **B.** 20-30mmhg\n- **C.** 10-20mmhg\n- **D.** 5-10mmhg\n- **E.** 7-12mmhg\n3. The phrenic nerve receive contributions from which of\nthe following?\n\n## Question 3 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior rami of C3 -C5\n- **B.** Anterior rami to C4 -C7\n- **C.** Posterior rami of C1 -C3\n- **D.** Anterior rami of C3 – C5\n- **E.** Anterior rami of C1 -C3\n\n## Question 2 of 70\n\nThe phrenic nerve originates from cervical spinal\nroots C3, C4 and C5\nprovide motor innervation to the diaphragm\n(RECALL).\nprovide sensory innervation the central part of the\ndiaphragm, the pericardium and the mediastinal\npart of the parietal pleura. (RECALL)\n7. Regarding the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease\nwhich one of the following statements is true?\nBrunner's glands are located in the submucosa of\nthe duodenum. They secrete an alkaline fluid\ncontaining mucin, which protects the mucosa from\nthe acidic stomach contents entering the\nduodenum and provide alkaline condition for the\nintestinal enzymes to be active\nHigh Yield:\nA cell of pancreas =Glucagon\nB cells of pancreas -=Insulin\n\n## Question 7 of 90\n\n- **A.** Bicarbonate secretion from pancreatic ductal\ncells\n- **B.** Bicarbonate secretion from Brunner's glands\n- **C.** Gastric inhibitory polypeptide secretion from K\ncells\n- **D.** H+ Secretion from Brunner's glands\n- **E.** H+ Secretion from pancreatic ductal cells\n\n## Question 3 of 70\n\nD cells of pancreas =Somatostatin\nE cells of pancreas = Ghrelin (Ghrelin released\nmainly by the stomach and in small amounts\nreleased by small intestine and pancreas. Ghrelin\nregulates appetite and satiety (Recall)\nF cells of pancreas = Pancreatic polypeptide\nG cells in pyloric antrum = Gastrin\nI cell in duodenum and jejunum = CCK\nK cells in duodenum and jejunum = GIP\nS cells in duodenum =Secretin\n8. A new born baby brought into ED by his mother with\ncomplaint of grunting and respiratory distress. You suspect\ninfant respiratory distress syndrome. Regarding surfactant\nwhich one of the following is true?\nsurfactant produced by type 2 alveolar epithelial\ncells pneumocytes (Recall). it decreases surface\ntension and increases lung compliance\nSurfactant is made-up of 80% of phospholipids\n(Recall)\n10% of proteins\n10% of neutral lipids, mainly cholesterol\n\n## Question 8 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decrease lung compliance\n- **B.** Decrease surface tension\n- **C.** Increase surface tension\n- **D.** Produced by type 1 pneumocytes\n- **E.** Surfactant increases in smokers\n\n## Question 4 of 70\n\nNote: Type 1 alveolar epithelium is responsible for\ngaseous exchange. Impaired gas exchange\n(Hypoxia) is due to deficiency of Type 1 (Recall)\n9. A patient presents with life-threatening asthma and\nrequires a salbutamol Infusion. Which electrolyte\nabnormality is most likely to occur during this\nadministration?\nSalbutamol Is beta 2 agonist reduces the serum\npotassium levels by increasing the shift of\nextracellular potassium into the intracellular space.\nSalbutamol also used in the treatment of\nhyperkalemia (RECALL)\nDrugs causing hypokalemia:\nB2 agonist (salbutamol)\nTheophylline\nCorticosteroids\nThiazide\nFurosemide\n\n## Question 9 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hypomagnesaemia\n- **B.** Hypokalemia\n- **C.** Hypercalcemia\n- **D.** Hyponatremia\n- **E.** Hyperkalemia\n11. A patient requires intubation due to respiratory failure.\nYour consultant wants to ensure you correctly\n\n## Question 11 of 90\n\n## Question 5 of 70\n\npreoxygenate the patient to replace nitrogen with oxygen,\nallowing a safer apnea time during Intubation\nWhich lung volume measurement best describes the\nvolume of nitrogen that you are displacing?\nThe functional residual capacity (FRC) is the most\nimportant store of oxygen in the body. The greater\nthe FRC, the longer apnoea can be tolerated before\ncritical hypoxia develops. The aim of preoxygenation\nis to replace nitrogen in the FRC with oxygen; this\nprocess is also referred to as denitrogenation . This\nhas a significant impact on body oxygen store and\ntherefore increases tolerance to apnoea\nsubstantially.\nIntubation in the supine position can lead to a\ndecrease in functional residual capacity (Recall)\n- **A.** Functional residual capacity\n- **B.** Inspiratory capacity\n- **C.** V Vital capacity\n- **D.** Total lung capacity\n- **E.** Residual volume\n12. Regarding the intrinsic muscles of hand, Froment's sign\ntests the function of which one of the following muscles?\n\n## Question 12 of 90\n\n- **A.** Adductor pollicis\n- **B.** Flexor policies Longus\n\n## Question 6 of 70\n\nFroment's sign: Patient is asked to hold a piece of\npaper between thumb and flat palm as paper is\npulled away, patient will flex thumb at inter-\nphalangeal joint to maintain hold, this sign tests the\nfunction of adductor pollicis muscle which is supply\nby the ulnar nerve. (RECALL)\n- **C.** Abductor pollicis\n- **D.** Flexor policies breves\n- **E.** Opponents pollicis\n14. What is the vertebral level of bifurcation of the trachea\nin a living person?\nThe trachea extends from inferior end of larynx at C6\nvertebra and ends at the level of sternal angle (T4-T5\nIntervertebral disc) where it divides into left and\nright main bronchi.\n\n## Question 14 of 90\n\n- **A.** T2/T3\n- **B.** T5/T6\n- **C.** T6/T7\n- **D.** T4/T5\n- **E.** T3/T4\n15. The conus medullaris of the spinal cord lies at which of\nthe following vertebral level in adults?\n\n## Question 15 of 90\n\n## Question 7 of 70\n\nConus medullaris lies at birth= L3 level\nBy the age of 21= L1/L2\n- **A.** L3/L4\n- **B.** L4/L5\n- **C.** T11/T12\n- **D.** L1/L2\n- **E.** T12/L1\n16. A 40-year-old male presents to ER with headache and\nhypertensive emergency.  You prescribe sodium\nnitroprusside. What is the mechanism of action of sodium\nnitroprusside?\nSodium nitroprusside breaks down in circulation to\nrelease nitric oxide (NO).\nNitrates are potent coronary vasodilators.\nNitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, causing\nincrease in the intracellular concentration of cGMP\nin vascular smooth muscle cells. cGMP activates\nthe protein kinase G, which ultimately cause\nvascular smooth muscle relaxation (RECALL).\n\n## Question 16 of 90\n\n- **A.** Alpha adrenoceptor agonist\n- **B.** Renin inhibitor\n- **C.** Ca+ channel blocker\n- **D.** Release nitric oxide\n- **E.** Ca+ channel activator\n\n## Question 8 of 70\n\n17. A 50-year-old woman presents to the ED with\ngeneralized weakness and breathlessness. she also gives\na history of flu like illness 10 days ago. Her chest X-ray\nshows cardiomegaly. A diagnosis of myocarditis is made.\nwhich of the following pathogen is most likely involved in\nmyocarditis?\nMyocarditis is inflammation of the heart muscle\n(myocardium)\nThe most commonly implicated etiology is viral, of\nwhich enteroviruses, notably Coxsackie B, are the\nmost common.\nCoxsackie is the most common cause of hand,\nfoot and mouth disease typically spread via\nrespiratory aerosol and feco-oral route (Recall)\n\n## Question 17 of 90\n\n- **A.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **B.** Corynebacterium diphtheria\n- **C.** Coxsackievirus\n- **D.** Varicella zoster virus\n- **E.** Epstein Barr virus\n18. A 45-year-old man with a history of longstanding\nexcessive alcohol intake presents with massive ascites,\nyou are required to perform large-volume paracentesis,\nwhich one of the following fluids is best given during\nparacentesis?\n\n## Question 18 of 90\n\n- **A.** 20% Albumin\n- **B.** Half Saline\n\n## Question 9 of 70\n\nAdminister 1 unit (100ml) HAS 20% (Human\nAlbumin Solution, 20% i.e., 20g albumin  per 100ml)\n(STAT) following every 3 liters of ascites drained.\nNote: Paracentesis without albumin replacement\nleads to a fall in pulmonary capillary wedge\npressure, maximal at 6 hours, and can result in\ncirculatory and renal dysfunction.\nNote:  Ascites results from high pressure in the\nblood vessels of the liver (portal hypertension\nRecall) and low levels of a albumin.\n- **C.** Ringer lactate\n- **D.** 10% Albumin\n- **E.** 0.9% N/Saline\n19. Sensory innervation of the nasopharynx is supplied by\nwhich of the following nerve?\nEach subdivision of the pharynx has different\nsensory innervation:\nThe nasopharynx is supplied by the maxillary nerve\n\n## Question 19 of 90\n\n- **A.** Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **B.** Vagus nerve\n- **C.** Maxillary nerve\n- **D.** Facial nerve\n- **E.** Mandibular nerve\n\n## Question 10 of 70\n\nThe oropharynx is supplied d by the\nglossopharyngeal (RECALL)\nThe laryngopharynx is supplied by the vagus nerve.\n21. A young athlete presents to the ED after sustaining an\ninjury to the right upper extremity. Physical examination\nreveals weak right forearm flexion and an absent biceps\nreflex. Given these findings, sensation loss in which of the\nfollowing areas is most likely to be found in this patient?\nHigh yield:\n\n## Question 21 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior forearm\n- **B.** Medial forearm\n- **C.** Posterior arm\n- **D.** Lateral forearm\n- **E.** Thenar eminence\n24. A 70-year-old woman has a pathological fracture of her\nfemur. Her blood tests reveal hypercalcemia,\nhypophosphatemia, and raised parathyroid hormone.\n\n## Question 24 of 90\n\n## Question 11 of 70\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\nNote: In osteomalacia : Elevated alkaline\nphosphatase, Decreased vit D, Hypocalcemia and\nincreased PTH\n- **A.** Primary hyperparathyroidism\n- **B.** Paget's disease\n- **C.** Myeloma\n- **D.** Bony metastasis\n- **E.** Osteomalacia\n27. Which one of the following receptors is mainly\nresponsible for smooth muscle contraction and blood\nvessels constriction?\n\n## Question 27 of 90\n\n- **A.** Beta 3\n- **B.** Alpha 2\n- **C.** Alpha 1\n- **D.** Beta 1\n\n## Question 12 of 70\n\nA useful way to remember: Alpha 1 for constriction,\nAlpha 2 for inhibition, B 1 for heart, and B2 for\nrelaxation\nHigh yield:\nNote: Nitroprusside is metabolized to nitric oxide,\nwhich causes both arterial and venous dilation\n(Recall) This effect is immediate and results in a\ndecrease in both preload and afterload. (Recall)\n- **E.** Beta 2\n29. A 35-year-old male presents with fever and cough\nproductive of small amounts of clear sputum. Chest\nradiography documents bilateral lower lobe infiltrates that\nare more pronounced on the right side. Causative agent is?\n\n## Question 29 of 90\n\n- **A.** Streptococcus pnemoniae\n- **B.** Mycoplasma pneumoniae\n- **C.** Pseudomonas\n- **D.** Influenza virus\n- **E.** S. aureus\n\n## Question 13 of 70\n\nPneumococcal pneumonia is a common lung\ninfection caused by the organism Streptococcus\npneumoniae.\nStreptococcus pneumoniae is one of the most\ncommon causes of community-acquired\npneumonia. The clinical presentation of\npneumococcal pneumonia includes fever, cough\nand malaise and may also include chest pain and\ndyspnea or tachypnoea. Radiographic appearances\nconsidered classic for pneumococcal pneumonia\ninclude lobar consolidations and air bronchograms\nin adults and round pneumonia in children.\nNote: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a commensal\npathogen (Recall).\n30. Which of the following is not a physiological change in\npregnancy?\nPhysiological changes in pregnancy\n\n## Question 30 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increased cardiac output\n- **B.** Decreased gastric motility\n- **C.** Decrease in tidal volume\n- **D.** Increased red cell volume\n- **E.** Increased plasma volume\n\n## Question 14 of 70\n\n31. A 40-year-old male presents to the ED with headache.\nHis BP was found to be 180/90. He is currently under\ninvestigation for suspected Cushing’s syndrome. What is\nthe most common cause of Cushing disease?\nCushing’s disease refers to the specific condition of\nexcessive corticosteroids as a result of ACTH-\nsecreting pituitary adenoma.\n\n## Question 31 of 90\n\n- **A.** Small cell lung cancer\n- **B.** Pituitary adenoma\n- **C.** Squamous cell lung cancer\n- **D.** Adrenal adenoma\n- **E.** Adrenal carcinoma\n32. A 35 years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof swelling in the right inguinal region. Regarding hernia\nwhich one of the following types protrudes through\nHesselbach's triangle?\n\n## Question 32 of 90\n\n- **A.** Direct inguinal hernia\n- **B.** Indirect inguinal hernia\n- **C.** Spigelian hernia\n- **D.** Femoral hernia\n- **E.** Paraumbilical hernia\n\n## Question 15 of 70\n\nDirect inguinal hernia lies medial to inferior\nepigastric vessels while indirect inguinal hernia lies\nlateral to inferior epigastric vessels. (RECALL)\nMost common type of inguinal hernia=indirect\ninguinal hernia (Recall)\nMost common side of inguinal hernia=Right side\n(RECALL)\nThe floor of the inguinal canal is the inguinal\nligament, otherwise known as the Poupart\nligament, which is formed by the external oblique\naponeurosis (RECALL)\nType of hernia that enters the deep inguinal\nring=indirect inguinal hernia (RECALL)\nType of hernia that protrudes through Hesselbach’s\ntriangle=Direct inguinal hernia (RECALL).\n33. A 40-year-old male presents to ER with a deep\nlaceration to the proximal part of the forearm. On\nexamination when patient is asked to make a fist, he is\nunable to flex the metacarpophalangeal joints or\ninterphalangeal joints of index and middle or thumb, ring\nand little fingers are intact but there is loss of sensation\nover the lateral palm and the Palmer surface of lateral three\nand a half fingers. Nerve has most likely been affected is?\nNerve roots: C6 – T1 (also contains fibers from C5 in\nsome individuals).\n\n## Question 33 of 90\n\n- **A.** Radial Nerve\n- **B.** Median nerve\n- **C.** Musculocutaneous nerve\n- **D.** Median and radial nerve\n- **E.** Ulnar nerve\n\n## Question 16 of 70\n\nMotor functions: Innervates the flexor and pronator\nmuscles in the anterior compartment of the forearm\n(except the flexor carpi ulnaris and part of the flexor\ndigitorum profundus, innervated by the ulnar nerve\n(RECALL)). Also supplies innervation to the thenar\nmuscles and lateral two lumbricals in the hand.\n(RECALL)\nSensory functions: Gives rise to the palmar\ncutaneous branch, which innervates the lateral\naspect of the palm, and the digital cutaneous\nbranch, which innervates the lateral three and a half\nfingers on the anterior (palmar) surface of the hand\nIn Median nerve injury little finger is spared.\n(Innervated by ulnar nerve) RECALL\nwasting of thenar eminence: nerve involved=\nmedian nerve (RECALL)\nwasting of Hypothenar eminence: nerve involved=\nulnar nerve (RECALL)\n34. A 64-year-old man presents to the emergency\ndepartment with sudden painless loss of vision in his left\neye. Which one of the following arteries is most likely\noccluded?\nCentral retinal artery occlusion causes sudden\npainless loss of vision\nCentral retinal artery is a branch of ophthalmic\nartery, and Opthalmic artery is a first major branch\n\n## Question 34 of 90\n\n- **A.** External carotid artery\n- **B.** Vertebral artery\n- **C.** Facial artery\n- **D.** Internal carotid artery\n- **E.** Posterior auricular artery\n\n## Question 17 of 70\n\nof internal carotid artery\n35. A 42-year-old man had a long history of heavy smoking,\npoorly controlled blood pressure (BP), and irregular health\ncheckups. He developed projectile vomiting and then\nsudden loss of consciousness. He was taken to the\nemergency room within 30 min. The vital signs showed BP\n165/95 mmHg, GCS score of E1M2V1, and bilateral\npinpoint pupil, this is most likely a case of?\nPontine hemorrhage is a form of intracerebral\nhemorrhage usually result from poorly controlled\nhypertension, characterized by sudden loss of\nconsciousness, quadriplegia, pinpoint pupil, and\nrespiratory difficulty\n\n## Question 35 of 90\n\n- **A.** Pontine hemorrhage\n- **B.** Metastatic thyroid carcinoma\n- **C.** Wallenberg syndrome\n- **D.** Subdural hematoma\n- **E.** Vertebral artery dissection\n36. A 55 years old male known case of HIV presented to\nthe ED with Complain of Cough and breathlessness. You\nsuspect pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia, which one of the\nfollowing is treatment of choice?\n\n## Question 36 of 90\n\n- **A.** Respiratory Quinolones\n- **B.** Trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole\n\n## Question 18 of 70\n\nTrimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole is\nrecommended first line treatment against\npneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia.\nNote: Trimethoprim causes folate deficiency that’s\nwhy teratogenic in early pregnancy.\n- **C.** Ceftriaxone\n- **D.** Gentamycin\n- **E.** Tetracyclines and sulfamethoxazole\n37. Regarding the epithelium of respiratory tract. Which of\nthe following is true?\nRespiratory tract from the trachea down until the\nrespiratory bronchioles is lined with Ciliated\ncolumnar epithelium\n\n## Question 37 of 90\n\n- **A.** Transitional epithelium\n- **B.** Simple squamous epithelium\n- **C.** Simple stratified epithelium\n- **D.** Ciliated columnar epithelium\n- **E.** Stratified columnar epithelium\n38. From sitting to standing there is compensation by which\nmechanism?\n\n## Question 38 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increase TPR\n\n## Question 19 of 70\n\nNormally from sitting to standing position, there is\nof pooling of blood in veins of the leg due to gravity\nthat's why central venous pressure, stroke volume\nand cardiac output fall and fall in blood pressure\nthis fall in BP rapidly sense by baroreceptor reflex\nwhich cause vasoconstriction and an increase in\nheart rate and contractility, so restoring the stroke\nvolume, cardiac output and blood pressure.\n- **B.** Increase heart rate\n- **C.** Increase venous return\n- **D.** Decrease heart rate\n- **E.** Decrease Cardiac output\n40. A 30 years old male presents to the ED with complain\nof abdominal bloating, nausea, and bouts of watery\ndiarrhea. You suspect infection of giardia lamblia. Giardia\nlamblia attaches to gut mucosa via?\nMicroorganism & Mechanism of Attachment\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae, E-Coli=Adheres to the\ngenital mucosa using fimbriae\n\n## Question 40 of 90\n\n- **A.** Specialized sucking disc\n- **B.** Attaches by its haemagglutinin antigen\n- **C.** Specialized hooks\n- **D.** Curli fibers\n- **E.** Using fimbriae\n\n## Question 20 of 70\n\nInfluenza Attaches =by its haemagglutinin\nantigen (Recall)\nGiardia lamblia=Attaches to gut mucosa via a\nspecialized sucking disc\n41. Whilst in your physiology lecture you ask medical\nstudents, which of the following causes a right shift in the\noxygen dissociation curve?\n\nCauses of left ward shift\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up\noxygen)\nIncreased pH\nDecreased pCO2\nDecreased temperature\nDecreased H+\nDecreased 2-3DPG\nCO poisoning\nMethemoglobinemia\nFetal hemoglobin (Recall)\nCauses of Right ward shift\n\n## Question 41 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increase PH\n- **B.** Decrease PCO2\n- **C.** Decrease H+\n- **D.** Fetal Hb\n- **E.** Decrease PH\n\n## Question 21 of 70\n\n: Decreased affinity for oxygen (More willing to give\nup oxygen)\nDecreased pH (Recall)\nIncreased pCO2\nIncreased H+\nIncreased Temperature\nIncreased 2-3DPG\nPregnancy (oxygen to the fetus)\n42. A 30-year-old male presents to the ED with a 3.5-week\nhistory of a cough and hemoptysis. These symptoms began\nafter he was diagnosed with upper respiratory tract\ninfection. He denies having any fever, night sweats, and\nhas not noticed any changes to his weight. Currently, his\nblood pressure is 155/90 mm Hg, and his pulse is 85 bpm.\nA physical exam is notable for bilateral crackles and he\nalso has edema on his lower extremities. Lab values reveal\na creatinine of 4.5 mg/dL. Urine tests show the presence of\nproteinuria and also hematuria. A chest X-ray demonstrates\nthe presence of bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Pulmonary\nfunction testing reveals an increased carbon monoxide\ndiffusion capacity (DLCO). What condition might this patient\nhave?\nChief complaints in case of Goodpasture syndrome\n(GPS):\n\n## Question 42 of 90\n\n- **A.** Nephrotic syndrome\n- **B.** IgA nephropathy\n- **C.** Goodpasture syndrome\n- **D.** PSGN\n- **E.** Lupus nephritis\n\n## Question 22 of 70\n\nShortness of breath: caused by alveolar\nhemorrhage.\nHemoptysis: due to lung involvement\nHematuria due to kidney involvement\nClinical Workup:\nPresence of pulmonary infiltrates on chest X-ray:\ncaused by pulmonary hemorrhage.\nIncreased carbon monoxide diffusion capacity\n(DLCO) on pulmonary function testing: the present\nof blood in the alveoli (due to alveolar hemorrhage)\nresults in increased DLCO\nLinear deposition of IgG in the glomeruli\n(immunofluorescence)\n44. Which of the following are the phagocytic cells derived\nfrom monocytes?\nMonocytes circulate in the bloodstream for about\none to three days and then typically migrate into\ntissues throughout the body where they\ndifferentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells.\n\n## Question 44 of 90\n\n- **A.** Macrophages\n- **B.** Mast cells\n- **C.** Eosinophils\n- **D.** NK cells\n- **E.** Neutrophils\n45. A 20 years old male presents to the ED following stab\nwound to antecubital fossa with complaint of unable to flex\nhis index and middle finger resulting in unopposed\n\n## Question 45 of 90\n\n## Question 23 of 70\n\nextension of index and middle finger. Which one of the\nfollowing nerves is most likely injured?\ncommon injuries affecting the median nerve\ninclude:\nSupracondylar fracture of humerus\nStab wound to antecubital fossa\nCompression of carpel tunnel syndrome (RECALL)\nMedian nerve innervation\nMotor supply: all the anterior compartment of\nforearm muscles (Flexor compartment) (except\nflexor Carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor\ndigitorum profundus), the thenar muscles (flexor\npolicies brevis, abductor pollicis brevis, opponent\npolicies) and lateral two lumbricals are supplied by\nmedian nerve\nSensory supply: lateral aspect of palm and palmar\nsurface and fingertips of lateral three and half\ndigits. (RECALL).\n- **A.** Radial nerve\n- **B.** Median nerve\n- **C.** Musculocutaneous nerve\n- **D.** Ulnar nerve\n- **E.** Axillary nerve\n46. A 32-year-old dialysis patient collapses at home. He is\ndue for dialysis today. He has severe hyperkaliemia k+ 7.4,\nand you perform a 12 lead ECG\n\n## Question 46 of 90\n\n## Question 24 of 70\n\nWhat ECG feature would you be most likely to see\nimmediately prior to deterioration to cardiac arrest?\n- **A.** Prolonged PR interval\n- **B.** Broad QRS complexes\n- **C.** Peaked T waves\n- **D.** Sinus tachycardia\n- **E.** Flattened P waves\n47. A 50-year-old male presents to ER with generalized\nweakness, on performing neurological examination you\nnoticed that he has unilateral absence of biceps reflex.\nWhich spinal nerves is responsible for this reflex?\n\n## Question 47 of 90\n\n- **A.** L1-L2\n- **B.** C5-C6\n- **C.** C7-C8\n\n## Question 25 of 70\n\nBiceps reflex root value =C5-C\nTriceps reflex root value= C7-C8\nknee = L3-L4(RECALL)\nAnkle= S1\n- **D.** C8- T1\n- **E.** C6-C7\n49. A 32-year-old male presents to ED with ongoing\ncontinuous vomiting. He is several dehydrated. Which of\nthe following hormone released in response to\ndehydration?\nADH reduces water loss via lowered urine volume.\nExtracellular dehydration (hypovolemia) stimulates\nspecific vascular receptors that signal brain centers\nto initiate drinking and ADH release\nADH acts on kidney to promote water retention and\non the vascular system cause vasoconstriction ADH\nalso known as vasopressin.\n• ADH and oxytocin are released from posterior\npituitary gland (RECALL).\n\n## Question 49 of 90\n\n- **A.** Antidiuretic hormone\n- **B.** Growth hormone\n- **C.** Oxytocin\n- **D.** Parathyroid\n- **E.** Somatostatin\n\n## Question 26 of 70\n\n50. A 30-year-old male presents to ED after being stabbed\nin the Axilla. On examination you note the loss of sensation\nover the lateral forearm and you suspect a\nMusculocutaneous nerve injury. The Musculocutaneous\nnerve arise from which of the following cord of brachial\nplexus?\nThe Musculocutaneous nerve(C5-C7) arise from the\nlateral cord of brachial plexus\nMotor supply: anterior compartment of arm\nmuscles (coracobrachialis, biceps brachii,\nbrachialis) RECALLL\nSENSORY supply: Lateral forearm (RECALL)\nFunction: flexion and supination of forearm\n(RECALL)\n\n## Question 50 of 90\n\n- **A.** Lateral posterior cord\n- **B.** Posterior cord\n- **C.** Medial cord\n- **D.** Lateral cord\n- **E.** Medial and posterior cord\n51. An 18-year-old boy presents to the ED with a\nnosebleed. On examination, you suspect the site of\nbleeding to be in little’s area which of the following blood\nvessels are likely to be involved?\n\n## Question 51 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anterior ethmoidal and posterior superior\nalveolar arteries\n\n## Question 27 of 70\n\nLittle’s area also known as Kieselbach's plexus is\nformed by:\nSuperior labial artery\nSphenopalatine artery\nThe greater palatine artery\nThe anterior ethmoidal artery\n- **B.** Descending palatine and ascending pharyngeal\narteries\n- **C.** Ascending pharyngeal and descending palatine\narteries\n- **D.** Sphenopalatine and superior labial arteries\n- **E.** Descending palatine and anterior ethmoidal\narteries\n52. A patient with a known spinal cord ependymoma\npresents to his neurologist for a checkup. He complains\nthat he has had difficulty walking, which he attributes to left\nleg weakness. On exam, he is noted to have 1/5 strength in\nhis left lower extremity, as well as decreased vibration and\nposition sensation in the left lower extremity and decreased\npain and temperature sensation in the right lower extremity.\nWhich of the following spinal cord lesions is most\nconsistent with his presentation?\n\n## Question 52 of 90\n\n- **A.** Syringomyelia\n- **B.** Posterior cord syndrome\n- **C.** Left-sided Brown-Sequard (hemisection)\n- **D.** Right-sided Brown-Sequard (hemisection)\n\n## Question 28 of 70\n\n- **E.** Anterior cord syndrome\n53. Regarding the blood supply of abdomen, superior\nmesenteric artery supply which one of the following\nstructures?\n1. coeliac trunk supply the\nAbdominal oesophagus\nstomach\npancreas\nliver\nspleen\ngallbladder\nduodenum proximal to major duodenal papilla\n2. Superior mesenteric artery supply the:\nDistal duodenum\n\n## Question 53 of 90\n\n- **A.** Kidneys\n- **B.** Ascending colon\n- **C.** Oesophagus\n- **D.** Sigmoid colon\n- **E.** Descending colon\n\n## Question 29 of 70\n\nJejunum\nIleum\ncecum\nAppendix\nAscending colon (RECALL)\nProximal two third of transverse colon\n3.Inferior mesenteric artery supply the:\nDistal transverse colon\nDescending colon (RECALL)\nSigmoid colon\nProximal rectum\n54. What is the quantitative relationship between gas\nsolubility and pressure?\nHenry's law: At a constant temperature, the amount\nof a given gas that dissolves in a given type and\nvolume of liquid is directly proportional to the\npartial pressure of that gas in equilibrium with that\nliquid." An equivalent way of stating the law is that\nthe solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly\nproportional to the partial pressure of the gas above\nthe liquid.\n\n## Question 54 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ficks law\n- **B.** Boyle’s law\n- **C.** Henry's law\n- **D.** Charles law\n- **E.** Entropy\n\n## Question 30 of 70\n\n55. A 20-year-old woman underwent arthroscopy on her\nright ankle because of chronic ankle pain after a sprain.\nAfter arthroscopy, the patient complained of pain on the\ndorsum of her right foot and felt a radiating pain from the\nanterolateral portal to the dorsomedial aspect of her foot,\nthis was most probably due to injury to which of the\nfollowing nerve?\nMotor innervation of superficial fibular nerve\nThe superficial fibular nerve supplies the fibularis\nlongus and the fibularis brevis. These muscles form\nthe lateral compartment of the lower leg.\nThey act to evert the foot (Recall) and thus act at\nthe subtalar joint (eversion is the action of turning\nthe foot outwards).\nSensory Functions\nSuperficial peroneal nerve carries sensations from\nthe anterolateral aspect of the leg and the greater\npart of the dorsum of the foot (except for the first\nweb space).\n\n## Question 55 of 90\n\n- **A.** Superficial fibular nerve\n- **B.** Sural nerve\n- **C.** Saphenous nerve\n- **D.** Deep fibular nerve\n- **E.** Tibial nerve\n57. The orbicularis Oris muscle perform which of the\nfollowing function?\n\n## Question 57 of 90\n\n## Question 31 of 70\n\nThe orbicularis Oris muscle act to close purse the\nlips. This muscle also helps in puckering the lips due\nto which it is sometimes known as the “kissing\nmuscle.” The musicians also use this muscle.\n- **A.** Blowing of the cheeks\n- **B.** Closing of eyes\n- **C.** Closing of the mouth\n- **D.** Pursing the lips\n- **E.** Opening of the mouth\n58. Which of the following type of blood vessels contributes\nthe most of systemic vascular resistance?\nResistance is a force that opposes the flow of a fluid.\nIn blood vessels, most of the resistance is due to\nvessel diameter. As vessel diameter decreases, the\nresistance increases and blood flow decreases.\nsmaller arteries and arterioles contain more muscle\nand are resistance vessels.\n\n## Question 58 of 90\n\n- **A.** Arteriole\n- **B.** Arteries\n- **C.** Venules\n- **D.** Capillaries\n- **E.** Veins\n\n## Question 32 of 70\n\nVeins have large diameter than equivalent arteries\nand provide less resistance\n59. A 5o years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof shortness of breath. He is known case of restrictive lung\ndisease. In restrictive lung disease which of the following\nresults would you expect to find on spirometry?\nIn restrictive pattern both FEV1 and FVC reduced\nbut FEV1/FVC ratio increased due to greater elastic\nrecoil\n\n## Question 59 of 90\n\n- **A.** Low FEV1/FVC ratio\n- **B.** Increased FEV1\n- **C.** Increased FEV1 /FVC ratio\n- **D.** Normal FVC\n- **E.** High FVC\n\n## Question 33 of 70\n\n60. Which one of the following organelles has essential role\nin achieving uterine involution?\nCell organelle involved in uterine involution is\nlysosome\nWhile Hormone uterine involution is oxytocin\n\n## Question 60 of 90\n\n- **A.** Lysosome\n- **B.** Mitochondria\n- **C.** Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n- **D.** Golgi body\n- **E.** Smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n\n## Question 34 of 70\n\nNote: Mitochondria are responsible for the\nproduction of chemical energy in the form of ATP.\n(Recall)\n61. What is the vertebral level of bifurcation of the trachea\nin a living person?\nThe trachea extends from inferior end of larynx at C6\nvertebra and ends at the level of sternal angle (T4-T5\nIntervertebral disc) where it divides into left and\nright main bronchi.\n\n## Question 61 of 90\n\n- **A.** T2/T3\n- **B.** T5/T6\n- **C.** T4/T5\n- **D.** T6/T7\n- **E.** T3/T4\n62. A 10 years old child admitted in hospital and being\ntreated for DKA. In DKA treating complication cerebral\nedema, hypertonic saline is used. How hypertonic saline\nwill decrease cerebral edema?\n\n## Question 62 of 90\n\n- **A.** There is no net movement of water\n- **B.** Na and k move out of cells\n- **C.** K move out of the cell\n\n## Question 35 of 70\n\nwhen hypertonic saline is administrated ECF\nbecomes more concentrated compared with ICF.\nOsmotic potential draw movement of water\nmolecules from inside to out of the cells.\nNote: After heavy exercise when person drink water\nit increases intracellular fluid ICF volume (Recall)\nbecause water is hypotonic to body fluids dilute\nextracellular fluid relative to intracellular fluid,\nosmotic potential draws water into cells.\n- **D.** Water moves out of the cells\n- **E.** Water moves into the cell\n63. A 60-year-old woman with a known malignancy on the\nanterior two thirds of the tongue presents with neck\nswelling.\nWhat is the most likely group of lymph nodes to be involved\nfirst?\nLymphatic Drainage of Oral Cavity\n1: The parotid nodes: sit upon the parotid gland\nand drain it\n\n## Question 63 of 90\n\n- **A.** Submandibular\n- **B.** Cervical\n- **C.** Preauricular\n- **D.** Submental\n- **E.** Superior clavicular\n\n## Question 36 of 70\n\n2: The submandibular nodes: lie on the lateral wall\nof the submandibular gland and drain the cheek,\nthe upper lip, the lower lip, the maxillary sinus, the\nupper and lower teeth, the anterior two thirds of\nthe tongue, the floor of the mouth, the vestibule\nand the gums\nSubmandibular nodes drain into deep cervical\nnodes\nAnterolateral tongue also drain into deep cervical\nnodes (Recall)\n3: The submental nodes: are found in the\nsubmental triangle below the chin and drain the\ntip of the tongue (Recall) and medial part of lower\nlip\nCervical Nodes\n1. The superficial cervical nodes: lie on the external\njugular vein and drain the skin over the angle of the\njaw and the skin covering the lower portion of the\nparotid gland.\n2. The deep cervical nodes are as follows:\n3. The jugulodigastric node sits posteroinferiorly to\nthe jaw and drains the tonsils and the tongue\n(Recall)\n4. The jugulo omohyoid node: is found close to the\nomohyoid muscle and drains the tongue (post 1/3).\n64. A 4-year-old African child with sickle cell disease was\nbrought into the ED with a complaint of bone pain\nassociated with a limp. Imaging revealed lucent bone\nlesion, you suspect osteomyelitis, what is the most likely\ncausative pathogen?\n\n## Question 64 of 90\n\n- **A.** Pseudomonas\n- **B.** Salmonella\n\n## Question 37 of 70\n\nSalmonella enterica is a gram-negative rod that\ncauses enterocolitis (Food poisoning), Enteric fever\n(Typhoid), Septicemia, and osteomyelitis\nPrimarily transmitted through fecal-oral route\nusually from ingestion of contaminated poultry,\neggs, and uncooked meat\nHigh yield:\nRegarding osteomyelitis\nMost common cause generally: Staph Aureus\n(Recall)\nThe most common cause in IV drug abuser: Staph\nAureus\nThe most common cause in sickle cell disease:\nSalmonella\nMost common cause after hip replacement surgery:\nStaph epidermidis\n- **C.** Staph aureus\n- **D.** E-Coli\n- **E.** Staph epidermidis\n66. A patient presents in ER with severe Constipation, his\nblood Reports show hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia causes\nan increase in which of the following hormone?\n\n## Question 66 of 90\n\n- **A.** Parathyroid\n- **B.** Thyroxine\n- **C.** T3, T4\n\n## Question 38 of 70\n\nParathyroid hormone (PTH) levels are mainly\ncontrolled by a feedback loop of calcium levels in\nyour blood to your parathyroid glands. In other\nwords, low calcium levels in your blood stimulate\nparathyroid hormone release, whereas high calcium\nlevels in your blood prevent your glands from\nreleasing parathyroid hormone\nIncrease in blood po4 concentration also stimulate\nthe PTH hormone release\nCirculating parathyroid hormone targets the distal\nconvoluted tubule and collecting duct, directly\nincreasing calcium reabsorption (RECALL).\n- **D.** ADH\n- **E.** Somatostatin\n67. A 30-year-old male patient brought into ED\nunresponsive after initial assessment you suspect opioid\ntoxicity you started naloxone. Naloxone works on which\none of the following receptors?\nNaloxone is opioid mu receptor antagonist rapidly\nreverse the symptoms of opioid toxicity.\n\n## Question 67 of 90\n\n- **A.** NMDA receptor Antagonist\n- **B.** Gaba receptor\n- **C.** NMDA receptor agonist\n- **D.** Opioid mu receptors antagonist\n- **E.** Opioid mu receptors agonist\n\n## Question 39 of 70\n\n68. A 45 years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof dizziness and increased pigmentation all over the body.\nInitial assessment at triage he was hypotensive. Lab value\nreveals hyponatremia and hyperkalemia. Which one of the\nfollowing hormone deficiency caused this condition?\nInsufficiency of adrenal cortex is called Addison's\ndisease\nDeficiency of aldosterone as seen is adrenal\nInsufficiency result in hyponatremia ,hyperkalemia\nand metabolic acidosis (Recall)\nCushing's Syndrome: Set of symptoms that occur\nwhen there is excess of cortisol\nMost common cause is exogenous Glucosteroids\nCushing's disease: when Cushing syndrome is\ncaused by ACTH producing pituitary tumor (Most\ncommon endogenous cause (Recall)\nACTH secreting pituitary adenoma\nExcess cortisol due to adrenal adenoma (Recall)\n\n## Question 68 of 90\n\n- **A.** PTH\n- **B.** Aldosterone\n- **C.** ADH\n- **D.** Androgens\n- **E.** Cortisol\n\n## Question 40 of 70\n\n69. A 50-year-old male presents to the Emergency\ndepartment with the complaint of swelling in ankle. She has\nrecently been started on loop diuretics by her GP. loops\ndiuretic act to inhibit the?\nloop diuretic e.g.: furosemide inhibit the Na+k+2CL-\nsymporter on luminal membrane in the thick\nascending limb of loop of Henle thus preventing the\nreabsorption of Nacl and water\nwhile thiazides act mainly on early segment of\ndistal tubule where they inhibit the Nacl\nreabsorption by binding to the Na+cl- cotransporter\n(RECALL).\n\n## Question 69 of 90\n\n- **A.** Na-K-2CL- channel\n- **B.** carbonic anhydrase\n- **C.** Na-K-channel\n- **D.** H-K- channel\n- **E.** Na-K-ATPase\n70. Detoxification of lipid drugs and other harmful\ncompounds in ER is carried out by?\n\n## Question 70 of 90\n\n- **A.** Cytochrome P450\n- **B.** Cytochrome 360\n- **C.** Cytochrome D\n- **D.** Cytochrome bf\n\n## Question 41 of 70\n\nCytochrome P450 enzymes function to metabolize\npotentially toxic compounds. There are 57 human\ngenes coding for various cytochrome450.\n- **E.** Cytochrome F\n71. Whilst performing neurological examination, you ask\nmedical students what is the sensory level of Umbilicus?\nT1= medial antecubital fossa (Recall)\nT2= Apex of axilla\nT3= third intercostal space\nT4= level of nipples (RECALL)\nT5= 5th intercostal space\nT6= level of xiphisternum (RECALL)\nT10= level of Umbilicus\nT11= midway between Umbilicus and inguinal\nligament\nT12= midpoint of inguinal ligament\n\n## Question 71 of 90\n\n- **A.** T11\n- **B.** T10\n- **C.** T8\n- **D.** T9\n- **E.** T7\n\n## Question 42 of 70\n\n72. A 40-year-old man injured his right wrist during a fall, X-\nrays demonstrate a lunate dislocation. He is complaining of\nsevere wrist pain and numbness in his hand\nDamage to what structure is most likely to be causing these\nsymptoms?\nLunate dislocation occurs by falling on a dorsiflexed\nwrist. The lunate is forced anteriorly, and\ncompresses the median nerve in carpal tunnel\ncausing the symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\n## Question 72 of 90\n\n- **A.** Distal radius\n- **B.** Ulnar nerve\n- **C.** Radial artery\n- **D.** Median nerve\n- **E.** Scapholunate ligament\n73. You have been asked to make a Quick assessment of\npatient's upper limb neurology as a part of secondary\nsurvey. You test each dermatome and note the patient has\nno sensation at the medial antecubital fossa. The medial\nantecubital fossa tests which of the following dermatomes?\n\n## Question 73 of 90\n\n- **A.** T1\n- **B.** C8\n- **C.** C5\n- **D.** C7\n\n## Question 43 of 70\n\nDERMATOMES\nC2= occipital protuberance (RECALL)\nC3= supraclavicular fossa\nC4= Acromioclavicular joint (RECALL)\nC5 = lateral antecubital fossa\nC6= thumb (RECALL)\nC7=middle finger\nC8=little finger\nT1= medial antecubital fossa\nT2= Apex of axilla\n- **E.** C6\n74. A 44-year-old male suffered from fracture of neck of the\nfibula now he is unable to do dorsiflexion of ankle, which\none of the following nerves is most likely injured?\nThe common peroneal nerve is also known as\ncommon fibular nerve is a branch of sciatic nerve,\narise at apex of popliteal fossa, continue by\n\n## Question 74 of 90\n\n- **A.** Tibial nerve\n- **B.** Obturator nerve\n- **C.** Femoral nerve\n- **D.** Common peroneal nerve\n- **E.** Sciatic nerve\n\n## Question 44 of 70\n\nwrapping around the neck of fibula where it is\nmost likely to be damaged (RECALL)\nFunction: The common peroneal nerve Innervate\nthe short head of the biceps femoris muscle.\ncommon fibular nerve innervates all the muscles of\nanterior and later compartments of the lower leg.\nThe anterior muscular compartment of the lower\nextremity consists of the tibialis anterior muscle,\nthe extensor hallucis longus muscle, and the\nextensor digitorum longus muscle. This\ncompartment is responsible primarily for the\ndorsiflexion of the foot (RECALL).\n76. A 25-year-old man presents with diabetic ketoacidosis.\nYou start to treat him with short-acting insulin and\nintravenous fluids as per your local protocol.\nWhich electrolyte disturbance is the most at risk of\ndeveloping as a result of your treatment?\nInsulin excess lowers the serum potassium\nconcentration by driving the K+ into the cells\nprimarily by enhancing the activity of NA-K-ATPase\npump.\nECG changes in hypokalemia and hyperkalemia\n\n## Question 76 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hypophosphatasemia\n- **B.** Hypokalemia\n- **C.** Hypermagnesemia\n- **D.** Hyponatremia\n- **E.** Hyperchloremia\n\n## Question 45 of 70\n\n77. A patient with a history of sinusitis presents unwell with\nfever, periorbital edema, visual disturbance, ptosis and\naltered sensation to the face. You suspect cavernous sinus\nthrombosis.\nWhich cranial nerve is most commonly affected?\nCavernous sinus thrombosis (CST) refers to the\nformation of a clot within the cavernous sinus. It is\nimportant to note that the superior ophthalmic vein\nforms an anastomosis with the facial vein.\nTherefore, the ophthalmic veins represent a\npotential route by which infection can spread from\nan extracranial to an intracranial site.\n\n## Question 77 of 90\n\n- **A.** Abducent\n- **B.** Trochlear\n- **C.** Trigeminal\n- **D.** Oculomotor\n- **E.** Facial\n\n## Question 46 of 70\n\nNote: The abducens nerve (CN VI) is most\ncommonly affected in cavernous sinus thrombosis\nStructures passing through cavernous sinus\nuseful mnemonic: O TOM CAT\nOTOM (oculomotor nerve, trochlear nerve,\nophthalmic branch, maxillary branch) refers to the\nlateral wall contents from superior to inferior\nCAT (internal carotid artery, abducens nerve,\ntrochlear nerve) refers to the horizontal contents,\nfrom medial to lateral.\n78. A 51-year-old-male patient with myasthenia gravis\npresents with difficulty swallowing and a cough that has\nbeen present for several weeks. A CT scan is performed,\nwhich shows the presence of a tumor of the thymus.\nWhich SINGLE statement regarding the thymus is true?\nThe thymus:\nIs a specialized primary lymphoid organ of the\nimmune system, within which T-lymphocytes\nmature.\n\n## Question 78 of 90\n\n- **A.** The cortex contains fewer lymphocytes than the\nmedulla\n- **B.** It is mainly located within the anterior\nmediastinum\n- **C.** The medulla contains Hassall’s corpuscles\n- **D.** The cortex is located centrally\n- **E.** It increases in size after puberty\n\n## Question 47 of 70\n\nAfter puberty, it decreases in size and is slowly\nreplaced by fat.\nIt is mainly located within the superior\nmediastinum, posterior to the manubrium sterni.\nA certain degree of anatomical variation exists, and\nin some individuals, it can extend superiorly into the\nneck or inferiorly into the anterior mediastinum.\nThe thymus has an asymmetrical, flat shape with a\nlobular structure.\nThe lobules are comprised of a series of follicles,\nwhich consist of a medullary and a cortical portion.\nThe cortex: Is located peripherally within each\nfollicle and is mainly composed of lymphocytes,\nsupported by a network of finely branched epithelial\nreticular cells.\nThe medulla: Is located centrally within each follicle\n79. Which one of the following hormones helps in the\nsecretion of HCL from the stomach?\nGastrin is a hormone that is produced by G-cells in\nthe lining of the stomach and upper small intestine.\nGastrin stimulates the stomach to release gastric\nacid.\n\n## Question 79 of 90\n\n- **A.** Renin\n- **B.** Pepsin\n- **C.** Gastrin\n- **D.** Secretin\n- **E.** Cholecystokinin\n\n## Question 48 of 70\n\n80. At journal club you present a paper on the use of a\nbiomarker to predict the presence of clinically important\ninfection.\nWhen assessing applicability to facilitate safe discharge\nwhat is the most useful statistical attribute?\nThe negative predictive value (NPV) describes the\nprobability of not having the disease. If the test\nresult is negative, the chances of the patient to have\nthe disease is low and can be safely discharge.\n\n## Question 80 of 90\n\n- **A.** Negative predictive value\n- **B.** Sensitivity\n- **C.** Positive predictive value\n- **D.** Positive likelihood ratio\n- **E.** Specificity\n82. Which of the following inhibits acetylcholine-mediated\nbronchospasm?\n\n## Question 82 of 90\n\n- **A.** Naproxen\n- **B.** Theophylline\n- **C.** Ipratropium\n- **D.** Salmeterol\n- **E.** Ephedrine\n\n## Question 49 of 70\n\nAcetylcholine plays an important role in the\npathophysiology of asthma via binding to airway\nmuscarinic receptors to trigger bronchoconstriction,\nmucus secretion and inflammation.\nIpratropium acts as an antagonist of the muscarinic\nacetylcholine receptor inhibit parasympathetic\ninduced bronchoconstriction.\n83. 45 years old male presented to the ED post thyroid\nsurgery with complain of periorbital numbness, paresthesia\nof hand and feet and abnormal movement what is the\ncause of his condition?\nPTH is primarily released in response to decreased\ncalcium. PTH causes demineralization of bones\nand causes increased plasma calcium level\n(Recall) and decreased phosphate levels\nPost-thyroid surgery most likely complication is\nparathyroid insufficiency leads to hypocalcemia\nNote: Parathyroid Harmone increases calcium\nreabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule of\nnephron (Recall)  and increases excretion of\nphosphate by inhibiting reabsorption in the\nproximal convoluted tubule of nephron\n\n## Question 83 of 90\n\n- **A.** Thyroid Crisis\n- **B.** Hypomagnesemia\n- **C.** Hypocalcemia\n- **D.** Hypothyroidism\n- **E.** Hypokalemia\n\n## Question 50 of 70\n\n84. Which of the following anesthetic agent cause vitamin\nB12 deficiency?\nExposure to nitrous oxide for prolonged periods, can\ncause vitamin B12 deficiency. Due to interference\nwith the action of vitamin B12, resulting in\nmegaloblastic anemia, neurologic manifestations\nsuch as myeloneuropathy and subacute combined\ndegeneration of the spinal cord.\n\n## Question 84 of 90\n\n- **A.** Thiopental sodium\n- **B.** Etomidate\n- **C.** Ketamine\n- **D.** Nitrous oxide\n- **E.** Propofol\n85. Elderly male presented with c/o high-grade fever with\nchills rigors and dysphagia. Also had h/o tooth sensitivity,\nfor which consulted dentist and found to have decayed\ntooth. On examination you bilateral swelling in the floor of\nmouth. Infection of which space is most common for above\npresentation?\n\n## Question 85 of 90\n\n- **A.** Parapharyngeal space\n- **B.** Retropharyngeal space\n- **C.** Submandibular space\n- **D.** Sub mental space\n\n## Question 51 of 70\n\nLudwigs's angina is life threatening cellulitis of the\nsoft tissue of the floor of the mouth and neck.\nOriginates from dental infection in the molars\nspecially 2nd and 3rd molars\ninvolves three compartments of the floor of the\nmouth, sublingual, submental, and submandibular\n(Most commonly).\n- **E.** Sub lingual space\n86. A Patient presents to the ED complaining of jaw pain\nwhich radiates towards his ear while eating, which of the\nfollowing nerve is responsible for this sensation of pain?\nThe Auriculotemporal nerve branch of mandibular\ninnervates the skin behind the temporomandibular\njoint and within the superior surface of the parotid\ngland. It has a course along a temporalis\nsuperficialis and innervates the tragus (RECALL)\nand part of the adjoining auricle of the ear and the\nposterior part of the temple.\n\n## Question 86 of 90\n\n- **A.** Auriculotemporal nerve\n- **B.** Zygomaticofacial Nerve\n- **C.** Infra trochlear nerve\n- **D.** Supra-Trochlear nerve\n- **E.** Zygomaticotemporal nerve\n87. A 30-year-old male unable to laterally rotate his arm\nfollowing fracture of surgical neck of humerus, which of the\nfollowing muscle most likely to be involved?\n\n## Question 87 of 90\n\n## Question 52 of 70\n\nThe axillary nerve is most commonly damaged due\nto fracture of surgical neck of humerus, which\nsupply the teres minor muscle which helps is lateral\nrotation of arm and also supply the deltoid muscle\nwhich cause abduction of arm...\nAxillary nerve root value =C5 and C6 (RECALL).\n- **A.** Teres major\n- **B.** Supraspinatus\n- **C.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **D.** Teres minor\n- **E.** Rhomboid major\n88. A 55-year-old male presented to ER with Achilles\ntendon rupture. He has recently started a course of\nantibiotics from his general physician. Which of the\nfollowing antibiotics was he most likely prescribed?\nSide effects of Quinolone (Ciprofloxacin)\nruptured tendon\nQT prolongation (recall)\n\n## Question 88 of 90\n\n- **A.** Penicillin\n- **B.** Metronidazole\n- **C.** Tetracycline\n- **D.** Ciprofloxacin\n- **E.** Doxycycline\n\n## Question 53 of 70\n\nPhotosensitivity\nconvulsions\nThere is increased risk of convulsion when\nQuinolones are taken with NSAIDS or theophylline.\nThere is an increased risk of tendon damage when\nQuinolones are taken with corticosteroids.\n(RECALL)\nCiprofloxacin increases plasma concentration of\ntheophylline(aminophylline). (RECALL)\n89. Regarding the blood supply of the colon, descending\ncolon receives its blood supply from which one of the\nfollowing arteries?\nAs a general rule, midgut-derived structures are\nsupplied by the superior mesenteric artery, and\nhindgut-derived structures by the inferior\nmesenteric artery.\nThe ascending colon: receives arterial supply from\ntwo branches of the superior mesenteric artery; the\nileocolic and right colic arteries.\nThe transverse colon: is derived from both the\nmidgut and hindgut, and so it is supplied by\nbranches of the superior mesenteric artery and\ninferior mesenteric artery:\n\n## Question 89 of 90\n\n- **A.** Gastro-pancreatic artery\n- **B.** Coeliac axis\n- **C.** Inferior mesenteric artery\n- **D.** Gastric artery\n- **E.** Superior mesenteric artery\n\n## Question 54 of 70\n\nRight colic artery (from the superior mesenteric\nartery\nMiddle colic artery (from the superior mesenteric\nartery)\nLeft colic artery (from the inferior mesenteric artery)\nThe descending colon: is supplied by a single\nbranch of the inferior mesenteric artery; the left\ncolic artery. The sigmoid colon receives arterial\nsupply via the sigmoid arteries (branches of the\ninferior mesenteric artery).\n90. All of the following drugs causes high anion gap\nmetabolic acidosis except?\nAnion gap can be calculated as (Na + K ) -- (Cl +\nHco3) Recall. Normal anion gap is 10-16 mmol/L\n\n## Question 90 of 90\n\n- **A.** Propylene glycol\n- **B.** Methylene glycol\n- **C.** Isoniazid\n- **D.** D lactates\n- **E.** Aspirin\n\n## Question 55 of 70\n\nNote: salicylate (Aspirin) overdose initially causes\nrespiratory alkalosis (Recall) followed by\nmetabolic acidosis\n4. A 17-year-old girl presents with a maculopapular rash.\nShe has a seven-day history of fever and sore throat and\nwas prescribed an antibiotic 3 days ago.\nWhich antibiotic is most likely to have been prescribed?\n\n## Question 4 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ciprofloxacin\n\n## Question 56 of 70\n\nIt is given primarily in streptococcal tonsillitis\nPhenoxymethylpenicillin also known as penicillin v\nis Used to treat bacterial infections, including ear,\nchest, throat and skin infection.\nIt is also used for prophylaxis against streptococcal\ninfection following rheumatic fever and against\npneumococcal infection following splenectomy or\nsickle cell disease.\n- **B.** Clarithromycin\n- **C.** Cephalexin\n- **D.** Amoxicillin\n- **E.** Phenoxymethylpenicillin\n5. Which of the following is the earliest electrocardiogram\n(EKG) finding in acute myocardial infarction (MI)?\n\n## Question 5 of 90\n\n- **A.** Q waves\n- **B.** ST elevation\n- **C.** ST depression\n- **D.** T-wave inversion\n- **E.** Hyperacute T waves\n\n## Question 57 of 70\n\n6. A 34-year-old male presents to ER with an upper limb\ninjury. He complains of the weakness of flexion of the\nthumb, index, and middle finger of his right hand. Which of\nthe following nerves is most likely affected?\nThe anterior interosseous nerve is the terminal\nmotor branch of the median nerve. It branches from\nthe median nerve in the proximal forearm between\nthe two heads of the pronator teres muscle to run\ndeep along the interosseous membrane. From\nproximal to distal, it innervates the flexor pollicis\n\n## Question 6 of 90\n\n- **A.** Palmer Digital branch\n- **B.** Posterior interosseous nerve\n- **C.** Posterior interosseous nerve\n- **D.** Deep branch of ulnar nerve\n- **E.** Anterior interosseous nerve\n\n## Question 58 of 70\n\nlongus, lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus\n, and the pronator quadratus\nAnterior interosseous nerve syndrome is an isolated\npalsy of these three muscles. It manifests mostly as\npain in the forearm accompanied frequently by a\ncharacteristic weakness of the flexion index and\nthumb finger.\nIn supracondylar fracture, the Most common nerve\ninjured is the median nerve (RECALL)\n10. A 50-year-old male presents to ED with the complaint of\nheadache and chest pain, he is currently taking captopril\nand Hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Thiazide diuretic\nact at which of the following sites in nephron?\nThiazides act mainly on early segment of distal\ntubule where they inhibit the nacl reabsorption by\nbinding to the Na-cl- cotransporter.\nwhile loop diuretic inhibits the Na-k-2CL- symporter\non luminal membrane in the thick ascending limb\nof loop of Henle thus preventing the reabsorption of\nNaCl and water (RECALL)\n\n## Question 10 of 90\n\n- **A.** Late part of distal convoluted tubule\n- **B.** Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **C.** Collecting duct\n- **D.** Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **E.** Early part of distal convoluted tubule\n13. An elderly male patient presents to the ED with acute\nconfusion. Lab value reveals Hyponatremia and\n\n## Question 13 of 90\n\n## Question 59 of 70\n\nhyperkalemia, which one of the following hormones is most\nlikely deficient?\nAldosterone deficit results in hyponatremia and\nhypovolemia, accompanied by hyperkalemia and\nmetabolic acidosis\nAldosterone acts mainly at renal distal convoluted\ntubule to cause sodium retention and potassium\nloss (RECALL)\nConn’s syndrome (hyperaldosteronism) cause\nhypernatremia and hypokalemia (RECALL).\n- **A.** Cortisol\n- **B.** Thyroxine\n- **C.** Growth hormone\n- **D.** ADH\n- **E.** Aldosterone\n20. A 40-year-old male presents to the ED with headache.\nHis BP was found to be 180/90. He is currently under\ninvestigation for suspected Cushing’s syndrome. What is\nthe most common cause of Cushing disease?\n\n## Question 20 of 90\n\n- **A.** Squamous cell lung cancer\n- **B.** Adrenal adenoma\n- **C.** Adrenal carcinoma\n- **D.** Small cell lung cancer\n- **E.** Pituitary adenoma\n\n## Question 60 of 70\n\nCushing’s disease refers to the specific condition of\nexcessive corticosteroids as a result of ACTH\nsecreting pituitary adenoma.\nCushing’s syndrome is the name given to clinical\nsymptoms and signs induced by chronic\nglucocorticoid excess.\nClinical findings:\nBuffalo hump\nPurple striae\nCentral Obesity\nThinning skin that bruises easily\nAcne\nFatigue\nMuscle weakness\nHigh blood sugar\nIncreased thirst\nIncreased urination\nHigh blood pressure (hypertension)\nAnxiety\n22. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with\nexacerbation of asthma. Which one of the following beta\nblockers is safe for asthma?\n\n## Question 22 of 90\n\n- **A.** Propanol\n- **B.** Carvedilol\n- **C.** Nadolol\n- **D.** Labetalol\n- **E.** Bisoprolol\n\n## Question 61 of 70\n\nCardio selective beta blockers like nebivolol,\nbisoprolol and metoprolol are safe in both asthma\nand diabetes\n23. A 38-year-old male brought into the ER having trauma\nto the face, his frontalis muscle is spared but the orbicularis\noculi muscle is affected. Which of the following branch of\nfacial nerve most likely involved?\nWithin the parotid gland, the facial nerve\nterminates by bifurcating into five motor branches.\nThese innervate the muscles of facial expression:\nTemporal: Innervates the frontalis, orbicularis oculi\n(upper half) and corrugator supercilii.\nZygomatic: Innervates the orbicularis oculi. (Lower\nhalf)\nIn this case frontalis muscle is spared which is\ninnervated by temporal branch, so here most likely\naffected nerve is zygomatic branch (lower half of\norbicularis oculi)\nBuccal: Innervates the orbicularis Oris, buccinators\nand zygomaticus.\nMarginal mandibular: Innervates the depressor labii\ninferioris, depressor anguli orris and mentalis.\nCervical : Innervates the platysma (RECALLL).\n\n## Question 23 of 90\n\n- **A.** Buccal Branch\n- **B.** Temporal branch\n- **C.** Cervical Branch\n- **D.** Mental Branch\n- **E.** Zygomatic branch\n\n## Question 62 of 70\n\n25. A 50-year-old man complains of intermittent abdominal\npain on lifting and coughing. You suspect a Spigelian\nhernia. Weakness of which structure is most likely to be\nresponsible?\nSpigelian hernia also known as hernia of Linea\nsemilunaris is a protrusion through a defect in the\naponeurosis of the transversus abdominis muscle.\nClinically, they present as a small tender mass at the\nlower lateral edge of the rectus abdominus*. They\nhave a high risk of strangulation, and so should be\nrepaired urgently.\n\n## Question 25 of 90\n\n- **A.** Transversalis fascia\n- **B.** Internal inguinal ring\n- **C.** Femoral sheath\n- **D.** Iliopsoas fascia\n- **E.** Transversus abdominus aponeurosis\n26. A 30-year-old man presents to the ED with an anterior\nshoulder dislocation while playing, now he is unable to\ninitiate abduction of the arm following reduction of a\ndislocated shoulder. Which one of the following muscles is\nmost likely injured?\n\n## Question 26 of 90\n\n- **A.** Teres major\n- **B.** Teres minor\n- **C.** Infraspinatus\n\n## Question 63 of 70\n\nSupraspinatus is part of the rotator cuff group of\nmuscles which initiate the abduction of shoulder\nfrom 0 to 15 degrees and then assists the deltoid\nwith continued abduction. Supraspinatus muscle\nsupplied by suprascapular nerve, "empty can test"\nuse to examine this muscle.\nTeres minor muscle assessment test=Hornblower’s\ntest) RECALL)\nSubscapularis muscle assessment test (lift-off Test)\nRECALL\nMost commonly torn tendon of rotator cuff is\nsupraspinatus muscle (RECALL)\nTeres minor and deltoid muscle innervated by\nAxillary nerve (RECALL)\nSupraspinatus Is innervated by suprascapular nerve\nSubscapular is innervated by subscapular nerve\n- **D.** Subscapularis\n- **E.** Supraspinatus\n28. A 50-year-old woman presents to the ED with\npalpitations. Her ECG reveals QT prolongation. She has\nbeen taking an antibiotic for a skin infection.\nWhich antibiotic is the most likely to have caused her ECG\nabnormalities?\n\n## Question 28 of 90\n\n- **A.** Flucloxacillin\n- **B.** Clindamycin\n- **C.** Doxycycline\n- **D.** Tetracycline\n\n## Question 64 of 70\n\nDRUGS CAUSING QT PROLONGATION\nRemember with ABCDEF\nANTI-ARRYTHMICS: amiodarone, procainamide,\nquinidine, sotalol\nANTIBIOTICS: macrolides, fluoroquinolones\nANTIPSYCHOTICS: chlorpromazine, haloperidol\nD ANTIDEPRESSANTS: SSRIS (citalopram,\nescitalopram); TCAS (amitriptyline)\nANTI-EMETICS: domperidone, ondansetron\nANTIFUNGALS: azoles (fluconazole), pentamidine\n- **E.** Clarithromycin\n39. You are teaching group of medical students' venous\ndrainage of lower limb, Regarding the course of great\nsaphenous vein, which one of the following is correct?\n\n## Question 39 of 90\n\n- **A.** Begins as the upward continuation of the lateral\nmarginal vein of the foot\n- **B.** Travels with saphenous nerve along its course\n- **C.** Join the femoral vein above the inguinal\nligament\n- **D.** Runs behind the medial malleolus\n- **E.** Enters the femoral vein on its anteromedial side\n\n## Question 65 of 70\n\nThe great saphenous vein: originate from medial\nside of the dorsal venous arch in the foot, passes\nanterior to the medial malleolus (not posterior),\ntravels up the medial side of leg, knee and thigh to\npass in to saphenous opening in deep fascia\ncovering the femoral triangle and join with the\nfemoral vein below the inguinal ligament.\nThe small saphenous vein: originate from lateral\nside of dorsal venous arch in the foot, passes\nposterior to lateral malleolus and up the back of leg\nto pierce the deep fascia and join the popliteal vein\nin popliteal fossa posterior to knee...\nNote: Cutaneous innervation to medial side of foot\nby=saphenous nerve (Recall).\n43. 45 years old male presented to the ED post thyroid\nsurgery with complain of periorbital numbness, paresthesia\nof hand and feet and abnormal movement what is the\ncause of his condition?\nPTH is primarily released in response to decreased\ncalcium. PTH causes demineralization of bones\nand causes increased plasma calcium level\n(Recall) and decreased phosphate levels\nPost-thyroid surgery most likely complication is\nparathyroid insufficiency leads to hypocalcemia\n\n## Question 43 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hypomagnesemia\n- **B.** Hypokalemia\n- **C.** Thyroid Crisis\n- **D.** Hypothyroidism\n- **E.** Hypocalcemia\n\n## Question 66 of 70\n\nNote: Parathyroid Harmone increases calcium\nreabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule of\nnephron (Recall)  and increases excretion of\nphosphate by inhibiting reabsorption in the\nproximal convoluted tubule of nephron\n48. A 54-year-old woman presents with acute back pain\nand complains of numbness around her rectum. Which\ndermatome best describes this area of numbness?\n\n## Question 48 of 90\n\n- **A.** S1\n- **B.** S3\n- **C.** L5\n- **D.** S2\n- **E.** S5\n56. A 30-year-old farmer has sustained a deep laceration to\nthe popliteal fossa and damaged the more medial nerve.\nWhich of the following clinical features you would most\nlikely to see on examination?\n\n## Question 56 of 90\n\n- **A.** Loss of flexion of the leg at the knee joint\n- **B.** Foot drop\n\n## Question 67 of 70\n\nTibial and common fibular nerve are the most\nsuperficial contents of the popliteal fossa. Both are\nthe branches of sciatic nerve\nThe common fibular nerve follows the biceps\nfemoris tendon, travelling along the lateral margin\nof the popliteal\nTibial nerve lies more medial\nBoundaries and contents of popliteal fossa\n- **C.** Weakness of dorsiflexion and eversion\n- **D.** Loss of extension of the leg at the knee joint\n- **E.** Weakness of plantarflexion and inversion\n65. A 32 years old male was brought into the ED by\nparamedics with a complaint of head injury. After initial\nassessment and imaging results empty Sella turcica.\nEmpty Sella turcica occurs due to fracture of which bone?\n\n## Question 65 of 90\n\n- **A.** Frontal\n- **B.** Parietal\n\n## Question 68 of 70\n\nThe Sella turcica is a saddle-shape depression in the\nsphenoid bone of the human skull contains the\npituitary gland and distal portion of the pituitary\nstalk\n- **C.** Occipital\n- **D.** Temporal\n- **E.** Sphenoid\n75. A 58-year-old male presents to ED with shortness of\nbreath caused by acute decompensated heart failure with\npulmonary congestion. which of the following pulmonary\nreceptor is likely to be involved in causing her dyspnea?\nJ-receptors respond to events such as pulmonary\nedema, pulmonary emboli pneumonia, and\nbarotrauma, which cause a decrease in\noxygenation and thus lead to an increase in\nventilation/respiration.\nJ receptors are activated in pulmonary edema in a\npatient with congestive heart failure and causes\nreflex increase in breathing rate (Recall) also\nthought to be involve in sensation of dyspnea\n\n## Question 75 of 90\n\n- **A.** Stretch receptors\n- **B.** Muscle receptors\n- **C.** Chemoreceptors\n- **D.** Irritant receptors\n- **E.** Juxtrapulmonary (J) receptors\n\n## Question 69 of 70\n\n0% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\nNote: Irritant receptors sense chemicals dust and\ncold air and activation causes coughing (Recall)\nand bronchoconstriction.\n81. A 40-year-old male is brought to the ER having\nsustained a stab wound to the posterolateral back. Which\nof the following muscle most likely affected?\nLatissimus dorsi muscle is a broad, flat muscle that\noccupies the majority of the lower posterior thorax.\nThe muscle's primary function is of the upper\nextremity but is also considered to be a respiratory\naccessory muscle\nlatissimus dorsi muscle is innervated by\nthoracodorsal nerve (RECALL)\nlatissimus dorsi produces adduction, medial\nrotation and extension of humerus at\nglenohumeral joint (RECALL).\n\n## Question 81 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anterior scalene muscle\n- **B.** Rhomboid major\n- **C.** Trapezius\n- **D.** Serratus anterior\n- **E.** Latissimus dorsi\n\n## Question 70 of 70	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
41d46148-516a-4069-a7bb-09bf485827a2	ef15ba2e-2dca-4639-9b94-5740ccb572f9	mock-ii-paper-i	Mock 2 — Paper 1 (Clinical SBA Exam)	quiz	# Mock – Ii Paper – I\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer scenario. Read carefully before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\nMrcem Primary Complete Course ,High Yield Stuff and Questions Bank ( For\nDiet ii Late 2026)\nOverview  Comments\nShare your score colleagues\n\nLinkedIn  Facebook  Twitter\n\n## Question 13 of 90\n\n2. A 35-year-old woman presents with fatigue and yellow\nsclera. Blood tests reveal a high bilirubin which is found to\nbe unconjugated bilirubin. What pathology is the most likely\ncause for her jaundice?\n\n## Question 2 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sickle cell disease\n- **B.** Hepatitis\n- **C.** Hepatocellular carcinoma\n- **D.** Gallstone in the bile duct\n- **E.** Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas\n\n## Question 1 of 81\n\n4. A 55 years old male known case of hypertension\npresents to the ED with complaint of tingling and numbness\nin his right index finger. You suspect carpal tunnel\nsyndrome. Which one of the following structures lie within\nthe carpal tunnel?\nThe carpel tunnel is the space that lies between the\nflexor retinaculum. The ulnar nerve, ulnar artery and\ntendon of Palmaris longus runs superficial to flexor\nretinaculum and thus are not within carpel tunnel.\nFlexor pollicis longus enter the carpel tunnel before\nreaching its insertion on the distal phalanx of the\nthumb\nPronator quadratus is confined to the distal forearm\nand does not reach the carpel tunnel.\nNote: Carpal tunnel syndrome is caused by pressure\non the median nerve (RECALL)\n\n## Question 4 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ulnar nerve\n- **B.** Palmaris longus tendon\n- **C.** Flexor pollicis longus tendon\n- **D.** Pronator quadratus\n- **E.** Ulnar artery\n\n## Question 2 of 81\n\n5. Blood brain barrier is the barrier between cerebral\ncapillary blood and the CSF consists of which one of the\nfollowing cells?\nBlood brain barrier is the barrier between cerebral\ncapillary blood and the CSE CSF fills the ventricles\nand the subarachnoid space.\nIt consists of the endothelial cells of the cerebral\ncapillaries and the choroid plexus epithelium.\n\n## Question 5 of 90\n\n- **A.** Endothelial cells\n- **B.** Oligodendrocytes\n- **C.** Astrocytes\n- **D.** Plasma cells\n- **E.** Ependymal cells\n6. In a randomised controlled trial, the following types of\nbiases are reduced by randomisation:\n\n## Question 6 of 90\n\n- **A.** Recall bias\n- **B.** Publication bias\n- **C.** Selection bias\n- **D.** Ascertainment bias\n- **E.** Bias in handling dropouts\n\n## Question 3 of 81\n\nSubjects are randomised in a randomised con -\ntrolled trial to ensure that the groups are iden tical\napart from the intervention. Randomisation means\nthat each subject has a predetermined chance of\nbeing allocated to each group, but the group to be\nallocated can not be predicted. This will minimise\nany selec tion bias.\nAscertainment bias occurs when the results of a\ntrial are systematically distorted by knowledge of\nwhich intervention each partici pant is receiving.\nThis type of bias is minimised by blinding of\nresearchers, clinicians, and patients. Bias caused by\ndropouts occur because characteristics of subjects\nwho left the trial before completion may be\ndiverent from those who do not. This type of bias is\nminimised by appropriate analysis (for exam ple,\nintention to treat analysis, worst case scenario\nanalysis).\nRecall bias occurs when the subjects’ recall of\npast events is distorted by subsequent events.\nThis type of bias occurs mainly in retrospective\nstudies (for example, case control studies Recall)\nrather than randomised controlled trials.\nPublication bias occurs dur ing the dissemination of\nthe results.\n8. A mother brought her 3-year-old child into ED with\ncomplaint of runny nose, severe coughing from the last 2\nweeks and developed subconjunctival hemorrhage. What is\nthe most likely causative agent?\n\n## Question 8 of 90\n\n- **A.** Bordetella Pertussis\n- **B.** Strep group A\n- **C.** Hemophilus influenzae type B\n\n## Question 4 of 81\n\nNote: Hemophilus influenza is also gram-\nnegative Cocco bacilli (Recall)\n- **D.** Parainfluenza virus\n- **E.** RSV\n9. Which vitamin is also known as anti-sterility vitamin?\n\n## Question 9 of 90\n\n- **A.** Vitamin D\n- **B.** Vitamin E\n- **C.** Vitamin C\n\n## Question 5 of 81\n\nvitamin e act as antioxidant prevents the cells from\ndamage caused by free radicals. Sperm DNA\ndamage is an emerging cause of male infertility\nthat is likely to be more common among men with\nincreased reactive oxygen species in the ejaculate.\nVitamin E can protect the sperm membrane from\noxidative damage and increased levels are\nassociated with low levels of reactive oxygen\nspecies.\n- **D.** Vitamin K\n- **E.** Vitamin A\n12. A 2-year-old child brought into the ED by his parents\nwith a history of fever associated with harsh cough, runny\nnose and hoarse of the voice for the last two days. On\nexamination the child has mild tachycardia, tachypnoea\nand his SpO2 is 98%. Which of the following is the most\nlikely infectious cause of his condition?\n\n## Question 12 of 90\n\n- **A.** Glandular fever\n- **B.** Strep group A\n- **C.** Hemophilus influenzae type B\n- **D.** Parainfluenza\n- **E.** RSV\n\n## Question 6 of 81\n\n13. Baby defecate after eating due to which one of the\nfollowing physiological reflex?\n\n## Question 13 of 90\n\n- **A.** Entero-colic reflex\n- **B.** Vagal reflex\n- **C.** Gastro-colic reflex\n- **D.** Oesophageo-gastric reflex\n- **E.** Oesophageo-colic reflex\n\n## Question 7 of 81\n\nThe gastrocolic reflex is a physiological reflex that\ncontrols the motility of the lower gastrointestinal\ntract following a meal. As a result of the gastrocolic\nreflex, the colon has increased motility in response\nto the stretch of the stomach with the ingestion of\nfood.\n14. Which one of the following pathogens causes\nophthalmia neonatorum occurring in the 1  28 days of life?\nOphthalmia neonatorum is conjunctivitis occurring\nwithin the first 4 weeks of life.\nThe most serious cause is infection with Neisseria\ngonorrhea in first week most likely in 3-4 days of\nlife, but in the UK, it is caused more commonly by\nChlamydia in first 28 days of life.\n\n## Question 14 of 90\n\nst\n- **A.** Treponema pallidum\n- **B.** Trichomonas vaginalis\n- **C.** Chlamydia trachomatis\n- **D.** Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- **E.** Gardnerella vaginalis\n15. Type-III hypersensitivity is triggered by?\n\n## Question 15 of 90\n\n- **A.** T-cells\n- **B.** Deposition of antigen antibody complexes\n\n## Question 8 of 81\n\nMechanism of hypersensitivity reactions:\nType I reactions (i.e., immediate hypersensitivity\nreactions) involve immunoglobulin E (IgE)–\nmediated release of histamine and other mediators\nfrom mast cells and basophils. (RECALL)\nType II reactions (i.e., cytotoxic hypersensitivity\nreactions) involve immunoglobulin G or\nimmunoglobulin M antibodies bound to cell\nsurface antigens, with subsequent complement\nfixation.\nType III reactions (i.e., Immune-complex reactions)\ninvolve circulating antigen-antibody immune\ncomplexes that deposit in post capillary venules,\nwith subsequent complement fixation. An example\nis serum sickness.\nType IV reactions (i.e., delayed hypersensitivity\nreactions, cell-mediated immunity) are mediated\nby T cells rather than by antibodies (RECALL).\n- **C.** Nk cells and IgG\n- **D.** Mast cells and IgE\n- **E.** IgM\n16. How does parathyroid (PTH) affect renal handling of\ncalcium?\n\n## Question 16 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increase calcium reabsorption of proximal\ntubule\n- **B.** Increase reabsorption of calcium at glomerulus\n\n## Question 9 of 81\n\nPTH increases calcium reabsorption exclusively in\nthe distal nephron.\n25-30% of dietary Calcium is absorbed from gut, out\nof this % of calcium, 50% is ionized (means free or\nunbound in plasma) and 45% is bound to albumin.\nRemaining 5% with other ions\nOnly free ionized Calcium is filtered freely in\nglomerulus:\n70% absorbed in PCT (RECALL)\n20% in thick ascending loop of Henle\n9% in DCT\n1% in Collecting duct\n- **C.** Decrease calcium reabsorption in the distal\ntubule\n- **D.** Increase calcium reabsorption in the distal\ntubule\n- **E.** Decrease calcium reabsorption of proximal\ntubule\n17. A 23-year-old male presents to the ED with a lacerated\nwound on his wrist. You ask junior fellows wrist joint is an\nexample of ?\n\n## Question 17 of 90\n\n- **A.** Synovial plane joint\n- **B.** Synovial ball and socket joint\n- **C.** Synovial condyloid joint\n- **D.** Synovial pivot joint\n\n## Question 10 of 81\n\nWrist joint: Synovial condyloid joint\nSternoclavicular joint: Synovial saddle-shaped\njoint\nAcromioclavicular joint: Synovial plane joint\nShoulder joint: Synovial ball and socket joint\nElbow joint: Synovial hinge joint\nRadioulnar joints: Synovial pivot joint\nThe joint between atlas and axis is Pivot joint\n- **E.** Synovial hinge joint\n18. Damage to Wernicke's area will most likely result in\nwhich of the following clinical features?\nBroca's aphasia: This is a non-fluent aphasia which\ncauses individuals to speak slower. It occurs when\nthe brain’s frontal lobe suffers damage. Individuals\nwith Broca’s Aphasia often feel frustrated due to\nbeing able to comprehend what others are saying,\nbut unable to express themselves. also called\nExpressive dysphasia. Individuals affected only can\nspeak in very short phrases, or have trouble getting\nwords out. (RECALL)\nWernicke’s aphasia: This is a fluent aphasia as this\ntype of aphasia does not affect the speed of the\nindividual's speech. Individuals with this type of\n\n## Question 18 of 90\n\n- **A.** Expressive dysphasia\n- **B.** Nystagmus\n- **C.** Receptive dysphasia\n- **D.** Visual loss\n- **E.** Dysarthria\n\n## Question 11 of 81\n\naphasia have damage to their brain’s temporal lobe.\nAs a result, those of Wernicke’s aphasia are\nsometimes unable to comprehend what others are\nsaying, but are able to speak in long sentences.\nHowever, their communication doesn't follow\nstandard grammar patterns and uses made-up\nwords. also called receptive dysphasia.\n19. An 8-year-old child brought into the ED with a complaint\nof severe vomiting. ABGs revealed metabolic alkalosis.\nMetabolic alkalosis occurs after vomiting due to?\nMetabolic alkalosis: Occurs due to loss of H+ in GI\ntract i.e., vomiting, pyloric stenosis or in kidney\n(diuretic therapy)\nMetabolic alkalosis is associated with\nhypoventilation and hypokalemia (Recall).\n\n## Question 19 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decreased pco2\n- **B.** Increase H+\n- **C.** Increased pco2\n- **D.** Decrease H+\n- **E.** Decrease HC03\n20. An 87-year-old woman from a residential home with a\n2-day history of diarrhea. She has had 10 episodes of\nwatery diarrhea but no vomiting. She complains of colicky\nlower abdominal pain. She had a 5-day course of\nantibiotics for a chest infection 2 weeks ago. Observations\n\n## Question 20 of 90\n\n## Question 12 of 81\n\nshow a respiratory rate 18 bpm, Sp02 95% in air, heart rate\n89 bpm,\nBP 146/89 mmHg, temp 36.8'C and BM 5.6 Examination\nreveals that her abdomen is soft but there is generalized\ntenderness especially across her lower abdomen. What is\nthe most likely causative organism?\nBroad spectrum antibiotics disturb the normal gut\nflora and cause overgrowth of clostridium difficile\nspores and causing Pseudomembranous colitis\n- **A.** Clostridium difficile\n- **B.** Rotavirus\n- **C.** E. coli\n- **D.** Norovirus\n- **E.** Campylobacter\n\n## Question 13 of 81\n\nAntibiotics causing clostridium difficile colitis: Co-\nAmoxiclav, Cephalosporins, Clindamycin and\nquinolones\nTreatment of choice of clostridium difficile colitis\nis Vancomycin (Recall)\n21. A 22-year-old woman involved in a road traffic accident\nsustains a fractured pelvis. You suspect an injury to sciatic\nnerve.\nWhich joint movements are most likely to be affected?\n\n## Question 21 of 90\n\n## Question 14 of 81\n\nSciatic nerve Innervates the muscles of the posterior\ncompartment of thigh (biceps femoris,\nsemimembranosus and semitendinosus) and the\nhamstring portion of the adductor magnus\n(remaining portion of which is supplied by the\nobturator nerve).\nSciatic nerve extend thigh at hip joint and flex leg at\nknee joint.\n- **A.** Hip adduction and rotation\n- **B.** Hip adduction and extension\n- **C.** Hip rotation and knee flexion\n- **D.** Extend thigh at hip and knee flexion\n- **E.** Hip adduction and flexion\n22. A 72-year-old woman with a history of progressively\nworsening dysphagia is discovered to have an\noesophageal tumor. The tumor is located on the anterior\naspect of the thoracic oesophagus. Which of the following\nis an anterior relation of the thoracic oesophagus?\n\n## Question 22 of 90\n\n- **A.** Azygous veins\n- **B.** Subclavian artery\n- **C.** Left recurrent laryngeal nerve\n- **D.** Right vagus nerve\n- **E.** Lesser sac of peritoneum\n\n## Question 15 of 81\n\n·Note: Larynx:  Innervation to the\nintrinsic muscles of the larynx is\nachieved via the recurrent laryngeal\nnerve. Injury of recurrent laryngeal\nnerve may result in hoarseness\n(Recall), respiratory obstruction and\ninability to speak\nsuperior laryngeal nerve provides both motor and\nsensory function to different areas of the throat and\nmouth that are primarily involved in speech\n(Recall) and keeping food and drink out of your\nairway.\nThe superior laryngeal nerve (SLN) provides motor\ninnervation to the cricothyroid muscle.\n23. A 16-year-old young male brought into the ED with\ncomplaint of dizziness, fatigue and edema on his face and\nfeet. After initial assessment you suspect Nephrotic\nsyndrome, regarding nephrotic syndrome what is the cause\nof edema?\n\n## Question 23 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hyperalbuminemia\n- **B.** Hypoalbuminemia\n- **C.** Increased intravascular oncotic pressure\n- **D.** Decreased extravascular oncotic pressure\n- **E.** Increased salt excretion\n\n## Question 16 of 81\n\nNephrotic syndrome leads to urinary loss of\nalbumin (Hypoalbuminemia) which reduces\nintravascular oncotic pressure and fluid shift to\nextracellular compartment.\n24. A 22-year-old male presents with palpitations. ECG was\ndone at triage which shows S.V.T. You are considering\nadenosine, in which of the following conditions adenosine\nis contraindicated?\nAdenosine is treatment of choice in terminating\nsupraventricular tachycardia\nContraindications of adenosine include:\nAsthma and COPD\nDecompensated heart failure\nLong QT syndrome\nSecond- and third-degree heart block\nSevere hypotension\n\n## Question 24 of 90\n\n- **A.** Diabetes mellitus\n- **B.** Blood dyscariasis\n- **C.** Myasthenia gravis\n- **D.** Asthma\n- **E.** Peripheral vascular disease\n25. Taste sensation from anterior 2/3 of tongue is carried\nby?\n\n## Question 25 of 90\n\n- **A.** Facial nerve\n\n## Question 17 of 81\n\nThe chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve\nsupplies taste sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the\ntongue (RECALL).\nIn the anterior 2/3, general sensation is supplied by\nthe trigeminal nerve (CNV). Specifically, the lingual\nnerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve (CN V3).\n(RECALL)\nThe posterior 1/3 of the tongue both touch and\ntaste are carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve\n(CNIX) (RECALL).\n- **B.** Hypoglossal nerve\n- **C.** Glossopharyngeal\n- **D.** Trigeminal nerve\n- **E.** Vagus nerve\n26. A 40-Year-old male who is known hypertensive taking\nACE inhibitors presents with dry cough. What is the\nmechanism of cough in ACE inhibitor?\nACE inhibitor decreases the breakdown of the\npotent vasodilator bradykinin which is the cause of\nthe persistent dry cough.\n\n## Question 26 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decreased bradykinin breakdown\n- **B.** Increased production of prostaglandin\n- **C.** Histamine release\n- **D.** Increased bradykinin breakdown\n- **E.** Stimulation of irritant receptors\n\n## Question 18 of 81\n\nSide effects of ACE inhibitor includes:\nAcute kidney injury\nHyperkalemia\nNon allergic angioedema (Recall)\nPersistent dry cough (Recall).\n27. Which of the following shift oxygen hemoglobin\ndissociation curve to left?\nLeftward shift: Increased affinity for oxygen (Less\nwilling to give up oxygen)\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up\noxygen)\nIncreased pH\nDecreased pCO2\nDecreased temperature\nDecreased H+(RECALL)\nDecreased 2-3DPG\nCO (RECALLL)\nMethemoglobinemia\nFetal hemoglobin (RECALL)\nRightward shift: Decreased affinity for oxygen\n(More willing to give up oxygen)\n\n## Question 27 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increase temperature\n- **B.** Increased Pco2\n- **C.** CO Poisoning\n- **D.** Decreased PH\n- **E.** Increased 2-3DPG\n\n## Question 19 of 81\n\nDecreased ph (RECALL)\nIncreased pCO2\nIncreased H+\nIncreased Temperature\nIncreased 2-3DPG\n28. A 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency\ndepartment with painful swollen digit, the infection appears\nto have spread to the common flexor sheath. Which of the\nfollowing digit is most likely to be involved with spread of\ninfection to this location?\nThe synovial sheath of the little finger is usually\ncontinued with the common flexor sheath which is\nalso called ulnar bursa thus infection of ulnar bursa\nmost likely to involve the little finger.\nIn a same way infection of thumb may spread to\nmid palmer space through the continuous synovial\nsheath of the flexor pollicis longus which is also\ncalled radial bursa.\n\n## Question 28 of 90\n\n- **A.** Thumb\n- **B.** Little finger\n- **C.** Index finger\n- **D.** Middle finger\n- **E.** Ring finger\n30. A 25-year-old man attends the ED with abdominal pain.\nHe has a history of Crohn's disease and has had a\nprevious resection of his terminal ileum.\nWhich of the following is most likely to be deficient due to\nthe absence of the terminal ileum?\n\n## Question 30 of 90\n\n## Question 20 of 81\n\nMaximal water reabsorption occurs in Jejunum\n(Recall)\nMaximal Iron absorption occurs in duodenum\n(Recall)\nMaximal calcium absorption occurs in duodenum\n(Recall)\nMaximal vit B12 and vit D absorption occurs in\nterminal ileum\n- **A.** Vitamin C\n- **B.** Vitamin D\n- **C.** Dietary carbohydrate\n- **D.** Zinc\n- **E.** Dietary protein\n31. A 55-year-old male known case of COPD came with a\ncomplaint of breathlessness. His observations include\nBP=100/70 Pulse=102bpm and Sa02 is 78%. You keep the\npatient on 02, what is the targeted 02 saturation prior to\ndischarge?\n\n## Question 31 of 90\n\n- **A.** 88 – 92%\n- **B.** Unless SaO2 becomes 100%\n- **C.** Unless SaO2 becomes 98%\n- **D.** Unless SaO2 becomes 94%\n- **E.** 80-84%\n\n## Question 21 of 81\n\nCOPD and conditions associated with chronic\nrespiratory failure. In the treatment of\nexacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary\ndisease (COPD), oxygen should be titrated to\nachieve a target oxygen saturation range of 88–\n92%.\n32. Whilst in your physiology lecture you ask medical\nstudents what is the physiological purpose of AV nodal\ndelay?\nSignificance of AV nodal delay\nThe cardiac impulse does not travel from the atria to\nthe ventricles too rapidly.\nThis delay allows time for the atria to empty their\nblood into the ventricles before ventricular\ncontraction begins. This increases the efficiency of\nthe pumping action of the heart.\nIt is primarily the AV node and its adjacent fibers\nthat delay this transmission into the ventricles.\n\n## Question 32 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increase time for ventricular filling\n- **B.** Parasympathetic activity decreases AV nodal\ndelay\n- **C.** Increase time for filling of atria\n- **D.** Ejection fraction time\n- **E.** Sympathetic activity causes increase in AV nodal\ndelay\n33. A 44-year-old woman presents with a two-month history\nof nausea, weight loss, and diarrhea. She returned from\n\n## Question 33 of 90\n\n## Question 22 of 81\n\nworking in Nigeria five months ago. Do you suspect\ntapeworm infestation\nWhat diagnostic method is most likely to confirm acute\nInfestation?\nStool: parasites Direct light microscopy\nSputum: TB Ziehl-Nelsen stain\n- **A.** Serum antibodies\n- **B.** Visualization of eggs in stool on light microscopy\n- **C.** Eosinophil count\n- **D.** Abdominal CT\n- **E.** Colonoscopy\n34. A 19-year-old man presents with an open tibial fracture\ncontaminated with soil. His immunizations are up to date.\nWith regard to tetanus prophylaxis what is the most\nappropriate management?\n\n## Question 34 of 90\n\n- **A.** No further tetanus prophylaxis\n- **B.** Administration of tetanus immunoglobulin\n\n## Question 23 of 81\n\nDiphtheria, tetanus, polio vaccine schedule:  2, 3, 4\nmonths, preschool and 14 years\nChild's immunization status is up to date and his\nlast vaccination schedule was in 14 years. Less than\n10 years are passed\n- **C.** Administration of tetanus immunoglobulin and\nvaccination\n- **D.** Administration of tetanus vaccination\n- **E.** Administration of tetanus vaccination now and\nin one month\n35. Repeated stimulation of a skeletal muscle fiber causes\na sustained contraction (tetanus). Accumulation of which\nsolute in intracellular fluid is responsible for the tetanus?\n\n## Question 35 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sodium\n- **B.** Calmodulin\n\n## Question 24 of 81\n\nDuring repeated stimulation of a muscle fiber,\nCalcium is released from the sarcoplasmic\nreticulum (SR) more quickly than it can be\nreaccumulated; therefore, the intracellular [Ca2+]\ndoes not return to resting levels as it would after a\nsingle twitch. The increased [Ca2+] allows more\ncross-bridges to form and, therefore, produces\nincreased tension (tetanus)\nNote: In skeletal muscle, Ca² binds to troponin C\n(Recall) initiating the cross-bridge cycle. In\nsmooth muscle, Ca2+ binds to calmodulin.\nHigh Yield:\nMuscle filaments:\nThick filaments: Present in the A band in the\ncenter of the sarcomere\nContains Myosin (Recall)\nThin filaments: Present in the I bands\nAnchored at the Z lines\nContains actin, tropomyosin and troponin\n- **C.** Calcium\n- **D.** Potassium\n- **E.** Troponin\n36. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) acts primarily at which of\nthe following sites in renal nephron?\n\n## Question 36 of 90\n\n- **A.** Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **B.** Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n\n## Question 25 of 81\n\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as\nvasopressin, is a small peptide hormone which\nregulates the body’s retention of water. The main\naction of ADH in the kidney is to regulate the\nvolume and osmolarity of the urine. Specifically, it\nacts in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and\ncollecting ducts (CD).\nADH synthesized by hypothalamus and secreted\nfrom Posterior pituitary (RECALL)\nADH binds to V2 receptors on renal principal cells\nof late distal tubules and collecting ducts\n(RECALL)\nADH also binds with V1 receptors on vascular\nsmooth muscles.\nCentral diabetes insipidus result from deficiency\nADH secretion (RECALL)\nNephrogenic diabetes insipidus result from\ninappropriate renal response to ADH\n- **C.** Descending limb of loop of Henle\n- **D.** Distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts\n- **E.** Proximal tubule\n37. A woman sustained a human bite from someone known\nto have hepatitis B two hours ago. She has not had any\nprevious hepatitis B vaccinations\nRegarding prophylaxis against hepatitis B, what is the most\nappropriate schedule to follow?\n\n## Question 37 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately and\nHepatitis B vaccine three doses over three months\n\n## Question 26 of 81\n\nEmergency hepatitis B vaccination\nIf you have been exposed to the hepatitis B virus\nand have not been vaccinated before, you should\nget immediate medical advice, as you may benefit\nfrom having the hepatitis B vaccine.\nIn some situations, you may also need to have an\ninjection of antibodies, called specific hepatitis B\nimmunoglobulin (HBIG), along with the hepatitis B\nvaccine.\nHBIG should ideally be given within 48 hours post\nexposure Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three\nmonths\n- **B.** Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over six months\n- **C.** Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three\nmonths\n- **D.** Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately and\nHepatitis 8 vaccine three doses over six months\n- **E.** Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately\n39. A 55-year-old patient presents to the emergency\ndepartment with a persistent cough and hemoptysis. on x-\nray you note hilar lymphadenopathy which of the following\nlymph nodes are most likely enlarged?\n\n## Question 39 of 90\n\n- **A.** Inferior tracheobronchial lymph node\n- **B.** Bronchopulmonary lymph node\n- **C.** Anterior mediastinal lymph node\n\n## Question 27 of 81\n\nThe Bronchopulmonary lymph node most\nfrequently involved in pathologies of lung.\n- **D.** Cervical lymph node\n- **E.** Superior tracheobronchial lymph node\n40. Regarding prophylaxis of malaria which one of the\nfollowing antibiotics is used?\nDoxycycline is used as prophylaxis for malaria who\ntravel to countries with extensive resistance to\nChloroquine.\n\n## Question 40 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rifampicin\n- **B.** Clarithromycin\n- **C.** Doxycycline\n- **D.** Vancomycin\n- **E.** Clindamycin\n41. Regarding the power of study which one of the\nfollowing statements is correct?\n\n## Question 41 of 90\n\n- **A.** Power increases with sample size\n- **B.** Power decreases with sample size\n- **C.** Power increases as the variability increases\n\n## Question 28 of 81\n\nThe power of a study is the probability of (correctly)\nrejecting the null hypothesis when it is false, i.e. the\nprobability of detecting a statistically significant\ndifference\npower can be increased by increasing the sample\nsize\nPower increases as the variability decrease\nNote: Increasing sample size can decrease bias\n/error. (Recall)\n- **D.** Power decreases as the variability decrease\n- **E.** Power does not change with sample size\n42. A 26-year-old woman attends complaining of lower\nabdominal pain. She has a positive pregnancy test despite\ntaking the contraceptive pill. She has epilepsy and has\nrecently had her medication changed. Which antiepileptic is\nmost likely to have had an effect on her oral contraceptive?\n\n## Question 42 of 90\n\n- **A.** Lamotrigine\n- **B.** Carbamazepine\n- **C.** Sodium valproate\n- **D.** Levetiracetam\n- **E.** Clobazam\n\n## Question 29 of 81\n\nNote: In pregnancy on contraceptive pills, enzyme\ninducer drugs will increase hepatic clearance of\ncontraceptive pills and pregnancy can still\nhappen\nEnzyme inhibitors raise plasma theophylline\nlevels\nEnzyme inducers decrease plasma theophylline\nlevel\n43. Regarding the sole of foot, which one of the following\nmuscles is a part of 1st layer of foot?\nMuscles of sole of foot. (4 layers)\n\n## Question 43 of 90\n\n- **A.** Flexor hallucis Brevis\n- **B.** Lumbricals\n- **C.** Interosseous muscles\n- **D.** Abductor hallucis\n- **E.** Adductor hallucis\n\n## Question 30 of 81\n\nFirst layer: abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis\n& abductor digitorum minimi\nSecond layer: flexor digitorum accessorius, 4\nlumbricals and tendons of flexor hallucis longus and\nflexor digitorum longus\nThird layer: flexor hallucis brevis, flexor digitorum\nminimi brevis & adductor hallucis\nFourth layer: Plantar and dorsal interossei muscles,\nTwo long tendons of extrinsic muscles - tibialis\nposterior and peroneus longus.\n44. Regarding respiratory muscles, which one of the\nfollowing muscles is a forced muscle of expiration?\nMuscles of forced expiration include:\nAnterior abdominal muscles (rectus abdominis is\nthe main muscle)\ninternal Intercostal muscles\nExternal intercostal muscles are the muscles of\ninspiration.\n\n## Question 44 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sternocleidomastoid\n- **B.** Scalenus anterior\n- **C.** Rectus abdominis\n- **D.** External Intercostal\n- **E.** Scalenus Medius\n45. A 34-year-old man presents to the ED complaining of a\ngreen urethral discharge and dysuria. He reports\nunprotected sexual intercourse with a new partner 3 days\nago. What is the most likely causative organism?\n\n## Question 45 of 90\n\n## Question 31 of 81\n\nNeisseria\ngonorrhoeae\nGram –Ve diplococci transmitted\nthrough Sexual and peripartum\ncausing Urethritis, epididymo-\norchitis, PID, septic arthritis,\nneonatal conjunctivitis (ophthalmia\nneonatorum)\nNote: Urethritis in 1st week (3-4\ndays) post exposure and\nunprotected sex while Chlamydia\ntrachomatis causes urethritis\nafter 2-3 weeks post exposure\nand unprotected sex\n- **A.** Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- **B.** Treponema pallidum\n- **C.** Gardnerella vaginalis\n- **D.** Chlamydia trachomatis\n- **E.** Trichomonas vaginalis\n47. Regarding central respiratory chemoreceptors, which of\nthe following statement is correct?\n\n## Question 47 of 90\n\n- **A.** Located in the medulla near exit of cranial nerve\nX,XI\n- **B.** Respond directly to changes in external PCO2\n- **C.** Respond directly to changes in arterial PCO2\n\n## Question 32 of 81\n\nCentral chemoreceptors are sensitive to the PH of\nCSF, decrease in the pH of the CSF produce\nincrease in breathing rate(hyperventilation). H+\ndoesn't Cross the blood brain barrier as well as co2\ndoes. co2 diffuse from arterial blood into the CSF\nbecause co2 is lipid soluble and readily cross blood\nbrain barrier. in the CSF, co2 combines with ho2 to\nproduce H+ and HCO3-. the resulting H+ act directly\non the central chemoreceptors, thus increase in\npco2 and H+ stimulate the breathing and decrease\nin pco2 and H+ inhibit the breathing. this resulting\nhyperventilation or hypoventilation then returns\nthe arterial Pco2 toward normal.\nCentral chemoreceptors are located near the\nventrolateral surface of the medulla.\nPeripheral chemoreceptors are located in carotid\nand aortic body. The carotid bodies are located at\nthe bifurcation of common carotid arteries, the\naortic bodies are located above and below the aortic\narch.\nDecrease in arterial po2: stimulate the peripheral\nchemoreceptor and increase breathing rate. po2\nmust decrease to low levels (<60mmHg) before\nbreathing is stimulated. when po2 is less than\n60mmHg, breathing rate is exquisitely sensitive to\npo2.\nincrease in arterial Pco2: stimulate the peripheral\nchemoreceptors and increase breathing rate\nincrease in arterial H+: stimulate the carotid body\nperipheral chemoreceptors directly, independent of\nChanges in pco2.\nIn metabolic acidosis, breathing rate is increased\n(hyperventilation) because arterial H+ is increased\nand pH is decreased.\n- **D.** Respond to pH of CSF\n- **E.** Located in dorsal surface of medulla\n\n## Question 33 of 81\n\n48. A 10 years old child brought into the ED with complaint\nof tiredness, fever and joint pain. His mother states that he\nhad acute sore throat 10 days back. You suspect acute\nrheumatic fever secondary to untreated sore throat.\nRheumatic fever is immunological sequalae of which of the\nfollowing pathogens?\nStreptococcus\nPyogenes\nStreptococcus Pyogenes is\ngram +Ve Cocci in chains\nBeta hemolytic group A\nCauses acute sore throat in\nchildren (Recall), skin and soft\ntissue infection and\nnecrotizing fasciitis\nErysipelas: Superficial skin\ninfection involving upper\ndermis and superficial\nlymphatics caused by Group\nA beta hemolytic\nstreptococcus pyogenes\n(Recall)\nPost-infectious immune-\nmediated reactions of\nStreptococcus Pyogenes\n\n## Question 48 of 90\n\n- **A.** Streptococcus pyogenes\n- **B.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **C.** Staph viridians\n- **D.** Staph epidermidis\n- **E.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n## Question 34 of 81\n\ninclude Rheumatic fever,\nerythema nodosum, acute\nglomerulonephritis\n50. Carbonic anhydrase enzyme is present in which one of\nthe following parts of nephron?\nCarbonic anhydrase pre-dominant location is\nepithelial cells of the Proximal convoluted tubule\nCarbonic anhydrase catalyzes formation of Hco3-\nand H+ from H20 and Co2\n\n## Question 50 of 90\n\n- **A.** The descending loop of Henle\n- **B.** The ascending loop of Henle\n- **C.** The collecting tubule and duct\n- **D.** The proximal convoluted tubules\n- **E.** The distal convoluted tubules\n51. During examination of cardiovascular system you are\ndiscussing heart sounds and cardiac cycle. which of the\nfollowing stage of cardiac cycle occurs immediately after\nthe aortic valve closes?\n\n## Question 51 of 90\n\n- **A.** Atrial systole\n- **B.** Isovolumetric contraction\n\n## Question 35 of 81\n\nIsovolumetric Relaxation\nWhen the aortic and pulmonary valves close, the\nheart relaxes isovolumetrically because both the\noutflow valves and inflow valves are closed, and so\nno fluid moves across them. This phase lasts until\nthe intraventricular pressure falls below the\npressure in the atria, at which time the mitral and\ntricuspid valves open again. The isovolumetric\nrelaxation lasts about 0.08 S. The volume\nthroughout this phase is the end systolic volume,\napproximately 50 ml. The atrioventricular (AV)\nvalves open at an atrial pressure of about 7 mmHg.\nIsovolumetric relaxation:\nlasts about 0.08 seconds\nAortic and pulmonary valves closed\nMitral and tricuspid valves closed.\n- **C.** Isovolumetric relaxation\n- **D.** Ventricular ejection\n- **E.** Ventricular filling\n52. A 42-year-old male with history of assault presents to\nthe ED with complaint of incised wound on the neck. Which\none of the following arteries is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 52 of 90\n\n- **A.** Facial artery\n- **B.** Vertebral artery\n- **C.** Brachial artery\n- **D.** Internal carotid artery\n\n## Question 36 of 81\n\nNeck injury may result in the laceration of major\nvessels, potentially leading to hemorrhagic shock.\nInternal carotid artery is most likely injured.\n- **E.** Basillar artery\n53. A 55-year-old female with history of fall brought into the\nED with complain of Severe right hip pain. Xray reveals\nposterior hip dislocation, which one of the following\nligaments is torn in posterior dislocation of hip?\nIntracapsular\nThe only intracapsular ligament is the ligament of\nhead of femur. It is a relatively small structure,\nwhich runs from the acetabular fossa to the fovea of\nthe femur.\nExtracapsular ligaments:\nIliofemoral ligament: Strongest of all located\nanteriorly (Recall)\nIschiofemoral:  Reinforcing the capsule posteriorly.\nPubofemoral:  Reinforcing the capsule anteriorly\nand inferiorly\n\n## Question 53 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ischiofemoral\n- **B.** Ligament of head of femur\n- **C.** Pubofemoral\n- **D.** All the extracapsular ligaments\n- **E.** Iliofemoral\n\n## Question 37 of 81\n\nNote: Most common hip dislocation is posterior and\nsciatic nerve is most commonly injured (Recall).\n55. An eight-year-old girl presents to the ED with impetigo\naround her mouth and nose.\nWhat is the most likely method of spread?\nImpetigo is highly contagious skin infection caused\nby staph aureus\nNote: Fusidic acid is  used for staphylococcus skin\ninfection (impetigo) Recall\n\n## Question 55 of 90\n\n- **A.** Droplet spread\n- **B.** Invasion of breached skin\n- **C.** Via fecal oral route\n- **D.** Hematogenous spread\n- **E.** Invasion of intact skin\n\n## Question 38 of 81\n\nNote: Malaria is blood borne pathogen( Recall)\nintroduced via the bite of a vector\n56. A 40-year-old man presents to the emergency\ndepartment intoxicated with an abdominal stab wound,\nsituated just above the umbilicus in the midline. Small\nbowel has herniated through the anterior abdominal wall\nthrough the hole created by the knife\nThe most superficial structure damaged in stabbing?\nCamper's fascia is the most superficial fatty layer\nof the anterior abdominal wall.\n\n## Question 56 of 90\n\n- **A.** Aponeurosis of internal oblique\n- **B.** Rectus abdominis muscle\n- **C.** Transversalis fascia\n- **D.** Camper’s fascia\n- **E.** Scarpa’s fascia\n57. 22 years old young female came into ED with complain\nof sudden onset of lower limb weakness. She has history of\nSore throat 10 days ago. On Examination her reflexes are\nabsent in his lower limbs. What is the most likely causative\norganism?\n\n## Question 57 of 90\n\n- **A.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **B.** Clostridium perfringens\n\n## Question 39 of 81\n\nGBS cases seem to occur after a viral or bacterial\ninfection like those that cause sore throats or\ndiarrhea characterized by lower limb weakness,\nparesthesia and hyporeflexia\n- **C.** Campylobacter jejuni\n- **D.** Salmonella\n- **E.** Clostridium difficile\n58. A 33-year-old woman presents with severe headache\nand double vision. She has had severe facial pain and\npurulent nasal discharge for the past week. CT shows\ncavernous sinus thrombosis. Which one of the following\nveins is source of infection of cavernous sinus?\nCavernous sinus thrombosis (CST) refers to the\nformation of a clot within the cavernous sinus. It is\nimportant to note that the superior ophthalmic\nvein forms an anastomosis with the facial vein.\nTherefore, the ophthalmic veins represent a\npotential route by which infection can spread from\nan extracranial to an intracranial site.\n\n## Question 58 of 90\n\n- **A.** Facial vein\n- **B.** Middle cerebral vein\n- **C.** Azygous vein\n- **D.** Ophthalmic vein\n- **E.** Central vein of retina\n\n## Question 40 of 81\n\nNote: The abducens nerve (CN VI) is most\ncommonly affected in cavernous sinus\nthrombosis\nStructures passing through cavernous sinus\nuseful mnemonic: O TOM CAT\nOTOM (oculomotor nerve, trochlear nerve,\nophthalmic branch, maxillary branch) refers to\nthe lateral wall contents from superior to inferior\nCAT (internal carotid artery, abducens nerve,\ntrochlear nerve) refers to the horizontal contents,\nfrom medial to lateral.\n59. Which factors contribute to the Bohr Effect?\nThe Bohr effect describes hemoglobin's affinity for\noxygen as a function of blood pH and carbon\ndioxide content. An increase in CO2 concentration\nwill lower the blood pH, causing the hemoglobin\naffinity for oxygen to reduce. High temperature also\ncauses oxygen to be released from hemoglobin but\nit's not related to Bohr's effect.\n\n## Question 59 of 90\n\n- **A.** Low pH, high CO2, low temperature\n- **B.** High pH, low CO2\n- **C.** Low pH, low CO2\n- **D.** Low pH, high CO2\n- **E.** Low pH, high CO2, high temperature\n\n## Question 41 of 81\n\n60. A 70-year-old man who is a chronic smoker presents\nwith shortness of breath. An arterial blood sample shows a\nraised PCO2. Which finding would best indicate this is a\nlong-standing increase in arterial PCO2?\nRespiratory acidosis: Compensated by Increased\nrenal bicarbonate formation and resulting\nmetabolic alkalosis\nMetabolic acidosis: Compensated by decreased\nPco2 formation through hyperventilating and\nresulting in respiratory alkalosis.\n\n## Question 60 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increased renal bicarbonate formation\n- **B.** Decreased plasma potassium concentration\n- **C.** Decreased urinary ammonium salts\n- **D.** Increased renal potassium excretion\n- **E.** Increased urinary bicarbonate excretion\n61. Berry aneurysms are the most common type of\nintracranial aneurysm arising from which one of the\nfollowing arteries?\n\n## Question 61 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior communicating artery\n- **B.** Anterior Cerebral artery\n- **C.** Middle cerebral artery\n- **D.** Anterior communicating artery\n\n## Question 42 of 81\n\nAnterior communicating artery is most common\nsite of berry aneurysm. Second most common site is\nposterior communicating.\nAneurysm of anterior communicating artery\ncompress the optic chiasma and cause bitemporal\nhemianopia, aneurysm of posterior\ncommunicating artery may cause 3rd nerve palsy.\n(RECALL).\n- **E.** Posterior cerebral artery\n62. A 25-year-old woman presents with shortness of breath\nand right-sided chest pain. You suspect pulmonary\nembolism.\nWhich parameter is most likely to be decreased?\nIn PE, pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR)\nincreases due to the mechanical obstruction of the\nvascular bed with thrombus and hypoxic\nvasoconstriction. Pulmonary artery pressure (PAP)\nincreases if thromboemboli occludes greater than\n30% to 50% of the total cross-sectional area of the\npulmonary arterial bed.\nIncreased PVR increases the right ventricular\nafterload, which impedes right ventricular outflow,\n\n## Question 62 of 90\n\n- **A.** Po2 in pulmonary venous blood\n- **B.** Right atrial pressure\n- **C.** Left atrial pressure\n- **D.** Ventilation to perfusion ratio\n- **E.** Pulmonary vascular resistance\n\n## Question 43 of 81\n\nwhich, in turn, causes right ventricular dilation and\nflattening or bowing of the interventricular septum.\nThe desynchronization of the ventricles may be\nincreased by the development of the right bundle\nbranch block. The decreased RV outflow and\nconcomitant RV dilation reduce left ventricular\nfilling, thereby compromising cardiac output. As a\nresult, LV filling is reduced in early diastole, and this\nleads to a reduction in the cardiac output (CO) and\ncause systemic hypotension and hemodynamic\ninstability. Low cardiac output leads to increased\nextraction of oxygen in the tissues, thereby\ndecreasing the partial pressure of oxygen in\nvenous blood below normal level. Right ventricle\n(RV) failure due to acute pressure overload is the\nprimary cause of death in severe PE.\nNote: Due to increase after load in right ventricle\nright ventricular dilation and right atrial pressure\nincreases in pulmonary embolism (Recall)\nPulmonary embolism: Increase physiological dead\nspace (Recall).\n63. A 55-year-old lady presents to the Emergency\ndepartment with complaint of visual loss in Lower left\nquadrant in both visual fields. The lesion would most likely\nbe in which of the following?\n\n## Question 63 of 90\n\n- **A.** Right temporal lobe\n- **B.** Right parietal lobe\n- **C.** Left parietal lobe\n- **D.** Left temporal lobe\n- **E.** Right occipital lobe\n\n## Question 44 of 81\n\nHomonymous denotes a condition which affects\nthe same portion of the visual field of each eye.\nHomonymous inferior quadrantanopia is a loss of\nvision in the same lower quadrant of visual field in\nboth eyes whereas a homonymous superior\nquadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same upper\nquadrant of visual field in both eyes.\nA lesion affecting one side of the temporal lobe may\ncause damage to the inferior optic radiations\n(known as the temporal pathway or Meyer's loop)\nwhich can lead to superior quadrantanopia on the\ncontralateral side of both eyes (RECALL), if the\nsuperior optic radiations (parietal pathway) are\nlesioned, the visual loss occurs on the inferior\ncontralateral side of the both eyes and is referred to\nas an inferior Quadrantanopia (RECALL).\n64. A 50-year-old male presents to ER with worsening\nbreathlessness, on examination you find signs of heart\nfailure. In heart failure, the frank starling curve is moved\ndownward due to:\nThe ability of the heart to change its force of\ncontraction and therefore stroke volume in\nresponse to changes in venous return is called the\nFrank-Starling mechanism.\n\n## Question 64 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decrease in contractility\n- **B.** Decrease in Afterload\n- **C.** Increase in preload\n- **D.** Decrease in preload\n- **E.** Increase in Afterload\n\n## Question 45 of 81\n\nThe heart will contract with greater force when\npreload/EDV is increased. Preload is diastolic filling\nvolume of left ventricle reflect stretch of cardiac\nmuscle cells, greater the stretch more forcefully\nheart will contract. when there is increased EDV/\npreload there is increased is Stroke volume this\nintrinsic relationship between EDV and stroke\nvolume is known as frank starling law of heart…\nIn HEART FAILURE, the Frank–Starling curve is\nmoved down and flattened due to reduced\ninotropy, so that more venous return and hence\npreload is required to increase stroke volume. As\nvenous return and preload rise, myocyte stretching\noccurs that increases sarcomere length.\nFACTORS AFFECTING THE FRANK STARLING\nCURVE\nincrease in contractility shift the curve upward and\nto the left e.g.: positive inotropic drugs (RECALL)\nDecrease in contractility= downward and to the\nright\nIncrease in preload = rightward shift\ndecrease in preload=leftward shift\nIncrease in Afterload=Downward and right\ndecrease in Afterload=upward and left\n65. 18 weeks Pregnant lady previously non-immune comes\nin contact with a chickenpox patient. Which one of the\nfollowing management plans is correct?\n\n## Question 65 of 90\n\n- **A.** Treat with IV antibiotics\n- **B.** Immunoglobulin+ iv acyclovir for 7 days\n- **C.** Immunoglobulin+ iv acyclovir for 14 days\n- **D.** Immunoglobulin and vaccine\n\n## Question 46 of 81\n\nChickenpox in pregnancy is managed by two\ndifferent methods - one is used before the onset of\nsymptoms and the other after the onset of rash.\nOne is varicella-zoster immune globulin (VZIG),\nwhich strengthens the immune system for a short\ntime. If you are not immune to chickenpox you can\nbe treated with VZIG, usually by injection, up to ten\ndays after you come into contact with chickenpox.\nIt is used before any symptoms appear; it does not\nwork afterwards. The other is Acyclovir, which may\nreduce fever and symptoms if it is started within 24\nhours of the rash developing and continue for 7\ndays\nKey points:\nIf you have already had chickenpox, you have\nnothing to worry about.\nOnce you have had chickenpox you cannot catch it\na second time, this is known as being immune.\nNine out of ten UK adults are immune to\nchickenpox.\nChickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in the\nUK.\nIt affects very few babies in the womb and\nalthough it can be very serious in pregnancy\nespecially in 1st trimester in first 12 weeks (Recall)\nIf you have been in contact with chickenpox and\nare not sure if you are immune, contact your GP or\nmidwife not attend the antenatal clinic.\nIf you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other\npregnant women and new babies until at least five\n- **E.** Vaccine and IV acyclovir\n\n## Question 47 of 81\n\ndays after the rash appears, or until all the blisters\nhave crusted over.\n66. A 5-year-old male child brought into the ED with an\ninfection after circumcision. The lymphatic from the penile\nskin and prepuce drains into which of the following group of\nlymph nodes?\nPenile skin and prepuce = superficial inguinal lymph\nnodes\nGlans penis and penile urethra=deep inguinal\nlymph nodes(recall)\nScrotum=Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\n(RECALL)\nTestes= lumber or para-aortic lymph nodes\n(RECALL)\nprostate= internal iliac nodes (recall)\nHigh Yield:\nPre aortic lymph nodes:\n1.coeliac group of preaortic lymph nodes drain:\nAbdominal oesophagus\nStomach\nPancreas(recall)\n\n## Question 66 of 90\n\n- **A.** Para aortic lymph nodes\n- **B.** Deep inguinal lymph nodes\n- **C.** Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\n- **D.** External iliac lymph nodes\n- **E.** Internal iliac lymph nodes\n\n## Question 48 of 81\n\nLiver\nSpleen\nGallbladder\nDuodenum proximal to major duodenal papilla\n2. Superior mesenteric group of preaortic lymph\nnodes drain:\ndistal duodenum\nJejunum\nIleum\nCecum\nAppendix\nAscending colon\nProximal two third of transverse colon\n3. 3. Inferior mesenteric group of preaortic lymph:\nDistal transverse colon\nDescending colon\nSigmoid colon\nProximal rectum\nLumbar or Paraaortic lymph nodes:\nKidney (Recall)\nAdrenal gland\nTestes (recall)\nOvaries (recall)\n67. 30 years old male patient brought into ED\nUnresponsive after initial assessment you suspect opioid\ntoxicity, what is the antidote of morphine toxicity?\n\n## Question 67 of 90\n\n- **A.** N–acetyl cysteine\n- **B.** Naloxone\n\n## Question 49 of 81\n\nNaloxone is an opioid antagonist rapidly reverse the\nadverse effects of opioid toxicity.\n- **C.** Atropine sulphate\n- **D.** Esmolol\n- **E.** Flumazenil\n68. A 50-year-old male presents to the emergency\ndepartment with complain of visual disturbance in his right\neye on examination you note when you show a light in his\nright eye, neither his right or Left pupil constrict but when\nyou show a light in his left eye both his left and right pupil\nconstrict. which of the following nerve is most likely\naffected?\nLoss of afferent pupillary light reflex is seen in\ncomplete palsy of optic nerve. the ipsilateral direct\nand contralateral consensual reflexes are lost as the\nafferent optic nerve does not sense the light shown\nin affected eye but the contralateral direct and\nipsilateral consensual reflexes are intact as the\nefferent oculomotor nerve is normal\n\n## Question 68 of 90\n\n- **A.** Oculomotor nerve\n- **B.** Trochlear nerve\n- **C.** Trigeminal nerve\n- **D.** Optic nerve\n- **E.** Abducens nerve\n\n## Question 50 of 81\n\n69. A comatose patient has a respiratory rate of 5 breaths\nper minute following an overdose of heroin. He receives\nnaloxone and his respiratory rate rapidly improves to 16\nbreaths per minute. Which component of arterial blood gas\nsampling is most likely to rapid decrease following this\nintervention?\nCo2 is vasodilator causes increases cerebral blood\nflow (Recall)\nHypoventilation increases C02 and increases\ncerebral blood flow\nHyperventilation causes decrease Pco2 and\ndecreases cerebral blood flow\n\n## Question 69 of 90\n\n- **A.** Base excess\n- **B.** Hco3\n- **C.** pH\n- **D.** Pco2\n- **E.** Po2\n70. Which one of the following nerves supply the skin\nabove the eye brow extending back to the vertex of the\nscalp?\n\n## Question 70 of 90\n\n- **A.** Zygomaticotemporal nerve\n- **B.** Greater occipital nerve\n- **C.** Zygomaticofacial nerve\n\n## Question 51 of 81\n\nsupraorbital nerve innervating the skin of upper eye\nlid and conjunctiva, skin of the upper forehead\nextending to the vertex of scalp.\n- **D.** Supraorbital nerve\n- **E.** Supratrochlear nerve\n71. A 30-year-old man presents with symmetrical\nascending muscle weakness. He had a diarrheal illness a\nfew weeks previously\nWhat is the most likely pathogen?\nGBS cases seem to occur after a viral or bacterial\ninfection like those that cause sore throats or\ndiarrhea characterized by lower limb weakness,\nparesthesia and hyporeflexia\n\n## Question 71 of 90\n\n- **A.** Epstein-Barr virus\n- **B.** Cytomegalovirus\n- **C.** Clostridium difficle\n- **D.** Campylobacter jejuni\n- **E.** Borrelia burgdorferi\n72. A 71-year-old man with a history of heart failure and\ntype 2 diabetes mellitus presents with an acutely painful,\nswollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. A clinical diagnosis\nof gout is made. Which treatment would be most\nappropriate for the acute management of this Patient?\n\n## Question 72 of 90\n\n## Question 52 of 81\n\nManagement of gout\nAcute gout: NSAIDS (specially naproxen) Recall\nGout with heart failure: Colchicine\nChronic gout: Allopurinol also reduces further\nattacks of gout by reducing uric acid\nNote: In gout under polarized light, gout crystals\nappear needle-shaped and yellow, a property called\nnegative birefringence (Recall)\nGout crystals are monosodium urate crystals\nIn Pseudo gout: Positive birefringence and\npyrophosphate crystals (Recall).\n- **A.** Febuxostat\n- **B.** Prednisolone\n- **C.** Colchicine\n- **D.** Allopurinol\n- **E.** Diclofenac\n73. Epinephrine cause vasoconstriction by acting on which\nreceptors?\n\n## Question 73 of 90\n\n- **A.** Beta-2\n- **B.** Alpha-1\n- **C.** Beta-1\n- **D.** AChR\n\n## Question 53 of 81\n\nAlpha 1: vasoconstriction\nAlpha 2: vasodilation\nBeta 1: centrally increases automaticity, heart rate,\nmyocardial contractility\nBeta 1: Peripherally vasodilates against alpha 1\nvasoconstriction\nBeta 2 - bronchodilation\n- **E.** Alpha-2\n74. Mifepristone is indicated in the medical termination of\npregnancy. It acts as an antagonist at which one of the\nfollowing receptors?\nMifepristone is an antagonist at progesterone and\nglucocorticoid receptors. It stimulates uterine\ncontractility and increases prostaglandins through\ninhibition of prostaglandin dehydrogenase. It also\nblocks cortisol receptor binding and reduces excess\ncortisol effects is cases of Cushing syndrome.\n\n## Question 74 of 90\n\n- **A.** Dopamine receptors\n- **B.** Estrogen receptors\n- **C.** Adrenergic receptors\n- **D.** Progesterone receptors\n- **E.** Muscarinic receptors\n75. Which of the following is the site of highest air way\nresistance?\n\n## Question 75 of 90\n\n## Question 54 of 81\n\nThe medium-sized bronchi actually constitute the\nsite of highest resistance along the bronchial tree,\nalthough small radii of the alveoli might predict that\nthey would have highest resistance, they don't\nbecause of parallel arrangement. In fact, early\nchanges in resistance in small air ways may be silent\nand go undetected because of their small overall\ncontribution to resistance.\n- **A.** Medium sized bronchi\n- **B.** Alveoli\n- **C.** Smallest bronchi\n- **D.** Trachea\n- **E.** Largest bronchi\n76. A 45-year-old woman with a newly diagnosis of type ii\ndiabetes has significant hyperglycemia.\nWhat is the most likely cause of her elevated blood\nglucose?\n\n## Question 76 of 90\n\n- **A.** Production of abnormal insulin\n- **B.** Increased cellular resistance to insulin\n- **C.** Reduced glucagon secretion\n- **D.** Excessive glucocorticoid production\n- **E.** Reduced insulin production by pancreatic beta\ncells\n\n## Question 55 of 81\n\nType 2 diabetes mellitus is characterized by\nhyperglycemia, insulin resistance, and relative\nimpairment in insulin secretion.\n78. The mean was calculated for study with normal\ndistribution. The values distribution around the mean are\nmeasured by?\nStandard error of the mean = standard deviation /\nsquare root (number of patients)\nHigh Yield:\nMean or average: is the sum of a collection of\nnumbers divided by the number of numbers in the\ncollection\nThe mode is the value that appears most often in a\nset of data\nThe median is the number separating the higher\nhalf of a data sample, a population, or a probability\ndistribution, from the lower half. The median of a\nfinite list of numbers can be found by arranging all\nthe observations from lowest value to highest value\nand picking the middle one\nFor example, a study looks at the number of\nattendances brittle asthmatics have at A&E's across\n\n## Question 78 of 90\n\n- **A.** Interquartal range\n- **B.** Standard deviation\n- **C.** Variance\n- **D.** Mode\n- **E.** Median\n\n## Question 56 of 81\n\nthe north of England each quarter. Each A&E\nsubmits the number of attendances as below:\n1,3,4,5,5,5,6,7,7,9,12,14,17\nMean = 7.5\nMode = 5\nMedian = 6\n79. In the control of breathing, which of the following\nstatements is CORRECT?\nInvoluntary respiration\nInvoluntary respiration is under subconscious\ncontrol. The diaphragm and intercostal muscles are\nthe primary respiratory muscles. They are\nstimulated by groups of neurons located in the\npons and medulla. These neurons form the\n\n## Question 79 of 90\n\n- **A.** Afferents from stretch receptors feed into the\npneumotaxic Centre to modulate breathing\n- **B.** The pneumotaxic Centre is located in the\nthalamus\n- **C.** When expiratory neurons fire they inhibit the\nactivity of inspiratory neurons\n- **D.** Voluntary control of breathing is mediated by\ncortical motor neurons directly innervating respiratory\ngroup neurons in the medulla, altering their activity\n- **E.** The pons contains dorsal and ventral respiratory\ngroups that receive input from chemoreceptors.\n\n## Question 57 of 81\n\nrespiratory control centre. They send impulses to\nthe primary respiratory muscles, via the phrenic\nand intercostal nerves, which stimulate their\ncontraction. There are three main groups of\nneurons involved in respiration:\nThe ventral respiratory group controls expiration\nThe dorsal respiratory group controls inspiration\nThe pontine respiratory group controls the rate and\npattern of breathing.\nNote: When expiratory neurons fire they inhibit the\nactivity of inspiratory neurons and once the\nneurons stop firing, the inspiratory muscles relax,\nand expiration occurs.\nVoluntary respiration\nVoluntary respiration is under conscious control. It is\ncontrolled via the motor cortex in the cerebrum,\nwhich receives inputs from the limbic system and\nhypothalamus. Signals are thought to be sent to the\nspinal cord from the motor cortex, which are then\npassed onto the respiratory muscles.\nCentral control of breathing\n(brainstem and cerebral cortex:\nHigh Yield)\n1. Medullary respiratory center\n(Recall)\n- **A.** Dorsal respiratory group is primarily responsible\nfor inspiration and generates the basic rhythm for\nbreathing\nInput to the dorsal respiratory group comes from\nthe vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves. The vagus\nnerve relays information from peripheral\nchemoreceptors in the lung. The glossopharyngeal\nnerve relays information from peripheral\nchemoreceptors and Output from the dorsal\nrespiratory group travels, via the phrenic nerve, to\nthe diaphragm\n\n## Question 58 of 81\n\n- **B.** Ventral respiratory group is primarily\nresponsible for expiration, is not active during\nnormal, quiet breathing, when expiration is passive.\nActivated during exercise, when expiration becomes\nan active process.\n2. Apneustic center is located in the lower pons.\nStimulates inspiration, producing a deep and\nprolonged inspiratory gasp (apneusis).\n3. Pneumotaxic center is located in the upper\npons. inhibits inspiration and, therefore, regulates\ninspiratory volume and respiratory rate.\n4.Cerebral cortex: Breathing can be under\nvoluntary control; therefore, a person can\nvoluntarily hyper ventilate or hypo ventilate.\nNote: Hypoventilation (breath-holding) is limited by\nthe resulting increase in Pco2 and decrease in P02\n81. A 40-year-old woman presents with a lump in the lateral\nupper right quadrant of her breast. You are suspicious this\nis breast cancer. Which lymph node would be most likely to\nbe affected first?\nThe lymphatic drainage of the breast is of great\nclinical importance due to its role in the metastasis\n\n## Question 81 of 90\n\n- **A.** Axillary\n- **B.** Parasternal\n- **C.** Supraclavicular\n- **D.** Interpectoral\n- **E.** Subclavian\n\n## Question 59 of 81\n\nof breast cancer cells.\nThere are three groups of lymph nodes that receive\nlymph from breast tissue\nAxillary nodes (75%): Receives lymphatic drainage\nfrom lateral and superior quadrant of breast\nparasternal nodes (20%): Receives lymphatic\ndrainage from medial quadrant\nNote: Breast cancer in axillary tail of breast most\ncommonly involved lymph node is axillary\n(Recall).\n82. The greatest increase in physiological dead space\nwould be expected with which of the following conditions?\nDead Space:  Volume of ventilated air that does not\nparticipate in gas exchange.\nTwo types of Dead Space;\n1: Anatomical Dead Space\n2: Physiologic Dead Space\n1: Anatomical Dead Space Volume of Air that fills\nthe conducting Zone of respiration made up by the\n\n## Question 82 of 90\n\n- **A.** Atelectasis\n- **B.** Pulmonary embolism\n- **C.** Obesity\n- **D.** Bronchoconstriction\n- **E.** Pneumothorax\n\n## Question 60 of 81\n\nNose, Trachea, and Bronchi. The value of anatomical\nDead Space is 150 ml.\n2: Physiologic or Total Dead Space Anatomic Plus\nAlveolar Dead Space (Volume of air in the\nRespiratory zone that does not take part in gas\nexchange).\nRespiratory Zone starts from Respiratory\nBronchioles, Alveolar Duct, Alveolar sac, and Alveoli.\nIn patients with acute PE, total dead space increases\nbecause lung units continue to be ventilated\ndespite diminished or absent perfusion.\nCauses of Increased Dead Space:\nEmphysema\nPneumonia\nARDS(RECALL)\nSmoking\nETT intubation\nBronchitis\nAsthma\nCardiac Failure\nPulmonary Embolism\nNeck Extension\nStanding\nHypotension\nbronchodilation\nCauses of decreased Dead Space:\nAtelectasis\nTracheostomy\nSleep\nMaxillectomy\nHyperventilation\nNeck Flexion\nSupine Position\nBronchoconstriction\n\n## Question 61 of 81\n\n83. Regarding the bursae of knee joint, which one of the\nfollowing statements is correct?\nBursa: synovial fluid filled sac within joints provide\nlow friction surfaces for the movement of tendons\nassociated with joint\nSuprapatellar bursa located between the\nquadriceps femoris and the femur (Recall)\nPrepatellar bursa found between the apex of the\npatella and the skin. Housemaid knee is due to\ninflammation of Prepatellar bursa\nInfrapatellar bursa: split into deep and superficial.\nThe deep bursa lies between the tibia and the\npatella ligament. The superficial lies between the\npatella ligament and the skin. Clergyman’s Knee is\ndue to inflammation of infrapatellar bursa.\n\n## Question 83 of 90\n\n- **A.** Suprapatellar bursa located between the\nquadriceps femoris and the femur\n- **B.** Prepatellar bursa located in the sartorius\n- **C.** Suprapatellar bursa located in the sartorius\n- **D.** Housemaid knee is due to inflammation of\ninfrapatellar bursa\n- **E.** Prepatellar bursa found between the quadriceps\nfemoris and the femur\n84. A 32-year-old male with history of trauma to the hand\npresents with pain in the right hand associated with tingling\nand numbness. You suspect median nerve injury. Which\n\n## Question 84 of 90\n\n## Question 62 of 81\n\none of the following is the most lateral structure forming the\ncarpal arch?\nCarpal tunnel structures\n- **A.** Median nerve\n- **B.** Trapezium\n- **C.** Hook of hamate\n- **D.** Ulnar nerve\n- **E.** Pisiform\n85. A patient complains of headache and visual loss. CT\nscan demonstrate a lesion of the temporal lobe what type\nof visual defect would u most expect to see in this patient?\n\n## Question 85 of 90\n\n- **A.** Bitemporal hemianopia\n- **B.** Contralateral homonymous superior\nQuadrantanopia\n- **C.** Contralateral homonymous inferior\nQuadrantanopia\n- **D.** Contralateral homonymous hemianopia\n\n## Question 63 of 81\n\nHomonymous denotes a condition which affects\nthe same portion of the visual field of each eye.\nHomonymous inferior quadrantanopia is a loss of\nvision in the same lower quadrant of visual field in\nboth eyes whereas a homonymous superior\nquadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same upper\nquadrant of visual field in both eyes.\nA lesion affecting one side of the temporal lobe may\ncause damage to the inferior optic radiations\n(known as the temporal pathway or Meyer's loop)\nwhich can lead to superior quadrantanopia on the\ncontralateral side of both eyes if the superior optic\nradiations (parietal pathway) are lesioned, the visual\nloss occurs on the inferior contralateral side of both\neyes and is referred to as an inferior\nquadrantanopia.\n- **E.** Homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing\n86. You are teaching group of medical students anatomy of\ninguinal region. You ask medical students Deep inguinal\nRing is a defect in which of the following structure?\nDeep inguinal ring is formed by transversalis fascia.\n\n## Question 86 of 90\n\n- **A.** Transversalis fascia\n- **B.** Rectus abdominis aponeurosis\n- **C.** External oblique aponeurosis\n- **D.** Internal oblique aponeurosis\n- **E.** Transversus abdominis aponeurosis\n\n## Question 64 of 81\n\nSuperficial inguinal ring is formed by External\nOblique. (Recall)\n87. A 25 years old woman from sub–Saharan Africa\npresents to the ED with complaint of fever and chills. Thick\nand thin films reveal malarial parasite. which organism\nwould most likely be seen?\n\n1. falciparum is the deadliest malaria parasite and the\nmost prevalent on the African continent. P. vivax is\nthe dominant malaria parasite in most countries\noutside of sub-Saharan Africa.\nNote: The Giemsa stain (Thick and thin film.\nRecall) is used as the gold standard for the\ndiagnosis of malaria on blood smears.\n\n## Question 87 of 90\n\n- **A.** Plasmodium falciparum\n- **B.** Plasmodium knowlesi\n- **C.** Plasmodium vivax\n- **D.** Plasmodium malariae\n- **E.** Plasmodium ovale\n88. you are prescribing ipratropium bromide for a patient\nwho has presented with an exacerbation of her COPD.\nwhat is mechanism of action of ipratropium bromide?\n\n## Question 88 of 90\n\n- **A.** Alpha agonist\n\n## Question 65 of 81\n\nIpratropium is an acetylcholine antagonist via\nblockade of muscarinic cholinergic receptors.\nBlocking cholinergic receptors decreases the\nproduction of cyclic guanosine monophosphate\n(cGMP). This decrease in the lung airways will lead\nto decreased contraction of the smooth muscles.\n- **B.** Beta agonist\n- **C.** Ca channel blocker\n- **D.** Muscarinic antagonist\n- **E.** Muscarinic agonist\n89. A 25-year-old woman presents with clinical features of\nanaphylaxis. You consider administering intramuscular\nadrenaline. Which strength of adrenaline is most\nappropriate in this situation?\nAdrenaline dose in anaphylaxis: 0.5 mg/ 0.5 ml of\n1:1000 adrenaline\nAdrenaline dose in cardiac arrest during CPR: 1mg\nof 1: 10000 concentration (Recall).\n\n## Question 89 of 90\n\n- **A.** 1:100\n- **B.** 1:10\n- **C.** 1:1000\n- **D.** 1:10,000\n- **E.** 1:100,000\n\n## Question 66 of 81\n\n1.\n17 years old female presented to the ED with complaint of\nfever since 3 days lethargy, stiff neck and non-blanching\nskin rash that appears on the lower limb.\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n## Question 1 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rheumatic fever\n- **B.** Autoimmune TTP\n- **C.** HSP\n- **D.** Endocarditis\n- **E.** Meningococcal Septicemia\n3. A 45-Year-old male suffered penetrating trauma to the\nparotid gland with facial nerve injury presents with\ncomplaint of inability to purse lips. Which of the following\nterminal branch of facial nerve is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 3 of 90\n\n- **A.** Greater petrosal nerve\n- **B.** Temporal branch\n- **C.** Marginal mandibular branch\n- **D.** Zygomatic branch\n- **E.** Buccal branch\n\n## Question 67 of 81\n\n7. A 72-year-old woman presents to the ED with a 24-hour\nhistory of blistering rash on the left side of her forehead.\nYou suspect varicella zoster virus infection (shingles). What\nis the most appropriate way to make the diagnosis prior to\nstarting treatment?\nVaricella zoster virus causes:\nChicken pox\nShingles usually affects older\n\n## Question 7 of 90\n\n- **A.** Polymerase chain reaction from skin scraping\nsample\n- **B.** IgG ELISA serology from blood sample\n- **C.** Direct fluorescent antibody staining from skin\nscraping sample\n- **D.** IgM ELISA serology from blood sample\n- **E.** Clinical history and examination alone\n\n## Question 68 of 81\n\nVaricella zoster Key Points\nThe incubation period is 10 - 21 days (Recall) (mean\ntime is 14 days).\nPrimary infection typically occurs in 4-10 years\nchildren\nDiagnosis is usually clinical\nAfter primary infection virus lies dormant in\nposterior root ganglion and reactivates in 20% of\npatients causing shingles\nSchool exclusion period: when all lesions are\ncrusted over (Recall\n10. Damage to Broca's area will most likely result in which\nof the following clinical features?\nBroca's aphasia: This is a non-fluent aphasia which\ncauses individuals to speak slower. It occurs when\nthe brain’s frontal lobe suffers damage. Individuals\nwith Broca’s aphasia often feel frustrated due to\nbeing able to comprehend what others are saying,\nbut unable to express themselves also called\nExpressive dysphasia. Individuals affected only can\nspeak in very short phrases, or have trouble getting\nwords out.\nWernicke's aphasia: This is a fluent aphasia as this\ntype of aphasia does not affect the speed of the\n\n## Question 10 of 90\n\n- **A.** Receptive dysphasia\n- **B.** Visual loss\n- **C.** Nystagmus\n- **D.** Dysarthria\n- **E.** Expressive dysphasia\n\n## Question 69 of 81\n\nindividual's speech. Individuals with this type of\naphasia have damage to their brain’s temporal lobe.\nAs a result, those of Wernicke's aphasia are\nsometimes unable to comprehend what others are\nsaying, but are able to speak in long sentences.\nHowever, their communication doesn't follow\nstandard grammar patterns and uses made-up\nwords. also called receptive diaphasia.(RECALL)\n11. Regarding preventive measures and spread of infection\nof thread worm, which one of the following statements is\ncorrect?\nThread worm is the most common helminth in the\nUK, commonly infect children\nMebendazole is the drug of choice and you must\ntreat everyone in your home even if they do not\nhave symptoms.\n\n## Question 11 of 90\n\n- **A.** Treat everyone in your home if they have\nsymptoms only\n- **B.** Treat only infected persons with mebendazole\n- **C.** Treat infected persons with mebendazole who\nhave symptoms\n- **D.** Do not treat other family members\n- **E.** Treat everyone in your home even if they do not\nhave symptoms\n\n## Question 70 of 81\n\n29. You are asked to review a 16-year-old girl who presents\nwith a right shoulder dislocation. This is her seventh right\nshoulder dislocation within a year. You suspect a\nhypermobility or ligament pathology. Which one of the\nfollowing ligaments is most likely injured in\nacromioclavicular fracture?\nAcromioclavicular joint\nTYPE= Plane type synovial joint\nThe acromioclavicular joint consists of an\narticulation between the lateral end of the clavicle\nand the acromion of the scapula\nLIGAMENTS OF THE JOINT\nThere are three main ligaments that support the\nacromioclavicular joint\nAcromioclavicular ligament\nConoid ligament\nTrapezoid ligament\nCollectively, the conoid and trapezoid ligaments are\nknown as the coracoclavicular ligament. It is the\nstrongest ligament and suspends weight of the\nupper limb\n\n## Question 29 of 90\n\n- **A.** Glenohumeral ligaments\n- **B.** Coracoacromial ligament\n- **C.** Transverse humeral ligament\n- **D.** Coracohumeral ligament\n- **E.** Coracoclavicular ligament\n\n## Question 71 of 81\n\nHigh yield:\nThe shoulder joint\nType: synovial ball and socket joint\nShoulder joint is formed by the articulation of the\nhead of the humerus with the glenoid cavity of the\nscapula\nLigaments of the shoulder joint:\nIn the shoulder joint, the ligaments play a key role\nin stabilizing the bony structures.\nGlenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle and\ninferior) They act to stabilize the anterior aspect of\nthe joint. They are the main source of stability for\nthe shoulder (Recall), holding it in place and\npreventing it from dislocating anteriorly.\nCoracohumeral ligament: It supports the superior\npart of the joint capsule.\nTransverse humeral ligament: This ligament spans\nthe distance between the two tubercles of the\nhumerus. It holds the tendon of the long head of\nthe biceps in the intertubercular groove\nCoraco–clavicular ligament: This ligament\ncomposed of the trapezoid and conoid ligaments\nand runs from the clavicle to the coracoid process of\nthe scapula. They work alongside the\nacromioclavicular ligament to maintain the\nalignment of the clavicle in relation to the scapula.\nCoracoacromial ligament. This ligament runs\nbetween the acromion and coracoid process of the\nscapula it forms the coraco-acromial arch.  This\nstructure overlies the shoulder joint, preventing\nsuperior displacement of the humeral head.\nStability of the joint\n\n## Question 72 of 81\n\nRotator cuff muscles [SITS MUSCLES] Recall\nGlenoid labrum: A fibrocartilaginous ridge\nsurrounding the glenoid cavity\nLigaments: Extra Capsular ligaments form the\ncoraco-acromial arch.\nBiceps tendon – it acts as a minor humeral head\ndepressor, thereby contributing to stability.\nMovements of the shoulder joint: Shoulder joint is\nhighly mobile ball and socket joint allows various\nmovements like flexion, extension, adduction,\nabduction, medial rotation, lateral rotation and\ncircumduction\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **CLINICAL PEARL:**\n> Shoulder joint dislocations\n> Anterior shoulder dislocation: Most common\n> injury results from forceful abduction, extension\n> and external rotation\n> Clinical features:\n> Arm usually abducted and externally rotated\n\n## Question 73 of 81\n\nHumeral head often palpated anteriorly\nPatient cannot touch contralateral shoulder with\nhand of affected extremity.\nComplications of anterior shoulder dislocation:\nInjury to axillary nerve (Recall)\nInjury to axillary artery\nInjury to rotator cuff\nPosterior Shoulder dislocation:  occurs\noccasionally results from direct blow to the arm\nthat produces forceful internal rotation and\nadduction.\nClinical features:\nArm usually adducted and internally rotated\nPatient unable to perform external rotation\nComplications of posterior shoulder dislocation [\nless common neurovascular injury]\nFracture of posterior glenoid labrum\nFracture of humeral shaft or lesser tuberosity\nNote: Light bulb sign on the X-ray is the diagnostic\nfeature of posterior shoulder dislocation\n38. Bilirubin is a byproduct of heme catabolism and is\nexcreted in the bile and stool. Bilirubin is likely derived\nfrom?\n\n## Question 38 of 90\n\n- **A.** Enterocytes\n- **B.** Mesangial cells\n\n## Question 74 of 81\n\nHeme is an important functional group of\nhemoglobin. Bilirubin, which is a byproduct of\nheme breakdown, comes from red blood cells. The\nliver receives unconjugated bilirubin from the blood\nand conjugates it via phase II metabolism to make\nit more soluble for excretion via the bile and stool.\nFat cells (adipocytes), skin cells (epithelial cells), and\nsmall intestine absorptive cells (enterocytes) do not\ncontain heme to be processed into bilirubin.\n- **C.** Epithelial cells\n- **D.** Adipocytes\n- **E.** Red blood cells\n46. A 54-year-old man presents with hematemesis. He has\nrecent diagnosed with a pulmonary embolus and started on\nlmwh. What is the most appropriate treatment to prevent\nadverse effects of heparin?\nCommon drugs and their antidote:\nHeparin=protamine sulphate\nAcetaminophen/paracetamol = Acetylcysteine\nOrganophosphates =Atropine\n\n## Question 46 of 90\n\n- **A.** Prothrombin complex\n- **B.** Heparan sulfate\n- **C.** Fresh frozen plasma\n- **D.** Dextran sulfate\n- **E.** Protamine sulfate\n\n## Question 75 of 81\n\nDigoxin=Digoxin immune fab\nBenzodiazepine= flumazenil\nOpioid= naloxone\nIron=Deferoxamine\nWarfarin=Vitamin K, Fresh frozen plasma,\nProthrombin complex concentrate\nTricyclic antidepressants=Sodium bicarbonate\nCalcium channel blocker=Calcium chloride\nTheophylline poisoning=beta blocker\nMethotrexate=Leucovorin\nAspirin=Sodium bicarbonate\nBeta blockers= glucagon\nInsulin = glucose\nIsoniazid= vitamin B6\n49. A 24-year-old female presents with severe headache,\nphotophobia, and stiffness of her back. Physical\nexamination reveals positive signs for meningitis. The\nattending physician decides to perform a lumbar puncture\nto determine if a pathogen is in the CSF. What is the last\nstructure the needle will penetrate before reaching the\nCSF?\nLumber puncture needle Peirce in order:\nSkin\nSubcutaneous tissue\nSupraspinous ligament\n\n## Question 49 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior longitudinal ligament\n- **B.** Dura mater\n- **C.** Ligamentum flavum\n- **D.** Pia mater\n- **E.** Arachnoid mater\n\n## Question 76 of 81\n\nInter spinous ligament\nligamentum flavum\nEpidural space\nDura\nArachnoid\nSub arachnoid space (contain CSF)\nNote: The subarachnoid space is filled with CSF\nwhich is produced by the choroid plexus within the\nventricles of the brain.\n54. Fetal hemoglobin shifts the o2 saturation curve to left\ndue to?\nFetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen\nthan adult hemoglobin. Maternal and Fetal blood\nnever mix during pregnancy, but they come close\nto each other in the placenta.  Oxygen diffuses\neasily to fetal hemoglobin here.\nThe reason fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity\nfor 02. It is composed of two alpha and two gamma\nsubunits, while adult hemoglobin is composed of\ntwo alpha and two beta subunits.\n2,3DPG present in RBCs enhance the ability of RBCs\nto release o2 near tissue and 2,3DPG only binds\nwith beta globulin chains, fetal hemoglobin (HbF)\n\n## Question 54 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increased P50\n- **B.** Decrease in pH\n- **C.** Strongly bind with 2,3DPG\n- **D.** Decreased affinity for O2\n- **E.** Increased affinity for O2\n\n## Question 77 of 81\n\nlack of beta globulin chains that’s why fetal\nhemoglobin has higher affinity for O2(RECALL).\n77. A 48-year-old man is found to have a right lower limb\nweakness and sensory loss. He also has apraxia. You\nsuspect a stroke.\nOcclusion of which artery is most likely to cause these\nsymptoms?\nHigh Yield: Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral\narteries\n\n## Question 77 of 90\n\n- **A.** Basilar artery\n- **B.** Posterior communicating artery\n- **C.** Internal carotid artery\n- **D.** Middle cerebral artery\n- **E.** Anterior cerebral artery\n\n## Question 78 of 81\n\nNote: Pontine infarct /Stroke: Results in ipsilateral\nfacial and contralateral limb weakness (Recall).\n80. A study was performed in two group of people one with\nheart disease and associated risk factor is compared to\nanother group with heart but no associated risk factor what\ntype of study is it?\n\n## Question 80 of 90\n\n- **A.** Cross Sectional\n- **B.** Case control\n- **C.** Randomised control trials\n- **D.** Interventional\n- **E.** Cohort\n\n## Question 79 of 81\n\nCase control study:\nRetrospective longitudinal observational\nstudy Disease vs non-Disease\nSubject to recall bias\nRelated to ODD Ratio\ncase-control studies are usually less expensive and\nalso shorter in duration.\nCohort Study:\nForward Study or Prospective longitudinal\nobservational study\nGroup with Risk Factors and Group without Risk\nFactors\nRelated to Relative Risk\nCause to Effect\nCompared to case control study, cohort study takes\nmore time\nCross-sectional:\nCross-sectional observational study\nDisease and Risk Factors\nSubject to recall bias\n90. A 40-year-old patient, known to be an IV drug user,\npresents to ED with 15 days history of fever, night sweats\nand malaise, and is noted to have a systolic murmur on\nexamination. You suspect acute infective endocarditis,\nwhich of the following pathogens is the most likely cause?\n\n## Question 90 of 90\n\n- **A.** Staph epidermidis\n- **B.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **C.** Staph viridans\n- **D.** Streptococcus pyogenes\n\n## Question 80 of 81\n\n0% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\nHigh Yield:\nMost common cause generally: Staph Aureus\n(Recall)\nMost common cause in IV drug abuser: Staph\nAureus\nMost common cause of infective endocarditis in\nprosthetic heart valves: Staph epidermidis\nMost common cause of infective endocarditis after\ntooth extraction surgery: Staph viridians\n- **E.** Staphylococcus aureus\n\n## Question 81 of 81	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
0ff670d4-ac42-40ea-aa1a-d25b267d2bfe	ef15ba2e-2dca-4639-9b94-5740ccb572f9	mock-ii-paper-ii	Mock 2 — Paper 2 (Clinical SBA Exam)	quiz	# Mock Ii - Paper Ii\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer scenario. Read carefully before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\nMrcem Primary Complete Course ,High Yield Stuff and Questions Bank ( For Diet ii Late 2026)\nOverview  Comments\nShare your score colleagues\n\nLinkedIn  Facebook  Twitter\n\n## Question 20 of 90\n\n1. A 32-year-old male presents to the ED with complain of\ndifficulty gripping objects in his right hand. On examination,\nhe had a weak grip strength in his right hand and was\nunable to flex his index and middle fingers accompanied by\nloss of opposition of thumb. Which one of the following\nnerves is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 1 of 90\n\n- **A.** Median nerve\n- **B.** Musculocutaneous nerve\n- **C.** Brachial plexus\n- **D.** Ulnar nerve\n- **E.** Radial nerve\n2.   A 43-year-old driver by profession presents to the ED\nwith seasonal allergies. Which of the following medications\nwould be most appropriate for the management of his\nallergy symptoms?\n\n## Question 2 of 90\n\n- **A.** Doxylamine\n- **B.** Fexofenadine\n\n## Question 1 of 58\n\nFirst generation H1 antihistamine are sedating and\nis contraindicated in the treatment of pilots,\nmachine operator and drivers.\nThe non-sedating antihistamine include (Recall):\nFexofenadine, loratadine, Desloratadine, and\ncertizine\n- **C.** Hydroxyzine\n- **D.** Cyclizine\n- **E.** Chlorphenamine\n3. A 40-year-old male was playing basketball when he fell\nand landed on his right shoulder. He experienced\nimmediate pain and had difficulty moving his arm. He was\ntaken to the emergency room, where an X-ray showed that\nhe had fractured the surgical neck of his right humerus. On\nexamination he has loss of sensation over inferior portion\nof deltoid. Which one of the following muscles is likely to be\ninjured?\nAxillary nerve injury is commonly caused\nby fracture of surgical neck of humerus or inferior\ndislocation of humerus.\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of the\narm. (Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but not to\na horizontal level).\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of the\ndeltoid (the ‘regimental badge area’).\nQuadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior\ncircumflex humeral artery and vein pass through it\nand can be compressed as a result of trauma,\nmuscle hypertrophy resulting in weakness of deltoid\nand teres minor.\n\n## Question 3 of 90\n\n- **A.** Teres minor\n- **B.** Supraspinatus\n- **C.** Subscapularis\n- **D.** Biceps Bracchi\n- **E.** Teres major\n4. A 25-year-old professional tennis player presents to the\nED with complaint of loss of flexion and abduction at wrist.\nShe states that during a practice match, she falls and\ninjures her wrist. Which one of the following forearm\nmuscles is likely to be injured?\n\n## Question 4 of 90\n\n- **A.** Extensor carpi radialis muscle\n\n## Question 2 of 58\n\nFlexor carpi radialis muscle= Flexion and abduction\nat wrist\nFlexion carpi ulnaris muscle= Flexion and adduction\nat wrist\nForearm muscles\nAnterior forearm muscles superficial\ncompartment\n- **B.** Flexor carpi ulnaris muscle\n- **C.** Pronator teres muscle\n- **D.** Flexor carpi radialis muscle\n- **E.** Extensor digitorum muscle\n5. A 35-year-old lady brought into the ED with history of\nRTA. She has fractured right femur with significant blood\nloss and in state of shock. What is the mechanism involving\nher presentation of shock?\nIn response to hypovolemia, the body tries to\ncompensate by activating the sympathetic nervous\nsystem. This leads to an increase in heart rate,\nvasoconstriction (narrowing of blood vessels), and\nthe release of hormones such as adrenaline and\nnorepinephrine. These responses are intended to\nmaintain blood flow to vital organs such as the\nbrain and heart, but they can also cause peripheral\nvasoconstriction, reduced blood flow to non-vital\norgans, and decreased urine output. If the blood\nloss continues, the body's compensatory\nmechanisms may become overwhelmed, and\nshock can occur.\n\n## Question 5 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sympathetic inhibition\n- **B.** Parasympathetic activation\n- **C.** Sympathetic activation\n- **D.** Cushing reaction\n- **E.** Parasympathetic inhibition\n\n## Question 3 of 58\n\n7.  A 74-year-old man with a previous anaphylactic reaction\nto amoxicillin has had symptoms and signs of pneumonia\nwith a CURB 65 score of 4. His blood cultures grew\nmycoplasma pneumonia.\nWhich single antibiotic is most appropriate for this\npatient??\nCURB-65 Score is used for Severity assessment of\ncommunity acquired pneumonia (Recall)\nConfusion (abbreviated mental test, AMT scores 8)\nUrea (>7 mmol/L)\nRespiratory rate (2 30/min)\nBlood pressure (< 90 systolic or $ 60 diastolic)\n65 (age ≥  65\nRisk\nScore 0 to 1 low risk\nScore 2: Intermediate risk\nScore 3 to 5: High risk\nLoe severity CAP: Ist choice Amoxicillin\nHigh severity CAP: Ist choice Co-Amoxiclav\nNote: Clarithromycin is widely used alternative\ntreatment in patients with penicillin allergy\n\n## Question 7 of 90\n\n- **A.** : Clavulanic acid\n- **B.** Ciprofloxacin\n- **C.** Co amoxiclav\n- **D.** Clarithromycin\n- **E.** Meropenem\n8. A 65-year-old man with a history of hypertension and\ndiabetes mellitus presents to the emergency room with\nsudden onset right-sided weakness and facial droop. Upon\nexamination, the physician notes right-sided hemiparesis,\ndysarthria, and neglect of the left side of his body. The\npatient reports a severe headache and dizziness prior to\nthe onset of his symptoms. Which one of the following\nvessels is most likely affected?\n\n## Question 8 of 90\n\n- **A.** Internal carotid artery (ICA)\n- **B.** Basilar artery\n- **C.** Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)\n\n## Question 4 of 58\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms, the most likely\naffected vessel is the Middle cerebral artery. The\nMCA supplies blood to the lateral surface of the\nbrain, including the motor and sensory areas\nresponsible for movement and sensation on the\nopposite side of the body. The patient's right-sided\nhemiparesis and neglect of the left side of his body\nare consistent with an MCA infarct\nMore easy to remember:\nACA: Contralateral weakness in lower limb\nMCA: Contralateral weakness in face/arm greater\nthan lower limb\n- **D.** Middle cerebral artery (MCA)\n- **E.** Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)\n9. A 30-year-old female had bloody diarrhea 2 weeks ago.\nNow she presents to the ED with complaint of joint pain,\nmouth ulcers and brown raised plaques on palms and\nsoles. Reactive arthritis diagnosis was made. Which one of\nthe following organisms cause reactive arthritis?\nReactive arthritis (Reiter's syndrome) is a triad of\nurethritis, arthritis and conjunctivitis.\n\n## Question 9 of 90\n\n- **A.** Campylobacter jejuni\n- **B.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **C.** Neisseria gonorrhea\n- **D.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **E.** Clostridium difficili\n\n## Question 5 of 58\n\nOrganisms causing reactive arthritis: Chlamydia,\nSalmonella, Campylobacter jejuni and Shigella\n10. A 35-year-old patient who works as a mechanic\npresents with complaints of numbness and tingling in the\nring finger and little finger of his right hand. On\nexamination, you suspect that he may have ulnar nerve\nentrapment at Guyon's canal. Which one of the following\ncarpal bone is most likely injured?\nFracture of both pisiform and hook of hamate can\nresult into entrapment of ulnar nerve in Guyon's\ncanal\nUlnar canal (Guyon's canal)\nTransmits ulnar neurovascular bundle from forearm\ninto the hand.\nBorders:\nMedial (ulnar): pisiform, flexor carpi ulnaris tendon\nLateral (radial): hook of hamate.\nRoof: palmar carpal ligament.\nFloor: flexor retinaculum\nContents: Ulnar nerve and artery\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Ulnar canal syndrome refers to compression of the\n> ulnar nerve within the ulnar canal.\n\n## Question 10 of 90\n\n- **A.** Lunate\n- **B.** Scaphoid\n- **C.** Trapezoid\n- **D.** Hook of hamate\n- **E.** Capitate\n11. During a routine physical examination, you perform\nauscultation of the heart to listen to the sounds produced\nby the valves. What is the area of auscultation of aortic\nvalve?\nHigh yield:\n\n## Question 11 of 90\n\n- **A.** Second right intercostal space\n- **B.** Left 5th intercostal space\n- **C.** Apex of heart\n- **D.** Right 5th intercostal space\n- **E.** Second left intercostal space\n\n## Question 6 of 58\n\nAreas of auscultation of heart. Remember on\nfingertips.\n12. A 60-year-old male with a history of hypertension and\nsmoking presents to the emergency department with chest\npain. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in the inferior\nleads, and a diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction (MI) is\nmade. The infarcted tissue in the heart is likely to undergo\nwhich type of necrosis?\nCell death:\nHigh yield: Be familiar with all types and examples.\n\n## Question 12 of 90\n\n- **A.** Fat necrosis\n- **B.** Liquefactive necrosis\n- **C.** Caseous necrosis\n- **D.** Coagulative necrosis\n- **E.** Fibrinoid necrosis\n13. Which part of the intestine becomes distended to\ntrigger the opening of the ileocecal valve, allowing the\ncontents of the small intestine to pass into the large\nintestine?\n\n## Question 13 of 90\n\n- **A.** Cecum\n- **B.** Jejunum\n\n## Question 7 of 58\n\nThe ileocecal valve is located at the junction of the\nileum (last part of the small intestine) and the\ncecum (first part of the large intestine). When the\nileum becomes distended, due to the accumulation\nof digested food, it triggers the opening of the\nileocecal valve, allowing the contents of the small\nintestine to pass into the large intestine\n- **C.** Appendix\n- **D.** Ileum\n- **E.** Duodenum\n14. A 30 years old laborer is brought into the ED by\nAmbulance after falling approximately 6m on building site\nand sustain head injury. His Gcs 5/15 early treatment given,\npatient intubated stabilized and shifted to icu. After some\ntime, patient started to deteriorate again with uncontrolled\nhypertension and bradycardia. Which one of the following\nreflex leads to this condition?\nCushing reflex is physiological response to increase\nintracranial pressure resulting in Cushing triad of:\nIncreased intracranial pressure\nIncreased blood pressure\nBradycardia\n\n## Question 14 of 90\n\n- **A.** Medullary reflex\n- **B.** Autonomic reflex\n- **C.** Cushing reflex\n- **D.** Cortical reflex\n- **E.** Baroreceptor reflex\n\n## Question 8 of 58\n\n15. A 30-year-old farmer has sustained a deep laceration to\nthe popliteal fossa. During examination your consultant ask\nwhat anatomical structure makes the upper lateral\nboundary of the popliteal fossa?\nBoundaries and contents of\npopliteal fossa\n\n## Question 15 of 90\n\n- **A.** Tibial collateral ligament\n- **B.** Lateral epicondyle of the femur\n- **C.** Biceps femoris muscle\n- **D.** Lateral head of the gastrocnemius muscle\n- **E.** Popliteus muscle\n16. A 6o years old lady was brought into the ED with\nfracture of right hip. Which of the following is the primary\ncause of postmenopausal fractures?\nPostmenopausal fractures are often caused by\nosteoporosis, which is a condition characterized by\ndecreased bone density and increased bone\nfragility. During menopause, there is a decrease in\nestrogen production, which can lead to\naccelerated bone loss and increased risk of\nfractures. Other factors that can contribute to\npostmenopausal fractures include age, genetics,\nnutrition, physical inactivity, smoking, and certain\nmedical conditions or medications that can affect\nbone health.\n\n## Question 16 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decreased estrogen production\n- **B.** Increased bone strength\n- **C.** Increased bone density\n- **D.** Excessive physical activity\n- **E.** Increased estrogen production\n\n## Question 9 of 58\n\nNote: Choose hormonal imbalance option if\nquestion stem is different from this and no other\ncharacter of osteoporosis is matched in options.\n18. A 14 years old male brought into the ED with complaint\nof fever and neck rigidity. You suspect meningitis, what are\nthe typical CSF changes in meningitis?\nNormal CSF glucose is less and 60% of the level of\nserum glucose\nGlucose is more than half of blood glucose (Normal)\nin viral meningitis. In bacterial and TB meningitis\nCSF to serum glucose ratio falls\nTuberculous meningitis has more mononuclear cells\nthan polymorphs in CSF. Predominant cells in viral\nand TB meningitis are Lymphocytes while\npredominant cells in bacterial meningitis are\nNeutrophils\nIn bacterial meningitis CSF protein is raised to 0.5–\n2.0 g/L.)\nCSF is clear in viral meningitis and CSF is turbid in\nbacterial meningitis\n\n## Question 18 of 90\n\n- **A.** CSF can appear turbid in Viral meningitis\n- **B.** Tuberculous meningitis has more polymorphs\nthan mononuclear cells in CSF\n- **C.** CSF can appear clear in viral meningitis\n- **D.** Glucose is less than half of blood glucose in viral\nmeningitis\n- **E.** In bacterial meningitis the CSF protein is 0.4 g/L\n20. A mother brought his five years old son into the ED with\na complaint of hematemesis after swallowing a button\nbattery. CT scan shows its stuck at T10. Which vessel has\nbeen damaged and bleeding?\nWhen a button battery is swallowed and becomes\nlodged in the esophagus or stomach, it can erode\n\n## Question 20 of 90\n\n- **A.** Inferior mesenteric artery\n- **B.** Left gastric artery\n- **C.** Splenic artery\n- **D.** Right gastric artery\n- **E.** Gastroduodenal artery\n\n## Question 10 of 58\n\nthrough the tissue and cause a perforation or fistula\nto form. In children, this is most seen at the site of\nthe left gastroesophageal junction, which is the\npoint where the esophagus meets the stomach on\nthe left side. The left gastric artery is a branch of the\nceliac artery that supplies blood to the stomach\nand is located in this area. If the battery erodes\nthrough the tissue and into the left gastric artery, it\ncan cause life-threatening bleeding.\nOesophageal constrictions\nThe anatomical relations of the oesophagus give\nrise to four constrictions, where food/foreign\nobjects are most likely to become impacted.\nOesophageal constrictions should not be confused\nwith pathological constrictions.\nCervical constriction: due to cricoid cartilage at the\nlevel of C5/6\nThoracic constriction: due to aortic arch at the level\nof T4/5\nAbdominal constriction: at oesophageal hiatus at\nT10/11 vertebral\nHigh yield:\nPeptic ulcer:\nBleeding gastric ulcer = Left gastric artery (Recall)\nBleeding duodenal ulcer = Gastroduodenal artery\n(Recall)\n21. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of\naction of botulinum toxin?\n\n## Question 21 of 90\n\n- **A.** It inhibits the breakdown of acetylcholine,\nprolonging muscle contraction.\n- **B.** It promotes the production of acetylcholine,\ncausing sustained muscle contraction.\n\n## Question 11 of 58\n\nBotulinum toxin blocks the release of acetylcholine\nfrom nerve endings at the neuromuscular junction\n(Recall), leading to muscle paralysis.\n- **C.** It blocks the release of acetylcholine, leading to\nmuscle paralysis\n- **D.** It enhances the release of acetylcholine, leading\nto increased muscle contraction\n- **E.** It binds to acetylcholine receptors on muscle\ncells, preventing muscle relaxation.\n22. A 25 years old pregnant lady presents to the ED with\ncomplaint of nausea, vomiting, pelvic cramps and spotting.\nShe gives history of taking doxycycline prescribed by her\nGP. Which of the following is common side effect of\ndoxycycline use in pregnancy?\nIntake of tetracycline (Doxycycline) by pregnant\nwomen results in the discoloration and deformity of\nteeth in the fetus. It can also produce impairment\nof bone growth. tetracyclines are also\ncontraindicated in children less than 10 years\nNote:\nDoxycycline is used for malaria prophylaxis\n(Recall)\nLithium can cause fetal goiter and cardiac\nabnormalities. (Recall)\n\n## Question 22 of 90\n\n- **A.** Neural tube defect\n- **B.** Cardiac defect\n- **C.** Congenital goiter\n- **D.** Tooth discoloration\n- **E.** CNS malformation\n23. A 35-year-old construction worker reports pain and\nswelling in the anatomical snuff box of his right hand after\nfalling off a ladder and landing on his outstretched hand.\nYou suspect injury of structures in anatomical snuff box.\nThe medial border of the snuff box is formed by the tendon\nof which muscle?\n\n## Question 23 of 90\n\n- **A.** Extensor pollicis longus\n- **B.** Flexor pollicis longus\n- **C.** Abductor pollicis longus\n\n## Question 12 of 58\n\nAnatomical snuffbox\nis a triangular depression found on the lateral\naspect of the dorsum of the hand. It is best seen\nwhen the thumb is extended.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Fracture of scaphoid bone: Falling on outstretched\n> hand. The scaphoid bone of the hand is the most\n> commonly fractured carpal bone\n> Clinical feature: Pain and tenderness in the\n> anatomical snuffbox.\n\n- **D.** Flexor pollicis brevis\n- **E.** Opponent pollicis\n24. A 27-year-old runner is out for her morning jog when\nshe steps on a rock and her left foot suddenly twists inward\nand she also sustained a lateral malleolus tear. Which one\nof the following ligaments is commonly torn in this injury?\nInversion injury along with tear of lateral\nmalleolus, tear of ATFL > CFL> PTFL\nAnkle ligaments\nEversion injury = medial ligament or medial deltoid\nligament injury associated with tear of medial\nmalleolus most commonly\ninversion injury= Lateral collateral ligament injured\n(subdivided into 3 ligaments) most frequent\nligament injury during ankle sprain or inversion\ninjury commonly affected ligament is Anterior\ntalofibular ligament followed by calcaneofibular\nligament (both are ligaments of lateral collateral\nligament) associated with tear of lateral malleolus\nmost commonly\n\n## Question 24 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)\n- **B.** Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL)\n- **C.** Deltoid ligament\n- **D.** Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)\n- **E.** Syndesmotic ligament\n\n## Question 13 of 58\n\nLeast injured during inversion injury post\ntalofibular ligament\nThe spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular\nligament) is a broad thick ligament that extends\nfrom the calcaneus to the navicular bone. It\nsupports the head of the talus, maintaining the\nstability of the talocalcaneonavicular joint and\nresists depression of the medial arch of the foot.\nThe spring ligament takes the weight of the body\nfrom the talus and transmits it to the forefoot.\n25. A 60-year-old man presents to ED complaining of\ndifficulty passing urine and nocturia. On examination, you\nnote the prostate is enlarged and you suspect BPH. Which\nof the following lobes of the prostate are most likely\nhypertrophied:\nProstate gland is largest accessory gland of the\nmale reproductive system. Histologically divided\ninto three zones\n1: Central zone: surrounds the ejaculatory ducts\n2: Transitional zone: centrally and surrounds the\nurethra. The glands of the transitional zone are\nthose that typically undergo hyperplasia (BPH) also\nmedian lobe of prostate most hypertrophied in\nBPH.\n3: Peripheral zone: makes up the main body of the\ngland and is located posteriorly. High incidence of\nprostatic carcinoma. This is zone palpated on PR\nexamination.\n\n## Question 25 of 90\n\n- **A.** Peripheral zone\n- **B.** Median lobe\n- **C.** Lateral lobe\n- **D.** Anterior lobe\n- **E.** Posterior lobe\n26. A 55-year-old man is admitted to the emergency\ndepartment with acute respiratory distress. His oxygen\nsaturation is low, and he is struggling to breathe. The\nmedical team determines that the patient requires\nintubation to secure his airway and provide mechanical\nventilation. Before intubation, the team prepares to\npreoxygenate the patient to minimize the risk of hypoxemia\nduring the procedure. Regarding preoxygenation for\nintubation, which lung volume needs to be replaced with\noxygen?\n\n## Question 26 of 90\n\n## Question 14 of 58\n\nDuring preoxygenation for intubation, the\nfunctional residual capacity (FRC) of the lungs\nneeds to be replaced with oxygen. The FRC is the\nvolume of air that remains in the lungs at the end of\na normal exhalation. During preoxygenation, the\ngoal is to fill the patient's lungs with oxygen-rich air\nto increase the amount of oxygen available in the\nbody before intubation and to prevent hypoxemia\n(low oxygen levels in the blood) during the\nprocedure.\nBy taking deep breaths and exhaling slowly, the\npatient can increase the amount of air they have in\ntheir lungs at end-expiration, which maximizes the\nFRC. When the patient is breathing 100% oxygen,\nthis oxygen-rich air replaces the air in the lungs and\nmaximizes the oxygen reservoir available for the\npatient. Replacing the FRC with oxygen ensures\nthat the patient has adequate oxygen reserves to\nprevent hypoxemia and allow for safe intubation.\nIntubation in the supine position can lead to a\ndecrease in functional residual capacity (Recall)\n- **A.** Functional residual capacity (FRC)\n- **B.** Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)\n- **C.** Residual volume (RV)\n- **D.** Total lung capacity (TLC)\n- **E.** Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)\n27. A 55 years old male known case of benign prostatic\nhyperplasia presents into the ED with complaint of urinary\nretention. He was prescribed doxazosin for urinary\nretention, drug given act on which receptor?\nDoxazosin is an Alpha 1 adrenoreceptor blocker. It\nrelieves the symptoms of urinary retention\nNote: Anti-muscarinic drugs cause urinary\nretention, dry mouth, pupil dilation and\nconstipation (Recall)\n\n## Question 27 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anticholinesterase\n- **B.** dopamine\n- **C.** Alpha 1 adrenoreceptor blocker\n- **D.** Acetylcholine\n- **E.** Anti muscarinic\n\n## Question 15 of 58\n\n29. A 22-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of\npain in his fingers, while assessing movements you\nsuspect loss of extension of distal interphalangeal joint, DIP\njoint injury causes?\n\n## Question 29 of 90\n\n- **A.** Metacarpo-phalangeal joint subluxation\n- **B.** Boutonniere deformity\n- **C.** Carpal tunnel syndrome\n- **D.** Mallet finger\n- **E.** Z-Deformity\n30. A 60-year-old diabetic patient presents to the ED with\ncomplaints of increased thirst, frequent urination, and\nfatigue. Lab results reveal elevated blood glucose levels\nand the presence of glucose in the patient's urine. Which\npart of the kidney reabsorbs maximal glucose?\nMaximal glucose reabsorption occurs in proximal\nconvoluted tubule.\n\n## Question 30 of 90\n\n- **A.** Proximal convoluted tubule\n- **B.** Collecting duct\n- **C.** : Renal papilla\n- **D.** Distal convoluted tubule\n- **E.** Loop of Henle\n31. A 27 years old male patient diagnosed and treated for\nhepatitis B. In hep-B infection, which one of the following\nserology reports you will see?\n\n## Question 31 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anti HBs\n- **B.** HbsAg+ and HbeAg+ve\n- **C.** HbsAg+ and HbeAg-ve\n\n## Question 16 of 58\n\nAcute hepatitis B\nHBsAg +Ve, HBeAg +Ve, anti-\nHBc IgM\nChronic hepatitis B\nHBsAg +Ve, HBeAg +Ve, anti-\nHBc IgG\nImmunity following\ninfection\nAnti-HBs, anti-HBe, anti-HBc\nIgG\nImmunity due to\nvaccination\nAnti-HBs\n- **D.** Anti-HBs, anti-HBe\n- **E.** HbeAg+ and HbeAb-ve\n32. A 55-year-old man with a history of hypertension and\nhigh cholesterol presents to the emergency room with\ncomplaints of chest pain and shortness of breath. Upon\nexamination, the physician notes ST segment elevation in\nleads V4-V6 of the electrocardiogram. The patient is\ndiagnosed with a myocardial infarction (MI) of the lateral\nwall of the heart. Further tests confirm the occlusion of\nwhich artery?\nEasy to remember:\nSeptal and Anterior wall MI: LAD (Anterior\ninterventricular branch) occluded.\nLateral wall MI: LCX occluded.\nCardiac blood supply:\n1: The Right coronary artery supplies:\nThe right atrium and most of right ventricle\nThe sinoatrial and atrioventricular node\nPosterior interventricular artery, also known as the\nposterior descending artery (PDA), is a branch of the\nright coronary artery supply posterior 1/3 of\ninterventricular septum and posterior aspect of\nright and left ventricle. Most common artery\ninfracted in posterior wall MI\nNote: Occlusion of right coronary artery can result\ninto ischemia of SA and AV node and\nsubsequently bradycardia or heart block\n2: The left coronary artery supplies via main\nbranches below:\nAnterior interventricular branch (LAD)\n\n## Question 32 of 90\n\n- **A.** Circumflex artery (LCX)\n- **B.** Left anterior descending artery (LAD)\n- **C.** Right coronary artery (RCA)\n- **D.** Posterior descending artery (PDA)\n- **E.** Left main coronary artery (LMCA)\n\n## Question 17 of 58\n\nLCX\n34. What is the main mechanism of action of diltiazem?\nDiltiazem and verapamil are non-dipyridamole\ncalcium channel blocker\nFDA-approved Indications of diltiazem\nAtrial arrhythmia, including atrial fibrillation with\nthe rapid ventricular rate (RVR)\nHypertension\nParoxysmal supraventricular tachycardia\nChronic stable angina\nAngina Due to Coronary Artery Spasm( Prinzmetal's\nangina)\nDihydropyridine calcium channel blockers include\namlodipine, felodipine and nifedipine\n\n## Question 34 of 90\n\n- **A.** Opens the Na+channel\n- **B.** Blocks Ca+ channels\n- **C.** Close the K+ channels\n- **D.** Blocks the Na+ channel\n- **E.** Opens the Ca+ channels\n35. Which one of the following feedback mechanisms is\ninvolved in the release of oxytocin from the posterior gland\nduring childbirth and labor?\nPositive feedback:\nPromotes changes in one direction leading\namplified effect. Cause vicious cycle and death,\nsometimes it can be useful.\nExamples:\n\n## Question 35 of 90\n\n- **A.** Negative feedback mechanism\n- **B.** Neutral feedback mechanism\n- **C.** Direct feedback mechanism\n- **D.** Positive feedback mechanism\n- **E.** Indirect feedback mechanism\n\n## Question 18 of 58\n\nChildbirth (During labor): The release of oxytocin\nfrom the posterior gland during childbirth and\nlabor\nBlood clotting.\nGeneration of nerve signals.\nSensation of needing to urinate.\nFollicular phase of menstrual cycle\nNegative feedback:\nComponents: Regulated variable, Sensor, Controller,\nand Effector.\nA regulated variable is sensed, information is fed\nback to the controller, and the effector acts to\noppose change (hence the term negative) Control\nmost systems of body."\nExamples: Osmoregulation, regulation of blood,\npressure, temperature regulation, regulation of CO2\nin ECF.\n36. A 47-year-old gardener has accidentally stabbed his\nright foot with a garden fork while working in sandals. He\ntells you that he had a full course of vaccinations as a child.\nwound is contaminated with manure. What would you\nadvise him regarding tetanus prophylaxis?\nWound contaminated with soil, manure is high risk\nwound. This patient is up to date with\nimmunisation and more than 10 years are passed\nfollowing last dose. He requires immediate\nreinforcing of both tetanus immunoglobulin and\nvaccine at different sites. Tetanus priming dose are\ngiven at 2,3,4, preschool booster (4 years and 3\nmonths) and then booster dose after every 10 years.\nTetanus prone wound\nClean wounds are defined as: wounds less than 6\nhours old, non-penetrating with negligible tissue\ndamage.\nTetanus-prone wounds include puncture-type\ninjuries acquired in a contaminated environment\nand likely therefore to contain tetanus spores e.g.,\ngardening injuries, compound fractures, wounds or\n\n## Question 36 of 90\n\n- **A.** He should have a tetanus booster and human\ntetanus immunoglobulin in the same site\n- **B.** There is no need for any treatment as he is fully\nvaccinated\n- **C.** He should have his tetanus antibodies checked\nbefore deciding on a booster\n- **D.** He should have a tetanus booster in his left arm\nand human tetanus immunoglobulin in his right arm\n- **E.** He should have a tetanus booster\n\n## Question 19 of 58\n\nburns with systemic sepsis and certain animal bites\nand scratches.\nHigh risk tetanus-prone wound: Heavy\ncontamination with material likely to contain\ntetanus spores e.g., soil, manure, wounds or burns\nthat show extensive devitalised tissue and wounds\nor burns that require surgical intervention that is\ndelayed for more than six hours are high risk even if\nthe contamination was not initially heavy.\n39. A 26-year-old male returns home from a holiday and\ncomplains of three days of dysuria and a purulent urethral\ndischarge. He is diagnosed to be a case of gonorrhea.\nWhich of the following is the appropriate treatment?\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae causes urethritis in 1st week\n(3-4 days) post exposure and unprotected sex while\nChlamydia trachomatis causes urethritis after 2-3\nweeks post exposure and unprotected sex\nGonorrhoea is usually treated with a single\nantibiotic injection (usually in the buttocks or\nthigh). With effective treatment, most of your\nsymptoms should improve within a few days.\nFirst line IM Ceftriaxone 500mg single dose.\n\n## Question 39 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ceftriaxone IM\n- **B.** Clarithromycin\n- **C.** Gentamicin\n- **D.** Vancomycin IV\n- **E.** Streptomycin\n\n## Question 20 of 58\n\n41. A 32-year-old presents to the ED with complaint of\ncough with sputum, fever, and night sweats for 1 month. On\nexamination, there is hilar lymphadenopathy. Regarding\nfeatures of Active TB accompanied with which one of the\nfollowing features?\nIn the case of active pulmonary TB, a patient can\nhave crepitations, and bronchial breath sounds, and\nspecially over upper lobes cavitations or\nconsolidations\nThe Mantoux test is a widely used test for latent\nTB (Recall): It involves injecting a small amount of a\nsubstance called PPD tuberculin into the skin of\nyour forearm. It's also called the tuberculin skin test\n(TST).\n\n## Question 41 of 90\n\n- **A.** Fibrotic lung\n- **B.** ZN staining negative\n- **C.** Positive Mantoux test\n- **D.** X-Ray cavitations\n- **E.** Usually lower lobe consolidation on X-Ray\n42. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof lacerated wound on medial compartment of thigh, on\nexamination he is unable to perform extension and medial\nrotation. Which one of the following muscles is most likely\ninjured?\nEXP: All the muscles of medial compartment of\nthigh are innervated by obturator nerve except\nhamstring portion of adductor magnus.\n\n## Question 42 of 90\n\n- **A.** Adductor magnus\n- **B.** Gracilis\n- **C.** Obturator externus\n- **D.** Adductor longus\n- **E.** Adductor brevis\n\n## Question 21 of 58\n\nAdductor\nmagnus\nAdduction and\nmedial rotation at\nhip\nHamstring part:\nAdduction and\nextension of thigh\nat hip\nAdductor part:\nObturator nerve\nHamstring part:\nSciatic nerve\n(tibial division)\nRecall\n43. Whilst in your surgical rotation your consultant performs\nappendectomy. What is the most common site for the\nappendix found at appendicectomy?\nThe most common position of the appendix overall\nis retrocecal, followed by pelvic type.\nNote: Pus from ruptured appendix accumulate in\nright subphrenic space (Recall)\n\n## Question 43 of 90\n\n- **A.** Below terminal ileum overlying psoas\n- **B.** Retrocecal\n- **C.** Anterior to terminal ileum\n- **D.** Retro-ileal\n- **E.** Pelvic\n44. 20 weeks pregnant Lady presents to the ED with\nherpetic rash. You plan to give iv acyclovir, what is the\nmechanism of action of acyclovir?\nAcyclovir acts to inhibit viral synthesis of DNA\nHigh yield:\nRegarding antibiotics:\n1: Protein synthesis inhibitor\nAminoglycosides\nMacrolides\nChloramphenicol\n\n## Question 44 of 90\n\n- **A.** Viral RNA synthesis inhibitor\n- **B.** Viral protein synthesis inhibitor\n- **C.** viral DNA synthesis inhibitor\n- **D.** Bacterial cell wall inhibitor\n- **E.** Bacterial DNA synthesis inhibitor\n\n## Question 22 of 58\n\nTetracycline\n2: Nucleic acid Synthesis inhibitor\nMetronidazole\nQuinolones\n3: Cell wall inhibitor\nB lactam antibiotics\n45. A 45-year-old male patient presents to the emergency\ndepartment with shortness of breath (SOB) and a normal\nalveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient. Regarding (A-a) gradient\nwhich one of the following diseases can present with it?\nA-a gradient\nDifference between alveolar Po2 and arterial Po2\nA-a gradient: PA02-Pao2 (where PA02 alveolar po2\nand Pao2 is arterial po2)\nThe A-a gradient is used to determine whether O2\nhas equilibrated between alveolar gas and arterial\nblood and helps determine causes of hypoxemia.\nThe normal A-a gradient is between 0- and 10mm\nHg. Since O2 normally equilibrates between alveolar\ngas and arterial blood, PAo2 is approximately equal\nto Pao2. g., Hypoventilation: CNS depression,\nneuromuscular disease (including myasthenia\ngravis and kyphoscoliosis), Obesity and Low\ninspired fio2 on high altitude\nThe A-a gradient is increased (>10 mm Hg) if O2\ndoes not equilibrate between alveolar gas and\narterial blood (e.g., diffusion defect, V/Q defect,\nand right-to-left shunt) and PAo2 is greater than\nPao ₂\n\n## Question 45 of 90\n\n- **A.** Pneumonia\n- **B.** COPD\n- **C.** Myasthenia gravis\n- **D.** Pulmonary emboli\n- **E.** Asthma\n46. A 24-year-old man is stabbed in the abdomen with a\nkitchen knife. A CT demonstrates a diaphragmatic injury\ninvolving the esophageal hiatus.\nWhich structure is most likely to be damaged?\n\n## Question 46 of 90\n\n- **A.** Inferior vena cava\n- **B.** Azygos vein\n\n## Question 23 of 58\n\n- **C.** Vagus nerve\n- **D.** Thoracic duct\n- **E.** Aorta\n47. A 40-year-old diabetic patient is brought to the\nemergency department with symptoms of hypoglycemia,\nincluding sweating, confusion, and lethargy. The team\nadministers a dose of glucagon to the patient to help raise\ntheir blood glucose levels and his symptoms resolved.\nWhat is the action of glucagon in hypoglycemia?\nGlucagon acts by binding to specific receptors on\nliver cells (Recall), which activates an enzyme called\nglycogen phosphorylase. This enzyme catalyzes the\nbreakdown of glycogen into glucose, which is then\nreleased into the bloodstream. In the case of\nhypoglycemia, glucagon stimulates the liver to\nbreak down its stored glycogen and release glucose\ninto the bloodstream, which helps to raise the\npatient's blood sugar levels.\n\n## Question 47 of 90\n\n- **A.** Glucogenolysis\n- **B.** Gluconeogenesis\n- **C.** Glycogenolysis\n- **D.** Inhibition of insulin secretion\n- **E.** Glycogenesis\n48. You see a 32-year-old woman in the resuscitation room\nwho has presented with tachyarrhythmia. You decide to\nperform carotid sinus massage. At what level in the neck is\nthe carotid sinus most likely to be found?\n\n## Question 48 of 90\n\n- **A.** hyoid bone\n- **B.** cricothyroid membrane\n- **C.** inferior border of 5th cervical vertebra\n- **D.** superior border of thyroid cartilage\n\n## Question 24 of 58\n\nCarotid Sinus Massage is a Vagal maneuver\ntransiently increase the arterial pressure in the\ncarotid sinuses and aortic arch. This action triggers\nthe baroreceptor reflex, which results in increased\nparasympathetic output to the heart via the vagus\nnerve and lowers the heart rate.\nThe carotid sinus Is located at the superior border\nof the thyroid cartilage, medial to the\nsternocleidomastoid muscle.\n- **E.** superior border of cricoid cartilage\n50. A 50 years old female known case of inflammatory\nbowel disease presents to the ED with complaint of pain in\nright lower quadrant and bloody diarrhea. Regarding\nmanagement of I.B.D which one of the following treatment\noptions is best given initially?\nA stepwise approach that is used for IBD\nmanagement is outlined below.\nThe first step in pharmacologic therapy for IBD is\naminosalicylates (Mesalazine)  If the patient does\nnot respond to an appropriate dose of\naminosalicylates, the second step is the addition of\ncorticosteroids, which tend to result in a significant\ndecrease in inflammation. Once the response is\nseen, the dose can be tapered\nFor those with mild to moderate disease limited to\nthe rectum, aminosalicylate agents like\nmesalamine are the mainstay of treatment and are\nbest given rectally\n\n## Question 50 of 90\n\n- **A.** Oral azathioprine\n- **B.** Hydrocortisone IV\n- **C.** Rectal prednisolone\n- **D.** Rectal mesalazine\n- **E.** Oral prednisolone\n51. A 55--year--old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof shortness of breath. He is known case of asthma. Which\nof the following results would you expect to find on\nspirometry?\n\n## Question 51 of 90\n\n- **A.** Low FEV1/FVC ratio\n- **B.** Increased FEV1\n- **C.** Low TLC\n\n## Question 25 of 58\n\nObstructive lung disease like (Asthma and COPD):\nLow FEV1/FVC ratio\nRestrictive lung disease: High FEV1/FVC ratio\nNote: Be familiar with all the spirometry findings in\nobstructive and restrictive lung disease.\n- **D.** Increased FEV1 /FVC ratio\n- **E.** High FVC\n52. Paramedic brought a man into the ED who suffered a\ntraumatic brain injury. Among other areas, his\nhypothalamus was damaged due to the impact of the\ncollision. Hypothalamic injury leads to increase in which\none of the following hormones?\n\nAn Injury to the hypothalamus can cause an increase\nin prolactin levels. Prolactin is a hormone that is\nprimarily produced by the pituitary gland, but its\nrelease is controlled by the hypothalamus.\nThe hypothalamus produces a hormone called\ndopamine, which inhibits the release of prolactin\nfrom the pituitary gland. If the hypothalamus is\ninjured, it may not produce enough dopamine,\nleading to an increase in prolactin levels. This\n\n## Question 52 of 90\n\n- **A.** ACTH\n- **B.** LH\n- **C.** TSH\n- **D.** Prolactin\n- **E.** FSH\n\n## Question 26 of 58\n\ncondition is known as hyperprolactinemia, and it can\ncause a range of symptoms, including irregular\nperiods, breast milk production (even in non-\npregnant or non-nursing women), decreased sex\ndrive, and erectile dysfunction in men. In severe\ncases, it can also cause infertility and osteoporosis.\n53. A 10-year-old child was brought into the ED following\nhead trauma with complaint of hydrocephalus. Imaging\nresulted in swelling of one lateral ventricle which of the\nfollowing is the most likely site of blockage\nThe flow of CSF Useful Mnemonic\nLittle Infants Try Crying for Food. Sub arachnoid\nspace\nLateral ventricle\nInterventricular foramina of (Monro)\nThird ventricle\nCerebral aqueduct\nForth ventricle\nForamina of Luschka\nSub arachnoid space\nNote: From subarachnoid space CSF is reabsorbed\nby arachnoid granulations (Recall) back into the\ncirculation\n\n## Question 53 of 90\n\n- **A.** Foramen Monro\n- **B.** Subarachnoid space\n- **C.** Foramen Luska\n- **D.** Sub arachnoid cisterns\n- **E.** Aqueduct of Sylvius\n55. Β-blocker was prescribed for hypertension in a female\nasthma patient. After about a week of treatment, the\nasthma attacks got worse, and the patient was asked to\nstop taking the β-blocker. Which of the following β-blockers\nwould you suggest as an alternative in this patient that is\nless likely to worsen her asthma?\n\n## Question 55 of 90\n\n- **A.** Carvedilol\n- **B.** Labetalol\n- **C.** Atenolol\n- **D.** Sotalol\n\n## Question 27 of 58\n\nBeta-blockers antagonize catecholamine-induced\nincreases in heart rate, reduce blood pressure and\nimprove left ventricular function, producing proven\nclinical benefits in people with cardiovascular\ndisease but blocking airway β 2-receptors can cause\nsevere and sometimes fatal bronchoconstriction in\npeople with asthma, although cardio selective β 1-\nblockers may be safer than non-selective\nThe cardio-selective beta-blockers include atenolol,\nBisoprolol, Esmolol, Acebutolol, Metoprolol, and\nNebivolol.\n- **E.** Propranolol\n56. Regarding the movements of extrinsic muscles of\nshoulder joint, which one of the following muscles performs\nadduction and internal rotation?\nExtrinsic muscles of shoulder Joint:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearl: Ask the patient to shrug his/her**\n> shoulder if the patient is unable to do this indicates\n> spinal accessory nerve injury and paralysis of\n> trapezius muscle\n\n## Question 56 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rhomboid major\n- **B.** Rhomboid minor\n- **C.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **D.** Levator scapulae\n- **E.** Pectoralis minor\n\n## Question 28 of 58\n\n57. You are required to perform procedural sedation in a\npatient who require ketamine. Which of the following are\namong the most common adverse effects associated with\nketamine?\n\nKetamine is an anesthetic, used to induce loss of\nconsciousness. Ketamine is NMDA receptor\nantagonist Psychosis and tachycardia are among\nthe most common adverse events associated with\nketamine.\nContraindications: Head trauma, Raised intracranial\npressure and hypertension\n\n## Question 57 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hyponatremia\n- **B.** Hyperkalemia\n- **C.** Hypotension\n- **D.** Tachycardia\n- **E.** Bradycardia\n58. A 15-year-old girl is found to be hyperglycemic. Urine\ntesting reveals glucose. This urinalysis result is most likely\nto be due to what process in the carrier protein of proximal\ntubule?\n\n## Question 58 of 90\n\n- **A.** . Activation\n- **B.** Saturation\n- **C.** Destruction\n- **D.** . Inborn error of metabolism\n- **E.** Inhibition\n\n## Question 29 of 58\n\nThe renal tubule plays a significant role in glucose\nreabsorption. If the plasma glucose rises, renal\ntubular reabsorption of glucose will increase\nlinearly until it reaches its maximum tubular\nresorptive capacity. The capacity of the proximal\ntubule to reabsorb glucose to prevent its passing to\nthe urine is known as the renal threshold. At\nthreshold, the system becomes saturated and the\nmaximal reabsorption rate, the glucose transport\nmaximum is reached and no more glucose can be\nabsorbed. Due to increase in plasma glucose, the\nfiltered glucose exceeds the capacity of the tubular\nsystem and the kidneys begin excreting it in the\nurine, the beginning of glycosuria\n59. A 36 years old male sustained trauma to his right foot\nand presents to the ED with infected wound. Which one of\nthe following local factors impede wound healing?\nWound healing is impaired by a poor blood supply\nto the wound and a poor venous drainage from the\nwound. Increased skin tension caused by tight\nsutures and the presence of infection, foreign\nbodies and necrotic tissue are other local factors\nthat impede healing.\n\n## Question 59 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decreased local mobility\n- **B.** The presence of necrotic tissue\n- **C.** Damage to the sensory nerves around the\nwound\n- **D.** Increased blood supply\n- **E.** Decreased skin tension\n60. You are called to attend to a patient with blood glucose\nof 2.0 mmol/L. He is conscious and alert. What is the most\nappropriate initial treatment?\n\n## Question 60 of 90\n\n- **A.** 1 mg subcutaneous glucagon\n- **B.** Intravenous 10% glucose infusion at rate of 100\nml/h\n- **C.** 15-20 g glucose orally\n- **D.** Intravenous 20% glucose infusion at rate of 100\nml/hr\n- **E.** 1 mg intramuscular glucagon\n\n## Question 30 of 58\n\nIn adults who are conscious, cooperative and can\nswallow: Give 15 - 20 g quick acting carbohydrate of\nthe patient's choice where possible e.g., 90 - 120 mL\nof Lucozade or 5-7 Dextrosol tablets • Repeat\ncapillary blood glucose 10 - 15 minutes later. If blood\nglucose is still < 4.0 mmol/L, repeat step 1 (no more\nthan 3 treatments in total) • If blood glucose remains\n< 4.0 mmol/L after 45 minutes or 3 cycles, consider 1\nmg glucagon IM OR IV 10% glucose infusion at\n100ml/hr\nNote: If the patient is unconscious and no iv line is\naccessed first choice would be im glucagon and in\nquestion iv line accessed then choose IV 10%\nglucose.\n62. A 24-year-old man self presents to ED following a fight\nin a nearby street. He has sustained multiple stab wounds\nand laceration medial compartment of thigh. Which one of\nthe following nerves is most likely injured in\nadductor canal?\nThe adductor canal serves as a passageway for\nstructures moving between the anterior thigh and\nposterior leg.\nIt transmits the femoral artery, femoral vein\n(posterior to the artery), nerve to the vastus\nmedialis and the saphenous nerve – the largest\ncutaneous branch of the femoral nerve.\n\n## Question 62 of 90\n\n- **A.** Femoral nerve\n- **B.** Saphenous nerve\n- **C.** Obturator nerve\n- **D.** Sciatic nerve\n- **E.** Tibial nerve\n63. What type of immunity is provided by intravenous (IV)\nadministration of immunoglobulin G?\n\n## Question 63 of 90\n\n- **A.** Cell mediated\n- **B.** . Humoral\n- **C.** Passive\n- **D.** . Active\n- **E.** Both humoral and cell mediated\n\n## Question 31 of 58\n\nIntravenous administration of immunoglobulin\nprovides the patient with passive immunity.\nCellular(cell-mediated) immune response acts\ndirectly to destroy pathogens without using\nantibodies but instead fights infection from\nmacrophages that kills pathogens. The humoral\nimmune response (or antibody-mediated response)\nprotects the body by circulating antibodies to fight\nagainst pathogens (e.g., bacteria).\nPassive immunity occurs when antibodies are\ntransferred by antibodies from an immune host,\nsuch as from a placenta to a fetus. Passive\nimmunity is short lived. Active immunity is longer\nlived and comes from the host itself.\n64. The following anti-tubercular drug is a potent CYP450\nenzyme inducer?\nRifampin is a potent inducer of cytochrome P450\nenzymes\nSide effects of Anti TB drugs:\n1: Rifampicin: Orange-red discoloration of body\nfluids, Hepatitis\n2: Isoniazid: Hepatitis, Peripheral neuritis\n3: Pyrazinamide: Hepatotoxicity, Hyperuricemia\n4: Ethambutol: Optic neuritis, Red-green color\nblindness\n\n## Question 64 of 90\n\n- **A.** Pyrazinamide\n- **B.** Rifampicin\n- **C.** Isoniazid\n- **D.** Streptomycin\n- **E.** Ethambutol\n\n## Question 32 of 58\n\n5: Streptomycin: ototoxicity, Nephrotoxicity, and\ncontraindicated in pregnancy\n65. A 22-year-old professional marathon runner presents\nwith shortness of breath. Which resting parameter would\nyou expect to have a higher value when compared to an\nuntrained patient?\n\n## Question 65 of 90\n\n- **A.** Blood pressure\n- **B.** Cardiac stroke volume\n- **C.** Heart rate\n- **D.** Cardiac output\n- **E.** Oxygen consumption\n67.  A 43-year-old overweight patient presents to ED\ncomplaining of fever and severe right upper quadrant pain.\nThe pain radiates to her right shoulder tip. Murphy’s sign is\npositive and you diagnose acute cholecystitis. Which of the\nfollowing nerves is responsible for the pain referred to as\nthe shoulder tip?\nMotor innervation: The phrenic nerve provides\nmotor innervation to the diaphragm; the main\nmuscle of respiration.\nSensory Functions Sensory fibers from the phrenic\nnerve supply the central part of the diaphragm,\nincluding the surrounding pleura and peritoneum.\nThe nerve also supplies sensation to the\nmediastinal pleura and the pericardium (Pain of\npericarditis)\nNote: Inflammation of gall bladder may present\nwith pain in the right shoulder due to irritation of\nphrenic nerve (Diaphragmatic peritoneum).\n\n## Question 67 of 90\n\n- **A.** Lesser thoracic splanchnic nerve\n- **B.** Greater thoracic splanchnic nerve\n- **C.** Vagus nerve\n- **D.** Phrenic nerve\n- **E.** Intercostal nerves\n\n## Question 33 of 58\n\n68. A 27-year-old patient presents to the emergency\ndepartment with a gluteal stab wound that he sustained\nduring an altercation. The patient reports difficulty moving\nhis right leg and is unable to flex his knee. On physical\nexamination, you note decreased sensation to light touch\nover the lateral aspect of his right leg and foot. Which one\nof the following nerves is most likely injured?\nTibial nerve or posterior tibial nerve gives sensory\ninnervation to posterolateral side of the leg, lateral\nside of the foot and heel (Plantar aspect of foot)\nTibial nerve or Posterior\ntibial nerve\nBranch of sciatic nerve. Arises at apex of popliteal\nfossa receiving nerve fibers from L4-S3.\nFunctions:\nMotor: Innervates all the muscles in posterior\ncompartment of the leg and the majority of the\nintrinsic foot muscles.\nSensory: In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve gives\noff cutaneous branches. These combine with\nbranches from the common fibular nerve to form\nthe sural nerve. This sensory nerve innervates the\nskin of the posterolateral side of the leg and the\nlateral side of the foot and heel.\nNote: In summary, the tibial nerve provides both\nmotor and sensory innervation to the lower leg and\nfoot, in this scenario patient unable to flex his knee\nis because of tibial nerve injury while the sural\nnerve only provides sensory innervation to a small\narea of the lateral lower leg and foot.\nAlso note sensory innervation specifically to\nAchilles tendon and plantar foot is because of\ntibial nerve and sensory innervation to lateral\naspect of lower leg and lateral heel is because of\nsural nerve (Arises from both tibial and common\nfibular nerve)\n\n## Question 68 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sural nerve\n- **B.** Tibial nerve\n- **C.** Saphenous nerve\n- **D.** Common fibular nerve\n- **E.** Obturator nerve\n\n## Question 34 of 58\n\n69.\nA 19-Year-old girl presents to the ED with complaint of\nfever and non-painful pump on site of injury. She gives\nhistory of exposure to cats and scratch by young cats. Cat\nscratch disease is caused by which one of the following\ntypes of bacteria?\nBartonella henselae: Gram negative bacilli or\ncoccobacilli\nCat-scratch disease (CSD) or felinosis is an\ninfectious disease caused by Bartonella henselae\nmost often results from a scratch or bite of a cat.\n\n## Question 69 of 90\n\n- **A.** Gram positive cocci\n- **B.** Gram negative bacilli\n- **C.** Gram positive bacilli\n- **D.** Gram negative cocci\n- **E.** Acid fast bacteria\n70. A 60-year-old man, fell down a flight of stairs and\nlanded awkwardly on his left hip. He immediately felt a\nsharp pain in his hip and was unable to bear the weight on\nhis left leg. He was taken to the emergency room, where X-\nrays revealed a fracture in his left femur and greater\ntrochanter. Regarding greater trochanter of femur which\none of the following muscles is inserted on it?\n\n## Question 70 of 90\n\n## Question 35 of 58\n\nThe greater trochanter of the femur serves as an\nattachment site for several muscles, including the\ngluteus medius and minimus, which are important\nfor hip stability and movement. The gluteus medius\nattaches to the lateral surface of the greater\ntrochanter and functions to abduct and medially\nrotate the hip.\n- **A.** Gluteus Medius\n- **B.** Quadratus femoris\n- **C.** Tensor fasciae Latae\n- **D.** Gluteus maximus\n- **E.** Sartorius\n72. A 15-year-old boy presents to the emergency\ndepartment with a history of fever, headache, and swollen\nand painful salivary glands for the past week. He reports\nhaving difficulty eating and drinking due to the pain in his\nglands. He also complains of easy bruising, petechiae, and\ngum bleeding for the past few days. On examination, his\nplatelet count is found to be decreased, and he is noted to\nhave acute kidney injury. Based on clinical presentation\nwhat is the likely diagnosis?\n\n## Question 72 of 90\n\n- **A.** Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)\n- **B.** Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\n- **C.** TTP\n\n## Question 36 of 58\n\nNote:  HSP can also present with similar symptoms.\nHSP is a systemic vasculitis that affects small blood\nvessels and is characterized by the deposition of\nimmune complexes in the vessel walls. It primarily\naffects children and is characterized by a triad of\nsymptoms including palpable purpura (red or\npurple spots on the skin), arthritis, and abdominal\npain. HSP can also cause renal involvement and\nhematuria. If in question stem mentioned\nabdominal pain, arthritis and renal involvement\ngo with HSP.\n- **D.** Infectious mononucleosis (IM)\n- **E.** Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP)\n74. A 26-year-old male brought into the ED by paramedics\nwith lacerated wound on left upper quadrant. You suspect\nsplenic injury. Which rib fracture is likely to be associated\nwith spleen injury?\nSpleen injury most Likely associated with fracture\nof rib 10.\n\n## Question 74 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rib 1\n- **B.** Rib 8\n- **C.** Rib 4\n- **D.** Rib 10\n- **E.** Rib 6\n75. An 18-year-old patient presents to the Emergency\nDepartment following an injury to the distal crease of her\nwrist. An image of her hand at rest is shown below.\n\n## Question 75 of 90\n\n## Question 37 of 58\n\nParalysis to which of the following muscles is most likely\nresponsible for this deformity?\nInterossei and medial two lumbricals are innervated\nby ulnar nerve\nClaw hand:  Injury to the ulnar nerve is commonly\ncaused by a fracture of the medial epicondyle and\nresults in a claw hand, in which the ring and little\nfingers are hyperextended at the\nmetacarpophalangeal joints and flexed at the\ninterphalangeal joints.\nPartial claw hand deformity is due to an isolated\nulnar nerve injury.\nComplete claw hand deformity: Involves all digits\nand results from combined ulnar and median nerve\ninjury.\nUlnar nerve injury at elbow and wrist\n- **A.** Interossei and medial two lumbricals\n- **B.** Extensor digiti minimi\n- **C.** Extensor digitorum\n- **D.** Flexor digitorum longus\n- **E.** Flexor digitorum profundus\n76. A 10 days old baby brought into the ED with complaint\nof intestinal obstruction and diagnosed to have\nHirschsprung disease. Hirschsprung disease occur due to\nabsence of which one of the following enteric neurons?\n\n## Question 76 of 90\n\n- **A.** Glial cells\n- **B.** Myenteric plexus\n- **C.** Endothelial cells\n- **D.** Pacinian corpuscles\n- **E.** Mucosal plexus\n\n## Question 38 of 58\n\nHirschsprung disease results from the absence of\nparasympathetic ganglion cells in the myenteric\nand submucosal plexus of the rectum and/or colon\n78. A 35-year-old patient presents to the clinic with a\ncomplaint of difficulty walking due to pain in the front of\ntheir lower leg. The patient reports that the pain started a\nfew days ago after a strenuous workout session. On\nphysical examination, you note tenderness and swelling\nover the tibialis anterior muscle. Injury of tibialis anterior\nmuscle will result in loss of which movement of foot?\nAnterior leg consists of four muscles\n\n## Question 78 of 90\n\n- **A.** Dorsiflexion and great toe extension\n- **B.** Dorsiflexion and inversion\n- **C.** Dorsiflexion and eversion\n- **D.** Plantarflexion and inversion\n- **E.** Plantarflexion and eversion\n79. A 14 years old child swallows a coin, it lodged in\nesophagus, which one of the following structures compress\nesophagus at thoracic level?\nThe anatomical relations of the oesophagus give\nrise to four constrictions, where food/foreign\nobjects are most likely to become impacted\nThe esophagus has 3 anatomic constrictions.\n\n## Question 79 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left vagus nerve\n- **B.** Left brachiocephalic vein\n- **C.** Diaphragmatic hiatus\n- **D.** Aortic Arch\n- **E.** Posterior surface of the heart\n\n## Question 39 of 58\n\n1: Pharyngoesophageal constriction is at the\njunction with the pharynx\n2: Thoracic(aortobronchial) constriction: The\nsecond is at the crossing with the aortic arch and\nthe left main bronchus\n3: Diaphragmatic constriction: The third is at the\njunction with the stomach\n81. A 50-year-old female presents to the emergency\ndepartment with severe abdominal pain that has been\nworsening for the past few days. The patient reports that\nthe pain is located in the upper right quadrant of her\nabdomen and is accompanied by nausea and vomiting.\nImaging studies reveal the presence of gallstones in the\npatient's gallbladder. What hormone is responsible for the\npatient's gallstone-associated pain?\nThe hormone responsible for gallstone-associated\npain is cholecystokinin (CCK). CCK is released from\nthe intestinal cells in response to the presence of\nfatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum. It\nstimulates the contraction of the gallbladder,\nleading to the release of bile into the duodenum. In\npatients with gallstones, the contraction of the\ngallbladder can cause intense pain as the stones\nare forced through the narrow bile ducts. The pain\ntypically occurs after a meal when CCK levels are\nhighest.\n\n## Question 81 of 90\n\n- **A.** Glucagon\n- **B.** CCK\n- **C.** Somatostatin\n- **D.** Gastrin\n- **E.** Secretin\n82. You are examining a patient of femoral hernia; your\nconsultant asks which structure is bordered medially to the\nfemoral canal?\n\n## Question 82 of 90\n\n- **A.** Inguinal ligament\n- **B.** Pectineal ligament\n- **C.** Lacunar ligament\n- **D.** Femoral vein\n- **E.** Femoral nerve\n\n## Question 40 of 58\n\nFemoral canal lies most medial of the femoral\nsheath.\nFemoral sheath encloses femoral artery, femoral\nvein and femoral canal. Femoral nerve does not lie\nwithin femoral sheath. (Recall)\nBorders of the femoral canal: (Useful mnemonic\nLAMP: FILP)\nLateral border: Femoral vein (Immediately lateral)\nRecall.\nAnterior border: Inguinal ligament\nMedial border: Lacunar ligament\nPosterior ligament: Pectineal ligament, Pectineus\nmuscle and superior ramus of pubic bone.\nHigh Yield:\nContents of femoral triangle:\nFrom lateral to medial: Useful mnemonic (NAVEL)\nFemoral nerve\nFemoral artery\nFemoral vein\nEmpty space (consists of femoral canal)\nLymph nodes\nNote: Femoral nerve lies lateral to femoral artery\nand vein (Recall)\n84.    A 34-year-old presents to ER with severe right\nshoulder pain after a fall from a height. X-ray reveals a\nfracture in the middle third of the right clavicle. The medial\nend of the fractured clavicle is displaced upward due to\ntraction by the?\nClavicle acts to transmit forces from upper limb to\naxial skeleton.\nAfter fracture:\nLateral end is displaced inferiorly by the weight of\nthe arm and displaced medially by the pectoralis\nmajor\nMedial end is pulled superiorly due to traction by\nthe sternocleidomastoid muscle.\n\n## Question 84 of 90\n\n- **A.** Pectoralis minor muscle\n- **B.** Serratus anterior muscle\n- **C.** Rhomboid muscle\n- **D.** Sternocleidomastoid muscle\n- **E.** Subclavius muscle\n\n## Question 41 of 58\n\n85. A 25-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of\nleft upper quadrant pain, which structure prevent diffuse\nperitonitis across abdomen?\nOmentum\nPeritoneal fold extending from the stomach to\nadjacent neurovascular structures divided into the\ngreater and lesser omentum\nThe greater omentum is a fatty apron that hangs\ndown from the greater curvature of the stomach,\nand it plays a role in the immune response to\ninfections and inflammation in the abdomen. The\ngreater omentum is known to have a protective\nfunction in the peritoneal cavity, where it can limit\nthe spread of infection or inflammation to other\nparts of the abdomen and help isolate the affected\narea.\nTherefore, if diffuse peritonitis is suspected due to a\nperforation in the LUQ, the greater omentum may\nplay a role in containing the spread of the infection\nor inflammation and preventing it from becoming\nmore widespread in the peritoneal cavity.\n\n## Question 85 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sigmoid colon\n- **B.** Small intestinal mesentery\n- **C.** Hepatogastric ligament\n- **D.** Greater omentum\n- **E.** Cecum\n89. A motorcyclist falls from his bike in an accident and gets\na deep gash that severs the superficial peroneal nerve near\nits origin. Which of the following muscles is paralyzed?\n\n## Question 89 of 90\n\n- **A.** Extensor hallucis longus\n- **B.** Fibularis tertius\n- **C.** Extensor digitorum brevis\n- **D.** Fibularis longus\n\n## Question 42 of 58\n\nMuscles in the Lateral\ncompartment of leg:\nConsists of two muscles, fibularis\nlongus and brevis, also called peroneus longus\nand peroneus brevis.\nNote: Insertion of fibularis longus is Ist metatarsal\nand medial cuneiform (Recall)\nInsertion of fibularis brevis is 5th metatarsal\n(Recall)\n- **E.** Extensor digitorum longus\n90. A 48-year-old woman complains of bilateral leg swelling\nwhich has developed over the last week. She has recently\nbeen diagnosed with hypertension and commenced on\ntreatment 2 weeks ago. Which hypertensive is most likely\nto have been prescribed?\nPedal edema is one of the most common adverse\neffects of calcium channel blockers. It has been\nmore common with dihydropyridine calcium\nchannel blockers such as: amlodipine, felodipine,\nnifedipine (the main cause for calcium channel\nblocker induced edema increased capillary\nhydrostatic pressure by arteriolar dilation). but it\nalso seems to occur to a lesser extent with non-\ndihydropyridine such as verapamil and diltiazem.\nThe most common side effect of verapamil is\nconstipation (Recall)\n\n## Question 90 of 90\n\n- **A.** . Atenolol\n- **B.** Bendroflumethiazide\n- **C.** Ramipril\n- **D.** Amlodipine\n- **E.** . Sotalol\n6. Regarding Einthoven's triangle, which one following\nshow maximum deflection during ventricular\ndepolarization?\n\n## Question 6 of 90\n\n## Question 43 of 58\n\nThe three limb electrodes, I, II and III form a triangle\n(Einthoven's Triangle), at the right arm (RA), left arm\n(LA) and left leg (LL).\nLead 1= Left arm(+Ve) and right arm (-Ve)\nLead2 = left leg(+Ve) and Right arm (-Ve)\nLead 3= Left leg(+Ve) and left arm (-Ve)\nLead 2 normally shows largest deflection during\nventricular depolarization as the muscle mass is\ngreatest and depolarization travels from apex to\nbase\n- **A.** Left arm and right arm\n- **B.** Right arm and right leg\n- **C.** Left arm and Right leg\n- **D.** Left arm and left leg\n- **E.** Right arm and left leg\n17. A 44 years old woman presents to the ED with pain in\nhis right eye, after initial assessment you suspect corneal\nulcer. Regarding sensory innervation, which one of the\nfollowing nerves carry pain sensation from cornea?\nOpthalmic division of trigeminal nerve gives\nsensory innervation to:\nForehead and scalp\nUpper eyelid and conjunctiva\nCornea\nDorsum of nose\nHigh Yield:\n1: Supraorbital nerve: (Branch of Opthalmic division\nof trigeminal nerve) innervate Skin of upper eyelid,\nconjunctiva and upper forehead\n2: Infraorbital nerve (Branch of maxillary division of\ntrigeminal nerve) innervating skin of lower eyelid,\nside of nose, cheek and upper lip\n\n## Question 17 of 90\n\n- **A.** Maxillary division of trigeminal nerve\n- **B.** Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve\n- **C.** Optic nerve\n- **D.** Facial nerve\n- **E.** Opthalmic division of trigeminal nerve\n\n## Question 44 of 58\n\n2:  Supratrochlear nerve:(Branch of Opthalmic\ndivision of trigeminal nerve) innervate Skin of\nlower forehead\n3: Naso ciliary nerve (Branch of Opthalmic division\nof trigeminal nerve) innervate cornea, ciliary body\nand iris.\nInfratrochlear nerve: Bridge of nose\n19. A 32-year-old male presents to the ED with complain of\ndifficulty gripping objects in his right hand. On examination,\nhe had a weak grip strength in his right hand and was\nunable to flex his index and middle fingers. However, he\nwas able to flex his ring and little finger. Which one of the\nfollowing nerves is most likely injured?\n\nHigh yield median nerve injury\nNote: Flexion of the ring and little fingers at\nMCPJ and DIPJ are preserved as these are\nfunctions of medial half of flexor digitorum\nprofundus and medial two lumbricals\ninnervated by ulnar nerve\n\n## Question 19 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ulnar nerve\n- **B.** Musculocutaneous nerve\n- **C.** Radial nerve\n- **D.** Brachial plexus\n- **E.** Median nerve\n28. A 49 years old deep sea driver having decompression\nsickness. Which one of the following laws demonstrate\nmechanism of dissolved gases in deep sea drivers?\n\n## Question 28 of 90\n\n- **A.** Boyles law\n- **B.** Ficks law\n- **C.** Daltons law\n- **D.** Charles law\n- **E.** Henry law\n\n## Question 45 of 58\n\nHenry law states that the amount of dissolved gas\nin a liquid is proportional to its partial pressure\nabove the liquid.\nSo, breathing gas at higher-than-normal pressures\nwill cause their bodies to absorb more gases than\nare absorbed at lower pressures. Absorbed gases\ncan cause decompression sickness as well as toxic\neffects.\nHenry law is applicable in Scuva diving (Recall) or\ndeep-sea diver\n33. You have been asked to give a teaching session on\nhead injuries to a group of medical students. You are\ndiscussing cerebral blood flow and the relation with\nintracranial pressure. Which of the following statements is\ntrue regarding. cerebral blood flow (CBF), cerebral\nperfusion pressure (CPP), and intracranial pressure (ICP)?\nCerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) is the pressure\ngradient that drives blood flow through the brain. It\nis defined as the difference between the mean\narterial pressure (MAP) and the intracranial pressure\n(ICP) and is represented by the following formula:\nCPP = MAP - ICP.\nNormal ICP ranges from 5 to 15 mmHg. The\nstatement "Normal ICP is 65 to 195 mmHg" is\nincorrect and represents a severe elevation of ICP\nthat could cause brain damage.\n\n## Question 33 of 90\n\n- **A.** CBF autoregulation is lost below a CPP of 60\nmmHg.\n- **B.** The only resistance to CBF is ICP\n- **C.** Normal ICP is 65 to 195 mmHg.\n- **D.** CPP closely parallels diastolic blood pressure\n- **E.** CPP means arterial pressure (MAP) - ICP\n37. A 50-year-old patient named John has been admitted to\nthe hospital with a hip fracture after a fall. He is scheduled\nto undergo surgery to repair the fracture the following day.\nThe anesthesiologist has recommended a fascia iliaca\nblock to manage his pain after the surgery. Which one of\nthe following nerves is blocked by fascia iliaca rather than\nfemoral block?\n\n## Question 37 of 90\n\n- **A.** Femoral nerve\n- **B.** Sciatic nerve\n- **C.** Sural nerve\n\n## Question 46 of 58\n\nBoth the fascia iliaca block and the femoral block\nare regional anesthesia techniques used to manage\npain in the lower extremities. The fascia iliaca block\ninvolves the injection of local anesthetic\nbupivacaine into the fascial compartment located\ndeep to the inguinal ligament, targeting the\nfemoral, lateral femoral cutaneous, and obturator\nnerves. This block provides analgesia to the hip\njoint, femur, and anterior thigh.\nThe femoral block, on the other hand, targets only\nthe femoral nerve by injecting local anesthetic\naround the nerve at the level of the inguinal crease.\nThis block provides analgesia to the anterior thigh\nand knee.\nWhile both blocks can be effective in managing\npain, the fascia iliaca block provides a broader area\nof analgesia and may be preferred for procedures\ninvolving the hip joint or for patients who have pain\nin the hip and thigh region. The femoral block may\nbe preferred for procedures involving the anterior\nthigh or knee. The choice of block will depend on\nthe specific needs of the patient and the nature of\nthe procedure\n- **D.** Saphenous nerve\n- **E.** Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve of thigh\n38. In patients with chronic kidney disease, which part of\nthe kidney is primarily targeted by parathyroid hormone\n(PTH) to regulate calcium and phosphorus homeostasis?\nPTH increases Ca reabsorption by activating\nadenylate cyclase in the distal tubule.\nThiazide diuretics increase Ca reabsorption in the\nearly distal tubule and therefore decrease Ca\nexcretion. For this reason, thiazides are used in the\ntreatment of idiopathic hypercalciuria.\nHigh yield:\nRenal calcium handling\n60% of plasma Ca is filtered across the glomerular\ncapillaries.\n\n## Question 38 of 90\n\n- **A.** Collecting duct\n- **B.** Loop of Henle\n- **C.** Glomerulus\n- **D.** Proximal convoluted tubule\n- **E.** Distal convoluted tubule\n\n## Question 47 of 58\n\nTogether the proximal tubule and thick ascending\nlimb reabsorb more than 90% of filtered Ca by\npassive processes that are coupled to Na\nreabsorption.\nLoop diuretics (furosemide) cause increased urinary\ncalcium excretion (hypercalciuria). Because Ca\nreabsorption is linked to Na reabsorption in LOH,\ninhibiting Na reabsorption with a loop diuretic also\ninhibits Ca reabsorption. If volume is replaced, loop\ndiuretics can be used in the treatment of\nhypercalcemia.\nTogether, distal tubule and collecting duct\nreabsorbs 8% of filtered Ca by an active process.\nPTH increases Ca reabsorption by activating\nadenylate cyclase in the distal tubule.\nThiazide diuretics increase Ca reabsorption in the\nearly distal tubule and therefore decrease Ca\nexcretion. For this reason, thiazides are used in the\ntreatment of idiopathic hypercalciuria.\n40. A 50 years old male with history of RTA brought into the\nED in Shock. After initial assessment CVP line placed in his\nleft subclavian, what is the most common complication of\nplacing CVP in subclavian vein?\n\n## Question 40 of 90\n\n- **A.** High risk of carotid artery injury\n- **B.** Local haematoma\n- **C.** External jugular injury\n- **D.** Hydrothorax\n- **E.** Pneumothorax\n49.\nA 32-year-old male with a history of RTA presented to ED\nwith Complain of pain in his Right shoulder. X-ray was done\nwhich shows anterior shoulder dislocation. Which one of\nthe following ligaments is most likely injured?\n\n## Question 49 of 90\n\n- **A.** Acromioclavicular ligament\n\n## Question 48 of 58\n\nThe shoulder joint\nType: synovial ball and socket joint\nShoulder joint is formed by the articulation of the\nhead of the humerus with the glenoid cavity of the\nscapula\nLigaments of the shoulder joint:\nIn the shoulder joint, the ligaments play a key role\nin stabilizing the bony structures.\nGlenohumeral ligaments (superior, middle and\ninferior) They act to stabilize the anterior aspect of\nthe joint. They are the main source of stability for\nthe shoulder (Recall), holding it in place and\npreventing it from dislocating anteriorly.\nCoracohumeral ligament: It supports the superior\npart of the joint capsule.\nTransverse humeral ligament: This ligament spans\nthe distance between the two tubercles of the\nhumerus. It holds the tendon of the long head of\nthe biceps in the intertubercular groove\nCoraco–clavicular ligament: This ligament\ncomposed of the trapezoid and conoid ligaments\nand runs from the clavicle to the coracoid process of\nthe scapula. They work alongside the\nacromioclavicular ligament to maintain the\nalignment of the clavicle in relation to the scapula.\nCoracoacromial ligament. This ligament runs\nbetween the acromion and coracoid process of the\nscapula it forms the coraco-acromial arch.  This\nstructure overlies the shoulder joint, preventing\nsuperior displacement of the humeral head.\nStability of the joint\nRotator cuff muscles [SITS MUSCLES] Recall\nGlenoid labrum: A fibrocartilaginous ridge\nsurrounding the glenoid cavity\nLigaments: Extra Capsular ligaments form the\ncoraco-acromial arch.\nBiceps tendon – it acts as a minor humeral head\ndepressor, thereby contributing to stability.\nMovements of the shoulder joint: Shoulder joint is\nhighly mobile ball and socket joint allows various\nmovements like flexion, extension, adduction,\nabduction, medial rotation, lateral rotation and\ncircumduction\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **CLINICAL PEARL:**\n> Shoulder joint dislocations\n- **B.** Transverse humeral ligament\n- **C.** Coracoacromial ligament\n- **D.** Coraco–clavicular ligament\n- **E.** Glenohumeral ligament\n\n## Question 49 of 58\n\nAnterior shoulder dislocation: Most common\ninjury results from forceful abduction, extension\nand external rotation\nClinical features:\nArm usually abducted and externally rotated\nHumeral head often palpated anteriorly\nPatient cannot touch contralateral shoulder with\nhand of affected extremity.\nComplications of anterior shoulder dislocation:\nInjury to axillary nerve (Recall)\nInjury to axillary artery\nInjury to rotator cuff\nPosterior Shoulder dislocation:  occurs\noccasionally results from direct blow to the arm\nthat produces forceful internal rotation and\nadduction.\nClinical features:\nArm usually adducted and internally rotated\nPatient unable to perform external rotation\nComplications of posterior shoulder dislocation [\nless common neurovascular injury]\nFracture of posterior glenoid labrum\nFracture of humeral shaft or lesser tuberosity\nNote: Light bulb sign on the X-ray is the diagnostic\nfeature of posterior shoulder dislocation\n54. Which one of the following hormones is involved in\nsecretion of bile?\nBile production is stimulated by: Bile salts, Secretin,\nglucagon and gastrin\nBile secretion/ release of bile is stimulated by CCK\nNote: For details check bile and bile salts in\ngastrointestinal physiology.\n\n## Question 54 of 90\n\n- **A.** Motilin\n- **B.** Glucagon\n- **C.** Somatostatin\n- **D.** GIP\n- **E.** CCK\n61. How is aspirin beneficial in inhibiting platelets\naggregation by blocking which of the following pathway?\n\n## Question 61 of 90\n\n## Question 50 of 58\n\nAspirin works as an antiplatelet agent by\nirreversibly blocking the enzyme cyclooxygenase-1\n(COX-1) inside the platelets. This enzyme is\nnecessary to generate thromboxane A2, a potent\nplatelet activator from arachidonic acid. Aspirin also\ninhibits COX-2 which explains part of its anti-\ninflammatory properties.\nClopidogrel selectively inhibits the binding of\nadenosine diphosphate (ADP) to its platelet P2Y12\nreceptor, inhibiting the platelet adhesion and\naggregation.\nAbciximab binds to the glycoprotein (GP) IIb/IIIa\nreceptor of platelets and inhibits platelet\naggregation.\n- **A.** Inhibition of GPilb/Illa receptor sites\n- **B.** Inhibition of binding of ADP to its platelet\nreceptor\n- **C.** Inhibition of the breakdown of cAMP\n- **D.** Inhibition of the calcium release\n- **E.** Inhibition of platelet thromboxane A2 synthesis\n66. A 35-year-old female patient presents to the emergency\ndepartment with palpitations. An electrocardiogram (ECG)\nreveals that she is experiencing supraventricular\ntachycardia (SVT). You plan to give her adenosine. What is\nthe mechanism of action of adenosine on the heart when\ngiven in SVT?\nAdenosine stimulates A1-adenosine receptors and\nopens acetylcholine sensitive K+ channels,\nincreasing K+ efflux. This hyperpolarises the cell\nmembrane in the atrioventricular node and, by\ninhibiting the calcium channels, slows conduction\n\n## Question 66 of 90\n\n- **A.** Adenosine dilates the coronary arteries\n- **B.** Adenosine increases the heart rate\n- **C.** Adenosine inhibits the sympathetic nervous\nsystem\n- **D.** Adenosine activates the parasympathetic\nnervous system\n- **E.** Adenosine slows the conduction through the AV\nnode\n\n## Question 51 of 58\n\nin the AVN. As it has a very short duration of action\n(half-life only about 8 - 10 seconds)\nContraindications:\nAdenosine is contraindicated in: Asthma and COPD\n(can cause bronchospasm), Decompensated heart\nfailure, Long QT syndrome, Second- or third-degree\nAV block and sick sinus syndrome (unless\npacemaker fitted) and Severe hypotension\n71. A 19-year-old man attends the ED after sustaining a\nhead injury. He has a facial nerve palsy and a CT sacn\nsacn shows a fracture of the temporal bone. His facial\nnerve has most likely been injured at which foramen of the\nskull base?\nThe facial nerve exits the cranial cavity through the\ninternal acoustic meatus (Recall) and enters the\nfacial canal of the temporal bone. The nerve then\nexits the skull through the Stylomastoid foramen\n(lie between the styloid and mastoid processes of\nthe temporal bone) to enter the parotid gland,\nultimately branching into five terminal branches to\nsupply the face.\nThe facial nerve and its branches pass through\nthe parotid gland, as does the external carotid\nartery, which gives off its two terminal branches,\nthe maxillary artery and the superficial temporal\nartery, inside the gland. Deep penetrating wound\nto the parotid gland will damage these vessels\n(Recall)\n\n## Question 71 of 90\n\n- **A.** Foramen lacerum\n- **B.** Jugular foramen\n- **C.** Foramen Magnum\n- **D.** Foramen ovale\n- **E.** Stylomastoid foramen\n73. A 35-year-old woman presents to the emergency\ndepartment with sudden onset of severe lower back pain\nand numbness in both legs. She reports difficulty with\nwalking and standing up from a seated position. Upon\nfurther evaluation, the healthcare provider notes decreased\nsensation in the saddle area and decreased anal tone,\nsuggestive of possible cauda equina syndrome. Regarding\nanatomy of cauda equina lies at which vertebral level?\n\n## Question 73 of 90\n\n- **A.** T12-S2\n- **B.** C5-C6\n\n## Question 52 of 58\n\nThe spinal cord is housed in the vertebral canal. It is\ncontinuation of medulla oblongata and terminates\nas the conus medullaris at the second lumbar\nvertebrae of adults.\nAt birth, the conus medullaris lies at L3. By the age\nof 21, it sits at L1/L2, thus the spinal cord only\noccupies about 2/3s of the space available to it in\nthe vertebral canal.\nBelow the conus medullaris is cauda equina which\nlies below L1 from L2 to sacrum\nNote: The cauda equina contains a bundle of nerves\nwhich project distally within the enclosed cavity of\nthe lumbar cistern from the spinal cord and conus\nmedullaris toward the coccyx. Each nerve exits at its\nrespective vertebral level toward targets which are\nsupplied by the L2-S5 spinal cord level.\n- **C.** T12-L1\n- **D.** C3-C5\n- **E.** S3-S5\n77. An 8-year-old child is planned for tonsillectomy for the\ngrade 3 enlarged tonsils. During surgery which artery will\nbe most common cause of primary haemorrhage during\nsurgery\nMain supply to tonsils is from tonsillar branch of\nfacial artery. While all others supply minutely to\ntonsils\n\n## Question 77 of 90\n\n- **A.** Branches of internal carotid\n- **B.** Descending palatine artery from maxillary\n- **C.** Ascending pharyngeal artery\n- **D.** Dorsal lingual branches\n- **E.** Tonsillar branch of facial artery\n80. A 45-year-old electrician presents to the ED with\ncomplaint of unable to abduct his little finger on his left\nhand. He tries to move it but finds that the muscle on the\nside of his palm, known as the hypothenar eminence, is no\nlonger functioning properly. Which one of the following\nhypothenar muscles is likely injured?\n\n## Question 80 of 90\n\n- **A.** Opponens digiti minimi muscle\n- **B.** Flexor digiti minimi muscle\n\n## Question 53 of 58\n\nMuscles of the hand:\nHypothenar muscles:\nHypothenar muscle located at base of middle\nfinger and form hypothenar eminence. All are\ninnervated by ulnar nerve\nThenar muscles:\nThree short muscles located in the base of the\nthumb. All are innervated by median nerve.\n- **C.** Palmaris brevis muscle\n- **D.** Adductor pollicis muscle\n- **E.** Abductor digiti minimi muscle\n83. A 28-year-old male patient with history of severe allergy\nto peanuts accidentally ingests some food containing\npeanuts. Within minutes, he developed difficulty breathing,\nswelling of the face and throat, and hives. You suspect\nanaphylaxis and one dose of adrenaline was given. Which\nof the following receptors does adrenaline act on during\nanaphylaxis?\nAdrenaline acts on beta-2 adrenergic receptors,\nwhich are found in the lungs, heart, and blood\nvessels. Stimulation of beta-2 adrenergic receptors\ncauses relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle,\nincreased cardiac output, and vasodilation, which\ncan help to counteract the effects of anaphylaxis.\n\n## Question 83 of 90\n\n- **A.** Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors\n- **B.** Dopamine receptors\n- **C.** Beta-1 adrenergic receptors\n- **D.** Alpha 2 adrenergic receptors\n- **E.** Beta-2 adrenergic receptors\n86. A 30 years old laborer is brought into the ED by\nAmbulance after falling approximately 6m on building site\nand sustained fracture to the base of skull. On Examination\nthere is numbness over lip and maxilla.\n\n## Question 86 of 90\n\n## Question 54 of 58\n\nWhich part of skull bone is fractured?\nMandibular branch of trigeminal: Passes through\nforamen ovale\nMaxillary branch of trigeminal:  Passes through\nforamen rotundum\nNote: Maxillary division of trigeminal via its branch\ninfraorbital nerve gives sensory innervation to\nlower eyelid, cheek, side of nose and upper lip\n(Recall)\n- **A.** Stylomastoid foramen\n- **B.** Foramen ovale\n- **C.** Superior orbital fissure\n- **D.** Foramen spinosum\n- **E.** Foramen rotundum\n87. 17-year-old male presented to the ED following road\ntraffic accident. On examination, he had penetrating wound\nover Achilles' tendon, and she had loss of sensation over\nposterolateral distal leg and planter foot.\n\nWhat nerve was injured in this injury?\n\nTibial nerve or posterior tibial nerve gives sensory\ninnervation to posterolateral side of the leg, lateral\nside of the foot and heel (Plantar aspect of foot)\nTibial nerve or Posterior tibial\nnerve\nBranch of sciatic nerve. Arises at apex of popliteal\nfossa receiving nerve fibers from L4-S3.\nFunctions:\n\n## Question 87 of 90\n\n- **A.** Sural nerve\n- **B.** Anterior-tibial nerve\n- **C.** Sciatic nerve\n- **D.** Common fibular nerve\n- **E.** Posterior tibial nerve\n\n## Question 55 of 58\n\nMotor: Innervates all the muscles in posterior\ncompartment of the leg and the majority of the\nintrinsic foot muscles.\nSensory: In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve gives\noff cutaneous branches. These combine with\nbranches from the common fibular nerve to form\nthe sural nerve. This sensory nerve innervates the\nskin of the posterolateral side of the leg and the\nlateral side of the foot and heel.\nSensory branches of tibial nerve:\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Damage to the tibial nerve**\n> causes loss of plantar flexion of the foot and\n> impaired inversion. This presents difficulty in\n> getting the heel off the ground and a shuffling\n> gait, resulting in characteristic clawing of the\n> toes and sensory loss on the sole of the foot,\n> affecting posture and locomotion.\n\nNote: The nerve block that is commonly used\nfor pain management in heel injuries is the\nposterior tibial nerve block. (RECALL)\n\n## Question 56 of 58\n\n88. After a bone fracture, the body initiates a healing\nprocess. Which one of the following cells are responsible\nfor re-modelling stage of bone healing?\n\n## Question 88 of 90\n\n- **A.** Osteocyte\n- **B.** Macrophages\n- **C.** Chondrocytes\n- **D.** Neutrophils\n- **E.** Osteoclasts\n\n## Question 57 of 58\n\n0% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\nStages of bone/fracture\nhealing:\nHematoma formation (Immediately in Day 1)\nInflammation and proliferation (8 hours to 1-2\nweeks) predominant cells neutrophils and\nmacrophages\nCallus formation (2-3 weeks)\nConsolidation (2 to 3 months)\nRe-modelling (2 months to years) Osteoclasts are\nresponsible.\nHigh yield:\nFactors that prevent efficient healing:\nMalnutrition\nInfection\nCorticosteroids therapy\nPoor blood supply\nComplications of Healing\nHypertrophic scar\nKeloid formation\nFailure to heal (abscess or empyema formation)\nFailure to unite (skin, muscle or fascia wound\nbreakdown)\nFracture complications\n\n## Question 58 of 58	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b8e0a75b-d267-4aa4-ab1e-3b8cdb20836e	66192b35-5116-4c41-a018-54b8468dc26e	muscles-of-the-neck	Muscles of the Neck	article	Platysma\nSuperficial muscle of the neck, covers most of the anterior and\nlateral aspect of the neck\nSternocleidomastoid muscle\nOne of the largest superficial muscles of the neck. Divides the\nneck into anterior and posterior triangle\nSuprahyoid and infrahyoid\nmuscles\nSuprahyoid muscles\n\n![Muscles of the Neck - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/muscles-of-the-neck/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Neck — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Neck - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/muscles-of-the-neck/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Muscles of the Neck — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nInfrahyoid muscles\nMusculature of tongue\n\n![Muscles of the Neck - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/muscles-of-the-neck/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Neck — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Neck - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/muscles-of-the-neck/figure_04_p2.png)\n*Muscles of the Neck — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Muscles of the Neck - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/muscles-of-the-neck/figure_05_p2.jpg)\n*Muscles of the Neck — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
eeb16a0d-f33a-4b6d-a249-be47d796cdfe	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-4	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4	article	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4\n\nB.LCD X O\n- **C)** LCX\n- **D)** LAD D\n- **E)** LCX and LAD\n- Explanation\n- ECG suggests extensive Anterior STEM' (acute)\n- ST elevation in VI-6 plus I and aVL (most rnarked in V2-4)\n- Minimal reciprocal ST depression in Ill and aVF\n- Q waves in V 1-2, reduced R wave height (a Q-wave equivalent) in V3-4\n- In anterior MI occluded coronary artery is LAD\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 49-year-old deep sea driver having decompression sickness. Which of the following is the least common in this condition? . Dizziness\n- **B)** Paresthesia x\n- **C)** Headache\n- **D)** Joint pain\n\nE\n- Itching\n- Explanation\n- In cutaneous decompression sickness, a transient pruritus can typically affect the distal upper or proximal lower limbs, the nose, or the ears. Allergies to dry- or wetsuit components may also cause itching.\n- Dysbarism Dysbarism includes decompression sickness, arterial gas embolism (ACE), and barotraumas\n- Decompression sickness is a complex of symptoms and disease caused by the forrnation of an excess of bubbles in body tissues.\n- This occurs after an exposure to breathing gases at increased pressure, followed by a reduction in environmental pressure.\n- Arterial gas embolism (ACE) refers to the precipitation of gas bubbles in the arterial circulation. Decompression illness encompasses decompression sickness and AGE.\n- Barotrauma is damage to tissues caused by the pressure-induced expansion and contraction of gases in enclosed spaces.\n- Nitrogen narcosis is a syndrome of reversible neurologic and behavioral changes produced by an increased partial pressure of nitrogen in the body.\n- Risk factors\n\nDecompression illness Diving or other activity involving increased atmospheric pressure Increasing depth and duration of dives Strenuous exercise during diving Repetitive diving dehydration Right-to-left shunt\n- Clinical features Barotrauma Breath-holding during ascent Lung disease Eustachian tube dysfunction Incorrect autoinflation Trapped gas in or near teeth Nitrogen narcosis deep diving - The risk of narcosis increases when diving beyond depths of 130 feet (40 m).\n- musculoskeletal pain(most often affecting the shoulder, elbow, or knee. Larger joints tend to be more involved than srnaller ones, and if multiple joints are affected they are usually unilateral and adjacent).\n- numbness\n- tingling\n- paresthesia\n- headache\n- dizziness or vertigo\n- Nausea and vomiting\n- Shortness of breath\n- Difficulty in walking\n- Pruritus (less common)\n- Paralysis (less common)\n- Rash (less common)\n- Reduced level of consciousness (less common)\n\n- Investigations\n- Chest x-ray — to assess pulmonary barotrauma, pneumothorax, pr pulmonary oedema\n- Consider FBC\n- Consider pure-tone audiometry\n- Consider facial x-ray\n- Consider ECG\n- Management\n\nDecompression illness\n- Follow ABCD protocol\n- High flow oxygen at 15L/min via a non- rebreathe mask regardless of their oxygen saturation.\n- Fluid—oral fluid tolerated, otherwise 1-2 litres Of IV fluids during the first post-injury hour is recommended.\n- Recompression Therapy\n- Recompression therapy is the definitive treatment of decompression illness.\n- The patient is re- pressurised in a chamber breathing 100% oxygen at a high partial pressure. Barotrauma\n- Middle ear\n- Middle-ear barotraumas can be treated with nasal or oral decongestants (ephedrine or pseudoephedrine) to reduce the inflammation.\n- Consider analgesia\n- Pulmonary\n- 100% oxygen is administered to correct arterial hypoxaemia, avoiding positive pressure ventilation where possible as this may exacerbate lung damage.\n- Pneumothorax: mild cases may resolve with normobaric 100% oxygen. Or insert a chest drain if there is evidence of severe pneumothorax.\n- Consider recompression therapy\n- Sinus\n- Oral or nasal decongestant\n- Analgesia\n- Consider intranasal corticosteroid\n- Consider endoscopic surgery Nitrogen narcosis\n- Ascent\n- This condition is entirely reversible by reduction Of ambient pressure (i.e., ascent to shallow depths).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nRegarding safety triangle of Chest drain which one of the following is included in safe triangle of chest drain?\n- **A)** Anterior border Of latissimus dorsi\n- **B)** Anterior border of Pectoralis major\n\nx\n- **C)** Posterior border of latissimus dorsi\n- **D)** Anterior border Of Serratus anterior\n- **E)** At the level Of Axilla\n- Explanation\n- Drain insertion site:\n- The most common pwtion for chest tube insertion is in the mid axillary line base of 6 rib or 5 intercostal space through the "safe triangle.\n- "safe triangle is the triangle bordered by the anterior border of the latissimus dorsi, the lateral border of the pectoralis major muscle, a line superior to the horizontal level of the nipple, and an apex below the axilla.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 65-year-old female with history of RTA brought into ED by her daughter. Her X-ray is given. What is your initial appropriate action?\n- **A)** Refer to Orthopedic X B\n- Pelvic binder\n- **C)** CT pelvis\n- **D)** IV fluids and antibiotics\n- **E)** Activate massive transfusion protocol\n- Explanation\n- Pelvic fractures are a life-threatening orthopaedic emergency; therefore, achieving pelvic stability is an early and critical goal in order to decrease bleeding, decrease pain, improve mobility and allow for transfers.\n- The pelvic binder is used for the emergency stabilisation of pelvic fractures and haemorrhage control before definitive treatment.\n- Managernent-otpelvic fracture\n- Initiate rapid hemorrhagic control to stabilize the pelvis with pelvic binder (devices) to reduce pelvic volume and stabilize the fracture end.\n- Pelvic binder diminishes blood loss in both open-book and vertical shear pelvic fracture however, longitudinal traction can also be applied in vertical shear injury.\n- Provide appropriate resuscitation with crystalloid, blood, and blood products.\n- Consider angiography early in a hemodynamically unstable patient with a pelvic fracture, after other sources of bleeding have been excluded.\n- Consultation should be made with experienced trauma surgeon to construct therapeutic plan\n- Early transfer to traurna center should be considered in limited resources environment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 35-year-old lady developed sudden onset occipital headache associated with multiple episodes of vomiting. She is currently on OCP. She just had her hair wash in the local saloon yesterday. What is the most likely the cause?\n- **A)** Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n- **B)** Cluster headache\n- **C)** Vertebral artery dissection\n- **D)** Venous sinus thrombosis\n- **E)** Stroke\n- Explanation\n- Beauty_parlor syndrome\n- Beauty parlor stroke syndrome (BPSS) is a rare condition characterized by mechanical impingement of a vertebral artery (VA) during neck rotation and/or hyperextension followed by vertebrobasilar insufficiency.\n- Vertebral\n\n- is a rare cause of stroke in the general population; however, represents one of the more common causes of stroke in patients younger than 45 years of age. Spontaneous dissections have been reported. However, incidental minor trauma often precipitates this potentially dangerous condition. Often some neck distortion such as chiropractic manipulation, bending of the neck, or blunt trauma causes the dissection.\n- Risk factors\n- Neck distortion such as chiropractic manipulation, bending of the neck, or blunt trauma\n- Others\n- Yoga\n- Nose blowing\n- Painting the ceiling\n- Judo, wrestling\n- Hypertension\n- Fibromuscular dysplasia\n- Female gender\n- Use of the birth control pill Cystic medial necrosis\n- Vasculitis\n- Postpartum\n- Clinical features\n- Acute and severe unilateral neck pain and headache (minor neck trauma often precipitates the pain). The pain is typically in the occipital-cervical area of the neck or head.\n- Extracranial dissections often have a bruit present, this group patient doesn't have neurological deficit.\n\n- The majority of dissections are intracranial. It is estimated that vertebral artery dissection may be responsible for at least 20% of ischemic strokes in young people.\n- Neurological symptoms are often delayed and may not be present at all. However, 70% of patients will have some type of neurological deficit which may present late.\n- When the dissection is intracranial, a subarachnoid hemorrhage is present 50% of the time.\n- This group of patients has severe\n- Nystagm us\n- Truncal ataxia\n- Ipsilateral loss of taste (hypogeusia), horner syndrome, impairment of proprioception and fine touch\n- Contralateral impairment in thermal and pain sensation in the extremities\n- Tongue deviation to the side of the lesion\n- Internuclear ophthalmoplegia\n- Investigations\n- CT and CTA first choice\n- MRI may detect an intimal flap and luminal thrombosis.\n- A cerebral angiogram may be required if the MRI and CT scan have failed to reveal any pathology.\n- Management\n- The goal of management is to prevent stroke, which is the complication of vertebral artery dissection.\n- The mainstay of treatment is anticoagulation, typically heparin. If there are no contraindications, then thrombolytic therapy can be started as long as the time is within 4.5 hours from the onset of symptoms.\n\n- Endovascular therapy is best reserved for patients who are not candidates for thrombolytics and/or have a subarachnoid hemorrhage.\n- Complications\n- Cerebellar and brain stem infarction\n- Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n- Vertebral artery pseudoaneurysm leading to compression neuropathy of the cranial nerves.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 2-year-old boy brought into ED by his mother with a two day history of barking cough and shortness of breath which is worse at night. He has had a mild fever. In the ED, he has audible stridor and has a hoarse voice. What is most likely causative organism? x A Parvovirus B19\n- **B)** Adenovirus\n- **C)** Haemophilus type b\n- **D)** Respiratory syncytial Virus   E\n- Parainfluenza\n- Explantion\n- Croup\n\n- Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a common respiratory disease that typically occurs in children aged between 6 months and 3 years. use\n- Parainfluenza virus types 1 or 3\n- Clinical features\n- Sudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which associated with:\n- Hoarseness\n- Stridor\n- Respiratory distress. Symptoms are typically worse at night and increase with agitation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 12-year-old boy brought into ED by his mother with complain of abdominal pain, lethargy and Vomiting. Blood gases revealed severe metabolic acidosis pH less than 7.1 and blood glucose high with ketonemia. According to paediatric DKA management guideline what is the insulin regime in this specific child?\n- **A)** 0.05 unit/kg/hr X\n- **B)** 20 units' stat dose\n- **D)** 0.03 unit/kg/hr\n- **E)** IO units' stat dose\n\nExplanation As the pH is less than 7.1, it's the severe case of OKA, therefore, commence insulin at the rate of 0.1 unit/kg/hour.\n- Severity-of-PKA\n- Mild - 5% dehydrated with Ph less than 7.3 Or Bicarbonate less than 15mmol/L\n- Moderate - 5% dehydrated with Ph less than 7.2 Or Bicarbonate less than 10mmol/L\n- Severe -10% dehydrated with Ph less than 7.1 or Serum Bicarbonate less than 5mmol/L\n- Treatment protocol\n- Use long-acting Insulin (Lantus)\n- Insulin 0.05 to 0.1 unit/kg/hour\n- Consider O.lunit/kg/hour in Severe Cases (i-e pH less than 7.1 or Serum bicarbonate less than 5mmol/L)\n- Always start insulin after 1 hour treating with IV NS 0.9 if started earlier or given in lv bolus or in fluid bag can result into Cerebral edema\n- Consider 5% dextrose along with insulin infusion if blood glucose less than 14mmol\n- Consider 10% glucose & NS 0.9 along with insulin infusion if blood glucose falls less than 6mmol/L\n- Consider 2ml/kg bolus of 10% dextrose if blood sugar falls less than 4mmol/L and temporarily pause the insulin infusion\n- Continue iv insulin and fluid therapy until pH normal or blood ketones less than Immol/L\n- Clinical condition improved able to eat and drink\n- Start subcutaneous insulin after 30 minutes of stopping insulin infusion.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 45-year-old male patient with history of facial trauma brought to the ED with signs of acute airway obstruction. What is the initial step of the management?\n- **A)** Call surgery team\n- **B)** Ventilation with bag valve mask\n- **C)** Cricothyroidotomy for ventilation x   D\n- Refer to ICU\n- **E)** Bring trolley for difficult intubation\n- Explanation\n- Patient with facial trauma or airway obstruction are more likely difficult to be intubated, therefore, rapid accessible to difficult airway equipment is recor-nmended and they are setting up in a difficult airway trolley (DAT).\n- Difficult-airway-trolley-(PAT)\n- Recommendation, at least local, standardization of the DAT, so that identical, adequate equipment can rapidly be accessed anywhere in the hospital where general anesthesia or airway management is provided (such as ICUs, emergency departments, operating theatres, and angiography labs). The location of the DAT should be clearly marked.\n\n- The Difficult Airway Society (DAS) webpage provides an excellent resource for "setting up a difficult airway trolley"\n- What does the ideal DAT look like? Top work surface and 4-5 drawers\n- Mobile\n- Robust\n- Stocked in a logical sequence\n- Clearly labelled\n- Easily cleaned\n- Attached documentation - DAS/modified local guidelines, Checklist for restocking, Logbook for daily checking\n- Reproducible\n- Top-of-trolley\n\n- Flexible intubating fibrescope - portable scope with battery light source, portable stack system e.g. Storz Tele Pack or single use fibrescope e.g. Ambu aScope\n- Side-of-trolley.\n- Bougies — adult and paediatric\n- Aintree Intubation Catheter\n- Airway exchange catheter\n- Drawer I — plan A Optimise head position . Bougie\n- Alternative laryngoscope nten\n- Bougie (ideally on side of trolley)\n- Short handle laryngoscope\n- McCoy blade and/or straight blade\n- Videolaryngoscope\n\nintestO\n- Drawer 2 — Plan B\n- LMA (SAD)\n- Followed by fibreoptic tracheal intubation through LMA\n- Contents\n- LMA #3,4,5 &/or 2nd generation device #3,4,5\n- Intubating LMA (iLMA) #3,4,5\n- Aintree Intubating Catheter (ideally on side of trolley)\n- Fibreoptic adjuvants*\n\n- Drawer 3- Plan C Bag mask ventilation +/- airway adjuncts Supraglottic airway device\n- Contents\n- Facemasks — various sizes\n- Oropharyngeal airways — various sizes\n- Nasopharyngeal airways — various sizes\n- LMA/Proseal LMA - #3,4,5\n\n- Drawer 4- Plan D\n- Surgical cricothyroidotomy\n- Large bore cannula device eg. Quicktrach I or II (VBM)\n- Scalpel (NO. 20 blade)\n- Tracheal dilator or tracheal hook\n- Bougie\n- Cuffed tracheal tube #6 & 7\n\n- Drawer 5- Plan D\n- Cannula cricothyroidotomy\n- Contents\n- Kink-resistant jet ventilation cannula e.g. Ravussin (VBM)\n- High &/or low pressure ventilation system\n- e.g. Manujet Ill (VBM) Ventrain (Ventinova)\n\nIII\n- Note- you don't need to memorise equipment. 10 / 10 IO. You are asked to review a 5-year-old girl brought to the ED by her parents. She is crying uncontrollably after falling from a trampoline. On assessment, she is unable to bear weight on her right leg. Her X-ray is given What's the classification on salter Harris type of fracture?   A Salter-Harris type Ill x\n- **B)** Salter-Harris type IV\n- **C)** Salter-Harris type V\n- **D)** Salter-Harris type II\n- **E)** Salter-Harris type I\n- Explanation Salter-harris Classification When the epiphysis separates from the metaphysis or fracture plane passes all the way through the growth plate. not involving the bone. cornmon)fracture line extends from physis to metaphysis upgA!Lfracture line extends from physis to epiphysis upgUfracture line extends from metaphysis. growth plate and down through the epiphysis uæy—Crushing injury to the physis\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nA 30-year-old male after sustaining burn injury on his legs. On arriving to the ED, he complaint of pain and tenderness of right legs. You are suspecting compartment syndrome. Which of the following is the earliest sign of compartment Syndrome?\n- **A)** Paralysis Of the limb\n- **B)** Pallor of skin\n- **C)** Loss of distal pulses\n- **D)** Ischemia\n- **E)** Pain on passive stretch Explanation\n- Clinical features\n- Pain - An early sign, especially with passive stretching of the muscles in the involved compartment.\n- pressure (muscle tightness) - Presence of tightness in the compartment is the earliest objective finding.\n- Paraesthesia - Early sign Pulselessness - Pulse examination may be normal, and unless an artery is injured (late sign)\n- Pallor - caused by vascular compromise (late sign)\n- paralysis - It is caused by prolonged nerve compression and ischaemia, or irreversible muscle damage (late sign)\n- Mneumonic 6Ps\n- Compartment syndrome Definition\n- Compartment syndrome is a pathological condition characterised by elevated interstitial pressure in a closed osteofascial compartment that results in microvascular compromise (restriction of capillary blood flow).\n- The compartments most commonly involved are those with relatively non-compliant osseous or fascial structures, such as the anterior and deep posterior compartments of the leg and the volar compartment of the forearm.\n\n- Risk factors Trauma_2Extremity fracture, soft-tissue trauma (with or without fracture), reperfusion injury, and/or penetrating trauma can lead to intracompartmental haemorrhage, vascular compromise, and/or muscle oedema with subsequent elevation of intracompartmental interstitial pressure.\n- Bleeding as haemophilia\n- of tight casts, dressings, or external wrappings can lead to increased external pressure, which subsequently increases the intracompartmental interstitial pressure.\n- thermal cases of circumferential burn eschar, there is a high risk of intracompartmental interstitial pressure elevation due to the restricting effect of the eschar on the swollen muscles.\n- Intense muscular activity_2Chronic exertional compartment syndrome is most frequently encountered among long-distance runners and other sports with intense muscular activity. - Infused solution that does not get absorbed can lead to elevation of intracompartmental interstitial pressure.\n- venous obstruction - Iliac venous obstruction secondary to DVT, tumour embolus, or extrinsic compression.\n- Clinical features\n- Pain - An early sign, especially with passive stretching of the muscles in the involved compartment.\n- pressure (muscle tightness) - Presence of tightness in the compartment is the earliest objective finding.\n- Paraesthesia - Early sign\n- Pulselessness - Pulse examination may be normal, and unless an artery is injured (late sign)\n\n- Pallor - caused by vascular compromise (late sign)\n- paralysis - It is caused by prolonged nerve compression and ischaemia, or irreversible muscle damage (late sign)\n- Mneurnonic 6Ps\n- Inyestigations st\n- compartment pressure measurement (1 investigation) - Pressure should be measured in all compartments and at multiple sites, particularly within 5 cm of the level of injury, and the highest measurement obtained should dictate management decisions.\n- For patients with suspected chronic exertional pallor, pressure should be measured after an episode of exertion. If specialised equipment is unavailable, a 16-gauge IV cannula connected to an arterial BP transducer and monitor via saline-filled arterial line tubing can be used to measure compartment pressures.\n- The differential pressure (i.e., the difference between diastolic BP and measured compartment pressure: diastolic BP minus ment pressure) is measured.\n- Differential pressure within 20-30 mmHg of the diastolic pressure (delta pressure) is considered a strong indicator for fasciotomy. However, care should be taken when using this criterion for patients who are receiving vasodilatory medications whose diastolic BP is low.\n- Other-investigations\n- serum creatine kinase\n- Urine myoglobin . Management\n\nAcute with occlusive dressing\n- Casts or o i ngs Sh be split completely. Padding or circumferential dresengs Should be released immediatelv Analgesia (morphine). Fasciotomy— Performed Within a 6-hour window from the initiation Of compartment syndrome. delayed diagnosis leads to significant muscle necrosis, amputation need to be If acute with occlusive dressing and With rhabdo m vo VSis:\n- Include above measures add hydration therapy urine alkalinization and consider haemodialysis  Acute without occlusive dressing\n- Fasciotomy — Performed Within a 6\n- hour window from the initiation of compa rtment synd rome. Amputation\n- If delayed diagnosis leads to significant muscle necrosis, amputation may need to be considered. acute with occlusive dressingand with rha bd Om v OIVSis: Include above measures add hydration therapy\n- /\n- urine alkalinization and consider haemodialysis Next Chronic\n- Exercise lirnitation and NSAIDs ibuprofen)\n- Fasciotomy\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n\n![High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4 - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-4/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4 — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4 - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-4/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4 — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4 - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-4/figure_03_p2.jpeg)\n*High-Yield Clinical SBA 