4cea3f96-4aa9-4bcf-b228-7a191d0554f6	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	brief-resolved-unexplained-event-brue	Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE)	article	# Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- It's an episode in an infant less than 12 months of age characterized by:\n- <1 minute duration (typically 20-30s)\n- A return to baseline state\n- Not explained by any identifiable medical condition (i.e. medically well).\n- Includes one or more of the following features:\n- Central cyanosis/pallor\n- Absent, decrease or irregular breathing\n- Marked change in tone (hyper- or hypotonia)\n- Altered level of consciousness\n\n## Differential Diagnosis\n- Gastro-esophageal reflux\n- infection\n- Epilepsy\n- Non-accidental injury\n- Arrhythmias (long QT)\n- Myocarditis\n- Metabolic error\n\n- Always take complete history from parents or eye witnessed including general description of event eg: choking gagging , last feed.\n- Gestational history, social history, family history.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\n- ABCDE assessment\n- exposure check for bruising, bleeding from nose/mouth, torn frenulum and subconjunctival hemorrhage.\n- measure weight, length and head circumference\n- Any dysmorphic features that might indicate underlying congenital abnormalities\n- Note: always documents any normal or negative finding in examination such as Mongolian blue spot.\n\n## Risk Stratification\n- —Investigation & management Gastro-esophageal reflux disease\n- It's one of the commonest causes of symptoms similar to a BRUE presentation, causing symptoms via laryngospasm. It is an important one to exclude as it is a very treatable cause of a presentation similar to a BRUE, and may cause repeated episodes.\n- Features from the history that are suggestive of it are:\n\n### Low-Risk Criteria\n- days of age Born\n- 32 weeks' gestation and have a corrected age of\n- 45 weeks. No CPR given by trained practitioner <1 minute duration First event\n-\n\n### High-Risk Features (Red Flags)\n- Infant< 2 months History of prematurity less than 32 weeks at birth More than one event\n- Episode occurred whilst infant was awake and supine\n- Occurred during feeding or shortly after feeding\n- Regurgitation or vomiting at time of event\n- Obstructive apnea (infant making respiratory efforts but not breathing successfully).\n- Note: Always consider NAI (e.g: drug ingestion, factitious illness, suffocation), especially if there are multiple or changing versions of the history\n\n### Low-Risk Criteria\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nMay obtain pertussis testing and 12 lead ECG. Briefly monitor with pulse oximetry\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nEducate caregiver about BRUE and engage in shared decision & follow- up. Offer resources and CPR training previOUS  Next High risk Investigation: For high risk patients do a capillary blood gas and ECG as a minimum, and keep these patients on a cardiorespiratory monitor\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nReferral to Paediatrics for a 24-hour period of monitoring +/\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\n.\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/brief-resolved-unexplained-event-brue/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/brief-resolved-unexplained-event-brue/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/brief-resolved-unexplained-event-brue/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/brief-resolved-unexplained-event-brue/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Brief Resolved Unexplained Event (BRUE) — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
2eac0ccd-5b4f-4545-bbad-e3c980ff42f6	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	acute-appendicitis	Acute Appendicitis	article	# Acute Appendicitis\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Children — infants and young children may present with non-specific abdominal pain and anorexia, and may appear withdrawn.\n- Older age —there may be minimal pain or fever; may present with acute confusion or shock.\n- Pregnancy — there may be displacement of the appendix by the gravid uterus. In the later stages of pregnancy, may present with right upper quadrant or right flank pain. Nausea and vomiting may be mistaken for pregnancy-related symptoms.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\nTenderness in the right lower quadrant,\n- The site of maximal tenderness is typically over 'McBurney's point', which lies two- thirds of the way along a line drawn from the umbilicus to the anterior superior iliac spine.\n- Children may present with right lower quadrant pain on coughing or hopping\n- A palpable abdominal mass may suggest an appendix mass or abscess.\n- Abdominal distension, guarding (muscular rigidity), and rebound tenderness or percussion tenderness may suggest peritonitis.\n- Consider-testing-focperitonealsigns-specif.ic-for-acute-appendici.tis:\n- Rovsing's sign — palpation of the left lower quadrant increases the pain felt in the right lower quadrant.\n- Psoas sign — passive extension of the right thigh with the person in the left lateral position elicits pain in the right lower quadrant.\n- Obturator sign — passive internal rotation of the flexed right thigh elicits pain in the right lower quadrant.\n- The-anatomical-position-of-theappendix\n- A retrocaecal/retrocolic appendix may present with right loin pain and tenderness, and a positive psoas test.\n\n- A pre-ileal and post-ileal appendix may present with vomiting and diarrhoea (due to irritation of the distal ileum).\n- A subcaecal and pelvic appendix may present with suprapubic pain and urinary frequency; diarrhoea and tenesmus\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nFull blood count — neutrophil-predominant leucocytosis is present in 80-90% of people with appendicitis.\n- C-reactive protein (CRP).\n- Urine dipstick test — to help exclude a urinary tract infection (UT I).\n- Pregnancy test— to exclude ectopic pregnancy and other pregnancy-related conditions. Ultrasonography, abdominal computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) are most commonly used.\n- Ultrasound should be the preferred imaging modality in children, pregnant, and breastfeeding women.\n- MRI scans are mainly reserved for pregnant women when ultrasound is non- diagnostic\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nNonsurgical\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nIV fluid and antibiotic can be a safe and effective approach in selected patients with uncomplicated acute appendicitis\n- Surgical\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nRemoval of the appendix is the gold standard treatrnent for uncomplicated appendicitis.\n- Complications\n- Perforation\n\n- Appendix mass\n- Appendix abscess\n- Sepsis\n- Bowel obstruction previOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Acute Appendicitis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-appendicitis/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Acute Appendicitis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a4bf4630-803d-4772-84c6-c4c77a0cb588	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	alcoholic-ld	Alcoholic Liver Disease & Decompensation	article	# Alcoholic Liver Disease & Decompensation\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nIn advanced liver disease\n- Bloating and weight gain (ascites)\n- Consciousness disturbance (hepatic encephalopathy)\n- Vomiting blood or black stools (bleeding from the gastrointestinal tract)\n- flapping tremor\n- Lower extremity swelling.\n- Dupuytren's contracture\n- Finger clubbing\n- skin telangiectasia\n-\n- Diagnosis Medical history\n- A comprehensive drinking history provided by the patient and family members is\n- critical. Questionnaires commonly used to assess the presence of alcohol dependence are the alcohol use disorders identification test (AUDIT), AUDIT-C, and CAGE. The CAGE questionnaire is easier to use, but the AUDIT and AUDIT-C are more reliable\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\nPatients with advanced alcoholic liver disease can present with manifestations of\n- portal hypertension, including: ascites\n- splenomegaly\n- Venous collateral circulation.\n-\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nLFTs- Serum aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) are markers of alcoholic liver injury.\n- AST levels are almost always elevated (usually higher than ALT levels) in patients with alcoholic liver disease. The usual ratio is AST/ALT>2, which occurs in about 70% of cases.\n- Elevated levels of both CGT and ALP suggest that the liver is the source of ALP\n- Albumin and bilirubin are markers of hepatocyte function. Low albumin reflects impaired hepatic synthesis; elevated bilirubin in the absence of biliary obstruction is usually the result of substantial liver disease.\n- FBC — Anemia is common in patients with alcoholic liver disease and has a variety of triggers, including iron deficiency, gastrointestinal bleeding, folate deficiency, hemolysis, and hypersplenism.\n- Prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and increased international normalised ratio (INR) suggest decreased hepatic synthetic function seen in advanced liver diseases such as cirrhosis or liver failure. Hyponatremia often occurs in patients with advanced cirrhosis. Hypokalemia and hypophosphatemia are common causes of myasthenia in alcoholic liver disease.\n- Serum laboratory tests to rule out other major diseases include: Viral hepatitis serology test (hepatitis A, hepatitis B, hepatitis C).\n- diagnostic and helpful in grading ALD in patients with a history of alcohol abuse, but is not required in every patient Management\n- The main goal of treatment is to reduce liver damage caused by alcohol overuse and prevent liver disease progression.\n- Other goals of treatment include reducing alcoholic liver disease-related symptoms and preventing complications.\n\n- Alcohol withdrawal\n- It is important for ALD management\n- Abstinence or simply reducing alcohol intake can improve the expected survival of patients with ALD, and even those with decompensated cirrhosis. Abstinence from alcohol reduces the risk of steatosis, fibrosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma.\n- Alcohol withdrawal syndrome (AWS) may occur when alcohol is withdrawn. Mild or moderate AWS can appear within 6 to 24 hours of a patient's last drink and may progress to more severe conditions\n\nMild AWS high blood pressure and tachycardia Anorexia, anxiety, mood swings, insomnia, irritability, sweating, headache, and fine tremors. Moderate AWS Mild AWS aggravation, plus, Agitation and Gross Tremor Severe AWS/delirium tremens moderate AWS exacerbation, plus, Confusion/delirium Generalized tonic- clonic seizures (in some patients this may be the initial presentation of AWS) auditory, visual or tactile hallucinations Hyperthermia secondary to psychomotor agitation.\n- Long-acting benzodiazepines (eg, diazepam) have better protection against seizures and delirium.\n- short-acting benzodiazepines (eg, oxazepam, lorazepam) Safer for the elderly and patients with liver dysfunction.\n- lose weight , quit smoking\n\n- Immunization — Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines are recommended for patients with chronic ALD.\n- If hepatitis B surface antibodies and hepatitis A immunoglobulin G (IgG) antibodies are negative, hepatitis A and B immunization should be considered in patients with all stages of ALD.\n- Review medications and note whether any changes to drugs or dosage are required in relation to liver function.\n- Nutritional-supplements— — advise him healthy eating and diet\n- Enteral nutrition is recommended. Enteral tube feeding should be considered if the patient cannot consume adequate calories by mouth. . Medical therapy_\n- Consider corticosteroid (prednisolone)\n- Pentoxifylline — guidelines do not recommend the use of pentoxifylline for the treatment of severe alcoholic hepatitis.\n- Ascites treatment\n- Diagnostic aspiration should be performed in patients with ascites to rule out spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.\n- Advise sodium-restricted diet.\n- A moderately salt-restricted diet (2 g sodium per day) alone may be effective in patients with milder disease.\n- Consider diuretic (furosemide combined with spironolactone) along with sodium- restricted diet if liver function worsens.\n- In massive ascites — a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) may be required.\n\n- Treatment of concomitant hepatitis C\n- The use of antiviral therapy in ALD patients with hepatitis C infection should be determined on an individual basis. Interferon or other antiviral drugs are generally not recommended for patients with active alcohol abuse,\n- Antiviral therapy may improve efficacy after alcohol withdrawal.\n- Terminal disease\n- Liver transplantation should be considered in patients with end-stage ALD.\n- ALD patients must be screened for alcohol-related comorbidities, and most transplant centers require patients to have a clear history of alcohol abstinence for at least 6 months. previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Alcoholic Ld - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/alcoholic-ld/figure_01_p5.jpeg)\n*Alcoholic Ld — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Alcoholic Ld - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/alcoholic-ld/figure_02_p7.jpeg)\n*Alcoholic Ld — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 7 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
55117329-db90-47d9-b358-aee5f7da4d5a	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	hyper-and-hypokalemia	Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia	article	# Potassium Disorders: Hyperkalaemia & Hypokalaemia\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nurine dipstick creatine kinase (if rhabdomyolysis is a possibility)\n-\n- Prolonged PR interval\n- Peaked T-waves\n-\n- Widening of the QRS Reduced or lost P wave\n- Sine wave pattern\n- AV dissociation\n- Asystole\n- Bradycardia\n- Example-I Example\n- 2\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nalgorithm (July-2022 guideline)\n\nI Assess using ABCDE approach 12-lead ECG and monitor cardiac rhythm if serum potassium (K+) 6.0 mmol/l Exclude pseudohyperkalaemia Give empirical treatment tor arrhythmia if hyperkalaemia suspected MILD K+5.5-5.g m mol/L Consider cause and Hert into cells MODERATE K + 6.0-6.4 mmol/L guided by clinical cmditim. ECG and of ECG Changes? Peaked T waves Broad QRS Bradycardia Flat/ absent P waves No 6.5 mmol/L Calcium Chloride OR Calcium Gluconate IV Insulin—Glucose IV Infusion: Give IO units soluble insulin in 25 g glucose over 15-30 min REDUCE THE RISK OF HYPOGLYCAEMIA 'f pre-treatment blood glucose < 7.0mmol/', follow with glucose infusion: 10% glucose   50ml/hr for 5 hrs (25g) Salbutamol IO — 20 mg Nebulised Calcium resonium 15g tds orally OR 30g bd per rectum Life threatening Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate log tds for 72 hrs OR patiromer 8.4B once daily Consider Dialysis Seek expert help\n\nand medical therapy Monitor serum K + and blood glucose\n- Dose Regimen IV Calcium (6.8 mmol)\n- IO ml 10% Calcium Chloride IV OR 30 ml 10% Calcium Gluconate IV\n-\n- Should be given use large IV access and give over 5 min Repeat ECG then\n- Glucose Regimen (25ggIucose)\n- 50ml glucose OR 125ml 20% glucose QR 250ml glucose\n- Complications Cardiac arrest\n- Weakness\n-\n- Arrhythmias Pseudo-hyperkalaemia\n- You must exclude psudohyperkalaemia before treating patient for hyperkalaemia\n- Pseudo-hyperkalaemia can be excluded by performing simultaneous rneasurements of plasma K+ in a lithium heparin anti-coagulated specimen and in a clotted sampled\n\n-\n- The importance of recognition of pseudo-hyperkalaemia is the avoidance of unnecessary treatment which could cause harm\n- Causes of pseudohyperkalemia prolonged transit time to the laboratory or poor storage conditions (most common)\n- difficult venepuncture,\n- A high platelet count, haemolysis, erythrocytosis, prolonged storage time of clotted samples, or cold storage conditions sampling from the arm into which potassium-containing fluids are being infused.\n- Recognition of pseudohyperkalaemia Pseudo-hyperkalaemia is detected when the serum K+ level exceeds that of the plasma by more than 0.4 mmol/L. Consider pseudo-hyperkalaemia in the context of normal renal function, normal ECG and in patients with haematological disorders.\n- It pseudo-hyperkalaemia is suspected ,Send paired blood samples in a clotted tube (serum) and a lithium heparin tube (plasma). Send FBC to exclude a haematological disorder. Pseudo-hyperkalaemia is present if: [Serum K+ — [Plasma\n- 0.4 mmol/l.\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Hyper and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hyper-and-hypokalemia/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Hyper and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Hyper and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hyper-and-hypokalemia/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Hyper and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Hyper and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hyper-and-hypokalemia/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Hyper and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Hyper and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hyper-and-hypokalemia/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Hyper and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Hyper and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hyper-and-hypokalemia/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n*Hyper and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Hyper and Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hyper-and-hypokalemia/figure_06_p7.jpeg)\n*Hyper and Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 7 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d2a13d29-8183-4162-ada6-c7930e3f3921	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	cont-98-food-barcode	Cont 98 Food Barcode	article	# Cont 98 Food Barcode\n\nAlKashfi Nour Mustafa\nCONTRACTOR\nNajran Cement Company 9204 King Saud Road\nNajran 66256-3579Saudi Arabia\nTel.: +966 (017) 529-9990\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n\n![Cont 98 Food Barcode - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/cont-98-food-barcode/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Cont 98 Food Barcode — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![Cont 98 Food Barcode - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/cont-98-food-barcode/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Cont 98 Food Barcode — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00
11841482-bb4a-4fe1-9eea-499ad4fdef78	66192b35-5116-4c41-a018-54b8468dc26e	structure-of-the-eyeball-and-eyelid	Structure of the Eyeball and Eyelid	article	Structure of the Eyeball\nThe eyeball consists of 3 coats:\n\n### 1. External protective fibrous coat: Made up of anterior\n\ntransparent part the cornea and a posterior opaque part the\nsclera\n\n### 2. middle: vascular pigmented coat consists from behind\n\nforward.\nChoroid\nCiliary body (connects the choroid to iris)\nIris: Circular pigmented diaphragm, its aperture known as\nthe pupil, lies Infront of the lens, divides space between\ncornea and lens into anterior and posterior chambers\n\n### 3. Internal nervous coat: Called the Retina\n\nOptic Disc: Where the optic nerve leaves the retina. Optic\ndisc is pierced by the central artery of the retina.\nContents of the Eyeball: Consists of aqueous humour, lens\nand vitreous body\nAqueous Humour: Fills anterior and posterior chambers of eye\nLens: Transparent and Bi-Convex\nVitreous body: Fills the eyeball behind the lens.\nSupports posterior surface of lens.\nBlood Supply: Opthalmic artery\nCentral retinal artery is a branch of ophthalmic artery, and\nOpthalmic artery is a first major branch of internal carotid\nartery\nVenous drainage: Into Cavernous sinus\nMovements of the eyeball (Extra\nocular muscles)\nAll the extraocular muscles are innervated by oculomotor\nnerve except LR-6 and S0-4\n\nEye Lid:\nThe eyelids are thin, soft tissue structures that cover the\neyeball anteriorly. They offer protection from excessive light\nor injury and maintain lubrication by distributing tears over\nthe surface of the eyeball. The eyelids split into upper and\nlower portions, which meet at the medial and lateral canthi\nof the eye. The opening between the two eyelids is called the\npalpebral aperture.\nThe eyelid consists of five main layers (superficial to deep):\nSkin and subcutaneous tissue (Most superficial structure)\nOrbicularis oculi\nTarsal plates\nLevator apparatus\nConjunctiva\nOrbicularis oculi (Closes the eyelids): The orbicularis oculi\nmuscle is innervated by the branches of facial nerve and\ncloses the eyelids. Loss of function of the orbicularis oculi\ncauses an inability to close the eyelids tightly and a drooping\nof the lower eyelid (ectropion) resulting in spillage of tears.\nThis leads to drying of the cornea. The orbicularis oculi\nmuscle has three distinct parts: palpebral, lacrimal and\norbital part.\nActions:\nPalpebral part: gently closes the eyelids.\nLacrimal part: involved in the drainage of tears.\n\n![Structure of the Eyeball and Eyelid - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/head-neck-anatomy/structure-of-the-eyeball-and-eyelid/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Structure of the Eyeball and Eyelid — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nOrbital part: tightly closes the eyelids.\nOther muscles: (High Yield)\nThe levator palpebrae superioris muscle: innervated by the\noculomotor nerve and acts to raise the upper eyelid. Loss of\nfunction of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle results in\na complete ptosis.\nThe superior tarsal muscle is innervated by postganglionic\nsympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion, which\nhelps to maintain upper eyelid opening. Loss of function of\nthe superior tarsal muscle results in a partial ptosis.\nTarsal plate: The tarsal plates are located deep to the\norbicularis oculi muscle. There are two plates; the superior\ntarsus (upper eyelid) and inferior tarsus (lower eyelid).\nThe tarsal plate in the eyelid provides structural support,\nserves as an attachment site for muscles, houses meibomian\nglands, and helps distribute tears, distributed tear film can\nhelp flush away foreign particles and potential pathogens,\nreducing the risk of infection.\nIn the tarsal plates lie the Meibomian glands (also known as\ntarsal glands). These are a specialised type of sebaceous\ngland that secretes an oily substance onto the eye to slow\nthe evaporation of the eye's tear film. The oily substance also\nprevents the eyelids from sticking together when closed.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Dysfunction in these glands, often due to**\n> blockages or inflammation, can lead to reduced tear stability\n> and dry eye.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
a7155aef-b5ea-4da5-814b-cb6cade4e9b7	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	calcium-absorption	Calcium Absorption	article	Calcium absorption\nAbsorption of calcium in small intestine depends on\npresence of adequate amounts of the active form of vitamin\nD, 1 ,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, which is produced in the\nkidney. 1 ,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the synthesis of\nan intestinal Ca+2 -binding protein, calbindin D-28K\nVitamin D deficiency or chronic renal failure results in\ninadequate intestinal Ca+ absorption, causing rickets in\nchildren and osteomalacia in adults.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
7a5abf67-a585-4487-b30f-d2339e11900c	159f090b-a89b-46d0-8bd6-6ea65653f830	updated-cv-elmokashfy-corrected	Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected	article	# Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected\n\n7 Nov 2018 – 4 Nov 2019\n20 Aug 2020 – 10 Sep 2021\n18 Aug 2020 – 9 Sept 2021\n10 Sep 2021 – 1 Oct 2022\n\nGENERAL PRACTITIONER DOCTOR | PHYSICIAN | ADMINISTRATION OFFICER\nCommitted and proactive Doctor posed to leverage 2+ years of internship and professional experience in medical services,\ngeneral surgery, medical record management, outbreak support, and relief operations. An all-rounder intellect enriched with\nthe latest treatments, medicines, trends, and techniques of the healthcare industry.\n\nIndustry Exposure: Hospitals & Healthcare Organizations/Administration Departments\n\nPROFILE SUMMARY\n\nProvided leadership to the medical & paramedical staff & integrated their\nefforts to optimize overall operational productivity. Received\nrecommendations by doctors & patients for providing exceptional client\nservice.\nAchieved organizational objectives by collaborating with third-party\nadministrators (TPAs) and healthcare providers to avoid fraud and abuse\nand ensure proper medical treatment in an ethical manner.\nAdept at handling various emergencies and making split-second decisions\nto alleviate patients' suffering. Proven expertise in diagnosing and\nadministering treatments to a cross-section of patients with multiple\nailments.\nDemonstrated capabilities in managing in-Patients/OPD operations and\nexplaining treatment procedures. Skilled in collecting and maintaining the\nconfidentiality of patient information, including medical history and\nreports.\nExcellent bedside manner and admirable ability to establish rapport with\npatients; exceptional skills in imparting health teachings for a complete and\nspeedy recovery.\n\nEDUCATION & CREDENTIALS\n\nKey Impact Areas\nMedical Consultancy\nOPD Administration\nPediatric ER\nPatient Care & Counselling\nObstetrics & Gynecology\nDiagnosis/Treatment\n\nKey Skills\nEmotional Intelligence\nDecision-making\nAttention to detail\nMotivator Team Work\n\n2017\n\nBachelor of Medicine and Surgery (MBBS) from\nAl-Yarmouk College of Medical Sciences, Sudan\n2018\n\nBasic Life Support Course\nfrom Federal Ministry of Health, Sudan\n\nCAREER TIMELINE\n\nHouse Officer (Internship)\nSudanese Ministry of Health\n(Internship Programs in Soba University\nHospital and Turkish Teaching Hospital)\n\nMedical Officer, Medicine Department\nGeneral Directorate of Medical Service,\nRepublic of Sudan Ministry of Interior\n\nGP Doctor (PCR Collector)\nAtlanta Medical Complex Clinic,\nMinistry of Health, Khartoum, Sudan\nMedical Officer\nTurkish Teaching Hospital\nMinistry of Health\nDR. ELMOKASHFY ELNOOR\n+966572985789\n Elmokashfy93 gmail.com\n\nEXPERIENCE SUMMARY\n\nSep’21 – Oct’22:\nTurkish Teaching Hospital Ministry of Health\nMedical Officer\nMain Duties:\nPrescribed and administered treatment, therapy, medication, vaccination, and other specialized medical care to treat and\nprevent illness, disease, injury, especially chest and abdominal pain, cerebrovascular accidents, and trauma.\nAnalyzed lab reports, ECG, X-Rays, and CT scans & performed IV canulation, urinary catheterization, & nasogastric tube\ninsertion.\nPlayed a stellar role in managing and stabilizing ill patients.\nKeeping up to date with the latest treatments, medicines and medical developments.\n18 Aug’20 – 9 Sept’21: General Directorate of Medical Service, Republic of Sudan Ministry of Interior\nMedical Officer – ER Medicine Department\nMain Duties:\nPrescribed and administered treatment, therapy, medication, vaccination, and other specialized medical care to treat and\nprevent illness, disease, injury, especially chest and abdominal pain, cerebrovascular accidents, and trauma.\nAnalyzed lab reports, ECG, X-Rays, and CT scans & performed IV canulation, urinary catheterization, & nasogastric tube\ninsertion.\nPlayed a stellar role in managing and stabilizing ill patients.\nKeeping up to date with the latest treatments, medicines and medical developments.\n01 jan – 2023  - 10- january’2024:dar alshefa medical complex   , Ministry of Health, Khartoum, Sudan\nGP Doctor –\nMain Duties:\nPrescribed and administered treatment, therapy, medication, vaccination, and other specialized medical care to treat and\nprevent illness, disease, injury, especially chest and abdominal pain, cerebrovascular accidents, and trauma.\nAnalyzed lab reports, ECG, X-Rays, and CT scans & performed IV canulation, urinary catheterization, & nasogastric tube\ninsertion.\nPlayed a stellar role in managing and stabilizing ill patients.\nKeeping up to date with the latest treatments, medicines and medical developments.\n\nINTERNSHIP SUMMARY\n7 Nov’18 – 7 Feb’19: Soba University Hospital, Obst & Gynae Department Medicine Department\nCollected patient registration and order forms and provided testing information.\nAdvised patients about diet, hygiene, and methods for the prevention of disease.\n7 Feb’19 – 5 May’19: Turkish Teaching Hospital, Medicine Department Obstetrics & Gynecology Department\nAdroitly coordinated with external specialists and consultants during emergencies and specialized operations.\nScheduled pre-admission tests and pre-operative education on treatment procedures, obtaining detailed patient history and\ninsurance authorization, if any.\nClosely monitored indoor patients and performed necessary ward procedures and CPCR.\nPrescribed medical treatment & clinical drugs to the patients and followed up on post-operative care as advised by surgeons.\nAssessed, diagnosed & treated various obstetric emergencies, including miscarriage, hyperemesis gravidarum, and eclampsia.\nEnsured all treatments done were successful and advised patients for further review, testing, and diagnosis.\n5 May’19 – 7 Aug’19: Turkish Teaching Hospital Pediatrics Department\nConducted thorough examinations on newborns and young children to check and record their health and normal physical\ndevelopment.\nDiagnosed and treated common pediatric emergencies, including acute severe asthma, bronchiolitis, pneumonia, convulsions,\nmalaria, diarrhea, and dehydration.\nPlanned and executed medical care programs to aid in children and adolescents' mental and physical growth and development.\n8 Aug’19 – 4 Nov’13: Turkish Teaching Hospital, General Surgery Departments\nPlayed a key role in preparing patients for elective surgeries and managing the preparation of pre-operative patients.\nInteracted with physicians to ensure documentation was clear and supported coding assignments.\nInterfaced and coordinated with the medical department to ensure essential medicines and vaccines were available.\nMEDICAL LICENSE\n\nRegistration Number: 22110000060269\nRegistration Type: Full Registration\nLicensing Body: Sudan Medical Council\nCountry of Registration: Sudan\nMEDICAL LICENSE\n\nRegistration Number:23489753\nRegistration Type: professional\n\n### classification\n\nLicensing Body: Saudi commission  for\n\nhealth   specialist\nCountry of Registration: Saudi Arabia\n\nPERSONAL DETAILS\nDate of Birth: 18th June 1993\nLanguages Known: English and Arabic\nMarital Status: Single\nNationality: Sudanese\n\nADDITIONAL EXPERIENCE\nJun’24 – Present: Elia Clinic, Najran, Saudi Arabia\nGeneral Practitioner Doctor\nMain Duties:\n- Providing general outpatient medical care and first-line management of various acute and chronic conditions.\n- Diagnosing and managing common cases of chest infections, abdominal pain, hypertension, diabetes, and tra\n- Performing minor procedures such as IV cannulation, catheterization, and wound care.\n- Maintaining medical records in accordance with the Saudi Ministry of Health regulations.\n- Collaborating with nursing staff and specialists to ensure continuity of care and patient satisfaction.\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n\n![Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/updated-cv-elmokashfy-corrected/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/updated-cv-elmokashfy-corrected/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/resuscitation-critical-care/updated-cv-elmokashfy-corrected/figure_03_p1.png)\n*Updated Cv Elmokashfy Corrected — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 1 Reference)*\n	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00
878a101e-d844-4ebd-9cb6-abe2319db2b9	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-anal-pain-rectal-bleeding	Anal Pain & Rectal Bleeding Differential — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Anal Pain & Rectal Bleeding Differential — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old male presents with excruciating, sharp tearing pain during defecation, described as 'passing broken glass', followed by a dull throbbing ache lasting 2-3 hours and bright red blood streaking the toilet paper. On gentle separation of the buttocks, what is the most likely physical finding?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Anal Pain & Rectal Bleeding Differential)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/anal-pain-rectal-bleeding/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** An acute Anal Fissure, most commonly in the posterior midline of the anal verge\n- **B.** A thrombosed external haemorrhoid in the anterior midline\n- **C.** Anorectal melanoma\n- **D.** Perianal abscess\n- **E.** Intersphincteric fistula\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAnal fissure presents characteristically with severe, sharp tearing pain during defecation ('passing razor blades/glass') followed by post-defecation burning ache and bright red blood on tissue paper. Over 90% occur in the posterior midline (due to poor perfusion and elliptical arrangement of external sphincter fibers). Treatment includes high-fibre diet, stool softeners, and topical 0.4% GTN ointment or diltiazem.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the clinical significance of finding an anal fissure located OFF the midline (e.g. lateral positions) or multiple anal fissures?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Anal Pain & Rectal Bleeding Differential)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/anal-pain-rectal-bleeding/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** It strongly suggests underlying systemic pathology such as Crohn's disease, Tuberculosis, HIV, Syphilis, or Anal Carcinoma\n- **B.** It is the normal anatomical variant in young men\n- **C.** It indicates internal haemorrhoids\n- **D.** It is caused by simple constipation\n- **E.** It requires no investigation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTypical primary anal fissures are located in the posterior midline (90%) or anterior midline (10%). Fissures located laterally, off-midline, painless, or multiple are considered secondary fissures and warrant immediate investigation for Crohn's disease, sexually transmitted infections (syphilis, herpes, LGV), tuberculosis, or anal cancer.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 45-year-old woman presents with severe, constant throbbing perianal pain that prevents her from sitting, accompanied by fever (38.4C) and rigors. Examination reveals a tender, fluctuant, erythematous swelling adjacent to the anal verge. What is the definitive management?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Anal Pain & Rectal Bleeding Differential)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/anal-pain-rectal-bleeding/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Emergency Operative Incision and Drainage (I&D) under general anaesthesia\n- **B.** Oral amoxicillin and warm sitz baths at home\n- **C.** Application of topical hydrocortisone cream\n- **D.** Rubber band ligation\n- **E.** Wait 7 days for spontaneous rupture\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nA perianal abscess originates from infected cryptoglandular anal glands in the intersphincteric space. Antibiotics alone cannot penetrate the collection. Definitive treatment is urgent surgical incision and drainage to relieve pain, evacuate pus, and prevent progression to ischiorectal sepsis or supralevator extension. Approximately 30-50% will subsequently form an anal fistula (fistula-in-ano).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 34-year-old male with a history of chronic constipation presents with a sudden onset of a tender, blue-purple, marble-sized lump at the anal verge that appeared after heavy straining 18 hours ago. What is the diagnosis?\n\n- **A.** Thrombosed External Haemorrhoid (perianal haematoma)\n- **B.** Internal haemorrhoid Grade IV\n- **C.** Anal fissure\n- **D.** Condyloma acuminata\n- **E.** Rectal prolapse\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nA thrombosed external haemorrhoid is a thrombosis of the subcutaneous perianal venous plexus below the dentate line covered by sensitive anoderm. It presents as an acute, severely painful, tense, purplish-blue solitary nodule. If presenting within 48-72 hours of onset with severe pain, complete surgical excision (or unroofing) under local anaesthesia provides immediate relief; after 72 hours, conservative management is indicated as the thrombus organizes.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the critical anatomical boundary that separates Internal Haemorrhoids (painless, visceral innervation above) from External Haemorrhoids (painful, somatic innervation below)?\n\n- **A.** The Dentate (Pectinate) Line\n- **B.** The Hilton's white line\n- **C.** The Puborectalis sling\n- **D.** The Sacral promontory\n- **E.** The Valves of Houston\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe Dentate (Pectinate) Line is the embryological junction between endodermal hindgut and ectodermal proctodeum. Internal haemorrhoids arise above the dentate line (covered by columnar/cuboidal epithelium innervated by visceral sensory nerves: painless, manifest with painless bright red bleeding). External haemorrhoids arise below the dentate line (covered by squamous anoderm innervated by somatic inferior rectal nerves: exquisitely painful).\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
51108db2-909e-4458-8f68-a3625d8d50a2	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	hypokalemia	Hypokalaemia Emergency Management	article	# Hypokalaemia Emergency Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nIncreased availability of insulin: insulin promotes the entry of potassium into\n- skeletal muscle and hepatic cells by increasing the activity of the Na-K-ATPase pump Losses from the GI tract:\n- Loss of gastric or intestinal secretions from any cause (vomiting, diarrhoea,\n- laxatives, or tube drainage) can cause hypokalaemia: Increased loss of potassium in urine\n- Diuretics (e.g., acetazolamide, loop diuretics, and thiazide-type diuretics)\n-\n- Mineralocorticoid excess Salt-wasting nephropathie\n- Metabolic acidosis\n- Increased loss via sweating\n- Exercising in a hot climate can produce more than IO L of sweat daily, leading to\n- potassium depletion if losses are not replaced: Burns and other dermatological conditions (i.e., eczema or psoriasis involving a\n- large surface area of the skin, especially with the use of topical steroids) can cause increased loss of potassium through the skin.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nCommon acute manifestations are:\n- muscle weakness\n-\n- ECG changes asymmetric or focal weakness\n-\n- Other features: rhabdomyolysis, renal abnormalities, and cardiac arrhythmias\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nBasic metabolic panel (includes serum sodium, potassium, glucose, chloride,\n- bicarbonate, urea, and creatinine).\n- ECG.\n- Urine electrolytes (potassium and chloride): useful in differentiating renal from non- renal causes of hypokalaemia when the aetiology of the hypokalaemia is not readily apparent. Urine creatinine: should always be measured with urine electrolytes. Urine\n- creatinine measurement (in addition to serum potassium and serum creatinine) allows for calculation of fractional excretion of potassium (FEK, 24-hour urine sample preferable) and potassium/creatinine ratio for evaluation of hypokalaemia.\n- ABG Serum magnesium, calcium, and/or phosphorus levels to exclude associated\n- electrolyte abnormalities. ECG findings depression of the ST segment,\n- decrease in the amplitude of the T wave\n- increase in the amplitude of U waves (often seen in the lateral precordial leads V4 to\n- Example-I\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\npotassium replacement — is required urgently in patients with severe hypokalaemia (serum potassium <2.5 mmol/L [<2.5 mEq/L]) or in patients who are symptomatic.\n- Patients able to tolerate oral intake:\n- Ifthe patient does not have diabetic ketoacidosis or HHS, potassium repletion is rnost easily done orally, even in people with severe hypokalaemia. The serum potassium concentration can rise acutely by as much as I to 1.5 mEq/L after an oral dose of 40 to 60 mEq of potassium.\n- Patients unable to tolerate oral intake:\n\n- intravenous potassium replacement is required\n- Patients with diabetic ketoacidosis or HHS:\n- intravenous potassium replacement is needed if hypokalaemia occurs, as there are usually marked potassium losses.\n- Follow local guideline for potassium administration and careful monitoring of the physiologic effects of severe hypokalaemia (ECG abnormalities with continuous cardiac monitoring, muscle weakness, or paralysis) is essential throughout repletion.\n- Hypomagnesaemia correction\n- It is important to determine whether there is hypomagnesaemia (<0.75 mmol/L [<1.5 mEq/L]) because hypokalaemia can often only be corrected once the magnesium deficit has been addressed.\n- Intravenous magnesium replacement therapy is indicated in cases of severe (<1 mEq/L) or symptomatic hypomagnesaemia.\n- Patients with mild magnesium depletion are usually asymptomatic and can be managed with oral magnesium replacement.\n- Complications\n- Severe muscle weakness or paralysis\n- Cardiac arrhythmias\n- Rahabdomyolysis\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypokalemia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypokalemia/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Hypokalemia - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypokalemia/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n*Hypokalemia — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
8dc33fb1-5c8c-4736-b8f6-267fd13aefe5	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	aortic-aneurysm-aa	Aortic Aneurysm (AA)	article	# Aortic Aneurysm (AA)\n\nAn abdominal aortic aneurysm is a pathological dilatation or enlargement of the\n- abdominal aorta to 1.5 times or greater to its normal diameter.\n- The maximum external diameter (measured from outer wall to outer wall) at different levels will vary: 3 cm at the epigastrium\n-\n- 1.5 cm at the bifurcation\n- Classification of aneurysm Suprarenal: the origin of one or more visceral arteries is involved\n- Pararenal: the origins of the renal arteries are involved\n- Infrarenal: commences beyond the renal arteries\n- Infrarenal AAA is the most common. Normal infrarenal diameter in men age 50 is\n- 1.7crY1 and 1.5cm in women An infrarenal aorta that is greater than 3 cm in diameter is considered to be\n- aneurysmal.\n- Clinical features\n- The vast majority of aortic aneurysms (75 %) are asymptomatic common presentingsymptoms Epigastric pain back/flank pain\n- Pulsatile epigastric mass\n- Malaise and weight loss (with inflammatory aneurysms)\n- Presentingsymptoms when rupture occurs\n-\n- Syncope/collapse\n\nSudden onset abdominal pain\n- Hypovolaemic shock\n- AAA-rupture-is-more-1ikeJy-jf-PT-has-the-fQ"owing-features\n- Diagnosed AAA\n- Age over 60 Hx of hypertension\n- Female sex\n-\n- Investigations Computed tomography is the gold standard of investigation\n- An early ultrasound scan is the primary investigation of choice. This is helpful for\n- those patients who are requiring resuscitation, and also for those patients in whom one wants to identify an aneurysm early.\n- Management Airway management if needed\n-\n- Obtain IV access Send FBC, U&E, and cross match\n- Activate massive transfusion protocol\n- Avoid excessive lv fluid\n- Contact the vascular team earlier\n- Emergency-transfer-to-regional-yascular-ser.yice\n-\n\n- The nice guideline recommends that When people with a suspected ruptured or symptomatic unruptured AAA have been accepted by a regional vascular service for emergency assessment, ensure that they leave the referring unit within 30 minutes of the decision to transfer.\n- Nn-c_uKßlinicaLpractice, elective\n- The aneurysm is larger than 5.5 cm in diameter\n- The aneurysm is larger than 4.5 cm in diameter and has increased in size by more than 0.5 cm in the past six months\n- UIS relevant to AAA\n- Aorta: left-side, thick-walled, round, non-compressible, and pulsatile.\n- IVC: right-side, thin-walled, almond, compressible, transmitted pulse previous  Next -9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n\n![Aortic Aneurysm (AA) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/aortic-aneurysm-aa/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Aortic Aneurysm (AA) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![Aortic Aneurysm (AA) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/aortic-aneurysm-aa/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Aortic Aneurysm (AA) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	\N
71d17b6f-73f1-495d-a44b-d7405d181839	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	aortic-dissection-ad	Aortic Dissection (AD)	article	# Aortic Dissection (AD)\n\nAn aortic dissection occurs following a tear in the aortic intima with subsequent\n- separation of the tissue within the weakened media by the propagation of blood.\n- Stanford classification Divides the dissections into Type A and Type B\n-\n- Type A dissection involves the ascending aorta and/or the arch of the aorta.\n- Type B dissections involve only the descending aorta and occur distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery.\n- Risk factors Hypertension Marfan syndrome\n- Ehlers-Danlos syndrome Annuloaortic ectasia and familial aortic dissection\n- Bicuspid aortic valve\n- Coarctation of the aorta\n- Pregnancy\n- Turner syndrome\n- Cocaine abuse\n- Giant cell arteritis\n- Iatrogenic\n-\n- Clinical Features Feature of pain-in-AAP\n- Sharp in nature describe as tearing pain\n\n- Occurs in anterior of chest 70-80% of Pt with Type A dissection Upper back pain may be experienced in Type-B Physical examination:\n- Faint or absent heart sounds\n- Distended neck veins\n- Presence of shock\n- A difference of >20 mmHg in the blood pressure of each arm\n- Acute or progressive aortic regurgitation A diastolic murmur over the aortic valve area\n- Investigations\n- ECC — (Non-specific ST segment and T wave changes or can be normal). . A widened mediastinum\n- An abnormal aortic contour\n- A globular heart suggests a large hemopericardium\n- Pleural effusion\n- Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) and trans-esophageal echocardiography (TOE) are available tests for AAD, maybe perform in ED, ICU or in OT.\n- CT is used where TOE isn't immediately available and CT is more common than TOE in Europe.\n\n-\n-\n-\n- Management Initial treatment Control BP IV beta blocker alone or with vasodilator GTN, nitroprusside to achieve HR less than 60bpm and BP less than 120mmHg Pain control with IV opiate\n\n- Definitive treatment: Type A dissections require open surgery to prevent rupture into the pericardial sac\n- Type B dissections are managed-medicaLly_but may require endovascular stenting if the patient has persistent pain, a rapidly expanding aortic diameter, or mal-perfusion of branch vessel organs previous  Next 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n\n![Aortic Dissection (AD) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/aortic-dissection-ad/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Aortic Dissection (AD) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n\n![Aortic Dissection (AD) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/aortic-dissection-ad/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Aortic Dissection (AD) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n\n![Aortic Dissection (AD) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/aortic-dissection-ad/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Aortic Dissection (AD) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	\N
7177cec8-a660-4af1-b6c5-b4b770f06f47	fdcaf6ed-e3d9-49ea-9134-f878bb6a6b67	streptococcus-and-staphylococcus-bactera	Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera	article	Streptococcus spp\nCommon features:\nStreptococcus Spp\n(Beta hemolytic group A) Is gram +Ve Cocci in chains\nCauses acute sore throat in children, skin and soft tissue\ninfection and necrotizing fasciitis.\nErysipelas: Superficial skin infection involving upper dermis\nand superficial lymphatics caused by Group A beta hemolytic\nstreptococcus pyogenes\nPost-infectious immune-mediated reactions of\nStreptococcus Pyogenes include Rheumatic fever, erythema\nnodosum, acute glomerulonephritis.\nStaphylococcus Epidermidis:\nGram positive cocci is an important pathogen involving\nprosthetic implants, intravascular lines, ventriculoperitoneal\nshunts and cardiac valves.\nStaphylococcus epidermidis is part of the normal flora of the\nskin and nasopharynx, and infection is usually endogenous.\nStreptococcus Viridans\n\n![Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/streptococcus-and-staphylococcus-bactera/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/streptococcus-and-staphylococcus-bactera/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nStreptococcus Pneumoniae:\nGram positive Alpha hemolytic encapsulated diplococci\ntransmitted via respiratory droplets causing pneumonia,\nMeningitis, sinusitis and otitis media\nCommunity acquired pneumonia:\nInvestigation\nBlood tests- FBC, CRP U&E\nCXR\nA definitive diagnosis of CAP requires evidence of\nconsolidation on chest x-ray.\nPulse oximetry ABGs\nBlood & Sputum culture (± Gram stain) - ideally before\nantibiotics are given, in all patients with moderate- or high-\nseverity CAP (as determined by the CURB-65 score) presenting\nin hospital.\nSeverity assessment and management (NICE & BTS\nguideline)\n\n![Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/streptococcus-and-staphylococcus-bactera/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nChoice of antibiotics\nStaphylococcus aureus:\nStaphylococcus aureus is a catalase-positive, coagulase-\npositive, beta-haemolytic, Gram-positive coccus found in\nclusters.\nTransmission:\nEndogenous spread: Staphylococcus aureus is normal gut\nflora of skin and nasopharynx. Endogenous spread causes\ninfection in immunocompromised individuals\nExogenous spread: via direct skin contact or indirect contact\nby clothing or other fomites\nDisease: Primary skin infection (e.g., impetigo, abscess and\ncellulitis)\n\n![Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/streptococcus-and-staphylococcus-bactera/figure_04_p3.png)\n*Streptococcus and Staphylococcus Bactera — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\nSecondary skin infections (e.g., in eczema, surgical wounds,\nintravenous devices)\nPneumonia\nInfective Endocarditis (most common cause of acute IE in IV\ndrug users\nOsteomyelitis\nSeptic arthritis\nSeptic Arthritis:\nis an infection of any kind that finds its way into joint space, it\nis a most rapid and destructive joint disease, acute onset of\nmonoarticular arthritis with pain swelling, and warmth, the\nknee is the most common site others include the hip, wrist,\nshoulder, and ankle.\nThe most common type of bacteria that causes septic\narthritis is called staphylococcus aureus. others include\nstreptococcus, gram-negative rods,\nNeisseria Gonorrhea causes septic arthritis in sexually\nactive young adults.\nTreatment: Flucloxacillin: Antibiotic of choice\nVancomycin is used to treat methicillin Resistant Staph\nAureus	\N	[]	\N	f	20	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
ec876730-2310-4e0f-a37c-8e435b6e59ad	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	lymphatic-drainage-of-upper-limb	Lymphatic Drainage of Upper Limb	article	Lymphatic drainage of the Upper\nLimb:\nThe superficial lymph vessels ascend the limb in the superficial\nfascia and accompany the superficial veins. The deep lymph\nvessels lie deep to the deep fascia and follow the deep arteries\nand veins. All lymph vessels of the limb ultimately drain into\nlymph nodes in axilla.\n\n### 1: Axillary lymph nodes: The axillary lymph nodes drain lymph\n\nvessels from the entire upper limb. In addition, they drain\nlymph vessels from the lateral part of the breast\n\n### 2: Infraclavicular nodes: They lie in the interval between the\n\ndeltoid and the pectoralis major muscles. They receive lymph\nfrom the superficial vessels of the radial side of hand, the\nforearm, and the arm.\n\n### 3: Supratrochlear lymph nodes: The supratrochlear lymph\n\nnode lies in the superficial fascia in the cubital fossa close to\nthe trochlea of the humerus. It receives lymph from the medial\nfingers, the medial part of the hand, and the medial side of the\nforearm.\nLymphatic drainage of the Breast:\nThe lymphatic drainage of the breast is of great clinical\nimportance due to its role in the metastasis of breast cancer\ncells.\nThere are three groups of lymph nodes that receive lymph\nfrom breast tissue.\nAxillary nodes (75%): Receives lymphatic drainage from lateral\nand superior quadrant of breast.\n\nparasternal nodes (20%): Receives lymphatic drainage from\nmedial quadrant.\nNote: Breast cancer in axillary tail of breast most commonly\ninvolved lymph node is axillary\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearl:**\n> Removal and analysis of the axillary lymph nodes is often a vital\n> tool for the staging of breast cancers. During this procedure\n> there is also a risk of damage to either of the long thoracic\n> nerve or the thoracodorsal nerve causing winged scapula.	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
98c37434-b205-44c6-9382-670983bcdf85	b69e167f-8caf-4315-bbaf-2779fd43f2f7	upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes	Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes	article	Dermatomes of Upper Limb:\nDermatome: A dermatome is defined as 'a strip of skin that is\ninnervated by a single spinal nerve and clinically very\nimportant and help in diagnosis of spinal cord injury\nMyotomes and associated\nmovements:\nMyotome: Group of muscles innervated by a single spinal\nnerve\nReflexes of upper Limb:\n\n![Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/upper-limb-anatomy/upper-limb-dermatomes-myotomes-and-reflexes/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Upper Limb Dermatomes, Myotomes and Reflexes — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
bb27db15-d5dc-4848-b768-ec768dee965d	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af	Atrial Fibrillation Management	article	# Atrial Fibrillation Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- AF is diagnosed using an ECG which typically shows an absolutely irregular RR interval and absent p waves. NICE guideline classification\n- paroxysmal AF\n- AF terminates spontaneously within seven days of onset and most often within 48 hours of onset.\n- Persistent AF AF presents continuously for seven days or more or is terminated by cardioversion.\n- Permanent AF\n- AF which is accepted without attempted cardioversion or which cannot be terminated by cardioversion\n- Pathophysiology: _\n- The primary pathological change seen in AF is progressive atrial fibrosis.\n- Prothrombotic state: Structural and functional changes in the atrial myocardium along with stasis of blood especially in the Left atrial appendage allow an environment in which a thrombus can form.\n- Damage to the myocardium causes the expression of prothrombotic factors on the atrial endothelial surface. This along with activation of platelets and inflammatory cells leads to clot formation.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nbreathlessness\n- palpitations\n\n- syncope or dizziness\n- chest discomfort\n- stroke or transient ischaemic attack\n- History and physical examination that is important in deciding the most appropriate treatment.\n- PIRATES\n- (secondary causes).\n- Ischemia,\n- Respiratory disease,\n- Atrial enlargement or myxoma,\n- Thyroid disease (check TSH and free T4 in first-time presenters),\n- Ethanol ("Holiday heart" after binging)\n- Sepsis or Sleep apnea\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nPhysical examination\n- 12-lead ECG\n- Blood sample for Thyroid function and biochemical analysis\n\nevÅ cVF RHYTHM STRI u: 11 ':cm/mV\n- Stroke & Bleeding Risk Stratification\n- Consider anticoagulation for men with a CHA2DS2-VASc score ofl\n- Offer anticoagulation to people with a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 2 or above I\n- Take bleeding risk into account using a HAS-BLED score of greater than 2 as a relative contraindication to anticoagulation.\n\n- CHAPSrVASc Score HF (heart failure) Hy rtension 275 Qiabetes troke yascular Disease 2 2 Condition H\n- Hypertensbn A\n- Abnormal renal or fmctbn (1 point each) S\n- Stroke B\n- Bleeding L\n- Labde INRs E\n- Ederty 65 years) D\n- Drugs or abohol (1 point each) 'Points 1 or 2 1 e 65-74 ex te (female)\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof hemodynamically-unstable AF\n- DC shock 120-1503 should be given if a patient presents with the following adverse features:\n- Shock\n- Syncope/reduce the level of consciousness.\n- Heart failure\n- Myocardial ischemia\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof previously- identified AF\n- In patients presenting to the ED who are known to have AF long-term (>48 hours). a reason for why they are tachycardic should be sorted. If a secondary/reversible cause has been excluded then rate control should be considered\n\n- NICE recommends rate control (beta blockers and rate-limiting CCB) as the first- line strategy for people with atrial fibrillation, except in people: whose atrial fibrillation has a reversible cause. who have heart failure thought to be primarily caused by atrial fibrillation. with new-onset atrial fibrillation (<48hrs) For whom a rhythm control strategy would be more suitable based on clinical judgment Note: digoxin can be used in sedentary patients.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof Recent-onset, Non-Life- threatening AF In the ED if a patient presents within 48 hrs of onset consider either pharmacological or electrical cardioversion. Electrical: synchronized DC 120-1503.\n- Pharmacological:\n- A choice of flecainide in people with no evidence of structural or ischemic heart disease. Amiodarone should be used in people with evidence of structural heart disease. Note: if patient with AF is uncertain of time or greater than 48 hrs then anti-coagulate this pt for 3 weeks before cardioversion and rate should be controlled with Beta blockers.\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af/figure_05_p6.jpeg)\n*Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/atrial-fibrillation-flutter-af/figure_06_p7.jpeg)\n*Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (AF) — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 7 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
bac974d1-e3aa-43fe-8ce0-5d44e9c49aad	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	emergency-bradycardia-management	Emergency Bradycardia Management	article	# Emergency Bradycardia Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nBradycardia is a heart rate of fewer than 60 beats per minute.\n-\n- Types Sinus bradycardia\n- Sinus arrest\n- Junctional bradycardia\n-\n- Atrioventricular blocks:\n- First degree Second degree (Mobitz types I and II)\n-\n- Third-degree (complete heart block)\n- Causes Normal physiological variants as seen in athletes\n- Cardiac causes (ischemic & structural heart disease)\n- Infectious\n- Systemic illness (hypothyroidism, hypothermia) Autonomic mediated (carotid hypersensitivity, vasovagal syncope)\n- Head injury\n-\n- Hyper-hypokalaemia\n- Drugs Beta-blockers\n-\n- CCB Amitriptyline\n- Quinidine\n- Clonidine\n-\n\nDigoxin\n- Organophosphate\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\napproach (unstable patient) The ALS bradycardia algorithm recommends Atropine 500 mcg IV up to 3mg\n- maximum if any patient presents with the following:\n- risk of asystole:\n- Shock\n- Syncope Myocardial ischemia Heart failure\n- Risk of asystole:\n- Recent asystole\n- Mobiztype-2 Av block\n- Complete heart block\n- Ventricular pause\n- 3 seconds\n- Bradycardia (Resus council of the UK and European resuscitation council\n- guideline)\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock, Syncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure as mentioned above) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 — 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n- If treatrnent with atropine is ineffective consider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and\n- adrenaline (2\n- 10mcg).\n\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 — 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECG carefully for the presence of P waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing.\n- If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment.\n- There is a controversy between RCEM learning and resus council of the UK guideline for bradycardia secondary to beta-blocker. however, you've to stick with resus council of the UK guideline.\n- Sinus-bradycardia\n\n- In sinus bradycardia, the heart rate is below 60 bpm and there is a P wave before every QRS complex. The PR interval is constant.\n- Sinus bradycardia may be normal in healthy young adults, especially athletes.\n- Causes (find in the previous slide)\n- If patient is a symptomatic treatment with atropine or according to the underlying cause. ( hypothermia. Beta-blocker, CCB, Organophosphate).\n- Sinus arrest\n\n- Sinus arrest is characterized by the absence of P waves and pauses of3 seconds or more. It is the result of either sinus node disease or carotid sinus hypersensitivity.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nexacerbating drugs such as beta-blockers or calcium channel antagonists should be stopped.\n\n- Junctional bradycardia is a regular rhythm (40-60 bpm) initiated in the AV nodal tissue and occurs when the SA-nodaLpacemake.Lfaj.ls:\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nif patient is symptomatic first-line treatment Atropine initially 0.5mg(500mcg) if persists then a permanent pacemaker may be required.\n- Atrioventricular block\n- First-degree heart block:\n- Constant prolongation of PR interval\n- 200ms and Qrs complex remain narrow.\n- It doesn't require treatment\n\n- Second:degree-heart-block\n- Mobitz type-I(Wenckebach):\n- The progressive lengthening of PR interval eventually dropped beat (ventricular conduction).\n- Required monitoring\n\n- Mobitz type-2: The intermittent dropping of ventricular conduction\n- 2nd degree 2:1 Av block:\n- Alternative P wave not conducted to the ventricle\n- Progress to complete heart block so requires urgent intervention\n- Third-degree-Hearth-ock\n- Complete dissociation between atria and ventricle (P wave and QRS complex)\n\nprevious  Next 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Emergency Bradycardia Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/emergency-bradycardia\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n*Emergency Bradycardia Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Emergency Bradycardia Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/emergency-bradycardia\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_02_p1.jpeg)\n*Emergency Bradycardia Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Emergency Bradycardia Management - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/cardiovascular-emergencies/emergency-bradycardia\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_03_p10.jpeg)\n*Emergency Bradycardia Management — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 10 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
70d48592-7bfc-4f68-a981-254d6fac00b1	31fbd97a-7577-4959-a0ed-132fda326997	deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt-guidelines	Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines	article	# Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Recent hospitilisation.\n- Recent traurna.\n- Chemotherapy.\n- Significant immobility (bedbound, unable to walk unaided or likely to spend a substantial portion of the day in bed or in a chair).\n- Prolonged travel (for more than 4 hours)\n- Pregnancy and the postpartum period.\n- Dehydration.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nUnilateral localised pain (this is usually throbbing in nature) that occurs when walking or bearing weight, and calf swelling (or more rarely, swelling of the entire leg). . Tenderness.\n- Skin changes, which include oedema, redness, and warmth.\n- Vein distension.\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\ns\n- Assess leg and thigh swelling — measure the circumference of the leg IO cm below the tibial tuberosity and compare with the asymptomatic leg. A difference of more than 3 cm between the extremities increases the probability of DVT.\n- Assess for oedema and dilated collateral superficial veins on the affected side.\n\n## Differential Diagnosis\nCellulitis (commonly mistaken as DVT)\n- Dependent (stasis) oedema.\n\nLymphatic obstruction.\n- Septic arthritis\n- Calf muscle tear/Achilles' tendon tear.\n- Calf muscle haematoma.\n- Fracture\n- Post-thrombotic syndrome\n- Venous obstruction or insufficiency,\n-\n- Arteriovenous fistula and congenital vascular abnormalities. Acute arterial ischaemia.\n- Vasculitis.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nRefer immediately for same-day assessment and management if deep vein\n- thrombosis (DVT) is suspected in a woman who is pregnant or has given birth within the past 6 weeks. For all other people with suspected score to assess the probability of DVT\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n- Active cancer (treatment ongoing, within 6 months, or palliative) Paralysis, paresis or recent plaster immobilisation of lower extremities Recently bedridden for 3 days or more, or major surgery within 12 weeks requiring general or regional anaesthesia Localised tenderness along the distribution of the deep venous system Entire leg swollen Calf swelling at least 3 cm larger than asymptomatic side Pitting oedema confined to the symptomatic leg Collateral superficial veins (non-varicose) Previously documented DVT An alternative diagnosis is at least as likely as DVT DVT likely: 2 points or more DVT unlikely: 1 point or less Score 1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1\n- 2\n\n- For people who are likely to have DVT\n- o points or mor on two-level DVT Wells score: .1.\n- Offer a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan with the results available within 4 hours if possible. If a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan cannot be carried out within 4 hours of being requested, offer a D-dimer test, then Interim therapeutic anticoagulation (apixaban or rivaroxaban first line, and if these are not suitable, Iow molecular weight heparin (LMWH). and A proximal leg vein ultrasound scan with the results available within 24 hours. For people who are unlikelv to have ne point or es on two-level DVT Wells score: Offer a D-dimer test with the results available within 4 hours. If the results cannot be obtained within 4 hours Offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation while awaiting the result If the D-dimer test is positive. offer a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan with the results available within 4 hours if possible.\n- If the results cannot be obtained within 4 hours: Offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation and a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan with the result available within 24 hours. If the D-dimer test is neqative: Stop interim therapeutic anticoagulation. Consider an alternative diagnosis.\n\n- Ensure adequate monitoring. Specialists will make clinical decisions such as the choice of anticoagulant and the duration of treatment.\n- Treatment is usually continued for at least 3 months, but duration may be longer depending on whether the DVT was unprovoked(no obvious, transient risk factor identified) or provoked(caused by an identifiable, transient, major risk factor).\n- For warfarin, the usual strategy is to aim for an international normalized ratio (INR) target of 2.5, keeping within the range of 2.0—3.0. Key_points\n- Interim therapeutic anticoagulation choice;\n- Apixaban or rivaroxaban first line, and if these are not suitable, low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) for at least 5 days followed by dabigatran or edoxaban, OR\n- LMVVH concurrently with a vitamin K antagonist for at least 5 days.\n- Please note, Take into account comorbidities, contraindications, and the person's preferences when choosing anticoagulation treatrnent.\n- Carry out baseline blood tests including full blood count, renal and hepatic function, prothrombin time (PT), and activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT). However, do not wait for the results of baseline blood tests before starting anticoagulation treatrnent.\n- Complication\n\n- Pulmonary embolism (The most serious complication DVT) Post-thrombotic syndrome Bleeding associated with anticoagulation treatment Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) Suspected DVT: diagnosis and initial management Determine 2-level DVT Wells score suspected Wells Score 2 2 points DVT likew Proximal leg vein ultrasound scan within 4 hours Quantitative D-dimer testif not already doneü, then Interim therapeutic anticoagulation»s and Scan within 24 hours Wells score s 1 point DVT unlikely Quantitative D-dimer test'with result in 4 hours Interim therapeutic anticoagulation3-S while awaiting result D — positive If D-dimer positive and DVT unlikely D-dimer negative Scan positive Diagnose DVT and Offer or continue treatment Scan negative Quantitative D-dimer test if not already done'; D-dimer positive D-dimer negative Stop any anticoagulation and think about other diagnoses Stop any anticoagulation and repeat scan 6 to 8 days later Second scan positive Second scan negative DVT algorithm\n\npreviOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt-guidelines/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt-guidelines/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n*Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt-guidelines/figure_03_p7.jpeg)\n*Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt-guidelines/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n*Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 08:18:52.750473+00	\N
01ac45be-555c-44fb-b501-2e758329b80f	5ccfa49a-decb-43f0-959f-77a44102113e	muscles-of-thorax	Muscles of Thorax	article	Muscles of the thoracic cage:\nThese are five muscles that make the thoracic cage\nExternal intercostals\nInternal intercostals\nInnermost intercostals\nSubcostals, and transversus thoracis\nNote: All these muscles are supplied by the corresponding\nintercostal nerves\nDiaphragm:\nMusculotendinous structure separates thoracic cavity from\nabdominal cavity. located at the inferior-most aspect of the\nribcage, filling the inferior thoracic aperture. It acts as the floor\nof the thoracic cavity and the roof of the abdominal cavity.\nDiaphragm has two attachments peripheral and central.\n\n### 1: Peripheral attachments: Three peripheral attachments\n\n![Muscles of Thorax - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/muscles-of-thorax/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Muscles of Thorax — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nXiphoid process of sternum\nCostal cartilages ribs 7 - 10, ends of ribs 11 and 12\nArcuate ligaments and lumbar vertebrae\n\n### 2: Central attachment: Muscles fibers of the diaphragm forms\n\ncentral tendon which fuses with fibrous pericardium superiorly\nNote: Diaphragm separates thoracic cavity from abdominal\ncavity. Thus, any structure that pass between the two cavities\nwill pierce the diaphragm.\nThere are three openings which conduct the structures\nFunction: The diaphragm is the primary muscle of respiration. ,\ncontracts and flattens during inspiration increasing the vertical\ndiameter of the thoracic cavity. This produces lung expansion,\nand air is drawn in.\nThe diaphragm During expiration passively relaxes and returns\nto its original dome shape. This reduces the volume of the\nthoracic cavity.\nInnervation:\nPhrenic nerve: The phrenic nerve originates from cervical\nspinal roots C3, C4 and C5\nprovide motor innervation to the diaphragm.\nprovide sensory innervation the central part of the\ndiaphragm, the pericardium and the mediastinal part of\nthe parietal pleura.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia\n\n![Muscles of Thorax - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/thorax-anatomy-mediastinum/muscles-of-thorax/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Muscles of Thorax — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nCongenital diaphragmatic hernia is a diaphragmatic defect,\nand are frequently diagnosed prenatally with US, which\nexpedites proper initial newborn resuscitation.\nMost commonly left sided.\nThe diaphragmatic defect allows intra-abdominal contents,\nincluding stomach, bowel, and, occasionally, liver, to enter\nthe chest during the second trimester of gestation, leading to\npulmonary hypoplasia\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> The clinical hallmark is persistent respiratory distress at birth,\n> often with a characteristic "seesaw" side-to-side respiratory\n> pattern due to the severely hypoplastic ipsilateral lung\n> Acquired diaphragmatic hernia: include traumatic\n> diaphragmatic rupture, hiatus hernia and iatrogenic\n> diaphragmatic hernias. Depending on the location and size of\n> the defect.\n\nNote: In hiatus hernia, the stomach herniates through the\noesophageal opening of the diaphragm, typically resulting in\nsymptoms of acid reflux.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **High yield:**\n> Diaphragm: Normal quiet inspiration\n> Normal expiration: Passive recoil of lungs\n> Inspiration: External intercostal muscle\n> Expiration: Internal intercostal muscle\n> Accessory muscle of inspiration: Sternocleidomastoid\n> Accessory muscle of expiration: Transversus abdominal\n> muscles\n> Forced expiration: Internal intercostal muscle and rectus\n> abdominus	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
1f160f54-ff4e-49fa-bc6b-370fb7c74959	31fbd97a-7577-4959-a0ed-132fda326997	sba-quiz-vascular-deep-vein-thrombosis	Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 52-year-old woman presents with a 2-day history of painful swelling of her left lower calf. On examination, the left calf circumference is 4 cm greater than the right calf (measured 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity), with localized tenderness along the deep venous system and pitting oedema. According to the two-level Wells DVT Score, what is her score, risk category, and recommended diagnostic pathway per NICE NG158?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Wells score 2 (DVT likely); Perform urgent Proximal Leg Vein Ultrasound within 4 hours (or give interim therapeutic anticoagulation and scan within 24 hours)\n- **B.** Wells score 0 (DVT unlikely); D-dimer test alone is sufficient\n- **C.** No investigation needed, prescribe compression stockings\n- **D.** Perform contrast venography immediately\n- **E.** Wells score only applies to pulmonary embolism\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nWells DVT criteria: Calf swelling >3 cm compared to asymptomatic side (1 point), localized tenderness along deep venous system (1 point) = Total 2 points ('DVT Likely', score >=2). NICE NG158 recommends: perform proximal leg vein ultrasound within 4 hours; if unavailable, administer an interim dose of therapeutic anticoagulation (DOAC) and perform ultrasound within 24 hours.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE NG158 guidelines, what is the first-line oral anticoagulation therapy of choice for confirmed acute lower limb Deep Vein Thrombosis or Pulmonary Embolism in patients without severe renal impairment or active cancer?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC: Apixaban or Rivaroxaban) as initial monotherapy\n- **B.** Warfarin monotherapy without heparin overlap\n- **C.** Aspirin 300mg daily for 6 months\n- **D.** Intravenous unfractionated heparin continuous infusion for 6 months\n- **E.** Dipyridamole 200mg BD\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG158 recommends a Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC: Apixaban or Rivaroxaban as single-drug regimens with higher initial loading doses, or Edoxaban/Dabigatran following initial 5 days of LMWH) as first-line anticoagulation for confirmed VTE. DOACs have predictable pharmacokinetics, do not require routine INR monitoring, and demonstrate lower rates of major intracranial bleeding compared to Warfarin.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 62-year-old female with advanced metastatic pelvic malignancy presents with sudden, massive, agonizing swelling of her entire left lower limb. On examination, the leg is cold, pale, marble-white, and pulseless with severe pitting oedema. What life-threatening limb ischemia syndrome from complete iliofemoral venous occlusion is this?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_03_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Phlegmasia Alba Dolens ('milk leg' / painful white oedema)\n- **B.** Acute bacterial cellulitis\n- **C.** Superficial thrombophlebitis\n- **D.** Ruptured Baker's cyst\n- **E.** Compartment syndrome from trauma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPhlegmasia alba dolens ('milk leg') is an acute, severe manifestation of massive iliofemoral deep venous thrombosis. Severe deep venous occlusion leads to profound interstitial oedema and arterial spasm, rendering the limb pale, white, cold, and painful. If collateral venous channels also thrombose, it progresses to Phlegmasia Cerulea Dolens (cyanotic, purple, gangrenous limb), carrying high risk of venous gangrene and amputation, requiring emergency catheter-directed thrombolysis or surgical thrombectomy.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich D-dimer testing threshold strategy is validated to reduce unnecessary imaging in patients aged >50 years presenting with 'DVT Unlikely' or 'PE Unlikely'?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Age-Adjusted D-dimer threshold (Age in years x 10 mcg/L for patients >50 years)\n- **B.** Fixed threshold of 200 mcg/L for all ages\n- **C.** Weight-adjusted D-dimer\n- **D.** D-dimer threshold is halved in elderly patients\n- **E.** Gender-adjusted threshold\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe ADJUST-PE and YEARS studies validated the Age-Adjusted D-dimer strategy for patients aged >50 years: Age (years) x 10 mcg/L (or Age x 0.05 mg/L in FEU). Baseline D-dimer naturally rises with physiological aging; using an age-adjusted cutoff substantially increases specificity in elderly patients without sacrificing safety or missing VTE.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended minimum duration of therapeutic anticoagulation for a patient with a first unprovoked (idiopathic) proximal DVT who has a low bleeding risk according to NICE guidelines?\n\n- **A.** At least 3 to 6 months, followed by consideration of long-term / indefinite anticoagulation\n- **B.** Exactly 5 days\n- **C.** 2 weeks only\n- **D.** 1 month\n- **E.** Lifelong mandatory anticoagulation without review\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor a first PROVOKED DVT (major transient reversible risk factor, e.g. surgery, trauma, immobilisation), anticoagulation is recommended for 3 months. For an UNPROVOKED (idiopathic) proximal DVT or PE, anticoagulation is continued for at least 3 to 6 months, after which multidisciplinary review assesses the risk-benefit balance of continuing long-term (indefinite) anticoagulation.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-02 08:18:52.750473+00	\N
f3ed7457-8c52-4c34-919d-213e33e6c37c	31fbd97a-7577-4959-a0ed-132fda326997	peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia	Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia	article	# Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Causes\n- Peripheral arterial disease of the lower limbs is most commonly caused by atherosclerosis.\n- Vasculitis\n- Fibromuscular dysplasia Thromboembolism\n- Thromboembolism\n- Buerger's disease . Trauma\n- Dissection\n- Physiological entrapment syndromes, and cystic adventitial disease\n- Acute limb ischaemia\n- Most commonly due to thrombosis within a diseased artery when an atherosclerotic plaque ruptures (80—85%).\n- Cardiac embolization\n- Aortic dissection or embolization\n- Graft thrombosis; thrombosis of a popliteal aneurysm\n- Hypercoagulable states\n- Iatrogenic complications of vascular intervention\n- Risk factors\n- Smoking\n- Diabetes mellitus\n- Advanced age\n\nHypertension\n- Hypercholesterolaemia Advanced age\n- Known atherosclerotic disease\n-\n- CKD High serum homocysteine\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nFeatures of acute limb ischaemia\n- Pain — constantly present and persistent.\n- Pulseless — ankle pulses are always absent.\n- Pallor (or cyanosis or mottling).\n- Power loss or paralysis.\n- Paraesthesia or reduced sensation or numbness.\n- Perishing with cold.\n-\n\nIschaemia due to an embolus Onset is acute. The limb appears white (because there is no collateral circulation). Vascular examination in the other leg is usually normal. Ischaemia due to thrombosis Onset is more gradual. The leg may not be white and symptoms may be less severe (due to collateral circulation, which is often well-developed in people with chronic peripheral vascular disease). Presentation is usually with worsening claudication and rest pain. Pulses in the other leg may also be absent\n- Classical features of intermittent claudication\n- Cramp-like pain in a muscle group after walking a predictable distance that is relieved by rest and reproduced by walking the same distance again.\n- Pain is not present at rest or altered by position.\n- the calves are more commonly affected than the thigh or buttock areas. gjschaemia\n- Chronic rest pain, which may be worse at night\n- People may report sleeping with the leg hanging out of bed, or sleep in a chair to relieve symptoms in the affected foot.\n- Dependent rubor, pallor on elevation of the extremity, and reduced capillary refill.\n\n- Skin changes including ischaemic ulcers, non-healing foot wounds, and gangrene. Usually affects the toes.\n- Absent foot pulses.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nProper history and examination\n-\n- Examining;\n- The legs and feet for ulceration, temperature differences between the legs, muscle atrophy, and skin changes (for example thin, shiny skin, discolouration, dependent rubor and elevation pallor, tissue loss on the heel or between the toes, hair loss). Femoral, popliteal, and foot pulses.\n- Capillary refill time (although this may not be reliable).\n-\n- The cardiovascular system (including for arterial bruits — femoral, aortic, carotid). ankle-brachial index (ABI)\n- segmental pressure examination\n- duplex ultrasound\n- continuous wave Doppler ultrasound\n- catheter angiography\n-\n- CT angiogram Investigation to find sources of embolus\n-\n- ECG Echocardiography\n- Aortic, popliteal and femoral artery ultrasound\n-\n\n- An ABPI ratio Less than 0.5 Greater than 0.5 to less than 0.8 Between 0.8 and 1.3 Greater than 1.3\n- suggests severe arterial disease.\n- Refer the person urgently for specialist vascular assessment. suggests the presence of arterial disease or mixed arterial/venous disease. Refer the person for specialist vascular assessment Suggests no evidence Of significant arterial disease. May suggest the presence of arterial calcification, such as in some people with diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic vasculitis, atherosclerotic disease, and advanced chronic renal failure. Care must be taken in interpreting ABPI results in people with these conditions, as they may be misleadingly high.\n- Note: don't need to memorize for exam purposes.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nAcute limb ischaemia management\n- Antiplatelet therapy_=Aspirin is recommended. Clopidogrel is recommended as an effective alternative antiplatelet therapy to aspirin.\n\nAnalgesia Paracetamol and an opioid (weak or strong) are recommended,\n- depending on the severity of pain. Anticoagulation— In patients with acute limb ischaemia, systemic anticoagulation\n- with unfractionated heparin should be administered, unless contraindicated. Endovascular therapies\n- Percutaneous catheter-directed thrombolytic therapy.\n- Percutaneous mechanical thrombus extraction.\n-\n- Surgical-interventions Surgical thromboembolectomy.\n- Endarterectomy.\n-\n- Bypass surgery. Amputation if the limb is unsalvageable.\n-\n- Critical limb ischaemia (Chronic severe limb ischaemia) Urgently refer to a vascular multidisciplinary team\n-\n- Manage-pain\n- Offer paracetamol and either weak or strong opioids, depending on the severity of the pain.\n- Intermittent claudication management offers a supervised exercise programme to all people with intermittent\n- claudication. If supervised exercise is not available, consider suggesting unsupervised\n- exercise — For example advise exercise for approximately 30 minutes three to five times per week, walking until the onset of symptoms, then resting to recover.\n\n- Refer for consideration of angioplasty or bypass surgery when:\n- Advice on the benefits of modifying risk factors has been reinforced, and\n- A supervised exercise programme has not led to a satisfactory improvement in symptoms.\n- supervised exercise has not led to a satisfactory improvement, and the person prefers not to be referred for consideration of angioplasty or bypass surgery.\n- Offer all people with peripheral arterial disease information, advice, support, and treatment regarding the secondary_prevention of cardiovascular disease.\n- Diet, weight management, and exercise\n- Lipid modification and statin therapy The prevention, diagnosis, and management of diabetes\n- The prevention, diagnosis, and management of high blood pressure\n- Antiplatelet therapy previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n*Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n*Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 08:18:52.750473+00	\N
fd07342a-3957-4147-a220-dd15f1b7279a	31fbd97a-7577-4959-a0ed-132fda326997	sba-quiz-vascular-peripheral-vascular-disease	Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 68-year-old male smoker with diabetes presents with sudden onset of severe, agonizing pain in his right leg. On examination, the right leg below the knee is cold to touch, pale, and pulseless. Sensation is decreased over the dorsum of the foot, and he cannot dorsiflex his toes. What are the classic '6 Ps' of Acute Limb Ischaemia (ALI)?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/peripheral-vascular-disease/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, Paralysis, and Perishingly cold (Poikilothermia)\n- **B.** Purpura, Petechiae, Pruritus, Pitting, Pyrexia, and Polyuria\n- **C.** Pain, Plethora, Palpitations, Priapism, Papules, and Phlebitis\n- **D.** Polydipsia, Polyphagia, Paresthesia, Ptosis, Proptosis, and Pallor\n- **E.** Pulsus alternans, Pulsus paradoxus, Papilloedema, Petechiae, Pallor, and Pain\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe 6 Ps of acute arterial limb ischaemia: (1) Pain (sudden, severe, continuous); (2) Pallor; (3) Pulselessness; (4) Paresthesia (sensory loss indicates nerve ischemia within 1-2 hours); (5) Paralysis (motor loss indicates irreversible muscle necrosis within 4-6 hours); (6) Perishingly cold (Poikilothermia / inability to regulate temperature). Presence of motor loss (paralysis) is an emergency requiring surgical revascularisation within 6 hours.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the Rutherford Classification category for an acutely ischemic limb with inaudible arterial Doppler signals, severe rest pain, sensory loss extending beyond the toes, and mild-to-moderate motor muscle weakness (incomplete paralysis), and what is the required intervention?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/peripheral-vascular-disease/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Rutherford Class IIb (Immediately Threatened); Requires immediate emergency surgical or endovascular revascularisation within 4-6 hours\n- **B.** Rutherford Class I (Viable); Elective outpatient angiography\n- **C.** Rutherford Class III (Irreversible); Primary major amputation\n- **D.** Rutherford Class 0; Reassurance\n- **E.** Conservative medical therapy with aspirin alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRutherford Classification of ALI: Class I (Viable, no sensory/motor deficit); Class IIa (Marginally threatened, salvageable if treated promptly, sensory loss in toes); Class IIb (Immediately threatened, sensory loss above toes, motor weakness, salvageable with emergency revascularisation); Class III (Irreversible, profound anesthesia, paralysis, rigor, inaudible venous/arterial Doppler, requires amputation).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat initial emergency medical therapy should be administered immediately upon diagnosis of acute arterial limb ischaemia in the ED to prevent propagation of the thrombus?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/peripheral-vascular-disease/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Unfractionated Heparin (weight-adjusted bolus e.g. 5,000 units followed by continuous infusion)\n- **B.** Oral warfarin 10mg single dose\n- **C.** Oral clopidogrel 75mg daily\n- **D.** Subcutaneous vitamin K\n- **E.** Tight compression bandages\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nImmediate systemic anticoagulation with IV Unfractionated Heparin (5,000 unit bolus followed by weight-adjusted infusion) is the critical initial medical intervention in acute limb ischaemia. It prevents proximal and distal propagation of thrombus, preserves the distal microcirculation and collaterals, and is easily reversible with protamine before emergency surgery.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nHow is the Ankle-Brachial Pressure Index (ABPI) calculated, and what value defines Chronic Critical Limb-Threatening Ischaemia (CLTI)?\n\n- **A.** Highest systolic pressure at the ankle divided by highest systolic pressure in the arm; ABPI < 0.40 (or resting ankle pressure <50 mmHg / toe pressure <30 mmHg)\n- **B.** Arm pressure divided by ankle pressure; value > 1.0\n- **C.** Ankle pressure minus arm pressure; value > 50\n- **D.** ABPI is only measured using pulse rate\n- **E.** ABPI between 0.90 and 1.20\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nABPI = Highest Doppler systolic pressure in dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial artery divided by the highest brachial systolic pressure. Normal is 0.90 - 1.30. Intermittent claudication is typically 0.50 - 0.85. Chronic Critical Limb-Threatening Ischaemia (CLTI: rest pain for >2 weeks, ischemic ulcers, or gangrene) is defined by ABPI < 0.40, ankle systolic pressure <50 mmHg, or toe pressure <30 mmHg.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 70-year-old diabetic male has an ABPI measured at 1.45 despite painful ischemic foot ulcers. What pathological vascular process explains this falsely elevated reading?\n\n- **A.** Mönckeberg's medial calcific sclerosis (arterial calcification rendering vessels non-compressible by standard sphygmomanometer cuffs)\n- **B.** Severe coarctation of the aorta\n- **C.** Superficial thrombophlebitis\n- **D.** Active deep vein thrombosis\n- **E.** Renal artery stenosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAn ABPI > 1.30 - 1.40 is abnormally high and indicates severely calcified, rigid, non-compressible arteries (Mönckeberg's arteriosclerosis), classic in long-standing diabetes mellitus and end-stage renal disease. The blood pressure cuff cannot compress the hardened vessel, generating artificially high pressure readings. In these patients, Toe-Brachial Index (TBI) is used because digital arteries are rarely calcified.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-02 08:18:52.750473+00	\N
0ec2f391-1db2-49d0-82b0-cbe390878d7e	31fbd97a-7577-4959-a0ed-132fda326997	pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis-management	Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management	article	# Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nIn severe cases, dizziness and/or syncope (due to right ventricular failure).\n-\n- Other signs that may be present include: Tachycardia (heart rate greater than 100 beats per minute).\n- Hypoxia. . pyrexia. Elevated jugular venous pressure.\n- Gallop rhythm, a widely split second heart sound, tricuspid regurgitant murmur.\n- pleural rub. Hypotension (systolic blood pressure less than 90 mmHg) and cardiogenic shock\n- (rare signs indicating central PE and/or a severely reduced.\n\n## Differential Diagnosis\nPneumothorax.\n- Pneumonia.\n- Acute bronchitis.\n- Acute exacerbation of asthma\n-\n- ACS Acute coronary syndrome.\n- Acute congestive heart failure.\n- Dissecting or rupturing aortic aneurysm.\n-\n- Diagnosis and initial management (NICE G u i déiifiéj¯\n\n- Pulmonary embolism rule-out criteria (the PERC rule) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) Age 250 HR 2100 02 sat on room air Unilateral leg swelling Hemoptysis Recent surgery or trauma Prior PE or DVT Hormone use Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1\n- Note — If no criteria are positive and clinician's pre-test probability is <15%, PERC Rule criteria are satisfied.\n\n- If-PE-is-suspected, probability of PE Clinical feature Clinical signs and symptoms of DVT (minimum of leg swelling and pain with palpation of the deep veins)\n- An alternative diagnosis is less likely than 100 beat/min Immobilisation for more than 3 days or\n- surgery in the previous 4 weeks Previous DVT/PE\n- Haemoptysis\n- Malignancy (on treatment, treated in the last 6 months, or palliative) Clinical probability simplified score\n- PE likely\n- PE unlikely Points 3 3 1.5 1.5 1.5 1 1 Points\n-\n- More than 4 points 4 points or less\n\n- PE score more than 4 points)\n- Offer a computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) immediately if possible OR\n- If patient allergic to contrast media or has renal impairment then assess the suitability of a ventilation/perfusion single photon emission computed tomography (WQ SPECT) scan or, if a V/Q SPECT scan is not available, a VIQ planar scan, as an alternative to CT PA.\n- If a CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan cannot be done immediately, offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation (DOAC — apixaban or rivaroxaban. If neither suitable then LMWH followed by dabigatran or edoxaban or LMWH and a VKA).\n- Steps-after-CTPA,MQSPECTMMQ-planar scan\n- If PE is identified by CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan:\n- offer or continue anticoagulation treatment at least 3 months to people with confirmed proximal DVT or PE.\n- if anticoagulation treatment is contraindicated, consider a mechanical intervention (Inferior vena caval filters, Elastic graduated compression stockings, Percutaneous mechanical thrombectomy)\n- take into account contraindications, comorbidities and the person's preferences\n- Measure baseline full blood count, renal and hepatic function, PT and APTT but start anticoagulation before results available.\n\n- For people with suspected/ confirmed PE and haemodynamic instability (unstable patient):\n- offer continuous LJFH infusion and consider thrombolytic therapy\n- Thrombolysis is indicated for patients with severe circulatory compromise or a picture of massive pulmonary embolism. Prior proof of PE is not needed if the patient is peri-arrest and thrombolysis should be administered immediately (50mg Alteplase as a bolus) in such patients.\n- If PE is not identified by CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan:\n- consider a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan if DVT is suspected\n- If DVT not suspected — stop interim therapeutic anticoagulation, think about alternative diagnoses.\n- PE unlikely_(WelIs score 4 points or less) A D-dimer test with the result available within 4 hours if possible OR\n- if the D-dimer test result cannot be obtained within 4 hours (in any setting), offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation while awaiting the result.\n- If the D-dimer test result is positive\n- offer a computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CT PA) and follow the pathway-as-mentioned-aboye±\n- If the D-dimer test result is negative\n- stop interim therapeutic anticoagulation (but do not stop long-term anticoagulation if being used for secondary prevention) Think about alternative diagnoses.\n\n- Long-term anticoagulation for secondary_ prevention\n- Assess and discuss the benefits and risks of continuing, stopping or changing the anticoagulant with people who have had anticoagulation treatment for 3 months (3 to 6 months for people with active cancer) after a proximal DVT or PE\n- Consider stopping anticoagulation treatrnent 3 months (3 to 6 months for people with active cancer) after a provoked DVT or PE if the provoking factor is no longer present and the clinical course has been uncomplicated\n- Consider continuing anticoagulation beyond 3 months (6 months for people with active cancer) after an unprovoked DVT or PE. Key_points\n- Any haemodynamically unstable patient with suspected/ confirmed should be immediately thrombolysed (alteplase 50mg bolus).\n- Anti-coagulant drugs — use DOAC (apixaban or rivaroxaban) as first choice. If neither suitable, offer one of:\n- LM\\NH for at least 5 days followed by dabigatran or edoxaban OR\n- LMVVH and a VKA for at least 5 days, or until INR at least 2.0 on 2 consecutive readings, then a VKA alone.\n- Anticoagulation treatrnent for DVT or PE with triple positive antiphospholipid syndrome — Offer LMWH and a VKA for at least 5 days or until INR at least 2.0 on 2 consecutive readings, then a VKA alone.\n- Avoid DOACs in positive antiphospholipid syndrome.\n\n- Suspected PE: diagnosis and initial management Determine 2—1evel PE Wells Score suspected Wells score\n- 4 points PE likely Immediate CT PAZ (CT pulmonary angiogram) Interim therapeutic anticoagulation" while awaiting CTPA Wells score s 4 points PE unlikely Quantitative D—dimer test' and result in 4 hours Interim therapeutic anticoagulations\n- s while awaiting result D-dimer positive DVT not suspected D-dimer negative CTPA positive Diagnose PE and offer or continue treatment  CTPA negative DVT suspected Consider proximal leg vein ultrasound scan Stop any anticoagulation and think about other diagnoses Consider outpatient treatment for Iow-risk PE PE algorithm\n- 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 08:18:52.750473+00	\N
5a9038b6-1713-464d-a414-f3435ab20e8f	a1913ad2-1832-4a7d-979b-9fb681701651	accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen	Accessory Organs of the Abdomen	article	The Liver:\nlargest visceral organ and the largest gland in the human body.\nPlays an important role in production and secretion of bile\nused in emulsification of fats and detoxification by filtering the\nblood to remove bacteria and foreign particles that have\ngained entrance from the intestine.\nStorage of carbohydrate as glycogen and to be broken down\nlater to glucose\nProduction and storage of lipids as triglycerides; plasma\nprotein synthesis albumin and globulin\nProduction of blood coagulants (fibrinogen and\nprothrombin), anticoagulants (heparin), and bile pigments\n(bilirubin and biliverdin) from the breakdown of hemoglobin\nReservoir for blood and platelets; and storage of certain\nvitamins, iron, and copper.\nIn the fetus, the liver is important in the manufacture of Rbcs\nContains internal portal triads contains the portal vein,\nhepatic artery, and bile ducts.\nAnatomical relations of the liver\nAnatomical surfaces of liver\nThe liver has a diaphragmatic surface in the anterior,\nsuperior, and posterior directions and a visceral surface in the\ninferior direction.\nThe diaphragmatic surface of the liver is related anteriorly to\nthe anterior abdominal wall and rib cage and superiorly to\nthe diaphragm.\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nThe visceral surface of the liver is related to the oesophagus,\nright anterior part of the stomach, superior part of the\nduodenum, lesser omentum, gallbladder, right colic flexure,\nright transverse colon, right kidney and right adrenal gland.\nLigaments of the Liver\nHepatic Recesses: They are of clinical importance, as infected\nfluids can collect in these areas, forming an abscess.\nStructure of the Liver\nThe entire liver is covered by a fibrous layer, known as Glisson's\ncapsule. The ligaments and surface depressions of the liver\ndivide it into four lobes.\n\n### 1: Right lobe and left lobe by the attachment of the falciform\n\nligament.\n\n### 2: There are two further 'accessory' lobes that arise from the\n\nright lobe, and are located on the visceral surface of liver:\nThe caudate lobe is located on the upper aspect of the\nvisceral surface. It lies between the inferior vena cava and a\nfossa produced by the ligamentum venosum.\nThe quadrate lobe is located on the lower aspect of the\nvisceral surface. It lies between the gallbladder and a fossa\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nproduced by the ligamentum trees.\nBetween the caudate and quadrate lobes is a deep fissure,\nknown as the porta hepatis. It transmits all the vessels,\nnerves and ducts entering or leaving the liver.\nArterial Supply and Venous Drainage\nThe liver has a unique dual blood supply:\nHepatic artery proper 25% Supplies the liver with arterial\nblood.\nHepatic portal vein 75% Supplies the liver with deoxygenated\nblood, carrying nutrients absorbed from the small intestine.\nThe portal vein is the final common pathway for the transport\nof venous blood from the spleen, pancreas, gallbladder and\nabdominal part of the gastrointestinal tract.\nPortal vein is formed from the union of the superior\nmesenteric and splenic vein posterior to the neck of the\npancreas at the level of vertebra L2.\nVenous drainage of the liver is achieved through three hepatic\nveins, which drain into the inferior vena cava.\nInnervation of liver\nThe parenchyma of the liver is innervated by the hepatic\nplexus, which contains sympathetic fibers from the coeliac\nplexus and parasympathetic fibers from vagus nerve\nThese fibers enter the liver at the porta hepatis and follow\nthe course of branches of the hepatic artery and portal vein\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_04_p3.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Liver cirrhosis and portal hypertension:\n> Liver cirrhosis is a condition in which liver cells are\n> progressively destroyed and replaced by fatty and fibrous\n> tissue that surrounds the intrahepatic blood vessels and biliary\n> radicals, impeding the circulation of blood through the liver. It\n> is caused by chronic alcohol abuse (alcoholism), viral hepatitis,\n> and ingestion of poisons.\n\nLiver cirrhosis causes portal hypertension and portosystemic\nanastomosis occur at specific points.\nThe gastroesophageal junction around the cardia of the\nstomach where the left gastric vein and its tributaries form a\nportosystemic anastomosis with tributaries to the azygos\nsystem of veins of the caval system.\nThe anus where the superior rectal vein of the portal system\nanastomoses with the middle and inferior rectal veins of the\nsystemic venous system.\nThe anterior abdominal wall around the umbilicus where the\nportal paraumbilical veins anastomose with systemic veins\non the anterior abdominal wall.\nClinical features:\nEsophageal varices dilated veins in the lower part of the\nesophagus\nHemorrhoids: dilated veins around the anal canal\nCaput medusa: dilated veins around the umbilicus\nSpider nevi or spider angioma (small, red, spiderlike arterioles\nin the cheeks, neck, and shoulder)\nAscites: accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity\nedema in the legs: lower albumin levels facilitate\nwater retention.\nSplenomegaly: enlarged spleen resulting from venous\ncongestion causing sequestered blood cells that lead to\nthrombocytopenia and easy bruising\nHepatomegaly due to fatty changes\nGall bladdar:\n\nIs a pear-shaped sac located in a fossa between the right and\nquadrate lobe of liver\nThe gallbladder has:\nThe fundus rounded blind end which may project from the\ninferior border of the liver.\nThe body: Major part which may lie against the transverse\ncolon and the superior part of the duodenum.\nThe neck: Narrow part which tapers to become continuous\nwith the cystic duct.\nNote: The neck contains a mucosal fold, known as Hartmann's\npouch, which is a common site for gallstones to become\nlodged.\nGall bladder blood supply: cystic artery branch of right hepatic\nartery. Right hepatic artery is itself branch of common hepatic\nartery one of branch of coeliac trunk.\nInnervation of gall bladder:The gallbladder receives both\nsympathetic and vagal supply:\nsympathetic: via the coeliac plexus\nvagal: via the hepatic branches of anterior vagal trunk\nNote: Inflammation of the gallbladder may present with pain\nin the right upper quadrant and also in the right shoulder due\nto irritation of the diaphragmatic peritoneum supplied by the\nphrenic nerve\nBilliary tree:\n1.Right and left hepatic ducts: Are formed by union of the\nintrahepatic ductules from each lobe of the liver and drain bile\nfrom their corresponding halves of the liver\n2.Common hepatic duct Is formed by union of the right and\nleft hepatic ducts.\nCommon hepatic duct is accompanied by the proper hepatic\nartery and the portal vein.\n\n3.Cystic duct Has spiral folds (valves) to keep it constantly\nopen, and thus bile can pass upward into the gallbladder when\nthe common bile duct is closed.\nCystic duct Joins the common hepatic duct to form the\ncommon bile duct.\nCystic duct is a common site of gallstone impaction.\n\n### 4: Common bile duct: Formed by the union of the common\n\nhepatic duct and the cystic duct The common bile duct can be\ndivided into three main regions, based on its relation to the\nduodenum:\nSupraduodenal region: located lateral to the proper hepatic\nartery and anterior to the portal vein in the free margin of the\nlesser omentum.\nSecond retroduodenal region: Descends behind the first\npart of the duodenum and runs through the head of the\npancreas. Joins the main pancreatic duct to form the\nhepatopancreatic duct which enters the second part of the\nduodenum at major duodenal papillae. The gastroduodenal\nartery lies on its left aspect and the portal vein on its\nposterior aspect\nThe third infraduodenal region is the lower one-third which\nlies in a groove on the posterior surface of the head of the\npancreas, to the left of the second part of the duodenum and\nanterior to the right renal vein and inferior vena cava.\nPancreas:\nThe pancreas lies mostly posterior to the stomach. It extends\nacross the posterior abdominal wall from the duodenum on\nthe right, to the spleen on the left. It lies in the epigastric\nregion and left hypochondrium.\nThe pancreas is a retroperitoneal organ (except for a small part\nof its tail) and consists of a head, uncinate process, neck, body\nand tail.\nHead: lies within the C-shaped concavity of the duodenum.\nThe uncinate process is a projection of the inferior part of\nthe head that extends medially behind the superior\nmesenteric vessels.\n\nThe neck connects the head and body of the pancreas and\nlies anterior to the Junction of the superior mesenteric and\nsplenic veins, forming the hepatic portal vein.\nThe body extends from the neck to the tail and the longest\npart of the pancreas lies behind the stomach and to the left\nof the superior mesenteric vessels.\nThe intraperitoneal tail passes between layers of the\nsplenorenal ligament. It is related to the left kidney\nposteriorly, the hilum of the spleen laterally, the left colic\nflexure inferiorly and the stomach anteriorly.\nDucts of pancreas:  Main pancreatic duct: Begins in the tail\nand runs to the right along the entire pancreas\nJoins the bile duct to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla\n(ampulla of Vater) enters second part of duodenum at\nduodenal papilla\nAccessory pancreatic duct: Begins in the inferior portion of\nthe head and drains a small portion of the head and the\nbody, empties at the minor duodenal papilla 2cm above the\nmajor papilla\nBlood supply:Receives blood from branches of the splenic\nartery\nThe head is additionally supplied by the superior and inferior\npancreaticoduodenal arteries which are branches of the\ngastroduodenal from (coeliac trunk) and superior mesenteric\narteries respectively.\nInnervation: Visceral afferent fibres travel in the greater and\nlesser splanchnic nerves.\nThe segmental level of innervation is T6 -T10.\nPain typically radiates to the middle of the back due to\nirritation of the parietal peritoneum covering the pancreas.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pancreatitis: Inflammation of pancreas causes pain in upper\n> abdomen typically epigastrium radiating to back\n> Spleen:\n\nis a large vascular lymphatic organ lying against the\ndiaphragm and ribs 9 to 11 in the hypochondriac region,\ncovered by peritoneum except at the hilum, and supported by\nthe  splenogastric and splenorenal ligaments\nRelations:The spleen lies posterior to the stomach, superior to\nthe left colic flexure and lateral to the left kidney and tail of the\npancreas.\nFunctional overview: Composed of white pulp and red pulp.\nWhite pulp: which consists of primarily lymphatic tissue\naround the central arteries and is the primary site of immune\nand phagocytic action, participates in the immune response\n(protection against infection); and produces mature\nlymphocytes, macrophages and antibodies in white pulp\nRed pulp: which consists of venous sinusoids and splenic\ncords and is the primary site of filtration. Filters blood\n(macrophages remove damaged and worn-out erythrocytes\nand platelets) blood reservoir, storing blood and platelets in\nthe red pulp.\nSpleen Is hematopoietic in early life and later destroys aged\n(i.e., worn-out) red blood cells in the red pulp and releases\nhemoglobin from the spent RBCs.\nImportant to remember here hematopoiesis at different\nstages of life.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: Spleen injury most Likely associated with**\n> fracture of rib 10\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_05_p8.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\nAdrenal glands:\nPaired endocrine glands located over medial aspect of upper\npoles of each kidney.\nPerinephric(or renal) fascia encloses the adrenal glands and\nthe kidneys.\nThe adrenal glands sit in close proximity to many other\nstructures in the abdomen.\nInnervation:\nThe adrenal glands are innervated by the coeliac plexus and\ngreater splanchnic nerves.\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 6](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_06_p9.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 6 (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 7](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_07_p9.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 7 (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n![Accessory Organs of the Abdomen - Clinical Figure 8](/lessons/images/abdomen-peritoneum/accessory-organs-of-the-abdomen/figure_08_p9.png)\n*Accessory Organs of the Abdomen — Clinical Figure 8 (Page 9 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.462237+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.812947+00	\N
c8643ca8-dc50-4f4b-9f6f-5b9404f7d312	8e9e07dc-008d-45f9-b4d1-4cc886b20dc5	painful-eye	Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies	article	# Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- C-reactive protein\n- B12 and folate\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nAcute treatment for idiopathic ON includes high-dose oral or intravenous corticosteroid treatrnent. Herpes simplex-Ocular infection\n- Herpes simplex virus (HSV) ocular infections can cause inflammation of the retina, iris, cornea, conjunctiva, eyelids, and surrounding skin.\n\nInfection is usually caused by HSV type 1 (HSV-I), and rarely by HSV type 2 (HSV-2),\n- typically in association with orogenital sex. Infection and inflammation of different parts of the eye causes:\n- Cornea — keratitis.\n- Iris and associated uveal tract — iritis or iridocyclitis; uveitis.\n- Retina — retinitis.\n- Conjunctiva — conjunctivitis.\n- Eyelids — blepharitis.\n- Surrounding skin — periocular dermatitis\n-\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nSymptoms Malaise and fever\n- Eye pain Eye irritation or photophobia Eye watering\n-\n- Blurred vision\n- Sign An acute red eye\n- Crops of vesicles and ulcers on an erythematous base, or pustules along the lid\n- margin or periocular skin A hazy cornea\n-\n- A fixed irregular pupil Reduced corneal sensitivity — suggests keratitis\n- Reduced visual acuity\n-\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n(NICE guideline)\n\n- Refer all cases of suspected ocular herpes simplex infection to eye casualty, or an emergency eye service for same-day assessrnent and specialist management.\n- if emergency same-day assessment is not possible or practical, seek specialist advice from an ophthalmologist regarding initiating drug treatment such as topical antivirals ( aciclovir ointment or ganciclovir gel)\n- Acute Herpes Simplex: in non-contact lens wearing adults and where HSK is confined to the epithelium with classic dendritic appearance, commence antiviral therapy with one of the following:\n- oc. aciclovir 3%, e.g. Aciclovir Agepha, ophthalmic preparation (unpreserved)\n- ganciclovir 0.15% ophthalmic gel\n\nherpes simplex keratitis/corneal ulcer/dendritic ulcer Sexually transmitted eye infection *Neisseria qonorrhoea Symptoms usually develops within 12-24 hours Patient will have copious mucopurulent discharge Eyelid swelling Tender peri-auricular lymphadenopathy Neisseria gonorrhoea conjunctivitis has a high risk of complications, including uveitis, severe keratitis and corneal perforation\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nSingle dose of lg of 1M ceftriaxone, together with single episode of ocular lavage with saline. Behget's disease\n- :\n- Chlamvdia trachomatis often presents longer than 2 weeks Low grade irritation and mucous discharge in a sexually active person. Pre-auricular lymphadenopathy maybe present Most cases are unilateral but can be bilateral Manaqement Topical tetracycline ointment ODS for 6 weeks Oral doxycycline 100mg BD for 1-2 weeks Oral azithromycin lg dose, then 500mg orally fortwo days Oral azithromycin 500mg QDS for 1 week Note: examiner may give you traveling history in most of cases for-example Thailand trip etc\n- it is a complex multi-system disorder of unknown aetiology characteristically presenting with recurrent oral ulcers\n- It is presumed to be an autoimmune disease and includes involvement of the mucocutaneous, ocular, cardiovascular, renal, gastrointestinal, pulmonary, vascular, musculoskeletal, urological and central nervous systems.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nClassic triad : oral ulceration, genital ulceration and eye disease\n- Other symptoms includes tiredness, malaise, muscle pains, and transient fevers. Headaches can be common\n- Eye-features;-uveitis, hypopyon, retinal vasculitis, retinal hemorrhage, blurred vision\n- Neurological: memory impairment is the most common CNS manifestation. Impairment of balance, speech and rnovement can also occur\n- Cardiovascular: vasculitis of large and small vessels\n- affects large joints\n- Gastrointestinal: cramping abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nFBC, CRP, ESR\n- Anti-phospholipid antibodies positive in 25% pts\n- Patients positive for HLA-B51 or HLA-B5 are more likely to be male and also have higher prevalences of genital ulcers, ocular manifestations and skin manifestation.\n- Pathergy test: minor skin traurna induces an inflammatory papule or pustule after 24-48 hours. This is positive in up to 60% of patients\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nTopical corticosteroids (eg, triamcinolone paste) are effective for oral or genital ulcerations\n- Patients with eye involvement need early and more aggressive treatment, usually with a brief course of corticosteroid with a longer-term immunosuppressant (eg, azathioprine)\n- Patients with CNS involvement require the most aggressive treatment, usually with a corticosteroid plus either azathioprine, cyclophosphamide, or a TNF-aIpha\n\ninhibitor. previous\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Painful Eye - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/painful-eye/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Painful Eye — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Painful Eye - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/painful-eye/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Painful Eye — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Painful Eye - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/painful-eye/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n*Painful Eye — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Painful Eye - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/painful-eye/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n*Painful Eye — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 7 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
66f7f024-b694-486d-bf59-b9c9dc126d75	8e9e07dc-008d-45f9-b4d1-4cc886b20dc5	sba-quiz-ophthalmology-painful-eye	Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 26-year-old soft contact lens wearer presents with a 24-hour history of severe, worsening right eye pain, photophobia, foreign body sensation, and purulent discharge. Slit-lamp biomicroscopy reveals a focal, round, white-yellowish corneal stromal opacity (infiltrate) measuring 2 mm with an overlying epithelial defect that stains with fluorescein, and an early 1 mm hypopyon. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies)](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology/painful-eye/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Bacterial Keratitis (Corneal Ulcer)\n- **B.** Simple corneal abrasion\n- **C.** Viral conjunctivitis\n- **D.** Recurrent corneal erosion\n- **E.** Subconjunctival haemorrhage\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMicrobial (Bacterial) Keratitis / Corneal Ulcer is a sight-threatening emergency, especially in contact lens wearers. Characterized by severe pain, photophobia, ciliary flush, epithelial defect overlying a dense stromal infiltrate (white spot on cornea), and anterior chamber inflammation / hypopyon. It can cause rapid corneal melting and perforation within 24-48 hours. Urgent corneal scrapings for microbiology and intensive topical fortified antibiotics are required.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich aggressive Gram-negative bacterium is notoriously associated with contact lens wear (especially overnight wear or rinsing lenses in tap water) and can cause rapid corneal stromal liquefactive necrosis and perforation within 24 hours?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies)](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology/painful-eye/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **B.** Streptococcus pneumoniae\n- **C.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **D.** Moraxella lacunata\n- **E.** Haemophilus influenzae\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPseudomonas aeruginosa accounts for over 60-70% of contact lens-associated bacterial ulcers. It produces potent extracellular virulence proteases and elastases that dissolve corneal collagen matrix and stroma rapidly, causing rapid corneal melting and perforation ('corneal melt') in less than 24 hours. First-line therapy is intensive topical fluoroquinolones (Ofloxacin / Ciprofloxacin hourly) or fortified aminoglycosides.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 40-year-old male presents with unilateral eye pain, photophobia, foreign body sensation, and watery redness. Slit-lamp examination after instilling fluorescein dye demonstrates a classic, delicate, branching linear epithelial ulcer with terminal bulbs (buds) under cobalt blue light. What pathogen causes this?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies)](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology/painful-eye/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1 (HSV-1) Dendritic Keratitis\n- **B.** Acanthamoeba\n- **C.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **D.** Adenovirus (Epidemic Keratoconjunctivitis)\n- **E.** Fungal keratitis (Fusarium)\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nDendritic corneal ulceration with terminal end-bulbs on fluorescein staining is pathognomonic of Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV-1) epithelial keratitis. Corneal sensation is characteristically reduced or absent (hypoesthesia). Treatment is topical antiviral ointment (Ganciclovir 0.15% gel or Aciclovir 3% ointment 5 times daily). Topical corticosteroids are strictly CONTRAINDICATED as they trigger uncontrolled viral replication and geographic ulceration.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhy are topical corticosteroid eye drops (e.g. Dexamethasone, Prednisolone) strictly CONTRAINDICATED in the emergency department for undiagnosed red, painful eyes or suspected viral keratitis?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Painful Eye Differential & Corneal Emergencies)](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology/painful-eye/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Corticosteroids inhibit local cell-mediated immunity; in Herpes Simplex keratitis, they accelerate viral replication, converting a superficial dendritic ulcer into an extensive, destructive 'geographic' corneal ulcer and precipitating corneal perforation or fungal superinfection\n- **B.** They cause severe conjunctival discolouration\n- **C.** They cause acute detachment of the retina\n- **D.** They cause systemic hypoglycaemia\n- **E.** They cause sudden cataracts within 1 hour\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTopical steroids in an undiagnosed red eye are dangerous. If HSV keratitis is present, steroids accelerate viral proliferation, transforming small dendritic ulcers into large, amoeboid, necrotizing 'geographic' ulcers, causing secondary fungal keratitis, rapid corneal thinning, and perforation. Furthermore, steroids elevate intraocular pressure (steroid-induced glaucoma).\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old metalworker presents 6 hours after arc-welding without a protective welding helmet. He wakes up with agonizing bilateral eye pain, severe photophobia, blepharospasm, and foreign body sensation. Slit-lamp biomicroscopy reveals diffuse, punctate, superficial fluorescein uptake across both corneas ('punctate epithelial erosions'). What is the diagnosis?\n\n- **A.** Ultraviolet Photokeratitis ('Welder's Arc Eye' / Snow Blindness)\n- **B.** Acute glaucoma\n- **C.** Traumatic hyphema\n- **D.** Bacterial keratitis\n- **E.** Central retinal artery occlusion\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPhotokeratitis (arc eye, welder's flash, snow blindness) is acute corneal radiation injury caused by exposure to ultraviolet (UVB/UVC) radiation from welding arcs, tanning lamps, or reflection from snow. After a characteristic symptom-free latent period of 6-12 hours, diffuse apoptosis of corneal epithelial cells causes severe pain, photophobia, foreign body sensation, and diffuse bilateral punctate epithelial erosions on fluorescein staining. Treatment is supportive: oral analgesia, antibiotic ointment, and recovery within 24-48 hours.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
67874436-46ef-4e2d-89ae-057299757a73	8e9e07dc-008d-45f9-b4d1-4cc886b20dc5	red-eye	Red Eye Assessment & Triage	article	# Red Eye Assessment & Triage\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Causes Allergens bind to rnast cells in the conjunctiva causing them to degranulate, which\n- initiates an inflammatory cascade. Allergic conjunctivitis can be exacerbated by problems with the eyebrows, eyelids,\n- or eyelashes which act as barriers to allergens. A normal tear film also helps to remove allergens from the eye surface.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nthe\n- bilateral) — if itching is not present an alternative cause should be considered. Itching may also be described as 'burning or stinging\n-\n- Watery or mucoid discharge — sometimes referred to as 'tearing'. Discharge may be stringy or ropey due to the presence of a small arnount of mucous. Conjunctival redness (hyperaemia injection).\n- Conjunctival swelling (chemosis) — bulbar and tarsal conjunctiva may bulge over\n- lid margin or limbus. Conjunctival papillary reaction.\n-\n- Eyelid oedema (swelling) — periorbital oedema can occur in severe cases Allergic conjunctivitis may coexist with other atopic conditions including Rhinitis, rhinosinusitis, Asthma, Urticaria, Eczema.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nAdvise the person on non-pharmacological management including; Avoidance of allergens for example through dust mite, mould, and animal dander\n- control, avoidance of pets and proper ventilation of home and office environments. Washing the hair before going to bed may help reduce allergen exposure.\n- Avoidance of eye rubbing.\n-\n\n- Application of cold compresses to the eyes (for 5—10 minutes once or twice daily) to relieve symptoms.\n- Advise the person that a long loading period is required and mast cell stabilizers need to be applied routinely for at least 2 weeks to provide prophylactic benefit. (note: certain products are not licensed in young children)\n- Advise the person that after using eye drops or eye ointments, they should not drive or perform other skilled tasks until vision is clear.\n- If the person is a contact lens wearer, advise them that contact lens use should be avoided for the duration of topical treatrnent.\n- Arrange review in 1 week to assess therapeutic response — safety net in the interim and ensure the patient is aware of red flag symptoms that indicate the need for urgent review. Infective conjunctivitis\n- Infective conjunctivitis can be acute (persisting for less than 4 weeks), chronic (persisting for more than 4 weeks), or recurrent.\n- Acute onset conjunctival erythema.\n- Discomfort which may be described as 'grittiness', 'foreign body' or 'burning' sensation.\n- Watering and discharge which may cause transient blurring of vision.\n- Viral Conjunctivitis\n- The rnost common cause of viral conjunctivitis is adenovirus (65—90% of cases)\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nMild to moderate erythema of the palpebral or bulbar conjunctiva, follicles on eyelid eversion and lid oedema\n- Petechial (pin-point) subconjunctival haemorrhages\n\nPseudomembranes— Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis (due to adenovirus) can lead\n- to pseudomembrane formation along with severe pain, subconjunctival haemorrhage, visual changes, and photophobia. Less discharge (usually watery) than bacterial conjunctivitis.\n-\n- Mild to moderate pruritus.\n- Pharyngoconjunctival fever (due to adenovirus) can lead to fever, pharyngitis, periauricular lymphadenopathy, and bilateral conjunctivitis occasionally with corneal involvement.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof acute (non-herpetic) viral conjunctivitis (NICE güiidÄiöEj¯ Reassure the person that most cases of acute, infectious conjunctivitis are self-\n- limiting and do not require antimicrobial treatment — viral (non-herpetic) conjunctivitis usually resolves within one to two weeks without treatment.\n- Advise the person that symptoms may be eased with self-care measures such as: Bathing/cleaning the eyelids with cotton wool soaked in sterile saline or boiled and\n- cooled water to remove any discharge. Cool compresses applied gently around the eye area.\n- Use of lubricating agents or artificial tears.\n- Avoid antibiotic prescription\n- Inform the person that infective conjunctivitis is contagious and they should try to\n- prevent spread of infection to their other eye and other people by:\n- Washing hands frequently with soap and water. Using separate towels and flannels.\n- Avoiding close contact with others especially if they are a healthcare professional or\n- child care provider — they may be infectious for up to 14 days from onset.\n- The UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) does not recommend an exclusion period from school, nursery or childminders unless an outbreak or cluster of cases occurs.\n\n- If the person re-attends with symptoms of conjunctivitis, consider sending swabs for viral PCR (for adenovirus and Herpes sirnplex virus [HSV]) and bacterial culture and if appropriate, offer empirical topical antibiotics. See the section on Prescribing for further information.\n- Consider discussion with/referral to ophthalmology if symptoms persist for more than 7—10 days after initiating treatment.\n- Bacterial conjunctivitis\n- The most common bacterial causes of conjunctivitis are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus and Haemophilus influenzae.\n- Other bacterial causes include Moraxella catarrhalis, Chlamydia trachomatis, and Neisseria gonorrhoea.\n- In children, bacterial conjunctivitis is most often caused by Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Moraxella catarrhalis.\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nPurulent or mucopurulent discharge with crusting of the lids which may be stuck together on waking.\n- If discharge is mucopurulent and copious, infection with Neisseria gonorrhoeae should be considered.\n- Mild or no pruritus.\n- Pre-auricular lymphadenopathy — often seen with hyperacute bacterial conjunctivitis (such as Neisseria gonorrhoea).\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n(NICE guideline)\n- Advise the person that most cases of bacterial conjunctivitis are self-limiting and resolve within 5—7 days without treatment.\n- Treat with topical antibiotics if severe or circumstances require rapid resolution. A delayed treatment strategy may be appropriate — advise the person to initiate\n\ntopical antibiotics if symptoms have not resolved within 3 days. Options for topical antibiotics include:\n- Chloramphenicol 0.5% drops — apply 1 drop 2 hourly for 2 days, then reduce frequency depending on the severity of infection (3-4 times daily is usually sufficient for less severe infection). Continue use until 48 hours after infection has cleared.\n- Chloramphenicol 1% ointment — apply 3-4 times daily. Continue use until 48 hours after infection has cleared.\n- Fusidic acid 1% eye drops — apply 1 drop twice daily. Continue use until 48 hours after infection has cleared.\n- Advise the person that there is no recommended exclusion period from school, nursery, or childminders for isolated cases but that many nursery and primary schools may nevertheless have an exclusion policy.\n- If the person re-attends with symptoms of conjunctivitis, consider sending swabs for viral PCR (for adenovirus and Herpes simplex virus [HSV]) and bacterial culture and if appropriate, offer empirical topical antibiotics. See the section on Prescribing for further information.\n- Consider discussion with/referral to ophthalmology if symptoms persist for more than 7—10 days after initiating treatment.\n\nMucopurulent discharge (bacterial conjunctivitis)\n- Contact lens associated conjunctivitis\n- Inflammation may be seen in the superior conjunctiva especially under the upper lid — topical fluorescein may identify corneal staining (epithelial defect).\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n(NICE guideline)\n- If topical fluorescein does not identify any corneal staining and the person does not require referral to ophthalmology:\n- Advise them to immediately stop contact lens use.\n\nAdvise regular bathing/cleaning of the eyelids with cotton wool soaked in sterile\n- saline or boiled and cooled water to remove any discharge.\n- Advise that contact lens use should be avoided until all symptoms of the infection have gone. Treat the infection — consider use of a topical antibiotic effective against Gram —ve\n- organisms, such as an aminoglycoside (e.g. gentamycin) or a quinolone (e.g. levofloxacin or moxifloxacin).\n- Arrange follow-up as described above.\n- Sub-conjunctival haemorrhage this is due to a bleed into the subconjunctival space. There may or may not be a\n- history of a contributory factor, such as straining with coughing or constipation. It may be associated with hypertension\n-\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\nrevealed: an area of localized, well-demarcated haemorrhage in one eye\n- absence of pain\n- no reduction of visual acuity\n- normal pupil reactions, and no corneal staining\n-\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n(NICE guideline) Reassure the person that the haemorrhage will clear in 1—2 weeks\n- Measure the person's blood pressure, and if raised, manage appropriately\n-\n- Check the person's international normalized ratio (INR) if they are taking warfarin, and manage accordingly\n\n- Sub-conjunctival hemorrhage previous  Next\n- 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Red Eye - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/red-eye/figure_01_p7.jpeg)\n*Red Eye — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 7 Reference)*\n\n![Red Eye - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/red-eye/figure_02_p9.jpeg)\n*Red Eye — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 9 Reference)*\n\n![Red Eye - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/ophthalmology-emergencies/red-eye/figure_03_p9.jpeg)\n*Red Eye — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 9 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+0