4d6922bc-0fdd-4e23-8a01-b62019984e88	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	expert-clinical-sba-mock-3	Expert Clinical SBA Mock 3	article	# Expert Clinical SBA Mock 3\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Netezdiagnosis depends on X-ray so be familiar with both per-lunate and lunate. / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 15:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED after fall into the canal. His immersion is estimated to be around 10 minutes. On examination, he is vitally stable. What would be your step of management? A\n- Discharge him\n- **B)** Immersion greater than 10minutes would be bad x prognosis\n- **C)** Prescribe antibiotics E\n- Admit him Explanation;\n- The appropriate step would be to request an ABC.\n- All asymptomatic can be discharged after 6 hours of observation. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Management of\n- Recognise distress and call for help.\n- Provide flotation to stop the process of drowning. Reaching, throwing, or dropping a buoyant object or manoeuvring a craft to the victim is preferred\n- The victim should be removed from the water in as near a horizontal position as possible, with the alrway open.\n- On land, the victim should be placed supine with trunk and head at the same level\n- If cervical spine injury is suspected, the cervical spine may be held in mid-line,\n- Those with confirmed cervical spine injury usually have obvious history, or signs of significant trauma; cervical spine imaging is not recommended for patients witnessed to have no preceding trauma prior to a drowning.\n- Follow ABCDE protocol\n- Maintain the airway. Remove regurgitated fluid/debris by suction of the upper airway. Ensure adequate ventilation and correction of hypoxia.\n- Manage hypothermia - passive re-warming (including removing wet clothing, and drying and covering the patient) is the preferred treatment option for mild cases.\n- Active internal re-warming techniques include airway re-warming with humidified oxygen at 406C (1040F), warmed intravenous fluids, and peritoneal lavage. 6 8 in\n- CPR'\n- Key-points\n- The initial management of drowning follows the ATLS principles. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Management of hypoxaemia and circulatory failure is key to the management of drowning.\n- Check an arterial blood gas on all patients with a history of submersion, as a surprising degree of hypoxia may be present in an asymptomatic individual.\n- Patients who are asymptomatic and have no evidence of respiratory compromise (no chest x-ray changes or hypoxia on ABC) after 6 hours can be safely discharged home. All symptomatic patients should be admitted for observation.\n- Remember to look for evidence of precipitating causes and associated injuries and manage them appropriately.\n- Submersion minutes is a good prognostic factor and minutes is a poor prognostic factor.\n- Consider child abuse in young children who are drowned in bath tubs, buckets or shallow water.\n- There is no good evidence to support the routine use of prophylactic antibiotics. The incidence of ventilator-associated pneumonia can be 34-52%, and treatment should be initiated as per local protocol. Treatment should ideally be guided by culture results. 23 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 23:\nA 60-year-old male presented to the ED complaining of suprapubic pain and dysuria. He has a history of recurrent Foley's catheter insertion for urethral stricture. His urine dipstick revealed blood. What would be the likely diagnosis in this patient?   A\n- Schtisomiasis\n- **B)** Renal carcinoma C' Bladder stone\n- **D)** Bladder carcinoma Explanation; The patient has urethral stricture and a history of recurrent use Of foleys catheter which cornmonly causes UTI and urinary stones (nephrolithiasis, urolithiasis).\n- Here in this case, the presence of haematuria in urine dipsticks can support the diagnosis of bladder stones The presence of nitrite (with or without leucocyte esterase) in the urine dipstick suggests a urinary tract infection https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper Bladder Stones\n- Clinical features\n- Lower abdominal pain frequent urge to urinate\n- painful or difficult urination\n- hematuria\n- Neurogenic bladder Bladder diverticulum\n- Urinary tract infections -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Enlarged prostate, and kidney stones\n- Risk Factors\n- Being born male Older age, radiation\n- Bladder augmentation\n- Urethral strictures\n- Schistosomiasis\n- Recurrent UTIs\n- Using a Foley catheter\n- Diagnosis Physical exam, medical history, urinalysis, blood test, CT/MRI\n- Medication, ureteral stenting, nephrostomy, shock wave lithotripsy, ureteroscopy Urethral stricture\n- A urethral stricture is a narrowing caused by scarring of the lining of the urethra and the surrounding corpus spongiosum.\n- Strictures can range from less than lcm long to those that extend the entire length of the urethra. They can occur at any point along the urethra, but are most often seen in the bulbar region.\n- Idiopathic - Cause unknown\n- Inflammatory - Caused by infection or inflammation in the urethra, e.g:\n- sexually-transmitted infections (STIs) e.g. gonorrhoea\n- chronic inflammation e.g. lichen sclerosus (LS) also known as balanitis xerotica obliterans (axo)\n- Traumatic - Caused by injury to the urethra, e.g:\n- a fall astride the cross-bar of a bicycle https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- other crushing pelvic trauma\n- Iatrogenic - Caused by medical intervention, e.g. repeated urological procedures that involve passage of instruments along the urethra\n- urethral catheterisation\n- radiotherapy or surgery to adjacent organs\n- Congenital - Inherited conditions e.g. hypospadias (or as a result of previous surgery to correct hypospadias)\n- With minor strictures, patient may not have any symptoms. With tighter strictures, you may experience one or more of the following:\n- poor urinary flow and needing to strain to pass urine\n- Spraying or splitting of the urinary stream\n- Dribbling of urine after urination\n- Needing to pass urine often (urinary frequency)\n- Urinary tract infections\n- Reduced force of ejaculation and low ejaculate volume; and\n- Pain on passing urine.\n- 'f left untreated:\n- A stricture can act as a "bottleneck" which prevents complete bladder emptying. This can cause to retain urine in the bladder after patient pass urine, which can result in bladder, prostate or kidney infections, and lead to the formation of stones. All these conditions can cause long-term bladder damage and kidney failure.\n- Diagnosis\n- A stricture may be diagnosed by:\n- examination - especially if the narrowing is visible at the tip of the penis\n- failure to pass a telescope or catheter - during investigation of patient urinary symptoms, especially when the stricture is close to the bladder\n- A urethrogram - an X-ray performed using contrast medium (dye) put into urethra to show the anatomy\n- An ultrasound scan; and an MRI scan https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Bladder Bulbar stricture\n- Management\n- Observation only - If symptoms are not troublesome, we may simply monitor and intervene only if patient symptoms deteriorate or risk harming patient's longterm health.\n- Under anaesthetic, urologist pass a plastic or metal dilator into urethra to stretch the narrow area.\n- Under an anaesthetic, urologist pass a telescope through urethra to cut the stricture.\n- urethroplasty\n- It involves repairing the diseased section of urethra to stop the stricture from coming back.\n- A-longterm urethral-catheter-gr-suprapu>ig catheter\n- If patient has multiple medical problems or wish to avoid invasive treatment, urologist may recommend putting in a long-term catheter. 32 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 32:\nA 9-months-old infant brought into the ED by his mom with apnea attack while feeding. It was lasted less than 15 seconds. He appears normal now. Which of the following is the sign of BRIJE/ALTE?\n- **A)** Irregular respiratory rate\n- **B)** Born less than 32 weeks\n- **C)** First event x\n- **D)** Vomiting\n- **E)** Pallor https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- an episode in an infant months-of age characterized by:\n- minute duration (typically 20\n- 30s)\n- A return to baseline state\n- Not explained by any identifiable medical condition (i.e. medically well).\n- Includes one or more of the following features:\n- Central cyanosis/pallor\n- Absent, decrease or irregular breathing\n- Marked change in tone (hyper- or hypotonia)\n- Altered level of consciousness\n- NQte=lf the feature has been asked then this question likely to be removed as both irregular breathing and pallor are the features of ALTE/aRUE. 33 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 33:\nAccording to NICE guideline, which of the following is the high-risk sepsis feature in under 5 years of age?\n- **A)** Nasal flaring\n- **B)** Crunting C\n- Poor feeding\n- **D)** HR>140 bpm x\n- **E)** Capillary refill time>3 seconds Explanation;\n- According to NICE guideline, grunting is a high-risk feature in those under 5 years of age. 41 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 41:\nA 1 ()-year-old child with an ankle injury presented to the ED with her mother. His x-ray is given. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC According to Salter-haris classification how you will classify it?\n- **A)** Salter-Harris type-II\n- **B)** Weber-A fracture\n- **C)** Salter-Harr