u can find more cases discussion in the form of examples. 63 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 63:\nWhich of the following finding/sign is an alternative to anterior uveitis?\n- **A)** Small irregular pupil\n- **B)** Posterior synechiae\n- **C)** Keratic precipitates\n- **D)** Increased intraocular pressure x\n- **E)** pain on accommodation Explanation;\n- Intraocular pressure (IOP) is commonly normal in uveitis and raised in acute closure glaucoma. 65 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 65:\nA 41-year-old Jehovah's witness man was brought to the ED after RTA. He bleeds massively. During initial resuscitation, IV fluid was given. However, he requires a blood transfusion. The patient refuses due to his religious belief. What would be your step in management? x\n- **A)** Transfuse blood products\n- **B)** Contact authority like Trust legal department\n- **C)** Control bleeding and give FFP only\n- **D)** DO not transfuse the blood products https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Take consent from his relative and transfuse blood\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> Many Jehovah's witnesses refuse transfusions of whole blood (including preoperative autologous donation) and the primary blood components — red cells, platelets, white cells, and unfractionated plasma.\n- The guideline suggests that if they're competent or if they carry the blood refusal card( witnessed written directives), we should respect their decision not to receive blood products even if this would lead to their death.\n- You can advise them to receive blood products. however, you aren't allowed to pressure them to accept it. 71 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 71:\nA 17-year-old male presented to the ED following road traffic accident. On examination, he had penetrating wound over Achilles' tendon, and she had loss of sensation over posterolateral distal leg and planter foot. What nerve injured in this injury?\n- **A)** Common fibular nerve\n- **B)** Posterior tibial nerve\n- **C)** Anterior-tibial nerve x   D, Sural nerve\n- **E)** Sciatic nerve Explanation\n- The Achilles tendon is innervated by the posterior tibial nerve, which is a branch of the sciatic nerve. Loss of sensation over the posterolateral distal leg and plantar foot suggests injury to the tibial nerve.\n- The posterior tibial nerve describes the distal end of the tibial nerve as it passes around the medial malleolus in the tarsal tunnel. Anatomically, the structure is a continuation its parent tibial nerve in a specific region of the distal extremity and does not represent a branch of the tibial nerve or a distinct structure. Accordingly, some texts will refer to it as the tibial nerve. Moreover, there is no anterior tibial nerve at the level of the foot or ankle. However, many authors and texts refer to the structure as the posterior tibial nerve due to its clinical significance. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 74 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 74:\nA 30-year-old male brought into the ED after RTA. Patient has developed seizure while you assess him. His eye image is shown Where is lesion in this image? x\n- **A)** Right third nerve palsy\n- **B)** Left 2nd nerve palsy\n- **C)** Left third nerve palsy\n- **D)** Right 4th nerve palsy Right 6th nerve palsy Explanation rd\n- The left eye is dilated which indicates 3 nerve palsy (CNIII). 76 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 76:\nA nurse has a needle stick injury, and she is looking for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis. Which of the following high risk of transmission required\n- **A)** Receptive vaginal sex with HIV positive source\n- **B)** Who lived 5 years or traveled to sub-Saharan x\n- **C)** Semen splash on eye from HIV positive source\n- **D)** Human bite from HIV positive source\n- **E)** Who has blood transfusion in the UK before 1990 Explanation\n- To follow British HIV association guideline, PEP is routinely offered in patients who has receptive vaginal sex with HIV positive source. PEP is not recommended in splash of semen into eye https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC High risk source for HIV\n- MSM ( men who have sex with men)\n- Female sexual contacts of MSM people reporting current or prior injecting drug use\n- Sex workers\n- Trans women\n- people from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence i-e Originally from sub- Saharan Africa. people reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where contact occurs\n- Individuals known to have/have had a mother living with HIV and who do not have documented HIV- negative status\n- Post-exposure HIV prophylaxis (British HIV association guideline). HIV-transmission-in-rhe.fQ.UQWing -scenarios; Following receptive anal intercourse with an index partner of unknown HIV status or known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n- Following receptive vaginal sex with an index partner known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n- Following an occupational exposure (sharps or mucosal splash) from an index case known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n- For people who inject drugs after sharing needles/equipment if their index injecting partner is known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load. circumstances;\n- Insertive vaginal intercourse with an index partner known to be HIV-positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n- Insertive anal intercourse with an index partner of unknown HIV status. _facter_which increases the risk of transmission.\n- Sharps and splash injuries, sharing of injecting equipment, receptive or insertive vaginal intercourse when the index case is from a high risk group but the HIV status is unknown. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Human bite if the index case is HIV-positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load\n- NOTE- PEP should only be considered after a bite if all three criteria are met: a) the biter's saliva was visibly contaminated with blood; b) the biter is known or suspected to have a plasma HIV viral load >3.0 log copies/ml; and\n- c) the bite has resulted in severe and/or deep tissue Injurles\n- IN ALL OTHER SCENARIOS PEP IS NOT\n- Fellatio with ejaculation (oral sex/stimulation)\n- Fellatio without ejaculation\n- Splash of semen into eye\n- Cunnilingus\n- Needlestick from a discarded needle in the community (here HIV +ve/-ve not relevant as source unknown)\n- A rislegroups are those where there is a significant likelihood of the index case individual being HIV positive. Within the UK at present, this is likely to be MSM (men who have sex with men), people who inject drugs from high-risk countries and individuals who have immigrated to the uK from areas of high HIV prevalence, particularly sub-Saharan Africa (high prevalence is 6.5 4 su recommendation https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- PEP (post-exposure prophylaxis) must be initiated as soon as possible, ideally within 2 hours, and preferably within 24 hours of exposure.\n- PEP is not recommended if the exposure was more than 72 hours before presentation.\n- Tenofovir disoproxil 24Smg/emtricitabine 200mg and raltegravir 1200mg once daily as the regimen of choice for PEP\n- If there is evidence that the index case has a current or past history of ART failure, expert advice should be sought as to whether the PEP regimen should be modified in relation to ART history and/or resistance testing.\n- For women who are pregnant, raltegravir 400mg twice daily is preferred (with tenofovir disoproxil 245mg/emtricitabine 200mg). Where accessing raltegravir 400mg might cause delay we recommend using raltegravir 600mg twice daily and switching at the earliest opportunity.\n- In established HIV infection, combination therapy with at least three medications from two medication classes is recommended for initial therapy. It is thus recommended, to use a triple agent regimen for PEP, though some international guidelines do recommend dual-class regimens in selected situations. NQte_2Exposure with urine, nasal secretions, saliva, sweat, or tears without the presence of blood is considered negligible risk for acquisition of HIV.\n- Receiving a blood transfusion or other products made from blood is safe in the (Jk as all blood products have been screened for infections such as HIV since 1985.\n- be careful with words receptive/insertive vaginal/anal sex in post-exposure prophylaxis recommendations\n- For more detail check HIV transmission and needle stick injury module in infection.\n- be familiar with entire guideline, you may get questions in different ways. 84 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 84:\nA mother presents with her 4-day-old son due to his worsening jaundice. She is a healthy 30-year-old with blood type O positive and a negative direct antibody test (DAT). She received adequate prenatal care, and her laboratory results were normal, including a negative group B streptococcus culture. The neonate has been exclusively breastfeeding on an every-4-hour schedule. His birth weight was 7 lbs 11 oz (3500 g), and his current weight is 6 lbs 13 oz (3100 g). He has had 1 stool and 3 wet diapers in https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC the last 24 hours. Physical exam findings are within normal limits except for marked jaundice. His blood tests show: total serum bilirubin: 16 mg/dL direct bilirubin: hematocrit: 0.8 mg/dL 55% reticulocyte count: 2.8%. What is the most likely mechanism of jaundice in this neonate?\n- **A)** pregnanediol in breast milk and increased bilirubin conjugation\n- **B)** Beta-glucuronidase in breast milk and increased enterohepatic circulation\n- **C)** Suboptimal intake With dehydration and increased enterohepatic circulation\n- **D)** Maternal immunoglobulin C-mediated hemolysis\n- **E)** pregnanediol in breast milk and decreased x bilirubin conjugation Explanation;\n- This neonate has breastfeeding jaundice secondary to suboptimal intake, with a weight loss of about 11%. Breastfeeding jaundice typically peaks between 3 and 5 days of age and is often associated with a weight loss greater than 10%.\n- Suboptimal breast milk intake results in dehydration and increased enterohepatic circulation, allowing unconjugated bilirubin to reenter the circulation and elevate the serum bilirubin concentration. Treatment involves improving the infant's hydration status with on-demand feeding at least 8 times daily. The mother's state of hydration, milk supply, and the babys latching and swallowing techniques must be assessed. The bilirubin level will usually return to normal within a few days. Phototherapy may occasionally be required.\n- Exclusive breastfeeding is recommended. The mother can supplement with pumped breastmilk, donor breastmilk, or infant formula if suboptimal intake persists. Additional water and dextrose water are not indicated. lgC-mediated hemolysis is seen in hemolytic disease of the newborn due to blood group https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC incompatibility. Pregnanediol and beta- glucuronidase are responsible for breast milk jaundice lasting up to 3 months, accompanied by adequate intake and weight gain. 88 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 88:\nA 50-year-old male known case of myocarditis came with a complaint of shortness of breath. After an initial assessment, ECHO was done which revealed LV failure. Which of the following drug is indicated in this patient? Lisinopril x\n- **B)** Digoxin\n- **C)** NSAIDs\n- **D)** Beta blocker\n- **E)** Spironolactone Explanation;\n- ACE inhibitor is the first line in haemodynamically stable myocarditis patients with evidence of left ventricular systolic dysfunction.\n- supportive treatment the cause if then\n-\n- (e. captoød, or , , u cause\n- ties beta I i n selected\n-\n- GIN ,\n- dob 90 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 90:\nA 41-year-old man brought by ambulance after RTA with an obvious scalp haematoma and bleeding from his nose. His GCS is  and BP  mmHg. His CXR is shown. His Sp02 with ventilation on Fi02 1 is 90% What is your immediate priority in the management? x\n- **A)** Withdrawal Of ETT 3cm\n- **B)** Intravenous antibiotics https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **C)** Transfer to intensive care\n- **D)** CT head to exclude a bleed\n- **E)** Suctioning down the ETT Explanation\n- Airway should be priority - the patient Sp02 of 90%with ventilation on Fi02 1.0 which indicates that there is an obstruction in the airway. The collapsed right upper lobe could be due to secretion accumulation. Therefore, suctioning down the ETT, is the most appropriate immediate priority. This is because the patient may have blood or secretions obstructing the endotracheal tube (ETT) leading to ineffective ventilation and oxygenation. After suctioning, the patient's airway should be reassessed, and his ventilation and oxygenation parameters should be optimized. If the patient remains hypoxic, additional measures such as increasing the Fi02, adjusting the ventilator settings, or providing positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) may be required.\n- Tracheobronchial anatomy.\n- Correct ET tube position Tube tip\n- ET Tubes - Complications https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- If inserted too far, an endotracheal tube (ET tube) can enter the right or left main bronchus. This results in ventilation of a single lung and can result in collapse of the contralateral lung or a lobe of the intubated lung.\n- Accidental intubation of a bronchus is more common on the right because the right main bronchus is more vertically orientated than the left main bronchus.\n- Oesophageal intubation - Oesophageal intubation may be a fatal complication and should be detected clinically before a patient is sent for a chest X-ray. Tra chea\n- El-tube-misplaced-in-right-main->r.qnghus https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- ETT should be withdrawal into the trachea.\n- below X-ray (A ) as above before and (B) after ETT withdrawal into the trachea 2 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 28-year-old female came to ED with short of breath, she has a history of similar episodes previously. Wh'le waiting in the waiting area she suddenly collapsed and hemodynamically unstable. You suspect a massive pulmonary embolism. What would be your next immediate step of management?\n- **A)** Order CTPA\n- **B)** Bedside 20 Echocardiography\n- **C)** Subcutaneous heparin\n- **D)** D\n- dimer\n- **E)** thrombolysis with Alteplase infusion Explanation;\n- Thrombolysis is indicated for patients with severe circulatory compromise or a picture of massive pulmonary embolism. Prior proof of PE is not needed if the patient is peri-arrest and thrombolysis should be administered immediately (50mg Alteplase as a bolus) in such patients. It is common practice to give thrombolysis as first-line treatment for any patient who is in peri- https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC arrest/cardiac arrest based on clinical suspicion of PE without waiting for results from investigations. 3 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA mother brought a child who weighs 4kg. On the initial assessment, pu found the baby was in cardiac arrest. What is the recommended dose of adrenaline for this baby?\n- **B)** 0.3ml\n- **C)** 0.6ml\n- **D)** 0.2ml\n- **E)** 0.1ml Explanation;\n- The recommended IV40 dose of frenaline in children is 10 mcgkg (0.1mL kg of 1 in 10,000 solution). The child's weight is 4kg, therefore, 4 x 10 = 40 mcg ( 0.4m')\n- Another way to memorize: weight is Skg then the adrenaline dose will be 0.5ml.\n- Similarly, if the weight is 3.5kg adrenaline dose would be 0.3Sml 4 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nYou suspect that your colleague a junior doctor is under illicit drugs. Therefore, you confront him, but he denied the allegation. You still suspect he is taking drugs. What would be your next action?\n- **A)** Seek advice from medical defense organization\n- **B)** Raise issue to the GMC\n- **C)** Ask him to get an appointment With psychiatrist\n- **D)** Inform the consultant\n- **E)** Ask the nurse to inform the consultant\n- In this case, y3u already confront him so here you need to inform the consultant/supervisor Eirst=-confront him with advice. If-he-still-continues-then=inform the consultant https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper the GMC - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC — raise it to 5 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 65-year-old female brought by her daughter with a complaint of vomiting and weight loss. Her daughter states that she stopped cooking for herself after the death of her husband. Her x-ray is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Inguinal hernia\n- **B)** Spigelian hernia\n- **C)** Para-umbilical hernia\n- **D)** Ischemic bowel E\n- Femoral hernia\n- X-ray shows small bowel distension with air-fluid levels; the large bowel is non-distended and there is no air seen in the rectum. Findings are in keeping with a small bowel obstruction. No pneumoperitoneum. A CT scan is required to confirm the diagnosis.\n- Femoral hernia is common in females gender.\n- Inguinal hernia less common in female\n- Femoral hernia Femoral hernias sometimes appear as a painful lump in the inner upper part of the thigh or groin. The lump can often be pushed back in or disappears when you lie down.\n- Coughing or straining may make the lump appear.\n- A femoral hernia usually occurs when fatty tissue or a part of your bowel pokes through into your groin at the top of your inner thigh. It pushes through a weak spot in the surrounding muscle wall (abdominal wall) into an area called the femoral canal. Femoral hernia are common in women Femoral hernias can sometimes a _ pear suddenly because of strain on the tummy, such_as;\n- straining on the toilet if have constipation\n- carrying and pushing heavy loads\n- They've also been linked to obesity and having a persistent, heavy cough. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Management Require surgical intervention, either open or laparoscopic\n- obstruction— where a section of the bowel becomes stuck in the femoral canal, causing nausea, vomiting and stomach pain, as well as a painful lump in the g ron\n- strangulation— where a section of the bowel becomes trapped and its blood supply is cut off; this requires emergency surgery within hours to release the trapped tissue and restore blood supply. 6 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 35-year-old male presented to the ED after stabbed wound in the peri-umbilical region. His vitals are: HR: 110bpm What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** IV morphine   B, Refer to surgery\n- **C)** One litter of blood\n- **D)** Observe in the ED and discharge\n- **E)** fluid bolus Explanation;\n- An emergent laparotomy is indicated in this patient, therefore, refer to surgery is required. Any Penetrating injury with haemodynamic instability required urgent laparotomy. 7 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nAn 81-year-old female presented to the ED with bleeding per rectum. She reports slightly loss of weight. Her lab is shown. What would be the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Von Willebrand disease (VWD) https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **B)** Congenital hemophilia\n- **C)** CML\n- **D)** ITP - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Acquired hemophilia Explanation\n- patient clinical features suggestive of acquired haemophilia\n- Acquired Haemophilia\n- Unlike patients with hereditary hemophilia, patients with acquired hemophilia do not have a personal or family history of bleeding episodes:\n- Acquired haemophilia typically occurs in older people age and should be suspected and investigated in older adults who present with a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time and/or new bleeding symptoms.\n- It's very rarer as compare to inherited disorder.\n- Investigations\n- In a patient with acquired hemophilia, the bleeding time, prothrombin time (PT), and platelet count are normal. However, the activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)\n- Management\n- Treatment for AHA (acquired Haemophila\n- **A)** (is directed at bleeding control, inhibitor eradication to prevent subsequent bleeding episodes, and treatment of any underlying causative disease. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** A 62-year-old male with a history of prostate cancer presents to the emergency department with acute onset low back pain, and midline tenderness. His MRI shows spinal cord compression. What corticosteroid would you recommend to this patient?\n- **A)** Hydrocortisone 50mg\n- **B)** Prednisolone 30mg\n- **C)** Fludrocortisone acetate 100mg\n- **D)** Dexamethasone 18mg\n- **E)** Methylprednisolone 60mg\n- Nice recommends dexamethasone at least 16mg in malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC) patients Malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC)\n- It is caused by a tumour pressing on the spinal cord or cauda equina. Spinal tumours can be primary, or secondary to metastatic disease (e.g., of breast, prostate, or lung cancer).\n- Clinical features\n- pain in the middle (thoracic) or upper (cervical) splne\n- progressive lower (lumbar) spinal pain\n- Severe unremitting lower spinal pain\n- Spinal pain aggravated by straining (for example, at stool, or when coughing or sneezing)\n- Localised spinal tenderness\n- Nocturnal spinal pain preventing sleep.\n- Bladder or bowel dysfunction\n- Sensory loss Weakness or paralysis\n- Numbness or paraesthesias\n- Back and neck pain usually the first symptom\n- Investigation\n- MRI\n- If patient is diabetes, IV drug user and exam findings (fever, midline spinal tenderness) concerning spinal epidural abscess, in such patients https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC MR imaging of the entire spine with gadolinium contrast recommended.\n- Consider targeted CT scan with 3-plane reconstruction to assess spinal stability and plan vertebroplasty, kyphoplasty or spinal surgery in patients with MSCC.\n- Consider myelography if other imaging modalities are contraindicated or inadequate.\n- Management (NICE guideline)\n- Analgesia - Offer conventional analgesia (including NSAIDs, non-opiate and opiate medication).\n- Bisphosphonate - Offer patients with vertebral involvement from myeloma or breast cancer bisphosphonates to reduce pain and the risk of vertebral fracture or collapse.\n- Radiography - Offer patients with spinal metastases causing nan-mechanical spinal pain 8 Gy single fraction palliative radiotherapy even if they are completely paralysed.\n- Vertebroplasty and kyphoplasty — recommended in selected patients who who have vertebral metastases and no evidence of MSCC or spinal instability if they have:\n- mechanical pain resistant to conventional analgesia, or\n- vertebral body collapse\n- Surgery - urgently consider patients with spinal metastases and imaging evidence of structural spinal failure with spinal instability for surgery to stabilise the spine and prevent MSCC.\n- Corticosteroid - offer all patients with MSCC a loading dose of at least 16 mg of dexamethasone as soon as possible after assessment, followed by a short course of 16 mg dexamethasone daily while treatment is being planned.\n- Continue dexamethasone 16 mg daily in patients awaiting surgery or radiotherapy for MSCC. After surgery or the start of radiotherapy the dose should be reduced gradually over 5 to 7 days and stopped.\n- Note — at least 16mg but you can give 18mg 9 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 46-year-old male presented to the ED with short of breath. His blood pressure is 240/120mmhg. His examination revealed bilateral crept on auscultation. What would be your next step of management in this patient? https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **A)** Nitroprusside\n- **B)** Amlodipine   C\n- Phentolamine\n- **D)** Furosernide\n- **E)** Labetalol Explanation -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Standard treatment NICE recommendation — diuretic (furosemide) and or CTN are the treatment choice for hypertensive emergency with LV failure or pulmonary oedema.\n- Nice guideline recommendation hypertension management in adults +BHS NQte_2Be caution with steps, more than 1 drug is recommended if hypertension is not controlled in adults taking step 1 treatment of an ACE inhibitor or ARE.\n- Do not combine an ACE inhibitor with an ARB to treat hypertension.\n- Hypertensive emergency\n- It is defined as the clinical situation in which there is a marked elevation of blood pressure (ap) usually >180/120mmhg and associated with acute or progressive end organ damage, e.g. cardiovascular, renal or neurological dysfunction.\n- Labetalol first line therapy in hypertensive encephalopathy/malignant hyperten