- Increased ACTH levels (rise in an attempt to stimulate the adrenal glands)\n- Hyponatraemia\n- Hyperkalaemia\n- Hypercalcaemia\n- Hypoglycaemia\n- Metabolic acidosis https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Addison's disease\n- Primary adrenal caused by destruction of the adrenal cortex. Causes—reduced production of glucocorticoids (such as cortisol), mineralocorticoids (such as aldosterone), and adrenal androgens (such as dehydroepiandrosterone).\n- The absence of cortisol leads to increased production of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) because negative feedback to the pituitary gland is reduced. Secondary-adrenalinsuffigiency=occurs when pituitary ACTH production is insufficient; this leads to reduced cortisol production and adrenal atrophy. intracranial disorders, such as pituitary tumours, subarachnoid haemorrhage, and traumatic brain injury. Tertiary-adrenal-insufficiency-=occurs n hypothalamic production of corticotropin-releasing hormone is disrupted and production of ACTH from the anterior pituitary is insufficient. - long-term administration of corticosteroids and less commonly tumours, radiotherapy, or surgery affecting the hypothalamus.\n- Causes\n- Auto-immune adrenalitis (most common) Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in children) and other genetic causes such as adrenoleukodystrophy.\n- Infections, such as tuberculosis, meningococcus, haemopholus influenzae, cryptococcosis, cytomegalovirus, and HIV.\n- Adrenal metastases (a cancer which has spread from another primary site to one or both adrenal glands)\n- Amyloidosis.\n- Haemochromatosis\n- Iatrogenic causes such as bilateral adrenalectomy, adrenal haemorrhage (for example from anticoagulant treatment), and use of immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies in the treatment of cancer.\n- Clinical features\n- A person with Addison's disease may present with a sudden crisis precipitated by intercurrent https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC infection or stress.\n- Features of Addison's disease include:\n- Weakness and lethargy\n- Hypotension (notably orthostatic hypotension)\n- Nausea and vomiting\n- Weight loss\n- Reduced axillary and pubic hair Depression Hyperpigmentation (palmar creases, buccal mucosa and exposed areas more commonly affected)\n- The classical biochemical features of Addison's disease are as follows:\n- Increased ACTH levels (rise in an attempt to stimulate the adrenal glands)\n- Hyponatraemia\n- Hyperkalaemia\n- Hypercalcaemia\n- Hypoglycaemia\n- Metabolic acidosis\n- Addison's disease is associated with an increased incidence of the following conditions:\n- Type I diabetes mellitus (not type II)\n- Hashimoto's thyroiditis\n- Crave's disease\n- premature ovarian failure Pernicious anaemia\n- Vitiligo\n- Alopecia\n- Investigations\n- Serum cortisol level - ideally be obtained at 8—9 am\n- Less than 100 nanomol/L — admit the person to hospital, adrenal insufficiency is highly likely.\n- Blood glucose\n- Plasma adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) (Synacthen ) test— to confirm the diagnosis.\n- Plasma renin and aldosterone levels — renin levels are typically high and aldosterone levels low in Addison's disease. In secondary adrenal insufficiency, the renin-angiotensin system can function normally.\n- Serum DHEA-S — typically low in Addison's disease. Thyroid function tests.\n- Autoantibody levels — adrenal cortex autoantibodies or antibodies against 21-hydroxylase are present in more than 80% of people with recent- onset autoimmune adrenalitis. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper\n- Management -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 13 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 13:\nA 28-year-old male presented to the ED with nausea and palpitation. His ECG revealed torsade-de-pointe