spects of your work, including clinical duties, teaching, research, leadership, and management where relevant.\n- Recognise and work within the limits of your competence. Practise only under appropriate supervision for your role, skills, and training. Keep up to date with guidelines, evidence, and developments that affect your work.\n- Follow the law, CMC professional standards, and other regulations relevant to medical practice.\n- Maintain adequate knowledge of English to provide safe and effective care in the L.JK.\n- NOTE - question has been revised based on current 2024 guideline. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 18 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 18:\nA 6-year-old girl is a Jehovah's Witness, brought to the ED with an abdominal injury after a road traffic collision (RTA). During initial resuscitation, lv fluid was given. She is deteriorating rapidly and requires an emergent blood transfusion. However, her mother refused a blood transfusion. What would be your step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Refer to surgery for definitive treatment\n- **B)** Respect mother decision and refer to surgery\n- **C)** Give lv fluid without transfuse blood products\n- **D)** Transfuse blood products\n- **E)** Give lv fluid only and refer to surgery\n- A blood transfusion should be given because the patient is not competent. The mother can not refuse her daughter's treatment. 19 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 19:\nA 43-year-old man presents following a collapse. he is cyanosed, tachycardic and has Sp02 of despite high- flow oxygen and a RR of 26 per minute. He admits to recent recreational drug use. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Methaemoglobinaemia from amyl nitrate use\n- **B)** PE secondary to IV drug use\n- **C)** CNS depression and hypoventilation from opiates\n- **D)** Pulmonary vasoconstriction from cannabis smoking\n- **E)** Hypoxia secondary to Camma Hydroxy Butyrate ingestion Explanation;\n- The patient has methemoglobinemia, a disorder of red blood cells that interferes with normal https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC hemoglobin function and oxygen transport. This can result from either congenital (or acquired processes. cyanido https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 20 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 20:\nA 35-year-old woman, who gave birth two weeks prior, presented to the ED with complaints of headache and convulsions. On examination, she was found to have weakness in her left arm and sensory disturbances. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Migraine\n- **B)** Stroke\n- **C)** Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n- **D)** Idiopathic intracranial hypertension\n- **E)** Cerebral venous thrombosis\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (CVST) Cerebral venous thrombosis (CVT)\n- It is rare and accounts for 0.5% of all strokes\n- It occurs more frequently in young adults and children. 2,3 It is three times more common in females than in males.\n- The principle pathology of CVT is thrombosis of cerebral veins and the commonest site of origin is believed to be the junction of cerebral veins and larger sinuses.\n- Once a thrombus is formed in the cerebral or cortical veins, its extension can occlude large draining venous sinuses.\n- Thrombophilias - — Factor V Leiden Acquired — eg antiphospholipid syndrome\n- Infection - Intracranial Regional eg ear, nose, throat, head, neck Systemic\n- Female - specific risk factors are more important in younger age groups (ie oestrogencontaining contraceptives, pregnancy and puerperium/postpartum - is the 6—8-week period from the time of delivery of the placenta up to the resumption of normal ovulation).\n- Malignancy — intracranial malignancy is more common in older age group\n- Inflammatory - Vasculitis eg Behqet's disease Systemic lupus erythematosus Inflammatory bowel disease Sarcoidosis. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Medications - Oral contraceptives Steroids Anti- neoplastic drugs (particularly L-asparaginase Sepsis\n- Endocrine disorder — hyperthyroidism\n- Headache — Migraine, raised ICP, Thunderclap, Ear/mastoid pain.\n- Focal neurological deficits - Hemiparesis, cranial nerve palsy, sensory disturbance, aphasia and ataxia.\n- Seizure — focal generalized\n- Visual disturbance - Reduced acuity, Reduced/altered visual field, Diplopia, papilloedema.\n- Meningism — neck pain/stiffness, photophobia\n- Reduced consciousness — Drowsiness, Coma\n- Cognitive impairment — Encephalopathy, Disorientation, Amnesia\n- MRI and magnetic resonance venography (MR-V) are also sensitive tools for identifying CVT\n- catheter angiography is useful when there is doubt about the anatomy, but the requirement of highly skilled operators limits its access to neuroscience centres. Management\n- Currently, the recommendation is immediate commencement of therapeutic LMWH or LJFH. In addition to anticoagulation, underlying aetiological factors should be investigated and managed.\n- European Federation of Neurological Societies (EFNS) guidelines recommend that patients should be anticoagulated even in the presence of haemorrhage. Evidence for this is limited to two small randomised controlled trials (RCTs) of unfractionated heparin (UFH) 22 and low-molecular weight heparin (LMWH). Both PCTs showed better clinical outcomes and reduced mortality rates in patients receiving anticoagulation compared with controls.\n- If anticoagulation is contraindicated, or in cases of severe CVT not responding to anticoagulants, endovascular thrombolysis or mechanical thrombectomy might be an option.\n- Steroids are not recommended and are linked to a poorer prognosis in CVT\n- Antiepileptic drugs should not be given routinely as prophylaxis but should be initiated quickly if a seizure cn:curs. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Following the immediate management of CVT, long-term vitamin K antagonists, such as warfarin, with a target international normalised ratio (INR) of 2—3 should be used.\n- Guidelines recommend anticoagulation for 3—6 months in provoked CVT, 6—12 months in unprovoked CVT and potentially lifelong in recurrent CVT, VTE following CVT, or CVT associated with severe thrombophilias. 21 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 21:\nAn 82-year-old male history of dementia was brought into the ED by her daughter. She states that he is restless and agitated. On further assessment, you have found he has multiple marks of bruise on his lower limb. What would be your next step in this patient?\n- **A)** Admit him for safeguarding review\n- **B)** Discharge him With advice\n- **C)** prescribe him antipsychotic\n- **D)** Refer to his C p\n- **E)** Call the police\n- This patient should be admitted for adult safeguarding review, as he is physically abused. Safeguarding lead and local authority should be involved. The Care Act: safeguarding adults People's wellbeing is at the heart of the care and support system under the Care Act 2014, and the prevention of abuse and neglect is one of the elements identified as going to make up a person's wellbeing.\n- In the context of the legislation, specific adult safeguarding duties apply to any adult who:\n- has care and support needs\n- is experiencing, or is at risk of, abuse or neglect\n- is unable to protect themselves because of their care and support needs.\n- The Care Act 2014 sets out a clear legal framework for how local authorities and other parts of the system should protect adults at risk of abuse or neglect.\n- Local authorities have new safeguarding duties. They must: https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- lead a multi-agency local adult safeguarding system - that seeks to prevent abuse and neglect and stop it quickly when it happens\n- make enquiries, or request others to make them - when they think an adult with care and support needs may be at risk of abuse or neglect and they need to find out what action may be needed\n- establish Safeguarding Adults Boards - including the local authority, NHS and police, which will develop, share and implement a joint safeguarding strategy\n- carry out Safeguarding Adults Reviews - when someone With care and support needs dies as a result of neglect or abuse and there is a concern that the local authority or its partners could have done more to protect them\n- arrange for an independent advocate - to represent and support a person who is the subject of a safeguarding enquiry or review, if required. The Safeguarding Adults Review Process\n- A Safeguarding Adults Review (SAR) is a multi- agency review which seeks to determine what relevant agencies and individuals involved could have done differently, that could have prevented Serious abuse or neglect or a death from taking place.\n- When someone with care and support needs —such as mental illness, learning or physical disabilities, or dementia, dies or is harmed as a result of abuse or neglect and it is thought that agencies could have worked better together to prevent the death or future harm.\n- The Care Act 2014 states that Safeguarding Adult Boards (SABS) must arrange a Safeguarding Adult Review (SAR) when: 1. an adult in its area dies as a result of abuse or neglect, whether known or suspected, or 2. the adult is still alive, and the SAB knows or suspects that the adult has experienced serious abuse or neglect, and 3. there is concern that partner agencies could have worked together more effectively to protect the adult from harm.\n- HOW DOES THE REVIEW PROCESS WORK? The exact process will vary, but in a typical review:\n- A multi-agency panel will be formed to gather and analyse as much information as possible from the professionals and agencies involved with the adult at risk. This may include Adult Social Care, hospital trusts, the person's GP, the police and the ambulance service. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- An independent person will usually be asked to review the information to understand what happened, consider how best to prevent future harm, and make recommendations for change.\n- The whole process generally lasts six months to a year.\n-\n- You don't need to memorize everything. 22 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 22:\nA 25-year-old male brought to the ED with a lacerated wound in his throat after being assaulted. He is deteriorating and losing consciousness. What initial step will you take in this patient? Orotracheal intubation\n- **B)** Suction the wound\n- **C)** Cricothyroidotomy\n- **D)** Intubation through the cut wound\n- **E)** Give oxygen and wait for anesthetic Explanation\n- Initial-management.-Qf-laryngqtracheaLtrauma\n- Setting up a secure airway is the first priority in ED.\n- All patients with hemodynamic instability or high risk of a difficult airway, should undergo orotracheal intubation by expert personnel, through direct vision, ideally using a fiberoptic bronchoscope, placing the balloon distal to the lesion and without applying pressure on the cricoid cartilage.\n- If orotracheal intubation is not possible, the need for an emergency tracheostorny or a cricothyroidotomy, as a last resource, should be evaluated.\n- Once the airway is deemed to be stable, further evaluation of the laryngeal injury is possible. Flexible fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a critical step in evaluating the status of the airway after laryngeal trauma https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Note: For more detail check laryngotracheal trauma in the major trauma module. 24 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 24:\nWhich of the following is a nan-notifiable disease?\n- **A)** Small POX\n- **B)** Measles\n- **C)** Pertussis\n- **D)** Rubella   E, Epstein-barr virus Explanation; Diseases notifiable to local authority proper officers under the Health Protect-on (Notification) Regulations 2010:\n- Acute encephalitis\n- Acute infectious hepatitis\n- Acute meningitis\n- Acute poliomyelitis\n- Anthrax\n- Botulism\n- Brucellosis\n- Cholera\n- COVID-19\n- Diphtheria\n- Enteric fever (typhoid or paratyphoid fever)\n- Food poisoning\n- Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS)\n- Infectious bloody diarrhoea\n- Invasive group A streptococcal disease Legionnaires' disease Leprosy\n- Malaria\n- Measles https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Meningococcal septicaemia\n- Monkeypox\n- Mumps\n- Plague\n- Rabies\n- Rubella\n- Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS)\n- Scarlet fever\n- Smallpox\n- Tetanus\n- Tuberculosis\n- Typhus\n- Viral haernorrhagic fever (VHF)\n- Whooping cough\n- Yellow fever Report other diseases that may present significant risk to human health under the category 'other significant disease 25 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 25:\nA 55-year-old make brought into the ED who has end stage of kidney disease CKD3. He has history of motor neuron disease. You've decided to sedate the patent under procedural sedation. Which of the following is contraindication to suxamethonium in this patient?\n- **A)** Hypercalcemia\n- **B)** Motor neuron disease (MND)\n- **C)** Hepatic failure   D\n- Hypokalemia\n- **E)** CKD Explanation\n- The effect of neuromuscular blocking agents depends on the disease process but, on the whole, suxamethonium should be avoided in all NMD disease) which includes MND (Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis, Spinal Muscular Atrophy, Spinal Bulbar, Muscular Atrophy).\n- Caution should be taken in use of suxamethonium in CKD patients. 26 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 26:\nA 23-year-old man is brought to the ED unconscious and intubated after his Glasgow coma scale (GCS) decreased in transport. Emergency services had been https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC called as he had been experiencing rigors and chills after ingesting an unknown compound. His observations are: sp02I Heart rate: apt Temp: GCS: 100% on IO L oxygen 63 beats per minute  mmHg  His examination reveals pinpoint pupils bilaterally. Corneal reflexes and doll's eye response were intact. Bilaterally down going planters were noted. Auscultation Of the chest reveals decreased air entry on the right side with crackles bilaterally. Detoxification is started for the suspected ingested substance. Rapid Infusion of the antidote g 'ven to treat the nicotinic effects of the suspected substance can cause which of the following complication?\n- **A)** Glove and stocking paresthesia\n- **B)** Acute respiratory distress syndrome\n- **C)** Diarrhea D' Cardiac arrest\n- **E)** Seizures Ingestion of organophosphates cause increased acetylcholine levels. The increased levels of acetylcholine lead to anxiety, seizures, coma, bradycardia, pinpoint pupils, salivation, lacrimation, and hyperactive bowel sounds.\n- Atropine is used to treat organophosphate poisoning, a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors. Adult dosing is initially 2 to 5 mg IV, which can be repeated every 3 to 5 minutes until the respiratory compromise has resolved.\n- Atropine acts on muscarinic receptors only; thus, pralidoxime (2-PAM) should also be administered to cover the nicotinic receptors. A bolus of at least 30 mg/kg in adults should be given. Rapid administration can cause cardiac arrest. Organophosphate exposure can occur from pesticide exposure, for example, in crop workers. Contamination can cn:cur following inhalation, ingestion, or dermal contact https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 27 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 27:\nA 29-year-old male brought into the ED following collapsed in a pub. He is cyanosed, tachycardiac and his saturation is which is not improving with high flow oxygen. His respiratory rate is 26 breath per minutes. On further assessment, he admits recent recreational drug use. What is the most likely diagnosis? Methemoglobinemia\n- **B)** Cyanide poisoning\n- **C)** MDMA\n- **D)** Cocaine overdose\n- **E)** CO poisoning Explanatign\n- Patient clinical feature; cyanosed and low saturation Sp02 88% which does not improve with supplemental oxygen suggestive of methaemoglobinaemia. Methemoglobinemia Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood. Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2+) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3+). An abnormal MetHb level is any level >\n- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital Or acquired\n- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood Benzocaine Dapsone Nitroglycerin Nitrofurantoin Nitroprusside Phenazopyridine\n- Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers") https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:\n- < - None (patients with underlying diseases may have more symptoms at lower level)\n- 10-20% - Slight discoloration (eg, pale, gray, blue) of the skin.\n- Chocolate brown colored blood\n- 2Q.4Q%_- Anxiety, headache, tachycardia, lightheadedness\n- 30-50% - Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain\n- so-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status, delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis\n- > 70% - Usually, death Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is >1.5 g/dl\n- Nnyestigations\n- VBG or ABC (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in a fresh sample of blood. utine pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%.\n- pa02 readings on blood gases may be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible. Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary)\n- Treat-anyone-who-is-symptomatig-(yitalsjgn\n- mg/kg (0.1-0.2 rnL/kg of solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes\n- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms.\n- Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in I hour, repeat the dose or consider consider C6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.\n- Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis.\n- Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Consider_exchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion\n- Methemoglobinemia can result from exposure to a number of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).\n- Consider the diagnosis Methemoglobinemia in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.\n- Administer methylene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs, metabolic acidosis, end organ dysfunction or, a serum level > 25% 28 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 28:\nA 5-year-old child presented to the ED by his mother. She reports that he has eaten detergent in the kitchen. Which of the following is not upper respiratory obstruction?\n- **A)** Hoarseness\n- **B)** Biphasic stridor\n- **C)** Stridor EXPiratory Wheeze\n- **E)** Drooling Explanation Features/ sign of airway