1/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC the normal infusion rate can be used for subsequent bags).\n- Cive acetylcysteine even if the patient has a history of a preyiqus-adye.r.se-r.eagtiqmas the benefits outweigh the risks.\n- Consider using a slower initial infusion of acetylcysteine in these patients to reduce the risk of a further reaction, as well as giving prophylactic treatment (e.g„ antihistamines).\n- Pretreatment with nebulised salbutamol in patients with a history of bronchospasm following acetylcysteine.\n- Monitor all patients closely for acetylcysteine infusion reactions over the first few hours.\n- Nausea, vomiting, flushing, urticarial rash, angioedema, tachycardia, and bronchospasm are relatively cornmon\n- Be aware that vomiting is relatively common during intravenous acetylcysteine administration. In clinical practice, manage with an anti-emetic (e.g., ondansetron). 31 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 31:\nA 30-year-old RTA patient presented to the ED with ankle pain. His x-ray is shown. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Analgesia and discharge with orthopedic follow- up   B, urgent referral to orthopedic\n- **C)** Order CT scan\n- **D)** Immobilize in plaster cast\n- **E)** Immediately reduce the ankle X-ray shows Maisonneuve fracture (fracture of the medial malleolus or widening of the medial ankle joint space due to deltoid ligament injury, as well as widening of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis plus proximal fibular fracture)\n- Patient requires urgent referral to orthopedic for internal fixation.\n- Maisonneuve fracture generally managed by operative treatment. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 34 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 34:\nA El-year-old male presented to the ED with chest pain radiating to the shoulder. Examination revealed a diastolic murmur. You suspect aortic dissection in this patient and You want to perform ECHO in this patient What will you find in ECHO in this patient?\n- **A)** Mitral regurgitation B\n- Mitral stenosis\n- **C)** RV failure   D' Pericardial fluid\n- **E)** pneumoperitoneum Explanatign;\n- Echocardiography plays an important role in the diagnosis and follow-up of aortic diseases.\n- Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) permits adequate assessment of several aortic segments, particularly the aortic root and proximal ascending aorta. Transoesophageal echocardiography (TOE) overcomes the limitations of TTE in thoracic aorta assessment.\n- TTE may be used as the initial modality in the emergency setting. Echo findingg\n- intimal flap\n- can "type" the dissection: type A associated features and complications:\n- aortic regurgitation (acute dilatation of the aortic root, aortic leaflet prolapse, dissection flap prolapse, pre-existing disease, e.g. bicuspid va Ive)\n- pericardial effusion and/or tamponade\n- regional wall motion abnormality heralding coronary artery occlusion\n- colour flow Doppler\n- identifies true and false lumen\n- detect aortic branch occlusion/ dissection (absent flow) 35 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 35:\nA 34-01d farmer presented to the ED with a wound injury on his foot a few days back on his farm and it's now\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC getting worse. After an assessment, you suspect necrotizing fasciitis. Which of the following organism is the most cause of this condition? A\n- Staph aureus\n- **B)** E-coli\n- **C)** Clostridium prifringes\n- **D)** Group A streptococcus E\n- Staph epidermis Explanation;\n- Necrotizing fasciitis - is most commonly a monomicrobial infection with Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococci). Acute cellulitis Necrot.zmg Iin I\n- Eron classification of cellulitis\n\n23/11/2025, Class II Class II paper - I I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- are no systome hg / uncontrolled mmorbk3ities.\n- The is systemically unwell or systemicaly we' but with a example arterial venous insufficiency, o' which cunplicate or delay r