into the stomach this tube should be withdrawn and its position confirmed either by a further attempt of aspiration of gastric fluid or by repeating the X-ray.\n- Management steps entirely depends on tip of Ng tube, If NC tube in the airway it should removed and reinserted\n- if it's in just below gastro-oesophageal junction then it should be advanced further, for safe positioning it is recommended that a NG tube tip is located at least 10 cm distal to the gastro- https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC oesophageal junction (green target area) (example with x-rays given below).\n- If tip of Ng tube in the duodenum it should be withdrawn and repositioned. Note - we haven't find similar x-ray of laryngectomy in situ with misplaced NC tube. however, below an x-ray, be familiar with that, it gives you an idea how it would be look like.\n- NG Tubes - Position confirmation\n- If aspiration of gastric fluid following placement of an nasogastric (NC) tube is unsuccessful, then a chest X-ray can be used to help determine tube position.\n- Oesophagus anatomy\n- A correctly positioned NC tube passes vertically down the oesophagus into the stomach. As the oesophagus is not visible on a plain chest X-ray, it is essential to have an understanding of its anatomical position in relation to other visible structures https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper - I I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Correct NG tube position\n- A correctly positioned nasogastric tube passes vertically in the midline, or just to the left of the midline. Below the level of the carina the tube must not follow the course Of either Of the main bronchi, but should remain in the midline down to the level of the diaphragm where it passes through the gastro-oesophageal junction.\n- The tip of the NG tube should be visible at least 10 cm beyond the gastro-oesophageal junction.\n- NG tube tiLposition - Image 2 - tip visible\n- An additional image shows the NC tube tip located below the diaphragm on the left side of the abdomen NG tube complications\n- Incorrect positioning of a nasogastric tube is common\n- Passing food or medication via a NC tube into the airways carries a high risk of mortality\n- The tube passes below the level of the carina and does not follow the course of the right or left bronchi\n- The tube is, therefore, in the oesophagus and has not been inhaled https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- The tube is looped back on itself so its tip is located in the upper oesophagus\n- Feeding via a tube in this position would risk aspiration of the feed into the lungs\n- The tube must be repositioned\n- The tube follows the course of the right main bronchus\n- Its tip is projected over the lower zone of the right lung\n- The NC tube has been inhaled rather than swa I lowed\n- The tube must be removed and repositioned\n- This tube has reached the stomach but it is not in a safe position for feeding\n- If left in this position there is a risk of it being displaced proximally when the patient changes posture\n- For safe positioning it is recommended that a NG tube tip is located at least 10 cm distal to the gastro- oesophageal junction (green target area). https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper - I I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- The gastroduodenal junction is located just to the right side of the midline (spine indicates midline)\n- To ensure the tube is not in the duodenum, its tip is ideally placed more proximally in the stomach to the left of the midline\n- For feeding into the stomach this tube should be withdrawn and its position confirmed either by a further attempt of aspiration of gastric fluid or by repeating the X-ray / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 51:\nA 20-year-old male with a history of trauma presented to the ED with a complaint of neck pain. According to NICE guideline of CT cervical indication, you didn't order CT cervical spine. When you were about to discharge. The patient's brother who is a medical consultant Insisted you to do CT cervical spine. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Get review from orthopedic surgeon\n- **B)** Tell him firmly no need of imaging and discharge him\n- **C)** Ask your senior consultant for review\n- **D)** Make a call to radiology unit and review any missed diagnosis\n- **E)** Get help from senior nurse Explanatign https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Resolving disagreements:\n- Sometimes members of the healthcare team disagree about what would be of overall benefit to the patient, or those close to the patient disagree with you and the healthcare team. It is preferable, and usually possible, to resolve disagreements about a patient's treatment and care through local p rocesses.\n- For example, by: 1. involving an independent advocate or local mediation service 2. consulting a more experienced colleague and/or an independent expert 3. holding a case conference or seeking advice from a clinical ethics committee. can't discharge the patient firmly if the patient doesn't agree with you. in this case you should explore their reasons for requesting it, their understanding of what it would involve, and their expectations about the likely outcome. This discussion will help you take account of factors that are significant to the patient and assess whether providing the treatment or care could serve the patient's needs. If after discussion you still consider that the treatment or care would not serve the patient's needs, then you should not provide it. But, you should explain your reasons to the patient and explore other options that might be available, including their right to seek a second opinion.\n- 53 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 53:\nYou're the registrar in the ED, one of the senior nurse from your department complained about a junior doctor that he smells alcohol and does not complete his tasks. What would be your action?\n- **A)** Ask the nurse to inform the consultant\n- **B)** Raise issue to the GMC\n- **C)** Inform the consultant\n- **D)** Seek advice from medical defense organization   E\n- Confront him Explanation;\n- First—confront him with advice.\n- If he still continues then — inform the consultant — raise it to the GMC https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 54 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 54:\nA 54-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of dizziness and chest pain. His BP is 80/65mmhg. His ECG is given below What would be your next Step of management?\n- **A)** Vagal maneuver\n- **B)** Adenosine\n- **C)** Bisoprolol\n- **D)** Amiodarone E' DC cardioversion Explanation\n- ECG shows AVNRT, patient is unstable therefore DC shock should be given.\n- Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is the most common form of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, or PSVT, in adults.\n- AVNRT occurs when a reentrant circuit is present within the AV node itself. In this situation, there are two separate conduction pathways within the AV node instead of just one (present in about 5% of the general population).\n- Stable patient management\n- first line - Vagal maneuvers such as carotid massage or the Valsalva maneuver.\n- Second line - adenosine 6mg then 12 and 18mg maximum 36mg (according to resus u K) OR 6mg then 12mg and 12mg maximum 30mg (according to NICE)\n- Third line - if above treatment ineffective then Beta blocker or rate limiting calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem\n- fourth line - if above treatment ineffective then synchronized cardioversion. 55 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 55:\nA 35 years old female who is paraplegic following a spinal cord injury, is brought into the ED with complaint of blurred vision. She has not opened her bowels for few days. She has a long-term catheter. Her heart rate is 55bpm, BP 147/89mmHg, and a bladder scan showed a 70ml residual volume. What is the most likely diagnosis? https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **A)** Cauda equina syndrome\n- **B)** Transverse myelitis\n- **C)** Guillain-Barre syndrome\n- **D)** Autonomic dysreflexia\n- **E)** Sciatica Explanation\n- check the autonomic dysreflexia module in major trauma. 56 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 56:\nAn 88-year-old female brought into the ED by her daughter. She reports that she is not well for the last few days. While in ED she was shouting and repeatedly saying she is seeing someone near the door. What would be the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Alcohol intoxication\n- **B)** Personality disorder   C\n- Dementia\n- **D)** Obsessive compulsive disorder\n- **E)** Delirium Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of delirium Delirium\n- Delirium(sometimes called\n- acute confusional state')is an acute, fluctuating syndrome of encephalopathy causing disturbed consciousness, attention, cognition, and perception.\n- Delirium usually develops over hours to days and behavioural disturbance, personality changes, and psychotic features may occur\n- Delirium typically occurs in people with predisposing factors(such as advanced age or multiple comorbidities) when new precipitating factors(such as some medications or infection) are added. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Hyperactive delirium— may present with inappropriate behaviour, hallucinations, or agitation.\n- Restlessness and wandering are common,\n- Hypoactive delirium (more common)— may present with lethargy and reduced concentration and appetite. The person may appear quiet or withdrawn\n- Mixed delirium— signs and symptoms of both hyperactive and hypoactive subtypes are present. c opo\n- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:\n- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.\n- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.\n- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.\n- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.\n- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).\n- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep-cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night- time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal). https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- mye.stigations\n- Delirium is a clinical diagnosis based on a detailed history, examination, and relevant investigations\n- Confirm a diagnosis of deliriumby carrying out a cognitive assessment based on the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-S) criteria, the short Confusion Assessment Method (short-CAM), or the 4A's test\n- Arrange targeted investigations based on findings from the history and examination, for example:\n- Urinalysis— to identify conditions such as infection or hyperglycaemia\n- CXR or sputum culture- For chest infection\n- FBC — for anaemia\n- Most people with delirium should be admitted for urgent assessment, close monitoring, and treatment.\n- If the person is deemed to not have capacity to consent, decisions should be made in the best interests of the person using the Mental Capacity Act 2005.\n- If-the-person-with-delirium-deyelqps.ghallenging shouting);\n- Address any underlying causes for the behaviour (such as discomfort, thirst, or need for the toilet).\n- Advise moving the person to a safe, low-stimulation environment (such as a quiet room).\n- Advise use of verbal and non-verbal de-escalation techniques (such as active listening, effective verbal responding, pictures, and symbols).\n- If these measures fail, consider pharmacological measure and seek advice from an elderly care psychiatrist, or elderly care physician. Depending on the specific situation (for example the person is a danger to themselves or to others).\n- NICE recommends - Short-term (for 1 week or less) low-dose haloperidol\n- Antipsychotic drugs should be avoided, or used with caution in Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies Management principles in end-of-life care include;\n- Considering non-pharmacological management of delirium in a person in the last days of life.\n- Treating any reversible causes of delirium, for example psychological causes or certain metabolic disorders (for example renal failure or hyponatraemia). https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Considering a trial of an antipsychotic medicine (such as haloperidol or levomepromazine) to manage delirium, in the presence of intractable distress.\n- Monitoring the benefits and side effects of these medicines at least daily and adjust the prescription as the clinical situation changes. 58 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 58:\nA 6-month-old infant brought to the ED by his mother. On assessment he appears mottled, CRT>4 seconds. ECG shows SVT- What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** IV fluid B\n- Adenosine\n- **C)** IV antibiotic D\n- Amiodarone\n- **E)** Synchronized DC shock Explanatien;\n- Decompensated SVT initially, treated with synchronized cardioversion Ij/kg. GUIDELINES Paediatric cardiac anhythmias 59 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 59:\nWhich of the following is not a typical feature of ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome?   A\n- Hyponatremia\n- **B)** Hypo-osmolality 282 mosm/kg\n- **C)** Haematocrit <0.45 https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **D)** Serum albumin e 35 g/l\n- **E)** potassium>5mmol Explanation; - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Haematocrit > 0.45 in OHSS criteria. Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome\n- Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS) is a complication of fertility treatment, particularly of In vitro fertilisation (IVF). It can be potentially serious and it is important that you are aware of it if you are having any fertility treatment.\n- OHSS only develops after the trigger injection (the final injection you will be instructed to take before egg collection) in IVF treatment, or after ovulation in other types of fertility treatment.\n- Clinicians need to be aware of the symptoms and signs of OHSS, as the diagnosis is based on clinical criteria.\n- Time of onset of symptoms relative to trigger\n- Medication used for trigger (hCG or GnRH agonist)\n- Number of follicles on final monitoring scan\n- Number of eggs collected\n- Were embryos replaced and how many? polycystic ovary syndrome diagnosis?\n- Symptoms\n- Abdominal bloating\n- Abdominal discomfort/pain, need for analgesia\n- Nausea and vomiting\n- Breathlessness, inability to lie flat or talk in full sentences Reduced urine output Leg swelling Vulval swelling\n- Associated comorbidities such as thrombosis\n- Investigations\n- Full blood count\n- Haematocrit (haemoconcentration)\n- C-reactive protein (severity)\n- Urea and electrolytes (hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia)\n- Serum osmolality (hypo-osmolality) https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Liver function tests (elevated enzymes and reduced albumin) Coagulation profile (elevated fibrinogen and reduced antithrombin)\n- hCC (to determine outcome of treatment cycle) if appropriate Ultrasound scan: ovarian size, pelvic and abdominal free fluid.\n- Consider ovarian Doppler if torsion suspected\n- D-dimer\n- ABCS\n- ECC\n- CXR,CT Category critic*' OHSS Features Abdominal t*oating\n- Mild abdominal pain\n- Ovarian Size usually 8\n- abdominal in\n- Nausea vomiting\n- Ultrasound evidence of ascites\n- Ovarian size usually 8—12 cm\n-\n- Clinical ascites (f hydrothorax) Oliguria 300 ml/day or 30 mVhour)\n- Haematocrit > 0.45\n- Hyponatraemia (sodium < 135 rnrml,'l)\n- Hypo-osmolality (osmolality 282 mOsmAg) (potassium > 5 mmoMI)\n- Hypoproteinæmia (serum albumin 35 g/"\n- Ovarian size usually > 12\n- Tense ascitesnarge hydrothorax\n- Haematocrit > O, 55\n- White cen count > 25 0001mI Oliguria/anuria\n- Thrombæmbolism\n- Acute respÉatory\n- Management Pain relief; Offer paracetamol or opiates including codeine.\n- NSAIDs is best avoided in OHSS\n- thromboprophylaxis-with-LM.W.H=Women with severe OHSS being managed on an outpatient basis should receive thromboprophylaxis with LMVVH. The duration oftreatment should be individualised, taking into account risk factors and whether or not conception occurs.\n- Paracentesis of ascitic fluid - may be carried out by the abdominal or transvaginal route under ultrasound guidance\n- There is insufficient evidence to support the use of GnRH antagonists or dopamine agonists in treating established OHSS.\n- with OHSS should be provided with verbal and written information about their condition and fluid intake and output monitoring. Admission — in critical cases or worsening of condition despite proper treatment. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 62 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 62:\nA paramedic calls you to bring a 4kg collapsed child to ED who is in cardiac arrest. What would be the correct adrenaline dose for this patient?\n- **A)** 0.6ml O\n- **B)** 0.2ml C, 0.4m I\n- **D)** 0.3ml\n- **E)** 0.1ml Explanation;\n- The recommended IV/IO dose of adrenaline in children is 10 mcgkg (0.1mL kg of 1 in 10,000 solution). The child's weight is 4kg, therefore, 4 x 10 = 40 mcg ( 0.4m')\n- Another way to memorize: weight is 5kg then the adrenaline dose will be O.Sml.\n- Similarly, if the weight is 3.5kg adrenaline dose would be 0.3Sml 64 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 64:\nA 14-year-old girl came to the ED and asked for contraception pills. Her partner is her teacher at school. She doesn't want anyone to know about her relationship with the teacher and she refuses to\n- nvolve her parents. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Give contraception and discharge her\n- **B)** Inform the safeguarding authority\n- **C)** Refuse to give her contraception\n- **D)** Call her parents immediately\n- **E)** Advise her to tell her parents Explanation\n- This is likely to be a case of exploitation.\n- The teacher has position of trust and this position shouldn't be abused\n- Inform the safeguarding. If this option isn't given then pick the option inform the police. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 66:\nA 64-year-old male patient had right lung lobectomy and presented to the ED with chest pain and short of His chest x-ray is shown. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Surgical chest drains at Sth intercostal space on the right side\n- **B)** Surgical chest drain at 5th intercostal space on the left side\n- **C)** Needle thoracocentasis at 5th intercostal space midaxillary line\n- **D)** Surgical chest drain at 2nd intercostal space on the right side\n- **E)** Surgical chest drain at 2nd intercostal space on the left side Explanation\n- Patient likely has developed post-operative complication, Chest tube placement following pulmonary lobectomy is common practice. postoperative pleural effusion is drained and in case Of an air leak, development Of a (tension-) pneumothorax is prevented.\n- prolonged air leak days): commonest complication post-lobectomy, up to\n- atelectasis: common\n- pneumonia(rarely empyema)\n- atrial fibrillation: common\n- postoperative pulmonary edema\n- ARDS\n- bronchial dehiscence\n- hemothorax: hemorrhage is common, 2.9% require 24 units packed cells\n- chylothorax: more common with aggressive mediastinal nodal dissections\n- bronchopleural fistula (BPF): very rare after lobectomy\n- lobar torsion: rare and potentially life-threatening, usually right middle lobe (70% cases)\n- pulmonary vein stump thrombosis: rare after lobectomy https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- anastomotic dehiscence: more common with sleeve Io bectomy\n- pulmonary embolism (PE)\n- nerve injury. phrenic nerve, recurrent laryngeal nerve\n- be familiar with entire complications, you may have question in different way. 67 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 67:\nA 35-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of headache and nausea. On examination, he has neck stiffness and nan-blanching rash in his lower limb. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Viral meningitis\n- **B)** HSP\n- **C)** Malaria D\n- Meningococcal sepsis\n- **E)** Hemorrhagic fever\n- patient clinical features suggestive of meningococcal septicemia Bacterial meningitis and merungococcal dlsease\n- Meningitis is inflammation of the two inner meninges (the pia and arachnoid mater) of the brain and spinal cord.\n- Bacterial meningitis is a life-threatening condition that affects all ages, but is most common in babies and children.\n- Meningococcal diseaseis infection with Neisseria meningitidis. It can result in meningococcal meningitis (15% Of cases) or meningococcal septicaemia (2S% of cases).\n- pneumococcal disease is infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae(also called pneumococcal). https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Overall in the I-JK, Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcus), Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus), and Haemophilus influenzaetype b (Hib) are the most common causative organisms of acute bacterial meningitis in children aged 3 months or older and adults.\n- In neonates (younger than 1 month of age),Streptococcus agalactiae, Escherichia coli, S. pneumoniae, and Listeria monocytogenes are the most common causative organisms.\n- In adults, S. pneumoniaeis the most common cause of bacterial meningitis.\n- Clinical features\n- Common-non-specific-symptom*igns;\n- Fever, Vomiting/nausea, Lethargy, Irritability/unsettled behaviour, Ill appearance, Headache.\n- More-specific-symptoms/signs-inglude;\n- Non-blanching rash - A rapidly evolving petechial or purpuric rash is a sign of very severe disease.\n- stiff neck\n- Capillary refill time of more than 2 seconds, cold hands and feet, unusual skin colour, Shock and hypotension, Leg pain.\n- Back rigidity, Bulging fontanelle, photophobia.\n- Kernig's sign - (person unable to fully extend at the knee when hip is flexed).\n- Brudzinski's sign - (person's knees and hips flex when neck is flexed).\n- Unconsciousness or toxic/moribund state.\n- Focal neurological deficit including cranial nerve involvement and abnormal pupils.\n- Seizures\n- Investigations\n- blood for culture within 1 hour of arrival at hospital and ideally prior to giving antibiotics\n- Serumpneumococcal and meningococcal PCR\n- FBC and differential, LFTs, LJ&E, blood glucose Do a lumbar puncture (LP; unless contraindicated) within 1 hour of arrival at hospital.\n- Consider CSF PCR for tuberculosis\n- Neuroimaging is not indicated unless you suspect alternative pathology because there are signs indicating risk of cerebral herniation ( secondary to raised intracranial pressure).\n- Management https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- ere-hospital-(gqmmunity)\n- Refer all patients with suspected meningitis and/or meningococcal sepsis to hospital immediately.\n- The British Infection Association recommends: - Benzylpenicillin (intramuscular or intravenous) OR\n- A third-generation cephalosporin such as cefotaxime or ceftriaxone (intramuscular or intravenous).\n- Do not delay transfer to hospital by attempting to give parenteral antibiotics.\n- Hospital_settingnanagement\n- Supportive measure * corticosteroid - Cive empirical intravenous dexamethasone (10mg IV 6 hourly) to all adults with acute bacterial meningitis within 1 hour of presentation to hospital.\n- Antibiotics British Infection Association recommends:\n- IV ceftriaxone:2 g intravenously every 12 hours OR\n- IV cefotaxime: 2 g intravenously every 6 hours\n- If patient allergic penicillin or cephalosporin : chloramphenicol: 25 mg/kg intravenously every 6 hours\n- In addition to above:\n- Consider Vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg or rifampicin: 600 mg IV to cover for penicillin- resistant pneumococci\n- Consider amoxicillin: 2 g IV or trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole: 10-20 mg/kg/day to cover cover for Listeria monocytogenes (neonates or immunocompromised patients). 68 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 68:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of sudden onset of chest pain while eating which is lasted for 20 minutes. He is pain free now. His examination revealed diastolic murmur. His lab markers given below. Trop-l : 16 (<14) D-dirner: 380 ng,'rnl What would be the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** pulmonary embolism\n- **B)** Aortic aneurysm\n- **C)** Cardiomyopathy\n- **D)** Thoracic aortic dissection\n- **E)** Acute coronary syndrome https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Patient clinical and lab features are suggestive of acute coronary syndrome, as Trop\n- l is elevated and D-dimer in the normal range. Here the diastolic murmur heard on examination may be indicative of an underlying valvular heart disease, which can be a risk factor for ACS.\n- In aortic dissection, D-dimer is elevated (<500ng/ml normal range) and Trap-I is usually negative. 69 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 69:\nA 63-year-old male known diabetic patient presented to the ED with complaint of vomiting and abdominal pain. He is on oral antidiabetic medication canagliflozin and insulin. His vac shown. What is the likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Alcohol ketoacidosis\n- **B)** Acute pancreatitis\n- **C)** Canagliflozin adverse effects\n- **D)** Sepsis\n- **E)** Euglycemic DKA Patient clinical features, history of diabetes and use of canagliflozin and insulin puts them at risk for DRA. The presence of metabolic acidosis with high lactate on VBC are all consistent with DRA. Additionally, the patient's blood glucose level of 6.7mmol/L (which is not particularly high) is consistent with euglycemic DRA. Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis\n- It is an uncommon diabetic complication associated with several risk factors such as fasting, surgery, pregnancy, and now the use of sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitors.\n- Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA, EDKA) is a clinical syndrome occurring both in type 1 (TIDM) and type 2 (T2DM) diabetes mellitus characterized by:\n- Confirm ketosis by measuring blood ketone (>3.0 mmol/L) https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Confirm acidosis with a venous bicarbonate (<15mmol/L) or venous\n- Euglycemia - Glucose levels may be normal People with type 2 diabetes taking SGLT-2 inhibitors are at higher risk (the relative risk is around 1.3 per 1000 person- years) of developing diabetic ketoacidosis.\n- Suspect OKA in presence of nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, difficulty breathing, confusion, fatigue and drowsiness\n- EDKA has grown with the introduction of sodium- glucose transporter 2 (SCLT2) inhibitors.\n- Ketone body production in EDKA is similar to DKA with acetoacetic acid, beta-hydroxybutyric acid (after acetoacetic acid reduction), and acetone (after acetoacetic acid decarboxylation). The resulting anion gap metabolic acidosis triggers respiratory compensation.\n- Anaerobic glycolysis due to inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygenation as well as the metabolic derangements itself present in OKA might contribute to the elevated lactate levels Name ot SGLT n i hlbitor ( 00) 00) Empaglinozn (1 00) Theraputic indication 2 ctvmic (CHF) kidney disease (CKD) 2 and Ch roNC Mart failure 2 Not an-rendy liconwd\n- Do not prescribe an SCLT-2 inhibitor to people with:\n- DRA\n- Moderate-to-severe renal impairment — in general avoid initiation if estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) is less than 60 mL/min/1.73 m2; avoid continuation if eCFR is less than 45 mL/min/l.73 m2.\n- Note: dapagliflozin is licensed for use in chronic kidney disease, but should not be initiated in people with eCFR less than 15mL/min/1.73 m2\n- Severe hepatic impairment\n- Increasing age — avoid empagliflozin if aged over 85 years\n- Active foot disease (such as skin ulceration, osteomyelitis, or gangrene) https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Benefits-of-using-SGLT:2-inhihitQrs-impeople with-type-2-diabetes\n- Reduction in HbA1c - Up to 10 mmol/mol with some agents, dependent on starting HbA1c level\n- Low incidence of hypoglycaemia 9\n- The mechanism of action means that their effect is proportional to blood glucose\n- Weight loss (up to 3kg)\n- With a significant effect to reduce visceral fat\n- Reduces prog ression of chronic complications affecting cardiovascular system and kidneys (see next pages)\n- Cenital infections\n- DRA Amputation and fracture in some trials (but not others) with canagliflozin\n- Rare-side-effects-reported-with-SGLT.:2 inhibiters Fournier's gangrene. It is a rare but this serious bacterial infection needs urgent treatment\n- Suspect if there is pain, redness, swelling or discomfort in perineal or genital area. Needs urgent surgery and IV antibiotics\n- Management\n- If confirmed to have ketosis with acidosis (regardless of the glucose level) then start a fixed rate intravenous insulin infusion and IV fluids as per JBOS guidelines (joint British diabetic society guideline)\n- Concomitant additional glucose infusion may be required to avoid hypoglycaemia\n- Revisit prescribing in those with increasing frailty. Ngte_zbe familiar with the entire module, you may have question in different ways. 70 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 70:\nA 30-year-old 34 weeks gravid female presents to the ED With complaint Of some fluid discharge from her pelvis while sitting at home. You suspect premature rupture of membrane (PROM). What antibiotic recommended for this patient?\n- **A)** Ciprofloxacin https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **B)** Co-amoxiclav\n- **C)** Ceftriaxone D\n- Amoxicillin\n- **E)** Erythromycin Explanation - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- women with p-PROM oral erythromycin 250 mg 4 times a day for a maximum of 10 days or until the woman is in established labour (whichever is sooner).\n- Alternative if contraindicate or patient can't tolerate then consider an oral penicillin for a maximum of 10 days or until the woman is in established labour (whichever is sooner).\n- Care of women presenting with suspected Prelabour Rupture of Membrane from 24th weeks of gestation\n- Premature rupture of membranes (PROM) is the rupture of gestational membranes prior to the onset of labor\n- The diagnosis of spontaneous rupture of the membranes is made by maternal history followed by a sterile speculum examination.\n- If, on speculum examination, no amniotic fluid is observed, clinicians should consider performing an insulin-like growth factor binding protein 1 (ICFBP-I) or placental alpha microglobulin-l (PAMG-I) test of vaginal fluid to guide further management.\n- A combination of clinical assessment, maternal blood tests (C\n- reactive protein and white cell count) and fetal heart rate should be used to diagnose chorioamnionitis in women with PPROM; these parameters should not be used in isolation.\n- An antibiotic - erythromycin should be given for 10 days or until the woman is in established labour (whichever is sooner) following the diagnosis of https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC PPROM, and corticosteroids and magnesium sulfate, considered or offered.\n- Expectant-management\n- Women whose pregnancy is complicated by PPROM who have no contraindications to continuing pregnancy should be offered expectant management until 37+0 weeks, as this is associated with better outcomes compared with early birth. Timing of birth should be discussed with each woman on a individual basis. 72 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 72:\nA 15-year-old girl brought into the ED with complaints of confusion and drowsiness. Her mother states she found empty packets of some med 'cations along with a suicidal note. Her lab is given. What is the most likely cause of her condition?   A\n- Opioid overdose\n- **B)** Salicylate toxicity C\n- TCA overdose\n- **D)** Paracetamol poisoning\n- **E)** Cocaine toxicity Explanation;\n- ABC results show a respiratory alkalosis with partial metabolic (HC03-) compensation\n- To calculate anion gap: serum anion gap using the formula [(Na + K) - (Cl + HC03)] = 27.2\n- Respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis with raised anion gap this combination is so characteristics of salicylate overdose. NQt%for more detail check the salicylate poisoning module in pharmacology & poisoning. 73 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 73:\nIn infants, intraosseous access should avoid which part of the bone? Epiphysis\n- **B)** Proximal tibia https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **C)** Diaphysis\n- **D)** Mid bone E\n- Metaphysis Explanation; - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- The needle should avoid epiphysis during intraosseous insertion as this can traumatize the growth plate.\n- The growth plate is located on the epiphysis; thus, this area should be avoided.\n- Extravasation is a very common complication of this route of fluid administration.\n- The extremity must be checked because compartment syndrome is a known complication. Infection is a very rare complication but cellulitis may result at the skin site. 75 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 75:\nA 36-year-old male brought into the ED with a spinal cord injury. He has ipsilateral loss of motor and sensory weakness on the right side of the body. What more examination findings will be in this patient?\n- **A)** Contralateral loss of vibration\n- **B)** Contralateral loss of temperature\n- **C)** Ipsilateral loss of temperature\n- **D)** Ipsilateral loss of pain\n- **E)** Ipsilateral facial weakness Explanation;\n- patient clinical features suggestive of Brown- Séquard syndrome\n- Ipsilateral hemiparesis\n- Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch sensation\n- Contralateral loss of crude touch, pain, and temperature sensation 77 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 77:\nWhat specific finding of CO differentiates between CO and cyanide poisoning? https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **A)** Hypotension - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** Increase respiratory rate C' Cherry red skin\n- **D)** High lactate level\n- **E)** Sa02 on air Explanation\n- New study and literature suggests that Cherry-red discolouration of the skin and mucous membranes is an often-quoted sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning. very rarer to be seen in cyanide.\n- Sa02 98% on air: This indicates good oxygen saturation and does not differentiate between CO and cyanide poisoning.\n- Rest findings are seen in both CO and cyanide. had revised CO and cyanide poisoning module, that Cherry-red discolouration of the skin and mucous membranes is an often-quoted sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning. very rarer to be seen in cyanide. 7B / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 78:\nA 32-week unconscious pregnant mother brought to the ED after RTA. Her observations are: sp02:  mmHg 120 bpm 25 breaths per minute You've decided to start a blood transfusion. However, she carries a witnessed written directive refusing blood and all blood products. What would be your next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Refers to OBS and Gynae team\n- **B)** Transfuse blood and refer to OBS and Cynae\n- **C)** Contact authority like Trust legal department https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **D)** Transfuse blood products\n- **E)** Cive IV fluid Without transfusing blood Explanation;\n- Many Jehovah's witnesses refuse transfusions of whole blood (including preoperative autologous donation) and the primary blood components — red cells, platelets, white cells, and unfractionated plasma. The guideline suggests that if they're competent or if they carry the blood refusal card( witnessed written directives), we should respect their decision not to receive blood products even if this M.nould lead to their death.\n- You can advise them to receive blood products. however, you aren't allowed to pressure them to accept it.\n- Child (less than 16) Jehovah's witnesses unconscious — transfuse blood in his/her best interest.\n- Child (less than 16) Jehovah's witnesses conscious — is child deemed competent? If yes! and refuse, then respect his decision and do not transfuse blood products. But if a child isn't competent and needs urgent transfusion then transfuse in his best interest.\n- Child Jehovah's witnesses conscious and refuse lifesaving transfusion - blood transfusion should not be given because the child is competent to make the decision\n- Adult Jehovah's witnesses unconscious and has blood refusal card ( witnessed written directives) - then don't transfuse blood products.\n- Adult Jehovah's witnesses unconscious and no blood refusal card (mentioned or given) — then transfuse blood products.\n- Adult Jehovah's witnesses competent— then don't transfuse blood products. 79 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 79:\nA 21-year-old male presented to the ED with priapism for the last 4 hours. Lidocaine has been given. What would be your next step of management? A Aspirate the blood With needle\n- **B)** Surgical intervention\n- **C)** Refer to urologist https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper\n- **D)** Ice compression - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Corpora cavernosa injection With phenylephrine Explanation\n- After application of anesthesia aspirate the blood.\n- Priapism- management algorithm Initial conservative measures Lcx:al anaesthesia o' the penis Insert wide bae butterfly through the into the corgx\n- ra cavernosa cavernosal until bright red arteria blood is '*Jtained\n- Cavernosal irrigation Intracavernosal therapy intracavemosal is with aliquots 200 being injected every 3-5 until detwnescence achieved Surgical therapy Surgical shunting Consider grimary prosthesis implantation if priaE*sm has t*en meseu for mcre than 36 hcwrs 80 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 80:\nA 24-year-old girl presented to the ED with a complaint vaginal discharge. She states that she had unprotected sex with her new partner. Her examination revealed an inflamed cervix. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Start HIV prophylaxis\n- **B)** Ask her about her husband symptoms\n- **C)** Blood culture\n- **D)** Send cervical swab culture\n- **E)** Refer her to GUM clinic for STI screening\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  The patient most likely has an uncomplicated chlamydia infection. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- If chlamydia infection is suspected or confirmed - NICE strongly recommends referral to a genito- urinary medicine (GUM) clinic for management. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper Chlamydia infection -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Gonorrhoea infection https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Common vaginal infections rial vaginosis Trichomonas Candida vulvovaginitis\n- Thin white discharge With fishy Odor\n- Innamrnation ( )\n- Frothy, , foul-smelling\n- Metronidazole\n- Treat sexual p Mrwr(s)\n- Clinical features\n- Innarnmation\n- Thick. White. d i S Charge\n- Vagina itching or soreness\n- FWconaz01eot Enricack*. nystatin. or flucytosine 81 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 81:\nA 56-year-old male brought into ED who has Severe bradycardia for which He has been put on a pacemaker with a demand pacing mode. pacing rate: 80 Pacing rate: 100mA His pacing rhythm is given. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Increase pacing rate\n- **B)** Increase pacing current\n- **C)** Change the PAD positions\n- **D)** Refer for transvenous pacing\n- **E)** Put percussion pacing mode Explanation;\n- Pacer spikes with no associated QRS complex. Therefore, increase the current until you regain capture, check for electrolyte abnormalities, and position the pads https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Pad positions\n- Pacing spike of a nd T waves aner each generated pacer spike Asæssing ekctmardiographic captuæ with pacing.\n- **A)** Intrinsic patient bradycardic rhythtn\n- **B)** NO electrical capture as iS the threshold level.\n- **C)** TCP With electrical Capture.\n- Threshold current values will vary depending on the clinical situation. Transcutaneous pacing thresholds tend to be lowest in healthy individuals or in patients with minimal hemodynamic compromise. In these settings, the threshold is usually in the range of 40 to 80 mA Most patients are paced using a current in the range of 20 to 140 mm\n- Electrical capture is best judged by the presence of a consistent ST segment and T wave after each generated pacer spike as in the above pacing image.\n- Once electrical capture is achieved, mechanical capture must be ensured by either a palpable pulse rate or arterial catheter blood pressure monitoring. The patient will have a pulse rate that is exactly equal to that of the paced rhythm on the cardiac monitor if mechanical capture is achieved. 82 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 82:\nA 5-year-old child brought into the ED by his mother who is confused and drowsy. His mother states that he was living with his grandparents for the last five days and has had coryzal symptoms and fever which was treated with antipyretic. His lab is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis? https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper   A\n- Reye syndrome\n- **B)** Marfan syndrome\n- **C)** Bacterial meningitis - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **D)** Henoch Schönlein purpura\n- **E)** Hepatitis Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of Reye's syndrome as child likely be treated with aspirin.\n- Reye_syndrome\n- Reye's syndrome is an illness that meets all of the following criteria:\n- An acute, non-inflammatory encephalopathy with either sterile CSF containing c9 Wac/ml_ or cerebral oedema without inflammatory cell infiltrate\n- hepatic dysfunction documented by either a threefold elevation of serum transaminases and/or serum ammonia, or liver biopsy demonstrating fatty infiltration.\n- There is no other diagnosis to account for the cerebral and hepatic derangement.\n- Age between 5 and 14 years,\n- white ethnicity\n- Recent hx of viral infection (e.g., influenza, varicella- zoster infection, or gastroenteritis).\n- Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) and other drug exposure, toxin exposure, a genetically predisposed individual, and winter/spring months.\n- Clinical features\n- Hyperventilation\n- Vomiting\n- Altered mental status\n- Hepatomegaly\n- hyper-reflexia/areflexia\n- diminished pain response\n- Seizure — late stage\n- Abnormal pupillary response\n- Absence of, or minimal, jaundice and scleral icterus\n- .lnyestigations https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Serum electrolyte - variable; metabolic acidosis\n- Serum glucose - may be <2.8 mmol/L (CSO mg/dL)\n- LFTs - elevated transaminase and aminotransferase; bilirubin mildly elevated, but usually normal\n- Serum ammonia - Ammonia levels are usually at least 1.5 to 20 times elevated at the time of presentation\n- PT/PTT- usually prolonged\n- Blood gas\n- Urine/serum toxicology\n- Urinalysis\n- lumbar puncture with cerebrospinal fluid analysis\n- Liver biopsy\n- Metabolic testing\n- Management\n- Treatment mainly supportive\n- In severe cases patient required intensive care monitoring.\n- Slow intravenous bicarbonate infusion recommended if 7.0\n- In fluid overload give furosemide\n- lv glucose in hypoglycaemia\n- lv fluid plus vasopressor in hypotension\n- Anticonvulsants (phenytoin) in seizures\n- ICP-lowering regimen in raised ICP 83 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 83:\nA 30-year-old female presented to the ED with painful leg. On examination, her was tender and swelled. You've order D-dimer which came negative. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Repeat D-dimer\n- **B)** Order proximal leg WS scan\n- **C)** Alternative diagnosis treat as cellulitis\n- **D)** Give LMWH and wait for WS scan\n- **E)** Order CTPA Explanation\n- DVT is likely as the well score for this patient is 2.\n- After D-dimer, a proximal u/S scan should be ordered with the result available within 24 hours.\n- NICE guideline DVT is likely if well score is 2 or more:\n- following steps should be taken: order proximal leg U/S with the result available within 4 hours if possible. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC If a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan result cannot be obtained within 4 hours, offer people with a DVT Wells score of 2 points or more:\n- a D-dimer test, then\n- interim therapeutic anticoagulation and\n- a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan with the result available within 24 hours.\n- both D-dimer and proximal IJ/S scan came negative then should be rule out and an alternative diagnosis should be considered. 85 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 85:\nA 15kg child brought into the ED by his mother with complaint od diarrhea and vomiting. On examination he was dehydrated and mottled skin. You want to initiate the resuscitation with IV fluid. What would be the hourly maintenance rate for this child?\n- **A)** 60ml\n- **B)** 40m!   c. soml\n- **D)** 20ml Explanation\n- It would be 50ml/hr as patient weight is 15kg 40ml/hr\n- 1st 10kg x 4ml\n- 2nd Skg x 2ml= IO total = 50ml/hr\n- Follow ABCDE protocol The preferred fluid type for IV maintenance is sodium chloride 0.9% with glucose\n- Resuscitation Step: - fluid bouls NICE recommends 10-20 ml/kg in shock patients and 10ml/kg in non shock patients.\n- Resus council 2021 guideline recommends 10ml/kg in both shock and non-clinical shock patient\n- Cive a second bolus, if shock persist\n- If remains shocked after second bolus, Cive a further bolus, consider discussion with paediatric intensive https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC care team.\n- Example-I A 7kg child brought into the ED with clinical shock, what would be fluid bolus for this patient? 7 140m' (to follow NICE) 7 (to follow resus 20m) M.A.inte.na.n.ge-5.tep_- Once symptoms and signs of shock have resolved\n- Calculate daily maintenance requirement\n- Use 10% estimate for deficit calculation (Note: use estimate for deficit calculation shock patient and 5% in non-shock patients, to assess that, check table-A in assessment) god' we igh t L IA 10k, 2" 10kg fluid requirements fluid requirement .1\n- Example -1 A 26 kg child responded to a 20 ml/kg or 10ml/kg (follow resus) fluid bolus and is no longer shocked. Calculate fluid deficit? formula: %age x weight x 10 10 26 10 = 2600ml (replace in 24-4B hrs)\n- Example-2 A 26 kg child responded to a 20 ml/kg or 10ml/kg (follow resus) fluid bolus and is no longer shocked. What is his maintenance hourly IV fluid requirement using the standard regime? Hourly maintenance = (10kg x 4ml) + (10kg x 2ml) + (6kg x Iml) = 66ml/hr if convert to daily maintenance = (100 rnl/kg x 10 kg) + (50 ml/kg x IO kg) + (20 ml/kg x 6 kg) = 1620m l/day Note—for more detail check acute gastroenteritis module in acute medicine- gastroenterology system. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper - I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 86 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 86:\nA 14-year-old male RTA patient brought into the ED by paramedics with facial trauma. Patient is alert and conscious. While transfer to resus he reported severe neck pam. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Call for anesthetic help\n- **B)** Intubate the patient\n- **C)** Cricothyroidotomy\n- **D)** Apply manual In-line cervical stabilization\n- **E)** Start fluid Explanation\n- Nice recommendation—First step in this case would be Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS) and then application of a full in-line spinal immobilization - Cervical collar + backboard, straps, (if no airway compromise or known spinal deformities, such as ankylosing spondylitis). However, patient has facial trauma which compromised patient airways. Therefore, full in- inline spinal immobilization shouldn't be carried out in this patient as application cervical collar is contraindicated.\n- An anesthetic should be called to secure his airway meanwhile, Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS) by an appropriately trained clinician should be carried out.\n- If option given as 3-points cervical spine immobilization should be incorrect in this scenario\n- Assessment for spinal injury_\n- On arrival at the scene of the incident, use a prioritising sequence to assess people with suspected trauma, for example\n- C - catastrophic haemorrhage\n- A - airway with in-line spinal immobilisation breathing\n- C- circulation\n- D - disability (neurological)\n- E - exposure and environment.\n- At all stages of the assessment:\n- protect the person's cervical spine with manual in-line spinal immobilisation, particularly during any airway intervention and\n- avoid moving the remainder of the spine. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Assess the person for spinal injury, initially taking into account the factors listed below. Check if the person:\n- has any significant distracting injuries\n- is under the influence of drugs or alcohol\n- is confused or uncooperative\n- has a reduced level of consciousness\n- has any spinal pain\n- has any hand or foot weakness (motor assessment)\n- has altered or absent sensation in the hands or feet (sensory assessment)\n- has priapism (unconscious or exposed male)\n- has a history of past spinal problems, including previous spinal surgery or conditions that predispose to instability of the spine.\n- Carry out full in-line spinal immobilisation if any of the factors mentioned above are present or if this assessment cannot be done.\n- Step — 01 Assessment for cervical spine Injury — use Canadian c-spme rule The Canadian C-Spine Rule'\n- Step -02 - Assessment for thoracic or lurnbosacral spine iruury_ https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, Paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Step -03 carry out or maintain full in-Ime spinal immobilisation\n- NOTE\n-\n- Step -04 how carry out full in-line spinal Immobilization\n- When carrying out full in-line spinal immobilisation in adults and children, manually stabilise the head with the spine in-line using the following stepwise approach:\n- Fit an appropriately Sized semi\n- rigid contraindicated by: o a ct*npromised airway spinal deformities, such as spondylit' (in these cases keep the spine in the person's current position).\n- Reassess atter applying the Place ana secure the person on a stretcher.\n- tape, vacuum mattress. When carrying m_Jt in-line in children, further o members and carers if appropriate o keeping infants in their car seat if o usinga scck)pstretcherwith blanket vacuum mattress, limb splints Kendrick extrication device,\n- Step-OS Diagnostic imaging\n- After pain relief https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Imaging for spinal injury should be performed urgently, and the images should be interpreted immediately by a healthcare professional with training and skills in this area. Key Differences Between MILS and cervical spine immobilization\n- Paediatric Traur\n- na - Stabilisation of the Cervical Spine https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper - I I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Note — Last revised November 2024 87 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 87:\nA 52-year-old male who has left the psychiatric ward and presented to the ED with palpitation, shortness of breath, and headache. On assessment, he admits to recent amyl nitrates use. He is cyanosed and his saturation is 82% on air. Given the likely diagnosis, what would be the treatment?\n- **A)** Sodium bicarbonate\n- **B)** Dantrolene   C\n- Hydroxocobalamin\n- **D)** Methylene blue\n- **E)** High flow oxygen Explanation; The patient has methemoglobinemia, a disorder of red blood cells that interferes with normal hemoglobin function and oxygen transport. This can result from either congenital (or acquired processes\n- Benzocaine Dapsone\n- Nitroglycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin\n- Nitroprusside\n- Phenazopyridine Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23/11/2025, paper -l I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary)\n- Treat-anyone-who-is-symptomatig-(yitalsjgn damp g e\n- mg/kg (0.1-0.2 mL/kg of solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes\n- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms.\n- Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in I hour, repeat the dose or consider C6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.\n- Management-ingep.defigienrp.atients\n- Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis.\n- Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C\n- Consider_exchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion 89 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 89:\nAccording to NICE guideline, which of the following is the red flag feature in adult sepsis?\n- **A)** New need for oxygen or more to maintain Sp02> 92%\n- **B)** Respiratory rate 24 breaths per minute\n- **C)** HR >IIO bpm\n- **D)** Decrease urine output\n- **E)** Systolic BP 91-100mrnhg Explanation; https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-i-2-2/\n\n23