160ed826-61d7-48c9-b8b2-49401ae55128	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	expert-clinical-sba-mock-4	Expert Clinical SBA Mock 4	article	# Expert Clinical SBA Mock 4\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Whole-body cold or iced water immersion - In practice, do not use water immersion in patients with an altered level of consciousness (Glasgow Coma Scale score <15), uncooperative patients, patients who require intravenous treatment, or patients who have a history of arrhythmia (e.g., atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia) or a recent. target core temperature when cooling should be 38.5C. Once this temperature is reached active cooling measures are stopped. 30 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 30:\nA 21-year-old male presented to the ED with a history Of fall on Outstretched hand. He experienced Severe pain in his right hand. His X-ray is given. What's the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Galeazzi fracture\n- **B)** Monteggia fracture C' Colles fracture D\n- Smith fracture   E, Barton fracture X-ray shows extra-articular transverse fracture through distal radius with dorsal angulation.\n- Colle's fractures\n- Mechanism of injury often results from a fall on the outstretched, extended hand.\n- The distal fragment angulated and point dorsally.\n- Patients may complain of palmar paresthesias from pressure on the median nerve.\n- Diagnosis;\n- angulation of distal fragment to point more radially on AP view wrist-ray. However normally distal radius tilt 22 degrees towards the ulnar side.\n- lateral view provides the best view of the dorsal angulation and comminution.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Undisplaced fractures manage with analgesia,immobilize in a backslab POP and elevate with sling and arrange orthopedic follow-up.\n- Reduce under anesthesia (iv sedation , bier's block or GA) if Pt has displaced fracture or significant intra-articular extension.\n- Following reduction apply a backslab POP, while maintaining the reduction with the wrist slightly flexed and pronated.\n- All open and neurovascularly compromised fractures require prompt evaluation by an orthopedic surgeon.\n- Complications\n- weak, stiff, and painful wrist\n- Malunion & cosmetic problem Carpal tunnel syndrome\n- Extensor pollicis longus rupture 31 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 31:\nA 29 year old man presented to the ED with complaint of fainting episodes and he feels that his heart is racing. He has diarrhea and vomiting for the past few days and has unable to keep anything down for 24 hours. He admits to daily drug use including cocaine and amphetamine. His observation are: HR 145 bpm BP  mmHg Temp 36.50C His ECG shown. What is most appropriate initial management?   A\n- 5mg bisoprolol x\n- **B)** Synchronised cardioversion\n- **C)** Vagal manouevres and adenosine\n- **D)** IV lorazepam Explanation\n- ECC reveals sinus tachycardia.\n- Beta-blockers should be avoided owing to the risk of coronary vasoconstriction and paradoxical\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC hypertension\n- Patient has history diarrhea and vomiting for the past few days, thus he is hypovolemic and requires IV fluid Cocaine toxicity.\n- Cocaine is a drug of abuse that is usually either insufflated (snorted), injected, or smoked in its freebase form (crack). Cocaine is a type IC anti- dysrhythmic, local anaesthetic, and sympathomimetic.\n- Cocaine toxicity occurs within minutes to hours of excessive cocaine use, High ambient temperature\n- Male sex\n- Plasma cholinesterase deficiency\n- Age 18-25 years\n- History of cocaine use\n- Concomitant use of other sympathomimetics\n- Clinical features\n- Tachycardia Hypertension\n- Hyperthermia Agitation Mydriasis\n- Diaphoresis\n- Investigations\n- ECG — reveals normal, sinus tachy:ardia, supraventricular tachycardia, ventricular dysrhythmia, ischaemic changes.\n- ECC should be performed in all patients with tachycardia, chest pain, or dyspnoea following cocaine use.\n- Bedside serum glucose - normal, hypoglycaemia or hyperglycaemia\n- Serum creatinine - elevated in volume depletion or rhabdomyolysis\n- Urea - elevated in hypovolaemia or renal insufficiency\n- Serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) - elevated in rhabdomyolysis\n- Serum troponin - elevated in myocardial infarction\n- Consider CXR, CT brain and urine cocaine assay\n- Management httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Note - Patient suspected/confirmed cocaine toxicity: body packer/stuffer — In addition to above treatment refer patient for immediate surgical decontamination If packet rupture occurs (based on symptoms, signs, or positive urine cocaine screen). 35 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 35:\nA 59-year-old man is brought to the ED with a two days history of shortness of breath. He has a past medical history of COPD. On examination, RR was 25 breath per minutes for which BiPAP was attached. According to BTS guideline wh'ch of the follow' ng is an indication to NIV?\n- **A)** RR>2S breath per minute x\n- **B)** PH less 7.25\n- **C)** Reduced conscious level\n- **D)** P02 12.6 kPa\n- **E)** pC02>8.s kPa Explanation\n- Indication of NIV in COPD\n- pH 0.35\n- pC02> 6.5\n- RR>23 If persistent after bronchodilators and controlled oxygen therapy usage evidence is for patients with arterial pH $7.25.\n- pH<7.25 has been suggested as a level below which IMV should be considered and <7.15 as the level that httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC IMV is indicated (following initial resuscitation and use of controlled oxygen).\n- Indications for invasive mechanical ventilation\n- Imminent respiratory arrest\n- Severe respiratory distress\n- Failure of or contra-indications to non-invasive ventilation (NIV)\n- Persisting pH<7.15 or deterioration in pH despite NIV Depressed consciousness (Glasgow Coma Score 45 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 45:\nA 7-year-old child brought to the ED by his father with history of fall wh'le playing on trampoline. His father states that child hasn't move his arm since fall. His X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis? x A\n- Pulled elbow\n- **B)** Radial fracture with subluxation\n- **C)** Supracondylar fracture\n- **D)** Radial head fracture\n- **E)** Ulnar fracture Explanation X-ray shows radial fracture with subluxation httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 1 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED with Lethargy, polyurea, and occasional constipation. Additionally, he is complaining of bone and flank pain. you suspect primary hyperparathyroidism. Which of the following investigation suggestive of primary hyperparathyroidism?\n- **A)** Elevated P04 level B' Elevated calcium\n- **C)** LOW PTH\n- **D)** Norrnal calcium\n- **E)** Decreased ALP Explanation\n- Investigations-of-primary-hyperthyroidism\n- Serum calcium - from high-normal to raised.\n- serum intact PTH with immunoradiometric or immunochemical assay - from high-normal to elevated\n- 2S-hydroxyvitamin D level - Vitamin D deficiency and PHPT frequently co-exist.\n- Serum alkaline phosphatase — maybe raised\n- Serum phosphorus — Low or low normal\n- 24-hour urinary calcium - high or normal in PHPT; low in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia\n- dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scan - T- score: -1 to = normal; -1 to -2S = osteopenia; < -2.5 = osteoporosis httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 2 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 20-year-old man presents with atraumatic right sided chest pain. His chest x-ray shows a right sided pneumothorax. The inter-pleural distance at the level of the hilum is 1.2cm. H