ortality.\n- Initial management of upper GI bleeding _\n- I_JGIB causing hypotension, tachycardia, orthostasis, or other signs of hypovolemic shock must be managed swiftly, and patients should be considered for admission to the intensive care unit.\n- Two large-bore intravenous lines should be placed immediately for adequate venous access. Crystalloid fluids - should be infused to maintain adequate blood pressure.\n- Blood product transfusions - Transfuse patients with massive bleeding with blood, platelets and clotting factors in line with local protocols for managing massive bleeding.\n- Do not offer platelet transfusion to patients who are not actively bleeding and are haemodynamically stable.\n- Offer platelet transfusion to patients who are actively bleeding and have a platelet count of less than 50 x 109/1itre.\n- Offer fresh frozen plasma to patients who are actively bleeding and have a prothrombin time (or international normalised ratio) or activated partial thromboplastin time greater than 1.5 times normal. Ifa patient's fibrinogen level remains less than 1.5 g/litre despite fresh frozen plasma use, offer cryoprecipitate as well.\n- Offer prothrombin complex concentrate to patients who are taking warfarin and actively bleeding.\n- Timing-of-endoscopy.\n- Offer endoscopy to unstable patients with severe acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding immediately after resuscitation.\n- Offer endoscopy within 24 hours of admission to all other patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n- Management-of-nonz.yar.igea.l-bleeding Endoscopic-treatment=Do not use adrenaline as monotherapy for the endoscopic treatment of non-\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding. gastrointestinal-bleeding Nse.one-Qf-Rhe-fQlNowing;\n- a mechanical method (for example, clips) with or without adrenaline\n- thermal coagulation with adrenaline\n- fibrin or thrombin with adrenaline.\n- Offer a repeat endoscopy to patients who re-bleed with