 Clinical Scenario 14:\nA 70-year-old patient attends with sudden, painless visual loss in his left eye wh'ch occurred one hour ago He has a past medical h 'story of hypertension and type-II diabetes. A photograph of his retina is shown What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Giant cell arteritis\n- **B)** Central retinal artery occlusion\n- **C)** Central retinal vein occlusion\n- **D)** Retinal detachment\n- **E)** Amaurosis fugax Sudden painless visual loss, pale retina and cherry red spots are the features of CRAO.\n- Central retinal artery occlusion Sudden painless vision loss in the affected eye\n- Commonly caused by emboli from atheromatous carotid arteries or thrombosis secondary to arteriosclerosis or hypertension and vasospasm secondary to giant cell arteritis\n- Affected eye pupil poorly reactive to the light with a normal counsel light reaction\n- Features of CRAO\n- pale retina (due to oedema)\n- Cherry red spot' (sparing of the center of macula due to supply by underlying choroid)\n- Attenuated vessels\n- Segmentation of blood columns in arteries (cattle trucking)\n- Optic atrophy develops over several weeks\n- Urgent referral to ophthalmologist\n- patient can be benefited from digital ocular massage\n- IV acetazolamide and mannitol\n- Timolol eye drops\n- Sublingual NCT\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 15 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 15:\nAn II-year-old soccer player brought to the ED with complaint of Knee pain that aggravated while doing sports On examination, there is tendemess over tibial tuberosity. HIS X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Distal femur fracture\n- **B)** Osgood schlatter disease\n- **C)** Avulsion Of tibial tuberosity fracture\n- **D)** proximal fibular fracture\n- **E)** Patellar fracture Explanation\n- X-ray shows bone fragmentation at the tibial tuberosity.\n- patient clinical features and X-ray suggests Osgood- Schlatter disease.\n- Qsgood-Schlatter disease\n- Osgood-Schlatter disease, also known as apophysitis of the tibial tubercle, is a chronic fatigue injury due to repeated microtrauma at the patellar tendon insertion onto the tibial tuberosity, usually affecting boys between ages 10- 15 yea rs. The typical age of onset in females may be slightly earlier (boys IO-IS years; girls 8-12 years) . Osgood-Schlatter disease is seen in active adolescents, especially those who jump and kick\n- The typical age of onset in females may be slightly earlier (boys IO-IS years; girls 8-12 years) .\n- Clinical features Clinically, patients present with pain and swelling over the tibial tuberosity exacerbated with exercise.\n- Investigations\n- tissue swelling with loss of the sharp margins of the patellar tendon is the earliest sign in the acute phase, thus, a compatible history is also essential in making the diagnosis.\n- gone fragmentation at the tibial tuberosity may be evident 3 to 4 weeks after the onset.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Treatment is usually conservative and involves rest, ice, activity modification (decreasing activities that stress the insertion, especially jumping and lunging sports), and quadriceps and hamstring strengthening exercises. Analgesia and padding to prevent pressure on the tibial tuberosity are also useful. 16 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 16:\nA 12-year-old boy brought to the ED with anaphylactic shock. Patient has given one doses of adrenaline. Which of the following is the risk factor of biphasic shock?\n- **A)** Relapse of shock after respond\n- **B)** Delay presentation to the ED more than 90 minutes\n- **C)** Adrenaline more than one time\n- **D)** No Improvement after adrenaline\n- **E)** Need Of ICU admission Explanation\n- Risk factors for biphasic reactions following anaphylaxis include:\n- more severe initial presentation of anaphylaxis\n- initial reaction requiring more than one dose of adrenaline\n- delay in adrenaline administration 30 — 60 min from symptom onset).\n- patients 