n with glucose IV infusion\n- **B)** Potassium sparing diuretics\n- **C)** IV Ca gluconate   D\n- Magnesium Sulphate\n- **E)** IV Calcium Chloride Explanation\n- Hyperkalemia management 25 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 25:\nA 45 year old man is brought to the ED following an RTC in wh'ch he was the driver in a rear end shunt. He is reporting pain in his neck. He has no neurological symptoms. Which of the following would indicate a need for cervical spine imaging based on nexus spine criteria?\n- **A)** Immediate onset of pain\n- **B)** Patient has not mobilised since the incident\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **C)** Midline tenderness\n- **D)** Inability to rotate the neck in a 90 degree arc\n- **E)** patient immobilised in collar and blocks\n- Explanation; Midline tenderness after a cervical spine injury is a significant clinical finding, as outlined by the NEXUS criteria. The presence of midline tenderness raises the concern for a potential cervical spine injury, making it an indication for CT in adult and MRI below 16 for an evaluation to rule out fractures or other structural damage.\n- NEXUS Cervical Spine Rule\n- The Canadian C-Spine Rule (CCR) in : Th in (CCR)\n- For patients at high risk, a CT scan should be performed in adults, and an MAI should be performed in those under 16 years of age.\n- If a patient has any one low-risk factor, proceed by asking them to rotate their neck 4S degrees On each side. If the patient cannot rotate their neck,\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC then a CT scan (for adults) or MRI (for those under 16) should be performed. If the patient can rotate their neck, no radiography is needed.\n- If none of the low-risk factors are present which are - e.g., absence of midline tenderness, delay onset of pain, ambulatory at any time, sitting position in the ED, simple rear MVC, then a CT scan for adults and an MRI for those under 16 should be performed.\n- please note - that we use both criteria in the Emergency Department (ED).\n- Question last revised 01/25. 26 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 26:\nWh'ch of following is not a sign of upper airway obstruction ?\n- **A)** Snoring\n- **B)** Grunting\n- **C)** Expiratory wheeze\n- **D)** Hoarseness\n- **E)** Stridor Explanation\n- Expiratory wheeze caused by lower airway obstruction. Features/ sign of airway obstruction caused by obstruction of upper airways caused by Obstruction Of upper airway 27 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 27:\nA 55-year-old female with history of fall brought to the ED with complaint of severe right hip pain. On examination, right limb is shortened and internally rotated. His X-ray is given. What complication most likely patient develop in this injury?\n- **A)** Obturator nerve injury\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **B)** Femoral artery injury\n- **C)** Femoral nerve injury - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **D)** Common peroneal nerve injury\n- **E)** Sciatic nerve injury Explanation\n- X-ray shows posterior hip dislocation\n- sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the femoral head and secondary OA\n- Posterior hip dislocation\n- posterior hip dislocations results from a posterior force applied to a flexed knee, most commonly a dashboard injury.\n- These injuries are associated with acetabular, femoral neck, and femoral shaft fractures\n- Clinical features: limb will be shortened and internally rotated, with the hip flexed and adducted.\n- Check for sciatic nerve damage: examine foot dorsiflexion and below-knee sensation Diagnosis; Order AP or lateral view radiograph Complications: Sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the femoral head and secondary OA 28 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 28:\nA man who drinks alcohol excessively is brought into the ED by paramedics having been found unwell He is agitated and tremulous and has marked ataxia. His observations are: HR: RR: Sp02: 97bpm  mmHg 22 breath per minute 96% room air His capillary blood sugar is 3.5mmoVL. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?\n- **A)** IV thiamine\n- **B)** Oral benzodiazipines\n- **D)** IV crystalloid bolus httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **E)** 1M haloperidol Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Patient clinical features suggests Wernicke's encephalopathy NICE guideline recommends - parenteral thiamine to people with suspected Wernicke's encephalopathy. Parenteral treatment should be given for a minimum of 5 days, unless Wernicke's encephalopathy is excluded. Oral thiamine treatment should follow parenteral therapy.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 29 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 29:\nA 62-year-old male presented to the ED with violent and aggressive behavior towards staff and patients. You've decided rapid tranquillization on this Patient According to NICE guideline, which of following is the drug of choice in this Patient?\n- **A)** IV diazepam   B\n- IV midazolam\n- **C)** 1M phenytoin D\n- IV haloperidole   E, 1M lorazepam\n- Choice of drugs_for rapid tranquilisation / Sedation Acute behavioural disturbance\n- Acute behavioural disturbance (also previously called excited delirium, acute behavioural disorder, or agitated delirium) is an umbrella term used to describe a presentation which may include abnormal physiology and/or behaviour. Substance intoxication (Cocaine, Methamphetamine , cocaine with recent binge use etc)\n- Mental health conditions\n- Substance withdrawal\n- Serotonin syndrome\n- Hypoxia\n- Hypoglycaemia\n- Electrolyte disturbance\n- Other conditions —Sepsis, Head injury ,Anticholinergic syndrome, Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, Thyroid storm, Heat stroke, Seizures.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC intoxication and Mental health conditions are the most common factors in ABD presentations Agitation\n- Constant physical activity\n- Bizarre behaviour (incl. paranoia, hypervigilance)\n- Fear, panic\n- Unusual or unexpected strength\n- Sustained non-compliance with police or ambulance staff\n- Pain tolerance, impervious to pain\n- Hot to touch, sweating\n- Rapid breathing\n- Tachycardia\n- Assess-and-manage-the-risk\n- Managingxi5k-tQ-thepa.tient=Patients experiencing ABD may lose their ability to interact with their environment safely, and their perception of risk may appear non-existent. they are likely to react in such a way as to put themselves at risk, especially while attempting to escape a perceived risk, the perception of which may be increased if restraint is applied. Patients presenting with severe ABD are likely to lack mental capacity (which should be formally assessed and documented) to make treatment decisions and may require emergency treatment under the appropriate legislation.\n- Verbal de-escalation should always be attempted. If safe and available, friends and family may be able to assist. Advice on verbal de-escalation is found in Section 3.\n- who have not had approved training should not be asked to restrain patients. Early escalation to on-site security services is recommended. Hospitals should have sufficient trained security staff available to be able to safely restrain a patient, to keep them from harm and to protect others.\n- Do not attempt to restrain patients with insufficient numbers of staff. There are significant risks to staff when attempting to restrain patients, and these are compounded by insufficient team numbers. RestraineAttempts should be made to remove any ongoing restraint at the earliest opportunity. There is concern that in ABD, continued exertion under restraint can contribute to poor outcomes (likely\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC due to increasing catecholamine levels, worsening hyperthermia, and metabolic acidosis).\n- Prolonged restraint should prompt consideration of rapid tranquilisation.\n- While the police should not normally be called to undertake restrictive practices in healthcare settings solely to facilitate clinical interventions, it has been established that there are scenarios in which police support should be requested:\n- if healthcare staff have been injured\n- if appropriate support is not available from healthcare colleagues in a sufficiently timely manner to ensure the safety of all those affected\n- where there is a risk Of serious injury or darnage, and safety is compromised.\n- De-escalation - Verbal de-escalation is a valuable tool with which to facilitate patient care and potentially avoid any requirement for restraint.\n- Offer oral sedative - lorazepam 1-2mg (max 4mg per day).\n- Features of ideal environment for verbal and environmental de-escalation:\n- Dedicated to management of patients with severe agitation (or if not possible, a space that can be created or adapted quickly)\n- Adequate and appropriately located exits so that staff can exit without being trapped by the patient\n- Doors which open outwards\n- Quiet, low stimulus\n- Not too warm\n- Absence of equipment/furniture and moveable objects that could be a potential weapon or used to barricade an exit\n- Absence of potential ligature points\n- Constantly observable\n- Staff able to signal need for additional support easily Rapid tranquilisation in ABD is important to prevent further sympathetic over-stimulation and excessive muscular activity from causing a metabolic storm and subsequent cardiovascular collapse.\n- Rapid tranquilisation can also prevent the patient from causing physical harm to themselves or others and facilitate investigations and treatments.\n- The use of rapid tranquilisation for more severe ABD presentations is associated with reduced mortality.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Parenteral rapid tranquilisation is more likely to be required if patients:\n- lack capacity to refuse treatment and are non- compliant, and\n- pose a danger to themselves or others, or have a clear need for further treatment/investigation\n- Choice-Qf-drugs.fqr rapid-granquilisa!ion-l-sedation\n- Once sedative taken effect:\n- Establish and secure intravenous access\n- Monitor in a high dependency area (Resus Room)\n- Maintain sedation with IV bolus medication until definitive management plan arranged\n- Correct electrolyte / glucose / acid-base abnormalities, correct hyperthermia, look for evidence of DIC or rhabdomyolysis.\n- Institute other emergency treatments and investigations as necessary.\n- Document clearly in the clinical notes;\n- Indication(s) for RT & any attempts at de-escalation\n- Mental capacity assessment\n- Restraint, Collateral history, Features consistent with ABD, Police involvement. 32 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 32:\nA 16 year-old child brought into the ED with complain of right ankle pain with restricted range of motion. His X-ray is shown. What is the appropriate step of management in this patent?\n- **A)** Ice packing\n- **B)** Backslab and follow up\n- **C)** Urgent referral to orthopedics for ORIF\n- **D)** Immediate reduction\n- **E)** Discharge after pain relief\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Displaced-fractur-es-ofthe medial or lateral malleolus and Bimalleolar or trimalleolar fractures are unstable. Having attempted to reduce any significant talar shift (with appropriate sedation), place in a BKPOP, obtain fresh X-rays, and refer to the orthopaedic team.\n- Ankle fracture\n- Radiographically, ankle fractures are described as unimalleolar, bimalleolar and trimalleolar.\n- A bimalleolar fracture is fracture of the lateral and medial malleoli.\n- A trimalleolar fracture additionally involves the posterior malleolus\n- The ankle mortice joint allows very little rotation or angulation at the ankle joint, so forced twisting or angulation of the ankle joint causes fractures,ligament injuries.it also disrupt the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis.\n- with rest, elevation, analgesia and early mobilization.\n- initial immobilization in a boot ± crutches and orthopaedic follow-up\n- Undisplaceg_isolated medial or lateral malleolar fractures are usually stable. provide analgesia and crutches, and immobilize in a well-padded BKPOP cast. Advise limb elevation, and arrange orthopaedic follow-up.\n- medial or lateral malleolus and Bimalleolar Or trimalleolar fractures are unstable. Having attempted to reduce any significant talar shift (with appropriate sedation), place in a BKPOP, obtain fresh X-rays, and refer to the orthopaedic team. 33 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 33:\nA 36-year-old male brought to the ED with an overdose of opioid. on examination, his BP is  mmHg , RR 11 breath per minutes, and He has pinpoint pupil. Following 800mcg IV naloxone patient condition has been improved, however, patient condition has been deteriorated after a short period of time and his GCS is 6 now. Which of following is your next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Administer flumazenil\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **B)** Cet a surgical airway - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **C)** Apply face mask oxygen\n- **D)** Intubate the patient\n- **E)** Set a naloxone infusion Explanation\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma.\n- When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion adjusted according to vital signs.\n- Clinical features of opioid overdose\n- Respiratory depression\n- pinpoint pupil\n- Coma\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma.\n- When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion adjusted according to vital signs. 34 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 34:\nA 12-year-old girl brought to the ED after falling on outstretched hand