e isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -1 Omcg).\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 - 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECG carefully for the presence of p waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing.\n- If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment. 37 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 37:\nA GO-year-old man brought to the ED with the complaint of dizziness and an episode of syncope. His heart rate was 43bpm and BP  mmHg was recorded. He admitted beta blocker for his heart condition. Which of the following is most appropriate next step of management for this patient?\n- **A)** External pacing\n- **B)** Atropine 3mg\n- **C)** Give Img glucagon\n- **D)** Set up an adrenaline infusion at 2-10 mcg/min\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **E)** Atropine 500mcg\n- Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- IV atropine remains the first choice if patient presents with adverse features. Intravenous glucagon [unlicensed] is a treatment option for severe hypotension, heart failure, or cardiogenic shock in beta blocker overdose.\n- eradygard.im(Resus-goungiLQf-the-UK-and European-resuscitation-gounGiLguideline)\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock, Syncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n- If treatment with atropine is ineffective\n- consider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 - 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECC carefully for the presence of p waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing. If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment. 38 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 38:\nA 40-year-old woman is brought to the ED after sustaining the injury to her right ankle, as shown, after falling down the stairs. Sensation to her foot is intact and she has a palpable dorsalis pedis pulse. She has no other injuries on examination. What is the most appropriate management of this patient?\n- **A)** Procedural sedation and reduction of the ankle httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** Reduce the ankle immediately with nitrous oxide\n- **C)** Organize an urgent x-ray Of her ankle joint\n- **D)** Refer to orthopedics for review\n- **E)** Procedural sedation and reduction after a CT scan\n- Explanation\n- Ankle dislocation - if vascular compromise is present, as evidenced by a dusky foot or absent pu