edgroupzanybody in this grp will be excluded from organ donation\n- Those under the age of 18 people who lack the mental capacity to understand the new arrangements and take the necessary action\n- Visitors to England, and those not living here voluntarily People who have lived in England for less than 12 months before their death 49 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 49:\nA 24 year old female with history of fall brought into the ED by his father with complain of pain in the elbow. Her X-ray is shown.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Olecranon fracture\n- **B)** Supracondylar fracture\n- **C)** Humeral shaft fracture\n- **D)** Radioulnar dislocation\n- **E)** Radial head fracture Explanation Suspect supracondylar fracture in children age 3-10 years, if X-ray shows Fat-pad sign or fracture.\n- Suspect radial head fracture in adults, if X-ray shows Fat-pad sign or fracture\n- Radial head/neck fracture\n- Radial head fractures are the most common fractures of the elbow. They result from a fall on an outstretched hand.\n- A specific associated injury, the le5i_Qn, occurs when there is disruption of the triangular fibrocartilage complex of the wrist and the interosseous membrane between the radius and ulna, causing dissociation of the distal radioulnar joint.\n- Pain the lateral elbow especially with pronation and supination of the arm\n- there may be swelling laterally and tenderness with palpation of the radial head.\n- Piagnpsis\n- Joint effusion (Fat pad sign) on x-ray\n- abnormal displacement of the radiocapitellar line away from the center of the capitellum\n- Nondisplaced fracture manage with sling immobilization, ice, elevation, analgesics, and referral to an orthopedic (fracture clinic) within week.\n- displaced fractures or those with restricted range of motion need surgical repair. Therefore, orthopedic referral should be made within 24 hours.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC so / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 50:\nA 46-year-old male brought to the ED by paramedics, with a history of fall near pedestrian crossing.. His observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 104bpm His lab is shown. what is our management in this patient?\n- **A)** IV Desmopressin B\n- 1M Adrenaline 0.5mg\n- **C)** Phenoxybenzamine IV hydrocortisone\n- **E)** Fludrocortisone\n- patient clinical features suggests adrenal crisis\n- be given to patients with a suspected but unconfirmed diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency.\n- should be administered to correct hypotension and dehydration. It is usually necessary to administer 1 L rapidly, and a further 4 to 6 L over the first 24 hours, to correct hypotension. should be administered when necessary to correct hypoglycaemia, but care should be taken to avoid worsening hyponatraemia Addison's disease\n- Primary-adrenal-insufficiency-=is caused by destruction of the adrenal cortex.\n- Causes—reduced production of glucocorticoids (such as cortisol), mineralocorticoids (such as aldosterone), and adrenal androgens (such as dehydroepiandrosterone).\n- The absence of cortisol leads to increased production of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) because negative feedback to the pituitary gland is reduced. Secondary-adrenal-insufficiency-zoccurs when pituitary ACTH production is insufficient; this leads to reduced cortisol production and adrenal atrophy.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC include intracranial disorders, such as pituitary tumours, subarachnoid haemorrhage, and traumatic brain injury. Tertiary-adrenal-insufficiengy=occurs when hypothalamic production of corticotropin-releasing hormone is disrupted and production of ACTH from the anterior pituitary is insufficient. - long-term administration of corticosteroids and less commonly tumours, radiotherapy, or surgery affecting the hypothalamus.\n- Causes\n- Auto-immune adrenalitis (most common) Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in children) and other genetic causes such as adrenoleukodystrophy.\n- Infections, such as tuberculosis, meningococcus, haemopholus influenzae, cryptococcosis, cytomegalovirus, and HIV.\n- Adrenal metastases.\n- Amyloidosis.\n- Haemochromatosis.\n- Iatrogenic causes such as bilateral adrenalectomy, adrenal haemorrhage (for example from anticoagulant treatment), and use of immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies in the treatment of cancer.\n- Clinical features\n- A person with Addison's disease may present with a sudden crisis precipitated by intercurrent infection or stress.\n- Features of Addison's disease include:\n- Weakness and lethargy\n- Hypotension (notably orthostatic hypotension) Nausea and vomiting\n- Weight loss\n- Reduced axillary and pubic hair Depression Hyperpigmentation (palmar creases, buccal mucosa and exposed areas more commonly affected)\n- The classical biochemical features of Addison's disease are as follows:\n- Increased ACTH levels (rise in an attempt to stimulate the adrenal glands) Hyponatraemia Hyperkalaemia Hypercalcaemia\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper\n- Hypoglycaemia\n- Metabolic acidosis - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Addison's disease is associated With an increased incidence of the following conditions:\n- Type I diabetes mellitus (not type II)\n- Hashimoto's thyroiditis\n- Crave's disease\n- premature ovarian failure Pernicious anaemia\n- Vitiligo\n- Alopecia\n- Investigations\n- Serum cortisol level - ideally be obtained at 8—9 am\n- Less than 100 nanomol/l_ — admit the person to hospital, adrenal insufficiency is highly likely.\n- Blood glucose\n- plasma adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) (Synacthen ) test— to confirm the diagnosis.\n- Plasma renin and aldosterone levels — renin levels are typically high and aldosterone levels low in Addison's disease. In secondary adrenal insufficiency, the renin-angiotensin system can function normally.\n- Serum DHEA-S — typically low in Addison's disease.\n- Thyroid function tests.\n- Autoantibody levels — adrenal cortex autoantibodies or antibodies against 21-hydroxylase are present in more than of people with recent- onset autoimmune adrenalitis. Management / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 51:\nA 3-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complaint of fever, bilateral conjunctival injection. On further assessment, he has unilateral cervical lymphadenopathy. you suspect Kawasaki disease.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Which of the following is the earliest abnormality in this patient?\n- **A)** Leukopenia\n- **B)** Anemia\n- **C)** Coronary aneurysm\n- **D)** Thrombocytosis\n- **E)** Increased S.albumin Explanatign\n- In the acute stage (early stage) , a mild to moderate normochromic anaemia is observed, along with a moderate to alarmingly raised white blood cell count (leukocytosis) with a left shift.\n- During the subacute stage, platelet count elevation (thrombocytosis) is the outstanding marker. It begins to rise in the second week and continues to rise during the third\n- Kawasaki disease\n- It's an acute, febrile, self-limiting, systemic vasculitis of unknown origin that almost exclusively affects young children.\n- Asian ancestry\n- age 3 months to 4 years\n- male sex\n- Diagnostic-features\n- Fever for more than S days at least 4 of the following features polymorphic rash\n- Bilateral conjunctival injection\n- mucosal erythema with strawberry tongue.\n- Swelling and erythema of the hands and feet occur in the acute stage, followed by desquamation in the second week.\n- Unilateral non-purulent cervical lymphadenopathy is present in about 40% of cases.\n- Coronary aneurysms develop in 20% to of untreated patients.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Haemoglobin low for age (Mild-moderate normocytic anemia present in acute phase)\n- (leukocytosis in acute phase)\n- Platelet > 450,000/mm ( thrombocytosis present in subacute phase)\n- Serum albumin<3.O g/dl\n- serum erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)\n- serum CRP\n- LFTs (Elevated alanine aminotransferase)\n- ECC\n- Echocardiogram( perform during and after acute illness and if echo reveal cardiac abnormality then refer child to paediatrician for further cardiac work- up\n- intravenous immunoglobulin and/or aspirin. In resistant cases, corticosteroids or a tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha inhibitor may be indicated. 52 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 52:\nA 13-year-old male is brought 'nto the ED with altered mental status after being found in a house fire. The fire department found burned couches in the house with melted plastic covering. The patient has been mildly responsive en route to the ED. His observations are: RR: 78,'40rnrnHg 155bpm 99.9F oral 40 breath per minute spoz 92% on 15L NRB He is somnolent but withdraws to pain. He has soot in his nares with carbonaceous sputum, and auscultation reveals rhonchorous and wheezing breath sounds bilaterally. Point of care VBG demonstrates a lactate of\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 16:\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis? A\n- Cyanide poisoning B\n- Type I respiratory failure c. Methemoglobinemia\n- **D)** CO poisoning\n- **E)** Neuroleptic malignant Syndrorne https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Explanation Patient clinical features suggests cyanide poisoning as he is hypotensive and lactate is 16. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper\n- Key_points - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC CO poisoning can be diagnosed with detection of carboxyhemoglobin levels in blood\n- Normal carboxyhaemoglobin levels are: Less than 1—3% for non-smokers. up to in women who are pregnant, or people with anaemia.\n- upto in smokers, and up to in heavy smokers.\n- Carboxyhemoglobin level and significant neurologic dysfunction (seizure or obtundation) suggests severe poisoning of CO.\n- CN toxicity remains a clinical diagnosis and there is no