ld be given ÄefQteany diagnostic studies (X-rays, CT scans etc.) are performed to evaluate a suspected bleeding problem, especially in the case of head trauma or suspected intracranial hemorrhage.\n- For routine joint bleeding, neradiographic studies are indicated.\n- For patients with hemophilia who have illnesses or disorders that necessitate an invasive procedure (lumbar puncture, arterial blood gas, arthrocentesis, etc.) or surgery, factor replacement therapy or bypass therapy to 100% must be administered in the emergency department planned procedure or surgery. In this situation, consultation with a hematologist is strongly recommended.\n- For an individual with known hemophilia, routine laboratory studies (PT, aPTT, factor levels), are not indicated in the treatment of a routine bleeding episode unless requested by the patient's hematologist.\n- Treatment should not be delayed waiting for test results which may take several hours.\n- Suspected bleeding into a joint or muscle.\n- Any significant injury to the head, neck, mouth or eyes or evidence of bleeding in these areas. Any new or unusual headache, particularly one following trauma.\n- Severe pain or swelling at any site.\n- All open wounds requiring surgical closure, wound adhesive, or steri-strips.\n- History of an accident or trauma that might result in internal bleeding.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Any invasive procedure or surgery. Heavy or persistent bleeding from any site.\n- Castrointestinal bleeding leading to moderate to severe anemia.\n- Acute fractures, dislocations and sprains. Heavy menstrual bleeding leading to moderate to severe anemia or volume instability\n- Management 54 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 54:\nYou have assessed a frail 80 year old woman with end- stage Lewy Body Dementia who is unable to communicate and has been driven to the ED by her husband after a choking episode. Due to the long wait, her husband tells you he has decided to take his wife home without being seen by a doctor. The following day she is brought to the department after a cardiac arrest and is dead on arrival. You have been asked to complete the death certificate by the bereavement team. What is the most appropriate course of action?\n- **A)** Obtain more details from the husband before completing the death certificate\n- **B)** Report the death to the Coroner or procurator Fiscal for investigation\n- **C)** Complete the death certificate citing Lewy Body Dementia as the primary cause of death\n- **D)** Ask the doctor who verified death in the resuscitation room to complete the certificate\n- **E)** Contact the patient's CP to ask them if they can complete a certificate Explanation\n- Repgr_ti_ EiscaJ) h p\n- Many deaths that occur in (or in transit to) the ED are sudden and unexpected, and/or follow trauma.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC The exact cause of death is seldom immediately apparent. Accordingly, do not be tempted to sign death certificates. Instead, report all deaths to the Coroner (the Procurator Fiscal in Scotland). 55 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 55:\nA 16-year-old male presented to the ED with facial trauma. He has avulsed two upper central incisors while playing football, which he has collected and brought them to the ED What would be your appropriate step of management?\n- **A)** Put in the milk B\n- Put in saline and replant the teeth again\n- **C)** Reassure and discharge\n- **D)** Get a dental x-ray\n- **E)** Refer patient to maxillofacial surgery Explanation\n- Patient permanent teeth are avulsed, they should be replanted immediately. 56 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 56:\nA 9-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complain of hip pain and unable to bear weight. His X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Polymyositis\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **B)** Transient Synovitis - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **C)** Slipped upper femoral epiphysis\n- **D)** Soft tissue injury E\n- 