gh fifth metatarsals is seen. 5B / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 58:\nA 9-year-old child presented to the ED with facial trauma. He has avulsed two upper central incisors while playing football, which has been collected by his father. What would be the initial step of management?\n- **A)** Irrigate with NS and replant the tooth again\n- **B)** Get dental x-ray Put in the milk\n- **D)** Put in the hypertonic saline\n- **E)** Refer patient to maxillofacial surgery Explanatign\n- Hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth.\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- Key-points\n- Central incisors lost at 6-7 years of age.\n- Between the ages of about 6 and 7 years, the primary teeth start to shed and the permanent teeth begin to come through.\n- Initial management of primary and permanent teeth is the same. However, If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldn't be re-implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 59 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 59:\nA two year old boy is brought into the ED by his mother with a two day history of barking cough and shortness of breath, which is worse at night. He has mild fever. In the ED, he has audible stridor and has a hoarse voice. Which is the most likely causative organism?\n- **A)** Respiratory syncytial Virus\n- **B)** Adenovirus\n- **C)** Haemophilus Influenza type B D\n- Parvovirus 819\n- **E)** Parainfluenza virus Explanation\n- Cause of croup\n- parainfluenza virus types or 3\n- Clinical features\n- Sudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which associated with :\n- Hoarseness\n- Stridor\n- Respiratory distress.\n- Fever\n- Symptoms are typically worse at night and increase with agitation. 60 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 60:\nA 10-year-old girl presented to the ED with complaint of pain in her elbow after a 