nt\n- **C)** Complete an adult With incapacity form and immediately thrombolyse the patient\n- **D)** Get consent from her husband to immediately thrombolyse the patient\n- **E)** Refer the patient to the mental health team for an urgent assessment Explanatien\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest\n- Mental Capacity Act (MCA)\n- The Mental Capacity Act (MCA) is designed to protect and empower people who may lack the mental capacity to make their own decisions about their care and treatment. It applies to people aged 16 and over.\n- Examples of people who may lack capacity include those with:\n- dementia\n- a severe learning disability\n- a brain injury\n- a mental health illness https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- a stroke\n- unconsciousness caused by an anaesthetic or sudden accident\n- however, just because a person has one of these health conditions does not necessarily mean they lack the capacity to make a specific decision\n- assume a person has the capacity to make a decision themselves, unless it's proved otherwise\n- wherever possible, help people to make their own decisions\n- do not treat a person as lacking the capacity to make a decision just because they make an unwise decision\n- ifyou make a decision for someone who does not have capacity, it must be in their best interests\n- treatment and care provided to someone who lacks capacity should be the least restrictive of their basic rights and freedoms\n- The MCA says a person is unable to make. a decision if they cannot;\n- understand the information relevant to the decision\n- retain that information\n- use or weigh up that information as part of the process of making the decision\n- to communicate his decision (whether by talking, using sign language or another means).\n- Key_pgints;\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest. If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then he/she shouldn't be treated without consents. 65 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 65:\nA 2 year old girl is brought to the ED in cardiac arrest. Resuscitation ongoing. With regards to the endotracheal tube, what is the correct length at the incisors recommended by ALSG?\n- **A)** 12cm\n- **B)** 14cm\n- **C)** 13cm https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **D)** 12.scm\n- **E)** Ilcm Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC tube recommendations:\n- Internal diameter (mm) = (Age/4) + 4\n- Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 12 for an oral tube\n- Length (cm) = (Age/2) + IS for nasal tube\n- Answer -  + 12 = 13cm 66 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 66:\nA 12-year-old boy brought to the ED with anaphylactic shock. Patient has given two doses of adrenaline. What is the meaning of biphasic shock?\n- **A)** Refractory shock and another protocol of management\n- **B)** Adrenaline more than one time\n- **C)** NO Improvement after adrenaline\n- **D)** Need intensive care admission\n- **E)** Relapse Of shock after respond\n- Biphasic reaction\n- Anaphylaxis can appear to resolve but then cause a recurrence of symptoms several hours later in the absence of further allergen exposure. This is known as a biphasic reaction and occurs in around 5% of patients.\n- Risk-factors-for-biphasic-reactions\n- more severe initial presentation of anaphylaxis\n- initial reaction requiring more than one dose of adrenaline\n- delay in adrenaline administration 30 — 60 min from symptom onset) https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 67 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 67:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED with 15 hours history of painful swelling of his left leg. On examination, you note his Calf muscle is red, hot and tender. His observations are: Heart rate 90bpm, BP 129