r antagonists (prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin, tamsulosin),\n- Anti-anxiety agents (hydroxyzine),\n- Anticoagulants (heparin, warfarin),\n- Antidepressants and antipsychotics (trazodone, bupropion, fluoxetine, sertraline, lithium, clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine, chlorpromazine, thioridazine), and antihypertensives (hydralazine, guanethidine, propranolol) 72 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 72:\nA 67-year-old female brought to the ED by ambulance with complaint of dysuria. She gives history of taking antibiotics from several days and you suspect UT'. On your review in the ED she looks profound anxious and lethargic. Her observations are: T 38.90C BP 80/45mmHg pulse 120bpm His haematology lab is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura\n- **B)** Acute myeloid leukaemia\n- **C)** Drug induced thrombocytopenia\n- **D)** Idiopathic thrombocytopenia\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Disseminated intravascular coagulation Explanation\n- Patient clinical picture suggests DIC\n- Investigations of DIC\n- platelet count — decrease\n- prothrombin time (PT) — prolong\n- activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) - prolong\n- Fibrinogen — Decrease\n- D-dimer/fibrin degradation products — elevated\n- thrombin time - prolong\n- ITP vs TTP vs DIC 73 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 73:\nA 70-year-old known alcoholic man, is brought to the ED after a massive GI bleed. A significant upper GI bleed transfusion protocol is triggered\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Which of these is a complication specifically of massive transfusion? A\n- Thrombotic events\n- **B)** Metabolic acidosis   C Hypocalcemia\n- **D)** ABO incompatibility\n- **E)** Blood borne virus transmission Explanatign Complications of massive transfusion Hypothermia - is a complication of massive transfusion. The rapid infusion of cold blood products can lower body temperatures. The use of warmers can reduce this risk.\n- Hyperkalemia - is a complication of massive transfusion. Stored blood products can accumulate higher concentrations of potassium. Hypokalemia cannot be attributed to massive transfusion.\n- Hypocalcemia and metabolic alkalosis - can be seen during or following massive transfusion due to sodium citrate that is added to blood for storage. Hypercalcemia is not associated with massive transfusion.\n- Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) and transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO) can occur with massive transfusion as well.\n- Note - there is controversy as some candidates didn't get the score for hypocalcaemia in Feb 2022 MRCEM SBA. however, in September 2022 diet everyone has got the mark for hypocalcaemia 74 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 74:\nA 62-year-old man brought to the ED with breathlessness. He has history of diabetes and hypertension but has had no medical admission. On examination, he is pale, sweaty and clammy. His observations are: H R 102bpm BP  mmHg RR 30 breath/m\n- n sp02 Temp 36.60c His CXR is show. What is the most likely diagnosis? httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **A)** Cardiomyopathy - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** Pulmonary Oedema C\n- Viral pneumonitis\n- **D)** Community acquired pneumonia\n- **E)** pericardial effusion Explanation\n- Patient X-ray shows:\n- Upper lobe diversion\n- Alveolar edema\n- pleural effusion\n- Cardiomegaly\n- Kerely a lines ( basal septal line)\n- Which are cornmon features on cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. 75 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 75:\nA 59 years old male with multinodular goiter presented to the ED with palpitations, sweating and tremors. His observations are: Pulse is 240bpm BP iS  mmHg What is the initial management?\n- **A)** Verapamil\n- **B)** Amiodarone\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **C)** Lugol Iodine D, Propranolol\n- **E)** Digoxin Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- to reduce heart rate and block effects of thyroid hormones.\n- Hyperthyroidism\n- It is a biochernical diagnosis which occurs when there is pathologically increased thyroid hormone production and secretion by the thyroid gland.\n- Thyrotoxicosis is the clinical manifestation of excess circulating thyroid hormones due to any cause, including hyperthyroidism.\n- primary hyperthyroidism - occurs when thyrotoxicosis is caused by an abnormality of the thyroid gland such as Graves' disease.\n- Overt primary hyperthyroidism - is diagnosed when thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is suppressed below the normal reference range, and free thyroxine (FT4) and/or free triiodothyronine (FT3) concentrations are above the normal reference range.\n- Subclinical hyperthyroidism - is diagnosed when TSH is suppressed below the normal reference range, but FT4 and FT3 concentrations are within the normal reference range.\n- Thyrotoxicosis without hyperthyroidism - describes thyrotoxicosis without thyroid gland overactivity, which is usually transient.\n- Risk factors\n- Female sex\n- Family history\n- Smoking - current smokers have a three-fold increased risk of developing Craves' orbitopathy compared with non-smokers.\n- Low iodine intake\n- Autoimmune disease\n- Causes\n- Graves' disease (most common) - It is a systemic autoimmune disorder mediated by thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)-receptor antibodies (TRAbs) that stimulate the TSH receptor, leading to thyroid hyperplasia and unregulated\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC excessive production and secretion of thyroid hormone.\n- Peak incidence is at 30—50 years of age\n- Toxic multinodular goitre - This is the second most common cause of hyperthyroidism in the I-JK. people over 60 years of age, and people living in iodine-deficient areas, such as Denmark.\n- Toxic thyroid nodule (adenoma) - This accounts for up to 5% of cases of hyperthyroidism, and is more common in older adults.\n- TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma - This is a rare tumour secreting large quantities of TSH\n- Iodine - Iodine may suppress TSH levels and is found in over-the-counter preparations, such as kelp supplements\n- Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis (AIT type 1) — if a person with underlying thyroid disease is exposed to the high iodine content of amiodarone, this may lead to excess thyroid hormone synthesis and release.\n- pituitary thyroid hormone resistance syndrome - This is a rare condition caused by genetic mutations in the thyroid hormone receptor-beta gene.\n- Gestational thyrotoxicosis — this is a benign and transient disorder typically seen in the first trimester of pregnancy, caused by the stimulatory action of placental beta-Hcg.\n- Clinical features\n- Heat intolerance\n- Sweating\n- Weight loss\n- palpitations - Including atrial fibrillation or other supraventricular tachycardias, especially in older people\n- Tremor Diffuse goitre\n- Orbitopathy\n- Exercise intolerance, fatigue, muscle weakness Polyuria, thirst, generalized itch. Reduced libido, gynaecomastia in men\n- Specific features Rapid-onset malaise, fever, and thyroid pain (may suggest subacute thyroiditis). Compression symptoms of breathlessness, hoarse voice, dysphagia, neck pressure (may be caused by a toxic multinodular goitre).\n- Specific-sign\n- Craves' disease - the thyroid gland is usually diffusely symmetrically enlarged without nodules, httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC and there may be a bruit.\n- Toxic multinodular goitre - typically presents with non-tender thyroid nodules.\n- Toxic adenoma - typically presents with a unilateral, non-tender thyroid mass.\n- Subacute thyroiditis -typically presents with a tender, firm, irregular, diffusely enlarged thyroid gland which may be asymmetrical.\n- Amiodarone-induced - thyroiditis, a small goitre is usually present. are clinical features of:\n- Eye irritation, photophobia, or excessive watering of the eyes.\n- Redness of the eyes or eyelids and/or lid swelling.\n- Change in the appearance of the eye or eyelids: Eyelid retraction (sclera is visible above the superior corneal limbus).\n- Lid lag (delay in moving the eyelid as the eye moves downward).\n- proptosis (exophthalmos, eyeball protrusion, an inability to fully close the eyes as the upper and lower lids do not fully appose).\n- Investigations\n- Thyroid function test — TSH level and serum free or total T3 or T4 level\n- TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb) Thyroid ultrasound scan with doppler\n- Measurement of 24-hour urinary iodine excretion may help to confirm suspected excessive iodine intake.\n- Thyroid peroxidase antibodies (TPOAb)\n- Consider CT or MRI scan of orbit\n- Management of thyrggexjggsE(NlCE guideline)\n- Admit-the-patient-if there are symptoms suggesting a serious complication, such as thyrotoxic crisis\n- follow caner referral pathway for thyroid cancer.\n- Other-a" patients\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 76 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 76:\nA 14 year old boy presents with his parents with self- harm to the left thigh. This is his first presentation and he explains he feels much better and it is related to bullying in school. He has no suicidal ideation and is looking forward to a family holiday in the coming weeks. On examination, he has superficial incised wounds which are cleaned and closed and multiple old scars on both thigh. What is the most appropriate action?\n- **A)** Advise his parents to arrange a CP appointment\n- **B)** Discharge home in the care of his parents\n- **C)** Admit under psychiatry for further assessment\n- **D)** Admit to the paediatric team for further investigation\n- **E)** Discharge home With an urgent referral to CAMHS Explanation\n- NICE guideline - When a person attends the emergency department or minor injury unit following an episode of self-harm, offer referral to age-appropriate liaison psychiatry services, or for children and young people, crisis response service (or an equivalent specialist mental health service or a suitably skilled mental health professional) as soon as possible after arrival, for a psychosocial assessment.\n- If the person is aged under 18 years, refer them to Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC (CAMHS).\n- If the person is aged 18-65 years old refer them to liaison psychiatrist/mental health service\n- Early referral should be considered ifthere is evidence of depression and/or self-harm Self harm Self-harm refers to an intentional act of self- poisoning or self-injury, irrespective of the motivation or apparent purpose of the act, and is an expression of emotional distress.\n- The majority of self-poisoning episodes involve prescribed or over-the-counter medication, and a minority involve illicit drugs, other household substances, or plant material.\n- The majority of self-injury episodes involve cutting.\n- Less common methods include burning, hanging, stabbing, drowning, swallowing objects, insertion, shooting, and jumping from heights or in front of vehicles.\n- Risk factors\n- Age— self-harm rates peak in 16 to 24-y\n- ear-old women and 2S to 34-year-old men. Suicide rates are highest in both men and hM3rnen aged 45—49 years. Bullying, suffering abuse , exam stress or pressure\n- a sudden change in your life, like a death, divorce or moving school\n- Socio-economic disadvantage.\n- Social isolation.\n- Stressful life events, for example relationship difficulties, previous experience in the armed forces, child maltreatment, or domestic violence.\n- Bereavement by suicide.\n- Mental health problems, such as depression, psychosis or schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, post- traumatic stress disorder, or a personality disorder.\n- Chronic physical health problems.\n- Alcohol and/or drug misuse.\n- Involvement with the criminal justice system (with people in prison being at particular risk). Features in children or in young people Self-harm is a way to show the feelings patient have inside on the outside. It might cause the patient to experience:\n- pain or discomfort\n- temporary physical marks\n- scarrlng feelings of sickness or dizziness\n- feelings of weakness, shame or disgust\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- feeling scared, out of control or confused by why you are doing this\n- feelings of isolation and loneliness\n- Assessment and care by non-mental health emergency department professionals ( NICE guideline)\n- When a person attends the emergency department or minor injury unit following an episode of self- harm, emergency department staff responsible for initial assessment or triage should establish the following as soon as possible:\n- the severity of the injury and how urgently physical treatment is needed\n- the person's emotional and mental state, and level of distress\n- whether there is immediate concern about the person 's safety\n- whether there are any safeguarding concerns\n- the person's willingness to accept medical treatment and mental healthcare\n- the appropriate nursing observation level\n- whether the person has a care plan.\n- When a person attends the emergency department or minor injury unit following an episode of self- harm, offer referral to age-appropriate liaison psychiatry services, or for children and young people, crisis response service (or an equivalent specialist mental health service or a suitably skilled mental health professional) as soon as possible after arrival, for a psychosocial assessment.\n- If the person is aged under 18 years, refer them to Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (CAMHS).\n- If the person is aged 18-65 years old refer them to liaison psychiatrist/mental health service\n- Early referral should be considered if there is evidence of depression and/or self-harm\n- An age-appropriate liaison psychiatry professional or a suitably skilled mental health professional should see and speak to the person at every attendance after an episode of self-harm. Ensure that the emergency department has a private, designated area for psychosocial assessments to take place, where it is possible to speak in confidence without being overheard.\n- Ensure that the waiting area in the emergency department for people who have self-harmed is close to staff who can provide care, support and observation. httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Ensure that appropriate joint governance arrangements are in place so that physical and mental healthcare can be delivered together in emergency departments. This should include:\n- Access to electronic record systems for both mental health services and medical treatment at the point of care\n- jointly agreed referral pathways for concurrent physical and mental healthcare\n- jointly agreed approaches to initial assessment and triage\n- monitoring of the use of mental health law and mental capacity law\n- joint safeguarding procedures\n- jointly agreed nursing observation policies\n- referral pathways to appropriate community servlces.\n- Evidence-based Treatment Pathway for Urgent and Emergency Liaison Mental psychiatry Health Services for Adults and Older Adults AND Specialist Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (under 18 years)\n- Summary Of the pathway for an emergency response from liaison mental healfserv\n- ces\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- The structure of CAMHS\n- The structure and operation Of CAMHS can seem complex, because it differs from the way children's physical health services and adult mental health services are set up. The structure of CAMHS is probably best explained in terms of how a child or young person accesses the service, with four 'tiers' of service provision. don't need to memorise liaison of mental health pathway or the structure of CAMHS for the exam purpose. httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 77 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 77:\nA 72-year-old man from nursing home presented to the ED with meatal bleeding due to foley's catheter out. What is the most appropriate next management?\n- **A)** Refer to urology team\n- **B)** Insert foley's catheter\n- **C)** perform retrograde urethrogram\n- **D)** Perform cystogram\n- **E)** Place suprapubic catheter\n- Explanation\n- The-folloyying-featur.e-indi.gates.gqnsidera.tiqn-gf retrograde-urethrogram-fqr-diag nosis.gf.ur.eth.ral inj patient_reg uires_urgent refer.ra.lkqnsultation-tq-a-ur.qlogist High-riding prostate on rectal examination\n- Meatal blood,\n- perineal ecchymosis,\n- Scrotal hematoma,\n- Haematuria and pain on urination may be present in incomplete ruptures\n- pelvic fracture, or gross hematuria,\n- Any resistance to gentle passage of a Foley catheter\n- Inability to void (with a palpable distended bladder) is another classic sign and is often associated with a complete rupture\n- Urethral trauma\n- The bulbar urethra is the most common site affected by blunt trauma\n- n bulbar injuries, the bulb is compressed against the pubic symphysis, resulting in rupture of the urethra at the site of compression. Possible mechanisms are straddle injuries or kicks to the perineum. A penile fracture can be complicated by a urethral injury in approximately 15% of cases.\n- Penetrating- anterior injuries are rare and are usually caused by gunshot wounds, stab wounds, dog bites, impalement, or penile amputations. Depending on the affected segment, penetrating injuries are usually associated with penile, testicular and/or pelvic injuries.\n- Insertion of foreign bodies is another rare cause of anterior injury. It is usually a result of autoerotic httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC stimulation or may be associated with psychiatric disorders.\n- Iatrogenic-injury_is the most common type of urethral trauma. The incidence of male urethral injury during transurethral catheterisation is 13.4 per 1,000 catheters inserted. Injuries can occur due to the creation of a false passage by the tip of the catheter or inadvertent inflation of the anchoring balloon in the urethra.\n- posterior male urethral injuries\n- Bluntposterior urethral injuries are almost exclusively related to pelvic fractures and the risk increases with fracture configuration severity. These injuries are referred to as pelvic fracture urethral injuries (PFUI), and are mainly caused by road traffic accidents pelvic fracture urethral injuries are divided into partial or complete ruptures.\n- In complete ruptures, there is a gap between the disrupted ends of the urethra, which fills up with scar tissue. There is no urethral wall in the scarred space and any lumen represents a fistulous tract between the urethral stumps\n- penetrating - injuries of the pelvis, perineum, or buttocks (mainly gunshot wounds) can also damage the posterior urethra but are extremely rare in the civilian setting. There is a high probability of associated injuries (approx. 90%), mainly intra- abdominal.\n- Iatrogenic injury— has been reported with transanal total mesorectal excision in 1-11% of cases. This injury is usually partial and located at the membranous urethra. High-riding prostate on rectal examination\n- Meatal blood,\n- perineal ecchymosis,\n- Scrotal hematoma,\n- Haematuria and pain on urination may be present in incomplete ruptures\n- pelvic fracture, or gross hematuria,\n- Any resistance to gentle passage of a Foley catheter\n- Inability to void (with a palpable distended bladder) is another classic sign and is often associated with a complete rupture\n- Inyestigation\n- Retrograde urethrography (RUG) is the standard in the early evaluation of a male urethral injury: RUC is\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC operator-dependent and is recommended to be performed by the treating urologist.\n- The diagnosis of urethral trauma is made with retrograde urethrography, which must be performed prior to the insertion of a urethral catheter to avoid further injury to the urethra. Extravasation of contrast demonstrates the location of the tear.\n- Flexible cysto-urethroscopy is a valuable alternative to diagnose an acute urethral injury and may distinguish between complete and partial rupture. Flexible cystourethroscopy is preferred to RUG in suspected penile fracture-associated urethral injury as RUG is associated with a high false-negative rate\n- In the acute phase, US scanning is used for guiding the placement of a suprapubic catheter. In complex PFUIs, MRI before deferred treatment provides valuable additional information, which can help to determine the most appropriate surgical strategy.\n- Management\n- Immediate exploration and urethral reconstruction;\n- This is indicated for penile fracture related injuries and non-life-threatening penetrating injuries.\n- Small lacerations can be repaired by simple closure. Complete ruptures without extensive tissue loss are treated with anastomotic repair. Only 2% of cases will develop a urethral stricture after immediate urethral reconstruction for penile fracture.\n- In the case of longer defects or apparent infection (particularly bite wounds), a staged repair with urethral marsupialisation is needed.\n- penetrating injuries require peri- and post- operative antibiotic treatment\n- In blunt injuries - Immediate urethroplasty should be performed.\n- The long term-outcomes (patency rate, potency rate) of patients treated with immediate urethroplasty is similar to these initially treated with suprapubic diversion and delayed urethroplasty. The main advantage of performing immediate urethroplasty is that this strategy significantly reduces the time to spontaneous voiding from two to six months to three weeks on average.\n- Spongiosal contusion and haematoma during immediate urethroplasty will make the operation technically more demanding; therefore, immediate urethroplasty should be performed by a dedicated urethral surgeon.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Blunt anterior urethral injuries are associated with spongiosal contusion. Evaluation of the limits of urethral debridement in the acute phase might be difficult and as a consequence, it is reasonable to start with urinary diversion only.\n- If urinary diversion is performed, the therapeutic options are suprapubic diversion or a trial of early endoscopic re-alignment with transurethral catheterisation; (refers to reestablishment of urethral alignment via indwelling urethral catheter by cystoscope) there is conflicting evidence as to which intervention is superior.\n- Urinary diversion is maintained for one to two weeks for partial ruptures and three weeks for complete ruptures.\n- Transurethral or suprapubic urinary diversion are treatment options for iatrogenic or life-threatening penetrating injuries. Minor iatrogenic urethral injuries and urethral contusions do not require urinary diversion.\n- Male posterior urethral injuries\n- Emergency management\n- As these injuries are usually associated with other severe injuries, resuscitation and immediate treatment of life-threatening injuries have absolute priority. penetrating injuries especially have a very high likelihood of associated injuries requiring immediate exploration. There is no urgency to treat the urethral injury and urinary diversion is not essential during the first hours after trauma, however, is preferable to establish early urinary diversion to:\n- monitor urinary output, since this is a valuable sign of the haemodynamic condition and the renal function of the patient; treat symptomatic retention if the patient is still conscious;\n- minimise urinary extravasation and its secondary effects, such as infection and fibrosis.\n- Insertion of a suprapubic catheter is an accepted practice in urgent situations. However, insertion of a suprapubic catheter is not without risk, especially in the unstable trauma patient where the bladder is often displaced by a pelvic haematoma or because of poor bladder filling due to haemodynamic shock or concomitant bladder injury. In these circumstances, an attempt at urethral catheterisation can be carried out by experienced personnel\n- It is extremely unlikely that the gentle passage of a urethral catheter will do any additional damage. If there is any difficulty, a suprapubic catheter should be placed under US guidance or under direct vision, for example, during laparotomy for associated Injures.\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Early urethral management (less than six weeks after injury): For partial injuries, urinary diversion (suprapubic or transurethral) is sufficient as these injuries can heal without significant scarring or obstruction. A complete injury will not heal, and formation Of an obliterated segment is inevitable in case of suprapubic diversion alone. To avoid this obliteration and a long period of suprapubic diversion followed by deferred urethroplasty, the urethral ends can be sutured (urethroplasty) or approximated over a transurethral catheter (re-alignment).\n- Early re-alignment:\n- Early re-alignment can be performed when a stable patient is on the operating table for other surgery or as a stand-alone procedure in the absence of concomitant injuries. In a partial injury, re- alignment, and transurethral catheterisation avoids extravasation of urine in the surrounding tissues reducing the inflammatory response. In complete injuries, the aim of re-alignment is to correct severe distraction injuries rather than to prevent a stricture.\n- Early urethroplasty:\n- Immediate urethroplasty within 48 hours after injury is difficult because of poor visualisation and the inability to accurately assess the degree of urethral disruption, due to extensive swelling and ecchymosis, which may result in extensive unjustified urethral debridement.\n- Early urethroplasty can be performed after two days and up to six weeks after the initial injury, if associated injuries have been stabilised, the distraction defect is short, the perineum is soft and the patient is able to lie down in the lithotomy position. This avoids a long period of suprapubic diversion with its discomfort and complications. As the results (complications, stricture recurrence, incontinence, and impotence) are equivalent to delayed urethroplasty, early urethroplasty might be an option for patients fulfilling the above- mentioned criteria.\n- Deferred management (greater than three months after injury):\n- The standard treatment remains deferred urethroplasty. In the case of a complete rupture, treated with an initial period of three months suprapubic diversion, obliteration of the posterior urethra is almost inevitable . Endoscopic treatment\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC of a complete obliteration is not successful. After at least three months of suprapubic diversion, the pelvic haematoma is nearly always resolved, the prostate has descended into a more normal position, the scar tissue has stabilised and the patient is clinically stable and able to lie down in the lithotomy position. Associated life-threatening injuries often preclude early management of penetrating membranous urethral injuries. In those cases, suprapubic diversion with delayed urethroplasty is also advised. perineal anastomotic repair is the surgical technique of choice, but a combined abdominoperineal approach is necessary in rare cases of concomitant bladder neck injury or recto-urethral fistula. posterior-urethraLinjury)\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 78 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 78:\nA six weeks old baby presents with his parents following a floppy episode lasting less than 30 seconds while feeding associated with pallor. He is back to his normal self and has since had a normal feed. He was born at 37 weeks and had an uncomplicated neonatal period. He has been otherwise well. Head to toe examination and his observations are unremarkable. His BM is 5.3. He is diagnosed with a brief resolved unexplained event (BRUE). WhlCh factor makes this a hlgh-risk episode?\n- **A)** Gestational age Of 37 weeks\n- **B)** Episode Of pallor C Six weeks old\n- **D)** Episode lasting 30 seconds E\n- Floppy episode Explanation\n- Infant< 2 months is high risk\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 79 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 79:\nA 30-year-old male brought to the ED with thoracic trauma. On examination, there is decreased in breath sound on the right side of chest. His observations are: BP iS  mmHg HR: 110 bpm RR 25 breath/min You suspect tension pneumothorax which of the following features suggest tension pneumothorax?\n- **A)** Increase in venous return\n- **B)** Dull percussion note\n- **C)** hyperresonant percussion note\n- **D)** Trachea towards the side of injury\n- **E)** Bradycardia Explanation\n- Clinical-features-Qt-gensiqmpne.umoghorax\n- Tachypnea, Tachycardia, hypotension, distended neck vein\n- Contralateral tracheal deviation\n- Unilateral hyper-resonant on percussion and absence of breath sound Cyanosis (late manifestation) 80 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 80:\nA 14-year-old male presented to the ED after being punched on his face and loss his one upper canine tooth which he has found and brought to the ED in milk. Which of the following the next step of management?\n- **A)** Implant after soaking the avulsed tooth in hydrogen peroxide https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **B)** Cet dental X-ray - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **C)** Refer to maxillofacial surgery\n- **D)** DO not replant\n- **E)** Irrigate With NS and replant the tooth Explanation\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth:\n- saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here\n- The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried out for up to 2 week.\n- DentaLtraummmanagement 81 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 81:\nA previously healthy 8-month-old boy presents to the ED with new onset of cyanosis and fatigue first noticed this morning. The child was recently treated for thrush and his parents have been using over the counter benzocaine for teething. The child is sleepy but easily awakens and cries. He is not in respiratory distress. The only vital sign abnormalities include a heart rate of 190 beats per minute at rest and oxygen saturation of 82% on room air. Placement of a non-rebreather facemask does not improve the patient's oxygenation. After confrming the diagnosis, which of the following treatments is indicated? httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **A)** Hydroxocobalamin - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** Congenital heart surgery\n- **C)** Sodium thiosulfate\n- **D)** Heparin\n- **E)** blue Explanation\n- Methemoglobinemia occurs when the iron associated with hemoglobin is changed through oxidation from a ferrous (Fe2*) to a ferric (Fe3+) state thus preventing the hemoglobin from binding oxygen. Patients with congenital methemoglobinemia generally have lifelong cyanosis but are otherwise asymptomatic. Substances known to cause acquired methemoglobinemia include dapsone and topical anesthetics, especially mucous membrane use of benzocaine, although it has been reported with lidocaine, tetracaine, or prilocaine use. Most reported cases are thought to be due to high doses or a break in the mucosal barrier such as by mucositis or thrush. Treatment for methemoglobinemia includes removing the offending agent, and if symptomatic, administering methylene blue which activates a non-physiologic NADPH pathway for faster reduction of the iron molecule. Congenital heart surgery (incorrect here) - would be indicated for a congenital heart defect such as Tetralogy of Fallot which can cause hypoxia in this age group, but is generally associated with fussiness or agitation during a "tet spell." 82 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 82:\nA 23-year-old girl presented to the ED with complaint of pain in her right arm she is unable to adduct her thumb. What is the synonyms of this presentation?\n- **A)** Carpal tunnel syndrome\n- **B)** Wrist drop   C Froment's sign\n- **D)** Smith fracture\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper\n- **E)** Colles fracture Explanation\n- Fromenes-sign - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- froment sign is used to evaluate the Ulnar nerve motor weakness due to entrapment at the elbow Or at the wrist, it is used to check the function of the adductor pollicis muscle. 83 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 83:\nA 68-year-old woman presents having suffered 4\n- fainting episodes' over the past 24 hours. Each episode was sudden and unexpected. Her examination is unremarkable. A copy of her rhythm strip is shown. What is most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Mobitz type 2 AV block B\n- Atrial fibrillation\n- **C)** Sick sinus syndrorne   D\n- Wenckebach\n- **E)** Complete heart block ECC reveals complete heart block. There is complete dissociation between atria and ventricle (P wave and QRS complex). 84 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 84:\nAn II-month-old infant brought to the ED by mother, she states that the baby turned pale and floppy for few seconds. Which of the following IS the risk factor for BRUE?\n- **A)** Breathlessness\n- **B)** Floppy\n- **C)** pallor\n- **D)** Cyanosis\n- **E)** Cestational age less than 32 weeks\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper Explanation\n- BRUE risk stratification - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 85 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 85:\nA 38-year-old presents to the ED with shortness of breath which started suddenly while he was waiting for his remote job interview to start. He has no recent illnesses but is currently in plaster awaiting an Achilles' tendon repair He has a PMH of anxiety and no family history and he takes propranolol. Auscultation of his chest is normal his observations are: HR 115 bpm RR 30 per minute BP 136/76mmHg sp02 on air Ecg shows normal sinus rhythm What is the most appropriate initial management step?\n- **A)** Check a D-dimer level   B\n- Request a CTPA\n- **C)** Discharge home With reassurance\n- **D)** Encourage re-breathing exercises\n- **E)** Start low dose benzodiazepine Explanation\n- The use of lower limb plaster casts increases a patient's risk for VTE\n- patient PE well score :\n- Short of breath indicates PE likely = 3 HR>100bpm 1.5 tota score = 4.5 https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- PEJikeb'-(Wellssgore.mor.e.Xhan-4pQints)\n- Offer a computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) immediately if possibleOR\n- If patient allergic to contrast media or has renal impairment then assess the suitability of a ventilation/perfusion single photon emission computed tomography (V/Q SPECT) scan or, if a V/Q SPECT scan is not available, a WQ planar scan, as an alternative to CTPA.\n- If a CTPA, V/QSPECT or V/Q planar scan cannot be done immediately, offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation (DOAC — apixaban or rivaroxaban. If neither suitable then LMWH followed by dabigatran or edoxaban or LMWH and a VKA). 86 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 86:\nA 2-year-old infant is noted to have mild cyanosis who assume a squatting position during long walking. He is noted to have increasing fussiness followed by increasing cyanosis, limpness, and unresponsiveness. What is the most likely underlying lesion is?\n- **A)** Transposition Of great arteries B\n- Tetralogy Of fallot\n- **C)** Anornalous pulmonary venous return\n- **D)** Hypoplastic left heart\n- **E)** Aspiration With Obstruction to air passage Explanation\n- TOF - clinical presentation varies based on the severity of the right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, more commonly presenting as neonates with a certain degree of cyanosis. In some patients, cyanosis presents months later in life, when the rate of obstructions worsens.\n- "Tet spells" (a child or infant becomes extremely blue and frequently agitated and out of breath) or hypercyanotic episodes present during infancy or toddler age and decrease after 4 to 5 years of age. Dehydration or agitation commonly precipitate tet spells, and if patients do not receive prompt and adequate treatment, they can develop severe cyanosis and hypoxia that, subsequently, can cause syncope and even death. 87 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 87:\nA 42-year-old industrial worker presented to the ED with complaint of blood in urine, weight-loss and occasionally abdominal pain. He has a 40 pack-year smoking history. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC His examination, revealed firm, non-tender and homogenous mass in the right flank. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Renal calculi\n- **B)** Renal cell carcinoma\n- **C)** Cystitis\n- **D)** Nephritis\n- **E)** Bladder carcinoma Explanation\n- Renal cell Carcinoma\n- Most cornrnon 10 renal malignancy\n- Most cornrnon in males 50—70 years old\n- Increase incidence with tobacco smoking and obesity.\n- Originate from PCT\n- Clinical features\n- flank pain\n- palpable mass\n- hematuria\n- Other feature (fever, polycythemia, weight-loss)\n- Investigation : CT scan\n- Managementzsurgical intervention , immunotherapy eg:ipilimumab 88 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 88:\nA 39 years old female with history of fall presented to the ED with complaint of wrist pain associated with difficulty in moving wrist. Her X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Monteggia fracture\n- **B)** Barton fracture   C Smith fracture\n- **D)** Colles fracture\n- **E)** Radial styloid fracture Explanatign httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC X-ray revealed extra-articular transverse fracture through the distal radius with volar angulation.\n- Smith fracture\n- Smith's fracture, or reverse Colles' fracture, is a volar angulated fracture of the distal radius. (volar means anterior hand or towards palmer surface) as its opposite in colle's where distal radius angulated dorsally (posteriorly).\n- The fracture line extends obliquely from the dorsal surface to the volar surface 1—2 cm proximal to the articular surface\n- MOI: result from a fall or direct blow on the dorsum of the hand and wrist, or from a fall on the outstretched hand in flexed position\n- The lateral radiograph reveal the volar angulated and displaced fracture.\n- Cive analgesia and immobilize in volar slab POP and require urgent referral to orthopedic for ORIF 89 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 89:\nA 25 year old woman presents to the ED with a 12 hour history of palpitations. She has been having intermittent palpitations for several weeks but these have never been associated with any chest pain or dizziness. Her observations are: sp02I 120 beats per minute 120,'65mrTIHg 17 breath per minute 99% on room air Her ECG shows atrial fibrillation. What is the most likely diagnosis in this patient? A\n- Thyroiditis\n- **B)** Multinodular goiter   C\n- Thyroid adenoma   D, Grave's disease   E\n- Thyroid storm Explanatign https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17 Paper\n- Heat intolerance\n- Sweating\n- Weight loss - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Palpitations - Including atrial fibrillation or other supraventricular tachycardias, especially in older people\n- Tremor\n- Diffuse goitre\n- Orbitopathy\n- Exercise intolerance, fatigue, muscle weakness polyuria, thirst, generalized itch.\n- Reduced libido, gynaecomastia in men\n- Specific features\n- Rapid-onset malaise, fever, and thyroid pain (may suggest subacute thyroiditis). Compression symptoms of breathlessness, hoarse voice, dysphagia, neck pressure (may be caused by a toxic multinodular goitre).\n- Eeatures of thyroid storm\n- Fever\n- Tachycardia, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, heart failure\n- Agitation, hyperthermia,\n- Jaundice\n- Delirium, and coma 90 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 90:\nA 58-year-old woman presents to the ED with sudden onset of breathlessness. An x-ray revealed a large sided pneumothorax and a Seldinger intercostal chest drain was successfully inserted one hour ago. She is now more unwell. Her observation is now Temp: 36.5C HR: 130bpm RR: 28 per minute sp02: 96% What is the most likely cause of her deterioration?\n- **A)** Injury of an intercostal artery\n- **B)** perforation Of the left ventricle Of the heart\n- **C)** Laceration Of lung parenchyrna httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-2-2/\n\n25/11/2025, 10:17  paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **D)** Recurrence of the pneumothorax\n- **E)** Division Of an intercostal nerve Explanation\n- Haemothorax due to intercostal artery injury Haemoperitonium due to liver or spleen injury\n- Persistent pneomthorax or pleural effusion due kinking or dislodgement of tube\n- Infection Injury to intrathoracic organs such as heart, esophagus    110/110\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	\N
e1225b95-b885-40a4-b914-f1827acc72e2	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	expert-clinical-sba-mock-2	Expert Clinical SBA Mock 2	article	# Expert Clinical SBA Mock 2\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC SVT with aberrancy\n- 160m\n- No AV\n- No\n- RSX\n- features\n- AV dissociation:\n- p and QRS complexes at different rates\n- p waves are often superimposed on QRS complexes and may be difficult to discern\n- Capture beats: Occur when the sinoatrial node transiently "captures" the ventricles in the midst of AV dissociation, producing a QRS complex of normal duration\n- Fusion beats: Occur when a sinus and ventricular beat coincide to produce a hybrid complex\n- RSR' complexes with a taller left rabbit ear:\n- This is the most specific finding in favour of VT\n- This is in contrast to RBBB, where the right rabbit ear is taller\n- Strip shows Taller left rabbit ear in VI https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Brugada sign: Distance from onset of R wave to nadir of S wave is > looms in leads VI-6 (discussed further below)\n- Josephson sign: Notching/slurring near the nadir of the S wave S wave Notched / Slurred R-S interval > 25Squares Lead V2 demonstrating both Josephson sign (blue) and Brugada sign (green)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nAn 18-year-old gid came into ED with complaint of irritability and drovwiness. On initial assessment her blood glucose was 1.5 mmol/l that became 5.7 after initial management. Now she wants to go home and refuse admission. What is your appropriate action?\n- **A)** Counsel the patient and discharge\n- **B)** Discuss With family\n- **C)** Discharge without capacity check\n- **D)** Admit the patient https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Perform capacity assessment then discharge Explanatien\n- The Mental Capacity Act (MCA) is designed to protect and empower people who may lack the mental capacity to make their own decisions about their care and treatment. It applies to people aged 16 and over\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest.\n- If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then he/she shouldn't be treated without consent.\n- assume a person has the capacity to make a decision themselves, unless it's proved otherwise\n- wherever possible, help people to make their own decisions\n- do not treat a person as lacking the capacity to make a decision just because they make an unwise decision\n- if you make a decision for someone who does not have capacity, it must be in their best interests\n- treatment and care provided to someone who lacks capacity should be the least restrictive of their basic rights and freedoms. 3 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 50-year-old male with history of RTA brought to the ED with shock. CVP line was placed, and resuscitation has been commenced. Initially, patient was stabilized, however, after 10 minutes patient condition has been deteriorated. His X-ray is given. What would be the cause of his deterioration?\n- **A)** Nerve injury\n- **B)** Catheter malposition\n- **C)** Arrhythmia   D' Internal bleeding\n- **E)** Infection Explanation\n- His X-ray suggest hemothorax as increased density of the right hemithorax compared to the left. httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Therefore, the cause of patient deterioration is internal bleeding.\n- Hemothorax _A hemothorax is a type of pleural effusion in which blood (<1500 mL) accumulates in the pleural cavity.> <1500ml) accumulates in the pleural cavity\n- laceration of the lung, great vessels, an intercostal vessel, or an internal mammary artery from penetrating or blunt trauma.\n- Thoracic spine fractures may also be associated with a hemothorax\n- Respiratory distress\n- Tachypnoea\n- dullness to percussion is heard on the affected side\n- decreased or absent breath sounds on affected side hypotension\n- small amount of blood will be identified as a homogeneous opacity on the affected side.\n- Bleeding is usually self-limited and does not require operative intervention.\n- An acute hemothorax that is large enough to appear on a chest x-ray may be treated with a 28-32 French chest tube.\n- The chest tube evacuates blood, reduces the risk of a clotted hemothorax, and, allows for continuous monitoring of blood loss. 4/ 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 50-year-old male patient working in hot sunny weather brought to the ED by his friend with rapid breathing. His initial observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 120bpm T 420C GCS  What is your initial step of management? https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper\n- **A)** Diazepam - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** Immersion in ice water and cooling C\n- Furosemide\n- **D)** Fan and exposure to cold weather\n- **E)** Dantrolene Explanation\n- External; Spraying the patient with tepid water and using a fan is the most practical method.\n- Immersing the patient's limbs or whole body in cool water is described but impractical. eg: not appropriate for very sick patient.\n- placing of icepacks in the groin and axillae.\n- Internal: the use of cooled peripheral intravenous fluids.\n- Cold fluid peritoneal and gastric lavage and cardiopulmonary bypass have also been described, but are not usually necessary.\n- are used to treat seizure and also to reduce shivering, make cooling techniques more tolerable.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 28-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of Palpitations and few fainting episodes, he has history of death of his father suddenly and unexpectedly in early 40s. His ECG is given. What is your most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Wellens Syndrome\n- **B)** Brugada Syndrome\n- **C)** Atrial fibrillation\n- **D)** Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\n- **E)** WPW Syndrome ECC demonstrates features consistent with Type Brugada syndrome: Right bundle branch block pattern Downsloping 'coved' ST elevation in leads \\.n-vz. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Brugada syndrome VS wellens syndrome Brugada syndrom https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 6 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 50-year-old male with no known comorbid presented to the ED with complaint of palpitations and recurrent attacks of collapse. His ECG is given What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Complete heart block\n- **B)** Mobitz type 2\n- **C)** Sinus tachycardia O\n- **D)** VT E\n- Mobitz type I Explanation\n- ECC suggests complete dissociation between atria and ventricle (P wave and QRS complex). 7 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nWhat "D' indicates in this graph?\n- **A)** Ascending expiratory phase\n- **B)** Dead space ventilation\n- **C)** End tidal C02\n- **D)** Alveolar plateau httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Descending inspiratory phase Explanation\n- The end tidal C02 is the highest concentration of C02 seen at the end of each breath and is represented as letter "D" on the figure. 8 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 35-years-old motorcyclist brought to the ED following road traffic collision. On initial assessment, you suspect abdominal injury and you commenced fluid resuscitation. His observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 120 bpm sp02 94% What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Obtain CT scan\n- **B)** DPL\n- **C)** Perform eFAST   D\n- Urgent laparotomy\n- **E)** Continuous observation in the ED Explanation\n- Haemodynamically unstable patient with blunt trauma should be investigated with eFAST before laparotomy/theater. if eFAST positive patient should be immediately transfer to theater. However, haemodynamically unstable patient with penetrating trauma doesn't require eFAST. Urgent laparotomy/ transfer to theater is required. abdominal trauma httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Indication-of-laparotomy\n- Blunt abdominal trauma with hypotension, with a positive FAST or clinical evidence of intraperitoneal bleeding, or without another source of bleeding Hypotension with an abdominal wound that penetrates the anterior fascia\n- Gunshot wounds that traverse the peritoneal cavity\n- Evisceration Bleeding from the stomach, rectum, or genitourinary tract following penetrating trauma Peritonitis\n- Free air, retroperitoneal air, or rupture of the hemidiaphragm\n- Contrast-enhanced CT that demonstrates ruptured gastrointestinal tract, intraperitoneal bladder injury, renal pedicle injury, or severe visceral parenchymal injury after blunt or penetrating trauma\n- Haemodynamically unstable patient with blunt trauma should be investigation With eFAST before laparotomy/theater Haemodynamically unstable patient with penetrating trauma doesn't require eFAST. urgent laparotomy/ transfer to theater is required. 9 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 25-year-old homeless patient is brought to the ED by paramedics. She is unconscious GCS 6 and having repeated attacks of seizures ongoing for more than 30 minutes. What is the initial step of management?   A\n- Intubation\n- **B)** Anticonvulsant drugs\n- **C)** Baseline investigation\n- **D)** Hypertonic saline bolus https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper\n- **E)** Withhold treatment Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Seizure ongoing more than 30 minutes and CCS 6, such patients need to be intubated under general anesthesia. 10 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 50-year-old male patient with history of Hep-C cirrhosis brought to the ED with complain of massive upper Gi bleed. Which of the following drug has shown to improve mortality in a patient with variceal upper GI bleed?   A\n- Omeparazole\n- **B)** Blackmore tube\n- **C)** Tranexamic acid\n- **D)** Vitamin- K E\n- Terlipressin Explanation\n- Terlipressin has been shown to reduce blood loss from actively bleeding varices and confers a 34% relative risk reduction in risk of mortality. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Offer-terlipresing_vasopressin, somatostatin to patients with suspected variceal bleeding at presentation. Stop treatment after definitive haemostasis has been achieved, or after 5 days, unless there is another indication for its use\n- Terlipressin has been shown to reduce blood loss from actively bleeding varices and confers a 34% relative risk reduction in risk of mortality. Ceftriaxone) at presentation to patients with suspected or confirmed variceal bleeding.\n- endoscopic band ligation in patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from oesophageal varices.\n- Consider transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunts (TIPS) if bleeding from oesophageal varices is not controlled by band ligation.\n- 'mgastrig-yar.iges\n- Offer endoscopic injection of N-butyl-2- cyanoacrylate to patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from gastric varices.\n- Offer TIPS if bleeding from gastric varices is not controlled by endoscopic injection of N-butyl-2- cyanoacrylate.\n- A balloon tamponade device can be used to quell the bleeding until the shunt is placed (Sengstaken- Blakemore for esophageal varices, Linton-Nachlas for gastric varices). 11 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 11:\nA 19-year-old female with history of asthma presents to the ED with increasing shortness of breath and wheeze. She has been using increasing amounts of her salbutamol inhaler over the past few days. She is unable to talk on full sentences and has widespread wheeze throughout her chest on auscultation. Her observations are: RR 35 breaths/rnin, HR 130 bpm SP02 91 Her ABGs is given How severe is this patient's asthma? A Life-threatening asthma\n- **B)** Moderate asthma\n- **C)** Acute severe asthma\n- **D)** Near fatal asthma httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper\n- **E)** Mild asthma Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Any patient with features of acute severe asthma along with any one feature of life-threatening asthma (as in above scenario his saturation is below ) would be classify as life-threatening asthma.\n- Levels of severity Of acute asthma attacks in 01 to with sp02 with infla t 12 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 12:\nA 37-year-old welder presented to the ED with a history of redness and foreign body sensation in the left eye. his eye image is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Corneal abrasion\n- **B)** Clobe rupture\n- **C)** Retrobulbar haemorrhage\n- **D)** Scleral penetrating injury\n- **E)** conjunctivitis Explanation Clobe Rupture\n- is a traumatic injury involving the breakdown in the integrity of the wall of the eye, either the sclera or cornea or both\n- blunt trauma, projectile injury, laceration of eyelid/periorbital area, metal on metal, high-speed httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo Paper machinery, explosions - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Protruding foreign body in eye\n- Eccentric/teardrop pupil\n- Decreased visual acuity\n- Afferent pupillary defect\n- Extrusion of vitreous\n- External prolapse of the iris or ciliary body\n- Tenting of the sclera or cornea at the site of globe puncture\n- Volume loss Of eye\n- mnyes.tigation\n- Seidel test å apply fluorescein dye to eye, which binds damaged corneal epithelium and turns green under light through a cobalt-blue filter or Wood lamp, and check for streaming of fluorescein-tinged aqueous humor; this indicates corneal laceration\n- CT of orbit\n- prevent increased intraocular pressure (lop)\n- Head of bed at 30 degrees\n- Avoid eye manipulation (do not measure eye pressure, retract lid, or perform ocular ultrasound)\n- Cover with an eye shield (do not place eye patch as it exerts pressure on globe) — A paper cup can be used if an eye shield is not available\n- Administer topical and systemic broad-spectrum antibiotics\n- Tetanus booster as required\n- Cive antiemetics to prevent nausea (which increases IOP) Cive pain medicine\n- Consult ophthalmology even if CT negative, formal surgical evaluation may be needed if occult injury suspected 13 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 13:\nA 25-year-old male with no known PMH or drug history presents to the ED with history of recurrent priapism usually resolve spontaneously but this time acute attack took longer time. Local anesthetic lidocaine given. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Corpora cavernosa injection With phenylephrine\n- **B)** Ice compression\n- **C)** Physical therapy\n- **D)** Irrigation therapy only https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** Aspirate blood With needle Explanation\n- Each acute episode of recurrent priapism manages as ischaemic priapism\n- After local anesthesia Insert wide bore butterfly through the glans into the corpora cavernosa and aspirate cavernosal blood until bright red arterial blood is obtained. Initial conservative measures ot the glans HO red arterial is Cavernosal irrigation Intracavernosal therapy Currant therapy is p Ywn"opnnno wth aliqut*s 200 pg Wiro 3-5 Surgical therapy SMgical Consic*r prirnuy zonile prosthesis invlantation if *'apism has than\n- Stuttering-(Regur.rent-oontermitten.t) algorit