 (also known as lipid resuscitation therapy, LRT) is the current recommended treatment for local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) 41 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 41:\nA 25-year-old male is brought to the ED following RTA with severe respiratory distress. after initial assessment, you plan to intubate the patient. Which one of following is the common complication after tracheal intubation?\n- **A)** Wheezing\n- **B)** LOW 02 Saturation\n- **C)** Type-2 respiratory failure D, Hoarse voice\n- **E)** Inspiratory stridor\n- Explanation Displaced vocal cords - are another complication of tracheal intubation, especially in difficult patients. The presence of a displaced vocal cord may not be apparent until extubation. The patient may have persistent hoarseness, stridor, or fail extubation completely due to airway obstruction. This occurs if the ETT lumen catches on an arytenoid and the tube is forced. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Consultation with otolaryngology should be sought immediately if this is identified.\n- Complications-of-tracheal-intubation\n- The most common form of failed intubation is inadvertent esophageal intubation.\n- This may present in a delayed fashion if adequate pre-oxygenation is performed.\n- Auscultation of breath sounds is notoriously ineffective in definitively determining esophageal intubation due to the proximity of the stomach to the diaphragm. The obvious sign then becomes desaturation despite the external appearance of adequate ventilation. In this case, the endotracheal tube should be removed immediately, and mask ventilation resumed to provide oxygenation.\n- the endotracheal tube (ETT) distal to the carina may lead to bronchial intubation. usually, the right mainstem bronchus branches at a more acute angle than the left main stem bronchus thus leading to right mainstem bronchial intubation.\n- Auscultation of right-sided breath sounds and absent or decreased left-sided sounds can confirm this. It may also become evident when the post- intubation chest x-ray is examined.\n- The ETT may be pulled back carefully until bilateral breath sounds are auscultated.\n- Displaced vocal cords - are another complication, especially in difficult patients. The presence of a displaced vocal cord may not be apparent until extubation. The patient may have persistent hoarseness, stridor, or fail extubation completely due to airway obstruction. This occurs if the ETT lumen catches on an arytenoid and the tube is forced.\n- Consultation with otolaryngology should be sought immediately if this is identified. Other less common complications can arise, such as mediastinal intubation, tracheoesophageal fistula, and tracheal stenosis. 42 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 42:\nA 32-year-old pregnant female presented to the ED with severe vomiting. Initial fluid resuscitation and antiemetic drug was given. In a short period of time, she develops lip smacking and tongue protrusion. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Which of the following is the drug of choice in her condition? A\n- Procyclidine\n- **B)** Midazolam C\n- Diazepam\n- **D)** Benztropine\n- **E)** Haloperidol with promethazine Explanatign\n- The movements described are characteristic of an acute dystonic reaction\n- An acute dystonic reaction is most often seen with antipsychotic or anti-emetic (metoclopramide) use.\n- Antimuscarinic drugs used in dystonias _ The antimuscarinic drugs procyclidine hydrochloride and trihexyphenidyl hydrochloride reduce the symptoms of dystonias, including those induced by antipsychotic drugs.\n- - Tardive dyskinesia is not improved by antimuscarinic drugs and may be made worse.\n- If treatment with an antimuscarinic is ineffective, intravenous diazepam can be given for life- threatening acute drug-induced dystonic reactions. 