ood count https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper\n- **C)** Geimsa stain\n- **D)** Viral serology\n- **E)** Abdominal ultrasound Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC thanu\n- full blood count (FBC) with differential white cell count and a monospot test (heterophile antibodies) in the second week of the illness.\n- Note — FBC option is incorrect as FBC without differential WCC.\n- FBC without differential counts the total number of white blood cells.\n- FBC with differential. There are five kinds of white blood cells. The differential looks at how many of each kind of white blood cell you have.\n- Glandular fever (infectious mononucleosis) is an infection most commonly caused by the Epstein- Barr virus (EBV)\n- It causes approximately 80—90% of cases of glandular fever, with the remainder due largely to cytomegalovirus, human herpesvirus 6, toxoplasmosis, HIV, and adenovirus.\n- Glandular fever is most common in people aged 15— 24 years and is spread mainly through contact with saliva, such as through kissing or sharing food and drink utensils.\n- Clinical features\n- Fever.\n- Lymphadenopathy - Bilateral posterior cervical lymphadenopathy is typical. There may also be anterior cervical, submandibular, suboccipital, postauricular, epitrochlear, axillary, and inguinal lymphadenopathy and lymph nodes are mildly tender and mobile.\n- Sore throat (usually severe) - There may be tonsillar enlargement, the tonsils may meet in the mid-line, and there is usually a 'whitewash' exudate on the tonsils and Sore throat may fail to improve, or become worse, after several days. Other clinical features:\n- Prodromal symptoms — this usually lasts for several days and includes general malaise, fatigue, myalgia, chills, sweats, anorexia, and retro-orbital headache. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Non-specific rash — after being treated with amoxicillin.\n- Splenomegaly\n- Hepatomegaly\n- ln.yestigagi.qns\n- the than\n- a if a rapid\n- Stridor.\n- Dehydration or difficulty swallowing fluids.\n- A suspected potentially serious complication of glandular fever, such as splenic rupture.\n- Other_ampatients\n- Advise on the use of paracetamol or ibuprofen to relieve pain and fever symptoms.\n- Symptoms usually last for 2—4 weeks.\n- Tiredness is common and is often the last symptom to resolve.\n- That exclusion from work or school is not necessary.\n- To limit spread of the disease by avoiding kissing and sharing eating or drinking utensils, and to thoroughly clean all items that may have been contaminated by saliva 46 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 46:\nA 50-year-old male brought to the ED by paramedics with complaint of vomiting and blurred vision. his observations include BP  pulse 46bpm. On further assessment, it has been revealed that patient on certain medications for his heart condition prescribed by his GR His ECG iS given. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC What is the most likely cause of his condition?\n- **A)** Beta blocker toxicity\n- **B)** Nitrate\n- **C)** Furosemide\n- **D)** Amitriptyline overdose   E, Digoxin toxicity Explanation\n- ECC suggest sinus bradycardia with PR and QRS prolongation. They are common features of digoxin toxicity. Qigoxin toxicity_ Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside indicated for the treatment of chronic heart failure and persistent atrial fibrillation. In therapeutic doses, digoxin increases cardiac contractility and controls the heart rate. Serum digoxin concentration is usually over the therapeutic range of 0.6 to 1.2 nanomol/L (0.5 to 0.9 nanograms/mL) in patients taking digoxin for heart failure. However, some patients can be toxic at therapeutic digoxin serum concentrations\n- Risk factors Age years\n- Decreased renal clearance\n- Electrolyte disturbance - hyperkalaemia, hypokalaemia,\n- Use of specific drugs (e.g., amiodarone, clarithromycin, ciclosporin, itraconazole, propafenone, spironolactone, verapamil, diltiazem, ketoconazole, vinblastine, doxorubicin, reserpine, and erythromycin).\n- Clinical features\n- History of digoxin intake for medical condition or exposure to digoxin\n- Palpitations, syncope, arrhythmias, and dyspnoea\n- Nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, or anorexia. Lethargy, weakness, and confusion.\n- Visual symptoms - disturbances of colour vision with a tendency to perceive yellow halos around https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC objects (xanthopsia), blurred vision, and diplopia.\n- Investigations\n- Other_inyestigations\n- serum digoxin concentration - severe toxic effects may be seen with digoxin concentrations greater than 4.0 microgram/L (4.0 nanogram/mL; 5.2 nanomol/L).\n- serum potassium level and magnesium level\n- serum creatinine and urea\n- Management\n- Perform an Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure (ABCDE) assessment and re-assess the patient regularly throughout treatment,\n- Be alert to the possibility of cardiac arrest, resuscitation and supportive treatment may be needed\n- Correct electrolyte abnormalities in line with local protocol i-e hyperkalaemia with IV calcium chloride or gluconate.\n- Urgentlyadminister digoxin-specific antibody (Fab) fragments (also known as digoxin immune Fab) in patients with strongly suspected or known digoxin toxicity and:\n- Cardiac arrest\n- Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia or severe bradyarrhythmia https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Severe hyperkalaemia (potassium concentrations >6.S mmol/L [>6.5 mEq/Ll) resistant to conventional treatment.\n- Repeated doses may be required, for example if the patient remains unstable with arrhythmias or hyperkalaemia.\n- Frequently monitor the patient's serum potassium and magnesium. Correct hypomagnesaemia and hypokalaemia, if present, before or during administration of digoxin-specific antibody (Fab) fragments.\n- Agtiyated.ghar.gqal=give the activated charcoal within 2 hours of acute ingestion, although even later administration may be beneficial.\n- Cive further doses every 4 hours by mouth or nasogastric tube. 47 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 47:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of severe painful eye associated with lacrimation, photophobia, conjunctival hyperemia and decreased visual acuity. His corneal fluorescein staining image is given. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?   A\n- Topical Canciclovir B\n- Corticosteroids\n- **C)** Ciprofloxacin drops D\n- IV acyclovir\n- **E)** Irrigation With water Herpes simplex keratitis HSK is confined to the epithelium with classic dendritic appearance, commence antiviral therapy with one of the following:\n- oc. aciclovir3%, e.g. Aciclovir Agepha, ophthalmic preparation (unpreserved)\n- ganciclovir 0.15% ophthalmic gel\n- Herpes simplex-Ocular infection\n- Herpes simplex virus (HSV) ocular infections can cause inflammation of the retina, iris, cornea, conjunctiva, eyelids, and surrounding skin. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC The hallmark of this disease is the branching or dentritic ulcer.\n- Infection is usually caused by HSV type 1 (HSV-I), and rarely by HSV type 2 (HSV-2), typically in association with orogenital sex.\n- Cornea — keratitis.\n- Iris and associated uveal tract — iritis or iridocyclitis; uveitis.\n- Retina — retinitis.\n- Conjunctiva — conjunctivitis.\n- Eyelids — blepharitis.\n- Surrounding skin — periocular dermatitis\n- Clinical features\n- Symptoms\n- Malaise and fever\n- Eye pain\n- Eye irritation or photophobia Eye watering\n- Blurred vision\n- Sign\n- An acute red eye\n- Crops of vesicles and ulcers on an erythematous base, or pustules along the lid margin or periocular skin\n- A hazy cornea\n- A fixed irregular pupil\n- Reduced corneal sensitivity\n- Reduced visual acuity suggests keratitis\n- Management (NICE guideline)\n- Refer all cases of suspected ocular herpes simplex infection to eye casualty, or an emergency eye service for same-day assessment and specialist management.\n- if emergency same-day assessment is not possible or practical, seek specialist advice from an ophthalmologist regarding initiating drug treatment such as topical antivirals ( aciclovir Ointment Or ganciclovir gel)\n- Acute Herpes Simplex: in non-contact lens wearing adults and where HSK is confined to the epithelium with classic dendritic appearance, commence antiviral therapy with one of the following:\n- oc. aciclovir3%, e.g. Aciclovir Agepha, ophthalmic preparation (unpreserved)\n- ganciclovir 0.15% ophthalmic gel httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Complications\n- Corneal scarring and visual impairment\n- Corneal perforation\n- Secondary glaucoma\n- Infection 48 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 48:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with shock, You've decided to insert central venous line to resuscitate the patient. After access to internal jugular vein, how would you confirm the placement of central venous catheter?\n- **A)** Transducer wavegraphy\n- **B)** 10ml heparinized saline flush\n- **C)** Back flow of blood\n- **D)** X-ray for confirmation\n- **E)** No need for confirmation Explanation\n- Confirmation of the catheter tip position with chest radiography is required prior to use when internal jugular and subclavian access is obtained.\n- A chest X-ray should be performed immediately for the internal jugular and subclavian lines to ensure proper placement and the absence of an iatrogenic pneumothorax.\n- ae sure you are withdrawing venous blood before dilation and cannulation of the vessel.\n- If the internal jugular CVL attempt is unsuccessful, move to the ipsilateral subclavian vein. Never attempt the opposite side without a chest X-ray or ultrasound first to avoid bilateral pneumothoraces. Never force the guidewire on insertion because it may cause damage to the vessel or surrounding structures. Forcing the wire could also cause it to kink, making removal difficult and causing damage to the vessel wall. It may also lead to an inaccurate position of the catheter.\n- Always place your finger over the open hub ofthe needle to prevent an air embolism.\n- Always confirm placement with ultrasound, looking for reverberation artifact of the needle and/or tenting of the vessel wall. Needles cannot be visualized on ultrasound. Wires can be visualized so the operator can also confirm at that step.\n- A venous blood gas can be aspirated off a femoral line to ensure it is not arterial. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 49 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 49:\nA 21-years old male RTA brought to the ED with devastating brain injury. Patient has been intubated and neurosurg'cal consultation has been made. however, neurosurgeon refuse to perform surgery. Patient attendants have been counselled for his condition. What would be your next step of management? Admit patient in intensive care B\n- Discharge home\n- **C)** perform brain death test\n- **D)** Discuss for Organ donation\n- **E)** Retain in the ED Explanation\n- Evaluated by neurosurgery deemed to be non- survivable with no benefit from neurosurgery intervention\n- Do not withdraw life supportive treatment up to 48-72 hours\n- Admit the Pt in the ICU\n- Reassess Pt every 24 hours pevastatingbrain injury algorithm https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo Paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC so / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 50:\nA 4-year-old child brought to ED by mother. Her mother reports that his child became suddenly turn blue and breath rapidly after playing football. Cardiac examination demonstrates right ventricular impulse, and harsh systolic murmur. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** PDA B\n- Tetralogy of fallot\n- **C)** ASD\n- **E)** Mitral Stenosis Explanation\n- This patient has tetralogy of Fallot (TOE), the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease. The right ventricular outflow obstruction creates a right to left shunt causing venous blood to be shunted to the overriding aorta and into the systemic circulation manifesting as cyanosis.\n- CXR - shows boot shaped heart. / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 51:\nA 57-year-old woman with breast cancer has taken an overdose of slow-release morphine. She says she doesnt need treatment and wants to the hospital and die at home. Her family, however, wanted her to be treated Which of the following is the most appropriate approach?\n- **A)** If she has completed an advance directive to say she does not want cardiopulmonary resuscitation, then she cannot be treated without consent\n- **B)** Consider discharge Without Capacity assessment\n- **C)** If She has capacity then she cannot be treated without consent\n- **D)** She is clearly unwell and can be treated Without consent httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **E)** If her family agree then she can be treated Without consent Explanatien\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest. If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then that patient should not be treated without consent. 52 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 52:\nA 70-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of scrotal pain and swelling associated with urethral discharge. On examination, pain relieve on elevation of testes. epididymo-orchitis diagnosis was made. What is the most likely causative agent in this patient?\n- **A)** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **B)** Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- **C)** Chlamydia\n- **D)** E.coli\n- **E)** pseudomonas aeruginosa Explanation\n- Impeople with a low-risk sexual history, it is usually caused by enteric organisms such as Escherichia coli . Epididymo-orchitis\n- Epididymo-orchitis is a clinical syndrome consisting of pain, swelling, and inflammation of the epididymis and/or testis.\n- In pre-pubertal children, epididymo-orchitis is usually idiopathic and self-limiting\n- It may be secondary to recent adenovirus, enterovirus, or other viral infection.\n- In sexually active people - usually caused by local spread of sexually transmitted infections (STIS) such as Chlamydia or Neisseria gonorrhoeae. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- In-people-aged.Ä5ygars.or_Qlder with a low-risk sexual history, it is usually caused by enteric organisms such as Escherichia coli .\n- Rare-causes-of-epididymo:qrghitisjnglude-mumps infection (causes unilateral or bilateral parotid swelling which precedes usually unilateral orchitis, often in young adults).\n- Clinical features\n- Feverand chills\n- Swelling and redness in your testicle (diffuse swelling)\n- Pain in your scrotum, sometimes moving to the rest of your groin\n- Blood in semen\n- Urethral discharge Dysuria (painful urination)\n- 'Prehn sign' (relief of pain with elevation of the testes)\n- presence of cremasteric reflex\n- Lnvestigations\n- Diagnosis made clinically\n- Urine dipstick test and midstream urine (MSU) for microscopy and culture, if acute epididymo-orchitis is suspected in a pre-pubertal child, or an adult aged over 35 years with a low-risk sexual history\n- If any doubt for STI then a referral for GUM assessment. Don't be tempted to start empirical therapy for STIS\n- Referra