icion of early-onset neonatal infection or sign of meningitis\n- Note: don't perform urine or skin swab microscopy or culture routinely in late-onset of neonatal sepsis 88 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 88:\nA two-year-old child weighing 15kg brought into the ED by his mother with complaint of vomiting and diarrhea. On examination, his eyes are sunken and skin pinch goes slowly. What is the maintenance fluid required in this patient?\n- **A)** 2000m1\n- **B)** 1500m!\n- **D)** 4000ml\n- **E)** 2500ml Explanation 80dv might 2\n- 10kg 'Kai d requi—ts fluid httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Patient weight is 1Skg\n- Daily fluid requirements would be:\n- 1 10kg x 100 = 1000\n- Further 5kg x SO = 250\n- Total daily requirement = 1250 ml/24hr\n- per hourly rate would be? 1 d10kg 2 5kg x 2=10\n- Total hourly fluid requirement = soml/hr 89 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 89:\nA 4-day-old baby is brought to the ED by his mother. His mother reports that baby became cyanosed and developed respiratory distress after feeding. You order immediate CXR that is shown. what is most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Pneumonia\n- **B)** BRUE\n- **C)** Congenital heart disease\n- **D)** Diaphragmatic hernia\n- **E)** Viscus perforation Explanation X-ray of a neonate demonstrates the left hemithorax to be filled with multiple radiolucent regions, with displacement of the cardiac silhouette to the right.\n- Patient history and investigation suggest diaphragmatic hernia. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia is a diaphragmatic defect, and are frequently diagnosed prenatally with US, which expedites proper initial newborn resuscitation.\n- Most commonly left sided.\n- The diaphragmatic defect allows intra-abdominal contents, including stomach, bowel, and, occasionally, liver, to enter the chest during the second trimester of gestation, leading to pulmonary hypoplasia\n- Clinical features:\n- The clinical hallmark is persistent respiratory distress at birth, often with a characteristic "seesaw" side-to-side respiratory pattern due to the severely hypoplastic ipsilateral lung https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Io:oo  paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Ln.y.e.s.tigati.qm-obtain chest and abdominal x-ray to confirm the diagnosis Management; immediate intubation is require to treat respiratory distress and then emergent referral to paediatric specialty.\n- Do not use bag-mask ventilation. which will inflate the GI contents in the chest and further compromise ventilation. 90 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 90:\nAn unresponsive 9-year-old child brought into the ED by his mother. On Examination he is pale and cool to touch. His observations are: BP 70/30rnmHg Pulse 270bpm Sp02 not detectable. What is the most common tachycardia in children? Supraventricular tachycardia B\n- Sinus tachycardia\n- **C)** Ventricular tachycardia\n- **D)** Ventricular fibrillation\n- **E)** Atrial fibrillation\n- SVT is the most common symptomatic dysrhythmia in infants in children. Children with congenital heart disease are it increased risk for SVT. Sinus-taghyga.r.d.i.a can be a normal increase in the heart rate if it is in response to exercise or physical or emotional stress. It's common in children, and usually no treatment is needed.\n- SVT another common tachycardia in children is supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). It presents in two forms: paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT) and paroxysmal atrial tachycardia (PAT).  https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/  104/104\n	\N	[]	\N	f	6	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	\N
95ca32b7-c57c-4009-883f-0c72e41233bf	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-vascular-deep-vein-thrombosis	Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 52-year-old woman presents with a 2-day history of painful swelling of her left lower calf. On examination, the left calf circumference is 4 cm greater than the right calf (measured 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity), with localized tenderness along the deep venous system and pitting oedema. According to the two-level Wells DVT Score, what is her score, risk category, and recommended diagnostic pathway per NICE NG158?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Wells score 2 (DVT likely); Perform urgent Proximal Leg Vein Ultrasound within 4 hours (or give interim therapeutic anticoagulation and scan within 24 hours)\n- **B.** Wells score 0 (DVT unlikely); D-dimer test alone is sufficient\n- **C.** No investigation needed, prescribe compression stockings\n- **D.** Perform contrast venography immediately\n- **E.** Wells score only applies to pulmonary embolism\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nWells DVT criteria: Calf swelling >3 cm compared to asymptomatic side (1 point), localized tenderness along deep venous system (1 point) = Total 2 points ('DVT Likely', score >=2). NICE NG158 recommends: perform proximal leg vein ultrasound within 4 hours; if unavailable, administer an interim dose of therapeutic anticoagulation (DOAC) and perform ultrasound within 24 hours.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE NG158 guidelines, what is the first-line oral anticoagulation therapy of choice for confirmed acute lower limb Deep Vein Thrombosis or Pulmonary Embolism in patients without severe renal impairment or active cancer?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC: Apixaban or Rivaroxaban) as initial monotherapy\n- **B.** Warfarin monotherapy without heparin overlap\n- **C.** Aspirin 300mg daily for 6 months\n- **D.** Intravenous unfractionated heparin continuous infusion for 6 months\n- **E.** Dipyridamole 200mg BD\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG158 recommends a Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC: Apixaban or Rivaroxaban as single-drug regimens with higher initial loading doses, or Edoxaban/Dabigatran following initial 5 days of LMWH) as first-line anticoagulation for confirmed VTE. DOACs have predictable pharmacokinetics, do not require routine INR monitoring, and demonstrate lower rates of major intracranial bleeding compared to Warfarin.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 62-year-old female with advanced metastatic pelvic malignancy presents with sudden, massive, agonizing swelling of her entire left lower limb. On examination, the leg is cold, pale, marble-white, and pulseless with severe pitting oedema. What life-threatening limb ischemia syndrome from complete iliofemoral venous occlusion is this?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_03_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Phlegmasia Alba Dolens ('milk leg' / painful white oedema)\n- **B.** Acute bacterial cellulitis\n- **C.** Superficial thrombophlebitis\n- **D.** Ruptured Baker's cyst\n- **E.** Compartment syndrome from trauma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPhlegmasia alba dolens ('milk leg') is an acute, severe manifestation of massive iliofemoral deep venous thrombosis. Severe deep venous occlusion leads to profound interstitial oedema and arterial spasm, rendering the limb pale, white, cold, and painful. If collateral venous channels also thrombose, it progresses to Phlegmasia Cerulea Dolens (cyanotic, purple, gangrenous limb), carrying high risk of venous gangrene and amputation, requiring emergency catheter-directed thrombolysis or surgical thrombectomy.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich D-dimer testing threshold strategy is validated to reduce unnecessary imaging in patients aged >50 years presenting with 'DVT Unlikely' or 'PE Unlikely'?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) Guidelines)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/deep-vein-thrombosis/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Age-Adjusted D-dimer threshold (Age in years x 10 mcg/L for patients >50 years)\n- **B.** Fixed threshold of 200 mcg/L for all ages\n- **C.** Weight-adjusted D-dimer\n- **D.** D-dimer threshold is halved in elderly patients\n- **E.** Gender-adjusted threshold\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe ADJUST-PE and YEARS studies validated the Age-Adjusted D-dimer strategy for patients aged >50 years: Age (years) x 10 mcg/L (or Age x 0.05 mg/L in FEU). Baseline D-dimer naturally rises with physiological aging; using an age-adjusted cutoff substantially increases specificity in elderly patients without sacrificing safety or missing VTE.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended minimum duration of therapeutic anticoagulation for a patient with a first unprovoked (idiopathic) proximal DVT who has a low bleeding risk according to NICE guidelines?\n\n- **A.** At least 3 to 6 months, followed by consideration of long-term / indefinite anticoagulation\n- **B.** Exactly 5 days\n- **C.** 2 weeks only\n- **D.** 1 month\n- **E.** Lifelong mandatory anticoagulation without review\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor a first PROVOKED DVT (major transient reversible risk factor, e.g. surgery, trauma, immobilisation), anticoagulation is recommended for 3 months. For an UNPROVOKED (idiopathic) proximal DVT or PE, anticoagulation is continued for at least 3 to 6 months, after which multidisciplinary review assesses the risk-benefit balance of continuing long-term (indefinite) anticoagulation.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b0252221-f6bf-492f-8017-3627d2971706	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia	Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia	article	# Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Causes\n- Peripheral arterial disease of the lower limbs is most commonly caused by atherosclerosis.\n- Vasculitis\n- Fibromuscular dysplasia Thromboembolism\n- Thromboembolism\n- Buerger's disease . Trauma\n- Dissection\n- Physiological entrapment syndromes, and cystic adventitial disease\n- Acute limb ischaemia\n- Most commonly due to thrombosis within a diseased artery when an atherosclerotic plaque ruptures (80—85%).\n- Cardiac embolization\n- Aortic dissection or embolization\n- Graft thrombosis; thrombosis of a popliteal aneurysm\n- Hypercoagulable states\n- Iatrogenic complications of vascular intervention\n- Risk factors\n- Smoking\n- Diabetes mellitus\n- Advanced age\n\nHypertension\n- Hypercholesterolaemia Advanced age\n- Known atherosclerotic disease\n-\n- CKD High serum homocysteine\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nFeatures of acute limb ischaemia\n- Pain — constantly present and persistent.\n- Pulseless — ankle pulses are always absent.\n- Pallor (or cyanosis or mottling).\n- Power loss or paralysis.\n- Paraesthesia or reduced sensation or numbness.\n- Perishing with cold.\n-\n\nIschaemia due to an embolus Onset is acute. The limb appears white (because there is no collateral circulation). Vascular examination in the other leg is usually normal. Ischaemia due to thrombosis Onset is more gradual. The leg may not be white and symptoms may be less severe (due to collateral circulation, which is often well-developed in people with chronic peripheral vascular disease). Presentation is usually with worsening claudication and rest pain. Pulses in the other leg may also be absent\n- Classical features of intermittent claudication\n- Cramp-like pain in a muscle group after walking a predictable distance that is relieved by rest and reproduced by walking the same distance again.\n- Pain is not present at rest or altered by position.\n- the calves are more commonly affected than the thigh or buttock areas. gjschaemia\n- Chronic rest pain, which may be worse at night\n- People may report sleeping with the leg hanging out of bed, or sleep in a chair to relieve symptoms in the affected foot.\n- Dependent rubor, pallor on elevation of the extremity, and reduced capillary refill.\n\n- Skin changes including ischaemic ulcers, non-healing foot wounds, and gangrene. Usually affects the toes.\n- Absent foot pulses.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nProper history and examination\n-\n- Examining;\n- The legs and feet for ulceration, temperature differences between the legs, muscle atrophy, and skin changes (for example thin, shiny skin, discolouration, dependent rubor and elevation pallor, tissue loss on the heel or between the toes, hair loss). Femoral, popliteal, and foot pulses.\n- Capillary refill time (although this may not be reliable).\n-\n- The cardiovascular system (including for arterial bruits — femoral, aortic, carotid). ankle-brachial index (ABI)\n- segmental pressure examination\n- duplex ultrasound\n- continuous wave Doppler ultrasound\n- catheter angiography\n-\n- CT angiogram Investigation to find sources of embolus\n-\n- ECG Echocardiography\n- Aortic, popliteal and femoral artery ultrasound\n-\n\n- An ABPI ratio Less than 0.5 Greater than 0.5 to less than 0.8 Between 0.8 and 1.3 Greater than 1.3\n- suggests severe arterial disease.\n- Refer the person urgently for specialist vascular assessment. suggests the presence of arterial disease or mixed arterial/venous disease. Refer the person for specialist vascular assessment Suggests no evidence Of significant arterial disease. May suggest the presence of arterial calcification, such as in some people with diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic vasculitis, atherosclerotic disease, and advanced chronic renal failure. Care must be taken in interpreting ABPI results in people with these conditions, as they may be misleadingly high.\n- Note: don't need to memorize for exam purposes.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nAcute limb ischaemia management\n- Antiplatelet therapy_=Aspirin is recommended. Clopidogrel is recommended as an effective alternative antiplatelet therapy to aspirin.\n\nAnalgesia Paracetamol and an opioid (weak or strong) are recommended,\n- depending on the severity of pain. Anticoagulation— In patients with acute limb ischaemia, systemic anticoagulation\n- with unfractionated heparin should be administered, unless contraindicated. Endovascular therapies\n- Percutaneous catheter-directed thrombolytic therapy.\n- Percutaneous mechanical thrombus extraction.\n-\n- Surgical-interventions Surgical thromboembolectomy.\n- Endarterectomy.\n-\n- Bypass surgery. Amputation if the limb is unsalvageable.\n-\n- Critical limb ischaemia (Chronic severe limb ischaemia) Urgently refer to a vascular multidisciplinary team\n-\n- Manage-pain\n- Offer paracetamol and either weak or strong opioids, depending on the severity of the pain.\n- Intermittent claudication management offers a supervised exercise programme to all people with intermittent\n- claudication. If supervised exercise is not available, consider suggesting unsupervised\n- exercise — For example advise exercise for approximately 30 minutes three to five times per week, walking until the onset of symptoms, then resting to recover.\n\n- Refer for consideration of angioplasty or bypass surgery when:\n- Advice on the benefits of modifying risk factors has been reinforced, and\n- A supervised exercise programme has not led to a satisfactory improvement in symptoms.\n- supervised exercise has not led to a satisfactory improvement, and the person prefers not to be referred for consideration of angioplasty or bypass surgery.\n- Offer all people with peripheral arterial disease information, advice, support, and treatment regarding the secondary_prevention of cardiovascular disease.\n- Diet, weight management, and exercise\n- Lipid modification and statin therapy The prevention, diagnosis, and management of diabetes\n- The prevention, diagnosis, and management of high blood pressure\n- Antiplatelet therapy previous  Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n*Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/peripheral-vascular-disease-critical-limb-ischemia/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n*Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischemia — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c489ed1a-ceb8-479c-b0a2-9bf3e080380e	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-vascular-peripheral-vascular-disease	Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 68-year-old male smoker with diabetes presents with sudden onset of severe, agonizing pain in his right leg. On examination, the right leg below the knee is cold to touch, pale, and pulseless. Sensation is decreased over the dorsum of the foot, and he cannot dorsiflex his toes. What are the classic '6 Ps' of Acute Limb Ischaemia (ALI)?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/peripheral-vascular-disease/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, Paralysis, and Perishingly cold (Poikilothermia)\n- **B.** Purpura, Petechiae, Pruritus, Pitting, Pyrexia, and Polyuria\n- **C.** Pain, Plethora, Palpitations, Priapism, Papules, and Phlebitis\n- **D.** Polydipsia, Polyphagia, Paresthesia, Ptosis, Proptosis, and Pallor\n- **E.** Pulsus alternans, Pulsus paradoxus, Papilloedema, Petechiae, Pallor, and Pain\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe 6 Ps of acute arterial limb ischaemia: (1) Pain (sudden, severe, continuous); (2) Pallor; (3) Pulselessness; (4) Paresthesia (sensory loss indicates nerve ischemia within 1-2 hours); (5) Paralysis (motor loss indicates irreversible muscle necrosis within 4-6 hours); (6) Perishingly cold (Poikilothermia / inability to regulate temperature). Presence of motor loss (paralysis) is an emergency requiring surgical revascularisation within 6 hours.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the Rutherford Classification category for an acutely ischemic limb with inaudible arterial Doppler signals, severe rest pain, sensory loss extending beyond the toes, and mild-to-moderate motor muscle weakness (incomplete paralysis), and what is the required intervention?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/peripheral-vascular-disease/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Rutherford Class IIb (Immediately Threatened); Requires immediate emergency surgical or endovascular revascularisation within 4-6 hours\n- **B.** Rutherford Class I (Viable); Elective outpatient angiography\n- **C.** Rutherford Class III (Irreversible); Primary major amputation\n- **D.** Rutherford Class 0; Reassurance\n- **E.** Conservative medical therapy with aspirin alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nRutherford Classification of ALI: Class I (Viable, no sensory/motor deficit); Class IIa (Marginally threatened, salvageable if treated promptly, sensory loss in toes); Class IIb (Immediately threatened, sensory loss above toes, motor weakness, salvageable with emergency revascularisation); Class III (Irreversible, profound anesthesia, paralysis, rigor, inaudible venous/arterial Doppler, requires amputation).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat initial emergency medical therapy should be administered immediately upon diagnosis of acute arterial limb ischaemia in the ED to prevent propagation of the thrombus?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Peripheral Vascular Disease & Critical Limb Ischaemia)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/peripheral-vascular-disease/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Unfractionated Heparin (weight-adjusted bolus e.g. 5,000 units followed by continuous infusion)\n- **B.** Oral warfarin 10mg single dose\n- **C.** Oral clopidogrel 75mg daily\n- **D.** Subcutaneous vitamin K\n- **E.** Tight compression bandages\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nImmediate systemic anticoagulation with IV Unfractionated Heparin (5,000 unit bolus followed by weight-adjusted infusion) is the critical initial medical intervention in acute limb ischaemia. It prevents proximal and distal propagation of thrombus, preserves the distal microcirculation and collaterals, and is easily reversible with protamine before emergency surgery.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nHow is the Ankle-Brachial Pressure Index (ABPI) calculated, and what value defines Chronic Critical Limb-Threatening Ischaemia (CLTI)?\n\n- **A.** Highest systolic pressure at the ankle divided by highest systolic pressure in the arm; ABPI < 0.40 (or resting ankle pressure <50 mmHg / toe pressure <30 mmHg)\n- **B.** Arm pressure divided by ankle pressure; value > 1.0\n- **C.** Ankle pressure minus arm pressure; value > 50\n- **D.** ABPI is only measured using pulse rate\n- **E.** ABPI between 0.90 and 1.20\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nABPI = Highest Doppler systolic pressure in dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial artery divided by the highest brachial systolic pressure. Normal is 0.90 - 1.30. Intermittent claudication is typically 0.50 - 0.85. Chronic Critical Limb-Threatening Ischaemia (CLTI: rest pain for >2 weeks, ischemic ulcers, or gangrene) is defined by ABPI < 0.40, ankle systolic pressure <50 mmHg, or toe pressure <30 mmHg.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 70-year-old diabetic male has an ABPI measured at 1.45 despite painful ischemic foot ulcers. What pathological vascular process explains this falsely elevated reading?\n\n- **A.** Mönckeberg's medial calcific sclerosis (arterial calcification rendering vessels non-compressible by standard sphygmomanometer cuffs)\n- **B.** Severe coarctation of the aorta\n- **C.** Superficial thrombophlebitis\n- **D.** Active deep vein thrombosis\n- **E.** Renal artery stenosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAn ABPI > 1.30 - 1.40 is abnormally high and indicates severely calcified, rigid, non-compressible arteries (Mönckeberg's arteriosclerosis), classic in long-standing diabetes mellitus and end-stage renal disease. The blood pressure cuff cannot compress the hardened vessel, generating artificially high pressure readings. In these patients, Toe-Brachial Index (TBI) is used because digital arteries are rarely calcified.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
ece88f40-da56-4683-9556-c9112e5e5fc9	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis-management	Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management	article	# Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nIn severe cases, dizziness and/or syncope (due to right ventricular failure).\n-\n- Other signs that may be present include: Tachycardia (heart rate greater than 100 beats per minute).\n- Hypoxia. . pyrexia. Elevated jugular venous pressure.\n- Gallop rhythm, a widely split second heart sound, tricuspid regurgitant murmur.\n- pleural rub. Hypotension (systolic blood pressure less than 90 mmHg) and cardiogenic shock\n- (rare signs indicating central PE and/or a severely reduced.\n\n## Differential Diagnosis\nPneumothorax.\n- Pneumonia.\n- Acute bronchitis.\n- Acute exacerbation of asthma\n-\n- ACS Acute coronary syndrome.\n- Acute congestive heart failure.\n- Dissecting or rupturing aortic aneurysm.\n-\n- Diagnosis and initial management (NICE G u i déiifiéj¯\n\n- Pulmonary embolism rule-out criteria (the PERC rule) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) Age 250 HR 2100 02 sat on room air Unilateral leg swelling Hemoptysis Recent surgery or trauma Prior PE or DVT Hormone use Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1\n- Note — If no criteria are positive and clinician's pre-test probability is <15%, PERC Rule criteria are satisfied.\n\n- If-PE-is-suspected, probability of PE Clinical feature Clinical signs and symptoms of DVT (minimum of leg swelling and pain with palpation of the deep veins)\n- An alternative diagnosis is less likely than 100 beat/min Immobilisation for more than 3 days or\n- surgery in the previous 4 weeks Previous DVT/PE\n- Haemoptysis\n- Malignancy (on treatment, treated in the last 6 months, or palliative) Clinical probability simplified score\n- PE likely\n- PE unlikely Points 3 3 1.5 1.5 1.5 1 1 Points\n-\n- More than 4 points 4 points or less\n\n- PE score more than 4 points)\n- Offer a computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) immediately if possible OR\n- If patient allergic to contrast media or has renal impairment then assess the suitability of a ventilation/perfusion single photon emission computed tomography (WQ SPECT) scan or, if a V/Q SPECT scan is not available, a VIQ planar scan, as an alternative to CT PA.\n- If a CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan cannot be done immediately, offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation (DOAC — apixaban or rivaroxaban. If neither suitable then LMWH followed by dabigatran or edoxaban or LMWH and a VKA).\n- Steps-after-CTPA,MQSPECTMMQ-planar scan\n- If PE is identified by CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan:\n- offer or continue anticoagulation treatment at least 3 months to people with confirmed proximal DVT or PE.\n- if anticoagulation treatment is contraindicated, consider a mechanical intervention (Inferior vena caval filters, Elastic graduated compression stockings, Percutaneous mechanical thrombectomy)\n- take into account contraindications, comorbidities and the person's preferences\n- Measure baseline full blood count, renal and hepatic function, PT and APTT but start anticoagulation before results available.\n\n- For people with suspected/ confirmed PE and haemodynamic instability (unstable patient):\n- offer continuous LJFH infusion and consider thrombolytic therapy\n- Thrombolysis is indicated for patients with severe circulatory compromise or a picture of massive pulmonary embolism. Prior proof of PE is not needed if the patient is peri-arrest and thrombolysis should be administered immediately (50mg Alteplase as a bolus) in such patients.\n- If PE is not identified by CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan:\n- consider a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan if DVT is suspected\n- If DVT not suspected — stop interim therapeutic anticoagulation, think about alternative diagnoses.\n- PE unlikely_(WelIs score 4 points or less) A D-dimer test with the result available within 4 hours if possible OR\n- if the D-dimer test result cannot be obtained within 4 hours (in any setting), offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation while awaiting the result.\n- If the D-dimer test result is positive\n- offer a computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CT PA) and follow the pathway-as-mentioned-aboye±\n- If the D-dimer test result is negative\n- stop interim therapeutic anticoagulation (but do not stop long-term anticoagulation if being used for secondary prevention) Think about alternative diagnoses.\n\n- Long-term anticoagulation for secondary_ prevention\n- Assess and discuss the benefits and risks of continuing, stopping or changing the anticoagulant with people who have had anticoagulation treatment for 3 months (3 to 6 months for people with active cancer) after a proximal DVT or PE\n- Consider stopping anticoagulation treatrnent 3 months (3 to 6 months for people with active cancer) after a provoked DVT or PE if the provoking factor is no longer present and the clinical course has been uncomplicated\n- Consider continuing anticoagulation beyond 3 months (6 months for people with active cancer) after an unprovoked DVT or PE. Key_points\n- Any haemodynamically unstable patient with suspected/ confirmed should be immediately thrombolysed (alteplase 50mg bolus).\n- Anti-coagulant drugs — use DOAC (apixaban or rivaroxaban) as first choice. If neither suitable, offer one of:\n- LM\\NH for at least 5 days followed by dabigatran or edoxaban OR\n- LMVVH and a VKA for at least 5 days, or until INR at least 2.0 on 2 consecutive readings, then a VKA alone.\n- Anticoagulation treatrnent for DVT or PE with triple positive antiphospholipid syndrome — Offer LMWH and a VKA for at least 5 days or until INR at least 2.0 on 2 consecutive readings, then a VKA alone.\n- Avoid DOACs in positive antiphospholipid syndrome.\n\n- Suspected PE: diagnosis and initial management Determine 2—1evel PE Wells Score suspected Wells score\n- 4 points PE likely Immediate CT PAZ (CT pulmonary angiogram) Interim therapeutic anticoagulation" while awaiting CTPA Wells score s 4 points PE unlikely Quantitative D—dimer test' and result in 4 hours Interim therapeutic anticoagulations\n- s while awaiting result D-dimer positive DVT not suspected D-dimer negative CTPA positive Diagnose PE and offer or continue treatment  CTPA negative DVT suspected Consider proximal leg vein ultrasound scan Stop any anticoagulation and think about other diagnoses Consider outpatient treatment for Iow-risk PE PE algorithm\n- 9\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 8 Reference)*\n\n![Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/vascular-emergencies/pulmonary-embolism-pe-diagnosis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n*Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a8e0662d-f2d0-49df-9ad5-c98622b3f39e	69b5a6ba-3b5d-4121-a4e7-ba476288a76f	sba-quiz-vascular-pulmonary-embolism	Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 50-year-old woman presents with sudden pleuritic chest pain, breathlessness, and haemoptysis. She had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy 2 weeks ago. Heart rate is 108 bpm, BP is 128/82 mmHg. She has no prior VTE history, no active cancer, and no leg swelling. According to the two-level Wells PE Score, how is she categorized, and what is the correct diagnostic pathway per NICE NG158?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/pulmonary-embolism/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Wells score 4.5 (PE likely); Perform urgent CT Pulmonary Angiography (CTPA) within 4 hours (or give interim therapeutic anticoagulation and scan within 24 hours)\n- **B.** Wells score 1.5 (PE unlikely); Perform high-sensitivity D-dimer first\n- **C.** Wells score 0; Discharge home without investigation\n- **D.** Perform urgent catheter pulmonary angiography immediately\n- **E.** Give systemic intravenous thrombolysis immediately\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nWells PE Score: HR >100 bpm (1.5), Surgery in past 4 weeks (1.5), Haemoptysis (1.0), and 'PE more likely than alternative' (3.0 if judged, but even with objective points alone = 4.0; with PE #1 alternative = 7.0). Under two-level score, >4 points is 'PE Likely', mandating immediate CTPA (or interim DOAC anticoagulation if imaging is delayed).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 58-year-old male with confirmed acute pulmonary embolism is in obstructive shock: cold and clammy, confused, heart rate 130 bpm, and systolic blood pressure 78/46 mmHg (refractory to initial 500 ml fluid challenge). According to ESC and British Thoracic Society guidelines, what is the immediate life-saving treatment of choice?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/pulmonary-embolism/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Systemic Intravenous Fibrinolysis (e.g. Alteplase 100mg IV infusion over 2 hours, or 0.6 mg/kg over 15 minutes in arrest)\n- **B.** Therapeutic oral apixaban 10mg BD alone\n- **C.** Subcutaneous enoxaparin 1.5 mg/kg once daily\n- **D.** Surgical pleurodesis\n- **E.** IV Furosemide 80mg bolus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHigh-risk (Massive) Pulmonary Embolism is defined by hemodynamic instability: sustained hypotension (systolic BP <90 mmHg or a drop >=40 mmHg for >15 minutes), obstructive shock, or cardiac arrest. The treatment of choice is immediate systemic thrombolysis (IV Alteplase / rtPA 100 mg over 2 hours, or 50 mg bolus in cardiac arrest) to rapidly dissolve the central clot and relieve right ventricular outflow obstruction.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic 12-lead ECG manifestation of acute Right Ventricular (RV) strain associated with severe Pulmonary Embolism, although present in only 15-20% of cases?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/pulmonary-embolism/figure_03_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** S1Q3T3 pattern (deep S wave in Lead I, prominent Q wave in Lead III, and inverted T wave in Lead III) with T-wave inversions in V1-V4\n- **B.** Left bundle branch block with LAD\n- **C.** Pathological Q waves in leads I and aVL\n- **D.** Short PR interval with delta waves\n- **E.** Delta waves and widespread ST elevation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic McGinn-White sign of acute cor pulmonale / RV strain: deep S wave in Lead I, prominent Q wave in Lead III, and T-wave inversion in Lead III (S1Q3T3). More commonly, sinus tachycardia is the most frequent finding (40%), along with T-wave inversions in the right precordial leads (V1-V4) and incomplete or complete RBBB.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhich diagnostic imaging modality is the investigation of choice for suspected acute Pulmonary Embolism in a patient with severe contrast allergy or severe renal failure (eGFR <30 ml/min)?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Diagnosis & Management)](/lessons/images/sba/vascular/pulmonary-embolism/figure_04_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Ventilation-Perfusion (V/Q) Lung Scintigraphy (SPECT or planar)\n- **B.** Standard Contrast CTPA\n- **C.** Plain chest radiograph alone\n- **D.** Barium swallow\n- **E.** Coronary angiography\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nVentilation-Perfusion (V/Q) scintigraphy is the preferred diagnostic modality for pulmonary embolism when iodinated contrast is contraindicated (severe renal impairment, severe contrast anaphylaxis) or in young pregnant women (substantially lower maternal breast radiation dose compared to CTPA). A normal V/Q scan reliably rules out PE; a high-probability scan confirms PE.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the primary indication for emergency insertion of an Inferior Vena Cava (IVC) Filter in a patient with acute confirmed proximal deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism?\n\n- **A.** Absolute contraindication to anticoagulation (e.g. active major intracranial bleeding or planned emergency surgery), or recurrent PE despite optimal therapeutic anticoagulation\n- **B.** Routine primary prophylaxis for all patients with PE\n- **C.** Mild calf DVT in an ambulatory patient\n- **D.** Superficial thrombophlebitis\n- **E.** All patients over 65 years of age\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAccording to NICE and ESC guidelines, IVC filter insertion is indicated ONLY for: (1) Patients with acute VTE (DVT or PE) who have an absolute contraindication to therapeutic anticoagulation (e.g. recent major intracranial haemorrhage, severe active internal bleeding, impending surgery); OR (2) Documented recurrent VTE despite proven therapeutic anticoagulation.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
