n.\n- Please note - in the exam picture can be different. 23 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 23:\nA 41-year-old male brought to the ED after a fall from height. you suspect neurogenic shock. Which of the following features will support your diagnosis?\n- **A)** Increased heart rate\n- **B)** Hypertension x\n- **C)** Increased vagal tone\n- **D)** Increased in body temperature\n- **E)** LOSS Of vasomotor activity Explanation\n- Neurogenic shock is a devastating consequence of spinal cord injury (SCI). It manifests as hypotension, bradyarrhythmia, and temperature dysregulation due to peripheral vasodilatation (due to loss of vasomotor activity) following an injury to the spinal cord.\n- Vasomotor refers to actions upon a blood vessel which alter its diameter. More specifically, it can refer to vasodilator action and vasoconstrictor action.\n- Neurogenicshogk\n- Neurogenic shock occurs with CNS or spinal cord injury that probably occurs in less than 20% of spinal cord—injured patients\n- Loss of peripheral sympathetic innervation results in extreme vasodilatation secondary to loss of sympathetic arterial tone. If the Tl through T4 cord levels are compromised, loss of sympathetic innervation to the heart leaves unopposed vagal parasympathetic cardiac innervation eventually leads to bradycardia or an absence of reflex tachycardia.\n- General features: Patients with neurogenic shock are warm, peripherally vasodilated, and hypotensive with a relative bradycardia. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Cive lv fluid along with vasopressors as Pt does not respond to IV fluid alone.\n- Neurogenic shock usually presents with hypovolemia so moderate fluid resuscitation should be given\n- Cive atropine if Pt has significant bradycardia so / 90 You have not answered this question —\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 50:\nA 35-years-old motorcyclist brought to the ED following road traffic collision