s (L4—S1) forming the sciatic nerve.\n- An 1.4/1.5 disc prolapse will tend to cause pressure on LS nerve root. Compression of IS nerve root presents with weakness of extension of great toe, decreased sensation in the first web space. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC An L5/S1 disc prolapse will tend to cause pressure on Sl nerve root. Compression of Sl nerve root presents with diminished sensation of the lateral small toe, impaired planter flexion, decreased or absent ankle jerk. The pain can have a sudden or slow onset, and vary in severity. It is felt in the back or buttock and radiates down the leg below the knee into the foot and toes in the distribution of the sciatic nerve.\n- Other symptoms include tingling or numbness and loss of muscle strength in the same leg.\n- Alternative names for sciatica include 'radicular pain', 'lumbar radiculopathy', and 'lumbosacral radicular syndrome\n- herniated intervertebral disc ('slipped disc') — about 90% of cases. This most commonly occurs at the L5/S1 level.The most common cause is age- related degenerative changes, but rarely it can be caused by trauma.\n- Spondylolisthesis —when a proximal vertebra moves forward relative to a distal vertebra.\n- Spinal stenosis —narrowing of the central and/or lateral spinal canals (typically causes pain, which is relieved by sitting and forward flexion and exacerbated with standing and lumbar extension).\n- Infection (rare) —for example, discitis, vertebral osteomyelitis, or spinal epidural abscess.\n- Cancer (rare) —more often due to metastatic disease of the spine than a primary tumour.\n- Breast, prostate, and lung cancer are responsible for more than of cases of metastatic bone disease and the spine is the most common site of bone metastasis.\n- Qi k\n- Smoking.\n- Obesity.\n- Occupational factors. o Whole body vibration — for example due to driving or operating machinery. The use of vibrating equipment is considered particularly hazardous. o Strenuous physical activity — for example, frequent heavy lifting, especially while bending and twisting.\n- General health and comorbidities.\n- Older age — peak incidence is in people aged 45—64 years.\n- Cen etic influences httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Paper\n- Clinical features - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- sharp pain, starting in the lower back, then shooting down the leg (most common)\n- constant pain in only one side of the buttock or leg, but rarely in both sides\n- pain that originates in the low back or buttock and continues along the path of the sciatic nerve down the back of the thigh and into the lower leg and foot\n- pain that feels better when patients lie down or are walking, but worsens when standing or sitting a "pins-and-needles" sensation, numbness or weakness, or a prickling sensation down the leg in some cases\n- weakness or numbness when moving the leg or foot\n- severe or shooting pain in one leg, making it difficult to stand up or walk\n- Red_flags Of sciatica Bowel/bladder dysfunction (most commonly urinary retention). Progressive neurological weakness. Saddle anaesthesia.\n- Bilateral radiculopathy.\n- Incapacitating pain.\n- Unrelenting night pain.\n- Use of steroids or intravenous drugs. may-overlap-with-sciatica-include;\n- Investigations\n- Take a medical history and conduct a focused musculoskeletal and neurological examination\n- Do not routinely X-ray the spine to confirm the diagnosis. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC Consider appropriate investigations if infection, malignancy, or inflammatory conditions are suspected — this may include:\n- FBC\n- ESR\n- C- reactive protein\n- Blood culture\n- Urinalysis and culture\n- ReferraLpathway_\n- If there are red flag symptoms and signsthat may suggest a serious underlying cause, admit or refer urgently for specialist assessment using clinical judgement.\n- Cauda equina syndrome.\n- Acute spinal cord compression: new/progressive widespread neurological deficit. progressive limb weakness including major motor radiculopathy.\n- There is suspicion of tumour or infection.\n- Red flags are present in the absence of neurological dysfunction.\n- Other_a" patients without red flags\n- Offer analgesia to manage low back NSAIDs first line Do not offer opioids for managing chronic sciatica.\n- Do not offer gabapentinoids, other antiepileptics, oral corticosteroids, or benzodiazepines for managing sciatica. Selfcare advise\n- Sciatica symptoms usually settle within a few weeks to a few months\n- Prolonged bed rest is not recommended.\n- Application of local heat (ensuring that the skin is protected) may relieve pain and have a muscle relaxing effect. Keeping as active as possible and exercising regularly is important to reduce the risk of recurrence. 8/ 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 23-year-old young female came to the ED with complaint of generalized fatigue and dizziness. She g\n- ves history of separation from her husband three months ago. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC According to SAD person score what is the cause of her symptoms? A\n- Separation and divorce\n- **B)** Anxiety\n- **C)** Mania\n- **D)** Infection\n- **E)** Depression Explanatign\n- According to modified SAD PERSONS scale, separation would be the cause of her condition.\n- The Modified SAD PERSONS SCALE\n- The 'sad persons scale' is an attempt to assist non- psychiatrists assess suicide risk. It may help as a guide regarding the need for referral or admission. S D p R Sex male Age or Depression/hopelessness Previous attempt/psychiatric care Excessive alcohol or drug use Rational thinking loss Separated/divorced/widowed Organised or serious attempt No social support Stated future 