OO cells/microliter, hematopoietic cell transplant https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC patients, solid-organ transplant patients, or patients on chronic immunosuppressive therapy.\n- Risk factor HIV-positive patients with a CD4 cell count QOO cells/microliter\n- immunocompromised state\n- chronic corticosteroid therapy\n- A history of previous pneumocystispneumonia\n- Fever\n- Dry cough\n- Dyspnoea\n- Fatigue\n- Normal chest examination\n- Weight loss\n- Tachypnea or respiratory distress\n- Lnyestigations\n- CXR - bilateral symmetrical interstitial infiltrates. Reticular or granular opacities that are typically perihilar but may be diffuse. Less often they are asymmetric or progress to a reticular-alveolar pattern.\n- Arterial blood gas\n- PCR\n- Serum LDH level - elevated (>220 international units/ L)\n- Induced sputum - positive for pneumocystis\n- Consider HRCT\n- Consider pulmonary function test\n- Management\n- First line - Co-Trimoxazole is a combination of two different antibiotics called sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim. Consider corticosteroid in selected patients pentamidine or atovaquone or clindamycin 14 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 14:\nA 24-year-old male presented to the ED with right shoulder pain. His arm is internally rotated, and adducted X-ray is given. You want to perform close reduction; how would you do that?\n- **A)** Abduct the arm to 1800 With simultaneous pressure on humeral head https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- **B)** Patient supine grasp the affected arm at wrist and lift gently vertical\n- **C)** Patient in prone affected arm hanging down with IOLbs weight applied to wrist\n- **D)** Flex the elbow at 900 then slowly externally rotate arm\n- **E)** Apply traction and external rotation to affected arm at 900 flexion at elbow Explanation\n- Posterior should dislocation can be reduce with traction, countertraction method and pep.a.l.ma method\n- Traction, countertraction method-apply traction and external rotation to affected arm at 90 degrees of flexion at elbow. The physician applies traction and assistant applies countertraction\n- Pepalma-methpd-=where the effected arm is first adducted and internally rotated, with caudal traction applied. Then, maintaining traction and internal rotation, the medial aspect of the upper arm is pushed laterally, disengaging the humeral head from the glenoid fossa. Finally the arm is extended, and the humerus falls back into place. / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 15:\nA 25-year-old male brought to the ED after falling from a bike. He is complaining of pain in the right leg. His X-ray is given. What type of fracture is it?\n- **A)** Pilon fracture B\n- Maisonneuve fracture\n- **C)** Denis Weber type B fracture\n- **D)** Denis Weber type A\n- **E)** Fibular fracture Explanatign X-ray shows widening of the distal tibio-fibular joint due to medial ankle ligament rupture (indicative of https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC a rupture of the syndesmosis and interosseous membrane) and proximal fibula fracture.\n- Maisonneuve fracture Mow Maisonneuve fracture results from an external rotation force applied to the foot. Injury starts at the medial ankle as either a deltoid ligament rupture or a medial malleolus injury. The injury then directed upward and laterally, tearing the interosseous membrane that tethers the distal tibia to the fibula.\n- The third component of this injury is a fracture of the proximal fibula\n- Clinical-features_ankle pain, swelling, bruising and restricted range of motion PiagnQSi$_request an ankle x-ray, however further X-rays of the proximal fibula should be performed.\n- Management: surgical intervention required for this injury to reduce and stabilize the fractured medial malleolus and to secure the fibula to the distal tibia, allowing the ruptured interosseous membrane to heal. 16 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 16:\nA 12-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complaint of non-blanching leg rash. His mother reports that he has sore throat two-week ago. What investigation should be done to support your diagnosis?\n- **A)** FBC\n- **B)** Urinalysis\n- **C)** ASOT   D\n- Blood Culture Explanation\n- usually the only investigation needed in a classic presentation of HSP.\n- HenochSchönleimpurpura-(HSP)\n- HSP is the most common vasculitis of childhood. It most commonly affects children 2-8 years of age\n- Recent upper respiratory tract infection is present in 50% of HSP cases, commonly viral or Croup A streptococcus infections https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- The features include rash, and one or more of:\n- Arthritis/arthralgia (50-75%)\n- Abdominal pain (50%)\n- Nephritis (25-50%)\n- Investigations\n- Urinalysis -is usually the only investigation needed in a classic presentation of HSP\n- If urinalysis is abnormal i-e If there is hypertension, macroscopic haematuria or significant proteinuria then perform urinary protein-creatinine ratio albumin.\n- Other investigations — FBC, blood or urine culture, to rule out differentials if the diagnosis is unclear (eg ITP, leukaemia, meningococcal infection).\n- Subcutaneous oedema is managed with bed rest and elevation of the affected area\n- Regular paracetamol and a short course of NSAIDs such as ibuprofen (IO mg/kg TDS) for pain relief.\n- Consider the use of steroids (glucocorticoids) in children with moderate to severe abdominal and joint pain (may reduce duration of symptoms).\n- Arrange regular follow-up to identify subsequent renal involvement which rarely requires a renal biopsy +/- immunosuppression.\n- Key_points\n- Urinalysis and blood pressure measurement must be done when Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) is suspected Most cases are self-limiting and only require symptomatic management\n- Close follow-up is critical to identify significant renal involvement requiring intervention. Renal involvement is usually asymptomatic. 17 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 17:\nA 22-year-old young male with history of Upper respiratory tract infection presented to the ED with complaint of fever, sharp pleuritic chest pain and palpitations. Severity of pain increases with lying flat and pain decreases when he sits upright. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Pneumonia\n- **B)** Acute pericarditis httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper\n- **C)** Rheumatic fever\n- **D)** Myocardial infarction E\n- Myocarditis Explanation - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- pericarditis is most commonly idiopathic however it can be caused virus, bacteria, post-MI, trauma, SLE and neoplasia.\n- Clinical features of acute pericarditis\n- The clinical presentation is usually one of acute onset of retrosternal chest pain which is pleuritic in nature and which is worse on coughing and lying flat and eased by sitting up and leaning forward. A characteristic feature of the pain which is specific for pericarditis is radiation to the trapezius ridge g Miteria\n- Characteristic chest pain (retrosternal, pleuritic)\n- Pericardial friction rub Suggestive ECG changes (diffuse ST elevation, typically concave up and depression of the PR segment New or worsening pericardial effusion.\n- Additional features: fever and raised CRP &ESR 18 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 18:\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation on a patient who require neuromuscular blocking agent suxamethonium. Suxamethonium causes prolong paralysis by what mechanism?\n- **A)** Acting on NMDA receptors B\n- Channel blocker\n- **C)** Induce the activity Of cholinesterase\n- **D)** Cholinesterase inhibitor\n- **E)** channel blocker Explanation;\n- Sux