— retinitis.\n- Conjunctiva — conjunctivitis.\n- Eyelids — blepharitis.\n- Surrounding skin — periocular dermatitis\n- Clinical features\n- Symptgms\n- Malaise and fever\n- Eye pain\n- Eye irritation or photophobia\n- Eye watering\n- Blurred vision\n- An acute red eye\n- Crops of vesicles and ulcers on an erythematous base, or pustules along the lid margin or periocular skin\n- A hazy cornea\n- A fixed irregular pupil\n- Reduced corneal sensitivity\n- Reduced visual acuity suggests keratitis\n- Refer all cases of suspected ocular herpes simplex infection to eye casualty, or an emergency eye service for same-day assessment and specialist management.\n- if emergency same-day assessment is not possible or practical, seek specialist advice from an ophthalmologist regarding initiating drug treatment such as topical antivirals ( aciclovir ointment or ganciclovir gel) Acute Herpes Simplex: in non-contact lens wearing adults and where HSK is confined to the epithelium with classic dendritic appearance, commence antiviral therapy with one of the following:\n- oc. aciclovir3%, e.g. Aciclovir Agepha, ophthalmic preparation (unpreserved)\n- ganciclovir 0.15% ophthalmic gel\n- Complications\n- Corneal scarring and visual impairment httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Paper\n- Corneal perforation\n- Secondary glaucoma\n- Infection - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 41 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 41:\nA 37-year-old welder presented to the ED with a history of redness and foreign body sensation in the left eye. his eye image is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Retrobulbar haemorrhage\n- **B)** Scleral penetrating injury\n- **C)** Corneal abrasion   E\n- Clobe rupture Explanatign Clobe Rupture\n- is a traumatic injury involving the breakdown in the integrity of the wall of the eye, either the sclera or cornea or both\n- blunt trauma, projectile injury, laceration of eyelid/periorbital area, metal on metal, high-spe