 $8) require intubation. - Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep paC02 at 4.5 to 5kpa.\n- Hyperventilation will lower ICP in deterioration Pt with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy.\n- ED management of serious head injury\n- Initial_management;\n- Follow ABC protocol The primary goals of treatment are to maintain cerebral perfusion and oxygenation by optimizing intravascular volume and ventilation.\n- Recognize and treat elevated ICP\n- Airway & breathing\n- Treat any condition that compromises ventilation (e.g., altered mental status, facial/neck trauma, pneumothorax).\n- Patients with severe injury (CCS score of s8) require intubation. provide aggressive fluid resuscitation to prevent hypotension and secondary brain injury. Normal saline is recommended for volume resuscitation.\n- Maintain systolic blood pressure at 2100 mm Hg for patients 50 to 69 years Old or at 2110 mm Hg for patients IS to 49 years old or >70 years old Pain-G.Q.ntr.Q.LCive titrated IV opioid analgesia https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- P.a.tienrpqsirioning\n- Raising the head Of the bed to 30 degrees/sit-up may improve cerebral blood flow by lowering ICP.\n- Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep pac02 at to 5kpa. Hyperventilation will lower ICP in deterioration pt with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy.\n- Mannitol is osmotic diuretic.it is used to decrease ICP\n- Avoid mannitol in hypotensive pts\n- Hypertonic saline is alternative to mannitol in the patient who is not adequately fluid resuscitated or hypotensive.\n-\n- prolonged seizures can worsen secondary injury.\n- Treat acute seizures with midazolam or lorazepam, and if seizures continue, treat as for status epilepticus.\n- hypothermia\n- Gluggse_ggntrgLmaintain at 100 to milligrams/dl_ or 5.55 to 9.99 mmol/L 45 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 45:\nA 43-year-old presented to the ED with upper GI bleed. Which of the following is not included in Glasgow Blatchford Score ? A Stroke B\n- Hemoglobin\n- **C)** Syncope D\n- Hepatic disease\n- **E)** Heart failure Explanation\n- Glasgow-:ßlatGhfor.d-Sqqr.e https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC The Glasgow-Blatchford score is the risk assessment tool of choice in the emergency department.\n- It is calculated using FOUR component:\n- Medical history (hepatic disease, heart failure),\n- Symptoms (melaena, syncope),\n- Signs (tachycardia, blood pressure),\n- Blood tests (haemoglobin, urea).\n- The maximum score is 29.\n- NICE recommends that patients with a Blatchford score Of O can be considered for early discharge with outpatient endoscopy follow up. Risk marker Haemoglobin (g/dL) for men Haemoglobin (g/dL for women Systolic blood pressure (mm H g) Other markers Range 6.5- 7.5 8.0-9.9 10.0 - 24.9 >250 12-12.9 10.0- 11.9 €10.0 10.0-11.9 10.0 100-109 90_99 Pulse >100 Melaena Syncope Hepatic disease Cardiac failure 6 6 46 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 46:\nA 23-year-old young male presented to the ED with complaint of chest pain associated with short of breath. Initial management started and CTPA was done which showed Pulmonary emboli. Patient is fit with no significant bleeding risk. What is next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Alteplase\n- **B)** LMWH\n- **C)** Dexamethasone\n- **D)** Unfractioned Heparin\n- **E)** Refer to cardiopulmonary center httpsumrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- If PE is identified by CTPA, WQ SPECT or WQ planar scan:\n- offer or continue anticoagulation treatment (DOAC - apixaban or rivaroxaban, if not suitable then LMVVH) at least 3 months to people with confirmed proximal DVT or PE.\n- if anticoagulation treatment is contraindicated, consider a mechanical intervention (Inferior vena caval filters, Elastic graduated compression stockings, percutaneous mechanical thrombectomy). 47 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 47:\nA one-week-old baby girl brought to the ED by her mother. She is pyretic, floppy and unresponsive to stimuli. You suspect neonatal sepsis. How would you take sample in this baby?\n- **A)** Send baby to NICU\n- **B)** Amount of sample should be more than Icc\n- **C)** urine for microscopy\n- **D)** No need of Sample in this child\n- **E)** From both sides of hand to do culture\n- The general rule of sending two to three blood culture sets from different sites in a period of 24 hours\n- Aim to collect 1 mL of blood: ; the accuracy of the result correlates with the volume of blood collected. Invert bottles gently several times to prevent clotting.\n- Investigations-before-start-of-anti>iotigs-in late-onset-neonatal-sepsis before 1 dose of antibiotic Measureereagtiyeprotein when starting antibiotic treatment and also during antibiotic treatment, 1B to 24 hours after presentation\n- If it safe then perform LP to obtain CSF before and during antibiotic treatment when: there is a strong clinical suspicion of early-onset neonatal infection or sign of meningitis\n- Note: don't perform urine or skin swab microscopy or culture routinely in late-onset of neonatal sepsis. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, Paper\n- Key_points - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- Blood culture remains the 'gold standard' for the detection of microbial pathogens. Optimal recovery of pathogens depends on culturing an adequate volume of blood and using the right technique\n- Aim to collect 1 mL of blood: ; the accuracy Of the result correlates with the volume of blood collected. Invert bottles gently several times to prevent clotting.\n- The general rule of sending two to three blood culture sets from different sites in a period of 24 hours\n- Current recommendation with regard to timing and interval include obtaining two sets within minutes apart from two distinct sites at the onset of the 24 hour period with two subsequent sets being taking at different time intervals over the 24 hour period if the clinical condition deteriorates.\n- Use aseptic technique including:\n- Skin preparation\n- Hand hygiene\n- Using sterile gloves by the performer Wiping blood culture bottle stopper with the alcohol wipe\n- Using sterile gloves by the helper while injecting blood into the culture bottle\n- Always collect blood culture from the hub of a newly inserted cannula\n- While collecting blood for multiple blood tests, always collect blood culture first in a separate syringe\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 48:\nA 28-year-old male brought into the ED by paramedics with drowsiness. On examination, his RR is 10 breath per minutes, HR 45 bpm and his pupils are abnormally small. What is most likely the cause of his condition? A\n- Opioid overdose\n- **B)** Alcohol overdose\n- **C)** Cannabis over use\n- **D)** Antidepressant toxicity\n- **E)** MDMA Explanation\n- Patient clinical picture suggests Opioid toxicity. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025, paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC\n- opioid-Q.yerdose-(na.r.GQtic-a.nalgesics)\n- Respiratory depression\n- Pinpoint pupil\n- Coma\n- Naloxone-hydrochloride\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma.\n- When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion adjusted according to vital signs. 49 / 90\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 49:\nA 1 g-year-old female with history of asthma presents to the ED with increasing shortness Of breath and wheeze. She has been using increasing amounts of her salbutamol inhaler Over the past few days. She is unable to talk on full sentences and has widespread wheeze throughout her chest on auscultation. Her observations are: RR 35 breaths/min, HR 130 bpm sp02 91 Her ABCs is given How severe is this patient's asthma?\n- **A)** Moderate asthma\n- **B)** Near fatal asthma\n- **C)** Mild asthma\n- **D)** Acute severe asthma\n- **E)** Life-threatening asthma Explanation\n- Any patient with features of acute severe asthma along with any one feature of life-threatening asthma (as in above scenario his saturation is below ) would be classify as life-threatening asthma. https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/\n\n20/11/2025,  paper - II I MRCEM EXPERT LLC 01 P" "-SOS to Am with sp02 with infla t   https\n- ]/mrcemexpert.com/courses/mrcem-intermediate-sba-diet-111esson/paper-ii-5/ 107/107\n	\N	[]	\N	f	66	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	\N
cbb4fa7b-20fa-4364-b499-284210922b10	ce292af3-8b02-477b-92ab-cfa288a8e275	insulin-and-glucagon	Insulin and Glucagon	article	Insulin\nInsulin is a polypeptide hormone secreted by the β  cells of\nthe pancreatic islets of Langerhans, containing an A chain\nand a B chain joined by disulfide bridges.\nProinsulin is synthesized as a single chain peptide. Within\nstorage granules, a connecting peptide (c peptide) is\nremoved by proteases to yield insulin. The c peptide is\npackaged and secreted along with insulin, and its\nconcentration is used to monitor beta cell function in\ndiabetic patients who are receiving exogenous insulin\nRegulation of insulin secretion\nGlucose is a major factor that regulates insulin secretion.\nInsulin is released from the beta cells primarily in response to\nthe elevated blood glucose levels and increased\nparasympathetic stimulation associated with digestion of a\nmeal. Increased blood levels of certain amino acids also\nstimulate insulin secretion.\nDecreased insulin secretion results from decreasing blood\nglucose levels and from stimulation of the pancreas by the\nsympathetic division of the nervous system, which occurs\nduring physical activity. Decreased insulin levels allow blood\nglucose to be conserved to provide the brain with adequate\nglucose and to allow other tissues to metabolize fatty acids\nand glycogen stored in the cells.\n\nFunction of insulin\nInsulin can be considered the "anabolic"or building hormone it\nassists processes that build compounds for storage and\ndecrease processes that break down those storage reserves.\nIn muscle and liver, insulin increases glycogenesis and\ndecreases glycogenolysis.\nDecreased gluconeogenesis in the liver.\nIncreased glycolysis in liver and adipose tissue.\nDecreased the breakdown of amino acids in the liver.\nIncreased amino acid uptake and protein synthesis in\nmuscle, liver, and adipose tissue (decrease blood amino acid\nconcentration)\nDecreased lipolysis (decrease blood fatty acid and ketoacid\nconcentration)\nIncreased lipogenesis and esterification of fatty acids in the\nliver and adipose tissue.\ninsulin increase K+ uptake into cells, thereby decreasing\nblood K+\nGlucagon\nGlucagon is produced by α  cells, located peripherally with in\nislets of Langerhans, in the endocrine tissue of pancreas.\nRegulation of glucagon secretion\nThe major factor that regulates glucagon secretion is the\nblood glucose concentration. Decreased blood glucose\nstimulates glucagon secretion.\n\n![Insulin and Glucagon - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/insulin-and-glucagon/figure_01_p2.png)\n*Insulin and Glucagon — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nIncreased blood amino acids stimulate glucagon secretion,\nwhich prevents hypoglycemia caused by unopposed insulin\nin response to a high protein meal.\nRegulation of glucagon secretion\nFunction of glucagon\nGlucagon acts on the liver and adipose tissue.\nThe second messenger for glucagon is CAMP.\nEffects of Glucagon:\nIncreased glycogenolysis\nDecreased glycogenesis.\nIncreased gluconeogenesis\nIncreased ketogenesis\nincreases lipolysis in adipose tissue.\nDifference between Insulin and Glucagon\n\n![Insulin and Glucagon - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/insulin-and-glucagon/figure_02_p3.png)\n*Insulin and Glucagon — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Insulin and Glucagon - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/insulin-and-glucagon/figure_03_p4.png)\n*Insulin and Glucagon — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
