079b7218-afa8-4d07-9201-5d6d8784ef66	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-25	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 25	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 25\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	25	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
5bc2c3fa-1c21-4e53-9186-7e0bbea4670d	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-hyperkalemic-cardiac-arrest	Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest Protocols — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest Protocols — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 55-year-old haemodialysis patient collapses in PEA cardiac arrest. The rhythm on the monitor shows a wide, bizarre, undulating sinusoidal wave ('sine wave' pattern) at 35 bpm. In addition to high-quality CPR and adrenaline, what specific emergency intravenous bolus therapy must be administered immediately?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest Protocols)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyperkalemic-cardiac-arrest/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous 10% Calcium Chloride (10ml IV push, repeated after 5 minutes if rhythm persists)\n- **B.** Intravenous Amiodarone 300mg\n- **C.** Intravenous Magnesium Sulphate 2g\n- **D.** Defibrillation at 200J biphasic immediately\n- **E.** Intravenous Atropine 3mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest or periarrest 'sine wave' rhythm, intravenous calcium is the single most urgent life-saving antidote. 10ml of 10% Calcium Chloride IV bolus (or 30ml 10% Calcium Gluconate) stabilizes the cardiac cell membrane within 1-2 minutes by restoring electrical resting potential. The dose can be repeated every 5-10 minutes if PEA or wide complexes persist.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nDuring active cardiac arrest resuscitation attributed to severe hyperkalaemia, what dose of Sodium Bicarbonate is recommended by Resuscitation Council UK guidelines to shift potassium and treat metabolic acidaemia?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest Protocols)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyperkalemic-cardiac-arrest/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 50 ml of 8.4% Sodium Bicarbonate IV (50 mmol) by rapid bolus\n- **B.** 10 ml of 1.26% Sodium Bicarbonate\n- **C.** 1000 ml of 8.4% Sodium Bicarbonate over 24 hours\n- **D.** Oral sodium bicarbonate tablets\n- **E.** Sodium bicarbonate is contraindicated in cardiac arrest\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn cardiac arrest caused by hyperkalaemia (or tricyclic antidepressant toxicity), 50 ml of 8.4% Sodium Bicarbonate (50 mmol) IV bolus is indicated. Alkalinisation drives hydrogen ions out of cells in exchange for extracellular potassium entering cells, reducing serum potassium and ameliorating severe metabolic acidosis.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nIn hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest, how should the standard insulin-dextrose shift therapy be administered while chest compressions are ongoing?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest Protocols)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyperkalemic-cardiac-arrest/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 10 units of soluble rapid-acting insulin (e.g. Actrapid) with 50ml of 50% Dextrose given as a rapid IV bolus\n- **B.** Subcutaneous insulin 20 units\n- **C.** 5% Dextrose infusion at 50 ml/hr without insulin\n- **D.** Wait until return of spontaneous circulation before giving insulin\n- **E.** Insulin is only given via central line\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nDuring active cardiac arrest from hyperkalaemia, 10 units of regular soluble insulin with 50 ml of 50% dextrose (or 100 ml 20% dextrose) is given as a rapid IV bolus to accelerate intracellular potassium shift. CPR ensures distribution through the central circulation.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat advanced life support intervention provides the definitive, physical removal of potassium from the blood during refractory hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest or prolonged periarrest resuscitation?\n\n- **A.** Emergency Haemodialysis / Continuous Veno-Venous Haemofiltration (CVVH) with Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO / ECPR)\n- **B.** Oral ion-exchange resins administered via nasogastric tube\n- **C.** Intravenous furosemide boluses\n- **D.** Surgical bilateral nephrectomy\n- **E.** Therapeutic hypothermia to 28C\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCalcium, insulin-dextrose, bicarbonate, and beta-agonists are temporary temporizing measures that only shift potassium intracellularly; they do not remove potassium from the body. Definitive elimination in anuric renal failure requires emergency Haemodialysis or CRRT. In refractory arrest, Extracorporeal Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (ECPR/ECMO) coupled with dialysis has demonstrated survival rates >75% with full neurological recovery.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhy must Calcium Chloride and Sodium Bicarbonate NEVER be infused together through the same intravenous cannula simultaneously without a thorough flush?\n\n- **A.** They react chemically to form an insoluble white precipitate of Calcium Carbonate (chalk) that can occlude vascular lines and cause embolic complications\n- **B.** They cause severe explosive boiling of the solution\n- **C.** They cancel out each other's metabolic effects completely\n- **D.** They cause immediate hyperthermia\n- **E.** They trigger severe anaphylactic shock\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCalcium salts (CaCl2) and Sodium Bicarbonate (NaHCO3) must never be mixed in the same IV line or syringe. They immediately react to form Calcium Carbonate (CaCO3), an insoluble, milky white crystalline precipitate (chalk) that precipitates in the lumen, blocks lines, and causes vascular embolisation. Always flush the line thoroughly with saline between injections.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9f67369f-4a5f-414d-8159-da45c7221e81	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	hypernatremia	Hypernatraemia Emergency Management	article	# Hypernatraemia Emergency Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nHyperaldosteronism\n-\n- Primary (Conn's) Secondary (CCF, nephrotic syndrome, steroids)\n-\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nSigns of hypernatraemia usually involve central nervous system (CNS) manifestations and includes: irritability, restlessness,\n- muscle twitching,\n- spasticity, and hyperreflexia,\n- All above sign are due to a decreased water content in the brain.\n- in hypernatraemia (volume depletion) dry mucous membranes poor skin turgor\n- sunken eyes\n- irritability, tachycardia, hypotension or postural hypotension,\n- decreased urine output, and weight loss\n- severe diarrhoea, vomiting, and significant burns should be excluded.\n- Temperature rneasurement is essential because fever may be the cause.\n-\n- Sign-and-symptoms in patient-with-hypovolaemic hypernagraemia-(yolume overload) Usually due to exogenous sodium ingestion and mineralocorticoid\n- excess.Therefore, signs of Cushing's syndrome as well as primary aldosteronism should be sought. weight gain\n- peripheral oedema\n-\n\nhypertension irritating cough\n- dyspnoea Jugular vein distention, and crepitations on auscultation\n- hypertension, proximal muscle weakness.(due to Primary aldosteronism) Moon face, facial plethora, supraclavicular and/or dorsicocervical fat pad (due to\n- cushing's syndrome). Euvolaemic hypernatraemia\n- Signs of volume depletion and overload absent. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus or central diabetes insipidus are usually the\n- underlying cause\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nU&Es, Mg, bicarbonate, LFTs, glucose Urine osmolality\n- Serum osmolality\n-\n- Urine electrolytes\n- Urine flow rate Consider desmopressin challenge test\n- Consider MRI or CT brain\n- Evaluation of hypernatraemia using_paired serum and urine osmolality\n-\n\n- Urine osmolality < serum osmolality Indicates urinary concentrating defect Causes: central DI, nephrogenic DI, renal disease, osmotic diuresis Urine osmolality\n- serum osmolality Indicates intact urinary concentration Causes: gastrointestinal losses, increased insensible losses eg burns, excess sodium intake Note: normal Serum osmolality between 275—295 mOsmoI/kg\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nFirst line\n- Severe-hypernatraemia may present with serious signs and symptoms, such as hyperthermia, delirium, seizures, and coma, prompting more urgent treatment of the condition.\n- Initial treatment relies on stabilising hypovolaemic patients using isotonic saline\n- Once the patient is stable, the free water deficit can be corrected orally or with intravenous fluids over 48 hours. (half in 24hr)\n- Free water deficit may be calculated using the following equation:\n- Free water deficit = body weight (kg) x percentage of total body weight (0.6 for men; 0.4 for women) x ([serum Na/140] — 1).\n- Euy.ola.emi.c-pa.ti.e.n.ts_can be treated with hypotonic fluids such as 5% dextrose water to correct the free water deficit.\n- Hypervolaemic patients may require diuretics (thiazide) in addition to hypotonic fluid correction.\n\n- To avoid cerebral oedema the serum sodium should not be corrected any faster than 0.5 mmol/L (0.5 mEq/L) every hour.\n- Hypernatraemia resulting from a hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS) in people with diabetes requires urgent correction due to the serious nature of the disease state.\n- Breastfeeding-associated hypernatraemia is a medical emergency. Infants are predisposed to dehydration due to their small total body water stores and relatively large evaporative water loss potential. Lack of maternal breast-milk production without supplementation, or trouble with infant suckling, may quickly lead to severe dehydration and hypovolaemic hypernatraemia.\n- Both central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus are associated with plasma sodium concentration of\n- 170mmoI/L (170 mEq/L), necessitating urgent correction with intravenous hypotonic fluids (e.g., 5% dextrose water, quarter or half isotonic saline) because of the serious risk of cerebrovascular damage and death.\n- Consider thiazide in nephrogenic diabetic insipidus\n- consider desmopressin in central diabetic insipidus\n- Exogenous sodium ingestion — Infusion of intravenous 5% dextrose water combined with diuretic administration, necessary to remove excess sodium, is the mainstay of treatrnent.\n- Second line\n- Renal replacement therapy — Haemodialysis and haemofiltration may correct the sodium level too quickly\n- Complications\n- Cerebral bleeding, subarachnoid haemorrhage, permanent brain damage and death secondary to brain shrinkage with acute hypernatraemia.\n\n- Cerebral oedema with overfast correction of chronic hypernatraemia. Also convulsions and permanent brain injury may occur. previous\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Hypernatremia - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypernatremia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Hypernatremia — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Hypernatremia - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology-acid-base/hypernatremia/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n*Hypernatremia — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
