8bb14097-fa9a-40e5-80ca-0c6f9b8414dd	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-43	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 43	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 43\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	43	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7730f25c-27eb-4e86-a3fa-2f79bb240f8a	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-44	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 44	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 44\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	44	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f0f991f4-6301-45c6-a4a4-d73624c92cf9	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	thyroid-gland	Thyroid Gland	article	Thyroid gland\nThe thyroid gland is a ductless alveolar gland found in the\nanterior neck, just below the laryngeal prominence (Adam's\napple). It has two lobes wrapping around the trachea and\nconnected in the middle by an isthmus. It weighs about 20 t0\n40g in adults. Thyroid is larger in females than in males. The\nstructure and function of thyroid gland change in different\nstages of sexual cycle in females. Its function increases slightly\nduring pregnancy and lactation and decreases during\nmenopause.\nHormones of thyroid gland\nThe function of the thyroid gland is to produce and store\nthyroid hormones. Thyroid epithelia form follicles filled with\ncolloid a protein-rich reservoir of the materials needed for\nthyroid hormone production.\nIn the spaces between the follicles, parafollicular cells can be\nfound. These cells secrete calcitonin, which is involved in the\nregulation of calcium metabolism in the body.\n\n![Thyroid Gland - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/thyroid-gland/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Thyroid Gland — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nThyroid gland secretes three hormones:\nTetraiodothyronine or T4 (thyroxine)\nTriiodothyronine or T3\nCalcitonin.\nProduction and Transport of Thyroid Hormones\nThyroid tissue is made up of colloid which contains iodinated\nthyroglobulin. Thyroglobulin is synthesized by the\nsurrounding follicular cells and is the large molecule from\nwhich thyroid hormones are made.\nThe thyroid gland produces two thyroid hormones, thyroxine\n(T4) and triiodothyronine (T3). More T4 than T3 is synthesized,\nhowever T4 is converted peripherally to the more potent and\nshorter-acting T3.\nThyroid hormones are released as part of the hypothalamic-\npituitary-thyroid axis. The Hypothalamus detects a low\nplasma concentration of thyroid hormone and releases\nThyrotropin-Releasing Hormone (TRH) into the hypophyseal\nportal system.\nTRH binds to receptors found on thyrotrophic cells of the\nanterior pituitary gland, causing them to release Thyroid\nStimulating Hormone (TSH) into the systemic circulation. TSH\nbinds to TSH receptors on the basolateral membrane of\nthyroid follicular cells and induces the synthesis and release\nof thyroid hormone.\nMost of the thyroid hormones in the blood are bound tightly\nto proteins in the circulation-thyroxine-binding protein (TBG),\ntransthyretin and albumin and are thus unavailable to their\nreceptors which are located inside the target cells. Only the\nsmall amounts of free T3 and T4 in plasma can readily cross\nthe cell membranes to bind to thyroid hormone receptors.\nThyroid receptors are present in almost all tissues, with\nparticularly high levels in the liver.\nFunctions of thyroid hormones\nOnly free hormone is active. T3 is most active (5 times more\npotent than T4)\nFunctions of thyroid hormones (t3, t4)\n\nBrain maturation\nBone growth (synergism with GH)\nB-adrenergic effects. ↑ B ₁  receptors in heart→ ↑ CO, HR, SV,\ncontractility (B-blockers alleviate adrenergic symptoms in\nthyrotoxicosis)\nBasal metabolic rate ↑ (Via Na/K+-ATPase activity →  ↑    0 ₂\nconsumption, RR, body temperature\nBlood sugar (increase glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis)\nBreakdown of lipids increase lipolysis.\nStimulates surfactant synthesis in babies.	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
082be22b-a617-4275-a409-52514d1c7223	ef15ba2e-2dca-4639-9b94-5740ccb572f9	mock-iii-paper-ii	Mock 3 — Paper 2 (Clinical SBA Exam)	quiz	# Mock Iii Paper Ii\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Instructions:** Single best answer scenario. Read carefully before reviewing the detailed rationale.\n\nMrcem Primary Complete Course ,High Yield Stuff and Questions Bank ( For Diet ii Late 2026)\nOverview  Comments\nShare your score colleagues\n\nLinkedIn  Facebook  Twitter\n\n## Question 17 of 90\n\n1. A 45 Years old male suffered penetrating trauma to the\nparotid gland with facial nerve injury. Orbicularis oculi\nmuscle is affected and frontalis muscle is spared. Which of\nthe following terminal branch of the facial nerve is most\nlikely injured?\nNote: Skin of upper forehead, eye lid and\nconjunctiva are innervated by supraorbital nerve\n(RECALL) branch of Opthalmic division of\ntrigeminal nerve\n\n## Question 1 of 90\n\n- **A.** Temporal branch\n- **B.** Zygomatic branch\n- **C.** Marginal mandibular branch\n- **D.** Greater petrosal nerve\n- **E.** Buccal branch\n\n## Question 1 of 58\n\nSupratrochlear nerve:  Innervate skin of lower\ncentral forehead and conjunctiva.\n2. A 20 Years old girl presents to the ED with complaint of\nheadache, Palpitations and diaphoresis. After initial\nassessment you suspect pheochromocytoma. This clinical\ncondition occurs due to excess of which of the following\nhormones?\nPheochromocytoma is catecholamine secreting\ntumor arises from chromaffin cells of adrenal\nmedulla\nCatecholamines include adrenaline, nor adrenaline\nand dopamine.\nAdrenaline is predominant Harmone in\npheochromocytoma\n\n## Question 2 of 90\n\n- **A.** Oxytocin\n- **B.** ADH\n- **C.** Catecholamines\n- **D.** PTH\n- **E.** ACTH\n4. A 52 years old chronic smoker presented to the ED with\ncomplaint of sudden severe headache. After initial\nassessment and imaging revealed a ruptured berry\naneurysm. Which of the following blood vessel is most\nlikely location of the aneurysm?\nBerry (saccular) aneurysms are the most common\ntype of intracranial aneurysm, representing 90% of\ncerebral aneurysms. The most frequent location is\nthe anterior communicating artery (35%), followed\nby the internal carotid artery\nHigh Yield: Clinical effects of occlusion of cerebral\narteries\n\n## Question 4 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anterior communicating artery\n- **B.** Middle cerebral artery\n- **C.** Internal carotid artery\n- **D.** Posterior cerebellar artery\n- **E.** Anterior cerebellar artery\n\n## Question 2 of 58\n\n6. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with chest pain\nand the following ECG is taken. Which artery is most likely\nbe occluded? ￼\nECG demonstrates Acute Inferior Myocardial\nInfarction due to occlusion of RCA\nSt-elevation in inferior leads (II, III,aVF) with\nreciprocal ST-depression in I, aVL\nNote: Both inferior wall MI and posterior wall MI\noccurs due to occlusion of Right coronary artery.\n\n## Question 6 of 90\n\n- **A.** Obtuse marginal artery\n- **B.** Right coronary artery\n- **C.** left main coronary artery\n- **D.** Left circumflex\n- **E.** Left anterior descending\n7. The probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when it is\ntrue;\nIn statistical hypothesis testing, a type1 error is the\nmistaken rejection of an actually true null\n\n## Question 7 of 90\n\n- **A.** Type 1 error\n- **B.** Power of statistical test\n- **C.** Type 2 error\n- **D.** Level of significance\n- **E.** Type 3 error\n\n## Question 3 of 58\n\nhypothesis (also known as false positive” finding or\nconclusion; example: innocent person is convicted”).\nWhile type 2 error is the failure to reject the null\nhypothesis that is actually false (also known as false\nnegative. Finding or conclusion example: guilty\nperson is not convicted) much of statistical theory\nrevolves around the minimization of one or both of\nthese errors, though the complete elimination of\neither is a statistical impossibility if the outcome is\nnot determined by a known, observable casual\nprocess. By selecting low threshold (cut-off) value\nand modifying the alpha level, the quality of\nhypothesis test can be increased. The knowledge of\ntype 1 error and type 2 error is widely used in\nmedical science, biometrics and computer science.\n8. A 12-year-old girl develops facial swelling and an\nerythematous itchy rash shortly after being administered\nthe first dose of the HPV vaccine. On arrival the\nparamedics note a bilateral expiratory wheeze and blood\npressure of 80/50 mmHg of the Gell and Coombs\nclassification of hypersensitivity reactions this is an\nexample of a:\n\n## Question 8 of 90\n\n- **A.** Type II reaction\n- **B.** Type V reaction\n- **C.** Type I reaction\n- **D.** Type III reaction\n- **E.** Type IV reaction\n9. you are prescribing ipratropium bromide for a patient\nwho has presented with an exacerbation of her COPD.\nwhat is mechanism of action of ipratropium bromide?\n\n## Question 9 of 90\n\n- **A.** Muscarinic agonist\n\n## Question 4 of 58\n\nIpratropium is an acetylcholine antagonist via\nblockade of muscarinic cholinergic receptors.\nBlocking cholinergic receptors decreases the\nproduction of cyclic guanosine monophosphate\n(cGMP). This decrease in the lung airways will lead\nto decreased contraction of the smooth muscles.\n- **B.** Muscarinic antagonist\n- **C.** Alpha agonist\n- **D.** Ca channel blocker\n- **E.** Beta agonist\n10. A 40-year-old man presents to the emergency\ndepartment intoxicated with an abdominal stab wound,\nsituated just above the umbilicus in the midline. Small\nbowel has herniated through the anterior abdominal wall\nthrough the hole created by the knife\nThe most superficial structure damaged in stabbing?\nCamper's fascia is the most superficial fatty layer\nof the anterior abdominal wall.\n\n## Question 10 of 90\n\n- **A.** Camper’s fascia\n- **B.** Scarpa’s fascia\n- **C.** Aponeurosis of internal oblique\n- **D.** Rectus abdominis muscle\n- **E.** Transversalis fascia\n11. A 25 Years old male presents to the ED with complaints\nof fever, photophobia, and neck stiffness. You suspect\nmeningitis. Which of the following is the feature of\nNeisseria meningitis?\n\n## Question 11 of 90\n\n- **A.** Gram +Ve diplococci\n- **B.** Gram +Ve rod\n- **C.** Gram –Ve rod\n- **D.** Gram –Ve diplococci\n- **E.** Gram +Ve Cocco bacilli\n\n## Question 5 of 58\n\nNeisseria meningitis is gram negative encapsulated\ndiplococci causing Neisseria meningitis and\nmeningococcal sepsis\n12. A 5-year-old male child brought into the ED with an\ninfection after circumcision. The lymphatic from the penile\nskin and prepuce drains into which of the following group of\nlymph nodes?\nPenile skin and prepuce = superficial inguinal lymph\nnodes\nGlans penis and penile urethra=deep inguinal\nlymph nodes(recall)\nScrotum=Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\n(RECALL)\nTestes= lumber or para-aortic lymph nodes\n(RECALL)\nprostate= internal iliac nodes (recall)\nHigh Yield:\nPre aortic lymph nodes:\n1.coeliac group of preaortic lymph nodes drain:\nAbdominal oesophagus\nStomach\nPancreas(recall)\nLiver\nSpleen\nGallbladder\nDuodenum proximal to major duodenal papilla\n2. Superior mesenteric group of preaortic lymph\nnodes drain:\ndistal duodenum\nJejunum\nIleum\nCecum\nAppendix\nAscending colon\nProximal two third of transverse colon\n3. 3. Inferior mesenteric group of preaortic lymph:\n\n## Question 12 of 90\n\n- **A.** Deep inguinal lymph nodes\n- **B.** Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\n- **C.** Para aortic lymph nodes\n- **D.** Internal iliac lymph nodes\n- **E.** External iliac lymph nodes\n\n## Question 6 of 58\n\nDistal transverse colon\nDescending colon\nSigmoid colon\nProximal rectum\nLumbar or Paraaortic lymph nodes:\nKidney (Recall)\nAdrenal gland\nTestes (recall)\nOvaries (recall)\n14. Which vitamin is also known as anti-sterility vitamin?\nvitamin e act as antioxidant prevents the cells from\ndamage caused by free radicals. Sperm DNA\ndamage is an emerging cause of male infertility\nthat is likely to be more common among men with\nincreased reactive oxygen species in the ejaculate.\nVitamin E can protect the sperm membrane from\noxidative damage and increased levels are\nassociated with low levels of reactive oxygen\nspecies.\n\n## Question 14 of 90\n\n- **A.** Vitamin E\n- **B.** Vitamin A\n- **C.** Vitamin D\n- **D.** Vitamin C\n- **E.** Vitamin K\n15. A 60-year-old male with a history of hypertension and\nsmoking presents to the emergency department with chest\npain. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in the inferior\nleads, and a diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction (MI) is\nmade. The infarcted tissue in the heart is likely to undergo\nwhich type of necrosis?\nCell death:\n\n## Question 15 of 90\n\n- **A.** Liquefactive necrosis\n- **B.** Caseous necrosis\n- **C.** Coagulative necrosis\n- **D.** Fat necrosis\n- **E.** Fibrinoid necrosis\n\n## Question 7 of 58\n\nHigh yield: Be familiar with all types and examples.\n17. A woman sustained a human bite from someone known\nto have hepatitis B two hours ago. She has not had any\nprevious hepatitis B vaccinations\nRegarding prophylaxis against hepatitis B, what is the most\nappropriate schedule to follow?\nEmergency hepatitis B vaccination\nIf you have been exposed to the hepatitis B virus\nand have not been vaccinated before, you should\nget immediate medical advice, as you may benefit\nfrom having the hepatitis B vaccine.\nIn some situations, you may also need to have an\ninjection of antibodies, called specific hepatitis B\nimmunoglobulin (HBIG), along with the hepatitis B\nvaccine.\nHBIG should ideally be given within 48 hours post\nexposure Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three\nmonths\n\n## Question 17 of 90\n\n- **A.** Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over three\nmonths\n- **B.** Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately\n- **C.** Hepatitis B vaccine three doses over six months\n- **D.** Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately and\nHepatitis B vaccine three doses over three months\n- **E.** Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin immediately and\nHepatitis 8 vaccine three doses over six months\n18. A 10 years old child brought into the ED with complaint\nof tiredness, fever and joint pain. His mother states that he\n\n## Question 18 of 90\n\n## Question 8 of 58\n\nhad acute sore throat 10 days back. You suspect acute\nrheumatic fever secondary to untreated sore throat.\nRheumatic fever is immunological sequalae of which of the\nfollowing pathogens?\nStreptococcus\nPyogenes\nStreptococcus Pyogenes is\ngram +Ve Cocci in chains\nBeta hemolytic group A\nCauses acute sore throat in\nchildren (Recall), skin and soft\ntissue infection and\nnecrotizing fasciitis\nErysipelas: Superficial skin\ninfection involving upper\ndermis and superficial\nlymphatics caused by Group\nA beta hemolytic\nstreptococcus pyogenes\n(Recall)\nPost-infectious immune-\nmediated reactions of\nStreptococcus Pyogenes\ninclude Rheumatic fever,\nerythema nodosum, acute\nglomerulonephritis\n- **A.** Staph viridians\n- **B.** Streptococcus pyogenes\n- **C.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- **D.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **E.** Staph epidermidis\n19. A 54-year-old man presents with visual impairment.\nWhen testing the pupillary light reaction, the right pupil\ndilates when the light source moves from the right pupil to\nthe left.\nWhich nerve is likely to be abnormal?\n\n## Question 19 of 90\n\n- **A.** Right oculomotor nerve\n- **B.** Ophthalmic division of the left trigeminal nerve\n- **C.** Left oculomotor nerve\n- **D.** Left optic nerve\n\n## Question 9 of 58\n\nIn this question examiner is more focused on left\neye that means when light source is tested in left\neye, left eye do not constrict that means loss of\nipsilateral direct pupillary reflex and right pupil\ndilation refers to loss of contralateral consensual\npupillary light reflex.\nIn Complete transection of optic nerve:\nloss of ipsilateral direct pupillary reflex\nloss of contralateral consensual pupillary light reflex\nOptic nerve: (High yield)\nTransmit special sensory information from the\nretina of the eye to the primary visual cortex of the\nbrain.\nEnters skull via optic canal of the sphenoid bone\nNote: Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD):\nSeen in optic nerve disease or asymmetrical disease\nof the retina in which pupils respond differently to\nlight stimuli shone in one eye at a time\nswinging light test if there is no RAPD the pupils of\nboth eyes constrict equally regardless of which eye\nis stimulated by the light.\nIn an abnormal swinging-light test if there is a\nRAPD there is less pupil constriction in the eye with\nthe retinal or optic nerve disease\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> Pituitary adenoma: Pituitary gland lies in close\n> proximity to optic chiasm,tumor of pituitary gland\n> compresses optic nerve at chiasm produces visual\n> defect called bitemporal hemianopia\n- **E.** Right optic nerve\n> 21. A 26-year-old male brought into the ED by paramedics\n> with lacerated wound on left upper quadrant. You suspect\n> splenic injury. Which rib fracture is likely to be associated\n> with spleen injury?\n\n## Question 21 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rib 1\n\n## Question 10 of 58\n\nSpleen injury most Likely associated with fracture\nof rib 10.\n- **B.** Rib 10\n- **C.** Rib 8\n- **D.** Rib 4\n- **E.** Rib 6\n22. A 30 years old man is brought into the Ed by\nparamedics with a lacerated wound on his abdomen. which\nof the following Structures is most likely affected by a tear\nof the superior mesenteric artery?\nThe superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut\nstructures from the second part of duodenum\nincluding the caecum, appendix, ascending colon,\nand proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.\nInferior mesenteric artery supplies the descending\ncolon and sigmoid colon.\n\n## Question 22 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ascending and transverse colon\n- **B.** Transverse and descending colon\n- **C.** Transverse colon only\n- **D.** Only ascending colon\n- **E.** Sigmoid colon\n23. Whilst in your cardiology rotation you are asked to\nperform cardiovascular examination. One of your senior\nregistrars asks, what is the best site to auscultate mitral\nvalve?\n\n## Question 23 of 90\n\n- **A.** Second left intercostal space\n- **B.** Right 5th ICS in midclavicular line\n- **C.** Apex of heart\n- **D.** 5th intercostal space to the lower left sternal\nborder\n\n## Question 11 of 58\n\nAreas of auscultation of heart:\nNote: Aortic valve: Second right intercostal space\n(Recall)\nHeart sounds, Mechanical event and\ncorrespondence with ECG (High Yield)\n- **E.** Second right intercostal space\n24. Which of the following statements regarding\nexperimental studies is true?\nExperimental studies are characterised by the fact\nthat the study subjects are allocated by the\ninvestigator to the different study groups through\nthe use of randomisation. This randomisation\nserves to remove any potential bias.\nClinical trials are experimental studies and may be\nDouble-blind: where neither the patient nor the\ninvestigator for the treating clinician) are aware of or\nwhich treatment the patient has been randomised\nto receive.\nSingle-blind: where either the patient or the\nresearcher (or the treating clinician) is not aware\nwhich treatment the patient has been randomised\nto, in these studies it is usually the patient that is\nnot aware of the treatment type.\n\n## Question 24 of 90\n\n- **A.** Examples include cohort studies\n- **B.** Randomisation serves to remove potential bias\n- **C.** Examples include case-control studies\n- **D.** They must be blinded\n- **E.** Patients can only receive treatment or placebo\n\n## Question 12 of 58\n\nUnblinded: these are also known as open studies.\nBoth the patient and the researcher (or treating\nclinician) are aware of the treatment type.\nCase-control and cohort studies are examples of\nobservational studies.\n25. Carbonic anhydrase enzyme is present in which one of\nthe following parts of nephron?\nCarbonic anhydrase pre-dominant location is\nepithelial cells of the Proximal convoluted tubule\nCarbonic anhydrase catalyzes formation of Hco3-\nand H+ from H20 and Co2\n\n## Question 25 of 90\n\n- **A.** The collecting tubule and duct\n- **B.** The descending loop of Henle\n- **C.** The proximal convoluted tubules\n- **D.** The distal convoluted tubules\n- **E.** The ascending loop of Henle\n27. A 40-year-old male with a history of chronic back pain\nunderwent a prolonged course of steroid injections to\nmanage his symptoms. Which one of the following\nhormones will increase in secondary adrenal insufficiency\ndue to prolong steroid injection?\nLong-term steroid therapy can result in secondary\nadrenal insufficiency due to suppression of the\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis. The aetiology\nof adrenal insufficiency can be differentiated by\nchecking serum ACTH which is high in Addison's\ndisease and suppressed in secondary adrenal\nfailure due to hypothalamic or pituitary causes.\nIn this condition, the hypothalamus may continue\nto release corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) in\nan attempt to stimulate the pituitary gland to\nproduce more ACTH. However, the lack of ACTH\nproduction by the pituitary gland means that\ncortisol levels will not increase, despite high levels of\nCRH. Therefore, CRH levels may be high or normal,\n\n## Question 27 of 90\n\n- **A.** Insulin\n- **B.** ACTH\n- **C.** CRH\n- **D.** Cortisol\n- **E.** Aldosterone\n\n## Question 13 of 58\n\nwhile ACTH levels are low in secondary adrenal\ninsufficiency.\nNote: If in question stem not mentioned secondary\nadrenal insufficiency then Cortisol would be correct\nbecause exogenous cushing syndrome is the most\nlikely due to prolong steroid injection while\nsecondary adrenal insufficiency is rare.\nPrimary v/s Secondary Adrenal insufficiency:\nIn contrast to Addison's disease (primary adrenal\ninsufficiency), secondary adrenal insufficiency is\ncharacterized by:\nDecreased pituitary secretion.\nNo skin hyperpigmentation (because there is\ndeficiency of ACTH)\nno hyperkalemia or metabolic acidosis (because\naldosterone levels are normal)\n28. Which of the following statements about cohort studies\nis true? Select ONE answer only.\nA cohort study is a form of longitudinal,\nobservational study that follows a group of patients\n(the cohort) forward in time to monitor the effects\nof a proposed aetiological factor upon them\nThey follow a cohort of patients without a disease\nand evaluate the absolute and relative risk of\ncontracting the disease state after exposure to the\naetiological agent. An example of a cohort would be\na group of patients that have been exposed to a\nparticular drug. This group could then be followed\nlongitudinally to see if they develop a particular side\neffect or disease as consequence of this drug\nexposure. The comparison group can be the general\npopulation from which the cohort was drawn, or\nalternatively another subgroup of the cohort itself\nThey are therefore good at investigating rare\nexposures as the study design sets the exposure. A\nrare outcome is unlikely to appear during the study\ntime and they are therefore poor at detecting rare\nCohort studies are the best way to determine the\nincidence of a disease: Cross-sectional studies are\n\n## Question 28 of 90\n\n- **A.** They are the best way to determine the\nprevalence of a disease\n- **B.** They are a type of experimental study\n- **C.** They are longudinal in nature\n- **D.** They are usually shorter in duration than case-\ncontrol studies\n- **E.** They look at groups of patients with the disease\nbeing studied\n\n## Question 14 of 58\n\nthe best way to determine the prevalence of a\ndisease.\nCohort studies are often compared to case-control\nstudies. They are generally more expensive and\nlonger in duration than case-control studies but\nusually provide more useful and reliable\ninformation\nSubject-selection and loss to follow-up are no\nmajor potential sources of bias with cohort\nstudies and confounding variables are the main\nproblem with analysis of the results.\n29. A 26-Year-old girl presents to the ED with trauma to the\ninternal auditory meatus. Which of the following nerves are\nmost likely injured:\nThe internal auditory meatus also referred to as the\ninternal acoustic meatus lies in the temporal bone\nand exists between the inner ear and posterior\ncranial fossa. It includes the vestibulocochlear nerve\n(CN VIII), facial nerve (CN VII), the labyrinthine\nartery, and the vestibular ganglion.\n\n## Question 29 of 90\n\n- **A.** Facial nerve and glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **B.** Vestibulocochlear nerve and accessory nerve\n- **C.** Vestibulocochlear nerve and facial nerve\n- **D.** Facial nerve and abducens nerve\n- **E.** Trochlear nerve and abducens nerve\n30. A 35-year-old woman presents to the emergency\ndepartment with sudden onset of severe lower back pain\nand numbness in both legs. She reports difficulty with\nwalking and standing up from a seated position. Upon\nfurther evaluation, the healthcare provider notes decreased\nsensation in the saddle area and decreased anal tone,\nsuggestive of possible cauda equina syndrome. Regarding\nanatomy of cauda equina lies at which vertebral level?\nThe spinal cord is housed in the vertebral canal. It is\ncontinuation of medulla oblongata and terminates\n\n## Question 30 of 90\n\n- **A.** T12-L1\n- **B.** T12-S2\n- **C.** C3-C5\n- **D.** S3-S5\n- **E.** C5-C6\n\n## Question 15 of 58\n\nas the conus medullaris at the second lumbar\nvertebrae of adults.\nAt birth, the conus medullaris lies at L3. By the age\nof 21, it sits at L1/L2, thus the spinal cord only\noccupies about 2/3s of the space available to it in\nthe vertebral canal.\nBelow the conus medullaris is cauda equina which\nlies below L1 from L2 to sacrum\nNote: The cauda equina contains a bundle of nerves\nwhich project distally within the enclosed cavity of\nthe lumbar cistern from the spinal cord and conus\nmedullaris toward the coccyx. Each nerve exits at its\nrespective vertebral level toward targets which are\nsupplied by the L2-S5 spinal cord level.\n31. The mean was calculated for study with normal\ndistribution. The values distribution around the mean are\nmeasured by?\nStandard error of the mean = standard deviation /\nsquare root (number of patients)\nHigh Yield:\nMean or average: is the sum of a collection of\nnumbers divided by the number of numbers in the\ncollection\nThe mode is the value that appears most often in a\nset of data\nThe median is the number separating the higher\nhalf of a data sample, a population, or a probability\ndistribution, from the lower half. The median of a\nfinite list of numbers can be found by arranging all\nthe observations from lowest value to highest value\nand picking the middle one\nFor example, a study looks at the number of\nattendances brittle asthmatics have at A&E's across\nthe north of England each quarter. Each A&E\nsubmits the number of attendances as below:\n1,3,4,5,5,5,6,7,7,9,12,14,17\nMean = 7.5\nMode = 5\n\n## Question 31 of 90\n\n- **A.** Interquartal range\n- **B.** Median\n- **C.** Variance\n- **D.** Standard deviation\n- **E.** Mode\n\n## Question 16 of 58\n\nMedian = 6\n32. A 30 years old male patient presents with right eye\nabduction deficit. Which nerve is likely affected?\nRight eye abduction deficit leads to adduction of\nright eye and unable to abduct right eye.\nThe right abducens nerve innervates the right\nlateral rectus muscle, which is responsible for\nabduction (movement away from the midline) of\nthe right eye\nHigh yield:\nMovements of the eyeball (Extra ocular muscles)\nAll the extraocular muscles are innervated by\noculomotor nerve except LR-6 and S0-4\nRemember the lesions of visual pathway (High\nyield)\n\n## Question 32 of 90\n\n- **A.** Right trochlear nerve\n- **B.** Left oculomotor nerve\n- **C.** Right oculomotor nerve\n- **D.** Right abducens nerve\n- **E.** Left oculomotor nerve\n\n## Question 17 of 58\n\n33. A 23-year-old male presents to the ED with a lacerated\nwound on his wrist. You ask junior fellows wrist joint is an\nexample of ?\nWrist joint: Synovial condyloid joint\nSternoclavicular joint: Synovial saddle-shaped\njoint\nAcromioclavicular joint: Synovial plane joint\nShoulder joint: Synovial ball and socket joint\nElbow joint: Synovial hinge joint\nRadioulnar joints: Synovial pivot joint\nThe joint between atlas and axis is Pivot joint\n\n## Question 33 of 90\n\n- **A.** Synovial hinge joint\n- **B.** Synovial plane joint\n- **C.** Synovial pivot joint\n- **D.** Synovial condyloid joint\n- **E.** Synovial ball and socket joint\n34. Damage to Wernicke's area will most likely result in\nwhich of the following clinical features?\nBroca's aphasia: This is a non-fluent aphasia which\ncauses individuals to speak slower. It occurs when\nthe brain’s frontal lobe suffers damage. Individuals\nwith Broca’s Aphasia often feel frustrated due to\nbeing able to comprehend what others are saying,\nbut unable to express themselves. also called\nExpressive dysphasia. Individuals affected only can\nspeak in very short phrases, or have trouble getting\nwords out. (RECALL)\nWernicke’s aphasia: This is a fluent aphasia as this\ntype of aphasia does not affect the speed of the\nindividual's speech. Individuals with this type of\naphasia have damage to their brain’s temporal lobe.\nAs a result, those of Wernicke’s aphasia are\nsometimes unable to comprehend what others are\nsaying, but are able to speak in long sentences.\nHowever, their communication doesn't follow\n\n## Question 34 of 90\n\n- **A.** Dysarthria\n- **B.** Expressive dysphasia\n- **C.** Nystagmus\n- **D.** Receptive dysphasia\n- **E.** Visual loss\n\n## Question 18 of 58\n\nstandard grammar patterns and uses made-up\nwords. also called receptive dysphasia.\n36. A 52-year-old man is unwell with crushing central chest\npain. An ECG shows significant ST segment changes.\nAngiography shows occlusion of the circumflex branch of\nthe left coronary artery.\nThis condition most commonly results in ischemia in which\narea of the heart?\nLCA divides into the anterior interventricular branch\nand the circumflex branch. The circumflex branch\nfollows the coronary sulcus to the left border and\nonto the posterior surface of the heart and supply\nleft atrium and left ventricle\n\n## Question 36 of 90\n\n- **A.** Anterior part of the left ventricle\n- **B.** Anterior part of the right ventricle\n- **C.** Posterior part of the left ventricle\n- **D.** Inferior part of the left ventricle\n- **E.** Inferior part of the right ventricle\n37. A 42-year-old male with history of assault presents to\nthe ED with complaint of incised wound on the neck. Which\none of the following arteries is most likely injured?\nNeck injury may result in the laceration of major\nvessels, potentially leading to hemorrhagic shock.\nInternal carotid artery is most likely injured.\n\n## Question 37 of 90\n\n- **A.** Internal carotid artery\n- **B.** Basillar artery\n- **C.** Facial artery\n- **D.** Vertebral artery\n- **E.** Brachial artery\n38. A 15-year-old girl is found to be hyperglycemic. Urine\ntesting reveals glucose. This urinalysis result is most likely\nto be due to what process in the carrier protein of proximal\ntubule?\n\n## Question 38 of 90\n\n## Question 19 of 58\n\nThe renal tubule plays a significant role in glucose\nreabsorption. If the plasma glucose rises, renal\ntubular reabsorption of glucose will increase\nlinearly until it reaches its maximum tubular\nresorptive capacity. The capacity of the proximal\ntubule to reabsorb glucose to prevent its passing to\nthe urine is known as the renal threshold. At\nthreshold, the system becomes saturated and the\nmaximal reabsorption rate, the glucose transport\nmaximum is reached and no more glucose can be\nabsorbed. Due to increase in plasma glucose, the\nfiltered glucose exceeds the capacity of the tubular\nsystem and the kidneys begin excreting it in the\nurine, the beginning of glycosuria\n- **A.** Inhibition\n- **B.** . Inborn error of metabolism\n- **C.** Saturation\n- **D.** . Activation\n- **E.** Destruction\n39. A well established, commonly used, anti-hypertensive\nmedication has been suggested as a potential cause of\ntype II diabetes mellitus. Which of the following study\ndesigns would be most appropriate to safely determine\nwhether an association exists?\n\nIn this particular case a meta-analysis would be the\nmost appropriate study design for the following\nreasons:\nThe medication is already established and\ncommonly used\nClinical trials are likely to have already been\nperformed in order for its use in clinical practice to\nbe licensed\nThe data needed should be able to be obtained\nrapidly and inexpensively\n\n## Question 39 of 90\n\n- **A.** double-blind randomised controlled trial\n- **B.** cohort study\n- **C.** A crossover study\n- **D.** meta-analysis\n- **E.** case-control study\n\n## Question 20 of 58\n\nNote: Funnel plots are a visual tool for\ninvestigating publication and other bias in meta-\nanalysis (Recall)\nA double-blind randomised controlled trial seems\nunreasonable in this case as the investigators would\nbe exposing a large number of new patients to a\npotential side effect (type I diabetes mellitus).\nThe crossover design is a modification of the\nrandomised controlled trial (a type of experimental\nstudy), in which each patient receives treatment\nand placebo in a random order. This again seems\nunreasonable in this case.\nA cohort study is a type of observational,\nlongitudinal study that looks at a group of people\nthat share a common characteristic or experience\nand follows them over a set time period to analyze\ntheir risk of developing a certain disease. The time\nperiod and expense involved in this type of study\nwould make it a less suitable option than a meta-\nanalysis in this case.\nA case-control study is another type of\nobservational study in which two groups of patients,\none with the disease and one without, are\ncompared on the basis of proposed causative\nfactors that occurred in the past. This is again,\nunsuitable in this case.\n41. A 25-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of\nleft upper quadrant pain, which structure prevent diffuse\nperitonitis across abdomen?\nOmentum\nPeritoneal fold extending from the stomach to\nadjacent neurovascular structures divided into the\ngreater and lesser omentum\nThe greater omentum is a fatty apron that hangs\ndown from the greater curvature of the stomach,\nand it plays a role in the immune response to\ninfections and inflammation in the abdomen. The\ngreater omentum is known to have a protective\nfunction in the peritoneal cavity, where it can limit\nthe spread of infection or inflammation to other\nparts of the abdomen and help isolate the affected\narea.\n\n## Question 41 of 90\n\n- **A.** Greater omentum\n- **B.** Cecum\n- **C.** Sigmoid colon\n- **D.** Small intestinal mesentery\n- **E.** Hepatogastric ligament\n\n## Question 21 of 58\n\nTherefore, if diffuse peritonitis is suspected due to a\nperforation in the LUQ, the greater omentum may\nplay a role in containing the spread of the infection\nor inflammation and preventing it from becoming\nmore widespread in the peritoneal cavity.\n42. You are teaching group of medical students' full\nneurological examination while assessing dermatomal\nsupply of trunk you ask medical students, what is the\ndermatome of nipple?\nDermatomal supply of trunk:\nHigh yield:\n\n## Question 42 of 90\n\n- **A.** T3\n- **B.** T4\n- **C.** T6\n- **D.** T8\n- **E.** T2\n\n## Question 22 of 58\n\n43.  Randomisation such as that used in randomised\ncontrol trials are particularly effective in eliminating which of\nthe following?\nRandomisation in RCTs if done correctly, should\neliminate bias in treatment assignment,\nspecifically selection bias and confounding.\nBias in Randomised controlled trials:\nPerformance bias occurs when participants know if\nthey are in the control group and seek other forms\nof medical care.\nObserver bias occurs where observers interpret a\nperson's behavior incorrectly and do not notice\nother behaviours, and attribute results according to\nthe group the participant is allocated. Double\nblinding reduces performance and observer bias.\nMeasurement bias is sometimes also referred to as\nsystemic error.\nAttrition bias results from systematic differences\nbetween treatment groups as a result of differential\nwithdrawals or exclusions of participants\nHigh yield: Bias and its types\n1: Selection bias is a distortion in the estimate of\nassociation between risk factor and disease that\nresults from how the subjects are selected for the\nstudy.\n2: Information bias is due to systematic\nmeasurement error or misclassification of subjects\non one or more variables, either risk factor or\ndisease status. There are several types of\ninformation bias, including:\nInterviewer Bias: An interviewer's knowledge may\ninfluence the structure of questions and the\nmanner of presentation, which may influence\nresponses.\nRecall Bias: Those with a particular outcome or\nexposure may remember events more clearly or\namplify their recollections\n\n## Question 43 of 90\n\n- **A.** Selection bias\n- **B.** Measurement bias\n- **C.** Attrition bias\n- **D.** Loss to follow up\n- **E.** Observer bias\n\n## Question 23 of 58\n\nObserver Bias: Observers may have preconceived\nexpectations of what they should find in an\nexamination\nLoss to follow-up: those that are lost to follow-up or\nwho withdraw from the study may be different from\nthose who are followed for the entire study\nReporting bias: occurs when a case emphasizes the\nimportance of exposures that he or she believes to\nbe important.\n3: Confounding: Results when the risk factor being\nstudied is so mixed up with other possible risk\nfactors that its single effect is very difficult to\ndistinguish.\n44. A 47-year-old gardener has accidentally stabbed his\nright foot with a garden fork while working in sandals. He\ntells you that he had a full course of vaccinations as a child.\nwound is contaminated with manure. What would you\nadvise him regarding tetanus prophylaxis?\nWound contaminated with soil, manure is high risk\nwound. This patient is up to date with\nimmunisation and more than 10 years are passed\nfollowing last dose. He requires immediate\nreinforcing of both tetanus immunoglobulin and\nvaccine at different sites. Tetanus priming dose are\ngiven at 2,3,4, preschool booster (4 years and 3\nmonths) and then booster dose after every 10 years.\nTetanus prone wound\nClean wounds are defined as: wounds less than 6\nhours old, non-penetrating with negligible tissue\ndamage.\nTetanus-prone wounds include puncture-type\ninjuries acquired in a contaminated environment\nand likely therefore to contain tetanus spores e.g.,\ngardening injuries, compound fractures, wounds or\nburns with systemic sepsis and certain animal bites\nand scratches.\n\n## Question 44 of 90\n\n- **A.** There is no need for any treatment as he is fully\nvaccinated\n- **B.** He should have his tetanus antibodies checked\nbefore deciding on a booster\n- **C.** He should have a tetanus booster\n- **D.** He should have a tetanus booster in his left arm\nand human tetanus immunoglobulin in his right arm\n- **E.** He should have a tetanus booster and human\ntetanus immunoglobulin in the same site\n\n## Question 24 of 58\n\nHigh risk tetanus-prone wound: Heavy\ncontamination with material likely to contain\ntetanus spores e.g., soil, manure, wounds or burns\nthat show extensive devitalised tissue and wounds\nor burns that require surgical intervention that is\ndelayed for more than six hours are high risk even if\nthe contamination was not initially heavy.\n46. A 55-year-old lady presents to the Emergency\ndepartment with complaint of visual loss in Lower left\nquadrant in both visual fields. The lesion would most likely\nbe in which of the following?\nHomonymous denotes a condition which affects\nthe same portion of the visual field of each eye.\nHomonymous inferior quadrantanopia is a loss of\nvision in the same lower quadrant of visual field in\nboth eyes whereas a homonymous superior\nquadrantanopia is a loss of vision in the same upper\nquadrant of visual field in both eyes.\nA lesion affecting one side of the temporal lobe may\ncause damage to the inferior optic radiations\n(known as the temporal pathway or Meyer's loop)\nwhich can lead to superior quadrantanopia on the\ncontralateral side of both eyes (RECALL), if the\nsuperior optic radiations (parietal pathway) are\n\n## Question 46 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left parietal lobe\n- **B.** Left temporal lobe\n- **C.** Right parietal lobe\n- **D.** Right temporal lobe\n- **E.** Right occipital lobe\n\n## Question 25 of 58\n\nlesioned, the visual loss occurs on the inferior\ncontralateral side of the both eyes and is referred to\nas an inferior Quadrantanopia (RECALL).\n47. ADH acts primarily at which of the following sites in\nrenal nephron?\nADH binds to V2 receptors (Recall) and increases\nthe number of aquaporin-2 at the apical plasma\nmembrane of Distal convoluted tubule and\ncollecting duct and increases water permeability\nallowing greater water reabsorption.\nDiabetes insipidus\nCentral DI: Low ADH (Recall) less water absorbed\nNephrogenic DI: ADH resistance, Lithium is the\nmost common acquired cause of nephrogenic DI.\n(Recall)\nBiochemical values include high plasma osmolality\n>295 mOsm/kg, low urine osmolality <300\nmOsm/kg (Recall) and hypernatremia > 145.\nNote:\nSyndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion:\nincreased ADH activity (Recall) (Euvolemic hypo\nosmolar hyponatremia) Recall\nBiochemical values include low plasma osmolality <\n275 mOsm/kg, increased urine osmolality > 100\nmOsm/kg and increased urine sodium or decreased\nplasma sodium (Recall)\nDrugs causing S.I.A.D.H= S.S.R.I (Fluoxetine Recall),\nantipsychotics, anticonvulsants and T.C.A\n\n## Question 47 of 90\n\n- **A.** Proximal convoluted tubule\n- **B.** Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **C.** Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **D.** Distal convoluted tubule\n- **E.** Descending limb of loop of Henle\n48. A 45 years old male presents to the ED with history of\nsevere vomiting for the last 4 days.. The patient is irritable,\nweak, and reporting muscle cramping and weakness. On\nassessment, the patient is experiencing bradypnea with a\nrespiratory rate of 10. The patient has the following ABGs\nresult: HCO3 36, pH 7.52, PaCO2 48. Which condition\nbelow is presenting?\n\n## Question 48 of 90\n\n- **A.** Respiratory alkalosis fully compensated\n- **B.** Metabolic acidosis partially compensated\n\n## Question 26 of 58\n\nIf the primary acid-base disorder is from a\nrespiratory cause, the kidneys will compensate\n(renal compensation).\nIf the primary disorder is from a metabolic cause,\nthe lungs will compensate (respiratory\ncompensation).\nMetabolic acidosis= Respiratory compensation\n(decrease in PaCO2)\nMetabolic alkalosis= Respiratory compensation\n(increase in PaCO2)\nRespiratory acidosis= Renal compensation (increase\nin HCO3-)\nRespiratory alkalosis= Renal compensation\n(decrease in HCO3-)\n- **C.** Metabolic acidosis not compensated\n- **D.** Metabolic alkalosis partially compensated\n- **E.** Metabolic alkalosis fully compensated\n49. Fetal hemoglobin shifts the o2 saturation curve to left\ndue to?\nFetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen\nthan adult hemoglobin. Maternal and Fetal blood\nnever mix during pregnancy, but they come close\nto each other in the placenta.  Oxygen diffuses\neasily to fetal hemoglobin here.\nThe reason fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity\nfor 02. It is composed of two alpha and two gamma\nsubunits, while adult hemoglobin is composed of\ntwo alpha and two beta subunits.\n2,3DPG present in RBCs enhance the ability of RBCs\nto release o2 near tissue and 2,3DPG only binds\nwith beta globulin chains, fetal hemoglobin (HbF)\nlack of beta globulin chains that’s why fetal\nhemoglobin has higher affinity for O2(RECALL).\n\n## Question 49 of 90\n\n- **A.** Decrease in pH\n- **B.** Increased affinity for O2\n- **C.** Increased P50\n- **D.** Strongly bind with 2,3DPG\n- **E.** Decreased affinity for O2\n50. A 29 years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof foot drop and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the\n\n## Question 50 of 90\n\n## Question 27 of 58\n\nfoot. Which of the following nerve is most likely damaged?\nCommon fibular nerve wraps around the neck of\nfibula and enter lateral compartment of leg where it\ngives rise terminal branches:\nSuperficial fibular nerve: Runs around lateral\ncompartment of leg and skin over lower\nanterolateral leg, and dorsum of foot (except skin\nover webspace of 1st and 2nd toe)\nDeep fibular nerve: Anterior compartment of leg\nand skin over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes\n(RECALL)\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls:**\n> 1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the\n> common fibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a\n> result of fracture of the head or neck of the fibula\n> because it passes behind the head of the fibula and\n> then winds laterally around the neck. The nerve\n> damage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion)\n> and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the foot and\n> lateral aspect of the leg and causes paralysis of all\n> muscles in the anterior and lateral compartments\n> of the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors muscles of the\n> foot).\n\n2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by\nfracture of the fibula. As the muscles that the\nsuperficial fibular nerve innervates are evertors,\ninjury to the nerve may result in a loss of eversion\nand loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral\nleg and dorsum of foot.\n3: Deep fibular nerve:\nMotor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in\nfoot drop. In foot drop foot will appear permanently\nplantarflexed, foot drop with high-stepping gait,\nweakness of foot inversion.\nSensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace\nbetween 1st and 2nd toes.\nNote: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or\ncommon peroneal nerve or also due to deep\nfibular nerve injury.\nNote: It is very important to remember direct\ninnervation of common fibular nerve.\nDeep fibular nerve root: L4-L5\nSuperficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.\n- **A.** Sciatic nerve\n- **B.** Tibial nerve\n- **C.** Posterior tibial nerve\n- **D.** Common peroneal nerve\n- **E.** Sural nerve\n\n## Question 28 of 58\n\nMuscles of lateral compartment of leg: (High\nYield)\n51. A 20 years old young boy after assault in the college\npresents to the ED with superficial lacerated wound on his\nanterior triangle of neck. Which of the following muscle is\nmost likely injured?\nNeck fascia is divided into superficial and deep\nfascia\nSuperficial fascia includes: Superficial veins (External\njugular), superficial lymph nodes, fat and platysma\nmuscle\nDeep fascia includes: Trapezius,\nsternocleidomastoid, Visceras of neck and\nvasculature includes common carotid artery,\ninternal jugular vein and vagus nerve in the carotid\nsheath.\n\n## Question 51 of 90\n\n- **A.** Scalene muscle\n- **B.** Trapezius muscle\n- **C.** Platysma\n- **D.** Sternocleidomastoid\n- **E.** Suprahyoid\n52. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) acts primarily at which of\nthe following sites in renal nephron?\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as\nvasopressin, is a small peptide hormone which\nregulates the body’s retention of water. The main\naction of ADH in the kidney is to regulate the\n\n## Question 52 of 90\n\n- **A.** Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle\n- **B.** Distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts\n- **C.** Proximal tubule\n- **D.** Descending limb of loop of Henle\n- **E.** Thin ascending limb of loop of Henle\n\n## Question 29 of 58\n\nvolume and osmolarity of the urine. Specifically, it\nacts in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and\ncollecting ducts (CD).\nADH synthesized by hypothalamus and secreted\nfrom Posterior pituitary (RECALL)\nADH binds to V2 receptors on renal principal cells\nof late distal tubules and collecting ducts\n(RECALL)\nADH also binds with V1 receptors on vascular\nsmooth muscles.\nCentral diabetes insipidus result from deficiency\nADH secretion (RECALL)\nNephrogenic diabetes insipidus result from\ninappropriate renal response to ADH\n54. A 25 years old male patient with a history of trauma\npresents into the ED with complaint of pain in epigastrium\nradiating to the back. Which of the following is the\nsegmental innervation of the pancreas?\nInnervations:\nThe segmental level of innervation of pancreas= T6-\nT10\nGallbladder=phrenic(C3-C5)\nAscending and transverse colon= fibers travel\nthrough lesser splanchnic nerve to =T10-T11 spinal\ncord segment\nDescending and sigmoid colon=pain fibers travel\nthrough lumber splanchnic nerve to= L1 and L2\nspinal segments\nUreter=Sympathetic supply from inferior\nhypogastric plexus\nparasympathetic supply from pelvic splanchnic\nnerve(S2-S4)\nVisceral afferent fibers follow sympathetic fibers to\nenter in spinal cord via =T10-L1 nerve fibers\nBladder= Efferent parasympathetic fibers from\npelvic splanchnic nerve=S2 to S4\novary=sympathetic fibers of ovarian and lumber\nsplanchnic nerve to cell bodies in the = T10-T12 and\nL1 spinal sensory ganglion\nTestes= visceral afferent fibers follow the\nsympathetic fibers to=T10-L1\nRectum= visceral afferent fibers follow\nparasympathetic supply to =S2-S4 sensory ganglia.\n\n## Question 54 of 90\n\n- **A.** T6 –T10\n- **B.** L1-L2\n- **C.** T4-T8\n- **D.** T4 –T10\n- **E.** L2-L4\n\n## Question 30 of 58\n\n55. Which of the following best describes the course of the\ngreat saphenous vein:\nThe great saphenous vein passes anterior to the\nmedial malleolus, travels up the medial side of the\nleg, and thigh to pass through the saphenous\nopening in the deep fascia of thigh, and joins with\nfemoral vein.\nSmall saphenous vein passes posterior to lateral\nmalleolus\n\n## Question 55 of 90\n\n- **A.** Posterior to lateral malleolus\n- **B.** Anterior to medial malleolus\n- **C.** Posterior to medial malleolus\n- **D.** Anterior to lateral malleolus\n- **E.** Passes behind posterior malleolus\n56. An 87-year-old woman from a residential home with a\n2-day history of diarrhea. She has had 10 episodes of\nwatery diarrhea but no vomiting. She complains of colicky\nlower abdominal pain. She had a 5-day course of\nantibiotics for a chest infection 2 weeks ago. Observations\nshow a respiratory rate 18 bpm, Sp02 95% in air, heart rate\n89 bpm,\nBP 146/89 mmHg, temp 36.8'C and BM 5.6 Examination\nreveals that her abdomen is soft but there is generalized\ntenderness especially across her lower abdomen. What is\nthe most likely causative organism?\nBroad spectrum antibiotics disturb the normal gut\nflora and cause overgrowth of clostridium difficile\nspores and causing Pseudomembranous colitis\n\n## Question 56 of 90\n\n- **A.** Norovirus\n- **B.** Rotavirus\n- **C.** Clostridium difficile\n- **D.** E. coli\n- **E.** Campylobacter\n\n## Question 31 of 58\n\nAntibiotics causing clostridium difficile colitis: Co-\nAmoxiclav, Cephalosporins, Clindamycin and\nquinolones\nTreatment of choice of clostridium difficile colitis\nis Vancomycin (Recall)\n57. Which of the following statistical statements is\nincorrect?\nThe prevalence of a disease is the proportion of the\npopulation affected by that disease at a specific\ntime.\n\n## Question 57 of 90\n\n- **A.** The prevalence of a disease is the rate at which\nnew cases occur in a population during a specified\nperiod\n- **B.** The specificity is the proportion of true\nnegatives correctly identified by the test\n- **C.** The number needed to treat (NNT) can be\ncalculated by 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction\n- **D.** The relative risk measures the strength of\nassociation between a factor and an outcome\n- **E.** In a normal distribution the median is the\nmiddle point of the observations\n\n## Question 32 of 58\n\nThe incidence of a disease is the rate at which new\ncases occur in a population during a specified\nperiod.\nRelative risk, or risk ratio, (RR) is used to compare\nthe risk in the two different groups. It is the ratio of\nthe absolute risks of the disease in the treatment\ngroup (ART) to the absolute risk of the disease in the\ncontrol group (ARC). It measures the strength of\nassociation between a factor and an outcome.\nIn a normal distribution the median is the middle\npoint of the observations.\nThe number needed to treat (NNT) is defined as the\ninverse of the absolute risk reduction (ARR) and can\ntherefore be calculated by: NNT = 1 / ARR\nThe specificity is the proportion of true negatives\ncorrectly identified by the test\n58. Regarding the blood supply of abdomen, superior\nmesenteric artery supply which one of the following\nstructures?\n1. coeliac trunk supply the\nAbdominal oesophagus\nstomach\npancreas\nliver\nspleen\ngallbladder\nduodenum proximal to major duodenal papilla\n2. Superior mesenteric artery supply the:\nDistal duodenum\nJejunum\nIleum\ncecum\nAppendix\nAscending colon (RECALL)\nProximal two third of transverse colon\n3.Inferior mesenteric artery supply the:\nDistal transverse colon\nDescending colon (RECALL)\nSigmoid colon\nProximal rectum\n\n## Question 58 of 90\n\n- **A.** Kidneys\n- **B.** Oesophagus\n- **C.** Sigmoid colon\n- **D.** Ascending colon\n- **E.** Descending colon\n\n## Question 33 of 58\n\n59. A 24 years old female who is 3 months pregnant came\ninto the ED with complaint of lower abdominal pain. You\nsuspect ectopic pregnancy. Ectopic pregnancy occurs most\ncommonly in which part of uterine tube?\nEctopic pregnancies most commonly occur in\nampulla.\n\n## Question 59 of 90\n\n- **A.** Fimbriae\n- **B.** Infundibulum\n- **C.** Ampulla\n- **D.** Isthmus\n- **E.** Cervix\n60. 30 years old man is brought into the Ed by paramedics\nwith a gunshot wound on his abdomen. On laparotomy\nretroperitoneal hemorrhage found. Which of the following\nstructures is the most likely source of bleeding?\nSADPUCKER useful mnemonic to remember\nretroperitoneal organs\nS: Suprarenal (adrenal) gland\n- **A.** aorta/IVC\n- **D.** duodenum (Second and third part)\nP: pancreas (except tail)\nU: ureters\n- **C.** colon (ascending and descending)\nK: kidneys\n- **E.** (o)esophagus\nR: rectum\n\n## Question 60 of 90\n\n- **A.** Liver\n- **B.** First part of duodenum\n- **C.** Spleen\n- **D.** Suprarenal glands\n- **E.** Sigmoid colon\n62. Which one of the following vessels is compressed in\nthoracic outlet syndrome?\n\n## Question 62 of 90\n\n- **A.** Subclavian artery and vein\n\n## Question 34 of 58\n\nThoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) refers to the clinical\nfeatures that arise from compression of the\nneurovascular bundle within the thoracic outlet.\nCompression of brachial plexus\nCompression of subclavian artery\nCompression of subclavian vein\nStructures that pass through the thoracic inlet\ninclude: (High Yield)\nTrachea and oesophagus\nThoracic duct and apices of the lung\nphrenic nerve\nvagus nerve\nRecurrent laryngeal nerves\nSympathetic trunks\nLeft and right common carotid arteries.\nleft subclavian arteries.\nInternal jugular veins\nBrachiocephalic veins\nSubclavian veins\nlymph nodes and lymphatic vessels\n- **B.** Vertebral artery\n- **C.** Common carotid artery\n- **D.** Brachiocephalic trunk\n- **E.** Internal carotid artery\n63. Which of the following is a cause of metabolic\nalkalosis?\nMetabolic alkalosis\nLoss of fixed H+ or gain of base produces an\nincrease in arterial [HCO3-]. This increase is the\nprimary disturbance in metabolic alkalosis.\nIn vomiting H is lost from stomach, HCO3 remains\nbehind in the blood, and the [HCO3] increases,\nincreased [HCO3] causes an increase in blood PH\n(Alkalemia). Alkalemia causes hypoventilation which\nis the respiratory compensation for metabolic\nalkalosis. It has features of hypocalcemia (decreased\nunbound plasma Ca) and hypokalemia (because of\nIC shift of K) e.g., tetany, paresthesia, muscle\ncramps.\n\n## Question 63 of 90\n\n- **A.** Treatment with acetazolamide\n- **B.** Chronic renal failure\n- **C.** Hyperaldosteronism\n- **D.** Ethylene glycol ingestion\n- **E.** Diarrhea\n\n## Question 35 of 58\n\nCorrection of metabolic alkalosis consists of\nincreased excretion of HCO3 because the filtered\nload of HCO3 exceeds the ability of renal tubule to\nreabsorb\nit.\nIf metabolic alkalosis is accompanied by ECF\nvolume contraction (vomiting), the reabsorption of\nHCO3 increases (secondary to ECF volume\ncontraction and activation of the renin angiotensin\nII aldosterone system), worsening the metabolic\nalkalosis (contraction alkalosis).\nCauses:\nGastrointestinal losses\nVomiting, nasogastric drainage, pyloric stenosis\nSome villous adenomas\nRenal losses\nChloruretic drugs (e.g., thiazides, furosemide)\nMineralocorticoid excess syndromes\nPrimary Hyperaldosteronism\nOther causes:\nAlkali intake or administration\nMilk alkali syndrome\nsevere potassium depletion (serum potassium <2\nmmol/L)\n65. Which one of the following pathogens causes\nophthalmia neonatorum occurring in the 1  28 days of life?\nOphthalmia neonatorum is conjunctivitis occurring\nwithin the first 4 weeks of life.\nThe most serious cause is infection with Neisseria\ngonorrhea in first week most likely in 3-4 days of\nlife, but in the UK, it is caused more commonly by\nChlamydia in first 28 days of life.\n\n## Question 65 of 90\n\nst\n- **A.** Gardnerella vaginalis\n- **B.** Treponema pallidum\n- **C.** Trichomonas vaginalis\n- **D.** Chlamydia trachomatis\n- **E.** Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n66. A 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency\ndepartment with painful swollen digit, the infection appears\nto have spread to the common flexor sheath. Which of the\n\n## Question 66 of 90\n\n## Question 36 of 58\n\nfollowing digit is most likely to be involved with spread of\ninfection to this location?\nThe synovial sheath of the little finger is usually\ncontinued with the common flexor sheath which is\nalso called ulnar bursa thus infection of ulnar bursa\nmost likely to involve the little finger.\nIn a same way infection of thumb may spread to\nmid palmer space through the continuous synovial\nsheath of the flexor pollicis longus which is also\ncalled radial bursa.\n- **A.** Index finger\n- **B.** Little finger\n- **C.** Ring finger\n- **D.** Middle finger\n- **E.** Thumb\n67. First part of duodenum lies at which level of vertebral\nlevel?\nFirst part of the duodenum extends from the\npyloric sphincter of the stomach wraps around\nhead of pancreas in C-shaped and ends at\nduodenojejunal flexure (Recall)\nFirst part lies at vertebral level L1.\n2nd part L2-L3\n3rd part longest section crossing inferior vena cava\nand Aorta at level L3\n4th part: pass upward on left of abdominal Aorta at\nL2\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical pearls: (High Yield)**\n> Duodenal ulcer: erosion of the mucosa in the\n> duodenum\n> most likely to occur in the superior portion of the\n> duodenum.\n\nThe most common causes of duodenal ulcers are\nHelicobacter pylori infection and chronic NSAID\ntherapy.\n\n## Question 67 of 90\n\n- **A.** T8\n- **B.** L2\n- **C.** T12\n- **D.** L1\n- **E.** T6\n\n## Question 37 of 58\n\nDuodenal ulcer most likely erosion of the\ngastroduodenal artery\n69. Type of bias in which knowledge, beliefs, or\nexpectations may influence the participant's responses and\ninterviewr is not blinded?\nThe type of bias in which interviewer are not\nblinded is known as interviewer bias. This occurs\nwhen the interviewer's knowledge, beliefs, or\nexpectations influence the participant's responses.\nFor example, if an interviewer believes that a\nparticular treatment is effective, they may\nunintentionally give cues or ask leading questions\nthat encourage participants to report positive\noutcomes, even if they did not experience them.\nSimilarly, if an interviewer is aware of a participant's\ndemographic or clinical characteristics, they may\nunintentionally treat them differently or interpret\ntheir responses in a biased way. To minimize\ninterviewer bias, researchers can use standardized\ninterview protocols, train interviewers to be neutral\nand non-directive, and blind interviewers to the\nstudy hypotheses or participant characteristics.\nHigh yield: Bias and its types\n1: Selection bias is a distortion in the estimate of\nassociation between risk factor and disease that\nresults from how the subjects are selected for the\nstudy.\n2: Information bias is due to systematic\nmeasurement error or misclassification of subjects\non one or more variables, either risk factor or\ndisease status. There are several types of\ninformation bias, including:\nInterviewer Bias: An interviewer's knowledge may\ninfluence the structure of questions and the\nmanner of presentation, which may influence\nresponses.\nRecall Bias: Those with a particular outcome or\nexposure may remember events more clearly or\namplify their recollections.\nObserver Bias: Observers may have preconceived\nexpectations of what they should find in an\nexamination.\n\n## Question 69 of 90\n\n- **A.** Recall bias\n- **B.** Observer bias\n- **C.** Confounding bias\n- **D.** Interviewer bias\n- **E.** Selection bias\n\n## Question 38 of 58\n\nLoss to follow-up: those that are lost to follow-up or\nwho withdraw from the study may be different from\nthose who are followed for the entire study.\nReporting bias: occurs when a case emphasizes the\nimportance of exposures that he or she believes to\nbe important.\n3: Confounding: Results when the risk factor being\nstudied is so mixed up with other possible risk\nfactors that its single effect is very difficult to\ndistinguish.\n71. A 30-year-old man presents to the ED with an anterior\nshoulder dislocation while playing, now he is unable to\ninitiate abduction of the arm following reduction of a\ndislocated shoulder. Which one of the following muscles is\nmost likely injured?\nSupraspinatus is part of the rotator cuff group of\nmuscles which initiate the abduction of shoulder\nfrom 0 to 15 degrees and then assists the deltoid\nwith continued abduction. Supraspinatus muscle\nsupplied by suprascapular nerve, "empty can test"\nuse to examine this muscle.\nTeres minor muscle assessment test=Hornblower’s\ntest) RECALL)\nSubscapularis muscle assessment test (lift-off Test)\nRECALL\nMost commonly torn tendon of rotator cuff is\nsupraspinatus muscle (RECALL)\nTeres minor and deltoid muscle innervated by\nAxillary nerve (RECALL)\nSupraspinatus Is innervated by suprascapular nerve\nSubscapular is innervated by subscapular nerve\n\n## Question 71 of 90\n\n- **A.** Teres minor\n- **B.** Teres major\n- **C.** Supraspinatus\n- **D.** Infraspinatus\n- **E.** Subscapularis\n72. Which of the following diet is a rich source of calcium?\n\n## Question 72 of 90\n\n- **A.** Soyabean\n- **B.** Broccoli\n- **C.** Sun flower\n- **D.** Broccoli\n\n## Question 39 of 58\n\nRcem key:  soyabean is a rich source of calcium\nNote: Dairy products which include cheese, leafy\nvegetables, and Soybean is rich in calcium.\nAlmond is also rich in calcium but if both almond\nand cheese are in option choose dairy product\ncheese because cheese has more calcium than\nalmond per 100gm.\n- **E.** Tomato\n73. You are going to attempt lumbar puncture in a patient\nwith suspected meningitis. What is the recommended\nvertebral level to perform LP?\nA lumbar puncture (LP) is a procedure in which a\nneedle is inserted into the spinal canal. It is most\ncommonly used to collect a sample of\ncerebrospinal fluid (CSF) for diagnostic testing (e.g.,\nfor suspected meningitis and subarachnoid\nhemorrhage)\nLumbar puncture is preferentially performed at\nL3/L4 or L4/L5 (cauda equina level), to avoid the\nspinal cord which ends at L1/L2.\nThe layers penetrated by the spinal needle in an LP\nare as follows:\nSkin\nsubcutaneous tissue\nsupraspinous ligament (RECALL)\nInterspinous ligament\nligamentum flavum\nEpidural space or extradural\ndura mater\narachnoid mater\nsubarachnoid space (contains CSF)\n\n## Question 73 of 90\n\n- **A.** L4/L5\n- **B.** L1/L2\n- **C.** L2/L3\n- **D.** T11/T12\n- **E.** T12/L1\n74. 70 years old male presents to the ED with complain of\nexpressive Broca's dysphasia. The brocas speech area is\nlocated in which of the following regions?\n\n## Question 74 of 90\n\n- **A.** Temporal lobe\n\n## Question 40 of 58\n\nBroca’s speech area is located in frontal lobe\nBroca’s aphasia or expressive aphasia is when\npeople find it very difficult to find and say the right\nwords. People with Broca's aphasia may only be\nable to say single words or very short sentences,\nalthough it’s usually possible for other people to\nunderstand what they mean\nWernicke's aphasia (fluent aphasia) in the\ntemporal lobe.\nWernicke’s aphasia or receptive aphasia is when\nsomeone is able to speak well and use long\nsentences, but what they say does not make any\nsense.\n- **B.** Parietal lobe\n- **C.** Midbrain\n- **D.** Frontal lobe\n- **E.** Occipital lobe\n75. During a cholecystectomy procedure, the surgeon\naccidentally lacerates the artery. Which one of the following\narteries supplies gall bladdar?\nGall bladder blood supply: cystic artery branch of\nright hepatic artery. Right hepatic artery is itself\nbranch of common hepatic artery one of branch of\ncoeliac trunk.\n\n## Question 75 of 90\n\n- **A.** Superior mesenteric artery\n- **B.** Right hepatic artery\n- **C.** Cystic artery\n- **D.** Inferior mesenteric artery\n- **E.** Splenic artery\n76. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with complaint\nof lacerated wound on medial compartment of thigh, on\nexamination he is unable to perform extension and medial\nrotation. Which one of the following muscles is most likely\ninjured?\n\n## Question 76 of 90\n\n- **A.** Adductor brevis\n- **B.** Adductor magnus\n\n## Question 41 of 58\n\nEXP: All the muscles of medial compartment of\nthigh are innervated by obturator nerve except\nhamstring portion of adductor magnus.\nAdductor\nmagnus\nAdduction and\nmedial rotation at\nhip\nHamstring part:\nAdduction and\nextension of thigh\nat hip\nAdductor part:\nObturator nerve\nHamstring part:\nSciatic nerve\n(tibial division)\nRecall\n- **C.** Adductor longus\n- **D.** Gracilis\n- **E.** Obturator externus\n78. A 30-year-old woman presents to the ED with\nweakness and fatigue. On examination, she has yellow\ntinge skin, jaundice, and mild splenomegaly. CBC shows\nher Hb 7g/dl. The patient only takes one medication. Which\nof the following medications is most likely to cause\nhemolytic anemia?\nAmong NSAIDs, Mefenamic acid causes hemolytic\nanemia.\nCo-amoxiclav causes Cholestatic jaundice\n(RECALL)\nSummary Of Drugs And Their\nSide Effects (High Yield)\n\n## Question 78 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ibuprofen\n- **B.** Lithium\n- **C.** Mefenamic acid\n- **D.** Propranolol\n- **E.** TCA\n\n## Question 42 of 58\n\n80. You are asked to perform ascitic tap on a CLD patient\nand teach a group of medical students. You ask medical\nstudents what is the site to perform ascitic tap.\nAscites or hemoperitoneum accumulates in\nMorrisons pouch or Hepatorenal recess or Right\nperihepatic space which is lowest space in\nperitoneal cavity in Supine position\nSubphrenic or right subphrenic (pus from\nappendicitis or ruptured duodenal ulcer accumulate\nhere\n\n## Question 80 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left perihepatic space\n- **B.** Right subphrenic space\n- **C.** Left lower quadrant\n- **D.** Right perihepatic space\n- **E.** Left subphrenic space\n82. You are required to perform resuscitative thoracotomy\nin a patient with massive hemothorax. Which one of the\nfollowing muscles are divided during anterolateral\nthoracotomy?\n\n## Question 82 of 90\n\n- **A.** Trapezius\n- **B.** Anterior scalene\n- **C.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **D.** Serratus anterior\n\n## Question 43 of 58\n\nAntero lateral thoracotomy muscle divided serratus\nanterior and pectoralis major\nAntero lateral thoracotomy: Nerve injury long\nthoracic nerve (Recall)\nPost: lateral thoracotomy muscle divided latissimus\ndorsi\nPost: lateral thoracotomy nerve injury:\nThoracodorsal nerve injury\nInternal mammary artery (RECALL) and Aorta are\nlikely to be damaged also\n- **E.** Pectoralis minor\n83. A 30 years old male presents to the ED with lacerated\nwound on his posterolateral back. Which of the following\nmuscle is most likely affected?\nLatissimus dorsi is the superficial muscle of the\nposterolateral back innervated by Thoracodorsal\nNerve (RECALL)\nAsk the patient to shrug his/her shoulder if patient\nunable to do this indicates spinal accessory nerve\ninjury and paralysis of trapezius muscle (RECALL)\nWinging of scapula is due to paralysis of serratus\nanterior muscle innervated by Long thoracic nerve\n(RECALL)\n\n## Question 83 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rhomboid minor\n- **B.** Sternocleidomastoid\n- **C.** Trapezius\n- **D.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **E.** Levator Scapulae\n84. Bilirubin is a byproduct of heme catabolism and is\nexcreted in the bile and stool. Bilirubin is likely derived\nfrom?\n\n## Question 84 of 90\n\n## Question 44 of 58\n\nHeme is an important functional group of\nhemoglobin. Bilirubin, which is a byproduct of\nheme breakdown, comes from red blood cells. The\nliver receives unconjugated bilirubin from the blood\nand conjugates it via phase II metabolism to make\nit more soluble for excretion via the bile and stool.\nFat cells (adipocytes), skin cells (epithelial cells), and\nsmall intestine absorptive cells (enterocytes) do not\ncontain heme to be processed into bilirubin.\n- **A.** Epithelial cells\n- **B.** Enterocytes\n- **C.** Red blood cells\n- **D.** Mesangial cells\n- **E.** Adipocytes\n85. Regarding culture medium for bacterial growth,\nneisseria spp grow on which one of the following culture\nmedia?\nCulture Medium For Bacterial Growth\n\n## Question 85 of 90\n\n- **A.** Choclate agar\n- **B.** Solid media\n- **C.** MacConkey medium\n- **D.** Loeffler's medium\n- **E.** Blood agar\n86. A 26-year-old female came into ED with complain of\ndiplopia on upward gaze in her right eye. She gives history\ntrauma to the face while dancing during the festival and\nsustaining orbital blow out fracture. Entrapment of which\none of the following muscles leads to this?\n\n## Question 86 of 90\n\n- **A.** Superior oblique muscle\n- **B.** Superior rectus muscle\n- **C.** Inferior rectus muscle\n\n## Question 45 of 58\n\nOrbital blow out fracture\nMOI: Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly\nfrom a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a\nfracture of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents\ninto the maxillary sinus.\nClinical features: Pt may have diplopia and can’t\nlook up and medially due to inferior rectus\nentrapment.\nTest infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital\nnerve commonly damage in this injury.\n- **D.** Lateral rectus muscle\n- **E.** Medial rectus muscle\n87. 22 years old young female came into ED with complain\nof sudden onset of lower limb weakness. She has history of\nSore throat 10 days ago. On Examination her reflexes are\nabsent in his lower limbs. What is the most likely causative\norganism?\nGBS cases seem to occur after a viral or bacterial\ninfection like those that cause sore throats or\ndiarrhea characterized by lower limb weakness,\nparesthesia and hyporeflexia\n\n## Question 87 of 90\n\n- **A.** Salmonella\n- **B.** Campylobacter jejuni\n- **C.** Clostridium difficile\n- **D.** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **E.** Clostridium perfringens\n88. A 32-year-old male with a history of RTA presented to\nED with Complain of pain in his Right shoulder. X-ray was\ndone which shows anterior shoulder dislocation. Which of\nthe following nerve is most likely damaged?\n\n## Question 88 of 90\n\n- **A.** Axillary nerve\n- **B.** Suprascapular nerve\n- **C.** Median nerve\n- **D.** Lower subscapular nerve\n- **E.** Ulnar nerve\n\n## Question 46 of 58\n\nIntrinsic muscles of the shoulder: Connecting the\nscapula to the humerus\nThere are six muscles in this group the deltoid, teres\nmajor, and the four rotator cuff muscles\n(supraspinatus, infraspinatus, subscapularis, and\nteres minor).\nAnatomical course: (High Yield)\nAxillary nerve arises in the axilla passes medially to\nthe surgical neck of the humerus, where it divides\ninto three terminal branches:\nAnterior terminal branch:  winds around the\nsurgical neck of the humerus and provides motor\ninnervation to the anterior aspect of the deltoid\nmuscle.\nPosterior terminal branch:  Gives motor innervation\nto the posterior aspect of the deltoid muscle and\nteres minor. It also innervates the skin over the\ninferior part of the deltoid as the upper lateral\ncutaneous nerve of the arm.\nArticular branch: supplies the glenohumeral joint\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Clinical Pearls:**\n> Axillary nerve injury is commonly caused by\n> fracture of surgical neck of humerus or inferior\n> dislocation of humerus.\n\nCauses weak lateral rotation and abduction of the\narm. (Supraspinatus can abduct the arm but not to\na horizontal level).\nLoss of sensation over the inferior portion of the\ndeltoid (the ‘regimental badge area’).\nQuadrangular space: Axillary nerve and posterior\ncircumflex humeral artery and vein pass through it\nand can be compressed as a result of trauma,\nmuscle hypertrophy resulting in weakness of deltoid\nand teres minor.\nNote: Axillary nerve injury also results in\nweakness of deltoid (RECALL) and teres minor\nand resulting in weakness of abduction and\nlateral rotation.\n89. You are teaching group of medical students anatomy of\ninguinal region. You ask medical students Deep inguinal\nRing is a defect in which of the following structure?\n\n## Question 89 of 90\n\n- **A.** Rectus abdominis aponeurosis\n\n## Question 47 of 58\n\nDeep inguinal ring is formed by transversalis fascia.\nSuperficial inguinal ring is formed by External\nOblique. (Recall)\n- **B.** Internal oblique aponeurosis\n- **C.** External oblique aponeurosis\n- **D.** Transversalis fascia\n- **E.** Transversus abdominis aponeurosis\n90. Epinephrine cause vasoconstriction by acting on which\nreceptors?\nAlpha 1: vasoconstriction\nAlpha 2: vasodilation\nBeta 1: centrally increases automaticity, heart rate,\nmyocardial contractility\nBeta 1: Peripherally vasodilates against alpha 1\nvasoconstriction\nBeta 2 - bronchodilation\n\n## Question 90 of 90\n\n- **A.** Alpha-2\n- **B.** Beta-1\n- **C.** AChR\n- **D.** Alpha-1\n- **E.** Beta-2\n3. A 40 years old male presents to the ED with complain of\nptosis with left-sided droopy eye and dry left face. You\nsuspect Horner's syndrome. Which of the following nerve is\nmost likely affected?\nSympathetic fibers if unilaterally damaged may\nproduce ipsilateral Horner's syndrome. Horner's\nsyndrome characterized by triad of:\n\n## Question 3 of 90\n\n- **A.** Long Ciliary nerve\n- **B.** Facial nerve\n- **C.** Vagus nerve\n- **D.** Infratrochlear nerve\n- **E.** Cervical Sympathetic chain\n\n## Question 48 of 58\n\nPtosis\nMiosis\nAnhidrosis\n5. A 50-year-old male presents to the emergency\ndepartment with complain of visual disturbance in his right\neye on examination you note when you show a light in his\nright eye, neither his right or Left pupil constrict but when\nyou show a light in his left eye both his left and right pupil\nconstrict. which of the following nerve is most likely\naffected?\nLoss of afferent pupillary light reflex is seen in\ncomplete palsy of optic nerve. the ipsilateral direct\nand contralateral consensual reflexes are lost as the\nafferent optic nerve does not sense the light shown\nin affected eye but the contralateral direct and\nipsilateral consensual reflexes are intact as the\nefferent oculomotor nerve is normal\n\n## Question 5 of 90\n\n- **A.** Abducens nerve\n- **B.** Trigeminal nerve\n- **C.** Oculomotor nerve\n- **D.** Trochlear nerve\n- **E.** Optic nerve\n13. Which of the following shift oxygen hemoglobin\ndissociation curve to left?\nLeftward shift: Increased affinity for oxygen (Less\nwilling to give up oxygen)\nIncreased affinity for oxygen (Less willing to give up\noxygen)\nIncreased pH\nDecreased pCO2\nDecreased temperature\nDecreased H+(RECALL)\nDecreased 2-3DPG\nCO (RECALLL)\nMethemoglobinemia\n\n## Question 13 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increased Pco2\n- **B.** Decreased PH\n- **C.** Increased 2-3DPG\n- **D.** Increase temperature\n- **E.** CO Poisoning\n\n## Question 49 of 58\n\nFetal hemoglobin (RECALL)\nRightward shift: Decreased affinity for oxygen\n(More willing to give up oxygen)\nDecreased ph (RECALL)\nIncreased pCO2\nIncreased H+\nIncreased Temperature\nIncreased 2-3DPG\n16. A 30 years old male was brought into the ED following a\nfall from a height. His examination reveals bilateral\nweakness in both arm power of 3/5 and his lower limb\npower is 4/5, he also has variable sensory impairment\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n-Spinal cord injuries spinal\ntracts and their normal\nfunction\nSpinal cord syndrome\nAnterior cord syndrome\n(Recall)\n\n## Question 16 of 90\n\n- **A.** Brown Sequard Syndrome\n- **B.** Posterior Cord Syndrome\n- **C.** Complete Cord Transection\n- **D.** Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- **E.** Central Cord Syndrome\n\n## Question 50 of 58\n\n20. Which of the following NSAIDs has the least\ngastrointestinal side effects?\nNSAIDS-induced GI side effects:\nLowest risk=ibuprofen\nHighest risk=piroxicam, ketorolac\nNSAIDs cause GERD by inhibiting the COX 1\nenzymes (cox 1 stimulation produce prostaglandins\nthat help to maintain the gastric mucosal integrity)\nNSAIDs can cause ulcers by interfering with the\nstomach's ability to protect itself from gastric acid\n(by inhibiting the function of prostaglandins)\nRECALL\nNSAID causes hemolytic anemia=Mefenamic acid\n(RECALL).\n\n## Question 20 of 90\n\n- **A.** Indomethacin\n- **B.** Naproxen\n- **C.** Piroxicam\n- **D.** Diclofenac\n- **E.** Ibuprofen\n26. You are teaching group of medical students' venous\ndrainage of lower limb, Regarding the course of great\nsaphenous vein, which one of the following is correct?\nThe great saphenous vein: originate from medial\nside of the dorsal venous arch in the foot, passes\n\n## Question 26 of 90\n\n- **A.** Begins as the upward continuation of the lateral\nmarginal vein of the foot\n- **B.** Travels with saphenous nerve along its course\n- **C.** Runs behind the medial malleolus\n- **D.** Join the femoral vein above the inguinal\nligament\n- **E.** Enters the femoral vein on its anteromedial side\n\n## Question 51 of 58\n\nanterior to the medial malleolus (not posterior),\ntravels up the medial side of leg, knee and thigh to\npass in to saphenous opening in deep fascia\ncovering the femoral triangle and join with the\nfemoral vein below the inguinal ligament.\nThe small saphenous vein: originate from lateral\nside of dorsal venous arch in the foot, passes\nposterior to lateral malleolus and up the back of leg\nto pierce the deep fascia and join the popliteal vein\nin popliteal fossa posterior to knee...\nNote: Cutaneous innervation to medial side of foot\nby=saphenous nerve (Recall).\n35. A 20-year-old man fell from the parallel bar during the\nOlympic trial. A neurologic ex- amination reveals that he\nhas a lesion of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus.\nWhich of the following muscles is most likely weakened by\nthis injury?\nThe pectoralis major is innervated by the lateral and\nmedial pectoral nerves originating from the lateral\nand medial cords of the brachial plexus,\nrespectively. The subscapularis, teres major,\nlatissimus dorsi, and teres minor muscles are\ninnervated by nerves originating from the posterior\ncord of the brachial plexus\nHigh yield:\nBRANCHES OF LATERAL CORD   "LML"\nLateral pectoral nerve\nMusculocutaneous nerve\nLateral root of median nerve\nBRANCHES OF MEDIAL CORD     "M4U"\nMedial pectoral nerve\nMedial cutaneous nerve of arm\nMedial cutaneous nerve of forearm\nMedial root of median nerve\nUlnar nerve\nBRANCHES OF POSTERIOR CORD   "ULTRA"\nUpper subscapular nerve\nLower subscapular nerve\n\n## Question 35 of 90\n\n- **A.** Teres major\n- **B.** Latissimus dorsi\n- **C.** Teres minor\n- **D.** Subscapularis\n- **E.** Pectoralis major\n\n## Question 52 of 58\n\n40. An 8-year-old child brought into the ED with a complaint\nof severe vomiting. ABGs revealed metabolic alkalosis.\nMetabolic alkalosis occurs after vomiting due to?\nMetabolic alkalosis: Occurs due to loss of H+ in GI\ntract i.e., vomiting, pyloric stenosis or in kidney\n(diuretic therapy)\nMetabolic alkalosis is associated with\nhypoventilation and hypokalemia (Recall).\n\n## Question 40 of 90\n\n- **A.** Increased pco2\n- **B.** Decrease HC03\n- **C.** Increase H+\n- **D.** Decreased pco2\n- **E.** Decrease H+\n45. What is the main mechanism of action of diltiazem?\nDiltiazem and verapamil are non-dipyridamole\ncalcium channel blocker\nFDA-approved Indications of diltiazem\nAtrial arrhythmia, including atrial fibrillation with\nthe rapid ventricular rate (RVR)\nHypertension\nParoxysmal supraventricular tachycardia\nChronic stable angina\nAngina Due to Coronary Artery Spasm( Prinzmetal's\nangina)\nDihydropyridine calcium channel blockers include\namlodipine, felodipine and nifedipine\n\n## Question 45 of 90\n\n- **A.** Close the K+ channels\n- **B.** Opens the Ca+ channels\n- **C.** Opens the Na+channel\n- **D.** Blocks the Na+ channel\n- **E.** Blocks Ca+ channels\n53. During a routine physical examination, you perform\nauscultation of the heart to listen to the sounds produced\n\n## Question 53 of 90\n\n## Question 53 of 58\n\nby the valves. What is the area of auscultation of aortic\nvalve?\nHigh yield:\nAreas of auscultation of heart. Remember on\nfingertips.\n- **A.** Right 5th intercostal space\n- **B.** Left 5th intercostal space\n- **C.** Second left intercostal space\n- **D.** Apex of heart\n- **E.** Second right intercostal space\n61. Regarding central respiratory chemoreceptors, which of\nthe following statement is correct?\nCentral chemoreceptors are sensitive to the PH of\nCSF, decrease in the pH of the CSF produce\nincrease in breathing rate(hyperventilation). H+\ndoesn't Cross the blood brain barrier as well as co2\ndoes. co2 diffuse from arterial blood into the CSF\nbecause co2 is lipid soluble and readily cross blood\nbrain barrier. in the CSF, co2 combines with ho2 to\nproduce H+ and HCO3-. the resulting H+ act directly\non the central chemoreceptors, thus increase in\npco2 and H+ stimulate the breathing and decrease\nin pco2 and H+ inhibit the breathing. this resulting\nhyperventilation or hypoventilation then returns\nthe arterial Pco2 toward normal.\nCentral chemoreceptors are located near the\nventrolateral surface of the medulla.\n\n## Question 61 of 90\n\n- **A.** Located in dorsal surface of medulla\n- **B.** Located in the medulla near exit of cranial nerve\nX,XI\n- **C.** Respond directly to changes in arterial PCO2\n- **D.** Respond directly to changes in external PCO2\n- **E.** Respond to pH of CSF\n\n## Question 54 of 58\n\nPeripheral chemoreceptors are located in carotid\nand aortic body. The carotid bodies are located at\nthe bifurcation of common carotid arteries, the\naortic bodies are located above and below the aortic\narch.\nDecrease in arterial po2: stimulate the peripheral\nchemoreceptor and increase breathing rate. po2\nmust decrease to low levels (<60mmHg) before\nbreathing is stimulated. when po2 is less than\n60mmHg, breathing rate is exquisitely sensitive to\npo2.\nincrease in arterial Pco2: stimulate the peripheral\nchemoreceptors and increase breathing rate\nincrease in arterial H+: stimulate the carotid body\nperipheral chemoreceptors directly, independent of\nChanges in pco2.\nIn metabolic acidosis, breathing rate is increased\n(hyperventilation) because arterial H+ is increased\nand pH is decreased.\n64. At which of the following site maximal absorption of\nvitamin B12 occur?\nMaximal water reabsorption occurs in Jejunum\n(Recall)\nMaximal Iron absorption occurs in duodenum\n(Recall)\nMaximal calcium absorption occurs in duodenum\n(Recall)\nMaximal vit B12 absorption occurs in terminal ileum\nNote: Sub acute cord syndrome: Due to vitamin B12\ndeficiency also known as subacute combined\ndegeneration of spinal cord (Recall) there is\nmainly degeneration pf posterior and lateral cord\nDrugs causing Vit B12 Deficiency: Ethanol,\nchloramphenicol, H2 receptor blockers, PPI\n(Omeprazole) Recall\n\n## Question 64 of 90\n\n- **A.** Jejunum\n- **B.** Proximal ileum\n- **C.** Stomach\n- **D.** Duodenum\n- **E.** Terminal ileum\n68. 27 years old male presents to the ED with complain of\ndysphagia. Which of the following nerve is responsible for\ncontracting the esophageal phase of swallowing?\n\n## Question 68 of 90\n\n## Question 55 of 58\n\nPharyngeal phase of swallowing: glossopharyngeal\nnerve and vagus nerve\nEsophageal phase of swallowing: Vagus nerve\n- **A.** Hypoglossal nerve\n- **B.** Facial nerve\n- **C.** Auriculotemporal nerve\n- **D.** Glossopharyngeal nerve\n- **E.** Vagus nerve\n70. Regarding the validity of a study, which of the following\nis correct statement?\nSelection bias is a form of systematic error.\nRandom error is always present in a measurement.\nThey are caused by unpredictable fluctuations in\nthe readings of a measurement apparatus or in the\nexperimenters' interpretation of the instrumental\nreading\nRandom error will reduce with increasing sample\nsize. (Recall)\nRandomisation will reduce confounding.\nA confounding factor is a background variable that\nis not of direct interest to the study.\n\n## Question 70 of 90\n\n- **A.** Randomization will increase confounding\n- **B.** Selection bias is a form of random error\n- **C.** A confounding factor is a variable that is of\ndirect interest to the study\n- **D.** Random error increases with increasing sample\nsize\n- **E.** Random error is always present in a\nmeasurement\n77. During examination of cardiovascular system you are\ndiscussing heart sounds and cardiac cycle. which of the\nfollowing stage of cardiac cycle occurs immediately after\nthe aortic valve closes?\n\n## Question 77 of 90\n\n- **A.** Ventricular filling\n- **B.** Atrial systole\n- **C.** Isovolumetric contraction\n\n## Question 56 of 58\n\nIsovolumetric Relaxation\nWhen the aortic and pulmonary valves close, the\nheart relaxes isovolumetrically because both the\noutflow valves and inflow valves are closed, and so\nno fluid moves across them. This phase lasts until\nthe intraventricular pressure falls below the\npressure in the atria, at which time the mitral and\ntricuspid valves open again. The isovolumetric\nrelaxation lasts about 0.08 S. The volume\nthroughout this phase is the end systolic volume,\napproximately 50 ml. The atrioventricular (AV)\nvalves open at an atrial pressure of about 7 mmHg.\nIsovolumetric relaxation:\nlasts about 0.08 seconds\nAortic and pulmonary valves closed\nMitral and tricuspid valves closed.\n- **D.** Ventricular ejection\n- **E.** Isovolumetric relaxation\n79. A 62-year-old patient with pericardial effusion comes to\na local hospital for aspiration of pericardial fluid by\npericardiocentesis. The needle is inserted into the\npericardial cavity through which of the following intercostal\nspaces adjacent to the sternum?\nPericardiocentesis is a procedure performed to\nremove pericardial fluid from the pericardial sac\nthat surrounds the myocardium\nThe needle insertion site is 1-2 cm lateral to the apex\nbeat within the fifth intercostal space. Advance the\nneedle over the superior border of the rib to avoid\nintercostal nerves and vessels.\nHigh yield:\nSites for Abdominal Paracentesis:\nMidline Approach =2 cm below Umbilicus in midline\nLateral Approach = 3 to 5 cm Superior and Medial to\nAnterior Superior Iliac Spine.\nNeedle Thoracocentesis in Pneumothorax =2nd\nIntercostal Space Midclavicular line. (RECALL)\n\n## Question 79 of 90\n\n- **A.** Left fourth intercostal space\n- **B.** Right fifth intercostal space\n- **C.** Right fourth intercostal space\n- **D.** Right sixth intercostal space\n- **E.** Left fifth intercostal space\n\n## Question 57 of 58\n\n0% Complete\nMark as Complete\nPrevious Next\nChest Tube Intubation, Pleural tap = Midaxillary Line\n5th Intercostal Space.\n81.\n17 years old female presented to the ED with complaint of\nfever since 3 days lethargy, stiff neck and non-blanching\nskin rash that appears on the lower limb.\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\n## Question 81 of 90\n\n- **A.** HSP\n- **B.** Rheumatic fever\n- **C.** Endocarditis\n- **D.** Autoimmune TTP\n- **E.** Meningococcal Septicemia\n\n## Question 58 of 58	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.152119+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
08fc44dd-1001-4185-a00b-91ef137c4d13	5bc80d5b-3c6c-4c8c-86c8-3e25d86d415d	stomach	Stomach	article	Stomach\nThe stomach is a gastrointestinal organ that is responsible for\npreliminary digestion and destroying any potential pathogenic\nmicroorganisms that may have been ingested. It is an acidic\nenvironment with a pH that can vary between 1.5-3.5.\nThe stomach is divided into:\nCardia: the part of the stomach that lies immediately distal to\nthe gastroesophageal sphincter\nGastric fundus: the dome-shaped region of the stomach just\nlateral to the cardia.\nBody (stomach): the main portion of the stomach that lies\nbetween the fundus and the pylorus.\nPylorus: the terminal conical narrowing of the stomach that\ncan be further subdivided into the proximal antrum and the\ndistal pyloric canal\nFunction of stomach\nMechanical digestion: peristalsis of the stomach against a\nclosed pyloric sphincter →  breakdown of food into smaller\n\n![Stomach - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/stomach/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Stomach — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nparticles\nChemical digestion: enzymatic breakdown of food into\nchyme (a semifluid mixture of digested food and digestive\nenzymes) by gastric acid and enzymes\nThe stomach act as a reservoir of food (stored food\ntemporarily) as it is very distensible, when empty it has\nvolume of 50 ml but it has capacity of up to 3-4 L\nRegulate the release of resulting chyme into the small\nintestine.\nSecrete intrinsic factor which is essential for absorption of\nvitamin B12.\nGastric cell types and their secretion\nGastric juice\nAbout 2 L of gastric juice are secreted daily by specialized\nsecretary in the mucosa it consists of\nGastric acid (HCL)\nMucus\nDigestive enzymes\nIntrinsic factor\nwater\n\n### 1. Gastric acid\n\nAn acid composed of water, hydrogen, and chloride that is\nsecreted by parietal cells in the stomach. Its main function is\nto:\nMaintaining the acidic environment of the stomach.\n\n![Stomach - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/gastrointestinal-git-physiology/stomach/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Stomach — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nConverts pepsinogen to pepsin for chemical digestion (aids\nprotein digestion).\nProvide optimal PH environment for pepsin.\nIt is a natural defense mechanism against microorganisms.\nStimulate the small intestinal mucosa to release secretin and\n\n## Cck.\n\nPromotes the absorption of Ca+ and Fe+ in small intestine.\nMechanism of gastric acid secretion\nGastric acid is secreted by the parietal cells.\nH+/K+-ATPase pump on the apical cell membrane of parietal\ncells secretes H+ ions into the lumen.\nCarbonic anhydrase in parietal cell cytoplasm converts CO2\ninto H+ and HCO3-.\nIntracellular HCO3- is exchanged for Cl- through the\nbasolateral membrane.\nH+ and Cl- are secreted into the gastric lumen as HCl.\nStimulation of gastric acid secretion\nThe secretion of HCI is stimulated by:\nVagal stimulation directly through the neurotransmitter\nacetylcholine acting at muscarinic (M3) receptors on the\nparietal cell.\nIndirectly through the neurotransmitter gastrin-releasing\npeptide (GRP) stimulating release of gastrin from G cells.\nHistamine acting at H2 receptors on the parietal cell\n(released from ECL cells in response to vagal activity and\ngastrin)\nGastrin released from antral G cells acting at the CCKB\nreceptor on parietal cells.\nInhibition of gastric acid secretion\nThe secretion of HCI is inhibited by:\nNegative feedback mechanism inhibits the secretion of H+\nby parietal cell\nLow pH (< 3) in the stomach\n\nProstaglandins (inhibit the gastric H+ secretion, maintain the\nmucosal barrier and stimulate the HCO3- secretion, thus\nprotecting the gastric mucosa from damaging effects of H+)\nSomatostatin (both directly through inhibition at parietal\ncells and indirectly through inhibition of gastrin and\nhistamine release)\nThe presence of chyme within the duodenum also stimulates\nentero-endocrine cells to release Secretin, Gastric inhibitory\npolypeptide (GIP and Cholecystokinin\nVagotomy eliminates both direct and indirect pathways.\nDrugs i.e., H2-receptor blockers e.g., cimetidine, Proton pump\ninhibitors e.g., omeprazole and Muscarinic receptor\nantagonists e.g., atropine\nMucus: Gastric mucus is a gel-mucous barrier secreted by\nepithelial cells and glandular cells in the stomach wall. It acts\nas part of a barrier that protects the stomach wall from the acid\nand digestive enzymes within the stomach lumen. This barrier\nis also made up of a bicarbonate secretion and the epithelial\ncells themselves, which are tightly joined together. Together,\nthese components prevent the stomach from effectively\ndigesting itself.\nDigestive enzyme: Pepsin is the main gastric enzyme. It is\nproduced by the stomach cells called "chief cells" in its inactive\nform pepsinogen, which is a zymogen. Pepsinogen is then\nactivated by the stomach acid into its active form, pepsin.\nPepsin breaks down the protein in the food into smaller\nparticles, such as peptide fragments and amino acids. Protein\ndigestion, therefore, primarily starts in the stomach, unlike\ncarbohydrate and lipids, which start their digestion in the\nmouth.\nGastric lipase is an acidic lipase secreted by the gastric chief\ncells in the fundic mucosa of the stomach.\nIntrinsic factor: The intrinsic factor is produced by parietal cells\nand is essential for vitamin B12 absorption.\nMotor functions of stomach (storage,\nmixing, and slow emptying)\n\nThe mixing of the food with gastric secretions takes place in\nthe distal body and antrum of the stomach where the\nmuscularis externa layer is thicker. The stomach has an\nadditional inner oblique smooth muscle layer (in addition to\nthe inner circular layer and outer longitudinal layer), allowing\nperistaltic contractions and thorough mixing. Gastric\ncontractions are increased by vagal stimulation and decreased\nby sympathetic stimulation.\nFactors influencing gastric emptying:\nThe presence of chyme in the pyloric antrum distends it and\ncauses antral contractions and opening of the pyloric sphincter.\nGastric emptying varies with different food types:\ncarbohydrates are emptied more quickly, proteins more slowly\nand fatty foods even more so, liquids empty more rapidly than\nsolid foods.\nFactors that promote gastric emptying\nGastrin (increases stomach contractility)\nDistension of pyloric antrum\nParasympathetic stimulation\nFactors that inhibit gastric emptying\n(There are osmoreceptors and chemoreceptors in the\nduodenal wall; they respond to physical properties and\nchemical composition of the duodenal contents and evoke\nenterogastric reflexes which are inhibitory.)\nHypertonic fluids in duodenum: (Osmoreceptors in\nduodenum -> sense tonicity of chyme --> evoke\nenterogastric reflex --> gastric emptying inhibited, so that\nthe tonicity of the contents can be normalized before they\ncan escape into duodenum)\nIrritation of duodenal mucosa: (if a poisonous substance\nreaches the duodenum, it may be absorbed into blood.to\nprevent its absorption, chemoreceptor in duodenum initiate\nenterogastric reflex and inhibit the gastric emptying\nAcidic chyme (pH < 3.5) in duodenum: (pH sensed by "S" cells\nin duodenum --> release secretin --> secretin causes\ncontraction of pyloric sphincter --> gastric emptying\nslowed/inhibited, so that the pH of the contents can be\noptimized.\n\nFat/protein breakdown products in duodenum: fat is the\nmost potent inhibitor of gastric emptying (acting via\nCCK); Entry of fats in duodenum -> release of CCK from "I"\ncells -> CCK decreases gastric motility and thus prevents or\nretards gastric emptying, so that the ingested fats enter\nslowly and in small amounts into duodenum.)\nHormones CCK, secretin, gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP) all\ncause constriction of pyloric sphincter, hence slow gastric\nemptying.	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
9501eaea-ec7b-490d-9f3c-e097a7c4ac60	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	calcitonin	Calcitonin	article	Calcitonin:\ncalcitonin is secreted by parafollicular (cells) of thyroid\ngland. Calcitonin is secreted in response to high levels of\nplasma calcium ions and acts to lower circulating levels of\ncalcium. Calcitonin acts to decrease serum calcium\nconcentrations in several ways.\nIt inhibits the activity of the osteoclasts in bone tissue,\nthereby preventing the resorption (breakdown) of bone, and\ninhibits the reabsorption of calcium by the cells of the kidney,\nresulting in increased calcium excretion in the urine. The\neffects of calcitonin counter the effects of parathyroid\nhormone that acts to increase serum calcium concentration.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
7307429b-4d13-4b96-a198-7f5142b5c26d	2f6a9a7f-851f-4285-9059-745028a67833	cushing-syndrome	Cushing Syndrome	article	Cushing syndrome\nIt is a disorder caused by any condition that produces\nexcessive glucocorticoids (cortisol). While the term "Cushing\nsyndrome" can be applied to any cause of hypercortisolism,\n"Cushing disease" refers specifically to secondary\nhypercortisolism that results from excessive production of\nACTH by pituitary adenomas.\ncauses\nIatrogenic Cushing's syndrome (most common cause)\ncaused by exogenous administration of glucocorticoids.\nEndogenous: Cushing's disease (i.e., Cushing's syndrome\nsecondary to pituitary tumor): Pituitary adenoma ACTH\nsecretion →  bilateral adrenal gland hyperplasia →  increases\ncortisol secretion\nAdrenal tumors: adrenal adenoma & adrenal carcinoma\nEctopic ACTH production\nSmall cell lung cancer\nClinical features\nThin, easily bruisable skin with ecchymoses\nStrech marks (purple abdominal striae)\nHirsutism\nAcne\nDelayed wound healing.\nAnxiety, irritability, Lethargy, fatigue, Sleep, disturbance,\nMemory deficits, Depression, Psychosis\nOsteopenia, osteoporosis, pathological fractures, avascular\nnecrosis of the femoral head\nEndocrine and metabolic effects: Insulin resistance →\nhyperglycemia\nDyslipidemia\nWeight gain characterized by central obesity, moon facies,\nand a dorsocervical fat pad (buffalo hump)\n\n♂ : Decreased libido\n♀ : Decreased libido, virilization, and/or irregular menstrual\ncycles (e.g., amenorrhea)\nGrowth delay (in children)\nNote: In Cushing disease: often hyperpigmentation (darkening\nof the skin due to an overproduction of melanin), especially in\nareas that are not normally exposed to the sun (e.g., palm\ncreases, oral cavity) Overproduction of melanin Caused by\nexcessive ACTH production because melanocyte-stimulating\nhormone (MSH) is cleaved from the same precursor as ACTH\ncalled proopiomelanocortin (POMC)\nTreatment\nFirst-line treatment: tumor resection (Life-long\nglucocorticoid + mineralocorticoid replacement after\nsurgery)\nSecond-line or adjunctive therapy: pharmacological\ntreatment\n\n![Cushing Syndrome - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/endocrine-physiology-metabolism/cushing-syndrome/figure_01_p3.png)\n*Cushing Syndrome — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
8ed9dac2-3e0a-499f-8e8f-8ab950444005	44fbc2a6-f5b1-4416-9f72-fb63379753c9	sba-quiz-pulmonology-foreign-body	Airway Foreign Body Aspiration — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Airway Foreign Body Aspiration — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 2-year-old child was playing with plastic building bricks when he suddenly experienced a violent bout of choking, coughing, and cyanosis. In the ED 2 hours later, he is comfortable at rest with no stridor, but chest examination reveals unilateral decreased breath sounds and monophonic expiratory wheeze over the right lung. What is the most appropriate next step in management?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Airway Foreign Body Aspiration)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/foreign-body/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Urgent referral to ENT / Paediatric Surgery for Rigid Bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia\n- **B.** Reassurance and discharge home since he is no longer coughing\n- **C.** Prescribe oral amoxicillin for 7 days\n- **D.** Perform blind manual finger sweeps of the oropharynx\n- **E.** Trial of inhaled salbutamol and discharge if wheeze persists\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nA definitive history of a choking episode followed by localized unilateral wheezing or decreased breath sounds is highly diagnostic of tracheobronchial foreign body aspiration, even if the child currently appears stable (the 'silent phase' after the object lodges in a bronchus). Rigid bronchoscopy is the gold standard diagnostic and therapeutic procedure to retrieve the foreign body and prevent fatal airway compromise or bronchiectasis.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich bronchus is the most frequent anatomical site of foreign body lodgement in children and adults, and why?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Airway Foreign Body Aspiration)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/foreign-body/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Right main bronchus; it is wider, shorter, and runs more vertically than the left main bronchus\n- **B.** Left main bronchus; it branches at a sharper angle\n- **C.** Left upper lobe bronchus\n- **D.** Lingular bronchus\n- **E.** Both bronchi are lodged at exactly equal frequencies in all age groups\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe right main bronchus is wider in diameter, shorter in length, and takes a more direct, vertical downward takeoff from the carina (~25 degrees vs ~45 degrees for the left). Consequently, aspirated foreign bodies preferentially enter the right tracheobronchial tree in over 60-70% of cases.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic radiographic sign of a radiolucent (e.g. food, peanut) foreign body causing a 'ball-valve' obstruction of a major bronchus on an inspiratory-expiratory chest radiograph or lateral decubitus view?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Airway Foreign Body Aspiration)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/foreign-body/figure_03_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Unilateral hyperinflation (air-trapping) of the affected lung with mediastinal shift away from the affected side on expiration\n- **B.** Dense opaque metallic density with no air trapping\n- **C.** Bilateral diffuse reticular opacities\n- **D.** Complete pneumothorax on the contralateral side\n- **E.** Normal chest radiograph excludes foreign body completely\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOrganic foreign bodies (e.g. peanuts) are radiolucent and invisible on standard X-rays. When acting as a one-way 'ball valve', air enters during inspiration (airway dilates) but cannot escape during expiration (airway constricts). This causes unilateral hyperlucency (hyperinflation) with flattening of the hemidiaphragm and mediastinal shift away from the affected lung on expiration.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhy are peanuts and tree nuts particularly dangerous foreign bodies when aspirated into the tracheobronchial tree?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Airway Foreign Body Aspiration)](/lessons/images/sba/pulmonology/foreign-body/figure_04_p7.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** They absorb moisture and swell, severely worsening obstruction, while releasing free fatty acids that provoke intense, destructive chemical bronchitis and granulomatous reaction\n- **B.** They contain botulinum toxin\n- **C.** They dissolve rapidly and enter the systemic circulation\n- **D.** They cause immediate malignant transformation\n- **E.** They can only be diagnosed by MRI\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nVegetable foreign bodies (peanuts, popcorn) are hygroscopic: they absorb bronchial secretions and swell over hours to days, converting a partial into a complete bronchial obstruction. Furthermore, vegetable oils and lipids incite severe acute chemical vegetative bronchitis, granulation tissue formation, and secondary bronchiectasis.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nIn an alert, conscious child who is choking with effective cough, which intervention is strictly CONTRAINDICATED?\n\n- **A.** Blind finger sweep of the mouth\n- **B.** Encouraging the child to cough\n- **C.** Monitoring oxygen saturation\n- **D.** Allowing the child to assume their position of comfort\n- **E.** Calling for senior medical assistance\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBlind finger sweeps are dangerous and strictly CONTRAINDICATED because the rescuer's finger can push a mobile foreign body deeper into the posterior oropharynx, converting a non-occlusive supraglottic object into an irreversible, complete laryngeal obstruction or causing pharyngeal lacerations.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
93d958ee-855e-4a47-9632-d03fa805374d	37efe4d6-061b-4e41-bc45-75d15360b38f	platelate-and-platelate-plug	Platelate and Platelate Plug	article	Platelets and platelet plug\nPlatelets:\nThese are minute, round or oval discs formed by fragmentation\nof megakaryocytes. The normal half-life of platelets is 10 days.\nPlatelets contain three types of storage granules.\ndense granules containing ADP, ATP, serotonin and calcium.\nalpha granules containing clotting factors, von Willebrand\nfactor (VWF), platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and\nother proteins.\nlysosomes containing hydrolytic enzymes.\nFunctional characteristics of platelets:\nHave no nucleus, so cannot reproduce.\nHave actin and myosin molecules and thrombosthenin, so\ncan contract to cause clot retraction.\nHave residual ER and Golgi apparatus to synthesize enzymes\nand to store Ca ions.\nHave enzyme systems to synthesize ATP from ADP.\nHave enzyme Systems to synthesize prostaglandins.\nHave fibrin-stabilizing factor that causes polymerization of\nfibrin monomers to form fibrin threads.\nHave growth factor that promote growth and multiplication\nof vascular endothelial cells, vascular smooth muscle cells\nand fibroblasts. So, repair damaged vascular wall.\nIts surface has a glycoprotein-coat that adheres it to injured\nendothelial cells and exposed collagen fibers form deeper\nvessel wall.\nIts membrane has phospholipids that activates intrinsic\nsystem of blood doting.\nIts membrane has Adenyl Cyclase that causes formation of\ncyclic AMP that excites other activities of platelets.\nFormation of Platelet Plug\n\nPlatelets get adhered to the collagen of ruptured blood\nvessel and secrete adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and\nthromboxane A2. These two substances attract more and\nmore platelets and activate them. All these platelets\naggregate together and form a loose temporary platelet plug\nor temporary hemostatic plug, which closes the ruptured\nvessel and prevents further blood loss.\nPlatelet aggregation is accelerated by platelet activating\nfactor (PAF)\nImportance:\nPlatelet plug is important to close small rents in vessel wall.\nNatural Inhibitors of Platelet\nFunction\nThe release reaction is inhibited by substances that increase\nthe level of platelet cAMP.\nProstacyclin (PGI2), synthesized by endothelial cells, is one\nsuch substance, has the opposing effects of TXA2; it is a\npotent inhibitor of platelet adhesion and aggregation on\nnormal vascular endothelium (and a potent vasodilator).\nNitric oxide is constitutively released from endothelial cells\nand also from macrophages and platelets; it inhibits platelet\nactivation and promotes vasodilation.\nAnti-clotting factor:\nSurface of the cell: Cell membrane is covered by the glycocalyx\n(it makes the cell surface slippery), clotting factor slip the cell\nsurface and move forward.\nAntithrombin III: It prevents to convert the prothrombin to\nthrombin.\nInactivates the already made thrombin.\nProtein C and Protein S formed by liver\nPrevent to convert the prothrombin to thrombin.\nPrevent to convert the fibrinogen to fibrin.\nHeparin: It increases the activity of antithrombin III by 1000\ntimes\n\nPlasmin:  It breaks already formed clot.\nReleased by all the tissues.\nConvert the inactive plasminogen to plasmin.	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.496915+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.969527+00	\N
a0991208-9ba2-497f-a525-8212c1131d3c	69aa3acc-66d4-42b4-92ed-670a190ab74d	asthma-in-adults-management	Asthma in Adults Management	article	Levels of severity of acute asthma\nattacks in adults\nExample -1 : In exam, if they give you a scenario that a 34-\nyear-old asthma patient who is unable to complete a\nsentence in one breath, his HR is 115 beats/min and SpO2 91%.\nHow would you classify the severity of asthma in this patient?\nAnswer: Life-threatening asthma\nExplanation: Any patient with features of acute severe\nasthma along with any one feature of life-threatening\nasthma (as in above scenario his saturation is below <92%)\nwould be classified as having life-threatening asthma.\n\n![Asthma in Adults Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/asthma-in-adults-management/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Asthma in Adults Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nManagement of acute asthma in\nadults in the emergency department\nDischarge plan\nIn all patients who received nebulized β 2 bronchodilator prior\nto presentation, consider an extended observation period\nprior to discharge.\nIf PEF <50% on presentation, give prednisolone 40-50\nmg/day until recovery (minimum 5 days).\nIn all patients ensure treatment supply of inhaled steroid and\nβ 2 bronchodilator and check inhaler technique\nArrange GP follow-up within 2 working days post-discharge.\nFax or email discharge letter to GP\nRefer to asthma liaison nurse/chest clinic.\n\n![Asthma in Adults Management - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/respiratory-pharmacology-asthma-management/asthma-in-adults-management/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Asthma in Adults Management — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
1886e8c0-8fae-48b6-939e-81a7a886c4bc	7d6ad555-2a31-4365-ad9b-d6abfb9b25c1	sites-of-haemopoiesis	Sites of Haemopoiesis	article	Haemopoiesis Sites:\nImportant to remember:\nHaemopoietic Stem Cells: All blood cells\ndevelop from Haemopoietic Stem Cells\n\n![Sites of Haemopoiesis - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/sites-of-haemopoiesis/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Sites of Haemopoiesis — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n![Sites of Haemopoiesis - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/blood-physiology-coagulation-pathways/sites-of-haemopoiesis/figure_02_p1.png)\n*Sites of Haemopoiesis — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-14 23:52:49.070424+00	2026-11-02 05:50:26.18528+00	\N
48c820e2-3ed6-43fe-ad89-d76b9296d15e	69aa3acc-66d4-42b4-92ed-670a190ab74d	corticosteroids	Corticosteroids	article	Corticosteroids:\nCorticosteroids are a class of steroid hormones that are\nnaturally produced in the adrenal cortex as well\nas administered as medications for therapeutic purposes.\nWe have already discussed naturally produced corticosteroids\nin physiology. Now we will discuss in detail pharmacology of\nadministrated steroids.\nNaturally occurring Classes of corticosteroids: Glucocorticoids\nand mineralocorticoids, are involved in a wide range of\nphysiological processes, including stress response,\nimmune response, and regulation of inflammation.\nClassification of administrated steroids:\nInhaled steroids: (beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone)\nUses: Prophylaxis in chronic asthma if: Exacerbations in last\n2 years requiring systemic corticosteroid or nebulized\nbronchodilator or B-agonists used more than twice per\nweek or Symptoms disturb sleep more than twice per week.\nHay fever (Nasal spray)\n\n## Copd\n\nSide effects: Rare which include Oral candidiasis, hoarseness\nand sore mouth.\nNote:  In high doses systemic adverse effects can occur which\ninclude Adrenal suppression, Osteoporosis and Glaucoma.\nOral and injectable corticosteroids:\nOral and intravenous steroids: (prednisolone, dexamethasone,\nhydrocortisone).\nSpecific uses:\n\nPrednisolone: Has mainly glucocorticoid action 4 times more\npotent than hydrocortisone primary used for disease\nsuppression in autoimmune/Rheumatoid conditions, Temporal\narteritis, Asthma and acute adrenal insufficiency.\nHydrocortisone: Short acting primarily known for its\nglucocorticoid activity, but it also possesses mineralocorticoid\nactivity. Because of mineralocorticoid effect result into fluid\nretention means unsuitable for long-term treatment for\ndisease suppression but is used long-term with fludrocortisone\nin adrenal suppression.\nUses:\nAdrenal insufficiency\nAddison's disease\nAcute allergy\nAsthma\nShock\nAngioedema\nHypersensitivity reactions\nSevere inflammatory bowel disease\nRheumatic disease\nDexamethasone: Long acting primarily known for its very high\nglucocorticoid activity and insignificant mineralocorticoid\nactivity.\nUses:\nCerebral oedema\nPerioperatively\nInflammatory disorders\nCroup\nIntractable nausea\nDiagnosis of Cushing's syndrome (Overnight Dexamethasone\nsuppression test)\nTemporal arteritis\nSide effects\nPatients on long-term steroid use are at risk of the following:\n\nAdrenal suppression: In long-term use, e.g., prednisolone\n>7.5mg a day →  adrenal atrophy due to corticotrophin\nsuppression, therefore, withdraw steroid slowly to prevent\nAddisonian crisis\nSteroids should be doubled in times of stress/illness (unless on\nhigh dose) because the body cannot respond to increased\nrequirements, to prevent Addisonian crisis .\nInfections: Due to the suppression of the inflammatory\nresponse patients are more susceptible to infection.\nReactivation of dormant infections, e.g., TB, may occur.\nComplications:\nPatients who are not immune to chicken pox (not previously\ninfected) are at risk of severe chicken pox, with complications\nsuch as encephalitis, pneumonia, and hepatitis. Varicella\nzoster immunoglobulin is required for postexposure\nprophylaxis; this provides passive immunity.\nPatients should also be advised to avoid exposure to measles\nand should receive postexposure immunoglobulin within 72\nhours if they are exposed.\nAll patients on long-term steroids should carry a 'steroid card'\nto inform medical practitioners that they are on steroids or\nhave been on steroids (for up to a year as adrenal suppression\ncan be prolonged).	\N	[]	\N	f	4	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
0c73b319-2e2f-4923-8fe0-95f56ee54574	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-hepatitis	Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 26-year-old man presents with malaise, dark tea-coloured urine, pale acholic stools, and scleral icterus 3 weeks after returning from an overseas holiday where he consumed raw shellfish. Bloods show ALT 2400 IU/L, AST 1800 IU/L, and Bilirubin 95 umol/L. What serological marker confirms acute Hepatitis A infection?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/hepatitis/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Anti-HAV IgM antibody\n- **B.** Anti-HAV IgG antibody alone\n- **C.** Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg)\n- **D.** Anti-HCV antibody\n- **E.** Hepatitis D RNA\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAnti-HAV IgM is the diagnostic serological marker for acute Hepatitis A virus infection (detectable from symptom onset and persisting for 3-6 months). Anti-HAV IgG denotes past infection or immunity following vaccination. Hepatitis A is transmitted via the faecal-oral route (shellfish, contaminated water) and does NOT cause chronic hepatitis.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich serological marker is the FIRST detectable viral marker in the serum during acute Hepatitis B infection, and indicates that the patient is infectious?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/hepatitis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hepatitis B Surface Antigen (HBsAg)\n- **B.** Hepatitis B Surface Antibody (anti-HBs)\n- **C.** Hepatitis B Core Antibody IgG (anti-HBc IgG)\n- **D.** Hepatitis A antibody\n- **E.** Hepatitis C core antigen\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHBsAg is the first serological marker to appear in acute Hepatitis B (appearing 1-10 weeks after exposure). Persistence of HBsAg for >6 months defines chronic Hepatitis B infection. Anti-HBs indicates recovery, clinical immunity, or successful vaccination.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich Hepatitis virus requires concurrent active co-infection or super-infection with Hepatitis B virus (HBV) to replicate, and significantly accelerates progression to acute fulminant hepatic failure or cirrhosis?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/hepatitis/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hepatitis D Virus (HDV / Delta agent)\n- **B.** Hepatitis A Virus\n- **C.** Hepatitis C Virus\n- **D.** Hepatitis E Virus\n- **E.** Hepatitis G Virus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHepatitis D (Delta virus) is an incomplete, defective RNA virus that cannot synthesize its own envelope protein; it utilizes the Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) coat to assemble infectious virions. HDV infection occurs only as a co-infection with HBV or as a super-infection in a chronic HBV carrier, carrying the highest risk of fulminant liver failure and rapid cirrhosis.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nIn which specific patient population does acute Hepatitis E virus (HEV) infection carry an alarming, exceptionally high mortality rate of up to 20 to 25% from acute fulminant hepatic failure?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/hepatitis/figure_04_p11.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Pregnant women (particularly in the third trimester)\n- **B.** Children under 5 years of age\n- **C.** Young healthy athletic males\n- **D.** Elderly patients with osteoarthritis\n- **E.** Patients with asthma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHepatitis E (HEV genotypes 1 and 2, transmitted enterically through contaminated water in developing nations) is typically self-limiting in healthy adults. However, in pregnant women (especially in the 3rd trimester), HEV infection carries a catastrophic 20-25% mortality rate due to fulminant hepatic necrosis, eclampsia, and DIC.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nAccording to the King's College Hospital Criteria, what single laboratory parameter predicts >85% mortality and warrants immediate listing for emergency liver transplantation in Acute Liver Failure secondary to Paracetamol (Acetaminophen) toxicity?\n\n![Figure 5: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Hepatitis & Liver Failure)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/hepatitis/figure_05_p12.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Arterial pH < 7.30 at 24 hours after overdose (after adequate fluid resuscitation)\n- **B.** Serum ALT > 10,000 IU/L\n- **C.** Serum Bilirubin > 50 umol/L\n- **D.** Platelet count < 100 x 10^9/L\n- **E.** Paracetamol level at 4 hours of 150 mg/L\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nKing's College Criteria for liver transplant in paracetamol-induced acute liver failure: Arterial pH < 7.30 (after fluid resuscitation) irrespective of encephalopathy grade; OR all 3 of the following within a 24-hour period: Grade III/IV hepatic encephalopathy, Prothrombin Time >100 seconds (INR >6.5), and Serum Creatinine >300 umol/L.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	23	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f760d628-b845-4728-96ba-3078c7abecf4	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	inflammatory-bowel-disease-crohn-uc	Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC)	article	# Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nArea of bowel affected Risk factors Common associations Clinical features Ulcerative colitis\n- Usually only affects rectum and colon. Terminal ileum may be affected if 'backwash ileitis' occurs\n- No 'skip lesions'\n- Primarily affects mucosa and submocosa\n- Mucosal ulcers, inflammatory cell infiltrate and crypt abscesses seen\n- Family history\n- Not smoking (smoking protect against UC)\n- Not Appendicectomy- Appendicectomy is thought to be protective against UC\n- Drugs — NSAID Liver associations:\n-\n- Primary biliary cirrhosis\n- Chronic active hepatitis Primary sclerosing cholangitis Other systemic manifestations occur but less common than in Crohn's disease\n- Abdominal pain less prominent\n- Bloody diarrhoea in 90%. Passage of mucous common\n- Fever can be present Urgency, tenesmus and pre- defecation pain that is relieved by passage of stools common\n- FBC, ESR, C- reactive protein,U&E Crohn's disease Anywhere from the mouth to the\n- anus. these are called 'skip lesions' because they leave normal areas in between.\n- Transmural inflammation, lymphoid aggregates and neutrophil infiltrates present\n- Family history Smoking\n-\n- Infectious gastroenteritis Appendicectomy\n- Drugs— NSAID Systemic manifestations more common than in UC: Erythema nodosum\n- Pyoderma gangrenosum\n- Iritis/uveitis\n- Cholelithiasis Joint pain/arthropathy\n-\n- Abdominal pain\n- Diarrhoea (can be bloody)\n- Pallor, clubbing, aphthous mouth ulcers\n- Perianal pain or tenderness fatigue, malaise, anorexia, or fever\n-\n- FBC, ESR, C- reactive protein,U&E\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nFTS, serum B12, folate and D level¯GQFTS, serum 812, folate and D leve\n- Colonoscopy with histology of\n- multiple intestinal biopsy specimens\n- Histology findings may include crypt distortion, acute inflammatory changes of cryptitis, crypt abscesses, and infiltrative changes.\n- Aminosalicylates — mesalazine and sulfasalazine (if corticosteroid Cl or not tolerated). previous\n- Immunosuppressive drugs — Next azathioprine, mercaptopurine.\n- Biologic therapy — the anti- tumour necrosis factor alpha monoclonal antibody agents infliximab and adalimumab Management & complications Complications\n- Fistulae rare 20-fold increase in 20-year risk of colonic carcinoma\n- Iron deficiency anaemia Colonoscopy: Findings may include discontinuous colonic or ileal inflammation or ulceration, a 'cobblestone' appearance, and rectal sparing. Endoscopy and biopsy gold-\n- standard for assessing mucosal activity\n- Corticosteroids —prednisolone\n- Immunosuppressive drugs — azathioprine, mercaptopurine agents infliximab and adalimumab\n- Aminosalicylates — mesalazine and sulfasalazine (if corticosteroid Cl or not tolerated). To maintain remission in people\n- with ileocolonic Crohn's disease who have had a complete macroscopic resection within the last 3 months- NICE guideline recommends azathioprine in combination with up to 3 months' postoperative metronidazole should be considered.\n- Complications\n- Fistulae and abscess formation common Slight increased risk of colonic carcinoma\n- 812 deficiency common\n\n- Iron aeTlclency anaemia\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/inflammatory-bowel-disease-crohn-uc/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/inflammatory-bowel-disease-crohn-uc/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/inflammatory-bowel-disease-crohn-uc/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/inflammatory-bowel-disease-crohn-uc/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n*Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/inflammatory-bowel-disease-crohn-uc/figure_05_p4.jpeg)\n*Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 4 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	24	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 07:07:45.576678+00	\N
145dbbd1-5771-4bab-9090-1d8ac650e9a8	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-inflammatory-bowel-disease	Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC) — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 26-year-old male presents with a 6-week history of bloody diarrhoea (8-10 episodes daily), lower abdominal tenesmus, and urgency. Flexible sigmoidoscopy reveals continuous, confluent mucosal erythema, loss of vascular pattern, and superficial ulceration starting at the anal verge and extending continuously into the sigmoid colon without skip lesions. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC))](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/inflammatory-bowel-disease/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Ulcerative Colitis (UC)\n- **B.** Crohn's Disease\n- **C.** Ischaemic colitis\n- **D.** Infectious amoebic colitis\n- **E.** Collagenous colitis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nUlcerative Colitis is characterized by continuous, confluent superficial mucosal inflammation that starts in the rectum (proctitis) and extends proximally into the colon without skip areas. The rectum is involved in >95% of cases. In contrast, Crohn's disease causes transmural, patchy ('skip lesions') inflammation with cobblestoning, deep linear ulcers, and can involve any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to the Truelove and Witts criteria, what clinical and laboratory parameters define Acute Severe Ulcerative Colitis (ASUC), a life-threatening medical emergency?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC))](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/inflammatory-bowel-disease/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** >=6 bloody bowel motions per day, PLUS at least one sign of systemic toxicity (Pulse >90 bpm, Temp >37.8C, Hb <105 g/L, or ESR >30 mm/h / CRP >45 mg/L)\n- **B.** 2-3 soft stools per day with normal pulse and ESR\n- **C.** Constipation alternating with diarrhoea\n- **D.** Isolated perianal skin tag without rectal bleeding\n- **E.** Abdominal pain relieved by defecation without blood\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTruelove and Witts criteria for Acute Severe Ulcerative Colitis (ASUC): >=6 bloody stools per day PLUS at least one feature of systemic disturbance: temperature >37.8C, pulse >90 bpm, haemoglobin <105 g/L, or ESR >30 mm/h (or CRP >45 mg/L). ASUC carries risk of toxic megacolon and perforation, requiring immediate admission for IV corticosteroids.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the recommended first-line medical therapy for an adult presenting with Acute Severe Ulcerative Colitis according to NICE and BSG guidelines?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC))](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/inflammatory-bowel-disease/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous Hydrocortisone (100mg QDS) or IV Methylprednisolone (60mg daily), plus prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin\n- **B.** Oral mesalazine 1g daily alone\n- **C.** Oral loperamide 4mg TDS\n- **D.** Immediate total proctocolectomy within 1 hour\n- **E.** Oral budesonide 3mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFirst-line therapy for ASUC: IV Hydrocortisone (100mg QDS) or IV Methylprednisolone (60mg/day). Prophylactic LMWH is mandatory because IBD flare is a major prothrombotic risk factor for venous thromboembolism. Response is formally assessed on Day 3 using the Travis/Oxford criteria (stool frequency >8/day or CRP >45 predicts 85% colectomy rate, indicating rescue therapy with Infliximab or Ciclosporin).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 30-year-old female with severe ulcerative colitis develops acute, massive abdominal distension, severe pain, tachycardia (HR 130 bpm), and fever (39.0C). Abdominal radiograph demonstrates transverse colonic dilation measuring 7.5 cm with loss of haustration. What catastrophic complication has occurred?\n\n![Figure 4: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn & UC))](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/inflammatory-bowel-disease/figure_04_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Toxic Megacolon\n- **B.** Small bowel volvulus\n- **C.** Intussusception\n- **D.** Acute appendicitis\n- **E.** Paralytic ileus without inflammation\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nToxic megacolon is non-obstructive acute toxic dilation of the colon (>6 cm, typically transverse colon on plain radiograph) associated with systemic toxicity (fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis, altered sensorium). It is caused by deep transmural inflammation paralyzing the colonic smooth muscle plexus. It carries extreme risk of perforation; emergency subtotal colectomy is indicated if medical therapy fails within 24-48 hours.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich of the following clinical features and complications is characteristic of Crohn's Disease but is NOT typical of Ulcerative Colitis?\n\n- **A.** Transmural inflammation leading to strictures, enterocutaneous / enteroenteric fistulae, deep fissures, perianal abscesses, and non-caseating granulomas on biopsy\n- **B.** Superficial mucosal inflammation limited to the colon\n- **C.** Continuous inflammation always involving the rectum\n- **D.** Crypt abscesses without granulomas\n- **E.** Complete absence of small bowel involvement\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nCrohn's disease produces transmural inflammation (affecting all layers of the bowel wall). Pathological hallmarks: skip lesions, cobblestone mucosa, non-caseating epithelioid granulomas (~50%), transmural lymphoid aggregates, fibrotic strictures, and penetrating sinus tracts causing fistulae (perianal, enterocutaneous, colovesical) and intra-abdominal abscesses.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	25	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
61d58deb-e648-4fdc-ba04-ee77b7a8855f	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	acute-mesenteric-ischemic-bowel-disease	Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease	article	# Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\n- Definition Risk factors Clinical feature Investigations Acute mesenteric ischaemia This is an umbrella term covering a number Of conditions, includingacute mesenteric arterial embolus and thrombus, mesenteric venous thrombus and non- occlusive mesenteric ischaemia (NOMI). They all have the features of impaired blood transfusion to the intestine Age>50 years History of smoking Hypercoagulable states (strong risk factor for mesenteric venous thrombosis)\n- Afib, MI, structural heart disease History of vasculitis Of proportion. there may be minimal or no tenderness. later sta s- typical symptoms of peritonism develop, with rebound guarding and tenderness Urgent CT contrast/angiogram FBC (elevated WCC) ABGs & Serum lactate (metabolic acidosis and elevated lactate)\n- LFTS ECG\n- ECHO Chronic mesenteric ischaemia This is a chronic atherosclerotic disease of the vessels supplying the intestine. It is also known as intestinalangina. Usually all three major mesenteric arteries are involved Common in female age b/w 50 and\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 70:\n- Generally caused by factors predisposi ng to atherosclerosis\n- eg, smoking, hypertension, diabetes mellitus and hyperlipidaemia. weight loss, postprandial pain ('intestinal angina') and a fear of eating nausea, vomiting, or bowel irregularity\n\n## Clinical Examination & Assessment\n- reveal\n- vague abdominal tenderness disproportionateto the severity of the pain\n- FBC,LFTs Arteriography (gold standard) Mesenteric duplex ultrasonography Ischaemic colitis This is caused by a compromise of the blood circulation supplying the colon. Ischaemic colitis may also be caused by venous occlusion. The average age for diagnosis is\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 70:\n- Thrombosis- Inferior mesenteric artery thrombosis. Emboli\n- Mesenteric arterial emboli. Strangulated hernia or volvulus. Vasculitis Hypercoagulable states acute-onset abdominal pain\n- most frequently located in the left iliac fossa. Tenderness to palpation over the affected bowel Nausea and vomiting loose motion containingdark blood (late stage) Urgent CT contrast/angiogram\n- FBC (elevated WCC) ABCs & Serum lactate (metabolic acidosis and elevated lactate)\n- LFTS\n- AXR Colonoscopy may show blue, swollen mucosa Barium enema shows 'thumb orintinø' in the earlv nhase that\n\n- Medical thera Start IV fluid and antibiotics NG tube Should be sited Surgical intervention Urgent laparotomy should be done for patients with peritonitis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n- The goals of surgery include re- establishmentof the blood supply to the ischaemic bowel; resection of all non-viable regions and preservation of all viablebowel. Endovascular revascularisation procedures may have a role with partial arterial occlusion.  Asymptomatic patients Managed conservatively, with smoking cessation and antiplatelettherapy. Symptomatic chronic mesenteric ischaemia open or endovascular revascularisation, Postoperative complication Renal failure Next may last for several days Medical therapy Start IV fluid and antibiotics The ischaemia may be transient and resolve once the cause of the hypoperfusion has been alleviated Surgical intervention Segmental colectomy Patients who have an acute episode of colonic ischaemia that evolves intoa segmental colitis pattern with symptoms persisting for\n- 2 weeks, or who develop a protein-losing colonopathyor Recurrent sepsis in a patient who has symptomatically recovered from an acute episode Of colonic ischaemia\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-mesenteric-ischemic-bowel-disease/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-mesenteric-ischemic-bowel-disease/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-mesenteric-ischemic-bowel-disease/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n*Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 3 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	26	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-02 07:07:45.576678+00	\N
6558fd84-cdc0-40fd-b90e-aa91bd87df3e	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-ischemic-bowel	Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 74-year-old female with atrial fibrillation not taking anticoagulation presents with severe, agonizing, unremitting mid-abdominal pain for 3 hours. On abdominal examination, the abdomen is soft and non-distended with no guarding or rebound tenderness. What is the classic clinical hallmark of early Acute Mesenteric Ischaemia (AMI)?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/ischemic-bowel/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Severe abdominal pain completely disproportionate to the benign physical examination findings\n- **B.** Board-like rigid peritonitis from the first minute of onset\n- **C.** Painless gross haematochezia\n- **D.** Palpable pulsatile mass\n- **E.** Normal white cell count and normal heart rate\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThe classic, cardinal presentation of acute mesenteric ischaemia is severe, agonizing abdominal pain out of proportion to physical examination findings (the abdomen remains soft and non-tender initially because the visceral ischaemia has not yet progressed to transmural bowel infarction and somatic parietal peritoneal irritation).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common cause of Acute Mesenteric Ischaemia, accounting for approximately 50% of all cases?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/ischemic-bowel/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA) Embolism (originating from atrial fibrillation, recent MI, or endocarditis)\n- **B.** Mesenteric venous thrombosis\n- **C.** Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia (NOMI)\n- **D.** Inferior mesenteric artery dissection\n- **E.** Celiac trunk thrombosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute SMA embolism causes ~50% of cases of acute mesenteric ischaemia. Cardiac emboli (from AF, ventricular mural thrombus post-MI, or valvular disease) lodge typically 3-10 cm distal to the origin of the SMA (just distal to the middle colic artery takeoff), sparing the proximal jejunum but devascularizing the ileum and ascending colon.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the definitive diagnostic imaging modality of choice for suspected Acute Mesenteric Ischaemia in the ED?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Mesenteric & Ischaemic Bowel Disease)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/ischemic-bowel/figure_03_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Multiphase Contrast-Enhanced CT Angiography (CTA) of the Abdomen and Pelvis (arterial and portal venous phases)\n- **B.** Plain abdominal X-ray\n- **C.** Abdominal ultrasound with Doppler alone\n- **D.** Colonoscopy\n- **E.** Barium enema\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBiphasic Contrast CT Angiography (arterial and portal venous phases) is the gold standard diagnostic test (sensitivity >95%). It demonstrates direct arterial filling defects (occlusion of the SMA), bowel wall thickening or lack of enhancement, mesenteric stranding, pneumatosis intestinalis, or portomesenteric venous gas.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nAn 80-year-old male with generalised atherosclerosis presents with mild lower left quadrant crampy pain and bright red rectal bleeding 12 hours after an episode of transient intraoperative hypotension. Contrast CT demonstrates thumbprinting (mucosal oedema) confined to the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction. What condition is this?\n\n- **A.** Colonic Ischaemia (Ischaemic Colitis) affecting 'watershed' areas (Griffiths' point and Sudeck's point)\n- **B.** Acute mesenteric arterial embolism\n- **C.** Sigmoid diverticulitis\n- **D.** Ulcerative colitis\n- **E.** Infectious dysentery\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIschaemic colitis (colonic ischaemia) is non-occlusive hypoperfusion of the microvasculature of the colon, typically triggered by transient hypotension or shock. It characteristically affects 'watershed' areas with vulnerable dual marginal blood supply: the splenic flexure (Griffiths' point between SMA and IMA) and rectosigmoid junction (Sudeck's point). It presents with crampy pain and bloody diarrhoea; over 80-90% resolve with supportive care.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat late radiological sign on abdominal CT indicates extensive, irreversible transmural bowel infarction and impending gangrenous rupture in mesenteric ischaemia?\n\n- **A.** Pneumatosis Intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall) and Hepatic Portal Venous Gas\n- **B.** Simple small bowel fluid levels\n- **C.** Mild mesenteric lymphadenopathy\n- **D.** Normal mucosal enhancement\n- **E.** Colonic diverticulosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPneumatosis intestinalis (intramural gas in the bowel wall) and hepatic portal venous gas (gas bubbles tracking via mesenteric veins into the periphery of the liver) are ominous radiological signs signifying transmural bowel wall infarction, bacterial gas translocation, and intestinal gangrene, mandating immediate emergency laparotomy.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	27	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e15958e5-884d-4cea-ba55-2b9d171c14f3	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	acute-jaundice-differential-in-the-ed	Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED	article	# Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nPre-hepatic\n- Haem molecules are degraded in macrophages via biliverdin to bilirubin. This occurs mainly in the spleen and liver but also in other tissues such as the skin and kidneys. . Causes Haemolytic anaemias\n- Sickle cell disease, thalassemia major, Hereditary spherocytosis, G6PD, Vitamin B12 deficiency, Autoimmune disorders) Drugs\n- Haemolysisof RBCs (for example, methyldopa, and sulphasalazine).\n- Hepatitis (for example, paracetamol overdose, pyrazinamide, rifampicin, and isoniazid).\n- Cholestasis (for example, co- amoxiclav, flucloxacillin, oral contraceptives, HRT, chlorpromazine, and corticosteroids).\n- Hepatic idiosyncratic reactions (for example, aspirin, NSAIDs, amiodarone, sodium valproate, and minocycline) Gilbert's syndron\n- e\n- Plasma bilirubin concentrations can rise in people with Gilbert's syndrome during intercurrent illness, periods of stress, when Intra-hepatic\n- Once in the liver, unconjugated bilirubin is selectively removed from the blood by hepatocytes. It is then conjugated in the hepatocytes with glucuronic acid, becomes water soluble, and can be excreted in bile. Causes Viral infection\n- HepatitisA, B, C, D, and E, Epstein-Barr virus, HIV infection. Alcohol Alcohol-related liver disease is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in Europe. Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)\n- Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease refers to excessive fat accumulation (steatosis) in the hepatocytes. Autoimmune liver disorders. Primarv biliary cholangitis (common in middle age women, 95% of people with PBC have high titres of anti- mitochondrial antibodies). Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC is more common in men. Nearly 70% of people with PSC also have inflammatory bowel disease and it can lead Post-hepatic\n- Soluble bilirubin is transported through the liver and cystic ducts in bile and then stored in the gallbladder or passes into the duodenum. In the intestine, some bilirubin is excreted in the stool and the rest is metabolized by gut flora into urobilinogen and reabsorbed and excreted by the kidneys. Causes\n- Gallstones\n- Biliary colic\n- this describes pain in the epigastrium or right upper quadrant that is usually persistent, lasting from 15 minutes up to several hours. Pain lasting more than 24 hours or accompanied by fever suggests acute cholecystitis.\n- Acute cholecystitis\n- it is inflammation or infection of the gallbladder with pain similar to biliary colic along with fever and tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n- Acute cholangitis\n- It is an inflammation or infection of the common bile duct. It is classically diagnosed by the symptoms of Charcot's triad fever, pain, and jaundice. Surqicalstrictures '&liary strictures and infectio\n\nmenstruation Criqler-Naiiarsyndrome\n- presents in neonates with progressive jaundice in the first few days of life. If untreated it leads to bilirubin encephalopathy (kernicterus) and death. cnolanglocarcmo Antinuclear cytoplasmic antibody is present in 30% of people with PSC). Autoimmune hepatitis Metabolic causes of intra- hepatic iaundice Dubin-Johnson and Rotor's syndromes, Haemochromatosis, Wilson's disease. Druqs (mentioned in pre-hepatic) Maliqnancv of the biliarv system should be considered in people with post-operative jaundice.\n- Biliary strictures can occur following liver transplantation or cholecystectomy. Extra-hepatic ma lig nancv Pancreatic cancer\n- Pancreatitis\n- The most common causes of pancreatitis are alcohol misuse and gallstones. Parasitic infection\n- Plasmodium falciparum, Fasciola, Chlonorchis.\n- Hepatocellular carcinoma, Cholangiocarcinoma, Gallbladder cancer. Jaundice in newborn\n\n- Physiological jaundice Breastmilk jaundice Causes Risk factors At usually appears at 2 days of age, peaks on days 3—5, and then decreases, usually by around day 10\n- Physiological jaundice can occur in breastfed and formula-fed babies and results from increased bilirubin load, decreased uptake by the liver, decreased conjugation in the liver, and decreased excretion into bile.\n- Decreased gestational age/preterm delivery.\n- Low infant birth weight.\n- Development of jaundice within first 24 hours Of life.\n- Male sex\n- Visible bruising, Cephalhaematoa\n- Maternal age older than 25 years, or Maternal DM\n- Asian, European, or native American ethnicity.\n- Sibling born with jaundice.\n- Dehydration, Poor caloric intake, breastfeeding\n- Reassurance & Advice leaflet Management At is prolongation of physiological jaundice in breastfed babies. At usually presents within the first week of life (day 2—4) and peaks on day 7—\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 15:\n- In some cases, it can persist for up to 12 weeks. Ahe mechanism is unknown, but possible reasons include a decreased caloric intake and inhibition of hepatic bilirubin excretion Same as in physiological\n- First line Reassurance\n- Temporary breast-feeding cessation and supplemental feeding maybe considered.\n- Second-line — phototherapy and hydration.\n- Third-line — exchange transfusion Pathological jaundice Vit usually appears less than 24 hours. At can be caused bv a number of factors. including:\n- most commonly Rhesus or ABO incompatibility.\n- biliary atresia Sepsis\n- Metabolic disorders (for example galactosaemia, hereditary fructose intolerance, alpha-I antitrypsin deficiency, hypothyroidism)\n- Gilbert's syndrome and Cigler-Najjar syndrome\n- G6PD deficiency. Same there is jaundice with features Of bilirubin encephalopathy (for example atypical sleepiness, poor feeding, irritability, vomiting, hypotonia followed by hypertonia).\n- Arranqe urgent admission to a neonatal or paediatric unit\n- within 2 hours if jaundice first appears at less than 24 hours of age. Arranae uraent admission to a neonatal or paediatric unit\n- as soon as possible and to be seen within 6 hours (using clinical\n\n- previous  uagemem regaralng more urgent rererral o admission) if:\n- Jaundice first appears at more than 7 days of age.\n- The neonate is unwell (for example, lethargy, fever, vomiting, irritability).\n- Gestational age is of less than 35 weeks.\n- Prolonged jaundice is suspected — that is a gestational age Of less than 37 weeks with more than 21 days Of jaundice; or a gestational age Of 37 weeks or more with more than 14 days of jaundice.\n- There are feeding problems and/or concerns about weight, particularly in breastfed infants.\n- Pale stools and dark urine are noted.\n- Specialist treatment include\n- Phototherapy, hydration and exchange\n- nsfusion (if not respond to phototherapy Next\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-jaundice-differential-in-the-ed/figure_01_p2.jpeg)\n*Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-jaundice-differential-in-the-ed/figure_02_p3.jpeg)\n*Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 3 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-jaundice-differential-in-the-ed/figure_03_p4.jpeg)\n*Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/acute-jaundice-differential-in-the-ed/figure_04_p5.jpeg)\n*Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 5 Reference)*\n\n![Acute Jaundice Differential in the ED - Clinical Figure 5](/les