3669a546-cedc-43b0-9a7f-663fb1447481	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	sba-quiz-gastroenterology-pyloric-stenosis	Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 4-week-old first-born male infant presents with a 5-day history of non-bilious, forceful projectile vomiting occurring immediately after every feed. He remains ravenously hungry and eager to feed again immediately after vomiting. What is the classic metabolic and electrolyte derangement in Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis (IHPS)?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/pyloric-stenosis/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Hypochloraemic, Hypokalaemic Metabolic Alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria\n- **B.** Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis\n- **C.** Hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis\n- **D.** Normal electrolytes and normal acid-base balance\n- **E.** Hyponatraemic respiratory acidosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPersistent gastric vomiting causes massive loss of hydrochloric acid (H+ and Cl-) and water. The kidneys attempt to compensate by retaining sodium at the expense of potassium and hydrogen ions in the distal tubules. The classic pathognomonic biochemical derangement is Hypochloraemic, Hypokalaemic Metabolic Alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria. It is a medical emergency requiring fluid resuscitation before surgery.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat physical examination finding is considered pathognomonic of Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis during a test feed?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis)](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology/pyloric-stenosis/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Palpation of a firm, mobile, olive-sized mass ('pyloric olive') in the right upper quadrant / epigastrium with visible gastric peristaltic waves from left to right\n- **B.** Sausage-shaped mass in the right lower quadrant\n- **C.** Distended tympanitic abdomen with absent bowel sounds\n- **D.** Rebound tenderness at McBurney's point\n- **E.** Massive splenomegaly\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nDuring or immediately after a milk feed (when the infant is relaxed), inspection may reveal visible gastric peristaltic waves traversing the epigastrium from left to right. Deep gentle palpation in the right hypochondrium/epigastrium reveals the hypertrophied pyloric muscle: a smooth, firm, mobile, olive-shaped mass (2-3 cm diameter).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the diagnostic imaging modality of choice to confirm Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis, and what sonographic measurements are diagnostic?\n\n- **A.** Pyloric Ultrasound; Pyloric muscle wall thickness >= 3 mm (or >=4 mm) and channel length >= 14-15 mm\n- **B.** Plain abdominal X-ray alone\n- **C.** Abdominal CT with IV contrast\n- **D.** Upper GI endoscopy\n- **E.** Nuclear gastric emptying scan\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTransabdominal Ultrasound is the non-invasive gold standard (sensitivity >98-100%). Diagnostic sonographic criteria: pyloric muscle thickness >= 3.0 mm (in cross-section) and pyloric canal length >= 14-15 mm (in longitudinal section), accompanied by non-opening of the pyloric channel during real-time observation.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhy is Pyloric Stenosis considered a 'Medical Emergency, Not a Surgical Emergency' upon arrival in the ED?\n\n- **A.** General anaesthesia in the presence of uncorrected severe hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis and dehydration carries severe risks of fatal cardiac arrhythmias, hypoventilation, and prolonged apnoea; surgery must be delayed until electrolytes and hydration are completely normalised\n- **B.** Pyloric stenosis never requires surgery\n- **C.** Surgery must be performed within 15 minutes of arrival\n- **D.** Oral feeding must be continued throughout\n- **E.** Metabolic alkalosis protects the infant from shock\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nInfantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is never an immediate surgical emergency. Administering general anaesthesia and muscle relaxants to an infant with severe dehydration, hypokalaemia, and metabolic alkalosis causes post-operative hypoventilation, apnoea, and fatal arrhythmias. Fluid resuscitation with 0.9% normal saline with potassium chloride is mandatory until chloride >100, bicarbonate <30, and urine output is restored before proceeding to surgery.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the definitive surgical procedure of choice for Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis?\n\n- **A.** Ramstedt's Pyloromyotomy (longitudinal incision of the hypertrophic pyloric muscle down to, but preserving, the underlying submucosa and mucosa)\n- **B.** Partial gastrectomy\n- **C.** Gastrojejunostomy bypass\n- **D.** Endoscopic balloon dilatation\n- **E.** Total duodenectomy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFredet-Ramstedt Pyloromyotomy (performed open via circumumbilical incision or laparoscopically) is the definitive surgical cure. The serosa and hypertrophied circular muscle of the pylorus are incised longitudinally down to the submucosa, allowing the intact mucosal lumen to herniate freely outwards and relieving the gastric outlet obstruction. Full feeds can typically be resumed within 24 hours.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	39	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d8f975a4-9f1e-4a85-9173-fe0cc5080e41	49448ce3-557e-470c-adb4-041498fa9ccc	upper-gi-bleeding	Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation	article	# Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding & Resuscitation\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nwith lower GI bleeding (LGIB), which is distal to the duodenojejunal junction at the ligament of Treitz.\n- Melena\n- overwhelmingly associated with UGIB.\n- Hematochezia — more common in LGIB then UGIB\n- Weakness & fatigue\n- hypotension or tachycardia\n- If bleed due to chronic liver disease, the following additional features can be seen:\n- Encephalopathy with or without asterixis\n- Scleral icterus Spider telangiectasias, also known as spider angioma, on the face, chest, or abdomen Gynecomastia\n- Hepatomegaly or splenomegaly\n- Ascites, caput medusae with or without Cruveilhier-Baumgarten bruit Hypogonadism\n- Terry's nails (white nails)\n- Palmar erythema\n\n## Risk Stratification\nBoth the Glasgow-Blatchford and Rockall scores are useful tools to aid the clinician in identifying high-risk upper Cl bleeds.\n- Glasgow-Blatchford Score\n- The Glasgow-Blatchford score is the risk assessrnent tool of choice in the emergency department.\n\nIt is calculated using FOUR component:\n- Medical history (hepatic disease, heart failure),\n- Symptoms (melaena, syncope),\n-\n- Signs (tachycardia, blood pressure), Blood tests (haemoglobin, urea).\n-\n\n- Risk marker Range 6.5-7.5 Urea 8.0-9.9 10.0\n- 24.9\n- 25.o Haemoglobin (g/dL) 12\n- 12.9 for men 10.0- 11 9 <IO.O Haemoglobin (g/dL ) 10.0- 11 9 for women 10.0 Systolic blood 100-109 pressure (mmHg) 90-99 Pulse\n- 100 Other markers Melaena Syncope Hepatic disease Cardiac failure Score 2 3 4 5 1 3 6 1 6 1 2 3 1 1 2 2 2\n\nThe maximum score is\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 29:\nNICE recommends that patients with a Blatchford score of O can be considered for early discharge with outpatient endoscopy follow-up.\n- Rockall score\n- The score consists of three clinical parameters pre-endoscopic (age, presence of shock, and comorbidity) and two parameters that rely on endoscopic findings (blood and diagnosis). The maximum pre-endoscopy Rockall score is 7 and post-endoscopy is II.\n\n- Variable Age Shock (BP & Pulse) Comorbidity Endoscopy Diagnosis BP>IOO p<100 None No blood or dark spot only Mallory- Weiss tear 60-79 BP>IOO P>IOO All other diagnoses BP<IOO Cardiac failure or IHD Blood in upper GI tract, adherent clot or spurting vessel GI tract malignancy Renal failure, liver failure or disseminated malignancy\n\n## Risk Stratification\nkey_points\n\n### Low-Risk Criteria\nGlasgow-Blatchford Score O — consider discharge with outpatient\n- endoscopy Moderate risk:admit to appropriate inpatient specialty for urgent endoscopy\n-\n\n### High-Risk Features (Red Flags)\nRockall Score (pre-endoscopy) 23, haemodynamic instability, known varices — resuscitate, admit to critical care area for emergency endoscopy.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nVBG- can help to assess haemoglobin level quickly.\n- FBC Coagulation screen (derangement of clotting in liver diseases)\n- Liver function tests (identifying liver disease)\n- Crossmatch (type specific or full cross-match depending on urgency)\n-\n- Chest x-ray, 12-lead ECG Biopsies of the gastric mucosa taken during endoscopy should be sent to the\n- laboratory for H pylori testing and histology. Endoscopy is highly effective in diagnosing and treating most causes of UGIB at\n- the same time.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nInitial management UGIB causing hypotension, tachycardia, orthostasis, or other signs of hypovolemic shock must be managed swiftly, and patients should be considered for admission to the intensive care unit.\n\n- Resuscitation\n- Two large-bore intravenous lines should be placed immediately for adequate venous access.\n- Crystalloid fluids — should be infused to maintain adequate blood pressure.\n- Blood product transfusions\n- Transfuse patients with massive bleeding with blood, platelets and clotting factors in line with local protocols for managing massive bleeding.\n- Do not offer platelet transfusion to patients who are not actively bleeding and are haemodynamically stable.\n- Offer platelet transfusion to patients who are actively bleeding and have a platelet count of less than 50 x 109/1itre.\n- Offer fresh frozen plasma to patients who are actively bleeding and have a prothrombin time (or international normalised ratio) or activated partial thromboplastin time greater than 1.5 times normal. If a patient's fibrinogen level remains less than 1.5 g/litre despite fresh frozen plasma use, offer cryoprecipitate as well.\n- Offer prothrombin complex concentrate to patients who are taking warfarin and actively bleeding.\n- Timing-of-endoscopy-\n- Offer endoscopy to unstable patients with severe acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding immediately after resuscitation.\n- Offer endoscopy within 24 hours of admission to all other patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof non-variceal bleeding\n\nnot use adrenaline as monotherapy for the endoscopic treatment of non-variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n- For the endoscopic treatment of non-variceal uppecgastrointestinaLbleedjng, use one-of-the-following;\n- a mechanical method (for example, clips) with or without adrenaline\n- thermal coagulation with adrenaline\n- fibrin or thrombin with adrenaline.\n- Offer a repeat endoscopy to patients who re-bleed with a view to further endoscopic treatment or emergency surgery.\n- Offer interventional radiology to unstable patients who re-bleed after endoscopic treatment. Refer urgently for surgery if interventional radiology is not promptly available.\n- Proton pump inhibitors (PPI therapy)=Offer proton pump inhibitors to patients with non-variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding and stigmata of recent haemorrhage shown at endoscopy.\n- Do not offer acid-suppression drugs (proton pump inhibitors or H2-receptor antagonists) before endoscopy to patients with suspected non-variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nof variceal bleeding\n- Offer—terlipressin-ocvasopressin, somatostatin to patients with suspected variceal bleeding at presentation. Stop treatment after definitive haemostasis has been achieved, or after 5 days, unless there is another indication for its use.\n\n- Offer prophylactic antibiotic therapy at presentation to patients with suspected or confirmed variceal bleeding.\n- In oesophageal varices\n- Use endoscopic band ligation in patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from oesophageal varices.\n- Non-selective beta blocker NSBB (propranolol or nadolol) +VBL (variceal band ligation) combination therapy are recommended as secondary prophylaxis.\n- VBL alone is used to eradicate varices if there are contraindications or intolerance to combined use with NSBB\n- Consider transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunts (TIPS) if bleeding from oesophageal varices is not controlled by band ligation alone or combined VBL and NSBB.\n- 'mgastric varices\n- Offer endoscopic injection of N-butyI-2-cyanoacryIate to patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from gastric varices.\n- Offer TIPS if bleeding from gastric varices is not controlled by endoscopic injection of N-butyl-2-cyanoacrylate.\n- A balloon tamponade device can be used to quell the bleeding until the shunt is placed (Sengstaken-BIakemore for esophageal varices, Linton-Nachlas for gastric varices).\n- Note: NSBB or variceal band ligation (VBL) also recommended in grade II and Ill oesophageal varices as primary prophylaxis of variceal haemorrhage in newly diagnosed cirrhosis.\n- Pharmacological treatrnent with propranolol as first line. VBI- is offered if there are contraindications to NSBB.\n- Some studies suggest NSBB and VBL decrease the mortality in variceal bleeding.\n\n- Prevention\n- Stop other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (including cyclooxygenase-2 [COX-2] inhibitors) during the acute phase in patients presenting with upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n- Continue low-dose aspirin for secondary prevention of vascular events in patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding in whom haemostasis has been achieved.\n- Acute variceal bleeding algorithm\n\n- Acute variceal bleeding in a cirrhotic patient RESUSCITATION Consider Intubation High flow 02 Intravenous access Blood Transfusion Antibiotics Terlipressin/somatostatin Gastroscopy as soon as possible but within 24 hours Oesophageal Variceal Bleeding Variceal Band Ligation Control of bleeding Secondary prophylaxis after day 5 with VBL+NSBB or NSBB/VBL Childs B with active bleeding or Childs C (IO- 13 points) and within 72h of Failure to control bleeding Resuscitation +/- balloon tamponade Gastric Variceal Bleeding Cyanoacrylate injection or thrombin. Secondary prophylaxis after day 5 with cyanoacrylate injection. Consider NSBB or thrombin. TIPSS is an option if large/multiple varices.\n\nalone (see text) Rebleeding index bleed* * Covered TIPSS if patent portal vein* Rebleeding\n- depending on local resources or consider referral to specialist centre.\n- consider shunt surgery in well compensated patients or if TIPSS not feasible. In segmental portal hypertension consider splenectomy or splenic artery embolization\n- GOV-2 and IGV. GOV-I to be treated as oesophageal varices.\n- TIPSS can be considered depending on local resources and clinical judgement. VBL — variceal band ligation. NSBB — non selective beta-blockers. GOV-I — gastro-oesophageal varices type\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\n- GOV-2\n- gastro-oesophageal varices type\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nIGV — isolated gastric varices. previOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Upper Gi Bleeding - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n*Upper Gi Bleeding — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 4 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Gi Bleeding - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_02_p6.jpeg)\n*Upper Gi Bleeding — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 6 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Gi Bleeding - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_03_p12.jpeg)\n*Upper Gi Bleeding — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 12 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Gi Bleeding - Clinical Figure 4](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_04_p13.jpeg)\n*Upper Gi Bleeding — Clinical Figure 4 (Page 13 Reference)*\n\n![Upper Gi Bleeding - Clinical Figure 5](/lessons/images/sba/gastroenterology-emergencies/upper-gi-bleeding/figure_05_p13.jpeg)\n*Upper Gi Bleeding — Clinical Figure 5 (Page 13 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	40	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
5f8456c4-a013-436a-a46d-6409825f09b7	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	clostridium-spp-bacteria	Clostridium Spp - Bacteria	article	Clostridium Species\nAll the Clostridium species are Gram +Ve spore forming\nobligate anaerobes.\nTetanus prone wound:\nClean wounds are defined as:\nwounds less than 6 hours old, non-penetrating with negligible\ntissue damage\nTetanus-prone wounds include:\npuncture-type injuries acquired in a contaminated\nenvironment and likely therefore to contain tetanus spores\ne.g. gardening injuries\ncompound fractures\nwounds or burns with systemic sepsis.\ncertain animal bites and scratches\nHigh risk tetanus-prone wound:\nheavy contamination with material likely to contain tetanus\nspores e.g. soil, manure\nwounds or burns that show extensive devitalised tissue.\nwounds or burns that require surgical intervention that is\ndelayed for more than six hours are high risk even if the\ncontamination was not initially heavy\nTetanus immunization schedule:\n\n![Clostridium Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/clostridium-spp-bacteria/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Clostridium Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\nMore easy to remeber:\n\n![Clostridium Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/clostridium-spp-bacteria/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Clostridium Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\n![Clostridium Spp - Bacteria - Clinical Figure 3](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/clostridium-spp-bacteria/figure_03_p2.png)\n*Clostridium Spp - Bacteria — Clinical Figure 3 (Page 2 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	0	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
1d045981-59a2-480b-bbd5-5ff984353b7c	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-45	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 45	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 45\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	45	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
6d6e8fd0-21fa-4d76-8b9f-62cf57dff44a	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-hypocalcemia	Hypocalcaemia & Tetany — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Hypocalcaemia & Tetany — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 34-year-old woman presents 24 hours after an uneventful total thyroidectomy with circumoral paresthesias, numbness in her fingertips, and painful muscle cramping. Tapping gently over the facial nerve anterior to the earlobe produces involuntary twitching of the ipsilateral facial and lip muscles. What is this classic clinical sign termed?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hypocalcaemia & Tetany)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hypocalcemia/figure_01_p3.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Chvostek's sign\n- **B.** Trousseau's sign\n- **C.** Kernig's sign\n- **D.** Brudzinski's sign\n- **E.** Tinel's sign\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nChvostek's sign is facial muscle spasm elicited by tapping lightly over the facial nerve trunk anterior to the external auditory meatus / tragus. Trousseau's sign is carpopedal spasm (flexion of wrist and metacarpophalangeal joints, extension of interphalangeal joints, and adduction of thumb - 'main d'accoucheur') elicited by inflating a sphygmomanometer cuff above systolic BP for 3 minutes. Both indicate neuromuscular irritability secondary to acute hypocalcaemia.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhy must Total Serum Calcium always be adjusted (corrected) for serum albumin concentration, and what is the standard correction formula?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hypocalcaemia & Tetany)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hypocalcemia/figure_02_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Approximately 40-45% of circulating calcium is bound to albumin; Adjusted Calcium = Measured Calcium + [0.02 x (40 - Albumin in g/L)]\n- **B.** Albumin and calcium are always equimolar\n- **C.** Free ionized calcium is completely unaffected by albumin\n- **D.** Adjusted Calcium = Measured Calcium minus Albumin\n- **E.** Correction is only required in hyperalbuminaemia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nOnly free ionized calcium is biologically active. In hypoalbuminaemia, total measured calcium is artificially low because the bound fraction is reduced, while free ionized calcium may remain normal. The standard formula adjusts for albumin: Corrected Calcium (mmol/L) = Measured Total Calcium + [0.02 x (40 - Albumin g/L)]. If accurate assessment is required, a direct point-of-care blood gas ionized calcium (iCa) should be measured.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhat is the immediate intravenous treatment of choice for severe acute symptomatic hypocalcaemia presenting with tetany, laryngeal stridor, or prolonged QT interval on ECG?\n\n- **A.** Intravenous 10% Calcium Gluconate (10 to 20ml in 100ml 5% dextrose infused over 10-20 minutes with cardiac monitoring)\n- **B.** Oral calcium carbonate 500mg once daily\n- **C.** Intravenous furosemide 40mg bolus\n- **D.** Oral vitamin D3 400 IU\n- **E.** Intravenous sodium bicarbonate\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSevere symptomatic hypocalcaemia (tetany, carpopedal spasm, seizures, laryngospasm, or QTc prolongation) is a medical emergency treated with IV 10% Calcium Gluconate (10-20 ml in 100 ml 5% dextrose over 10-20 minutes under continuous ECG monitoring). Calcium gluconate is preferred over calcium chloride in peripheral veins because it is less irritant and does not cause tissue necrosis if extravasated.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the classic 12-lead ECG finding in acute severe Hypocalcaemia?\n\n- **A.** Prolongation of the QT interval (specifically prolongation of the ST segment with a normal T-wave duration)\n- **B.** Shortening of the QT interval\n- **C.** Delta waves\n- **D.** Prominent U waves with ST depression\n- **E.** Sine-wave pattern\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nHypocalcaemia prolongs phase 2 of the myocardial action potential (the plateau phase), which manifests on 12-lead ECG as a prolonged ST segment, resulting in QT-interval prolongation (prolonged QTc). Unlike other causes of prolonged QT, the T wave morphology and duration itself remain completely normal. It predisposes to Torsades de pointes and ventricular arrhythmias.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common cause of acute hypocalcaemia encountered in the emergency department within the first 24 to 48 hours following neck surgery?\n\n- **A.** Post-surgical Hypoparathyroidism (accidental devascularisation, manipulation, or removal of the parathyroid glands during total thyroidectomy or parathyroidectomy)\n- **B.** Acute respiratory failure\n- **C.** Excessive calcium consumption\n- **D.** Severe vitamin C deficiency\n- **E.** Primary aldosteronism\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nTransient (or permanent) hypoparathyroidism occurs in up to 10-30% of total thyroidectomy patients due to inadvertent intraoperative surgical trauma, thermal injury, accidental excision, or devascularisation of the delicate parathyroid glands. Serum calcium typically drops to a nadir at 24-48 hours post-operatively.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c2967b20-4891-4e75-a1a8-02d0d863c962	797f6930-be16-40f1-b028-303cf92c244a	summary-of-drugs-and-their-side-effects	Summary of Drugs and Their Side Effects	article	Summary Of Drugs And Their Side\nEffects\n\n![Summary of Drugs and Their Side Effects - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/emergency-toxicology-antidotes/summary-of-drugs-and-their-side-effects/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Summary of Drugs and Their Side Effects — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.529169+00	2026-10-18 00:34:58.992551+00	\N
4353a9fc-4a89-4bcb-8978-3b620e17ee58	8aa9bd02-d2ae-4b52-83da-d1ee8071e3f4	blood-groups	Blood Groups	article	Blood group\nThe ABO blood group phenotypes are O (genotype OO), A\n(genotype AA or AO), B (genotype BB or BO) and AB (genotype\n\n## Ab).\n\nBlood group O is the most common in the UK (46 %), followed\nby group A (42 %), group B (9 %) and finally group AB (3 %).\nBlood group O has no antigens, but both anti-A and anti-B\nantibodies and thus is the universal donor.\nBlood group AB has both A and B antigens but no antibodies\nand thus is the universal recipient.\nBlood group A has A antigens and anti-B antibodies, and\nblood group B has B antigens and anti-A antibodies.\n\n![Blood Groups - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/blood-groups/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Blood Groups — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*\n\n## Rh-D\n\nThe Rh D gene may be either absent or present, giving the RhD\nnegative or RhD positive phenotype respectively. About 85% of\nthe UK population is Rh D positive. Anti-D antibodies don't\noccur naturally and are therefore immune antibodies that\nresult from previous transfusions or pregnancy.\nRhesus Disease:  A condition that occurs during pregnancy if\na woman has Rh-ve blood and her baby has Rh+ve blood.\nMother's body create Rh antibodies against the baby's blood\nalso known as hemolytic disease of newborn\n.\nPathogenesis: Rh-ve mother carrying Rh+ve fetus⟶  Entry of\nfetus Rh+ve Rbc into maternal circulation⟶  Development of\nRh antibodies by the mother\nPrevention and Prophylaxis\nanti-D immunoglobulin to all Rh-negative women at 28 and 34\nweeks routinely\nOr give anti-D immunoglobulin if she has a:\nTherapeutic abortion.\nSpontaneous abortion/ectopic pregnancy.\nAmniocentesis.\nAny bleeding in pregnancy/threatened miscarriage.\n\n![Blood Groups - Clinical Figure 2](/lessons/images/general-pathology-inflammation-anemia/blood-groups/figure_02_p2.png)\n*Blood Groups — Clinical Figure 2 (Page 2 Reference)*\n\nAfter delivery at any gestation within 72Hours.	\N	[]	\N	f	2	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
e058b93b-e787-4ba6-b6cc-a6d254c973e9	e5b2638f-eb02-43b8-80ff-462dc7c99d2f	culture-medium-for-bacterial-growth	Culture Medium for Bacterial Growth	article	Culture Medium For Bacterial\nGrowth\n\n![Culture Medium for Bacterial Growth - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/clinical-microbiology-bacteria-antibiotics/culture-medium-for-bacterial-growth/figure_01_p1.png)\n*Culture Medium for Bacterial Growth — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 1 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-10-17 03:09:07.542104+00	2026-10-18 00:34:59.004021+00	\N
34f274c6-3107-48d4-90a6-06ec61a58b75	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-hypokalemia	Hypokalaemia Emergency Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Hypokalaemia Emergency Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 48-year-old woman with a history of bulimia and thiazide diuretic use presents with generalized muscle weakness and palpitations. Serum potassium is 2.1 mmol/L. What is the maximum safe peripheral intravenous infusion rate of Potassium Chloride (KCl) in an unmonitored setting to prevent severe local pain, thrombophlebitis, and lethal hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hypokalaemia Emergency Management)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hypokalemia/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** 10 to 20 mmol/hour (maximum peripheral concentration 40 mmol/L)\n- **B.** 40 mmol/hour via a small butterfly needle in the hand\n- **C.** 100 mmol/hour rapid bolus push\n- **D.** 200 mmol over 15 minutes\n- **E.** Potassium can never be administered intravenously\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSafe peripheral IV potassium guidelines: Standard infusion rate should NOT exceed 10 mmol/hour (maximum 20 mmol/hr in emergency monitored resus setting). Maximum concentration in peripheral veins is 40 mmol/L to avoid severe burning chemical pain and thrombophlebitis. Higher concentrations (>40 mmol/L, e.g. up to 100 mmol/L) or faster rates mandate a central venous line with continuous cardiac telemetry.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhich life-threatening neuromuscular complication can occur when severe hypokalaemia (K+ < 2.0-2.5 mmol/L) causes acute necrosis of skeletal muscle fibers and secondary myoglobinuria?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hypokalaemia Emergency Management)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hypokalemia/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Rhabdomyolysis and acute pigment-induced kidney injury\n- **B.** Myasthenia gravis\n- **C.** Guillain-Barré syndrome\n- **D.** Multiple sclerosis\n- **E.** Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPotassium plays an essential role in mediating physiological exercise-induced vasodilation in skeletal muscle microcirculation. Severe hypokalaemia (<2.0-2.5 mmol/L) impairs hyperaemic vasodilation, causing skeletal muscle ischaemia, membrane lysis, and Rhabdomyolysis (marked elevation of creatine kinase and myoglobinuria), which can precipitate acute tubular necrosis.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old Asian male wakes up at 4 AM with acute, flaccid symmetrical paralysis of both lower limbs following a heavy carbohydrate-rich meal (pizza) and alcohol the evening before. Bloods reveal: Potassium 2.0 mmol/L, Free T4 48 pmol/L (markedly elevated), TSH <0.01 mU/L. What is the diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hypokalaemia Emergency Management)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hypokalemia/figure_03_p6.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (TPP)\n- **B.** Guillain-Barré syndrome\n- **C.** Acute intermittent porphyria\n- **D.** Transverse myelitis\n- **E.** Botulism\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nThyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (TPP) is an endocrine emergency seen predominantly in young Asian and Hispanic males with hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease). High thyroid hormone levels upregulate Na+/K+-ATPase pumps. A high-carbohydrate meal stimulates insulin release, driving potassium massively into cells, producing profound hypokalaemia and acute flaccid motor paralysis. It resolves rapidly with low-dose potassium and non-selective beta-blockers (propranolol).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhy must intravenous potassium NEVER be administered as an undiluted IV push or bolus?\n\n- **A.** A rapid IV bolus of potassium causes immediate cardiac arrest in diastole due to instant inexcitability of the myocardium\n- **B.** It causes massive acute renal stones\n- **C.** It causes severe hypernatraemia\n- **D.** It causes instantaneous liver infarction\n- **E.** It precipitates severe hypothermia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPotassium chloride injected as an undiluted IV bolus is a lethal injection protocol. It instantly abolishes the transcellular resting membrane potential gradient across cardiac myocytes, producing immediate electrical silence and refractory asystolic cardiac arrest. IV potassium must ALWAYS be pre-mixed and delivered via an infusion pump.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhich of the following endocrine disorders is a classical cause of primary mineralocorticoid excess presenting with hypertension, hypokalaemia, and metabolic alkalosis?\n\n- **A.** Conn's Syndrome (Primary Hyperaldosteronism)\n- **B.** Addison's disease (Primary adrenal insufficiency)\n- **C.** Hashimoto's thyroiditis\n- **D.** Hypoparathyroidism\n- **E.** Type 1 diabetes mellitus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nConn's syndrome (aldosterone-producing adrenal adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia) causes autonomous hyperaldosteronism. Excessive aldosterone stimulates epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) in renal cortical collecting ducts, causing sodium retention (hypertension) and reciprocal potassium and hydrogen ion excretion, producing hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with suppressed plasma renin.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
243b7198-f1fa-4921-9c5e-f9350be3d1ab	2944df4d-b9d7-49ad-8d30-f011cdc4c745	sba-quiz-nephrology-hyponatremia	Hyponatraemia & Osmotic Demyelination — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Hyponatraemia & Osmotic Demyelination — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 70-year-old female presents with acute confusion and generalised tonic-clonic status epilepticus. Laboratory tests reveal severe hyponatraemia: Serum Sodium 112 mmol/L. According to European Clinical Practice guidelines, what is the immediate treatment of choice for severe hyponatraemia presenting with severe neurological symptoms?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hyponatraemia & Osmotic Demyelination)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyponatremia/figure_01_p1.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Intravenous 3% Hypertonic Saline (150ml bolus infused over 20 minutes, repeatable up to 3 times or until symptoms resolve and sodium rises by 5 mmol/L)\n- **B.** Oral fluid restriction of 500 ml/day alone\n- **C.** Rapid infusion of 2 litres of 5% Dextrose\n- **D.** Intravenous furosemide 80mg bolus\n- **E.** Oral sodium chloride tablets\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSevere acute symptomatic hyponatraemia presenting with seizures, coma, or respiratory arrest is an emergency caused by acute cerebral oedema. European and UK guidelines recommend an immediate IV bolus of 150 ml of 3% Hypertonic Saline over 20 minutes (repeatable twice if needed) to rapidly raise serum sodium by 4-6 mmol/L, which is sufficient to shrink brain oedema, stop seizures, and prevent fatal transtentorial herniation.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nWhat catastrophic, permanent neurological complication occurs if chronic hyponatraemia (>48 hours duration) is corrected too rapidly (>8 to 10 mmol/L in 24 hours)?\n\n![Figure 2: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hyponatraemia & Osmotic Demyelination)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyponatremia/figure_02_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS / Central Pontine Myelinolysis), presenting with dysarthria, dysphagia, spastic quadriparesis, and 'Locked-In' syndrome\n- **B.** Wernicke-Korsakoff encephalopathy\n- **C.** Acute viral encephalitis\n- **D.** Transient ischemic attack\n- **E.** Alzheimer's dementia\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn chronic hyponatraemia, brain cells adapt to hypotonicity by extruding organic osmoles. When sodium is raised too rapidly (>8-10 mmol/L in 24h), hypertonic stress shrinks astrocytes and oligodendrocytes, triggering massive demyelination of the central pons (Central Pontine Myelinolysis). Patients present 2-7 days later with dysarthria, dysphagia, spastic flaccid-to-spastic quadriparesis, and horizontal gaze paralysis ('locked-in' syndrome).\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhich laboratory profile confirms the diagnosis of Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH)?\n\n![Figure 3: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Hyponatraemia & Osmotic Demyelination)](/lessons/images/sba/nephrology/hyponatremia/figure_03_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Euvolaemic hyponatraemia with Serum Osmolality < 275 mOsm/kg, Inappropriately concentrated Urine Osmolality (>100 mOsm/kg), and Urine Sodium > 30 mmol/L, in the presence of normal thyroid and adrenal function\n- **B.** Hypovolaemic hyponatraemia with urine sodium < 10 mmol/L\n- **C.** Hypervolaemic hyponatraemia with urine osmolality < 100 mOsm/kg\n- **D.** High serum osmolality with normal sodium\n- **E.** SIADH cannot be diagnosed without an MRI brain\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nDiagnostic criteria for SIADH (Bartter-Schwartz): (1) True hypotonic hyponatraemia (serum osmolality <275 mOsm/kg); (2) Clinically EUVOLAEMIC (no edema, no dehydration); (3) Inappropriately concentrated urine (urine osmolality >100 mOsm/kg, typically >serum osmolality); (4) Elevated urine sodium (>30-40 mmol/L on normal salt intake); (5) Normal renal, adrenal, and thyroid function, and no recent diuretic use.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nA 55-year-old male with severe diabetic ketoacidosis has blood glucose of 44 mmol/L (792 mg/dL) and a measured serum sodium of 126 mmol/L. What is his calculated 'Corrected' serum sodium level, and what phenomenon does this represent?\n\n- **A.** Corrected Sodium = 136 mmol/L; Hyperglycaemic Pseudohyponatraemia (osmotic water shift from intracellular to extracellular space)\n- **B.** Corrected Sodium = 116 mmol/L\n- **C.** Corrected Sodium = 126 mmol/L (glucose has no effect on sodium)\n- **D.** Corrected Sodium = 155 mmol/L\n- **E.** True hypovolaemic hyponatraemia requiring hypertonic saline\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nKatz formula for hyperglycaemic pseudohyponatraemia: Corrected Na = Measured Na + [0.4 x (Glucose in mmol/L - 5.5)]. Here: 126 + [0.4 x (44 - 5.5)] = 126 + 15.4 = 141.4 mmol/L (~136-141 mmol/L). High intravascular glucose draws free water from intracellular space into the vascular compartment, diluting serum sodium. As insulin therapy normalizes glucose, water moves back into cells and measured sodium normalizes without saline.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat is the cornerstone first-line medical therapy for chronic, asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic euvolaemic hyponatraemia secondary to SIADH?\n\n- **A.** Fluid restriction (typically 500 to 1,000 ml/day)\n- **B.** 0.9% Normal Saline intravenous infusion at 150 ml/hr\n- **C.** 3% Hypertonic saline infusion at 200 ml/hr\n- **D.** High-dose hydrocortisone\n- **E.** Desmopressin administration\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFor euvolaemic hyponatraemia caused by SIADH, the cornerstone of first-line therapy is strict Fluid Restriction (500-1000 ml/day). Giving 0.9% Normal Saline is contraindicated in SIADH because the administered sodium is rapidly excreted in the concentrated urine while the free water is retained, which paradoxically worsens the hyponatraemia.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b7083c07-70c7-47a8-a118-36aaf9d6ed8e	1a55d389-4f82-4d5b-aef6-803c12e21e68	acute-pyelonephritis-management	Acute Pyelonephritis Management	article	# Acute Pyelonephritis Management\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nArrange collection of a mid-steam urine (MSU) or catheter specimen of urine\n- (CSU), to determine the infecting micro-organism.\n- Obtain a urine sample for culture before starting empirical drug treatment Dipstick testing of urine for nitrites and leukocytes is not necessary A definitive diagnosis of acute pyelonephritis is made in people with loin pain\n- and/or fever if a UTI is confirmed by culturing a urinary pathogen from the urine.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nReferral pathway Consider referring or seeking specialist advice for people with acute\n- pyelonephritis if they; Are pregnant\n- Are significantly dehydrated and unable take oral fluid or medicine\n- Have a higher risk of developing complications — people with underlying disease\n- (such as DM, immunocompromise or genitourinary tract abnormalities) Have recurrent episodes of UTI (for example, two or more episodes in a 6-month\n- period). For all children presenting with an unexplained fever of 380C or higher, or\n- loin pain/tenderness suggesting pyelonephritis — send a urine sample for microscopy and culture within 24 hours, and consider referral to a paediatric specialist. Refer urgently using a suspected cancer pathway referral for an appointment\n- within 2 weeks for people: Aged 45 years and over who have unexplained visible haematuria without urinary tract infection, or visible haematuria that persists or recurs after successful treatnnent of urinary tract infection.\n\n- Aged 60 years and over who have unexplained non-visible haematuria and either dysuria or a raised white cell count on a blood test\n- Consider non-urgent referral for bladder cancer in men aged 60 years and over with recurrent or persistent UTI.\n- Admission pathway\n- Admit people with acute pyelonephritis to hospital if it is severe or patient developed sign and symptoms of sepsis include: Significant tachycardia, hypotension, or breathlessness.\n- Marked signs of illness (such as impaired level of consciousness, perfuse sweating, rigors, pallor, significantly reduced mobility).\n- A temperature greater than 380C or less than 360C\n- severity of symptoms\n- Previous urine culture and susceptibility results\n- previous antibiotic use\n- First line for women who are not pregnant, men,and people with indwelling catheters\n- Cefalexin500mg twice or three times a day (up to 1— 1.5g three or four times a day for severe infections) for 7-10 days.\n- Co-amoxiclav(only if appropriate in line with culture and sensitivity results)  mg three times a day for 7-10 days.\n- Trimethoprim(only if appropriate in line with culture and sensitivity results) 200mg twice a day for 14 days.\n- Ciprofloxacin500 mg twice a day for 7 days\n- First line IV (intravenous)\n\n- Co-amoxiclav (only in combination or if culture results available and susceptible), Cefuroxime, Ceftriaxone, ciprofloxacin, gentamicin, amikacin.\n- Second line— consult with microbiologist\n- Eicst-/ine;-Choice-of-antibioticzpregnant-women-aged-12years-and-oyer;\n- Cefalexin 500 mg twice or three times a day (up to I to 1.5 g three or four times a day for severe infections) for 7 to IO days.\n- First choice 'V: Cefuroxime 750 mg to 1.5 g three or four times a day\n- Second line: consult with microbiologists.\n- Choice of antibiotic: children and young_people under 16 years\n- Refer children under 3 months to paediatric specialist and treat with intravenous antibiotics in line with the NICE guideline on fever in under 5s\n- Cefalexin — 3months to 4 years 12.5 mg/kg or 125 mg twice a day for 7 to IO days (25 mg/kg two to four times a day [maximum 1 g per dose four times a day] for severe infections)\n- Sto 11 years, 12.5 mg/kg twice a day or 250 mg three times a day for 7 to 10 days (25 mg/kg two to four times a day [maximum I g per dose four times a day] for severe infections)\n- 12 to 15 years, 500 mg twice or three times a day (up to I to 1.5 g three or four times a day for severe infections) for 7 to 10 days\n- Co-amoxiclav (only if culture results available and susceptible)\n- First choice IV antibiotics : Co-amoxiclav (only in combination or if culture results available and susceptible), Cefuroxime, Ceftriaxone, ciprofloxacin, gentamicin, amikacin.\n- Complications\n- sepsis.\n- Parenchyma renal scarring.\n\n- Recurrent urinary tract infections. Renal abscess formation. Preterm labour in pregnancy. Emphysematous pyelonephritis previOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Acute Pyelonephritis Management - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/urological-emergencies/acute-pyelonephritis\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\n/figure_01_p5.jpeg)\n*Acute Pyelonephritis Management — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 5 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	f	0	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
396e4687-7caf-4ee1-99fe-1ed83908c7c3	1a55d389-4f82-4d5b-aef6-803c12e21e68	sba-quiz-urology-acute-pyelonephritis	Acute Pyelonephritis Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Acute Pyelonephritis Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 28-year-old non-pregnant woman presents with high fevers (39.2C), rigors, nausea, vomiting, dysuria, and severe right loin pain with marked tenderness to gentle percussion over the right costovertebral angle (Murphy's kidney punch sign). What is the clinical diagnosis?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Acute Pyelonephritis Management)](/lessons/images/sba/urology/acute-pyelonephritis/figure_01_p5.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Pyelonephritis (upper urinary tract infection)\n- **B.** Uncomplicated lower cystitis\n- **C.** Acute appendicitis\n- **D.** Acute cholecystitis\n- **E.** Bacterial vaginosis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute pyelonephritis is acute infectious inflammation of the renal parenchyma and pelvicalyceal system, typically caused by ascending Gram-negative enteric bacilli (most commonly uropathogenic Escherichia coli, >75-80%). Hallmark features: systemic toxic features (high fever, rigors, vomiting) and flank/loin pain with exquisite costovertebral angle tenderness.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to NICE guideline NG111, what is the recommended empirical first-line oral antibiotic therapy for an adult woman with mild-to-moderate acute uncomplicated pyelonephritis who is suitable for outpatient management?\n\n- **A.** Oral Cefalexin (500mg TDS for 7-10 days) or Oral Co-amoxiclav (500/125mg TDS for 14 days), or Ciprofloxacin if resistance rates are low\n- **B.** Oral Nitrofurantoin 100mg modified-release for 3 days\n- **C.** Oral Fosfomycin single dose\n- **D.** Oral Trimethoprim 200mg single dose\n- **E.** Oral Metronidazole 400mg TDS\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG111 recommends oral Cefalexin, Co-amoxiclav, or Ciprofloxacin (where local resistance is low) for outpatient pyelonephritis. Crucially, Nitrofurantoin and Fosfomycin are strictly CONTRAINDICATED in pyelonephritis because they achieve therapeutic concentrations only within the lower bladder lumen and fail to achieve adequate tissue levels in the renal parenchyma.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 45-year-old diabetic female with acute pyelonephritis remains persistently febrile and toxic with worsening flank pain despite 72 hours of appropriate intravenous antibiotics. What diagnostic investigation is urgently indicated?\n\n- **A.** Contrast-Enhanced CT of the Abdomen and Pelvis to evaluate for Renal Abscess, Perinephric Collection, or Emphysematous Pyelonephritis\n- **B.** Plain abdominal X-ray alone\n- **C.** Repeat midstream urine culture and wait another 5 days\n- **D.** Barium enema\n- **E.** Renal biopsy\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nFailure of acute pyelonephritis to improve after 48-72 hours of targeted IV antibiotic therapy mandates urgent contrast-enhanced CT (or renal ultrasound) to exclude suppurative complications: intra-renal or perinephric abscess, ureteric obstruction (infected hydronephrosis), renal papillary necrosis, or emphysematous pyelonephritis (gas formation by E. coli/Klebsiella in diabetics).\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat is the single most common bacterial microorganism responsible for more than 75 to 80% of all community-acquired cases of acute pyelonephritis and urinary tract infections?\n\n- **A.** Uropathogenic Escherichia coli (UPEC)\n- **B.** Staphylococcus saprophyticus\n- **C.** Proteus mirabilis\n- **D.** Enterococcus faecalis\n- **E.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nUropathogenic Escherichia coli (UPEC) accounts for 75-85% of uncomplicated pyelonephritis and cystitis. UPEC possesses specialized virulence factors: P fimbriae (pyelonephritis-associated pili) that adhere specifically to digalactoside receptors on uroepithelial cells, resisting urine flow and facilitating ascent into the kidney.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 60-year-old male with acute pyelonephritis and an obstructing proximal ureteric calculus (stone) presents in severe septic shock (BP 78/44 mmHg, lactate 4.8 mmol/L). What is the mandatory, emergency life-saving intervention?\n\n- **A.** Emergency Biliary / Renal tract Decompression via Percutaneous Nephrostomy (PCN) or Retrograde Ureteral Stenting (JJ stent) combined with IV antibiotics and resuscitation\n- **B.** Extracorporeal shock-wave lithotripsy (ESWL) immediately\n- **C.** Oral tamsulosin and observation\n- **D.** Immediate open nephrectomy\n- **E.** High-volume IV fluid diuresis alone\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nInfected hydronephrosis / pyonephrosis (obstructing stone with upper UTI / sepsis) is an absolute urological emergency. Antibiotics cannot penetrate an obstructed, high-pressure, infected collecting system. Immediate decompression—either by percutaneous nephrostomy (PCN) placed by interventional radiology or retrograde double-J ureteral stenting by urology—is life-saving.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	1	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d6afa584-93c0-4a5b-b8bb-ac46d90c2314	1a55d389-4f82-4d5b-aef6-803c12e21e68	dysuria-lower-urinary-tract-infections	Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections	article	# Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **MRCEM SBA Curriculum Notes:** Clinical review aligned with UK RCEM, NICE, and Resuscitation Council guidelines.\n\nurgent microscopy and culture.\n- If UTI is suspected in children aged 3 months or over — perform dipstick analysis:\n- If-both-leukocyte-esterase-gnd-nitcitegce-positiue then treat as UTI Start antibiotics\n- Send a urine sample for culture if the child is aged under 3 years, has a high or\n- intermediate risk of serious illness, (high or intermediate risk refer to sepsis risk stratification/traffic light under 5 years age Nice guideline). If leukocyte esterase is positive and nitrite is negative\n- send a urine sample for microscopy and culture.\n- For children aged under 3 years, start antibiotic treatrnent and reassess once\n- culture results are know For children aged 3 year or over, only start antibiotic treatrnent if there is good\n- clinical evidence of UTI (leucocytes may indicate infection outside the urinary tract) Start antibiotic treatment\n- Send urine for culture to confirm diagnosis and reassess once results are known.\n-\n- (f both leukocyte esterase and nitrite are negative, UTI is unlikely Do not start antibiotic treatrnent. Consider differential diagnoses\n- Antibiotic choice\n- First line\n-\n- Trimethoprim — 3 months to 5 years, 4 mg/kg (maximum 200 mg per dose) or 25-50\n- mg twice a day for 3 days 6 to II years, 4 mg/kg (maximum 200 mg per dose) or 100 mg twice a day for 3 days\n-\n- 12 to 15 years, 200 mg twice a day for 3 days\n\nNitrofurantoin: if estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) 245 ml/minute\n- 3 months to II years, 750 micrograms/kg four times a day for 3 days\n-\n- 12 to 15 years, 50 mg four times a day or 100 mg modified-release twice a day for 3 days Second line\n-\n- Nitrofurantoin: if eGFR 245 ml/minute6 and not first choice (dose same as above) Amoxicillin : (only if culture results available and susceptible)\n- 1 to 11 months, 125 mg three times a day for 3 days\n- I to 4 years, 250 mg three times a day for 3 days\n- 5 to 15 years, 500 mg three times a day for 3 days\n- Cefalexin: 3months to 4 years 12.5 mg/kg or 125 mg twice a day for 7 to IO days (25\n- mg/kg two to four times a day [maximum 1 g per dose four times a day] for severe infections)\n- 5 to 11 years, 12.5 mg/kg twice a day or 250 mg three times a day for 7 to 10 days (25 mg/kg two to four times a day [maximum I g per dose four times a day] for severe infections)\n- 12 to 15 years, 500 mg twice or three times a day (up to I to 1.5 g three or four times a day for severe infections) for 7 to IO days\n- Complication Renal scarring/damage\n-\n- Hypertension Renal insufficiency and failure\n- lower urinary tract infection (UT') in women\n- It is an infection of the bladder (also known as cystitis) usually caused by bacteria from the gastrointestinal tract.\n\n- Uncomplicated UTI — UTI caused by typical pathogens in people with a normal urinary tract and kidney function, and no predisposing co-morbidities.\n- Complicated UTI — UTI with an increased likelihood of complications such as persistent infection, treatment failure and recurrent infection\n- Causes\n- Most common E-COLI\n- Less-common:-staphy/ococcus saprophyticus, Klebsiella species, Streptococcus, and Proteus mirabilis (more common in males, associated with renal tract abnormalities.\n- Entry route\n- Retrograde bacteria ascend through the urethra into the bladder.\n- Via the blood strearn — more likely in people who are immunosuppressed.\n- Direct — for example with insertion of a catheter into the bladder, instrumentation, or surgery\n\n## Key Clinical Features\nDysuria— discomfort, pain, burning, tingling or stinging associated with urination.\n- Frequency —passing urine more often than usual.\n- Urgency —a strong desire to empty the bladder, which may lead to urinary incontinence.\n- Nocturia— passing urine more often than usual at night.\n- Suprapubic discomfort/tenderness\n- Urine may appear cloudy to the naked eye, or change colour or odour.\n- Haematuria may present as red/brown discolouration of urine or as frank blood\n- Typical features may be absent, in particular in elderly women with underlying cognitive impairment (Alzheimer's disease, early onset dementia) — consider UTI if the woman presents with:\n\n- Generalized non-specific clinical features such as delirium, lethargy, reduced ability to carry out activities of daily living and anorexia.\n- Alternative sources of infection and causes of delirium other than UTI must be excluded before a working diagnosis of UTI is made\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nPain relief: paracetamol or ibuprofen\n- Antibiotic-therapy:non-pregnant women aged 76 years and over, treat according to sensitivities from recent urine culture (if available), otherwise treat empirically taking account of local antimicrobial resistance patterns\n- First choice — Nitrofurantoin 100mg modified-release twice a day for 3 days (if eGFR 245ml/minute)\n- OR-Trimethoprim 200mg twice a day for 3 days (if Iow risk of resistance)\n- Second choice —Nitrofurantoin 100mg modified-release twice a day for 3 days (if eGFR 245mI/minute and not used as first-choice) or\n- Pivmecillinam (a penicillin) 400mg initial dose, then 200mg three times a day for a total of 3 days or\n- Fosfomycin 3g single dose sachet\n- Antibiotic-therapy-pregnant women aged 72 years and over\n- First choice: Nitrofurantoin (avoid at term): if estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) 245 mi/minute IOOmg BD 7 days\n- Second choice: Amoxicillin (only if culture results available and susceptible) or Cefalexin 500 mg twice a day for 7 days\n- Advise\n- Encourage intake of enough fluids to avoid dehydration.\n- Do not recommend cranberry products or urine alkalinising agents\n\n- Complications Pyelonephritis\n- renal and peri-renal abscess\n- impaired renal function\n- renal failure\n- Urosepsis\n- Lower UTI in men Causes & definition same as UTI in women.\n-\n- Risk factors Age over 50 years.\n- Benign prostatic hypertrophy (BPH) and other causes of urine outflow obstruction\n- (for example, urinary tract stones, urethral stricture). Catheterisation — UTI is the rnost common hospital acquired infection, and the\n- majority of cases result from indwelling catheters. Previous urinary tract instrumentation or surgery.\n- Previous UTI\n-\n\n## Key Clinical Features\n(same as in UTI in women)\n- Other-specific features In men who are frail and elderly, and/or catheterized, and/or in institutional care, do\n- not rely on classical symptoms or signs to predict the likelihood of UTI — in these men, UTI may present with atypical symptoms. In men aged 65 years or over, UTI is likely if dysuria alone is present, or two or more\n- of the following: Temperature 1.50C above normal twice in 12 hours.\n- New frequency or urgency.\n-\n\n- New incontinence.\n- New or worsening delirium/debility.\n- New suprapubic pain.\n- Visible haematuria.\n\n## Investigations & Diagnostic Workup\nIn men with symptoms suggestive of a UT', confirm the diagnosis by urine culture and sensitivity, by arranging collection of a mid-steam urine (MSU) or catheter specimen of urine (CSU).\n- Obtain a urine sample for culture before starting empirical drug treatment.\n- Do not use urine dipstick tests or microscopy to diagnose UTI in men:\n- who are not catheterized\n- If the dipstick test is negative for nitrites and leukocyte esterase, UTI is less likely, although positive nitrite makes UTI more likely (positive predictive value 96%).\n- Patient With an indwelling catheter — make a working diagnosis based on clinical judgement.\n- Aged over 65 years — dipsticks become more unreliable with increasing age over 65 years.\n\n## Emergency Management Protocol\nRefer-to-urology-men-who;\n- Have ongoing symptoms despite appropriate antibiotic treatment. May have an underlying cause or risk factor for the UTI (such as suspected bladder outlet obstruction, or have a history of pyelonephritis, urinary calculi, or previous genitourinary tract surgery).\n- Have recurrent episodes of IJTI (for example, two or more episodes in a 6-month period).\n\n- Arrange emergency admission to hospital if\n- Patient is severely unwell with symptoms or signs suggestive of urosepsis (for example, nausea and vomiting, confusion, tachypnoea, tachycardia, or hypotension).\n- Other-a/Lpatient-offer-antj_iotics;\n- Must-ta—e-ACCQ.un.t-Previous urine culture and susceptibility results and previous antibiotic use, which may have led to resistant bacteria.\n- First choice\n- Trimethoprim 200 mg twice daily for 7 days.\n- Nitrofurantoin 100 mg modified-release twice daily (or if unavailable 50 mg four times daily) for 7 days\n- Note: Nitrofurantoin is not recommended for men with prostate involvement as it is unlikely to reach therapeutic levels in the prostate. If acute prostatitis is suspected quinolones are the first-choice antibiotic.\n- Second choice —Nitrofurantoin 100mg modified-release twice a day for 3 days (if eGFR 245ml/minute and not used as first-choice) or\n- Pivmecillinam (a penicillin) 400mg initial dose, then 200mg three times a day for a total of 3 days or\n- Fosfomycin 3g single dose sachet\n- Arrange follow up, for example after 48 hours, depending on clinical judgement, to check the man's response to treatment and the urine culture results. previOUS\n\n### Clinical Figures & Diagnostic Algorithms\n\n![Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections - Clinical Figure 1](/lessons/images/sba/urological-emergencies/dysuria-lower-urinary-tract-infections/figure_01_p8.jpeg)\n*Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections — Clinical Figure 1 (Page 8 Reference)*	\N	[]	\N	t	2	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9236920f-4adb-48c4-ab2d-807daa533eb1	1a55d389-4f82-4d5b-aef6-803c12e21e68	sba-quiz-urology-dysuria	Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 24-year-old sexually active woman presents with a 2-day history of acute painful micturition (dysuria), urinary frequency, and suprapubic discomfort. She has no fever, flank pain, or vaginal discharge. According to NICE guideline NG109, what is the recommended empirical first-line oral antibiotic for acute uncomplicated cystitis in non-pregnant adult women?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Dysuria & Lower Urinary Tract Infections)](/lessons/images/sba/urology/dysuria/figure_01_p8.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Oral Nitrofurantoin (50mg QDS or 100mg modified-release BD for 3 days) or Trimethoprim (200mg BD for 3 days)\n- **B.** Oral Ciprofloxacin 500mg BD for 14 days\n- **C.** Intravenous Ceftriaxone 1g daily for 7 days\n- **D.** Oral Co-amoxiclav for 21 days\n- **E.** Oral Fluconazole single dose\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nNICE NG109 recommends a short 3-day course of Nitrofurantoin (100mg MR BD) or Trimethoprim (200mg BD) as first-line empirical therapy for acute uncomplicated lower UTI in non-pregnant women. A 3-day course is as effective as a 7-day course, with fewer side effects and lower resistance selection. (Note: Nitrofurantoin requires eGFR >=45 ml/min to achieve adequate urinary concentration).\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nA 22-year-old male presents with severe burning dysuria and a copious, thick, purulent yellow-white urethral discharge for 3 days. What is the most likely pathogen and recommended empirical therapy according to BASHH (British Association for Sexual Health and HIV) guidelines?\n\n- **A.** Neisseria gonorrhoeae; Ceftriaxone 1g intramuscular (IM) single dose\n- **B.** Escherichia coli; Trimethoprim 200mg BD for 3 days\n- **C.** Candida albicans; Clotrimazole cream\n- **D.** Treponema pallidum; Oral penicillin V\n- **E.** Herpes simplex virus; Oral aciclovir 200mg\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nAcute urethritis in young males presenting with profuse, purulent urethral discharge is characteristically caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Gram-negative intracellular diplococcus). BASHH guidelines recommend high-dose Ceftriaxone 1g IM single dose (with oral Doxycycline 100mg BD for 7 days if Chlamydia trachomatis has not been excluded), alongside partner notification and NAAT testing.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nWhy is a urinary tract infection (UTI) in an adult male ALWAYS considered a 'Complicated UTI' requiring further clinical evaluation?\n\n- **A.** The long male urethra and antibacterial prostatic secretions normally protect against ascending infection; a UTI in a male implies an anatomical, functional, or structural abnormality (e.g. BPH, urethral stricture, prostatitis)\n- **B.** Males cannot excrete antibiotics in urine\n- **C.** UTI is genetic in males\n- **D.** All male UTIs are caused by tuberculosis\n- **E.** Men do not have urinary bladders\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn adult males, the long urethra, dry periurethral environment, and zinc-rich antibacterial prostatic secretions make ascending bacterial cystitis very rare. A male UTI is by definition complicated, warranting a minimum 7-day antibiotic course, midstream urine culture, examination for acute prostatitis, and urological investigation for bladder outlet obstruction or urethral stricture.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nOn urine dipstick testing of a patient with acute dysuria, what do a positive 'Leukocyte Esterase' and a positive 'Nitrite' test specifically indicate?\n\n- **A.** Leukocyte esterase indicates pyuria (presence of white blood cells / inflammation); Nitrite indicates the presence of bacteria possessing nitrate reductase enzymes (Gram-negative enterobacteria like E. coli)\n- **B.** Nitrite indicates viral infection\n- **C.** Leukocyte esterase indicates renal glycosuria\n- **D.** Both tests indicate acute renal failure\n- **E.** Nitrite indicates calcium oxalate crystals\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nLeukocyte esterase detects an enzyme produced by neutrophils, confirming pyuria (white cells in urine). Nitrite tests detect the bacterial reduction of dietary dietary nitrate to nitrite by Gram-negative uropathogens (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus). Gram-positive organisms (Enterococcus, Staph saprophyticus) do not reduce nitrate, giving a negative nitrite test despite active infection.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nA 30-year-old pregnant woman at 14 weeks gestation is found to have Asymptomatic Bacteriuria (>10^5 CFU/ml of E. coli on routine booking antenatal culture). Why MUST asymptomatic bacteriuria be treated with antibiotics in pregnancy?\n\n- **A.** Untreated asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy carries a 20-40% risk of progressing to acute severe pyelonephritis, pre-term labour, low birth weight, and perinatal mortality\n- **B.** It causes immediate fetal cardiac arrest\n- **C.** It causes gestational diabetes\n- **D.** It causes amniotic fluid embolism\n- **E.** It causes maternal hyperemesis\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nPregnancy causes physiological hydroureter and urinary stasis (progesterone relaxes ureteric smooth muscle, and the gravid uterus compresses the ureters). Untreated asymptomatic bacteriuria progresses to acute maternal pyelonephritis in 20-40% of pregnancies, significantly increasing the risk of pre-term labour and low birth weight. Treatment with a 7-day course of pregnancy-safe antibiotics is mandatory.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	3	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
30ab7c15-ee25-4d55-9646-03bdc336fe38	1a55d389-4f82-4d5b-aef6-803c12e21e68	sba-quiz-urology-epididymo-orchitis	Epididymo-Orchitis Management — SBA Interactive Quiz	quiz	# Epididymo-Orchitis Management — SBA Interactive Quiz\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Curriculum Examination:** Single Best Answer (SBA) scenario. Read the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings carefully, then select the optimal management step. Full evidence-based rationales follow each question.\n\n## Question 1 of 5\n\nA 23-year-old sexually active male presents with gradual onset of severe right hemiscrotal pain, swelling, and fever developing over 3 days. Examination shows an enlarged, exquisitely tender right epididymis and testis with scrotal erythema. Elevation of the right hemiscrotum relieves the pain (positive Prehn's sign). What is the most likely diagnosis and predominant microbiological aetiology in this age group?\n\n![Figure 1: Clinical Findings & Diagnostic Reference (Epididymo-Orchitis Management)](/lessons/images/sba/urology/epididymo-orchitis/figure_01_p4.jpeg)\n\n\n- **A.** Acute Epididymo-Orchitis; Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- **B.** Testicular torsion; vascular occlusion\n- **C.** Incarcerated inguinal hernia; E. coli\n- **D.** Varicocele; venous incompetence\n- **E.** Testicular teratoma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn sexually active males aged <35 years, acute epididymitis is predominantly caused by sexually transmitted pathogens: Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae ascending via the vas deferens. Prehn's sign (pain relief on physical scrotal elevation) is classically positive in epididymitis (though unreliable to distinguish from torsion). In men >35 years, enteric coliforms (E. coli) predominate.\n\n## Question 2 of 5\n\nAccording to UK BASHH and NICE guidelines, what is the recommended empirical antibiotic regimen for suspected sexually transmitted acute Epididymo-Orchitis in a 24-year-old male?\n\n- **A.** Intramuscular Ceftriaxone (500mg or 1g IM single dose) PLUS Oral Doxycycline (100mg twice daily for 10-14 days)\n- **B.** Oral Ciprofloxacin 500mg BD for 3 days\n- **C.** Oral Nitrofurantoin 100mg BD for 7 days\n- **D.** Oral Amoxicillin 500mg TDS for 5 days\n- **E.** Oral Metronidazole 400mg single dose\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nBASHH guidelines recommend dual antimicrobial therapy for sexually transmitted epididymo-orchitis: Ceftriaxone (500mg-1g IM single dose to cover Neisseria gonorrhoeae) plus Oral Doxycycline (100mg BD for 10-14 days to cover Chlamydia trachomatis). Bed rest, scrotal support, and analgesia are essential adjuncts.\n\n## Question 3 of 5\n\nA 68-year-old male with benign prostatic hyperplasia and chronic urinary retention presents with acute left epididymo-orchitis. What is the most likely causative pathogen, and what oral antibiotic is recommended by NICE?\n\n- **A.** Escherichia coli (or other enteric Gram-negative coliforms); Oral Ofloxacin 200mg BD (or Ciprofloxacin 500mg BD / Co-amoxiclav) for 14 days\n- **B.** Chlamydia trachomatis; Doxycycline\n- **C.** Treponema pallidum; Benzathine penicillin\n- **D.** Ureaplasma urealyticum; Erythromycin\n- **E.** Herpes simplex virus; Aciclovir\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nIn men aged >35 years, acute epididymo-orchitis is almost always non-sexually transmitted, arising from reflux of infected urine secondary to bladder outlet obstruction or urinary catheterisation. Enteric coliforms (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, Pseudomonas) are the causative agents. Quinolones (Ofloxacin or Ciprofloxacin) or Co-amoxiclav for 14 days are first-line due to excellent tissue penetration into the prostate and epididymis.\n\n## Question 4 of 5\n\nWhat acute viral systemic infection classically causes secondary orchitis (typically unilateral in 70-80% and bilateral in 15-30%) in post-pubertal adolescent males, occurring 4-8 days after parotid gland swelling?\n\n- **A.** Mumps Virus (Paramyxovirus)\n- **B.** Epstein-Barr Virus\n- **C.** Coxsackie B virus\n- **D.** Cytomegalovirus\n- **E.** Varicella-Zoster virus\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nMumps orchitis occurs in up to 20-30% of post-pubertal males infected with the mumps virus (Paramyxovirus). It typically develops 4-8 days following acute parotitis, presenting with abrupt high fever, severe testicular pain, and marked testicular swelling. Although it can cause testicular atrophy in 30-50%, permanent infertility is uncommon unless bilateral.\n\n## Question 5 of 5\n\nWhat serious suppurative complication of untreated or severe bacterial epididymo-orchitis is visualised on scrotal ultrasound as a complex, thick-walled, cystic intra-testicular fluid collection with internal debris?\n\n- **A.** Testicular Abscess (requiring surgical drainage or orchidectomy)\n- **B.** Spermatocele\n- **C.** Varicocele\n- **D.** Hydrocele\n- **E.** Seminoma\n\n**Correct Answer:** A\n**Explanation:**\nSuppurative bacterial epididymo-orchitis can lead to a Testicular Abscess or Fournier's gangrene if swelling compromises testicular venous outflow, causing secondary testicular infarction. Diagnostic ultrasound reveals a complex, hypoechoic, thick-walled fluid collection with internal septations and avascular center. Management requires surgical drainage or orchidectomy.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-11-02 05:50:29.531607+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
1604fe0e-0505-48ec-a6db-8cb381131dc2	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-46	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 46	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 46\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	46	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
f3babcc8-4534-46db-9af6-0d21a48a7968	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-54	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 54	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 54\n\n- **C)** No need for confirmation\n- **D)** 10ml heparinized saline flush\n- **E)** Back flow Of blood Explanation\n- Confirrnation of the catheter tip position with chest radiography is required prior to use when internal jugular and subclavian access is obtained. Key_points\n- A chest X-ray should be performed immediately for the internal jugular and subclavian lines to ensure proper placement and the absence Of an iatrogenic pneumothorax.\n- Be sure you are withdrawing venous blood before dilation and cannulation of the vessel.\n- If the internal jugular CVL attempt is unsuccessful, move to the ipsilateral subclavian vein. Never attempt the opposite side without a chest X-ray or ultrasound first to avoid bilateral pneumothoraces.\n- Never force the guidewire on insertion because it may cause damage to the vessel or surrounding structures. Forcing the wire could also cause it to kink, making removal difficult and causing damage to the vessel wall. It may also lead to an inaccurate position of the catheter.\n- Always place your finger over the open hub of the needle to prevent an air embolism.\n- Always confirm placement with ultrasound, looking for reverberation artifact of the needle and/or tenting of the vessel wall. Needles cannot be visualized on ultrasound. Wires can be visualized so the operator can also confirm at that step.\n\n- A venous blood gas can be aspirated off a femoral line to ensure it is not arterial.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 4-year-old child brought to ED by mother. Her mother reports that his child became suddenly turn blue and breath rapidly after playing football. Cardiac examination demonstrates right ventricular impulse, and harsh systolic murmur. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** VSD\n- **C)** Tetralogy of fallot\n- **D)** ASD\n- **E)** Mitral Stenosis Explanation\n- This patient has tetralogy of Fallot (TOF), the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease. The right ventricular outflow obstruction creates a right to left shunt causing venous blood to be shunted to the overriding aorta and into the systemic circulation manifesting as cyanosis.\n- CXR - shows boot shaped heart.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nYou are teaching group of medical students in the ED regarding Modified HEART Score. Which of the following is not included in this Score?\n- **A)** Hypercholesterolemia\n- **B)** Age>4S\n- **C)** Stroke\n- **D)** Diabetes mellitus\n- **E)** History Of coronary artery disease Explanation\n- Stroke is not included in modified heart score\n- Suspected ACS patients are evaluated with a standard ED focused history and evaluation. Based upon five different variables, a score is summed for the patient under evaluation, including history (H), 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG; E), age (A), risk factors (R), and troponin (T).\n\nHEART score for chest pain patients History (Anamnesis) Bisk factors Iroponin Highly suspicious Moderately suspicious Slightly suspicious Significant ST-deviation Non-specific repolarisation disturbance / LBBB / PM Normal 2 65 years 45 — 65 years s 45 years 2 3 risk factors or history of atherosclerotic disease 1 or 2 risk factors No risk factors known 2 3x normal limit I-3x normal limit s normal limit 2 2 2 1 2 1 2 1 Total\n- Risk factors for atherosclerotic disease\n- Hypertension\n- Diabetic mellitus\n- Hypercholesterolemia Cigarette smoking\n- Positive family history\n- Obesity (BMI>30)\n\nstrategy.\n- 0.4; IOW risk, potential candidate for early discharge.\n- moderate risk, potential candidate for observation & further evaluation.\n- ENO: high risk, candidate for urgent or emergent intervention.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 25-year-old homeless patient is brought to the ED by paramedics. She is unconscious GCS 6 and having repeated attacks of seizures ongoing for more than 30 minutes. What is the initial step of management?\n- **A)** Anticonvulsant drugs\n- **B)** Hypertonic saline bolus\n- **C)** Intubation\n- **D)** Baseline investigation\n- **E)** Withhold treatment Explanation\n- Seizure ongoing more than 30 minutes and GCS 6, such patients need to be intubated under general anesthesia.\n\n- Convulsive/status epilepticus management 0-5 minutes: Immediate management 5-10 minutes: Early SE 20-40 minutes: established SE 1 40-60 minutes: Refractory SE the patient. Do not Consider airway adjunct.\n- Administer oxygen.\n- Place patient in a semi-prone position, head dowm Gain IV access\n- Obtain BM - If hypoglycaemic; Give 150-200mI glucose IV Stat\n- If suspicion of alcohol excess or malnutrition commence 1 pair PabrinexlV BEFORE glucose replacement.\n- no IV access or (pre-hospital. paramedics hospital transfer)\n- Buccal midazolam 10mg or Rectal diazepam 10—20 mg if buccal midazolam is not available.\n- Lorazepam (intravenous) 0.1 mg/kg (usually a 4 mg bolus, repeated once after 10-20 minutes; rate not critical).\n- if seizures continue, treat as below\n- Alert the anesthetic and ICU\n- Give 2nd\n- IV Levetiracetam 60mg/kg, maximum (in Itnrnl sodium chloride 0.9% over IO minutes) Phenytoin infusion at a dose of 15—18 mg/kg at a rate of 50 mg/minute or fosphenytoin infusion at a dose Of IS-20 mg phenytoin equivalents (PEVkg at a rate Of SO-100 mg PE/minute\n- Phenobarbital Of IO—IS mg/kg at a rate Of 100 m g/minute (If any patient already on phenytoin, consider phenobarbital in such patients)\n- propofol (1—2 mg/kg bolus, then 2—10 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect\n- Midazolam (0.1—0.2 mg/kg bolus, then 0.05—0.5 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect.\n- Thiopental sodium mg/kg bolus, then mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect; after 2—3 days infusion rate needs reduction as fat stores are saturated. Anaesthetic continued for 12—24 hours after the last clinical or electrographic seizure, then dose tapered.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nWhat is antidote of dabigatran?\n- **A)** Protamine Sulfate B' Pralidoxime\n- **C)** Phytomenadione\n\nD\n- Idarucizumab\n- **E)** Andexanet Alfa Explanation\n- Idarucizumab (Praxbind ) is a specific reversal agent indicated in adults treated with dabigatran when rapid reversal of its anticoagulant effects is required. Dabigatran\n- Agtiqn_2is a direct thrombin (Factor-Ila) inhibitor with a rapid onset of action\n- Dose — recommended dose is 150 mg twice a day. However, A reduced dose of 110—150 mg twice daily is recommended in some patients.\n- Indications\n- Treatment of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE) and the prevention of recurrent DVT and PE\n- For the prophylaxis of stroke and systemic embolism in adults with non-valvular atrial fibrillation (NVAF).\n- For the prevention of venous thromboembolism (VTE) in people who have undergone knee/Hip replacement surgery\n- Contraindications . People with:\n- Severe renal impairment (creatine clearance [CrCl] less than 30 mL/minute).\n\n- Active bleeding.\n- A prosthetic heart valve — increased risk of thromboembolic and bleeding events.\n- Antiphospholipid syndrome — increased risk of recurrent thrombotic events.\n- Severe hepatic impairment or liver disease expected to have any impact on life expectancy.\n- Elevated liver enzymes (if they are more than twice the upper limit of normal).\n- People with significant risk of major bleeding, such as:\n- Current or recent gastrointestinal ulcer.\n- Oesophageal varices.\n- Recent brain or spinal injury.\n- Recent brain, spine, or ophthalmic surgery.\n- Recent intracranial haemorrhage.\n- Malignant neoplasm.\n- Vascular aneurysm.\n- Adverse effects\n- Anaemia\n- Diarrhoea\n- Gastrointestinal discomfort\n- Haemorrhage\n- Abnormal hepatic function\n- Nausea\n- Antidote\n- Idarucizumab (Praxbind ) is a specific reversal agent indicated in adults treated with dabigatran when rapid reversal of its anticoagulant effects is required.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 12-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complaint of non- blanching leg rash. His mother reports that he has sore throat two-week ago. What investigation should be done to support your diagnosis?\n- **A)** Urinalysis\n- **B)** ASOT\n- **C)** FBC\n- **D)** Blood Culture Explanation usually the only investigation needed in a classic presentation Of HSP.\n- HenochSchönleimpurpurm(HSP)\n- HSP is the most common vasculitis of childhood. It most commonly affects children 2-8 years of age\n- Recent upper respiratory tract infection is present in 50% of HSP cases, commonly viral or Croup A streptococcus infections\n- Clinical features\n- The features include rash, and one or more Of:\n\n- Arthritis/arthralgia (50-75%)\n- Abdominal pain (50%)\n- Nephritis (25-50%)\n- Investigations\n- Urinalysis -is usually the only investigation needed in a classic presentation of HSP\n- If urinalysis is abnormal i-e If there is hypertension, macroscopic haematuria or significant proteinuria then perform urinary protein- creatinine ratio (UPCR),and albumin. Other investigations — FBC, blood or urine culture, to rule out differentials if the diagnosis is unclear (eg ITP, leukaemia, meningococcal infection).\n- Management\n- Subcutaneous oedema is managed with bed rest and elevation of the affected area\n- Regular paracetamol and a short course of NSAIDs such as ibuprofen (10 mg/kg TDS) for pain relief.\n- Consider the use of steroids (glucocorticoids) in children with moderate to severe abdominal and joint pain (may reduce duration of symptoms).\n- Arrange regular follow-up to identify subsequent renal involvement which rarely requires a renal biopsy +/- immunosuppression.\n- Key_pgints\n- Urinalysis and blood pressure measurement must be done when Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) is suspected\n\n- Most cases are self-limiting and only require symptomatic management\n- Close follow-up is critical to identify significant renal involvement requiring intervention. Renal involvement is usually asymptomatic.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nWhich of the following disease is included in Glasgow Blatchford Score?\n- **A)** Myasthenia Gravis B\n- Liver disease\n- **C)** Kidney failure\n- **D)** Pericarditis\n- **E)** Stroke\n- Glasgow-Blatchford Score\n- The Glasgow-Blatchford score is the risk assessment tool of choice in the emergency department.\n- It is calculated using FOUR component:\n- Medical history (hepatic disease, heart failure),\n- Symptoms (melaena, syncope),\n- Signs (tachycardia, blood pressure),\n- Blood tests (haemoglobin, urea). The maximum score is 29.\n\n- NICE recommends that patients with a Blatchford score of O can be considered for early discharge with outpatient endoscopy follow up. Risk marker Urea Haemoglobin (g/dL) for men Haemoglobin (g/dL ) for women Systolic blood pressure (mmHg) Other markers Range 6.5-7.5 8.0-9.9 10.0 - 24.9 >25.0 12 - 12.9 10.0-11 9 <10.0 10.0-11 9 10.0 100-109 90-99 Pulse >100 Melaena Syncope Hepatic disease Cardiac failure Score 2 3 4 5 1 3 6 1 6 1 2 3 1 1 2 2 2\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 23-year-old female with her boyfriend came to the ED with life threatening asthma. Early resuscitation has been started. On further assessment, you've decided to intubate the patient with RSI. Which of the following sedation drug is indicated in this patient?\n- **A)** Propofol\n- **B)** Nitrous oxide   C Thiopental\n- **D)** Ketamine\n- **E)** Etomidate Explanation\n- Rapid Sequence Intubation (RSI) Medications for asthmatic patient:\n- Ketamine - is preferred agent because it is relatively hemodynamically stable and it has bronchodilatory properties.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 50-year-old male brought to the ED by paramedics with complaint of vomiting and blurred vision. his observations include BP  pulse 46bpm. On further assessment, it has been revealed that patient on certain medications for his heart condition prescribed by his GP. His ECG is given. What is the most likely cause Of his condition?\n- **A)** Furosemide\n- **B)** Nitrate C\n- Digoxin toxicity\n- **D)** Amitriptyline overdose\n- **E)** Beta blocker toxicity Explanation\n- ECG suggest sinus bradycardia with PR and QRS prolongation. They are common features of digoxin toxicity. Digoxin toxicity_ Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside indicated for the treatrnent Of chronic heart failure and persistent atrial fibrillation. In therapeutic doses, digoxin increases cardiac contractility and controls the heart rate.\n- Serum digoxin concentration\n- Serum digoxin concentration is usually over the therapeutic range of 0.6 to 1.2 nanomol/L (0.5 to 0.9 nanograms/mL) in patients taking digoxin for heart failure. However, some patients can be toxic at therapeutic digoxin serum concentrations\n- Risk factors\n- Age years\n- Decreased renal clearance\n- Electrolyte disturbance - hyperkalaemia, hypokalaemia,\n- Use of specific drugs (e.g., amiodarone, clarithromycin, ciclosporin, itraconazole, propafenone, spironolactone, verapamil, diltiazem, ketoconazole, vinblastine, doxorubicin, reserpine, and erythromycin).\n\n- Clinical features\n- History Of digoxin intake for medical condition or exposure to digoxin\n- Palpitations, syncope, arrhythmias, and dyspnoea\n- Nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, or anorexia.\n- Lethargy, weakness, and confusion.\n- Visual symptoms - disturbances of colour vision with a tendency to perceive yellow halos around objects (xanthopsia), blurred vision, and diplopia.\n- Investigations\n- ECG features\n- Acute overdose usually causes a marked bradycardia with PR and QRS prolongation Premature ventricular contractions are the most common dysrhythmia. Bigeminy or trigeminy occur frequently Ventricular bigeminy - each sinus beats is followed by a heartbeat that arrives too quickly with prolonged duration and no apparent P wave. These findings are indicative of premature ventricular contraction/ ventricular ectopic beat. A— represents normal sinus beat. B — represents premature ventricular contraction Let'S make it simple premature ventricular contraction (PVC) occurs in every two cardiac cycle that's why this ECG pattern called bigeminy.\n\n- Ventricular trigeminy - This pattern can be two normal (sinus) beats and one abnormal one or two premature ventricular contractions (PVCs) With one sinus beat. Aß=represents normal sinus beat. C— represents premature ventricular contraction\n- alsoseen in Other conditions i-e cocaine, anxiety etc.\n- Other-investigations\n- serum digoxin concentration - severe toxic effects may be seen with digoxin concentrations greater than 4.0 microgram/L (4.0 nanogram/mL; 5.2 nanomol/L).\n- serum potassium level and magnesium level\n- serum creatinine and urea\n- Management\n- Perform an Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure (ABCDE) assessment and re-assess the patient regularly throughout treatment,\n- Be alert to the possibility of cardiac arrest, resuscitation and supportive treatment may be needed\n- Correct electrolyte abnormalities in line with local protocol i-e hyperkalaemia with IV calcium chloride or gluconate.\n- djgoxin&indjng-therapy.\n\n- Urgentlyadminister digoxin-specific antibody (Fab) fragments (also known as digoxin immune Fab) in patients with strongly suspected or known digoxin toxicity and:\n- Cardiac arrest\n- Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia or severe bradyarrhythmia\n- Severe hyperkalaemia (potassium concentrations >6.5 mmol/L [>6.5 mEq/L]) resistant to conventional treatment.\n- Repeated doses may be required, for example if the patient remains unstable with arrhythmias or hyperkalaemia.\n- Frequently monitor the patient's serum potassium and magnesium. Correct hypomagnesaemia and hypokalaemia, if present, before or during administration of digoxin-specific antibody (Fab) fragments.\n- Agti.yated_ghar.gpaEgive the activated charcoal within 2 hours of acute ingestion, although even later administration may be beneficial.\n- Give further doses every 4 hours by mouth or nasogastric tube. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 35-year-old male brought to the ED after collapsing at home, he has been intubated and ventilated. Following assessment, his ETC02 is recorded. a What is the waveform capnography of this patient indicates?\n- **A)** COPD\n- **B)** Hyperventilation\n- **C)** Sudden loss Of waveform\n- **D)** Cardiac arrest   E\n- Hypoventilation Explanation\n- Hypoventilation increase in\n- Decrease in RR or decrease in tidal volume\n- Increase in metabolic rate previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	56	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
