551a7e37-47c2-4364-a8ca-deeb12905b70	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-59-590	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 59	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 59\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	590	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
48ab9efa-df9b-467d-902b-d18226f6c28d	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-59	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 59	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 59\n\ne VIRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA   Overview . Comments\n\nWhat is the target systolic blood pressure in the initial management of this patient?\n- **A)** 110 mmHg\n- **B)** 130 mmHg x   C 120 mmHg\n- **D)** 90 mmHg   E\n- 100 mmHg\n- Explanation\n- RCEM (Royal College of Emergency Medicine) guidelines for the management of acute aortic dissection, the key aim is to reduce the systolic blood pressure to less than 120 mmHg in order to limit the forces acting on the aorta, which can prevent further dissection and rupture.\n- Most of literature recommends target Systolic blood pressure 100-120 mmHg and Heart rate beats per minute.\n- Manag m n i n (TAD)\n\nPain relief BP&ratecontrot\n- Definitive Opioids Recommendation - a cut Off target for systolic blood pressure Of <120 mmHg is generally used. HR- the target HR should be at least bpm, though some publications advocate the more restrictive target of around 60 bpm. Drugs — Beta blockers or rate control CCB: First— line: labetalol 0.25 mg/kg (usually 20 mg) as a bolus over 2 min. Further boluses of 20-80 mg are usually required every 10 min once BP controlled a continuous infusion at 2 mg/min. Cl.Cardiogenic shock, AV block, bradycardia, COPD (relative contraindication) Others — esmolol or metoprolol If BB contraindicates then use rate control CCB — Verapamil or Diltiazem already be on a beta blocker) Glyceryl trinitrate - 2-10 mg/hour infusion — titrate to blood pressure Sodium nitroprusside - 0.5-1.5 mcg/kg/min infusion — titrate to blood pressure. Type—Axurgent surgery is required for type A dissections because they have the potential to rupture into the pericardium with rapidly fatal consequences. Type—EVA meta-analysis of endovascular stent-graft placements for type B AAD showed that the procedure was successful in 95% of the patients selected for such treatment. Depending upon the aetiology of the branch vessel occlusion either a stent placed in the origin of the vessel or percutaneous balloon fenestration can be used to alleviate pain or other complications  1 . A 40-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department after twisting his ankle during a fall. Examination reveals a deformed, swollen ankle with a posterior dislocation. Distal pulses are intact. You attempt a closed reduction under sedation, but the post-reduction X-ray confirms persistent dislocation of the ankle. What is the most appropriate next step in management?\n- **A)** Refer to orthopedics for urgent surgical intervention\n- **B)** Perform bedside ultrasound to assess soft tissue interposition\n- **C)** Perform a second closed reduction attempt\n- **D)** Discharge the patient with crutches and advice on elevation\n- **E)** Immobilize in a below-knee plaster splint and arrange outpatient follow-up\n- Explanation\n- Persistent dislocation after an initial closed reduction attempt suggests underlying factors such as soft tissue interposition (e.g., deltoid ligament, posterior tibial tendon) or a fracture-dislocation like a Bosworth fracture-dislocation, where the distal fibula is locked behind the posterior tibial tubercle.\n- Further closed reduction attempts are typically discouraged, as they risk further damage to surrounding structures. Immediate referral to orthopedics is essential for surgical management, often requiring open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) to address the underlying pathology and restore joint alignment.\n- Bosworth fracture a special type of locked ankle fracture- dislocation, characterized by displacement of a fragment of the\n\nfractured fibula from the fibular notch behind the posterior surface of the distal tibia.\n- BF is a complex injury affecting multiple structures of the ankle joint, which is still frequently misjudged even today, potentially leading to severe complications.\n- The basic radiographic examination of the ankle includes anteroposterior, mortise, and lateral views. When there is a suspected MF (Maisonneuve fracture), anteroposterior and lateral radiographs of the entire lower leg, including the knee joint, should be added.\n- CT examination, including 3D reconstructions, should be the diagnostic standard in BF, as it provides a complete picture of the fracture pathoanatomy, most prominently the morphology of the frequently associated posterior malleolar fracture.\n- BF requires early reduction of the displaced fibular fragment without repeated attempts on closed reduction. Non-operative treatment of BF almost always fails. The standard treatrnent procedure is early open reduction internal fixation.\n- Due to the relative severity and paucity of the injury, BF seems to be particularly prone to soft tissue complications, including compartment syndrome.\n- The results of operative treatrnent are mixed. Many studies report persistent pain even after a short time interval, with limitations of the range of motion or even stiffness of the ankle joint, and development of degenerative changes. Larger studies with long-term results are still missing.\n\nUnsuccessful attempt at closed reduction of a Bosworth fracture. (A) Postinjury radiographs; (B) postreduction radiographs with persisting mild posterior subluxation of the talus and persisting tibiofibular diastasis.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 38-year-old presents to the ED with shortness of breath which started suddenly while he was waiting for his remote job interview to start. He has no recent illnesses but is currently in plaster awaiting an Achilles' tendon repair He has a PMH of anxiety and no family history and he takes propranolol. Auscultation of his chest is normal his observations are:\n\nHR 115 bpm RR 30 per minute BP 136/76mmHg sp02 95% on air Ecg shows normal sinus rhythm What is the most appropriate initial management step?\n- **A)** Encourage re-breathing exercises\n- **B)** Check a D-dimer level\n- **C)** Start low dose benzodiazepine   D\n- Request a CTPA\n- **E)** Discharge home with reassurance Explanation\n- The use of lower limb plaster casts increases a patient's risk for VTE\n- Patient PE well score :\n- Short of breath indicates PE likely = 3 HR>100bpm = 1.5\n\ntotal score 4 5\n- PE score more than 4 points)\n- Offer a computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) immediately if possible-OR\n- If patient allergic to contrast media or has renal impairment then assess the suitability of a ventilation/perfusion single photon emission computed tomography (WQ SPECT) scan or, if a V/Q SPECT scan is not available, a V/Q planar scan, as an alternative to CTPA.\n- If a CTPA, V/Q SPECT or V/Q planar scan cannot be done immediately, offer interim therapeutic anticoagulation (DOAC — apixaban or rivaroxaban. If neither suitable then LMWH followed by dabigatran or edoxaban or LMWH and a VKA).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 21 -year-old male presented to the ED with a wrist injury. His x-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Scaphoid fracture\n- **B)** Lunate dislocation\n- **C)** Normal x-ray\n\nD\n- Peri-lunate dislocation\n- **E)** Scapholunate dislocation Explanation;\n- X-ray show lunate stays in position while carpus dislocates.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Note: diagnosis depends on X-ray so be familiar with both per-lunate and lunate.**\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 67-year-old female brought to the ED by ambulance with complaint of dysuria. She gives history of taking antibiotics from several days and you suspect UT l. On your review in the ED she looks profound anxious and lethargic. Her observations are: T 38.90C BP 80/45mmHg Pulse 120bpm His haematology lab is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura\n- **B)** Drug induced thrombocytopenia\n- **C)** Idiopathic thrombocytopenia\n- **D)** Acute myeloid leukaemia\n- **E)** Disseminated intravascular coagulation\n\nExplanation Patient clinical picture suggests DIC\n- Investigations of DIC\n- platelet count — decrease\n- prothrombin time (PT) — prolong\n- activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) - prolong\n-\n- Fibrinogen — Decrease\n- D-dimer/fibrin degradation products — elevated thrombin time - prolong\n-\n- ITP vs TTP vs DIC\n\nIdiopathic thrombotic Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura purpura\n- TTP is a clinical syndrome Primary immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is a characterised by microangiopathic haematological disorder haemolytic anaemia and characterised by isolated thrombocytopenic purpura thrombocytopenia (platelet count Classic pentad <100 x 109/L 1<100 x Microangiopathic haemolytic 103/microlitrel) in the absence of anaemia, an identifiable cause Thrombocytopenic purpura Neurological dysfunction Renal dysfunction, and fever\n- Risk factors involves the Women of childbearing age absence of von Willebrand factor Age <10 or >65 years cleaving enzyme (ADAMTS-13).\n- Risk factors black ethnicity female gender obesity pregnancy (near term or post- partum period) Cancer therapies HIV infection bone marrow transplantation antiplatelet agents quinine\n- Clinical features\n- Clinical features Bleeding - Signs of bleeding (e.g., Severe neurological symptoms (coma, focal abnormalities, bruising, petechiae, haemorrhagic seizures) bullae) Absence of systemic symptoms Digestive symptoms - nausea, Absence of medicines that cause vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal thrombocytopenia -The use of paln bleeding symptoms (purpura, heparin, alcohol, quinine/quinidine, sulfa drugs, ecchymosis, menorrhagia) and many other drugs may cause Fever. drug-induced thrombocytopenia. Absent splenomegaly or hepatomegaly Absent lymphadenopathy Investigations\n- Investigations FBC and peripheral blood smear-\n- Platelet count — Decreased Platelet count <100 x 109/L (<100 Haemoglobin - g/L x IOYmicrolitre). peripheral smear - Platelet count decrease microangiopathic blood film with Bleeding time increase schistocytes Reticulocyte count — raised Disseminated intravascular coagulation Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is an acquired syndrome characterised by activation Of coagulation pathways, resulting in formation Of intravascular thrombi and depletion of platelets and coagulation factors.\n- Risk factors major trauma/burn/organ destruction or sepsis/severe infection severe obstetric disorders or complications solid tumours and haematological malignancies severe toxic or immunological reaction\n- Clinical features Oliguria, hypotension, or tachycardia\n- Purpura fulminans, gangrene, or acral cyanosis Delirium or coma Petechiae, ecchymosis, oozing, or haematuria Investi tions\n- platelet count — decrease prothrombin time (PT) — prolong activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) - prolong Fibrinogen — Decrease D-dimer/fibrin degradation\n\n- Management All patients (child or adult): with life- or organ-threatening bleeding - IVIG plus corticosteroid and platelet transfusion Acute without major bleeding — require observation Persistent or chronic disease - mycophenolate rituximab\n- thrombopoietin receptor agonist Consider splenectomy (plus treatment to achieve target platelet level) Urinalysis — proteinuria urea and creatinine — raised ADAMTS-13 activity assay and inhibitor titres — decreased activity direct Coombs' test — negative\n- Management Acquired (idiopathic) TTP acute episode - plasma exchange and corticosteroids Consider caplacizumab Consider Aspirin 2nd line - immunosuppression 3rd line Splenectomy — elevated thrombin time - prolong D-dimer (monoclonal antibody test) antithrombin Ill\n- Management\n- Treat the underlying cause\n- Transfuse platelets + coagulation factors and coagulation inhibitors\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 36-year-old male brought into the ED with a spinal cord injury. He has ipsilateral loss of motor and sensory weakness on the right side of the body. What more examination findings will be in this patient?\n- **A)** Ipsilateral facial weakness\n- **B)** Ipsilateral loss of temperature\n- **C)** Contralateral loss of vibration\n- **D)** Contralateral loss of temperature\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- **E)** Ipsilateral loss of pain Explanation; Patient clinical features suggestive of Brown-Séquard syndrome Features of Brown-Séquard syndrome Ipsilateral hemiparesis Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch sensation Contralateral loss of crude touch, pain, and temperature sensation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 5-year-old child presented to the ED by his mother. She reports that he has eaten detergent in the kitchen. Which of the following is not upper respiratory obstruction?\n- **A)** Drooling\n- **B)** Biphasic stridor C\n- Hoarseness\n- **D)** Stridor\n\nE\n- Expiratory Wheeze Explanation Features/ sign of airway obstruction Hoarseness Stridor Drooling Snoring Gurgling Expiratory wheeze caused by obstruction of upper airways caused by obstruction of upper airway unable to swallow secretion (upper airway obstruction) caused by obstruction at or above the laryngeal level (upper airway) vomit, blood or secretions in airway (upper airway ) caused by obstruction of lower airways\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 24-year-old girl presented to the ED with a complaint vaginal discharge. She states that she had unprotected sex with her new partner. Her examination revealed an inflamed cervix. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Send cervical swab culture\n- **B)** Refer her to GUM clinic for STI screening\n- **C)** Ask her about her husband symptoms\n- **D)** Start HIV prophylaxis\n- **E)** Blood culture\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  The patient rnost likely has an uncomplicated chlamydia infection.\n- If chlamydia infection is suspected or confirmed - NICE strongly recommends referral to a genito-urinary medicine (GUM) clinic for management.\n\nChlamydia infection Genital chlamydial infection is the most commonly reported, curable, bacterial sexually transmitted infection (STI) in the UK. It is caused by the obligate intracellular bacterium Chlamydia trachomatis_\n- At least 70% of women and 50% of men infected with\n- **C)** trachomatis are asymptomatic.\n- Infection of the urogenital tract typically causes inflammation of the: Urethra in men.\n- Cervix andlor urethra in women Chlamydial infection is termed:\n- Uncomplicated' - when the infection has not ascended to the upper genital tract. Complicated' - when the infection has spread to the upper genital tract, causing pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) in women and epididymo-orchitis in men. Risk factors Age under 25 years.\n- A new sexual partner. More than one sexual partner in the last year.\n- Lack of consistent condom use.\n- Infection is primarily through penetrative sex, but can also occur via autoinoculation or splash from genital fluids. Clinical features\n- Suspect chlamydia in sexually active women with:\n- Increased vaginal discharge.\n- Post-coital or intermenstrual bleeding.\n- Purulent vaginal discharge. Mucopurulent cervical discharge. Deep dyspareunia. Dysuria.\n- Pelvic pain and tenderness.\n- Cervical motion tenderness.\n- Inflamed or friable cervix (which may bleed on contact).\n- Suspect chlamydia in sexually active men with: Dysuria.\n- Mucoid or mucopurulent urethral discharge.\n- Urethral discomfort/urethritis.\n- Epididymo-orchitis.\n- Reactive arthritis. Symptoms of lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV) include: Gonorrhoea infection\n- Gonorrhoea is a sexually transmitted infection (STI) caused by the Gram-negative bacterium Neisseria gonorrhoeae.\n- Uncomplicated gonorrhoea infection primarily affects the mucous membranes of the urethra. endocervix, rectum, pharynx, and conjunctiva.\n- Gonorrhoea is transmitted by direct inoculation of secretions from one mucous membrane to another. Risk factors\n- Young age (15—24 years).\n- New sexual contact in the last year, or more than one partner in the last year\n- Inconsistent condom use. Certain sexual activities, for example men who have sex with men (MSM). Current or prior history of sexually transmitted infection (risk factor for repeat infections).\n- History of sexual or physical abuse. Previous incarceration. Deprivation Clinical features\n- Gonorrhoea is primarily associated with uncomplicated infection of the lower genital tract, which is symptomatic in most men (over 90%) and approximately 50% of women.\n- In men:\n- Urethral infection - mucopurulent or purulent urethral discharge.\n- And/or dysuria (in more than 50% of cases)\n- Rectal infection - usually asymptomatic but may cause anal discharge, acute proctitis, perianal/anal pain or discomfort, tenesmus, or rectal bleeding. 'n women:\n- Urethral infection - ay present with dysuria without urinary frequency.\n- Endocervical infection — may presents with Increased or altered vaginal discharge.\n- Lower abdominal pain (up to 25% of cases).\n- Intermenstrual bleeding or menorrhagia (rarely).\n- pain during intercourse (dyspareunia) if the infection spreads from the endocervix.\n- Rectal infection - is usually asymptomatic but may cause anal discharge and perianal/anal pain or discomfort. Pharyngeal infection - is asymptomatic in most cases but is occasionally associated with a sore throat.\n\nlenesmus.\n- Anorectal discharge (often bloody) and discomfort.\n- Diarrhoea or altered bowel habit. Symptoms of rectal chlamydia include:\n- Anal discharge and anorectal discomfort, although rectal infection is usually asymptomatic. Investiqations If chlamydia is suspected - samples should be taken for nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) to confirm the diagnosis. 'n women:\n- A vulvoaaaginal or endocervical swab can be taken — a vulvo-vaginal swab is the sample Of choice In women.\n- Alternatively, a first-catch urine (FCU) sample can be collected - The first 20 mL of the urinary stream should be captured.\n- In men: A FCU is the specimen of choice\n- A urethral swab is an alternative Extra-qenital samples Rectal swabs in symptomatic men and women can be taken 'blind' by the person or a clinician. All people with proctitis should have rectal swabs taken to test for lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV). Manaqement If chlamydia infection is suspected or confirmed - NICE strongly recommend referral to a genito- urinary medicine (GUM) clinic for management.\n- If the person declines, or is unable to attend a GUM clinic, manage uncomplicated genital chlamydia infection in primary care. If a sexually transmitted infection (STI) is suspected or diagnosed in a child or young person: Have a low threshold for discussing with a GUM specialist or paediatrician — local child safeguarding procedures should be followed. Advise & written information Offer information on transmission, treatment, possible complications, and measures to reduce the risk Of further STIs.\n- Their current partner(s) must also be treated for chlamydia to reduce the risk of re-infection and onward transmission.\n- Sexual intercourse (including Oral sex) should be avoided until they and their partner(s) have completed treatment (or waited 7 days after treatment with azithromycin). Antibiotics For non-pregnant adults and children over the age of 13 years - First-line doxycycline 100 mg Bid for 7 days If doxycycline is contraindicated or not tolerated Investiqations All people with suspected gonorrhoea should be referred to a genito-urinary medicine (GUM) clinic or other local specialist sexual health service for confirmation of the diagnosis.\n- If the person is unwilling, or unable, to attend a GUM clinic or other local specialist sexual health service, a NAAT should be arranged in line with local procedures and protocols\n- In women - a vulvovaginal swab (which may be self- taken) should be used.\n- In men - a first pass urine specimen should be used. Specimens for culture (urethral, endocervical, neovaginal, anorectal, and pharyngeal swabs) should be taken alongside NAATs from people suspected clinically of having gonorrhoea (and from their sexual contacts). Manaqement\n- All people with gonorrhoea should be referred to a genito-urinary medicine (GUM) clinic Or other local specialist sexual health service for management.\n- If the person is unwilling, or unable, to attend a GUM clinic or other local specialist sexual health service despite receiving appropriate information and advice:\n- Antibiotic treatment When the antimicrobial susceptibility is known prior to treatment, prescribe ciprofloxacin 500 mg orally as a single dose.\n- When antimicrobial susceptibility is not known prior to treatment, prescribe ceftriaxone 1 g intramuscular (1M) injection as a single dose. Altemative treatments are recommended for people with an allergy, needle phobia, or other contraindications:\n- Gentamicin 240 mg 1M as a single dose plus azithromycin 2 g orally OR cefixime 400 mg orally as a single dose plus azithromycin 2 g orally.\n- For people with penicillin allergy -ceftriaxone and cefixime are suitable\n- For preqnant or breastfeedinq women: prescribe ceftriaxone 1 g 1M injection as a single dose. Azithromycin 2 g as a single oral dose can be used\n\nconsider azithromycin 1 g orally as a single dose for 1 day, followed by 500 mg orally OD for 2 days.\n- Ofloxacin 200 mg twice daily for 7 days, or 400 mg Once daily for 7 days is a possible alternative, but it is contraindicated in pregnancy, children, and growing adolescents. For preqnant women Azithromycin, 1 g orally for 1 day, then 500 mg orally once daily for 2 days, or Erythromycin 500 mg four times daily for 7 days, or erythromycin 500 mg twice daily for 14 days, or Amoxicillin 500 mg three times a day for 7 days. Discuss further investigation and partner notification:\n- Strongly encourage all people diagnosed with chlamydia to attend GLJM for screening for other STIS (including gonorrhoea, hepatitis B, HIV, and syphilis) and partner notification. Arrange follow up:\n- Ask about adherence to treatment and resolution of symptoms. Offer repeat testing to all people under the age Of 25 years diagnosed with chlamydia 3—6 months after completion Of treatment to check for re-infection. Complications\n- Epididymo-orchitis Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV)\n- Sexually acquired reactive arthritis (SARA)\n- Perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome) Conjunctivitis adequate altematives. Do not prescribe ciprofloxacin. Encourage patient-led partner notification\n- Partner notification is essential for all people with newly diagnosed gonorrhoea. Ideally, this should be done by a trained health adviser in genito- urinary medicine (GUM).\n- Advice:\n- Advise the person to attend a GUM clinic or other local specialist sexual health service if possible.\n- Discuss the possible complications of untreated gonorrhoea.\n- Advise the person to abstain from sex until 7 days after they and their partner(s) have completed treatment.\n- Advise the person on using safer sexual practice in the future.\n- Follow up all people with gonorrhoea about 1 week after treatment to:\n- Confirm that the person has adhered to treatment and symptoms have resolved.\n- Ask about adverse reactions.\n- Confirm that partner notification has been carried out.\n- Ask about recent sexual history (and the possibility Of re-infection).\n- Reinforce advice on safe sexual practice. Complications Epididymo-orchitis\n- Prostatitis, infertility\n- Urethral stricture, Infection of Mullerian or Cowper glands Pregnancy complications, including spontaneous abortion\n- Septic arthritis, polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, petechial/pustular skin lesions, endocarditis, or meningitis Common vaginal infections\n\nBacterial vaginosis Trichomonas vaginitis Candida vulvovaginitis Clinical features No inflammation\n- Thin, white discharge with fishy odor\n- Treatment Metronidazole or clindamycin\n- Clinical features\n- Inflammation ("strawberry cervix")\n- Frothy, yellow-green, foul-smelling discharge Treatment Metronidazole\n- Treat sexual partner(s)\n- Clinical features\n- Inflammation\n- Thick, white, "cottage cheese" discharge Vaginal itching or soreness\n- Treatment\n- Fluconazole or boric acid, nystatin, or flucytosine\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nYou suspect that your colleague a junior doctor is under illicit drugs. Therefore, you confront him, but he denied the allegation. You still suspect he is taking drugs. What would be your next action?\n- **A)** Raise issue to the GMC\n- **B)** Ask him to get an appointment with psychiatrist\n- **C)** Ask the nurse to inform the consultant\n- **D)** Seek advice from medical defense organization\n- **E)** Inform the consultant In this case, you already confront him so here you need to inform the\n- consultant/supervisor First — confront him with advice.\n-\n- lf-he-stilLcontinues-then=inform the consultant Still not satisfied and still has concerns - raise it to the CMC\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 23-year-old man is brought to the ED unconscious and intubated after his Glasgow coma scale (GCS) decreased in transport. Emergency services had been called as he had been experiencing rigors and chills after ingesting an unknown compound. His observations are: sp02: Heart rate: Temp: GCS: 100% on 10 L oxygen 63 beats per minute  mmHg 98 F.  His examination reveals pinpoint pupils bilaterally. Corneal reflexes and doll's eye response were intact. Bilaterally down going planters were noted. Auscultation of the chest reveals decreased air entry on the right side with crackles bilaterally. Detoxification is started for the suspected ingested substance. Rapid infusion of the antidote given to treat the nicotinic effects of the suspected substance can cause which of the following complication?\n- **A)** Glove and stocking paresthesia B\n- Cardiac arrest\n\nC, Seizures\n- **D)** Acute respiratory distress syndrome\n- **E)** Diarrhea Explanation\n- Ingestion of organophosphates cause increased acetylcholine levels. The increased levels of acetylcholine lead to anxiety, seizures, coma, bradycardia, pinpoint pupils, salivation, lacrimation, and hyperactive bowel sounds.\n- Atropine is used to treat organophosphate poisoning, a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors. Adult dosing is initially 2 to 5 mg IV, which can be repeated every 3 to 5 minutes until the respiratory compromise has resolved.\n- Atropine acts on muscarinic receptors only; thus, pralidoxime (2-PAM) should also be administered to cover the nicotinic receptors. A bolus of at least 30 mg/kg in adults should be given. Rapid administration can cause cardiac arrest. Organophosphate exposure can occur from pesticide exposure, for example, in crop workers. Contamination can occur following inhalation, ingestion, or dermal contact e previOUS\n	\N	[]	\N	f	5	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
08139cec-512c-4bc2-a5e5-700e0c3b1bc5	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-1	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 1	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 1\n\nA intranasal diamorphine\n- **B)** Give Entonox\n- **C)** Cive him toys\n- **D)** Dressing and cold compression\n- **E)** Parents included with him\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> The question relevant to pain management as other psychiatric strategies of pain management are given in the options.\n- Pain management in children (RCEM guideline)\n- psychological strategies: involving parents, cuddles, child-friendly environment, and explanation with reassurance all help build trust. Also, distraction with toys, blowing bubbles, reading, or story-telling using superhero or magical imagery to make the pain go away.\n- The use of non-pharmacological-adjunctszsuch as limb immobilisation, dressings for burns\n- Pharmacological-agents, via a variety of routes described below\n\nAssess pain severity Use splints/slings/dressings etc Consider other causes of distress* For procedures consider regional blocks and conscious sedation MILD PAIN (1-3) Oral/rectal pacagetame! 20 mg/kg loading dose, then 15 mgfkg 4-6 hourly Oral ibuprg&n 10 mg/kg 6-8 hourly MODERATE PAIN (4-6) As for mild pain Oral/rectal diGlQtgnac 1 mg/kg 8 hourly (unless already had ibuprofen) Oral 1 mg/kg 4-6 hourly (over 12 years) OR Oral 0.2-0.5 mgAg stat SEVERE PAIN (7-10) Consider Entonox as holding measure Intranasal 0.2 (20.1 rng/kg) (see table) followed by / or IV O. I -0.2 mg,'kg Supplemented by oral analgesics\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA paramedic calls for advise from a nearby house. They were called to an unresponsive infant, and they have found a four-month-old baby who has died in their cot. He is unsure where to take the body of body who is currently with its parents in the house. Following SODIC guidance. What should you advise him to do?\n- **A)** Inform the duty social worker and ask them to attend the house\n- **B)** Bring the baby to the ED\n- **C)** Ask the child protection team to contact the family directly\n- **D)** Leave the baby at home and ask the GP to attend\n- **E)** Advise that the police should take the body\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> Sudden unexpected death in childhood (SUDIC) Child found collapsed Anvwhere but not in hospital If Gp present at the Scene : call ambulance immediately and it is important GP should contact the police or Coroner's Office it they are the first on the Ambulance called. Control Centre inform Police Attempt resuscitation Observations in the honr History Update Police If death declared at the Paramedic should inform hospital and Child to the ER inform Children's Services that SUDiC has been initiated and deceased child is being transferred to the mortuary At the earliest opportunity the hospital Safeguarding Team should be notified by A&E Post-mortem requ i red Family / main carers accompanied at all times by appropriate Health Professional and / or Family Liaison Officer Coroner's Report SUDiC case Meeting Concluding SUDic case by ED team Call senior paediatrician or ED consultant Child declared dead Inform coroner\n- Investigation Check any concerns Inform Social care Inform Safeguarding Team Follow death of a child checklist Explain need for sample (not need in NN) SUDiC Case Discussion Request hospital notes Of Child & Siblings Request all agency records Note: there maybe some variationsto guideline so follow local protocol.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nAn 87-year-old woman from a residential home with a 2-day history of diarrhea. She has had 10 episodes of watery diarrhea but no vomiting. She complains of colicky lower abdominal pain. She has had a 5-day course of antibiotics for a chest infection two weeks ago. Her observations are: RR 18/min, sp02 95% on air HR 89 bpm BP  mmHg Temp 36.8'C BM 5.6 On examination, her abdomen is soft with generalized tenderness especially across her lower abdomen. What is the most likely causative organism?\n- **A)** E. coli\n- **B)** Rotavirus   C Norovirus\n\nD\n- CIOStridium difficile\n- **E)** Campylobacter\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> her recent course of antibiotic rynost likely the cause of her condition.\n- Diarrhea — antibiotic associated\n- Disruption of the bowel microbiota and mucosal integrity. Overgrowth of toxin-producing strains of Clostridium (Clostridioides)\n- difficile (a Gram-positive, spore-forming, anaerobic bacillus) due to disruption of harmless bacteria in the gut:\n- Risk factors Age more than 65 years Underlying morbidity — Abdominal surgery, CKD, immunosuppression.\n- Prolonged hospitalization or residence in a nursing home\n- Antibiotics that have been frequently associated with\n- difficileinfection include clindamycin,\n- cephalosporins (especially third and fourth generation),\n- fluoroquinolones,\n-\n\n- broad-spectrum penicillins.\n- Other drugs_(acid suppression)\n- PPI and H—z-receptor antagonists\n- Investigations\n- FBC\n- Send stool sample to test for difficile infection\n- Management\n- Manage fluid loss and symptoms as for acute gastroenteriti*\n- Avoid the use of antimotility drugs (such as loperamide) to treat diarrhoeal symptoms.\n- NICE recommends:\n- For the initial episode of mild-to-moderate difficile infection: vancomycin 125mg orally four times a day for IO days.\n- Second-line antibiotic advice for a first epsiode of mild-to-moderate difficile infection: fidaxomicin 200mg orally twice a day for IO days.\n- Advise\n- Wash hand frequently with soap water\n- Note that alcohol-based hand rubs are not effective in removing difficilespores.\n\n- The diarrhoea should resolve in 1—2 weeks. It is not usually possible to determine whether antibiotic treatment is effective until day 7.\n- They should not return to work or school until they have been free from diarrhoea for 48 hours. If medication is prescribed, they should ensure that the full course is completed and there is no further diarrhoea or vomiting for 48 hours afterwards.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 45-years-old male presented to the ED with 2 days history of back pain, skin rash, abdominal pain and jaundice. When you review him, he admits to finishing a bottle of 60 paracetamol tablets over the past 2 days. On Examination, his right upper quadrant is tender. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Acute Cholangitis\n- **B)** Viral hepatitis\n- **C)** HIV\n- **D)** Acute fulminant hepatic failure\n- **E)** Cholecystitis\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> - Patients are often asymptomatic or have only mild gastrointestinal symptoms at initial presentation.\n- Untreated paracetamol poisoning may cause varying degrees of liver injury over the 2 to 4 days following ingestion, including fulminant hepatic failure\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nWhilst in a restaurant you find a mother who tells you that her 18 months old son is having choking episodes, you found the baby is gasping and unresponsive. What is your next action to rescue the child?\n- **A)** Back blows and abdominal thrusts\n- **B)** Start chest compressions   C\n- 5 rescue breaths\n- **D)** 5 back blows and Chest thrust\n- **E)** 5 back blows\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> cou hin . Encourage the child to cough Management in Paeds Shout for help\n- Absent or Ineffective cough.\n- Unconscious child. 5 back blows Place the child on the firm/flat If not relieved surface and call for help 5 abdominal thrusts in children. 5 chest thrusts in Open airway lift jaw/ tongue infant. if obstruction relieved then Give S rescue breaths urgent medical follow-up. If chest isn't rise then reposition the head and try another attempt. If still not relieved then repeat back blows, Chest compression and CPR abdominal thrusts (children) (BLS) chest thrusts (infant)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 55-year-old male patient presented to the ED with 2 hours history of weakness and reduced sensation in his right face, arm and leg. There is no any seizure activity, Syncope ,Visual disturbance or Loss of Consciousness. He is known to be hypertensive and gives history of DC cardioversion last year for AE According to his clinical condition, what is his Rosier Score?\n\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  ROSIER scale\n- The Recognition of Stroke in the Emergency Room (ROSIER) scale was developed to help in the initial differentiation of acute stroke from stroke mimics in the ED.\n- This patient has right facial weakness +1 Arm weakness +1 Leg weakness +1 total score = 3\n\nClinical findings Loss of consciousness or syncope Seizure activity Value No (0) No (0) Yes-I Yes-I New, acute onset (or on awakening from sleep) finding of any of the following Asymmetric facial weakness Asymmetric arm weakness Asymmetric leg weakness Speech disturbance Visual field defect No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) Yes+l Yes+l Yes+l Yes+l Yes+l\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 13-month-old baby is brought into the ED by his mother with five days history of diarrhea and vomiting. On triage assessment, he appears alert and interacts appropriately but is slightly miserable. His observations include normal Bp with\n\nmild tachycardia and afebrile. His Serum glucose is 2.1 mmol/L and Ketone 1.3. He has no previous medical record. What is the rnost appropriate management?\n- **A)** Give Sg Dextrogel orally, then recheck blood sugar in 15 mints\n- **B)** Discharge with advice\n- **C)** Apply ametop, then give Sg Dextrogel orally\n- **D)** Immediately gain IV access, administer 2ml/kg 10% dextrose bolus followed by a continuous maintenance infusion of dextrose\n- **E)** Take blood for hypoglycemia screen and admit under General pediatrics\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> Paediatric by.pggJycaemia management\n\nNo Hypoglycaemia confirmed BM<2.6mmoVl Known diabetic patient No Collect blood sample Paediatric hypoglycaemia "Grab bag" bloods (1.5ml minimum — ideally 5ml) Patient able to tolerate oral fluids Yes Yes IV access obtained Yes IV bolus 2ml/kg 10% dextrose followed by continuous infusion 3-5mVkg/hr dextrose Recheck in 30mins then hourly Once BM >5mmol/l for 2 consecutive hours then reduce frequency of testing to 3-4hrly Maintenance Plasmalyte +/- KCL to be run separately No 1M Glucagon S25kg - >25kg — Img Recheck glucose in 15 mns. Continue to attempt IV Trial of oral carbohydrates 50ml Lucozade OR Glucogel Recheck BM in 15mins <2.6mmol/l 22.6mmoVl Offer starchy\n\ncarbohydrate if Aim BM 4-8mmol/l able to tolerate All patients with hypoglycaemia of unknown e.g toast/cereal cause require admission\n- Note;-if the patient is an infant (< 1 year) then milk feed the baby 60ml/kg/day.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 39-year-old female patient is brought into ED following an explosion after rescuing her dog from a house fire. On examination, she is noted to have a hoarse voice and be drooling. There is decreased air entry in the right lung base and there is a bleeding gash over her right thigh. She has burns on her arms and face. There is an obvious deformity to her wrist. Her observations are: Heart rate 115 bpm RR 35 breath/min Sat 92% on room air BP  mmHg Temp 36.4C GCS  (E3 M5 V4). On examination, her abdomen is tender in the RUQ. But there is no guarding. What is your immediate priority?\n- **A)** Plastics review\n- **B)** Orthopedic review   C\n- Anesthetic review\n- **D)** ENT review\n- **E)** Surgical review\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Followingfeatures-t\n- t-increase-the-suspicion-of-airway- obstruction or inhalation injury_ Singed nasal/facial hairs\n- Burns to the face/oropharynx\n- Inflamed oropharynx\n- Hoarse voice\n- Respiratory distress/ stridor\n- Carbonaceous sputum\n- History of burns in an enclosed space\n- Raised blood gas carbon monoxide (CO) level\n-\n- All patients with inhalation injury, burns, trauma or any critical illness are anticipated to have a risk of difficult endotracheal intubation,\n\ndifficult mask ventilation, pulmonary aspiration and cervical spine instability if there is coexisting trauma.\n- In inhalation injury, the airway is dynamically evolving with increasing facial and airway swelling over time, particularly after the initiation of intravenous fluid therapy.This makes failed intubation, potentially resulting in hypoxic cardiac arrest and/or hypoxic ischaemic encephalopathy, more likely with any delay.\n- Hence, early transfer to the EOT for definitive airway management is considered a time-critical intervention in patients with inhalation injury, as specialised airway equipment and staff, including anaesthesiologists and surgeons, would be available to perform a surgical airway or local anaesthetic tracheostomy if required.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 22-year-old man presents to ED with sudden onset of pleuritic chest pain, He is not breathless and feels otherwise well. He has no significant past medical history and is a non-smoker. His observations are: RR 18 breath/min sp02 97% on air HR 72 bpm BP  mmHg Temp 36.5'C His chest X-ray confirms a small right apical pneumothorax which is (1.8cm).\n\nWhat is the most appropriate further management step?\n- **A)** Discharge and follow up with respiratory outpatients\n- **B)** Admit to medicine for overnight observation\n- **C)** Observe in the ED for up to 4 hours\n- **D)** Aspirate with an 180 cannula\n- **E)** Insert Seldinger chest drain\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  As this patient is very young and a non-smoker. His history is unremarkable for any lung disease, therefore he would be categorise in primary spontaneous pneumothorax.\n- Primary_spontaneous pneumothorax Pneumothorax size <2cm\n-\n- discharge the Pt review in OPD in 2-4 weeks Pneumothorax >2cm or breathless\n- Aspirate with 16-180 cannula Aspirate <2.51\n- successful pneumothorax <2cm now and breathing improved then\n- consider discharge and follow in OPD 2-4 weeks Not successful, insert Chest drain Size 8-14Fr and admit the patient.\n-\n\nWNAGEMENT OF SPONTANEOUS ORAX 2 Of Of BTS guidelines  IO. A 33-year-old woman is presented to the ED with sudden onset of shortness of breath whilst shopping in the supermarket. She has no other symptoms but had a laparotomy and removal of uterine fibroids 3 weeks ago. She has no other Past medical history and is a non-smoker. Her observations are: Respiratory rate 26 breath/min, sp02 94% on air\n\nHeart rate 95bpm. Her examination is unremarkable. There is no swelling or tenderness in her lower legs. What is the correct Wells score for this patient?\n- **A)** 1.0   c. 1.5\n- **D)** 4.5\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  She has a history of surgery =1.5 points\n- An alternative diagnosis is less likely as she has sudden onset of SOB. =3 points\n- well score: 4.5\n\nClinical feature\n- Clinical signs and symptoms of DVT (minimum of leg swelling and pain with palpation of the deep veins)\n- An alternative diagnosis is less likely than 100 beat/min Immobilisation for more than 3 days or surgery in the previous 4 weeks\n- Previous DVT/PE Haemoptysis\n- Malignancy (on treatment, treated in the last 6 months, or palliative) Clinical probability simplified score PE likely\n- PE unlikely  9  Points 3 3 1.5 1.5 1.5 1 1 Points More than 4 points 4 points or less\n	\N	[]	\N	f	7	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
1dc9be5f-1837-46b7-a7a5-7087c96e178f	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-10	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 10	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 10\n\nA\n- Brown Sequard Syndrome\n- **B)** Connus medularis\n- **C)** Central Cord Syndrome\n- **D)** Posterior Cord Syndrome\n- **E)** Anterior Cord Syndrome EXPIanation Spinal cord syndrome syndrome Complete cord transection Brown- Sequard syndrome Central cord syndrome Mechanism Major trauma Hemitransection or unilateral compression of the usually seen in older Tract injury All tracts All tract on one side Corticospinal patients with preexisting tract and cervical spondylosis who spinothalamic sustain a hyperextension tract lnJury Symptoms Death if cl-c3 injured. Quadriplegia Paraplegia Complete sensory loss below lesion Urinary and faecal continence Ipsilateral hemiparesis Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration and fine touch sensation Contralateral loss of crude touch, pain and temperature sensation Bilateral motor loss Varying degree of sensory loss Greater loss in upper limb than lower limb Greater loss of motor in upper limb than sensory function\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 4-year-old child brought into the ED in cardiac arrest. You are asked to intubate this child. What's the diameter of ETT recommended by APLS?   B.5   c. 4.5 Explanation\n- ETT diameter: Age/4+4 4/4+4= 5\n\nETT formula Weigh formula ETT diameter : Age/4+4 ETT oral length: Age/2+12 ETT nasal length: Age/2+15 1-12 months: 0.5 x age +4 1-5 years: 2 x age +8 >5 years : 3 x age + 7\n- Note - Neonates usually require a tube of internal diameter 3-3.5 mm, although pre-term infants may need one of diameter 2.5 mm.\n- Cuffed tubes should not be used in neonates\n- Uncuffed sizes are 2.5 mm for a premature newborn kg, 3.0 mm for infants 1—3.5 kg, 3.5 mm for infants >3.5 kg and up to age of six months, size 4 mm for infants seven months to one year.\n- The approximate size for children over one year by the formula: size (mm) = age (years)/4 + 4.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 32-year-old pregnant female presented to the ED with severe vomiting. Initial fluid resuscitation and antiemetic drug was given. In a short period of time, she develops lip smacking and tongue protrusion. Which of the following is the drug of choice in her condition?   A Midazolam\n- **B)** Benztropine\n- **C)** Haloperidol with promethazine\n- **D)** Diazepam   E\n- Procyclidine Explanation\n- The movements described are characteristic of an acute dystonic reaction\n- An acute dystonic reaction is rnost often seen with antipsychotic or anti-emetic (metoclopramide) use.\n- Antimuscarinic drugs used in dystonias _ The antimuscarinic drugs procyclidine hydrochloride and trihexyphenidyl hydrochloride reduce the symptoms of dystonias, including those induced by antipsychotic drugs.\n- Note - Tardive dyskinesia is not improved by antimuscarinic drugs and may be made worse.\n- If treatment with an antimuscarinic is ineffective, intravenous diazepam can be given for life-threatening acute drug-induced dystonic reactions.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nAn 8-week pregnant lady presents to the ED with fresh vaginal bleeding. Her only PMH, she was diagnosed with inevitable abortion few days back at obs- gynae clinic. Her observations are: BP  mmHg, Pulse 50bpm. After initial assessment, you've started IV fluids. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Anti-D immunoglobulin\n- **B)** Refer to OBG\n- **C)** Give Tranexamic acid\n- **D)** Ultrasounds E\n- SPeculum examination Explanation\n\n- The most likely diagnoses of a haemodynamically unstable patient with early pregnancy bleeding are a ruptured ectopic pregnancy an incomplete miscarriage with 'cervical shock' (parasympathetic stimulation caused by products in the cervical os leading to hypotension and bradycardia) or massive haemorrhage secondary to miscarriage. A speculum examination must be performed and any products of conception (POC) removed from the cervical os. These patients may require urgent transfer to the operating theatre for a suction curettage, laparoscopy or laparotomy.\n- Miscarriage\n- A miscarriage is the spontaneous loss of a pregnancy before 24 weeks of gestation.\n- Early miscarriage - occurs before 13 weeks of gestation\n- Late miscarriage - occurs between 13 and 24 weeks of gestation\n- Miscarriage is classification\n\nComplete miscarriage Incomplete miscarriage Missed miscarriage Threatened miscarriage Inevitable miscarriage Recurrent miscarriage\n- Risk factors All the products of conception have been expelled from the uterus and bleeding has stopped. is a diagnosed non-viable pregnancy in which bleeding has begun but pregnancy tissue remains in the uterus (also known as delayed or silent miscarriage) is diagnosed when a non-viable pregnancy is identified on ultrasound scan, without associated pain and bleeding. is diagnosed when there is vaginal bleeding in the presence Of a viable pregnancy in the first 24 weeks of gestation. is a diagnosed non-viable pregnancy in which bleeding has begun and the cervical os is open, but pregnancy tissue remains in the uterus. The pregnancy will proceed to incomplete or complete miscarriage. is defined as the loss of three or more consecutive pregnancies before 24 weeks Of gestation __J\n- Chromosomal abnormalities are the most common cause of first trimester\n- Genetic abnormalities and defects in the development of the placenta or embryo\n- No cause of recurrent miscarriage can be determined in about 50% of couples\n- Maternal age and number of previous miscarriages\n- Occupational and environmental factors (such as heavy metals, pesticide, high dose radiation, and lack of micronutrients)\n- Lifestyle factors, such as stress, obesity, and smoking\n- Clinical features\n\n- Suspect a miscarriage in women who are pregnant, or with symptoms of pregnancy (amenorrhoea, missed period, breast tenderness), presenting with vaginal bleeding in the first 24 weeks of pregnancy.\n- Bleeding is typically scanty, varying from a brownish discharge to bright red bleeding, and may recur over several days.\n- Lower abdominal cramping pain or lower backache, when it occurs, usually develops after the onset of bleeding.\n- Ask all women with vaginal bleeding who are (or could be) within the-first-Eweeks-otpregnancy-about-symptoms-of-an-ectopic pregnancy, including;\n- Abdominal or pelvic pain.\n- Gastrointestinal symptoms (such as nausea and vomiting).\n- Dizziness, fainting or syncope.\n- Shoulder tip pain.\n- Urinary symptoms.\n- Passage of tissue.\n- Rectal pressure or pain on defecation\n- Assessment\n- Speculum-examination_is performed to assess the at-nount and origin of ongoing bleeding.\n- The vagina and cervix should be inspected for other causes of bleeding (eg polyps).\n\n- Tissue present in the open cervical os must always be removed and should be sent for histopathological examination to confirm retained POC. Bimanual examination allows assessment of uterine size, dilatation of\n- the cervical os, pelvic tenderness and cervical motion tenderness. Investigations\n- Ultrasonography (usually a transvaginal ultrasound scan) is used to assess the location and viability of the pregnancy. Serum beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) measurements.\n- Laparoscopy (if ectopic pregnancy is considered likely).\n-\n- Management\n- If-patient is haemodynamica.lJy- unstable (including-pallor, shock, and collapse) Follow ABCD protocol\n- Correct the coagulopathy\n- Consider central venous access\n- Suspected cervical shock: remove products of conception from the os\n- with the aid of a speculum and sponge forceps. Continued haemorrhage: consider administration\n- of ergometrine and oxytocin. (to promote uterine contraction)\n- Offer anti-D immunoglobulin\n\n- The majority of patients presenting to the ED with vaginal bleeding in pregnancy will not require resuscitation. Those that do require a multidisciplinary approach and involvement of senior obstetricians, paediatricians, anaesthetists, intensivists and haematologists should be sought early.\n- Other patients\n\nThreatened miscarriage\n- Analgesia paracetamol: 500-1000 mg every 4-6 hours when required. Counselling - Counselling for the mothers (or couples) should be given. Pregnancies that continue require closer follow-up and targeted fetal surveillance Consider progesterone - offering vaginal micronised progesterone to women with an intrauterine pregnancy confirmed by a scan, if they have vaginal bleeding and have previously had a mlscarnage.\n- If a fetal heartbeat is confirmed, progesterone should be continued until 16 completed weeks of pregnancy. Consider — anti-D immunoglobulin inevitable/incomplete/ missed miscarriage Expectant management is offered first line. Expectant management lasts for 7—14 days, and all women are given information on what to expect, advice on analgesia, and advice on how to get help in an emergency. If the bleeding and pain settle (suggesting complete miscarriage), - the woman will be advised to take a urine pregnancy test after 3 weeks and to return to the hospital if it is positive. Ifthe bleeding and pain persist or are increasing (suggesting incomplete miscarriage), or if bleeding and pain has not started (suggesting a missed miscarriage), a repeat scan is done and expectant, medical, and surgical options are discussed. Medical management is offered if expectant management is not clinically appropriate or a woman has ongoing symptoms after 14 days of expectant management. Vaginal or oral misoprostol is used for the treatment Of missed Or incomplete miscarriage to stimulate uterine expulsion of the products of conception.\n- Women are advised to do a pregnancy test after 3 weeks and return if it is positive Surgical intervention may be required if products of conception are retained despite medical treatment, or offered if the woman has ongoing symptoms after 14 days of expectant management.\n- Anti-D immunoglobulin is offered to all rhesus- negative women who have had a surgical procedure to manage a miscarriage.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 24-year-old young lady brought to the ED by her family after postpartum period with complaint of low back pain associated with lower limb numbness and generalized weakness. She states that she hasn't passed stool for many days. However, able to pass flatus.\n\nHer observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 88bpm What is your diagnosis?\n- **A)** Cauda equina syndrome\n- **B)** Myasthenia gravis\n- **C)** Multiple sclerosis\n- **D)** Appendicitis\n- **E)** Intestinal obstruction Explanation\n- Cauda equina syndrome is a neurological condition that happens when a bundle of nerves — called the cauda equina nerves — are compressed and injured. this compression can occur as a result of pregnancy.\n- It arises because pregnancy is associated with mechanical and positional stresses on the lower back. This frequently leads to lower back pain in women. For some, however, the strain will be so severe that it actually causes a disc to herniate.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 62-year-old male diabetic brought to the ED with complain of nausea, vomiting and confusion, his wife states that he has not passed urine for over 10 hours, and he is on multiple drugs for his medical condition. Which of the following drug is responsible for his condition?\n- **A)** Acetaminophen\n- **B)** Simvastatin   C Metformin\n- **C)** -  ACE inhibitor\n- **E)** Codeine Explanation\n- ACEI cause renal impairment. It requires monitoring during treatment.\n- ACEI\n- Indications\n- Hypertension\n- Symptomatic heart failure (adjunct)\n\n- Prophylaxis after myocardial infarction in patients with clinical evidence of heart failure (started at least 48 hours after infarction)\n- Prevention of cardiovascular events in patients with atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease or with diabetes mellitus and at least one additional risk factor for cardiovascular disease.\n- Contra-indications\n- Hereditary or idiopathic angioedema\n- history of angioedema associated with prior ACE inhibitor therapy\n- The combination of an ACE inhibitor with aliskiren is contra-indicated in patients with diabetes mellitus\n- Concomitant diuretics\n- diabetes (may lower blood glucose; increased risk of hyperkalaemia)\n- _use with care in patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\n- Side-effects\n- Alopecia; angina pectoris\n- angioedema (can be delayed; more common in black patients)\n- arrhythmias\n- dizziness\n- drowsiness\n- dry mouth\n- dyspnoea\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- electrolyte imbalance paraesthesia renal impairment nausea & vomiting rhinitis skin reactions Renal function and electrolytes should be checked before starting ACE inhibitors (or increasing the dose) and monitored during treatment (more frequently if side effects mentioned are present).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 25-year-old male is brought to the ED following RTA with severe respiratory distress. after initial assessment, you plan to intubate the patient. Which one of following is the common complication after tracheal intubation?   A\n- Hoarse voice\n- **B)** Wheezing\n- **C)** Type-2 respiratory failure\n- **D)** Low 02 Saturation\n- **E)** Inspiratory stridor\n- Explanation\n- Displaced vocal cords - are another complication of tracheal intubation, especially in difficult patients. The presence of a displaced vocal cord may not be apparent until extubation. The patient may have persistent hoarseness, stridor, or fail extubation completely due to airway obstruction. This occurs if the ETT lumen catches on an arytenoid and the tube is forced.\n- Consultation with otolaryngology should be sought immediately if this is identified.\n- Complications of tracheal intubation\n- The most common form of failed intubation is inadvertent esophageal intubation.\n- This may present in a delayed fashion if adequate pre-oxygenation is performed.\n- Auscultation of breath sounds is notoriously ineffective in definitively determining esophageal intubation due to the proximity of the stomach to the diaphragm. The obvious sign then becomes desaturation despite the external appearance of adequate ventilation. In this case, the endotracheal tube should be removed immediately, and mask ventilation resumed to provide oxygenation.\n\n- Bronchial intubation - Advancing the endotracheal tube (ETT) distal to the carina may lead to bronchial intubation. Usually, the right mainstem bronchus branches at a more acute angle than the left main stem bronchus thus leading to right mainstem bronchial intubation.\n- Auscultation of right-sided breath sounds and absent or decreased left-sided sounds can confirm this. It may also become evident when the post-intubation chest x-ray is examined.\n- The ETT may be pulled back carefully until bilateral breath sounds are auscultated.\n- Displaced-yocaLcordE- are another complication, especially in difficult patients. The presence of a displaced vocal cord may not be apparent until extubation. The patient may have persistent hoarseness, stridor, or fail extubation completely due to airway obstruction. This occurs if the ETT lumen catches on an arytenoid and the tube is forced.\n- Consultation with otolaryngology should be sought immediately if this is identified.\n- Other less common complications can arise, such as mediastinal intubation, tracheoesophageal fistula, and tracheal stenosis.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA two-year-old child weighing 15kg brought into the ED by his mother with complaint of vomiting and diarrhea. On examination, his eyes are sunken and skin pinch goes slowly.\n\nWhat is the maintenance fluid required in this patient?\n- **A)** 1250mI\n- **B)** 4000ml O\n- **C)** 1500ml\n- **D)** 2000ml O\n- **E)** 2500ml Explanation\n- Maintenance fluid chart Body weight 10kg 2nd 10kg 3rd 10kg Daily/per-day fluid requirements 100ml/kg 50mI/kg 20ml/kg Hourly fluid requirement 4ml/kg 2ml/kg Iml/kg\n- Patient weight is 15kg\n- Daily fluid requirements would be:\n\n- 1 10kg 100 = 1000\n- Further 5kg x 50 = 250\n- Total daily requirement 1250 ml/24hr\n- Per hourly rate would be?\n- 1 10kg x 4=40 nd\n- 2 5kgx2=10\n- Total hourly fluid requirement = 50ml/hr\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 48-year-old male presented to the ED with complaining of vertigo, nausea and vomiting. Which of the following features support central vertigo?\n- **A)** No skew deviation\n- **B)** Severe nausea and vomiting\n- **C)** Normal neurological signs D\n- Directional nystagmus\n- **E)** Unidirectional nystagmus Explanation\n\n- Directional nystagmus is the feature of central vertigo\n- Summary of clinical features of peripheral and central vertigo\n\nClinical features postural stability Hearing loss or tinnitus Other neurological symptoms Nystagmus Head impulse test Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre Alternate cover test Peripheral Can walk, although may be unstable and may not wish to mobilise. Possible with some causes (for example Meniere's disease, labyrinthitis). NO\n- Horizontal nystagmus with a torsional component that does not alter in direction when the gaze changes. Beats away from the affected side. Disappears with fixation of the gaze. Large amplitude nystagmus is usually only seen early in the course of Meniere's disease or vestibular neuronitis. May be positive with acute unilateral vestibular loss. In BPPV: latency of symptoms and nystagmus with fatiguability and habituation; severe vertigo. Normal Central Inability to stand up or walk even with the eyes open. Uncommon but may occur (for example stroke or intracranial tumour). Usually (for example cranial nerve dysfunction, visual disturbance, speech defects, dysarthria, weakness, sensory changes, memory loss, and gait ataxia). However, not all people with vertigo due to a stroke will have focal neurological signs.\n- Direction-changing nystagmus on lateral gaze (right beating on right gaze, left beating on left gaze).\n- Purely vertical or torsional. Not suppressed by visual fixation. Non-fatiguable.\n- Commonly large amplitude nystagmus. Negative, indicating a normal vestibulo-ocular reflex.\n- Abnormal response (for example vertical nystagmus without latency or fatiguability; direction not classical horizontal towards the downward ear). Slight vertical correction (up on one side, down on the other) suggestive of a central lesion such as stroke.  IO. A 37-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of breathlessness. His chest x-ray shows a left sided pneumothorax. The intrapleural distance at the level of hilum is 3cm.\n\nAs per BTS guideline what is the most appropriate management of this Patient?   A\n- Needle aspiration\n- **B)** Discharge with advice and OPD follow-up\n- **C)** Insert 24Fr surgical chest drain\n- **D)** Seldinger technique chest drain insertion\n- **E)** Administer high flow oxygen Explanation\n- Patient history suggests primary spontaneous pneumothorax as patient has no known comorbid and his PMH is unremarkable\n- Primary pneumothorax >2cm or breathless requires aspiration with 16- 180 cannula.\n- Primary_spontaneous pneumothorax\n- Pneumothorax size <2cm\n- discharge the Pt review in OPD in 2-4 weeks\n- Pneumothorax >2cm or breathless\n- Aspirate with 16-180 cannula Aspirate <2.51\n- successful pneumothorax <2cm now and breathing improved then consider discharge and follow in OPD 2-4 weeks\n\n- Not successful, insert Chest drain Size 8-14Fr and admit the patient. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	8	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
cd13a4ad-7664-45a0-ad28-bd4cc2aa5d87	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-11	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 11	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 11\n\n- **B)** Acute respiratory distress syndrome\n- **C)** Community acquired pneumonia\n- **D)** Pulmonary oedema\n- **E)** Pulmonary embolism Explanation\n- Posteroanterior chest x-ray showing severe, bilateral pulmonary interstitial infiltrates with pneumatoceles, commonly seen in PCP.\n- Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)\n- Pneumocystispneumonia (PCP) is an infection of the lung caused by the fungal organism Pneumocystisjirovecii(formerly known as Pneumocystis carinii). Typically, it causes clinical disease in severely immunocompromised patients, such as HIV-positive patients with CD4 cell counts <200 cells/microliter, hematopoietic cell transplant patients, solid-organ transplant patients, or patients on chronic immunosuppressive therapy.\n- Risk factor\n- HIV-positive patients with a CD4 cell count <200 cells/microliter\n- immunocompromised state\n- chronic corticosteroid therapy\n- A history of previous Pneumocystispneumonia\n\n- Clinical features\n- Fever\n- Dry cough Dyspnoea Fatigue\n- Normal chest examination\n- Weight loss\n- Tachypnea or respiratory distress\n- Investigations\n- CXR - bilateral symmetrical interstitial infiltrates. Reticular or granular opacities that are typically perihilar but may be diffuse. Less often they are asymmetric or progress to a reticular-alveolar pattern.\n- Arterial blood gas\n- PCR\n- Serum LDH level - elevated (>220 international units/L)\n- Induced sputum - positive for Pneumocystis\n- Consider HRCT\n- Consider pulmonary function test\n- Management\n- First line - Co-Trirnoxazole is a combination of two different antibiotics called sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim.\n- Consider corticosteroid in selected patients\n- Second line — pentamidine or atovaquone or clindamycin\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 54-year-old male presented to the ED with 6 weeks history of short of breath associated with dry cough ,fever and some loss of weight . You order covid PCR which is negative. His chest x-ray is given. What would be your step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** IV co-amoxiclav +clarithromycin   B\n- Furosemide   C\n- CO-Trimoxazole\n- **D)** Ostemsavir E\n- Amoxicillin Explanation\n- Patient clinical features (dry cough, fever and loss of weight) and his X- ray showing bilateral pulmonary interstitial infiltrates with pneumatoceles suggests PCP which treated with pharmacotherapy Co-Trimoxazole.\n- Pneumocystispneumonia-(PCP)\n- Pneumocystispneumonia (PCP) is an infection of the lung caused by the fungal organism Pneumocystisjirovecii(formerly known as Pneumocystis carinii). Typically, it causes clinical disease in severely\n\nimmunocompromised patients, such as HIV-positive patients with CD4 cell counts <200 cells/microliter, hematopoietic cell transplant patients, solid-organ transplant patients, or patients on chronic immunosuppressive therapy.\n- Risk factor\n- HIV-positive patients with a CD4 cell count <200 cells/microliter\n- immunocompromised state\n- chronic corticosteroid therapy\n- A history of previous Pneumocystispneumonia\n- Clinical features\n- Fever Dry cough [Dyspnoea\n- Fatigue Normal chest exarnination\n- Weight loss\n- Tachypnea or respiratory distress\n- Investigations\n- CXR - bilateral symmetrical interstitial infiltrates. Reticular or granular opacities that are typically perihilar but may be diffuse. Less often they are asymmetric or progress to a reticular-alveolar pattern.\n- Arterial blood gas\n- PCR\n- Serum LDH level - elevated (>220 international units/L)\n- Induced sputum - positive for Pneumocystis\n\n- Consider HRCT\n- Consider pulmonary function test\n- Management\n- is a combination of two different antibiotics called sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim.\n- Consider corticosteroid in selected patients\n- Second line — pentamidine or atovaquone or clindamycin\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 28-year-old male brought into the ED by paramedics with drowsiness. On examination, his RR is IO breath per minutes, HR 45 bpm and his pupils are abnormally small. What is most likely the cause of his condition?\n- **A)** Cannabis over use\n- **B)** Antidepressant toxicity\n- **C)** Alcohol overdose\n- **D)** Opioid overdose\n- **C)** E. MDMA Explanation\n- Patient clinical picture suggests Opioid toxicity.\n\nOpioid overdose (narcotic analgesics)\n- Clinical features of opioid overdose\n- Respiratory depression\n- Pinpoint pupil\n- Coma\n- Antidote\n- Naloxone hydrochloride\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma. When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion adjusted according to vital signs.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 23-year-old young male presented to the ED with complaint of chest pain associated with short of breath. Initial management started and C TPA was done which showed Pulmonary emboli. Patient is fit with no significant bleeding risk. What is next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Dexamethasone\n- **C)** Unfractioned Heparin\n- **D)** Refer to cardiopulmonary center\n- **E)** Alteplase Explanation\n- If PE is identified by CTPA, v/Q SPECT or v/Q planar scan:\n- offer or continue anticoagulation treatment (DOAC -apixaban or rivaroxaban, if not suitable then LMWH) at least 3 months to people with confirmed proximal DVT or PE.\n- if anticoagulation treatment is contraindicated, consider a mechanical intervention (Inferior vena caval filters, Elastic graduated compression stockings, Percutaneous mechanical thrombectomy).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA IO-Year-old child brought to the ED with complain of limping without any significant history of trauma and fall. His X-ray is given What sign gives diagnostic clue of Slipped capital femoral epiphysis?\n- **A)** Metaphyseal blanch sign   B\n- Trethowan sign\n- **C)** History gives diagnostic clue\n- **D)** Klein line\n- **E)** Sham's Sign EXPIanation\n- The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper femoral epiphysis. In this sign, the line of Klein passes above the femoral head instead of intersecting the femoral epiphysis as in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 45-year-old male with a history of tooth extraction presented to the ED with complaint of inability to swallow, drooling and speech problems. What's the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Infective endocarditis\n- **B)** Sublingual haematoma\n- **C)** Peritonsillar abscess\n- **D)** Mandibular abscess E' Ludwig angina Explanation\n- a serious, potentially life-threatening infection of the tissues of the floor of the mouth.\n- Symptoms include:\n\n- swelling\n- pain on raising of the tongue\n- swelling of the neck and the tissues of the submandibular and sublingual spaces.\n- Malaise, fever, dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), trismus and, in severe cases, stridor or difficulty breathing can occur.\n- Dental abscess\n- A dental abscess is a localized collection of pus in the teeth, supporting structures of the teeth (periodontal ligament, alveolar bone), or gums\n- The two main types of dental abscess are:\n- known as acute apical abscess, dentoalveolar abscess, or periradicular abscess) — caused by infection of the root canal of the tooth.\n- It is usually secondary to dental caries (tooth decay)\n- originates in deep periodontal pockets between the tooth and the gum and occurs when there is occlusion of drainage of the periodontal pocket.\n- Risk factors\n- Poor oral hygiene.\n- Dental caries.\n- Periodontal disease.\n- Dental trauma.\n- Partially erupted or impacted tooth.\n- Alcohol or drug misuse.\n\n- Malnutrition.\n- Immunocompromise (may also be seen at extremes of age).\n- History of radiotherapy.\n- Medications causing dry mouth (for example antihistamines, anticholinergics, antidepressants).\n- Clinical features\n- Pain — this is usually of sudden onset, intense and throbbing in nature\n- Unpleasant taste in the mouth.\n- Fever and malaise.\n- Trismus (inability to open the mouth) or dysphagia\n- Listlessness, lethargy\n- Facial swelling, with or without cellulitis.\n- Regional lymphadenopathy Oral_cayity exarnination\n- Observation - may reveal:\n- Facial swelling, with or without cellulitis.\n- Regional lymphadenopathy.\n- Altered tooth appearance: the affected tooth may be elevated, broken, or show signs of decay.\n- Cum swelling.\n- Purulent drainage: may be intra- or extra-oral.\n- Palpation - may reveal:\n- Tooth: increased mobility and tenderness.\n- Gum: tenderness, warmth, and a purulent exudate.\n- Management\n\n- Admit a person to hospital as an emergency if they have a dental abscess and. Signs of airway compromise (for example difficulty breathing or speaking, unable to swallow their own saliva, drooling, uvular deviation, trismus, or unable to push their tongue forward out of their mouth).\n- Are unwell with a high temperature and cardio-respiratory compromise (rapid pulse rate or low blood pressure, high respiratory rate).\n- A rapidly progressing infection.\n- Significant mandibular, submandibular, or infraorbital swelling (or difficulty opening the eye).\n- 'Floor of mouth' swelling.\n- A spreading facial infection or orbital cellulitis.\n- Neurological signs (for example decreased level of consciousness, headache, eye signs [such as diplopia, papilloedema, pupil dilation, proptosis]).\n- Dehydration.\n- Seek immediate assessment and treatment from dentist\n- Definitive treatment can only be given by a dentist and medication will not eliminate the source of infection.\n- Complications\n- Loss of the affected tooth.\n- Airway obstruction\n- A fistula or sinus tract may develop that can discharge intraorally or into the overlying skin Spread of infection(more common in immunocompromised and elderly people), for example:Maxillary sinusitis, Cellulitis/periorbital cellulitis,Osteomyelitis, Retropharyngeal abscess, Cavernous sinus thrombosis, Encephalitis and meningitis.\n\n- Ludwig's angina — a serious, potentially life-threatening infection of the tissues of the floor of the mouth. Symptoms include:\n- swelling\n- pain on raising of the tongue\n- swelling of the neck and the tissues of the submandibular and sublingual spaces.\n- Malaise, fever, dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), trismus and, in severe cases, stridor or difficulty breathing can occur.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 28-years-old male with sucking wound on his chest. You have applied occlusive dressing and started fluid resuscitation and stabilized the patient. In a short period of time patient condition has been deteriorated Which of the following features will you find?\n- **A)** Dull on percussion\n- **B)** Wide mediastinum\n- **C)** Muffled heart sounds\n- **D)** Trachea deviated away from the affected side\n- **E)** Hypertension Explanation\n\n_ Patient has developed tension pneumothorax as the wound is completely close i-e dressing has been applied all sides. Open Pneumothorax\n- Think 'hole in the chest'. It is also known as a communicating pneumothorax or sucking chest wound.\n- Prompt clinical inspection front and back is required; a small sucking chest wound is usually audible\n- Initial management (pre-hospital)\n- Close the defect (hole) with any sterile occlusive dressing e.g. plastic wrap, sheet of jolnet or Ashermann device\n- Tape dressing 3 sides to provide flutter valve effect.\n- Don't completely occlude/close the wound (risk of tension pneumothorax) until chest tube is in place\n- Further management\n- Place Chest tube remote from open wound (means not from the site of wound) Definitive treatment is surgical repair (wound closure)\n- Tension pneumothorax clinical features\n- Tachypnea, Tachycardia, hypotension, distended neck vein\n- Contralateral tracheal deviation\n- Unilateral hyper-resonant on percussion and absence of breath sound Cyanosis (late manifestation)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 62-year-old male brought to the ED with cardiac arrest. You started early CPR but no improvement. Now you want to declare death in this patient. How\n\nwould you confirm death in this patient?\n- **A)** Absence of oculo-vestibular reflexes\n- **B)** No peripheral pulse\n- **C)** Observe patient for 5 minutes\n- **D)** No breathing\n- **E)** No Gag reflex Explanation\n- The individual should be observed by the person responsible for confirming death for a minimum of five minutes to establish that irreversible cardiorespiratory arrest has occurred.\n- Confirmation-and-Piagnosis-of-peath\n\nDiagnosing and confirming death after cardiorespiratory arrest The individual should be observed by the person responsible for confirming death for a minimum of five minutes to establish that irreversible cardiorespiratory arrest has occurred.\n- The absence of mechanical cardiac function is normally confirmed using a combination of the following:\n- Absence Of a central pulse on palpation\n- Absence of heart sounds on auscultation\n- In the hospital setting by one or more of the following: Asystole on a continuous ECG display\n- Absence of pulsatile flow using direct intra-arterial pressure monitoring\n- Absence of contractile activity using echocardiography Any spontaneous retum of cardiac or respiratory activity during this period of observation should prompt a further five minutes observation from the next point of cardiorespiratory arrest.\n- After five minutes of continued cardiorespiratory arrest the absence of the pupillary responses to light, of the corneal reflexes, and of any motor response to Supra-orbital pressure should be The diagnosis of death following irreversible cessation Of brain-stem function\n- Absence of brain-stem reflexes The pupils are fixed and do not respond to sharp changes in the intensity of incident light.\n- There is no comeal reflex — care should be taken to avoid damage to the comea.\n- The oculo-vestibular reflexes are absent No motor responses within the cranial nerve distribution can be elicited by adequate stimulation of any somatic area\n- There is no cough reflex response to bronchial stimulation by a suction catheter placed down the trachea to the carina. or gag response to stimulation of the posterior pharynx with a spatula The process for testing the respiratory response to hypercarbia (apnoea test) should be the last brain- stem reflex to be tested and should not be performed if any of the preceding tests confirm the presence of brain-stem reflexes\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 50-year-old male with history of RTA brought to the ED with shock. CVP line was placed, and resuscitation has been commenced. Initially, patient was stabilized, however, after IO minutes patient condition has been deteriorated. His X-ray is given. What would be the cause of his deterioration?   A\n- Nerve injury\n- **B)** Infection\n- **C)** Catheter malposition\n- **D)** Arrhythmia   E\n- Internal bleeding Explanation\n- His X-ray suggest hemothorax as increased density of the right hemithorax compared to the left. Therefore, the cause of patient deterioration is internal bleeding.\n- Hemothorax _A hemothorax is a type of pleural effusion in which blood (<1500 mL) accumulates in the pleural cavity.> <1500ml) accumulates in the pleural cavity\n- Causes\n- laceration of the lung, great vessels, an intercostal vessel, or an internal mammary artery from penetrating or blunt trauma.\n- Thoracic spine fractures may also be associated with a hemothorax\n- Clinical features\n- Respiratory distress\n- Tachypnoea\n- dullness to percussion is heard on the affected side\n\n- decreased or absent breath sounds on affected side\n- hypotension\n- Investigations\n- CXR - A small at-nount of blood will be identified as a homogeneous opacity on the affected side.\n- Management\n- Bleeding is usually self-limited and does not require operative intervention.\n- An acute hemothorax that is large enough to appear on a chest x-ray may be treated with a 28-32 French chest tube.\n- The chest tube evacuates blood, reduces the risk of a clotted hemothorax, and, allows for continuous monitoring of blood loss. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nAn II-year-old boy brought to the ED with complaint of limping and groin pain aggravated with walking. His X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Perthes disease\n- **B)** Septic arthritis\n- **C)** Slipped upper femoral epiphysis\n- **D)** Osteomyelitis\n- **E)** Juvenile idiopathic arthritis Explanation His X-ray shows displacement of femoral epiphysis from metaphysis on\n- Slipped_upper femoral epiphysis:\n- It's a displacement of femoral epiphysis from metaphysis\n- More common in children who are overweight and common in 10-15 years of age\n- It may associated with endocrine pathology\n- Clinical features:\n- Pt will present with severe pain with externally rotated leg.\n- There maybe shortening of the affected limb\n- Investigation: AP or frog-leg lateral view x-ray will reveal displacement of femoral head.\n- The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper femoral epiphysis. In this sign, the line of Klein passes above the femoral head instead of intersecting the fernoral epiphysis as in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.\n- Management\n- Urgent evaluation and management is crucial to avoid further displacement and development of avascular necrosis. Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	9	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e461372d-e952-4d43-82a9-6b119d40de30	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-12	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 12	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 12\n\nWhat is the most common tachycardia in children?\n- **A)** Ventricular fibrillation\n- **B)** Ventricular tachycardia\n- **C)** Atrial fibrillation\n- **D)** Sinus tachycardia\n- **E)** Supraventricular tachycardia Explanation\n- SVT is the most common symptomatic dysrhythmia in infants in children. Children with congenital heart disease are it increased risk for SVT.\n- Sinus tachycardia can be a normal increase in the heart rate if it is in response to exercise or physical or emotional stress. It's common in children, and usually no treatment is needed. in children is supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). It presents in two forms: paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT) and paroxysmal atrial tachycardia (PAT).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of pain in his right hand. On examination, there was swelling and tenderness on his finger. On further assessment, it has been discovered that he was bitten by his friend few days back.\n\nWhat is the appropriate management in this patient?\n- **A)** Dressing and discharge\n- **B)** Refer to surgery\n- **C)** Commence CO-Amoxiclav\n- **D)** Analgesia and drainage\n- **E)** X-ray and refer to Ortho Explanation\n- If the wound is infected NICE recommendation:\n- Send pus or a deep wound swab for culture, before cleaning the wound. State on the form that the swab is from an infected human bite.\n- Treat empirically with oral antibiotics, particularly if there are symptoms or signs of infection, including increased pain, inflammation, fever, discharge, or an unpleasant smell emanating from the wound.\n- Admit anyone who has a severe infection or who is systemically unwell as intravenous antibiotics may be required\n- Antibiotic choice - co-amoxiclav for 5 days. For adults who are allergic to penicillin, prescribe metronidazole plus doxycycline for 5 days.\n- Human and animal bites\n- Document how and when the bite occurred:\n- Who was bitten, and by whom.\n- Whether the skin was broken or blood was involved.\n\n- The nature of the bite (i.e. occlusal or clenched fist).\n- Examine the bite using gloves:\n- The location of the wound. Photographs or diagrams may be useful.\n- The size and depth of the injury.\n- The type of wound (for example laceration, puncture, abrasion, crush, haematoma, avulsion, amputation).\n- The degree of crush injury, devitalized tissue, nerve or tendon damage, and involvement of muscle, bones, joints, or blood vessels\n- assess the risk of tetanus and blood born viruses.\n- Management\n- Initial step\n- If possible remove any foreign bodies\n- Encourage the wound to bleed (if it has just occurred), unless it is already bleeding freely.\n- Irrigate thoroughly with warm, running water.\n- Consider the need for debridement (for example if the wound is dirty or there is non-viable tissue)\n- Advise analgesia (paracetamol or ibuprofen) h Iwi h f rm I I\n- Bite wounds over 24 hours old.\n- Infected bite wounds.\n- Deep puncture wounds.\n- Crush injuries.\n- Heavy contamination.\n- Uncertain adequacy of debridement.\n- Bites to the limbs, hands, and feet\n\nAntibiotic prophylaxis for an uninfected bite Type of Human Cat bite Dog or other traditional pet bite Bite has not broken the skin Do not offer antibiotics Do not offer antibiotics Do not offer antibiotics Bite has broken the skin but not drawn blood Consider antibiotics if it is in a high-risk area Or person at high risk Consider antibiotics if the wound could be deep Do not offer antibiotics Bite has broken the skin and drawn blood Offer antibiotics Offer antibiotics Offer antibiotics if it has caused considerable, deep tissue damage or is visibly contaminated (for example, with dirt or a tooth) Consider antibiotics if it is in a high-risk area or person at high risk High-risk areas include the hands, feet. face, genitals, skin overlying cartilaginous structures Or an area Of poor circulation People at high risk include those at risk Of a serious wound infection because of a co-morbidity (such as diabetes, immunosuppression, asplenia or decompensated liver disease)\n- If the wound is infected:\n- Send pus or a deep wound swab for culture, before cleaning the wound. State on the form that the swab is from an infected human bite.\n- Treat empirically with oral antibiotics, particularly if there are symptoms or signs of infection, including increased pain, inflammation, fever, discharge, or an unpleasant smell emanating from the wound.\n- Admit anyone who has a severe infection or who is systemically unwell as intravenous antibiotics may be required.\n\nAntibiotic choices for prophylaxis and treatment ISt choice Oral If allergic to penicillin ISt choice IV (severe infection) If allergic to penicillin Co-amoxiclav Doxycycline with Metronidazole Co-amoxiclav Cefuroxime/Ceftriaxone with Metronidazole children and young people under 18 years ISt choice Oral If allergic to penicillin ISt choice IV (severe infection) If allergic to penicillin\n- Key_points Co-amoxiclav Co-trimoxazole or Doxycycline with Metronidazole (if age 12 to 17 year) Co-amoxiclav Cefuroxime/Ceftriaxone with Metronidazole\n- Do not offer antibiotic prophylaxis to people with a human bite that has not broken the skin.\n- Offer antibiotic prophylaxis to people with a human bite that has broken the skin and drawn blood.\n- Consider antibiotic prophylaxis for people with a human bite that has broken the skin but not drawn blood if it involves a high-risk area such as the hands, feet, face, genitals, skin overlying cartilaginous structures, or an area of poor circulation.\n\n- Please note - in the exam picture can be different.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 60-year-old male brought to the ED with complaint of urinary retention. He is known case of prostatic carcinoma. Which of the following is the early symptom of prostatic carcinoma? Frequent urination\n- **B)** Pain in the back\n- **C)** Cauda equina syndrome\n- **D)** Anemia\n- **E)** Urinary retention Explanation\n- Early symptoms may include frequent urination, nocturia(increased urination at night), difficulty starting and maintaining a steady stream of urine, haematuria.\n- Clinical features of prostate cancer\n- Lower back or bone pain.\n- Erectile dysfunction.\n- Visible haematuria.\n- Anorexia/weight loss.\n\n- Lower urinary tract symptoms (LOTS), such as nocturia, frequency, urgency, hesitancy, retention, terminal dribbling, and/or overactive bladder — these symptoms are common in older men and early prostate cancer will not usually produce these symptoms. However, locally advanced prostate cancer may cause obstructive LUTS.\n- Lower back pain, bone pain, weight loss (possible symptoms Of advanced prostate cancer).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 35-year-old male patient is being treated in your ED for multiple lacerations sustained. your junior colleague has infiltrated multiple wounds with 1% lidocaine. After a short period of time, patient started to deteriorate. You started appropriate initial management and spontaneous circulation achieved. With regard to treatment Of local anesthetic toxicity, what complication patient may develop some days after?\n- **A)** Hyperkalemia\n- **B)** Infection\n- **C)** Acute pancreatitis\n- **D)** Diabetic mellitus\n- **E)** Hyperthermia\n- Intravenous lipid emulsion (ILE) may cause significant lipemia.\n- Acute pancreatitis may occur from hypertriglyceridemia and severe lipemia.\n\n- Additional adverse effects from ILE include allergic reaction or hypersensitivity, fat overload syndrome, and lung injury.\n- Management of Severe Local Anaesthetic Toxicity_ 1 Recognition 2 Immediate management 3 Treatment 4 Follow-up\n- Signs Of severe toxicitv\n- .\n- Sudden alteration in mental status, severe agitation or loss of consciousness, With or Without tonic-clonic convulsions Cardiovascular collapse: sinus bradycardia, conduction blocks, asystole and ventricular tachyarrhythmias may all occur Local anaesthetic (LA) toxicity may occur some time after an initial injection\n- Stop iniecting the LA\n- Call for help Maintain the airway and, if necessary, secure it with a tracheal tube Give 100% oxygen and ensure adequate lung ventilation (hyperventilation may help by increasing plasma pH in the presence of metabolic acidosis) Confirm or establish intravenous access Control seizures: give a benzodiazepine, thiopental or propofol in small incremental doses Assess cardiovascular status throughout\n- Consider drawing blood for analysis, but do not delay definitive treatment to do this\n- In circulatorv arrest\n- Start cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) using standard protocols\n- Manage arrhythmias using the same protocols, recognising that arrhythmias may be very refractory to treatment\n- Consider the use of cardiopulmonary bypass if available dose regime mentioned below) Continue CPR throughout treatment with lipid emulsion Recovery from LA-induced cardiac arrest may take >1 h Propofol is not a suitable substitute for lipid emulsion Without circulatory arrest - Use conventional therapies to treat hypotension, bradycardia,\n- tachyarrhythmia Consider intravenous lipid emulsion Arrange safe transfer to a clinical area with appropriate equipment and suitable staff until sustained recovery is achieved\n- Exclude pancreatitis by regular clinical review, including daily amylase or lipase assays for two days Report cases as follows: in the United Kingdom to the National Patient Safety Agency 1\n\nImmediately After 5 min Give an initial intravenous bolus injection of lipid emulsion 1.5 ml/kg over 1 min And Start an intravenous infusion of lipid emulsion at 15 ml/kg/hour Give a maximum of two repeat boluses (same dose) if: Cardiovascular stability has not been restored or An adequate circulation deteriorates Leave 5 min between boluses, a maximum of three boluses can be given (including the initial bolus). And Continue infusion at same rate but: Double the rate to 30 ml/kg/hour at anv time after 5 min. if: Cardiovascular stability has not been restored or An adequate circulation deteriorates, Continue infusion until stable and adequate circulation restored or maximum dose of lipid emulsion given\n- not exceed a rnaxirnum cumulative dose Of 12 ml.kg—l\n- Cornpljcations\n- Intravenous lipid emulsion (ILE) may cause significant lipemia.\n- Acute pancreatitis may occur from hypertriglyceridemia and severe lipemia. Additional adverse effects from ILE include allergic reaction or hypersensitivity, fat overload syndrome, and lung injury.\n- Key-points\n- Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) classically presents with neurological and cardiac symptoms.\n- Bupivacaine is often associated with LAST; however, other local anesthetics may also lead to toxicity.\n\n- Local anesthetic cardiotoxic effects occur through its sodium channel blockade in the cardiac myocytes.\n- Local anesthetic neurological effects occur through its sodium channel blockade in the brain. Intravenous lipid emulsion (ILE) therapy (also known as lipid resuscitation therapy, LRT) is the current recommended treatment for local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 25-year-old male presented to the ED after taking intentional overdose of twenty 500mg paracetamol tablets 6 hours ago. His weight is 70kg His PMH and examination is unremarkable. You took initial samples which revealed serum paracetamol level 80mg/L. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** 50g single dose of activated charcoal\n- **B)** No treatment and discharge O\n- **C)** Await results of VBG, INR, urea and electrolytes, LFT, FBC\n- **D)** Start iv N-Acetylcysteine 150mg/kg over lhour\n- **E)** IV N- Acetylcysteine 100mg/kg over 1 hour Explanation\n- To follow nomogram, paracetamol plasma concentration with a reference line (treatment line) is 70mg/L at 6 hours. However, the patient paracetamol plasma concentration above the treatment line which is 80mg/L. Therefore, NAC should be commenced.\n\n110 100 6 e 10 12 14 16 18 20 22 24 T irne (hours) patients Whose plasma.paracetamol concentrations are on or the treatment tine should be treated With by infusion. The accuracy after IS hours is uncertain. but a plasma-paracetamol cc"teentration on the One ca ryi g a risk of damge.\n- To calculate the ingestion dose: 20 x  142mg/kg\n- NAC should not be commenced based on ingestion dose in this patient as the ingestion dose is below 150mg/kg.\n- Give acetylcysteine if:\n- Serum paracetamol concentration is detectable (>10mg/L) in 18- 24hrs or >24hrs\n- OR\n- ALT is above the upper limit of normal\n- OR\n- INR >1.3 (in the absence of another cause, e.g., warfarin).\n- OR\n\n- have ingested 150 mg/kg or more of paracetamol\n- Acetylcysteine dose and administration\n- For the standard 21-hour regimen, acetylcysteine is given in a total dose that is divided into 3 consecutive intravenous infusions over a total of 21 hours.\n- The requisite dose of acetylcysteine is added to glucose5% or sodium chloride 0.9% Intravenous Infusion. Infusion ISt 2 nd NAC dose 150 mg/kg 50 mg/kg 100 mg/kg Infusion fluid 200 mi 500 ml 1000 ml Duration lh 1 4h 16h\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 76-year man brought by ambulance after a fall at a day care center. His ankle is clearly causing him significant pain however, he does not want anyone to look at it and says that he wants to go home without treatment. Which of the following is most appropriate as a first step?\n- **A)** Tell the patient that he must wait for x-rays\n- **B)** Sedate the patient to facilitate assessment and treatment\n- **C)** Detain the patient under section 5.2 of the Mental Health Act\n- **D)** Discharge without checking capacity\n- **E)** Perform a capacity assessment Explanation\n- Perform mental capacity assessment, if he has capacity he shouldn't not be treated without consent. The Mental capacity_Act_(MCA)\n- The Mental Capacity Act (MCA) is designed to protect and empower people who may lack the mental capacity to make their own decisions about their care and treatment. It applies to people aged 16 and over.\n- Examples of people who may lack capacity include those with:\n- dementia\n- a severe learning disability\n- a brain injury\n- a mental health illness\n- a stroke\n- unconsciousness caused by an anaesthetic or sudden accident\n- however, just because a person has one of these health conditions does not necessarily mean they lack the capacity to make a specific decision The MCA says;\n\n- assume a person has the capacity to make a decision themselves, unless it's proved otherwise\n- wherever possible, help people to make their own decisions\n- do not treat a person as lacking the capacity to make a decision just because they make an unwise decision\n- if you make a decision for someone who does not have capacity, it must be in their best interests\n- treatrnent and care provided to someone who lacks capacity should be the least restrictive of their basic rights and freedoms The MCA says a person is unable to make a decision if they cannot:\n- understand the information relevant to the decision\n- retain that information\n- use or weigh up that information as part of the process of making the decision\n- to communicate his decision (whether by talking, using sign language or another means). Key_points:\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest.\n- If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then he shouldn't be treated without consent\n- Case example-I: A 72-year-female brought to the ED in ALOC due to stoke, you want to thrombolysis the patient. In this situation patient should be treated in her best interest as she can't make decision/ sign the consent.\n\nCase-example.:2-A 76-year man brought by ambulance after a fall at a day care center. His ankle is clearly causing him significant pain however, he does not want anyone to look at it and says that he wants to go home without treatment. Perform mental capacity assessment, if he has capacity he shouldn't not be treated without consent.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 50-year-old male with no known comorbid presented to the ED with complaint of palpitations and recurrent attacks of collapse. His ECG is given What is the most likely diagnosis? A Mobitz type 2\n- **B)** Sinus tachycardia   C\n- C0mplete heart block\n- **E)** Mobitz type 1 Explanation\n- ECG suggests complete dissociation between atria and ventricle (P wave and QRS complex).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 50-year-old male patient working in hot sunny weather brought to the ED by his friend with rapid breathing. His initial observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 120bpm T 420C GCS  What is your initial step of management?\n- **A)** Furosemide\n- **B)** Immersion in ice water and cooling\n- **C)** Diazepam\n- **D)** Dantrolene\n- **E)** Fan and exposure to cold weather Explanation\n- CQQlingßeghniques; External; Spraying the patient with tepid water and using a fan is the most practical method.\n- Immersing the patient's limbs or whole body in cool water is described but impractical. eg: not appropriate for very sick patient.\n\n- placing of icepacks in the groin and axillae.\n- Internal: the use of cooled peripheral intravenous fluids.\n- Cold fluid peritoneal and gastric lavage and cardiopulmonary bypass have also been described, but are not usually necessary. Role-of-benzodiazepines;they are used to treat seizure and also to reduce shivering, rnake cooling techniques more tolerable.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 55-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of fever associated with cough and mild respiratory distress you suspect community acquired pneumonia. Which of the following score is used to predict the mortality in community acquired pneumonia?\n- **A)** Cantor criteria\n- **B)** Curb-60 c. cop Score\n- **D)** Curb-6S\n- **E)** NO scoring in CAP\n- CURB65 scoring_for CAP in hospital setting\n- CRB-65 scoring_for CAP in community setting\n\n10 / 10 IO. A 22-year-old young male with history of Upper respiratory tract infection presented to the ED with complaint of fever, sharp pleuritic chest pain and palpitations. Severity of pain increases with lying fiat and pain decreases when he sits upright. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Rheumatic fever\n- **B)** Myocardial infarction\n- **C)** Acute pericarditis\n- **D)** Myocarditis\n- **E)** Pneumonia Explanation\n- Pericarditis is most commonly idiopathic however it can be caused virus, bacteria, post-MI, trauma, SLE and neoplasia.\n- The clinical presentation is usually one of acute onset of retrosternal chest pain which is pleuritic in nature and which is worse on coughing and lying flat and eased by sitting up and leaning forward. A characteristic feature of the pain which is specific for pericarditis is radiation to the trapezius ridge\n\n- A-diagnosis-of-acute- of-4-of-the-fo"owingsriteria-arenet;\n- Characteristic chest pain (retrosternal, pleuritic)\n- Pericardial friction rub\n- Suggestive ECC changes (diffuse ST elevation, typically concave up and depression of the PR segment\n- New or worsening pericardial effusion.\n- Additional features: fever and raised CRP &ESR previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	10	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
95a20dec-fd98-4bca-8998-23f2e9bd8893	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-13	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 13	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 13\n\n- **B)** Send baby to NICU\n- **C)** Urine for microscopy\n- **D)** Amount of sample should be more than Icc\n- **E)** From both sides of hand to do culture Explanation\n- The general rule of sending two to three blood culture sets from different sites in a period of 24 hours\n- Aim to collect 1 mL of blood: ; the accuracy of the result correlates with the volume of blood collected. Invert bottles gently several times to prevent clotting.\n- Investigations before start of antibiotics in late onset neonatal sepsis st\n- Perform-blood-culturebeforel dose of antibiotic\n- Measure-g:.r.eagtiy.e-protein when starting antibiotic treatment and also during antibiotic treatment, 18 to 24 hours after presentation\n- If it safe then perform LP to obtain CSF before and during antibiotic treatment when: there is a strong clinical suspicion of early-onset neonatal infection or sign of meningitis\n- Note: don't perform urine or skin swab microscopy or culture routinely in late-onset of neonatal sepsis.\n\n- Neonatal Blood Culture Guideline\n- Key_points\n- Blood culture remains the 'gold standard' for the detection of microbial pathogens. Optimal recovery of pathogens depends on culturing an adequate volume of blood and using the right technique\n- Aim to collect 1 mL of blood: ; the accuracy of the result correlates with the volume of blood collected. Invert bottles gently several times to prevent clotting.\n- The general rule of sending two to three blood culture sets from different sites in a period of 24 hours\n- Current recommendation with regard to timing and interval include obtaining two sets within minutes apart from two distinct sites at the onset of the 24 hour period with two subsequent sets being taking at different time intervals over the 24 hour period if the clinical condition deteriorates.\n- Use aseptic technique including:\n- Skin preparation\n- Hand hygiene\n- Using sterile gloves by the performer Wiping blood culture bottle stopper with the alcohol wipe\n- Using sterile gloves by the helper while injecting blood into the culture bottle\n- Always collect blood culture from the hub of a newly inserted cannula\n- While collecting blood for multiple blood tests, always collect blood culture first in a separate syringe\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 25-year-old male with no known PMH or drug history presents to the ED with history of recurrent priapism usually resolve spontaneously but this time acute attack took longer time. Local anesthetic lidocaine given. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Aspirate blood with needle\n- **B)** Ice compression\n- **C)** C. Corpora cavernosa injection with phenylephrine\n- **D)** Irrigation therapy only\n- **E)** Physical therapy Explanation\n- Each acute episode of recurrent priapism manages as ischaemic priapism\n- After local anesthesia Insert wide bore butterfly through the glans into the corpora cavernosa and aspirate cavernosal blood until bright red arterial blood is obtained.\n- Ischemic priapism management algorithm\n\n- Initial conservative measures > Local anaesthesia of the penis Insert wide bore butterfly through the glans into the corpora cavernosa Aspiration cavernosal blood until bright red arterial blood is obtained\n- Cavernosal irrigation Irrigate with 0.9% w/v saline solution\n- Intracavernosal therapy inject intracavernosal adrenoceptor agonist Current first-line therapy is phenylephrine with aliquots of 200 pg being injected every 3-5 minutes until detumescence is achieved (Maximum dose of phenylephrine is Img within I hour) Surgical therapy Surgical shunting Consider primary penile prosthesis implantation if priapism has been present for more than 36 hours\n- Stuttering_(Recurrent or Intermittent) priapism: management algorithm\n\n- Manage each acute episode similar to that for ischaemic priapism\n- Prophylaxis/prevention of future episode Hormonal therapies (mainly gonadotropin receptor hormone agonists or antagonists) and/or antiandrogens Initiate treatment with phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors (PDE51s) only when the penis is in its flaccid state Use digoxin, a-adrenergic agonists, baclofen, gabapentin or terbutaline only in patients with very frequent and uncontrolled relapses. Use intracavernous self-injections at home of sympathomimetic drugs for the treatment of acute episodes on an interim basis until ischaemic priapism has been alleviated.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 2-year-old girl brought to the ED with complaint of lethargy, vomiting, and intermittent crying episodes since past 12 hours. During this painful crying episodes, she draws her legs on her abdomen, she had passed currant jelly Stool.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Peptic ulcer disease\n- **B)** Acute appendicitis   C\n- Intussusception\n- **D)** Acute gastroenteritis   E\n- Pyloric stenosis Explanation Intussusceptiom(Babies and Toddlers)\n- classic triad of symptoms: episodic screaming with drawing up their legs,\n- vomiting (can be bilious)\n- redcurrant jelly stools\n- it affects those of 3-18 months after a viral illness\n- Redcurrant jelly stools, if present, don't always look like the red\n- mucousy stuff (think bloody diarrhoea explosion) Usually, mother noticed blood in nappies\n- Investigation: AXR, US, contrast enema (contraindicated in pt with free intra-abdominal air) or CT\n- Management: IV fluids, analgesia, NG tube on free drainage and urgent surgical referral\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation on a patient who require neuromuscular blocking agent. What is the contraindication of Suxamethonium?\n- **A)** Hyperkalemia\n- **B)** Renal impairment\n- **C)** Hypotension\n- **D)** Hypokalemia\n- **E)** Hyponatremia Explanation Contra-indications For suxamethonium chloride\n- Hyperkalaemia; low plasma-cholinesterase activity (including severe liver disease) major trauma\n- neurological disease involving acute wasting of major muscle\n- personal or family history of congenital myotonic disease\n- personal or family history of malignant hyperthermia\n- prolonged immobilisation (risk of hyperkalaemia)\n- severe burns; skeletal muscle myopathies (e.g. Duchenne muscular\n- dyst rophy)\n\n- Suxamethonium chloride:\n- it's a depolarising neuromuscular blocking agent, commonly use in tracheal intubation.\n- Dose: 1.2-2mg/kg\n- Prolong paralysis may occurs in Pt with low or atypical cholinesterase\n- CI: hyperkalaemia, ECG changes due to hyperkalaemia or\n- Risk of developing hyperkalaemia (burn, trauma, spinal cord injury, muscle dystrophy, infection, desquamating skin condition, crush injuries).\n- Adverse effects: bradycardia ,muscle pain , fasciculation, anaphylaxis, malignant hyperthermia, trismus/masseter.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nYou are required to perform procedural sedation on a patient who require neuromuscular blocking agent suxamethonium. Suxamethonium causes prolong paralysis by what mechanism?   A\n- Cholinesterase inhibitor\n- **B)** Ca+ channel blocker\n- **C)** Acting on NMDA receptors\n- **D)** Na+ channel blocker\n- **E)** Induce the activity of cholinesterase\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Suxamethonium is metabolised by an enzyme in the blood called plasma cholinesterase. Metabolism is normally complete within 5-10 minutes. Suxamethonium action prolong with cholinesterase inhibitors or those patients who lack this enzyme (cholinesterase) or have an altered enzyme that does not metabolise the suxamethonium as rapidly. These patients may remain paralysed for many hours after a standard dose of suxamethonium, and must be kept anaesthetised and ventilated until the suxamethonium has been eliminated by other, slower methods.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nAn 18-year-old Somalian girl presented to the ED with complaint of dysuria and feelings of urinary retention. On examination, there is visible blood on undergarments, and she is not able to do eye contact. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Cervical Shock\n- **B)** Genital traurna\n- **C)** C. Sexual abuse\n- **D)** Urinary tract infection\n- **E)** Female genital mutilation\n\nExplanation Indicators that a girl may have already experienced FGM include:\n- A girl has problems walking/standing/sitting A girl doesn't take part in Physical Education or swimming\n- She spends a long time in the bathroom/toilet She has bladder or menstrual problems She has prolonged or repeated absences from school\n-\n- She has a reluctance to undergo pelvic medical examinations\n- She is asking for help but giving a lack of explicit information Complication of FCM Short-term complication: Haemorrhage\n- Urinary retention\n- Genital swelling\n- I nfection\n- Long-term complication Genital scarring\n- Recurrent urinary infection\n- Dyspareunia Genital infection and pelvic inflammatory disease\n-\n- HIV, HepB I nfertility\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 57-year-old woman with breast cancer has taken an overdose of slow- release morphine. She says she doesn't need treatment and wants to leave the hospital and die at home. Her family, however, wanted her to be treated.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate approach?\n- **A)** If she has capacity then she cannot be treated without consent\n- **B)** She is clearly unwell and can be treated without consent\n- **C)** If her family agree then she can be treated without consent\n- **D)** If she has completed an advance directive to say she does not want cardiopulmonary resuscitation, then she cannot be treated without consent\n- **E)** Consider discharge without Capacity assessment Explanation\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest.\n- If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then that patient should not be treated without consent.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 34-year-old woman presents to the ED with one hour history of palpitations. Her ECG is given What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nA\n- Mobitz type 2\n- **C)** Mobitz type 1   D\n- SVT with aberrancy O\n- **E)** VT Explanation\n- The ECG shows a LBBB morphology with a rapid rate. However, the QRS is not very wide at all. This is SVT with a LBBB. VT vs SVT (SVT with aberrancy)\n\nSVT with aberrancy Very broad QRS complex (>160ms) QRS complex <160ms AV dissociation\n- NO AV dissociation Capture beats\n- No capture beats Fusion beats\n- No fusion beats Absence Of typical RBBB or LBBB\n- presence 0ftypical RBBB or LBBB\n- Positive or negative concordance throughout\n- NO positive or negative the precordial leads concordance throughout the precordial leads RSR' complexes with a taller left rabbit ear\n- RSR' complexes with a taller right rabbit ear Brugada sign\n- No Brugada sign\n- Josephson sign\n- No Josephson sign\n- Age > 35 (positive predictive value Of 85%)\n- Previous ECGs show a bundle branch block Structural heart disease pattern with identical morphology to the broad\n- Ischaemic heart disease complex tachycardia\n- previous ECGs show evidence Of WPVV (short . previous MI\n- Family history of sudden cardiac death ( such as PR < 120ms, broad QRS, delta wave) HOCM, congenital long QT syndrome, Brugada The patient has a history Of paroxysmal syndrome or arrhythmogenic right ventricular tachycardias that have been successfully dysplasia that are associated with episodes of terminated with adenosine or vagal manoeuvres VT)\n- ECG features\n- AV dissociation:\n- P and QRS complexes at different rates\n\n- P waves are often superimposed on QRS complexes and may be difficult to discern\n- Capture beats: Occur when the sinoatrial node transiently "captures" the ventricles in the midst of AV dissociation, producing a QRS complex of normal duration\n- Fusion beats: Occur when a sinus and ventricular beat coincide to produce a hybrid complex C Capture beat F Fusion beat p p wave in AV dissociation\n- RSR' complexes with a taller left rabbit ear:\n- This is the most specific finding in favour of VT\n- This is in contrast to RBBB, where the right rabbit ear is taller\n- Strip shows Taller left rabbit ear in VI\n\n- Brugada sign: Distance from onset of R wave to nadir of S wave is > 100ms in leads VI-6 (discussed further below)\n- Josephson sign: Notching/slurring near the nadir of the S wave\n\nS wave Notched / Slurred R-S interval > 100 ms > 2:5squareS Lead V2 demonstrating both Josephson sign (blue) and Brugada sign (green)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 24-year-old male patient came to the ED with complaint of fever and sore throat in the past ten days. On examination, you find posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. You suspect glandular fever what investigation is needed to confirm your diagnosis?\n- **A)** Viral serology\n- **B)** Full blood count\n- **C)** Abdominal ultrasound\n- **D)** Geimsa stain   E\n- Monospot test Explanation\n- Glandular fever investigations in children older than 12 years of age and in immunocompetent adults:\n- full blood count (FBC) with differential white cell count and a monospot test (heterophile antibodies) in the second week of the illness.\n- Note — FBC option is incorrect as FBC without differential WCC.\n- FBC without differential counts the total number of white blood cells.\n- FBC with differential. There are five kinds of white blood cells. The differential looks at how many of each kind of white blood cell you have. Glandular fever (infectious mononucleosis)\n- Glandular fever (infectious mononucleosis) is an infection nnost commonly caused by the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)\n- It causes approximately 80—90% of cases of glandular fever, with the remainder due largely to cytomegalovirus, human herpesvirus 6, toxoplasmosis, HIV, and adenovirus.\n\n- Glandular fever is most common in people aged 15—24 years and is spread mainly through contact with saliva, such as through kissing or sharing food and drink utensils.\n- Clinical features\n- Lymphadenopathy - Bilateral posterior cervical lymphadenopathy is typical. There may also be anterior cervical, submandibular, suboccipital, postauricular, epitrochlear, axillary, and inguinal lymphadenopathy and lymph nodes are mildly tender and mobile.\n- Sore throat (usually severe) - There may be tonsillar enlargement, the tonsils may meet in the mid-line, and there is usually a 'whitewash' exudate on the tonsils and Sore throat may fail to improve, or become worse, after several days.\n- Other clinical features\n-\n- Prodromal symptoms — this usually lasts for several days and includes general malaise, fatigue, myalgia, chills, sweats, anorexia, and retro- orbital headache.\n- Non-specific rash — after being treated with amoxicillin.\n- Splenomegaly\n- Hepatomegaly\n- Investigations\n\nIn children younger than 12 years of age and in people who are immunocompromized at any age\n- Arrange blood tests for Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) viral serology after the person has been ill for at least 7 days. In children older than 12 years of age and in immunocompetent adults Arrange a full blood count (FBC) with differential white cell count and a monospot test (heterophile antibodies) in the second week of the illness.\n- Glandular fever is likely - If the monospot test is positive the FBC has more than 20% atypical or 'reactive' lymphocytes, or more than 10% atypical lymphocytes and the lymphocyte count is more than 50% Of the total white cell count. If—gspot-test-is-negatiye/not SUpportive of infectious mononucleosis due to EBV, repeat the test in 5—7 days.\n- If the person has clinical features of glandular fever and a negative monospot test, or if a rapid diagnosis is needed then arrange blood tests for EBV viral serology. If a second monospot test is negative Consider testing for cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis if the person is pregnant or immunocompromized. And consider HIV testing Consider checking liver function tests (LFTs) - sually aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) are elevated to 2—3 times the upper limit of normal. Management\n- Admit the patient if he/she has:\n-\n- Stridor. Dehydration or difficulty swallowing fluids.\n-\n\n- A suspected potentially serious complication of glandular fever, such as splenic rupture.\n- Other all patients\n- Advise on the use of paracetamol or ibuprofen to relieve pain and fever symptoms.\n- Advise\n- Symptoms usually last for 2—4 weeks.\n- Tiredness is common and is often the last symptom to resolve.\n- That exclusion from work or school is not necessary.\n- To limit spread of the disease by avoiding kissing and sharing eating or drinking utensils, and to thoroughly clean all items that may have been contaminated by saliva 10 / 10 IO. A 23-year-old young female came to the ED with complaint of generalized fatigue and dizziness. She gives history of separation from her husband three months ago. According to SAD person score what is the cause of her symptoms?\n- **A)** Infection\n- **B)** Separation and divorce\n- **C)** Mania\n- **D)** Anxiety\n- **E)** Depression\n\nExplanation\n- According to modified SAD PERSONS scale, separation would be the cause of her condition.\n- The Modified SAD PERSONS SCALE\n- The 'sad persons scale' is an attempt to assist non-psychiatrists assess suicide risk. It may help as a guide regarding the need for referral or admission.\n\nD p E Sex male Age <19 or >45 Depression/hopelessness Previous attempt/psychiatric care Excessive alcohol or drug use Rational thinking loss Separated/divorced/widowed Organised or serious attempt No social support Stated future attempt 1 1 2 1 1 2 1 2 1 2\n- require full psychiatric evaluation/treatment Score-9-ocgreater=immediate psychiatric hospitalization Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	11	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
30549498-3f74-4c5e-98f4-a891d5e74181	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-14	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 14	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 14\n\n- **A)** Admission for observation\n- **B)** Activated charcoal\n- **C)** Refer to Paediatric\n- **D)** Discharge home with advice\n- **E)** Prepare for laparotomy Explanation\n- urgent surgical intervention.\n\n- 1" line\n- Watchful waiting and serial radiographic follow-up\n- 80% of ingested or inserted foreign bodies pass through the gastrointestinal (GI) Stable patients: tract without symptoms oropharyngeal foreign body\n- 2nd line\n- fiberoptic nasopharyngoscopy - should be considered for patients with an object in the oropharynx that could not be seen during the physical exam. Foreign bodies can be removed using tongue depressor and laryngoscopy line\n- Watchful waiting and serial radiographic follow-up in selective cases\n- Endoscopy is required—urgent endoscopic extraction, irrespective of a full stomach or not, must be performed without delay in the following cases:\n- (1) foreign body in the upper third part of the oesophagus, Stable patients: esophageal or rectal\n- (2) complete obstruction, foreign body (excluding multiple\n- (3) sharp foreign bodies or button batteries magnets and batteries) for foreign bodies that are nonprogressive, sharp, or nonradiopaque, for multiple foreign bodies, for foreign bodies in the stomach >2.5 cm in diameter or cm long, or for oddly shaped foreign bodies such as open safety pins.\n- 2nd line Foley catheter removal - is a widely used technique for the removal of a single, smooth, blunt, radiopaque foreign body from the esophagus or rectum. Surgery - For failed extraction or object of >10 cm in rectum. line Stable patients: esophageal or rectal\n- Emergency endoscopic or surgical removal foreign body - multiple magnets Multiple magnets beyond reach of endoscope - urgent surgical consultation Stable patients: esophageal or rectal\n- Emergency endoscopic or surgical removal if impacted foreign body\n- batteries Batteries beyond reach Of endoscope - watchful waiting radiology\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 35-year-old lady presented to the ED with complaint of severe headache and sudden painful vision loss as soon after started watching TV in her bedroom in the dark. On further assessment she states that she could see haloes around light and her pupils were found to be abnormally dilated. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Episcleritis\n- **B)** Sub-conjunctival haemorhage\n- **C)** Glauconna D Optic neuritis\n- **E)** Corneal ulcer Explanation\n- Clinical-features-of-glaucoma\n- Hx of blurred vision\n- Headache, vomiting, nausea which is worse on watching TV in darkened room or reading,\n- Semi-dilated and fixed pupil\n- Impaired visual acuity\n- Acute closure glaucoma\n- Glaucoma is an eye diseases that cause progressive optic neuropathy and associated with raised intraocular pressure locular hypertension >21mmHg. Normal IOP in between 10-21mmHg\n- Risk factors\n- old age\n- Female gender\n- Hyperopia (long sighted)\n- Asian ethnicity\n\n### Clinical features\n\n- Hx of blurred vision\n- Headache, vomiting, nausea which is worse on watching TV in\n- darkened room or reading: Semi-dilated and fixed pupil\n- Impaired visual acuity\n- Management:\n-\n- Urgently refer to ophthalmology/ admit\n- If admission not possible then start primary care: Patient should lie flat with their face up and head not supported by\n- pillows.\n- Pilocarpine drops: one drop of 2% in blue eyes or 4% in brown eyes Acetazolamide 500 mg orally\n- Analgesia and an anti-emetic, if required\n-\n- First line treatment for people with an IOP of 24mmHg or more PACO, and at risk of visual impairment is 3600 selective laser trabeculoplasty.\n- Complication;-irreversible loss of vision\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 17-year-old male came to the ED with complaint of severe sudden pain in testicle associated with swelling. On examination, there is blue dot sign on testes. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Epididymo-orchitis\n- **B)** Hydrocele\n- **C)** Testicular torsion\n- **D)** Testicular tumor\n- **E)** Testicular Appendix torsion Explanation\n- Testicular torsion\n- Twisting of spermatic cord leads to reduce blood flow to testicle.\n- Its common in under 20s\n- Individuals with bell-clapper deformity predispose to testicular torsion Clinical features:\n- Severe pain usually associated with nausea and vomiting Examination reveal swollen, tender, high-riding testis, with an abnormal transverse lie. Opposite testis lies horizontally (Angell's sign) Loss of ipsilateral cremasteric reflex Negative prehn's sign (elevate the testis will relive pain in epididymitis but not in torsion) Investiqation: Doppler ultrasonography and nuclear scintigraphy. However, surgical exploration for suspected testicular torsion should never be delayed for imaging Management: Urgent surgical exploration with intraoperative detorsion and fixation of the testes\n- Torsion testicular Appendix testis and appendix epididymis are testicular embryologic remnants and that can twist and eventually leads to infarction of appendage.\n- Common in age between 7-12 years Clinical features:\n- Pain is less severe compare to testicular torsion and no systemic symptoms\n- "Blue Dot Sign"- it's an infarcted appendage through the scrotal skin Investigation:\n- Doppler US management\n- Scrotal support and NSAID\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 19-year-old female with history of asthma presents to the ED with increasing shortness of breath and wheeze. She has been using increasing amounts of her salbutamol inhaler over the past few days. She is unable to talk on full sentences and has widespread wheeze throughout her chest on auscultation. Her observations are: RR 35 breaths/min, HR 130 bpm sp02 91 Her ABGs is given How severe is this patient's asthma?\n- **A)** Near fatal asthma\n- **B)** Moderate asthma\n- **C)** C. Acute severe asthma D, Mild asthma E\n- Life-threatening asthma\n\nExplanation\n- Any patient with features of acute severe asthma along with any one feature of life-threatening asthma (as in above scenario his saturation is below <92% ) would be classify as life-threatening asthma.\n- Leve's-of-severity-of-acute-asth—a-attacks-in-adults Moderate acute asthma Acute severe asthma Life-threatening asthma Near-fatal asthma Increasing symptoms PEF >50—75% best or predicted No features of acute severe asthma PEF 33—50% best or predicted Respiratory rate >25/min Heart rate 2110/min Inability to complete sentences in one breath Any one of the following in a patient with severe asthma: Clinical signs Altered conscious level PEF Exhaustion Arrhythmia Hypotension Cyanosis Silent chest Poor respiratory effort Measurements PEF <33% best predicted sp02 pa02 <8kPa 'normal' pac02 (4.6-6.0 kPa) Raised PaC02 and/or requiring mechanical ventilation with raised inflation pressures\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 5-year-old child presents to the emergency department with a sore throat and fever over the last few days. He has developed a rash on the trunk and limbs, which has a sandpaper-like texture. You suspect scarlet fever. What advice would you give to the parents?\n- **A)** use over-the-counter antihistamines to manage the rash\n- **B)** Avoid contact with siblings for one week\n- **C)** No school exclusion needed\n- **D)** Increase fluid intake and rest; no medication required\n- **E)** School exclusion for at least 24 hours after treatrnent initiation\n- Explanation\n- Exclusion from nursery, school, or work is needed for at least 24 hours after starting appropriate antibiotic treatment in Scarlet fever.\n- Scarlet fever (or 'scarlatina')\n- It is a notifiable infectious disease caused by the bacterium Streptococcus pyogenes, also known as group A streptococcus (GAS).\n\n- Risk factors\n- At extremes of the age range, such as the very young and old, or postpartum women.\n- Who are immunocompromised or immunosuppressed.\n- With comorbidities such as skin breakdown, diabetes mellitus, or underlying malignancy.\n- With concurrent chickenpox or influenza.\n- Who inject drugs or are alcohol dependent.\n- Clinical features\n- Sore throat.\n- Fever (typically greater than 38.30C).\n- Headache, fatigue, nausea, and vomiting.\n- Rash-:-A blanching rash usually develops on the trunk 12—48 hours after initial symptoms, before spreading to the rest of the body and flexures.\n- The rash is characteristically red, generalized and pinpoint (punctate) with a rough, sandpaper-like texture.\n- It may be accentuated in the skin folds of the neck, axillae, groin, elbows, and knees with a deep red, linear appearance (so-called 'Pastia's lines').\n- The palms and soles are typically spared.\n- Examination may also reveal:\n- Strawberry tongue — initially the tongue is covered with a white coat through which red papillae may be seen. Later, the white covering disappears, leaving the tongue with a beefy red appearance.\n- Associated cervical lymphadenopathy.\n\n- Flushed face, with marked circumoral pallor.\n- Pharyngitis and small red spots (petechiae) on the hard and soft palate ('Forchheimer spots'). . Investigations\n- Throat swabs and blood tests are not routinely indicated for the diagnosis of scarlet fever.\n- Consider taking a throat swab for culture of Group A streptococcus (GAS) (prior to starting treatment) if:\n- There is uncertainty about the clinical diagnosis.\n- A case is suspected to be part of an outbreak\n- Measurement of serum anti-streptolysin O (ASO) antibody titres are not useful in acute infection, but may be helpful in the diagnosis of post infection complications, such as acute rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis.\n- Management\n- Urgent hospital admission if patient has developed life threatening complications\n- If the person does not need hospital admission,_prescribe appropriate antibiotics promptly,xegardless of the severity of illness:\n- First line - Prescribe a IO-day course of phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V).\n- Second line - options for people with penicillin allergy are:\n- Birth to 6 months — clarithromycin for IO days.\n\n- Non-pregnant adults and children aged 6 months to 17 years — azithromycin for 5 days, or clarithromycin for IO days.\n- Pregnant or postpartum (within 28 days of childbirth) — erythromycin for IO days.\n- Advise and other measures\n- Advise on the use of over-the-counter paracetamol or ibuprofen for symptom relief if there are no contraindications.\n- Encourage the person to rest and drink adequate fluids.\n- Advise to clean and cover any skin breaks including bites, cuts, grazes, or wounds.\n- Exclusion from nursery, school, or work is needed for at least 24 hours after starting appropriate antibiotic treatment\n- Please note, notify the local health protection team promptly within 3 days by completing a notification form if a diagnosis of scarlet fever is suspected.\n- Complication\n- Otitis media\n- Throat infection and abscess (peritonsillar cellulitis, peritonsillar abscess, retropharyngeal abscess)\n- Acute sinusitis and mastoiditis\n- Non-suppurative (immune-mediated) complications tend to occur later in the course of infection,particularly in untreated people, and include:\n\n- Acute rheumatic fever — this is an immunologically-mediated response and can cause carditis and endocarditis (leading to valvular heart disease) and reactive arthritis.\n- Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis — this typically occurs 2 weeks or more after the acute initial infection, and may present with haematuria, reduced urine output, peripheral oedema, proteinuria, and hypertension. Permanent kidney damage is rare.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED after erupting an itchy rash on his left arm. He has no significant past medical history, and his observations are within normal limit. What support anaphylaxis opposite to anaphylactoid?\n- **A)** History of allergic reaction\n- **B)** Patient with mild symptoms\n- **C)** Patient vitally stable\n- **D)** Non-immune mediated\n- **E)** Onset and Recovery Explanation\n\n- Anaphylactoid reaction: first time exposure to allergen , direct release of histamine and other mediators from rnast cells and doesn't cause degranulation. Non-immune (IgE) mediated reaction.\n- Anaphydaxis-react-onz i On further exposure to that allergen, the responsible allergenic antigen binds to an antigen-specific lgE antibody, leading to mast cell degranulation Histamine and other mediators, including leukotrienes and cytokines are released from mast cells and basophils following exposure to this antigen.\n- Summery: anaphylactoid first exposure and non-immune mediated\n- Anaphylaxis: further exposure/ Pt has exposed to allergen previously (history) and immune mediated\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 12-year-old boy presents to the ED with severe right sided testicular pain. He gives history of similar attack before which resolved spontaneously. On examination, right testicle is severely tender on palpation. His doppler ultrasound revealed testicular torsion. What is the most common predisposing factor of testicular torsion in this age?\n- **A)** Bell Clapper Deformity\n- **B)** History of undescended testes c. Trauma\n- **D)** Testicles with horizontal lie\n- **E)** Testicular tumor Explanation\n- Individuals with bell-clapper deformity predispose to testicular torsion\n- Testicular torsion Twisting of spermatic cord leads to reduce blood flow to testicle.\n- Its common in under 20s\n- Individuals with bell-clapper deformity predispose to testicular torsion Clinical features:\n- Severe pain usually associated with nausea and vomiting swollen, tender, high-riding testis, with an abnormal transverse lie.\n- Opposite testis lies horizontally (Angell's sign)\n- LOSS Of ipsilateral cremasteric reflex Negative prehn's sign (elevate the testis will relive pain in epididymitis but not in torsion) vesti tio Doppler ultrasonography and nuclear scintigraphy. However, surgical exploration for suspected testicular torsion should never be delayed for imaging Management: Urgent surgical exploration with intraoperative detorsion and fixation of the testes\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nWhat "D" indicates in this graph?\n- Torsion testicular\n- Appendix testis and appendix epididymis are testicular embryologic remnants and that can twist and eventually leads to infarction of appendage.\n- Common in age between 7-12 years Clinical features:\n- pain is less severe compare to testicular torsion and no systemic symptoms\n- "Blue Dot Sign"- it's an infarcted appendage through the scrotal skin vesti atio\n- Doppler LIS Manaqement:\n- Scrotal support and NSAID\n- **A)** End tidal C02\n- **B)** Ascending expiratory phase\n- **C)** Alveolar plateau\n- **D)** Descending inspiratory phase\n- **E)** Dead space ventilation Explanation\n- The end tidal C02 is the highest concentration of C02 seen at the end of each breath and is represented as letter "D" on the figure.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 3-day-old baby is brought to ED by his mother with complaint of difficulty in feeding and respiratory distress. On auscultation, there is continuous murmur over left infraclavicular region What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **B)** Pneumonia\n- **C)** Bronchiolitis Explanation\n- machinery murmur (continuous systolic murmur) characteristics of PDA. It is best heard at second left intercostals space or immediately left infraclavicular. 10 / 10 IO. A 4-day-old baby is brought to the ED by his mother. His mother reports that baby became cyanosed and developed respiratory distress after feeding. You\n\norder immediate CXR that is shown. what is most likely diagnosis?   A Pneumonia D\n- **B)** BRUE   C Diaphragmatic hernia\n- **D)** Congenital heart disease\n- **E)** Viscus perforation Explanation\n- X-ray of a neonate demonstrates the left hemithorax to be filled with multiple radiolucent regions, with displacement of the cardiac silhouette to the right.\n- Patient history and investigation suggest diaphragmatic hernia.\n- Congenital-diaphragmatic-hernia\n- Congenital diaphragmatic hernia is a diaphragmatic defect, and are frequently diagnosed prenatally with US, which expedites proper initial newborn resuscitation.\n- Most commonly left sided.\n\n- The diaphragmatic defect allows intra-abdominal contents, including stomach, bowel, and, occasionally, liver, to enter the chest during the second trimester of gestation, leading to pulmonary hypoplasia\n- Clinical features:\n- The clinical hallmark is persistent respiratory distress at birth, often with a characteristic "seesaw" side-to-side respiratory pattern due to the severely hypoplastic ipsilateral lung\n- Investigation: obtain chest and abdominal x-ray to confirm the diagnosis\n- Management: immediate intubation is require to treat respiratory distress and then emergent referral to paediatric specialty.\n- Do not use bag-mask ventilation. which will inflate the GI contents in the chest and further compromise ventilation. Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	12	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
5a46e96f-6f61-46f9-aaf4-aa0d822c4f0c	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-15	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 15	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 15\n\nC\n- LUnate dislocation\n- **D)** peri-lunate dislocation\n- **E)** Trapezoid fracture Explanation Lunate dislocation Lunate dislocation produces posterior dislocation Of carpal bones with the concavity Of the lunate facing anteriorly.\n- GLin.igal&turE-Generalized swelling, pain, and tenderness antenorly over the wrist.\n- Lateral view radiograph will show the lunate (represented by the middle\n- **C)** iS pushed Off the radius into the palm. This has been called the\n- spilled teacup" Sign because it resembles a cup spillingin the direction ot the palm.\n- Give analgesia and refer to orthogec for reduction.\n- Complications in both peri-lunate and lunate dislocation Malunion , nonunion avascular necrosis, degenerative arthritis and median nerve compression from' the volar dislocation Of the lunate into the carpal tunnel Spilled teacup RCEM E\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 25-year-old male brought to the ED after falling from a bike. He is complaining of pain in the right leg. His X-ray is given. What type of fracture is it?\n- **A)** Maisonneuve fracture\n- **B)** Denis Weber type A\n- **C)** Pilon fracture\n- **D)** Fibular fracture\n- **E)** Denis Weber type B fracture Explanation\n- X-ray shows widening of the distal tibio-fibular joint due to rnedial ankle ligament rupture (indicative of a rupture of the syndesmosis and interosseous membrane) and proximal fibula fracture.\n- Maisonneuve fracture\n- Moi: Maisonneuve fracture results from an external rotation force applied to the foot.\n- Injury starts at the medial ankle as either a deltoid ligament rupture or a medial malleolus injury. The injury then directed upward and laterally, tearing the interosseous membrane that tethers the distal tibia to the fibula.\n- The third component of this injury is a fracture of the proximal fibula\n- Clinical features: ankle pain, swelling, bruising and restricted range of motion\n- Piagngsiszrequest an ankle x-ray, however further X-rays of the proximal fibula should be performed. Manage.m.e.n.t: surgical intervention required for this injury to reduce and stabilize the fractured medial malleolus and to secure the fibula to the distal tibia, allowing the ruptured interosseous membrane to heal.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nAn 18-year-old girl came into ED with complaint of irritability and drowsiness. On initial assessment her blood glucose was 1.5 mmol/l that became 5.7 after initial management. Now she wants to go home and refuse admission. What is your appropriate action?\n- **A)** Discuss with family\n- **B)** Perform capacity assessment then discharge\n- **C)** Counsel the patient and discharge\n- **D)** Discharge without capacity check\n- **E)** Admit the patient Explanation\n- The Mental Capacity Act (MCA) is designed to protect and empower people who may lack the mental capacity to make their own decisions about their care and treatment. It applies to people aged 16 and over\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest.\n- If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then he/she shouldn't be treated without consent. The-MCA-says;\n- assume a person has the capacity to make a decision themselves, unless it's proved otherwise\n\n- wherever possible, help people to make their own decisions\n- do not treat a person as lacking the capacity to make a decision just because they make an unwise decision\n- if you make a decision for someone who does not have capacity, it must be in their best interests\n- treatment and care provided to someone who lacks capacity should be the least restrictive of their basic rights and freedoms.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nYou are asked to see a 24 year old female who has taken an overdose of 8 ibuprofen 2 hours before attending the ED. The triage nurse has noted the timing of the overdose and that the patient was cooperative and happy to wait to see a doctor. When you call the patient's name, there is no reply and it appears she has left before being seen. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Call mental team and inform them Of his absconsion\n- **B)** Reach out to the patient via phone and consider getting in touch with the patient's CP.\n- **C)** Call next Of kin to bring her back\n- **D)** Call security to look around compound and CCTV\n- **E)** Call police to check safety\n- Explanation\n- There are no grounds for bringing this patient back by force as there is no reason to believe she lacks capacity due to her mental state or from\n\nwhat she has taken (as she was cooperative and happy to wait to see a doctor) However, it would be prudent to review her notes looking for previous mental health issues or domestic violence. It is recommended that attempts are made to contact the patient by phone and to consider contacting the patient's CP.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 46-year-old male RTA patient with head injury brought to the ED by paramedics. Patient has been intubated and hyperventilated (pc02 4.5kPa). after resuscitation you've made transfer this patient to theater while transferring patient condition has been deteriorated. His blood pressure increased and pulse decrease. What would be your next immediate step in this patient?\n- **A)** Hyperventilate\n- **B)** Start 3% hypertonic saline\n- **C)** Start mannitol\n- **D)** Sit-up to 30 degrees\n- **E)** Give fluid bolus Explanation\n- Head Position - Raise the head of the bed and maintain the head in midline position at 30 degrees: potential to improve cerebral blood flow by improving cerebral venous drainage.\n\n- ED management of serious head injury_\n- Initial-management;\n- Follow ABC protocol\n- The primary goals of treatment are to maintain cerebral perfusion and oxygenation by optimizing intravascular volume and ventilation.\n- Recognize and treat elevated ICP\n- Treat any condition that compromises ventilation (e.g., altered mental status, facial/neck trauma, pneumothorax).\n- Patients with severe injury (CCS score of S) require intubation. Circulation Provide aggressive fluid resuscitation to prevent hypotension and secondary brain injury. Normal saline is recommended for volume resuscitation.\n- Maintain systolic blood pressure at 2100 mm Hg for patients 50 to 69 years old or at 2110 mm Hg for patients 15 to 49 years old or >70 years old\n- Pain control Give titrated IV opioid analgesia\n- Patient positioning\n- Raising the head of the bed to 30 degrees/sit-up may improve cerebral blood flow by lowering ICP.\n- T.emperary-hyperyentilation\n\n- Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep paC02 at 4.5 to 5kpa.\n- Hyperventilation will lower ICP in deterioration Pt with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy.\n- Mannitol and hypertQ—ig.salj-e\n- Mannitol is osmotic diuretic.it is used to decrease ICP\n- Avoid mannitol in hypotensive Pts Hypertonic saline is alternative to mannitol in the patient who is not adequately fluid resuscitated or hypotensive.\n- Seizure treatment and prophylaxis\n- Prolonged seizures can worsen secondary injury.\n- Treat acute seizures with midazolam or lorazepam, and if seizures continue, treat as for status epilepticus. n I — avoid hypothermia\n- Glucose control rnaintain at 100 to 180 milligrams/dL or 5.55 to 9.99 mmol/L\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 20-year-old male who took a venlafaxine overdose presented to the ED. He was agitated and refused to provide the nurse with any information. He subsequently absconded from the department. What is your next line of action?\n- **A)** Send him a message on his phone\n- **B)** Call police to check safety\n- **C)** Call next Of kin to bring him back\n- **D)** Call mental team and inform them of his absconsion\n- **E)** Call security to look around compound and CCTV\n- Explanation\n- Based on the RCEM guidelines, the correct initial action when a patient absconds after presenting with an overdose (intoxicated) and agitated or distressed is to perform a local search using available resources like security personnel and CCTV. If found, she could be brought back using persuasion or if this fails, using means that are proportionate to the situation. When she returns, a full assessment of her capacity to decide to leave and her risks should be carried out. If she is not found, it is recommended the police are called and asked to do a welfare check.\n- Patients presenting with mental health problems should have a Mental Health Triage on arrival. Nursing Staff should be trained to triage patients with mental health problems and to make an initial assessment of the capacity of a patient who may decide to leave. Wherever restraint or force is used for a patient who does not have capacity to decide to leave or refuse treatment:l: The person taking action rnust reasonably believe that restraint is necessary to prevent harm to the person who lacks capacity, and 2: The arnount or type of restraint used and the arnount of time it lasts must be proportionate response to the likelihood and\n\nseriousness of harm. Guidance on managing the patient who has absconded can be here, and includes a mental health triage tool.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 20-year-old presented to the ED with complain of pain in his left hand after sustaining motor vehicle crash. X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Fracture of hamate\n- **B)** Carpometacarpal dislocation of 5th digit\n- **C)** Fracture of 5th metacarpal base\n- **D)** Fracture Of 4th metacarpal base\n- **E)** Scapholunate dislocation Explanation\n- The fifth carpometacarpal joint is dislocated medially (ulnar direction), with no evidence of associated fracture. Alignment of the remaining joints is within normal limits.\n- 5th careemetacarpaLjQj.ng-dislecagjen (CMC)-jQjnt dislocation\n- CMC joint dislocations most commonly involve the 5th CMCjoint\n- CMC joint dislocations can be easily missed and failure to diagnose this injury may predispose the patient to pain and weakness of grip.\n\n- Mechanism of\n- punching (most common)\n- fall\n- Associated-injuries\n- The following injuries should raise the suspicion of carpometacarpal joint dislocations:\n- base of metacarpal fracture(common)\n- hook of hamate fracture\n- 'nyestigations\n- of the clear joint space and zigzag or M-shape pattern between the carpals and metacarpals on the PA projection, although this may be sometimes seen in normal patients.\n- The dislocation may only clearly be shown on oblique or lateral projections. . Management\n- These injuries are usually unstable after reduction and therefore are managed operatively by either closed or open reduction with K-wire fixation.\n- Normal vs abnormal radiograph\n\nThe fifth metacarpal base is anteriorly dislocated (in X-ray A). no longer articulating With the hamate. The radio-carpal joint and carpal bones appear anatomically aligned. No acute fracture is seen. The fifth metacarpal base is on it position, articulation with hamate. (X-ray\n- **B)** Showing and no injuries\n- Example -1 X-ray showing carpometacarpaldislocation\n- The fifth carponnetacarpal joint is dislocated medially (ulnar direction). With no evidence Of associated fracture. Alignment of the remaining joints is within normal limits.\n- The alignment is now within normal limits after appropriate management. No fracture is seen. Showing anatomy and no injuries X-ray Showing\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 28-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of Palpitations and few fainting episodes, he has history of death of his father suddenly and unexpectedly in early 40s. His ECG is given. What is your most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** WPW Syndrome\n- **B)** Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\n- **C)** Wellens Syndrome\n- **D)** Atrial fibrillation\n- **E)** Brugada Syndrome Explanation\n- ECG demonstrates features consistent with Type 1 Brugada syndrome: Right bundle branch block pattern Downsloping 'coved' ST elevation in leads VI-V3.\n- Brugada syndrome VS wellens syndrome\n\nThailand.\n- Family history Of sudden cardiac death at years Old. Death is due to ventricular fibrillation due to a group of genetically coded ion channel defects.\n- Clinical features Palpitations, chest pain Breath lessness, dizziness Blackouts Fits (seizures)\n- nvesti iO ECG features Type I Brugada syndrome - Right bundle branch block pattern Downsloping\n- coved' ST elevation in leads VI-V3_ Type 2 Brugada syndrome — ST elevation >2mrn "saddleback" ST\n- T wave configuration. Upright or biphasic T wave in lead VI-3 Management\n- implantable rdioverter defibrillator (ICD) Quinidine has been proposed as an alternative in settings where ICtYs are unavailable Brugada syndrome Brugada syndrome is an autosomal dominant inherited disorder characterised by abnormal ECG findings and sudden cardiac death. It has an increased incidence in South East Asia and is the commonest cause Of sudden unexplained cardiac death in _agngstic_grjterja Wellens syndrome Wellens Syndrome is a clinical syndrome characterised bv biphasic or deeply inverted T waves in V2\n- 3.\n- It is highly specific for critical Stenosis Of the left anterior descending artery (LAD)\n- Clinical features Many patients present with chest pain, some are pain-free by the the ECG is taken, and thev Can also have normal or minimally elevated cardiac enzymes. These patients are, however, at extremely high risk for an extensive anterior wall MI within the next two to three weeks.\n- Di\n- Deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in V2-3 (may extend\n- ECG pattern present in pain-free Sta te Isoelectric or minimally-elevated ST segment Imm) NO precordial Q waves\n- Preserved precordial R wave progression\n- Recent history Of angina\n- Normal or slightly elevated serum cardiac markers\n- There are two patterns of T\n- wave abnormality in Wellens Type A — Biphasic, with initial positivity and terminal negativity (2S% Of cases) Type B — Deeply and symmetrically inverted (75% of cases)\n- Management\n- Wellens syndrome patients -usually require invasive therapy, do poorly with medical management, and may suffer MI or cardiac arrest if inapprogriately stress tested.\n\nBrugada I Examp I e — Brugada type 2 'i4ey "saddleback" ST.T Wave tion\n\nExample — type A Biphasic T Wave 25% That means: Up-Oom T- Example — B\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 22-year-old male brought to the ED with head trauma following road traffic collision. His examination reveals left side hemiparesis, non-reactive pupil, and GCS\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nWhat would be your next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Short dose of steroid\n- **B)** Start mannitol infusion\n- **C)** Intubation and hyperventilation\n- **D)** 3% hypertonic normal saline\n- **E)** commence benzodiazepines Explanation\n- Patients with severe injury (CCS score of s8) require intubation.\n- Temporary_hyperventilation - Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep paC02 at 4.5 to 5kpa.\n- Hyperventilation will lower ICP in deterioration Pt with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy.\n- ED-management-ofserious-head-injury\n- Initial management:\n- Follow ABC protocol\n- The primary goals of treatment are to maintain cerebral perfusion and oxygenation by optimizing intravascular volume and ventilation.\n- Recognize and treat elevated ICP\n- Treat any condition that compromises ventilation (e.g., altered mental status, facial/neck trauma, pneumothorax).\n\n- Patients with severe injury (CCS score of s8) require intubation.\n- Circulation Provide aggressive fluid resuscitation to prevent hypotension and secondary brain injury. Normal saline is recommended for volume resuscitation.\n- Maintain systolic blood pressure at 2100 mm Hg for patients 50 to 69 years old or at 2110 mm Hg for patients 15 to 49 years old or years Old Pain-gont.roLGive titrated IV opioid analgesia\n- Patiencposition.ing\n- Raising the head of the bed to 30 degrees/sit-up may improve cerebral blood flow by lowering ICP.\n- Temporary_hyperventilation\n- Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep paC02 at 4.5 to 5kpa.\n- Hyperventilation will lower ICP in deterioration Pt with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy.\n- Mannitol and hypertonic saline\n- Mannitol is osmotic diuretic.it is used to decrease ICP\n- Avoid mannitol in hypotensive Pts\n- Hypertonic saline is alternative to mannitol in the patient who is not adequately fluid resuscitated or hypotensive.\n\n- Seizure-treatment-and-prophymjs\n- Prolonged seizures can worsen secondary injury.\n- Treat acute seizures with midazolam or lorazepam, and if seizures continue, treat as for status epilepticus.\n- Temperature control — avoid hypothermia\n- Glucose control maintain at 100 to 180 milligrams/dL or 5.55 to 9.99 mmol/L 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 30-year-old male brought to the ED following a fall from a height. His examination reveals bilateral weakness in both arm power  and his lower limb power is 4/5, he also has variable sensory impairment. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Central Cord Syndrome\n- **B)** Complete Cord Transection\n- **C)** Anterior Cord Syndrome\n- **D)** Brown Sequard Syndrome\n- **E)** Posterior Cord Syndrome\n\nExplanation\n- Central-cord-syndrome-glinigal-features\n- Bilateral motor loss Varying degree of sensory loss\n- Greater loss in upper limb than lower limb\n- Greater loss of motor in upper limb than sensory function\n- Spinal cord syndrome syndrome Cornplete cord transection Séquard syndrome Central cord syndrome Mechanism Major trauma Hemitransection or unilateral compression of the usually seen in older patients With preexisting cervical spondylosis who sustain a hyperextension injury Tract injury All tracts AI tract on one Corticospinal tract and spinothalamic previOUS  Symptorns Death if cl-c3 injured. Quadriplegia Paraplegia Complete sensory loss below lesion urinary and faecal continence Ipsilateral hemiparesis Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration and fine touch sensation Contralateral loss of crude touch. pain and temperature sensation atera motor Varying degree of sensory loss Greater loss in upper limb than lower Ii Greater loss of motor in upper limb than sensory function Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	13	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7877dbfa-f431-40c5-91c3-2d8922e176c6	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-16	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 16	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 16\n\n- **B)** Increased heart rate\n- **C)** C. Increased vagal tone   D\n- Hypertension\n- **E)** Increased in body temperature Explanation\n- Neurogenic shock is a devastating consequence of spinal cord injury (SCI). It manifests as hypotension, bradyarrhythmia, and temperature dysregulation due to peripheral vasodilatation (due to loss of vasomotor activity) following an injury to the spinal cord.\n- Vasomotor refers to actions upon a blood vessel which alter its diameter. More specifically, it can refer to vasodilator action and vasoconstrictor action.\n- Neurogenic shock\n- Neurogenic shock occurs with CNS or spinal cord injury that probably occurs in less than 20% of spinal cord—injured patients\n- Loss of peripheral sympathetic innervation results in extreme vasodilatation secondary to loss of sympathetic arterial tone.\n- If the Tl through T4 cord levels are compromised, loss of sympathetic innervation to the heart leaves unopposed vagal parasympathetic\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- cardiac innervation eventually leads to bradycardia or an absence of reflex tachycardia. General features: Patients with neurogenic shock are warm, peripherally vasodilated, and hypotensive with a relative bradycardia. Management; Give lv fluid along with vasopressors as Pt does not respond to IV fluid alone. Neurogenic shock usually presents with hypovolemia so moderate fluid resuscitation should be given Give atropine if Pt has significant bradycardia\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 32 years old male presented to the ED with right shoulder pain. His right arm is externally rotated and abducted. His X-RAY is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Anterior dislocation of shoulder\n- **B)** Clavicular fracture\n- **C)** Humeral head fracture\n- **D)** Humeral neck fracture\n- **E)** Posterior dislocation of shoulder\n\nExplanation\n- X-ray shows loss of congruity b/w humeral head and the glenoid, which indicative of anterior shoulder dislocation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 50-year-old male presented to the ED after falling from stairs at home. Which of the following is not an indication of CT head within 1 hour?\n- **A)** Basal skull fracture\n- **B)** GCS less than 15 on initial assessment\n- **C)** Post-traumatic seizures\n- **D)** Two episodes of vomiting\n- **E)** CCS less than 15 at 2 hours after injury Explanation\n- GCS less than 15 on initial assessment doesn't require CT head in adult\n\nCT head within 1 hour GCS less than 13 on initial assessment\n- GCS less than 15 at 2 hours after the injury on assessment\n- Suspected open or depressed skull fracture (fluid running from nose,ears, black eyes)\n- Any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum,\n- panda' eyes, CSF leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign).\n- Post-traumatic seizure.\n- Focal neurological deficit (difficulties with understanding, speaking, reading or writing; decreased sensation; loss of balance; general weakness; visual changes; abnormal reflexes).\n- More than 1 episode of vomiting\n-\n-\n- CT head within 8 hours Age 65 years or older. Any history of bleeding or clotting disorders. Dangerous mechanism of injury (a pedestrian or cyclist struck by a motor vehicle, an occupant ejected from a motor vehicle or a fall from a height Of greater than 1 metre or 5 stairs). More than 30 minutes' retrograde amnesia Of events immediately before the head injury.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nRegarding the prognostic factors for acute pancreatitis, which of the following is not typically included?   A Glucose > IO mmol/L\n- **B)** LDH > 350 IU/L\n- **C)** Age > 55 years\n- **D)** WCC > 16 x IOA9/L\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Explanation These are all included as prognostic factors in the Ranson criteria. However, CGT (Gamma-glutamyl transferase) > 500 is not typically used as a prognostic factor for acute pancreatitis. Di v ri in r m n in pancreatitis\n- As acute pancreatitis is associated with significant morbidity and mortality risks, different severity scores have been developed to aid prognosis. One measure of severity of acute pancreatitis is the Ranson score which scores II criteria (higher scores worse). At admission: age in years > 55 years white blood cell count > 16,000 cells/mm3 blood glucose > IO mmol/litre (200 mg/IOO ml) serum aspartate aminotransferase (AST) > 250 IU/litre serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) > 350 IU/litre At 48 hours; serum calcium < 2.0 mmol/litre (8.0 mg/100 ml) haematocrit fall > 10%\n\n- hypoxemia P02 < 60 mmHg\n- blood urea nitrogen (BUN) increase of 1.8 mmol/litre (5 mg/IOO mi) or more after intravenous fluid hydration\n- base deficit (negative base excess) > 4 mEq/Iitre\n- sequestration of fluids > 6 litres.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 46-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of fever associated with pain in urination and urgency and rectal pain on defecation for the last three months. He has been prescribed several courses of antibiotics by his GP however, his condition hasn't improved. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Epididymo-orchitis   B\n- Lower UTI\n- **C)** Acute Pyelonephritis D Chronic Prostatitis\n- **E)** Prostatic Abscess Explanation\n- This patient has chronic Prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome\n\n- Chronic Prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome It is defined as at least 3 months of urogenital pain, which may be\n- perineal, suprapubic, inguinal, rectal, testicular, or penile and is often associated with lower urinary tract symptoms (such as dysuria, frequency, hesitancy, and urgency), and sexual dysfunction (erectile dysfunction, painful ejaculation, or postcoital pelvic discomfort).\n- Chronic-prostati.tis-classi-gcation;\n- Chronic prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome (CP/CPPS) (sometimes also referred to as abacterial prostatitis or prostate pain syndrome) — accounts for over 90% of men with chronic prostatitis (there is no proven bacterial infection).\n- Chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP) — less than 10% of men with chronic prostatitis.\n- The exact cause of chronic prostatitis is unknown but is thought to be multifactorial (infection, inflammation)\n- The chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP) is thought to be caused by: An ascending urethral infection, or\n- Lymphogenous spread of rectal bacteria, or Undertreated acute bacterial prostatitis, or\n- Recurrent urinary tract infection with prostatic reflux\n-\n\n- Most common pathogen Escherichia coli (most common), Klebsiella species, Proteus mirabilis, Enterococcus faecalis, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa.\n- Investigation\n- Urine dipstick to exclude UTI\n- Perform a sexually transmitted infection (STI) screen (first pass urine for gonorrhoea/chlamydia NAAT [Nucleic Acid Amplification Test]), and consider sending a urethral swab for trichomoniasis. Particularly in sexually active men younger than 35 years and men with multiple sexual partners or recent partner change.\n- Consider a prostate specific antigen (PSA) blood test to rule out prostate cancer\n- Assess renal function by measuring serum creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) if clinically indicated (for-example in patient with recurrent urinary tract infection, Chronic urinary retention, a history of renal stones.\n- Management\n- Reassurance-and-counselling\n- The cause is not always understood, but is thought to be multifactorial.\n- The condition is chronic and treatment can be difficult, but most men notice improvement within six months.\n- Treatment is often more about controlling symptoms rather than effecting an immediate cure.\n\n- Drug therapy_\n- Paracetamol or NSAID for pain relief\n- Do not prescribe opioid\n- An alpha-blocker for 4—6 weeks if significant lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) are present.\n- 200 mg twice a day for 4—6 weeks, or Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 4—6 weeks\n- A stool softener such as lactulose or docusate if defecation is painful\n- Targeted cognitive behavioural therapy, counselling and antidepressants for men with psychosocial symptoms (for example, depression, stress and poor coping mechanisms).\n- Acupuncture\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nMechanism of action of Dabigatran on which factor?\n- **A)** Factor Xa\n- **B)** Fibrinogen   C Factor Villa\n- **D)** Prothrombin\n\nE\n- Factor Ila Explanation\n- Dabigatran is a direct thrombin (Factor-Ila) inhibitor with a rapid onset of action Dabigatran\n- Action-ds a direct thrombin (Factor-Ila) inhibitor with a rapid onset of action\n- dose is 150 mg twice a day. However, A reduced dose of 110—150 mg twice daily is recommended in some patients.\n- Indications\n- Treatrnent of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE) and the prevention of recurrent DVT and PE\n- For the prophylaxis of stroke and systemic embolism in adults with non-valvular atrial fibrillation (NVAF).\n- For the prevention of venous thromboembolism (VTE) in people who have undergone knee/Hip replacement surgery\n- Contraindications\n- people with:\n\nSevere renal impairment (creatine clearance [CrCl] less than 30\n- mL/minute).\n- Active bleeding.\n- A prosthetic heart valve — increased risk of thromboembolic and bleeding events. Antiphospholipid syndrome — increased risk of recurrent thrombotic\n- eve Severe hepatic impairment or liver disease expected to have any\n- impact on life expectancy. Elevated liver enzymes (if they are more than twice the upper limit of\n- normal). People with significant risk of major bleeding, such as:\n- Current or recent gastrointestinal ulcer.\n- Oesophageal varices.\n- Recent brain or spinal injury.\n- Recent brain, spine, or ophthalmic surgery.\n- Recent intracranial haemorrhage.\n- Malignant neoplasm.\n- Vascular aneurysm.\n-\n- Adverse effects Anaemia\n-\n- Diarrhoea Gastrointestinal discomfort\n- Haemorrhage\n- Abnormal hepatic function\n- Nausea\n-\n\n- Antidote\n- Idarucizumab (Praxbind ) is a specific reversal agent indicated in adults treated with dabigatran when rapid reversal of its anticoagulant effects is required.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 26-year-old male brought to ED by paramedics following a burn injury. He has full thickness burn to anterior trunk and both legs which you estimate greater than 25% TBSA. His Weight is 65kg. How much fluid should be given in the first 8 hours?\n- **A)** 1200ml\n- **B)** 1000ml\n- **C)** 3250ml O\n- **D)** 2800ml Explanation\n- parkland formula (2-4ml x weight in kg x %TBSA).\n- Adult: 2-ml (Hartman or plasma-lyte) x weight in kg x %TBSA\n\n2 x 65 x 3250ml in 24hours\n- One half of fluid should be given in first 8hrs and remaining half in-16 hours. Therefore  1625ml should be given in first 8 hours\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 29-year-old man presents with fever, muscle aches, and jaundice after returning from a camping trip. His history and symptoms raise suspicion for leptospirosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic test to confirm leptospirosis?\n- **A)** Blood culture\n- **B)** Liver function tests\n- **C)** Serology\n- **D)** Urine microscopy\n- **E)** Chest X-ray\n- Explanation\n- Serology is the rnost appropriate diagnostic test to confirm leptospirosis. It detects antibodies against Leptospira bacteria, which are usually present after the first week of infection. Other methods like\n\nurine microscopy, blood culture, and liver function tests may provide supportive information but are less definitive for diagnosing leptospirosis. Leptospirosis\n- Leptospirosis is re-emerging as a result of increased international travel and climate change. Outbreaks have been associated with flooding and natural disasters.\n- In developing countries, infections more commonly occur in rural subsistence farmers and urban slum dwellers. In developed nations, infections are associated with occupational exposures, water sports, and flooding. Higher rates have been reported in tropical areas during the rainy season and temperate regions during late summer.\n- The infection is caused by motile spirochetes from the genus Leptospiraand is maintained in nature by chronic renal infection of carrier animals.\n- Investigations\n- FBC — shows high WBC count, Iow platelet count, anaemia\n- enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) - is used commonly for early diagnosis (IgM).A positive result should be confirmed with another test, ideally MAT or PCR if available.\n- polymerase chain reaction (PCR) - Can provide a more timely diagnosis during the acute phase, if available. Can be amplified from serum, urine, aqueous humor, and other tissues; however, the best\n\nspecimen is serum. The transience of leptospires in body fluids means that a negative PCR test does not exclude leptospirosis. Blood/CSF culture - Leptospires can be isolated from CSF/blood but\n- they are not recommended as the sole diagnostic method.\n- Urinalysis — shows mild proteinuria, pyuria, haematuria, hyaline or granular casts. ECG - Should be performed in all patients owing to associated\n- arrhythmias secondary to myocarditis. Cardiac monitoring is also recommended during treatrnent. Other investigations to be considered - I-FTS, renal biopsy, lung biopsy,\n- CXR, serum pancreatic enzymes, conjugated bilirubin.\n- Differentials Hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome\n- Dengue fever\n- Malaria infection\n- Haemorrhagic fevers\n- Rickettsioses\n- Influenza infection\n-\n- Viral hepatitis Acute fatty liver\n- HELI-P syndrome\n- Gestational hypertension\n- Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19)\n-\n\n- Management\n- Mild disease\n- Oral doxycycline, in children 2-4 mg/kg/day maximum 200 mg/day (not recommended in children 8 years of age or less), and in adults 100 mg orally every 12 hours with ampicillin, azithromycin, or amoxicillin as alternative first-line agents.\n- Patients must be carefully monitored for adverse reactions including the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, which can be fatal.\n- Antibiotic treatment course: 7 days (except azithromycin, for which treatment course is 3 days in adults and not established in children).\n- Moderate to severe disease\n- IV Benzylpenicillin 100 mg/kg/day given in divided doses every 6 hours; adults 2.4 to 4.8 g/day is recommended in adults and children first-line, with ceftriaxone or cefotaxime as alternative first-line agents.\n- In case of penicillin and/or cephalosporin allergy, IV azithromycin (the intravenous formulation is not recommended below the age of 16 years) or doxycycline (Above age 8 years). Erythromycin is a possible alternative and can be given to children below the age of 8 years.\n- Other measures\n- Leptospirosis with cardiac involvement— manage arrhythmia according to resus guideline and initiate cardiac monitoring.\n- Leptospirosis-withpu/monary-inyolyementzmechanical ventilation ± intravenous methylprednisolone.\n\n- In case Renal or hepatic failure - consider acute dialysis and supportive care.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 9-year-old child presented to the ED by his mother with complaint of painful knee and decrease in range of motion. On examination, there is swelling over the region of metaphysis. His X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Rickets\n- **B)** Giant Cell tumor\n- **C)** Ewing Sarcoma   D\n- Osteosarcoma\n- **E)** Osteomyelitis Explanation\n- X-ray shows sunburst pattern, which is commonly seen in osteosarcorna.\n- Osteosarcoma-(osteogenicsarcoma)\n\n- Primary turnor in less than 20 years of age\n- In adults usually secondary predisposing factors, such as Paget disease of bone, bone infarcts, radiation, familial retinoblastoma, Li-Fraumeni syndrome.\n- Location; Metaphysis of long bones (often in knee region).\n- enlarging mass or pathologic fractures\n- Investigation; sunburst pattern on x-ray\n- Management: surgery, chemotherapy  IO. A 24-year-old male presented to the ED with right shoulder pain. His arm is internally rotated, and adducted X-ray is given. You want to perform close reduction; how would you do that?\n- **A)** Patient supine grasp the affected arm at wrist and lift gently vertical\n- **B)** Abduct the arm to 1800 with simultaneous pressure on humeral head\n- **C)** Patient in prone affected arm hanging down with IOLbs weight applied to wrist\n- **D)** Apply traction and external rotation to affected arm at 900 flexion at elbow\n- **E)** Flex the elbow at 900 then slowly externally rotate arm\n\nExplanation\n- Posterior should dislocation can be reduce with traction, countertraction method and pepalma method\n- Traction, countertraction method-apply traction and external rotation to affected arm at 90 degrees of flexion at elbow. The physician applies traction and assistant applies countertraction\n- the effected arm is first adducted and internally rotated, with caudal traction applied. Then, maintaining traction and internal rotation, the medial aspect of the upper arm is pushed laterally, disengaging the humeral head from the glenoid fossa. Finally the arm is extended, and the humerus falls back into place. previOUS  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	14	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
25a10c40-56bb-4014-b8fd-ed44e082366b	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-17	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 17	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 17\n\nmmHg, and a temperature of 350C. On examination, she appears pale and diaphoretic. What is the most likely cause of her bradycardia?\n- **A)** Vagal response due to cervical trauma\n- **B)** Myocardial infarction\n- **C)** Acute blood loss leading to hypovolemic shock\n- **D)** Infection leading to septic shock\n- **E)** Electrolyte imbalance\n- Explanation\n- Vagal Response due to Cervical Trauma - The presentation of bradycardia and low temperature is consistent with a vagal response triggered by cervical traurna during the miscarriage. The vagus nerve, when stimulated, can lead to a decrease in heart rate and blood pressure. The hypothermia (temperature of 350C) can also result from peripheral vasoconstriction and the body's response to shock.\n- Cervical shock\n- Bradycardia and hypotension resulting from cervical stimulation or instrumentation is referred to as "cervical shock". It may result from\n\nthe presence of products of conception in the cervix during miscarriage, which should be removed if present. More commonly, in primary care settings, cervical shock can occur during insertion of an intrauterine device (IUD). It is thought to be due to stimulation of the vagal nerve. Whilst uncommon, it can rapidly cause a woman to become unwell with circulatory compromise. The UK Faculty of Sexual and Reproductive Healthcare (FSRH) states that the availability of appropriate emergency medication, including atropine, during IUD insertion is essential and this is a service standard for resuscitation in sexual health services in the I-JK.\n- For a patient with symptomatic bradycardia secondary to cervical shock, cease manipulation of the cervix and remove all instruments. Call for help. Assess the patient with the ABCDE approach (Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure). Assess vital signs and provide oxygen, if required. Ensure the patient is supine and elevate the legs to improve venous return. If these are insufficient to improve the patient's haemodynamic status, 500-600mcg IV atropine is recommended followed by a saline flush. It is typically used to ensure that the medication is fully delivered into the bloodstream.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nYou are required to perform RSI in a 30-year-old traumatic patient for which neuromuscular blocking agent suxamethonium has been used. What is the contraindication to use of this drug?\n- **A)** Trismus\n- **B)** Hypertension\n- **C)** Muscle fasciculations\n- **D)** Duchenne muscular dystrophy\n- **E)** Bradycardia Explanation\n- Suxamethonium/Succinylcholine chloride contraindicated in Duchenne muscular dystrophy\n- Suxamethonium chloride:\n- it's a depolarising neuromuscular blocking agent, commonly use in tracheal intubation.\n- Dose: 1.2-2mg/kg\n- Prolong paralysis may occurs in Pt with low or atypical cholinesterase\n- Contraindications: hyperkalaemia, ECG changes due to hyperkalaemia or\n- Risk of developing hyperkalaemia (burn, trauma, spinal cord injury, muscle dystrophy, infection, desquamating skin condition, crush injuries).\n- Adverse effects: bradycardia ,muscle pain , fasciculation, anaphylaxis, malignant hyperthermia, trismus/masseter.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 37-year-old welder presented to the ED with a history of redness and foreign body sensation in the left eye. his eye image is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Scleral penetrating injury\n- **B)** Corneal abrasion\n- **C)** Globe rupture\n- **D)** Retrobulbar haemorrhage   E\n- conjunctivitis Explanation Globe Rupture\n- is a traumatic injury involving the breakdown in the integrity of the wall of the eye, either the sclera or cornea or both\n- Causes\n- blunt trauma, projectile injury, laceration of eyelid/periorbital area, metal on metal, high-speed machinery, explosions\n- Clinical features\n- Protruding foreign body in eye\n\nEccentric/teardrop pupil\n- Decreased visual acuity\n-\n- Afferent pupillary defect Extrusion of vitreous\n- External prolapse of the iris or ciliary body\n- Tenting of the sclera or cornea at the site of globe puncture\n- Volume loss of eye\n- Investigation\n- Seidel test apply fluorescein dye to eye, which binds damaged\n- corneal epithelium and turns green under light through a cobalt-blue filter or Wood lamp, and check for streaming of fluorescein-tinged aqueous humor; this indicates corneal laceration\n- CT of orbit Management\n- Prevent increased intraocular pressure (IOP)\n-\n- Head of bed at 30 degrees\n- Avoid eye manipulation (do not measure eye pressure, retract lid, or perform ocular ultrasound) Cover with an eye shield (do not place eye patch as it exerts pressure\n- on globe) — A paper cup can be used if an eye shield is not available\n- Administer topical and systemic broad-spectrum antibiotics Tetanus booster as required\n- Give antiemetics to prevent nausea (which increases IOP)\n- Give pain medicine\n-\n\n- Consult ophthalmology even if CT negative, formal surgical evaluation may be needed if occult injury suspected\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 35-years-old motorcyclist brought to the ED following road traffic collision. On initial assessment, you suspect abdominal injury and you commenced fluid resuscitation. His observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 120 bpm sp02 94% What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Continuous observation in the ED\n- **B)** Obtain CT scan\n- **C)** DPL   D\n- Urgent laparotomy\n- **E)** Perform eFAST\n\nExplanation\n- Haemodynamically unstable patient with blunt trauma should be investigated with eFAST before laparotomy/theater. if eFAST positive patient should be immediately transfer to theater. However, haemodynamically unstable patient with penetrating trauma doesn't require eFAST. Urgent laparotomy/ transfer to theater is required.\n- Comparison-of-EAST, Indications Advantages Disadvantages FAST\n- Abnormal hemodynamics in blunt abdominal trauma Penetrating abdominal traumma without other indications for immediate laparotomy Early operative determination Noninvasive Performed rapidly Repeatable NO need for transport from resuscitation area Operator-dependent Bowel gas and subcutaneous air distort images\n- Can miss diaphragm, bowel, and pancreatic injuries Does not completely assess retroperitoneal structures Does not visualize extraluminal air Body habitus can limit image clarity DPL\n- Abnormal hemodynamics in blunt abdominal trauma\n- Penetrating abdominal trauma without other indications for immediate laparotomy Early operative determination\n- Performed rapidly\n- Can detect bowel injury No need for transport from resuscitation area\n- Invasive\n- Risk Of procedure-related injury Requires gastric and urinary decompression for prevention of complications\n- Not repeatable\n- Interferes with interpretation Of subsequent CT or FAST\n- Low specificity\n- Can miss diaphragm injuries CT SCAN Normal hemodynamics in blunt or penetrating abdominal trauma\n- Penetrating back/flank trauma Without Other indications for immediate laparotomy Anatomic diagnosis Noninvasive Repeatable\n- Visua lizes retroperitoneal structures\n- Visualizes bony and soft- tissue structures\n- Visualizes extraluminal air Higher cost and longer time Radiation and IV contrast exposure Can miss diaphragm injuries Can miss some bowel and pancreatic injuries Requires transport from resuscitation area\n\n- Indication of laparotomy Blunt abdominal trauma with hypotension, with a positive FAST or\n- clinical evidence of intraperitoneal bleeding, or without another source of bleeding Hypotension with an abdominal wound that penetrates the anterior\n- fascia Gunshot wounds that traverse the peritoneal cavity\n- Evisceration\n- Bleeding from the stomach, rectum, or genitourinary tract following\n- penetrating trauma peritonitis Free air, retroperitoneal air, or rupture of the hemidiaphragm\n- Contrast-enhanced CT that demonstrates ruptured gastrointestinal\n- tract, intraperitoneal bladder injury, renal pedicle injury, or severe visceral parenchymal injury after blunt or penetrating trauma Note- Haemodynamically unstable patient with blunt trauma should\n- be investigation with eFAST before laparotomy/theater Haemodynamically unstable patient with penetrating trauma doesn't require eFAST. Urgent laparotomy/ transfer to theater is required.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with painful left eye and blurred vision. In addition, he is complaining of floater associated with dull throbbing headache. Further investigation reveals he is taking certain medications for his back pain. On examination, his eye is red and inflamed.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Conjunctivitis   B\n- Anterior Uveitis\n- **C)** Episcleritis\n- **D)** Corneal abrasions\n- **E)** Keratitis Explanation\n- Clinical features of uveitis\n- Pain dull or throbbing ache (pain may be worse when the person is contracting the ciliary muscle — for example, when reading) and the intensity and the location (ophthalmic, or non-ophthalmic eye pain).\n- Pain may extend from the eye to the surrounding orbit and may be tender to palpation through the eyelid\n- Posterior uveitis may be painless\n- Diminished or blurred vision\n- Red eye (this is not always present)\n- Watering of the eye.\n- Photophobia.\n- Flashes and floaters.\n- An unreactive or distorted pupil (due to the iris sticking to the lens)\n\n- Uveitis (iritis) it is is inflammation of the uveal tract (iris, ciliary body, and choroid).\n- Anterior uveitis (the most common form of uveitis) — inflammation in\n- the anterior segment of the eye; this includes iritis (inflammation in the anterior chamber alone), iridocyclitis (inflammation in the anterior chamber and anterior vitreous), and anterior cyclitis. Intermediate uveitis — inflammation of the vitreous; this includes pars\n- planitis, posterior cyclitis, and hyalitis. Posterior uveitis — inflammation of the retina or choroid; this includes\n- focal, multifocal or diffuse choroiditis, chorioretinitis, retinochoroiditis, retinitis, and neuronitis. Panuveitis— inflammation in the anterior chamber, vitreous and retina\n- or choroid. Risk factor\n- Systemic autoimmune disorders, such as: Seronegative spondyloarthropathies (ankylosing spondylitis, juvenile\n- rheumatoid arthritis, Reiter's syndrome, or inflammatory bowel disease). Behqet's disease.\n- Sarcoidosis.\n- Psoriatic arthropathy.\n- Multiple sclerosis.\n- Infection.Common infectious causes are herpes simplex, herpes zoster\n- virus, cytomegalovirus, and toxoplasmosis.\n\n- Trauma\n- Neoplasm\n- Having the genetic marker HLA (Human Leukocyte Antigen) -B27.\n- Clinical features\n- Pain dull or throbbing ache (pain may be worse when the person is contracting the ciliary muscle — for example, when reading) and the intensity and the location (ophthalmic, or non-ophthalmic eye pain).\n- Pain may extend from the eye to the surrounding orbit and may be tender to palpation through the eyelid\n- Posterior uveitis may be painless\n- Diminished or blurred vision\n- Red eye (this is not always present)\n- Watering of the eye.\n- Photophobia.\n- Flashes and floaters.\n- An unreactive or distorted pupil (due to the iris sticking to the lens)\n- Management\n- corticosteroid and referral to ophthalmologist\n- Complication\n- Visual loss . Cataract\n- Glaucoma\n\n- Band keratopathy (calcium deposits in the cornea)\n- Posterior synechiae (adhesions between the posterior iris and the anterior lens surface)\n- Optic neuropathy or optic atrophy\n- Retinal detachment/retinal scars\n- Cystoid macular oedema\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of severe painful eye associated with lacrimation, photophobia, conjunctival hyperemia and decreased visual acuity. His corneal fluorescein staining image is given. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?\n- **A)** Corticosteroids   B\n- IV acyclovir\n- **C)** Ciprofloxacin drops D\n- TOPical Ganciclovir\n- **E)** Irrigation with water Explanation\n\nHerpes simplex keratitis HSK is confined to the epithelium with classic\n- dendritic appearance, commence antiviral therapy with one of the following: oc. aciclovir 3%, e.g. Aciclovir Agepha, ophthalmic preparation\n- (unpreserved) ganciclovir 0.15% ophthalmic gel\n-\n- Herpes simplex-Ocular infection Herpes simplex virus (HSV) ocular infections can cause inflammation of\n- the retina, iris, cornea, conjunctiva, eyelids, and surrounding skin. The hallmark of this disease is the branching or dentritic ulcer.\n- Infection is usually caused by HSV type I (HSV-I), and rarely by HSV type\n- 2 (HSV-2), typically in association with orogenital sex. Infection and inflammation of different parts of the eye causes:\n- Cornea — keratitis.\n- Iris and associated uveal tract — iritis or iridocyclitis; uveitis.\n- Retina — retinitis.\n- Conjunctiva — conjunctivitis.\n- Eyelids — blepharitis.\n- Surrounding skin — periocular dermatitis\n- Clinical features\n-\n- Symptoms\n\n- Malaise and fever Eye pain Eye irritation or photophobia Eye watering\n- Blurred vision . Sign\n- An acute red eye\n- Crops of vesicles and ulcers on an erythematous base, or pustules along the lid margin or periocular skin\n- A hazy cornea\n- A fixed irregular pupil\n- Reduced corneal sensitivity — suggests keratitis\n- Reduced visual acuity\n- Management (NICE guideline)\n- Refer all cases of suspected ocular herpes simplex infection to eye casualty, or an emergency eye service for same-day assessment and specialist management.\n- if emergency same-day assessment is not possible or practical, seek specialist advice from an ophthalmologist regarding initiating drug treatment such as topical antivirals ( aciclovir ointment or ganciclovir gel)\n- Acute Herpes Simplex: in non-contact lens wearing adults and where HSK is confined to the epithelium with classic dendritic appearance, commence antiviral therapy with one of the following:\n- oc. aciclovir3%, e.g. Aciclovir Agepha, ophthalmic preparation (unpreserved)\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- ganciclovir 0.15% ophthalmic gel Complications Corneal scarring and visual impairment Corneal perforation Secondary glaucoma Infection\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nWhich of the following is a known risk factor for Sudden Unexpected Death in Infancy and Childhood (SUDIC)?   A\n- Parental smoking\n- **B)** Lying supine\n- **C)** use of a pacifier during sleep\n- **D)** Room-sharing without bed-sharing\n- **E)** Exclusive breastfeeding\n- Explanation\n- Parental Smoking: Smoking is a significant risk factor for SUDIC as it can affect an infant's environment and respiratory health. Reducing\n\nexposure to tobacco smoke is an important preventive measure. Lying Supine is actually recommended as a safe sleeping position to reduce the risk of SUDIC.\n- SUDIC protocol\n- The sudden, unexpected death of an infant or child is a tragedy for the family and all involved. Such deaths may result from previously unrecognised medical conditions or as a result of unintentional incidents. However, a significant proportion of sudden unexpected deaths in infancy (SUDI) remain unexplained!\n- Relevant terminology\n- SUDI (sudden unexpected death in infancy)— as above, but used to refer to infants up to 24 months of age.\n- SUDC (sudden unexpected death in childhood) — as above, but refers to children over 24 months of age.\n- SIDS (sudden infant death syndrome)— refers to the sudden and unexpected death of an infant under 12 months of age, with the episode apparently occurring during normal sleep, and which remains unexplained in spite of thorough investigation, including a post- mortem examination, examination of the scene of death and review of the clinical history.\n- Causes of SIDS\n\n- Data from epidemiological studies indicate that the numerous risk factors for SIDS act together to increase or decrease the chance of death. This is known as the "Triple Risk Hypothesis: I.The infant is vulnerable (e.g. they have a physiological abnormality of some kind) 2.The infant is at a critical period of development (e.g. the first six months of life, when SIDS is more common) 3.There is an external factor in action that serves as a physiological stressor to the infant, (e.g. being placed prone to sleep and having their breathing compromised).\n- Risk factors\n\nRisk Factor\n- prone/side sleeping position\n- Co-sleeping (beds, sofas or armchairs) Temperature and overwrapping\n- Soft sleeping surfaces/loose bedding Second-hand mattresses\n- Poor antenatal care Maternal smoking during pregnancy Household smokers after birth\n- Drug and a Icohol use Factors increasing risk further\n- LOW birth weight (<2.5kg/5.51bs)\n- Pre-term (07 weeks)\n- IUGR\n- LOW birth weight (<2.5kg/5.51bs) pre-term (<37 weeks)\n- IUGR\n- Smoking\n- Alcohol/drug consumption by parent Excessively tired parent Prone sleeping Prone sleeping (The mechanism is unclear but thought to be related to bacterial carriage and allergens) (Later initiation and fewer appointments potentially increase likelihood Of pre-term birth and IUGR)\n- (Increases likelihood Of pre\n- term delivery, low birth weight and IUGR) (Greater susceptibility to bacterial and viral infections9) Co-sleeping under the influence Substance misuse is related to other SIDS risk factors such as low SES, poor antenatal care, IOW birth weight, Advice to parents\n- Always place an infant to sleep on their backs at the start of every sleep period. .4\n- Avoid sleeping with baby ona sofa, avoid bedsharing where alcohol/drugs/cigarettes have been consumed by either party, ideally baby should sleep in their own cot or moses basket.\n- If bedsharing is required, signpost to the Safe Sleep Guidance on Lullaby Trust7.\n- Checkroom temperature is not too hot and that clothing and bedding is appropriate. Babies should not wear hats to sleep either during day or night. Signpost to the Lullaby Trust's guides.\n- Remove pillows, duvets and quilts.\n- The sleeping surface should be firm enough that it does not indent/conform to the shape of the baby's head.\n- Place baby feet-to-foot of cotto prevent them wriggling down under\n- There is no established link between SIDS and products such as nests or pods, specifically marketed for infant sleep. They are, however, not recommended for use by The Lullaby Trust.\n- "woid second-hand mattress use (risk has not been found to be significant if it is from the same home and used previously, e.g. for a sibling). Covering it With a waterproof mattress protector will help keep it clean and dry. Encourage timely booking and attendance at all antenatal appointments.\n- Offer smoking cessation advice to parents\n- Offer drug and alcohol liaison services to parents. Complete a referral.\n\nOther maternal factors, e.g. young age, increased parity, short inter- pregnancy interval, twin pregnancy, low SES, previous safeguarding concerns, family history of SUDIC infant neglect. (Impair parental decision-making around baby's safety)\n- (Likely to be due to increased risk- taking behaviour, poor engagement with healthcare services, mental ill- health, increased likelihood Of child being in a neglectful environment10) alternative care arrangements for baby for the whole night if they plan to drink or use drugs.\n- Encourage engagement with midwifery and health visiting teams and GP\n- An unexpected death may be sufficiently explained — by its clinical presentation, or early laboratory or radiological findings — so that the attending doctor is able to issue a medical certificate of the cause of death (MCCD). In those situations, it may not be necessary or appropriate to institute Sudden unexpected death in infancy and childhood.\n- In all unexpected deaths where a medical practitioner is unable to issue a MCCD, it is the responsibility of the coroner to determine the cause of death and to ensure all statutory requirements around registration are met. However, to do this, the coroner is dependent on the information provided by the professionals involved in caring for the infant and responding to the death. All professionals involved in this joint agency response have a responsibility to work with the coroner in achieving these aims:\n- The joint agency_response consists of the following essential may vary in accordance with local priorities, needs and resources, no response-should-be-considered-comp.lete-withoug-t—ese-cor.e components: a) careful multi-agency planning of the response\n\nb) ongoing consideration of the psychological and emotional needs of the family, including referral for bereavement support c) initial assessment and management, including a detailed and careful history, examination of the infant, preliminary medical and forensic investigations, and immediate care of the family, including siblings d) an assessment of the environment and circumstances of the death e)A standardised and thorough post-mortem examination f) a final multi-professional case discussion meeting\n- The joint agency response to an unexpected infant death\n\nThe joint agency response to an unexpected infant death From Togeth«, 2015 2 Unexpected child death Arnbulance and police response Assess immedate risks/cmcerns Resuscitation if appropriate Police consider appropriate scene security Of siblings Other family Where appropriate. carer(S) transferred to hospital With paediatric facilities; continued/dedsion to stop - Hospital staff notify police - Lead police investigator attends hospital Responsible clinician confirms death - Support for carer(s) and other family members - Initial between paediatrician and police officer\n- paediatrician (where possible, jointly With atteruding police Officer) takes initial history. examinati«x. and immediate investigations Initial sharing and discussion\n- ConsideraHM Of need for s47 Strategy meeting Joint home Visit by paediatriciam\n- 'nurse Coroner mortem Further police investigations\n- Review Of and care information Discussion\n- Review Of circumstances Of the death\n- Ongoing farnily support including appropriate feedback of outcomes of Case Discussion Inquest Ch Nd Overview Hosp ital Staff notify:\n- Other health organisatims\n- care provid eS > report for coroner and pathologist and mortem m report provided to coroner. and with c agreement to Report of Local Case Discussion provided to coroner and COOP\n\n- Routine-suggested-samples-to-be-taken-immediate]y-after sudden unexpected deaths in infancy and childhood\n- In unexpected deaths in older children, the appropriate clinical samples will be guided by the circumstances of the death and the clinical findings. Sample\n- FBC, U&Es, LFTs Cultures Microarray\n- Toxicology\n- Inherited Metabolic Diseases (IMD) Amino Acids Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)\n- Nasopharyngeal aspirate Nasopharyngeal aspirate Swabs from any identifiable lesions Urine (if available) Purpose of test\n- Infection, electrolyte disturbances, renal failure, liver failure\n- Infection Genetic abnormalities Poisoning (intentional and non- intentional) 1 Guthrie card for specific metabolic disorders Metabolic disorders Microscopy, culture and sensitivity Nucleic acid amplification techniques Culture and sensitivity Culture and sensitivity Toxicology if indicated, inherited metabolic diseases 1\n\n- Additional samples and steps to be considered after\n- Skin biopsy for fibroblast culture in all cases of suspected metabolic\n- Muscle biopsy if history is suggestive of mitochondrial disorder.\n- In suspected carbon monoxide poisoning, blood sample for carboxyhaemoglobin.\n- Ensure the coroner has given permission to take samples.\n- All samples taken must be documented and labelled to ensure there is an unbroken 'chain of evidence', using an appropriate 'chain of evidence' proforma.\n- This may mean handing samples to a police officer directly, or having the laboratory technician sign upon receiving them in the laboratory.\n- Ensure that samples given to the police or coroner's officer are signed for.\n- Record the sites from which all samples were taken.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 26-year-old male came to the ED after being punched several times on his face. his X-ray is given. Which of the following complications patient will likely develop?\n- **A)** Numbness over the chin\n- **B)** Forehead numbness\n- **C)** Sensory loss in lower teeth\n- **D)** Loss of sensation of inferior nasal septum\n- **E)** Numbness in the lower lip Explanation\n- X-ray shows a depression of the left orbital floor in keeping with an undisplaced fracture and a pathognomoniic "teardrop sign" is present. Additional minimally displaced fracture seen at the lateral wall of the left maxillary sinus with an air-fluid level in the left maxillary sinus.\n- Infra-orbital nerve commonly injured in orbital blow out fractures. it innervates lower eyelid, upper lip and nose.\n- Orbital blow out fracture\n- MOI: Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly from a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a fracture of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents into the maxillary sinus.\n- Clinical features: Pt may have diplopia and can't look up and medially due to inferior rectus entrapment.\n- Test infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital nerve commonly damage in this injury.\n\n- Investigation: X-ray will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema.\n- Management: Urgently Refer all Pt with orbital blow-out fracture (e.g. diplopia or reduced eye movements) to a maxillofacial surgeon and/or an ophthalmologist\n- facial CT scan will be needed to visualise the fracture in detail and plan surgical repair. Normal orbital floor' Teardrop\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 70-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of scrotal pain and swelling associated with urethral discharge. On examination, pain relieve on elevation of testes. epididymo-orchitis diagnosis was made. What is the most likely causative agent in this patient?\n- **A)** Chlamydia\n- **B)** Staphylococcus aureus\n- **C)** Neisseria gonorrhoeae\n- **E)** Pseudomonas aeruginosa EXPIanation\n- Impeople aged 35 years or older with a low-risk sexual history, it is usually caused by enteric organisms such as Escherichia coli. Epididymo-orchitis\n- Epididymo-orchitis is a clinical syndrome consisting of pain, swelling, and inflammation of the epididymis and/or testis.\n- Causes\n- Impre:puber.talshildren, epididymo-orchitis is usually idiopathic and self-limiting\n- It may be secondary to recent adenovirus, enterovirus, or other viral infection.\n\nIn sexually active people aged 14—35 years - usually caused by local\n- spread of sexually transmitted infections (STIS) such as Chlamydia or Neisseria gonorrhoeae.\n- Impeopleaged35years-gr-o!der with a low-risk sexual history, it is usually caused by enteric organisms such as Escherichia coli . Rare causes of epididymo:or.chitis-jnclude-mumps-infection (causes unilateral or bilateral parotid swelling which precedes usually unilateral orchitis, often in young adults). Clinical features Feverand chills\n- Swelling and redness in your testicle (diffuse swelling)\n- Pain in your scrotum, sometimes moving to the rest of your groin\n- Blood in semen\n- Urethral discharge\n- Dysuria (painful urination)\n-\n- 'Prehn sign' (relief of pain with elevation of the testes) Presence of cremasteric reflex\n- Investigations\n-\n- Diagnosis made clinically\n- Urine dipstick test and mid-stream urine (MSU) for microscopy and culture, if acute epididymo-orchitis is suspected in a pre-pubertal child, or an adult aged over 35 years with a low-risk sexual history If any doubt for STI then a referral for GUM assessment. Don't be\n- tempted to start empirical therapy for STIS\n\n- Management\n- ReferraLpathway If an STI is the most likely cause of epididymo-orchitis\n- advise urgent referral to a local specialist sexual health clinicfor STI\n- testing, treatment, and possible contact tracing. if urgent referral to a sexual health specialist is not possible then:\n- Treat with ceftriaxone I g 1M plusoraldoxycycline 100 mg twice daily\n- for 10-14 days. If a cephalosporin and/or tetracycline antibiotic is contraindicated, treat\n- with oral ofloxacin 200 mg twice daily for 14 days. If epididymo-orchitis is most likely due to chlamydia or other non-\n- gonococcal organisms (if no risk factors for gonorrhoea) then: Treat with oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 10—14 days, ororal\n- ofloxacin 200 mg twice daily for 14 days If a quinolone antibiotic is contraindicated, treat with oral co-amoxiclav\n- mg three times a day for IO days.\n- Admissiompathway\n- If symptoms are severe, the person is systemically unwell, or there is a suspected serious complication, (sepsis, abecess formation, chronic scrotal pain) arrange emergency hospital admission. If an enteric organism (E-COLI) is the most likely cause of epididymo-\n- orchitis\n\n- Ensure a urine dipstick test and mid-stream urine (MSU) sample for microscopy and culture have been arranged.\n- Treat the patient oral ofloxacin 200 mg twice daily for 14 days, ororal levofloxacin 500 mg once daily for IO days. antibiotic is contraindicated,_treat with oral co-amoxiclav  mg three times a day for IO days.\n- If-mpre:pubertal-ghild-has-suspected-epidißymo-orchitis:\n- If symptoms are severe, the child is systemically unwell, or there is a suspected serious complication,arrange emergency hospital admission.\n- If a diagnosis of mumps orchitisis suspected, follow mumps managemencguideline\n- Arrange a urine dipstick test and mid-stream urine (MSU) sample for microscopy and culture,and manage depending on the result.\n- If a urinary tract infection (UTI) is confirmed, seek specialist advice on the choice of antibiotic, dose, and duration of treatment, and arrange onward referral to a paediatrician as appropriate.\n- If a UTI is not confirmed, do not start antibiotic treatment routinely in primary care.\n- Reassure parents/carers that epididymitis is usually a self-limiting condition which resolves with rest and analgesia such as paracetamol and/or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) as needed.\n- Give advice on self-management measures and when to arrange follow-up,\n\n- Advise on rest scrotal support (such as with supportive underwear)\n- Paracetamol OR NSAID as per need.\n- Complications\n- Sepsis\n- Abscess formation and ischaemia/infarction of the testicle\n- Reactive hydrocele\n- Testicular atrophy and subfertility, for example due to mumps orchitis\n-\n- Chronic scrotal pain  IO. You are required to perform procedural sedation in 38-year- old female who requires propofol for shoulder dislocation. What feature supports deep sedation?\n- **A)** Purposeful response following repeated or painful stimulation   B\n- Hypertension\n- **C)** cardiovascular is unaffected\n- **D)** ventilation usually affected\n- **E)** Airway unaffected\n\nExplanation\n- Propofol cause hypotension and respiratory depression. there is purposeful response following repeated or painful stimulation in deep sedation. Sedation Minimal sedation (anxiolysis) e.g: Entonox Moderate sedation/Analgesia e.g: opioid [benzodiazepine Deep Sedation/Analgesia e.g: propofol General anaesthesia  Responsiveness Normal response to verbal communication Purposeful response to verbal or tactile stimulation Purposeful response following repeated or painful stimulation unarousable even with painful stimulation Airway Unaffected NO intervention required Intervention may be required Intervention usually required Ventilation Unaffected Adequate may not be adequate Usually not adequate CVS Unaffected Usually maintained Unusually maintained Maybe impaired previous\n	\N	[]	\N	f	15	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
6c08dcb7-d362-4334-a9b6-fb6bf883582f	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-18	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 18	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 18\n\nA Blood culture O\n- **B)** FBC\n- **C)** CRP\n- **D)** Blood culture and LP\n- **E)** ESR EXPIanation\n- Investigations in late onset neonatal sepsis before start of\n- Perform blood culture before 1st dose of antibiotic\n- Measure c-reactive protein when starting antibiotic treatment and also during antibiotic treatment, 18 to 24 hours after presentation\n- If it safe then perform LP to obtain CSF before and during antibiotic treatment when:\n- there is a strong clinical suspicion of early-onset neonatal infection or sign of meningitis\n- Note: don't perform urine or skin swab microscopy or culture routinely in late-onset of neonatal sepsis\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 12-year-old girl brought to the ED after falling on outstretched hand with elbow hyperextended. She is complaining of pain in her elbow.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Which neurovascular structure is likely to be injured?\n- **A)** Axillary artery   B\n- Cephalic vein\n- **C)** Musculocutaneous nerve   D Brachial artery\n- **E)** Profunda brachii artery Explanation X-ray shows supracondylar fracture. Complications of supracondylar fractures Neurological injury_ Radial nerve injury Median nerve injury (anterior interosseous branch) Ulnar nerve injury (iatrogenic) Vascular injury. Brachial artery injury\n\n- Volkmann's ischemic contracture (compartment syndrome of the forearm)\n- Late-complications\n- Nonunion, Malunion Myositis ossificans\n- Loss of motion\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 70-year-old male with history of uncontrolled hypertension presented to the ED with sudden onset of chest pain while having his meal. pain was severe in nature and lasted for 20 minutes. He is pain free now. His examination revealed diastolic murmur. His observations are: Heart rate I I Obpm BP180/110 mmHg sp02 96% What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Aortic aneurysm\n\nB\n- Aortic dissection\n- **C)** C. Infective endocarditis\n- **D)** Acute coronary syndrome\n- **E)** Pulmonary emboli Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggest Aortic dissection (uncontrolled hypertension, severe chest pain, diastolic murmur).\n- Aortic dissection\n- An aortic dissection occurs following a tear in the aortic intima with subsequent separation of the tissue within the weakened media by the propagation of blood.\n- Stanford classification:\n- divides the dissections into Type A and Type B\n- Type A dissection involves the ascending aorta and/or the arch of the aorta.\n- Type B dissections involve only the descending aorta and occur distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery.\n\nRisk factor\n- Hypertension Marfan syndrome\n- Ehlers-Danlos syndrome Annuloaortic ectasia and familial aortic dissection\n- Bicuspid aortic valve\n- Coarctation of the aorta\n- Pregnancy\n- Turner syndrome\n- Cocaine abuse\n-\n- Giant cell arteritis Iatrogenic\n- Clinical features Feature of pain in AAD:\n-\n- Sharp in nature describe as tearing pain Occurs in anterior of chest 70-80% of Pt with Type A dissection\n-\n- Upper back pain maybe experienced in Type-B Physical-examination;\n- Faint or absent heart sounds\n- Distended neck veins\n- Presence of shock\n-\n- A difference of >20 mmHg in the blood pressure of each arm Acute or progressive aortic regurgitation\n-\n- A diastolic murmur over the aortic valve area\n\n- Investigations\n- ECG (Non-specific ST segment and T wave changes or can be normal). . A widened mediastinum\n- An abnormal aortic contour A globular heart suggesting a large hemopericardium\n- Pleural effusion\n- Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) and trans-esophageal echocardiography (TOE) are available tests for AAD, maybe perform in ED, ICU or in OT.\n- CT is used where TOE isn't immediately available and CT is more common than TOE in Europe.\n- Management\n- Initial treatment:\n- Control BP\n- IV beta blocker alone or with vasodilator GTN, nitroprusside to achieve HR less than 60bpm and BP less than 120mmHg\n- Pain control with IV opiate\n\n- Definitive treatment:\n- Type A dissections require open surgery to prevent rupture into the pericardial sac\n- Type B dissections are managed-medically-but may require endovascular-stenting if the patient has persistent pain, a rapidly expanding aortic diameter, or mal-perfusion of branch vessel organs\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 39 years old female with history of fall presented to the ED with complaint of wrist pain associated with difficulty in moving wrist. Her X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Radial styloid fracture\n- **B)** Monteggia fracture   C, Smith fracture\n- **D)** Colles fracture\n- **E)** Barton fracture Explanation\n\n- X-ray revealed extra-articular transverse fracture through the distal radius with volar angulation.\n- Smith fracture\n- Smith's fracture, or reverse Colles' fracture, is a volar angulated fracture of the distal radius. (volar means anterior hand or towards palmer surface) as its opposite in colle's where distal radius angulated dorsally (posteriorly).\n- The fracture line extends obliquely from the dorsal surface to the volar surface 1-2 cm proximal to the articular surface\n- MOI: result from a fall or direct blow on the dorsum of the hand and wrist, or from a fall on the outstretched hand in flexed position\n- Diagnosis:\n- The lateral radiograph reveal the volar angulated and displaced fracture.\n- Management;\n- Give analgesia and immobilize in volar slab POP and require urgent referral to orthopedic for ORIF\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 43-year-old presented to the ED with upper GI bleed. Which of the following is not included in Glasgow Blatchford Score ?\n- **A)** Syncope\n- **B)** stroke\n- **C)** Hepatic disease   D\n- Hemoglobin\n- **E)** Heart failure Explanation\n- Glasgow-Blatchford Score\n- The Glasgow-Blatchford score is the risk assessrnent tool of choice in the emergency department.\n- It is calculated using FOUR component:\n- Medical history (hepatic disease, heart failure), Symptoms (melaena, syncope),\n- Signs (tachycardia, blood pressure),\n- Blood tests (haemoglobin, urea).\n- The maximum score is 29.\n- NICE recommends that patients with a Blatchford score of O can be considered for early discharge with outpatient endoscopy follow up.\n\nRisk marker Range 6.5- 7.5 Urea 8.0 - 9.9 10.0 - 24.9 >25.o Haemoglobin (g/dL) 12 - 12.9 for men 10.0-11 9 <10.0 Haemoglobin (g/dL ) 10.0-11 9 for women 10.0 Systolic blood 100-109 pressure (mmHg) 90-99 Pulse >100 Other markers Melaena Syncope Hepatic disease Cardiac failure Score 2 3 4 5 1 3 6 1 6 1 2 3 1 1 2 2 2\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 40-year-old male presented to the ED with a complaint of a rash which you suspect is Lyme disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for Lyme disease?\n- **A)** Azithromycin for 5 days\n- **B)** Doxycycline for 7 days\n- **C)** Metronidazole for 14 days\n- **D)** Ceftriaxone lg stat   E\n- Amoxicillin for 21 days\n- Explanation\n- Amoxicillin for 21 days is a recommended treatment for early Lyme disease. Lyme disease management\n\nAdults and children aged 12 years or older\n- Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily (or 200 mg once daily) for 21 days.\n- Amoxicillin 1000 mg three times daily for 21 days (if doxycycline is not suitable).\n- Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 17 days (if doxycycline and amoxicillin are not suitable) Children aged 9—12 years\n- Doxycycline — 5 mg/kg in two divided doses on day one, followed by 2.5 mg/kg daily in one or two divided doses for a total of 21 days.\n- Amoxicillin — 30 mg[kg three times daily for 21 days, for children 33 kg and under (if doxycycline is not suitable).\n- Azithromycin — IO mg/kg daily for 17 days, for children 50 kg and under (if doxycycline and amoxicillin are not suitable).\n- Note: Doxycycline — best avoided below age 9.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 25-year-old male presented to the ED with a history of electrical burn on his right hand while fixing electric wire. On examination, he is vitally stable and now your considering to discharge the patient. What investigation should be done before discharge?\n- **B)** CPK\n- **C)** CRP\n- **D)** Echocardiogram\n\nExplanation\n- Patient can be discharged if Patient is asymptomatic and ECG is normal.\n- Management-of-electrical-burn ECC should be obtained on admission for every patient presenting with an electrical burn.\n- Pt can be discharged if Pt is asymptomatic and ECC is normal. There is no need for admission.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 5-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complaint of fever 38.7 oc and unable to weight bear. His mother reports that his symptoms began approximately 7 days ago and his ESR is 50mm/h. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Transient Synovitis\n- **B)** Osteomyelitis   C\n- Septic Arthritis\n- **D)** Avascular necrosis\n- **E)** Perthes' disease Explanation\n- To use Kocher's criteria patient likely to have septic arthritis. useful to differentiate between septic arthritis and transient synovitis.\n- The four predictors are:\n- Inability to walk\n- Fever of 38.5 oc or greater\n- ESR> 40 mm/h\n- WBC> 12,000/mm3 (12 x 109/L)\n- Mnemonics: WALK FEW\n- Probability of Septic Arthritis (Kocher)\n- OPredictor — <0.2 % (less likely SA)\n- IPredictor —3%\n- 2Predictors-40%\n- 3Predictors — 93.1%\n- 4Predictors — 99.6% (highest likelihood of SA)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 21 -years old male RTA brought to the ED with devastating brain injury. Patient has been intubated and neurosurgical consultation has been made.\n\n-\n-\n-\n- however, neurosurgeon refuse to perform surgery. Patient attendants have been counselled for his condition. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Admit patient in intensive care\n- **B)** Perform brain death test\n- **C)** Discuss for Organ donation\n- **D)** Discharge home\n- **E)** Retain in the ED Explanation Evaluated by neurosurgery deemed to be non-survivable with no benefit from neurosurgery intervention Do not withdraw life supportive treatment up to 48-72 hours Admit the Pt in the ICU Reassess Pt every 24 hours\n\nDevastating brain iniury algorithm Progressing toward brain death Discuss With donor coordinator Decision to test for brain death and brain death confirmed Consider or offer donation of organ after brain stem death (DBD) Pt in ED with devasting brain injury\n- Initial investigation and imaging prognosis uncertain or considered very poor\n- Evaluated by neurosurgery deemed to be non-survivable With no benefit from neurosurgery intervention Do not withdraw life supportive treatment up to 48-72 hours\n- Admit the Pt in the ICU Reassess Ptevery 24 hours No Improvement Consider withdraw life support Discuss with donor coordinator Decision to withdraw life support treatment Consider DCD(donation after cardiac death) in those Pts who's DBI isn't improved even after 72 hrs of life support treatment Improved Review management plan\n	\N	[]	\N	f	16	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
32736eb9-d1e6-41c9-bcb1-f34c0c2ea768	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-19	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 19	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 19\n\n- **B)** Irrigate with NS and replant the tooth again   C\n- Put in the milk\n- **D)** Put in the hypertonic saline\n- **E)** Refer patient to maxillofacial surgery Explanation\n- \\mmediate-management-Qf-ayulsed-tQQth\n- Hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth.\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- Key_points\n- Central incisors lost at 6-7 years of age.\n- Between the ages of about 6 and 7 years, the primary teeth start to shed and the permanent teeth begin to come through.\n- Initial management of primary and permanent teeth is the same. However, Ifthe primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldn't be re- implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth.\n\nImmediate manawment of avulsed tooth primary teeth management Adult tooth management\n- The appropriate management of an avulsed tooth within the first 30 minutes and an organized treatment plan are essential to increase tooth survival.\n- hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth.\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- Primary teeth management\n- If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldn't be re- implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth.\n- f the primary teeth are very loose and presenta potential airway risk, then they should be removed\n- Adult tooth management should be replaced into the correct position, and a splint(wire splint or silk suture splints) should be used to stabilize them and arrange follow-up with dentist. be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth: saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here\n- The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried out for up to 2 week.\n- Tetanus booster should be consider and antibiotic (tetracycline or penicillin V) should be recommended\n- patients advised to rinse with chlorhexidine 0.1% mouthwash for 1-2 weeks\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA IO-year-old girl presented to the ED with complaint of pain in her elbow after a fall on outstretched hand with elbow hyperextended. Her X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Radial head fracture\n- **B)** Humeral shaft fracture\n- **C)** Olecranon fracture\n- **D)** Elbow dislocation\n- **E)** Supracondylar fracture Explanation\n- X-ray shows supracondylar fracture\n- Supracondylar fracture\n- Common in children 3-10 years of age however peak at the age of 7.\n- It is caused by a fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow hyperextended\n- Classi-ggation-of-supracondsdar-fracture\n- Type-I fractures are displaced Q mm and may have a posterior fat pad sign on X-ray\n- Type-2 fractures are angulated to varying degrees, but the posterior cortex of the humerus is intact are completely displaced with no cortical contact\n- Diagnosis\n- X-ray will show Fat-pad sign or fracture\n- Draw anterior humeral line along the front of the humeral shaft on the lateral view normally it passes though the middle of ossification center of capitulum\n- Management\n\n- Type-I fracture manage with analgesia and immobilization with a long- arm posterior splint or collar and cuff with the elbow at 90 degrees and the forearm in pronation or neutral rotation and arrange fracture clinic within 2-7 days\n- Urgent referral for type-II and type-III supracondylar fracture or those with neurovascular deficit or >50% displacement. Complications\n- Neurological injury\n- Radial nerve injury Median nerve injury (anterior interosseous branch)\n- Ulnar nerve injury (iatrogenic)\n- Vascular injury_\n- Brachial artery injury\n- Volkmann's ischemic contracture (compartment syndrome of the forearm)\n- Late complications\n- Nonunion, Malunion Myositis ossificans\n- Loss of motion\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 7-year-old child brought to the ED by his father with history of fall while playing on trampoline. His father states that child hasn't move his arm since fall. His X-ray is given. What is the most likely diagnosis? A Ulnar fracture\n- **B)** Pulled elbow\n- **C)** Supracondylar fracture\n- **D)** Radial head fracture\n- **E)** Radial fracture with subluxation Explanation\n- X-ray shows radial fracture with subluxation Subluxation/ partial dislocation Fracture\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 3-year-old child brought to the ED with shock and you've decided to get IO access, which of the following is the site of IO placement in this patient?   A Distal femur\n- **B)** Clavicle\n- **C)** Small size needle for sternum\n- **D)** Illiac crest\n- **E)** Proximal humerus Explanation\n- Proximal tibia is another site for insertion, however this option isn't given.\n- Proximal humerus should be consider in children over 6 years of age.\n\nProximal Humerus (first choice) Distal Femur Proximal Tibia Distal Tibia Insertion site\n- The insertion site is on the most prominent aspect of the greater tubercle, 1 to 2 cm above the surgical neck.\n- Palpate 2-3cm above external condyle, Insertion just medial to midline to avoid\n- Palpate tibial tuberosity, Insert 2- 3cm below + medial to tibial tuberosity\n- Insertion site is located approx. 3 cm proximal to the most prominent aspect Of the medial malleolus\n- Aim the needle tip downward at a 450 angle to the horizontal plane\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone Age\n- Both adult and paediatric (>6 year)\n- Only in years age\n- Both adult and paediatric\n- Both adult and paediatric\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 16-year-old male presented to the ED with facial trauma. He has avulsed two upper central incisors while playing football, which he has collected and brought them to the ED What would be your appropriate step of management?   A Put in the milk\n- **B)** Refer patient to maxillofacial surgery\n- **C)** Get a dental x-ray\n- **D)** Put in saline and replant the teeth again\n- **E)** Reassure and discharge Explanation\n- Patient permanent teeth are avulsed, they should be replanted immediately.\n- Management of dental trauma Immediate management of avulsed primary teeth management Adult tooth management\n- The appropriate management of an avulsed tooth within the first 30 minutes and an organized treatment plan are essential to increase tooth survival.\n- hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth.\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- Primary teeth management\n- If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldntbe re- implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth.\n- f the primary teeth are very loose and presenta potential airway risk, then they should be removed\n- Adult tooth management should be replaced into the correct position, and a splint(wire splint or silk Suture splints) should be used to stabilize them and arrange follow-up with dentist. Awbed_teethshould be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth; saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here\n- The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried Out for up to 2 week.\n- Tetanus booster should be consider and antibiotic (tetracycline or penicillin V) should be recommended\n- patients advised to rinse with chlorhexidine 0.1% mouthwash for 1-2 weeks\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED with 15 hours history of painful swelling of his left leg. On examination, you note his Calf muscle is red, hot and tender. His observations are: Heart rate 90bpm, BP 129/67mmHg 20 T 380C His leg image is given What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Acute cellulitis\n- **B)** Erysipelas\n- **C)** Erythema multiform\n- **D)** Steven Johnson syndrome\n- **E)** Necrotizing fasciitis\n- Patient clinical features suggest acute cellulitis\n\n- Acute cellulitis VS Necrotizing fasciitis\n\nAcute cellulitis It is an acute bacterial infection of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue. Cellulitis most commonly affects the lower limbs, but Other areas, such as the upper limbs, face, ears, and trunk, can also be affected\n- Causes Streptococcus pyogenes. Staphylococcus aureus Pseudomonas aeruginosa Vibrio vulnificus Aeromonas hydrophila etc\n- Risk factors Trauma (due to a bite, burn, or laceration), Leg ulceration Maceration or fungal infection between the or a concomitant skin disorder (such as atopic eczema) Comorbidities (such as diabetes mellitus) lymphoedema and leg oedema\n- Clinical features Red, painful, hot, swollen, and tender skin, that spreads rapid ly, Fever, malaise, nausea, shivering, and rigors On examination Diffuse redness or a well-demarcated edge Blisters and bullae may be seen There is usually an obvious skin break where the infecting organism may have entered, such as a wound.\n- Investigations Investigations are not usually necessary but may be considered in certain cases. Swab for culture — if there is an open wound, penetrating injury. drainage. Ultrasonography — for distinguishing nonpurulent cellulitis from cellulitis With underlying abscess Skin biopsy WCC, ESR and CRP\n- Management\n- If cellulitis is diagnosed, categorize the severity using the Eron classification system, to help guide management decisions. (table is given below) Has Class III or IV cellulitis IS Very young (under 1 Of age) or frail, [s immunocompromized Has significant lymphoedema.\n- Has facial cellulitis (unless Very mild).\n- Has suspected orbital or periorbital cellulitis Necrotizing fasciitis It is a life-threatening subcutaneous soft-tissue infection that requires a high index of suspicion for diagnosis.\n- Always Suspect necrotising fasciitis in a patient With a rapidly progressing soft-tissue infection and any of the following:\n- Severe pain (disproportionate to the clinical findings) Anaesthesia over the site of infection\n- Oedema and erythema (oedema will typically extend beyond the erythema) Systemic signs of infection\n- Causes Type I necrotising fasciitis - is a polymicrobial infection such as Bacteroides or Peptostreptococcus or Enterobacterales or mon-group A streptococcus Type II necrotising fasciitis - is most commonly a monomicrobial infection with Streptococcus pyogenes (group A\n- Risk factors Immunosuppression due to chronic illness (e.g., diabetes mellitus, alcohol dependence) Cutaneous trauma, Surgery, or ulcerative conditions Varicella zoster infections Intravenous drug use and hospitalization\n- Clinical features Anaesthesia or severe pain over site of infection Fever Palpitations, tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypotension, and lightheadedness Nausea and vomiting Oedema or induration Vesicles or bullae Delirium\n- Investigations Surgical exploration\n- The 'finger test' is a surgical method that can be performed under local anaesthesia at the bedside for the diagnosis Of necrotising fasciitis. Blood and tissue culture, CRP, ESR\n- Clotting SC reen Consider X-ray, MRI, ultrasonography\n- Management Emergency surgical - Refer the patient immediately to the surgical team; necrotising fasciitis requires rapid debridement of all infected tissues in combination With empirical antibiotic therapy. MRSA antibiotic coverage - Continue vancomycin, linezolid, tedizolid, or daptomycin if MRSA is confirmed. Analgesia and fluid resuscitation\n\nperson has a penicillin allergy. prescribe either: Clarithromycin SOO mg twice daily for days, Or Doxycycline 200 mg on the first day then 100 mg once daily, for a total of 5—7 days. Erythromycin (in pregnancy) SOO mg four times daily for 5— 7 days.\n- Note\n- dose should be adjusted in children accordingly.\n- Complications Necrotizing fasciitis Myositis Sepsis, Subcutaneous abscesses, Post-streptococcal nephritis.\n- Lymphoedema, leg ulceration\n- Eron classification of cellulitis Class I Class II Class II Class IV\n- There are no signs of systemic toxicity and the person has no uncontrolled comorbidities. The person is either systemically unwell or systemically well but with a comorbidity (for example peripheral arterial disease, chronic venous insufficiency, or morbid obesity) which may complicate or delay resolution of infection. The person has significant systemic upset, such as acute confusion, tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypotension, or unstable comorbidities that may interfere with a response to treatment, or a limb- threatening infection due to vascular compromize. The person has sepsis or a severe life-threatening infection, such as necrotizing fasciitis.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 28-year-old boxer presents to the emergency department after sustaining a punch to his ear during a match. On examination, his ear is swollen and\n\ndeformed. Image is shown. What is the most appropriate initial management?\n- **A)** Immediate surgical drainage\n- **B)** Apply ice and observe\n- **C)** Refer to ENT specialist\n- **D)** Prescribe oral antibiotics\n- **E)** Aspirate and apply a tight bandage Explanation\n- The first step in managing this condition is to aspirate the hematorna and apply a tight bandage to prevent re-accumulation. Referral to ENT may be necessary for follow-up, but initial aspiration is crucial.\n- Acute auricular haematoma\n- Auricular haematorna occurs following blunt trauma when blood and serum accumulate between the perichondrium and cartilage.\n- most commonly affects the helical rim of the pinna but sometimes, can affect the conchal bowl.\n- if left untreated a 'plate' of fibroneocartilage develops from the damaged perichondrium and the resulting scarring and regeneration of the cartilage leaves the cosmetically unsightly\n- cauliflower ear', characterised by thickening and irregular projection of the ear.\n\n- it is a relatively common condition in contact sports, particularly in rugby and boxing\n- Management\n- The first step in managing this condition is to aspirate the hematoma, however, it is complicated by re-accumulation which occurs unless adequate pressure can be maintained with packing or apply tight bandage.\n- a more definitive result can be obtained by incision and drainage and either leaving a suction drain in situ or suturing a 'button' over the area to force apposition of the skin, perichondrium, and cartilage\n- if treatment is delayed and a plate of fibroneocartilage has formed, then permanent ear deformity will result unless formal surgical excision is undertaken, which has been done up to 1 month following injury\n- once a mature cauliflower ear has formed, then reconstructive plastic surgery is required to restore normal form to the pinna\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 55-year-old female with history of fall brought to the ED with complaint of severe right hip pain. On examination, right limb is shortened and internally rotated. His X-ray is given. What complication most likely patient develop in this injury?\n- **A)** Sciatic nerve injury\n- **B)** Femoral nerve injury\n- **C)** Femoral artery injury\n- **D)** Common peroneal nerve injury\n- **E)** Obturator nerve injury Explanation\n- X-ray shows posterior hip dislocation\n- Cemp\\igations-otpgsterior-hip.ßislogatign: Sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the femoral head and secondary OA\n- Posterior hip dislocation\n- Posterior hip dislocations results from a posterior force applied to a flexed knee, most commonly a dashboard injury.\n- These injuries are associated with acetabular, femoral neck, and fernoral shaft fractures\n- Clinical features: limb will be shortened and internally rotated, with the hip flexed and adducted.\n- Check for sciatic nerve damage: exar-nine foot dorsiflexion and below- knee sensation\n- Diagnosis: Order AP or lateral view radiograph Complications: Sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the femoral head and secondary OA\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 41-year-old female presents with headaches, visual disturbances, and nausea. Fundoscopy reveals the image shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely underlying condition?\n- **A)** Retinal Detachment\n- **B)** Central Retinal Artery Occlusion\n- **C)** Intracranial Hypertension\n- **D)** Optic Neuritis\n- **E)** Diabetic Retinopathy\n- Explanation\n- The image shows papilledema, which is often associated with increased intracranial pressure, as seen in intracranial hypertension. This condition can present with symptoms like headaches, visual disturbances, and nausea.\n- Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (UH)\n- Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) is a disorder of increased intracranial pressure in an alert and orientated patient. The most popular hypothesis is that IIH is a syndrome of reduced cerebrospinal fluid absorption.\n- IIH, also known as pseudotumor cerebri, is a disorder of increased intracranial pressure that occurs mainly in overweight women of childbearing years, often in the setting of weight cause is unknown. It is characterised by increased intracranial pressure and its associated signs and symptoms in an alert and orientated patient but without localising neurological findings\n\n- female sex\n- weight gain\n- using medications - nalidixic acid, nitrofurantoin, ketoprofen or indometacin (in Bartter's syndrome), vitamin A intoxication, isotretinoin, all-trans retinoic acid, thyroid replacement therapy in hypothyroid children, lithium, and anabolic steroids (which may cause venous sinus thrombosis).\n- associated causal diseases - such as meningitis, sequel to subarachnoid haemorrhage, middle ear or mastoid-otitic hydrocephalus), bilateral radical neck dissections, superior vena cava syndrome, or increased right heart pressure. IIH may also be associated with endocrine conditions such as Addison's disease, hypoparathyroidism, obesity, and corticosteroid withdrawal\n- Clinical features\n- visual field loss\n- headache\n- transient visual obscurations - Episodes of transient visual loss usually last for <30 seconds and are followed by full restoration of vision\n- pulse-synchronous tinnitus\n- Photophobia\n- Retrobulbar pain optical disc swelling - Care must be taken to differentiate true papilloedema from pseudopapilloedema. Optic disc oedema is the cause of the loss of vision in IIH.\n- decreased visual acuity\n\n- Investigation\n- visual field testing (perimetry) — reveals visual field defects; enlarged blind spot, inferonasal loss, other nerve fibre bundle defects, or constriction of the field.\n- dilated fundoscopy — shows papilloedema. Frisén grading stages papilloedema from stage O (normal optical disc) to stage 5 (severe). Grades 3 and greater are risk factors for poor visual outcome.\n- visual acuity test - usually normal, or near normal, in patients with papilloedema, except when the condition is long-standing and severe, or if there is a neurosensory detachment. MRI of brain with or without contrast\n- lumbar puncture at spinal 1-3/1.4\n- other investigations to be considered\n- magnetic resonance venogram of head\n- optical coherence tomography\n- Management\n- Manage-the-rsik-fagtprs-that-may-ingr.ease-intragraniaLpressur.e\n- In overweight patients - a weight-reduction programme can be instituted in overweight patients.\n- other presumed causal factors - should be eliminated or treated (e.g., discontinue tetracyclines, retinoids, or excessive vitamin A; or, for corticosteroid withdrawal, restart corticosteroid therapy and taper more slowly).\n- Patients are advised to follow a low-sodium diet and to only drink when thirsty (instead of forcing water intake).\n- Prug-therapy\n\n- Acetazolamide - 500 mg orally twice daily initially, increase by 250 mg/day increments every 4 days according to response, maximum 4000 mg/day.\n- Furosemide - 20-40 mg orally two to three times daily\n- Acetazolamide can also be given with furosemide in cases of refractory disease, but close monitoring of serum potassium is necessary.\n- Analgesia for headache\n- amitriptyline: 10-50 mg orally once daily at bedtime\n- OR\n- naproxen: 250-500 mg orally twice daily when required, maximum 1250 mg/day (maximum 2 days per week)\n- alternative option, dihydroergotamine: 1 mg intravenously initially, repeat in 1 hour if no improvement, maximum 2 mg/total dose and 6 mg/week\n- and\n- metoclopramide: 5-10 mg intravenously every 8 hours when required for a maximum of 5 days, maximum 30 mg/day\n- Consider cerebrospinal fluid shunting - Various shunting procedures have been used for intractable headache or failed medical therapy, including lumbar subarachnoid-peritoneal, cisternoatrial, ventriculoatrial, ventriculojugular, and ventriculoperitoneal shunts.\n- Management of progressive visual loss - If patients lose vision in spite of full medical therapy, surgical treatment by optic nerve sheath fenestration or cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) shunting can be done. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 30-year-old male brought to the ED with thoracic trauma. On examination, there is decreased in breath sound on the right side of chest.\n\nHis observations are: BP is  mmHg HR: 110 bpm RR 25 breath/min You suspect tension pneumothorax which of the following features suggest tension pneumothorax?\n- **A)** Dull percussion note\n- **B)** hyperresonant percussion note\n- **C)** Bradycardia\n- **D)** Trachea towards the side Of injury\n- **E)** Increase in venous return Explanation\n- Clinical features of tension pneumothorax\n- Tachypnea, Tachycardia, hypotension, distended neck vein\n- Contralateral tracheal deviation\n- Unilateral hyper-resonant on percussion and absence of breath sound\n- Cyanosis (late manifestation)  -3\n	\N	[]	\N	f	17	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
8320d32a-9bf5-443a-a0a7-4ca04bb8a1de	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-2	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 2	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 2\n\ne VIRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA   Overview . Comments\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?   A Biliary Colic\n- **B)** Pancreatitis   C Acute Cholangitis\n- **D)** Intestinal obstruction\n- **E)** Cholecystitis\n- Explanation\n- Acute-cholangitis\n- Charcot triad —\n- jaundice (total bilirubin> 34 pmol/L),\n- Fever > ,\n- Pain - right upper quadrant pain/epigastric pain\n- Ryenold's pentad — it includes\n- Charcot triad + altered mental status and hypotension secondary to septic shock\n\n- Biliary colic — complication of gall stone. Liver enzymes are in normal range and no jaundice.\n- Ch-Qlecystitis — patient presents with right upper quadrant pain associated with fever/chills.\n- Examination-revealed presence of murphy sign\n- Lab - Liver enzymes are normal/mildly elevated, and jaundice presents up to 10% patients.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 10-month-old child brought into the ED by his mother. she describes him having 30 seconds episode of unconsciousness and stated that he stopped breathing. On reaching ED when you see child is active, well and play normally. What is the most likely diagnosis?   A Child abuse\n- **B)** Congenital heart disease   C Choking\n- **D)** N.A.I\n\nExplanation\n- BRUE/ALTE: Is an episode in an infant less-than-12-monttE0f age characterized by:\n- minute duration (typically 20-30s)\n- A return to baseline state\n- Not explained by any identifiable medical condition (i.e., medically well).\n- Includes one or more of the following features:\n- Central cyanosis/pallor\n- Absent, decrease or irregular breathing\n- Marked change in tone (hyper- or hypotonia)\n- Altered level of consciousness\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 43-year-old male patient presents with one day history of fresh hematemesis. He has had 3to 4 episodes of vomit, each consisting of a mug full of fresh blood. He is alcohol dependent with documented alcoholic cirrhosis and known to hepatology. His observations are: Respiratory rate 24 breath/min sp02 95% on air Heart rate 116 bpm BP  mmHg\n\nWeight 85 kg his blood result is given. Which of the following initial intravenous fluid is the most appropriate for this patient?\n- **A)** 20 ml/kg 0.9% saline\n- **B)** 500-1000 mi Hartman's C 500-1000 ml 0.9% saline\n- **D)** 30 ml/kg Hartman's\n- **E)** 10 ml/kg 10% dextrose\n- Explanation\n- Hypovolemic [Hypervolemic Hyponatremia\n- Hyponatremia (Na<130mmol/L) is a common finding in cirrhosis\n- The first step in treatrnent is to determine whether the patient is hypovolemic or hypervolemic\n- Hypovolemic hyponatremia is less typical and presents as a patient with Iow serum sodium in the absence of edema and ascites. This is\n\noften secondary to overdiuresis and the first step in management is to withhold diuretics and administration of normal saline (500-1000mI).\n- Avoid large volume of fluid in this patient.\n- Hypervolemic hyponatremia is the production of oversecretion of antidiuretic hormone leading to an unbalanced retention of water and sodium. The primary treatment of hypervolemic hyponatremia is free water restriction (<1000ml/d).\n- There are no data to support saline administration in this setting. The European Association for the Study of the Liver guidelines support the use of albumin in this situation to serve as potential volume expander to decrease RAAS activity based on B2 practice guidelines.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 38-year-old after falling from a window presents to ED with complain of pain in right shoulder. X-ray was done which showed anterior dislocation of shoulder. You decided to relocate the shoulder and sedation by propofol in ER Room. There is another patient who needs for the room. What is your next action?\n- **A)** Discharge the patient immediately\n- **B)** Discharge the patient when pain subside, and all the vitals retained to the baseline\n- **C)** Shift to the Ward\n- **D)** Don't discharge the patient any way\n- **E)** Wait for relative of patient to arrive and then discharge Explanation\n-\n- Discharge criteria after procedural sedation & analgesia : Patient has stable vital signs and oxygen saturation level\n- Patient's swallow, cough and gag reflexes are present\n- Patient is alert or appropriate to baseline\n- Patient can sit unaided if appropriate to baseline and procedure\n- Patient can walk without/with assistance if appropriate to baseline and\n- procedure Nausea and dizziness are minimal or hydration is adequate\n- Adequate analgesia\n-\n- Discharge order and instructions has been written by physician Responsible adult to accompany patient if discharged\n-\n- Appropriate follow up has been arranged\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 24-year-old male previous N.K.C.M Came into ED with Complain of Generalized fatigue, nausea, and abdominal pain in the right upper quadrant with deep jaundice.\n\nHis Observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 98bpm Temp alf Sp02 98% on air His lab is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?   A Viral hepatitis\n- **B)** Biliary Colic\n- **C)** Acute Pancreatitis\n- **D)** Gilbert Syndrome\n- **E)** Cholecystitis\n- Explanation\n- Alanine aminotransferase (AL T) raised more than IO times the upper limit of normal may indicate acute viral hepatitis.\n\n- Biliary colic — complication of gall stone. Liver enzymes are in normal range and no jaundice.\n- Cholecystitis — patient presents with right upper quadrant pain associated with fever/chills.\n- Examination-revealed presence of murphy sign\n- Lab - Liver enzymes are normal/mildly elevated, and jaundice presents up to 10% patients.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 7-year-old child brought into the ED with burn injury at the level of arm, he has severe pain. What is the drug of choice for his immediate pain management?\n- **A)** Oral codeine\n- **B)** NSAID\n- **C)** IV morphine\n- **D)** Intranasal diamorphine\n- **E)** IV Ketamine\n- Explanation\n\nAssess pain severity Use splints/slings/dressings etc Consider other causes of distress* For procedures consider regional blocks and conscious sedation MILD PAIN (1-3) Oral}rectal paracetamol 20 mg/kg loading dose, then 15 mg/kg 4-6 hourly Oral ibuug_ten 10 mg/kg 6-8 hourly MODERATE PAIN (4-6) As for mild pain plus Oral/rectal diGlQtenac 1 mgJkg 8 hourly (unless already had ibuprofen) Oral 1 mg/kg 4-6 hourly (over 12 years) OR Oral 02-0.5 mg'kg stat SEVERE PAIN (7-10) Consider Entonox as holding measure Intranasal 0.2 (20.1 rng/kg) (see table) followed by / or IV O. I -02 mg,kg Supplemented by oral analgesics\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 20-year-old male came to the ED with heavy nasal bleeding after traumatic injury. After initial assessment, you have decided to do cautery. What is the most common complication of cauterization?\n- **A)** Infection\n- **B)** Sinusitis\n- **C)** Bleeding   D Septal Perforation\n- **E)** Haernatoma\n- Explanation\n- Apossible-complica.tion-of-nasal-cautery-treatment;\n- septal perforation\n- Epistaxis (nosebleeds)\n- Definition\n- In most cases (80-95%), epistaxis originates from Little's area on the anterior nasal septum, which contains the Kiesselbach plexus of\n- Less commonly, epistaxis originates from branches of the sphenopalatine artery in the posterior nasal cavity.\n- Posterior nosebleeds usually occur in older people, are more profuse, result in bleeding from both nostrils, and the bleeding site cannot be identified on examination.\n- Most epistaxis is self-limiting and harmless, and the cause of damage to the blood vessels is not identified.\n- Cause\n\nTrauma — injury from nose-picking, nasal fractures, septal ulcers or\n- perforations, foreign body, or blunt trauma (such as falls in children). Inflammation — infection (for example chronic sinusitis), allergic\n- rhinosinusitis, or nasal polyps. Topical drugs — for example cocaine, decongestants, or\n- corticosteroids. Vascular causes — for example hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia\n- or granulomatosis with polyangitis (previously known as Wegener's granulomatosis). Post-operative bleeding — for example following ear, nose, and throat\n- surgery; maxillofacial surgery; or ophthalmic surgery. Tumours — benign (such as angiofibroma — a rare nasopharnygeal\n- turnour largely affecting adolescent males) or malignant (such as squamous cell carcinoma — more likely in older people) Haematological conditions affecting clotting — such as\n- thrombocytopenia, platelet dysfunction, Von Willebrand disease, leukaemia, and haemophilia. Environmental factors — temperature, humidity, altitude, exposure to\n- irritants such as dust, certain chemicals, and cigarette smoke. Systemic drugs — including anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs (for example aspirin and clopidogrel).\n- Management Acute-epistaxis if the patient is haemodynamically unstable\n- Follow ABCD protocol Call for help immediately\n-\n\n- Seek senior or ENT help early in children and elderly patients with severe bleeding as these patients may require aggressive resuscitation and specialist input:\n- apply nasal first aid (Sit with their upper body tilted forward and their mouth open,They should avoid lying down, unless they are feeling faint and/or Pinch the cartilaginous (soft) part of the nose firmly and hold it for 10—15 minutes without releasing the pressure).\n- if the patient-is-haemodynamicaoystable\n\n- Consider applying a topical anti\n- c re ration prescribe Naseptin\n- (chlorhexidine and neomycin)\n- If patientallergic to naseptin then prescribe mupirocin nasal ointment Recurrent epistaxis Yes First aid measure Sit with their upper body tilted forward and their mouth open. Pinch the cartilaginous (soft) part Of the nose firmly and hold it for minutes\n- Bleedingstops with first aid measures If first aid measure fails then consider: the bleeding point can be seen and the procedure can be tolerated (eg in adults and older children)\n- Nasal packing — if nasal cautery is ineffective or the bleeding point cannot Epistaxis resolved Offer selfcare advise Offer topical antisepticpreparation (as mentioned above) Arrange\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Initial measure same as acute epistaxis, however consider referral to ENT or paediatrician in children. Key_points To cauterize: Identify the bleeding point — it will look like a small red dot (less than 1 mm) and may not be actively bleeding. Only cauterize one side of the septum to avoid nasal septal perforation.\n\nAvoid touching areas which do not need treatment (for example facial\n- skin). Nasal packing products include:\n- Nasal tampons (for example Merocel ) — effective and easy to use.\n- Inflatable packs (for example Rapid-Rhino ) — effective and may be\n- easier and more comfortable to insert and remove than nasal tampons.\n- Ribbon gauze impregnated with Vaseline or bismuth-iodoform paraffin paste. Complications\n- Rare complications of nasal bleeding include:\n- Anaemia\n- Aspiration from dislodgement or malpositioning (especially with posterior epistaxis).\n- Hypovolaemia Death\n- Potential complications of nasal packing treatment may include:\n- Sinusitis\n- Septal haematoma or abscess (due to traumatic packing).\n- Pressure necrosis (secondary to excessively tight packing).\n- Toxic shock syndrome (from prolonged packing).\n- Apnoeic episodes (associated with bilateral anterior or posterior nasal packs).\n\n& possible complication of nasal cautery treatment:\n- septal perforation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 50-year-old male known case of HTN came into ED with complain of loss of weakness in right side of body which lasted only for 2 hours. He gives you a history of similar episodes 2-3 times earlier. According to DVLA guidelines, what will be your advice, when he can drive a car?\n- **A)** 3 months   B\n- 5 months\n- **C)** 2 months\n- **D)** 1 month\n- **E)** 4 months\n- Explanation\n\n- Stroke, TIA or mini- stroke, This includes amaurosis fugax or retinal artery fugax\n- Multiple TIAs/ recurrent TIA Group— A Car or Motorcycle Must not drive for I month\n- No need to notify DVLA Must not drive for 3 months\n- Must notify DVLA Group— B Bus or lorry Must not drive for I years\n- Must notify DVLA Same as above\n-\n- Patients with recurrent TIAs or strokes will be required to undergo functional cardiac testing.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 69-year-old man presents with a two-day history of vomiting and lethargy. He is a type-2 diabetic and known hypertensive. His observations are: RR 22 breath/min sp02 on air Heart rate 104bpm  Temp 37.8'C His VBG and blood glucose is given\n\nWhat is the next best step in this patient's immediate management?   A\n- Magnesium IV\n- **B)** Calcium Resonium orally\n- **C)** Calcium chloride IV D\n- Repeat the blood sample\n- **E)** Insulin and dextrose IV\n- Explanation Potassium result doesn't fit the clinical picture, it is important to repeat\n- this, as patient clinical picture more fit to hypokalaemia. Causes of pseudohyperkalemia\n- prolonged transit time to the laboratory or poor storage conditions\n- (most common)\n- difficult venepuncture,\n- A high platelet count, haemolysis,\n- erythrocytosis,\n- prolonged storage time of clotted samples, or cold storage conditions\n-\n\n- sampling from the arm into which potassium-containing fluids are being infused\n- Recognition-otpseudohyperkalaemia\n- Pseudo-hyperkalaemia is detected when the serum K+ level exceeds that of the plasma by more than 0.4 mmol/L\n- Consider pseudo-hyperkalaemia in the context of normal renal function, normal ECG and in patients with haematological disorders.\n- Send paired blood samples in a clotted tube (serum) and a lithium heparin tube (plasma).\n- Send FBC to exclude a haematological disorder.\n- Pseudo-hyperkalaemia is present if: [Serum 1<+1 — [Plasma K+] > 0.4 mmol/l. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 22-year-old male who is a known to be Insulin dependent DM presented to the ED with polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss. Intravenous access has been obtained. His blood tests are given. What is the anion gap in this patient?\n\nO\n- **A)** 34.0\n- **B)** 38.2   c. 33\n- **D)** 65.4\n- **E)** 44.2\n- Explanation\n- Anion Cap = (Na+ + K+) - (Cl- + HC03-) 135 + 4.2 - 95 +6 38.2 previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	18	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7452ee95-73ae-4fa9-ac72-b81d7b7fb491	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-20	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 20	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 20\n\nA Salter-Harris type-I\n- **B)** Weber-A fracture\n- **C)** Salter-Harris type-II   D\n- Salter-Harris type-IV\n- **E)** Salter-Harris type-III EXPIanation;\n- X-ray shows vertical lucency extends through the medial aspect of the distal tibial metaphysis and epiphysis consistent with non-displaced Salter-Harris type IV fracture.\n\nly-P-e.:LOCCUr when the epiphysis separates from the metaphysis or fracture plane passes all the way through the growth plate, not involving the bone. common)fracture line extends from physis to metaphysis line e)dends from physis to epiphysis line extends from metaphysis, growth plate and down through the epiphysis upg&Crushing injury to the physis\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nIn infants, intraosseous access should avoid which part of the bone?\n- **A)** Epiphysis\n- **B)** Diaphysis   C Metaphysis\n- **D)** Proximal tibia\n- **E)** Mid bone Explanation;\n- The needle should avoid epiphysis during intraosseous insertion as this can traumatize the growth plate.\n- The growth plate is located on the epiphysis; thus, this area should be avoided.\n- Extravasation is a very common complication of this route of fluid administration.\n- The extremity must be checked because compartment syndrome is a known complication. Infection is a very rare complication but cellulitis may result at the skin site.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 41 -year-old female presents with headaches, visual disturbances, and nausea. Fundoscopy reveals the image shown. Which of the following is the most likely underlying condition?\n- **A)** Retinal Detachment\n- **B)** Optic Neuritis\n- **C)** Diabetic Retinopathy\n- **D)** Intracranial Hypertension\n- **E)** Central Retinal Artery Occlusion\n- Explanation\n- The image shows papilledema, which is often associated with increased intracranial pressure, as seen in intracranial hypertension. This condition can present with symptoms like headaches, visual disturbances, and nausea.\n- Idiopathic intracranial hypertension\n- Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) is a disorder of increased intracranial pressure in an alert and orientated patient. The most popular hypothesis is that IIH is a syndrome of reduced cerebrospinal fluid absorption.\n- IIH, also known as pseudotumor cerebri, is a disorder of increased intracranial pressure that occurs mainly in overweight women of childbearing years, often in the setting of weight cause is unknown. It is characterised by increased intracranial pressure and its associated signs and symptoms in an alert and orientated patient but without localising neurological findings\n\n### Risk factors\n\n- female sex\n- weight gain\n- using medications - nalidixic acid, nitrofurantoin, ketoprofen or\n- indometacin (in Bartter's syndrome), vitamin A intoxication, isotretinoin, all-trans retinoic acid, thyroid replacement therapy in hypothyroid children, lithium, and anabolic steroids (which may cause venous sinus thrombosis). associated causal diseases - such as meningitis, sequel to\n- subarachnoid haemorrhage, middle ear or mastoid-otitic hydrocephalus), bilateral radical neck dissections, superior vena cava syndrome, or increased right heart pressure. IIH may also be associated with endocrine conditions such as Addison's disease, hypoparathyroidism, obesity, and corticosteroid withdrawal Clinical features\n- visual field loss\n- headache\n- transient visual obscurations - Episodes of transient visual loss usually\n- last for <30 seconds and are followed by full restoration of vision pulse-synchronous tinnitus\n-\n- Photophobia Retrobulbar pain\n- optical disc swelling - Care rnust be taken to differentiate true\n- papilloedema from pseudopapilloedema. Optic disc oedema is the cause of the loss of vision in IIH. decreased visual acuity\n-\n\n- Investigation visual field testing (perimetry) — reveals visual field defects; enlarged\n- blind spot, inferonasal loss, other nerve fibre bundle defects, or constriction of the field.\n- dilated fundoscopy — shows papilloedema. Frisén grading stages papilloedema from stage O (normal optical disc) to stage 5 (severe). Grades 3 and greater are risk factors for poor visual outcome. visual acuity test - usually normal, or near normal, in patients with\n- papilloedema, except when the condition is long-standing and severe, or if there is a neurosensory detachment.\n- MRI of brain with or without contrast lumbar puncture at spinal L3/L4\n- other investigations to be considered\n- magnetic resonance venogram of head\n- optical coherence tomography\n- Management\n- Manage the rsik factors that may increase intracranial pressure\n- In overweight patients - a weight-reduction programme can be\n- instituted in overweight patients. other presumed causal factors - should be eliminated or treated (e.g.,\n- discontinue tetracyclines, retinoids, or excessive vitamin A; or, for corticosteroid withdrawal, restart corticosteroid therapy and taper more slowly).\n\nPatients are advised to follow a low-sodium diet and to only drink when\n- thirsty (instead of forcing water intake).\n- Drug-therapy Acetazolamide - 500 mg orally twice daily initially, increase by 250\n- mg/day increments every 4 days according to response, maximum 4000 mg/day. Furosemide - 20-40 mg orally two to three times daily\n- Acetazolamide can also be given with furosemide in cases of refractory\n- disease, but close monitoring of serum potassium is necessary. Analgesia for headache\n- amitriptyline: 10-50 mg orally once daily at bedtime\n- OR naproxen: 250-500 mg orally twice daily when required, maximum 1250\n- mg/day (maximum 2 days per week) alternative option, dihydroergotamine: I mg intravenously initially,\n- repeat in I hour if no improvement, maximum 2 mg/total dose and 6 mg/week and\n- metoclopramide: 5-10 mg intravenously every 8 hours when required\n- for a maximum of 5 days, maximum 30 mg/day Consider cerebrospinal fluid shunting - Various shunting procedures\n- have been used for intractable headache or failed medical therapy, including lumbar subarachnoid-peritoneal, cisternoatrial, ventriculoatrial, ventriculojugular, and ventriculoperitoneal shunts.\n\n- Management of progressive visual loss - If patients lose vision in spite of full medical therapy, surgical treatrnent by optic nerve sheath fenestration or cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) shunting can be done.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nYou haven't put a femoral line before but you have seen being performed. Some causalities are on the way. Your consultant asks you to put a femoral line and goes to handle another case. Equipment are all ready and you have a competent nurse. What would be your step?\n- **A)** Browse through your notes, read and be competent and do under IJ/S guidance\n- **B)** Ask anesthetic registrar to observe you while you do it C\n- Inform consultant that you haven't done it before\n- **D)** Ask nurse to pass femoral line\n- **E)** Ask anesthetic registrar to do it while you do other things\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> - GMC Good Medical Practice - You must recognise and work within the limits of your competence.\n- RCEM standards emphasise patient safety first and escalation when tasks exceed your skill level. By informing your consultant, alternative safe arrangements (supervision or referral) can be made.\n- GMC (Generic Medical Council standards)\n- Be competent in all aspects of your work, including clinical duties, teaching, research, leadership, and management where relevant.\n- Recognise and work within the limits of your competence. Practise only under appropriate supervision for your role, skills, and training.\n- Keep up to date with guidelines, evidence, and developments that affect your work.\n- Follow the law, CMC professional standards, and other regulations relevant to medical practice.\n- Maintain adequate knowledge of English to provide safe and effective care in the I-JK.\n- NOTE - question has been revised based on current 2024 guideline.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 3-year-old child presents with severe hypotension. Peripheral IV access cannot be obtained. You've decided to get intraosseous. Which of the following would be the correct insertion site?\n- **A)** Palpate 2-3cm below the external condyle, and just medial to midline\n- **B)** prominent aspect of the greater tubercle, 1 to 2 cm above the surgical neck\n- **C)** Proximal tibia 2-3cm below and medial to tibial tuberosity\n- **D)** Distal tibia 3 cm proximal to the most prominent aspect of the lateral malleolus\n- **E)** Proximal tibia 2-3cm above and medial to tibial tuberosity\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> Bone Proximal Humerus (first choice) Distal Femur Proximal Tibia Distal Tibia Insertion site\n- The insertion site is on the most prominent aspect of the greater tubercle, 1 to 2 cm above the surgical neck.\n- Palpate 2-3cm above external condyle, Insertion just medial to midline to avoid tendon\n- Palpate tibial tuberosity, Insert 2- 3cm below + medial to tibial tuberosity\n- Insertion site is located approx. 3 cm proximal to the most prominent aspect of the medial malleolus Angle\n- Aim the needle tip downward at a 450 angle to the horizontal plane\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone Age\n- Both adult and paediatric (>6 year)\n- Only in years age\n- Both adult and paediatric\n- Both adult and paediatric\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nAssess cognitive functions as per AMT4. Which of the following is a key component of this assessment?\n- **A)** Recognize two familiar persons\n- **B)** Solve a basic arithmetic problem\n- **C)** Name five common objects\n- **D)** Spell a simple word backwards\n- **E)** Ask the person for the date of an important event\n- Explanation\n- When referring to the "date of an important event" in a cognitive assessment context, it generally involves asking the individual to recall and state the date of an event that holds personal significance to them. This could include: DOB, Anniversary, National or public holidays etc.\n- 4AT - Screening for Delirium & Cognitive Impairment\n- The 4AT is a screening instrument designed for rapid initial assessment of delirium and cognitive impairment.\n- Screening: for all people over 65 (all over 45 ATSI) or all with known predisposing factors and all with known related conditions.\n- as part of routine risk screening on presentation or admission (within 2 hours if there are behavioural or cognitive issues evident, or 8 hours if not).\n- re-screen if new behaviour or cognitive issues become evident or change significantly.\n\nAMT4(Abb revi ated Mental Test4) ATTENTION ACUTE CHANGE OR 4AT screening Normal (fully alert, but not agitated, throughout assessment) Mild sleepiness for < IO seconds after waking, then normal Clearly abnormal\n- Ask the person to tell you their: Age, date of birth,\n- Place (name Of the hospital or building), Current year. NO mistake in telling 1 mistake 2 or more mistakes/untestable Ask the person to tell you the months of the year backwards, e.g December, November, October: Achieves 7 months or more correct Starts but scores < 7 months / refuses to start Untestable (cannot start because unwell, drowsy, inattentive) No Score 4 2 4 FLUCTUATING COURSE\n- Score 4 or above: possible delirium +1- cognitive impairment 1-3: possible cognitive impairment\n- O: delirium or severe cognitive impairment unlikely (butdelirium still possible if (41 information incomplete)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 46-year-old male brought to the ED by paramedics, with a history of fall near pedestrian crossing.. His observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 104bpm\n\nHis lab is shown. what is our management in this patient?\n- **A)** 1M Adrenaline 0.5mg   B\n- IV hydrocortisone\n- **C)** Fludrocortisone   D\n- IV Desmopressin\n- **E)** Phenoxybenzamine Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggests adrenal crisis\n- Intravenous hydrocortisone should be given to patients with a suspected but unconfirmed diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency.\n- Saline should be administered to correct hypotension and dehydration. It is usually necessary to administer I L rapidly, and a further 4 to 6 L over the first 24 hours, to correct hypotension.\n- Glucose should be administered when necessary to correct hypoglycaemia, but care should be taken to avoid worsening hyponatraemia Addison's disease\n\n- Primary adrenal caused by destruction of the adrenal cortex.\n- Causes - reduced production of glucocorticoids (such as cortisol), mineralocorticoids (such as aldosterone), and adrenal androgens (such as dehydroepiandrosterone).\n- The absence of cortisol leads to increased production of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) because negative feedback to the pituitary gland is reduced.\n- when pituitary ACTH production is insufficient; this leads to reduced cortisol production and adrenal atrophy.\n- Causes z include intracranial disorders, such as pituitary tumours, subarachnoid haemorrhage, and traumatic brain injury.\n- Tertiary adrenal insufficiency_=occurs when hypothalamic production of corticotropin-releasing hormone is disrupted and production of ACTH from the anterior pituitary is insufficient.\n- Causes - long-term administration of corticosteroids and less commonly tumours, radiotherapy, or surgery affecting the hypothalamus.\n- Causes\n- Auto-immune adrenalitis (most common)\n\nCongenital adrenal hyperplasia (the rnost common cause of primary\n- adrenal insufficiency in children) and other genetic causes such as adrenoleukodystrophy. Infections, such as tuberculosis, meningococcus, haemopholus\n- influenzae, cryptococcosis, cytomegalovirus, and HIV.\n- Adrenal metastases. Amyloidosis.\n- Haemochromatosis.\n- Iatrogenic causes such as bilateral adrenalectomy, adrenal\n- haemorrhage (for example from anticoagulant treatment), and use of immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies in the treatment of cancer.\n- Clinical features\n- A person with Addison's disease may present with a sudden crisis precipitated by intercurrent infection or stress. Features of Addison's disease include:\n- Weakness and lethargy\n-\n- Hypotension (notably orthostatic hypotension) Nausea and vomiting\n- Weight loss\n- Reduced axillary and pubic hair\n- Depression Hyperpigmentation (palmar creases, buccal mucosa and\n- exposed areas more commonly affected) The classical biochemical features of Addison's disease are as\n- follows:\n\n- Increased ACTH levels (rise in an attempt to stimulate the adrenal glands) Hyponatraemia\n- Hyperkalaemia Hypercalcaemia\n- Hypoglycaemia\n- Metabolic acidosis\n- Addison's disease is associated with an increased incidence of the following conditions:\n- Type I diabetes mellitus (not type II)\n- Hashimoto's thyroiditis\n- Grave's disease\n- Premature ovarian failure\n- Pernicious anaemia\n- Vitiligo . Alopecia\n- Investigations\n- Serum cortisol level - ideally be obtained at 8—9 am\n- Less than 100 nanomol/L — admit the person to hospital, adrenal insufficiency is highly likely.\n- Blood glucose\n- Plasma adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) (Synacthen ) test— to confirm the diagnosis.\n\nPlasma renin and aldosterone levels — renin levels are typically high\n- and aldosterone levels Iow in Addison's disease. In secondary adrenal insufficiency, the renin-angiotensin system can function normally.\n- Serum DHEA-S — typically Iow in Addison's disease. Thyroid function tests. Autoantibody levels — adrenal cortex autoantibodies or antibodies\n- against 21-hydroxylase are present in more than 80% of people with recent-onset autoimmune adrenalitis.\n- Management Adrenal crisis\n- Intravenous hydrocortisone should be given to patients with a suspected but unconfirmed diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency. Saline should be administered to correct hypotension and dehydration. It is usually necessary to administer 1 L rapidly, and a further 4 to 6 L over the first 24 hours, to correct hypotension. Glucose should be administered when necessary to correct hypoglycaemia. but care should be\n- taken to avoid worsening hyponatraemia Acute episode Minor intercurrent stress Patients with primary adrenal insufficiency experiencing minor intercurrent stress (e.g., febrile illness; minor procedure/surgery not requiring fasting such as tooth extraction Or local anaesthesia) should be instructed to double their chronic maintenance dose of corticosteroid on the day of the procedure Or for the duration Of illness Severe intercurrent stress intravenous hydrocortisone (stress dose) - Patients with primary adrenal insufficiency who undergo severe Stress situations (e.g., unable to take oral glucocorticoid, such as acute gastroententis or prolonged fasting for colonoscopy; surgery under general or; major trauma; active phase of labour and delivery). stable and/or after treatment of acute episode Glucocorticoid and Mineralocorticoid\n- Oral glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement therapy is given in physiological doses for life.\n- Short-acting glucocorticoids such as hydrocortisone are preferred by most physicians. If hydrocortisone is not available, alternative glucocorticoids are cortisone and prednisolone\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 24-year-old woman presents after 12 hours history of vomiting after binge drink. She is now complaining of epigastric and interscapular pain. Her respiratory rate is 25 breath per minutes. Her CXR shown. What is most likely diagnosis ?   A Achalasia\n- **B)** Peptic ulcer disease\n- **C)** Referred cardiac pain\n- **D)** Oesophagitis   E\n- OesOphageaI rupture Explanation\n- Esophageal rupture secondary to vomiting called Boerhaave's syndrome\n\nDefinition Clinical features Investigations Management Mallory-Weiss Tear Mallory-Weiss tear, also known as Mallory- Weiss syndrome, is a longitudinal mucosal tear or laceration of the mucous membrane in the region of the gastroesophageal junction and gastric cardia. Vomiting Retching Hematemesis Epigastric pain Endoscopy confirm diagnosis Resuscitation & supportive care. Consider anti-emetic Endoscopic intervention for continual bleed Boerhaave syndrome It due to the rupture of the esophagus due to forceful emesis after excessive food or alcohol consumptions. Esophageal transmural tear Sudden, severe chest pain. Nausea and vomiting. Pain or coughing when swallowing. Short, rapid breathing. Swelling in the tissues Of the chest cavity with air or fluid. Hard, rigid abdomen. Signs of infection like fever and sweating. Esophagram CT CXR findings Pneumomediastinum Left sided pleural effusion Pneumothorax Subcutaneous emphysema lv fluid & antibiotics Surgical repair. Ideally, the rupture should be repaired directly within 24 hours.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 41 -year-old female presented to the ED with fever, sore throat and difficulty in swallowing. On further assessment, it has been discovered that she has been talking carbimazole for her thyroid condition. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Throat swab\n\nB\n- Urine culture\n- **C)** IV antibiotics\n- **D)** Full blood count (FBC)\n- **E)** Blood culture Explanation\n- If the person is taking carbimazole or propylthiouracil, advise them to: Seek urgent medical advice if they develop possible symptoms of agranulocytosis or neutropenia such as fever, sore throat, mouth ulcers, febrile or non-specific illness, bruising, or malaise, and to stop the medication immediately. Arrange an urgent blood test for differential white cell count to exclude agranulocytosis and neutropenia.  IO. A 58-year-old woman presents to the ED with sudden onset of breathlessness. An x-ray revealed a large left-sided pneumothorax and a Seldinger intercostal chest drain was successfully inserted one hour ago. She is now more unwell. Her observation is now Temp: 36.5C\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- HR: 130bpm BP: 80/40mmHg RR: 28 per minute sp02: 96% What is the most likely cause of her deterioration?\n- **A)** Recurrence of the pneumothorax\n- **B)** Division of an intercostal nerve   C, Injury Of an intercostal artery\n- **D)** Laceration of lung parenchyma\n- **E)** Perforation of the left ventricle of the heart Explanation Complications chest tube Haemothorax due to intercostal artery injury Haemoperitonium due to liver or spleen injury Persistent pneomthorax or pleural effusion due kinking or dislodgement of tube Infection Injury to intrathoracic organs such as heart, esophagus\n\nprevious  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	19	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
44881750-f8d0-4f6c-9b9f-20f974bb4f1f	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-21	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 21	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 21\n\nO\n- **B)** KCL\n- **D)** Calcium gluconate\n- **E)** Amiodarone Explanation;\n- Torsade de pointes here due to electrolyte imbalance: hypomagnesemia and hypocalcemia\n- Hypocalcaemia secondary to hypomagnesemia\n- Emergency treatment of torsade de pointes consists of removal of any torsadogenic stimulus and suppression of EADs(early after depolarization), which may include acceleration of heart rate to reduce the QT interval.\n- Magnesium sulphate suppresses torsade by decreasing the influx of calcium ions, which in turn results in decreased amplitude of EADs. The initial dose is 2 g (20 ml of 10% solution), given intravenously over one to two minutes. This can be repeated up to a total of 6 g, with 5—15 minutes between doses; care should be taken to avoid magnesium toxicity.\n- Initial treatment should also include the rapid correction of electrolytes including serum potassium concentrations, aiming for a target serum concentration of 5 mmol/l\n- In some circumstances, urgent transvenous pacing may be necessary to suppress arrhythmias by increasing the baseline heart rate to 100 to\n\n150 beats per minute. Temporary transcutaneous pacing has also been successfully used to treat torsade de pointes.\n- If the patient is unstable :\n- Immediate defibrillation - Technically the appropriate amount of energy is 2003 biphasic.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 20-year-old patient has died following isolated head trauma after an assault in prison. He has a history of HIV infection which he takes medication for appropriately and he moved in the UK six months ago. He has no next of kin and is not on the organ donation register. In this patient, what would exclude him from organ donation?\n- **A)** He has no next of kin\n- **B)** HIV infection\n- **C)** He has lived in the I-JK for less than 12 months\n- **D)** Not on organ donation register\n- **E)** He is a prisoner Explanation\n- This person is in exclude group of organ donation law in England.\n- People who have lived in England for less than 12 months before their death.\n\n- Organ donation law in England\n- it will be considered that you agree to becorne an organ donor when you die, if:\n- you are over 18 year\n- you have not opted out;\n- you are not in an excluded group\n- Excluded-group;-anybody in this grp will be excluded from organ donation\n- Those under the age of 18\n- People who lack the mental capacity to understand the new arrangements and take the necessary action\n- Visitors to England, and those not living here voluntarily\n- People who have lived in England for less than 12 months before their death\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 74-year-old male brought into the ED with a decreased level of consciousness. He has a history of disseminated lung carcinoma. His lab is shown After glucose what would be your next step?\n- **A)** 3% saline\n- **B)** Dextrose infusion\n\nIV hydrocortisone\n- **D)** Calcium gluconate\n- **E)** KCL\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Explanation;\n- Patient has adrenal crisis due to adrenal metastases (a cancer which has spread from another primary site to one or both adrenal glands)\n- The classical biochemical features of Addison's disease are as follows:\n- Increased ACTH levels (rise in an attempt to stimulate the adrenal glands)\n- Hyponatraemia\n- Hyperkalaemia\n- Hypercalcaemia\n- Hypoglycaemia . Metabolic acidosis Addison's disease\n- Primary adrenal caused by destruction of the adrenal cortex.\n- Causes - reduced production of glucocorticoids (such as cortisol), mineralocorticoids (such as aldosterone), and adrenal androgens (such as dehydroepiandrosterone).\n- The absence of cortisol leads to increased production of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) because negative feedback to the pituitary gland is reduced.\n\nSecondary-adrenal-insufficiency-zoccurs when pituitary ACTH production is insufficient; this leads to reduced cortisol production and adrenal atrophy.\n- Causes - include intracranial disorders, such as pituitary tumours, subarachnoid haemorrhage, and traumatic brain injury.\n- Tertiary adrenal insufficiency_=occurs when hypothalamic production of corticotropin-releasing hormone is disrupted and production of ACTH from the anterior pituitary is insufficient. Causes - long-term administration of corticosteroids and less commonly tumours, radiotherapy, or surgery affecting the hypothalamus. . Causes\n- Auto-immune adrenalitis (most common)\n- Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in children) and other genetic causes such as adrenoleukodystrophy.\n- Infections, such as tuberculosis, meningococcus, haemopholus influenzae, cryptococcosis, cytomegalovirus, and HIV.\n- Adrenal metastases (a cancer which has spread from another primary site to one or both adrenal glands)\n- Amyloidosis.\n- Haemochromatosis.\n- Iatrogenic causes such as bilateral adrenalectomy, adrenal haemorrhage (for example from anticoagulant treatment), and use of irnmune checkpoint inhibitor therapies in the treatment of cancer.\n\n- Clinical features\n- A person with Addison's disease may present with a sudden crisis precipitated by intercurrent infection or stress.\n- Features of Addison's disease include:\n- Weakness and lethargy Hypotension (notably orthostatic hypotension)\n- Nausea and vomiting\n- Weight loss\n- Reduced axillary and pubic hair Depression Hyperpigmentation (palmar creases, buccal mucosa and exposed areas more commonly affected)\n- The classical biochemical features of Addison's disease are as follows:\n- Increased ACTH levels (rise in an attempt to stimulate the adrenal glands)\n- Hyponatraemia\n- Hyperkalaemia\n- Hypercalcaemia\n- Hypoglycaemia\n- Metabolic acidosis\n- Addison's disease is associated with an increased incidence of the following conditions:\n- Type I diabetes mellitus (not type II)\n- Hashimoto's thyroiditis\n- Grave's disease\n- Premature ovarian failure\n- Pernicious anaemia\n- Vitiligo\n\n- Alopecia\n- Investigations\n- Serum cortisol level - ideally be obtained at 8—9 am\n- Less than 100 nanomol/L — admit the person to hospital, adrenal insufficiency is highly likely.\n- Blood glucose\n- Plasma adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) (Synacthen ) test to confirm the diagnosis.\n- Plasma renin and aldosterone levels — renin levels are typically high and aldosterone levels low in Addison's disease. In secondary adrenal insufficiency, the renin-angiotensin system can function normally.\n- Serum DHEA-S — typically low in Addison's disease.\n- Thyroid function tests.\n- Autoantibody levels — adrenal cortex autoantibodies or antibodies against 21-hydroxylase are present in more than 80% of people with recent-onset autoimmune adrenalitis.\n- Management\n\nAdrenal crisis Acute episode\n- Intravenous hydrocortisone Minor intercurrent stress should be given to patients with a Patients with primary adrenal Suspected but unconfirmed insufficiency experiencing minor diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency. intercurrent stress (e.g., febrile Saline should administered to illness; minor procedureJsurgery correct hypotension and not requiring fasting such as tooth dehydration, It is usually extraction or local anaesthesia) necessary to administer 1 L should be instructed to double rapidly. and a further 4 to 6 L over their chronic maintenance dose the first 24 hours, to correct of corticosteroid on the day of the procedure or for the duration of hypotension Glucose should be administered illness. when necessary to correct\n- severe intercurrent stress hypoglycaemia, but care should be\n- intravenous hydrocortisone taken to avoid worsening (stress dose) - patients With hyponatraemia primary adrenal insufficiency who undergo severe stress situations (e.g., unable to take oral glucocorticoid, such as acute gastroenteritis or prolonged fasting for colonoscopy; surgery under general or; major trauma; active phase of labour and delivery). stable and/or after treatment of acute episode Glucocorticoid and Mineralocorticoid\n- Oral glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement therapy is given in physiological doses for life.\n- Short-acting glucocorticoids such as hydrocortisone are preferred by most physicians. If hydrocortisone is not available, alternative are cortisone and prednisolone\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 6-month-old infant brought to the ED by his mother. On assessment he appears mottled, CRT>4 seconds. ECG shows SVT. What would be your next step Of management?\n- **A)** Amiodarone B\n- IV antibiotic\n- **C)** IV fluid\n- **D)** Synchronized DC shock\n- **E)** Adenosine Explanation;\n\n- Decompensated SVT initially, treated with synchronized cardioversion Ij/kg. Resuscitation Council UK Paediatric cardiac arrhythmias Signs of circulation? - help GUIDELINES —42021\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 70-year-old patient attends with sudden, painless visual loss in his left eye which occurred one hour ago He has a past medical history of hypertension and type-II diabetes. A photograph of his retina is shown What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Retinal detachment\n- **B)** Giant cell arteritis\n- **C)** Central retinal vein occlusion\n- **D)** Central retinal artery occlusion\n- **E)** Amaurosis fugax Explanation\n- Sudden painless visual loss, pale retina and cherry red spots are the features of CRAO.\n- Central retinal artery occlusion\n- Sudden painless vision loss in the affected eye\n- Commonly caused by emboli from atheromatous carotid arteries or thrombosis secondary to arteriosclerosis or hypertension and vasospasm secondary to giant cell arteritis\n- Affected eye pupil poorly reactive to the light with a normal counsel light reaction\n- Features Of CRAO\n- pale retina (due to oedema)\n- 'Cherry red spot' (sparing of the center of macula due to supply by underlying choroid)\n- Attenuated vessels\n- Segmentation of blood columns in arteries (cattle trucking) Optic atrophy develops over several weeks\n- Management\n\nUrgent referral to ophthalmologist\n- Patient can be benefited from digital ocular massage\n- IV acetazolamide and mannitol\n- Timolol eye drops\n- Sublingual NOT\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 70-year-old known alcoholic man, is brought to the ED after a massive GI bleed. A significant upper GI bleed transfusion protocol is triggered. Which of these is a complication specifically of massive transfusion?   A\n- Hypocalcemia\n- **B)** ABO incompatibility\n- **C)** Thrombotic events\n- **D)** Blood borne virus transmission\n- **E)** Metabolic acidosis Explanation Complications of massive transfusion\n- Hypothermia - is a complication of massive transfusion. The rapid infusion of cold blood products can lower body temperatures. The use of warmers can reduce this risk. Hyperkalemia - is a complication of massive transfusion. Stored blood products can accumulate higher concentrations Of potassium.\n\nHypokalemia cannot be attributed to massive transfusion.\n- Hypocalcemia and metabolic alkalosis - can be seen during or following massive transfusion due to sodium citrate that is added to blood for storage. Hypercalcemia is not associated with massive transfusion.\n- Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRAI-I) and transfusion- associated circulatory overload (TACO) can occur with massive transfusion as well.\n- Note - there is controversy as some candidates didn't get the score for hypocalcaemia in Feb 2022 MRCEM SBA. however, in September 2022 diet everyone has got the mark for hypocalcaemia\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 68-year-old female presents to the ED with her husband with a one hour history of right-sided weakness and aphasia. She has had three episodes of weakness lasting 30 minutes in the last week but did not want to come to the hospital. Type -II diabetes Hypertension On examination, she has dense right-sided hemiparesis and is aphasic with a receptive defect. CT head shows hyperdense MCA sign and no intracranial bleed. What is the most appropriate next management step?\n- **A)** Ask the on call stroke team to come urgently assess her for treatment options\n- **B)** Complete an adult with incapacity form and immediately thrombolyse the patient\n- **C)** Get consent from her husband to immediately thrombolyse the patient\n- **D)** Admit the patient to the medical receiving team for ongoing management\n- **E)** Refer the patient to the mental health team for an urgent assessment Explanation\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest\n- Mental Capacity Act (MCA)\n- The Mental Capacity Act (MCA) is designed to protect and empower people who may lack the mental capacity to make their own decisions about their care and treatment. It applies to people aged 16 and over.\n- Examples of people who may lack capacity include those with:\n- dementia\n- a severe learning disability\n- a brain injury\n- a mental health illness\n- a stroke\n\n- unconsciousness caused by an anaesthetic or sudden accident\n- however, just because a person has one of these health conditions does not necessarily mean they lack the capacity to make a specific decision\n- The MCA says;\n- assume a person has the capacity to make a decision themselves, unless it's proved otherwise\n- wherever possible, help people to make their own decisions\n- do not treat a person as lacking the capacity to make a decision just because they make an unwise decision\n- if you make a decision for someone who does not have capacity, it must be in their best interests\n- treatment and care provided to someone who lacks capacity should be the least restrictive of their basic rights and freedoms\n- r ni nabl mak\n- understand the information relevant to the decision\n- retain that information\n- use or weigh up that information as part of the process of making the decision\n- to communicate his decision (whether by talking, using sign language or another means).\n- Key_points;\n- Any patient who lacks capacity he/she should be treated as in their best interest.\n\n- If patient has capacity and refuse to get treatment, then he/she shouldn't be treated without consents.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 17-year-old male presented to the ED with foot pain. on initial assessment, there is edema and swelling on his foot. The image is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Ankle dislocation\n- **B)** Navicular fracture\n- **C)** 5th metatarsal fracture\n- **D)** Calcaneal fracture\n- **E)** Lisfranc injury\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  MoWHeavy Objects falling onto the feet or being run over by a vehicle tyre or wheel.\n- It is common in football and soccer players.\n- Diagnosiszrequest a standard foot x-ray.\n- Look to check that the medial side of the second MT is correctly aligned with the medial side of the middle cuneiform.\n- Management;\n\n- Support in a back slab POP, and refer if there are multiple, displaced, or dislocated MT fractures.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 56-year-old male brought into ED who has severe bradycardia for which He has been put on a pacemaker with a demand pacing mode. Pacing rate: 80 Pacing rate: 100mA His pacing rhythm is given. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Change the PAD positions\n- **B)** Increase pacing rate\n- **C)** Put percussion pacing mode\n- **D)** Refer for transvenous pacing\n- **E)** Increase pacing current\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Pacer spikes with no associated QRS complex. Therefore, increase the current until you regain capture, check for electrolyte abnormalities, and position the pads\n\nPad positions Transcutaneous cardiac pacing allows efficient, and noninvasive ventricular stimulation conscious patients to treat symptomatic bradycardias, Attach pads Select mode fixed or demand Select rate 60-80 Select energy step up/step down method Look electrical capture then feel for mechanical capture Increase energy for 5mAmp\n- Note: Transcutaneous pacing maybe uncomfortable for the Pt so sedabon should be considered.\n\n- Pacing spike presence Of a consistent ST segment and tall T waves after each generated pacer spike Assessing electrocardiographic capture with transcutaneous pacing.\n- **A)** Intrinsic patient bradycardic rhythm.\n- **B)** No electrical capture as TCP is below the threshold level.\n- **C)** TCP with electrical capture.\n- Threshold current values will vary depending on the clinical situation. Transcutaneous pacing thresholds tend to be lowest in healthy individuals or in patients with minimal hemodynamic compromise. In these settings, the threshold is usually in the range of 40 to 80 mA. Most patients are paced using a current in the range of 20 to 140 mA.\n- Electrical capture is best judged by the presence of a consistent ST segment and T wave after each generated pacer spike as in the above pacing image.\n- Once electrical capture is achieved, mechanical capture must be ensured by either a palpable pulse rate or arterial catheter blood pressure monitoring. The patient will have a pulse rate that is exactly\n\nequal to that of the paced rhythm on the cardiac monitor if mechanical capture is achieved. 10 / 10 IO. A 25-year-old man presents to the ED with a two-week history of bloody diarrhoea after returning from a holiday in Morocco. On examination, his observations are all within normal limits and his abdomen is generally mildly tender with no guarding or rebound tenderness. His blood report is shown. What is the most appropriate investigation to establish the cause of his symptoms?\n- **A)** Stool culture including ova, cysts and parasites\n- **B)** CT abdomen with contrast\n- **C)** Arrange out-patient colonoscopy\n- **D)** Faecal calprotectin\n- **E)** Abdominal x-ray Explanation\n- Patient travel history and clinical features suggests schistosomiasis.\n- Schistosomiasis\n- A snail-borne parasitic disease caused by multicellular trematodes of the Schistosoma blood fluke species.\n\n- People are infected during routine agricultural, domestic, occupational, and recreational activities, which expose them to infested water.\n- Risk factors\n- Skin exposure to contaminated freshwater\n- Travel to endemic areas - Africa, the Middle East, South America\n- Occupational exposure - Adult fishermen, canal cleaners, car washers, and irrigation workers\n- Clinical features Schistosoma mansoni\n- S. mansoni and S. japonicum cause:\n- Abdominal pain\n- Fever\n- Bloody diarrhea\n- Portal hypertension\n- Hematemesis\n- Investigations Schistosoma haematobium\n- Chronic infection S. Haematobium Can lead to squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder (painless hematuria) Pulmonary hypertension Pelvic pain\n- Schistosomiasis is diagnosed through the detection of parasite eggs in stool or urine specimens\n- Antibodies and/or antigens detected in blood or urine samples are also indications of infection.\n- FBC - normocytic, normochromic mild anemia often present; eosinophilia, occasionally thrombocytopenia\n- Urinalysis\n\nConsider LFT, renal function test , abdominal ultrasounds\n- Management\n- Praziquantel\n- Consider anti-histamine and corticosteroid for dermatitis or reduce the severity of symptoms previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	20	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4120db64-b21e-40c5-9bd9-0773a0571f24	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-22	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 22	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 22\n\n- **C)** Family history of allergy\n- **D)** Delay onset Of symptoms\n- **E)** History Of asthma Explanation\n- The frequency of food sensitization in asthmatic children is higher than expected in a general population. it has been found that asthma is a risk factor for severe or fatal anaphylactic reactions to foods.\n- The risk of death may be increased in those with pre-existing asthma, particularly if this is poorly controlled or if treatment with adrenaline is delayed.\n- Food is the most common cause of anaphylaxis in young people. Pre- school-aged children have the highest rate of hospitalisation due to food anaphylaxis, but a disproportionately low rate of fatal outcomes. The greatest risk from fatal food allergy appears to be in teenagers and adults up to age 30 years.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 43-year-old man presents following a collapse. he is cyanosed, tachycardic and has Sp02 of 88% despite high-flow oxygen and a RR of 26 per minute. He admits to recent recreational drug use. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Pulmonary vasoconstriction from cannabis smoking\n- **B)** PE secondary to IV drug use\n- **C)** Methaemoglobinaemia from amyl nitrate use\n- **D)** CNS depression and hypoventilation from opiates\n- **E)** Hypoxia secondary to Gamma Hydroxy Butyrate ingestion Explanation;\n- The patient has methemoglobinemia, a disorder of red blood cells that interferes with normal hemoglobin function and oxygen transport. This can result from either congenital (or acquired processes.\n\nCO poisoning Carbon monoxide is a colourless, odourless tasteless gas produced by incomplete combustion of fuels — this occurs when insufficient oxygen is present.\n- Exposure to carbon monoxide causes tissue hypoxia. because carbon monoxide binds to haemogk)bin with about 240 times the affinity of oxygen forms carboxyhaernoglobin. Exposure to CO mav occur:\n- Chimneys or flues are bltx*ed\n- lack Of ventaation in the room\n- Barbecues are used inside homes. caravans or tents\n- Smc*e in burnng buildings\n- Soot or yellow/brown staining around or on appliances.\n- people are at Of co isonin\n- Older people. children\n- Women who are pregnant People with anaemia. Respiratory or cardiovascular disease Clinical features Headache. nausea & vomiting Vertigo, dizziness\n- altered consciousness Dyspnoea. pain (in chest or stomach)\n- sleep changes. emotional lability delayed neuropsychiatric features (memory impairment, disorientation, irritability. inability to concentrate, personality change, emotional lability. neuropathy, incontinence,) severe neuroloqical Cyanide poisoning Cyanide poisoning is most often linked to house fires and combustion of products containing carbon and nitrogen, primarily foam rubber, woo. plastics. and Other synthetic materials.\n- CN disrupts the electron trans-;Mt chain in the mitochondrial production of ATP by binding to and inhibiting oxidase a.\n- As aerobic cellular metabolism ceases. cens are forced into anaerobic metabolism with the subsequent generation of lactate\n- Clinical features Neurologic symptoms\n- confusk)n, agitation. vertigo, headache. seizure,coma. Gastrointestinal\n- nausea,vomiting,\n- Cardiac symptoms - chest pain, cardiac arrythmia Hypotension and bradycardia are common in cyanide poisoning\n- pulmonary symptoms - dyspnea tachypnae. pulmonary edema\n- Bright red bleeding of mucous membranes\n- Flushing of the skin\n- Necroses in mouth and esophagus\n- Bright red retinal vein on fundoscosic exam ination\n- cherry red skin (very late and often present). cyanosis\n- Patient usuary not cyanotic but maybe after prolonged respiratory failure and shock.\n- Investiqations Arterial or Venous blood qas - Cyanide toxicity is characterized by a normal arterial oxygen tension and an abnormally high venous oxygen tension.\n- Normal to elevated Pa02 level (very high levels of Pa02 suggestive of CN reflection of cell's inability to extract and use oxygen)\n- A high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Methemoglobinemia Methaemoglobinaemia is a discrder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemogbbin (MetHb) in the blood\n- Methaemoglobin in Which iron (ferrous Fe2\n- ) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3'). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%\n- is either congenital or acquired\n- Patients With methemoglobinernia will typica"y have brown blocK1\n- Causes Benzocaine\n- Dapsone\n- Nitrogtycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin\n- Nitroprusside\n- Phenazopyridine\n- Scylium N itrite\n- Amy' nitrite\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite (Apoppers-)\n- Clinical features\n- Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:\n- 10% - None (patients with underlying diseases may have more symptoms at lower level)\n- 10-20% - Slight discoloration (eg, pale. gray blue) of the skim\n- Chocolate brown colored blt»d\n- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia. lightheadedness\n- Dyspnea, weakness. confusion, chest pain 50-70%\n- Arrhythmias; altered mental status, delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis\n- > 70% usually. death\n- Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is > I .5 g/d\n- Investiqations\n- or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in a fresh sample Of blood. Caution on estimatinz omen level\n- Routine pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%.\n- paOZ readings on blood gases mavT)/\n\n(Seizu re may develop in people With\n- Normal Oxygen saturation on severe poisoning) pulse oximetry expected as CO poisoning should be suspected there is adequate oxygen in the in fire victims. in patients with flu\n- blood yet inability to use the oxygen like symptoms in