.\n- The half-life Of carboxyhaemoglobin in a patient breathing high-concentration oxygen via a reservoir mask is 90 minutes. Supportive measures — follow ABCDE protocol\n- Start fluid resuscitation fcy hypotensive patents\n- Obtain specialist advice for any patient: Presenting with a carboxyhaemoglobin of Who is pregnant\n- You suspect of poisoning be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia.\n- Mana eme\n- Remove exsX)Sures if possible.\n- Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilatiM if necessary) Treat anvone who is symptomatic (vital sign acidosis OT signs end organ damage) or anvone who has a methemoglobin leve! 1-2 mg'kg (0.1-02 mL'kg of 1% solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes Start with I mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg'kg for rnore severe sym ptoms_ Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative d\n- osis. patients Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis. Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C gensÅduexchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinania can be treated with blood transfusion\n- Kev points\n- can result from exposure to a number of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).\n- Consider the diagnosis in any patient with cyanosis and hyv»xia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration\n- Administer methyiene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs.\n\nhyperbaric oxygen therapy - Hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be beneficial in selected patients however, the evidence is w\n- eak and inconsistent. The UK NPIS does not currently recommend hyperbaric oxygen therapy dysfunction a serum level > 25%\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 2-year-old infant is noted to have mild cyanosis who assume a squatting position during long walking. He is noted to have increasing fussiness followed by increasing cyanosis, limpness, and unresponsiveness. What is the most likely underlying lesion is?\n- **A)** Transposition of great arteries\n- **B)** Tetralogy offallot\n- **C)** Anomalous pulmonary venous return\n- **D)** Hypoplastic left heart\n- **E)** Aspiration with obstruction to air passage Explanation\n- TOF - clinical presentation varies based on the severity of the right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, more commonly presenting as neonates with a certain degree of cyanosis. In some patients, cyanosis presents rnonths later in life, when the rate Of obstructions worsens.\n- "Tet spells" (a child or infant becomes extremely blue and frequently agitated and out of breath) or hypercyanotic episodes present during infancy or toddler age and decrease after 4 to 5 years of age.\n- Dehydration or agitation commonly precipitate tet spells, and if patients do not receive prompt and adequate treatment, they can\n\ndevelop severe cyanosis and hypoxia that, subsequently, can cause syncope and even death.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 28-year-old female came to ED with short of breath, she has a history of similar episodes previously. While waiting in the waiting area she suddenly collapsed and hemodynamically unstable. You suspect a massive pulmonary embolism. What would be your next immediate step of management?\n- **A)** Subcutaneous heparin\n- **B)** thrombolysis with Alteplase infusion O\n- **C)** Order CTPA\n- **D)** D-dimer\n- **E)** Bedside 2D Echocardiography\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Thrombolysis is indicated for patients with severe circulatory compromise or a picture Of massive pulmonary embolism. Prior proof Of PE is not needed if the patient is peri-arrest and thrombolysis should be adrninistered immediately (50mg Alteplase as a bolus) in such patients. It is common practice to give thrombolysis as first-line treatment for any patient who is in peri-arrest/cardiac arrest based on clinical suspicion Of PE without waiting for results frorm investigations.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 61-year-old male presented to the ED with chest pain radiating to the shoulder. Examination revealed a diastolic murmur. You suspect aortic dissection in this patient and You want to perform ECHO in this patient. What will you find in ECHO in this patient?\n- **A)** RVfailure   B\n- Pericardial fluid\n- **C)** pneumoperitoneum D\n- Mitral stenosis\n- **E)** Mitral regurgitation Explanation;\n- Echocardiography plays an important role in the diagnosis and follow- up of aortic diseases.\n- Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) permits adequate assessment of several aortic segments, particularly the aortic root and proximal ascending aorta. Transoesophageal echocardiography (TOE) overcomes the limitations of TTE in thoracic aorta assessment.\n- TTE may be used as the initial modality in the emergency setting. EghQ_finding_;\n\n- intimal flap\n- can "type" the dissection: type A associated features and complications:\n- aortic regurgitation (acute dilatation of the aortic root, aortic leaflet prolapse, dissection flap prolapse, pre-existing disease, e.g. bicuspid valve)\n- pericardial effusion and/or tamponade\n- regional wall motion abnormality heralding coronary artery occlusion\n- colour flow Doppler\n- identifies true and false lumen\n- detect aortic branch occlusion/ dissection (absent flow)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 12-year-old boy brought to the ED with anaphylactic shock. Patient has given one doses of adrenaline. Which of the following is the risk factor of biphasic shock?\n- **A)** Adrenaline more than one time\n- **B)** NO Improvement after adrenaline\n- **C)** Relapse of shock after respond\n- **D)** Need Of ICIJ admission\n- **E)** Delay presentation to the ED more than 90 minutes Explanation\n- Risk factors for biphasic reactions following anaphylaxis include:\n- more severe initial presentation of anaphylaxis\n\n- initial reaction requiring more than one dose of adrenaline delay in adrenaline administration (> 30 — 60 min from symptom onset).\n- Patients with a history of a previous biphasic reaction may also be at an increased risk\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 68-year-old woman presents having suffered 4 'fainting episodes' over the past 24 hours. Each episode was sudden and unexpected. Her examination is unremarkable. A copy of her rhythm strip is shown. What is most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Mobitz type 2 AV block\n- **B)** Atrial fibrillation\n- **C)** Wenckebach\n- **D)** Complete heart block\n- **E)** Sick sinus syndrome Explanation\n- ECG reveals complete heart block. There is complete dissociation between atria and ventricle (P wave and QRS complex).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 52-year-old male who has left the psychiatric ward and presented to the ED with palpitation, shortness of breath, and headache. On assessment, he admits to recent amyl nitrates use. He is cyanosed and his saturation is 82% on air. Given the likely diagnosis, what would be the treatment?\n- **A)** High flow oxygen   B\n- Methylene blue\n- **C)** Hydroxocobalamin\n- **D)** Dantrolene\n- **E)** Sodium bicarbonate Explanation;\n- The patient has methemoglobinemia, a disorder of red blood cells that interferes with normal hemoglobin function and oxygen transport. This can result from either congenital (or acquired processes\n- Causes\n- Benzoca ine . Dapsone\n- Nitroglycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin\n- Nitroprusside\n- Phenazopyridine\n- Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite\n\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible.\n- Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary)\n- Treat anyone who is symptomatic (vital sign abnormality, acidosis or or anyone who has a methemoglobin level\n- Give methylene blue: 1-2 mg/kg (0.1-0.2 mL/kg of solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes\n- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms.\n- Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.\n- Management in G6PD deficient patients\n- Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis.\n- Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C\n- Consider_exchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED after fall into the canal. His immersion is estimated to be around 10 minutes. On examination, he is vitally stable. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Discharge him\n- **B)** Request ABG\n- **C)** prescribe antibiotics\n- **D)** Admit him\n- **E)** Immersion greater than 10minutes would be bad prognosis Explanation;\n- The appropriate step would be to request an ABG.\n- All asymptomatic can be discharged after 6 hours of observation. Management of drowning and near- drowning_patient\n- water rescue\n- Recognise distress and call for help.\n- Provide flotation to stop the process of drowning. Reaching, throwing, or dropping a buoyant object or manoeuvring a craft to the victim is preferred\n- The victim should be removed from the water in as near a horizontal position as possible, with the airway open.\n- On land, the victim should be placed supine with trunk and head at the same level\n- ger.yigal.spine-gonsiderations\n- If cervical spine injury is suspected, the cervical spine may be held in mid-lineg\n\n- Those with confirmed cervical spine injury usually have obvious history, or signs of significant trauma; cervical spine imaging is not recommended for patients witnessed to have no preceding trauma prior to a drowning.\n- Hospital-management\n- Follow ABCDE protocol\n- Maintain the airway. Remove regurgitated fluid/debris by suction of the upper airway. Ensure adequate ventilation and correction of hypoxia.\n- Manage hypothermia - Passive re-warming (including removing wet clothing, and drying and covering the patient) is the preferred treatment option for mild cases.\n- Active internal re-warming techniques include airway re-warming with humidified oxygen at 400C (1040F), warmed intravenous fluids, and peritoneal lavage.\n\nGrade 1 (cough with\n- Grade 1 patients (conscious and alert: cough with normal lung auscultation) who normal lung present at the emergency department can be observed off oxygen for a few auscultation) hours (6 hours) and discharge if Vital signs, symptoms, lung examination, and mentation remain normal Deliver high flow oxygen, maintain saturation between 92% and 96%. Grade 2 (rales in\n- Consider an observation period Of 6 to 8 hours in the emergency department. some pulmonary\n- if the patient is off supplemental oxygen for that time. with normalised vital signs fields) and mentation, consider discharge with close follow-up. High flow oxygen via non-invasive or invasive ventilation.\n- Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV) may be considered.\n- If the patient does not improve after a short course of NIPPV, quickly convert to Grade 3-5 (acute endotracheal intubation (ETI) pulmonary oedema\n- Admit to intensive care unit, With no\n- Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) may be indicated in cases Of hypotension/shock or hypoxia refractory to initial treatment measures\n- intravenous fluids ± vasopressor (adrenaline) with hypotension or OCk)\n- If the patient severely hypothermic, withheld intravenous drugs if the patient's core body temperature is <300C (<860F). If the core body temperature is >30'C (>86'F), intravenous medications may be administered but with increased intervals between doses. Grade 6\n- Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) ± advanced life support medications (cardiopulmonary arrest)\n- Key_pgints\n- The initial management of drowning follows the ATLS principles.\n- Management of hypoxaemia and circulatory failure is keyto the management Of drowning.\n- Check an arterial blood gas on all patients with a history of submersion, as a surprising degree of hypoxia may be present in an asymptomatic individual.\n- Patients who are asymptomatic and have no evidence of respiratory compromise (no chest x-ray changes or hypoxia on ABC) after 6 hours can be safely discharged hor\n- ne. All symptomatic patients should be admitted for observation.\n- Remember to look for evidence of precipitating causes and associated injuries and manage them appropriately.\n- Submersion minutes is a good prognostic factor and >2S minutes is a poor prognostic factor.\n\n- Consider child abuse in young children who are drowned in bath tubs, buckets or shallow water.\n- There is no good evidence to support the routine use of prophylactic antibiotics. The incidence of ventilator-associated pneumonia can be 34-52%, and treatment should be initiated as per local protocol. Treatment should ideally be guided by culture results. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 62-year-old man brought to the ED with breathlessness. He has history of diabetes and hypertension but has had no medical admission. On examination, he is pale, sweaty and clammy. His observations are: HR 102bpm BP  mmHg RR 30 breath/min sp02 89% Temp 36.60c His CXR is show. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** pericardial effusion\n- **B)** Pulmonary Oedema\n- **C)** Cardiomyopathy\n- **D)** Community acquired pneumonia\n- **E)** Viral pneumonitis Explanation\n- Patient X-ray shows:\n- Upper lobe diversion\n- Alveolar edema\n- Pleural effusion\n- Cardiomegaly\n- Kerely B lines ( basal septal line) Which are common features on cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.\n\npreviOUS  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	21	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
6b2a81ae-a6dd-419e-a5ef-29fb6154250b	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-23	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 23	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 23\n\n- **B)** Previous exposure to allergens\n- **C)** Lip swelling\n- **D)** Vomiting & diarrhea\n- **E)** Hypotension Explanation\n- Anaphylactoid vs anaphylaxis:\n- Anaphylactoid reaction: first time exposure to allergen , direct release of histamine and other mediators from mast cells and doesn't cause degranulation. Non-immune (IgE) mediated reaction.\n- Anaphylaxis reaction: On further exposure to that allergen, the responsible allergenic antigen binds to an antigen-specific lgE antibody, leading to mast cell degranulation Histamine and other mediators, including leukotrienes and cytokines are released from rnast cells and basophils following exposure to this antigen.\n- Summery: anaphylactoid first exposure and non-immune mediated\n- Anaphylaxis: further exposure/ Patient has exposed to allergen previously (history) and immune mediated\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 40-year-old nurse from your hospital has taken thirty 500mg paracetamols at IO am and she presented to the ED at 18:00.\n\nWhat would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Observe her condition\n- **B)** Send labs and wait for result\n- **C)** Weigh her and start NAC, if more than 150mg/kg\n- **D)** Discharge her\n- **E)** Start NAC and send labs\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Give acetylcysteine if;\n- Serum paracetamol concentration is detectable (>10mg/L)\n- OR\n- ALT is above the upper limit of normal\n- INR >1.3 (in the absence of another cause, e.g., warfarin).\n- OR\n- have ingested 150 mg/kg or more of paracetamol Note; For more detail check the Paracetamol overdose module in pharmacology & poisoning.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 12-year-old boy brought to the ED with anaphylactic shock. Patient has given two doses of adrenaline. What is the meaning of biphasic shock?\n- **A)** Relapse of shock after respond\n- **B)** Need intensive care admission\n- **C)** Adrenaline more than one time\n- **D)** No Improvement after adrenaline\n- **E)** Refractory shock and another protocol of management Explanation\n- Biphasic reaction Anaphylaxis can appear to resolve but then cause a recurrence of\n- symptoms several hours later in the absence of further allergen exposure. This is known as a biphasic reaction and occurs in around 5% of patients. Risk factors for biphasic reactions\n- more severe initial presentation of anaphylaxis\n- initial reaction requiring more than one dose of adrenaline\n-\n\n- delay in adrenaline administration (> 30 — 60 min from symptom onset)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 32-year-old female brought to the ED with a paracetamol overdose. She is known to be allergic to NAC. How would you manage this patient?\n- **A)** Start NAC half dose rate\n- **B)** Start NAC with hydrocortisone cover\n- **C)** Start NAC and treat symptoms as arise\n- **D)** Give antihistarnine prior to NAC\n- **E)** Give oral methionine\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Anaphylactic reaction due to NAC\n- In patients experiencing an adverse reaction consider temporarily stopping the acetylcysteine\n- Re-start acetylcysteine infusion once the reaction has settled. Consider slowing the infusion rate (e.g., administer the first bag over twice as long as usual; the normal infusion rate can be used for subsequent bags).\n\n- Give acetylcysteine even if the patient has a history of a previous adverse-reactiomas the benefits outweigh the risks.\n- Consider using a slower initial infusion of acetylcysteine in these patients to reduce the risk of a further reaction, as well as giving prophylactic treatment (e.g., antihistamines).\n- Pretreatment with nebulised salbutamol in patients with a history of bronchospasm following acetylcysteine.\n- Monitor all patients closely for acetylcysteine infusion reactions over the first few hours.\n- Nausea, vomiting, flushing, urticarial rash, angioedema, tachycardia, and bronchospasm are relatively common\n- Be aware that vomiting is relatively common during intravenous acetylcysteine administration. In clinical practice, manage with an anti- emetic (e.g., ondansetron).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nAn II-month-old infant brought to the ED by mother, she states that the baby turned pale and floppy for few seconds. Which of the following is the risk factor for BRUE?\n- **A)** Cyanosis\n- **B)** Breathlessness\n- **C)** Pallor\n- **D)** Floppy\n- **E)** Gestational age less than 32 weeks Explanation\n- BRUE risk stratification Risk stratification Low risk days of age Born weeks' gestation and have a corrected age of weeks. NO CPR given by trained practitioner minute duration First event High risk\n- Infant< 2 months Hx Of prematurity less than 32 weeks at birth More than one event\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 24 year old female with history of fall brought into the ED by his father with complain of pain in the elbow. Her X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Radioulnar dislocation\n- **B)** Olecranon fracture C\n- Radial head fracture\n- **D)** Supracondylar fracture\n- **E)** Humeral shaft fracture EXPIanation\n- Suspect supracondylar fracture in children age 3- IO years, if X-ray shows Fat-pad sign or fracture.\n- Suspect radial head fracture in adults, if X-ray shows Fat-pad sign or fracture\n- Radial head/neck fracture\n- Radial head fractures are the most common fractures of the elbow.\n- They result from a fall on an outstretched hand.\n- A specific associated injury, the Essex-Lopresti lesion occurs when there is disruption of the triangular fibrocartilage complex of the wrist and the interosseous membrane between the radius and ulna, causing dissociation of the distal radioulnar joint.\n\n- Clinical features\n- Pain the lateral elbow especially with pronation and supination of the arm\n- there may be swelling laterally and tenderness with palpation of the radial head.\n- Diagnosis\n- Joint effusion (Fat pad sign) on x-ray\n- abnormal displacement of the radiocapitellar line away from the center of the capitellum\n- Management\n- Nondisplaced fracture manage with sling immobilization, ice, elevation, analgesics, and referral to an orthopedic (fracture clinic) within I week.\n- displaced fractures or those with restricted range of motion need surgical repair. Therefore, orthopedic referral should be made within 24 hours.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 59-year-old man is brought to the ED with a two days history of shortness of breath. He has a past medical history of COPD. On examination, RR was 25 breath per minutes for which BiPAP was attached. According to BTS guideline which of the following is an indication to NIV?\n- **A)** Reduced conscious level\n\nO\n- **B)** pC02>8.5 kPa C\n- RR>25 breath per minute D\n- PH less 7.25\n- **E)** P02 12.6 kPa Explanation\n- Indication of NIV in COPD PH < 7.35\n- pC02> 6.5\n- RR>23\n- If persistent after bronchodilators and controlled oxygen therapy\n- Note: NIV usage evidence is less clear for patients with arterial pH <7.25.\n- pH<7.25 has been suggested as a level below which IMV should be considered and <7.15 as the level that IMV is indicated (following initial resuscitation and use of controlled oxygen).\n- Indications for invasive mechanical ventilation (IMV) in acute exacerbation-of-COPP-(AEC0PD)\n- Imminent respiratory arrest\n\n-\n-\n-\n- Severe respiratory distress Failure of or contra-indications to non-invasive ventilation (NIV) Persisting pH<7.15 or deterioration in pH despite NIV Depressed consciousness (Glasgow Coma Score <8)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 36-year-old male brought into the ED by police. He has acute behavioral disturbance for which de-escalation measure has been taken however its unsuccessful. According to NICE guidelines, what should be given for acute behavioral disturbance?   A 1M lorazepam\n- **B)** IV promethazine\n- **C)** Refer to psychiatrist\n- **D)** IV ketamine   E\n- IV diazepam\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Choice of drugs for rapid tranquilization / Sedation\n\nNICE guideline Intramuscular lorazepam OR\n- Intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine\n- If no response or partial response to above drugs, consider a further dose\n- If there is evidence Of cardiovascular disease, including a prolonged QT interval, or no electrocardiogram has been carried out, avoid intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine RCEM guideline\n- Ketamine 4mg/kg 1M OR Droperidol 5-10mg 1M If these agents are unavailable, midazolam (5-10mg 1M), lorazepam (4mg 1M) or haloperidol (5mg 1M +/- 2mg lorazepam 1M) could be considered.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 35-year-old male presented to ED with complain of chest pain and short of breath. On examination he was febrile and tachycardic. He has history of viral illness and drug abuse. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Rheumatic fever\n- **B)** Valvular heart disease\n- **C)** Endocarditis\n- **D)** Myocardial infarction E\n- Myocarditis\n\nExplanation\n-\n- Patient presents with chest pain and 90% of them has a history of viral prodrome illness such UPRTI or gastroenteritis (sore throat, vomiting, diarrhea.\n- Mostly patients are young below 50 years 10 / 10 IO. A 72-year-old man from nursing home presented to the ED with meatal bleeding due to foley's catheter out. What is the most appropriate next management?\n- **A)** Place suprapubic catheter\n- **B)** Insert foley's catheter\n- **C)** Perform retrograde urethrogram\n- **D)** Perform cystogram   E\n- Refer to urology team\n- Explanation\n- The followin feature indicates consideration of suprapubic catheter for-bladder-drainage-and-retrograde-ur.ethrogram-f.or-diagnosis-of\n\nurethral injury, so for that patient requires urgent referrallconsultation-to-a-urologist High-riding prostate on rectal examination Meatal blood,\n- Perineal ecchymosis,\n- Scrotal hematoma,\n- Haematuria and pain on urination may be present in incomplete\n- ruptures Pelvic fracture, or gross hematuria,\n- Any resistance to gentle passage of a Foley catheter\n- Inability to void (with a palpable distended bladder) is another classic\n- sign and is often associated with a complete rupture\n- Urethral trauma Anterior male urethral injury_\n-\n- The bulbar urethra is the most common site affected by blunt trauma n bulbar injuries, the bulb is compressed against the pubic symphysis,\n- resulting in rupture of the urethra at the site of compression. Possible mechanisms are straddle injuries or kicks to the perineum. A penile fracture can be complicated by a urethral injury in approximately 15% of cases. Penetrating- anterior injuries are rare and are usually caused by\n- gunshot wounds, stab wounds, dog bites, impalement, or penile amputations. Depending on the affected segment, penetrating injuries are usually associated with penile, testicular and/or pelvic injuries. Insertion of foreign bodies is another rare cause of anterior injury. It is\n- usually a result of autoerotic stimulation or may be associated with\n\npsychiatric disorders.\n- Iatrogenic-injury-is the rnost common type of urethral trauma. The incidence of male urethral injury during transurethral catheterisation is 13.4 per 1,000 catheters inserted. Injuries can occur due to the creation of a false passage by the tip of the catheter or inadvertent inflation of the anchoring balloon in the urethra.\n- Posterior-male-urethral-injuries\n- Bluntposterior urethral injuries are almost exclusively related to pelvic fractures and the risk increases with fracture configuration severity. These injuries are referred to as pelvic fracture urethral injuries (PFUI), and are mainly caused by road traffic accidents\n- Pelvic fracture urethral injuries are divided into partial or complete ruptures.\n- In complete ruptures, there is a gap between the disrupted ends of the urethra, which fills up with scar tissue. There is no urethral wall in the scarred space and any lumen represents a fistulous tract between the urethral stumps\n- Penetrating - injuries of the pelvis, perineum, or buttocks (mainly gunshot wounds) can also damage the posterior urethra but are extremely rare in the civilian setting. There is a high probability of associated injuries (approx. 90%), mainly intra-abdominal.\n- Iatrogenic injury— has been reported with transanal total mesorectal excision in 1-11% of cases. This injury is usually partial and located at the membranous urethra.\n\n- Clinical sign/features High-riding prostate on rectal examination Meatal blood,\n- Perineal ecchymosis,\n- Scrotal hematoma,\n- Haematuria and pain on urination may be present in incomplete\n- ruptures Pelvic fracture, or gross hematuria,\n- Any resistance to gentle passage of a Foley catheter\n- Inability to void (with a palpable distended bladder) is another classic\n- sign and is often associated with a complete rupture Investigation\n-\n- urethrogram Retrograde urethrography (RUG) is the standard in the early evaluation\n- of a male urethral injury, RUC is operator-dependent and is recommended to be performed by the treating urologist. The diagnosis of urethral trauma is made with retrograde urethrography, which rnust be performed prior to the insertion of a urethral catheter to avoid further injury to the urethra. Extravasation of contrast demonstrates the location of the tear.\n- Cysto:urethrosgopy- Flexible cysto-urethroscopy is a valuable alternative to diagnose an\n- acute urethral injury and may distinguish between complete and partial rupture. Flexible cystourethroscopy is preferred to RUG in suspected penile fracture-associated urethral injury as RUG is associated with a high false-negative rate\n\n- Ultrasound and magnetic resonance imaging\n- In the acute phase, US scanning is used for guiding the placement of a suprapubic catheter. In complex PFUIs, MRI before deferred treatr-nent provides valuable additional information, which can help to determine the most appropriate surgical strategy.\n- Management\n- Male anterior urethral injuries\n- Immediate exploration and urethral reconstruction; This is indicated for penile fracture related injuries and non-life- threatening penetrating injuries.\n- Small lacerations can be repaired by simple closure.\n- Complete ruptures without extensive tissue loss are treated with anastomotic repair. Only 2% of cases will develop a urethral stricture after immediate urethral reconstruction for penile fracture.\n- In the case of longer defects or apparent infection (particularly bite wounds), a staged repair with urethral marsupialisation is needed.\n- Penetrating injuries require peri- and post-operative antibiotic treatrnent\n- In blunt injuries - Immediate urethroplasty should be performed.\n- The long terrn-outcornes (patency rate, potency rate) of patients treated with immediate urethroplasty is similar to these initially treated with suprapubic diversion and delayed urethroplasty. The main advantage of performing immediate urethroplasty is that this strategy significantly reduces the time to spontaneous voiding from two to six months to three weeks on average.\n\nSpongiosal contusion and haematoma during immediate urethroplasty will make the operation technically more demanding; therefore, immediate urethroplasty should be performed by a dedicated urethral surgeon.\n- urinary-diversion\n- Blunt anterior urethral injuries are associated with spongiosal contusion. Evaluation of the limits of urethral debridement in the acute phase might be difficult and as a consequence, it is reasonable to start with urinary diversion only.\n- If urinary diversion is performed, the therapeutic options are suprapubic diversion or a trial of early endoscopic re-alignment with transurethral catheterisation; (refers to reestablishment of urethral alignment via indwelling urethral catheter by cystoscope) there is conflicting evidence as to which intervention is superior. Urinary diversion is maintained for one to two weeks for partial ruptures and three weeks for complete ruptures.\n- Transurethral or suprapubic urinary diversion are treatment options for iatrogenic or life-threatening penetrating injuries. Minor iatrogenic urethral injuries and urethral contusions do not require urinary diversion.\n- Maleposterior-urethraLinjuries\n- Emergency management\n- As these injuries are usually associated with other severe injuries, resuscitation and immediate treatment of life-threatening injuries have absolute priority. Penetrating injuries especially have a very high likelihood of associated injuries requiring immediate exploration. There\n\nis no urgency to treat the urethral injury and urinary diversion is not essential during the first hours after trauma, however, it is preferable to establish early urinary diversion to:\n- monitor urinary output, since this is a valuable sign of the haemodynamic condition and the renal function of the patient; treat symptomatic retention if the patient is still conscious;\n- minimise urinary extravasation and its secondary effects, such as infection and fibrosis.\n- Insertion of a suprapubic catheter is an accepted practice in urgent situations. However, insertion of a suprapubic catheter is not without risk, especially in the unstable traurna patient where the bladder is often displaced by a pelvic haematoma or because of poor bladder filling due to haemodynamic shock or concomitant bladder injury. In these circumstances, an attempt at urethral catheterisation can be carried out by experienced personnel\n- It is extremely unlikely that the gentle passage of a urethral catheter will do any additional damage. If there is any difficulty, a suprapubic catheter should be placed under US guidance or under direct vision, for example, during laparotomy for associated injuries.\n- Early urethral management (less than six weeks after injury):\n- For partial injuries, urinary diversion (suprapubic or transurethral) is sufficient as these injuries can heal without significant scarring or obstruction. A complete injury will not heal, and formation of an obliterated segment is inevitable in case of suprapubic diversion alone. To avoid this obliteration and a long period of suprapubic diversion followed by deferred urethroplasty, the urethral ends can be sutured\n\n(urethroplasty) or approximated over a transurethral catheter (re- alignment).\n- Early re-alignment:\n- Early re-alignment can be performed when a stable patient is on the operating table for other surgery or as a stand-alone procedure in the absence of concomitant injuries. In a partial injury, re-alignment, and transurethral catheterisation avoids extravasation of urine in the surrounding tissues reducing the inflammatory response. In complete injuries, the aim of re-alignment is to correct severe distraction injuries rather than to prevent a stricture.\n- Early urethroplasty:\n- Immediate urethroplasty within 48 hours after injury is difficult because of poor visualisation and the inability to accurately assess the degree of urethral disruption, due to extensive swelling and ecchymosis, which may result in extensive unjustified urethral debridement.\n- Early urethroplasty can be performed after two days and up to six weeks after the initial injury, if associated injuries have been stabilised, the distraction defect is short, the perineum is soft and the patient is able to lie down in the lithotomy position. This avoids a long period of suprapubic diversion with its discomfort and complications. As the results (complications, stricture recurrence, incontinence, and impotence) are equivalent to delayed urethroplasty, early urethroplasty might be an option for patients fulfilling the above-mentioned criteria.\n\n- Deferred management (greater than three months after injury): The standard treatr-nent remains deferred urethroplasty. In the case of a complete rupture, treated with an initial period of three months suprapubic diversion, obliteration of the posterior urethra is almost inevitable . Endoscopic treatment of a complete obliteration is not successful. After at least three months of suprapubic diversion, the pelvic haematoma is nearly always resolved, the prostate has descended into a more normal position, the scar tissue has stabilised and the patient is clinically stable and able to lie down in the lithotomy position. Associated life-threatening injuries often preclude early management of penetrating membranous urethral injuries. In those cases, suprapubic diversion with delayed urethroplasty is also advised. Perineal anastomotic repair is the surgical technique of choice, but a combined abdominoperineal approach is necessary in rare cases of concomitant bladder neck injury or recto-urethral fistula.\n- Treatment algorithms (Anterior and posterior urethral injury)\n\nIatrogenic Blunt Anterior urethral injury Penetrating Stable Penile fracture Partial Complete No Urinary diversion Urethral or suprapubic catheter Partial 1-2 weeks Complete 3 weeks Yes Immediate repair Urinary diversion Urethral or suprapubic catheter (2-3 weeks) Urethrogram Follow-up\n\nUnstable Resuscitation plus win d i version Urethral catheter I attempt Suprapubic catheter Male posterior urethral injury Assess Stat partial injun,' endoscopic re-al me nt Fail Perform RUG/ urethroscopy Complete injury Injury to bladder neck and/or prostate Suprapubic Surge" fM Follow up Stricture short, flimsy & non.obliterative Failure 1 OVIU Early endoscopic realignment Dela urethmplasty 3 months) Earw repair injuries I ow\n- up Suprapubic Cat heter Failure Assess 2 days-6 weeks defæt\n- lithotomy n ble Earlv ureth asty\n\nprevious  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	22	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
333e8f07-d00f-45ed-aa87-f788652f1ef6	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-24	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 24	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 24\n\n- **B)** Insert chest drain\n- **C)** Perform eFAST scan\n- **D)** Perform needle pericardiocentesis\n- **E)** Perform needle pericardiocentesis Explanation\n- Initial-management.opempneumothorax\n- Close the defect (hole) with any sterile occlusive dressing eg: plastic wrap, sheet of jolnet or Ashermann device Tape dressing 3 sides to provide flutter valve effect.\n- Don't completely occlude/close the wound (risk of tension pneumothorax) until chest tube is in place\n- Further management\n- Place Chest tube rernote from open wound(means not frorn the site of wound)\n- Definitive treatment is surgical repair (wound closure)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 50-year-old male known case of Sarcoidosis came with compliant of of blurred vision and watering of the right eye What other symptoms associated with this condition?\n- **A)** Permanent visual loss\n- **B)** Pain on accommodation\n- **C)** Purulent discharge\n- **D)** Irregular pupils\n- **E)** Eye tender to palpation Explanation\n- Clinical features of anterior uveitis\n- Symptoms\n- Watering of the eye\n- Dir-ninished or blurred vision\n- Painful red eye (pain maybe worse when the person is contracting the ciliary muscle eg: reading)\n- Photophobia\n- Sign\n- Ciliary flush\n- Small fixed irregular pupil\n- Eye tender to palpation through eyelid\n- Positive Talbot's test (pain increases as the eyes converge and pupils constricts)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 35-year-old male brought to the ED with history of knee trauma.\n\nHis X-ray is shown. What is the most common structure injured in this patient?   A Popliteal artery\n- **B)** Obturator nerve\n- **C)** femoral nerve\n- **D)** Femoral artery\n- **E)** popliteal nerve EXPIanation\n- X-ray shows posterior knee dislocation\n- Complications of posterior knee dislocation\n- Injury to popliteal artery and vein\n- Peroneal nerve injury\n- Compartment syndrome\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 16 year-old child brought into the ED with complain of right ankle pain with restricted range of motion. His X-ray is shown.\n\nWhat is the appropriate step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Immediate reduction\n- **B)** Urgent referral to orthopedics for ORIF\n- **C)** Backslab and follow up\n- **D)** Discharge after pain relief\n- **E)** Ice packing Explanation\n- Displaced fractures of the medial or lateral malleolus and Bimalleolar or trimalleolar fractures are unstable. Having attempted to reduce any significant talar shift (with appropriate sedation), place in a BKPOP, obtain fresh X-rays, and refer to the orthopaedic team.\n- Ankle fracture\n- Radiographically, ankle fractures are described as unimalleolar, bimalleolar and trimalleolar.\n- A bimalleolar fracture is fracture of the lateral and medial malleoli.\n- A trimalleolar fracture additionally involves the posterior malleolus\n- The ankle mortice joint allows very little rotation or angulation at the ankle joint, so forced twisting or angulation of the ankle joint causes fractures,ligament injuries.it also disrupt the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis.\n\n- Management\n- Small avulsion fracture treated with rest, elevation, analgesia and early mobilization.\n- initial imrnobilization in a boot ± crutches and orthopaedic follow-up\n- Undisplaced,-isolated medial or lateral malleolar fractures are usually stable. provide analgesia and crutches, and immobilize in a well- padded BKPOP cast. Advise limb elevation, and arrange orthopaedic follow-up.\n- the medial or lateral malleolus and Bimalleolar or trimalleolar fractures are unstable. Having attempted to reduce any significant talar shift (with appropriate sedation), place in a BKPOP, obtain fresh X-rays, and refer to the orthopaedic team.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 68-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of breathless on exertion associated with an episode of fainting. His examination reveals systolic murmur radiate to the carotid. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Mitral regurgitation\n- **B)** Aortic regurgitation   C\n- Aortic stenosis\n- **D)** Mitral stenosis\n- **E)** Tricuspid regurgitation Explanation\n- Clinical features of aortic stenosis\n- Classic triad (breathlessness, chest pain, exertional syncope)\n- rising small volurne with a sustained peak (pulsus parvus et tardus)\n- Cardiac impulse: Laterally displaced apex beat indicates onset of heart failure.\n- Auscultation: Harsh systolic ejection murmur second intercostal space right sternal edge and radiating to the carotids.\n- Fourth heart sound 'gallop rhythm' LVH criteria (or strain pattern) but may be absent despite severe obstruction: 10-15% of severe AS have normal ECGs, may show RBBB or LBBB.\n- CXR: May show normal sized heart and a dilated proximal ascending aorta. Late signs are of LV/LA dilatation and pulmonary oedema\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 55-year-old male known case of CKD with hyperkalemia 6.1 presented to the ED with complaint of respiratory distress On initial assessment ECG was done which revealed no significant changes. What is the most appropriate management in this patient?\n- **A)** Magnesium Sulphate\n- **B)** Potassium sparing diuretics\n- **C)** Insulin with glucose IV infusion\n- **D)** IV Ca gluconate\n- **E)** IV Calcium Chloride Explanation Hyperkalemia management algorithm\n\nusing ABCDE appro*h 12\n- Iead ECG and monitor cardiac rhythm serum potassium (K+) 6.0 Exclude pseudohyperkalaemia embAriga! 5-5-5.9 K.6.o.6.4mml/L ECG ECG Changes? peaked T Waves Broad QRS Bradycardia Flat/ absent P waves NO OR Calcium GI u cmate IV Insulin—Glucose IV Infusion: Give IO units soluble imsunn in 25 g glucose over 15-30 min REDUCE THE RISK OV HYPOGLYCAEMIA If Ore-treatment blood 21ucOse\n- Z 7 Omrnol/l, follow with glucose infusion: 10% glucose   50ml/hr for 5 hrs (258) Salbutamol IO — 20 mg Nebulised\n- **C)** 15g tdS Ora% OR cwlosikate 108 tds for 77 OR 8.48 once daily k twlp K +\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nS despite medical therapy Monitor serum K + and blood glucose Consider cause and prevent further rise or recurrence\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 9-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complain of hip pain and unable to bear weight. His X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Slipped upper femoral epiphysis\n- **B)** Polymyositis\n- **C)** Soft tissue injury\n- **D)** Perthes disease\n- **E)** Transient Synovitis Explanation\n- X-ray shows femoral head deformity and flattening with widening of joint space and blurring of the physeal plate on the left.\n- Patient clinical features and X-ray suggests Perthes disease\n- Perthes' disease\n- It's also known as Legg-Calvé-Perthes.\n- It's an idiopathic avascular necrosis of the hip, usually affecting children from 3 to 12 years\n- Clinical features:\n\n- Pt present with a slow onset pain and with an antalgic gait. And limitation to internal rotation and abduction of the hip.\n- Investigation: frog-leg lateral view x-ray will reveal widening of joint space and decrease in size of femoral head with patchy density. MRI can evaluate better.\n- Complications: chronic pain and osteoarthritis\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nAn II-year-old soccer player brought to the ED with complaint of Knee pain that aggravated while doing sports. On examination, there is tenderness over tibial tuberosity. HIS X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Proximal fibular fracture\n- **B)** Avulsion of tibial tuberosity fracture C\n- Osgood schlatter disease\n- **D)** Distal femur fracture\n- **E)** Patellar fracture Explanation\n- X-ray shows bone fragmentation at the tibial tuberosity.\n- Patient clinical features and X-ray suggests Osgood-Schlatter disease.\n\n- Osgood-Schlatter disease\n- Osgood-Schlatter disease, also known as apophysitis of the tibial tubercle, is a chronic fatigue injury due to repeated microtrauma at the patellar tendon insertion onto the tibial tuberosity, usually affecting boys between ages 10-15 years.\n- The typical age of onset in females may be slightly earlier (boys 10-15 years; girls 8-12 years) .\n- Osgood-Schlatter disease is seen in active adolescents, especially those who jump and kick\n- The typical age of onset in females may be slightly earlier (boys 10-15 years; girls 8-12 years) .\n- Clinical features\n- Clinically, patients present with pain and swelling over the tibial tuberosity exacerbated with exercise.\n- Investigations\n- Plain radiograph - Soft tissue swelling with loss of the sharp margins of the patellar tendon is the earliest sign in the acute phase, thus, a compatible history is also essential in making the diagnosis.\n- Bone fragmentation at the tibial tuberosity may be evident 3 to 4 weeks after the onset.\n- Management\n\n- Treatment is usually conservative and involves rest, ice, activity modification (decreasing activities that stress the insertion, especially jumping and lunging sports), and quadriceps and hamstring strengthening exercises. Analgesia and padding to prevent pressure on the tibial tuberosity are also useful.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 22 year old male presented to ED with complaint of progressive painful lower limb weakness and difficulty in walking. You note sensory loss and loss of reflex on his lower limb examination. His upper limb examination is normal. Initial blood investigations have been sent. What is the most likely diagnosis in this Patient?\n- **A)** Poliomyelitis\n- **B)** Myasthenia gravis   C' Transverse myelitis\n- **D)** Guillain-Barre syndrome\n- **E)** Acute spinal stroke Explanation\n- motor weakness\n\n- paraesthesias or sensory loss\n- bladder symptoms: urinary frequency, urgency, incontinence, or retention\n- bowel symptoms: incontinence or constipation\n- Chermitte sign\n- McArdle's sign\n- paroxysmal tonic spasms\n- upper motor neuron signs: hyper-reflexia, positive Babinski's sign, limb spasticity\n- sensory loss/sensory level\n- areflexia/hyporeflexia\n\nGuillain-Barre syndrome (GBS)\n- It is a clinically defined syndrome characterized by motor difficulty, absence Of deep tendon reflexes, paresthesias without objective sensory loss, and increased cerebrospinal fluid albumin With a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation).\n- Risk factors Viral illness - Of patients with CBS have a history Of gastroenteritis or influenza-like illness. hepatitis E infection Bacterial illness - to Campylo cter jejun i hepatitis E infection 1\n- Clinical features Muscle wea kness -Progressive symmetric muscle weakness usually affecting lower extremities before upper extremities and proximal muscles before distal muscles (ascending paralysis) accompanied by paresthesias in the feet and hands is typical, The paralysis is typically flaccid with areflexia and progresses acutely over days\n- Paresthesia\n- Back/leg pain\n- Respiratory distress\n- Areflexia/hyporeflexia dysarth ria. Investigations Nerve conduction studies -\n- Facial weakness, dysphagia, diplopia,\n- areflexia/hyporefiexia prolonged distal and F-wave latencies and reduced conduction velocities; H reflex prolonged or absent\n- lumbar puncture - elevated CSF protein, normal/slightly high lymphocytes (<50 cells/mm3). LFT- AST and ALT as high as SOO U/L Spirometry\n- may show reduced vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure.\n- Consider antigarÜside antibody, serology, stool culture. Transverse myelitis\n- Transverse myelitis (TM) is a pathogenetically heterogeneous focal inflammatory disorder Of the spinal cord characterised by acute or subacute development of motor weakness, sensory impairment, and autonomic dysfunction ,\n- It is typically associated With multiple sclerosis.\n- Risk factors preceding infectious illness recent vaccination female sex\n- history of recent physical trauma\n- Clinical features motor weakness paraesthesias or sensory loss\n- bladder symptoms: urinary frequency, urgency, incontinence, or retention\n- bowel symptoms: incontinence or constipation\n- Lhermitte sign\n- McArdle's sign paroxysmal tonic spasms upper motor neuron signs; hyper- reflexia, positive Babinski's sign, limb spasticity\n- sensory loss/sensory level\n- Investigations MRI spinal cord\n- MRI brain\n- Serum aquaporin-4 auto-antibodies and myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein auto-antibodies Cerebrospinal fluid cell count, cell differential, protein level, IgG index, oligoclonal band Myasthenia gravis (MG) Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a chronic autoimmune disorder of the post- synaptic membrane at the neuromuscular junction in skeletal muscle. Circulating antibodies against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (AChR) or associated proteins impair neuromuscular transmission.\n- Risk factors\n- family history Of autoimmune disorders genetic markers - Specific HLA genes cancer-targeted therapy\n- Clinical features\n- muscle strength fatigability\n- The weakness worsens with activity or repetition (fatigable weakness) and improves on rest.\n- ptosis\n- diplopia, dysphagia , dysarthria facial paresis proximal limb weakness shortness Of breath\n- Investigations serum acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody analysis\n- muscle-specific tyrosine kinase (MUSK) antibodies serial pulmonary function tests\n- single-fibre EMG\n- CT of chest Should be performed in all newly diagnosed patients With adult onset MG to detect thymoma\n- Extractable nuclear antigen (including SSA and SS8) serum and cerebrospinal fluid para neopla stic auto-antibodies\n- cerebrospinal fluid cytology and flow cytometry\n- serology for herpes simplex virus HSV\n- 2, varicella zoster virus, —cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus—\n\n- management\n- intravenous immune globulin (IVIG) or plasma exchange NeggzIVIG is contraindicate in patient With IgA deficiency and renal failure. Supportive treatment\n- vital monitoring and appropriate prophylactic anticoagulation for DVT.\n- Complications Respiratory failure\n- Bladder areflexia Adynamic ileus\n- paralysis and West Nile virus\n- Mana ment\n- with 3 to 5 consecutive daily infusions Of corticosteroids is associated with more rapid recovery Of neurological deficits. methylprednisolone: 1000 mg intravenously OD\n- active rehabilitation. DVT prophylaxis, bladder catheterization. Seggnd.Une.&Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis)\n- immunosuppressive treatment (azathioprine. mycophenolate— recommended in patient with positive aquaporin-4 auto-antibody seropositive Or myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein-IgG auto-antibody seropositive\n- management\n- intubation and mechanical ventilation plasma exchange or intravenous immunoglobulin Corticosteroid - prednisolone: 1 to 1.5 rng/kg orally once daily\n- Mild to moderate disease (class I to pyridostigmine\n- Consider — corticosteroid\n- Consider— immunosuppressant (Azathioprine) Consider — rituximab\n- Consider- thymectomy 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 42-year-old industrial worker presented to the ED with complaint of blood in urine, weight-loss and occasionally abdominal pain. He has a 40 pack-year smoking history. His examination, revealed firm, non-tender and homogenous mass in the right flank. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Bladder carcinoma\n- **B)** Nephritis\n- **C)** Renal calculi   D\n- Renal cell carcinoma\n- **E)** Cystitis\n\nExplanation\n- Renal cell Carcinoma Most common 10 renal malignancy\n- Most common in males 50—70 years old\n- Increase incidence with tobacco smoking and obesity.\n- Originate from PCT\n- Clinical features\n- Classic triad :\n- flank pain\n- palpable mass\n- hematuria\n- Other feature (fever, polycythemia, weight-loss)\n- Investigations-CT-scan\n- Management;-surgical intervention , immunotherapy eg:ipilimumab previOUS  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	23	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
74b6c1b4-4a7e-4d00-a5f6-b8c30d74c3d1	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-25	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 25	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 25\n\nA\n- IV haloperidole\n- **B)** 1M phenytoin D C\n- IV diazepam\n- **D)** IV midazolam E\n- 1M lorazepam EXPIanation\n- Choice of drugs for rapid tranquilisation / Sedation NICE guideline\n- Intramuscular lorazepam QR\n- Intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine\n- If no response Or partial response to above drugs, consider a further dose If there is evidence of cardiovascular disease, including a prolonged QT interval, or no electrocardiogram has been carried out, avoid intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine RCEM guideline\n- Ketamine 4mg/kglM 08 Droperidol 5-10mg 1M\n- If these agents are unavailable, midazolam (5-10mg 1M), lorazepam (4mg 1M) or haloperidol (5mg 1M +/- 2mg lorazepam 1M) could be considered. Acute behavioural disturbance\n\nAcute behavioural disturbance (also previously called excited delirium,\n- acute behavioural disorder, or agitated delirium) is an umbrella term used to describe a presentation which may include abnormal physiology and/or behaviour. Risk factors\n- Substance intoxication (Cocaine, Methamphetamine , cocaine with\n- recent binge use etc) Mental health conditions\n- Substance withdrawal\n- Serotonin syndrome\n-\n- Hypoxia\n- Hypog lycaemia Electrolyte disturbance\n- Other conditions —Sepsis, Head injury ,Anticholinergic syndrome,\n- Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, Thyroid storm, Heat stroke, Seizures.\n- Note—Substance intoxication and Mental health conditions are the most common factors in ABD presentations\n- Clinical features Agitation Constant physical activity\n-\n- Bizarre behaviour (incl. paranoia, hypervigilance) Fear, panic\n- Unusual or unexpected strength\n- Sustained non-compliance with police or ambulance staff\n-\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Pain tolerance, impervious to pain Hot to touch, sweating Rapid breathing Tachycardia Assess-and-manage the risk Managing - Patients experiencing ABD may lose their ability to interact with their environment safely, and their perception of risk may appear non-existent. they are likely to react in such a way as to put themselves at risk, especially while attempting to escape a perceived risk, the perception of which may be increased if restraint is applied. Patients presenting with severe ABD are likely to lack mental capacity (which should be formally assessed and documented) to make treatrnent decisions and may require emergency treatrnent under the appropriate legislation. Verbal de-escalation should always be attempted. If safe and available, friends and family may be able to assist. Advice on verbal de-escalation is found in Section\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nManaging risks to staff - Staff who have not had approved training should not be asked to restrain patients. Early escalation to on-site security services is recommended. Hospitals should have sufficient trained security staff available to be able to safely restrain a patient, to keep them from harm and to protect others. Do not attempt to restrain patients with insufficient numbers of staff. There are significant risks to staff when attempting to restrain patients,\n\nand these are compounded by insufficient team numbers.\n- Restraint - Attempts should be made to remove any ongoing restraint at the earliest opportunity. There is concern that in ABD, continued exertion under restraint can contribute to poor outcomes (likely due to increasing catecholamine levels, worsening hyperthermia, and metabolic acidosis).\n- Prolonged restraint should prompt consideration of rapid tranquilisation.\n- While the police should not normally be called to undertake restrictive practices in healthcare settings solely to facilitate clinical interventions, it has been established that there are scenarios in which police support should be requested:\n- if healthcare staff have been injured\n- if appropriate support is not available from healthcare colleagues in a sufficiently timely manner to ensure the safety of all those affected\n- where there is a risk of serious injury or damage, and safety is compromised.\n- De-escalation - Verbal de-escalation is a valuable tool with which to facilitate patient care and potentially avoid any requirement for restraint.\n- Offer oral sedative - lorazepam 1-2mg (max 4mg per day).\n\nFeatures of ideal environment for verbal and environmental de-\n- escalation: Dedicated to management of patients with severe agitation (or if not\n- possible, a space that can be created or adapted quickly)\n- Adequate and appropriately located exits so that staff can exit without being trapped by the patient Doors which open outwards\n- Quiet, Iow stimulus\n- Not too warm\n- Absence of equipment/furniture and moveable objects that could be a\n- potential weapon or used to barricade an exit\n- Absence of potential ligature points Constantly observable\n- Staff able to signal need for additional support easily\n-\n- Rapid-trangui/isationlsedation Rapid tranquilisation in ABD is important to prevent further\n- sympathetic over-stimulation and excessive muscular activity from causing a metabolic storm and subsequent cardiovascular collapse. Rapid tranquilisation can also prevent the patient from causing\n- physical harm to themselves or others and facilitate investigations and treatments.\n- The use of rapid tranquilisation for more severe ABD presentations is associated with reduced mortality.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Parenteral rapid tranquilisation is more likely to be required if patients: lack capacity to refuse treatment and are non-compliant, and pose a danger to themselves or others, or have a clear need for further treatment/investigation Choice of drugs for rapid tranquilisation / Sedation NICE guideline\n- Intramuscular lorazepam QR\n- Intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine\n- If no response Or partial response to above drugs, consider a further dose If there is evidence of cardiovascular disease, including a prolonged QT interval, or no electrocardiogram has been carried out, avoid intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine Once sedative taken effect: RCEM guideline\n- Ketamine 4mg/kglM 08 Droperidol 5-10mg 1M\n- If these agents are unavailable, midazolam (5-10mg 1M), lorazepam (4mg 1M) Or haloperidol (5mg 1M +/- 2mg lorazepam 1M) could be considered. Establish and secure intravenous access Monitor in a high dependency area (Resus Room) Maintain sedation with IV bolus medication until definitive management plan arranged Correct electrolyte / glucose / acid-base abnormalities, correct hyperthermia, look for evidence of DIC or rhabdomyolysis. Institute other emergency treatments and investigations as necessary.\n\n- Document clearly in the clinical notes:\n- Indication(s) for RT & any attempts at de-escalation\n- Mental capacity assessrnent\n- Restraint, Collateral history, Features consistent with ABD, Police involvement.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nWhich of following is not a sign of upper airway obstruction ?\n- **A)** Stridor\n- **B)** Snoring c. Grunting   D\n- Expiratory wheeze\n- **E)** Hoarseness Explanation\n- Expiratory wheeze caused by lower airway obstruction.\n\nFeatures/ sign of airway obstruction Hoarseness Stridor Drooling Snoring Gurgling Expiratory wheeze caused by obstruction of upper airways caused by obstruction of upper airway unable to swallow secretion (upper airway obstruction) caused by obstruction at or above the laryngeal level (upper airway) vomit, blood or secretions in airway (upper airway ) caused by obstruction of lower airways\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 15-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of foot pain while doing sports. He states that he feels difficulty in walking and running. His foot x-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Navicular fracture\n- **B)** Avulsion fracture of metatarsal base\n- **C)** Lis-franc fracture\n- **D)** Jones fracture\n- **E)** Stress fracture\n\nExplanation\n- X-ray shows widening of lisfranc interspce foot\n- Moi:Heavy objects falling onto the feet or being run over by a vehicle tyre or wheel.\n- It is common in football and soccer players.\n- Pjagnosjszrequest a standard foot x-ray.\n- Look to check that the medial side of the second MT is correctly aligned with the medial side of the middle cuneiform.\n- Management;\n- Support in a backslab POP, and refer if there are multiple, displaced, or dislocated MT fractures.\n\n- X-ray shows fracture fragment at the lateral aspect of the base of the first metatarsal (arrow) and lateral displacement of the first metatarsal relative to the first cuneiform. Additionally, lateral dislocation of the second through fifth metatarsals is seen.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA IO year old girl has fallen off her skateboard and injured her elbow. His X-ray is shown. What abnormality is demonstrated on x-ray? D\n- **A)** Olecranon fracture\n- **B)** Supracondylar fracture\n- **C)** Salter harris type 2 distal humerus\n- **D)** Buckle fracture proximal radial shaft\n- **E)** Radial head fracture Explanation\n- X-ray shows supracondylar fractures\n- Supracondylar fracture\n- Common in children 3-10 years of age however peak at the age of 7.\n- It is caused by a fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow hyperextended\n\n- Classification of supracondylar fracture\n- Type-I fractures are displaced Q mm and may have a posterior fat pad sign on X-ray\n- Type-2 fractures are angulated to varying degrees, but the posterior cortex of the humerus is intact Type4fractures are completely displaced with no cortical contact\n- Diagnosis\n- X-ray will show Fat-pad sign or fracture\n- Draw anterior humeral line along the front of the humeral shaft on the lateral view normally it passes though the middle of ossification center of capitulum . Management\n- Type-I fracture manage with analgesia and immobilization with a long- arm posterior splint or collar and cuff with the elbow at 90 degrees and the forearm in pronation or neutral rotation and arrange fracture clinic within 2-7 days\n- Urgent referral for type-II and type-III supracondylar fracture or those with neurovascular deficit or >50% displacement.\n- Complications\n- Early complications\n\n- Neurological-injury\n- Radial nerve injury\n- Median nerve injury (anterior interosseous branch)\n- Ulnar nerve injury (iatrogenic)\n- Vascular-injury\n- Brachial artery injury\n- Volkmann's ischemic contracture (compartment syndrome of the forearm)\n- Late-complications\n- Nonunion, Malunion Myositis ossificans\n- Loss of motion\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 36-year-old male brought to the ED with an overdose of opioid. On examination, his BP is  mmHg , RR 11 breath per minutes, and He has pinpoint pupil. Following 800mcg IV naloxone patient condition has been improved, however, patient condition has been deteriorated after a short period of time and his GCS is 6 now. Which of following is your next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Set a naloxone infusion\n- **B)** Apply face mask oxygen\n- **C)** Administer flumazenil\n- **D)** Get a surgical airway\n- **E)** Intubate the patient Explanation\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma.\n- When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion adjusted according to vital signs. Opioid overdose (narcotic analgesics)\n- Clinical features of opioid overdose\n- Respiratory depression\n- Pinpoint pupil . Coma\n- Antidote\n- Naloxone-hydrochloride\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma.\n- When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion\n\nadjusted according to vital signs.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 16-year-old male presented to the ED with sudden onset of severe testicular pain. Which of the following feature will support testicular torsion?\n- **A)** Blue dot sign\n- **B)** LOSS of cremesteric reflex\n- **C)** Increase in alpha feto-protein D\n- **D)** Opposite testis lie vertically\n- **E)** Positive prehn's sign Explanation\n- There is Loss of ipsilateral cremesteric reflex in testicular torsion\n\n- Testicular torsion\n- Twisting of spermatic cord leads to reduce blood flow to testicle.\n- Its common in under 20s\n- Individuals with bellÆlapper deformity predispose to testicular torsion Clinical features:\n- Severe pain usually associated with nausea and vomiting Examination reveal swollen, tender, high-riding testis, with an abnormal transverse lie.\n- Opposite testis lies horizontally (Angell's sign)\n- Loss of ipsilateral cremasteric reflex Negative prehn's sign (elevate the testis will relive pain in epididymitis but not in torsion) Investigation: Doppler ultrasonography and nuclear scintigraphy. However, surgical exploration for suspected testicular torsion should never be delayed for imaging ana eme Urgent surgical exploration with intraoperative detorsion and fixation of the testes\n- Torsion of testicular appendaqe/Hvdatid e! Morqaqni Appendix testis and appendix epididymis are testicular embryologic remnants and that can twist and eventually leads to infarction of appendage.\n- Common in age between 7-12 years Clinical features:\n- pain is less severe compare to testicular torsion and no systemic symptoms\n- "Blue Dot Sign"- it's an infarcted appendage through the scrotal skin Investigation: Doppler US managemeng\n- Scrotal support and NSAID\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 45 year old man presents with significant haematemesis. His PMH includes alcoholic liver disease and oesophageal varices. His initial observations are: HR: 110 beats per minute BP: 90/50mmHg, sp02: 95% on air, Temp: 35 90C, RR: 28 per minute GCS: 12 E3/V4/M5\n\nHe has resuscitation ongoing with blood, terlipressin, vitamin K and pantoprazole and has been referred for emergency endoscopy. Which one of the following additional treatments is most appropriate?\n- **A)** Administer prothrombin complex\n- **B)** Ondansetron\n- **C)** Start tranexamic acid\n- **D)** Give calcium chloride\n- **E)** Give a broad-spectrum antibiotic Explanation Offer prophylactic antibiotic therapy_(usually Ceftriaxone) at presentation to patients with suspected or confirmed variceal bleeding.\n- Prophylactic antibiotics reduce the rate of bacterial infection, treatment failure, rebleeding, and mortality.\n- Initial management of upper GI bleeding_\n- UGIB causing hypotension, tachycardia, orthostasis, or other signs of hypovolemic shock must be managed swiftly, and patients should be\n\nconsidered for admission to the intensive care unit.\n- Resuscitation\n- Two large-bore intravenous lines should be placed immediately for adequate venous access.\n- Crystalloid fluids - should be infused to maintain adequate blood pressure.\n- Blood product transfusions - Transfuse patients with massive bleeding with blood, platelets and clotting factors in line with local protocols for managing massive bleeding.\n- Do not offer platelet transfusion to patients who are not actively bleeding and are haemodynamically stable.\n- Offer platelet transfusion to patients who are actively bleeding and have a platelet count of less than 50 x 109/1itre.\n- Offer fresh frozen plasma to patients who are actively bleeding and have a prothrombin time (or international normalised ratio) or activated partial thromboplastin time greater than 1.5 times normal. If a patient's fibrinogen level remains less than 1.5 g/litre despite fresh frozen plasma use, offer cryoprecipitate as well.\n- Offer prothrombin complex concentrate to patients who are taking warfarin and actively bleeding.\n- Timing of endoscopy_\n- Offer endoscopy to unstable patients with severe acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding immediately after resuscitation.\n- Offer endoscopy within 24 hours of admission to all other patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n\n- Management of non-variceal bleeding\n- Endoscopic treatment - Do not use adrenaline as monotherapy for the endoscopic treatrnent of non-variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding. bleeding use one of the following;\n- a mechanical method (for example, clips) with or without adrenaline\n- thermal coagulation with adrenaline\n- fibrin or thrombin with adrenaline.\n- Offer a repeat endoscopy to patients who re-bleed with a view to further endoscopic treatrnent or emergency surgery.\n- Offer interventional radiology to unstable patients who re-bleed after endoscopic treatment. Refer urgently for surgery if interventional radiology is not promptly available.\n- proton pump inhibitors to patients with non-variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding and stigmata of recent haemorrhage shown at endoscopy.\n- Management-of-variceal-bleeding Offer-terlipressin-or_vasopressin, somatostatin to patients with suspected variceal bleeding at presentation. Stop treatrnent after definitive haemostasis has been achieved, or after 5 days, unless there is another indication for its use\n- Terlipressin has been shown to reduce blood loss from actively bleeding varices and confers a 34% relative risk reduction in risk of\n\nmortality.\n- Ceftriaxone) at presentation to patients with suspected or confirmed variceal bleeding.\n- In oesophageal varices — Use endoscopic band ligation in patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from oesophageal varices.\n- Consider transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunts (TIPS) if bleeding from oesophageal varices is not controlled by band ligation.\n- Imgastr.icya.r.i.ces\n- Offer endoscopic injection of N-butyI-2-cyanoacryIate to patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from gastric varices.\n- Offer TIPS if bleeding from gastric varices is not controlled by endoscopic injection of N-butyI-2-cyanoacryIate.\n- A balloon tamponade device can be used to quell the bleeding until the shunt is placed (Sengstaken-Blakemore for esophageal varices, Linton-Nachlas for gastric varices).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 29 year old man presented to the ED with complaint of fainting episodes and he feels that his heart is racing. He has diarrhea and vomiting for the past few days and has unable to keep anything down for 24 hours. He admits to daily drug use including cocaine and amphetamine. His observation are: HR 145 bpm Bp  mmHg\n\nTemp 36.50C His ECG shown. What is most appropriate initial management?\n- **A)** Synchronised cardioversion\n- **C)** Vagal manouevres and adenosine D\n- IV lorazepam\n- **E)** 5mg bisoprolol Explanation\n- ECC reveals sinus tachycardia.\n- Beta-blockers should be avoided owing to the risk of coronary vasoconstriction and paradoxical hypertension\n- Patient has history diarrhea and vomiting for the past few days, thus he is hypovolemic and requires IV fluid Cocaine toxicity_\n- Cocaine is a drug of abuse that is usually either insufflated (snorted), injected, or smoked in its freebase form (crack). Cocaine is a type IC\n\nanti-dysrhythmic, local anaesthetic, and sympathomimetic. Cocaine toxicity occurs within minutes to hours of excessive cocaine\n- use. Risk factors\n- High ambient temperature Male sex\n- Plasma cholinesterase deficiency\n- Age 18-25 years History of cocaine use Concomitant use of other sympathomimetics\n-\n- Clinical features Tachycardia\n- Hypertension Hyperthermia\n-\n- Agitation Mydriasis\n- Diaphoresis Investigations\n- ECG — reveals normal, sinus tachycardia, supraventricular tachycardia,\n- ventricular dysrhythmia, ischaemic changes.\n- ECC should be performed in all patients with tachycardia, chest pain, or dyspnoea following cocaine use.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Bedside serum glucose - normal, hypoglycaemia or hyperglycaemia Serum creatinine - elevated in volume depletion or rhabdomyolysis Urea - elevated in hypovolaemia or renal insufficiency Serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) - elevated in rhabdomyolysis Serum troponin - elevated in myocardial infarction Consider CXR, CT brain and urine cocaine assay\n- Management Observation and monitoring Agitation and or seizures hypertension volume depletion hyperthermia, temperature 39'C to 41.2'C or more with/without severe shivering, agitation, or muscle rigidity Arrhythmias co-ingestion Of opioid\n- All patients should be monitored in the ED, including cardiac monitoring Benzodiazepines (diazepam, midazolam, lorazepam) are the drugs of choice for the management Of agitation/ seizures.\n- Benzodiazepine or antihypertensive therapy (intravenous nitrates or calcium-channel blockers)\n- Beta-blockers should be avoided owing to the risk Of coronary vasoconstriction and paradoxical hypertension\n- Correct volume depletion with intravenous isotonic saline External cooling + sedation + paralysis (vecuronium) + mechanical ventilation Ventricular fibrillation or non-perfusing (pulseless) ventricular tachycardia requires immediate defibrillation. Wide complex tachycardias are managed with sodium bicarbonate, or lidocaine if sodium bicarbonate is ineffective Consider naloxone 1\n\n- Note—Patient suspected/confirmed cocaine toxicity: body packer/stuffer — In addition to above treatment refer patient for immediate surgical decontamination If packet rupture occurs (based on symptoms, signs, or positive urine cocaine screen).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 32-year-old male presented to the ED with complaint of painless bleeding. He states that he has found fresh bright red blood on the toilet paper. What is the most likely diagnosis ?\n- **A)** Peptic ulcer disease\n- **B)** Irritable bowel syndrome\n- **C)** Diverticulitis\n- **D)** Anal fissure   E\n- Hemorrhoids Explanation\n- Suspect Hemorrhoids if young patient has painless bright red blood on the toilet paper\n\nClinical features Management Haemorrhoids Bright red, painless rectal bleeding\n- Anal itching or irritation\n- A feeling of rectal fullness, discomfort Soiling (due to mucus discharge or impaired continence) Pain is not usually reported in both internal & external haemorrhoids.\n- perform DRE\n- A rigid anoscope. proctoscope, or rectoscope can be used to make a diagnosis of haemorrhoids\n- Conservative-lifestyle and d ietary modification, specifically increased fibre intake and adequate fluids. Non-surgical\n- Rubber band ligation, Injection sclerotherapy, Infrared coagulation. Surgical- Haemorrhoidectomy, Stapled haemorrhoidectomy, Haemorrhoidal artery ligation. Peri-anal hematoma A perianal hematoma is a pool Of blood that collects in the tissue surrounding the anuse\n- bubbling or bulging skin near the anus (lump)\n- mild to severe pain, depending on the size\n- bloody stools (fresh blood) physical exam to diagnose a perianal hematoma. They only appear around the Outside of anus, so don't need a colonoscopy. Most perianal hematomas resolve on their own Within five to seven days.\n- If not, consider drainage under local anesthesia. Anal fissure\n- Anal pain always occurs with passing a stool\n- Bleeding may occur with defecation.\n- A tearing sensation on passing stool may also be described. In children, anal fissure typically presents with pain and/or crying during defecation and rectal bleeding Examine the person- Acute anal fissures are typically superficial with well\n- dema rcated edges.\n- Stool softener and bulk agents. Analgesia\n- Topical 0.4% or diltiazem 2%.\n- Sphincterotomy— reserved for non-healing fissure.  IO. A two year old boy is brought into the ED by his mother with a two day history of barking cough and shortness of breath, which is worse at night. He has mild fever. In the ED, he has audible stridor and has a hoarse voice. Which is the most likely causative organism?\n- **A)** Parvovirus B19\n- **B)** Adenovirus\n- **C)** Haemophilus Influenza type B\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- D\n- Parainfluenza virus\n- **E)** Respiratory syncytial virus Explanation Cause of croup Parainfluenza virus types I or 3 Clinical features Sudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which associated with\n- Stridor Respiratory distress. Symptoms are typically worse at night and increase with agitation. e\n	\N	[]	\N	f	24	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a8901204-78f5-4853-94a6-c80444f1acb2	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-26	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 26	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 26\n\n- **A)** Pulmonary vasoconstriction from cannabis smoking\n- **B)** Methaemoglobinaemia from amyl nitrate use\n- **C)** Hypoxia secondary to Camma Hydroxy Butyrate ingestion\n- **D)** Pulmonary vasoconstriction from cannabis smoking\n- **E)** CNS depression and hypoventilation from opiates EXPIanation\n- Consider Methaemoglobinaemia diagnosis in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.\n\nCO poisoning Carbon monoxide is a colourless, odourless, tasteless gas produced by incomplete combustion of fuels — this occurs when insufficient oxygen is present. Exposure to carbon monoxide causes tissue hypoxia, because carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin with about 240 times the affinity of oxygen and forms carboxyhaemoglobin.\n- Exoosure to CO mav occur:\n- Chimneys or flues are blocked\n- lack of ventilation in the room\n- Barbecues are used inside homes, caravans or tents\n- Smoke in burning buildings Soot or yellow/brown staining around or on appliances. People are at risk of CO isonin\n- Older people, children Women who are pregnant People with anaemia, Respiratory or cardiovascular disease\n- Clinical features Headache, nausea & vomiting Vertigo, dizziness Cyanide poisoning Cyanide poisoning IS most often linked to house fires and combustion of products containing carbon and nitrogen. primarily foam rubber, wool, plastics, and other synthetic materials.\n- CN disrupts the electron transport chain in the mitochondrial production of ATP by binding to and inhibiting cytochrome oxidase a.\n- As aerobic cellular metabolism ceases, cells are forced into anaerobic metabolism With the subsequent generation of lactate\n- Clinical features\n- Neurologic symptoms - confusion, agitation, vertigo, headache, seizure,coma.\n- Gastrointestinal - nausea, vomiting,\n- Cardiac symptoms - chest pain, cardiac arrythmia Hypotension and bradycardia are common in cyanide poisoning\n- pulmonary symptoms\n- dyspnea tachypnae, pulmonary edema Bright red bleeding of mucous membranes\n- Flushing of the skin Necroses in mouth and esophagus Bright red retinal vein on fundoscopic examination cherry red skin (very late and not often present), cyanosis Patient usually not cyanotic but maybe after prolonged respiratory failure and shock.\n- Investiqations\n- Arterial or Venous blood qas - Methemoglobinemia Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood.\n- Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2\n- ) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3\n- ). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%\n- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital or acquired\n- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood\n- Causes\n- Benzocaine Dapsone\n- Nitroglycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin Nitroprusside Phenazopyridine\n- Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")\n- Clinical features\n- Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:\n- < 10% - None (patients with underlying diseases may have mc.re symptoms at lower level) Slight discoloration (eg, pale, gray, blue) of the skin.\n- Chocolate brown colored blood\n- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia, lightheadedness\n- Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain\n- 50-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status. delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis\n- > 70% - Usually, death Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is > 1.5 gld\n- Investiqations VBG or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in conscicmsness— toxicitv is\n\nDyspnoea, pain (in chest or stomach)\n- sleep changes, emotional lability\n- delayed neuropsychiatric features (memory impairment, disorientation, irritability, inability to concentrate, personality change, emotional lability, neuropathy, incontinence,)\n- severe neurological symptoms (Seizure may develop in people with\n- severe poisoning) CO poisoning should be suspected in fire victims, in patients with flu- like symptoms in cold climates, especially if any other cohabitants or a normal arterial oxygen tension and an abnormally high venous oxygen tension. Normal to elevated Pa02 level (very high levels of Pa02 suggestive of CN poisoning, reflection of cell's inability to extract and use oxygen) pets are also feeling ill\n- Investi ations give falsely normal readings because it is not capable of distinguishing between oxyhemoglobin and carboxyhemoglobin saturation gap refers to a patient with carbon monoxide poisoning and hypoxemia, This produces a "gap" between the pulse oximetry (which is close to 100%) compared to the Pa02 on ABG (which may be low). ABGs\n- Pa02 — Normal or Maybe low\n- Carboxyhaemoglobin levels (COHb) - carboxyhaemoglobin level increased above the baseline.\n- This is the gold standard test.\n- pH - Tlssue hypoxia may lead to high anion-gap metabolic acidosis. This is due to the buildup of lactate and byproducts of anaerobic metabolism.(severe cases)\n- 12-lead ECG — check for sinus tachycardia or cardiac ischaemia\n- CT or MRI-Overtime, CT or MRI scanning may show decreased density in central white matter (e.g., globus pallidus. putamen, and\n- A high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Normal oxygen saturation on pulse oximetry is expected as there is adequate oxygen in the blood yet inability to use the oxygen that is present\n- Blood lactate level - elevated lactate>Bmmol/L 94% sensitive for cyanide toxicity ECC to assess for dysrhythmia\n- FBC, LFTs\n- Mana ement Patient decontamination (e.g., remove clothes, wash skin)\n- Administration of 100% oxygen regardless of saturation readings Supportive care - Crystalloids and vasopressors, as needed, for hypotension\n- Antidote Hydroxocobalamin - (precursor of vitamin 812) - binds cyanide directly and forms cyanocobolmin, which is excreted in urine (first- line antidote)\n- Dose - The standard dose is 5 grams given intravenously\n- Sodium nitrite, amyl nitrite (Cyanide antidote kit) - or 4. dimethylaminophenol ( 4-DMAP) to induce methemoglobinemia (via oxidation of Hb) Methemoglobin binds to cyanide to form cyanomethemoglobin, which diverts cyanide from cytochrome complex IV and increases Oxidative phosphorylation. Sodium thiosulfate — (25%) 1.65 ml/kg up to 50ml IV\n- Caution on estimating pxvggn level - Routine pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%. pa02 readings on blood gases may be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia.\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible. Supportive care (IV access, hydration Or ventilation if necessary) anvone Who is svmptomatic (vital siqn abnormalitv. acidosis or signs of end organ damaqe) or anvone Who has a methemoqlobin level Give methylene blue: 1-2 mgfkg (0.1-0.2 mL/kg of 1% solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms. Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis. Manaqement in G6PD deficient patients Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis. Administration of an altemative reducing agent like vitamin C Consider exchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion\n\ncaudate nuclei).\n- Others — FBC, BR Troponin. Manaqement of CO Give 100% oxygen - Use a reservoir mask at 15 Uminute.\n- The half-life of carboxyhaemoglobin in a patient breathing air is about 300 minutes.\n- The half-life of carboxyhaemoglobin in a patient breathing high-concentration Oxygen via a reservoir mask is 90 minutes. Supportive measures — follow ABCDE protocol\n- Start fluid resuscitaton for hypotensive patients Obtain specialist advice for any patient:\n- Presenting with a carboxyhaemoglobin level of >25%\n- Who is pregnant\n- You suspect of self-harm/intentional poisoning hyperbaric oxygen therapy - Hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be beneficial in selected patients however, the evidence is weak and inconsistent. The UK NPIS does not currently recommend hyperbaric oxygen therapy Key points\n- Methemoglobinemia can result from exposure to a number of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).\n- Consider the diagnosis in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.\n- Administer methylene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs, metabolic acidosis, end organ dysfunction or, a serum level > 25%\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nYou have assessed a frail 80 year old woman with end-stage Lewy Body Dementia who is unable to communicate and has been driven to the ED by her husband after a choking episode. Due to the long wait, her husband tells you he has decided to take his wife home without being seen by a doctor. The following day she is brought to the department after a cardiac arrest and is dead on arrival. You have been asked to complete the death certificate by the bereavement team. What is the most appropriate course of action?\n- **A)** Contact the patient's CP to ask them if they can complete a certificate\n- **B)** Obtain more details from the husband before completing the death certificate\n- **C)** Complete the death certificate citing Lewy Body Dementia as the primary cause of death\n- **D)** Ask the doctor who verified death in the resuscitation room to complete the certificate\n- **E)** Report the death to the Coroner or Procurator Fiscal for investigation Explanation\n- Reporting deaths to the Coroner (or Procurator Fiscal)\n- Many deaths that occur in (or in transit to) the ED are sudden and unexpected, and/or follow trauma. The exact cause of death is seldom immediately apparent. Accordingly, do not be tempted to sign death certificates. Instead, report all deaths to the Coroner (the Procurator Fiscal in Scotland).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA previously healthy 8-month-old boy presents to the ED with new onset of cyanosis and fatigue first noticed this morning. The child was recently treated for thrush and his parents have been using over the counter benzocaine for teething. The child is sleepy but easily awakens and cries. He is not in respiratory distress.\n\nThe only vital sign abnormalities include a heart rate of 190 beats per minute at rest and oxygen saturation of 82% on room air. Placement of a non-rebreather facemask does not improve the patient's oxygenation. After confirming the diagnosis, which of the following treatments is indicated?\n- **A)** Hydroxocobalamin\n- **B)** Sodium thiosulfate\n- **C)** Congenital heart surgery D\n- Methylene blue\n- **E)** Heparin Explanation\n- Methemoglobinemia occurs when the iron associated with hemoglobin is changed through oxidation from a ferrous (Fe2+) to a ferric (Fe3+) state thus preventing the hemoglobin from binding oxygen. Patients with congenital methemoglobinemia generally have lifelong cyanosis but are otherwise asymptomatic. Substances known to cause acquired methemoglobinemia include dapsone and topical anesthetics, especially mucous membrane use of benzocaine, although it has been reported with lidocaine, tetracaine, or prilocaine use. Most reported cases are thought to be due to high doses or a break in the mucosal barrier such as by mucositis or thrush. Treatment\n\nfor methemoglobinemia includes removing the offending agent, and if symptomatic, administering methylene blue which activates a non- physiologic NADPH pathway for faster reduction of the iron molecule.\n- Congenital heart surgery (incorrect here) - would be indicated for a congenital heart defect such as Tetralogy of Fallot which can cause hypoxia in this age group, but is generally associated with fussiness or agitation during a "tet spell."\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 23 year old man with no previous medical history. While running on a hot marathon he has collapsed with dizziness. On arrival into the ED he is confused and combative. Pupils are dilated and slow to react. His observations are. BP: 90145mmHg HR: 120bpm Temp 42.10C His skin is pale, warm to touch and dry. Which is the correct statement regarding the management of this patient?\n- **A)** Targets for treatment include a core temperature below 390C\n- **B)** If rapid sequence induction anaesthesia is required, suxamethonium is preferred\n- **C)** Full ice-water immersion is recommended as method\n- **D)** Blood tests are not indicated in this patient\n- **E)** Paracetamol should be given as an anti-pyretic Explanation\n- Whole-body cold or iced water immersion - In practice, do not use water immersion in patients with an altered level of consciousness (Glasgow Coma Scale score <15), uncooperative patients, patients who require intravenous treatment, or patients who have a history of arrhythmia (e.g., atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia) or a recent.\n- target core temperature when cooling should be 38.5C. Once this temperature is reached active cooling measures are stopped.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 25 year old woman presents to the ED with a 12 hour history of palpitations. She has been having intermittent palpitations for several weeks but these have never been associated with any chest pain or dizziness. Her observations are:\n\nRR: sp02: 120 beats per minute 120/65mmHg 17 breath per minute 99% on room air\n-\n-\n-\n- Her ECG shows atrial fibrillation. What is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\n- **A)** Multinodular goiter B\n- Crave's disease\n- **C)** Thyroid adenoma   D\n- Thyroid storm\n- **E)** Thyroiditis Explanation Clinical features Heat intolerance Sweating Weight loss\n\nPalpitations - Including atrial fibrillation or other supraventricular\n- tachycardias, especially in older people Tremor\n-\n- Diffuse goitre\n- Orbitopathy Exercise intolerance, fatigue, muscle weakness\n- Polyuria, thirst, generalized itch.\n- Reduced libido, gynaecomastia in men\n- Specific features Rapid-onset malaise, fever, and thyroid pain (may suggest subacute\n- thyroiditis). Compression symptoms of breathlessness, hoarse voice, dysphagia,\n- neck pressure (may be caused by a toxic multinodular goitre). Features of thyroid storm\n- Fever\n- Tachycardia, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, heart failure\n- Agitation, hyperthermia,\n- Jaundice\n- Delirium, and coma\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 13-year-old male is brought into the ED with altered mental status after being found in a house fire. The fire department found burned couches in the house\n\nwith melted plastic covering. The patient has been mildly responsive en route to the ED. His observations are: RR: 78/40mmHg 155bpm 99.9F Oral 40 breath per minute sp02: 92% on 15L NRB He is somnolent but withdraws to pain. He has soot in his nares with carbonaceous sputum, and auscultation reveals rhonchorous and wheezing breath sounds bilaterally. Point of care VBG demonstrates a lactate of\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 16:\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?   A Methemoglobinemia\n- **B)** Type 1 respiratory failure\n- **C)** CO Poisoning   D\n- Cyanide Poisoning\n- **E)** Neuroleptic malignant Syndrome\n\nExplanation\n- Patient clinical features suggests cyanide poisoning as he is hypotensive and lactate is\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 16:\nCO poisoning Carbon monoxide is a colourless, odourless, tasteless gas produced by incomplete combustion of fuels — this occurs when insufficient oxygen is present. Exposure to carbon monoxide causes tissue hypoxia, because carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin with about 240 times the affinity of oxygen and forms carboxyhaemoglobin.\n- Exoosure to CO mav occur:\n- Chimneys or flues are blocked\n- lack of ventilation in the room Barbecues are used inside homes, caravans or tents\n- Smoke in burning buildings Soot or yellow/brown staining around or on appliances. People are at risk of CO poisoning\n- Older people, children Women who are pregnant People with anaemia, Respiratory or cardiovascular disease\n- Clinical features Headache, nausea & vomiting Vertigo, dizziness Cvanide poisoninq Cyanide poisoning IS most often linked to house fires and combustion of products containing carbon and nitrogen. primarily foam rubber, wool, plastics, and other synthetic materials.\n- CN disrupts the electron transport chain in the mitochondrial production of ATP by binding to and inhibiting cytochrome oxidase a.\n- As aerobic cellular metabolism ceases, cells are forced into anaerobic metabolism With the subsequent generation of lactate\n- Clinical features Neurologic symptoms - confusion, agitation, vertigo, headache, seizure,coma.\n- Gastrointestinal - nausea. vomiting,\n- Cardiac symptoms - chest pain, cardiac arrythmia Hypotension and bradycardia are common in cyanide poisoning\n- pulmonary symptoms\n- dyspnea tachypnae, pulmonary edema Bright red bleeding of mucous membranes\n- Flushing of the skin\n- Necroses in mouth and esophagus Bright red retinal vein on fundoscopic examination cherry red skin (very late and very rare), cyanosis Patient usually not cyanotic but maybe after prolonged respiratory failure and shock.\n- Investiqations\n- Arterial or Venous blood qas - Methemoglobinemia\n- Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood.\n- Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2\n- ) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3\n- ). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%\n- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital or acquired\n- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood\n- Causes\n- Benzocaine Dapsone\n- Nitroglycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin Nitroprusside\n- Phenazopyridine\n- Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite\n- Contaminated well water Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")\n- Clinical features Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:\n- < 10% - None (patients with underlying diseases may have more symptoms at lower level)\n- 10-20% - Slight discoloration (eg , pale, gray, blue) of the skin.\n- Chocolate brown colored blood\n- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia. lightheadedness Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain\n- 50-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status. delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis\n- > 70% - Usually, death Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is > 1.5 g/d\n- Investiqations\n- VBG or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in conscicmsness— qwanide toxicitv is\n\nDyspnoea, pain (in chest or stomach)\n- sleep changes, emotional lability\n- delayed neuropsychiatric features (memory impairment, disorientation, irritability, inability to concentrate, personality change. emotional lability, neuropathy, incontinence,) severe neurological symptoms (Seizure may develop in people with severe poisoning) CO poisoning should be suspected in fire victims, in patients with flu- like symptoms in cold climates, especially if any other cohabitants or pets are also feeling ill\n- Cherry-red discolouration of the skin and mucous membranes is an often-quoted sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning.\n- Investiqations pulse oximetry may give falsely normal readings because it is not capable of distinguishing between oxyhemoglobin and carboxyhemoglobin Oxygen-satur.atÅgn-gap=Oxygen saturation gap refers to a patient with carbon monoxide poisoning and hypoxemia This produces a "gap" between the pulse 0X1metry (which is close to 100%) compared to the Pa02 on ABG (which may be low). ABG±\n- Pa02 — Normal or Maybe low\n- Carboxyhaemoglobin levels (COHE)) - carboxyhaemoglobin level increased above the baseline.\n- This is the gold standard test.\n- pH - Tissue hypoxia may lead to high anion-gap metabolic acidosis. This is due to the buildup of lactate and byproducts of anaerobic metabolism.(severe cases)\n- 12-lead ECG — check for sinus tachycardia or cardiac ischaemia CT or MRI - over time, CT or MRI scanning may show decreased density in central white matter (e.g., globus pallidus. putamen, and a normal arterial oxygen tension and an abnormally high venous oxygen tension.\n- Normal to elevated Pa02 level (very high levels of Pa02 suggestive of CN poisoning, reflection of cell's inability to extract and use oxygen)\n- A high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Normal oxygen saturation on pulse oximetry is expected as there is adequate oxygen in the blood yet inability to use the oxygen that is present\n- Blood lactate level - elevated lactate>8mmol/L 94% sensitive for cyanide toxicity ECG to assess for dysrhythmia\n- FBC, LETS\n- Management Patient decontamination (e.g. , remove clothes, wash skin)\n- Administration of 100% oxygen regardless of saturation readings Supportive care - Crystalloids and vasopressors, as needed, for hypotension\n- Antidote Hydroxocobalamin - (precursor of vitamin 812) - binds cyanide directly and forms cyanocobolmin, which is excreted in urine (first- line antidote)\n- Dose - The standard dose is 5 grams given intravenously Sodium nitrite, amyl nitrite (Cyanide antidote kit) - or 4- dimethylaminophenol ( 4-DMAP) to induce methemoglobinemia (via oxidation of Hb) Methemoglobin binds to cyanide to form cyanomethemoglobin, which diverts cyanide from cytochrome complex IV and increases oxidative phosphorylation.\n- Sodium thiosulfate — (25%) I .65 ml/kg up to 50ml IVA Caution on estimating oxygen JeyeERoutine pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%. pa02 readings on blood gases may be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia.\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible.\n- Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary) Treat anyone who is symptomatic (Vital Siqn abnormalitv. acidosis or signs of end organ damaqe) or anyone who has a methemoqlobin level Give methylene blue: 1-2 mgfkg (O. 1-0.2 mukg of 1% solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes\n- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms. Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.\n- Manaqement in G6PD deficient patients Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis. Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C Consider exchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion\n\ncaudate nuclei).\n- Others — FBC, BR Troponin. Manaqement of CO Give 100% oxygen - Use a reservoir mask at 15 Uminute.\n- The half-life of carboxyhaemoglobin in a patient breathing air is about 300 minutes.\n- The half-life of carboxyhaemoglobin in a patient breathing high-concentration oxygen via a reservoir mask is 90 minutes. Supportive measures — follow ABCDE protocol\n- Start fluid resuscitation for hypotensive patients\n- Obtain specialist advice for any patient:\n- Presenting with a carboxyhaemoglobin level of\n- Who is pregnant\n- You suspect of self-harm/intentional poisoning hyperbaric oxygen therapy Hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be beneficial in selected patients however, the evidence is weak and inconsistent.\n- The UK NPIS does not currently recommend hyperbaric oxygen therapy\n- Key_points\n- Key points\n- Methemoglobinemia can result from exposure to a number of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).\n- Consider the diagnosis in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration\n- Administer methylene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs, metabolic acidosis, end organ dysfunction or, a serum level > 25%\n- CO poisoning can be diagnosed with detection of carboxyhemoglobin levels in blood Normal carboxyhaemoglobin levels are:\n-\n- Less than 1—3% for non-smokers. Up to 5% in women who are pregnant, or people with anaemia.\n- Up to 10% in smokers, and up to 13% in heavy smokers. Carboxyhemoglobin level >25% and significant neurologic dysfunction\n- (seizure or obtundation) suggests severe poisoning of CO.\n- CN toxicity remains a clinical diagnosis and there is no specific and quick bedside lab confirmatory test, although lactate levels assist greatly. Elevated lactate>8mmol/L 94% sensitive for cyanide toxicity\n- Pa02 - is normal or elevated in cyanide poisoning\n- Pa02 -usually appears normal or maybe low\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 37 year old man is brought to the ED following a house fire He has a GCS IO, is agitated on arrival with a HR of 120 bpm and BP 105/85. He has sooty deposit around his face and mouth. A 100% oxygen has been commenced through face mask. His ABGs result is shown. What is the most likely explanation for the patient?\n- **A)** Alcohol intoxication\n- **B)** Carbon monoxide poisoning\n- **C)** Type 2 respiratory failure\n- **D)** Cyanide toxicity\n- **E)** Methaemoglobinaemia Explanation\n- Patient clinical features and blood gas suggest CO poisoning\n\nPatient features GCS 10 agitated Bp  mmHg, HR: 115 Sooty deposits/ carbonaceous sputum PH: 7.1 (acidosis) Pa02: 7kPa Normal >10kPa CO poisoning yes (in severe CO poisoning) hypotension less common Yes Yes (in severe cases) Normal or low Cyanide poisoning Yes Hypotension and brady or tacyarrhythmias common May be/may not be Plastic fumes (common) Yes Normal or high Conclusion: in this case patient more likely to have CO poisoning.\n\nCO poisoning Carbon monoxide is a colourless, odourless, tasteless gas produced by incomplete combustion of fuels — this occurs when insufficient oxygen is present. Exposure to carbon monoxide causes tissue hypoxia, because carbon m