 on multiple features/ factors , unless carboxyhaemoglobin or lactate is given.\n- Suspect CO if carboxyhaemoglobin given above its normal range\n- Suspect CN if lactate level given more than 8 mmol/L\n- If in the exam, they don't give you the lactate or carboxyhaemoglobin levels then you need to think broadly. Let's take an example:\n- Example—I A 34- year-old male brought to the ED following a house fire. He has GCS 9 and is agitated on arrival with a HR 115bpm and Bp 105/85mmHg. He has sooty deposit around his face and mouth.\n- His ABGs shows.\n- PH: 7.15\n- PC02: 6kPa\n- Pa02: 7kPa\n- HC03: 25mmol/L\n- 02 stats: 98%\n\nPatient features GCS 9 agitated Bp  mmHg, HR: 115 Sooty deposits/ carbonaceous sputum PH: 7.1 (acidosis) Pa02: 7kPa Normal >10kPa CO poisoning yes (in severe CO poisoning) hypotension less common Yes Yes (in severe cases) Normal or low Cyanide poisoning Yes Hypotension and brady or tacyarrhythmias common May be/may not be Plastic fumes (common) Yes Normal or high Conclusion: in this case patient more likely to have CO poisoning.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA man who drinks alcohol excessively is brought into the ED by paramedics having been found unwell He is agitated and tremulous and has marked ataxia. His observations are: HR: 97bpm 112/21mmHg\n\nRR: sp02: 22 breath per minute 96% room air His capillary blood sugar is 3.5mmol/L. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?   A IV glucose\n- **B)** 1M haloperidol\n- **C)** IV crystalloid bolus D\n- IV thiamine\n- **E)** Oral benzodiazipines Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggests Wernicke's encephalopathy\n- NICE guideline recommends - parenteral thiamine to people with suspected Wernicke's encephalopathy. Parenteral treatment should be given for a minimum of 5 days, unless Wernicke's encephalopathy is excluded. Oral thiamine treatment should follow parenteral therapy.\n\nAlcohol withdrawal\n- Alcohol withdrawal occurs in patients who are alcohol-dependent and who have stopped or reduced their alcohol intake within hours Or days Of presentation.\n- Symptoms typically begin 6 to 12 hours after the patient's last alcoholic drink, and may progress to life- threatening alcohol withdrawal delirium (also known as delirium tremens), with or without seizures. Mild alcoholic withdrawal symptoms CAWS): high blood pressure and tachycardia Anorexia, anxiety, mood swings, insomnia, irritability, sweating, headache, and fine tremors.\n- Moderate AWS:\n- Mild AWS aggravation, plus, Agitation and Gross Tremor\n- Severe AWS/delirium tremens:\n- moderate AWS exacerbation, plus, Confusion/delirium\n- Generalized tonic-clonic seizures (in some patients this may be the initial presentation of AWS)\n- auditory, visual or tactile hallucinations\n- Hyperthermia secondary to psychomotor agitation.\n- Alcohol withdrawal delirium tends to Start 48 to 72 hours after the patient's last alcoholic drink and peaks at 5 days. Inve ion Blood gas\n- Blood glucose\n- FBC\n- LETS\n- I-J&E Coagulation screen\n- Management\n- Use a validated scoring system, such as CIWA-Ar ( Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment Of Alcohol, revised Opens in new window) or GMAWS ( Glasgow Modified Alcohol Withdrawal Scale Opens in new window), with your clinical judgement to assess all patients with alcohol withdrawal, to gauge severity and guide management.\n- In general, patients with a CIWA-Ar score <100r GMAWS do not require drug treatment. However, Wernicke's encephalopathy\n- Wernicke's encephalopathy is a neurological emergency resulting from thiamine deficiency with varied neurocognitive manifestations, typically involving mental status changes (confusion) and gait (ataxia) and oculomotor dysfunction. Alcohol dependence AIDS Cancer and treatment with chemotherapeutic agents Malnutrition History Of gastrointestinal surgery\n- Clinical features Classic triad mental status changes, ophthalmoplegia, and gait dysfunction Mental slowing, impaired concentration, and apathy Frank confusion Ocular motor findings - These include gaze palsies, sixth nerve palsies, and impaired vestibulo-ocular reflexes, and affect one third of patients.\n- Investigations Therapeutic trial of parenteral thiamine - clinical response to treatment (if drawn, thiamine level should be obtained before administration). Finger-prick glucose - usually normal unless there are co-existent conditions (e.g., alcoholic liver disease, acute alcohol intoxication, sepsis, hypotension). Others— FBC, LFTs, renal function etc\n- Management Offer parenteral thiamine to people with suspected Wernicke's encephalopathy. Parenteral treatment should be given for a minimum of 5 days, unless Wernicke's encephalopathy is excluded. Oral thiamine treatment should follow parenteral therapy.\n- Offer prophylactic parenteral thiamine followed by oral thiamine to harmful or dependent drinkers:\n- if they are malnourished or at risk of\n\nsupportive treatment.\n- Suspected alcohol withdrawal and CIWA-Ar score 210 or GMAWS score 22\n- benzodiazepine or carbamazepine or clomethiazole\n- Use a benzodiazepine first line to any patient who have alcohol withdrawal delirium (also known as delirium tremens) or alcohol withdrawal seizures.\n- Chlordiazepoxide is commonly used, but diazepam is also an option.\n- Clomethiazole may be offered as an alternative to a benzodiazepine or carbamazepine.\n- Consider short acting (lorazepam) in alcohol withdrawal seizures.\n- supportive care + treatment of concurrent acute medical illness\n- Correct electrolyte imbalance\n- Correct hypoglycaemia by giving — Give oral glucose/ IV glucose depends on patient condition.\n- If you give glucose, give it at the same time Or after thiamine.\n- thiamine (vitamin 31) to any patient with alcohol withdrawal to prevent or treat Wernicws encephalopathy.\n- Give at least one intravenous dose of a high potency vitamin B preparation (e.g., Pabrinexs in the IJK)\n- Switch to an oral formulation (e.g., vitamin B compound strong in the JK, which contains nicotinamide, pyridoxine, riboflavin, and thiamine).\n- Consider airway management if airway compromised\n- If alcoholic withdrawal patients presents with anti- psychotic symptoms - consider Haloperidol and olanzapine or rapid tranquilisation.\n- Suspected alcohol withdrawal and CIWA-Ar score or GMAWS score Treat same as above and consider outpatient management. malnounsnmenr or\n- if they have decompensated liver disease and in addition\n- they attend an emergency department or\n- are admitted to hospital with an acute illness or injury.  IO. A 14 year old boy presents with his parents with self-harm to the left thigh. This is his first presentation and he explains he feels much better and it is related\n\nto bullying in school. He has no suicidal ideation and is looking forward to a family holiday in the coming weeks. On examination, he has superficial incised wounds which are cleaned and closed and multiple old scars on both thigh. What is the most appropriate action?\n- **A)** Advise his parents to arrange a GP appointment\n- **B)** Discharge home in the care of his parents\n- **C)** Admit to the paediatric team for further investigation\n- **D)** Admit under psychiatry for further assessment\n- **E)** Discharge home with an urgent referral to CAMHS Explanation\n- NICE guideline - When a person attends the emergency department or minor injury unit following an episode of self-harm, offer referral to age- appropriate liaison psychiatry services, or for children and young people, crisis response service (or an equivalent specialist mental health service or a suitably skilled mental health professional) as soon as possible after arrival, for a psychosocial assessrnent.\n- If the person is aged under 18 years, refer them to Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (CAMHS).\n- If the person is aged 18-65 years old refer them to liaison psychiatrist/mental health service\n\n- Early referral should be considered if there is evidence of depression and/or self-harm Self harm Self-harm refers to an intentional act of self-poisoning or self-injury,\n- irrespective of the motivation or apparent purpose of the act, and is an expression of emotional distress.\n- The majority of self-poisoning episodes involve prescribed or over-the- counter medication, and a minority involve illicit drugs, other household substances, or plant material. The majority of self-injury episodes involve cutting.\n- Less common methods include burning, hanging, stabbing, drowning,\n- swallowing objects, insertion, shooting, and jumping from heights or in front of vehicles.\n- Risk factors\n- Age — self-harm rates peak in 16 to 24-year-old women and 25 to 34- year-old men. Suicide rates are highest in both men and women aged 45—49 years. Bullying, suffering abuse , exam stress or pressure\n- a sudden change in your life, like a death, divorce or moving school\n- Socio-economic disadvantage.\n- Social isolation.\n-\n- Stressful life events, for example relationship difficulties, previous experience in the armed forces, child maltreatment, or domestic\n\nviolence.\n- Bereavement by suicide.\n- Mental health problems, such as depression, psychosis or schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, post-traumatic stress disorder, or a personality disorder.\n- Chronic physical health problems.\n- Alcohol and/or drug misuse.\n- Involvement with the criminal justice system (with people in prison being at particular risk). Features in children or in young_peopJe Self-harm is a way to show the feelings patient have inside on the outside. It might cause the patient to experience: pain or discomfort\n- temporary physical marks\n- scarring\n- feelings of sickness or dizziness\n- feelings of weakness, shame or disgust\n- feeling scared, out of control or confused by why you are doing this\n- feelings of isolation and loneliness\n-\n- Assessment and care by non-mental health emergency department professionals ( NICE guideline)\n- When a person attends the emergency department or minor injury unit following an episode of self-harm, emergency department staff\n\nresponsible for initial assessment or triage should establish the following as soon as possible:\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- the severity of the injury and how urgently physical treatment is needed the person's emotional and mental state, and level of distress whether there is immediate concern about the person's safety whether there are any safeguarding concerns the person's willingness to accept medical treatment and mental healthcare the appropriate nursing observation level whether the person has a care plan.\n- When a person attends the emergency department or minor injury unit following an episode of self-harm, offer referral to age-appropriate liaison psychiatry services, or for children and young people, crisis response service (or an equivalent specialist mental health service or a suitably skilled mental health professional) as soon as possible after arrival, for a psychosocial assessment.\n- If the person is aged under 18 years, refer them to Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (CAMHS).\n- If the person is aged 18-65 years old refer them to liaison psychiatrist/mental health service\n- Early referral should be considered if there is evidence of depression and/or self-harm\n- An age-appropriate liaison psychiatry professional or a suitably skilled mental health professional should see and speak to the person at every attendance after an episode of self-harm.\n\n- Ensure that the emergency department has a private, designated area for psychosocial assessments to take place, where it is possible to speak in confidence without being overheard.\n- Ensure that the waiting area in the emergency department for people who have self-harmed is close to staff who can provide care, support and observation.\n- Ensure that appropriate joint governance arrangements are in place so that physical and mental healthcare can be delivered together in emergency departments. This should include:\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Access to electronic record systems for both mental health services and medical treatment at the point of care jointly agreed referral pathways for concurrent physical and mental healthcare jointly agreed approaches to initial assessment and triage monitoring of the use of mental health law and mental capacity law joint safeguarding procedures jointly agreed nursing observation policies referral pathways to appropriate community services.\n- Evidence-based Treatment Pathway for Urgent Health Services for Adults and Older Adults AND Specialist Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (under 18 years)\n\nEvidence-based Treatment Pathway for Urgent and Emergency Liaison Mental/Psychiatry Health Services for Adults and Older Adults Liaison mental health services are specialist services providing mental health care in a physical health setting\n- liaison mental health teams provide a range Of services to people (which can include follow-up care), this guide is concerned with the aspect of liaison mental health services that provides unplanned, nonelective urgent and emergency mental health care in general hospitals. Referrals come from EDs, medical assessment units and clinical decision units, and may also be made from any other ward within a general hospital.\n- Liaison mental health service made of: Psychiatrists (doctors who have specialised in psychiatry)\n- Mental health nurses\n- Administrative staff.\n- Teams may also have some or all of the following staff: Psychologists, Psychological therapists Pharmacists, Social workers\n- Occupational therapists Specialists in alcohol or drug misuse. liaison mental health services commonly see people When thev are experiencing or have experienced anv Of the following: Self-harm leading to medical or surgical treatment Suicidal ideation\n- The consequences Of alcohol and drug use, including When co-occurring With a mental health problem Dementia or delirium A severe mental illness, such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder or severe depression, or a personality disorder\n- Social vulnerability that may have a mental health problem or trauma as a component or root cause, for example, homelessness or domestic abuse.\n- EBTP STANDARD (Evidence-based Treatment Pathway) Any person experiencing a mental health crisis should receive a response from the liaison mental health service within a maximum Of I hour Of the service receiving a referral.\n- EBTP STANDARD Within 4 hours of arriving at an emergency department or being referred from a ward, any person experiencing a mental health crisis should have received the appropriate response or outcome to meet their needs and have an evidence-based care package Specialist Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (under 18 years)\n- Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (CAMHS) is a broad term for all services that work With children and young people Who have difficulties With their emotional or behavioural wellbeing. CAMH services are also configured slightly differently, but all contain essentially the same mix of preventive, early intervention and specialist services.\n- The vast majority of services are provided in outpatient and community settings, with very few inpatient beds\n- The CAMHS workforce includes psychiatrists, psychologists and paediatricians nurses, including health visitors, schools nurses and specialist substance misuse and learning disability nurses social workers support workers, such as school counsellors and youth workers occupational therapists psychological therapists: this may include child psychotherapists, family psychotherapists, play therapists and creative art therapists primary mental health link workers\n- CAMHS professionals deal with a wide range Of emotional. behavioura' and psvchiatric problems. This include : Eating disorders (anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa) Autistic spectrum disorders,\n- ADHD depression and anxiety post-traumatic stress and early developmental trauma psychosis and those at risk of suicide OR severe self harm Referral routes and access to CAMHS - varies, and who can refer and how will differ from area to area. Usually health professionals such as GPs and nurses, teachers, social workers and youth offending teams can refer children and young people. In many places, young people themselves (if they are old enough) and parents can also make a referral.\n\n- Summary_ gf the pathway. f,QE an emergency_ response from liaison mental Fealth services EBTP 4 hour Cbck StartS Referral to liaison mental health service EO staff liaison lth Contut iaison mental Step I Lia healt h service respo nd'S to æfe"al_ Initial 'is k assessment decision on the next steps to step 2 Lia i son mental hea lth CO#CtS a full if required a Helth Act EBTP clock is Stan Within hours: biopsi<h Health uWH tare and to the next location accepted and advice\n- The structure of CAMHS\n- The structure and operation of CAMHS can seem complex, because it differs from the way children's physical health services and adult mental health services are set up. The structure of CAMHS is probably\n\nbest explained in terms of how a child or young person accesses the service, with four 'tiers' of service provision. Tier One Tier Two Tier Three Tier Four\n- Universal services such as early years services and all primary care agencies including general medical practice (GP), school nursing, health visiting and schools.\n- Tier-one services aim to promote mental well-being, recognise when a child or young person may have developmental or mental health problems that this level of service cannot meet, and know what to do when this is the case.\n- More targeted services such as youth offending teams, primary mental health workers, and school and youth counselling (including social care and education). This includes support for children with less severe mental health problems. Professionals providing the service include clinical child psychologists, paediatricians with specialist training in mental health, educational psychologists, child and adolescent psychiatrists, child and adolescent psychotherapists, counsellors, community nurses or nurse specialists, and family therapists. Function/services include training and consultation to other professionals (who might be within tier 1), consultation to families, outreach, and assessment. Specialist community CAMHS. These are multidisciplinary teams of child and adolescent mental health professionals providing a range of interventions, including teams with specific remits, such as CAMHS learning disability teams community forensic CAMHS\n- adolescent substance misuse teams crisis/home treatment teams preventing admission to hospital\n- liaison teams providing CAMHS input to children and young people in acute care settings. Highly specialist services, such as day and inpatient services, very specialised outpatient services and, increasingly, services such as crisis/home treatment services which provide an alternative to admission. These are generally services for a small number of children and young people who are deemed to be at greatest risk of rapidly declining mental health, or from serious self- harm, who need a period of intensive input. Tier four services are often provided on a regional or supra-regional basis. There are also a small number of very highly specialised services including medium secure adolescent units, services for those with gender dysphoria and highly specialist obsessive compulsive disorder services.\n\n- Note - you don't need to memorise liaison of mental health pathway or the structure of CAMHS for the exam purpose. Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	25	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
a2887678-3e42-4e1c-b1a0-73ca22a625c9	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-27	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 27	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 27\n\nA 12cm\n- **B)** 13cm\n- **C)** Ilcm\n- **D)** 12.5cm\n- **E)** 14cm EXPIanation\n- Advanced life support grouæ(ALSG) endotracheal tube recommendations:\n- Internal diameter (mm) = (Age/4) + 4\n- Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 12 for an oral tube\n- Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 15 for nasal tube\n- Answer -  + 12 = 13cm\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 17 year old with haemophilia presents into the ED after falling from his bike. He presents with a scalp haematoma, headache and bruising over the chest wall. His GCS is 14 E3/M6/V5. What is your immediate priority?\n- **A)** Administer fresh frozen plasma\n- **B)** 10mg IV vitamin K\n- **C)** Give factor VIII frorn haematology\n- **D)** Give tranexamic acid\n- **E)** Urgent CT scan of his head Explanation\n- Clotting factor replacement therapy should be given before any diagnostic studies (X-rays, CT scans etc.) are performed to evaluate a suspected bleeding problem, especially in the case of head trauma or suspected intracranial hemorrhage.\n\n- GUIDELINES FOR EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT MANAGEMENT OF INDIVIDUALS WITH HEMOPHILIA AND OTHER BLEEDING DISORDERS\n- Triage\n- Individuals with bleeding disorders should be triaged urgently as delays in administering appropriate therapy, such as infusion of factor concentrate, can significantly affect morbidity and mortality.\n- Consultation with the patient's primary provider of bleeding disorder care, in most cases a hematologist, is strongly advised. However, Administration of clotting factor replacement to the patient should not be delayed waiting for a consultation.\n- Assessment\n- Treatment for a suspected bleeding episode is based on clinical history. Physical exam findings may be normal in the early phases of most bleeding episodes associated with an underlying bleeding disorder,\n- Spontaneous bleeding is common in those with severe disease (baseline factor levels <1%). When in doubt, administer clotting factor replacement therapy immediately.\n- Diagnostic studies\n- Clotting factor replacement therapy should be given beforeany diagnostic studies (X-rays, CT scans etc.) are performed to evaluate a suspected bleeding problem, especially in the case of head trauma or suspected intracranial hemorrhage.\n- For routine joint bleeding, no radiographic studies are indicated.\n- For patients with hemophilia who have illnesses or disorders that necessitate an invasive procedure (lumbar puncture, arterial blood gas, arthrocentesis, etc.) or surgery, factor replacement therapy or bypass therapy to 100% must be administered in the emergency\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- department prior to the planned procedure or surgery. In this situation, consultation with a hematologist is strongly recommended. For an individual with known hemophilia, routine laboratory studies (PT, aPTT, factor levels), are not indicated in the treatment of a routine bleeding episode unless requested by the patient's hematologist. Treatment should not be delayed waiting for test results which may take several hours. Indications for Factor Replacement Therapy include\n- Suspected bleeding into a joint or muscle. Any significant injury to the head, neck, mouth or eyes or evidence of bleeding in these areas. Any new or unusual headache, particularly one following trauma. Severe pain or swelling at any site. All open wounds requiring surgical closure, wound adhesive, or steri- strips. History of an accident or trauma that might result in internal bleeding. Any invasive procedure or surgery. Heavy or persistent bleeding from any site. Gastrointestinal bleeding leading to moderate to severe anemia. Acute fractures, dislocations and sprains. Heavy menstrual bleeding leading to moderate to severe anemia or volume instability Management\n\nHemophilia A\n- Hemophilia A without Inhibitor 1ST Choice - Recombinant factor VIII Plasma-derived concentrate is a suitable alternative in an emergency situation when recombinant factor VIII is not available.\n- E.tepb.æs=emicizumab\n- Hemophilia A or B with inhibitor Hemophilia B Hemophilia B without Inhibitor 1ST Choice - Recombinant factor IX\n- Plasma-derived concentrate is a suitable alternative in an emergency situation when recombinantfactor VIII is not available.\n- For individuals with inhibitors (antibodiesto factorVlll or IX), require urgently discussion with the patient's hematologist.\n- If an individual with an inhibitorpresents ina life-or limb threatening scenario, the safest immediate action is to prescribe recombinant factor Vila (rFVlla) at a dose of 90 mcg/kg or activated prothrombin complex concentrates (FEIBA) at 75-100 units/kg.\n- Note- rFVIla is recommended over FEIBA for acute bleedingevents or procedures in hemophilia A inhibitor patientson emicizumab prophylaxisas aPCCs may cause thrombosis or thrombotic microangiopathy in those receiving emicizumab and should be avoided.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 15-year-old girl presents to the ED with visual hallucinations for one week. She is accompanied by her parents. She denies taking illicit drugs or using alcohol. She looks pale and has a history of ongoing menorrhagia, Neurological system examination is normal. Her observations are. 110 beats per minute 100/60mmHg 4.0 mmol/L What is the most likely underlying cause of her symptoms?\n- **A)** Hypoglycemia\n- **B)** Viral encephalitis\n- **C)** Schizoaffective disorder\n- **D)** Eating disorder\n- **E)** Anaemia EXPIanation\n- Patient's ongoing history of menorrhagia, lack of drugs abuse (laxative or appetite suppressant) and a feature of pallor with normal neurological examination suggests anaemia.\n\nAnaemia - iron deficiency Iron deficiency anaemia is diminished red blood cell production due to low iron stores in the body.\n- It is the most common cause of microcytic anaemia. Anaemia is defined as a haemoglobin (Hb) level two standard deviations below the normal for age and sex: o In men aged over 15 years — Hb below 130 g/l_ o In non-pregnant women aged over 15 years — Hb below 120 gm. o In children aged 12—14 years — Hb below 120 o In pregnant women — Hb below 110 g/L throughout pregnancy\n- A serum ferritin level Of less than 30 micrograms/L confirms iron deficiency. 1\n- Causes Dietary deficiency - anaemia due to a poor diet, or malabsorption resulting in no iron intake Malabsorption — due to coeliac disease, gastrectomy, or Helicobacter pylori infection. Increased loss — GI blood loss is the most common cause of iron deficiency anaemia, in adult men and postmenopausal women. Menstruation especially heavy menstruation - is the most common cause of iron deficiency anaemia in premenopausal women. Increased requirement — physiological iron requirements are three times higher in pregnancy. Other causes — these include: blood donation, self- harm Clinical features\n- Fatigue\n- Headache Panor Hair loss Dyspepsia Dyspnea on exertion\n- Pica — It is the abnormal craving or appetite for nonfood substances, such as soil, ice, paint, or clay.\n- Restless legs syndrome Spooning of the nails (koilonychia Eating disorder\n- The main types of eating disorder are:\n- Anorexia nervosa— restriction of food intake or persistent behaviour which interferes with weight gain and leads to low body weight.\n- Anorexia nervosa is associated with body image disturbance and an intense fear of gaining weight. E.uum.ia-netygsa_— recurrent (at least once per week for 3 months) episodes of uncontrolled eating of an abnormally large amount of food over a short time period (binge eating) followed by compensatory behaviour such as self-induced vomiting, laxative abuse or excessive exercise. Binge eatinq disorder— recurrent episodes Of binge eating in the absence of compensatory behaviours. Episodes are marked by feelings of lack Of control.\n- Causes\n- General factors such as female sex, adolescence and early adulthood.\n- Neurobiological factors - Eating disorders have been associated with abnormal neurotransmitter activity (including serotonin and dopamine) and satiety-related hormones (including adiponectin and ghrelin). Psychosocial factors such as - Anxiety, depression, perfectionism, low self-esteem, body dissatisfaction EamiLLbiEtQ!Y0f an eating disorder, psychiatric disorder or substance misuse.\n- Clinical features Features of anorexia nervosa\n- Restriction Of energy intake resulting in low body weight (BMI less than 18.5)\n- Intense fear of gaining weight - Preoccupation with food and weight is Often related to a pursuit Of thinness.\n- Compensatory behaviours - such as self-induced purging (by vomiting or misuse of laxatives), excessive exercise\n- Low self-esteern and a drive for perfection.\n\nDizziness or lightheadedness\n- Palpitation, Tinnitus Cognitive and behavioral impairment (less common)\n- Investiqations\n- FBC\n- low hemoglobin Or hematocrit\n- MCV<80fL\n- Serum iron — low\n- Total iron-binding capacity — High\n- serum ferritin — low\n- Consider H. pylori testing, urinalysis, celiac serology\n- Consider fecal occult blood tests\n- Management\n- Address the underlying causes as necessary (for example treat menorrhagia or stop nonsteroidal anti- inflammatory drugs, if possible).\n- Prescribe all people with iron deficiency anaemia one tablet once daily of oral ferrous sulfate, ferrous fumarate or ferrous gluconate.\n- Continue treatment for 3 months after iron deficiency is corrected to allow stores to be replenished. Monitor the person to ensure that there is an adequate response to iron treatment\n- Referral pathway Urgently refer people with iron deficiency anaemia due to cancer Refer women to gynaecology if menorrhagia is unresponsive to medical management\n- Refer people to gastroenterology If coeliac serology is positive.\n- Over-evaluation Of self-worth in terms Of body weight and shape Amenorrhoea. hypotension Features ot bulimia nervosa\n- Recurrent episodes of binge eating\n- Recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour to prevent weight gain Compensatory behaviours include vomiting, purging, fasting, excessive exercise, laxative, diuretic or diet pill use\n- Over-evaluation Of self-worth in terms Of body weight and shape.\n- Persistent preoccupation and craving for food and feelings of guilt and shame about binge eating\n- Self-harm, often by scratching or cutting.\n- Menstrual irregularities Russell's sign - Scarring over the dorsum of the hands\n- Investiqations\n- Serum electrolyte, creatinine, creatine kinase (CK)\n- FBC, LFTs, ECG\n- urine pregnancy test\n- Serum B12\n- Consider dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry scan for bone density.\n- Manaqement Refer immediately to an age appropriate eating disorder service for specialist assessment and management — do not use a watchful waiting strategy for managing eating disorders. Depending on locally agreed care pathways and service provision referral may be to a community mental health team (CMH T), child and adolescent services (CAMHS), or a specialist eating disorder unit.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 33 year old man is brought into the ED by his friends with a single stab wound to the right side of his chest just below the nipple. Initial observations during your primary survey are: sp02: GCS: 100 beats per minute 100/60mmHg 26 breath per minute 94% on air 15 You transfer him into the resuscitation room and arrange an immediate chest x- ray, which is shown. What is the most appropriate next step?\n- **A)** Surgical intercostal chest drain\n- **B)** CT chest and abdomen with contrast\n- **C)** Lung ultrasound\n- **D)** Start blood transfusion\n- **E)** Bedside echocardiogram Explanation\n- CXR suggests massive hemothorax\n- Massive hemothorax is initially managed by the simultaneous restoration of blood volume and decompression of the chest cavity. Thoracostomy tube (chest drain) placement is required to decompress the lung by draining the blood from the pleural space.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 45 year old man is brought to the ED following an RTC in which he was the driver in a rear end shunt. He is reporting pain in his neck. He has no neurological symptoms. Which of the following would indicate a need for cervical spine imaging based on nexus spine criteria?\n- **A)** Immediate onset of pain\n- **B)** Patient immobilised in collar and blocks\n- **C)** Patient has not mobilised since the incident\n- **D)** Inability to rotate the neck in a 90 degree arc\n- **E)** Midline tenderness\n\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Midline tenderness after a cervical spine injury is a significant clinical finding, as outlined by the NEXUS criteria. The presence of midline tenderness raises the concern for a potential cervical spine injury, making it an indication for CT in adult and MRI below 16 for an evaluation to rule out fractures or other structural damage.\n- NEXUS Cervical Spine Rule Radiographyisnot necessary ifthepatient satisfies ALL of the following risk criteria: NO midline cervical tenderness NO focal neuro deficits Normal alertness No intoxication No painful distracting injury be considered it patient meet any one of the risk mentioned below Neuro deficit Spinal midline tenderness in C-spine Altered level Of consciousness present Intoxication Distracting injury Mnemonic - NSAID Distracting injuries make it difficult for a patient to focus on or report neck pain or discomfort, increasing the risk of missing a cervical spine injury. eg. long bone fracture, crush injuries.\n- The Canadian C-Spine Rule (CCR)\n\nAny Risk Factors? ANY Of the fouowing: - 65 yeus\n- Da\n- -wous Paresthesiæ in extremities None? You may proceed.. Any Low Risk Factors? ANY of the fonowing: - Simple rear-end MVC\n- Sting in ED - Armulatory at ANY TIME\n- Ddayed (i.e. rot irnrrøate) Of re pain - midine C-søne Pt has high risk factor? Well. then you should get. Not even one? aren t low risk! Radiography One M the above* Excellent... proceed with ROM me to Rotate Necu actively? i.e. Rotate rEk 45 left & r\n- t. Cant move their neck? Then... they aren risk! Based on the CCR... No Radiography\n- Key Points: The Canadian C-Spine Rule (CCR) BY NC SA Image by Teresa M. Chan\n- For patients at high risk, a CT scan should be performed in adults, and an MRI should be performed in those under 16 years of age.\n- If a patient has any one low-risk factor, proceed by asking them to rotate their neck 45 degrees on each side. If the patient cannot rotate\n\ntheir neck, then a CT scan (for adults) or MRI (for those under 16) should be performed. If the patient can rotate their neck, no radiography is need ed.\n- If none of the Iow-risk factors are present which are - e.g., absence of midline tenderness, delay onset of pain, ambulatory at any time, sitting position in the ED, simple rear MVC, then a CT scan for adults and an MRI for those under 16 should be performed.\n- Please note - that we use both criteria in the Emergency Department (ED).\n- Question last revised 01/25.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 40-year-old woman is brought to the ED after sustaining the injury to her right ankle, as shown, after falling down the stairs. Sensation to her foot is intact and she has a palpable dorsalis pedis pulse. She has no other injuries on examination. What is the most appropriate management of this patient?\n- **A)** Reduce the ankle immediately with nitrous oxide\n- **B)** Procedural sedation and reduction of the ankle\n- **C)** Procedural sedation and reduction after a CT scan\n- **D)** Refer to orthopedics for review\n- **E)** Organize an urgent x-ray of her ankle joint\n- Explanation\n- Ankle dislocation - if vascular compromise is present, as evidenced by a dusky foot or absent pulses, or there is tenting of the skin, an immediate reduction by the emergency physician is warranted without any pre-reduction radiographs.\n- First provide appropriate sedation and analgesia before attempts at reduction.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 40-year-old man has several hours of pain in his left flank. He describes his pain as severe. The triage nurse asks you to see him for pain relief he is otherwise well and with no allergies. He has not taken any medication today. According to the NICE renal colic guideline, what is the correct initial drug to prescribe?\n- **A)** IV buscopan   B\n- Tamsulosin\n\nC, Morphine\n- **D)** PR NSAID   E\n- IV Paracetamol Explanation\n- Renal-calculLpain-relieE(NJCEguideline)\n- First line NSAID any route (PR, oral, IV)\n- IV Paracetamol if NSAID contraindicated or not giving sufficient pain relief\n- If both NSAIDs and IV paracetamol are contraindicated or are not giving sufficient pain relief, or IV paracetamol is unavailable, consider an opioid analgesic (such as tramadol).\n- Do not offer antispasmodics to people with suspected renal or ureteric colic\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 20-year-old man presents with atraumatic right sided chest pain. His chest x- ray shows a right sided pneumothorax. The inter-pleural distance at the level of the hilum is 1.2cm. He has never smoked. As per British Thoracic Society Guidelines, what is the most appropriate management of this patient?\n- **A)** Insertion of a 24F surgical chest drain\n- **B)** Needle aspiration\n- **C)** Admission overnight for high-flow oxygen\n- **D)** Discharge with advice and outpatient follow up\n- **E)** Seldinger technique chest drain insertion EXPIanation\n- Patient history suggests primary spontaneous pneumothorax as patient is under 65, non-smoker and has no known underlying disease.\n- Primary pneumothorax <2cm - discharge the patient review in OPD in 2-4 weeks Primary_spontaneous pneumothorax\n- Pneumothorax size <2cm\n- discharge the patient review in OPD in 2-4 weeks\n- Pneumothorax >2cm or breathless Aspirate with 16-180 cannula Aspirate <2.51\n- successful pneumothorax <2cm now and breathing improved then consider discharge and follow in OPD 2-4 weeks\n- Not successful, insert Chest drain Size 8-14Fr and admit the patient.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 59-year-old man brought to the ED with a severe exacerbation of his COPD. Despite back-to-back nebulized salbutamol steroids and antibiotics, he remains tachypneic with ongoing hypoxia: You think that he needs BIPAR According to the BTS guidelines, which of the following is an absolute contraindication of NIV?\n- **A)** Reduced conscious level\n- **B)** Hypotension\n- **C)** pH less than 7.25\n- **D)** Vomiting patient\n- **E)** Facial deformity Explanation Absolute contraindication to NIV Severe facial deformity Facial burn Fixed upper airway obstruction\n\nIO. A 3-month-old baby is brought into ED with complain of intermittent episodes of unconsciousness and crying after feeding. Mother tells you that she has noticed some blood on nappy today. He was born at 35 weeks but otherwise well. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Colic   B\n- Intussusception\n- **C)** Necrotizing enterocolitis\n- **D)** Pyloric Stenosis\n- **E)** Cow's milk intolerance Explanation\n- Intussusception (Babies and Toddlers) classic triad of symptoms:\n- episodic screaming with drawing up their legs,\n- vomiting (can be bilious)\n- redcurrant jelly stools\n- it affects those of 3-18 months after a viral illness\n-\n\n- Redcurrant jelly stools, if present, don't always look like the red mucousy stuff (think bloody diarrhoea explosion)\n- Usually, mother noticed blood in nappies\n- Investigation: AXR, US, contrast enema (contraindicated in pt with free intra-abdominal air) or CT\n- Management;-lV fluids, analgesia, NC tube on free drainage and urgent surgical referral previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	26	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7605fb93-4423-4434-9159-6e5cfcfee95a	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-28	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 28	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 28\n\nWhat's the %TBSA affected? Explanation\n- To follow Lund and Browder chart:\n- Each anterior forearm counts 1.5 %BSA\n- Both anterior forearm: 3%BSA\n- Both volar part of hands: 3%BSA = 696TBSA\n- Lund and Browder chart\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 35-year-old male presents with abdominal pain six hours after falling onto the edge of a table. On examination, he has left upper quadrant guarding. A CT scan shows a splenic laceration with active arterial bleeding and a large haemoperitoneum and he is hypotensive. Investigations: INR: 10.2 g/dl 9 98x10 1.2\n\nThe major haemorrhage protocol has been activated. He has been given 2 units of O-negative blood. What is the most appropriate next management step?\n- **A)** Transfuse further O-negative blood\n- **B)** lg of tranexamic acid\n- **C)** 1 unit of platelet\n- **D)** Prothrombin complex O\n- **E)** 1 unit of FFP Explanation\n- Once you activate the massive hemorrhage protocol, give 4 units blood via fluid warmer.\n- Give Group O if immediate need and/ or blood group unknown . 4 or 6 units RBC . 4 units FFP\n- Add 1 dose of PLT in trauma patients earlier\n- Transfusion of MHP-2 depends on lab results i-e if Hb in lab value <8 then transfuse RBC otherwise not\n\n- If trauma and <3hrs from injury, give tranexamic acid Ig bolus over IO minutes followed by infusion of Ig over 8hr\n\nTransfusion management of major haemorrhage\n- Pt bleeding/collapsed or in clinical shock\n- Trigger the major blood loss protocol\n- Iftrauma and <3hrs from injury, give tranexamicacid lg bolus Over IO minutes followed by infusion of Ig over (Consider tranexamicacid Ig bolusin non-traumaticpt)\n- Take baseline blood sample for: Cross-match. FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Give initial MHP-IWhich include: 4 or 6 units RBC 4 units Add 1 dose of pit in trauma patients earlier\n- Reassess the pt If require further transfusion then take baseline sample for:\n- FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, LJ&E and Calcium level Order MHP-2 which include 4-6 units ofR3C, 4unitS0fFFP, 1 dose of plt 2 packs of cryoprecipitate\n- Transfusion of MHP-2 depends on lab results eg: if Hb in lab value <8 then transfuse RBC Otherwise not Repeat the baseline investigation which include: FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, LJ&E and Calcium level Correct complications: hypocalcaemia, hypothermia, acidosis\n- Aim to maintain following values PLT PT ratio APTT ratio Fibrinogen ca2+ 8-1 og/dl X 109 IL >1.5g/l > 1 mmoUI\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 14-year-old girl presents to ED who is requesting for contraceptive pills. it's her third time in a short period. To use Fraser guideline, which of the following is not included?\n- **A)** She doesn't understand the consequences arise from her decision\n- **B)** Previous contraception prescription\n- **C)** She doesn't want to inform her parents\n- **D)** She doesn't want to take contraception regularly\n- **E)** She said she will continue sex Explanation\n- "Fraser Guidance" then dictates that a doctor can prescribe contraception if:\n- The girl (although under the age of 16 years of age) will understand his advice.\n- They cannot persuade her to inform her parents or to allow him to inform the parents that she is seeking contraceptive advice.\n\n- She is very likely to continue having sexual intercourse with or without contraceptive treatment.\n- She receives contraceptive advice or treatment her physical or mental health or both are likely to suffer.\n- Her best interests require him to give her contraceptive advice, treatment or both without the parental consent.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 2-year-old child brought into the ED by his parents with history of fever associated with harsh cough, runny nose and hoarse of the voice for the last two days. On examination the child has mild tachycardia, tachypnoea and his Sp02 is 98%. Which of the following is the most likely infectious cause of his condition?\n- **A)** Parainfluenza\n- **B)** Hib-b\n- **C)** RSV\n- **D)** Strep group A\n- **E)** Glandular fever Explanation\n\nCroup\n- Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a common respiratory disease that\n- typically occurs in children aged between 6 months and 3 years. us Parainfluenza virus types 1 or 3\n- Clinical features\n- Sudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which associated with :\n- Hoarseness\n-\n- Stridor Respiratory distress.\n- Fever\n- Symptoms are typically worse at night and increase with agitation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 15 years old male patient with sickle cell disease is brought into the ED with complaint of pain in his left knee and two days history of increasing short of breath and fatigue. His full blood shows anemia and you suspect aplastic crisis. What is not included in your next management?\n- **A)** Bronchodilator therapy   B\n- Antibiotics\n- **C)** Analgesia\n\nD\n- COrticosteroids O\n- **E)** IV Fluids Explanation\n- Corticosteroids increase the risk of painful vaso-occlusive crises (VOCs) leading to hospitalization in children and adults with sickle cell disease (SCD), therefore, corticosteroids should be avoided.\n- Sickle cell anaemia\n- Sickle cell anaemia is caused by an autosomal-recessive single gene defect in the beta chain of haemoglobin, which results in production of sickle cell haemoglobin (HbS).\n- Other forms of sickle cell disease may occur if HbS is inherited from one parent and another abnormal haemoglobin, or beta thalassaemia, is inherited from the other parent (e.g., HbSC or HbSB thalassaemia).\n- Sickle cell disease is associated with varying degrees of anaemia, red cell haemolysis, and obstruction of small blood capillaries causing painful crises, damage to major organs, and increased vulnerability to severe infections. Clinical features\n- Vaso-occlusive/painful crises - persistent pain in skeleton, chest, and/or abdomen\n\nDactylitis - Swollen dorsa of hands and feet consistent with hand-foot\n- syndrome Acute chest syndrome (pneumonia-like syndrome) - Sickling of red\n- blood cells within the pulmonary vasculature results in acute chest syndrome. This can be clinically indistinguishable from pneumonia and can cause chest pain, fever, dyspnoea, tachypnoea, and hypoxaemia. Diagnosed when the patient has fever, chest pain, and infiltrate on chest x-ray.\n- High temperature - Oral temperature over 38.50C (101.30F), cough, watery stools, tender abdomen, malaise, chest pain, diarrhoea, and/or vomiting are suggestive of infection Pallor\n- Failure to thrive\n- Bone pain\n- Jaundice\n- Tachycardia\n- Lethergy\n- Cardiac flow murmur\n-\n- Investigations Diagnosis is uncommon in childhood or adulthood. The majority of new\n- cases of sickle cell disease are diagnosed as a result of the National Newborn Screening Programme in the 1.1K. Sickle cell disease is always diagnosed after both an initial and\n- confirmatory test (using a different analytical method) are positive.\n\n- The choice of test will depend on local guidelines and facilities. Possible tests include: Full blood count\n- Reticulocyte count\n- Blood film\n- DNA-based assays\n- haemoglobin isoelectric focusing (Hb IEF)\n- Other investigations\n- Consider long bone x-ray\n-\n- Chest x-ray Blood culture\n- Management Analgesia — offer paracetamol or NSAID in mild to moderate pain or\n- Opioid in severe pain Oxygen - given nasally at a rate of 2 L/minute to patients with moderate hypoxaemia (Pa02 <IOkPa or 02 saturation 92% to 95%). Patients with more severe hypoxaemia will require a higher flow rate. Broad-spectrum antibiotics - intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics\n- are given because bacterial pneumonia cannot always be ruled out. Consider bronchodilators therapy in acute chest syndrome\n- Consider antihistamine - Many opioids cause pruritus, which should be\n- managed with an oral antihistamine. Consider blood transfusion or hydration — if indicated\n- Chronic disease\n-\n\nTake supportive and complication preventive measures\n- Consider hydroxycarbamide and repeated blood transfusion\n- Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation\n- Complication Acute painful crisis (vasculo-occlusive crisis)\n- Abdominal complications — abdominal pain due to sequestration\n- syndromes, mesenteric syndrome, constipation, gallstone. Anaemia\n- Acute chest syndrome (ACS) - This is a form of acute lung injury that\n- can be severe and life-threatening Infection\n- Osteomyelitis - the commonest sites are the femur, tibia, and humerus\n- and it is most commonly caused by Salmonellaspecies.\n- Priapism AKI Neurological complications - headache, seizures, focal neurological\n- signs, visual impairment, altered consciousness, stroke and acute deterioration in cognition\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 69-year-old woman presents with a four-day history of nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain. she states that she is taking prednisolone daily for polymyalgia rheumatica. Her abdomen is distended but no rebound tenderness. An abdomen X-ray was performed.\n\nWhich is the most likely radiological diagnosis?\n- **A)** Small bowel obstruction\n- **B)** Large bowel obstruction\n- **C)** Perforated bowel\n- **D)** Inflammatory bowel disease\n- **E)** Pancreatitis Explanation\n- AXR shows small bowel obstruction\n\nClinical features Investigations Management Small bowel obstruction It's a medical emergencies requiring early diagnosis and intervention Intra-abdominal adhesion is the most common cause of SBO. Other- hernias, Crohn's disease, intestinal malignancy,appendicitis Clinical features bdominal pain, bloating, vomiting (possibly biliary), rectal flatus and cessation of defecation. abdominal tenderness computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen and pelvis Plain x-ray features dilation Of the small bowel (>3cm diameter) more prominent valvulae conni ventes creating a 'coiled-spring appearance small bowel usually lies more centrally Blood tests -FBC, Serum amylase & lipase Fluid resuscitation, bowel decompression (using a nasogastric tube), and analgesia Surgical consultation— as patient need urgent surgical intervention. Large bowel obstruction A mechanical interruption (either complete or partial) to the flm\n- v Of intestinal contents, with multiple potential causes (e.g., malignant colorectal disease, colonic volvulus, benign stricture) Approximately 60% of large bowel Obstructions are caused by an underlying colorectal ma lignancy. Sigmoid volvulus is more usually seen in frail or Older patients. more rare and more commonly seen in younger patients. Clinica I features intermittent abdominal pain abdominal distention Nausea & vomiting recent weight loss rectal bleeding, palpable rectal mass & tympanic abdomen. tenesmus urgent computed tomography (CT) scan Of the abdomen and pelvis to be performed as soon as possible. Plain dilation of colon>6cm dilation of cecum > 9cm The small bowel usually lies more centrally, with the large bowel framing it (lateral). Sigmoid volvulus: a characteristic 'coffee bean' appearance. Caecal volvulus: often described as having a fetal appearance (comma' shaped). Medical emergencies requiring early diagnosis and intervention. Initiallv supportive management Consider bowel decompression via nasogastric tube. Consider urgent flexible sigmoidoscopy in volvulus - Often endoscopic decompression using flexible sigmoidoscopy allows the twist in the bowel to Ileus Ileus is a slowing of gastrointestinal motility accompanied by distention, in the absence of a mechanical intestinal obstruction. It is a diagnosis Of exclusion after bowel obstruction has been ruled out. It usually occurs in response to physiological stress, including surgery (usually gastrointestinal), sepsis, metabolic derangements, and gastrointestinal diseases. Ileus is commonly seen in the postoperative setting. Vomiting Abdominal distension Inability to tolerate oral feeding Absence of flatus. FBC abdomen and pelvis CT scan ABGs Postoperative ileus Nil by mouth and intravenous fluids reduction in opioid analgesia ± replacement With non- opioid analgesia (to avoid narcotic ileus). Consider nasogastric decompression.\n\nto be relieved. If decompression fail then refer to surgery. Referto colorectal surgeon for emergency surgery — if patient has malignant Obstruction or has developed peritonitis or perforation. Non-surgical ileus Nil by mouth and intravenous flu ids management of the underlying condition (acute/systemic illnesses associated with intestinal hypomotility such as diabetes mellitus, Chagas disease, scleroderma, and neurological diseases, Sepsis) reduce opioids, anticholinergics. Consider NG decompression\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 19-year-old man is brought to the ED having had a seizure whilst in a nightclub. He is confused and drowsy. His observations are shown: Temp: 400C, 136 bpm 140/90mmHg 8.0 Which of the following substances is most likely to cause this?   A Amyl nitrate u\n- **B)** MDMA\n\nC, Ketamine\n- **D)** Heroin\n- **E)** Cannabis Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of MDMA toxicity.\n\nAmphetamines Overdose\n- Amfetamines are a class of sympathomimetic agents, which include methamfetamine, dexamfetamine, and methylphenidate.\n- These drugs are used in the lawful treatment of attention deficit disorder (ADHD) and narcolepsy Several are notable for their misuse potential, including, amfetamine sulfate,various forms of methamfetamine (crystal meth or ice), and so-called designer drugs, including 3,4- methylenedioxymethamfetamine (MDMA or ecstasy), 3M-methylenedioxyamfetamine (MDA or Adam), and 3,4-methylenedi0xy-N-ethylamfetamine (MDEA or Eve).\n- Risk factors High ambient temperature - 3.4- methylenedioxymethamfetamine (MDMA or ecstasy) inhibits thermoregulation, leading to hyperthermia Volume depletion, exercise and sweating, excessive alcohol intake, polydrug usage, anxiety and depression. Clinical features Agitation, irrationality, restlessness Hyperthermia hyperthermia >38'C If hyperthermia >39.50C (>103'F) Indicates severe toxicity. Seizures, diaphoresis, flushed facial skin Tachycardia and palpitations, hypertension Headache, hyperreflexia and clonus Mydriasis, hallucinations or delusions\n- Investiqations Serum glucose, electrolyte\n- ABG, LETS , ECG, CXR urine toxicology screen Manaqement Mild symptoms — need observation and monitoring Consider activated charcoal in first hour presentation Start benzodiazepines, muscle relaxants and active cooling if patient has hyperthermia and seizures. Start hydration if patient is dehydrated or presents with sign Of rhabdomyolysis. Cardiac arrhythmia — SVT treat with esmolol and VT with amiodarone. Hypertension — treat with labetalol or GTN Cannabinoid Hyperemesis Syndrome (CHS)\n- Cannabinoid Hyperemesis Syndrome (CHS) is an episodic syndrome of cyclical vomiting in the context Of the prolonged use Of cannabis.\n- features #hould prompt suspici2Q Of of CHS Severe nausea and vomiting that recurs in a cyclic pattern over months. Age < 50 years at time of evaluation\n- At least weekly cannabis use\n- Resolution of symptoms after cannabis cessation\n- Compulsive hot showers or baths with symptom relief\n- Abdominal pain\n- History of regular cannabis use for > 1 year\n- Investigations\n- Serum glucose\n- ABGs Manaaement Offer routine antiemetic treatments such as cyclizine, dexamethasone, domperidone, metoclopramide, ondansetron, prochlorperazine and promethazine. However it has been recognized that CHS often being resistant to routine antiemetic treatments. Refractory symptoms For refractory nausea or vomiting in CHS:\n- Consider the use of 1M haloperidol 0.05mg/kg (max 5mg) Or Topical capsaicin 0.1% cream 5g applied to abdomen.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA mother of 15-year-old girl came to the ED to know about her daughter who is on contraceptive pill and diagnosed with PID for which she has been referred to the PID clinic. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Ask her to talk with PID clinic\n- **B)** Tell her she has capacity\n- **C)** Ask her to talk with her daughter\n- **D)** Give her daughter information\n- **E)** Ask her to talk with her GP Explanation\n- The Fraser guidelines apply specifically to advice and treatment about contraception and sexual health. They may be used by a range of healthcare professionals working with under 16-year-olds, including doctors and nurse practitioners.\n\n- "Fraser Guidance" then dictates that a doctor can prescribe contraception if:\n- The girl (although under the age of 16 years of age) will understand his advice.\n- They cannot persuade her to inform her parents or to allow him to inform the parents that she is seeking contraceptive advice. She is very likely to continue having sexual intercourse with or without contraceptive treatment.\n- She receives contraceptive advice or treatment her physical or mental health or both are likely to suffer.\n- Her best interests require him to give her contraceptive advice, treatment or both without the parental consent.\n- Gillick competency and Fraser guidelines\n- When consenting children to medical treatment, the terrns 'Gillick competence' and 'Fraser guidelines' are frequently used interchangeably despite there being a clear distinction between them.\n- Gillick competence is concerned with determining a child's capacity to consent. Fraser guidelines, on the other hand, are used specifically to decide if a child can consent to contraceptive or sexual health advice and treatment. Age of consent\n- In law, a person's 18th birthday draws the line between childhood and adulthood (Children Act 1989 s105) - so in health care matters, an 18 year old enjoys as much autonomy as any other adult. To a more\n\nlimited extent, 16 and 17 year-olds can also take medical decisions independently of their parents. The right of younger children to provide independent consent is proportionate to their competence - a child's age alone is clearly an unreliable predictor of his or her competence to make decisions.\n\nGillick competence\n- Gillick competency applies mainly to medical advice but it is also used by practitioners in other settings. For example, if a child or young person:\n- would like to have therapeutic support but doesn't want their parents or carers to know about it\n- is seeking confidential support for substance misuse\n- has strong wishes about their future living arrangements which may conflict with their parents' or carers' views.\n- It was determined that children under 16 can consent if they have sufficient understanding and intelligence to fully understand what is involved in a proposed treatment, including its purpose, nature, likely effects and risks, chances of success and the availability of other options.\n- Assess inq Gillick competence There is no set of defined questions to assess Gillick competency. Professionals need to consider several things when assessing a child's capacity to Consent, including:\n- the child's age, maturity and mental capacity\n- their understanding Of the issue and what it involves - including advantages, disadvantages and potential long-term impact\n- their understanding Of the risks, implications and consequences that may arise from their decision\n- how well they understand any advice or information they have been given\n- their understanding Of any alternative options, if available\n- their ability to explain a rationale around their reasoning and decision making. Fraser guidance and contraception in a sexually active child\n- The Fraser guidelines apply specifically to advice and treatment about contraception and sexual health. They may be used by a range of healthcare professionals working with under 16-year-olds, including doctors and nurse practitioners.\n- Establish whether the children is already sexually active and in what circumstances is the sexual activity occurring and with whom.\n- a child cannot consent to with an adult Of 18 years and Older\n- a child aged 12 and under cannot consent to sexual activity\n- a child cannot consent to with an adult in a position of authority "Fraser Guidance" then dictates that a doctor can prescribe contraception if: The girl (although under the age of 16 years of age) will understand his advice. They cannot persuade her to inform her parents or to allow him to inform the parents that she is seeking contraceptive advice.\n- She is very likely to continue having sexual intercourse with or without contraceptive treatment.\n- She receives contraceptive advice or treatment her physical or mental health or both are likely to suffer.\n- Her best interests require him to give her contraceptive advice, treatment or both without the parental consent. Note - Health professionals should still encourage the young person to inform his or her parent(s) or get permission to do so on their behalf, but if this permission is not given they can still give the child advice and treatment.\n\n- Key_points\n- If a child passes the Gillick test, he or she is considered 'Gillick competent' to consent to that medical treatment or intervention.\n- If a child does not pass the Gillick test, then the consent of a person with parental responsibility (or sometimes the courts) is needed in order to proceed with treatrnent.\n- Health professionals should still encourage the young person to inform his or her parent(s) or get permission to do so on their behalf, but if this permission is not given they can still give the child advice and treatment. If the conditions are not all met, however, or there is reason to believe that the child is under pressure to give consent or is being exploited, there would be grounds to break confidentiality.\n- Under 16: safeguarding considerations\n- If a young person under the age of 16 presents to a health care professional, then discloses a history raising safeguarding concerns:\n- If they are not deemed to be Gillick competent, the health professional is obliged to raise the issue as a safeguarding concern and escalate their concerns through the safeguarding process\n- If they are deemed to be Gillick competent and disclosure is considered essential to protect them from harm or to be in the public interest, the health professional should escalate concerns through the safeguarding processes\n- In both cases, the health professional should inform the young person of this action, unless doing so could pose significant additional risk for their safe care.\n\n- It is reasonable for the local authority or police to decide whether it is appropriate to inform the parents of the concerns raised. In some circumstances this may not be in the best interest of the young person.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA known 15-year-old girl with a history of multiple sex partners, initially, presented to the ED for emergency contraception after unprotected sex with her partner for which contraception advice was given. On her today's visit, she is complaining of abdominal pain associated with mild fever. You've diagnosed her with PD. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Inform her mother\n- **B)** Treat her as she has capacity\n- **C)** Refuse to treat her\n- **D)** Ask her to inform her parents\n- **E)** Inform her mother after take consent from her Explanation\n\n- Children under the age of 16 can consent to medical treatment if they have sufficient maturity and judgement to enable them fully to understand what is proposed. This was clarified in England and Wales by the House of Lords in the case of Gillick vs West Norfolk and Wisbech AHA & DHSS in 1985\n- In making his judgement the Law Lord, Lord Fraser, offered a set of criteria which rnust apply when medical practitioners are offering contraceptive services to under 161s without parental knowledge or permission. The so-called Fraser Guidelines (some people refer to assessing whether the young person is Gillick competent) state that all the following requirements should be fulfilled:\n- The girl (although under the age of 16 years of age) will understand his advice.\n- They cannot persuade her to inform her parents or to allow him to inform the parents that she is seeking contraceptive advice.\n- She is very likely to continue having sexual intercourse with or without contraceptive treatment.\n- She receives contraceptive advice or treatment her physical or mental health or both are likely to suffer.\n- Her best interests require him to give her contraceptive advice, treatment or both without the parental consent.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nAn II-year-old child is brought to the ED after his mother unable to rouse him. she tells you that he has been unwell for a few days with vomiting and lethargy. His initial observations are:\n\nBoth pupils are equal and reactive to light. His initial blood glucose is reading high. Which bedside test would be most useful to confirm the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Urinary ketones B\n- Blood ketones\n- **C)** Venous blood gas\n- **D)** Chest radiograph Explanation\n- British Society of paediatric Endocrinology-and-Di_betes (BSPED) 2021 guideline recommend following Initial Investigations\n- Blood glucose Capillary refill time: sp02: GCS:\n- **E)** An ECG 130 bpm 3 seconds, 99% on air, 12/15E3 V4 M5\n\n- FBC, Urea and electrolytes (electrolytes on blood gas machine give a guide until accurate results available) and CRP\n- Blood gases (venous or capillary)\n- Ketones - Near patient blood ketones (beta-hydroxybutyrate) testing should be used.\n- If able to obtain sufficient blood, send new diagnosis investigations (HbA1c, TFT, Coeliac screen)\n- Other investigations should be done only if indicated e.g. CXR, CSF, throat swab, blood cultures, urinalysis, culture and sensitivity etc. (A raised white blood cell count is common in DKA and does not necessarily indicate sepsis).\n- Note - you may find difference in Rcemlearning DKA guideline, however you need to stick with BSPED guideline. previous  Next -9\n	\N	[]	\N	f	27	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
24bbeccd-e981-4652-a5cf-c6b5dfb130d3	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-29	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 29	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 29\n\n- **A)** Immediate refer for laparotomy\n- **B)** Advise his mother magnets will pass in his stool\n- **C)** Arrange CT abdomen\n- **D)** Review him after two hours monitoring in the ED\n- **E)** Prescribe laxative Explanation\n- Multiple magnets require endoscopic or surgical intervention.\n- Multiple magnets beyond reach of endoscope require urgent surgical intervention.\n- Management of foreign body ingestion\n- unstable-patient-management\n- Patients should be considered unstable if they present with airway compromise, drooling, inability to tolerate fluids, evidence of sepsis, perforation, or active bleeding.\n- Resuscitations\n- Follow ABCD protocol and resuscitate appropriately\n- Emergency surgery is mandatory in patients with perforation due to obstruction. Resect the perforated segment.\n- consider NC/OG — If patient has trouble handling secretions (drooling and/or spitting)\n- Consider IV antibiotic— if patient has sign of sepsis.\n\n- Stablepatient-management Stable patients: oropharyngeal foreign body Stable patients: esophageal or rectal foreign body (excluding multiple magnets and batteries) Stable patients: esophageal or rectal foreign body - multiple magnets Stable patients: esophageal or rectal foreign body - batteries line\n- Watchful waiting and serial radiographic follow-up\n- Of ingested Or inserted foreign bodies pass through the gastrointestinal (GI) tract without symptoms 2nd line\n- - Should be considered for patients With an Object in the oropharynx that could not be seen during the physical exam. Foreign bodies can be removed using tongue depressor and laryngoscopy\n- Watchful waiting and serial radiographic follow-up in selective Cases Endoscopy is required—urgent endoscopic extraction, irrespective of a full stomach or not, must be performed without delay in the following cases:\n- (I) foreign body in the upper third part of the oesophagus,\n- (2) complete Obstruction,\n- (3) sharp foreign bodies or button batteries for foreign bodies that are nonprogressive, sharp, or nonradiopaque, for multiple foreign bodies, for foreign bodies in the Stomach >2.S Cm in Cm long, Or for Oddly shaped foreign bodies such as open safety pins. Foley catheter removal - is a widely used technique for the removal of a single, smooth, blunt, radiopaque foreign body from the esophagus or rectum. 3M line\n- Surgery\n- For failed extraction or object of cm in rectum.\n- E.Jine\n- Emergency endoscopic or surgical removal Multiple magnets beyond reach of endoscope\n- urgent surgical consultation Emergency endoscopic or surgical removal if impacted Batteries beyond reach Of\n- watchful waiting\n- radiology\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA one-month-old baby presents with his mother to ED with complaint of continuous cry. She states that baby had rolled off a changing table. On examination, he appears distressed and hypoxic. His observations are normal, and his x-ray is shown. The orthopedic team advise conservative management. What is the most appropriate action?\n- **A)** Refer to health visitor to support the family\n- **B)** Complete skeletal survey and discharge home\n- **C)** Discharge with fracture immobilization\n- **D)** Contact the police to arrest the mother\n- **E)** Adrnit to the paediatric team for further evaluation Explanation\n- Management of child abuse\n- Any suspicion of child abuse, involve senior clinician paediatrician or ED consultant\n- All hospitals have their own protocols and designated lead professional ,who is available for advice\n- He or she will assess the child and arrange hospital admission for further investigations (eg: skeletal survey).\n- Involve the police and social care Child may require forensic examination\n- Treat painful or life threatening injuries\n- Ensure that all documentation is legible and meticulous\n- Follow local protocol for multi-agency referral and social care\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 35 years old female who is paraplegic following a spinal cord injury, is brought into the ED with complain of blurred vision. She has not opened her bowels for few days. She has a long term catheter. Her heart rate is 55bpm, BP 147/89mmHg and a bladder scan showed a 70ml residual volume.\n\nYou suspect autonomic dysreflexia. Which of the following statements is correct regarding its pathophysiology?\n- **A)** Reduced vagal tone leading to systemic hypertension\n- **B)** Driven by increase circulating catecholamines\n- **C)** Unopposed sympathetic activity in the upper part of body\n- **D)** Loss of sympathetic tone to the vasculature causes flushing in lower part of body\n- **E)** Increased vagal tone causing relative bradycardia Explanation\n- The sympathetic response is exaggerated due to a lack of compensatory descending parasympathetic stimulation and intrinsic post-traumatic hypersensitivity. This leads to diffuse vasoconstriction, typically to the lower two-thirds of the body, and a significant rise in blood pressure despite maximum parasympathetic vasodilatory efforts above the level of injury.\n- In an intact autonornic system, this increased blood pressure activates the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors leading to a parasympathetic response slowing the heart rate via vagal nerve activity and causing diffuse vasodilation to correct the original increased sympathetic tone.\n\n- PathophysiologyÄgorithm Brain response to rise in Bp BPI rain sends messages via parasympathetic Vagus nerve to the heart to slow it down (bradycardia) and dilatation of blood vessels above the injury level as an attempt to compensate for the rise in BP. Below the cord iniucv Attempted compensation message cannot pass through the spinal cord and blood pressure fontinues to rise Rise in Bp detected by baroreceptors ese then send signal to the brain is cause blood vessels in the skin and abdomen to constrict. When impulse reach at T6 level sympathetic neurons activated and release chemical called nor- inephrine (catecholamine Stretch bladder send nerve impulse to spinal cord Catheter blockage (example), too much urine in bladder O o O\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 70-year-old man brought into the ED after an episode of collapse. He is found to be third degree heart block. He is clinically shocked and you commence transcutaneous pacing. His rhythm strip is shown on the cardiac monitor. What should be your next step?\n- **A)** Refer for transvenous pacing\n- **B)** Increasing the pacing rate\n- **C)** Percussion compression\n- **D)** Increasing the pacing current\n- **E)** Change pads position Explanation\n- Strip suggests intermittent capture beat, Thus, pacing current should be increased.\n- Incase, there is no electrical capture then you need to take following steps: Change pad orientation i-e from A-P pad placement to anterior-apex placement for pacing and don't reuse same pads as most pads are single use.\n\n- Shave hairy chest before pad application\n- Clean skin with alcohol wipe\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 70-year-old male brought to the ED, who is aggressive, agitated, and sweating profusely. He has a history of alcohol and multiple drug abuse. On examination, he was hypertensive, tachycardiac and tachypneic. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Acute exhaustion syndrome\n- **B)** Neuroleptic malignant syndrome\n- **C)** Serotonin syndrome\n- **D)** Acute behavior disorder\n- **E)** Acute alcohol intoxication Explanation\n- Patients clinical features and history of drug abuse suggests Acute behavioural disturbance\n- Acute behavioural disturbance\n- Acute behavioural disturbance (also previously called excited delirium, acute behavioural disorder, or agitated delirium) is an umbrella term\n\nused to describe a presentation which may include abnormal physiology and/or behaviour.\n- Risk factors\n- Substance intoxication (Cocaine, Methamphetamine , cocaine with recent binge use etc)\n- Mental health conditions\n- Substance withdrawal\n- Serotonin syndrome Hypoxia Hypoglycaemia\n- Electrolyte disturbance\n- Other conditions — Sepsis, Head injury ,Anticholinergic syndrome, Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, Thyroid storr-n, Heat stroke, Seizures.\n- NQte_2Substance intoxication and Mental health conditions are the most common factors in ABD presentations\n- Clinical features Agitation\n- Constant physical activity\n- Bizarre behaviour (incl. paranoia, hypervigilance)\n- Fear, panic\n- Unusual or unexpected strength\n- Sustained non-compliance with police or ambulance staff\n- Pain tolerance, impervious to pain\n- Hot to touch, sweating Rapid breathing\n- Tachycardia\n\n- Assess-and-manage-the-risk\n- Managing risk to the patient - Patients experiencing ABD may lose their ability to interact with their environment safely, and their perception of risk may appear non-existent. they are likely to react in such a way as to put themselves at risk, especially while attempting to escape a perceived risk, the perception of which may be increased if restraint is applied. Patients presenting with severe ABD are likely to lack mental capacity (which should be formally assessed and documented) to make treatment decisions and may require emergency treatment under the appropriate legislation.\n- Verbal de-escalation should always be attempted. If safe and available, friends and family may be able to assist. Advice on verbal de-escalation is found in Section 3.\n- Mn in rl who have not had approved training should not be asked to restrain patients. Early escalation to on-site security services is recommended. Hospitals should have sufficient trained security staff available to be able to safely restrain a patient, to keep them from harm and to protect others.\n- Do not attempt to restrain patients with insufficient numbers of staff. There are significant risks to staff when attempting to restrain patients, and these are compounded by insufficient team numbers.\n- RestrainEAttempts should be made to remove any ongoing restraint at the earliest opportunity. There is concern that in ABD, continued exertion under restraint can contribute to poor outcomes (likely due to increasing catecholamine levels, worsening hyperthermia, and metabolic acidosis).\n\n- Prolonged restraint should prompt consideration of rapid tranquilisation.\n- While the police should not normally be called to undertake restrictive practices in healthcare settings solely to facilitate clinical interventions, it has been established that there are scenarios in which police support should be requested:\n- if healthcare staff have been injured\n- if appropriate support is not available from healthcare colleagues in a sufficiently timely manner to ensure the safety of all those affected\n- where there is a risk of serious injury or damage, and safety is compromised\n- De-escalation\n- Verbal and environmental de-escalation - Verbal de-escalation is a valuable tool with which to facilitate patient care and potentially avoid any requirement for restraint.\n- Offer oral sedative - lorazepam 1-2mg (max 4mg per day).\n- Features of ideal environment for verbal and environmental de- escalation:\n- Dedicated to management of patients with severe agitation (or if not possible, a space that can be created or adapted quickly)\n- Adequate and appropriately located exits so that staff can exit without being trapped by the patient\n- Doors which open outwards\n- Quiet, low stimulus\n- Not too warm\n- Absence of equipment/furniture and moveable objects that could be a potential weapon or used to barricade an exit\n- Absence of potential ligature points\n- Constantly observable\n- Staff able to signal need for additional support easily\n\n- Rapid-tranquilisation / Sedation\n- Rapid tranquilisation in ABD is important to prevent further sympathetic over-stimulation and excessive muscular activity from causing a metabolic storm and subsequent cardiovascular collapse.\n- Rapid tranquilisation can also prevent the patient from causing physical harm to themselves or others and facilitate investigations and treatments. The use of rapid tranquilisation for more severe ABD presentations is associated with reduced mortality.\n- Parenteral rapid tranquilisation is more likely to be required if patients:\n- lack capacity to refuse treatment and are non-compliant, and pose a danger to themselves or others, or have a clear need for further treatment/investigation\n- Choice of / Sedation\n\nNICE guideline\n- Intramuscular lorazepam OR\n- Intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine\n- If no response or partial response to above drugs, consider a further dose\n- If there is evidence Of cardiovascular disease, including a prolonged QT interval, or no electrocardiogram has been carried out, avoid intramuscular haloperidol combined with intramuscular promethazine\n- Once sedative taken effect: RCEM guideline\n- Ketamine 4mg/kg 1M\n- Droperidol 5-10mg 1M If these agents are unavailable, midazolam (5-10mg 1M), lorazepam (4mg 1M) or haloperidol (5mg 1M +/- 2mg lorazepam 1M) could be considered. Establish and secure intravenous access\n- Monitor in a high dependency area (Resus Room)\n- Maintain sedation with IV bolus medication until definitive management plan arranged\n- Correct electrolyte / glucose / acid-base abnormalities, correct hyperthermia, look for evidence of DIC or rhabdomyolysis.\n- Institute other emergency treatments and investigations as necessary.\n- Indication(s) for RT & any attempts at de-escalation\n- Mental capacity assessment\n- Restraint, Collateral history, Features consistent with ABD, Police involvement.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nYou have been asked to assess 30 years old male for his mental condition. What is not included in the modified Sad persons Score?\n- **A)** Drug use\n- **B)** Male sex\n- **C)** No social support\n- **C)** -  Celiac disease\n- **E)** Age less than 19 years Explanation\n- Celiac disease is not included in modified SADPERSONS scale.\n\nS D p E Sex male Age <19 or >45 Depression/hopelessness Previous attempt/psychiatric care Excessive alcohol or drug use Rational thinking loss Separated/divorced/widowed Organised or serious attempt No social support Stated future attempt 1 1 2 1 1 2 1 2 1 2\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 1 7 year old Ethiopian man presents to the ED with a two month history of atraumatic bilateral knee pain. He complains of feeling generally tired and you note that he is very small for his age and only weighs 43kg although he denies any recent weight loss. On examination of his knees, he complains of generalised pain on movement but there is no obvious deformity or swelling\n\nResults of blood tests are shown. What is the most likely cause of his knee pain?\n- **A)** Chondromalacia patellae\n- **B)** Osgood-Schlatter's disease\n- **C)** Primary hyperparathyroidism\n- **D)** Osteomalacia\n- **E)** Rickets Explanation\n- Patient features and blood result suggests osteomalacia.\n\nPrimary hyperparathyroidism Over production of PTH, parathyroid adenoma (most common cause)\n- Clinical features\n- Incidental finding on biochemical testing\n- Thirst, frequent or excessive urination, constipation Osteoporosis or fragility fracture, or renal stone\n- Myalgia Investigations Serum calcium - from high-normal to raised. serum intact PTH With immunoradiometric or immunochemical assay - from high-normal to elevated 25-hydroxyvitamin D level - Vitamin D deficiency and PHPT frequently co-exist. Serum alkaline phosphatase — maybe raised Serum phosphorus — Low or low normal 24-hour urinary calcium - high or normal in PHPT; Iow in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scan - T-score: -I to +1 normal; -I to -2.5 osteopenia; < -2.5 osteoporosis\n- Mana ent Refer to a surgeon with expertise in parathyroid surgery if PHPT is confirmed and the person has: Osteomalacia Osteomalacia is a metabolic bone disease characterised by incomplete mineralisation of the underlying mature organic bone matrix (osteoid) following growth plate closure in adults.\n- Clinical features\n- Old age\n- Vitamin D and calcium deficient diets\n- Lack of sunlight exposure\n- Fractures\n- Malabsorption syndromes\n- Diffuse bone pain and tenderness\n- proximal m uscle weakness\n- Family history of osteomalacia\n- Waddling gait\n- Investigations serum calcium level - normal or serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D level\n- serum phosphate level - IOW serum urea and creatinine - ratio elevated\n- intact PTH - high serum alkaline phosphatase - high 24-hour urinary calcium - IOW Consider bone x-rays 24-hour urinary phosphate - high dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry iliac crest biopsy With double tetracycline labelling\n- Management\n- Calcium plus vitamin D supplement,\n- Consider phosphate in some Rickets\n- Rickets is a metabolic bone disease characterised by defective mineralisation of the epiphyseal growth plate cartilage in children.\n- Rickets only occurs in growing children before fusion of the epiphyses\n- Risk factors Age 6 to 23 months Inadequate sunlight exposure Breastfeeding Calcium deficiency phosphate deficiency\n- Family history of rickets Antacids, anticonvulsant\n- Clinical features Bone pain\n- Growth retardation Delayed achievement of motor milestones Bony deformities Muscle weakness\n- Investigations X-ray Of a long bone - widening Of the epiphyseal plate. Serum calcium — may be decreased in hypocalcaemic rickets; normal in hypophosphataemic rickets.\n- Serum inorganic phosphorus - may be decreased in hypocalcaemic rickets; decreased in hypophosphataemic rickets. Serum parathyroid hormone level - high With hypocalcaemic rickets and normal With hypophosphataemic rickets.\n- Alkaline phosphatase and liver function tests -alkaline phosphatase is elevated in rickets 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels (calcidiol) - IOW in vitamin D- deficient rickets, usually nanomoles/L 1<10 nanograms/mL); normal in genetic forms Of hypocalcaemic rickets.\n- Management Symptomatic hypocalcaemia\n- is a medical emergency and requires hospitalisation.\n\n- thirst, rrequent or excessive urination, constipation\n- osteoporosis or fragility fracture, or renal stone\n- albumin-adjusted serum calcium (Ca) 2.85 mmol/litre\n- Consider referral to surgeon if PHPT is confirmed With no symptoms or signs.\n- Consider a bisphosphonate to reduce fracture risk\n- Consider vitamin D supplement\n- Monitoring - If patients decline surgery or are not surgical candidates, serum calcium and creatinine levels should be measured every 12 months and bone density measured every I to 2 years pauents. Hypocalcaemtc seizures and/or cardiovascular instability require an intensive care environment and intravenous calcium infusion.\n- calcium-deficient rickets - calcium and vitamin D supplementation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nYou are treating a 19-year-old man for minor stab wounds sustained in an alleged gang-related incident, Police officers arrive and inform you that this is an ongoing incident and request details of the patient and his injuries. What is the most appropriate response?\n- **A)** Inform the patient that you are obliged to disclose information to the police\n- **B)** Give the police his name date of birth address and a description of his injuries\n- **C)** Inform the police they should send an urgent written request to the hospital\n- **D)** Obtain the patient's consent before speaking to the police\n- **E)** Advise the police you cannot breach the patient confidentiality Explanation\n- Patient has minor stab wounds sustained in an alleged gang-related incident, therefore police should be informed or information to police should be given, however, the police should not usually be informed if a knife or blade injury appears to be accidental, or a result of self- harm.\n- Reporting-gunshot-and-knife-wounds\n- The police are responsible for assessing the risk posed by a member of the public who is armed with, and has used, a gun or knife in a violent attack. They need to consider:\n- the risk of a further attack on the patient\n- the risk to staff, patients and visitors in the emergency department or hospital\n- the risk of another attack near to, or at, the site of the original incident.\n- The police also need statistical information about the number of gunshot and knife injuries, and when and where they occur, to inform their own and their crirne reduction partners' operational and strategic priorities. For these reasons, the police should usually be informed whenever a person presents with a gunshot wound. Even accidental shootings involving lawfully held guns raise serious issues for the police about, for example, firearms licensing. The police should also usually be informed when a person presents with a wound from an attack with a knife, blade or other sharp instrument.\n\n- The police should not usually be informed if a knife or blade injury appears to be accidental, or a result of self-harm. There may also be other circurnstances in which you consider that contacting the police is not proportionate. For example, this might be the case if you consider that no one other than the patient is at risk of harm, and that contacting the police might cause the patient harm or distress, or might damage their trust in you or in doctors generally.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nAn 11 month old baby is brought to the ED with a rash for two hours, as shown in image. His mother gives history of anaphylaxis to cows milk, although he has not eaten any food in the last six hours. On examination, he has pharyngitis and is coryzal. His temperature is 37 80C He has been given some paracetamol at home and has not received any other medication. Which is the likeliest cause of this rash in this patient?   A Food allergy B\n- Acute viral infection\n- **C)** Anaphylactoid reaction\n- **D)** Drug-induced reaction\n- **E)** Vasculitis Explanation\n\n- infant has developed urticaria secondary to viral infection.\n- Urticaria\n- It is a superficial swelling of the the skin (epidermal and mucous membrane) which results in red intensely itchy rash with a pale center.\n- Pathophysiology\n- Degranulation of mast cells release histamine and inflammatory mediators that result in pruritus and oedema due to increase in vascular permeability. . Cause\n- It occurs in response to triggers, typically an acute viral infection commonly in children and certain allergic reaction eg; nut allergy, shellfish, penicillin, vaccine.\n- Management\n- Treat the underlying cause\n- Mild urticaria self-limiting\n- Offer non-sedating antihistamine eg: loratadine and cetirizine.\n- In Severe cases add short course of oral corticosteroid 40mg along with antihistamine. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 45-year-old motorcyclist is brought to the ED with severe left leg pain after falling off the motorbike. The x-ray of his right knee shown. What does the X-ray demonstrate?\n- **A)** Tibial femoral subluxation\n- **B)** Posterior knee dislocation\n- **C)** Patellar tendon rupture\n- **D)** Anterior knee dislocation\n- **E)** Segond fracture dislocation Explanation\n- X-ray demonstrate posterior knee dislocation. previOUS\n	\N	[]	\N	f	28	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
05104305-67ad-48f9-a2a1-b169f496f61e	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-3	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 3	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 3\n\n- **B)** Discusses with your team\n- **C)** Continue resuscitation until your consultant is free\n- **D)** Arrange instruments for thoracotomy and ask the surgical registrar if you can assist him\n- **E)** Seek help from your junior colleague\n- Explanation As you are leading the traurna you've certain extra duties however, your primary duty is to patients. Their care, dignity and safety must be your first concern and you must follow the guidance in Cood medical practice and Raising and acting on concerns about patient safety when you have reason to believe that systems, policies, procedures or colleagues are, or may be, placing patients at risk of harm.\n- TO follow the guidance in Cood medical practice, as per AT LS thoracotomy is indicated in any patient in cardiac arrest <IO minutes in blunt trauma. Thus, in this scenario thoracotomy isn't indicated and you've to take appropriate action as in this case overrule the surgical registrar.\n- You must follow the guidance in Good rnedical practice and Raising and acting on concerns about patient safety when you have reason to believe that systems, policies, procedures or colleagues are, or may be, placing patients at risk of harm.\n- GQ-d-medigaLpragtice-inc\\u-gfour-important domains\n\n- You must be competent in all aspects of your work, including management, research and teaching\n- You must keep your professional knowledge and skills up to date.\n- You must prescribe drugs or treatment, including repeat prescriptions, only when you have adequate knowledge of the patient's health and are satisfied that the drugs or treatment serve the patient's needs\n- You must provide effective treatments based on the best available evidence\n- You must take all possible steps to alleviate pain and distress whether or not a cure may be possible\n- You must consult colleagues where appropriate\n- You must respect the patient's right to seek a second opinion\n- You must check that the care or treatment you provide for each patient is compatible with any other treatments the patient is receiving, including (where possible) self-prescribed over-the-counter medications.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 32-year-old woman is brought into ED with 7 days history of postpartum bleeding following delivery at home. she is complaining of fever and easily fatiguability. On Examination, her hands are cold and clammy. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Uremic shock syndrome\n- **B)** Hemolysis\n- **C)** DIC\n- **D)** Septic shock syndrome\n- **E)** Cervical infection\n- Explanation\n- Sometirnes the entire placenta (also called "afterbirth") remains in the uterus after childbirth. A retained placenta or fetal tissue can lead to hemorrhaging (bleeding), severe infection or even death.\n- Retained placental or fetal tissue can happen after:\n- Abortion (terminated pregnancy).\n- Cesarean birth (C-section).\n- Miscarriage.\n- Vaginal delivery.\n- Postpartum haemorrhage(PPH)\n- Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) is heavy bleeding after birth. PPH can be primary or secondary:\n- Primary PPH is when you lose 500 ml (a pint) or more of blood within the first 24 hours after the birth of your baby. Primary PPH can be minor, where you lose 500—1000 ml (one or two pints), or major, where you lose more than 1000 ml (more than two pints).\n- Secondary PPH occurs when you have abnormal or heavy vaginal bleeding between 24 hours and 12 weeks after the birth.\n- Causes of PPH\n- Th\n- of uterine contraction (uterine atony)\n- Tissuezretained products of conception\n- tract injury\n\n- Thrombin: abnormalities of coagulation (haemophilia, vWD, DIC, severe infection, ITP, abruption, amniotic fluid embolus)\n- Risk factor (notes\n- Polyhydramnios, multiple gestation, macrosomia\n- Fever, prolonged rupture of membrane\n- Rapid labour, prolonged labour,fibroids, placentapraevia, uterine anomalies\n- History of hereditary coagulopathies or liver disease, Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura Bruising etc\n- for major PPH\n- 14-gauge cannula x 2\n- FBC, coagulation, U&Es, I-FTS\n- Cross-match (4 units, FFP, PLT, cryoprecipitate)\n- ECG, oximeter\n- Foley catheter\n- Hb bedside testing\n- Blood products\n- Consider central and arterial lines\n- Commence record chart\n- Weigh all swabs and estimate blood loss\n- Management\n\nManagement of maior PPH Maior obstetric haemorrhage Blood loss greater than 1000 ml Continuing major obstetric haemorrhage or clinical shock CaU_fgr-help Senior midwife/obstetrician and anaesthetist\n- Alert haematologist Alert blood transfusion laboratory Alert consultantobstetrician on call Resuscitation Airway Breathing Circulation Oxygen mask (15 1) Fluid balance (e.g. 2 1 isotonic crystalloid, 1.51 colloid) Blood transfusion (O RhD\n- negative or group-specific blood) Blood products (FFP, PLT, cryoprecipitate, factor VIIa) Keep patient warm Medical treatment Rub up the uterine fundus (bimanual uterine compression) Empty bladder Oxytocin 5 iu, slow IV (repeat if necessary) Ergometrine 0.5 mg. slow IV or 1M Oxytocin infusion (40 iu in 500 m') CarboprostO.25 mg 1M every 15 minutes up to 8 times Carboprost (intramyometrial) 0.5 mg Misoprostol 800 micrograms sublingually Consider tranexamic acid 1 g IV If above fails Intrauterine balloon tamponade Brace suture Consider interventional radiol\n\nSurgery\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 65-year-old female known case of cholangiocarcinoma on chemotherapy came into ED with complain of Dysuria. She had last session of chemotherapy 10 days ago. Her observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 90bpm Temp AIF What is the next step of management in this specific patient?\n- **A)** Start Antibiotics after IJA result\n- **B)** Send FBC, blood culture and\n- **C)** Consider discharge as vital signs are normal\n- **D)** Discharge home with advice\n- **E)** Give IV fluids and antibiotics\n- Explanation\n\n- Neutropenic-Sepsis\n- Diagnose neutropenic sepsis inspatients having anticancer treatment whose neutrophil count 0.5 x IO /L or less and who have either:\n- temperature >38 C or\n- other Symptoms or signs indicating possible infection such as dysuria, diarrhoea, or productive cough.\n- Cause of neutropenia:\n- Drugs/treatments, e.g: cytotoxic chemotherpy, heamtopoietic stem cells transplantation and irnmuno supressive drugs.\n- infection:Hiv, hepatitis , influenza , shigella\n- Autoimune disease: crohn,RA and SLE\n- Bone marrow failure e.g: aplastic anemia , myelodysplastic syndrome, acute luekemia\n- B12 and folate deficiency\n- Emergency treatment and assessment :\n- Give-oxygen-therapy_to people with reduced oxygen saturation\n- Take-blood-tests-and-microbiology-samples-including\n- Blood gas including glucose and lactate measurernent\n- Blood culture — ideally done before antibiotic administration.\n- FBC, C-reactive protein (CRP), Clotting screen\n- Creatinine, LJ&E\n- CXR or CT as per indication\n- IV antibiotic therapy.\n- Treat suspected neutropenic sepsis as an acute medical emergency and offer beta lactam monotherapy with piperacillin with tazobactam\n\nas initial empiric antibiotic therapy to patients with suspected neutropenic sepsis immediately.\n- offer prophylaxis with a fluoroquinolone during the expected period of neutropenia only. Give-an-intravenous-fluid-bolus to restore tissue perfusion.(as per need)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 25-year-old male previous no known comorbid came into ED with a complaint of pain in right hand which worsens on movement, for which he has had a course of antibiotics. However, his condition hasn't improved. What is your next step of management?\n- **A)** IV antibiotics B\n- Refer to Surgery\n- **C)** IV fluids, analgesia and IV antibiotics\n- **D)** Aspiration\n- **E)** Advice strong pain killer and discharge\n- Explanation\n- Pyogenic-flexor-tenosynovitis\n- Infection of a finger flexor tendon sheath maybe due to penetrating injury.\n- Kanayecs-griteria-for-flexor-tenosynoyitjs\n\n- Tenderness along the course of the flexor tendon\n- Fusiform or symmetrical swelling of the finger\n- Pain with passive range of motion\n- A flexed posture of the finger\n- Management Urgent referral for surgical exploration, irrigation draining and IV antibiotics\n- Consider tetanus prophylaxis\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 45-year-old male sustained lacerated wound on his right knee two days back came into ED with complaint of Severe pain in his right knee associated with fever and swelling of joint. You suspect septic arthritis. what is the single most causative agent in this case?   A\n- Staph Aureus\n- **B)** Streptococcus\n- **C)** Staph pyogenes\n- **D)** E.coli\n- **E)** Staph epidermidis\n- Explanation Septic arthritis\n- The hip is the most common joint affected, followed by the knee.\n\n- Causative agent: Staph aureus (most common)\n- Clinical features: unable to weight bear and fever and joint inflammation\n- 'nyestjgatjgn: lab work can be helpful, as well as adjunct use of US of the hip to look for an effusion.\n- Management; Prompt surgical drainage and IV antibiotics\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 30-year-old motorcyclist is brought into ED following a Road traffic accident. After initial assessment, you suspect massive haemothorax. What is the initial bedside investigation to confirm the diagnosis?\n- **A)** CT scan\n- **B)** DPL\n- **D)** Chest X-ray\n- **E)** MRI\n- Explanation\n- Use CXR and eFAST to confirm diagnosis\n- eFAST is accurate, rapid, noninvasive, repeatable, and portable.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 30-year-old laborer is brought into ED by Ambulance after falling approximately 6m on building site and sustaining head injury. His GCS  early treatment given, patient intubated stabilized and shifted to ICIJ. After sometimes\n\npatient started to deteriorate again with uncontrolled hypertension and bradycardia. What would be your Immediate step?\n- **A)** Raise the head Of the bed to 300\n- **B)** Hypertonic Saline\n- **C)** IV mannitol\n- **D)** IV labetalol\n- **E)** Anti-epilepticus Explanation\n- ED\n- Initial management:\n- Follow ABC protocol\n- The primary goals of treatment are to maintain cerebral perfusion and oxygenation by optimizing intravascular volume and ventilation.\n- Recognize and treat elevated ICP\n- Airway & breathing\n- Treat any condition that compromises ventilation (e.g., altered mental status, facial/neck trauma, pneumothorax).\n- Patients with severe injury (CCS score of Sd) require intubation.\n- Circulation _ Provide aggressive fluid resuscitation to prevent hypotension and secondary brain injury. Normal saline is recommended for volume\n\nresuscitation.\n- Maintain systolic blood pressure at 2100 mm Hg for patients 50 to 69 years old or at 2110 mm Hg for patients 15 to 49 years old or >70 years old\n- Give titrated IV opioid analgesia\n- Patientpositioning\n- Raising the head of the bed to 30 degrees may improve cerebral blood flow by lowering ICP.\n- Temporary_byperventilation\n- Use hyperventilation only in moderation and for as limited a period as possible. It is preferable to keep paC02 at 4.5 to 5kpa.\n- Hyperventilation will lower ICP in deterioration Pt with expanding intracranial haematoma until surgeon perform craniotomy.\n- Mannitol and hypertonic saline\n- Mannitol is osmotic diuretic.it is used to decrease ICP\n- Avoid mannitol in hypotensive Pts\n- Hypertonic saline is alternative to mannitol in the patient who is not adequately fluid resuscitated or hypotensive.\n- Seizure-treatment-and-prophyJaxis\n- Prolonged seizures can worsen secondary injury.\n- Treat acute seizures with midazolam or lorazepam, and if seizures continue, treat as for status epilepticus.\n- Temperature control\n- avoid hypothermia\n- Glucose control\n- maintain at 100 to 180 milligrams/dL or 5.55 to 9.99 mmol/L\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 68-year-old male known case of H TN presented in ED with 3 hours history of central chest pain. He feels generally unwell and appears pale and clammy. A repeat ECG is given.\n\nWhat is your next step of management?\n- **A)** Clopidogrel 300mg\n- **B)** Discharge the patient\n- **C)** Aspirin 300mg\n- **D)** STEMI Alert\n- **E)** Transfer to the ER room\n- Explanation\n- His ECC revealed ST-elevation anterior myocardial infarction (STEMI)\n- Morphine (analgesia)\n- Oxygen (if needed)\n- Nitrites (GTN) Aspirin\n- Mnemonic - MONA\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 40-year-old male working in a farm bitten by venomous snake showing double fang marks. On examination, you note Petechiae around the wound. His general condition is stable with no systemic compromise. What is your management plan?\n\nA Give analgesia\n- **B)** Cive 1M Adrenaline\n- **C)** Discharge home\n- **D)** Commence Antivenom\n- **E)** Give antihistamine\n- Explanation\n- Envenoming from snake bite is uncommon in the I-JK.\n- Many exotic snakes are kept, some illegally, but the only indigenous venomous snake is the adder (Vipera berus).\n- The bite may cause local and systemic effects\n- Local effects\n- ocal effects include pain, swelling, bruising, and tender enlargement of regional lymph nodes:\n- Systemic effects\n- Systemic effects include early anaphylactic symptoms (transient hypotension with syncope, angioedema, urticaria, abdominal colic, diarrhoea, and vomiting), with later persistent or recurrent hypotension, ECG abnormalities, spontaneous systemic bleeding, coagulopathy, adult respiratory distress syndrome, and acute renal failure.\n- Fatal envenoming is rare but the potential for severe envenoming must not be underestimated.\n\n- Management\n- Give analgesia\n- Early anaphylactic symptoms should be treated with intramuscular adrenaline/epinephrine\n- Indjcagion.s-for-eurgpe.a.n-y.ipe.r-snake-ye.nom-angise.ru.n(a.ntiyenom)\n- Treatment include systemic envenoming, especially hypotension, ECC abnormalities, vomiting, haemostatic abnormalities, and marked local envenoming such that after bites on the hand or foot, swelling extends beyond the wrist or ankle within 4 hours of the bite.\n- For those patients who present with clinical features of severe envenoming(e.g. shock, ECC abnormalities, or local swelling that has advanced from the foot to above the knee or from the hand to above the elbow within 2 hours of the bite), a higher initial dose of the european viper snake venom antiserum is recommended.\n- if symptoms of systemic envenomingpersist contact the National Poisons Information Service (NIPS). viper snake venom antiserum, must be immediately treated with adrenaline/epinephrine. 10 / 10 IO. A 33-year-old man presented to the ED with two hours history of palpitations. His PMH is unremarkable, and he denies any chest pain and breathlessness. His observations are: Pulse 270bpm\n\nRR 20 breath/min GCS 15/15. His ECG is given What would be your initial step of management?\n- **A)** Valsalva maneuver\n- **B)** IV fluids\n- **C)** DC cardioversion\n- **D)** IV Arniodarone   E\n- IV adenosine\n- Explanation\n- ECG revealed SVT - Narrow complex tachycardia\n- Retrograde P waves are visible preceding each QRS complex (upright in VI, inverted in lead II)\n- Management\n\nFirst line Vagal manoeuvre Carotid massage Valsalva manoeuvre previous  Second line Adenosine 6mg if no response 12mg if no response further 18mg adenosine If contraindicates then verapamil Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	29	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
8c6d581c-4be3-45f4-8945-b22ad8cc307d	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-30	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 30	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 30\n\n- **D)** lymphoma O\n- **E)** Active TB Explanation\n- Absolute Contraindications to consideration of deceased donation\n- Age >85 years (on or after their 85th birthday)\n- Primary intra-cerebral lymphoma\n- All secondary intracerebral tumours\n- Any active cancer with evidence of spread outside affected organ within 3 years of donation\n- Melanoma (except completely excised Stage 1 cancers)\n- Active (not in remission) haematological malignancy (myeloma, lymphoma, leukaemia)\n- Definite, probable or possible case of human transmissible spongiform encephalopathy (TSE), including CJD and vCJD, individuals whose blood relatives have had familial CJD, other neurodegenerative diseases associated with infectious agents.\n- TB: active and untreated\n- West Nile Virus (WNV) infection\n- HIV disease (but not HIV infection)\n- A history of infection with Ebola virus\n- HIV infection means people who have infection with HIV but none Of the associated complications.\n- Active TB means not in remission; spread outside affected organ includes spread to lymph nodes\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 67-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department (ED) with a 2-hour history of central chest pain, described as a tight, band-like sensation. The pain does not radiate but is associated with mild shortness of breath. He has a past medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and obesity. His observations are: Temp: 36.80C HR: 88 bpm BP:  mmHg RR: 18 breaths/min Sp02: 98% on room air A 12-lead ECG is performed, as shown below. What is the most appropriate next step in his management in the ED?\n- **A)** Perform a repeat ECC in IO minutes\n- **B)** Perform an urgent arterial blood gas to assess potassium levels\n- **C)** Obtain a blood sample to measure troponin levels\n- **D)** Conduct a repeat ECG with posterior leads\n- **E)** Arrange for an urgent chest X-ray Explanation\n\n- In this ECG, posterior MI is suggested by the presence of:\n- ST depression in V2-3\n- Tall, broad R waves (> 30ms) in V2-3\n- Dominant R wave (R/S ratio > 1) in V2 Upright terminal portions of the T waves in V2-3\n- ECG should be repeated with posterior leads - Posterior infarction is diagnosed based on the presence of ST segment elevation >O.5mm in leads V7-9.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nYou are reviewing a 90-year-old nursing home patient who is vomiting and confused. He is very frail and has advanced dementia and has a distended abdomen. Hix abdominal x-ray is shown What is the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Gastric outflow obstruction\n- **B)** Toxic megacolon\n- **C)** Small bowel obstruction D\n- Sigmoid volvulus\n- **E)** Pneumoperitoneum Explanation\n- This radiograph demonstrates a greatly dilated sigmoid (coffee bean shaped) that almost fills the entire abdomen.\n\nSmall bowel obstruction At's a medical emergencies requiring early diagnosis and intervention Antra-abdominal adhesion the most common cause of sao, > Other- hernias, Crohns disease,intestnal malignancy, appendicitis Clinical presentations >AbdominaI pain, > bbatng, vomitmg bdiary), > rectal flatus and cessatbn Of abdominal tenderness '*computed tomography (CT) scan Of the abdomen and pelvis >ddation of the small bowel (>3cm diameter) > more prominent valvulae con Investigations niventes creating a\n- coiled- spring appearance >small bowel usually lies more centrally tests - LJ&E '*Serum amylase & lipase Fluid resuscitation, > bowel decompression (using a nasogastric tube), and analgesia > Surgical consultation — as patient need urgent surgical intervention. Management Large bowel obstruction mechanical interruption (either compkte or partial) to the now of intestinal contents. with multiple potential causes (e.g., ma\n- ant cobrectal disease. colonic volvulus. benign stricture) Of large Ileus 'Ileus is a slowing of gastrointestinal motibty accompanéd by distenton, in the absence of a mechanical intestnal obstruction. At is a of exclusOn bowel obstructions are caused by after bowel obstructön has an underlying colorectal malignancy. > Sigmoid-y.Q!yuLuEis more usually seen frad or Older patients. > Caecal volvulus more rare and more commonly seen in younger patients. >intermittent abdominal pain > abdominal distentan > Nausea & vomiting > recent weight Oss > rectal bleeding, rectal mass & tympanic abdomen. tenesmus urgent computed tomography (CT) scan Of the abdomen and pelvis to be performed as soon as possible. > dilation Of colorp6cm >dilation Of cecum > 9cm > The smal more centrally, wdh the large bowel framing it (lateral). >Sigmoid volvulus: a characteristic Coffee bean' appearance. ; Caecal volvulus: often as having a fetal appearance (comma' shaped). Medical emergencies requiring early diagnosis and htervention, Innally- supportive Consider bowel decompression via nasogastric Consider urgent flexible sigmoidoscopy in volvulus - Often endoscopic decompression using flexible sOmoidoscopy aJows the twist in the bowel to be untwisted and the obstruction to be relieved. If fad then refer to surgery. Refer to colorectal surgeon for emergency surgery — if patient has malignant obstructan Or has developed peritonitis or been ruled out. At usually occurs in response to physiobgical stress, ncluding surgery (usually gastrointestinal), sepsis, metaboz derangements, and gastrointestinal diseases. ; Ileus is commonly seen the postoperative setting Vomiting Abdominal ; Inabiity to tolerate oral feeding Absence Of flatus. A-J&E >abdomen and pelvis CT >NiI by mouth and intravenous fluids >reduction in analgesa replacement with non- ophid analgesia (to avod narcotic ileus). >Consider nasogastric decompressk.n. Aon\n- suraical ileus Ail by mouth and intravenous fh_Jids > management of the underlying condition (acuteJsystemic Alnesses associated with htestinal hypomotibty such as diabetes mellitus Chagas disease, scleroderma, and neurological diseases,\n\nreduce . i Chdiner\n- CS. NG d«anpresshn\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 19-year-old girl presented to the ED with complaint of 4 days history of sore throat. On assessment you note hot potato voice and drooling. Her throat is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Bacterial tonsillitis\n- **B)** Peri-tonsillar abscess\n- **C)** Viral pharyngitis\n- **D)** Epiglottitis\n- **E)** Para-pharyngeal abscess Explanation\n- Patient clinical features and image suggest peritonsillar abscess\n- Her throat show deviated uvula\n\nPeritonsillar abscess (quinsy) Peritonsillar abscess is a complication of acute tonsillitis. Tonsillitis is inflammation of the pharyngeal tonsils.\n- It most common in children 2 to 4 years of age, but its incidence is increasing in adults.\n- Cause\n- Streptococcus pyogenes (most common) Staphylococcus aureus,\n- Haemophilus influenzae.\n- Anaerobic organisms including prevotella spp. , and Fusobacterium spp\n- Clinical features Fever\n- Neck pain\n- Trismus (Lock jaw)\n- A muffled voice ( hot potato voice) A displaced uvula\n- An enlarged, displaced tonsil, With swelling Of the peri-tonsillar region\n- Investiqations\n- The diagnosis is clinical\n- Ultrasound may be helpful in differentiating a peritonsillar abscess from peritonsillitis. It may also help to locate the optimal site for drainage.\n- CT scan in not required Mana em n Analgesia Hydration if required Broad spectrum antibiotic Surgical drainage or Ultrasound-guided aspiration Retropharyngeal abscess Retropharyngeal abscess (RPA) is a neck infection involving abscess formation in the space between the pre\n- vertebral fascia and the constrictor muscles.\n- The condition occurs most frequently in children under 5 years Of age, but its incidence is increasing in adults\n- Risk factors\n- Foreign body ingestion\n- Trauma to posterior pharyngeal wall\n- Dental carieshnfection\n- Diabetes mellitus\n- Clinical features Fever Neck pain Or torticollis Odynophagia (painful swallowing) Dysphagia Neck swelling/massnymphadenopathy Anorexia, malaise Trismus — caused by irritation of masseters\n- Investigations FBC. ESR CT neck With contrast X-ray of neck, US of neck Blood culture\n- Manaqement\n- Intravenous corticosteroid (dexamethasone) nebulised adrenaline (epinephrine) Start empirical antibiotic therapy Supportive care and analgesia (ParacetamolJibuprofen)\n- Surgery - If an RPA is confirmed on surgical examination, the patient should undergo transoral ncision and drainage.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA three year Old girl is brought to the ED after eating plants in the garden. She appears asymptomatic. Her observations are: Temp: 37.10C\n\nBM: 60 beats per minute 90/50mmHg 6.5 Which of the following plants is most likely to be responsible for this presentation?\n- **A)** Wolfsbane\n- **B)** Deadly nightshade\n- **C)** Hemlock\n- **D)** Willow\n- **E)** Foxglove Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of Foxglove poisoning.\n- This questions has been revised and the correct answer is Foxglove, as there are two types of hemlock and they posses different symptoms.\n\nPoisonous plants Convulsants (Water Belladonna (Nightshade. éimsonweeg Henbane) Aconitum napellus (wolfsbane) Clinical features\n- Cardioactive steroids are found in many plants, including foxglove (Digitalis spp_)\n- Acute toxicity closely resembles that from digoxin\n- Palpitations, syncope, arrhythmias, and dyspnoea\n- Hypotension - May be direct effect of digitalis- like glycosides.\n- Bradycardia - May be direct effect of muscarinics digitalis-like glycosides\n- Nausea. vomiting, diarrhoea. abdominal pain, or anorexia.\n- Lethargy. weakness. and confusion.\n- Visual disturbance\n- Cicutoxin a diol found in the water hembck (Cicuta maculata), western water hemlock (Cicuta douglasii).\n- Toxicity can manifest as early as 15 minutes fdlowing exposure.\n- Mild effects include GI discomfort. followed by bradycardia, hypotension, respiratory distress. seizures. and death.\n- Seizures maybe severe and refractory to conventional anticonvulsant therapy.\n- Deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna), jimsonweed (Datura spp.), and henbane (Hyoscyamus niger) all contain atropine atropine-like alkaloids\n- Ingestion or smoking results in antimuscarinic effects such as tachycardia, hyperthermia, mydriasis, decreased bowel sounds, urinary retention, altered mental status. and dry, flushed skin.\n- Symptoms can include numbness and tingling, slow or fast heart rate, and gastrointestinal manifestations such as nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. Respiratory paralysis and heart rhythm abnormalities can lead to death Treatment\n- Activated charcoal within first of presentation.\n- Assess serum potassium concentration and obtain an ECG to aid prognosis and therapy.\n- Administer digoxin immune Fab fragments to patients with a serum mEq/L\n- Activated charcoal with n first of presentation.\n- Treat seizures with benzodiazepines or barbiturates.\n- Supportive care\n- Benzodiazepines are useful in calming patients, but avoid antipsychotics such as haloperidol to prevent further antimuscarinic activity.\n- Consider Physostigmine\n- The treatment is synptomatic and supportive; there is specific antidote. Note - there is another plant called NICOTINIC AND NICOTINE-LIKE TOXINS (POISON HEMLOCK) which cause overstimulation of nicotinic cholinergic receptors can rapidly progress from seemingly mild symptoms to death from respiratory failure. other features - nervousness and tremor due to sympathomimetic stimulation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 45 years old male presented with intermittent palpitation associated with dizziness for four days. An ECG shows atrial fibrillation with ventricular rate 147\n\nbeats per minutes. His BP is  . He had no chest pain or breathlessness On auscultation you hear a regurgitant murmur on the mitral area. He is normally fit and healthy and he doesn't take any medication. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?\n- **A)** IV flecainide 2mg/kg\n- **B)** Metoprolol 2.5-5mg IV\n- **C)** Synchronised DC cardioversion\n- **D)** Oral digoxin 500mcg\n- **E)** IV amiodarone 300mg Explanation\n- In patients presenting to the ED who are known to have (>48 hours). a reason for why they are tachycardic should be sort. If a secondary/reversible cause has been excluded then rate control should be considered\n- NICE recommends rate control (beta blockers and rate limiting CCB) as the first-line strategy to people with atrial fibrillation, except in people:\n- whose atrial fibrillation has a reversible cause.\n\n- who have heart failure thought to be primarily caused by atrial fibrillation.\n- with new-onset atrial fibrillation (<48hrs)\n- For whom a rhythm control strategy would be more suitable based on clinical judgement\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA motorcyclist involved in RTC presented to the ED with complain of wrist pain. His x-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Fracture radial styloid\n- **B)** Lunate dislocation\n- **C)** Scaphoid fracture\n- **D)** Scaphoid lunate dislocation\n- **E)** Rolando fracture dislocation\n- Wrist radiograph reveals scaphoid lunate gap more than 3mm (terry- Thomson sign) Scapholunate dissociation\n- Scapholunate dissociation is a widening of the scapholunate joint space Of >3 mm on the posteroanterior view or >8 mm on the\n\nulnar/deviation/clenched fist view.\n- It's due to scapholunate ligament injury of the wrist\n- It results from a fall on an outstretched hand with impact on the thenar eminence.\n- Piagnosis\n- Wrist radiograph will reveal scaphoid lunate gap more than 3mm (terry-Thomson sign)\n- "cortical ring sign" can be found on radiograph, its due to scaphoid tilts toward the observer so that it appears shorter as it is viewed more on its end. This causes the circular cortex of the bone to become more prominent and appear as a ring\n- Management : refer to hand surgeon or orthopedic.\n\n"scaphoid cortical ring sign" "Terry Thomas sign"\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 70-year-old man arrives at a rural emergency department with acute chest pain that started three hours ago. He appears sweaty and feels nauseous. The nearest cardiac catheterization center is located two hours away by transport. His observations are: Temp: 36.40C\n\nHR: 108 bpm BP:  mmHg RR: 18 breaths per minute Sp02: 98% on room air Her ECG is shown. What is the most appropriate next step in management, alongside aspirin and symptom relief?\n- **A)** Administer prasugrel and prepare for transfer\n- **B)** Administer alteplase and reassess the ECG before deciding on transfer\n- **C)** Initiate fondaparinux and manage locally\n- **D)** Provide aspirin only\n- **E)** Start alteplase and arrange transfer\n- Explanation\n- In STEM' NICE recommends:\n- Offer fibrinolysis if presenting in 12 hours of symptoms and PCl not possible in 120 mins\n- When treating people with fibrinolysis, Give an antithrombin at the same time :\n- Alteplase (recombinant tPA (rtPA)): give by an accelerated regimen, eg 15mg IV bolus, followed by 0.75mg/ kg (max 50mg) IV\n\ninfusion for 30min, then 0.5mg/ kg (max 35mg) IV infusion over 60min.\n- Antithrombin - Give LMWH (eg enoxaparin Img/ kg stat) or heparin concomitantly through a separate IV line. (50000 IV bolus, then IOOOU/ hr IV), according to local protocols.\n- ECG - Offer ECC 60-90 mins after fibrinolysis\n- Dural Antiplatelet therapy - Aspirin with clopidogrel 300mg PO (or prasugrel 60mg PO or ticagrelor 180mg PO). if not given already.\n- Do not repeat fibrinolysis - offer immediate angiography with follow- on PCl if indicated by ECG\n- Seek specialist advice for recurrent myocardial\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 27 years old Woman presents with a severe asthma attack and has a cardio- respiratory arrest during the initial assessment ALS resuscitation is commenced and a supraglottic air way placed. Which of the following statements is correct regarding her initial management?\n- **A)** A supra-glottic away will provide effective ventilation during resuscitation\n- **B)** Magnesium sulphate is contra-indicated as it may worsen hypotension\n- **C)** Due to hyperinflation higher energies for defibrillation may be required\n- **D)** Ventilation rate should be high (20 breaths per minute)\n- **E)** Bilateral pneumothoraces can be effectively ruled out using chest x- ray Explanation Hyperinflation increase transthoracic impedance increasedefibrillator energy if first shock is unsuccessful.\n- Cardiac arrest in Asthma\n- follow standard BLS and ALS protocol\n- Early intubation\n- respiratory rate IO breath/minutes\n- if dynamic hyperinflation suspected the disconnect tracheal tube to relive the gas trapping.\n- hyperinflation increase transthoracic impedance (resistance). Therefore increase defibrillator energy if first shock is unsuccessful.\n- if tension pneumothorax suspected then go for pleural ultrasound which is faster and more sensitive than CXR. early decompression followed by chest drain insertion is needed and always consider bilateral pneumothoraces in asthma related cardiac arrest\n- consider Extracorporeal life support(ECLS) in case of refractory respiratory and cardiac failure. 10 / 10 IO. A patient with severe sepsis secondary to pneumonia is being intubated for transfer to ICIJ. The anaesthetic team feels that ketamine should be use as an induction agent. Which of the following receptors would the ketamine interact with?\n\nO\n- **A)** GABA\n- **B)** Serotonin\n- **D)** Dopamine\n- **E)** AMPA Explanation\n- KetaminezNoveI agent causing dissociative anaesthesia via NMDA receptors. It is a SNS stimulant and as such a bronchodilator and inotrope. presentatiqmclear liquid at either 10mg/ml or 100mg/ml in 20ml phials ED use: 10mg/ml in 20 ml syringe PQSe; 0.1mg/kg analgesic, 0.5-1.0mg/kg as sedation. Initial dose in young adults is 0.5-1mg/kg with 0.5mg/kg top ups as required every 5 — 10 minutes Pharmacokinetics-onset;-30sec. Duration: 5-10min Adverse effects: Can increase secretions and as s such is occasionally associated with laryngospasm. Emergence phenomenon, hence consider Pre- treating adults with 1 — 2mg of Midazolam. Post use confusion — a particular problem in the elderly -Adya.n.tages;-excellent analgesic, little CVS depression. Excellent safety profile in particular in pre-hospital and paediatric use\n\ne_ previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	30	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
0a12ecee-94ba-497c-afb5-7b789193a1be	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-31	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 31	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 31\n\nBlood pressure: 90/ 55 mmHg Which of the following medications is contraindicated in this scenario? Respiratory rate: 22 breaths per minute Oxygen saturation: 96% on room air Her ECG is shown. Which of the following medications is contraindicated in this scenario? A\n- Clopidogrel\n- **B)** Morphine\n- **C)** Alteplase\n- **D)** Ticagrelor   E\n- Glyceryl trinitrate\n- Explanation\n- Analgesia-a-ant:emetic- Administer IV opioid analgesia and antiemetic as required. If pain is unrelieved, commence CTN intravenous infusion (IVI). Start at 0.6mg/ hr and as necessary, provided systolic BP is >90mmHg and should not be given to patients with, marked bradycardia or tachycardia, RV infarction, known severe aortic stenosis, or phosphodiesterase 5 inhibitor use within the previous 24— 48 hrs\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 75 year old male known case of rheumatoid arthritis came to the ED with compliant of severe worsening pain in both hands. His examination reveals multiple swollen and tender joints with decreased range of motion in the hands, elbows, and hips bilaterally. His hand image is shown. What abnormality is it?\n- **A)** Heberden's node\n- **B)** Z deformity of thumb   C\n- Swan neck\n- **D)** Boutonniere deformity\n- **E)** Bouchard's node Explanation\n- Image shows Z deformity of the thumb.\n\nOsteoarthritis\n- Osteoarthritis is defined as a disorder Of synovial joints which occurs when damage triggers repair processes leading to structural changes within a joint.\n- These repair processes alter the structure of the joint over time. causing typical features of:\n- Localized loss Of cartilage _\n- Remodelling of adjacent bone and the formation of osteoph',nes (new bone at joint margins). Mild synovitis (inflammation of the synovial membrane that lines the joint capsule).\n- Risk factors\n- Genetic\n- Adv'anced age\n- Obesity female sex\n- Low or high bone density\n- Joint injury or malalignment Clinical feature! Pain - OA\n- reIated pain is usually associated With activities, with pain in weight-bearing joints,\n- No moming joint-related stiffness. or morning stiffness lasting no longer than 30 minutes.\n- Knee giving way or locking\n- Commonly involved joints are the knee, hip, hands, and lumbar and cervical spine Hand OA spares the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and involves the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) and distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints, which helps to distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis. Bony deformities\n- common in the hands and lead to enlargement of the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints (Bouchard's nodes) and distal (DIP) joints (Heberden's nodes), as well as squaring at the base of the thumb (the first carpometacarpal joint).\n- In advanced knee OA, there may also be new bone formation, causing bony swellings around the knee joint\n- limited range of motion, malalignment\n- Tenderness and crepitus\n- Investigations\n- X-ray of affected joints - new bone formation (osteophytes). joint space narrowing. and subchondral sclerosis and Cysts.\n- Serum CRP and ESR — Normal Rheumatoid arthritis\n- Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a chronic systemic inflammatory disease. Early RA iS defined as disease duration of 5 years or less from the onset of symptoms.\n- RA presents as inflammatory arthritis typically affecting the small joints of the hands and the feet. and usually both sides equally and symmetrically, although any synovial joint can be affected\n- Common in Age 50 to 55 years, female sex\n- Risk factors\n- Genetic predisposition , smoking\n- Clinical features\n- Active symmetrical arthritis lasting weeks\n- Joint pain - Most commonly bilateral metacarpophalangeal (MCP), proximal interphalangeal (PIP). and metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joints are invdved_ Morning stiffness - hour of morning stiffness is considered a sign Of inflammatory disease.\n- Swan neck deformity\n- Classically, there is distal interphalangeal (DIP) hyperflexion with proximal interphalangeal (PIP) hyperextension.\n- Z\n- Deformity Of the Thumb\n- flexion of the MCP joint\n- hyperextension Of the IP (interphalangeal joint) Boutonniere's deformity\n- there is proximal interphalangeal (PIP) flexion with distal interphalangeal (DIP) hyperextensicm.\n- Rheumatoid nodules\n- Scleritis and/or uveitis\n- Ulnar deviation\n- Investi ations\n- Rheumatoid factor (RF)\n- positive (60% to Of patients) Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibody positive (70% of patients) Radiographs - Erosions start at the margins of the joint, affecting the subchondral first, and progress to cause joint space narrowing.\n- consider\n- Manaqement\n- moderate - severe disease\n- Conventional synthetic disease-modifying anti- rheumatic drug (DMARD)_\n- Consider corticosteroid and NSAIDS Pregnant or planning preqnpncv\n- Corticosteroids are considered the safest option for patients planning pregnancy or who are pregnant, although sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine can also be used.\n- to reach 'ow disease activitv 3 months Of therapy: not pregnant or planning\n\n- Rheumatoid factor (RF) — Negative\n- CTorMRl Manaqement Offer a topical non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) If topical ineffective or unsuitable. consider oral NSAID\n- Offer a gastroprotective treatment ppl for people with osteoarthritis while they are taking an NSAID. Offer paracetamol Or weak opioids unless: They are only used infrequently for short-term pain relief\n- Consider referral to phyisotherapist or occupational therapist.\n- Consider referral to orthopaedic surgeon - treatment usually involves joint replacement or fusion. Advise on daily exercise\n- Methotrexate and disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drug (DMARD)\n- Consider NSAIDs and corticosteroids\n- Triple DMARD therapy\n- Triple therapy With synthetic DMARDs (e.g., methotrexate plus hydroxychbroquine plus sulfasalazine) may be a second-line option in select patients who fail to reach low disease activity after 3 months.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 31 year old male patient attends the ED with a rash and malaise. she attended five days ago with a sore throat and was prescribed co-amoxiclav and ibuprofen. She is concerned that she is having an allergic reaction to one of these medications. What features would be most indicative of a life-threatening drug reaction?\n- **A)** Itchy urticarial rash, lymphadenopathy and arthralgia\n- **B)** pruritic rnorbilliform rash covering 50% body surface area\n- **C)** Desquamation of palms and feet with associated fever\n- **D)** Skin inflammation worsens after each dose of the medication\n- **E)** Painful generalised rash, ulceration Of oral mucosa and fever Explanation\n\n- Patient has developed non - immediate cutaneous reaction and the severe form is SJS and TEN\n- prug allergy_(NICE guideline) Signs and allergic patterns of suspected drug Allergy with onset\n- Table — A Immediate, rapidly evolving reactions\n\nSigns and allergic patterns of suspected drug allergy a severe multi-system reaction characterised by: erythema, urticaria or angioedema and hypotension and/or bronchospasm\n- Urticaria or angioedema Without systemic features example, with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs [NSAIDS]) Timing of onset\n- Onset usually less than 1 hour after drug exposure (previous exposure not always confirmed)\n- Onset usually than I hour after drug exposure (previous exposure not always confirmed) -l\n- Onset usually less than 1 hour after drug (previous exposure not always confirmed) Table —\n- **B)** Non-immediate reactions with systemic involvement Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS) or drug hypersensitivity syndrome (DHS)\n- wk}espread red macules, papules or erythroderma\n- fever\n- lymphadenopathy\n- liver dysfunction eosinophilia\n- Toxic epidermal necrolysis or Stevens—Johnson syndrome characterised\n- painful rash and fever (Often early signs)\n- mucosa or cutaneous erosions\n- vesicles. bistering or epidermal detachment red purpuric macules or erythema multiforme Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP) widespread pustules\n- fever neutrophilia\n- Common disorders caused, rarely, by drug allergy: eczema\n- hepatitis\n- nephritis\n- photosensitivity\n- vasculitis\n- Management\n- Onset usually 2 to 6 weeks after first drug exposure Or within 3 days of second exposure\n- Onset usually 7 to 14 days after first drug exposure or within 3 days of second exposure\n- Onset usually 3 to 5 days after firstdrug exposure\n- Time of onset variable\n- Consider stopping the drug suspected to have caused the allergic reaction and advising the person to avoid that drug in future\n- Follow anaphylactic protocol if patient has developed anaphylaxis\n- Treat the symptoms of the acute reaction if needed or Refer people to a specialist drug allergy service if they have had a severe non-immediate\n\ncutaneous reaction (for example, drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms, Stevens—Johnson Syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis). Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN). SJS is a severe skin detachment with mucocutaneous complications. It is an immune reaction to foreign antigens. SJS is a less severe manifestation of TEN.\n- Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is an acute-onset, potentially life- threatening, idiosyncratic mucocutaneous reaction, usually occurring after commencement of a new medication.\n- Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) are severe skin detachments with mucocutaneous complications associated with a history of medication use, most commonly anticonvulsants, antibiotics, and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.\n- Classification is dependent on the percentage of skin involvement: SJS has total body surface area (TBSA) involvement and TEN has >30% TBSA involvement.\n- Risk_factors\n- Anticonvulsant medicines - The most common anticonvulsants precipitating SJS and TEN are carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, lamotrigine, and valproic acid.\n- Recent antibiotic use - The greatest risk is posed by the use of trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole and other sulfonamide antibiotics, aminopenicillins, quinolones, and cephalosporins.\n- Other rnedicine - that may put the patient at risk are: antifungals, antivirals (e.g., telaprevir, aciclovir), antiretrovirals (e.g., nevirapine, abacavir), anthelmintics, analgesics (e.g., paracetamol), non-steroidal\n\nanti-inflammatory drugs, selective COX-2 inhibitors, antimalarials, corticosteroids, azathioprine.\n- SLE\n- HIV-positive\n- Small pox vaccination\n- Radiotherapy\n- Clinical features . F eve r\n- Rash - A sudden rash or a rash that develops after a new medicine is sta rted\n- Mucosal involvement - Erosions or ulceration of the eyes, lips, mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, gastrointestinal tract, kidneys, liver, anus, genital area, or urethra. Nikolsky's sign - Epidermal layer easily sloughs off when pressure is applied to the affected area\n- Blisters\n- Investigations\n- FBC, ESR, C-reactive protein, LFTs, Blood culture\n- Coagulation screen\n- Serum glucose\n- Skin biopsy\n- Management\n- The causative agent should be identified and withdrawn immediately.\n- Analgesia\n- Conservative or surgical wound care\n\n- Fluid and electrolyte correction\n- Consider venous thromboembolism prophylaxis - immobile patients should receive prophylactic low molecular weight heparin (e.g., enoxaparin)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 4-day-old term baby is brought to the ED by his mother. She reports that her baby is struggling with feeding as he becomes very breathless and occasionally appears change in color. His examination revealed continuous cardiac murmur.\n- **A)** ASD\n- **B)** PDA\n- **C)** TOF\n- **D)** Tricuspid atresia\n- **E)** TGA Explanation\n- The breathless baby with symptoms of feeding difficulty sweating/tachypnoea with feeds, failure to thrive, respiratory distress and continuous cardiac murmur suggests PDA.\n- The baby is occasionally cyanotic in the scenario. However baby always cyanotic is patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) dependent lesions i -e TAPVD, Truncus arteriosus, TOA, TOF, Tricuspid atresia which are\n\npresents at birth. the baby survival dependent on the patency of the ductus arteriosus (PDA).\n- RCEM do pick some question indirectly for-example they give you a scenario of baby with cyanotic congenital lesion and give you an option (A) PDA dependent lesion (B) VSD (C) ASD. the correct would be PDA dependent lesion. It's very common to get confuse with PDA dependent lesion and PDA itself.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 59-year-old male is brought to the resuscitation cubicle by paramedics with a few hours' history of chest pain. On arrival, his blood pressure is recorded at  mmHg. A 12-lead ECG is obtained, as shown below. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for his hypotension?\n- **A)** Loss of right ventricular contractility\n- **B)** Aortic dissection\n- **C)** Loss of left ventricular function\n- **D)** Dressler's syndrome\n- **E)** Cardiac tamponade\n- Explanation\n- ECG suggests Inferior STEMI as there is:\n- ST elevation in II, Ill and aVF.\n\n- Q-wave formation in Ill and aVF.\n- Reciprocal ST depression and T wave inversion in aVL\n- RV infarct: RV-related shock should be considered whenever hypotension occurs in inferior MI. RV infarct occurs in 30% of inferior MIS, mainly with proximal RCA occlusion. Only one-half of RV infarcts produce clinical RV failure.\n- please note, check more detail in STEMI complications\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 17 years old female presented to the ED with complain of headache, fever and neck stiffness for the last three days. Her observations are:  mmHg 110 beats per minute Temp: 38.5C On examination, she has non blanching skin rash that appears on the lower limb. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Meningococcal Septicemia\n- **B)** ITP\n- **C)** Henoch-Schönlein purpura\n- **D)** Viral encephalitis\n- **E)** Rheumatic fever\n\nExplanation\n\nBacterial meningitis and meningococcal disease Meningitis is inflammation of the two inner meninges (the pia and arachnoid mater) of the brain and spinal cord. Bacterial meningitis is a life-threatening condition that affects all ages, but is most common in babies and children. Meningococcal disease is infection with Neisseria meningitidis. It can result in meningococcal meningitis (15% of cases) or meningococcal septicaemia (25% of cases). pneumococcal disease is infection With Streptococcus pneumoniae (also called pneumococcal). 1\n- Causes\n- Overall in the UK, Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcus), Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus), and Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) are the most common causative organisms of acute bacterial meningitis in children aged 3 months or older and adults.\n- In neonates (younger than I month of age), Streptococcus Escherichia coli, S pneumoniae, and monocytogenes are the most common causative organisms.\n- In adults, S. pneumoniae is the m Ost Cause Of bacterial meningitis. Clinical features Common\n- Fever, Vomitingfnausea,Lethargv, Irritability/unsettled behaviour, III appearance, Headache.\n- Non-blanching rash - A rapidly evolving petechial Or purpuric rash is a sign of very severe disease. Stiff neck Capillary refill time of more than 2 seconds, cold hands and feet, Unusual skin colour. Shock and hypotension, Leg pain. Back rigidity, Bulging fontanelle, Photophobia. Kernig's sign\n- (person unable to fully extend at the knee when hip is flexed). Brudzinski's sign\n- (person's knees and hips flex when neck is flexed).\n- Unconsciousness or toxic/moribund State.\n- deficit cranial nerve involvement and abnormal pupils,\n- Investigations\n- Blood culture\n- Take blood for culture Within I hour Of arrival at hospital and ideally prior to giving antibiotics Ser_pneurnococcal and PCR\n- FBC and differential, LFTS, u blood glucose CSF Gram stain, Culture. and sensitivities - DO a lumbar (up; unless contraindicated) Within hour Of arrival at hospital. CSF PCR for tuberculosis Neuroimaeirw (CT or MRn - Neuroimaging is not indicated unless you suspect alternative pathology because there are signs indicating risk of cerebral herniation ( secondary to raised intracranial pressure), Encephalitis\n- Encephalitis is defined as inflammation of the brain parenchyma associated with neurological dysfunction, such as altered state of consciousness, seizures, personality changes, cranial nerve palsies, speech problems, and motor and sensory deficits.\n- It is the result of direct inflammation of the brain tissue, as opposed to the inflammation of the meninges (meningitis)\n- Risk factors Age CI or 365 years Immunodeficiency Blood/body fluid exposure\n- HIV and West Nile virus can be tra nsmitted by contaminated blood products. Organ transplantation\n- Rabies, West Nile virus, and cytomegalovirus infections have occurred in transplant patients. Naegleria infect iO n\n- Clinical features\n- Fever , rash\n- Altered mental status\n- focal neurological deficit\n- These include aphasia, hemianopia, hemiparesis, ataxia, brisk tendon reflexes, Babinski's sign, cranial nerve deficits\n- Meningismus\n- Some patients have evidence of meningeal inflammation with headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness.\n- Seizures- Generalised tonic-donicseizures and focal Myo/perica rditis\n- Arthritis\n- Autonomic and hypothalamic disturbances - LOSS Of temperature and vasomotor control\n- Jaundice\n- Invest\n- tions FBC, Blood culture, LFTs, LJ&E Throat swab, Nasopharyngeal aspirate CSF analysis, culture, serology, PCR. CT or MRI Brain biopsy\n\nRefer all patients with suspected meningitis and/or meningococcal sepsis to hospital immediately. The British Infection Association recommends:\n- Benzylpen icillin (intramuscular or intravenous) OR\n- A third\n- generation cephalosporin such as cefotaxime or ceftriaxone (intramuscular or intravenous).\n- DO transfer to hospital by attempting to give parenteral Supportive measure corticosteroid - Give empirical intravenous dexamethasone (10mg IV 6 hourly) to all adults with acute bacterial meningitis within I hour of presentation to hospital. Antibiotics British Infection Association recommends:\n- IV ceftriaxone: 2 g intravenously every 12 hours\n- IV cefotaxirne: 2 g mtravenouswevery 6 hours\n- If patient allergic penicillin or cephalosporin : Chloramphenicd: 2S mg/kg every 6 hours\n- Consider 15-20 or rifampicin; 600 mg I V to Cover for pneumococci\n- Consider 2 g Or trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole: 10-20 rng/kg/day to cover cover for Listeria monocytogenes (neonates or immunocompromised patients). Supportive measure acyclovir\n- Combination antiviral therapy - ganciclovir: 5 mg/kg intravenously every 12 hours for 14\n- 21 days\n- foscarnet: 60 mg/kg intravenously every 8 hours; or 90 mgJkg intravenously every 12 hours for 14-21 days\n- aciclovir: 10 mg/kg intravenously every 8 hours for 21 days\n- Confirmed HSV — a Confirmed VZV - aciclovir or ganciclovir Confirmed CMV - ganciclovir plus foscarnet Confirmed EBV - aciclOvir, ganciclovir, or Cidofovir Corticosteroid\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 23 year old drug abuser brought into the ED after starting to act strangely in a festival. He has no significant PMH. His image is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Right oculomotor nerve palsy\n- **B)** Left optic nerve\n- **C)** Right abducent nerve palsy\n- **D)** Left oculomotor nerve palsy\n- **E)** Ophthalmic division Of trigeminal nerve palsy Explanation\n- Image shows 3rd nerve palsy: Right eye turns downward and outwards Looking Right Normal CN Ill Palsy Small angle of horizontal squint CN IV Palsy 12 CN VI Palsy NO Obvious squint Unable to abduct right eye, larger angle of squint, double vün further aoart Looking Forward Right eye tums downward\n- outwards Right eye tums upward\n- outwards Righteye turn medially Looking Left to adduct right eve, large angle squint, double vision further apart Right eye elevates more as it moves medially. Double vision further apart Able to adduct right eve, NO\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 65-year-old male patient with a history of type-2 diabetes mellitus presents to the emergency department with intractable vomiting. His past medical history includes gastroparesis and hypertension. On exam, he is afebrile with a blood pressure of  mmHg and a heart rate of 104 beats per minute. Mild epigastric tenderness is noted on the abdominal exam. The remainder of the exam is\n\nunremarkable. Laboratory data show a white blood cell count of 5000/microL. Due to diffcult peripheral access, a femoral central venous catheter (CVC) is inserted. 48 hours later, the patient develops a high-grade fever. Urinalysis and chest radiograph performed are not remarkable for significant pathological findings. A set Of blood cultures obtained both from the femoral catheter and peripheral vein reveal the growth of Staphylococcus aureus at 72 hours. What is the treatment for this patient's infection?\n- **A)** Parenteral vancomycin, and catheter removal\n- **B)** Cefepime, and catheter removal\n- **C)** Daptomycin and repeat the blood culture within 7 to 10 days\n- **D)** Blood culture from peripheral vein\n- **E)** Carbapenem with or without an aminoglycoside Explanation\n- Management of CRBSI\n- Obtain blood cultures before antibiotics.\n- administration of antimicrobial therapy (vancomycin and ceftazidime), and catheter removal or antibiotic lock therapy.\n- Indication for catheter removal\n- Indications for removal of a CVC from hemodialysis access include persistent symptoms for greater than 36 hours or severe sepsis, hemodynamic instability (shock), metastatic infection, and blood\n\ncultures that remain positive for greater than 72 hours of appropriate antimicrobial therapy.\n- Other indications for catheter removal include, difficult to clear organisms (S. aureus, Pseudomonas, or fungi), recurrence of uncomplicated CRBSI (any non-virulent organism) when salvaged for difficult access.\n- Note : it's a practice question not a previous exam question\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 41 year old male presented to the ED with one side facial numbness and sore right eye that started 24hrs ago. He denies a history of these symptoms before this episode. His PMH is unremarkable. What is the most appropriate next step in management for this patient?\n- **A)** CT brain\n- **B)** Aciclovir and antibiotics\n- **C)** Refer to neurologist\n- **D)** Prednisolone SOmg\n- **E)** IV fluids Explanation\n- Patient unilateral facial weakness and excessive tearing/sore right eye suggests Bell's palsy\n\n- Bell's palsy.\n- Bell's palsy is an acute, unilateral facial nerve weakness or paralysis of rapid onset (less than 72 hours) and unknown cause.\n- Weakness and paralysis may be due to inflammation and oedema of the facial nerve causing nerve compression and damage during its passage through the temporal bone\n- The cause of Bell's palsy is unknown\n- Herpes simplex virus, varicella zoster virus, and autoimmunity may contribute to the development of Bell's palsy\n- Clinical features\n- Rapid onset (less than 72 hours).\n- Facial muscle weakness (almost always unilateral) involving the upper and lower parts of the face. This causes a reduction in movement on the affected side, often with drooping of the eyebrow and corner of the mouth and loss of the nasolabial fold.\n- Ear and postauricular region pain on the affected side (in around half of people with Bell's palsy).\n- Difficulty chewing, dry mouth (in 20%), and changes in taste (in around 35%).\n- Incomplete eye closure, dry eye (in 30%), eye pain, or excessive tearing.\n- Numbness or tingling of the cheek and/or mouth.\n- Speech articulation problems, drooling.\n- Hyperacusis (in less than 5%).\n\n- Note - Sparing of forehead movement (which may indicate an upper motor neuronelesion such as stroke) and bilateral signs (may be indicative of Lyme disease or sarcoidosis). Lower motor neurone lesions (such as Bell's palsy) do not spare the upper face.\n- Inyestigations\n- Diagnosis is clinical, based on history and physical examination alone.\n- Consider electroneuronography (ENoG) (evoked electromyography - eEMG) - Indicated in acute cases where physical examination demonstrates near-complete or complete facial paralysis.\n- Consider serology for Borrelia burgdorferi - Indicated in all patients with recent travel to a Lyme disease-endemic area.\n- Management\n- Reassure the person that the prognosis is good. Most people with Bell's palsy make a full recovery within 3—4 months\n- Corticosteroid - Giving 50 mg daily for IO days or Giving 60 mg daily for five days followed by a daily reduction in dose of IO mg (for a total treatrnent time of 10 days) if a reducing dose is preferred.\n- Eye protection - It is important to keep the affected eye lubricated. Lubricating eye drops should be used frequently during the day and eye ointment used at night. 10 / 10 A 27-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of painful left eye. He was playing squash when the ball hit him directly in the left eye at some force. On examination, his face is bruised and swollen and he experienced double vision when he looks upwards. His X-ray has been performed which is shown.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\n- **A)** Zygomaticomaxillary Complex fracture\n- **B)** Zygomatic arch fractures\n- **C)** Le fort fractures II\n- **D)** Orbital blow out fracture\n- **E)** Mandibular fracture Explanation\n- X-ray will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema. It's the classical sign of orbital blow out fracture.\n- Orbital blow out fracture\n- MOE Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly from a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a fracture Of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents into the maxillary sinus.\n- Clinical features: Pt may have diplopia and can't look up and medially due to inferior rectus entrapment.\n- Test infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital nerve commonly damage in this injury.\n- will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema.\n- Management: Urgently Refer all Pt with orbital blow-out fracture (e.g. diplopia or reduced eye movements) to a rnaxillofacial surgeon and/or an ophthalmologist\n\n- facial CT scan will be needed to visualise the fracture in detail and plan surgical repair. previOUS  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	31	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
5c7ecddd-b177-428b-bcaf-cff8d1afe248	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-32	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 32	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 32\n\n- **B)** 3 mm\n- **C)** 3.5 mm\n- **D)** 4.5 mm\n- **E)** 5 mm Explanation\n- Advanced life support grouæ(ALSC) endotracheal tube recommendations:\n- Internal diameter (mm) = (Age/4) + 4\n- Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 12 for an oral tube\n- Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 15 for nasal tube\n- Answer -2/4+4 = 4.5mm\n- Note—Neonates usually require a tube of internal diameter 3—3.5 mm, although pre-term infants may need one of diameter 2.5 mm.\n- Cuffed tubes should not be used in neonates\n- Uncuffed sizes are 2.5 mm for a premature newborn kg, 3.0 mm for infants 1—3.5 kg, 3.5 mm for infants >3.5 kg and up to age of six months, size 4 mm for infants seven months to one year.\n\n- The approximate size for children over one year by the formula: size (mm) = age (years)/4 + 4.\n- As long as they don't mention cuffed tube use uncuffed tube formula size (mm) = age (years)/4 + 4. if they mention cuffed tube diameter then use cuffed tube formula: size (mm) = age (years)/4 + 3.5\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 6-year-old boy presents with a serum potassium level of 7.2 mmol/L. He is asymptomatic, and his ECG shows peaked T waves. Pseudohyperkalemia has been ruled out, and no obvious source of potassium release (e.g., hemolysis, rhabdomyolysis) is identified. His renal function is normal, but his potassium intake has been high due to recent dietary changes. What is the most appropriate next step in management?\n- **A)** Dialysis\n- **B)** Nebulized salbutamol\n- **C)** Calcium Resonium\n- **D)** Intravenous calcium gluconate\n- **E)** Intravenous insulin with glucose\n- Explanation\n\n- Elevated potassium (7.2mmol/L) with ECG changes (peaked T waves), indicating that urgent intervention is required to stabilize the cardiac membrane, even if the child is asymptomatic.\n- Hyperkalaemia in children\n- Causes-of-Hyperkalaemia\n- Renal failure Drugs (e.g. K supplements, ACE inhibitors, ß-blockers, suxamethonium, DKA trimethoprim, diuretics)\n- Adrenal insufficiency (e.g. Addison's disease, CAH)\n- Pseudohyperkalaemia (esp. from haemolysis) Renal failure Drugs (e.g. K supplements, ACE inhibitors, ß-blockers, suxamethonium, DKA trimethoprim, diuretics)\n- Cell lysis (tumour lysis syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, severe burns, trauma)\n- Clinical feature\n- ECG changes - simultaneous/progressive: T waves >5mm tall; long PR; flatter/absent P waves, widened QRS, bradycardia/ ventricular fibrillation (VF).\n- Other: muscle weakness; ileus\n\nSevere ECG changes / symptomatic K+ >7.6mm01/L > 1 month to < 14vrs >7.1mmol/L >7.1mmol/L Give IV calcium (stabilises the cardiac membrane). Neonate\n- Calcium Gluconate 0.5ml/kg over 10 minutes Give centrally whenever possible. If no central access available, dilute x 5 volume with Sodium Chloride 0.9%.\n- month to <14vrs\n- Calcium Gluconate 0.5mI/kg over 10 minutes (max 20ml) diluted to 50ml with Sodium Chloride 0.9% Calcium Gluconate 20ml over IO minutes\n- Nebulised Salbutamol (moves K into cells) Neonate\n- 2.5mg diluted to 2ml with Sodium Chloride 0.9%\n- month to <14vears\n- 2.5mg for < 10kg 5mg for 2 1 Okg\n- 21410mg :\n- 10mg\n- Give Insulin/Glucose (moves K * into cells) Monitor blood glucose every 30 mins for 6 hours. Neonate. month to <14vears Add O.lunits/kg soluble insulin (actrapid) to lg/kg Of glucose. Give over 10 minutes.\n- 214 years: Add IO units of soluble Insulin (actrapid) to 50ml Glucose or 125ml glucose -Give over 5-10 minutes. 1 Moderate No ECG changes / asymptomatic K+7.1-7.5mm01/L 6.1-7mm01/L 6.1-7mmol/L Management\n- Nebulised Salbutamol (moves + into cells) Neonate\n- 2.5mg diluted to 2ml With Sodium Chloride 0.9%\n- month to <14vears\n- 2.5mg for < 10kg 5mg for? 10kg\n- 21410mg:\n- 10mg Give Insulin/Glucose (moves K + into cells) Monitor blood glucose every 30 mins for 6 hours. Neonate, month to <14vears Add O. lunits/kg soluble insulin (actrapid) to lg/kg of glucose. Give over IO minutes. 214 years: Add 10 units of soluble Insulin (actrapid) to 50ml Glucose 50% or 125m' glucose .Give over 5- IO minutes.\n- Sodium bicarbonate used to counteract serum potassium leak from cells in acidosis (blood pH < 7.34)\n- Onset Of action IO - 30 minutes, duration up to 2 hours.\n- Dose: Intravenous: 1-2 mmol/kg over 30 minutes (1-2 mL/kg of 8.4% or 2-4 ml/kg of 4.2%) Maximum 50 mL Increases K* elimination IV Furosemide Neonate\n- Img/kg/dose over 5 mins Mild No ECG changes / asymptomatic 5.5-6mm01/L 5.5-6mmoVL\n- Nebulised Salbutamol (moves K + into cells) Neonate\n- 2.5mg diluted to 2ml With Sodium Chloride 0.9% month to <14vears\n- 2.5mg for < 10kg 5mg for 10kg\n- 21410mg:\n- Increases elimination\n- 'V Furosemide\n- Neonate- Img/kg/dose over 5 mins\n- month to <14years - I mg/kg/dose Imax 20 mg) over 5 mins\n- 214 years - 20mg over 5 mins Calcium Resonium\n- Neonate\n- Rectally 125- 250mg/kg/dose four times a day month to <14years - Oral/rectally 125-250mg/kg/dose four times day\n- 214 years - Oral/rectally 15 grams four times a day\n\nSodium bicarbonate Used to Counteract serum potassium leak from cells in acidosis (blood pH < 7.34)\n- Onset Of action IO - 30 minutes, duration up to 2 hours. Dose: Intravenous: 1-2 mmol/kg over 30 minutes (1-2 mL/kg of or 2-4 ml/kg of 4.2%) Maximum 50 mL Increases K+ elimination IV Furosemide\n- Neonate - Img/kg/dose over 5 mins\n- month to <14years - Img/kg/dose (max 20 mg) over 5 mins 214 years - 20mg over 5 mins Calcium Resonium Neonate\n- Rectally 125- 250mg/kg/dose four times a day\n- month to <14years - Ora l/rectally 125-250mg/kg/dose four times day\n- 214 years - Oral/rectally 15 grams four times a day (max 20 mg) over 5 mins 214 years - 20mg over 5 mins\n- Calcium Resonium\n- Neonate\n- Rectally 125- 250mg/kg/dose four times a day\n- month to <14years - Oral 'rectally 125-250mg/kg/dose four times day\n- 214 years - Oral/rectally 15 grams four times a day\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with epistaxis. His PMH is atrial fibrillation for which he is on warfarin. His INR is\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nWhat is your step of management in this patient?   A Give vitamin K IV\n- **B)** Protamine sulphate\n- **C)** Vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate\n- **D)** Hold the dose of warfarin\n- **E)** Prothrombin complex concentrate Explanation\n- The patient has minor bleeding, therefore, stop the warfarin and give vitamin-k IV INR > 8.0 5.0 - 8.0 No bleeding\n- Stop warfarin\n- Vitamin-K PO\n- Stop warfarin Minor bleeding\n- Stop warfarin\n- Vitamin-K IV\n- Stop warfarin\n- Vitamin-K IV Major bleeding\n- Stop warfarin\n- Dried prothrombin complex concentrate or FFP if PCC unavailable\n- Vitamin-K IV Key_points\n- Major bleeding - Macroscopic hematuria, gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal, cranial, and intra-abdominal bleeding, bleeding requiring hospitalization or an invasive procedure, bleeding causing a 2-unit decrease in the hemoglobin level, and bleeding requiring > 3\n\nunits of blood product transfusion were considered major bleeding events.\n- Minor bleeding - skin hematoma > 25 cm2, spontaneous nosebleed of > 5-min duration, gingival bleeding for > 5 min, any bleeding leading to hospitalization, any bleeding leading to transfusion < 3 units of blood product, or any other bleeding considered to be minor bleeding.\n- Any bleeding requires vitamin-K (phytomenadione) IV not oral.\n- Repeat the vitamin K dose after 24hrs if INR is still too high.\n- PCC and Vitamin K both should be given in major bleeding\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 10-year-old child presented to the ED with a complaint of being unable to weight bearing. He has no history of trauma or injury. His x-ray is given. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis of this patient?\n- **A)** Ewing sarcoma\n- **B)** Fibrous cortical defect\n- **C)** Osteosarcoma\n- **D)** Giant cell tumor\n- **E)** Osteoma lacia Explanation\n- Radiograph shows an eccentric osteolytic metaphyseal lesion in the proximal tibial (17 mm) with sclerotic margins.\n- Fibrous cortical defect\n- Fibrous cortical defects (FCD)are benign bone lesions histologically identical to non-ossifying fibromas (are benign and generally self- limiting osteoclastic giant cell-rich bone tumors).\n- Fibrous cortical defects typically occur in children (usually 2-15 years), and are one of the most common benign bone lesions, which combined with non-ossifying fibromas are seen in up to 40% of skeletally immature children/adolescents.\n- They typically occur in the metaphysis or diametaphyseal junction and appear as small (<2-3 cm) lucent defects within the cortex that over time become sclerotic as they heal. They are typically located in the distal femur or proximal or distal tibia. They are much less frequently seen in the upper limb\n- They are asymptomatic and self-limiting, completely healing by adulthood.\n- Plain radiograph and CT\n- lucent intracortical defects\n\n- outlined by a thin rim of sclerosis\n- no involvement of the underlying medullary cavity\n- no periosteal reaction\n- Management\n- As these lesions are benign, characteristic in appearance and self- limiting, no treatment, biopsy or follow-up is required in typical cases. If associated with pathological fracture (more common in non-ossifying fibromas) then cast immobilization until the fracture has healed, followed by biopsy with or without curettage, and bone grafting may be necessary.\n- Example - 1\n\n- Well-marginated unilocular lucencies with sharp sclerotic borders are seen in the distal meta-diaphyseal cortex of the tibia and fibula.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 56-year-old male brought to the ED with a complaint of intermittent syncope. His ECG is given What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Trifascicular heart block\n- **B)** Sinus bradycardia\n- **C)** Complete heart block\n- **D)** first-degree heart block\n- **E)** Mobitz type-I block Explanation\n- ECC shows: Incomplete tri fascicular heart block:\n-\n- Right bundle branch block Left axis deviation (z left anterior fascicular block)\n- First-degree AV block\n-\n\n-\n-\n-\n- Note: The answer depends on ECC interpretation, therefore be familiar with relevant ECGs. For more detail check the cardiology module.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 4-year-old child collapsed paramedics informed you arrival at ED. Which of the following Is the correct ETT tube diameter for this patient?\n- **A)** 6 mm\n- **B)** 4.5 mm\n- **C)** 5.5 mm   D.5mm\n- **E)** 3.5 mm Explanation Advanced life support grouæ(ALSG) endotracheal tube recommendations\n- Internal diameter (mm) = (Age/4) + 4 Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 12 for an oral tube Length (cm) = (Age/2) + 15 for nasal tube\n\n- Answer:  + 4 = 5mm\n- Note-ZNeonates usually require a tube of internal diameter 3—3.5 mm, although pre-term infants may need one of diameter 2.5 mm.\n- Cuffed tubes should not be used in neonates\n- Uncuffed sizes are 2.5 mm for a premature newborn kg, 3.0 mm for infants 1—3.5 kg, 3.5 mm for infants >3.5 kg and up to age of six months, size 4 mm for infants seven months to one year. The approximate size for children over one year by the formula: size (mm) = age (years)/4 + 4.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 40-year-old male presented to the ED with a complaint of rash which you suspect is Lyme disease. Which of the following rash is frequently seen in Lyme disease?\n- **A)** Erythema multiform   B\n- Erythema migrans\n- **C)** Bullous pemphigus\n- **D)** Erythema nodosum\n- **E)** Erythema marginetum\n\nExplanation\n- Rasherythema migrans - Round or oval in shape; pink, red, There is often a central clearing or a target-like appearance Lyme disease Lyme disease, or Lyme borreliosis, is an infection caused by a group of\n- spiral-shaped bacteria (spirochetes) termed Borrelia burgdorferis sensu lato (sl). which are transmitted to humans following a bite from an infected tick. Transmission - Borreliae are usually transmitted to humans by nymph\n- or adult ticks. Nymphs are the most likely to transmit infection: they can remain very small even after a feed (about the size of a poppy seed), and so are likely to be missed and to remain attached to the skin for longer. Transmission of Borrelia burgdorferi from infected nymphal ticks\n- generally occurs after 36—48 hours of attachment\n- Clinical features Rash erythema migrans - Round or oval in shape; pink, red, There is\n- often a central clearing or a target-like appearance Cognitive impairment, such as memory problems and difficulty\n- concentrating. Fatigue.\n- Fever and sweats.\n-\n\nHeadache.\n- Malaise.\n- Migratory joint or muscle aches and pain. Neck pain or stiffness.\n- Paraesthesia\n- Swollen glands\n-\n- Diagnosis Make a clinical diagnosis of Lyme disease in people with erythema\n- migrans.\n- If Lyme disease is suspected in people without erythema migrans, offer an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) test for Lyme disease.\n- If the ELISA test (in people without erythema migrans) is: Positive or equivocal —perform an immunoblot test for Lyme disease.\n- Consider starting treatment with antibiotics while waiting for the\n- results Negative: and the person still has symptoms — review their history and\n- symptoms, and consider an alternative diagnosis.\n- If the immunoblot test is: Positive— diagnose Lyme disease. Start treatment with oral antibiotics.\n- Negative, but symptoms persist — consider a discussion with or\n- referral to a specialist (regardless of the ELISA result) to review whether\n\nfurther tests are required or to consider alternative diagnoses.\n- Negative, and symptoms have resolved — no treatrnent is required.\n- Management Adults and children aged 12 years or older Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily (or 200 mg once daily) for 21 days. Amoxicillin 1000 mg three times daily for 21 days (if doxycycline is not suitable).\n- Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 17 days (if doxycycline and amoxicillin are not suitable) Children aged 9—12 years Doxycycline — 5 mg/kg in two divided doses on day one, followed by 2.5 mg/kg daily in one or two divided doses for a total of 21 days.\n- Amoxicillin — 30 mg/kg three times daily for 21 days, for children 33 kg and under (if doxycycline is not suitable).\n- Azithromycin — IO mg/kg daily for 17 days, for children 50 kg and under (if doxycycline and amoxicillin are not suitable). Note: Doxycycline — best avoided below age 9.\n- Note: a Jarisch-Herxheimerreaction may develop (in up to 15% of people) in the first 24 hours of treatment with any antibiotic for Lyme disease. This is a systemic reaction thought to be caused by the release of cytokines when antibiotics kill large numbers of bacteria.\n- The reaction can start between 1—12 hours after antibiotics are started, but can also occur later and can last for a few hours or 1—2 days. The reaction is self-limiting and usually resolves within 24—48 hours.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 34-year-old presents to the ED with a complaint of a rash on his back. Who has recently traveled to Turkey. He recalled he has a tick bite a few days back. What would suggest Lyme disease rash?\n- **A)** A rash that turns into itchy, fluid-filled blisters and then scabs\n- **B)** Circular red rash with central clearing\n- **C)** Itchy raised rash with blisters\n- **D)** Rash that involve skin and mucosa membrane\n- **E)** Rash of spots with dark centers and pale red rings Explanation\n- Rash erythema migrans - Round or oval in shape; pink, red, There is often a central clearing or a target-like appearance. Lyme disease\n\nLyme disease, or Lyme borreliosis, is an infection caused by a group of\n- spiral-shaped bacteria (spirochetes) termed Borrelia burgdorferis sensu lato (sl). which are transmitted to humans following a bite from an infected tick. Transmission - Borreliae are usually transmitted to humans by nymph\n- or adult ticks. Nymphs are the most likely to transmit infection: they can remain very small even after a feed (about the size of a poppy seed), and so are likely to be missed and to remain attached to the skin for longer. Transmission of Borrelia burgdorferi from infected nymphal ticks\n- generally occurs after 36—48 hours of attachment Clinical features\n-\n- Rasherythema migrans - Round or oval in shape; pink, red, There is often a central clearing or a target-like appearance Cognitive impairment, such as memory problems and difficulty\n- concentrating. FatigUe. Fever and sweats.\n- Headache.\n- Migratory joint or muscle aches and pain.\n- Neck pain or stiffness.\n- Swollen glands\n-\n\n- Diagnosis\n- Make a clinical diagnosis of Lyme disease in people with erythema migrans.\n- If Lyme disease is suspected in people without erythema migrans, offer an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) test for Lyme disease.\n- If the ELISA test (in people without erythema migrans) is:\n- Positive or equivocal —perform an immunoblot test for Lyme disease.\n- Consider starting treatment with antibiotics while waiting for the results\n- Negative: and the person still has symptoms — review their history and symptoms, and consider an alternative diagnosis.\n- If the immunoblot test is:\n- Positive— diagnose Lyme disease. Start treatrnent with oral antibiotics.\n- Negative, but symptoms persist — consider a discussion with or referral to a specialist (regardless of the ELISA result) to review whether further tests are required or to consider alternative diagnoses.\n- Negative, and symptoms have resolved — no treatment is required.\n- Management\n\nAdults and children aged 12 years or older\n- Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily (or 200 mg once daily) for 21 days.\n- Amoxicillin 1000 mg three times daily for 21 days (if doxycycline is not suitable).\n- Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 17 days (if doxycycline and amoxicillin are not suitable) Children aged 9—12 years Doxycycline — 5 mg/kg in two divided doses on day one, followed by 2.5 mg/kg daily in one or two divided doses for a total of 21 days.\n- Amoxicillin — 30 mg/kg three times daily for 21 days, for children 33 kg and under (if doxycycline is not suitable).\n- Azithromycin — 10 mg/kg daily for 17 days, for children 50 kg and under (if doxycycline and amoxicillin are not suitable).\n- Note: Doxycycline — best avoided below age 9.\n- Note: a Jarisch-Herxheimerreaction may develop (in up to 15% of people) in the first 24 hours of treatrnent with any antibiotic for Lyme disease. This is a systemic reaction thought to be caused by the release of cytokines when antibiotics kill large numbers of bacteria.\n- The reaction can start between 1—12 hours after antibiotics are started, but can also occur later and can last for a few hours or 1—2 days.\n- The reaction is self-limiting and usually resolves within 24—48 hours.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 46-year-old female with end-stage colorectal cancer presents to the ED with near syncope. She was brought to the ER by her husband where she was found to be in torsade-de-pointes. Which of the following electrolyte is the most likely the cause of her condition?   A\n- Hypokalemia\n- **B)** Hypocalcemia   C\n- Hyperkalemia   D\n- Hypomagnesemia   E\n- Hyponatremia Explanation\n- Platinum-based chemotherapy with cisplatin (the prototype of platinum agents) and its derivatives, carboplatin and oxaliplatin, is traditionally the first-line cytotoxic treatment of lung, colorectal, ovarian, breast, head/neck, bladder and testicular cancers.\n- Oxaliplatin is the only third-generation platinum derivative routinely used in cancer therapy. It is particularly useful in the treatrnent of colorectal cancer. The acute toxicities of oxaliplatin include nausea,\n\nvomiting, diarrhea, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, peripheral sensory neuropathy, and hypersensitivity reaction, long QT syndrome torsade- de-pointes.\n- Platinum agents are a cornerstone of treatment for approximately half of all cancer patients. Besides the well-publicized myelosuppressive toxicities, platinum agents are also commonly associated with specific electrolyte deficiencies, in particular magnesium, that in turn directly or indirectly interferes with absorption and/or promotes excretion of calcium, potassium, and phosphates cause hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, hyponatremia, and hypophosphatemia. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 5-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department after being referred by his GP for a serum potassium level of 6.8 mmol/L. He has a history of lethargy for the past two days but no other symptoms. On examination, he appears well- hydrated, is alert, and his observations are: Heart rate: 98 bpm Blood pressure:  mmHg Respiratory rate: 22 breaths/min Oxygen saturation: 98% on room air An ECG is performed and shows no abnormalities. Pseudohyperkalemia is ruled out by ensuring proper sample handling. A repeat blood test confirms hyperkalemia. What would be your next step of management?\n\nA Nebulized salbutamol\n- **B)** Intravenous calcium gluconate\n- **C)** Intravenous insulin with glucose\n- **D)** Calcium Resonium\n- **E)** Immediate referral for urgent dialysis\n- Explanation\n- The patient is asymptomatic, and the ECG shows no abnormalities. Therefore, the first-line intervention would be nebulization with salbutamol.\n- Hyperkalaemia in children\n- Causes of Hyperkalaemia\n- Renal failure Drugs (e.g. K supplements, ACE inhibitors, ß-blockers, suxamethonium, DKA trimethoprim, diuretics)\n- Adrenal insufficiency (e.g. Addison's disease, CAH)\n- Pseudohyperkalaemia (esp. from haemolysis) Renal failure Drugs (e.g. K supplements, ACE inhibitors, ß-blockers, suxamethonium, DKA trimethoprim, diuretics)\n- Cell lysis (tumour lysis syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, severe burns, trauma)\n\n- Clinical feature\n- ECC changes - simultaneous/progressive: T waves >5mm tall; long PR; flatter/absent P waves, widened QRS, bradycardia/ ventricular fibrillation (VF).\n- Other: muscle weakness; ileus\n\nSevere ECG changes / symptomatic K+ >7.6mm01/L > 1 month to < 14vrs >7.1mmol/L >7.1mmol/L Give IV calcium (stabilises the cardiac membrane). Neonate\n- Calcium Gluconate 0.5ml/kg over 10 minutes Give centrally whenever possible. If no central access available, dilute x 5 volume with Sodium Chloride 0.9%.\n- month to <14vrs\n- Calcium Gluconate 0.5mI/kg over 10 minutes (max 20ml) diluted to 50ml with Sodium Chloride 0.9% Calcium Gluconate 20ml over IO minutes\n- Nebulised Salbutamol (moves K into cells) Neonate\n- 2.5mg diluted to 2ml with Sodium Chloride 0.9%\n- month to <14vears\n- 2.5mg for < 10kg 5mg for 2 1 Okg\n- 21410mg :\n- 10mg\n- Give Insulin/Glucose (moves K * into cells) Monitor blood glucose every 30 mins for 6 hours. Neonate. month to <14vears Add O.lunits/kg soluble insulin (actrapid) to lg/kg Of glucose. Give over 10 minutes.\n- 214 years: Add IO units of soluble Insulin (actrapid) to 50ml Glucose or 125ml glucose -Give over 5-10 minutes. 1 Moderate No ECG changes / asymptomatic K+7.1-7.5mm01/L 6.1-7mm01/L 6.1-7mmol/L Management\n- Nebulised Salbutamol (moves + into cells) Neonate\n- 2.5mg diluted to 2ml With Sodium Chloride 0.9%\n- month to <14vears\n- 2.5mg for < 10kg 5mg for? 10kg\n- 21410mg:\n- 10mg Give Insulin/Glucose (moves K + into cells) Monitor blood glucose every 30 mins for 6 hours. Neonate, month to <14vears Add O. lunits/kg soluble insulin (actrapid) to lg/kg of glucose. Give over IO minutes. 214 years: Add 10 units of soluble Insulin (actrapid) to 50ml Glucose 50% or 125m' glucose .Give over 5- IO minutes.\n- Sodium bicarbonate used to counteract serum potassium leak from cells in acidosis (blood pH < 7.34)\n- Onset Of action IO - 30 minutes, duration up to 2 hours.\n- Dose: Intravenous: 1-2 mmol/kg over 30 minutes (1-2 mL/kg of 8.4% or 2-4 ml/kg of 4.2%) Maximum 50 mL Increases K* elimination IV Furosemide Neonate\n- Img/kg/dose over 5 mins Mild No ECG changes / asymptomatic 5.5-6mm01/L 5.5-6mmoVL\n- Nebulised Salbutamol (moves K + into cells) Neonate\n- 2.5mg diluted to 2ml With Sodium Chloride 0.9% month to <14vears\n- 2.5mg for < 10kg 5mg for 10kg\n- 21410mg:\n- Increases elimination\n- 'V Furosemide\n- Neonate- Img/kg/dose over 5 mins\n- month to <14years - I mg/kg/dose Imax 20 mg) over 5 mins\n- 214 years - 20mg over 5 mins Calcium Resonium\n- Neonate\n- Rectally 125- 250mg/kg/dose four times a day month to <14years - Oral/rectally 125-250mg/kg/dose four times day\n- 214 years - Oral/rectally 15 grams four times a day\n\nSodium bicarbonate Used to Counteract serum potassium leak from cells in acidosis (blood pH < 7.34)\n- Onset Of action IO - 30 minutes, duration up to 2 hours. Dose: Intravenous: 1-2 mmol/kg over 30 minutes (1-2 mL/kg of or 2-4 ml/kg of 4.2%) Maximum 50 mL Increases K+ elimination IV Furosemide\n- Neonate - Img/kg/dose over 5 mins\n- month to <14years - Img/kg/dose (max 20 mg) over 5 mins 214 years - 20mg over 5 mins Calcium Resonium Neonate\n- Rectally 125- 250mg/kg/dose four times a day\n- month to <14years - Ora l/rectally 125-250mg/kg/dose four times day\n- 214 years - Oral/rectally 15 grams four times a day (max 20 mg) over 5 mins 214 years - 20mg over 5 mins\n- Calcium Resonium\n- Neonate\n- Rectally 125- 250mg/kg/dose four times a day\n- month to <14years - Oral 'rectally 125-250mg/kg/dose four times day\n- 214 years - Oral/rectally 15 grams four times a day\n- Please note, for adult follow hyperkalaemia management in adult in nephrology section.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	32	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
650b0b69-7c0c-4bf8-a087-8c67b5a1e7ad	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-33	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 33	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 33\n\nB\n- Transfuse blood products\n- **C)** Respect mother decision and refer to surgery\n- **D)** Give lv fluid without transfuse blood products\n- **E)** Refer to surgery for definitive treatrnent\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  A blood transfusion should be given because the patient is not competent. The mother can not refuse her daughter's treatment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 32-week unconscious pregnant mother brought to the ED after RTA. Her observations are: sp02: 94%  mmHg 120 bpm 25 breaths per minute You've decided to start a blood transfusion. However, she carries a witnessed written directive refusing blood and all blood products. What would be your next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Transfuse blood and refer to OBS and Gynae team\n- **B)** Transfuse blood products\n- **C)** Refers to OBS and Gynae team\n- **D)** Give IV fluid without transfusing blood\n- **E)** Contact authority like Trust legal department\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Many Jehovah's witnesses refuse transfusions of whole blood (including preoperative autologous donation) and the primary blood components — red cells, platelets, white cells, and unfractionated plasma.\n- The guideline suggests that if they're competent or if they carry the blood refusal card( witnessed written directives), we should respect their decision not to receive blood products even if this would lead to their death.\n- You can advise them to receive blood products. however, you aren't allowed to pressure them to accept it. Key_pojnts\n- Child (less than 16) Jehovah's witnesses unconscious — transfuse blood in his/her best interest.\n- Child (less than 16) Jehovah's witnesses conscious — is child deemed competent? If yes! and refuse, then respect his decision and do not transfuse blood products. But if a child isn't competent and needs urgent transfusion then transfuse in his best interest.\n- Child >16 Jehovah's witnesses conscious and refuse lifesaving transfusion - blood transfusion should not be given because the child is competent to make the decision\n\n- Adult Jehovah's witnesses unconscious and has blood refusal card ( witnessed written directives) - then don't transfuse blood products.\n- Adult Jehovah's witnesses unconscious and no blood refusal card (mentioned or given) — then transfuse blood products.\n- Adult Jehovah's witnesses competent — then don't transfuse blood products.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 32-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department after accidentally hitting his thumb with a hammer. He complains of severe throbbing pain in his thumb. X-rays confirm no underlying fracture. His thumb image is shown. What is the next best step in management?   A Trephination of the nail\n- **B)** Imrnobilize the finger in a splint\n- **C)** Administer oral antibiotics\n- **D)** Reassure and discharge with analgesia\n- **E)** Perforrn nail removal\n- Explanation\n- The image shows a subungual hematoma. It is results from trauma to the nail bed, leading to blood accumulation under the nail and significant pain due to pressure.\n\nTrephination involves creating a small hole in the nail (using a needle,\n- cautery, or a specific device) to drain the hematoma and relieve pressure. Antibiotics Not indicated unless there are signs of infection.\n-\n- Subungual hematoma Collection of blood under the nail. There is a strong association with\n- distal phalanx fractures.\n- Types Simple subungual hematoma - No nailbed dislocation or evidence of\n- open fracture\n- Complex subungual hematoma - Aassociated with fracture or nail plate disruption Nailtip anatomy\n- Mentioned in picture below\n- The perinychium includes the nail, the nailbed, and the surrounding\n- tissue. The paronychia is the lateral nail folds\n- The hyponychium is the palmar surface skin distal to the nail.\n- The lunula is that white semi-moon shaped proximal portion of the\n- nail. The sterile matrix is deep to the nail, adheres to it and is distal to the\n- lunule. The germinal portion is proximal to the matrix and is responsible\n- for nail growth.\n- **A)** H. G. F.\n- **E)** -\n- **A)** Nail plate;\n- **B)** lunula;\n- **C)** root;\n- **D)** sinus;\n- **E)** matrix; F. nail bed; G. hyponychium; H. free margin\n- Diagnosis\n- Clinical diagnosis\n- Evaluate percentage of nail bed involved, test extensor/flexor tendons and distal cap refill . Management\n- Trephination\n- Cleanse with povidone-iodine solution (not flammable alcohol)\n- Handheld cautery works best - no anesthesia is required\n\n- Alternatively a needle spun in a drilling fashion\n- Sharp object (i.e. safety pin) heated with flame in an austere environment\n- Indicated for acute sublingual hematomas (less than 24 to 48 hours old)\n- If a fracture is present, the digit should be splinted\n- Instruct patients to soak affected finger in warm water BID-TID x7days\n- Prophylactic antibiotics are not needed after trephination of uncomplicated hematomas\n- In complex - Nail removal only recommended if there is associated nail avulsion or nail fold disruption\n- Repair nailbed laceration using absorbable sutures\n- Contraindications\n- Electrocautery - Presence of acrylic nails secondary to fire risk. Remove acrylic nails before using electrocautery tool.\n\n- All blood has been expelled through the trephination hole.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 63-year-old female presented to the ED with a complaint of abdominal pain associated with vomiting and the pain is not relieved by morphine. On examination, her abdomen was soft and tender and there was no guarding movement. Her investigation is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Acute ischaemic bowel\n- **B)** Diverticulosis\n- **C)** Sigmoid volvulus\n- **D)** Rupture aortic aneurysm\n- **E)** Small bowel obstruction Explanation;\n- Elderly with severe pain and elevated WCC and lactate level suggests ischaemic bowel.\n\nDefinition Risk factors Clinical feature Investigations Management Acute mesenteric Chronic mesenteric ischaemia ischaemia This is an umbrella This is a coveringa number of atherosclerotic disease of the conditions, including acute vessels supplyingthe mesenteric arterial embolus intestine. It is also known as andthrombus, mesenteric intestinal angina. venous thrombus and non- Usually all three major occlusive mesenteric ischaemia mesenteric arteries a (NOMII involved They all havethe features of Common in female age b/w impaired blood transfusion to SO and 70. the intestine Age>50 Generally caused by factors History of smoking predisposing to e states (Strong risk factor for mesenteric smoking, hypertension, venous thrombosis) diabetes mellitus and Afib, W, structural heart hyperlipidaemia. disease\n- History of vasculitis pa in out\n- weight loss, of proportion. postpran dial pain ('intestinal angina 'land a fear Of eating there may be minimal or no nausea, vomiting, or bowel tenderness. irregularity. symptoms Examination reveal - Vague Of peritonism develop, with abdominal tenderness rebound and disproportionate to the tendemess severity of the pain Urgent CT contrast/angiogram FBC,LFTs FBC (elevated WCC)\n- ABGs & Serum lactate\n- Arteriography (gold sta ndard) (metabolic acidosis and Mesenteric duplex elevated lactate) ultrasonography\n- ECHO Medical therapy\n- Start I V fluid and antibiotics Managed With smoking cessation and\n- NG tube Should be Sited platelet therapy. Su -cal intervention Svmptomatic chronic urgent laparotomy should be mesenteric ischaemia done for patients with open or endovascular peritonitis.\n- The goals of surgery include re- establishmentof the blood Postoperative complication supply to the ischaemic bowel; Renal failure resection Of all non-viable and preservation Of all viable