 finding/sign is an alternative to anterior uveitis?\n- **A)** Pain on accomrnodation\n- **B)** Small irregular pupil\n- **C)** Increased intraocular pressure\n- **D)** Posterior synechiae\n- **E)** Keratic precipitates\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Intraocular pressure (IOP) is commonly normal in uveitis and raised in acute closure glaucoma.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nAccording to NICE guideline, which of the following is the red flag feature in adult sepsis?\n- **A)** New need for 40% oxygen or more to maintain Sp02> 92%\n- **B)** Systolic BP 91-100mmhg C\n- HR >110 bpm\n- **D)** Respiratory rate 24 breaths per minute\n- **E)** Decrease urine output Explanation; Green (Iow risk) Beha viour Respiratory Heart rate Blcx»d pressure other Amber (moderate risk)\n- hist«y ot\n- 21—24\n- Systolic presure\n- Not passed in the past Red (High risk) Not in\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA mother brought a child who weighs 4kg. On the initial assessment, you found the baby was in cardiac arrest. What is the recommended dose of adrenaline for this baby?\n- **A)** 0.4ml O\n- **B)** 0.3ml O\n- **C)** 0.1ml O\n- **D)** 0.2ml\n- **E)** 0.6ml\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  The recommended IV/IO dose of adrenaline in children is IO mcg kg (0.1mLkg ofl in 10,000 solution). The child's weight is 4kg, therefore, 4 x IO = 40 mcg ( 0.4ml)\n- Another way to memorize: weight is 5kg then the adrenaline dose will be 0.5m'.\n- Similarly, if the weight is 3.5kg adrenaline dose would be 0.35ml\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 20-year-old male with a history of trauma presented to the ED with a complaint of neck pain. According to NICE guideline of CT cervical indication, you didn't order CT cervical spine. When you were about to discharge. The patient's brother who is a medical consultant insisted you to do CT cervical spine.\n\nWhat would be your next step?\n- **A)** Get review from orthopedic surgeon\n- **B)** Get help from senior nurse k your senior consultant for review\n- **D)** Tell him firmly no need of imaging and discharge him\n- **E)** Make a call to radiology unit and review any missed diagnosis Explanation\n- Resolving disagreements:\n- Sometimes members of the healthcare team disagree about what would be of overall benefit to the patient, or those close to the patient disagree with you and the healthcare team. It is preferable, and usually possible, to resolve disagreements about a patient's treatment and care through local processes.\n- For example, by: 1. involving an independent advocate or local mediation service 2. consulting a more experienced colleague and/or an independent expert 3. holding a case conference or seeking advice from a clinical ethics committee. can't discharge the patient firmly if the patient doesn't agree with you. in this case you should explore their reasons for requesting it, their understanding of what it would involve, and their\n\nexpectations about the likely outcome. This discussion will help you take account of factors that are significant to the patient and assess whether providing the treatment or care could serve the patient's needs. If after discussion you still consider that the treatment or care would not serve the patient's needs, then you should not provide it. But, you should explain your reasons to the patient and explore other options that might be available, including their right to seek a second opinion.*\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 65-year-old male is brought to the Emergency Department with a 3-day history of fever, productive cough, and fatigue. On arrival at triage, his observations reveal: Temperature: 38.80C Heart rate: 125 bpm Respiratory rate: 28/min Blood pressure:  mmHg Oxygen saturation: 92% on air His NEWS2 score is calculated as\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nWhat is the next best step in management?\n- **A)** Administer a IL intravenous fluid\n- **B)** Administer broad-spectrum antibiotics\n- **C)** Obtain blood cultures and delay treatment until results are available\n- **D)** Perform arterial blood gas analysis\n- **E)** Refer urgently to intensive care\n- Explanation\n- A score of 7 or more suggests high risk of severe illness or death from sepsis. Hence, administer broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotic within 1 hour of calculating the person's NEWS2 score on initial assessment in the emergency department.\n- NICE recommends 500ml bolus within 15 minutes, however, should in caution, patient with heart failure or patient has developed oedema or start with 250ml bolus.\n- In patients meeting fluid resuscitation criteria (systolic BP <90 mmHg or lactate 22 mmol/L indicating hypoperfusion), antibiotics and intravenous fluids should generally be initiated simultaneously. However, if required to select a single best answer and the fluid bolus (500 mL over 15 minutes) is provided as an option, antibiotics remain the priority intervention.\n- NICE 2024 recommendation\n- Initial assessment In people aged 16 or over, grade risk of severe illness or death from sepsis using the person's:\n- history\n- physical examination, and\n- NEWS2 score. National\n\nThe NEVVS is based on a simple aggregate scoring system in which a\n- score is allocated to physiological measurements, already recorded in routine practice, when patients present to, or are being monitored in hospital. Six simple physiological parameters form the basis of the scoring system: l.respiration rate\n- 2.oxygen saturation\n-\n- 3.systolic blood pressure 4.pulse rate\n- 5.1evel of consciousness or new confusion\n- 6.temperature.\n-\n- NICE Guideline classifies red-band (score of 3) vital signs as high-risk criteria, and orange-band (score of 2) as moderate-to-high risk criteria for adult patients with suspected sepsis\n\ns91 s35.o\n- When-evaluating Physiological parameter Respiration late (per minute) SP02SCde1 Sp02Sca1e2(X) Air or oxygen? Systolic blood 101-110 pressure (mmHg) Pulse (per minute) Consciousness TerrFature (\n-\n- **C)** 9243 84-85 Oxygen 91-100 9-11 86-87 41 35.1 _ 50 12-20 88-92 uil 111-219 51 _90 36.1-38.0 oxygen 91-110 38.1-390 71-24 95-96 on oxygen 111-130 239.1 oxygen 2220 *131 CVPU people-aged-For-oyer-with-suspegted-or-gonf.irmed-jnfection,-use clinical nise-that;\n- a score of 7 or more suggests high risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- a score of 5 or 6 suggests a moderate risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- a score ofl to 4 suggests a low risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- a score of O suggests a very low risk of severe illness or death frorn sepsis\n- if a single parameter contributes 3 points to their NEVVS2 score, request a high-priority review by a clinician with core competencies in the care of acutely ill patients (FY2 or above), for a definite decision on the person's level of risk of severe illness or death from sepsis.\n\n- Consider evaluating the person's risk of severe illness or death from sepsis as being higher than suggested by their NEWS2 score alone if any of the following is present:\n- mottled or ashen appearance\n- non-blanching petechial or purpuric rash\n- cyanosis of skin, lips or tongue.\n- When to recalculate a NEWS2 score\n- Recalculate the NEWS2 score and re-evaluate risk of sepsis periodically, as mentioned in table below:\n- Every 30 minutes, for those at high risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- Every hour, for those at moderate risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- Every 4 to 6 hours, for those at low risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- when standard observations are carried out, in line with local protocol, for those at very low risk of severe illness or death from sepsis.\n- Clinical Decision Sup psis in\n\n- NICE 2024: settings Carer Sep". Of PS lab evidence Of organ dysfunction including elevated serum upgrade actions at least to N'WS\n- 2 of frailty e thnjdty NEWS\n- Z Managing suspected sepsis in acute hospital\n- Start looking for the source of infection\n- Take microbiological and blood samples before giving an antimicrobial\n\nHigh Risk Hizh risk Of severe illness or death from seosis A person is at high risk Of severe illness Or death from sepsis if they have suspected or confirmed infection and a NEWS2 score Of 7 or A person is also at high risk of severe illness or death from sepsis if they have suspected or confirmed infection, a NEWS2 score below 7, and:\n- a single parameter (from six parameters: respiration rate, oxygen saturation, systolic blood pressure, pulse rate, level of consciousness or new confusion, temperature) contributes 3 points to their NEWS2 score and a medical review has confirmed that they are at high risk Moderate to high risk Moderate risk Of severe illness or death from seosis\n- A person is at moderate risk Of severe illness or death from sepsis if they have suspected or confirmed infection and a NEWS2 score Of 5 or\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA person is also at moderate risk Of severe illness or death from sepsis if they have suspected or confirmed infection, a NEWS2 score below 5, and: a single parameter contributes 3 points to their NEWS2 score, and a medical review has confirmed that they are at moderate risk or\n- there are any other clinical reasons for concern Low risk LOW risk Of severe illness o! death from sepsis\n- A person is at IOW risk Of severe illness Or death from sepsis if they have suspected or confirmed infection and a NEWS2 score Of I to 4 or a NEWS2 score of O and cause for clinical concern\n- When evaluating the risk Of severe illness or death from sepsis in people azed 16 or over With suspected or confirmed infection. use clinical judgement to interpret the NEWS2 score and recognise that:\n- a score of 7 or more suggests high risk of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- a score Of 5 or 6 suggests a moderate risk Of severe illness or death from sepsis\n- a Score of I to 4 suggests a Iow risk of severe illness Or death from sepsis\n- a score of O suggests a very low risk of severe illness or death from sepsis Investigations\n- carry out a venous blood test, including for:\n- blood gas, including glucose and lactate measurement\n- blood culture\n- full blood count\n-\n- C-reactive protein urea and electrolytes\n- creatinine\n- liver function tests\n- a clotting screen\n- In low risk - perform blood tests if indicated, use clinical judgement\n-\n\n- Management\n- Empirical antirnicrobial combinations for sepsis of unknown source in adults, choices are co-amoxiclav + aminoglycosides or piperacillin- tazobactam or piperacillin-tazobactam + aminoglycosides or amoxicillin + aminoglycosides + metronidazole.\n- National paediatric early warning system (PEWS)\n- The PEWS chart contains multiple elements:\n\n- A PEVVS score made up of Heart Rate, Respiratory Rate, Extent of Respiratory Distress, Blood Pressure, Oxygen Saturations, Oxygen Delivery and Capillary Refill Time (CRT) Other vital signs and observations, which don't score but must be recorded, rnade up of Temperature, AVPU (Alert, Voice, Pain, Unresponsive) and Pain score A Parent Concern Trigger A Clinical Intuition Trigger. PEWS Observation and escalation chart can be view here\n-\n- and-escalation-charts/ Clinical Decision Sup years Child appears unwell to health professional YES Within\n- CChe\n\n- Intervention based on PEWS\n- The working group recommends that the most severely ill - including those with (i) septic shock, (ii) sepsis associated with a likely need for an emergency surgical procedure to control a source of sepsis (iii) sepsis associated with rapid deterioration, or (PEWS 29) - should continue to receive broad-spectrum antirnicrobials within one hour of presentation. For children in PEWS band 1-4, the time window is within 4 hours. These severity-adjusted treatment intervals will allow clinical staff more time to make patient-focused and informed decisions and limit unnecessary antimicrobial prescribing. The term 'antimicrobial' is used throughout to cover antibacterial, antifungal and antiviral agents. previous  Next —y\n	\N	[]	\N	f	33	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e82c93c9-cf2e-4477-9b79-49295ef43d67	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-34	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 34	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 34\n\nO\n- **B)** 100 systolic\n- **C)** 120 systolic\n- **D)** 90 systolic\n- **E)** 110 systolic\n- Explanation\n- The ideal target might vary based on clinical circumstances, 120 mmHg is the upper limit of this target range and is considered acceptable in most cases to ensure adequate perfusion while avoiding excessive strain on the aneurysm.\n- The European Society for Vascular Surgery recommends aiming for a target systolic blood pressure (SBP) of 70 to 90 mmHg. In practice, this target SBP is a good approach if definitive surgical repair is imminent.\n- However, the Royal College of Emergency Medicine recommends a higher target SBP Of 90 to 120 mmHg. The previously recomrnended lower range of systolic BP of 70mmHg may be too low, particularly in the elderly. Blood products may be required but cross-matching must not delay transfer. Rarely, inotropic support may be required if the patient is not fluid responsive.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 25-year-old male brought to the ED semi-conscious having taken an overdose of a mixture of tablets three hours previously. His observations are: Temperature: Heart rate: 120 beats per minute\n\nBlood pressure: Respiratory rate: Oxygen saturations: Her ECG is given:  14 breaths per minute 96 % on room air What is the most likely cause of his condition?\n- **A)** TCA toxicity\n- **B)** Opioid toxicity\n- **C)** Salicylate toxicity\n- **D)** Paracetamol overdose\n- **E)** Benzodiazepines overdose\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  ECG shows Sinus tachycardia, widened QRS, and dominant terminal R wave in aVR which gives a classical picture of TCA toxicity. Note For more detail check the TCA toxicity module in pharmacology & poisoning.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nWhile you're on a break, you've found one of your colleague browsing X-rays pictures on his phone. You confront him and he tells you that he is picturing x- rays of many patients and upload on social media for teaching purposes.\n\nWhat would be your action?\n- **A)** Seek advise from medical defense organization\n- **B)** Advise him to delete the images and not to do this again\n- **C)** Raise this issue to the GMC\n- **D)** Inform his clinical supervisor\n- **E)** Ask him to tell his supervisor Explanation;\n- First — advise him to delete the images and not do this again.\n- If he continues — advise him to tell his clinical supervisor.\n- If he doesn't obey and continues - his practice, then inform his clinical supervisor yourself.\n- Pictures of a patient's x-rays or CT for educational purposes should be anonymized (without the name and the other details of a patient and the hospital).\n- Picture of patient's faces or parts of the body should not be taken unless consent from the patient is obtained.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 15-year-old girl brought into the ED with complaints of confusion and drowsiness. Her mother states she found empty packets of some medications along with a suicidal note. Her lab is given.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of her condition?\n- **A)** Salicylate toxicity\n- **B)** Paracetamol poisoning\n- **C)** Cocaine toxicity\n- **D)** Opioid overdose\n- **E)** TCA overdose Explanation;\n- ABC results show a respiratory alkalosis with partial metabolic (HC03-) compensation\n- To calculate anion gap: serum anion gap using the formula [(Na + K) - (Cl + HC03)] = 27.2\n- Respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis with raised anion gap this combination is so characteristics of salicylate overdose. Notezfor more detail check the salicylate poisoning module in pharmacology & poisoning.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 22-year-old male returning from his work suddenly went unconscious with eye-rolling and abnormal jerky movement muscle. He has no previous medical, drug or any seizure activity. According to DVLA guideline when he can start driving?\n- **A)** 3 months\n- **B)** 1 month\n- **C)** 5 years\n- **D)** 1 years\n- **E)** 6 months Explanation;\n- first seizure, the patient can start driving after 6 months. First unprovoked epileptic seizure/ isolated seizure Epilepsy or multiple unprovoked seizures Group 1 car and motorcycle Must not drive and must notify DVLA. Driving must cease 6 months from the date of the seizure\n- Must not drive and must notify DVLA.\n- Driving must cease for 12 months from the date Of the most recent seizure Group 2 bus and lorry\n- Must not drive and must notify DVLA.\n- Driving must cease 5 years from the date of the seizure\n- Must not drive and must notify DVLA.\n- The person with epilepsy must remain seizure-free for IO years (without epilepsy medication) before licensing may be considered\n- Withdrawal of epilepsy medication - Individuals should not drive whilst anti-epilepsy medication is being withdrawn and for 6 months\n\nafter the last dose.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 28-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department with an inability to pass urine. He heard a snap during intercourse and immediately experienced a loss of erection. There is bruising and swelling over the penis. Since then, he has been unable to pass urine, and his bladder is distended. What is your next immediate next step?\n- **A)** Retrograde urethrogram\n- **B)** urgent MRI\n- **C)** Insert Suprapubic catheter\n- **D)** Insert Foley's catheter E\n- Refer to urology\n- Explanation\n- Bladder distension suggests urethral injury or obstruction resulting from the penile fracture\n- Guideline recommends - Urgent surgical exploration and repair Of the tunica albuginea rupture and urethral injury should be performed within 24 h or sooner if there is an associated urethral injury.\n- Do NOT pass foley until confirming patient can pass urine OR Retrograde urethrogram may be necessary to assure urethral integrity (especially important if pt unable to urinate).\n\n- Penile fracture\n- Penile fracture is a genitourethral emergency secondary to rupture of the tunica albuginea during an erection. It can be associated with a urethral injury in approximately 25% of cases.\n- Presentation Penile fracture needs to be distinguished from other similar injuries including:\n- Superficial vein rupture/superficial haematoma.\n- Suspensory ligament rupture.\n- The usual mechanism of penile fracture is buckling during vigorous sexual intercourse, when the penis slips out and impacts against the pubic bone or perineum. Blunt trauma to the flaccid penis does not usually result in tunical rupture.\n- It can also occur following:\n- Masturbation.\n- Self-inflicted, as a result of bending the erect penis to produce immediate detumescence.\n- Assessment\n- The patient history specifically detailing the mechanism of injury usually confirms the diagnosis of a penile fracture. The classic presentation for a penile fracture includes:\n- Sudden severe penile pain.\n- A simultaneous 'cracking' or 'popping' sound.\n- Immediate detumescence and the inability to carry on with sexual intercourse.\n- Gross swelling and haematoma ofthe penis ('Aubergine sign' or 'Eggplant sign').\n- However, not all of these features need to be present in order to make a diagnosis of penile fracture.\n\nThe penis is swollen often with severe bruising.\n- The bruising is limited to the penis if Buck's fascia is intact. It may extend to the scrotum, perineum and lower abdominal wall if Buck's fascia is breached.\n- A tender, palpable defect may be felt over the ruptured area of the tunica albuginea.\n- The commonest site of injury is ventro-lateral, as this is where the tunica albuginea is thinnest.\n- There may be blood at the urethral meatus, haematuria, pain on voiding, or urinary retention if the urethra is also injured.\n- Recommendation - The history and clinical examination should confirm the diagnosis and exclude other similar presentations.\n- Investigations\n- Specialist-Nnyestigatiom(urologist-may-request)\n- Ultrasonography of the penis (without an artificial erection) may help identify both the location and extent of the tunical rupture. It is important to ask the radiologist to mark the site if it can be identified. This can help with planning the surgical approach.\n- Where there is a diagnostic doubt, penile MRI may aid the clinical diagnosis.\n- Recommendation - Ultrasonography can confirm the diagnosis and locate the tunical tear, which aids planning the surgical approach.\n- ED evaluation:\n- It's a Clinical diagnosis\n- Rule out other trauma\n- Do NOT pass foley until confirming patient can pass urine OR\n\n- Retrograde urethrogram may be necessary to assure urethral integrity (especially important if pt unable to urinate)\n- Management\n- Specialist-inter.yen.tiom(urologist)\n- Once the diagnosis has been made, urgent exploration and repair of the tunica albuginea is required to prevent later penile curvature and erectile dysfunction. This can still be performed in delayed presentations for up to 1 week after the injury or even later if there is an associated urethral injury. Re.GQ.m.m.e.n.d.a.tiQ.n;-Urgent surgical exploration and repair of the tunica albuginea rupture and urethral injury should be performed within 24 h or sooner if there is an associated urethral injury.\n- TechnicaLP.Qi.nts-for-SurgigaLManagement\n- Broad spectrum antibiotics should be given preoperatively.\n- The preferred incision is penoscrotal unless the site of the defect is unknown or suspected to be in the distal penile shaft, in which case a de-gloving incision may be required. Once exposed, the site of the tunical injury will typically be identifiable by the presence of an overlying haematoma.\n- If a urethral injury is suspected then an on-table urethrogram should be performed, or diluted methylene blue inserted into the urethra once the urethra is exposed.\n- If there is no urethral injury, a Foley catheter can be inserted.\n- If a urethral injury is identified, the urethral edges should be exposed and repaired in two layers where possible using  polyglactin sutures. A Foley catheter should be left in situ for 14 days and a peri-catheter urethrogram performed before removing the catheter.\n\n- Postoperative Follow-up\n- Patients should be followed-up after 2 weeks and refrain from sexual intercourse for 6 weeks.\n- Postoperative erectile dysfunction can be treated with oral pharmacotherapies, followed by intracavernosal/ intraurethral prostaglandin, and finally a penile prosthesis for those with end-stage erectile dysfunction.\n- ED management Urgent referral to urologist for Surgical exploration/repair required for most injuries if there is obvious or suspected fracture\n- Penile hematoma with no fracture can be treated as an outpatient with NSAIDs\n- Penile laceration with no fracture can be closed with 4-0 or 5-0 absorbable sutures.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 50-year-old male known case of myocarditis came with a complaint of shortness of breath. After an initial assessment, ECHO was done which revealed LV failure. Which of the following drug is indicated in this patient?\n- **A)** Beta blocker\n- **B)** Spironolactone\n- **C)** NSAIDs\n- **D)** Lisinopril\n- **E)** Digoxin Explanation;\n- ACE inhibitor is the first line in haemodynamically stable myocarditis patients with evidence of left ventricular systolic dysfunction. First line - supportive care ± treatment of underlying Haemodynamically stable: no evidence of LV systolic dysfunction Haemodynamically stable: evidence of left ventricular systolic dysfunction Haemodynamically unstable: adults Haemodynamically unstable: children End-stage heart failure or refractory life-threatening arrhythmias cause depends on the cause if autoimmune then methylprednisolone etc.\n- Consider IVIG inhibitor (e.g., captopril, enalapril, lisinopril) or angiotensin-II receptor antagonist (e.g„ candesartan, valsartan).\n- Consider IVIG and treat underlying cause Consider diuretics and beta blockers (in selected patients only) First line - arterial vasodilator (Sodium nitroprusside) + invasive haemodynamic monitoring Consider IVIG, GTN, inotropes or vesopressors\n- First line —inotropes (milrinone or dobutamine) Consider IVIG and mechanical circulatory support First line - Heart transplantation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 25-year-old male brought to the ED with a lacerated wound in his throat after being assaulted. He is deteriorating and losing consciousness. What initial step will you take in this patient?\n- **A)** Orotracheal intubation\n- **B)** Cive oxygen and wait for anesthetic\n- **C)** Suction the wound\n- **D)** Cricothyroidotomy\n- **E)** Intubation through the cut wound Explanation\n- Initial management of laryngotracheal trauma\n- Setting up a secure airway is the first priority in ED.\n- All patients with hemodynamic instability or high risk ofa difficult airway, should undergo orotracheal intubation by expert personnel, through direct vision, ideally using a fiberoptic bronchoscope, placing the balloon distal to the lesion and without applying pressure on the cricoid cartilage.\n- If orotracheal intubation is not possible, the need for an emergency tracheostomy or a cricothyroidotomy, as a last resource, should be evaluated.\n- Once the airway is deemed to be stable, further evaluation of the laryngeal injury is possible. Flexible fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a critical step in evaluating the status Of the airway after laryngeal traurna\n\nAirway via Grade Ill Laryngeal Injury N.Q.te;-For more detail check laryngotracheal trauma in the major trauma module.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 34-old farmer presented to the ED with a wound injury on his foot a few days back on his farm and it's now getting worse. After an assessment, you suspect necrotizing fasciitis. Which of the following organism is the most cause of this condition?\n- **A)** Croup A streptococcus\n- **B)** E-coli\n- **C)** Staph epidermis\n- **D)** Staph aureus\n- **E)** Clostridium prifringes\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Necrotizing fasciitis - is rnost comrnonly a monomicrobial infection with Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococci).\n\nAcute cellulitis\n- It is an acute bacterial infection of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue. Cellulitis most commonly affects the lower limbs, but other areas, such as the upper face, ears, and trunk. can also be affected\n- Causes Streptococcus pmenes. Staphylococcus aureus Less common Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n- Vü)rio vulnificus\n- Aeromonas hydrophila etc\n- Risk factors\n- Trauma (due to a bite. burn, or laceration), Leg ulceration Maceration or fungal hfection between the toes. or a concomitant skin disorder (such as atopic eczema) Comorbidities (such as diabetes mellitus) Fyrnphoedema and leg oedema\n- Clinical features Red. painful. hot. swollen, and tender skin, that spreads rapidly. Fever, malaise, nausea, shivering, and rigors Diffuse redness or a well-demarcated edge Blisters and bullae may be seen There is usually an obvious skin break where the infecting organism may have entered. such as a Investiqations Investigations are not usually necessary but may be considered in certain cases. Swab for culture — there is an open wound, penetrating injury, drainage. ultrasonography — for distinguishing nonpurulent cellulitis from cellulitis with underlying abscess Skin biopsy WCC, ESR and CRP\n- Mana emen\n- If cellulitis is diagnosed, categorize the severity using the Eron classification system, to help guide management decisions, (table iS given below)\n- Arranqe hospita' if the person:\n- Has Class Ill or IV cellulitis\n- Is very young (under 1 year of age) or frail.\n- Is immunocompromized Has significant lymphoedema.\n- Has facial cellulitis (unless very mild)_\n- Has suspected orbital or periorbital cellulitis Necrotizing fasciitis\n- It is a life-threatening subcutaneous soft-tissue infection that requires a high index of suspicion for diagnosis. Aly.'ays suspect necrotising fasciitis in a patient with a rapidly progressing soft-tissue infection and any of the following:\n- Severe pain (disproportionate to the clinical findings)\n- Anaesthesia over the site Of infection\n- Oedema and erythema (oedema will typically extend beyond the erythema)\n- Systemic signs of infection\n- Causes Type I necrotising fasciitis - is a polymicrobial infection such as Bacteroides or peptostreptococcus or Enterobacterales or non-group A streptococcus Type II necrotising fasciitis - is most commonly a monomicrobial infection With Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococci)\n- Risk factors Immunosuppression due to chronic illness (e.g.. diabetes mellitus, alcohol dependence) Cutaneous trauma. surgery, or ulcerative conditions Varicella zoster infections\n- Intravenous drug use and hospitalization\n- Clinical features Anaesthesia or severe pain over site of infection Fe ver Palpltations, tachycardia, tachypnoea. hypotension, and lightheadedness Nausea and vomiting Oedema or induration Vesicles or bullae Delirium\n- Investigations\n- Surgical exploration The 'finger test' is a surgical method that can be performed under local anaesthesia at the bedside for the diagnosis of necrotising fasciitis.\n- Bbod tissue culture. CRP, ESR\n- LFTs, Clotting screen\n- Consider X-ray, MRI, ultrasonography\n- Mana emen Emergency surgical debridement - Refer the patient immediately to the surgical team; necrotising fasciitis requires rapid debridement of all infected tissues in combination with empirical antibiotic therapy. MRSA antibiotic coverage - Continue vancomycin, linezolid. tedizolid, or daptomycin if MRSAis confirmed. Analgesia and fluid resuscitation\n\n- flucloxacillin 500—1000 mg four times daily for 5—7 days. If person has a penicillin allergy, prescribe either: Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 5—7 days. Or\n- Doxycycline 200 mg on the first day then 100 mg once daily, for a total of 5—7 days.\n- Erythromycin (in pregnancy) 500 mg four times daily for 5—7 days.\n- Note\n- dose should be adjusted in children accordingly.\n- Complications\n- Necrotizing fasciitis\n- Myositis\n- Sepsis. Subcutaneous abscesses.\n- Post-streptococcal nephritis.\n- Lymphoedema, leg ulceration\n- Eron classification of cellulitis Class I Class II Class II Class IV\n- There are no signs of systemic toxicity and the person has no uncontrolled comorbidities.\n- The person is either systemically unwell or systemically well but with a comorbidity (for example peripheral arterial disease, chronic venous insufficiency, or morbid obesity) which may complicate or delay resolution of infection.\n- The person has significant systemic upset, such as acute confusion, tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypotension, or unstable comorbidities that may interfere with a response to treatment, or a limb-threatening infection due to vascular compromize.\n- The person has sepsis or a severe life-threatening infection, such as necrotizing fasciitis. 10 / 10 IO. You're the registrar in the ED, one of the senior nurse from your department complained about a junior doctor that he smells alcohol and does not complete his tasks. What would be your action?\n- **A)** Ask the nurse to inform the consultant\n- **B)** Raise issue to the CMC\n- **C)** Confront him\n- **D)** Seek advice from medical defense organization\n- **E)** Inform the consultant\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> confront him with advice.\n- the consultant previOUS  — raise it to the GMC\n	\N	[]	\N	f	34	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
46d809e0-6268-45c1-9070-94a997912ec5	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-35	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 35	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 35\n\nHR: 130 bpm Sp02: 87% on room air RR: 30 breaths/min What is the best next step of management?   A Alteplase\n- **B)** enoxaparin\n- **C)** CTPA\n- **D)** Rivaroxaban\n- **E)** 2D echocardiography Explanation\n- Patients with suspected or confirmed PE who present with shock or hypotension are at high risk of in-hospital death, particularly in the first few hours after presentation. Hence, It is common practice to give thrombolysis as first-line treatment for any patient who is in peri-arrest/cardiac arrest based on clinical suspicion of PE without waiting for results from investigations.\n- Management of pulmonary embolism\n\nPresentation haemodynamicallv unstable D OR 1 PE confirmed (on echocardioeraphv or CTPA): haemodvoamical'v unstable Intervention Respiratory su ppon\n- Give high-concentration oxygen if oxygen saturations are +0%, targeting to 98% (this may need to be adjusted to 88% to 92% in patients at risk Of hypercapnic respiratory failure). Assess oxygen saturation early. Hypoxaemia is a typical finding in PE; it may be present at rest if a high-risk (massive) PE is present or only on exertion with a smaller PE use high-flow oxygen and consider mechanical ventilation if the patient is extremely unstable (e.g., cardiac arrest).\n- Patients with suspected or confirmed PE who present with shock or hypotension are at high risk of in-hospital death, particularly in the first few hours after presentation. Hence, It is common practice to give thrombolysis as first-line treatment for any patient Who is in peri-arrest/cardiac arrest based on clinical suspicion Of PE Without waiting for results from investigations.\n- fluid resuscitation\n- Give intravenous fluids if SBP is €90 mmHg and the JVP is not elevated\n- Give intravenous fluids with caution and start with a fluid challenge of SOO mL over 15-30 minutes. Determine further fluid based on clinical response.\n- Getan urgent senior review if systolic blood pressure (SBP) is c90 mmHg and the jugular venous pressure (JVP) is elevated to determine whether intravenous fluids need to be given\n- Monitor closely for Sims Of rizht heart failure.\n- The leading cause of death in patients with high-risk (massive) PE is acute right ventricular (RV) failure with resulting hypotension. Patients with haemodynamically unstable PE require early intravenous fluid resuscitation but should be monitored for signs of right heart failure.\n- Start unfractionated heparin - Start unfractionated heparin (UFH) in haemodynamicallv unstable patients prior to thrombolysis.\n- In patients Who are haemodynamicallv unstable, evidence Of right ventricular dysfunction on echocardiography is sufficient to warrant urgent anticoagulation and thrombolysis. These patients are normally too unstable to undergo computed tomographic pulmonary angiography.\n- Continue anticoagulation with LJFH for several hours after the end of thrombolysis. UFH may be administered during continuous infusion of alteplase.\n- There is insufficient evidence to support use of low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) in haemodynamically unstable (high-risk) PE. Thrombolvsis - is the first-line intervention for most patients. Surgical embolectomy or catheter-directed therapy are alternatives.\n- Administer thrombolysis (unless contraindicated) to any patient who is haemodynamically unstable Systemic thrombolysis is associated with lower all-cause mortality in these patients when compared with unfractionated heparin (UFH) alone. Iteplase; IO mg intravenous bolus over 1-2 minutes, followed by 90 mg intravenous infusion over 2 hours (maximum 1.5 mg/kg in patients kg body weight).\n- Further manazement switch to alternative anticoagulant If switching to rivaroxaban or apixaban, or Iow molecular weight heparin (LMWH) is an alternative DOAC are unsuitable. these drugs may be started after stopping LJFH without the need for lead-in therapy with a parenteral anticoagulant. In the it is common practice to stop UFH within 24 hours. o Acute-phase treatment consists of an increased dose of the oral anticoagulant over the first 3 weeks (for rivaroxaban), or over the first 7 days (for apixaban). o In pregnant patient— give enoxaparin (LMWH)\n- Give a vasooressor such as noradrenaline [noreoineohrine) or adrenaline\n\nconfirmed (on CTPA) or highly suspected (Wells or positive O-dimer): haemodvnamicallv (epinephrine), or an inotrope such as dobutamine concurrently with, or while waiting for, thrombolysis if the patient remains haemodynamically unstable despite adequate intravenous fluids.\n- - Anticoagulation\n- Start initial anticoagulation as soon as possible With apixaban or rivaroxaban (DOAC); IOW molecular weight heparin (LMWH) is an alternative if these are unsuitable. Acute-phase treatment consists Of an increased dose Of the oral anticoagulant over the first 3 weeks (for rivaroxaban), or over the first 7 days (for apixaban).\n- If using LMWH, continue treatment forat least S days.\n- If ongoing anticoagulation will be with edoxaban or dabigatran, at least 5 days of lead-in therapy with LMWH is required first. Stop LMWH before starting dabigatran or edoxaban. OR\n- If ongoing anticoagulation will be with warfarin, start warfarin within 24 hours of diagnosis and ensure overlap With LMWH for at least 5 days or until the INR is 22 for at least two consecutive readings (Whichever is the longer), followed bv a vitamin K antagonist (VKA) on its own.\n- Definition-of-haemodynamic-instability,-which-delineates acute high-risk pulmonary embolism (one of the following clinical-nanifestations-acpresentation) (I) Cardiac arrest\n- Need for cardiopulmonary resuscitation (2) Obstructive shock Systolic BP 90 mmHg or vasopressors required to achieve a BP 290 mmHg despite adequate filling status. And\n- End-organ hypoperfusion (altered mental Status; cold, clammy skin; oliguria/anuria; increased serum lactate) (3) Persistent hypotension\n- Systolic BP < 90 mmHg or systolic BP drop 240 mmHg, lasting longer than 15 min and not caused by new-onset arrhythmia, hypovolaemia,or sepsis\n- Recommendations of Echocardiography\n- Use bedside echocardiography for haemodynamically unstable patients who cannot have CTPA.\n-\n- If CTPA is not immediately available or the patient is too unwell to be moved, perform a bedside echocardiography. The presence of any signs of right ventricular (RV) dysfunction is sufficiently suggestive of PE to confirm the diagnosis and justify urgent reperfusion treatment (usually thrombolysis).\n\n- Do not use echocardiography routinely in haemodynamically stable patients with suspected PE. However, if it has been performed as part of the diagnostic work-up and shows evidence of RV dysfunction, consider further risk stratification by measuring a cardiac biomarker.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 30-year-old female presented to the ED with complaint of palpitations, tremor, and restlessness. her observations include temperature is 39 0 C, blood pressure  mmHg and irregular pulse. She has history of hyperthyroidism, however, she is uncompliant with her treatment. What is the best initial management plan?\n- **A)** Carbimazole\n- **B)** Lugol iodine\n- **C)** Propranolol\n- **D)** Verapamil\n- **E)** Propylthiouracil Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggests thyroid storm\n- Beta blockers - Propranolol 80mg tds PO to reduce heart rate and block effects of thyroid hormones. Alternatively, propranolol 2mg iv\n\nover 10 minutes can be used in a very unstable patient or one who cannot take oral medication.\n- Thyrotoxic crisis (thyroid storm)\n- This is a rare and potentially life-threatening complication, which may occur after a trigger such as acute infection, trauma, pregnancy, surgery including thyroidectomy, or stroke.\n- It may occur in people with previously undiagnosed hyperthyroidism or those who have abruptly stopped antithyroid medication.\n- Clinical features\n- Tachycardia, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, heart failure\n- Agitation, hyperthermia,\n- Jaundice\n- Delirium, and coma\n- Management-of thyroid-storm\n- as cooling, correction of volume status, respiratory support if indicated, and treatment of underlying sepsis if appropriate.\n- IV access\n- Take blood for TSH, T4, T3, U+E, FBC, liver function, calcium, glucose\n- Perform a septic screen: chest radiograph, blood and urine cultures, and ECG Inform HDU and contact endocrinology team\n- Consider central venous monitoring to help guide fluid status.\n\n- Beta blockers - Propranolol 80mg tds PO to reduce heart rate and block effects of thyroid horrnones. Alternatively, propranolol 2mg iv over 10 minutes can be used in a very unstable patient or one who cannot take oral medication.Calcium channel blockers may be considered if beta blockade is contraindicated e.g. in patients with asthma.\n- Antizthyroid drugs—Propylthiouracil 200mg tds PO / NG / PR to stop release and production of thyroid hormones. This is more suitable than carbimazole in emergency situations as it also prevents peripheral T4 - T3 interconversion.\n- Hydrocortisone 200mg, followed by prednisolone 20mg tds PO. This prevents peripheral conversion ofT4 to T3 and is particularly effective in Graves' disease.\n- Cholestyramine - 3g po tds may also be given to aid clearance of thyroid hormones by blocking their enterohepatic circulation should also be administered. Lugol solution must be given no earlier than 30 minutes after the first dose of antithyroid medication in order to avoid exacerbation of thyrotoxicosis due to escape from Wolff- Chaikoff effect.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA45 years old male presented to the ED with priapism after taking some medications prescribed by his GR Which of the following medication is likely the cause of his condition?\n- **A)** NSAIDS\n- **B)** Amitriptyline\n- **C)** Digoxin\n- **D)** Beta blocker\n- **E)** Fluoxetine Explanation\n- Following medications cause priapism: Alpha-adrenergic receptor antagonists (prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin, tamsulosin),\n- Anti-anxiety agents (hydroxyzine),\n- Anticoagulants (heparin, warfarin),\n- Antidepressants and antipsychotics (trazodone, bupropion, fluoxetine, sertraline, lithium, clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine, chlorpromazine, thioridazine), and antihypertensives (hydralazine, guanethidine, propranolol)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 50-year-old male, a non-smoker, presents to the Emergency Department with pleuritic chest pain and shortness of breath. A chest X-ray is performed and is incorrectly interpreted as normal by the FY 2 doctor. The patient is discharged with analgesia for suspected musculoskeletal pain. The next day, the radiologist reviews the X-ray and identifies a 1 cm pneumothorax. You, as the registrar, are informed of this missed diagnosis. What is the most appropriate next step?\n- **A)** Discuss the case with the FY2 to gather further details and reflect on the error\n- **B)** Advise the patient to follow up with CP\n- **C)** Do nothing, as a 1 cm pneumothorax is insignificant and will resolve on its own\n- **D)** Inform the consultant for advice and further management\n- **E)** Contact the patient and arrange regular outpatient follow-up for monitoring Explanation\n- The patient is symptomatic; however, he does not present with high- risk features, and the pneumothorax is not of sufficient size. Consequently, urgent intervention is not required. However, according to the BTS guidelines, outpatient follow-up is necessary. Patient safety remains the priority, and the next steps should ensure that the patient is stable and receives proper follow-up.\n- Contacting the patient directly ensures they are informed about the missed diagnosis and their clinical status can be assessed. This approach avoids unnecessary escalation while ensuring patient safety.\n- Clinical pathways/decision trees Pneumothorax Pathway_\n\nSodety disease Appendix 1 — Clinical pathways/decision trees Pneumothorax Pathway Is the patÆnt pneu 50 ycvs nsk characterisbr:r Sale Io intem*ne?\n- neumothorax su'ficient size\n- Wnat 's lhe patient's man priority? symptom relief drainage) Take review 24 days) irvatiw't (every 2-3 days) Opo 2-4 If in opo 2.4 review OPO in 2.' weeks Sale to intervene? SuffR;ient Size CT\n- mag.g Reg r as hin when\n- 2-4 me s S. CXR CXR, chest X-ray; COPD, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; OPD, outpatient department; PSP, primary SSP. secondary spontaneous pneumothorax.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 69-year-old man presents with sudden severe chest pain that radiates through to his back. He is sweaty and nauseous. he has history of uncontrolled hypertension. On examination, his BP is  mmHg in his right arm and  in his left arm and there is early diastolic murmur on auscultation. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Thoracic outlet syndrome\n- **B)** Aortic aneurysm\n- **C)** Aortic valve perforation D' Aortic dissection\n- **E)** Myocardial infarction Explanation\n- Clinical features —re.of-pain in AAD\n- . Featu\n- Sharp in nature describe as tearing pain\n- Occurs in anterior of chest 70-80% of Pt with Type A dissection\n- Upper back pain maybe experienced in Type-B Physical examination:\n- Faint or absent heart sounds\n\n- Distended neck veins\n- Presence of shock\n- A difference of >20 mmHg in the blood pressure of each arm\n- Acute or progressive aortic regurgitation\n- A diastolic murmur over the aortic valve area\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 59 years old male with multinodular goiter presented to the ED with palpitations, sweating and tremors. His observations are: Pulse is 240bpm BP is  mmHg What is the initial management?\n- **A)** Amiodarone   B\n- Verapamil\n- **C)** Digoxin\n- **D)** Lugol Iodine\n- **E)** Propranolol Explanation\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Beta blocker — to reduce heart rate and block effects of thyroid hormones. Hyperthyroidism\n- It is a biochemical diagnosis which occurs when there is pathologically increased thyroid hormone production and secretion by the thyroid gland. Thyrotoxicosis is the clinical manifestation of excess circulating thyroid hormones due to any cause, including hyperthyroidism.\n- Primary hyperthyroidism - occurs when thyrotoxicosis is caused by an abnormality of the thyroid gland such as Craves' disease.\n- Overt primary hyperthyroidism - is diagnosed when thyroid- stimulating hormone (TSH) is suppressed below the normal reference range, and free thyroxine (FT4) and/or free triiodothyronine (FT3) concentrations are above the normal reference range. Subclinical hyperthyroidism - is diagnosed when TSH is suppressed below the normal reference range, but FT4 and FT3 concentrations are within the normal reference range.\n- Thyrotoxicosis without hyperthyroidism - describes thyrotoxicosis without thyroid gland overactivity, which is usually transient.\n- Risk factors Female sex Family history Smoking - current smokers have a three-fold increased risk of developing Graves' orbitopathy compared with non-smokers. Low iodine intake Autoimmune disease\n\n- Causes\n- Graves' disease (most common) - It is a systemic autoimmune disorder mediated by thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)-receptor antibodies (TRAbs) that stimulate the TSH receptor, leading to thyroid hyperplasia and unregulated excessive production and secretion of thyroid hormone.\n- Peak incidence is at 30—50 years of age\n- Toxic multinodular goitre - This is the second most common cause of hyperthyroidism in the I-JK.\n- People over 60 years of age, and people living in iodine-deficient areas, such as Denmark.\n- Toxic thyroid nodule (adenoma) - This accounts for up to 5% of cases of hyperthyroidism, and is more common in older adults. TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma - This is a rare tumour secreting large quantities of TSH\n- Iodine - Iodine may suppress TSH levels and is found in over-the- counter preparations, such as kelp supplements\n- Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis (AIT type 1) — if a person with underlying thyroid disease is exposed to the high iodine content of amiodarone, this may lead to excess thyroid hormone synthesis and release.\n- Pituitary thyroid hormone resistance syndrome - This is a rare condition caused by genetic mutations in the thyroid hormone receptor-beta gene.\n- Gestational thyrotoxicosis — this is a benign and transient disorder typically seen in the first trimester of pregnancy, caused by the stimulatory action of placental beta-Hcg.\n\nClinical features Heat intolerance\n- Sweating\n- Weight loss\n- Palpitations - Including atrial fibrillation or other supraventricular\n- tachycardias, especially in older people Trernor\n-\n- Diffuse goitre\n- Orbitopathy Exercise intolerance, fatigue, muscle weakness\n- Polyuria, thirst, generalized itch.\n- Reduced libido, gynaecomastia in men\n- Specific features\n- Rapid-onset malaise, fever, and thyroid pain (may suggest subacute\n- thyroiditis). Compression symptoms of breathlessness, hoarse voice, dysphagia, neck pressure (may be caused by a toxic multinodular goitre). Specific sign\n- Graves' disease - the thyroid gland is usually diffusely symmetrically\n- enlarged without nodules, and there may be a bruit. Toxic multinodular goitre - typically presents with non-tender thyroid\n- nodules. Toxic adenoma - typically presents with a unilateral, non-tender thyroid\n- mass. Subacute thyroiditis -typically presents with a tender, firm, irregular,\n- diffusely enlarged thyroid gland which may be asymmetrical. Amiodarone-induced - thyroiditis, a small goitre is usually present.\n-\n\n- Suspect-a-diagnosis-of-grayee-orbitopathy-jf-there-are-glinical features of:\n- Eye irritation, photophobia, or excessive watering of the eyes.\n- Redness of the eyes or eyelids and/or lid swelling.\n- Change in the appearance of the eye or eyelids:\n- Eyelid retraction (sclera is visible above the superior corneal limbus).\n- Lid lag (delay in moving the eyelid as the eye moves downward).\n- Proptosis (exophthalmos, eyeball protrusion, an inability to fully close the eyes as the upper and lower lids do not fully appose).\n- Investigations\n- Thyroid function test — TSH level and serum free or total T3 or T4 level\n- TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb) Thyroid ultrasound scan with doppler\n- Measurement of 24-hour urinary iodine excretion may help to confirm suspected excessive iodine intake.\n- Thyroid peroxidase antibodies (TPOAb)\n- Consider CT or MRI scan of orbit\n- Management of thyrotoxicosis (NICE guideline)\n- Admit-the-patient_if there are symptoms suggesting a serious complication, such as thyrotoxic crisis\n- follow caner referral pathway for thyroid cancer.\n\n- Other all patients Adults with Graves' disease symptomatic treatment i-e palpitation, tachycardia carbimazole a 1st line a 12- to 18-month course as first- line definitive treatment for adults with Graves' disease. in patients where radioactive iodine and surgery are unsuitable. i-e radioactive iodine contra-indicated in pregnancy. Radioactive iodine therapy - Offer radioactive iodine as first-line definitive treatment for adults with Graves' disease, if ant-thyroid drugs ineffective or not suitable. total thyroidectomy as first-line definitive treatment for adults with Graves' disease if:\n- There are concerns about compression, or Thyroid malignancy is suspected, Or radioactive iodine and antithyroid drugs are unsuitable. Adults with toxic nodular goitre egte_Wegkeefor symptomatic treatment palpitation, tachycardia\n- 8Adipagiye-i9dine.gherapEOffer radioactive iodine as first-line definitive treatment for adults with hyperthyroidism secondary to multiple nodules\n- Sumer_EOffer total thyroidectomy or life-long antithyroid drugs as first-line definitive treatment for adults with hyperthyroidism ry to multiple nodules if radioactive iodine is unsuitable.\n- Offer radioactive iodine (if suitable) or Surgery (hemithyroidectomy) as first- line definitive treatment for adults with hypertrwroidism secondary to a single nodule, or life-long antithyroid drugs if these options are unsuitable. Children and young people with Graves' disease or toxic nodular goitre symptomatic treatment i-e palpitation, tachycardia\n- Anti:thyrgid.drgß.zoffer antithyroid drugs for at least 2 years and possibly longer as first-line definitive treatment for children and young people With Graves' disease\n- Consider continuing or restarting antithyroid drugs or discussing radioactive iodine or surgery (total thyroidectomy) for Children and young people with Graves' disease who have had a course of antithyroid drugs but have relapsed hyperthyroidism\n- Children and voune people with hvoerthvroidism to Offer antithyroid drugs using a titration regimen Of carbimazole, and discuss the role of surgery and radioactive iodine with the child and the person is taking carbimazole or propylthiouracil, advise them to: Seek urgent medical advice if they develop possible symptoms of agranulocytosis or neutropenia such as fever, sore throat, mouth ulcers, febrile or non\n- specific illness, bruising, or malaise, and to stop the medication immediately. Arrange an urgent blood test for differential white cell count to exclude agranulocytosis and neutropenia,\n- If the is taking carbimazole, advise them to: Seek urgent medical advice if they develop possible symptoms of acute\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED with Lethargy, polyurea, and occasional constipation. Additionally, he is complaining of bone and flank pain. you suspect primary hyperparathyroidism.\n\nWhich of the following investigation suggestive of primary hyperparathyroidism?\n- **A)** Normal calcium\n- **B)** Elevated P04 level\n- **C)** Elevated calcium\n- **D)** Low PTH\n- **E)** Decreased ALP Explanation Investigations of primary_hyperthyroidism\n- Serum calcium - from high-normal to raised.\n- serum intact PTH with immunoradiometric or immunochemical\n- assay - from high-normal to elevated 25-hydroxyvitamin D level - Vitamin D deficiency and PHPT frequently\n- co-exist. Serum alkaline phosphatase — maybe raised\n- Serum phosphorus — Low or low normal\n-\n- 24-hour urinary calcium - high or normal in PHPT; low in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scan - T-score: -1 to +1 =\n- normal; -1 to -2.5 = osteopenia; < -2.5 = osteoporosis\n\nPrimary hyperparathyroidism\n- Over production Of PTH.\n- parathyroid adenoma (most common cause)\n- Clinical features\n- Incidental finding on biochemical testing\n- Thirst, frequent or excessive urination, constipation Osteoporosis or fragility fracture, or renal stone\n- Myalgia Investigations Serum calcium - from high-normal to raised.\n- Serum intact PTH with immunoradiometric or immunochemical assay - from high-normal to elevated 25-hydroxyvitamin D level - Vitamin D deficiency and PHPT frequently co-exist.\n- Serum alkaline phosphatase — maybe raised\n- Serum phosphorus — Low or low normal\n- 24-hour urinary calcium - high or normal in PHPT; low in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia\n- dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scan - T-score: -1 to +1 normal; -I to -2.5 = osteopenia; < -2.5 osteoporosis\n- Management Refer to a surgeon with expertise if DVDT Rickets Osteomalacia\n- Osteomalacia is a metabolic bone\n- Rickets is a metabolic bone disease characterised by disease characterised by defective incomplete mineralisation Of the mineralisation Of the epiphyseal underlying mature organic bone growth plate cartilage in children. matrix (osteoid) following growth\n- Rickets only occurs in growing plate closure in adults. children before fusion of the epiphyses\n- Clinical features\n- Risk factors Age 6 to 23 months\n- Old age\n- Vitamin D and calcium deficient Inadequate sunlight exposure diets Breastfeeding\n- Lack of sunlight exposure Calcium deficiency Phosphate deficiency\n- Fractures\n- Malabsorption syndromes Family history Of rickets\n- Diffuse bone pain and tenderness Antacids, anticonvulsant\n- proximal muscle weakness\n- Clinical features\n- Family history of osteomalacia Bone pain\n- Waddling gait Growth retardation Delayed achievement of motor milestones Bonv deformities Muscle weakness\n- Investigations\n- Investigations serum calcium level - normal or X-ray Of a long bone - widening low of the epiphyseal plate.\n- serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D level\n- Serum calcium — may be\n- low decreased in hypocalcaemic serum phosphate level - IOW rickets; normal in Serum urea and creatinine\n- ratio hypophosphataemic rickets. elevated Serum inorganic phosphorus - intact PTH - high may be decreased in serum alkaline phosphatase - hypocalcaemic rickets; decreased high in hypophosphataemic rickets. 24-hour urinary calcium - low Serum parathyroid hormone Consider bone x-rays level - high with hypocalcaemic 24-hour urinary phosphate - high rickets and normal with hypophosphataemic rickets. dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry iliac crest biopsy with double Alkaline phosphatase and liver tetracycline labelling function tests -alkaline phosphatase is elevated in rickets 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels (calcidiol)\n- low in vitamin D, deficient rickets, usually Q5 nanomoles/L 1<10 nanograms/mL); normal in genetic forrns Of hypocalcaemic rickets.\n- Management\n- Management\n- Calcium plus vitamin D Symptomatic hypocalcaemia - is a mm-oral\n\nconfirmed and the person has:\n- thirst, frequent or excessive urination, constipation\n- osteoporosis or fragility fracture, Or renal stone\n- albumin-adjusted serum calcium (Ca) 2.85 mmol/litre Consider referral to surgeon if PHPT is confirmed With no symptoms or signs.\n- Consider a bisphosphonate to reduce fracture risk\n- Consider vitamin D supplement\n- Monitoring - If patients decline surgery or are not surgical candidates, serum calcium and creatinine levels should be measured every 12 months and bone density measured every 1 to 2 years\n- Consider phosphate in some patients. requires hospitalisation. Hypocalcaemic seizures and/or cardiovascular instability require an intensive care environment and intravenous calcium infusion. calcium-deficient rickets - calcium and vitamin D supplementation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nAn 18-year-old boy presented to the ED with mallet finger deformity. Which of the following is the initial management of this condition?\n- **A)** Steroids\n- **B)** Ice packing\n- **C)** Mallet finger splint\n- **D)** Refer to Orthopedic\n- **E)** IV antibiotics Explanation\n- Mallet finger\n\n- MOI: It occurs due to forced flexion of the DIP while the joint is in extension. It is the most common tendon injury in athletes.\n- There will be loss of full active extension at the DIP].\n- Order x-ray to exclude associated injuries\n- Management\n- Immobilize DIP joint in a mallet splint with continuous full extension for 6 to 8 weeks. No flexion of the DIP joint is permitted for the duration of splinting. Thus, instruct patients to not take off the splint.\n- If mallet finger with large fragment of fracture require referral to orthopedic for internal fixation untreated mallet finger may result in a swan neck deformity.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 14-year-old male presented to the ED after being punched on his face and loss his one upper canine tooth which he has found and brought to the ED in milk. Which of the following is the next step of management?\n- **A)** Get dental X-ray\n- **B)** Implant after soaking the avulsed tooth in hydrogen peroxide\n- **C)** Irrigate with NS and replant the tooth D\n- DO not replant\n- **E)** Refer to maxillofacial surgery Explanation\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- v Ise be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth:\n- saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here\n- The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried out for up to 2 week.\n- Dental trauma management\n\nImmediate management of avulsed Primary teeth management Adult tooth management The appropriate management of an avulsed tooth within the first 30 minutes and an organized treatment plan are essential to increase tooth survival.\n- hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth.\n- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.\n- Primarv teeth management\n- If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldn't bere- implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth, f the primary teeth are very loose and present a potential airway risk, then they should be removed\n- Adult tooth management be replaced into the correct position, and a splint(wire splint or silk suture splints) should be used to stabilize them and arrange follow-up with dentist.\n- Avulsed adult teeth should be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth:\n- saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried out for up to 2 week.\n- Tetanus booster should be consider and antibiotic (tetracycline or penicillin V) should be recommended\n- patients advised to rinse with chlorhexidine 0.1% mouthwash for 1-2 weeks 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 21-year-old male presented to the ED with a history of fall on outstretched hand. He experienced severe pain in his right hand. His X-ray is given. What's the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Galeazzi fracture\n- **B)** Smith fracture   C Colles fracture\n- **D)** Monteggia fracture\n- **E)** Barton fracture Explanation\n- X-ray shows extra-articular transverse fracture through distal radius with dorsal angulation.\n- Colle's fractures\n- Mechanism of injury often results from a fall on the outstretched, extended hand.\n- The distal fragment angulated and point dorsally.\n- Clinical features\n- Patients may complain of palmar paresthesias from pressure on the median nerve.\n- Diagnosis;\n- angulation of distal fragment to point more radially on AP view wrist- ray. However normally distal radius tilt 22 degrees towards the ulnar side.\n- lateral view provides the best view of the dorsal angulation and cornminution.\n\n. Management\n- Undisplaced fractures manage with analgesia,immobilize in a backslab POP and elevate with sling and arrange orthopedic follow-up.\n- Reduce under anesthesia (iv sedation , bier's block or GA) if Pt has displaced fracture or significant intra-articular extension.\n- Following reduction apply a backslab POP, while maintaining the reduction with the wrist slightly flexed and pronated.\n- All open and neurovascularly compromised fractures require prompt evaluation by an orthopedic surgeon.\n- Complications\n- weak, stiff, and painful wrist\n- Malunion & cosmetic problem\n- Carpal tunnel syndrome\n- Extensor pollicis longus rupture previous  Next 9\n	\N	[]	\N	f	35	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
81fe168d-4a25-40ba-88ac-e463eb887dc3	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-36	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 36	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 36\n\n- **A)** Gastric lavage\n- **B)** 1M Procyclidine   C\n- IV Atropine\n- **D)** IV Adrenaline\n- **E)** Activate Charcoal EXPIanation\n- Give Atropine: 2 mg intravenously initially, followed by double doses (e.g., 4 mg, 8 mg, 16 mg, etc.) every 5 minutes until secretions are controlled.\n- Management of organophosphate poisoning\n- Follow ABC protocol\n- All patients should have an intravenous line inserted.\n- Decontaminations pry_2Contaminated clothes should be personal belongings.\n\n- Wet - the skin should be washed with water and or soap and stomach contents aspirated if the airway is protected.\n- Note - Decontamination should never take priority over supportive care and resuscitation (ABC protocol)\n- Atropine\n- Atropine: 2 mg intravenously initially, followed by double doses (e.g., 4 mg, 8 mg, 16 mg, etc.) every 5 minutes until secretions are controlled.\n- In case of deliberate ingestion or terrorism/warfare with nerve agent\n- Consider benzodiazepine and pralidoxime with above measures (decontamination, atropine).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 45-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset of shortness of breath and dyspnea. He was recently immobilized for the past 6 days following a tibia and fibula fracture, for which a cast was applied, and surgery was planned. His observations are: BP:  mmHg HR: 130 bpm Sp02: 90% on room air RR: 30 breaths/min\n\nWhat is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis in this patient? A\n- echocardiography\n- **B)** D-dimer   C Chest X-ray\n- **D)** CTPA\n- **E)** Ultrasound of the leg\n- Explanation\n- Recommendations of Echocardiography\n- Use bedside echocardiography for haemodynamically unstable patients who cannot have CT PA.\n- If CTPA is not immediately available or the patient is too unwell to be moved, perform a bedside echocardiography.\n- The presence of any signs of right ventricular (RV) dysfunction is sufficiently suggestive of PE to confirm the diagnosis and justify urgent reperfusion treatment (usually thrombolysis).\n- Do not use echocardiography routinely in haemodynamically stable patients with suspected PE. However, if it has been performed as part of the diagnostic work-up and shows evidence of RV dysfunction, consider further risk stratification by measuring a cardiac biomarker.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 65-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department with acute onset of pain, coldness, and discoloration in the right leg. On examination, the leg is pale and pulseless, with partial paralysis and impaired sensation over the toes and dorsum of the foot. The patient is in significant distress. What is the definitive management?\n- **A)** Discharge with advice to return if symptoms worsen\n- **B)** Administer 5000 units of IV heparin\n- **C)** Perform bedside arterial Doppler and await further imaging\n- **D)** Refer urgently for vascular surgery\n- **E)** Provide analgesia and arrange outpatient vascular follow-up\n- Explanation\n- The definitive management of acute limb ischemia (ALI) involves urgent intervention by a vascular surgeon to restore blood flow to the affected limb. This could involve procedures such as embolectomy, thrombolysis, angioplasty, or bypass surgery, depending on the underlying cause and severity of ischemia.\n\n- Patients presenting with acute limb ischaemia, the Rutherford classification for acute limb ischaemia is recommended for clinical evaluation. Clinical categories of acute limb ischaemia according to Rutherford Grade Category JIA — Viable Immediately threatened - Irreversible senspry.Jgss None None or minimal (toes) More than toes profound, anaesthetic mgtgr.ßeticit None None Mild/moderate profound, paralysis (rigor*) NO immediate threat Salvageable if promptly treated Salvageable if promptly revascularised Major tissue loss amputation. permanent nerve damage inevitable DO Arterial Audible Inaudible Inaudible Inaudible al venous Audible Audible Audible Inaudible\n- Investigation depends on local protocol\n- ankle-brachial index (ABI)\n- segmental pressure examination\n- duplex ultrasound - Is the rnost widely used modality to assess location and degree of stenosis as well as patency of bypass grafts. The sensitivity and specificity of 250% stenosis from the iliac artery to popliteal artery are 90% and 95%.\n- continuous wave Doppler ultrasound\n- catheter angiography\n- CT angiogram - One of the gold standard tests for diagnosis. However, still requires intravenous contrast, although there is less radiation than\n\nwith traditional angiography. sensitivity from 89% to 100% and specificity from 92% to 100% for >50% stenosis.\n- Magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) - One of the gold standard tests for diagnosis. MRA is useful to diagnose anatomical location and stenosis. Sensitivity and specificity of MRA in a meta-analysis to detect a stenosis >50% was 90% to 100%, with the greatest accuracy when gadolinium was used.\n- Investigation to find sources of embolus\n- ECC\n- Echocardiography\n- Aortic, popliteal and femoral artery ultrasound\n- En-management\n- Analgesia - consider IV opiates\n- Oxygen — supplemental oxygen is recommended in all patient with acute limb ischaemia\n- Heparin intravenous administration of unfractionated heparin (IJFH): initially 5 000 IU, or 70-100 IU/kg, followed by infusion, dose adjusted to patient response, and monitored by activated clotting time or activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT). The aim is to reduce further embolism or clot propagation, and to provide an anti-inflammatory effect.\n- IV Fluids - For patients with acute limb ischaemia awaiting revascularisation, adequate analgesia and intravenous rehydration are recommended.\n- Refer —to vascular surgeon for definitive treatment after initial measure\n\nSpecialist management\n- Revasculrisation - recommened in Rutherford Grade I to 11b category of acute limb ischaemia.\n- Revasculrisation, (endovascular therapy/Thrombo- embolectomy/thrombolysis) however not recommends in Rutherford Grade III category of acute limb ischaemia.\n- The option in this case are either amputation or palliation. In an acute scenario with evidence of infection a so-called guillotine amputation may be performed in order to allow a quick operation to remove necrotic/infected tissue, with a definitive amputation at a later date once the patient is more stable and infection has been treated.\n- Revascularisation in patient with Rutherford Grade III category will be fatal, due to the massive release of potassium, creatine kinase, myoglobin, lactate and oxygen free radicals from the ischaemic tissue which can cause renal failure, myocardial toxicity and multi-organ failure.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA six weeks old baby presents with his parents following a floppy episode lasting less than 30 seconds while feeding associated with pallor. He is back to his normal self and has since had a normal feed. He was born at 37 weeks and had an uncomplicated neonatal period. He has been otherwise well. Head to toe examination and his observations are unremarkable. His BM is 5.3. He is diagnosed with a brief resolved unexplained event (BRUE).\n\nWhich factor makes this a high-risk episode?\n- **A)** Gestational age of 37 weeks   B, Six weeks old\n- **C)** Episode of pallor   D\n- Floppy episode\n- **E)** Episode lasting 30 seconds Explanation\n- Infant< 2 months is high risk\n\nRisk stratification Low risk days of age Born weeks' gestation and have a corrected age Of weeks. No CPR given by trained practitioner CI minute duration First event High risk\n- Infant< 2 months\n- Hx of prematurity less than 32 weeks at birth More than one event\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 60-year-old man brought to the ED with the complaint of dizziness and an episode of syncope. His heart rate was 43bpm and BP  mmHg was recorded. He admitted beta blocker for his heart condition. Which of the following is most appropriate next step of management for this patient? A\n- Atropine 500mcg\n- **B)** Set up an adrenaline infusion at 2-10 mcg/min\n- **C)** External pacing\n- **D)** Give Img glucagon\n- **E)** Atropine 3mg\n- Explanation\n- IV atropine remains the first choice if patient presents with adverse features. Intravenous glucagon [unlicensed] is a treatment option for severe hypotension, heart failure, or cardiogenic shock in beta blocker overdose.\n- Bradygar.dja-(Resus-counciLof-theNK-and-Eur-opean-r.esuscitatjon council guideline)\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock, Syncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n- If treatment with atropine is ineffective\n- consider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 - 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECG carefully for the presence of P waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing.\n- If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA mother runs into the ED with her three years old daughter who she found unresponsive. You confirm she is in cardiac arrest and initiate APLS. Chest pads are applied. Which of the following correct about DC shock?\n- **A)** Synchronous DC 4j/kg\n- **B)** Synchronous Ij/kg\n- **C)** Asynchronous DC 4j/kg\n- **D)** Asynchronous DC IO j/kg\n- **E)** Synchronous DC 4j/kg\n\nExplanation\n- APLS recommends asynchronous 4j/kg shock in cardiac arrest.\n- APLS recommendation for cardiac arrhythmia for-example SVT. Give first synchronised DC shock Ij/kg and second shock 2j/kg, consider up to 4j/kg.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 12-year-old boy brought to the ED with anaphylactic shock. Patient has given one dose of adrenaline and patient condition has been improved. However, following 6 hours monitoring in the ED patient condition has been deteriorated. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Give second dose of adrenaline\n- **B)** IV hydrocortisone\n- **C)** Set-up adrenaline infusion\n- **D)** Increase the dose of adrenaline\n- **E)** Need of intensive care admission Explanation r n lin if n ph)dactic-reac.t-o— i n persist-after 5 minutes interval. After two doses, adrenaline infusion should be\n\nsta rted.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 65-year-old female presents with chest pain and intermittent syncope. Her heart rate is 35 bpm and BP  mmHg for which atropine 500mcg was given and her rhythm strip is shown below. Which of the following would be the most appropriate next step in her management?   A\n- Repeat atropine dose\n- **B)** Set up an adrenaline infusion at 2-10 mcg/min\n- **C)** External pacing\n- **D)** Observe Patient on cardia monitor\n- **E)** Cive glucagon 1 mg Explanation\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs, give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 72-year-old female brought to the ED with the complaint of dizziness and an episode of syncope. On examination, she is found to be bradycardic with a heart rate of 45bpm and BP  mmHg was recorded for which Atropine total 3mg IV was given, however, its unsuccessful. ECG given. Which of the following is most appropriate next step management in this patient?\n- **A)** Repeat atropine\n- **B)** Admitted her for permanent pacing\n- **C)** Refer to cardiologist D\n- External pacing\n- **E)** Observe her on cardiac monitor Explanation\n- ECG reveals Mobitz type-2 which increase the risk of asystole. Therefore , should be initially treated with atropine if atropine ineffective then consider second-line drugs or cardiac pacing.\n\n- Bradycardia (Resus council of the UK and European resuscitation-E6ü6EiCßüidéji6éj¯\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock, Syncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure as mentioned above) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n- If treatrnent with atropine is ineffective\n- consider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 - 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECG carefully for the presence of P waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing.\n- If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment.\n\nIO. A 43-year-old male brought to the ED by paramedics after accidental exposure to organophosphate over the skin. His observations are: BP:  mmHg Pulse: 58bpm After wearing PPE, what would be your next immediate step?\n- **A)** Shift to the side room   B\n- Dry decontamination\n- **C)** Gastric lavage\n- **D)** Pralidoxime\n- **E)** Wet decontamination Explanation\n- Management of organophosphate poisoning\n- Resuscitation + supportive care\n\n- Cardiorespiratory resuscitation, intravenous fluids, airway maintenance, and ventilation should be used early on. All patients should have an intravenous line inserted.\n- Frequent observations are needed, as a rapid deterioration may be seen, even in patients with initially minor symptoms.\n- Gastric aspiration or lavage may be performed if the airway is protected. However, organophosphates are rapidly absorbed, and there is no evidence to support the effectiveness of gastric aspiration or lavage.\n- Evidence suggests that activated charcoal is ineffective in reducing clinical effects.\n- Decontaminations\n- pry_2Contaminated clothes should be removed and or containing personal belongings.\n- Wet - the skin should be washed with water and or soap and stomach contents aspirated if the airway is protected.\n- Note - Decontamination should never take priority over supportive care and resuscitation (ABC protocol)\n- Atropine\n- Atropine: 2 mg intravenously initially, followed by double doses (e.g., 4 mg, 8 mg, 16 mg, etc.) every 5 minutes until secretions are controlled.\n- Antidote\n- Pralidoxime\n\n- In case of deliberate ingestion or terrorism/warfare with nerve-agent\n- Consider benzodiazepine and pralidoxime with above measures (decontamination, atropine). Resuscitation supportive care Plus Decontaminations Plus Atropine\n\nprevious  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	36	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
3bd1e215-67dd-46e5-a810-cfa5664e2791	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-37	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 37	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 37\n\nWhat is your next best step of management?\n- **A)** Administer 5000 units of IV heparin\n- **B)** Refer urgently for vascular surgery\n- **C)** Perform bedside arterial Doppler and await further imaging\n- **D)** Provide analgesia and arrange outpatient vascular follow-up\n- **E)** Discharge with advice to return if symptoms worsen\n- Explanation\n- The best next step in management for this patient is to administer 5000 units of IV heparin.\n- En-management-of-acute-limb-ischaemia-\n- Analgesia - consider IV opiates\n- oxygen is recommended in all patient with acute limb ischaemia\n- Heparin intravenous administration of unfractionated heparin (UFH): initially 5 000 IU, or 70-100 IU/kg, followed by infusion, dose adjusted to patient response, and monitored by activated clotting time or activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT). The aim is to reduce further embolism or clot propagation, and to provide an anti-inflammatory effect.\n- IV Fluids - For patients with acute limb ischaemia awaiting revascularisation, adequate analgesia and intravenous rehydration are\n\nrecommended. Refer —to vascular surgeon for definitive treatment after initial measure\n- Specialist-management\n- Revasculrisation - recommended in Rutherford Grade I to 11b category of acute limb ischaemia.\n- Revasculrisation, (endovascular therapy/Thrombo- embolectomy/thrombolysis) however not recommends in Rutherford Grade III category of acute limb ischaemia.\n- The option in this case are either amputation or palliation. In an acute scenario with evidence of infection a so-called guillotine amputation may be performed in order to allow a quick operation to remove necrotic/infected tissue, with a definitive amputation at a later date once the patient is more stable and infection has been treated.\n- Revascularisation in patient with Rutherford Grade Ill category will be fatal, due to the massive release of potassium, creatine kinase, myoglobin, lactate and oxygen free radicals from the ischaemic tissue which can cause renal failure, myocardial toxicity and multi-organ failure\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA mother presents with an 18hours old baby in the ED with worsening jaundice who has been discharged 8 hours ago. On examination, his observations are within normal limit except for marked jaundice. What would be your management plan?\n- **A)** Reassurance & discharge\n- **B)** Admit for further assessment\n- **C)** Refer tp paeditric surgeon\n- **D)** Do heel prick test\n- **E)** Temporary breastfeeding cessation EXPIanation;\n- Arrange urgent admission to a neonatal or paediatric unit-within 2 hours if jaundice first appears at less than 24 hours of age. Jaundice\n- Jaundice(also known as icterus) describes the yellow pigmentation of the skin, sclera, and mucous membranes resulting from raised plasma bilirubin.\n- Normal plasma bilirubin levels are below 21 micromol/L.\n- Clinical jaundice may not become apparent until serum bilirubin is greater than 51 micromol/L.\n\nBreastmilk jaundice\n- It is prolongation of physiological jaundice in breastfed babies.\n- It usually presents within the first week of life (day 2—4) and peaks on day 7—15. In some cases, it can persist for up to 12 weeks. The mechanism is unknown, but possible reasons include a decreased caloric intake and inhibition of hepatic bilirubin excretion Same as in physiological factors Physiological jaundice\n- It usually appears at 2 days of age, peaks on days 3—5, and then decreases, usually by around day 10 Physiological jaundice can occur in breastfed and formula-fed babies and results from increased bilirubin bad, decreased uptake by the liver, decreased conjugation in the liver, and decreased excretion into bile.\n- Decreased gestational age/preterm delivery.\n- Low infant birth weight.\n- Development of jaundice within first 24 hours Of life.\n- Male sex\n- Visible bruising, Cephalhaematoma\n- Maternal age older than 25 years.\n- Maternal diabetes mellitus.\n- Asian, European, or native American ethnicity.\n- Sibling born with jaundice requiring phototherapy/other treatment.\n- Dehydration.\n- Poor caloric intakefincreased neonatal weight loss\n- Breastfeeding Pathological jaundice\n- it usually appears less than 24 hours.\n- It can be caused bv a number of factors. including:\n- most commonly Rhesus or ABO incompatibility.\n- biliary atresia Sepsis\n- Metabolic disorders (for example galactosaemia, hereditary fructose intolerance, alpha- 1 antitrypsin deficiency, hypothyroidism)\n- Gilbert's syndrome and Crigler-Najjar syndrome\n- G6PD deficiency. Same Manaqement of Physiological iaundice Reassurance & Advice leaflet Manaqement of Breastmilk iaundice First line Reassurance Temporary breast-feeding cessation and supplemental feeding maybe considered. and hydration. Third-line — exchange transfusion Manaqement of Patholoqical iaundice Arrange emerqencv admission - if there is jaundice with features of bilirubin encephalopathy (for example atypical sleepiness, poor feeding, irritability, vomiting, hypotonia followed by hypertonia).\n\n24 hours of age. -within 2 hours if jaundice first appears at less man—I Arrange urqent admission to a neonatal or paediatric unit - as soon as possible and to be seen within 6 hours (using clinical judgement regarding more urgent referral Or admission) if: Jaundice first appears at more than 7 days of age. The neonate is unwell (for example, lethargy, fever, vomiting, irritability). Gestational age is of less than 35 weeks. prolonged jaundice is suspected — that is a gestational age Of less than 37 weeks with more than 21 days Of jaundice; or a gestational age of 37 weeks or more with more than 14 days of jaundice. There are feeding problems and/or concerns about weight, particularly in breastfed infants. Pale stools and dark urine are noted. Specialist treatment include Phototherapy, hydration and exchange transfusion (if not respond to phototherapy) Newborn blood spot test Every baby is offered newborn blood spot screening, also known as the heel prick test, ideally when they're 5 days old.\n- When the baby is 5 days old, a healthcare professional will prick their heel and collect 4 drops of blood on a special card.\n- Newborn blood spot screening involves taking a blood sample to find out if your baby has I of 9 rare but serious health conditions. which includes:\n- Sickle cell disease\n- Cystic fibrosis\n- Congenital hypothyroidism\n- Inherited metabolic disease these are:\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Phenylketonuria (PKU) medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (MCADD) maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) isovaleric acidaemia (IVA) glutaric aciduria type 1 (GAI) homocystinuria (pyridoxine unresponsive) (HCU)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nWhich of the following is a non-notifiable disease?   A\n- Epstein-barr virus   B\n- Small pox\n- **C)** Measles\n- **D)** Rubella\n- **E)** Pertussis Explanation; List of notifiable diseases Diseases notifiable to local authority proper officers under the Health Protection (Notification) Regulations 2010: Acute encephalitis\n- Acute infectious hepatitis\n- Acute meningitis\n- Acute poliomyelitis\n- Anthrax\n- Botulism\n-\n- Brucellosis\n\n- Cholera\n- COVID-19 Diphtheria Enteric fever (typhoid or paratyphoid fever)\n- Food poisoning\n- Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS)\n- Infectious bloody diarrhoea\n- Invasive group A streptococcal disease\n- Legionnaires' disease\n- Leprosy\n- Malaria\n- Meningococcal septicaemia\n- Monkeypox\n- M u m ps\n- Plague\n- Rabies\n- Rubella\n- Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS)\n- Scarlet fever\n- Smallpox\n- Tetanus\n- Tuberculosis\n- Typhus\n-\n- Viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF) Whooping cough\n- Yellow fever\n-\n\nReport other diseases that may present significant risk to human health under the category 'other significant disease\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nAn 88-year-old female brought into the ED by her daughter. She reports that she is not well for the last few days. While in ED she was shouting and repeatedly saying she is seeing someone near the door. What would be the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Delirium\n- **B)** Alcohol intoxication\n- **C)** Obsessive compulsive disorder\n- **D)** Personality disorder   E\n- Dementia Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of delirium Delirium\n\n- Delirium(sometimes called 'acute confusional state')is an acute, fluctuating syndrome of encephalopathy causing disturbed consciousness, attention, cognition, and perception.\n- Delirium usually develops over hours to days and behavioural disturbance, personality changes, and psychotic features may occur\n- Delirium typically occurs in people with predisposing factors(such as advanced age or multiple comorbidities) when new precipitating factors(such as some medications or infection) are added.\n- Classification Hyperactive delirium— may present with inappropriate behaviour, hallucinations, or agitation.\n- Restlessness and wandering are common:\n- Hypoactive delirium (more common)— may present with lethargy and reduced concentration and appetite. The person may appear quiet or withdrawn\n- Mixed delirium— signs and symptoms of both hyperactive and hypoactive subtypes are present.\n- Risk factors\n\nPredisposing factors\n- Older age (over 65 years). Cognitive impairment (such as dementia).\n- Frailty/multiple comorbidities (such as stroke or heart failure). Significant injuries such as hip fracture.\n- Functional impairment (for example immobility or the use of physical restraints such as cot sides).\n- Iatrogenic events (such as bladder catheterization, polypharmacy, or surgery).\n- History of, or current, alcohol excess.\n- Sensory impairment (such as visual impairment or hearing loss). Poor nutrition.\n- Lack of stimulation.\n- Terminal phase Of illness\n- Clinical features Precipitating factors\n- Infection - urinary tract infection, infected pressure sore, or pneumonia.\n- Metabolic disturbance - hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia (including advanced carcinomatosis), or electrolyte abnormalities (including dehydration).\n- Cardiovascular disorders - MI or heart failure. Respiratory disorders - pulmonary embolism or exacerbation COPD Neurological disorders - stroke, encephalitis, or subdural haematoma.\n- Endocrine disorders - thyroid dysfunction or Cushing's syndrome.\n- Urological disorders - urinary retention.\n- Gastrointestinal disorders - hepatic failure, constipation or malnutrition.\n- Severe uncontrolled pain.\n- Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal Medications — antihistamine, antipsychotic, antidepressant, antispasmodics, NSAIDs , anti- hypertensive, corticosteroid. Psychosocial factors - depression, sleep deprivation, visual or hearing impairment, emotional stress, or change of environment\n- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:\n- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.\n- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.\n- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.\n\n- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.\n- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).\n- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal).\n- Investigations\n- Delirium is a clinical diagnosis based on a detailed history, examination, and relevant investigations\n- Confirm a diagnosis of deliriumby carrying out a cognitive assessment based on the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) criteria, the short Confusion Assessment Method (short-CAM), or the 4A's test\n- Arrange targeted investigations based on findings from the history and examination, for example:\n- Urinalysis— to identify conditions such as infection or hyperglycaemia\n- CXR or sputum culture- For chest infection\n- FBC —for anaemia\n- Management\n\n- Most people with delirium should be admitted for urgent assessment, close monitoring, and treatment.\n- If the person is deemed to not have capacity to consent, decisions should be made in the best interests of the person using the Mental Capacity Act 2005.\n- If the person with delirium develops challenging behaviour (such as\n- Address any underlying causes for the behaviour (such as discomfort, thirst, or need for the toilet).\n- Advise moving the person to a safe, Iow-stimulation environment (such as a quiet room).\n- Advise use of verbal and non-verbal de-escalation techniques (such as active listening, effective verbal responding, pictures, and symbols).\n- If these measures fail, consider pharmacological measure and seek advice from an elderly care psychiatrist, or elderly care physician. Depending on the specific situation (for example the person is a danger to themselves or to others).\n- NICE recommends - Short-term (for 1 week or less) low-dose haloperidol\n- Antipsychotic drugs should be avoided, or used with caution in Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies\n- Managemencprinciples in end-of-life care include:\n- Considering non-pharmacological management of delirium in a person in the last days of life.\n\n- Treating any reversible causes of delirium, for example psychological causes or certain metabolic disorders (for example renal failure or hyponatraemia).\n- Considering a trial of an antipsychotic medicine (such as haloperidol or levomepromazine) to manage delirium, in the presence of intractable distress.\n- Monitoring the benefits and side effects of these medicines at least daily and adjust the prescription as the clinical situation changes.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 60-year-old male presented to the ED complaining of suprapubic pain and dysuria. He has a history of recurrent Foley's catheter insertion for urethral stricture. His urine dipstick revealed blood. What would be the likely diagnosis in this patient?   A\n- Bladder stone O\n- **B)** UTI\n- **C)** Bladder carcinoma\n- **D)** Schtisomiasis\n- **E)** Renal carcinoma Explanation;\n\n- The patient has urethral stricture and a history of recurrent use of foley's catheter which commonly causes IJTI and urinary stones (nephrolithiasis, urolithiasis).\n- Here in this case, the presence of haematuria in urine dipsticks can support the diagnosis of bladder stones\n- The presence of nitrite (with or without leucocyte esterase) in the urine dipstick suggests a urinary tract infection Bladder Stones\n- Clinical features Lower abdominal pain\n- frequent urge to urinate\n- painful or difficult urination\n- hematuria\n- Causes\n- Neurogenic bladder\n-\n- Bladder diverticulum\n- Urinary tract infections Enlarged prostate, and kidney stones\n- Risk Factors\n- Being born male\n-\n- Older age, radiation\n- Bladder augmentation Urethral strictures\n- Schistosomiasis\n-\n\n- Recurrent UT Is\n- Using a Foley catheter\n- Diagnosis Physical exam, medical history, urinalysis, blood test, CT/MRI\n- Treatment\n- Medication, ureteral stenting, nephrostomy, shock wave lithotripsy, ureteroscopy Urethral stricture\n- A urethral stricture is a narrowing caused by scarring of the lining of the urethra and the surrounding corpus spongiosum.\n- Strictures can range from less than lcm long to those that extend the entire length of the urethra.\n- They can occur at any point along the urethra, but are most often seen in the bulbar region. Causes\n- Idiopathic - Cause unknown\n- Inflammatory - Caused by infection or inflammation in the urethra, e.g:\n- sexually-transmitted infections (STIS) e.g. gonorrhoea\n-\n\nchronic inflammation e.g. lichen sclerosus (LS) also known as balanitis\n- xerotica obliterans (BXO) Traumatic - Caused by injury to the urethra, e.g:\n- a fall astride the cross-bar of a bicycle\n- other crushing pelvic trauma\n- Iatrogenic - Caused by medical intervention, e.g.\n- repeated urological procedures that involve passage of instruments\n- along the urethra urethral catheterisation\n- radiotherapy or surgery to adjacent organs\n- Congenital - Inherited conditions e.g.\n- hypospadias (or as a result of previous surgery to correct hypospadias)\n- Clinical features\n-\n- With minor strictures, patient may not have any symptoms. With tighter strictures, you may experience one or more of the following: Poor urinary flow and needing to strain to pass urine\n-\n- Spraying or splitting of the urinary stream Dribbling of urine after urination\n- Needing to pass urine often (urinary frequency)\n-\n- Urinary tract infections Reduced force of ejaculation and low ejaculate volume; and\n- Pain on passing urine.\n-\n- [f left untreated; A stricture can act as a "bottleneck" which prevents complete bladder\n- emptying. This can cause to retain urine in the bladder after patient\n\npass urine, which can result in bladder, prostate or kidney infections, and lead to the formation of stones. All these conditions can cause long-term bladder damage and kidney failure.\n- Diagnosis\n- A stricture may be diagnosed by:\n- examination - especially if the narrowing is visible at the tip of the penis\n- failure to pass a telescope or catheter - during investigation of patient urinary symptoms, especially when the stricture is close to the bladder\n- A urethrogram - an X-ray performed using contrast medium (dye) put into urethra to show the anatomy\n- An ultrasound scan; and an MRI scan Bladder Bulbar stricture\n- Management\n\n- Observation only - If symptoms are not troublesome, we may simply monitor and intervene only if patient symptoms deteriorate or risk harming patient's longterm health.\n- Urethral dilatation\n- Under anaesthetic, urologist pass a plastic or metal dilator into urethra to stretch the narrow area.\n- Internal (optical) urethrotomy_\n- Under an anaesthetic, urologist pass a telescope through urethra to cut the stricture.\n- urethroplasty\n- It involves repairing the diseased section of urethra to stop the stricture from coming back.\n- mongterm urethral-catheter-or-suprapubic-catheter\n- If patient has multiple medical problems or wish to avoid invasive treatment, urologist may recommend putting in a long-term catheter.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 35-year-old male presented to the ED after stabbed wound in the peri- umbilical region.\n\nHis vitals are: BP; 80/50mmhg HR: 110bpm What would be your next step of management?   A\n- Refer to surgery\n- **B)** Observe in the ED and discharge   C\n- IV morphine\n- **D)** IV fluid bolus\n- **E)** One litter of blood\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  An emergent laparotomy is indicated in this patient, therefore, refer to surgery is required.\n- Any Penetrating injury with haemodynamic instability required urgent laparotomy.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 35 years old female who is paraplegic following a spinal cord injury, is brought into the ED with complaint of blurred vision. She has not opened her\n\nbowels for few days. She has a long-term catheter. Her heart rate is 55bpm, BP 147/89mmHg, and a bladder scan showed a 70ml residual volume. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Guillain-Barre syndrome\n- **B)** Cauda equina syndrome\n- **C)** Transverse myelitis D\n- Autonomic dysreflexia\n- **E)** Sciatica Explanation\n- check the autonomic dysreflexia module in major traurna.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA mother presents with her 4-day-old son due to his worsening jaundice. She is a healthy 30-year-old with blood type O positive and a negative direct antibody test (DAT). She received adequate prenatal care, and her laboratory results were normal, including a negative group B streptococcus culture. The neonate has been exclusively breastfeeding on an every-4-hour schedule. His birth weight was 7 lbs 11 oz (3500 g), and his current weight is 6 lbs 13 oz (3100 g). He has\n\nhad 1 stool and 3 wet diapers in the last 24 hours. Physical exam findings are within normal limits except for marked jaundice. His blood tests show: total serum bilirubin: 16 mg/dL direct bilirubin: hematocrit: 0.8 mg/dL 55% reticulocyte count: 2.8%. What is the most likely mechanism of jaundice in this neonate?\n- **A)** Pregnanediol in breast milk and decreased bilirubin conjugation\n- **B)** Maternal immunoglobulin G-mediated hemolysis\n- **C)** Beta-glucuronidase in breast milk and increased enterohepatic circulation\n- **D)** Suboptimal intake with dehydration and increased enterohepatic circulation\n- **E)** Pregnanediol in breast milk and increased bilirubin conjugation Explanation;\n\n- This neonate has breastfeeding jaundice secondary to suboptimal intake, with a weight loss of about 11%. Breastfeeding jaundice typically peaks between 3 and 5 days of age and is often associated with a weight loss greater than 10%.\n- Suboptimal breast milk intake results in dehydration and increased enterohepatic circulation, allowing unconjugated bilirubin to reenter the circulation and elevate the serum bilirubin concentration. Treatrnent involves improving the infant's hydration status with on- demand feeding at least 8 times daily. The mother's state of hydration, milk supply, and the baby's latching and swallowing techniques must be assessed. The bilirubin level will usually return to normal within a few days. Phototherapy may occasionally be required.\n- Exclusive breastfeeding is recommended. The mother can supplement with pumped breastmilk, donor breastmilk, or infant formula if suboptimal intake persists. Additional water and dextrose water are not indicated.\n- IgG-mediated hemolysis is seen in hemolytic disease of the newborn due to blood group incompatibility. Pregnanediol and beta- glucuronidase are responsible for breast milk jaundice lasting up to 3 months, accompanied by adequate intake and weight gain.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA paramedic calls you to bring a 4kg collapsed child to ED who is in cardiac arrest. What would be the correct adrenaline dose for this patient? O\n- **A)** 0.2nnl\n- **C)** 0.3ml\n- **D)** 0.6nnl\n- **E)** 0.4ml Explanation; {he recom ended IV/IO dose of adrenaline in children is 10 mcg kg (0.1mLkg ofl in 10,000 solution). The child's weight is 4kg, therefore, 4 x IO = 40 mcg ( 0.4ml)\n- Another way to memorize: weight is 5kg then the adrenaline dose will be 0.5mI.\n- Similarly, if the weight is 3.5kg adrenaline dose would be 0.35mI\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 52-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department with dizziness. He has a history of alcohol dependence and known chronic liver disease. On examination, his temperature is 360C, heart rate 118 bpm, respiratory rate 19, and oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. His blood pressure is  mmHg when lying and  mmHg when standing. A cannula is inserted, and intravenous antibiotics and terlipressin are administered. The major hemorrhage protocol has been activated. What is the most appropriate next step in his management?\n- **A)** Arrange an urgent endoscopy post immediate resuscitation\n- **B)** Ensure he receives tranexamic acid\n- **C)** Ensure he receives tranexamic acid\n- **D)** Undertake a rectal examination\n- **E)** Omeprazole 40mg IV\n- Explanation\n- Offer endoscopy to unstable patients with severe acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding immediately after resuscitation.\n- Offer endoscopy within 24 hours of admission to all other patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\n- Initial management of UCIB\n- UGIB causing hypotension, tachycardia, orthostasis, or other signs of hypovolemic shock must be managed swiftly, and patients should be considered for admission to the intensive care unit.\n- Resuscitation\n- Two large-bore intravenous lines should be placed immediately for adequate venous access.\n- Crystalloid fluids - should be infused to maintain adequate blood pressure.\n\n- Blood product transfusions - Transfuse patients with massive bleeding with blood, platelets and clotting factors in line with local protocols for managing massive bleeding.\n- Do not offer platelet transfusion to patients who are not actively bleeding and are haemodynamically stable.\n- Offer platelet transfusion to patients who are actively bleeding and have a platelet count of less than 50 x 109/1itre.\n- Offer fresh frozen plasma to patients who are actively bleeding and have a prothrombin time (or international normalised ratio) or activated partial thromboplastin time greater than 1.5 times normal. If a patient's fibrinogen level remains less than 1.5 g/litre despite fresh frozen plasma use, offer cryoprecipitate as well.\n- Offer prothrombin complex concentrate to patients who are taking warfarin and actively bleeding.\n- Timing of endoscopy\n- Offer endoscopy to unstable patients with severe acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding immediately after resuscitation.\n- Offer endoscopy within 24 hours of admission to all other patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	37	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b034dab5-1035-4260-8f46-eeacef469ecc	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-38	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 38	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 38\n\n- **B)** Cyanide poisoning C' Methemoglobinemia\n- **D)** Cocaine overdose D\n- **E)** MDMA Explanation\n- Patient clinical feature; cyanosed and low saturation Sp02 88% which does not improve with supplemental oxygen suggestive of methaemoglobinaemia. Methemoglobinemia\n- Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood.\n- Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2+) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3+). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%\n- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital or acquired\n- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood . Causes\n- Benzocaine . Dapsone\n- Nitroglycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin\n- Nitroprusside\n- Phenazopyridine\n\n- Sodium Nitrite\n- Amyl nitrite\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")\n- Clinical features\n- Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:\n- < 10% - None (patients with underlying diseases may have more symptoms at lower level)\n- 10-20% - Slight discoloration (eg, pale, gray, blue) of the skin.\n- Chocolate brown colored blood\n- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia, lightheadedness\n- 30-50% - Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain\n- 50-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status, delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis\n- > 70% - Usually, death\n- Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is >1.5 g/dl\n- Inyestigations\n- VBG or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in a fresh sample of blood.\n- Caution on estimating oxygen level - Routine pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%.\n- Pa02 readings on blood gases may be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia\n\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible.\n- Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary) or anyone who has a methemoglobin level\n- Give methylene blue: 1-2 mg/kg (0.1-0.2 mL/kg of solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes\n- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms.\n- Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.\n- Management in G6PD deficient patients\n- Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis.\n- Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion\n- Key_points\n- Methemoglobinemia can result from exposure to a number of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).\n- Consider the diagnosis Methemoglobinemia in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.\n- Administer methylene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs, metabolic acidosis, end organ dysfunction or, a serum level > 25%\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 65-year-old brought into the ED with compliant palpitation and dizziness. His ECG is given. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** DC cardioversion   B\n- Beta blockers\n- **C)** Adenosine\n- **D)** Amiodarone\n- **E)** Mgs04 Explanation\n- ECG_finding;\n- Normal sinus rhythm\n- Non-sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a rate of slightly less than 300.\n- Management — depends on clinical features, usually patients are asymptomatic, hence required reassurance and monitoring only. Symptomatic cause without electrolyte abnormality require beta blockers or calcium channel blockers therapy as the first line.\n- Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance is the cause.\n- ICD (implantable cardioverter defibrillator) if criteria match.\n\n- Management of Non — sustained ventricular tachycardia\n- Re-assurance + self-monitoring - NSVT is by definition a self-terminating NO cardiac comorbidity: Asymptomatic No cardiac comorbidity: Symptomatic Post-MI idiopathic dilated or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy event, and therefore usually no specific treatment is indicated Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance is the cause. Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance the cause. Symptomatic Without electrolvte abnormality:\n- line - Beta-blockers (eg_, metoprolol, atenolol) or calcium-channel blockers (generally used when beta-blockers are contraindicated, e.g., asthma) are usually sufficient to control symptoms. line - Catheter ablation above ineffective\n- 3«' line Antiarrhythmic agents such as flecainide or propafenone or amiodarone if above ineffective or who are not candidates for catheter\n- With or Without heart failure Early re-perfusion + optimisation of medical therapy - Early re-perfusion decreases overall prevalence of NSVT following acute MI. Optimisation of medical therapy including beta-blockers. ACE inhibitors. antiplatelet therapy. and statins, reduces the risk of sudden cardiac death following acute MI\n- Life style modification - Modifiable risk factors such as obesity and smoking should be addressed with a carefully planned exercise. diet, and weight loss programme.\n- Consider ICD in heart failure patients. ICD is recommended for patients who are at least 40 days post-MI with class  heart failure, left ventricular EF (LVEF) 35% Or less,\n- 1st line - implantable cardioverter defibrillator (ICD)\n- ICD placement should be considered for patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and one Of the major risk factors for sudden cardiac death, including sudden cardiac arrest, spontaneous sustained VT, unexplained syncope, family history Of sudden cardiac death in a first-degree relative.\n- It should be also implant in patient With\n- If\n- Implantable cardioverter defibrillator (ICD) is not indicated for patients With NSVT and idiopathic cardiomyopathy With good left ventricular function. Only Close observation and monitoring required.\n- Note —for more detail check ventricular arrhythmia module in cardiology.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 42-year-old female presents with a severely painful, red left eye and blurred vision. She wears contact lenses but denies any trauma. She is systemically well. An image is shown. After providing analgesia, what is the most appropriate next step in her management?\n- **A)** Prescribe topical chloramphenicol and advise GP follow-up\n- **B)** Oral acyclovir and self-referral to ophthalmology if no improvement\n- **C)** Prescribe topical ganciclovir and steroid eye drops\n- **D)** prescribe topical ganciclovir and self-referral to ophthalmology if no improvement\n- **E)** Refer to ophthalmology for same-day review\n- Contact lens wear is a well-known risk factor for bacterial keratitis, especially due to organisms like Pseudomonas aeruginosa.\n- It can also predispose to fungal or Acanthamoeba keratitis, though these are less common.\n- A dendritic ulcer is the hallmark of herpetic keratitis and would show a branching pattern with terminal bulbs under fluorescein staining. However, in above case (question) its not the branching pattern.\n\n- Microbial keratitis (bacterial, fungal)\n- Microbial keratitis is a potentially sight-threatening infection of the cornea. Bacterial cause\n- The commonest bacterial corneal pathogens are:\n- Pseudomonas sp. (Gram -ve)\n- Staphylococcus sp. (Gram +ve)\n- Streptococcus sp.(Cram +ve)\n- other Gram -ve organisms\n- Note: severe contact lens-related infections tend to be Cram -ve, particularly Pseudomonas species.\n- EungaLgause\n- Most common fungal corneal pathogens are:\n- Candidasp. (yeast-like)\n- Fusariumsp. (filamentous)\n- Aspergillussp (filamentous)\n- Predisposingfictors\n- Bacterial keratitis is usually associated with one or more of the following:\n- contact lens wear (incidence 4—5 times higher in CL wearers than non- wea rers)\n- increased days Of wear\n- overnight wear\n- poor hand, lens and storage case hygiene\n- youth\n- male gender\n- smoking\n- internet purchase of lenses, particularly cosmetic lenses\n\n- ocular surface disease, including:\n- corneal exposure\n- corneal decompensation\n- chronic epithelial defect or recurrent corneal erosions\n- neurotrophic keratopathy, e.g. secondary to HSK or diabetes\n- ocular trauma or surgery, including loose or broken sutures\n- Immune compromse\n- topical steroid use\n- lid margin infection (usually Staphyloccocal)\n- Clinical features Svmptoms of microbial keratitis (bacterial. funqal)\n- Usually unilateral\n- Pain, moderate to severe (usually acute onset, rapid progression)\n- Redness, photophobia (may be severe). watering/discharge, blurred vision (especially if lesion on or near visual axis)\n- Awareness of white or yellow spot on cornea Siqns of microbial keratitis (bacterial. funqal)\n- Lid oedema\n- Epiphora\n- Discharge (mucopurulent or purulent)\n- Reduced visual acuity\n- Conjunctival hyperaemia and infiltration\n- Corneal lesion usually single (central or mid- peripheral) o o o o excavation of epithelium, Bowman's layer, stroma (ulcer resulting from tissue necrosis) stromal infiltration beneath lesion stromal oedema with folds in Descemet's membrane endothelial fibrin plaque beneath lesion Optical coherence tomography (OCT) may be helpful in determining depth of involvement\n\n- Fungal keratitis produces similar signs to bacterial keratitis; however, it has been claimed that deep lesions, those having a feathery edge, raised profile, presence of satellite lesions and the presence of endothelial plaque are all features suggestive of a fungal as opposed to a bacterial infection. Hypopyon is seen in more than 50% of cases. Fungal keratitis may develop more slowly (however Fusarium infection can progress rapidly and invasively)\n- ED management\n- Cease contact lens wear - Warn contact lens wearers not to discard their lenses or lens cases, but to retain them if needed for culture\n- Analgesia—if required\n- Emergency (same day) referral is indicted if any of the following signs are present:\n- infiltrate >lmm\n- 2 or more adjacent lesions\n- location 3mm or less from corneal centre\n- AC reaction (210 cells in a Imm beam (2 1+ on the SUN scale)\n- signs suggestive or fungal or acanthamoeba keratitis\n- high likelihood of poor patient compliance to treatment\n- These sight-threatening characteristics are indications for corneal scraping for microbiological Cram stain and culture, which should be performed prior to initiating antimicrobial therapy\n- Eyedrops=Empirical treatment for lesions (<lmm) in the absence of the above clinical characteristics is based fluoroquinolone monotherapy e.g. gutt levofloxacin/moxifloxacin/ofloxacin hourly day and night for 48 hours, then every 2 hours daily for 72 hours, then every 4 hours for 7 days (The term "gutt therapy" refers to topical eye drop treatment). Monitor closely during this period and refer same day if not healing or if symptoms worsen.\n\n- Fungal-keratitis-management;\n- emergency (same day) referral to ophthalmologist without intervention if fungal keratitis is suspected,_after cease contact lens wear and analgesia.\n- In summary_\n- Bacterial keratitis:\n- Correct to suspect in contact lens wearers who are not immunocompromised.\n- However, bacterial keratitis should also be considered in any patient with corneal trauma, pre-existing ocular surface disease, or post- surgical complications. Contact lens-associated bacterial keratitis is frequently caused by organisms like Pseudomonas aeruginosa.\n- Fungal keratitis:\n- Correct to suspect in immunocompromised patients or those using steroids, but this is not exclusive.\n- Fungal keratitis should also be suspected in cases of trauma with organic material (e.g., plant matter) or in warm, humid climates.\n- Viral keratitis:\n- It's true that viral keratitis (e.g., herpes simplex keratitis) can follow flu- like or cold symptoms.\n- Additional features may include dendritic ulcers on fluorescein staining, recurrent episodes, or history Of herpes simplex infection\n\nKeratitis Risk factors photoqraph Bacterial Contact lens uses, dry eye, abrasion Round/ white oval lesion (hypopyon) Viral History of nu or cold sores Fungal Trauma vegetative/outdoor, immunocompromised, steroid use Branching epithelial defect Infiltrates with feathery (dendritic (Grey-white lesion)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 46-year-old male presented to the ED with short of breath. His blood pressure is 2401120mmhg. His examination revealed bilateral crept on auscultation. What would be your next step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Phentolamine\n- **B)** Labetalol\n- **C)** Nitroprusside   D\n- Furosemide\n- **E)** Amlodipine Explanation\n- Standard treatment NICE recommendation — diuretic (furosemide) and or CTN are the treatment choice for hypertensive emergency with LV failure or pulmonary oedema.\n\n- Nice guideline recommendation hypertension management In adults Of drug', treatment BP targets ACE ACE'\n- cce\n- to BP tMge *BIHS caution with steps, more than 1 drug is recommended if hypertension is not controlled in adults taking step 1 treatment of an ACE inhibitor or ARB.\n- Do not combine an ACE inhibitor with an ARB to treat hypertension.\n- Hypertensive emergency_\n- It is defined as the clinical situation in which there is a marked elevation of blood pressure (BP) usually >180/120mmhg and associated\n\nwith acute or progressive end organ damage, e.g. cardiovascular, renal or neurological dysfunction.\n- Hypertensive\n- Labetalol first line therapy in hypertensive encephalopathy/malignant hypertension or intracranial haemorrhage.\n- Nimodipine 60 mg orally, nasogastric, or gastric tube every 4 h, usually started within 96 h of onset of subarachnoid haemorrhage\n- IV nitrates (GTN) and or IV labetalol are choice of agents for HE complicated with cardiac ischemia/Ml\n- Standard treatment NICE recommendation — diuretic (furosemide) and or CTN are the treatment choice for hypertensive emergency with LV failure or pulmonary oedema.\n- Beta blocker (labetalol atenolol, esmolol) along with nitrates or nitroprusside treatment of choice for aortic dissection\n- Alpha and Beta blocker (phentolamine and labetalol) choice for phaeochromocytoma\n- Fenoldopam choice for AKI\n- IV labetalol/ nifedipine or methyldopa or hydralazine drug of choice for pre-eclampsia and MgS04 for eclampsia.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 30-year-old RTA patient presented to the ED with ankle pain. His x-ray is shown. What would be your step of management? A urgent referral to orthopedic\n- **B)** Analgesia and discharge with orthopedic follow-up\n- **C)** Immobilize in plaster cast\n- **D)** Order CT scan\n- **E)** Immediately reduce the ankle Explanation\n- X-ray shows Maisonneuve fracture (fracture of the rnedial malleolus or widening of the medial ankle joint space due to deltoid ligament injury, as well as widening of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis plus proximal fibular fracture)\n- Patient requires urgent referral to orthopedic for internal fixation.\n- Maisonneuve fracture generally managed by operative treatment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA nurse asks you to review a 40-year-old female with a NEWS2 score of 9 who fulfills the criteria for sepsis. IV access has been obtained, and appropriate blood tests have been sent. Her observations are: Temp 390C HR 132 bpm BP  mmHg RR 22 Sp02 94% on room air. According to NICE sepsis guidelines, what is the most appropriate initial step in management?\n- **A)** Start intravenous antibiotics\n- **B)** Adrninister Miter bolus Of crystalloid fluid\n- **C)** Insert a catheter to monitor urine output\n- **D)** Consider vasopressor support\n- **E)** Commence oxygen therapy\n- Explanation\n- NICE are at high risk (NEWS2 score of 7 or above) of severe illness or death from sepsis, start broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotic treatment, within 1 hour of calculating the person's NEVVS2 score on initial assessment in the emergency department.\n- IV fluid - Cive intravenous fluid bolus 5001 in 15 minutes without delay (within 1 hour of identifying that they are at high risk) in patient either lactate over 2 mmol/litre or systolic blood pressure less than 90 mmHg.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 65-year-old male presented to the ED with short of breath. He has history of COPD He was nebulize with salbutamol twice and steroid has been given. His ABG is shown. What is the ABG interpretation?\n- **A)** Acute on chronic respiratory acidosis\n- **B)** Type-I respiratory failure\n- **C)** Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis\n- **D)** Type-2 respiratory failure\n- **E)** Normal anion gab metabolic acidosis Explanation\n- This is the case of acute on chronic respiratory acidosis\n- ABG interpretation;\n- 1st stepclt shows respiratory acidosis, bicarb slightly raised but has not developed appropriate metabolic compensation.\n- 2nd step to check: is it acute or chronic?\n- To use rule for acute: For every rise of 10mmHg/1.33kpa of the PaC02 above 40 mmHg, the bicarbonate will rise by Immol/L Therefore, bicarb should be elevated 5mmol/L to 12.1kpa/90mmHg PaC02, however, in this patient bicarb is elevated more than that its 8mmol/L. (it's not acute respiratory acidosis. Let's move to chronic)\n- TO use 1 to IO rule for chronic: For every rise Of 10mmHg Of the PaC02 above 40mmHg, the bicarbonate will rise by 4mmol/L Therefore, bicarb should be elevated 20mmol/L to 12.1kpa/90mmHg PaC02. However, in this patient bicarb is elevated less than that which is 8mmol/L. is the case of acute on chronic respiratory acidosis (commonly seen in acute COPD exacerbation)\n- Note; for more detail check ABC interpretation module in procedural & skills. You can find more cases discussion in the form of examples.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 29-year-old male brought into the ED following collapsed in a pub. He is cyanosed, tachycardiac and his saturation is 88% which is not improving with high flow oxygen. His respiratory rate is 26 breath per minutes. On further assessment, he admits recent recreational drug use. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Cocaine overdose\n- **B)** MDMA\n- **C)** CO poisoning D\n- Methemoglobinemia\n- **E)** Cyanide poisoning Explanation\n- Patient clinical feature; cyanosed and low saturation Sp02 88% which does not improve with supplemental oxygen suggestive of methaemoglobinaemia. Methemoglobinemia\n- Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood.\n- Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2+) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3+). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%\n\n- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital or acquired\n- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood\n- Causes\n- Benzocaine Dapsone\n- Nitroglycerin\n- Nitrofurantoin\n- Nitroprusside\n- Phenazopyridine\n- Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite\n- Contaminated well water\n- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")\n- Clinical features\n- Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:\n- < - None (patients with underlying diseases may have more symptoms at lower level)\n- 10-20% - Slight discoloration (eg, pale, gray, blue) of the skin.\n- Chocolate brown colored blood\n- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia, lightheadedness\n- Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain\n- 50-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status, delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis\n- Usually, death Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is >1.5 g/dl\n\n- Investigations\n- VBG or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in a fresh sample of blood.\n- pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%.\n- Pa02 readings on blood gases may be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia\n- Management\n- Remove exposures if possible.\n- Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary) signs-of-end-organ-damage)\n- r-anyone-who-has-a-methemogl#in level\n- Give methylene blue: 1-2 mg/kg (0.1-0.2 mL/kg of 1% solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes\n- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms.\n- Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.\n- Management-in-GQ-Pßeficiencpatients\n- Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis.\n- Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C\n\n- Consider exchange transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion\n- Key_points\n- Methemoglobinemia can result from exposure to a number Of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).\n- Consider the diagnosis Methemoglobinemia in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.\n- Administer methylene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs, metabolic acidosis, end organ dysfunction or, a serum level > 25%\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 45-year-old presented to the ED with a sensorineural loss in his left ear. Which Of the following statement is correct?\n- **A)** Weber's test lateralizes to left ear\n- **B)** patient can't hear whisper\n- **C)** Rinne's test would be -ve\n- **E)** BC>AC Explanation\n\nInterpretation of Rinne & Weber tests Interpretation of Rinne & Weber tests 1 Normal Conductive hearing loss Sensorineural hearing loss Mixed hearing loss Rinne results\n- AC>BC in both ears\n- BC>AC in affected ear, (Rinne test —ve)\n- AC>BC in unaffected ear (Rinne test +ve)\n- AC>BC in both ears\n- Unilateral (one ear) — in both affected and unaffected ear AC>BC and Rinne test would be\n- BC>AC in affected ear, AC>BC in unaffected ear Weber results\n- Midline\n- Lateralize to affected ear 1\n- Lateralize to unaffected ear, away from the affected ear 1\n- Lateralize to unaffected ear, away from affected ear AC = Air conduction: BC = bone conduction\n\nWeber test Normal Conductive loss, same ear Sensorineural loss, opposite ear Rinne test Air conduction (AC) Bone conduction (BC) AC > BC Normal BC > AC = Conductive loss\n- Key_points:\n- Weber test z tuning fork (512HZ) placed in middle of the forehead\n- Rinne_testztuning fork placed on the mastoid bone and then adjacent to the outer ear\n- Rinne test positive — (normal and sensorineural hearing loss)\n\n- Rinne test negative — B>A ( conducting hearing loss)\n- False negative— in severe sensorineural loss air conduction is absent, bone conduction present due to transcranial transmission of sound which is picked by the cochlea of good ear. Prevented by masking\n- For example, if the left ear is completely dead, the sound waves travel to the good right ear on testing the bone conduction on the left. But the sounds are not heard when the tuning fork is held next to the external auditory meatus on the side being tested (left). In this case, the patient will state that bone conduction is louder than air conduction, giving a falsely negative Rinne test. 10 / 10 IO. A 54-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of dizziness and chest pain. His BP is 80/65mmhg. His ECG is given below What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Adenosine\n- **B)** DC cardioversion\n- **C)** Vagal maneuver\n- **D)** Bisoprolol\n- **E)** Amiodarone Explanation\n\n- ECG shows AVNRT, patient is unstable therefore DC shock should be given.\n- Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is the most common form of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, or PSVT, in adults.\n- AVNRT occurs when a reentrant circuit is present within the AV node itself. In this situation, there are two separate conduction pathways within the AV node instead of just one (present in about 5% of the general population).\n- Stable patient management\n- first line - Vagal maneuvers such as carotid massage or the Valsalva maneuver.\n- Second line - adenosine 6mg then 12 and 18mg maximum 36mg (according to resus 0K) OR 6mg then 12mg and 12mg maximum 30mg (according to NICE)\n- Third line - if above treatment ineffective then Beta blocker or rate limiting calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem\n- fourth line - if above treatment ineffective then synchronized cardioversion. previous\n	\N	[]	\N	f	38	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
52d62b99-2467-4e60-9efa-95d50569c7c7	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-39	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 39	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 39\n\nWhat would be the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** ITP   B\n- Acquired hemophilia O\n- **C)** CML\n- **D)** Congenital hemophilia\n- **E)** Von Willebrand disease (VWD) Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of acquired haemophilia\n- Acquired Haemophilia\n- Unlike patients with hereditary hemophilia, patients with acquired hemophilia do not have a personal or family history of bleeding episodes:\n- Acquired haemophilia typically occurs in older people age >60 and should be suspected and investigated in older adults who present with a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time and/or new bleeding symptoms.\n- It's very rarer as compare to inherited disorder.\n\n- Inyestigations\n- In a patient with acquired hemophilia, the bleeding time, prothrombin time (PT), and platelet count are normal. However, the activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)\n- Management\n- Treatrnent for AHA (acquired Haemophila\n- **A)** (is directed at bleeding control, inhibitor eradication to prevent subsequent bleeding episodes, and treatrnent of any underlying causative disease. First-line treatment Second-line strategies Bleeding control aPCC or rFVlla Bypassing agent: Alternate Sequential Parallel Immunoadsorption protocol Inhibitor eradication Steroid ± yclophosphamide Rituximab ± Steroid Cyclophosphamide Ciclosporin Azathioprine\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nAn 82-year-old male history of dementia was brought into the ED by her daughter. She states that he is restless and agitated. On further assessment, you have found he has multiple marks of bruise on his lower limb. What would be your next step in this patient?\n- **A)** Discharge him with advice\n- **B)** Prescribe him antipsychotic\n- **C)** Refer to his GP\n- **D)** Admit him for safeguarding review\n- **E)** Call the police Explanation\n- This patient should be admitted for adult safeguarding review, as he is physically abused.\n- Safeguarding lead and local authority should be involved. The Care Act: safeguarding adults\n- People's wellbeing is at the heart of the care and support system under the Care Act 2014, and the prevention of abuse and neglect is one of the elements identified as going to make up a person's wellbeing.\n\n- In the context of the legislation, specific adult safeguarding duties apply to any adult who:\n- has care and support needs\n- is experiencing, or is at risk of, abuse or neglect\n- is unable to protect themselves because of their care and support needs.\n- The Care Act 2014 sets out a clear legal framework for how local authorities and other parts of the system should protect adults at risk of abuse or neglect.\n- Local authorities have new safeguarding duties. They must:\n- lead a multi-agency local adult safeguarding system - that seeks to prevent abuse and neglect and stop it quickly when it happens\n- make enquiries, or request others to make them - when they think an adult with care and support needs may be at risk of abuse or neglect and they need to find out what action may be needed\n- establish Safeguarding Adults Boards - including the local authority, NHS and police, which will develop, share and implement a joint safeguarding strategy\n- carry out Safeguarding Adults Reviews - when someone with care and support needs dies as a result of neglect or abuse and there is a concern that the local authority or its partners could have done more to protect them\n- arrange for an independent advocate - to represent and support a person who is the subject of a safeguarding enquiry or review, if required. The Safeguarding Adults Review Process\n\n- A Safeguarding Adults Review (SAR) is a multi-agency review which seeks to determine what relevant agencies and individuals involved could have done differently, that could have prevented Serious abuse or neglect or a death from taking place.\n- When someone with care and support needs - such as mental illness, learning or physical disabilities, or dementia, dies or is harmed as a result of abuse or neglect and it is thought that agencies could have worked better together to prevent the death or future harm.\n- The Care Act 2014 states that Safeguarding Adult Boards (SABs) must arrange a Safeguarding Adult Review (SAR) when: I. an adult in its area dies as a result of abuse or neglect, whether known or suspected, or 2. the adult is still alive, and the SAB knows or suspects that the adult has experienced serious abuse or neglect, and 3. there is concern that partner agencies could have worked together more effectively to protect the adult from harm.\n- HOW DOES THE REVIEW PROCESS WORK? The exact process will vary, but in a typical review:\n- A multi-agency panel will be formed to gather and analyse as much information as possible from the professionals and agencies involved with the adult at risk. This may include Adult Social Care, hospital trusts, the person's CP, the police and the ambulance service.\n- An independent person will usually be asked to review the information to understand what happened, consider how best to prevent future harm, and make recommendations for change.\n- The whole process generally lasts six months to a year.\n\n- Note - You don't need to memorize everything.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 2-year-old child presented to ED by his mother. You suspect NAI. Which of the following will suggest NAI in this child?\n- **A)** Femur fracture after being slipped\n- **B)** Bruise over medial thigh\n- **C)** Bruise over shins\n- **D)** Distal tibial spiral fracture\n- **E)** Bruise over knee Explanation\n- Bruise over medial aspect of thighs suggests NAI. Child abuse-Non-accidental injuries\n- Bruising\n- Bruise over the knees and shins is consider as normal finding in children (toddler)\n\nbruises over the lateral aspect of their elbows and hips in older children\n- during play are considered as normal finding.\n- Followjngfeatures-suggest-NAl\n- Bruise over medial aspect of upper arms or thighs\n- Bruise in non-mobile babies bruises on any non-bony part of the body or face including the eyes,\n- ears and buttocks Uncommon injuries bilaterally\n- Finger 'imprinting eg: slap mark\n- Imprints or marks of belt or stick\n- Petechiae on the face may reflect smothering and asphyxiation (it was\n- suggested that 2—10% of SIDS may have been smothered), however, petechiae also occur with forceful coughing or vomiting. Fractures\n- Following fracture suggests NAI\n- Rib and spinal fractures\n- Multiple fractures of different ages\n-\n- Fracture in infant who are not independently mobile Long bone fracture (eg: humerus, femur, radius , ulna etc) in children<3\n- years Salter—Harris type I and II fractures of knee, wrist and elbow.\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 9-months-old infant brought into the ED by his mom with apnea attack while feeding. It was lasted less than 15 seconds. He appears normal now. Which of the following is the sign of BRUE/ALTE?\n- **A)** Vomiting\n- **C)** Irregular respiratory rate   D\n- First event\n- **E)** Born less than 32 weeks Explanation\n- BRUE/ALTE: Is an episode in an infant less than 12 months of age characterized by: minute duration (typically 20-30s)\n-\n- A return to baseline state Not explained by any identifiable medical condition (i.e. medically well).\n- Includes one or more of the following features:\n- Central cyanosis/pallor\n-\n\n- Absent, decrease or irregular breathing\n- Marked change in tone (hyper- or hypotonia) Altered level of consciousness\n- Note=lfthe feature has been asked then this question likely to be removed as both irregular breathing and pallor are the features of ALTE/BRUE.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 30-year-old female presented to the ED with painful leg. On examination, her leg was tender and swelled. You've order D-dimer which came negative. What would be your next step of management? O\n- **A)** Order CTPA\n- **B)** Order proximal leg U/S scan\n- **C)** Repeat D-dimer\n- **D)** Alternative diagnosis treat as cellulitis\n- **E)** Cive LMWH and wait for U/S scan Explanation\n\n- DVT is likely as the well score for this patient is 2.\n- After D-dimer, a proximal IJ/S scan should be ordered with the result available within 24 hours.\n- NICE guideline DVT is likely if well score is 2 or more:\n- following steps should be taken: order proximal leg U/S with the result available within 4 hours if possible. If a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan result cannot be obtained within 4 hours, offer people with a DVT Wells score of 2 points or more:\n- a D-dimer test, then\n- interim therapeutic anticoagulation and\n- a proximal leg vein ultrasound scan with the result available within 24 hours.\n- Note - if both D-dimer and proximal U/S scan came negative then DVT should be rule out and an alternative diagnosis should be considered.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 35-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of headache and nausea. On examination, he has neck stiffness and non-blanching rash in his lower limb. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Malaria\n- **B)** Viral meningitis\n- **C)** Hemorrhagic fever   D\n- Meningococcal sepsis\n- **E)** HSP EXPIanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of meningococcal septicemia Bacterial meningitis and meningococcal disease\n- Meningitis is inflammation of the two inner meninges (the pia and arachnoid mater) of the brain and spinal cord.\n- Bacterial meningitis is a life-threatening condition that affects all ages, but is most common in babies and children.\n- Meningococcal diseaseis infection with Neisseria meningitidis. It can result in meningococcal meningitis (15% of cases) or meningococcal septicaemia (25% of cases).\n- Pneumococcal disease is infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae(also called pneumococcal).\n\n. Causes\n- Overall in the UK, Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcus), Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus), and Haemophilus influenzaetype b (Hib) are the rnost common causative organisms of acute bacterial meningitis in children aged 3 months or older and adults.\n- In neonates (younger than 1 month of age),Streptococcus agalactiae, Escherichia coli, S. pneumoniae, and Listeria monocytogenes are the most common causative organisms.\n- In adults, S. pneumoniaeis the most common cause of bacterial meningitis.\n- Clinical features\n- Common non-specific_symptoms/signs;\n- Fever, Vomiting/nausea, Lethargy, Irritability/unsettled behaviour, Ill appearance, Headache.\n- More specific symptoms/signs_inglude;\n- Non-blanching rash - A rapidly evolving petechial or purpuric rash is a sign of very severe disease.\n- Stiff neck\n- Capillary refill time of more than 2 seconds, cold hands and feet, Unusual skin colour, Shock and hypotension, Leg pain.\n- Back rigidity, Bulging fontanelle, Photophobia.\n- Kernig's sign - (person unable to fully extend at the knee when hip is flexed).\n- Brudzinski's sign - (person's knees and hips flex when neck is flexed).\n- Unconsciousness or toxic/moribund state.\n\n- Focal neurological deficit including cranial nerve involvement and abnormal pupils.\n- Seizures\n- Investigations\n- Blood-culture—Take blood for culture within I hour of arrival at hospital and ideally prior to giving antibiotics\n- Serum pneumococcal and meningococcal PCR\n- FBC and differential, LFTs, U&E, blood glucose\n- CSF Do a lumbar puncture (LP; unless contraindicated) within 1 hour of arrival at hospital.\n- Consider CSF PCR for tuberculosis\n- Neuroimaging is not indicated unless you suspect alternative pathology because there are signs indicating risk of cerebral herniation ( secondary to raised intracranial pressure).\n- Management\n- Pre-hospital (community)\n- Refer all patients with suspected meningitis and/or meningococcal sepsis to hospital immediately.\n- The British Infection Association recommends: - Benzylpenicillin (intramuscular or intravenous) OR A third-generation cephalosporin such as cefotaxime or ceftriaxone (intramuscular or intravenous).\n\n- Do not delay transfer to hospital by attempting to give parenteral antibiotics.\n- Hospital-settingn)anagement\n- Supportive measure + corticosteroid - Give empirical intravenous dexamethasone (10mg IV 6 hourly) to all adults with acute bacterial meningitis within 1 hour of presentation to hospital.\n- Antibiotics British Infection Association recommends:\n- IV ceftriaxone:2 g intravenously every 12 hours OR\n- IV cefotaxime: 2 g intravenously every 6 hours\n- If patient allergic penicillin or cephalosporin : chloramphenicol: 25 mg/kg intravenously every 6 hours\n- In addition to above:\n- Consider Vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg or rifampicin: 600 mg IV to cover for penicillin-resistant pneumococci\n- Consider amoxicillin: 2 g IV or trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole: 10-20 mg/kg/day to cover cover for Listeria monocytogenes (neonates or immunocompromised patients).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 15kg child brought into the ED by his mother with complaint od diarrhea and vomiting. On examination he was dehydrated and mottled skin. You want to initiate the resuscitation with IV fluid. What would be the hourly maintenance rate for this child?\n- **A)** 30ml O\n- **B)** 20ml   c.soml\n- **D)** 40ml\n- **E)** 60ml EXPIanation\n- It would be 50ml/hr as patient weight is 15kg\n- 1st 10kg x 4ml = 40mI/hr\n- 2nd 5kg x 2ml = 10 total = 50mI/hr\n- Steps of management in clinical shock acute gastroenteritis patients\n- Follow ABCDE protocol\n- The preferred fluid type for IV maintenance is sodium chloride 0.9% with glucose 5%\n- Resuscitation step: - fluid bouls NICE recommends 10-20 ml/kg in shock patients and 10mI/kg in non shock patients.\n- Resus council 2021 guideline recommends 10ml/kg in both shock and non-clinical shock patient\n\n- Give a second bolus, if shock persist\n- If remains shocked after second bolus, Give a further bolus, consider discussion with paediatric intensive care team.\n- Example-I A 7kg child brought into the ED with clinical shock, what would be fluid bolus for this patient? 7 x20= 140ml (to follow NICE) 7 xlO=70mI (to follow resus 2021)\n- Maintenance steæ- Once symptoms and signs of shock have resolved\n- Calculate daily maintenance requirement\n- Use 10% estimate for deficit calculation (Note: use 10% estimate for deficit calculation shock patient and 5% in non-shock patients, to assess that, check table-A in assessment) Body weight ISt 10kg 2nd 10kg 3rd 10kg Daily/per-day fluid requirements 100ml/kg 50ml/kg 20ml/kg Hourly fluid requirement 4ml/kg 2ml/kg Iml/kg\n\n- Example -1 A 26 kg child responded to a 20 ml/kg or 10ml/kg (follow resus) fluid bolus and is no longer shocked. Calculate fluid deficit? formula: %age x weight x IO IO x 26 x IO = 2600mI (replace in 24-48 hrs)\n- Example-2 A 26 kg child responded to a 20 ml/kg or 10mI/kg (follow resus) fluid bolus and is no longer shocked. What is his maintenance hourly IV fluid requirement using the standard regime? Hourly maintenance = (10kg x 4mI) + (10kg x 2mI) + (6kg x Imi) = 66mI/hr if convert to daily maintenance = (100 ml/kg x IO kg) + (50 ml/kg x IO kg) + (20 ml/kg x 6 kg) 1620ml/day\n- Note - for more detail check acute gastroenteritis module in acute medicine- gastroenterology system.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 5-year-old child brought into the ED by his mother who is confused and drowsy. His mother states that he was living with his grandparents for the last five days and has had coryzal symptoms and fever which was treated with antipyretic. His lab is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Hepatitis\n- **B)** Bacterial meningitis\n- **C)** Henoch Schönlein Purpura   D' Reye syndrome\n- **E)** Marfan syndrome Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of Reye's syndrome as child likely be treated with aspirin.\n- Reye syndrome\n\nReye's syndrome is an illness that meets all of the following criteria:\n- An acute, non-inflammatory encephalopathy with either sterile CSF\n- containing <9 WBC/mL or cerebral oedema without inflammatory cell infiltrate hepatic dysfunction documented by either a threefold elevation of\n- serum transaminases and/or serum ammonia, or liver biopsy demonstrating fatty infiltration. There is no other diagnosis to account for the cerebral and hepatic\n- derangement. Risk factors\n-\n- Age between 5 and 14 years, white ethnicity\n- Recent hx of viral infection (e.g., influenza, varicella-zoster infection, or\n- gastroenteritis). Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) and other drug exposure, toxin exposure, a\n- genetically predisposed individual, and winter/spring months. Clinical features\n- Hyperventilation\n- Vomiting\n- Altered mental status\n- Hepatomegaly\n-\n- hyper-reflexia/areflexia diminished pain response\n- Seizure - late stage\n-\n\n- Abnormal pupillary response\n- Absence of, or minimal, jaundice and scleral icterus\n- .lnyestigatjons\n- Serum electrolyte - variable; metabolic acidosis\n- Serum glucose - may be <2.8 mmol/L (<50 mg/dL) LFTs - elevated transaminase and aminotransferase; bilirubin mildly\n- elevated, but usually normal\n- Serum ammonia - Ammonia levels are usually at least 1.5 to 20 times elevated at the time of presentation\n- PT/PTT- usually prolonged Blood gas\n-\n- Urine/serum toxicology\n- Urinalysis lumbar puncture with cerebrospinal fluid analysis\n- Liver biopsy\n- Metabolic testing\n- Management\n- Treatment mainly supportive\n- In severe cases patient required intensive care monitoring.\n- Slow intravenous bicarbonate infusion recommended if pH< 7.0\n- In fluid overload give furosemide\n- lv glucose in hypoglycaemia\n- Iv fluid plus vasopressor in hypotension\n- Anticonvulsants (phenytoin) in seizures\n-\n\n- ICP-lowering regimen in raised ICP\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 55-year-old make brought into the ED who has end stage of kidney disease CKD3. He has history of motor neuron disease. You've decided to sedate the patient under procedural sedation. Which of the following is contraindication to suxamethonium in this patient? O\n- **A)** CKD\n- **B)** Motor neuron disease (MND)\n- **C)** Hypercalcemia   D\n- Hypokalemia\n- **E)** Hepatic failure Explanation\n- The effect of neuromuscular blocking agents depends on the disease process but, on the whole, suxamethonium should be avoided in all NMD (neuromuscular disease) which includes MND (Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis, Spinal Muscular Atrophy, Spinal Bulbar, Muscular Atrophy).\n- Caution should be taken in use of suxamethonium in CKD patients.\n\nIO. A 21-year-old male presented to the ED with priapism for the last 4 hours. Lidocaine has been given. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Corpora cavernosa injection with phenylephrine\n- **B)** Aspirate the blood with needle\n- **C)** Surgical intervention\n- **D)** Refer to urologist\n- **E)** Ice compression Explanation\n- After application of anesthesia aspirate the blood.\n- Priapism- management algorithm\n\n- Initial conservative measures Local anaesthesia of the penis Insert wide bore butterfly through the glans into the corpora cavernosa Aspiration cavernosal blood until bright red arterial blood is obtained\n- Cavernosal irrigation Irrigate with 0.9% w/v saline solution\n- Intracavernosal therapy inject intracavernosal adrenoceptor agonist Current first-line therapy is phenylephrine with aliquots of 200 pg being injected every 3-5 minutes until detumescence is achieved (Maximum dose of phenylephrine is Img within 1 hour)\n- Surgical therapy Surgical shunting Consider primary penile prosthesis implantation if priapism has been present for more than 36 hours\n	\N	[]	\N	f	39	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
9f23afc2-3522-4186-ab58-5ac997da80f5	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-4-2	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 4\n\nExplanation\n- In cutaneous decompression sickness, a transient pruritus can typically affect the distal upper or proximal lower limbs, the nose, or the ears. Allergies to dry- or wetsuit components may also cause itching. Dysbarism includes decompression sickness, arterial gas embolism (AGE), and barotraumas\n- Decompression sickness is a complex of symptoms and disease caused by the formation of an excess of bubbles in body tissues.\n- This occurs after an exposure to breathing gases at increased pressure, followed by a reduction in environmental pressure.\n- Arterial gas embolism (AGE) refers to the precipitation of gas bubbles in the arterial circulation. Decompression illness encompasses decompression sickness and ACE.\n- Barotrauma is damage to tissues caused by the pressure-induced expansion and contraction of gases in enclosed spaces.\n- Nitrogen narcosis is a syndrome of reversible neurologic and behavioral changes produced by an increased partial pressure of nitrogen in the body.\n- Risk factors\n\nDecompression illness Barotrauma Breath-holding during ascent Lung disease Eustachian tube dysfunction Incorrect autoinflation Trapped gas in or near teeth\n- Nitrogen narcosis deep diving - The risk of narcosis increases when diving beyond depths of 130 feet (40 m).\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Diving or other activity involving increased atmospheric pressure Increasing depth and duration of dives Strenuous exercise during diving Repetitive diving dehydration Right-to-left shunt Clinical features musculoskeletal pain(most often affecting the shoulder, elbow, or knee. Larger joints tend to be more involved than smaller ones, and if multiple joints are affected they are usually unilateral and adjacent). numbness tingling paresthesia headache\n- dizziness or vertigo Nausea and vomiting\n- Shortness of breath\n- Difficulty in walking\n- Pruritus (less common)\n\n- Paralysis (less common)\n- Rash (less common)\n- Reduced level of consciousness (less common)\n- Investigations\n- Chest x-ray — to assess pulmonary barotrauma, pneumothorax, pr pulmonary oedema\n- Consider FBC\n- Consider pure-tone audiometry\n- Consider facial x-ray\n- Consider ECG\n- Management\n\nDecompression illness\n- Follow ABCD protocol\n- High flow oxygen at 15L/min via a non- rebreathe mask regardless of their oxygen saturation.\n- Fluid—oral fluid if tolerated, otherwise 1-2 litres of IV fluids during the first post-injury hour is recommended.\n- Recompression Therapy\n- Recompression therapy is the definitive treatment Of decompression illness.\n- The patient is re- pressurised in a chamber breathing 100% oxygen at a high partial pressure. Barotrauma\n- Middle ear\n- Middle-ear barotraumas can be treated with nasal or oral decongestants (ephedrine or pseudoephedrine) to reduce the inflammation.\n- Consider analgesia\n- Pulmonary\n- 100% oxygen is administered to correct arterial hypoxaemia, avoiding positive pressure ventilation where possible as this may exacerbate lung damage.\n- Pneumothorax: mild cases may resolve with normobaric 100% oxygen. Or insert a chest drain if there is evidence Of severe pneumothorax.\n- Consider recompression therapy\n- Sinus\n- Oral or nasal decongestant\n- Analgesia\n- Consider intranasal corticosteroid\n- Consider endoscopic surgery Next Nitrogen narcosis\n- Ascent\n- This condition is entirely reversible by reduction Of ambient pressure (i.e., ascent to shallow depths).\n	\N	[]	\N	f	40	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
84659483-b4c2-40be-a46c-efe3f0f17e37	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-40	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 40	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 40\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Heart Rate: 120 bpm Blood Pressure:  mmHg Respiratory Rate: 24/min Sp02: 96% on room air What would be your next step in management?\n- **A)** Begin intravenous fluid resuscitation\n- **B)** Apply 3-Point C-Spine Immobilization\n- **C)** Perform needle cricothyroidotomy\n- **D)** Administer tranexamic acid (TXA)O\n- **E)** Consult anesthetics for airway management Explanation Nice Recommendation - Assess the person for spinal injury, initially taking into account the factors listed below. Check if the person: has any significant distracting injuries is under the influence of drugs or alcohol is confused or uncooperative has a reduced level of consciousness has any spinal pain has any hand or foot weakness (motor assessment) has altered or absent sensation in the hands or feet (sensory assessment)\n\n- has priapism (unconscious or exposed male)\n- has a history of past spinal problems, including previous spinal surgery or conditions that predispose to instability of the spine.\n- Carry out full in-line spinal immobilisation if any of the factors mentioned above are present or if this assessment cannot be done.\n- Patient also meet with high risk criteria of C-spine rule hence full in-line spinal immobilization should be carried out as there is no contraindication to it.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 15-year-old male was involved in a cycling accident where he fell headfirst onto the road. He is experiencing severe facial and neck pain, particularly at the back of his neck. Examination reveals facial injuries as shown in the image provided. His observations are: Heart Rate 110 bpm Blood Pressure  mmHg Respiratory Rate 23/min Sp02 94% on room air What would be your next best step of management?\n- **A)** Consult anesthetics for airway management\n- **B)** Begin intravenous fluid resuscitation\n- **C)** Administer tranexamic acid (TXA)\n- **D)** Perform needle cricothyroidotomy\n- **E)** Apply 3-Point C-Spine Immobilization Explanation;\n- The patient has significant trauma to the face, including visible deformities, bleeding, and soft tissue disruption. This can obstruct the airway due to swelling, blood pooling, or tissue fragments.\n- Airway compromise is a contraindication to full in-line spine immobilization (Cervical collar + backboard, straps,) however, Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS) by an appropriately trained clinician should be carried out. As rnanual stabilization hasn't given, hence the next step is the secure the airway.\n- Airway should be secured before application three points c-spine immobilizations.\n- Please note, you can find similar question with correct option manual in-line stabilization.\n- Assessment for spinal injury_\n- On arrival at the scene of the incident, use a prioritising sequence to assess people with suspected trauma, for example <C>ABCDE:\n- C - catastrophic haemorrhage\n- A - airway with in-line spinal immobilisation breathing\n\nC- circulation\n-\n- D - disability (neurological) E - exposure and environment.\n-\n- At all stages of the assessment: protect the person's cervical spine with manual in-line spinal\n- immobilisation, particularly during any airway intervention and avoid moving the remainder of the spine.\n-\n- Assess the person for spinal injury, initially taking into account the factors listed below. Check if the person: has any significant distracting injuries\n- is under the influence of drugs or alcohol\n- is confused or uncooperative\n- has a reduced level of consciousness\n- has any spinal pain\n- has any hand or foot weakness (motor assessment)\n- has altered or absent sensation in the hands or feet (sensory\n- assessment) has priapism (unconscious or exposed male)\n- has a history of past spinal problems, including previous spinal surgery\n- or conditions that predispose to instability of the spine. Carry out full in-line spinal immobilisation if any of the factors\n- mentioned above are present or if this assessment cannot be done.\n- Step — 01 Assessment for cervical spine injury= use Canadian c-spine rule\n\nThe Canadian C-Spine Rule' For alert (GCS Smre = 15) and stable trauma patients when cervical spine injury is a ü)ncern. HIGH RISK Age 265yr or Dangerous mechanism' or Paresthesias in extremities ASSESSMENT OF RANGE OF MOTION? Simple rear-end MVC" or Sitting position in ED Or Ambulatory at any time or Delayed onset of neck painm or Absence Of midline cervical spine tenderness " YES' IltYEsj/ 45 degrees left and right NO RADIOGRAPHY\n- Axial head. eg diving\n- WC high\n- " Simple WC "Clu&S\n- Pushed into traffic\n- Hit by truck\n- by\n- Step -02 - Assessment for thoracic or lumbosacral spine Injury_\n\nAssess the person with suspected thoracic or lumbosacral spine injury using these factors; Age 65 years or older and reported pain in the thoracic or lumbosacral spine Dangerous mechanism of injury (fall from a height of greater than 3 metres, axial load to the head or base of the spine— tor example falls landing on teet or buttocks, high-speed motor vehicle collision, rollover motor accident, lap belt restraint only, ejection from a motorvehicle, accident involving motorised recreational vehicles, bicycle collision, horse riding accidents). Pre-existing spinal pathology, or known or at risk Of osteoporosis— for example steroid use Suspected spinal fracture in another region of the spine Abnormal neurological symptoms (paraesthesia or weakness or numbness)\n- On examination:\n- abnormal neurological signs (motor or sensory deficit)\n- new deformity Or bony midline tenderness (on palpation) bony midline tenderness (on percussion)\n- midline or spinal pain (on coughing\n- on mobilisation (sit, stand, Step, assess walking): pain or abnormal neurological symptoms (stop ifthis occurs). Note - Be aware that assessing children with suspected thoracic or lumbosacral spine injury is difficult and the Fhild's developmental stage should be taken into account.\n- Step -03 carry out or maintain full in-line spinal Immobilisation\n\nCar ry out or maintainfuuin.tinespinal immobilisation if:\n- A high-risk factor for cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C\n- spine rule\n- A low-risktactortor cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C-spine rule and the person is unable to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right Indicated by one or more of the factors listed in step-02 table ot thoracic or lumbosacral spine\n- NOTE— f uu in-line\n- A compromised ainvay Do not carry out or maintainfuWinAinespinat immobilisation in people if: They have low-risk factors for cervical spine injury as identified and indicated by the Canadian C\n- spine rule, are pain free and are able to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right\n- they do not have any ot the factors listed instep-02 table of thoracic or lumbosacral spine injury\n- known spinal deformities, such as ankylosing spondylitis (in these cases keep the spine in the person's Current position).\n- However manual immobilization is recommended.\n- Causes of airway compromise: Physical obstruction: Foreign body, swelling (e.g., anaphylaxis). blood, or vomit in the airway. Reduced consciousness: A patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score ot less than 8 may lose control of their airway, leading to collapse ot soft tissues and obstruction.\n- Trauma: Injuries such as facial fracture, neck injuries, or tracheal damage. Signs Of a compromised airway:\n- Stridor (high-pitched sound during inspiration). Gurgling (fluid in the airway). Cyanosis (bluish discoloration Of lips or skin due to IOW oxygen). Use Of accessory muscles for breathing. Decreased or absent breath sounds. Agitation or reduced consciousness.\n- Step -04 How to carry out full in-line spinal Immobilization When carrying out full in-line spinal imrnobilisation in adults and\n- children, manually stabilise the head with the spine in-line using the following stepwise approach:\n\nFit an appropriately sized semi-rigid collar unless contraindicated by: o a compromised airway o known spinal deformities, such as ankylosing spondylitis (in these cases keep the spine in the person's current position). Reassess the airway after applying the collar. Place and secure the person on a scoop stretcher. Secure the person with head blocks and tape, ideally in a vacuum mattress. When carrying out full in-line spinal immobilisation in children, further consider: o involving family members and carers if appropriate o keeping infants in their car seat if possible o using a scoop stretcher with blanket rolls, vacuum mattress, vacuum limb splints or Kendrick extrication device.\n- Step-05 Diagnostic imaging\n- After pain relief\n- Imaging for spinal injury should be performed urgently, and the images should be interpreted immediately by a healthcare professional with training and skills in this area.\n\nKey Differences Between MILS and Full In-Line immobilization\n\nAspect purpo Method Equipment Level Of Stability When Used Who performs It Limitations Manual In-Line Stabilization (MILS) Immediate stabilization Of the head and neck Manual support of the head and neck, often without a rigid collar or backboard. No equipment (sometimes cervical collar, but not Less stable, just holds the head and neck in place. Often in early stages, during airway management, or initial assessments. Often by the first responder or EMS personnel. Doesn't immobilize the entire spine. Full In-Line Stabilization Complete stabilization Of the entire spine Use Of a cervical collar, backboard, or vacuum mattress for full immobilization. Full equipment (cervical collar, backboard, straps, etc.). More stable, immobilizes the entire spine to prevent movement. Used when full immobilization is needed. after airway is secured. Performed by EMS or trauma teams after initial assessment. Provides full immobilization, but may be time-consuming to apply.\n- a-point Cervical Spine Immobilization\n- This refers to immobilization of the cervical spine using three points of support, which are typically:\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nManual In-Line Stabilization (MILS): Maintainingthe head and neck in a neutral position manuallyto prevent move ment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nCervical Cottar: Applying a rigid cervical collar to immobilize the neck externally.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nBackboard or Head Blocks with Straps: Securingthe patientg body and head toa backboard or usinghead blocks with straps to prevent movement in any direction. Together, these three components provide complete immobilization Of the cervical spine, minim izing the risk Of secondary injuryto the spinal cord\n- Paediatric Trauma - Stabilisation of the Cervical Spine\n\nRCEM recommendation immobilisation of the cervical spine in the Emergency Department should initially be achieved by lying the patient supine With Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS) by an appropriatelytrained clinician.\n- It this is not practical, consider using head blocks rolled blankets and tape, with semi-rigid collar as determined by local policy. It the child does not tolerate either MILS or head blocks, carefully consider the risks / benefits of trauma anaesthesia and immobilisation versus allowing the child to find a position Of comfort.\n- In summary RCEM recommendation: NICE recommendation Carry out or maintain full in-line spinal immobilisation if: A high-risk factor for cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C-spine rule A low-risk factor for cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C-spine rule and the person is unable to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right\n- Indicated by one or more of the factors listed in step- 02 table of thoracic-or-lumbosacral-spine-injury\n- The use Of semi-rigid collars in children is controversial. Not usinga semi-rigid collar might be considered reasonable in this group, and use should be determined locally, with this protocolised. If possible, lie the patient supine and provide Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS). VVhen MILS is not practical, consider using blocks or rolled blankets and tape. TO prevent secondary injury Of the cervical spine, focus on minimising movement Of the entire spine, rather than the use of a specific method or device.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 14-year-old male RTA patient brought into the ED by paramedics with facial trauma. Patient is alert and conscious. While transfer to resus he reported severe neck pain. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Intubate the patient\n- **B)** Cricothyroidotomy\n- **C)** Apply manual In-line cervical stabilization\n- **D)** Call for anesthetic help\n- **E)** Start lv fluid\n\nExplanation Nice recommendation—First step in this case would be Manual In Line\n- Stabilisation (MILS) and then application of a full in-line spinal immobilization - Cervical collar + backboard, straps, (if no airway compromise or known spinal deformities, such as ankylosing spondylitis). However, patient has facial trauma which compromised patient airways. Therefore, full in-inline spinal immobilization shouldn't be carried out in this patient as application cervical collar is contraindicated. An anesthetic should be called to secure his airway meanwhile, Manual\n- In Line Stabilisation (MILS) by an appropriately trained clinician should be carried out. If option given as 3-points cervical spine immobilization should be\n- incorrect in this scenario\n- Assessment for spinal injury_ On arrival at the scene of the incident, use a prioritising sequence to\n- assess people with suspected trauma, for example\n- C - catastrophic haemorrhage\n- A - airway with in-line spinal immobilisation breathing\n- C- circulation\n- D - disability (neurological)\n- E - exposure and environment. At all stages of the assessment:\n- protect the person's cervical spine with manual in-line spinal\n- immobilisation, particularly during any airway intervention and avoid moving the remainder of the spine.\n-\n\nAssess the person for spinal injury, initially taking into account the\n- factors listed below. Check if the person: has any significant distracting injuries\n- is under the influence of drugs or alcohol\n- is confused or uncooperative\n- has a reduced level of consciousness\n- has any spinal pain\n- has any hand or foot weakness (motor assessment)\n- has altered or absent sensation in the hands or feet (sensory\n- assessment) has priapism (unconscious or exposed male)\n- has a history of past spinal problems, including previous spinal surgery\n- or conditions that predispose to instability of the spine.\n- Carry out full in-line spinal immobilisation if any of the factors mentioned above are present or if this assessment cannot be done.\n- Step — 01 Assessment for cervical spine injury= use Canadian c-sp\n- ne rule\n\nThe Canadian C-Spine Rule' For alert (GCS Smre = 15) and stable trauma patients when cervical spine injury is a ü)ncern. HIGH RISK Age 265yr or Dangerous mechanism' or Paresthesias in extremities ASSESSMENT OF RANGE OF MOTION? Simple rear-end MVC" or Sitting position in ED Or Ambulatory at any time or Delayed onset of neck painm or Absence Of midline cervical spine tenderness " YES' IltYEsj/ 45 degrees left and right NO RADIOGRAPHY\n- Axial head. eg diving\n- WC high\n- " Simple WC "Clu&S\n- Pushed into traffic\n- Hit by truck\n- by\n- Step -02 - Assessment for thoracic or lumbosacral spine Injury_\n\nAssess the person with suspected thoracic or lumbosacral spine injury using these factors; Age 65 years or older and reported pain in the thoracic or lumbosacral spine Dangerous mechanism of injury (fall from a height of greater than 3 metres, axial load to the head or base of the spine— tor example falls landing on teet or buttocks, high-speed motor vehicle collision, rollover motor accident, lap belt restraint only, ejection from a motorvehicle, accident involving motorised recreational vehicles, bicycle collision, horse riding accidents). Pre-existing spinal pathology, or known or at risk Of osteoporosis— for example steroid use Suspected spinal fracture in another region of the spine Abnormal neurological symptoms (paraesthesia or weakness or numbness)\n- On examination:\n- abnormal neurological signs (motor or sensory deficit)\n- new deformity Or bony midline tenderness (on palpation) bony midline tenderness (on percussion)\n- midline or spinal pain (on coughing\n- on mobilisation (sit, stand, Step, assess walking): pain or abnormal neurological symptoms (stop ifthis occurs). Note - Be aware that assessing children with suspected thoracic or lumbosacral spine injury is difficult and the Fhild's developmental stage should be taken into account.\n- Step -03 carry out or maintain full in-line spinal Immobilisation\n\nCar ry out or maintainfuuin.tinespinal immobilisation if:\n- A high-risk factor for cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C\n- spine rule\n- A low-risktactortor cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C-spine rule and the person is unable to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right Indicated by one or more of the factors listed in step-02 table ot thoracic or lumbosacral spine\n- NOTE— f uu in-line\n- A compromised ainvay Do not carry out or maintainfuWinAinespinat immobilisation in people if: They have low-risk factors for cervical spine injury as identified and indicated by the Canadian C\n- spine rule, are pain free and are able to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right\n- they do not have any ot the factors listed instep-02 table of thoracic or lumbosacral spine injury\n- known spinal deformities, such as ankylosing spondylitis (in these cases keep the spine in the person's Current position).\n- However manual immobilization is recommended.\n- Causes of airway compromise: Physical obstruction: Foreign body, swelling (e.g., anaphylaxis). blood, or vomit in the airway. Reduced consciousness: A patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score ot less than 8 may lose control of their airway, leading to collapse ot soft tissues and obstruction.\n- Trauma: Injuries such as facial fracture, neck injuries, or tracheal damage. Signs Of a compromised airway:\n- Stridor (high-pitched sound during inspiration). Gurgling (fluid in the airway). Cyanosis (bluish discoloration Of lips or skin due to IOW oxygen). Use Of accessory muscles for breathing. Decreased or absent breath sounds. Agitation or reduced consciousness.\n- Step -04 how carry out full in-line spinal Immobilization When carrying out full in-line spinal imrnobilisation in adults and\n- children, manually stabilise the head with the spine in-line using the following stepwise approach:\n\nFit an appropriately sized semi-rigid collar unless contraindicated by: o a compromised airway o known spinal deformities, such as ankylosing spondylitis (in these cases keep the spine in the person's current position). Reassess the airway after applying the collar. Place and secure the person on a scoop stretcher. Secure the person with head blocks and tape, ideally in a vacuum mattress. When carrying out full in-line spinal immobilisation in children, further consider: o involving family members and carers if appropriate o keeping infants in their car seat if possible o using a scoop stretcher with blanket rolls, vacuum mattress, vacuum limb splints or Kendrick extrication device.\n- Step-05 Diagnostic imaging\n- After pain relief\n- Imaging for spinal injury should be performed urgently, and the images should be interpreted immediately by a healthcare professional with training and skills in this area.\n\nKey Differences Between MILS and Full In-Line immobilization\n\nAspect purpo Method Equipment Level Of Stability When Used Who performs It Limitations Manual In-Line Stabilization (MILS) Immediate stabilization Of the head and neck Manual support of the head and neck, often without a rigid collar or backboard. No equipment (sometimes cervical collar, but not Less stable, just holds the head and neck in place. Often in early stages, during airway management, or initial assessments. Often by the first responder or EMS personnel. Doesn't immobilize the entire spine. Full In-Line Stabilization Complete stabilization Of the entire spine Use Of a cervical collar, backboard, or vacuum mattress for full immobilization. Full equipment (cervical collar, backboard, straps, etc.). More stable, immobilizes the entire spine to prevent movement. Used when full immobilization is needed. after airway is secured. Performed by EMS or trauma teams after initial assessment. Provides full immobilization, but may be time-consuming to apply.\n- a-point Cervical Spine Immobilization\n- This refers to immobilization of the cervical spine using three points of support, which are typically:\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nManual In-Line Stabilization (MILS): Maintainingthe head and neck in a neutral position manuallyto prevent move ment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nCervical Cottar: Applying a rigid cervical collar to immobilize the neck externally.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nBackboard or Head Blocks with Straps: Securingthe patientg body and head toa backboard or usinghead blocks with straps to prevent movement in any direction. Together, these three components provide complete immobilization Of the cervical spine, minim izing the risk Of secondary injuryto the spinal cord\n- Paediatric Trauma - Stabilisation of the Cervical Spine\n\nRCEM recommendation immobilisation of the cervical spine in the Emergency Department should initially be achieved by lying the patient supine With Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS) by an appropriatelytrained clinician.\n- It this is not practical, consider using head blocks rolled blankets and tape, with semi-rigid collar as determined by local policy. It the child does not tolerate either MILS or head blocks, carefully consider the risks / benefits of trauma anaesthesia and immobilisation versus allowing the child to find a position Of comfort.\n- In summary RCEM recommendation: NICE recommendation Carry out or maintain full in-line spinal immobilisation if: A high-risk factor for cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C-spine rule A low-risk factor for cervical spine injury is identified and indicated by the Canadian C-spine rule and the person is unable to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right\n- Indicated by one or more of the factors listed in step- 02 table of thoracic-or-lumbosacral-spine-injury\n- The use Of semi-rigid collars in children is controversial. Not usinga semi-rigid collar might be considered reasonable in this group, and use should be determined locally, with this protocolised. If possible, lie the patient supine and provide Manual In Line Stabilisation (MILS). VVhen MILS is not practical, consider using blocks or rolled blankets and tape. TO prevent secondary injury Of the cervical spine, focus on minimising movement Of the entire spine, rather than the use of a specific method or device.\n- Note — Last revised November 2024\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 30-year-old 34 weeks gravid female presents to the ED with complaint of some fluid discharge from her pelvis while sitting at home. You suspect premature rupture of membrane (PROM). What antibiotic recommended for this patient?\n- **A)** Amoxicillin\n- **B)** Ceftriaxone\n- **C)** Erythromycin\n- **D)** Co-amoxiclav\n- **E)** Ciprofloxacin Explanation\n- Nice-recommendation—offer women with P-PROM oral erythromycin 250 mg 4 times a day for a maximum of 10 days or until the woman is in established labour (whichever is sooner).\n- Alternative if contraindicate or patient can't tolerate then consider an oral penicillin for a maximum of IO days or until the woman is in established labour (whichever is sooner).\n- Care of women presenting with suspected Prelabour Rupture of Membrane from 24th weeks QI gestation\n- Premature rupture of membranes (PROM) is the rupture of gestational membranes prior to the onset of labor\n- Diagnosis\n- The diagnosis of spontaneous rupture of the membranes is made by rnaternal history followed by a sterile speculum examination.\n- If, on speculum examination, no amniotic fluid is observed, clinicians should consider performing an insulin-like growth factor binding protein 1 (IGFBP-I) or placental alpha microglobulin-l (PAMG-I) test of vaginal fluid to guide further management.\n\n- Assessments\n- A combination of clinical assessment, maternal blood tests (C-reactive protein and white cell count) and fetal heart rate should be used to diagnose chorioamnionitis in women with PPROM; these parameters should not be used in isolation.\n- Antibiotics, corticosteroids and magnesium sulfate\n- An antibiotic - erythromycin should be given for IO days or until the woman is in established labour (whichever is sooner) following the diagnosis of PPROM, and corticosteroids and magnesium sulfate, considered or offered.\n- Expectant management\n- Wor-nen whose pregnancy is complicated by PPROM who have no contraindications to continuing pregnancy should be offered expectant management until 37+0 weeks, as this is associated with better outconnes compared with early birth. Timing of birth should be discussed with each woman on a individual basis.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 64-year-old male patient had right lung lobectomy and presented to the ED with chest pain and short of breath. His chest x-ray is shown. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Surgical chest drains at 5th intercostal space on the right side\n- **B)** Surgical chest drain at 2nd intercostal space on the right side\n- **C)** Surgical chest drain at 5th intercostal space on the left side\n- **D)** Surgical chest drain at 2nd intercostal space on the left side\n- **E)** Needle thoracocentasis at 5th intercostal space midaxillary line Explanation Patient likely has developed post-operative complication, Chest tube\n- placement following pulmonary lobectomy is common practice. Postoperative pleural effusion is drained and in case of an air leak, development of a (tension-) pneumothorax is prevented.\n- prolonged air leak (>7 days): commonest complication post-lobectomy,\n- up to 18% atelectasis: common\n- pneumonia(rarely empyema)\n- atrial fibrillation: common\n- postoperative pulmonary edema\n-\n- ARDS bronchial dehiscence\n- hemothorax: hemorrhage is common, 2.9% require 24 units packed\n- cells chylothorax: more common with aggressive mediastinal nodal\n- dissections bronchopleural fistula (BPF): very rare after lobectomy\n-\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- lobar torsion: rare and potentially life-threatening, usually right middle lobe (70% cases) pulmonary vein stump thrombosis: rare after lobectomy anastomotic dehiscence: more common with sleeve lobectomy pulmonary embolism (PE) nerve injury: phrenic nerve, recurrent laryngeal nerve Note - steps of treatments findings! be familiar with entire complications, you may have question in different way.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA nurse has a needle stick injury, and she is looking for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis. Which of the following high risk of transmission required PEP?\n- **A)** Human bite from HIV positive source\n- **B)** Who has blood transfusion in the UK before 1990\n- **C)** Semen splash on eye from HIV positive source\n- **D)** Who lived 5 years or traveled to sub-Saharan\n- **E)** Receptive vaginal sex with HIV positive source\n\nExplanation\n- To follow British HIV association guideline, PEP is routinely offered in patients who has receptive vaginal sex with HIV positive source.\n- PEP is not recommended in splash of semen into eye High risk source for HIV\n- MSM ( men who have sex with men) Female sexual contacts of MSM\n- People reporting current or prior injecting drug use\n- Sex workers\n- Trans women\n- People from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence (>1%)* i-e\n- Originally from sub-Saharan Africa. People reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country with high\n- diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where contact occurs Individuals known to have/have had a mother living with HIV and who\n- do not have documented HIV-negative status\n- Post-exposure HIV prgpbylaxis (British HIV association guideline) PEP should be routinely offered to reduce risk of HIV transmission in\n- the-following-scenarios; Following receptive anal intercourse with an index partner of\n- unknown HIV status or known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load. Following receptive vaginal sex with an index partner known to be HIV\n- positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n\n- Following an occupational exposure (sharps or mucosal splash) from an index case known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n- For people who inject drugs after sharing needles/equipment if their index injecting partner is known to be HIV positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load. E ul side 110 i\n- Insertive vaginal intercourse with an index partner known to be HIV- positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load.\n- Insertive anal intercourse with an index partner of unknown HIV status.\n- PEP is pecific-extenuating factor which increases the risk of transmission,\n- Sharps and splash injuries, sharing of injecting equipment, receptive or insertive vaginal intercourse when the index case is from a high risk group but the HIV status is unknown.\n- Hurnan bite if the index case is HIV-positive with an unknown or detectable HIV viral load\n- NOTE- PEP should only be considered after a bite if all three criteria are met:\n- a) the biter's saliva was visibly contaminated with blood; b) the biter is known or suspected to have a plasma HIV viral load >3.0 log copies/ml; and\n- c) the bite has resulted in severe and/or deep tissue injuries\n\n- even HIV\n-\n-\n-\n- IN ALL OTHER SCENARIOS PEP IS NOT RECOMMENDED positive Fellatio with ejaculation (oral sex/stimulation) Fellatio without ejaculation Splash of semen into eye Cunnilingus Needlestick from a discarded needle in the community (here HIV +ve/- ve not relevant as source unknown)\n- A High prevalence countries or risk-groups are those where there is a significant likelihood of the index case individual being HIV positive. Within the UK at present, this is likely to be MSM (men who have sex with men), people who inject drugs from high-risk countries and individuals who have immigrated to the UK from areas of high HIV prevalence, particularly sub-Saharan Africa (high prevalence is\n\n6.5 Table 4: Summary table of PEP prescribing recommendations Index HIV HIV VL unknown Or Not recommended Not recommended Not recommended Index of unknown HIV status Receptive anal anal sex Receptive vaginal sa IngrtWe\n- inal Sa F€lado With ejaculatim FCIatio Splash Of into eye Of Mucosal splash bite Needlesti& a disardai in the HM VL Not recommended Not recommended Not recommended Not recommended Not recommended Not recommended Not Not recommended From Mgh "e\n- alence country / (e. Consider c. d Gen e rally not recommended Generally not recommended "d N Ot d Not recommended Not Not recommended OCCUPATIOt\n- aAL AND OTHER EXPOSURES n ded Recommended Generally not recommended Not recommended Not recommended b Not recommended Not recommended Generally not Generally not recom rrwnded Generally not recom mended' Not recommended Not recommended Iow country / group N Ot recommended Not recommended N Ot recomrnended Not recommended N Ot recomrnended N ot recommended Not Not mended Not recomnmended Not recommended N Ot recommended Not recommended Not recommended Recommended: the benefits of PEP are likely to outweigh the risks, PEP should be given unless there is a clear reason not to, . the risk of HIV transmission is low, the risk / benefit balance of PEP is less dear. The risk should be assessed on a case bv case basis taking into consideration factors shown in footnotes c and d below. Generally not recommended: the risk of HIV transmission is very low, the potential toxicity and inconvenience of PEP is likely to outweigh the benefit unless there is a dear specific extenuating factor Which increases the risk (see footnotes c, d, e, f below). We anticipate PEP should very rarely be given when the risk has been assessed and discussed (section 6.1.2 and 6.2.1.2) Not recornmended: the risk Of HIV transmission is n Ii ble and PEP should not be en\n- Timing-ofMposure\n- nd-PEP-recommendation\n- PEP (post-exposure prophylaxis) must be initiated as soon as possible, ideally within 2 hours, and preferably within 24 hours of exposure. PEP is not recommended if the exposure was more than 72 hours\n- before presentation.\n\n- PEP first line\n- Tenofovir disoproxil 245mg/emtricitabine 200mg and raltegravir 1200mg once daily as the regimen of choice for PEP\n- If there is evidence that the index case has a current or past history of ART failure, expert advice should be sought as to whether the PEP regimen should be modified in relation to ART history and/or resistance testing.\n- For women who are pregnant, raltegravir 400mg twice daily is preferred (with tenofovir disoproxil 24Smg/emtricitabine 200mg). Where accessing raltegravir 400mg might cause delay we recommend using raltegravir 600mg twice daily and switching at the earliest opportunity.\n- In established HIV infection, combination therapy with at least three medications from two medication classes is recommended for initial therapy. It is thus recommended, to use a triple agent regimen for PEP, though some international guidelines do recommend dual-class regimens in selected situations. NQte=Exposure with urine, nasal secretions, saliva, sweat, or tears without the presence of blood is considered negligible risk for acquisition of HIV.\n- Receiving a blood transfusion or other products made from blood is safe in the UK as all blood products have been screened for infections such as HIV since 1985.\n- be careful with words receptive/insertive vaginal/anal sex in post- exposure prophylaxis recommendations\n- For more detail check HIV transmission and needle stick injury module in infection.\n- be familiar with entire guideline, you may get questions in different ways.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 35-year-old woman, who gave birth two weeks prior, presented to the ED with complaints of headache and convulsions. On examination, she was found to have weakness in her left arm and sensory disturbances. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Idiopathic intracranial hypertension\n- **B)** Migraine\n- **C)** Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n- **D)** Stroke\n- **E)** Cerebral venous thrombosis Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (CVST) Cerebral venous thrombosis\n- It is rare and accounts for 0.5% of all strokes\n- It occurs more frequently in young adults and children. 2,3 It is three times more common in females than in males.\n- The principle pathology of CVT is thrombosis of cerebral veins and the cornr-nonest site of origin is believed to be the junction of cerebral veins\n\nand larger sinuses.\n- Once a thrombus is formed in the cerebral or cortical veins, its extension can occlude large draining venous sinuses.\n- Risk factors\n- Thrombophilias - Genetic — eg Factor V Leiden Acquired — eg antiphospholipid syndrome\n- Infection - Intracranial Regional eg ear, nose, throat, head, neck Systemic\n- Female - specific risk factors are more important in younger age groups (ie oestrogencontaining contraceptives, pregnancy and puerperium/postpartum - is the 6—8-week period from the time of delivery of the placenta up to the resumption of normal ovulation).\n- Malignancy - intracranial malignancy is more common in older age group\n- Inflammatory - Vasculitis eg Behqet's disease Systemic lupus erythematosus Inflammatory bowel disease Sarcoidosis.\n- Medications - Oral contraceptives Steroids Anti-neoplastic drugs (particularly L-asparaginase\n- Sepsis\n- Endocrine disorder — hyperthyroidism\n- Clinical features\n- Headache — Migraine, raised ICP, Thunderclap, Ear/mastoid pain.\n- Focal neurological deficits - Hemiparesis, cranial nerve palsy, sensory disturbance, aphasia and ataxia.\n- Seizure— focal generalized\n- Visual disturbance - Reduced acuity, Reduced/altered visual field, Diplopia, Papilloedema.\n\n- Meningism — neck pain/stiffness, Photophobia\n- Reduced consciousness — Drowsiness, Corna\n- Cognitive impairment — Encephalopathy, Disorientation, Amnesia\n- Investigations MRI and magnetic resonance venography (MR-V) are also sensitive tools for identifying CVT\n- catheter angiography is useful when there is doubt about the anatomy, but the requirement of highly skilled operators limits its access to neuroscience centres.\n- Management\n- Currently, the recommendation is immediate commencement of therapeutic LMWH or UFH. In addition to anticoagulation, underlying aetiological factors should be investigated and managed.\n- European Federation of Neurological Societies (EFNS) guidelines recommend that patients should be anticoagulated even in the presence of haemorrhage. Evidence for this is limited to two small randomised controlled trials (RCTs) of unfractionated heparin (UFH) 22 and low-r-nolecular weight heparin (LMVVH). Both RCTs showed better clinical outcor-nes and reduced mortality rates in patients receiving anticoagulation compared with controls.\n- If anticoagulation is contraindicated, or in cases of severe CVT not responding to anticoagulants, endovascular thrombolysis or mechanical thrombectomy might be an option.\n- Steroids are not recommended and are linked to a poorer prognosis in\n\n- Antiepileptic drugs should not be given routinely as prophylaxis but should be initiated quickly if a seizure occurs.\n- Following the immediate management of CVT, long-term vitamin K antagonists, such as warfarin, with a target international normalised ratio (INR) of 2—3 should be used.\n- Guidelines recornmend anticoagulation for 3—6 months in provoked CVT, 6—12 months in unprovoked CVT and potentially lifelong in recurrent CVT, VTE following CVT, or CVT associated with severe thrombophilias.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 62-year-old male with a history of prostate cancer presents to the emergency department with acute onset low back pain, and midline tenderness. His MRI shows spinal cord compression. What corticosteroid would you recommend to this patient?\n- **A)** Fludrocortisone acetate 100mg\n- **B)** Dexamethasone 18mg\n- **C)** Methylprednisolone 60mg\n- **D)** Hydrocortisone 50mg\n- **E)** Prednisolone 30mg Explanation\n\n- Nice recommends dexamethasone at least 16mg in malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC) patients Malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC) It is caused by a tumour pressing on the spinal cord or cauda equina.\n- Spinal tumours can be primary, or secondary to metastatic disease (e.g., of breast, prostate, or lung cancer). Clinical features\n- Pain in the middle (thoracic) or upper (cervical) spine\n- Progressive lower (lumbar) spinal pain\n- Severe unremitting lower spinal pain\n-\n- Spinal pain aggravated by straining (for example, at stool, or when coughing or sneezing) Localised spinal tenderness\n- Nocturnal spinal pain preventing sleep.\n-\n- Bladder or bowel dysfunction Sensory loss\n- Weakness or paralysis\n- Numbness or paraesthesias\n- Back and neck pain usually the first symptom\n- Investigation\n-\n- MRI\n- If patient is diabetes, IV drug user and exam findings (fever, rnidline spinal tenderness) concerning spinal epidural abscess, in such patients\n\nMR imaging of the entire spine with gadolinium contrast recommended.\n- Consider targeted CT scan with 3-plane reconstruction to assess spinal stability and plan vertebroplasty, kyphoplasty or spinal surgery in patients with MSCC.\n- Consider myelography if other imaging modalities are contraindicated or inadequate.\n- Management (NICE guideline)\n- Analgesia - Offer conventional analgesia (including NSAIDs, non-opiate and opiate medication).\n- Bisphosphonate - Offer patients with vertebral involvement from myeloma or breast cancer bisphosphonates to reduce pain and the risk of vertebral fracture or collapse.\n- Radiography - Offer patients with spinal metastases causing non- mechanical spinal pain 8 Gy single fraction palliative radiotherapy even if they are completely paralysed.\n- Vertebroplasty and kyphoplasty — recommended in selected patients who who have vertebral metastases and no evidence of MSCC or spinal instability if they have:\n- mechanical pain resistant to conventional analgesia, or\n- vertebral body collapse\n- Surgery - Urgently consider patients with spinal metastases and imaging evidence of structural spinal failure with spinal instability for surgery to stabilise the spine and prevent MSCC.\n- Corticosteroid - offer all patients with MSCC a loading dose ofat least 16 mg of dexamethasone as soon as possible after assessment,\n\nfollowed by a short course of 16 mg dexamethasone daily while treatment is being planned.\n- Continue dexamethasone 16 mg daily in patients awaiting surgery or radiotherapy for MSCC. After surgery or the start of radiotherapy the dose should be reduced gradually over 5 to 7 days and stopped. . Note — at least 16mg but you can give 18mg\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nWhich of the following is the high-risk source of HIV?\n- **A)** Needlestick from a discarded needle in the community\n- **B)** Who has blood transfusion in the UK before 1990\n- **C)** Blood transfusion in the USA\n- **D)** Semen splash on eye from HIV positive source\n- **E)** Unprotected anal course with sub-Saharan people Explanation\n- High-risk-source-for-HLV MSM ( men who have sex with men)\n- Female sexual contacts of MSM\n- People reporting current or prior injecting drug use\n- Sex workers\n- Trans women\n-\n\n- People from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence (>1%)* i-e Originally from sub-Saharan Africa.\n- People reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where contact occurs\n- Individuals known to have/have had a mother living with HIV and who do not have docur\n- nented HIV-negative status\n- Note - For more detail check HIV transmission and needle stick injury module in infection.  IO. A 17-year-old male presented to the ED following road traffic accident. On examination, he had penetrating wound over Achilles' tendon, and she had loss of sensation over posterolateral distal leg and planter foot. What nerve injured in this injury?\n- **A)** Sciatic nerve   B\n- POSterior tibial nerve\n- **C)** Anterior-tibial nerve\n- **D)** Common fibular nerve\n- **E)** Sural nerve Explanation\n\n- The Achilles tendon is innervated by the posterior tibial nerve, which is a branch of the sciatic nerve. Loss of sensation over the posterolateral distal leg and plantar foot suggests injury to the tibial nerve.\n- The posterior tibial nerve describes the distal end of the tibial nerve as it passes around the medial rnalleolus in the tarsal tunnel. Anatomically, the structure is a continuation its parent tibial nerve in a specific region of the distal extremity and does not represent a branch of the tibial nerve or a distinct structure. Accordingly, some texts will refer to it as the tibial nerve. Moreover, there is no anterior tibial nerve at the level of the foot or ankle. However, many authors and texts refer to the structure as the posterior tibial nerve due to its clinical significance. previous\n	\N	[]	\N	f	41	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
23870db9-33b5-418a-ae7e-4db053bd0860	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-41	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 41	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 41\n\nWhat is your immediate priority in the management?   A Transfer to intensive care\n- **B)** Intravenous antibiotics\n- **C)** withdrawal of ETT 3cm\n- **D)** CT head to exclude a bleed\n- **E)** Suctioning down the ETT Explanation\n- Airway should be priority - the patient Sp02 of 90%with ventilation on Fi02 1.0 which indicates that there is an obstruction in the airway. The collapsed right upper lobe could be due to secretion accumulation. Therefore, suctioning down the ETT, is the most appropriate immediate priority. This is because the patient may have blood or secretions obstructing the endotracheal tube (ETT) leading to ineffective ventilation and oxygenation. After suctioning, the patient's airway should be reassessed, and his ventilation and oxygenation parameters should be optimized. If the patient remains hypoxic, additional measures such as increasing the Fi02, adjusting the ventilator settings, or providing positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) may be required.\n\n- Tracheobronchial anatomy.\n- Correct ET tube position\n\nTube tip r: '5cm Carina\n- ET Tubes - Complications\n- If inserted too far, an endotracheal tube (ET tube) can enter the right or left main bronchus. This results in ventilation of a single lung and can result in collapse of the contralateral lung or a lobe of the intubated lung.\n- Accidental intubation ofa bronchus is more common on the right because the right main bronchus is more vertically orientated than the left main bronchus.\n- Oesophageal intubation - Oesophageal intubation may be a fatal complication and should be detected clinically before a patient is sent for a chest X-ray.\n\n- ET tube misplaced at carina Trachea Tube tip Carin\n- El-tube-misplaced-in-right-main-bronchus\n\nJul\n- Misplaced ET tube with lung collapse\n\nnn\n- ETT should be withdrawal into the trachea.\n- below X-ray (A ) as above before and (B) after ETT withdrawal into the trachea\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nYou've inserted CVP line on the left side internal jugular vein in a 32-year-old female patient. She is now complaining of pain in her left arm and appear pallor. What would be the cause of her condition?\n- **A)** Hematoma\n\nB\n- Air embolism\n- **C)** Arterial cannulation\n- **D)** Compartment syndrome   E\n- Thrombosis Explanation Apart from compartment syndrome all are the complication of CVP line, however they symptoms differently.\n- Based on above scenario the rnost likely cause would be thrombosis as its mentioned CVP line inserted in IJV.\n- Air-embolism patient would presents chest pain, shortness of breath, and neurological symptoms. However, it is less likely to cause pain and pallor in the arm.\n- Arterial puncture occurs in 4.2-9.3% of line placements, and is often easily recognized secondary to pulsatile flow, but recognition may be difficult in a hypotensive and critically-ill patient. Arterial injury occurs rnost commonly in femoral catheter placement and least commonly in subclavian placement. Prolonged arterial catheterization can result in thrombus, neurologic deficits, and stroke.\n- Note - you may have this question in a different way. Therefore, be familiar with the entire complication of CVP line.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nWhat specific finding of CO differentiates between CO and cyanide poisoning?\n- **A)** Increase respiratory rate   B\n- High lactate level\n- **C)** Sa02 98% on air   D\n- Cherry red skin\n- **E)** Hypotension Explanation\n- New study and literature suggests that Cherry-red discolouration of the skin and mucous membranes is an often-quoted sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning. very rarer to be seen in cyanide.\n- Sa02 98% on air: This indicates good oxygen saturation and does not differentiate between CO and cyanide poisoning.\n- Rest findings are seen in both CO and cyanide. Note — we had revised CO and cyanide poisoning module, that Cherry- red discolouration of the skin and mucous membranes is an often-\n\nquoted sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning. very rarer to be seen in cyanide.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 15-year-old girl with her 24 years old boyfriend came to the ED with a complaint of lower abdominal pain. After examination, she is fine to be discharge. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Advise her to tell her parents\n- **B)** Give antibiotics and discharge her\n- **C)** Call her parents immediately   D Inform the police\n- **E)** Discharge her with advice Explanation\n- In this case you should inform the police or safeguarding authority as her partner is much older than her, there is possibility of child abuse or exploitation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 41 -year-old male presented to the ED after sustaining a traumatic left lower limb injury as shown. There are no other injuries and his observations are all within normal limit. What would be your next immediate step of management?\n- **A)** Irrigation and tetanus booster\n- **B)** Tetanus booster and antibiotics\n- **C)** Saline-soaked swab and antibiotics\n- **D)** Irrigation and IV antibiotics\n- **E)** Saline-soaked swab and tetanus Explanation\n- The image shows open fracture.\n- Management of open fractures before wound excision/debridement (NICE guideline)\n- Do not irrigate open fractures of the long bones, hindfoot or midfoot in the emergency department before wound excision.\n\n- Consider a saline-soaked dressing covered with an occlusive layer (if not already applied) for open fractures in the emergency department before wound excision.\n- In the emergency department, administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics immediately to people with open fractures if not already given.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 63-year-old male known diabetic patient presented to the ED with complaint of vomiting and abdominal pain. He is on oral antidiabetic medication canagliflozin and insulin. His VBG shown. What is the likely diagnosis?   A\n- Euglycemic DKA\n- **B)** Acute Pancreatitis\n- **C)** Canagliflozin adverse effects\n- **D)** Sepsis\n- **E)** Alcohol ketoacidosis Explanation\n\n- Patient clinical features, history of diabetes and use of canagliflozin and insulin puts them at risk for DKA. The presence of metabolic acidosis with high lactate on VBG are all consistent with DKA. Additionally, the patient's blood glucose level of 6.7mmoI/L (which is not particularly high) is consistent with euglycemic DKA. Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis (EDKA)\n- It is an uncommon diabetic complication associated with several risk factors such as fasting, surgery, pregnancy, and now the use of sodium- glucose cotransporter 2 (SCLT2) inhibitors.\n- Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA, EDKA) is a clinical syndrome occurring both in type I (TIDM) and type 2 (T2DM) diabetes mellitus characterized by:\n- Confirm ketosis by measuring blood ketone (>3.0 mmol/L)\n- Confirm acidosis with a venous bicarbonate (<15mmol/L) or venous\n- Euglycemia - Glucose levels may be normal\n- People with type 2 diabetes taking SCLT-2 inhibitors are at higher risk (the relative risk is around 1.3 per 1000 person- years) of developing diabetic ketoacidosis.\n- Suspect DKA in presence of nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, difficulty breathing, confusion, fatigue and drowsiness\n- EDKA has grown with the introduction of sodium-glucose transporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitors.\n\n- Ketone body production in EDKA is similar to DKA with acetoacetic acid, beta-hydroxybutyric acid (after acetoacetic acid reduction), and acetone (after acetoacetic acid decarboxylation). The resulting anion gap metabolic acidosis triggers respiratory compensation.\n- Anaerobic glycolysis due to inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygenation as well as the metabolic derangements itself present in DKA might contribute to the elevated lactate levels\n- Preparation and use of SCLT inhibitors in the UK Name of SGLT inhibitor SGLT-2 inhibitors: Dapagliflozin (1 Omg OD) Canagliflozin (100-300mg OD) Empagliflozin (10-25mg OD) Ertugliflozin (5 — 15mg OD) SGLT 1+2 inhibitors: Sotagliflozin\n- Contraindications Theraputic indication Type 2 diabetes, symptomatic chronic heart failure (CHF) and chronic kidney disease (CKD) Type 2 diabetes Type 2 diabetes and symptomatic chronic heart failure Type 2 diabetes Not currently licensed\n\n- Do not prescribe an SGLT-2 inhibitor to people with:\n- DKA\n- Moderate-to-severe renal impairment — in general avoid initiation if estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) is less than 60 mL/min/I.73 m2; avoid continuation if eGFR is less than 45 mL/min/1.73 m2.\n- Note: dapagliflozin is licensed for use in chronic kidney disease, but should not be initiated in people with eGFR less than 15mL/min/1.73 m2\n- Severe hepatic impairment\n- Increasing age — avoid empagliflozin if aged over 85 years\n- Active foot disease (such as skin ulceration, osteomyelitis, or gangrene) . enefit B_sofusin gSGLTZinhibitorsjmpeopNe-with-type-2 diabetes\n- Reduction in HbA1c - Up to 10 mmol/mol with some agents, dependent on starting HbAIc level\n- Low incidence of hypoglycaemia 9\n- The mechanism of action means that their effect is proportional to blood glucose\n- Weight loss (up to 3kg)\n- With a significant effect to reduce visceral fat\n- Reduces progression of chronic complications affecting cardiovascular system and kidneys (see next pages)\n- Risks-of-SGLT.:2-inhibitors-in-ty.pe-2-diabetes\n- Genital infections\n- DKA\n- Amputation and fracture in some trials (but not others) with canagliflozin\n\n- Rare-side-effects-reported-withSGLT:2--inhibitors\n- Fournier's gangrene. It is a rare but this serious bacterial infection needs urgent treatment\n- Suspect if there is pain, redness, swelling or discomfort in perineal or genital area. Needs urgent surgery and IV antibiotics\n- Management\n- If confirmed to have ketosis with acidosis (regardless of the glucose level) then start a fixed rate intravenous insulin infusion and IV fluids as per JBDS guidelines (joint British diabetic society guideline)\n- Concomitant additional glucose infusion may be required to avoid hypoglycaemia\n- Revisit prescribing in those with increasing frailty.\n- Note — be familiar with the entire module, you may have question in different ways.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 30-year-old male brought into the ED after RTA. Patient has developed seizure while you assess him. His eye image is shown Where is lesion in this image?\n- **A)** Right 4th nerve palsy\n- **B)** Left third nerve palsy\n- **C)** Left 2nd nerve palsy\n- **D)** Right third nerve palsy\n- **E)** Right 6th nerve palsy EXPIanation rd\n- The left eye is dilated which indicates 3 nerve palsy (CNIII).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 14-year-old girl came to the ED and asked for contraception pills. Her partner is her teacher at school. She doesn't want anyone to know about her relationship with the teacher and she refuses to involve her parents. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Inform the safeguarding authority\n- **B)** Refuse to give her contraception\n- **C)** Give contraception and discharge her\n- **D)** Advise her to tell her parents\n- **E)** Call her parents immediately Explanation\n- This is likely to be a case of exploitation.\n- The teacher has position of trust and this position shouldn't be abused\n- Inform the safeguarding. If this option isn't given then pick the option inform the police.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED after sustaining a traumatic left lower limb injury as shown. There are no other injuries and his observations are all within normal limit. what would be your next immediate step of management?\n- **A)** Irrigation and IV antibiotics\n- **B)** Saline-soaked swab and tetanus\n- **C)** Tetanus booster and antibiotics\n- **D)** Irrigation and tetanus booster\n- **E)** Saline-soaked swab and antibiotics Explanation\n- The image shows lacerated wound and the wound is at high risk of infection. This wound is at taken Clean the wound as mentioned below (disinfection, Debride\n- devitalized tissue, Irrigate the wound with potable water or normal saline do not close the wound as mentioned below\n- For infected wounds or wounds with a high risk of infection, the\n- wound should be cleaned and dressed, and then observed for 2—5 days to ensure no infection is apparent before it is closed (delayed primary closure). For chronic wounds or wounds in which the edges cannot be\n- approximated, the wound should be allowed to heal naturally with no attempts to aid wound closure (secondary closure). Leaving the wound open allows\n- a swab of the wound before starting antibiotic treatment. Consider the need for tetanus prophylaxis for susceptible wounds,\n- such as those exposed to soil or other external contaminants. Provide verbal and written advice on wound management.\n- Review the person 2—5 days after the initial presentation\n-\n\n- If there are signs of infection and the person has not received antibiotic treatment, prescribe empirical antibiotics and arrange a further review. Take a swab of the wound before starting antibiotic treatment.\n- If there are signs of infection despite empirical antibiotics, review the swab results, change the antibiotics if indicated, and arrange further review.\n- If there are no signs of an infection, close the wound and advise on wound closure removal.\n- Note - you may have image in different ways. Laceration\n- A laceration is a deep cut or tear in the skin and/or underlying tissue commonly caused by blunt traurna (such as a fall or collision), incision by a sharp object, or mammalian bite:\n- Assessment\n- Detail time of the injury.\n- The cause of the injury, for example, a sharp object or fall.\n- Past medical history, including conditions that could impair wound healing and/or increase the risk of complications, for example, diabetes, vascular disease, nutritional deficiency, or use of immunosuppressive drugs (such as chemotherapy).\n- Immunization status, specifically the tetanus vaccine history.\n\n- Allergies, specifically to latex, anaesthetics, or antibiotics Examination - Assess for :\n- Vascular damage, characterized by arterial bleeding from the wound,\n- loss of pulse, or poor perfusion distal to the injury. Nerve damage, characterized by loss of light touch or motor function\n- distal to the injury. Injury to a tendon, bone, or joint.\n- Impairment of blood supply to surrounding tissue.\n- Assess for signs or symptoms of infection - such as general malaise,\n- fever, rigors, erythema, and/or lymphadenopathy. If there are no signs of infection, assess the risk of infection\n-\n- A laceration is at high risk of infection if it is contaminated with soil, Other risk factors for infection include: Wound length of more than 5 cm.\n-\n- Age older than 65 years.\n- Diabetes mellitus. Stellate shape or jagged wound margins.\n- Devitalized tissue and/or foreign body in the wound.\n- Presentation more than 6 hours after injury (although there is some\n- evidence that this may not be as important as previously thought).\n- Although-rare,sus abrasions, or scars and the explanation is inconsistent or\n\nunreliable.Examples include lacerations, abrasions, or scars:\n- On a child who is not independently mobile.\n- That are multiple.\n- With a symmetrical distribution.\n- On areas usually protected by clothing (such as the back, chest, abdomen, axilla, and genital area).\n- On the eyes, ears, or sides of the face.\n- On the neck, ankles, or wrists, that look like ligature marks.\n- If the wound is at Iow risk of infection\n- Clean the wound\n- Disinfect the surrounding skin with an antiseptic - Avoid getting antiseptic into the wound.\n- Keep hair out of the wound\n- Debride devitalized tissue, and pick out as much foreign material as possible.\n- Irrigate the wound - with potable water, cooled boiled water, or normal saline.\n- Close the wound Determine an appropriate method of wound closure.\n- For clean, non-infected wounds - immediate (primary) closure (with a meticulous approximation of wound edges) is recommended\n\n- Sutures - are used to close wounds and it should be done under local anesthesia\n- Suturing is preferred for wounds longer than 5 cm, or those 5 cm or shorter when the wound:\n- Requires careful approximation, for example, lacerations that cross skin tension lines\n- Is subject to excessive flexing and tension (such as over joints or thick dermis), or excessive wetting.\n- There are two types of sutures : - Absorbable sutures are made from either natural mammalian collagen or synthetic polymers.\n- Nonabsorbable sutures are made from synthetic materials, such as nylon, polybutester, polypropylene, or natural silk, and require removal after placement.\n- Nonabsorbable sutures are made from synthetic materials, such as nylon, polybutester, polypropylene, or natural silk, and require removal after placement.\n- Surgical staples- are alternatives to sutures and are useful for linear lacerations on the scalp or extremities.\n- Tissue adhesives (or glues)- are liquid cyanoacrylate monomers that polymerize into long solid chains upon contact with blood or a wound surface, thereby holding wound edges together.\n- They can be used to close wounds that:\n\nAre small (5 cm or shorter), clean, and non-infected.\n- Have edges that are easily apposed without leaving any dead space.\n- Are not subject to excessive flexing, tension, or wetting.\n- Are not in a hair-bearing area, unless hair apposition technique is being\n- used. Are not a result of mammalian bite.\n- sterile strips that are used for wound closure.\n- Skin closure strips can be effective for closing small, simple lacerations in Iow-tension areas with well-approximated edges; however, their lack of tensile strength can lead to wound dehiscence.\n- They are more commonly useful in achieving initial wound edge approximation, to be followed by definitive fixation with a tissue adhesive. When to remove wound closure? Sutures\n- Nonabsorbable sutures - should be removed within 14 days of\n- placement Sutures on the head and neck are usually removed 5—7 days after\n- placement Sutures on the trunk or extremities are usually removed 10—14 days\n- after placement.\n- Absorbable sutures - break down in the body and do not require removal. most types should start to dissolve or fall out within 7—14 days.\n\n- Surgical-staples\n- should be removed within 14 days of placement, depending on the depth and location of the wound\n- Staples on the head and neck are usually removed 5—7 days after placement.\n- Staples on the trunk or extremities are usually removed 10—14 days after placement.\n- Tissue adhesives\n- The glue will peel off in 5—8 days.\n- An antibiotic ointment, acetone, or petroleum jelly can help facilitate the removal. Adhesive strips\n- The adhesive strips need to stay in place for 5—7 days to allow the wound to heal.\n- To remove the strips, they should begin at one end of the incision and gently peel the skin closure off toward the other end of the incision.\n- Wound dressing\n- British National Formulary(BNF) for suggestions on choices of dressings for different types of wounds.\n\n- Non-absorbent, low-adherence dressings - are suitable for clean, granulating, lightly exuding wounds without necrosis. They are placed directly on the wound bed.\n- Perforated film absorbent dressings - are suitable only for wounds with mild to moderate announts of exudate. Dressings with an absorbent cellulose or polymer wadding layer are suitable for use on moderately to heavily exuding wounds.\n- Advanced wound dressings -are designed to control the environment for wound healing, for example, to donate fluid (hydrogels), maintain hydration (hydrocolloids), or absorb wound exudate (alginates and foams).\n- Hydrogel, hydrocolloid, and medical grade honey dressings - can be used to deslough wounds by promoting autolytic debridement.\n- Sterile larvae (maggots) - are also available for biosurgical removal of wound debris.\n- Tetanus pæpbylaxis\n- Check the tetanus immunization status of the person with a laceration and give accordingly .\n- If the wound is at high risk of infection\n- Clean-the-wound_as mentioned above (disinfection, Debride devitalized tissue, Irrigate the wound with potable water or normal saline\n- Initially, dress but do not close the wound as mentioned above\n\n- For infected wounds or wounds with a high risk of infection, the wound should be cleaned and dressed, and then observed for 2—5 days to ensure no infection is apparent before it is closed (delayed primary closure).\n- For chronic wounds or wounds in which the edges cannot be approximated, the wound should be allowed to heal naturally with no attempts to aid wound closure (secondary closure). Leaving the wound open allows granulation tissue to form contraction of the wound edges and eventual epithelialization.\n- Consider the need for antibiotics. Take a swab of the wound before starting antibiotic treatrnent.\n- Consider the need for tetanus prophylaxis for susceptible wounds, such as those exposed to soil or other external contaminants.\n- Provide verbal and written advice on wound management.\n- Review the person 2—5 days after the initial presentation\n- If there are signs of infection and the person has not received antibiotic treatment, prescribe empirical antibiotics and arrange a further review. Take a swab of the wound before starting antibiotic treatment.\n- If there are signs of infection despite empirical antibiotics, review the swab results, change the antibiotics if indicated, and arrange further review.\n- If there are no signs of an infection, close the wound and advise on wound closure removal.\n- Management of open fractures before wound (NICE\n\n- Do not irrigate open fractures of the long bones, hindfoot or midfoot in the emergency department before wound excision.\n- Consider a saline-soaked dressing covered with an occlusive layer (if not already applied) for open fractures in the emergency department before wound excision.\n- In the emergency department, administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics immediately to people with open fractures if not already given. 10 / 12 IO. A 50-year-old male patient with laryngectomy in situ brought to the ED with complain of dizziness, vomiting. One of your junior colleague has passed NG tube. To confirm NG tube, x-ray was done which is shown. What would be your next step?\n- **A)** Remove the NG tube immediately and reinsert it O\n- **B)** Check PH\n- **C)** Advance the NG tube 2-3 cm\n- **D)** Withdraw the NC tube 2-3cm\n- **E)** Start feeding Explanation\n\n- The X-ray shows NG tube in the right side of midline and it's in the duodenum, the appropriate next step would be withdraw the tube and reposition it.\n- The gastroduodenal junction is located just to the right side of the midline (spine indicates midline)\n- To ensure the tube is not in the duodenum, its tip is ideally placed more proximally in the stomach to the left of the midline. However in this X-ray NG tube in the right side of midline and it's in the duodenum, therefore, for feeding into the stomach this tube should be withdrawn and its position confirmed either by a further attempt of aspiration of gastric fluid or by repeating the X-ray.\n- Management steps entirely depends on tip of Ng tube, If NG tube in the airway it should removed and reinserted\n- if it's in just below gastro-oesophageal junction then it should be advanced further, for safe positioning it is recommended that a NG tube tip is located at least 10 cm distal to the gastro-oesophageal junction (green target area) (example with x-rays given below).\n- If tip of Ng tube in the duodenum it should be withdrawn and repositioned. Note - we haven't find similar x-ray of laryngectomy in situ with misplaced NG tube. however, below an x-ray, be familiar with that, it gives you an idea how it would be look like.\n\n- NC Tubes - Position confirmation\n- If aspiration of gastric fluid following placement of an nasogastric (NG) tube is unsuccessful, then a chest X-ray can be used to help determine tube position.\n\n- Oesophagus anatomy.\n- A correctly positioned NG tube passes vertically down the oesophagus into the stomach. As the oesophagus is not visible on a plain chest X- ray, it is essential to have an understanding of its anatomical position in relation to other visible structures Trachea Aortic knuckle Carina Oesophagus 'E. Stomach luncti\n- Correct NG tube position\n- A correctly positioned nasogastric tube passes vertically in the midline, or just to the left of the midline. Below the level of the carina the tube must not follow the course of either of the main bronchi, but should remain in the midline down to the level of the diaphragm where it passes through the gastro-oesophageal junction.\n\n- The tip of the NG tube should be visible at least 10 cm beyond the gastro-oesophageal junction.\n- not visible nn\n- NG tube tiæposition - Image 2 - tip_y.i.sj.b.le\n- An additional image shows the NG tube tip located below the diaphragm on the left side of the abdomen\n\n- NC tube complications\n- Incorrect positioning of a nasogastric tube is common\n- Passing food or medication via a NC tube into the airways carries a high risk of mortality\n- NG tube\n- The tube passes below the level of the carina and does not follow the course of the right or left bronchi\n- The tube is, therefore, in the oesophagus and has not been inhaled\n- The tube is looped back on itself so its tip is located in the upper oesophagus\n- Feeding via a tube in this position would risk aspiration of the feed into the lungs\n- The tube must be repositioned\n\nTube Trac hea Carina D_iaphrogth\n- Tube-misplacement-in-right-main-bronchus The tube follows the course of the right main bronchus\n- Its tip is projected over the lower zone of the right lung\n-\n- The NC tube has been inhaled rather than swallowed The tube must be removed and repositioned\n- unction,\n\n- Tube misplacement in proximal stomach\n- This tube has reached the stomach but it is not in a safe position for feeding\n- If left in this position there is a risk of it being displaced proximally when the patient changes posture\n- For safe positioning it is recommended that a NG tube tip is located at least IO cm distal to the gastro-oesophageal junction (green target\n\n- Tube misplacement in duodenum\n- The gastroduodenal junction is located just to the right side of the midline (spine indicates midline)\n- To ensure the tube is not in the duodenum, its tip is ideally placed more proximally in the stomach to the left of the midline\n- For feeding into the stomach this tube should be withdrawn and its position confirmed either by a further attempt of aspiration of gastric fluid or by repeating the X-ray\n\n11 / 12 11 . A 75-year-old female stroke patient brought to the ED by her daughter with a complaint of abdominal pain. On examination, you've found multiple bruises on her body, later you've discovered she was physically abused by her daughter. You've encouraged her to let you contact social services about her situation, however, she refused. What would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Respect her wish and do not involve the social services\n- **B)** Admit her in psychiatric ward\n- **C)** Call the police immediately and arrest her daughter\n- **D)** Ask more detail from her daughter\n- **E)** Involve the social service Explanation\n- You've to assume she has mental capacity to make her own decision around disclosure of information.\n- You've encouraged her to allow you to contact social services, however she refused. Therefore, you need to respect her decision of not allowing disclosure, even if you consider this decision to be unwise.\n- Note - If you suspect any patient who doesn't have capacity, after performing mental capacity assessment and you found he/she lacks capacity, then social services or appropriate authority should be involved. 12 / 12\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 12:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of sudden onset of chest pain while eating which is lasted for 20 minutes. He is pain free now. His examination revealed diastolic murmur. His lab markers given below. Trop-l : 16 (<14) D-dimer: 380 ng/ml What would be the most likely diagnosis?   A\n- Acute coronary syndrome\n- **B)** Thoracic aortic dissection\n- **C)** Cardiomyopathy\n- **D)** Pulmonary embolism\n- **E)** Aortic aneurysm Explanation\n- Patient clinical and lab features are suggestive of acute coronary syndrome, as Trop-l is elevated and D-dimer in the normal range. Here the diastolic murmur heard on examination may be indicative of an underlying valvular heart disease, which can be a risk factor for ACS.\n- In aortic dissection, D-dimer is elevated (<500ng/mI normal range) and Trop-l is usually negative. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	42	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
520c3b10-4779-4672-b1e1-c2041081cd3b	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-42	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 42	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 42\n\n- **B)** Remove inner tube\n- **C)** use a small sized suction catheter\n- **D)** Close stoma and perform bag mask ventilation\n- **E)** Remove and replace tracheostomy tube\n- Explanation\n- After your initial steps (removal Of humidifier, speaking valve, and inner tube change if present) your next step would be suction to assess the tracheostomy patency.\n- Appropriately-sized suction catheters\n- Suction catheters should be no greater than half the internal diameter of the tube. A new sterile catheter needs to be used for each suctioning procedure.\n- When suctioning, insert the catheter 0.5 cm past the end of the tracheostomy tube. You can measure this using the spare tube in the emergency box.\n\nAIRWAY: Emergency Paediatric Tracheostomy Management SAFETY - STIMULATE - SHOUT FOR HELP - OXYGEN Ched Safe area, Stimulate, and Shout for help Open ChMs airway: head tilt/ chin lift/ pillow or towel under shoulders may help Ensure hO flow oxygen to the tracheostomy AND the as soon as oxygen CAPNOGRAPHY: Exhaled carbon dioxidewaveform mav indicatea patent airway(advanced response) SUCTION TO ASSESS TRACHEOSTOMY PATENCY Q) c o u c o Q > Remove attachments: humidifier (HME), speaking valve Change inner tube (if present) Inner m n to to Can you pass a SUCTION catheter? Yes The tracheostomy tube is patent Perform tracheal suction Consider partial Obstruction CONTINUE ASSESSMENT (ABCDE) nee EMERGENCY TRACHE TOMY TUBE CHANGE ate cuff (if present). Reassess patency after any tu 1st chanee — same size tube 2nd change — one-half size smaller tube 3rd change\n- over suction Zu ide IF UNSUCCESSFUL - REMOVE THE TUBE IS THE PATIENT BREATHING? - Look, listen and feel at the mouth and tracheostorny/stoma 0 CALL FOR HELP: 2222 in hospital, 999 in community 5 RESCUE BREATHS Patent pper Airway— use the nose/mouth Obstructed Upper Airway— use the tracheostomy/stoma NO SIGNS OF LIFE? START CPR IS compressions : 2 rescue breaths Ensure help or resuscitation team called Yes Continue oxygen Stabilize Reassess Plan for defin itive airway if tube change failure primary emergency ox-yeenation Standard ORAL airway manoeuvres Cover the stoma (swabs / hand). Bag\n- valve-face mask Oral or nasal airway adjuncts Supraglottic Airway (SGA) e.g. Laryngeal Mask Airway (IMA) Tracheostomy STOMA ventilation Paediatric face mask applied to stoma SGA applied to stoma Secondary emereerwy oxwenatim ORAL intubation with endotracheal tube Uncut tube, advanced beyond stoma One half-size smalkrthan tracheostomy tube 'Difficult Airway' Expert and Equipment\n- Attempt intubation Of STOMA 3.0 IO tracheostomy or endotracheal tube 'Difficult Airway' Expert and Equipment\n- *EQUIPMENT: Fibreoptic scope. bougie, airway exchange catheter, Airway trolley NBP (Paediatric Working Group) www.tracheostomy.org.uk Review January 2020\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 22-year-old man presents after he was assaulted while walking in the park. He is complaining of loin pain. Which of the following would indicate the need for CT of the kidney?\n- **A)** Raised urea\n- **B)** Microscopic haematuria\n- **C)** Pain on passing urine\n- **D)** Loin tenderness   E\n- Visible haematuria\n- Explanation\n- Perform-a multiphase CT scan in renal trauma patient with:\n- Visible Haematuria\n- Non-visible haematuria and one episode of hypotension and systolic blood pressure < 90 mm Hg\n- In stable trauma patients whose mechanism of injury or physical exam findings raise concern for renal injury (eg, rapid deceleration; a significant blow to the flank; rib fracture; significant flank ecchymosis; penetrating injury of the abdomen, flank, or lower chest).\n- Renal trauma evaluation and management\n\n- Kidney_injury scale (based on AAST 2018 revision . AAST: American Association for the Surgery of Trauma Grade r Grade Grade Arterial Grade Grade v Of h i Ium\n\nGRADE DESCRIPTION OF INJURY Contusion or non-expanding sub-capsular haemotoma No laceration Non-expanding peri-renal haematoma Cortical laceration <lcm deep (without urinary extravasation) 1 Cortical laceration >lcm deep (without urinary extravasation) Laceration: Through corticomedullary junction into collecting system OR Vascular: Segmental renal artery or vein injury with contained haemotoma, or partial vessel laceration or vessel thrombosis\n- Laceration: Shattered kidney OR Vascular: renal pedicle avulsion CT Sith Anfibobcs should ftv *retratÉV irV*S. M unstable. CT\n- Ht\n- ME seetn\n- e JJ Stent "n\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 55-year-old male with a history of motor neuron disease presents in respiratory distress. He has a tracheostomy in situ and is recovering from bronchitis. On assessment, you notice he is tachypneic with paradoxical abdominal movement, whereby his abdomen is drawn inwards during inspiration. Which of the following actions is the most likely to be adequate to resolve this problem?\n- **A)** Chest physiotherapy to help him expectorate\n- **B)** Saline nebulizer to help loosen his secretions\n- **C)** Intravenous antibiotics to treat the infection\n- **D)** Replacement Of entire tracheostomy tube\n- **E)** Check tracheostomy inner tube for a blockage\n- Explanation\n- In this case, an appropriate action would be inner tube removal.\n\nEmergency tracheostomy management - Patent upper airway Call for airway expert help Look, listen & feel at the mouth and tracheostomy A Mapleson C System (e,g_ Waters Circuit') may help assessment if available use waveform capnography when available: eihaled carbon dioxide indicates a patent or partially patent airway No Call Resuscitation Team CPR if no pulse / Signs Of life Is the patient breathing? Assess tracheostomy patency Remove speaking valve or cap (if present) Remove inner tube Some inner tubes need to connect to breathing circuits Can you pass a suction catheter? No Deflate the cuff (if present) Look, listen feel at the mouth and tracheostomy use waveform capnography or Mapleson C if available Is the patient stable or improving? NO Yes Yes Apply high now oxygen to BOTH the face and the tracheostomy The tracheostomy tube is patent Perform tracheal suction Consider partial Obstruction Ventilate (via tracheostomy) if not breathing Continue ABCDE assessment Tracheostomy tube partially obstructed or displaced Continue ABCDE assessment REMOVE THE TRACHEOSTOMY TUBE Look, listen & feel at the mouth and tracheostomy. Ensure oxygen reapplied to face and Stoma use waveform capnography or Mapleson C if available Call Resuscitation team CPR if no pulse / signs of life No Is the patient breathing? Yes continue ABCDE assessment Primary emergency oxygenation ndary emergency oxygenation Standard ORAL airway manoeuvres Attempt ORAL intubation Cover the stoma (swabs / hand). use: prepare for difficult intubation Bag.\n- alve-mask Uncut tube, advanced beyond Stoma Oral or nasal airway adjuncts Supraglottic airway device e.g. LMA Attempt intubation of STOMA Small tracheostomy tube / 6.0 cuffed ETT Tracheostomy STOMA ventilation Consider Aintree catheter and fibreoptic paediatric face mask applied to stoma 'scope / Bougie / Airway exchange catheter LMA applied to Stoma National Tracheostomy Safety Project. Review date 1/4/16_ Feedback & resources at www.tracheostomy.org.uk\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 35-year-old lady developed a sudden onset occipital headache associated with multiple episodes of vomiting. She is currently on OCR She just had her hair washed in the local saloon yesterday. What is an investigation of choice for Vertebral artery dissection?\n- **A)** X-ray neck angiogram\n- **C)** CT spine D\n- MR angiography\n- **E)** Doppler ultrasound\n- Explanation\n- In the UK MR angiography is the investigation of choice for VAD and with advances in scanner technology the sensitivity of MRA is approaching that of the gold standard of cerebral angiography.\n- Non-contrast CT scan often is the initial choice however, it usually comes normal and this can be falsely reassuring unless the clinician has a high degree of suspicion for a cervical artery injury.\n- CT angiography(CTA) may provide a higher yield for those in whom the clinical suspicion of dissection is high. It may also be useful in trauma patients who are undergoing CT for other indications.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 41-year-old male presented to the ED after sustaining a traumatic left lower limb injury as shown. There are no other injuries and his observations are all within normal limit. What would be your next immediate step of management?\n- **A)** Irrigation and tetanus booster\n- **B)** Saline-soaked swab and antibiotics\n- **C)** Irrigation and IV antibiotics\n- **D)** Tetanus booster and antibiotics\n- **E)** Saline-soaked swab and tetanus Explanation\n- The image shows open fracture.\n- Management of open fractures before wound excision/debridement (NICE guideline)\n- Do not irrigate open fractures of the long bones, hindfoot or midfoot in the emergency department before wound excision.\n- Consider a saline-soaked dressing covered with an occlusive layer (if not already applied) for open fractures in the emergency department before wound excision.\n- In the emergency department, administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics immediately to people with open fractures if not\n\nalready given.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 47-year-old gardener has accidentally stabbed his right foot with a garden fork while working in sandals. He has an open wound over his right foot contaminated with manure. He tells you that he had a full course of vaccinations as a child. His image is shown. What would you advise him regarding tetanus prophylaxis?\n- **A)** He should have a tetanus booster and human tetanus immunoglobulin in the same site in his left arm\n- **B)** He should have a tetanus booster in his left arm and human tetanus immunoglobulin in his right arm\n- **C)** He should have his tetanus antibodies checked before deciding on a booster\n- **D)** There is no need for any treatment as he is fully vaccinated\n- **E)** He should have a tetanus booster\n- Explanatiom A patient has a high-risk tetanus-prone wound as it is contaminated with manure and His tetanus immunization status is incomplete. He hasn't received an adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine with the last dose within 10 years. Therefore, he required a dose of vaccine and human tetanus immunoglobulin at different sites.\n\nImmunisation Status Those aged 1 1 years and over. who have received an adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine* with the last dose within 10 years Children aged 5-10 years who have received priming course and pre- school booster Children under S years who have received an adequate priming course Received adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine: but last dose more than 10 years ago Children aged 5-10 years who have received an adequate priming course but no preschool booster Includes UK born after 1961 with history of accepting vaccinations Not received adequate priming course Of tetanus vaccine' Includes uncertain immunisation Status and/ or born before 961 Immediate treatment Clean None required None required Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine Tetanus Prone None required Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine One dose of human tetanus immunoglobulin* in a different site High risk tetanus prone None required Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine One dose Of human tetanus immunoglobulin* in a different site Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine One dose of human tetanus immunoglobulin* in a different site\n- can be different in the exam. Further doses as required to complete the recom mended schedule (to ensure future immunity) Further doses as required to complete the recom mended schedule (to ensure future immunity)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 19-year-old man has presented with a rash which he first noticed after a walking holiday. It has slowly enlarged over ten days. He is systemically well with normal vital signs. His image is shown.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate action?\n- **A)** Refer to dermatology\n- **B)** Arrange test before giving antibiotics\n- **C)** Treat with topical steroids\n- **D)** Treat with antibiotics and then perform Elisa (Serology)\n- **E)** Treat with antibiotics without further investigation\n- Explanation\n- This patient has erythema migrans. Therefore, antibiotics should be started without further testing.\n- Nice guideline recommendations:\n- For all other people with erythema migrans who have no evidence of focal symptoms (for example, neurological, cardiac, or joint involvement), start treatment with oral antibiotics.\n- Positive or equivocal perform an imrnunoblot test for Lyme disease.\n- Consider starting treatment with antibiotics while waiting for the results if there is a high clinical suspicion of Lyme disease.\n- Negative and the person still has symptoms — review their history and symptoms, and consider an alternative diagnosis.\n- If Lyme disease is still suspected in people tested within 4 weeks from symptom onset, repeat the ELISA 4—6 weeks after the first ELISA test.\n\n- If Lyme disease is still suspected in people who have had symptoms for 12 weeks or more, perform an immunoblot test.\n- If the immunoblot test is:\n- Positive — diagnose Lyme disease. Start treatment with oral antibiotics.\n- Negative, but symptoms persist — consider a discussion with or referral to a specialist (regardless of the ELISA result) to review whether further tests are required or to consider alternative diagnoses.\n- Negative, and symptoms have resolved — no treatment is required.\n- Be cautious about diagnosing Lyme disease in people without a supportive history or positive serological testing because of the risk of:\n- Missing an alternative diagnosis.\n- Providing inappropriate treatment.\n- Note: Refer people with focal symptoms to the appropriate specialist, or consider a discussion with a specialist, but do not delay treatment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 43-year-old male patient with a Laryngectomy tube in situ presents to the ED with a blocked tube and dropping Sa02. After calling for help what is your initial step in management?\n- **A)** Remove Inner tube B\n- Apply 02 only to the stoma\n- **C)** Apply 02 to the face and stoma\n- **D)** Remove Laryngectomy tube\n- **E)** Deflate the cuff\n- Explanation\n- In patients with laryngectomy, we apply oxygen only to the laryngectomy stoma. However, in patients with tracheostomy, we apply high-flow oxygen to Both the face and tracheostomy.\n\nEmergency laryngectomy management Call for airway expert help Look, listen & feel at the mouth and laryngectomy stoma A Mapleson C System 'Waters circuit') may help assessment if available waveform capnography Whenever available: exhaled diOiide indicates a patent or partially patent airway No Call Resuscitation Team CPR if no pulse / Signs Of life Is the patient breathing? Yes Apply high flow oxygen to laryngectomy stoma If any doubt whether patient has a laryngectomy, apply oxygen to face also* Assess la n ectom stoma atenc Most laryngectomy stomas will NOT have a tube in situ Remove stoma cover (if present) Remove inner tube (if present ) inner tubes need to breathing Circuits 00 not remove a tracheoesophageal puncture (TEPI prosthesis Yes Can you pass a suction catheter? NO Deflate the cuff Of present) Look, listen & feel at the laryngectomy Stoma tube Use waveform capnography or Mapleson C if available Yes The laryngectomy stoma is patent Perform tracheal suction Consider partial obstruction Ventilate via stoma if not breathing Continue ABCDE assessment Continue ABCDE assessment Is the patient stable or improving? No REMOVE THE TUBE FROM THE LARYNGECTOMY STOMA if present Look, listen & feel at the laryngectomy stoma. Ensure oxygen is re-applied to stoma Use waveform capnography or Mapleson C if available No Call Resuscitation Team Is the patient breathing? Yes CPR if no pulse / signs of life Primary emergency oxygenation Laryngectomy stoma ventilation via either paediatric face mask applied to stoma LMA applied to Stoma continue ABCDE assessment Secondary emergency oxygenation Attempt intubation Of laryngectomy stoma Small tracheostomy tube / 6.0 cuffed ETT Consider Aintree catheter and fibreoptic 'scope / Bougie / Airway exchange catheter Laryngectomy patients have an end stoma and cannot be oxygenated via the mouth or nose Applying oxygen to the face and stoma is the default emergency action for all patients with a tracheostomy National Tracheostomy Safety Project. Review date I /1/20 Feedback & resources at www.tracheostomy.org.uk\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nAn otherwise healthy 3-year-old girl with a 3-day history of upper respiratory infection (URI) symptoms and 2 days of "barking cough" presents with fever and drooling. The patient's mother reports that over the past 2 to 3 hours, the child has had a significant increase in fever and is now drooling and has dysphagia. The child will not lie flat due to difficulty breathing. The child appears lethargic, however, sitting up, leaning forward, and drooling. She has inspiratory and expiratory stridor. Her observations are: BP  mmHg HR 140 beats/minute RR 40 breaths/minute Her X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Retropharyngeal abscess\n- **B)** peritonsillar abscess\n- **C)** Epiglottitis\n- **D)** Croup\n- **E)** Bacterial tracheitis\n- Explanation\n- Child clinical features "barking cough, drooling, and fever suggest Epiglottitis.\n- Her x-ray shows a "thumbprint sign" commonly seen in epiglottitis\n-\n- Acute eejglottis\n- commonly seen in 3 to 6 years of age\n- It's uncommon after the introduction of the Haemophilus influenza type b (Hib) vaccine\n-\n- It can also be caused by group A beta-hemolytic streptococcus\n- .Clinical feature\n-\n- pyrexia\n-\n- sore throat\n-\n- muffled speech\n- .drooling and stridor\n- .unable to speak,\n- .sitting upright drooling saliva in a 'sniffing position\n- Avoid oropharyngeal inspection as this could precipitate complete airway obstruction\n-\n- Thumb print sign on lateral view neck x-ray\n-\n- Management\n-\n\n-\n- Cive humidified 02\n- .1V antibiotic cefotaxime\n- .Call urgently for anaesthetic and ENT help, children under 6 years require urgent intubation\n-\n- In case of loss of airway obtain a surgical airway 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nYou've inserted CVP line on the left side internal jugular vein in a 32-year-old female patient. She is now complaining of pain in her left arm and appear pallor. What would be the cause of her condition?\n- **A)** Air embolism\n- **B)** Arterial cannulation\n- **C)** Thrombosis\n- **D)** Compartment syndrome\n- **E)** Hematoma Explanation Apart from compartment syndrome all are the complication Of CVP line, however they symptoms differently.\n- Based on above scenario the most likely cause would be thrombosis as its mentioned CVP line inserted in IJV.\n- Air-embolism patient would presents chest pain, shortness of breath, and neurological symptoms. However, it is less likely to cause pain and pallor in the arm.\n\n- Arterial puncture occurs in 4.2—9.3% of line placements, and is often easily recognized secondary to pulsatile flow, but recognition may be difficult in a hypotensive and critically-ill patient. Arterial injury occurs most commonly in femoral catheter placement and least commonly in subclavian placement. Prolonged arterial catheterization can result in thrombus, neurologic deficits, and stroke. may have this question in a different way. Therefore, be . ot familiar with the entire complication of CVP line. e_ previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	43	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
fe7cd4c7-7874-4315-8868-17d4155b91ce	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-43	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 43	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 43\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Streptococcal tonsillitis\n- **B)** Influenza A infection\n- **C)** Paramyxovirus infection (mumps)\n- **D)** Infectious mononucleosis\n- **E)** Viral pharyngitis\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggest glandular fever (infectious mononucleosis)\n- Glandular fever (infectious mononucleosis)\n- Glandular fever (infectious mononucleosis) is an infection rnost commonly caused by the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)\n- It causes approximately 80—90% of cases of glandular fever, with the remainder due largely to cytomegalovirus, human herpesvirus 6, toxoplasmosis, HIV, and adenovirus.\n- Glandular fever is most common in people aged 15—24 years and is spread mainly through contact with saliva, such as through kissing or sharing food and drink utensils.\n\n- Clinical features\n- Lymphadenopathy - Bilateral posterior cervical lymphadenopathy is typical. There may also be anterior cervical, submandibular, suboccipital, postauricular, epitrochlear, axillary, and inguinal lymphadenopathy and lymph nodes are mildly tender and mobile.\n- Sore throat (usually severe) - There may be tonsillar enlargement, the tonsils may meet in the mid-line, and there is usually a 'whitewash' exudate on the tonsils and Sore throat may fail to improve, or become worse, after several days.\n- Other clinical features:\n- Prodromal symptoms — this usually lasts for several days and includes general malaise, fatigue, myalgia, chills, sweats, anorexia, and retro- orbital headache.\n- Non-specific rash — after being treated with amoxicillin.\n- Splenomegaly\n- Hepatomegaly\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 45-year-old woman with recently diagnosed breast cancer. She is about to undergo her first chemotherapy session as part of her breast cancer treatment plan. She has expressed concerns about potential side effects, especially nausea and vomiting. According to nice guidelines which one of the following is antiemetic of choice?\n- **A)** Metoclopramide\n- **B)** 5HT3 Antagonist   C\n- Betahistine\n- **D)** Hyoscine\n- **E)** Olanzapine\n- Explanation\n- NICE and MASSC (multinational association of supportive care in cancer) antiemetic guideline recommend regimens including both a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist and a neurokinin-I receptor antagonist (palonosetron hydrochloride, netupitant) for the prevention of nausea and vomiting in people receiving with highly emetogenic cisplatin-based cancer chemotherapy or moderately emetogenic cancer chemotherapy. It is given as a single dose approximately I hour before the start of each chemotherapy cycle.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 31 -year-old woman comes to Accidents and Emergency for a worsening cough and rigors. During the consultation, her husband translates for her as she speaks no English at all. On auscultating her chest, you've noticed multiple old and new bruises on her chest. Her husband states that they were from a fall a week ago.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate immediate action?\n- **A)** Arrange an outpatient clinic appointment to see woman on her own\n- **B)** Contact the police\n- **C)** Discharge and contact adult safeguarding team\n- **D)** Admit and arrange an independent translator\n- **E)** Ask her relatives if she is experiencing domestic abuse\n- Explanation\n- Domestic violence needs to be taken seriously. As doctors, we need to be vigilant and look out for cases like this. Admission overnight allows the woman to be alone away from her partner and using an independent translator such as the NHS "language line" can be used. The NHS "language line" is a 24-hour interpreting service that allows medical staff to communicate in almost all languages to their patients. You would need to call the hospital operator to put you through and the translator on the phone would help translate your conversations. One should find out the whole story before contacting the police or the safeguarding team.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 28-year-old young child is brought in with a rash and a high temperature. You are concerned about a possible diagnosis of bacterial meningitis and plan for\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Lumbar puncture. What is the contraindication of Lumbar puncture?\n- **A)** CCS (E4V5M5)\n- **B)** Confusion\n- **C)** Treatment with clopidogrel\n- **D)** Respiratory compromise\n- **E)** Skin petechiae Explanation Nice guideline recommendations In children and young people with suspected meningitis or suspected meningococcal disease, perform a lumbar puncture unless any of the following contraindications are present: signs suggesting raised intracranial pressure reduced or fluctuating level of consciousness (Glasgow Coma Scale score less than 9 or a drop of 3 or more) relative bradycardia and hypertension focal neurological signs abnormal posture or posturing unequal, dilated or poorly responsive pupils papilloedema abnormal 'doll's eye' rnovements\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- shock extensive or spreading purpura after convulsions until stabilised coagulation abnormalities coagulation results (if obtained) outside the normal range platelet count below IOOx109/litre receiving anticoagulant therapy local superficial infection at the lumbar puncture site respiratory insufficiency (lumbar puncture is considered to have a high risk of precipitating respiratory failure in the presence of respiratory insufficiency).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 34-year-old female has been experiencing a range of unusual symptoms for the past several months. She reports progressive muscle weakness, particularly in her lower limbs. She finds it increasingly difficult to engage in activities that require sustained muscle use and pain in the muscle worse after exercise. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Central cord syndrome\n- **B)** Cauda equina syndrome C\n- Myasthenia gravis\n- **D)** Multiple sclerosis\n- **E)** Guillain—Barré syndrome\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features: muscle strength fatigability, weakness worsens with activity or repetition suggests Myasthenia gravis (MO).\n\nGuillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) It is a clinically defined syndrome characterized by motor difficulty, absence Of deep tendon reflexes, paresthesias without objective sensory loss, and increased cerebrospinal fluid albumin with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation).\n- Risk factors of patients with GBS have a history of gastroenteritis or influenza-like illness. hepatitis E infection Bacterial illness - to Ca mp ter jejuni\n- hepatitis E infection\n- Clinical features Muscle weakness -progressive symmetric muscle weakness usually affecting lower extremities before upper extremities and proximal muscles before distal muscles (ascending paralysis) accompanied by paresthesias in the feet and hands is typical. The paralysis is typically flaccid with areflexia and progresses acutely over days Paresthesia\n- Back/leg pain Respiratory distress\n- Areflexia/hyporeflexia dysarthria. Investigations Nerve conduction studies -\n- Facial weakness, dysphagia, diplopia,\n- areflexia/hyporeflexia prolonged distal and F-wave latencies and reduced conduction velocities; H reflex prolonged or absent\n- lumbar puncture - elevated CSF protein, normal/slightly high lymphocytes (<50 cells/mma).\n- LET-AST and AIT as high as 500 U/L Spirometry - may show reduced Vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure.\n- Consider antiganglioside antibody, Transverse myelitis\n- Transverse myelitis (TM) is a pathogenetically heterogeneous focal inflammatory disorder Of the spinal cord characterised by acute or subacute development Of motor weakness, sensory impairment, and autonomic dysfunction.\n- It is typically associated with multiple sclerosis. Risk factors\n- preceding infectious illness recent vaccination female sex history Of recent physical trauma Clinical features\n- motor weakness paraesthesias or sensory loss bladder symptoms: urinary frequency, urgency, incontinence, or retention bowel symptoms: incontinence or constipation Lhermitte sign\n- McArdle's sign paroxysmal tonic spasms upper motor neuron signs: hyper- reflexia, positive Babinski's sign, limb spasticity sensory loss/sensory level Investigations\n- MRI spinal cord MRI brain Serum aquaporin—4 auto-antibodies and myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein auto-antibodies Cerebrospinal fluid cell count, cell differential, protein level, IgG index, oligoclonal band Myasthenia gravis (MG) Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a chronic autoimmune disorder of the post- synaptic membrane at the neuromuscular junction in skeletal muscle. Circulating antibodies against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (AChR) or associated proteins impair neuromuscu lar transmission.\n- Risk factors family history Of autoimmune disorders genetic markers - Specific HLA genes cancer-targeted therapy\n- Clinical features\n- muscle strength fatigability The weakness worsens With activity or repetition (fatigable weakness) and improves on rest. ptosis diplopia, dysphagia , dysarthria facial paresis proximal limb weakness\n- shortness of breath\n- Investigations serum acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody analysis muscle-specific tyrosine kinase (MuSK) antibodies serial pulmonary function tests single-fibre EMG\n- CT Of chest - Should be performed in all newly diagnosed patients with adult onset MG to detect thymoma Extractable nuclear antigen (including SSA and SSB) serum and cerebrospinal fluid paraneoplastic auto-antibodies serolozv. stool culture— '—cerebrosoinal fluid cvtoloev and flovv\n\n- Management\n- intravenous immune globulin OVIG) or plasma exchange Np!ezIVIG is contraindicate in patient with IgA deficiency and renal failure. Supportive treatment - Vital monitoring and appropriate prophylactic anticoagulation for DVT.\n- Complications Respiratory failure\n- Bladder areflexia Adynamic ileus\n- Paralysis cytometry\n- serology for herpes simplex Virus (HSV)-I, HSV-2, varicella zoster virus, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, and West Nile virus Management\n- Corticosteroid - Treatment With 3 to 5 consecutive daily infusions of corticosteroids is associated With more rapid recovery of neurological deficits. methylprednisolone: 1000 mg intravenously OD Supportive measures — active rehabilitation, DVT prophylaxis, bladder catheterization. exchange (plasmapheresis)\n- munosuppressive treatment (azathioprine. mvcophenolate — recommended in patient with positive aquaporin-4 auto-antibody seropositive or myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein-IgG auto-antibody seropositive\n- Management\n- Myasthenia crisis\n- intubation and mechanical ventilation plasma exchange or intravenous immunoglobulin Corticosteroid - prednisolone: 1 to 1.5 mg/kg orally once daily\n- Mild to moderate disease (class to pyridostigmine Consider — corticosteroid Consider— immunosuppressant (Azathioprine) Consider — rituximab Consider— thymectomy\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nYou are required to perform RSI in a 30-year-old traumatic patient for which neuromuscular blocking agent suxamethonium has been used. Suxamethonium is avoided in which one of the following conditions?\n- **A)** Mixed overdose\n- **B)** Hypothermia\n- **C)** Hypotension\n- **D)** Seizure\n- **E)** Bradycardia\n- Explanation Suxamethonium should be avoided in bradycardia as it will worsen it\n- further. Suxamethonium adverse effects\n- Common or very common:\n-\n- Arrhythmias; bradycardia (may occur with repeated doses); flushing; muscle contractions involuntary; myoglobinuria; myopathy; post- procedural muscle pain; rash Rare or very rare\n- Apnoea; cardiac arrest; hypersensitivity; malignant hyperthermia;\n- respiratory disorders; trismus Contra-indications For suxamethonium chloride\n- Hyperkalaemia; Iow plasma-cholinesterase activity (including severe liver disease) major trauma\n- neurological disease involving acute wasting of major muscle\n- personal or family history of congenital myotonic disease\n- personal or family history of malignant hyperthermia\n- prolonged immobilisation (risk of hyperkalaemia)\n-\n\n- severe burns; skeletal muscle myopathies (e.g. Duchenne muscular dystrophy)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 56-year-old man diagnosed as case of epilepsy develops a severe skin rash two weeks after being prescribed some medications by his GP. He initially felt generally unwell with a mild fever and flu-like symptoms. He subsequently developed a rash, which started as numerous 'target lesions' and has now progressed to severe bullous, ulcerating skin lesions with areas of epidermal detachment. Which of the following drugs cause toxic epidermal necrolysis?   A Lorazepam\n- **B)** Beta blockers\n- **C)** Fluoxetine\n- **C)** -  Carbamazepine\n- **E)** Erythromycin\n- Explanation\n- Risk factors of Stevens-Johnson necrolysis\n\n- Anticonvulsant medicines - The most common anticonvulsants precipitating SJS and TEN are carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, lamotrigine, and valproic acid.\n- Recent antibiotic use - The greatest risk is posed by the use of trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole and other sulfonamide antibiotics, aminopenicillins, quinolones, and cephalosporins.\n- Other medicines - that may put the patient at risk are: antifungals, antivirals (e.g., telaprevir, aciclovir), antiretrovirals (e.g., nevirapine, abacavir), anthelmintics, analgesics (e.g., paracetamol), non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, selective COX-2 inhibitors, antimalarials, corticosteroids, azathioprine.\n- Others — SLE, HIV-positive, Smallpox vaccination, Radiotherapy\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 17-year female student, with known epilepsy, is brought into ED by ambulance with ongoing generalized tonic-clonic seizures for 30 minutes. She has received two doses of benzodiazepines, the First dose at home buccal midazolam, and the second dose of midazolam by paramedics on the way to the hospital. The most recent dose was IO minutes ago but her seizure is ongoing. What is the next most appropriate management step?\n- **A)** Phenytoin\n- **B)** Phenobarbital bolus   C\n- IV Levetiracetam\n- **D)** RSI with thiopental sodium\n- **E)** Third dose of IV lorazepam\n- Explanation\n- Nice guideline second line recommendation:\n- If convulsive status epilepticus does not respond to 2 doses of a benzodiazepine, give any of the following medicines intravenously as a second-line treatment: I eve t i m\n- phenytoin\n- sodium valproate.\n- Take into account that levetiracetam may be quicker to administer and have fewer adverse effects than the alternative options.\n\n0-5 minutes: Immediate management 5-10 minutes: Early SE 20-40 minutes: established SE 40-60 minutes: Refractory SE Protect the patient. Do not restrain. Consider airway adjunct. Administer oxygen\n- place patient in a semi-prone position, head down\n- Gain I V access\n- Obtain BM - If hypoglycaemic: Give 150-200ml glucose stat\n- If suspicion of alcohol excess or malnutrition commence 1 pair pabrinexlV BEFORE glucose replacement.\n- If no IV access or (pre\n- hosøital, paramedics hospital transfer)\n- Buccal midazolam 10mg or Rectal diazepam 10-20 mg if buccal midazolam is not available.\n- If IV access gained\n- Lorazepam (intravenous) 0.1 mg/kg (usually a 4 mg bolus, repeated once after 10-20 minutes; rate not critical). if seizures continue, treat as below\n- Alert the a and ICIJ\n- Give 2nd line anticonvulsants\n- IV Levetiracetam 60mg/kg, maximum (in 100m I sodium chloride 0.9% over IO minutes) OR Phenytoin infusion at a dose of 15—18 mg/kg at a rate of 50 mg/minute or fosphenytoin infusion at a dose of 15—20 mg phenytoin equivalents (PE)/kg at a rate of 50—100 mg PE/minute OR\n- phenobarbital bolus Of 10—15 mg/kg at a rate Of 100 mg/minute (If any patient already on phenytoin, consider phenobarbital in such patients)\n- General anaesthesia with one of: Propofol (1—2 mg/kg bolus, then 2—10 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect Midazolam (0.1—0.2 mg/kg bolus, then 0.05—0.5 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect.\n- Thiopental sodium (3—5 mg/kg bolus, then 3—5 mg/kg/hour) titrated to effect; after 2—3 days infusion rate needs reduction as fat stores are saturated.\n- Anaesthetic continued for 12—24 hours after the last clinical or electrographic seizure, then dose tapered.  . On\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old male presented to the ED with complaints of fever and fits examination of the eye, he had 3rd nerve palsy. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Status epilepticus\n- **B)** Cerebral malaria\n- **C)** Meningitis\n- **D)** Transient ischemic attack   E\n- Encephalitis\n- Explanation\n- Fever with Focal neurological deficit and seizures commonly seen in encephalitis.\n- Encephalitis clinical features . Fever, rash\n- Altered mental status\n- focal neurological deficit - These include aphasia, hemianopia, hemiparesis, ataxia, brisk tendon reflexes, Babinski's sign, cranial nerve deficits\n- Meningismus - Some patients have evidence of meningeal inflammation with headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness.\n- Seizures - Generalised tonic-clonic seizures and focal seizures  IO. An 81-year-old male patient with a devastating brain injury was brought into the ED. He is a known case of myeloma with metastasis.\n\nAccording to UK laws which one of the following is the contraindication of organ donation in this patient?\n- **A)** Myeloma in remission from 8 years\n- **B)** His age\n- **C)** Solid organ CA T2, Nl, MO\n- **D)** GCS<5\n- **E)** Leukemia in remission\n- Explanation\n- Absolute Contraindications to consideration of deceased donation\n- Age >85 years (on or after their 85th birthday)\n- Primary intra-cerebral lymphoma\n- All secondary intracerebral tumors\n- Any active cancer with evidence of spread outside the affected organ within 3 years of donation\n- Melanoma (except completely excised Stage I cancers)\n- Active (not in remission) haematological malignancy (myeloma, lymphoma, leukaemia)\n- Definite, probable or possible case of human transmissible spongiform encephalopathy (TSE), including CJD and vCJD, individuals whose blood relatives have had familial CJD, other neurodegenerative diseases associated with infectious agents.\n\n- T\n- **B)** active and untreated\n- West Nile Virus (\\NNV) infection\n- HIV disease (but not HIV infection)\n- A history of infection with Ebola virus\n- Note: HIV infection means people who have an infection with HIV but none of the associated complications and they can donate organs.\n- Agtiye_Lmeans not in remission; spread outside affected organ includes spread to lymph nodes.\n- Organ donation law in England\n- it will be considered that you agree to become an organ donor when you die, if\n- .\n- you are over 18 year\n- you have not opted out;\n- you are not in an excluded group\n- Excluded-group;-anybody in this group will be excluded from organ donation\n- Those under the age of 18\n- People who lack the mental capacity to understand the new arrangements and take the necessary action\n- Visitors to England, and those not living here voluntarily\n- People who have lived in England for less than 12 months before their death\n\n- Cancer-TNMstagingsystem\n- It is the widest cancer staging system for doctors. It is used to stage malignant cancers that can spread to various parts of the body. The TNM staging system is the standard system of classification used by doctors, health centers, and hospitals that enables sharing of information and provides a better understanding of cancer to other doctors or to the patient itself.\n- T- Tumor:The size of the primary tumor and its spread to surrounding tissues is analyzed. T values are assessed differently, depending on the structures involved. For example, Tl is used when there is a submucosal invasion, and T4 is used when there is an invasion of all layers of the colon.\n- N- Nodal Involvement: This is used to determine the spread of cancer to the lymph nodes. Lymph nodes act as a filter when the tissue fluids enter lymphatic capillaries. N values given to a tumor are different depending on the regional lymphatic drainage and are tumor-specific.\n- M- Metastasis:This is used to determine the spread of the tumor beyond regional lymph nodes.\n\nT — Tumor\n- Tx-unable to assess primary tumor TO — There is no evidence of primary lesion Tis — Carcinoma in situ\n- Tl — Tumor penetrates submucosa and mucosa\n- T2— The tumor invades but fail to penetrates the mucosal layer T3 — The tumor penetrates the subserosa\n- T4— Tumors penetrates deep into the peritoneum or other organs N — Lymph Node NX — Unable to assess lymph node involvement\n- NO — No lymph node involvement\n- NI — There is metastasis in relevant organ/tissue nodes around 1 to 3 N2 — There is metastasis of four or more nodes.   M - Metastasis MX — Unable to assess distant metastasis MO — There is distant metastasis\n- MI — There is distant metastasis\n	\N	[]	\N	f	44	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
4106fa79-d29b-474e-a668-8d5bf9418efc	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-44	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 44	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 44\n\nD.VT\n- **E)** Complete heart block\n- Explanation\n- All too often, syncope or sudden cardiac death is the only symptom in patients with Brugada syndrome. In some cases, sudden death is the first symptom of the disease. Monitoring of such patients has revealed rapid polymorphic ventricular tachycardias (VTs) as the underlying cause. VT usually starts with a short coupling interval. Self-terminating episodes typically lead to repeated episodes Of syncope. Indeed, 80% Of patients with documented VF have a history of syncope.\n- Clinical reports indicate that sudden death in patients with Brugada syndrome most commonly occurs during sleep, in particular during the early morning hours\n- Diagnostic criteria for Brugada syndrome.\n- Typical ECG abnormalities and one of the following clinical criteria:\n- Documented ventricular fibrillation (VF) or polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (VT)\n- Family history of sudden cardiac death at <45 years old\n- Coved-type ECGs in family members\n- Inducible of VT with programmed electrical stimulation\n- Syncope\n- Nocturnal agonal respiration.\n- Note: Brugada pattern — is considered typical ECG abnormalities without any clinical criteria.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA mother brought her one-year-old child with choking, agonal Breaths, and cyanosed, and became unresponsive while arriving in the ED. You can not palpate the central pulse. What is the next step of management?\n- **A)** Commence CPR at rate of 15:2\n- **B)** five back blows and five chest thrusts\n- **C)** Five Back blows and five abdominal thrusts\n- **D)** Five rescue breaths and then start CPR x\n- **E)** Commence CPR and then five rescue breaths\n- Explanation\n- Once the patient is unconscious and there is no central pulse that indicates the patient is in cardiac arrest, five initial rescue breaths should be given and CPR 15:2 should be commenced as per Paediatric BLS (Hospital settings) and 30:2 (Out of Hospital settings).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 55-year-old male known case of Hypertension, taking enalapril for the last five years presented to the ED with a complaint of sudden facial and lip swelling. You suspect Ace-induced angioedema. What would be your management strategy?\n- **A)** FFP\n- **B)** Adrenaline\n- **C)** Diphenhydramine\n- **D)** Hydrocortisone\n- **E)** Prednisolone\n- Explanation\n- Angioedema due to ACEI management is controversial.\n- According to NICE and British Society for Allergy and Clinical Immunology (BSACI), first drug should be stopped.\n- For people with symptoms requiring treatment:\n- Offer a non-sedating antihistamine (such as cetirizine, fexofenadine, or loratadine) for up to 6 weeks\n- If symptoms are severe, give a short course of an oral corticosteroid(for example prednisolone 40 mg daily for up to 7 days) in addition to the non-sedating oral antihistamine.\n- Important points of the British Society for Allergy and Clinical Immunology_(BSACl) and Its recommendation\n- The incidence of ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema may be as high as 0.68% [42] as most cases were initially thought to occur in the first weeks of treatment, but it is now appreciated that later onset angioedema, occurring after many years of uneventful drug use, is quite common.\n\n- The mechanism underlying the angioedema is likely to be due to the reduced metabolism of bradykinin; this effect may also aggravate angioedema associated with HAE.\n- Angioedema associated with angiotensin receptor blockers (ARB's) has been occasionally reported and hence their use in individuals with ACE inhibitor related angioedema has been questioned but is not contra- indicated .\n- The patient usually presents with swelling of the tongue, but the lips, pharynx, larynx and viscera may also be involved.\n- The episodes of angioedema may persist for several months after withdrawal of the ACE inhibitor without undermining the validity of the drug-related diagnosis.\n- Individuals of Afro-Caribbean origin are at increased risk of ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema. As this group of drugs are less effective in such individuals, an alternative choice of antihypertensive is prudent.\n- Antihistamines, corticosteroids and adrenaline have traditionally been used to treat these individuals although their efficacy rernains unproven. However, bradykinin antagonists, such as icatibant, may be effective.\n- Other guidelines\n- Approach to angioedema:\n\nDoes the patient defin have angioedema? (e.g. visible swelling of lips/tongue) Diagnosis secure Is immediate intubation necessary? (e.g. stridor, dyspnea, pooling secretions) Yes Nasolaryngoscopy Or bronchosco py Severe Obstruction Seen Convert to fiberoptic intubation Intubation (use EXTREME CAUTION, double setup & PRN CRIC) Does the patient clearly have either histamine- or bradykinin-mediated angioedema? C— is Anti-Histamine - 1M (O.Smg) or IV epinephrine (—5-15 mcg/min infusion)\n- IV methylprednisolone 125 mg\n- Diphenhydramine SO mg IV - Famotidine 20 mg IV or ranitidine 150m Reconsider possibility Of bradykinin-mediated angioedema treatment: gram tranexamic add IV Not d Consider fresh frozen plasma (2 Units) May repeat xl if inadequate response persistent Consider CI-inhibitor concentrate (especially if known Ci-inhibitor\n- Medical therapy for bradykinin-induced angioedema\n\nModerate to severe bradykinin-induced angioedema Immediate treatment: I gram tranexamic acid IV Not ACEi-induced ACEi-induced Consider fresh frozen plasma (2 Units). May repeat x1 if inadequate response If persistent Consider CI-inhibitor concentrate (especially if known CI-inhibitor deficiency) Please note: Emergency treatment bradykinin-induced angtoedema remains controversial (especially ACE- inhibitor induced). High-level data does not exist.\n- Note: Candidates got marks who have chosen FFP, However, pick the answer according to the question in the exam.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 2-year-old child was brought into the ED after he swallowed a single strong magnet. He has no GI symptoms. What is the next step of management for this child? Consult with surgery\n- **B)** Obtain CXR and\n- **C)** Single magnet likely to pass without intervention\n- **D)** Discharge with follow up X-ray after 6-12 hours\n- **E)** Discharge with safety briefing Explanation\n- According to RCEM guidelines - All patients with suspected or witnessed rare earth magnet ingestion required urgent CXR and AXR. furthermore, if a single magnet is seen, it requires another lateral AXR.\n- The following patients should be considered suitable for discharge after-rare-earth-magnet-jngestion;\n- single magnet ingestion\n- accidental ingestion\n- no co-rnorbidities\n- tolerating oral intake\n\n- presents within 24hr of ingestion\n- care-giver able to provide close observation (there is no need to examine the child's faeces)\n- All patients who are being discharged with rare earth magnet ingestion require follow-up imaging after 6-12 hours\n- If a single magnet is ingested, it can be expected to be passed spontaneously, if the magnet is not too large.\n- Patients who do not meet discharge criteria eg. symptomatic patients, signs of deterioration, ingestion of two or more rare earth magnets should be discussed with a specialist regional paediatric surgical centre in the first instance.\n- Admission under the care of a local surgical team may be appropriate\n\nAppendix 2- Management Algorithm If a rare earth magnet ingested with a button battery this is a TIME CRmCAL EMERGENCY (reter to local button battery URGENT Discussion with Specialist Paediatric Surgical Centre to plan further management transfer Discussion with Specialist Paediatric Surgical Centre to make plan for further imaging, observation removal Witnessed or suspected rare earth magnet ingestion OR Unexplained GI symptoms With rare earth magnets in the child's environment Request Urgent Erect CXR and AXR With Request a lateral AXR only if a Single magnet Seen\n- Symptoms Or Signs Of Intestinal Obstruction\n- Multiple Magnets\n- Evidence Of Intestinal Discuss with local surgical team paediatric) for admission and observation and repeat X-ray Symptoms Or Signs at any time Failure of magnet to progress but remains asymptomatic\n- Single magnet Discharge Criteria Met ? .sirøe within Patient Advice leaflet Return for repeat X-ray Follow-up AXRS at least 6-12hrs apart until it Can den-lonstrated that the magnet has passed through the stomach and it is progressing through the small bowel or beyond. progression of single magnet (confirmed by radiologist) remains asymptomatic Discharge with safety net advice and patient advice leaflet\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 45-year-old woman known as diabetic sustained a minor trauma with a IOcm lacerated wound on the shin. What is the next step of management?\n- **A)** Compression bandage\n- **B)** Leave the wound open C\n- Apply adhesive Strips\n- **D)** Refer to surgery\n- **E)** Suture the wound\n- Explanation\n- Laceration Repair Agents Adhesive tapes or\n- Low tension\n- Linear Superficial\n- Areas where the skin is thin and may not hold sutures (e.g., skin tears in the elderly)\n- Contraindications\n\nHigh tension wounds (e.g., over joints, gaping wounds)\n- Wounds that require layered closure\n- Wounds in high moisture areas (e.g., axilla/groin)\n- Technique\n- The wound should be cleaned and prepped as described above.\n- Thoroughly dry the wound.\n- Apply tincture of benzoin (or medical adhesive) to the skin where the tape is to be applied to increase adhesion of the tape to the wound.\n- Cut the adhesive tape to the desired length. Leave approximately 2-3 cm on each side of the wound.\n- Remove one strip from the backing and apply to one side of the wound.\n- Gently oppose the two margins of the wound and place the second  of the strip over the wound while pressing down on the tape.\n- You may apply extra tape approximately 2-3cm parallel to the wound over the initial tape to secure it.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 55-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department with worsening difficulty in breathing. He has been diagnosed with Guillian barre syndrome. How do you monitor respiratory function in Guillian barre syndrome?\n- **A)** CXR\n- **B)** ABCS\n- **C)** Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR)\n- **D)** FVC\n- **E)** Lactate\n- Explanation\n- Spirometry — would show reduced vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure\n- Guillain:ßarre-syndrome-/nyestigations\n- Nerve conduction studies - prolonged distal and F-wave latencies and reduced conduction velocities; H reflex prolonged or absent\n- lumbar puncture - elevated CSF protein, normal/slightly high lymphocytes (<50 cells/mm3).\n- LFT -AST and ALT as high as 500 u/L\n- Spirometry - may show reduced vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure.\n- Consider antiganglioside antibody, serology, and stool culture.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 12-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with a painful and swollen eye. The child has a fever and complains of difficulty moving the affected eye. On examination, there is significant periorbital swelling, redness, and pain on ocular movement. Visual acuity is slightly reduced in the affected eye. Her vital signs include BP  mmHg Pulse 116bpm sa02 98% What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Anterior uveitis B\n- Orbital cellulitis\n- **C)** Bacterial conjunctivitis\n- **D)** Acute angle closure glaucoma\n- **E)** Peri-orbital cellulitis\n- Explanation\n- The patient's clinical symptoms suggest Orbital cellulitis Orbital the orbit. (É the to the orbital to bacterial Rea eye Visual acuity. afferent detect proptosis headache features Of fasted need Surgery Seek ENT urgentb' CT scan ot send Stat 'V Permanent brain abscess Meningitis siruS ttvombOSiS bSCeSS   Peri-orbital celluliti± the EdS not nvOlWng the the SYuCtureS antero' to the orDCal secoMary to bacterial sinusis Red eye lid + Management 'o care\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 30-year-old woman presents to the ED with numbness and tingling sensation in her legs. Sensory tests also show a reduced ability to sense light touch and to feel a pinprick in her legs. In addition, she has now experienced double vision and loss of Color vision. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Transverse myelitis   B\n- Multiple sclerosis\n- **C)** Papilledema\n- **D)** Gullian barre syndrome\n- **E)** Optic neuritis\n- Explanation\n- MS clinical features\n- Multiple sclerosis (MS) typically presents between 20—50 years Of age.\n- About 0.5% of adults with MS first develop symptoms aged 60 years or older — older age at onset is associated with a progressive course.\n- The person may have:\n- A history of previous neurological symptoms.\n- Symptoms that evolve over more than 24 hours, may persist over several days or weeks and then improve.\n- MS can affect nearly any part of the central nervous system.\n\n- Presenting symptoms and signs can vary greatly and may include:\n- Loss or reduction of vision in one eye with painful eye movements.\n- Diplopia.\n- Ascending sensory disturbance and/or weakness.\n- Balance or gait problems, unsteadiness, or clumsiness.\n- Altered sensation radiating down the back and sometimes into the limbs on neck flexion (Lhermitte's symptom). Optic neuritis(most common feature)— inflamrnation of the optic nerve. Optic neuritis is the initial presentation in about 20—30% of people with MS.\n- The person may describe partial or total unilateral visual loss developing over a few days, pain behind the eye (in particular on eye movement) and/or loss of colour discrimination (particularly reds).\n- Fundoscopy is often normal but the disc may appear pale or swollen. There may be paradoxical dilation of the pupil when light is rapidly shifted from the unaffected eye to the affected eye (relative afferent pupillary defect).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 57-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a 2-day history of vaginal bleeding. She has had two previous vaginal deliveries and tells you she experienced menopause five years ago. Her observations are all within normal limits and her hemoglobin is normal. Which of the following management plans is most appropriate for this patient? A Refer to gynaecology\n- **B)** Perform CT scan\n- **C)** Perform coagulation screen\n- **D)** Reassure and discharge\n- **E)** Perform pelvic ultrasound Explanation\n- Nice guideline recomrnendation for endometrial cancer\n- Refer women using a suspected cancer pathway referral (for an appointment within 2 weeks) for endornetrial cancer if they are aged 55 years and over with post-menopausal bleeding (unexplained vaginal bleeding more than 12 months after menstruation has stopped because of the menopause).\n- Consider a suspected cancer pathway referral (for an appointment within 2 weeks) for endometrial cancer in women aged under 55 years with post-menopausal bleeding.\n- Consider a direct access ultrasound scan to assess for endometrial ganger-in-women-aged-55years-and-oyer-with;\n- Unexplained symptoms of vaginal discharge who:\n- Are presenting with these symptoms for the first time, or\n- Have thrombocytosis, or Report haematuria, or\n- Visible haematuria, and:\n- Low haemoglobin levels, or\n- Thrombocytosis, or\n- High blood glucose levels. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nAn intoxicated 35-year-old alcoholic father was brought to the ED by paramedics with agitation and restlessness. He needs admission, His 5-year-old son is alone at home. After some time, his son was also brought to the ED, he smelled alcohol too.\n\nWhat is the next step to be taken?\n- **A)** Admit the son as well, for safeguarding review\n- **B)** Check records Of any child abuse\n- **C)** Discharge son with hospital contacted Taxi\n- **D)** Wait for the hospital social service to come\n- **E)** Inform police\n- Explanation\n- The risk of ingesting drugs or alcohol is high in children whose parents misuse substances\n- Child has accessible to alcohol as it wasn't stored securely at all times, out of the reach of children. Hence, Child should be admitted for safeguarding review.\n- Guideline recommends that, a child-focused assessment must be carried out with regards to the needs, safety and welfare of every child who is looked after by adults who misuse drugs or alcohol.\n- The assessment must consider whether safeguarding action is necessary. 'Professionals must be alert to and take note of new or increased risks and be prepared to take safeguarding action at any point, if and when it becomes necessary.\n- Parents who misuse substances\n- Assessment and professional awareness and skills\n- A child-focused assessment must be carried out with regards to the needs, safety and welfare Of every child who is looked after by adults\n\nwho misuse drugs or alcohol.\n- The assessment must look at parenting capacity and must analyse the adults' substance misuse and behaviour to understand its impact on the children in their care.\n- Professionals should treat with caution the parent's / carer's account of how much and how often they drink / take drugs.\n- The assessment must consider whether safeguarding action is necessary. 'Professionals must be alert to and take note of new or increased risks and be prepared to take safeguarding action at any point, if and when it becomes necessary.\n- Professionals must remain alert to risks even if the parents seem to be complying at the moment.\n- Professionals must be aware of, and understand, the risks to children posed by parents' substance misuse. They must be confident in challenging carers about the risks they are exposing their children to.\n- Professionals must be aware of, and understand, the risks to children posed by co-sleeping. They must be comfortable with discussing these risks with carers and crucially know how to give advice around safe sleeping arrangements.\n- Recommendations-around-working-with-the-famj-ly.\n- Drugs and alcohol in the home must be stored securely at all times, out of the reach of children.\n- The risks of children ingesting drugs or alcohol must be explained to parents/carers, in a way they understand and are able to take this information on board.\n- Children must be unable to get close to potential hazards in the home (eg ponds or heating appliances).\n- Reassessing-risk-as-farniJy-gircumstan.ges-change-New.and-ingreased\n- new partner (or previous partner coming back)\n- involvement of extended family members in family life\n\n- non-family visitors to the home\n- missed appointments with any agency\n- dropping out of treatment programme\n- domestic violence or mental ill health problems\n- criminal activity or anti-social behaviour incidents\n- stress (which may impact upon substance misuse habits)\n- moving home. previous\n	\N	[]	\N	f	45	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
7ae1ed06-27ba-4eaa-877e-800e96cb9d40	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-45	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 45	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 45\n\nA\n- Politely refuse and do not give him information\n- **B)** Ask nurse to speak to him\n- **C)** Cive him information\n- **D)** Only give necessary details\n- **E)** Consult with your senior\n- Explanation\n- Patient personal information can not be given without the patient's consent.\n- Confidentiality is an important ethical and legal duty but it is not absolute.\n- You may disclose personal information without breaching duties of confidentiality-when-any-of-the-fo"owing-circumstances-applies.\n- The patient consents, whether implicitly or explicitly, for the sake of their own care or for local clinical audit.\n- The patient has given their explicit consent to disclosure for other purposes\n- The disclosure is of overall benefit to a patient who lacks the capacity to consent\n\n- The disclosure is required by law, or the disclosure is permitted or has been approved under a statutory process that sets aside the common law duty of confidentiality.\n- The disclosure can be justified in the public interest.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 25-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by paramedics. She had been found intoxicated at home. Her ABGs shown. What is the management?\n- **A)** Atropine\n- **B)** Methylene blue\n- **C)** Hydroxocobalamin\n- **D)** Flumazenil E\n- Fomepizole\n- Explanation\n- ABCs revealed high anion-gap metabolic acidosis (Na+ + K+) — (CI- + HC03-)= Anion Gap, which is likely due to ethylene glycol/methanol ingestion.\n\nCyanide wouldn't be considered as there is no history of a house fire or other features.\n- Antidote - Fomepizole (or Ethanol) 5 mg/mL, 20 mL ampoule OR 1 g/mL, 1.5 mL vial or 100% ethanol, 5mL ampoule.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 45-year-old lady was brought to the Emergency Department with a complaint of abdominal pain for the last two days. An abdominal x-ray is shown. Regarding your suspected diagnosis, What is the most common cause?\n- **A)** Small bowel neoplasm   B\n- Adhesion\n- **C)** Diverticulitis   D\n- Constipation\n- **E)** Incarcerated hernia\n- Explanation\n- Intra-abdominal adhesion is the rnost common cause of small bowel obstruction.\n\nSmall bowel obstruction\n- It's a medical emergency requiring early diagnosis and intervention\n- Intra-abdominal adhesion is the rnost common cause of SBO.\n-\n- Other- hernias, Crohn's disease, intestinal malignancy, appendicitis Clinical features\n- abdominal pain,\n- bloating, vomiting (possibly biliary),\n- rectal flatus and cessation of defecation.\n- abdominal tenderness\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 30-year-old patient was brought into the ED following RTA with massive blood loss. You start the transfusion protocol, and after ten minutes of blood transfusion, the patient develops limb pain, Iow BP, raised temperature, and tachycardia. Which of the following is the most likely explanation?\n- **A)** Immune mediated haemolytic reaction\n- **B)** Nonimmune mediated Haemolytic reaction\n- **C)** Transfusion related acute lung injury   D\n- Hypokalemia\n- **E)** Transfusion associated circulatory overload\n\n- Explanation\n- The patient has developed an acute haemolytic reaction which is an acute immunologic reaction.\n- Transfusion reactions\n\nAcute haemolytic reaction Febrile transfusion reaction Delayed haemolytic reaction Allergic reaction Transfusion of a blood component contaminated by bacteria TRALI (Transfusion related Acute lung injury) Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO) Clinical features\n- Fever, chills, pain at transfusion site, nausea, vomiting, dark urine. Feelings of 'impeding doom' often reported early on. Most serious type of reaction. Often ABO incompatibility due to administration error. Presents with 1 degree rise in temperature from baseline. patient may also have chills and malaise.\n- Usually caused by cytokines from leukocytes in transfused red cell or platelet component. Most commonly occurs 4-8 days after a blood transfusion. Patient presents with fever, anaemia, jaundice and haemoglobuinuria. Direct antiglobulin(coombs) test positive. Due to low titire antibody too weak to detect in cross-match ans unable to cause lysis at time of transfusion. Usually caused by foreign plasma proteins but may be due to anti\n- lgA. Allergic type reactions usually present with urticaria, pruritis, hives. May be associated with laryngeal oedema or bronchospasm. Anaphylaxis rare.\n- this more often occurs with platelet components (which are stored at 22—240C) than with red cells refrigerated at 2—6'C and can rapidly be fatal. Typical symptoms and signs include rigors, fever (usually >26C above baseline), hypotension and rapidly developing shock and impaired consciousness. Most cases present within 2 hours of transfusion (maximum 6 hours) with severe breathlessness and cough productive of frothy pink sputum. It is often associated With hypotension (due to loss of plasma volume), fever and rigors and transient peripheral blood neutropenia or monocytopenia.\n- Associated with the presence of antibodies in the donor blood to recipient leukocytes antigens. TACO is defined as acute or worsening pulmonary oedema within 6 hours Of transfusion. Typical features include acute respiratory distress, tachycardia, raised blood pressure and evidence of positive fluid balance.\n- It has probably been significantly under-reported in the past and may now be the most common cause of transfusion- related death in developed countries. Management Stop the transfusion\n- Administer IV fluids.\n- Diuretics maybe required\n- Supportive measure, paracetamol helpful.\n- Most delayed haemolytic reaction have a benign course and require no treatment. Monitor anaemia and renal function and treat as required.\n- For allergic reaction treat symptomatically with antihistamines. There is no need to stop transfusion. If anaphylaxis occurs the transfusion should be administered adrenaline and treated as per ALS guideline.\n- Blood cultures\n- Intravenous broad spectrum antibiotic\n- Implicated components must be sealed to avoid leakage or contamination and returned to the transfusion laboratory for further investigation and Withdrawn from hospital blood banks. Stop the transfusion. Oxygen should be administered.\n- Approximately 75% Of proteins require aggressive respiratory support.\n- Diuretics usage should be avoided.\n- Stopping the transfusion and administering oxygen and diuretic therapy with careful monitoring and critical care support if required.\n\nTransfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease (TA-GvHD) Acute immunologic reactions Delayed immunologic reaction Acute non- immunologic reactions Delayed non- immunologic reaction This rare and almost always fatal complication occurs when Viable lymphocytes in a blood donation engraft in the patient and mount an immune response against the recipient's cells of a different HLA type. Symptoms classically occur 7 to 14 days (maximum 30 days) after transfusion with fever, skin rash, diarrhoea, disturbed liver function and worsening bone marrow aplasia.\n- Supportive care\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction Allergic reaction Febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction Transfusion —related acute lung injury Delayed haemolytic reaction Post-transfusion purpura Volume overload Citrate toxicity Hypothermia Bacterial contamination Infectious disease transmission\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 3-year-old child is brought in by their parents with a barking cough. You suspect croup. Which one of the following features has a point in the Westley croup score?   A\n- Decreased air entry\n- **B)** Altered level of consciousness\n- **C)** Moderate chest wall retractions\n- **D)** Stridor at rest\n- **E)** Cyanosis at rest\n- Explanation\n- This question is asking about "a"/one point/score on the Westly scale. However, it's not specified severity as mild or moderate. The best suitable in this case would be decreased air entry. Score 1 2 3 4 5 Stridor None On agitation On rest Intercostal recession None Mild Moderate Severe Air entry Normal Mild decrease None Marked decrease On agitation At rest Consciousness Normal Decreased\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 40-year-old male was brought into the ED after he sustained a road traffic accident. He was unable to bear weight after he stood from falling. He has an open fracture. His radiograph image is shown. What could be the earliest complication in this patient?\n- **A)** Osteomyelitis\n- **B)** Bleeding\n- **C)** Gangrene\n- **D)** Compartment syndrome\n- **E)** Infection\n- Explanation Open fractures pose an immediate risk of infection. In general, the greater the damage to bone and soft tissues, the greater the risk of infection.\n- An open fracture requires different treatment than a closed fracture, in which there is no open wound. Once the skin is broken, bacteria from dirt and other contaminants can enter the wound and cause infection.\n\n- A bone infection can be difficult to treat. The patient may require long- term antibiotics and multiple surgical procedures. In extreme cases where the infection cannot be cured and the patient's life is threatened, amputation may even be necessary. For this reason, preventing infection is the focus of early treatment. If not managed appropriately, infection can lead to more severe complications like osteomyelitis. Compartment syndrome usually develops slightly later due to swelling\n- and increased pressure within the compartments of the affected limb. However, it's important to monitor for compartment syndrome as well and manage it promptly when it arises.\n- Ear-ly-complications-of-fractures Arterial injury\n- Nerve injury\n- Compartment syndrome\n- Infection — common in open fracture\n-\n- Thromboembolism Fat embolism\n- Fracture blisters\n- Late complication\n- Osteomyelitis\n- Nonunion and Malunion\n- Avascular necrosis\n- Complex regional pain syndrome\n- Post-traumatic arthritis\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 50-year-old builder is brought into the ED after falling from some scaffolding and sustaining a head injury. Which of the following is not an indication for performing a CT head within one hour in an adult presenting with a head injury? A\n- Thirty minutes of retrograde amnesia\n- **B)** More than one episode of vomiting\n- **C)** Depressed skull fracture\n- **D)** GCS less than 15 at 2 hours after injury\n- **E)** Seizure\n- Explanation\n\nCT head within 1 hour GCS less than 13 on initial assessment\n- GCS less than 15 at 2 hours after the injury on assessment\n- Suspected open or depressed skull fracture (fluid running from nose,ears, black eyes)\n- Any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum,\n- panda' eyes, CSF leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign).\n- Post-traumatic seizure.\n- Focal neurological deficit (difficulties with understanding, speaking, reading or writing; decreased sensation; loss of balance; general weakness; visual changes; abnormal reflexes).\n- More than 1 episode of vomiting\n-\n-\n- CT head within 8 hours Age 65 years or older. Any history of bleeding or clotting disorders. Dangerous mechanism of injury (a pedestrian or cyclist struck by a motor vehicle, an occupant ejected from a motor vehicle or a fall from a height of greater than 1 metre or 5 stairs). More than 30 minutes' retrograde amnesia Of events immediately before the head injury.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA one-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department for evaluation of poor feeding, irritability, and sweating. The child is lethargic but arousable. He has labored breathing, very rapid pulses, and a dusky color. His respiratory rate is 681min, heart rate is 300/min. He has weak brachial pulses and absent radial pulses, a capillary refill of 6 seconds, Sp02 85% in room air, and good bilateral breath sounds. You administer high-flow oxygen place the child on a cardiac monitor and the child has no history of congenital heart disease. IV access has been established.\n\nHis rhythm is shown. Which therapy is most appropriate for this child?   A Asynchronized shock\n- **B)** Adenosine\n- **C)** Vagal maneuver\n- **D)** Five rescue breath\n- **E)** Amiodarone\n- Explanation\n- A rhythm strip shows SVT\n- Paediatric SVT Council of the I-JK)\n- Synchronised cardioversion with appropriate sedation + analgesia (e.g. 1M/intranasal ketamine if delay in IV access)\n- Chemical cardioversion may be 1st choice if suitable IV access is in place and delay in synchronised cardioversion. As Adenosine\n- Consider amiodarone before 3rd shock\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA IO-year-old child had trauma when another child's head struck the child's nose. The child presents with nasal bleeding and swelling on the nasal bridge. On examination, there is tenderness over the nasal bridge and visible swelling. The image is shown. What is the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Nasal bone fracture\n- **B)** Septal haematoma\n- **C)** Nasal polyps\n- **D)** Septal perforation\n- **E)** Hypertrophic turbinate\n- Explanation\n- Image shows nasal septal haematoma\n- Nasal septal haematoma\n- Nasal septal hematoma is a blood-filled cavity between the cartilage and the supporting perichondrium.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- it is a condition with similarities to the subchondral haematoma seen in ear injuries - and in fact may have similar clinical consequences. it occurs when there has been a shearing injury causing the perichondrium to be stripped from the underlying cartilage. Unless corrected, necrosis results as the cartilage is dependent upon the perichondrium for its blood supply, by diffusion. Management Urgent drainage and firm nasal packing to ensure adherence of the perichondrium to the cartilage. It should be aspirated immediately or can be incised with the aid of local anesthesia antibiotics are recommended. septal abscess - causing accelerated cartilage necrosis, and possibly, intracranial sepsis collapse of the nasal bridge - from septal fibrosis; also known as saddle nose\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 37-year-old female presents to the ED with two weeks history of palpitations, feeling hot and flushed all the time, anxiety, increased appetite, and weight loss. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Cushing disease\n- **B)** Primary hyperparathyroidism\n- **C)** Conns syndrome   D, Hyperthyroidism   E\n- Hypothyroidism Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggests hyperthyroidism\n- Clinical features of hyperthyroidism\n- Heat intolerance\n- Sweating\n- Weight loss\n- Palpitations - Including atrial fibrillation or other supraventricular\n- tachycardias, especially in older people Trernor\n- Diffuse goitre\n- Orbitopathy Exercise intolerance, fatigue, muscle weakness\n- Polyuria, thirst, generalized itch.\n- Reduced libido, gynaecomastia in men\n-\n- Specific features\n\n- Rapid-onset malaise, fever, and thyroid pain (may suggest subacute thyroiditis).\n- Compression symptoms of breathlessness, hoarse voice, dysphagia, neck pressure (may be caused by a toxic multinodular goitre). previous\n	\N	[]	\N	f	46	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b194279d-e8bc-41f4-a257-3525ed2d0c64	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-46	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 46	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 46\n\nIf you violate this directive and disclose the information improperly, which specific legal act could lead to charges against you?\n- **A)** SNOD\n- **B)** Caldicott principle\n- **C)** Health and safety at work act D\n- Data protection act\n- **E)** Human rights equality act\n- Explanation\n- Violation of the directive to ensure the secure handling of patient records and disclosure of the information improperly, healthcare professionals could potentially face charges under the Data Protection Act.\n- Caldicott Principles provide valuable guidance for ensuring the secure handling of patient records. However, they are not a specific legal act. Instead, the Caldicott Principles are a set of guidelines and best practices for the management and use of patient-identifiable information in the UK National Health Service (NHS).\n- Managing and protecting_personal information\n\n- Health and care records can include a wide range of material, including but not limited to: l.handwritten notes 2.eIectronic records 3.correspondence between health professionals 4.visuaI and audio recordings 5.1aboratory reports 6.communications with patients (including texts and emails).\n- Many improper disclosures of patient information are unintentional. Conversations in reception areas, at a patient's bedside and in public places may be overheard. Notes and records may be seen by other patients, unauthorised staff, or the public if they are not managed securely. Patient details can be lost if handover lists are misplaced, or when patient notes are in transit.\n- You rnust make sure any personal information about patients that you hold or control is effectively protected at all times against improper access, disclosure or loss. You should not leave patients' records, or other notes you make about patients, either on paper or on screen, unattended. You should not share passwords.\n- You must not access a patient's personal information unless you have a legitimate reason to view it.\n- You should not share personal information about patients where you can be overheard, for example in a public place or in an internet chat forum. While there are some practice environments in which it\n\nmay be difficult to avoid conversations with (or about) patients being overheard by others, you should try to minimise breaches of confidentiality and privacy as far as it is possible to do so.\n- Processing\n- formatio\n- n ith da protection law\n- The General Data Protection Regulation read with the Data Protection Act 2018 sets out the responsibilities of data controllers when processing personal data, as well as a number of rights for individuals (known as data subjects).\n- If you are a data controller, you rnust understand and meet your obligations under data protection law. This includes responsibilities to make sure patients' personal information that you hold is handled in ways that are transparent and in ways that patients would reasonably expect, and appropriate technical and organisational measures are in place to guard against data loss. You must also make sure information is readily available to patients that explains how their information is processed, including: I.who has access to information you hold that might identify them and for what purposes 2.their options for restricting access to some or all of their records 3.their rights to complain about how their information is processed, and how to make a complaint.\n- When deciding how to provide this information, you should take into account the ICO's guidance (information commissioner's office) on fair processing or privacy notices.\n\n- Whether or not you are a data controller, you must be familiar with, and follow, the confidentiality, data protection and record management policies and procedures where you work and know where to get advice on these issues. This includes policies on the use of laptops and mobile devices\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 42-year-old male with a history of RTA was brought to the ED by paramedics with massive blood loss. The triage nurse is unable to record BP and you are having difficulty getting IV access. You plan for IO access. Which one of the following is a recommended site for IO access?\n- **A)** Small size needle for rib\n- **B)** Three centimeter distal to most prominent aspect of medial malleolus\n- **C)** Adduct arm for humeral insertion\n- **D)** Three centimeter above tibial tuberosity\n- **E)** Three centimeter below lateral condyle in distal femur insertion\n-\n-\n- Explanation Proximal humerus IO insertion Position — arm should be adducted, hand over the umbilicus.\n\nBone Proximal Humerus (first choice) 1 Distal Femur Proximal Tibia Distal Tibia Insertion site\n- The insertion site is on the most prominent aspect of the greater tubercle, 1 to 2 cm above the surgical neck.\n- Palpate 2-3cm above external condyle, Insertion just medial to midline to avoid tendon\n- Palpate tibial tuberosity, Insert 2- 3cm below + medial to tibial tuberosity\n- Insertion site is located approx. 3 cm proximal to the most prominent aspect of the medial malleolus Angle\n- Aim the needle tip downward at a 450 angle to the horizontal plane\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone Age\n- Both adult and paediatric (>6 year)\n- Only in years age Both adult and paediatric\n- Both adult and paediatric\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 23-year-old boy was brought into the ED with anaphylactic shock. The patient has been given two doses of adrenaline, despite the patient's condition not being improved. Which one of the following is the appropriate next step for management?\n- **A)** Adrenaline infusion\n\nB\n- Fluid bolus\n- **C)** IV Glucagon   D\n- IV Hydrocortisone\n- **E)** IV diphenhydramine\n- Explanation\n- The patient is in refractory anaphylaxis. Hence, an adrenaline infusion should be started.\n- Explanation\n- The patient is in refractory anaphylaxis. Hence, an adrenaline infusion should be started.\n- Refractory anaphylaxis\n- IL is defined as anaphylaxis requiring ongoing treatment (due to persisting respiratory or cardiovascular symptoms) despite two appropriate doses of 1M adrenaline.\n- Management\n- Seek expert help\n- Set IV adrenaline infusion and can be given by IV or IO until central venous access is obtained. Where this protocol isn't available or Pt being outside of hospital then Continue to repeat 1M adrenaline after 5 minutes until the infusion has been started\n\n- Mix 1 mg_(l mL ofl 000]) adrenaline in 100 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride_and connect using an infusion pump via a dedicated line\n- Start at 0.5 - 1.0 mL/kg/h rate depending on severity in both children and adult\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 50-year-old woman with a known history of hypertension presented to the emergency department with a sudden altered mental status, confusion, agitation, and visual disturbances. She had been experiencing a severe headache for the past 24 hours, along with nausea and vomiting. Upon arrival at the hospital, her blood pressure was markedly elevated, with a systolic reading of 210 mm Hg and a diastolic reading of 140 mm Hg. What would be the possible next step of management?\n- **A)** IV Labetalol\n- **B)** Nimodipine oral\n- **C)** Phentolamine\n- **D)** GTN\n- **E)** Mannitol\n- Explanation\n\n- Patient clinical features suggest a hypertensive emergency. IV Labetalol first-line therapy in hypertensive encephalopathy/malignant hypertension or intracranial hemorrhage.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 60-year-old man was brought to the ED with a complaint of dizziness and an episode of syncope. His heart rate was 43 bpm and a BP of  mmHg was recorded. He admitted beta blocker for his heart condition. He has been given 3mg of atropine despite his condition has not been improved. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step of management for this patient?\n- **A)** Set up an adrenaline infusion at 2-10 mcg/min\n- **B)** Isoprenaline infusion   C\n- Glucagon\n- **D)** External pacing\n- **E)** Repeat atropine 500mcg\n- Explanation\n- IV atropine remains the first choice if a patient presents with adverse features. If no response to the first line, consider giving glucagon if\n\nbeta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradyca rdia council guideline)\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock, Syncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n- If treatment with atropine is ineffective\n- consider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 - 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECG carefully for the presence of P waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing.\n- If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 28-year-old woman presents to the ED with wheezing and shortness of breath. She is a known case of asthma. The patient has increased work of breathing and is unable to talk in full sentences. Which one of the following is the diagnostic feature of life-threatening asthma?\n- **A)** Sp02 less than 92% on air\n- **B)** PEFR 33-50%\n- **C)** Respiratory rate 30 breath/min O\n- **D)** PaC02 7kPa\n- **E)** Heart rate 120 beat/min\n- Explanation\n- Levels of severi of acute asthma attacks in adults\n\nModerate acute asthma Acute severe asthma Life-threatening asthma Near-fatal asthma Increasing symptoms PEF >50—75% best or predicted No features of acute severe asthma Anv one of: PEF 33—50% best or predicted\n- Respiratory rate 225/min Heart rate 2110/min\n- Inability to complete sentences in one breath Any one of the following in a patient with severe asthma: Clinical signs Altered conscious level PEF Exhaustion Arrhythmia Hypotension Cyanosis Silent chest Poor respiratory effort Measurements PEF <33% best predicted sp02 Pa02 <8kPa 'normal' PaC02 (4.6-6.0 kPa) Raised PaC02 and/or requiring mechanical ventilation with raised inflation pressures\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nAn 80-year-old woman has been found collapsed on the floor and brought into the ED. The patient has mild dementia, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, and heart failure. She is on various medications for her condition. She refused the treatment. Which one of the following drugs you would continue in end-of-life care for this woman?\n\nA Simvastatin\n- **B)** Amiodarone\n- **C)** Digoxin\n- **D)** Ace inhibitor\n- **E)** Beta blocker\n- Explanation\n- Nice guideline recommendation - When it is recognized that a person may be entering the last days of life, review their current medicines and, after discussion and agreement with the dying person and those important to them (as appropriate), stop any previously prescribed medicines that are not providing symptomatic benefit or that may cause harm.\n- In this case, Digoxin is a medication that could be considered for continued use in end-of-life care for this patient. This patient is sedentary and has heart failure and atrial fibrillation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 50-year-old man was brought to the ED with a complaint of dizziness and an episode of syncope. His heart rate was 43 bpm and a BP of  mmHg was recorded. His ECG rhythm shows a first-degree AV block. He has been given 3mg of atropine despite his condition has not been improved.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step of management for this patient?\n- **A)** Admit him for permanent pacing\n- **B)** Seek expert help   C\n- Adrenaline infusion\n- **D)** Glucagon\n- **E)** Dopamine infusion\n- Explanation\n- IV atropine remains the first choice if a patient presents with adverse features. If no response to the first line, consider second-line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\n- Bradycardia (Resus council of the UK and European resuscitation council guideline)\n- If bradycardia is accompanied by adverse signs (Shock, Syncope, Myocardial ischaemia ,Heart failure) give atropine 500mcg IV (IO) and, if necessary, repeat every 3 - 5 min to a total of 3 mg.\n- If treatment with atropine is ineffective\n\n- consider second line drugs: These include isoprenaline (5mcg starting dose), and adrenaline (2 -10mcg).\n- For bradycardia caused by inferior myocardial infarction, cardiac transplant or spinal cord injury, consider giving aminophylline (100 - 200 mcg slow intravenous injection).\n- Consider giving glucagon if beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers are a potential cause of the bradycardia.\n- Do not give atropine to patients with cardiac transplants it can cause a high-degree AV block or even sinus arrest use aminophylline.\n- Consider pacing in patients who are unstable, with symptomatic bradycardia refractory to drug therapies.\n- If transthoracic pacing is ineffective, consider transvenous pacing.\n- Whenever a diagnosis of asystole is made, check the ECG carefully for the presence of P waves because unlike true asystole, this is more likely to respond to cardiac pacing.\n- If atropine is ineffective and transcutaneous pacing is not immediately available, fist pacing can be attempted while waiting for pacing equipment.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 45-year-old car driver was brought into the ED with transient loss of consciousness and a solitary episode of syncope. According to DVLA guidelines, which one of the following statements is correct?\n- **A)** May drive without notifying DVLA\n- **B)** May drive after 1 year but must notify DVLA\n- **C)** May not drive and Must notify DVLA\n- **D)** May drive after 6 months but must notify DVLA\n- **E)** May drive after 1 month but must notify DVLA\n- Explanation\n- According to DVLA guideline, unexplained/ reflex vasovagal syncope without cough syncope, DVLA need not to be notified.\n- Driving must cease for 6 months if a single episode of cough syncope, increased to 12 months if multiple attacks. (AUGUST-2011)ffO-THE-ME01CAL STANDARDS OF\n\nDISORDER FIRST UNPROVOKED EPILEPTIC SEIZURE/SOLITARY FIT Reflex Vasovagal Syncope Syncope With the 3"Ps\n- ( ral) If recurrent, will need to check the\n- 3 Ps\n- apply on each occasion. Loss of consciousness/ loss of or altered awareness likely to be unexplained syncope but with a high probability Of relex vasovagal LOSS Of consciousness/ loss Of or awareness with High Risk Factors. (Includes more than one episode in previous six months) presumed Loss of Consciousnesstloss Of or altered awareness With Seizure COUGH SYNCOPE CEREBROVASCULAR DISEASE: including stroke due to occkßive vascular disease, spontaneous intracerebral haemorrhage, TIA, amaurosis fugax and intracranial venous thrombosis. GROUP 1 ENTITLEMENT ODL - C M/CYCLE 6 months Off driving from the date Of the seizure unless there are dinical tactors or investigation results which suggest an unacceptably high risk of a further seizure, i.e. 20% greater per annum. NO driving restrictions. ( cope ) DVLA need not be notified. No driving restrictions. DVLA need not be notified. Licence refused/revoked for 6 months if no cause dentified. Can drive 4 weeks after the event if the cause has been identified and treated. months off from the date of episode. If a perscm suffers recurrent episodes of loss of consciousness with seizure markers. freedom from such episodes must be attained. Driving must cease for 6 months if a single episode, increased to 12 months if multiple attacks Must not drive for I month. May resume driving atter this period it tme clinical recovery is satisfactory. There is no need to notify DVLA unless there is residual deficit I month after the episode; Multiple TIAs over a short period may require at least 3 months free from further attacks before resuming driving and should m)tity DVLA. GROUP 2 ENTITLEMENT VOC - LGV/PCV Off driving from the date Of the seizure if recent assessment by a neurologist indicates that the risk of a further seizure is greater than 2% per annum NO driving restrictions (Except Cough Syn«npe) DVLA need not be notified Can drive 3 months aner the event (Except Cough Syncope) Licence for if no cause identified. Can drive 3 months after the event if the cause has been identified and treated. S vearS off driving from the date of an episode if the licence holder has undergone assessment by an appropriate specialist and no relevant abnormality has been identified S years off driving from the date of the last attack. Licence refused or revoked for I year following a stroke or TIA. Can be considered for "censing after this period provided that there is no debarring residual impairment likely to affect safe driving and there are no other significant risk factors.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 22-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a decreased level of consciousness. His Glasgow Coma Scale score is 13/15. You suspect meningitis and are considering a lumbar puncture.\n\nWhich of the following is a contraindication to performing an immediate LP in this patient?   A Temperature of 38.50C\n- **B)** Respiratory insufficiency\n- **C)** Platelet count of 110 x IOA9/L\n- **D)** INRof1.2\n- **E)** Skin petechia Explanation\n- The complications of lumbar puncture are:\n- coning\n- introduction of infection into the CSF\n-\n- For-these-reasons-the-followingsigns-are-gontraindications-for lumbar puncture: local skin sepsis\n- bleeding diatheses, e.g. anticoagulant therapy\n- signs of spinal cord compression\n- papilloedema or other signs of raised intracranial pressure\n- suspicion of intracranial or cord mass\n- congenital neurological lesions in lumbrosacral region\n-\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- NICE suggest that in children and young_pegple with suspected meningitis-or-suspected-meningococcal-disease,-perform a lumbar puncture unless any of the following contraindications are present: signs suggesting raised intracranial pressure reduced or fluctuating level of consciousness (Glasgow Coma Scale score less than 9 or a drop of 3 or more) relative bradycardia and hypertension focal neurological signs abnormal posture or posturing unequal, dilated or poorly responsive pupils papilloedema abnormal 'doll's eye' movements " shock extensive or spreading purpura after convulsions until stabilised coagulation abnormalities: (coagulation results (if obtained) outside the normal range, platelet count below 100 x IOA9/litre, receiving anticoagulant therapy) local superficial infection at the lumbar puncture site respiratory insufficiency (lumbar puncture is considered to have a high risk of precipitating respiratory failure in the presence of respiratory insufficiency). Note - you can find similar question in quiz 43. previOUS\n	\N	[]	\N	f	47	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
63064c6b-c5ee-4c15-a0f4-d3cd0ce56a10	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-47	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 47	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 47\n\nWhat would be your next action? A Inform police\n- **B)** Advise IUD\n- **C)** Inform her mother\n- **D)** Inform social services\n- **E)** Prescribe OCP\n- Explanation\n- NICE guideline recommends: If the woman is taking liver enzyme- inducing drugs or has stopped taking them within 28 days (such as rifampicin, carbamazepine, and phenytoin), the copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) is the preferred option for emergency contraception.\n- Note —for more detail on emergency contraception check quiz no:\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 48:\nThere is a similar question in it.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nYou are performing a point of care ultrasound (POCUS) of a patient who presents with trauma in the emergency department. A parasternal long-axis view is shown. What is the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Aortic dissection\n- **B)** Pulmonary embolism\n- **C)** Pneumothorax\n- **D)** Pleural effusion\n- **E)** Cardiac tamponade\n- Explanation\n- POCUS revealed pericardial effusion. Pericardial effusion(PCE) is a fluid collection in the pericardial sac that results in life-threatening cardiac tamponade. Normal PLAX view Abnormal PLAX view Pericardial Effusion Descend ng\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 55-year-old male was brought into the ED with severe respiratory distress with signs of obstruction drooling. He had a molar extraction due to infection 2 days before symptoms developed. What is the most appropriate next management step?\n- **A)** Oxygen and bronchodilator\n- **B)** RSI and urgent intubation\n- **C)** ENT consultation for surgical airway\n- **D)** Antibiotics\n- **E)** Surgical review\n- Explanation\n- Airway management is crucial in Ludwig's angina.\n- The airway is compromised in this patient, therefore, surgical airway consultation should be taken immediately.\n\n- Ludwig's angina It is a serious, potentially life-threatening infection of the tissues of the\n- floor of the mouth. Ludwig angina involves 3 compartments of the floor of the mouth:\n- sublingual, submental, and submandibular. The infection is rapidly progressive, leading to potential airway obstruction. The most common etiology is a dental infection in the lower molars, mainly second and third, accounting for over 90% of cases. Symptoms include: swelling\n- pain on raising of the tongue\n- swelling of the neck and the tissues of the submandibular and\n- sublingual spaces. Malaise, fever, dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), trismus and, in severe\n- cases, stridor or difficulty breathing can occur.\n- Investigations Ludwig angina is primarily diagnosed based on clinical evaluation.\n- culture and sensitivities of exudate from duct\n- FBC Facial radiographs (occlusal and/or soft tissue films)\n- Consider ultrasound of affected gland\n- Consider CT scan\n-\n\n- Management\n- Airway management\n- Airway compromise may be due to acute glandular swelling. The establishment of an open airway is essential for any patient with worsening airway compromise. Surgical airway may be required if intubation is unsuccessful.\n- Flexible nasotracheal intubation is the favored method of intubation, but arrangements for a surgical airway like cricothyrotomy must be in place before any attempt on airway intervention.\n- It is recommended that patients are intubated in a seated position and awake, using a flexible intubating endoscope.\n- Flexible nasotracheal intubation requires an experienced physician; if not possible, a cricothyrotomy or tracheostomy is sometimes performed as an emergency maneuver in the advanced stages of the infection.\n- Blind nasotracheal intubation, which involves passing an endotracheal tube without a direct view of the larynx, should be avoided in cases of Ludwig angina.\n- Intravenous antibiotics\n- Once the airway is secured, broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics serve as the first-line treatment. Antibiotics should cover aerobic, anaerobic, and oral microflora. Ampicillin-sulbactam or clindamycin are the rnost commonly prescribed antibiotics. In immunocompromised patients, the antibiotic coverage should include gram-negative rods\n\nand beta-lactamase-producing aerobes and anaerobes. Some options include cefepime, meropenem, or piperacillin-tazobactam.\n- For MRSA coverage includes the addition of vancomycin IV or linezolid IV to the mentioned antibiotic coverage.\n- Corticosteroid\n- If soft tissue swelling is significant and there are no contraindications, corticosteroid therapy (dexamethasone sodium phosphate) may be given in addition to antibiotics and analgesia,\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nRegarding the Wells Score and the likelihood of a pulmonary embolism (PE), which of the following statements is accurate?\n- **A)** 4 or more\n- **B)** 3 or more\n- **C)** 5 or more   D, 2 or more\n- **E)** 1 or more\n- Explanation\n\n- According to NICE guideline PE is likely if Wells score more than 4 points.\n- PE is unlikely if Wells's score is 4 or less.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nYou are tasked by a senior colleague to conduct research on the frequency of blood transfusions at your hospital. In addition, your senior asks you to include sensitive data, such as patients' religious beliefs, in your research report. Under the Data Protection Act 2018, which conduct might this request breach?\n- **A)** Failure to inform data subjects Show drafts\n- **B)** Incomplete data minimization\n- **C)** Breach of confidentiality\n- **D)** Data is not protected\n- **E)** Processing special category data without lawful basis\n- Explanation Under the Data Protection Act 2018 and the GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation), religious beliefs are classified as special category data (Article 9 of GDPR). This type of data requires explicit consent or a lawful basis for processing due to its sensitive nature. Including religious information in research without following the\n\nnecessary legal requirements would be a breach of the Data Protection Act 2018, particularly related to the lawful processing of special category data.\n- Principles involved:\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nSpecial category data: Includes racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, genetic data, biometric data, and data concerning health or sexual orientation.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nLawfulness, fairness, and transparency: Processing must have a lawful basis, and individuals must be informed about how their data will be used.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nData minimization: Only data necessary for the purpose of research should be collected. Including sensitive data without justification violates this principle.\n- Thus, in this case, including religious details in research without meeting GDPR conditions (explicit consent, or meeting specific lawful exceptions) would likely breach the Data Protection Act.\n- Article 9 Processing of special categories of personal data\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nProcessing of personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, or trade union membership, and the processing of genetic data, biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person, data concerning health or data concerning a natural person's sex life or sexual orientation shall be prohibited.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nParagraph I (mentioned above) shall not apply if one of the following applies:\n\n- (a)the data subject has given explicit consent to the processing of those personal data for one or more specified purposes, except where [domestic law provides] that the prohibition referred to in paragraph I may not be lifted by the data subject;\n- (b)processing is necessary for the purposes of carrying out the obligations and exercising specific rights of the controller or of the data subject in the field of employment and social security and social protection law in so far as it is authorised by [domestic law] or a collective agreement pursuant [to domestic law] providing for appropriate safeguards for the fundamental rights and the interests of the data subject;\n- (c)processing is necessary to protect the vital interests of the data subject or of another natural person where the data subject is physically or legally incapable of giving consent; (d)processing is carried out in the course of its legitimate activities with appropriate safeguards by a foundation, association or any other not- for-profit body with a political, philosophical, religious or trade union aim and on condition that the processing relates solely to the members or to former members of the body or to persons who have regular contact with it in connection with its purposes and that the personal data are not disclosed outside that body without the consent of the data subjects;\n- (e)processing relates to personal data which are manifestly made public by the data subject;\n- (f)processing is necessary for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims or whenever courts are acting in their judicial capacity;\n- (g)processing is necessary for reasons of substantial public interest, on the basis of [domestic law] which shall be proportionate to the aim\n\npursued and provide for suitable and specific measures to safeguard the fundamental rights and the interests of the data subject;\n- (h)processing is necessary for the purposes of preventive or occupational medicine, for the assessment of the working capacity of the employee, medical diagnosis, the provision of health or social care or treatrnent or the management of health or social care systems and services on the basis of [domestic law] or pursuant to contract with a health professional and subject to the conditions and safeguards referred to in paragraph 3;\n- (i)processing is necessary for reasons of public interest in the area of public health, such as protecting against serious cross-border threats to health or ensuring high standards of quality and safety of health care and of medicinal products or medical devices, on the basis of [domestic law] which provides for suitable and specific measures to safeguard the rights and freedoms of the data subject, in particular professional secrecy;\n- (j)processing is necessary for archiving purposes in the public interest, scientific or historical research purposes or statistical purposes in accordance with Article 89(1) [(as supplemented by section 19 of the 2018 Act)] based on [domestic law] which shall be proportionate to the aim pursued and provide for suitable and specific measures to safeguard the fundamental rights and the interests of the data subject.\n- 3.PersonaI data referred to in paragraph I may be processed for the purposes referred to in point (h) of paragraph 2 when those data are processed by or under the responsibility of a professional subject to the obligation of professional secrecy under [domestic law] or rules\n\nestablished by national competent bodies or by another person also subject to an obligation of secrecy under [domestic law] or rules established by national competent bodies.\n- Note- more details about data protection domestic law check data protection act.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 60-year-old male was admitted to ITU and being treated for sepsis. A patient is unresponsive and unable to take oral, your consultant asks you to perform an NG tube. An X-ray was done to confirm NG tube placement. His x-ray is shown. What is your next action?\n- **A)** Check pH\n- **B)** Commence feeding\n- **C)** Remove and reinsert the tube\n- **D)** Pull NC tube 2-3 cm back\n- **E)** Advance the NG tube by 2-3cm\n- Explanation\n\n- X-ray shows nasogastric tube is positioned within the left lower lobe bronchus. Hence, repositioning is required.\n- NC-tube-correcmplacement-confirmatjon\n- Attempt to aspirate gastric contents:\n- If aspiration is successful, test the PH: a value of <4 suggests correct placement.\n- If aspiration is unsuccessful or the pH is >4 the patient will require a chest x-ray (CXR).\n- Acceptable pH ranges also vary between hospitals, so always check local guidelines.\n- X-ray confirmation of NG tube position is a second-line investigation.\n- The CXR should be exposed and centered appropriately to demonstrate the position of the NG tube. The film must show the bottom of both hemi-diaphragms in the midline.\n- Note —you may have different x-rays in the exam. Please check Quiz 41 for further details.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 30-year-old female was brought into the ED with a complaint of speech disturbance and weakness in her arm. She also complains of visual neglect. She cannot see her husband on the left side. What is the rosier score?\n\n- Explanation\n- The rosier score depends on clinical findings.\n- This patient has:\n- Speech disturbance = I point score\n- Arm wea kness\n- Total = 1 point score = 2 points score\n- There is a difference between visual field defect and visual neglect.\n- Visual neglect is not included in Rosier's score.\n- Rosier score\n\nClinical findings Loss of consciousness or syncope Seizure activity Value No (0) No (0) Yes-I Yes-I New, acute onset (or on awakening from sleep) finding of any of the following Asymmetric facial weakness Asymmetric arm weakness Asymmetric leg weakness Speech disturbance Visual field defect No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) No (0) Yes+l Yes+l Yes+l Yes+l Yes+l\n- Visual field loss/defect VS Visual neglect\n\nVisual filed loss/defect Physical loss of visual filed to one side Decreased perceptual filed\n- It is also called Homonymous Hemianopia Visual neglect Attention impairment where patient is unable to attend one side of their body. It is also called: Hemi-spatial neglect Hemiagnosia Hemi-neglect Unilateral neglect Hemi — inattention Unilateral visual inattention Neglect syndrome\n- Visual neglect (visual hemi-inattention) is a neuropsychological disorder of attention in which patients exhibit a lack of response to stimuli in one-half of their visual field that cannot be explained by primary damage to the visual geniculostriate pathways. It is part of the broader hemispatial neglect syndrome which frequently occurs following cerebral injury to the right parietal lobe and almost always affects the hemispace contralateral to the cerebral lesion. It is frequently seen in the context of cerebrovascular disease, affecting up to 5 million stroke patients each year.\n- TIA vs Stroke\n\nStrokes TIAs\n- Transient ischaemic attack (TIA) is a transient (less\n- Stroke is a clinical syndrome of presumed vascular than 24 hours) neurological dysfunction caused by origin characterized by rapidly developing signs of focal brain, spinal cord, or retinal ischemia, without focal or global disturbance of cerebral functions evidence of acute infarction. which lasts longer than 24 hours or leads to death.\n- Ischaemic stroke (85% of strokes are ischaemic) A TIA has a sudden onset and can last from a few — an episode of neurological dysfunction caused by minutes to 24 hours. Most people have complete focal cerebral, spinal, or retinal cell death due to resolution of symptoms and signs within 1 hour. infarction following vascular occlusion or stenosis. Haemorrhagic stroke — rapidly developing neurological dysfunction due to a focal collection Of blood from within the brain parenchyma or ventricular system (intracerebral haemorrhage), or bleeding into the arachnoid space (subarachnoid haemorrhage) that is not caused by trauma.\n- Silent stroke — radiological or pathological evidence of an infarction or haemorrhage not caused by trauma without an attributable history of acute neurological dysfunction attributable to the lesion. 1\n- Clinical features linical featu\n- Most TIAs are thought to resolve within 1 hour but The person presents with sudden onset, focal can persist for up to 24 hours. neurological deficit which is ongoing or has persisted\n- Focal neurological deficits may include: for longer than 24 hours and cannot be explained\n- Unilateral weakness or sensory loss. by another condition such as hypoglycaemia.\n- Dysphasia. Confusion, altered level of consciousness, and\n- Ataxia, vertigo, or loss Of balance. coma.\n- Syncope.\n- Headache — usually of insidious onset and\n- Sudden transient loss of vision in one eye gradually increasing intensity in intracranial (amaurosis fugax), diplopia, or homonymous haemorrhage, and sudden, severe headache in hemianopia. subarachnoid haemorrhage which may be\n- Cranial nerve defects associated with neck stiffness. Sentinel headache(s) may occur in the preceding weeks.\n- Unilateral weakness or paralysis in the face, arm, or leg. Sensory loss — paraesthesia or numbness.\n- Ataxia, Dysphasia, Dysarthria.\n- Visual disturbance — homonymous hemianopia, diplopia. Gaze paresis — this is often horizontal and unidirectional. Photophobia.\n- Dizziness, vertigo, Or loss Of balance — isolated dizziness is not usually a symptom Of TIA.\n- Nausea and/or vomiting. Specific cranial nerve deficits such as unilateral tongue weakness or Horner's syndrome (miosis, ptosis. and facial anhidrosis).\n- Difficulty with fine motor coordination and gait.\n- Neck or facial pain (associated with arterial dissection)\n- Posterior circulation strokes mav be difficult to diagnose and should be suspected if the person\n\nManaqement\n- suspected TIA Offer aspirin 300 mg immediately unless contraindicated — give a proton-pump inhibitor to anyone with dyspepsia associated with aspirin use. If aspirin is contraindicated discuss management urgently with the specialist team. Advise people already taking low dose aspirin regularly to continue — do not offer them aspirin 300 mg. Refer the person for specialist assessment ang investiaationt to be seen within 24 hours of onset Ofs m toms.\n- People with a bleeding disorder, or taking an anticoagulant should have an urgent computed tomography (CT) scan to exclude haemorrhage. DO not use scoring systems, such as ABCD2, to assess risk of subsequent stroke or to inform urgency of referral for people who have had a suspected or confirmed TIA. Symptoms of acute vestibular syndrome — acute, persistent, continuous vertigo or dizziness with nystagmus, nausea or vomiting, head motion intolerance, and new gait unsteadiness.\n- Management\n- Admission Arrange immediate emergency admission to an acute stroke facility for anyone with suspected acute stroke. DO not start anticoagulation (for example in people with atrial fibrillation) or antiplatelet treatment in people following ischaemic stroke until intracerebral haemorrhage has been excluded by brain imaging. Monitor and manage any deterioration in clinical condition (airway, breathing, and circulation [ABCs]).\n- Give supplemental oxygen to people with acute stroke if oxygen saturations are less than 95% and there are no contraindications.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA IO-year-old child was brought to ED with cardiac arrest with dilated pupils and unresponsive to light paramedics had been doing his cpr before for 5 mins. The consultant is 20 minutes late; the Child has developed rigor mortis, but the mother wants to continue CPR. What would be your next action?\n- **A)** Send mother to other room\n- **B)** Wait for consultant to review\n- **C)** Ask peadiatric registrar\n- **D)** Tell rnother CPR will have no benefit and stop CPR\n- **E)** Continue CPR Explanation\n-\n- Recognition of death and termination of cardiac resuscitation\n- CROUP A Conditions unequivocally associated with death Decapitation\n- Massive cranial and cerebral destruction\n- Hemicorporectomy (or similar massive injury)\n- Decomposition\n- Incineration\n- Rigor mortis Fetal maceration\n- Note- In these groups, death can be recognised by the clinical\n- confirmation of cardiac arrest CROUP B Conditions requiring ECG evidence of asystole\n- Submersion for more than three hours in adults over 18 years of age,\n- with or without hypothermia Continuous asystole, despite cardiopulmonary resuscitation, for more\n- than 20 minutes in a normothermic patient\n\n- Patients who have recieved no resuscitation for at least 15 minutes after collapse and have no pulse or respiratory effort on arrival of the ambulance personnel\n- Notezln all these cases, at least one minute's duration of continuous ECG record must be free from artifact and demonstrate asystole without QRS or P waves. There rnust be no positive history of sedative, hypnotic, anxiolytic, opioid or anaesthetic drugs in the preceding 24 hou rs.\n- CROUP C Terminal illness\n-\n- Cases of terminal illness where the doctor has given clear, written and signed instructions that the patient is is not for resuscitation.\n\nPulseless apnoeic Mortal iniury Incineration, Yes putrefaction, rigor mortis, etc? No Yes Yes Adult submersed for >3 hours Continuous asystole for > 5 mins? Normothermic? No drugs? Adults? NO Defibrillate if shockable rhythm Terminal illness with DNR order? No Attach ECG defib analyse Asystole No Yes Evidence of CPR in past 15 mins? No Yes Yes Evidence of drowning? Hypothermia? Drugs? Child aged <18? No CPR 1 minute Pulse? Breathi ? Reanalyse* 2 Shock advised? Yes Yes Yes\n\nDo not resuscitate shockable rhythm Asystole? Resuscitate Yes\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 32-year-old man presents to the emergency department following a fight. He complains of pain around the base of the right thumb metacarpal. His thumb X-ray is shown. What is the management?\n- **A)** Incision and drainage\n- **B)** Mallet splint\n- **C)** Antibiotics\n- **D)** Neighbour splint   E, Surgical fixation\n- Explanation\n\nX-ray shows an unstable Bennett fracture. An operative treatment is\n- recommended for unstable fracture patterns and intra-articular displacement of mm. While open reduction and internal fixation with a screw or K-wire are both common practice, screws are often preferred as K-wires must be removed after union.\n- Bennett fracture\n- It is a fracture of the base of the thumb resulting from forced abduction of the first metacarpal. It is defined as an intra-articular two-part fracture of the base of the first metacarpal bone. Investigations\n- Plain radiograph;\n- two piece fracture of the base of the thumb metacarpal\n- intra-articular extension\n- dorsolateral displacement\n- small fragment of Istmetacarpal, attached to anterior oblique ligament,\n- continues to articulate with trapezium, lateral retraction of the first metacarpal shaft by abductor pollicis\n- longus. Management\n- Non-operative treatment in a thumb spica cast for 3-4 weeks can be\n- considered in stable, non-displaced fractures.\n\nOperative treatment is recommended for unstable fracture patterns and intra-articular displacement of mm. While open reduction and internal fixation with a screw or K-wire are both common practice, screws are often preferred as K-wires must be removed after union.\n- Complications\n- Untreated or malreduced fractures can lead to post-traumatic osteoarthritis, which can cause significant pain and functional decline.\n\nThis x-ray shows stable Bennett fracture  IO. A 70-year-old elderly person suffering from mesothelioma presents to the ED with a complaint of submandibular pain and swelling especially worsens while eating and chewing. What is the likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Salivary gland tumor\n\nB\n- Sialolithiasis\n- **C)** Sjogren syndrome   D\n- Sialadenitis\n- **E)** Sarcoidosis\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features without fever, dry eyes, or mouth suggest Sialolithiasis.\n- Sialolithiasis is the formation of salivary gland stones, most commonly in the submandibular gland, which can cause pain and swelling, especially when eating or chewing.\n- Sialolithiasis VS Sialadenitis\n\nSialolithiasis Sialolithiasis is a condition where a calcified stone (sialolith) forms within a salivary gland. The salivary glands\n- The salivary glands are exocrine glands that produce saliva and secrete it into the oral cavity. There are three main salivary glands and several smaller ones of less significance. The three main salivary glands are: The parotid glands\n- The submandibular glands\n- The sublingual glands Approximately 90% of salivary gland stones occur in the submandibular gland (Wharton's duct), With the majority of the remainder occurring within the parotid gland. Occasionally they can also occur in the sublingual gland or minor salivary glands.\n- Risk factors Dehydration\n- Smoking\n- Local trauma Sjogren's syndrome\n- History of nephrolithiasis\n- Hyperparathyroidism Chronic periodontal disease Gout Chronic periodontal disease\n- Head and neck radiotherapy Drugs, e.g. diuretics and anti-cholinergic medications Clinical features Sialolithiasis is commonly asymptomatic; however, a small percentage of people become symptomatic and experience intermittent symptoms, The sialolith obstructs the passage Of saliva, which can cause pain and swelling Of the affected gland during eating.\n- Pain is typically at its worst when salivary flow is high, immediately before and during eating, and then slowly subsides in the hour after. Symptoms are typically unilateral in nature.\n- Bimanual palpation Of the floor Of the mouth may reveal a stone, and occasionally, the stone may actually be visible intra-orally at the duct orifice. If superimposed infection is present, it may be possible to express pus from the gland. Investigations X-rays of the floor of the mouth, which can demonstrate the stone as around 80% are radio-opaque, or ultrasound scans— minimally invasive, cheap and very good at analysing the glands and peri-glandular structures. or Sialadenitis Sialadenitis denotes inflammation and swelling of the parotid, submandibular, sublingual, or minor salivary glands. Aetiology includes bacterial or Viral infection, obstruction, radiotherapy damage, or autoimmune causes. Acute bacterial sialadenitis is characterised by rapid onset Of pain and swelling. In contrast, chronic sialadenitis is characterised by intermittent, recurrent episodes of tender swelling. Painless swellings (unless secondarily infected) classically occur in autoimmune sialadenitis (i.e., Sjogren syndrome) and may be unilateral or bilateral.\n- Risk factors\n- volume depletion and malnutrition\n- immunosuppression Sjogren syndrome connective tissue diseases women aged 50 to 60\n- Sjogren syndrome is most common in peri- and post-menopausal women. sialolithiasis\n- dental/orthodontic procedures\n- trauma (cheek biting)\n- sialectasis, diverticuli, and strictures\n- Clinical features\n- fever pain and dysphagia facial swelling recurrent painful swellings episodic swelling during eating exudates of pus from salivary gland opening use Of xerostomic medications dry eyes and mouth - Dryness of the oral cavity and eyes are key symptoms of Sjogren syndrome and may be seen in combination With a connective tissue disease (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis, dermatomyositis, or scleroderma). oral candidiasis - May be present in Sjogren syndrome or in association With connective tissue disorders. iodine contrast exposure\n- investigations culture and sensitivities of exudate from duct\n- FBC facial radiographs (occlusal and/or soft tissue films) - sialoliths identified if present Consider ultrasound Of affected eland\n\n- Sialography— but this is not routinely performed because it is invasive compared to the Other tests as it requires the gland to be cannulated and injected with dye. Management\n- Most cases can be managed conservatively with rehydration, analgesia and the use of sialagogues, such as lemon juice, Which can help to promote saliva production. In cases where the gland becomes infected, and sialadenitis develops, antibiotics are usually required. Recurrent or persistent cases require definitive treatment with either interventional radiological procedures or surgery. Sjggren syndrome\n- Consider CT scan - Indicated if conventional plain films are negative or When clinical presentation is severe. When ordering CT, consider concurrent sialography in non-acute patients. digital subtraction sialography - Used in combination With CT when sialolithiasis, ductal strictures, or loss of parenchymal integrity is suspected (i.e., chronic sialadenitis).\n- Management Sizn_gfriEYAEAirway compromise may be due to acute glandular swelling. Surgical airway may be required if intubation is unsuccessful. Elevating the head Of the bed may help temporarily, but careful monitoring is crucial.\n- acute bacterial sialadenitis (non-obstructive)\n- IV antibiotics - Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy should be started pending sensitivity results based on cultures. If a patient is intolerant Of beta-lactam antibiotics or vancomycin, clindamycin or metronidazole are alternative options. conservative management (hydration, pain relief, and sialogogues) - Acute medical treatment for bacterial sialadenitis includes conservative therapies such as hydration, analgesia(NSAlD), sialogogues (e.g. pilocarpine, cevimeline) to stimulate salivary secretion, and regular, gentle gland massage.\n- Consider corticosteroid — dexamethasone surgical drainage— if abscess identified Obstructive sialadenitis\n- conservative management (hydration, pain relief, and sialogogues) — follow as mentioned above\n- interventional stone removal - Interventional sialendoscopy using a semi-rigid endoscope with irrigation is increasingly used as a first-line therapy for stones in the distal ducts for both the submandibular and parotid glands. treatment as mentioned above treat the underlying cause. recurrent sialadenitis: anv cause times/vear or severe attacks) —surgical excision of gland\n\n- Sjogren syndrome is a systemic auto-immune disorder characterised by the presence of dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca) and dry mouth (xerostomia) as a consequence of lymphocytic infiltration into the lacrimal and salivary glands:\n- Sjogren syndrome can occur alone (primary Sjogren syndrome) or be associated with another auto-immune disease (secondary Sjogren syndrome) such as lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, and systemic sclerosis:\n- Risk factors\n\nsystemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\n- rheumatoid arthritis\n- systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)\n-\n- HLA class II markers - HLA class II markers -AI, -B8, or -DR3/DQ2 haplotype are linked with susceptibility to Sjogren syndrome. age 40 to 70 - Sjogren syndrome predominantly affects middle-aged\n- women. Clinical features\n- fatigue\n- dry eyes - Recurrent sensation of sand/gravel, itch, or burning in eyes. Persisting more than 3 months, multiple episodes per week. Redness of the eyes and sensitivity to light and wind are common. dry mouth - Can be associated with difficulties in chewing and swallowing food, difficulties in speaking, and altered or decreased taste acuities. Vasculitis - Common cutaneous manifestations may include dry skin\n- (xeroderma), palpable and non-palpable purpura, and/or urticaria-like lesions in around 50% of patients with Sjogren syndrome. dental caries\n- Less common\n- no saliva pool\n- enlarged salivary glands\n- Facial pain\n- burning mouth syndrome etc\n-\n\n- Investigations\n- Schirmer's test - Quantitatively measures tears. A filter paper is placed in the lower conjunctival sac. The test is positive if less than 5 mm of paper is wetted after 5 minutes. anti-60 kD (SS-A) Ro and anti-La (SS-B) - Antibodies to the\n- ribonucleoproteins 60 kD Ro and La are found in up to 90% of patients with Sjogren syndrome.\n- Other-investigations-to-be-considered sialometry\n- parotid sialography\n- lissamine green test etc\n- Management\n-\n- dry_eyes Artificial tear alone\n- ophthalmic ciclosporin drops - ciclosporin ophthalmic: (0.05%, 0.09%,\n- 0.1%) 1 drop into the affected eye(s) every 12 hours. cholinergic drug (third line) - pilocarpine: 5 mg orally three times daily.\n- Best to avoid systemic pilocarpine for patients with respiratory disease\n- (e.g., chronic bronchitis, asthma, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) and those taking antihypertensive medicines because, even though no notable drug interactions have been reported, interactions with beta-blockers appear to be possible.\n\n- dry-mouth\n- salivary substitutes alone - Salivary substitutes for improving lubrication and hydration of oral tissues are used alone as first-line therapy.\n- Gels (fluoride gels), saliva-stimulating lozenges or chewing gums, mouthwashes, prescription-strength toothpastes, and oral rinses have been used for this purpose.\n- Cholinergic drugs (second line) - as above\n- Patient with musculoskeletal manifestations\n- Analgesia — paracetamol or NSAID\n- disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drug - Hydroxychloroquine is recommended as a first-line disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drug (DMARD) for inflammatory musculoskeletal pain in patients with primary Sjogren syndrome.\n- If both monotherapies are ineffective, combination treatment with hydroxychloroquine plus methotrexate can be considered.\n- Corticosteroid (Third line) - prednisolone: 1 mg/kg orally once daily initially, then taper dose gradually in 2-3 week period.\n- Patient with vasculitis\n- Corticosteroid - The most common manifestation of vasculitis is skin rash, usually palpable purpura. Most patients have only a single lifetime episode of skin vasculitis. Treatment is a short course of corticosteroids.\n\n- intravenous immunoglobulin (second line) - Intravenous immunoglobulin treatrnent in more severe/resistant cases may be effective, but therapeutic experience in vasculitis is limited.\n- normal immunoglobulin human: 0.4 g/kg/day intravenously for 5 days\n- Rituximab(third line) - Rituximab may be considered for patients with primary Sjogren syndrome with vasculitis or cryoglobulinaemia associated with vasculitis manifestations if they have experienced unacceptable adverse effects or toxicity with corticosteroids or other agents, or they are not able to taper and discontinue corticosteroid treatment.\n- Patient with renal tubular acidosis\n- A few patients may have severe manifestations of proximal renal tubular acidosis (ATA), even presenting with profound hypokalaemia. The usual therapy of type 2 RTA of any aetiology should be instituted. This includes potassium repletion and alkali by mouth:\n- Patient with neuropathy_\n- intravenous immunoglobulin - normal immunoglobulin human: 0.4 g/kg/day intravenously for 5 days. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	48	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
03bd9447-661d-4871-9528-d24d9abfddec	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-48	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 48	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 48\n\nNeedle cricothyroidotomy\n- **B)** Needle thoracostomy\n- **C)** Reverse rocuronium with Sugammadex\n- **D)** Intubate using fiberoptic laryngoscope\n- **E)** Provide 02 via face mask\n- Explanation\n- Thirty minutes have passed, and initial attempts at ventilation and supraglottic airway have not been successful in a patient with face trauma who is hypoventilating, which indicates Plan A and B have failed. The next step would be Plan C- face mask ventilation.\n\n2015 Management of unanticipated difficult tracheal intubation in adults If in difficulty call for help tracheal intubation w:th (mnimum 3 SAD STOP AND THINK Options risks and benefitsE pätiH1t up 2 Intubate tmchea via the SAO\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nBithWt VVake the up up\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 60-year-old woman presents to the ED with complaints of left lower abdominal pain, urinary urgency, and suprapubic pain along with she has noticed weight loss. What is the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Diverticulosis\n- **B)** CA ovary\n- **C)** CA bladder\n- **D)** Pelvic inflammatory disease\n- **E)** Renal cell carcinoma\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggest ovarian cancer\n- CA ovary_\n- Ovarian cancer arises from the ovary.\n- Epithelial carcinomas account for approximately 90% of primary ovarian cancers.\n- Most are thought to arise from the fimbria of the fallopian tube. Primary ovarian-type tumours may also develop within the peritoneum.\n- The World Health Organization (WHO) classification of ovarian tumours recognizes six major histotypes: serous (70-85% of cases), mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, transitional cell, and other carcinomas.\n- Tumours of each type are further subdivided into benign, malignant, and intermediate (or borderline malignancy).\n- Non-epithelial cancers account for approximately 10% of primary ovarian cancers.\n- They include germ cell tumours, and sex cord/stromal cell turnours.\n\n- Secondary metastatic tumours can occur in the ovary, most notably from cancers of the: Endometrium.\n- Breast.\n- Gastrointestinal tract.\n- Ovarian cancer may spread to:\n- Intraperitoneal structures and organs, causing intestinal obstruction\n- and cachexia.\n- The liver. Para-aortic lymph nodes.\n-\n- The lung, causing pleural effusions. Increasing age — the incidence of ovarian cancer increases with age\n- and peaks in the eighth decade. Cene mutations — BRCAI and BRCA2 Family history of ovarian cancer\n- Conditions that increase the number of ovulatory cycles, such as\n- nulliparity, early menarche, and late menopause Use of hormone replacement therapy (HRT) — there appears to be a\n- small increased risk of ovarian cancer associated with the use of HRT (both oestrogen-only and combined HRT). Cancer — a personal history of ovarian, breast, or bowel cancer is\n- associated with an increased risk of ovarian cancer. Endometriosis — endometriosis is sometimes found next to\n- endometrioid or clear cell ovarian cancers, and evidence has shown an\n\nincreased incidence of ovarian cancer in women with documented endometriosis.\n- Diabetes— particularly in women who use insulin. Lifestyle factors, including:\n- Tobacco smoking.\n- Being overweight or obese.\n- Occupational exposure to asbestos.\n-\n- The risk of ovarian cancer is reduced by: Conditions that decrease the number of ovulatory cycles, including:\n-\n- A higher number of pregnancies. Breastfeeding.\n- Use of the combined oral contraceptive pill.\n- Tubal ligation and hysterectomy.\n- Clinical features\n- In any woman (particularly if over 50 years of age) if any of the following\n- symptoms are persistent or frequent (particularly more than 12 times per month):\n- Abdominal distension (bloating). Feeling full (early satiety) and/or loss of appetite.\n- Pelvic or abdominal pain.\n- Increased urinary urgency and/or frequency.\n- In any woman over 50 years of age, if she has had:\n-\n- Symptoms suggestive of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) within the last 12\n\nConsider the possibility of ovarian cancer and consider carrying out\n- tests in any woman who reports any of the following unexplained symptoms: Weight loss.\n- Malaise or fatigue.\n-\n- Change in bowel habit. Other symptoms of ovarian cancer that may be present include:\n- Abnormal or postmenopausal bleeding.\n- Gastrointestinal symptoms, such as dyspepsia, nausea, or bowel\n- obstruction.\n- Shortness of breath (due to pleural effusion). Management Carry out an abdominal and pelvic examination.\n-\n- If there is ascites, or a pelvic or abdominal mass (which is not caused by known uterine fibroids), refer the woman urgently to a gynaecological cancer service (to be seen within 2 weeks) for further investigation. If the examination is normal, measure serum CA125 concentration.\n- If serum CA125 concentration is raised (35 IU/mL or greater), arrange\n- an urgent ultrasound scan of the abdomen and pelvis. If the ultrasound is suggestive of ovarian cancer, refer the woman\n- urgently to a gynaecological cancer service (to be seen within 2 weeks) for further investigation. If serum CA125 is normal (less than 35 IU/mL), or raised (35 IU/mL or\n- greater) but with a normal ultrasound:\n\n- Consider other causes for symptoms or a raised CA125.\n- If no other clinical cause is apparent, advise the woman to return for review if her symptoms become more frequent and/or persistent.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 71 -year-old man presents to the Emergency Department (ED) with a 3-week history of worsening pain and erythema in his lower limb, despite having changed antibiotics four times as prescribed by his GP. An X-ray of his condition is shown. What is the likely diagnosis? A\n- Osteomyelitis\n- **B)** Cellulitis\n- **C)** Fractured fibula\n- **D)** Gout\n- **E)** Osteoarthritis\n- Explanation\n- An x-ray and patient clinical features suggest osteomyelitis\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Osteomyelitis Osteomyelitis is an inflammatory condition of bone caused by an infecting organism, rnost commonly Staphylococcus aureus. It usually involves a single bone but may rarely affect multiple sites. It may occur in the peripheral or axial skeleton. Risk factors previous osteomyelitis penetrating injury intravenous drug misuse\n- Diabetes HIV infection recent surgery distant or local infection sickle cell disease rheumatoid arthritis chronic kidney disease immunocompromising conditions or, in children upper respiratory tract or varicella infection In children, it is important to know whether they have received the Haemophilus influenzaetype B (Hib) vaccination. Clinical features limp or reluctance to weight-bear - Common presentation of acute osteomyelitis in children.\n\nnon-specific pain at site of infection\n- malaise and fatigue\n- paravertebral muscle tenderness and spasm\n- local inflammation, tenderness, erythema, or swelling\n- Pain:\n-\n- Is localized to the infected disc space area\n- Is worsened by physical activity or percussion to the affected area\n- May radiate to the abdomen, hip, leg, scrotum, groin, or perineum In the lower back may indicate axial osteomyelitis, or lumbar discitis -\n- Lumbar discitis is particularly common in older people Spinal cord or nerve root compression and meningitis may occur. Neurological signs occur more commonly with cervical or thoracic\n- involvement. wound drainage, acute or old healed sinuses\n- scars, previous flaps, fracture fixation\n- reduced range of movement - Movement may be restricted due to pain\n- at the site of infection or an associated septic arthritis, particularly in children.\n- Investigations\n- FBC\n- ESR Blood culture\n- plain x-rays of affected area\n-\n\nOther investigations to be considered\n- bone samples and bone biopsy\n- polymerase chain reaction (PCR)\n-\n- MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry Swabs\n- bone MRI\n- Management (antibiotics + supportive care)\n- suspected acute peripheral osteomyelitis - low MRSA prevalence\n- Antibiotic in adults - If the patient is unwell, give intravenous\n- flucloxacillin within 1 hour of reaching hospital. If the patient has a penicillin allergy, give intravenous vancomycin. Antibiotic in children - For children aged 3 months to 5 years, give an\n- intravenous cephalosporin such as cefazolin or cefuroxime. In UK practice, ceftriaxone is considered a better option for methicillin- sensitive Staphylococcus aureus(MSSA) than cefuroxime.\n- First-generation cephalosporins (e.g., cefazolin) may be suboptimal if the osteomyelitis is caused by Streptococcus pneumoniaeor Haemophilus influenzaetype b in unvaccinated children. For children aged 25 years - give an intravenous antistaphylococcal\n- penicillin, or cefazolin. When risk factors for atypical organisms are present (e.g., sickle cell\n- disease), consider ceftriaxone with or without an antistaphylococcal\n\npenicillin or clindamycin.\n- Consider switching from intravenous to oral antibiotics after 2 to 4 days when the child:\n- Has been afebrile for 24 to 48 hours\n- Shows clinical improvement with reduced pain, inflammation, and improved mobility\n- Has a C-reactive protein (CRP) level that has decreased by at least 30% of the highest value\n- suspected acute peripheral osteomyelitis - high MRSA prevalence\n- Antibiotic in adult - If the patient is unwell, give intravenous vancomycin within I hour of reaching hospital.\n- Teicoplanin and linezolid are reasonable alternatives if the patient cannot tolerate vancomycin, or if vancomycin is contraindicated.\n- Antibiotic in children 2 For children aged <2 years, if the prevalence of MRSA is to 15% in the community, add clindamycinto the empirical regimen as indicated for Iow MRSA prevalencein the patient group above. If, however, the prevalence of clindamycin resistance in MRSA is 210%, prescribe vancomycin or linezolid.\n- For children aged 22 years - if the prevalence of MRSA is >10% to 15% in the community, give clindamycin alone, as long as the prevalence of clindamycin resistance in MRSA is <10%.\n- Rifampicin or an antistaphylococcal beta-lactam (e.g., amoxicillin/clavulanate) may be added depending on patient and prevalence factors.\n\n- If there is a high rate of MRSA and there is severe infection, a high rate of clindamycin resistance (210%), or initial treatment failure, give vancomycin or teicoplanin. Rifampicin may be added; however, there is little evidence to support this.\n- sus n iv v r myelitis\n- Immediate referral to infectious disease specialist and spine surgeon\n- Supportive + antibiotics - Give an empirical antibiotic regimen that covers staphylococci (including MRSA), streptococci, and gram- negative bacilli.\n- Vancomycin plus a third- or fourth-generation cephalosporin (e.g., ceftriaxone) is an example of a suitable regimen.\n- For patients with an allergy or intolerance, give daptomycin plus a fluoroquinolone such as ciprofloxacin\n- Consider surgery - Surgical intervention is required for progressive neurological deficits, progressive deformity, and spinal instability with or without pain despite adequate antimicrobial therapy.\n- suspected acute osteomyelitis in diabetic foot\n- referral to multidisciplinary foot care team - The specialist from the multidisciplinary foot care service should take responsibility for managing a patient with a diabetic foot and suspected osteomyelitis.\n- Antibiotic - The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends giving one of the following intravenous antibiotic regimens for 48 hours in an adult aged 218 years with suspected osteomyelitis (moderate to severe diabetic foot problem):\n\n- Flucloxacillin with or without gentamicin and/or metronidazole\n- Amoxicillin/clavulanate with or without gentamicin\n- Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole.\n- If you suspect Pseudomonas infection, add in: Piperacillin/tazobactam\n- Clindamycin plus ciprofloxacin and/or gentamicin, if the patient has a penicillin allergy.\n- chronic osteomyelitis\n- referral to specialist team\n- Note - check the osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis module in your quiz session.\n- you may have different X-rays in your exam.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 25-year-old male presents to the ED with eye pain. His eye image is shown. What is the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Penetrating eye trauma\n- **B)** Conjunctivitis\n- **C)** Retrobulbar hemorrhage\n- **D)** Corneal foreign body\n- **E)** Hyphemia Explanation\n-\n- The image shows a penetrating globe injury Globe Rupture\n- is a traumatic injury involving the breakdown in the integrity of the wall\n- of the eye, either the sclera or cornea or both Causes\n- blunt trauma, projectile injury, laceration of eyelid/periorbital area,\n- metal on metal, high-speed machinery, explosions Clinical features\n- Protruding foreign body in eye\n- Eccentric/teardrop pupil\n- Decreased visual acuity\n-\n\n- Afferent pupillary defect Extrusion of vitreous\n-\n- External prolapse of the iris or ciliary body Tenting of the sclera or cornea at the site of globe puncture\n- Volume loss of eye\n- Investigation\n- Seidel test apply fluorescein dye to eye, which binds damaged\n- corneal epithelium and turns green under light through a cobalt-blue filter or Wood lamp, and check for streaming of fluorescein-tinged aqueous humor; this indicates corneal laceration CT of orbit\n- Management\n- Prevent increased intraocular pressure (IOP)\n-\n- Head of bed at 30 degrees\n- Avoid eye manipulation (do not measure eye pressure, retract lid, or perform ocular ultrasound) Cover with an eye shield (do not place eye patch as it exerts pressure\n- on globe) — A paper cup can be used if an eye shield is not available\n- Administer topical and systemic broad-spectrum antibiotics Tetanus booster as required\n- Give antiemetics to prevent nausea (which increases IOP)\n- Give pain medicine\n- Consult ophthalmology even if CT negative, formal surgical evaluation\n- may be needed if occult injury suspected\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nWhich of the following is true regarding bacterial tracheitis?\n- **A)** Most cases present with wheezing\n- **B)** Does not respond to adrenaline\n- **C)** Caused by Strep pyogenes\n- **D)** Every child requires intubation\n- **E)** Typically seen in children under 6 months of age\n- Explanation\n- Bacterial tracheitis is commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus.\n- Not every child requires intubation, though airway management is a concern.\n- Adrenaline, which is effective in croup, does not usually work in bacterial tracheitis.\n- Wheezing is not a common presentation; stridor due to airway obstruction is more typical.\n- It generally affects children older than 6 months.\n- Bacterial Tracheitis\n\nBacterial (exudative) tracheitis is a potentially lethal infection of the\n- subglottic trachea, which can occur in isolation or as a post-viral respiratory infection complication. Bacterial tracheitis has a peak incidence between the ages of three to\n- eight years old, although it has been described in both infants and adults. Symptoms of bacterial tracheitis may be intermediately between those of epiglottitis and croup. Presentation is either acute or subacute,\n- A high index of suspicion for bacterial tracheitis is needed in children with viral croup—like symptoms who do not respond to standard croup treatment or clinically worsen. Causes\n- Most common cause is Staphylococcus aureus other include:\n- Haemophilus influenzae(18%)\n- Streptococcus pneumoniae(15%)\n- Moraxella\n- Streptococcus pyogenes(9%).\n-\n- Clinical features Inspiratory stridor (with or without expiratory stridor)\n- Fever\n- Bark like or brassy cough\n- Hoarseness\n- Worsening or abruptly occurring stridor\n-\n\n- Varying degrees of respiratory distress ( Retractions, Dyspnea, Nasal flaring, Cyanosis)\n- Sore throat, odynophagia Dysphonia\n- No drooling\n- No specific position of comfort (The patient may lie supine.)\n- Differential-Diagnoses\n- Croup\n- Angioedema\n- Candidiasis\n- Diphtheria Epiglottitis\n- Peritonsillar Abscess\n- Tuberculosis\n- Retropharyngeal Abscess\n- Investigations\n- Radiography of the neck may be indicated, however, it's neither definitive nor essential.\n- Radiograph may reveal subglottic narrowing on anteroposterior (AP) views - Steeple sign, similar to croup.\n- It may reveal clouding of tracheal air column or irregular tracheal margin on lateral view.\n- Definitive diagnosis is made by direct visualization by laryngotracheobronchoscopy.\n\n- although it is not necessary with strong clinical suspicion. Laryngoscopy and bronchoscopy allow visualization of the infected airway and will demonstrate a normal or mildly erythematous epiglottis and an erythematous, edematous trachea with thick mucopurulent exudates.\n- Management\n- Airway_\n- Maintenance of an adequate airway is of primary importance.\n- Avoid agitating the child. If the patient's respiratory status deteriorates, it is usually because of movement of the membrane, and bag-valve- mask ventilation should be effective.\n- If intubation is required, use an endotracheal tube 0.5-1 size smaller than expected in order to minimize trauma in the inflamed subglottic area.\n- Tracheostomy is rarely necessary unless injury or trauma to the airway has caused scarring and documented narrowing of the airway.\n- Further treatment\n- Once the airway is stabilized, obtain intravenous access for initiation of antibiotics.\n- Antibiotic regimens have traditionally included a third-generation cephalosporin (eg, cefotaxime, ceftriaxone) and a penicillinase- resistant penicillin (eg, oxacillin, nafcillin). More recently, clindamycin (40 mg/kg/d intravenously [IV], divided every 8 h) has been used instead of penicillinase-resistant penicillin against community\n\nacquired—methicillin-resistant S aureus(CA-MRSA) in places where resistance rates of CA-MRSA to clindamycin is Iow.\n- Vancomycin (45 mg/kg/d 'V, divided every 8 h), with or without clindamycin, should be started in patients who appear toxic or have multiorgan involvement or if MRSA is prevalent in the community.\n- the radiograph show subglottic or tracheal narrowing (steeple sign).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nAn 8-month-old infant brought into the ED by his mom with apnea attack while feeding. It was lasted less than 15 seconds. He appears normal now. Which of the following is the sign of BRUE/ALTE?\n- **A)** HR 160 bpm\n- **B)** Born less than 32 weeks   C First even   D, Vomiting\n- **E)** Absent respiration Explanation\n-\n- BRUE/ALTE: Is an episode in an age characterized by: minute duration (typically 20-30s)\n-\n- A return to baseline state Not explained by any identifiable medical condition (i.e. medically well).\n- Includes one or more of the following features:\n- Central cyanosis/pallor\n-\n- Absent, decrease or irregular breathing Marked change in tone (hyper- or hypotonia)\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 75-year-old male with Parkinson disease is brought to the hospital with pain in abdomen. He is not eating properly; His abdomen is swelled and tender with decreased bowel sounds. His abdominal x-ray is shown. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Incarcerated hernia\n- **B)** Viscus perforation   C Sigmoid volvulus\n- **D)** Small bowel ischemia\n- **E)** Gastric outlet obstruction\n- Explanation\n- Plain abdominal X-ray. Image depicting the sigmoid volvulus indicating the typical coffee bean sign.\n- Note — X-rays can be different in the exam. For more details check the acute medicine — gastroenterology module.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 45-year-old male patient arrived with a complaint of severe chest pain after she fell on the side of the table. She has left side multiple rib fractures and developed subcutaneous emphysema. After IV morphine, he is pain-free now. What is the recommended management of the flail chest?\n- **A)** Chest drain towards the side of injury\n- **B)** Needle aspiration\n- **C)** Intercostal nerve block\n- **D)** Chest drain away from site of injury\n- **E)** Ice\n- Explanation\n- As lung contusions are usually_present with a flail chest the initial management consists of:\n- high-flow, warmed, humidified oxygen\n- adequate but cautious fluid resuscitation to prevent overload/pulmonary oedema\n- analgesia, usually in the form of intravenous opioids, to allow maximal thoracic cage movement with breathing.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Intercostal blocks, epidural analgesia and opioid/ ketamine infusions or patient—controlled analgesia should be considered later during the secondary survey, depending on the expertise available. Some patients will require tracheal intubation and controlled ventilation . In these cases be aware that flail chests can co-exist with an underlying pneumothorax so a chest drain should be inserted if a pneumothorax is present or suspected. Indications for tracheal intubation and ventilation in cases of flail chest Falling Pa02 or Pa02 < 7kPa (55mmHg) breathing air Pa02<10kPa (75mmHg) on high-flow oxygen Increasing PaC02 or >6kPa (45mmHg) Exhaustion (respiratory rate <8 breaths/min Respiratory rate >30 breaths/min\n- Associated injuries compromising ventilation Complications - ARDS\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old male patient was brought into the ED, appearing to have suffered a penetrating chest trauma from a stab wound. The patient is unresponsive and has been in cardiac arrest for the past 15 minutes. Paramedics have been performing CPR, but it has been ineffective. What is the next step of management?\n\nA Clamshell thoracotomy\n- **B)** Bilateral thoracostomies\n- **C)** Needle decompression\n- **D)** Continue CPR no intervention required   E\n- Finger thoracostomy\n- Explanation\n- To follow the algorithm, the correct answer would be bilateral thoracostomies.\n- Resuscitative thoracotomy is indicated in less than 15 minutes. However, the patient is in cardiac arrest for the past fifteen minutes.\n- Emergency Department thoracotomy_\n- A resuscitative thoracotomy (i.e. one performed as an integral part of the primary survey) is potentially indicated in three groups of patients:\n- Cardiac arrest after penetrating trauma and less than 15 minutes of\n- Cardiac arrest after blunt trauma and less than IO minutes of CPR\n- Refractory hypotension (systolic pressure <60mmHg or signs of life) despite vigorous fluid resuscitation following penetrating or blunt thoracic injury.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nTrauma Patient in Arrest/ Peri-Arrest Hypoxaemia Hypovolaemia Tension pneumothorax Tamponade Pre-hospita l: I n-hospital: Non-traumatic arrest likely ? Address reversible causes simultaneously:\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nControl external catastrophic haemorrhage\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nSecure airway and maximise oxygenation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nBilateral chest decompression\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nRelieve tamponade (penetrating chest injury) 5. proximal vascular control aortic compression)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nPelvic splint\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nBlood products / Massive Haemorrhage Protocol ROSC immediate transport to appropriate hospital damage control surgery / resuscitation Consider termination Of resuscitation ALS Equipment? Environment ? Elapsed time since of vital Resuscitative Thoracotomy\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nAn 18-year-old girl presents to the emergency department (ED) following unprotected sex with her boyfriend. She is currently taking phenytoin for a\n\nmedical condition. She is concerned about the possibility of pregnancy and requests oral contraception. What would be your next action?\n- **A)** Inform police\n- **B)** Inform social services\n- **C)** Advise IUD\n- **D)** Prescribe OCP\n- **E)** Inform her mother\n- Explanation\n- Nice guideline recommends: If the woman is taking liver enzyme- inducing drugs or is within 28 days of stopping aliver enzyme-inducing drug(such as rifampicin and carbamazepine, phenytoin) - The copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) is the preferred option. Emergency contraception\n- Emergency contraception (EC) is an intervention aimed at preventing unintended pregnancy after unprotected sexual intercourse (UPSI) or contraceptive failure.\n\n- EC should be considered if a woman does not wish to conceive and has had UPSI:\n- On any day ofa natural menstrual cycle.\n- After regular hormonal contraception has been compromised or used incorrectly.\n- From day 21 after childbirth, unless all the lactational amenorrhea method (LAM) criteria are met.\n- From day 5 after miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, or uterine evacuation for gestational trophoblastic disease.\n- Three methods of EC are currently available in the UK:\n- the copper intrauterine device (Cu-IUD)\n- oral ulipristal acetate 30 mg (single dose) tablet\n- oral levonorgestrel 1.5 mg (single dose) tablet.\n- The Cu-IUD - inhibits fertilization by its toxic effect on sperm and ova. If fertilization does occur, the Cu-IlJD has an anti-implantation effect.\n- Ulipristal acetate - acts by delaying ovulation for at least 5 days, until sperm from the UPSI for which it was taken are no longer viable. It delays ovulation even after the start of the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge.\n- Levonorgestrel - acts by inhibiting ovulation. If taken prior to the start of the LH surge, levonorgestrel inhibits ovulation for the next 5 days, until sperm from the UPSI for which it was taken are no longer viable. In the late follicular phase, however, levonorgestrel becomes ineffective (unlike ulipristal acetate).\n\n- The Cu-IUD is the most effective method of EC and should be offered to all women requiring EC, provided the criteria for insertion are met and the method is acceptable to the woman.\n- The Cu-IUD can be inserted for EC within 5 days after the first UPSI in a cycle or within 5 days of the earliest estimated date of ovulation. If the Cu-IlJD is unsuitable or not acceptable to the woman, oral EC should be considered\n- Three methods of emergency contraception (EC) are currently available in the I-JK:\n- The copper intrauterine device (Cu-IUD)— can be inserted for EC within 5 days (120 hours) after the first unprotected sexual intercourse (UPSI) in a cycle or within 5 days of the earliest estimated date of ovulation, whichever is later.\n- Oral ulipristal acetate 30 mg tablet — licensed to be used within 5 days (120 hours) after UPSI or contraceptive failure.\n- Oral levonorgestrel 1.5 mg tablet— licensed to be used within 72 hours after UPSI or contraceptive failure. It can also be used between 72—96 hours after UPSI or contraceptive failure (off-label use), but efficacy decreases with time. r ri k consensual sex,_particularly if the woman is considered to be vulnerable (that is, younger than 16 years of age; is from a disadvantaged background; is in, or is leaving, care; has Iow educational\n\nattainment).The legal age of consent to sexual activity is 16 years in the\n- Sexual activity under the age of consent is an offence, even if consensual.\n- Offences are considered more serious (statutory rape) when the person is younger than 13 years of age.\n- Where required follow Fraser criteria\n- Examples of indications for emergency_ contraception following_potential failure of hormonal and intrauterine methods of contraception are as follows:\n\nCombined hormonal contraception (CHO Combined hormonal transdermal patch or combined hormonal vaginal Combined oral contraceptive (COC) pill (monophasic pill containing ethinvlestradiol) progestogen-only pill (POP) Late or missed pill (more than 27 hours since last traditional POP or more than 36 hours since last desogestrel-onlv Failure to use additional contraceptive precautions whilst using liver enzyme-inducing drugs or in the 28 days after use: EC is indicated if there has been UPSI or barrier failure during, or in the 28 days following, use Of liver enzyme-inducing drugs. Offer a copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD), which is unaffected by liver enzyme-inducing drugs or a double dose (3 mg) of levonorgestrel. Ulipristal acetate is not recommended in this situation.\n- Patch detachment/ring removal for more than 48 hours, or extension Of patch-free or ring-free interval by more than 48 hours: EC is indicated if patch detachment occurs in week 1 and there has been UPSI or barrier failure during the hormone-free interval (HFI) or week 1.\n- If the HFI is extended, a Cu-lUD can be offered up to 13 days after the start of the HFI assuming previous perfect use.\n- If CHC has been used in the 7 days prior to EC, the effectiveness of ulipristal acetate could theoretically be reduced. Consider use of levonorgestrel. Missed pills (if two or more active pills are missed): EC is indicated if the pills are missed in week 1 and there has been UPSI or barrier failure during the pill-free interval or in week\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 1:\nIf the pill-free interval is extended (this includes missing pills in week 1), a Cu-lUD can be offered up to 13 days after the start of the HFI assuming previous perfect use. If COC has been used in the 7 days prior to EC, the effectiveness of ulipristal acetate could theoretically be reduced. Consider use of levonorgestrel.\n- Failure to use additional contraceptive precautions whilst using liver enzyme-inducing drugs or in the 28 days after use:EC is indicated if there has been UPSI or barrier failure during, or in the 28 days following, use of liver enzyme-inducing drugs.\n- Offer a Cu-lUD, which is unaffected by liver enzyme-inducing drugs or a double dose (3 mg) of levonorgestrel. EC is indicated if a pill is late or missed and there has been UPSI or barrier failure before efficacy has been re-established (that is, 48 hours after restarting).\n- The timing of ovulation after missed pills cannot be accurately predicted. A Cu-lUD is therefore only recommended up to 5 days after the first UPSI following a missed pop.\n- If POP has been taken in the 7 days prior to EC, the effectiveness of ulipristal acetate could theoretically be reduced. Consider using levonorgestrel.\n- Late injection (more than 14 weeks since the last injection of DMPA):EC is indicated if there has been UPSI or barrier failure more than 14 weeks after the last injection, and within the first 7 days after late injection.\n\n- Progestogen-only iniecta ble (depot medroxyprogesterone tate DMPA Progestogen-only implant (Nexplanon\n- ) Copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) The timing of ovulation after expiry of the progestogen-only injectable is extremely variable.\n- A Cu-lUD is only recommended up to 5 days after the first UPSI that takes place more than 14 weeks after the last DMPA injection. The effectiveness of ulipristal acetate could theoretically be reduced by residual circulating progestogen. Consider using levonorgestrel. Failure to use additional contraceptive precautions whilst using liver enzyme-inducing drugs or in the 28 days after use: EC is indicated if there has been UPSI or barrier failure during, or in the 28 days following, use of liver enzyme-inducing drugs. Offer a copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD), which is unaffected by liver enzyme-inducing drugs or a double dose (3 mg) of levonorgestrel. Ulipristal acetate is not recommended in this situation. Expired implant:\n- The risk Of pregnancy in the fourth year Of use Of the progestogen-only implant is extremely low. EC is unlikely to be required but may be considered.\n- Removal without immediate replacement; partial or complete expulsion; threads missing and Cu-lUD location unknown: EC is indicated if UPSI has taken place in the 7 days prior to removal, perforation, or partial or complete expulsion. Oral EC is indicated if there has been UPSI in the last 5 days. Depending on the timing Of UPSI and time since IUD known to be correctly placed, it may be appropriate to fit another Cu-lUD for EC.\n- Removal without immediate replacement; partial or complete expulsion; threads missing and LNG-IUS location unknown: EC is indicated if UPSI has taken place in the 7 days prior to removal, perforation, or partial or complete expulsion.\n- Oral EC is indicated if there has been UPSI in the last 5 days. Depending on the timing of UPSI and time since IUD known to be correctly placed, it may be appropriate to fit another ING-IUS for EC. Expired IUS:\n- The risk of pregnancy in the sixth year of use of the LNG-IUS (Mirena\n- ) is extremely low. EC is unlikely to be required but may be considered. Liver enzyme:inducingßrugs\n\nAntibiotics Antiepileptics Antiretrovirals Others Liver enzyme-inducing drugs Rifampicin (potent inducer), Rifabutin. Carbamazepine, Eslicarbazepine, Oxcarbazepine, Phenytoin, Phenobarbital, Primidone, Rufinamide, Topiramate (weak inducer) Protease inhibitors: ritonavir, atazanavir, darunavir, fosamprenavir, lopinavir, nelfinavir, saquinavir, and tipranavir. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors: efavirenz, nevirapine. Bosentan, Modafinil, Aprepitant, St John's Wort.\n\n- Following the initial assessment, the choice of EC should be made by the woman taking additional factors into consideration, such as:\n- Time since UPSI — within 120 hours since UPSI or 120 hours or more since UPSI (or time is unknown).\n- Current medications that may interact with oral EC (such as a liver enzyme-inducing drug).\n- Whether the woman is postpartum or breastfeeding.\n- The woman's body mass index/weight.\n- Contraindications/restrictions to the use of the different methods of EC\n- Emergency contraception within 120 hours since unprotected sexual intercourse\n- If it is currently within 120 hours since the last episode of unprotected sexual intercourse (UPSI), ask whether additional UPSI has occurred in the same cycle (more than 120 hours ago): If additional UPSI has not occurred in the same cycle OR has occurred in the same cycle or the woman is unsure and it is currently. 5 days or less after the earliest likely date of ovulation (estimated as the date of the start of the last menstrual period [LMP] plus the number of days in the shortest cycle minus 14):\n- Offer the copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD).\n- If the Cu-IUD is contraindicated or not acceptable to the woman, offer oral emergency contraception (EC) and suitable ongoing contraception.\n\n- If additional UPSI has occurred in the same cycle or the woman is unsure and it is currently more-than-Sdays-aftecthe earliest likely date of ovulation or the woman is unsure:\n- Offer oral EC and suitable ongoing contraception.\n- Consider pregnancy testing if the earlier UPSI occurred more than 21 days ago and the woman has not had a normal menstrual period since then (that is, UPSI occurred in the same cycle).\n- Choice of oral EC\n- If the last UPSI occurred between 96—120 hours ago or the woman is unsure:\n- Offer ulipristal acetate plus regular contraception (to be started after 5 days of taking ulipristal acetate).\n- Levonorgestrel is unlikely to be effective.\n- Reconsider the Cu-IUD if all UPSI occurred within 120 hours, or if it is currently within 5 days after the likely date of ovulation.\n- If the last episode of UPSI occurred less than 96 hours ago, work out the earliest likely date of ovulation(estimated as the date of the start of the LMP plus the number of days in the shortest cycle minus 14):\n- If UPSI is likely to have taken place during the 5 days before the earliest likely date of ovulation or the woman is unsure:\n- Offer ulipristal acetate plus regular contraception (to be started after 5 days of taking ulipristal acetate).\n- If ulipristal acetate is not suitable, offer levonorgestrel plus immediate quick start of suitable hormonal contraception.\n\n- Reconsider the Cu-lUD if UPSI is within 120 hours, or if it is currently within 5 days after likely ovulation.\n- If UPSI is unlikely to have taken place during the 5 days before the earliest likely date of ovulation, consider the woman's weight and body mass index (BMI):\n- If the woman's BMI is less than 26 kg/m20r body weight is less than 70 kg,offer levonorgestrel plus immediate quick start of suitable hormonal contraception, or ulipristal acetate plus regular contraception (to be started after 5 days of taking ulipristal acetate).\n- If the woman's BMI is more than 26 kg/m20r body weight is more than 70 kg, offer ulipristal acetate plus regular contraception (to be started after 5 days of taking ulipristal acetate), or double dose (3 mg) levonorgestrel plus immediate quick start of suitable hormonal contraception.\n- Note that:\n- There is no evidence that oral EC is effective if ovulation has already occurred.\n- Ulipristal acetate is not suitable for use by women who have severe asthma controlled by oral glucocorticoids.\n- Emergency contraception 120 hours or more since unprotected sexual intercourse\n- If it is currently hours or more since last unprotected sexual intercourse (UPSI), work out the earliest likely date of\n\novulation (estimated as the date of the start of the last menstrual period [L MP] the number of days in the shortest cycle minus 14):\n- If it is currently 5 days or less after the earliest likely date of ovulation:\n- Offer the copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) and suitable quick-start contraception.\n- Oral emergency contraception (EC) is unlikely to be effective.\n- If it is currently more than 5 days after the earliest likely date of ovulation or the woman is unsure:\n- Offer suitable quick-start contraception.\n- Oral EC is unlikely to be effective.\n- If the woman is unsure how long it has been since UPSI, work out the earliest likely date of ovulation (estimated as the date of the start of the LMP plus the number of days in the shortest cycle minus14):\n- If it is currently 5 days or less after the earliest likely date of ovulation:\n- Offer the Cu-IUD.\n- If the Cu-lUD is contraindicated or not acceptable to the woman, offer oral EC and suitable ongoing contraception. Note that oral EC is unlikely to be effective if taken after ovulation.\n- If the woman needs to be referred on for the Cu-lUD to be fitted, offer oral EC at the time of referral in case the Cu-lUD cannot be inserted or the woman changes her mind.\n- If it is currently more than 5 days after the earliest likely date of ovulation or the woman is unsure:\n\n- Offer oral EC and suitable ongoing contraception. Note that oral EC is unlikely to be effective if taken after ovulation.\n- Choice of oral EC\n- If it is currently 120 hours or more since the last UPSI:\n- Oral EC is unlikely to be effective.\n- Consider the immediate quick start of suitable hormonal contraception only.\n- Reconsider the Cu-IUD if it is currently within 5 days after likely ovulation.\n- Emergency contraception to women taking certain medications\n- If the woman is taking liver enzyme-inducing drugs or is within 28 days of stopping a liver enzyme-inducing drug(such as rifampicin and carbamazepine, phenytoin):\n- The copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) is the preferred option.\n- If the Cu-IlJD is contraindicated or not acceptable to the woman, offer double dose (3 mg) levonorgestrel to be taken as a single dose as soon as possible and within 72 hours of unprotected sexual intercourse (UPSI). Explain to the woman that this recommendation is outside the product licence and is based on expert clinical judgement.\n- Ulipristal acetate is not recommended.\n\n- If the woman has recently taken a product containing progestogen or progesterone(whether for contraceptive purposes, EC, gynaecological indications, or hormone replacement therapy [HRT]):\n- Be aware that the effectiveness of ulipristal acetate:\n- Could be reduced if the progestogen was taken in the 5 days after taking ulipristal acetate.\n- Could theoretically be reduced if the progestogen was taken in the 7 days prior to taking ulipristal acetate.\n- It is unknown whether ulipristal acetate taken when there may still be circulating progestogen is more or less effective than levonorgestrel.\n- Emergency contraception for postpartum and breastfeeding women\n- In postpartum and breastfeeding women:\n- The copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) is suitable from 4 weeks or more postpartum.\n- If the Cu-lUD is contraindicated or not acceptable to the woman, consider oral emergency contraception (EC). The choice of EC will depend on factors such as:\n- Time since unprotected sexual intercourse (UPSI) — within 120 hours since UPSI or 120 hours or more since UPSI (or time is unknown).\n- Current medications that may interact with oral EC (such as a liver enzyme-inducing drug).\n- The woman's body mass index/weight.\n- Contraindications/restrictions— ulipristal acetate is not suitable for use by women who have severe asthma controlled by oral glucocorticoids.\n\nIn breastfeeding women:\n-\n- If levonorgestrel is prescribed, advise the woman that a small amount is excreted into breast milk. To minimize exposure to the infant, she should take levonorgestrel immediately after breastfeeding and avoid nursing for at least 8 hours following. If ulipristal acetate is prescribed, advise the woman not to breastfeed\n- and to express and discard milk for a week after she has taken ulipristal acetate.\n- Possible risks and adverse effects of the different types of emergency contraception copper intrauterine device (Cu-lUD) include:\n- Pain on insertion— this may cause discomfort for a few hours but can\n- be relieved with analgesics. Pelvic pain.\n- Perforation of the wall of the uterus\n- Expulsion — the risk of expulsion is around I in 20 and is more\n- common in the first year of use, especially within the first 3 months of insertion. Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)— there is a low risk of PID (less than\n- 1% of users). The risk of pelvic infection is greatest in the first 3 weeks following IUC insertion. Ectopic pregnancy— the overall risk of ectopic pregnancy when using\n- an IUC is very Iow, at about 0.5 per 100 users.\n\nUnscheduled bleeding— irregular, prolonged, or frequent bleeding\n- may occur in the 3—6 months following Cu-IUD insertion, but menstrual bleeding patterns tend to improve with time. Malpositioning— IUC malposition is estimated affect 7-19% of users. It\n- is unclear if malposition affects efficacy of the IUC.\n- Thread problems — issues with threads affect 1.4-18% of users. ulipristal acetate:\n- Vomiting is common with ulipristal acetate.\n- If the woman vomits up to 3 hours after taking ulipristal acetate, she\n- should take a second dose as soon as possible. Menstrual irregularities are also common.\n- Ectopic pregnancy can occur if the woman becomes pregnant after\n- ulipristal acetate; however, the risk is very small. Other possible adverse effects of ulipristal acetate include:\n- Common — mood disorders, headache, dizziness, nausea, abdominal\n- pain/discomfort, fatigue, back pain, pelvic pain, dysmenorrhoea, breast tenderness, and myalgia. Uncommon — anxiety, appetite disorder, chills, concentration impaired,\n- diarrhoea, drowsiness, dry mouth, fever, flatulence, hot flush, increased risk of infection, insomnia, libido disorder, malaise, skin reactions, vision disorders, and vulvovaginal disorders Levonorgestrel:\n- Vomitingis common with levonorgestrel.\n-\n\n- If the woman vomits up to 3 hours after taking levonorgestrel, she should take a second dose as soon as possible. Menstrual irregularities are also common.\n- Ectopic pregnancy can occur if the woman becomes pregnant after\n- levonorgestrel Other possible adverse effects of levonorgestrel include:\n- Headache, Lower abdominal pain, Fatigue, Dizziness, Diarrhoea, Breast\n- tenderness.\n- The Cu-lUD is a safe option for many women. However: It is contraindicated in the following circumstances: Post-abortion and postpartum sepsis.\n- Unexplained vaginal bleeding (suspicious for serious condition) before\n- evaluation (initiation only). Gestational trophoblastic disease (CTD): persistently elevated human\n- chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) levels or malignant disease. Cervical cancer: awaiting treatment (initiation only).\n- Endometrial cancer (initiation only).\n- Current symptomatic chlamydial infection (initiation only).\n- Current purulent cervicitis or gonorrhoea (initiation only).\n- Pelvic tuberculosis (initiation only).\n- Current pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) (initiation only).\n- Note - If a woman has known symptomatic Chlamydia trachomatis\n- infection or current Neisseria gonorrhoea infection, antibiotic treatment should be completed prior to insertion of a Cu-lUD.\n\n- If a woman has asymptomatic\n- **C)** trachomatis infection, insertion of a Cu-lUD for EC may be considered after discussion with the woman regarding risk and benefit. Treatrnent with appropriate antibiotics should be given at the time of insertion (or sooner if possible). previous\n	\N	[]	\N	f	49	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
ce8e4394-ac0f-4bd1-9a67-7b6ce833e643	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-49	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 49	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 49\n\n- **A)** Transamine\n- **B)** eFast Scan\n- **C)** Continuous observation in the ED\n- **D)** CT abdornen\n- **E)** More IVfluid\n- Explanation\n- There's a possibility that this question is incomplete or possible noted incorrectly\n- The patient is hemodynamically unstable which is an indication of laparotomy. Based on the above choices IV fluid would be correct.\n- Transamine (tranexamic acid) recommendation in traurna is <3 hours\n- efast is recommended incase haemounstable in blunt trauma\n- ATLS recommendation: The initial resuscitation with crystalloid fluid still begins with a I-liter bolus of warmed isotonic fluid. Large-volume fluid resuscitation is not a substitute for prompt control of hemorrhage. Infusion of more than 1.5 liters of crystalloid fluid has been associated with increased mortality.\n- The Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS) guidelines define shock as a state of inadequate tissue perfusion that results in impaired\n\ncellular metabolism and organ dysfunction. The signs of shock include: Hypotension (systolic blood pressure < 90 mmHg or mean arterial pressure < 60 mmHg) Tachycardia (heart rate > 100 beats per minute)\n- Tachypnea (respiratory rate > 20 breaths per minute)\n- Cool, clammy skin\n- Decreased urine output\n-\n- Altered mental status In addition to these signs, the AT LS guidelines recommend that\n- healthcare professionals use clinical judgment to identify other signs of shock, such as decreased capillary refill time, delayed or weak peripheral pulses, and decreased skin turgor.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 45-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department after He was involved in a motor vehicle accident where his lower leg was crushed between the car door and another object. X-ray shows a fracture of the distal fibula and the patient is experiencing pain, swelling, and difficulty bearing weight on the affected leg. Which one of the following structures is most likely damaged?\n- **A)** Posterior tibial artery\n- **B)** Sural nerve\n- **C)** Saphenous nerve\n\nD\n- Peroneus longus tendon\n- **E)** Deep fibular nerve\n- Explanation\n- The following structures can be damaged in fibular fracture:\n- Fracture or injury to the neck fibula the common peroneal/fibular nerve will be damaged.\n- Superficial branch of peroneal/fibular nerve damaged if fracture to shaft or distal fibula\n- peroneus/fibularis longs tendon will be damaged if fracture to distal fibula (features can be pain and swelling).\n- Fibular artery can be damaged in fracture to fibula.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 26-year-old woman who is 32 weeks pregnant presents to the Emergency Department with vaginal bleeding for the last few hours associated with abdominal pain. Her observations are: Heart rate: 112 beats per minute, Blood pressure:  mmHg, Respiratory rate: 22 breaths per minute. On examination, her uterus is tender. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Placenta praevia\n- **B)** Vasa praevia\n- **C)** Cervical ectropium\n- **D)** Pelvic inflammatory disease\n- **E)** Placental abruption\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggest placental abruption. Antepartum hemorrhage\n- Antepartum haemorrhage (APH) is defined as bleeding from or into the genital tract, occurring from 24+0 weeks of pregnancy and prior to the birth of the baby. The most important causes of APH are placenta praevia and placental abruption, although these are not the most common. APH complicates 3—5% of pregnancies and is a leading cause of perinatal and maternal mortality worldwide.\n\nPlacenta previa Placenta praevia occurs when the placenta lies in the lower uterine segment. It can lead to the complete or partial covering of the internal cervical os classical qradinq of placenta praevig\n- Image is given below Risk factors Previous caesarean section\n- Previous termination of pregnancy\n- Multiparity\n- Advanced maternal age (over 40)\n- Multiple pregnancy\n- Smoking Deficient endometrium (e.g. due to manual removal of the placenta)\n- Assisted conception Clinical features Painless vaginal bleeding Light contractions may be present Clinical examination: Typical clinical findings in placenta praevia include:\n- Non-tender uterus Vaginal bleeding\n- Lie and presentation may be abnormal Low lying placenta on 20-week anomaly scan Placental abruption\n- Placental abruption is the complete or partial detachment of the placenta before delivery. Complete abruption accounts for 7% of cases whilst partial abruption is more common, accounting for 93% of cases.\n- Overall placental abruption accounts for around a quarter Of all cases Of APH. It is a large cause Of perinatal mortality accounting for 10-20% of all perinatal deaths.\n- The cause of placental abruption is often unknown. It may occur due to trauma or injury to the abdomen\n- Maternal age greater than 35\n- Multiparity\n- Current pre-eclampsia, HELLP\n- History of hypertension\n- History of previous abruptions\n- History of anti-phospholipid syndrome\n- History of thrombophilia\n- Smoking during pregnancy\n- use of cocaine during pregnancy\n- Trauma Clinical features Abdominal pain (most common) — posterior placental abruptions may present with back pain Vaginal bleeding Uterine contractions Dizziness and/or loss of consciousness Clinical examination: Woody, tense uterus Fetal heart: absent or distressed\n- Investigations\n- Vital signs: respiratory rate, BR oxygen saturations, pulse and temperature Relevant laboratory investigations include:\n- Full blood count, urea and electrolytes and LFTs: helps to exclude hypertensive conditions such as HELLP or pre-eclampsia\n- Clotting profile\n- Kleihauer test\n- Group and save Crossmatch\n- Relevant imaging investigations include:\n- Ultrasound scan: if placental abruption is suspected an ultrasound scan is performed to identify the location of the bleed.\n- Cardiotocography (CTG) is used to monitor the fetus.\n\n- Vital signs: respiratory rate, BP, oxygen saturations, pulse and temperature Relevant laboratory investigations include:\n- Full blood count, urea and electrolytes and LFTs: helps to exclude hypertensive conditions such as HELLP or pre-eclampsia\n- Clotting profile Kleihauer test\n- Group and save\n- Crossmatch Relevant imaging investigations include: Ultrasound scan\n- Cardiotocography (CTG) is used to monitor the fetus. Mana m\n- Placenta praevia is typically identified at the 16-20 week scan. If placenta praevia is identified the mother is rescanned at 32 weeks and if it is still present, again at 36 weeks.\n- If placenta praevia is still present at 36 weeks gestation, delivery via caesarean section is recommended, due to the significant risk Of spontaneous labour and associated haemorrhage. Man\n- Fetus alive - No siqns of fetal distress\n- If fetus under 36 weeks - Observe closely\n- If fetus over 36 weeks - Induce and deliver vaginally\n- Fetus alive - Signs of fetal distress\n- Immediate caesarean section Fetus dead\n- Induce vaginal delivery (unless mother haemodynamically compromised and/or ongoing massive haemorrhage, delivery then expedited by caesarean section to arrest bleeding) lication\n- Complications of placental abruption for the mother include: Major haemorrhage: if suspected, the major haemorrhage protocol should be activated\n- Shock: can result in Sheehan syndrome Compression of uterine muscles prevents good contraction Of muscles during labour\n- Release of thromboplastin from placental haematoma: can lead to disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\n- Post-partum haemorrhage.\n- Complications of placental abruption for ghe fetus include:\n- Placental insufficiency. results in hypoxia and intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) due to lack Of nutrients\n- Premature birth\n- Stillbirth\n\n- Grade I — (minor) the placenta is mainly in the upper part of the womb, but some extends to the lower part.\n- Grade 2 — (marginal) the placenta reaches the cervix, but doesn't cover it.\n- Grade 3 — (major) the placenta partially covers the cervix.\n- Grade 4 — (major) the placenta completely covers the cervix (most serious type of placenta praevia).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 65-year-old woman was brought to the ED complaining of flank pain radiating to her back. She is mildly dizzy. Upon assessment in the ED, her vitals are stable. An USG shows an outer-to-outer abdominal aortic diameter of 3.5 cm, and no fluid leakage is seen. What is your next step in management?\n- **A)** Confirm AAA and refer to vascular surgery\n- **B)** Think other diagnosis O\n- **C)** Send for CT KUB now\n- **D)** Order CT to confirm\n- **E)** Surgical review\n- Explanation\n- aortic ultrasound - The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends bedside aortic ultrasound as first line.\n- Discuss the patient with a regional vascular service if:\n- AAA is confirmed on bedside aortic ultrasound\n- OR\n- Bedside aortic ultrasound is not immediately available or is non- diagnostic but AAA is still suspected.\n- Bedside ultrasound can confirm the presence and size of an AAA, but cannot definitively rule out a ruptured AAA.\n- computed tomography angiography (CTA) - Once the diagnosis of AAA is made, contrast-enhanced CTA is used to assess the extent and anatomy of the disease and to assist with operative planning (open or endovascular).\n- It is the first choice for pre- and intraoperative planning; 0.5 mm slices are desirable and 3D reconstruction is essential for accurate planning.\n- It is useful in diagnosing aortic aneurysms close to the origins of, or proximal to, the renal arteries\n\nContrast-enhanced CTA is the recommended definitive imaging\n- modality for AAA rupture, and is also recommended as first-line imaging, over bedside aortic ultrasound, by some guidelines. Do not delay diagnosis and management of a ruptured AAA while waiting for the results of imaging. CTA should be performed in a haemodynamically stable patient, and\n- preferably after transfer to a regional vascular centre if the clinical diagnosis of ruptured AAA is clear without CTA. It should also be considered for definitive diagnosis of ruptured AAA if a\n- normal-sized aorta is viewed on ultrasound in the presence of severe hypotension. In this scenario, ultrasound does not exclude the diagnosis of ruptured AAA or other abdominal pelvic aneurysm.\n- CTA is also important for follow-up after surgical repair with EVAR Abdominal aortic aneurysm\n- Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is a permanent pathological dilation of the aorta with a diameter >1.5 times the expected anteroposterior (AP) diameter of that segment, given the patient's sex and body size.\n- The most commonly adopted threshold is a diameter of 3 cm or more. More than 90% of aneurysms originate below the renal arteries.\n-\n- Risk factors cigarette smoking\n- family history\n- increased age\n-\n\nmale sex for prevalence and female sex for rupture, and\n- congenital/connective tissue disorders\n- The prevalence of AAA is higher in men than in women, meaning that men account for around 4 to 5 times the overall total number of ruptured AAAs compared with women.\n- Clinical features\n- The patient may present with the following features: palpable pulsatile abdominal mass\n- abdominal, flank, or back pain - Patients with ruptured AAA may\n- present with new abdominal and/or back pain. The classic triad of abdominal and/or back pain, hypotension, and a pulsatile abdominal mass is present in about 50% of patients with a ruptured AAA. Symptoms of ruptured AAA may mimic those of renal colic, especially\n- in older patients\n- hypotension loss of consciousness\n- pallor\n- abdominal distension\n- Fever\n-\n- Investigations aortic ultrasound - The National Institute for Health and Care\n- Excellence (NICE) recommends bedside aortic ultrasound as first line.\n- Discuss the patient with a regional vascular service if:\n\n- AAA is confirmed on bedside aortic ultrasound\n- OR\n- Bedside aortic ultrasound is not immediately available or is non- diagnostic but AAA is still suspected.\n- Bedside ultrasound can confirm the presence and size of an AAA, but cannot definitively rule out a ruptured AAA\n- A normal-sized aorta viewed on ultrasound in the presence of severe hypotension does not exclude the diagnosis of ruptured AAA or other abdominal pelvic aneurysm. Consider contrast-enhanced CTA for these patients.\n- However, ultrasound confirmation of AAA in a patient with symptoms or signs of rupture supports the diagnosis of a ruptured AAA.\n- computed tomography angiography (CTA) - Once the diagnosis of AAA is made, contrast-enhanced CTA is used to assess the extent and anatomy of disease and to assist with operative planning (open or endovascular).\n- It is the first choice for pre- and intraoperative planning; 0.5 mm slices are desirable and 3D reconstruction is essential for accurate planning.\n- It is useful in diagnosing aortic aneurysms close to the origins of, or proximal to, the renal arteries\n- Contrast-enhanced CTA is the recommended definitive imaging modality for AAA rupture, and is also recommended as first-line imaging, over bedside aortic ultrasound, by some guidelines. Do not delay diagnosis and management of a ruptured AAA while waiting for the results of imaging.\n\n- CTAshould be performed in a haemodynamically stable patient, and preferably after transfer to a regional vascular centre if the clinical diagnosis of ruptured AAA is clear without CTA.\n- It should also be considered for definitive diagnosis of ruptured AAA if a normal-sized aorta is viewed on ultrasound in the presence of severe hypotension. In this scenario, ultrasound does not exclude the diagnosis of ruptured AAA or other abdominal pelvic aneurysm.\n- CTA is also important for follow-up after surgical repair with EVAR\n- magnetic resonance angiography (MRA)/MRI - MRA may be used as an alternative to CTA if a patient requires repeated imaging or has an iodinated contrast allergy because it does not require radiation or injection of iodinated contrast agents.\n- However, contraindications to MRA include claustrophobia and some metal implants, and it is not as widely available as CTA.\n- Other-in-yestigations\n- Cross match & clotting screen - Indicated if ruptured or symptomatic AAA is suspected\n- ESR & CRP elevation - Suggests inflammatory AAA.\n- FBC - May show anaemia if there is haemorrhage due to ruptured AAA\n- Management\n- Management of ruptured AAA\n\n- If the patient has a suspected ruptured AAA, get immediate help from a senior colleague and discuss the patient with a regional vascular serviceto consider transfer for urgent surgical repair if:\n- AAA is confirmed on bedside aortic ultrasound\n- OR\n- Bedside aortic ultrasound is not immediately available, or is non- diagnostic but AAA is still suspected.\n- If the patient has been accepted by a regional vascular service, ensure they leave the referring unit within 30 minutes of the decision to transfer.\n- Specialist input from a regional vascular service should inform the decision to transfer a patient and their suitability for urgent surgical repair.\n- No single symptom, sign, or patient-related risk factor, or patient risk assessment tool (scoring system), should be used to determine whether surgical repair is suitable for a patient.\n- However, certain patients (such as those who are in cardiac arrest and/or have a persistent loss of consciousness) are unlikely to survive surgical repair.\n- If surgery is deemed appropriate, either endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR) or open surgical repair may be performed.\n- EVAR is recommended in most patients with ruptured infrarenal AAA. It provides greater benefit than open surgical repair in most patients, particularly for men over 70 years and women of any age. Benefits of EVAR include lower perioperative mortality, and less time in hospital in general (and critical care in particular). However, open surgical repairmay provide a better risk/benefit balance for men aged under 70 years.\n\nLocal anaesthesia should be used, if tolerated, for a patient undergoing\n- EVAR Resuscitation measures\n- Initiate immediate resuscitation measures while waiting for transfer to\n- a regional vascular service. These include: Supplemental oxygen\n- Intravenous access\n- Arterial catheter; urinary catheter\n- Hypotensive resuscitation (permissive hypotension) if the patient is\n- conscious. The European Society for Vascular Surgery recommends aiming for a\n- target systolic blood pressure (SBP) of 70 to 90 mmHg. In practice, this target SBP is a good approach if definitive surgical repair is imminent. However, the Royal College of Emergency Medicine recommends a\n- higher target SBP of 90 to 120 mmHg, which may be used by the emergency department team to ensure adequate perfusion while waiting for input from a regional vascular service. If available, use blood and blood products with a suggested ratio of\n- fresh frozen plasma/red blood cells close to\n- perioperative antibiotic therapy - Give the patient prophylactic antibiotics if they are undergoing EVAR or open surgical repair to cover gram-positive and gram-negative organisms (i.e., Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus epidermidis, and enteric gram-negative bacilli) and prevent graft infection Analgesia - Ensure the patient has adequate pain relief\n-\n\nVTE prophylaxis - Give venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis (enoxaparin) to all patients undergoing surgical repair of AAA\n- first-line—surgical repair\n- Urgently discuss any patient with a symptomatic, unruptured AAA with a regional vascular service.\n- The development of new or worsening pain may herald aneurysm expansion and impending rupture.\n- Urgent surgical repair is indicated regardless of AAA diameter\n- perioperative antibiotic therapy - Give the patient prophylactic antibiotics if they are undergoing EVAR or open surgical repair to cover gram-positive and gram-negative organisms (i.e., Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus epidermidis, and enteric gram-negative bacilli) and prevent graft infection\n- Analgesia - Ensure the patient has adequate pain relief\n- VTE prophylaxis - Give venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis (enoxaparin) to all patients undergoing surgical repair of AAA\n- Management of incidental finding of asymptomatic AAA: <5.5 cm(but-not240-cm-and-rapidJy-growjng)\n- 1ST LINE — surveillance (close observation):\n- However, repair may be considered if the AAA measures >4 cm and is growing rapidly (>1 cm in 1 year).\n- In some countries, repair may be considered for women if their AAA measures 25 cm.\n\nOrganise referral to a regional vascular service for any patient with a small (<5.5 cm) asymptomatic AAA (that is detected outside of a screening programme). Following diagnosis, ensure they are seen within 12 weeks.\n- The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence recommends the following intervals for surveillance:\n- Annually if the AAA measures 3.0 to 4.4 cm\n- Every 3 months if the AAA measures 4.5 to 5.4 cm\n- Aggressive cardiovascular risk management\n- Refer all patients to a stop smoking service.This is key because smoking is the most important risk factor for development, expansion, and rupture of AAA2\n- All patients with AAA should receive antiplatelet therapy unless contraindicated\n- The European Society of Cardiology recommends that all patients with symptomatic peripheral vascular disease should also be started on lipid lowering agents if Iow density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol >2.5 mmol/L (>97 mg/dL), and antihypertensives if systolic blood pressure >140 mmHg, unless contraindicated.\n- Other considerations include:\n- Strategies targeted at a healthy lifestyle including weight management, diet, nutrition, and exercise.\n- Management of other comorbidities such as diabetes. >4.0 cm and rapidJy_growing)\n- 1ST LINE - elective surgical repair\n\n- In general, surgical repair is indicated for patients with large (25.5 cm) asymptomatic AAA. Surgery may also be considered if the AAA measures >4 cm and is growing rapidly (>1 cm in 1 year). In some countries, repair may be considered for women if their AAA measures 25 cm. Organise referral to a regional vascular service for any patient with an asymptomatic AAA. Following diagnosis, ensure they are seen:\n- Within 2 weeks if the AAA measures 25.5 cm\n- Within 12 weeks if the AAA measures 3.0 to 5.4 cm.\n- Discuss the risks and benefits of elective surgical repair versus conservative management (with ultrasound surveillance) with the patient based on their current and expected future health.\n- For AAA detected as an incidental finding, conservative management is preferred to repair until the theoretical risk of rupture exceeds the estimated risk of operative mortality.\n- Aggressive-cardioyascular-risk-management\n- Refer all patients to a stop smoking service. This is key because smoking is the most important risk factor for development, expansion, and rupture of AAA,\n- All patients with AAA should receive antiplatelet therapy unless contraindicated\n- The European Society of Cardiology recommends that all patients with symptomatic peripheral vascular disease should also be started on lipid lowering agents if Iow density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol >2.5 mmol/L (>97 mg/dL), and antihypertensives if systolic blood pressure >140 mmHg, unless contraindicated.\n\n- Start a statin at least 4 weeks before elective AAA surgery, if possible, to reduce cardiovascular morbidity and mortality; continue indefinitely\n- Other considerations include:\n- Strategies targeted at a healthy lifestyle including weight management, diet, nutrition, and exercise.\n- Management of other comorbidities such as diabetes.\n- perioperative antibiotic therapy - Give the patient prophylactic antibiotics if they are undergoing EVAR or open surgical repair to cover gram-positive and gram-negative organisms (i.e., Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus epidermidis, and enteric gram-negative bacilli) and prevent graft infection\n- Analgesia - Ensure the patient has adequate pain relief\n- VTE prophylaxis - Give venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis (enoxaparin) to all patients undergoing surgical repair of AAA\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 70-year-old elderly male with delirium was presented to ED with agitation. On initial assessment all his baseline reports are normal, after some time in ED he became more agitated. He is currently on furosemide and many other medications. What is the Cause of agitation?\n- **A)** His daily drugs side effects\n- **B)** Occult hip fracture\n- **C)** Postural hypotension\n\nO\n- **D)** UTI\n- **E)** Sub Dural Hematoma All baselines normal most likely cause of agitation and delirium would be drugs side effect in this. Delirium:\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Delirium(sometimes called 'acute confusional state')is an acute, fluctuating syndrome of encephalopathy causing disturbed consciousness, attention, cognition, and perception. Delirium usually develops over hours to days and behavioural disturbance, personality changes, and psychotic features may occur Delirium typically occurs in people with predisposing factors(such as advanced age or multiple comorbidities) when new precipitating factors(such as some medications or infection) are added. Classification Hyperactive delirium— may present with inappropriate behaviour, hallucinations, or agitation. Restlessness and wandering are common: Hypoactive delirium (more common)— may present with lethargy and reduced concentration and appetite. The person may appear quiet or withdrawn Mixed delirium— signs and symptoms of both hyperactive and hypoactive subtypes are present. Clinical features of delirium\n\n- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:\n- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.\n- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.\n- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.\n- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.\n- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).\n- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal).\n- Risk factors\n\nPredisposing factors\n- Older age (over 65 years). Cognitive impairment (such as dementia). Frailty/multiple comorbidities (such as stroke or heart failure). Significant injuries such as hip fracture.\n- Functional impairment (for example immobility or the use of physical restraints such as cot sides).\n- Iatrogenic events (such as bladder catheterization, polypharmacy, or surgery). History of, or current, alcohol excess.\n- Sensory impairment (such as visual impairment or hearing loss). Poor nutrition. Lack of stimulation.\n- Terminal phase of illness\n- Clinical features Precipitating factors\n- Infection - urinary tract infection, infected pressure sore, or pneumonia. Metabolic disturbance - hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia (including advanced carcinomatosis), or electrolyte abnormalities (including dehydration). Cardiovascular disorders- MI or heart failure. Respiratory disorders - pulmonary embolism or exacerbation COPD Neurological disorders - stroke, encephalitis, or subdural haematoma.\n- Endocrine disorders - thyroid dysfunction or Cushing's syndrome. Urological disorders - urinary retention.\n- Gastrointestinal disorders- hepatic failure, constipation or malnutrition.\n- Severe uncontrolled pain. Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal Medications — antihistamine, antipsychotic, antidepressant, antispasmodics, NSAIDs , anti- hypertensive, corticosteroid. Psychosocial factors - depression, sleep deprivation, visual or hearing impairment, emotional stress, or change of environment\n- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:\n- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.\n- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.\n- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.\n\n- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.\n- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).\n- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal).\n- Investigations\n- Delirium is a clinical diagnosis based on a detailed history, examination, and relevant investigations\n- Confirm a diagnosis of deliriumby carrying out a cognitive assessment based on the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) criteria, the short Confusion Assessment Method (short-CAM), or the 4As test\n- Arrange targeted investigations based on findings from the history and examination, for example:\n- Urinalysis— to identify conditions such as infection or hyperglycaemia\n- CXR or sputum culture- For chest infection\n- FBC -for anaemia\n- Management\n\n- Most people with delirium should be admitted for urgent assessment, close monitoring, and treatment.\n- If the person is deemed to not have capacity to consent, decisions should be made in the best interests of the person using the Mental Capacity Act 2005.\n- If the person with delirium develops challenging behaviour (such as\n- Address any underlying causes for the behaviour (such as discomfort, thirst, or need for the toilet).\n- Advise moving the person to a safe, Iow-stimulation environment (such as a quiet room).\n- Advise use of verbal and non-verbal de-escalation techniques (such as active listening, effective verbal responding, pictures, and symbols).\n- If these measures fail, consider pharmacological measure and seek advice from an elderly care psychiatrist, or elderly care physician. Depending on the specific situation (for example the person is a danger to themselves or to others).\n- NICE recommends - Short-term (for 1 week or less) low-dose haloperidol\n- Antipsychotic drugs should be avoided, or used with caution in Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies\n- Managemencprinciples in end-of-life care include:\n- Considering non-pharmacological management of delirium in a person in the last days of life.\n\n- Treating any reversible causes of delirium, for example psychological causes or certain metabolic disorders (for example renal failure or hyponatraemia).\n- Considering a trial of an antipsychotic medicine (such as haloperidol or levomepromazine) to manage delirium, in the presence of intractable distress.\n- Monitoring the benefits and side effects of these medicines at least daily and adjust the prescription as the clinical situation changes.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 17-year-old male presents to the emergency department after being knocked unconscious during a boxing match. He was seen to have a brief loss of consciousness (<1 minute) and was confused upon waking. A CT scan of the head was performed and is reported as normal. He is now back to his baseline with no headache or other symptoms. He is keen to play in an important match in 2 weeks. What is the most appropriate advice to give his parents?\n- **A)** He should stay away from all sports competitions for 21 days\n- **B)** He should present to the hospital if he experiences a headache during any activity\n- **C)** He can return to non-contact training imrnediately and full contact training after 1 week\n- **D)** If he experiences any headache with activity, he should not return to full training for at least 2 weeks\n- **E)** He can return to playing immediately as his CT scan is normal and he has returned to his baseline\n- Explanation\n- Current concussion management guidelines recommend a gradual return to play protocol following a concussion, even if symptoms have resolved and neuroimaging is normal. This typically involves a stepwise progression of activity with increasing physical and cognitive demands.\n- Risk of Second Impact Syndrome: Returning to play too soon after a concussion significantly increases the risk of Second Impact Syndrome, a rare but potentially fatal condition where a second concussion occurs before the brain has fully recovered from the first.\n- Concussion\n- Concussion is a traumatic brain injury resulting in a disturbance of brain function. It affects the way a person thinks, feels and remembers things.\n- Loss of consciousness (being 'knocked out') occurs in less than 10% of concussions and is not required to diagnose concussion. However,\n\nanyone who loses consciousness because of a head injury has had a concussion.\n- Concussion-can-affectpeop/e-in-four-main-areas Physical e.g. headaches, dizziness, vision changes\n- Mood e.g. short tempered, sad, emotional\n- Mental processing e.g. not thinking clearly, feeling slowed down\n- Sleep e.g. not being able to sleep or sleeping too much\n- Causes of concussion\n- Concussion can be caused by a direct blow to the head but can also\n- occur when knocks to other parts of the body result in rapid movernent of the head (e.g. whiplash type injuries). Concussions can happen at any age. However, children and\n- adolescents may be more susceptible to concussion\n- Initial assessment Red requiring urgent medical assessrnent t from an appropriate\n- Healthcare Professional onsite or in a hospital Accident and Emergency (A&E) Department using emergency ambulance transfer if necessary; Any loss of consciousness because of the injury\n- Deteriorating consciousness (more drowsy)\n- Amnesia (no memory) for events before or after the injury\n- Increasing confusion or irritability\n-\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Unusual behaviour change Any new neurological deficit - e.g. Difficulties with understanding, speaking, reading or writing Decreased sensation Loss of balance Weakness Double vision Seizure/convulsion or limb twitching or lying rigid/ motionless due to muscle spasm Severe or increasing headache Repeated vomiting Severe neck pain Any suspicion of a skull fracture (e.g. cut, bruise, swelling, severe pain at site of injury) Previous history of brain surgery or bleeding disorder Current 'blood-thinning' therapy Current drug or alcohol intoxication Recognition of concussion\n- Onset-of-symptoms The first symptoms of concussion typically appear immediately or within minutes of injury but may be delayed and appear over the first 24-48 hours following a head injury. Over the next several days, additional symptoms may become apparent (e.g. mood changes, sleep disorders, problems with concentration), Remember that the primary aim is to protect the individual from further injury by immediately removing them from play. Return to play should not be permitted until after evaluation by an appropriate\n\nHealthcare Professional and the successful completion of a graduated return to activity (education/work) and sport programme. Visible clues (signs) Of concussion\n- Any oneormore of thefouowingvisiblecluescan indicateagoncussion: Loss of consciousness or responsiveness Lying motionless on ground/slow to get up Unsteady on feetJbalance problems or falling over/ incoordination\n- Dazed, blank or vacant look Slow to respond to questions Confused/not aware ot plays or events Grabbing/clutching of head An impact seizure/convulsion\n- Tonic posturing— lying rigid/ motionless due to muscle spasm (may appear to be unconscious) More emotional/irritable than normal for that person\n- Vomiting Symptoms Of concussion at Or shortly after injury\n- Presenceotanyone ornoreofthe fouowingsigns& symptoms may suggestaconcussion: Disoriented (not aware of their surroundings e.g. opponent, period, score) Headache\n- Dizziness/feelingoff-balance Mental clouding, confusion or feeling slowed down Drowsiness/feeling like 'in a fog'/ difficulty concentrating Visual problems Nausea Fatigue Pressure in head'\n- Sensitivity to light or sound More emotional Don't feel right\n- Concerns expressed by parent, official, spectators about a player\n-\n- Immediate management of a suspected concussion Anyone with a suspected concussion should be immediately removed from play.\n\nAnyone with a suspected concussion should: Be removed from play immediately. Get assessed by an appropriate Healthcare professional onsite or access the NHS by calling 111 within 24 hours of the incident. Rest & sleep as needed for the first 24-48 hours — this is good for recovery. Easy activities Of daily living and walking are also acceptable. Minimise smartphone, screen and computer use for at least the first 48 hours. Limiting screentime has been shown to improve recovery Anyone with a suspected concussion should not: Be left alone in the first 24 hours. Consume alcohol in the first 24 hours and/or if symptoms persist. Drive a motor vehicle within the first 24 hours. Commercial drivers (HGV etc.) should seek review by an appropriate Healthcare professional before driving.\n- Graduated return to activity_(education/work) and sport\n- Generally, a short period of relative rest (first 24-48 hours) followed by a gradual stepwise return to normal life (education, work, Iow level exercise), then subsequently to sport is safe and effective.\n- Progression through the stages below is dependent upon the activity not more than mildly exacerbating symptoms. Medical advice should be sought if symptoms deteriorate or do not improve by 14 days after the injury. Those with symptoms after 28 days should seek medical advice via their CP.\n- Note - Above question relevant to stage 6 (mentioned in table below)- return to sport competition. patient can not return to competition before day 21.\n\n1 Stage 2 Stage 3 Stage 4 stage 5 Stage 6 GRADUATED RETURN TO EDUCATION/WORK & SPORT SUMMARY Relative Rest for 24—48 hours\n- Minimise time\n- Gentle exercise\n- Gradually introduce daily activities\n- Activities away from SChOOI,'WOrk (introduce TV, increase reading, etc)\n-\n- Exercise Hight physical activity (e.g. short walks)\n- Increase tolerance for mental & exercise activities\n- Increase study/work-related activities with rest periods\n-\n- Increase intensity ot exercise* Return to study/work and sport training\n- Part-time return to education/work\n-\n- Start training activities withut risk ot head fipact\n- Return to normal work/education and full training\n- Full work/education\n- at rest for days Consider full training Return to sports competition (NOT before day 21) as long as symptom free at rest for 14 days and during the pre-competition training of Stage 5 *rest until the following day if this activity more than mildly increases symptoms.  7. 28-year-old female presents to the emergency department with a 3-day history of progressive weakness in her legs. She describes difficulty climbing stairs and getting out of a chair. On examination, she has reduced power in both legs, absent knee and ankle reflexes, and mild sensory disturbance in her feet. You suspect Guillain-Barré Syndrome. Which of the following investigations is the most important to manage this patient?\n- **A)** Serum creatine kinase\n- **B)** Arterial blood gas analysis\n- **C)** Chest X-ray\n- **D)** Electrocardiogram   E\n- Forced Vital Capacity\n- Explanation\n- Respiratory muscle weakness is a serious complication of CBS, and monitoring respiratory function is crucial.\n- FVC measures the maximum volume of air a person can forcefully exhale after a full inhalation. It's a sensitive indicator of respiratory muscle strength and can detect early signs of respiratory compromise in CBS, even before changes in blood gases or clinical signs become apparent.\n- 1st investigations to order in CBS\n- The diagnosis of GBS is largely clinical, but the standard investigations are listed below.\n- Nerve conduction studies — revealed prolonged distal and F-wave latencies and reduced conduction velocities, H reflex prolonged or absent.\n- lumbar puncture - elevated CSF protein, normal/slightly high lymphocytes (<50 cells/mm3).\n- A raised CSF protein (>0.4 g/L) with normal white cell count is the typical CSF finding in GBS.\n\n- LFTs — reveal elevated aspartate aminotransferase and alanine aminotransferase as high as 500 U/L; bilirubin may be transiently elevated but rarely high enough to cause jaundice\n- Spirometry/Lung function test - may show reduced vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure. Patients with an FVC less than 20mL/kg are candidates for prophylactic intubation and mechanical ventilation. This test Should be carried out at 6-hour intervals initially at the\n- bedside. Intensive care unit monitoring and elective. Lung function tests are vital for safe management of the patient.\n- Note - The key diagnostic tests for the ED are the lumbar puncture,\n- along with a screen of blood tests. Lung function tests are vital for safe management of the patient. intubation should be considered if any of the following is present:\n- vital capacity <20 mL/kg (odds ratio 15.0)\n- maximal inspiratory pressure worse than -30 cmH20\n- maximal expiratory pressure <40 cmH20; or reduction of 30% or more\n- of vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure.\n- Other investigations to be considered anti-ganglioside antibody\n- Blood tests: FBC, ESA, U+E, LFT, CPK, CAP, glucose, Mg, calcium and\n- phosphate Stool culture (for C jejuni and others)\n- ECC spinal MRI\n-\n\nBorrelia burgdorferi serology\n-\n- Differential\n- Transverse myelitis Myasthenia gravis Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)\n- Botulism\n- Polymyositis\n- Vasculitic neuropathy\n- Management\n- IVIG or PE (plasma exchange)\n- Other treatment\n- Pain relief: try to avoid opioids\n-\n- Chronic neuropathic pain: amitriptyline or antiepileptic drugs DVT prophylaxis Monitoring\n- Lung function initially every four/six hours\n-\n- ECG Autonomic function — pupils and ileus\n-\n- Check for swallowing dysfunction Note — check more detail in Transverse myelitis and GBS table\n-\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 30-year-old male presents to the ED after he fell directly onto his outstretched arm and shoulder. The fall from a height of approximately 6 feet resulted in significant trauma to his right shoulder. X-ray shows posterior shoulder dislocation. Which one of the following is the feature of posterior dislocation of the shoulder?\n- **A)** Marked gap in posterior on palpation\n- **B)** squared off appearance of shoulder\n- **C)** Limited internal rotation\n- **D)** Can not touch the other shoulder\n- **E)** Arm held in abduction\n- Explanation\n- A marked gap in posterior on palpation is typically associated with a posterior shoulder dislocation. In a posterior shoulder dislocation, the humeral head (the upper arm bone) is displaced to the posterior (back) of the shoulder joint. This displacement creates a noticeable gap or space in the posterior aspect of the shoulder when touched or palpated. This is one of the clinical findings that can help healthcare providers diagnose a posterior shoulder dislocation.\n\n- General-symptoms-of-shoulder-dislocations Severe shoulder pain\n- Inability to move the shoulder\n- Empty glenoid fossa: A palpable dent may be present at the point\n- where the head of the humerus is supposed to lie. Anterioror anterior-inferior dislocation\n- The humeral head can usually be palpated below the coracoid process.\n-\n- The arm is typically held in external rotation and slight abduction. Posterior dislocation\n- Prominence of the posterior shoulder with anterior flattening\n- Prominent coracoid process\n-\n- The arm is held in adduction and internal rotation, with the patient unable to actively rotate it in the outward direction. Inferior dislocation\n-\n- The arm is held above the head, with the patient unable to actively adduct the arm. Neurologic dysfunction, especially with involvement of the axillary\n- nerve, is common.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old patient was referred from his general practitioner (GP) to the Accident & Emergency (A&E) department. The GP has sent an email to the A&E doctor regarding the patient's condition. The patient is now requesting a copy of that email. What would be your next action?\n- **A)** Give him print after counselling him\n- **B)** Give him copy of that email\n- **C)** Politely refuse and do not give him the copy\n- **D)** Ask your senior consultant\n- **E)** Refer him back to GP\n- Explanation\n- If a patient requests a copy of their medical records, including an email sent by their GP to the A&E doctor, you should provide them with a copy of the email. However, you may need to veriß.' the patient's identity and ensure that the request is legitimate before providing them with a copy of the email,\n- The Data Protection Act 2018 is a UK law that controls how the personal data of UK data subjects is used by organizations, businesses, and government institutions. It transposes the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), an EU Regulation, into I-JK law. The act has adopted the 7 principles of the GDPR enshrined in Article\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nThe seventh principle of the GDPR is the right of access, which entitles a person to access personal data held by a data user and also correct it in order to update its data.\n- The seventh principJe_of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) is the Right of Access. It states that individuals have the right to access their personal data held by an organization and can correct it\n\nin order to update their data. The Data Protection Act 2018 (DPA 2018) has adopted these principles and transposes the GDPR into UK law. The DPA 2018 requires organizations to be more transparent and accountable for their personal data processing activities. It also emphasizes the rights of data subjects regarding their personal data, including the right of access, to be informed, to rectification, data portability, process restriction, and objection.\n- In simple words, this principle gives individuals the right to know what personal information an organization holds about them and how it is being used. They can also request a copy of their personal data and ask for it to be corrected if it is inaccurate.  IO. A 62-year-old woman was brought into the ED with a history of AAA complaining of lion pain radiating to the groin and there is a trace of blood in the urine dipstick. What is the appropriate next step?\n- **A)** Refer to surgery\n- **B)** Urgent CT KUB\n- **C)** Bedside aortic ultrasound\n- **D)** Outpatient CT KUB\n- **E)** CT abdomen with contrast\n- Explanation\n- aortic ultrasound - The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends bedside aortic ultrasound as the first line.\n- Discuss the patient with a regional vascular service if:\n- AAA is confirmed on bedside aortic ultrasound\n- Bedside aortic ultrasound is not immediately available or is non- diagnostic but AAA is still suspected.\n- Bedside ultrasound can confirm the presence and size of an AAA, but cannot definitively rule out a ruptured AAA. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	50	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
d262da57-c7e3-4b8d-958e-306d8ca026c2	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-49	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 49	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 49\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	49	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b76ef806-337e-4aa8-8ad2-2f88f9e35ba6	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-5	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 5	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 5\n\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Asystole x\n- **C)** Complete heart block\n- **D)** Polymorphic VT E\n- Mobitz type-II\n-\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Clinical Explanation & RCEM Guidance:**\n> -  Irregular ventricular response\n- No evidence of organised atrial activity\n- Fine fibrillatory waves seen in VI  1 . A 21 -year-old female brought her IO-month-old son to the ED with severe respiratory distress after eating. On examination, the baby is having weak cry. what is your management plan?\n- **A)** 5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts\n- **B)** Intubation\n- **C)** 5 back blows and abdominal thrust\n- **D)** Start CPR\n- **E)** 5 rescue breaths\n- Explanation Management in Paeds Encourage the child to cough Shout for help Absent or Ineffective cough,\n- Unconscious child. 5 back blows Place the child on the firm/flat If not relieved surface and call for help 5 abdominalthrusts in children. Or 5 chest thrusts in Open airway : lift jaw/ tongue infant. if obstruction relieved then Give 5 rescue breaths urgent medical follow-up. If chest isn't rise then reposition the head and try another attempt. If still not relieved then repeat back blows, Chest compression and CPR abdominal thrusts (children) (BLS) gg chest thrusts (infant)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 25-year-old young female brought into ED after being rescued from house fire. She has deep partial thickness burns covering her anterior trunk and both anterior upper limbs. Burn on upper extremity is complicated with severe pain. What is the appropriate management for her condition?\n- **A)** Refer to surgery\n- **B)** Analgesia and Antibiotics\n- **C)** Analgesia and IV fluids\n- **C)** -  Fasciotomy\n- **E)** Drainage and debridement\n- Explanation\n- Patient has developed compartment syndrome. Therefore, fasciotomy should be done as soon as possible to avoid limb loss (amputation).\n- Compartment syndrome\n- Definition\n\n- Compartment syndrome is a pathological condition characterised by elevated interstitial pressure in a closed osteofascial compartment that results in microvascular compromise (restriction of capillary blood flow).\n- The compartments most commonly involved are those with relatively non-compliant osseous or fascial structures, such as the anterior and deep posterior compartments of the leg and the volar compartment of the forearm.\n- Risk factors\n- Trauma - Extremity fracture, soft-tissue trauma (with or without fracture), reperfusion injury, and/or penetrating traurna can lead to intracompartmental haemorrhage, vascular compromise, and/or muscle oedema with subsequent elevation of intracompartmental interstitial pressure.\n- Bleeding disorder - such as haemophilia\n- of tight casts, dressings, or external wrappings can lead to increased external pressure, which subsequently increases the intracompartmental interstitial pressure. thermal-injury—dn cases Of circumferential burn eschar, there is a high risk of intracompartmental interstitial pressure elevation due to the restricting effect of the eschar on the swollen muscles.\n- Intense muscular activity=Chronic exertional compartment syndrome is rnost frequently encountered among long-distance runners and other sports with intense muscular activity.\n- Extravasation of intravenous infusion - Infused solution that does not get absorbed can lead to elevation of intracompartmental interstitial\n\npressure. venous obstruction - Iliac venous obstruction secondary to DVT,\n- tumour embolus, or extrinsic compression.\n- Clinical features Pain - An early sign, especially with passive stretching of the muscles\n- in the involved compartment. pressure (muscle tightness) - Presence of tightness in the\n- compartment is the earliest objective finding. Paraesthesia - Early sign\n- Pulselessness - Pulse examination may be normal, and unless an\n- artery is injured (late sign) Pallor - caused by vascular compromise (late sign)\n- paralysis - It is caused by prolonged nerve compression and ischaemia,\n- or irreversible muscle damage (late sign) Mneumonic 6Ps\n- Investigations\n- st compartment pressure measurement (1 investigation) - Pressure\n- should be measured in all compartments and at multiple sites, particularly within 5 cm of the level of injury, and the highest rneasurernent obtained should dictate management decisions. For patients with suspected chronic exertional pallor, pressure should\n- be measured after an episode of exertion. If specialised equipment is unavailable, a 16-gauge IV cannula connected to an arterial BP\n\ntransducer and monitor via saline-filled arterial line tubing can be used to measure compartment pressures.\n- The differential pressure (i.e., the difference between diastolic BP and measured compartment pressure: diastolic BP minus ment pressure) is measured.\n- Differential pressure within 20-30 mmHg of the diastolic pressure (delta pressure) is considered a strong indicator for fasciotomy. However, care should be taken when using this criterion for patients who are receiving vasodilatory medications whose diastolic BP is Iow.\n- Other investigations\n- serum creatine kinase\n- Urine myoglobin\n- Management\n\nAcute with occlusive dressing line\n- Casts or occlusive dressings should be split completely.\n- padding or ci dressings should be released immediately.\n- Analgesia (morphine). Fasciotomy— Performed Within a 6-hour window from the initiation of compartment syndrome. line del ayed diagnosis leads to significant muscle necrosis, amputation may need to be considered. If acute with occlusive dressing and with rhabdomyolvsis:\n- Include above measures add hydration therapy urine alkalinization and consider haemodialysis Acute without occlusive dressing line\n- Fasciotomy— Performed within a 6-hour\n- windowfrom the initiation Of compartrnentsyndrome. 2nd line Amputation - If delayed diagnosis leads to significant muscle necrosis, amputation may need to be considered. acute with occlusive dressing and with rhabdom I is:\n- Include abov\n- e measures add hydration therapy urine alkalinization and consider haemodialysis Chronic Exercise limitation and NSAIDs (eg ibuprofen) 2nd line Fasciotomy\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 32-year-old female presents to the ED with a severe generalized headache, vomiting flashing before her eyes and light-headedness. She has no past medical history of note and is 30 weeks pregnant. What is the most important diagnosis to exclude in this patient?   A\n- Pre- eclampsia\n- **B)** Vertebral artery dissection\n- **C)** Subarachnoid haemorrhage\n- **D)** Pituitary apoplexy\n- **E)** Venous sinus thrombosis\n- Explanation\n- Clinical features of pre-eclampsia: Asymptomatic hypertension (pick up or screening) Headache (usually frontal)\n- RI-JQ or epigastric pain Nausea and vomiting\n- Oedema(hand and face)\n-\n- Visual disturbance(scotoma)\n- SOB Hyper-reflexia and/or clonus Initial management of pre-eclampsia\n- Contact obstetrics early\n-\n- Be-management; Labetalol first line unless Contraindication eg: asthma.\n-\n- Nifedipine MR second line Methyldopa third line (not used postpartum due to risk of depression)\n- Consider IV mgs04 for eclampsia prophylaxis\n- Monitor urine output\n-\n\n- Fluid restriction to avoid pulmonary edema\n- Definitive-management-otprezeclampsia-is-u]timately-deljyery-of-the fetus.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 30-week pregnant lady is brought into ED by paramedics with history of RTA. Her observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 120bpm. She is confused and anxious. You are considering emergency caesarean section. What would indicate fetal death?\n- **A)** If the fundus below umbilical this indicates of unviable baby\n- **B)** If the fundus below umbilicus this indicates viable baby\n- **C)** Refer to OBG Specialist for fetus viability check\n- **D)** The palpable part of fetus in abdomen is the indication of operation\n- **E)** Abdominal ultrasound by OBs specialist Pregnancy trauma management\n- Pregnant Trauma Patient primary Survey_\n\nPregnant trauma patient 1>20 weeks gestation) Pri su Aim.ALOxygen, early intubation, difficultairway box for intubation, high aspiration risk, consider cricoid pressure/ smaller ETT, pC02- 27-32mmhg, c/spine protection, insert NG tube Breathing: SatS> 95%, finger thoracostomy/ ICC 1-2 spaces higher Circulation: Left lateral tilt and/or manual uterus displacement, control Obvious external h\n- age, activate MEP, 2k large bore IV access bloods (group & hold, antibody screen & kleihauertest, HCG), FAST exam; est & vis Yes mal Activate trauma Cardiac Arrest guideline Is fetus > 2CiÄ"eéks fingers above the mbili Supportive management fetus as per obstetrics induced/ Obstetric Examination Is the BP < 90 mmhg and Fast Positive? Is the fetus FHR 120 dary survey a trauma pathway consider Optimise oxygenation and mal circu\n\nfor fetus as per obstetrics induced/ sponta neous labour\n- Pregnant-Trauma-Patient-Seco—da- n ry-and-TertiarySuryey-\n\n& tertiary md Spec\n- Aum by obstenician to out spMtarrws rupture Of feta I EStnte Maternal with plan or heart for ot with team Admit to CTG mon fM 24 NO he risk Estimated date Fundal measurenwtt ute rine rupture Feto ma tern* haemorrh. Fetal distress pal Recent anti-o Past medd and maternal history Doppkr Ot (Heard from 14-16 Normal (Average dia (S\n- n Of Fetal movement Fetal Heart Amniotic fluid pre*nt Vaginal bleeding Meconium staining to With "tstru«tms to return for to hospital to' review if is anv Needing, loss Of or ter&mesS\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 50-year-old male known case of diabetic mellitus and HTN came into ED with complaint of chest pain. Whilst examining the patient, He becomes unresponsive. You cannot feel a central pulse and can't see any respiratory efforts. You decided to start CPR. What is the normal CPR rate in a minute?\n- **A)** 100-120\n- **B)** 40-60   c. 80-100 O\n- **D)** 60-80\n- **E)** 150-160\n- Explanation\n- Adult ALS\n- CPR: compression : two rescue breaths)\n- CPR rate: 100-120\n- Depth 5-6cm\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nYou are discussing bleeding in pregnancy with some of junior colleagues. While you're discussing, a female 32-week pregnant brought into the ED with\n\nvaginal bleeding. Her observations are within normal limit, and She is Rh -ve. what is your appropriate step of management?\n- **A)** Start Blood transfusion\n- **B)** Commence Tranexamic acid\n- **C)** Refer to OBG\n- **D)** Immediate delivery\n- **E)** Anti- D\n- Explanation\n\nAnti-D Administration Checklist Always confirm\n- the woman's identity\n- that the woman is RhD Negative using the latest laboratory report\n- that the woman does not have immune anti-D using the latest laboratory repon\n- that informed consent for administration of anti-D lg is recorded in notes Potentially Sensitising Events (PSEs) during pregnancy Gestation LESS than 12 weeks Vaginal bleeding associated with severe pain ERPC / Instrumentation of uterus Medical or surgical termination Of pregnancy Ectopic / Molar Pregnancy Gestation 12 to 20 weeks For any Potentially Sensitising Event (PSE) Gestation 20 weeks to term For any potentially Sensitising Event (PSE) (Irrespective of whether RAADP has been given) Does the Kleihauer / FMH test indicate that further anti-D lg is required ? Administer at least 250 IU anti-D lg within 72 hours of event. Confirm product / dose / expiry and patient ID pre adrninistration Administer at least 250 IU anti-D lg within 72 hours of event. Confirm product / dose / expiry and patient ID pre adnlinistration Request a Kleihauer Test (FMH Test) and immediately administer at least 500 ILI anti-D lg within 72 hours of event. Confirm product / dose / expiry and patient ID pre adrninistration Administer more anti-D lg following discussion with laboratory FCY continucws vaginal bleeding at least 500iu anti-D Ig should be administered at a minimum Ot 6-weekly intervals. irrespective the præence of detectable anti\n- D, and a Kleihauer / FMH Test requested every two weeks in case more anti\n- D is needed Routine Antenatal Anti-D Prophylaxis (RAADP) For Routine Antenatal Anti-D Prophylaxis (Irrespective Of whether anti-D lg already given for PSE) Take a blood sample to confirm group & check antitm,t screen — do not wait for results before administering anti\n- D lg Administer 1500 anti-D lg at 28 - 30 weeks Administer at least 500 IU anti-D lg at 28 weeks and then administer at least 500 IU anti-D at 34 weeks Confirm product / dose / expiry and patient ID pre adrninistration At Delivery (or on diagnosis of Intra Uterine Death >20 weeks) Request a Kleihauer Test (FMH Test) Is the baby's group as RhD positive ? OR Are cord samples not available ? Administer at least 500 IU anti-D lg within 72 hours of delivery Confirm product dose / expiry and patient ID pre administration\n\nanti-D lg is required ? Administer more anti-D following ciscussion with lüratory SHOT 2012\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Potentially sensitising events in pregnancy (PSEs) Amniocentesis, chorionic villus biopsy and cordocentesis Antepartum haemorrhage/Uterine (PV) bleeding in pregnancy External cephalic version Abdominal trauma (sharp/blunt, open/closed) Ectopic pregnancy Evacuation of molar pregnancy Intrauterine death and stillbirth In-utero therapeutic interventions (transfusion, surgery, insertion of shunts, laser) Miscarriage, threatened miscarriage Therapeutic termination of pregnancy Delivery — normal, instrumental or Caesarean section Intra-operative cell salvage\n- Key_points\n- In pregnancies <12weeks gestation, anti-D Ig prophylaxis is only indicated following ectopic pregnancy, molar pregnancy, therapeutic termination of pregnancy and in cases of uterine bleeding where this is repeated, heavy or associated with abdominal pain. The minimum dose should be 250 II-J. A test for fetomaternal haemorrhage (FMH) is not required.\n\n- For potentially sensitising events between 12and 20 weeks gestation, a minimum dose of 250 IU should be administered within 72 h of the event. A test for FMH is not required.\n- For potentially sensitising events after 20 weeks gestation, a minimum anti-D lg dose of 500 IU should be administered within 72h of the event. A test for FMH is required.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 25-year-old female who is 37 weeks pregnant delivered a baby girl in the ED who is not crying and breathing. After calling for a help, you took first step and decided to dry the baby and put her in a wet towel. According to neonatal resuscitation guideline.\n- **A)** Commencing CPR\n- **B)** Suctioning the airway\n- **C)** Give five inflation breath\n- **D)** Intubate the baby\n- **E)** Assess the Colour, Tone, Reflexes\n- Explanation\n\nResuscitation Council UK Newborn life support GUIDELINES 42021 Preterm < 32 weeks in PIM tic\n- 28 wee" Birth Stut doe ret. heart breathing\n- S ( Cm Hao) —\n- PEE P HA if\n- ApøySpO..'.ECG\n- mask.\n- Repeat breaths\n- prezure Once cimt is CMtin\n- » v«ttjiabon if is Mt detectable Reassess tw\n- rt ate and chest movement every 30 seconds the SO z\n\n- otMr e hypme.—mu. parents and tearn\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nYou are called urgently to assess a woman in active labour. She is reported to be at 37 weeks of gestation but if from out of the local area. As you arrive, she gives birth to a baby girl who is not breathing. After calling for help, what is your immediate priority?\n- **A)** Intubate the baby\n- **B)** Suctioning of the airway\n- **C)** Giving five inflation breath\n- **D)** Commencing CPR   E\n- Dry the baby\n- Explanation\n- After calling for help and start clock, next step would be dry the baby.\n\nResuscitation Council UK Newborn life support GUIDELINES 42021 Preterm < 32 weeks in PIM tic\n- 28 wee" Birth Stut doe ret. heart breathing\n- S ( Cm Hao) —\n- PEE P HA if\n- ApøySpO..'.ECG\n- mask.\n- Repeat breaths\n- prezure Once cimt is CMtin\n- » v«ttjiabon if is Mt detectable Reassess tw\n- rt ate and chest movement every 30 seconds the SO z\n\n- otMr e hypme.—mu. parents and tearn IO. A 40-year-old male presents to the ED with complain of joints pain and swelling. After examination, fluid aspiration is done and synovial fluid crystal analysis revealed positive crystal and no microorganism in culture. What is the most likely diagnosis?   A\n- Pseudo gout\n- **B)** Septic arthritis\n- **C)** Ankylosing spondylitis\n- **D)** Reactive arthritis\n- **E)** Gouty arthritis\n- Explanation\n\nGout Male gender, impaired renal function, Obesity, metabolic syndrome. dietary (red meat alcohol. seafood). diuretics (thiazides), tumour lysis syndrome Acute monoarthropathy typically in MTP of first toe (podagra) Monosodium urate Negatively birefringent needle crystal Well-defined periarticular erosions\n- 1st line: NSAID\n- 2nd line: Colchicine*\n- Prophylaxis: Allopurinol (do not start during an acute attack)\n- Key-points Gout vs Pseudogout Risk factors Acute presentation Crystal Polarised light X-ray findings Pseudogout Elderly, hyperparathyroidism, haemochromatosis. hypophosphataemia, osteoarthritis Acute monoarthropathy typically of knee Calcium pyrophosphate Weakly positive birefringent rhomboid crystal Chondrocalcinosis 1st line: NSAID\n- 2nd line: Colchicine*\n- Prescribe NSAID at the maximum dose (for example, naproxen).\n- Consider the need to prescribe a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) for gastric protection.\n- Colchicine — if patient with CKD or heart failure or where NSAIDs are contraindicated\n- Colchicine is contraindicated in blood disorders\n- A short course of oral corticosteroid — for example prednisolone 30-35 mg once a day for 3-5 days in acute gout is recommended.\n- Consider an intra-articular or intramuscular corticosteroid injection if NSAIDs and colchicine are not tolerated or are ineffective.\n- Prophylaxis\n- Offer Allopurinol, or febuxostat urate-lowering therapy (ULT) using a treat-to-target strategy to people with gout who have:\n- Multiple or troublesome flares.\n\n- Chronic kidney disease (CKD) stages 3 to 5 (glomerular filtration rate [CFR] categories 03 to GS).\n- Diuretic therapy.\n- Chronic gouty arthritis.\n- Start ULT at least 2-4 weeks after a gout flare has settled.\n- Aim for a target serum urate level below 360 micromol/L (6 mg/dL) previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	51	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
ea7ef1fb-125f-4d76-9fe1-c3b93d16055e	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-50	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 50	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 50\n\n- **A)** Change position\n- **B)** wait for IT u consultant   C\n- Vasopressor\n- **D)** Give long-acting steroids\n- **E)** Give further one liter IV fluid\n- Explanation\n- The patient does not respond to initial fluid resuscitation and a dilated IVC is often an indicator of decreased intravascular volume and supports the need for vasopressor support in this case.\n- Vasopressors can help increase blood pressure and improve tissue perfusion, which is crucial in the management of septic shock when fluid resuscitation alone is insufficient. Waiting for the ICU consultant might not be advisable in this critical situation, and additional fluid may not be as effective as vasopressor support.\n\nNormal IVC Dilated IVC\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 35-year-old male factory worker presents to the emergency department after an accidental spill at work. He complains of intense pain on his right hand and forearm. On examination, you note erythema and blistering with a clear demarcation line. He tells you he believes it was hydrofluoric acid that he came into contact with. What is the definitive treatment for this patient's condition?\n- **A)** Copious irrigation with normal saline\n- **B)** Topical Magnesium Sulfate\n- **C)** Calcium Gluconate Cel\n- **D)** Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate\n- **E)** Oral Activated Charcoal\n- Explanation\n- Calcium Gluconate Gel is the definitive treatment because it directly addresses the toxic effects of hydrofluoric acid by providing a source of calcium ions that bind to fluoride ions. This helps to prevent further tissue damage and alleviate pain.\n- While copious irrigation with normal saline is extremely important as an initial step, it's not considered the definitive treatment on its own. It's best used in conjunction with Calcium Gluconate Gel for the most effective management of hydrofluoric acid burns. Hydrogen Fluoride and Hydrofluoric Acid (HE)\n- Major route of exposure - ingestion, inhalation, dermal or eye contact\n- Immediate signs or symptoms of acute exposure\n\nInhalation Ingestion Dermal Ocular Systemic features Signs and symptoms\n- Irritation ot eyes and nose with sore throat, cough, chest tightness, headache, ataxia and confusion. Dyspnoea and stridor due to laryngeal oedema may follow. Haemorrhagic pulmonary oedema With increasing breathlessness, wheeze, hypoxia and cyanosis may take up to 36 hours to develop\n- Burningofthe mouth and throatwith retrosternal and abdominalpain. The larynx may also be affected causing oedema, airway Obstruction and difficulty in clearingbronchial secretions. There is often hypersalivation, vomiting, haematemesis and hypotension. Oesophageal or gastric perforation may occur Severe and deep burns Which are extremely painful and difficult to heal. Pain may appear to be disproportionate to physical findings. There may be blue-grey discoloration of the skin in severe cases Conjunctivitis, conjunctival oedema, corneal epithelial coagulation and necrosis Hypocalcaemia, hypomagnesaemia and metabolic acidosis. Hyperkalaemia is also common. Myoclonus, tetany, convulsions, CNS depression, cardiac conduction disturbances and arrhythmias (prolonged QT interval, ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation) may occur secondary to hypocalcaemia and Other electrolyte disturbances.\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Management Do not delay decontamination if the patient is responsive Dry decontanmination - remove contaminated clothing Wet decontanmination - irrigate the contaminated area with copious amounts of water as soon as possible for at least I minute. Dermal exposure. following decontamination (as mentioned above) apply calcium gluconate gel repeatedly to the burn. For burns to the hand the use of a surgical glove containing calcium gluconate may be effective at reducing pain note if calcium gluconate gel is not available continue to flush with water until it becomes available\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- if skin contamination is extensive and clothing affected, be aware of the possibility of inhalation injury other supportive measures as indicated by the patient's clinical condition. Ocular exposure remove contact lenses if necessary and immediately irrigate the affected eye thoroughly for at least 30 mins with water or 0.9% saline the evidence for the efficacy of calcium gluconate following ocular exposure is lacking and it may be toxic to the conjunctiva. repeated installation of local anaesthetics (e.g. amethocaine) may reduce discomfort and help more thorough decontamination. An anaesthetised eye should be covered to protect from traumatic injury patients with corneal damage, those who have been exposed to strong acids or alkalis and those whose symptoms do not resolve rapidly should be discussed urgently with an ophthalmologist other supportive measures as indicated by the patient's clinical condition. Inhalation remove from exposure and give oxygen maintain a clear airway and ensure adequate ventilation urgent assessment of the airway is required in severely affected patients, especially those with tachypnoea, stridor or upper airway damage, critical care input is essential with urgent assessment of the airway. A supraglottic-epiglottic burn with erythema\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- and oedema is usually a sign that further oedema will occur that may lead to airway obstruction children are at increased risk of airway obstruction monitor cardiac rhythm and perform a 12-lead ECG in all patient who require assessment other supportive measures as indicated by the patient's clinical condition. Ingestion maintain airway and establish haemodynamic stability gut decontamination is contra-indicated do not give neutralising chemicals as heat produced during neutralisation reactions may increase injury maintain a clear airway and ensure adequate ventilation in severely affected patients, especially those with tachypnoea, stridor or upper airway damage, critical care input is essential with urgent assessment of the airway. A supraglottic-epiglottic burn with erythema and oedema is usually a sign that further oedema will occur that may lead to airway obstruction children are at increased risk of airway obstruction monitor cardiac rhythm and perform a 12-lead ECC in all patient who require assessment other supportive measures as indicated by the patient's clinical condition. Antidotes\n\n- calcium gluconate gel is available\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 68-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department by his family members. They describe 2 days of increased thirst and urination, alongside increased confusion. He has a history of type II diabetes. His blood results are: What is the next management step?   A IV Antibiotics\n- **B)** KCL 40 mmol/L\n- **C)** Insulin infusion\n- **D)** IV Fluid\n- **E)** NS with 5% dextrose\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggest HHS\n- IV 0.9% sodium chloride —I litre to run over I hour should be commenced.\n\nHyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State(HHS)\n- The Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS) is a medical emergency. It occurs much less frequently than the other hyperglycaemic emergency, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), and its treatrnent requires a different approach. HHS usually affects those with preexisting type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) but may sometimes be the first presentation of this condition. Typically HHS occurs in those aged over 45 years old, but it can affect younger adults and teenagers (1; 2). HHS often develops over several days, and consequently the dehydration and metabolic disturbances are more extreme. The predominant cause of HHS is usually a chest or urinary tract infection.\n- The characteristic features of a person-with-HHSare; marked hypovolaemia\n- measured or calculated serum osmolality usually 2320 mOsm/kg\n- marked hyperglycaemia (230 mmol/L)\n- without significant hyperketonaemia (ketones S3.O mmol/L)\n- without\n- significant acidosis (pH 27.3 and blood or serum bicarbonate 215.0 mmol/L). Investigations Capillary blood glucose (BC) (finger prick point of care)\n- Venous plasma BG (blood in a fluoride bottle)\n-\n\nUrea and electrolytes\n- Measured or calculated serum osmolality [(2xNa+) + glucose + urea].\n- Until the urea is available, calculate using (2xNa+ + glucose). Recalculate osmolality once urea is available, and then use (2xNa+ + glucose + urea). Venous blood gas (VBG) for: PH, bicarbonate, lactate, and electrolytes\n- Capillary blood ketones (finger prick point of care)\n- Full blood count\n- Blood cultures\n-\n- ECC\n- Chest x-ray Urinalysis and culture\n-\n- CRP (if indicated) Management\n- Intravenous fluid\n- Commence IV 0.9% sodium chloride — I litre to run over I hour\n- Consider more rapid replacement if systolic blood pressure (SBP) < 90\n- mmHg Caution in the elderly where too rapid rehydration may precipitate\n- heart failure but insufficient may fail to reverse acute kidney injury To achieve a gradual decline in osmolality (i.e. 3.0-8.0 mOsm/kg/hr)\n- Using 0.9% sodium chloride solution aim to give a further 0.5-1 litre/hr\n- depending on clinical assessrnent of dehydration vs. risk of precipitating heart failure. The target is to achieve positive fluid balance of 2-3 litres by 6 hours\n\n- Measure glucose, urea and electrolytes hourly and calculate osmolality [(2xNa+) + glucose + urea]\n- If plasma Na+ increasing but osmolality declining at appropriate rate, continue 0.9% sodium chloride\n- If plasma Na+ increasing AND osmolality increasing (or declining at less than 3.0 mOsm/ kg/hr), check fluid balance. If positive balance is inadequate, then increase the rate of infusion of 0.9% sodium chloride solution\n- If the osmolality is increasing and fluid balance adequate, then consider switching to 0.45% sodium chloride at same infusion rate.\n- If osmolality falling at rate exceeding 8.0 mosm/kg/hr consider reducing infusion rate of IV fluids and/or insulin (if already commenced)\n- If glucose falling less than 5.0 mmol/L per hour check fluid balance If positive balance inadequate, increase rate of infusion of 0.9% sodium chloride\n- Fluid replacement should be adjusted for those who are <50Kg in body weight or with pre-existing heart and renal disease. More cautious fluid replacement is necessary e.g. 0.25 ml/kg/hr as recommended by NICE.\n- Intravenous insulin\n- ONLY commence insulin infusion quickly in the following ci rcurnstances:\n- If there is HHS and ketonaemia (blood ketones 3ß-hydroxybutyrate >1.0 - s3.o mmol/L or urine ketones < 2+) and not acidotic (venous pH >7.3 and bicarbonate >15.0 mmol/L) then use 0.05 units/kg/hr\n\n- If there is significant ketonaemia (3ß-hydroxybutyrate >3.0 mmol/L) or ketonuria (2 2+) with a pH\n- If positive fluid balance is adequate, commence low dose IV insulin as a fixed rate intravenous insulin infusion (FRIII) at 0.05 units/kg/hr or if already running, increase rate to 0.1 units/kg/hr if glucose concentrations are not falling\n- Potassium-replacement\n- Maintain potassium in the normal range:\n- Hypokalaemia (<3.5 mmol/L) and hyperkalaemia (>6.0 mmol/L) are life threatening conditions and warrant senior review. They are less common in HHS than DKA but monitoring and replacement are essential Potassium concentration in first 24h (mmol/L) Over 5.5 3.5 -5.5 Below 3.5\n- Avoidance-of-hypoglycaemia Potassium replacement in infusion solution Nil 40 mmol/L Senior review as additional potassium required\n- Aim to keep BG 10-15 mmol/L in first 24 hours o If BC falls below 14 mmol/L commence 5% or 10% glucose at 125 ml/hr AND CONTINUE 0.9% sodium chloride solution\n\n- Monitor vital signs and chart National Early Warning Score (NEWS)\n- Check ketone concentrations hourly until HHS resolution\n- Maintain accurate fluid balance chart (minimum urine output 0.5 ml/kg/hr)\n- Heel protectors and an appropriate mattress should be provided for those with immobility, neuropathy, peripheral vascular disease or lower limb deformity.\n- assessment of complications of treatment e.g. fluid overload, cerebral oedema or CPM / osmotic demyelination syndrome (as indicated by a deteriorating conscious level) must be undertaken frequently (every 1-2 hours).\n- prophylactic I-MWIH is required in most people.\n- HHS can be considered to be resolved when the following criteria are met:\n- when measured or calculated serum osmolality falls to < 300 mosm/kg, hypovolaemia has been corrected (urine outputs 0.5 ml/kg/hr)\n- cognitive status has returned to the premorbid state and blood glucose< 15 mmol/L\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 10-year-old girl has fallen off her skateboard and injured her elbow. His X-ray is shown. What is the management plan?\n- **A)** Collar and cuff   B\n- Apply cast above elbow\n- **C)** Reassure and discharge\n- **D)** Refer to orthopedics\n- **E)** Closed reduction and then refer Supracondylar fracture management: The procedure for the management of paediatric supracondylar fractures includes:\n- Analgesia: Initial assessment should include provision of analgesia and a search for associated neurovascular complications. Intranasal administration of analgesia (diamorphine) is a useful option in children and can delay attempts at intravenous access until topical anaesthesia has taken effect. Treatment :\n- On-displaced fractures (type-I): Immobilization in an above-elbow backslab (Cast) in 90 degrees elbow flexion with sling for 3 weeks. Avoid putting on a short, flimsy backslab. The backslab should extend as high above the elbow as possible (i.e., close to the axilla) and down to the metacarpophalangeal joints (MCP) joints.\n\nUrgent referral for type-II and type-III supracondylar fracture or those with neurovascular deficit or >50% displacement. Complications:\n- Cubitus varus (gun stock deformity)\n- Malunion and stiffness Myositis ossificans\n- Nerve injury (most commonly the median nerve)\n- Brachial artery (due to stretch and posterior displacement)\n- Volkmann's ischaemic contracture (due to compartment swelling)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 22-year-old patient presents to the Emergency Department with back pain and lower limb numbness that has progressively worsened. The patient now experiences bladder dysfunction and demonstrates reduced sensation in the perineal area upon examination. Given these symptoms, which of the following conditions warrants an urgent MRI?\n- **A)** L5/S1 nerve root compression\n- **B)** 1.4/1.5 nerve root compression\n- **C)** L2/L3 nerve root compression\n- **D)** S4/S5 nerve root compression\n- **E)** L3/L4 nerve root compression\n- Explanation\n- The combination of back pain, lower limb numbness, bladder dysfunction, and reduced perineal sensation strongly suggests involvement of the S4/S5 nerve roots, which control these functions. This presentation is a red flag for serious spinal pathology, requiring an urgent MRI to confirm the diagnosis and prevent permanent neurological damage. Other options involve different nerve roots, which typically present with pain, numbness, and weakness in areas corresponding to their dermatomes but do not usually cause bowel and bladder dysfunction. L5/SI, L4/L5, L3/L4, L2/L3 nerve root compression: While these levels can cause back pain and leg symptoms (sciatica), they are less likely to cause bowel and bladder dysfunction or perineal sensory loss. These symptoms point to involvement of the sacral nerve roots.\n- The patient's symptoms (worsening lower back pain, lower limb numbness, bowel and bladder dysfunction, perineal sensory loss) strongly suggest Cauda Equina Syndrome. CES is a serious condition that involves the compression of the nerve roots at the lower end of the spinal cord (specifically S2-S5).\n- Cauda equina syndrome (CES) is caused by compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots that extend below the spinal cord. The most common cause is due to disc prolapse at the 1.4/5 or L5/SI level and stenosis of the spinal canal. Less commonly, compression may be due to pathology in a higher disc, or to a tumour, infection, or trauma. Typical symptoms and signs of CES include bladder dysfunction, low\n\nback pain, bilateral or unilateral sciatica, lower limb weakness or numbness, bowel dysfunction, and sexual dysfunction. Although not all patients show all symptoms and signs, bladder dysfunction is always apparent at some stage in the progression of CES.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 25-year-old man present to ED with tooth pain, he has a molar tooth extraction. On oral examination there is no visible clot or bleeding. What is the next step of management in this patient?   A Antibiotics\n- **B)** Tetanus booster   C Pain killer   D, Irrigation with NS\n- **E)** Chlorohexidine mouth wash pry Sockets management: Dry sockets are best managed by irrigating the area with saline, and then placing aveogyl, which is a dressing material containing eugenol, which helps relieve pain and aid healing.\n- Use of chlorhexidine as a direct socket irrigant has results in 2 deaths in the UK due to previously unknown allergy, and therefore current\n\nguidance is not to use chlorhexidine for socket irrigation.\n- The most recent guidance states that antibiotics should only be considered in those with signs of spreading infection, systemic infections of for immunocompromised patients. Bleedingsoc_ets-management;\n- Application of damp gauze Application of gauze soaked in tranexamic acid or Local infiltration of adrenaline (or adrenaline containing local anaesthetic)\n- Consider the use of a 5% tranexamic mouthrinse to be used 4x daily as a prophylactic measure afterwards.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 50-year-old woman is brought into ED following a road traffic collision and sustained pelvic trauma, her vital signs include BP 100/50mmHg, Pulse 94, RR:14, and Sa02 98%. His pelvic X-ray is shown. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?\n- **A)** Activate major hemorrhage protocol\n- **B)** Apply pelvic binder\n- **C)** Refer to surgery\n- **D)** Start IV fluids and antibiotics   E, Traction set up\n- Explanation\n- X-ray shows Vertical shear fracture\n- The patient is stable, therefore pelvic binder isn't indicated\n- Traction should be set up to prevent the movement of fracture fragments, stabilize the pelvis, and reduce pelvic volume.\n- Emergency Department Management of Vertical Shear Pöiüc Fractures\n- Early recognition and treatment of hemorrhagic shock is the most important factor for survival.\n- 63% prevalence of hemorrhagic shock in these injuries\n- Potential for massive retroperitoneal hemorrhage from bony fragments and lacerated blood vessels\n- Venous bleeding more common but arterial hemorrhage can also occur\n- Retroperitoneal space can accumulate up to 4 liters of blood before venous tamponade occurs\n- With only 3 cm of additional pubic symphysis diastasis (separation), the pelvis doubles in volume, allowing for significantly more bleeding before tamponade.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Emergency Stabilization Approach\n- Place pelvic binder — indicated in an unstable patient\n- Focus on traurna resuscitation\n- Traction Set Up Orthopedic Stabilization Approach\n- Goals: Prevent movement of fracture fragments, stabilize pelvis and reduce pelvic volume.\n- Apply axial traction to vertically align the two pelvic fracture fragments\n- Place supracondylar femoral pin into side with migrated fragment\n- Apply 25-30 lbs of traction to reduce pelvis\n- Attempt to obtain equal leg lengths as indicator of alignment\n- After traction accomplished, apply a pelvic binder or bedsheet Continued management depends on other traumatic injuries and priorities but may include external fixation, angiography with embolization, laparotomy with pelvic packing. Consult trauma surgery, orthopedics, and interventional radiology for embolization. The Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS) guidelines define hemodynamic instability/shock as a state of inadequate tissue perfusion that results in impaired cellular metabolism and organ dysfunction. The signs of shock include: Hypotension (systolic blood pressure < 90 mmHg or mean arterial pressure < 60 mmHg) Tachycardia (heart rate > 100 beats per minute) Tachypnea (respiratory rate > 20 breaths per minute) Cool, clammy skin\n\n- Decreased urine output\n- Altered mental status\n- In addition to these signs, the AT LS guidelines recommend that healthcare professionals use clinical judgment to identify other signs of shock, such as decreased capillary refill time, delayed or weak peripheral pulses, and decreased skin turgor.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 23-year-old male presented to the ED with a one-day history of vomiting and lethargy. His observations are: T 370c HR 125bpm RR 25 breath/min BP 80/60mmHg His investigation is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Sepsis\n- **B)** Gastroenteritis\n- **C)** Salicylate intoxication\n- **D)** Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar non ketotic coma\n- **E)** DKA\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features and investigation met the criteria of DKA.\n- Lactate acidosis in DKA is multifactorial in aetiology— anaerobic glycolysis due to inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygenation as well as the metabolic derangements itself present in DKA might contribute to the elevated lactate levels.\n- Elevated lactate levels in DKA are usually a sign of severe illness and metabolic derangement. g-must-be present:\n- Capillary blood glucose above II mmol/L\n- Capillary ketones above 3 mmol/L or urine ketones ++ or more\n- Venous pH less than 7.3 and/or bicarbonate less than 15 mmol/L\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old man, known for poorly controlled diabetes, presents to the ED with abdominal pain associate with vomiting and reduced consciousness. He is started on an insulin infusion. His initial blood glucose level is documented as 32 mmol/L. Which of the following statement is the correct?\n- **A)** Minimum rate of blood glucose reduction that should be blood glucose fall 4 mmol/L/hr\n- **B)** Start 5% dextrose 125mls/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- **C)** Start 10% dextrose 125mls/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- **D)** Start insulin at fixed rate 0.05 units/ kg/hour\n- **E)** Rate of fall of ketones of at least 0.1 mmol/L/hr Explanation\n- JBDS (Joint British Diabetic Society) DKA adult recommends 10%\n- dextrose 125mls/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- DKA key_points Diagnostic criteria: all three of the following must be present:\n- capillary blood glucose above 11 mmol/L\n- capillary ketones above 3 mmol/L or urine ketones ++ or more\n- venous pH less than 7.3 and/or bicarbonate less than 15 mmol/L\n- Immediate intervention\n-\n\n- Commence 0.9% sodium chloride solution (use a large bore cannula) via an infusion pump. (Rate is mentioned below in fluid challenge)\n- Commence a fixed rate intravenous insulin infusion (FRIII). (O.lunit/kg/hr based on estimate of weight) 50 units human soluble insulin (Actrapid  or Humulin SO) made up to 50ml withO.9% sodium chloride solution. If patient normally takes long acting insulin analogue (glargine, detemir, degludec) continue at usual dose and time\n- Fluid challenge\n- Shock patient SBP<90mmHg - Give 500mIs 0.9% sodium chloride solution over 10-15 minutes. If SBP remains low repeat the bolus. Most people require 500-100mI. Once SBP.\n- Once SBP is >90mmHg, give IL 0.9% sodium chloride over the next 60 minutes. The addition of potassium is likely to be required in this second litre of fluid.\n- If SBP>90mmHg - Give IL 0.9% sodium chloride over the first 60 minutes\n- Potassium-replacement\n\nPotassium replacement (mmol/L) infusion solution > 5.5 3.5-5.5 < 3.5 Aims of treatment: Potassium replacement mmol/L of infusion solution Nil\n- 40 mmol/L senior review — additional\n- potassium required\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Rate of fall of ketones of at least 0.5 mmol/L/hr OR bicarbonate rise 3 mmol/L/hr and blood glucose fall 3 mmol/L/hr Maintain serum potassium in normal range Avoid hypoglycaemia Consider reducing the rate of intravenous insulin infusion to 0.05 units/ kg/hour when glucose falls below 14 mmol/L.\n- Add 10% dextrose 125mls/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L Accurate fluid balance chart, minimum urine output 0.5ml/kg/hr Resolution of DKA parameters It is defined at ketones < 0.6 mmol/L AND venous pH >7.3 (do not use bicarbonate as a marker at this stage).\n\nIO. A 45-year-old male presented to the ED with a complaint of sudden onset of chest pain while eating which is lasted for 20 minutes. He is pain free now. His examination revealed diastolic murmur. His lab markers are given below. Trop-l : 16 (<14) D-dimer: 850 ng/ml (<500 ng/ml) What would be the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Cardiomyopathy\n- **B)** Aortic aneurysm\n- **C)** Pulmonary embolism\n- **D)** Thoracic aortic dissection\n- **E)** Acute coronary syndrome\n- Explanation\n- It's a debated question, initially, it was given with low level of D-dimers. However, in previous diet D-dimer was elevated than the reference range.\n- Trop-l is usually negative in TAD but can be elevated in case pt has developed complication.\n\n- RCEM guideline\n- TAD (Thoracic aortic dissection) is a relatively uncommon cause of chest pain (acute coronary syndrome is 100-200 times more common) but can be catastrophic with an in-hospital mortality rate of 27% . This makes deciding which patient to request a CT scan on particularly difficult. There are numerous examples of patients attending EDS with chest pain and being discharged without the diagnosis of TAD having been made, Risk factors\n- Hypertension collagen disorders (Marfan's, Ehler-DanIos)\n- inflammatory vasculitis (giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis,\n- rheumatoid arthritis) instrumentation or structural abnormalities of the aorta (cardiac\n- catheterisation, bicuspid valve, aortic co-arctation, valve replacement) Pregnancy\n- Male sex and advancing age\n- Clinical features\n- chest pain which is said to be tearing in nature located in the inter-\n- scapular region, however the most discriminating feature of the chest pain is that it is of sudden onset with its worst severity being at its onset\n\n- Chest pain may occur alone or in combination with back pain, syncope, or new onset neurological deficit.\n- Patients may also present with complications from TAD including myocardial ischaemia, heart failure, pericardial effusion, pleural effusion, renal failure and mesenteric infarction.\n- Clinical findings in TAD may include pulse deficits, aortic regurgitation, unequal blood pressure in both arms, unexplained hypotension.\n- Investigations\n- The diagnostic modality of choice is CT Aortagram (CTA) which has high diagnostic accuracy in discriminating the various components of TAD. Initial non-contrast acquisition should form part of the scanning protocol for its value in assessment of intramural haematoma. A post contrast CT of the aorta will delineate the full extent of dissection and involvement of branch vessels.\n- Emergency department access to CT scans is key to the rapid diagnosis and institution of appropriate management. Ensuring there are minimum barriers to CT scans includes ensuring EPs (ED physicians) consider the diagnosis in appropriate cases and local protocols are in place for urgent CT scans meeting the appropriate criteria.\n- TAD is a time critical emergency and provision must be available for the ED to rapidly access urgently reported CTAs throughout the whole 24hr period.\n- CXR\n\n- 15% of patients with TAD have a normal chest X-ray (changes suggestive of TAD include: widened mediastinum >8cm on a PA film\n- abnormal (ie. blunted) aortic knob, ring sign [displacement of the aorta\n- >5 mm past the calcified aortic intima] pleural effusion\n- pleural cap (fluid in the apex of the hemi-thorax)\n- deviation of the trachea & left main bronchus\n- D-Dimer\n- dDimer levels rise abruptly following some types (but not all) of TAD with some evidence to suggest that a level of <500ng excludes the diagnosis (however the false negative rate may be between 4%-18%), the causes of elevated dDimers are multiple.\n- RCEM recommendations For patients presenting to the emergency department with chest pain,\n- a CTA should be requested if any of the high risk features below are present or in the case of more than one high risk feature from different boxes a CTA whole aorta UNLESS another cause of the chest pain is clearly identified and evidenced eg. acute myocardial infarction, pneumothorax, pulmonary embolus.\n\nHigh Risk CONDITIONS\n- Marfan syndrome Connective tissue disease\n- Family History Aortic Disease\n- Known aortic Valve Disease Recent Aortic Manipulation Known thoracic aortic aneurysm High Risk Pain FEATURES\n- Chest, back or abdominal pain described as: ABRUPT in onset / severe in intensity\n- Or Ripping/tearing/sharp or stabbing quality radiating to back High Risk CLINCAL FINDINGS\n- Pulse deficit Systolic BP differential (>20mmHg)\n- Focal neurological deficit (in conjunction with pain)\n- Aortic regurgitation murmur (new or not known and with pairw\n- For patients presenting with chest pain who do not have any high risk features listed above, further investigation should occur along standard lines. In the event of diagnostic uncertainty the decision to proceed to CTA should be taken by a senior emergency medicine clinician taking into account the clinical history, examination and investigation results. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	52	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
ccbfdade-7441-4aa4-8435-0ff627e8a9ef	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-51	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 51	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 51\n\nB\n- Scarlet fever\n- **C)** Rubella\n- **D)** Chicken pox\n- **E)** Kawasaki disease\n- Explanation\n- The child exhibits symptoms Of scarlet fever, which is caused by the bacterium Streptococcus pyogenes, also known as group A streptococcus (GAS).\n- Scarlet fever (or 'scarlatina')\n- It is a notifiable infectious disease caused by the bacterium Streptococcus pyogenes, also known as group A streptococcus (GAS).\n- Risk factors\n- At extremes of the age range, such as the very young and old, or postpartum women. Who are immunocompromised or immunosuppressed.\n- With comorbidities such as skin breakdown, diabetes mellitus, or underlying malignancy.\n- With concurrent chickenpox or influenza.\n- Who inject drugs or are alcohol dependent.\n\n- Clinical features\n- Sore throat.\n- Fever (typically greater than 38.30C).\n- Headache, fatigue, nausea, and vomiting. Rash—A blanching rash usually develops on the trunk 12—48 hours after initial symptoms, before spreading to the rest Of the body and flexures.\n- The rash is characteristically red, generalized and pinpoint (punctate) with a rough, sandpaper-like texture.\n- It may be accentuated in the skin folds of the neck, axillae, groin, elbows, and knees with a deep red, linear appearance (so-called 'Pastia's lines').\n- The palms and soles are typically spared.\n- Examination-may-also-reveal;-\n- Strawberry tongue — initially the tongue is covered with a white coat through which red papillae may be seen. Later, the white covering disappears, leaving the tongue with a beefy red appearance.\n- Associated cervical lymphadenopathy.\n- Flushed face, with marked circumoral pallor.\n- Pharyngitis and small red spots (petechiae) on the hard and soft palate ('Forchheimer spots').\n- Throat swabs and blood tests are not routinely indicated for the diagnosis of scarlet fever.\n- Consider taking a throat swab for culture of Croup A streptococcus (GAS) (prior to starting treatment) if:\n- There is uncertainty about the clinical diagnosis.\n- A case is suspected to be part of an outbreak\n- Measurement of serum anti-streptolysin O (ASO) antibody titres are not useful in acute infection, but may be helpful in the diagnosis of post infection complications, such as acute rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis.\n\n- Management Urgent hospital admission if patient has developed life threatening cornplications\n- If the person does not need hospital admissiomprescribe appropriate antibiotics promptJya_egardless of the severity of illness:\n- First line - Prescribe a IO-day course of phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V).\n- Second line - options for people with penicillin allergy are:\n- Birth to 6 months— clarithromycin for IO days.\n- Non-pregnant adults and children aged 6 months to 17 years — azithromycin for 5 days, or clarithromycin for 10 days.\n- Pregnant or postpartum (within 28 days of childbirth) — erythromycin for 10 days.\n- Advise and other measures\n- Advise on the use of over-the-counter paracetamol or ibuprofen for symptom relief if there are no contraindications.\n- Encourage the person to rest and drink adequate fluids.\n- Advise to clean and cover any skin breaks including bites, cuts, grazes, or wounds.\n- Exclusion from nursery, school, or work is needed for at least 24 hours after starting appropriate antibiotic treatrnent\n- Please note, notify the local health protection team promptly within 3 days by completing a notification form if a diagnosis of scarlet fever is suspected.\n- complicatien\n- Otitis media\n\nThroat infection and abscess (peritonsillar cellulitis, peritonsillar abscess, retropharyngeal abscess)\n- Acute sinusitis and mastoiditis\n- Non-suppurative (immune-mediated) complications tend to occur later in the course of infection,particularly in untreated people, and include:\n- Acute rheumatic fever — this is an immunologically-mediated response and can cause carditis and endocarditis (leading to valvular heart disease) and reactive arthritis.\n- Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis — this typically occurs 2 weeks or more after the acute initial infection, and may present with haematuria, reduced urine output, peripheral oedema, proteinuria, and hypertension. Permanent kidney damage is rare.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 16-year-old girl presents with hair loss and amenorrhea. She is on the dance team and says she has "a fear of becoming fat." The patient denies sexual activity. Vital signs are blood pressure  mm Hg, heart rate 40 bpm, and body mass index 16.9 kg/m2. Physical examination reveals a cachectic adolescent female with thinning scalp hair and fine hair over her chest, enlarged parotid glands, and skin thickening over her third dorsal finger. Laboratory results are significant for hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. Urine beta-hCG is negative. You suspect anorexia nervosa. What would be your step of management ?\n- **A)** Check ECG for electrolyte abnormalities\n- **B)** Start short course Of dexamethasone\n- **C)** IV atropine\n- **D)** Start high caloric feeding\n- **E)** Discharge with multivitamins\n- Explanation\n- Early nutritional rehabilitation is key to improving the patient's condition. psychosocial support through this with conveyance of empathy and compassion is a cornerstone of effective treatment.\n- Anorexia Nervosa\n- Anorexia nervosa (AN) is an eating disorder characterised by restriction of caloric intake leading to low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a body image disturbance.\n- female sex\n- adolescence/puberty,\n- Obsessive and perfectionist traits\n- Clinical features\n- significantly low body weight - While there is no single definition of low weight, adults with AN commonly present with body mass index (BMI) <18.5 kg/m2.\n- fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or behaviours that interfere with weight gain despite evidence of significantly low body weight\n- disturbed body image - Patients are unduly influenced by thoughts of body shape and weight.\n\n- calorie restriction\n- binge-eating and/or purging\n- misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or diet pills\n- amenorrhoea\n- decreased subcutaneous fat\n- general fatigue, weakness, and poor concentration\n- cardiac symptoms and signs - Bradycardia is the most common cardiac feature. Other abnormalities include QTc prolongation, first-degree atrioventricular heart block, and, especially among individuals with electrolyte disturbances, non-specific T-wave changes.\n- non-specific gastrointestinal symptoms - Patients often report fullness, bloating, cramping gas, and constipation.\n- Investigations FBC — reveal normocytic normochromic anaemia; mild leukopenia; thrombocytopenia\n- serum chemistry - metabolic alkalosis and hypokalaemia (if vomiting is present); metabolic acidosis, hyponatraemia and hypokalaemia (if laxative use is present).\n- LFTs - elevated alanine aminotransferase, aspartate aminotransferase; elevated cholesterol\n- Urinalysis - low specific gravity may indicate consumption of large quantities of free water; ketonuria may indicate significant semi- starvation.\n- ECG - bradycardia, conduction defects; prolongation of QT interval (corrected for rate).\n- thyroid function tests\n- estradiol in females - low or non-detectable levels\n- Management\n\n- Ltpatient is medically stable and suitable for outpatient treatment\n- First line - Early nutritional rehabilitation and psychotherapy\n- Early nutritional rehabilitation is key to improving the patient's condition. psychosocial support through this with conveyance of empathy and compassion is a cornerstone of effective treatment\n- Nutritional rehabilitation for individuals with AN requires a nutritionally balanced meal plan with adequate calories for weight restoration.\n- Guidelines on initiation of caloric prescription vary significantly, and international practices vary; note that patients with risk factors for re- feeding syndrome may require slower rates of caloric increase.\n- Treatment approach for low weight patients who are not at significant risk of developing re-feeding syndrome would be to begin therapeutic feeding at caloric prescriptions Of 1500-1800 kcal/day. Caloric prescriptions may increase by 400 kcal/day every 48-72 hours if patients tolerate prior caloric level, in order to achieve consistent weight change approximating 1-2 kg/week for inpatients (with 0.5-1 kg/week more common in outpatient programmes).\n- Vitamins and minerals (e.g., multivitamin, phosphorus, magnesium, and calcium) - should be given until the patient's diet contains enough to meet his or her dietary reference values. Thiamine supplementation should be considered.\n- Fluid intake should be monitored - During treatment, patients may consume large quantities of water in order to falsely elevate weights. Additional supervision and/or urinalysis for specific gravity determination may be necessary for successful re-feeding with appropriate levels of fluid consumption.\n- PsyshQtherapy\n- Psychotherapy is recommended for all people with AN. Several psychotherapeutic approaches have been examined in adult patients, although no single approach has demonstrated clear superiority.\n- National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends that first-line treatment options for adults are: Individual eating\n\ndisorder-focused cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT-ED), MANTRA, SSCM.\n- National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends AN-focused family therapy for children and young people with AN delivered as single-family therapy or a combination of single- and multi-family therapy (with children and young people offered the option to have some sessions separately from their family or carers).\n- If AN-focused family therapy is unacceptable, contraindicated, or ineffective, the second-line recommendations are individual eating disorder-focused cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT-ED) or adolescent- focused psychotherapy for AN (AFP-AN).\n- Consider oral potassium_supplementation\n- Hypokalaemia is often the result of purging by vomiting and/or laxative misuse. Repletion for those with potassium levels >3.2 mmol/L (>3.2 mEq/L) can usually be achieved with foods alone:\n- It patient medically unstable or outpatient failure\n- Inpatient admission - First line treatment as mentioned above (for medically stable patient)\n- Further treatment\n- Consider olanzapine - Several studies in recent years have found that low-dose olanzapine, compared with placebo, significantly increases the rate of weight gain among patients who do not respond sufficiently to nutritional rehabilitation and psychotherapy.\n- Complications Long-term complications of anorexia nervosa include anemia, cardiac problems, osteoporosis, amenorrhea, gastrointestinal problems, electrolyte abnormalities, kidney problems, and alopecia.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 4-year-old boy presents with a sore throat, reduced appetite, malaise, and low-grade fever for the past few days. He has no significant past medical history. His temperature is 1000 F (37.80 C). Physical examination demonstrates several vesicular lesions with an erythematous base involving the buccal mucosa, palate and other parts. His image is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Chickenpox\n- **B)** Viral pharyngitis\n- **C)** Hand, foot, and mouth disease\n- **D)** Herpes simplex Erythema multiforme\n- Explanation\n- Small reddish spots and bumps in hands and around mouth suggests HFMD.\n- Hand, foot, and mouth disease (HFMD)\n- It is an acute viral illness characterized by vesicular eruptions in the mouth and papulovesicular lesions of the distal limbs. It is not to be\n\nconfused with foot and mouth disease of animals, which is caused by a different virus.\n- HFMD is usually mild and self-limiting. The incubation period is 3—7 days. The mucocutaneous lesions appear 1—2 days after a prodromal period of fever, malaise, and myalgia; and last approximately 7—10 days with spontaneous resolution.\n- Causative-agent\n- HFMD is caused by enteroviruses from the picornavirus family The main enterovirus serotypes responsible for HFMD are the Coxsackie viruses.\n- In Europe and the US, HFMD is most commonly associated with group A Coxsackie viruses - Coxsackie A16 is the most common cause, although outbreaks have been associated with the A6 and AIO strains.\n- Least cause enterovirus 71\n- Clinical features\n- Early symptoms are fever (typically 38—390C), malaise, loss of appetite, cough, abdominal pain, and sore mouth.\n- Mild illness - Sore throat, with or without a low-grade fever.\n- Tender lesions in the mouth (enanthem) and/or a rash on the body (exanthem) The prodromal period may last 12—36 hours\n- Examine the rash for characteristic features.\n- Macules and papules of the hands and feet usually soon follow the oral lesions.\n- Typically, they are 2—5 mm sparse erythematous macules and papules, often with a central greyish vesicle.\n- The sides of the fingers, dorsum of the hands, and margins of the heels are more frequently affected than the palms or the soles.\n- Lesions are frequently elliptical with the long axis running parallel to the skin lines.\n- The buttocks and groin area may also be affected.\n- Lesions may be asymptomatic or painful. Examination of mouth reveal\n\n- Scattered ulcerative lesions of the oral cavity occur within 1—2 days.\n- Typically, they begin as 2—8 mm erythematous macules and papules, and appear most commonly on the hard palate, tongue, and buccal mucosa, but also on the lips and pharynx.\n- Lesions progress rapidly to vesicles, which readily erode leaving shallow yellow-grey ulcers surrounded by an erythematous halo.\n- In severe cases, the lesions may coalesce leaving the tongue red and oedematous.\n- Diagnosis\n- The diagnosis of hand, foot, and mouth disease (HFMD) is usually clinical, based on typical symptoms and signs (clinical features).\n- Laboratory investigations (such as oral, skin, or rectal swabs) are not usually necessary in primary care owing to the self-limiting nature of the illness.\n- Management\n- Admission-itpatient-has-following-feature\n- Persistent or severe headache or fever. Myoclonus with sleep disturbances.\n- Confusion, weakness, lethargy, drowsiness, irritability, generalized seizures, and coma (may suggest encephalitis).\n- Other all patient - Where features are typical, reassure the person, parent, or guardian that hand, foot, and mouth disease is usually a mild, self-limiting illness.\n- Advise on self-care measures\n- Ensure fluid intake is adequate.\n\n- Advise that a soft diet may be necessary if mouth ulcers are painful, and that foods that are hot, spicy, salty, or acidic may cause oral pain.\n- Consider advising use of paracetamol or ibuprofen, if required, to reduce fever and pain.\n- Do not prescribe antibiotics or antiviral medication\n- Children do not need to be excluded from nursery, childcare, or school for infection control purposes.\n- Women who are pregnant and have suspected hand, foot, and mouth disease (HFMD) (or who have had contact with a person with HFMD) should be generally managed as for non-pregnant women. However, consider seeking advice from an obstetrician if the woman is immunocompromised, or is within 3 weeks of delivery, as investigations to confirm the diagnosis may be necessary, and observation of the neonate may be indicated.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 66-year-old patient presents to the Emergency Department with confusion, dry mouth, dilated pupils, and urinary retention. The patient is currently on multiple medications and was recently prescribed a new drug by his GP for vertigo. On examination, the patient appears agitated and febrile. Which of the following medications is most likely responsible for this presentation?\n- **A)** Cyclizine\n- **B)** Atropine\n- **C)** Metoclopramide\n- **D)** Morphine\n- **E)** Amitriptyline\n- Explanation\n- patient clinical features suggestive of anti-cholinergic syndrome. Cyclizine, used to treat vertigo and rnotion sickness, has strong anticholinergic properties, which can lead to symptoms like confusion, dry mouth, dilated pupils, and urinary retention. The other options, such as atropine, and amitriptyline, also have anticholinergic effects, but Cyclizine is the most relevant in the context of a medication typically used for vertigo and motion sickness. Metoclopramide, while used for nausea, does not have anticholinergic properties and is less likely to cause these symptoms.\n- Anticholinergic drugs\n- Prescribe anticholinergic drugs with caution in older or frail people or people with complex multimorbidities.\n- Older/frail patients are more likely to experience adverse effects with anticholinergics such as constipation, urinary retention, dry mouth/eyes, sedation, confusion, delirium, photophobia, falls and reduced cognition.\n- Recent guideline recommends that drugs with strong anticholinergic activity such as tricyclic antidepressants (e.g. amitriptyline), older drugs for bladder problems (e.g. oxybutynin) and first generation antihistamines (promethazine, chlorpheniramine, cyclizine) should be stopped if possible or substituted for a drug with less anticholinergic activity.\n- Anticholinergic-syndror:ne\n\n- Anticholinergic syndrome results from competitive antagonism of acetylcholine at central and peripheral muscarinic receptors. Central inhibition leads to an agitated (hyperactive) delirium - typically including confusion, restlessness and picking at imaginary objects - which characterises this toxidrome. Peripheral inhibition is variable - but the symptoms may include: hot, dry skin, flushed appearance, mydriasis, tachycardia, decreased bowel sounds and urinary retention. nti Olin\n- Atropine is the prototypical anticholinergic (anti-muscarinic) drug Class Anti-histamines Anti-tussives Antidepressants Anti-psychotics Anti-convulsants Anti-emetics/travel sickness Topical ophthalmoplegics Plants ether Agent Diphenhydramine, Doxylamine, Promethazine, Chlorpheniramine, Cyproheptadine, cyclizine Dextromethorphan Tricyclic antidepressants (Amitriptyline, Imipramine, Doxepin) Chlorpromazine, Droperidol, Haloperidol, Quetiapine, Olanzapine Carbamazepine Hyoscine (scopolamine) Cyclopentolate, Homatropine Deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna) , jimsonweed, mandrake root, lupin beans, Angel's Trumpet / Datura Oxybutynin, benztropine, glycopyrrolate\n- Clinical features of anticholinergic syndrome\n- Flushed skin Mydriasis Dry mouth, dry eyes and decreased sweating . Delirium\n- Urinary retention\n- Investigations\n\n- Screening tests - 12 lead ECG, blood glucose and paracetamol concentration - in deliberate self-poisoning.\n- Some cough medications may also contain paracetamol - so a concentration should be considered in these accidental ingestions as well.\n- Consider salicylate concentrations if differential includes salicylate toxicity.\n- Consider concentrations for specific agents if available - eg carbamazepine\n- Consider OEC and CK\n- Management\n- Stop any causative agents\n- Active resuscitation is infrequently required. Attention should be paid to the maintenance of airway, breathing and circulation.\n- Hypotension should be addressed initially with a 500ml crystalloid bolus/10ml/kg for children.\n- Treat seizures with benzodiazepines\n- Treat hypoglycaemia\n- Treat hyperthermia with cooling measures. Antipyretics are unhelpful.\n- Reassurance and management in a quiet area may be sufficient for those with mild signs/symptoms.\n- Agitation / delirium is typically managed with titrated diazepam\n- Avoid haloperidol and droperidol, which may worsen anticholinergic effects.\n- Urinary retention may contribute to the agitation. Bladder scan will reveal the need for a urinary catheter.\n- The use of decontamination / enhanced elimination depend on the actual medication exposure, and should be discussed with a toxicologist. There exists some controversy over the use Of physostigmine- a cholinesterase inhibitor - which has been used to reduce delirium in\n\nanticholinergic syndrome. The use of physostigmine may be indicated following discussion with the toxicology service.\n- When to admit/consult local paediatric team/lCU team\n- Symptomatic adult/children require admission for monitoring and treatment if necessary\n- Resolution of symptoms is variable, and delirium can persist for days following an acute ingestion.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 26-year-old man presents with cramping spasmodic abdominal pain, nausea, and non-bloody, non-bilious vomiting for the past three days. He admits to significant daily marijuana use. He is given metaclopromide and promethazine but remains nauseated and vomits thrice during the evaluation. The patient's electrocardiogram is normal. What would be your step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Repeat IV metoclopramide   B\n- Haloperidol I/M\n- **C)** Cyclizine I/M\n- **D)** IV dexamethasone\n- **E)** IV ondansetron\n- Explanation\n\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of Cannabinoid Hyperemesis Syndrome (CHS).\n- Hyperemesis syndrome can occur with any type of cannabinoids abuse including inhalation, ingestion, as well as synthetic cannabinoid use.\n- For refractory nausea or vomiting in CHS, consider the use of haloperidol or capsaicin.\n- It also is known as cyclical vomiting syndrome and occurs generally with chronic long-term heavy marijuana (cannabis) usage. CHS is recognised as often being resistant to routine antiemetic treatments such as cyclizine, dexamethasone, domperidone, metoclopramide, ondansetron, prochlorperazine and promethazine. Additionally, pain relief with opiates is frequently unsuccessful.\n- Patients with an established diagnosis of CHS who have continued to use cannabis may present in the prodromal stage Of an episode, with nausea, abdorninal discomfort and fear of vomiting. Initiating treatrnent prior to vomiting may be helpful in this group..\n- For refractory nausea or vomiting in CHS, consider the use of haloperidol or capsaicin.\n- Prior to administering haloperidol, obtain an ECG to check for QTc prolongation and correct any electrolyte abnormalities if conduction defects are present. Do not give haloperidol if the QTc is prolonged, or if a patient has Parkinson's disease or Lewy body dementia (LBD). Patients should be monitored for acute dystonia after administration. Do not discharge patients from the ED with a prescription for haloperidol unless this forms part of a specialty management plan. If treatment fails to relieve symptoms and a patient requires admission, access to a hot shower or bath should be offered for symptom relief.\n- only definitive treatment that has been identified to prevent CHS is abstinence from cannabis.\n\nMedication Haloperidol Capsaicin 0.1% cream Route Intramuscular\n- Topical Suggested dose 0.05mg/kg (max 5mg) Sg of 0.1% cream applied to abdomen Note - (*Although the trial used intravenous haloperidol, there is no UK intravenous licence. There is a UK licence for postoperative nausea and vomiting, though doses are lower).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 28-year-old woman, who is thirteen weeks pregnant, presents to the Emergency Department with abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding following a miscarriage. Her vital signs reveal a heart rate of 45 beats per minute, a blood pressure of  mmHg, and a temperature of 350C. On examination, she appears pale and diaphoretic. What is the most likely cause Of her bradycardia?\n- **A)** Acute blood loss leading to hypovolemic shock B Electrolyte imbalance\n- **C)** Infection leading to septic shock\n- **D)** Myocardial infarction   E\n- Vagal stimulation\n\nExplanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of cervical shock.\n- The patient presents with abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and bradycardia following a miscarriage. Vagal stimulation can occur due to pain, emotional stress, or physical factors associated with the miscarriage, leading to a reflexive slowing Of the heart rate (bradycardia).\n- Cervical shock\n- Bradycardia and hypotension resulting from cervical stir-nulation or instrumentation is referred to as "cervical shock". It may result from the presence of products of conception in the cervix during miscarriage, which should be removed if present. More commonly, in primary care settings, cervical shock can occur during insertion of an intrauterine device (IUD). It is thought to be due to stimulation of the vagal nerve. Whilst uncommon, it can rapidly cause a woman to become unwell with circulatory compromise. The UK Faculty of Sexual and Reproductive Healthcare (FSRH) states that the availability of appropriate emergency medication, including atropine, during IUD insertion is essential and this is a service standard for resuscitation in sexual health services in the I-JK.\n- For a patient with symptomatic bradycardia secondary to cervical shock, cease manipulation of the cervix and remove all instruments. Call for help. Assess the patient with the ABCDE approach (Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure). Assess vital signs and provide oxygen, if required. Ensure the patient is supine and elevate the legs to improve venous return. If these are insufficient to improve the patient's haemodynamic status, 500-600mcg IV atropine is recommended followed by a saline flush. It is typically used to ensure that the medication is fully delivered into the bloodstream.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nAn 80-year-old elderly male palliative care patient was brought to the emergency department (ED) with shortness of breath. He has a history of advanced dementia and COPD. The patient is oriented only to a person, but not to place, time, or situation. He cannot answer simple questions appropriately. Despite repetitive nebulizer treatments, an intravenous corticosteroid injection, and an escalation of oxygen therapy, the patient remains hypoxic and hypercarbic. He is now altered and requires mechanical ventilation. He had signed an advanced directive one year ago that states he doesn't want mechanical ventilation if he deteriorates. He recently selected his son to be his lasting power of attorney. The son insists that his father should be put on mechanical ventilation. His daughter, the next of kin, also insists on putting her father on mechanical ventilation. What would be your step in this patient?\n- **A)** Initiate the treatment in his best interest\n- **B)** Lasting power of attorney (LPA) override the Advanced directive (AD)\n- **C)** Follow the daughter, the next kin\n- **D)** Discharge with palliative care advise\n- **E)** Advanced directive (AD) overrule the Lasting power of attorney (LPA)\n\nExplanation\n- First we've to exclude the next of kin "the closest relative" as they legally can't take decisions.\n- Since the AD was signed one year ago while the LPA was appointed recently the LPA is to be followed as it is more recent.\n- Incase Of AD vs LAP — the rnost recent one is one to be followed. Advance directive (decision)\n- Advanced directives (ADS), Often living wills or do-not- resuscitate (DNR) orders, express a patient'S wishes for end- of-life care when they are unable to make decisions themselves. They are considered legally binding documents in many jurisdictions, Advance decisions allow people who understand the implications of their choices to state their treatnment wishes in advance. They can be used to: Authorise or request specific procedures. Refuse treatment in a predefined future Situation,\n- Advance refusals Of treatment are legally binding (under CM'mOn law) if: The person iS an adult, and '\n- WaS Competent and fully informed when making the decision, and The decision is clearly applicable to the current circumstances, and There is no reason to believe that they have since changed their mind Lasting power of attorney (LPA) LPA is a document in which a person Can nominate else to make Certain decisions on their behalf (for example on finances, health, and personal welfare) when they are unable to do so themselves, The LPA must make decisions in the individual's best interest.\n- The Mental Capacity Act 2W5 provides the legal framework to give a named person authority to make these decisions on another person's behalf. LPA replaces what was previously known as 'enduring power of attorney'.\n- TO be valid, LPA documents be registered With the Office Of the Public Guardian, If there are COrEernS relating to decisions taken under the authority Of a CPA, the case can be referred to the Court of protection. treatment:\n- When the donor has the capacity to consent When the donor has made an advance decision to refuse treatment (unless the LPA was appointed after the AD and the donor gave permission to the LPA to override the AD)\n- When the decision relates to life-sustaining treatrnentand this has not been expressly authorized,\n- When the donor iS detained under the Mental Health Act\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 13-year-old boy presents with a 4-day history of sore throat and nasal congestion. Examination reveals cervical lymphadenopathy. His image is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Diphtheria\n- **B)** Per-tonsillar abscess\n- **C)** Retropharyngeal abscess\n- **D)** Croup\n- **E)** Epiglottitis\n- Explanation\n- The image shows an adherent, dense, grey pseudomembrane covering the tonsils which is classically seen in diphtheria. Diphtheria\n- Respiratory diphtheria is an upper respiratory tract illness caused by exotoxin-producing strains of Corynebacterium diphtheriae. Rarely, it may also be caused by other corynebacteria that produce diphtheria toxin, such as Corynebacterium ulceransor Corynebacterium pseudotuberculosis.\n- Risk factors\n- typical age group (<15 years or >25 years) - In endemic areas, where vaccination is uncommon, diphtheria mainly affects children years Of age\n- In countries where hygiene is poor, cutaneous diphtheria is the predominant clinical manifestation and source of infection.\n- Poor living conditions and lack of immunisation, especially where there is not an immunisation programme, increase risk.\n\n- Spread is via respiratory droplets or contact with exudate from skin lesions.\n- Adults are at risk as they lose protection from childhood vaccines unless they have boosters.\n- Clinical features\n- Sore throat\n- Dysphagia or dysphonia\n- Dyspnoea\n- Croup cough\n- pseudomembrane formation - Formation of an adherent brown- greyish membrane covering the tonsils, pharynx, and larynx is highly suggestive of diphtheria, and helps to differentiate diphtheria from pyogenic pharyngitis due to Streptococcus pyogenesor Epstein-Barr virus infection.\n- swelling Of the neck - This feature is associated with lymphadenopathy and is characteristic of severe diphtheria.\n- Stridor Skin lesions\n- Investigations\n- bacteriological culture and microscopy\n- Consider PCR\n- Consider diphtheria antibodies\n- Management UK guidelines support use of oral azithromycin or clarithromycin, which are more tolerable to patients, simpler to administer, and have\n\nnot been associated with any reported treatment failures.\n- azithromycin: children: 10-20 mg/kg orally once daily for 5-7 days, maximum 500 mg/day; adults: 500 mg orally once daily for 5-7 days\n- clarithromycin: children: 7.5 mg/kg orally twice daily for IO days, maximum 1000 mg/day; adults: 500 mg orally twice daily for IO days\n- Complications\n- Paralysis. This often involves the muscles of the palate and the hypopharynx is seen in 10-20% of patients, beginning as early as the first IO days of illness. Difficulty swallowing and nasal speech - These are often the first signs of neurological involvement.\n- Involvement of other cranial nerves - This may be delayed until as late as seven weeks after infection and produce oculomotor paralysis and blurred vision. Diffuse, usually bilateral, motor function deficits resulting from involvement of the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord may be seen as late as three months after initial disease, with progression of weakness either from proximal-to-distal regions or, more commonly, from distal-to-proximal regions.\n- Diaphragmatic paralysis - This will result if the phrenic nerve is involved. This may occur at any time between the 1st and 7th weeks of illness.\n- Cardiac complications — Myocarditis , heart failure\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nAn elderly man with Alzheimer's disease living in the nursing home have been deteriorating lately. He had signed an advanced directive one year ago that states he doesn't want to be admitted in the hospital if he deteriorates. He recently selected his son to be his lasting power of attorney wo years ago. The son insists that his father should be admitted to a hospital. You've assessed the patient and found to be lack Of mental capacity\n\nWhat would be your step in this patient?\n- **A)** Follow the son "the lasting power of attorney for health"\n- **B)** Refer him back to his GP\n- **C)** Follow the advance directive\n- **D)** Discharge with palliative care advise\n- **E)** Initiate the treatment in his best interest\n- Explanation\n- Since the AD was singed recently while the last power of attorney one year ago, the AD is to be followed as it is more recent. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nAn 80-year-old female is brought to the ED with short of breath and severe chest pain. She has mesothelioma and has been admitted three times over the past six months. As part of her management, which of the following analgesia is recommended in the end life care of this patient?   A Oral Morphine\n- **B)** Fentanyl patch\n- **C)** Oral Ibuprofen\n- **D)** IV paracetamol\n- **E)** Buprenorphine patch\n- Explanation\n- Patient has severe pain, therefore, strong opioid such as morphine, with or without a non-opioid analgesic should be consider.\n- Palliative cancer care (NICE guideline)\n- Management of acute severe pain\n- Immediately relieve pain using a subcutaneous or slow intravenous dose of a strong opioid:\n- If the person is opioid naive (means a patient who has not used opioids for more than seven consecutive days during the previous 30 days), consider a subcutaneous or slow intravenous dose of 5 mg of morphine (2.5 mg if the person is elderly or frail).\n- If the person is already taking a regular opioid - calculate the 4-hourly dose by taking the total dose given over the previous 24 hours (including doses required for breakthrough pain but excluding those for incident pain) and dividing it by six, and then give the equivalent subcutaneous dose Of morphine. The subcutaneous dose Of morphine is approximately half of the 4-hourly oral morphine dose.\n- Management of pain in non-emergency situations\n- Consider a stepwise approach, using the World Health Organization analgesic ladder. Start at the appropriate point of the analgesic ladder, moving up the ladder when the dose at each step is reached until the person is comfortable. The steps are:\n- Step 1- mild pain non-opioid analgesic such as paracetamol and/or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (mild pain). Paracetamol 1 g 4 times daily or reduced to 500 mg 4 times daily if the person has a poor\n\nnutritional status or low body weight (less than 50 kg), hepatic impairment, chronic alcohol abuse, or may be on chemotherapy. Or NSAID The oral route is preferred, but if this is not possible, consider alternative routes (such as rectal).\n- as codeine, dihydrocodeine, or tramadol (controlled drug), with or without a non- opioid analgesic.\n- Step-3-z-seyerepain-strong opioid such as morphine, with or without a non-opioid analgesic .\n- Strong pioid drug\n- Use the oral route of administration where possible.\n- Initially offer oral sustained-release morphine or oral immediate- release morphine (depending on the person's preference) with rescue doses of oral immediate-release morphine for break through pain.\n- Breakthrough pain, which may come on suddenly, is pain that may occur while you are taking medicine to manage chronic pain. It usually lasts for a short time and may be intense. Breakthrough pain may happen even when you're taking the correct amount of pain medicine, if the current medicine is wearing off.\n- Irnmediate-release morphine is useful for titration if the person's pain is severe and rapid titration is required, usually on an inpatient basis. Oramorph  solution and Sevredol  tablets are both immediate-release morphine products.\n- If compliance with oral morphine is good, but the person cannot tolerate an adequate dose — then consider using an alternative oral opioid and seek specialist help.\n- If the oral route is not appropriate (such as the person has nausea\n\nanalgesia);\n- Unstable analgesic requirements — consider switching to a subcutaneous morphine infusion and seek specialist advice where needed.\n- Stable analgesic requirements — consider switching to a transdermal patch if the person has previously tolerated opioids. Seek specialist advice.\n- Note: buprenorphine patches and fentanyl patches are contraindicated in people who are opioid naive ( opioid naive means a patient who has not used opioids for more than seven consecutive days during the previous 30 days). They also should be used with caution in people with cachexia as absorption may be unpredictable, so conversion charts may not accurately translate for these people.\n- If fentanyl patches are prescribed - advise the person that accidental exposure to fentanyl can cause life-threatening harm. This can occur if the patch is swallowed or accidentally transferred to another person.\n- To reduce this risk, advise the person:\n- To choose the patch application site carefully.\n- To check the adhesion of the patch once applied, especially the edges.\n- To fold the used patch as soon as it is removed so that the adhesive side of the patch sticks firmly to itself, and dispose of the folded patch safely.\n- If a patch is accidentally transferred to another person, remove it immediately and seek medical advice.\n- If a patch is swallowed, seek rnedical help immediately. 'tptmper.ienged-nausea-and-yomiting=prescribe an anti-ernetic such as metoclopramide for gastric stasis, otherwise low-dose haloperidol (nausea usually settles within the first week of\n\n- To prevent constipation (common side effect)- prescribe a stimulant laxative such as senna or bisacodyl and a softening laxative such as docusate.\n- Avoid dantron-containing laxatives in people who are incontinent as these drugs can cause a chemical burn (reddening) of the perianal area.\n- Dantron can also colour the urine red and alarm the person.\n- Note - Unstable analgesic requirements" refers to a situation where a patient's need for pain relief rnedications fluctuates significantly over time. This can be a challenging medical issue because it makes it difficult to predict and manage the patient's pain effectively.\n- Management neuropathic pain including painful diabetic neuropathy\n- If pain is purely neuropathic: and reversible conditions (for example vitamin B12deficiency) have been excluded:\n- Consider offering a tricyclic antidepressant (such as amitriptyline) or pregabalin (or gabapentin if there is a local decision to prefer gabapentin over pregabalin).\n- Titrate the dosage according to response and tolerability.\n- Consider capsaicin 0.075% cream (Axsain ) for people with localized neuropathic pain who wish to avoid, or cannot tolerate, oral treatments.\n- If pain is of mixed origin: use standard analgesics in addition to a tricyclic antidepressant or pregabalin (or gabapentin) if pain is not adequately controlled with standard analgesia alone.\n- Management of pain from raised intracranial pressure\n\n- Consider whether a treatable underlying cause is present.\n- Discuss with an oncologist regarding the need for radiotherapy.\n- Use standard analgesics in a stepwise approach as mentioned above\n- consider a trial of dexamethasone at a dose of 8—16 mg daily (taken in the morning), titrated down to the lowest dose that controls symptoms.\n- Response to dexarnethasone should be assessed after 3 days, but extensive cerebral oedema may take 2—3 weeks to resolve.\n- If there has been no response, discontinue dexamethasone immediately.\n- If there has been a benefit, review frequently and reduce to the lowest dose that controls symptoms (for example reduce by 2 mg every fifth day).\n- Management of intestinal colic\n- If symptomatic management is appropriate, consider hyoscine butylbromide(an antispasmodic), 20 mg immediately by subcutaneous injection, then 60—100 mg/24 hours via syringe driver continuous infusion.\n- Management of bone pain\n- Consider whether there is a treatable underlying cause and discuss with an oncologist if this is suspected (for example regarding radiotherapy or bisphosphonates for bone metastases).\n- Seek urgent advice from an orthopaedic surgeon if there is evidence or suspicion of an actual or imminent fracture.\n- For symptomatic relief:\n- Apply hot or cold packs.\n- Use standard analgesia in a stepwise approach mentioned above in non-emergency pain management\n\n- If incident pain occurs on movement, encourage the person to take a dose of their breakthrough analgesia 20—30 minutes before anticipated movement. See Management of breakthrough pain.\n- Management of muscle spasm\n- Consider whether there is a treatable underlying cause. Try simple measures (such as heat pad, massage, relaxation).\n- Consider transcutaneous electric nerve stimulation over the trigger point if the pain is myofascial.\n- If trigger points are multiple or the muscle spasm is widespread, consider a muscle relaxant:\n- benzodiazepines (such as diazepam) or baclofen:\n- Several different doses of diazepam have been suggested by experts. These range from 2—10 mg at night to 2—5 mg three times a day; the higher doses may be helpful if there is co-existing anxiety. The dose may need to be reduced depending on clinical response.\n- The suggested dose of baclofen to treat muscle spasm is 5—10 mg three times a day.\n- Management Of spinal cord compression\n- If spinal metastases are thought to be the cause of the pain, seek urgent (within 24 hours) specialist advice from a metastatic spinal cord compression coordinator if available, or alternatively the person's palliative care consultant or oncologist.\n- If there are associated neurological features suggestive of spinal cord compression, seek immediate specialist advice.\n- Unless contraindicated (including a significant suspicion of lymphoma), offer all people with metastatic spinal cord compression a loading dose of 16 mg of dexamethasone as soon as possible after assessment.\n\nprevious  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	53	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
98b38c02-abac-49b4-ae01-c9465763398a	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-52	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 52	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 52\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis? A Anterior Uveitis\n- **B)** Acute angle closure glaucoma\n- **C)** Retrobulbar haematorna\n- **D)** Penetrating eye trauma\n- **E)** Retinal detachment\n- Explanation\n- Patient clinical features suggestive of Retrobulbar hematoma.\n- Retrobulbar hematoma\n- Retrobulbar hematoma is the phenomenon of blood collecting in the retrobulbar space behind the globe\n- The development of a retrobulbar hematoma is rnost commonly associated with trauma, especially orbital floor fractures:\n- Clinical features\n- Reduced visual acuity\n- proptosis\n\n- Resistance to retropulsion of the globe when pressure is applied through the eyelid\n- Inyestigation\n- CT -will reveal hyperdense area in the affected side of retrobulbar space\n- Management\n- IV acetazolamide\n- IV mannitol\n- Lateral canthotomy with acantholysis is the definitive treatment\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 72-year-old man, who underwent an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) repair three years ago, presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset of bright red bleeding per rectum and hypotension. He also reports severe abdominal pain and vomiting. What is the most likely condition patient has developed?\n- **A)** Diverticulitis B\n- AOrto-enteric fistula\n- **C)** Ischemic colitis\n- **D)** Peptic ulcer disease\n- **E)** Mesenteric ischemia\n- Explanation\n- An aorto-enteric fistula is a serious and potentially life-threatening condition that can occur after abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) repair. It typically presents with gastrointestinal bleeding, hypotension, and sometimes with symptoms like severe abdominal pain and vomiting. Given the patient's history of AAA repair and the acute presentation of bright red bleeding per rectum (hematochezia) and hypotension, an aorto-enteric fistula is the most critical diagnosis to consider.\n- Long term complications after open surgical cepair of abdominal aortic aneurysm, and their incidence within five and 10 - 15 years\n\nComplications Para-anastomotic aneurysm Graftocclusion Incisional hernia Graft infection Secondary aorto-enteric fistula Estimated incidence within five years 1% 5- 12% 0.5 Estimated incidence within 10-15 years 4% at 10 years e 12% at 15 years 5% at 15 years 5-21% 1\n- Long term complications after endovascular cepair of abdominal aortic aneurysm (EVAR)\n\nComplications Endoleaks Post-EVAR growth without endoleak Graft infection Post-EVAR rupture Graft obstruction Migration\n- Secondary aorto-enteric fistula Estimated incidence within five years 5% - 40% depends on endoleak types 1% 0.5- 0.5-1% Rupture risk High Intermediate High Low High if associated with Type 1 endoleak 1\n- Surveillance post repair of AAA\n- NICE recommends that all patients who have had endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR) should be enrolled into a surveillance imaging programme. They also recommend that:\n- Frequency of surveillance should be based on the patient's risk of EVAR-reIated complications.\n- Contrast-enhanced CT angiography (CTA) or colour duplex ultrasound may be used for assessing AAA diameter and EVAR device limb kinking\n- If an endoleak is suspected, contrast-enhanced CTA should be used as first-line imaging. However, if this is contraindicated, contrast- enhanced ultrasound may be used.\n- An endoleak should not be excluded using a negative colour duplex ultrasound alone following an EVAR. An endoleak is vascular flow\n\noutside of the endograft, but still contained in the aortic aneurysm sac. There are 5 different types of endoleaks on the basis of location.\n- Follow-up non-contrast CT imaging is recommended at 5-year intervals after open repair or EVAR.\n- Women are at greater risk of postoperative complications (limb ischaemia, renal and cardiovascular complications) and mortality (in- hospital and 30-day) than men; strict long-term surveillance is essential in female patients undergoing AAA repair.\n- Antibiotic prophylaxis of graft infection is required prior to bronchoscopy, gastrointestinal or genitourinary endoscopy, and any dental procedure that may lead to bleeding.\n- Generalised sepsis, groin drainage, pseudoaneurysm formation, or ill- defined pain after open repair or EVAR should prompt evaluation of graft infection.\n- Gastrointestinal bleeding after open repair or EVAR should prompt evaluation of an aorto-enteric fistula\n- Aortoenteric fistula\n- Aortoenteric fistula is arguably the deadliest complication with a mortality rate as high as 50%, even with operative intervention,\n- Although much more common after open AAA repair aortoenteric fistula may occur after EVAR. Fistulas are more common after open repair because of the difficulty of obtaining complete tissue coverage of the graft. These fistulas may occur anywhere along the GI tract (esophagus, duodenum, small bowel).\n\n- Clinical features\n- nausea, vomiting,\n- back pain\n- hematochezia, and nonspecific abdominal pain.\n- aortoenteric fistulas initially present with minor "herald" gastrointestinal (GI) bleed followed later by catastrophic rapid exsanguination that can be life-threatening. However, this is certainly not always the case, and many patient's initial presentations include dramatic hemorrhage. One described symptomological triad of AEF includes GI bleeding, abdominal pain, and a palpable abdominal mass; nevertheless, in clinical practice, this triad has only been demonstrated in an abysmal 6% to 12% of patients at initial presentation.\n- if patients suffers from an underlying graft infection with secondary fistulization. These patients present with less obvious symptoms such as fever, weight loss, sepsis, and lack of obvious GI hemorrhage. Although patients with EVAR and GI bleeding more commonly have gastritis or ulcer as the cause of bleeding.\n- Investigations\n- Computed tomography angiography (CTA) is the most sensitive, fastest, and least invasive diagnostic modality for this condition\n- Management\n- Vascular surgery consultation. Hemodynamic support and broad- spectrum antimicrobials are required in initial management.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 26-year-old female presented to the ED with her husband with a fractured arm and bruising around her neck. She reported injuries due to fall and wanted to fix her arm. On assessment, you've suspected domestic abuse and discussed with patient to involve the legal authority however patient refuse it. What would be your next step of management?\n- **A)** Apply plaster & cast and Discharge her with advice\n- **B)** Involve safeguarding authority\n- **C)** Persist with his line of enquiry and try to persuade her to seek help\n- **D)** Call the police immediately\n- **E)** Call the hospital security Explanation\n- A competent adult patient's refusal to consent to disclosure should usually be respected, even if that decision leaves the patient at risk of serious harm. However, disclosure without consent may be justified if others (apart from the patient) are at risk.\n\n- It is appropriate sometimes to encourage patients to consent to disclosures that are necessary for their own protection, and to warn them of the risks of refusing to consent.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 24-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department following a skiing accident. He describes catching his ski edge and twisting his ankle. His plain radiograph shows distal fibula fracture. Which of the following actions is most likely to be impaired due to the distal fibula fractu re?\n- **A)** Eversion\n- **B)** Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion\n- **C)** Dorsiflexion and eversion\n- **D)** Plantarflexion and inversion\n- **E)** Eversion and inversion\n- Explanation\n- The patient experienced an ankle twist while skiing, a common mechanism for lateral ankle injuries, including distal fibula fractures. Given the mechanism of injury and the potential for associated soft\n\ntissue damage, the superficial peroneal nerve is the most likely to be injured in this case.\n- The superficial fibular/peroneal nerve may be damaged by fracture of the fibula, or by a perforating wound to the lateral side of the leg. As the muscles that the superficial fibular nerve innervates are evertors, injury to the nerve may result in a loss of eversion. A loss of sensation over the majority of the dorsum of the foot and the anterolateral aspect of the lower leg could also result.\n- There are two muscles in the lateral compartment of the leg; the fibularis longus and brevis (also known as peroneal longus and brevis).\n- The common function of the muscles is eversion — turning the sole of the foot outwards. They are both innervated by the superficial fibular nerve.\n- Damage to the Common Fibular Nerve\n- The common fibular nerve is most commonly damaged by a fracture of the fibula, or the use of a tight plaster cast. The anatomical course of the common fibular nerve causes it to wrap round the neck of the fibular, and so any fractures of the fibular neck can cause nerve palsy.\n- Patients with common fibular nerve damage will lose the ability to dorsiflex the foot at the ankle joint. Hence the foot will appear permanently plantarflexed - known as footdrop. They may also present with a characteristic gait, as a result of the footdrop (for more information, see Walking and Gaits).\n- There will also be a loss of sensation over the dorsum of the foot, and lateral side of the leg. Innervation is preserved on the medial side\n\nof the leg (supplied by the saphenous nerve, a branch of the femoral), and the heel and sole (supplied by the tibial nerve, a branch of the sciatic).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 35-year-old male patient with history of facial trauma brought to the ED with signs of acute airway obstruction. Neither you, nor a senior anaesthetist, can intubate and or insert the SAD, and effectively ventilate the patient with a facemask despite using two-person technique. The ' oxygen saturations have dropped to 86%. What is the single next best step in managing this ' airway?   A\n- Emergency cricothyrotomy\n- **B)** Repeat supraglottic airway device insertion\n- **C)** Re -attempt laryngoscopy with BURP laryngeal manipulation\n- **D)** Wake the patient up\n- **E)** Attempt facemask ventilation with one-person technique\n- Explanation\n- To follow DAS guideline, next step would be cricothyrotomy as plan A to C are failed.\n\n- Surgical airway should be gain as neither you, nor a senior anaesthetist, can intubate and or insert the SAD, and effectively ventilate the patient with a facemask despite using two-person technique. DAS Difficult intubation guidelines — overview 2015 Plan A; "acemask ventilation and :racheal intubatiM1 M a i nt*ning Plan 0: frmt of neck Laryngoscopy Su 'OttiC Failed SAO I at Chea STOP AND THINK Opbons risks 1. up 2, Intubate trachea Via the SAD 3. intubatmg trachea TrEt-eostomy or Wake patient up OAS 1 S\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nAn II-year-old girl presents with periumbilical abdominal pain for about 2 months. There is no associated vomiting, diarrhea, or urinary symptoms. The pain is worse on weekdays but is rare on weekends or vacation days. Her growth and development are normal. On physical examination, she is well-appearing. Her abdomen is soft, non-tender, and non-distended. Bowel sounds are\n\npresent and no masses are appreciated. Laboratory test results, including a urinalysis, FBC, and chemistry panel, are negative. The stool is guaiac negative. What is the next best step in management?\n- **A)** Initiation of an elimination diet\n- **B)** Barium swallow with upper gastrointestinal x-ray\n- **C)** Admit her for further evaluation\n- **D)** Ultrasound of the abdomen\n- **E)** Reassurance and follow-up\n- Explanation\n- In this case, the most appropriate next step in management is reassurance and follow-up\n- Chronic or functional abdominal pain (FAP)\n- In most cases, functional abdominal pain is ill-defined and poorly localized or periumbilical. Episodes of pain usually last for less than one hour, and resolve spontaneously. Pain may be triggered or exacerbated during times of stress (e.g. school transitions, parental divorce and emotional trauma).\n\n-\n- Functional abdominal pain is not a diagnosis of exclusion. However, an appropriate workup for the presenting symptoms should be performed. Red flag symptoms, including but not limited to fever, anemia, weight loss, decreased rate of linear growth, and blood in the stool should be taken seriously and prompt a thorough investigation. Rome IV Criteria can help differentiate between the types of functional abdominal pain Diagnosis Functional abdominal painNot otherwise specified) (now includes FAP disorder, and FAP syndrome Functional Dyspepsia 1 Irritable Bowel syndrome Abdominal Migraine Criteria Episodic or continuous abdominal pain occurring at least 4 times per month\n- Insufficient criteria for IBS, functional dyspepsia or abdominal migraine Cannot fully be explained by another medical condition after appropriate evaluation Does not solely occur with physiological events e.g. menses Can coexist with Other medical conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease Persistent/recurrent epigastric pain No relief on opening bowels No change in stool frequency or form\n- Abdominal pain present for 1 day per week in the last 3 months with symptoms present for at least 6 months prior to diagnosis. Associated with 2 of more of the following at least 25% of the time: Pain related to defecation\n- Associated change in stool frequency Or form Paroxysmal episodes of intense, acute, peri-umbilical pain lasting more than 1 hour\n- Intervening periods of usual health lasting weeks to months Pain interferes with normal activity Associations (2+): anorexia, nausea, vomiting, photophobia, pallor Must occur more than 2 times in the preceding 12 months\n- Management-approach-to-functional-abdominaLpajn\n\n- At the time of presentation, the parents and child may be frustrated or increasingly concerned that the child has a serious disorder. The parents should be asked (non- confrontational) what they are worried is causing the pain, so that their concerns can be directly addressed.\n- Important to clearly state from the beginning that although the pain is real, it does not necessarily mean it is caused by an abnormality in the workings of the bowel.\n- If performing baseline tests - explain that you expect these to be normal as the pick-up rate is less than 5-10%\n- Mention Rome Criteria if it helps\n- Analgesia often has little impact (this is because analgesia rarely impacts on visceral hypersensitivity).\n- Trial of PPI if there features of dyspepsia - but again set expectations that for functional dyspepsia, PPI often has little impact, due to different mechanism.\n- Psychological treatment\n- Distraction has been found in studies to be rnost effective in symptom reduction, together with addressing concomitant anxiety symptoms.\n- Social treatment\n- Explain 'social' aims of treatrnent e.g. going back to school 3 days a week after prolonged absence, going for a walk along the beach once a day after being bedbound.\n- Developing coping mechanisms and relaxation tips to deal with pain e.g. for younger children conceptualising pain as a creature in their tummy and breathing it out.\n- Follow up\n- Most children should be able to be followed up by their GP\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 33-year-old woman presents with headache and a one-year history of amenorrhea and galactorrhea, blurred vision. Her brain MRI reveals a 0.9 cm pituitary mass. Which hormone is likely the cause of the condition?\n- **A)** Prolactin\n- **B)** TSH\n- **C)** Growth hormone\n- **D)** Cortisol\n- **E)** ACTH\n- Explanation\n- The patient's presentation of amenorrhea (absence of periods), galactorrhea (milk production from the breasts), and blurred vision are classic symptoms of hyperprolactinemia, a condition with elevated prolactin levels.\n- Pituitary tumor\n\nNot all pituitary tumors cause symptoms. Sometimes these tumors are\n- found during an imaging test, such as an MRI or a CT scan, that is done for another reason. If they don't cause symptoms, pituitary tumors usually don't need treatment.\n- Pituitary turnor symptoms may be caused by a tumor putting pressure on the brain or on other parts of the body nearby. Symptoms also can be caused by a hormone imbalance. Hormone levels can rise when a pituitary tumor makes too much of one or more hormones. Or a large tumor that disrupts the way the pituitary gland works may cause hormone levels to fall.\n- Symptoms from tumor pressure Headache.\n- Eye problems due to pressure on the optic nerve, especially loss of side vision, also called peripheral vision, and double vision. Pain in the face, sometimes including sinus pain or ear pain.\n- Drooping eyelid.\n- Seizures.\n- Nausea and vomiting.\n-\n- Pituitary tumors that make adrenocorticotropic hormone\n- Pituitary tumors that make adrenocorticotropic hormone are called corticotroph adenomas. Adrenocorticotropic hormone, also called ACT H, causes the adrenal glands to make the hormone cortisol. ACTH tumors trigger the adrenal glands to make too much\n\ncortisol. This causes a condition called Cushing disease. Cushing disease is one cause of Cushing syndrome. Cushing disease symptoms include:\n- Weight gain and fatty tissue deposits around the midsection and upper\n- back. Rounded face.\n-\n- Stretch marks. Thin skin that bruises easily.\n-\n- Thinning of the arms and legs with muscle weakness.\n- Thicker or more visible body hair. Slow healing of cuts, insect bites and infections.\n- Areas of darkened skin.\n- Acne.\n-\n- Changes in menstrual cycles. Sexual problems, including problems with erections and less interest in\n-\n- Pituitary tumors that make growth hormone Pituitary tumors that make growth hormone also are called growth\n- hormone-secreting tumors or somatotroph adenomas. Too much growth hormone leads to a condition known as acromegaly. Acromegaly can cause:\n- Changes in facial features, including larger lips, nose and tongue; longer lower jaw; and wide spaces between teeth. Growth of hands and feet.\n- Thicker skin.\n- More sweating and body odor.\n-\n\nJoint pain.\n-\n- A deeper voice.\n- Children and teens who have too much growth hormone might also grow faster or taller than usual. This condition is called gigantism. Pituitary-tumors-that-make-/utei.nizing-hormone-and-fo".icle:\n- stimulating hormone Luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) are\n- also known as gonadotropins. Pituitary turnors that make these hormones are called gonadotroph adenomas. It's uncommon for these adenomas to make too many hormones that\n- then trigger symptoms. Instead, symptoms from these adenomas usually are due to tumor pressure. If symptoms do happen because of too much LH and FSH, they affect women and men differently. Symptoms in women may include:\n-\n- Change in menstrual cycles. Fertility problems.\n- Enlargement of and pain in the ovaries caused by a condition called\n- ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome. Symptoms in men may include:\n- Enlarged testicles.\n- Higher levels of testosterone.\n-\n- Pituitary-tumors-that-makeprolactin\n- These adenomas are called prolactinomas. Too much of the hormone prolactin can lead to a decrease in the body's levels of sex hormones —\n\nestrogen and testosterone. Too much prolactin affects men and women differently. In women, too much prolactin might cause:\n- Irregular menstrual cycles.\n- Lack of menstrual cycles.\n-\n- Milky discharge from the breasts. Breast tenderness.\n- Problems with fertility.\n- Less interest in sex.\n- In men, too much prolactin can cause a condition called male\n- hypogonadism. Symptoms might include: Problems with erections.\n- Less interest in sex.\n- Breast growth.\n- Problems with fertility.\n- Less body and facial hair.\n-\n- Pituitary tumors that make thyroid-stimulating hormone Pituitary tumors that make thyroid-stimulating hormone are called\n- thyrotroph adenomas. They also may be referred to as thyroid- stimulating hormone-secreting tumors. They cause the thyroid gland to make too much of the hormone thyroxine, also called T-4. That leads to a condition called hyperthyroidism, also known as overactive thyroid disease. Hyperthyroidism can speed up the body's metabolism causing many symptoms. Some of the rnost common include: Weight loss.\n- Rapid or irregular heartbeat.\n-\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Nervousness, anxiety or irritability. Frequent bowel movements. Sweating. Tremor. Sleep problems.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 35-year-old female presents to the ED complaining substernal chest pain that is sharp in nature. The pain is worse when she is lying down on the stretcher and improves when she sits up. She smokes cigarettes occasionally and was told she has borderline diabetes. She denies any recent surgery or travel. Her observations are: BP: 145/85mmHg Pulse: 99bpm RR: 18 breath/minute Temperature: 100.6 F Her ECG is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Stable angina\n- **B)** Endocarditis\n\nC\n- Pericarditis\n- **D)** Hypertrophic obstructive myocardiopathy\n- **E)** Anterior myocardial infarction\n- Explanation\n- The patient clinical features and ECG suggestive of pericarditis.\n- ECG features - Upward concave ST-eIevation and PR depression in most of leads.\n- T-depression and PR elevation in VI and aVR.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 17-year-old boy presents with recent trauma to his face sustained during a physical altercation. The physical examination demonstrates limited jaw mobility due to pain. His Orthopantomogram (OPG) is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Left coronoid and right body fracture\n- **B)** Right angle of mandible and left body fracture\n- **C)** Bilateral condylar fracture\n- **D)** Mandibular ramus fracture\n- **E)** Right body and left coronoid and ramus fracture\n- Explanation\n- OPC shows right body and left coronoid and ramus fracture\n- The distribution of fractures of the mandible are:\n\nCondyle 15-20% Coronoid process 1-2% Ramus 3-9% Angle 20-31% Body 21-40% Symphysis menti 10-15% Alveolar ridge 3-4%\n\nCondyle 30 % Ramus 3 % Angle 25 %\n- Normal OPG Coronoid process 2 % Body 25 % Parasymphyseal / Mental 15 %\n\norOnoi roeess oronoi ody ymphysis ondyle Angle Angle\n- Example 01\n- X-ray shows bilateral condyle fracture RED DOT\n\nIO. A 23-year-old woman presents suffering a snakebite to her right forearm while hiking in mountains. Two large-bore IVs are placed, and she is given a bolus of normal saline. Which of the following is an indication of antivenoum?   A Pain at the site\n- **B)** Puncture wound\n- **C)** Vomiting\n- **D)** Transient hypotension\n- **E)** Bruise at the site\n- Explanation\n- Indications for european viper snake venom antiserum (antivenom)\n- Treatment include systemic envenoming, especially hypotension, ECG abnormalities, vomiting, haemostatic abnormalities, and marked local envenoming such that after bites on the hand or foot, swelling extends beyond the wrist or ankle within 4 hours of the bite.\n- For those patients who present with clinical features of severe envenoming(e.g. shock, ECG abnormalities, or local swelling that has advanced from the foot to above the knee or from the hand to above\n\nthe elbow within 2 hours of the bite), a higher initial dose of the european viper snake venom antiserum is recommended.\n- if symptoms of systemic envenoming persist contact the National Poisons Information Service (NIPS)\n- It patient developed_anaphylactic reaction to european viper snake venom antiserum, rnust be immediately treated with adrenaline/epinephrine. Transient hypotension\n- Definition: A temporary, sudden drop in blood pressure that quickly corrects itself within a few minutes. Causes: Often triggered by changes in posture, such as standing up too quickly (orthostatic hypotension), dehydration, anxiety, pain, certain medications, or hypoglycemia. Symptgms$vlay not cause any noticeable symptoms, or could lead to lightheadedness, dizziness, blurry vision, or even fainting. Significance: Typically not considered a serious condition in itself, unless causing frequent fainting or impacting daily activities.  Hypotension Definition: A sustained, abnormally low blood pressure that doesn't necessarily normalize quickly. Causes: More diverse than transient hypotension, including underlying medical conditions like heart failure, shock, sepsis, severe dehydration, malnutrition, electrolyte imbalances, or endocrine disorders. SymptgmszOften depends on the cause and severity of hypotension. Can range from no symptoms to dizziness, fatigue, confusion, difficulty concentrating, rapid heartbeat, shallow breathing, or even shock and organ failure. Significance: Can be a serious medical condition, especially if caused by a critical underlying illness. Can lead to organ damage or even death if not treated promptly. Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	54	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
66469639-c3f5-4d89-800e-b0c3894858bb	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-53	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 53	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 53\n\nWhat would be your step of management?\n- **A)** Give single dose activated charcoal 50 g per oral\n- **B)** Start NAC and send blood sample\n- **C)** Start IV N-Acetylcysteine 150 mg/kg over 4 hours\n- **D)** Arrange mental health review as the patient does not require medical treatment\n- **E)** Observe him and send investigations after 4 hours of ingestion\n- Explanation\n- Calculate the ingestion dose: 30 x  = 187.5mg/kg\n- In this case patient need to observe and investigations should be send after four hours of ingestion. As ingestion is three hours ago, therefore NAC shouldn't be started without lab result.\n- Nf_single ingestion more than 1 hour but less than 4 hour ago;\n- Delay blood sampling until 4 h post-ingestion\n- Admit to observation ward while sampling awaited\n- After 4 h, obtain venous gas, INR, U&E, I-FT, FBC and paracetamol level and await results.\n\nFeatures Ingestion < 1 h ago AND dose > 150 mg/kg Single ingestion > 1 h but < 4 h ago AND dose > 150 mg/kg Single ingestion 2 4 h AND dose > 150 mg/kg, not possible to act on blood results within 8 h of ingestion Means delay in result obtaining serum PCM level. Other interventions\n- Activated charcoal 50 g PO Delay blood sampling until 4 h post-ingestion Admit to observe After 4 h, send blood sample Delay blood sampling until 4 h post-ingestion Admit to observe\n- After 4 h, send blood sample\n- Start NAC within 1 h of arrival and obtain venous gas, INR, U&E, LFT, FBC and paracetamol level.\n- Note- dose < 150 mg/kg OR dose > 150 mg/kg and it will be possible to act on blood results within 8 h of ingestion then Obtain venous gas, INR, U&E, LFT, FBC and paracetamol level and await result NAC NO NO Yes NO\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 35-year-old male presented to the emergency department following a traumatic injury to the mandible. On examination, there is noticeable loss of movement of lower lip, particularly affecting his ability to smile. Which branch of the facial nerve is most likely involved?\n- **A)** Digastric branch\n- **B)** Posterior auricular branch\n- **C)** Buccal branch\n- **D)** Marginal mandibular branch\n- **E)** Zygomatic branch\n- Explanation\n- Marginal mandibular branch - is the branch of the facial nerve innervates the muscles responsible for movernents of the lower lip, such as the depressor anguli oris and the depressor labii inferioris. Damage to this branch can cause weakness or paralysis of the lower lip, leading to difficulty in rnovernents like smiling.\n- Buccal branch - This branch innervates muscles of the upper lip and cheek, such as the orbicularis oris and buccinator. Damage to this branch would more likely affect movements related to the upper lip and cheek, rather than the lower lip.\n- Cervical branch - This branch innervates the platysma muscle in the neck and is not typically involved in lower lip movement. However, it is depressing the lower lip and corners of the mouth and tensing the skin of the neck.\n- Note - choose the answer based on the options provided in the exam.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA nurse accidentally pricked herself with a needle while drawing blood from a patient known to have hepatitis\n- **B)** The nurse received a full hepatitis B vaccination two years ago. What actions should be taken in this situation?\n- **A)** Reassure As she is fully vaccinated\n- **B)** Check HBS-Ab titres level\n- **C)** Give her hepatitis B vaccine booster\n- **D)** Giver both hepatitis B vaccine and immunoglobulin\n- **E)** Cive her HBIG only\n- Explanation\n- Current guidelines recommend administering a hepatitis B vaccine booster dose if the individual was fully vaccinated one year or more ago. Although checking the Hepatitis B surface antibody (HBS-Ab) level was suggested, the booster dose of the Hepatitis B vaccine should still be given without waiting for the test results.\n- Please note - current guideline is different than 2013 and 2021.\n- Management of needle stick injuries and exposure to blood and high-riskGüÜ¯ fluids\n- The rylost significant risk from needlestick injuries and exposures is the transmission of blood borne viruses.\n- Hurnan immunodeficiency virus (HIV)\n- Hepatitis B\n- Hepatitis C\n- Asses the injury\n\n- In order for an injury to be considered significant, both the type of injury incurred and the body fluid involved must be high-risk. See the table 1 and 2 below for definitions of high-risk injuries and body fluids.\n- Injury-ty_pe\n-\n- High-Risk Injury Percutaneous exposure e.g. needlestick / other sharps injury Exposure on broken skin Mucous membrane exposure (e.g. eye) Human Bite\n- Body fluid High-Risk Body Fluid Blood Blood-stained low risk fluid Semen\n- Vaginal Secretions\n-\n- CSF Pericardial fluid\n- Peritoneal fluid\n- Pleural fluid Saliva associated with dentistry\n- Amniotic fluid\n- Breast milk\n- Synovial fluid Unfixed tissues or organs Low-Risk Injury Splash on intact skin — there is no known risk of BBV transmission from exposures to intact skin. Low-Risk Body Fluid (unless blood- stained) Urine Vomit Saliva Faeces\n\n- Immediate active after exposure\n- Encourage local bleeding of accidental puncture wounds by gently squeezing but do not suck the wound\n- Wash the affected area with soap and warm water but do not scrub the area\n- Treat mucosal surfaces such as mouth or conjunctiva by rinsing with warm water or saline. Water used for rinsing the mouth must not be swal lowed.\n- Further management\n- If the injury is significant (both the type of injury and the body fluid involved rnust be high-risk)\n- If source patient is known, liaise with clinician undertaking the source patient risk assessment to establish: the risk status of the source, whether blood has been taken for BBV testing, and when the blood results will be available.\n- Assess the need for HIV PEP, HBV prophylaxis and follow-up BBV testing (see guidance below).\n- Arrange all apprgpriate follow-up;\n- Appointment with Infectious Disease physicians if HIV PEP started.\n- Occupational Health (Health Care Worker) or GP (others) if further hepatitis B vaccination and follow up BBV testing required.\n- Referral to counselling services if required.\n- Advise health care workers to complete an Datix form and to inform their Occupational Health Dept. as soon as possible.\n\nInstruct the injured Health Care Worker (HCW) to inform occupational\n- health regardless of the outcome of the risk assessment.\n- If-the-jnjury-jsntt-significant Reassure no further action required. If the injury involved contact with\n- HIV positive blood discuss with Infection Disease physician on call.\n- Advise health care workers to complete a Datix form and to inform their Occupational Health Department as soon as possible.\n- If there is potential for repeated exposure to Bloodborne Virus (BBV), check hepatitis B vaccination history. If unvaccinated or incomplete, advise to attend their Occupational Health Department for vaccination. Members of the public requiring further reassurance should be advised\n- to contact their GP who can arrange further support if required\n- HIV assessment and post exposure pæp.hylaxis (PEP)\n- If HIV PEP is to be prescribed, it should be started as soon as possible after exposure, ideally within I hour. It is not recommended beyond 72 hours post exposure. Emergency departments should have packs containing a 3-5 day\n- supply of PEP: Packs should contain:\n- Truvada (245mg tenofovir and 200mg emtricitabine) once daily.\n- Raltegravir 400mg twice per day.\n-\n\n- Caution\n- HIV PEP should not be taken with rifampicin.\n- It should be taken at least 4 hours after taking any vitamin supplements, or calcium, iron, and magnesium supplements.\n- Patients using oral contraception should be advised to use barrier contraception while taking PEP and awaiting results.\n- If pregnancy cannot be excluded then a pre-treatment pregnancy test should be performed, but pregnancy should not preclude the use of PEP. There is no evidence to suggest decreased efficacy or toxicity, and HIV PEP is commonly used during pregnancy.\n\nHas source patient ben ident i fed? YES IS source patient known to be HIV Positive? Has a significant injwy occurred? (see for definition of significant injury) HIV PEP NOT INDICATED Reassure NO Was the from a ward with known HIV positive patients? YES HIV PEP Indicated 2 See guidance starting HIV PEP Discuss with on-call Infectious Disease IS the patient high-risk for HIV inf8:tjon? NO YES HIV PEP may Indicated DÉCuSs with Disease NO or Unable to determine HIV PEP NOT INDICATED\n- Offer to Store Egv testing HIV PEP Indicated guidance starting HIV PEP Discuss with Infectious Disease physician HIV PEP NOT INDICATED\n- to store\n- CHfet support\n- fdm-up BBV testing\n- If me injury invdves contact with HIV positive blCHDd (whether or not it is a signi injury) discuss wit' Infectious Disease Physician on call. Persons who have had an injury which involved exgxjsure to HIV infected bund Should have follow-up post-exposure testing, be Offered specialist and support, have received HIV PEP. When patient is krx»wn to be HIV positive. determine (if possible) What anti-retroviral therapy they are currently receiving (or have taken in the past) and which consultant has responsibility for their care. HIV PEP can be discmtinued if sowce patients HIV antibody test is (taking into account the risk Of a source patmt Note — further detail check British HIV association guideline module. Hepatitis B prgpbylaxis for reported exposure in cidZfiE¯\n\nNon-Significant exposure HBV status of exposed person Unvaccinated Partially vaccinated Fully vaccinated With rima course 1 Known non- responder to HepB vaccine (anti-HBs < 10mlU/ml 1-2 months post- immunisation) HBsAg positive source Accelerated hep B vaccine course\n- HBIG with first dose One dose of hep B vaccine and finish course Hep B vaccine booster if last dose 21year ago Hep B vaccine booster Second dose Of HBIG at one month Significant exposure HBsAg negative Source unknown source Accelerated hep B vaccine One dose of hep B vaccine and finish Consider hep B vaccine booster if last dose 21year ago\n- Consider hep B vaccine booster Second dose Of HBIG at one month Consider course of hep B vaccine Complete course of hep B vaccine\n- No hep B prophylaxis\n- No HBIG Consider hep B vaccine booster Continued risk\n- Start course of hep B vaccine Complete course of hep B vaccine\n- No hep B prophylaxis\n- No HBIG Consider hep B vaccine booster No further risk NO hep B prophylaxis\n- Complete course of hep B vaccine\n- No hep B prophylaxis\n- No hep B prophylaxi Note - An accelerated course of hep B vaccine consists of doses at O, I,\n- and 2 months. Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin\n- Hep B Immunoglobulin is administered as an intramuscular injection.\n- The doses are as follows:\n- Adults and children over 10 years of age — 50010.\n- Children 5-9 years of age 3001U. Newborn and children 0-4 years of age 20010\n-\n\n- HBIG provides passive immunity and can give immediate but temporary protection. It does not have any effect on long term active immunity. It is prepared from donated plasma of immunised and screened human donors and is usually available via the blood transfusion service.\n- Hepatitis B vaccination\n- The hep B vaccines available are Energix B and HBVaxPRO. As with HIV PEP medications Hep B vaccine should be available in the ED. An accelerated course of hep B vaccine consists of doses at O, I, and 2 months.\n- Hepatitis C assessment\n- There is no vaccine or post exposure prophylaxis available for HCV. There are effective treatments, however, and it is important that those exposed receive appropriate follow-up so that treatrnent can be initiated should they become infected.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 24-year-old patient of mine, who was 23 weeks pregnant at the time, suffered a electric shock while cooking in electrical appliance. She said she felt the current in her right hand. What would be the appropriate management steps for this patient?\n- **A)** Admit her under obstetrician\n- **B)** Observe her in the ED for 6 hours\n- **C)** Discharge her with GP follow-up\n- **D)** Perform abdominal IJ/S immediately\n- **E)** Discharge the patient if ECG is normal and the patient is asymptomatic\n- Explanation\n- This patient requires admission under obstetrician for fetal cardiac monitoring Electrical injury\n- Electrocution can occur from domestic or industrial electricity. A Low voltage is one below 1000 volts. A High voltage is above this level. The UK domestic supply is 240 V alternating current 50 Hz.\n- Factors influencing the severity of electrical injury include\n- Whether the current is alternating (AC) or direct (DC) - Contact with alternating current may cause titanic contraction of skeletal muscle. This may prevent release from the source of electricity and may also cause respiratory arrest. It may also cause VF (analogous to A-on-T phenomenon). Asystole is more common after DC shock.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Assess Voltage Low voltage < 1000 v UK household 240v Workshops 380v High voltage >IOOO v Overhead lines 1500v Railway lines 25,000 v Lightning strike 10-200 million v Patient may_present with follow features Cardiovascular\n- Arrhythmias (most common) Heart muscle injury\n- Bradycardia Clotting in blood vessels Respiratory arrest Diaphragm paralysis Tetanic contraction Inhibition of control in brain\n- Neurological Loss of consciousness Impaired recall\n- Spinal cord injury Paralysis Loss of sensations in limbs\n- Skin burns Infection Dehydration Musculoskeletal Fractures/luxations\n\nMuscle damage\n-\n- Rhabdomyolysis Compartment syndrome\n- Renal failure\n- Myoglobin tubular precipitation Generalized hypotension\n- Management-of-e-ectricaLjnjuries Initial management\n- Turn off electrical source (if safe to do so)\n- Perform basic life support if needed\n- Immobilise head and neck (in severe injury)\n- Follow ABCDE approach\n-\n\nLow voltage < 1000 v High voltage >IOOO v Pregnant women Extensive burns\n- Normal ECG If there is no cause for concern, consider prompt discharge Abnormal ECG or/and abnormal urinalyfis\n- All patients will be admitted to critical care for a minimum of 24 hours and further investigations.\n- If Arrhythmia-free for at least 24 hours then discharge\n- Hospital admission Patients with initial loss of consciousness, cardiac anomalies, or high-voltage injury require continuous ECG monitoring Manage organ failure Investi ions Echocardiography\n- blood tests ECG anomaly Arrhythmias Troponin level\n- Coronary angiography With or without: Depending on clinical findings Monitor ECG for at least 24 hours - Explain to patient the occasional risk of delayed cardiac arrhythmias (which can very rarely lead to sudden death). Arrhythmia-free for at least 24 hours — consider discharge\n- Admit to obstetrics — for Fetal cardiac monitoring Admit to burns unit - Specialised surgical care, such as skin grafts\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 30-year-old man, known for poorly controlled diabetes, presents to the ED with abdominal pain associate with vomiting and reduced consciousness. He is started on an insulin infusion. His initial blood glucose level is documented as 32 mmol/L. Which of the following statement is the correct?\n- **A)** Start insulin at fixed rate 0.05 units/ kg/hour\n- **B)** Start 5% dextrose 125mls/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- **C)** Minimum rate of blood glucose reduction that should be blood glucose fall 4 mmol/L/hr\n- **D)** Start 10% dextrose 125mls/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- **E)** Rate of fall of ketones of at least 0.1 mmol/L/hr\n- Explanation\n- JBDS (Joint British Diabetic Society) DKA adult recommends 10% dextrose 125mIs/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- DKA key_points Diagnostic criteria: all three of the following rnust be present:\n- capillary blood glucose above II mmol/L\n- capillary ketones above 3 mmol/L or urine ketones ++ or more\n- venous pH less than 7.3 and/or bicarbonate less than 15 mmol/L\n- Immediate intervention\n- Commence 0.9% sodium chloride solution (use a large bore cannula)\n- via an infusion pump. (Rate is mentioned below in fluid challenge) Commence a fixed rate intravenous insulin infusion (FRIII).\n- (O.lunit/kg/hr based on estimate of weight) 50 units human soluble insulin (Actrapid  or Humulin SO) made up to 50ml withO.9% sodium\n\nchloride solution. If patient normally takes long acting insulin analogue (glargine, detemir, degludec) continue at usual dose and time\n- Fluid challenge\n- Shock patient SBP<90mmHg - Give 500mIs 0.9% sodium chloride solution over 10-15 minutes. If SBP remains low repeat the bolus. Most people require 500-100ml. Once SBP.\n- Once SBP is >90mmHg, give IL 0.9% sodium chloride over the next 60 minutes. The addition of potassium is likely to be required in this second litre of fluid.\n- If SBP>90mmHg - Give IL 0.9% sodium chloride over the first 60 minutes\n- Potassium-replacement Potassium replacement (mmol/L) infusion solution > 5.5\n- 3.5-5.5 < 3.5 Potassium replacement mmol/L of infusion solution Nil\n- 40 mmol/L senior review — additional\n- potassium required\n\nAims of treatment:\n- Rate of fall of ketones of at least 0.5 mmol/L/hr OR bicarbonate rise 3\n- mmol/L/hr and blood glucose fall 3 mmol/L/hr Maintain serum potassium in normal range\n- Avoid hypoglycaemia\n- Consider reducing the rate of intravenous insulin infusion to 0.05 units/\n- kg/hour when glucose falls below 14 mmol/L\n- Add 10% dextrose 125mIs/hr if the glucose falls below 14 mmol/L Accurate fluid balance chart, minimum urine output 0.5ml/kg/hr\n- Resolution of DKA parameters\n- It is defined at ketones < 0.6 mmol/L AND venous pH >7.3 (do not use\n- bicarbonate as a marker at this stage).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 76-year-old man with chronic kidney disease is found to have hyperkalemia with correlating abnormalities on an ECG. You've recommends initiation of dialysis, but the patient refuses. The patient says that he does not want to die but also does not want to live on machines. He states that he knows that without dialysis, he will likely die from elevated potassium levels. He adds that he would rather go home to be with his family and "let nature take its course.\n\nWhat is the next step in the management of this patient?\n- **A)** Admit him under urology team\n- **B)** Refer him to psychiatrist for mental state examination\n- **C)** Treat him in his best interest\n- **D)** Respect his wish and discharge him\n- **E)** Consult with his family\n- Explanation\n- The patient has capacity as this patient can communicate his refusal, understands the link between dialysis and his potassium levels, and that he will likely die without treatrnent. His decision should be respected.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 19-year-old male presents to the ED with left testicular pain. He describes a sudden onset of pain whilst sitting. You suspect testicular torsion. What would be your step of management in this patient?\n- **A)** Urgent intravenous antibiotics to cover potential epididymo-orchitis   B\n- Urgent ultrasound of the testes\n- **C)** Reassure and discharge with safety advice\n- **D)** Refer to urologist for urgent surgical exploration   E\n- Manual detorsion\n- Explanation\n- Twisting (torsion) of the testicle (testis) is an emergency. If the blood supply to testis is cut off for more than about six hours then permanent damage is likely to occur. A careful history and physical examination that is suspicious for testicular torsion may warrant immediate surgical exploration for repair without further delay by diagnostic testing.\n- In the setting of acute testicular torsion, immediate urological consultation for operative repair is essential to optimise testicular salvageability.\n- Occasionally, the twisted testis can be untwisted without needing an operation - this is called manual detorsion. However, this procedure can be painful and often not successful. An operation is usually still needed afterwards to fix the testis in place.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 75-year-old male brought to the ED following a road traffic accident. Patient has been intubated and mechanically ventilated. IV fluid and medications were given. You believe that he is not good candidate for CPR if needed, if resuscitating him would result in poor quality of life. However, his family wants him to be resuscitated and treatment continued.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate action?\n- **A)** Ignore the family wish and do not perform the CPR\n- **B)** Seek legal advice\n- **C)** Perform CPR if required\n- **D)** Discuss your decision with the family\n- **E)** Withdraw all treatment\n- Explanation\n- Patient or patient family (if patient lacks capacity) should be involved regrading the decision of the DNR (do not resuscitate)/Do Not Attempt CPR (DNACPR) and discuss it with them. However, there is no need for their consent as the DNR is decision of the doctors only.\n- CPR (cardiopulmonary resuscitation) is invasive, involving compressions and possible rib fracture, electrical shocks, injected drugs, may damage internal organs, lead to brain damage leading to permanent physical disability and poor quality of life if succeeded. Thus, it is a decision of doctors.\n- If a patient or family wishes to receive CPR and it is your considered judgement that CPR would not be clinically appropriate for the patient, you rnust sensitively explore their reasons for requesting it, their understanding of what it would involve, and their expectations about the likely outcome. As part of this, you should make sure that they have\n\naccurate information about the nature of and, for example, the length of survival and level of recovery that they might realistically expect if they were successfully resuscitated. You should also try to reach agreement; for example, limited CPR interventions could be agreed in some cases. When the benefits, burdens and risks are finely balanced, the patient's request will usually be the deciding factor. If, after discussion, you still consider that CPR would not be clinically appropriate, there is no obligation to provide it in the circumstances envisaged. You must explain your reasons and any other options that may be available to the patient, including their right to seek a second opinion.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 21 -year-old female presents with a painless swelling on the lateral aspect of her neck, which has been present for several months. She recently developed an upper respiratory tract infection (URTI), and the swelling has become slightly tender. Her image is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?   A Lymphadenitis\n- **B)** Thyroglossal duct cyst   C\n- Cystic hygroma\n\nD\n- Branchial cyst\n- **E)** Lipoma\n- Explanation\n- A branchial cyst is often located on the lateral aspect of the neck and can become painful following a URTI. It is typically soft, cystic, and non- transilluminant, and is often located at the anteromedial border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle.\n- Branchial cyst vs Lymphatic Malformation (Cystic Hygroma)\n\nBranchial_cyst Branchial cleft cysts are congenital epithelial cysts, which arise On the lateral part of the neck from a failure of obliteration of the second branchial cleft in embryonic development.\n- Usual age: 15-30 years.\n- Location: Lateral aspect Of the neck, at the anteromedial border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle at the junction of its upper and middle third. Symptoms: Mostly asymptomatic, a history of intermittent swellingand tenderness of the lesion during upper respiratory tract infection may exist. Discharge may be reported if the lesion is associated with a sinus tract. Physical characteristics: Soft, cystic, non- transilluminant. Aspiration findings: May reveal cholesterol contents. Differential dia gnosis Lymphadenopathy (reactive, neoplastic, lymphoma,\n- metastasis) Vascular neoplasms and malformations Capillary hemangioma\n- Carotid body tumor Lymphatic malformation (cystic hygroma) Ectopic thyroid tissue Ectopic salivary tissue Hydatid cyst of the neck Investigations No specific laboratory investigations need to be obtained in the workup of a branchial cleft cyst.\n- Ultrasonography helps to delineate the cystic nature of these lesions. 111.121 A contrast-enhanced CT scan shows a cystic and enhancing mass in the neck. It may aid preoperative planning and identify compromise of local structures Management Medical therapy - Antibiotics are required to treat infections or abscesses related to branchial cleft cysts. Surgical care - Surgical excision is definitive treatment for branchial cleft Cysts. Lymphatic Malformation (Cystic\n- It is a type of Lymphangiomas\n- Usually at infancy, early childhood, or at birth.\n- Soft, cystic, partially compressible. Brilliantlytransilluminant.\n- A comprehensive head and neck examination should be performed to evaluate for any compressive cranial neuropathies related to LM.\n- In children who present with neck LM, attention must be given to assess for tracheal deviation or Other evidence Of impending airway Obstruction.\n- Flexible laryngoscopy performed by an otolaryngologist can be a useful adjunctto evaluate vocal fold mobility as well as airway patency. Differential diagnosis Thyroglossal duct cyst Thyroid goiter Soft-tissue tumors ( rhabdomyosarcoma, lipoma, etc) Neck abscess Differential Diagnoses\n- Branchial Cleft Cyst Mucocele and Ranula Pediatric Teratomas and Other Germ Cell Tumors Investigations\n- Studies have suggested that fluorescent in-situ hybridization (FISH) can be used to evaluate for lymphatic malformation (LM) in antenatal chromosomal analysis (chromosomes 13, 18, 21 , X, and Y).\n- CT and MRI reveal a ringlike margin enhancement with sharp demarcation of cystic areas. Management Medical therapy - treatment of LMs consists of the administration of sclerosing agents, such as OK-432 (an inactive strain of group A Streptococcus pyogenes), bleomycin, pure ethanol, bleomycin, sodium tetradecyl sulfate, and doxycycline. Surgical care - definitive surgery should be\n\n¯¯ performed once the infection has resolved.\n- Incision and drainage or aspiration results in only temporary shrinkage, and subsequent fibrosis can further complicate the resection.  IO. A 35-year-old male patient, with a history of peptic ulcer disease, has been admitted to the Emergency Department due to a significant upper gastrointestinal bleed. Upon examination, he is hemodynamically unstable. The decision has been made to initiate a massive hemorrhage protocol. Which of the following best describe transfusion replacement in 24hrs?\n- **A)** 10 units PRBC 10 units FFP\n- **B)** 5 units pRBC and 5 units FFP\n- **C)** 2 units pRBC3 units FFP\n- **D)** IO units pRBC 5 units platelets O\n- **E)** 10 units PRBC\n- Explanation\n- The classic definition of massive transfusion is 10 units of pRBC transfusion over 24 hours. It only focuses on the number of blood products transfused. Instead, the emphasis should be placed on hemorrhage control which not only includes transfusions, but also\n\nmonitoring blood work/considering targets, administration of other medications including tranexamic acid, keeping the patient warm and source control of the bleeding.\n- Massi—e-he.morrhage protocol refers to rapid administration of large announts of blood products (at least 4 units of PRBC) in fixed ratios pRBC, FFP, PLTS (usually for the management of hemorrhagic shock. To follow MHP (10 units pRBC along with IO units FFP) should be given in 24 hours.\n- clarifying some terminology: "massive transfusion" and "massive hemorrhagicprotocol\n- Massive Transfusion" refers to any situation where a patient is getting a lot of blood transfusions. Different definitions exist, with a common one being 210 in 24 hours. Definitions involving shorter time increments (e.g., >5 units within four hours), could be more useful for identifying massive transfusion early.\n- Massive hemorrhage protocol (MHP) can be seen as an umbrella term encompassing the overall management of massive hemorrhage, including prevention, hemorrhage control, resuscitation, and transfusion\n- It not only includes transfusions, but also monitoring blood work/considering targets, administration of other medications including tranexamic acid, keeping the patient warm and source control of the bleeding.\n- It refers to rapid administration of large arnounts of blood products (at least 4 units of PRBC) in fixed ratios (usually for the management of hemorrhagic shock.\n\n- Note - for more information check massive hemorrhagic protocol\n- Ultramassive transfusion" has been defined as the administration of >20 units PRBC within a 48-hour time span.\n- Note — incase of massive transfusion definition choose IO units pRBC over 24hrs previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	55	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
195e203b-40c9-4da6-bfec-1855817bdc0f	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	moka-high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-50	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 50	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 50\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This is a High-Yield MRCEM SBA practice session from the MOKA clinical revision series.\n> Work through each clinical scenario and select the single best answer.\n\n## About This Session\n\n- **Series:** High-Yield Rapid Clinical Revision\n- **Format:** Single Best Answer (SBA)\n- **Source:** MOKA Clinical SBA Question Bank\n- **Guidelines:** RCEM, NICE, BTS, Surviving Sepsis Campaign\n\n---\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Review all answer rationales carefully after completing the quiz.\n> Focus on understanding the clinical reasoning, not just memorising answers.\n	\N	[]	\N	f	50	published	2026-11-02 06:29:32.229679+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
855fd2ec-3829-4b98-b003-fb0115c95df7	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-55	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 55	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 55\n\n- **A)** Normal shoulder x-ray\n- **B)** Scapular fracture C\n- Luxatio erecta\n- **D)** Posterior shoulder dislocation\n- **E)** Anterior shoulder dislocation\n- Explanation\n- The X-ray shoulder shows inferior shoulder dislocation. This condition is also known as luxatio erecta because the arm appears to be permanently held upward, in fixed abduction.\n- Mechanism of injury\n- sudden forceful arm hyperabduction\n- less commonly, direct loading force on a fully abducted arm, with an extended elbow and pronated forearm\n- The humeral head is forced against the acromion, usually with resultant inferior glenohumeral capsule rupture and rotator cuff disruption.\n- Radiographic findings\n- The humeral head is displaced directly below and slightly medial to the glenoid fossa, with the arm often in marked abduction (luxatio\n\nerecta).\n- An inferior dislocation can mimic a subcategory of glenohumeral dislocation known as subglenoid anterior dislocation, where the humeral head rests directly inferior to the glenoid in the AP and lateral projections . It is distinguished from the latter by the humeral shaft's position parallel to the scapular spine (shoulder blade).\n- MRI findings\n- rotator cuff tears\n- injuries to the glenoid labrum\n- injuries to both the anterior and posterior band of the inferior glenohumeral ligament(lCHL)\n- bone bruisesor impaction fractures (Hill-Sachs defect) at the superolateral aspect of the humeral head\n- Management\n- Close reductions\n- Closed reduction and immobilization indicated in:\n- inactive elderly patients may be considered in the absence of acute traumatic rotator cuff tear\n- Techniques\n- Traction-countertraction - similar technique as for anterior shoulder dislocations\n- Two-step technique - converts inferior dislocation to anterior dislocation\n- clinician stands at patient's head, pushes laterally on humerus (one hand) while pulling superiorly on medial epicondyle (other hand),\n\nwhich should rotate HH from inferior to anterior around the glenoid rim\n- when successful, shoulder position will have changed from abduction to adduction against chest wall\n- then use any anterior-dislocation technique to reduce shoulder\n- post-reduction - brief period of immobilizer\n- followed by ROM exercises assuming intact rotator cuff\n- physical therapy should focus on periscapular and rotator cuff strengthening.\n- Arthroscopic or open repair\n- Indications - active younger patients\n- Advantage of arthoroscopic approach - allows assessment and addressing multiple concomitant pathologies including capsulolabral damage, traumatic rotator cuff tear.\n- prompt surgical repair for acute RTC tear typically recommended\n- prolonged non-operative treatrnent may result in significant retraction and rapid progression to nonrepairable condition.\n- Complications\n- Axillary nerve palsy (most common) - may resolve with reduction of shoulder.\n- Brachial plexopathy - will usually resolve following reduction of shoulder and observation\n- Axillary artery thrombosis - may occur late\n- Rotator cuff tear - common, especially in older patients, but also in young patients as well\n\n- prompt MRI warranted in young patients following reduction to avoid missed diagnosis/ treatment\n- Note — X-ray can be different in exam, therefore check more relevant to inferior shoulder dislocations.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 67-year-old male presents to the Emergency Department with severe, diffuse abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting for the past 48 hours. He has a history of chronic kidney disease and is on diuretics for heart failure. On examination, his abdomen is distended and tender with hypoactive bowel sounds. Blood tests reveal a potassium level of 2.4 mmol/L. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Paralytic ileus\n- **B)** Acute pancreatitis\n- **C)** Gastroenteritis\n- **D)** Sigmoid volvulus\n- **E)** Small bowel obstruction\n- Explanation\n\n- In this scenario, the patient's symptoms, combined with hypokalemia and a history of diuretic use, point toward paralytic ileus. Supine abdominal radiograph shows proportional dilation of the large and small bowel with no clear transition point.\n- Ileus\n- Ileus is a slowing of gastrointestinal motility accompanied by distention, in the absence of a mechanical intestinal is a diagnosis of exclusion after bowel obstruction has been ruled out.\n- It usually occurs in response to physiological stress, including surgery (usually gastrointestinal), sepsis, metabolic derangements, and gastrointestinal diseases.\n- Risk factors\n- Abdominal surgery\n- non-abdominal surgery; acute conditions (e.g., myocardial infarction, pneumonia, acute cholecystitis, pancreatitis, trauma); and systemic conditions (e.g., sepsis).\n- Medications known to slow motility include:\n- Anticholinergics. Opioids.\n- Tricyclic antidepressants. Phenothiazines.\n- Electrolyte imbalancesthat may be involved include: Hypokalemia Hyperca Icemia\n\n- Hypermagnesemia Hypophosphatemia\n- Clinical features\n- nausea and vomiting\n- abdominal distention or tenderness - Present in 71% of patients with postoperative ileus.\n- no features of mechanical obstruction or peritoneal inflammation (e.g., abdominal hernia, peritoneal signs)\n- Constipation - Severe constipation with no passage of stool or flatus is a common finding.\n- discomfort and abdominal pain - Discomfort from gaseous distention is common, with pain. Cramping or colicky pain is unusual in ileus.\n- decreased or hypoactive bowel sounds\n- Hypovolaemia - Tachycardia, hypotension, and oliguria are specific findings in this condition.\n- l.nyestigation\n- FBC - may show normal or slightly elevated WBC\n- Serum electrolytes — shows hypokalaemia, hypokalaemia, hypo- chloraemia, alkalosis\n- Serum magnesium — hyper-magnesaemia may be present. hyper- magnesaemia, has been associated with ileus.\n- urea and creatinine - elevated blood urea and serum creatinine may occur with dehydration\n- arterial blood gases - alkalosis may present in dehydrated patients, acidosis may be present in intestinal ischaemia.\n\n- Plain radiograph - generalized, uniform, gaseous distension of the large and small bowel. involvement of large bowel and lack of a transition point help distinguish it from small bowel obstruction. when localized, there may be a sentinel loop.\n- abdomen and pelvis CT scan (with intravenous contrast and oral water-soluble contrast) - CT scan using oral water-soluble contrast and intravenous contrast is the method of choice in differentiating prolonged ileus and mechanical obstruction.ln ileus, the CT scan shows distention of the stomach, fluid filled intestines, and no evidence of a transition zone between dilated and collapsed bowel (a key indicator of obstruction). It is important to distinguish between small bowel obstruction and ileus; small bowel obstruction may progress to a more serious condition with bowel ischaemia if there is a twist of the intestines or vascular compromise.\n- Other-inyestigation-to-be-conside.red\n- serum LFTs\n- serum lipase or amylase\n- gastric emptying study\n- small bowel series with oral water-soluble contrast\n- Differentials Mechanical bowel obstruction\n- Pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)\n- Gastroenteritis\n- Pancreatitis\n- Acute cholecystitis\n- Bowel perforation\n- intra-abdominal abscess\n\n- Peritonitis\n- Intestinal pneumatosis\n- Management\n- Management_gtpostoperative ileus\n- nil by mouth\n- intravenous fluids - UK guidelines recommend replacing fluid losses volume for volume using Hartmann's solution (Ringer's lactate/acetate solution) according to body weight. An intravenous physiological saline with appropriate additions of potassium can be used as an alternative.\n- Electrolyte abnormalities (hypokalaemia, hypo-chloraemia, alkalosis, and hyper-magnesaemia) may be a consequence of ileus or an exacerbating factor.\n- Reduction in opioid analgesia ± replacement with non-opioid analgesia - such as paracetamol, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as ketorolac, and analgesics and local anaesthetics administered via epidural. The use of epidural analgesia helps to prevent postoperative ileus, although in practice can be challenging due to its impact on the patients' mobility.\n- Consider nasogastric tube - Place a nasogastric (NG) tube for decompression of the gut in patients with significant abdominal distention and repeated vomiting or who are at high risk of aspiration.\n- Management-of-nonsurgical-cause\n- nil by mouth\n- intravenous fluids (mentioned as above)\n\n- management of the underlying condition(s) - Treat any underlying condition, such as sepsis, intra-abdominal infections, or other acute/systemic illnesses associated with intestinal hypomotility such as diabetes mellitus, Chagas disease, scleroderma, and neurological diseases.\n- Consider nasogastric tube - Place a nasogastric (NG) tube for decompression of the gut in patients with significant abdominal distention and repeated vomiting or who are at high risk of aspiration.\n- Management of ileus lasting 4 days or longer post-surgery_ (prolonged-ileus)\n- Parenteral nutrition - Give parenteral nutrition to patients who do not have any oral intake for more than 7 days. Consult with a dietitian as it is important to avoid refeeding syndrome, which can be fatal in patients with malnutrition.\n- Patients with prolonged postoperative ileus (lasting 4 days or longer post-surgery) may be nil by mouth for several weeks.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA young woman presents to the Emergency Department accompanied by her husband, complaining of neck pain. During the examination, multiple bruises are observed around her neck. The husband reports that she fell from stairs a week ago and mentions that he holds the power of attorney. What next step would you take in managing this situation?\n- **A)** Inform the police immediately\n- **B)** Discharge her with painkiller\n- **C)** Check the patient medical record\n- **D)** Admit the patient for further evaluation\n- **E)** Husband isn't carrying the attorney so ask him to show the proof\n- Explanation\n- The presence of neck pain and bruises observed during the examination, along with the husband's inconsistent explanation and power of attorney claim raise serious concerns about potential domestic abuse. The patient's safety is paramount, and informing the police immediately is essential to ensure her protection.\n- If informing police option isn't given then choose contact local safeguarding authority.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 23-year-old girl presented to the ED with complaint of pain in her right arm she is unable to adduct her thumb. What is the synonyms of this presentation?\n- **A)** Wrist drop\n- **B)** Colles fracture\n- **C)** Carpal tunnel syndrome\n\nD\n- Froment's sign\n- **E)** Smith fracture Explanation\n- Froment's sign\n- froment sign is used to evaluate the Ulnar nerve motor weakness due to entrapment at the elbow or at the wrist, it is used to check the function of the adductor pollicis muscle.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 3-year-old child brought to the ED by his mother with complaint of fever, bilateral conjunctival injection. On further assessment, he has unilateral cervical lymphadenopathy. you suspect Kawasaki disease. Which of the following is the earliest abnormality in this patient?\n- **A)** Coronary aneurysm\n- **B)** Leukopenia\n- **C)** Thrombocytosis\n- **D)** Increased S.albumin\n- **E)** Anemia Explanation\n- In the acute stage (early stage) , a mild to moderate normochromic anaemia is observed, along with a moderate to alarmingly raised white blood cell count (leukocytosis) with a left shift.\n- During the subacute stage, platelet count elevation (thrombocytosis) is the outstanding marker. It begins to rise in the second week and continues to rise during the third\n- Kawasaki disease\n- It's an acute, febrile, self-limiting, systemic vasculitis of unknown origin that almost exclusively affects young children.\n- Risk factors\n- Asian ancestry\n- age 3 months to 4 years male sex\n- Diagnostic-features\n- Fever for more than 5 days at least 4 of the following features\n- Polymorphic rash Bilateral conjunctival injection\n- mucosal erythema with strawberry tongue.\n\n- Swelling and erythema of the hands and feet occur in the acute stage, followed by desquamation in the second week.\n- Unilateral non-purulent cervical lymphadenopathy is present in about 40% of cases.\n- Coronary aneurysms develop in 20% to 25% of untreated patients.\n- Investigation:\n- FBC\n- Haemoglobin low for age (Mild-moderate normocytic anemia present in acute phase)\n- (leukocytosis in acute phase)\n- Platelet > 450,000/mm ( thrombocytosis present in subacute phase)\n- Serum albumin<3.O g/dl\n- serum erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)\n- serum CRP\n- LFTs (Elevated alanine aminotransferase)\n- ECG\n- Echocardiogram( perform during and after acute illness and if echo reveal cardiac abnormality then refer child to paediatrician for further cardiac work-up\n- Management;\n- intravenous immunoglobulin and/or aspirin. In resistant cases, corticosteroids or a tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha inhibitor may be indicated.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 30-year-old female presents to the Emergency Department accompanied by her husband, complaining of abdominal pain. She does not speak English and her husband translates to both of you. During her abdominal examination, multiple bruises are observed. The husband reports that she fell from stairs a week ago. What next step would you take in managing this situation?\n- **A)** Admit the patient and request an independent translator\n- **B)** Call the hospital security\n- **C)** Call the police immediately to arrest her husband\n- **D)** Discharge her with painkiller\n- **E)** Discharge her with painkiller\n- Explanation\n- The presence of abdominal pain and bruises observed during the examination, along with the husband's inconsistent explanation raise serious concerns about potential domestic abuse.\n- Admitting the patient would prevent her contact with her husband who might continue hurting her.it will also give a chance arrange an independent translator via NHS language line so you an understand\n\nthe real story as the patient will most likely speak freely away from her husband.\n- In this case police/safeguarding shouldn't be informed before knowing the whole story.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 36-year-old male presented to the ED with a history of snake bite. He experienced severe right lower limb pain with tenderness. On examination his limb is swelled and paled. His is observations are BP  mmHg HR 105 bpm RR 20 breath/min What would be your appropriate step of management?\n- **A)** IV fluids\n- **B)** IV antibiotics   C\n- Antivenom\n- **D)** Urgent referral to surgery\n- **E)** Fasciotomy\n\nExplanation\n- Indications-for-european-yiper-snake-yeno.m-antise.r.un(anti.uenom)\n- Treatment include systemic envenoming, especially hypotension, ECG abnormalities, vomiting, haemostatic abnormalities, and marked local envenoming such that after bites on the hand or foot, swelling extends beyond the wrist or ankle within 4 hours of the bite.\n- For those patients who present with clinical features of severe envenoming(e.g. shock, ECG abnormalities, or local swelling that has advanced from the foot to above the knee or from the hand to above the elbow within 2 hours of the bite), a higher initial dose of the european viper snake venom antiserumis recommended.\n- if symptoms of systemic envenomingpersist contact the National Poisons Information Service (NIPS)\n- Snake bite\n- Envenoming from snake bite is uncommon in the UK.\n- Many exotic snakes are kept, some illegally, but the only indigenous venomous snake is the adder (Vipera berus).\n- The bite may cause local and systemic effects\n- Local effects\n- ocal effects include pain, swelling, bruising, and tender enlargement of regional lymph nodes, Systemic effects Systemic effects include early anaphylactic symptoms (transient hypotension with syncope, angioedema, urticaria, abdominal colic, diarrhoea, and vomiting), with later persistent or recurrent hypotension, ECG abnormalities, spontaneous systemic bleeding,\n\ncoagulopathy, adult respiratory distress syndrome, and acute renal failure.\n- Fatal envenoming is rare but the potential for severe envenoming rnust not be underestimated.\n- Management\n- Give analgesia\n- Early anaphylactic symptoms should be treated with intramuscular adrenaline/epinephrine\n- Indications-for-european-yiper-snake-ye.nom-an.tiserum(antiye.nom)\n- Treatrnent include systemic envenoming, especially hypotension, ECG abnormalities, vomiting, haemostatic abnormalities, and marked local envenoming such that after bites on the hand or foot, swelling extends beyond the wrist or ankle within 4 hours of the bite.\n- For those patients who present with clinical features of severe envenoming(e.g. shock, ECG abnormalities, or local swelling that has advanced from the foot to above the knee or from the hand to above the elbow within 2 hours of the bite), a higher initial dose of the european viper snake venom antiserumis recommended.\n- if symptoms of systemic envenomingpersist contact the National Poisons Information Service (NIPS)\n- lipatient developed anaphylactic reaction to european viper snake venom antiserum, must be immediately treated with adrenaline/epinephrine.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 30-year-old construction worker, was involved in a serious workplace accident. While working on an elevated platform, he lost his balance and fell, landing on his back. The impact was severe, resulting in a spinal cord injury. Which of the following is correct regarding complete spinal cord injury?\n- **A)** Some preservation of function below the complete level can sometimes occur\n- **B)** Sensory perception is preserved below the neurological level, but motor function is not\n- **C)** The spinal level of injury always exactly aligns with the level of bony injury seen on imaging\n- **D)** The motor level always corresponds to the last myotome with full strength (5/5)\n- **E)** The sensory level precisely matches the lowest dermatome with preserved sensation\n- Explanation\n- Complete injuries can have a zone of partial preservation (ZPP) below the complete level, where some motor and sensory function remains.\n\n- Regarding the rnotor level always corresponds to the last myotome with full strength (5/5)- This is not always true. In a complete spinal cord injury, there may be some weakness or absent reflexes above the last myotome with full strength.\n- The severity of spinal cord damage may not precisely match the level of bony injury seen on imaging due to factors like vertebral displacement or ligamentous support.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 6-year-old child with a history of meningitis has received appropriate antibiotic therapy in the hospital. What is the next step in management?\n- **A)** Perform a repeat lumbar puncture to confirm bacterial clearance\n- **B)** Start antiviral therapy as a precaution\n- **C)** Discharge the child home with oral antibiotics\n- **D)** Arrange for auditory testing to assess for potential hearing loss\n- **E)** Initiate corticosteroid therapy to reduce inflammation\n- Explanation\n- Children who have had bacterial meningitis are at risk of developing hearing loss, which can occur as a complication. Therefore, it is crucial to arrange for a hearing test as part of the follow-up care.\n\n- Complication of acute bacterial meningitis\n- Although overall mortality from acute bacterial meningitis has fallen in recent years, there has been no change in the rate of complications.\n- Complications are more common following pneumococcal meningitis and occur in about 30% of people compared with 7% with meningococcal meningitis. The frequency of complications is much higher in meningococcal septicaemia (up to 57%).\n- Cerebral infarction occurs in one in four people with bacterial meningitis — this leads to focal neurological deficits. These are present in 50% of people on admission and develop during the clinical course in the others.However, neurological complications may also be caused by other pathologies, such as subdural empyema, cerebral abscess, or intracerebral bleeding.\n- Neurological complications include:\n- Hearing loss (34%) - This has been reported in up to 19% of infants, 13% of children, 12% of adolescents, and 8% of adults with meningococcal meningitis.\n- Seizures (13%) - This has been reported in up to 5% of infants, 9% of children, and 2% of adolescents with meningococcal meningitis.\n- Cognitive impairment (9%).\n- Motor deficits (12%).\n- Visual impairment (6%).\n\n- Physical complications include: Amputations — these have been reported in up to 8% of children, and 3% of adults/adolescents with meningococcal meningitis.\n- Skin scars — these have been reported in up to 55% of children, 18% of adolescents, and 2% of adults with meningococcal meningitis.\n- Other complications include:\n- Hydrocephalus (7%).\n- Reduced quality of life.\n- Anxiety.\n- Learning difficulties.\n- Emotional and behavioural difficulties 10 / 10 IO. A 25-year-old male presents with pain in the midfoot following an injury sustained while riding a horse. Inspection of the affected foot reveals significant swelling, ecchymosis, and obvious anatomic deformity and there is pain with palpation over the dorsal mid-foot, more specifically the tarsometatarsal joints. His foot image is shown. What is the mechanism of this injury A\n- Hyperdorsiflexion\n- **B)** External rotation and dorsiflexion   C\n- Hyperplantarflexion\n- **D)** Crush injury\n- **E)** Abduction and dorsiflexion\n- Explanation\n- injury mechanisms are varied and include:\n- direct crush injury or an indirect load onto a plantarflexed foot.\n- The mechanisms of Lisfranc injuries in football players and horse riders can differ slightly due to the specific stresses and movements involved in each sport:\n- Football players:\n- Planting and twisting: When a player firmly plants their foot and suddenly changes direction, a combined force of plantarflexion (downward bending) and axial rotation (twisting) can occur. This can stress the Lisfranc joint beyond its limits, leading to ligament tears and potential dislocation.\n- Horse riders:\n- Stirrup entrapment: This is a classic mechanism for Lisfranc injuries in horse riders. When the rider falls from the horse with their foot still in the stirrup, the foot is forced into extreme plantarflexion and abduction (pulling outwards) as the horse pulls away. This can cause severe tears and disruption of the Lisfranc joint ligaments and bones.\n- Landing after jumps: High impacts from landing after jumps can also stress the Lisfranc joint, particularly if the landing is uneven or awkward\n\ne previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	57	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
69d2f837-1f16-43a6-8be1-5a9d3a1ddbba	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-56-2	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 56	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 56\n\nA Talus fracture\n- **B)** Navicular fracture   C\n- Normal foot x-ray\n- **D)** Calcaneal fracture\n- **E)** Lisfranc injury\n- Explanation\n- It's a normal foot x-ray in children/adolescents.\n- Unlike an adult heel bone the heel bone in a child is in two parts connected by a cartilage growth plate.\n- Calcaneal fracture\n- MOI: Calcaneal fractures most often follow a fall from height directly onto the heels.\n- Always exclude associated injuries of the cervical and lumbar spine, pelvis, hips, or knees\n- Calcaneal fractures are usually bilateral\n- Clinical features: swelling, bruising, and tenderness over the calcaneum.\n\n- Diagnosis;\n- Plain radiographs - specifically the lateral view, are needed for fracture diagnosis. The Boehler angle is measured using the lateral view and represents the intersection of two lines\n- CT - is the modality of choice to evaluate calcaneal fracture. It can show the extent and extra- or intra-articular components of the fracture and hematoma along the sole of the foot (Mondor sign). Intra-articular fractures are often classified using the Sanders classification system, which is one of the only systems that correlates well with patient outcome\n- Note - If bilateral calcaneal fractures are seen, then the spine should also be evaluated for fracture as the mechanism of injury is often a large load to the axial skeleton, such as a fall from height.\n- If an intra-articular calcaneal fracture is seen, the images should be scrutinized for a lateral malleolar fleck sign (ankle), which raises the likelihood of peroneal tendon instability.\n- Management: Refer all fractures to an orthopedic\n\nChild Adult\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 50-year-old farmer was presented to the emergency department after sustaining an injury to his thigh while working in his farm's field. He had accidentally stepped on a rusty piece of metal. The wound was deep and dirty, but he initially did not seek immediate medical attention. Over the course of the next 48 hours, he experienced increasing pain, swelling, development of foul- smelling and blisters in his thigh with rapid spread. What would be the appropriate next management strategy?\n- **A)** Broad spectrum antibiotics\n- **B)** Pain killer\n- **C)** Irrigation with NS\n- **D)** Refer to surgery for debridement\n- **E)** IV fluids\n- Explanation\n- Gas gangrene requires intensive supportive care+ surgical debridement ± amputation\n- Aggressive and thorough surgical debridement is mandatory to improve survival, preserve limbs, and prevent complications.\n- Antibiotics - Currently, a combination of penicillin and clindamycin is widely used. Protein synthesis inhibitors (e.g., clindamycin, chloramphenicol, rifampicin, tetracycline) are effective because they inhibit the synthesis of clostridial exotoxins and lessen the local and systemic toxic effects of these proteins.\n- Gangrene\n- Gangrene is complication of necrosis characterised by the decay of body tissues. Results from ischaemia, infection, or trauma (or a combination of these processes).\n- There are two major categories: infectious gangrene (wet gangrene) and ischaemic gangrene (dry gangrene).\n- Ischaemia may result from either arterial or venous compromise, and may be an acute or chronic process (or a combination of both). Critically insufficient blood supply is the most common cause of\n\ngangrene, and is often associated with diabetes and long-term smoking. Risk factors\n- diabetes mellitus, atherosclerosis, smoking, hypercoagulable states,\n- drug abuse, malignancy, renal disease, traurna or abdominal surgery, alcoholism, contaminated wounds (infectious gangrene), and malnutrition\n- Clinical features pain\n- Oedema or swelling\n- skin discoloration\n- crepitus (gas gangrene)\n- diminished pedal pulses and ankle-brachial index (ischaemic\n- gangrene) Iow-grade fever and chills (infectious gangrene)\n- Investigations\n- FBC Blood culture\n- CRP\n- U&Es Serum LDH\n- Coagulation screen\n-\n\n- CT affected site\n- MRI affected site\n- Doppler U/S\n- Management\n- confirmed-type-Land-Ll-necrotising-fasciitis-(polymicrobia/)\n- intensive supportive care + surgical debridement ± amputation\n- Anti i —b-Q.ti.cs=Options include vancomycin or linezolid combined with either: piperacillin/tazobactam; a carbapenem; ceftriaxone plus metronidazole; or a fluoroquinolone plus metronidazole.\n- consider in type-II necrotising fasciitis can be considered as adjuvant therapy after prompt debridement in patients with necrotising soft tissue infections if no improvement in clinical condition is seen.\n- Gas gangrene - intensive supportive care+ surgical debridement ± amputation\n- Aggressive and thorough surgical debridement is mandatory to improve survival, preserve limbs, and prevent complications.\n- In patients with extremity involvement, fasciotomy could be necessary to treat compartment syndrome, and it should be done immediately after the diagnosis is made. Daily debridement is necessary, and it is extremely important to remove all necrotic and infected tissue.\n- a combination of penicillin and clindamycin is widely used.\n\n- Protein synthesis inhibitors (e.g., clindamycin, chloramphenicol, rifampicin, tetracycline) are effective because they inhibit the synthesis of clostridial exotoxins and lessen the local and systemic toxic effects of these proteins.\n- ischaem.i.c-gangrene=intravenous heparin 80 units/kg intravenous bolus, followed by 18 units/kg/hour infusion.\n- Consider surgical revascularisation ± amputation in ischaemic gangrene with threatened or non-viable extremity.\n- Consider thrombolytic therapy in ischaemic gangrene with vial extremity\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 59-year-old man is brought to the ED with a two days history of shortness of breath. He has a past medical history of COPD. On examination, RR was 28 breath per minutes for which BiPAP was attached. According to BTS guideline which of the following is an indication to NIV?\n- **A)** PH less 7.25\n- **B)** RR>28 breath per minute\n- **C)** Reduced conscious level\n- **D)** PC02>6.5 kPa O\n- **E)** P0212.6 kPa\n- Explanation Indication of NIV in COPD\n- pH <7.35\n- pC02> 6.5 RR>23\n- If persistent after bronchodilators and controlled oxygen therapy Note: NIV usage evidence is less clear for patients with arterial pH\n- <7.25.\n- pH<7.25 has been suggested as a level below which IMV should be considered and <7.15 as the level that IMV is indicated (following initial resuscitation and use of controlled oxygen).\n- ('MY)-in-acute exacerbation of COPD (AECOPD) Imminent respiratory arrest\n- Severe respiratory distress\n- Failure of or contra-indications to non-invasive ventilation (NIV)\n- Persisting pH<7.15 or deterioration in pH despite NIV\n- Depressed consciousness (Glasgow Coma Score <8)\n-\n\n- Note - you can find similar question in another quiz. you need to pick the exact answer as mentioned in indication of NIV. forexample in previous question pC02>8.5 was given and RR>25 hence closest is the RR, therefore to choose RR>25 if other option isn't matched. However if PH<7.35 or pC02>6.5 and RR>25 given, in this case PH or PC02 should be marked over RR>25.  4. 28-year-old female presents to the emergency department with a 3-day history of progressive weakness in her legs. She describes difficulty climbing stairs and getting out of a chair. On examination, she has reduced power in both legs, absent knee and ankle reflexes, and mild sensory disturbance in her feet. You suspect Guillain-Barré Syndrome. Which of the following investigations is the most important to manage this patient?\n- **A)** Chest X-ray\n- **B)** Electrocardiogram C\n- Forced Vital Capacity\n- **D)** Serum creatine kinase\n- **E)** Arterial blood gas analysis\n\n- Explanation\n- Respiratory muscle weakness is a serious complication of CBS, and monitoring respiratory function is crucial.\n- FVC measures the maximum volume of air a person can forcefully exhale after a full inhalation. It's a sensitive indicator of respiratory muscle strength and can detect early signs of respiratory compromise in CBS, even before changes in blood gases or clinical signs become apparent.\n- 1st investigations to order in GBS\n- The diagnosis of GBS is largely clinical, but the standard investigations are listed below.\n- Nerve conduction studies — revealed prolonged distal and F-wave latencies and reduced conduction velocities, H reflex prolonged or absent.\n- lumbar puncture - elevated CSF protein, normal/slightly high lymphocytes (<50 cells/mm3).\n- A raised CSF protein (>0.4 g/L) with normal white cell count is the typical CSF finding in CBS.\n- LFTs — reveal elevated aspartate aminotransferase and alanine aminotransferase as high as 500 U/L; bilirubin may be transiently elevated but rarely high enough to cause jaundice\n- Spirometry/Lung function test - may show reduced vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure. Patients with an FVC less than 20mL/kg are candidates for prophylactic intubation and mechanical ventilation.\n\n- This test Should be carried out at 6-hour intervals initially at the bedside. Intensive care unit monitoring and elective. Lung function tests are vital for safe management of the patient.\n- intubation should be considered if any of the following is present:\n- vital capacity <20 mL/kg (odds ratio 15.0)\n- maximal inspiratory pressure worse than -30 cmH20\n- maximal expiratory pressure <40 cmH20; or reduction of 30% or more\n- of vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure. Note - The key diagnostic tests for the ED are the lumbar puncture,\n- along with a screen of blood tests\n- Other-investigations-to-be-considered anti-ganglioside antibody\n- Blood tests: FBC, ESR, U+E, LFT, CPK, CAP, glucose, Mg, calcium and phosphate Stool culture (for C jejuni and others)\n-\n- ECC spinal MRI\n- Borrelia burgdorferi serology\n- Differential\n-\n- Transverse myelitis Myasthenia gravis Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)\n- Botulism\n-\n\nPolymyositis\n- Vasculitic neuropathy\n-\n- Management\n- IVIG or PE (plasma exchange) Other treatment\n- Pain relief: try to avoid opioids\n-\n- Chronic neuropathic pain: amitriptyline or antiepileptic drugs DVT prophylaxis Monitoring\n- Lung function initially every four/six hours\n-\n- ECG Autonomic function — pupils and ileus\n-\n- Check for swallowing dysfunction Complications\n- Respiratory failure\n-\n- Bladder areflexia Paralysis\n- Fatigue\n-\n- DVT\n\n- Note - check more detail in Transverse myelitis and GBS table in quiz 43\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 28-year-old woman presented to the ED after she sustained a household chemical burn. She has a partial thickness burn covering the anterior of her trunk and her skin is blistered. Which one of the following is not advisable?\n- **A)** Analgesia   B\n- Antibiotic and tetanus\n- **C)** This patient can be referred to burn center\n- **D)** Irrigation with NS and Cold compression\n- **E)** Fluid can be given\n- Antibiotics: There is no indication for prophylactic antibiotics in the early postburn period. Reserve use of antibiotics for the treatment of infection.\n- Tetanus: Determination of the patient's tetanus immunization status and initiation of appropriate management is important Fluid resuscitation:\n\n- Lady has sustained burn on anterior of trunk that is 18% of the total TBSA. Adults with burns TBSA and children with burns TBSA require fluid resuscitation. Analgesia; Recommended\n- Non-pharmacological\n- cooling the burn under cold running water and covering with cling film. Cling film also helps to reduce heat loss and the risk of infection.\n- Pharmacological: paracetamol and NSAIDs or Opiods and ketamine Irrigation with NS and cold compression\n- Recommended\n- Irrigate and cool thermal burns with cool (150C) running tap water for 20 minutes.\n- Do not use ice/iced water/ice packs/gel-based cooling products for this purpose\n- Clean the wound with normal saline and cover any areas of skin loss with cling film\n- Do not use cling film on the face\n- Do not wrap cling film circumferentially around a limb (this creates a constrictive eschar)\n- Cover chemical burns with a non-adherent dressing Referral-to-specialist-burn-center;\n- All burns  TBSA in children or TBSA in adults\n- All full thickness burns All circumferential burns\n- Any burn not healed in 2 weeks\n\n-\n-\n-\n- Any burn with suspicion of non-accidental injury should be referred to a burn unit/centre for expert assessment within 24 hours All burns to hands, feet, face, perineum, or genitalia Any chemical, electrical or friction burn Any cold injury Any unwell/febrile child with a burn Any concerns regarding burn injuries and co-morbidities (e.g., serious medical conditions (such cardiac dysfunction, diabetes, cancer or immunosuppression) that may affect treatment or healing of the burn\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA IO-year-old child was presented to the ED after falling on an outstretched hand. He is complaining of severe pain in his hand. An X-ray of his hand is shown. What is the management?\n- **A)** Analgesia and discharge B\n- Short arm splint\n- **C)** Mallet splint\n- **D)** Surgical fixation\n- **E)** Cast above elbow\n\nExplanation\n- X-ray shows buckle fracture. Buckle fractures are self-limiting and\n- typically do not require operative intervention. A splint is required with a period of rest and immobilization.\n- Buckle fractures Torus fractures, also known as buckle fractures\n- Buckle fractures are incomplete compression fracture at the\n- metaphysis of the distal radius, ulna or both in which one cortex is disrupted, and the other remains intact. Buckle fractures are common injuries sustained by children >2 years\n- old, typically following a fall on the outstretched hand. As the developing bone is relatively soft, the trabeculae is compressed,\n- resulting in the appearance of a bulge. This is most frequently seen on the dorsal aspect of the distal radius but can appear on distal ulna or both distal ulna and radius. The bulge may be seen at both the ulnar and radial aspects of the bone. Investigations\n- & plain radiograph will show\n-\n-\n- distinct fracture lines are not seen subtle deformity or buckle of the cortex may be evident\n- in some cases, angulation is the only diagnostic clue\n-\n\n- Management\n- Buckle fractures have excellent cosmetic and functional prognosis. A rigid cast is not recommended, instead a removable splint should be applied. This has been shown to be at least as effective as a cast in terms of facilitating healing but can promote earlier functional recovery.\n- Advise\n- The splint should be worn for 3 weeks, but can be removed for bathing, showering and for sleep.\n- Once the splint is removed the children should avoid contact sports or high-energy activities (such as rough and tumble play) for a further 3 weeks to avoid further injury2\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 5-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with complaints of abdominal pain and vomiting for the past few hours. The child appears uncomfortable but afebrile, with no history of recent illness. Upon further questioning, the father reveals that his packet of small, magnetic stress-relief toys has been missing since the morning, and he suspects the child may have swallowed them. The child denies knowing where the toys are, and the physical exam reveals generalized abdominal tenderness without signs of peritonitis. What is the most appropriate next investigation?\n- **A)** CT scan of the abdomen\n- **B)** Observation without imaging\n- **C)** MRI of the abdomen\n- **D)** X-rays to determine the nurnber and position of toys\n- **E)** Ultrasound of the abdornen\n- Explanation\n- X-rays are the first-line imaging modality to evaluate for swallowed foreign bodies, particularly if they are radiopaque, like many toys or magnets. The goal is to identify the location and number of objects, as this will help guide further management (e.g., whether urgent removal is necessary).\n- Note - for more detail check gastroenterology module - foreign body ingestion.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 14-year-old boy presents with his parents after he started feeling depressed due to bad results in school. He explains he feels much better now and will start a new college. What is the next step of management in this?\n- **A)** Send to child well-being team\n- **B)** Wait till CAMHS team arrives for assessment\n- **C)** Self-care outlet   D\n- Needs admission\n- **E)** Start on fluoxetine\n- Explanation\n- Specialist CAMHS services are available to children and young people who have severe, complex and enduring emotional/behavioural/mental health difficulties. Specialist CAMHS services are, for example, not appropriate for children / young people who are experiencing a normal reaction to a significant life event (e.g. a normal bereavement or a normal reaction to parental separation).\n- Anyone in the following category is not appropriate for CAMHS referral\n- Child/Young Person's Response to Normal Life Events. Eg: bereavement, parental separation.\n- Children/Young People Whose Difficulties Occur only at School e.g. behaviour settled at home; unsettled in school.\n- Children/Young People Whose Parents are in Dispute within Legal Proceedings\n- Children of separated/divorced parents who are in legal dispute about residence and / or contact arrangements\n\n- For more detail check CAMHS referral in your notes or in your quiz.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 41 -year-old man brought by ambulance after RTA with an obvious scalp haematoma and bleeding from his nose. His GCS is  and his BP is  mmHg. His CXR is shown. His Sp02 with ventilation on Fi02 1.0 is 90%. What is the diagnosis?\n- **A)** Bronchopneumonia   B\n- Right lung collapsed\n- **C)** Aspiration pneumonia\n- **D)** Septic emboli\n- **E)** Dressler syndrome\n- Explanation\n- Patient has ETT in placed. His oxygen saturation is 90% with ventilation on Fi02 1.0 which indicates that there is an obstruction in the airway. The X-ray shows a collapsed right lung upper lobe which could be due\n\nto secretion accumulation. immediate priority in this case would be suction down the ETT.\n- Note- check quiz 41 for similar question and more details.  IO. A 35-year-old lady presented to the ED with complaint of severe headache and sudden painful vision loss as soon after she started watching TV in her bedroom in the dark. On further assessment she states that she could see haloes around the light and her pupils were found to be abnormally dilated. Which one of the following drugs caused this condition?   A\n- Tricyclic antidepressants\n- **B)** Monoamine oxidase\n- **C)** Fluoxetine\n- **D)** Pilocarpine\n- **E)** Beta blockers Drugs causing angle closure glaucoma:\n- Angle-closure glaucoma is a serious condition that has been mainly associated with TCAs, low-potency antipsychotics, topiramate and, to a\n\nlesser extent, SSRIs. When patients with narrow angles are given TCAs, they all appear to experience induction of glaucomatous attacks. Antipsychotics and SSRIs may lead to an added risk of developing angle-closure glaucoma, but only in predisposed eyes. previOUS\n	\N	[]	\N	f	58	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
b1afb392-9144-45b9-8870-a7eacd9e8e73	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-6	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 6	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 6\n\n- **C)** Give antibiotics if indicated\n- **D)** Discharge home with advice\n- **E)** Cive oxygen, IV antibiotics and order CBC, IJA Explanation\n- Neutropenic sepsis emergency treatment and assessment :\n- Give oxygen therapy_to people with reduced oxygen saturation\n- Take blood tests and microbiology samples including\n- Blood gas including glucose and lactate measurement\n- Blood culture — ideally done before antibiotic administration.\n- FBC, C-reactive protein (CRP), Clotting screen\n- Creatinine, IJ&E\n- LFTs\n- CXR or CT as per indication\n- IV antibiotic therapy_\n- Treat suspected neutropenic sepsis as an acute medical emergency and offer beta lactam monotherapy with piperacillin with tazobactam as initial empiric antibiotic therapy to patients with suspected neutropenic sepsis immediately.\n- offer prophylaxis with a fluoroquinolone during the expected period of neutropenia only.\n- Give an intravenous fluid bolus to restore tissue perfusion.(as per need)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 62-year-old known smoker presents to the ED with complain of Severe respiratory distress. His observations are: BP 110/79mmHg Pulse 120bpm sp02 on air His ABGs is given. What is the ABGs interpretation?\n- **A)** Type-2 respiratory failure\n- **B)** Acute on Chronic respiratory failure   C Type-I respiratory failure\n- **D)** Mixed\n- **E)** Respiratory acidosis with compensated metabolic alkalosis\n- Explanation\n- Patient ABC interpret acute type-II respiratory failure\n\nType — I Respiratory failure Hypoxemic RF Pa02 < 60 mmHg Normal or PaC02 Associated with acute diseases of the lun Pulmonary edema (Cardiogenic, noncardiogenic(ARDS), pneumoma pulmonary hemorrhage, and collapse Ill. I Type — 2 Respiratory failure erca nic RF PaC02 > 50 mmHg Hypoxemia is common Cause Drug overdose Neuromuscular disease Chest wall deformity COPD, and Bronchial asthma\n- Distinction between Acute and Chronic RF Acute respiratory failure (RF)\n-\n-\n- Develops over minutes to hours No time for renal compensation pH quicklyto <7.2 Clinical markers of chronic hypoxaemia (polycythemia and cor pulmonale) are absent. Example- Pneumonia Chronic respiratory failure (RF)\n- Develops over several days, allowing time renal compensation.\n- An increase in bicarbonate concentration.\n- PH— only slightly increase\n- Clinical markers of chronic hypoxaemia (polycythemia and cor pulmonale) are present. Example - COPD\n- Acute-on-chronic respiratory failure (ACRF)\n\n- It occurs when relatively minor, although often multiple, insults cause acute deterioration in a patient with chronic respiratory insufficiency.\n- ACRF is usually seen in patients known to have severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), but occasionally it manifests as cryptic respiratory failure or postoperative ventilator dependence in a patient with no known lung disease.\n- The most important therapeutic interventions are administration of oxygen, bronchodilators, and corticosteroids, and noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV).\n- In short Types Type- I RF Type- 11 RF Acute on Chronic type — II RF pH Normal/high Normal/low Norma Illow Pa02 Low Low PaC02 NormalJlow High High Bicarbonate Normal Normal/rising Raised 1 Abbreviation: RF (respiratory failure) Note—for more detail check ABG interpretation module in procedural & skills.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 75-year-old female is brought into the ED after having syncopal episodes at home. Her observations are: Pulse 35bpm\n\nBp 85/70mmHg Atropine 3mg given along with inotrope. ECG is given What is next step of management?\n- **A)** Refer to cardiologist\n- **B)** Repeat atropine C\n- External pacing\n- **D)** Repeat inotropes\n- **E)** Admit her for permanent pacing\n- Explanation\n- His ECC suggest mobitz type-II heart block\n\n- The ALS bradycardia algorithm recommends Atropine 500 mcg IV up-to 3rng maximum if any Pt presents with following Adygg features or risk Of asystole: Shock Syncope Myocardial ischaemia Heart failure Recent asystole Mobiztype-2 Av block Complete heart block Ventricular pause seconds If no response to Atropine then follow second line treatment: cardiac pacing Isoprenaline infusion 5 mcg/min, Adrenaline infusion 2-10 mcg/minutes Alternative drugs (aminophylline, dopamine, glucagon, glycopyrrolate)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 45-year-old male presented to the ED post-thyroid surgery with complain of periorbital numbness, paresthesia of hand and feet and abnormal movement. What is the cause of his condition?\n- **A)** Thyroid crisis\n- **B)** Hypokalemia\n- **C)** Hypomagnesemia D\n- Hypocalcemia\n- **E)** Hypothyroidism\n\n- Explanation\n- hypocalcaemia is the common complication of thyroid surgery.\n- Postoperative hypocalcemia is mainly caused by secondary hypoparathyroidism, which occurs owing to damage, ischemia, or removal of one or more parathyroid glands during operation.\n- Hypocalcaemia\n- It is defined as a total serum calcium is less than <2.1-2.6 mmol/L\n- Causes Of hypocalcaemia\n- Hypoparathyrodism - (congenital, autoimmune, after thyroid or parathyroid surgery)\n- Vitamin D deficiency - (decreased intake, malabsorption or failure of synthesis or activation), inadequate dietary calcium\n- Renal disease\n- Hypomagnesaemia - (consider PPI associated hypomagnesaemia)\n- Drugs - (e.g. phenytoin, bisphosphonates, rifampicin, chemotherapy), blood transfusion, contrast dye)\n- Hyperphosphataemia\n- Respiratory alkalosis - (increased albumin binding, relative fall in free ionised calcium)\n- Acute pancreatitis - (free fatty acids chelate calcium)\n- Malignancy - osteoblastic metastases (e.g. breast cancer, prostate cancer), tumour lysis syndrome (following chemotherapy)\n- Clinical features\n- Symptoms\n- mild hypocalcaemia (2.00-2.12 mmol/L) can be asymptomatic, whereas acute symptoms of neuromuscular irritability can develop in the more severe form (<1.9 mmol/L\n- Paraesthesia (usually fingers, toes and around mouth).\n\n. Tetany\n- Carpopedal spasm (wrist flexion and fingers drawn together).\n- Muscle cramps\n- Hypocalcaemia signs\n- Chvostek's sign (tapping over facial nerve causes facial muscles to twitch)\n- Trousseau's signoccurs in patients with hypocalcaemia (or hypomagnesaemia)\n- To elicit this sign, inflate a blood pressure cuff to a pressure above the patient's systolic level and maintain the pressure for several minutes.\n- Look for carpopedal spasm which involves flexion at the wrist, flexion at the metacarpophalangeal joints, extension of the interphalangeal joints and adduction of the thumbs and fingers.\n- Seizure\n- Prolonged QT interval which may progress to ventricular fibrillation (VF) or heart block. Laryngospasm\n- Bronchospasm\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 10-Year-old child brought to the ED with complain of limping without any significant history of trauma and fall. His X-ray is given What line should be drawn to confirm slipped capital femoral epiphysis?\n- **A)** Iliopectineal line\n- **B)** Perkin's line\n- **C)** Ilioischial line\n- **D)** Klein line\n- **E)** Shenton line\n- Explanation\n- The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper femoral epiphysis. In this sign, the line of Klein passes above the femoral head instead of intersecting the femoral epiphysis as in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.\n- Slipped-upper-femoraLgpiphysis;\n- It's a displacement of femoral epiphysis from metaphysis\n- More common in children who are overweight and common in 10-15 years of age\n- It may associated with endocrine pathology\n- Clinical features:\n- Pt will present with severe pain with externally rotated leg.\n- There maybe shortening of the affected limb\n- 'nyestigatipnzAP or frog-leg lateral view x-ray will reveal displacement of femoral head.\n- The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper femoral epiphysis. In this sign, the line of Klein passes above the femoral head instead of intersecting the femoral epiphysis as in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.\n\n- Management; Urgent evaluation and management is crucial to avoid further displacement and development of avascular necrosis.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 40-year-old woman presents to the ED with palpitations. She has no other symptoms. She has been prescribed fluoxetine for psychiatric disorder and recently completed a course of erythromycin for chest infection. ECG is performed at triage. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nA\n- TOrsade de pointes\n- **C)** Monomorphic VT\n- **E)** Third degree AV block\n- Explanation\n- Macrolides cause QT prolongation.\n- Current literature suggests fluoxetine has lowest risk . ongQT_syndrome\n- Definition\n- Long QT syndrome (LQTS) is a congenital or acquired condition that is characterized by a prolonged QT interval on the surface ECG and is associated with a high risk of sudden cardiac death due to ventricular tachyarrhythmias.\n- In congenital LQTS, genetic mutations affect ion channels important in myocardial repolarization.\n- Acquired LQTS may occur secondary to ingestion of QT interval- prolonging drugs, electrolyte imbalances, or bradyarrhythmias.\n- Cause-of-AcquiredAQTS\n- Drugs - including non-sedating antihistamines (such as terfenadine), r\n- nacrolide antibiotics (such as clarithromycin and erythromycin),\n\nantipsychotic drugs (such as risperidone), some antidepressant drugs (such as citalopram and escitalopram, tricyclics, and monoamine- oxidase inhibitors), and antifungals (such as itraconazole).\n- Electrolyte imbalances - in particular hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypocalcemia.\n- Bradyarrhythmias - any sudden bradycardia or atrioventricular (AV) nodal block may result in QT prolongation or pause-dependent QT prolongation.\n- Central nervous system (CNS) lesions - such as intracranial hemorrhage (especially subarachnoid hemorrhage) and ischemic strokes.\n- Malnutrition - liquid protein diet, starvation.\n- Management-of-torsade-de-pointes\n- Treat or remove the causative agents\n- Correct electrolyte imbalance\n- If patient has adverse features (hypotension, shock, etc) treat immediately with synchronized cardioversion\n- Other all patients give intravenous magnesium sulphate 2g over 10 minutes\n- Complications\n- Torsades de pointes\n- Cardiac arrest\n- Sudden cardiac death\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 50-year-old smoker presents to the ED with respiratory distress. ABGs was done at triage which shows type-2 respiratory failure. You decided to start NIV.\n\nWhich of the following is absolute contraindication to NIV?\n- **A)** GCS less than 8\n- **B)** Confusion D c. pH 7.15\n- **D)** Fixed upper airway obstruction\n- **E)** Cognitive impairment\n- Explanation\n- British thoracic\n\nIndications for pC02 S Contraindications for NIV to ICIJ NIV SETUP Initial P EPAP: up rate ratio SEEK AND TREAT REVERSIBLE OF AHRF\n- possible need for > 8 Asth u monia NIV Monitoring tian Red CONSIDER\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 2-year-old brought to the ED by his father, who he found unresponsive. You confirm he is in cardiac arrest and initiate APLS chest pads are applied. He is 12kg. What energy shock should be given to this patient?   A\n- Synchronous 80J C:]\n- **B)** Synchronous 403   C\n- Asynchronous SOJ\n- **D)** Asynchronous 603\n- **E)** Synchronous 703\n- Explanation\n- APLS recommends 4j/kg shock As patient weight is 12 x 4 48J/50J.\n- If 483 given prefer that.\n\nResuscitation Council UK GUIDELINES 2021 Paediatric advanced life support Recognise cardiac arrest Commence/continue CPR (5 initial breaths then CV ratio 15:2) SHOCKABLE After 3 shocks give:\n- Ad'enalirw 'VAO IO mcg kg' (and every (and repeat S mg ' only after 5th During Attach defibri Assess rhythm Return of spontaneous circulation Post car"c arrest care:\n- use an ABCDE approach\n- Aim Paco,\n- Avoid hypaensOn\n- Competent an an\n- mg NON-SHOCKABLE PEA 60 Gwe 'V/'O then treat t in\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 30-year-old male patient brought to the ED unresponsive after initial assessment, you suspect opioid toxicity and you've started naloxone. His GCS started to be improved after two doses of naloxone, however after sometimes the patient started deteriorating again. What is the next step of management?\n- **A)** Intubation and ventilation B\n- Shift to ICU\n- **C)** Give IV fluids\n- **D)** Start naloxone infusion\n- **E)** Give sodium bicarbonate bolus\n- Explanation Opioid overdose (narcotic analgesics)\n- Clinical-features-of-opioid-oyerdose\n- Respiratory depression . Pinpoint pupil . Coma\n- Antidote\n- Naloxone hydrochloride\n\n- Since naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, close monitoring and repeated injections are necessary according to the respiratory rate and depth of coma.\n- When repeated administration of naloxone is required, it can be given by continuous intravenous infusion instead and the rate of infusion adjusted according to vital signs. 10 / 10\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 10:\nA 30-year-old male brought to the ED by paramedics found unconscious from glacier mountains. His core body temperature is 28'C. His ECG was done at triage which shows non-Shockable rhythm. According to ALS guidelines of hypothermia, when you will give adrenaline?\n- **A)** After rewarming, core body temperature become 300C\n- **B)** Cive Img Adrenaline after 3 shocks\n- **C)** Img adrenaline when temperature below 350C\n- **D)** Img as soon as IV line available\n- **E)** Img adrenaline when temperature below 20\n- Explanation\n- Management-of-hypothermia-(ALSguideline)\n- Defibrillation\n\n- For VF/VT defibrillation may be tried up to three times. Delay further attempt until core body temperature >28-30\n- Pacing is generally ineffective. Do not try it unless bradycardia persists when normothermia is reached\n- Resuscitation-drugs;\n- Withhold adrenaline and other CPR drugs until Pt has been warmed to a core temperature of >30 oc. Once 300C the interval between drug doses should be doubled adrenaline would be given about every 8- IO rninutes.\n- Intubation;-don't delay where it's indicated.\n- Muscular stiffness,_use mechanical compression devices. previous  Next —Y\n	\N	[]	\N	f	59	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
1d273bee-7025-47bc-ae38-16f42dcb2596	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-64	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 64	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 64\n\nThe following x-ray is obtained. What is the most appropriate management?\n- **A)** Apply a plaster-of-Paris hanging U-slab x\n- **B)** Use procedural sedation to undertake a reduction   C\n- Apply a collar-and-cuff\n- **D)** Apply a shoulder immobiliser\n- **E)** Refer to orthopedic for open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF)\n- Explanation\n- Apply a collar-and-cuff. In an elderly patient with an impacted or minimally displaced proximal humerus fracture, use conservative management with a collar-and-cuff, analgesia, early pendulum exercises, and fracture clinic follow-up.  I. A 21 -year-old woman attends the emergency department four hours after a rape at a party. She is stable, wants the police involved, and asks what should happen next. There are no injuries requiring urgent treatment. What is the most appropriate next step to preserve evidence?\n- **A)** Take vaginal and oral swabs in the ED as soon as possible\n- **B)** Ask her not to wash and report to police in the morning\n- **C)** Refer immediately to a Sexual Assault Referral Centre (SARC) for forensic samples and support\n- **D)** Isolate her in a side room to reduce risk of DNA contamination\n- **E)** Place her underwear in a plastic bag and refrigerate\n- Explanation\n- Forensic sampling should be performed by trained clinicians at a SARC, maintaining chain of evidence, consent, and access to advocacy/support. ED swabs risk poor evidence handling and duplicate examinations.\n- RCEM Recommendations\n- Where possible and practical, victims of sexual assault and rape should be assessed in a Sexual Assault Referral Centre.\n- Any forensic examination should only be performed by a clinician with suitable specialist training in an appropriate environment.\n- Person identifiable information about sexual assaults and rapes should not normally be shared without consent, except in exceptional ci rcumstances.\n\n- Emergency contraception should be available to victims attending the emergency department.\n- There is no requirement for emergency physicians to take pre- transfusion blood samples for the police.\n- Post exposure prophylaxis for sexually transmitted infections should be available to victims attending the emergency department.\n- The Sexual Assault Referral Centre (SARC)\n- Patients who have been sexually assaulted may present to the ED after the assault. Whilst the ED can manage some aspects of their care, it is best practise to encourage patients to go to a Sexual Assault Referral Centre (SARC.) A SARC is a one-stop location where victims of sexual assault can receive a forensic examination, emergency contraception and post exposure prophylaxis, psychological counselling, legal advice and other support, all in one place from professionally trained staff. They bring together all of the different legal and medical agencies and departments, which helps both the victims and those investigating the crimes. Victims can choose to be dealt with anonymously if they do not wish any involvement form the police. A SARC cannot deal with acute injuries\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 32-year-old man is brought to your ED after multiple stab wounds to the chest. On arrival he is unresponsive, pulseless and ventilated, with high-quality CPR in progress. Monitoring is attached and large-bore access is in situ. You are in a standard ED resuscitation bay with ultrasound and sterile packs immediately available. You perform a PoCUS during a pulse check. The following image is obtained.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate next step? A\n- Perform ultrasound-guided needle aspiration, advancing until fluid is obtained\n- **B)** Insert a needle 5 cm below the xiphisternum for pericardiocentesis\n- **C)** From a subxiphoid entry, aim the needle toward the left scapular base\n- **D)** Avoid pericardiocentesis because a three-way catheter is contraindicated\n- **E)** From a subxiphoid entry, aim the needle toward the right shoulder\n- Explanation\n- In traumatic cardiac arrest the priority is to reverse treatable causes. PoCUS demonstrates tamponade. Immediate echo-guided needle aspiration decompresses the pericardium, restores ventricular filling and may achieve ROSC. It is the right ED action while arranging definitive surgical care\n- Note - In a traumatic cardiac arrest or peri-arrest trauma patient thoracotomy is preferred over pericardiocentesis\n- Pericardiocentesis (Q, echo-guided)\n\n- Emergency drainage or diagnostic sampling ofa clinically important pericardial effusion. It is the definitive bedside treatr-nent for cardiac tamponade and can rapidly restore cardiac output.\n- In acute traumatic cardiac tamponade, as little as 50mIs of blood may impair cardiac function7.\n- A pericardial effusion 10-20 mm deep is likely to contain 250-500 mL of fluid\n- Haemorrhagic tamponade will reaccumulate and requires cardiothoracic operative management.\n- In a traumatic cardiac arrest or peri-arrest trauma patient thoracotomy is preferred over pericardiocentesis\n- Suspected or confirmed tamponade with haemodynamic compromise, for example hypotension, shock, obstructed venous return, pulsus paradoxus, raised JVP.\n- Urgent or diagnostic (plan within hours) - Symptomatic moderate to large effusion not responding to medical therapy.\n- Effusion with suspected TB, bacterial or malignant aetiology.\n- Chronic large effusion lasting longer than three months.\n- Usually not indicated - Small or moderate effusions without compromise when diagnosis is likely and non-invasive tests suffice.\n- Contraindications\n- Aortic dissection with haemopericardium.\n\n- Suspected post-MI free-wall rupture. If surgery is unavailable and the patient is dying, drain very small volumes to maintain perfusion as a bridge. . Relative:\n- Uncorrected coagulopathy or thrombocytopenia (PLTc <50,OOO/mm3).\n- Therapeutic anticoagulation. These do not preclude the procedure in life-threatening tamponade.\n- Equipment\n- Equipment for Echo-Cuided Pericardiocentesis:\n- Echocardiography with a cardiac probe\n- Sterile probe cover and sterile echo gel\n- 16-18 gauge, Teflon-sheathed needle\n- 6 Fr to 8 Fr dilator and introducer sheath\n- J-tipped guidewire\n- Drainage catheter: pigtail angiocatheter 6 Fr to 8 Fr or specific pericardial drainage set\n- Disposable flushing system to maintain patency of the catheter\n- Equipment for Real-Time Echo-Monitored Procedure:\n- Multi-angle bracket to be mounted on the echo probe\n- Needle guide sterile kit\n- 18 gauge, 9 cm needle (or 15 cm for subxiphoid approach)\n- 6 Frto 8 Fr dilator\n- Puncture site\n\nApproach Apical Parasternal Subxiphoid Entry point and needle direction cm lateral to the apex beat in the 5th—7th intercostal space; advance just over the rib to avoid neurovascular bundle. Left 5th intercostal space, close to sternal edge; needle perpendicularto skin at level Of the cardiac notch. Between xiphisternum and left costal margin; once under costal cartilage, angle 15—300 toward left shoulder. Advantage Short path to pericardium. Thick LV wall tends to selt- seal. Easy to keep lung out of plane under echo. Excellent echo visualisation of pericardium and needle. Lower pneumothorax risk. disadvantages Risk of LV puncture. Higher risk of left pneumothorax (close to pleura). Risk of pneumothorax. Risk of internal thoracic vessel injury if > 1 cm lateral. Longer path. Possible peritoneal entry. Risk of right atrial puncture. May traverse liver it no alternative site.\n\nTechnique (echo-guided, Seldinger)\n- Prep and drape. Infiltrate local anaesthetic down to the pericardium\n- along the planned trajectory. Under ultrasound guidance, advance the needle while aspirating until\n- pericardial fluid is obtained. Advance the needle one to two millimetres to ensure intrapericardial\n- position, then slide the catheter or guidewire into the space. Rernove the needle, pass a dilator over the wire, then insert a pigtail\n- catheter. Confirm position by ultrasound or by injecting 5 ml agitated saline and\n- visualising bubbles in the pericardial space. Drain until blood pressure and venous pressures improve. Avoid\n- immediate removal of more than about I litre. Connect to a closed drainage system. Send fluid for protein, LDH,\n- glucose, cell count, Gram stain and culture, cytology, and TB studies when indicated. post_-procedure care\n- Repeat echo to assess residual effusion and chamber filling.\n-\n- Chest radiograph to exclude pneumothorax if the path was pleural. Aspirate every four to six hours. Remove the catheter when drainage is less than 25—30 ml in 24 hours. Manage the catheter as you would a central line.\n- Continue diagnostic work-up for the underlying cause.\n- Medical measures while preparing:\n- Small bolus of isotonic saline in hypotensive patients can transiently\n- improve preload.\n\n- Avoid diuretics.\n- Inotropes may be used briefly but are not definitive therapy.\n- Complications\n- Major - Death, ventricular or coronary laceration, haemopericardium, pneumopericardium, pneumothorax requiring tube, injury to abdominal viscera, pericardial decompression syndrome, malignant arrhythmias.\n- Minor - Vasovagal hypotension or bradycardia, supraventricular arrhythmias, small pneumothorax, pleuro-pericardial fistula.\n- Pericardial decompression syndrome - Rare pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock after successful drainage. Reduce risk by draining only to restore perfusion and completing evacuation gradually over the next few hours.\n- Documentation\n- Record indication, consent status, site and approach, ultrasound findings, volumes removed, samples sent, immediate response, and any complications.\n- Key_points\n- Use ultrasound to choose the safest path and guide the needle.\n- In crashing tamponade, do not delay for coagulopathy correction.\n- Drain enough to stabilise, then complete evacuation under monitoring.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 39-year-old woman presents at the weekend with heavy menstrual bleeding. Cycles are regular but increasingly heavy over the last few years. Today is day 5 of bleeding. Pregnancy test negative. She is haemodynamically stable and awaiting outpatient gynaecology review. What is the most appropriate intervention?\n- **A)** Norethisterone 5 mg three times daily for four days\n- **B)** Start oral iron and reassure that bleeding will settle naturally\n- **C)** Tranexamic acid 1 g three times daily for four days\n- **D)** Withhold pharmacological treatment until hormone assessments are completed\n- **E)** Advise urgent CP follow-up only\n- Menorrhagia\n- It defines menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding) as excessive menstrual blood loss which interferes with a woman's physical, social, emotional, and/or material quality of life, and which can occur alone or in combination with other symptoms.\n\nMenstruation is the cyclic shedding of the uterine lining in response to\n- the interactions of hormones produced by the hypothalamus, pituitary, and ovaries.\n- A normal menstrual cycle is 21—35 days long, with bleeding lasting an average of 5—7 days and blood loss of between 25—80 ml. Menorrhagia has been defined, in research and clinical settings, as\n- blood loss of more than 80 ml and/or a duration of more than 7 day. Causes\n- In about 50% of women with menorrhagia, no cause is identified.\n- Possible causes of menorrhagia include uterine and ovarian pathologies, such as: Uterine fibroids\n- Endometrial polyps\n- Cancer of the ovary, uterus, cervix, or endometrium\n- Endometriosis and adenomyosis\n- Polycystic ovary syndrome (causes anovulatory menorrhagia and\n- irregular bleeding) PID\n- Systemic conditions, such as - Coagulation disorders (for example, von Willebrand disease), Hypothyroidism, Diabetes mellitus, Hyperprolactinaemia, Liver or renal disease. Other causes, including - Medications such as anticoagulants,\n- antiplatelets, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), oral contraceptive pill, and herbal supplements (such as ginseng, ginkgo, and soya)\n\n- Assessment Detailed clinical history. Ask about the nature of the bleeding,\n- including: Onset (for example, peri-menarcheal, sudden, or gradual).\n- Frequency/pattern (cyclic or non-cyclic).\n- Duration (acute or chronic).\n- Temporal association (for example, postcoital or postpartum).\n- Related symptoms (such as persistent intermenstrual bleeding, pelvic\n- pain, and/or pressure symptoms) — may suggest uterine cavity abnormality, histological abnormality, adenomyosis, or fibroids.\n- Ask about her menstrual history, including: Age of menarche.\n-\n- Gravidity and parity. Normal menstrual cycle and any variation of this pattern (including\n- regularity, duration, and number of pads/tampons per day). Last menstrual period (the start date of the rnost recent menstrual\n- period). Medical history - including recent medical procedures and\n- comorbidities (such as endometriosis or history of coagulation disorders [for example, von Willebrand disease]).\n- Drug history - including current medications and previous treatment for menorrhagia. Sexual history - including current contraceptive use (hormonal\n- contraceptives may cause irregular bleeding), contraceptive plans, and future plans for a family (as this may impact the choice of treatment). Cervical screening history — to confirm she has attended as\n- scheduled and results were normal.\n\n- Signs and symptoms of anaemia, such as dyspnoea, fatigue, headache, and sore tongue.\n- The impact of the bleeding on the woman's quality of life. Examination\n-\n- If the woman has a history of menorrhagia withother related symptoms, offer a physical examination (as appropriate) to exclude an underlying cause. Perform an abdominal examination — to assess for large fibroids and\n- other masses. Perform a bimanual pelvic examination - including a speculum\n- examination of the cervix (except in young girls who are not sexually active) — to exclude an underlying cause, such as ascites, fibroids, or gynaecological cancer. Look for features of an underlying systemic disease, for example:\n- Hypothyroidism goitre.\n- Coagulation disorders (for example, von Willebrand disease) —\n- bruises or petechiae. Polycystic ovary syndrome — acne, hirsutism\n-\n- Inyestigations\n- FBC—to rule out iron deficiency anaemia (which is a strong indicator of excessive menstrual bleeding) Pregnancy test\n- A vaginal or cervical swab — if an infection is suspected.\n- Thyroid function tests — if there are features of hypothyroidism\n-\n\n- Tests for coagulation disorders (for example, von Willebrand disease) — in women who have had heavy menstrual bleeding since menarche and a personal or a family history of a coagulation disorder.\n- Do not — routinely carry out serum ferritin test, hormone level and Measure blood loss for women with menorrhagia\n- Management\n- Refer using a suspected cancer pathway referral(for an appointment within 2 weeks) if:\n- The woman has a pelvic mass associated with any other features of cancer (such as unexplained bleeding or weight loss).\n- The woman is aged 55 years and over with post-menopausal bleeding (unexplained vaginal bleeding more than 12 months after menstruation has stopped because of the menopause).\n- On examination, the appearance of their cervix is consistent with cervical cancer.\n- Refer urgently to gynaecology if- physical examination identifies ascites and/or a pelvic or abdominal mass or uterine fibroids suspected.\n- Pharmacological treatment\n- Consider a levonorgestrel intrauterine system (I-NG-IUS) as the first-line treatment.\n- The non-hormonal treatment options aretranexamic acid or a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID, off-label use).\n- The hormonal treatment options arecombined hormonal contraception (CHC, off-label use for some CHCs) or cyclical oral progestogen (norethisterone).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 32-year-old man slips while running and lands on the posterior aspect of his left elbow. The elbow is swollen and tender with reduced active extension. Distal pulses and capillary refill are normal. An X-ray is shown. Which single clinical finding would most specifically indicate an associated nerve injury from this fracture?\n- **A)** Thenar muscle wasting\n- **B)** Loss of elbow extension C\n- Wrist drop\n- **D)** Reduced sensation over the regimental badge area\n- **E)** Weakness of finger abduction\n- Explanation\n- Weakness of finger abduction - The ulnar nerve runs in the cubital tunnel between the medial epicondyle and the olecranon and may be contused or entrapped with olecranon fractures. Ulnar palsy classically weakens the dorsal interossei, resulting in impaired finger abduction.\n\nOccasionally ulnar nerve injury can result in long-term sensory and motor impairment, most marked in the hand. Loss of elbow extension - Due to triceps disruption/mechanical pain,\n- not a nerve sign. Other complications of an olecranon fracture\n- Loss of active elbow extension or extensor lag from triceps disruption.\n- Elbow stiffness and reduced range of motion (especially extension).\n- Delayed union/non-union or malunion altering the ulnohumeral\n- articulation. Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the elbow.\n- Wound problems/infection (subcutaneous bone; open injuries are\n- common). Symptomatic hardware after ORIF (prominent wires/plates, K-wire migration, need for removal). Heterotopic ossification/myositis ossificans.\n-\n- Elbow instability if associated injuries are present (e.g., coronoid or radial triad"). Chronic olecranon bursitis from prominence or hardware irritation\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 3-year-old is brought to the ED with a barking cough and noisy breathing after 3 to 4 days of coryza. He is sitting quietly on his mother's lap, has audible stridor at rest, Sp02 90% on air, and reduced air entry. Oxygen has been started. Calculated westley croup score is\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 12:\nWhat is the most appropriate next step?\n- **A)** Take the child from the mother's lap to the resuscitation room, assess and manage\n- **B)** Nebulise with adrenaline\n- **C)** Immediate ENT consultation\n- **D)** Give oral dexamethasone\n- **E)** Prepare for intubation\n- Explanation\n- Westley Score 212 - impending respiratory failure. Give nebulised epinephrine (1:1000, 5 mL or 0.5 mL/kg up to 5 mL) driven by oxygen, keep the child calm on the parent's lap, and summon senior/airway help. Once improving, give dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg PO/IM/IV (or nebulised budesonide 2 mg) and observe for recurrence.\n- Croup\n- Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a common respiratory disease that typically occurs in children aged between 6 months and 3 years.\n- It is rnost commonly caused by a virus, typically parainfluenza virus types 1 or 3, and is characterized by the sudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which may be accompanied by voice hoarseness,\n\nstridor, and/or respiratory distress. Symptoms are typically worse at night and increase with agitation. Prodromal, non-specific upper respiratory tract symptoms (cough,\n- rhinorrhoea, coryza, and fever) may have been present for between 12 and 72 hours. Risk factors\n- Young age — croup most commonly affects children aged between 6\n- months and 3 years. However, it has been reported to occur in infants younger than 6 months, adolescents, and, more rarely, in adults. Male sex — more boys are affected, with a male to female ratio of 1.4 to\n- Previous intubation — a weak link between a history of previous\n- intubation and croup has been suggested, but the evidence for this is currently considered to be weak. Cause\n- Parainfluenza virus types I or 3\n- Clinical features\n- Sudden onset of a seal-like barking cough, which associated with :\n- Hoarseness\n-\n- Stridor Respiratory distress.\n- Fever < 39C\n\n- No drooling\n- Symptoms are typically worse at night and increase with agitation\n- The-Westley-croup-score-is-yalidated-and-commonly-used-in-clinical practice. Children with croup-can-becategorize into four levels of v ri\n- Mild - croup score 0-2\n- Moderate - croup score 3-5\n- Severe - croup score 6-11\n- Impending respiratory failure - croup score 12-17\n- Most of children have mild croup. Around 5% of children with croups require admission to hospital and of these 1-3% require intubation. In a IO-year study of those intubated there was 0.5% mortality rate. Uncommon complications include pneumonia and bacterial tracheitis Score Stridor o 3 4 5 None Upon agitation At rest Retractions None Mild Moderate Air Entry Normal Mild decrease Marked decrease saoa None Upon agitation At rest Level of consciousness Normal Decreased\n- Differential diagnoses Epiglottitis\n\n- Bacterial tracheitis\n- Foreign body in upper airway\n- Retropharyngeal/peritonsillar abscess\n- Angioneurotic oedema\n- Allergic reaction\n- Management\n- Oxygen if saturation <92%\n- Oral dexamethasone 150mcg/kg (0.15mg) or prednisolone 0.5 to Img/kg\n- Inhaled nebulised budesonide 2mg.\n- Oral steroids are the treatrnent choice. They give rise to clinical improvement within 30 minutes, but if child will not take oral medication or is vomiting, then inhaled budesonide should be used. Both can repeated after 12 hours if clinically indicated.\n- Nebulised adrenaline - is only used in children with severe and life- threatening croup. Dose is 0.5 mL/kg of concentration to a maximum dose of 5 ml.\n- A very small proportion of children admitted to hospital with croup require tracheal intubation. The decision to intubate is a clinical one based on increasing tachycardia, tachypnoea and chest retraction, or the appearance of cyanosis, exhaustion or apnoea, unless there has been respiratory arrest. Tracheal intubation should be performed under general anaesthetic by an experienced paediatric anaesthetist. If there is uncertainty about the diagnosis, or difficulty in intubation is anticipated, it is recommended that an ENT surgeon capable of performing a tracheostomy is present.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 28-year-old man presents 40 minutes after cleaning equipment with dilute hydrofluoric acid (HF). He was wearing gloves but noticed a leak in the right glove. He washed the area thoroughly at work. He now has intense, deep pain in the distal fingers with only mild erythema. An image is shown. Which treatment to the affected area is most appropriate now?\n- **A)** Immerse the hand in sodium bicarbonate solution\n- **B)** Irrigate with water for a further 20 minutes\n- **C)** Apply calcium gluconate 2.5% gel liberally and massage in D Apply lignocaine gel for analgesia\n- **E)** Immerse the hand in Hartmann's solution\n- Explanation\n- Apply calcium gluconate 2.5% gel liberally and massage in. Hydrofluoric acid causes tissue injury and binds calcium and magnesium, leading to severe pain and potentially fatal hypocalcaemia/hyperkalaemia. After initial water decontamination (already done), the specific treatment is\n\ntopical calcium to neutralise fluoride ions. Reapply every 15—20 minutes until pain settles; use gloves to avoid staff exposure.\n- Chemical Burns Caused by acids, alkalis, or petroleum products; severity depends on duration, concentration, and amount.\n- Management\n- Immediate priority is rapid removal of the chemical. Brush off dry powders before irrigating with warm water for 20—30 minutes (longer for alkalis). Check skin pH to assess adequacy of irrigation. Avoid neutralizing agents as they may cause heat injury. For eyes, irrigate using a small-caliber cannula in the palpebral sulcus.\n- Apply topical calcium promptly for hydrofluoric acid burns. Identify the chemical, review the material safety data sheet, follow listed first-aid steps, and consult poison control for systemic toxicity management.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nAn 84-year-old man trips on a pavement edge and strikes his right shin. He denies any other injuries or loss of consciousness. He can bear weight and walk, and his observations are within normal limits. After analgesia and irrigation, a curved pretibial skin-flap laceration is approximately 5 cm, and no tendon or bone is visible on examination. An image is shown. What is the most appropriate next step of management?\n- **A)** Refer to a plastic surgeon\n\nB\n- Apply Steri-Strips gently without tension\n- **C)** Close the wound with staples\n- **D)** Leave the wound open\n- **E)** Suture the wound without tension Explanation\n- For a superficial, viable pretibial skin-flap in an older adult, UK ED\n- practice favours minimal-trauma re-approximation using Steri-Strips placed perpendicular to the tear without traction. This aligns the flap while avoiding further damage to fragile pretibial skin. Arrange routine wound review.\n- Pretibial lacerations Pretibial lacerations are an increasingly common condition with an\n- estimated incidence of 40—70 per 100 000 per year. They account for up to 5.2 per 1000 emergency department presentations in the I-JK . Pretibial lacerations typically occur in the elderly, and are much more common in women. Predisposing factors to pretibial lacerations\n- Increasing age\n-\n\nSystemic steroid use Poor mobility or balance\n- Poor vision\n- Very thin flaps\n-\n- Distally-based flaps Haematoma beneath the flap\n- The clinical examination - should start with a generalized assessment\n- based on Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATL S) principles to exclude and manage associated life-threatening and then limb-threatening injuries. A focused assessrnent of the injured limb including its neurovascular status and the wound itself should ensue. Details to note on wound assessment given below. Wound assessment\n- Morphology of laceration (linear, flap, jagged)\n- Dimensions of wound\n- Depth of wound (epidermis, dermis, fascia, muscle, bone)\n- Active bleeding\n- Haematoma\n- Signs of infection\n- Viability of skin edges and skin flap - usually judged by colour; a dark flap or one with a dusky blue edge is likely to be unviable\n- Skin quality Visual representation of Dunkin classification\n-\n\nType-I\n- Management Type-II Type-ill Type-IV\n- Modified Dunkin classification for Management of Pretibial Laceration Type Description Linear laceration without skin loss Flap laceration — viable flap Flap laceration — non-viable flap Skin loss Laceration with haematoma Management Non-operative management (clean, dress, elevate, review).or Steri-strips Steri-Strips to gently re-approximate in ED; non- operative care with non-adherent dressing and elevation. Small flap: excise and manage conservatively with dressings. Large flap: excise and skin graft under local anaesthetic. Treat conservatively if <1% TBSA. If not healed by 2—3 months, consider delayed primary skin graft under LA (or primary graft if local resources allow). Often needs surgery: evacuate haematoma and graft.\n\nNon-operative management\n- Following initial wound irrigation, a decision can be made as to\n- whether operative or non-operative management is indicated. even severe lacerations can heal with non-operative management. Indications for non-operative management include:\n- Laceration with no skin loss or necrosis\n- Clean wound\n- Smaller wounds.\n- Wound closure should be attempted only if the wound is clean and if it\n- is possible to do so without undue tension on the skin. Approximating the wound edges with adhesive paper strips (Steri-strips) is superior to suturing the wound in terms of faster healing times and lower rates of skin necrosis. If sterile closure strips are used, these should be applied without\n- placing tension and with space between the tapes to allow for drainage of exudate and blood.\n- Dressings A wide array of dressings have been described for pretibial wound\n- coverage. Dressings in common use include sterile paraffin tulle gras dressing made from open weave gauze (Jelonet), silicone-based, silver and hydrocolloid dressings.\n- Silicone-based dressings (e.g. Mepitel) are non-adherent to the wound bed but gently adherent to surrounding skin and are atraumatic on removal. For patients presenting with haematoma or necrotic skin edges, the application of moist wound dressings such as hydrogels, in gel or sheet form, can promote autolytic debridement of non-viable\n\ntissue without traumatizing the wound bed. Often the flap sloughs off with non-operative management. In the non-operatively managed patient, gentle normal daily activity\n- should be encouraged. However, the patient should also be advised to elevate the limb when seated to minimize oedema which can delay wound healing.\n- Operative-management Despite the frequency of pretibial lacerations, their management varies\n- widely within and across hospitals. Even for the more severe lacerations which require tertiary referral, there is conflicting evidence whether to manage these operatively or non-operatively. Indications for surgical management represent expert opinion, but will\n- also be guided by the individual surgeon's preference. Indications for surgery (pretibial lacerations)\n- Lacerations with large necrotic skin flaps\n- Large skin loss total body surface area)\n- Major haematoma\n- Gross contamination or infection\n- Failure of conservative management after 2—3 months\n- Anaesthesia\n- Excision and grafting of pretibial lacerations has been shown to be as\n- effective (in terms of graft take) when performed under local anaesthesia as when general anaesthesia is used. When feasible, it is therefore advisable to use local anaesthesia.\n\n- Surgical-management Debridement and washout with warm 0.9% saline. Remove debris and\n- haematoma. Excise all non-viable tissue. Delay grafting if contaminated or infected until the wound is clean and\n- cultures guide antibiotics. Severe defects with exposed bone or tendon may need advanced\n- reconstruction such as a free flap. Non-viable skin flaps are excised and covered with a primary split-\n- thickness skin graft rather than defatting and replacing the flap. Split-thickness graft harvest from the anterolateral thigh using a dermatome or Watson knife. Mesh the graft, place on a prepared bed, and secure with tissue glue, absorbable sutures, or staples.\n- Graft take begins within about 72 hours. Infection, shear, and haematoma commonly prevent take.\n- Dress with a non-adherent contact layer such as Mepitel or Jelonet, then gauze, wool, and crepe bandage. Antibiotics - Prophylactic antibiotics are not routinely recommended\n- in patients with pretibial lacerations. Complications related to pretibial lacerations\n- Wound infection\n-\n- Skin necrosis Delayed wound healing\n- Problems from debridement and grafting including bleeding, infection,\n- graft failure, donor-site morbidity, adverse scars, contour deformity Decline in mobility\n-\n\n- Death Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	61	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
e47a60b5-fbcf-41f3-b4c7-716b2a4a977f	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-65-2	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 65	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 65\n\ntwo-week trip that included overnight stays in rural areas. Vaccination history is unclear. The triage nurse inform you and wait for your decision. What is the single most appropriate immediate action? x\n- **A)** Transfer directly to a regional major-isolation facility\n- **B)** Take a full history and examination first, then decide about isolation\n- **C)** Move the patient to a single side room\n- **D)** Keep the patient in triage and complete a full assessment there\n- **E)** Move straight to the resuscitation bay and quarantine there\n- Explanation\n- The primary concern is infection control. The symptoms of fever, myalgia, headache, and vomiting in a traveler from sub-Saharan Africa raise immediate concern for potentially serious and transmissible infectious diseases. These include viral hemorrhagic fevers (VHFs) like Marburg or Lassa fever, as well as other infections like malaria or meningococcal disease.\n- The risk of transmission must be mitigated first. The triage area is a public space with other patients and staff. Moving the patient directly to a single side room with the door closed is a crucial first step to isolate them, minimize potential exposure to others, and allow for a safe assessment.\n\n- This action aligns with standard public health and infection prevention protocols for evaluating a returning traveler with a febrile illness. After isolation is ensured, a full history, examination, and initial investigations (e.g., malaria rapid diagnostic test) can be conducted using appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE).\n- Patient placement/assessment of infection risk\n- The potential for transmission of infection must be assessed when a patient enters a care area. If hospitalised/in a care home setting, this should be continuously reviewed throughout the stay/period of care. The assessment should influence patient placement decisions in line with clinical/care need(s).\n- Patients who may present a cross-infection risk in any setting includes those:\n- with diarrhoea, vomiting, an unexplained rash, fever or respiratory symptoms\n- known to have been previously positive with multidrug-resistant organisms (MDRO) for example, meticillin—resistant staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), carbapenemase-producing enterobacterales (CPE)\n- who have been an inpatient in any hospital in the UK or abroad or are a known epidemiological link to a carrier of CPE\n- who have a known or suspected infection or colonisation.\n- Isolation facilities should be prioritised depending on the known/suspected infectious agent.\n- All patient placement decisions and assessr-nent of infection risk (including isolation requirements) must be clearly documented in the\n\npatient notes and provided in patient handovers with other healthcare/care providers.\n- The clinical judgement and expertise of the staff involved in a patient's management and the infection prevention and control team (IPCT) should be sought, particularly for the application of TBPs, eg, isolation prioritization, when single rooms are in short supply.\n- Single room isolation in hospital settings:\n- isolation of infectious patients can be in specialised isolation facilities, single room isolation, cohorting of infectious patients where appropriate, ensuring that they are separated by at least 3 feet (1 metre) with the door closed.\n- isolation room doors should remain closed, if this is not possible, eg, paediatrics, there should be a documented risk assessment.\n- signage should be used on doors/areas to communicate isolation requirements and prevent entry of unnecessary visitors, and non- essential staff. Patient confidentiality must be maintained.\n- if single rooms are limited, infectious patients who have conditions that could increase the risk of transmission of infection to other patients, such as, excessive cough or an MDRO should be prioritised for placement in a single room.\n- single room prioritisation should be reviewed daily and the clinical judgement and expertise of the staff involved in a patient's management and the Infection Prevention and Control Team (IPCT) should be sought particularly for the application ofTBPs.\n- infectious patients should only be transferred to other departments if clinically necessary. If the patient has an infectious agent transmitted by the airborne/droplet route, then if possible/tolerated the patient should wear a surgical face mask in communal areas during transfer.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- receiving department/hospital and transporting staff must be aware of the necessary precautions. Cohortingin hospital settings: Cohorting of infectious patients can be considered when: single rooms are in short supply and if there are two or more patients (a cohort) with the same confirmed infection there are situations of service pressure eg, winter, and patients may have different or multiple infections. In these situations, a preparedness plan must be in place ensuring that Organisation/Board level assurance on IPC systems and processes are in place. Infectious patients who must not be cohorted with others with different or multiple infections include: those at increased risk of acquisition and adverse outcomes resulting from infection (e.g., immunosuppression) individuals who are unlikely to comply with TBPs.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 28-year-old motorcyclist arrives after a high-speed crash. Paramedics have applied a traction splint to a deformed mid-thigh consistent with a femoral shaft fracture. On arrival, the lower limb cold, pale, and pulseless with delayed capillary refill. No open wounds are seen; pelvis is stable. What is the most appropriate immediate step of management ?\n- **A)** Refer to orthopaedics for manipulation under general anaesthesia\n- **B)** Reduce the amount of traction on the splint and reassess distal perfusion\n- **C)** Observe while waiting for vascular surgery to arrive\n- **D)** Remove all immobilisation and simply elevate the limb\n- **E)** Apply a long-leg backslab and await inpatient review Explanation\n- Reduce the amount of traction and reassess perfusion. Over-traction or\n- malposition of a traction splint can kink/occlude femoral vessels. The first, reversible bedside step is to adjust (reduce/neutralise) traction and immediately recheck pulses/capillary refill. If perfusion does not return, escalate urgently to ortho/vascular and image as indicated\n- Extremity fractures - describing an isolated fracture (ATLS guideline) State bone and segment (diaphysis, metaphysis, intra-articular).\n- Describe character (simple or comminuted).\n- Describe pattern (transverse, oblique, spiral; complete or incomplete).\n- Assessment\n- Hallmarks: pain, tenderness, swelling, deformity.\n- Do not elicit abnormal mobility or crepitus.\n-\n\n- Initial management\n- Immobilise including the joint above and below.\n- Check and document neurovascular status before and after splinting.\n- Provide analgesia/sedation as needed.\n- Orthopaedic consultation for definitive care.\n- Adjuncts Open fractures: document bone/skin breach, irrigate, cover, weight- based antibiotics, tetanus.\n- Dislocations: attempt closed reduction if trained; then immobilise. If reduction fails, splint as found and refer.\n- Prioritise life-saving tasks over splinting, except when a femoral shaft fracture contributes to shock, where urgent reduction supports resuscitation.\n- Use caution with unstable pelvis, compromised splint anchors, additional distal fractures, partial/complete amputation, or non- viable limbs.\n- Principles of splinting/immobilisation\n- Long-bone fracture: include joints above and below.\n- Joint injury: include bones above and below.\n- Maintain functional alignment; for knees, immobilise in —100 flexion to reduce posterior NV tension.\n- Femoral shaft fractures\n- Apply inline traction to restore length, then immobilise.\n- Traction splints are for mid-shaft (diaphyseal) femur fractures.\n\n- Not suitable for pelvic fractures, femoral neck/peritrochanteric, or floating knee (ipsilateral femur + tibia).\n- Assess/document distal Neurovascular status before application.\n- Goals: improved alignment, pain control, reduced bleeding (tamponade).\n- Avoid excess traction; if Neurovascular status worsens, reduce traction or remove and reposition.\n- If no traction splint is available, use a long posterior plaster from ankle to waist or buddy-splint to the contralateral leg\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 62-year-old male collapses while participating in a long-distance run during a heatwave. He is brought into the Emergency Department confused and disoriented. On examination, his skin is dry and flushed. His observation are: BP:  mmHg HR: 145 bpm RR: 23 breaths/min Sp02: 96% on room air Temperature: 41.50C ECG shows sinus tachycardia. Blood tests reveal elevated creatine kinase and metabolic acidosis. What is the most appropriate initial management step?\n- **A)** Initiate immersion in ice water\n- **B)** Administer intravenous dexamethasone and secure the airway\n- **C)** Begin broad-spectrum antibiotics with aggressive intravenous fluid therapy\n- **D)** Administer intravenous paracetamol and chilled IV fluids\n- **E)** Apply cooling blankets and provide oral rehydration therapy\n- Explanation This presentation is classic for exertional heat stroke (core temperature >400C + CNS dysfunction + evidence of end-organ injury such as raised CK). The most important priority is rapid cooling to prevent further cellular damage.\n- Cold water immersion is the gold standard for exertional heat stroke and should be initiated immediately.\n- Hyperthermia\n- There are two types of hyperthermic injury that require consideration in the Primary Survey: exertional heatstroke and classic heatstroke.\n- Exertional heat - stroke is when heavy physical exertion, usually in young people, combines with environmental heat exposure. Heat dissipation mechanisms are overwhelmed and heat stroke results.\n\n- Non-exertional (Classic heatstroke )- heat stroke is classical heat stroke and is more commonly encountered in elderly or debilitated patients. It occurs when simple environmental heat exposure overwhelms homeostatic control mechanisms.\n- pathophysiology\n- Body temperatures in excess of 400C can cause direct cellular injury. Temperatures above 420C can directly cause cell death.\n- Increased core temperatures can activate numerous pro-inflammatory pathways. A systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) occurs in heat stroke, leading to multi-organ failure.\n- Dehydration through excessive sweat production, combined with vasodilatation, causes decreased renal and splanchnic perfusion.\n- Clinical features Nausea, vomiting, dizziness, mild alteration in mental status, temperature under 400c Marked elevation of core body temperature (>400C) with encephalopathy. The central nervous system alteration seen with heatstroke often presents rapidly, is nonfocal, and may include confusion, lethargy, or delirium\n\nTest Acid/base balance Electrolytes Renal function Liver function Muscle markers FBC & coagulation findings Metabolic acidosis ± respiratory compensation; elevated lactate Hyper- or hyponatraemia; hyper- or hypokalaemia Elevated urea and creatinine Elevated ALT and AST Markedly raised creatine kinase; hyperkalaemia; hypocalcaemia Neutrophilia; thrombocytopenia; prolonged clotting times; low fibrinogen explanation Poor peripheral perfusion Water and electrolyte loss Reduced renal perfusion; myoglobinuria in rhabdomyolysis Centrilobular hepatocyte necrosis Rhabdomyolysis Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Differential Diagnosis of Hyperthermia with Altered Mental Status Sepsis (e.g. pneumonia, urinary tract infection, meningitis, endocarditis) Tropical infection (e.g. malaria, typhoid) Endocrinopathy (e.g. thyroid storm, phaeochromocytoma) Status epilepticus Intracerebral haemorrhage, especially thalamic haemorrhage Alcohol withdrawal Anticholinergic toxicity Salicylate toxicity\n- Illicit drug use (e.g. cocaine, amphetamine) Neuroleptic malignant syndrome Malignant hyperpyrexia Resus 2021 guideline — Management of hyperthermia\n\nHeatsyncope Heat\n- Remove patient to a cool environment, cool passively Provide oral isotonic or hypertonic fluids.\n- Remove patient to a cool environment, lie them flat,\n- Administer IV isotonic or hypertonic fluids, consider additional electrolyte replacement therapy with isotonic fluids. Replacement Of 1—2 L crystalloid at 500 mL h-1 is often adequate. Simple external cooling measures are usually not required but may involve conductive, convective and evaporative measures. A 'cool and run' approach is recommended - Remove patient to a cool environment.\n- Lie them flat.\n- Immersion- Immediately active cool usingwhole body (from neck down) water immersion technique (1-26 oc) until core temperature < 390C. Where water immersion is not available, use any technique that provides the most rapid rate of cooling.\n- Internal cooling measures- Administer IV isotonic or hypertonic fluids (with blood sodium s 130 mmol L'I up to 3x 100 mL NaCl 3%).\n- Consider additional electrolyte replacement with isotonic fluids. Substantial amounts of fluids may be required.\n- In exertional heat stroke, a cooling rate faster than 0.1 OOC mimi is safe and desirable.\n- Assessment and Heatstroke (Current ATLS Guideline)\n- There are two types of hyperthermic injury that require consideration in the Primary Survey: exertional heatstroke and classic heatstroke.\n- Exertional heatstroke - typically occurs in younger individuals performing vigorous exercise in a hot environment. The heat generated from intense musculoskeletal activity cannot dissipate well, and leads to a rise in body\n- temperature.\n- Classic heatstroke - results from prolonged passive exposure to heat, such as during heatwaves.\n\n- The diagnosis of heatstroke - is based on a triad of hyperthermia (400C—43.60C), altered central nervous system function, and exposure to extreme heat or exertion. The central nervous system alteration seen with heatstroke often presents rapidly, is non-focal, and may include confusion, lethargy, or delirium.\n- Management\n- Treatment of Heatstroke/Hyperthermia. Heatstroke can lead to organ dysfunction and death if not recognized and treated early. Although less common than hypothermia in trauma patients, it should still be excluded if patients present with injuries after exertion/working outside in hot environments\n- First-line-2Cool water immersion is the first-line intervention; cooling the patient should proceed to 38.90C. Section Intervention Measure core temperature. If > 400C, heatstroke is likely. 1) Move the patient to a cooler environment 2) Start external cooling : Immediate.ooting\n- Immerse in ice water Ice packs/cold towels to junctional vascular points (neck, axillae, groin) Spray skin with water and continuously fan. "ospitatgooling Consider external cooling devices and intravascular cooling cannulas.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 28-year-old motorcyclist arrives after a high-speed crash. Paramedics have applied a traction splint to a deformed distal femur fracture. On arrival, the foot is cold, pale and pulseless with delayed capillary refill. There are no open wounds and the pelvis is stable. What is the most appropriate immediate step of management?\n- **A)** Increase traction on the splint to restore length\n- **B)** Refer to orthopaedics for manipulation under general anaesthesia\n- **C)** Remove the traction splint, perform urgent gentle inline reduction, immobilise in a long-leg backslab, and reassess distal perfusion\n- **D)** Observe while waiting for vascular surgery to review\n- **E)** Apply a long-leg backslab without attempting reduction\n- Explanation\n- Remove the traction splint, perform urgent gentle inline reduction, immobilise in a long-leg backslab, and reassess distal perfusion. Traction splints are for mid-shaft femoral fractures, not distal femur(supracondylar) injuries. In distal fractures, traction splint can worsen displacement at the knee and compromise the popliteal\n\n- First ED step in an ischaemic limb is a bedside closed reduction (gentle inline traction to correct length/angulation/rotation), then immobilise in a well-padded long-leg backslab and recheck pulses/DoppIer. Escalate urgently to vascular/orthopedic if perfusion does not return and for definitive treatment. previOUS\n	\N	[]	\N	f	62	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
c75b5baf-af1f-413c-bec1-fc072fd7fa18	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-7	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 7	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 7\n\ne VIRCEM INTERMEDIATE SBA   Overview . Comments\n\nWhich of the following correct about DC shock?\n- **A)** Synchronous DC Ij/kg\n- **B)** Synchronous DC 4j/kg\n- **C)** Asynchronous DC IO j/kg\n- **D)** Synchronous DC 4j/kg\n- **E)** Asynchronous DC 4j/kg\n- Explanation\n- APLS recommends 4j/kg shock\n\nResuscitation —.1 Council UK GUIDELINES 42021 Paediatric advanced life support Recognise cardiac arrest for he!p Commence/continue CPR (5 initial breaths then CV ratio 15:2) Attach de SHOCKABLE VT 1 shock J resume CPR interruptkXt5 After 3 shocks\n- Adrenaline 'WIO 10mcq (and every attemate cycle\n- Ambdarone IVBO S mg (and repeat S rnq ' once nure after Sth During\n- Chest - and\n- movide BMV with '00% o\n- yqen 12 Assess rhythm Retum of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) post arrest cue:\n- use an ABCDE approach\n- Aim for Spa, of 94-98% normal Paco,\n- Avoid hypotenson\n- Targeted temp\n- Glucose NON-SHOCKABLE PEA/asystote/brady 60 min for min Min interruptions Give adrenaline IV/IO IO mcg as as possible and then every '—S min Identity and treat reversible\n\nI 10-12 j\n- Vase\n- AN\n- started. V.\n- e 3-5\n-\n- rote dose mg\n- Key-points APLS\n- CPR - 15:2\n- Shock - asynchronous 4j/kg\n- Adrenaline: IO mcg/kg iv/lO\n- Amiodarone: 5mg/kg\n- If no Amiodarone then lidocaine: Img/kg\n- Mgs04 - 25-50 mg/kg for pvt/ torsade Asystole is most common arrest rhythm in children\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 4-week-old baby brought to the ED by her mother with complain of vomiting after every feed and loss of weight His VBGs is given. What is the most likely diagnosis?   A Pyloric Stenosis\n- **B)** Congenital diaphragmatic hernia\n- **C)** Biliary atresia\n- **D)** Intestinal obstruction\n- **E)** Congenital heart disease\n- Explanation\n- VBGs suggests hypokalaemia hypochloraemia and metabolic alkalosis Pyloric stenosis\n- In infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (HPS), hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter results in narrowing of the pyloric canal.\n- Clinical features\n- Recurrent projectile non-bilious vomiting, typically in a 3- to-6-week- old infant (usually male),but may occur in older infants.\n- Failure to thrive/weight loss may progress to increasing volume depletion\n- An olive-shaped mass may be palpable in the right upper abdomen.\n- Other features: multiple formula changes,tachycardia, decreased wet nappies\n- Investigation:\n- An electrolyte investigation reveals hypokalaemia hypochloraemia and metabolic alkalosis\n\n- Ultrasound shows pyloric channel length >17 mm and pyloric muscle thickness >4 mm.\n- Management;\n- intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement, followed by pyloromyotomy (open or laparascopic).\n- Complications of pyloromyotomy;\n- wound infection, gastric or duodenal mucosal perforation, or incomplete myotomy.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 45-year-old male patient with a history of alcoholic liver disease brought to the ED with massive upper GI bleed. On Examination, he is haemodynamically unstable. Initial fluid resuscitation has been started and now you've decided to activate massive hemorrhagic protocol. How will you transfuse the blood products?\n- **A)** 10 units RBC\n- **B)** 2 units RBC3 units FFP\n- **C)** 10 units RBC 5 units FFP\n- **D)** 4 units RBC and 4 units FFP\n- **E)** IO units RBC 5 units platelets Explanation NLC.E-recommends-for adults (16 or over) use a ratio ofl unit of plasma to I unit of red blood cells to replace fluid volume.\n\nTransfusion management of maior haemorrhaqe\n- pt bleeding/collapsed or in clinical shock\n- Trigger the major blood loss protocol\n- If trauma and <3hrs from injury, give tranexamic acid Ig bolus over IO minutes followed by infusion of Ig over 8hr (Consider tranexamicacid Ig bolus in non-traumatic Pt)\n- Aim to maintain following\n-\n- Take baseline blood sample for: Cross-match, FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Give initial MHP-IWhich include: 4 or 6 units RBC 4 units FFP Add 1 dose of plt in trauma patients earlier Reassess the pt If require further transfusion then take baseline sample for:\n- FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, LJ&E and Calcium level Order MHP-2 which include 4-6 units of RBC, 4unitS0fFFP, 1 dose of plt 2 packs of cryoprecipitate\n- Transfusion of MHP-2 depends On lab results eg: f Hb in lab value then transfuse RBC otherwise not Repeat the baseline investigation which include: FBC, clotting screen, fibrinogen level, IJ&E and Calcium level Correct complications: hypocalcaemia, hypothermia, acidosis Note: MHP indicates massive hemorrhage pack HB PLT PT ratio APTT ratio Fibrinogen 8-1 Ogldl x 109/L > 1 mm\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 55-year-old male with history of fall brought into the ED with head injury. Which of the following is not an indication of CT within 1 hour?   A Photophobia\n- **B)** More than 1 episode of vomiting\n- **C)** Basal skull fracture\n- **D)** Active seizure\n- **E)** Dangerous mechanism of injury Explanation guideline)\n\nCT head within 1 hour GCS less than 13 on initial assessment\n- GCS less than 15 at 2 hours after the injury on assessment\n- Suspected open or depressed skull fracture (fluid running from nose,ears, black eyes)\n- Any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum, 'panda' eyes, CSF leakage from the ear or nose, Battle's sign).\n- Post-traumatic seizure.\n- Focal neurological deficit (difficulties with understanding, speaking, reading or writing; decreased sensation; loss of balance; general weakness; visual changes; abnormal reflexes).\n- More than 1 episode of vomiting CT head within 8 hours Age 65 years or older. Any history of bleeding or clotting disorders. Dangerous mechanism of injury (a pedestrian or cyclist struck by a motor vehicle, an occupant ejected from a motor vehicle or a fall from a height Of greater than 1 metre or 5 stairs).\n- More than 30 minutes' retrograde amnesia of events immediately before the head injury.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 42-year-old male with history of RTA brought to the ED by paramedics with massive blood loss. Triage nurse unable to record BP and you are having difficulty to get IV lines access. What other sites you can access?\n- **A)** Internal jugular vein\n- **B)** Femoral vein\n- **C)** Subclavian vein\n- **D)** Intraosseous access\n- **E)** Saphenous vein\n- Explanation\n- IO insertion sites\n\nProximal Humerus (first choice) Distal Femur Proximal Tibia Distal Tibia Insertion site\n- The insertion site is on the most prominent aspect of the greater tubercle, 1 to 2 cm above the surgical neck.\n- Palpate 2-3cm above external condyle, Insertion just medial to midline to avoid tendon\n- Palpate tibial tuberosity, Insert 2- 3cm below + medial to tibial tuberosity\n- Insertion site is located approx. 3 cm proximal to the most prominent aspect of the medial malleolus\n- Aim the needle tip downward at a 450 angle to the horizontal plane\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone\n- Place needle at 900 angle to bone Age\n- Both adult and paediatric (>6 year)\n- Only in years age\n- Both adult and paediatric 1\n- Both adult and paediatric\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 4-month-old baby girl brought to the ED by her parents with complain of pain and bruises in her right thigh. Her X-ray is given. What is the indication of non-accidental injury?\n- **A)** Shaft of hernerus fracture\n\no\n- **B)** Hygienic healthy baby\n- **C)** This type of fracture expected in this age\n- **D)** Vitamin D deficiency\n- **E)** Crying baby\n- Explanation\n- EQMowingfragture-suggests NAI\n- Rib and spinal fractures\n- Multiple fractures of different ages\n- Fracture in infant who are not independently mobile\n- Long bone fracture (eg: humerus, femur, radius , ulna etc) in children<3 years\n- Salter—Harris type I and II fractures of knee, wrist and elbow.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 32-year-old male with history of RTA presented to the ED with Complain of pain in the Right shoulder. X-ray is given. What is the method of reduction of shoulder without traction?\n- **A)** Stimson technique   B, Kocher's method\n- **C)** Spaso technique\n- **D)** Traction Countertraction   E\n- Milch method\n- Explanation\n- There are several reduction methods, however, no traction in Kocher's method\n- Management of anterior shoulder dislocation\n- Give analgesia\n- Procedural analgesia for joint reduction eg: Entonox etc\n- After reduction check the pulse and sensation upper arm and obtain post-reduction x-ray.\n- Place the arm in a shoulder immobilizer or sling that maintains the shoulder in adduction and internal rotation and arrange follow-up with orthopedic\n- Shoulder dislocation with proximal humerus fracture require orthopedic referral.\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- Reduction technique Modified Kocher's method (external rotation) Place Pt supine with adducted arm to the Pt side Flex the elbow at 90 degrees then slowly externally rotate the arm No longitudinal traction is applied Perform the movement slowly to allow time for spasm and pain to resolve the elbow may be brought anteriorly and internally rotated to the opposite shoulder NOTE: reduction may occur at anytime during maneuver\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 40-year-old farmer while working in his farm, accidently ingested some fertilizers brought to the ED with excessive sweating and lacrimation. His observations are: BP  mmHg Pulse 45bpm what is next step of management?\n- **A)** Atropine\n- **B)** Adrenaline\n- **C)** Gastric lavage\n- **D)** Activated charcoal   E\n- Procyclidine Explanation\n-\n- Management of organophosphate poisioning Follow ABC protocol\n- All patients should have an intravenous line inserted.\n- Decontaminations\n- pry_2Contaminated clothes should be removed and or containing\n- personal belongings. Wet - the skin should be washed with water and or soap and stomach\n- contents aspirated if the airway is protected. Note - Decontamination should never take priority over supportive\n- care and resuscitation (ABC protocol)\n- Atropine\n- Atropine: 2 mg intravenously initially, followed by double doses (e.g., 4 mg, 8 mg, 16 mg, etc.) every 5 minutes until secretions are controlled. In case of deliberate ingestion or terrorism/warfare with nerve agent\n- Consider benzodiazepine and pralidoxime with above measures\n- (decontamination, atropine).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 55-year-old male with history of sleep apnea brought into the ED with respiratory distress. After initial assessment, you've decided to intubate the patient. When you evaluate him, you found distance between mandible and floor of mouth is 2 fingers breath. In this specific patient what makes the patient difficult to intubate?\n- **A)** Distance between incisors teeth should be three-finger breath\n- **B)** Two-finger distance between mandible and floor of mouth\n- **C)** Vocal cards fully visible\n- **D)** Distance between hyoid bone and Chin should be at least three- finger breath\n- **E)** Soft palate, Uvula, fauces, pillars visible\n- Explanation\n- It should be three-finger breath distance between mandible and floor of mouth\n\nLook externally Body habitus, beards, midface trauma, jaw mabcclusion, short neck, large tongue. Evaluate at-least 3 firws front base Of mandible to hyoid _ distmcm_2 finger from hyoid to thyrdd Mallampati o Obstruction epigbttits, head and neck cancer, ludwig's angina, neck haematoma, airway burn, 00 foreign body neck mobility rheumatoid , Ankylosing spondylitis Cervical spinal nnmobilization Prevbus neck injury or surgery 10 / 10 IO. A 40-year-old male came into ED with severe pain in the right flank. You are suspecting renal colic and want to commence NSAID for pain relief. However, Patient is allergic to NSAID. According to NICE guideline, what is the first line management in this patient?\n- **A)** Oral paracetamol B\n- IV Paracetamol\n- **C)** IV opioids   D\n- IV Ketamine\n\nO\n- **E)** 1M NSAIDs\n- Explanation\n- NICE guideline\n- Pain relief\n- First line NSAID any route (PR, oral, IV)\n- IV Paracetamol if NSAID contraindicated or not giving sufficient pain\n- If both NSAIDs and IV paracetamol are contraindicated or are not giving sufficient pain relief, or IV paracetamol is unavailable, consider an opioid analgesic (such as tramadol).\n- Do notoffer antispasmodics to people with suspected renal or ureteric colic. previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	63	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
36098257-7719-4278-9e5b-987fa6cf8128	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-8	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 8	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 8\n\nWhich nerve is the most likely damaged?   A\n- Long thoracic nerve   B\n- Axillary nerve\n- **C)** Pectoral nerves\n- **D)** Thoracodorsal nerve\n- **E)** Dorsal scapular nerve\n- Explanation\n- Long thoracic nerve paralysis is well known complication of breast cancer surgery\n- The incidence of long thoracic nerve palsy after radical mastectomy has been documented to be approximately 10%.\n- The serratus anterior muscle is supplied by the long thoracic\n- nerve, which takes its origin from the fifth, sixth, and seventh cervical nerve roots.\n- SA muscle function — enables to lift the arm above 900 (elevation).\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nYou are teaching a group of medical students and topic of learning is IV cannulation.\n\nYou ask the medical students what is the best method to differentiate needle in the Vein or Artery?\n- **A)** Ultrasound guide the tip of cannula\n- **B)** ABCs   C CBC result\n- **D)** Colour of blood   E\n- Waveform\n- Explanation\n- In routine Emergency Medicine practice, we use peripheral venous, central venous and arterial catheters in adults and children. Ultrasound has been used to assist line placement for over 20 years. We know that Ultrasound aids placement of central lines as well as peripheral lines, both in terms of speed of access and reduction of complications.\n- All cannulations, ultrasound guided or otherwise, are obviously undertaken in the light of the clinical setting.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 50-year-old female known case of HTN came into ED with complain of Severe headache. You are suspecting cluster headache. According to NICE guideline, what is the treatment of choice for this patient?\n- **A)** Ibuprofen\n- **B)** Sumatriptan\n- **C)** Aspirin\n- **D)** Metoclopramide\n- **E)** IV paracetamol\n- Explanation _ NICE recommends: Oxygen and/or a subcutaneous or nasal triptan for the acute treatrnent of cluster headache.\n\nTension-type headache Migraine Recurrnt of headache\n- Clinical features lasting from 30 minutes to 7 days which are not associated with nausea or vomiti\n- Headache attacks lasting 4—72 hours Clinical features\n- The headache has at least two Of the following four characteristics: Bilateral location. "essing, tightening, non-pulsating unilateral location\n- Pulsating quality. quality.\n- o r severe pain intensity. Mild or moderate intensity. Not aggravated by routine physical Aggravation or causing avoidance of, routine physical activity (such as walking or climbing activity (such as walking or stairs). Climbing Stairsl\n- During the headache at of the follcm\n- ing: Nausea and/or vomiting.\n- Photophobia _\n- ptwnophobia. One or more Of the following fully reversible aura\n- Visual symptMns such as zigzag lines, flickering lights, and/or Scotoma (visual aura iS the most common type of aura). Sensory symptoms such as numbness or pins and needles of the hand, arm, and/or face. Speech and/or language disturbance such as aphasia. Motor weakness (rare). Brainstem »mptOmS Such as vertigo, diplopia, tinnitus, ataxia. Retinal symptoms such as monocular scintillations or SCOtom a.\n- At least two of the following four characteristics:\n- At least one aura symptom spreads gradually at least 5 minutes, and/or two or more symptoms occur in succession.\n- Each individual aura symptom lasts 5-60 minutes At least one aura symptom is unilateral.\n- The aura is accornpanied, or followed within 60 minutes, by\n- Management\n- Mana me\n- Acute attack as (ibuprofen, aspirin or paracetamol)\n- paracetamol. aspirin, or a or in nonsteroidal anti\n- inflammatory combination with, paracetamol or drug (NSAID) an NSAID\n- Dose: Oral Sumatriptan\n- Chronic tvøe tension\n- Advise that a Of 6—10 as metoclopramide IOrng or sessions of acupuncture, prochlorperazine 10mg),\n- Consider advising that a course of of known physiotherapy and/or regular triggers and lifestyle changes such exercise be helpful for as Stress good Sleep people adequate hydration, Cluster headache\n- At least five attacks of severe o very severe unilateral orbital, supraorbital, and/or temporal pain lasting IS—IBO minutes (untreated), and\n- The headache is associated with a sense of restlessness or agitation and/or at least one Of the following to the heada Che Conjunctival injection and/or lacrimation.\n- Nasal a rhinorrhoea.\n- Eyelid s Welling. Forehvaad and facial Sweating.\n- Forehead and facial flushing.\n- Sensation of fullness in the ear. Miosis (excessive pupillary constriction) and/or ptosis\n- Manazement\n- Offer ox a a Subcutaneous Or nasal triptan for the of cluster headache. use omen at a flow rate of at least 12 litres per minute with a non-rebreathing mask Giant cell arteritis (GCA) It is a chronic vasculitis characterized by granulomatous inflammation in the walls of medium and large arteries.\n- as temporal arteritis) occurs when the extracranial branches of the carotid artery and branches Of the Ophthalmic artery, such as ciliary branches. are involved. can affect the its major brarwhes_\n- Cause Of G CA iS un known. New-onset headache\n- is usually temporal but location may vary generalized, occipital, or parietal temporal artery abnormality\n- Such as tenderness, thickening, or nod u I arity Visual disturbances Such as ot vision, diplopia, or changes to colour Vision. Scalp tenderness - particular the temporal and occipital arteries for example on brushing Intermittent claudication - typically over the masseter after minutes of chewing Systemic features\n- such as fever, anorexia, loss, de press ion polymyalgia rheumatic (PMR)\n- Such as proximal pain, Stiffness, and tenderness. Respiratory or ear, nose, and throat symptoms Features associated With cranial large vessel - such as Bruits (for example the carotid artery). Intermittent claudication\n- Management\n- Giant cell arteritis is a emergency\n- Early treatment with effective doses Of glucocorticoids may prevent serious complications such as Vision loss. Acute or intermittent Visual loss due to GCA is usually treated with one- off high intravenous glucocorticoid therapy.\n- Arange an urgent (same day ) assessment by an\n\nregular meals, etc with amitriptyline IO mg nocte, maximum up-to ISOmg Offer topiramate or proprardol after a full discussion of the benefits and risks Of each optim\n- Q indicated i n medkation to Sta+rd DO NOT Offer aspirin to young people under 16 years of age\n- consider a nasal triptan if simple analgesia ineffective\n- Combination therapy with a nasal triptan and an NSAID' Paracetamol if nasal triptan alone not effective Don't offer NSAID in pregnancy in second and third trimester. Offer sumtriptan or paracetamol\n- Medication overuse headache\n- Headache occurring on 15 or more days per month in a person with a pre-existing primary headache disorder, which develops as a consequence of regular overuse of one or more drugs that can be taken for acute and/or symptomatic treatment of headache, for more than 3 months.\n- It usually, but not always, resolves after the overused medication is stopped.\n- Causes\n- frequent use of pain medication\n- Ergotamines, triptans, opioids, or combination analgesics are taken on IO days or more per month.\n\n- Simple analgesics such as paracetamol, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), or aspirin (either alone or in any combination) are taken on 15 days or more per month.\n- — psychological comorbidities (such as anxiety and depression).\n- Genetics — the risk appears to be greater in females and in people with a family history of medication overuse headache\n- Neurological — acquired central sensitization may have a role in the cause of chronic headache, and appears to normalize after withdrawal of overused medications.\n- Management\n- If there are any red flags or a serious underlying cause of secondary headache (stroke, meningitis etc) is suspected,arrange admission or referral, the urgency depending on clinical judgement.\n- Early withdrawal of the overused medication(s) is the mainstay of treatment.\n- Advise to stop taking all overused acute headache medications for at least I month.\n- Advise that headache usually improves 1-2 weeks after drug withdrawal but recovery may continue for 2—3 months.\n- Arrange follow-up with primary care to assess symptom response to drug withdrawal and provide ongoing support.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 55-year-old male with history of sleep apnea brought into the ED with respiratory distress. After initial assessment, you've decided to intubate the patient. When you evaluate him, you found distance between mandible and floor of mouth is two fingers breath. In this specific patient what makes the patient difficult to intubate?\n- **A)** Distance between hyoid bone and chin should be at least three- finger breath\n- **B)** Soft palate, Uvula, fauces, pillars visible\n- **C)** Vocal cards fully visible\n- **D)** Distance between incisors teeth should be three-finger breath\n- **E)** Two-finger distance between mandible and floor of mouth\n- Explanation\n- It should be three-finger breath distance between mandible and floor of mouth\n\nAirway Assessment > LEMON airway assessment method can be used to assess for difficult intubation Look externally Body habitus. beards. rndface trauma, jaw mabcclusön. short neck. Wge tongue. Evaluate be at-\n- t 3 finvs base mancit* to from hbOd bone Mallampati 00 o Obstruction and neck cancer, Odwg's neck haemabma. aiway burn. body. neck mobility An kybsing spondylis Cerbcal spinal irnrnob&ation neck Éljury or\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 60-year-old male came to the ED with complaint of diarrhea and vomiting after an overdose of certain medications which prescribed by his GP for his clinical condition. You decided to give him activated charcoal, however his condition hasn't improved even after repeated doses. What medication would likely to cause of his condition?\n- **A)** Morphine   B\n- Propranolol\n- **C)** Paracetamol\n- **D)** Lithium\n- **E)** Digoxin\n- Explanation\n- Activated charcoal isn't useful in lithium toxicity.\n\nList of selected toxins where activated charcoal may be considered Aspirin Paracetamol Barbiturates Tricyclic antidepressants (e.g. amitriptyline) Digoxin Amphetamines Beta blockers Morphine Cocaine Phenothiazines List of toxins where Charcoal is NOT helpful and contraindicated Acid,and Alkalis / corrosives Cyanide Ethanol/methanol/glycols Eucalyptus and Essential Oils Fluoride Hydrocarbons Metals - including Lithium, Iron compounds, potassium, lead Malathion Iron Mineral acids - Boric acid\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 72-year-old male patient presented to the ED with history of dog bite on his face and lacerated wound on lower lip. The old man is unsure about his immunization status. You are decided to give him tetanus immunoglobulin. What is the reason to give immunoglobulin in this specific patient?\n- **A)** He is unsure of his vaccination status\n\nB\n- Age of Him\n- **C)** Wound on face\n- **D)** All dog bite need immunoglobulin\n- **E)** Prone wound need immunoglobulin\n- Explanation\n- Patients who are born before 1961 either sure about his/her immunization status or unsure we have to give immunoglobulin.\n- While those patients who are born after 1961 we have to follow tetanus immunization schedule accordingly and whether he is unsure about his immunization status immunoglobulin should be given.\n- Tetanus immunisations\n- Tetanus:p.rone-wounds-include;\n- Puncture-type injuries acquired in a contaminated environment and likely therefore to contain tetanus spores, e.g. gardening injuries\n- Wounds containing foreign bodies\n- Open (compound) fractures\n- Wounds or burns with systemic sepsis\n- Certain animal bites and scratches - although smaller bites from domestic pets are generally puncture injuries animal saliva should not\n\ncontain tetanus spores unless the animal has been routing in soil or lives in an agricultural setting. High-risk tetanus-prone wounds include any of the above with either; Heavy contamination with material likely to contain tetanus spores, e.g.\n- soil, manure Wounds or burns that show extensive devitalised tissue\n- Wounds or burns that require surgical intervention that is delayed for\n- more than six hours are high risk even if the contamination was not initially heavy. Tetanus prone wounds The wound is high risk and an adequate priming course of tetanus\n- vaccine received, but the last dose was more than ten years ago\n- The wound is high risk in a child aged 5-10 years who has received an adequate priming course but no preschool booster\n- The wound is tetanus prone, and the patient has not received an adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine\n- The wound is tetanus prone and there is uncertain immunisation status and/or the patient was born before 1961.\n- The preventative dose of tetanus immunoglobulin is 250 IU in most cases unless over 24 hours have passed since the injury or the wound is heavily contaminated, then SOO IU should be given. Immunisation table summarised below\n-\n\nImmunisation Status Those aged 11 years and over, who have received an adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine with the last dose within 10 years Children aged 5-10 years who have received priming course and pre- school booster Children under 5 years who have received an adequate priming course Received adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine but last dose more than 10 years ago Children aged 5-10 years who have received an adequate priming course but no preschool booster Includes UK bom after 1961 with history Of accepting vaccinations Not received adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine2 Includes uncertain immunisation status and/ or born before 1961 Immediate treatment Clean woundl None required None required Immediate reinforcing dose Of vacane Tetanus Prone None required Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine Immediate reinforcing dose Of vaccine One dose of human tetanus immunoglobulin3 in a different site High risk tetanus prone None required Immediate reinforcing dose of vaccine One dose of human tetanus immunoglobulins in a different site Immediate reinforcing dose Of vaccine One dose of human tetanus immunoglobulin* in a different site Later treatment Further doses as required to complete the recommended schedule (to ensure future immunity) Further doses as required to complete the recommended schedule (to ensure future immunity)\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA IO-year-old boy with history of asthma brought into ED with complain of widespread urticarial rash associated with breathlessness. You suspect anaphylaxis. What is the dose of adrenaline for this patient?\n- **A)** 0.3mg\n- **B)** 0.01mg\n- **C)** 0.1mg\n- **D)** 0.4mg\n- **E)** 0.5mg\n- Explanation\n\nAge group Adult Child>12y Child 6-12y Child 6month -6y Child <6months 1M adrenaline 1:1000 500mcg (0.5mI) 500mcg (0.5ml) 300mcg (0.3ml) 150mcg (0.15ml) 100-150mcg (0.1-0.15ml) .1\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nA 70-year-old alcoholic brought to the ED with complain of drowsiness, agitation and intoxication after initial assessment, diazepam was given. What is the next step in the management?\n- **A)** Lipid emulsion\n- **B)** Morphine\n- **C)** Dantrolene\n- **C)** -  Fomepizole   E\n- Ketamine\n\n-\n- Explanation Fomepizole (eg, 4-methylpyrizole, 4-MP, Antizol) has greater affinity for alcohol dehydrogenase than ethanol or methanol and has a considerably better safety profile than ethanol. CATEGORY B - Available within 1 hour The following drugs should be vailable within 1 h ur (i.e. usual within the hospital Andexanet alfa Cyproheptadine Dantrolene Desferrioxamine Folinic Acid (either calcium folinate or disodium folinate) Fomepizole (or Ethanol). Fomepizole is the antidote Of choice. Ethanol should be held i Idarucizumab L-Carnitine (levocarnitine) is not available. Indication Reversal Of anticoagulation from apixaban or rivaroxaban in adults With life- threatening or uncontrolled gastrointestinal bleeding (use according to local and national idelines —discuss with local haematolo\n- Sts and N PIS . Serotonin syndrome Neurol tic mali ant drome NMS Other dr -related h exia seek NP'S advice Iron Methotrexate Methanol, formic acid Ethylene glycol, diethylene glycol, methanol Dabigatran etexilate related active, life-threatening bleeding (use according to local and national guidelines — discuss with local haematologists and NPIS) Severe sodium valproate toxicity Mesna (in hospitals commonly using cycloph osph amide) Octreotide Phytomenadione (Vitamin Kl Protamine sulfate idoxine h dose in ection Cyclophosphamide Sulfonylureas Vitamin K de ndent anticoa He arin Isoniazid lants\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 2.5-year-old child brought to the ED by carer with bruises on his face. Child interaction with carers appears abnormal. You are suspecting NAI. What is the most common site of fracture in child abuse?\n- **A)** Skull fracture\n- **B)** Humerus and femur fracture\n- **C)** Ribs fracture\n- **D)** Clavicular fracture\n- **E)** Spine fracture Explanation\n- Following fracture suggests NAI\n-\n- Rib and spinal fractures Multiple fractures of different ages\n- Fracture in infant who are not independently mobile\n- Long bone fracture (eg: humerus, femur, radius , ulna etc) in children<3\n- years Salter—Harris type I and II fractures of knee, wrist and elbow.\n- IO. Which of following is not a sign of upper airway obstruction?   A\n- Expiratory wheeze\n- **B)** Stridor\n- **C)** Snoring   D\n- Hoarseness\n- **E)** Gurgling\n- Explanation Features/ sign of airway obstruction Hoarseness Stridor Drooling Snoring Gurgling Expiratory wheeze  caused by obstruction of upper airways caused by obstruction of upper airway unable to swallow secretion (upper airway obstruction) caused by obstruction at or above the laryngeal level (upper airway) vomit, blood or secretions in airway (upper airway ) caused by obstruction of lower airways\n	\N	[]	\N	f	64	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
63af734b-c050-4984-9876-8c16eda216d8	37d02a84-bffb-43c9-9290-0c6bd51d98df	high-yield-clinical-sba-practice-9	High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 9	quiz	# High-Yield Clinical SBA Practice 9\n\nwhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n- **B)** Entrapment of inferior rectus\n- **C)** Zygomatic Bone fracture\n- **D)** Entrapment of inferior oblique\n- **E)** Entrapment of superior rectus Explanation\n- There is a depression of the left orbital floor in keeping with an undisplaced fracture and a pathognomoniic "teardrop sign" is present. Orbital blow out fracture\n- MOI: Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly from a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a fracture of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents into the maxillary sinus.\n- Clinical features: Pt may have diplopia and can't look up and medially due to inferior rectus entrapment.\n- Test infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital nerve commonly damage in this injury.\n\n- Investigation: X-ray will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema.\n- Management: Urgently Refer all Pt with orbital blow-out fracture (e.g. diplopia or reduced eye movements) to a maxillofacial surgeon and/or an ophthalmologist\n- facial CT scan will be needed to visualise the fracture in detail and plan surgical repair.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 2:\nA 27-year-old sub-Saharan African triage nurse sustained needle stick injury while performing venipuncture. Which of the following is the high-risk source for HIV?\n- **A)** Semen Splash on eyes\n- **B)** Blood transfusion in UK before 1991\n- **C)** Sub-Saharan travel\n- **D)** Blood transfusion in USA\n- **E)** Anal intercourse Ex I n ti n High risk source for HIV\n\n- MSM ( men who have sex with men)\n- Female sexual contacts of MSM\n- People reporting current or prior injecting drug use\n- Sex workers\n- Trans women\n- People from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence (>I%)* i-e Originally from sub-Saharan Africa.\n- People reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where contact occurs\n- Individuals known to have/have had a mother living with HIV and who do not have documented HIV-negative status\n- Note - Anal course is incorrect unless, in the option they mentioned unprotected anal course with sub-Saharan people/ high risk source. HIV-Testing\n- people belonging_tmgroups at increased risk of testing_HUpositive HIV testing should be routinely recommended to the following individuals as they are the high risk source of HIV:\n- MSM ( men who have sex with men)\n- Female sexual contacts of MSM\n- People reporting current or prior injecting drug use\n- Sex workers\n- Trans women\n- People from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence (>1%)* i-e Originally from sub-Saharan Africa.\n- People reporting sexual contact with anyone from a country with high diagnosed seroprevalence regardless of where contact occurs.\n\nHIV testing should be considered for the following individuals:\n- Trans men\n- Heterosexuals who have changed sexual partner(s). People attending certain health services HIV opt-out testing is recommended in the following settings : Specialist sexual health services\n- Addiction and substance misuse services\n- Antenatal services\n- Termination of pregnancy services\n- Healthcare services for hepatitis B and C, TB and lymphoma Needlestick injuries and blood-borne viruses: decisions about testing-adults-who-lack-thesapacity-tos—nt\n- Where the patient is expected to regain capacity before a decision on testing is needed, testing should not take place until consent has been obtained.\n- If the patient lacks capacity, doctors should determine whether the patient has a valid and applicable advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT) or whether there is anyone with legal authority to make the decision (eg in England and Wales, an attorney with the relevant decisionmaking authority or a court-appointed deputy; in Scotland a welfare attorney, a sheriff-appointed guardian or a person authorised under an intervention order).\n\n- Where a patient is not expected to regain capacity before a decision needs to be made, and there is no ADRT, attorney or appointed deputy/guardian etc, doctors are entitled to make a decision whether to test the patient without consent and must do so in accordance with the law in that jurisdiction.\n- The doctor must make that decision by assessing whether testing is in the best interests of the patient (in England, Wales and Northern Ireland) or will benefit the patient and is reasonable in the circumstances to safeguard or promote the physical or mental health of the patient (in Scotland). The doctor must follow a structured decision-making process in making the decision, including seeking views from the patient and consulting a range of parties including relatives and those caring for the patient.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 3:\nA 32-year-old male brought to the ED with confusion, sweating and limb stiffness. His history revealed he is on quetiapine for his mental condition. His observations are: BP:  mmHg Pulse 80 beat/min RR: 22 breath/min Temperature: 38.2 oc What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Meningitis\n- **B)** Psychosis\n- **C)** Serotonin Syndrome\n- **D)** Malignant hyperthermia\n- **E)** Neuroleptic malignant syndrome EXPIanation\n- Serotonin syndrome should be rule out as clonus (involuntary and rhythmic muscle contractions) is common in serotonin toxicity.\n\nNeuroleptic syndrome NMS is an uncommon, idiosyncratic, life-threatening complication Of treatment with dopamine antagonists. NMS has also been associated With Other agents that block central dopamine pathways (e.g., metoclopramide).\n- Risk factors\n- antipsychotic medication — haloperidol, clozapine, olanzapine, quetiapine, and risperidone.\n- dopamine antagonists/ modulators other than antipsychotics, including- metoclopramide, lithium, tolcapone, amantadine, and certain (particularly tricyclic) antidepressants. Clinical features Hyperthermia - Temperature >380c on ONO occasions Muscle rigidity - can develop new muscle rigidity Altered mental status\n- Confusion, delirium, or stupor Sympathetic nervous system lability - Blood pressure elevation or fluctuation, sweating, or urinary incontinence. Hypermetabolism - 25% increase of heart rate and increase in respiratory rate above baseline.\n- Investigations\n- Blood tests — FBC, clotting screen, blood gas, LFTs, blood glucose.\n- Serum creatine kinase (CKJ myoglobin levels and urinalysis\n- Consider CT brain Consider CXR\n- Consider blood culture Management Stop dopamine antagonist or restart/continue dopamine agonist Su ortive the Ensure the patient has a patent airway and is adequately ventilated Rehydration - Aggressive hydration With intravenous fluids is recommended if the patient has more severe NMS Or rhabdomyolysis.\n- Ensure the patient is adequately perfused and aim for a urine output of 0.5 mL/kg/hOur, as long as rhabdomyolysis is not present, however, If the patient has rhabdomyolysis (creatine kinase >5 times the upper limit of normal range). Maintain a '—Airine outoutof 21 mL/ka/hou Serotonin syndrome An excess of synaptic serotonin in the central nervous system that clinically manifests as the triad of neuromuscular excitation, autonomic effects, and altered mental Status. It can result from therapeutic use Of medications, drug overdose, or specific drug- drug interactions.\n- Risk factors SSRI - fluoxetine, fluvoxamine, paroxetine, citalopram, sertraline, escitalopram, or dapoxetine. SNRI - venlafaxine, desvenlafaxine, duloxetine Tricyclic antidepressants\n- clomipramine, imipramine Opioid analgesics - pethidine, tramadol, fentanyl, dextromethorphan MOAIS\n- linezolid, methyl blue, isoniazid, phenelzine, tranylcypromine Serotonin-releasing agents - fenfluramine, amfetamines, methamphetamine. Synthetic stimulants-methylenedioxymethamphetamine [MDMA or ecstasy), cocaine, cathinones.\n- Clinical features Clonus (most common) - It ranges in severity from inducible clonus beats) to spontaneous clonus and is best elicited at the ankle. hyper-reflexia - It is usually more pronounced in the lovær limbs. her featu Anxiety, agitation, tachycardia, diaphoresis, tremor, sweating, hyperthermia(less common), dilated pupil, flushing, myoclonuse\n- Investigations Diagnosis of serotonin toxicity is primarily clinical, and diagnostic tests are rarely required. Consider F3C, ECG, blood culture, CT or MRI head\n- Management Severe serotonin toxicity Follow ABCD protocol In the majority Of patients it is best to sedate, intubate, and ventilate early, including induction of muscle paralysis to treat spontaneous clonus and hyperthermia. Sedation can be achieved either With morphine and midazolam or with propofol. hyperthermia treated with rapid cooling depending on the patient's temperature If severe serotonin toxicity is a result Of an overdose, then decontamination with a single dose of activated charcoal may be considered. -y—Consider — chlororomazine cvoroheotadine\n\nIf needed, replace potassium intravenously to maintain the plasma potassium around 4 to 4.5 mmol/L\n- Aim to increase the urine pH to >7.5 (rhabdomyolysis). Cooling technique for mild to moderate hyperthermia —\n- Mist and fan techniques\n- Ice packs to the patient's groin and axillae External cooling devices. For severe hyperthermia 1>38.50C despite conventional cooling methods) —\n- Internal or invasive measures such as cold fluid lavage (such as gastric, bladder, or peritoneal) or intravascular cooling techniques\n- Ice baths (although these are rarely used in practice). Consider benzodiazepine - should be given if the patient is agitated or has severe hyperthermia (rising temperature despite conventional cooling methods). Consider dopamine agonist (bromocriptine or amantadine) if supportive measures are failed. Dopamine agonists are preferred over dantrolene. Dantrolene is generally used if the patient has severe or rapidly progressive NMS.\n- It may aid resolution Of NMS-associated muscular rigidity and hyperthermia\n- After resolution NMS\n- re\n- start any withdrawn medication\n- Always start the antipsychotic at a low dose and slowly titrate while monitoring the patient closely to assess for signs Of recurrence. Delay restarting antipsychotic medication for at least 2 weeks following complete resolution of the NMS episode to reduce the risk Of recurrence.\n- Consider— cessation Of offending medication(s) Rhabdomyolysis can be prevented with early treatment of severe serotonin toxicity plus muscle paralysis and cooling. If already developed treat accordingly. Mild to moderate serotonin toxicity cessation Of Offending medication(s) benzodiazepine or cyproheptadine\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 4:\nA 23-year-old young man came into ED with complain of back pain radiating to the lower limb and associated with weakness and paresthesia. On examination, there is loss of ankle jerk. What other findings support your diagnosis?\n- **A)** S2 dermatome absent\n- **B)** Loss of distal pulses\n- **C)** L3-L4 dermatome absent\n- **D)** LI dermatome absent\n- **E)** Sl Dermatome absent Explanation\n- Sciatica describes radiating leg pain caused by inflammation or compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots (L4—S1) forming the sciatic nerve.\n- An L4/L5 disc prolapse will tend to cause pressure on L5 nerve root. Compression of L5 nerve root presents with weakness of extension of great toe, decreased sensation in the first web space.\n- An L5/SI disc prolapse will tend to cause pressure on SI nerve root. Compression of SI nerve root presents with diminished sensation of the lateral small toe, impaired planter flexion, decreased or absent ankle jerk. Sciatica (lumbar radiculopathy)\n- Sciatica describes radiating leg pain caused by inflammation or compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots (L4—S1) forming the sciatic nerve.\n- An L4/L5 disc prolapse will tend to cause pressure on L5 nerve root. Compression of L5 nerve root presents with weakness of extension of great toe, decreased sensation in the first web space.\n\n- An L5/S1 disc prolapse will tend to cause pressure on Sl nerve root. Compression of SI nerve root presents with diminished sensation of the lateral small toe, impaired planter flexion, decreased or absent ankle jerk.\n- The pain can have a sudden or slow onset, and vary in severity. It is felt in the back or buttock and radiates down the leg below the knee into the foot and toes in the distribution of the sciatic nerve. Other symptoms include tingling or numbness and loss of muscle strength in the same leg.\n- Alternative names for sciatica include 'radicular pain', 'lumbar radiculopathy', and 'lumbosacral radicular syndrome\n- herniated intervertebral disc ('slipped disc') —about 90% of cases. This rnost commonly occurs at the L5/SI level.The tmost common cause is age-related degenerative changes, but rarely it can be caused by trauma.\n- Spondylolisthesis —when a proximal vertebra moves forward relative to a distal vertebra.\n- Spinal stenosis —narrowing of the central and/or lateral spinal canals (typically causes pain, which is relieved by sitting and forward flexion and exacerbated with standing and lumbar extension).\n- Infection (rare) —for example, discitis, vertebral osteomyelitis, or spinal epidural abscess.\n- Cancer (rare) —more often due to metastatic disease of the spine than a primary tumour.\n\n- Breast, prostate, and lung cancer are responsible for more than 80% of cases of metastatic bone disease and the spine is the most common site of bone metastasis.\n- Risk factors\n- Smoking.\n- Obesity.\n- Occupational factors. o Whole body vibration — for example due to driving or operating machinery. The use of vibrating equipment is considered particularly hazardous. o Strenuous physical activity — for example, frequent heavy lifting, especially while bending and twisting.\n- General health and comorbidities.\n- Older age — peak incidence is in people aged 45—64 years.\n- Genetic influences\n- Clinical features\n- sharp pain, starting in the lower back, then shooting down the leg (most common)\n- constant pain in only one side of the buttock or leg, but rarely in both sides\n- pain that originates in the low back or buttock and continues along the path of the sciatic nerve down the back of the thigh and into the lower\n\nleg and foot pain that feels better when patients lie down or are walking, but\n- worsens when standing or sitting a "pins-and-needles" sensation, numbness or weakness, or a prickling\n- sensation down the leg in some cases weakness or numbness when moving the leg or foot\n- severe or shooting pain in one leg, making it difficult to stand up or\n- walk Red flags of sciatica\n- Bowel/bladder dysfunction (most commonly urinary retention).\n- Progressive neurological weakness.\n- Saddle anaesthesia.\n-\n- Bilateral radiculopathy. Incapacitating pain.\n- Unrelenting night pain.\n- Use of steroids or intravenous drugs.\n- Serious conditions with that may overlap with\n- sciatica include:\n\nCauda equina syndrome red flags Bilateral sciatica. Severe or progressive bilateral neurological deficit of the legs, such as major motor weakness with knee extension, ankle eversion, or foot dorsiflexion. Difficulty initiating micturition or impaired sensation of urinary flow, if untreated this may lead to irreversible urinary retention with overflow urinary incontinence. Loss of sensation of rectal fullness, if untreated this may lead to irreversible faecal incontinence. Perianal, perineal, or genital sensory loss (saddle anaesthesia or paraesthesia). Laxity of the anal sphincter.\n- Erectile dysfunction Investigations Spinal fracture red flags Older age. Major trauma at any age (such as a road traffic collision or fall from a height), mild trauma in people aged over 70 years, prolonged corticosteroid use, history of osteoporosis.\n- Structural deformity of the spine (such as a step from one vertebra to an adjacent vertebra) may be present.\n- Contusion or abrasion.\n- There may be point tendemess over a vertebral body. Cancer red flags Age over 50 years or under 18 years.\n- Gradual onset of symptoms.\n- Severe unremitting pain that remains when the person is supine or at rest, aching night pain that prevents or disturbs sleep, pain aggravated by straining (for example, at stool, or when coughing or sneezing), and thoracic pain.\n- Localized spinal tenderness. NO symptomatic improvement With therapy.\n- Unexplained weight loss.\n- Past history Of cancer — breast, lung, gastrointestinal, prostate, renal, and thyroid cancers are more likely to metastasize to the spine. Infection red flags\n- Infection (such as discitis, vertebral osteomyelitis, or spinal epidural abscess).\n- Tuberculosis, or recent urinary tract infection. Diabetes mellitus.\n- History of intravenous drug\n- HIV infection, use of immunosuppressants, or the person is Otherwise immunocompromised.\n- pain at rest.\n- Raised inflammatory markers.\n-\n-\n-\n- Take a medical history and conduct a focused musculoskeletal and neurological examination Do not routinely X-ray the spine to confirm the diagnosis. Consider appropriate investigations if infection, malignancy, or inflammatory conditions are suspected — this may include: FBC ESR\n\n- C- reactive protein\n- Blood culture\n- Urinalysis and culture\n- Management\n- Referral pathway\n- If there are red flag symptoms and signsthat may suggest a serious underlying cause, admit or refer urgently for specialist assessment using clinical judgement.\n- Arrange emergency referral to a spinal surgery service if there is suspicion of:\n- Cauda equina syndrome.\n- Acute spinal cord compression: new/progressive widespread neurological deficit.\n- Progressive limb weakness including major motor radiculopathy.\n- Arrange-urgent-refer.raLtQ-aspinaLsurgery-ser-uice-ocurgent-MRl (within 2\n- There is suspicion of tumour or infection.\n- Red flags are present in the absence of neurological dysfunction. . other all patients without red flags\n- Offer analgesia to manage low back NSAIDs first line\n- Do not offer opioids for managing chronic sciatica.\n\n- Do not offer gabapentinoids, other antiepileptics, oral corticosteroids, or benzodiazepines for managing sciatica.\n- Selfcare advise\n- Sciatica symptoms usually settle within a few weeks to a few months\n- Prolonged bed rest is not recommended.\n- Application of local heat (ensuring that the skin is protected) may relieve pain and have a muscle relaxing effect.\n- Keeping as active as possible and exercising regularly is important to reduce the risk of recurrence.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 5:\nA 23-year-old young female came to the ED with complain of fever abdominal pain and loose motions. She states that her symptoms began to appear 3 days ago after eating uncooked cold meat. Her observations are Temp: 38.50C BP  mmHg Pulse 98bpm What is the most likely causative organism?\n- **A)** Staph Aureus\n- **B)** Norovirus\n- **C)** Giardia\n- **D)** Rotavirus\n- **E)** Listeria Explanation\n- Listeriosis Listeriosis is a foodborne illness caused by the bacteria Listeria\n- monocytogenes. Pregnant people and people with weakened immune systems are at\n- the greatest risk of a severe infection. Contaminated foods are the most common cause of listeriosis\n- infections. The most common foods that cause listeriosis include:\n- Hot dogs.\n- Deli meats.\n- Fresh vegetables.\n- Fresh fruits, especially melons.\n- Unpasteurized dairy products (sterilized to kill bacteria).\n- Clinical features\n-\n\n- Chills Headache\n- Upset stomach.\n- Diarrhea Nausea and vomiting\n- Muscle aches.\n- Loss of balance.\n- Convulsions (muscle contractions that you can't control).\n- Management\n- Antibiotics commonly used to treat listeriosis\n- include sulfamethoxazole and ampicillin\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 6:\nA 22-year-old young female came to the ED with complain of sudden onset of lower limb weakness. She has history of sore throat IO days ago. On examination, her reflexes are absent in her lower limbs. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Guillain-Barre syndrome (CBS)\n- **B)** Central cord syndrome\n- **C)** Rheumatoid arthritis\n- **D)** Multiple sclerosis\n- **E)** Myasthenia gravis Explanation\n- Two-thirds of patients with GBS have a history of gastroenteritis or influenza-like illness (runny nose, sore throat, fever etc) in the weeks before onset of neurological symptoms.\n\nGuillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) It is a clinically defined syndrome characterized by motor difficulty, absence Of deep tendon reflexes, paresthesias without objective sensory loss, and increased cerebrospinal fluid albumin with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation).\n- Risk factors of patients with GBS have a history of gastroenteritis or influenza-like illness. hepatitis E infection Bacterial illness - to Ca mp ter jejuni\n- hepatitis E infection\n- Clinical features Muscle weakness -progressive symmetric muscle weakness usually affecting lower extremities before upper extremities and proximal muscles before distal muscles (ascending paralysis) accompanied by paresthesias in the feet and hands is typical. The paralysis is typically flaccid with areflexia and progresses acutely over days Paresthesia\n- Back/leg pain Respiratory distress\n- Areflexia/hyporeflexia dysarthria. Investigations Nerve conduction studies -\n- Facial weakness, dysphagia, diplopia,\n- areflexia/hyporeflexia prolonged distal and F-wave latencies and reduced conduction velocities; H reflex prolonged or absent\n- lumbar puncture - elevated CSF protein, normal/slightly high lymphocytes (<50 cells/mma).\n- LET-AST and AIT as high as 500 U/L Spirometry - may show reduced Vital capacity, maximal inspiratory pressure, or maximal expiratory pressure.\n- Consider antiganglioside antibody, Transverse myelitis\n- Transverse myelitis (TM) is a pathogenetically heterogeneous focal inflammatory disorder Of the spinal cord characterised by acute or subacute development Of motor weakness, sensory impairment, and autonomic dysfunction.\n- It is typically associated with multiple sclerosis. Risk factors\n- preceding infectious illness recent vaccination female sex history Of recent physical trauma Clinical features\n- motor weakness paraesthesias or sensory loss bladder symptoms: urinary frequency, urgency, incontinence, or retention bowel symptoms: incontinence or constipation Lhermitte sign\n- McArdle's sign paroxysmal tonic spasms upper motor neuron signs: hyper- reflexia, positive Babinski's sign, limb spasticity sensory loss/sensory level Investigations\n- MRI spinal cord MRI brain Serum aquaporin—4 auto-antibodies and myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein auto-antibodies Cerebrospinal fluid cell count, cell differential, protein level, IgG index, oligoclonal band Myasthenia gravis (MG) Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a chronic autoimmune disorder of the post- synaptic membrane at the neuromuscular junction in skeletal muscle. Circulating antibodies against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (AChR) or associated proteins impair neuromuscu lar transmission.\n- Risk factors family history Of autoimmune disorders genetic markers - Specific HLA genes cancer-targeted therapy\n- Clinical features\n- muscle strength fatigability The weakness worsens With activity or repetition (fatigable weakness) and improves on rest. ptosis diplopia, dysphagia , dysarthria facial paresis proximal limb weakness\n- shortness of breath\n- Investigations serum acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody analysis muscle-specific tyrosine kinase (MuSK) antibodies serial pulmonary function tests single-fibre EMG\n- CT Of chest - Should be performed in all newly diagnosed patients with adult onset MG to detect thymoma Extractable nuclear antigen (including SSA and SSB) serum and cerebrospinal fluid paraneoplastic auto-antibodies serolozv. stool culture— '—cerebrosoinal fluid cvtoloev and flovv\n\n- Management\n- intravenous immune globulin OVIG) or plasma exchange Np!ezIVIG is contraindicate in patient with IgA deficiency and renal failure. Supportive treatment - Vital monitoring and appropriate prophylactic anticoagulation for DVT.\n- Complications Respiratory failure\n- Bladder areflexia Adynamic ileus\n- Paralysis cytometry\n- serology for herpes simplex Virus (HSV)-I, HSV-2, varicella zoster virus, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, and West Nile virus Management\n- Corticosteroid - Treatment With 3 to 5 consecutive daily infusions of corticosteroids is associated With more rapid recovery of neurological deficits. methylprednisolone: 1000 mg intravenously OD Supportive measures — active rehabilitation, DVT prophylaxis, bladder catheterization. exchange (plasmapheresis)\n- munosuppressive treatment (azathioprine. mvcophenolate — recommended in patient with positive aquaporin-4 auto-antibody seropositive or myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein-IgG auto-antibody seropositive Management\n- Myasthenia crisis\n- intubation and mechanical ventilation plasma exchange or intravenous immunoglobulin Corticosteroid - prednisolone: 1 to 1.5 mg/kg orally once daily\n- Mild to moderate disease (class to pyridostigmine Consider — corticosteroid Consider— immunosuppressant (Azathioprine) Consider — rituximab Consider— thymectomy\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 7:\nA 40-year-old male patient arrives in Emergency department for his baseline blood test checkup. All his blood tests are normal apart from a potassium of 2.8 mmol/L Which of the following statements regarding the management of hypokalemia is correct?\n- **A)** IV potassium and magnesium\n- **B)** Fluids\n- **C)** IV fluids and IV potassium chloride\n- **D)** Potassium administration and Magnesium supplementation\n- **E)** Potassium chloride administration\n- Explanation\n- Management of hypokalaemia potassium replacement - is required urgently in patients with severe hypokalaemia (serum potassium <2.5 mmol/L [<2.5 mEq/L]) or in patients who are\n- Patients able to tolerate oral intake:\n- If the patient does not have diabetic ketoacidosis or HHS, potassium repletion is most easily done orally, even in people with severe hypokalaemia.\n- The serum potassium concentration can rise acutely by as much as 1 to 1.5 mEq/L after an oral dose of 40 to 60 mEq of potassium.\n- Patients unable to tolerate oral intake: intravenous potassium replacement is required\n- patients with diabetic ketoacidosis or HHS: intravenous potassium replacement is needed if hypokalaemia occurs, as there are usually marked potassium losses.\n- Follow local guideline for potassium administration and careful monitoring of the physiologic effects of severe hypokalaemia (ECG abnormalities with continuous cardiac monitoring, muscle weakness, or paralysis) is essential throughout repletion.\n\n- Hypomagnesaemia-correction\n- It is important to determine whether there is hypomagnesaemia (<0.75 mmol/L [<1.5 mEq/L]) because hypokalaemia can often only be corrected once the magnesium deficit has been addressed.\n- Intravenous magnesium replacement therapy is indicated in cases of severe mEq/L) or symptomatic hypomagnesaemia.\n- Patients with mild magnesium depletion are usually asymptomatic and can be managed with oral magnesium replacement.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 8:\nAn 8-year-old boy presented to the ED with scalp wound. He is known case of Haemophilia\n- **A)** After initial assessment, you plan for transfusion. What will you arrange and write in haematology sheet?\n- **A)** Platelets with complex factors\n- **B)** Two FFP\n- **C)** Factor VIII\n- **D)** RBCs\n- **E)** Factor IX\n\nExplanation\n- -Diagnostic-studies\n- Clotting factor replacement therapy should be given before any diagnostic studies (X-rays, CT scans etc.) are performed to evaluate a suspected bleeding problem, especially in the case of head trauma or suspected intracranial hemorrhage.\n- For routine joint bleeding, no radiographic studies are indicated.\n- GUIDELINES FOR EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT MANAGEMENT OF INDIVIDUALS WITH HEMOPHILIA AND OTHER BLEEDING DISORDERS\n- Triage\n- Individuals with bleeding disorders should be triaged urgently as delays in administering appropriate therapy, such as infusion of factor concentrate, can significantly affect morbidity and mortality.\n- Consultation with the patient's primary provider of bleeding disorder care, in most cases a hematologist, is strongly advised. However, Administration of clotting factor replacement to the patient should not be delayed waiting for a consultation.\n- Assessment\n- Treatrnent for a suspected bleeding episode is based on clinical history. Physical exam findings may be normal in the early phases of most bleeding episodes associated with an underlying bleeding disorder:\n- Spontaneous bleeding is common in those with severe disease (baseline factor levels <1%). When in doubt, administer clotting factor\n\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n-\n- replacement therapy immediately. Diagnostic studies Clotting factor replacement therapy should be given befoæany diagnostic studies (X-rays, CT scans etc.) are performed to evaluate a suspected bleeding problem, especially in the case of head trauma or suspected intracranial hemorrhage. For routine joint bleeding, neradiographic studies are indicated. For patients with hemophilia who have illnesses or disorders that necessitate an invasive procedure (lumbar puncture, arterial blood gas, arthrocentesis, etc.) or surgery, factor replacement therapy or bypass therapy to 100% rnust be administered in the emergency department pcioc_tQthe planned procedure or surgery. In this situation, consultation with a hematologist is strongly recommended. For an individual with known hemophilia, routine laboratory studies (PT, aPTT, factor levels), are not indicated in the treatment of a routine bleeding episode unless requested by the patient's hematologist. Treatment should not be delayed waiting for test results which may take several hours. Indications for Factor Replacement Therapy include: Suspected bleeding into a joint or muscle. Any significant injury to the head, neck, mouth or eyes or evidence of bleeding in these areas. Any new or unusual headache, particularly one following trauma. Severe pain or swelling at any site.\n\n- All open wounds requiring surgical closure, wound adhesive, or steri- strips.\n- History of an accident or trauma that might result in internal bleeding. Any invasive procedure or surgery.\n- Heavy or persistent bleeding from any site.\n- Gastrointestinal bleeding leading to moderate to severe anemia.\n- Acute fractures, dislocations and sprains.\n- Heavy menstrual bleeding leading to moderate to severe anemia or\n- volume instability.\n- Management Hemophilia A\n- Hemophilia A without Inhibitor\n- 1ST Choice - Recombinant factor VIII\n- Plasma-derived concentrate is a suitable alternative in an emergency situation when recombinant factor VIII is not available.\n- Hemophilia A or B with inhibitor Hemophilia B Hemophilia B without Inhibitor 1ST Choice - Recombinantfactor IX\n- Plasma-derived concentrate is a suitable altemative in an emergency situation when recombinantfactor VIII is not available.\n- For individuals with inhibitors (antibodiesto factor VIII or IX), require urgently discussion with the patient's hematologist.\n- If an individual with an inhibitorpresents ina life-or limb threatening scenario, the safest immediate action is to prescribe recombinant factor VIIa (rFVIla) at a dose Of 90 mcg/kg or activated prothrombin complex concentrates (FEIBA) at 75-100 units/kg.\n- Note- rFVlla is recommended over FEIBA for acute bleedingevents or procedures in hemophilia A inhibitor patientson emicizumab prophylaxisas aPCCs may cause thrombosis or thrombotic microangiopathy in those receiving emicizumab and should be avoided.\n\n#### Clinical Scenario 9:\nA 70-year-old male known case of HTN, and DM brought to the ED by his son with complaint of sudden behavior changes, confusion and agitation. You noticed; he is talking aggressive against staff members. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n- **A)** Delirium\n- **B)** Psychosis   C Parkinsonism\n- **D)** Alcohol intoxication\n- **E)** Alcohol withdrawal Explanation\n- Clinical features of delirium\n- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:\n- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.\n\n- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.\n- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.\n- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.\n- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).\n- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal). Delirium\n- Delirium(sometimes called 'acute confusional state')is an acute, fluctuating syndrome of encephalopathy causing disturbed consciousness, attention, cognition, and perception.\n- Delirium usually develops over hours to days and behavioural disturbance, personality changes, and psychotic features may occur\n- Delirium typically occurs in people with predisposing factors(such as advanced age or multiple comorbidities) when new precipitating factors(such as some medications or infection) are added.\n- Classification\n\nHyperactive delirium— may present with inappropriate behaviour, hallucinations, or agitation.\n- Restlessness and wandering are common,\n- Hypoactive delirium (more common)— may present with lethargy and reduced concentration and appetite. The person may appear quiet or withdrawn\n- Mixed delirium— signs and symptoms of both hyperactive and hypoactive subtypes are present.\n- Risk factors\n\nPredisposing factors\n- Older age (over 65 years). Cognitive impairment (such as dementia). Frailty/multiple comorbidities (such as stroke or heart failure). Significant injuries such as hip fracture.\n- Functional impairment (for example immobility or the use of physical restraints such as cot sides).\n- Iatrogenic events (such as bladder catheterization, polypharmacy, or surgery). History of, or current, alcohol excess.\n- Sensory impairment (such as visual impairment or hearing loss). Poor nutrition. Lack of stimulation.\n- Terminal phase of illness\n- Clinical features Precipitating factors\n- Infection - urinary tract infection, infected pressure sore, or pneumonia. Metabolic disturbance - hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia (including advanced carcinomatosis), or electrolyte abnormalities (including dehydration). Cardiovascular disorders- MI or heart failure. Respiratory disorders - pulmonary embolism or exacerbation COPD Neurological disorders - stroke, encephalitis, or subdural haematoma.\n- Endocrine disorders - thyroid dysfunction or Cushing's syndrome. Urological disorders - urinary retention.\n- Gastrointestinal disorders- hepatic failure, constipation or malnutrition.\n- Severe uncontrolled pain. Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal Medications — antihistamine, antipsychotic, antidepressant, antispasmodics, NSAIDs , anti- hypertensive, corticosteroid. Psychosocial factors - depression, sleep deprivation, visual or hearing impairment, emotional stress, or change of environment\n- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:\n- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.\n- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.\n- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.\n\n- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.\n- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).\n- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal).\n- Investigations\n- Delirium is a clinical diagnosis based on a detailed history, examination, and relevant investigations\n- Confirm a diagnosis of deliriumby carrying out a cognitive assessment based on the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) criteria, the short Confusion Assessment Method (short-CAM), or the 4As test\n- Arrange targeted investigations based on findings from the history and examination, for example:\n- Urinalysis— to identify conditions such as infection or hyperglycaemia\n- CXR or sputum culture- For chest infection\n- FBC -for anaemia\n- Management\n\n- Most people with delirium should be admitted for urgent assessment, close monitoring, and treatment.\n- If the person is deemed to not have capacity to consent, decisions should be made in the best interests of the person using the Mental Capacity Act 2005.\n- If the person with delirium develops challenging behaviour (such as\n- Address any underlying causes for the behaviour (such as discomfort, thirst, or need for the toilet).\n- Advise moving the person to a safe, Iow-stimulation environment (such as a quiet room).\n- Advise use of verbal and non-verbal de-escalation techniques (such as active listening, effective verbal responding, pictures, and symbols).\n- If these measures fail, consider pharmacological measure and seek advice from an elderly care psychiatrist, or elderly care physician. Depending on the specific situation (for example the person is a danger to themselves or to others).\n- NICE recommends - Short-term (for 1 week or less) low-dose haloperidol\n- Antipsychotic drugs should be avoided, or used with caution in Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies\n- Managemencprjnciples in end-of-life care include:\n- Considering non-pharmacological management of delirium in a person in the last days of life.\n\n- Treating any reversible causes of delirium, for example psychological causes or certain metabolic disorders (for example renal failure or hyponatraemia).\n- Considering a trial of an antipsychotic medicine (such as haloperidol or levomepromazine) to manage delirium, in the presence of intractable distress.\n- Monitoring the benefits and side effects of these medicines at least daily and adjust the prescription as the clinical situation changes.  IO. A 50-year-old male patient with history of Hep-C cirrhosis brought to the ED with complain of massive upper Gi bleed. Which of the following drug has shown to improve mortality in a patient with variceal upper GI bleed?\n- **A)** Tranexamic acid   B\n- Vitamin- K\n- **C)** Terlipressin   D\n- Omeparazole\n- **E)** Blackmore tube\n\nExplanation\n- Terlipressin has been shown to reduce blood loss from actively bleeding varices and confers a 34% relative risk reduction in risk of mortality.\n- Management of variceal bleeding Offer terlipressin or vasopressin, somatostatin to patients with suspected variceal bleeding at presentation. Stop treatment after definitive haemostasis has been achieved, or after 5 days, unless there is another indication for its use\n- Terlipressin has been shown to reduce blood loss from actively bleeding varices and confers a 34% relative risk reduction in risk of mortality.\n- Offer prophylactic antibiotic therapy_(usually Ceftriaxone) at presentation to patients with suspected or confirmed variceal bleeding.\n- In oesophageal varices — Use endoscopic band ligation in patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from oesophageal varices.\n- Consider transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunts (TIPS) if bleeding from oesophageal varices is not controlled by band ligation.\n- 1m gastric varices\n- Offer endoscopic injection of N-butyl-2-cyanoacrylate to patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding from gastric varices.\n- Offer TIPS if bleeding from gastric varices is not controlled by endoscopic injection of N-butyl-2-cyanoacrylate.\n\n- A balloon tamponade device can be used to quell the bleeding until the shunt is placed (Sengstaken-Blakemore for esophageal varices, Linton-Nachlas for gastric varices). previous  Next\n	\N	[]	\N	f	65	published	2026-10-21 04:59:27.642465+00	2026-11-10 04:31:15.49768+00	\N
